if(typeof BODIES_ORIG!=="undefined") BODIES_ORIG["gb-345"]="(Kept in shorthand.)\n3 May. Bistritz.--Left Munich at 8:35 P. M., on 1st May, arriving at Vienna early next morning; should have arrived at 6:46, but train was an hour late. Buda-Pesth seems a wonderful place, from the glimpse which I got of it from the train and the little I could walk through the streets. I feared to go very far from the station, as we had arrived late and would start as near the correct time as possible. The impression I had was that we were leaving the West and entering the East; the most western of splendid bridges over the Danube, which is here of noble width and depth, took us among the traditions of Turkish rule.\n\nWe left in pretty good time, and came after nightfall to Klausenburgh. Here I stopped for the night at the Hotel Royale. I had for dinner, or rather supper, a chicken done up some way with red pepper, which was very good but thirsty. (Mem., get recipe for Mina.) I asked the waiter, and he said it was called “paprika hendl,” and that, as it was a national dish, I should be able to get it anywhere along the Carpathians. I found my smattering of German very useful here; indeed, I don’t know how I should be able to get on without it.\n\nHaving had some time at my disposal when in London, I had visited the British Museum, and made search among the books and maps in the library regarding Transylvania; it had struck me that some foreknowledge of the country could hardly fail to have some importance in dealing with a nobleman of that country. I find that the district he named is in the extreme east of the country, just on the borders of three states, Transylvania, Moldavia and Bukovina, in the midst of the Carpathian mountains; one of the wildest and least known portions of Europe. I was not able to light on any map or work giving the exact locality of the Castle Dracula, as there are no maps of this country as yet to compare with our own Ordnance Survey maps; but I found that Bistritz, the post town named by Count Dracula, is a fairly well-known place. I shall enter here some of my notes, as they may refresh my memory when I talk over my travels with Mina.\n\nIn the population of Transylvania there are four distinct nationalities: Saxons in the South, and mixed with them the Wallachs, who are the descendants of the Dacians; Magyars in the West, and Szekelys in the East and North. I am going among the latter, who claim to be descended from Attila and the Huns. This may be so, for when the Magyars conquered the country in the eleventh century they found the Huns settled in it. I read that every known superstition in the world is gathered into the horseshoe of the Carpathians, as if it were the centre of some sort of imaginative whirlpool; if so my stay may be very interesting. (Mem., I must ask the Count all about them.)\n\nI did not sleep well, though my bed was comfortable enough, for I had all sorts of queer dreams. There was a dog howling all night under my window, which may have had something to do with it; or it may have been the paprika, for I had to drink up all the water in my carafe, and was still thirsty. Towards morning I slept and was wakened by the continuous knocking at my door, so I guess I must have been sleeping soundly then. I had for breakfast more paprika, and a sort of porridge of maize flour which they said was “mamaliga,” and egg-plant stuffed with forcemeat, a very excellent dish, which they call “impletata.” (Mem., get recipe for this also.) I had to hurry breakfast, for the train started a little before eight, or rather it ought to have done so, for after rushing to the station at 7:30 I had to sit in the carriage for more than an hour before we began to move. It seems to me that the further east you go the more unpunctual are the trains. What ought they to be in China?\n\nAll day long we seemed to dawdle through a country which was full of beauty of every kind. Sometimes we saw little towns or castles on the top of steep hills such as we see in old missals; sometimes we ran by rivers and streams which seemed from the wide stony margin on each side of them to be subject to great floods. It takes a lot of water, and running strong, to sweep the outside edge of a river clear. At every station there were groups of people, sometimes crowds, and in all sorts of attire. Some of them were just like the peasants at home or those I saw coming through France and Germany, with short jackets and round hats and home-made trousers; but others were very picturesque. The women looked pretty, except when you got near them, but they were very clumsy about the waist. They had all full white sleeves of some kind or other, and most of them had big belts with a lot of strips of something fluttering from them like the dresses in a ballet, but of course there were petticoats under them. The strangest figures we saw were the Slovaks, who were more barbarian than the rest, with their big cow-boy hats, great baggy dirty-white trousers, white linen shirts, and enormous heavy leather belts, nearly a foot wide, all studded over with brass nails. They wore high boots, with their trousers tucked into them, and had long black hair and heavy black moustaches. They are very picturesque, but do not look prepossessing. On the stage they would be set down at once as some old Oriental band of brigands. They are, however, I am told, very harmless and rather wanting in natural self-assertion.\n\nIt was on the dark side of twilight when we got to Bistritz, which is a very interesting old place. Being practically on the frontier--for the Borgo Pass leads from it into Bukovina--it has had a very stormy existence, and it certainly shows marks of it. Fifty years ago a series of great fires took place, which made terrible havoc on five separate occasions. At the very beginning of the seventeenth century it underwent a siege of three weeks and lost 13,000 people, the casualties of war proper being assisted by famine and disease.\n\nCount Dracula had directed me to go to the Golden Krone Hotel, which I found, to my great delight, to be thoroughly old-fashioned, for of course I wanted to see all I could of the ways of the country. I was evidently expected, for when I got near the door I faced a cheery-looking elderly woman in the usual peasant dress--white undergarment with long double apron, front, and back, of coloured stuff fitting almost too tight for modesty. When I came close she bowed and said, “The Herr Englishman?” “Yes,” I said, “Jonathan Harker.” She smiled, and gave some message to an elderly man in white shirt-sleeves, who had followed her to the door. He went, but immediately returned with a letter:--\n\n“My Friend.--Welcome to the Carpathians. I am anxiously expecting you. Sleep well to-night. At three to-morrow the diligence will start for Bukovina; a place on it is kept for you. At the Borgo Pass my carriage will await you and will bring you to me. I trust that your journey from London has been a happy one, and that you will enjoy your stay in my beautiful land.\n\n“Your friend,\n\n4 May.--I found that my landlord had got a letter from the Count, directing him to secure the best place on the coach for me; but on making inquiries as to details he seemed somewhat reticent, and pretended that he could not understand my German. This could not be true, because up to then he had understood it perfectly; at least, he answered my questions exactly as if he did. He and his wife, the old lady who had received me, looked at each other in a frightened sort of way. He mumbled out that the money had been sent in a letter, and that was all he knew. When I asked him if he knew Count Dracula, and could tell me anything of his castle, both he and his wife crossed themselves, and, saying that they knew nothing at all, simply refused to speak further. It was so near the time of starting that I had no time to ask any one else, for it was all very mysterious and not by any means comforting.\n\nJust before I was leaving, the old lady came up to my room and said in a very hysterical way:\n\n“Must you go? Oh! young Herr, must you go?” She was in such an excited state that she seemed to have lost her grip of what German she knew, and mixed it all up with some other language which I did not know at all. I was just able to follow her by asking many questions. When I told her that I must go at once, and that I was engaged on important business, she asked again:\n\n“Do you know what day it is?” I answered that it was the fourth of May. She shook her head as she said again:\n\n“Oh, yes! I know that! I know that, but do you know what day it is?” On my saying that I did not understand, she went on:\n\n“It is the eve of St. George’s Day. Do you not know that to-night, when the clock strikes midnight, all the evil things in the world will have full sway? Do you know where you are going, and what you are going to?” She was in such evident distress that I tried to comfort her, but without effect. Finally she went down on her knees and implored me not to go; at least to wait a day or two before starting. It was all very ridiculous but I did not feel comfortable. However, there was business to be done, and I could allow nothing to interfere with it. I therefore tried to raise her up, and said, as gravely as I could, that I thanked her, but my duty was imperative, and that I must go. She then rose and dried her eyes, and taking a crucifix from her neck offered it to me. I did not know what to do, for, as an English Churchman, I have been taught to regard such things as in some measure idolatrous, and yet it seemed so ungracious to refuse an old lady meaning so well and in such a state of mind. She saw, I suppose, the doubt in my face, for she put the rosary round my neck, and said, “For your mother’s sake,” and went out of the room. I am writing up this part of the diary whilst I am waiting for the coach, which is, of course, late; and the crucifix is still round my neck. Whether it is the old lady’s fear, or the many ghostly traditions of this place, or the crucifix itself, I do not know, but I am not feeling nearly as easy in my mind as usual. If this book should ever reach Mina before I do, let it bring my good-bye. Here comes the coach!\n\n5 May. The Castle.--The grey of the morning has passed, and the sun is high over the distant horizon, which seems jagged, whether with trees or hills I know not, for it is so far off that big things and little are mixed. I am not sleepy, and, as I am not to be called till I awake, naturally I write till sleep comes. There are many odd things to put down, and, lest who reads them may fancy that I dined too well before I left Bistritz, let me put down my dinner exactly. I dined on what they called “robber steak”--bits of bacon, onion, and beef, seasoned with red pepper, and strung on sticks and roasted over the fire, in the simple style of the London cat’s meat! The wine was Golden Mediasch, which produces a queer sting on the tongue, which is, however, not disagreeable. I had only a couple of glasses of this, and nothing else.\n\nWhen I got on the coach the driver had not taken his seat, and I saw him talking with the landlady. They were evidently talking of me, for every now and then they looked at me, and some of the people who were sitting on the bench outside the door--which they call by a name meaning “word-bearer”--came and listened, and then looked at me, most of them pityingly. I could hear a lot of words often repeated, queer words, for there were many nationalities in the crowd; so I quietly got my polyglot dictionary from my bag and looked them out. I must say they were not cheering to me, for amongst them were “Ordog”--Satan, “pokol”--hell, “stregoica”--witch, “vrolok” and “vlkoslak”--both of which mean the same thing, one being Slovak and the other Servian for something that is either were-wolf or vampire. (Mem., I must ask the Count about these superstitions)\n\nWhen we started, the crowd round the inn door, which had by this time swelled to a considerable size, all made the sign of the cross and pointed two fingers towards me. With some difficulty I got a fellow-passenger to tell me what they meant; he would not answer at first, but on learning that I was English, he explained that it was a charm or guard against the evil eye. This was not very pleasant for me, just starting for an unknown place to meet an unknown man; but every one seemed so kind-hearted, and so sorrowful, and so sympathetic that I could not but be touched. I shall never forget the last glimpse which I had of the inn-yard and its crowd of picturesque figures, all crossing themselves, as they stood round the wide archway, with its background of rich foliage of oleander and orange trees in green tubs clustered in the centre of the yard. Then our driver, whose wide linen drawers covered the whole front of the box-seat--“gotza” they call them--cracked his big whip over his four small horses, which ran abreast, and we set off on our journey.\n\nI soon lost sight and recollection of ghostly fears in the beauty of the scene as we drove along, although had I known the language, or rather languages, which my fellow-passengers were speaking, I might not have been able to throw them off so easily. Before us lay a green sloping land full of forests and woods, with here and there steep hills, crowned with clumps of trees or with farmhouses, the blank gable end to the road. There was everywhere a bewildering mass of fruit blossom--apple, plum, pear, cherry; and as we drove by I could see the green grass under the trees spangled with the fallen petals. In and out amongst these green hills of what they call here the “Mittel Land” ran the road, losing itself as it swept round the grassy curve, or was shut out by the straggling ends of pine woods, which here and there ran down the hillsides like tongues of flame. The road was rugged, but still we seemed to fly over it with a feverish haste. I could not understand then what the haste meant, but the driver was evidently bent on losing no time in reaching Borgo Prund. I was told that this road is in summertime excellent, but that it had not yet been put in order after the winter snows. In this respect it is different from the general run of roads in the Carpathians, for it is an old tradition that they are not to be kept in too good order. Of old the Hospadars would not repair them, lest the Turk should think that they were preparing to bring in foreign troops, and so hasten the war which was always really at loading point.\n\nBeyond the green swelling hills of the Mittel Land rose mighty slopes of forest up to the lofty steeps of the Carpathians themselves. Right and left of us they towered, with the afternoon sun falling full upon them and bringing out all the glorious colours of this beautiful range, deep blue and purple in the shadows of the peaks, green and brown where grass and rock mingled, and an endless perspective of jagged rock and pointed crags, till these were themselves lost in the distance, where the snowy peaks rose grandly. Here and there seemed mighty rifts in the mountains, through which, as the sun began to sink, we saw now and again the white gleam of falling water. One of my companions touched my arm as we swept round the base of a hill and opened up the lofty, snow-covered peak of a mountain, which seemed, as we wound on our serpentine way, to be right before us:--\n\n“Look! Isten szek!”--“God’s seat!”--and he crossed himself reverently.\n\nAs we wound on our endless way, and the sun sank lower and lower behind us, the shadows of the evening began to creep round us. This was emphasised by the fact that the snowy mountain-top still held the sunset, and seemed to glow out with a delicate cool pink. Here and there we passed Cszeks and Slovaks, all in picturesque attire, but I noticed that goitre was painfully prevalent. By the roadside were many crosses, and as we swept by, my companions all crossed themselves. Here and there was a peasant man or woman kneeling before a shrine, who did not even turn round as we approached, but seemed in the self-surrender of devotion to have neither eyes nor ears for the outer world. There were many things new to me: for instance, hay-ricks in the trees, and here and there very beautiful masses of weeping birch, their white stems shining like silver through the delicate green of the leaves. Now and again we passed a leiter-wagon--the ordinary peasant’s cart--with its long, snake-like vertebra, calculated to suit the inequalities of the road. On this were sure to be seated quite a group of home-coming peasants, the Cszeks with their white, and the Slovaks with their coloured, sheepskins, the latter carrying lance-fashion their long staves, with axe at end. As the evening fell it began to get very cold, and the growing twilight seemed to merge into one dark mistiness the gloom of the trees, oak, beech, and pine, though in the valleys which ran deep between the spurs of the hills, as we ascended through the Pass, the dark firs stood out here and there against the background of late-lying snow. Sometimes, as the road was cut through the pine woods that seemed in the darkness to be closing down upon us, great masses of greyness, which here and there bestrewed the trees, produced a peculiarly weird and solemn effect, which carried on the thoughts and grim fancies engendered earlier in the evening, when the falling sunset threw into strange relief the ghost-like clouds which amongst the Carpathians seem to wind ceaselessly through the valleys. Sometimes the hills were so steep that, despite our driver’s haste, the horses could only go slowly. I wished to get down and walk up them, as we do at home, but the driver would not hear of it. “No, no,” he said; “you must not walk here; the dogs are too fierce”; and then he added, with what he evidently meant for grim pleasantry--for he looked round to catch the approving smile of the rest--“and you may have enough of such matters before you go to sleep.” The only stop he would make was a moment’s pause to light his lamps.\n\nWhen it grew dark there seemed to be some excitement amongst the passengers, and they kept speaking to him, one after the other, as though urging him to further speed. He lashed the horses unmercifully with his long whip, and with wild cries of encouragement urged them on to further exertions. Then through the darkness I could see a sort of patch of grey light ahead of us, as though there were a cleft in the hills. The excitement of the passengers grew greater; the crazy coach rocked on its great leather springs, and swayed like a boat tossed on a stormy sea. I had to hold on. The road grew more level, and we appeared to fly along. Then the mountains seemed to come nearer to us on each side and to frown down upon us; we were entering on the Borgo Pass. One by one several of the passengers offered me gifts, which they pressed upon me with an earnestness which would take no denial; these were certainly of an odd and varied kind, but each was given in simple good faith, with a kindly word, and a blessing, and that strange mixture of fear-meaning movements which I had seen outside the hotel at Bistritz--the sign of the cross and the guard against the evil eye. Then, as we flew along, the driver leaned forward, and on each side the passengers, craning over the edge of the coach, peered eagerly into the darkness. It was evident that something very exciting was either happening or expected, but though I asked each passenger, no one would give me the slightest explanation. This state of excitement kept on for some little time; and at last we saw before us the Pass opening out on the eastern side. There were dark, rolling clouds overhead, and in the air the heavy, oppressive sense of thunder. It seemed as though the mountain range had separated two atmospheres, and that now we had got into the thunderous one. I was now myself looking out for the conveyance which was to take me to the Count. Each moment I expected to see the glare of lamps through the blackness; but all was dark. The only light was the flickering rays of our own lamps, in which the steam from our hard-driven horses rose in a white cloud. We could see now the sandy road lying white before us, but there was on it no sign of a vehicle. The passengers drew back with a sigh of gladness, which seemed to mock my own disappointment. I was already thinking what I had best do, when the driver, looking at his watch, said to the others something which I could hardly hear, it was spoken so quietly and in so low a tone; I thought it was “An hour less than the time.” Then turning to me, he said in German worse than my own:--\n\n“There is no carriage here. The Herr is not expected after all. He will now come on to Bukovina, and return to-morrow or the next day; better the next day.” Whilst he was speaking the horses began to neigh and snort and plunge wildly, so that the driver had to hold them up. Then, amongst a chorus of screams from the peasants and a universal crossing of themselves, a calèche, with four horses, drove up behind us, overtook us, and drew up beside the coach. I could see from the flash of our lamps, as the rays fell on them, that the horses were coal-black and splendid animals. They were driven by a tall man, with a long brown beard and a great black hat, which seemed to hide his face from us. I could only see the gleam of a pair of very bright eyes, which seemed red in the lamplight, as he turned to us. He said to the driver:--\n\n“You are early to-night, my friend.” The man stammered in reply:--\n\n“The English Herr was in a hurry,” to which the stranger replied:--\n\n“That is why, I suppose, you wished him to go on to Bukovina. You cannot deceive me, my friend; I know too much, and my horses are swift.” As he spoke he smiled, and the lamplight fell on a hard-looking mouth, with very red lips and sharp-looking teeth, as white as ivory. One of my companions whispered to another the line from Burger’s “Lenore”:--\n\n“Denn die Todten reiten schnell”-- (“For the dead travel fast.”)\n\nThe strange driver evidently heard the words, for he looked up with a gleaming smile. The passenger turned his face away, at the same time putting out his two fingers and crossing himself. “Give me the Herr’s luggage,” said the driver; and with exceeding alacrity my bags were handed out and put in the calèche. Then I descended from the side of the coach, as the calèche was close alongside, the driver helping me with a hand which caught my arm in a grip of steel; his strength must have been prodigious. Without a word he shook his reins, the horses turned, and we swept into the darkness of the Pass. As I looked back I saw the steam from the horses of the coach by the light of the lamps, and projected against it the figures of my late companions crossing themselves. Then the driver cracked his whip and called to his horses, and off they swept on their way to Bukovina. As they sank into the darkness I felt a strange chill, and a lonely feeling came over me; but a cloak was thrown over my shoulders, and a rug across my knees, and the driver said in excellent German:--\n\n“The night is chill, mein Herr, and my master the Count bade me take all care of you. There is a flask of slivovitz (the plum brandy of the country) underneath the seat, if you should require it.” I did not take any, but it was a comfort to know it was there all the same. I felt a little strangely, and not a little frightened. I think had there been any alternative I should have taken it, instead of prosecuting that unknown night journey. The carriage went at a hard pace straight along, then we made a complete turn and went along another straight road. It seemed to me that we were simply going over and over the same ground again; and so I took note of some salient point, and found that this was so. I would have liked to have asked the driver what this all meant, but I really feared to do so, for I thought that, placed as I was, any protest would have had no effect in case there had been an intention to delay. By-and-by, however, as I was curious to know how time was passing, I struck a match, and by its flame looked at my watch; it was within a few minutes of midnight. This gave me a sort of shock, for I suppose the general superstition about midnight was increased by my recent experiences. I waited with a sick feeling of suspense.\n\nThen a dog began to howl somewhere in a farmhouse far down the road--a long, agonised wailing, as if from fear. The sound was taken up by another dog, and then another and another, till, borne on the wind which now sighed softly through the Pass, a wild howling began, which seemed to come from all over the country, as far as the imagination could grasp it through the gloom of the night. At the first howl the horses began to strain and rear, but the driver spoke to them soothingly, and they quieted down, but shivered and sweated as though after a runaway from sudden fright. Then, far off in the distance, from the mountains on each side of us began a louder and a sharper howling--that of wolves--which affected both the horses and myself in the same way--for I was minded to jump from the calèche and run, whilst they reared again and plunged madly, so that the driver had to use all his great strength to keep them from bolting. In a few minutes, however, my own ears got accustomed to the sound, and the horses so far became quiet that the driver was able to descend and to stand before them. He petted and soothed them, and whispered something in their ears, as I have heard of horse-tamers doing, and with extraordinary effect, for under his caresses they became quite manageable again, though they still trembled. The driver again took his seat, and shaking his reins, started off at a great pace. This time, after going to the far side of the Pass, he suddenly turned down a narrow roadway which ran sharply to the right.\n\nSoon we were hemmed in with trees, which in places arched right over the roadway till we passed as through a tunnel; and again great frowning rocks guarded us boldly on either side. Though we were in shelter, we could hear the rising wind, for it moaned and whistled through the rocks, and the branches of the trees crashed together as we swept along. It grew colder and colder still, and fine, powdery snow began to fall, so that soon we and all around us were covered with a white blanket. The keen wind still carried the howling of the dogs, though this grew fainter as we went on our way. The baying of the wolves sounded nearer and nearer, as though they were closing round on us from every side. I grew dreadfully afraid, and the horses shared my fear. The driver, however, was not in the least disturbed; he kept turning his head to left and right, but I could not see anything through the darkness.\n\nSuddenly, away on our left, I saw a faint flickering blue flame. The driver saw it at the same moment; he at once checked the horses, and, jumping to the ground, disappeared into the darkness. I did not know what to do, the less as the howling of the wolves grew closer; but while I wondered the driver suddenly appeared again, and without a word took his seat, and we resumed our journey. I think I must have fallen asleep and kept dreaming of the incident, for it seemed to be repeated endlessly, and now looking back, it is like a sort of awful nightmare. Once the flame appeared so near the road, that even in the darkness around us I could watch the driver’s motions. He went rapidly to where the blue flame arose--it must have been very faint, for it did not seem to illumine the place around it at all--and gathering a few stones, formed them into some device. Once there appeared a strange optical effect: when he stood between me and the flame he did not obstruct it, for I could see its ghostly flicker all the same. This startled me, but as the effect was only momentary, I took it that my eyes deceived me straining through the darkness. Then for a time there were no blue flames, and we sped onwards through the gloom, with the howling of the wolves around us, as though they were following in a moving circle.\n\nAt last there came a time when the driver went further afield than he had yet gone, and during his absence, the horses began to tremble worse than ever and to snort and scream with fright. I could not see any cause for it, for the howling of the wolves had ceased altogether; but just then the moon, sailing through the black clouds, appeared behind the jagged crest of a beetling, pine-clad rock, and by its light I saw around us a ring of wolves, with white teeth and lolling red tongues, with long, sinewy limbs and shaggy hair. They were a hundred times more terrible in the grim silence which held them than even when they howled. For myself, I felt a sort of paralysis of fear. It is only when a man feels himself face to face with such horrors that he can understand their true import.\n\nAll at once the wolves began to howl as though the moonlight had had some peculiar effect on them. The horses jumped about and reared, and looked helplessly round with eyes that rolled in a way painful to see; but the living ring of terror encompassed them on every side; and they had perforce to remain within it. I called to the coachman to come, for it seemed to me that our only chance was to try to break out through the ring and to aid his approach. I shouted and beat the side of the calèche, hoping by the noise to scare the wolves from that side, so as to give him a chance of reaching the trap. How he came there, I know not, but I heard his voice raised in a tone of imperious command, and looking towards the sound, saw him stand in the roadway. As he swept his long arms, as though brushing aside some impalpable obstacle, the wolves fell back and back further still. Just then a heavy cloud passed across the face of the moon, so that we were again in darkness.\n\nWhen I could see again the driver was climbing into the calèche, and the wolves had disappeared. This was all so strange and uncanny that a dreadful fear came upon me, and I was afraid to speak or move. The time seemed interminable as we swept on our way, now in almost complete darkness, for the rolling clouds obscured the moon. We kept on ascending, with occasional periods of quick descent, but in the main always ascending. Suddenly, I became conscious of the fact that the driver was in the act of pulling up the horses in the courtyard of a vast ruined castle, from whose tall black windows came no ray of light, and whose broken battlements showed a jagged line against the moonlit sky.\n\n5 May.--I must have been asleep, for certainly if I had been fully awake I must have noticed the approach of such a remarkable place. In the gloom the courtyard looked of considerable size, and as several dark ways led from it under great round arches, it perhaps seemed bigger than it really is. I have not yet been able to see it by daylight.\n\nWhen the calèche stopped, the driver jumped down and held out his hand to assist me to alight. Again I could not but notice his prodigious strength. His hand actually seemed like a steel vice that could have crushed mine if he had chosen. Then he took out my traps, and placed them on the ground beside me as I stood close to a great door, old and studded with large iron nails, and set in a projecting doorway of massive stone. I could see even in the dim light that the stone was massively carved, but that the carving had been much worn by time and weather. As I stood, the driver jumped again into his seat and shook the reins; the horses started forward, and trap and all disappeared down one of the dark openings.\n\nI stood in silence where I was, for I did not know what to do. Of bell or knocker there was no sign; through these frowning walls and dark window openings it was not likely that my voice could penetrate. The time I waited seemed endless, and I felt doubts and fears crowding upon me. What sort of place had I come to, and among what kind of people? What sort of grim adventure was it on which I had embarked? Was this a customary incident in the life of a solicitor’s clerk sent out to explain the purchase of a London estate to a foreigner? Solicitor’s clerk! Mina would not like that. Solicitor--for just before leaving London I got word that my examination was successful; and I am now a full-blown solicitor! I began to rub my eyes and pinch myself to see if I were awake. It all seemed like a horrible nightmare to me, and I expected that I should suddenly awake, and find myself at home, with the dawn struggling in through the windows, as I had now and again felt in the morning after a day of overwork. But my flesh answered the pinching test, and my eyes were not to be deceived. I was indeed awake and among the Carpathians. All I could do now was to be patient, and to wait the coming of the morning.\n\nJust as I had come to this conclusion I heard a heavy step approaching behind the great door, and saw through the chinks the gleam of a coming light. Then there was the sound of rattling chains and the clanking of massive bolts drawn back. A key was turned with the loud grating noise of long disuse, and the great door swung back.\n\nWithin, stood a tall old man, clean shaven save for a long white moustache, and clad in black from head to foot, without a single speck of colour about him anywhere. He held in his hand an antique silver lamp, in which the flame burned without chimney or globe of any kind, throwing long quivering shadows as it flickered in the draught of the open door. The old man motioned me in with his right hand with a courtly gesture, saying in excellent English, but with a strange intonation:--\n\n“Welcome to my house! Enter freely and of your own will!” He made no motion of stepping to meet me, but stood like a statue, as though his gesture of welcome had fixed him into stone. The instant, however, that I had stepped over the threshold, he moved impulsively forward, and holding out his hand grasped mine with a strength which made me wince, an effect which was not lessened by the fact that it seemed as cold as ice--more like the hand of a dead than a living man. Again he said:--\n\n“Welcome to my house. Come freely. Go safely; and leave something of the happiness you bring!” The strength of the handshake was so much akin to that which I had noticed in the driver, whose face I had not seen, that for a moment I doubted if it were not the same person to whom I was speaking; so to make sure, I said interrogatively:--\n\n“Count Dracula?” He bowed in a courtly way as he replied:--\n\n“I am Dracula; and I bid you welcome, Mr. Harker, to my house. Come in; the night air is chill, and you must need to eat and rest.” As he was speaking, he put the lamp on a bracket on the wall, and stepping out, took my luggage; he had carried it in before I could forestall him. I protested but he insisted:--\n\n“Nay, sir, you are my guest. It is late, and my people are not available. Let me see to your comfort myself.” He insisted on carrying my traps along the passage, and then up a great winding stair, and along another great passage, on whose stone floor our steps rang heavily. At the end of this he threw open a heavy door, and I rejoiced to see within a well-lit room in which a table was spread for supper, and on whose mighty hearth a great fire of logs, freshly replenished, flamed and flared.\n\nThe Count halted, putting down my bags, closed the door, and crossing the room, opened another door, which led into a small octagonal room lit by a single lamp, and seemingly without a window of any sort. Passing through this, he opened another door, and motioned me to enter. It was a welcome sight; for here was a great bedroom well lighted and warmed with another log fire,--also added to but lately, for the top logs were fresh--which sent a hollow roar up the wide chimney. The Count himself left my luggage inside and withdrew, saying, before he closed the door:--\n\n“You will need, after your journey, to refresh yourself by making your toilet. I trust you will find all you wish. When you are ready, come into the other room, where you will find your supper prepared.”\n\nThe light and warmth and the Count’s courteous welcome seemed to have dissipated all my doubts and fears. Having then reached my normal state, I discovered that I was half famished with hunger; so making a hasty toilet, I went into the other room.\n\nI found supper already laid out. My host, who stood on one side of the great fireplace, leaning against the stonework, made a graceful wave of his hand to the table, and said:--\n\n“I pray you, be seated and sup how you please. You will, I trust, excuse me that I do not join you; but I have dined already, and I do not sup.”\n\nI handed to him the sealed letter which Mr. Hawkins had entrusted to me. He opened it and read it gravely; then, with a charming smile, he handed it to me to read. One passage of it, at least, gave me a thrill of pleasure.\n\n“I must regret that an attack of gout, from which malady I am a constant sufferer, forbids absolutely any travelling on my part for some time to come; but I am happy to say I can send a sufficient substitute, one in whom I have every possible confidence. He is a young man, full of energy and talent in his own way, and of a very faithful disposition. He is discreet and silent, and has grown into manhood in my service. He shall be ready to attend on you when you will during his stay, and shall take your instructions in all matters.”\n\nThe Count himself came forward and took off the cover of a dish, and I fell to at once on an excellent roast chicken. This, with some cheese and a salad and a bottle of old Tokay, of which I had two glasses, was my supper. During the time I was eating it the Count asked me many questions as to my journey, and I told him by degrees all I had experienced.\n\nBy this time I had finished my supper, and by my host’s desire had drawn up a chair by the fire and begun to smoke a cigar which he offered me, at the same time excusing himself that he did not smoke. I had now an opportunity of observing him, and found him of a very marked physiognomy.\n\nHis face was a strong--a very strong--aquiline, with high bridge of the thin nose and peculiarly arched nostrils; with lofty domed forehead, and hair growing scantily round the temples but profusely elsewhere. His eyebrows were very massive, almost meeting over the nose, and with bushy hair that seemed to curl in its own profusion. The mouth, so far as I could see it under the heavy moustache, was fixed and rather cruel-looking, with peculiarly sharp white teeth; these protruded over the lips, whose remarkable ruddiness showed astonishing vitality in a man of his years. For the rest, his ears were pale, and at the tops extremely pointed; the chin was broad and strong, and the cheeks firm though thin. The general effect was one of extraordinary pallor.\n\nHitherto I had noticed the backs of his hands as they lay on his knees in the firelight, and they had seemed rather white and fine; but seeing them now close to me, I could not but notice that they were rather coarse--broad, with squat fingers. Strange to say, there were hairs in the centre of the palm. The nails were long and fine, and cut to a sharp point. As the Count leaned over me and his hands touched me, I could not repress a shudder. It may have been that his breath was rank, but a horrible feeling of nausea came over me, which, do what I would, I could not conceal. The Count, evidently noticing it, drew back; and with a grim sort of smile, which showed more than he had yet done his protuberant teeth, sat himself down again on his own side of the fireplace. We were both silent for a while; and as I looked towards the window I saw the first dim streak of the coming dawn. There seemed a strange stillness over everything; but as I listened I heard as if from down below in the valley the howling of many wolves. The Count’s eyes gleamed, and he said:--\n\n“Listen to them--the children of the night. What music they make!” Seeing, I suppose, some expression in my face strange to him, he added:--\n\n“Ah, sir, you dwellers in the city cannot enter into the feelings of the hunter.” Then he rose and said:--\n\n“But you must be tired. Your bedroom is all ready, and to-morrow you shall sleep as late as you will. I have to be away till the afternoon; so sleep well and dream well!” With a courteous bow, he opened for me himself the door to the octagonal room, and I entered my bedroom....\n\nI am all in a sea of wonders. I doubt; I fear; I think strange things, which I dare not confess to my own soul. God keep me, if only for the sake of those dear to me!\n\n7 May.--It is again early morning, but I have rested and enjoyed the last twenty-four hours. I slept till late in the day, and awoke of my own accord. When I had dressed myself I went into the room where we had supped, and found a cold breakfast laid out, with coffee kept hot by the pot being placed on the hearth. There was a card on the table, on which was written:--\n\n“I have to be absent for a while. Do not wait for me.--D.” I set to and enjoyed a hearty meal. When I had done, I looked for a bell, so that I might let the servants know I had finished; but I could not find one. There are certainly odd deficiencies in the house, considering the extraordinary evidences of wealth which are round me. The table service is of gold, and so beautifully wrought that it must be of immense value. The curtains and upholstery of the chairs and sofas and the hangings of my bed are of the costliest and most beautiful fabrics, and must have been of fabulous value when they were made, for they are centuries old, though in excellent order. I saw something like them in Hampton Court, but there they were worn and frayed and moth-eaten. But still in none of the rooms is there a mirror. There is not even a toilet glass on my table, and I had to get the little shaving glass from my bag before I could either shave or brush my hair. I have not yet seen a servant anywhere, or heard a sound near the castle except the howling of wolves. Some time after I had finished my meal--I do not know whether to call it breakfast or dinner, for it was between five and six o’clock when I had it--I looked about for something to read, for I did not like to go about the castle until I had asked the Count’s permission. There was absolutely nothing in the room, book, newspaper, or even writing materials; so I opened another door in the room and found a sort of library. The door opposite mine I tried, but found it locked.\n\nIn the library I found, to my great delight, a vast number of English books, whole shelves full of them, and bound volumes of magazines and newspapers. A table in the centre was littered with English magazines and newspapers, though none of them were of very recent date. The books were of the most varied kind--history, geography, politics, political economy, botany, geology, law--all relating to England and English life and customs and manners. There were even such books of reference as the London Directory, the “Red” and “Blue” books, Whitaker’s Almanac, the Army and Navy Lists, and--it somehow gladdened my heart to see it--the Law List.\n\nWhilst I was looking at the books, the door opened, and the Count entered. He saluted me in a hearty way, and hoped that I had had a good night’s rest. Then he went on:--\n\n“I am glad you found your way in here, for I am sure there is much that will interest you. These companions”--and he laid his hand on some of the books--“have been good friends to me, and for some years past, ever since I had the idea of going to London, have given me many, many hours of pleasure. Through them I have come to know your great England; and to know her is to love her. I long to go through the crowded streets of your mighty London, to be in the midst of the whirl and rush of humanity, to share its life, its change, its death, and all that makes it what it is. But alas! as yet I only know your tongue through books. To you, my friend, I look that I know it to speak.”\n\n“But, Count,” I said, “you know and speak English thoroughly!” He bowed gravely.\n\n“I thank you, my friend, for your all too-flattering estimate, but yet I fear that I am but a little way on the road I would travel. True, I know the grammar and the words, but yet I know not how to speak them.”\n\n“Indeed,” I said, “you speak excellently.”\n\n“Not so,” he answered. “Well, I know that, did I move and speak in your London, none there are who would not know me for a stranger. That is not enough for me. Here I am noble; I am boyar; the common people know me, and I am master. But a stranger in a strange land, he is no one; men know him not--and to know not is to care not for. I am content if I am like the rest, so that no man stops if he see me, or pause in his speaking if he hear my words, ‘Ha, ha! a stranger!’ I have been so long master that I would be master still--or at least that none other should be master of me. You come to me not alone as agent of my friend Peter Hawkins, of Exeter, to tell me all about my new estate in London. You shall, I trust, rest here with me awhile, so that by our talking I may learn the English intonation; and I would that you tell me when I make error, even of the smallest, in my speaking. I am sorry that I had to be away so long to-day; but you will, I know, forgive one who has so many important affairs in hand.”\n\nOf course I said all I could about being willing, and asked if I might come into that room when I chose. He answered: “Yes, certainly,” and added:--\n\n“You may go anywhere you wish in the castle, except where the doors are locked, where of course you will not wish to go. There is reason that all things are as they are, and did you see with my eyes and know with my knowledge, you would perhaps better understand.” I said I was sure of this, and then he went on:--\n\n“We are in Transylvania; and Transylvania is not England. Our ways are not your ways, and there shall be to you many strange things. Nay, from what you have told me of your experiences already, you know something of what strange things there may be.”\n\nThis led to much conversation; and as it was evident that he wanted to talk, if only for talking’s sake, I asked him many questions regarding things that had already happened to me or come within my notice. Sometimes he sheered off the subject, or turned the conversation by pretending not to understand; but generally he answered all I asked most frankly. Then as time went on, and I had got somewhat bolder, I asked him of some of the strange things of the preceding night, as, for instance, why the coachman went to the places where he had seen the blue flames. He then explained to me that it was commonly believed that on a certain night of the year--last night, in fact, when all evil spirits are supposed to have unchecked sway--a blue flame is seen over any place where treasure has been concealed. “That treasure has been hidden,” he went on, “in the region through which you came last night, there can be but little doubt; for it was the ground fought over for centuries by the Wallachian, the Saxon, and the Turk. Why, there is hardly a foot of soil in all this region that has not been enriched by the blood of men, patriots or invaders. In old days there were stirring times, when the Austrian and the Hungarian came up in hordes, and the patriots went out to meet them--men and women, the aged and the children too--and waited their coming on the rocks above the passes, that they might sweep destruction on them with their artificial avalanches. When the invader was triumphant he found but little, for whatever there was had been sheltered in the friendly soil.”\n\n“But how,” said I, “can it have remained so long undiscovered, when there is a sure index to it if men will but take the trouble to look?” The Count smiled, and as his lips ran back over his gums, the long, sharp, canine teeth showed out strangely; he answered:--\n\n“Because your peasant is at heart a coward and a fool! Those flames only appear on one night; and on that night no man of this land will, if he can help it, stir without his doors. And, dear sir, even if he did he would not know what to do. Why, even the peasant that you tell me of who marked the place of the flame would not know where to look in daylight even for his own work. Even you would not, I dare be sworn, be able to find these places again?”\n\n“There you are right,” I said. “I know no more than the dead where even to look for them.” Then we drifted into other matters.\n\n“Come,” he said at last, “tell me of London and of the house which you have procured for me.” With an apology for my remissness, I went into my own room to get the papers from my bag. Whilst I was placing them in order I heard a rattling of china and silver in the next room, and as I passed through, noticed that the table had been cleared and the lamp lit, for it was by this time deep into the dark. The lamps were also lit in the study or library, and I found the Count lying on the sofa, reading, of all things in the world, an English Bradshaw’s Guide. When I came in he cleared the books and papers from the table; and with him I went into plans and deeds and figures of all sorts. He was interested in everything, and asked me a myriad questions about the place and its surroundings. He clearly had studied beforehand all he could get on the subject of the neighbourhood, for he evidently at the end knew very much more than I did. When I remarked this, he answered:--\n\n“Well, but, my friend, is it not needful that I should? When I go there I shall be all alone, and my friend Harker Jonathan--nay, pardon me, I fall into my country’s habit of putting your patronymic first--my friend Jonathan Harker will not be by my side to correct and aid me. He will be in Exeter, miles away, probably working at papers of the law with my other friend, Peter Hawkins. So!”\n\nWe went thoroughly into the business of the purchase of the estate at Purfleet. When I had told him the facts and got his signature to the necessary papers, and had written a letter with them ready to post to Mr. Hawkins, he began to ask me how I had come across so suitable a place. I read to him the notes which I had made at the time, and which I inscribe here:--\n\n“At Purfleet, on a by-road, I came across just such a place as seemed to be required, and where was displayed a dilapidated notice that the place was for sale. It is surrounded by a high wall, of ancient structure, built of heavy stones, and has not been repaired for a large number of years. The closed gates are of heavy old oak and iron, all eaten with rust.\n\n“The estate is called Carfax, no doubt a corruption of the old Quatre Face, as the house is four-sided, agreeing with the cardinal points of the compass. It contains in all some twenty acres, quite surrounded by the solid stone wall above mentioned. There are many trees on it, which make it in places gloomy, and there is a deep, dark-looking pond or small lake, evidently fed by some springs, as the water is clear and flows away in a fair-sized stream. The house is very large and of all periods back, I should say, to mediæval times, for one part is of stone immensely thick, with only a few windows high up and heavily barred with iron. It looks like part of a keep, and is close to an old chapel or church. I could not enter it, as I had not the key of the door leading to it from the house, but I have taken with my kodak views of it from various points. The house has been added to, but in a very straggling way, and I can only guess at the amount of ground it covers, which must be very great. There are but few houses close at hand, one being a very large house only recently added to and formed into a private lunatic asylum. It is not, however, visible from the grounds.”\n\nWhen I had finished, he said:--\n\n“I am glad that it is old and big. I myself am of an old family, and to live in a new house would kill me. A house cannot be made habitable in a day; and, after all, how few days go to make up a century. I rejoice also that there is a chapel of old times. We Transylvanian nobles love not to think that our bones may lie amongst the common dead. I seek not gaiety nor mirth, not the bright voluptuousness of much sunshine and sparkling waters which please the young and gay. I am no longer young; and my heart, through weary years of mourning over the dead, is not attuned to mirth. Moreover, the walls of my castle are broken; the shadows are many, and the wind breathes cold through the broken battlements and casements. I love the shade and the shadow, and would be alone with my thoughts when I may.” Somehow his words and his look did not seem to accord, or else it was that his cast of face made his smile look malignant and saturnine.\n\nPresently, with an excuse, he left me, asking me to put all my papers together. He was some little time away, and I began to look at some of the books around me. One was an atlas, which I found opened naturally at England, as if that map had been much used. On looking at it I found in certain places little rings marked, and on examining these I noticed that one was near London on the east side, manifestly where his new estate was situated; the other two were Exeter, and Whitby on the Yorkshire coast.\n\nIt was the better part of an hour when the Count returned. “Aha!” he said; “still at your books? Good! But you must not work always. Come; I am informed that your supper is ready.” He took my arm, and we went into the next room, where I found an excellent supper ready on the table. The Count again excused himself, as he had dined out on his being away from home. But he sat as on the previous night, and chatted whilst I ate. After supper I smoked, as on the last evening, and the Count stayed with me, chatting and asking questions on every conceivable subject, hour after hour. I felt that it was getting very late indeed, but I did not say anything, for I felt under obligation to meet my host’s wishes in every way. I was not sleepy, as the long sleep yesterday had fortified me; but I could not help experiencing that chill which comes over one at the coming of the dawn, which is like, in its way, the turn of the tide. They say that people who are near death die generally at the change to the dawn or at the turn of the tide; any one who has when tired, and tied as it were to his post, experienced this change in the atmosphere can well believe it. All at once we heard the crow of a cock coming up with preternatural shrillness through the clear morning air; Count Dracula, jumping to his feet, said:--\n\n“Why, there is the morning again! How remiss I am to let you stay up so long. You must make your conversation regarding my dear new country of England less interesting, so that I may not forget how time flies by us,” and, with a courtly bow, he quickly left me.\n\nI went into my own room and drew the curtains, but there was little to notice; my window opened into the courtyard, all I could see was the warm grey of quickening sky. So I pulled the curtains again, and have written of this day.\n\n8 May.--I began to fear as I wrote in this book that I was getting too diffuse; but now I am glad that I went into detail from the first, for there is something so strange about this place and all in it that I cannot but feel uneasy. I wish I were safe out of it, or that I had never come. It may be that this strange night-existence is telling on me; but would that that were all! If there were any one to talk to I could bear it, but there is no one. I have only the Count to speak with, and he!--I fear I am myself the only living soul within the place. Let me be prosaic so far as facts can be; it will help me to bear up, and imagination must not run riot with me. If it does I am lost. Let me say at once how I stand--or seem to.\n\nI only slept a few hours when I went to bed, and feeling that I could not sleep any more, got up. I had hung my shaving glass by the window, and was just beginning to shave. Suddenly I felt a hand on my shoulder, and heard the Count’s voice saying to me, “Good-morning.” I started, for it amazed me that I had not seen him, since the reflection of the glass covered the whole room behind me. In starting I had cut myself slightly, but did not notice it at the moment. Having answered the Count’s salutation, I turned to the glass again to see how I had been mistaken. This time there could be no error, for the man was close to me, and I could see him over my shoulder. But there was no reflection of him in the mirror! The whole room behind me was displayed; but there was no sign of a man in it, except myself. This was startling, and, coming on the top of so many strange things, was beginning to increase that vague feeling of uneasiness which I always have when the Count is near; but at the instant I saw that the cut had bled a little, and the blood was trickling over my chin. I laid down the razor, turning as I did so half round to look for some sticking plaster. When the Count saw my face, his eyes blazed with a sort of demoniac fury, and he suddenly made a grab at my throat. I drew away, and his hand touched the string of beads which held the crucifix. It made an instant change in him, for the fury passed so quickly that I could hardly believe that it was ever there.\n\n“Take care,” he said, “take care how you cut yourself. It is more dangerous than you think in this country.” Then seizing the shaving glass, he went on: “And this is the wretched thing that has done the mischief. It is a foul bauble of man’s vanity. Away with it!” and opening the heavy window with one wrench of his terrible hand, he flung out the glass, which was shattered into a thousand pieces on the stones of the courtyard far below. Then he withdrew without a word. It is very annoying, for I do not see how I am to shave, unless in my watch-case or the bottom of the shaving-pot, which is fortunately of metal.\n\nWhen I went into the dining-room, breakfast was prepared; but I could not find the Count anywhere. So I breakfasted alone. It is strange that as yet I have not seen the Count eat or drink. He must be a very peculiar man! After breakfast I did a little exploring in the castle. I went out on the stairs, and found a room looking towards the South. The view was magnificent, and from where I stood there was every opportunity of seeing it. The castle is on the very edge of a terrible precipice. A stone falling from the window would fall a thousand feet without touching anything! As far as the eye can reach is a sea of green tree tops, with occasionally a deep rift where there is a chasm. Here and there are silver threads where the rivers wind in deep gorges through the forests.\n\nBut I am not in heart to describe beauty, for when I had seen the view I explored further; doors, doors, doors everywhere, and all locked and bolted. In no place save from the windows in the castle walls is there an available exit.\n\nThe castle is a veritable prison, and I am a prisoner!\n\nWhen I found that I was a prisoner a sort of wild feeling came over me. I rushed up and down the stairs, trying every door and peering out of every window I could find; but after a little the conviction of my helplessness overpowered all other feelings. When I look back after a few hours I think I must have been mad for the time, for I behaved much as a rat does in a trap. When, however, the conviction had come to me that I was helpless I sat down quietly--as quietly as I have ever done anything in my life--and began to think over what was best to be done. I am thinking still, and as yet have come to no definite conclusion. Of one thing only am I certain; that it is no use making my ideas known to the Count. He knows well that I am imprisoned; and as he has done it himself, and has doubtless his own motives for it, he would only deceive me if I trusted him fully with the facts. So far as I can see, my only plan will be to keep my knowledge and my fears to myself, and my eyes open. I am, I know, either being deceived, like a baby, by my own fears, or else I am in desperate straits; and if the latter be so, I need, and shall need, all my brains to get through.\n\nI had hardly come to this conclusion when I heard the great door below shut, and knew that the Count had returned. He did not come at once into the library, so I went cautiously to my own room and found him making the bed. This was odd, but only confirmed what I had all along thought--that there were no servants in the house. When later I saw him through the chink of the hinges of the door laying the table in the dining-room, I was assured of it; for if he does himself all these menial offices, surely it is proof that there is no one else to do them. This gave me a fright, for if there is no one else in the castle, it must have been the Count himself who was the driver of the coach that brought me here. This is a terrible thought; for if so, what does it mean that he could control the wolves, as he did, by only holding up his hand in silence. How was it that all the people at Bistritz and on the coach had some terrible fear for me? What meant the giving of the crucifix, of the garlic, of the wild rose, of the mountain ash? Bless that good, good woman who hung the crucifix round my neck! for it is a comfort and a strength to me whenever I touch it. It is odd that a thing which I have been taught to regard with disfavour and as idolatrous should in a time of loneliness and trouble be of help. Is it that there is something in the essence of the thing itself, or that it is a medium, a tangible help, in conveying memories of sympathy and comfort? Some time, if it may be, I must examine this matter and try to make up my mind about it. In the meantime I must find out all I can about Count Dracula, as it may help me to understand. To-night he may talk of himself, if I turn the conversation that way. I must be very careful, however, not to awake his suspicion.\n\nMidnight.--I have had a long talk with the Count. I asked him a few questions on Transylvania history, and he warmed up to the subject wonderfully. In his speaking of things and people, and especially of battles, he spoke as if he had been present at them all. This he afterwards explained by saying that to a boyar the pride of his house and name is his own pride, that their glory is his glory, that their fate is his fate. Whenever he spoke of his house he always said “we,” and spoke almost in the plural, like a king speaking. I wish I could put down all he said exactly as he said it, for to me it was most fascinating. It seemed to have in it a whole history of the country. He grew excited as he spoke, and walked about the room pulling his great white moustache and grasping anything on which he laid his hands as though he would crush it by main strength. One thing he said which I shall put down as nearly as I can; for it tells in its way the story of his race:--\n\n“We Szekelys have a right to be proud, for in our veins flows the blood of many brave races who fought as the lion fights, for lordship. Here, in the whirlpool of European races, the Ugric tribe bore down from Iceland the fighting spirit which Thor and Wodin gave them, which their Berserkers displayed to such fell intent on the seaboards of Europe, ay, and of Asia and Africa too, till the peoples thought that the were-wolves themselves had come. Here, too, when they came, they found the Huns, whose warlike fury had swept the earth like a living flame, till the dying peoples held that in their veins ran the blood of those old witches, who, expelled from Scythia had mated with the devils in the desert. Fools, fools! What devil or what witch was ever so great as Attila, whose blood is in these veins?” He held up his arms. “Is it a wonder that we were a conquering race; that we were proud; that when the Magyar, the Lombard, the Avar, the Bulgar, or the Turk poured his thousands on our frontiers, we drove them back? Is it strange that when Arpad and his legions swept through the Hungarian fatherland he found us here when he reached the frontier; that the Honfoglalas was completed there? And when the Hungarian flood swept eastward, the Szekelys were claimed as kindred by the victorious Magyars, and to us for centuries was trusted the guarding of the frontier of Turkey-land; ay, and more than that, endless duty of the frontier guard, for, as the Turks say, ‘water sleeps, and enemy is sleepless.’ Who more gladly than we throughout the Four Nations received the ‘bloody sword,’ or at its warlike call flocked quicker to the standard of the King? When was redeemed that great shame of my nation, the shame of Cassova, when the flags of the Wallach and the Magyar went down beneath the Crescent? Who was it but one of my own race who as Voivode crossed the Danube and beat the Turk on his own ground? This was a Dracula indeed! Woe was it that his own unworthy brother, when he had fallen, sold his people to the Turk and brought the shame of slavery on them! Was it not this Dracula, indeed, who inspired that other of his race who in a later age again and again brought his forces over the great river into Turkey-land; who, when he was beaten back, came again, and again, and again, though he had to come alone from the bloody field where his troops were being slaughtered, since he knew that he alone could ultimately triumph! They said that he thought only of himself. Bah! what good are peasants without a leader? Where ends the war without a brain and heart to conduct it? Again, when, after the battle of Mohács, we threw off the Hungarian yoke, we of the Dracula blood were amongst their leaders, for our spirit would not brook that we were not free. Ah, young sir, the Szekelys--and the Dracula as their heart’s blood, their brains, and their swords--can boast a record that mushroom growths like the Hapsburgs and the Romanoffs can never reach. The warlike days are over. Blood is too precious a thing in these days of dishonourable peace; and the glories of the great races are as a tale that is told.”\n\nIt was by this time close on morning, and we went to bed. (Mem., this diary seems horribly like the beginning of the “Arabian Nights,” for everything has to break off at cockcrow--or like the ghost of Hamlet’s father.)\n\n12 May.--Let me begin with facts--bare, meagre facts, verified by books and figures, and of which there can be no doubt. I must not confuse them with experiences which will have to rest on my own observation, or my memory of them. Last evening when the Count came from his room he began by asking me questions on legal matters and on the doing of certain kinds of business. I had spent the day wearily over books, and, simply to keep my mind occupied, went over some of the matters I had been examining at Lincoln’s Inn. There was a certain method in the Count’s inquiries, so I shall try to put them down in sequence; the knowledge may somehow or some time be useful to me.\n\nFirst, he asked if a man in England might have two solicitors or more. I told him he might have a dozen if he wished, but that it would not be wise to have more than one solicitor engaged in one transaction, as only one could act at a time, and that to change would be certain to militate against his interest. He seemed thoroughly to understand, and went on to ask if there would be any practical difficulty in having one man to attend, say, to banking, and another to look after shipping, in case local help were needed in a place far from the home of the banking solicitor. I asked him to explain more fully, so that I might not by any chance mislead him, so he said:--\n\n“I shall illustrate. Your friend and mine, Mr. Peter Hawkins, from under the shadow of your beautiful cathedral at Exeter, which is far from London, buys for me through your good self my place at London. Good! Now here let me say frankly, lest you should think it strange that I have sought the services of one so far off from London instead of some one resident there, that my motive was that no local interest might be served save my wish only; and as one of London residence might, perhaps, have some purpose of himself or friend to serve, I went thus afield to seek my agent, whose labours should be only to my interest. Now, suppose I, who have much of affairs, wish to ship goods, say, to Newcastle, or Durham, or Harwich, or Dover, might it not be that it could with more ease be done by consigning to one in these ports?” I answered that certainly it would be most easy, but that we solicitors had a system of agency one for the other, so that local work could be done locally on instruction from any solicitor, so that the client, simply placing himself in the hands of one man, could have his wishes carried out by him without further trouble.\n\n“But,” said he, “I could be at liberty to direct myself. Is it not so?”\n\n“Of course,” I replied; and “such is often done by men of business, who do not like the whole of their affairs to be known by any one person.”\n\n“Good!” he said, and then went on to ask about the means of making consignments and the forms to be gone through, and of all sorts of difficulties which might arise, but by forethought could be guarded against. I explained all these things to him to the best of my ability, and he certainly left me under the impression that he would have made a wonderful solicitor, for there was nothing that he did not think of or foresee. For a man who was never in the country, and who did not evidently do much in the way of business, his knowledge and acumen were wonderful. When he had satisfied himself on these points of which he had spoken, and I had verified all as well as I could by the books available, he suddenly stood up and said:--\n\n“Have you written since your first letter to our friend Mr. Peter Hawkins, or to any other?” It was with some bitterness in my heart that I answered that I had not, that as yet I had not seen any opportunity of sending letters to anybody.\n\n“Then write now, my young friend,” he said, laying a heavy hand on my shoulder: “write to our friend and to any other; and say, if it will please you, that you shall stay with me until a month from now.”\n\n“Do you wish me to stay so long?” I asked, for my heart grew cold at the thought.\n\n“I desire it much; nay, I will take no refusal. When your master, employer, what you will, engaged that someone should come on his behalf, it was understood that my needs only were to be consulted. I have not stinted. Is it not so?”\n\nWhat could I do but bow acceptance? It was Mr. Hawkins’s interest, not mine, and I had to think of him, not myself; and besides, while Count Dracula was speaking, there was that in his eyes and in his bearing which made me remember that I was a prisoner, and that if I wished it I could have no choice. The Count saw his victory in my bow, and his mastery in the trouble of my face, for he began at once to use them, but in his own smooth, resistless way:--\n\n“I pray you, my good young friend, that you will not discourse of things other than business in your letters. It will doubtless please your friends to know that you are well, and that you look forward to getting home to them. Is it not so?” As he spoke he handed me three sheets of note-paper and three envelopes. They were all of the thinnest foreign post, and looking at them, then at him, and noticing his quiet smile, with the sharp, canine teeth lying over the red underlip, I understood as well as if he had spoken that I should be careful what I wrote, for he would be able to read it. So I determined to write only formal notes now, but to write fully to Mr. Hawkins in secret, and also to Mina, for to her I could write in shorthand, which would puzzle the Count, if he did see it. When I had written my two letters I sat quiet, reading a book whilst the Count wrote several notes, referring as he wrote them to some books on his table. Then he took up my two and placed them with his own, and put by his writing materials, after which, the instant the door had closed behind him, I leaned over and looked at the letters, which were face down on the table. I felt no compunction in doing so, for under the circumstances I felt that I should protect myself in every way I could.\n\nOne of the letters was directed to Samuel F. Billington, No. 7, The Crescent, Whitby, another to Herr Leutner, Varna; the third was to Coutts & Co., London, and the fourth to Herren Klopstock & Billreuth, bankers, Buda-Pesth. The second and fourth were unsealed. I was just about to look at them when I saw the door-handle move. I sank back in my seat, having just had time to replace the letters as they had been and to resume my book before the Count, holding still another letter in his hand, entered the room. He took up the letters on the table and stamped them carefully, and then turning to me, said:--\n\n“I trust you will forgive me, but I have much work to do in private this evening. You will, I hope, find all things as you wish.” At the door he turned, and after a moment’s pause said:--\n\n“Let me advise you, my dear young friend--nay, let me warn you with all seriousness, that should you leave these rooms you will not by any chance go to sleep in any other part of the castle. It is old, and has many memories, and there are bad dreams for those who sleep unwisely. Be warned! Should sleep now or ever overcome you, or be like to do, then haste to your own chamber or to these rooms, for your rest will then be safe. But if you be not careful in this respect, then”--He finished his speech in a gruesome way, for he motioned with his hands as if he were washing them. I quite understood; my only doubt was as to whether any dream could be more terrible than the unnatural, horrible net of gloom and mystery which seemed closing around me.\n\nLater.--I endorse the last words written, but this time there is no doubt in question. I shall not fear to sleep in any place where he is not. I have placed the crucifix over the head of my bed--I imagine that my rest is thus freer from dreams; and there it shall remain.\n\nWhen he left me I went to my room. After a little while, not hearing any sound, I came out and went up the stone stair to where I could look out towards the South. There was some sense of freedom in the vast expanse, inaccessible though it was to me, as compared with the narrow darkness of the courtyard. Looking out on this, I felt that I was indeed in prison, and I seemed to want a breath of fresh air, though it were of the night. I am beginning to feel this nocturnal existence tell on me. It is destroying my nerve. I start at my own shadow, and am full of all sorts of horrible imaginings. God knows that there is ground for my terrible fear in this accursed place! I looked out over the beautiful expanse, bathed in soft yellow moonlight till it was almost as light as day. In the soft light the distant hills became melted, and the shadows in the valleys and gorges of velvety blackness. The mere beauty seemed to cheer me; there was peace and comfort in every breath I drew. As I leaned from the window my eye was caught by something moving a storey below me, and somewhat to my left, where I imagined, from the order of the rooms, that the windows of the Count’s own room would look out. The window at which I stood was tall and deep, stone-mullioned, and though weatherworn, was still complete; but it was evidently many a day since the case had been there. I drew back behind the stonework, and looked carefully out.\n\nWhat I saw was the Count’s head coming out from the window. I did not see the face, but I knew the man by the neck and the movement of his back and arms. In any case I could not mistake the hands which I had had so many opportunities of studying. I was at first interested and somewhat amused, for it is wonderful how small a matter will interest and amuse a man when he is a prisoner. But my very feelings changed to repulsion and terror when I saw the whole man slowly emerge from the window and begin to crawl down the castle wall over that dreadful abyss, face down with his cloak spreading out around him like great wings. At first I could not believe my eyes. I thought it was some trick of the moonlight, some weird effect of shadow; but I kept looking, and it could be no delusion. I saw the fingers and toes grasp the corners of the stones, worn clear of the mortar by the stress of years, and by thus using every projection and inequality move downwards with considerable speed, just as a lizard moves along a wall.\n\nWhat manner of man is this, or what manner of creature is it in the semblance of man? I feel the dread of this horrible place overpowering me; I am in fear--in awful fear--and there is no escape for me; I am encompassed about with terrors that I dare not think of....\n\n15 May.--Once more have I seen the Count go out in his lizard fashion. He moved downwards in a sidelong way, some hundred feet down, and a good deal to the left. He vanished into some hole or window. When his head had disappeared, I leaned out to try and see more, but without avail--the distance was too great to allow a proper angle of sight. I knew he had left the castle now, and thought to use the opportunity to explore more than I had dared to do as yet. I went back to the room, and taking a lamp, tried all the doors. They were all locked, as I had expected, and the locks were comparatively new; but I went down the stone stairs to the hall where I had entered originally. I found I could pull back the bolts easily enough and unhook the great chains; but the door was locked, and the key was gone! That key must be in the Count’s room; I must watch should his door be unlocked, so that I may get it and escape. I went on to make a thorough examination of the various stairs and passages, and to try the doors that opened from them. One or two small rooms near the hall were open, but there was nothing to see in them except old furniture, dusty with age and moth-eaten. At last, however, I found one door at the top of the stairway which, though it seemed to be locked, gave a little under pressure. I tried it harder, and found that it was not really locked, but that the resistance came from the fact that the hinges had fallen somewhat, and the heavy door rested on the floor. Here was an opportunity which I might not have again, so I exerted myself, and with many efforts forced it back so that I could enter. I was now in a wing of the castle further to the right than the rooms I knew and a storey lower down. From the windows I could see that the suite of rooms lay along to the south of the castle, the windows of the end room looking out both west and south. On the latter side, as well as to the former, there was a great precipice. The castle was built on the corner of a great rock, so that on three sides it was quite impregnable, and great windows were placed here where sling, or bow, or culverin could not reach, and consequently light and comfort, impossible to a position which had to be guarded, were secured. To the west was a great valley, and then, rising far away, great jagged mountain fastnesses, rising peak on peak, the sheer rock studded with mountain ash and thorn, whose roots clung in cracks and crevices and crannies of the stone. This was evidently the portion of the castle occupied by the ladies in bygone days, for the furniture had more air of comfort than any I had seen. The windows were curtainless, and the yellow moonlight, flooding in through the diamond panes, enabled one to see even colours, whilst it softened the wealth of dust which lay over all and disguised in some measure the ravages of time and the moth. My lamp seemed to be of little effect in the brilliant moonlight, but I was glad to have it with me, for there was a dread loneliness in the place which chilled my heart and made my nerves tremble. Still, it was better than living alone in the rooms which I had come to hate from the presence of the Count, and after trying a little to school my nerves, I found a soft quietude come over me. Here I am, sitting at a little oak table where in old times possibly some fair lady sat to pen, with much thought and many blushes, her ill-spelt love-letter, and writing in my diary in shorthand all that has happened since I closed it last. It is nineteenth century up-to-date with a vengeance. And yet, unless my senses deceive me, the old centuries had, and have, powers of their own which mere “modernity” cannot kill.\n\nLater: the Morning of 16 May.--God preserve my sanity, for to this I am reduced. Safety and the assurance of safety are things of the past. Whilst I live on here there is but one thing to hope for, that I may not go mad, if, indeed, I be not mad already. If I be sane, then surely it is maddening to think that of all the foul things that lurk in this hateful place the Count is the least dreadful to me; that to him alone I can look for safety, even though this be only whilst I can serve his purpose. Great God! merciful God! Let me be calm, for out of that way lies madness indeed. I begin to get new lights on certain things which have puzzled me. Up to now I never quite knew what Shakespeare meant when he made Hamlet say:--\n\n“My tablets! quick, my tablets! ’Tis meet that I put it down,” etc.,\n\nfor now, feeling as though my own brain were unhinged or as if the shock had come which must end in its undoing, I turn to my diary for repose. The habit of entering accurately must help to soothe me.\n\nThe Count’s mysterious warning frightened me at the time; it frightens me more now when I think of it, for in future he has a fearful hold upon me. I shall fear to doubt what he may say!\n\nWhen I had written in my diary and had fortunately replaced the book and pen in my pocket I felt sleepy. The Count’s warning came into my mind, but I took a pleasure in disobeying it. The sense of sleep was upon me, and with it the obstinacy which sleep brings as outrider. The soft moonlight soothed, and the wide expanse without gave a sense of freedom which refreshed me. I determined not to return to-night to the gloom-haunted rooms, but to sleep here, where, of old, ladies had sat and sung and lived sweet lives whilst their gentle breasts were sad for their menfolk away in the midst of remorseless wars. I drew a great couch out of its place near the corner, so that as I lay, I could look at the lovely view to east and south, and unthinking of and uncaring for the dust, composed myself for sleep. I suppose I must have fallen asleep; I hope so, but I fear, for all that followed was startlingly real--so real that now sitting here in the broad, full sunlight of the morning, I cannot in the least believe that it was all sleep.\n\nI was not alone. The room was the same, unchanged in any way since I came into it; I could see along the floor, in the brilliant moonlight, my own footsteps marked where I had disturbed the long accumulation of dust. In the moonlight opposite me were three young women, ladies by their dress and manner. I thought at the time that I must be dreaming when I saw them, for, though the moonlight was behind them, they threw no shadow on the floor. They came close to me, and looked at me for some time, and then whispered together. Two were dark, and had high aquiline noses, like the Count, and great dark, piercing eyes that seemed to be almost red when contrasted with the pale yellow moon. The other was fair, as fair as can be, with great wavy masses of golden hair and eyes like pale sapphires. I seemed somehow to know her face, and to know it in connection with some dreamy fear, but I could not recollect at the moment how or where. All three had brilliant white teeth that shone like pearls against the ruby of their voluptuous lips. There was something about them that made me uneasy, some longing and at the same time some deadly fear. I felt in my heart a wicked, burning desire that they would kiss me with those red lips. It is not good to note this down, lest some day it should meet Mina’s eyes and cause her pain; but it is the truth. They whispered together, and then they all three laughed--such a silvery, musical laugh, but as hard as though the sound never could have come through the softness of human lips. It was like the intolerable, tingling sweetness of water-glasses when played on by a cunning hand. The fair girl shook her head coquettishly, and the other two urged her on. One said:--\n\n“Go on! You are first, and we shall follow; yours is the right to begin.” The other added:--\n\n“He is young and strong; there are kisses for us all.” I lay quiet, looking out under my eyelashes in an agony of delightful anticipation. The fair girl advanced and bent over me till I could feel the movement of her breath upon me. Sweet it was in one sense, honey-sweet, and sent the same tingling through the nerves as her voice, but with a bitter underlying the sweet, a bitter offensiveness, as one smells in blood.\n\nI was afraid to raise my eyelids, but looked out and saw perfectly under the lashes. The girl went on her knees, and bent over me, simply gloating. There was a deliberate voluptuousness which was both thrilling and repulsive, and as she arched her neck she actually licked her lips like an animal, till I could see in the moonlight the moisture shining on the scarlet lips and on the red tongue as it lapped the white sharp teeth. Lower and lower went her head as the lips went below the range of my mouth and chin and seemed about to fasten on my throat. Then she paused, and I could hear the churning sound of her tongue as it licked her teeth and lips, and could feel the hot breath on my neck. Then the skin of my throat began to tingle as one’s flesh does when the hand that is to tickle it approaches nearer--nearer. I could feel the soft, shivering touch of the lips on the super-sensitive skin of my throat, and the hard dents of two sharp teeth, just touching and pausing there. I closed my eyes in a languorous ecstasy and waited--waited with beating heart.\n\nBut at that instant, another sensation swept through me as quick as lightning. I was conscious of the presence of the Count, and of his being as if lapped in a storm of fury. As my eyes opened involuntarily I saw his strong hand grasp the slender neck of the fair woman and with giant’s power draw it back, the blue eyes transformed with fury, the white teeth champing with rage, and the fair cheeks blazing red with passion. But the Count! Never did I imagine such wrath and fury, even to the demons of the pit. His eyes were positively blazing. The red light in them was lurid, as if the flames of hell-fire blazed behind them. His face was deathly pale, and the lines of it were hard like drawn wires; the thick eyebrows that met over the nose now seemed like a heaving bar of white-hot metal. With a fierce sweep of his arm, he hurled the woman from him, and then motioned to the others, as though he were beating them back; it was the same imperious gesture that I had seen used to the wolves. In a voice which, though low and almost in a whisper seemed to cut through the air and then ring round the room he said:--\n\n“How dare you touch him, any of you? How dare you cast eyes on him when I had forbidden it? Back, I tell you all! This man belongs to me! Beware how you meddle with him, or you’ll have to deal with me.” The fair girl, with a laugh of ribald coquetry, turned to answer him:--\n\n“You yourself never loved; you never love!” On this the other women joined, and such a mirthless, hard, soulless laughter rang through the room that it almost made me faint to hear; it seemed like the pleasure of fiends. Then the Count turned, after looking at my face attentively, and said in a soft whisper:--\n\n“Yes, I too can love; you yourselves can tell it from the past. Is it not so? Well, now I promise you that when I am done with him you shall kiss him at your will. Now go! go! I must awaken him, for there is work to be done.”\n\n“Are we to have nothing to-night?” said one of them, with a low laugh, as she pointed to the bag which he had thrown upon the floor, and which moved as though there were some living thing within it. For answer he nodded his head. One of the women jumped forward and opened it. If my ears did not deceive me there was a gasp and a low wail, as of a half-smothered child. The women closed round, whilst I was aghast with horror; but as I looked they disappeared, and with them the dreadful bag. There was no door near them, and they could not have passed me without my noticing. They simply seemed to fade into the rays of the moonlight and pass out through the window, for I could see outside the dim, shadowy forms for a moment before they entirely faded away.\n\nThen the horror overcame me, and I sank down unconscious.\n\nI awoke in my own bed. If it be that I had not dreamt, the Count must have carried me here. I tried to satisfy myself on the subject, but could not arrive at any unquestionable result. To be sure, there were certain small evidences, such as that my clothes were folded and laid by in a manner which was not my habit. My watch was still unwound, and I am rigorously accustomed to wind it the last thing before going to bed, and many such details. But these things are no proof, for they may have been evidences that my mind was not as usual, and, from some cause or another, I had certainly been much upset. I must watch for proof. Of one thing I am glad: if it was that the Count carried me here and undressed me, he must have been hurried in his task, for my pockets are intact. I am sure this diary would have been a mystery to him which he would not have brooked. He would have taken or destroyed it. As I look round this room, although it has been to me so full of fear, it is now a sort of sanctuary, for nothing can be more dreadful than those awful women, who were--who are--waiting to suck my blood.\n\n18 May.--I have been down to look at that room again in daylight, for I must know the truth. When I got to the doorway at the top of the stairs I found it closed. It had been so forcibly driven against the jamb that part of the woodwork was splintered. I could see that the bolt of the lock had not been shot, but the door is fastened from the inside. I fear it was no dream, and must act on this surmise.\n\n19 May.--I am surely in the toils. Last night the Count asked me in the suavest tones to write three letters, one saying that my work here was nearly done, and that I should start for home within a few days, another that I was starting on the next morning from the time of the letter, and the third that I had left the castle and arrived at Bistritz. I would fain have rebelled, but felt that in the present state of things it would be madness to quarrel openly with the Count whilst I am so absolutely in his power; and to refuse would be to excite his suspicion and to arouse his anger. He knows that I know too much, and that I must not live, lest I be dangerous to him; my only chance is to prolong my opportunities. Something may occur which will give me a chance to escape. I saw in his eyes something of that gathering wrath which was manifest when he hurled that fair woman from him. He explained to me that posts were few and uncertain, and that my writing now would ensure ease of mind to my friends; and he assured me with so much impressiveness that he would countermand the later letters, which would be held over at Bistritz until due time in case chance would admit of my prolonging my stay, that to oppose him would have been to create new suspicion. I therefore pretended to fall in with his views, and asked him what dates I should put on the letters. He calculated a minute, and then said:--\n\n“The first should be June 12, the second June 19, and the third June 29.”\n\nI know now the span of my life. God help me!\n\n28 May.--There is a chance of escape, or at any rate of being able to send word home. A band of Szgany have come to the castle, and are encamped in the courtyard. These Szgany are gipsies; I have notes of them in my book. They are peculiar to this part of the world, though allied to the ordinary gipsies all the world over. There are thousands of them in Hungary and Transylvania, who are almost outside all law. They attach themselves as a rule to some great noble or boyar, and call themselves by his name. They are fearless and without religion, save superstition, and they talk only their own varieties of the Romany tongue.\n\nI shall write some letters home, and shall try to get them to have them posted. I have already spoken them through my window to begin acquaintanceship. They took their hats off and made obeisance and many signs, which, however, I could not understand any more than I could their spoken language....\n\nI have written the letters. Mina’s is in shorthand, and I simply ask Mr. Hawkins to communicate with her. To her I have explained my situation, but without the horrors which I may only surmise. It would shock and frighten her to death were I to expose my heart to her. Should the letters not carry, then the Count shall not yet know my secret or the extent of my knowledge....\n\nI have given the letters; I threw them through the bars of my window with a gold piece, and made what signs I could to have them posted. The man who took them pressed them to his heart and bowed, and then put them in his cap. I could do no more. I stole back to the study, and began to read. As the Count did not come in, I have written here....\n\nThe Count has come. He sat down beside me, and said in his smoothest voice as he opened two letters:--\n\n“The Szgany has given me these, of which, though I know not whence they come, I shall, of course, take care. See!”--he must have looked at it--“one is from you, and to my friend Peter Hawkins; the other”--here he caught sight of the strange symbols as he opened the envelope, and the dark look came into his face, and his eyes blazed wickedly--“the other is a vile thing, an outrage upon friendship and hospitality! It is not signed. Well! so it cannot matter to us.” And he calmly held letter and envelope in the flame of the lamp till they were consumed. Then he went on:--\n\n“The letter to Hawkins--that I shall, of course, send on, since it is yours. Your letters are sacred to me. Your pardon, my friend, that unknowingly I did break the seal. Will you not cover it again?” He held out the letter to me, and with a courteous bow handed me a clean envelope. I could only redirect it and hand it to him in silence. When he went out of the room I could hear the key turn softly. A minute later I went over and tried it, and the door was locked.\n\nWhen, an hour or two after, the Count came quietly into the room, his coming awakened me, for I had gone to sleep on the sofa. He was very courteous and very cheery in his manner, and seeing that I had been sleeping, he said:--\n\n“So, my friend, you are tired? Get to bed. There is the surest rest. I may not have the pleasure to talk to-night, since there are many labours to me; but you will sleep, I pray.” I passed to my room and went to bed, and, strange to say, slept without dreaming. Despair has its own calms.\n\n31 May.--This morning when I woke I thought I would provide myself with some paper and envelopes from my bag and keep them in my pocket, so that I might write in case I should get an opportunity, but again a surprise, again a shock!\n\nEvery scrap of paper was gone, and with it all my notes, my memoranda, relating to railways and travel, my letter of credit, in fact all that might be useful to me were I once outside the castle. I sat and pondered awhile, and then some thought occurred to me, and I made search of my portmanteau and in the wardrobe where I had placed my clothes.\n\nThe suit in which I had travelled was gone, and also my overcoat and rug; I could find no trace of them anywhere. This looked like some new scheme of villainy....\n\n17 June.--This morning, as I was sitting on the edge of my bed cudgelling my brains, I heard without a cracking of whips and pounding and scraping of horses’ feet up the rocky path beyond the courtyard. With joy I hurried to the window, and saw drive into the yard two great leiter-wagons, each drawn by eight sturdy horses, and at the head of each pair a Slovak, with his wide hat, great nail-studded belt, dirty sheepskin, and high boots. They had also their long staves in hand. I ran to the door, intending to descend and try and join them through the main hall, as I thought that way might be opened for them. Again a shock: my door was fastened on the outside.\n\nThen I ran to the window and cried to them. They looked up at me stupidly and pointed, but just then the “hetman” of the Szgany came out, and seeing them pointing to my window, said something, at which they laughed. Henceforth no effort of mine, no piteous cry or agonised entreaty, would make them even look at me. They resolutely turned away. The leiter-wagons contained great, square boxes, with handles of thick rope; these were evidently empty by the ease with which the Slovaks handled them, and by their resonance as they were roughly moved. When they were all unloaded and packed in a great heap in one corner of the yard, the Slovaks were given some money by the Szgany, and spitting on it for luck, lazily went each to his horse’s head. Shortly afterwards, I heard the cracking of their whips die away in the distance.\n\n24 June, before morning.--Last night the Count left me early, and locked himself into his own room. As soon as I dared I ran up the winding stair, and looked out of the window, which opened south. I thought I would watch for the Count, for there is something going on. The Szgany are quartered somewhere in the castle and are doing work of some kind. I know it, for now and then I hear a far-away muffled sound as of mattock and spade, and, whatever it is, it must be the end of some ruthless villainy.\n\nI had been at the window somewhat less than half an hour, when I saw something coming out of the Count’s window. I drew back and watched carefully, and saw the whole man emerge. It was a new shock to me to find that he had on the suit of clothes which I had worn whilst travelling here, and slung over his shoulder the terrible bag which I had seen the women take away. There could be no doubt as to his quest, and in my garb, too! This, then, is his new scheme of evil: that he will allow others to see me, as they think, so that he may both leave evidence that I have been seen in the towns or villages posting my own letters, and that any wickedness which he may do shall by the local people be attributed to me.\n\nIt makes me rage to think that this can go on, and whilst I am shut up here, a veritable prisoner, but without that protection of the law which is even a criminal’s right and consolation.\n\nI thought I would watch for the Count’s return, and for a long time sat doggedly at the window. Then I began to notice that there were some quaint little specks floating in the rays of the moonlight. They were like the tiniest grains of dust, and they whirled round and gathered in clusters in a nebulous sort of way. I watched them with a sense of soothing, and a sort of calm stole over me. I leaned back in the embrasure in a more comfortable position, so that I could enjoy more fully the aërial gambolling.\n\nSomething made me start up, a low, piteous howling of dogs somewhere far below in the valley, which was hidden from my sight. Louder it seemed to ring in my ears, and the floating motes of dust to take new shapes to the sound as they danced in the moonlight. I felt myself struggling to awake to some call of my instincts; nay, my very soul was struggling, and my half-remembered sensibilities were striving to answer the call. I was becoming hypnotised! Quicker and quicker danced the dust; the moonbeams seemed to quiver as they went by me into the mass of gloom beyond. More and more they gathered till they seemed to take dim phantom shapes. And then I started, broad awake and in full possession of my senses, and ran screaming from the place. The phantom shapes, which were becoming gradually materialised from the moonbeams, were those of the three ghostly women to whom I was doomed. I fled, and felt somewhat safer in my own room, where there was no moonlight and where the lamp was burning brightly.\n\nWhen a couple of hours had passed I heard something stirring in the Count’s room, something like a sharp wail quickly suppressed; and then there was silence, deep, awful silence, which chilled me. With a beating heart, I tried the door; but I was locked in my prison, and could do nothing. I sat down and simply cried.\n\nAs I sat I heard a sound in the courtyard without--the agonised cry of a woman. I rushed to the window, and throwing it up, peered out between the bars. There, indeed, was a woman with dishevelled hair, holding her hands over her heart as one distressed with running. She was leaning against a corner of the gateway. When she saw my face at the window she threw herself forward, and shouted in a voice laden with menace:--\n\n“Monster, give me my child!”\n\nShe threw herself on her knees, and raising up her hands, cried the same words in tones which wrung my heart. Then she tore her hair and beat her breast, and abandoned herself to all the violences of extravagant emotion. Finally, she threw herself forward, and, though I could not see her, I could hear the beating of her naked hands against the door.\n\nSomewhere high overhead, probably on the tower, I heard the voice of the Count calling in his harsh, metallic whisper. His call seemed to be answered from far and wide by the howling of wolves. Before many minutes had passed a pack of them poured, like a pent-up dam when liberated, through the wide entrance into the courtyard.\n\nThere was no cry from the woman, and the howling of the wolves was but short. Before long they streamed away singly, licking their lips.\n\nI could not pity her, for I knew now what had become of her child, and she was better dead.\n\nWhat shall I do? what can I do? How can I escape from this dreadful thing of night and gloom and fear?\n\n25 June, morning.--No man knows till he has suffered from the night how sweet and how dear to his heart and eye the morning can be. When the sun grew so high this morning that it struck the top of the great gateway opposite my window, the high spot which it touched seemed to me as if the dove from the ark had lighted there. My fear fell from me as if it had been a vaporous garment which dissolved in the warmth. I must take action of some sort whilst the courage of the day is upon me. Last night one of my post-dated letters went to post, the first of that fatal series which is to blot out the very traces of my existence from the earth.\n\nLet me not think of it. Action!\n\nIt has always been at night-time that I have been molested or threatened, or in some way in danger or in fear. I have not yet seen the Count in the daylight. Can it be that he sleeps when others wake, that he may be awake whilst they sleep? If I could only get into his room! But there is no possible way. The door is always locked, no way for me.\n\nYes, there is a way, if one dares to take it. Where his body has gone why may not another body go? I have seen him myself crawl from his window. Why should not I imitate him, and go in by his window? The chances are desperate, but my need is more desperate still. I shall risk it. At the worst it can only be death; and a man’s death is not a calf’s, and the dreaded Hereafter may still be open to me. God help me in my task! Good-bye, Mina, if I fail; good-bye, my faithful friend and second father; good-bye, all, and last of all Mina!\n\nSame day, later.--I have made the effort, and God, helping me, have come safely back to this room. I must put down every detail in order. I went whilst my courage was fresh straight to the window on the south side, and at once got outside on the narrow ledge of stone which runs around the building on this side. The stones are big and roughly cut, and the mortar has by process of time been washed away between them. I took off my boots, and ventured out on the desperate way. I looked down once, so as to make sure that a sudden glimpse of the awful depth would not overcome me, but after that kept my eyes away from it. I knew pretty well the direction and distance of the Count’s window, and made for it as well as I could, having regard to the opportunities available. I did not feel dizzy--I suppose I was too excited--and the time seemed ridiculously short till I found myself standing on the window-sill and trying to raise up the sash. I was filled with agitation, however, when I bent down and slid feet foremost in through the window. Then I looked around for the Count, but, with surprise and gladness, made a discovery. The room was empty! It was barely furnished with odd things, which seemed to have never been used; the furniture was something the same style as that in the south rooms, and was covered with dust. I looked for the key, but it was not in the lock, and I could not find it anywhere. The only thing I found was a great heap of gold in one corner--gold of all kinds, Roman, and British, and Austrian, and Hungarian, and Greek and Turkish money, covered with a film of dust, as though it had lain long in the ground. None of it that I noticed was less than three hundred years old. There were also chains and ornaments, some jewelled, but all of them old and stained.\n\nAt one corner of the room was a heavy door. I tried it, for, since I could not find the key of the room or the key of the outer door, which was the main object of my search, I must make further examination, or all my efforts would be in vain. It was open, and led through a stone passage to a circular stairway, which went steeply down. I descended, minding carefully where I went, for the stairs were dark, being only lit by loopholes in the heavy masonry. At the bottom there was a dark, tunnel-like passage, through which came a deathly, sickly odour, the odour of old earth newly turned. As I went through the passage the smell grew closer and heavier. At last I pulled open a heavy door which stood ajar, and found myself in an old, ruined chapel, which had evidently been used as a graveyard. The roof was broken, and in two places were steps leading to vaults, but the ground had recently been dug over, and the earth placed in great wooden boxes, manifestly those which had been brought by the Slovaks. There was nobody about, and I made search for any further outlet, but there was none. Then I went over every inch of the ground, so as not to lose a chance. I went down even into the vaults, where the dim light struggled, although to do so was a dread to my very soul. Into two of these I went, but saw nothing except fragments of old coffins and piles of dust; in the third, however, I made a discovery.\n\nThere, in one of the great boxes, of which there were fifty in all, on a pile of newly dug earth, lay the Count! He was either dead or asleep, I could not say which--for the eyes were open and stony, but without the glassiness of death--and the cheeks had the warmth of life through all their pallor; the lips were as red as ever. But there was no sign of movement, no pulse, no breath, no beating of the heart. I bent over him, and tried to find any sign of life, but in vain. He could not have lain there long, for the earthy smell would have passed away in a few hours. By the side of the box was its cover, pierced with holes here and there. I thought he might have the keys on him, but when I went to search I saw the dead eyes, and in them, dead though they were, such a look of hate, though unconscious of me or my presence, that I fled from the place, and leaving the Count’s room by the window, crawled again up the castle wall. Regaining my room, I threw myself panting upon the bed and tried to think....\n\n29 June.--To-day is the date of my last letter, and the Count has taken steps to prove that it was genuine, for again I saw him leave the castle by the same window, and in my clothes. As he went down the wall, lizard fashion, I wished I had a gun or some lethal weapon, that I might destroy him; but I fear that no weapon wrought alone by man’s hand would have any effect on him. I dared not wait to see him return, for I feared to see those weird sisters. I came back to the library, and read there till I fell asleep.\n\nI was awakened by the Count, who looked at me as grimly as a man can look as he said:--\n\n“To-morrow, my friend, we must part. You return to your beautiful England, I to some work which may have such an end that we may never meet. Your letter home has been despatched; to-morrow I shall not be here, but all shall be ready for your journey. In the morning come the Szgany, who have some labours of their own here, and also come some Slovaks. When they have gone, my carriage shall come for you, and shall bear you to the Borgo Pass to meet the diligence from Bukovina to Bistritz. But I am in hopes that I shall see more of you at Castle Dracula.” I suspected him, and determined to test his sincerity. Sincerity! It seems like a profanation of the word to write it in connection with such a monster, so asked him point-blank:--\n\n“Why may I not go to-night?”\n\n“Because, dear sir, my coachman and horses are away on a mission.”\n\n“But I would walk with pleasure. I want to get away at once.” He smiled, such a soft, smooth, diabolical smile that I knew there was some trick behind his smoothness. He said:--\n\n“And your baggage?”\n\n“I do not care about it. I can send for it some other time.”\n\nThe Count stood up, and said, with a sweet courtesy which made me rub my eyes, it seemed so real:--\n\n“You English have a saying which is close to my heart, for its spirit is that which rules our boyars: ‘Welcome the coming; speed the parting guest.’ Come with me, my dear young friend. Not an hour shall you wait in my house against your will, though sad am I at your going, and that you so suddenly desire it. Come!” With a stately gravity, he, with the lamp, preceded me down the stairs and along the hall. Suddenly he stopped.\n\n“Hark!”\n\nClose at hand came the howling of many wolves. It was almost as if the sound sprang up at the rising of his hand, just as the music of a great orchestra seems to leap under the bâton of the conductor. After a pause of a moment, he proceeded, in his stately way, to the door, drew back the ponderous bolts, unhooked the heavy chains, and began to draw it open.\n\nTo my intense astonishment I saw that it was unlocked. Suspiciously, I looked all round, but could see no key of any kind.\n\nAs the door began to open, the howling of the wolves without grew louder and angrier; their red jaws, with champing teeth, and their blunt-clawed feet as they leaped, came in through the opening door. I knew then that to struggle at the moment against the Count was useless. With such allies as these at his command, I could do nothing. But still the door continued slowly to open, and only the Count’s body stood in the gap. Suddenly it struck me that this might be the moment and means of my doom; I was to be given to the wolves, and at my own instigation. There was a diabolical wickedness in the idea great enough for the Count, and as a last chance I cried out:--\n\n“Shut the door; I shall wait till morning!” and covered my face with my hands to hide my tears of bitter disappointment. With one sweep of his powerful arm, the Count threw the door shut, and the great bolts clanged and echoed through the hall as they shot back into their places.\n\nIn silence we returned to the library, and after a minute or two I went to my own room. The last I saw of Count Dracula was his kissing his hand to me; with a red light of triumph in his eyes, and with a smile that Judas in hell might be proud of.\n\nWhen I was in my room and about to lie down, I thought I heard a whispering at my door. I went to it softly and listened. Unless my ears deceived me, I heard the voice of the Count:--\n\n“Back, back, to your own place! Your time is not yet come. Wait! Have patience! To-night is mine. To-morrow night is yours!” There was a low, sweet ripple of laughter, and in a rage I threw open the door, and saw without the three terrible women licking their lips. As I appeared they all joined in a horrible laugh, and ran away.\n\nI came back to my room and threw myself on my knees. It is then so near the end? To-morrow! to-morrow! Lord, help me, and those to whom I am dear!\n\n30 June, morning.--These may be the last words I ever write in this diary. I slept till just before the dawn, and when I woke threw myself on my knees, for I determined that if Death came he should find me ready.\n\nAt last I felt that subtle change in the air, and knew that the morning had come. Then came the welcome cock-crow, and I felt that I was safe. With a glad heart, I opened my door and ran down to the hall. I had seen that the door was unlocked, and now escape was before me. With hands that trembled with eagerness, I unhooked the chains and drew back the massive bolts.\n\nBut the door would not move. Despair seized me. I pulled, and pulled, at the door, and shook it till, massive as it was, it rattled in its casement. I could see the bolt shot. It had been locked after I left the Count.\n\nThen a wild desire took me to obtain that key at any risk, and I determined then and there to scale the wall again and gain the Count’s room. He might kill me, but death now seemed the happier choice of evils. Without a pause I rushed up to the east window, and scrambled down the wall, as before, into the Count’s room. It was empty, but that was as I expected. I could not see a key anywhere, but the heap of gold remained. I went through the door in the corner and down the winding stair and along the dark passage to the old chapel. I knew now well enough where to find the monster I sought.\n\nThe great box was in the same place, close against the wall, but the lid was laid on it, not fastened down, but with the nails ready in their places to be hammered home. I knew I must reach the body for the key, so I raised the lid, and laid it back against the wall; and then I saw something which filled my very soul with horror. There lay the Count, but looking as if his youth had been half renewed, for the white hair and moustache were changed to dark iron-grey; the cheeks were fuller, and the white skin seemed ruby-red underneath; the mouth was redder than ever, for on the lips were gouts of fresh blood, which trickled from the corners of the mouth and ran over the chin and neck. Even the deep, burning eyes seemed set amongst swollen flesh, for the lids and pouches underneath were bloated. It seemed as if the whole awful creature were simply gorged with blood. He lay like a filthy leech, exhausted with his repletion. I shuddered as I bent over to touch him, and every sense in me revolted at the contact; but I had to search, or I was lost. The coming night might see my own body a banquet in a similar way to those horrid three. I felt all over the body, but no sign could I find of the key. Then I stopped and looked at the Count. There was a mocking smile on the bloated face which seemed to drive me mad. This was the being I was helping to transfer to London, where, perhaps, for centuries to come he might, amongst its teeming millions, satiate his lust for blood, and create a new and ever-widening circle of semi-demons to batten on the helpless. The very thought drove me mad. A terrible desire came upon me to rid the world of such a monster. There was no lethal weapon at hand, but I seized a shovel which the workmen had been using to fill the cases, and lifting it high, struck, with the edge downward, at the hateful face. But as I did so the head turned, and the eyes fell full upon me, with all their blaze of basilisk horror. The sight seemed to paralyse me, and the shovel turned in my hand and glanced from the face, merely making a deep gash above the forehead. The shovel fell from my hand across the box, and as I pulled it away the flange of the blade caught the edge of the lid which fell over again, and hid the horrid thing from my sight. The last glimpse I had was of the bloated face, blood-stained and fixed with a grin of malice which would have held its own in the nethermost hell.\n\nI thought and thought what should be my next move, but my brain seemed on fire, and I waited with a despairing feeling growing over me. As I waited I heard in the distance a gipsy song sung by merry voices coming closer, and through their song the rolling of heavy wheels and the cracking of whips; the Szgany and the Slovaks of whom the Count had spoken were coming. With a last look around and at the box which contained the vile body, I ran from the place and gained the Count’s room, determined to rush out at the moment the door should be opened. With strained ears, I listened, and heard downstairs the grinding of the key in the great lock and the falling back of the heavy door. There must have been some other means of entry, or some one had a key for one of the locked doors. Then there came the sound of many feet tramping and dying away in some passage which sent up a clanging echo. I turned to run down again towards the vault, where I might find the new entrance; but at the moment there seemed to come a violent puff of wind, and the door to the winding stair blew to with a shock that set the dust from the lintels flying. When I ran to push it open, I found that it was hopelessly fast. I was again a prisoner, and the net of doom was closing round me more closely.\n\nAs I write there is in the passage below a sound of many tramping feet and the crash of weights being set down heavily, doubtless the boxes, with their freight of earth. There is a sound of hammering; it is the box being nailed down. Now I can hear the heavy feet tramping again along the hall, with many other idle feet coming behind them.\n\nThe door is shut, and the chains rattle; there is a grinding of the key in the lock; I can hear the key withdraw: then another door opens and shuts; I hear the creaking of lock and bolt.\n\nHark! in the courtyard and down the rocky way the roll of heavy wheels, the crack of whips, and the chorus of the Szgany as they pass into the distance.\n\nI am alone in the castle with those awful women. Faugh! Mina is a woman, and there is nought in common. They are devils of the Pit!\n\nI shall not remain alone with them; I shall try to scale the castle wall farther than I have yet attempted. I shall take some of the gold with me, lest I want it later. I may find a way from this dreadful place.\n\nAnd then away for home! away to the quickest and nearest train! away from this cursed spot, from this cursed land, where the devil and his children still walk with earthly feet!\n\nAt least God’s mercy is better than that of these monsters, and the precipice is steep and high. At its foot a man may sleep--as a man. Good-bye, all! Mina!\n\n“My dearest Lucy,--\n\n“Forgive my long delay in writing, but I have been simply overwhelmed with work. The life of an assistant schoolmistress is sometimes trying. I am longing to be with you, and by the sea, where we can talk together freely and build our castles in the air. I have been working very hard lately, because I want to keep up with Jonathan’s studies, and I have been practising shorthand very assiduously. When we are married I shall be able to be useful to Jonathan, and if I can stenograph well enough I can take down what he wants to say in this way and write it out for him on the typewriter, at which also I am practising very hard. He and I sometimes write letters in shorthand, and he is keeping a stenographic journal of his travels abroad. When I am with you I shall keep a diary in the same way. I don’t mean one of those two-pages-to-the-week-with-Sunday-squeezed-in-a-corner diaries, but a sort of journal which I can write in whenever I feel inclined. I do not suppose there will be much of interest to other people; but it is not intended for them. I may show it to Jonathan some day if there is in it anything worth sharing, but it is really an exercise book. I shall try to do what I see lady journalists do: interviewing and writing descriptions and trying to remember conversations. I am told that, with a little practice, one can remember all that goes on or that one hears said during a day. However, we shall see. I will tell you of my little plans when we meet. I have just had a few hurried lines from Jonathan from Transylvania. He is well, and will be returning in about a week. I am longing to hear all his news. It must be so nice to see strange countries. I wonder if we--I mean Jonathan and I--shall ever see them together. There is the ten o’clock bell ringing. Good-bye.\n\n“Your loving\n\n“Tell me all the news when you write. You have not told me anything for a long time. I hear rumours, and especially of a tall, handsome, curly-haired man???”\n\n“17, Chatham Street,\n\n“Wednesday.\n\n“My dearest Mina,--\n\n“I must say you tax me very unfairly with being a bad correspondent. I wrote to you twice since we parted, and your last letter was only your second. Besides, I have nothing to tell you. There is really nothing to interest you. Town is very pleasant just now, and we go a good deal to picture-galleries and for walks and rides in the park. As to the tall, curly-haired man, I suppose it was the one who was with me at the last Pop. Some one has evidently been telling tales. That was Mr. Holmwood. He often comes to see us, and he and mamma get on very well together; they have so many things to talk about in common. We met some time ago a man that would just do for you, if you were not already engaged to Jonathan. He is an excellent parti, being handsome, well off, and of good birth. He is a doctor and really clever. Just fancy! He is only nine-and-twenty, and he has an immense lunatic asylum all under his own care. Mr. Holmwood introduced him to me, and he called here to see us, and often comes now. I think he is one of the most resolute men I ever saw, and yet the most calm. He seems absolutely imperturbable. I can fancy what a wonderful power he must have over his patients. He has a curious habit of looking one straight in the face, as if trying to read one’s thoughts. He tries this on very much with me, but I flatter myself he has got a tough nut to crack. I know that from my glass. Do you ever try to read your own face? I do, and I can tell you it is not a bad study, and gives you more trouble than you can well fancy if you have never tried it. He says that I afford him a curious psychological study, and I humbly think I do. I do not, as you know, take sufficient interest in dress to be able to describe the new fashions. Dress is a bore. That is slang again, but never mind; Arthur says that every day. There, it is all out. Mina, we have told all our secrets to each other since we were children; we have slept together and eaten together, and laughed and cried together; and now, though I have spoken, I would like to speak more. Oh, Mina, couldn’t you guess? I love him. I am blushing as I write, for although I think he loves me, he has not told me so in words. But oh, Mina, I love him; I love him; I love him! There, that does me good. I wish I were with you, dear, sitting by the fire undressing, as we used to sit; and I would try to tell you what I feel. I do not know how I am writing this even to you. I am afraid to stop, or I should tear up the letter, and I don’t want to stop, for I do so want to tell you all. Let me hear from you at once, and tell me all that you think about it. Mina, I must stop. Good-night. Bless me in your prayers; and, Mina, pray for my happiness.\n\n“P.S.--I need not tell you this is a secret. Good-night again.\n\n“L.”\n\n“24 May.\n“My dearest Mina,--\n\n“Thanks, and thanks, and thanks again for your sweet letter. It was so nice to be able to tell you and to have your sympathy.\n\n“My dear, it never rains but it pours. How true the old proverbs are. Here am I, who shall be twenty in September, and yet I never had a proposal till to-day, not a real proposal, and to-day I have had three. Just fancy! THREE proposals in one day! Isn’t it awful! I feel sorry, really and truly sorry, for two of the poor fellows. Oh, Mina, I am so happy that I don’t know what to do with myself. And three proposals! But, for goodness’ sake, don’t tell any of the girls, or they would be getting all sorts of extravagant ideas and imagining themselves injured and slighted if in their very first day at home they did not get six at least. Some girls are so vain! You and I, Mina dear, who are engaged and are going to settle down soon soberly into old married women, can despise vanity. Well, I must tell you about the three, but you must keep it a secret, dear, from every one, except, of course, Jonathan. You will tell him, because I would, if I were in your place, certainly tell Arthur. A woman ought to tell her husband everything--don’t you think so, dear?--and I must be fair. Men like women, certainly their wives, to be quite as fair as they are; and women, I am afraid, are not always quite as fair as they should be. Well, my dear, number One came just before lunch. I told you of him, Dr. John Seward, the lunatic-asylum man, with the strong jaw and the good forehead. He was very cool outwardly, but was nervous all the same. He had evidently been schooling himself as to all sorts of little things, and remembered them; but he almost managed to sit down on his silk hat, which men don’t generally do when they are cool, and then when he wanted to appear at ease he kept playing with a lancet in a way that made me nearly scream. He spoke to me, Mina, very straightforwardly. He told me how dear I was to him, though he had known me so little, and what his life would be with me to help and cheer him. He was going to tell me how unhappy he would be if I did not care for him, but when he saw me cry he said that he was a brute and would not add to my present trouble. Then he broke off and asked if I could love him in time; and when I shook my head his hands trembled, and then with some hesitation he asked me if I cared already for any one else. He put it very nicely, saying that he did not want to wring my confidence from me, but only to know, because if a woman’s heart was free a man might have hope. And then, Mina, I felt a sort of duty to tell him that there was some one. I only told him that much, and then he stood up, and he looked very strong and very grave as he took both my hands in his and said he hoped I would be happy, and that if I ever wanted a friend I must count him one of my best. Oh, Mina dear, I can’t help crying: and you must excuse this letter being all blotted. Being proposed to is all very nice and all that sort of thing, but it isn’t at all a happy thing when you have to see a poor fellow, whom you know loves you honestly, going away and looking all broken-hearted, and to know that, no matter what he may say at the moment, you are passing quite out of his life. My dear, I must stop here at present, I feel so miserable, though I am so happy.\n\n“Evening.\n\n“Arthur has just gone, and I feel in better spirits than when I left off, so I can go on telling you about the day. Well, my dear, number Two came after lunch. He is such a nice fellow, an American from Texas, and he looks so young and so fresh that it seems almost impossible that he has been to so many places and has had such adventures. I sympathise with poor Desdemona when she had such a dangerous stream poured in her ear, even by a black man. I suppose that we women are such cowards that we think a man will save us from fears, and we marry him. I know now what I would do if I were a man and wanted to make a girl love me. No, I don’t, for there was Mr. Morris telling us his stories, and Arthur never told any, and yet---- My dear, I am somewhat previous. Mr. Quincey P. Morris found me alone. It seems that a man always does find a girl alone. No, he doesn’t, for Arthur tried twice to make a chance, and I helping him all I could; I am not ashamed to say it now. I must tell you beforehand that Mr. Morris doesn’t always speak slang--that is to say, he never does so to strangers or before them, for he is really well educated and has exquisite manners--but he found out that it amused me to hear him talk American slang, and whenever I was present, and there was no one to be shocked, he said such funny things. I am afraid, my dear, he has to invent it all, for it fits exactly into whatever else he has to say. But this is a way slang has. I do not know myself if I shall ever speak slang; I do not know if Arthur likes it, as I have never heard him use any as yet. Well, Mr. Morris sat down beside me and looked as happy and jolly as he could, but I could see all the same that he was very nervous. He took my hand in his, and said ever so sweetly:--\n\n“‘Miss Lucy, I know I ain’t good enough to regulate the fixin’s of your little shoes, but I guess if you wait till you find a man that is you will go join them seven young women with the lamps when you quit. Won’t you just hitch up alongside of me and let us go down the long road together, driving in double harness?’\n\n“Well, he did look so good-humoured and so jolly that it didn’t seem half so hard to refuse him as it did poor Dr. Seward; so I said, as lightly as I could, that I did not know anything of hitching, and that I wasn’t broken to harness at all yet. Then he said that he had spoken in a light manner, and he hoped that if he had made a mistake in doing so on so grave, so momentous, an occasion for him, I would forgive him. He really did look serious when he was saying it, and I couldn’t help feeling a bit serious too--I know, Mina, you will think me a horrid flirt--though I couldn’t help feeling a sort of exultation that he was number two in one day. And then, my dear, before I could say a word he began pouring out a perfect torrent of love-making, laying his very heart and soul at my feet. He looked so earnest over it that I shall never again think that a man must be playful always, and never earnest, because he is merry at times. I suppose he saw something in my face which checked him, for he suddenly stopped, and said with a sort of manly fervour that I could have loved him for if I had been free:--\n\n“‘Lucy, you are an honest-hearted girl, I know. I should not be here speaking to you as I am now if I did not believe you clean grit, right through to the very depths of your soul. Tell me, like one good fellow to another, is there any one else that you care for? And if there is I’ll never trouble you a hair’s breadth again, but will be, if you will let me, a very faithful friend.’\n\n“My dear Mina, why are men so noble when we women are so little worthy of them? Here was I almost making fun of this great-hearted, true gentleman. I burst into tears--I am afraid, my dear, you will think this a very sloppy letter in more ways than one--and I really felt very badly. Why can’t they let a girl marry three men, or as many as want her, and save all this trouble? But this is heresy, and I must not say it. I am glad to say that, though I was crying, I was able to look into Mr. Morris’s brave eyes, and I told him out straight:--\n\n“‘Yes, there is some one I love, though he has not told me yet that he even loves me.’ I was right to speak to him so frankly, for quite a light came into his face, and he put out both his hands and took mine--I think I put them into his--and said in a hearty way:--\n\n“‘That’s my brave girl. It’s better worth being late for a chance of winning you than being in time for any other girl in the world. Don’t cry, my dear. If it’s for me, I’m a hard nut to crack; and I take it standing up. If that other fellow doesn’t know his happiness, well, he’d better look for it soon, or he’ll have to deal with me. Little girl, your honesty and pluck have made me a friend, and that’s rarer than a lover; it’s more unselfish anyhow. My dear, I’m going to have a pretty lonely walk between this and Kingdom Come. Won’t you give me one kiss? It’ll be something to keep off the darkness now and then. You can, you know, if you like, for that other good fellow--he must be a good fellow, my dear, and a fine fellow, or you could not love him--hasn’t spoken yet.’ That quite won me, Mina, for it was brave and sweet of him, and noble, too, to a rival--wasn’t it?--and he so sad; so I leant over and kissed him. He stood up with my two hands in his, and as he looked down into my face--I am afraid I was blushing very much--he said:--\n\n“‘Little girl, I hold your hand, and you’ve kissed me, and if these things don’t make us friends nothing ever will. Thank you for your sweet honesty to me, and good-bye.’ He wrung my hand, and taking up his hat, went straight out of the room without looking back, without a tear or a quiver or a pause; and I am crying like a baby. Oh, why must a man like that be made unhappy when there are lots of girls about who would worship the very ground he trod on? I know I would if I were free--only I don’t want to be free. My dear, this quite upset me, and I feel I cannot write of happiness just at once, after telling you of it; and I don’t wish to tell of the number three until it can be all happy.\n\n“Ever your loving\n\n“P.S.--Oh, about number Three--I needn’t tell you of number Three, need I? Besides, it was all so confused; it seemed only a moment from his coming into the room till both his arms were round me, and he was kissing me. I am very, very happy, and I don’t know what I have done to deserve it. I must only try in the future to show that I am not ungrateful to God for all His goodness to me in sending to me such a lover, such a husband, and such a friend.\n\n“Good-bye.”\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n(Kept in phonograph)\n\n25 May.--Ebb tide in appetite to-day. Cannot eat, cannot rest, so diary instead. Since my rebuff of yesterday I have a sort of empty feeling; nothing in the world seems of sufficient importance to be worth the doing.... As I knew that the only cure for this sort of thing was work, I went down amongst the patients. I picked out one who has afforded me a study of much interest. He is so quaint that I am determined to understand him as well as I can. To-day I seemed to get nearer than ever before to the heart of his mystery.\n\nI questioned him more fully than I had ever done, with a view to making myself master of the facts of his hallucination. In my manner of doing it there was, I now see, something of cruelty. I seemed to wish to keep him to the point of his madness--a thing which I avoid with the patients as I would the mouth of hell.\n\n(Mem., under what circumstances would I not avoid the pit of hell?) Omnia Romæ venalia sunt. Hell has its price! verb. sap. If there be anything behind this instinct it will be valuable to trace it afterwards accurately, so I had better commence to do so, therefore--\n\nR. M. Renfield, ætat 59.--Sanguine temperament; great physical strength; morbidly excitable; periods of gloom, ending in some fixed idea which I cannot make out. I presume that the sanguine temperament itself and the disturbing influence end in a mentally-accomplished finish; a possibly dangerous man, probably dangerous if unselfish. In selfish men caution is as secure an armour for their foes as for themselves. What I think of on this point is, when self is the fixed point the centripetal force is balanced with the centrifugal; when duty, a cause, etc., is the fixed point, the latter force is paramount, and only accident or a series of accidents can balance it.\n\n“25 May.\n\n“My dear Art,--\n\n“We’ve told yarns by the camp-fire in the prairies; and dressed one another’s wounds after trying a landing at the Marquesas; and drunk healths on the shore of Titicaca. There are more yarns to be told, and other wounds to be healed, and another health to be drunk. Won’t you let this be at my camp-fire to-morrow night? I have no hesitation in asking you, as I know a certain lady is engaged to a certain dinner-party, and that you are free. There will only be one other, our old pal at the Korea, Jack Seward. He’s coming, too, and we both want to mingle our weeps over the wine-cup, and to drink a health with all our hearts to the happiest man in all the wide world, who has won the noblest heart that God has made and the best worth winning. We promise you a hearty welcome, and a loving greeting, and a health as true as your own right hand. We shall both swear to leave you at home if you drink too deep to a certain pair of eyes. Come!\n\n“Yours, as ever and always,\n\nTelegram from Arthur Holmwood to Quincey P. Morris.\n\n“26 May.\n\n“Count me in every time. I bear messages which will make both your ears tingle.\n\n# MINA MURRAY’S JOURNAL\n\n24 July. Whitby.--Lucy met me at the station, looking sweeter and lovelier than ever, and we drove up to the house at the Crescent in which they have rooms. This is a lovely place. The little river, the Esk, runs through a deep valley, which broadens out as it comes near the harbour. A great viaduct runs across, with high piers, through which the view seems somehow further away than it really is. The valley is beautifully green, and it is so steep that when you are on the high land on either side you look right across it, unless you are near enough to see down. The houses of the old town--the side away from us--are all red-roofed, and seem piled up one over the other anyhow, like the pictures we see of Nuremberg. Right over the town is the ruin of Whitby Abbey, which was sacked by the Danes, and which is the scene of part of “Marmion,” where the girl was built up in the wall. It is a most noble ruin, of immense size, and full of beautiful and romantic bits; there is a legend that a white lady is seen in one of the windows. Between it and the town there is another church, the parish one, round which is a big graveyard, all full of tombstones. This is to my mind the nicest spot in Whitby, for it lies right over the town, and has a full view of the harbour and all up the bay to where the headland called Kettleness stretches out into the sea. It descends so steeply over the harbour that part of the bank has fallen away, and some of the graves have been destroyed. In one place part of the stonework of the graves stretches out over the sandy pathway far below. There are walks, with seats beside them, through the churchyard; and people go and sit there all day long looking at the beautiful view and enjoying the breeze. I shall come and sit here very often myself and work. Indeed, I am writing now, with my book on my knee, and listening to the talk of three old men who are sitting beside me. They seem to do nothing all day but sit up here and talk.\n\nThe harbour lies below me, with, on the far side, one long granite wall stretching out into the sea, with a curve outwards at the end of it, in the middle of which is a lighthouse. A heavy sea-wall runs along outside of it. On the near side, the sea-wall makes an elbow crooked inversely, and its end too has a lighthouse. Between the two piers there is a narrow opening into the harbour, which then suddenly widens.\n\nIt is nice at high water; but when the tide is out it shoals away to nothing, and there is merely the stream of the Esk, running between banks of sand, with rocks here and there. Outside the harbour on this side there rises for about half a mile a great reef, the sharp edge of which runs straight out from behind the south lighthouse. At the end of it is a buoy with a bell, which swings in bad weather, and sends in a mournful sound on the wind. They have a legend here that when a ship is lost bells are heard out at sea. I must ask the old man about this; he is coming this way....\n\nHe is a funny old man. He must be awfully old, for his face is all gnarled and twisted like the bark of a tree. He tells me that he is nearly a hundred, and that he was a sailor in the Greenland fishing fleet when Waterloo was fought. He is, I am afraid, a very sceptical person, for when I asked him about the bells at sea and the White Lady at the abbey he said very brusquely:--\n\n“I wouldn’t fash masel’ about them, miss. Them things be all wore out. Mind, I don’t say that they never was, but I do say that they wasn’t in my time. They be all very well for comers and trippers, an’ the like, but not for a nice young lady like you. Them feet-folks from York and Leeds that be always eatin’ cured herrin’s an’ drinkin’ tea an’ lookin’ out to buy cheap jet would creed aught. I wonder masel’ who’d be bothered tellin’ lies to them--even the newspapers, which is full of fool-talk.” I thought he would be a good person to learn interesting things from, so I asked him if he would mind telling me something about the whale-fishing in the old days. He was just settling himself to begin when the clock struck six, whereupon he laboured to get up, and said:--\n\n“I must gang ageeanwards home now, miss. My grand-daughter doesn’t like to be kept waitin’ when the tea is ready, for it takes me time to crammle aboon the grees, for there be a many of ’em; an’, miss, I lack belly-timber sairly by the clock.”\n\nHe hobbled away, and I could see him hurrying, as well as he could, down the steps. The steps are a great feature on the place. They lead from the town up to the church, there are hundreds of them--I do not know how many--and they wind up in a delicate curve; the slope is so gentle that a horse could easily walk up and down them. I think they must originally have had something to do with the abbey. I shall go home too. Lucy went out visiting with her mother, and as they were only duty calls, I did not go. They will be home by this.\n\n1 August.--I came up here an hour ago with Lucy, and we had a most interesting talk with my old friend and the two others who always come and join him. He is evidently the Sir Oracle of them, and I should think must have been in his time a most dictatorial person. He will not admit anything, and downfaces everybody. If he can’t out-argue them he bullies them, and then takes their silence for agreement with his views. Lucy was looking sweetly pretty in her white lawn frock; she has got a beautiful colour since she has been here. I noticed that the old men did not lose any time in coming up and sitting near her when we sat down. She is so sweet with old people; I think they all fell in love with her on the spot. Even my old man succumbed and did not contradict her, but gave me double share instead. I got him on the subject of the legends, and he went off at once into a sort of sermon. I must try to remember it and put it down:--\n\n“It be all fool-talk, lock, stock, and barrel; that’s what it be, an’ nowt else. These bans an’ wafts an’ boh-ghosts an’ barguests an’ bogles an’ all anent them is only fit to set bairns an’ dizzy women a-belderin’. They be nowt but air-blebs. They, an’ all grims an’ signs an’ warnin’s, be all invented by parsons an’ illsome beuk-bodies an’ railway touters to skeer an’ scunner hafflin’s, an’ to get folks to do somethin’ that they don’t other incline to. It makes me ireful to think o’ them. Why, it’s them that, not content with printin’ lies on paper an’ preachin’ them out of pulpits, does want to be cuttin’ them on the tombstones. Look here all around you in what airt ye will; all them steans, holdin’ up their heads as well as they can out of their pride, is acant--simply tumblin’ down with the weight o’ the lies wrote on them, ‘Here lies the body’ or ‘Sacred to the memory’ wrote on all of them, an’ yet in nigh half of them there bean’t no bodies at all; an’ the memories of them bean’t cared a pinch of snuff about, much less sacred. Lies all of them, nothin’ but lies of one kind or another! My gog, but it’ll be a quare scowderment at the Day of Judgment when they come tumblin’ up in their death-sarks, all jouped together an’ tryin’ to drag their tombsteans with them to prove how good they was; some of them trimmlin’ and ditherin’, with their hands that dozzened an’ slippy from lyin’ in the sea that they can’t even keep their grup o’ them.”\n\nI could see from the old fellow’s self-satisfied air and the way in which he looked round for the approval of his cronies that he was “showing off,” so I put in a word to keep him going:--\n\n“Oh, Mr. Swales, you can’t be serious. Surely these tombstones are not all wrong?”\n\n“Yabblins! There may be a poorish few not wrong, savin’ where they make out the people too good; for there be folk that do think a balm-bowl be like the sea, if only it be their own. The whole thing be only lies. Now look you here; you come here a stranger, an’ you see this kirk-garth.” I nodded, for I thought it better to assent, though I did not quite understand his dialect. I knew it had something to do with the church. He went on: “And you consate that all these steans be aboon folk that be happed here, snod an’ snog?” I assented again. “Then that be just where the lie comes in. Why, there be scores of these lay-beds that be toom as old Dun’s ’bacca-box on Friday night.” He nudged one of his companions, and they all laughed. “And my gog! how could they be otherwise? Look at that one, the aftest abaft the bier-bank: read it!” I went over and read:--\n\n“Edward Spencelagh, master mariner, murdered by pirates off the coast of Andres, April, 1854, æt. 30.” When I came back Mr. Swales went on:--\n\n“Who brought him home, I wonder, to hap him here? Murdered off the coast of Andres! an’ you consated his body lay under! Why, I could name ye a dozen whose bones lie in the Greenland seas above”--he pointed northwards--“or where the currents may have drifted them. There be the steans around ye. Ye can, with your young eyes, read the small-print of the lies from here. This Braithwaite Lowrey--I knew his father, lost in the Lively off Greenland in ’20; or Andrew Woodhouse, drowned in the same seas in 1777; or John Paxton, drowned off Cape Farewell a year later; or old John Rawlings, whose grandfather sailed with me, drowned in the Gulf of Finland in ’50. Do ye think that all these men will have to make a rush to Whitby when the trumpet sounds? I have me antherums aboot it! I tell ye that when they got here they’d be jommlin’ an’ jostlin’ one another that way that it ’ud be like a fight up on the ice in the old days, when we’d be at one another from daylight to dark, an’ tryin’ to tie up our cuts by the light of the aurora borealis.” This was evidently local pleasantry, for the old man cackled over it, and his cronies joined in with gusto.\n\n“But,” I said, “surely you are not quite correct, for you start on the assumption that all the poor people, or their spirits, will have to take their tombstones with them on the Day of Judgment. Do you think that will be really necessary?”\n\n“Well, what else be they tombstones for? Answer me that, miss!”\n\n“To please their relatives, I suppose.”\n\n“To please their relatives, you suppose!” This he said with intense scorn. “How will it pleasure their relatives to know that lies is wrote over them, and that everybody in the place knows that they be lies?” He pointed to a stone at our feet which had been laid down as a slab, on which the seat was rested, close to the edge of the cliff. “Read the lies on that thruff-stean,” he said. The letters were upside down to me from where I sat, but Lucy was more opposite to them, so she leant over and read:--\n\n“Sacred to the memory of George Canon, who died, in the hope of a glorious resurrection, on July, 29, 1873, falling from the rocks at Kettleness. This tomb was erected by his sorrowing mother to her dearly beloved son. ‘He was the only son of his mother, and she was a widow.’ Really, Mr. Swales, I don’t see anything very funny in that!” She spoke her comment very gravely and somewhat severely.\n\n“Ye don’t see aught funny! Ha! ha! But that’s because ye don’t gawm the sorrowin’ mother was a hell-cat that hated him because he was acrewk’d--a regular lamiter he was--an’ he hated her so that he committed suicide in order that she mightn’t get an insurance she put on his life. He blew nigh the top of his head off with an old musket that they had for scarin’ the crows with. ’Twarn’t for crows then, for it brought the clegs and the dowps to him. That’s the way he fell off the rocks. And, as to hopes of a glorious resurrection, I’ve often heard him say masel’ that he hoped he’d go to hell, for his mother was so pious that she’d be sure to go to heaven, an’ he didn’t want to addle where she was. Now isn’t that stean at any rate”--he hammered it with his stick as he spoke--“a pack of lies? and won’t it make Gabriel keckle when Geordie comes pantin’ up the grees with the tombstean balanced on his hump, and asks it to be took as evidence!”\n\nI did not know what to say, but Lucy turned the conversation as she said, rising up:--\n\n“Oh, why did you tell us of this? It is my favourite seat, and I cannot leave it; and now I find I must go on sitting over the grave of a suicide.”\n\n“That won’t harm ye, my pretty; an’ it may make poor Geordie gladsome to have so trim a lass sittin’ on his lap. That won’t hurt ye. Why, I’ve sat here off an’ on for nigh twenty years past, an’ it hasn’t done me no harm. Don’t ye fash about them as lies under ye, or that doesn’ lie there either! It’ll be time for ye to be getting scart when ye see the tombsteans all run away with, and the place as bare as a stubble-field. There’s the clock, an’ I must gang. My service to ye, ladies!” And off he hobbled.\n\nLucy and I sat awhile, and it was all so beautiful before us that we took hands as we sat; and she told me all over again about Arthur and their coming marriage. That made me just a little heart-sick, for I haven’t heard from Jonathan for a whole month.\n\nThe same day. I came up here alone, for I am very sad. There was no letter for me. I hope there cannot be anything the matter with Jonathan. The clock has just struck nine. I see the lights scattered all over the town, sometimes in rows where the streets are, and sometimes singly; they run right up the Esk and die away in the curve of the valley. To my left the view is cut off by a black line of roof of the old house next the abbey. The sheep and lambs are bleating in the fields away behind me, and there is a clatter of a donkey’s hoofs up the paved road below. The band on the pier is playing a harsh waltz in good time, and further along the quay there is a Salvation Army meeting in a back street. Neither of the bands hears the other, but up here I hear and see them both. I wonder where Jonathan is and if he is thinking of me! I wish he were here.\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n5 June.--The case of Renfield grows more interesting the more I get to understand the man. He has certain qualities very largely developed; selfishness, secrecy, and purpose. I wish I could get at what is the object of the latter. He seems to have some settled scheme of his own, but what it is I do not yet know. His redeeming quality is a love of animals, though, indeed, he has such curious turns in it that I sometimes imagine he is only abnormally cruel. His pets are of odd sorts. Just now his hobby is catching flies. He has at present such a quantity that I have had myself to expostulate. To my astonishment, he did not break out into a fury, as I expected, but took the matter in simple seriousness. He thought for a moment, and then said: “May I have three days? I shall clear them away.” Of course, I said that would do. I must watch him.\n\n18 June.--He has turned his mind now to spiders, and has got several very big fellows in a box. He keeps feeding them with his flies, and the number of the latter is becoming sensibly diminished, although he has used half his food in attracting more flies from outside to his room.\n\n1 July.--His spiders are now becoming as great a nuisance as his flies, and to-day I told him that he must get rid of them. He looked very sad at this, so I said that he must clear out some of them, at all events. He cheerfully acquiesced in this, and I gave him the same time as before for reduction. He disgusted me much while with him, for when a horrid blow-fly, bloated with some carrion food, buzzed into the room, he caught it, held it exultantly for a few moments between his finger and thumb, and, before I knew what he was going to do, put it in his mouth and ate it. I scolded him for it, but he argued quietly that it was very good and very wholesome; that it was life, strong life, and gave life to him. This gave me an idea, or the rudiment of one. I must watch how he gets rid of his spiders. He has evidently some deep problem in his mind, for he keeps a little note-book in which he is always jotting down something. Whole pages of it are filled with masses of figures, generally single numbers added up in batches, and then the totals added in batches again, as though he were “focussing” some account, as the auditors put it.\n\n8 July.--There is a method in his madness, and the rudimentary idea in my mind is growing. It will be a whole idea soon, and then, oh, unconscious cerebration! you will have to give the wall to your conscious brother. I kept away from my friend for a few days, so that I might notice if there were any change. Things remain as they were except that he has parted with some of his pets and got a new one. He has managed to get a sparrow, and has already partially tamed it. His means of taming is simple, for already the spiders have diminished. Those that do remain, however, are well fed, for he still brings in the flies by tempting them with his food.\n\n19 July.--We are progressing. My friend has now a whole colony of sparrows, and his flies and spiders are almost obliterated. When I came in he ran to me and said he wanted to ask me a great favour--a very, very great favour; and as he spoke he fawned on me like a dog. I asked him what it was, and he said, with a sort of rapture in his voice and bearing:--\n\n“A kitten, a nice little, sleek playful kitten, that I can play with, and teach, and feed--and feed--and feed!” I was not unprepared for this request, for I had noticed how his pets went on increasing in size and vivacity, but I did not care that his pretty family of tame sparrows should be wiped out in the same manner as the flies and the spiders; so I said I would see about it, and asked him if he would not rather have a cat than a kitten. His eagerness betrayed him as he answered:--\n\n“Oh, yes, I would like a cat! I only asked for a kitten lest you should refuse me a cat. No one would refuse me a kitten, would they?” I shook my head, and said that at present I feared it would not be possible, but that I would see about it. His face fell, and I could see a warning of danger in it, for there was a sudden fierce, sidelong look which meant killing. The man is an undeveloped homicidal maniac. I shall test him with his present craving and see how it will work out; then I shall know more.\n\n10 p. m.--I have visited him again and found him sitting in a corner brooding. When I came in he threw himself on his knees before me and implored me to let him have a cat; that his salvation depended upon it. I was firm, however, and told him that he could not have it, whereupon he went without a word, and sat down, gnawing his fingers, in the corner where I had found him. I shall see him in the morning early.\n\n20 July.--Visited Renfield very early, before the attendant went his rounds. Found him up and humming a tune. He was spreading out his sugar, which he had saved, in the window, and was manifestly beginning his fly-catching again; and beginning it cheerfully and with a good grace. I looked around for his birds, and not seeing them, asked him where they were. He replied, without turning round, that they had all flown away. There were a few feathers about the room and on his pillow a drop of blood. I said nothing, but went and told the keeper to report to me if there were anything odd about him during the day.\n\n11 a. m.--The attendant has just been to me to say that Renfield has been very sick and has disgorged a whole lot of feathers. “My belief is, doctor,” he said, “that he has eaten his birds, and that he just took and ate them raw!”\n\n11 p. m.--I gave Renfield a strong opiate to-night, enough to make even him sleep, and took away his pocket-book to look at it. The thought that has been buzzing about my brain lately is complete, and the theory proved. My homicidal maniac is of a peculiar kind. I shall have to invent a new classification for him, and call him a zoöphagous (life-eating) maniac; what he desires is to absorb as many lives as he can, and he has laid himself out to achieve it in a cumulative way. He gave many flies to one spider and many spiders to one bird, and then wanted a cat to eat the many birds. What would have been his later steps? It would almost be worth while to complete the experiment. It might be done if there were only a sufficient cause. Men sneered at vivisection, and yet look at its results to-day! Why not advance science in its most difficult and vital aspect--the knowledge of the brain? Had I even the secret of one such mind--did I hold the key to the fancy of even one lunatic--I might advance my own branch of science to a pitch compared with which Burdon-Sanderson’s physiology or Ferrier’s brain-knowledge would be as nothing. If only there were a sufficient cause! I must not think too much of this, or I may be tempted; a good cause might turn the scale with me, for may not I too be of an exceptional brain, congenitally?\n\nHow well the man reasoned; lunatics always do within their own scope. I wonder at how many lives he values a man, or if at only one. He has closed the account most accurately, and to-day begun a new record. How many of us begin a new record with each day of our lives?\n\nTo me it seems only yesterday that my whole life ended with my new hope, and that truly I began a new record. So it will be until the Great Recorder sums me up and closes my ledger account with a balance to profit or loss. Oh, Lucy, Lucy, I cannot be angry with you, nor can I be angry with my friend whose happiness is yours; but I must only wait on hopeless and work. Work! work!\n\nIf I only could have as strong a cause as my poor mad friend there--a good, unselfish cause to make me work--that would be indeed happiness.\n\nMina Murray’s Journal.\n\n26 July.--I am anxious, and it soothes me to express myself here; it is like whispering to one’s self and listening at the same time. And there is also something about the shorthand symbols that makes it different from writing. I am unhappy about Lucy and about Jonathan. I had not heard from Jonathan for some time, and was very concerned; but yesterday dear Mr. Hawkins, who is always so kind, sent me a letter from him. I had written asking him if he had heard, and he said the enclosed had just been received. It is only a line dated from Castle Dracula, and says that he is just starting for home. That is not like Jonathan; I do not understand it, and it makes me uneasy. Then, too, Lucy, although she is so well, has lately taken to her old habit of walking in her sleep. Her mother has spoken to me about it, and we have decided that I am to lock the door of our room every night. Mrs. Westenra has got an idea that sleep-walkers always go out on roofs of houses and along the edges of cliffs and then get suddenly wakened and fall over with a despairing cry that echoes all over the place. Poor dear, she is naturally anxious about Lucy, and she tells me that her husband, Lucy’s father, had the same habit; that he would get up in the night and dress himself and go out, if he were not stopped. Lucy is to be married in the autumn, and she is already planning out her dresses and how her house is to be arranged. I sympathise with her, for I do the same, only Jonathan and I will start in life in a very simple way, and shall have to try to make both ends meet. Mr. Holmwood--he is the Hon. Arthur Holmwood, only son of Lord Godalming--is coming up here very shortly--as soon as he can leave town, for his father is not very well, and I think dear Lucy is counting the moments till he comes. She wants to take him up to the seat on the churchyard cliff and show him the beauty of Whitby. I daresay it is the waiting which disturbs her; she will be all right when he arrives.\n\n27 July.--No news from Jonathan. I am getting quite uneasy about him, though why I should I do not know; but I do wish that he would write, if it were only a single line. Lucy walks more than ever, and each night I am awakened by her moving about the room. Fortunately, the weather is so hot that she cannot get cold; but still the anxiety and the perpetually being wakened is beginning to tell on me, and I am getting nervous and wakeful myself. Thank God, Lucy’s health keeps up. Mr. Holmwood has been suddenly called to Ring to see his father, who has been taken seriously ill. Lucy frets at the postponement of seeing him, but it does not touch her looks; she is a trifle stouter, and her cheeks are a lovely rose-pink. She has lost that anæmic look which she had. I pray it will all last.\n\n3 August.--Another week gone, and no news from Jonathan, not even to Mr. Hawkins, from whom I have heard. Oh, I do hope he is not ill. He surely would have written. I look at that last letter of his, but somehow it does not satisfy me. It does not read like him, and yet it is his writing. There is no mistake of that. Lucy has not walked much in her sleep the last week, but there is an odd concentration about her which I do not understand; even in her sleep she seems to be watching me. She tries the door, and finding it locked, goes about the room searching for the key.\n\n6 August.--Another three days, and no news. This suspense is getting dreadful. If I only knew where to write to or where to go to, I should feel easier; but no one has heard a word of Jonathan since that last letter. I must only pray to God for patience. Lucy is more excitable than ever, but is otherwise well. Last night was very threatening, and the fishermen say that we are in for a storm. I must try to watch it and learn the weather signs. To-day is a grey day, and the sun as I write is hidden in thick clouds, high over Kettleness. Everything is grey--except the green grass, which seems like emerald amongst it; grey earthy rock; grey clouds, tinged with the sunburst at the far edge, hang over the grey sea, into which the sand-points stretch like grey fingers. The sea is tumbling in over the shallows and the sandy flats with a roar, muffled in the sea-mists drifting inland. The horizon is lost in a grey mist. All is vastness; the clouds are piled up like giant rocks, and there is a “brool” over the sea that sounds like some presage of doom. Dark figures are on the beach here and there, sometimes half shrouded in the mist, and seem “men like trees walking.” The fishing-boats are racing for home, and rise and dip in the ground swell as they sweep into the harbour, bending to the scuppers. Here comes old Mr. Swales. He is making straight for me, and I can see, by the way he lifts his hat, that he wants to talk....\n\nI have been quite touched by the change in the poor old man. When he sat down beside me, he said in a very gentle way:--\n\n“I want to say something to you, miss.” I could see he was not at ease, so I took his poor old wrinkled hand in mine and asked him to speak fully; so he said, leaving his hand in mine:--\n\n“I’m afraid, my deary, that I must have shocked you by all the wicked things I’ve been sayin’ about the dead, and such like, for weeks past; but I didn’t mean them, and I want ye to remember that when I’m gone. We aud folks that be daffled, and with one foot abaft the krok-hooal, don’t altogether like to think of it, and we don’t want to feel scart of it; an’ that’s why I’ve took to makin’ light of it, so that I’d cheer up my own heart a bit. But, Lord love ye, miss, I ain’t afraid of dyin’, not a bit; only I don’t want to die if I can help it. My time must be nigh at hand now, for I be aud, and a hundred years is too much for any man to expect; and I’m so nigh it that the Aud Man is already whettin’ his scythe. Ye see, I can’t get out o’ the habit of caffin’ about it all at once; the chafts will wag as they be used to. Some day soon the Angel of Death will sound his trumpet for me. But don’t ye dooal an’ greet, my deary!”--for he saw that I was crying--“if he should come this very night I’d not refuse to answer his call. For life be, after all, only a waitin’ for somethin’ else than what we’re doin’; and death be all that we can rightly depend on. But I’m content, for it’s comin’ to me, my deary, and comin’ quick. It may be comin’ while we be lookin’ and wonderin’. Maybe it’s in that wind out over the sea that’s bringin’ with it loss and wreck, and sore distress, and sad hearts. Look! look!” he cried suddenly. “There’s something in that wind and in the hoast beyont that sounds, and looks, and tastes, and smells like death. It’s in the air; I feel it comin’. Lord, make me answer cheerful when my call comes!” He held up his arms devoutly, and raised his hat. His mouth moved as though he were praying. After a few minutes’ silence, he got up, shook hands with me, and blessed me, and said good-bye, and hobbled off. It all touched me, and upset me very much.\n\nI was glad when the coastguard came along, with his spy-glass under his arm. He stopped to talk with me, as he always does, but all the time kept looking at a strange ship.\n\n“I can’t make her out,” he said; “she’s a Russian, by the look of her; but she’s knocking about in the queerest way. She doesn’t know her mind a bit; she seems to see the storm coming, but can’t decide whether to run up north in the open, or to put in here. Look there again! She is steered mighty strangely, for she doesn’t mind the hand on the wheel; changes about with every puff of wind. We’ll hear more of her before this time to-morrow.”\n\n(Pasted in Mina Murray’s Journal.)\n\nFrom a Correspondent.\n\nWhitby.\n\nOne of the greatest and suddenest storms on record has just been experienced here, with results both strange and unique. The weather had been somewhat sultry, but not to any degree uncommon in the month of August. Saturday evening was as fine as was ever known, and the great body of holiday-makers laid out yesterday for visits to Mulgrave Woods, Robin Hood’s Bay, Rig Mill, Runswick, Staithes, and the various trips in the neighbourhood of Whitby. The steamers Emma and Scarborough made trips up and down the coast, and there was an unusual amount of “tripping” both to and from Whitby. The day was unusually fine till the afternoon, when some of the gossips who frequent the East Cliff churchyard, and from that commanding eminence watch the wide sweep of sea visible to the north and east, called attention to a sudden show of “mares’-tails” high in the sky to the north-west. The wind was then blowing from the south-west in the mild degree which in barometrical language is ranked “No. 2: light breeze.” The coastguard on duty at once made report, and one old fisherman, who for more than half a century has kept watch on weather signs from the East Cliff, foretold in an emphatic manner the coming of a sudden storm. The approach of sunset was so very beautiful, so grand in its masses of splendidly-coloured clouds, that there was quite an assemblage on the walk along the cliff in the old churchyard to enjoy the beauty. Before the sun dipped below the black mass of Kettleness, standing boldly athwart the western sky, its downward way was marked by myriad clouds of every sunset-colour--flame, purple, pink, green, violet, and all the tints of gold; with here and there masses not large, but of seemingly absolute blackness, in all sorts of shapes, as well outlined as colossal silhouettes. The experience was not lost on the painters, and doubtless some of the sketches of the “Prelude to the Great Storm” will grace the R. A. and R. I. walls in May next. More than one captain made up his mind then and there that his “cobble” or his “mule,” as they term the different classes of boats, would remain in the harbour till the storm had passed. The wind fell away entirely during the evening, and at midnight there was a dead calm, a sultry heat, and that prevailing intensity which, on the approach of thunder, affects persons of a sensitive nature. There were but few lights in sight at sea, for even the coasting steamers, which usually “hug” the shore so closely, kept well to seaward, and but few fishing-boats were in sight. The only sail noticeable was a foreign schooner with all sails set, which was seemingly going westwards. The foolhardiness or ignorance of her officers was a prolific theme for comment whilst she remained in sight, and efforts were made to signal her to reduce sail in face of her danger. Before the night shut down she was seen with sails idly flapping as she gently rolled on the undulating swell of the sea,\n\n“As idle as a painted ship upon a painted ocean.”\n\nShortly before ten o’clock the stillness of the air grew quite oppressive, and the silence was so marked that the bleating of a sheep inland or the barking of a dog in the town was distinctly heard, and the band on the pier, with its lively French air, was like a discord in the great harmony of nature’s silence. A little after midnight came a strange sound from over the sea, and high overhead the air began to carry a strange, faint, hollow booming.\n\nThen without warning the tempest broke. With a rapidity which, at the time, seemed incredible, and even afterwards is impossible to realize, the whole aspect of nature at once became convulsed. The waves rose in growing fury, each overtopping its fellow, till in a very few minutes the lately glassy sea was like a roaring and devouring monster. White-crested waves beat madly on the level sands and rushed up the shelving cliffs; others broke over the piers, and with their spume swept the lanthorns of the lighthouses which rise from the end of either pier of Whitby Harbour. The wind roared like thunder, and blew with such force that it was with difficulty that even strong men kept their feet, or clung with grim clasp to the iron stanchions. It was found necessary to clear the entire piers from the mass of onlookers, or else the fatalities of the night would have been increased manifold. To add to the difficulties and dangers of the time, masses of sea-fog came drifting inland--white, wet clouds, which swept by in ghostly fashion, so dank and damp and cold that it needed but little effort of imagination to think that the spirits of those lost at sea were touching their living brethren with the clammy hands of death, and many a one shuddered as the wreaths of sea-mist swept by. At times the mist cleared, and the sea for some distance could be seen in the glare of the lightning, which now came thick and fast, followed by such sudden peals of thunder that the whole sky overhead seemed trembling under the shock of the footsteps of the storm.\n\nSome of the scenes thus revealed were of immeasurable grandeur and of absorbing interest--the sea, running mountains high, threw skywards with each wave mighty masses of white foam, which the tempest seemed to snatch at and whirl away into space; here and there a fishing-boat, with a rag of sail, running madly for shelter before the blast; now and again the white wings of a storm-tossed sea-bird. On the summit of the East Cliff the new searchlight was ready for experiment, but had not yet been tried. The officers in charge of it got it into working order, and in the pauses of the inrushing mist swept with it the surface of the sea. Once or twice its service was most effective, as when a fishing-boat, with gunwale under water, rushed into the harbour, able, by the guidance of the sheltering light, to avoid the danger of dashing against the piers. As each boat achieved the safety of the port there was a shout of joy from the mass of people on shore, a shout which for a moment seemed to cleave the gale and was then swept away in its rush.\n\nBefore long the searchlight discovered some distance away a schooner with all sails set, apparently the same vessel which had been noticed earlier in the evening. The wind had by this time backed to the east, and there was a shudder amongst the watchers on the cliff as they realized the terrible danger in which she now was. Between her and the port lay the great flat reef on which so many good ships have from time to time suffered, and, with the wind blowing from its present quarter, it would be quite impossible that she should fetch the entrance of the harbour. It was now nearly the hour of high tide, but the waves were so great that in their troughs the shallows of the shore were almost visible, and the schooner, with all sails set, was rushing with such speed that, in the words of one old salt, “she must fetch up somewhere, if it was only in hell.” Then came another rush of sea-fog, greater than any hitherto--a mass of dank mist, which seemed to close on all things like a grey pall, and left available to men only the organ of hearing, for the roar of the tempest, and the crash of the thunder, and the booming of the mighty billows came through the damp oblivion even louder than before. The rays of the searchlight were kept fixed on the harbour mouth across the East Pier, where the shock was expected, and men waited breathless. The wind suddenly shifted to the north-east, and the remnant of the sea-fog melted in the blast; and then, mirabile dictu, between the piers, leaping from wave to wave as it rushed at headlong speed, swept the strange schooner before the blast, with all sail set, and gained the safety of the harbour. The searchlight followed her, and a shudder ran through all who saw her, for lashed to the helm was a corpse, with drooping head, which swung horribly to and fro at each motion of the ship. No other form could be seen on deck at all. A great awe came on all as they realised that the ship, as if by a miracle, had found the harbour, unsteered save by the hand of a dead man! However, all took place more quickly than it takes to write these words. The schooner paused not, but rushing across the harbour, pitched herself on that accumulation of sand and gravel washed by many tides and many storms into the south-east corner of the pier jutting under the East Cliff, known locally as Tate Hill Pier.\n\nThere was of course a considerable concussion as the vessel drove up on the sand heap. Every spar, rope, and stay was strained, and some of the “top-hammer” came crashing down. But, strangest of all, the very instant the shore was touched, an immense dog sprang up on deck from below, as if shot up by the concussion, and running forward, jumped from the bow on the sand. Making straight for the steep cliff, where the churchyard hangs over the laneway to the East Pier so steeply that some of the flat tombstones--“thruff-steans” or “through-stones,” as they call them in the Whitby vernacular--actually project over where the sustaining cliff has fallen away, it disappeared in the darkness, which seemed intensified just beyond the focus of the searchlight.\n\nIt so happened that there was no one at the moment on Tate Hill Pier, as all those whose houses are in close proximity were either in bed or were out on the heights above. Thus the coastguard on duty on the eastern side of the harbour, who at once ran down to the little pier, was the first to climb on board. The men working the searchlight, after scouring the entrance of the harbour without seeing anything, then turned the light on the derelict and kept it there. The coastguard ran aft, and when he came beside the wheel, bent over to examine it, and recoiled at once as though under some sudden emotion. This seemed to pique general curiosity, and quite a number of people began to run. It is a good way round from the West Cliff by the Drawbridge to Tate Hill Pier, but your correspondent is a fairly good runner, and came well ahead of the crowd. When I arrived, however, I found already assembled on the pier a crowd, whom the coastguard and police refused to allow to come on board. By the courtesy of the chief boatman, I was, as your correspondent, permitted to climb on deck, and was one of a small group who saw the dead seaman whilst actually lashed to the wheel.\n\nIt was no wonder that the coastguard was surprised, or even awed, for not often can such a sight have been seen. The man was simply fastened by his hands, tied one over the other, to a spoke of the wheel. Between the inner hand and the wood was a crucifix, the set of beads on which it was fastened being around both wrists and wheel, and all kept fast by the binding cords. The poor fellow may have been seated at one time, but the flapping and buffeting of the sails had worked through the rudder of the wheel and dragged him to and fro, so that the cords with which he was tied had cut the flesh to the bone. Accurate note was made of the state of things, and a doctor--Surgeon J. M. Caffyn, of 33, East Elliot Place--who came immediately after me, declared, after making examination, that the man must have been dead for quite two days. In his pocket was a bottle, carefully corked, empty save for a little roll of paper, which proved to be the addendum to the log. The coastguard said the man must have tied up his own hands, fastening the knots with his teeth. The fact that a coastguard was the first on board may save some complications, later on, in the Admiralty Court; for coastguards cannot claim the salvage which is the right of the first civilian entering on a derelict. Already, however, the legal tongues are wagging, and one young law student is loudly asserting that the rights of the owner are already completely sacrificed, his property being held in contravention of the statutes of mortmain, since the tiller, as emblemship, if not proof, of delegated possession, is held in a dead hand. It is needless to say that the dead steersman has been reverently removed from the place where he held his honourable watch and ward till death--a steadfastness as noble as that of the young Casabianca--and placed in the mortuary to await inquest.\n\nAlready the sudden storm is passing, and its fierceness is abating; crowds are scattering homeward, and the sky is beginning to redden over the Yorkshire wolds. I shall send, in time for your next issue, further details of the derelict ship which found her way so miraculously into harbour in the storm.\n\nWhitby\n\n9 August.--The sequel to the strange arrival of the derelict in the storm last night is almost more startling than the thing itself. It turns out that the schooner is a Russian from Varna, and is called the Demeter. She is almost entirely in ballast of silver sand, with only a small amount of cargo--a number of great wooden boxes filled with mould. This cargo was consigned to a Whitby solicitor, Mr. S. F. Billington, of 7, The Crescent, who this morning went aboard and formally took possession of the goods consigned to him. The Russian consul, too, acting for the charter-party, took formal possession of the ship, and paid all harbour dues, etc. Nothing is talked about here to-day except the strange coincidence; the officials of the Board of Trade have been most exacting in seeing that every compliance has been made with existing regulations. As the matter is to be a “nine days’ wonder,” they are evidently determined that there shall be no cause of after complaint. A good deal of interest was abroad concerning the dog which landed when the ship struck, and more than a few of the members of the S. P. C. A., which is very strong in Whitby, have tried to befriend the animal. To the general disappointment, however, it was not to be found; it seems to have disappeared entirely from the town. It may be that it was frightened and made its way on to the moors, where it is still hiding in terror. There are some who look with dread on such a possibility, lest later on it should in itself become a danger, for it is evidently a fierce brute. Early this morning a large dog, a half-bred mastiff belonging to a coal merchant close to Tate Hill Pier, was found dead in the roadway opposite to its master’s yard. It had been fighting, and manifestly had had a savage opponent, for its throat was torn away, and its belly was slit open as if with a savage claw.\n\nLater.--By the kindness of the Board of Trade inspector, I have been permitted to look over the log-book of the Demeter, which was in order up to within three days, but contained nothing of special interest except as to facts of missing men. The greatest interest, however, is with regard to the paper found in the bottle, which was to-day produced at the inquest; and a more strange narrative than the two between them unfold it has not been my lot to come across. As there is no motive for concealment, I am permitted to use them, and accordingly send you a rescript, simply omitting technical details of seamanship and supercargo. It almost seems as though the captain had been seized with some kind of mania before he had got well into blue water, and that this had developed persistently throughout the voyage. Of course my statement must be taken cum grano, since I am writing from the dictation of a clerk of the Russian consul, who kindly translated for me, time being short.\n\nVarna to Whitby.\n\nWritten 18 July, things so strange happening, that I shall keep accurate note henceforth till we land.\n\nOn 6 July we finished taking in cargo, silver sand and boxes of earth. At noon set sail. East wind, fresh. Crew, five hands ... two mates, cook, and myself (captain).\n\nOn 11 July at dawn entered Bosphorus. Boarded by Turkish Customs officers. Backsheesh. All correct. Under way at 4 p. m.\n\nOn 12 July through Dardanelles. More Customs officers and flagboat of guarding squadron. Backsheesh again. Work of officers thorough, but quick. Want us off soon. At dark passed into Archipelago.\n\nOn 13 July passed Cape Matapan. Crew dissatisfied about something. Seemed scared, but would not speak out.\n\nOn 14 July was somewhat anxious about crew. Men all steady fellows, who sailed with me before. Mate could not make out what was wrong; they only told him there was something, and crossed themselves. Mate lost temper with one of them that day and struck him. Expected fierce quarrel, but all was quiet.\n\nOn 16 July mate reported in the morning that one of crew, Petrofsky, was missing. Could not account for it. Took larboard watch eight bells last night; was relieved by Abramoff, but did not go to bunk. Men more downcast than ever. All said they expected something of the kind, but would not say more than there was something aboard. Mate getting very impatient with them; feared some trouble ahead.\n\nOn 17 July, yesterday, one of the men, Olgaren, came to my cabin, and in an awestruck way confided to me that he thought there was a strange man aboard the ship. He said that in his watch he had been sheltering behind the deck-house, as there was a rain-storm, when he saw a tall, thin man, who was not like any of the crew, come up the companion-way, and go along the deck forward, and disappear. He followed cautiously, but when he got to bows found no one, and the hatchways were all closed. He was in a panic of superstitious fear, and I am afraid the panic may spread. To allay it, I shall to-day search entire ship carefully from stem to stern.\n\nLater in the day I got together the whole crew, and told them, as they evidently thought there was some one in the ship, we would search from stem to stern. First mate angry; said it was folly, and to yield to such foolish ideas would demoralise the men; said he would engage to keep them out of trouble with a handspike. I let him take the helm, while the rest began thorough search, all keeping abreast, with lanterns: we left no corner unsearched. As there were only the big wooden boxes, there were no odd corners where a man could hide. Men much relieved when search over, and went back to work cheerfully. First mate scowled, but said nothing.\n\n22 July.--Rough weather last three days, and all hands busy with sails--no time to be frightened. Men seem to have forgotten their dread. Mate cheerful again, and all on good terms. Praised men for work in bad weather. Passed Gibralter and out through Straits. All well.\n\n24 July.--There seems some doom over this ship. Already a hand short, and entering on the Bay of Biscay with wild weather ahead, and yet last night another man lost--disappeared. Like the first, he came off his watch and was not seen again. Men all in a panic of fear; sent a round robin, asking to have double watch, as they fear to be alone. Mate angry. Fear there will be some trouble, as either he or the men will do some violence.\n\n28 July.--Four days in hell, knocking about in a sort of maelstrom, and the wind a tempest. No sleep for any one. Men all worn out. Hardly know how to set a watch, since no one fit to go on. Second mate volunteered to steer and watch, and let men snatch a few hours’ sleep. Wind abating; seas still terrific, but feel them less, as ship is steadier.\n\n29 July.--Another tragedy. Had single watch to-night, as crew too tired to double. When morning watch came on deck could find no one except steersman. Raised outcry, and all came on deck. Thorough search, but no one found. Are now without second mate, and crew in a panic. Mate and I agreed to go armed henceforth and wait for any sign of cause.\n\n30 July.--Last night. Rejoiced we are nearing England. Weather fine, all sails set. Retired worn out; slept soundly; awaked by mate telling me that both man of watch and steersman missing. Only self and mate and two hands left to work ship.\n\n1 August.--Two days of fog, and not a sail sighted. Had hoped when in the English Channel to be able to signal for help or get in somewhere. Not having power to work sails, have to run before wind. Dare not lower, as could not raise them again. We seem to be drifting to some terrible doom. Mate now more demoralised than either of men. His stronger nature seems to have worked inwardly against himself. Men are beyond fear, working stolidly and patiently, with minds made up to worst. They are Russian, he Roumanian.\n\n2 August, midnight.--Woke up from few minutes’ sleep by hearing a cry, seemingly outside my port. Could see nothing in fog. Rushed on deck, and ran against mate. Tells me heard cry and ran, but no sign of man on watch. One more gone. Lord, help us! Mate says we must be past Straits of Dover, as in a moment of fog lifting he saw North Foreland, just as he heard the man cry out. If so we are now off in the North Sea, and only God can guide us in the fog, which seems to move with us; and God seems to have deserted us.\n\n3 August.--At midnight I went to relieve the man at the wheel, and when I got to it found no one there. The wind was steady, and as we ran before it there was no yawing. I dared not leave it, so shouted for the mate. After a few seconds he rushed up on deck in his flannels. He looked wild-eyed and haggard, and I greatly fear his reason has given way. He came close to me and whispered hoarsely, with his mouth to my ear, as though fearing the very air might hear: “It is here; I know it, now. On the watch last night I saw It, like a man, tall and thin, and ghastly pale. It was in the bows, and looking out. I crept behind It, and gave It my knife; but the knife went through It, empty as the air.” And as he spoke he took his knife and drove it savagely into space. Then he went on: “But It is here, and I’ll find It. It is in the hold, perhaps in one of those boxes. I’ll unscrew them one by one and see. You work the helm.” And, with a warning look and his finger on his lip, he went below. There was springing up a choppy wind, and I could not leave the helm. I saw him come out on deck again with a tool-chest and a lantern, and go down the forward hatchway. He is mad, stark, raving mad, and it’s no use my trying to stop him. He can’t hurt those big boxes: they are invoiced as “clay,” and to pull them about is as harmless a thing as he can do. So here I stay, and mind the helm, and write these notes. I can only trust in God and wait till the fog clears. Then, if I can’t steer to any harbour with the wind that is, I shall cut down sails and lie by, and signal for help....\n\nIt is nearly all over now. Just as I was beginning to hope that the mate would come out calmer--for I heard him knocking away at something in the hold, and work is good for him--there came up the hatchway a sudden, startled scream, which made my blood run cold, and up on the deck he came as if shot from a gun--a raging madman, with his eyes rolling and his face convulsed with fear. “Save me! save me!” he cried, and then looked round on the blanket of fog. His horror turned to despair, and in a steady voice he said: “You had better come too, captain, before it is too late. He is there. I know the secret now. The sea will save me from Him, and it is all that is left!” Before I could say a word, or move forward to seize him, he sprang on the bulwark and deliberately threw himself into the sea. I suppose I know the secret too, now. It was this madman who had got rid of the men one by one, and now he has followed them himself. God help me! How am I to account for all these horrors when I get to port? When I get to port! Will that ever be?\n\n4 August.--Still fog, which the sunrise cannot pierce. I know there is sunrise because I am a sailor, why else I know not. I dared not go below, I dared not leave the helm; so here all night I stayed, and in the dimness of the night I saw It--Him! God forgive me, but the mate was right to jump overboard. It was better to die like a man; to die like a sailor in blue water no man can object. But I am captain, and I must not leave my ship. But I shall baffle this fiend or monster, for I shall tie my hands to the wheel when my strength begins to fail, and along with them I shall tie that which He--It!--dare not touch; and then, come good wind or foul, I shall save my soul, and my honour as a captain. I am growing weaker, and the night is coming on. If He can look me in the face again, I may not have time to act.... If we are wrecked, mayhap this bottle may be found, and those who find it may understand; if not, ... well, then all men shall know that I have been true to my trust. God and the Blessed Virgin and the saints help a poor ignorant soul trying to do his duty....\n\nOf course the verdict was an open one. There is no evidence to adduce; and whether or not the man himself committed the murders there is now none to say. The folk here hold almost universally that the captain is simply a hero, and he is to be given a public funeral. Already it is arranged that his body is to be taken with a train of boats up the Esk for a piece and then brought back to Tate Hill Pier and up the abbey steps; for he is to be buried in the churchyard on the cliff. The owners of more than a hundred boats have already given in their names as wishing to follow him to the grave.\n\nNo trace has ever been found of the great dog; at which there is much mourning, for, with public opinion in its present state, he would, I believe, be adopted by the town. To-morrow will see the funeral; and so will end this one more “mystery of the sea.”\n\nMina Murray’s Journal.\n\n8 August.--Lucy was very restless all night, and I, too, could not sleep. The storm was fearful, and as it boomed loudly among the chimney-pots, it made me shudder. When a sharp puff came it seemed to be like a distant gun. Strangely enough, Lucy did not wake; but she got up twice and dressed herself. Fortunately, each time I awoke in time and managed to undress her without waking her, and got her back to bed. It is a very strange thing, this sleep-walking, for as soon as her will is thwarted in any physical way, her intention, if there be any, disappears, and she yields herself almost exactly to the routine of her life.\n\nEarly in the morning we both got up and went down to the harbour to see if anything had happened in the night. There were very few people about, and though the sun was bright, and the air clear and fresh, the big, grim-looking waves, that seemed dark themselves because the foam that topped them was like snow, forced themselves in through the narrow mouth of the harbour--like a bullying man going through a crowd. Somehow I felt glad that Jonathan was not on the sea last night, but on land. But, oh, is he on land or sea? Where is he, and how? I am getting fearfully anxious about him. If I only knew what to do, and could do anything!\n\n10 August.--The funeral of the poor sea-captain to-day was most touching. Every boat in the harbour seemed to be there, and the coffin was carried by captains all the way from Tate Hill Pier up to the churchyard. Lucy came with me, and we went early to our old seat, whilst the cortège of boats went up the river to the Viaduct and came down again. We had a lovely view, and saw the procession nearly all the way. The poor fellow was laid to rest quite near our seat so that we stood on it when the time came and saw everything. Poor Lucy seemed much upset. She was restless and uneasy all the time, and I cannot but think that her dreaming at night is telling on her. She is quite odd in one thing: she will not admit to me that there is any cause for restlessness; or if there be, she does not understand it herself. There is an additional cause in that poor old Mr. Swales was found dead this morning on our seat, his neck being broken. He had evidently, as the doctor said, fallen back in the seat in some sort of fright, for there was a look of fear and horror on his face that the men said made them shudder. Poor dear old man! Perhaps he had seen Death with his dying eyes! Lucy is so sweet and sensitive that she feels influences more acutely than other people do. Just now she was quite upset by a little thing which I did not much heed, though I am myself very fond of animals. One of the men who came up here often to look for the boats was followed by his dog. The dog is always with him. They are both quiet persons, and I never saw the man angry, nor heard the dog bark. During the service the dog would not come to its master, who was on the seat with us, but kept a few yards off, barking and howling. Its master spoke to it gently, and then harshly, and then angrily; but it would neither come nor cease to make a noise. It was in a sort of fury, with its eyes savage, and all its hairs bristling out like a cat’s tail when puss is on the war-path. Finally the man, too, got angry, and jumped down and kicked the dog, and then took it by the scruff of the neck and half dragged and half threw it on the tombstone on which the seat is fixed. The moment it touched the stone the poor thing became quiet and fell all into a tremble. It did not try to get away, but crouched down, quivering and cowering, and was in such a pitiable state of terror that I tried, though without effect, to comfort it. Lucy was full of pity, too, but she did not attempt to touch the dog, but looked at it in an agonised sort of way. I greatly fear that she is of too super-sensitive a nature to go through the world without trouble. She will be dreaming of this to-night, I am sure. The whole agglomeration of things--the ship steered into port by a dead man; his attitude, tied to the wheel with a crucifix and beads; the touching funeral; the dog, now furious and now in terror--will all afford material for her dreams.\n\nI think it will be best for her to go to bed tired out physically, so I shall take her for a long walk by the cliffs to Robin Hood’s Bay and back. She ought not to have much inclination for sleep-walking then.\n\n# MINA MURRAY’S JOURNAL\n\nSame day, 11 o’clock p. m.--Oh, but I am tired! If it were not that I had made my diary a duty I should not open it to-night. We had a lovely walk. Lucy, after a while, was in gay spirits, owing, I think, to some dear cows who came nosing towards us in a field close to the lighthouse, and frightened the wits out of us. I believe we forgot everything except, of course, personal fear, and it seemed to wipe the slate clean and give us a fresh start. We had a capital “severe tea” at Robin Hood’s Bay in a sweet little old-fashioned inn, with a bow-window right over the seaweed-covered rocks of the strand. I believe we should have shocked the “New Woman” with our appetites. Men are more tolerant, bless them! Then we walked home with some, or rather many, stoppages to rest, and with our hearts full of a constant dread of wild bulls. Lucy was really tired, and we intended to creep off to bed as soon as we could. The young curate came in, however, and Mrs. Westenra asked him to stay for supper. Lucy and I had both a fight for it with the dusty miller; I know it was a hard fight on my part, and I am quite heroic. I think that some day the bishops must get together and see about breeding up a new class of curates, who don’t take supper, no matter how they may be pressed to, and who will know when girls are tired. Lucy is asleep and breathing softly. She has more colour in her cheeks than usual, and looks, oh, so sweet. If Mr. Holmwood fell in love with her seeing her only in the drawing-room, I wonder what he would say if he saw her now. Some of the “New Women” writers will some day start an idea that men and women should be allowed to see each other asleep before proposing or accepting. But I suppose the New Woman won’t condescend in future to accept; she will do the proposing herself. And a nice job she will make of it, too! There’s some consolation in that. I am so happy to-night, because dear Lucy seems better. I really believe she has turned the corner, and that we are over her troubles with dreaming. I should be quite happy if I only knew if Jonathan.... God bless and keep him.\n\n11 August, 3 a. m.--Diary again. No sleep now, so I may as well write. I am too agitated to sleep. We have had such an adventure, such an agonising experience. I fell asleep as soon as I had closed my diary.... Suddenly I became broad awake, and sat up, with a horrible sense of fear upon me, and of some feeling of emptiness around me. The room was dark, so I could not see Lucy’s bed; I stole across and felt for her. The bed was empty. I lit a match and found that she was not in the room. The door was shut, but not locked, as I had left it. I feared to wake her mother, who has been more than usually ill lately, so threw on some clothes and got ready to look for her. As I was leaving the room it struck me that the clothes she wore might give me some clue to her dreaming intention. Dressing-gown would mean house; dress, outside. Dressing-gown and dress were both in their places. “Thank God,” I said to myself, “she cannot be far, as she is only in her nightdress.” I ran downstairs and looked in the sitting-room. Not there! Then I looked in all the other open rooms of the house, with an ever-growing fear chilling my heart. Finally I came to the hall door and found it open. It was not wide open, but the catch of the lock had not caught. The people of the house are careful to lock the door every night, so I feared that Lucy must have gone out as she was. There was no time to think of what might happen; a vague, overmastering fear obscured all details. I took a big, heavy shawl and ran out. The clock was striking one as I was in the Crescent, and there was not a soul in sight. I ran along the North Terrace, but could see no sign of the white figure which I expected. At the edge of the West Cliff above the pier I looked across the harbour to the East Cliff, in the hope or fear--I don’t know which--of seeing Lucy in our favourite seat. There was a bright full moon, with heavy black, driving clouds, which threw the whole scene into a fleeting diorama of light and shade as they sailed across. For a moment or two I could see nothing, as the shadow of a cloud obscured St. Mary’s Church and all around it. Then as the cloud passed I could see the ruins of the abbey coming into view; and as the edge of a narrow band of light as sharp as a sword-cut moved along, the church and the churchyard became gradually visible. Whatever my expectation was, it was not disappointed, for there, on our favourite seat, the silver light of the moon struck a half-reclining figure, snowy white. The coming of the cloud was too quick for me to see much, for shadow shut down on light almost immediately; but it seemed to me as though something dark stood behind the seat where the white figure shone, and bent over it. What it was, whether man or beast, I could not tell; I did not wait to catch another glance, but flew down the steep steps to the pier and along by the fish-market to the bridge, which was the only way to reach the East Cliff. The town seemed as dead, for not a soul did I see; I rejoiced that it was so, for I wanted no witness of poor Lucy’s condition. The time and distance seemed endless, and my knees trembled and my breath came laboured as I toiled up the endless steps to the abbey. I must have gone fast, and yet it seemed to me as if my feet were weighted with lead, and as though every joint in my body were rusty. When I got almost to the top I could see the seat and the white figure, for I was now close enough to distinguish it even through the spells of shadow. There was undoubtedly something, long and black, bending over the half-reclining white figure. I called in fright, “Lucy! Lucy!” and something raised a head, and from where I was I could see a white face and red, gleaming eyes. Lucy did not answer, and I ran on to the entrance of the churchyard. As I entered, the church was between me and the seat, and for a minute or so I lost sight of her. When I came in view again the cloud had passed, and the moonlight struck so brilliantly that I could see Lucy half reclining with her head lying over the back of the seat. She was quite alone, and there was not a sign of any living thing about.\n\nWhen I bent over her I could see that she was still asleep. Her lips were parted, and she was breathing--not softly as usual with her, but in long, heavy gasps, as though striving to get her lungs full at every breath. As I came close, she put up her hand in her sleep and pulled the collar of her nightdress close around her throat. Whilst she did so there came a little shudder through her, as though she felt the cold. I flung the warm shawl over her, and drew the edges tight round her neck, for I dreaded lest she should get some deadly chill from the night air, unclad as she was. I feared to wake her all at once, so, in order to have my hands free that I might help her, I fastened the shawl at her throat with a big safety-pin; but I must have been clumsy in my anxiety and pinched or pricked her with it, for by-and-by, when her breathing became quieter, she put her hand to her throat again and moaned. When I had her carefully wrapped up I put my shoes on her feet and then began very gently to wake her. At first she did not respond; but gradually she became more and more uneasy in her sleep, moaning and sighing occasionally. At last, as time was passing fast, and, for many other reasons, I wished to get her home at once, I shook her more forcibly, till finally she opened her eyes and awoke. She did not seem surprised to see me, as, of course, she did not realise all at once where she was. Lucy always wakes prettily, and even at such a time, when her body must have been chilled with cold, and her mind somewhat appalled at waking unclad in a churchyard at night, she did not lose her grace. She trembled a little, and clung to me; when I told her to come at once with me home she rose without a word, with the obedience of a child. As we passed along, the gravel hurt my feet, and Lucy noticed me wince. She stopped and wanted to insist upon my taking my shoes; but I would not. However, when we got to the pathway outside the churchyard, where there was a puddle of water, remaining from the storm, I daubed my feet with mud, using each foot in turn on the other, so that as we went home, no one, in case we should meet any one, should notice my bare feet.\n\nFortune favoured us, and we got home without meeting a soul. Once we saw a man, who seemed not quite sober, passing along a street in front of us; but we hid in a door till he had disappeared up an opening such as there are here, steep little closes, or “wynds,” as they call them in Scotland. My heart beat so loud all the time that sometimes I thought I should faint. I was filled with anxiety about Lucy, not only for her health, lest she should suffer from the exposure, but for her reputation in case the story should get wind. When we got in, and had washed our feet, and had said a prayer of thankfulness together, I tucked her into bed. Before falling asleep she asked--even implored--me not to say a word to any one, even her mother, about her sleep-walking adventure. I hesitated at first to promise; but on thinking of the state of her mother’s health, and how the knowledge of such a thing would fret her, and thinking, too, of how such a story might become distorted--nay, infallibly would--in case it should leak out, I thought it wiser to do so. I hope I did right. I have locked the door, and the key is tied to my wrist, so perhaps I shall not be again disturbed. Lucy is sleeping soundly; the reflex of the dawn is high and far over the sea....\n\nSame day, noon.--All goes well. Lucy slept till I woke her and seemed not to have even changed her side. The adventure of the night does not seem to have harmed her; on the contrary, it has benefited her, for she looks better this morning than she has done for weeks. I was sorry to notice that my clumsiness with the safety-pin hurt her. Indeed, it might have been serious, for the skin of her throat was pierced. I must have pinched up a piece of loose skin and have transfixed it, for there are two little red points like pin-pricks, and on the band of her nightdress was a drop of blood. When I apologised and was concerned about it, she laughed and petted me, and said she did not even feel it. Fortunately it cannot leave a scar, as it is so tiny.\n\nSame day, night.--We passed a happy day. The air was clear, and the sun bright, and there was a cool breeze. We took our lunch to Mulgrave Woods, Mrs. Westenra driving by the road and Lucy and I walking by the cliff-path and joining her at the gate. I felt a little sad myself, for I could not but feel how absolutely happy it would have been had Jonathan been with me. But there! I must only be patient. In the evening we strolled in the Casino Terrace, and heard some good music by Spohr and Mackenzie, and went to bed early. Lucy seems more restful than she has been for some time, and fell asleep at once. I shall lock the door and secure the key the same as before, though I do not expect any trouble to-night.\n\n12 August.--My expectations were wrong, for twice during the night I was wakened by Lucy trying to get out. She seemed, even in her sleep, to be a little impatient at finding the door shut, and went back to bed under a sort of protest. I woke with the dawn, and heard the birds chirping outside of the window. Lucy woke, too, and, I was glad to see, was even better than on the previous morning. All her old gaiety of manner seemed to have come back, and she came and snuggled in beside me and told me all about Arthur. I told her how anxious I was about Jonathan, and then she tried to comfort me. Well, she succeeded somewhat, for, though sympathy can’t alter facts, it can help to make them more bearable.\n\n13 August.--Another quiet day, and to bed with the key on my wrist as before. Again I awoke in the night, and found Lucy sitting up in bed, still asleep, pointing to the window. I got up quietly, and pulling aside the blind, looked out. It was brilliant moonlight, and the soft effect of the light over the sea and sky--merged together in one great, silent mystery--was beautiful beyond words. Between me and the moonlight flitted a great bat, coming and going in great whirling circles. Once or twice it came quite close, but was, I suppose, frightened at seeing me, and flitted away across the harbour towards the abbey. When I came back from the window Lucy had lain down again, and was sleeping peacefully. She did not stir again all night.\n\n14 August.--On the East Cliff, reading and writing all day. Lucy seems to have become as much in love with the spot as I am, and it is hard to get her away from it when it is time to come home for lunch or tea or dinner. This afternoon she made a funny remark. We were coming home for dinner, and had come to the top of the steps up from the West Pier and stopped to look at the view, as we generally do. The setting sun, low down in the sky, was just dropping behind Kettleness; the red light was thrown over on the East Cliff and the old abbey, and seemed to bathe everything in a beautiful rosy glow. We were silent for a while, and suddenly Lucy murmured as if to herself:--\n\n“His red eyes again! They are just the same.” It was such an odd expression, coming apropos of nothing, that it quite startled me. I slewed round a little, so as to see Lucy well without seeming to stare at her, and saw that she was in a half-dreamy state, with an odd look on her face that I could not quite make out; so I said nothing, but followed her eyes. She appeared to be looking over at our own seat, whereon was a dark figure seated alone. I was a little startled myself, for it seemed for an instant as if the stranger had great eyes like burning flames; but a second look dispelled the illusion. The red sunlight was shining on the windows of St. Mary’s Church behind our seat, and as the sun dipped there was just sufficient change in the refraction and reflection to make it appear as if the light moved. I called Lucy’s attention to the peculiar effect, and she became herself with a start, but she looked sad all the same; it may have been that she was thinking of that terrible night up there. We never refer to it; so I said nothing, and we went home to dinner. Lucy had a headache and went early to bed. I saw her asleep, and went out for a little stroll myself; I walked along the cliffs to the westward, and was full of sweet sadness, for I was thinking of Jonathan. When coming home--it was then bright moonlight, so bright that, though the front of our part of the Crescent was in shadow, everything could be well seen--I threw a glance up at our window, and saw Lucy’s head leaning out. I thought that perhaps she was looking out for me, so I opened my handkerchief and waved it. She did not notice or make any movement whatever. Just then, the moonlight crept round an angle of the building, and the light fell on the window. There distinctly was Lucy with her head lying up against the side of the window-sill and her eyes shut. She was fast asleep, and by her, seated on the window-sill, was something that looked like a good-sized bird. I was afraid she might get a chill, so I ran upstairs, but as I came into the room she was moving back to her bed, fast asleep, and breathing heavily; she was holding her hand to her throat, as though to protect it from cold.\n\nI did not wake her, but tucked her up warmly; I have taken care that the door is locked and the window securely fastened.\n\nShe looks so sweet as she sleeps; but she is paler than is her wont, and there is a drawn, haggard look under her eyes which I do not like. I fear she is fretting about something. I wish I could find out what it is.\n\n15 August.--Rose later than usual. Lucy was languid and tired, and slept on after we had been called. We had a happy surprise at breakfast. Arthur’s father is better, and wants the marriage to come off soon. Lucy is full of quiet joy, and her mother is glad and sorry at once. Later on in the day she told me the cause. She is grieved to lose Lucy as her very own, but she is rejoiced that she is soon to have some one to protect her. Poor dear, sweet lady! She confided to me that she has got her death-warrant. She has not told Lucy, and made me promise secrecy; her doctor told her that within a few months, at most, she must die, for her heart is weakening. At any time, even now, a sudden shock would be almost sure to kill her. Ah, we were wise to keep from her the affair of the dreadful night of Lucy’s sleep-walking.\n\n17 August.--No diary for two whole days. I have not had the heart to write. Some sort of shadowy pall seems to be coming over our happiness. No news from Jonathan, and Lucy seems to be growing weaker, whilst her mother’s hours are numbering to a close. I do not understand Lucy’s fading away as she is doing. She eats well and sleeps well, and enjoys the fresh air; but all the time the roses in her cheeks are fading, and she gets weaker and more languid day by day; at night I hear her gasping as if for air. I keep the key of our door always fastened to my wrist at night, but she gets up and walks about the room, and sits at the open window. Last night I found her leaning out when I woke up, and when I tried to wake her I could not; she was in a faint. When I managed to restore her she was as weak as water, and cried silently between long, painful struggles for breath. When I asked her how she came to be at the window she shook her head and turned away. I trust her feeling ill may not be from that unlucky prick of the safety-pin. I looked at her throat just now as she lay asleep, and the tiny wounds seem not to have healed. They are still open, and, if anything, larger than before, and the edges of them are faintly white. They are like little white dots with red centres. Unless they heal within a day or two, I shall insist on the doctor seeing about them.\n\nLetter, Samuel F. Billington & Son, Solicitors, Whitby, to Messrs. Carter, Paterson & Co., London.\n\n“17 August.\n\n“Dear Sirs,--\n\n“Herewith please receive invoice of goods sent by Great Northern Railway. Same are to be delivered at Carfax, near Purfleet, immediately on receipt at goods station King’s Cross. The house is at present empty, but enclosed please find keys, all of which are labelled.\n\n“You will please deposit the boxes, fifty in number, which form the consignment, in the partially ruined building forming part of the house and marked ‘A’ on rough diagram enclosed. Your agent will easily recognise the locality, as it is the ancient chapel of the mansion. The goods leave by the train at 9:30 to-night, and will be due at King’s Cross at 4:30 to-morrow afternoon. As our client wishes the delivery made as soon as possible, we shall be obliged by your having teams ready at King’s Cross at the time named and forthwith conveying the goods to destination. In order to obviate any delays possible through any routine requirements as to payment in your departments, we enclose cheque herewith for ten pounds (£10), receipt of which please acknowledge. Should the charge be less than this amount, you can return balance; if greater, we shall at once send cheque for difference on hearing from you. You are to leave the keys on coming away in the main hall of the house, where the proprietor may get them on his entering the house by means of his duplicate key.\n\n“Pray do not take us as exceeding the bounds of business courtesy in pressing you in all ways to use the utmost expedition.\n\n_“We are, dear Sirs,\n\n“Faithfully yours,\n\nLetter, Messrs. Carter, Paterson & Co., London, to Messrs. Billington & Son, Whitby.\n\n“21 August.\n\n“Dear Sirs,--\n\n“We beg to acknowledge £10 received and to return cheque £1 17s. 9d, amount of overplus, as shown in receipted account herewith. Goods are delivered in exact accordance with instructions, and keys left in parcel in main hall, as directed.\n\n“We are, dear Sirs,\n\n“Yours respectfully.\n\nMina Murray’s Journal.\n\n18 August.--I am happy to-day, and write sitting on the seat in the churchyard. Lucy is ever so much better. Last night she slept well all night, and did not disturb me once. The roses seem coming back already to her cheeks, though she is still sadly pale and wan-looking. If she were in any way anæmic I could understand it, but she is not. She is in gay spirits and full of life and cheerfulness. All the morbid reticence seems to have passed from her, and she has just reminded me, as if I needed any reminding, of that night, and that it was here, on this very seat, I found her asleep. As she told me she tapped playfully with the heel of her boot on the stone slab and said:--\n\n“My poor little feet didn’t make much noise then! I daresay poor old Mr. Swales would have told me that it was because I didn’t want to wake up Geordie.” As she was in such a communicative humour, I asked her if she had dreamed at all that night. Before she answered, that sweet, puckered look came into her forehead, which Arthur--I call him Arthur from her habit--says he loves; and, indeed, I don’t wonder that he does. Then she went on in a half-dreaming kind of way, as if trying to recall it to herself:--\n\n“I didn’t quite dream; but it all seemed to be real. I only wanted to be here in this spot--I don’t know why, for I was afraid of something--I don’t know what. I remember, though I suppose I was asleep, passing through the streets and over the bridge. A fish leaped as I went by, and I leaned over to look at it, and I heard a lot of dogs howling--the whole town seemed as if it must be full of dogs all howling at once--as I went up the steps. Then I had a vague memory of something long and dark with red eyes, just as we saw in the sunset, and something very sweet and very bitter all around me at once; and then I seemed sinking into deep green water, and there was a singing in my ears, as I have heard there is to drowning men; and then everything seemed passing away from me; my soul seemed to go out from my body and float about the air. I seem to remember that once the West Lighthouse was right under me, and then there was a sort of agonising feeling, as if I were in an earthquake, and I came back and found you shaking my body. I saw you do it before I felt you.”\n\nThen she began to laugh. It seemed a little uncanny to me, and I listened to her breathlessly. I did not quite like it, and thought it better not to keep her mind on the subject, so we drifted on to other subjects, and Lucy was like her old self again. When we got home the fresh breeze had braced her up, and her pale cheeks were really more rosy. Her mother rejoiced when she saw her, and we all spent a very happy evening together.\n\n19 August.--Joy, joy, joy! although not all joy. At last, news of Jonathan. The dear fellow has been ill; that is why he did not write. I am not afraid to think it or say it, now that I know. Mr. Hawkins sent me on the letter, and wrote himself, oh, so kindly. I am to leave in the morning and go over to Jonathan, and to help to nurse him if necessary, and to bring him home. Mr. Hawkins says it would not be a bad thing if we were to be married out there. I have cried over the good Sister’s letter till I can feel it wet against my bosom, where it lies. It is of Jonathan, and must be next my heart, for he is in my heart. My journey is all mapped out, and my luggage ready. I am only taking one change of dress; Lucy will bring my trunk to London and keep it till I send for it, for it may be that ... I must write no more; I must keep it to say to Jonathan, my husband. The letter that he has seen and touched must comfort me till we meet.\n\nLetter, Sister Agatha, Hospital of St. Joseph and Ste. Mary, Buda-Pesth, to Miss Wilhelmina Murray.\n\n“12 August.\n\n“Dear Madam,--\n\n“I write by desire of Mr. Jonathan Harker, who is himself not strong enough to write, though progressing well, thanks to God and St. Joseph and Ste. Mary. He has been under our care for nearly six weeks, suffering from a violent brain fever. He wishes me to convey his love, and to say that by this post I write for him to Mr. Peter Hawkins, Exeter, to say, with his dutiful respects, that he is sorry for his delay, and that all of his work is completed. He will require some few weeks’ rest in our sanatorium in the hills, but will then return. He wishes me to say that he has not sufficient money with him, and that he would like to pay for his staying here, so that others who need shall not be wanting for help.\n\n“Believe me,\n\n“Yours, with sympathy and all blessings,\n\n“P. S.--My patient being asleep, I open this to let you know something more. He has told me all about you, and that you are shortly to be his wife. All blessings to you both! He has had some fearful shock--so says our doctor--and in his delirium his ravings have been dreadful; of wolves and poison and blood; of ghosts and demons; and I fear to say of what. Be careful with him always that there may be nothing to excite him of this kind for a long time to come; the traces of such an illness as his do not lightly die away. We should have written long ago, but we knew nothing of his friends, and there was on him nothing that any one could understand. He came in the train from Klausenburg, and the guard was told by the station-master there that he rushed into the station shouting for a ticket for home. Seeing from his violent demeanour that he was English, they gave him a ticket for the furthest station on the way thither that the train reached.\n\n“Be assured that he is well cared for. He has won all hearts by his sweetness and gentleness. He is truly getting on well, and I have no doubt will in a few weeks be all himself. But be careful of him for safety’s sake. There are, I pray God and St. Joseph and Ste. Mary, many, many, happy years for you both.”\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n19 August.--Strange and sudden change in Renfield last night. About eight o’clock he began to get excited and sniff about as a dog does when setting. The attendant was struck by his manner, and knowing my interest in him, encouraged him to talk. He is usually respectful to the attendant and at times servile; but to-night, the man tells me, he was quite haughty. Would not condescend to talk with him at all. All he would say was:--\n\n“I don’t want to talk to you: you don’t count now; the Master is at hand.”\n\nThe attendant thinks it is some sudden form of religious mania which has seized him. If so, we must look out for squalls, for a strong man with homicidal and religious mania at once might be dangerous. The combination is a dreadful one. At nine o’clock I visited him myself. His attitude to me was the same as that to the attendant; in his sublime self-feeling the difference between myself and attendant seemed to him as nothing. It looks like religious mania, and he will soon think that he himself is God. These infinitesimal distinctions between man and man are too paltry for an Omnipotent Being. How these madmen give themselves away! The real God taketh heed lest a sparrow fall; but the God created from human vanity sees no difference between an eagle and a sparrow. Oh, if men only knew!\n\nFor half an hour or more Renfield kept getting excited in greater and greater degree. I did not pretend to be watching him, but I kept strict observation all the same. All at once that shifty look came into his eyes which we always see when a madman has seized an idea, and with it the shifty movement of the head and back which asylum attendants come to know so well. He became quite quiet, and went and sat on the edge of his bed resignedly, and looked into space with lack-lustre eyes. I thought I would find out if his apathy were real or only assumed, and tried to lead him to talk of his pets, a theme which had never failed to excite his attention. At first he made no reply, but at length said testily:--\n\n“Bother them all! I don’t care a pin about them.”\n\n“What?” I said. “You don’t mean to tell me you don’t care about spiders?” (Spiders at present are his hobby and the note-book is filling up with columns of small figures.) To this he answered enigmatically:--\n\n“The bride-maidens rejoice the eyes that wait the coming of the bride; but when the bride draweth nigh, then the maidens shine not to the eyes that are filled.”\n\nHe would not explain himself, but remained obstinately seated on his bed all the time I remained with him.\n\nI am weary to-night and low in spirits. I cannot but think of Lucy, and how different things might have been. If I don’t sleep at once, chloral, the modern Morpheus--C{2}HCl{3}O. H_{2}O! I must be careful not to let it grow into a habit. No, I shall take none to-night! I have thought of Lucy, and I shall not dishonour her by mixing the two. If need be, to-night shall be sleepless....\n\nLater.--Glad I made the resolution; gladder that I kept to it. I had lain tossing about, and had heard the clock strike only twice, when the night-watchman came to me, sent up from the ward, to say that Renfield had escaped. I threw on my clothes and ran down at once; my patient is too dangerous a person to be roaming about. Those ideas of his might work out dangerously with strangers. The attendant was waiting for me. He said he had seen him not ten minutes before, seemingly asleep in his bed, when he had looked through the observation-trap in the door. His attention was called by the sound of the window being wrenched out. He ran back and saw his feet disappear through the window, and had at once sent up for me. He was only in his night-gear, and cannot be far off. The attendant thought it would be more useful to watch where he should go than to follow him, as he might lose sight of him whilst getting out of the building by the door. He is a bulky man, and couldn’t get through the window. I am thin, so, with his aid, I got out, but feet foremost, and, as we were only a few feet above ground, landed unhurt. The attendant told me the patient had gone to the left, and had taken a straight line, so I ran as quickly as I could. As I got through the belt of trees I saw a white figure scale the high wall which separates our grounds from those of the deserted house.\n\nI ran back at once, told the watchman to get three or four men immediately and follow me into the grounds of Carfax, in case our friend might be dangerous. I got a ladder myself, and crossing the wall, dropped down on the other side. I could see Renfield’s figure just disappearing behind the angle of the house, so I ran after him. On the far side of the house I found him pressed close against the old ironbound oak door of the chapel. He was talking, apparently to some one, but I was afraid to go near enough to hear what he was saying, lest I might frighten him, and he should run off. Chasing an errant swarm of bees is nothing to following a naked lunatic, when the fit of escaping is upon him! After a few minutes, however, I could see that he did not take note of anything around him, and so ventured to draw nearer to him--the more so as my men had now crossed the wall and were closing him in. I heard him say:--\n\n“I am here to do Your bidding, Master. I am Your slave, and You will reward me, for I shall be faithful. I have worshipped You long and afar off. Now that You are near, I await Your commands, and You will not pass me by, will You, dear Master, in Your distribution of good things?”\n\nHe is a selfish old beggar anyhow. He thinks of the loaves and fishes even when he believes he is in a Real Presence. His manias make a startling combination. When we closed in on him he fought like a tiger. He is immensely strong, for he was more like a wild beast than a man. I never saw a lunatic in such a paroxysm of rage before; and I hope I shall not again. It is a mercy that we have found out his strength and his danger in good time. With strength and determination like his, he might have done wild work before he was caged. He is safe now at any rate. Jack Sheppard himself couldn’t get free from the strait-waistcoat that keeps him restrained, and he’s chained to the wall in the padded room. His cries are at times awful, but the silences that follow are more deadly still, for he means murder in every turn and movement.\n\nJust now he spoke coherent words for the first time:--\n\n“I shall be patient, Master. It is coming--coming--coming!”\n\nSo I took the hint, and came too. I was too excited to sleep, but this diary has quieted me, and I feel I shall get some sleep to-night.\n\n“Buda-Pesth, 24 August.\n\n“My dearest Lucy,--\n\n“I know you will be anxious to hear all that has happened since we parted at the railway station at Whitby. Well, my dear, I got to Hull all right, and caught the boat to Hamburg, and then the train on here. I feel that I can hardly recall anything of the journey, except that I knew I was coming to Jonathan, and, that as I should have to do some nursing, I had better get all the sleep I could.... I found my dear one, oh, so thin and pale and weak-looking. All the resolution has gone out of his dear eyes, and that quiet dignity which I told you was in his face has vanished. He is only a wreck of himself, and he does not remember anything that has happened to him for a long time past. At least, he wants me to believe so, and I shall never ask. He has had some terrible shock, and I fear it might tax his poor brain if he were to try to recall it. Sister Agatha, who is a good creature and a born nurse, tells me that he raved of dreadful things whilst he was off his head. I wanted her to tell me what they were; but she would only cross herself, and say she would never tell; that the ravings of the sick were the secrets of God, and that if a nurse through her vocation should hear them, she should respect her trust. She is a sweet, good soul, and the next day, when she saw I was troubled, she opened up the subject again, and after saying that she could never mention what my poor dear raved about, added: ‘I can tell you this much, my dear: that it was not about anything which he has done wrong himself; and you, as his wife to be, have no cause to be concerned. He has not forgotten you or what he owes to you. His fear was of great and terrible things, which no mortal can treat of.’ I do believe the dear soul thought I might be jealous lest my poor dear should have fallen in love with any other girl. The idea of my being jealous about Jonathan! And yet, my dear, let me whisper, I felt a thrill of joy through me when I knew that no other woman was a cause of trouble. I am now sitting by his bedside, where I can see his face while he sleeps. He is waking!...\n\n“When he woke he asked me for his coat, as he wanted to get something from the pocket; I asked Sister Agatha, and she brought all his things. I saw that amongst them was his note-book, and was going to ask him to let me look at it--for I knew then that I might find some clue to his trouble--but I suppose he must have seen my wish in my eyes, for he sent me over to the window, saying he wanted to be quite alone for a moment. Then he called me back, and when I came he had his hand over the note-book, and he said to me very solemnly:--\n\n“‘Wilhelmina’--I knew then that he was in deadly earnest, for he has never called me by that name since he asked me to marry him--‘you know, dear, my ideas of the trust between husband and wife: there should be no secret, no concealment. I have had a great shock, and when I try to think of what it is I feel my head spin round, and I do not know if it was all real or the dreaming of a madman. You know I have had brain fever, and that is to be mad. The secret is here, and I do not want to know it. I want to take up my life here, with our marriage.’ For, my dear, we had decided to be married as soon as the formalities are complete. ‘Are you willing, Wilhelmina, to share my ignorance? Here is the book. Take it and keep it, read it if you will, but never let me know; unless, indeed, some solemn duty should come upon me to go back to the bitter hours, asleep or awake, sane or mad, recorded here.’ He fell back exhausted, and I put the book under his pillow, and kissed him. I have asked Sister Agatha to beg the Superior to let our wedding be this afternoon, and am waiting her reply....\n\n“She has come and told me that the chaplain of the English mission church has been sent for. We are to be married in an hour, or as soon after as Jonathan awakes....\n\n“Lucy, the time has come and gone. I feel very solemn, but very, very happy. Jonathan woke a little after the hour, and all was ready, and he sat up in bed, propped up with pillows. He answered his ‘I will’ firmly and strongly. I could hardly speak; my heart was so full that even those words seemed to choke me. The dear sisters were so kind. Please God, I shall never, never forget them, nor the grave and sweet responsibilities I have taken upon me. I must tell you of my wedding present. When the chaplain and the sisters had left me alone with my husband--oh, Lucy, it is the first time I have written the words ‘my husband’--left me alone with my husband, I took the book from under his pillow, and wrapped it up in white paper, and tied it with a little bit of pale blue ribbon which was round my neck, and sealed it over the knot with sealing-wax, and for my seal I used my wedding ring. Then I kissed it and showed it to my husband, and told him that I would keep it so, and then it would be an outward and visible sign for us all our lives that we trusted each other; that I would never open it unless it were for his own dear sake or for the sake of some stern duty. Then he took my hand in his, and oh, Lucy, it was the first time he took his wife’s hand, and said that it was the dearest thing in all the wide world, and that he would go through all the past again to win it, if need be. The poor dear meant to have said a part of the past, but he cannot think of time yet, and I shall not wonder if at first he mixes up not only the month, but the year.\n\n“Well, my dear, what could I say? I could only tell him that I was the happiest woman in all the wide world, and that I had nothing to give him except myself, my life, and my trust, and that with these went my love and duty for all the days of my life. And, my dear, when he kissed me, and drew me to him with his poor weak hands, it was like a very solemn pledge between us....\n\n“Lucy dear, do you know why I tell you all this? It is not only because it is all sweet to me, but because you have been, and are, very dear to me. It was my privilege to be your friend and guide when you came from the schoolroom to prepare for the world of life. I want you to see now, and with the eyes of a very happy wife, whither duty has led me; so that in your own married life you too may be all happy as I am. My dear, please Almighty God, your life may be all it promises: a long day of sunshine, with no harsh wind, no forgetting duty, no distrust. I must not wish you no pain, for that can never be; but I do hope you will be always as happy as I am now. Good-bye, my dear. I shall post this at once, and, perhaps, write you very soon again. I must stop, for Jonathan is waking--I must attend to my husband!\n\n“Your ever-loving\n\n“Whitby, 30 August.\n\n“My dearest Mina,--\n\n“Oceans of love and millions of kisses, and may you soon be in your own home with your husband. I wish you could be coming home soon enough to stay with us here. The strong air would soon restore Jonathan; it has quite restored me. I have an appetite like a cormorant, am full of life, and sleep well. You will be glad to know that I have quite given up walking in my sleep. I think I have not stirred out of my bed for a week, that is when I once got into it at night. Arthur says I am getting fat. By the way, I forgot to tell you that Arthur is here. We have such walks and drives, and rides, and rowing, and tennis, and fishing together; and I love him more than ever. He tells me that he loves me more, but I doubt that, for at first he told me that he couldn’t love me more than he did then. But this is nonsense. There he is, calling to me. So no more just at present from your loving\n\n“P. S.--Mother sends her love. She seems better, poor dear. “P. P. S.--We are to be married on 28 September.”\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n20 August.--The case of Renfield grows even more interesting. He has now so far quieted that there are spells of cessation from his passion. For the first week after his attack he was perpetually violent. Then one night, just as the moon rose, he grew quiet, and kept murmuring to himself: “Now I can wait; now I can wait.” The attendant came to tell me, so I ran down at once to have a look at him. He was still in the strait-waistcoat and in the padded room, but the suffused look had gone from his face, and his eyes had something of their old pleading--I might almost say, “cringing”--softness. I was satisfied with his present condition, and directed him to be relieved. The attendants hesitated, but finally carried out my wishes without protest. It was a strange thing that the patient had humour enough to see their distrust, for, coming close to me, he said in a whisper, all the while looking furtively at them:--\n\n“They think I could hurt you! Fancy me hurting you! The fools!”\n\nIt was soothing, somehow, to the feelings to find myself dissociated even in the mind of this poor madman from the others; but all the same I do not follow his thought. Am I to take it that I have anything in common with him, so that we are, as it were, to stand together; or has he to gain from me some good so stupendous that my well-being is needful to him? I must find out later on. To-night he will not speak. Even the offer of a kitten or even a full-grown cat will not tempt him. He will only say: “I don’t take any stock in cats. I have more to think of now, and I can wait; I can wait.”\n\nAfter a while I left him. The attendant tells me that he was quiet until just before dawn, and that then he began to get uneasy, and at length violent, until at last he fell into a paroxysm which exhausted him so that he swooned into a sort of coma.\n\n... Three nights has the same thing happened--violent all day then quiet from moonrise to sunrise. I wish I could get some clue to the cause. It would almost seem as if there was some influence which came and went. Happy thought! We shall to-night play sane wits against mad ones. He escaped before without our help; to-night he shall escape with it. We shall give him a chance, and have the men ready to follow in case they are required....\n\n23 August.--“The unexpected always happens.” How well Disraeli knew life. Our bird when he found the cage open would not fly, so all our subtle arrangements were for nought. At any rate, we have proved one thing; that the spells of quietness last a reasonable time. We shall in future be able to ease his bonds for a few hours each day. I have given orders to the night attendant merely to shut him in the padded room, when once he is quiet, until an hour before sunrise. The poor soul’s body will enjoy the relief even if his mind cannot appreciate it. Hark! The unexpected again! I am called; the patient has once more escaped.\n\nLater.--Another night adventure. Renfield artfully waited until the attendant was entering the room to inspect. Then he dashed out past him and flew down the passage. I sent word for the attendants to follow. Again he went into the grounds of the deserted house, and we found him in the same place, pressed against the old chapel door. When he saw me he became furious, and had not the attendants seized him in time, he would have tried to kill me. As we were holding him a strange thing happened. He suddenly redoubled his efforts, and then as suddenly grew calm. I looked round instinctively, but could see nothing. Then I caught the patient’s eye and followed it, but could trace nothing as it looked into the moonlit sky except a big bat, which was flapping its silent and ghostly way to the west. Bats usually wheel and flit about, but this one seemed to go straight on, as if it knew where it was bound for or had some intention of its own. The patient grew calmer every instant, and presently said:--\n\n“You needn’t tie me; I shall go quietly!” Without trouble we came back to the house. I feel there is something ominous in his calm, and shall not forget this night....\n\nLucy Westenra’s Diary\n\nHillingham, 24 August.--I must imitate Mina, and keep writing things down. Then we can have long talks when we do meet. I wonder when it will be. I wish she were with me again, for I feel so unhappy. Last night I seemed to be dreaming again just as I was at Whitby. Perhaps it is the change of air, or getting home again. It is all dark and horrid to me, for I can remember nothing; but I am full of vague fear, and I feel so weak and worn out. When Arthur came to lunch he looked quite grieved when he saw me, and I hadn’t the spirit to try to be cheerful. I wonder if I could sleep in mother’s room to-night. I shall make an excuse and try.\n\n25 August.--Another bad night. Mother did not seem to take to my proposal. She seems not too well herself, and doubtless she fears to worry me. I tried to keep awake, and succeeded for a while; but when the clock struck twelve it waked me from a doze, so I must have been falling asleep. There was a sort of scratching or flapping at the window, but I did not mind it, and as I remember no more, I suppose I must then have fallen asleep. More bad dreams. I wish I could remember them. This morning I am horribly weak. My face is ghastly pale, and my throat pains me. It must be something wrong with my lungs, for I don’t seem ever to get air enough. I shall try to cheer up when Arthur comes, or else I know he will be miserable to see me so.\n\n“Albemarle Hotel, 31 August.\n\n“My dear Jack,--\n\n“I want you to do me a favour. Lucy is ill; that is, she has no special disease, but she looks awful, and is getting worse every day. I have asked her if there is any cause; I do not dare to ask her mother, for to disturb the poor lady’s mind about her daughter in her present state of health would be fatal. Mrs. Westenra has confided to me that her doom is spoken--disease of the heart--though poor Lucy does not know it yet. I am sure that there is something preying on my dear girl’s mind. I am almost distracted when I think of her; to look at her gives me a pang. I told her I should ask you to see her, and though she demurred at first--I know why, old fellow--she finally consented. It will be a painful task for you, I know, old friend, but it is for her sake, and I must not hesitate to ask, or you to act. You are to come to lunch at Hillingham to-morrow, two o’clock, so as not to arouse any suspicion in Mrs. Westenra, and after lunch Lucy will take an opportunity of being alone with you. I shall come in for tea, and we can go away together; I am filled with anxiety, and want to consult with you alone as soon as I can after you have seen her. Do not fail!\n\nTelegram, Arthur Holmwood to Seward.\n\n“1 September.\n\n“Am summoned to see my father, who is worse. Am writing. Write me fully by to-night’s post to Ring. Wire me if necessary.”\n\n“2 September.\n\n“My dear old fellow,--\n\n“With regard to Miss Westenra’s health I hasten to let you know at once that in my opinion there is not any functional disturbance or any malady that I know of. At the same time, I am not by any means satisfied with her appearance; she is woefully different from what she was when I saw her last. Of course you must bear in mind that I did not have full opportunity of examination such as I should wish; our very friendship makes a little difficulty which not even medical science or custom can bridge over. I had better tell you exactly what happened, leaving you to draw, in a measure, your own conclusions. I shall then say what I have done and propose doing.\n\n“I found Miss Westenra in seemingly gay spirits. Her mother was present, and in a few seconds I made up my mind that she was trying all she knew to mislead her mother and prevent her from being anxious. I have no doubt she guesses, if she does not know, what need of caution there is. We lunched alone, and as we all exerted ourselves to be cheerful, we got, as some kind of reward for our labours, some real cheerfulness amongst us. Then Mrs. Westenra went to lie down, and Lucy was left with me. We went into her boudoir, and till we got there her gaiety remained, for the servants were coming and going. As soon as the door was closed, however, the mask fell from her face, and she sank down into a chair with a great sigh, and hid her eyes with her hand. When I saw that her high spirits had failed, I at once took advantage of her reaction to make a diagnosis. She said to me very sweetly:--\n\n“‘I cannot tell you how I loathe talking about myself.’ I reminded her that a doctor’s confidence was sacred, but that you were grievously anxious about her. She caught on to my meaning at once, and settled that matter in a word. ‘Tell Arthur everything you choose. I do not care for myself, but all for him!’ So I am quite free.\n\n“I could easily see that she is somewhat bloodless, but I could not see the usual anæmic signs, and by a chance I was actually able to test the quality of her blood, for in opening a window which was stiff a cord gave way, and she cut her hand slightly with broken glass. It was a slight matter in itself, but it gave me an evident chance, and I secured a few drops of the blood and have analysed them. The qualitative analysis gives a quite normal condition, and shows, I should infer, in itself a vigorous state of health. In other physical matters I was quite satisfied that there is no need for anxiety; but as there must be a cause somewhere, I have come to the conclusion that it must be something mental. She complains of difficulty in breathing satisfactorily at times, and of heavy, lethargic sleep, with dreams that frighten her, but regarding which she can remember nothing. She says that as a child she used to walk in her sleep, and that when in Whitby the habit came back, and that once she walked out in the night and went to East Cliff, where Miss Murray found her; but she assures me that of late the habit has not returned. I am in doubt, and so have done the best thing I know of; I have written to my old friend and master, Professor Van Helsing, of Amsterdam, who knows as much about obscure diseases as any one in the world. I have asked him to come over, and as you told me that all things were to be at your charge, I have mentioned to him who you are and your relations to Miss Westenra. This, my dear fellow, is in obedience to your wishes, for I am only too proud and happy to do anything I can for her. Van Helsing would, I know, do anything for me for a personal reason, so, no matter on what ground he comes, we must accept his wishes. He is a seemingly arbitrary man, but this is because he knows what he is talking about better than any one else. He is a philosopher and a metaphysician, and one of the most advanced scientists of his day; and he has, I believe, an absolutely open mind. This, with an iron nerve, a temper of the ice-brook, an indomitable resolution, self-command, and toleration exalted from virtues to blessings, and the kindliest and truest heart that beats--these form his equipment for the noble work that he is doing for mankind--work both in theory and practice, for his views are as wide as his all-embracing sympathy. I tell you these facts that you may know why I have such confidence in him. I have asked him to come at once. I shall see Miss Westenra to-morrow again. She is to meet me at the Stores, so that I may not alarm her mother by too early a repetition of my call.\n\n“Yours always,\n\n“2 September.\n\n“My good Friend,--\n\n“When I have received your letter I am already coming to you. By good fortune I can leave just at once, without wrong to any of those who have trusted me. Were fortune other, then it were bad for those who have trusted, for I come to my friend when he call me to aid those he holds dear. Tell your friend that when that time you suck from my wound so swiftly the poison of the gangrene from that knife that our other friend, too nervous, let slip, you did more for him when he wants my aids and you call for them than all his great fortune could do. But it is pleasure added to do for him, your friend; it is to you that I come. Have then rooms for me at the Great Eastern Hotel, so that I may be near to hand, and please it so arrange that we may see the young lady not too late on to-morrow, for it is likely that I may have to return here that night. But if need be I shall come again in three days, and stay longer if it must. Till then good-bye, my friend John.\n\n“3 September.\n\n“My dear Art,--\n\n“Van Helsing has come and gone. He came on with me to Hillingham, and found that, by Lucy’s discretion, her mother was lunching out, so that we were alone with her. Van Helsing made a very careful examination of the patient. He is to report to me, and I shall advise you, for of course I was not present all the time. He is, I fear, much concerned, but says he must think. When I told him of our friendship and how you trust to me in the matter, he said: ‘You must tell him all you think. Tell him what I think, if you can guess it, if you will. Nay, I am not jesting. This is no jest, but life and death, perhaps more.’ I asked what he meant by that, for he was very serious. This was when we had come back to town, and he was having a cup of tea before starting on his return to Amsterdam. He would not give me any further clue. You must not be angry with me, Art, because his very reticence means that all his brains are working for her good. He will speak plainly enough when the time comes, be sure. So I told him I would simply write an account of our visit, just as if I were doing a descriptive special article for The Daily Telegraph. He seemed not to notice, but remarked that the smuts in London were not quite so bad as they used to be when he was a student here. I am to get his report to-morrow if he can possibly make it. In any case I am to have a letter.\n\n“Well, as to the visit. Lucy was more cheerful than on the day I first saw her, and certainly looked better. She had lost something of the ghastly look that so upset you, and her breathing was normal. She was very sweet to the professor (as she always is), and tried to make him feel at ease; though I could see that the poor girl was making a hard struggle for it. I believe Van Helsing saw it, too, for I saw the quick look under his bushy brows that I knew of old. Then he began to chat of all things except ourselves and diseases and with such an infinite geniality that I could see poor Lucy’s pretense of animation merge into reality. Then, without any seeming change, he brought the conversation gently round to his visit, and suavely said:--\n\n“‘My dear young miss, I have the so great pleasure because you are so much beloved. That is much, my dear, ever were there that which I do not see. They told me you were down in the spirit, and that you were of a ghastly pale. To them I say: “Pouf!”’ And he snapped his fingers at me and went on: ‘But you and I shall show them how wrong they are. How can he’--and he pointed at me with the same look and gesture as that with which once he pointed me out to his class, on, or rather after, a particular occasion which he never fails to remind me of--‘know anything of a young ladies? He has his madmans to play with, and to bring them back to happiness, and to those that love them. It is much to do, and, oh, but there are rewards, in that we can bestow such happiness. But the young ladies! He has no wife nor daughter, and the young do not tell themselves to the young, but to the old, like me, who have known so many sorrows and the causes of them. So, my dear, we will send him away to smoke the cigarette in the garden, whiles you and I have little talk all to ourselves.’ I took the hint, and strolled about, and presently the professor came to the window and called me in. He looked grave, but said: ‘I have made careful examination, but there is no functional cause. With you I agree that there has been much blood lost; it has been, but is not. But the conditions of her are in no way anæmic. I have asked her to send me her maid, that I may ask just one or two question, that so I may not chance to miss nothing. I know well what she will say. And yet there is cause; there is always cause for everything. I must go back home and think. You must send to me the telegram every day; and if there be cause I shall come again. The disease--for not to be all well is a disease--interest me, and the sweet young dear, she interest me too. She charm me, and for her, if not for you or disease, I come.’\n\n“As I tell you, he would not say a word more, even when we were alone. And so now, Art, you know all I know. I shall keep stern watch. I trust your poor father is rallying. It must be a terrible thing to you, my dear old fellow, to be placed in such a position between two people who are both so dear to you. I know your idea of duty to your father, and you are right to stick to it; but, if need be, I shall send you word to come at once to Lucy; so do not be over-anxious unless you hear from me.”\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n4 September.--Zoöphagous patient still keeps up our interest in him. He had only one outburst and that was yesterday at an unusual time. Just before the stroke of noon he began to grow restless. The attendant knew the symptoms, and at once summoned aid. Fortunately the men came at a run, and were just in time, for at the stroke of noon he became so violent that it took all their strength to hold him. In about five minutes, however, he began to get more and more quiet, and finally sank into a sort of melancholy, in which state he has remained up to now. The attendant tells me that his screams whilst in the paroxysm were really appalling; I found my hands full when I got in, attending to some of the other patients who were frightened by him. Indeed, I can quite understand the effect, for the sounds disturbed even me, though I was some distance away. It is now after the dinner-hour of the asylum, and as yet my patient sits in a corner brooding, with a dull, sullen, woe-begone look in his face, which seems rather to indicate than to show something directly. I cannot quite understand it.\n\nLater.--Another change in my patient. At five o’clock I looked in on him, and found him seemingly as happy and contented as he used to be. He was catching flies and eating them, and was keeping note of his capture by making nail-marks on the edge of the door between the ridges of padding. When he saw me, he came over and apologised for his bad conduct, and asked me in a very humble, cringing way to be led back to his own room and to have his note-book again. I thought it well to humour him: so he is back in his room with the window open. He has the sugar of his tea spread out on the window-sill, and is reaping quite a harvest of flies. He is not now eating them, but putting them into a box, as of old, and is already examining the corners of his room to find a spider. I tried to get him to talk about the past few days, for any clue to his thoughts would be of immense help to me; but he would not rise. For a moment or two he looked very sad, and said in a sort of far-away voice, as though saying it rather to himself than to me:--\n\n“All over! all over! He has deserted me. No hope for me now unless I do it for myself!” Then suddenly turning to me in a resolute way, he said: “Doctor, won’t you be very good to me and let me have a little more sugar? I think it would be good for me.”\n\n“And the flies?” I said.\n\n“Yes! The flies like it, too, and I like the flies; therefore I like it.” And there are people who know so little as to think that madmen do not argue. I procured him a double supply, and left him as happy a man as, I suppose, any in the world. I wish I could fathom his mind.\n\nMidnight.--Another change in him. I had been to see Miss Westenra, whom I found much better, and had just returned, and was standing at our own gate looking at the sunset, when once more I heard him yelling. As his room is on this side of the house, I could hear it better than in the morning. It was a shock to me to turn from the wonderful smoky beauty of a sunset over London, with its lurid lights and inky shadows and all the marvellous tints that come on foul clouds even as on foul water, and to realise all the grim sternness of my own cold stone building, with its wealth of breathing misery, and my own desolate heart to endure it all. I reached him just as the sun was going down, and from his window saw the red disc sink. As it sank he became less and less frenzied; and just as it dipped he slid from the hands that held him, an inert mass, on the floor. It is wonderful, however, what intellectual recuperative power lunatics have, for within a few minutes he stood up quite calmly and looked around him. I signalled to the attendants not to hold him, for I was anxious to see what he would do. He went straight over to the window and brushed out the crumbs of sugar; then he took his fly-box, and emptied it outside, and threw away the box; then he shut the window, and crossing over, sat down on his bed. All this surprised me, so I asked him: “Are you not going to keep flies any more?”\n\n“No,” said he; “I am sick of all that rubbish!” He certainly is a wonderfully interesting study. I wish I could get some glimpse of his mind or of the cause of his sudden passion. Stop; there may be a clue after all, if we can find why to-day his paroxysms came on at high noon and at sunset. Can it be that there is a malign influence of the sun at periods which affects certain natures--as at times the moon does others? We shall see.\n\nTelegram, Seward, London, to Van Helsing, Amsterdam.\n\n“4 September.--Patient still better to-day.”\n\nTelegram, Seward, London, to Van Helsing, Amsterdam.\n\n“5 September.--Patient greatly improved. Good appetite; sleeps naturally; good spirits; colour coming back.”\n\nTelegram, Seward, London, to Van Helsing, Amsterdam.\n\n“6 September.--Terrible change for the worse. Come at once; do not lose an hour. I hold over telegram to Holmwood till have seen you.”\n\n“6 September.\n\n“My dear Art,--\n\n“My news to-day is not so good. Lucy this morning had gone back a bit. There is, however, one good thing which has arisen from it; Mrs. Westenra was naturally anxious concerning Lucy, and has consulted me professionally about her. I took advantage of the opportunity, and told her that my old master, Van Helsing, the great specialist, was coming to stay with me, and that I would put her in his charge conjointly with myself; so now we can come and go without alarming her unduly, for a shock to her would mean sudden death, and this, in Lucy’s weak condition, might be disastrous to her. We are hedged in with difficulties, all of us, my poor old fellow; but, please God, we shall come through them all right. If any need I shall write, so that, if you do not hear from me, take it for granted that I am simply waiting for news. In haste\n\n“Yours ever,\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n7 September.--The first thing Van Helsing said to me when we met at Liverpool Street was:--\n\n“Have you said anything to our young friend the lover of her?”\n\n“No,” I said. “I waited till I had seen you, as I said in my telegram. I wrote him a letter simply telling him that you were coming, as Miss Westenra was not so well, and that I should let him know if need be.”\n\n“Right, my friend,” he said, “quite right! Better he not know as yet; perhaps he shall never know. I pray so; but if it be needed, then he shall know all. And, my good friend John, let me caution you. You deal with the madmen. All men are mad in some way or the other; and inasmuch as you deal discreetly with your madmen, so deal with God’s madmen, too--the rest of the world. You tell not your madmen what you do nor why you do it; you tell them not what you think. So you shall keep knowledge in its place, where it may rest--where it may gather its kind around it and breed. You and I shall keep as yet what we know here, and here.” He touched me on the heart and on the forehead, and then touched himself the same way. “I have for myself thoughts at the present. Later I shall unfold to you.”\n\n“Why not now?” I asked. “It may do some good; we may arrive at some decision.” He stopped and looked at me, and said:--\n\n“My friend John, when the corn is grown, even before it has ripened--while the milk of its mother-earth is in him, and the sunshine has not yet begun to paint him with his gold, the husbandman he pull the ear and rub him between his rough hands, and blow away the green chaff, and say to you: ‘Look! he’s good corn; he will make good crop when the time comes.’” I did not see the application, and told him so. For reply he reached over and took my ear in his hand and pulled it playfully, as he used long ago to do at lectures, and said: “The good husbandman tell you so then because he knows, but not till then. But you do not find the good husbandman dig up his planted corn to see if he grow; that is for the children who play at husbandry, and not for those who take it as of the work of their life. See you now, friend John? I have sown my corn, and Nature has her work to do in making it sprout; if he sprout at all, there’s some promise; and I wait till the ear begins to swell.” He broke off, for he evidently saw that I understood. Then he went on, and very gravely:--\n\n“You were always a careful student, and your case-book was ever more full than the rest. You were only student then; now you are master, and I trust that good habit have not fail. Remember, my friend, that knowledge is stronger than memory, and we should not trust the weaker. Even if you have not kept the good practise, let me tell you that this case of our dear miss is one that may be--mind, I say may be--of such interest to us and others that all the rest may not make him kick the beam, as your peoples say. Take then good note of it. Nothing is too small. I counsel you, put down in record even your doubts and surmises. Hereafter it may be of interest to you to see how true you guess. We learn from failure, not from success!”\n\nWhen I described Lucy’s symptoms--the same as before, but infinitely more marked--he looked very grave, but said nothing. He took with him a bag in which were many instruments and drugs, “the ghastly paraphernalia of our beneficial trade,” as he once called, in one of his lectures, the equipment of a professor of the healing craft. When we were shown in, Mrs. Westenra met us. She was alarmed, but not nearly so much as I expected to find her. Nature in one of her beneficent moods has ordained that even death has some antidote to its own terrors. Here, in a case where any shock may prove fatal, matters are so ordered that, from some cause or other, the things not personal--even the terrible change in her daughter to whom she is so attached--do not seem to reach her. It is something like the way Dame Nature gathers round a foreign body an envelope of some insensitive tissue which can protect from evil that which it would otherwise harm by contact. If this be an ordered selfishness, then we should pause before we condemn any one for the vice of egoism, for there may be deeper root for its causes than we have knowledge of.\n\nI used my knowledge of this phase of spiritual pathology, and laid down a rule that she should not be present with Lucy or think of her illness more than was absolutely required. She assented readily, so readily that I saw again the hand of Nature fighting for life. Van Helsing and I were shown up to Lucy’s room. If I was shocked when I saw her yesterday, I was horrified when I saw her to-day. She was ghastly, chalkily pale; the red seemed to have gone even from her lips and gums, and the bones of her face stood out prominently; her breathing was painful to see or hear. Van Helsing’s face grew set as marble, and his eyebrows converged till they almost touched over his nose. Lucy lay motionless, and did not seem to have strength to speak, so for a while we were all silent. Then Van Helsing beckoned to me, and we went gently out of the room. The instant we had closed the door he stepped quickly along the passage to the next door, which was open. Then he pulled me quickly in with him and closed the door. “My God!” he said; “this is dreadful. There is no time to be lost. She will die for sheer want of blood to keep the heart’s action as it should be. There must be transfusion of blood at once. Is it you or me?”\n\n“I am younger and stronger, Professor. It must be me.”\n\n“Then get ready at once. I will bring up my bag. I am prepared.”\n\nI went downstairs with him, and as we were going there was a knock at the hall-door. When we reached the hall the maid had just opened the door, and Arthur was stepping quickly in. He rushed up to me, saying in an eager whisper:--\n\n“Jack, I was so anxious. I read between the lines of your letter, and have been in an agony. The dad was better, so I ran down here to see for myself. Is not that gentleman Dr. Van Helsing? I am so thankful to you, sir, for coming.” When first the Professor’s eye had lit upon him he had been angry at his interruption at such a time; but now, as he took in his stalwart proportions and recognised the strong young manhood which seemed to emanate from him, his eyes gleamed. Without a pause he said to him gravely as he held out his hand:--\n\n“Sir, you have come in time. You are the lover of our dear miss. She is bad, very, very bad. Nay, my child, do not go like that.” For he suddenly grew pale and sat down in a chair almost fainting. “You are to help her. You can do more than any that live, and your courage is your best help.”\n\n“What can I do?” asked Arthur hoarsely. “Tell me, and I shall do it. My life is hers, and I would give the last drop of blood in my body for her.” The Professor has a strongly humorous side, and I could from old knowledge detect a trace of its origin in his answer:--\n\n“My young sir, I do not ask so much as that--not the last!”\n\n“What shall I do?” There was fire in his eyes, and his open nostril quivered with intent. Van Helsing slapped him on the shoulder. “Come!” he said. “You are a man, and it is a man we want. You are better than me, better than my friend John.” Arthur looked bewildered, and the Professor went on by explaining in a kindly way:--\n\n“Young miss is bad, very bad. She wants blood, and blood she must have or die. My friend John and I have consulted; and we are about to perform what we call transfusion of blood--to transfer from full veins of one to the empty veins which pine for him. John was to give his blood, as he is the more young and strong than me”--here Arthur took my hand and wrung it hard in silence--“but, now you are here, you are more good than us, old or young, who toil much in the world of thought. Our nerves are not so calm and our blood not so bright than yours!” Arthur turned to him and said:--\n\n“If you only knew how gladly I would die for her you would understand----”\n\nHe stopped, with a sort of choke in his voice.\n\n“Good boy!” said Van Helsing. “In the not-so-far-off you will be happy that you have done all for her you love. Come now and be silent. You shall kiss her once before it is done, but then you must go; and you must leave at my sign. Say no word to Madame; you know how it is with her! There must be no shock; any knowledge of this would be one. Come!”\n\nWe all went up to Lucy’s room. Arthur by direction remained outside. Lucy turned her head and looked at us, but said nothing. She was not asleep, but she was simply too weak to make the effort. Her eyes spoke to us; that was all. Van Helsing took some things from his bag and laid them on a little table out of sight. Then he mixed a narcotic, and coming over to the bed, said cheerily:--\n\n“Now, little miss, here is your medicine. Drink it off, like a good child. See, I lift you so that to swallow is easy. Yes.” She had made the effort with success.\n\nIt astonished me how long the drug took to act. This, in fact, marked the extent of her weakness. The time seemed endless until sleep began to flicker in her eyelids. At last, however, the narcotic began to manifest its potency; and she fell into a deep sleep. When the Professor was satisfied he called Arthur into the room, and bade him strip off his coat. Then he added: “You may take that one little kiss whiles I bring over the table. Friend John, help to me!” So neither of us looked whilst he bent over her.\n\nVan Helsing turning to me, said:\n\n“He is so young and strong and of blood so pure that we need not defibrinate it.”\n\nThen with swiftness, but with absolute method, Van Helsing performed the operation. As the transfusion went on something like life seemed to come back to poor Lucy’s cheeks, and through Arthur’s growing pallor the joy of his face seemed absolutely to shine. After a bit I began to grow anxious, for the loss of blood was telling on Arthur, strong man as he was. It gave me an idea of what a terrible strain Lucy’s system must have undergone that what weakened Arthur only partially restored her. But the Professor’s face was set, and he stood watch in hand and with his eyes fixed now on the patient and now on Arthur. I could hear my own heart beat. Presently he said in a soft voice: “Do not stir an instant. It is enough. You attend him; I will look to her.” When all was over I could see how much Arthur was weakened. I dressed the wound and took his arm to bring him away, when Van Helsing spoke without turning round--the man seems to have eyes in the back of his head:--\n\n“The brave lover, I think, deserve another kiss, which he shall have presently.” And as he had now finished his operation, he adjusted the pillow to the patient’s head. As he did so the narrow black velvet band which she seems always to wear round her throat, buckled with an old diamond buckle which her lover had given her, was dragged a little up, and showed a red mark on her throat. Arthur did not notice it, but I could hear the deep hiss of indrawn breath which is one of Van Helsing’s ways of betraying emotion. He said nothing at the moment, but turned to me, saying: “Now take down our brave young lover, give him of the port wine, and let him lie down a while. He must then go home and rest, sleep much and eat much, that he may be recruited of what he has so given to his love. He must not stay here. Hold! a moment. I may take it, sir, that you are anxious of result. Then bring it with you that in all ways the operation is successful. You have saved her life this time, and you can go home and rest easy in mind that all that can be is. I shall tell her all when she is well; she shall love you none the less for what you have done. Good-bye.”\n\nWhen Arthur had gone I went back to the room. Lucy was sleeping gently, but her breathing was stronger; I could see the counterpane move as her breast heaved. By the bedside sat Van Helsing, looking at her intently. The velvet band again covered the red mark. I asked the Professor in a whisper:--\n\n“What do you make of that mark on her throat?”\n\n“What do you make of it?”\n\n“I have not examined it yet,” I answered, and then and there proceeded to loose the band. Just over the external jugular vein there were two punctures, not large, but not wholesome-looking. There was no sign of disease, but the edges were white and worn-looking, as if by some trituration. It at once occurred to me that this wound, or whatever it was, might be the means of that manifest loss of blood; but I abandoned the idea as soon as formed, for such a thing could not be. The whole bed would have been drenched to a scarlet with the blood which the girl must have lost to leave such a pallor as she had before the transfusion.\n\n“Well?” said Van Helsing.\n\n“Well,” said I, “I can make nothing of it.” The Professor stood up. “I must go back to Amsterdam to-night,” he said. “There are books and things there which I want. You must remain here all the night, and you must not let your sight pass from her.”\n\n“Shall I have a nurse?” I asked.\n\n“We are the best nurses, you and I. You keep watch all night; see that she is well fed, and that nothing disturbs her. You must not sleep all the night. Later on we can sleep, you and I. I shall be back as soon as possible. And then we may begin.”\n\n“May begin?” I said. “What on earth do you mean?”\n\n“We shall see!” he answered, as he hurried out. He came back a moment later and put his head inside the door and said with warning finger held up:--\n\n“Remember, she is your charge. If you leave her, and harm befall, you shall not sleep easy hereafter!”\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary--continued.\n\n8 September.--I sat up all night with Lucy. The opiate worked itself off towards dusk, and she waked naturally; she looked a different being from what she had been before the operation. Her spirits even were good, and she was full of a happy vivacity, but I could see evidences of the absolute prostration which she had undergone. When I told Mrs. Westenra that Dr. Van Helsing had directed that I should sit up with her she almost pooh-poohed the idea, pointing out her daughter’s renewed strength and excellent spirits. I was firm, however, and made preparations for my long vigil. When her maid had prepared her for the night I came in, having in the meantime had supper, and took a seat by the bedside. She did not in any way make objection, but looked at me gratefully whenever I caught her eye. After a long spell she seemed sinking off to sleep, but with an effort seemed to pull herself together and shook it off. This was repeated several times, with greater effort and with shorter pauses as the time moved on. It was apparent that she did not want to sleep, so I tackled the subject at once:--\n\n“You do not want to go to sleep?”\n\n“No; I am afraid.”\n\n“Afraid to go to sleep! Why so? It is the boon we all crave for.”\n\n“Ah, not if you were like me--if sleep was to you a presage of horror!”\n\n“A presage of horror! What on earth do you mean?”\n\n“I don’t know; oh, I don’t know. And that is what is so terrible. All this weakness comes to me in sleep; until I dread the very thought.”\n\n“But, my dear girl, you may sleep to-night. I am here watching you, and I can promise that nothing will happen.”\n\n“Ah, I can trust you!” I seized the opportunity, and said: “I promise you that if I see any evidence of bad dreams I will wake you at once.”\n\n“You will? Oh, will you really? How good you are to me. Then I will sleep!” And almost at the word she gave a deep sigh of relief, and sank back, asleep.\n\nAll night long I watched by her. She never stirred, but slept on and on in a deep, tranquil, life-giving, health-giving sleep. Her lips were slightly parted, and her breast rose and fell with the regularity of a pendulum. There was a smile on her face, and it was evident that no bad dreams had come to disturb her peace of mind.\n\nIn the early morning her maid came, and I left her in her care and took myself back home, for I was anxious about many things. I sent a short wire to Van Helsing and to Arthur, telling them of the excellent result of the operation. My own work, with its manifold arrears, took me all day to clear off; it was dark when I was able to inquire about my zoöphagous patient. The report was good; he had been quite quiet for the past day and night. A telegram came from Van Helsing at Amsterdam whilst I was at dinner, suggesting that I should be at Hillingham to-night, as it might be well to be at hand, and stating that he was leaving by the night mail and would join me early in the morning.\n\n9 September.--I was pretty tired and worn out when I got to Hillingham. For two nights I had hardly had a wink of sleep, and my brain was beginning to feel that numbness which marks cerebral exhaustion. Lucy was up and in cheerful spirits. When she shook hands with me she looked sharply in my face and said:--\n\n“No sitting up to-night for you. You are worn out. I am quite well again; indeed, I am; and if there is to be any sitting up, it is I who will sit up with you.” I would not argue the point, but went and had my supper. Lucy came with me, and, enlivened by her charming presence, I made an excellent meal, and had a couple of glasses of the more than excellent port. Then Lucy took me upstairs, and showed me a room next her own, where a cozy fire was burning. “Now,” she said, “you must stay here. I shall leave this door open and my door too. You can lie on the sofa for I know that nothing would induce any of you doctors to go to bed whilst there is a patient above the horizon. If I want anything I shall call out, and you can come to me at once.” I could not but acquiesce, for I was “dog-tired,” and could not have sat up had I tried. So, on her renewing her promise to call me if she should want anything, I lay on the sofa, and forgot all about everything.\n\nLucy Westenra’s Diary.\n\n9 September.--I feel so happy to-night. I have been so miserably weak, that to be able to think and move about is like feeling sunshine after a long spell of east wind out of a steel sky. Somehow Arthur feels very, very close to me. I seem to feel his presence warm about me. I suppose it is that sickness and weakness are selfish things and turn our inner eyes and sympathy on ourselves, whilst health and strength give Love rein, and in thought and feeling he can wander where he wills. I know where my thoughts are. If Arthur only knew! My dear, my dear, your ears must tingle as you sleep, as mine do waking. Oh, the blissful rest of last night! How I slept, with that dear, good Dr. Seward watching me. And to-night I shall not fear to sleep, since he is close at hand and within call. Thank everybody for being so good to me! Thank God! Good-night, Arthur.\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n10 September.--I was conscious of the Professor’s hand on my head, and started awake all in a second. That is one of the things that we learn in an asylum, at any rate.\n\n“And how is our patient?”\n\n“Well, when I left her, or rather when she left me,” I answered.\n\n“Come, let us see,” he said. And together we went into the room.\n\nThe blind was down, and I went over to raise it gently, whilst Van Helsing stepped, with his soft, cat-like tread, over to the bed.\n\nAs I raised the blind, and the morning sunlight flooded the room, I heard the Professor’s low hiss of inspiration, and knowing its rarity, a deadly fear shot through my heart. As I passed over he moved back, and his exclamation of horror, “Gott in Himmel!” needed no enforcement from his agonised face. He raised his hand and pointed to the bed, and his iron face was drawn and ashen white. I felt my knees begin to tremble.\n\nThere on the bed, seemingly in a swoon, lay poor Lucy, more horribly white and wan-looking than ever. Even the lips were white, and the gums seemed to have shrunken back from the teeth, as we sometimes see in a corpse after a prolonged illness. Van Helsing raised his foot to stamp in anger, but the instinct of his life and all the long years of habit stood to him, and he put it down again softly. “Quick!” he said. “Bring the brandy.” I flew to the dining-room, and returned with the decanter. He wetted the poor white lips with it, and together we rubbed palm and wrist and heart. He felt her heart, and after a few moments of agonising suspense said:--\n\n“It is not too late. It beats, though but feebly. All our work is undone; we must begin again. There is no young Arthur here now; I have to call on you yourself this time, friend John.” As he spoke, he was dipping into his bag and producing the instruments for transfusion; I had taken off my coat and rolled up my shirt-sleeve. There was no possibility of an opiate just at present, and no need of one; and so, without a moment’s delay, we began the operation. After a time--it did not seem a short time either, for the draining away of one’s blood, no matter how willingly it be given, is a terrible feeling--Van Helsing held up a warning finger. “Do not stir,” he said, “but I fear that with growing strength she may wake; and that would make danger, oh, so much danger. But I shall precaution take. I shall give hypodermic injection of morphia.” He proceeded then, swiftly and deftly, to carry out his intent. The effect on Lucy was not bad, for the faint seemed to merge subtly into the narcotic sleep. It was with a feeling of personal pride that I could see a faint tinge of colour steal back into the pallid cheeks and lips. No man knows, till he experiences it, what it is to feel his own life-blood drawn away into the veins of the woman he loves.\n\nThe Professor watched me critically. “That will do,” he said. “Already?” I remonstrated. “You took a great deal more from Art.” To which he smiled a sad sort of smile as he replied:--\n\n“He is her lover, her fiancé. You have work, much work, to do for her and for others; and the present will suffice.”\n\nWhen we stopped the operation, he attended to Lucy, whilst I applied digital pressure to my own incision. I laid down, whilst I waited his leisure to attend to me, for I felt faint and a little sick. By-and-by he bound up my wound, and sent me downstairs to get a glass of wine for myself. As I was leaving the room, he came after me, and half whispered:--\n\n“Mind, nothing must be said of this. If our young lover should turn up unexpected, as before, no word to him. It would at once frighten him and enjealous him, too. There must be none. So!”\n\nWhen I came back he looked at me carefully, and then said:--\n\n“You are not much the worse. Go into the room, and lie on your sofa, and rest awhile; then have much breakfast, and come here to me.”\n\nI followed out his orders, for I knew how right and wise they were. I had done my part, and now my next duty was to keep up my strength. I felt very weak, and in the weakness lost something of the amazement at what had occurred. I fell asleep on the sofa, however, wondering over and over again how Lucy had made such a retrograde movement, and how she could have been drained of so much blood with no sign anywhere to show for it. I think I must have continued my wonder in my dreams, for, sleeping and waking, my thoughts always came back to the little punctures in her throat and the ragged, exhausted appearance of their edges--tiny though they were.\n\nLucy slept well into the day, and when she woke she was fairly well and strong, though not nearly so much so as the day before. When Van Helsing had seen her, he went out for a walk, leaving me in charge, with strict injunctions that I was not to leave her for a moment. I could hear his voice in the hall, asking the way to the nearest telegraph office.\n\nLucy chatted with me freely, and seemed quite unconscious that anything had happened. I tried to keep her amused and interested. When her mother came up to see her, she did not seem to notice any change whatever, but said to me gratefully:--\n\n“We owe you so much, Dr. Seward, for all you have done, but you really must now take care not to overwork yourself. You are looking pale yourself. You want a wife to nurse and look after you a bit; that you do!” As she spoke, Lucy turned crimson, though it was only momentarily, for her poor wasted veins could not stand for long such an unwonted drain to the head. The reaction came in excessive pallor as she turned imploring eyes on me. I smiled and nodded, and laid my finger on my lips; with a sigh, she sank back amid her pillows.\n\nVan Helsing returned in a couple of hours, and presently said to me: “Now you go home, and eat much and drink enough. Make yourself strong. I stay here to-night, and I shall sit up with little miss myself. You and I must watch the case, and we must have none other to know. I have grave reasons. No, do not ask them; think what you will. Do not fear to think even the most not-probable. Good-night.”\n\nIn the hall two of the maids came to me, and asked if they or either of them might not sit up with Miss Lucy. They implored me to let them; and when I said it was Dr. Van Helsing’s wish that either he or I should sit up, they asked me quite piteously to intercede with the “foreign gentleman.” I was much touched by their kindness. Perhaps it is because I am weak at present, and perhaps because it was on Lucy’s account, that their devotion was manifested; for over and over again have I seen similar instances of woman’s kindness. I got back here in time for a late dinner; went my rounds--all well; and set this down whilst waiting for sleep. It is coming.\n\n11 September.--This afternoon I went over to Hillingham. Found Van Helsing in excellent spirits, and Lucy much better. Shortly after I had arrived, a big parcel from abroad came for the Professor. He opened it with much impressment--assumed, of course--and showed a great bundle of white flowers.\n\n“These are for you, Miss Lucy,” he said.\n\n“For me? Oh, Dr. Van Helsing!”\n\n“Yes, my dear, but not for you to play with. These are medicines.” Here Lucy made a wry face. “Nay, but they are not to take in a decoction or in nauseous form, so you need not snub that so charming nose, or I shall point out to my friend Arthur what woes he may have to endure in seeing so much beauty that he so loves so much distort. Aha, my pretty miss, that bring the so nice nose all straight again. This is medicinal, but you do not know how. I put him in your window, I make pretty wreath, and hang him round your neck, so that you sleep well. Oh yes! they, like the lotus flower, make your trouble forgotten. It smell so like the waters of Lethe, and of that fountain of youth that the Conquistadores sought for in the Floridas, and find him all too late.”\n\nWhilst he was speaking, Lucy had been examining the flowers and smelling them. Now she threw them down, saying, with half-laughter, and half-disgust:--\n\n“Oh, Professor, I believe you are only putting up a joke on me. Why, these flowers are only common garlic.”\n\nTo my surprise, Van Helsing rose up and said with all his sternness, his iron jaw set and his bushy eyebrows meeting:--\n\n“No trifling with me! I never jest! There is grim purpose in all I do; and I warn you that you do not thwart me. Take care, for the sake of others if not for your own.” Then seeing poor Lucy scared, as she might well be, he went on more gently: “Oh, little miss, my dear, do not fear me. I only do for your good; but there is much virtue to you in those so common flowers. See, I place them myself in your room. I make myself the wreath that you are to wear. But hush! no telling to others that make so inquisitive questions. We must obey, and silence is a part of obedience; and obedience is to bring you strong and well into loving arms that wait for you. Now sit still awhile. Come with me, friend John, and you shall help me deck the room with my garlic, which is all the way from Haarlem, where my friend Vanderpool raise herb in his glass-houses all the year. I had to telegraph yesterday, or they would not have been here.”\n\nWe went into the room, taking the flowers with us. The Professor’s actions were certainly odd and not to be found in any pharmacopoeia that I ever heard of. First he fastened up the windows and latched them securely; next, taking a handful of the flowers, he rubbed them all over the sashes, as though to ensure that every whiff of air that might get in would be laden with the garlic smell. Then with the wisp he rubbed all over the jamb of the door, above, below, and at each side, and round the fireplace in the same way. It all seemed grotesque to me, and presently I said:--\n\n“Well, Professor, I know you always have a reason for what you do, but this certainly puzzles me. It is well we have no sceptic here, or he would say that you were working some spell to keep out an evil spirit.”\n\n“Perhaps I am!” he answered quietly as he began to make the wreath which Lucy was to wear round her neck.\n\nWe then waited whilst Lucy made her toilet for the night, and when she was in bed he came and himself fixed the wreath of garlic round her neck. The last words he said to her were:--\n\n“Take care you do not disturb it; and even if the room feel close, do not to-night open the window or the door.”\n\n“I promise,” said Lucy, “and thank you both a thousand times for all your kindness to me! Oh, what have I done to be blessed with such friends?”\n\nAs we left the house in my fly, which was waiting, Van Helsing said:--\n\n“To-night I can sleep in peace, and sleep I want--two nights of travel, much reading in the day between, and much anxiety on the day to follow, and a night to sit up, without to wink. To-morrow in the morning early you call for me, and we come together to see our pretty miss, so much more strong for my ‘spell’ which I have work. Ho! ho!”\n\nHe seemed so confident that I, remembering my own confidence two nights before and with the baneful result, felt awe and vague terror. It must have been my weakness that made me hesitate to tell it to my friend, but I felt it all the more, like unshed tears.\n\nLucy Westenra’s Diary.\n\n12 September.--How good they all are to me. I quite love that dear Dr. Van Helsing. I wonder why he was so anxious about these flowers. He positively frightened me, he was so fierce. And yet he must have been right, for I feel comfort from them already. Somehow, I do not dread being alone to-night, and I can go to sleep without fear. I shall not mind any flapping outside the window. Oh, the terrible struggle that I have had against sleep so often of late; the pain of the sleeplessness, or the pain of the fear of sleep, with such unknown horrors as it has for me! How blessed are some people, whose lives have no fears, no dreads; to whom sleep is a blessing that comes nightly, and brings nothing but sweet dreams. Well, here I am to-night, hoping for sleep, and lying like Ophelia in the play, with “virgin crants and maiden strewments.” I never liked garlic before, but to-night it is delightful! There is peace in its smell; I feel sleep coming already. Good-night, everybody.\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n13 September.--Called at the Berkeley and found Van Helsing, as usual, up to time. The carriage ordered from the hotel was waiting. The Professor took his bag, which he always brings with him now.\n\nLet all be put down exactly. Van Helsing and I arrived at Hillingham at eight o’clock. It was a lovely morning; the bright sunshine and all the fresh feeling of early autumn seemed like the completion of nature’s annual work. The leaves were turning to all kinds of beautiful colours, but had not yet begun to drop from the trees. When we entered we met Mrs. Westenra coming out of the morning room. She is always an early riser. She greeted us warmly and said:--\n\n“You will be glad to know that Lucy is better. The dear child is still asleep. I looked into her room and saw her, but did not go in, lest I should disturb her.” The Professor smiled, and looked quite jubilant. He rubbed his hands together, and said:--\n\n“Aha! I thought I had diagnosed the case. My treatment is working,” to which she answered:--\n\n“You must not take all the credit to yourself, doctor. Lucy’s state this morning is due in part to me.”\n\n“How you do mean, ma’am?” asked the Professor.\n\n“Well, I was anxious about the dear child in the night, and went into her room. She was sleeping soundly--so soundly that even my coming did not wake her. But the room was awfully stuffy. There were a lot of those horrible, strong-smelling flowers about everywhere, and she had actually a bunch of them round her neck. I feared that the heavy odour would be too much for the dear child in her weak state, so I took them all away and opened a bit of the window to let in a little fresh air. You will be pleased with her, I am sure.”\n\nShe moved off into her boudoir, where she usually breakfasted early. As she had spoken, I watched the Professor’s face, and saw it turn ashen grey. He had been able to retain his self-command whilst the poor lady was present, for he knew her state and how mischievous a shock would be; he actually smiled on her as he held open the door for her to pass into her room. But the instant she had disappeared he pulled me, suddenly and forcibly, into the dining-room and closed the door.\n\nThen, for the first time in my life, I saw Van Helsing break down. He raised his hands over his head in a sort of mute despair, and then beat his palms together in a helpless way; finally he sat down on a chair, and putting his hands before his face, began to sob, with loud, dry sobs that seemed to come from the very racking of his heart. Then he raised his arms again, as though appealing to the whole universe. “God! God! God!” he said. “What have we done, what has this poor thing done, that we are so sore beset? Is there fate amongst us still, sent down from the pagan world of old, that such things must be, and in such way? This poor mother, all unknowing, and all for the best as she think, does such thing as lose her daughter body and soul; and we must not tell her, we must not even warn her, or she die, and then both die. Oh, how we are beset! How are all the powers of the devils against us!” Suddenly he jumped to his feet. “Come,” he said, “come, we must see and act. Devils or no devils, or all the devils at once, it matters not; we fight him all the same.” He went to the hall-door for his bag; and together we went up to Lucy’s room.\n\nOnce again I drew up the blind, whilst Van Helsing went towards the bed. This time he did not start as he looked on the poor face with the same awful, waxen pallor as before. He wore a look of stern sadness and infinite pity.\n\n“As I expected,” he murmured, with that hissing inspiration of his which meant so much. Without a word he went and locked the door, and then began to set out on the little table the instruments for yet another operation of transfusion of blood. I had long ago recognised the necessity, and begun to take off my coat, but he stopped me with a warning hand. “No!” he said. “To-day you must operate. I shall provide. You are weakened already.” As he spoke he took off his coat and rolled up his shirt-sleeve.\n\nAgain the operation; again the narcotic; again some return of colour to the ashy cheeks, and the regular breathing of healthy sleep. This time I watched whilst Van Helsing recruited himself and rested.\n\nPresently he took an opportunity of telling Mrs. Westenra that she must not remove anything from Lucy’s room without consulting him; that the flowers were of medicinal value, and that the breathing of their odour was a part of the system of cure. Then he took over the care of the case himself, saying that he would watch this night and the next and would send me word when to come.\n\nAfter another hour Lucy waked from her sleep, fresh and bright and seemingly not much the worse for her terrible ordeal.\n\nWhat does it all mean? I am beginning to wonder if my long habit of life amongst the insane is beginning to tell upon my own brain.\n\nLucy Westenra’s Diary.\n\n17 September.--Four days and nights of peace. I am getting so strong again that I hardly know myself. It is as if I had passed through some long nightmare, and had just awakened to see the beautiful sunshine and feel the fresh air of the morning around me. I have a dim half-remembrance of long, anxious times of waiting and fearing; darkness in which there was not even the pain of hope to make present distress more poignant: and then long spells of oblivion, and the rising back to life as a diver coming up through a great press of water. Since, however, Dr. Van Helsing has been with me, all this bad dreaming seems to have passed away; the noises that used to frighten me out of my wits--the flapping against the windows, the distant voices which seemed so close to me, the harsh sounds that came from I know not where and commanded me to do I know not what--have all ceased. I go to bed now without any fear of sleep. I do not even try to keep awake. I have grown quite fond of the garlic, and a boxful arrives for me every day from Haarlem. To-night Dr. Van Helsing is going away, as he has to be for a day in Amsterdam. But I need not be watched; I am well enough to be left alone. Thank God for mother’s sake, and dear Arthur’s, and for all our friends who have been so kind! I shall not even feel the change, for last night Dr. Van Helsing slept in his chair a lot of the time. I found him asleep twice when I awoke; but I did not fear to go to sleep again, although the boughs or bats or something napped almost angrily against the window-panes.\n\n“The Pall Mall Gazette,” 18 September.\n\nInterview with the Keeper in the Zoölogical Gardens.\n\nAfter many inquiries and almost as many refusals, and perpetually using the words “Pall Mall Gazette” as a sort of talisman, I managed to find the keeper of the section of the Zoölogical Gardens in which the wolf department is included. Thomas Bilder lives in one of the cottages in the enclosure behind the elephant-house, and was just sitting down to his tea when I found him. Thomas and his wife are hospitable folk, elderly, and without children, and if the specimen I enjoyed of their hospitality be of the average kind, their lives must be pretty comfortable. The keeper would not enter on what he called “business” until the supper was over, and we were all satisfied. Then when the table was cleared, and he had lit his pipe, he said:--\n\n“Now, sir, you can go on and arsk me what you want. You’ll excoose me refoosin’ to talk of perfeshunal subjects afore meals. I gives the wolves and the jackals and the hyenas in all our section their tea afore I begins to arsk them questions.”\n\n“How do you mean, ask them questions?” I queried, wishful to get him into a talkative humour.\n\n“’Ittin’ of them over the ’ead with a pole is one way; scratchin’ of their hears is another, when gents as is flush wants a bit of a show-orf to their gals. I don’t so much mind the fust--the ’ittin’ with a pole afore I chucks in their dinner; but I waits till they’ve ’ad their sherry and kawffee, so to speak, afore I tries on with the ear-scratchin’. Mind you,” he added philosophically, “there’s a deal of the same nature in us as in them theer animiles. Here’s you a-comin’ and arskin’ of me questions about my business, and I that grumpy-like that only for your bloomin’ ’arf-quid I’d ’a’ seen you blowed fust ’fore I’d answer. Not even when you arsked me sarcastic-like if I’d like you to arsk the Superintendent if you might arsk me questions. Without offence did I tell yer to go to ’ell?”\n\n“You did.”\n\n“An’ when you said you’d report me for usin’ of obscene language that was ’ittin’ me over the ’ead; but the ’arf-quid made that all right. I weren’t a-goin’ to fight, so I waited for the food, and did with my ’owl as the wolves, and lions, and tigers does. But, Lor’ love yer ’art, now that the old ’ooman has stuck a chunk of her tea-cake in me, an’ rinsed me out with her bloomin’ old teapot, and I’ve lit hup, you may scratch my ears for all you’re worth, and won’t git even a growl out of me. Drive along with your questions. I know what yer a-comin’ at, that ’ere escaped wolf.”\n\n“Exactly. I want you to give me your view of it. Just tell me how it happened; and when I know the facts I’ll get you to say what you consider was the cause of it, and how you think the whole affair will end.”\n\n“All right, guv’nor. This ’ere is about the ’ole story. That ’ere wolf what we called Bersicker was one of three grey ones that came from Norway to Jamrach’s, which we bought off him four years ago. He was a nice well-behaved wolf, that never gave no trouble to talk of. I’m more surprised at ’im for wantin’ to get out nor any other animile in the place. But, there, you can’t trust wolves no more nor women.”\n\n“Don’t you mind him, sir!” broke in Mrs. Tom, with a cheery laugh. “’E’s got mindin’ the animiles so long that blest if he ain’t like a old wolf ’isself! But there ain’t no ’arm in ’im.”\n\n“Well, sir, it was about two hours after feedin’ yesterday when I first hear my disturbance. I was makin’ up a litter in the monkey-house for a young puma which is ill; but when I heard the yelpin’ and ’owlin’ I kem away straight. There was Bersicker a-tearin’ like a mad thing at the bars as if he wanted to get out. There wasn’t much people about that day, and close at hand was only one man, a tall, thin chap, with a ’ook nose and a pointed beard, with a few white hairs runnin’ through it. He had a ’ard, cold look and red eyes, and I took a sort of mislike to him, for it seemed as if it was ’im as they was hirritated at. He ’ad white kid gloves on ’is ’ands, and he pointed out the animiles to me and says: ‘Keeper, these wolves seem upset at something.’\n\n“‘Maybe it’s you,’ says I, for I did not like the airs as he give ’isself. He didn’t git angry, as I ’oped he would, but he smiled a kind of insolent smile, with a mouth full of white, sharp teeth. ‘Oh no, they wouldn’t like me,’ ’e says.\n\n“‘Ow yes, they would,’ says I, a-imitatin’ of him. ‘They always likes a bone or two to clean their teeth on about tea-time, which you ’as a bagful.’\n\n“Well, it was a odd thing, but when the animiles see us a-talkin’ they lay down, and when I went over to Bersicker he let me stroke his ears same as ever. That there man kem over, and blessed but if he didn’t put in his hand and stroke the old wolf’s ears too!\n\n“‘Tyke care,’ says I. ‘Bersicker is quick.’\n\n“‘Never mind,’ he says. ‘I’m used to ’em!’\n\n“‘Are you in the business yourself?’ I says, tyking off my ’at, for a man what trades in wolves, anceterer, is a good friend to keepers.\n\n“‘No,’ says he, ‘not exactly in the business, but I ’ave made pets of several.’ And with that he lifts his ’at as perlite as a lord, and walks away. Old Bersicker kep’ a-lookin’ arter ’im till ’e was out of sight, and then went and lay down in a corner and wouldn’t come hout the ’ole hevening. Well, larst night, so soon as the moon was hup, the wolves here all began a-’owling. There warn’t nothing for them to ’owl at. There warn’t no one near, except some one that was evidently a-callin’ a dog somewheres out back of the gardings in the Park road. Once or twice I went out to see that all was right, and it was, and then the ’owling stopped. Just before twelve o’clock I just took a look round afore turnin’ in, an’, bust me, but when I kem opposite to old Bersicker’s cage I see the rails broken and twisted about and the cage empty. And that’s all I know for certing.”\n\n“Did any one else see anything?”\n\n“One of our gard’ners was a-comin’ ’ome about that time from a ’armony, when he sees a big grey dog comin’ out through the garding ’edges. At least, so he says, but I don’t give much for it myself, for if he did ’e never said a word about it to his missis when ’e got ’ome, and it was only after the escape of the wolf was made known, and we had been up all night-a-huntin’ of the Park for Bersicker, that he remembered seein’ anything. My own belief was that the ’armony ’ad got into his ’ead.”\n\n“Now, Mr. Bilder, can you account in any way for the escape of the wolf?”\n\n“Well, sir,” he said, with a suspicious sort of modesty, “I think I can; but I don’t know as ’ow you’d be satisfied with the theory.”\n\n“Certainly I shall. If a man like you, who knows the animals from experience, can’t hazard a good guess at any rate, who is even to try?”\n\n“Well then, sir, I accounts for it this way; it seems to me that ’ere wolf escaped--simply because he wanted to get out.”\n\nFrom the hearty way that both Thomas and his wife laughed at the joke I could see that it had done service before, and that the whole explanation was simply an elaborate sell. I couldn’t cope in badinage with the worthy Thomas, but I thought I knew a surer way to his heart, so I said:--\n\n“Now, Mr. Bilder, we’ll consider that first half-sovereign worked off, and this brother of his is waiting to be claimed when you’ve told me what you think will happen.”\n\n“Right y’are, sir,” he said briskly. “Ye’ll excoose me, I know, for a-chaffin’ of ye, but the old woman here winked at me, which was as much as telling me to go on.”\n\n“Well, I never!” said the old lady.\n\n“My opinion is this: that ’ere wolf is a-’idin’ of, somewheres. The gard’ner wot didn’t remember said he was a-gallopin’ northward faster than a horse could go; but I don’t believe him, for, yer see, sir, wolves don’t gallop no more nor dogs does, they not bein’ built that way. Wolves is fine things in a storybook, and I dessay when they gets in packs and does be chivyin’ somethin’ that’s more afeared than they is they can make a devil of a noise and chop it up, whatever it is. But, Lor’ bless you, in real life a wolf is only a low creature, not half so clever or bold as a good dog; and not half a quarter so much fight in ’im. This one ain’t been used to fightin’ or even to providin’ for hisself, and more like he’s somewhere round the Park a-’idin’ an’ a-shiverin’ of, and, if he thinks at all, wonderin’ where he is to get his breakfast from; or maybe he’s got down some area and is in a coal-cellar. My eye, won’t some cook get a rum start when she sees his green eyes a-shining at her out of the dark! If he can’t get food he’s bound to look for it, and mayhap he may chance to light on a butcher’s shop in time. If he doesn’t, and some nursemaid goes a-walkin’ orf with a soldier, leavin’ of the hinfant in the perambulator--well, then I shouldn’t be surprised if the census is one babby the less. That’s all.”\n\nI was handing him the half-sovereign, when something came bobbing up against the window, and Mr. Bilder’s face doubled its natural length with surprise.\n\n“God bless me!” he said. “If there ain’t old Bersicker come back by ’isself!”\n\nHe went to the door and opened it; a most unnecessary proceeding it seemed to me. I have always thought that a wild animal never looks so well as when some obstacle of pronounced durability is between us; a personal experience has intensified rather than diminished that idea.\n\nAfter all, however, there is nothing like custom, for neither Bilder nor his wife thought any more of the wolf than I should of a dog. The animal itself was as peaceful and well-behaved as that father of all picture-wolves--Red Riding Hood’s quondam friend, whilst moving her confidence in masquerade.\n\nThe whole scene was an unutterable mixture of comedy and pathos. The wicked wolf that for half a day had paralysed London and set all the children in the town shivering in their shoes, was there in a sort of penitent mood, and was received and petted like a sort of vulpine prodigal son. Old Bilder examined him all over with most tender solicitude, and when he had finished with his penitent said:--\n\n“There, I knew the poor old chap would get into some kind of trouble; didn’t I say it all along? Here’s his head all cut and full of broken glass. ’E’s been a-gettin’ over some bloomin’ wall or other. It’s a shyme that people are allowed to top their walls with broken bottles. This ’ere’s what comes of it. Come along, Bersicker.”\n\nHe took the wolf and locked him up in a cage, with a piece of meat that satisfied, in quantity at any rate, the elementary conditions of the fatted calf, and went off to report.\n\nI came off, too, to report the only exclusive information that is given to-day regarding the strange escapade at the Zoo.\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n17 September.--I was engaged after dinner in my study posting up my books, which, through press of other work and the many visits to Lucy, had fallen sadly into arrear. Suddenly the door was burst open, and in rushed my patient, with his face distorted with passion. I was thunderstruck, for such a thing as a patient getting of his own accord into the Superintendent’s study is almost unknown. Without an instant’s pause he made straight at me. He had a dinner-knife in his hand, and, as I saw he was dangerous, I tried to keep the table between us. He was too quick and too strong for me, however; for before I could get my balance he had struck at me and cut my left wrist rather severely. Before he could strike again, however, I got in my right and he was sprawling on his back on the floor. My wrist bled freely, and quite a little pool trickled on to the carpet. I saw that my friend was not intent on further effort, and occupied myself binding up my wrist, keeping a wary eye on the prostrate figure all the time. When the attendants rushed in, and we turned our attention to him, his employment positively sickened me. He was lying on his belly on the floor licking up, like a dog, the blood which had fallen from my wounded wrist. He was easily secured, and, to my surprise, went with the attendants quite placidly, simply repeating over and over again: “The blood is the life! The blood is the life!”\n\nI cannot afford to lose blood just at present; I have lost too much of late for my physical good, and then the prolonged strain of Lucy’s illness and its horrible phases is telling on me. I am over-excited and weary, and I need rest, rest, rest. Happily Van Helsing has not summoned me, so I need not forego my sleep; to-night I could not well do without it.\n\nTelegram, Van Helsing, Antwerp, to Seward, Carfax.\n\n(Sent to Carfax, Sussex, as no county given; delivered late by twenty-two hours.)\n\n“17 September.--Do not fail to be at Hillingham to-night. If not watching all the time frequently, visit and see that flowers are as placed; very important; do not fail. Shall be with you as soon as possible after arrival.”\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n18 September.--Just off for train to London. The arrival of Van Helsing’s telegram filled me with dismay. A whole night lost, and I know by bitter experience what may happen in a night. Of course it is possible that all may be well, but what may have happened? Surely there is some horrible doom hanging over us that every possible accident should thwart us in all we try to do. I shall take this cylinder with me, and then I can complete my entry on Lucy’s phonograph.\n\nMemorandum left by Lucy Westenra.\n\n17 September. Night.--I write this and leave it to be seen, so that no one may by any chance get into trouble through me. This is an exact record of what took place to-night. I feel I am dying of weakness, and have barely strength to write, but it must be done if I die in the doing.\n\nI went to bed as usual, taking care that the flowers were placed as Dr. Van Helsing directed, and soon fell asleep.\n\nI was waked by the flapping at the window, which had begun after that sleep-walking on the cliff at Whitby when Mina saved me, and which now I know so well. I was not afraid, but I did wish that Dr. Seward was in the next room--as Dr. Van Helsing said he would be--so that I might have called him. I tried to go to sleep, but could not. Then there came to me the old fear of sleep, and I determined to keep awake. Perversely sleep would try to come then when I did not want it; so, as I feared to be alone, I opened my door and called out: “Is there anybody there?” There was no answer. I was afraid to wake mother, and so closed my door again. Then outside in the shrubbery I heard a sort of howl like a dog’s, but more fierce and deeper. I went to the window and looked out, but could see nothing, except a big bat, which had evidently been buffeting its wings against the window. So I went back to bed again, but determined not to go to sleep. Presently the door opened, and mother looked in; seeing by my moving that I was not asleep, came in, and sat by me. She said to me even more sweetly and softly than her wont:--\n\n“I was uneasy about you, darling, and came in to see that you were all right.”\n\nI feared she might catch cold sitting there, and asked her to come in and sleep with me, so she came into bed, and lay down beside me; she did not take off her dressing gown, for she said she would only stay a while and then go back to her own bed. As she lay there in my arms, and I in hers, the flapping and buffeting came to the window again. She was startled and a little frightened, and cried out: “What is that?” I tried to pacify her, and at last succeeded, and she lay quiet; but I could hear her poor dear heart still beating terribly. After a while there was the low howl again out in the shrubbery, and shortly after there was a crash at the window, and a lot of broken glass was hurled on the floor. The window blind blew back with the wind that rushed in, and in the aperture of the broken panes there was the head of a great, gaunt grey wolf. Mother cried out in a fright, and struggled up into a sitting posture, and clutched wildly at anything that would help her. Amongst other things, she clutched the wreath of flowers that Dr. Van Helsing insisted on my wearing round my neck, and tore it away from me. For a second or two she sat up, pointing at the wolf, and there was a strange and horrible gurgling in her throat; then she fell over--as if struck with lightning, and her head hit my forehead and made me dizzy for a moment or two. The room and all round seemed to spin round. I kept my eyes fixed on the window, but the wolf drew his head back, and a whole myriad of little specks seemed to come blowing in through the broken window, and wheeling and circling round like the pillar of dust that travellers describe when there is a simoon in the desert. I tried to stir, but there was some spell upon me, and dear mother’s poor body, which seemed to grow cold already--for her dear heart had ceased to beat--weighed me down; and I remembered no more for a while.\n\nThe time did not seem long, but very, very awful, till I recovered consciousness again. Somewhere near, a passing bell was tolling; the dogs all round the neighbourhood were howling; and in our shrubbery, seemingly just outside, a nightingale was singing. I was dazed and stupid with pain and terror and weakness, but the sound of the nightingale seemed like the voice of my dead mother come back to comfort me. The sounds seemed to have awakened the maids, too, for I could hear their bare feet pattering outside my door. I called to them, and they came in, and when they saw what had happened, and what it was that lay over me on the bed, they screamed out. The wind rushed in through the broken window, and the door slammed to. They lifted off the body of my dear mother, and laid her, covered up with a sheet, on the bed after I had got up. They were all so frightened and nervous that I directed them to go to the dining-room and have each a glass of wine. The door flew open for an instant and closed again. The maids shrieked, and then went in a body to the dining-room; and I laid what flowers I had on my dear mother’s breast. When they were there I remembered what Dr. Van Helsing had told me, but I didn’t like to remove them, and, besides, I would have some of the servants to sit up with me now. I was surprised that the maids did not come back. I called them, but got no answer, so I went to the dining-room to look for them.\n\nMy heart sank when I saw what had happened. They all four lay helpless on the floor, breathing heavily. The decanter of sherry was on the table half full, but there was a queer, acrid smell about. I was suspicious, and examined the decanter. It smelt of laudanum, and looking on the sideboard, I found that the bottle which mother’s doctor uses for her--oh! did use--was empty. What am I to do? what am I to do? I am back in the room with mother. I cannot leave her, and I am alone, save for the sleeping servants, whom some one has drugged. Alone with the dead! I dare not go out, for I can hear the low howl of the wolf through the broken window.\n\nThe air seems full of specks, floating and circling in the draught from the window, and the lights burn blue and dim. What am I to do? God shield me from harm this night! I shall hide this paper in my breast, where they shall find it when they come to lay me out. My dear mother gone! It is time that I go too. Good-bye, dear Arthur, if I should not survive this night. God keep you, dear, and God help me!\n\n# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY\n\n18 September.--I drove at once to Hillingham and arrived early. Keeping my cab at the gate, I went up the avenue alone. I knocked gently and rang as quietly as possible, for I feared to disturb Lucy or her mother, and hoped to only bring a servant to the door. After a while, finding no response, I knocked and rang again; still no answer. I cursed the laziness of the servants that they should lie abed at such an hour--for it was now ten o’clock--and so rang and knocked again, but more impatiently, but still without response. Hitherto I had blamed only the servants, but now a terrible fear began to assail me. Was this desolation but another link in the chain of doom which seemed drawing tight around us? Was it indeed a house of death to which I had come, too late? I knew that minutes, even seconds of delay, might mean hours of danger to Lucy, if she had had again one of those frightful relapses; and I went round the house to try if I could find by chance an entry anywhere.\n\nI could find no means of ingress. Every window and door was fastened and locked, and I returned baffled to the porch. As I did so, I heard the rapid pit-pat of a swiftly driven horse’s feet. They stopped at the gate, and a few seconds later I met Van Helsing running up the avenue. When he saw me, he gasped out:--\n\n“Then it was you, and just arrived. How is she? Are we too late? Did you not get my telegram?”\n\nI answered as quickly and coherently as I could that I had only got his telegram early in the morning, and had not lost a minute in coming here, and that I could not make any one in the house hear me. He paused and raised his hat as he said solemnly:--\n\n“Then I fear we are too late. God’s will be done!” With his usual recuperative energy, he went on: “Come. If there be no way open to get in, we must make one. Time is all in all to us now.”\n\nWe went round to the back of the house, where there was a kitchen window. The Professor took a small surgical saw from his case, and handing it to me, pointed to the iron bars which guarded the window. I attacked them at once and had very soon cut through three of them. Then with a long, thin knife we pushed back the fastening of the sashes and opened the window. I helped the Professor in, and followed him. There was no one in the kitchen or in the servants’ rooms, which were close at hand. We tried all the rooms as we went along, and in the dining-room, dimly lit by rays of light through the shutters, found four servant-women lying on the floor. There was no need to think them dead, for their stertorous breathing and the acrid smell of laudanum in the room left no doubt as to their condition. Van Helsing and I looked at each other, and as we moved away he said: “We can attend to them later.” Then we ascended to Lucy’s room. For an instant or two we paused at the door to listen, but there was no sound that we could hear. With white faces and trembling hands, we opened the door gently, and entered the room.\n\nHow shall I describe what we saw? On the bed lay two women, Lucy and her mother. The latter lay farthest in, and she was covered with a white sheet, the edge of which had been blown back by the draught through the broken window, showing the drawn, white face, with a look of terror fixed upon it. By her side lay Lucy, with face white and still more drawn. The flowers which had been round her neck we found upon her mother’s bosom, and her throat was bare, showing the two little wounds which we had noticed before, but looking horribly white and mangled. Without a word the Professor bent over the bed, his head almost touching poor Lucy’s breast; then he gave a quick turn of his head, as of one who listens, and leaping to his feet, he cried out to me:--\n\n“It is not yet too late! Quick! quick! Bring the brandy!”\n\nI flew downstairs and returned with it, taking care to smell and taste it, lest it, too, were drugged like the decanter of sherry which I found on the table. The maids were still breathing, but more restlessly, and I fancied that the narcotic was wearing off. I did not stay to make sure, but returned to Van Helsing. He rubbed the brandy, as on another occasion, on her lips and gums and on her wrists and the palms of her hands. He said to me:--\n\n“I can do this, all that can be at the present. You go wake those maids. Flick them in the face with a wet towel, and flick them hard. Make them get heat and fire and a warm bath. This poor soul is nearly as cold as that beside her. She will need be heated before we can do anything more.”\n\nI went at once, and found little difficulty in waking three of the women. The fourth was only a young girl, and the drug had evidently affected her more strongly, so I lifted her on the sofa and let her sleep. The others were dazed at first, but as remembrance came back to them they cried and sobbed in a hysterical manner. I was stern with them, however, and would not let them talk. I told them that one life was bad enough to lose, and that if they delayed they would sacrifice Miss Lucy. So, sobbing and crying, they went about their way, half clad as they were, and prepared fire and water. Fortunately, the kitchen and boiler fires were still alive, and there was no lack of hot water. We got a bath and carried Lucy out as she was and placed her in it. Whilst we were busy chafing her limbs there was a knock at the hall door. One of the maids ran off, hurried on some more clothes, and opened it. Then she returned and whispered to us that there was a gentleman who had come with a message from Mr. Holmwood. I bade her simply tell him that he must wait, for we could see no one now. She went away with the message, and, engrossed with our work, I clean forgot all about him.\n\nI never saw in all my experience the Professor work in such deadly earnest. I knew--as he knew--that it was a stand-up fight with death, and in a pause told him so. He answered me in a way that I did not understand, but with the sternest look that his face could wear:--\n\n“If that were all, I would stop here where we are now, and let her fade away into peace, for I see no light in life over her horizon.” He went on with his work with, if possible, renewed and more frenzied vigour.\n\nPresently we both began to be conscious that the heat was beginning to be of some effect. Lucy’s heart beat a trifle more audibly to the stethoscope, and her lungs had a perceptible movement. Van Helsing’s face almost beamed, and as we lifted her from the bath and rolled her in a hot sheet to dry her he said to me:--\n\n“The first gain is ours! Check to the King!”\n\nWe took Lucy into another room, which had by now been prepared, and laid her in bed and forced a few drops of brandy down her throat. I noticed that Van Helsing tied a soft silk handkerchief round her throat. She was still unconscious, and was quite as bad as, if not worse than, we had ever seen her.\n\nVan Helsing called in one of the women, and told her to stay with her and not to take her eyes off her till we returned, and then beckoned me out of the room.\n\n“We must consult as to what is to be done,” he said as we descended the stairs. In the hall he opened the dining-room door, and we passed in, he closing the door carefully behind him. The shutters had been opened, but the blinds were already down, with that obedience to the etiquette of death which the British woman of the lower classes always rigidly observes. The room was, therefore, dimly dark. It was, however, light enough for our purposes. Van Helsing’s sternness was somewhat relieved by a look of perplexity. He was evidently torturing his mind about something, so I waited for an instant, and he spoke:--\n\n“What are we to do now? Where are we to turn for help? We must have another transfusion of blood, and that soon, or that poor girl’s life won’t be worth an hour’s purchase. You are exhausted already; I am exhausted too. I fear to trust those women, even if they would have courage to submit. What are we to do for some one who will open his veins for her?”\n\n“What’s the matter with me, anyhow?”\n\nThe voice came from the sofa across the room, and its tones brought relief and joy to my heart, for they were those of Quincey Morris. Van Helsing started angrily at the first sound, but his face softened and a glad look came into his eyes as I cried out: “Quincey Morris!” and rushed towards him with outstretched hands.\n\n“What brought you here?” I cried as our hands met.\n\n“I guess Art is the cause.”\n\nHe handed me a telegram:--\n\n“Have not heard from Seward for three days, and am terribly anxious. Cannot leave. Father still in same condition. Send me word how Lucy is. Do not delay.--HOLMWOOD.”\n\n“I think I came just in the nick of time. You know you have only to tell me what to do.”\n\nVan Helsing strode forward, and took his hand, looking him straight in the eyes as he said:--\n\n“A brave man’s blood is the best thing on this earth when a woman is in trouble. You’re a man and no mistake. Well, the devil may work against us for all he’s worth, but God sends us men when we want them.”\n\nOnce again we went through that ghastly operation. I have not the heart to go through with the details. Lucy had got a terrible shock and it told on her more than before, for though plenty of blood went into her veins, her body did not respond to the treatment as well as on the other occasions. Her struggle back into life was something frightful to see and hear. However, the action of both heart and lungs improved, and Van Helsing made a subcutaneous injection of morphia, as before, and with good effect. Her faint became a profound slumber. The Professor watched whilst I went downstairs with Quincey Morris, and sent one of the maids to pay off one of the cabmen who were waiting. I left Quincey lying down after having a glass of wine, and told the cook to get ready a good breakfast. Then a thought struck me, and I went back to the room where Lucy now was. When I came softly in, I found Van Helsing with a sheet or two of note-paper in his hand. He had evidently read it, and was thinking it over as he sat with his hand to his brow. There was a look of grim satisfaction in his face, as of one who has had a doubt solved. He handed me the paper saying only: “It dropped from Lucy’s breast when we carried her to the bath.”\n\nWhen I had read it, I stood looking at the Professor, and after a pause asked him: “In God’s name, what does it all mean? Was she, or is she, mad; or what sort of horrible danger is it?” I was so bewildered that I did not know what to say more. Van Helsing put out his hand and took the paper, saying:--\n\n“Do not trouble about it now. Forget it for the present. You shall know and understand it all in good time; but it will be later. And now what is it that you came to me to say?” This brought me back to fact, and I was all myself again.\n\n“I came to speak about the certificate of death. If we do not act properly and wisely, there may be an inquest, and that paper would have to be produced. I am in hopes that we need have no inquest, for if we had it would surely kill poor Lucy, if nothing else did. I know, and you know, and the other doctor who attended her knows, that Mrs. Westenra had disease of the heart, and we can certify that she died of it. Let us fill up the certificate at once, and I shall take it myself to the registrar and go on to the undertaker.”\n\n“Good, oh my friend John! Well thought of! Truly Miss Lucy, if she be sad in the foes that beset her, is at least happy in the friends that love her. One, two, three, all open their veins for her, besides one old man. Ah yes, I know, friend John; I am not blind! I love you all the more for it! Now go.”\n\nIn the hall I met Quincey Morris, with a telegram for Arthur telling him that Mrs. Westenra was dead; that Lucy also had been ill, but was now going on better; and that Van Helsing and I were with her. I told him where I was going, and he hurried me out, but as I was going said:--\n\n“When you come back, Jack, may I have two words with you all to ourselves?” I nodded in reply and went out. I found no difficulty about the registration, and arranged with the local undertaker to come up in the evening to measure for the coffin and to make arrangements.\n\nWhen I got back Quincey was waiting for me. I told him I would see him as soon as I knew about Lucy, and went up to her room. She was still sleeping, and the Professor seemingly had not moved from his seat at her side. From his putting his finger to his lips, I gathered that he expected her to wake before long and was afraid of forestalling nature. So I went down to Quincey and took him into the breakfast-room, where the blinds were not drawn down, and which was a little more cheerful, or rather less cheerless, than the other rooms. When we were alone, he said to me:--\n\n“Jack Seward, I don’t want to shove myself in anywhere where I’ve no right to be; but this is no ordinary case. You know I loved that girl and wanted to marry her; but, although that’s all past and gone, I can’t help feeling anxious about her all the same. What is it that’s wrong with her? The Dutchman--and a fine old fellow he is; I can see that--said, that time you two came into the room, that you must have another transfusion of blood, and that both you and he were exhausted. Now I know well that you medical men speak in camera, and that a man must not expect to know what they consult about in private. But this is no common matter, and, whatever it is, I have done my part. Is not that so?”\n\n“That’s so,” I said, and he went on:--\n\n“I take it that both you and Van Helsing had done already what I did to-day. Is not that so?”\n\n“That’s so.”\n\n“And I guess Art was in it too. When I saw him four days ago down at his own place he looked queer. I have not seen anything pulled down so quick since I was on the Pampas and had a mare that I was fond of go to grass all in a night. One of those big bats that they call vampires had got at her in the night, and what with his gorge and the vein left open, there wasn’t enough blood in her to let her stand up, and I had to put a bullet through her as she lay. Jack, if you may tell me without betraying confidence, Arthur was the first, is not that so?” As he spoke the poor fellow looked terribly anxious. He was in a torture of suspense regarding the woman he loved, and his utter ignorance of the terrible mystery which seemed to surround her intensified his pain. His very heart was bleeding, and it took all the manhood of him--and there was a royal lot of it, too--to keep him from breaking down. I paused before answering, for I felt that I must not betray anything which the Professor wished kept secret; but already he knew so much, and guessed so much, that there could be no reason for not answering, so I answered in the same phrase: “That’s so.”\n\n“And how long has this been going on?”\n\n“About ten days.”\n\n“Ten days! Then I guess, Jack Seward, that that poor pretty creature that we all love has had put into her veins within that time the blood of four strong men. Man alive, her whole body wouldn’t hold it.” Then, coming close to me, he spoke in a fierce half-whisper: “What took it out?”\n\nI shook my head. “That,” I said, “is the crux. Van Helsing is simply frantic about it, and I am at my wits’ end. I can’t even hazard a guess. There has been a series of little circumstances which have thrown out all our calculations as to Lucy being properly watched. But these shall not occur again. Here we stay until all be well--or ill.” Quincey held out his hand. “Count me in,” he said. “You and the Dutchman will tell me what to do, and I’ll do it.”\n\nWhen she woke late in the afternoon, Lucy’s first movement was to feel in her breast, and, to my surprise, produced the paper which Van Helsing had given me to read. The careful Professor had replaced it where it had come from, lest on waking she should be alarmed. Her eye then lit on Van Helsing and on me too, and gladdened. Then she looked around the room, and seeing where she was, shuddered; she gave a loud cry, and put her poor thin hands before her pale face. We both understood what that meant--that she had realised to the full her mother’s death; so we tried what we could to comfort her. Doubtless sympathy eased her somewhat, but she was very low in thought and spirit, and wept silently and weakly for a long time. We told her that either or both of us would now remain with her all the time, and that seemed to comfort her. Towards dusk she fell into a doze. Here a very odd thing occurred. Whilst still asleep she took the paper from her breast and tore it in two. Van Helsing stepped over and took the pieces from her. All the same, however, she went on with the action of tearing, as though the material were still in her hands; finally she lifted her hands and opened them as though scattering the fragments. Van Helsing seemed surprised, and his brows gathered as if in thought, but he said nothing.\n\n19 September.--All last night she slept fitfully, being always afraid to sleep, and something weaker when she woke from it. The Professor and I took it in turns to watch, and we never left her for a moment unattended. Quincey Morris said nothing about his intention, but I knew that all night long he patrolled round and round the house.\n\nWhen the day came, its searching light showed the ravages in poor Lucy’s strength. She was hardly able to turn her head, and the little nourishment which she could take seemed to do her no good. At times she slept, and both Van Helsing and I noticed the difference in her, between sleeping and waking. Whilst asleep she looked stronger, although more haggard, and her breathing was softer; her open mouth showed the pale gums drawn back from the teeth, which thus looked positively longer and sharper than usual; when she woke the softness of her eyes evidently changed the expression, for she looked her own self, although a dying one. In the afternoon she asked for Arthur, and we telegraphed for him. Quincey went off to meet him at the station.\n\nWhen he arrived it was nearly six o’clock, and the sun was setting full and warm, and the red light streamed in through the window and gave more colour to the pale cheeks. When he saw her, Arthur was simply choking with emotion, and none of us could speak. In the hours that had passed, the fits of sleep, or the comatose condition that passed for it, had grown more frequent, so that the pauses when conversation was possible were shortened. Arthur’s presence, however, seemed to act as a stimulant; she rallied a little, and spoke to him more brightly than she had done since we arrived. He too pulled himself together, and spoke as cheerily as he could, so that the best was made of everything.\n\nIt was now nearly one o’clock, and he and Van Helsing are sitting with her. I am to relieve them in a quarter of an hour, and I am entering this on Lucy’s phonograph. Until six o’clock they are to try to rest. I fear that to-morrow will end our watching, for the shock has been too great; the poor child cannot rally. God help us all.\n\n(Unopened by her.)\n\n“17 September.\n\n“My dearest Lucy,--\n\n“It seems an age since I heard from you, or indeed since I wrote. You will pardon me, I know, for all my faults when you have read all my budget of news. Well, I got my husband back all right; when we arrived at Exeter there was a carriage waiting for us, and in it, though he had an attack of gout, Mr. Hawkins. He took us to his house, where there were rooms for us all nice and comfortable, and we dined together. After dinner Mr. Hawkins said:--\n\n“‘My dears, I want to drink your health and prosperity; and may every blessing attend you both. I know you both from children, and have, with love and pride, seen you grow up. Now I want you to make your home here with me. I have left to me neither chick nor child; all are gone, and in my will I have left you everything.’ I cried, Lucy dear, as Jonathan and the old man clasped hands. Our evening was a very, very happy one.\n\n“So here we are, installed in this beautiful old house, and from both my bedroom and the drawing-room I can see the great elms of the cathedral close, with their great black stems standing out against the old yellow stone of the cathedral and I can hear the rooks overhead cawing and cawing and chattering and gossiping all day, after the manner of rooks--and humans. I am busy, I need not tell you, arranging things and housekeeping. Jonathan and Mr. Hawkins are busy all day; for, now that Jonathan is a partner, Mr. Hawkins wants to tell him all about the clients.\n\n“How is your dear mother getting on? I wish I could run up to town for a day or two to see you, dear, but I dare not go yet, with so much on my shoulders; and Jonathan wants looking after still. He is beginning to put some flesh on his bones again, but he was terribly weakened by the long illness; even now he sometimes starts out of his sleep in a sudden way and awakes all trembling until I can coax him back to his usual placidity. However, thank God, these occasions grow less frequent as the days go on, and they will in time pass away altogether, I trust. And now I have told you my news, let me ask yours. When are you to be married, and where, and who is to perform the ceremony, and what are you to wear, and is it to be a public or a private wedding? Tell me all about it, dear; tell me all about everything, for there is nothing which interests you which will not be dear to me. Jonathan asks me to send his ‘respectful duty,’ but I do not think that is good enough from the junior partner of the important firm Hawkins & Harker; and so, as you love me, and he loves me, and I love you with all the moods and tenses of the verb, I send you simply his ‘love’ instead. Good-bye, my dearest Lucy, and all blessings on you.\n\n“Yours,\n\nReport from Patrick Hennessey, M. D., M. R. C. S. L. K. Q. C. P. I., etc., etc., to John Seward, M. D.\n\n“20 September.\n\n“My dear Sir,--\n\n“In accordance with your wishes, I enclose report of the conditions of everything left in my charge.... With regard to patient, Renfield, there is more to say. He has had another outbreak, which might have had a dreadful ending, but which, as it fortunately happened, was unattended with any unhappy results. This afternoon a carrier’s cart with two men made a call at the empty house whose grounds abut on ours--the house to which, you will remember, the patient twice ran away. The men stopped at our gate to ask the porter their way, as they were strangers. I was myself looking out of the study window, having a smoke after dinner, and saw one of them come up to the house. As he passed the window of Renfield’s room, the patient began to rate him from within, and called him all the foul names he could lay his tongue to. The man, who seemed a decent fellow enough, contented himself by telling him to “shut up for a foul-mouthed beggar,” whereon our man accused him of robbing him and wanting to murder him and said that he would hinder him if he were to swing for it. I opened the window and signed to the man not to notice, so he contented himself after looking the place over and making up his mind as to what kind of a place he had got to by saying: ‘Lor’ bless yer, sir, I wouldn’t mind what was said to me in a bloomin’ madhouse. I pity ye and the guv’nor for havin’ to live in the house with a wild beast like that.’ Then he asked his way civilly enough, and I told him where the gate of the empty house was; he went away, followed by threats and curses and revilings from our man. I went down to see if I could make out any cause for his anger, since he is usually such a well-behaved man, and except his violent fits nothing of the kind had ever occurred. I found him, to my astonishment, quite composed and most genial in his manner. I tried to get him to talk of the incident, but he blandly asked me questions as to what I meant, and led me to believe that he was completely oblivious of the affair. It was, I am sorry to say, however, only another instance of his cunning, for within half an hour I heard of him again. This time he had broken out through the window of his room, and was running down the avenue. I called to the attendants to follow me, and ran after him, for I feared he was intent on some mischief. My fear was justified when I saw the same cart which had passed before coming down the road, having on it some great wooden boxes. The men were wiping their foreheads, and were flushed in the face, as if with violent exercise. Before I could get up to him the patient rushed at them, and pulling one of them off the cart, began to knock his head against the ground. If I had not seized him just at the moment I believe he would have killed the man there and then. The other fellow jumped down and struck him over the head with the butt-end of his heavy whip. It was a terrible blow; but he did not seem to mind it, but seized him also, and struggled with the three of us, pulling us to and fro as if we were kittens. You know I am no light weight, and the others were both burly men. At first he was silent in his fighting; but as we began to master him, and the attendants were putting a strait-waistcoat on him, he began to shout: ‘I’ll frustrate them! They shan’t rob me! they shan’t murder me by inches! I’ll fight for my Lord and Master!’ and all sorts of similar incoherent ravings. It was with very considerable difficulty that they got him back to the house and put him in the padded room. One of the attendants, Hardy, had a finger broken. However, I set it all right; and he is going on well.\n\n“The two carriers were at first loud in their threats of actions for damages, and promised to rain all the penalties of the law on us. Their threats were, however, mingled with some sort of indirect apology for the defeat of the two of them by a feeble madman. They said that if it had not been for the way their strength had been spent in carrying and raising the heavy boxes to the cart they would have made short work of him. They gave as another reason for their defeat the extraordinary state of drouth to which they had been reduced by the dusty nature of their occupation and the reprehensible distance from the scene of their labours of any place of public entertainment. I quite understood their drift, and after a stiff glass of grog, or rather more of the same, and with each a sovereign in hand, they made light of the attack, and swore that they would encounter a worse madman any day for the pleasure of meeting so ‘bloomin’ good a bloke’ as your correspondent. I took their names and addresses, in case they might be needed. They are as follows:--Jack Smollet, of Dudding’s Rents, King George’s Road, Great Walworth, and Thomas Snelling, Peter Farley’s Row, Guide Court, Bethnal Green. They are both in the employment of Harris & Sons, Moving and Shipment Company, Orange Master’s Yard, Soho.\n\n“I shall report to you any matter of interest occurring here, and shall wire you at once if there is anything of importance.\n\n“Believe me, dear Sir,\n\n“Yours faithfully,\n\n(Unopened by her.)\n\n“18 September.\n\n“My dearest Lucy,--\n\n“Such a sad blow has befallen us. Mr. Hawkins has died very suddenly. Some may not think it so sad for us, but we had both come to so love him that it really seems as though we had lost a father. I never knew either father or mother, so that the dear old man’s death is a real blow to me. Jonathan is greatly distressed. It is not only that he feels sorrow, deep sorrow, for the dear, good man who has befriended him all his life, and now at the end has treated him like his own son and left him a fortune which to people of our modest bringing up is wealth beyond the dream of avarice, but Jonathan feels it on another account. He says the amount of responsibility which it puts upon him makes him nervous. He begins to doubt himself. I try to cheer him up, and my belief in him helps him to have a belief in himself. But it is here that the grave shock that he experienced tells upon him the most. Oh, it is too hard that a sweet, simple, noble, strong nature such as his--a nature which enabled him by our dear, good friend’s aid to rise from clerk to master in a few years--should be so injured that the very essence of its strength is gone. Forgive me, dear, if I worry you with my troubles in the midst of your own happiness; but, Lucy dear, I must tell some one, for the strain of keeping up a brave and cheerful appearance to Jonathan tries me, and I have no one here that I can confide in. I dread coming up to London, as we must do the day after to-morrow; for poor Mr. Hawkins left in his will that he was to be buried in the grave with his father. As there are no relations at all, Jonathan will have to be chief mourner. I shall try to run over to see you, dearest, if only for a few minutes. Forgive me for troubling you. With all blessings,\n\n“Your loving\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n20 September.--Only resolution and habit can let me make an entry to-night. I am too miserable, too low-spirited, too sick of the world and all in it, including life itself, that I would not care if I heard this moment the flapping of the wings of the angel of death. And he has been flapping those grim wings to some purpose of late--Lucy’s mother and Arthur’s father, and now.... Let me get on with my work.\n\nI duly relieved Van Helsing in his watch over Lucy. We wanted Arthur to go to rest also, but he refused at first. It was only when I told him that we should want him to help us during the day, and that we must not all break down for want of rest, lest Lucy should suffer, that he agreed to go. Van Helsing was very kind to him. “Come, my child,” he said; “come with me. You are sick and weak, and have had much sorrow and much mental pain, as well as that tax on your strength that we know of. You must not be alone; for to be alone is to be full of fears and alarms. Come to the drawing-room, where there is a big fire, and there are two sofas. You shall lie on one, and I on the other, and our sympathy will be comfort to each other, even though we do not speak, and even if we sleep.” Arthur went off with him, casting back a longing look on Lucy’s face, which lay in her pillow, almost whiter than the lawn. She lay quite still, and I looked round the room to see that all was as it should be. I could see that the Professor had carried out in this room, as in the other, his purpose of using the garlic; the whole of the window-sashes reeked with it, and round Lucy’s neck, over the silk handkerchief which Van Helsing made her keep on, was a rough chaplet of the same odorous flowers. Lucy was breathing somewhat stertorously, and her face was at its worst, for the open mouth showed the pale gums. Her teeth, in the dim, uncertain light, seemed longer and sharper than they had been in the morning. In particular, by some trick of the light, the canine teeth looked longer and sharper than the rest. I sat down by her, and presently she moved uneasily. At the same moment there came a sort of dull flapping or buffeting at the window. I went over to it softly, and peeped out by the corner of the blind. There was a full moonlight, and I could see that the noise was made by a great bat, which wheeled round--doubtless attracted by the light, although so dim--and every now and again struck the window with its wings. When I came back to my seat, I found that Lucy had moved slightly, and had torn away the garlic flowers from her throat. I replaced them as well as I could, and sat watching her.\n\nPresently she woke, and I gave her food, as Van Helsing had prescribed. She took but a little, and that languidly. There did not seem to be with her now the unconscious struggle for life and strength that had hitherto so marked her illness. It struck me as curious that the moment she became conscious she pressed the garlic flowers close to her. It was certainly odd that whenever she got into that lethargic state, with the stertorous breathing, she put the flowers from her; but that when she waked she clutched them close. There was no possibility of making any mistake about this, for in the long hours that followed, she had many spells of sleeping and waking and repeated both actions many times.\n\nAt six o’clock Van Helsing came to relieve me. Arthur had then fallen into a doze, and he mercifully let him sleep on. When he saw Lucy’s face I could hear the sissing indraw of his breath, and he said to me in a sharp whisper: “Draw up the blind; I want light!” Then he bent down, and, with his face almost touching Lucy’s, examined her carefully. He removed the flowers and lifted the silk handkerchief from her throat. As he did so he started back, and I could hear his ejaculation, “Mein Gott!” as it was smothered in his throat. I bent over and looked, too, and as I noticed some queer chill came over me.\n\nThe wounds on the throat had absolutely disappeared.\n\nFor fully five minutes Van Helsing stood looking at her, with his face at its sternest. Then he turned to me and said calmly:--\n\n“She is dying. It will not be long now. It will be much difference, mark me, whether she dies conscious or in her sleep. Wake that poor boy, and let him come and see the last; he trusts us, and we have promised him.”\n\nI went to the dining-room and waked him. He was dazed for a moment, but when he saw the sunlight streaming in through the edges of the shutters he thought he was late, and expressed his fear. I assured him that Lucy was still asleep, but told him as gently as I could that both Van Helsing and I feared that the end was near. He covered his face with his hands, and slid down on his knees by the sofa, where he remained, perhaps a minute, with his head buried, praying, whilst his shoulders shook with grief. I took him by the hand and raised him up. “Come,” I said, “my dear old fellow, summon all your fortitude: it will be best and easiest for her.”\n\nWhen we came into Lucy’s room I could see that Van Helsing had, with his usual forethought, been putting matters straight and making everything look as pleasing as possible. He had even brushed Lucy’s hair, so that it lay on the pillow in its usual sunny ripples. When we came into the room she opened her eyes, and seeing him, whispered softly:--\n\n“Arthur! Oh, my love, I am so glad you have come!” He was stooping to kiss her, when Van Helsing motioned him back. “No,” he whispered, “not yet! Hold her hand; it will comfort her more.”\n\nSo Arthur took her hand and knelt beside her, and she looked her best, with all the soft lines matching the angelic beauty of her eyes. Then gradually her eyes closed, and she sank to sleep. For a little bit her breast heaved softly, and her breath came and went like a tired child’s.\n\nAnd then insensibly there came the strange change which I had noticed in the night. Her breathing grew stertorous, the mouth opened, and the pale gums, drawn back, made the teeth look longer and sharper than ever. In a sort of sleep-waking, vague, unconscious way she opened her eyes, which were now dull and hard at once, and said in a soft, voluptuous voice, such as I had never heard from her lips:--\n\n“Arthur! Oh, my love, I am so glad you have come! Kiss me!” Arthur bent eagerly over to kiss her; but at that instant Van Helsing, who, like me, had been startled by her voice, swooped upon him, and catching him by the neck with both hands, dragged him back with a fury of strength which I never thought he could have possessed, and actually hurled him almost across the room.\n\n“Not for your life!” he said; “not for your living soul and hers!” And he stood between them like a lion at bay.\n\nArthur was so taken aback that he did not for a moment know what to do or say; and before any impulse of violence could seize him he realised the place and the occasion, and stood silent, waiting.\n\nI kept my eyes fixed on Lucy, as did Van Helsing, and we saw a spasm as of rage flit like a shadow over her face; the sharp teeth champed together. Then her eyes closed, and she breathed heavily.\n\nVery shortly after she opened her eyes in all their softness, and putting out her poor, pale, thin hand, took Van Helsing’s great brown one; drawing it to her, she kissed it. “My true friend,” she said, in a faint voice, but with untellable pathos, “My true friend, and his! Oh, guard him, and give me peace!”\n\n“I swear it!” he said solemnly, kneeling beside her and holding up his hand, as one who registers an oath. Then he turned to Arthur, and said to him: “Come, my child, take her hand in yours, and kiss her on the forehead, and only once.”\n\nTheir eyes met instead of their lips; and so they parted.\n\nLucy’s eyes closed; and Van Helsing, who had been watching closely, took Arthur’s arm, and drew him away.\n\nAnd then Lucy’s breathing became stertorous again, and all at once it ceased.\n\n“It is all over,” said Van Helsing. “She is dead!”\n\nI took Arthur by the arm, and led him away to the drawing-room, where he sat down, and covered his face with his hands, sobbing in a way that nearly broke me down to see.\n\nI went back to the room, and found Van Helsing looking at poor Lucy, and his face was sterner than ever. Some change had come over her body. Death had given back part of her beauty, for her brow and cheeks had recovered some of their flowing lines; even the lips had lost their deadly pallor. It was as if the blood, no longer needed for the working of the heart, had gone to make the harshness of death as little rude as might be.\n\n“We thought her dying whilst she slept, And sleeping when she died.”\n\nI stood beside Van Helsing, and said:--\n\n“Ah, well, poor girl, there is peace for her at last. It is the end!”\n\nHe turned to me, and said with grave solemnity:--\n\n“Not so; alas! not so. It is only the beginning!”\n\nWhen I asked him what he meant, he only shook his head and answered:--\n\n“We can do nothing as yet. Wait and see.”\n\nDR. SEWARD’S DIARY--continued.\n\nThe funeral was arranged for the next succeeding day, so that Lucy and her mother might be buried together. I attended to all the ghastly formalities, and the urbane undertaker proved that his staff were afflicted--or blessed--with something of his own obsequious suavity. Even the woman who performed the last offices for the dead remarked to me, in a confidential, brother-professional way, when she had come out from the death-chamber:--\n\n“She makes a very beautiful corpse, sir. It’s quite a privilege to attend on her. It’s not too much to say that she will do credit to our establishment!”\n\nI noticed that Van Helsing never kept far away. This was possible from the disordered state of things in the household. There were no relatives at hand; and as Arthur had to be back the next day to attend at his father’s funeral, we were unable to notify any one who should have been bidden. Under the circumstances, Van Helsing and I took it upon ourselves to examine papers, etc. He insisted upon looking over Lucy’s papers himself. I asked him why, for I feared that he, being a foreigner, might not be quite aware of English legal requirements, and so might in ignorance make some unnecessary trouble. He answered me:--\n\n“I know; I know. You forget that I am a lawyer as well as a doctor. But this is not altogether for the law. You knew that, when you avoided the coroner. I have more than him to avoid. There may be papers more--such as this.”\n\nAs he spoke he took from his pocket-book the memorandum which had been in Lucy’s breast, and which she had torn in her sleep.\n\n“When you find anything of the solicitor who is for the late Mrs. Westenra, seal all her papers, and write him to-night. For me, I watch here in the room and in Miss Lucy’s old room all night, and I myself search for what may be. It is not well that her very thoughts go into the hands of strangers.”\n\nI went on with my part of the work, and in another half hour had found the name and address of Mrs. Westenra’s solicitor and had written to him. All the poor lady’s papers were in order; explicit directions regarding the place of burial were given. I had hardly sealed the letter, when, to my surprise, Van Helsing walked into the room, saying:--\n\n“Can I help you, friend John? I am free, and if I may, my service is to you.”\n\n“Have you got what you looked for?” I asked, to which he replied:--\n\n“I did not look for any specific thing. I only hoped to find, and find I have, all that there was--only some letters and a few memoranda, and a diary new begun. But I have them here, and we shall for the present say nothing of them. I shall see that poor lad to-morrow evening, and, with his sanction, I shall use some.”\n\nWhen we had finished the work in hand, he said to me:--\n\n“And now, friend John, I think we may to bed. We want sleep, both you and I, and rest to recuperate. To-morrow we shall have much to do, but for the to-night there is no need of us. Alas!”\n\nBefore turning in we went to look at poor Lucy. The undertaker had certainly done his work well, for the room was turned into a small chapelle ardente. There was a wilderness of beautiful white flowers, and death was made as little repulsive as might be. The end of the winding-sheet was laid over the face; when the Professor bent over and turned it gently back, we both started at the beauty before us, the tall wax candles showing a sufficient light to note it well. All Lucy’s loveliness had come back to her in death, and the hours that had passed, instead of leaving traces of “decay’s effacing fingers,” had but restored the beauty of life, till positively I could not believe my eyes that I was looking at a corpse.\n\nThe Professor looked sternly grave. He had not loved her as I had, and there was no need for tears in his eyes. He said to me: “Remain till I return,” and left the room. He came back with a handful of wild garlic from the box waiting in the hall, but which had not been opened, and placed the flowers amongst the others on and around the bed. Then he took from his neck, inside his collar, a little gold crucifix, and placed it over the mouth. He restored the sheet to its place, and we came away.\n\nI was undressing in my own room, when, with a premonitory tap at the door, he entered, and at once began to speak:--\n\n“To-morrow I want you to bring me, before night, a set of post-mortem knives.”\n\n“Must we make an autopsy?” I asked.\n\n“Yes and no. I want to operate, but not as you think. Let me tell you now, but not a word to another. I want to cut off her head and take out her heart. Ah! you a surgeon, and so shocked! You, whom I have seen with no tremble of hand or heart, do operations of life and death that make the rest shudder. Oh, but I must not forget, my dear friend John, that you loved her; and I have not forgotten it, for it is I that shall operate, and you must only help. I would like to do it to-night, but for Arthur I must not; he will be free after his father’s funeral to-morrow, and he will want to see her--to see it. Then, when she is coffined ready for the next day, you and I shall come when all sleep. We shall unscrew the coffin-lid, and shall do our operation: and then replace all, so that none know, save we alone.”\n\n“But why do it at all? The girl is dead. Why mutilate her poor body without need? And if there is no necessity for a post-mortem and nothing to gain by it--no good to her, to us, to science, to human knowledge--why do it? Without such it is monstrous.”\n\nFor answer he put his hand on my shoulder, and said, with infinite tenderness:--\n\n“Friend John, I pity your poor bleeding heart; and I love you the more because it does so bleed. If I could, I would take on myself the burden that you do bear. But there are things that you know not, but that you shall know, and bless me for knowing, though they are not pleasant things. John, my child, you have been my friend now many years, and yet did you ever know me to do any without good cause? I may err--I am but man; but I believe in all I do. Was it not for these causes that you send for me when the great trouble came? Yes! Were you not amazed, nay horrified, when I would not let Arthur kiss his love--though she was dying--and snatched him away by all my strength? Yes! And yet you saw how she thanked me, with her so beautiful dying eyes, her voice, too, so weak, and she kiss my rough old hand and bless me? Yes! And did you not hear me swear promise to her, that so she closed her eyes grateful? Yes!\n\n“Well, I have good reason now for all I want to do. You have for many years trust me; you have believe me weeks past, when there be things so strange that you might have well doubt. Believe me yet a little, friend John. If you trust me not, then I must tell what I think; and that is not perhaps well. And if I work--as work I shall, no matter trust or no trust--without my friend trust in me, I work with heavy heart and feel, oh! so lonely when I want all help and courage that may be!” He paused a moment and went on solemnly: “Friend John, there are strange and terrible days before us. Let us not be two, but one, that so we work to a good end. Will you not have faith in me?”\n\nI took his hand, and promised him. I held my door open as he went away, and watched him go into his room and close the door. As I stood without moving, I saw one of the maids pass silently along the passage--she had her back towards me, so did not see me--and go into the room where Lucy lay. The sight touched me. Devotion is so rare, and we are so grateful to those who show it unasked to those we love. Here was a poor girl putting aside the terrors which she naturally had of death to go watch alone by the bier of the mistress whom she loved, so that the poor clay might not be lonely till laid to eternal rest....\n\nI must have slept long and soundly, for it was broad daylight when Van Helsing waked me by coming into my room. He came over to my bedside and said:--\n\n“You need not trouble about the knives; we shall not do it.”\n\n“Why not?” I asked. For his solemnity of the night before had greatly impressed me.\n\n“Because,” he said sternly, “it is too late--or too early. See!” Here he held up the little golden crucifix. “This was stolen in the night.”\n\n“How, stolen,” I asked in wonder, “since you have it now?”\n\n“Because I get it back from the worthless wretch who stole it, from the woman who robbed the dead and the living. Her punishment will surely come, but not through me; she knew not altogether what she did and thus unknowing, she only stole. Now we must wait.”\n\nHe went away on the word, leaving me with a new mystery to think of, a new puzzle to grapple with.\n\nThe forenoon was a dreary time, but at noon the solicitor came: Mr. Marquand, of Wholeman, Sons, Marquand & Lidderdale. He was very genial and very appreciative of what we had done, and took off our hands all cares as to details. During lunch he told us that Mrs. Westenra had for some time expected sudden death from her heart, and had put her affairs in absolute order; he informed us that, with the exception of a certain entailed property of Lucy’s father’s which now, in default of direct issue, went back to a distant branch of the family, the whole estate, real and personal, was left absolutely to Arthur Holmwood. When he had told us so much he went on:--\n\n“Frankly we did our best to prevent such a testamentary disposition, and pointed out certain contingencies that might leave her daughter either penniless or not so free as she should be to act regarding a matrimonial alliance. Indeed, we pressed the matter so far that we almost came into collision, for she asked us if we were or were not prepared to carry out her wishes. Of course, we had then no alternative but to accept. We were right in principle, and ninety-nine times out of a hundred we should have proved, by the logic of events, the accuracy of our judgment. Frankly, however, I must admit that in this case any other form of disposition would have rendered impossible the carrying out of her wishes. For by her predeceasing her daughter the latter would have come into possession of the property, and, even had she only survived her mother by five minutes, her property would, in case there were no will--and a will was a practical impossibility in such a case--have been treated at her decease as under intestacy. In which case Lord Godalming, though so dear a friend, would have had no claim in the world; and the inheritors, being remote, would not be likely to abandon their just rights, for sentimental reasons regarding an entire stranger. I assure you, my dear sirs, I am rejoiced at the result, perfectly rejoiced.”\n\nHe was a good fellow, but his rejoicing at the one little part--in which he was officially interested--of so great a tragedy, was an object-lesson in the limitations of sympathetic understanding.\n\nHe did not remain long, but said he would look in later in the day and see Lord Godalming. His coming, however, had been a certain comfort to us, since it assured us that we should not have to dread hostile criticism as to any of our acts. Arthur was expected at five o’clock, so a little before that time we visited the death-chamber. It was so in very truth, for now both mother and daughter lay in it. The undertaker, true to his craft, had made the best display he could of his goods, and there was a mortuary air about the place that lowered our spirits at once. Van Helsing ordered the former arrangement to be adhered to, explaining that, as Lord Godalming was coming very soon, it would be less harrowing to his feelings to see all that was left of his fiancée quite alone. The undertaker seemed shocked at his own stupidity and exerted himself to restore things to the condition in which we left them the night before, so that when Arthur came such shocks to his feelings as we could avoid were saved.\n\nPoor fellow! He looked desperately sad and broken; even his stalwart manhood seemed to have shrunk somewhat under the strain of his much-tried emotions. He had, I knew, been very genuinely and devotedly attached to his father; and to lose him, and at such a time, was a bitter blow to him. With me he was warm as ever, and to Van Helsing he was sweetly courteous; but I could not help seeing that there was some constraint with him. The Professor noticed it, too, and motioned me to bring him upstairs. I did so, and left him at the door of the room, as I felt he would like to be quite alone with her, but he took my arm and led me in, saying huskily:--\n\n“You loved her too, old fellow; she told me all about it, and there was no friend had a closer place in her heart than you. I don’t know how to thank you for all you have done for her. I can’t think yet....”\n\nHere he suddenly broke down, and threw his arms round my shoulders and laid his head on my breast, crying:--\n\n“Oh, Jack! Jack! What shall I do! The whole of life seems gone from me all at once, and there is nothing in the wide world for me to live for.”\n\nI comforted him as well as I could. In such cases men do not need much expression. A grip of the hand, the tightening of an arm over the shoulder, a sob in unison, are expressions of sympathy dear to a man’s heart. I stood still and silent till his sobs died away, and then I said softly to him:--\n\n“Come and look at her.”\n\nTogether we moved over to the bed, and I lifted the lawn from her face. God! how beautiful she was. Every hour seemed to be enhancing her loveliness. It frightened and amazed me somewhat; and as for Arthur, he fell a-trembling, and finally was shaken with doubt as with an ague. At last, after a long pause, he said to me in a faint whisper:--\n\n“Jack, is she really dead?”\n\nI assured him sadly that it was so, and went on to suggest--for I felt that such a horrible doubt should not have life for a moment longer than I could help--that it often happened that after death faces became softened and even resolved into their youthful beauty; that this was especially so when death had been preceded by any acute or prolonged suffering. It seemed to quite do away with any doubt, and, after kneeling beside the couch for a while and looking at her lovingly and long, he turned aside. I told him that that must be good-bye, as the coffin had to be prepared; so he went back and took her dead hand in his and kissed it, and bent over and kissed her forehead. He came away, fondly looking back over his shoulder at her as he came.\n\nI left him in the drawing-room, and told Van Helsing that he had said good-bye; so the latter went to the kitchen to tell the undertaker’s men to proceed with the preparations and to screw up the coffin. When he came out of the room again I told him of Arthur’s question, and he replied:--\n\n“I am not surprised. Just now I doubted for a moment myself!”\n\nWe all dined together, and I could see that poor Art was trying to make the best of things. Van Helsing had been silent all dinner-time; but when we had lit our cigars he said--\n\n“Lord----”; but Arthur interrupted him:--\n\n“No, no, not that, for God’s sake! not yet at any rate. Forgive me, sir: I did not mean to speak offensively; it is only because my loss is so recent.”\n\nThe Professor answered very sweetly:--\n\n“I only used that name because I was in doubt. I must not call you ‘Mr.,’ and I have grown to love you--yes, my dear boy, to love you--as Arthur.”\n\nArthur held out his hand, and took the old man’s warmly.\n\n“Call me what you will,” he said. “I hope I may always have the title of a friend. And let me say that I am at a loss for words to thank you for your goodness to my poor dear.” He paused a moment, and went on: “I know that she understood your goodness even better than I do; and if I was rude or in any way wanting at that time you acted so--you remember”--the Professor nodded--“you must forgive me.”\n\nHe answered with a grave kindness:--\n\n“I know it was hard for you to quite trust me then, for to trust such violence needs to understand; and I take it that you do not--that you cannot--trust me now, for you do not yet understand. And there may be more times when I shall want you to trust when you cannot--and may not--and must not yet understand. But the time will come when your trust shall be whole and complete in me, and when you shall understand as though the sunlight himself shone through. Then you shall bless me from first to last for your own sake, and for the sake of others and for her dear sake to whom I swore to protect.”\n\n“And, indeed, indeed, sir,” said Arthur warmly, “I shall in all ways trust you. I know and believe you have a very noble heart, and you are Jack’s friend, and you were hers. You shall do what you like.”\n\nThe Professor cleared his throat a couple of times, as though about to speak, and finally said:--\n\n“May I ask you something now?”\n\n“Certainly.”\n\n“You know that Mrs. Westenra left you all her property?”\n\n“No, poor dear; I never thought of it.”\n\n“And as it is all yours, you have a right to deal with it as you will. I want you to give me permission to read all Miss Lucy’s papers and letters. Believe me, it is no idle curiosity. I have a motive of which, be sure, she would have approved. I have them all here. I took them before we knew that all was yours, so that no strange hand might touch them--no strange eye look through words into her soul. I shall keep them, if I may; even you may not see them yet, but I shall keep them safe. No word shall be lost; and in the good time I shall give them back to you. It’s a hard thing I ask, but you will do it, will you not, for Lucy’s sake?”\n\nArthur spoke out heartily, like his old self:--\n\n“Dr. Van Helsing, you may do what you will. I feel that in saying this I am doing what my dear one would have approved. I shall not trouble you with questions till the time comes.”\n\nThe old Professor stood up as he said solemnly:--\n\n“And you are right. There will be pain for us all; but it will not be all pain, nor will this pain be the last. We and you too--you most of all, my dear boy--will have to pass through the bitter water before we reach the sweet. But we must be brave of heart and unselfish, and do our duty, and all will be well!”\n\nI slept on a sofa in Arthur’s room that night. Van Helsing did not go to bed at all. He went to and fro, as if patrolling the house, and was never out of sight of the room where Lucy lay in her coffin, strewn with the wild garlic flowers, which sent, through the odour of lily and rose, a heavy, overpowering smell into the night.\n\nMina Harker’s Journal.\n\n22 September.--In the train to Exeter. Jonathan sleeping.\n\nIt seems only yesterday that the last entry was made, and yet how much between then, in Whitby and all the world before me, Jonathan away and no news of him; and now, married to Jonathan, Jonathan a solicitor, a partner, rich, master of his business, Mr. Hawkins dead and buried, and Jonathan with another attack that may harm him. Some day he may ask me about it. Down it all goes. I am rusty in my shorthand--see what unexpected prosperity does for us--so it may be as well to freshen it up again with an exercise anyhow....\n\nThe service was very simple and very solemn. There were only ourselves and the servants there, one or two old friends of his from Exeter, his London agent, and a gentleman representing Sir John Paxton, the President of the Incorporated Law Society. Jonathan and I stood hand in hand, and we felt that our best and dearest friend was gone from us....\n\nWe came back to town quietly, taking a ’bus to Hyde Park Corner. Jonathan thought it would interest me to go into the Row for a while, so we sat down; but there were very few people there, and it was sad-looking and desolate to see so many empty chairs. It made us think of the empty chair at home; so we got up and walked down Piccadilly. Jonathan was holding me by the arm, the way he used to in old days before I went to school. I felt it very improper, for you can’t go on for some years teaching etiquette and decorum to other girls without the pedantry of it biting into yourself a bit; but it was Jonathan, and he was my husband, and we didn’t know anybody who saw us--and we didn’t care if they did--so on we walked. I was looking at a very beautiful girl, in a big cart-wheel hat, sitting in a victoria outside Guiliano’s, when I felt Jonathan clutch my arm so tight that he hurt me, and he said under his breath: “My God!” I am always anxious about Jonathan, for I fear that some nervous fit may upset him again; so I turned to him quickly, and asked him what it was that disturbed him.\n\nHe was very pale, and his eyes seemed bulging out as, half in terror and half in amazement, he gazed at a tall, thin man, with a beaky nose and black moustache and pointed beard, who was also observing the pretty girl. He was looking at her so hard that he did not see either of us, and so I had a good view of him. His face was not a good face; it was hard, and cruel, and sensual, and his big white teeth, that looked all the whiter because his lips were so red, were pointed like an animal’s. Jonathan kept staring at him, till I was afraid he would notice. I feared he might take it ill, he looked so fierce and nasty. I asked Jonathan why he was disturbed, and he answered, evidently thinking that I knew as much about it as he did: “Do you see who it is?”\n\n“No, dear,” I said; “I don’t know him; who is it?” His answer seemed to shock and thrill me, for it was said as if he did not know that it was to me, Mina, to whom he was speaking:--\n\n“It is the man himself!”\n\nThe poor dear was evidently terrified at something--very greatly terrified; I do believe that if he had not had me to lean on and to support him he would have sunk down. He kept staring; a man came out of the shop with a small parcel, and gave it to the lady, who then drove off. The dark man kept his eyes fixed on her, and when the carriage moved up Piccadilly he followed in the same direction, and hailed a hansom. Jonathan kept looking after him, and said, as if to himself:--\n\n“I believe it is the Count, but he has grown young. My God, if this be so! Oh, my God! my God! If I only knew! if I only knew!” He was distressing himself so much that I feared to keep his mind on the subject by asking him any questions, so I remained silent. I drew him away quietly, and he, holding my arm, came easily. We walked a little further, and then went in and sat for a while in the Green Park. It was a hot day for autumn, and there was a comfortable seat in a shady place. After a few minutes’ staring at nothing, Jonathan’s eyes closed, and he went quietly into a sleep, with his head on my shoulder. I thought it was the best thing for him, so did not disturb him. In about twenty minutes he woke up, and said to me quite cheerfully:--\n\n“Why, Mina, have I been asleep! Oh, do forgive me for being so rude. Come, and we’ll have a cup of tea somewhere.” He had evidently forgotten all about the dark stranger, as in his illness he had forgotten all that this episode had reminded him of. I don’t like this lapsing into forgetfulness; it may make or continue some injury to the brain. I must not ask him, for fear I shall do more harm than good; but I must somehow learn the facts of his journey abroad. The time is come, I fear, when I must open that parcel, and know what is written. Oh, Jonathan, you will, I know, forgive me if I do wrong, but it is for your own dear sake.\n\nLater.--A sad home-coming in every way--the house empty of the dear soul who was so good to us; Jonathan still pale and dizzy under a slight relapse of his malady; and now a telegram from Van Helsing, whoever he may be:--\n\n“You will be grieved to hear that Mrs. Westenra died five days ago, and that Lucy died the day before yesterday. They were both buried to-day.”\n\nOh, what a wealth of sorrow in a few words! Poor Mrs. Westenra! poor Lucy! Gone, gone, never to return to us! And poor, poor Arthur, to have lost such sweetness out of his life! God help us all to bear our troubles.\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n22 September.--It is all over. Arthur has gone back to Ring, and has taken Quincey Morris with him. What a fine fellow is Quincey! I believe in my heart of hearts that he suffered as much about Lucy’s death as any of us; but he bore himself through it like a moral Viking. If America can go on breeding men like that, she will be a power in the world indeed. Van Helsing is lying down, having a rest preparatory to his journey. He goes over to Amsterdam to-night, but says he returns to-morrow night; that he only wants to make some arrangements which can only be made personally. He is to stop with me then, if he can; he says he has work to do in London which may take him some time. Poor old fellow! I fear that the strain of the past week has broken down even his iron strength. All the time of the burial he was, I could see, putting some terrible restraint on himself. When it was all over, we were standing beside Arthur, who, poor fellow, was speaking of his part in the operation where his blood had been transfused to his Lucy’s veins; I could see Van Helsing’s face grow white and purple by turns. Arthur was saying that he felt since then as if they two had been really married and that she was his wife in the sight of God. None of us said a word of the other operations, and none of us ever shall. Arthur and Quincey went away together to the station, and Van Helsing and I came on here. The moment we were alone in the carriage he gave way to a regular fit of hysterics. He has denied to me since that it was hysterics, and insisted that it was only his sense of humour asserting itself under very terrible conditions. He laughed till he cried, and I had to draw down the blinds lest any one should see us and misjudge; and then he cried, till he laughed again; and laughed and cried together, just as a woman does. I tried to be stern with him, as one is to a woman under the circumstances; but it had no effect. Men and women are so different in manifestations of nervous strength or weakness! Then when his face grew grave and stern again I asked him why his mirth, and why at such a time. His reply was in a way characteristic of him, for it was logical and forceful and mysterious. He said:--\n\n“Ah, you don’t comprehend, friend John. Do not think that I am not sad, though I laugh. See, I have cried even when the laugh did choke me. But no more think that I am all sorry when I cry, for the laugh he come just the same. Keep it always with you that laughter who knock at your door and say, ‘May I come in?’ is not the true laughter. No! he is a king, and he come when and how he like. He ask no person; he choose no time of suitability. He say, ‘I am here.’ Behold, in example I grieve my heart out for that so sweet young girl; I give my blood for her, though I am old and worn; I give my time, my skill, my sleep; I let my other sufferers want that so she may have all. And yet I can laugh at her very grave--laugh when the clay from the spade of the sexton drop upon her coffin and say ‘Thud! thud!’ to my heart, till it send back the blood from my cheek. My heart bleed for that poor boy--that dear boy, so of the age of mine own boy had I been so blessed that he live, and with his hair and eyes the same. There, you know now why I love him so. And yet when he say things that touch my husband-heart to the quick, and make my father-heart yearn to him as to no other man--not even to you, friend John, for we are more level in experiences than father and son--yet even at such moment King Laugh he come to me and shout and bellow in my ear, ‘Here I am! here I am!’ till the blood come dance back and bring some of the sunshine that he carry with him to my cheek. Oh, friend John, it is a strange world, a sad world, a world full of miseries, and woes, and troubles; and yet when King Laugh come he make them all dance to the tune he play. Bleeding hearts, and dry bones of the churchyard, and tears that burn as they fall--all dance together to the music that he make with that smileless mouth of him. And believe me, friend John, that he is good to come, and kind. Ah, we men and women are like ropes drawn tight with strain that pull us different ways. Then tears come; and, like the rain on the ropes, they brace us up, until perhaps the strain become too great, and we break. But King Laugh he come like the sunshine, and he ease off the strain again; and we bear to go on with our labour, what it may be.”\n\nI did not like to wound him by pretending not to see his idea; but, as I did not yet understand the cause of his laughter, I asked him. As he answered me his face grew stern, and he said in quite a different tone:--\n\n“Oh, it was the grim irony of it all--this so lovely lady garlanded with flowers, that looked so fair as life, till one by one we wondered if she were truly dead; she laid in that so fine marble house in that lonely churchyard, where rest so many of her kin, laid there with the mother who loved her, and whom she loved; and that sacred bell going ‘Toll! toll! toll!’ so sad and slow; and those holy men, with the white garments of the angel, pretending to read books, and yet all the time their eyes never on the page; and all of us with the bowed head. And all for what? She is dead; so! Is it not?”\n\n“Well, for the life of me, Professor,” I said, “I can’t see anything to laugh at in all that. Why, your explanation makes it a harder puzzle than before. But even if the burial service was comic, what about poor Art and his trouble? Why, his heart was simply breaking.”\n\n“Just so. Said he not that the transfusion of his blood to her veins had made her truly his bride?”\n\n“Yes, and it was a sweet and comforting idea for him.”\n\n“Quite so. But there was a difficulty, friend John. If so that, then what about the others? Ho, ho! Then this so sweet maid is a polyandrist, and me, with my poor wife dead to me, but alive by Church’s law, though no wits, all gone--even I, who am faithful husband to this now-no-wife, am bigamist.”\n\n“I don’t see where the joke comes in there either!” I said; and I did not feel particularly pleased with him for saying such things. He laid his hand on my arm, and said:--\n\n“Friend John, forgive me if I pain. I showed not my feeling to others when it would wound, but only to you, my old friend, whom I can trust. If you could have looked into my very heart then when I want to laugh; if you could have done so when the laugh arrived; if you could do so now, when King Laugh have pack up his crown, and all that is to him--for he go far, far away from me, and for a long, long time--maybe you would perhaps pity me the most of all.”\n\nI was touched by the tenderness of his tone, and asked why.\n\n“Because I know!”\n\nAnd now we are all scattered; and for many a long day loneliness will sit over our roofs with brooding wings. Lucy lies in the tomb of her kin, a lordly death-house in a lonely churchyard, away from teeming London; where the air is fresh, and the sun rises over Hampstead Hill, and where wild flowers grow of their own accord.\n\nSo I can finish this diary; and God only knows if I shall ever begin another. If I do, or if I even open this again, it will be to deal with different people and different themes; for here at the end, where the romance of my life is told, ere I go back to take up the thread of my life-work, I say sadly and without hope,\n\n“The Westminster Gazette,” 25 September.\n\n# A HAMPSTEAD MYSTERY.\n\nThe neighbourhood of Hampstead is just at present exercised with a series of events which seem to run on lines parallel to those of what was known to the writers of headlines as “The Kensington Horror,” or “The Stabbing Woman,” or “The Woman in Black.” During the past two or three days several cases have occurred of young children straying from home or neglecting to return from their playing on the Heath. In all these cases the children were too young to give any properly intelligible account of themselves, but the consensus of their excuses is that they had been with a “bloofer lady.” It has always been late in the evening when they have been missed, and on two occasions the children have not been found until early in the following morning. It is generally supposed in the neighbourhood that, as the first child missed gave as his reason for being away that a “bloofer lady” had asked him to come for a walk, the others had picked up the phrase and used it as occasion served. This is the more natural as the favourite game of the little ones at present is luring each other away by wiles. A correspondent writes us that to see some of the tiny tots pretending to be the “bloofer lady” is supremely funny. Some of our caricaturists might, he says, take a lesson in the irony of grotesque by comparing the reality and the picture. It is only in accordance with general principles of human nature that the “bloofer lady” should be the popular rôle at these al fresco performances. Our correspondent naïvely says that even Ellen Terry could not be so winningly attractive as some of these grubby-faced little children pretend--and even imagine themselves--to be.\n\nThere is, however, possibly a serious side to the question, for some of the children, indeed all who have been missed at night, have been slightly torn or wounded in the throat. The wounds seem such as might be made by a rat or a small dog, and although of not much importance individually, would tend to show that whatever animal inflicts them has a system or method of its own. The police of the division have been instructed to keep a sharp look-out for straying children, especially when very young, in and around Hampstead Heath, and for any stray dog which may be about.\n\n“The Westminster Gazette,” 25 September.\n\nExtra Special.\n\nThe “Bloofer Lady.”\n\nWe have just received intelligence that another child, missed last night, was only discovered late in the morning under a furze bush at the Shooter’s Hill side of Hampstead Heath, which is, perhaps, less frequented than the other parts. It has the same tiny wound in the throat as has been noticed in other cases. It was terribly weak, and looked quite emaciated. It too, when partially restored, had the common story to tell of being lured away by the “bloofer lady.”\n\n# MINA HARKER’S JOURNAL\n\n23 September.--Jonathan is better after a bad night. I am so glad that he has plenty of work to do, for that keeps his mind off the terrible things; and oh, I am rejoiced that he is not now weighed down with the responsibility of his new position. I knew he would be true to himself, and now how proud I am to see my Jonathan rising to the height of his advancement and keeping pace in all ways with the duties that come upon him. He will be away all day till late, for he said he could not lunch at home. My household work is done, so I shall take his foreign journal, and lock myself up in my room and read it....\n\n24 September.--I hadn’t the heart to write last night; that terrible record of Jonathan’s upset me so. Poor dear! How he must have suffered, whether it be true or only imagination. I wonder if there is any truth in it at all. Did he get his brain fever, and then write all those terrible things, or had he some cause for it all? I suppose I shall never know, for I dare not open the subject to him.... And yet that man we saw yesterday! He seemed quite certain of him.... Poor fellow! I suppose it was the funeral upset him and sent his mind back on some train of thought.... He believes it all himself. I remember how on our wedding-day he said: “Unless some solemn duty come upon me to go back to the bitter hours, asleep or awake, mad or sane.” There seems to be through it all some thread of continuity.... That fearful Count was coming to London.... If it should be, and he came to London, with his teeming millions.... There may be a solemn duty; and if it come we must not shrink from it.... I shall be prepared. I shall get my typewriter this very hour and begin transcribing. Then we shall be ready for other eyes if required. And if it be wanted; then, perhaps, if I am ready, poor Jonathan may not be upset, for I can speak for him and never let him be troubled or worried with it at all. If ever Jonathan quite gets over the nervousness he may want to tell me of it all, and I can ask him questions and find out things, and see how I may comfort him.\n\n“24 September.\n\n(Confidence)\n\n“Dear Madam,--\n\n“I pray you to pardon my writing, in that I am so far friend as that I sent to you sad news of Miss Lucy Westenra’s death. By the kindness of Lord Godalming, I am empowered to read her letters and papers, for I am deeply concerned about certain matters vitally important. In them I find some letters from you, which show how great friends you were and how you love her. Oh, Madam Mina, by that love, I implore you, help me. It is for others’ good that I ask--to redress great wrong, and to lift much and terrible troubles--that may be more great than you can know. May it be that I see you? You can trust me. I am friend of Dr. John Seward and of Lord Godalming (that was Arthur of Miss Lucy). I must keep it private for the present from all. I should come to Exeter to see you at once if you tell me I am privilege to come, and where and when. I implore your pardon, madam. I have read your letters to poor Lucy, and know how good you are and how your husband suffer; so I pray you, if it may be, enlighten him not, lest it may harm. Again your pardon, and forgive me.\n\nTelegram, Mrs. Harker to Van Helsing.\n\n“25 September.--Come to-day by quarter-past ten train if you can catch it. Can see you any time you call.\n\n# MINA HARKER’S JOURNAL.\n\n25 September.--I cannot help feeling terribly excited as the time draws near for the visit of Dr. Van Helsing, for somehow I expect that it will throw some light upon Jonathan’s sad experience; and as he attended poor dear Lucy in her last illness, he can tell me all about her. That is the reason of his coming; it is concerning Lucy and her sleep-walking, and not about Jonathan. Then I shall never know the real truth now! How silly I am. That awful journal gets hold of my imagination and tinges everything with something of its own colour. Of course it is about Lucy. That habit came back to the poor dear, and that awful night on the cliff must have made her ill. I had almost forgotten in my own affairs how ill she was afterwards. She must have told him of her sleep-walking adventure on the cliff, and that I knew all about it; and now he wants me to tell him what she knows, so that he may understand. I hope I did right in not saying anything of it to Mrs. Westenra; I should never forgive myself if any act of mine, were it even a negative one, brought harm on poor dear Lucy. I hope, too, Dr. Van Helsing will not blame me; I have had so much trouble and anxiety of late that I feel I cannot bear more just at present.\n\nI suppose a cry does us all good at times--clears the air as other rain does. Perhaps it was reading the journal yesterday that upset me, and then Jonathan went away this morning to stay away from me a whole day and night, the first time we have been parted since our marriage. I do hope the dear fellow will take care of himself, and that nothing will occur to upset him. It is two o’clock, and the doctor will be here soon now. I shall say nothing of Jonathan’s journal unless he asks me. I am so glad I have type-written out my own journal, so that, in case he asks about Lucy, I can hand it to him; it will save much questioning.\n\nLater.--He has come and gone. Oh, what a strange meeting, and how it all makes my head whirl round! I feel like one in a dream. Can it be all possible, or even a part of it? If I had not read Jonathan’s journal first, I should never have accepted even a possibility. Poor, poor, dear Jonathan! How he must have suffered. Please the good God, all this may not upset him again. I shall try to save him from it; but it may be even a consolation and a help to him--terrible though it be and awful in its consequences--to know for certain that his eyes and ears and brain did not deceive him, and that it is all true. It may be that it is the doubt which haunts him; that when the doubt is removed, no matter which--waking or dreaming--may prove the truth, he will be more satisfied and better able to bear the shock. Dr. Van Helsing must be a good man as well as a clever one if he is Arthur’s friend and Dr. Seward’s, and if they brought him all the way from Holland to look after Lucy. I feel from having seen him that he is good and kind and of a noble nature. When he comes to-morrow I shall ask him about Jonathan; and then, please God, all this sorrow and anxiety may lead to a good end. I used to think I would like to practise interviewing; Jonathan’s friend on “The Exeter News” told him that memory was everything in such work--that you must be able to put down exactly almost every word spoken, even if you had to refine some of it afterwards. Here was a rare interview; I shall try to record it verbatim.\n\nIt was half-past two o’clock when the knock came. I took my courage à deux mains and waited. In a few minutes Mary opened the door, and announced “Dr. Van Helsing.”\n\nI rose and bowed, and he came towards me; a man of medium weight, strongly built, with his shoulders set back over a broad, deep chest and a neck well balanced on the trunk as the head is on the neck. The poise of the head strikes one at once as indicative of thought and power; the head is noble, well-sized, broad, and large behind the ears. The face, clean-shaven, shows a hard, square chin, a large, resolute, mobile mouth, a good-sized nose, rather straight, but with quick, sensitive nostrils, that seem to broaden as the big, bushy brows come down and the mouth tightens. The forehead is broad and fine, rising at first almost straight and then sloping back above two bumps or ridges wide apart; such a forehead that the reddish hair cannot possibly tumble over it, but falls naturally back and to the sides. Big, dark blue eyes are set widely apart, and are quick and tender or stern with the man’s moods. He said to me:--\n\n“Mrs. Harker, is it not?” I bowed assent.\n\n“That was Miss Mina Murray?” Again I assented.\n\n“It is Mina Murray that I came to see that was friend of that poor dear child Lucy Westenra. Madam Mina, it is on account of the dead I come.”\n\n“Sir,” I said, “you could have no better claim on me than that you were a friend and helper of Lucy Westenra.” And I held out my hand. He took it and said tenderly:--\n\n“Oh, Madam Mina, I knew that the friend of that poor lily girl must be good, but I had yet to learn----” He finished his speech with a courtly bow. I asked him what it was that he wanted to see me about, so he at once began:--\n\n“I have read your letters to Miss Lucy. Forgive me, but I had to begin to inquire somewhere, and there was none to ask. I know that you were with her at Whitby. She sometimes kept a diary--you need not look surprised, Madam Mina; it was begun after you had left, and was in imitation of you--and in that diary she traces by inference certain things to a sleep-walking in which she puts down that you saved her. In great perplexity then I come to you, and ask you out of your so much kindness to tell me all of it that you can remember.”\n\n“I can tell you, I think, Dr. Van Helsing, all about it.”\n\n“Ah, then you have good memory for facts, for details? It is not always so with young ladies.”\n\n“No, doctor, but I wrote it all down at the time. I can show it to you if you like.”\n\n“Oh, Madam Mina, I will be grateful; you will do me much favour.” I could not resist the temptation of mystifying him a bit--I suppose it is some of the taste of the original apple that remains still in our mouths--so I handed him the shorthand diary. He took it with a grateful bow, and said:--\n\n“May I read it?”\n\n“If you wish,” I answered as demurely as I could. He opened it, and for an instant his face fell. Then he stood up and bowed.\n\n“Oh, you so clever woman!” he said. “I knew long that Mr. Jonathan was a man of much thankfulness; but see, his wife have all the good things. And will you not so much honour me and so help me as to read it for me? Alas! I know not the shorthand.” By this time my little joke was over, and I was almost ashamed; so I took the typewritten copy from my workbasket and handed it to him.\n\n“Forgive me,” I said: “I could not help it; but I had been thinking that it was of dear Lucy that you wished to ask, and so that you might not have time to wait--not on my account, but because I know your time must be precious--I have written it out on the typewriter for you.”\n\nHe took it and his eyes glistened. “You are so good,” he said. “And may I read it now? I may want to ask you some things when I have read.”\n\n“By all means,” I said, “read it over whilst I order lunch; and then you can ask me questions whilst we eat.” He bowed and settled himself in a chair with his back to the light, and became absorbed in the papers, whilst I went to see after lunch chiefly in order that he might not be disturbed. When I came back, I found him walking hurriedly up and down the room, his face all ablaze with excitement. He rushed up to me and took me by both hands.\n\n“Oh, Madam Mina,” he said, “how can I say what I owe to you? This paper is as sunshine. It opens the gate to me. I am daze, I am dazzle, with so much light, and yet clouds roll in behind the light every time. But that you do not, cannot, comprehend. Oh, but I am grateful to you, you so clever woman. Madam”--he said this very solemnly--“if ever Abraham Van Helsing can do anything for you or yours, I trust you will let me know. It will be pleasure and delight if I may serve you as a friend; as a friend, but all I have ever learned, all I can ever do, shall be for you and those you love. There are darknesses in life, and there are lights; you are one of the lights. You will have happy life and good life, and your husband will be blessed in you.”\n\n“But, doctor, you praise me too much, and--and you do not know me.”\n\n“Not know you--I, who am old, and who have studied all my life men and women; I, who have made my specialty the brain and all that belongs to him and all that follow from him! And I have read your diary that you have so goodly written for me, and which breathes out truth in every line. I, who have read your so sweet letter to poor Lucy of your marriage and your trust, not know you! Oh, Madam Mina, good women tell all their lives, and by day and by hour and by minute, such things that angels can read; and we men who wish to know have in us something of angels’ eyes. Your husband is noble nature, and you are noble too, for you trust, and trust cannot be where there is mean nature. And your husband--tell me of him. Is he quite well? Is all that fever gone, and is he strong and hearty?” I saw here an opening to ask him about Jonathan, so I said:--\n\n“He was almost recovered, but he has been greatly upset by Mr. Hawkins’s death.” He interrupted:--\n\n“Oh, yes, I know, I know. I have read your last two letters.” I went on:--\n\n“I suppose this upset him, for when we were in town on Thursday last he had a sort of shock.”\n\n“A shock, and after brain fever so soon! That was not good. What kind of a shock was it?”\n\n“He thought he saw some one who recalled something terrible, something which led to his brain fever.” And here the whole thing seemed to overwhelm me in a rush. The pity for Jonathan, the horror which he experienced, the whole fearful mystery of his diary, and the fear that has been brooding over me ever since, all came in a tumult. I suppose I was hysterical, for I threw myself on my knees and held up my hands to him, and implored him to make my husband well again. He took my hands and raised me up, and made me sit on the sofa, and sat by me; he held my hand in his, and said to me with, oh, such infinite sweetness:--\n\n“My life is a barren and lonely one, and so full of work that I have not had much time for friendships; but since I have been summoned to here by my friend John Seward I have known so many good people and seen such nobility that I feel more than ever--and it has grown with my advancing years--the loneliness of my life. Believe, me, then, that I come here full of respect for you, and you have given me hope--hope, not in what I am seeking of, but that there are good women still left to make life happy--good women, whose lives and whose truths may make good lesson for the children that are to be. I am glad, glad, that I may here be of some use to you; for if your husband suffer, he suffer within the range of my study and experience. I promise you that I will gladly do all for him that I can--all to make his life strong and manly, and your life a happy one. Now you must eat. You are overwrought and perhaps over-anxious. Husband Jonathan would not like to see you so pale; and what he like not where he love, is not to his good. Therefore for his sake you must eat and smile. You have told me all about Lucy, and so now we shall not speak of it, lest it distress. I shall stay in Exeter to-night, for I want to think much over what you have told me, and when I have thought I will ask you questions, if I may. And then, too, you will tell me of husband Jonathan’s trouble so far as you can, but not yet. You must eat now; afterwards you shall tell me all.”\n\nAfter lunch, when we went back to the drawing-room, he said to me:--\n\n“And now tell me all about him.” When it came to speaking to this great learned man, I began to fear that he would think me a weak fool, and Jonathan a madman--that journal is all so strange--and I hesitated to go on. But he was so sweet and kind, and he had promised to help, and I trusted him, so I said:--\n\n“Dr. Van Helsing, what I have to tell you is so queer that you must not laugh at me or at my husband. I have been since yesterday in a sort of fever of doubt; you must be kind to me, and not think me foolish that I have even half believed some very strange things.” He reassured me by his manner as well as his words when he said:--\n\n“Oh, my dear, if you only know how strange is the matter regarding which I am here, it is you who would laugh. I have learned not to think little of any one’s belief, no matter how strange it be. I have tried to keep an open mind; and it is not the ordinary things of life that could close it, but the strange things, the extraordinary things, the things that make one doubt if they be mad or sane.”\n\n“Thank you, thank you, a thousand times! You have taken a weight off my mind. If you will let me, I shall give you a paper to read. It is long, but I have typewritten it out. It will tell you my trouble and Jonathan’s. It is the copy of his journal when abroad, and all that happened. I dare not say anything of it; you will read for yourself and judge. And then when I see you, perhaps, you will be very kind and tell me what you think.”\n\n“I promise,” he said as I gave him the papers; “I shall in the morning, so soon as I can, come to see you and your husband, if I may.”\n\n“Jonathan will be here at half-past eleven, and you must come to lunch with us and see him then; you could catch the quick 3:34 train, which will leave you at Paddington before eight.” He was surprised at my knowledge of the trains off-hand, but he does not know that I have made up all the trains to and from Exeter, so that I may help Jonathan in case he is in a hurry.\n\nSo he took the papers with him and went away, and I sit here thinking--thinking I don’t know what.\n\n“25 September, 6 o’clock.\n\n“Dear Madam Mina,--\n\n“I have read your husband’s so wonderful diary. You may sleep without doubt. Strange and terrible as it is, it is true! I will pledge my life on it. It may be worse for others; but for him and you there is no dread. He is a noble fellow; and let me tell you from experience of men, that one who would do as he did in going down that wall and to that room--ay, and going a second time--is not one to be injured in permanence by a shock. His brain and his heart are all right; this I swear, before I have even seen him; so be at rest. I shall have much to ask him of other things. I am blessed that to-day I come to see you, for I have learn all at once so much that again I am dazzle--dazzle more than ever, and I must think.\n\n“Yours the most faithful,\n\n“25 September, 6:30 p. m.\n\n“My dear Dr. Van Helsing,--\n\n“A thousand thanks for your kind letter, which has taken a great weight off my mind. And yet, if it be true, what terrible things there are in the world, and what an awful thing if that man, that monster, be really in London! I fear to think. I have this moment, whilst writing, had a wire from Jonathan, saying that he leaves by the 6:25 to-night from Launceston and will be here at 10:18, so that I shall have no fear to-night. Will you, therefore, instead of lunching with us, please come to breakfast at eight o’clock, if this be not too early for you? You can get away, if you are in a hurry, by the 10:30 train, which will bring you to Paddington by 2:35. Do not answer this, as I shall take it that, if I do not hear, you will come to breakfast.\n\n“Believe me,\n\n“Your faithful and grateful friend,\n\nJonathan Harker’s Journal.\n\n26 September.--I thought never to write in this diary again, but the time has come. When I got home last night Mina had supper ready, and when we had supped she told me of Van Helsing’s visit, and of her having given him the two diaries copied out, and of how anxious she has been about me. She showed me in the doctor’s letter that all I wrote down was true. It seems to have made a new man of me. It was the doubt as to the reality of the whole thing that knocked me over. I felt impotent, and in the dark, and distrustful. But, now that I know, I am not afraid, even of the Count. He has succeeded after all, then, in his design in getting to London, and it was he I saw. He has got younger, and how? Van Helsing is the man to unmask him and hunt him out, if he is anything like what Mina says. We sat late, and talked it all over. Mina is dressing, and I shall call at the hotel in a few minutes and bring him over....\n\nHe was, I think, surprised to see me. When I came into the room where he was, and introduced myself, he took me by the shoulder, and turned my face round to the light, and said, after a sharp scrutiny:--\n\n“But Madam Mina told me you were ill, that you had had a shock.” It was so funny to hear my wife called “Madam Mina” by this kindly, strong-faced old man. I smiled, and said:--\n\n“I was ill, I have had a shock; but you have cured me already.”\n“And how?”\n\n“By your letter to Mina last night. I was in doubt, and then everything took a hue of unreality, and I did not know what to trust, even the evidence of my own senses. Not knowing what to trust, I did not know what to do; and so had only to keep on working in what had hitherto been the groove of my life. The groove ceased to avail me, and I mistrusted myself. Doctor, you don’t know what it is to doubt everything, even yourself. No, you don’t; you couldn’t with eyebrows like yours.” He seemed pleased, and laughed as he said:--\n\n“So! You are physiognomist. I learn more here with each hour. I am with so much pleasure coming to you to breakfast; and, oh, sir, you will pardon praise from an old man, but you are blessed in your wife.” I would listen to him go on praising Mina for a day, so I simply nodded and stood silent.\n\n“She is one of God’s women, fashioned by His own hand to show us men and other women that there is a heaven where we can enter, and that its light can be here on earth. So true, so sweet, so noble, so little an egoist--and that, let me tell you, is much in this age, so sceptical and selfish. And you, sir--I have read all the letters to poor Miss Lucy, and some of them speak of you, so I know you since some days from the knowing of others; but I have seen your true self since last night. You will give me your hand, will you not? And let us be friends for all our lives.”\n\nWe shook hands, and he was so earnest and so kind that it made me quite choky.\n\n“And now,” he said, “may I ask you for some more help? I have a great task to do, and at the beginning it is to know. You can help me here. Can you tell me what went before your going to Transylvania? Later on I may ask more help, and of a different kind; but at first this will do.”\n\n“Look here, sir,” I said, “does what you have to do concern the Count?”\n\n“It does,” he said solemnly.\n\n“Then I am with you heart and soul. As you go by the 10:30 train, you will not have time to read them; but I shall get the bundle of papers. You can take them with you and read them in the train.”\n\nAfter breakfast I saw him to the station. When we were parting he said:--\n\n“Perhaps you will come to town if I send to you, and take Madam Mina too.”\n\n“We shall both come when you will,” I said.\n\nI had got him the morning papers and the London papers of the previous night, and while we were talking at the carriage window, waiting for the train to start, he was turning them over. His eyes suddenly seemed to catch something in one of them, “The Westminster Gazette”--I knew it by the colour--and he grew quite white. He read something intently, groaning to himself: “Mein Gott! Mein Gott! So soon! so soon!” I do not think he remembered me at the moment. Just then the whistle blew, and the train moved off. This recalled him to himself, and he leaned out of the window and waved his hand, calling out: “Love to Madam Mina; I shall write so soon as ever I can.”\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n26 September.--Truly there is no such thing as finality. Not a week since I said “Finis,” and yet here I am starting fresh again, or rather going on with the same record. Until this afternoon I had no cause to think of what is done. Renfield had become, to all intents, as sane as he ever was. He was already well ahead with his fly business; and he had just started in the spider line also; so he had not been of any trouble to me. I had a letter from Arthur, written on Sunday, and from it I gather that he is bearing up wonderfully well. Quincey Morris is with him, and that is much of a help, for he himself is a bubbling well of good spirits. Quincey wrote me a line too, and from him I hear that Arthur is beginning to recover something of his old buoyancy; so as to them all my mind is at rest. As for myself, I was settling down to my work with the enthusiasm which I used to have for it, so that I might fairly have said that the wound which poor Lucy left on me was becoming cicatrised. Everything is, however, now reopened; and what is to be the end God only knows. I have an idea that Van Helsing thinks he knows, too, but he will only let out enough at a time to whet curiosity. He went to Exeter yesterday, and stayed there all night. To-day he came back, and almost bounded into the room at about half-past five o’clock, and thrust last night’s “Westminster Gazette” into my hand.\n\n“What do you think of that?” he asked as he stood back and folded his arms.\n\nI looked over the paper, for I really did not know what he meant; but he took it from me and pointed out a paragraph about children being decoyed away at Hampstead. It did not convey much to me, until I reached a passage where it described small punctured wounds on their throats. An idea struck me, and I looked up. “Well?” he said.\n\n“It is like poor Lucy’s.”\n\n“And what do you make of it?”\n\n“Simply that there is some cause in common. Whatever it was that injured her has injured them.” I did not quite understand his answer:--\n\n“That is true indirectly, but not directly.”\n\n“How do you mean, Professor?” I asked. I was a little inclined to take his seriousness lightly--for, after all, four days of rest and freedom from burning, harrowing anxiety does help to restore one’s spirits--but when I saw his face, it sobered me. Never, even in the midst of our despair about poor Lucy, had he looked more stern.\n\n“Tell me!” I said. “I can hazard no opinion. I do not know what to think, and I have no data on which to found a conjecture.”\n\n“Do you mean to tell me, friend John, that you have no suspicion as to what poor Lucy died of; not after all the hints given, not only by events, but by me?”\n\n“Of nervous prostration following on great loss or waste of blood.”\n\n“And how the blood lost or waste?” I shook my head. He stepped over and sat down beside me, and went on:--\n\n“You are clever man, friend John; you reason well, and your wit is bold; but you are too prejudiced. You do not let your eyes see nor your ears hear, and that which is outside your daily life is not of account to you. Do you not think that there are things which you cannot understand, and yet which are; that some people see things that others cannot? But there are things old and new which must not be contemplate by men’s eyes, because they know--or think they know--some things which other men have told them. Ah, it is the fault of our science that it wants to explain all; and if it explain not, then it says there is nothing to explain. But yet we see around us every day the growth of new beliefs, which think themselves new; and which are yet but the old, which pretend to be young--like the fine ladies at the opera. I suppose now you do not believe in corporeal transference. No? Nor in materialisation. No? Nor in astral bodies. No? Nor in the reading of thought. No? Nor in hypnotism----”\n\n“Yes,” I said. “Charcot has proved that pretty well.” He smiled as he went on: “Then you are satisfied as to it. Yes? And of course then you understand how it act, and can follow the mind of the great Charcot--alas that he is no more!--into the very soul of the patient that he influence. No? Then, friend John, am I to take it that you simply accept fact, and are satisfied to let from premise to conclusion be a blank? No? Then tell me--for I am student of the brain--how you accept the hypnotism and reject the thought reading. Let me tell you, my friend, that there are things done to-day in electrical science which would have been deemed unholy by the very men who discovered electricity--who would themselves not so long before have been burned as wizards. There are always mysteries in life. Why was it that Methuselah lived nine hundred years, and ‘Old Parr’ one hundred and sixty-nine, and yet that poor Lucy, with four men’s blood in her poor veins, could not live even one day? For, had she live one more day, we could have save her. Do you know all the mystery of life and death? Do you know the altogether of comparative anatomy and can say wherefore the qualities of brutes are in some men, and not in others? Can you tell me why, when other spiders die small and soon, that one great spider lived for centuries in the tower of the old Spanish church and grew and grew, till, on descending, he could drink the oil of all the church lamps? Can you tell me why in the Pampas, ay and elsewhere, there are bats that come at night and open the veins of cattle and horses and suck dry their veins; how in some islands of the Western seas there are bats which hang on the trees all day, and those who have seen describe as like giant nuts or pods, and that when the sailors sleep on the deck, because that it is hot, flit down on them, and then--and then in the morning are found dead men, white as even Miss Lucy was?”\n\n“Good God, Professor!” I said, starting up. “Do you mean to tell me that Lucy was bitten by such a bat; and that such a thing is here in London in the nineteenth century?” He waved his hand for silence, and went on:--\n\n“Can you tell me why the tortoise lives more long than generations of men; why the elephant goes on and on till he have seen dynasties; and why the parrot never die only of bite of cat or dog or other complaint? Can you tell me why men believe in all ages and places that there are some few who live on always if they be permit; that there are men and women who cannot die? We all know--because science has vouched for the fact--that there have been toads shut up in rocks for thousands of years, shut in one so small hole that only hold him since the youth of the world. Can you tell me how the Indian fakir can make himself to die and have been buried, and his grave sealed and corn sowed on it, and the corn reaped and be cut and sown and reaped and cut again, and then men come and take away the unbroken seal and that there lie the Indian fakir, not dead, but that rise up and walk amongst them as before?” Here I interrupted him. I was getting bewildered; he so crowded on my mind his list of nature’s eccentricities and possible impossibilities that my imagination was getting fired. I had a dim idea that he was teaching me some lesson, as long ago he used to do in his study at Amsterdam; but he used then to tell me the thing, so that I could have the object of thought in mind all the time. But now I was without this help, yet I wanted to follow him, so I said:--\n\n“Professor, let me be your pet student again. Tell me the thesis, so that I may apply your knowledge as you go on. At present I am going in my mind from point to point as a mad man, and not a sane one, follows an idea. I feel like a novice lumbering through a bog in a mist, jumping from one tussock to another in the mere blind effort to move on without knowing where I am going.”\n\n“That is good image,” he said. “Well, I shall tell you. My thesis is this: I want you to believe.”\n\n“To believe what?”\n\n“To believe in things that you cannot. Let me illustrate. I heard once of an American who so defined faith: ‘that faculty which enables us to believe things which we know to be untrue.’ For one, I follow that man. He meant that we shall have an open mind, and not let a little bit of truth check the rush of a big truth, like a small rock does a railway truck. We get the small truth first. Good! We keep him, and we value him; but all the same we must not let him think himself all the truth in the universe.”\n\n“Then you want me not to let some previous conviction injure the receptivity of my mind with regard to some strange matter. Do I read your lesson aright?”\n\n“Ah, you are my favourite pupil still. It is worth to teach you. Now that you are willing to understand, you have taken the first step to understand. You think then that those so small holes in the children’s throats were made by the same that made the hole in Miss Lucy?”\n\n“I suppose so.” He stood up and said solemnly:--\n\n“Then you are wrong. Oh, would it were so! but alas! no. It is worse, far, far worse.”\n\n“In God’s name, Professor Van Helsing, what do you mean?” I cried.\n\nHe threw himself with a despairing gesture into a chair, and placed his elbows on the table, covering his face with his hands as he spoke:--\n\n“They were made by Miss Lucy!”\n\nDR. SEWARD’S DIARY--continued.\n\nFor a while sheer anger mastered me; it was as if he had during her life struck Lucy on the face. I smote the table hard and rose up as I said to him:--\n\n“Dr. Van Helsing, are you mad?” He raised his head and looked at me, and somehow the tenderness of his face calmed me at once. “Would I were!” he said. “Madness were easy to bear compared with truth like this. Oh, my friend, why, think you, did I go so far round, why take so long to tell you so simple a thing? Was it because I hate you and have hated you all my life? Was it because I wished to give you pain? Was it that I wanted, now so late, revenge for that time when you saved my life, and from a fearful death? Ah no!”\n\n“Forgive me,” said I. He went on:--\n\n“My friend, it was because I wished to be gentle in the breaking to you, for I know you have loved that so sweet lady. But even yet I do not expect you to believe. It is so hard to accept at once any abstract truth, that we may doubt such to be possible when we have always believed the ‘no’ of it; it is more hard still to accept so sad a concrete truth, and of such a one as Miss Lucy. To-night I go to prove it. Dare you come with me?”\n\nThis staggered me. A man does not like to prove such a truth; Byron excepted from the category, jealousy.\n\n“And prove the very truth he most abhorred.”\n\nHe saw my hesitation, and spoke:--\n\n“The logic is simple, no madman’s logic this time, jumping from tussock to tussock in a misty bog. If it be not true, then proof will be relief; at worst it will not harm. If it be true! Ah, there is the dread; yet very dread should help my cause, for in it is some need of belief. Come, I tell you what I propose: first, that we go off now and see that child in the hospital. Dr. Vincent, of the North Hospital, where the papers say the child is, is friend of mine, and I think of yours since you were in class at Amsterdam. He will let two scientists see his case, if he will not let two friends. We shall tell him nothing, but only that we wish to learn. And then----”\n\n“And then?” He took a key from his pocket and held it up. “And then we spend the night, you and I, in the churchyard where Lucy lies. This is the key that lock the tomb. I had it from the coffin-man to give to Arthur.” My heart sank within me, for I felt that there was some fearful ordeal before us. I could do nothing, however, so I plucked up what heart I could and said that we had better hasten, as the afternoon was passing....\n\nWe found the child awake. It had had a sleep and taken some food, and altogether was going on well. Dr. Vincent took the bandage from its throat, and showed us the punctures. There was no mistaking the similarity to those which had been on Lucy’s throat. They were smaller, and the edges looked fresher; that was all. We asked Vincent to what he attributed them, and he replied that it must have been a bite of some animal, perhaps a rat; but, for his own part, he was inclined to think that it was one of the bats which are so numerous on the northern heights of London. “Out of so many harmless ones,” he said, “there may be some wild specimen from the South of a more malignant species. Some sailor may have brought one home, and it managed to escape; or even from the Zoölogical Gardens a young one may have got loose, or one be bred there from a vampire. These things do occur, you know. Only ten days ago a wolf got out, and was, I believe, traced up in this direction. For a week after, the children were playing nothing but Red Riding Hood on the Heath and in every alley in the place until this ‘bloofer lady’ scare came along, since when it has been quite a gala-time with them. Even this poor little mite, when he woke up to-day, asked the nurse if he might go away. When she asked him why he wanted to go, he said he wanted to play with the ‘bloofer lady.’”\n\n“I hope,” said Van Helsing, “that when you are sending the child home you will caution its parents to keep strict watch over it. These fancies to stray are most dangerous; and if the child were to remain out another night, it would probably be fatal. But in any case I suppose you will not let it away for some days?”\n\n“Certainly not, not for a week at least; longer if the wound is not healed.”\n\nOur visit to the hospital took more time than we had reckoned on, and the sun had dipped before we came out. When Van Helsing saw how dark it was, he said:--\n\n“There is no hurry. It is more late than I thought. Come, let us seek somewhere that we may eat, and then we shall go on our way.”\n\nWe dined at “Jack Straw’s Castle” along with a little crowd of bicyclists and others who were genially noisy. About ten o’clock we started from the inn. It was then very dark, and the scattered lamps made the darkness greater when we were once outside their individual radius. The Professor had evidently noted the road we were to go, for he went on unhesitatingly; but, as for me, I was in quite a mixup as to locality. As we went further, we met fewer and fewer people, till at last we were somewhat surprised when we met even the patrol of horse police going their usual suburban round. At last we reached the wall of the churchyard, which we climbed over. With some little difficulty--for it was very dark, and the whole place seemed so strange to us--we found the Westenra tomb. The Professor took the key, opened the creaky door, and standing back, politely, but quite unconsciously, motioned me to precede him. There was a delicious irony in the offer, in the courtliness of giving preference on such a ghastly occasion. My companion followed me quickly, and cautiously drew the door to, after carefully ascertaining that the lock was a falling, and not a spring, one. In the latter case we should have been in a bad plight. Then he fumbled in his bag, and taking out a matchbox and a piece of candle, proceeded to make a light. The tomb in the day-time, and when wreathed with fresh flowers, had looked grim and gruesome enough; but now, some days afterwards, when the flowers hung lank and dead, their whites turning to rust and their greens to browns; when the spider and the beetle had resumed their accustomed dominance; when time-discoloured stone, and dust-encrusted mortar, and rusty, dank iron, and tarnished brass, and clouded silver-plating gave back the feeble glimmer of a candle, the effect was more miserable and sordid than could have been imagined. It conveyed irresistibly the idea that life--animal life--was not the only thing which could pass away.\n\nVan Helsing went about his work systematically. Holding his candle so that he could read the coffin plates, and so holding it that the sperm dropped in white patches which congealed as they touched the metal, he made assurance of Lucy’s coffin. Another search in his bag, and he took out a turnscrew.\n\n“What are you going to do?” I asked.\n\n“To open the coffin. You shall yet be convinced.” Straightway he began taking out the screws, and finally lifted off the lid, showing the casing of lead beneath. The sight was almost too much for me. It seemed to be as much an affront to the dead as it would have been to have stripped off her clothing in her sleep whilst living; I actually took hold of his hand to stop him. He only said: “You shall see,” and again fumbling in his bag, took out a tiny fret-saw. Striking the turnscrew through the lead with a swift downward stab, which made me wince, he made a small hole, which was, however, big enough to admit the point of the saw. I had expected a rush of gas from the week-old corpse. We doctors, who have had to study our dangers, have to become accustomed to such things, and I drew back towards the door. But the Professor never stopped for a moment; he sawed down a couple of feet along one side of the lead coffin, and then across, and down the other side. Taking the edge of the loose flange, he bent it back towards the foot of the coffin, and holding up the candle into the aperture, motioned to me to look.\n\nI drew near and looked. The coffin was empty.\n\nIt was certainly a surprise to me, and gave me a considerable shock, but Van Helsing was unmoved. He was now more sure than ever of his ground, and so emboldened to proceed in his task. “Are you satisfied now, friend John?” he asked.\n\nI felt all the dogged argumentativeness of my nature awake within me as I answered him:--\n\n“I am satisfied that Lucy’s body is not in that coffin; but that only proves one thing.”\n\n“And what is that, friend John?”\n\n“That it is not there.”\n\n“That is good logic,” he said, “so far as it goes. But how do you--how can you--account for it not being there?”\n\n“Perhaps a body-snatcher,” I suggested. “Some of the undertaker’s people may have stolen it.” I felt that I was speaking folly, and yet it was the only real cause which I could suggest. The Professor sighed. “Ah well!” he said, “we must have more proof. Come with me.”\n\nHe put on the coffin-lid again, gathered up all his things and placed them in the bag, blew out the light, and placed the candle also in the bag. We opened the door, and went out. Behind us he closed the door and locked it. He handed me the key, saying: “Will you keep it? You had better be assured.” I laughed--it was not a very cheerful laugh, I am bound to say--as I motioned him to keep it. “A key is nothing,” I said; “there may be duplicates; and anyhow it is not difficult to pick a lock of that kind.” He said nothing, but put the key in his pocket. Then he told me to watch at one side of the churchyard whilst he would watch at the other. I took up my place behind a yew-tree, and I saw his dark figure move until the intervening headstones and trees hid it from my sight.\n\nIt was a lonely vigil. Just after I had taken my place I heard a distant clock strike twelve, and in time came one and two. I was chilled and unnerved, and angry with the Professor for taking me on such an errand and with myself for coming. I was too cold and too sleepy to be keenly observant, and not sleepy enough to betray my trust so altogether I had a dreary, miserable time.\n\nSuddenly, as I turned round, I thought I saw something like a white streak, moving between two dark yew-trees at the side of the churchyard farthest from the tomb; at the same time a dark mass moved from the Professor’s side of the ground, and hurriedly went towards it. Then I too moved; but I had to go round headstones and railed-off tombs, and I stumbled over graves. The sky was overcast, and somewhere far off an early cock crew. A little way off, beyond a line of scattered juniper-trees, which marked the pathway to the church, a white, dim figure flitted in the direction of the tomb. The tomb itself was hidden by trees, and I could not see where the figure disappeared. I heard the rustle of actual movement where I had first seen the white figure, and coming over, found the Professor holding in his arms a tiny child. When he saw me he held it out to me, and said:--\n\n“Are you satisfied now?”\n\n“No,” I said, in a way that I felt was aggressive.\n\n“Do you not see the child?”\n\n“Yes, it is a child, but who brought it here? And is it wounded?” I asked.\n\n“We shall see,” said the Professor, and with one impulse we took our way out of the churchyard, he carrying the sleeping child.\n\nWhen we had got some little distance away, we went into a clump of trees, and struck a match, and looked at the child’s throat. It was without a scratch or scar of any kind.\n\n“Was I right?” I asked triumphantly.\n\n“We were just in time,” said the Professor thankfully.\n\nWe had now to decide what we were to do with the child, and so consulted about it. If we were to take it to a police-station we should have to give some account of our movements during the night; at least, we should have had to make some statement as to how we had come to find the child. So finally we decided that we would take it to the Heath, and when we heard a policeman coming, would leave it where he could not fail to find it; we would then seek our way home as quickly as we could. All fell out well. At the edge of Hampstead Heath we heard a policeman’s heavy tramp, and laying the child on the pathway, we waited and watched until he saw it as he flashed his lantern to and fro. We heard his exclamation of astonishment, and then we went away silently. By good chance we got a cab near the “Spaniards,” and drove to town.\n\nI cannot sleep, so I make this entry. But I must try to get a few hours’ sleep, as Van Helsing is to call for me at noon. He insists that I shall go with him on another expedition.\n\n27 September.--It was two o’clock before we found a suitable opportunity for our attempt. The funeral held at noon was all completed, and the last stragglers of the mourners had taken themselves lazily away, when, looking carefully from behind a clump of alder-trees, we saw the sexton lock the gate after him. We knew then that we were safe till morning did we desire it; but the Professor told me that we should not want more than an hour at most. Again I felt that horrid sense of the reality of things, in which any effort of imagination seemed out of place; and I realised distinctly the perils of the law which we were incurring in our unhallowed work. Besides, I felt it was all so useless. Outrageous as it was to open a leaden coffin, to see if a woman dead nearly a week were really dead, it now seemed the height of folly to open the tomb again, when we knew, from the evidence of our own eyesight, that the coffin was empty. I shrugged my shoulders, however, and rested silent, for Van Helsing had a way of going on his own road, no matter who remonstrated. He took the key, opened the vault, and again courteously motioned me to precede. The place was not so gruesome as last night, but oh, how unutterably mean-looking when the sunshine streamed in. Van Helsing walked over to Lucy’s coffin, and I followed. He bent over and again forced back the leaden flange; and then a shock of surprise and dismay shot through me.\n\nThere lay Lucy, seemingly just as we had seen her the night before her funeral. She was, if possible, more radiantly beautiful than ever; and I could not believe that she was dead. The lips were red, nay redder than before; and on the cheeks was a delicate bloom.\n\n“Is this a juggle?” I said to him.\n\n“Are you convinced now?” said the Professor in response, and as he spoke he put over his hand, and in a way that made me shudder, pulled back the dead lips and showed the white teeth.\n\n“See,” he went on, “see, they are even sharper than before. With this and this”--and he touched one of the canine teeth and that below it--“the little children can be bitten. Are you of belief now, friend John?” Once more, argumentative hostility woke within me. I could not accept such an overwhelming idea as he suggested; so, with an attempt to argue of which I was even at the moment ashamed, I said:--\n\n“She may have been placed here since last night.”\n\n“Indeed? That is so, and by whom?”\n\n“I do not know. Some one has done it.”\n\n“And yet she has been dead one week. Most peoples in that time would not look so.” I had no answer for this, so was silent. Van Helsing did not seem to notice my silence; at any rate, he showed neither chagrin nor triumph. He was looking intently at the face of the dead woman, raising the eyelids and looking at the eyes, and once more opening the lips and examining the teeth. Then he turned to me and said:--\n\n“Here, there is one thing which is different from all recorded; here is some dual life that is not as the common. She was bitten by the vampire when she was in a trance, sleep-walking--oh, you start; you do not know that, friend John, but you shall know it all later--and in trance could he best come to take more blood. In trance she died, and in trance she is Un-Dead, too. So it is that she differ from all other. Usually when the Un-Dead sleep at home”--as he spoke he made a comprehensive sweep of his arm to designate what to a vampire was “home”--“their face show what they are, but this so sweet that was when she not Un-Dead she go back to the nothings of the common dead. There is no malign there, see, and so it make hard that I must kill her in her sleep.” This turned my blood cold, and it began to dawn upon me that I was accepting Van Helsing’s theories; but if she were really dead, what was there of terror in the idea of killing her? He looked up at me, and evidently saw the change in my face, for he said almost joyously:--\n\n“Ah, you believe now?”\n\nI answered: “Do not press me too hard all at once. I am willing to accept. How will you do this bloody work?”\n\n“I shall cut off her head and fill her mouth with garlic, and I shall drive a stake through her body.” It made me shudder to think of so mutilating the body of the woman whom I had loved. And yet the feeling was not so strong as I had expected. I was, in fact, beginning to shudder at the presence of this being, this Un-Dead, as Van Helsing called it, and to loathe it. Is it possible that love is all subjective, or all objective?\n\nI waited a considerable time for Van Helsing to begin, but he stood as if wrapped in thought. Presently he closed the catch of his bag with a snap, and said:--\n\n“I have been thinking, and have made up my mind as to what is best. If I did simply follow my inclining I would do now, at this moment, what is to be done; but there are other things to follow, and things that are thousand times more difficult in that them we do not know. This is simple. She have yet no life taken, though that is of time; and to act now would be to take danger from her for ever. But then we may have to want Arthur, and how shall we tell him of this? If you, who saw the wounds on Lucy’s throat, and saw the wounds so similar on the child’s at the hospital; if you, who saw the coffin empty last night and full to-day with a woman who have not change only to be more rose and more beautiful in a whole week, after she die--if you know of this and know of the white figure last night that brought the child to the churchyard, and yet of your own senses you did not believe, how, then, can I expect Arthur, who know none of those things, to believe? He doubted me when I took him from her kiss when she was dying. I know he has forgiven me because in some mistaken idea I have done things that prevent him say good-bye as he ought; and he may think that in some more mistaken idea this woman was buried alive; and that in most mistake of all we have killed her. He will then argue back that it is we, mistaken ones, that have killed her by our ideas; and so he will be much unhappy always. Yet he never can be sure; and that is the worst of all. And he will sometimes think that she he loved was buried alive, and that will paint his dreams with horrors of what she must have suffered; and again, he will think that we may be right, and that his so beloved was, after all, an Un-Dead. No! I told him once, and since then I learn much. Now, since I know it is all true, a hundred thousand times more do I know that he must pass through the bitter waters to reach the sweet. He, poor fellow, must have one hour that will make the very face of heaven grow black to him; then we can act for good all round and send him peace. My mind is made up. Let us go. You return home for to-night to your asylum, and see that all be well. As for me, I shall spend the night here in this churchyard in my own way. To-morrow night you will come to me to the Berkeley Hotel at ten of the clock. I shall send for Arthur to come too, and also that so fine young man of America that gave his blood. Later we shall all have work to do. I come with you so far as Piccadilly and there dine, for I must be back here before the sun set.”\n\nSo we locked the tomb and came away, and got over the wall of the churchyard, which was not much of a task, and drove back to Piccadilly.\n\nNote left by Van Helsing in his portmanteau, Berkeley Hotel directed to John Seward, M. D.\n\n(Not delivered.)\n\n“27 September.\n\n“Friend John,--\n\n“I write this in case anything should happen. I go alone to watch in that churchyard. It pleases me that the Un-Dead, Miss Lucy, shall not leave to-night, that so on the morrow night she may be more eager. Therefore I shall fix some things she like not--garlic and a crucifix--and so seal up the door of the tomb. She is young as Un-Dead, and will heed. Moreover, these are only to prevent her coming out; they may not prevail on her wanting to get in; for then the Un-Dead is desperate, and must find the line of least resistance, whatsoever it may be. I shall be at hand all the night from sunset till after the sunrise, and if there be aught that may be learned I shall learn it. For Miss Lucy or from her, I have no fear; but that other to whom is there that she is Un-Dead, he have now the power to seek her tomb and find shelter. He is cunning, as I know from Mr. Jonathan and from the way that all along he have fooled us when he played with us for Miss Lucy’s life, and we lost; and in many ways the Un-Dead are strong. He have always the strength in his hand of twenty men; even we four who gave our strength to Miss Lucy it also is all to him. Besides, he can summon his wolf and I know not what. So if it be that he come thither on this night he shall find me; but none other shall--until it be too late. But it may be that he will not attempt the place. There is no reason why he should; his hunting ground is more full of game than the churchyard where the Un-Dead woman sleep, and the one old man watch.\n\n“Therefore I write this in case.... Take the papers that are with this, the diaries of Harker and the rest, and read them, and then find this great Un-Dead, and cut off his head and burn his heart or drive a stake through it, so that the world may rest from him.\n\n“If it be so, farewell.\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n28 September.--It is wonderful what a good night’s sleep will do for one. Yesterday I was almost willing to accept Van Helsing’s monstrous ideas; but now they seem to start out lurid before me as outrages on common sense. I have no doubt that he believes it all. I wonder if his mind can have become in any way unhinged. Surely there must be some rational explanation of all these mysterious things. Is it possible that the Professor can have done it himself? He is so abnormally clever that if he went off his head he would carry out his intent with regard to some fixed idea in a wonderful way. I am loath to think it, and indeed it would be almost as great a marvel as the other to find that Van Helsing was mad; but anyhow I shall watch him carefully. I may get some light on the mystery.\n\n29 September, morning..... Last night, at a little before ten o’clock, Arthur and Quincey came into Van Helsing’s room; he told us all that he wanted us to do, but especially addressing himself to Arthur, as if all our wills were centred in his. He began by saying that he hoped we would all come with him too, “for,” he said, “there is a grave duty to be done there. You were doubtless surprised at my letter?” This query was directly addressed to Lord Godalming.\n\n“I was. It rather upset me for a bit. There has been so much trouble around my house of late that I could do without any more. I have been curious, too, as to what you mean. Quincey and I talked it over; but the more we talked, the more puzzled we got, till now I can say for myself that I’m about up a tree as to any meaning about anything.”\n\n“Me too,” said Quincey Morris laconically.\n\n“Oh,” said the Professor, “then you are nearer the beginning, both of you, than friend John here, who has to go a long way back before he can even get so far as to begin.”\n\nIt was evident that he recognised my return to my old doubting frame of mind without my saying a word. Then, turning to the other two, he said with intense gravity:--\n\n“I want your permission to do what I think good this night. It is, I know, much to ask; and when you know what it is I propose to do you will know, and only then, how much. Therefore may I ask that you promise me in the dark, so that afterwards, though you may be angry with me for a time--I must not disguise from myself the possibility that such may be--you shall not blame yourselves for anything.”\n\n“That’s frank anyhow,” broke in Quincey. “I’ll answer for the Professor. I don’t quite see his drift, but I swear he’s honest; and that’s good enough for me.”\n\n“I thank you, sir,” said Van Helsing proudly. “I have done myself the honour of counting you one trusting friend, and such endorsement is dear to me.” He held out a hand, which Quincey took.\n\nThen Arthur spoke out:--\n\n“Dr. Van Helsing, I don’t quite like to ‘buy a pig in a poke,’ as they say in Scotland, and if it be anything in which my honour as a gentleman or my faith as a Christian is concerned, I cannot make such a promise. If you can assure me that what you intend does not violate either of these two, then I give my consent at once; though for the life of me, I cannot understand what you are driving at.”\n\n“I accept your limitation,” said Van Helsing, “and all I ask of you is that if you feel it necessary to condemn any act of mine, you will first consider it well and be satisfied that it does not violate your reservations.”\n\n“Agreed!” said Arthur; “that is only fair. And now that the pourparlers are over, may I ask what it is we are to do?”\n\n“I want you to come with me, and to come in secret, to the churchyard at Kingstead.”\n\nArthur’s face fell as he said in an amazed sort of way:--\n\n“Where poor Lucy is buried?” The Professor bowed. Arthur went on: “And when there?”\n\n“To enter the tomb!” Arthur stood up.\n\n“Professor, are you in earnest; or it is some monstrous joke? Pardon me, I see that you are in earnest.” He sat down again, but I could see that he sat firmly and proudly, as one who is on his dignity. There was silence until he asked again:--\n\n“And when in the tomb?”\n\n“To open the coffin.”\n\n“This is too much!” he said, angrily rising again. “I am willing to be patient in all things that are reasonable; but in this--this desecration of the grave--of one who----” He fairly choked with indignation. The Professor looked pityingly at him.\n\n“If I could spare you one pang, my poor friend,” he said, “God knows I would. But this night our feet must tread in thorny paths; or later, and for ever, the feet you love must walk in paths of flame!”\n\nArthur looked up with set white face and said:--\n\n“Take care, sir, take care!”\n\n“Would it not be well to hear what I have to say?” said Van Helsing. “And then you will at least know the limit of my purpose. Shall I go on?”\n\n“That’s fair enough,” broke in Morris.\n\nAfter a pause Van Helsing went on, evidently with an effort:--\n\n“Miss Lucy is dead; is it not so? Yes! Then there can be no wrong to her. But if she be not dead----”\n\nArthur jumped to his feet.\n\n“Good God!” he cried. “What do you mean? Has there been any mistake; has she been buried alive?” He groaned in anguish that not even hope could soften.\n\n“I did not say she was alive, my child; I did not think it. I go no further than to say that she might be Un-Dead.”\n\n“Un-Dead! Not alive! What do you mean? Is this all a nightmare, or what is it?”\n\n“There are mysteries which men can only guess at, which age by age they may solve only in part. Believe me, we are now on the verge of one. But I have not done. May I cut off the head of dead Miss Lucy?”\n\n“Heavens and earth, no!” cried Arthur in a storm of passion. “Not for the wide world will I consent to any mutilation of her dead body. Dr. Van Helsing, you try me too far. What have I done to you that you should torture me so? What did that poor, sweet girl do that you should want to cast such dishonour on her grave? Are you mad to speak such things, or am I mad to listen to them? Don’t dare to think more of such a desecration; I shall not give my consent to anything you do. I have a duty to do in protecting her grave from outrage; and, by God, I shall do it!”\n\nVan Helsing rose up from where he had all the time been seated, and said, gravely and sternly:--\n\n“My Lord Godalming, I, too, have a duty to do, a duty to others, a duty to you, a duty to the dead; and, by God, I shall do it! All I ask you now is that you come with me, that you look and listen; and if when later I make the same request you do not be more eager for its fulfilment even than I am, then--then I shall do my duty, whatever it may seem to me. And then, to follow of your Lordship’s wishes I shall hold myself at your disposal to render an account to you, when and where you will.” His voice broke a little, and he went on with a voice full of pity:--\n\n“But, I beseech you, do not go forth in anger with me. In a long life of acts which were often not pleasant to do, and which sometimes did wring my heart, I have never had so heavy a task as now. Believe me that if the time comes for you to change your mind towards me, one look from you will wipe away all this so sad hour, for I would do what a man can to save you from sorrow. Just think. For why should I give myself so much of labour and so much of sorrow? I have come here from my own land to do what I can of good; at the first to please my friend John, and then to help a sweet young lady, whom, too, I came to love. For her--I am ashamed to say so much, but I say it in kindness--I gave what you gave; the blood of my veins; I gave it, I, who was not, like you, her lover, but only her physician and her friend. I gave to her my nights and days--before death, after death; and if my death can do her good even now, when she is the dead Un-Dead, she shall have it freely.” He said this with a very grave, sweet pride, and Arthur was much affected by it. He took the old man’s hand and said in a broken voice:--\n\n“Oh, it is hard to think of it, and I cannot understand; but at least I shall go with you and wait.”\n\nDR. SEWARD’S DIARY--continued\nIt was just a quarter before twelve o’clock when we got into the churchyard over the low wall. The night was dark with occasional gleams of moonlight between the rents of the heavy clouds that scudded across the sky. We all kept somehow close together, with Van Helsing slightly in front as he led the way. When we had come close to the tomb I looked well at Arthur, for I feared that the proximity to a place laden with so sorrowful a memory would upset him; but he bore himself well. I took it that the very mystery of the proceeding was in some way a counteractant to his grief. The Professor unlocked the door, and seeing a natural hesitation amongst us for various reasons, solved the difficulty by entering first himself. The rest of us followed, and he closed the door. He then lit a dark lantern and pointed to the coffin. Arthur stepped forward hesitatingly; Van Helsing said to me:--\n\n“You were with me here yesterday. Was the body of Miss Lucy in that coffin?”\n\n“It was.” The Professor turned to the rest saying:--\n\n“You hear; and yet there is no one who does not believe with me.” He took his screwdriver and again took off the lid of the coffin. Arthur looked on, very pale but silent; when the lid was removed he stepped forward. He evidently did not know that there was a leaden coffin, or, at any rate, had not thought of it. When he saw the rent in the lead, the blood rushed to his face for an instant, but as quickly fell away again, so that he remained of a ghastly whiteness; he was still silent. Van Helsing forced back the leaden flange, and we all looked in and recoiled.\n\nThe coffin was empty!\n\nFor several minutes no one spoke a word. The silence was broken by Quincey Morris:--\n\n“Professor, I answered for you. Your word is all I want. I wouldn’t ask such a thing ordinarily--I wouldn’t so dishonour you as to imply a doubt; but this is a mystery that goes beyond any honour or dishonour. Is this your doing?”\n\n“I swear to you by all that I hold sacred that I have not removed nor touched her. What happened was this: Two nights ago my friend Seward and I came here--with good purpose, believe me. I opened that coffin, which was then sealed up, and we found it, as now, empty. We then waited, and saw something white come through the trees. The next day we came here in day-time, and she lay there. Did she not, friend John?”\n“Yes.”\n\n“That night we were just in time. One more so small child was missing, and we find it, thank God, unharmed amongst the graves. Yesterday I came here before sundown, for at sundown the Un-Dead can move. I waited here all the night till the sun rose, but I saw nothing. It was most probable that it was because I had laid over the clamps of those doors garlic, which the Un-Dead cannot bear, and other things which they shun. Last night there was no exodus, so to-night before the sundown I took away my garlic and other things. And so it is we find this coffin empty. But bear with me. So far there is much that is strange. Wait you with me outside, unseen and unheard, and things much stranger are yet to be. So”--here he shut the dark slide of his lantern--“now to the outside.” He opened the door, and we filed out, he coming last and locking the door behind him.\n\nOh! but it seemed fresh and pure in the night air after the terror of that vault. How sweet it was to see the clouds race by, and the passing gleams of the moonlight between the scudding clouds crossing and passing--like the gladness and sorrow of a man’s life; how sweet it was to breathe the fresh air, that had no taint of death and decay; how humanising to see the red lighting of the sky beyond the hill, and to hear far away the muffled roar that marks the life of a great city. Each in his own way was solemn and overcome. Arthur was silent, and was, I could see, striving to grasp the purpose and the inner meaning of the mystery. I was myself tolerably patient, and half inclined again to throw aside doubt and to accept Van Helsing’s conclusions. Quincey Morris was phlegmatic in the way of a man who accepts all things, and accepts them in the spirit of cool bravery, with hazard of all he has to stake. Not being able to smoke, he cut himself a good-sized plug of tobacco and began to chew. As to Van Helsing, he was employed in a definite way. First he took from his bag a mass of what looked like thin, wafer-like biscuit, which was carefully rolled up in a white napkin; next he took out a double-handful of some whitish stuff, like dough or putty. He crumbled the wafer up fine and worked it into the mass between his hands. This he then took, and rolling it into thin strips, began to lay them into the crevices between the door and its setting in the tomb. I was somewhat puzzled at this, and being close, asked him what it was that he was doing. Arthur and Quincey drew near also, as they too were curious. He answered:--\n\n“I am closing the tomb, so that the Un-Dead may not enter.”\n\n“And is that stuff you have put there going to do it?” asked Quincey. “Great Scott! Is this a game?”\n“It is.”\n\n“What is that which you are using?” This time the question was by Arthur. Van Helsing reverently lifted his hat as he answered:--\n\n“The Host. I brought it from Amsterdam. I have an Indulgence.” It was an answer that appalled the most sceptical of us, and we felt individually that in the presence of such earnest purpose as the Professor’s, a purpose which could thus use the to him most sacred of things, it was impossible to distrust. In respectful silence we took the places assigned to us close round the tomb, but hidden from the sight of any one approaching. I pitied the others, especially Arthur. I had myself been apprenticed by my former visits to this watching horror; and yet I, who had up to an hour ago repudiated the proofs, felt my heart sink within me. Never did tombs look so ghastly white; never did cypress, or yew, or juniper so seem the embodiment of funereal gloom; never did tree or grass wave or rustle so ominously; never did bough creak so mysteriously; and never did the far-away howling of dogs send such a woeful presage through the night.\n\nThere was a long spell of silence, a big, aching void, and then from the Professor a keen “S-s-s-s!” He pointed; and far down the avenue of yews we saw a white figure advance--a dim white figure, which held something dark at its breast. The figure stopped, and at the moment a ray of moonlight fell upon the masses of driving clouds and showed in startling prominence a dark-haired woman, dressed in the cerements of the grave. We could not see the face, for it was bent down over what we saw to be a fair-haired child. There was a pause and a sharp little cry, such as a child gives in sleep, or a dog as it lies before the fire and dreams. We were starting forward, but the Professor’s warning hand, seen by us as he stood behind a yew-tree, kept us back; and then as we looked the white figure moved forwards again. It was now near enough for us to see clearly, and the moonlight still held. My own heart grew cold as ice, and I could hear the gasp of Arthur, as we recognised the features of Lucy Westenra. Lucy Westenra, but yet how changed. The sweetness was turned to adamantine, heartless cruelty, and the purity to voluptuous wantonness. Van Helsing stepped out, and, obedient to his gesture, we all advanced too; the four of us ranged in a line before the door of the tomb. Van Helsing raised his lantern and drew the slide; by the concentrated light that fell on Lucy’s face we could see that the lips were crimson with fresh blood, and that the stream had trickled over her chin and stained the purity of her lawn death-robe.\n\nWe shuddered with horror. I could see by the tremulous light that even Van Helsing’s iron nerve had failed. Arthur was next to me, and if I had not seized his arm and held him up, he would have fallen.\n\nWhen Lucy--I call the thing that was before us Lucy because it bore her shape--saw us she drew back with an angry snarl, such as a cat gives when taken unawares; then her eyes ranged over us. Lucy’s eyes in form and colour; but Lucy’s eyes unclean and full of hell-fire, instead of the pure, gentle orbs we knew. At that moment the remnant of my love passed into hate and loathing; had she then to be killed, I could have done it with savage delight. As she looked, her eyes blazed with unholy light, and the face became wreathed with a voluptuous smile. Oh, God, how it made me shudder to see it! With a careless motion, she flung to the ground, callous as a devil, the child that up to now she had clutched strenuously to her breast, growling over it as a dog growls over a bone. The child gave a sharp cry, and lay there moaning. There was a cold-bloodedness in the act which wrung a groan from Arthur; when she advanced to him with outstretched arms and a wanton smile he fell back and hid his face in his hands.\n\nShe still advanced, however, and with a languorous, voluptuous grace, said:--\n\n“Come to me, Arthur. Leave these others and come to me. My arms are hungry for you. Come, and we can rest together. Come, my husband, come!”\n\nThere was something diabolically sweet in her tones--something of the tingling of glass when struck--which rang through the brains even of us who heard the words addressed to another. As for Arthur, he seemed under a spell; moving his hands from his face, he opened wide his arms. She was leaping for them, when Van Helsing sprang forward and held between them his little golden crucifix. She recoiled from it, and, with a suddenly distorted face, full of rage, dashed past him as if to enter the tomb.\n\nWhen within a foot or two of the door, however, she stopped, as if arrested by some irresistible force. Then she turned, and her face was shown in the clear burst of moonlight and by the lamp, which had now no quiver from Van Helsing’s iron nerves. Never did I see such baffled malice on a face; and never, I trust, shall such ever be seen again by mortal eyes. The beautiful colour became livid, the eyes seemed to throw out sparks of hell-fire, the brows were wrinkled as though the folds of the flesh were the coils of Medusa’s snakes, and the lovely, blood-stained mouth grew to an open square, as in the passion masks of the Greeks and Japanese. If ever a face meant death--if looks could kill--we saw it at that moment.\n\nAnd so for full half a minute, which seemed an eternity, she remained between the lifted crucifix and the sacred closing of her means of entry. Van Helsing broke the silence by asking Arthur:--\n\n“Answer me, oh my friend! Am I to proceed in my work?”\n\nArthur threw himself on his knees, and hid his face in his hands, as he answered:--\n\n“Do as you will, friend; do as you will. There can be no horror like this ever any more;” and he groaned in spirit. Quincey and I simultaneously moved towards him, and took his arms. We could hear the click of the closing lantern as Van Helsing held it down; coming close to the tomb, he began to remove from the chinks some of the sacred emblem which he had placed there. We all looked on in horrified amazement as we saw, when he stood back, the woman, with a corporeal body as real at that moment as our own, pass in through the interstice where scarce a knife-blade could have gone. We all felt a glad sense of relief when we saw the Professor calmly restoring the strings of putty to the edges of the door.\n\nWhen this was done, he lifted the child and said:\n\n“Come now, my friends; we can do no more till to-morrow. There is a funeral at noon, so here we shall all come before long after that. The friends of the dead will all be gone by two, and when the sexton lock the gate we shall remain. Then there is more to do; but not like this of to-night. As for this little one, he is not much harm, and by to-morrow night he shall be well. We shall leave him where the police will find him, as on the other night; and then to home.” Coming close to Arthur, he said:--\n\n“My friend Arthur, you have had a sore trial; but after, when you look back, you will see how it was necessary. You are now in the bitter waters, my child. By this time to-morrow you will, please God, have passed them, and have drunk of the sweet waters; so do not mourn overmuch. Till then I shall not ask you to forgive me.”\n\nArthur and Quincey came home with me, and we tried to cheer each other on the way. We had left the child in safety, and were tired; so we all slept with more or less reality of sleep.\n\n29 September, night.--A little before twelve o’clock we three--Arthur, Quincey Morris, and myself--called for the Professor. It was odd to notice that by common consent we had all put on black clothes. Of course, Arthur wore black, for he was in deep mourning, but the rest of us wore it by instinct. We got to the churchyard by half-past one, and strolled about, keeping out of official observation, so that when the gravediggers had completed their task and the sexton under the belief that every one had gone, had locked the gate, we had the place all to ourselves. Van Helsing, instead of his little black bag, had with him a long leather one, something like a cricketing bag; it was manifestly of fair weight.\n\nWhen we were alone and had heard the last of the footsteps die out up the road, we silently, and as if by ordered intention, followed the Professor to the tomb. He unlocked the door, and we entered, closing it behind us. Then he took from his bag the lantern, which he lit, and also two wax candles, which, when lighted, he stuck, by melting their own ends, on other coffins, so that they might give light sufficient to work by. When he again lifted the lid off Lucy’s coffin we all looked--Arthur trembling like an aspen--and saw that the body lay there in all its death-beauty. But there was no love in my own heart, nothing but loathing for the foul Thing which had taken Lucy’s shape without her soul. I could see even Arthur’s face grow hard as he looked. Presently he said to Van Helsing:--\n\n“Is this really Lucy’s body, or only a demon in her shape?”\n\n“It is her body, and yet not it. But wait a while, and you all see her as she was, and is.”\n\nShe seemed like a nightmare of Lucy as she lay there; the pointed teeth, the bloodstained, voluptuous mouth--which it made one shudder to see--the whole carnal and unspiritual appearance, seeming like a devilish mockery of Lucy’s sweet purity. Van Helsing, with his usual methodicalness, began taking the various contents from his bag and placing them ready for use. First he took out a soldering iron and some plumbing solder, and then a small oil-lamp, which gave out, when lit in a corner of the tomb, gas which burned at fierce heat with a blue flame; then his operating knives, which he placed to hand; and last a round wooden stake, some two and a half or three inches thick and about three feet long. One end of it was hardened by charring in the fire, and was sharpened to a fine point. With this stake came a heavy hammer, such as in households is used in the coal-cellar for breaking the lumps. To me, a doctor’s preparations for work of any kind are stimulating and bracing, but the effect of these things on both Arthur and Quincey was to cause them a sort of consternation. They both, however, kept their courage, and remained silent and quiet.\n\nWhen all was ready, Van Helsing said:--\n\n“Before we do anything, let me tell you this; it is out of the lore and experience of the ancients and of all those who have studied the powers of the Un-Dead. When they become such, there comes with the change the curse of immortality; they cannot die, but must go on age after age adding new victims and multiplying the evils of the world; for all that die from the preying of the Un-Dead becomes themselves Un-Dead, and prey on their kind. And so the circle goes on ever widening, like as the ripples from a stone thrown in the water. Friend Arthur, if you had met that kiss which you know of before poor Lucy die; or again, last night when you open your arms to her, you would in time, when you had died, have become nosferatu, as they call it in Eastern Europe, and would all time make more of those Un-Deads that so have fill us with horror. The career of this so unhappy dear lady is but just begun. Those children whose blood she suck are not as yet so much the worse; but if she live on, Un-Dead, more and more they lose their blood and by her power over them they come to her; and so she draw their blood with that so wicked mouth. But if she die in truth, then all cease; the tiny wounds of the throats disappear, and they go back to their plays unknowing ever of what has been. But of the most blessed of all, when this now Un-Dead be made to rest as true dead, then the soul of the poor lady whom we love shall again be free. Instead of working wickedness by night and growing more debased in the assimilating of it by day, she shall take her place with the other Angels. So that, my friend, it will be a blessed hand for her that shall strike the blow that sets her free. To this I am willing; but is there none amongst us who has a better right? Will it be no joy to think of hereafter in the silence of the night when sleep is not: ‘It was my hand that sent her to the stars; it was the hand of him that loved her best; the hand that of all she would herself have chosen, had it been to her to choose?’ Tell me if there be such a one amongst us?”\n\nWe all looked at Arthur. He saw, too, what we all did, the infinite kindness which suggested that his should be the hand which would restore Lucy to us as a holy, and not an unholy, memory; he stepped forward and said bravely, though his hand trembled, and his face was as pale as snow:--\n\n“My true friend, from the bottom of my broken heart I thank you. Tell me what I am to do, and I shall not falter!” Van Helsing laid a hand on his shoulder, and said:--\n\n“Brave lad! A moment’s courage, and it is done. This stake must be driven through her. It will be a fearful ordeal--be not deceived in that--but it will be only a short time, and you will then rejoice more than your pain was great; from this grim tomb you will emerge as though you tread on air. But you must not falter when once you have begun. Only think that we, your true friends, are round you, and that we pray for you all the time.”\n\n“Go on,” said Arthur hoarsely. “Tell me what I am to do.”\n\n“Take this stake in your left hand, ready to place the point over the heart, and the hammer in your right. Then when we begin our prayer for the dead--I shall read him, I have here the book, and the others shall follow--strike in God’s name, that so all may be well with the dead that we love and that the Un-Dead pass away.”\n\nArthur took the stake and the hammer, and when once his mind was set on action his hands never trembled nor even quivered. Van Helsing opened his missal and began to read, and Quincey and I followed as well as we could. Arthur placed the point over the heart, and as I looked I could see its dint in the white flesh. Then he struck with all his might.\n\nThe Thing in the coffin writhed; and a hideous, blood-curdling screech came from the opened red lips. The body shook and quivered and twisted in wild contortions; the sharp white teeth champed together till the lips were cut, and the mouth was smeared with a crimson foam. But Arthur never faltered. He looked like a figure of Thor as his untrembling arm rose and fell, driving deeper and deeper the mercy-bearing stake, whilst the blood from the pierced heart welled and spurted up around it. His face was set, and high duty seemed to shine through it; the sight of it gave us courage so that our voices seemed to ring through the little vault.\n\nAnd then the writhing and quivering of the body became less, and the teeth seemed to champ, and the face to quiver. Finally it lay still. The terrible task was over.\n\nThe hammer fell from Arthur’s hand. He reeled and would have fallen had we not caught him. The great drops of sweat sprang from his forehead, and his breath came in broken gasps. It had indeed been an awful strain on him; and had he not been forced to his task by more than human considerations he could never have gone through with it. For a few minutes we were so taken up with him that we did not look towards the coffin. When we did, however, a murmur of startled surprise ran from one to the other of us. We gazed so eagerly that Arthur rose, for he had been seated on the ground, and came and looked too; and then a glad, strange light broke over his face and dispelled altogether the gloom of horror that lay upon it.\n\nThere, in the coffin lay no longer the foul Thing that we had so dreaded and grown to hate that the work of her destruction was yielded as a privilege to the one best entitled to it, but Lucy as we had seen her in her life, with her face of unequalled sweetness and purity. True that there were there, as we had seen them in life, the traces of care and pain and waste; but these were all dear to us, for they marked her truth to what we knew. One and all we felt that the holy calm that lay like sunshine over the wasted face and form was only an earthly token and symbol of the calm that was to reign for ever.\n\nVan Helsing came and laid his hand on Arthur’s shoulder, and said to him:--\n\n“And now, Arthur my friend, dear lad, am I not forgiven?”\n\nThe reaction of the terrible strain came as he took the old man’s hand in his, and raising it to his lips, pressed it, and said:--\n\n“Forgiven! God bless you that you have given my dear one her soul again, and me peace.” He put his hands on the Professor’s shoulder, and laying his head on his breast, cried for a while silently, whilst we stood unmoving. When he raised his head Van Helsing said to him:--\n\n“And now, my child, you may kiss her. Kiss her dead lips if you will, as she would have you to, if for her to choose. For she is not a grinning devil now--not any more a foul Thing for all eternity. No longer she is the devil’s Un-Dead. She is God’s true dead, whose soul is with Him!”\n\nArthur bent and kissed her, and then we sent him and Quincey out of the tomb; the Professor and I sawed the top off the stake, leaving the point of it in the body. Then we cut off the head and filled the mouth with garlic. We soldered up the leaden coffin, screwed on the coffin-lid, and gathering up our belongings, came away. When the Professor locked the door he gave the key to Arthur.\n\nOutside the air was sweet, the sun shone, and the birds sang, and it seemed as if all nature were tuned to a different pitch. There was gladness and mirth and peace everywhere, for we were at rest ourselves on one account, and we were glad, though it was with a tempered joy.\n\nBefore we moved away Van Helsing said:--\n\n“Now, my friends, one step of our work is done, one the most harrowing to ourselves. But there remains a greater task: to find out the author of all this our sorrow and to stamp him out. I have clues which we can follow; but it is a long task, and a difficult, and there is danger in it, and pain. Shall you not all help me? We have learned to believe, all of us--is it not so? And since so, do we not see our duty? Yes! And do we not promise to go on to the bitter end?”\n\nEach in turn, we took his hand, and the promise was made. Then said the Professor as we moved off:--\n\n“Two nights hence you shall meet with me and dine together at seven of the clock with friend John. I shall entreat two others, two that you know not as yet; and I shall be ready to all our work show and our plans unfold. Friend John, you come with me home, for I have much to consult about, and you can help me. To-night I leave for Amsterdam, but shall return to-morrow night. And then begins our great quest. But first I shall have much to say, so that you may know what is to do and to dread. Then our promise shall be made to each other anew; for there is a terrible task before us, and once our feet are on the ploughshare we must not draw back.”\n\nWhen we arrived at the Berkeley Hotel, Van Helsing found a telegram waiting for him:--\n\n“Am coming up by train. Jonathan at Whitby. Important news.--MINA HARKER.”\n\nThe Professor was delighted. “Ah, that wonderful Madam Mina,” he said, “pearl among women! She arrive, but I cannot stay. She must go to your house, friend John. You must meet her at the station. Telegraph her en route, so that she may be prepared.”\n\nWhen the wire was despatched he had a cup of tea; over it he told me of a diary kept by Jonathan Harker when abroad, and gave me a typewritten copy of it, as also of Mrs. Harker’s diary at Whitby. “Take these,” he said, “and study them well. When I have returned you will be master of all the facts, and we can then better enter on our inquisition. Keep them safe, for there is in them much of treasure. You will need all your faith, even you who have had such an experience as that of to-day. What is here told,” he laid his hand heavily and gravely on the packet of papers as he spoke, “may be the beginning of the end to you and me and many another; or it may sound the knell of the Un-Dead who walk the earth. Read all, I pray you, with the open mind; and if you can add in any way to the story here told do so, for it is all-important. You have kept diary of all these so strange things; is it not so? Yes! Then we shall go through all these together when we meet.” He then made ready for his departure, and shortly after drove off to Liverpool Street. I took my way to Paddington, where I arrived about fifteen minutes before the train came in.\n\nThe crowd melted away, after the bustling fashion common to arrival platforms; and I was beginning to feel uneasy, lest I might miss my guest, when a sweet-faced, dainty-looking girl stepped up to me, and, after a quick glance, said: “Dr. Seward, is it not?”\n\n“And you are Mrs. Harker!” I answered at once; whereupon she held out her hand.\n\n“I knew you from the description of poor dear Lucy; but----” She stopped suddenly, and a quick blush overspread her face.\n\nThe blush that rose to my own cheeks somehow set us both at ease, for it was a tacit answer to her own. I got her luggage, which included a typewriter, and we took the Underground to Fenchurch Street, after I had sent a wire to my housekeeper to have a sitting-room and bedroom prepared at once for Mrs. Harker.\n\nIn due time we arrived. She knew, of course, that the place was a lunatic asylum, but I could see that she was unable to repress a shudder when we entered.\n\nShe told me that, if she might, she would come presently to my study, as she had much to say. So here I am finishing my entry in my phonograph diary whilst I await her. As yet I have not had the chance of looking at the papers which Van Helsing left with me, though they lie open before me. I must get her interested in something, so that I may have an opportunity of reading them. She does not know how precious time is, or what a task we have in hand. I must be careful not to frighten her. Here she is!\n\nMina Harker’s Journal.\n\n29 September.--After I had tidied myself, I went down to Dr. Seward’s study. At the door I paused a moment, for I thought I heard him talking with some one. As, however, he had pressed me to be quick, I knocked at the door, and on his calling out, “Come in,” I entered.\n\nTo my intense surprise, there was no one with him. He was quite alone, and on the table opposite him was what I knew at once from the description to be a phonograph. I had never seen one, and was much interested.\n\n“I hope I did not keep you waiting,” I said; “but I stayed at the door as I heard you talking, and thought there was some one with you.”\n\n“Oh,” he replied with a smile, “I was only entering my diary.”\n\n“Your diary?” I asked him in surprise.\n\n“Yes,” he answered. “I keep it in this.” As he spoke he laid his hand on the phonograph. I felt quite excited over it, and blurted out:--\n\n“Why, this beats even shorthand! May I hear it say something?”\n\n“Certainly,” he replied with alacrity, and stood up to put it in train for speaking. Then he paused, and a troubled look overspread his face.\n\n“The fact is,” he began awkwardly, “I only keep my diary in it; and as it is entirely--almost entirely--about my cases, it may be awkward--that is, I mean----” He stopped, and I tried to help him out of his embarrassment:--\n\n“You helped to attend dear Lucy at the end. Let me hear how she died; for all that I know of her, I shall be very grateful. She was very, very dear to me.”\n\nTo my surprise, he answered, with a horrorstruck look in his face:--\n\n“Tell you of her death? Not for the wide world!”\n\n“Why not?” I asked, for some grave, terrible feeling was coming over me. Again he paused, and I could see that he was trying to invent an excuse. At length he stammered out:--\n\n“You see, I do not know how to pick out any particular part of the diary.” Even while he was speaking an idea dawned upon him, and he said with unconscious simplicity, in a different voice, and with the naïveté of a child: “That’s quite true, upon my honour. Honest Indian!” I could not but smile, at which he grimaced. “I gave myself away that time!” he said. “But do you know that, although I have kept the diary for months past, it never once struck me how I was going to find any particular part of it in case I wanted to look it up?” By this time my mind was made up that the diary of a doctor who attended Lucy might have something to add to the sum of our knowledge of that terrible Being, and I said boldly:--\n\n“Then, Dr. Seward, you had better let me copy it out for you on my typewriter.” He grew to a positively deathly pallor as he said:--\n\n“No! no! no! For all the world, I wouldn’t let you know that terrible story!”\n\nThen it was terrible; my intuition was right! For a moment I thought, and as my eyes ranged the room, unconsciously looking for something or some opportunity to aid me, they lit on a great batch of typewriting on the table. His eyes caught the look in mine, and, without his thinking, followed their direction. As they saw the parcel he realised my meaning.\n\n“You do not know me,” I said. “When you have read those papers--my own diary and my husband’s also, which I have typed--you will know me better. I have not faltered in giving every thought of my own heart in this cause; but, of course, you do not know me--yet; and I must not expect you to trust me so far.”\n\nHe is certainly a man of noble nature; poor dear Lucy was right about him. He stood up and opened a large drawer, in which were arranged in order a number of hollow cylinders of metal covered with dark wax, and said:--\n\n“You are quite right. I did not trust you because I did not know you. But I know you now; and let me say that I should have known you long ago. I know that Lucy told you of me; she told me of you too. May I make the only atonement in my power? Take the cylinders and hear them--the first half-dozen of them are personal to me, and they will not horrify you; then you will know me better. Dinner will by then be ready. In the meantime I shall read over some of these documents, and shall be better able to understand certain things.” He carried the phonograph himself up to my sitting-room and adjusted it for me. Now I shall learn something pleasant, I am sure; for it will tell me the other side of a true love episode of which I know one side already....\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n29 September.--I was so absorbed in that wonderful diary of Jonathan Harker and that other of his wife that I let the time run on without thinking. Mrs. Harker was not down when the maid came to announce dinner, so I said: “She is possibly tired; let dinner wait an hour,” and I went on with my work. I had just finished Mrs. Harker’s diary, when she came in. She looked sweetly pretty, but very sad, and her eyes were flushed with crying. This somehow moved me much. Of late I have had cause for tears, God knows! but the relief of them was denied me; and now the sight of those sweet eyes, brightened with recent tears, went straight to my heart. So I said as gently as I could:--\n\n“I greatly fear I have distressed you.”\n\n“Oh, no, not distressed me,” she replied, “but I have been more touched than I can say by your grief. That is a wonderful machine, but it is cruelly true. It told me, in its very tones, the anguish of your heart. It was like a soul crying out to Almighty God. No one must hear them spoken ever again! See, I have tried to be useful. I have copied out the words on my typewriter, and none other need now hear your heart beat, as I did.”\n\n“No one need ever know, shall ever know,” I said in a low voice. She laid her hand on mine and said very gravely:--\n“Ah, but they must!”\n\n“Must! But why?” I asked.\n\n“Because it is a part of the terrible story, a part of poor dear Lucy’s death and all that led to it; because in the struggle which we have before us to rid the earth of this terrible monster we must have all the knowledge and all the help which we can get. I think that the cylinders which you gave me contained more than you intended me to know; but I can see that there are in your record many lights to this dark mystery. You will let me help, will you not? I know all up to a certain point; and I see already, though your diary only took me to 7 September, how poor Lucy was beset, and how her terrible doom was being wrought out. Jonathan and I have been working day and night since Professor Van Helsing saw us. He is gone to Whitby to get more information, and he will be here to-morrow to help us. We need have no secrets amongst us; working together and with absolute trust, we can surely be stronger than if some of us were in the dark.” She looked at me so appealingly, and at the same time manifested such courage and resolution in her bearing, that I gave in at once to her wishes. “You shall,” I said, “do as you like in the matter. God forgive me if I do wrong! There are terrible things yet to learn of; but if you have so far travelled on the road to poor Lucy’s death, you will not be content, I know, to remain in the dark. Nay, the end--the very end--may give you a gleam of peace. Come, there is dinner. We must keep one another strong for what is before us; we have a cruel and dreadful task. When you have eaten you shall learn the rest, and I shall answer any questions you ask--if there be anything which you do not understand, though it was apparent to us who were present.”\n\nMina Harker’s Journal.\n\n29 September.--After dinner I came with Dr. Seward to his study. He brought back the phonograph from my room, and I took my typewriter. He placed me in a comfortable chair, and arranged the phonograph so that I could touch it without getting up, and showed me how to stop it in case I should want to pause. Then he very thoughtfully took a chair, with his back to me, so that I might be as free as possible, and began to read. I put the forked metal to my ears and listened.\n\nWhen the terrible story of Lucy’s death, and--and all that followed, was done, I lay back in my chair powerless. Fortunately I am not of a fainting disposition. When Dr. Seward saw me he jumped up with a horrified exclamation, and hurriedly taking a case-bottle from a cupboard, gave me some brandy, which in a few minutes somewhat restored me. My brain was all in a whirl, and only that there came through all the multitude of horrors, the holy ray of light that my dear, dear Lucy was at last at peace, I do not think I could have borne it without making a scene. It is all so wild, and mysterious, and strange that if I had not known Jonathan’s experience in Transylvania I could not have believed. As it was, I didn’t know what to believe, and so got out of my difficulty by attending to something else. I took the cover off my typewriter, and said to Dr. Seward:--\n\n“Let me write this all out now. We must be ready for Dr. Van Helsing when he comes. I have sent a telegram to Jonathan to come on here when he arrives in London from Whitby. In this matter dates are everything, and I think that if we get all our material ready, and have every item put in chronological order, we shall have done much. You tell me that Lord Godalming and Mr. Morris are coming too. Let us be able to tell him when they come.” He accordingly set the phonograph at a slow pace, and I began to typewrite from the beginning of the seventh cylinder. I used manifold, and so took three copies of the diary, just as I had done with all the rest. It was late when I got through, but Dr. Seward went about his work of going his round of the patients; when he had finished he came back and sat near me, reading, so that I did not feel too lonely whilst I worked. How good and thoughtful he is; the world seems full of good men--even if there are monsters in it. Before I left him I remembered what Jonathan put in his diary of the Professor’s perturbation at reading something in an evening paper at the station at Exeter; so, seeing that Dr. Seward keeps his newspapers, I borrowed the files of “The Westminster Gazette” and “The Pall Mall Gazette,” and took them to my room. I remember how much “The Dailygraph” and “The Whitby Gazette,” of which I had made cuttings, helped us to understand the terrible events at Whitby when Count Dracula landed, so I shall look through the evening papers since then, and perhaps I shall get some new light. I am not sleepy, and the work will help to keep me quiet.\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n30 September.--Mr. Harker arrived at nine o’clock. He had got his wife’s wire just before starting. He is uncommonly clever, if one can judge from his face, and full of energy. If this journal be true--and judging by one’s own wonderful experiences, it must be--he is also a man of great nerve. That going down to the vault a second time was a remarkable piece of daring. After reading his account of it I was prepared to meet a good specimen of manhood, but hardly the quiet, business-like gentleman who came here to-day.\n\nLater.--After lunch Harker and his wife went back to their own room, and as I passed a while ago I heard the click of the typewriter. They are hard at it. Mrs. Harker says that they are knitting together in chronological order every scrap of evidence they have. Harker has got the letters between the consignee of the boxes at Whitby and the carriers in London who took charge of them. He is now reading his wife’s typescript of my diary. I wonder what they make out of it. Here it is....\n\nStrange that it never struck me that the very next house might be the Count’s hiding-place! Goodness knows that we had enough clues from the conduct of the patient Renfield! The bundle of letters relating to the purchase of the house were with the typescript. Oh, if we had only had them earlier we might have saved poor Lucy! Stop; that way madness lies! Harker has gone back, and is again collating his material. He says that by dinner-time they will be able to show a whole connected narrative. He thinks that in the meantime I should see Renfield, as hitherto he has been a sort of index to the coming and going of the Count. I hardly see this yet, but when I get at the dates I suppose I shall. What a good thing that Mrs. Harker put my cylinders into type! We never could have found the dates otherwise....\n\nI found Renfield sitting placidly in his room with his hands folded, smiling benignly. At the moment he seemed as sane as any one I ever saw. I sat down and talked with him on a lot of subjects, all of which he treated naturally. He then, of his own accord, spoke of going home, a subject he has never mentioned to my knowledge during his sojourn here. In fact, he spoke quite confidently of getting his discharge at once. I believe that, had I not had the chat with Harker and read the letters and the dates of his outbursts, I should have been prepared to sign for him after a brief time of observation. As it is, I am darkly suspicious. All those outbreaks were in some way linked with the proximity of the Count. What then does this absolute content mean? Can it be that his instinct is satisfied as to the vampire’s ultimate triumph? Stay; he is himself zoöphagous, and in his wild ravings outside the chapel door of the deserted house he always spoke of “master.” This all seems confirmation of our idea. However, after a while I came away; my friend is just a little too sane at present to make it safe to probe him too deep with questions. He might begin to think, and then--! So I came away. I mistrust these quiet moods of his; so I have given the attendant a hint to look closely after him, and to have a strait-waistcoat ready in case of need.\n\nJonathan Harker’s Journal.\n\n29 September, in train to London.--When I received Mr. Billington’s courteous message that he would give me any information in his power I thought it best to go down to Whitby and make, on the spot, such inquiries as I wanted. It was now my object to trace that horrid cargo of the Count’s to its place in London. Later, we may be able to deal with it. Billington junior, a nice lad, met me at the station, and brought me to his father’s house, where they had decided that I must stay the night. They are hospitable, with true Yorkshire hospitality: give a guest everything, and leave him free to do as he likes. They all knew that I was busy, and that my stay was short, and Mr. Billington had ready in his office all the papers concerning the consignment of boxes. It gave me almost a turn to see again one of the letters which I had seen on the Count’s table before I knew of his diabolical plans. Everything had been carefully thought out, and done systematically and with precision. He seemed to have been prepared for every obstacle which might be placed by accident in the way of his intentions being carried out. To use an Americanism, he had “taken no chances,” and the absolute accuracy with which his instructions were fulfilled, was simply the logical result of his care. I saw the invoice, and took note of it: “Fifty cases of common earth, to be used for experimental purposes.” Also the copy of letter to Carter Paterson, and their reply; of both of these I got copies. This was all the information Mr. Billington could give me, so I went down to the port and saw the coastguards, the Customs officers and the harbour-master. They had all something to say of the strange entry of the ship, which is already taking its place in local tradition; but no one could add to the simple description “Fifty cases of common earth.” I then saw the station-master, who kindly put me in communication with the men who had actually received the boxes. Their tally was exact with the list, and they had nothing to add except that the boxes were “main and mortal heavy,” and that shifting them was dry work. One of them added that it was hard lines that there wasn’t any gentleman “such-like as yourself, squire,” to show some sort of appreciation of their efforts in a liquid form; another put in a rider that the thirst then generated was such that even the time which had elapsed had not completely allayed it. Needless to add, I took care before leaving to lift, for ever and adequately, this source of reproach.\n\n30 September.--The station-master was good enough to give me a line to his old companion the station-master at King’s Cross, so that when I arrived there in the morning I was able to ask him about the arrival of the boxes. He, too, put me at once in communication with the proper officials, and I saw that their tally was correct with the original invoice. The opportunities of acquiring an abnormal thirst had been here limited; a noble use of them had, however, been made, and again I was compelled to deal with the result in an ex post facto manner.\n\nFrom thence I went on to Carter Paterson’s central office, where I met with the utmost courtesy. They looked up the transaction in their day-book and letter-book, and at once telephoned to their King’s Cross office for more details. By good fortune, the men who did the teaming were waiting for work, and the official at once sent them over, sending also by one of them the way-bill and all the papers connected with the delivery of the boxes at Carfax. Here again I found the tally agreeing exactly; the carriers’ men were able to supplement the paucity of the written words with a few details. These were, I shortly found, connected almost solely with the dusty nature of the job, and of the consequent thirst engendered in the operators. On my affording an opportunity, through the medium of the currency of the realm, of the allaying, at a later period, this beneficial evil, one of the men remarked:--\n\n“That ’ere ’ouse, guv’nor, is the rummiest I ever was in. Blyme! but it ain’t been touched sence a hundred years. There was dust that thick in the place that you might have slep’ on it without ’urtin’ of yer bones; an’ the place was that neglected that yer might ’ave smelled ole Jerusalem in it. But the ole chapel--that took the cike, that did! Me and my mate, we thort we wouldn’t never git out quick enough. Lor’, I wouldn’t take less nor a quid a moment to stay there arter dark.”\n\nHaving been in the house, I could well believe him; but if he knew what I know, he would, I think, have raised his terms.\n\nOf one thing I am now satisfied: that all the boxes which arrived at Whitby from Varna in the Demeter were safely deposited in the old chapel at Carfax. There should be fifty of them there, unless any have since been removed--as from Dr. Seward’s diary I fear.\n\nI shall try to see the carter who took away the boxes from Carfax when Renfield attacked them. By following up this clue we may learn a good deal.\n\nLater.--Mina and I have worked all day, and we have put all the papers into order.\n\nMina Harker’s Journal\n\n30 September.--I am so glad that I hardly know how to contain myself. It is, I suppose, the reaction from the haunting fear which I have had: that this terrible affair and the reopening of his old wound might act detrimentally on Jonathan. I saw him leave for Whitby with as brave a face as I could, but I was sick with apprehension. The effort has, however, done him good. He was never so resolute, never so strong, never so full of volcanic energy, as at present. It is just as that dear, good Professor Van Helsing said: he is true grit, and he improves under strain that would kill a weaker nature. He came back full of life and hope and determination; we have got everything in order for to-night. I feel myself quite wild with excitement. I suppose one ought to pity any thing so hunted as is the Count. That is just it: this Thing is not human--not even beast. To read Dr. Seward’s account of poor Lucy’s death, and what followed, is enough to dry up the springs of pity in one’s heart.\n\nLater.--Lord Godalming and Mr. Morris arrived earlier than we expected. Dr. Seward was out on business, and had taken Jonathan with him, so I had to see them. It was to me a painful meeting, for it brought back all poor dear Lucy’s hopes of only a few months ago. Of course they had heard Lucy speak of me, and it seemed that Dr. Van Helsing, too, has been quite “blowing my trumpet,” as Mr. Morris expressed it. Poor fellows, neither of them is aware that I know all about the proposals they made to Lucy. They did not quite know what to say or do, as they were ignorant of the amount of my knowledge; so they had to keep on neutral subjects. However, I thought the matter over, and came to the conclusion that the best thing I could do would be to post them in affairs right up to date. I knew from Dr. Seward’s diary that they had been at Lucy’s death--her real death--and that I need not fear to betray any secret before the time. So I told them, as well as I could, that I had read all the papers and diaries, and that my husband and I, having typewritten them, had just finished putting them in order. I gave them each a copy to read in the library. When Lord Godalming got his and turned it over--it does make a pretty good pile--he said:--\n\n“Did you write all this, Mrs. Harker?”\n\nI nodded, and he went on:--\n\n“I don’t quite see the drift of it; but you people are all so good and kind, and have been working so earnestly and so energetically, that all I can do is to accept your ideas blindfold and try to help you. I have had one lesson already in accepting facts that should make a man humble to the last hour of his life. Besides, I know you loved my poor Lucy--” Here he turned away and covered his face with his hands. I could hear the tears in his voice. Mr. Morris, with instinctive delicacy, just laid a hand for a moment on his shoulder, and then walked quietly out of the room. I suppose there is something in woman’s nature that makes a man free to break down before her and express his feelings on the tender or emotional side without feeling it derogatory to his manhood; for when Lord Godalming found himself alone with me he sat down on the sofa and gave way utterly and openly. I sat down beside him and took his hand. I hope he didn’t think it forward of me, and that if he ever thinks of it afterwards he never will have such a thought. There I wrong him; I know he never will--he is too true a gentleman. I said to him, for I could see that his heart was breaking:--\n\n“I loved dear Lucy, and I know what she was to you, and what you were to her. She and I were like sisters; and now she is gone, will you not let me be like a sister to you in your trouble? I know what sorrows you have had, though I cannot measure the depth of them. If sympathy and pity can help in your affliction, won’t you let me be of some little service--for Lucy’s sake?”\n\nIn an instant the poor dear fellow was overwhelmed with grief. It seemed to me that all that he had of late been suffering in silence found a vent at once. He grew quite hysterical, and raising his open hands, beat his palms together in a perfect agony of grief. He stood up and then sat down again, and the tears rained down his cheeks. I felt an infinite pity for him, and opened my arms unthinkingly. With a sob he laid his head on my shoulder and cried like a wearied child, whilst he shook with emotion.\n\nWe women have something of the mother in us that makes us rise above smaller matters when the mother-spirit is invoked; I felt this big sorrowing man’s head resting on me, as though it were that of the baby that some day may lie on my bosom, and I stroked his hair as though he were my own child. I never thought at the time how strange it all was.\n\nAfter a little bit his sobs ceased, and he raised himself with an apology, though he made no disguise of his emotion. He told me that for days and nights past--weary days and sleepless nights--he had been unable to speak with any one, as a man must speak in his time of sorrow. There was no woman whose sympathy could be given to him, or with whom, owing to the terrible circumstance with which his sorrow was surrounded, he could speak freely. “I know now how I suffered,” he said, as he dried his eyes, “but I do not know even yet--and none other can ever know--how much your sweet sympathy has been to me to-day. I shall know better in time; and believe me that, though I am not ungrateful now, my gratitude will grow with my understanding. You will let me be like a brother, will you not, for all our lives--for dear Lucy’s sake?”\n\n“For dear Lucy’s sake,” I said as we clasped hands. “Ay, and for your own sake,” he added, “for if a man’s esteem and gratitude are ever worth the winning, you have won mine to-day. If ever the future should bring to you a time when you need a man’s help, believe me, you will not call in vain. God grant that no such time may ever come to you to break the sunshine of your life; but if it should ever come, promise me that you will let me know.” He was so earnest, and his sorrow was so fresh, that I felt it would comfort him, so I said:--\n\n“I promise.”\n\nAs I came along the corridor I saw Mr. Morris looking out of a window. He turned as he heard my footsteps. “How is Art?” he said. Then noticing my red eyes, he went on: “Ah, I see you have been comforting him. Poor old fellow! he needs it. No one but a woman can help a man when he is in trouble of the heart; and he had no one to comfort him.”\n\nHe bore his own trouble so bravely that my heart bled for him. I saw the manuscript in his hand, and I knew that when he read it he would realise how much I knew; so I said to him:--\n\n“I wish I could comfort all who suffer from the heart. Will you let me be your friend, and will you come to me for comfort if you need it? You will know, later on, why I speak.” He saw that I was in earnest, and stooping, took my hand, and raising it to his lips, kissed it. It seemed but poor comfort to so brave and unselfish a soul, and impulsively I bent over and kissed him. The tears rose in his eyes, and there was a momentary choking in his throat; he said quite calmly:--\n\n“Little girl, you will never regret that true-hearted kindness, so long as ever you live!” Then he went into the study to his friend.\n\n“Little girl!”--the very words he had used to Lucy, and oh, but he proved himself a friend!\n\n# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY\n\n30 September.--I got home at five o’clock, and found that Godalming and Morris had not only arrived, but had already studied the transcript of the various diaries and letters which Harker and his wonderful wife had made and arranged. Harker had not yet returned from his visit to the carriers’ men, of whom Dr. Hennessey had written to me. Mrs. Harker gave us a cup of tea, and I can honestly say that, for the first time since I have lived in it, this old house seemed like home. When we had finished, Mrs. Harker said:--\n\n“Dr. Seward, may I ask a favour? I want to see your patient, Mr. Renfield. Do let me see him. What you have said of him in your diary interests me so much!” She looked so appealing and so pretty that I could not refuse her, and there was no possible reason why I should; so I took her with me. When I went into the room, I told the man that a lady would like to see him; to which he simply answered: “Why?”\n\n“She is going through the house, and wants to see every one in it,” I answered. “Oh, very well,” he said; “let her come in, by all means; but just wait a minute till I tidy up the place.” His method of tidying was peculiar: he simply swallowed all the flies and spiders in the boxes before I could stop him. It was quite evident that he feared, or was jealous of, some interference. When he had got through his disgusting task, he said cheerfully: “Let the lady come in,” and sat down on the edge of his bed with his head down, but with his eyelids raised so that he could see her as she entered. For a moment I thought that he might have some homicidal intent; I remembered how quiet he had been just before he attacked me in my own study, and I took care to stand where I could seize him at once if he attempted to make a spring at her. She came into the room with an easy gracefulness which would at once command the respect of any lunatic--for easiness is one of the qualities mad people most respect. She walked over to him, smiling pleasantly, and held out her hand.\n\n“Good-evening, Mr. Renfield,” said she. “You see, I know you, for Dr. Seward has told me of you.” He made no immediate reply, but eyed her all over intently with a set frown on his face. This look gave way to one of wonder, which merged in doubt; then, to my intense astonishment, he said:--\n\n“You’re not the girl the doctor wanted to marry, are you? You can’t be, you know, for she’s dead.” Mrs. Harker smiled sweetly as she replied:--\n\n“Oh no! I have a husband of my own, to whom I was married before I ever saw Dr. Seward, or he me. I am Mrs. Harker.”\n\n“Then what are you doing here?”\n\n“My husband and I are staying on a visit with Dr. Seward.”\n\n“Then don’t stay.”\n\n“But why not?” I thought that this style of conversation might not be pleasant to Mrs. Harker, any more than it was to me, so I joined in:--\n\n“How did you know I wanted to marry any one?” His reply was simply contemptuous, given in a pause in which he turned his eyes from Mrs. Harker to me, instantly turning them back again:--\n\n“What an asinine question!”\n\n“I don’t see that at all, Mr. Renfield,” said Mrs. Harker, at once championing me. He replied to her with as much courtesy and respect as he had shown contempt to me:--\n\n“You will, of course, understand, Mrs. Harker, that when a man is so loved and honoured as our host is, everything regarding him is of interest in our little community. Dr. Seward is loved not only by his household and his friends, but even by his patients, who, being some of them hardly in mental equilibrium, are apt to distort causes and effects. Since I myself have been an inmate of a lunatic asylum, I cannot but notice that the sophistic tendencies of some of its inmates lean towards the errors of non causa and ignoratio elenchi.” I positively opened my eyes at this new development. Here was my own pet lunatic--the most pronounced of his type that I had ever met with--talking elemental philosophy, and with the manner of a polished gentleman. I wonder if it was Mrs. Harker’s presence which had touched some chord in his memory. If this new phase was spontaneous, or in any way due to her unconscious influence, she must have some rare gift or power.\n\nWe continued to talk for some time; and, seeing that he was seemingly quite reasonable, she ventured, looking at me questioningly as she began, to lead him to his favourite topic. I was again astonished, for he addressed himself to the question with the impartiality of the completest sanity; he even took himself as an example when he mentioned certain things.\n\n“Why, I myself am an instance of a man who had a strange belief. Indeed, it was no wonder that my friends were alarmed, and insisted on my being put under control. I used to fancy that life was a positive and perpetual entity, and that by consuming a multitude of live things, no matter how low in the scale of creation, one might indefinitely prolong life. At times I held the belief so strongly that I actually tried to take human life. The doctor here will bear me out that on one occasion I tried to kill him for the purpose of strengthening my vital powers by the assimilation with my own body of his life through the medium of his blood--relying, of course, upon the Scriptural phrase, ‘For the blood is the life.’ Though, indeed, the vendor of a certain nostrum has vulgarised the truism to the very point of contempt. Isn’t that true, doctor?” I nodded assent, for I was so amazed that I hardly knew what to either think or say; it was hard to imagine that I had seen him eat up his spiders and flies not five minutes before. Looking at my watch, I saw that I should go to the station to meet Van Helsing, so I told Mrs. Harker that it was time to leave. She came at once, after saying pleasantly to Mr. Renfield: “Good-bye, and I hope I may see you often, under auspices pleasanter to yourself,” to which, to my astonishment, he replied:--\n\n“Good-bye, my dear. I pray God I may never see your sweet face again. May He bless and keep you!”\n\nWhen I went to the station to meet Van Helsing I left the boys behind me. Poor Art seemed more cheerful than he has been since Lucy first took ill, and Quincey is more like his own bright self than he has been for many a long day.\n\nVan Helsing stepped from the carriage with the eager nimbleness of a boy. He saw me at once, and rushed up to me, saying:--\n\n“Ah, friend John, how goes all? Well? So! I have been busy, for I come here to stay if need be. All affairs are settled with me, and I have much to tell. Madam Mina is with you? Yes. And her so fine husband? And Arthur and my friend Quincey, they are with you, too? Good!”\n\nAs I drove to the house I told him of what had passed, and of how my own diary had come to be of some use through Mrs. Harker’s suggestion; at which the Professor interrupted me:--\n\n“Ah, that wonderful Madam Mina! She has man’s brain--a brain that a man should have were he much gifted--and a woman’s heart. The good God fashioned her for a purpose, believe me, when He made that so good combination. Friend John, up to now fortune has made that woman of help to us; after to-night she must not have to do with this so terrible affair. It is not good that she run a risk so great. We men are determined--nay, are we not pledged?--to destroy this monster; but it is no part for a woman. Even if she be not harmed, her heart may fail her in so much and so many horrors; and hereafter she may suffer--both in waking, from her nerves, and in sleep, from her dreams. And, besides, she is young woman and not so long married; there may be other things to think of some time, if not now. You tell me she has wrote all, then she must consult with us; but to-morrow she say good-bye to this work, and we go alone.” I agreed heartily with him, and then I told him what we had found in his absence: that the house which Dracula had bought was the very next one to my own. He was amazed, and a great concern seemed to come on him. “Oh that we had known it before!” he said, “for then we might have reached him in time to save poor Lucy. However, ‘the milk that is spilt cries not out afterwards,’ as you say. We shall not think of that, but go on our way to the end.” Then he fell into a silence that lasted till we entered my own gateway. Before we went to prepare for dinner he said to Mrs. Harker:--\n\n“I am told, Madam Mina, by my friend John that you and your husband have put up in exact order all things that have been, up to this moment.”\n\n“Not up to this moment, Professor,” she said impulsively, “but up to this morning.”\n\n“But why not up to now? We have seen hitherto how good light all the little things have made. We have told our secrets, and yet no one who has told is the worse for it.”\n\nMrs. Harker began to blush, and taking a paper from her pockets, she said:--\n\n“Dr. Van Helsing, will you read this, and tell me if it must go in. It is my record of to-day. I too have seen the need of putting down at present everything, however trivial; but there is little in this except what is personal. Must it go in?” The Professor read it over gravely, and handed it back, saying:--\n\n“It need not go in if you do not wish it; but I pray that it may. It can but make your husband love you the more, and all us, your friends, more honour you--as well as more esteem and love.” She took it back with another blush and a bright smile.\n\nAnd so now, up to this very hour, all the records we have are complete and in order. The Professor took away one copy to study after dinner, and before our meeting, which is fixed for nine o’clock. The rest of us have already read everything; so when we meet in the study we shall all be informed as to facts, and can arrange our plan of battle with this terrible and mysterious enemy.\n\nMina Harker’s Journal.\n\n30 September.--When we met in Dr. Seward’s study two hours after dinner, which had been at six o’clock, we unconsciously formed a sort of board or committee. Professor Van Helsing took the head of the table, to which Dr. Seward motioned him as he came into the room. He made me sit next to him on his right, and asked me to act as secretary; Jonathan sat next to me. Opposite us were Lord Godalming, Dr. Seward, and Mr. Morris--Lord Godalming being next the Professor, and Dr. Seward in the centre. The Professor said:--\n\n“I may, I suppose, take it that we are all acquainted with the facts that are in these papers.” We all expressed assent, and he went on:--\n\n“Then it were, I think good that I tell you something of the kind of enemy with which we have to deal. I shall then make known to you something of the history of this man, which has been ascertained for me. So we then can discuss how we shall act, and can take our measure according.\n\n“There are such beings as vampires; some of us have evidence that they exist. Even had we not the proof of our own unhappy experience, the teachings and the records of the past give proof enough for sane peoples. I admit that at the first I was sceptic. Were it not that through long years I have train myself to keep an open mind, I could not have believe until such time as that fact thunder on my ear. ‘See! see! I prove; I prove.’ Alas! Had I known at the first what now I know--nay, had I even guess at him--one so precious life had been spared to many of us who did love her. But that is gone; and we must so work, that other poor souls perish not, whilst we can save. The nosferatu do not die like the bee when he sting once. He is only stronger; and being stronger, have yet more power to work evil. This vampire which is amongst us is of himself so strong in person as twenty men; he is of cunning more than mortal, for his cunning be the growth of ages; he have still the aids of necromancy, which is, as his etymology imply, the divination by the dead, and all the dead that he can come nigh to are for him at command; he is brute, and more than brute; he is devil in callous, and the heart of him is not; he can, within limitations, appear at will when, and where, and in any of the forms that are to him; he can, within his range, direct the elements; the storm, the fog, the thunder; he can command all the meaner things: the rat, and the owl, and the bat--the moth, and the fox, and the wolf; he can grow and become small; and he can at times vanish and come unknown. How then are we to begin our strike to destroy him? How shall we find his where; and having found it, how can we destroy? My friends, this is much; it is a terrible task that we undertake, and there may be consequence to make the brave shudder. For if we fail in this our fight he must surely win; and then where end we? Life is nothings; I heed him not. But to fail here, is not mere life or death. It is that we become as him; that we henceforward become foul things of the night like him--without heart or conscience, preying on the bodies and the souls of those we love best. To us for ever are the gates of heaven shut; for who shall open them to us again? We go on for all time abhorred by all; a blot on the face of God’s sunshine; an arrow in the side of Him who died for man. But we are face to face with duty; and in such case must we shrink? For me, I say, no; but then I am old, and life, with his sunshine, his fair places, his song of birds, his music and his love, lie far behind. You others are young. Some have seen sorrow; but there are fair days yet in store. What say you?”\n\nWhilst he was speaking, Jonathan had taken my hand. I feared, oh so much, that the appalling nature of our danger was overcoming him when I saw his hand stretch out; but it was life to me to feel its touch--so strong, so self-reliant, so resolute. A brave man’s hand can speak for itself; it does not even need a woman’s love to hear its music.\n\nWhen the Professor had done speaking my husband looked in my eyes, and I in his; there was no need for speaking between us.\n\n“I answer for Mina and myself,” he said.\n\n“Count me in, Professor,” said Mr. Quincey Morris, laconically as usual.\n\n“I am with you,” said Lord Godalming, “for Lucy’s sake, if for no other reason.”\n\nDr. Seward simply nodded. The Professor stood up and, after laying his golden crucifix on the table, held out his hand on either side. I took his right hand, and Lord Godalming his left; Jonathan held my right with his left and stretched across to Mr. Morris. So as we all took hands our solemn compact was made. I felt my heart icy cold, but it did not even occur to me to draw back. We resumed our places, and Dr. Van Helsing went on with a sort of cheerfulness which showed that the serious work had begun. It was to be taken as gravely, and in as businesslike a way, as any other transaction of life:--\n\n“Well, you know what we have to contend against; but we, too, are not without strength. We have on our side power of combination--a power denied to the vampire kind; we have sources of science; we are free to act and think; and the hours of the day and the night are ours equally. In fact, so far as our powers extend, they are unfettered, and we are free to use them. We have self-devotion in a cause, and an end to achieve which is not a selfish one. These things are much.\n\n“Now let us see how far the general powers arrayed against us are restrict, and how the individual cannot. In fine, let us consider the limitations of the vampire in general, and of this one in particular.\n\n“All we have to go upon are traditions and superstitions. These do not at the first appear much, when the matter is one of life and death--nay of more than either life or death. Yet must we be satisfied; in the first place because we have to be--no other means is at our control--and secondly, because, after all, these things--tradition and superstition--are everything. Does not the belief in vampires rest for others--though not, alas! for us--on them? A year ago which of us would have received such a possibility, in the midst of our scientific, sceptical, matter-of-fact nineteenth century? We even scouted a belief that we saw justified under our very eyes. Take it, then, that the vampire, and the belief in his limitations and his cure, rest for the moment on the same base. For, let me tell you, he is known everywhere that men have been. In old Greece, in old Rome; he flourish in Germany all over, in France, in India, even in the Chernosese; and in China, so far from us in all ways, there even is he, and the peoples fear him at this day. He have follow the wake of the berserker Icelander, the devil-begotten Hun, the Slav, the Saxon, the Magyar. So far, then, we have all we may act upon; and let me tell you that very much of the beliefs are justified by what we have seen in our own so unhappy experience. The vampire live on, and cannot die by mere passing of the time; he can flourish when that he can fatten on the blood of the living. Even more, we have seen amongst us that he can even grow younger; that his vital faculties grow strenuous, and seem as though they refresh themselves when his special pabulum is plenty. But he cannot flourish without this diet; he eat not as others. Even friend Jonathan, who lived with him for weeks, did never see him to eat, never! He throws no shadow; he make in the mirror no reflect, as again Jonathan observe. He has the strength of many of his hand--witness again Jonathan when he shut the door against the wolfs, and when he help him from the diligence too. He can transform himself to wolf, as we gather from the ship arrival in Whitby, when he tear open the dog; he can be as bat, as Madam Mina saw him on the window at Whitby, and as friend John saw him fly from this so near house, and as my friend Quincey saw him at the window of Miss Lucy. He can come in mist which he create--that noble ship’s captain proved him of this; but, from what we know, the distance he can make this mist is limited, and it can only be round himself. He come on moonlight rays as elemental dust--as again Jonathan saw those sisters in the castle of Dracula. He become so small--we ourselves saw Miss Lucy, ere she was at peace, slip through a hairbreadth space at the tomb door. He can, when once he find his way, come out from anything or into anything, no matter how close it be bound or even fused up with fire--solder you call it. He can see in the dark--no small power this, in a world which is one half shut from the light. Ah, but hear me through. He can do all these things, yet he is not free. Nay; he is even more prisoner than the slave of the galley, than the madman in his cell. He cannot go where he lists; he who is not of nature has yet to obey some of nature’s laws--why we know not. He may not enter anywhere at the first, unless there be some one of the household who bid him to come; though afterwards he can come as he please. His power ceases, as does that of all evil things, at the coming of the day. Only at certain times can he have limited freedom. If he be not at the place whither he is bound, he can only change himself at noon or at exact sunrise or sunset. These things are we told, and in this record of ours we have proof by inference. Thus, whereas he can do as he will within his limit, when he have his earth-home, his coffin-home, his hell-home, the place unhallowed, as we saw when he went to the grave of the suicide at Whitby; still at other time he can only change when the time come. It is said, too, that he can only pass running water at the slack or the flood of the tide. Then there are things which so afflict him that he has no power, as the garlic that we know of; and as for things sacred, as this symbol, my crucifix, that was amongst us even now when we resolve, to them he is nothing, but in their presence he take his place far off and silent with respect. There are others, too, which I shall tell you of, lest in our seeking we may need them. The branch of wild rose on his coffin keep him that he move not from it; a sacred bullet fired into the coffin kill him so that he be true dead; and as for the stake through him, we know already of its peace; or the cut-off head that giveth rest. We have seen it with our eyes.\n\n“Thus when we find the habitation of this man-that-was, we can confine him to his coffin and destroy him, if we obey what we know. But he is clever. I have asked my friend Arminius, of Buda-Pesth University, to make his record; and, from all the means that are, he tell me of what he has been. He must, indeed, have been that Voivode Dracula who won his name against the Turk, over the great river on the very frontier of Turkey-land. If it be so, then was he no common man; for in that time, and for centuries after, he was spoken of as the cleverest and the most cunning, as well as the bravest of the sons of the ‘land beyond the forest.’ That mighty brain and that iron resolution went with him to his grave, and are even now arrayed against us. The Draculas were, says Arminius, a great and noble race, though now and again were scions who were held by their coevals to have had dealings with the Evil One. They learned his secrets in the Scholomance, amongst the mountains over Lake Hermanstadt, where the devil claims the tenth scholar as his due. In the records are such words as ‘stregoica’--witch, ‘ordog,’ and ‘pokol’--Satan and hell; and in one manuscript this very Dracula is spoken of as ‘wampyr,’ which we all understand too well. There have been from the loins of this very one great men and good women, and their graves make sacred the earth where alone this foulness can dwell. For it is not the least of its terrors that this evil thing is rooted deep in all good; in soil barren of holy memories it cannot rest.”\n\nWhilst they were talking Mr. Morris was looking steadily at the window, and he now got up quietly, and went out of the room. There was a little pause, and then the Professor went on:--\n\n“And now we must settle what we do. We have here much data, and we must proceed to lay out our campaign. We know from the inquiry of Jonathan that from the castle to Whitby came fifty boxes of earth, all of which were delivered at Carfax; we also know that at least some of these boxes have been removed. It seems to me, that our first step should be to ascertain whether all the rest remain in the house beyond that wall where we look to-day; or whether any more have been removed. If the latter, we must trace----”\n\nHere we were interrupted in a very startling way. Outside the house came the sound of a pistol-shot; the glass of the window was shattered with a bullet, which, ricochetting from the top of the embrasure, struck the far wall of the room. I am afraid I am at heart a coward, for I shrieked out. The men all jumped to their feet; Lord Godalming flew over to the window and threw up the sash. As he did so we heard Mr. Morris’s voice without:--\n\n“Sorry! I fear I have alarmed you. I shall come in and tell you about it.” A minute later he came in and said:--\n\n“It was an idiotic thing of me to do, and I ask your pardon, Mrs. Harker, most sincerely; I fear I must have frightened you terribly. But the fact is that whilst the Professor was talking there came a big bat and sat on the window-sill. I have got such a horror of the damned brutes from recent events that I cannot stand them, and I went out to have a shot, as I have been doing of late of evenings, whenever I have seen one. You used to laugh at me for it then, Art.”\n\n“Did you hit it?” asked Dr. Van Helsing.\n\n“I don’t know; I fancy not, for it flew away into the wood.” Without saying any more he took his seat, and the Professor began to resume his statement:--\n\n“We must trace each of these boxes; and when we are ready, we must either capture or kill this monster in his lair; or we must, so to speak, sterilise the earth, so that no more he can seek safety in it. Thus in the end we may find him in his form of man between the hours of noon and sunset, and so engage with him when he is at his most weak.\n\n“And now for you, Madam Mina, this night is the end until all be well. You are too precious to us to have such risk. When we part to-night, you no more must question. We shall tell you all in good time. We are men and are able to bear; but you must be our star and our hope, and we shall act all the more free that you are not in the danger, such as we are.”\n\nAll the men, even Jonathan, seemed relieved; but it did not seem to me good that they should brave danger and, perhaps, lessen their safety--strength being the best safety--through care of me; but their minds were made up, and, though it was a bitter pill for me to swallow, I could say nothing, save to accept their chivalrous care of me.\n\nMr. Morris resumed the discussion:--\n\n“As there is no time to lose, I vote we have a look at his house right now. Time is everything with him; and swift action on our part may save another victim.”\n\nI own that my heart began to fail me when the time for action came so close, but I did not say anything, for I had a greater fear that if I appeared as a drag or a hindrance to their work, they might even leave me out of their counsels altogether. They have now gone off to Carfax, with means to get into the house.\n\nManlike, they had told me to go to bed and sleep; as if a woman can sleep when those she loves are in danger! I shall lie down and pretend to sleep, lest Jonathan have added anxiety about me when he returns.\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n1 October, 4 a. m.--Just as we were about to leave the house, an urgent message was brought to me from Renfield to know if I would see him at once, as he had something of the utmost importance to say to me. I told the messenger to say that I would attend to his wishes in the morning; I was busy just at the moment. The attendant added:--\n\n“He seems very importunate, sir. I have never seen him so eager. I don’t know but what, if you don’t see him soon, he will have one of his violent fits.” I knew the man would not have said this without some cause, so I said: “All right; I’ll go now”; and I asked the others to wait a few minutes for me, as I had to go and see my “patient.”\n\n“Take me with you, friend John,” said the Professor. “His case in your diary interest me much, and it had bearing, too, now and again on our case. I should much like to see him, and especial when his mind is disturbed.”\n\n“May I come also?” asked Lord Godalming.\n\n“Me too?” said Quincey Morris. “May I come?” said Harker. I nodded, and we all went down the passage together.\n\nWe found him in a state of considerable excitement, but far more rational in his speech and manner than I had ever seen him. There was an unusual understanding of himself, which was unlike anything I had ever met with in a lunatic; and he took it for granted that his reasons would prevail with others entirely sane. We all four went into the room, but none of the others at first said anything. His request was that I would at once release him from the asylum and send him home. This he backed up with arguments regarding his complete recovery, and adduced his own existing sanity. “I appeal to your friends,” he said, “they will, perhaps, not mind sitting in judgment on my case. By the way, you have not introduced me.” I was so much astonished, that the oddness of introducing a madman in an asylum did not strike me at the moment; and, besides, there was a certain dignity in the man’s manner, so much of the habit of equality, that I at once made the introduction: “Lord Godalming; Professor Van Helsing; Mr. Quincey Morris, of Texas; Mr. Renfield.” He shook hands with each of them, saying in turn:--\n\n“Lord Godalming, I had the honour of seconding your father at the Windham; I grieve to know, by your holding the title, that he is no more. He was a man loved and honoured by all who knew him; and in his youth was, I have heard, the inventor of a burnt rum punch, much patronised on Derby night. Mr. Morris, you should be proud of your great state. Its reception into the Union was a precedent which may have far-reaching effects hereafter, when the Pole and the Tropics may hold alliance to the Stars and Stripes. The power of Treaty may yet prove a vast engine of enlargement, when the Monroe doctrine takes its true place as a political fable. What shall any man say of his pleasure at meeting Van Helsing? Sir, I make no apology for dropping all forms of conventional prefix. When an individual has revolutionised therapeutics by his discovery of the continuous evolution of brain-matter, conventional forms are unfitting, since they would seem to limit him to one of a class. You, gentlemen, who by nationality, by heredity, or by the possession of natural gifts, are fitted to hold your respective places in the moving world, I take to witness that I am as sane as at least the majority of men who are in full possession of their liberties. And I am sure that you, Dr. Seward, humanitarian and medico-jurist as well as scientist, will deem it a moral duty to deal with me as one to be considered as under exceptional circumstances.” He made this last appeal with a courtly air of conviction which was not without its own charm.\n\nI think we were all staggered. For my own part, I was under the conviction, despite my knowledge of the man’s character and history, that his reason had been restored; and I felt under a strong impulse to tell him that I was satisfied as to his sanity, and would see about the necessary formalities for his release in the morning. I thought it better to wait, however, before making so grave a statement, for of old I knew the sudden changes to which this particular patient was liable. So I contented myself with making a general statement that he appeared to be improving very rapidly; that I would have a longer chat with him in the morning, and would then see what I could do in the direction of meeting his wishes. This did not at all satisfy him, for he said quickly:--\n\n“But I fear, Dr. Seward, that you hardly apprehend my wish. I desire to go at once--here--now--this very hour--this very moment, if I may. Time presses, and in our implied agreement with the old scytheman it is of the essence of the contract. I am sure it is only necessary to put before so admirable a practitioner as Dr. Seward so simple, yet so momentous a wish, to ensure its fulfilment.” He looked at me keenly, and seeing the negative in my face, turned to the others, and scrutinised them closely. Not meeting any sufficient response, he went on:--\n\n“Is it possible that I have erred in my supposition?”\n\n“You have,” I said frankly, but at the same time, as I felt, brutally. There was a considerable pause, and then he said slowly:--\n\n“Then I suppose I must only shift my ground of request. Let me ask for this concession--boon, privilege, what you will. I am content to implore in such a case, not on personal grounds, but for the sake of others. I am not at liberty to give you the whole of my reasons; but you may, I assure you, take it from me that they are good ones, sound and unselfish, and spring from the highest sense of duty. Could you look, sir, into my heart, you would approve to the full the sentiments which animate me. Nay, more, you would count me amongst the best and truest of your friends.” Again he looked at us all keenly. I had a growing conviction that this sudden change of his entire intellectual method was but yet another form or phase of his madness, and so determined to let him go on a little longer, knowing from experience that he would, like all lunatics, give himself away in the end. Van Helsing was gazing at him with a look of utmost intensity, his bushy eyebrows almost meeting with the fixed concentration of his look. He said to Renfield in a tone which did not surprise me at the time, but only when I thought of it afterwards--for it was as of one addressing an equal:--\n\n“Can you not tell frankly your real reason for wishing to be free to-night? I will undertake that if you will satisfy even me--a stranger, without prejudice, and with the habit of keeping an open mind--Dr. Seward will give you, at his own risk and on his own responsibility, the privilege you seek.” He shook his head sadly, and with a look of poignant regret on his face. The Professor went on:--\n\n“Come, sir, bethink yourself. You claim the privilege of reason in the highest degree, since you seek to impress us with your complete reasonableness. You do this, whose sanity we have reason to doubt, since you are not yet released from medical treatment for this very defect. If you will not help us in our effort to choose the wisest course, how can we perform the duty which you yourself put upon us? Be wise, and help us; and if we can we shall aid you to achieve your wish.” He still shook his head as he said:--\n\n“Dr. Van Helsing, I have nothing to say. Your argument is complete, and if I were free to speak I should not hesitate a moment; but I am not my own master in the matter. I can only ask you to trust me. If I am refused, the responsibility does not rest with me.” I thought it was now time to end the scene, which was becoming too comically grave, so I went towards the door, simply saying:--\n\n“Come, my friends, we have work to do. Good-night.”\n\nAs, however, I got near the door, a new change came over the patient. He moved towards me so quickly that for the moment I feared that he was about to make another homicidal attack. My fears, however, were groundless, for he held up his two hands imploringly, and made his petition in a moving manner. As he saw that the very excess of his emotion was militating against him, by restoring us more to our old relations, he became still more demonstrative. I glanced at Van Helsing, and saw my conviction reflected in his eyes; so I became a little more fixed in my manner, if not more stern, and motioned to him that his efforts were unavailing. I had previously seen something of the same constantly growing excitement in him when he had to make some request of which at the time he had thought much, such, for instance, as when he wanted a cat; and I was prepared to see the collapse into the same sullen acquiescence on this occasion. My expectation was not realised, for, when he found that his appeal would not be successful, he got into quite a frantic condition. He threw himself on his knees, and held up his hands, wringing them in plaintive supplication, and poured forth a torrent of entreaty, with the tears rolling down his cheeks, and his whole face and form expressive of the deepest emotion:--\n\n“Let me entreat you, Dr. Seward, oh, let me implore you, to let me out of this house at once. Send me away how you will and where you will; send keepers with me with whips and chains; let them take me in a strait-waistcoat, manacled and leg-ironed, even to a gaol; but let me go out of this. You don’t know what you do by keeping me here. I am speaking from the depths of my heart--of my very soul. You don’t know whom you wrong, or how; and I may not tell. Woe is me! I may not tell. By all you hold sacred--by all you hold dear--by your love that is lost--by your hope that lives--for the sake of the Almighty, take me out of this and save my soul from guilt! Can’t you hear me, man? Can’t you understand? Will you never learn? Don’t you know that I am sane and earnest now; that I am no lunatic in a mad fit, but a sane man fighting for his soul? Oh, hear me! hear me! Let me go! let me go! let me go!”\n\nI thought that the longer this went on the wilder he would get, and so would bring on a fit; so I took him by the hand and raised him up.\n\n“Come,” I said sternly, “no more of this; we have had quite enough already. Get to your bed and try to behave more discreetly.”\n\nHe suddenly stopped and looked at me intently for several moments. Then, without a word, he rose and moving over, sat down on the side of the bed. The collapse had come, as on former occasion, just as I had expected.\n\nWhen I was leaving the room, last of our party, he said to me in a quiet, well-bred voice:--\n\n“You will, I trust, Dr. Seward, do me the justice to bear in mind, later on, that I did what I could to convince you to-night.”\n\n# JONATHAN HARKER’S JOURNAL\n\n1 October, 5 a. m.--I went with the party to the search with an easy mind, for I think I never saw Mina so absolutely strong and well. I am so glad that she consented to hold back and let us men do the work. Somehow, it was a dread to me that she was in this fearful business at all; but now that her work is done, and that it is due to her energy and brains and foresight that the whole story is put together in such a way that every point tells, she may well feel that her part is finished, and that she can henceforth leave the rest to us. We were, I think, all a little upset by the scene with Mr. Renfield. When we came away from his room we were silent till we got back to the study. Then Mr. Morris said to Dr. Seward:--\n\n“Say, Jack, if that man wasn’t attempting a bluff, he is about the sanest lunatic I ever saw. I’m not sure, but I believe that he had some serious purpose, and if he had, it was pretty rough on him not to get a chance.” Lord Godalming and I were silent, but Dr. Van Helsing added:--\n\n“Friend John, you know more of lunatics than I do, and I’m glad of it, for I fear that if it had been to me to decide I would before that last hysterical outburst have given him free. But we live and learn, and in our present task we must take no chance, as my friend Quincey would say. All is best as they are.” Dr. Seward seemed to answer them both in a dreamy kind of way:--\n\n“I don’t know but that I agree with you. If that man had been an ordinary lunatic I would have taken my chance of trusting him; but he seems so mixed up with the Count in an indexy kind of way that I am afraid of doing anything wrong by helping his fads. I can’t forget how he prayed with almost equal fervour for a cat, and then tried to tear my throat out with his teeth. Besides, he called the Count ‘lord and master,’ and he may want to get out to help him in some diabolical way. That horrid thing has the wolves and the rats and his own kind to help him, so I suppose he isn’t above trying to use a respectable lunatic. He certainly did seem earnest, though. I only hope we have done what is best. These things, in conjunction with the wild work we have in hand, help to unnerve a man.” The Professor stepped over, and laying his hand on his shoulder, said in his grave, kindly way:--\n\n“Friend John, have no fear. We are trying to do our duty in a very sad and terrible case; we can only do as we deem best. What else have we to hope for, except the pity of the good God?” Lord Godalming had slipped away for a few minutes, but now he returned. He held up a little silver whistle, as he remarked:--\n\n“That old place may be full of rats, and if so, I’ve got an antidote on call.” Having passed the wall, we took our way to the house, taking care to keep in the shadows of the trees on the lawn when the moonlight shone out. When we got to the porch the Professor opened his bag and took out a lot of things, which he laid on the step, sorting them into four little groups, evidently one for each. Then he spoke:--\n\n“My friends, we are going into a terrible danger, and we need arms of many kinds. Our enemy is not merely spiritual. Remember that he has the strength of twenty men, and that, though our necks or our windpipes are of the common kind--and therefore breakable or crushable--his are not amenable to mere strength. A stronger man, or a body of men more strong in all than him, can at certain times hold him; but they cannot hurt him as we can be hurt by him. We must, therefore, guard ourselves from his touch. Keep this near your heart”--as he spoke he lifted a little silver crucifix and held it out to me, I being nearest to him--“put these flowers round your neck”--here he handed to me a wreath of withered garlic blossoms--“for other enemies more mundane, this revolver and this knife; and for aid in all, these so small electric lamps, which you can fasten to your breast; and for all, and above all at the last, this, which we must not desecrate needless.” This was a portion of Sacred Wafer, which he put in an envelope and handed to me. Each of the others was similarly equipped. “Now,” he said, “friend John, where are the skeleton keys? If so that we can open the door, we need not break house by the window, as before at Miss Lucy’s.”\n\nDr. Seward tried one or two skeleton keys, his mechanical dexterity as a surgeon standing him in good stead. Presently he got one to suit; after a little play back and forward the bolt yielded, and, with a rusty clang, shot back. We pressed on the door, the rusty hinges creaked, and it slowly opened. It was startlingly like the image conveyed to me in Dr. Seward’s diary of the opening of Miss Westenra’s tomb; I fancy that the same idea seemed to strike the others, for with one accord they shrank back. The Professor was the first to move forward, and stepped into the open door.\n\n“In manus tuas, Domine!” he said, crossing himself as he passed over the threshold. We closed the door behind us, lest when we should have lit our lamps we should possibly attract attention from the road. The Professor carefully tried the lock, lest we might not be able to open it from within should we be in a hurry making our exit. Then we all lit our lamps and proceeded on our search.\n\nThe light from the tiny lamps fell in all sorts of odd forms, as the rays crossed each other, or the opacity of our bodies threw great shadows. I could not for my life get away from the feeling that there was some one else amongst us. I suppose it was the recollection, so powerfully brought home to me by the grim surroundings, of that terrible experience in Transylvania. I think the feeling was common to us all, for I noticed that the others kept looking over their shoulders at every sound and every new shadow, just as I felt myself doing.\n\nThe whole place was thick with dust. The floor was seemingly inches deep, except where there were recent footsteps, in which on holding down my lamp I could see marks of hobnails where the dust was cracked. The walls were fluffy and heavy with dust, and in the corners were masses of spider’s webs, whereon the dust had gathered till they looked like old tattered rags as the weight had torn them partly down. On a table in the hall was a great bunch of keys, with a time-yellowed label on each. They had been used several times, for on the table were several similar rents in the blanket of dust, similar to that exposed when the Professor lifted them. He turned to me and said:--\n\n“You know this place, Jonathan. You have copied maps of it, and you know it at least more than we do. Which is the way to the chapel?” I had an idea of its direction, though on my former visit I had not been able to get admission to it; so I led the way, and after a few wrong turnings found myself opposite a low, arched oaken door, ribbed with iron bands. “This is the spot,” said the Professor as he turned his lamp on a small map of the house, copied from the file of my original correspondence regarding the purchase. With a little trouble we found the key on the bunch and opened the door. We were prepared for some unpleasantness, for as we were opening the door a faint, malodorous air seemed to exhale through the gaps, but none of us ever expected such an odour as we encountered. None of the others had met the Count at all at close quarters, and when I had seen him he was either in the fasting stage of his existence in his rooms or, when he was gloated with fresh blood, in a ruined building open to the air; but here the place was small and close, and the long disuse had made the air stagnant and foul. There was an earthy smell, as of some dry miasma, which came through the fouler air. But as to the odour itself, how shall I describe it? It was not alone that it was composed of all the ills of mortality and with the pungent, acrid smell of blood, but it seemed as though corruption had become itself corrupt. Faugh! it sickens me to think of it. Every breath exhaled by that monster seemed to have clung to the place and intensified its loathsomeness.\n\nUnder ordinary circumstances such a stench would have brought our enterprise to an end; but this was no ordinary case, and the high and terrible purpose in which we were involved gave us a strength which rose above merely physical considerations. After the involuntary shrinking consequent on the first nauseous whiff, we one and all set about our work as though that loathsome place were a garden of roses.\n\nWe made an accurate examination of the place, the Professor saying as we began:--\n\n“The first thing is to see how many of the boxes are left; we must then examine every hole and corner and cranny and see if we cannot get some clue as to what has become of the rest.” A glance was sufficient to show how many remained, for the great earth chests were bulky, and there was no mistaking them.\n\nThere were only twenty-nine left out of the fifty! Once I got a fright, for, seeing Lord Godalming suddenly turn and look out of the vaulted door into the dark passage beyond, I looked too, and for an instant my heart stood still. Somewhere, looking out from the shadow, I seemed to see the high lights of the Count’s evil face, the ridge of the nose, the red eyes, the red lips, the awful pallor. It was only for a moment, for, as Lord Godalming said, “I thought I saw a face, but it was only the shadows,” and resumed his inquiry, I turned my lamp in the direction, and stepped into the passage. There was no sign of any one; and as there were no corners, no doors, no aperture of any kind, but only the solid walls of the passage, there could be no hiding-place even for him. I took it that fear had helped imagination, and said nothing.\n\nA few minutes later I saw Morris step suddenly back from a corner, which he was examining. We all followed his movements with our eyes, for undoubtedly some nervousness was growing on us, and we saw a whole mass of phosphorescence, which twinkled like stars. We all instinctively drew back. The whole place was becoming alive with rats.\n\nFor a moment or two we stood appalled, all save Lord Godalming, who was seemingly prepared for such an emergency. Rushing over to the great iron-bound oaken door, which Dr. Seward had described from the outside, and which I had seen myself, he turned the key in the lock, drew the huge bolts, and swung the door open. Then, taking his little silver whistle from his pocket, he blew a low, shrill call. It was answered from behind Dr. Seward’s house by the yelping of dogs, and after about a minute three terriers came dashing round the corner of the house. Unconsciously we had all moved towards the door, and as we moved I noticed that the dust had been much disturbed: the boxes which had been taken out had been brought this way. But even in the minute that had elapsed the number of the rats had vastly increased. They seemed to swarm over the place all at once, till the lamplight, shining on their moving dark bodies and glittering, baleful eyes, made the place look like a bank of earth set with fireflies. The dogs dashed on, but at the threshold suddenly stopped and snarled, and then, simultaneously lifting their noses, began to howl in most lugubrious fashion. The rats were multiplying in thousands, and we moved out.\n\nLord Godalming lifted one of the dogs, and carrying him in, placed him on the floor. The instant his feet touched the ground he seemed to recover his courage, and rushed at his natural enemies. They fled before him so fast that before he had shaken the life out of a score, the other dogs, who had by now been lifted in the same manner, had but small prey ere the whole mass had vanished.\n\nWith their going it seemed as if some evil presence had departed, for the dogs frisked about and barked merrily as they made sudden darts at their prostrate foes, and turned them over and over and tossed them in the air with vicious shakes. We all seemed to find our spirits rise. Whether it was the purifying of the deadly atmosphere by the opening of the chapel door, or the relief which we experienced by finding ourselves in the open I know not; but most certainly the shadow of dread seemed to slip from us like a robe, and the occasion of our coming lost something of its grim significance, though we did not slacken a whit in our resolution. We closed the outer door and barred and locked it, and bringing the dogs with us, began our search of the house. We found nothing throughout except dust in extraordinary proportions, and all untouched save for my own footsteps when I had made my first visit. Never once did the dogs exhibit any symptom of uneasiness, and even when we returned to the chapel they frisked about as though they had been rabbit-hunting in a summer wood.\n\nThe morning was quickening in the east when we emerged from the front. Dr. Van Helsing had taken the key of the hall-door from the bunch, and locked the door in orthodox fashion, putting the key into his pocket when he had done.\n\n“So far,” he said, “our night has been eminently successful. No harm has come to us such as I feared might be and yet we have ascertained how many boxes are missing. More than all do I rejoice that this, our first--and perhaps our most difficult and dangerous--step has been accomplished without the bringing thereinto our most sweet Madam Mina or troubling her waking or sleeping thoughts with sights and sounds and smells of horror which she might never forget. One lesson, too, we have learned, if it be allowable to argue a particulari: that the brute beasts which are to the Count’s command are yet themselves not amenable to his spiritual power; for look, these rats that would come to his call, just as from his castle top he summon the wolves to your going and to that poor mother’s cry, though they come to him, they run pell-mell from the so little dogs of my friend Arthur. We have other matters before us, other dangers, other fears; and that monster--he has not used his power over the brute world for the only or the last time to-night. So be it that he has gone elsewhere. Good! It has given us opportunity to cry ‘check’ in some ways in this chess game, which we play for the stake of human souls. And now let us go home. The dawn is close at hand, and we have reason to be content with our first night’s work. It may be ordained that we have many nights and days to follow, if full of peril; but we must go on, and from no danger shall we shrink.”\n\nThe house was silent when we got back, save for some poor creature who was screaming away in one of the distant wards, and a low, moaning sound from Renfield’s room. The poor wretch was doubtless torturing himself, after the manner of the insane, with needless thoughts of pain.\n\nI came tiptoe into our own room, and found Mina asleep, breathing so softly that I had to put my ear down to hear it. She looks paler than usual. I hope the meeting to-night has not upset her. I am truly thankful that she is to be left out of our future work, and even of our deliberations. It is too great a strain for a woman to bear. I did not think so at first, but I know better now. Therefore I am glad that it is settled. There may be things which would frighten her to hear; and yet to conceal them from her might be worse than to tell her if once she suspected that there was any concealment. Henceforth our work is to be a sealed book to her, till at least such time as we can tell her that all is finished, and the earth free from a monster of the nether world. I daresay it will be difficult to begin to keep silence after such confidence as ours; but I must be resolute, and to-morrow I shall keep dark over to-night’s doings, and shall refuse to speak of anything that has happened. I rest on the sofa, so as not to disturb her.\n\n1 October, later.--I suppose it was natural that we should have all overslept ourselves, for the day was a busy one, and the night had no rest at all. Even Mina must have felt its exhaustion, for though I slept till the sun was high, I was awake before her, and had to call two or three times before she awoke. Indeed, she was so sound asleep that for a few seconds she did not recognize me, but looked at me with a sort of blank terror, as one looks who has been waked out of a bad dream. She complained a little of being tired, and I let her rest till later in the day. We now know of twenty-one boxes having been removed, and if it be that several were taken in any of these removals we may be able to trace them all. Such will, of course, immensely simplify our labour, and the sooner the matter is attended to the better. I shall look up Thomas Snelling to-day.\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n1 October.--It was towards noon when I was awakened by the Professor walking into my room. He was more jolly and cheerful than usual, and it is quite evident that last night’s work has helped to take some of the brooding weight off his mind. After going over the adventure of the night he suddenly said:--\n\n“Your patient interests me much. May it be that with you I visit him this morning? Or if that you are too occupy, I can go alone if it may be. It is a new experience to me to find a lunatic who talk philosophy, and reason so sound.” I had some work to do which pressed, so I told him that if he would go alone I would be glad, as then I should not have to keep him waiting; so I called an attendant and gave him the necessary instructions. Before the Professor left the room I cautioned him against getting any false impression from my patient. “But,” he answered, “I want him to talk of himself and of his delusion as to consuming live things. He said to Madam Mina, as I see in your diary of yesterday, that he had once had such a belief. Why do you smile, friend John?”\n\n“Excuse me,” I said, “but the answer is here.” I laid my hand on the type-written matter. “When our sane and learned lunatic made that very statement of how he used to consume life, his mouth was actually nauseous with the flies and spiders which he had eaten just before Mrs. Harker entered the room.” Van Helsing smiled in turn. “Good!” he said. “Your memory is true, friend John. I should have remembered. And yet it is this very obliquity of thought and memory which makes mental disease such a fascinating study. Perhaps I may gain more knowledge out of the folly of this madman than I shall from the teaching of the most wise. Who knows?” I went on with my work, and before long was through that in hand. It seemed that the time had been very short indeed, but there was Van Helsing back in the study. “Do I interrupt?” he asked politely as he stood at the door.\n\n“Not at all,” I answered. “Come in. My work is finished, and I am free. I can go with you now, if you like.\n\n“It is needless; I have seen him!”\n\n“Well?”\n\n“I fear that he does not appraise me at much. Our interview was short. When I entered his room he was sitting on a stool in the centre, with his elbows on his knees, and his face was the picture of sullen discontent. I spoke to him as cheerfully as I could, and with such a measure of respect as I could assume. He made no reply whatever. “Don’t you know me?” I asked. His answer was not reassuring: “I know you well enough; you are the old fool Van Helsing. I wish you would take yourself and your idiotic brain theories somewhere else. Damn all thick-headed Dutchmen!” Not a word more would he say, but sat in his implacable sullenness as indifferent to me as though I had not been in the room at all. Thus departed for this time my chance of much learning from this so clever lunatic; so I shall go, if I may, and cheer myself with a few happy words with that sweet soul Madam Mina. Friend John, it does rejoice me unspeakable that she is no more to be pained, no more to be worried with our terrible things. Though we shall much miss her help, it is better so.”\n\n“I agree with you with all my heart,” I answered earnestly, for I did not want him to weaken in this matter. “Mrs. Harker is better out of it. Things are quite bad enough for us, all men of the world, and who have been in many tight places in our time; but it is no place for a woman, and if she had remained in touch with the affair, it would in time infallibly have wrecked her.”\n\nSo Van Helsing has gone to confer with Mrs. Harker and Harker; Quincey and Art are all out following up the clues as to the earth-boxes. I shall finish my round of work and we shall meet to-night.\n\nMina Harker’s Journal.\n\n1 October.--It is strange to me to be kept in the dark as I am to-day; after Jonathan’s full confidence for so many years, to see him manifestly avoid certain matters, and those the most vital of all. This morning I slept late after the fatigues of yesterday, and though Jonathan was late too, he was the earlier. He spoke to me before he went out, never more sweetly or tenderly, but he never mentioned a word of what had happened in the visit to the Count’s house. And yet he must have known how terribly anxious I was. Poor dear fellow! I suppose it must have distressed him even more than it did me. They all agreed that it was best that I should not be drawn further into this awful work, and I acquiesced. But to think that he keeps anything from me! And now I am crying like a silly fool, when I know it comes from my husband’s great love and from the good, good wishes of those other strong men.\n\nThat has done me good. Well, some day Jonathan will tell me all; and lest it should ever be that he should think for a moment that I kept anything from him, I still keep my journal as usual. Then if he has feared of my trust I shall show it to him, with every thought of my heart put down for his dear eyes to read. I feel strangely sad and low-spirited to-day. I suppose it is the reaction from the terrible excitement.\n\nLast night I went to bed when the men had gone, simply because they told me to. I didn’t feel sleepy, and I did feel full of devouring anxiety. I kept thinking over everything that has been ever since Jonathan came to see me in London, and it all seems like a horrible tragedy, with fate pressing on relentlessly to some destined end. Everything that one does seems, no matter how right it may be, to bring on the very thing which is most to be deplored. If I hadn’t gone to Whitby, perhaps poor dear Lucy would be with us now. She hadn’t taken to visiting the churchyard till I came, and if she hadn’t come there in the day-time with me she wouldn’t have walked there in her sleep; and if she hadn’t gone there at night and asleep, that monster couldn’t have destroyed her as he did. Oh, why did I ever go to Whitby? There now, crying again! I wonder what has come over me to-day. I must hide it from Jonathan, for if he knew that I had been crying twice in one morning--I, who never cried on my own account, and whom he has never caused to shed a tear--the dear fellow would fret his heart out. I shall put a bold face on, and if I do feel weepy, he shall never see it. I suppose it is one of the lessons that we poor women have to learn....\n\nI can’t quite remember how I fell asleep last night. I remember hearing the sudden barking of the dogs and a lot of queer sounds, like praying on a very tumultuous scale, from Mr. Renfield’s room, which is somewhere under this. And then there was silence over everything, silence so profound that it startled me, and I got up and looked out of the window. All was dark and silent, the black shadows thrown by the moonlight seeming full of a silent mystery of their own. Not a thing seemed to be stirring, but all to be grim and fixed as death or fate; so that a thin streak of white mist, that crept with almost imperceptible slowness across the grass towards the house, seemed to have a sentience and a vitality of its own. I think that the digression of my thoughts must have done me good, for when I got back to bed I found a lethargy creeping over me. I lay a while, but could not quite sleep, so I got out and looked out of the window again. The mist was spreading, and was now close up to the house, so that I could see it lying thick against the wall, as though it were stealing up to the windows. The poor man was more loud than ever, and though I could not distinguish a word he said, I could in some way recognise in his tones some passionate entreaty on his part. Then there was the sound of a struggle, and I knew that the attendants were dealing with him. I was so frightened that I crept into bed, and pulled the clothes over my head, putting my fingers in my ears. I was not then a bit sleepy, at least so I thought; but I must have fallen asleep, for, except dreams, I do not remember anything until the morning, when Jonathan woke me. I think that it took me an effort and a little time to realise where I was, and that it was Jonathan who was bending over me. My dream was very peculiar, and was almost typical of the way that waking thoughts become merged in, or continued in, dreams.\n\nI thought that I was asleep, and waiting for Jonathan to come back. I was very anxious about him, and I was powerless to act; my feet, and my hands, and my brain were weighted, so that nothing could proceed at the usual pace. And so I slept uneasily and thought. Then it began to dawn upon me that the air was heavy, and dank, and cold. I put back the clothes from my face, and found, to my surprise, that all was dim around. The gaslight which I had left lit for Jonathan, but turned down, came only like a tiny red spark through the fog, which had evidently grown thicker and poured into the room. Then it occurred to me that I had shut the window before I had come to bed. I would have got out to make certain on the point, but some leaden lethargy seemed to chain my limbs and even my will. I lay still and endured; that was all. I closed my eyes, but could still see through my eyelids. (It is wonderful what tricks our dreams play us, and how conveniently we can imagine.) The mist grew thicker and thicker and I could see now how it came in, for I could see it like smoke--or with the white energy of boiling water--pouring in, not through the window, but through the joinings of the door. It got thicker and thicker, till it seemed as if it became concentrated into a sort of pillar of cloud in the room, through the top of which I could see the light of the gas shining like a red eye. Things began to whirl through my brain just as the cloudy column was now whirling in the room, and through it all came the scriptural words “a pillar of cloud by day and of fire by night.” Was it indeed some such spiritual guidance that was coming to me in my sleep? But the pillar was composed of both the day and the night-guiding, for the fire was in the red eye, which at the thought got a new fascination for me; till, as I looked, the fire divided, and seemed to shine on me through the fog like two red eyes, such as Lucy told me of in her momentary mental wandering when, on the cliff, the dying sunlight struck the windows of St. Mary’s Church. Suddenly the horror burst upon me that it was thus that Jonathan had seen those awful women growing into reality through the whirling mist in the moonlight, and in my dream I must have fainted, for all became black darkness. The last conscious effort which imagination made was to show me a livid white face bending over me out of the mist. I must be careful of such dreams, for they would unseat one’s reason if there were too much of them. I would get Dr. Van Helsing or Dr. Seward to prescribe something for me which would make me sleep, only that I fear to alarm them. Such a dream at the present time would become woven into their fears for me. To-night I shall strive hard to sleep naturally. If I do not, I shall to-morrow night get them to give me a dose of chloral; that cannot hurt me for once, and it will give me a good night’s sleep. Last night tired me more than if I had not slept at all.\n\n2 October 10 p. m.--Last night I slept, but did not dream. I must have slept soundly, for I was not waked by Jonathan coming to bed; but the sleep has not refreshed me, for to-day I feel terribly weak and spiritless. I spent all yesterday trying to read, or lying down dozing. In the afternoon Mr. Renfield asked if he might see me. Poor man, he was very gentle, and when I came away he kissed my hand and bade God bless me. Some way it affected me much; I am crying when I think of him. This is a new weakness, of which I must be careful. Jonathan would be miserable if he knew I had been crying. He and the others were out till dinner-time, and they all came in tired. I did what I could to brighten them up, and I suppose that the effort did me good, for I forgot how tired I was. After dinner they sent me to bed, and all went off to smoke together, as they said, but I knew that they wanted to tell each other of what had occurred to each during the day; I could see from Jonathan’s manner that he had something important to communicate. I was not so sleepy as I should have been; so before they went I asked Dr. Seward to give me a little opiate of some kind, as I had not slept well the night before. He very kindly made me up a sleeping draught, which he gave to me, telling me that it would do me no harm, as it was very mild.... I have taken it, and am waiting for sleep, which still keeps aloof. I hope I have not done wrong, for as sleep begins to flirt with me, a new fear comes: that I may have been foolish in thus depriving myself of the power of waking. I might want it. Here comes sleep. Good-night.\n\n# JONATHAN HARKER’S JOURNAL\n\n1 October, evening.--I found Thomas Snelling in his house at Bethnal Green, but unhappily he was not in a condition to remember anything. The very prospect of beer which my expected coming had opened to him had proved too much, and he had begun too early on his expected debauch. I learned, however, from his wife, who seemed a decent, poor soul, that he was only the assistant to Smollet, who of the two mates was the responsible person. So off I drove to Walworth, and found Mr. Joseph Smollet at home and in his shirtsleeves, taking a late tea out of a saucer. He is a decent, intelligent fellow, distinctly a good, reliable type of workman, and with a headpiece of his own. He remembered all about the incident of the boxes, and from a wonderful dog’s-eared notebook, which he produced from some mysterious receptacle about the seat of his trousers, and which had hieroglyphical entries in thick, half-obliterated pencil, he gave me the destinations of the boxes. There were, he said, six in the cartload which he took from Carfax and left at 197, Chicksand Street, Mile End New Town, and another six which he deposited at Jamaica Lane, Bermondsey. If then the Count meant to scatter these ghastly refuges of his over London, these places were chosen as the first of delivery, so that later he might distribute more fully. The systematic manner in which this was done made me think that he could not mean to confine himself to two sides of London. He was now fixed on the far east of the northern shore, on the east of the southern shore, and on the south. The north and west were surely never meant to be left out of his diabolical scheme--let alone the City itself and the very heart of fashionable London in the south-west and west. I went back to Smollet, and asked him if he could tell us if any other boxes had been taken from Carfax.\n\nHe replied:--\n\n“Well, guv’nor, you’ve treated me wery ’an’some”--I had given him half a sovereign--“an’ I’ll tell yer all I know. I heard a man by the name of Bloxam say four nights ago in the ’Are an’ ’Ounds, in Pincher’s Alley, as ’ow he an’ his mate ’ad ’ad a rare dusty job in a old ’ouse at Purfect. There ain’t a-many such jobs as this ’ere, an’ I’m thinkin’ that maybe Sam Bloxam could tell ye summut.” I asked if he could tell me where to find him. I told him that if he could get me the address it would be worth another half-sovereign to him. So he gulped down the rest of his tea and stood up, saying that he was going to begin the search then and there. At the door he stopped, and said:--\n\n“Look ’ere, guv’nor, there ain’t no sense in me a-keepin’ you ’ere. I may find Sam soon, or I mayn’t; but anyhow he ain’t like to be in a way to tell ye much to-night. Sam is a rare one when he starts on the booze. If you can give me a envelope with a stamp on it, and put yer address on it, I’ll find out where Sam is to be found and post it ye to-night. But ye’d better be up arter ’im soon in the mornin’, or maybe ye won’t ketch ’im; for Sam gets off main early, never mind the booze the night afore.”\n\nThis was all practical, so one of the children went off with a penny to buy an envelope and a sheet of paper, and to keep the change. When she came back, I addressed the envelope and stamped it, and when Smollet had again faithfully promised to post the address when found, I took my way to home. We’re on the track anyhow. I am tired to-night, and want sleep. Mina is fast asleep, and looks a little too pale; her eyes look as though she had been crying. Poor dear, I’ve no doubt it frets her to be kept in the dark, and it may make her doubly anxious about me and the others. But it is best as it is. It is better to be disappointed and worried in such a way now than to have her nerve broken. The doctors were quite right to insist on her being kept out of this dreadful business. I must be firm, for on me this particular burden of silence must rest. I shall not ever enter on the subject with her under any circumstances. Indeed, it may not be a hard task, after all, for she herself has become reticent on the subject, and has not spoken of the Count or his doings ever since we told her of our decision.\n\n2 October, evening.--A long and trying and exciting day. By the first post I got my directed envelope with a dirty scrap of paper enclosed, on which was written with a carpenter’s pencil in a sprawling hand:--\n\n“Sam Bloxam, Korkrans, 4, Poters Cort, Bartel Street, Walworth. Arsk for the depite.”\n\nI got the letter in bed, and rose without waking Mina. She looked heavy and sleepy and pale, and far from well. I determined not to wake her, but that, when I should return from this new search, I would arrange for her going back to Exeter. I think she would be happier in our own home, with her daily tasks to interest her, than in being here amongst us and in ignorance. I only saw Dr. Seward for a moment, and told him where I was off to, promising to come back and tell the rest so soon as I should have found out anything. I drove to Walworth and found, with some difficulty, Potter’s Court. Mr. Smollet’s spelling misled me, as I asked for Poter’s Court instead of Potter’s Court. However, when I had found the court, I had no difficulty in discovering Corcoran’s lodging-house. When I asked the man who came to the door for the “depite,” he shook his head, and said: “I dunno ’im. There ain’t no such a person ’ere; I never ’eard of ’im in all my bloomin’ days. Don’t believe there ain’t nobody of that kind livin’ ere or anywheres.” I took out Smollet’s letter, and as I read it it seemed to me that the lesson of the spelling of the name of the court might guide me. “What are you?” I asked.\n\n“I’m the depity,” he answered. I saw at once that I was on the right track; phonetic spelling had again misled me. A half-crown tip put the deputy’s knowledge at my disposal, and I learned that Mr. Bloxam, who had slept off the remains of his beer on the previous night at Corcoran’s, had left for his work at Poplar at five o’clock that morning. He could not tell me where the place of work was situated, but he had a vague idea that it was some kind of a “new-fangled ware’us”; and with this slender clue I had to start for Poplar. It was twelve o’clock before I got any satisfactory hint of such a building, and this I got at a coffee-shop, where some workmen were having their dinner. One of these suggested that there was being erected at Cross Angel Street a new “cold storage” building; and as this suited the condition of a “new-fangled ware’us,” I at once drove to it. An interview with a surly gatekeeper and a surlier foreman, both of whom were appeased with the coin of the realm, put me on the track of Bloxam; he was sent for on my suggesting that I was willing to pay his day’s wages to his foreman for the privilege of asking him a few questions on a private matter. He was a smart enough fellow, though rough of speech and bearing. When I had promised to pay for his information and given him an earnest, he told me that he had made two journeys between Carfax and a house in Piccadilly, and had taken from this house to the latter nine great boxes--“main heavy ones”--with a horse and cart hired by him for this purpose. I asked him if he could tell me the number of the house in Piccadilly, to which he replied:--\n\n“Well, guv’nor, I forgits the number, but it was only a few doors from a big white church or somethink of the kind, not long built. It was a dusty old ’ouse, too, though nothin’ to the dustiness of the ’ouse we tooked the bloomin’ boxes from.”\n\n“How did you get into the houses if they were both empty?”\n\n“There was the old party what engaged me a-waitin’ in the ’ouse at Purfleet. He ’elped me to lift the boxes and put them in the dray. Curse me, but he was the strongest chap I ever struck, an’ him a old feller, with a white moustache, one that thin you would think he couldn’t throw a shadder.”\n\nHow this phrase thrilled through me!\n\n“Why, ’e took up ’is end o’ the boxes like they was pounds of tea, and me a-puffin’ an’ a-blowin’ afore I could up-end mine anyhow--an’ I’m no chicken, neither.”\n\n“How did you get into the house in Piccadilly?” I asked.\n\n“He was there too. He must ’a’ started off and got there afore me, for when I rung of the bell he kem an’ opened the door ’isself an’ ’elped me to carry the boxes into the ’all.”\n\n“The whole nine?” I asked.\n\n“Yus; there was five in the first load an’ four in the second. It was main dry work, an’ I don’t so well remember ’ow I got ’ome.” I interrupted him:--\n\n“Were the boxes left in the hall?”\n\n“Yus; it was a big ’all, an’ there was nothin’ else in it.” I made one more attempt to further matters:--\n\n“You didn’t have any key?”\n\n“Never used no key nor nothink. The old gent, he opened the door ’isself an’ shut it again when I druv off. I don’t remember the last time--but that was the beer.”\n\n“And you can’t remember the number of the house?”\n\n“No, sir. But ye needn’t have no difficulty about that. It’s a ’igh ’un with a stone front with a bow on it, an’ ’igh steps up to the door. I know them steps, ’avin’ ’ad to carry the boxes up with three loafers what come round to earn a copper. The old gent give them shillin’s, an’ they seein’ they got so much, they wanted more; but ’e took one of them by the shoulder and was like to throw ’im down the steps, till the lot of them went away cussin’.” I thought that with this description I could find the house, so, having paid my friend for his information, I started off for Piccadilly. I had gained a new painful experience; the Count could, it was evident, handle the earth-boxes himself. If so, time was precious; for, now that he had achieved a certain amount of distribution, he could, by choosing his own time, complete the task unobserved. At Piccadilly Circus I discharged my cab, and walked westward; beyond the Junior Constitutional I came across the house described, and was satisfied that this was the next of the lairs arranged by Dracula. The house looked as though it had been long untenanted. The windows were encrusted with dust, and the shutters were up. All the framework was black with time, and from the iron the paint had mostly scaled away. It was evident that up to lately there had been a large notice-board in front of the balcony; it had, however, been roughly torn away, the uprights which had supported it still remaining. Behind the rails of the balcony I saw there were some loose boards, whose raw edges looked white. I would have given a good deal to have been able to see the notice-board intact, as it would, perhaps, have given some clue to the ownership of the house. I remembered my experience of the investigation and purchase of Carfax, and I could not but feel that if I could find the former owner there might be some means discovered of gaining access to the house.\n\nThere was at present nothing to be learned from the Piccadilly side, and nothing could be done; so I went round to the back to see if anything could be gathered from this quarter. The mews were active, the Piccadilly houses being mostly in occupation. I asked one or two of the grooms and helpers whom I saw around if they could tell me anything about the empty house. One of them said that he heard it had lately been taken, but he couldn’t say from whom. He told me, however, that up to very lately there had been a notice-board of “For Sale” up, and that perhaps Mitchell, Sons, & Candy, the house agents, could tell me something, as he thought he remembered seeing the name of that firm on the board. I did not wish to seem too eager, or to let my informant know or guess too much, so, thanking him in the usual manner, I strolled away. It was now growing dusk, and the autumn night was closing in, so I did not lose any time. Having learned the address of Mitchell, Sons, & Candy from a directory at the Berkeley, I was soon at their office in Sackville Street.\n\nThe gentleman who saw me was particularly suave in manner, but uncommunicative in equal proportion. Having once told me that the Piccadilly house--which throughout our interview he called a “mansion”--was sold, he considered my business as concluded. When I asked who had purchased it, he opened his eyes a thought wider, and paused a few seconds before replying:--\n\n“It is sold, sir.”\n\n“Pardon me,” I said, with equal politeness, “but I have a special reason for wishing to know who purchased it.”\n\nAgain he paused longer, and raised his eyebrows still more. “It is sold, sir,” was again his laconic reply.\n\n“Surely,” I said, “you do not mind letting me know so much.”\n\n“But I do mind,” he answered. “The affairs of their clients are absolutely safe in the hands of Mitchell, Sons, & Candy.” This was manifestly a prig of the first water, and there was no use arguing with him. I thought I had best meet him on his own ground, so I said:--\n\n“Your clients, sir, are happy in having so resolute a guardian of their confidence. I am myself a professional man.” Here I handed him my card. “In this instance I am not prompted by curiosity; I act on the part of Lord Godalming, who wishes to know something of the property which was, he understood, lately for sale.” These words put a different complexion on affairs. He said:--\n\n“I would like to oblige you if I could, Mr. Harker, and especially would I like to oblige his lordship. We once carried out a small matter of renting some chambers for him when he was the Honourable Arthur Holmwood. If you will let me have his lordship’s address I will consult the House on the subject, and will, in any case, communicate with his lordship by to-night’s post. It will be a pleasure if we can so far deviate from our rules as to give the required information to his lordship.”\n\nI wanted to secure a friend, and not to make an enemy, so I thanked him, gave the address at Dr. Seward’s and came away. It was now dark, and I was tired and hungry. I got a cup of tea at the Aërated Bread Company and came down to Purfleet by the next train.\n\nI found all the others at home. Mina was looking tired and pale, but she made a gallant effort to be bright and cheerful, it wrung my heart to think that I had had to keep anything from her and so caused her inquietude. Thank God, this will be the last night of her looking on at our conferences, and feeling the sting of our not showing our confidence. It took all my courage to hold to the wise resolution of keeping her out of our grim task. She seems somehow more reconciled; or else the very subject seems to have become repugnant to her, for when any accidental allusion is made she actually shudders. I am glad we made our resolution in time, as with such a feeling as this, our growing knowledge would be torture to her.\n\nI could not tell the others of the day’s discovery till we were alone; so after dinner--followed by a little music to save appearances even amongst ourselves--I took Mina to her room and left her to go to bed. The dear girl was more affectionate with me than ever, and clung to me as though she would detain me; but there was much to be talked of and I came away. Thank God, the ceasing of telling things has made no difference between us.\n\nWhen I came down again I found the others all gathered round the fire in the study. In the train I had written my diary so far, and simply read it off to them as the best means of letting them get abreast of my own information; when I had finished Van Helsing said:--\n\n“This has been a great day’s work, friend Jonathan. Doubtless we are on the track of the missing boxes. If we find them all in that house, then our work is near the end. But if there be some missing, we must search until we find them. Then shall we make our final coup, and hunt the wretch to his real death.” We all sat silent awhile and all at once Mr. Morris spoke:--\n\n“Say! how are we going to get into that house?”\n\n“We got into the other,” answered Lord Godalming quickly.\n\n“But, Art, this is different. We broke house at Carfax, but we had night and a walled park to protect us. It will be a mighty different thing to commit burglary in Piccadilly, either by day or night. I confess I don’t see how we are going to get in unless that agency duck can find us a key of some sort; perhaps we shall know when you get his letter in the morning.” Lord Godalming’s brows contracted, and he stood up and walked about the room. By-and-by he stopped and said, turning from one to another of us:--\n\n“Quincey’s head is level. This burglary business is getting serious; we got off once all right; but we have now a rare job on hand--unless we can find the Count’s key basket.”\n\nAs nothing could well be done before morning, and as it would be at least advisable to wait till Lord Godalming should hear from Mitchell’s, we decided not to take any active step before breakfast time. For a good while we sat and smoked, discussing the matter in its various lights and bearings; I took the opportunity of bringing this diary right up to the moment. I am very sleepy and shall go to bed....\n\nJust a line. Mina sleeps soundly and her breathing is regular. Her forehead is puckered up into little wrinkles, as though she thinks even in her sleep. She is still too pale, but does not look so haggard as she did this morning. To-morrow will, I hope, mend all this; she will be herself at home in Exeter. Oh, but I am sleepy!\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n1 October.--I am puzzled afresh about Renfield. His moods change so rapidly that I find it difficult to keep touch of them, and as they always mean something more than his own well-being, they form a more than interesting study. This morning, when I went to see him after his repulse of Van Helsing, his manner was that of a man commanding destiny. He was, in fact, commanding destiny--subjectively. He did not really care for any of the things of mere earth; he was in the clouds and looked down on all the weaknesses and wants of us poor mortals. I thought I would improve the occasion and learn something, so I asked him:--\n\n“What about the flies these times?” He smiled on me in quite a superior sort of way--such a smile as would have become the face of Malvolio--as he answered me:--\n\n“The fly, my dear sir, has one striking feature; its wings are typical of the aërial powers of the psychic faculties. The ancients did well when they typified the soul as a butterfly!”\n\nI thought I would push his analogy to its utmost logically, so I said quickly:--\n\n“Oh, it is a soul you are after now, is it?” His madness foiled his reason, and a puzzled look spread over his face as, shaking his head with a decision which I had but seldom seen in him, he said:--\n\n“Oh, no, oh no! I want no souls. Life is all I want.” Here he brightened up; “I am pretty indifferent about it at present. Life is all right; I have all I want. You must get a new patient, doctor, if you wish to study zoöphagy!”\n\nThis puzzled me a little, so I drew him on:--\n\n“Then you command life; you are a god, I suppose?” He smiled with an ineffably benign superiority.\n\n“Oh no! Far be it from me to arrogate to myself the attributes of the Deity. I am not even concerned in His especially spiritual doings. If I may state my intellectual position I am, so far as concerns things purely terrestrial, somewhat in the position which Enoch occupied spiritually!” This was a poser to me. I could not at the moment recall Enoch’s appositeness; so I had to ask a simple question, though I felt that by so doing I was lowering myself in the eyes of the lunatic:--\n\n“And why with Enoch?”\n\n“Because he walked with God.” I could not see the analogy, but did not like to admit it; so I harked back to what he had denied:--\n\n“So you don’t care about life and you don’t want souls. Why not?” I put my question quickly and somewhat sternly, on purpose to disconcert him. The effort succeeded; for an instant he unconsciously relapsed into his old servile manner, bent low before me, and actually fawned upon me as he replied:--\n\n“I don’t want any souls, indeed, indeed! I don’t. I couldn’t use them if I had them; they would be no manner of use to me. I couldn’t eat them or----” He suddenly stopped and the old cunning look spread over his face, like a wind-sweep on the surface of the water. “And doctor, as to life, what is it after all? When you’ve got all you require, and you know that you will never want, that is all. I have friends--good friends--like you, Dr. Seward”; this was said with a leer of inexpressible cunning. “I know that I shall never lack the means of life!”\n\nI think that through the cloudiness of his insanity he saw some antagonism in me, for he at once fell back on the last refuge of such as he--a dogged silence. After a short time I saw that for the present it was useless to speak to him. He was sulky, and so I came away.\n\nLater in the day he sent for me. Ordinarily I would not have come without special reason, but just at present I am so interested in him that I would gladly make an effort. Besides, I am glad to have anything to help to pass the time. Harker is out, following up clues; and so are Lord Godalming and Quincey. Van Helsing sits in my study poring over the record prepared by the Harkers; he seems to think that by accurate knowledge of all details he will light upon some clue. He does not wish to be disturbed in the work, without cause. I would have taken him with me to see the patient, only I thought that after his last repulse he might not care to go again. There was also another reason: Renfield might not speak so freely before a third person as when he and I were alone.\n\nI found him sitting out in the middle of the floor on his stool, a pose which is generally indicative of some mental energy on his part. When I came in, he said at once, as though the question had been waiting on his lips:--\n\n“What about souls?” It was evident then that my surmise had been correct. Unconscious cerebration was doing its work, even with the lunatic. I determined to have the matter out. “What about them yourself?” I asked. He did not reply for a moment but looked all round him, and up and down, as though he expected to find some inspiration for an answer.\n\n“I don’t want any souls!” he said in a feeble, apologetic way. The matter seemed preying on his mind, and so I determined to use it--to “be cruel only to be kind.” So I said:--\n\n“You like life, and you want life?”\n\n“Oh yes! but that is all right; you needn’t worry about that!”\n\n“But,” I asked, “how are we to get the life without getting the soul also?” This seemed to puzzle him, so I followed it up:--\n\n“A nice time you’ll have some time when you’re flying out there, with the souls of thousands of flies and spiders and birds and cats buzzing and twittering and miauing all round you. You’ve got their lives, you know, and you must put up with their souls!” Something seemed to affect his imagination, for he put his fingers to his ears and shut his eyes, screwing them up tightly just as a small boy does when his face is being soaped. There was something pathetic in it that touched me; it also gave me a lesson, for it seemed that before me was a child--only a child, though the features were worn, and the stubble on the jaws was white. It was evident that he was undergoing some process of mental disturbance, and, knowing how his past moods had interpreted things seemingly foreign to himself, I thought I would enter into his mind as well as I could and go with him. The first step was to restore confidence, so I asked him, speaking pretty loud so that he would hear me through his closed ears:--\n\n“Would you like some sugar to get your flies round again?” He seemed to wake up all at once, and shook his head. With a laugh he replied:--\n\n“Not much! flies are poor things, after all!” After a pause he added, “But I don’t want their souls buzzing round me, all the same.”\n\n“Or spiders?” I went on.\n\n“Blow spiders! What’s the use of spiders? There isn’t anything in them to eat or”--he stopped suddenly, as though reminded of a forbidden topic.\n\n“So, so!” I thought to myself, “this is the second time he has suddenly stopped at the word ‘drink’; what does it mean?” Renfield seemed himself aware of having made a lapse, for he hurried on, as though to distract my attention from it:--\n\n“I don’t take any stock at all in such matters. ‘Rats and mice and such small deer,’ as Shakespeare has it, ‘chicken-feed of the larder’ they might be called. I’m past all that sort of nonsense. You might as well ask a man to eat molecules with a pair of chop-sticks, as to try to interest me about the lesser carnivora, when I know of what is before me.”\n\n“I see,” I said. “You want big things that you can make your teeth meet in? How would you like to breakfast on elephant?”\n\n“What ridiculous nonsense you are talking!” He was getting too wide awake, so I thought I would press him hard. “I wonder,” I said reflectively, “what an elephant’s soul is like!”\n\nThe effect I desired was obtained, for he at once fell from his high-horse and became a child again.\n\n“I don’t want an elephant’s soul, or any soul at all!” he said. For a few moments he sat despondently. Suddenly he jumped to his feet, with his eyes blazing and all the signs of intense cerebral excitement. “To hell with you and your souls!” he shouted. “Why do you plague me about souls? Haven’t I got enough to worry, and pain, and distract me already, without thinking of souls!” He looked so hostile that I thought he was in for another homicidal fit, so I blew my whistle. The instant, however, that I did so he became calm, and said apologetically:--\n\n“Forgive me, Doctor; I forgot myself. You do not need any help. I am so worried in my mind that I am apt to be irritable. If you only knew the problem I have to face, and that I am working out, you would pity, and tolerate, and pardon me. Pray do not put me in a strait-waistcoat. I want to think and I cannot think freely when my body is confined. I am sure you will understand!” He had evidently self-control; so when the attendants came I told them not to mind, and they withdrew. Renfield watched them go; when the door was closed he said, with considerable dignity and sweetness:--\n\n“Dr. Seward, you have been very considerate towards me. Believe me that I am very, very grateful to you!” I thought it well to leave him in this mood, and so I came away. There is certainly something to ponder over in this man’s state. Several points seem to make what the American interviewer calls “a story,” if one could only get them in proper order. Here they are:--\n\nWill not mention “drinking.”\n\nFears the thought of being burdened with the “soul” of anything.\n\nHas no dread of wanting “life” in the future.\n\nDespises the meaner forms of life altogether, though he dreads being haunted by their souls.\n\nLogically all these things point one way! he has assurance of some kind that he will acquire some higher life. He dreads the consequence--the burden of a soul. Then it is a human life he looks to!\n\nAnd the assurance--?\n\nMerciful God! the Count has been to him, and there is some new scheme of terror afoot!\n\nLater.--I went after my round to Van Helsing and told him my suspicion. He grew very grave; and, after thinking the matter over for a while asked me to take him to Renfield. I did so. As we came to the door we heard the lunatic within singing gaily, as he used to do in the time which now seems so long ago. When we entered we saw with amazement that he had spread out his sugar as of old; the flies, lethargic with the autumn, were beginning to buzz into the room. We tried to make him talk of the subject of our previous conversation, but he would not attend. He went on with his singing, just as though we had not been present. He had got a scrap of paper and was folding it into a note-book. We had to come away as ignorant as we went in.\n\nHis is a curious case indeed; we must watch him to-night.\n\n“1 October.\n\n“My Lord,\n\n“We are at all times only too happy to meet your wishes. We beg, with regard to the desire of your Lordship, expressed by Mr. Harker on your behalf, to supply the following information concerning the sale and purchase of No. 347, Piccadilly. The original vendors are the executors of the late Mr. Archibald Winter-Suffield. The purchaser is a foreign nobleman, Count de Ville, who effected the purchase himself paying the purchase money in notes ‘over the counter,’ if your Lordship will pardon us using so vulgar an expression. Beyond this we know nothing whatever of him.\n\n“We are, my Lord,\n\n“Your Lordship’s humble servants,\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n2 October.--I placed a man in the corridor last night, and told him to make an accurate note of any sound he might hear from Renfield’s room, and gave him instructions that if there should be anything strange he was to call me. After dinner, when we had all gathered round the fire in the study--Mrs. Harker having gone to bed--we discussed the attempts and discoveries of the day. Harker was the only one who had any result, and we are in great hopes that his clue may be an important one.\n\nBefore going to bed I went round to the patient’s room and looked in through the observation trap. He was sleeping soundly, and his heart rose and fell with regular respiration.\n\nThis morning the man on duty reported to me that a little after midnight he was restless and kept saying his prayers somewhat loudly. I asked him if that was all; he replied that it was all he heard. There was something about his manner so suspicious that I asked him point blank if he had been asleep. He denied sleep, but admitted to having “dozed” for a while. It is too bad that men cannot be trusted unless they are watched.\n\nTo-day Harker is out following up his clue, and Art and Quincey are looking after horses. Godalming thinks that it will be well to have horses always in readiness, for when we get the information which we seek there will be no time to lose. We must sterilise all the imported earth between sunrise and sunset; we shall thus catch the Count at his weakest, and without a refuge to fly to. Van Helsing is off to the British Museum looking up some authorities on ancient medicine. The old physicians took account of things which their followers do not accept, and the Professor is searching for witch and demon cures which may be useful to us later.\n\nI sometimes think we must be all mad and that we shall wake to sanity in strait-waistcoats.\n\nLater.--We have met again. We seem at last to be on the track, and our work of to-morrow may be the beginning of the end. I wonder if Renfield’s quiet has anything to do with this. His moods have so followed the doings of the Count, that the coming destruction of the monster may be carried to him in some subtle way. If we could only get some hint as to what passed in his mind, between the time of my argument with him to-day and his resumption of fly-catching, it might afford us a valuable clue. He is now seemingly quiet for a spell.... Is he?---- That wild yell seemed to come from his room....\n\nThe attendant came bursting into my room and told me that Renfield had somehow met with some accident. He had heard him yell; and when he went to him found him lying on his face on the floor, all covered with blood. I must go at once....\n\n# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY\n\n3 October.--Let me put down with exactness all that happened, as well as I can remember it, since last I made an entry. Not a detail that I can recall must be forgotten; in all calmness I must proceed.\n\nWhen I came to Renfield’s room I found him lying on the floor on his left side in a glittering pool of blood. When I went to move him, it became at once apparent that he had received some terrible injuries; there seemed none of that unity of purpose between the parts of the body which marks even lethargic sanity. As the face was exposed I could see that it was horribly bruised, as though it had been beaten against the floor--indeed it was from the face wounds that the pool of blood originated. The attendant who was kneeling beside the body said to me as we turned him over:--\n\n“I think, sir, his back is broken. See, both his right arm and leg and the whole side of his face are paralysed.” How such a thing could have happened puzzled the attendant beyond measure. He seemed quite bewildered, and his brows were gathered in as he said:--\n\n“I can’t understand the two things. He could mark his face like that by beating his own head on the floor. I saw a young woman do it once at the Eversfield Asylum before anyone could lay hands on her. And I suppose he might have broke his neck by falling out of bed, if he got in an awkward kink. But for the life of me I can’t imagine how the two things occurred. If his back was broke, he couldn’t beat his head; and if his face was like that before the fall out of bed, there would be marks of it.” I said to him:--\n\n“Go to Dr. Van Helsing, and ask him to kindly come here at once. I want him without an instant’s delay.” The man ran off, and within a few minutes the Professor, in his dressing gown and slippers, appeared. When he saw Renfield on the ground, he looked keenly at him a moment, and then turned to me. I think he recognised my thought in my eyes, for he said very quietly, manifestly for the ears of the attendant:--\n\n“Ah, a sad accident! He will need very careful watching, and much attention. I shall stay with you myself; but I shall first dress myself. If you will remain I shall in a few minutes join you.”\n\nThe patient was now breathing stertorously and it was easy to see that he had suffered some terrible injury. Van Helsing returned with extraordinary celerity, bearing with him a surgical case. He had evidently been thinking and had his mind made up; for, almost before he looked at the patient, he whispered to me:--\n\n“Send the attendant away. We must be alone with him when he becomes conscious, after the operation.” So I said:--\n\n“I think that will do now, Simmons. We have done all that we can at present. You had better go your round, and Dr. Van Helsing will operate. Let me know instantly if there be anything unusual anywhere.”\n\nThe man withdrew, and we went into a strict examination of the patient. The wounds of the face was superficial; the real injury was a depressed fracture of the skull, extending right up through the motor area. The Professor thought a moment and said:--\n\n“We must reduce the pressure and get back to normal conditions, as far as can be; the rapidity of the suffusion shows the terrible nature of his injury. The whole motor area seems affected. The suffusion of the brain will increase quickly, so we must trephine at once or it may be too late.” As he was speaking there was a soft tapping at the door. I went over and opened it and found in the corridor without, Arthur and Quincey in pajamas and slippers: the former spoke:--\n\n“I heard your man call up Dr. Van Helsing and tell him of an accident. So I woke Quincey or rather called for him as he was not asleep. Things are moving too quickly and too strangely for sound sleep for any of us these times. I’ve been thinking that to-morrow night will not see things as they have been. We’ll have to look back--and forward a little more than we have done. May we come in?” I nodded, and held the door open till they had entered; then I closed it again. When Quincey saw the attitude and state of the patient, and noted the horrible pool on the floor, he said softly:--\n\n“My God! what has happened to him? Poor, poor devil!” I told him briefly, and added that we expected he would recover consciousness after the operation--for a short time, at all events. He went at once and sat down on the edge of the bed, with Godalming beside him; we all watched in patience.\n\n“We shall wait,” said Van Helsing, “just long enough to fix the best spot for trephining, so that we may most quickly and perfectly remove the blood clot; for it is evident that the hæmorrhage is increasing.”\n\nThe minutes during which we waited passed with fearful slowness. I had a horrible sinking in my heart, and from Van Helsing’s face I gathered that he felt some fear or apprehension as to what was to come. I dreaded the words that Renfield might speak. I was positively afraid to think; but the conviction of what was coming was on me, as I have read of men who have heard the death-watch. The poor man’s breathing came in uncertain gasps. Each instant he seemed as though he would open his eyes and speak; but then would follow a prolonged stertorous breath, and he would relapse into a more fixed insensibility. Inured as I was to sick beds and death, this suspense grew, and grew upon me. I could almost hear the beating of my own heart; and the blood surging through my temples sounded like blows from a hammer. The silence finally became agonising. I looked at my companions, one after another, and saw from their flushed faces and damp brows that they were enduring equal torture. There was a nervous suspense over us all, as though overhead some dread bell would peal out powerfully when we should least expect it.\n\nAt last there came a time when it was evident that the patient was sinking fast; he might die at any moment. I looked up at the Professor and caught his eyes fixed on mine. His face was sternly set as he spoke:--\n\n“There is no time to lose. His words may be worth many lives; I have been thinking so, as I stood here. It may be there is a soul at stake! We shall operate just above the ear.”\n\nWithout another word he made the operation. For a few moments the breathing continued to be stertorous. Then there came a breath so prolonged that it seemed as though it would tear open his chest. Suddenly his eyes opened, and became fixed in a wild, helpless stare. This was continued for a few moments; then it softened into a glad surprise, and from the lips came a sigh of relief. He moved convulsively, and as he did so, said:--\n\n“I’ll be quiet, Doctor. Tell them to take off the strait-waistcoat. I have had a terrible dream, and it has left me so weak that I cannot move. What’s wrong with my face? it feels all swollen, and it smarts dreadfully.” He tried to turn his head; but even with the effort his eyes seemed to grow glassy again so I gently put it back. Then Van Helsing said in a quiet grave tone:--\n\n“Tell us your dream, Mr. Renfield.” As he heard the voice his face brightened, through its mutilation, and he said:--\n\n“That is Dr. Van Helsing. How good it is of you to be here. Give me some water, my lips are dry; and I shall try to tell you. I dreamed”--he stopped and seemed fainting, I called quietly to Quincey--“The brandy--it is in my study--quick!” He flew and returned with a glass, the decanter of brandy and a carafe of water. We moistened the parched lips, and the patient quickly revived. It seemed, however, that his poor injured brain had been working in the interval, for, when he was quite conscious, he looked at me piercingly with an agonised confusion which I shall never forget, and said:--\n\n“I must not deceive myself; it was no dream, but all a grim reality.” Then his eyes roved round the room; as they caught sight of the two figures sitting patiently on the edge of the bed he went on:--\n\n“If I were not sure already, I would know from them.” For an instant his eyes closed--not with pain or sleep but voluntarily, as though he were bringing all his faculties to bear; when he opened them he said, hurriedly, and with more energy than he had yet displayed:--\n\n“Quick, Doctor, quick. I am dying! I feel that I have but a few minutes; and then I must go back to death--or worse! Wet my lips with brandy again. I have something that I must say before I die; or before my poor crushed brain dies anyhow. Thank you! It was that night after you left me, when I implored you to let me go away. I couldn’t speak then, for I felt my tongue was tied; but I was as sane then, except in that way, as I am now. I was in an agony of despair for a long time after you left me; it seemed hours. Then there came a sudden peace to me. My brain seemed to become cool again, and I realised where I was. I heard the dogs bark behind our house, but not where He was!” As he spoke, Van Helsing’s eyes never blinked, but his hand came out and met mine and gripped it hard. He did not, however, betray himself; he nodded slightly and said: “Go on,” in a low voice. Renfield proceeded:--\n\n“He came up to the window in the mist, as I had seen him often before; but he was solid then--not a ghost, and his eyes were fierce like a man’s when angry. He was laughing with his red mouth; the sharp white teeth glinted in the moonlight when he turned to look back over the belt of trees, to where the dogs were barking. I wouldn’t ask him to come in at first, though I knew he wanted to--just as he had wanted all along. Then he began promising me things--not in words but by doing them.” He was interrupted by a word from the Professor:--\n“How?”\n\n“By making them happen; just as he used to send in the flies when the sun was shining. Great big fat ones with steel and sapphire on their wings; and big moths, in the night, with skull and cross-bones on their backs.” Van Helsing nodded to him as he whispered to me unconsciously:--\n\n“The Acherontia Aitetropos of the Sphinges--what you call the ‘Death’s-head Moth’?” The patient went on without stopping.\n\n“Then he began to whisper: ‘Rats, rats, rats! Hundreds, thousands, millions of them, and every one a life; and dogs to eat them, and cats too. All lives! all red blood, with years of life in it; and not merely buzzing flies!’ I laughed at him, for I wanted to see what he could do. Then the dogs howled, away beyond the dark trees in His house. He beckoned me to the window. I got up and looked out, and He raised his hands, and seemed to call out without using any words. A dark mass spread over the grass, coming on like the shape of a flame of fire; and then He moved the mist to the right and left, and I could see that there were thousands of rats with their eyes blazing red--like His, only smaller. He held up his hand, and they all stopped; and I thought he seemed to be saying: ‘All these lives will I give you, ay, and many more and greater, through countless ages, if you will fall down and worship me!’ And then a red cloud, like the colour of blood, seemed to close over my eyes; and before I knew what I was doing, I found myself opening the sash and saying to Him: ‘Come in, Lord and Master!’ The rats were all gone, but He slid into the room through the sash, though it was only open an inch wide--just as the Moon herself has often come in through the tiniest crack and has stood before me in all her size and splendour.”\n\nHis voice was weaker, so I moistened his lips with the brandy again, and he continued; but it seemed as though his memory had gone on working in the interval for his story was further advanced. I was about to call him back to the point, but Van Helsing whispered to me: “Let him go on. Do not interrupt him; he cannot go back, and maybe could not proceed at all if once he lost the thread of his thought.” He proceeded:--\n\n“All day I waited to hear from him, but he did not send me anything, not even a blow-fly, and when the moon got up I was pretty angry with him. When he slid in through the window, though it was shut, and did not even knock, I got mad with him. He sneered at me, and his white face looked out of the mist with his red eyes gleaming, and he went on as though he owned the whole place, and I was no one. He didn’t even smell the same as he went by me. I couldn’t hold him. I thought that, somehow, Mrs. Harker had come into the room.”\n\nThe two men sitting on the bed stood up and came over, standing behind him so that he could not see them, but where they could hear better. They were both silent, but the Professor started and quivered; his face, however, grew grimmer and sterner still. Renfield went on without noticing:--\n\n“When Mrs. Harker came in to see me this afternoon she wasn’t the same; it was like tea after the teapot had been watered.” Here we all moved, but no one said a word; he went on:--\n\n“I didn’t know that she was here till she spoke; and she didn’t look the same. I don’t care for the pale people; I like them with lots of blood in them, and hers had all seemed to have run out. I didn’t think of it at the time; but when she went away I began to think, and it made me mad to know that He had been taking the life out of her.” I could feel that the rest quivered, as I did, but we remained otherwise still. “So when He came to-night I was ready for Him. I saw the mist stealing in, and I grabbed it tight. I had heard that madmen have unnatural strength; and as I knew I was a madman--at times anyhow--I resolved to use my power. Ay, and He felt it too, for He had to come out of the mist to struggle with me. I held tight; and I thought I was going to win, for I didn’t mean Him to take any more of her life, till I saw His eyes. They burned into me, and my strength became like water. He slipped through it, and when I tried to cling to Him, He raised me up and flung me down. There was a red cloud before me, and a noise like thunder, and the mist seemed to steal away under the door.” His voice was becoming fainter and his breath more stertorous. Van Helsing stood up instinctively.\n\n“We know the worst now,” he said. “He is here, and we know his purpose. It may not be too late. Let us be armed--the same as we were the other night, but lose no time; there is not an instant to spare.” There was no need to put our fear, nay our conviction, into words--we shared them in common. We all hurried and took from our rooms the same things that we had when we entered the Count’s house. The Professor had his ready, and as we met in the corridor he pointed to them significantly as he said:--\n\n“They never leave me; and they shall not till this unhappy business is over. Be wise also, my friends. It is no common enemy that we deal with. Alas! alas! that that dear Madam Mina should suffer!” He stopped; his voice was breaking, and I do not know if rage or terror predominated in my own heart.\n\nOutside the Harkers’ door we paused. Art and Quincey held back, and the latter said:--\n\n“Should we disturb her?”\n\n“We must,” said Van Helsing grimly. “If the door be locked, I shall break it in.”\n\n“May it not frighten her terribly? It is unusual to break into a lady’s room!”\n\nVan Helsing said solemnly, “You are always right; but this is life and death. All chambers are alike to the doctor; and even were they not they are all as one to me to-night. Friend John, when I turn the handle, if the door does not open, do you put your shoulder down and shove; and you too, my friends. Now!”\n\nHe turned the handle as he spoke, but the door did not yield. We threw ourselves against it; with a crash it burst open, and we almost fell headlong into the room. The Professor did actually fall, and I saw across him as he gathered himself up from hands and knees. What I saw appalled me. I felt my hair rise like bristles on the back of my neck, and my heart seemed to stand still.\n\nThe moonlight was so bright that through the thick yellow blind the room was light enough to see. On the bed beside the window lay Jonathan Harker, his face flushed and breathing heavily as though in a stupor. Kneeling on the near edge of the bed facing outwards was the white-clad figure of his wife. By her side stood a tall, thin man, clad in black. His face was turned from us, but the instant we saw we all recognised the Count--in every way, even to the scar on his forehead. With his left hand he held both Mrs. Harker’s hands, keeping them away with her arms at full tension; his right hand gripped her by the back of the neck, forcing her face down on his bosom. Her white nightdress was smeared with blood, and a thin stream trickled down the man’s bare breast which was shown by his torn-open dress. The attitude of the two had a terrible resemblance to a child forcing a kitten’s nose into a saucer of milk to compel it to drink. As we burst into the room, the Count turned his face, and the hellish look that I had heard described seemed to leap into it. His eyes flamed red with devilish passion; the great nostrils of the white aquiline nose opened wide and quivered at the edge; and the white sharp teeth, behind the full lips of the blood-dripping mouth, champed together like those of a wild beast. With a wrench, which threw his victim back upon the bed as though hurled from a height, he turned and sprang at us. But by this time the Professor had gained his feet, and was holding towards him the envelope which contained the Sacred Wafer. The Count suddenly stopped, just as poor Lucy had done outside the tomb, and cowered back. Further and further back he cowered, as we, lifting our crucifixes, advanced. The moonlight suddenly failed, as a great black cloud sailed across the sky; and when the gaslight sprang up under Quincey’s match, we saw nothing but a faint vapour. This, as we looked, trailed under the door, which with the recoil from its bursting open, had swung back to its old position. Van Helsing, Art, and I moved forward to Mrs. Harker, who by this time had drawn her breath and with it had given a scream so wild, so ear-piercing, so despairing that it seems to me now that it will ring in my ears till my dying day. For a few seconds she lay in her helpless attitude and disarray. Her face was ghastly, with a pallor which was accentuated by the blood which smeared her lips and cheeks and chin; from her throat trickled a thin stream of blood; her eyes were mad with terror. Then she put before her face her poor crushed hands, which bore on their whiteness the red mark of the Count’s terrible grip, and from behind them came a low desolate wail which made the terrible scream seem only the quick expression of an endless grief. Van Helsing stepped forward and drew the coverlet gently over her body, whilst Art, after looking at her face for an instant despairingly, ran out of the room. Van Helsing whispered to me:--\n\n“Jonathan is in a stupor such as we know the Vampire can produce. We can do nothing with poor Madam Mina for a few moments till she recovers herself; I must wake him!” He dipped the end of a towel in cold water and with it began to flick him on the face, his wife all the while holding her face between her hands and sobbing in a way that was heart-breaking to hear. I raised the blind, and looked out of the window. There was much moonshine; and as I looked I could see Quincey Morris run across the lawn and hide himself in the shadow of a great yew-tree. It puzzled me to think why he was doing this; but at the instant I heard Harker’s quick exclamation as he woke to partial consciousness, and turned to the bed. On his face, as there might well be, was a look of wild amazement. He seemed dazed for a few seconds, and then full consciousness seemed to burst upon him all at once, and he started up. His wife was aroused by the quick movement, and turned to him with her arms stretched out, as though to embrace him; instantly, however, she drew them in again, and putting her elbows together, held her hands before her face, and shuddered till the bed beneath her shook.\n\n“In God’s name what does this mean?” Harker cried out. “Dr. Seward, Dr. Van Helsing, what is it? What has happened? What is wrong? Mina, dear, what is it? What does that blood mean? My God, my God! has it come to this!” and, raising himself to his knees, he beat his hands wildly together. “Good God help us! help her! oh, help her!” With a quick movement he jumped from bed, and began to pull on his clothes,--all the man in him awake at the need for instant exertion. “What has happened? Tell me all about it!” he cried without pausing. “Dr. Van Helsing, you love Mina, I know. Oh, do something to save her. It cannot have gone too far yet. Guard her while I look for him!” His wife, through her terror and horror and distress, saw some sure danger to him: instantly forgetting her own grief, she seized hold of him and cried out:--\n\n“No! no! Jonathan, you must not leave me. I have suffered enough to-night, God knows, without the dread of his harming you. You must stay with me. Stay with these friends who will watch over you!” Her expression became frantic as she spoke; and, he yielding to her, she pulled him down sitting on the bed side, and clung to him fiercely.\n\nVan Helsing and I tried to calm them both. The Professor held up his little golden crucifix, and said with wonderful calmness:--\n\n“Do not fear, my dear. We are here; and whilst this is close to you no foul thing can approach. You are safe for to-night; and we must be calm and take counsel together.” She shuddered and was silent, holding down her head on her husband’s breast. When she raised it, his white night-robe was stained with blood where her lips had touched, and where the thin open wound in her neck had sent forth drops. The instant she saw it she drew back, with a low wail, and whispered, amidst choking sobs:--\n\n“Unclean, unclean! I must touch him or kiss him no more. Oh, that it should be that it is I who am now his worst enemy, and whom he may have most cause to fear.” To this he spoke out resolutely:--\n\n“Nonsense, Mina. It is a shame to me to hear such a word. I would not hear it of you; and I shall not hear it from you. May God judge me by my deserts, and punish me with more bitter suffering than even this hour, if by any act or will of mine anything ever come between us!” He put out his arms and folded her to his breast; and for a while she lay there sobbing. He looked at us over her bowed head, with eyes that blinked damply above his quivering nostrils; his mouth was set as steel. After a while her sobs became less frequent and more faint, and then he said to me, speaking with a studied calmness which I felt tried his nervous power to the utmost:--\n\n“And now, Dr. Seward, tell me all about it. Too well I know the broad fact; tell me all that has been.” I told him exactly what had happened, and he listened with seeming impassiveness; but his nostrils twitched and his eyes blazed as I told how the ruthless hands of the Count had held his wife in that terrible and horrid position, with her mouth to the open wound in his breast. It interested me, even at that moment, to see, that, whilst the face of white set passion worked convulsively over the bowed head, the hands tenderly and lovingly stroked the ruffled hair. Just as I had finished, Quincey and Godalming knocked at the door. They entered in obedience to our summons. Van Helsing looked at me questioningly. I understood him to mean if we were to take advantage of their coming to divert if possible the thoughts of the unhappy husband and wife from each other and from themselves; so on nodding acquiescence to him he asked them what they had seen or done. To which Lord Godalming answered:--\n\n“I could not see him anywhere in the passage, or in any of our rooms. I looked in the study but, though he had been there, he had gone. He had, however----” He stopped suddenly, looking at the poor drooping figure on the bed. Van Helsing said gravely:--\n\n“Go on, friend Arthur. We want here no more concealments. Our hope now is in knowing all. Tell freely!” So Art went on:--\n\n“He had been there, and though it could only have been for a few seconds, he made rare hay of the place. All the manuscript had been burned, and the blue flames were flickering amongst the white ashes; the cylinders of your phonograph too were thrown on the fire, and the wax had helped the flames.” Here I interrupted. “Thank God there is the other copy in the safe!” His face lit for a moment, but fell again as he went on: “I ran downstairs then, but could see no sign of him. I looked into Renfield’s room; but there was no trace there except----!” Again he paused. “Go on,” said Harker hoarsely; so he bowed his head and moistening his lips with his tongue, added: “except that the poor fellow is dead.” Mrs. Harker raised her head, looking from one to the other of us she said solemnly:--\n\n“God’s will be done!” I could not but feel that Art was keeping back something; but, as I took it that it was with a purpose, I said nothing. Van Helsing turned to Morris and asked:--\n\n“And you, friend Quincey, have you any to tell?”\n\n“A little,” he answered. “It may be much eventually, but at present I can’t say. I thought it well to know if possible where the Count would go when he left the house. I did not see him; but I saw a bat rise from Renfield’s window, and flap westward. I expected to see him in some shape go back to Carfax; but he evidently sought some other lair. He will not be back to-night; for the sky is reddening in the east, and the dawn is close. We must work to-morrow!”\n\nHe said the latter words through his shut teeth. For a space of perhaps a couple of minutes there was silence, and I could fancy that I could hear the sound of our hearts beating; then Van Helsing said, placing his hand very tenderly on Mrs. Harker’s head:--\n\n“And now, Madam Mina--poor, dear, dear Madam Mina--tell us exactly what happened. God knows that I do not want that you be pained; but it is need that we know all. For now more than ever has all work to be done quick and sharp, and in deadly earnest. The day is close to us that must end all, if it may be so; and now is the chance that we may live and learn.”\n\nThe poor, dear lady shivered, and I could see the tension of her nerves as she clasped her husband closer to her and bent her head lower and lower still on his breast. Then she raised her head proudly, and held out one hand to Van Helsing who took it in his, and, after stooping and kissing it reverently, held it fast. The other hand was locked in that of her husband, who held his other arm thrown round her protectingly. After a pause in which she was evidently ordering her thoughts, she began:--\n\n“I took the sleeping draught which you had so kindly given me, but for a long time it did not act. I seemed to become more wakeful, and myriads of horrible fancies began to crowd in upon my mind--all of them connected with death, and vampires; with blood, and pain, and trouble.” Her husband involuntarily groaned as she turned to him and said lovingly: “Do not fret, dear. You must be brave and strong, and help me through the horrible task. If you only knew what an effort it is to me to tell of this fearful thing at all, you would understand how much I need your help. Well, I saw I must try to help the medicine to its work with my will, if it was to do me any good, so I resolutely set myself to sleep. Sure enough sleep must soon have come to me, for I remember no more. Jonathan coming in had not waked me, for he lay by my side when next I remember. There was in the room the same thin white mist that I had before noticed. But I forget now if you know of this; you will find it in my diary which I shall show you later. I felt the same vague terror which had come to me before and the same sense of some presence. I turned to wake Jonathan, but found that he slept so soundly that it seemed as if it was he who had taken the sleeping draught, and not I. I tried, but I could not wake him. This caused me a great fear, and I looked around terrified. Then indeed, my heart sank within me: beside the bed, as if he had stepped out of the mist--or rather as if the mist had turned into his figure, for it had entirely disappeared--stood a tall, thin man, all in black. I knew him at once from the description of the others. The waxen face; the high aquiline nose, on which the light fell in a thin white line; the parted red lips, with the sharp white teeth showing between; and the red eyes that I had seemed to see in the sunset on the windows of St. Mary’s Church at Whitby. I knew, too, the red scar on his forehead where Jonathan had struck him. For an instant my heart stood still, and I would have screamed out, only that I was paralysed. In the pause he spoke in a sort of keen, cutting whisper, pointing as he spoke to Jonathan:--\n\n“‘Silence! If you make a sound I shall take him and dash his brains out before your very eyes.’ I was appalled and was too bewildered to do or say anything. With a mocking smile, he placed one hand upon my shoulder and, holding me tight, bared my throat with the other, saying as he did so, ‘First, a little refreshment to reward my exertions. You may as well be quiet; it is not the first time, or the second, that your veins have appeased my thirst!’ I was bewildered, and, strangely enough, I did not want to hinder him. I suppose it is a part of the horrible curse that such is, when his touch is on his victim. And oh, my God, my God, pity me! He placed his reeking lips upon my throat!” Her husband groaned again. She clasped his hand harder, and looked at him pityingly, as if he were the injured one, and went on:--\n\n“I felt my strength fading away, and I was in a half swoon. How long this horrible thing lasted I know not; but it seemed that a long time must have passed before he took his foul, awful, sneering mouth away. I saw it drip with the fresh blood!” The remembrance seemed for a while to overpower her, and she drooped and would have sunk down but for her husband’s sustaining arm. With a great effort she recovered herself and went on:--\n\n“Then he spoke to me mockingly, ‘And so you, like the others, would play your brains against mine. You would help these men to hunt me and frustrate me in my designs! You know now, and they know in part already, and will know in full before long, what it is to cross my path. They should have kept their energies for use closer to home. Whilst they played wits against me--against me who commanded nations, and intrigued for them, and fought for them, hundreds of years before they were born--I was countermining them. And you, their best beloved one, are now to me, flesh of my flesh; blood of my blood; kin of my kin; my bountiful wine-press for a while; and shall be later on my companion and my helper. You shall be avenged in turn; for not one of them but shall minister to your needs. But as yet you are to be punished for what you have done. You have aided in thwarting me; now you shall come to my call. When my brain says “Come!” to you, you shall cross land or sea to do my bidding; and to that end this!’ With that he pulled open his shirt, and with his long sharp nails opened a vein in his breast. When the blood began to spurt out, he took my hands in one of his, holding them tight, and with the other seized my neck and pressed my mouth to the wound, so that I must either suffocate or swallow some of the---- Oh my God! my God! what have I done? What have I done to deserve such a fate, I who have tried to walk in meekness and righteousness all my days. God pity me! Look down on a poor soul in worse than mortal peril; and in mercy pity those to whom she is dear!” Then she began to rub her lips as though to cleanse them from pollution.\n\nAs she was telling her terrible story, the eastern sky began to quicken, and everything became more and more clear. Harker was still and quiet; but over his face, as the awful narrative went on, came a grey look which deepened and deepened in the morning light, till when the first red streak of the coming dawn shot up, the flesh stood darkly out against the whitening hair.\n\nWe have arranged that one of us is to stay within call of the unhappy pair till we can meet together and arrange about taking action.\n\nOf this I am sure: the sun rises to-day on no more miserable house in all the great round of its daily course.\n\n# JONATHAN HARKER’S JOURNAL\n\n3 October.--As I must do something or go mad, I write this diary. It is now six o’clock, and we are to meet in the study in half an hour and take something to eat; for Dr. Van Helsing and Dr. Seward are agreed that if we do not eat we cannot work our best. Our best will be, God knows, required to-day. I must keep writing at every chance, for I dare not stop to think. All, big and little, must go down; perhaps at the end the little things may teach us most. The teaching, big or little, could not have landed Mina or me anywhere worse than we are to-day. However, we must trust and hope. Poor Mina told me just now, with the tears running down her dear cheeks, that it is in trouble and trial that our faith is tested--that we must keep on trusting; and that God will aid us up to the end. The end! oh my God! what end?... To work! To work!\n\nWhen Dr. Van Helsing and Dr. Seward had come back from seeing poor Renfield, we went gravely into what was to be done. First, Dr. Seward told us that when he and Dr. Van Helsing had gone down to the room below they had found Renfield lying on the floor, all in a heap. His face was all bruised and crushed in, and the bones of the neck were broken.\n\nDr. Seward asked the attendant who was on duty in the passage if he had heard anything. He said that he had been sitting down--he confessed to half dozing--when he heard loud voices in the room, and then Renfield had called out loudly several times, “God! God! God!” after that there was a sound of falling, and when he entered the room he found him lying on the floor, face down, just as the doctors had seen him. Van Helsing asked if he had heard “voices” or “a voice,” and he said he could not say; that at first it had seemed to him as if there were two, but as there was no one in the room it could have been only one. He could swear to it, if required, that the word “God” was spoken by the patient. Dr. Seward said to us, when we were alone, that he did not wish to go into the matter; the question of an inquest had to be considered, and it would never do to put forward the truth, as no one would believe it. As it was, he thought that on the attendant’s evidence he could give a certificate of death by misadventure in falling from bed. In case the coroner should demand it, there would be a formal inquest, necessarily to the same result.\n\nWhen the question began to be discussed as to what should be our next step, the very first thing we decided was that Mina should be in full confidence; that nothing of any sort--no matter how painful--should be kept from her. She herself agreed as to its wisdom, and it was pitiful to see her so brave and yet so sorrowful, and in such a depth of despair. “There must be no concealment,” she said, “Alas! we have had too much already. And besides there is nothing in all the world that can give me more pain than I have already endured--than I suffer now! Whatever may happen, it must be of new hope or of new courage to me!” Van Helsing was looking at her fixedly as she spoke, and said, suddenly but quietly:--\n\n“But dear Madam Mina, are you not afraid; not for yourself, but for others from yourself, after what has happened?” Her face grew set in its lines, but her eyes shone with the devotion of a martyr as she answered:--\n\n“Ah no! for my mind is made up!”\n\n“To what?” he asked gently, whilst we were all very still; for each in our own way we had a sort of vague idea of what she meant. Her answer came with direct simplicity, as though she were simply stating a fact:--\n\n“Because if I find in myself--and I shall watch keenly for it--a sign of harm to any that I love, I shall die!”\n\n“You would not kill yourself?” he asked, hoarsely.\n\n“I would; if there were no friend who loved me, who would save me such a pain, and so desperate an effort!” She looked at him meaningly as she spoke. He was sitting down; but now he rose and came close to her and put his hand on her head as he said solemnly:\n\n“My child, there is such an one if it were for your good. For myself I could hold it in my account with God to find such an euthanasia for you, even at this moment if it were best. Nay, were it safe! But my child----” For a moment he seemed choked, and a great sob rose in his throat; he gulped it down and went on:--\n\n“There are here some who would stand between you and death. You must not die. You must not die by any hand; but least of all by your own. Until the other, who has fouled your sweet life, is true dead you must not die; for if he is still with the quick Un-Dead, your death would make you even as he is. No, you must live! You must struggle and strive to live, though death would seem a boon unspeakable. You must fight Death himself, though he come to you in pain or in joy; by the day, or the night; in safety or in peril! On your living soul I charge you that you do not die--nay, nor think of death--till this great evil be past.” The poor dear grew white as death, and shock and shivered, as I have seen a quicksand shake and shiver at the incoming of the tide. We were all silent; we could do nothing. At length she grew more calm and turning to him said, sweetly, but oh! so sorrowfully, as she held out her hand:--\n\n“I promise you, my dear friend, that if God will let me live, I shall strive to do so; till, if it may be in His good time, this horror may have passed away from me.” She was so good and brave that we all felt that our hearts were strengthened to work and endure for her, and we began to discuss what we were to do. I told her that she was to have all the papers in the safe, and all the papers or diaries and phonographs we might hereafter use; and was to keep the record as she had done before. She was pleased with the prospect of anything to do--if “pleased” could be used in connection with so grim an interest.\n\nAs usual Van Helsing had thought ahead of everyone else, and was prepared with an exact ordering of our work.\n\n“It is perhaps well,” he said, “that at our meeting after our visit to Carfax we decided not to do anything with the earth-boxes that lay there. Had we done so, the Count must have guessed our purpose, and would doubtless have taken measures in advance to frustrate such an effort with regard to the others; but now he does not know our intentions. Nay, more, in all probability, he does not know that such a power exists to us as can sterilise his lairs, so that he cannot use them as of old. We are now so much further advanced in our knowledge as to their disposition that, when we have examined the house in Piccadilly, we may track the very last of them. To-day, then, is ours; and in it rests our hope. The sun that rose on our sorrow this morning guards us in its course. Until it sets to-night, that monster must retain whatever form he now has. He is confined within the limitations of his earthly envelope. He cannot melt into thin air nor disappear through cracks or chinks or crannies. If he go through a doorway, he must open the door like a mortal. And so we have this day to hunt out all his lairs and sterilise them. So we shall, if we have not yet catch him and destroy him, drive him to bay in some place where the catching and the destroying shall be, in time, sure.” Here I started up for I could not contain myself at the thought that the minutes and seconds so preciously laden with Mina’s life and happiness were flying from us, since whilst we talked action was impossible. But Van Helsing held up his hand warningly. “Nay, friend Jonathan,” he said, “in this, the quickest way home is the longest way, so your proverb say. We shall all act and act with desperate quick, when the time has come. But think, in all probable the key of the situation is in that house in Piccadilly. The Count may have many houses which he has bought. Of them he will have deeds of purchase, keys and other things. He will have paper that he write on; he will have his book of cheques. There are many belongings that he must have somewhere; why not in this place so central, so quiet, where he come and go by the front or the back at all hour, when in the very vast of the traffic there is none to notice. We shall go there and search that house; and when we learn what it holds, then we do what our friend Arthur call, in his phrases of hunt ‘stop the earths’ and so we run down our old fox--so? is it not?”\n\n“Then let us come at once,” I cried, “we are wasting the precious, precious time!” The Professor did not move, but simply said:--\n\n“And how are we to get into that house in Piccadilly?”\n\n“Any way!” I cried. “We shall break in if need be.”\n\n“And your police; where will they be, and what will they say?”\n\nI was staggered; but I knew that if he wished to delay he had a good reason for it. So I said, as quietly as I could:--\n\n“Don’t wait more than need be; you know, I am sure, what torture I am in.”\n\n“Ah, my child, that I do; and indeed there is no wish of me to add to your anguish. But just think, what can we do, until all the world be at movement. Then will come our time. I have thought and thought, and it seems to me that the simplest way is the best of all. Now we wish to get into the house, but we have no key; is it not so?” I nodded.\n\n“Now suppose that you were, in truth, the owner of that house, and could not still get in; and think there was to you no conscience of the housebreaker, what would you do?”\n\n“I should get a respectable locksmith, and set him to work to pick the lock for me.”\n\n“And your police, they would interfere, would they not?”\n\n“Oh, no! not if they knew the man was properly employed.”\n\n“Then,” he looked at me as keenly as he spoke, “all that is in doubt is the conscience of the employer, and the belief of your policemen as to whether or no that employer has a good conscience or a bad one. Your police must indeed be zealous men and clever--oh, so clever!--in reading the heart, that they trouble themselves in such matter. No, no, my friend Jonathan, you go take the lock off a hundred empty house in this your London, or of any city in the world; and if you do it as such things are rightly done, and at the time such things are rightly done, no one will interfere. I have read of a gentleman who owned a so fine house in London, and when he went for months of summer to Switzerland and lock up his house, some burglar came and broke window at back and got in. Then he went and made open the shutters in front and walk out and in through the door, before the very eyes of the police. Then he have an auction in that house, and advertise it, and put up big notice; and when the day come he sell off by a great auctioneer all the goods of that other man who own them. Then he go to a builder, and he sell him that house, making an agreement that he pull it down and take all away within a certain time. And your police and other authority help him all they can. And when that owner come back from his holiday in Switzerland he find only an empty hole where his house had been. This was all done en règle; and in our work we shall be en règle too. We shall not go so early that the policemen who have then little to think of, shall deem it strange; but we shall go after ten o’clock, when there are many about, and such things would be done were we indeed owners of the house.”\n\nI could not but see how right he was and the terrible despair of Mina’s face became relaxed a thought; there was hope in such good counsel. Van Helsing went on:--\n\n“When once within that house we may find more clues; at any rate some of us can remain there whilst the rest find the other places where there be more earth-boxes--at Bermondsey and Mile End.”\n\nLord Godalming stood up. “I can be of some use here,” he said. “I shall wire to my people to have horses and carriages where they will be most convenient.”\n\n“Look here, old fellow,” said Morris, “it is a capital idea to have all ready in case we want to go horsebacking; but don’t you think that one of your snappy carriages with its heraldic adornments in a byway of Walworth or Mile End would attract too much attention for our purposes? It seems to me that we ought to take cabs when we go south or east; and even leave them somewhere near the neighbourhood we are going to.”\n\n“Friend Quincey is right!” said the Professor. “His head is what you call in plane with the horizon. It is a difficult thing that we go to do, and we do not want no peoples to watch us if so it may.”\n\nMina took a growing interest in everything and I was rejoiced to see that the exigency of affairs was helping her to forget for a time the terrible experience of the night. She was very, very pale--almost ghastly, and so thin that her lips were drawn away, showing her teeth in somewhat of prominence. I did not mention this last, lest it should give her needless pain; but it made my blood run cold in my veins to think of what had occurred with poor Lucy when the Count had sucked her blood. As yet there was no sign of the teeth growing sharper; but the time as yet was short, and there was time for fear.\n\nWhen we came to the discussion of the sequence of our efforts and of the disposition of our forces, there were new sources of doubt. It was finally agreed that before starting for Piccadilly we should destroy the Count’s lair close at hand. In case he should find it out too soon, we should thus be still ahead of him in our work of destruction; and his presence in his purely material shape, and at his weakest, might give us some new clue.\n\nAs to the disposal of forces, it was suggested by the Professor that, after our visit to Carfax, we should all enter the house in Piccadilly; that the two doctors and I should remain there, whilst Lord Godalming and Quincey found the lairs at Walworth and Mile End and destroyed them. It was possible, if not likely, the Professor urged, that the Count might appear in Piccadilly during the day, and that if so we might be able to cope with him then and there. At any rate, we might be able to follow him in force. To this plan I strenuously objected, and so far as my going was concerned, for I said that I intended to stay and protect Mina, I thought that my mind was made up on the subject; but Mina would not listen to my objection. She said that there might be some law matter in which I could be useful; that amongst the Count’s papers might be some clue which I could understand out of my experience in Transylvania; and that, as it was, all the strength we could muster was required to cope with the Count’s extraordinary power. I had to give in, for Mina’s resolution was fixed; she said that it was the last hope for her that we should all work together. “As for me,” she said, “I have no fear. Things have been as bad as they can be; and whatever may happen must have in it some element of hope or comfort. Go, my husband! God can, if He wishes it, guard me as well alone as with any one present.” So I started up crying out: “Then in God’s name let us come at once, for we are losing time. The Count may come to Piccadilly earlier than we think.”\n\n“Not so!” said Van Helsing, holding up his hand.\n\n“But why?” I asked.\n\n“Do you forget,” he said, with actually a smile, “that last night he banqueted heavily, and will sleep late?”\n\nDid I forget! shall I ever--can I ever! Can any of us ever forget that terrible scene! Mina struggled hard to keep her brave countenance; but the pain overmastered her and she put her hands before her face, and shuddered whilst she moaned. Van Helsing had not intended to recall her frightful experience. He had simply lost sight of her and her part in the affair in his intellectual effort. When it struck him what he said, he was horrified at his thoughtlessness and tried to comfort her. “Oh, Madam Mina,” he said, “dear, dear Madam Mina, alas! that I of all who so reverence you should have said anything so forgetful. These stupid old lips of mine and this stupid old head do not deserve so; but you will forget it, will you not?” He bent low beside her as he spoke; she took his hand, and looking at him through her tears, said hoarsely:--\n\n“No, I shall not forget, for it is well that I remember; and with it I have so much in memory of you that is sweet, that I take it all together. Now, you must all be going soon. Breakfast is ready, and we must all eat that we may be strong.”\n\nBreakfast was a strange meal to us all. We tried to be cheerful and encourage each other, and Mina was the brightest and most cheerful of us. When it was over, Van Helsing stood up and said:--\n\n“Now, my dear friends, we go forth to our terrible enterprise. Are we all armed, as we were on that night when first we visited our enemy’s lair; armed against ghostly as well as carnal attack?” We all assured him. “Then it is well. Now, Madam Mina, you are in any case quite safe here until the sunset; and before then we shall return--if---- We shall return! But before we go let me see you armed against personal attack. I have myself, since you came down, prepared your chamber by the placing of things of which we know, so that He may not enter. Now let me guard yourself. On your forehead I touch this piece of Sacred Wafer in the name of the Father, the Son, and----”\n\nThere was a fearful scream which almost froze our hearts to hear. As he had placed the Wafer on Mina’s forehead, it had seared it--had burned into the flesh as though it had been a piece of white-hot metal. My poor darling’s brain had told her the significance of the fact as quickly as her nerves received the pain of it; and the two so overwhelmed her that her overwrought nature had its voice in that dreadful scream. But the words to her thought came quickly; the echo of the scream had not ceased to ring on the air when there came the reaction, and she sank on her knees on the floor in an agony of abasement. Pulling her beautiful hair over her face, as the leper of old his mantle, she wailed out:--\n\n“Unclean! Unclean! Even the Almighty shuns my polluted flesh! I must bear this mark of shame upon my forehead until the Judgment Day.” They all paused. I had thrown myself beside her in an agony of helpless grief, and putting my arms around held her tight. For a few minutes our sorrowful hearts beat together, whilst the friends around us turned away their eyes that ran tears silently. Then Van Helsing turned and said gravely; so gravely that I could not help feeling that he was in some way inspired, and was stating things outside himself:--\n\n“It may be that you may have to bear that mark till God himself see fit, as He most surely shall, on the Judgment Day, to redress all wrongs of the earth and of His children that He has placed thereon. And oh, Madam Mina, my dear, my dear, may we who love you be there to see, when that red scar, the sign of God’s knowledge of what has been, shall pass away, and leave your forehead as pure as the heart we know. For so surely as we live, that scar shall pass away when God sees right to lift the burden that is hard upon us. Till then we bear our Cross, as His Son did in obedience to His Will. It may be that we are chosen instruments of His good pleasure, and that we ascend to His bidding as that other through stripes and shame; through tears and blood; through doubts and fears, and all that makes the difference between God and man.”\n\nThere was hope in his words, and comfort; and they made for resignation. Mina and I both felt so, and simultaneously we each took one of the old man’s hands and bent over and kissed it. Then without a word we all knelt down together, and, all holding hands, swore to be true to each other. We men pledged ourselves to raise the veil of sorrow from the head of her whom, each in his own way, we loved; and we prayed for help and guidance in the terrible task which lay before us.\n\nIt was then time to start. So I said farewell to Mina, a parting which neither of us shall forget to our dying day; and we set out.\n\nTo one thing I have made up my mind: if we find out that Mina must be a vampire in the end, then she shall not go into that unknown and terrible land alone. I suppose it is thus that in old times one vampire meant many; just as their hideous bodies could only rest in sacred earth, so the holiest love was the recruiting sergeant for their ghastly ranks.\n\nWe entered Carfax without trouble and found all things the same as on the first occasion. It was hard to believe that amongst so prosaic surroundings of neglect and dust and decay there was any ground for such fear as already we knew. Had not our minds been made up, and had there not been terrible memories to spur us on, we could hardly have proceeded with our task. We found no papers, or any sign of use in the house; and in the old chapel the great boxes looked just as we had seen them last. Dr. Van Helsing said to us solemnly as we stood before them:--\n\n“And now, my friends, we have a duty here to do. We must sterilise this earth, so sacred of holy memories, that he has brought from a far distant land for such fell use. He has chosen this earth because it has been holy. Thus we defeat him with his own weapon, for we make it more holy still. It was sanctified to such use of man, now we sanctify it to God.” As he spoke he took from his bag a screwdriver and a wrench, and very soon the top of one of the cases was thrown open. The earth smelled musty and close; but we did not somehow seem to mind, for our attention was concentrated on the Professor. Taking from his box a piece of the Sacred Wafer he laid it reverently on the earth, and then shutting down the lid began to screw it home, we aiding him as he worked.\n\nOne by one we treated in the same way each of the great boxes, and left them as we had found them to all appearance; but in each was a portion of the Host.\n\nWhen we closed the door behind us, the Professor said solemnly:--\n\n“So much is already done. If it may be that with all the others we can be so successful, then the sunset of this evening may shine on Madam Mina’s forehead all white as ivory and with no stain!”\n\nAs we passed across the lawn on our way to the station to catch our train we could see the front of the asylum. I looked eagerly, and in the window of my own room saw Mina. I waved my hand to her, and nodded to tell that our work there was successfully accomplished. She nodded in reply to show that she understood. The last I saw, she was waving her hand in farewell. It was with a heavy heart that we sought the station and just caught the train, which was steaming in as we reached the platform.\n\nI have written this in the train.\n\nPiccadilly, 12:30 o’clock.--Just before we reached Fenchurch Street Lord Godalming said to me:--\n\n“Quincey and I will find a locksmith. You had better not come with us in case there should be any difficulty; for under the circumstances it wouldn’t seem so bad for us to break into an empty house. But you are a solicitor and the Incorporated Law Society might tell you that you should have known better.” I demurred as to my not sharing any danger even of odium, but he went on: “Besides, it will attract less attention if there are not too many of us. My title will make it all right with the locksmith, and with any policeman that may come along. You had better go with Jack and the Professor and stay in the Green Park, somewhere in sight of the house; and when you see the door opened and the smith has gone away, do you all come across. We shall be on the lookout for you, and shall let you in.”\n\n“The advice is good!” said Van Helsing, so we said no more. Godalming and Morris hurried off in a cab, we following in another. At the corner of Arlington Street our contingent got out and strolled into the Green Park. My heart beat as I saw the house on which so much of our hope was centred, looming up grim and silent in its deserted condition amongst its more lively and spruce-looking neighbours. We sat down on a bench within good view, and began to smoke cigars so as to attract as little attention as possible. The minutes seemed to pass with leaden feet as we waited for the coming of the others.\n\nAt length we saw a four-wheeler drive up. Out of it, in leisurely fashion, got Lord Godalming and Morris; and down from the box descended a thick-set working man with his rush-woven basket of tools. Morris paid the cabman, who touched his hat and drove away. Together the two ascended the steps, and Lord Godalming pointed out what he wanted done. The workman took off his coat leisurely and hung it on one of the spikes of the rail, saying something to a policeman who just then sauntered along. The policeman nodded acquiescence, and the man kneeling down placed his bag beside him. After searching through it, he took out a selection of tools which he produced to lay beside him in orderly fashion. Then he stood up, looked into the keyhole, blew into it, and turning to his employers, made some remark. Lord Godalming smiled, and the man lifted a good-sized bunch of keys; selecting one of them, he began to probe the lock, as if feeling his way with it. After fumbling about for a bit he tried a second, and then a third. All at once the door opened under a slight push from him, and he and the two others entered the hall. We sat still; my own cigar burnt furiously, but Van Helsing’s went cold altogether. We waited patiently as we saw the workman come out and bring in his bag. Then he held the door partly open, steadying it with his knees, whilst he fitted a key to the lock. This he finally handed to Lord Godalming, who took out his purse and gave him something. The man touched his hat, took his bag, put on his coat and departed; not a soul took the slightest notice of the whole transaction.\n\nWhen the man had fairly gone, we three crossed the street and knocked at the door. It was immediately opened by Quincey Morris, beside whom stood Lord Godalming lighting a cigar.\n\n“The place smells so vilely,” said the latter as we came in. It did indeed smell vilely--like the old chapel at Carfax--and with our previous experience it was plain to us that the Count had been using the place pretty freely. We moved to explore the house, all keeping together in case of attack; for we knew we had a strong and wily enemy to deal with, and as yet we did not know whether the Count might not be in the house. In the dining-room, which lay at the back of the hall, we found eight boxes of earth. Eight boxes only out of the nine, which we sought! Our work was not over, and would never be until we should have found the missing box. First we opened the shutters of the window which looked out across a narrow stone-flagged yard at the blank face of a stable, pointed to look like the front of a miniature house. There were no windows in it, so we were not afraid of being over-looked. We did not lose any time in examining the chests. With the tools which we had brought with us we opened them, one by one, and treated them as we had treated those others in the old chapel. It was evident to us that the Count was not at present in the house, and we proceeded to search for any of his effects.\n\nAfter a cursory glance at the rest of the rooms, from basement to attic, we came to the conclusion that the dining-room contained any effects which might belong to the Count; and so we proceeded to minutely examine them. They lay in a sort of orderly disorder on the great dining-room table. There were title deeds of the Piccadilly house in a great bundle; deeds of the purchase of the houses at Mile End and Bermondsey; note-paper, envelopes, and pens and ink. All were covered up in thin wrapping paper to keep them from the dust. There were also a clothes brush, a brush and comb, and a jug and basin--the latter containing dirty water which was reddened as if with blood. Last of all was a little heap of keys of all sorts and sizes, probably those belonging to the other houses. When we had examined this last find, Lord Godalming and Quincey Morris taking accurate notes of the various addresses of the houses in the East and the South, took with them the keys in a great bunch, and set out to destroy the boxes in these places. The rest of us are, with what patience we can, waiting their return--or the coming of the Count.\n\n# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY\n\n3 October.--The time seemed terribly long whilst we were waiting for the coming of Godalming and Quincey Morris. The Professor tried to keep our minds active by using them all the time. I could see his beneficent purpose, by the side glances which he threw from time to time at Harker. The poor fellow is overwhelmed in a misery that is appalling to see. Last night he was a frank, happy-looking man, with strong, youthful face, full of energy, and with dark brown hair. To-day he is a drawn, haggard old man, whose white hair matches well with the hollow burning eyes and grief-written lines of his face. His energy is still intact; in fact, he is like a living flame. This may yet be his salvation, for, if all go well, it will tide him over the despairing period; he will then, in a kind of way, wake again to the realities of life. Poor fellow, I thought my own trouble was bad enough, but his----! The Professor knows this well enough, and is doing his best to keep his mind active. What he has been saying was, under the circumstances, of absorbing interest. So well as I can remember, here it is:--\n\n“I have studied, over and over again since they came into my hands, all the papers relating to this monster; and the more I have studied, the greater seems the necessity to utterly stamp him out. All through there are signs of his advance; not only of his power, but of his knowledge of it. As I learned from the researches of my friend Arminus of Buda-Pesth, he was in life a most wonderful man. Soldier, statesman, and alchemist--which latter was the highest development of the science-knowledge of his time. He had a mighty brain, a learning beyond compare, and a heart that knew no fear and no remorse. He dared even to attend the Scholomance, and there was no branch of knowledge of his time that he did not essay. Well, in him the brain powers survived the physical death; though it would seem that memory was not all complete. In some faculties of mind he has been, and is, only a child; but he is growing, and some things that were childish at the first are now of man’s stature. He is experimenting, and doing it well; and if it had not been that we have crossed his path he would be yet--he may be yet if we fail--the father or furtherer of a new order of beings, whose road must lead through Death, not Life.”\n\nHarker groaned and said, “And this is all arrayed against my darling! But how is he experimenting? The knowledge may help us to defeat him!”\n\n“He has all along, since his coming, been trying his power, slowly but surely; that big child-brain of his is working. Well for us, it is, as yet, a child-brain; for had he dared, at the first, to attempt certain things he would long ago have been beyond our power. However, he means to succeed, and a man who has centuries before him can afford to wait and to go slow. Festina lente may well be his motto.”\n\n“I fail to understand,” said Harker wearily. “Oh, do be more plain to me! Perhaps grief and trouble are dulling my brain.”\n\nThe Professor laid his hand tenderly on his shoulder as he spoke:--\n\n“Ah, my child, I will be plain. Do you not see how, of late, this monster has been creeping into knowledge experimentally. How he has been making use of the zoöphagous patient to effect his entry into friend John’s home; for your Vampire, though in all afterwards he can come when and how he will, must at the first make entry only when asked thereto by an inmate. But these are not his most important experiments. Do we not see how at the first all these so great boxes were moved by others. He knew not then but that must be so. But all the time that so great child-brain of his was growing, and he began to consider whether he might not himself move the box. So he began to help; and then, when he found that this be all-right, he try to move them all alone. And so he progress, and he scatter these graves of him; and none but he know where they are hidden. He may have intend to bury them deep in the ground. So that he only use them in the night, or at such time as he can change his form, they do him equal well; and none may know these are his hiding-place! But, my child, do not despair; this knowledge come to him just too late! Already all of his lairs but one be sterilise as for him; and before the sunset this shall be so. Then he have no place where he can move and hide. I delayed this morning that so we might be sure. Is there not more at stake for us than for him? Then why we not be even more careful than him? By my clock it is one hour and already, if all be well, friend Arthur and Quincey are on their way to us. To-day is our day, and we must go sure, if slow, and lose no chance. See! there are five of us when those absent ones return.”\n\nWhilst he was speaking we were startled by a knock at the hall door, the double postman’s knock of the telegraph boy. We all moved out to the hall with one impulse, and Van Helsing, holding up his hand to us to keep silence, stepped to the door and opened it. The boy handed in a despatch. The Professor closed the door again, and, after looking at the direction, opened it and read aloud.\n\n“Look out for D. He has just now, 12:45, come from Carfax hurriedly and hastened towards the South. He seems to be going the round and may want to see you: Mina.”\n\nThere was a pause, broken by Jonathan Harker’s voice:--\n\n“Now, God be thanked, we shall soon meet!” Van Helsing turned to him quickly and said:--\n\n“God will act in His own way and time. Do not fear, and do not rejoice as yet; for what we wish for at the moment may be our undoings.”\n\n“I care for nothing now,” he answered hotly, “except to wipe out this brute from the face of creation. I would sell my soul to do it!”\n\n“Oh, hush, hush, my child!” said Van Helsing. “God does not purchase souls in this wise; and the Devil, though he may purchase, does not keep faith. But God is merciful and just, and knows your pain and your devotion to that dear Madam Mina. Think you, how her pain would be doubled, did she but hear your wild words. Do not fear any of us, we are all devoted to this cause, and to-day shall see the end. The time is coming for action; to-day this Vampire is limit to the powers of man, and till sunset he may not change. It will take him time to arrive here--see, it is twenty minutes past one--and there are yet some times before he can hither come, be he never so quick. What we must hope for is that my Lord Arthur and Quincey arrive first.”\n\nAbout half an hour after we had received Mrs. Harker’s telegram, there came a quiet, resolute knock at the hall door. It was just an ordinary knock, such as is given hourly by thousands of gentlemen, but it made the Professor’s heart and mine beat loudly. We looked at each other, and together moved out into the hall; we each held ready to use our various armaments--the spiritual in the left hand, the mortal in the right. Van Helsing pulled back the latch, and, holding the door half open, stood back, having both hands ready for action. The gladness of our hearts must have shown upon our faces when on the step, close to the door, we saw Lord Godalming and Quincey Morris. They came quickly in and closed the door behind them, the former saying, as they moved along the hall:--\n\n“It is all right. We found both places; six boxes in each and we destroyed them all!”\n\n“Destroyed?” asked the Professor.\n\n“For him!” We were silent for a minute, and then Quincey said:--\n\n“There’s nothing to do but to wait here. If, however, he doesn’t turn up by five o’clock, we must start off; for it won’t do to leave Mrs. Harker alone after sunset.”\n\n“He will be here before long now,” said Van Helsing, who had been consulting his pocket-book. “Nota bene, in Madam’s telegram he went south from Carfax, that means he went to cross the river, and he could only do so at slack of tide, which should be something before one o’clock. That he went south has a meaning for us. He is as yet only suspicious; and he went from Carfax first to the place where he would suspect interference least. You must have been at Bermondsey only a short time before him. That he is not here already shows that he went to Mile End next. This took him some time; for he would then have to be carried over the river in some way. Believe me, my friends, we shall not have long to wait now. We should have ready some plan of attack, so that we may throw away no chance. Hush, there is no time now. Have all your arms! Be ready!” He held up a warning hand as he spoke, for we all could hear a key softly inserted in the lock of the hall door.\n\nI could not but admire, even at such a moment, the way in which a dominant spirit asserted itself. In all our hunting parties and adventures in different parts of the world, Quincey Morris had always been the one to arrange the plan of action, and Arthur and I had been accustomed to obey him implicitly. Now, the old habit seemed to be renewed instinctively. With a swift glance around the room, he at once laid out our plan of attack, and, without speaking a word, with a gesture, placed us each in position. Van Helsing, Harker, and I were just behind the door, so that when it was opened the Professor could guard it whilst we two stepped between the incomer and the door. Godalming behind and Quincey in front stood just out of sight ready to move in front of the window. We waited in a suspense that made the seconds pass with nightmare slowness. The slow, careful steps came along the hall; the Count was evidently prepared for some surprise--at least he feared it.\n\nSuddenly with a single bound he leaped into the room, winning a way past us before any of us could raise a hand to stay him. There was something so panther-like in the movement--something so unhuman, that it seemed to sober us all from the shock of his coming. The first to act was Harker, who, with a quick movement, threw himself before the door leading into the room in the front of the house. As the Count saw us, a horrible sort of snarl passed over his face, showing the eye-teeth long and pointed; but the evil smile as quickly passed into a cold stare of lion-like disdain. His expression again changed as, with a single impulse, we all advanced upon him. It was a pity that we had not some better organised plan of attack, for even at the moment I wondered what we were to do. I did not myself know whether our lethal weapons would avail us anything. Harker evidently meant to try the matter, for he had ready his great Kukri knife and made a fierce and sudden cut at him. The blow was a powerful one; only the diabolical quickness of the Count’s leap back saved him. A second less and the trenchant blade had shorne through his heart. As it was, the point just cut the cloth of his coat, making a wide gap whence a bundle of bank-notes and a stream of gold fell out. The expression of the Count’s face was so hellish, that for a moment I feared for Harker, though I saw him throw the terrible knife aloft again for another stroke. Instinctively I moved forward with a protective impulse, holding the Crucifix and Wafer in my left hand. I felt a mighty power fly along my arm; and it was without surprise that I saw the monster cower back before a similar movement made spontaneously by each one of us. It would be impossible to describe the expression of hate and baffled malignity--of anger and hellish rage--which came over the Count’s face. His waxen hue became greenish-yellow by the contrast of his burning eyes, and the red scar on the forehead showed on the pallid skin like a palpitating wound. The next instant, with a sinuous dive he swept under Harker’s arm, ere his blow could fall, and, grasping a handful of the money from the floor, dashed across the room, threw himself at the window. Amid the crash and glitter of the falling glass, he tumbled into the flagged area below. Through the sound of the shivering glass I could hear the “ting” of the gold, as some of the sovereigns fell on the flagging.\n\nWe ran over and saw him spring unhurt from the ground. He, rushing up the steps, crossed the flagged yard, and pushed open the stable door. There he turned and spoke to us:--\n\n“You think to baffle me, you--with your pale faces all in a row, like sheep in a butcher’s. You shall be sorry yet, each one of you! You think you have left me without a place to rest; but I have more. My revenge is just begun! I spread it over centuries, and time is on my side. Your girls that you all love are mine already; and through them you and others shall yet be mine--my creatures, to do my bidding and to be my jackals when I want to feed. Bah!” With a contemptuous sneer, he passed quickly through the door, and we heard the rusty bolt creak as he fastened it behind him. A door beyond opened and shut. The first of us to speak was the Professor, as, realising the difficulty of following him through the stable, we moved toward the hall.\n\n“We have learnt something--much! Notwithstanding his brave words, he fears us; he fear time, he fear want! For if not, why he hurry so? His very tone betray him, or my ears deceive. Why take that money? You follow quick. You are hunters of wild beast, and understand it so. For me, I make sure that nothing here may be of use to him, if so that he return.” As he spoke he put the money remaining into his pocket; took the title-deeds in the bundle as Harker had left them, and swept the remaining things into the open fireplace, where he set fire to them with a match.\n\nGodalming and Morris had rushed out into the yard, and Harker had lowered himself from the window to follow the Count. He had, however, bolted the stable door; and by the time they had forced it open there was no sign of him. Van Helsing and I tried to make inquiry at the back of the house; but the mews was deserted and no one had seen him depart.\n\nIt was now late in the afternoon, and sunset was not far off. We had to recognise that our game was up; with heavy hearts we agreed with the Professor when he said:--\n\n“Let us go back to Madam Mina--poor, poor dear Madam Mina. All we can do just now is done; and we can there, at least, protect her. But we need not despair. There is but one more earth-box, and we must try to find it; when that is done all may yet be well.” I could see that he spoke as bravely as he could to comfort Harker. The poor fellow was quite broken down; now and again he gave a low groan which he could not suppress--he was thinking of his wife.\n\nWith sad hearts we came back to my house, where we found Mrs. Harker waiting us, with an appearance of cheerfulness which did honour to her bravery and unselfishness. When she saw our faces, her own became as pale as death: for a second or two her eyes were closed as if she were in secret prayer; and then she said cheerfully:--\n\n“I can never thank you all enough. Oh, my poor darling!” As she spoke, she took her husband’s grey head in her hands and kissed it--“Lay your poor head here and rest it. All will yet be well, dear! God will protect us if He so will it in His good intent.” The poor fellow groaned. There was no place for words in his sublime misery.\n\nWe had a sort of perfunctory supper together, and I think it cheered us all up somewhat. It was, perhaps, the mere animal heat of food to hungry people--for none of us had eaten anything since breakfast--or the sense of companionship may have helped us; but anyhow we were all less miserable, and saw the morrow as not altogether without hope. True to our promise, we told Mrs. Harker everything which had passed; and although she grew snowy white at times when danger had seemed to threaten her husband, and red at others when his devotion to her was manifested, she listened bravely and with calmness. When we came to the part where Harker had rushed at the Count so recklessly, she clung to her husband’s arm, and held it tight as though her clinging could protect him from any harm that might come. She said nothing, however, till the narration was all done, and matters had been brought right up to the present time. Then without letting go her husband’s hand she stood up amongst us and spoke. Oh, that I could give any idea of the scene; of that sweet, sweet, good, good woman in all the radiant beauty of her youth and animation, with the red scar on her forehead, of which she was conscious, and which we saw with grinding of our teeth--remembering whence and how it came; her loving kindness against our grim hate; her tender faith against all our fears and doubting; and we, knowing that so far as symbols went, she with all her goodness and purity and faith, was outcast from God.\n\n“Jonathan,” she said, and the word sounded like music on her lips it was so full of love and tenderness, “Jonathan dear, and you all my true, true friends, I want you to bear something in mind through all this dreadful time. I know that you must fight--that you must destroy even as you destroyed the false Lucy so that the true Lucy might live hereafter; but it is not a work of hate. That poor soul who has wrought all this misery is the saddest case of all. Just think what will be his joy when he, too, is destroyed in his worser part that his better part may have spiritual immortality. You must be pitiful to him, too, though it may not hold your hands from his destruction.”\n\nAs she spoke I could see her husband’s face darken and draw together, as though the passion in him were shrivelling his being to its core. Instinctively the clasp on his wife’s hand grew closer, till his knuckles looked white. She did not flinch from the pain which I knew she must have suffered, but looked at him with eyes that were more appealing than ever. As she stopped speaking he leaped to his feet, almost tearing his hand from hers as he spoke:--\n\n“May God give him into my hand just for long enough to destroy that earthly life of him which we are aiming at. If beyond it I could send his soul for ever and ever to burning hell I would do it!”\n\n“Oh, hush! oh, hush! in the name of the good God. Don’t say such things, Jonathan, my husband; or you will crush me with fear and horror. Just think, my dear--I have been thinking all this long, long day of it--that ... perhaps ... some day ... I, too, may need such pity; and that some other like you--and with equal cause for anger--may deny it to me! Oh, my husband! my husband, indeed I would have spared you such a thought had there been another way; but I pray that God may not have treasured your wild words, except as the heart-broken wail of a very loving and sorely stricken man. Oh, God, let these poor white hairs go in evidence of what he has suffered, who all his life has done no wrong, and on whom so many sorrows have come.”\n\nWe men were all in tears now. There was no resisting them, and we wept openly. She wept, too, to see that her sweeter counsels had prevailed. Her husband flung himself on his knees beside her, and putting his arms round her, hid his face in the folds of her dress. Van Helsing beckoned to us and we stole out of the room, leaving the two loving hearts alone with their God.\n\nBefore they retired the Professor fixed up the room against any coming of the Vampire, and assured Mrs. Harker that she might rest in peace. She tried to school herself to the belief, and, manifestly for her husband’s sake, tried to seem content. It was a brave struggle; and was, I think and believe, not without its reward. Van Helsing had placed at hand a bell which either of them was to sound in case of any emergency. When they had retired, Quincey, Godalming, and I arranged that we should sit up, dividing the night between us, and watch over the safety of the poor stricken lady. The first watch falls to Quincey, so the rest of us shall be off to bed as soon as we can. Godalming has already turned in, for his is the second watch. Now that my work is done I, too, shall go to bed.\n\nJonathan Harker’s Journal.\n\n3-4 October, close to midnight.--I thought yesterday would never end. There was over me a yearning for sleep, in some sort of blind belief that to wake would be to find things changed, and that any change must now be for the better. Before we parted, we discussed what our next step was to be, but we could arrive at no result. All we knew was that one earth-box remained, and that the Count alone knew where it was. If he chooses to lie hidden, he may baffle us for years; and in the meantime!--the thought is too horrible, I dare not think of it even now. This I know: that if ever there was a woman who was all perfection, that one is my poor wronged darling. I love her a thousand times more for her sweet pity of last night, a pity that made my own hate of the monster seem despicable. Surely God will not permit the world to be the poorer by the loss of such a creature. This is hope to me. We are all drifting reefwards now, and faith is our only anchor. Thank God! Mina is sleeping, and sleeping without dreams. I fear what her dreams might be like, with such terrible memories to ground them in. She has not been so calm, within my seeing, since the sunset. Then, for a while, there came over her face a repose which was like spring after the blasts of March. I thought at the time that it was the softness of the red sunset on her face, but somehow now I think it has a deeper meaning. I am not sleepy myself, though I am weary--weary to death. However, I must try to sleep; for there is to-morrow to think of, and there is no rest for me until....\n\nLater.--I must have fallen asleep, for I was awaked by Mina, who was sitting up in bed, with a startled look on her face. I could see easily, for we did not leave the room in darkness; she had placed a warning hand over my mouth, and now she whispered in my ear:--\n\n“Hush! there is someone in the corridor!” I got up softly, and crossing the room, gently opened the door.\n\nJust outside, stretched on a mattress, lay Mr. Morris, wide awake. He raised a warning hand for silence as he whispered to me:--\n\n“Hush! go back to bed; it is all right. One of us will be here all night. We don’t mean to take any chances!”\n\nHis look and gesture forbade discussion, so I came back and told Mina. She sighed and positively a shadow of a smile stole over her poor, pale face as she put her arms round me and said softly:--\n\n“Oh, thank God for good brave men!” With a sigh she sank back again to sleep. I write this now as I am not sleepy, though I must try again.\n\n4 October, morning.--Once again during the night I was wakened by Mina. This time we had all had a good sleep, for the grey of the coming dawn was making the windows into sharp oblongs, and the gas flame was like a speck rather than a disc of light. She said to me hurriedly:--\n\n“Go, call the Professor. I want to see him at once.”\n\n“Why?” I asked.\n\n“I have an idea. I suppose it must have come in the night, and matured without my knowing it. He must hypnotise me before the dawn, and then I shall be able to speak. Go quick, dearest; the time is getting close.” I went to the door. Dr. Seward was resting on the mattress, and, seeing me, he sprang to his feet.\n\n“Is anything wrong?” he asked, in alarm.\n\n“No,” I replied; “but Mina wants to see Dr. Van Helsing at once.”\n\n“I will go,” he said, and hurried into the Professor’s room.\n\nIn two or three minutes later Van Helsing was in the room in his dressing-gown, and Mr. Morris and Lord Godalming were with Dr. Seward at the door asking questions. When the Professor saw Mina smile--a positive smile ousted the anxiety of his face; he rubbed his hands as he said:--\n\n“Oh, my dear Madam Mina, this is indeed a change. See! friend Jonathan, we have got our dear Madam Mina, as of old, back to us to-day!” Then turning to her, he said, cheerfully: “And what am I do for you? For at this hour you do not want me for nothings.”\n\n“I want you to hypnotise me!” she said. “Do it before the dawn, for I feel that then I can speak, and speak freely. Be quick, for the time is short!” Without a word he motioned her to sit up in bed.\n\nLooking fixedly at her, he commenced to make passes in front of her, from over the top of her head downward, with each hand in turn. Mina gazed at him fixedly for a few minutes, during which my own heart beat like a trip hammer, for I felt that some crisis was at hand. Gradually her eyes closed, and she sat, stock still; only by the gentle heaving of her bosom could one know that she was alive. The Professor made a few more passes and then stopped, and I could see that his forehead was covered with great beads of perspiration. Mina opened her eyes; but she did not seem the same woman. There was a far-away look in her eyes, and her voice had a sad dreaminess which was new to me. Raising his hand to impose silence, the Professor motioned to me to bring the others in. They came on tip-toe, closing the door behind them, and stood at the foot of the bed, looking on. Mina appeared not to see them. The stillness was broken by Van Helsing’s voice speaking in a low level tone which would not break the current of her thoughts:--\n\n“Where are you?” The answer came in a neutral way:--\n\n“I do not know. Sleep has no place it can call its own.” For several minutes there was silence. Mina sat rigid, and the Professor stood staring at her fixedly; the rest of us hardly dared to breathe. The room was growing lighter; without taking his eyes from Mina’s face, Dr. Van Helsing motioned me to pull up the blind. I did so, and the day seemed just upon us. A red streak shot up, and a rosy light seemed to diffuse itself through the room. On the instant the Professor spoke again:--\n\n“Where are you now?” The answer came dreamily, but with intention; it were as though she were interpreting something. I have heard her use the same tone when reading her shorthand notes.\n\n“I do not know. It is all strange to me!”\n\n“What do you see?”\n\n“I can see nothing; it is all dark.”\n\n“What do you hear?” I could detect the strain in the Professor’s patient voice.\n\n“The lapping of water. It is gurgling by, and little waves leap. I can hear them on the outside.”\n\n“Then you are on a ship?” We all looked at each other, trying to glean something each from the other. We were afraid to think. The answer came quick:--\n\n“Oh, yes!”\n\n“What else do you hear?”\n\n“The sound of men stamping overhead as they run about. There is the creaking of a chain, and the loud tinkle as the check of the capstan falls into the rachet.”\n\n“What are you doing?”\n\n“I am still--oh, so still. It is like death!” The voice faded away into a deep breath as of one sleeping, and the open eyes closed again.\n\nBy this time the sun had risen, and we were all in the full light of day. Dr. Van Helsing placed his hands on Mina’s shoulders, and laid her head down softly on her pillow. She lay like a sleeping child for a few moments, and then, with a long sigh, awoke and stared in wonder to see us all around her. “Have I been talking in my sleep?” was all she said. She seemed, however, to know the situation without telling, though she was eager to know what she had told. The Professor repeated the conversation, and she said:--\n\n“Then there is not a moment to lose: it may not be yet too late!” Mr. Morris and Lord Godalming started for the door but the Professor’s calm voice called them back:--\n\n“Stay, my friends. That ship, wherever it was, was weighing anchor whilst she spoke. There are many ships weighing anchor at the moment in your so great Port of London. Which of them is it that you seek? God be thanked that we have once again a clue, though whither it may lead us we know not. We have been blind somewhat; blind after the manner of men, since when we can look back we see what we might have seen looking forward if we had been able to see what we might have seen! Alas, but that sentence is a puddle; is it not? We can know now what was in the Count’s mind, when he seize that money, though Jonathan’s so fierce knife put him in the danger that even he dread. He meant escape. Hear me, ESCAPE! He saw that with but one earth-box left, and a pack of men following like dogs after a fox, this London was no place for him. He have take his last earth-box on board a ship, and he leave the land. He think to escape, but no! we follow him. Tally Ho! as friend Arthur would say when he put on his red frock! Our old fox is wily; oh! so wily, and we must follow with wile. I, too, am wily and I think his mind in a little while. In meantime we may rest and in peace, for there are waters between us which he do not want to pass, and which he could not if he would--unless the ship were to touch the land, and then only at full or slack tide. See, and the sun is just rose, and all day to sunset is to us. Let us take bath, and dress, and have breakfast which we all need, and which we can eat comfortably since he be not in the same land with us.” Mina looked at him appealingly as she asked:--\n\n“But why need we seek him further, when he is gone away from us?” He took her hand and patted it as he replied:--\n\n“Ask me nothings as yet. When we have breakfast, then I answer all questions.” He would say no more, and we separated to dress.\n\nAfter breakfast Mina repeated her question. He looked at her gravely for a minute and then said sorrowfully:--\n\n“Because my dear, dear Madam Mina, now more than ever must we find him even if we have to follow him to the jaws of Hell!” She grew paler as she asked faintly:--\n\n“Why?”\n\n“Because,” he answered solemnly, “he can live for centuries, and you are but mortal woman. Time is now to be dreaded--since once he put that mark upon your throat.”\n\nI was just in time to catch her as she fell forward in a faint.\n\nThis to Jonathan Harker.\n\nYou are to stay with your dear Madam Mina. We shall go to make our search--if I can call it so, for it is not search but knowing, and we seek confirmation only. But do you stay and take care of her to-day. This is your best and most holiest office. This day nothing can find him here. Let me tell you that so you will know what we four know already, for I have tell them. He, our enemy, have gone away; he have gone back to his Castle in Transylvania. I know it so well, as if a great hand of fire wrote it on the wall. He have prepare for this in some way, and that last earth-box was ready to ship somewheres. For this he took the money; for this he hurry at the last, lest we catch him before the sun go down. It was his last hope, save that he might hide in the tomb that he think poor Miss Lucy, being as he thought like him, keep open to him. But there was not of time. When that fail he make straight for his last resource--his last earth-work I might say did I wish double entente. He is clever, oh, so clever! he know that his game here was finish; and so he decide he go back home. He find ship going by the route he came, and he go in it. We go off now to find what ship, and whither bound; when we have discover that, we come back and tell you all. Then we will comfort you and poor dear Madam Mina with new hope. For it will be hope when you think it over: that all is not lost. This very creature that we pursue, he take hundreds of years to get so far as London; and yet in one day, when we know of the disposal of him we drive him out. He is finite, though he is powerful to do much harm and suffers not as we do. But we are strong, each in our purpose; and we are all more strong together. Take heart afresh, dear husband of Madam Mina. This battle is but begun, and in the end we shall win--so sure as that God sits on high to watch over His children. Therefore be of much comfort till we return.\n\nJonathan Harker’s Journal.\n\n4 October.--When I read to Mina, Van Helsing’s message in the phonograph, the poor girl brightened up considerably. Already the certainty that the Count is out of the country has given her comfort; and comfort is strength to her. For my own part, now that his horrible danger is not face to face with us, it seems almost impossible to believe in it. Even my own terrible experiences in Castle Dracula seem like a long-forgotten dream. Here in the crisp autumn air in the bright sunlight----\n\nAlas! how can I disbelieve! In the midst of my thought my eye fell on the red scar on my poor darling’s white forehead. Whilst that lasts, there can be no disbelief. And afterwards the very memory of it will keep faith crystal clear. Mina and I fear to be idle, so we have been over all the diaries again and again. Somehow, although the reality seems greater each time, the pain and the fear seem less. There is something of a guiding purpose manifest throughout, which is comforting. Mina says that perhaps we are the instruments of ultimate good. It may be! I shall try to think as she does. We have never spoken to each other yet of the future. It is better to wait till we see the Professor and the others after their investigations.\n\nThe day is running by more quickly than I ever thought a day could run for me again. It is now three o’clock.\n\nMina Harker’s Journal.\n\n5 October, 5 p. m.--Our meeting for report. Present: Professor Van Helsing, Lord Godalming, Dr. Seward, Mr. Quincey Morris, Jonathan Harker, Mina Harker.\n\nDr. Van Helsing described what steps were taken during the day to discover on what boat and whither bound Count Dracula made his escape:--\n\n“As I knew that he wanted to get back to Transylvania, I felt sure that he must go by the Danube mouth; or by somewhere in the Black Sea, since by that way he come. It was a dreary blank that was before us. Omne ignotum pro magnifico; and so with heavy hearts we start to find what ships leave for the Black Sea last night. He was in sailing ship, since Madam Mina tell of sails being set. These not so important as to go in your list of the shipping in the Times, and so we go, by suggestion of Lord Godalming, to your Lloyd’s, where are note of all ships that sail, however so small. There we find that only one Black-Sea-bound ship go out with the tide. She is the Czarina Catherine, and she sail from Doolittle’s Wharf for Varna, and thence on to other parts and up the Danube. ‘Soh!’ said I, ‘this is the ship whereon is the Count.’ So off we go to Doolittle’s Wharf, and there we find a man in an office of wood so small that the man look bigger than the office. From him we inquire of the goings of the Czarina Catherine. He swear much, and he red face and loud of voice, but he good fellow all the same; and when Quincey give him something from his pocket which crackle as he roll it up, and put it in a so small bag which he have hid deep in his clothing, he still better fellow and humble servant to us. He come with us, and ask many men who are rough and hot; these be better fellows too when they have been no more thirsty. They say much of blood and bloom, and of others which I comprehend not, though I guess what they mean; but nevertheless they tell us all things which we want to know.\n\n“They make known to us among them, how last afternoon at about five o’clock comes a man so hurry. A tall man, thin and pale, with high nose and teeth so white, and eyes that seem to be burning. That he be all in black, except that he have a hat of straw which suit not him or the time. That he scatter his money in making quick inquiry as to what ship sails for the Black Sea and for where. Some took him to the office and then to the ship, where he will not go aboard but halt at shore end of gang-plank, and ask that the captain come to him. The captain come, when told that he will be pay well; and though he swear much at the first he agree to term. Then the thin man go and some one tell him where horse and cart can be hired. He go there and soon he come again, himself driving cart on which a great box; this he himself lift down, though it take several to put it on truck for the ship. He give much talk to captain as to how and where his box is to be place; but the captain like it not and swear at him in many tongues, and tell him that if he like he can come and see where it shall be. But he say ‘no’; that he come not yet, for that he have much to do. Whereupon the captain tell him that he had better be quick--with blood--for that his ship will leave the place--of blood--before the turn of the tide--with blood. Then the thin man smile and say that of course he must go when he think fit; but he will be surprise if he go quite so soon. The captain swear again, polyglot, and the thin man make him bow, and thank him, and say that he will so far intrude on his kindness as to come aboard before the sailing. Final the captain, more red than ever, and in more tongues tell him that he doesn’t want no Frenchmen--with bloom upon them and also with blood--in his ship--with blood on her also. And so, after asking where there might be close at hand a ship where he might purchase ship forms, he departed.\n\n“No one knew where he went ‘or bloomin’ well cared,’ as they said, for they had something else to think of--well with blood again; for it soon became apparent to all that the Czarina Catherine would not sail as was expected. A thin mist began to creep up from the river, and it grew, and grew; till soon a dense fog enveloped the ship and all around her. The captain swore polyglot--very polyglot--polyglot with bloom and blood; but he could do nothing. The water rose and rose; and he began to fear that he would lose the tide altogether. He was in no friendly mood, when just at full tide, the thin man came up the gang-plank again and asked to see where his box had been stowed. Then the captain replied that he wished that he and his box--old and with much bloom and blood--were in hell. But the thin man did not be offend, and went down with the mate and saw where it was place, and came up and stood awhile on deck in fog. He must have come off by himself, for none notice him. Indeed they thought not of him; for soon the fog begin to melt away, and all was clear again. My friends of the thirst and the language that was of bloom and blood laughed, as they told how the captain’s swears exceeded even his usual polyglot, and was more than ever full of picturesque, when on questioning other mariners who were on movement up and down on the river that hour, he found that few of them had seen any of fog at all, except where it lay round the wharf. However, the ship went out on the ebb tide; and was doubtless by morning far down the river mouth. She was by then, when they told us, well out to sea.\n\n“And so, my dear Madam Mina, it is that we have to rest for a time, for our enemy is on the sea, with the fog at his command, on his way to the Danube mouth. To sail a ship takes time, go she never so quick; and when we start we go on land more quick, and we meet him there. Our best hope is to come on him when in the box between sunrise and sunset; for then he can make no struggle, and we may deal with him as we should. There are days for us, in which we can make ready our plan. We know all about where he go; for we have seen the owner of the ship, who have shown us invoices and all papers that can be. The box we seek is to be landed in Varna, and to be given to an agent, one Ristics who will there present his credentials; and so our merchant friend will have done his part. When he ask if there be any wrong, for that so, he can telegraph and have inquiry made at Varna, we say ‘no’; for what is to be done is not for police or of the customs. It must be done by us alone and in our own way.”\n\nWhen Dr. Van Helsing had done speaking, I asked him if he were certain that the Count had remained on board the ship. He replied: “We have the best proof of that: your own evidence, when in the hypnotic trance this morning.” I asked him again if it were really necessary that they should pursue the Count, for oh! I dread Jonathan leaving me, and I know that he would surely go if the others went. He answered in growing passion, at first quietly. As he went on, however, he grew more angry and more forceful, till in the end we could not but see wherein was at least some of that personal dominance which made him so long a master amongst men:--\n\n“Yes, it is necessary--necessary--necessary! For your sake in the first, and then for the sake of humanity. This monster has done much harm already, in the narrow scope where he find himself, and in the short time when as yet he was only as a body groping his so small measure in darkness and not knowing. All this have I told these others; you, my dear Madam Mina, will learn it in the phonograph of my friend John, or in that of your husband. I have told them how the measure of leaving his own barren land--barren of peoples--and coming to a new land where life of man teems till they are like the multitude of standing corn, was the work of centuries. Were another of the Un-Dead, like him, to try to do what he has done, perhaps not all the centuries of the world that have been, or that will be, could aid him. With this one, all the forces of nature that are occult and deep and strong must have worked together in some wondrous way. The very place, where he have been alive, Un-Dead for all these centuries, is full of strangeness of the geologic and chemical world. There are deep caverns and fissures that reach none know whither. There have been volcanoes, some of whose openings still send out waters of strange properties, and gases that kill or make to vivify. Doubtless, there is something magnetic or electric in some of these combinations of occult forces which work for physical life in strange way; and in himself were from the first some great qualities. In a hard and warlike time he was celebrate that he have more iron nerve, more subtle brain, more braver heart, than any man. In him some vital principle have in strange way found their utmost; and as his body keep strong and grow and thrive, so his brain grow too. All this without that diabolic aid which is surely to him; for it have to yield to the powers that come from, and are, symbolic of good. And now this is what he is to us. He have infect you--oh, forgive me, my dear, that I must say such; but it is for good of you that I speak. He infect you in such wise, that even if he do no more, you have only to live--to live in your own old, sweet way; and so in time, death, which is of man’s common lot and with God’s sanction, shall make you like to him. This must not be! We have sworn together that it must not. Thus are we ministers of God’s own wish: that the world, and men for whom His Son die, will not be given over to monsters, whose very existence would defame Him. He have allowed us to redeem one soul already, and we go out as the old knights of the Cross to redeem more. Like them we shall travel towards the sunrise; and like them, if we fall, we fall in good cause.” He paused and I said:--\n\n“But will not the Count take his rebuff wisely? Since he has been driven from England, will he not avoid it, as a tiger does the village from which he has been hunted?”\n\n“Aha!” he said, “your simile of the tiger good, for me, and I shall adopt him. Your man-eater, as they of India call the tiger who has once tasted blood of the human, care no more for the other prey, but prowl unceasing till he get him. This that we hunt from our village is a tiger, too, a man-eater, and he never cease to prowl. Nay, in himself he is not one to retire and stay afar. In his life, his living life, he go over the Turkey frontier and attack his enemy on his own ground; he be beaten back, but did he stay? No! He come again, and again, and again. Look at his persistence and endurance. With the child-brain that was to him he have long since conceive the idea of coming to a great city. What does he do? He find out the place of all the world most of promise for him. Then he deliberately set himself down to prepare for the task. He find in patience just how is his strength, and what are his powers. He study new tongues. He learn new social life; new environment of old ways, the politic, the law, the finance, the science, the habit of a new land and a new people who have come to be since he was. His glimpse that he have had, whet his appetite only and enkeen his desire. Nay, it help him to grow as to his brain; for it all prove to him how right he was at the first in his surmises. He have done this alone; all alone! from a ruin tomb in a forgotten land. What more may he not do when the greater world of thought is open to him. He that can smile at death, as we know him; who can flourish in the midst of diseases that kill off whole peoples. Oh, if such an one was to come from God, and not the Devil, what a force for good might he not be in this old world of ours. But we are pledged to set the world free. Our toil must be in silence, and our efforts all in secret; for in this enlightened age, when men believe not even what they see, the doubting of wise men would be his greatest strength. It would be at once his sheath and his armour, and his weapons to destroy us, his enemies, who are willing to peril even our own souls for the safety of one we love--for the good of mankind, and for the honour and glory of God.”\n\nAfter a general discussion it was determined that for to-night nothing be definitely settled; that we should all sleep on the facts, and try to think out the proper conclusions. To-morrow, at breakfast, we are to meet again, and, after making our conclusions known to one another, we shall decide on some definite cause of action.\n\nI feel a wonderful peace and rest to-night. It is as if some haunting presence were removed from me. Perhaps ...\n\nMy surmise was not finished, could not be; for I caught sight in the mirror of the red mark upon my forehead; and I knew that I was still unclean.\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n5 October.--We all rose early, and I think that sleep did much for each and all of us. When we met at early breakfast there was more general cheerfulness than any of us had ever expected to experience again.\n\nIt is really wonderful how much resilience there is in human nature. Let any obstructing cause, no matter what, be removed in any way--even by death--and we fly back to first principles of hope and enjoyment. More than once as we sat around the table, my eyes opened in wonder whether the whole of the past days had not been a dream. It was only when I caught sight of the red blotch on Mrs. Harker’s forehead that I was brought back to reality. Even now, when I am gravely revolving the matter, it is almost impossible to realise that the cause of all our trouble is still existent. Even Mrs. Harker seems to lose sight of her trouble for whole spells; it is only now and again, when something recalls it to her mind, that she thinks of her terrible scar. We are to meet here in my study in half an hour and decide on our course of action. I see only one immediate difficulty, I know it by instinct rather than reason: we shall all have to speak frankly; and yet I fear that in some mysterious way poor Mrs. Harker’s tongue is tied. I know that she forms conclusions of her own, and from all that has been I can guess how brilliant and how true they must be; but she will not, or cannot, give them utterance. I have mentioned this to Van Helsing, and he and I are to talk it over when we are alone. I suppose it is some of that horrid poison which has got into her veins beginning to work. The Count had his own purposes when he gave her what Van Helsing called “the Vampire’s baptism of blood.” Well, there may be a poison that distils itself out of good things; in an age when the existence of ptomaines is a mystery we should not wonder at anything! One thing I know: that if my instinct be true regarding poor Mrs. Harker’s silences, then there is a terrible difficulty--an unknown danger--in the work before us. The same power that compels her silence may compel her speech. I dare not think further; for so I should in my thoughts dishonour a noble woman!\n\nVan Helsing is coming to my study a little before the others. I shall try to open the subject with him.\n\nLater.--When the Professor came in, we talked over the state of things. I could see that he had something on his mind which he wanted to say, but felt some hesitancy about broaching the subject. After beating about the bush a little, he said suddenly:--\n\n“Friend John, there is something that you and I must talk of alone, just at the first at any rate. Later, we may have to take the others into our confidence”; then he stopped, so I waited; he went on:--\n\n“Madam Mina, our poor, dear Madam Mina is changing.” A cold shiver ran through me to find my worst fears thus endorsed. Van Helsing continued:--\n\n“With the sad experience of Miss Lucy, we must this time be warned before things go too far. Our task is now in reality more difficult than ever, and this new trouble makes every hour of the direst importance. I can see the characteristics of the vampire coming in her face. It is now but very, very slight; but it is to be seen if we have eyes to notice without to prejudge. Her teeth are some sharper, and at times her eyes are more hard. But these are not all, there is to her the silence now often; as so it was with Miss Lucy. She did not speak, even when she wrote that which she wished to be known later. Now my fear is this. If it be that she can, by our hypnotic trance, tell what the Count see and hear, is it not more true that he who have hypnotise her first, and who have drink of her very blood and make her drink of his, should, if he will, compel her mind to disclose to him that which she know?” I nodded acquiescence; he went on:--\n\n“Then, what we must do is to prevent this; we must keep her ignorant of our intent, and so she cannot tell what she know not. This is a painful task! Oh, so painful that it heart-break me to think of; but it must be. When to-day we meet, I must tell her that for reason which we will not to speak she must not more be of our council, but be simply guarded by us.” He wiped his forehead, which had broken out in profuse perspiration at the thought of the pain which he might have to inflict upon the poor soul already so tortured. I knew that it would be some sort of comfort to him if I told him that I also had come to the same conclusion; for at any rate it would take away the pain of doubt. I told him, and the effect was as I expected.\n\nIt is now close to the time of our general gathering. Van Helsing has gone away to prepare for the meeting, and his painful part of it. I really believe his purpose is to be able to pray alone.\n\nLater.--At the very outset of our meeting a great personal relief was experienced by both Van Helsing and myself. Mrs. Harker had sent a message by her husband to say that she would not join us at present, as she thought it better that we should be free to discuss our movements without her presence to embarrass us. The Professor and I looked at each other for an instant, and somehow we both seemed relieved. For my own part, I thought that if Mrs. Harker realised the danger herself, it was much pain as well as much danger averted. Under the circumstances we agreed, by a questioning look and answer, with finger on lip, to preserve silence in our suspicions, until we should have been able to confer alone again. We went at once into our Plan of Campaign. Van Helsing roughly put the facts before us first:--\n\n“The Czarina Catherine left the Thames yesterday morning. It will take her at the quickest speed she has ever made at least three weeks to reach Varna; but we can travel overland to the same place in three days. Now, if we allow for two days less for the ship’s voyage, owing to such weather influences as we know that the Count can bring to bear; and if we allow a whole day and night for any delays which may occur to us, then we have a margin of nearly two weeks. Thus, in order to be quite safe, we must leave here on 17th at latest. Then we shall at any rate be in Varna a day before the ship arrives, and able to make such preparations as may be necessary. Of course we shall all go armed--armed against evil things, spiritual as well as physical.” Here Quincey Morris added:--\n\n“I understand that the Count comes from a wolf country, and it may be that he shall get there before us. I propose that we add Winchesters to our armament. I have a kind of belief in a Winchester when there is any trouble of that sort around. Do you remember, Art, when we had the pack after us at Tobolsk? What wouldn’t we have given then for a repeater apiece!”\n\n“Good!” said Van Helsing, “Winchesters it shall be. Quincey’s head is level at all times, but most so when there is to hunt, metaphor be more dishonour to science than wolves be of danger to man. In the meantime we can do nothing here; and as I think that Varna is not familiar to any of us, why not go there more soon? It is as long to wait here as there. To-night and to-morrow we can get ready, and then, if all be well, we four can set out on our journey.”\n\n“We four?” said Harker interrogatively, looking from one to another of us.\n\n“Of course!” answered the Professor quickly, “you must remain to take care of your so sweet wife!” Harker was silent for awhile and then said in a hollow voice:--\n\n“Let us talk of that part of it in the morning. I want to consult with Mina.” I thought that now was the time for Van Helsing to warn him not to disclose our plans to her; but he took no notice. I looked at him significantly and coughed. For answer he put his finger on his lips and turned away.\n\nJonathan Harker’s Journal.\n\n5 October, afternoon.--For some time after our meeting this morning I could not think. The new phases of things leave my mind in a state of wonder which allows no room for active thought. Mina’s determination not to take any part in the discussion set me thinking; and as I could not argue the matter with her, I could only guess. I am as far as ever from a solution now. The way the others received it, too, puzzled me; the last time we talked of the subject we agreed that there was to be no more concealment of anything amongst us. Mina is sleeping now, calmly and sweetly like a little child. Her lips are curved and her face beams with happiness. Thank God, there are such moments still for her.\n\nLater.--How strange it all is. I sat watching Mina’s happy sleep, and came as near to being happy myself as I suppose I shall ever be. As the evening drew on, and the earth took its shadows from the sun sinking lower, the silence of the room grew more and more solemn to me. All at once Mina opened her eyes, and looking at me tenderly, said:--\n\n“Jonathan, I want you to promise me something on your word of honour. A promise made to me, but made holily in God’s hearing, and not to be broken though I should go down on my knees and implore you with bitter tears. Quick, you must make it to me at once.”\n\n“Mina,” I said, “a promise like that, I cannot make at once. I may have no right to make it.”\n\n“But, dear one,” she said, with such spiritual intensity that her eyes were like pole stars, “it is I who wish it; and it is not for myself. You can ask Dr. Van Helsing if I am not right; if he disagrees you may do as you will. Nay, more, if you all agree, later, you are absolved from the promise.”\n\n“I promise!” I said, and for a moment she looked supremely happy; though to me all happiness for her was denied by the red scar on her forehead. She said:--\n\n“Promise me that you will not tell me anything of the plans formed for the campaign against the Count. Not by word, or inference, or implication; not at any time whilst this remains to me!” and she solemnly pointed to the scar. I saw that she was in earnest, and said solemnly:--\n\n“I promise!” and as I said it I felt that from that instant a door had been shut between us.\n\nLater, midnight.--Mina has been bright and cheerful all the evening. So much so that all the rest seemed to take courage, as if infected somewhat with her gaiety; as a result even I myself felt as if the pall of gloom which weighs us down were somewhat lifted. We all retired early. Mina is now sleeping like a little child; it is a wonderful thing that her faculty of sleep remains to her in the midst of her terrible trouble. Thank God for it, for then at least she can forget her care. Perhaps her example may affect me as her gaiety did to-night. I shall try it. Oh! for a dreamless sleep.\n\n6 October, morning.--Another surprise. Mina woke me early, about the same time as yesterday, and asked me to bring Dr. Van Helsing. I thought that it was another occasion for hypnotism, and without question went for the Professor. He had evidently expected some such call, for I found him dressed in his room. His door was ajar, so that he could hear the opening of the door of our room. He came at once; as he passed into the room, he asked Mina if the others might come, too.\n\n“No,” she said quite simply, “it will not be necessary. You can tell them just as well. I must go with you on your journey.”\n\nDr. Van Helsing was as startled as I was. After a moment’s pause he asked:--\n\n“But why?”\n\n“You must take me with you. I am safer with you, and you shall be safer, too.”\n\n“But why, dear Madam Mina? You know that your safety is our solemnest duty. We go into danger, to which you are, or may be, more liable than any of us from--from circumstances--things that have been.” He paused, embarrassed.\n\nAs she replied, she raised her finger and pointed to her forehead:--\n\n“I know. That is why I must go. I can tell you now, whilst the sun is coming up; I may not be able again. I know that when the Count wills me I must go. I know that if he tells me to come in secret, I must come by wile; by any device to hoodwink--even Jonathan.” God saw the look that she turned on me as she spoke, and if there be indeed a Recording Angel that look is noted to her everlasting honour. I could only clasp her hand. I could not speak; my emotion was too great for even the relief of tears. She went on:--\n\n“You men are brave and strong. You are strong in your numbers, for you can defy that which would break down the human endurance of one who had to guard alone. Besides, I may be of service, since you can hypnotise me and so learn that which even I myself do not know.” Dr. Van Helsing said very gravely:--\n\n“Madam Mina, you are, as always, most wise. You shall with us come; and together we shall do that which we go forth to achieve.” When he had spoken, Mina’s long spell of silence made me look at her. She had fallen back on her pillow asleep; she did not even wake when I had pulled up the blind and let in the sunlight which flooded the room. Van Helsing motioned to me to come with him quietly. We went to his room, and within a minute Lord Godalming, Dr. Seward, and Mr. Morris were with us also. He told them what Mina had said, and went on:--\n\n“In the morning we shall leave for Varna. We have now to deal with a new factor: Madam Mina. Oh, but her soul is true. It is to her an agony to tell us so much as she has done; but it is most right, and we are warned in time. There must be no chance lost, and in Varna we must be ready to act the instant when that ship arrives.”\n\n“What shall we do exactly?” asked Mr. Morris laconically. The Professor paused before replying:--\n\n“We shall at the first board that ship; then, when we have identified the box, we shall place a branch of the wild rose on it. This we shall fasten, for when it is there none can emerge; so at least says the superstition. And to superstition must we trust at the first; it was man’s faith in the early, and it have its root in faith still. Then, when we get the opportunity that we seek, when none are near to see, we shall open the box, and--and all will be well.”\n\n“I shall not wait for any opportunity,” said Morris. “When I see the box I shall open it and destroy the monster, though there were a thousand men looking on, and if I am to be wiped out for it the next moment!” I grasped his hand instinctively and found it as firm as a piece of steel. I think he understood my look; I hope he did.\n\n“Good boy,” said Dr. Van Helsing. “Brave boy. Quincey is all man. God bless him for it. My child, believe me none of us shall lag behind or pause from any fear. I do but say what we may do--what we must do. But, indeed, indeed we cannot say what we shall do. There are so many things which may happen, and their ways and their ends are so various that until the moment we may not say. We shall all be armed, in all ways; and when the time for the end has come, our effort shall not be lack. Now let us to-day put all our affairs in order. Let all things which touch on others dear to us, and who on us depend, be complete; for none of us can tell what, or when, or how, the end may be. As for me, my own affairs are regulate; and as I have nothing else to do, I shall go make arrangements for the travel. I shall have all tickets and so forth for our journey.”\n\nThere was nothing further to be said, and we parted. I shall now settle up all my affairs of earth, and be ready for whatever may come....\n\nLater.--It is all done; my will is made, and all complete. Mina if she survive is my sole heir. If it should not be so, then the others who have been so good to us shall have remainder.\n\nIt is now drawing towards the sunset; Mina’s uneasiness calls my attention to it. I am sure that there is something on her mind which the time of exact sunset will reveal. These occasions are becoming harrowing times for us all, for each sunrise and sunset opens up some new danger--some new pain, which, however, may in God’s will be means to a good end. I write all these things in the diary since my darling must not hear them now; but if it may be that she can see them again, they shall be ready.\n\nShe is calling to me.\n\n# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY\n\n11 October, Evening.--Jonathan Harker has asked me to note this, as he says he is hardly equal to the task, and he wants an exact record kept.\n\nI think that none of us were surprised when we were asked to see Mrs. Harker a little before the time of sunset. We have of late come to understand that sunrise and sunset are to her times of peculiar freedom; when her old self can be manifest without any controlling force subduing or restraining her, or inciting her to action. This mood or condition begins some half hour or more before actual sunrise or sunset, and lasts till either the sun is high, or whilst the clouds are still aglow with the rays streaming above the horizon. At first there is a sort of negative condition, as if some tie were loosened, and then the absolute freedom quickly follows; when, however, the freedom ceases the change-back or relapse comes quickly, preceded only by a spell of warning silence.\n\nTo-night, when we met, she was somewhat constrained, and bore all the signs of an internal struggle. I put it down myself to her making a violent effort at the earliest instant she could do so. A very few minutes, however, gave her complete control of herself; then, motioning her husband to sit beside her on the sofa where she was half reclining, she made the rest of us bring chairs up close. Taking her husband’s hand in hers began:--\n\n“We are all here together in freedom, for perhaps the last time! I know, dear; I know that you will always be with me to the end.” This was to her husband whose hand had, as we could see, tightened upon hers. “In the morning we go out upon our task, and God alone knows what may be in store for any of us. You are going to be so good to me as to take me with you. I know that all that brave earnest men can do for a poor weak woman, whose soul perhaps is lost--no, no, not yet, but is at any rate at stake--you will do. But you must remember that I am not as you are. There is a poison in my blood, in my soul, which may destroy me; which must destroy me, unless some relief comes to us. Oh, my friends, you know as well as I do, that my soul is at stake; and though I know there is one way out for me, you must not and I must not take it!” She looked appealingly to us all in turn, beginning and ending with her husband.\n\n“What is that way?” asked Van Helsing in a hoarse voice. “What is that way, which we must not--may not--take?”\n\n“That I may die now, either by my own hand or that of another, before the greater evil is entirely wrought. I know, and you know, that were I once dead you could and would set free my immortal spirit, even as you did my poor Lucy’s. Were death, or the fear of death, the only thing that stood in the way I would not shrink to die here, now, amidst the friends who love me. But death is not all. I cannot believe that to die in such a case, when there is hope before us and a bitter task to be done, is God’s will. Therefore, I, on my part, give up here the certainty of eternal rest, and go out into the dark where may be the blackest things that the world or the nether world holds!” We were all silent, for we knew instinctively that this was only a prelude. The faces of the others were set and Harker’s grew ashen grey; perhaps he guessed better than any of us what was coming. She continued:--\n\n“This is what I can give into the hotch-pot.” I could not but note the quaint legal phrase which she used in such a place, and with all seriousness. “What will each of you give? Your lives I know,” she went on quickly, “that is easy for brave men. Your lives are God’s, and you can give them back to Him; but what will you give to me?” She looked again questioningly, but this time avoided her husband’s face. Quincey seemed to understand; he nodded, and her face lit up. “Then I shall tell you plainly what I want, for there must be no doubtful matter in this connection between us now. You must promise me, one and all--even you, my beloved husband--that, should the time come, you will kill me.”\n\n“What is that time?” The voice was Quincey’s, but it was low and strained.\n\n“When you shall be convinced that I am so changed that it is better that I die than I may live. When I am thus dead in the flesh, then you will, without a moment’s delay, drive a stake through me and cut off my head; or do whatever else may be wanting to give me rest!”\n\nQuincey was the first to rise after the pause. He knelt down before her and taking her hand in his said solemnly:--\n\n“I’m only a rough fellow, who hasn’t, perhaps, lived as a man should to win such a distinction, but I swear to you by all that I hold sacred and dear that, should the time ever come, I shall not flinch from the duty that you have set us. And I promise you, too, that I shall make all certain, for if I am only doubtful I shall take it that the time has come!”\n\n“My true friend!” was all she could say amid her fast-falling tears, as, bending over, she kissed his hand.\n\n“I swear the same, my dear Madam Mina!” said Van Helsing.\n\n“And I!” said Lord Godalming, each of them in turn kneeling to her to take the oath. I followed, myself. Then her husband turned to her wan-eyed and with a greenish pallor which subdued the snowy whiteness of his hair, and asked:--\n\n“And must I, too, make such a promise, oh, my wife?”\n\n“You too, my dearest,” she said, with infinite yearning of pity in her voice and eyes. “You must not shrink. You are nearest and dearest and all the world to me; our souls are knit into one, for all life and all time. Think, dear, that there have been times when brave men have killed their wives and their womenkind, to keep them from falling into the hands of the enemy. Their hands did not falter any the more because those that they loved implored them to slay them. It is men’s duty towards those whom they love, in such times of sore trial! And oh, my dear, if it is to be that I must meet death at any hand, let it be at the hand of him that loves me best. Dr. Van Helsing, I have not forgotten your mercy in poor Lucy’s case to him who loved”--she stopped with a flying blush, and changed her phrase--“to him who had best right to give her peace. If that time shall come again, I look to you to make it a happy memory of my husband’s life that it was his loving hand which set me free from the awful thrall upon me.”\n\n“Again I swear!” came the Professor’s resonant voice. Mrs. Harker smiled, positively smiled, as with a sigh of relief she leaned back and said:--\n\n“And now one word of warning, a warning which you must never forget: this time, if it ever come, may come quickly and unexpectedly, and in such case you must lose no time in using your opportunity. At such a time I myself might be--nay! if the time ever comes, shall be--leagued with your enemy against you.”\n\n“One more request;” she became very solemn as she said this, “it is not vital and necessary like the other, but I want you to do one thing for me, if you will.” We all acquiesced, but no one spoke; there was no need to speak:--\n\n“I want you to read the Burial Service.” She was interrupted by a deep groan from her husband; taking his hand in hers, she held it over her heart, and continued: “You must read it over me some day. Whatever may be the issue of all this fearful state of things, it will be a sweet thought to all or some of us. You, my dearest, will I hope read it, for then it will be in your voice in my memory for ever--come what may!”\n\n“But oh, my dear one,” he pleaded, “death is afar off from you.”\n\n“Nay,” she said, holding up a warning hand. “I am deeper in death at this moment than if the weight of an earthly grave lay heavy upon me!”\n\n“Oh, my wife, must I read it?” he said, before he began.\n\n“It would comfort me, my husband!” was all she said; and he began to read when she had got the book ready.\n\n“How can I--how could any one--tell of that strange scene, its solemnity, its gloom, its sadness, its horror; and, withal, its sweetness. Even a sceptic, who can see nothing but a travesty of bitter truth in anything holy or emotional, would have been melted to the heart had he seen that little group of loving and devoted friends kneeling round that stricken and sorrowing lady; or heard the tender passion of her husband’s voice, as in tones so broken with emotion that often he had to pause, he read the simple and beautiful service from the Burial of the Dead. I--I cannot go on--words--and--v-voice--f-fail m-me!”\n\nShe was right in her instinct. Strange as it all was, bizarre as it may hereafter seem even to us who felt its potent influence at the time, it comforted us much; and the silence, which showed Mrs. Harker’s coming relapse from her freedom of soul, did not seem so full of despair to any of us as we had dreaded.\n\nJonathan Harker’s Journal.\n\n15 October, Varna.--We left Charing Cross on the morning of the 12th, got to Paris the same night, and took the places secured for us in the Orient Express. We travelled night and day, arriving here at about five o’clock. Lord Godalming went to the Consulate to see if any telegram had arrived for him, whilst the rest of us came on to this hotel--“the Odessus.” The journey may have had incidents; I was, however, too eager to get on, to care for them. Until the Czarina Catherine comes into port there will be no interest for me in anything in the wide world. Thank God! Mina is well, and looks to be getting stronger; her colour is coming back. She sleeps a great deal; throughout the journey she slept nearly all the time. Before sunrise and sunset, however, she is very wakeful and alert; and it has become a habit for Van Helsing to hypnotise her at such times. At first, some effort was needed, and he had to make many passes; but now, she seems to yield at once, as if by habit, and scarcely any action is needed. He seems to have power at these particular moments to simply will, and her thoughts obey him. He always asks her what she can see and hear. She answers to the first:--\n\n“Nothing; all is dark.” And to the second:--\n\n“I can hear the waves lapping against the ship, and the water rushing by. Canvas and cordage strain and masts and yards creak. The wind is high--I can hear it in the shrouds, and the bow throws back the foam.” It is evident that the Czarina Catherine is still at sea, hastening on her way to Varna. Lord Godalming has just returned. He had four telegrams, one each day since we started, and all to the same effect: that the Czarina Catherine had not been reported to Lloyd’s from anywhere. He had arranged before leaving London that his agent should send him every day a telegram saying if the ship had been reported. He was to have a message even if she were not reported, so that he might be sure that there was a watch being kept at the other end of the wire.\n\nWe had dinner and went to bed early. To-morrow we are to see the Vice-Consul, and to arrange, if we can, about getting on board the ship as soon as she arrives. Van Helsing says that our chance will be to get on the boat between sunrise and sunset. The Count, even if he takes the form of a bat, cannot cross the running water of his own volition, and so cannot leave the ship. As he dare not change to man’s form without suspicion--which he evidently wishes to avoid--he must remain in the box. If, then, we can come on board after sunrise, he is at our mercy; for we can open the box and make sure of him, as we did of poor Lucy, before he wakes. What mercy he shall get from us will not count for much. We think that we shall not have much trouble with officials or the seamen. Thank God! this is the country where bribery can do anything, and we are well supplied with money. We have only to make sure that the ship cannot come into port between sunset and sunrise without our being warned, and we shall be safe. Judge Moneybag will settle this case, I think!\n\n16 October.--Mina’s report still the same: lapping waves and rushing water, darkness and favouring winds. We are evidently in good time, and when we hear of the Czarina Catherine we shall be ready. As she must pass the Dardanelles we are sure to have some report.\n\n17 October.--Everything is pretty well fixed now, I think, to welcome the Count on his return from his tour. Godalming told the shippers that he fancied that the box sent aboard might contain something stolen from a friend of his, and got a half consent that he might open it at his own risk. The owner gave him a paper telling the Captain to give him every facility in doing whatever he chose on board the ship, and also a similar authorisation to his agent at Varna. We have seen the agent, who was much impressed with Godalming’s kindly manner to him, and we are all satisfied that whatever he can do to aid our wishes will be done. We have already arranged what to do in case we get the box open. If the Count is there, Van Helsing and Seward will cut off his head at once and drive a stake through his heart. Morris and Godalming and I shall prevent interference, even if we have to use the arms which we shall have ready. The Professor says that if we can so treat the Count’s body, it will soon after fall into dust. In such case there would be no evidence against us, in case any suspicion of murder were aroused. But even if it were not, we should stand or fall by our act, and perhaps some day this very script may be evidence to come between some of us and a rope. For myself, I should take the chance only too thankfully if it were to come. We mean to leave no stone unturned to carry out our intent. We have arranged with certain officials that the instant the Czarina Catherine is seen, we are to be informed by a special messenger.\n\n24 October.--A whole week of waiting. Daily telegrams to Godalming, but only the same story: “Not yet reported.” Mina’s morning and evening hypnotic answer is unvaried: lapping waves, rushing water, and creaking masts.\n\nTelegram, October 24th.\n\nRufus Smith, Lloyd’s, London, to Lord Godalming, care of H. B. M. Vice-Consul, Varna.\n\n“Czarina Catherine reported this morning from Dardanelles.”\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n25 October.--How I miss my phonograph! To write diary with a pen is irksome to me; but Van Helsing says I must. We were all wild with excitement yesterday when Godalming got his telegram from Lloyd’s. I know now what men feel in battle when the call to action is heard. Mrs. Harker, alone of our party, did not show any signs of emotion. After all, it is not strange that she did not; for we took special care not to let her know anything about it, and we all tried not to show any excitement when we were in her presence. In old days she would, I am sure, have noticed, no matter how we might have tried to conceal it; but in this way she is greatly changed during the past three weeks. The lethargy grows upon her, and though she seems strong and well, and is getting back some of her colour, Van Helsing and I are not satisfied. We talk of her often; we have not, however, said a word to the others. It would break poor Harker’s heart--certainly his nerve--if he knew that we had even a suspicion on the subject. Van Helsing examines, he tells me, her teeth very carefully, whilst she is in the hypnotic condition, for he says that so long as they do not begin to sharpen there is no active danger of a change in her. If this change should come, it would be necessary to take steps!... We both know what those steps would have to be, though we do not mention our thoughts to each other. We should neither of us shrink from the task--awful though it be to contemplate. “Euthanasia” is an excellent and a comforting word! I am grateful to whoever invented it.\n\nIt is only about 24 hours’ sail from the Dardanelles to here, at the rate the Czarina Catherine has come from London. She should therefore arrive some time in the morning; but as she cannot possibly get in before then, we are all about to retire early. We shall get up at one o’clock, so as to be ready.\n\n25 October, Noon.--No news yet of the ship’s arrival. Mrs. Harker’s hypnotic report this morning was the same as usual, so it is possible that we may get news at any moment. We men are all in a fever of excitement, except Harker, who is calm; his hands are cold as ice, and an hour ago I found him whetting the edge of the great Ghoorka knife which he now always carries with him. It will be a bad lookout for the Count if the edge of that “Kukri” ever touches his throat, driven by that stern, ice-cold hand!\n\nVan Helsing and I were a little alarmed about Mrs. Harker to-day. About noon she got into a sort of lethargy which we did not like; although we kept silence to the others, we were neither of us happy about it. She had been restless all the morning, so that we were at first glad to know that she was sleeping. When, however, her husband mentioned casually that she was sleeping so soundly that he could not wake her, we went to her room to see for ourselves. She was breathing naturally and looked so well and peaceful that we agreed that the sleep was better for her than anything else. Poor girl, she has so much to forget that it is no wonder that sleep, if it brings oblivion to her, does her good.\n\nLater.--Our opinion was justified, for when after a refreshing sleep of some hours she woke up, she seemed brighter and better than she had been for days. At sunset she made the usual hypnotic report. Wherever he may be in the Black Sea, the Count is hurrying to his destination. To his doom, I trust!\n\n26 October.--Another day and no tidings of the Czarina Catherine. She ought to be here by now. That she is still journeying somewhere is apparent, for Mrs. Harker’s hypnotic report at sunrise was still the same. It is possible that the vessel may be lying by, at times, for fog; some of the steamers which came in last evening reported patches of fog both to north and south of the port. We must continue our watching, as the ship may now be signalled any moment.\n\n27 October, Noon.--Most strange; no news yet of the ship we wait for. Mrs. Harker reported last night and this morning as usual: “lapping waves and rushing water,” though she added that “the waves were very faint.” The telegrams from London have been the same: “no further report.” Van Helsing is terribly anxious, and told me just now that he fears the Count is escaping us. He added significantly:--\n\n“I did not like that lethargy of Madam Mina’s. Souls and memories can do strange things during trance.” I was about to ask him more, but Harker just then came in, and he held up a warning hand. We must try to-night at sunset to make her speak more fully when in her hypnotic state.\n\n28 October.--Telegram. Rufus Smith, London, to Lord Godalming, care H. B. M. Vice Consul, Varna.\n\n“Czarina Catherine reported entering Galatz at one o’clock to-day.”\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n28 October.--When the telegram came announcing the arrival in Galatz I do not think it was such a shock to any of us as might have been expected. True, we did not know whence, or how, or when, the bolt would come; but I think we all expected that something strange would happen. The delay of arrival at Varna made us individually satisfied that things would not be just as we had expected; we only waited to learn where the change would occur. None the less, however, was it a surprise. I suppose that nature works on such a hopeful basis that we believe against ourselves that things will be as they ought to be, not as we should know that they will be. Transcendentalism is a beacon to the angels, even if it be a will-o’-the-wisp to man. It was an odd experience and we all took it differently. Van Helsing raised his hand over his head for a moment, as though in remonstrance with the Almighty; but he said not a word, and in a few seconds stood up with his face sternly set. Lord Godalming grew very pale, and sat breathing heavily. I was myself half stunned and looked in wonder at one after another. Quincey Morris tightened his belt with that quick movement which I knew so well; in our old wandering days it meant “action.” Mrs. Harker grew ghastly white, so that the scar on her forehead seemed to burn, but she folded her hands meekly and looked up in prayer. Harker smiled--actually smiled--the dark, bitter smile of one who is without hope; but at the same time his action belied his words, for his hands instinctively sought the hilt of the great Kukri knife and rested there. “When does the next train start for Galatz?” said Van Helsing to us generally.\n\n“At 6:30 to-morrow morning!” We all started, for the answer came from Mrs. Harker.\n\n“How on earth do you know?” said Art.\n\n“You forget--or perhaps you do not know, though Jonathan does and so does Dr. Van Helsing--that I am the train fiend. At home in Exeter I always used to make up the time-tables, so as to be helpful to my husband. I found it so useful sometimes, that I always make a study of the time-tables now. I knew that if anything were to take us to Castle Dracula we should go by Galatz, or at any rate through Bucharest, so I learned the times very carefully. Unhappily there are not many to learn, as the only train to-morrow leaves as I say.”\n\n“Wonderful woman!” murmured the Professor.\n\n“Can’t we get a special?” asked Lord Godalming. Van Helsing shook his head: “I fear not. This land is very different from yours or mine; even if we did have a special, it would probably not arrive as soon as our regular train. Moreover, we have something to prepare. We must think. Now let us organize. You, friend Arthur, go to the train and get the tickets and arrange that all be ready for us to go in the morning. Do you, friend Jonathan, go to the agent of the ship and get from him letters to the agent in Galatz, with authority to make search the ship just as it was here. Morris Quincey, you see the Vice-Consul, and get his aid with his fellow in Galatz and all he can do to make our way smooth, so that no times be lost when over the Danube. John will stay with Madam Mina and me, and we shall consult. For so if time be long you may be delayed; and it will not matter when the sun set, since I am here with Madam to make report.”\n\n“And I,” said Mrs. Harker brightly, and more like her old self than she had been for many a long day, “shall try to be of use in all ways, and shall think and write for you as I used to do. Something is shifting from me in some strange way, and I feel freer than I have been of late!” The three younger men looked happier at the moment as they seemed to realise the significance of her words; but Van Helsing and I, turning to each other, met each a grave and troubled glance. We said nothing at the time, however.\n\nWhen the three men had gone out to their tasks Van Helsing asked Mrs. Harker to look up the copy of the diaries and find him the part of Harker’s journal at the Castle. She went away to get it; when the door was shut upon her he said to me:--\n\n“We mean the same! speak out!”\n\n“There is some change. It is a hope that makes me sick, for it may deceive us.”\n\n“Quite so. Do you know why I asked her to get the manuscript?”\n\n“No!” said I, “unless it was to get an opportunity of seeing me alone.”\n\n“You are in part right, friend John, but only in part. I want to tell you something. And oh, my friend, I am taking a great--a terrible--risk; but I believe it is right. In the moment when Madam Mina said those words that arrest both our understanding, an inspiration came to me. In the trance of three days ago the Count sent her his spirit to read her mind; or more like he took her to see him in his earth-box in the ship with water rushing, just as it go free at rise and set of sun. He learn then that we are here; for she have more to tell in her open life with eyes to see and ears to hear than he, shut, as he is, in his coffin-box. Now he make his most effort to escape us. At present he want her not.\n\n“He is sure with his so great knowledge that she will come at his call; but he cut her off--take her, as he can do, out of his own power, that so she come not to him. Ah! there I have hope that our man-brains that have been of man so long and that have not lost the grace of God, will come higher than his child-brain that lie in his tomb for centuries, that grow not yet to our stature, and that do only work selfish and therefore small. Here comes Madam Mina; not a word to her of her trance! She know it not; and it would overwhelm her and make despair just when we want all her hope, all her courage; when most we want all her great brain which is trained like man’s brain, but is of sweet woman and have a special power which the Count give her, and which he may not take away altogether--though he think not so. Hush! let me speak, and you shall learn. Oh, John, my friend, we are in awful straits. I fear, as I never feared before. We can only trust the good God. Silence! here she comes!”\n\nI thought that the Professor was going to break down and have hysterics, just as he had when Lucy died, but with a great effort he controlled himself and was at perfect nervous poise when Mrs. Harker tripped into the room, bright and happy-looking and, in the doing of work, seemingly forgetful of her misery. As she came in, she handed a number of sheets of typewriting to Van Helsing. He looked over them gravely, his face brightening up as he read. Then holding the pages between his finger and thumb he said:--\n\n“Friend John, to you with so much of experience already--and you, too, dear Madam Mina, that are young--here is a lesson: do not fear ever to think. A half-thought has been buzzing often in my brain, but I fear to let him loose his wings. Here now, with more knowledge, I go back to where that half-thought come from and I find that he be no half-thought at all; that be a whole thought, though so young that he is not yet strong to use his little wings. Nay, like the “Ugly Duck” of my friend Hans Andersen, he be no duck-thought at all, but a big swan-thought that sail nobly on big wings, when the time come for him to try them. See I read here what Jonathan have written:--\n\n“That other of his race who, in a later age, again and again, brought his forces over The Great River into Turkey Land; who, when he was beaten back, came again, and again, and again, though he had to come alone from the bloody field where his troops were being slaughtered, since he knew that he alone could ultimately triumph.”\n\n“What does this tell us? Not much? no! The Count’s child-thought see nothing; therefore he speak so free. Your man-thought see nothing; my man-thought see nothing, till just now. No! But there comes another word from some one who speak without thought because she, too, know not what it mean--what it might mean. Just as there are elements which rest, yet when in nature’s course they move on their way and they touch--then pouf! and there comes a flash of light, heaven wide, that blind and kill and destroy some; but that show up all earth below for leagues and leagues. Is it not so? Well, I shall explain. To begin, have you ever study the philosophy of crime? ‘Yes’ and ‘No.’ You, John, yes; for it is a study of insanity. You, no, Madam Mina; for crime touch you not--not but once. Still, your mind works true, and argues not a particulari ad universale. There is this peculiarity in criminals. It is so constant, in all countries and at all times, that even police, who know not much from philosophy, come to know it empirically, that it is. That is to be empiric. The criminal always work at one crime--that is the true criminal who seems predestinate to crime, and who will of none other. This criminal has not full man-brain. He is clever and cunning and resourceful; but he be not of man-stature as to brain. He be of child-brain in much. Now this criminal of ours is predestinate to crime also; he, too, have child-brain, and it is of the child to do what he have done. The little bird, the little fish, the little animal learn not by principle, but empirically; and when he learn to do, then there is to him the ground to start from to do more. ‘Dos pou sto,’ said Archimedes. ‘Give me a fulcrum, and I shall move the world!’ To do once, is the fulcrum whereby child-brain become man-brain; and until he have the purpose to do more, he continue to do the same again every time, just as he have done before! Oh, my dear, I see that your eyes are opened, and that to you the lightning flash show all the leagues,” for Mrs. Harker began to clap her hands and her eyes sparkled. He went on:--\n\n“Now you shall speak. Tell us two dry men of science what you see with those so bright eyes.” He took her hand and held it whilst she spoke. His finger and thumb closed on her pulse, as I thought instinctively and unconsciously, as she spoke:--\n\n“The Count is a criminal and of criminal type. Nordau and Lombroso would so classify him, and quâ criminal he is of imperfectly formed mind. Thus, in a difficulty he has to seek resource in habit. His past is a clue, and the one page of it that we know--and that from his own lips--tells that once before, when in what Mr. Morris would call a ‘tight place,’ he went back to his own country from the land he had tried to invade, and thence, without losing purpose, prepared himself for a new effort. He came again better equipped for his work; and won. So he came to London to invade a new land. He was beaten, and when all hope of success was lost, and his existence in danger, he fled back over the sea to his home; just as formerly he had fled back over the Danube from Turkey Land.”\n\n“Good, good! oh, you so clever lady!” said Van Helsing, enthusiastically, as he stooped and kissed her hand. A moment later he said to me, as calmly as though we had been having a sick-room consultation:--\n\n“Seventy-two only; and in all this excitement. I have hope.” Turning to her again, he said with keen expectation:--\n\n“But go on. Go on! there is more to tell if you will. Be not afraid; John and I know. I do in any case, and shall tell you if you are right. Speak, without fear!”\n\n“I will try to; but you will forgive me if I seem egotistical.”\n\n“Nay! fear not, you must be egotist, for it is of you that we think.”\n\n“Then, as he is criminal he is selfish; and as his intellect is small and his action is based on selfishness, he confines himself to one purpose. That purpose is remorseless. As he fled back over the Danube, leaving his forces to be cut to pieces, so now he is intent on being safe, careless of all. So his own selfishness frees my soul somewhat from the terrible power which he acquired over me on that dreadful night. I felt it! Oh, I felt it! Thank God, for His great mercy! My soul is freer than it has been since that awful hour; and all that haunts me is a fear lest in some trance or dream he may have used my knowledge for his ends.” The Professor stood up:--\n\n“He has so used your mind; and by it he has left us here in Varna, whilst the ship that carried him rushed through enveloping fog up to Galatz, where, doubtless, he had made preparation for escaping from us. But his child-mind only saw so far; and it may be that, as ever is in God’s Providence, the very thing that the evil-doer most reckoned on for his selfish good, turns out to be his chiefest harm. The hunter is taken in his own snare, as the great Psalmist says. For now that he think he is free from every trace of us all, and that he has escaped us with so many hours to him, then his selfish child-brain will whisper him to sleep. He think, too, that as he cut himself off from knowing your mind, there can be no knowledge of him to you; there is where he fail! That terrible baptism of blood which he give you makes you free to go to him in spirit, as you have as yet done in your times of freedom, when the sun rise and set. At such times you go by my volition and not by his; and this power to good of you and others, as you have won from your suffering at his hands. This is now all the more precious that he know it not, and to guard himself have even cut himself off from his knowledge of our where. We, however, are not selfish, and we believe that God is with us through all this blackness, and these many dark hours. We shall follow him; and we shall not flinch; even if we peril ourselves that we become like him. Friend John, this has been a great hour; and it have done much to advance us on our way. You must be scribe and write him all down, so that when the others return from their work you can give it to them; then they shall know as we do.”\n\nAnd so I have written it whilst we wait their return, and Mrs. Harker has written with her typewriter all since she brought the MS. to us.\n\n# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY\n\n29 October.--This is written in the train from Varna to Galatz. Last night we all assembled a little before the time of sunset. Each of us had done his work as well as he could; so far as thought, and endeavour, and opportunity go, we are prepared for the whole of our journey, and for our work when we get to Galatz. When the usual time came round Mrs. Harker prepared herself for her hypnotic effort; and after a longer and more serious effort on the part of Van Helsing than has been usually necessary, she sank into the trance. Usually she speaks on a hint; but this time the Professor had to ask her questions, and to ask them pretty resolutely, before we could learn anything; at last her answer came:--\n\n“I can see nothing; we are still; there are no waves lapping, but only a steady swirl of water softly running against the hawser. I can hear men’s voices calling, near and far, and the roll and creak of oars in the rowlocks. A gun is fired somewhere; the echo of it seems far away. There is tramping of feet overhead, and ropes and chains are dragged along. What is this? There is a gleam of light; I can feel the air blowing upon me.”\n\nHere she stopped. She had risen, as if impulsively, from where she lay on the sofa, and raised both her hands, palms upwards, as if lifting a weight. Van Helsing and I looked at each other with understanding. Quincey raised his eyebrows slightly and looked at her intently, whilst Harker’s hand instinctively closed round the hilt of his Kukri. There was a long pause. We all knew that the time when she could speak was passing; but we felt that it was useless to say anything. Suddenly she sat up, and, as she opened her eyes, said sweetly:--\n\n“Would none of you like a cup of tea? You must all be so tired!” We could only make her happy, and so acquiesced. She bustled off to get tea; when she had gone Van Helsing said:--\n\n“You see, my friends. He is close to land: he has left his earth-chest. But he has yet to get on shore. In the night he may lie hidden somewhere; but if he be not carried on shore, or if the ship do not touch it, he cannot achieve the land. In such case he can, if it be in the night, change his form and can jump or fly on shore, as he did at Whitby. But if the day come before he get on shore, then, unless he be carried he cannot escape. And if he be carried, then the customs men may discover what the box contain. Thus, in fine, if he escape not on shore to-night, or before dawn, there will be the whole day lost to him. We may then arrive in time; for if he escape not at night we shall come on him in daytime, boxed up and at our mercy; for he dare not be his true self, awake and visible, lest he be discovered.”\n\nThere was no more to be said, so we waited in patience until the dawn; at which time we might learn more from Mrs. Harker.\n\nEarly this morning we listened, with breathless anxiety, for her response in her trance. The hypnotic stage was even longer in coming than before; and when it came the time remaining until full sunrise was so short that we began to despair. Van Helsing seemed to throw his whole soul into the effort; at last, in obedience to his will she made reply:--\n\n“All is dark. I hear lapping water, level with me, and some creaking as of wood on wood.” She paused, and the red sun shot up. We must wait till to-night.\n\nAnd so it is that we are travelling towards Galatz in an agony of expectation. We are due to arrive between two and three in the morning; but already, at Bucharest, we are three hours late, so we cannot possibly get in till well after sun-up. Thus we shall have two more hypnotic messages from Mrs. Harker; either or both may possibly throw more light on what is happening.\n\nLater.--Sunset has come and gone. Fortunately it came at a time when there was no distraction; for had it occurred whilst we were at a station, we might not have secured the necessary calm and isolation. Mrs. Harker yielded to the hypnotic influence even less readily than this morning. I am in fear that her power of reading the Count’s sensations may die away, just when we want it most. It seems to me that her imagination is beginning to work. Whilst she has been in the trance hitherto she has confined herself to the simplest of facts. If this goes on it may ultimately mislead us. If I thought that the Count’s power over her would die away equally with her power of knowledge it would be a happy thought; but I am afraid that it may not be so. When she did speak, her words were enigmatical:--\n\n“Something is going out; I can feel it pass me like a cold wind. I can hear, far off, confused sounds--as of men talking in strange tongues, fierce-falling water, and the howling of wolves.” She stopped and a shudder ran through her, increasing in intensity for a few seconds, till, at the end, she shook as though in a palsy. She said no more, even in answer to the Professor’s imperative questioning. When she woke from the trance, she was cold, and exhausted, and languid; but her mind was all alert. She could not remember anything, but asked what she had said; when she was told, she pondered over it deeply for a long time and in silence.\n\n30 October, 7 a. m.--We are near Galatz now, and I may not have time to write later. Sunrise this morning was anxiously looked for by us all. Knowing of the increasing difficulty of procuring the hypnotic trance, Van Helsing began his passes earlier than usual. They produced no effect, however, until the regular time, when she yielded with a still greater difficulty, only a minute before the sun rose. The Professor lost no time in his questioning; her answer came with equal quickness:--\n\n“All is dark. I hear water swirling by, level with my ears, and the creaking of wood on wood. Cattle low far off. There is another sound, a queer one like----” She stopped and grew white, and whiter still.\n\n“Go on; go on! Speak, I command you!” said Van Helsing in an agonised voice. At the same time there was despair in his eyes, for the risen sun was reddening even Mrs. Harker’s pale face. She opened her eyes, and we all started as she said, sweetly and seemingly with the utmost unconcern:--\n\n“Oh, Professor, why ask me to do what you know I can’t? I don’t remember anything.” Then, seeing the look of amazement on our faces, she said, turning from one to the other with a troubled look:--\n\n“What have I said? What have I done? I know nothing, only that I was lying here, half asleep, and heard you say ‘go on! speak, I command you!’ It seemed so funny to hear you order me about, as if I were a bad child!”\n\n“Oh, Madam Mina,” he said, sadly, “it is proof, if proof be needed, of how I love and honour you, when a word for your good, spoken more earnest than ever, can seem so strange because it is to order her whom I am proud to obey!”\n\nThe whistles are sounding; we are nearing Galatz. We are on fire with anxiety and eagerness.\n\nMina Harker’s Journal.\n\n30 October.--Mr. Morris took me to the hotel where our rooms had been ordered by telegraph, he being the one who could best be spared, since he does not speak any foreign language. The forces were distributed much as they had been at Varna, except that Lord Godalming went to the Vice-Consul, as his rank might serve as an immediate guarantee of some sort to the official, we being in extreme hurry. Jonathan and the two doctors went to the shipping agent to learn particulars of the arrival of the Czarina Catherine.\n\nLater.--Lord Godalming has returned. The Consul is away, and the Vice-Consul sick; so the routine work has been attended to by a clerk. He was very obliging, and offered to do anything in his power.\n\nJonathan Harker’s Journal.\n\n30 October.--At nine o’clock Dr. Van Helsing, Dr. Seward, and I called on Messrs. Mackenzie & Steinkoff, the agents of the London firm of Hapgood. They had received a wire from London, in answer to Lord Godalming’s telegraphed request, asking us to show them any civility in their power. They were more than kind and courteous, and took us at once on board the Czarina Catherine, which lay at anchor out in the river harbour. There we saw the Captain, Donelson by name, who told us of his voyage. He said that in all his life he had never had so favourable a run.\n\n“Man!” he said, “but it made us afeard, for we expeckit that we should have to pay for it wi’ some rare piece o’ ill luck, so as to keep up the average. It’s no canny to run frae London to the Black Sea wi’ a wind ahint ye, as though the Deil himself were blawin’ on yer sail for his ain purpose. An’ a’ the time we could no speer a thing. Gin we were nigh a ship, or a port, or a headland, a fog fell on us and travelled wi’ us, till when after it had lifted and we looked out, the deil a thing could we see. We ran by Gibraltar wi’oot bein’ able to signal; an’ till we came to the Dardanelles and had to wait to get our permit to pass, we never were within hail o’ aught. At first I inclined to slack off sail and beat about till the fog was lifted; but whiles, I thocht that if the Deil was minded to get us into the Black Sea quick, he was like to do it whether we would or no. If we had a quick voyage it would be no to our miscredit wi’ the owners, or no hurt to our traffic; an’ the Old Mon who had served his ain purpose wad be decently grateful to us for no hinderin’ him.” This mixture of simplicity and cunning, of superstition and commercial reasoning, aroused Van Helsing, who said:--\n\n“Mine friend, that Devil is more clever than he is thought by some; and he know when he meet his match!” The skipper was not displeased with the compliment, and went on:--\n\n“When we got past the Bosphorus the men began to grumble; some o’ them, the Roumanians, came and asked me to heave overboard a big box which had been put on board by a queer lookin’ old man just before we had started frae London. I had seen them speer at the fellow, and put out their twa fingers when they saw him, to guard against the evil eye. Man! but the supersteetion of foreigners is pairfectly rideeculous! I sent them aboot their business pretty quick; but as just after a fog closed in on us I felt a wee bit as they did anent something, though I wouldn’t say it was agin the big box. Well, on we went, and as the fog didn’t let up for five days I joost let the wind carry us; for if the Deil wanted to get somewheres--well, he would fetch it up a’reet. An’ if he didn’t, well, we’d keep a sharp lookout anyhow. Sure eneuch, we had a fair way and deep water all the time; and two days ago, when the mornin’ sun came through the fog, we found ourselves just in the river opposite Galatz. The Roumanians were wild, and wanted me right or wrong to take out the box and fling it in the river. I had to argy wi’ them aboot it wi’ a handspike; an’ when the last o’ them rose off the deck wi’ his head in his hand, I had convinced them that, evil eye or no evil eye, the property and the trust of my owners were better in my hands than in the river Danube. They had, mind ye, taken the box on the deck ready to fling in, and as it was marked Galatz via Varna, I thocht I’d let it lie till we discharged in the port an’ get rid o’t althegither. We didn’t do much clearin’ that day, an’ had to remain the nicht at anchor; but in the mornin’, braw an’ airly, an hour before sun-up, a man came aboard wi’ an order, written to him from England, to receive a box marked for one Count Dracula. Sure eneuch the matter was one ready to his hand. He had his papers a’ reet, an’ glad I was to be rid o’ the dam’ thing, for I was beginnin’ masel’ to feel uneasy at it. If the Deil did have any luggage aboord the ship, I’m thinkin’ it was nane ither than that same!”\n\n“What was the name of the man who took it?” asked Dr. Van Helsing with restrained eagerness.\n\n“I’ll be tellin’ ye quick!” he answered, and, stepping down to his cabin, produced a receipt signed “Immanuel Hildesheim.” Burgen-strasse 16 was the address. We found out that this was all the Captain knew; so with thanks we came away.\n\nWe found Hildesheim in his office, a Hebrew of rather the Adelphi Theatre type, with a nose like a sheep, and a fez. His arguments were pointed with specie--we doing the punctuation--and with a little bargaining he told us what he knew. This turned out to be simple but important. He had received a letter from Mr. de Ville of London, telling him to receive, if possible before sunrise so as to avoid customs, a box which would arrive at Galatz in the Czarina Catherine. This he was to give in charge to a certain Petrof Skinsky, who dealt with the Slovaks who traded down the river to the port. He had been paid for his work by an English bank note, which had been duly cashed for gold at the Danube International Bank. When Skinsky had come to him, he had taken him to the ship and handed over the box, so as to save porterage. That was all he knew.\n\nWe then sought for Skinsky, but were unable to find him. One of his neighbours, who did not seem to bear him any affection, said that he had gone away two days before, no one knew whither. This was corroborated by his landlord, who had received by messenger the key of the house together with the rent due, in English money. This had been between ten and eleven o’clock last night. We were at a standstill again.\n\nWhilst we were talking one came running and breathlessly gasped out that the body of Skinsky had been found inside the wall of the churchyard of St. Peter, and that the throat had been torn open as if by some wild animal. Those we had been speaking with ran off to see the horror, the women crying out “This is the work of a Slovak!” We hurried away lest we should have been in some way drawn into the affair, and so detained.\n\nAs we came home we could arrive at no definite conclusion. We were all convinced that the box was on its way, by water, to somewhere; but where that might be we would have to discover. With heavy hearts we came home to the hotel to Mina.\n\nWhen we met together, the first thing was to consult as to taking Mina again into our confidence. Things are getting desperate, and it is at least a chance, though a hazardous one. As a preliminary step, I was released from my promise to her.\n\nMina Harker’s Journal.\n\n30 October, evening.--They were so tired and worn out and dispirited that there was nothing to be done till they had some rest; so I asked them all to lie down for half an hour whilst I should enter everything up to the moment. I feel so grateful to the man who invented the “Traveller’s” typewriter, and to Mr. Morris for getting this one for me. I should have felt quite astray doing the work if I had to write with a pen....\n\nIt is all done; poor dear, dear Jonathan, what he must have suffered, what must he be suffering now. He lies on the sofa hardly seeming to breathe, and his whole body appears in collapse. His brows are knit; his face is drawn with pain. Poor fellow, maybe he is thinking, and I can see his face all wrinkled up with the concentration of his thoughts. Oh! if I could only help at all.... I shall do what I can.\n\nI have asked Dr. Van Helsing, and he has got me all the papers that I have not yet seen.... Whilst they are resting, I shall go over all carefully, and perhaps I may arrive at some conclusion. I shall try to follow the Professor’s example, and think without prejudice on the facts before me....\n\nI do believe that under God’s providence I have made a discovery. I shall get the maps and look over them....\n\nI am more than ever sure that I am right. My new conclusion is ready, so I shall get our party together and read it. They can judge it; it is well to be accurate, and every minute is precious.\n\nMina Harker’s Memorandum.\n\n(Entered in her Journal.)\n\nGround of inquiry.--Count Dracula’s problem is to get back to his own place.\n\n(a) He must be brought back by some one. This is evident; for had he power to move himself as he wished he could go either as man, or wolf, or bat, or in some other way. He evidently fears discovery or interference, in the state of helplessness in which he must be--confined as he is between dawn and sunset in his wooden box.\n\n(b) How is he to be taken?--Here a process of exclusions may help us. By road, by rail, by water?\n\n1. By Road.--There are endless difficulties, especially in leaving the city.\n\n(x) There are people; and people are curious, and investigate. A hint, a surmise, a doubt as to what might be in the box, would destroy him.\n\n(y) There are, or there may be, customs and octroi officers to pass.\n\n(z) His pursuers might follow. This is his highest fear; and in order to prevent his being betrayed he has repelled, so far as he can, even his victim--me!\n\n2. By Rail.--There is no one in charge of the box. It would have to take its chance of being delayed; and delay would be fatal, with enemies on the track. True, he might escape at night; but what would he be, if left in a strange place with no refuge that he could fly to? This is not what he intends; and he does not mean to risk it.\n\n3. By Water.--Here is the safest way, in one respect, but with most danger in another. On the water he is powerless except at night; even then he can only summon fog and storm and snow and his wolves. But were he wrecked, the living water would engulf him, helpless; and he would indeed be lost. He could have the vessel drive to land; but if it were unfriendly land, wherein he was not free to move, his position would still be desperate.\n\nWe know from the record that he was on the water; so what we have to do is to ascertain what water.\n\nThe first thing is to realise exactly what he has done as yet; we may, then, get a light on what his later task is to be.\n\nFirstly.--We must differentiate between what he did in London as part of his general plan of action, when he was pressed for moments and had to arrange as best he could.\n\nSecondly we must see, as well as we can surmise it from the facts we know of, what he has done here.\n\nAs to the first, he evidently intended to arrive at Galatz, and sent invoice to Varna to deceive us lest we should ascertain his means of exit from England; his immediate and sole purpose then was to escape. The proof of this, is the letter of instructions sent to Immanuel Hildesheim to clear and take away the box before sunrise. There is also the instruction to Petrof Skinsky. These we must only guess at; but there must have been some letter or message, since Skinsky came to Hildesheim.\n\nThat, so far, his plans were successful we know. The Czarina Catherine made a phenomenally quick journey--so much so that Captain Donelson’s suspicions were aroused; but his superstition united with his canniness played the Count’s game for him, and he ran with his favouring wind through fogs and all till he brought up blindfold at Galatz. That the Count’s arrangements were well made, has been proved. Hildesheim cleared the box, took it off, and gave it to Skinsky. Skinsky took it--and here we lose the trail. We only know that the box is somewhere on the water, moving along. The customs and the octroi, if there be any, have been avoided.\n\nNow we come to what the Count must have done after his arrival--on land, at Galatz.\n\nThe box was given to Skinsky before sunrise. At sunrise the Count could appear in his own form. Here, we ask why Skinsky was chosen at all to aid in the work? In my husband’s diary, Skinsky is mentioned as dealing with the Slovaks who trade down the river to the port; and the man’s remark, that the murder was the work of a Slovak, showed the general feeling against his class. The Count wanted isolation.\n\nMy surmise is, this: that in London the Count decided to get back to his castle by water, as the most safe and secret way. He was brought from the castle by Szgany, and probably they delivered their cargo to Slovaks who took the boxes to Varna, for there they were shipped for London. Thus the Count had knowledge of the persons who could arrange this service. When the box was on land, before sunrise or after sunset, he came out from his box, met Skinsky and instructed him what to do as to arranging the carriage of the box up some river. When this was done, and he knew that all was in train, he blotted out his traces, as he thought, by murdering his agent.\n\nI have examined the map and find that the river most suitable for the Slovaks to have ascended is either the Pruth or the Sereth. I read in the typescript that in my trance I heard cows low and water swirling level with my ears and the creaking of wood. The Count in his box, then, was on a river in an open boat--propelled probably either by oars or poles, for the banks are near and it is working against stream. There would be no such sound if floating down stream.\n\nOf course it may not be either the Sereth or the Pruth, but we may possibly investigate further. Now of these two, the Pruth is the more easily navigated, but the Sereth is, at Fundu, joined by the Bistritza which runs up round the Borgo Pass. The loop it makes is manifestly as close to Dracula’s castle as can be got by water.\n\nMina Harker’s Journal--continued.\n\nWhen I had done reading, Jonathan took me in his arms and kissed me. The others kept shaking me by both hands, and Dr. Van Helsing said:--\n\n“Our dear Madam Mina is once more our teacher. Her eyes have been where we were blinded. Now we are on the track once again, and this time we may succeed. Our enemy is at his most helpless; and if we can come on him by day, on the water, our task will be over. He has a start, but he is powerless to hasten, as he may not leave his box lest those who carry him may suspect; for them to suspect would be to prompt them to throw him in the stream where he perish. This he knows, and will not. Now men, to our Council of War; for, here and now, we must plan what each and all shall do.”\n\n“I shall get a steam launch and follow him,” said Lord Godalming.\n\n“And I, horses to follow on the bank lest by chance he land,” said Mr. Morris.\n\n“Good!” said the Professor, “both good. But neither must go alone. There must be force to overcome force if need be; the Slovak is strong and rough, and he carries rude arms.” All the men smiled, for amongst them they carried a small arsenal. Said Mr. Morris:--\n\n“I have brought some Winchesters; they are pretty handy in a crowd, and there may be wolves. The Count, if you remember, took some other precautions; he made some requisitions on others that Mrs. Harker could not quite hear or understand. We must be ready at all points.” Dr. Seward said:--\n\n“I think I had better go with Quincey. We have been accustomed to hunt together, and we two, well armed, will be a match for whatever may come along. You must not be alone, Art. It may be necessary to fight the Slovaks, and a chance thrust--for I don’t suppose these fellows carry guns--would undo all our plans. There must be no chances, this time; we shall not rest until the Count’s head and body have been separated, and we are sure that he cannot re-incarnate.” He looked at Jonathan as he spoke, and Jonathan looked at me. I could see that the poor dear was torn about in his mind. Of course he wanted to be with me; but then the boat service would, most likely, be the one which would destroy the ... the ... the ... Vampire. (Why did I hesitate to write the word?) He was silent awhile, and during his silence Dr. Van Helsing spoke:--\n\n“Friend Jonathan, this is to you for twice reasons. First, because you are young and brave and can fight, and all energies may be needed at the last; and again that it is your right to destroy him--that--which has wrought such woe to you and yours. Be not afraid for Madam Mina; she will be my care, if I may. I am old. My legs are not so quick to run as once; and I am not used to ride so long or to pursue as need be, or to fight with lethal weapons. But I can be of other service; I can fight in other way. And I can die, if need be, as well as younger men. Now let me say that what I would is this: while you, my Lord Godalming and friend Jonathan go in your so swift little steamboat up the river, and whilst John and Quincey guard the bank where perchance he might be landed, I will take Madam Mina right into the heart of the enemy’s country. Whilst the old fox is tied in his box, floating on the running stream whence he cannot escape to land--where he dares not raise the lid of his coffin-box lest his Slovak carriers should in fear leave him to perish--we shall go in the track where Jonathan went,--from Bistritz over the Borgo, and find our way to the Castle of Dracula. Here, Madam Mina’s hypnotic power will surely help, and we shall find our way--all dark and unknown otherwise--after the first sunrise when we are near that fateful place. There is much to be done, and other places to be made sanctify, so that that nest of vipers be obliterated.” Here Jonathan interrupted him hotly:--\n\n“Do you mean to say, Professor Van Helsing, that you would bring Mina, in her sad case and tainted as she is with that devil’s illness, right into the jaws of his death-trap? Not for the world! Not for Heaven or Hell!” He became almost speechless for a minute, and then went on:--\n\n“Do you know what the place is? Have you seen that awful den of hellish infamy--with the very moonlight alive with grisly shapes, and every speck of dust that whirls in the wind a devouring monster in embryo? Have you felt the Vampire’s lips upon your throat?” Here he turned to me, and as his eyes lit on my forehead he threw up his arms with a cry: “Oh, my God, what have we done to have this terror upon us!” and he sank down on the sofa in a collapse of misery. The Professor’s voice, as he spoke in clear, sweet tones, which seemed to vibrate in the air, calmed us all:--\n\n“Oh, my friend, it is because I would save Madam Mina from that awful place that I would go. God forbid that I should take her into that place. There is work--wild work--to be done there, that her eyes may not see. We men here, all save Jonathan, have seen with their own eyes what is to be done before that place can be purify. Remember that we are in terrible straits. If the Count escape us this time--and he is strong and subtle and cunning--he may choose to sleep him for a century, and then in time our dear one”--he took my hand--“would come to him to keep him company, and would be as those others that you, Jonathan, saw. You have told us of their gloating lips; you heard their ribald laugh as they clutched the moving bag that the Count threw to them. You shudder; and well may it be. Forgive me that I make you so much pain, but it is necessary. My friend, is it not a dire need for the which I am giving, possibly my life? If it were that any one went into that place to stay, it is I who would have to go to keep them company.”\n\n“Do as you will,” said Jonathan, with a sob that shook him all over, “we are in the hands of God!”\n\nLater.--Oh, it did me good to see the way that these brave men worked. How can women help loving men when they are so earnest, and so true, and so brave! And, too, it made me think of the wonderful power of money! What can it not do when it is properly applied; and what might it do when basely used. I felt so thankful that Lord Godalming is rich, and that both he and Mr. Morris, who also has plenty of money, are willing to spend it so freely. For if they did not, our little expedition could not start, either so promptly or so well equipped, as it will within another hour. It is not three hours since it was arranged what part each of us was to do; and now Lord Godalming and Jonathan have a lovely steam launch, with steam up ready to start at a moment’s notice. Dr. Seward and Mr. Morris have half a dozen good horses, well appointed. We have all the maps and appliances of various kinds that can be had. Professor Van Helsing and I are to leave by the 11:40 train to-night for Veresti, where we are to get a carriage to drive to the Borgo Pass. We are bringing a good deal of ready money, as we are to buy a carriage and horses. We shall drive ourselves, for we have no one whom we can trust in the matter. The Professor knows something of a great many languages, so we shall get on all right. We have all got arms, even for me a large-bore revolver; Jonathan would not be happy unless I was armed like the rest. Alas! I cannot carry one arm that the rest do; the scar on my forehead forbids that. Dear Dr. Van Helsing comforts me by telling me that I am fully armed as there may be wolves; the weather is getting colder every hour, and there are snow-flurries which come and go as warnings.\n\nLater.--It took all my courage to say good-bye to my darling. We may never meet again. Courage, Mina! the Professor is looking at you keenly; his look is a warning. There must be no tears now--unless it may be that God will let them fall in gladness.\n\nJonathan Harker’s Journal.\n\nOctober 30. Night.--I am writing this in the light from the furnace door of the steam launch: Lord Godalming is firing up. He is an experienced hand at the work, as he has had for years a launch of his own on the Thames, and another on the Norfolk Broads. Regarding our plans, we finally decided that Mina’s guess was correct, and that if any waterway was chosen for the Count’s escape back to his Castle, the Sereth and then the Bistritza at its junction, would be the one. We took it, that somewhere about the 47th degree, north latitude, would be the place chosen for the crossing the country between the river and the Carpathians. We have no fear in running at good speed up the river at night; there is plenty of water, and the banks are wide enough apart to make steaming, even in the dark, easy enough. Lord Godalming tells me to sleep for a while, as it is enough for the present for one to be on watch. But I cannot sleep--how can I with the terrible danger hanging over my darling, and her going out into that awful place.... My only comfort is that we are in the hands of God. Only for that faith it would be easier to die than to live, and so be quit of all the trouble. Mr. Morris and Dr. Seward were off on their long ride before we started; they are to keep up the right bank, far enough off to get on higher lands where they can see a good stretch of river and avoid the following of its curves. They have, for the first stages, two men to ride and lead their spare horses--four in all, so as not to excite curiosity. When they dismiss the men, which shall be shortly, they shall themselves look after the horses. It may be necessary for us to join forces; if so they can mount our whole party. One of the saddles has a movable horn, and can be easily adapted for Mina, if required.\n\nIt is a wild adventure we are on. Here, as we are rushing along through the darkness, with the cold from the river seeming to rise up and strike us; with all the mysterious voices of the night around us, it all comes home. We seem to be drifting into unknown places and unknown ways; into a whole world of dark and dreadful things. Godalming is shutting the furnace door....\n\n31 October.--Still hurrying along. The day has come, and Godalming is sleeping. I am on watch. The morning is bitterly cold; the furnace heat is grateful, though we have heavy fur coats. As yet we have passed only a few open boats, but none of them had on board any box or package of anything like the size of the one we seek. The men were scared every time we turned our electric lamp on them, and fell on their knees and prayed.\n\n1 November, evening.--No news all day; we have found nothing of the kind we seek. We have now passed into the Bistritza; and if we are wrong in our surmise our chance is gone. We have over-hauled every boat, big and little. Early this morning, one crew took us for a Government boat, and treated us accordingly. We saw in this a way of smoothing matters, so at Fundu, where the Bistritza runs into the Sereth, we got a Roumanian flag which we now fly conspicuously. With every boat which we have over-hauled since then this trick has succeeded; we have had every deference shown to us, and not once any objection to whatever we chose to ask or do. Some of the Slovaks tell us that a big boat passed them, going at more than usual speed as she had a double crew on board. This was before they came to Fundu, so they could not tell us whether the boat turned into the Bistritza or continued on up the Sereth. At Fundu we could not hear of any such boat, so she must have passed there in the night. I am feeling very sleepy; the cold is perhaps beginning to tell upon me, and nature must have rest some time. Godalming insists that he shall keep the first watch. God bless him for all his goodness to poor dear Mina and me.\n\n2 November, morning.--It is broad daylight. That good fellow would not wake me. He says it would have been a sin to, for I slept peacefully and was forgetting my trouble. It seems brutally selfish to me to have slept so long, and let him watch all night; but he was quite right. I am a new man this morning; and, as I sit here and watch him sleeping, I can do all that is necessary both as to minding the engine, steering, and keeping watch. I can feel that my strength and energy are coming back to me. I wonder where Mina is now, and Van Helsing. They should have got to Veresti about noon on Wednesday. It would take them some time to get the carriage and horses; so if they had started and travelled hard, they would be about now at the Borgo Pass. God guide and help them! I am afraid to think what may happen. If we could only go faster! but we cannot; the engines are throbbing and doing their utmost. I wonder how Dr. Seward and Mr. Morris are getting on. There seem to be endless streams running down the mountains into this river, but as none of them are very large--at present, at all events, though they are terrible doubtless in winter and when the snow melts--the horsemen may not have met much obstruction. I hope that before we get to Strasba we may see them; for if by that time we have not overtaken the Count, it may be necessary to take counsel together what to do next.\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n2 November.--Three days on the road. No news, and no time to write it if there had been, for every moment is precious. We have had only the rest needful for the horses; but we are both bearing it wonderfully. Those adventurous days of ours are turning up useful. We must push on; we shall never feel happy till we get the launch in sight again.\n\n3 November.--We heard at Fundu that the launch had gone up the Bistritza. I wish it wasn’t so cold. There are signs of snow coming; and if it falls heavy it will stop us. In such case we must get a sledge and go on, Russian fashion.\n\n4 November.--To-day we heard of the launch having been detained by an accident when trying to force a way up the rapids. The Slovak boats get up all right, by aid of a rope and steering with knowledge. Some went up only a few hours before. Godalming is an amateur fitter himself, and evidently it was he who put the launch in trim again. Finally, they got up the rapids all right, with local help, and are off on the chase afresh. I fear that the boat is not any better for the accident; the peasantry tell us that after she got upon smooth water again, she kept stopping every now and again so long as she was in sight. We must push on harder than ever; our help may be wanted soon.\n\nMina Harker’s Journal.\n\n31 October.--Arrived at Veresti at noon. The Professor tells me that this morning at dawn he could hardly hypnotise me at all, and that all I could say was: “dark and quiet.” He is off now buying a carriage and horses. He says that he will later on try to buy additional horses, so that we may be able to change them on the way. We have something more than 70 miles before us. The country is lovely, and most interesting; if only we were under different conditions, how delightful it would be to see it all. If Jonathan and I were driving through it alone what a pleasure it would be. To stop and see people, and learn something of their life, and to fill our minds and memories with all the colour and picturesqueness of the whole wild, beautiful country and the quaint people! But, alas!--\n\nLater.--Dr. Van Helsing has returned. He has got the carriage and horses; we are to have some dinner, and to start in an hour. The landlady is putting us up a huge basket of provisions; it seems enough for a company of soldiers. The Professor encourages her, and whispers to me that it may be a week before we can get any good food again. He has been shopping too, and has sent home such a wonderful lot of fur coats and wraps, and all sorts of warm things. There will not be any chance of our being cold.\n\nWe shall soon be off. I am afraid to think what may happen to us. We are truly in the hands of God. He alone knows what may be, and I pray Him, with all the strength of my sad and humble soul, that He will watch over my beloved husband; that whatever may happen, Jonathan may know that I loved him and honoured him more than I can say, and that my latest and truest thought will be always for him.\n\n# MINA HARKER’S JOURNAL\n\n1 November.--All day long we have travelled, and at a good speed. The horses seem to know that they are being kindly treated, for they go willingly their full stage at best speed. We have now had so many changes and find the same thing so constantly that we are encouraged to think that the journey will be an easy one. Dr. Van Helsing is laconic; he tells the farmers that he is hurrying to Bistritz, and pays them well to make the exchange of horses. We get hot soup, or coffee, or tea; and off we go. It is a lovely country; full of beauties of all imaginable kinds, and the people are brave, and strong, and simple, and seem full of nice qualities. They are very, very superstitious. In the first house where we stopped, when the woman who served us saw the scar on my forehead, she crossed herself and put out two fingers towards me, to keep off the evil eye. I believe they went to the trouble of putting an extra amount of garlic into our food; and I can’t abide garlic. Ever since then I have taken care not to take off my hat or veil, and so have escaped their suspicions. We are travelling fast, and as we have no driver with us to carry tales, we go ahead of scandal; but I daresay that fear of the evil eye will follow hard behind us all the way. The Professor seems tireless; all day he would not take any rest, though he made me sleep for a long spell. At sunset time he hypnotised me, and he says that I answered as usual “darkness, lapping water and creaking wood”; so our enemy is still on the river. I am afraid to think of Jonathan, but somehow I have now no fear for him, or for myself. I write this whilst we wait in a farmhouse for the horses to be got ready. Dr. Van Helsing is sleeping. Poor dear, he looks very tired and old and grey, but his mouth is set as firmly as a conqueror’s; even in his sleep he is instinct with resolution. When we have well started I must make him rest whilst I drive. I shall tell him that we have days before us, and we must not break down when most of all his strength will be needed.... All is ready; we are off shortly.\n\n2 November, morning.--I was successful, and we took turns driving all night; now the day is on us, bright though cold. There is a strange heaviness in the air--I say heaviness for want of a better word; I mean that it oppresses us both. It is very cold, and only our warm furs keep us comfortable. At dawn Van Helsing hypnotised me; he says I answered “darkness, creaking wood and roaring water,” so the river is changing as they ascend. I do hope that my darling will not run any chance of danger--more than need be; but we are in God’s hands.\n\n2 November, night.--All day long driving. The country gets wilder as we go, and the great spurs of the Carpathians, which at Veresti seemed so far from us and so low on the horizon, now seem to gather round us and tower in front. We both seem in good spirits; I think we make an effort each to cheer the other; in the doing so we cheer ourselves. Dr. Van Helsing says that by morning we shall reach the Borgo Pass. The houses are very few here now, and the Professor says that the last horse we got will have to go on with us, as we may not be able to change. He got two in addition to the two we changed, so that now we have a rude four-in-hand. The dear horses are patient and good, and they give us no trouble. We are not worried with other travellers, and so even I can drive. We shall get to the Pass in daylight; we do not want to arrive before. So we take it easy, and have each a long rest in turn. Oh, what will to-morrow bring to us? We go to seek the place where my poor darling suffered so much. God grant that we may be guided aright, and that He will deign to watch over my husband and those dear to us both, and who are in such deadly peril. As for me, I am not worthy in His sight. Alas! I am unclean to His eyes, and shall be until He may deign to let me stand forth in His sight as one of those who have not incurred His wrath.\n\nMemorandum by Abraham Van Helsing.\n\n4 November.--This to my old and true friend John Seward, M.D., of Purfleet, London, in case I may not see him. It may explain. It is morning, and I write by a fire which all the night I have kept alive--Madam Mina aiding me. It is cold, cold; so cold that the grey heavy sky is full of snow, which when it falls will settle for all winter as the ground is hardening to receive it. It seems to have affected Madam Mina; she has been so heavy of head all day that she was not like herself. She sleeps, and sleeps, and sleeps! She who is usual so alert, have done literally nothing all the day; she even have lost her appetite. She make no entry into her little diary, she who write so faithful at every pause. Something whisper to me that all is not well. However, to-night she is more vif. Her long sleep all day have refresh and restore her, for now she is all sweet and bright as ever. At sunset I try to hypnotise her, but alas! with no effect; the power has grown less and less with each day, and to-night it fail me altogether. Well, God’s will be done--whatever it may be, and whithersoever it may lead!\n\nNow to the historical, for as Madam Mina write not in her stenography, I must, in my cumbrous old fashion, that so each day of us may not go unrecorded.\n\nWe got to the Borgo Pass just after sunrise yesterday morning. When I saw the signs of the dawn I got ready for the hypnotism. We stopped our carriage, and got down so that there might be no disturbance. I made a couch with furs, and Madam Mina, lying down, yield herself as usual, but more slow and more short time than ever, to the hypnotic sleep. As before, came the answer: “darkness and the swirling of water.” Then she woke, bright and radiant and we go on our way and soon reach the Pass. At this time and place, she become all on fire with zeal; some new guiding power be in her manifested, for she point to a road and say:--\n\n“This is the way.”\n\n“How know you it?” I ask.\n\n“Of course I know it,” she answer, and with a pause, add: “Have not my Jonathan travelled it and wrote of his travel?”\n\nAt first I think somewhat strange, but soon I see that there be only one such by-road. It is used but little, and very different from the coach road from the Bukovina to Bistritz, which is more wide and hard, and more of use.\n\nSo we came down this road; when we meet other ways--not always were we sure that they were roads at all, for they be neglect and light snow have fallen--the horses know and they only. I give rein to them, and they go on so patient. By-and-by we find all the things which Jonathan have note in that wonderful diary of him. Then we go on for long, long hours and hours. At the first, I tell Madam Mina to sleep; she try, and she succeed. She sleep all the time; till at the last, I feel myself to suspicious grow, and attempt to wake her. But she sleep on, and I may not wake her though I try. I do not wish to try too hard lest I harm her; for I know that she have suffer much, and sleep at times be all-in-all to her. I think I drowse myself, for all of sudden I feel guilt, as though I have done something; I find myself bolt up, with the reins in my hand, and the good horses go along jog, jog, just as ever. I look down and find Madam Mina still sleep. It is now not far off sunset time, and over the snow the light of the sun flow in big yellow flood, so that we throw great long shadow on where the mountain rise so steep. For we are going up, and up; and all is oh! so wild and rocky, as though it were the end of the world.\n\nThen I arouse Madam Mina. This time she wake with not much trouble, and then I try to put her to hypnotic sleep. But she sleep not, being as though I were not. Still I try and try, till all at once I find her and myself in dark; so I look round, and find that the sun have gone down. Madam Mina laugh, and I turn and look at her. She is now quite awake, and look so well as I never saw her since that night at Carfax when we first enter the Count’s house. I am amaze, and not at ease then; but she is so bright and tender and thoughtful for me that I forget all fear. I light a fire, for we have brought supply of wood with us, and she prepare food while I undo the horses and set them, tethered in shelter, to feed. Then when I return to the fire she have my supper ready. I go to help her; but she smile, and tell me that she have eat already--that she was so hungry that she would not wait. I like it not, and I have grave doubts; but I fear to affright her, and so I am silent of it. She help me and I eat alone; and then we wrap in fur and lie beside the fire, and I tell her to sleep while I watch. But presently I forget all of watching; and when I sudden remember that I watch, I find her lying quiet, but awake, and looking at me with so bright eyes. Once, twice more the same occur, and I get much sleep till before morning. When I wake I try to hypnotise her; but alas! though she shut her eyes obedient, she may not sleep. The sun rise up, and up, and up; and then sleep come to her too late, but so heavy that she will not wake. I have to lift her up, and place her sleeping in the carriage when I have harnessed the horses and made all ready. Madam still sleep, and she look in her sleep more healthy and more redder than before. And I like it not. And I am afraid, afraid, afraid!--I am afraid of all things--even to think but I must go on my way. The stake we play for is life and death, or more than these, and we must not flinch.\n\n5 November, morning.--Let me be accurate in everything, for though you and I have seen some strange things together, you may at the first think that I, Van Helsing, am mad--that the many horrors and the so long strain on nerves has at the last turn my brain.\n\nAll yesterday we travel, ever getting closer to the mountains, and moving into a more and more wild and desert land. There are great, frowning precipices and much falling water, and Nature seem to have held sometime her carnival. Madam Mina still sleep and sleep; and though I did have hunger and appeased it, I could not waken her--even for food. I began to fear that the fatal spell of the place was upon her, tainted as she is with that Vampire baptism. “Well,” said I to myself, “if it be that she sleep all the day, it shall also be that I do not sleep at night.” As we travel on the rough road, for a road of an ancient and imperfect kind there was, I held down my head and slept. Again I waked with a sense of guilt and of time passed, and found Madam Mina still sleeping, and the sun low down. But all was indeed changed; the frowning mountains seemed further away, and we were near the top of a steep-rising hill, on summit of which was such a castle as Jonathan tell of in his diary. At once I exulted and feared; for now, for good or ill, the end was near.\n\nI woke Madam Mina, and again tried to hypnotise her; but alas! unavailing till too late. Then, ere the great dark came upon us--for even after down-sun the heavens reflected the gone sun on the snow, and all was for a time in a great twilight--I took out the horses and fed them in what shelter I could. Then I make a fire; and near it I make Madam Mina, now awake and more charming than ever, sit comfortable amid her rugs. I got ready food: but she would not eat, simply saying that she had not hunger. I did not press her, knowing her unavailingness. But I myself eat, for I must needs now be strong for all. Then, with the fear on me of what might be, I drew a ring so big for her comfort, round where Madam Mina sat; and over the ring I passed some of the wafer, and I broke it fine so that all was well guarded. She sat still all the time--so still as one dead; and she grew whiter and ever whiter till the snow was not more pale; and no word she said. But when I drew near, she clung to me, and I could know that the poor soul shook her from head to feet with a tremor that was pain to feel. I said to her presently, when she had grown more quiet:--\n\n“Will you not come over to the fire?” for I wished to make a test of what she could. She rose obedient, but when she have made a step she stopped, and stood as one stricken.\n\n“Why not go on?” I asked. She shook her head, and, coming back, sat down in her place. Then, looking at me with open eyes, as of one waked from sleep, she said simply:--\n\n“I cannot!” and remained silent. I rejoiced, for I knew that what she could not, none of those that we dreaded could. Though there might be danger to her body, yet her soul was safe!\n\nPresently the horses began to scream, and tore at their tethers till I came to them and quieted them. When they did feel my hands on them, they whinnied low as in joy, and licked at my hands and were quiet for a time. Many times through the night did I come to them, till it arrive to the cold hour when all nature is at lowest; and every time my coming was with quiet of them. In the cold hour the fire began to die, and I was about stepping forth to replenish it, for now the snow came in flying sweeps and with it a chill mist. Even in the dark there was a light of some kind, as there ever is over snow; and it seemed as though the snow-flurries and the wreaths of mist took shape as of women with trailing garments. All was in dead, grim silence only that the horses whinnied and cowered, as if in terror of the worst. I began to fear--horrible fears; but then came to me the sense of safety in that ring wherein I stood. I began, too, to think that my imaginings were of the night, and the gloom, and the unrest that I have gone through, and all the terrible anxiety. It was as though my memories of all Jonathan’s horrid experience were befooling me; for the snow flakes and the mist began to wheel and circle round, till I could get as though a shadowy glimpse of those women that would have kissed him. And then the horses cowered lower and lower, and moaned in terror as men do in pain. Even the madness of fright was not to them, so that they could break away. I feared for my dear Madam Mina when these weird figures drew near and circled round. I looked at her, but she sat calm, and smiled at me; when I would have stepped to the fire to replenish it, she caught me and held me back, and whispered, like a voice that one hears in a dream, so low it was:--\n\n“No! No! Do not go without. Here you are safe!” I turned to her, and looking in her eyes, said:--\n\n“But you? It is for you that I fear!” whereat she laughed--a laugh, low and unreal, and said:--\n\n“Fear for me! Why fear for me? None safer in all the world from them than I am,” and as I wondered at the meaning of her words, a puff of wind made the flame leap up, and I see the red scar on her forehead. Then, alas! I knew. Did I not, I would soon have learned, for the wheeling figures of mist and snow came closer, but keeping ever without the Holy circle. Then they began to materialise till--if God have not take away my reason, for I saw it through my eyes--there were before me in actual flesh the same three women that Jonathan saw in the room, when they would have kissed his throat. I knew the swaying round forms, the bright hard eyes, the white teeth, the ruddy colour, the voluptuous lips. They smiled ever at poor dear Madam Mina; and as their laugh came through the silence of the night, they twined their arms and pointed to her, and said in those so sweet tingling tones that Jonathan said were of the intolerable sweetness of the water-glasses:--\n\n“Come, sister. Come to us. Come! Come!” In fear I turned to my poor Madam Mina, and my heart with gladness leapt like flame; for oh! the terror in her sweet eyes, the repulsion, the horror, told a story to my heart that was all of hope. God be thanked she was not, yet, of them. I seized some of the firewood which was by me, and holding out some of the Wafer, advanced on them towards the fire. They drew back before me, and laughed their low horrid laugh. I fed the fire, and feared them not; for I knew that we were safe within our protections. They could not approach, me, whilst so armed, nor Madam Mina whilst she remained within the ring, which she could not leave no more than they could enter. The horses had ceased to moan, and lay still on the ground; the snow fell on them softly, and they grew whiter. I knew that there was for the poor beasts no more of terror.\n\nAnd so we remained till the red of the dawn to fall through the snow-gloom. I was desolate and afraid, and full of woe and terror; but when that beautiful sun began to climb the horizon life was to me again. At the first coming of the dawn the horrid figures melted in the whirling mist and snow; the wreaths of transparent gloom moved away towards the castle, and were lost.\n\nInstinctively, with the dawn coming, I turned to Madam Mina, intending to hypnotise her; but she lay in a deep and sudden sleep, from which I could not wake her. I tried to hypnotise through her sleep, but she made no response, none at all; and the day broke. I fear yet to stir. I have made my fire and have seen the horses, they are all dead. To-day I have much to do here, and I keep waiting till the sun is up high; for there may be places where I must go, where that sunlight, though snow and mist obscure it, will be to me a safety.\n\nI will strengthen me with breakfast, and then I will to my terrible work. Madam Mina still sleeps; and, God be thanked! she is calm in her sleep....\n\nJonathan Harker’s Journal.\n\n4 November, evening.--The accident to the launch has been a terrible thing for us. Only for it we should have overtaken the boat long ago; and by now my dear Mina would have been free. I fear to think of her, off on the wolds near that horrid place. We have got horses, and we follow on the track. I note this whilst Godalming is getting ready. We have our arms. The Szgany must look out if they mean fight. Oh, if only Morris and Seward were with us. We must only hope! If I write no more Good-bye, Mina! God bless and keep you.\n\nDr. Seward’s Diary.\n\n5 November.--With the dawn we saw the body of Szgany before us dashing away from the river with their leiter-wagon. They surrounded it in a cluster, and hurried along as though beset. The snow is falling lightly and there is a strange excitement in the air. It may be our own feelings, but the depression is strange. Far off I hear the howling of wolves; the snow brings them down from the mountains, and there are dangers to all of us, and from all sides. The horses are nearly ready, and we are soon off. We ride to death of some one. God alone knows who, or where, or what, or when, or how it may be....\n\nDr. Van Helsing’s Memorandum.\n\n5 November, afternoon.--I am at least sane. Thank God for that mercy at all events, though the proving it has been dreadful. When I left Madam Mina sleeping within the Holy circle, I took my way to the castle. The blacksmith hammer which I took in the carriage from Veresti was useful; though the doors were all open I broke them off the rusty hinges, lest some ill-intent or ill-chance should close them, so that being entered I might not get out. Jonathan’s bitter experience served me here. By memory of his diary I found my way to the old chapel, for I knew that here my work lay. The air was oppressive; it seemed as if there was some sulphurous fume, which at times made me dizzy. Either there was a roaring in my ears or I heard afar off the howl of wolves. Then I bethought me of my dear Madam Mina, and I was in terrible plight. The dilemma had me between his horns.\n\nHer, I had not dare to take into this place, but left safe from the Vampire in that Holy circle; and yet even there would be the wolf! I resolve me that my work lay here, and that as to the wolves we must submit, if it were God’s will. At any rate it was only death and freedom beyond. So did I choose for her. Had it but been for myself the choice had been easy, the maw of the wolf were better to rest in than the grave of the Vampire! So I make my choice to go on with my work.\n\nI knew that there were at least three graves to find--graves that are inhabit; so I search, and search, and I find one of them. She lay in her Vampire sleep, so full of life and voluptuous beauty that I shudder as though I have come to do murder. Ah, I doubt not that in old time, when such things were, many a man who set forth to do such a task as mine, found at the last his heart fail him, and then his nerve. So he delay, and delay, and delay, till the mere beauty and the fascination of the wanton Un-Dead have hypnotise him; and he remain on and on, till sunset come, and the Vampire sleep be over. Then the beautiful eyes of the fair woman open and look love, and the voluptuous mouth present to a kiss--and man is weak. And there remain one more victim in the Vampire fold; one more to swell the grim and grisly ranks of the Un-Dead!...\n\nThere is some fascination, surely, when I am moved by the mere presence of such an one, even lying as she lay in a tomb fretted with age and heavy with the dust of centuries, though there be that horrid odour such as the lairs of the Count have had. Yes, I was moved--I, Van Helsing, with all my purpose and with my motive for hate--I was moved to a yearning for delay which seemed to paralyse my faculties and to clog my very soul. It may have been that the need of natural sleep, and the strange oppression of the air were beginning to overcome me. Certain it was that I was lapsing into sleep, the open-eyed sleep of one who yields to a sweet fascination, when there came through the snow-stilled air a long, low wail, so full of woe and pity that it woke me like the sound of a clarion. For it was the voice of my dear Madam Mina that I heard.\n\nThen I braced myself again to my horrid task, and found by wrenching away tomb-tops one other of the sisters, the other dark one. I dared not pause to look on her as I had on her sister, lest once more I should begin to be enthrall; but I go on searching until, presently, I find in a high great tomb as if made to one much beloved that other fair sister which, like Jonathan I had seen to gather herself out of the atoms of the mist. She was so fair to look on, so radiantly beautiful, so exquisitely voluptuous, that the very instinct of man in me, which calls some of my sex to love and to protect one of hers, made my head whirl with new emotion. But God be thanked, that soul-wail of my dear Madam Mina had not died out of my ears; and, before the spell could be wrought further upon me, I had nerved myself to my wild work. By this time I had searched all the tombs in the chapel, so far as I could tell; and as there had been only three of these Un-Dead phantoms around us in the night, I took it that there were no more of active Un-Dead existent. There was one great tomb more lordly than all the rest; huge it was, and nobly proportioned. On it was but one word\n\nThis then was the Un-Dead home of the King-Vampire, to whom so many more were due. Its emptiness spoke eloquent to make certain what I knew. Before I began to restore these women to their dead selves through my awful work, I laid in Dracula’s tomb some of the Wafer, and so banished him from it, Un-Dead, for ever.\n\nThen began my terrible task, and I dreaded it. Had it been but one, it had been easy, comparative. But three! To begin twice more after I had been through a deed of horror; for if it was terrible with the sweet Miss Lucy, what would it not be with these strange ones who had survived through centuries, and who had been strengthened by the passing of the years; who would, if they could, have fought for their foul lives....\n\nOh, my friend John, but it was butcher work; had I not been nerved by thoughts of other dead, and of the living over whom hung such a pall of fear, I could not have gone on. I tremble and tremble even yet, though till all was over, God be thanked, my nerve did stand. Had I not seen the repose in the first place, and the gladness that stole over it just ere the final dissolution came, as realisation that the soul had been won, I could not have gone further with my butchery. I could not have endured the horrid screeching as the stake drove home; the plunging of writhing form, and lips of bloody foam. I should have fled in terror and left my work undone. But it is over! And the poor souls, I can pity them now and weep, as I think of them placid each in her full sleep of death for a short moment ere fading. For, friend John, hardly had my knife severed the head of each, before the whole body began to melt away and crumble in to its native dust, as though the death that should have come centuries agone had at last assert himself and say at once and loud “I am here!”\n\nBefore I left the castle I so fixed its entrances that never more can the Count enter there Un-Dead.\n\nWhen I stepped into the circle where Madam Mina slept, she woke from her sleep, and, seeing, me, cried out in pain that I had endured too much.\n\n“Come!” she said, “come away from this awful place! Let us go to meet my husband who is, I know, coming towards us.” She was looking thin and pale and weak; but her eyes were pure and glowed with fervour. I was glad to see her paleness and her illness, for my mind was full of the fresh horror of that ruddy vampire sleep.\n\nAnd so with trust and hope, and yet full of fear, we go eastward to meet our friends--and him--whom Madam Mina tell me that she know are coming to meet us.\n\nMina Harker’s Journal.\n\n6 November.--It was late in the afternoon when the Professor and I took our way towards the east whence I knew Jonathan was coming. We did not go fast, though the way was steeply downhill, for we had to take heavy rugs and wraps with us; we dared not face the possibility of being left without warmth in the cold and the snow. We had to take some of our provisions, too, for we were in a perfect desolation, and, so far as we could see through the snowfall, there was not even the sign of habitation. When we had gone about a mile, I was tired with the heavy walking and sat down to rest. Then we looked back and saw where the clear line of Dracula’s castle cut the sky; for we were so deep under the hill whereon it was set that the angle of perspective of the Carpathian mountains was far below it. We saw it in all its grandeur, perched a thousand feet on the summit of a sheer precipice, and with seemingly a great gap between it and the steep of the adjacent mountain on any side. There was something wild and uncanny about the place. We could hear the distant howling of wolves. They were far off, but the sound, even though coming muffled through the deadening snowfall, was full of terror. I knew from the way Dr. Van Helsing was searching about that he was trying to seek some strategic point, where we would be less exposed in case of attack. The rough roadway still led downwards; we could trace it through the drifted snow.\n\nIn a little while the Professor signalled to me, so I got up and joined him. He had found a wonderful spot, a sort of natural hollow in a rock, with an entrance like a doorway between two boulders. He took me by the hand and drew me in: “See!” he said, “here you will be in shelter; and if the wolves do come I can meet them one by one.” He brought in our furs, and made a snug nest for me, and got out some provisions and forced them upon me. But I could not eat; to even try to do so was repulsive to me, and, much as I would have liked to please him, I could not bring myself to the attempt. He looked very sad, but did not reproach me. Taking his field-glasses from the case, he stood on the top of the rock, and began to search the horizon. Suddenly he called out:--\n\n“Look! Madam Mina, look! look!” I sprang up and stood beside him on the rock; he handed me his glasses and pointed. The snow was now falling more heavily, and swirled about fiercely, for a high wind was beginning to blow. However, there were times when there were pauses between the snow flurries and I could see a long way round. From the height where we were it was possible to see a great distance; and far off, beyond the white waste of snow, I could see the river lying like a black ribbon in kinks and curls as it wound its way. Straight in front of us and not far off--in fact, so near that I wondered we had not noticed before--came a group of mounted men hurrying along. In the midst of them was a cart, a long leiter-wagon which swept from side to side, like a dog’s tail wagging, with each stern inequality of the road. Outlined against the snow as they were, I could see from the men’s clothes that they were peasants or gypsies of some kind.\n\nOn the cart was a great square chest. My heart leaped as I saw it, for I felt that the end was coming. The evening was now drawing close, and well I knew that at sunset the Thing, which was till then imprisoned there, would take new freedom and could in any of many forms elude all pursuit. In fear I turned to the Professor; to my consternation, however, he was not there. An instant later, I saw him below me. Round the rock he had drawn a circle, such as we had found shelter in last night. When he had completed it he stood beside me again, saying:--\n\n“At least you shall be safe here from him!” He took the glasses from me, and at the next lull of the snow swept the whole space below us. “See,” he said, “they come quickly; they are flogging the horses, and galloping as hard as they can.” He paused and went on in a hollow voice:--\n\n“They are racing for the sunset. We may be too late. God’s will be done!” Down came another blinding rush of driving snow, and the whole landscape was blotted out. It soon passed, however, and once more his glasses were fixed on the plain. Then came a sudden cry:--\n\n“Look! Look! Look! See, two horsemen follow fast, coming up from the south. It must be Quincey and John. Take the glass. Look before the snow blots it all out!” I took it and looked. The two men might be Dr. Seward and Mr. Morris. I knew at all events that neither of them was Jonathan. At the same time I knew that Jonathan was not far off; looking around I saw on the north side of the coming party two other men, riding at break-neck speed. One of them I knew was Jonathan, and the other I took, of course, to be Lord Godalming. They, too, were pursuing the party with the cart. When I told the Professor he shouted in glee like a schoolboy, and, after looking intently till a snow fall made sight impossible, he laid his Winchester rifle ready for use against the boulder at the opening of our shelter. “They are all converging,” he said. “When the time comes we shall have gypsies on all sides.” I got out my revolver ready to hand, for whilst we were speaking the howling of wolves came louder and closer. When the snow storm abated a moment we looked again. It was strange to see the snow falling in such heavy flakes close to us, and beyond, the sun shining more and more brightly as it sank down towards the far mountain tops. Sweeping the glass all around us I could see here and there dots moving singly and in twos and threes and larger numbers--the wolves were gathering for their prey.\n\nEvery instant seemed an age whilst we waited. The wind came now in fierce bursts, and the snow was driven with fury as it swept upon us in circling eddies. At times we could not see an arm’s length before us; but at others, as the hollow-sounding wind swept by us, it seemed to clear the air-space around us so that we could see afar off. We had of late been so accustomed to watch for sunrise and sunset, that we knew with fair accuracy when it would be; and we knew that before long the sun would set. It was hard to believe that by our watches it was less than an hour that we waited in that rocky shelter before the various bodies began to converge close upon us. The wind came now with fiercer and more bitter sweeps, and more steadily from the north. It seemingly had driven the snow clouds from us, for, with only occasional bursts, the snow fell. We could distinguish clearly the individuals of each party, the pursued and the pursuers. Strangely enough those pursued did not seem to realise, or at least to care, that they were pursued; they seemed, however, to hasten with redoubled speed as the sun dropped lower and lower on the mountain tops.\n\nCloser and closer they drew. The Professor and I crouched down behind our rock, and held our weapons ready; I could see that he was determined that they should not pass. One and all were quite unaware of our presence.\n\nAll at once two voices shouted out to: “Halt!” One was my Jonathan’s, raised in a high key of passion; the other Mr. Morris’ strong resolute tone of quiet command. The gypsies may not have known the language, but there was no mistaking the tone, in whatever tongue the words were spoken. Instinctively they reined in, and at the instant Lord Godalming and Jonathan dashed up at one side and Dr. Seward and Mr. Morris on the other. The leader of the gypsies, a splendid-looking fellow who sat his horse like a centaur, waved them back, and in a fierce voice gave to his companions some word to proceed. They lashed the horses which sprang forward; but the four men raised their Winchester rifles, and in an unmistakable way commanded them to stop. At the same moment Dr. Van Helsing and I rose behind the rock and pointed our weapons at them. Seeing that they were surrounded the men tightened their reins and drew up. The leader turned to them and gave a word at which every man of the gypsy party drew what weapon he carried, knife or pistol, and held himself in readiness to attack. Issue was joined in an instant.\n\nThe leader, with a quick movement of his rein, threw his horse out in front, and pointing first to the sun--now close down on the hill tops--and then to the castle, said something which I did not understand. For answer, all four men of our party threw themselves from their horses and dashed towards the cart. I should have felt terrible fear at seeing Jonathan in such danger, but that the ardour of battle must have been upon me as well as the rest of them; I felt no fear, but only a wild, surging desire to do something. Seeing the quick movement of our parties, the leader of the gypsies gave a command; his men instantly formed round the cart in a sort of undisciplined endeavour, each one shouldering and pushing the other in his eagerness to carry out the order.\n\nIn the midst of this I could see that Jonathan on one side of the ring of men, and Quincey on the other, were forcing a way to the cart; it was evident that they were bent on finishing their task before the sun should set. Nothing seemed to stop or even to hinder them. Neither the levelled weapons nor the flashing knives of the gypsies in front, nor the howling of the wolves behind, appeared to even attract their attention. Jonathan’s impetuosity, and the manifest singleness of his purpose, seemed to overawe those in front of him; instinctively they cowered, aside and let him pass. In an instant he had jumped upon the cart, and, with a strength which seemed incredible, raised the great box, and flung it over the wheel to the ground. In the meantime, Mr. Morris had had to use force to pass through his side of the ring of Szgany. All the time I had been breathlessly watching Jonathan I had, with the tail of my eye, seen him pressing desperately forward, and had seen the knives of the gypsies flash as he won a way through them, and they cut at him. He had parried with his great bowie knife, and at first I thought that he too had come through in safety; but as he sprang beside Jonathan, who had by now jumped from the cart, I could see that with his left hand he was clutching at his side, and that the blood was spurting through his fingers. He did not delay notwithstanding this, for as Jonathan, with desperate energy, attacked one end of the chest, attempting to prize off the lid with his great Kukri knife, he attacked the other frantically with his bowie. Under the efforts of both men the lid began to yield; the nails drew with a quick screeching sound, and the top of the box was thrown back.\n\nBy this time the gypsies, seeing themselves covered by the Winchesters, and at the mercy of Lord Godalming and Dr. Seward, had given in and made no resistance. The sun was almost down on the mountain tops, and the shadows of the whole group fell long upon the snow. I saw the Count lying within the box upon the earth, some of which the rude falling from the cart had scattered over him. He was deathly pale, just like a waxen image, and the red eyes glared with the horrible vindictive look which I knew too well.\n\nAs I looked, the eyes saw the sinking sun, and the look of hate in them turned to triumph.\n\nBut, on the instant, came the sweep and flash of Jonathan’s great knife. I shrieked as I saw it shear through the throat; whilst at the same moment Mr. Morris’s bowie knife plunged into the heart.\n\nIt was like a miracle; but before our very eyes, and almost in the drawing of a breath, the whole body crumbled into dust and passed from our sight.\n\nI shall be glad as long as I live that even in that moment of final dissolution, there was in the face a look of peace, such as I never could have imagined might have rested there.\n\nThe Castle of Dracula now stood out against the red sky, and every stone of its broken battlements was articulated against the light of the setting sun.\n\nThe gypsies, taking us as in some way the cause of the extraordinary disappearance of the dead man, turned, without a word, and rode away as if for their lives. Those who were unmounted jumped upon the leiter-wagon and shouted to the horsemen not to desert them. The wolves, which had withdrawn to a safe distance, followed in their wake, leaving us alone.\n\nMr. Morris, who had sunk to the ground, leaned on his elbow, holding his hand pressed to his side; the blood still gushed through his fingers. I flew to him, for the Holy circle did not now keep me back; so did the two doctors. Jonathan knelt behind him and the wounded man laid back his head on his shoulder. With a sigh he took, with a feeble effort, my hand in that of his own which was unstained. He must have seen the anguish of my heart in my face, for he smiled at me and said:--\n\n“I am only too happy to have been of any service! Oh, God!” he cried suddenly, struggling up to a sitting posture and pointing to me, “It was worth for this to die! Look! look!”\n\nThe sun was now right down upon the mountain top, and the red gleams fell upon my face, so that it was bathed in rosy light. With one impulse the men sank on their knees and a deep and earnest “Amen” broke from all as their eyes followed the pointing of his finger. The dying man spoke:--\n\n“Now God be thanked that all has not been in vain! See! the snow is not more stainless than her forehead! The curse has passed away!”\n\nAnd, to our bitter grief, with a smile and in silence, he died, a gallant gentleman.\n\nSeven years ago we all went through the flames; and the happiness of some of us since then is, we think, well worth the pain we endured. It is an added joy to Mina and to me that our boy’s birthday is the same day as that on which Quincey Morris died. His mother holds, I know, the secret belief that some of our brave friend’s spirit has passed into him. His bundle of names links all our little band of men together; but we call him Quincey.\n\nIn the summer of this year we made a journey to Transylvania, and went over the old ground which was, and is, to us so full of vivid and terrible memories. It was almost impossible to believe that the things which we had seen with our own eyes and heard with our own ears were living truths. Every trace of all that had been was blotted out. The castle stood as before, reared high above a waste of desolation.\n\nWhen we got home we were talking of the old time--which we could all look back on without despair, for Godalming and Seward are both happily married. I took the papers from the safe where they had been ever since our return so long ago. We were struck with the fact, that in all the mass of material of which the record is composed, there is hardly one authentic document; nothing but a mass of typewriting, except the later note-books of Mina and Seward and myself, and Van Helsing’s memorandum. We could hardly ask any one, even did we wish to, to accept these as proofs of so wild a story. Van Helsing summed it all up as he said, with our boy on his knee:--\n\n“We want no proofs; we ask none to believe us! This boy will some day know what a brave and gallant woman his mother is. Already he knows her sweetness and loving care; later on he will understand how some men so loved her, that they did dare much for her sake.”";
if(typeof BODIES_TRANS!=="undefined") BODIES_TRANS["gb-345"]="5월 3일. 비스트리차. — 5월 1일 오후 8시 35분에 뮌헨을 떠나 이튿날 새벽 빈에 도착. 6시 46분에 도착해야 했으나 기차가 한 시간 연착되었다. 부다페스트는 기차에서 흘끗 본 것과 거리를 잠깐 거닐어본 정도이지만, 참으로 놀라운 곳인 듯하다. 도착이 늦은 데다 가능한 한 정시에 가깝게 출발해야 했으므로 역에서 멀리 가기가 두려웠다. 내가 받은 인상은 우리가 서방을 떠나 동방으로 들어서고 있다는 것이었다. 이곳에서 당당한 폭과 깊이를 자랑하는 도나우 강에 놓인 화려한 다리들 가운데 가장 서쪽의 것을 건너자, 우리는 터키 지배의 전통 속으로 들어서게 되었다.\n\n우리는 꽤 정확한 시각에 출발하여, 날이 저문 뒤 클라우젠부르크에 닿았다. 나는 이곳 로열 호텔에서 하룻밤 묵었다. 저녁, 아니 야식으로 닭고기를 무언가 붉은 고추를 넣어 요리한 것을 먹었는데, 아주 맛이 좋았으나 목이 몹시 말랐다. (비망. 미나에게 줄 요리법을 알아둘 것.) 웨이터에게 물었더니 \"파프리카 헨들\"이라고 부른다며, 이것이 국민 요리이므로 카르파티아 산맥을 따라 어디서든 먹을 수 있을 거라고 했다. 이곳에서 내 어설픈 독일어가 무척 쓸모 있음을 알았다. 사실 그것이 없었다면 어떻게 헤쳐 나갔을지 모르겠다.\n\n런던에 있을 때 시간 여유가 좀 있어, 나는 대영박물관을 찾아가 도서관의 책과 지도들 가운데 트란실바니아에 관한 것을 뒤져보았다. 그 나라의 귀족을 상대하는 데에는 그 지역에 대한 사전 지식이 어느 정도 중요하지 않을 리 없다는 생각이 들었던 것이다. 그가 말한 지역은 그 나라의 극동쪽, 트란실바니아·몰다비아·부코비나 세 지방의 경계가 만나는 바로 그곳, 카르파티아 산맥 한가운데에 있었다. 유럽에서 가장 거칠고 가장 알려지지 않은 지역 가운데 하나였다. 드라큘라 성의 정확한 위치를 알려주는 지도나 책은 찾을 수 없었는데, 이 나라에는 아직 우리의 육지측량부 지도에 견줄 만한 지도가 없기 때문이었다. 그러나 드라큘라 백작이 말한 역참 도시 비스트리차는 꽤 잘 알려진 곳임을 알 수 있었다. 미나와 여행 이야기를 나눌 때 기억을 되살리는 데 도움이 될지 모르니, 여기에 내 메모 몇 가지를 적어둔다.\n\n트란실바니아의 주민 가운데에는 뚜렷이 구별되는 네 민족이 있다. 남부의 색슨인, 그리고 그들과 섞여 사는 왈라키아인은 다키아인의 후손이다. 서부의 마자르인, 동부와 북부의 세케이인이 있다. 나는 후자 쪽으로 가는 길인데, 그들은 자신들이 아틸라와 훈족의 후손이라고 주장한다. 그럴 법도 한 것이, 11세기에 마자르인이 이 나라를 정복했을 때 이미 훈족이 이곳에 정착해 있었기 때문이다. 세상에 알려진 온갖 미신이 카르파티아의 말발굽 모양 산맥 안에 모여 있다고, 마치 그곳이 어떤 상상의 소용돌이 중심이기라도 한 것처럼 적혀 있는 글을 읽었다. 그렇다면 내 체류는 매우 흥미로울지도 모르겠다. (비망. 백작에게 그 미신들에 관해 모두 물어볼 것.)\n\n잠자리가 충분히 편안했는데도 나는 잠을 설쳤다. 온갖 기이한 꿈에 시달렸기 때문이다. 밤새도록 창문 아래에서 개 한 마리가 짖어댔는데, 그것이 한몫했을 수도 있겠다. 아니면 파프리카 탓이었을지도 모른다. 물병의 물을 다 마셔버리고도 여전히 목이 말랐으니 말이다. 새벽녘에야 잠이 들었다가, 문을 끊임없이 두드리는 소리에 잠이 깼으니, 그때에는 꽤 깊이 잠들었던 모양이다. 아침식사로 또 파프리카와, 그들이 \"마말리가\"라고 부르는 옥수숫가루 죽 같은 것, 그리고 다진 고기를 채운 가지요리를 먹었는데, 이것은 무척 훌륭한 음식으로 \"임플레타타\"라고 부른다. (비망. 이 요리법도 알아둘 것.) 아침식사를 서둘러야 했으니, 기차가 8시 조금 전에 출발하기로 — 아니, 출발하기로 되어 있었기 때문이다. 7시 30분에 역으로 달려갔으나, 열차가 움직이기 시작하기 전까지 한 시간도 넘게 객차에 앉아 기다려야 했다. 동쪽으로 갈수록 기차는 더 시간을 지키지 않는 것 같다. 중국에서는 대체 어떨까?\n\n온종일 우리는 온갖 종류의 아름다움이 가득한 시골을 느릿느릿 지나는 듯했다. 때로는 옛 기도서에서 보는 것 같은 가파른 언덕 꼭대기의 작은 마을이나 성들이 보였고, 때로는 강과 시내를 따라 달렸는데, 양편의 넓은 자갈밭으로 보아 큰 홍수가 잦은 모양이었다. 강의 바깥쪽 가장자리까지 씻어내려면 거센 물이 많이 흘러야 한다. 정거장마다 사람들이 무리지어, 때로는 떼지어 모여 있었고, 차림새도 가지가지였다. 더러는 고향의 농부들이나 내가 프랑스와 독일을 거쳐 오며 본 사람들과 똑같이, 짧은 윗옷에 둥근 모자, 손수 지은 바지 차림이었다. 그러나 다른 이들은 매우 그림처럼 보였다. 여인들은 가까이 다가가기 전까지는 예뻐 보였으나, 허리께가 무척 둔해 보였다. 모두 어떤 식으로든 헐렁한 흰 소매가 달린 옷을 입었고, 대부분 큼직한 허리띠를 둘렀는데, 거기에서 발레 의상처럼 무언가의 가닥들이 잔뜩 나부꼈으나, 물론 그 안에는 속치마를 받쳐 입고 있었다. 우리가 본 가장 기이한 차림은 슬로바키아인들로, 다른 이들보다 더 야만스러워 보였다. 그들은 카우보이 같은 큰 모자에, 헐렁하고 더러운 흰빛 바지, 흰 아마포 셔츠, 그리고 거의 한 자나 되는 너비에 놋쇠 못이 빼곡히 박힌 엄청나게 무거운 가죽 허리띠를 두르고 있었다. 그들은 긴 장화를 신고 그 안에 바지를 쑤셔넣었으며, 머리칼은 길고 검었고 콧수염도 검고 무성했다. 매우 그림 같았으나 호감이 가는 모습은 아니었다. 무대 위라면 당장 동방의 옛 산적 떼쯤으로 여겨졌을 것이다. 그러나 듣자 하니 그들은 매우 무해하며, 오히려 천성적으로 자기를 내세울 줄 모르는 편이라고 한다.\n\n비스트리차에 닿은 것은 황혼이 어둑해질 무렵이었는데, 이곳은 매우 흥미로운 옛 도시다. 사실상 국경에 자리잡고 있어서 — 보르고 고개가 이곳에서 부코비나로 이어지기 때문이다 — 매우 파란만장한 역사를 겪었고, 분명 그 흔적이 남아 있다. 오십 년 전에 큰 화재가 연이어 일어나 다섯 차례나 끔찍한 참화를 빚었다. 17세기 초에는 삼 주에 걸친 포위 공격을 당해 1만 3천 명을 잃었는데, 정작 전투로 인한 사상자에 기근과 질병까지 더해진 것이었다.\n\n드라큘라 백작은 나에게 골든 크로네 호텔로 가라고 일러주었는데, 가서 보니 더없이 기뻐하게도 그곳은 철저히 옛스러운 곳이었다. 물론 나는 이 나라의 풍습을 가능한 한 모두 보고 싶었던 것이다. 내가 올 것을 분명히 기다리고 있었던 모양으로, 문 가까이 다가가자 흔한 농부 차림 — 흰 속옷에, 앞뒤로 긴 이중 앞치마를 두른 색색의 천이 정숙해 보이기에는 좀 지나치다 싶을 만큼 몸에 꼭 맞는 — 의 명랑해 보이는 늙수그레한 여인과 마주쳤다. 가까이 다가가자 그녀는 절을 하며 \"영국 신사분이신가요?\"라고 물었다. \"그렇소,\" 하고 내가 답했다. \"조너선 하커요.\" 그녀는 미소를 짓더니, 문까지 그녀를 따라온 흰 셔츠 차림의 늙수그레한 남자에게 무언가 말을 전했다. 그는 나갔다가 곧 편지 한 통을 들고 돌아왔다.\n\n\"나의 벗에게. — 카르파티아에 온 것을 환영하오. 나는 당신을 애타게 기다리고 있소. 오늘 밤은 푹 자도록 하시오. 내일 세 시에 부코비나행 역마차가 출발할 것이며, 당신의 자리는 잡아두었소. 보르고 고개에서 내 마차가 당신을 기다렸다가 나에게로 데려올 것이오. 런던에서의 여정이 즐거웠기를, 그리고 나의 아름다운 땅에서의 체류를 즐기게 되기를 바라오.\n당신의 벗,\n\n드라큘라.\"\n\n5월 4일. — 알고 보니 호텔 주인도 백작으로부터 편지를 받았는데, 나를 위해 역마차의 가장 좋은 자리를 잡아두라는 지시였다. 그러나 자세한 것을 물어보자 그는 어쩐지 말을 아끼며, 내 독일어를 알아듣지 못하는 척했다. 그럴 리가 없는 것이, 그때까지는 완벽히 알아들었기 때문이다. 적어도 내 물음에 정확히 답한 것으로 보아 알아듣는 듯했다. 그와 그의 아내, 곧 나를 맞아준 늙은 부인은 겁먹은 듯한 눈빛으로 서로를 마주보았다. 그는 돈은 편지에 동봉되어 왔으며, 자기가 아는 건 그뿐이라고 우물거렸다. 드라큘라 백작을 아느냐, 그의 성에 대해 무언가 말해줄 수 있느냐고 묻자, 그와 그의 아내는 둘 다 성호를 긋고는, 자기들은 아무것도 모른다고 하면서 더는 입을 떼려 하지 않았다. 출발 시각이 너무 가까워 다른 사람에게 물어볼 겨를도 없었으니, 이 모든 것이 참으로 알 수 없고 결코 마음 편한 일이 아니었다.\n\n내가 막 떠나려 할 때, 늙은 부인이 내 방으로 올라와 몹시 신경질적인 투로 말했다.\n\n\"꼭 가셔야 하나요? 아! 젊은 양반, 꼭 가셔야 해요?\" 그녀가 어찌나 흥분한 상태였던지, 알던 독일어마저 종잡지 못하고 내가 전혀 모르는 다른 언어와 온통 뒤섞어버렸다. 나는 여러 번 되물은 끝에야 겨우 그녀의 말을 따라잡을 수 있었다. 내가 당장 떠나야 하며 중요한 일로 가는 길이라고 말하자, 그녀는 다시 물었다.\n\n\"오늘이 무슨 날인지 아세요?\" 5월 4일이라고 내가 답했다. 그녀는 고개를 저으며 다시 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그럼요! 그건 저도 알아요! 그건 알지만, 오늘이 무슨 날인지 아시느냐고요?\" 무슨 말인지 모르겠다고 하자, 그녀는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"오늘은 성 게오르기우스 축일 전야예요. 오늘 밤 자정을 알리는 종이 울리면 세상의 온갖 악한 것들이 활개를 친다는 걸 모르세요? 어디로 가시는지, 무엇을 향해 가시는지 아시기나 하세요?\" 그녀가 어찌나 애가 타하는 게 분명한지, 나는 그녀를 달래보려 했으나 소용없었다. 마침내 그녀는 무릎을 꿇고, 가지 말라고, 적어도 하루 이틀이라도 출발을 미루라고 애원했다. 모두 우스꽝스러운 일이었지만 나는 마음이 편치 않았다. 그러나 처리해야 할 일이 있었고, 어떤 것도 그것을 방해하게 둘 수는 없었다. 그래서 나는 그녀를 일으키려 하면서, 되도록 진지하게 고맙다고, 그러나 내 임무는 시급하니 가야만 한다고 말했다. 그러자 그녀는 일어서서 눈물을 닦고는, 목에서 십자고상(十字苦像)을 풀어 나에게 내밀었다. 나는 어찌해야 할지 몰랐으니, 영국 국교회 신자로서 그런 것을 어느 정도 우상숭배적인 물건으로 여기도록 배웠던 까닭이다. 그렇다고 이토록 호의를 품고 또 이런 마음 상태에 있는 늙은 부인의 청을 거절하기란 너무도 매정한 일로 여겨졌다. 짐작건대 그녀는 내 얼굴에 어린 망설임을 보았던 모양으로, 묵주를 내 목에 걸어주며 \"당신 어머니를 봐서라도\"라고 말하고는 방을 나갔다. 나는 지금 역마차를 기다리며 일기의 이 대목을 적고 있는데, 마차는 물론 늦고 있고, 십자고상은 여전히 내 목에 걸려 있다. 그 늙은 부인의 두려움 탓인지, 이 고장의 숱한 유령 전설 탓인지, 아니면 십자고상 그 자체 탓인지는 모르겠으나, 나는 평소만큼 마음이 편하지가 않다. 만일 이 책이 나보다 먼저 미나에게 닿거든, 부디 내 작별 인사를 전해주기를. 마차가 온다!\n\n5월 5일. 성(城). — 아침의 잿빛은 가시고, 해가 먼 지평선 위로 높이 떴는데, 그 지평선이 들쭉날쭉한 것이 나무 탓인지 언덕 탓인지는 알 수 없다. 너무 멀어서 큰 것과 작은 것이 한데 뒤섞여 보이기 때문이다. 졸리지도 않고, 내가 깰 때까지 깨우지 말라고 했으니, 자연히 잠이 올 때까지 글을 쓴다. 적어둘 이상한 일들이 많은데, 이 글을 읽는 이가 내가 비스트리차를 떠나기 전에 식사를 너무 잘한 탓이라 여길까 봐, 내 저녁식사를 정확히 적어둔다. 나는 그들이 \"강도 스테이크\"라 부르는 것을 먹었는데 — 베이컨과 양파, 쇠고기 조각에 붉은 고추로 양념하여 꼬챙이에 꿰어 불에 구운 것으로, 런던의 고양이 먹이만큼이나 단순한 방식이었다! 포도주는 골든 메디아슈였는데, 혀에 묘하게 톡 쏘는 맛을 남겼지만 불쾌하지는 않았다. 나는 이것을 두어 잔밖에 마시지 않았고, 다른 것은 들지 않았다.\n\n내가 마차에 올랐을 때 마부는 아직 자리에 앉지 않은 채 여주인과 이야기를 나누고 있었다. 분명 내 이야기를 하고 있었으니, 이따금 나를 쳐다보았기 때문이다. 그리고 문밖의 긴 의자에 — 그들은 그것을 \"말 전하는 사람\"이라는 뜻의 이름으로 불렀다 — 앉아 있던 몇몇 사람들이 다가와 귀를 기울이더니, 나를 바라보았는데, 대부분 가엾다는 표정이었다. 자주 되풀이되는 말이 여럿 들렸는데, 군중 가운데 여러 민족이 섞여 있었으므로 기이한 말들이었다. 그래서 나는 가방에서 여러 나라 말 사전을 슬그머니 꺼내어 찾아보았다. 솔직히 말하건대 그 말들은 나에게 위안이 되지 못했으니, 그 가운데에는 \"오르도그\" — 사탄, \"포콜\" — 지옥, \"스트레고이카\" — 마녀, \"브롤로크\"와 \"블코슬라크\" — 둘 다 같은 뜻으로, 하나는 슬로바키아어, 다른 하나는 세르비아어로 늑대인간 혹은 흡혈귀를 뜻하는 말이 있었기 때문이다. (비망. 이 미신들에 관해 백작에게 물어볼 것.)\n\n우리가 출발할 때, 그 무렵에는 상당히 불어나 있던 여관 문 앞의 군중이 모두 성호를 긋고 나를 향해 두 손가락을 내밀었다. 나는 어렵사리 동행 한 사람을 붙들어 그것이 무슨 뜻인지 물었다. 그는 처음에는 답하려 하지 않았으나, 내가 영국인임을 알자 그것이 흉안(凶眼)을 막는 부적이나 방패라고 설명해주었다. 알지 못하는 곳으로, 알지 못하는 사람을 만나러 막 떠나는 나에게 그리 유쾌한 일은 아니었다. 그러나 다들 어찌나 마음씨가 곱고, 어찌나 애처로워하고, 어찌나 동정 어린 표정이었던지 나는 감동하지 않을 수 없었다. 여관 마당과 그 안에 그림처럼 모여 선 사람들의 무리가, 안마당 한가운데 녹색 화분에 무리지어 심긴 협죽도와 오렌지 나무의 무성한 잎을 배경으로, 넓은 아치문 둘레에 서서 모두 성호를 긋던 그 마지막 광경을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다. 그때 우리 마부가 — 그의 넓은 아마포 바지가 마부석 앞면을 온통 덮고 있었는데, 그들은 그것을 \"고차\"라 부른다 — 나란히 달리는 네 마리 작은 말 위로 큰 채찍을 휘둘러 소리를 내자, 우리는 여정에 올랐다.\n\n마차가 나아가는 동안 풍경의 아름다움 속에서 나는 곧 유령 같은 두려움을 보지도, 기억하지도 못하게 되었다. 하기야 동행들이 쓰는 언어를, 아니 언어들을 알아들었더라면 그토록 쉽게 떨쳐내지는 못했을지도 모른다. 우리 앞에는 숲과 삼림이 가득한 푸르게 비탈진 땅이 펼쳐졌고, 군데군데 가파른 언덕이 나무 무더기나 농가로 꼭대기를 이루고 있었는데, 농가의 휑한 박공 끝이 길을 향하고 있었다. 어디에나 사과, 자두, 배, 벚나무 등 과일꽃이 어지러울 만큼 무더기로 피어 있었고, 지나가면서 보니 나무 아래 푸른 잔디가 떨어진 꽃잎으로 점점이 수놓여 있었다. 이곳에서 \"미텔 란트\"라 부르는 이 푸른 언덕들 사이로 길이 들고 나며, 잔디 덮인 굽이를 돌아 사라지기도 하고, 군데군데 불길의 혀처럼 산비탈을 타고 내려온 소나무 숲의 들쭉날쭉한 끝자락에 가려지기도 했다. 길은 험했으나, 그래도 우리는 열에 들뜬 듯 서둘러 그 위를 날듯이 달리는 것 같았다. 그 서두름이 무엇을 뜻하는지 그때는 알 수 없었으나, 마부는 분명 보르고 프룬트에 닿기까지 한시도 지체하지 않으려는 작정이었다. 듣자 하니 이 길은 여름철에는 더없이 좋으나, 겨울 눈이 내린 뒤로 아직 정비되지 않았다고 했다. 이 점에서 이 길은 카르파티아의 보통 길들과는 다른데, 길을 너무 좋게 관리해서는 안 된다는 오랜 전통이 있기 때문이다. 옛날에 호스파다르들은 길을 보수하려 하지 않았으니, 터키인들이 그들이 외국 군대를 끌어들일 채비를 한다고 여겨, 언제나 정작 일촉즉발의 상태에 있던 전쟁을 재촉하지나 않을까 해서였다.\n\n미텔 란트의 푸르게 부풀어오른 언덕들 너머로는 카르파티아 산맥 자체의 드높은 가파른 봉우리까지 거대한 숲의 비탈이 솟아 있었다. 그것들은 우리 좌우로 우뚝 솟아, 오후의 햇살을 가득 받으며 이 아름다운 산맥의 온갖 영광스러운 빛깔을 드러냈다. 봉우리들의 그늘진 곳은 짙은 청색과 보라색, 풀과 바위가 뒤섞인 곳은 초록과 갈색이었고, 들쭉날쭉한 바위와 뾰족한 절벽이 끝없이 이어지다가, 마침내 그것들 자체도 멀리 사라지는 그곳에서는 눈 덮인 봉우리들이 장엄하게 솟아 있었다. 군데군데 산에 거대한 틈이 벌어진 듯한 곳이 보였고, 해가 기울기 시작하면서 그 틈으로 이따금 떨어지는 물줄기의 흰 빛이 반짝이는 것이 보였다. 우리가 어느 언덕 기슭을 돌아 나오자, 우리가 구불구불한 길을 따라 나아갈 때 바로 앞에 있는 듯이 보이는, 눈 덮인 드높은 산봉우리가 모습을 드러냈는데, 그때 동행 하나가 내 팔을 건드렸다.\n\n\"보시오! 이슈텐 세크!\" — \"신의 옥좌!\" — 그러고 나서 그는 경건하게 성호를 그었다.\n\n우리가 끝없는 길을 따라 구불구불 나아가고, 해가 우리 뒤로 점점 더 낮게 가라앉으면서, 저녁의 그림자가 우리 주위로 기어들기 시작했다. 눈 덮인 산꼭대기는 여전히 석양을 머금어 은은하고 서늘한 분홍빛으로 빛나는 듯했으므로, 그 어둠은 더욱 도드라졌다. 군데군데 우리는 체히인과 슬로바키아인을 지나쳤는데, 모두 그림 같은 차림이었으나, 갑상선종(甲狀腺腫)이 가슴 아플 만큼 흔한 것이 눈에 띄었다. 길가에는 십자가가 많이 서 있었고, 우리가 지나칠 때마다 동행들은 모두 성호를 그었다. 군데군데 한 농부 남자나 여자가 사당 앞에 무릎 꿇고 있었는데, 우리가 다가가도 돌아보지조차 않았고, 신앙에 자신을 온통 내맡긴 나머지 바깥세상에 대해서는 눈도 귀도 없는 듯했다. 나에게 새로운 것이 많았으니, 이를테면 나무 위에 얹어둔 건초더미라든가, 군데군데 매우 아름답게 무리진 능수자작나무는 그 흰 줄기가 여린 초록 잎새 사이로 은빛처럼 반짝였다. 이따금 우리는 라이터바겐 — 흔한 농부의 수레 — 을 지나쳤는데, 그 길고 뱀 같은 척추 모양의 차체는 길의 울퉁불퉁함에 맞도록 고안된 것이었다. 그 위에는 으레 집으로 돌아가는 농부들이 한 무리 올라타 있었는데, 체히인은 흰 양가죽을, 슬로바키아인은 색색의 양가죽을 둘렀고, 후자는 끝에 도끼가 달린 긴 막대기를 창처럼 들고 있었다. 저녁이 내리면서 몹시 추워지기 시작했고, 짙어가는 황혼은 떡갈나무, 너도밤나무, 소나무의 음울함을 하나의 어두운 안개 속으로 녹여내는 듯했다. 다만 우리가 고개를 따라 오르는 동안, 언덕 줄기 사이로 깊게 뻗은 골짜기에서는 검은 전나무들이 늦게까지 남은 눈을 배경으로 군데군데 우뚝 솟아 있었다. 이따금 길이 소나무 숲을 가로질러 뚫려 있었는데, 어둠 속에서 그 숲이 우리를 향해 내려덮이는 듯했고, 군데군데 나무를 뒤덮은 거대한 잿빛 덩어리가 묘하게 으스스하고 엄숙한 느낌을 자아내어, 그날 저녁 일찍 — 기우는 석양이 카르파티아 골짜기 사이로 끊임없이 휘감겨 도는 듯한 유령 같은 구름을 기이하게 두드러지게 했을 때 — 떠올랐던 생각과 음산한 공상을 이어가게 했다. 때로는 언덕이 어찌나 가파른지, 마부가 서두르는데도 말들은 천천히 갈 수밖에 없었다. 나는 고향에서 하듯 내려서 걸어 올라가고 싶었으나, 마부는 들으려고도 하지 않았다. \"안 됩니다, 안 돼요,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"여기서 걸으시면 안 됩니다. 개들이 너무 사납거든요.\" 그러고는 분명 음산한 농담이랍시고 — 다른 이들의 동의하는 미소를 구하려는 듯 둘러보았으니 — 덧붙였다. \"그리고 잠자리에 들기 전에 그런 일이라면 진저리나게 겪게 될지도 모르지요.\" 그가 멈춘 것은 등불을 켜기 위해 잠시 쉰 것뿐이었다.\n\n날이 어두워지자 승객들 사이에 어떤 동요가 이는 듯했고, 그들은 더 속력을 내라고 재촉하기라도 하듯 차례차례 마부에게 말을 걸었다. 그는 긴 채찍으로 말들을 무자비하게 후려치며, 거친 격려의 외침으로 말들을 몰아 더욱 분발하게 했다. 그러자 어둠 너머로 우리 앞쪽에 잿빛 빛이 비치는 듯한 자리가 보였는데, 마치 언덕에 갈라진 틈이 있는 것 같았다. 승객들의 동요는 더 커졌고, 낡은 마차는 커다란 가죽 용수철 위에서 흔들리며, 거친 바다에 내던져진 배처럼 출렁였다. 나는 붙들고 있어야 했다. 길이 더 평탄해지더니, 우리는 날듯이 달리는 것 같았다. 그러더니 산들이 양편에서 우리에게 더 가까이 다가와 우리를 내려다보며 노려보는 듯했다. 우리는 보르고 고개로 들어서고 있었다. 승객들이 하나둘 나에게 선물을 내밀었는데, 그것들을 거절을 용납하지 않을 만큼 간곡한 태도로 안겨주었다. 그것들은 분명 기이하고 가지각색이었으나, 하나하나가 소박한 선의로, 다정한 말과 축복과 함께, 그리고 비스트리차의 호텔 밖에서 보았던 그 두려움을 뜻하는 몸짓들의 기이한 뒤섞임 — 성호를 긋는 것과 흉안을 막는 손짓 — 과 함께 건네진 것이었다. 그러더니 우리가 날듯이 달리는 동안, 마부는 몸을 앞으로 기울였고, 양편의 승객들은 마차 가장자리 너머로 목을 빼고 어둠 속을 열심히 들여다보았다. 무언가 매우 흥분되는 일이 벌어지고 있거나 곧 일어나리라는 것이 분명했으나, 승객마다 물어보아도 누구 하나 나에게 조금도 설명해주려 하지 않았다. 이런 흥분 상태는 한동안 이어졌고, 마침내 우리 앞에 고개가 동쪽으로 트이는 것이 보였다. 머리 위에는 검은 구름이 굽이치며 떠 있었고, 공기 중에는 천둥을 머금은 무겁고 답답한 기운이 감돌았다. 마치 산맥이 두 개의 대기를 갈라놓았고, 이제 우리가 천둥이 이는 쪽으로 들어선 듯했다. 이제 나는 나를 백작에게 데려다줄 마차를 직접 살펴보고 있었다. 매 순간 어둠 너머로 등불의 불빛이 보이기를 기대했으나, 사방이 어두웠다. 유일한 빛은 우리 마차 등불의 깜박이는 광선뿐으로, 그 빛 속에서 거세게 몰린 말들의 김이 흰 구름처럼 피어올랐다. 이제 우리 앞에 모랫길이 희끄무레하게 뻗어 있는 것이 보였으나, 그 위에 마차의 흔적은 없었다. 승객들은 안도의 한숨을 내쉬며 몸을 뒤로 젖혔는데, 그 모습이 마치 나 자신의 실망을 조롱하는 것 같았다. 내가 이제 어찌하는 게 최선일지 궁리하고 있을 때, 마부가 시계를 보더니 다른 이들에게 무어라 말했는데, 어찌나 조용하고 낮은 소리로 말했던지 거의 알아들을 수 없었다. \"예정보다 한 시간 이르군\"이라고 한 것 같았다. 그러더니 나를 돌아보며 내 것보다 더 서툰 독일어로 말했다.\n\n\"여기엔 마차가 없습니다. 결국 나리는 기다려지지 않는 모양입니다. 이제 부코비나까지 갔다가, 내일이나 모레 돌아오시는 게 좋겠어요. 모레가 더 낫겠지요.\" 그가 말하는 동안 말들이 울부짖고 콧김을 내뿜으며 거칠게 날뛰기 시작해서 마부가 붙들어야 했다. 그때, 농부들의 비명 소리가 한바탕 일고 모두가 성호를 긋는 가운데, 네 마리 말이 끄는 칼레슈 한 대가 우리 뒤에서 다가와 우리를 따라잡더니 마차 옆에 멈춰 섰다. 우리 등불의 빛이 말들 위로 비치며 번뜩였는데, 그 빛에 그것들이 칠흑같이 검은, 훌륭한 짐승들임을 알 수 있었다. 마차를 모는 이는 키 큰 남자로, 긴 갈색 수염에 큼직한 검은 모자를 썼는데, 그 모자가 우리에게서 그의 얼굴을 가리는 듯했다. 그가 우리 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸을 때, 등불 빛에 붉게 보이는 매우 빛나는 한 쌍의 눈이 번뜩이는 것만 겨우 보였다. 그가 마부에게 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤은 일찍 왔군, 친구.\" 마부가 더듬거리며 대답했다.\n\n\"영국 나리께서 급하셨거든요.\" 그러자 낯선 이가 대답했다.\n\n\"그래서 자네가 이분을 부코비나까지 모셔가게 하고 싶었던 거겠지. 나를 속일 수는 없네, 친구. 나는 너무 많이 알고, 내 말들은 빠르니까.\" 그가 말하면서 미소 짓자, 등불 빛이 단단해 보이는 입매를 비추었는데, 입술은 무척 붉고 날카로워 보이는 이는 상아처럼 희었다. 내 동행 가운데 하나가 다른 이에게 뷔르거의 「레노레」의 한 구절을 속삭였다.\n\n\"덴 디 토텐 라이텐 슈넬\" — (\"죽은 자는 빨리 달리는 법이니.\")\n\n그 낯선 마부는 분명 그 말을 들었으니, 번뜩이는 미소를 지으며 고개를 들었기 때문이다. 그 승객은 얼굴을 돌리는 동시에 두 손가락을 내밀고 성호를 그었다. \"나리의 짐을 이리 주게,\" 하고 마부가 말했다. 그러자 무척 잽싸게 내 가방들이 건네져 칼레슈에 실렸다. 칼레슈가 바로 옆에 붙어 있었으므로, 나는 마차 옆에서 내려섰는데, 마부가 강철 같은 힘으로 내 팔을 움켜쥐며 손을 내밀어 거들어주었다. 그의 힘은 어마어마한 것임에 틀림없었다. 그는 말없이 고삐를 흔들었고, 말들이 방향을 틀자 우리는 고개의 어둠 속으로 휩쓸려 들어갔다. 뒤돌아보니 등불 빛에 마차 말들의 김이 보였고, 그것을 배경으로 좀 전의 동행들이 성호를 긋는 모습이 비쳤다. 그러더니 마부가 채찍을 휘둘러 소리를 내며 말들을 부르자, 그들은 부코비나를 향한 길을 휩쓸 듯 떠나갔다. 그들이 어둠 속으로 가라앉아 사라지자 나는 기묘한 한기를 느꼈고, 외로운 기분이 엄습했다. 그러나 망토가 내 어깨에 걸쳐지고, 무릎에 깔개가 덮였으며, 마부가 훌륭한 독일어로 말했다.\n\n\"밤이 쌀쌀합니다, 나리. 제 주인이신 백작님께서 나리를 극진히 보살피라 분부하셨습니다. 좌석 아래에 슬리보비츠(이 나라의 자두 브랜디) 한 병이 있으니, 필요하시면 드십시오.\" 나는 마시지 않았으나, 그것이 거기 있다는 것을 아는 것만으로도 위안이 되었다. 나는 약간 기묘한 기분이었고, 적잖이 겁이 났다. 만일 다른 방도가 있었더라면, 그 알 수 없는 밤 여정을 강행하느니 차라리 그쪽을 택했을 것이다. 마차는 곧장 빠른 속도로 나아갔고, 그러다 우리는 완전히 한 바퀴 돌아 또 다른 곧은 길을 따라 나아갔다. 내 생각에는 우리가 그저 같은 곳을 몇 번이고 돌고 있는 것 같았다. 그래서 나는 두드러진 지점 하나를 기억해두었고, 과연 그러함을 알았다. 마부에게 이게 다 무슨 영문인지 묻고 싶었으나, 정말이지 그러기가 두려웠으니, 내 처지에서는 만일 지체시키려는 의도가 있다면 어떤 항의도 소용없으리라 생각했기 때문이다. 그러나 이윽고 나는 시간이 어떻게 흐르고 있는지 궁금하여 성냥을 켰고, 그 불빛으로 시계를 보았다. 자정이 몇 분 남지 않았다. 이것이 나에게 일종의 충격을 주었으니, 짐작건대 자정에 관한 흔한 미신이 최근의 경험으로 더 부풀려졌던 모양이다. 나는 메스꺼운 긴장 속에서 기다렸다.\n\n그때 길 저 아래 어느 농가에서 개 한 마리가 울부짖기 시작했다 — 마치 두려움에 사로잡힌 듯, 길고 고통스러운 통곡이었다. 그 소리를 다른 개가 받았고, 또 다른 개가, 또 다른 개가 받더니, 이제 고개 사이로 부드럽게 한숨짓듯 부는 바람을 타고, 거친 울부짖음이 일기 시작했다. 그것은 밤의 어둠을 통해 상상이 미칠 수 있는 데까지, 온 고장에서 들려오는 듯했다. 첫 울부짖음에 말들이 안달하며 앞발을 들고 일어서기 시작했으나, 마부가 달래듯 말을 건네자 진정되었다. 그래도 갑작스러운 공포에 내달리다 멈춘 듯 부르르 떨며 땀을 흘렸다. 그러더니 멀리, 우리 양편의 산에서 더 크고 더 날카로운 울부짖음이 — 늑대들의 울부짖음이 — 일기 시작했는데, 그것은 말들과 나에게 똑같은 영향을 끼쳤다. 나는 칼레슈에서 뛰어내려 달아나고 싶었고, 말들은 다시 앞발을 들고 일어서며 미친 듯이 날뛰어, 마부가 그 엄청난 힘을 다해야 말들이 내빼는 것을 막을 수 있었다. 그러나 몇 분 지나자 내 귀도 그 소리에 익숙해졌고, 말들도 어느 정도 진정되어 마부가 내려서서 그 앞에 설 수 있게 되었다. 그는 말들을 어르고 달래며 그 귀에 무언가 속삭였는데, 말 길들이는 이들이 그리한다고 들은 적이 있었거니와, 그 효과는 놀라웠다. 그의 어루만짐을 받자 말들은 여전히 떨고는 있었으나 다시 꽤 다루기 쉬워졌기 때문이다. 마부는 다시 자리에 앉아 고삐를 흔들며 빠른 속도로 출발했다. 이번에는 고개의 먼 쪽까지 가더니, 갑자기 오른쪽으로 가파르게 꺾이는 좁은 길로 접어들었다.\n\n이내 우리는 나무들에 둘러싸였는데, 그 나무들이 군데군데 길 위로 아치를 이루어, 우리는 마치 터널을 지나듯 나아갔다. 그리고 다시 노려보는 듯한 거대한 바위들이 양편에서 우리를 굳건히 호위했다. 우리는 가려진 곳에 있었으나, 이는 바람이 점점 거세지는 소리를 들을 수 있었으니, 바람은 바위 사이로 신음하고 휘파람 소리를 냈고, 우리가 휩쓸 듯 지나가는 동안 나뭇가지들이 서로 부딪쳐 우지끈거렸다. 점점 더 추워지더니, 곱고 가루 같은 눈이 내리기 시작해, 이내 우리와 우리 주위의 모든 것이 흰 담요에 덮였다. 매서운 바람은 여전히 개들의 울부짖음을 실어 날랐으나, 우리가 길을 나아갈수록 그 소리는 점점 희미해졌다. 늑대들의 울부짖음은 마치 사방에서 우리를 에워싸며 좁혀 오기라도 하듯 점점 더 가까이 들려왔다. 나는 끔찍하게 겁이 났고, 말들도 내 두려움을 함께 느꼈다. 그러나 마부는 조금도 동요하지 않았다. 그는 줄곧 고개를 좌우로 돌렸으나, 나는 어둠 속에서 아무것도 볼 수 없었다.\n\n갑자기 우리 왼편 저쪽에서, 희미하게 깜박이는 푸른 불꽃이 보였다. 마부도 동시에 그것을 보았다. 그는 즉시 말들을 멈추더니, 땅으로 뛰어내려 어둠 속으로 사라졌다. 늑대들의 울부짖음이 점점 가까워지는 터라 더더욱 나는 어찌해야 할지 몰랐다. 그러나 내가 어리둥절해하는 동안 마부가 갑자기 다시 나타나, 말없이 자리에 앉았고, 우리는 여정을 재개했다. 내 생각에는 잠이 들어 그 일을 계속 꿈꾼 모양인데, 그 일이 끝없이 되풀이되는 것 같았다. 이제 돌이켜보면 그것은 일종의 끔찍한 악몽 같다. 한번은 그 불꽃이 길 가까이 나타나, 우리 주위의 어둠 속에서도 나는 마부의 동작을 지켜볼 수 있었다. 그는 푸른 불꽃이 이는 곳으로 잽싸게 가더니 — 그 불꽃은 매우 희미했던 것이 틀림없으니, 주위를 조금도 비추지 않는 듯했다 — 돌 몇 개를 모아 어떤 형상을 만들었다. 한번은 기이한 착시가 일어났는데, 그가 나와 불꽃 사이에 섰는데도 불꽃을 가리지 못했으니, 나는 그 유령 같은 깜박임을 변함없이 볼 수 있었기 때문이다. 이것이 나를 놀라게 했으나, 그 현상이 한순간뿐이었으므로, 나는 어둠을 뚫어보느라 애쓴 내 눈이 나를 속인 것이려니 여겼다. 그러고는 한동안 푸른 불꽃이 보이지 않았고, 우리는 어둠을 뚫고 앞으로 내달렸는데, 늑대들의 울부짖음이 마치 움직이는 원을 이루어 우리를 뒤따르기라도 하듯 우리 주위를 맴돌았다.\n\n마침내 마부가 그때까지 갔던 것보다 더 멀리 나아간 때가 왔는데, 그가 없는 동안 말들이 그 어느 때보다 심하게 떨기 시작하고, 콧김을 내뿜으며 공포에 질려 비명을 질러댔다. 나는 그 까닭을 알 수 없었으니, 늑대들의 울부짖음이 완전히 멎었기 때문이다. 그러나 바로 그때, 검은 구름 사이를 헤치며 떠가던 달이 소나무로 덮인 험준한 바위의 들쭉날쭉한 마루 뒤에서 모습을 드러냈고, 그 빛에 나는 우리 주위로 늑대 떼가 둥글게 에워싸고 있는 것을 보았다. 흰 이빨에 붉은 혀를 늘어뜨리고, 길고 힘줄이 불거진 다리에 털이 텁수룩했다. 그것들은 울부짖을 때보다도, 그것들을 사로잡은 그 음산한 침묵 속에서 백 배는 더 무시무시했다. 나로 말하자면 일종의 공포에 의한 마비를 느꼈다. 사람이 그런 공포와 정면으로 마주할 때에야 비로소 그 참된 의미를 이해할 수 있는 법이다.\n\n별안간 늑대들이, 마치 달빛이 그것들에게 어떤 기묘한 영향을 끼치기라도 한 듯, 울부짖기 시작했다. 말들은 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰며 앞발을 들고 일어섰고, 보기에 고통스러울 만큼 눈알을 굴리며 어쩔 줄 모르고 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러나 살아 있는 공포의 고리가 사방에서 그것들을 에워쌌으니, 그것들은 어쩔 수 없이 그 안에 머물러 있을 수밖에 없었다. 나는 마부에게 오라고 외쳤으니, 우리의 유일한 가망은 그 고리를 뚫고 나가 그가 다가오는 것을 도우려는 것뿐인 듯했기 때문이다. 나는 소리를 지르고 칼레슈 옆구리를 두드렸는데, 그 소음으로 그쪽의 늑대들을 겁주어 쫓아내, 그가 마차에 닿을 틈을 주려는 것이었다. 그가 어떻게 거기 왔는지는 모르겠으나, 나는 위엄 있는 명령조로 높아진 그의 목소리를 들었고, 소리 나는 쪽을 바라보니 그가 길 한가운데 서 있는 것이 보였다. 그가 마치 무슨 만질 수 없는 장애물을 옆으로 밀어내듯 긴 팔을 휘젓자, 늑대들이 뒤로, 더 뒤로 물러섰다. 바로 그때 짙은 구름 한 점이 달의 얼굴을 가로질러, 우리는 다시 어둠 속에 잠겼다.\n\n내가 다시 볼 수 있게 되었을 때, 마부는 칼레슈에 기어오르고 있었고, 늑대들은 사라지고 없었다. 이 모든 것이 어찌나 기이하고 으스스했던지, 끔찍한 공포가 나를 덮쳐, 나는 말하기도 움직이기도 두려웠다. 우리가 휩쓸 듯 길을 나아가는 동안 시간이 끝없이 흐르는 것 같았는데, 이제는 굽이치는 구름이 달을 가려 거의 완전한 어둠 속이었다. 우리는 이따금 빠르게 내려가는 때도 있었으나, 대체로 줄곧 오르막을 올랐다. 그러다 문득 나는 마부가 거대한 폐허가 된 성의 안마당에서 말들을 멈춰 세우는 중임을 깨달았다. 그 성의 높다란 검은 창들에서는 한 줄기 빛도 새어나오지 않았고, 부서진 흉벽은 달빛 어린 하늘을 배경으로 들쭉날쭉한 윤곽을 드러내고 있었다.\n\n「조너선 하커의 일기」\n(이어서)\n\n5월 5일. — 나는 분명 잠이 들었던 모양이다. 만일 완전히 깨어 있었더라면 이토록 예사롭지 않은 곳에 다가가는 것을 알아채지 못했을 리 없으니 말이다. 어스름 속에서 안마당은 상당히 넓어 보였고, 거기서 커다란 둥근 아치 아래로 어두운 통로가 여럿 뻗어 있어, 아마 실제보다 더 커 보였을 것이다. 아직 대낮에 그곳을 볼 기회는 없었다.\n마차가 멈추자 마부가 뛰어내려 내가 내리는 것을 돕고자 손을 내밀었다. 다시금 나는 그의 엄청난 힘을 알아채지 않을 수 없었다. 그의 손은 마음만 먹으면 내 손을 으스러뜨릴 수 있는 강철 죔쇠 같았다. 그러고 나서 그는 내 짐을 꺼내 내 곁의 땅바닥에 놓아주었는데, 그때 나는 크고 오래된, 커다란 쇠못이 빼곡히 박힌, 돌출된 육중한 석조 문틀에 앉은 거대한 문 가까이에 서 있었다. 희미한 빛 속에서도 그 돌이 육중하게 조각되어 있으나, 그 조각이 세월과 풍상으로 몹시 닳았음을 알 수 있었다. 내가 서 있는 동안 마부는 다시 자리로 뛰어올라 고삐를 흔들었다. 말들이 앞으로 나아가더니, 마차와 짐 모두 어두운 통로 가운데 하나로 사라졌다.\n\n나는 어찌해야 할지 몰라 그 자리에 말없이 서 있었다. 종이나 문고리 같은 것은 흔적도 없었다. 이 찌푸린 듯한 벽과 어두운 창구멍을 뚫고 내 목소리가 닿을 성싶지도 않았다. 기다리는 시간이 끝없이 느껴졌고, 의심과 두려움이 밀려드는 것을 느꼈다. 나는 대체 어떤 곳에, 어떤 부류의 사람들 사이에 온 것인가? 내가 발을 들인 것은 대체 어떤 무시무시한 모험인가? 런던의 부동산 매입을 외국인에게 설명하러 파견된 사무 변호사 서기의 삶에서 이런 일이 흔히 있는 일이던가? 사무 변호사 서기라니! 미나가 그것을 좋아하지 않을 것이다. 변호사라 해야지 — 런던을 떠나기 직전에 내 시험이 합격했다는 소식을 받았으니, 이제 나는 어엿한 변호사다! 나는 깨어 있는지 확인하려고 눈을 비비고 살을 꼬집기 시작했다. 모든 것이 끔찍한 악몽 같았고, 과로한 다음 날 아침이면 이따금 느끼곤 했듯이, 문득 깨어나 새벽빛이 창으로 비집고 드는 우리 집에 있는 나 자신을 발견하리라고 기대했다. 그러나 내 살은 꼬집는 시험에 답했고, 내 눈은 속을 것이 아니었다. 나는 정말로 깨어 있었고, 카르파티아 한가운데에 있었다. 이제 내가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 참고서 아침이 오기를 기다리는 것뿐이었다.\n\n내가 막 이 결론에 이르렀을 때, 거대한 문 뒤로 묵직한 발소리가 다가오는 것이 들렸고, 틈새로 다가오는 불빛이 어른거리는 것이 보였다. 그러고 나서 쇠사슬이 절그럭거리는 소리와 육중한 빗장이 뒤로 당겨지며 철컹거리는 소리가 났다. 오래 쓰지 않아 요란하게 삐걱대는 소리를 내며 열쇠가 돌아갔고, 거대한 문이 뒤로 열렸다.\n\n안에는 키 큰 노인이 서 있었는데, 긴 흰 콧수염만 빼고는 말끔히 면도를 했고, 머리끝에서 발끝까지 검은 옷을 입었으며, 어디에도 다른 빛깔이라곤 한 점도 없었다. 그는 손에 골동품 같은 은제 등잔을 들고 있었는데, 그 불꽃은 등피나 둥근 갓 따위 없이 타오르며, 열린 문으로 들이치는 바람에 일렁일 때마다 길게 떨리는 그림자를 던졌다. 노인은 오른손으로 정중한 몸짓을 지으며 나를 안으로 청했는데, 훌륭한 영어였으나 기이한 억양으로 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"내 집에 온 것을 환영하오! 자유로이, 그대 자신의 뜻으로 들어오시오!\" 그는 나를 맞으러 한 발짝도 내딛지 않고, 마치 환영의 몸짓이 그를 돌로 굳혀버리기라도 한 듯 조각상처럼 서 있었다. 그러나 내가 문지방을 넘어선 순간, 그는 충동적으로 앞으로 나서더니, 손을 내밀어 내 손을 움켜쥐었는데 그 힘이 어찌나 셌던지 나는 움찔했다. 그 손이 얼음장처럼 차가운 — 산 사람의 손이라기보다 죽은 사람의 손에 가까운 — 느낌이었던 탓에 그 효과는 줄어들지 않았다. 그가 다시 말했다.\n\n\"내 집에 온 것을 환영하오. 자유로이 들어오시오. 무사히 떠나시오. 그리고 그대가 가져온 행복의 얼마간을 남기고 가시오!\" 그 악수의 힘이 얼굴은 보지 못했던 그 마부에게서 느꼈던 것과 어찌나 비슷했던지, 나는 한순간 내가 말을 건네는 이가 바로 그 사람이 아닌가 의심했다. 그래서 확인하려고, 나는 묻는 투로 말했다.\n\n\"드라큘라 백작이신지요?\" 그는 정중하게 절을 하며 대답했다.\n\n\"나는 드라큘라요. 하커 씨, 내 집에 온 것을 환영하오. 들어오시오. 밤공기가 차고, 그대는 분명 먹고 쉬어야 할 것이오.\" 그는 말하면서 등잔을 벽의 받침대에 올려놓고, 밖으로 나서더니 내 짐을 들었다. 내가 미처 앞질러 막기도 전에 그는 짐을 안으로 들여놓았다. 내가 만류했으나 그는 고집했다.\n\n\"아니오, 선생. 그대는 내 손님이오. 시간도 늦었고, 내 사람들도 지금은 부릴 수가 없소. 그대의 편안함은 내가 직접 살피게 해주시오.\" 그는 통로를 따라, 그러고는 거대한 나선형 계단을 올라, 또 다른 큰 통로를 따라 내 짐을 나르겠다고 고집했는데, 그 돌바닥 위로 우리 발소리가 묵직하게 울렸다. 그 끝에서 그는 육중한 문 하나를 활짝 열었고, 나는 그 안에 환히 밝혀진 방을 보고 기뻤다. 그 방에는 저녁식사를 차린 식탁이 펼쳐져 있었고, 거대한 벽난로에서는 갓 더 지핀 통나무 장작더미가 활활 타오르며 너울거렸다.\n\n백작은 멈추어 서서 내 가방을 내려놓고 문을 닫은 뒤, 방을 가로질러 또 다른 문을 열었는데, 그 문은 등잔 하나로만 밝혀진, 어떤 종류의 창문도 없어 보이는 작은 팔각형 방으로 이어졌다. 이곳을 지나 그는 또 다른 문을 열고는 나에게 들어가라고 손짓했다. 반가운 광경이었다. 그곳에는 또 다른 통나무 불로 환히 밝혀지고 따뜻해진 큰 침실이 있었던 것이다 — 이 불 역시 얼마 전에 더 지핀 것으로, 맨 위 통나무가 새것이었으니 — 그 불이 넓은 굴뚝 위로 공허한 울음소리를 내며 타올랐다. 백작은 직접 내 짐을 안에 들여놓고 물러났는데, 문을 닫기 전에 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"여행 끝이니, 그대는 몸단장을 하여 기운을 차려야 할 것이오. 원하는 것이 모두 갖추어져 있기를 바라오. 채비가 되거든 저쪽 방으로 오시오. 그곳에 그대의 저녁식사가 차려져 있을 것이오.\"\n\n빛과 온기, 그리고 백작의 정중한 환대가 내 모든 의심과 두려움을 흩어버린 듯했다. 그렇게 정상적인 상태로 돌아오자, 나는 내가 허기로 반쯤 굶주려 있음을 알아챘다. 그래서 서둘러 몸단장을 마치고 저쪽 방으로 갔다.\n\n저녁식사는 이미 차려져 있었다. 거대한 벽난로 한쪽에 서서 석조 부분에 기대어 있던 주인은 우아하게 손을 저어 식탁을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"청하건대, 앉아서 마음껏 드시오. 내가 함께하지 못하는 것을 부디 양해해주시오. 나는 이미 식사를 했고, 야식은 들지 않으니 말이오.\"\n\n나는 호킨스 씨가 나에게 맡긴 봉인된 편지를 그에게 건넸다. 그는 그것을 뜯어 엄숙히 읽었다. 그러고는 매혹적인 미소를 지으며 나에게 읽어보라고 건네주었다. 적어도 그 가운데 한 대목은 나에게 기쁨의 전율을 주었다.\n\n\"통풍 발작 때문에 — 나는 이 병으로 늘 고생하고 있소 — 당분간은 어떤 여행도 절대로 할 수 없게 된 것을 유감으로 여기지 않을 수 없소. 그러나 충분한 대리인을 보낼 수 있게 되어 기쁘오. 내가 온갖 신뢰를 다 두고 있는 사람이오. 그는 젊은이로, 나름대로 활기와 재능이 넘치며 매우 충직한 성품을 지녔소. 그는 신중하고 입이 무거우며, 내 밑에서 일하며 어른이 되었소. 그가 머무는 동안 그대가 원하실 때 언제든 그대를 모실 채비가 되어 있을 것이며, 모든 일에서 그대의 지시를 받을 것이오.\"\n\n백작이 직접 앞으로 나와 접시 덮개를 벗겨주었고, 나는 곧장 훌륭한 통닭구이에 손을 댔다. 이것에 치즈와 샐러드 약간, 그리고 오래된 토카이 포도주 한 병 — 나는 그것을 두 잔 마셨다 — 이 내 저녁식사였다. 내가 그것을 먹는 동안 백작은 내 여행에 관해 여러 가지를 물었고, 나는 차츰차츰 내가 겪은 모든 것을 그에게 이야기했다.\n\n이즈음 나는 저녁식사를 마쳤고, 주인이 권하는 대로 벽난로 옆으로 의자를 끌어다 놓고 그가 권한 시가를 피우기 시작했는데, 그러면서 그는 자기는 담배를 피우지 않는다며 양해를 구했다. 이제 나는 그를 관찰할 기회를 얻었고, 그의 인상이 매우 두드러진다는 것을 알았다.\n\n그의 얼굴은 억센 — 매우 억센 — 매부리 형이었고, 가느다란 코는 콧대가 높고 콧구멍이 유난히 활처럼 휘어 있었다. 이마는 우뚝 솟아 둥글었고, 머리칼은 관자놀이 둘레에는 듬성듬성하나 다른 곳에는 무성하게 자랐다. 눈썹은 매우 짙어 코 위에서 거의 맞닿을 듯했고, 무성한 털이 그 자체의 풍성함으로 곱슬거리는 듯했다. 입은, 두꺼운 콧수염 아래로 보이는 한에서는, 굳게 다물려 다소 잔인해 보였고, 유난히 날카로운 흰 이가 있었다. 그 이들은 입술 위로 튀어나와 있었는데, 그 입술의 놀라운 붉은빛은 그 나이의 사람치고는 놀라운 생기를 드러냈다. 그 밖에 그의 귀는 창백했고, 끝이 몹시 뾰족했으며, 턱은 넓고 억셌고, 뺨은 여위었으나 단단했다. 전체적인 인상은 유난한 창백함이었다.\n\n지금까지 나는 그의 손이 불빛 속에서 무릎 위에 놓여 있을 때 그 손등을 보았는데, 다소 희고 고와 보였다. 그러나 이제 그것을 가까이서 보니, 그것이 다소 거칠다는 것을 — 넓적하고 손가락이 뭉툭하다는 것을 — 알아채지 않을 수 없었다. 이상하게도 손바닥 한가운데에 털이 나 있었다. 손톱은 길고 가늘었으며, 날카로운 끝으로 깎여 있었다. 백작이 나에게 몸을 기울여 그의 손이 나를 스쳤을 때, 나는 몸서리를 억누를 수 없었다. 그의 입김이 역겨웠던 탓일 수도 있겠으나, 끔찍한 구역질이 밀려와, 아무리 애를 써도 감출 수가 없었다. 백작은 그것을 알아챈 것이 분명한지 뒤로 물러났고, 지금껏 보였던 것보다 더 그 튀어나온 이를 드러내는 음산한 미소를 지으며 다시 벽난로의 제 쪽에 앉았다. 우리는 한동안 둘 다 말이 없었다. 그리고 내가 창문 쪽을 바라보니 다가오는 새벽의 첫 희미한 빛줄기가 보였다. 모든 것 위에 기이한 정적이 깃든 듯했다. 그러나 귀를 기울이자, 마치 저 아래 골짜기에서 들려오는 듯한 늑대 여러 마리의 울부짖음이 들렸다. 백작의 두 눈이 번득였고, 그가 말했다.\n\n\"저들의 소리를 들어보시오 — 밤의 자식들이오. 얼마나 멋진 음악을 만들어내는지!\" 짐작건대 그에게 낯선 어떤 표정을 내 얼굴에서 보았는지, 그가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"아, 선생, 도시에 사는 그대들은 사냥꾼의 심정을 헤아릴 수 없소.\" 그러고는 일어서서 말했다.\n\n\"그러나 그대는 분명 피곤할 것이오. 그대의 침실은 모두 채비가 되어 있으니, 내일은 원하는 만큼 늦게까지 자도 좋소. 나는 오후까지 자리를 비워야 하오. 그러니 푹 자고, 좋은 꿈을 꾸시오!\" 정중하게 절을 하며 그는 직접 나를 위해 팔각형 방으로 통하는 문을 열어주었고, 나는 내 침실로 들어갔다…….\n\n나는 온통 경이의 바다 속에 있다. 나는 의심한다. 나는 두려워한다. 나는 내 영혼에마저 감히 고백하지 못할 기이한 것들을 생각한다. 신이여, 나를 지켜주소서, 적어도 나에게 소중한 이들을 위해서라도!\n\n5월 7일. — 다시 이른 아침이지만, 나는 지난 스물네 시간을 쉬며 즐겁게 보냈다. 나는 낮 늦게까지 자고 저절로 깨어났다. 옷을 차려입고 우리가 저녁식사를 했던 방으로 가니, 차가운 아침식사가 차려져 있었고, 커피는 주전자를 벽난로에 올려놓아 따뜻하게 유지되고 있었다. 식탁 위에는 카드 한 장이 놓여 있었는데, 거기에 이렇게 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"나는 잠시 자리를 비워야 하오. 나를 기다리지 마시오. — D.\" 나는 시작하여 든든한 식사를 즐겼다. 식사를 마치고 나는 종을 찾아보았다. 하인들에게 내가 식사를 끝냈음을 알리기 위해서였다. 그러나 종을 찾을 수 없었다. 내 주위에 둘러싼 부의 놀라운 증거를 생각하면, 이 집에는 확실히 기이한 결핍이 있다. 식탁 식기는 금으로 되어 있는데, 어찌나 아름답게 세공되었는지 분명 엄청난 값어치가 있을 것이다. 의자와 소파의 커튼과 천, 그리고 내 침대의 휘장은 가장 값비싸고 가장 아름다운 직물로 되어 있어, 만들어졌을 당시에는 엄청난 값어치가 있었을 것이 분명한데, 수백 년이나 되었는데도 더없이 좋은 상태였다. 나는 햄프턴 코트에서 그와 비슷한 것을 본 적이 있으나, 그곳의 것은 닳고 해지고 좀먹어 있었다. 그러나 어느 방에도 여전히 거울은 없다. 내 탁자에는 화장 거울조차 없어, 면도를 하거나 머리를 빗으려면 가방에서 작은 면도 거울을 꺼내야 했다. 나는 아직 어디에서도 하인을 본 적이 없고, 성 근처에서 늑대 울음소리 말고는 어떤 소리도 듣지 못했다. 식사를 마치고 얼마쯤 지난 뒤 — 그것을 아침식사라 불러야 할지 저녁식사라 불러야 할지 모르겠다. 그것을 먹은 것이 다섯 시에서 여섯 시 사이였으니 — 나는 읽을거리를 찾아보았다. 백작의 허락을 구하기 전에는 성안을 돌아다니고 싶지 않았기 때문이다. 방안에는 책이든 신문이든, 심지어 필기도구든 정말 아무것도 없었다. 그래서 방의 또 다른 문을 열어보니 서재 같은 곳이 있었다. 내 방 맞은편 문을 열어보려 했으나 잠겨 있었다.\n\n서재에서 나는 더없이 기쁘게도 수많은 영어 책들을 발견했다. 책장 가득히, 잡지와 신문의 제본된 권들도 있었다. 한가운데 탁자에는 영어 잡지와 신문이 어지러이 널려 있었는데, 최근 날짜의 것은 하나도 없었다. 책들은 더없이 다양한 종류였다 — 역사, 지리, 정치, 정치경제, 식물학, 지질학, 법률 — 모두 영국과 영국의 생활, 풍습, 예법에 관한 것이었다. 심지어 런던 인명록, \"적서(赤書)\"와 \"청서(靑書)\", 위터커 연감, 육군 및 해군 명부, 그리고 — 어쩐지 그것을 보니 마음이 흐뭇했는데 — 법조 명부 같은 참고 서적들까지 있었다.\n\n내가 책들을 바라보고 있는데 문이 열리고 백작이 들어왔다. 그는 정겹게 나에게 인사하고는, 내가 밤에 잘 쉬었기를 바란다고 했다. 그러고는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그대가 이곳으로 길을 찾아든 것이 기쁘오. 그대의 흥미를 끌 만한 것이 분명 많을 테니 말이오. 이 동무들이\" — 그러면서 그는 몇몇 책 위에 손을 얹었다 — \"나에게 좋은 벗이 되어주었고, 지난 몇 해 동안, 런던으로 갈 생각을 품은 이래로, 나에게 수많은, 수많은 즐거운 시간을 안겨주었소. 이것들을 통해 나는 그대의 위대한 영국을 알게 되었소. 그리고 영국을 안다는 것은 영국을 사랑하는 것이오. 나는 그대의 웅장한 런던의 붐비는 거리를 거닐고, 인류의 소용돌이와 분주함 한가운데에 있으며, 그 삶과 그 변화와 그 죽음, 그리고 그것을 그것이게 하는 그 모든 것을 함께하기를 갈망하오. 그러나 아아! 아직 나는 책을 통해서만 그대의 언어를 알 뿐이오. 나의 벗이여, 그것을 말로 할 줄 알게 되는 것을 그대에게 기대하오.\"\n\n\"하지만, 백작님,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"당신은 영어를 완벽하게 알고 또 말하시지 않습니까!\" 그는 엄숙히 절을 했다.\n\n\"나의 벗이여, 너무도 과분한 평가를 해주니 고맙소. 그러나 아직도 나는 내가 가고자 하는 길을 조금밖에 가지 못했음을 두려워하오. 사실 나는 문법과 단어는 알지만, 아직 그것을 어떻게 말해야 하는지는 모른다오.\"\n\n\"정말이지,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"당신은 훌륭하게 말씀하십니다.\"\n\n\"그렇지 않소,\" 하고 그가 대답했다. \"글쎄, 나는 알고 있소. 내가 그대의 런던에서 거동하고 말한다면, 그곳에서 나를 이방인으로 알아보지 못할 자가 없으리라는 것을. 나에게는 그것으로 충분치 않소. 이곳에서 나는 귀족이오. 나는 보야르요. 평민들은 나를 알고, 나는 주인이오. 그러나 낯선 땅의 이방인은 아무도 아니오. 사람들은 그를 알지 못하고 — 알지 못한다는 것은 마음을 두지 않는다는 것이오. 나는 다른 이들과 같기만 하다면 만족하오. 그래서 나를 보아도 멈춰 서는 자가 없고, 내 말을 들어도 말을 멈추고 '하, 하! 이방인이로군!' 하지 않는다면 말이오. 나는 너무도 오래 주인이었기에 여전히 주인이고 싶소 — 적어도 다른 누구도 나의 주인이 되지는 않게 하고 싶소. 그대는 단지 엑서터의 내 벗 피터 호킨스의 대리인으로서, 런던의 내 새 부동산에 관해 모두 일러주러 나에게 온 것만은 아니오. 부디 그대가 여기 나와 함께 얼마간 머물러주기를 바라오. 그리하여 우리의 대화로 내가 영어의 억양을 익힐 수 있도록 말이오. 그리고 내가 말하다가 아주 사소한 것이라도 잘못을 저지르거든 그대가 일러주기를 바라오. 오늘 내가 그토록 오래 자리를 비워야 했던 것은 미안하오. 그러나 그대는, 분명히, 그토록 많은 중대한 일을 떠맡고 있는 자를 용서해줄 것이오.\"\n\n물론 나는 기꺼이 그러겠다고 할 수 있는 말을 다 했고, 내가 원할 때 그 방으로 와도 되는지 물었다. 그는 \"그렇소, 물론이오\"라고 대답하고는 덧붙였다.\n\n\"그대는 문이 잠긴 곳을 빼고는 성안 어디든 원하는 곳으로 가도 좋소. 잠긴 곳에는 물론 그대도 가고 싶지 않을 테지만 말이오. 모든 것이 지금 이대로 있는 데에는 까닭이 있소. 그대가 내 눈으로 보고 내 지식으로 안다면, 아마 더 잘 이해하게 될 것이오.\" 나는 분명 그러리라고 말했고, 그러자 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"우리는 트란실바니아에 있소. 그리고 트란실바니아는 영국이 아니오. 우리의 방식은 그대의 방식이 아니며, 그대에게는 기이한 것들이 많을 것이오. 아니, 그대가 이미 겪은 일들을 나에게 이야기해준 것으로 보아, 그대는 어떤 기이한 것들이 있을 수 있는지 얼마간 알고 있구려.\"\n\n이 말에서 많은 대화가 이어졌다. 그리고 그가 말 자체를 위해서라도 이야기하고 싶어한다는 것이 분명했으므로, 나는 이미 나에게 일어났거나 내 눈에 들어왔던 일들에 관해 그에게 여러 가지를 물었다. 때로 그는 화제를 비껴가거나, 못 알아듣는 척하며 대화를 돌리기도 했다. 그러나 대체로 그는 내가 묻는 모든 것에 더없이 솔직하게 대답했다. 그러다 시간이 지나 내가 다소 대담해지자, 나는 전날 밤의 기이한 일들 가운데 몇 가지를 그에게 물었다. 이를테면, 어째서 마부가 푸른 불꽃이 보이던 곳으로 갔는가 하는 것이었다. 그러자 그는, 한 해의 어느 특정한 밤 — 사실 어젯밤으로, 온갖 악령이 거침없이 위세를 떨친다고 여겨지는 밤 — 에는 보물이 숨겨진 곳 위에 푸른 불꽃이 보인다고 흔히들 믿는다고 나에게 설명해주었다. \"그 보물이 숨겨져 있다는 데에는,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"그대가 어젯밤 지나온 그 지역에 말이오, 의심의 여지가 거의 없소. 그곳은 수백 년 동안 왈라키아인, 색슨인, 터키인이 다투어 싸운 땅이었기 때문이오. 아니, 이 온 지역에서 사람의 피로, 애국자든 침략자든 그 피로 비옥해지지 않은 땅은 한 뼘도 없을 지경이오. 옛날에는 격동의 시절이 있었소. 오스트리아인과 헝가리인이 떼지어 몰려오면, 애국자들은 그들을 맞으러 나갔소 — 남자도 여자도, 노인도 아이도 — 그리고 고개 위쪽 바위에서 그들이 오기를 기다렸다가, 인공으로 일으킨 눈사태로 그들에게 파멸을 쓸어 내릴 수 있도록 말이오. 침략자가 승리했을 때에도 그가 얻은 것은 거의 없었으니, 무엇이 있었든 정다운 땅속에 감추어져 있었기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 어떻게,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"사람들이 수고를 마다하지 않고 찾아보기만 한다면 확실한 표지가 있는데, 어찌 그토록 오래 발견되지 않은 채로 남아 있을 수 있단 말입니까?\" 백작은 미소를 지었고, 그의 입술이 잇몸 위로 뒤로 당겨지자 길고 날카로운 송곳니가 기이하게 드러났다. 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"그대의 농부는 마음속으로는 겁쟁이에 바보이기 때문이오! 그 불꽃들은 단 하룻밤에만 나타나오. 그리고 그 밤에는 이 땅의 어떤 사람도, 어쩔 수 있다면, 제 집 문밖으로 나서지 않을 것이오. 그리고, 선생, 설령 나선다 해도 그는 무엇을 해야 할지 모를 것이오. 아니, 그대가 나에게 말한, 불꽃이 있던 자리를 표시해 두었다는 그 농부조차도 대낮에는 제가 한 일이 어디인지조차 찾지 못할 것이오. 그대조차도, 내 감히 맹세하건대, 그 장소들을 다시 찾을 수 없을 것이오, 안 그렇소?\"\n\n\"그 점은 옳습니다,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"저는 그것들을 어디서 찾아야 할지조차 죽은 자만큼이나 알지 못합니다.\" 그러고 나서 우리는 다른 문제들로 흘러갔다.\n\n\"자,\" 하고 마침내 그가 말했다. \"런던에 대해, 그리고 그대가 나를 위해 구해준 그 집에 대해 이야기해주시오.\" 나는 내 태만을 사과하고, 가방에서 서류를 꺼내러 내 방으로 갔다. 서류를 정리하고 있는데 옆방에서 도자기와 은그릇이 달그락거리는 소리가 들렸고, 지나가면서 보니 식탁은 치워지고 등잔이 밝혀져 있었다. 이즈음에는 깊은 어둠이 깔려 있었기 때문이다. 서재, 곧 도서실에도 등잔이 밝혀져 있었고, 나는 백작이 소파에 누워, 세상에서 하필이면, 영국의 브래드쇼 안내서를 읽고 있는 것을 발견했다. 내가 들어가자 그는 탁자에서 책과 서류를 치웠다. 그리고 나는 그와 함께 온갖 종류의 도면과 증서와 수치를 살폈다. 그는 모든 것에 관심을 보였고, 그 장소와 그 주변에 관해 수많은 질문을 나에게 던졌다. 그는 분명 그 인근에 관해 손에 넣을 수 있는 모든 것을 미리 연구해 두었던 것이, 결국 나보다 훨씬 더 많이 알고 있는 것이 분명했기 때문이다. 내가 이 점을 말하자 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"글쎄, 하지만, 나의 벗이여, 내가 그래야 하는 것이 마땅하지 않소? 내가 그곳에 가면 나는 완전히 혼자일 것이고, 내 벗 하커 조너선은 — 아니, 미안하오, 그대의 성을 앞에 두는 우리 나라의 습관에 빠졌구려 — 내 벗 조너선 하커는 나를 바로잡고 도와주려고 내 곁에 있지 않을 테니 말이오. 그는 몇 마일이나 떨어진 엑서터에 있을 것이고, 아마 내 또 다른 벗 피터 호킨스와 함께 법률 서류를 다루고 있겠지. 그렇소!\"\n\n우리는 퍼플리트의 부동산 매입 건을 철저히 살폈다. 내가 그에게 사실들을 다 일러주고, 필요한 서류에 그의 서명을 받고, 그것들과 함께 호킨스 씨에게 부칠 편지까지 써두자, 그는 어떻게 그토록 알맞은 곳을 찾아냈는지 나에게 묻기 시작했다. 나는 그 당시에 적어 두었던 메모를 그에게 읽어주었는데, 여기에 그것을 적어둔다.\n\n\"퍼플리트에서, 샛길을 따라가다가, 나는 꼭 요구되는 듯한 그런 곳을 우연히 발견했는데, 거기에는 그곳이 매물이라는 낡아빠진 안내문이 내걸려 있었다. 그곳은 묵직한 돌로 쌓은 고색창연한 높은 담으로 둘러싸여 있고, 오랜 세월 동안 보수된 적이 없었다. 닫힌 대문은 묵직한 오래된 참나무와 쇠로 되어 있는데, 모두 녹으로 삭아 있었다.\n\n이 부동산은 카팩스라 불리는데, 의심할 바 없이 옛 콰트르 파스가 와전된 것이다. 그 집이 사방으로 되어 있어 나침반의 기본 방위와 들어맞기 때문이다. 그곳은 모두 합쳐 약 이십 에이커쯤 되며, 앞서 말한 단단한 돌담으로 빙 둘러싸여 있다. 그곳에는 나무가 많아 군데군데 음침하고, 깊고 어두워 보이는 못, 곧 작은 호수가 있는데, 물이 맑고 제법 큰 시내를 이루며 흘러 나가는 것으로 보아 분명 어떤 샘물에서 물을 받는 듯하다. 집은 매우 크고, 내가 보기에 중세 시대까지 거슬러 올라가는 온갖 시대의 것이 섞여 있다. 한 부분은 엄청나게 두꺼운 돌로 되어 있고, 높은 곳에 창문이 몇 개밖에 없으며 쇠로 육중하게 빗장이 질러져 있기 때문이다. 그것은 성채의 일부처럼 보이고, 오래된 예배당, 곧 교회 가까이에 있다. 집에서 거기로 통하는 문의 열쇠가 없어 나는 그 안으로 들어갈 수 없었으나, 코닥으로 여러 지점에서 그곳의 모습을 찍어 두었다. 집은 증축되었으나 매우 들쭉날쭉한 방식으로 되어 있어, 그것이 차지하는 땅의 넓이는 짐작만 할 뿐인데, 분명 매우 클 것이다. 가까이에 집은 몇 채 없는데, 한 채는 최근에야 증축되어 사립 정신병원으로 꾸며진 매우 큰 집이다. 그러나 그것은 이 부지에서는 보이지 않는다.\"\n\n내가 다 읽고 나자 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그곳이 오래되고 크다니 기쁘오. 나 자신이 오래된 가문 출신이라, 새 집에서 사는 것은 나를 죽일 것이오. 집은 하루 만에 살 만하게 만들어질 수 없소. 그리고 결국, 한 세기를 이루는 데에 며칠이나 필요하겠소. 또 옛 시대의 예배당이 있다니 그것도 기쁘오. 우리 트란실바니아 귀족은 우리 뼈가 천한 죽은 자들 사이에 묻힐지도 모른다는 생각을 좋아하지 않소. 나는 환락도 흥겨움도, 젊고 명랑한 이들을 기쁘게 하는 풍부한 햇빛과 반짝이는 물의 밝은 관능도 구하지 않소. 나는 더 이상 젊지 않소. 그리고 내 마음은, 죽은 자들을 애도하는 지친 세월을 거치며, 흥겨움에 맞추어져 있지 않소. 게다가 내 성의 벽은 허물어져 있소. 그림자가 많고, 부서진 흉벽과 창틀 사이로 바람이 차갑게 숨쉬오. 나는 그늘과 그림자를 사랑하며, 할 수 있을 때면 내 생각과 더불어 혼자이고 싶소.\" 어쩐지 그의 말과 그의 표정은 들어맞지 않는 듯했다. 아니면 그의 얼굴 생김새가 그의 미소를 악의에 차고 음울해 보이게 한 것이었으리라.\n\n이윽고 그는 양해를 구하고 나를 떠나며, 내 서류를 모두 한데 모아 두라고 부탁했다. 그는 잠시 자리를 비웠고, 나는 내 주위의 책 몇 권을 살펴보기 시작했다. 한 권은 지도책이었는데, 자연스럽게 영국 면에서 펼쳐지는 것이 마치 그 지도가 많이 사용된 것 같았다. 그것을 보니 어떤 곳들에 작은 동그라미가 표시되어 있었고, 그것들을 살펴보니 하나는 런던의 동쪽 가까이로, 분명 그의 새 부동산이 자리한 곳이었다. 다른 두 개는 엑서터와, 요크셔 해안의 휘트비였다.\n\n백작이 돌아온 것은 한 시간이 다 되어갈 무렵이었다. \"아하!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"아직도 책을 보고 있구려? 좋소! 그러나 언제나 일만 해서는 안 되오. 자, 그대의 저녁식사가 준비되었다고 알려 왔소.\" 그는 내 팔을 잡았고, 우리는 옆방으로 갔는데, 거기에는 훌륭한 저녁식사가 식탁에 준비되어 있었다. 백작은 집을 비웠던 동안 밖에서 식사를 했다며 다시 양해를 구했다. 그러나 그는 전날 밤처럼 앉아, 내가 먹는 동안 이야기를 나누었다. 식사 후 나는 지난 저녁처럼 담배를 피웠고, 백작은 나와 함께 머물며 생각할 수 있는 온갖 주제에 관해 이야기하고 질문하기를 한 시간이고 한 시간이고 이어갔다. 나는 정말로 매우 늦어지고 있음을 느꼈으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 모든 면에서 주인의 바람을 맞추어야 할 의무를 느꼈기 때문이다. 어제 오래 잔 덕에 기운이 났던 터라 졸리지는 않았다. 그러나 새벽이 다가올 때 사람에게 엄습하는 그 한기를 느끼지 않을 수 없었는데, 그것은 나름대로 조수가 바뀌는 때와 비슷하다. 죽음에 가까운 사람들은 대체로 새벽으로 바뀔 때나 조수가 바뀔 때 죽는다고들 한다. 지쳐 있으면서도 말하자면 제 자리에 매여, 대기의 이 변화를 겪어본 사람이라면 누구든 그것을 충분히 믿을 수 있을 것이다. 갑자기 우리는 맑은 아침 공기를 뚫고 초자연적인 날카로움으로 솟아오르는 수탉 울음소리를 들었다. 드라큘라 백작은 벌떡 일어서며 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 또 아침이 왔구려! 그대를 이토록 오래 깨어 있게 하다니, 내가 어찌 이리 무심한지. 그대는 나의 사랑스러운 새 나라 영국에 관한 그대의 이야기를 좀 덜 흥미롭게 해주어야겠소. 그래야 시간이 우리 곁을 흘러가는 것을 내가 잊지 않을 테니 말이오.\" 그러고는 정중하게 절을 하며 그는 서둘러 나를 떠났다.\n\n나는 내 방으로 들어가 커튼을 쳤으나, 눈여겨볼 것은 거의 없었다. 내 창문은 안마당으로 나 있어, 보이는 것이라곤 깨어나는 하늘의 따뜻한 잿빛뿐이었다. 그래서 나는 다시 커튼을 치고, 이 하루에 관해 적었다.\n\n5월 8일. — 이 책에 글을 쓰면서 나는 내가 너무 장황해지는 것이 아닌가 두려워지기 시작했다. 그러나 이제 나는 처음부터 세세하게 적은 것이 기쁘다. 이곳과 이곳의 모든 것에는 무언가 너무도 기이한 데가 있어 나는 불안을 느끼지 않을 수 없기 때문이다. 나는 이곳에서 무사히 벗어나 있기를, 아니면 아예 오지 않았기를 바란다. 어쩌면 이 기이한 밤의 삶이 나에게 영향을 미치고 있는지도 모른다. 그러나 그것만이라면 좋으련만! 이야기 나눌 누군가가 있다면 나는 견딜 수 있겠으나, 아무도 없다. 나에게는 말을 건넬 상대라곤 백작뿐인데, 그런데 그가! — 두렵건대 이곳에서 살아 있는 영혼이라곤 나 자신뿐인 듯하다. 사실에 관한 한 나는 무미건조하게 굴자. 그것이 내가 버텨내는 데 도움이 될 것이고, 상상이 나를 제멋대로 날뛰게 두어서는 안 된다. 만일 그렇게 되면 나는 끝장이다. 내가 어떤 처지에 있는지 — 혹은 있는 듯한지 — 곧장 말해 두자.\n\n나는 잠자리에 들었을 때 몇 시간밖에 자지 못했고, 더는 잘 수 없겠다 싶어 일어났다. 나는 면도 거울을 창가에 걸어두었고, 막 면도를 시작하려던 참이었다. 갑자기 어깨에 손이 닿는 것이 느껴졌고, 백작이 나에게 \"안녕히 주무셨소\" 하고 말하는 목소리가 들렸다. 나는 깜짝 놀랐다. 거울에 비친 상이 내 뒤의 방 전체를 담고 있었는데도 그를 보지 못했다는 것이 놀라웠기 때문이다. 놀라는 바람에 나는 살짝 베었으나, 그 순간에는 알아채지 못했다. 백작의 인사에 답하고 나서, 나는 내가 어떻게 잘못 보았는지 알아보려고 다시 거울을 향했다. 이번에는 착오일 리가 없었다. 그 사람이 내 가까이에 있어 어깨 너머로 그를 볼 수 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 거울에는 그의 모습이 비치지 않았다! 내 뒤의 방 전체가 비쳤으나, 그 안에는 나 자신 말고는 사람의 흔적이 없었다. 이것은 깜짝 놀랄 일이었고, 그토록 많은 기이한 일들에 더해진 터라, 백작이 가까이 있을 때면 늘 느끼는 그 막연한 불안감을 키우기 시작했다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 나는 베인 자리에서 피가 조금 났고, 피가 턱을 타고 흐르는 것을 보았다. 나는 면도칼을 내려놓고, 그러면서 반쯤 돌아서서 반창고를 찾았다. 백작은 내 얼굴을 보자 두 눈에 일종의 악마적인 분노가 타올랐고, 갑자기 내 목을 움켜쥐려 달려들었다. 내가 몸을 빼자 그의 손이 십자고상을 매단 묵주 끈에 닿았다. 그것은 그를 순식간에 바꾸어 놓았으니, 그 분노가 어찌나 빨리 가셨던지 나는 그것이 정말로 거기 있었던 것인지 도무지 믿을 수 없을 정도였다.\n\n\"조심하시오,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"베이지 않도록 조심하시오. 이 나라에서는 그대가 생각하는 것보다 더 위험하오.\" 그러고는 면도 거울을 움켜쥐고 말을 이었다. \"그리고 이것이 그 못된 짓을 한 형편없는 물건이오. 인간의 허영심이 빚은 더러운 노리개지. 치워버려야 하오!\" 그러고는 그 끔찍한 손으로 한 번 비틀어 육중한 창문을 열고는 거울을 밖으로 던져버렸고, 거울은 저 아래 안마당의 돌바닥에 떨어져 산산조각이 났다. 그러고 나서 그는 한마디 말도 없이 물러갔다. 이것은 매우 성가신 일인데, 회중시계 뚜껑이나, 다행히 금속으로 된 면도용 그릇 바닥이 아니고서는 어떻게 면도를 해야 할지 모르겠기 때문이다.\n\n내가 식당으로 갔을 때 아침식사는 준비되어 있었다. 그러나 어디에서도 백작을 찾을 수 없었다. 그래서 나는 혼자 아침을 먹었다. 아직 백작이 먹거나 마시는 것을 보지 못한 것이 이상하다. 그는 분명 매우 별난 사람임에 틀림없다! 아침식사 후 나는 성안을 조금 탐험했다. 나는 계단으로 나가 남쪽을 향한 방 하나를 발견했다. 경치는 장관이었고, 내가 선 곳에서 그것을 볼 기회가 충분했다. 성은 끔찍한 절벽의 바로 가장자리에 있다. 창문에서 떨어진 돌은 아무것에도 닿지 않고 천 피트를 떨어질 것이다! 눈이 닿는 데까지 초록빛 나무 우듬지의 바다였고, 군데군데 협곡이 있는 깊은 틈이 있었다. 여기저기에 은빛 실 같은 것들이 보였는데, 강이 숲을 가로질러 깊은 골짜기를 굽이쳐 흐르는 것이었다.\n\n그러나 나는 아름다움을 묘사할 마음이 들지 않으니, 그 경치를 본 뒤 나는 더 탐험했기 때문이다. 문, 문, 어디에나 문이었고, 모두 잠기고 빗장이 질러져 있었다. 성벽의 창문 말고는 어디에도 나갈 수 있는 출구가 없었다.\n\n성은 그야말로 감옥이고, 나는 죄수다!\n\n「조너선 하커의 일기」(계속)\n내가 갇힌 몸임을 깨닫자 일종의 광적인 감정이 나를 사로잡았다. 나는 계단을 오르내리며 닥치는 대로 문이란 문은 모두 열어보고, 눈에 띄는 창이란 창은 모두 내다보았다. 그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 내가 무력하다는 확신이 다른 모든 감정을 압도했다. 몇 시간이 지난 뒤 돌이켜보면, 그때 나는 잠시 제정신이 아니었던 모양이다. 마치 덫에 걸린 쥐처럼 행동했으니 말이다. 그러나 내가 무력하다는 확신이 들자, 나는 조용히 — 내 평생 그토록 조용히 무언가를 한 적이 없을 만큼 조용히 — 자리에 앉아, 어떻게 하는 것이 최선일지 곰곰이 생각하기 시작했다. 나는 지금도 생각하고 있으나, 아직 뚜렷한 결론에는 이르지 못했다. 다만 한 가지만은 확실하다. 곧, 내 생각을 백작에게 알려보아야 아무 소용이 없다는 것이다. 그는 내가 갇혀 있음을 잘 알고 있다. 게다가 그 일을 그 자신이 저질렀고, 거기에는 의심할 바 없이 그 나름의 동기가 있을 터이니, 내가 이 사실을 그에게 모두 털어놓고 믿는다면 그는 나를 속이기만 할 것이다. 내가 보기에 내 유일한 방책은 내가 아는 것과 두려워하는 것을 나 혼자만 간직하고, 눈을 부릅뜨고 있는 것뿐이다. 나는 알고 있다. 나는 갓난아기처럼 내 자신의 두려움에 속고 있거나, 아니면 절체절명의 궁지에 빠져 있거나 둘 중 하나라는 것을. 그리고 만일 후자라면, 나는 이 고비를 헤쳐 나가기 위해 내 모든 지혜가 필요하며, 앞으로도 필요할 것이다.\n\n이런 결론에 막 다다랐을 때, 나는 아래쪽의 큰 문이 닫히는 소리를 듣고 백작이 돌아왔음을 알았다. 그는 곧바로 서재로 오지 않았으므로, 나는 조심스레 내 방으로 갔는데, 가서 보니 그가 침대를 정돈하고 있었다. 이것은 이상한 일이었으나, 내가 줄곧 생각해온 바 — 곧 이 집에는 하인이 없다는 사실 — 을 확인해줄 뿐이었다. 그 뒤에 그가 식당에서 식탁을 차리고 있는 것을 문 경첩 틈으로 보았을 때, 나는 그것을 확신하게 되었다. 그가 이런 잡일을 모두 손수 한다면, 그것을 대신 해줄 다른 사람이 없다는 증거임이 분명하기 때문이다. 이것은 나를 두렵게 했으니, 만일 이 성에 다른 사람이 없다면, 나를 이곳까지 태워온 마차의 마부가 바로 백작 자신이었음에 틀림없기 때문이다. 이것은 끔찍한 생각이다. 만일 그렇다면, 그가 그랬듯이 말없이 손을 들어올리는 것만으로 늑대들을 부릴 수 있었다는 것은 대체 무슨 뜻인가? 비스트리차의 사람들과 마차 안의 사람들이 모두 나를 위해 그토록 끔찍하게 두려워한 것은 어찌된 일인가? 십자고상과 마늘과 들장미와 마가목을 건넨 것은 무슨 뜻이었던가? 내 목에 십자고상을 걸어준 그 착하고 착한 부인에게 축복이 있기를! 그것을 만질 때마다 그것이 나에게 위안과 힘이 되어주니 말이다. 내가 못마땅하게, 또 우상숭배적인 것으로 여기도록 배워온 물건이 외로움과 곤경의 때에 도움이 된다는 것은 참으로 기이한 일이다. 그것은 그 물건 자체의 본질에 무언가가 있기 때문일까, 아니면 그것이 동정과 위안의 기억을 전해주는 매개물, 곧 손에 잡히는 도움이기 때문일까? 언젠가 가능하다면, 나는 이 문제를 살펴 마음을 정해보아야겠다. 그동안에는 드라큘라 백작에 관해 알 수 있는 모든 것을 알아내야겠다. 그것이 이해에 도움이 될지도 모르니. 오늘 밤 내가 대화를 그쪽으로 돌린다면, 그가 자신에 관해 이야기할지도 모른다. 그러나 그의 의심을 사지 않도록 매우 조심해야 한다.\n\n자정. — 나는 백작과 긴 이야기를 나누었다. 트란실바니아 역사에 관해 몇 가지 물었더니, 그는 그 주제에 놀랄 만큼 열을 올렸다. 사물과 사람들에 관해, 특히 전투에 관해 이야기할 때, 그는 마치 그 모든 자리에 직접 있었던 것처럼 말했다. 이것을 그는 나중에 이렇게 설명했다. 보야르에게는 가문과 이름의 긍지가 곧 자신의 긍지이며, 그들의 영광이 곧 그의 영광이요, 그들의 운명이 곧 그의 운명이라고. 그는 자기 가문에 관해 이야기할 때면 언제나 \"우리\"라고 했고, 마치 왕이 말하듯 거의 복수형으로 말했다. 그가 한 말을 모두 그가 말한 그대로 적어둘 수 있다면 좋으련만. 나에게 그것은 더없이 매혹적이었으니 말이다. 그 안에 이 나라의 역사 전체가 담겨 있는 듯했다. 그는 이야기하면서 점점 흥분하여, 방 안을 거닐며 큼직한 흰 콧수염을 잡아당기고, 손에 닿는 것은 무엇이든 마치 온 힘으로 으스러뜨리려는 듯 움켜쥐었다. 그가 한 말 가운데 한 대목을 나는 되도록 그대로 옮겨 적어두겠다. 그것이 그 나름의 방식으로 그 종족의 이야기를 전해주기 때문이다.\n* 보야르: 동유럽의 귀족 계급.\n\n\"우리 세케이인은 긍지를 가질 자격이 있소. 우리의 핏줄에는 사자가 싸우듯 패권을 위해 싸운 숱한 용맹한 종족들의 피가 흐르고 있으니 말이오. 이곳, 유럽 종족들의 소용돌이 속으로, 우그리아 부족이 아이슬란드에서 토르와 보덴이 그들에게 준 그 투쟁의 기상을 가지고 밀고 내려왔소. 그들의 베르세르크들이 유럽의 해안에서, 그렇소, 아시아와 아프리카의 해안에서도 그토록 무시무시한 의지로 그 기상을 떨쳐 보였으니, 사람들은 늑대인간 자체가 나타난 줄로 여겼소. 이곳에서, 그들이 왔을 때, 그들은 또한 훈족과 마주쳤소. 그 호전적인 광포함은 살아 있는 불길처럼 온 땅을 휩쓸어, 죽어가던 백성들은 그들의 핏줄에 스키타이에서 쫓겨나 사막에서 악마들과 짝지은 그 옛 마녀들의 피가 흐른다고 믿었소. 어리석은 자들, 어리석은 자들! 이 핏줄 속에 그 피가 흐르고 있는 아틸라만큼 위대한 악마나 마녀가 일찍이 어디 있었소?\" 그는 두 팔을 들어올렸다. \"우리가 정복하는 종족이었다는 것이, 우리가 긍지를 가졌다는 것이, 마자르인이든 롬바르드인이든 아바르인이든 불가르인이든 터키인이든 그 수천의 무리를 우리 국경으로 쏟아부었을 때 우리가 그들을 물리쳤다는 것이 놀라운 일이오? 아르파드와 그의 군단이 헝가리 조국을 휩쓸고 지나갔을 때, 그가 국경에 이르러 이곳에서 우리를 발견했다는 것이, 혼포글랄라시가 그곳에서 완성되었다는 것이 이상한 일이오? 그리고 헝가리의 물결이 동쪽으로 밀려갔을 때, 세케이인은 승리한 마자르인에게 한 핏줄로 인정받았고, 그리하여 수 세기 동안 우리에게 터키 땅의 국경 수비가 맡겨졌소. 그렇소, 그뿐 아니라, 국경 수비병의 끝없는 임무까지도 말이오. 터키인들이 말하듯, '물은 잠들어도 적은 잠들지 않는' 법이니. 네 민족 가운데 누가 우리보다 더 기꺼이 '피 묻은 칼'을 받았으며, 그 호전적인 부름에 누가 우리보다 더 빨리 왕의 깃발 아래로 모여들었소? 왈라키아인과 마자르인의 깃발이 초승달 깃발 아래 무너졌던, 내 민족의 그 크나큰 치욕, 카소바의 치욕을 씻은 것은 언제였소? 보이보드로서 도나우 강을 건너 터키인을 그들의 땅에서 무찌른 것이 내 종족의 한 사람이 아니고 누구였소? 이야말로 진정한 드라큘라였소! 슬프게도, 그가 쓰러진 뒤 그의 못난 아우가 자기 백성을 터키인에게 팔아넘겨 그들에게 노예의 치욕을 안겼으니! 후대에 거듭거듭 자기 군대를 이끌고 그 큰 강을 건너 터키 땅으로 들어간, 격퇴당하고도 다시, 또다시, 또다시 쳐들어간, 그의 군대가 도륙당하는 피의 전장에서 홀로 돌아와야 했음에도 끝내 자기 혼자만이 승리할 수 있음을 알았기에 그리한, 그 종족의 또 다른 한 사람을 분기시킨 것이 바로 이 드라큘라가 아니었소? 사람들은 그가 오직 자기 자신만을 생각했다고 했소. 흥! 지휘자 없는 농민이 무슨 소용이오? 그것을 이끌 두뇌와 심장이 없으면 전쟁은 어디서 끝나겠소? 또한, 모하치 전투 뒤에 우리가 헝가리의 멍에를 떨쳐버렸을 때, 우리 드라큘라의 피를 이은 자들이 그 지도자들 가운데 있었으니, 우리의 기상은 우리가 자유롭지 못함을 견디지 못했기 때문이오. 아, 젊은 양반, 세케이인은 — 그리고 그들의 심장의 피요, 두뇌요, 칼인 드라큘라 가문은 — 합스부르크나 로마노프 같은 버섯처럼 솟아난 것들이 결코 따를 수 없는 기록을 자랑할 수 있소. 호전의 시대는 끝났소. 이 명예롭지 못한 평화의 시대에 피는 너무도 귀한 것이 되었고, 위대한 종족들의 영광은 다 들려준 옛이야기가 되어버렸소.\"\n\n이 무렵에는 어느덧 아침이 가까워, 우리는 잠자리에 들었다. (비망. 이 일기는 어쩐지 「아라비안나이트」의 첫머리와 끔찍하리만큼 닮은 듯하다. 모든 것이 닭 우는 소리에 끊겨야 하니 말이다. 아니면 햄릿의 아버지 유령과도 닮았다.)\n\n5월 12일. — 사실부터, 곧 책과 수치로 검증된, 의심할 여지가 없는 적나라하고 빈약한 사실부터 시작하자. 나는 그것을 내 자신의 관찰이나 그에 대한 기억에 의지할 수밖에 없는 경험들과 혼동해서는 안 된다. 어젯밤 백작은 자기 방에서 나오자, 먼저 법률 문제와 어떤 종류의 사업 처리에 관해 나에게 질문을 하기 시작했다. 나는 그날 하루를 책과 씨름하며 지루하게 보냈는데, 그저 머리를 다른 데에 쓰려고 링컨 법학원에서 검토하던 몇 가지 문제를 다시 짚어보던 참이었다. 백작의 질문에는 어떤 방법론적 짜임새가 있었으므로, 나는 그것을 차례대로 적어보려 한다. 이 지식이 어떻게든, 어느 때엔가 나에게 쓸모가 있을지도 모르니.\n\n먼저 그는 영국에서 한 사람이 변호사를 둘 이상 둘 수 있는지 물었다. 나는 원한다면 열둘이라도 둘 수 있으나, 한 거래에 한 명 이상의 변호사를 쓰는 것은 현명하지 못하다고 답했다. 한 번에 한 사람만 일을 처리할 수 있고, 변호사를 바꾸는 것은 틀림없이 그의 이익에 불리하게 작용할 것이기 때문이라고 했다. 그는 충분히 이해한 듯하더니, 이어서 만일 은행 거래를 맡은 변호사의 본거지에서 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 현지의 도움이 필요할 경우, 한 사람에게는 이를테면 은행 일을, 다른 사람에게는 선적 일을 맡기는 데에 실제로 어떤 어려움이 있는지 물었다. 나는 혹시라도 그를 그릇 인도하지 않도록 좀 더 자세히 설명해달라고 청했고, 그러자 그가 말했다.\n\n\"예를 들어 설명하리다. 당신의 친구이자 나의 친구인 피터 호킨스 씨가, 런던에서 멀리 떨어진 엑서터의 그 아름다운 대성당 그늘 아래에서, 당신이라는 훌륭한 분을 통해 나를 위하여 런던의 부동산을 사들인다고 합시다. 좋소! 그런데 여기서 솔직히 말해두리다. 내가 런던에 사는 누군가가 아니라 런던에서 그토록 멀리 떨어진 사람의 도움을 구한 것을 당신이 이상하게 여길지 모르니 말이오. 나의 동기는, 오직 나의 바람 외에 어떤 현지의 이해관계도 끼어들지 않도록 하려는 것이었소. 런던에 사는 사람이라면 어쩌면 자기 자신이나 친구의 어떤 속셈을 챙기려 할지도 모르기에, 나는 이렇게 멀리까지 와서 오직 나의 이익을 위해서만 일할 대리인을 찾은 것이오. 자, 이제 처리할 일이 많은 내가 이를테면 뉴캐슬이나 더럼, 하리치, 또는 도버로 물건을 부치고자 한다면, 그 항구들에 있는 누군가에게 맡기는 편이 더 수월하지 않겠소?\" 나는 분명 그편이 가장 수월할 것이나, 우리 변호사들은 서로를 위한 대리 제도를 갖추고 있어, 어느 변호사의 지시에 따라 현지의 일은 현지에서 처리될 수 있다고 답했다. 그러므로 의뢰인은 그저 한 사람에게 몸을 맡기기만 하면, 더 이상의 수고 없이 그 사람을 통해 자신의 바람을 이룰 수 있다고 했다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 자유로이 직접 지시할 수도 있는 것 아니오?\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\" 나는 답했다. \"사업가들 가운데에는 자기 일의 전모를 어느 한 사람이 다 알게 되는 것을 꺼려 종종 그렇게 하는 이들이 있습니다.\"\n\n\"좋소!\" 그가 말하더니, 이어서 물건을 부치는 방법과 거쳐야 할 절차들, 그리고 일어날 법한 온갖 어려움들과, 그러나 미리 헤아려 대비할 수 있는 것들에 관해 물었다. 나는 이 모든 것을 내 능력껏 그에게 설명해주었는데, 그는 분명 자기가 훌륭한 변호사가 되었으리라는 인상을 나에게 남겼다. 그가 생각하거나 내다보지 못한 것이 하나도 없었기 때문이다. 한 번도 그 나라에 가본 적이 없고, 또 사업 방면으로 별다른 일을 해본 적이 없는 게 분명한 사람치고는, 그의 지식과 통찰력은 놀라웠다. 그가 이야기한 이 점들에 관해 스스로 만족하고, 또 내가 구할 수 있는 책으로 모든 것을 되도록 잘 검증해주자, 그는 갑자기 일어서서 말했다.\n\n\"우리의 친구 피터 호킨스 씨에게, 또는 다른 누구에게라도, 첫 편지 이후로 편지를 쓴 적이 있소?\" 나는 가슴에 약간의 쓰라림을 안고, 그런 적이 없으며, 아직까지 누구에게도 편지를 부칠 기회를 보지 못했다고 답했다.\n\n\"그러면 지금 쓰시오, 나의 젊은 친구.\" 그가 묵직한 손을 내 어깨에 얹으며 말했다. \"우리의 친구에게, 그리고 다른 누구에게라도 쓰시오. 그리고 괜찮다면, 지금부터 한 달 뒤까지 나와 함께 머물 것이라고 적으시오.\"\n\n\"제가 그토록 오래 머물기를 바라십니까?\" 나는 물었다. 그 생각에 가슴이 서늘해졌기 때문이다.\n\n\"진심으로 그것을 바라오. 아니, 거절은 받아들이지 않겠소. 당신의 주인이든, 고용주든, 무어라 부르든 그가 자기를 대신해 누군가를 보내기로 약속했을 때, 오직 나의 필요만을 살피기로 양해된 것이오. 나는 인색하게 굴지 않았소. 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n내가 고개 숙여 승낙하는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가? 그것은 나의 이익이 아니라 호킨스 씨의 이익이었으니, 나는 나 자신이 아니라 그를 생각해야 했다. 게다가 드라큘라 백작이 말하는 동안, 그의 눈빛과 그의 태도에는 내가 갇힌 몸이며, 내가 원한다 한들 나에게는 아무런 선택의 여지가 없음을 떠올리게 하는 무언가가 있었다. 백작은 내가 고개를 숙이는 데에서 자신의 승리를 보았고, 내 얼굴에 어린 곤혹스러움에서 자신의 지배력을 보았다. 그는 곧바로 그것을 써먹기 시작했으니, 다만 그 나름의 매끄럽고 거역할 수 없는 방식으로였다.\n\n\"부탁하오, 나의 좋은 젊은 친구, 편지에 사업 외의 일은 적지 말기를. 당신이 잘 지내고 있으며, 그들에게로 돌아갈 날을 고대하고 있다는 것을 알면 당신의 벗들도 분명 기뻐할 것이오. 그렇지 않소?\" 그는 이렇게 말하며 나에게 편지지 석 장과 봉투 세 개를 건넸다. 모두 더없이 얇은 외국제 편지지였는데, 그것들을 보고, 이어서 그를 보고, 붉은 아랫입술 위로 드러난 날카로운 송곳니와 함께 그가 짓는 조용한 미소를 알아챘을 때, 나는 그가 말로 한 것이나 다름없이 분명히 깨달았다. 곧, 내가 쓰는 것을 그가 읽을 수 있을 터이니 무엇을 쓰는지 조심해야 한다는 것을. 그래서 나는 이제 형식적인 짧은 편지만 쓰되, 호킨스 씨에게는 몰래 자세히 쓰고, 또 미나에게도 그리하기로 마음먹었다. 미나에게는 속기로 쓸 수 있으니, 백작이 설령 그것을 본다 해도 어리둥절할 것이기 때문이다. 두 통의 편지를 다 쓰고 나서, 나는 조용히 앉아 책을 읽었고, 그동안 백작은 식탁 위의 책 몇 권을 참조해가며 여러 통의 편지를 썼다. 그러고 나서 그는 내 두 통을 집어 자기 것과 함께 두고, 필기도구를 치웠다. 그가 나가고 문이 등 뒤에서 닫히는 순간, 나는 몸을 기울여 식탁 위에 엎어놓인 편지들을 들여다보았다. 그렇게 하는 데에 아무런 거리낌도 느끼지 않았으니, 이런 처지에서는 내가 할 수 있는 모든 방법으로 나 자신을 지켜야 한다고 느꼈기 때문이다.\n\n편지 가운데 하나는 휘트비 더 크레슨트 7번지의 새뮤얼 F. 빌링턴 앞으로, 또 하나는 바르나의 로이트너 씨 앞으로 되어 있었다. 세 번째는 런던의 쿠츠 상회 앞으로, 네 번째는 부다페스트의 은행가 클롭슈토크 운트 빌로이트 상사 앞으로 되어 있었다. 두 번째와 네 번째는 봉해져 있지 않았다. 내가 막 그것들을 들여다보려는데 문손잡이가 움직이는 것이 보였다. 나는 자리에 도로 주저앉았는데, 편지들을 본래 있던 대로 되돌려놓고 다시 책을 집어들 시간을 가까스로 가진 직후, 백작이 또 다른 편지 한 통을 손에 든 채 방으로 들어왔다. 그는 식탁 위의 편지들을 집어 조심스레 우표를 붙이고, 그러고는 나를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"용서해주기 바라오. 오늘 저녁에는 혼자서 처리할 일이 많소. 모든 것이 당신 바라는 대로 갖추어져 있기를 바라오.\" 문가에서 그는 돌아서더니, 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다.\n\n\"한마디 충고하리다, 나의 친애하는 젊은 친구 — 아니, 더없이 진지하게 경고하리다. 만일 이 방들을 떠나게 되거든, 무슨 일이 있어도 이 성의 다른 어느 곳에서도 잠들지 마시오. 이 성은 오래되었고 많은 기억을 품고 있어, 어리석게 잠드는 자에게는 흉한 꿈이 따르오. 명심하시오! 지금이든 언제든 잠이 당신을 덮치거나 덮칠 듯하거든, 곧장 당신의 방이나 이 방들로 서둘러 가시오. 그래야 당신의 휴식이 안전할 것이오. 그러나 이 점에서 조심하지 않는다면, 그때에는 —\" 그는 으스스한 방식으로 말을 맺었으니, 마치 손을 씻는 듯한 시늉을 했기 때문이다. 나는 똑똑히 알아들었다. 다만 한 가지 의심스러운 것은, 어떤 꿈인들 나를 에워싸며 조여드는 듯한 이 부자연스럽고 끔찍한 음울함과 수수께끼의 그물보다 더 무서울 수 있겠느냐는 것이었다.\n\n그 뒤. — 나는 앞서 적은 마지막 말을 그대로 두지만, 이번에는 의심할 여지가 없다. 나는 그가 없는 곳이라면 어디서든 두려움 없이 잠들 것이다. 나는 십자고상을 침대 머리맡에 두었다. 그렇게 하면 내 휴식이 꿈에서 더 자유로워지리라 여겨서이다. 그것은 그곳에 그대로 둘 것이다.\n\n그가 나를 떠난 뒤 나는 내 방으로 갔다. 잠시 뒤 아무 소리도 들리지 않기에, 나는 밖으로 나와 돌계단을 올라 남쪽을 내다볼 수 있는 곳으로 갔다. 비록 나로서는 닿을 수 없는 곳이었으나, 그 광활한 펼쳐짐에는 안마당의 좁은 어둠에 비하면 어떤 자유로움의 느낌이 있었다. 그것을 내다보며 나는 내가 정말로 감옥에 갇혀 있음을 느꼈고, 비록 밤공기일망정 신선한 바람을 한 모금 쐬고 싶어졌다. 나는 이 밤의 생활이 나에게 영향을 미치기 시작했음을 느끼고 있다. 그것이 내 신경을 망가뜨리고 있다. 나는 내 그림자에도 흠칫 놀라고, 온갖 끔찍한 상상으로 가득 차 있다. 이 저주받은 곳에 내 끔찍한 두려움의 근거가 있음을 신은 아신다! 나는 부드러운 노란 달빛에 잠겨 거의 대낮처럼 밝아진 그 아름다운 펼쳐짐을 내다보았다. 그 부드러운 빛 속에서 먼 언덕들은 녹아드는 듯했고, 골짜기와 협곡의 그림자는 벨벳 같은 검은빛이었다. 그저 그 아름다움만으로도 내 마음이 밝아지는 듯했다. 내가 들이쉬는 숨결마다 평화와 위안이 깃들어 있었다. 창에서 몸을 내미는데, 한 층 아래, 내 왼쪽으로 다소 치우친 곳에서 무언가 움직이는 것이 내 눈길을 끌었다. 방들의 배치로 미루어, 백작 자신의 방 창문이 내다보일 만한 곳이라고 짐작되는 자리였다. 내가 선 창은 높고 깊었으며, 돌 중간설주가 있었고, 풍상에 닳기는 했어도 여전히 온전했다. 그러나 창틀이 그곳에 있었던 것은 분명 오래전의 일인 듯했다. 나는 돌벽 뒤로 물러나 조심스레 밖을 내다보았다.\n\n내가 본 것은 창에서 나오는 백작의 머리였다. 얼굴은 보이지 않았으나, 나는 목과 등과 팔의 움직임으로 그가 누구인지 알 수 있었다. 어쨌거나 그토록 여러 번 살펴볼 기회가 있었던 그 손만은 잘못 볼 리 없었다. 처음에 나는 흥미가 일었고, 다소 재미있기도 했다. 사람이 갇힌 몸이 되면 얼마나 사소한 일에도 흥미를 느끼고 재미있어하는지 참으로 놀라운 일이다. 그러나 그 사람 전체가 창에서 서서히 나와, 그 무시무시한 심연 위로 성벽을 타고 기어 내려가기 시작하는 것을 보았을 때 — 얼굴을 아래로 향한 채, 망토를 거대한 날개처럼 사방으로 펼치고 — 나의 그 감정은 혐오와 공포로 바뀌었다. 처음에 나는 내 눈을 믿을 수 없었다. 달빛의 어떤 장난이려니, 그림자가 빚어낸 어떤 기괴한 효과려니 여겼다. 그러나 계속 지켜보았으나, 그것은 결코 착각일 수 없었다. 나는 그의 손가락과 발가락이, 오랜 세월의 풍파에 모르타르가 떨어져나가 드러난 돌의 모서리들을 움켜쥐는 것을, 그리하여 온갖 돌출부와 굴곡을 이용하여 마치 도마뱀이 벽을 기어가듯 상당한 속도로 아래로 내려가는 것을 보았다.\n\n이것은 어떤 종류의 인간인가, 아니면 인간의 형상을 한 어떤 종류의 생물인가? 나는 이 끔찍한 곳에 대한 두려움이 나를 압도하는 것을 느낀다. 나는 두렵다 — 끔찍하게 두렵다 — 그리고 나에게는 빠져나갈 길이 없다. 나는 감히 생각조차 할 수 없는 공포들에 둘러싸여 있다…….\n\n5월 15일. — 나는 또다시 백작이 그 도마뱀 같은 방식으로 나가는 것을 보았다. 그는 비스듬히 아래로, 백 피트쯤 내려갔고, 상당히 왼쪽으로 치우쳐갔다. 그는 어떤 구멍인지 창인지로 사라졌다. 그의 머리가 사라진 뒤, 나는 더 보려고 몸을 내밀었으나 소용이 없었다 — 거리가 너무 멀어 제대로 된 시야 각도를 얻을 수 없었던 것이다. 이제 그가 성을 떠났음을 알았으므로, 나는 지금까지 감히 엄두 내지 못했던 것보다 더 많이 탐색해볼 기회로 삼고자 했다. 나는 방으로 돌아가 등불을 들고 모든 문을 열어보았다. 예상대로 모두 잠겨 있었고, 자물쇠는 비교적 새것이었다. 그러나 나는 돌계단을 내려가 처음에 들어왔던 현관으로 갔다. 빗장은 어렵지 않게 뒤로 밀어낼 수 있었고, 큰 사슬도 풀 수 있었다. 그러나 문은 잠겨 있었고, 열쇠는 사라지고 없었다! 그 열쇠는 분명 백작의 방에 있을 것이다. 나는 그의 방문이 열려 있는지 지켜보아, 열쇠를 손에 넣어 탈출해야 한다. 나는 계속해서 여러 계단과 통로를 샅샅이 살피고, 거기서 열리는 문들을 열어보았다. 현관 가까이의 작은 방 한둘은 열려 있었으나, 그 안에는 세월에 먼지가 앉고 좀이 슨 낡은 가구 말고는 볼 것이 없었다. 그러나 마침내 나는 계단 꼭대기에서 문 하나를 발견했는데, 잠긴 듯 보였으나 밀자 약간 움직였다. 더 세게 밀어보니, 실은 잠긴 것이 아니라, 경첩이 다소 내려앉아 묵직한 문이 바닥에 닿아 있어서 그렇게 버티는 것임을 알았다. 다시는 없을지 모를 기회였으므로, 나는 힘을 다해 여러 번 애쓴 끝에 문을 밀어젖혀 들어갈 수 있었다. 이제 나는 내가 아는 방들보다 더 오른쪽으로, 또 한 층 아래로 치우친 성의 한 부속 건물 안에 있었다. 창문들로 보아 그 방들은 성의 남쪽을 따라 죽 이어져 있었고, 끝 방의 창문은 서쪽과 남쪽 양편으로 내다보였다. 후자 쪽으로도, 전자 쪽으로도 커다란 절벽이 있었다. 성은 거대한 바위의 모퉁이 위에 세워져 있어, 세 방면으로는 도무지 난공불락이었고, 투석기나 활이나 컬버린포가 닿을 수 없는 이곳에 큰 창문들이 자리잡고 있었다. 그리하여 수비해야 하는 자리에서는 불가능한 빛과 안락함이 확보되어 있었다. 서쪽으로는 큰 골짜기가 있었고, 그 너머로 멀리, 봉우리에 봉우리를 잇대어 솟아오른 들쭉날쭉한 산악 요새들이 솟아 있었다. 그 깎아지른 바위에는 마가목과 가시나무가 점점이 박혀 있었는데, 그 뿌리는 돌의 갈라진 틈과 균열과 구멍에 매달려 있었다. 이곳은 분명 지난날 귀부인들이 거처하던 성의 한 부분이었다. 가구가 내가 본 어떤 것보다도 더 안락한 분위기를 풍기고 있었기 때문이다. 창문에는 커튼이 없었고, 마름모꼴 유리창으로 쏟아져 들어오는 노란 달빛 덕분에 빛깔까지도 분간할 수 있었으며, 그 빛은 모든 것 위에 두텁게 쌓인 먼지를 부드럽게 가려주고, 세월과 좀이 끼친 손상을 어느 정도 감추어주었다. 내 등불은 그 찬란한 달빛 속에서 거의 효력이 없는 듯했으나, 그래도 그것을 가지고 있는 것이 다행스러웠다. 그곳에는 내 심장을 서늘하게 하고 신경을 떨리게 하는 무시무시한 외로움이 깃들어 있었기 때문이다. 그래도 백작이 있어 미워하게 된 그 방들에서 혼자 지내는 것보다는 나았다. 그래서 나는 신경을 다잡으려 잠시 애쓴 끝에, 부드러운 고요가 나를 감싸오는 것을 느꼈다. 나는 지금 여기, 작은 떡갈나무 탁자 앞에 앉아 있다. 옛날에는 어쩌면 어느 아리따운 귀부인이 이 탁자 앞에 앉아, 깊은 생각과 숱한 얼굴 붉힘 속에 철자가 서툰 연애편지를 써내려갔을지도 모른다. 그리고 나는 일기를 마지막으로 덮은 이후 일어난 모든 일을 속기로 내 일기에 적고 있다. 이야말로 더없이 철저한 19세기의 최신 방식이다. 그럼에도, 내 감각이 나를 속이는 것이 아니라면, 그 옛 세기들에는 한낱 '현대성'으로는 결코 죽일 수 없는 그들 나름의 힘이 있었고, 또 지금도 있다.\n\n그 뒤, 5월 16일 아침. — 신이여, 부디 내 정신을 지켜주소서. 나는 여기까지 내몰렸나이다. 안전과 안전에 대한 확신은 이미 지나간 일이 되었다. 내가 이곳에서 살아가는 한 바랄 수 있는 것은 오직 하나, 만일 이미 미치지 않았다면, 내가 미치지 않는 것뿐이다. 만일 내가 제정신이라면, 이 가증스러운 곳에 도사린 온갖 더러운 것들 가운데 백작이 나에게는 가장 덜 끔찍한 존재라는 것을, 비록 내가 그의 목적에 쓸모가 있는 동안에 한할지언정 오직 그에게서만 안전을 구할 수 있다는 것을 생각하면, 이는 분명 사람을 미치게 하는 일이다. 위대한 신이여! 자비로운 신이여! 부디 내가 침착하게 하소서. 그 길의 끝에는 정말로 광기가 놓여 있으니. 나는 그동안 나를 어리둥절하게 했던 어떤 일들에 관해 새로운 깨달음을 얻기 시작한다. 지금까지 나는 셰익스피어가 햄릿에게 이런 말을 시켰을 때 그것이 무슨 뜻인지 결코 제대로 알지 못했다.\n\n\"내 수첩을! 어서, 내 수첩을! 이것을 적어두는 것이 마땅하다.\" 운운.\n\n이제 마치 내 자신의 머리가 빗장이 풀린 듯, 혹은 마침내 그것을 무너뜨리고야 말 그 충격이 닥쳐온 듯한 느낌이 들기에, 나는 안식을 찾아 내 일기로 눈을 돌린다. 정확히 기록하는 습관이 나를 진정시키는 데에 도움이 될 것임에 틀림없다.\n\n백작의 알 수 없는 경고는 그때에도 나를 두렵게 했으나, 지금 그것을 떠올리니 더욱 두렵다. 이제부터 그가 나에 대해 무서운 지배력을 쥐게 되었기 때문이다. 나는 그가 하는 말을 의심하기조차 두려워하게 될 것이다!\n\n일기를 다 쓰고, 다행히 책과 펜을 도로 주머니에 넣고 나자 졸음이 몰려왔다. 백작의 경고가 떠올랐으나, 나는 그것을 거역하는 데에 일종의 즐거움을 느꼈다. 졸음이 나를 덮쳐왔고, 그와 함께 잠이 길잡이처럼 데려오는 고집이 일었다. 부드러운 달빛은 마음을 달래주었고, 바깥의 광활한 펼쳐짐은 나를 새롭게 하는 자유로움의 느낌을 주었다. 나는 오늘 밤 그 음울함이 도사린 방들로 돌아가지 않고, 옛날에 귀부인들이 앉아 노래하며 — 가혹한 전쟁 한복판에 나가 있는 자기 남정네들 때문에 그 부드러운 가슴이 슬픔에 잠긴 채로도 — 아름다운 삶을 살았던 이곳에서 잠들기로 마음먹었다. 나는 모퉁이 가까이에 놓인 큰 긴 의자를 끌어내, 누웠을 때 동쪽과 남쪽의 아름다운 경치를 바라볼 수 있도록 했다. 그리고 먼지 따위는 생각하지도, 개의치도 않고 잠을 청했다. 나는 분명 잠이 들었던 모양이다. 그러기를 바라지만, 두렵기도 하다. 뒤이어 일어난 모든 일이 소스라치게 생생했기 때문이다 — 어찌나 생생한지, 지금 환한 아침 햇살 속에 이렇게 앉아 있으면서도, 나는 그것이 모두 꿈이었다고는 도무지 믿을 수가 없다.\n\n나는 혼자가 아니었다. 방은 내가 들어왔을 때와 똑같았고, 어떤 식으로도 달라지지 않았다. 찬란한 달빛 속에서, 나는 바닥을 따라 내가 오래 쌓인 먼지를 흩뜨려놓은 곳에 찍힌 내 발자국을 볼 수 있었다. 내 맞은편 달빛 속에 세 젊은 여인이 있었는데, 옷차림과 거동으로 보아 귀부인들이었다. 그들을 보았을 때 나는 분명 꿈을 꾸고 있는 것이려니 여겼다. 달빛이 그들 뒤에 있었는데도, 그들은 바닥에 그림자를 드리우지 않았기 때문이다. 그들은 내게 가까이 다가와, 한참 동안 나를 바라보더니, 자기들끼리 소곤거렸다. 둘은 검은 머리에, 백작처럼 높다란 매부리코를 가졌고, 창백한 노란 달빛과 대비되어 거의 붉어 보이는 크고 검은, 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈을 가졌다. 나머지 하나는 금발이었는데, 더없이 아름다운 금발로, 물결치는 풍성한 황금빛 머리채와 옅은 사파이어 같은 눈을 가졌다. 나는 어쩐지 그녀의 얼굴을 아는 듯했고, 어떤 꿈결 같은 두려움과 결부지어 아는 듯했으나, 그 순간에는 어떻게, 또 어디서였는지 도무지 떠올릴 수 없었다. 셋 모두 관능적인 루비빛 입술과 대비되어 진주처럼 빛나는 눈부신 흰 이를 가졌다. 그들에게는 나를 불안하게 하는 무언가가, 어떤 갈망과 동시에 어떤 치명적인 두려움이 있었다. 나는 마음속으로, 그들이 그 붉은 입술로 나에게 입맞춰주었으면 하는 사악하고 불타는 욕망을 느꼈다. 이것을 적어두는 것은 좋지 못한 일이다. 언젠가 미나의 눈에 띄어 그녀를 아프게 할지 모르니. 그러나 이것은 진실이다. 그들은 자기들끼리 소곤거리더니, 셋 다 함께 웃었다 — 그토록 은빛으로 낭랑한 웃음, 그러나 그 소리는 결코 인간의 입술의 부드러움을 거쳐 나올 수 없었으리라 싶을 만큼 모질었다. 그것은 능란한 손이 유리잔들을 켤 때 울리는, 참을 수 없이 짜릿하게 감미로운 소리 같았다. 금발 처녀가 요염하게 고개를 저었고, 다른 두 여인이 그녀를 부추겼다. 하나가 말했다.\n\n\"어서! 네가 먼저야, 우리는 뒤따를게. 시작할 권리는 너에게 있어.\" 다른 하나가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"이 남자는 젊고 튼튼해. 우리 모두에게 돌아갈 입맞춤이 있어.\" 나는 가만히 누워, 황홀한 기대의 고통 속에서 속눈썹 아래로 내다보았다. 금발 처녀가 다가와 내 위로 몸을 굽혔고, 마침내 나는 그녀의 숨결이 일렁이는 것을 느낄 수 있었다. 그것은 어떤 의미로는 달콤했다, 꿀처럼 달콤했고, 그녀의 목소리와 똑같은 짜릿함을 신경을 타고 보내왔다. 그러나 그 달콤함 밑에는 쓴맛이, 피에서 맡아지는 것 같은 쓰디쓴 역겨움이 깔려 있었다.\n\n나는 눈꺼풀을 들어올리기가 두려웠으나, 속눈썹 아래로 내다보니 완벽하게 보였다. 처녀는 무릎을 꿇고 내 위로 몸을 굽혀, 그저 음탕하게 나를 탐했다. 거기에는 짜릿하면서도 혐오스러운 어떤 계산된 관능이 있었으니, 그녀가 목을 활처럼 젖히며 정말로 짐승처럼 입술을 핥는 통에, 나는 달빛 속에서 그 진홍빛 입술 위에, 또 희고 날카로운 이를 핥는 붉은 혀 위에 침이 번득이는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그녀의 머리는 점점 더 낮게 내려와, 입술이 내 입과 턱의 높이 아래로 내려갔고, 이윽고 내 목에 들러붙을 듯했다. 그러다 그녀는 멈추었고, 나는 그녀의 혀가 이와 입술을 핥는 휘젓는 듯한 소리를 들을 수 있었으며, 내 목에 닿는 뜨거운 숨결을 느낄 수 있었다. 이윽고 내 목의 살갗이 따끔거리기 시작했다, 마치 간지럽힐 손이 점점 더, 더 가까이 다가올 때 살갗이 그러하듯이. 나는 더없이 예민해진 내 목의 살갗에 닿는 입술의 부드럽게 떨리는 감촉을, 그리고 막 닿아 그곳에서 멈춘 두 날카로운 이의 단단한 자국을 느낄 수 있었다. 나는 나른한 황홀경 속에 눈을 감고 기다렸다 — 두근거리는 가슴으로 기다렸다.\n\n그러나 그 순간, 또 다른 감각이 번개처럼 빠르게 나를 휩쓸고 지나갔다. 나는 백작의 존재를, 그리고 그가 마치 분노의 폭풍에 휩싸인 듯한 것을 알아차렸다. 나도 모르게 눈이 뜨이자, 나는 그의 억센 손이 금발 여인의 가녀린 목을 움켜쥐고 거인 같은 힘으로 잡아채는 것을 보았다. 푸른 눈은 분노로 일그러졌고, 흰 이는 노여움으로 으드득거렸으며, 어여쁜 두 뺨은 격정으로 시뻘겋게 타올랐다. 그러나 백작이라니! 나는 지옥 구덩이의 악마들에게서조차 그런 진노와 광포함을 상상해본 적이 없다. 그의 두 눈은 정말로 이글거리고 있었다. 그 안의 붉은 빛은 마치 지옥불의 화염이 그 뒤에서 타오르기라도 하는 듯 음산했다. 그의 얼굴은 죽은 사람처럼 창백했고, 그 윤곽은 팽팽히 당긴 철사처럼 굳어 있었다. 콧등 위에서 맞붙은 그 짙은 눈썹은 이제 마치 백열로 달구어진 쇠막대가 일렁이는 것 같았다. 그는 사납게 팔을 휘둘러 그 여인을 자기에게서 내던지고, 그러고는 다른 여인들을 향해 마치 그들을 쳐서 물리치기라도 하듯 손짓했다. 그것은 늑대들에게 쓰는 것을 내가 보았던, 바로 그 위압적인 몸짓이었다. 낮고 거의 속삭임에 가까웠으나 공기를 가르고 이윽고 방 안을 빙 둘러 울리는 듯한 목소리로 그가 말했다.\n\n\"어찌 감히 너희 가운데 누구든 이자에게 손을 대느냐? 내가 금했거늘 어찌 감히 이자에게 눈을 돌리느냐? 물러서라, 너희 모두에게 이르노라! 이 사람은 나의 것이다! 이자에게 함부로 손댈 생각일랑 마라. 그랬다간 나를 상대해야 할 테니.\" 금발 처녀가 음탕하고 요염한 웃음을 띠며 그에게 대꾸하려 돌아섰다.\n\n\"당신이야말로 결코 사랑한 적이 없잖아요. 당신은 사랑이란 걸 모르잖아요!\" 이 말에 다른 여인들도 가세하여, 어찌나 흥 없고 모질고 혼이 없는 웃음이 방 안에 울려퍼지던지, 듣고 있는 나는 거의 까무러칠 지경이었다. 그것은 마치 악귀들의 환락 같았다. 그러자 백작이 내 얼굴을 유심히 들여다본 뒤 돌아서서, 부드러운 속삭임으로 말했다.\n\n\"그래, 나도 사랑할 수 있다. 너희도 지난날을 보면 알 수 있을 것이다. 그렇지 않으냐? 자, 이제 너희에게 약속하마. 내가 이자를 다 쓰고 나거든, 너희 마음대로 그에게 입맞춰도 좋다. 자 이제 가라! 가라! 나는 그를 깨워야 한다. 해야 할 일이 있으니.\"\n\n\"오늘 밤은 우리에게 아무것도 없나요?\" 그들 가운데 하나가 낮게 웃으며, 그가 바닥에 내던진 자루를 가리키며 말했다. 그 자루는 마치 안에 무언가 살아 있는 것이 들어 있기라도 한 듯 꿈틀거렸다. 대답 대신 그는 고개를 끄덕였다. 여인들 가운데 하나가 앞으로 뛰어나가 그것을 열었다. 내 귀가 나를 속인 것이 아니라면, 거기서 반쯤 숨이 막힌 아이의 것 같은 헐떡임과 낮은 흐느낌이 새어나왔다. 여인들이 빙 둘러쌌고, 나는 공포에 질려 기겁했다. 그러나 내가 바라보는 사이 그들은 사라졌고, 그 무시무시한 자루도 그들과 함께 사라졌다. 그들 가까이에는 문이 없었고, 그들이 나를 지나쳤다면 내가 알아차리지 못했을 리 없다. 그들은 그저 달빛 속으로 녹아들어 창문을 통해 빠져나가는 듯했으니, 바깥에서 그 흐릿하고 어슴푸레한 형체들이 완전히 사라지기 전에 잠시 보였기 때문이다.\n\n그러자 공포가 나를 압도했고, 나는 의식을 잃고 쓰러졌다.\n\n나는 내 침대에서 깨어났다. 만일 내가 꿈을 꾼 것이 아니라면, 백작이 나를 이리로 옮겨놓았음이 틀림없다. 나는 이 일을 두고 마음을 가라앉혀 보려 했으나, 의심의 여지 없는 결론에는 이를 수 없었다. 물론 사소한 증거들이 몇 가지 있기는 했다. 이를테면 내 옷이 내 버릇과는 다른 방식으로 개켜져 한쪽에 놓여 있었다는 것이다. 내 시계는 여전히 태엽이 감겨 있지 않았는데, 나는 잠자리에 들기 전 마지막으로 반드시 시계태엽을 감는 습관이 철저했으며, 그 밖에도 이런 자질구레한 일들이 많았다. 그러나 이런 것들은 증거가 되지 못한다. 내 정신이 평소 같지 않았다는, 그리고 어떤 까닭에선지 내가 분명 몹시 동요했다는 증거일 뿐일지도 모르기 때문이다. 나는 증거를 찾아내야 한다. 한 가지 다행스러운 것이 있다. 만일 백작이 나를 이리로 옮겨와 옷을 벗긴 것이라면, 그는 그 일을 서둘러 했음이 틀림없다. 내 주머니가 그대로였으니 말이다. 이 일기가 그에게는 풀 수 없는 수수께끼였을 것이며, 그는 그것을 그냥 두지 않았으리라 나는 확신한다. 가져갔거나 없애버렸을 것이다. 이 방을 둘러보니, 비록 이곳이 나에게 그토록 공포로 가득 찬 곳이었으나 지금은 일종의 성소(聖所)처럼 느껴진다. 내 피를 빨려고 기다리고 있던 — 아니, 기다리고 있는 그 끔찍한 여인들보다 더 무서운 것은 없으니 말이다.\n\n5월 18일. — 나는 그 방을 대낮에 다시 보려고 내려가 보았다. 진실을 반드시 알아야만 했기 때문이다. 계단 꼭대기의 문간에 이르니 문이 닫혀 있었다. 어찌나 거센 힘으로 문설주에 밀어붙여졌던지 목재 일부가 쪼개져 있었다. 자물쇠의 빗장이 걸려 있지 않은 것은 알 수 있었으나, 문은 안쪽에서 잠겨 있었다. 그것이 꿈이 아니었을까 두렵고, 이 추측에 따라 행동해야만 한다.\n\n5월 19일. — 나는 분명 덫에 걸려 있다. 어젯밤 백작은 더없이 부드러운 어조로 나에게 편지 세 통을 쓰라고 했다. 하나는 이곳에서의 일이 거의 끝났으니 며칠 안에 집으로 떠나겠다는 것, 또 하나는 편지를 쓴 날로부터 다음 날 아침에 출발하겠다는 것, 셋째는 성을 떠나 비스트리차에 도착했다는 것이었다. 나는 기꺼이 거역하고 싶었으나, 내가 이토록 철저히 그의 손아귀에 들어 있는 지금의 상황에서 백작과 대놓고 다투는 것은 미친 짓이라 느꼈다. 거절한다는 것은 그의 의심을 사고 그의 분노를 일으키는 일이 될 터였다. 그는 내가 너무 많이 안다는 것, 그리고 내가 그에게 위험한 존재가 될까 하여 살려두어서는 안 된다는 것을 알고 있다. 내게 남은 유일한 기회는 기회의 시간을 늘리는 것뿐이다. 무언가 일이 벌어져 탈출할 기회를 줄지도 모른다. 그가 그 아름다운 여인을 내던질 때 드러났던 그 부풀어오르는 분노 같은 것을 나는 그의 눈에서 보았다. 그는 우편이 드물고 또 믿을 수 없으며, 내가 지금 글을 써두면 내 벗들의 마음을 편하게 해줄 것이라고 설명했다. 그리고 혹시 내 체류를 연장할 형편이 된다면 나중의 편지들은 비스트리차에 적당한 때까지 보관해두었다가 취소시키겠노라고, 어찌나 무게 있는 어조로 장담하던지, 그에게 맞선다는 것은 새로운 의심을 불러일으키는 일이 되었을 것이다. 그래서 나는 그의 뜻을 따르는 척하며, 편지에 어떤 날짜를 적어야 하느냐고 물었다. 그는 잠시 셈을 하더니 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"첫 번째 것은 6월 12일, 두 번째 것은 6월 19일, 세 번째 것은 6월 29일이오.\"\n\n이제 나는 내 목숨의 길이를 안다. 하느님, 부디 저를 도우소서!\n\n5월 28일. — 탈출할 기회가, 아니 적어도 집으로 소식을 전할 수 있는 기회가 생겼다. 한 무리의 즈가니가 성에 와서 안마당에 진을 쳤다. 이 즈가니는 집시들로, 나는 내 수첩에 그들에 관한 메모를 적어두었다. 그들은 온 세상에 흔한 보통 집시들과 한 갈래이긴 하나, 이 지방 특유의 사람들이다. 헝가리와 트란실바니아에는 그들이 수천 명이나 되며, 거의 모든 법의 바깥에 있다. 그들은 대개 어떤 대귀족이나 보야르(귀족)에게 몸을 의탁하고는 그의 이름을 따 자신들을 부른다. 그들은 두려움을 모르고, 미신을 빼면 종교도 없으며, 자기들 나름의 로마니어 사투리만을 쓴다.\n\n* 보야르: 동유럽의 귀족 계급.\n나는 집으로 보낼 편지를 몇 통 써서, 그들에게 부쳐달라고 부탁해 볼 작정이다. 나는 이미 친분을 트려고 창문 너머로 그들에게 말을 건넸다. 그들은 모자를 벗고 절을 하며 여러 가지 손짓을 했으나, 그 손짓도 그들의 말만큼이나 도무지 알아들을 수 없었다…….\n\n편지를 다 썼다. 미나에게 보내는 것은 속기로 적었고, 호킨스 씨에게는 그저 그녀에게 연락해달라고 부탁했다. 그녀에게는 내 처지를 설명했으나, 짐작만 할 뿐인 그 공포에 대해서는 적지 않았다. 만일 내 속마음을 그녀에게 드러낸다면 그녀는 충격과 두려움에 죽고 말 것이다. 혹시 편지가 전해지지 않더라도, 백작은 아직 내 비밀이나 내가 아는 것의 정도를 알지는 못할 것이다…….\n\n편지를 건넸다. 금화 한 닢과 함께 창살 사이로 던져 넣고, 부쳐달라고 할 수 있는 한 손짓을 다 해 보였다. 그것을 받아든 남자는 편지를 가슴에 꼭 누르며 고개를 숙이더니, 모자 속에 넣었다. 나로서는 더 할 수 있는 일이 없었다. 나는 살그머니 서재로 돌아와 책을 읽기 시작했다. 백작이 들어오지 않기에, 여기 이렇게 적는다…….\n\n백작이 왔다. 그는 내 곁에 앉더니, 편지 두 통을 펼치며 더없이 매끄러운 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"즈가니가 나에게 이것들을 주었소. 어디서 난 것인지는 모르겠으나, 물론 내가 잘 간수하겠소. 보시오!\" — 그는 그것을 들여다보았던 모양이다 — \"하나는 당신이 쓴 것으로, 내 벗 피터 호킨스에게 보내는 것이군. 다른 하나는\" — 여기서 그는 봉투를 뜯다가 그 이상한 기호들을 보고는, 얼굴에 어두운 빛이 떠오르고 두 눈이 사악하게 번득였다 — \"다른 하나는 비열한 것이오. 우정과 환대에 대한 모독이오! 서명도 되어 있지 않군. 흥! 그러니 우리에게는 아무 상관 없겠소.\" 그러고는 편지와 봉투를 램프 불꽃에 차분히 갖다 대어 다 태워버렸다. 그런 다음 말을 이었다.\n\n\"호킨스에게 보내는 편지는, 당신이 쓴 것이니 물론 내가 부쳐드리겠소. 당신의 편지는 나에게 신성한 것이오. 미안하오, 나의 벗, 나도 모르고 봉인을 뜯고 말았구려. 다시 봉해주지 않겠소?\" 그는 나에게 편지를 내밀더니, 정중히 고개를 숙이며 깨끗한 봉투 하나를 건넸다. 나는 잠자코 다시 수신인 주소를 적어 그에게 넘겨주는 수밖에 없었다. 그가 방을 나가자 열쇠가 살그머니 돌아가는 소리가 들렸다. 잠시 후 가서 문을 흔들어 보았더니, 문은 잠겨 있었다.\n\n한두 시간쯤 지난 뒤 백작이 소리 없이 방으로 들어왔는데, 그가 들어오는 바람에 나는 잠에서 깨었다. 소파에서 잠이 들었던 것이다. 그는 매우 정중하고 무척 명랑한 태도였으며, 내가 자고 있던 것을 보더니 말했다.\n\n\"이런, 나의 벗, 피곤하오? 침대에 가서 누우시오. 거기가 가장 확실한 안식처요. 오늘 밤은 할 일이 많아 당신과 이야기할 즐거움은 누리지 못하겠으나, 부디 잘 자기를 바라오.\" 나는 내 방으로 건너가 잠자리에 들었고, 이상하게도 꿈 하나 꾸지 않고 잤다. 절망에는 절망 나름의 평온이 있는 법이다.\n\n5월 31일. — 오늘 아침 잠에서 깨어, 가방에서 종이와 봉투를 좀 꺼내 주머니에 넣어두었다가 기회가 생기면 편지를 쓸 수 있도록 해야겠다고 생각했다. 그런데 또 한 번 놀라움이, 또 한 번 충격이 닥쳤다!\n\n종잇조각이란 종잇조각은 죄다 사라지고 없었으며, 그와 함께 철도며 여행에 관한 내 메모와 비망록, 신용장까지, 요컨대 일단 성 밖으로 나가기만 하면 나에게 쓸모가 있을 만한 것이 모조리 없어졌다. 나는 잠시 앉아 곰곰이 생각하다가, 문득 어떤 생각이 떠올라 내 여행 가방과, 옷을 넣어두었던 옷장을 뒤져보았다.\n\n내가 여행할 때 입었던 양복이 사라졌고, 외투와 무릎덮개도 없었다. 어디에서도 그 흔적을 찾을 수 없었다. 이것은 무언가 새로운 악행의 술책처럼 보였다…….\n\n6월 17일. — 오늘 아침, 침대 가장자리에 앉아 머리를 쥐어짜고 있는데, 밖에서 채찍이 갈라지는 소리와 안마당 너머 바위투성이 길을 올라오는 말발굽이 쿵쿵 짓밟고 긁히는 소리가 들렸다. 나는 기쁨에 들떠 서둘러 창가로 갔고, 마당으로 들어오는 커다란 짐마차 두 대를 보았다. 저마다 건장한 말 여덟 마리가 끌고 있었으며, 각 마차의 앞쪽 두 마리 곁에는 슬로바키아인이 한 명씩 있었는데, 챙 넓은 모자에, 못이 빼곡히 박힌 큼직한 허리띠, 더러운 양가죽 옷, 긴 장화 차림이었다. 그들은 손에 긴 막대기도 들고 있었다. 나는 문으로 달려갔다. 그들을 위해 본관으로 통하는 길이 열려 있으리라 생각하고, 아래로 내려가 큰 홀을 거쳐 그들과 합류해 보려는 것이었다. 또 한 번 충격이 닥쳤다. 내 방문이 바깥에서 잠겨 있었던 것이다.\n\n그래서 나는 창가로 달려가 그들에게 소리쳤다. 그들은 멍하니 나를 올려다보며 손가락질을 했으나, 바로 그때 즈가니의 \"헤트만\"(우두머리)이 나오더니, 그들이 내 창문을 가리키는 것을 보고는 무언가 말을 했고, 그 말에 그들은 웃음을 터뜨렸다. 그 뒤로는 내가 아무리 애를 써도, 아무리 애처롭게 외치고 애끓도록 사정해도 그들은 나를 쳐다보려고조차 하지 않았다. 그들은 단호히 등을 돌렸다. 그 짐마차에는 굵은 밧줄 손잡이가 달린 크고 네모난 상자들이 실려 있었다. 슬로바키아인들이 그것들을 다루는 손놀림이 쉬운 것으로 보아, 또 거칠게 옮길 때 울리는 소리로 보아, 분명 빈 상자들이었다. 그것들을 모두 부려 마당 한구석에 큰 무더기로 쌓아놓자, 슬로바키아인들은 즈가니에게서 돈을 좀 받더니, 행운을 빌며 거기에 침을 뱉고는 느릿느릿 저마다 제 말의 머리께로 갔다. 곧이어 그들의 채찍 소리가 멀리서 잦아드는 것이 들렸다.\n\n* 헤트만: 동유럽에서 군사·집단의 우두머리를 가리키는 칭호.\n6월 24일, 동트기 전. — 어젯밤 백작은 일찍 나를 떠나 자기 방에 틀어박혔다. 엄두가 나자마자 나는 나선 계단을 뛰어올라가, 남쪽으로 난 창문 밖을 내다보았다. 무언가 일이 벌어지고 있으니, 백작을 지켜보아야겠다고 생각했던 것이다. 즈가니는 성 어딘가에 숙소를 잡고 무슨 일인가를 하고 있다. 나는 그것을 안다. 이따금 곡괭이질과 삽질 같은 멀고 둔탁한 소리가 들려오기 때문이며, 그것이 무엇이든 어떤 무자비한 악행의 끝일 것이 틀림없다.\n\n창가에 머문 지 채 반 시간도 못 되었을 때, 나는 무언가가 백작의 창문에서 나오는 것을 보았다. 나는 뒤로 물러나 조심스레 지켜보았고, 그 사람의 온몸이 빠져나오는 것을 보았다. 새삼 충격이었던 것은, 그가 내가 이곳으로 오는 길에 입었던 양복을 입고 있었으며, 어깨에는 그 여인들이 가져가던 그 끔찍한 자루를 메고 있었다는 점이다. 그의 행각이 무엇인지, 게다가 내 옷차림을 하고 있으니, 의심의 여지가 없었다! 그러니 이것이 그의 새로운 악의 술책인 것이다. 그는 사람들로 하여금 나를, 그들 생각으로는 나를 보았다고 여기게 함으로써, 내가 그 도시나 마을에서 내 편지를 직접 부치는 모습을 사람들이 보았다는 증거를 남기는 한편, 그가 저지를지 모를 온갖 악행을 그곳 주민들이 나의 짓으로 돌리게 하려는 것이다.\n\n이런 일이 계속될 수 있다는 것, 그것도 내가 이곳에 갇혀, 그야말로 죄수의 신세이면서, 죄인에게마저 주어지는 권리이자 위안인 그 법의 보호조차 받지 못한 채 갇혀 있는 동안 그러하다는 것을 생각하면 나는 분노가 치민다.\n\n나는 백작이 돌아오는 것을 지켜보아야겠다고 생각하고, 오랫동안 끈질기게 창가에 앉아 있었다. 그러다 달빛 줄기 속에 무언가 기이한 작은 알갱이들이 떠다니는 것이 눈에 띄기 시작했다. 그것들은 더없이 작은 먼지 알갱이 같았는데, 빙글빙글 돌며 어슴푸레하게 무리를 지어 모여들었다. 나는 마음이 누그러지는 느낌으로 그것들을 바라보았고, 일종의 평온이 슬며시 나를 감쌌다. 나는 그 공중의 춤을 더 마음껏 즐길 수 있도록, 창의 후미진 자리에 한결 편안한 자세로 몸을 기댔다.\n\n무언가가 나를 벌떡 일어나게 했다. 내 시야에서 가려진 저 아래 골짜기 어딘가에서 개들이 낮고 애처롭게 울부짖는 소리였다. 그 소리가 점점 더 크게 내 귓속에 울리는 듯했고, 떠다니던 먼지 티끌들은 달빛 속에서 춤추며 그 소리에 맞추어 새로운 모양을 빚어내는 듯했다. 나는 어떤 본능의 부름에 깨어나려고 발버둥치는 나 자신을 느꼈다. 아니, 내 영혼 자체가 발버둥치고 있었고, 반쯤만 기억나는 내 감각들이 그 부름에 응하려 안간힘을 쓰고 있었다. 나는 최면에 빠져들고 있었던 것이다! 먼지는 점점 더 빠르게 춤을 추었고, 달빛은 내 곁을 지나 저 너머 어둠의 덩어리 속으로 흘러들며 가물거리는 듯했다. 그것들은 점점 더 모여들더니 마침내 어렴풋한 유령의 형상을 띠는 듯했다. 그때 나는 흠칫 놀라, 완전히 깨어나 온전히 제정신으로 돌아와서는, 비명을 지르며 그곳에서 달아났다. 달빛에서 차츰 형체를 갖추어가던 그 유령의 형상들은, 내가 운명적으로 매여 있는 그 세 유령 같은 여인들의 것이었다. 나는 달아났고, 달빛도 없고 램프가 환히 타고 있는 내 방으로 돌아오니 다소 안전한 느낌이 들었다.\n\n두어 시간쯤 지났을 때, 나는 백작의 방에서 무언가 움직이는 소리를 들었다. 날카로운 비명이 재빨리 눌려 잦아드는 것 같은 소리였다. 그러고는 정적이, 깊고 무서운 정적이 찾아와 나를 오싹하게 했다. 나는 두근거리는 가슴으로 문을 흔들어 보았으나, 나는 내 감옥에 갇혀 있어 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 나는 주저앉아 그저 울었다.\n\n그렇게 앉아 있는데, 바깥 안마당에서 어떤 소리가 들렸다. 한 여인의 애끓는 울부짖음이었다. 나는 창가로 달려가 창을 밀어 올리고는 창살 사이로 밖을 내다보았다. 거기에는 정말로 한 여인이, 머리는 헝클어진 채, 마치 뛰어와 숨이 가쁜 사람처럼 두 손을 가슴에 얹고 서 있었다. 그녀는 문간의 한 모퉁이에 기대어 있었다. 창문에 비친 내 얼굴을 보더니 그녀는 앞으로 몸을 내던지며, 위협이 가득 실린 목소리로 외쳤다.\n\n\"이 괴물아, 내 아이를 내놔라!\"\n\n그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 치켜들며, 내 가슴을 쥐어짜는 듯한 어조로 같은 말을 외쳤다. 그러고는 머리를 쥐어뜯고 가슴을 치며, 격렬한 감정의 온갖 발작에 자신을 내맡겼다. 마침내 그녀는 앞으로 몸을 던졌고, 나는 그녀를 볼 수는 없었으나, 그녀가 맨손으로 문을 두드리는 소리는 들을 수 있었다.\n\n저 위 어딘가 높은 곳, 아마도 탑에서, 백작이 거칠고 쇳소리 나는 속삭임으로 부르는 목소리가 들렸다. 그의 부름에 사방 멀리서 늑대들의 울부짖음이 응답하는 듯했다. 몇 분도 채 지나기 전에 늑대 떼가, 마치 막아두었던 둑이 터진 듯, 넓은 입구를 통해 안마당으로 쏟아져 들어왔다.\n\n여인에게서는 비명 소리가 없었고, 늑대들의 울부짖음도 짧을 뿐이었다. 이윽고 그것들은 입술을 핥으며 한 마리씩 빠져나갔다.\n\n나는 그녀를 가엾이 여길 수 없었다. 그녀의 아이가 어떻게 되었는지 이제 알았기 때문이며, 그녀로서는 죽는 편이 차라리 나았다.\n\n나는 어찌해야 하나? 내가 무엇을 할 수 있나? 밤과 어둠과 공포로 이루어진 이 끔찍한 것으로부터 나는 어떻게 벗어날 수 있단 말인가?\n\n6월 25일, 아침. — 밤의 고통을 겪어보기 전에는 그 누구도 아침이 자기 마음과 눈에 얼마나 달콤하고 소중할 수 있는지 알지 못한다. 오늘 아침 해가 높이 떠올라 내 창문 맞은편 큰 문간의 꼭대기에 비치자, 그 빛이 닿은 높은 자리가 마치 방주에서 날아온 비둘기가 내려앉은 곳인 양 여겨졌다. 내 공포가, 마치 온기에 녹아 사라지는 안개 같은 옷이라도 되는 듯 나에게서 떨어져 나갔다. 낮의 용기가 나에게 깃들어 있는 동안 나는 어떤 식으로든 행동에 나서야 한다. 어젯밤 날짜를 미리 적은 내 편지 가운데 하나가 부쳐졌는데, 이 땅에서 내 존재의 흔적마저 지워버릴 그 치명적인 연속물의 첫 번째 것이다.\n\n이 일은 생각하지 말자. 행동이다!\n\n내가 시달리거나 위협받거나, 또는 어떤 식으로든 위험에 처하거나 두려움에 떨었던 것은 언제나 밤이었다. 나는 아직 대낮에 백작을 본 적이 없다. 그는 남들이 깨어 있을 때 자고, 남들이 잘 때 깨어 있을 수 있도록 하는 것이 아닐까? 그의 방에만 들어갈 수 있다면! 그러나 그럴 방도가 없다. 문은 늘 잠겨 있으니, 나로서는 길이 없다.\n\n그렇다, 한 가지 길이 있다, 감히 그것을 택하기만 한다면. 그의 몸이 갔던 곳이라면, 어찌 다른 몸이라고 못 가겠는가? 나는 그가 자기 창문에서 기어 나오는 것을 내 눈으로 보았다. 어찌 나라고 그를 흉내 내어 그의 창문으로 들어가지 못하겠는가? 가망은 절망적이나, 내 처지는 그보다 더 절망적이다. 나는 그것을 감행하겠다. 최악이라야 죽음일 뿐이며, 사람의 죽음은 송아지의 죽음과는 다르니, 그 두려운 내세(來世)가 아직 나에게 열려 있을지도 모른다. 하느님, 이 일에서 저를 도우소서! 잘 있으오, 미나, 내가 실패한다면. 잘 있으십시오, 나의 충실한 벗이자 또 한 분의 아버지여. 모두들 잘 있으오, 그리고 마지막으로 또, 미나여!\n\n같은 날, 나중에. — 나는 그 일을 시도했고, 하느님의 도우심으로 이 방에 무사히 돌아왔다. 모든 것을 빠짐없이 순서대로 적어두어야겠다. 나는 용기가 식기 전에 곧장 남쪽 창문으로 가서, 곧바로 이쪽 면을 따라 건물을 빙 두른 좁은 돌 턱으로 나섰다. 돌들은 크고 거칠게 깎여 있었으며, 그 사이의 회반죽은 오랜 세월에 씻겨 없어진 상태였다. 나는 장화를 벗고 그 절망적인 길로 나섰다. 끔찍한 깊이가 문득 눈에 들어와 나를 압도하지 않도록 한 번만 아래를 내려다보았고, 그 뒤로는 거기서 눈을 거두었다. 나는 백작의 창문이 어느 방향에 얼마쯤 떨어져 있는지 꽤 잘 알고 있었으므로, 주어진 발판들을 살피면서 할 수 있는 한 그쪽으로 나아갔다. 어지럽지는 않았다 — 너무 흥분해 있었던 모양이다 — 그리고 어느새 창턱에 서서 창틀을 들어 올리려 하고 있었으니, 시간은 우습도록 짧게 느껴졌다. 그러나 몸을 숙여 발부터 창문 안으로 미끄러져 들어갈 때에는 동요로 가슴이 가득 찼다. 그런 다음 나는 백작을 찾아 둘러보았으나, 놀랍고도 기쁘게도 한 가지 발견을 했다. 방이 비어 있었던 것이다! 방에는 한 번도 쓰인 적 없어 보이는 잡다한 물건들이 듬성듬성 놓여 있을 뿐이었다. 가구는 남쪽 방들의 것과 다소 같은 양식이었으며, 먼지가 덮여 있었다. 나는 열쇠를 찾아보았으나 자물쇠에 꽂혀 있지 않았고, 어디에서도 찾을 수 없었다. 내가 찾아낸 유일한 것은 한구석에 쌓인 거대한 금더미였다 — 로마, 영국, 오스트리아, 헝가리, 그리스, 터키의 온갖 화폐가, 마치 땅속에 오래 묻혀 있었던 듯 먼지의 막을 뒤집어쓰고 있었다. 내가 눈여겨본 것 중에 삼백 년이 안 된 것은 하나도 없었다. 사슬과 장신구도 있었는데, 더러는 보석이 박혀 있었으나 하나같이 오래되고 빛이 바래 있었다.\n\n방 한쪽 구석에는 묵직한 문이 하나 있었다. 나는 그것을 흔들어 보았다. 방의 열쇠도, 내가 찾는 주된 목적이던 바깥문의 열쇠도 찾을 수 없는 마당이니, 더 살펴보지 않으면 내 모든 노력이 헛수고가 될 터였기 때문이다. 문은 열려 있었고, 돌로 된 통로를 지나 가파르게 아래로 내려가는 원형 계단으로 이어졌다. 나는 발걸음을 조심스레 살피며 내려갔다. 계단은 두꺼운 석조 벽에 난 총안(銃眼)에서 들어오는 빛만으로 비칠 뿐이라 어두웠기 때문이다. 맨 아래에는 어둡고 굴 같은 통로가 있었고, 그곳으로부터 죽음 같은, 메스꺼운 냄새가, 갓 파헤친 오래된 흙냄새가 풍겨 왔다. 통로를 지날수록 냄새는 더 가까워지고 더 짙어졌다. 마침내 나는 반쯤 열려 있던 묵직한 문 하나를 잡아당겨 열고는, 오래되어 허물어진 예배당 안에 서 있는 나 자신을 발견했는데, 분명 묘지로 쓰여 온 곳이었다. 지붕은 무너져 있었고, 두 군데에는 지하 납골소로 내려가는 계단이 있었으나, 땅은 최근에 파헤쳐졌고, 그 흙은 커다란 나무 상자들에, 분명 슬로바키아인들이 가져온 그 상자들에 담겨 있었다. 주위에는 아무도 없었으며, 나는 더 빠져나갈 출구가 있는지 찾아보았으나 없었다. 그래서 나는 한 치의 기회도 놓치지 않으려고 땅을 한 뼘 한 뼘 샅샅이 살폈다. 어슴푸레한 빛이 간신히 비쳐드는 지하 납골소까지도 내려갔는데, 그렇게 하는 것이 내 영혼 자체에 두려운 일이긴 했다. 그중 두 곳에 들어가 보았으나 낡은 관 조각들과 먼지 더미밖에 보이지 않았다. 그러나 세 번째 곳에서 나는 한 가지 발견을 했다.\n\n거기, 모두 합쳐 쉰 개나 되는 큰 상자들 가운데 하나에, 갓 파낸 흙더미 위에, 백작이 누워 있었다! 그가 죽은 것인지 잠든 것인지 나로서는 알 수 없었다 — 두 눈은 뜬 채 돌처럼 굳어 있었으나 죽음의 흐릿함은 없었고, 두 뺨은 온통 창백하면서도 살아 있는 온기를 띠었으며, 입술은 여느 때처럼 붉었다. 그러나 움직임의 기색은 전혀 없었고, 맥박도, 숨결도, 심장의 고동도 없었다. 나는 그 위로 몸을 숙여 생명의 흔적을 찾아보려 했으나 헛일이었다. 그가 거기 누운 지 오래되었을 리는 없었으니, 오래되었다면 그 흙냄새가 몇 시간 안에 가셨을 것이기 때문이다. 상자 옆에는 군데군데 구멍이 뚫린 그 뚜껑이 놓여 있었다. 나는 그가 열쇠를 몸에 지니고 있을지도 모른다고 생각했으나, 뒤져보려고 다가가자 그 죽은 눈이 보였고, 비록 죽은 눈이었으나, 또 나나 내 존재를 의식하지는 못했으나, 그 안에 어찌나 깊은 증오의 빛이 어려 있던지, 나는 그곳에서 달아나, 백작의 방을 창문으로 빠져나와 다시 성벽을 기어올랐다. 내 방에 돌아온 나는 헐떡이며 침대에 몸을 던지고 생각해 보려 애썼다…….\n\n6월 29일. — 오늘은 내 마지막 편지의 날짜이며, 백작은 그것이 진짜임을 입증할 조치를 취했다. 그가 또다시 같은 창문으로, 내 옷을 입고 성을 떠나는 것을 보았기 때문이다. 그가 도마뱀처럼 벽을 타고 내려갈 때, 나는 총이나 무슨 살상 무기라도 있어서 그를 없애버릴 수 있었으면 했다. 그러나 사람의 손만으로 만들어진 무기로는 그에게 아무런 효력도 없을까 두렵다. 나는 그가 돌아오는 것을 기다려 볼 엄두가 나지 않았다. 그 기괴한 자매들을 볼까 두려웠기 때문이다. 나는 서재로 돌아와, 잠이 들 때까지 거기서 책을 읽었다.\n\n나는 백작 때문에 잠에서 깨었는데, 그는 사람이 지을 수 있는 가장 험상궂은 표정으로 나를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"내일이면, 나의 벗, 우리는 헤어져야 하오. 당신은 당신의 아름다운 영국으로 돌아가고, 나는 어쩌면 다시는 만나지 못할 그런 결말을 맞을지도 모를 어떤 일에 나서야 하오. 당신이 집으로 보내는 편지는 부쳐졌소. 내일이면 나는 여기 없겠으나, 당신의 여정을 위한 모든 준비는 갖추어져 있을 것이오. 아침이면 이곳에 자기들 나름의 일이 있는 즈가니가 오고, 슬로바키아인들도 좀 올 것이오. 그들이 떠나면 내 마차가 당신을 데리러 와서, 부코비나에서 비스트리차로 가는 역마차를 만나도록 보르고 고개까지 당신을 태워다 줄 것이오. 그러나 나는 당신을 드라큘라 성에서 더 보게 되기를 바라고 있소.\" 나는 그를 의심했고, 그의 진심을 시험해 보기로 했다. 진심이라니! 그런 괴물과 결부지어 이 말을 적는다는 것이 그 말에 대한 모독처럼 여겨지나, 어쨌든 나는 단도직입적으로 물었다.\n\n\"어찌하여 오늘 밤에는 갈 수 없는 것이오?\"\n\n\"그건, 친애하는 선생, 내 마부와 말들이 어떤 볼일로 나가 있기 때문이오.\"\n\n\"그러나 나는 기꺼이 걸어가겠소. 당장 떠나고 싶소.\" 그는 미소를 지었는데, 어찌나 부드럽고 매끄러운, 그러나 악마 같은 미소였던지, 나는 그 매끄러움 뒤에 무슨 술책이 숨어 있음을 알았다. 그가 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 당신의 짐은?\"\n\n\"그런 건 상관없소. 나중에 사람을 보내 가져가게 하면 되오.\"\n\n백작은 일어서더니, 어찌나 진실해 보였던지 내 눈을 비비게 할 만큼 달콤한 정중함으로 말했다.\n\n\"당신네 영국인에게는 내 마음에 꼭 드는 격언이 하나 있소. 그 정신이 곧 우리 보야르를 지배하는 것이기 때문이오. '오는 손님은 반기고, 가는 손님은 서둘러 보내라.' 함께 가십시다, 친애하는 나의 젊은 벗이여. 당신의 떠남이 슬프고, 또 당신이 그토록 갑작스레 그것을 바라는 것이 슬프기는 하나, 단 한 시간도 당신을 뜻에 반하여 내 집에 붙들어두지는 않겠소. 갑시다!\" 위엄 있고 묵직한 태도로 그는 램프를 들고 나를 앞장서 계단을 내려가 홀을 따라 걸었다. 그러더니 문득 걸음을 멈추었다.\n\n\"쉿!\"\n\n바로 가까이에서 수많은 늑대들의 울부짖음이 들려왔다. 그것은 마치 그가 손을 들어 올림과 동시에 그 소리가 솟구쳐 오르는 듯, 흡사 큰 관현악단의 음악이 지휘자의 지휘봉 아래 뛰어오르는 것 같았다. 잠시 멈추었다가 그는 그 위엄 있는 태도로 문 쪽으로 나아가, 묵직한 빗장을 뒤로 물리고, 두꺼운 사슬을 풀어, 문을 당겨 열기 시작했다.\n\n나는 그것이 잠겨 있지 않은 것을 보고 몹시 놀랐다. 미심쩍어 사방을 다 둘러보았으나, 어떤 종류의 열쇠도 보이지 않았다.\n\n문이 열리기 시작하자 바깥 늑대들의 울부짖음은 더 크고 더 사납게 변했다. 그것들의 붉은 아가리가, 으드득거리는 이빨과 함께, 그리고 펄쩍 뛰어오를 때의 뭉툭한 발톱 달린 발이, 열리는 문틈으로 들어왔다. 그때 나는 지금 이 순간 백작에게 맞서 발버둥치는 것이 부질없다는 것을 알았다. 이런 것들을 수하로 부리는 그에게 나는 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 그래도 문은 천천히 계속 열려갔고, 그 틈을 막고 선 것은 오직 백작의 몸뚱이뿐이었다. 문득, 어쩌면 이것이 내 파멸의 순간이자 그 방편일지도 모른다는 생각이 머리를 스쳤다. 나는, 그것도 내 스스로의 부추김으로, 늑대들에게 넘겨지려는 것이었다. 이 발상에는 백작에게 어울릴 만큼 악마적인 사악함이 담겨 있었고, 나는 마지막 기회로 외쳤다.\n\n\"문을 닫으시오. 아침까지 기다리겠소!\" 그리고 두 손으로 얼굴을 가려, 쓰디쓴 실망의 눈물을 감추었다. 백작은 그 억센 팔을 한 번 휘둘러 문을 닫았고, 커다란 빗장들이 제자리로 되돌아가며 홀 안을 쾅 하고 울리며 메아리쳤다.\n\n우리는 말없이 서재로 돌아왔고, 한두 분쯤 뒤에 나는 내 방으로 갔다. 드라큘라 백작을 마지막으로 본 것은, 그가 나에게 손에 입을 맞추어 보내던 모습이었다. 두 눈에는 붉은 승리의 빛이 어려 있었고, 입가에는 지옥의 유다라도 자랑스러워했을 법한 미소가 떠올라 있었다.\n\n내 방에 들어가 막 누우려 할 때, 나는 문 밖에서 무언가 속삭이는 소리를 들은 것 같았다. 나는 살그머니 문으로 다가가 귀를 기울였다. 내 귀가 나를 속인 것이 아니라면, 나는 백작의 목소리를 들었다.\n\n\"물러가라, 물러가, 너희 자리로! 너희 때는 아직 오지 않았다. 기다려라! 참아라! 오늘 밤은 내 것이다. 내일 밤은 너희 것이다!\" 낮고 달콤한 웃음소리의 물결이 일었고, 나는 분노에 차 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다. 그러자 밖에는 그 무서운 세 여인이 입술을 핥고 있었다. 내가 나타나자 그들은 다 함께 소름 끼치는 웃음을 터뜨리며 달아났다.\n\n나는 내 방으로 돌아와 무릎을 꿇었다. 그렇다면 끝이 이토록 가까운 것인가? 내일! 내일! 주여, 저를, 그리고 저를 사랑하는 이들을 도우소서!\n\n6월 30일, 아침. — 이것이 내가 이 일기에 적는 마지막 말이 될지도 모른다. 나는 동트기 직전까지 잤고, 깨어나서는 무릎을 꿇었다. 죽음이 찾아온다면, 그것이 나를 채비된 모습으로 발견하게 하리라 마음먹었기 때문이다.\n\n마침내 나는 공기 속의 그 미묘한 변화를 느꼈고, 아침이 왔음을 알았다. 이윽고 반가운 닭 울음소리가 들려왔고, 나는 내가 안전하다고 느꼈다. 기쁜 마음으로 나는 문을 열고 홀로 달려 내려갔다. 문이 잠겨 있지 않은 것을 보아두었던 터라, 이제 탈출이 내 앞에 놓여 있었다. 조바심에 떨리는 손으로 나는 사슬을 풀고 육중한 빗장을 뒤로 물렸다.\n\n그러나 문은 꼼짝도 하지 않았다. 절망이 나를 사로잡았다. 나는 문을 당기고 또 당기며, 그토록 육중한 문이 문틀 안에서 덜컹거릴 때까지 흔들어댔다. 빗장이 걸려 있는 것이 보였다. 내가 백작을 떠난 뒤에 잠긴 것이었다.\n\n그러자 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 그 열쇠를 손에 넣겠다는 격렬한 욕구가 나를 사로잡았고, 나는 그 자리에서 곧장 다시 벽을 기어올라 백작의 방으로 가기로 마음먹었다. 그가 나를 죽일지도 몰랐으나, 이제 죽음은 차라리 더 행복한 쪽의 화(禍)로 여겨졌다. 나는 한순간도 멈추지 않고 동쪽 창문으로 달려가, 전처럼 벽을 기어 내려가 백작의 방으로 들어갔다. 방은 비어 있었으나, 그것은 내가 예상한 대로였다. 어디에서도 열쇠는 보이지 않았으나, 금더미는 그대로 있었다. 나는 구석의 문을 지나 나선 계단을 내려가, 어두운 통로를 따라 그 오래된 예배당으로 갔다. 이제 나는 내가 찾는 그 괴물을 어디서 찾아야 하는지 충분히 잘 알고 있었다.\n\n그 큰 상자는 벽에 바짝 붙은, 같은 자리에 있었으나, 뚜껑이 못으로 박혀 있지는 않은 채 그 위에 덮여만 있었는데, 못은 박아 넣을 채비를 한 듯 제자리에 꽂혀 있었다. 열쇠를 얻으려면 그 몸에 닿아야 한다는 것을 알았기에, 나는 뚜껑을 들어 올려 벽에 기대어 놓았다. 그러자 나는 내 영혼 자체를 공포로 가득 채우는 무언가를 보았다. 거기 백작이 누워 있었는데, 흡사 젊음이 절반쯤 되살아난 것처럼 보였다. 흰 머리칼과 콧수염은 짙은 무쇠빛 회색으로 바뀌었고, 두 뺨은 더 통통해졌으며, 흰 살갗 아래는 루비처럼 붉어 보였다. 입은 어느 때보다 더 붉었으니, 입술 위에 신선한 피가 방울방울 맺혀 입가에서 흘러내려 턱과 목으로 흐르고 있었기 때문이다. 깊고 이글거리는 두 눈마저 부풀어 오른 살 속에 박힌 듯했으니, 눈꺼풀과 그 아래 처진 살이 부어 있었다. 마치 그 끔찍한 괴물 전체가 그저 피로 잔뜩 배불러 있는 듯했다. 그는 포식으로 늘어진 더러운 거머리처럼 누워 있었다. 나는 그를 만지려 몸을 숙이면서 몸서리를 쳤고, 내 안의 모든 감각이 그 접촉에 거부감을 일으켰다. 그러나 나는 뒤져야만 했으니, 그러지 않으면 나는 끝장이었다. 다가오는 밤이면 내 몸뚱이도 그 끔찍한 세 여인의 잔치처럼 비슷한 식으로 잔칫상이 될지도 몰랐다. 나는 그 몸을 구석구석 더듬어 보았으나, 열쇠의 흔적은 찾을 수 없었다. 그러다 나는 멈추고 백작을 바라보았다. 그 부어오른 얼굴에는 조롱하는 미소가 어려 있었는데, 그것은 나를 미치게 만들 것만 같았다. 이자가 바로, 내가 런던으로 옮겨가도록 돕고 있는 그 존재였다. 그곳에 가면 그는 어쩌면 앞으로 수백 년 동안, 그 우글거리는 수백만 명 가운데서 자기의 피에 대한 욕망을 채우고, 무력한 자들에게 빌붙어 살아갈 반(半)악마들의 무리를, 새롭고 끝없이 넓어져 가는 무리를 만들어낼지도 몰랐다. 그 생각만으로도 나는 미칠 것 같았다. 이런 괴물을 세상에서 없애버리고 싶다는 무서운 욕망이 나를 사로잡았다. 손 닿는 곳에 살상 무기는 없었으나, 나는 인부들이 상자에 흙을 채우는 데 쓰던 삽 하나를 움켜잡고는, 그것을 높이 쳐들어 날이 아래로 가게 하여 그 가증스러운 얼굴을 내리쳤다. 그러나 내가 그렇게 하는 순간 머리가 돌아가, 두 눈이 온전히 나에게로 떨어졌는데, 거기에는 바실리스크 같은 공포의 불길이 가득했다. 그 광경에 나는 마비되는 듯했고, 삽은 내 손안에서 비틀려 그 얼굴을 빗맞고 미끄러지면서, 이마 위에 깊은 상처만 하나 냈을 뿐이다. 삽은 내 손에서 떨어져 상자를 가로질러 놓였고, 내가 그것을 빼내려 하자 칼날의 가장자리가 뚜껑의 모서리에 걸려, 뚜껑이 다시 위로 덮이며 그 끔찍한 것을 내 시야에서 가렸다. 마지막으로 흘끗 본 것은 그 부어오른 얼굴이었는데, 피로 얼룩지고, 더없이 깊은 지옥에서도 능히 버텨낼 만한 악의의 히죽거림으로 굳어 있었다.\n\n나는 다음에 무엇을 해야 할지 생각하고 또 생각했으나, 머릿속이 불타는 듯했고, 절망감이 점점 더 나를 뒤덮어 가는 가운데 나는 기다렸다. 그렇게 기다리는데, 멀리서 흥겨운 목소리들이 부르는 집시 노래가 점점 가까이 다가오는 것이 들렸고, 그 노랫소리 사이로 무거운 바퀴가 굴러오는 소리와 채찍이 갈라지는 소리가 들렸다. 백작이 말한 그 즈가니와 슬로바키아인들이 오고 있는 것이었다. 마지막으로 주위를, 그리고 그 비열한 몸뚱이가 든 상자를 한 번 둘러본 뒤, 나는 그곳에서 달아나 백작의 방으로 들어가서는, 문이 열리는 순간 뛰쳐나가기로 마음먹었다. 나는 귀를 곤두세우고 들었고, 아래층에서 커다란 자물쇠에 열쇠가 갈리는 소리와 묵직한 문이 열려 젖혀지는 소리를 들었다. 무언가 다른 출입 방도가 있었거나, 아니면 누군가가 잠긴 문들 가운데 하나의 열쇠를 가지고 있었던 것이리라. 그러고는 여러 발이 쿵쿵 짓밟으며 걸어가다가, 쩌렁쩌렁한 메아리를 울려 보내는 어떤 통로에서 잦아드는 소리가 들렸다. 나는 다시 지하 납골소 쪽으로 달려 내려가, 그 새로운 입구를 찾아보려고 돌아섰다. 그런데 바로 그 순간 거센 바람이 한 차례 훅 불어오는 듯하더니, 나선 계단으로 통하는 문이 쾅 하고 닫혀, 그 충격으로 상인방(上引枋)에서 먼지가 풀썩 날렸다. 그것을 밀어 열려고 달려갔으나, 그것은 어찌해 볼 도리 없이 단단히 닫혀 있었다. 나는 또다시 죄수가 되었고, 파멸의 그물이 점점 더 바짝 나를 옥죄어 오고 있었다.\n\n이 글을 쓰는 지금, 아래 통로에서 여러 발이 쿵쿵 짓밟는 소리와 무거운 것들이 둔중하게 내려놓이는 소리가 들린다. 분명 흙을 실은 그 상자들이리라. 망치질 소리가 들린다. 상자에 못을 박는 소리다. 이제 무거운 발들이 다시 홀을 따라 쿵쿵 걸어가는 소리가 들리고, 그 뒤로 빈둥거리는 여러 다른 발들이 따라오는 소리도 들린다.\n\n문이 닫히고, 사슬이 절그럭거린다. 자물쇠에 열쇠가 갈리고, 열쇠를 빼는 소리가 들린다. 그러더니 또 다른 문이 열렸다 닫히고, 자물쇠와 빗장이 삐걱대는 소리가 들린다.\n\n쉿! 안마당에서, 그리고 바위투성이 길 아래로, 무거운 바퀴가 굴러가는 소리, 채찍이 갈라지는 소리, 그리고 멀리 지나가는 즈가니의 합창 소리가 들린다.\n\n나는 그 끔찍한 여인들과 함께 성에 홀로 남았다. 윽! 미나도 여자이나, 그들과는 어떤 공통점도 없다. 그들은 구렁텅이에서 온 악마들이다!\n\n나는 그들과 단둘이 남아 있지는 않겠다. 지금까지 시도한 것보다 더 멀리 성벽을 기어 내려가 보겠다. 나중에 필요할지 모르니 금을 좀 가지고 가겠다. 어쩌면 이 끔찍한 곳에서 빠져나갈 길을 찾을지도 모른다.\n\n그리고 그다음엔 집으로! 가장 빠르고 가장 가까운 기차로! 이 저주받은 곳에서, 이 저주받은 땅에서, 악마와 그 자식들이 아직도 지상의 발로 걸어 다니는 이곳에서 벗어나는 것이다!\n\n적어도 하느님의 자비가 이 괴물들의 자비보다는 나으며, 그 벼랑은 가파르고 높다. 그 기슭에서라면 사람은 잠들 수 있으리라 — 사람으로서. 모두들 잘 있으오! 미나여!\n\n「미나 머리 양이 루시 웨스텐라 양에게 보내는 편지」\n「5월 9일.\n\n「나의 가장 사랑하는 루시에게.—\n「편지가 이리 늦어진 것을 용서해줘. 일에 그야말로 파묻혀 지냈단다. 보조 여교사의 삶이란 때로 고된 법이지. 나는 너와 함께, 그것도 바닷가에서, 마음껏 이야기를 나누며 허황된 공중누각을 지어보고 싶어 견딜 수가 없어. 요즘 무척 열심히 공부하고 있는데, 조너선의 학업을 따라가고 싶은 까닭이야. 그래서 속기를 아주 부지런히 연습하고 있지. 우리가 결혼하면 나는 조너선에게 도움이 될 수 있을 테고, 속기를 충분히 잘하게 되면 그가 하고 싶은 말을 이런 식으로 받아 적었다가 타자기로 옮겨 적어줄 수 있을 거야. 타자기도 아주 열심히 연습하고 있단다. 그와 나는 가끔 속기로 편지를 주고받는데, 그는 외국 여행기를 속기로 일기에 적고 있어. 너와 함께 있게 되면 나도 그런 식으로 일기를 쓰려고 해. 일요일을 한구석에 욱여넣은, 한 주에 두 쪽짜리 그런 일기 말고, 마음이 내킬 때면 언제든 쓸 수 있는 그런 일지 말이야. 다른 사람들에게 흥미로울 만한 것은 별로 없을 거라고 생각하지만, 어차피 그들을 위한 것이 아니니까. 함께 나눌 만한 무언가가 그 안에 있다면 언젠가 조너선에게 보여줄지도 모르지만, 사실은 연습장 같은 거야. 나는 여기자들이 하는 것을 따라 해볼 생각이야. 인터뷰를 하고, 묘사를 적고, 나눈 대화를 기억해내려 애쓰는 거지. 조금만 연습하면 하루 동안 일어난 일이나 들은 말을 모두 기억할 수 있다고 하더라. 어쨌든 두고 보면 알겠지. 우리가 만나면 내 자그마한 계획들을 들려줄게. 방금 조너선에게서 트란실바니아발 짤막한 몇 줄을 급히 받았어. 그는 잘 지내고 있고, 일주일쯤 뒤면 돌아온대. 그의 소식을 모두 듣고 싶어 견딜 수가 없어. 낯선 나라들을 보는 건 참 멋진 일이겠지. 우리가—그러니까 조너선과 내가—언젠가 함께 그곳들을 보게 될까. 마침 열 시를 알리는 종이 울리네. 잘 있어.\n\n「너를 사랑하는\n「미나.\n\n「편지 쓸 때 모든 소식을 다 들려줘. 오랫동안 아무것도 말해주지 않았잖아. 소문이 들리는데, 특히 키 크고 잘생긴 곱슬머리 남자에 관한 소문이…???」\n「루시 웨스텐라가 미나 머리에게 보내는 편지.」\n\n「채텀 가 17번지.\n\n「수요일.\n\n「나의 가장 사랑하는 미나에게.—\n\n「네가 나더러 편지를 잘 안 쓴다고 몹시도 부당하게 몰아세운다는 말을 꼭 해야겠어. 우리가 헤어진 뒤로 나는 너에게 두 번이나 편지를 썼는데, 네가 마지막으로 보낸 건 겨우 두 번째 편지였잖아. 게다가 나는 들려줄 이야기가 없어. 정말이지 너에게 흥미로울 만한 게 하나도 없단다. 요즘 도회지는 무척 즐겁고, 우리는 그림 전시관에도 자주 가고 공원으로 산책이며 승마도 많이 다녀. 그 키 크고 곱슬머리 남자로 말하자면, 지난번 팝 음악회 때 나와 함께 있던 사람일 거야. 누군가가 분명 소문을 퍼뜨렸나 봐. 그건 홈우드 씨였어. 그는 우리를 보러 자주 오는데, 그와 엄마는 아주 사이가 좋아. 함께 이야기 나눌 거리가 무척 많거든. 얼마 전에 우리는 한 남자를 만났는데, 네가 이미 조너선과 약혼한 사이만 아니었다면 꼭 너에게 어울릴 사람이야. 그는 더없이 훌륭한 신랑감이거든. 잘생기고, 형편도 넉넉하고, 가문도 좋아. 의사인데 정말로 영리하단다. 글쎄 상상해봐! 그는 겨우 스물아홉인데, 거대한 정신병원을 혼자서 도맡아 관리하고 있어. 홈우드 씨가 그를 나에게 소개해주었는데, 그가 우리를 보러 여기까지 찾아왔고, 이제는 자주 온단다. 내가 본 사람 가운데 가장 결단력 있으면서도 가장 침착한 사람인 것 같아. 그는 도무지 흔들리는 법이 없어 보여. 그가 환자들에게 얼마나 놀라운 힘을 발휘할지 상상이 가. 그에게는 사람의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보는 묘한 버릇이 있는데, 마치 그 속내를 읽어내려는 것 같아. 나에게도 이걸 자주 시도하는데, 내심 나는 그가 만만찮은 상대를 만났다고 자부하고 있어. 그건 내 거울을 보면 알지. 너는 네 얼굴을 읽어보려 한 적이 있니? 나는 해봐. 분명히 말하건대 그건 꽤 괜찮은 공부거리이고, 한 번도 해보지 않았다면 짐작도 못할 만큼 너를 애먹인단다. 그는 내가 자기에게 흥미로운 심리학적 연구 대상이라고 하는데, 변변치 못하나마 나도 그런 것 같아. 너도 알다시피 나는 새로운 유행을 묘사할 만큼 옷차림에 충분히 관심이 없어. 옷이란 따분한 것이지. 또 속어를 썼네, 그래도 상관없어. 아서는 매일 그 말을 하니까. 자, 다 털어놓았네. 미나, 우리는 어린아이였던 시절부터 서로에게 모든 비밀을 다 말해왔지. 함께 자고 함께 먹고, 함께 웃고 함께 울었잖아. 그런데 이렇게 말을 꺼내고 보니 더 말하고 싶어. 아, 미나, 너는 짐작 못하겠니? 나는 그를 사랑해. 이 글을 쓰면서도 얼굴이 붉어지는데, 그도 나를 사랑한다고 생각은 하지만 그가 말로 그렇게 표현한 적은 없거든. 하지만 아, 미나, 나는 그를 사랑해. 그를 사랑해. 그를 사랑해! 자, 이렇게 말하니 마음이 한결 가볍네. 네가 곁에 있다면 좋을 텐데. 예전에 그러던 것처럼 난롯가에 앉아 옷을 벗으며, 내가 느끼는 것을 너에게 들려주려 애써볼 텐데. 너에게조차 이걸 어떻게 쓰고 있는지 나도 모르겠어. 멈추기가 두려워. 멈추면 이 편지를 찢어버릴 것 같거든. 그리고 멈추고 싶지도 않아, 너에게 모든 걸 정말이지 너무도 말하고 싶으니까. 당장 답장 줘. 그리고 이 일을 어떻게 생각하는지 다 말해줘. 미나, 이제 그만 써야겠어. 잘 자. 기도할 때 나를 축복해줘. 그리고 미나, 내 행복을 위해 기도해줘.\n「루시.\n\n「추신.—이건 비밀이라는 말은 굳이 안 해도 알겠지. 다시 한번 잘 자.\n\n「엘.」\n\n「루시 웨스텐라가 미나 머리에게 보내는 편지.」\n\n「5월 24일.\n「나의 가장 사랑하는 미나에게.—\n\n「고마워, 고마워, 다시 한번 고마워, 그 다정한 편지를 보내줘서. 너에게 털어놓을 수 있고 또 네 위로를 받을 수 있어서 정말 좋았어.\n「얘, 비가 왔다 하면 퍼붓는다더니. 옛 속담이 어찌나 들어맞는지. 나는 9월이면 스무 살이 되는데도 오늘까지 한 번도 청혼을 받아본 적이 없었어, 진짜 청혼 말이야. 그런데 오늘 세 번이나 받았단다. 글쎄 상상해봐! 하루에 청혼을 세 번이라니! 끔찍하지 않니! 그 가엾은 두 사람에게는 정말로, 진정으로 미안한 마음이야. 아, 미나, 나는 너무 행복해서 어쩔 줄을 모르겠어. 게다가 청혼을 세 번이라니! 하지만 제발, 다른 여자애들한테는 한마디도 하지 마. 안 그러면 별별 터무니없는 생각을 다 하면서, 집에 돌아온 첫날에 적어도 여섯 번은 받지 못하면 자기가 상처받고 무시당했다고 여길 테니까. 어떤 여자애들은 어찌나 허영심이 많은지! 약혼해서 곧 차분한 노부인네로 자리잡을 너와 나, 사랑하는 미나야, 우리는 허영 따위는 경멸할 수 있지. 자, 그 세 사람 이야기를 들려줘야겠는데, 너는 이걸 비밀로 지켜야 해, 얘, 모든 사람에게, 물론 조너선만 빼고 말이야. 너는 그에게 말하겠지. 내가 네 처지라면 분명 아서에게 말할 테니까. 여자는 남편에게 모든 걸 말해야 하잖아—그렇게 생각하지 않니, 얘?—나도 공정해야겠지. 남자들은 여자가, 분명 자기 아내가, 자기들만큼 떳떳하기를 바라는데, 여자들은 안타깝게도 늘 마땅한 만큼 떳떳하지는 못한 것 같아. 자, 얘, 첫 번째 사람은 점심 직전에 왔어. 내가 너에게 말했던 그 사람, 정신병원을 맡고 있는 존 수어드 박사야. 억센 턱과 멋진 이마를 가진. 그는 겉으로는 무척 침착했지만 그래도 긴장하고 있었어. 그는 분명 온갖 자잘한 것들을 미리 연습해두고 잊지 않고 있었지. 그런데 하마터면 자기 실크해트를 깔고 앉을 뻔했는데, 침착한 남자라면 보통 그러지 않는 법이거든. 그러고는 편안해 보이려 할 때 외과용 칼을 만지작거리는 통에 나는 하마터면 비명을 지를 뻔했어. 그는 나에게, 미나, 아주 솔직하게 말했어. 비록 나를 안 지는 얼마 되지 않았지만 내가 자기에게 얼마나 소중한지, 내가 곁에서 돕고 격려해준다면 자기 삶이 어떠할지를 들려주었지. 내가 자기를 마음에 두지 않으면 자기가 얼마나 불행할지를 말하려 했는데, 내가 우는 걸 보더니 자기는 짐승만도 못한 사람이라며 지금 내 괴로움에 더 보태지 않겠다고 하더라. 그러고는 말을 멈추고, 언젠가 자기를 사랑할 수 있겠느냐고 물었어. 내가 고개를 젓자 그의 두 손이 떨렸고, 그러더니 다소 머뭇거리며 이미 다른 누군가를 마음에 두고 있느냐고 묻더라. 그는 그걸 아주 점잖게 표현했어. 내 속내를 억지로 짜내려는 게 아니라 그저 알고 싶을 뿐이라며, 여자의 마음이 자유롭다면 남자도 희망을 품을 수 있으니까, 라고 말이야. 그래서, 미나, 나는 그에게 어떤 사람이 있다고 말해야 한다는 일종의 의무감을 느꼈어. 딱 그만큼만 말했더니, 그는 일어서서 내 두 손을 자기 두 손에 감싸쥐고는, 무척 단단하고 무척 엄숙한 얼굴로, 내가 행복하기를 바란다고, 그리고 내가 언젠가 벗이 필요하거든 자기를 가장 좋은 벗 가운데 하나로 여겨달라고 말했어. 아, 사랑하는 미나, 나는 울지 않을 수가 없어. 이 편지가 온통 얼룩진 걸 부디 양해해줘. 청혼을 받는 건 다 좋고 그런 멋진 일이지만, 너를 진심으로 사랑하는 줄 아는 가엾은 사람이 가슴이 온통 찢어진 얼굴로 떠나가는 것을 지켜보아야 하고, 또 그가 그 순간에 무슨 말을 하든 너는 그의 삶에서 완전히 사라져가고 있다는 걸 알아야 하는 건 결코 행복한 일이 아니야. 얘, 지금은 여기서 그만 써야겠어. 이렇게 행복한데도 너무 비참한 기분이 들어.\n\n「저녁.\n\n「아서가 방금 떠났는데, 글을 멈추었을 때보다 기분이 한결 나아져서 그날 이야기를 계속 들려줄 수 있겠어. 자, 얘, 두 번째 사람은 점심 뒤에 왔어. 그는 정말 멋진 사람인데, 텍사스에서 온 미국인이야. 어찌나 젊고 싱싱해 보이는지, 그가 그토록 많은 곳을 다니고 그토록 많은 모험을 했다는 게 거의 믿기지 않을 정도야. 나는 가엾은 데스데모나가, 비록 흑인의 입에서 나온 것일망정, 그토록 위태로운 이야기들을 귓속에 쏟아부음받았을 때의 심정에 공감이 가. 우리 여자들은 어찌나 겁이 많은지, 남자가 우리를 두려움에서 구해주리라 여기고 그와 결혼하는 것 같아. 내가 만약 남자여서 한 여자가 나를 사랑하게 만들고 싶다면 어떻게 할지 이제 알겠어. 아니, 모르겠어. 모리스 씨가 우리에게 자기 이야기들을 들려준 데 반해, 아서는 단 하나도 들려준 적이 없는데도 그런데도—얘, 내가 좀 앞질러 갔네. 퀸시 피 모리스 씨는 나를 혼자 있는 데서 발견했어. 남자란 늘 여자가 혼자 있을 때를 잘도 찾아내는 것 같아. 아니, 그렇지 않아. 아서는 두 번이나 기회를 만들려고 애썼고, 나도 할 수 있는 한 그를 도왔으니까. 이제는 그 말을 하기가 부끄럽지 않아. 미리 말해두어야겠는데, 모리스 씨가 늘 속어를 쓰는 건 아니야—그러니까 낯선 사람들에게나 그들 앞에서는 결코 그러지 않아. 사실 교육을 잘 받았고 예의범절도 더없이 훌륭한 사람이거든—그런데 내가 자기의 미국식 속어를 듣는 걸 재미있어한다는 걸 알아채고는, 내가 곁에 있고 또 놀랄 사람이 아무도 없을 때면 그렇게 우스운 말들을 하더라. 얘, 안타깝지만 그는 그걸 죄다 지어내는 게 분명해. 무슨 말을 하든 거기에 정확히 들어맞으니 말이야. 하지만 속어란 원래 그런 거지. 나 자신도 언젠가 속어를 쓰게 될지 모르겠어. 아서가 그걸 좋아하는지도 모르겠고, 그가 아직껏 속어를 쓰는 걸 들어본 적이 없으니까. 자, 모리스 씨가 내 곁에 앉아 한껏 행복하고 유쾌해 보이려 애썼지만, 그래도 나는 그가 무척 긴장한 걸 알아볼 수 있었어. 그는 내 손을 자기 손에 쥐고 더없이 다정하게 말했어.—\n\n「'루시 양, 나는 당신의 그 자그마한 신발의 끈 매무새를 다듬어드릴 자격조차 없는 사람인 줄 압니다. 하지만 그럴 자격이 있는 남자를 찾을 때까지 기다리신다면, 당신은 결국 등불을 든 그 일곱 처녀들 곁으로 가게 되고 말 겁니다. 그러지 마시고 그냥 나와 짝을 지어, 두 마리가 한 멍에를 메고 마차를 몰듯, 함께 그 긴 길을 가지 않으시겠습니까?'\n\n「자, 그가 어찌나 사람 좋고 유쾌해 보였던지, 가엾은 수어드 박사를 거절할 때만큼 거절하기가 어렵게 느껴지지는 않았어. 그래서 나는 되도록 가볍게, 나는 멍에를 메는 것에 대해선 아무것도 모르고, 아직 마구에 길들지도 전혀 않았다고 말했어. 그러자 그는 자기가 가벼운 투로 말했다고, 자기로서는 이토록 엄숙하고 이토록 중대한 자리에서 그런 잘못을 저질렀다면 부디 용서해달라고 했어. 그 말을 할 때 그는 정말로 진지해 보였고, 나도 덩달아 조금 진지해지지 않을 수 없었어—미나, 나를 끔찍한 바람둥이로 여기겠지—그래도 그가 하루에 두 번째 청혼자라는 데에 일종의 우쭐함을 느끼지 않을 수 없었지. 그러더니, 얘, 내가 한마디 꺼내기도 전에 그는 완벽한 사랑 고백의 격류를 쏟아내기 시작했어. 자기 심장과 영혼을 통째로 내 발치에 내려놓듯이 말이야. 그가 그것을 너무도 진지하게 하는 통에, 나는 다시는 남자가 늘 장난스러워야 하고, 가끔 유쾌하다고 해서 결코 진지할 수 없다고는 여기지 않게 될 거야. 내 얼굴에서 무언가를 본 모양으로 그는 갑자기 멈추더니, 내가 자유로운 몸이었다면 그것만으로도 사랑할 수 있었을 그런 사내다운 열정으로 말했어.—\n\n「'루시, 당신은 마음이 정직한 분이에요, 나는 알아요. 당신의 영혼 가장 깊은 곳까지 그야말로 진국이라고 믿지 않았다면, 나는 지금처럼 이렇게 당신에게 말하고 있지 않을 겁니다. 한 좋은 벗이 다른 벗에게 묻듯이 말해주세요. 당신이 마음에 둔 다른 누군가가 있습니까? 만약 있다면 나는 다시는 당신을 터럭만큼도 괴롭히지 않고, 당신이 허락한다면, 더없이 충실한 벗이 되겠습니다.'\n\n「사랑하는 미나야, 우리 여자들은 그들에게 그토록 어울리지 않는데 남자들은 어찌 이리 고결할까? 나는 이 큰 마음을 지닌 참된 신사를 거의 놀리다시피 하고 있었던 거야. 나는 울음을 터뜨렸어—얘, 이 편지가 여러모로 정말 칠칠치 못한 편지라고 여기겠지—나는 정말로 마음이 몹시 안 좋았어. 어째서 한 여자가 세 남자, 아니 자기를 원하는 만큼 많은 남자와 결혼해서 이 모든 괴로움을 덜 수는 없는 걸까? 하지만 이건 이단 같은 소리니 입에 담아선 안 되겠지. 다행히도 나는 울면서도 모리스 씨의 용감한 눈을 바라볼 수 있었고, 그에게 솔직하게 말했어.—\n\n「'네, 내가 사랑하는 사람이 있어요. 비록 그는 아직 나를 사랑한다는 말조차 해준 적이 없지만요.' 그렇게 솔직히 말하기를 잘했어. 그의 얼굴에 환한 빛이 어리더니, 그가 두 손을 내밀어 내 손을 잡았거든—내가 그의 손에 내 손을 맡긴 것 같기도 해—그러고는 진심 어린 투로 말했어.—\n\n「'역시 내 용감한 아가씨로군요. 당신을 얻을 기회를 위해 늦는 편이, 세상 어떤 다른 여자에게 제때 닿는 것보다 더 값진 일이지요. 울지 마세요, 그대여. 나 때문이라면, 나는 만만찮은 사내랍니다. 나는 이걸 똑바로 서서 받아들이겠어요. 만약 그 다른 친구가 자기 행복을 모른다면, 글쎄, 어서 그걸 찾아보는 게 좋을 거예요. 안 그러면 나를 상대해야 할 테니까. 아가씨, 당신의 정직함과 용기가 나를 벗으로 만들었어요. 그건 연인보다 더 드문 것이고, 어쨌든 더 욕심 없는 것이지요. 그대여, 나는 이제부터 저세상에 갈 때까지 꽤나 외로운 길을 걷게 되겠지요. 입맞춤 한 번만 해주지 않겠어요? 이따금 어둠을 물리치는 데 도움이 될 무언가가 될 테니까요. 당신은 그래도 돼요, 알다시피, 원한다면요. 그 다른 좋은 친구는—그는 좋은 사람일 거예요, 그대여, 그것도 훌륭한 사람일 거예요. 안 그러면 당신이 그를 사랑할 리 없으니까요—아직 말을 꺼내지도 않았으니까요.' 그 말에 나는 완전히 마음을 빼앗기고 말았어, 미나. 그건 정말이지 용감하고 다정한 일이었고, 게다가 연적에게마저 고결한 일이었거든—그렇지 않니?—게다가 그는 그토록 슬퍼하고 있었지. 그래서 나는 몸을 기울여 그에게 입을 맞추었어. 그는 내 두 손을 자기 손에 쥐고 일어서서, 내 얼굴을 내려다보며—나는 무척 얼굴을 붉히고 있었던 것 같아—이렇게 말했어.—\n\n「'아가씨, 내가 당신의 손을 잡았고, 당신이 나에게 입을 맞추었으니, 이것들이 우리를 벗으로 만들지 못한다면 그 무엇도 결코 그러지 못할 거예요. 나에게 베풀어준 당신의 그 다정한 정직함에 감사해요. 그럼 안녕히.' 그는 내 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 모자를 집어 들고, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고, 눈물 한 방울도 떨림도 멈칫거림도 없이 곧장 방을 나갔어. 그리고 나는 어린아이처럼 울고 있어. 아, 그가 밟고 다니는 땅마저 떠받들 여자애들이 사방에 널렸는데, 어째서 그런 남자가 불행해져야 하는 걸까? 내가 자유로운 몸이라면 나도 그럴 텐데—다만 나는 자유로워지고 싶지 않은 거야. 얘, 이 일로 나는 마음이 온통 헝클어져서, 너에게 행복을 이야기한 직후에 곧바로 또 행복을 쓸 수는 없을 것 같아. 그리고 세 번째 사람 이야기는 모든 게 다 행복해질 수 있을 때까지는 말하고 싶지 않아.\n\n「언제까지나 너를 사랑하는\n\n「루시.\n「추신.—아, 세 번째 사람에 대해서는—굳이 세 번째 사람 이야기를 들려줄 필요는 없겠지? 게다가 모든 게 너무 정신없었어. 그가 방에 들어온 순간부터 그의 두 팔이 나를 감싸안고 그가 나에게 입을 맞추기까지가 그저 한순간 같았으니까. 나는 정말, 정말 행복해. 내가 무슨 일을 했기에 이런 자격이 생겼는지 모르겠어. 다만 앞으로는 나에게 이런 연인을, 이런 남편을, 이런 벗을 보내주신 하느님의 그 모든 은총에 내가 감사할 줄 모르는 사람이 아님을 보여드리려 애쓸 수밖에.\n\n「그럼 안녕.」\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기.」\n\n(축음기로 기록함)\n\n5월 25일.—오늘은 식욕이 썰물처럼 빠져나갔다. 먹을 수도, 쉴 수도 없으니, 대신 일기를 쓴다. 어제 거절당한 뒤로 일종의 공허한 느낌이 든다. 세상 그 무엇도 할 만한 가치가 있을 만큼 충분히 중요해 보이지 않는다.…… 이런 종류의 일에 유일한 치료법이 일이라는 것을 알기에, 나는 환자들에게로 내려갔다. 나는 무척 흥미로운 연구거리가 되어준 한 사람을 골라냈다. 그가 어찌나 기묘한지, 나는 그를 되도록 잘 이해해보기로 마음먹었다. 오늘은 그 어느 때보다 그의 수수께끼의 핵심에 가까이 다가간 것 같았다.\n\n나는 그의 환각에 관한 사실들을 파악하기 위해, 그 어느 때보다 더 충분히 그에게 질문했다. 지금 와서 보니, 그렇게 하는 나의 태도에는 어딘가 잔인한 구석이 있었다. 나는 그를 그의 광기의 요점에 붙들어두려는 것 같았는데—이는 내가 환자들을 대할 때 지옥의 구렁을 피하듯 피하려 하는 일이었다.\n\n(비망록. 어떤 상황에서라면 나는 지옥의 구렁을 피하지 않으려 할까?) 옴니아 로마이 베날리아 순트. 지옥에도 값이 있다! 현명한 자에게는 한마디면 족하다. 만일 이 본능 뒤에 무언가가 있다면 나중에 그것을 정확히 추적해 보는 일이 가치 있으리라. 그러니 지금부터 그렇게 시작하는 편이 낫겠다. 따라서--\n\n알 엠 렌필드, 59세.—다혈질의 기질, 대단한 체력, 병적인 흥분성, 침울한 시기를 거쳐 내가 종잡을 수 없는 어떤 고정관념으로 끝맺음. 짐작건대 이 다혈질의 기질 자체와 교란하는 영향이 정신적으로 완성된 결말로 이어지는 듯하다. 위험할 수 있는 인물, 사심이 없다면 아마 위험한 인물. 사심이 있는 자들의 경우, 신중함은 그들 자신에게만큼이나 적들에게도 든든한 갑옷이 된다. 이 점에 관해 내가 생각하는 바는 이렇다. 자아가 그 고정점일 때는 구심력과 원심력이 균형을 이루지만, 의무나 어떤 대의 따위가 고정점일 때는 후자의 힘이 압도적이어서, 오직 우연이나 일련의 우연들만이 그것을 상쇄할 수 있다.\n「퀸시 피 모리스가 아서 홈우드 경에게 보내는 편지.」\n\n「5월 25일.\n\n「친애하는 아트에게.—\n\n「우리는 대초원의 모닥불 가에서 이야기를 나누었고, 마르키즈 제도에 상륙을 시도하다 서로의 상처를 싸매주었으며, 티티카카 호숫가에서 건배를 들었지. 아직 나눌 이야기가 더 있고, 치료할 상처가 더 있고, 들 건배가 또 하나 남았네. 내일 밤 내 모닥불 가에서 그렇게 하지 않으려나? 자네에게 청하는 데 망설임이 없는 것은, 어떤 숙녀분께서 어떤 만찬 약속이 있고 그래서 자네가 자유로운 몸이라는 걸 알기 때문이지. 다른 한 사람이 더 올 텐데, 코리아 호 시절의 옛 동료인 잭 수어드라네. 그도 올 거야. 우리 둘 다 술잔을 사이에 두고 함께 눈물을 섞고 싶고, 또 온 마음을 다해, 하느님이 빚으신 가장 고결하고 가장 얻을 가치 있는 마음을 얻어낸, 이 드넓은 세상에서 가장 행복한 남자를 위해 건배를 들고 싶으니까. 우리는 자네에게 진심 어린 환영과 다정한 인사, 그리고 자네 오른손만큼이나 진실된 축배를 약속하네. 자네가 어떤 한 쌍의 눈동자를 위해 너무 깊이 들이켠다면, 우리 둘 다 자네를 집에 두고 가겠노라 맹세하지. 오게나!\n\n「언제까지나 변함없이 자네의 벗,\n\n「퀸시 피 모리스.」\n「아서 홈우드가 퀸시 피 모리스에게 보내는 전보.」\n\n「5월 26일.\n\n「언제든 나를 끼워주게. 자네들 두 사람의 귀가 다 얼얼해질 소식을 전하러 가겠네.\n「아트.」\n\n# MINA MURRAY’S JOURNAL\n\n「미나 머리의 일기」\n7월 24일. 휘트비. — 루시가 역으로 마중을 나왔는데, 그 어느 때보다도 더 사랑스럽고 어여뻐 보였다. 우리는 크레슨트에 있는, 그들이 방을 얻어 묵고 있는 집으로 마차를 타고 올라갔다. 이곳은 아름다운 곳이다. 작은 강 에스크가 깊은 골짜기 사이로 흐르는데, 항구에 가까워질수록 골짜기가 넓어진다. 거대한 고가교가 높은 교각을 세우고 가로질러 놓여 있는데, 그 너머로 보이는 풍경은 어쩐지 실제보다 더 멀어 보인다. 골짜기는 아름답게 푸르고, 어찌나 가파른지 양편의 높은 땅에 서면 골짜기를 곧장 가로질러 건너편이 보인다. 아래를 들여다볼 만큼 가까이 다가가지 않는 한은 그렇다. 우리 쪽 반대편에 있는 옛 시가지의 집들은 모두 붉은 지붕이고, 마치 우리가 그림에서 보는 뉘른베르크처럼 아무렇게나 차곡차곡 포개어 쌓아 올린 듯하다. 시가지 바로 위에는 휘트비 수도원의 폐허가 있는데, 이곳은 덴마크인들에게 약탈당한 곳이며, 「마미언」의 일부 무대가 된 곳으로, 거기에서 한 소녀가 벽 속에 산 채로 갇혔다고 한다. 그것은 더없이 장엄한 폐허로, 규모가 어마어마하며 아름답고 낭만적인 정취가 가득하다. 창문 가운데 하나에서 흰옷 입은 여인이 보인다는 전설도 있다. 그 폐허와 시가지 사이에는 또 다른 교회, 곧 교구 교회가 있고, 그 둘레에는 비석으로 가득한 큰 묘지가 있다. 내 생각에는 이곳이 휘트비에서 가장 좋은 자리인데, 시가지 바로 위에 자리잡고 있어 항구가 한눈에 들어오고, 케틀네스라 부르는 곶이 바다로 뻗어나간 곳까지 만(灣) 전체가 다 보이기 때문이다. 항구 위로 어찌나 가파르게 내리뻗었는지 둑의 일부가 무너져내렸고, 무덤 몇 기는 부서져버렸다. 한 곳에서는 무덤의 석조물 일부가 저 멀리 아래의 모래 오솔길 위로 비죽 튀어나와 있다. 교회 묘지에는 옆에 의자를 둔 산책로가 나 있는데, 사람들은 온종일 그곳에 앉아 아름다운 경치를 바라보며 바람을 즐긴다. 나도 이곳에 자주 와서 앉아 일을 할 것이다. 사실 지금도 무릎에 공책을 올려놓고 글을 쓰면서, 내 옆에 앉은 노인 셋이 나누는 이야기에 귀를 기울이고 있다. 그들은 온종일 여기 앉아 이야기하는 것 말고는 달리 하는 일이 없는 듯하다.\n\n항구는 내 발치 아래에 펼쳐져 있는데, 저편에는 화강암으로 쌓은 긴 방파제 하나가 바다로 뻗어나가 끝에서 바깥쪽으로 휘어져 있고, 그 휘어진 한가운데에 등대가 서 있다. 그 바깥쪽으로는 묵직한 방파제가 나란히 뻗어 있다. 이쪽 편에서는 방파제가 반대 방향으로 팔꿈치처럼 꺾여 있고, 그 끝에도 등대가 하나 있다. 두 방파제 사이로 항구로 들어가는 좁은 입구가 있는데, 그 안으로 들어서면 항구가 갑자기 넓어진다.\n\n만조 때는 보기 좋지만, 썰물이 빠지면 물이 얕아져 거의 사라지고, 그저 모래톱 사이를 흐르는 에스크 강의 물줄기와 군데군데 바위만 남는다. 이쪽 편 항구 바깥으로는 반 마일쯤에 걸쳐 큰 암초가 솟아 있는데, 그 날카로운 가장자리가 남쪽 등대 뒤에서 곧장 바다로 뻗어나가 있다. 그 끝에는 종이 달린 부표가 있어, 날씨가 사나우면 흔들리며 바람결에 구슬픈 소리를 실어 보낸다. 이곳에는 배가 난파하면 먼바다에서 종소리가 들린다는 전설이 있다. 이것에 대해 저 노인에게 물어봐야겠다. 마침 그가 이쪽으로 오고 있다…….\n\n그는 우스꽝스러운 노인이다. 얼굴이 온통 나무껍질처럼 옹이지고 비틀려 있는 걸 보면 무척 늙었음이 틀림없다. 그는 자기가 거의 백 살이 다 되었으며, 워털루 전투가 벌어졌을 때 그린란드 어선단에서 선원으로 일했다고 한다. 안타깝게도 그는 매우 회의적인 사람인데, 내가 바다의 종소리와 수도원의 흰옷 입은 여인에 대해 묻자 그는 아주 퉁명스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"그딴 거 신경 안 쓸라우, 아가씨. 그런 것들은 죄다 한물간 거지. 명심허우, 그런 게 아주 없었다는 게 아니라, 내가 살아온 시절엔 없었다는 거유. 그런 건 외지에서 온 사람이나 놀러온 사람들, 뭐 그런 부류한테나 그럴듯헌 거지, 아가씨처럼 곱상한 젊은 여자한테 할 소리는 아니유. 요크나 리즈에서 걸어 들어온 그 작자들, 늘 절인 청어나 처먹고 차나 들이켜고 싸구려 흑옥(黑玉)이나 사려고 두리번거리는 그런 작자들이야 뭐든 곧이듣겠지. 누가 일부러 그들헌테 거짓말을 해줄 생각을 다 헐꼬, 나도 참 궁금허네 — 신문조차도, 바보 같은 소리로 가득한 그 신문조차도 말이여.\" 나는 그가 흥미로운 이야기를 들려줄 만한 사람이겠거니 싶어, 옛날의 고래잡이에 대해 이야기를 좀 해주지 않겠느냐고 물었다. 그가 막 이야기를 시작하려고 자세를 고쳐 앉는데 시계가 여섯 시를 알렸고, 그러자 그는 끙끙대며 몸을 일으키더니 말했다.\n\n\"이제 집으로 돌아가야겠수, 아가씨. 내 손녀딸이 차 준비가 다 됐는데 날 기다리게 하는 걸 안 좋아해서 말이여. 나야 계단을 기어오르는 데 시간이 한참 걸리니까, 그 계단이 어찌나 많은지. 그리구 아가씨, 시계로 보면 난 뱃속 양식이 영 부족허단 말이여.\"\n\n그는 절뚝거리며 떠났고, 나는 그가 할 수 있는 한 서둘러 계단을 내려가는 모습을 볼 수 있었다. 그 계단은 이곳의 큰 명물이다. 시가지에서 교회까지 이어지는 그 계단은 수백 개나 되는데 — 정확히 몇 개인지는 모른다 — 우아한 곡선을 그리며 굽이굽이 올라간다. 경사가 어찌나 완만한지 말도 쉽게 오르내릴 수 있을 정도다. 본디 수도원과 무슨 관련이 있었으리라 생각한다. 나도 집으로 돌아가야겠다. 루시는 어머니와 함께 방문을 나갔는데, 그저 의례적인 방문일 뿐이어서 나는 따라가지 않았다. 이제쯤이면 돌아왔을 것이다.\n\n8월 1일. — 나는 한 시간 전에 루시와 함께 이곳으로 올라왔는데, 내 늙은 친구와, 언제나 그에게 와서 합류하는 다른 두 사람과 무척 흥미로운 이야기를 나누었다. 그는 분명 그들 가운데 신탁(神託)을 내리는 어른이며, 한창때에는 매우 독선적인 사람이었으리라 짐작된다. 그는 어떤 것도 인정하려 들지 않고, 모두를 면박 준다. 논쟁에서 이기지 못하면 윽박지르고, 그러고는 상대의 침묵을 자기 견해에 동의한 것으로 받아들인다. 루시는 흰색 론 천 원피스를 입고 사랑스럽도록 예뻐 보였다. 이곳에 온 뒤로 그녀의 혈색이 아름다워졌다. 우리가 자리에 앉자 노인들이 한시도 지체하지 않고 다가와 그녀 곁에 앉는 것을 나는 알아챘다. 그녀는 노인들에게 어찌나 다정한지, 모두가 그 자리에서 그녀에게 반해버린 것 같다. 내 늙은 친구조차도 굴복하여 그녀의 말에 반박하지 않았고, 대신 나에게 두 배로 면박을 주었다. 나는 그를 전설 이야기로 끌어들였고, 그는 곧장 일종의 설교를 늘어놓기 시작했다. 그것을 기억해두었다가 적어두어야겠다.\n\n\"그딴 건 죄다 바보 같은 소리여, 통째로 몽땅 다 말이여. 그게 다지, 다른 건 없어. 그 저주니, 망령이니, 도깨비니, 요귀(妖鬼)니, 귀신이니, 그것들에 관한 모든 게 그저 어린애나 정신없는 여편네들 비명이나 지르게 하기 딱 좋은 거지. 그것들은 다 헛된 물거품일 뿐이여. 그것들이며, 온갖 흉조니 징조니 경고니 하는 것들은 죄다 목사들이며 못된 책장수들이며 철도 호객꾼들이 어린것들 겁주고 질겁하게 해서, 사람들로 하여금 안 그러면 안 할 짓을 하게 만들려고 지어낸 거여. 그것들을 생각하면 부아가 치미는구먼. 아니 글쎄, 종이에 거짓말을 박아내고 강단에서 거짓말을 설교하는 걸로도 모자라서, 이젠 비석에다 그걸 새기려고 든단 말이여. 자, 여기 둘레를 한번 둘러보우, 어느 쪽이든 좋수. 저 비석들이 죄다, 제 잘난 멋에 머리를 빳빳이 쳐들고 있지만, 다들 한쪽으로 기울어 있다우 — 거기 적힌 거짓말 무게에 못 이겨 그냥 폭삭 주저앉고 있는 거지. '여기 잠들다'느니 '추억을 기리며'느니 죄다 그렇게 적혀 있지만, 그 절반쯤에는 아예 시신이 없다우. 그리구 그들에 대한 추억이라는 것도 코담배 한 줌만큼도 마음 쓰는 이가 없지, 기리기는커녕 말이여. 죄다 거짓말이여, 이러쿵저러쿵 갖가지 거짓말일 뿐이라우! 아이고 맙소사, 최후의 심판 날에 그자들이 죄다 수의(壽衣) 차림으로 한데 뒤엉켜 비척비척 기어오르며, 제가 얼마나 착했는지 증명하겠다고 제 비석을 질질 끌고 오려 들 때면 그것 참 가관일 거구먼. 더러는 바다에 누워 있던 탓에 손이 다 곱고 미끄러워서 비석을 붙잡지도 못한 채 부들부들 벌벌 떨겠지.\"\n\n나는 그 노인의 흡족해하는 태도와, 친구들의 동의를 구하려고 둘러보는 모양새로 보아 그가 \"뽐내고\" 있음을 알 수 있었으므로, 그가 계속 이야기하도록 한마디 거들었다.\n\n\"어머, 스웨일스 씨, 진심은 아니시겠지요. 설마 이 비석들이 모두 다 틀렸겠어요?\"\n\n\"아마도 그렇진 않겠지! 틀리지 않은 게 몇 개쯤은 있을 거유, 하기야 그 사람들을 너무 좋게 꾸며놓은 데는 빼고 말이여. 자기 것이기만 하면 세숫대야가 바다만 하다고 여기는 작자들이 있으니까. 어쨌든 그 전체가 죄다 거짓말이여. 자, 여기 보우. 아가씨는 여기 낯선 사람으로 와서 이 교회 묘지를 보고 있지.\" 나는 고개를 끄덕였는데, 그의 사투리를 다 알아듣지는 못했지만 동의하는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했기 때문이다. 그것이 교회와 관계있는 말이라는 건 알았다. 그는 말을 이었다. \"그럼 아가씨는 이 비석들이 죄다 여기 곱게 편안히 묻힌 사람들 위에 놓인 거라고 짐작하시겠지?\" 나는 다시 동의했다. \"바로 거기서 거짓말이 끼어드는 거여. 아니 글쎄, 이 무덤 자리들 가운데 수십 개가 금요일 밤 던 영감네 담배통처럼 텅텅 비어 있다우.\" 그는 동료 하나를 팔꿈치로 쿡 찌르더니, 다들 웃음을 터뜨렸다. \"아이고 맙소사! 어찌 안 그러겠수? 저거 좀 보우, 상여 길 맨 끝에 있는 저거 말이여. 한번 읽어보우!\" 나는 건너가서 읽었다.\n\n\"에드워드 스펜슬러, 선장(船長), 1854년 4월 안드레스 연안에서 해적에게 살해당하다, 향년 30세.\" 내가 돌아오자 스웨일스 씨는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"누가 저 사람을 집으로 데려와서 여기 묻었을까, 나는 그게 궁금하구먼. 안드레스 연안에서 살해당했는데! 그런데도 아가씨는 그 시신이 저 아래 누워 있다고 짐작했단 말이지! 아니 글쎄, 그린란드 바다 저 위에 뼈가 누워 있는 사람들을 내 열댓 명은 꼽을 수 있다우 —\" 그가 북쪽을 가리켰다 \"— 아니면 해류가 어디로 떠밀어갔는지도 모를 사람들이지. 아가씨 둘레에 비석들이 있잖수. 아가씨야 젊은 눈이니, 여기서 그 거짓말의 잔글씨까지 다 읽을 수 있을 거유. 이 브레이스웨이트 라우리 — 내가 그 아비를 알았는데, 20년에 그린란드 앞바다에서 '라이블리'호와 함께 사라졌지. 또 앤드루 우드하우스, 1777년에 같은 바다에서 익사했고, 존 팩스턴, 그 이듬해에 페어웰 곶 앞바다에서 익사했고, 늙은 존 롤링스, 그 할아비가 나랑 같이 배를 탔었는데, 50년에 핀란드 만에서 익사했지. 아가씨는 이 사람들이 죄다 나팔 소리가 울리면 휘트비로 부랴부랴 달려와야 한다고 생각하우? 나는 그게 영 미심쩍단 말이여! 내 장담컨대, 그들이 여기 닿으면 서로 밀치고 떠밀고 하면서 한바탕 난리가 날 거유, 옛날 얼음판 위에서 우리가 동틀 녘부터 해 질 녘까지 서로 치고받다가 북극광 빛에 비춰가며 상처를 싸매던 그 싸움판처럼 말이여.\" 이건 분명 이 고장식 농담이었으니, 노인은 그 말에 깔깔 웃어댔고, 그의 친구들도 신이 나서 함께 웃었다.\n\n\"하지만,\" 내가 말했다, \"정말로 옳지만은 않으세요. 모든 가엾은 사람들이, 아니면 그 혼령들이 최후의 심판 날에 제 비석을 가지고 가야 한다는 전제에서 출발하고 계시잖아요. 정말로 그래야만 한다고 생각하세요?\"\n\n\"아니, 그럼 그 비석들이 뭣 하러 있는 건데? 그것 좀 대답해보우, 아가씨!\"\n\n\"유족들을 기쁘게 하려는 거겠지요, 아마도.\"\n\n\"유족들을 기쁘게 하려는 거다, 아마도!\" 그는 이 말을 지독한 경멸을 담아 내뱉었다. \"거기에 거짓말이 적혀 있고, 이 동네 사람이 다 그게 거짓말인 줄 안다는 걸 알면 그게 유족들한테 무슨 기쁨이 되겠수?\" 그는 우리 발치의, 의자를 받치는 받침대로 깔아놓은, 절벽 가장자리에 바짝 붙은 돌 하나를 가리켰다. \"저 묘석에 적힌 거짓말을 읽어보우,\" 그가 말했다. 내가 앉은 자리에서는 글자가 거꾸로 보였지만, 루시는 그것을 더 마주보는 위치에 있어서 몸을 숙여 읽었다.\n\n\"케틀네스 바위에서 떨어져, 영광스러운 부활을 소망하며 1873년 7월 29일에 세상을 떠난 조지 캐넌을 기리며. 이 무덤은 슬픔에 잠긴 어머니가 더없이 사랑하는 아들을 위해 세웠노라. '그는 어머니의 외아들이었으며, 그 어머니는 과부였더라.' 정말이지, 스웨일스 씨, 저는 여기서 우스운 점을 하나도 못 찾겠어요!\" 그녀는 자기 의견을 매우 진지하게, 다소 엄하게 말했다.\n\n\"우스운 게 안 보인다고! 하! 하! 그건 아가씨가 그 슬픔에 잠긴 어머니가 실은 못된 마귀할멈이었다는 걸 모르니까 그렇지. 그 어미가 아들을 미워한 건 그 애가 절름발이여서였다우 — 영락없는 앉은뱅이였지 — 그리구 그 애는 어미를 어찌나 미워했던지, 어미가 제 목숨에 들어둔 보험금을 못 타게 하려고 일부러 자살을 했다우. 까마귀 쫓는 데 쓰던 낡은 머스킷 총으로 제 머리통을 거의 절반이나 날려버렸지. 그래서 까마귀 대신 쇠파리랑 까마귀 떼만 그자한테 꼬였구먼. 그게 바로 그 애가 바위에서 떨어진 사연이여. 그리구 영광스러운 부활을 소망했다는 거 말인데, 나는 그자가 제 입으로 지옥에 가고 싶다고 하는 걸 여러 번 들었다우. 제 어미가 어찌나 신앙심이 깊은지 틀림없이 천국에 갈 텐데, 자기는 어미가 있는 데서 썩고 싶지 않다는 거였지. 자, 어쨌든 저 비석이 —\" 그는 말하면서 지팡이로 그것을 탕탕 두드렸다 \"— 새빨간 거짓말 덩어리가 아니냐고? 그리구 최후의 심판 날에 조디가 제 등의 혹 위에 비석을 얹고 헐떡거리며 계단을 기어 올라와서, 이걸 증거로 받아달라고 하면 가브리엘 천사가 픽 웃지 않겠수!\"\n\n나는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐는데, 루시가 일어서며 화제를 돌렸다.\n\n\"아, 그런 말씀을 왜 우리한테 해주셨어요? 여기는 제가 제일 좋아하는 자리라 떠날 수가 없는데, 이제 보니 저는 자살한 사람의 무덤 위에 계속 앉아 있어야 하는 거잖아요.\"\n\n\"그래도 그게 아가씨를 해치진 않을 거유, 어여쁜 아가씨. 그리구 이렇게 참한 처녀가 제 무릎에 앉아 있으면 가엾은 조디도 외려 기뻐할지 모르지. 그게 아가씨를 다치게 하진 않아. 아니 글쎄, 나도 지난 이십 년 가까이 여기 들락날락 앉아 있었지만 아무 탈도 없었다우. 아가씨 밑에 누워 있는 자들이나, 아니 거기 누워 있지도 않은 자들 때문에 안달복달하지 마우! 비석들이 죄다 달아나버리고, 이 자리가 그루터기 밭처럼 텅 비어버린 걸 보거든 그때 가서 겁먹어도 늦지 않다우. 저기 시계 소리가 나는구먼, 나는 이제 가봐야겠수. 그럼 안녕히 계시우, 아가씨들!\" 그러고는 절뚝거리며 떠나갔다.\n\n루시와 나는 한동안 앉아 있었는데, 눈앞의 풍경이 어찌나 아름다운지 우리는 앉은 채로 서로 손을 잡았다. 그리고 그녀는 아서와 다가오는 결혼식에 대해 처음부터 다시 다 이야기해주었다. 그 이야기에 나는 마음이 조금 아렸으니, 조너선에게서 꼬박 한 달째 소식을 듣지 못했기 때문이다.\n\n같은 날. 나는 이곳에 혼자 올라왔는데, 몹시 슬프기 때문이다. 나에게 온 편지는 없었다. 조너선에게 무슨 일이 있는 건 아니길 바란다. 방금 시계가 아홉 시를 쳤다. 시가지 전체에 불빛이 흩어져 있는 것이 보이는데, 더러는 거리를 따라 줄지어 있고, 더러는 하나씩 따로 있다. 그 불빛들은 에스크 강을 따라 곧장 올라가다가 골짜기의 굽이에서 스러진다. 내 왼편으로는 수도원 옆 옛집 지붕의 검은 선에 시야가 가로막혀 있다. 내 뒤편 멀리 들판에서는 양들과 새끼 양들이 울고 있고, 아래쪽 포장된 길에서는 당나귀 발굽 소리가 또각또각 들려온다. 방파제의 악단은 거친 왈츠를 박자 맞춰 연주하고 있고, 부두를 따라 좀 더 가면 뒷골목에서 구세군 집회가 열리고 있다. 두 악단은 서로의 소리를 듣지 못하지만, 이 위에서는 두 소리가 다 들리고 두 광경이 다 보인다. 조너선은 지금 어디에 있을까, 나를 생각하고 있을까! 그가 여기 있었으면 좋겠다.\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n6월 5일. — 렌필드라는 사례는 그 사람을 이해하면 할수록 더욱 흥미로워진다. 그에게는 어떤 자질들이 매우 크게 발달해 있다. 이기심, 비밀스러움, 그리고 목적의식이다. 마지막 것의 목표가 무엇인지를 알아낼 수 있다면 좋겠다. 그에게는 자기 나름의 어떤 확고한 계획이 있는 듯한데, 그것이 무엇인지는 아직 모른다. 그의 구원할 만한 자질은 동물을 사랑하는 것인데, 그러나 그 사랑에 어찌나 기이한 면이 있는지 나는 이따금 그가 그저 비정상적으로 잔인한 게 아닐까 싶기도 하다. 그가 기르는 것들은 별난 종류다. 지금 그의 취미는 파리 잡기다. 현재 어찌나 많은 파리를 모았는지 나는 직접 타일러야 했다. 놀랍게도 그는 내가 예상한 대로 분통을 터뜨리지 않고, 그 문제를 담담하고 진지하게 받아들였다. 그는 잠시 생각하더니 말했다. \"사흘만 시간을 주시겠습니까? 다 치우겠습니다.\" 물론 나는 그래도 좋다고 했다. 그를 지켜봐야겠다.\n\n6월 18일. — 그는 이제 거미로 마음을 돌려, 아주 큰 놈들 여러 마리를 상자 안에 모아두었다. 그는 자기 파리들을 거미에게 계속 먹이고 있어서 파리의 수가 눈에 띄게 줄어들고 있다. 하지만 그는 자기 식량의 절반을 써서 바깥에서 더 많은 파리를 방 안으로 꾀어들이고 있다.\n\n7월 1일. — 그의 거미들이 이제 파리만큼이나 큰 골칫거리가 되어, 오늘 나는 그에게 거미들을 없애야 한다고 말했다. 그가 이 말에 무척 슬퍼 보이기에, 나는 적어도 일부는 치워야 한다고 했다. 그는 이 말에 흔쾌히 응했고, 나는 전과 같은 시간을 줄여 없애도록 주었다. 그와 함께 있는 동안 그는 나를 몹시 역겹게 했는데, 썩은 고기로 살이 통통하게 오른 끔찍한 검정파리 한 마리가 윙윙거리며 방 안으로 들어오자, 그는 그것을 붙잡아 잠시 의기양양하게 엄지와 검지 사이에 끼고 있다가, 내가 그가 무엇을 하려는지 알아채기도 전에 그것을 입에 넣고 먹어버렸다. 내가 그 일로 그를 나무라자, 그는 그것이 아주 좋고 아주 몸에 이로우며, 그것은 생명, 강한 생명이고 자기에게 생명을 준다고 차분하게 우겼다. 이것이 나에게 한 가지 생각을, 적어도 그 단초를 떠오르게 했다. 그가 거미들을 어떻게 없애는지 지켜봐야겠다. 그에게는 분명 마음속에 어떤 깊은 문제가 있는데, 그는 작은 수첩을 하나 지니고 다니며 거기에 늘 무언가를 끄적이고 있기 때문이다. 그 수첩의 여러 면이 숫자 더미로 가득 차 있는데, 대개 한 자리 숫자들을 무더기로 더하고, 그 합계를 다시 무더기로 더해놓은 것이, 마치 회계 감사관들의 표현대로 어떤 회계 장부를 \"맞추어보고\" 있는 것 같았다.\n\n7월 8일. — 그의 광기에는 나름의 방법이 있고, 내 마음속의 그 어렴풋한 생각도 점점 자라나고 있다. 곧 온전한 생각이 될 것이며, 그러면 아, 무의식적 두뇌 작용이여! 너는 너의 의식 있는 형제에게 길을 비켜주어야 하리라. 나는 며칠 동안 내 친구를 멀리했는데, 무슨 변화가 있는지 살펴보려는 것이었다. 그가 기르던 것들 가운데 일부를 처분하고 새로운 것을 하나 얻었다는 점을 빼면 상황은 전과 같았다. 그는 참새 한 마리를 손에 넣어 이미 어느 정도 길들여놓았다. 그 길들이는 수단은 단순한데, 이미 거미들이 줄어들었기 때문이다. 그러나 남아 있는 거미들은 잘 먹고 있는데, 그가 여전히 식량으로 꾀어들여 파리를 잡아들이기 때문이다.\n\n7월 19일. — 우리는 진전을 보이고 있다. 내 친구는 이제 참새 한 무리를 통째로 거느리게 되었고, 그의 파리와 거미는 거의 자취를 감추었다. 내가 들어가자 그는 나에게 달려와 큰 부탁이 하나 있다고, 아주아주 큰 부탁이라고 했다. 그리고 말하는 동안 개처럼 나에게 알랑거렸다. 무슨 부탁이냐고 묻자, 그는 목소리와 태도에 일종의 황홀감을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"새끼고양이요, 윤기 흐르고 잘 노는 예쁘고 작은 새끼고양이 한 마리요. 같이 놀고, 가르치고, 먹이고 — 먹이고 — 먹일 수 있는 놈으로요!\" 나는 이 부탁에 전혀 대비가 없던 것은 아니었으니, 그가 기르는 것들이 어떻게 점점 덩치가 커지고 활기가 넘쳐 갔는지를 눈여겨봐 왔기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 그가 길들인 참새들의 그 예쁜 식구가 파리와 거미와 똑같은 방식으로 사라지는 것은 바라지 않았으므로, 알아보겠다고 하면서, 새끼고양이보다는 차라리 다 큰 고양이가 낫지 않겠느냐고 물었다. 그는 대답하면서 그 간절함으로 자기 속내를 드러내고 말았다.\n\n\"아, 그럼요, 고양이라면 좋지요! 제가 새끼고양이를 청한 건 선생님이 고양이를 거절하실까 봐서였습니다. 새끼고양이라면 아무도 거절하지 않을 테니까요, 안 그렇습니까?\" 나는 고개를 저으며, 지금으로서는 안 될 것 같지만 알아보겠다고 했다. 그의 얼굴이 어두워졌고, 나는 거기서 위험을 알리는 경고를 볼 수 있었으니, 갑자기 살기를 띤 사납고 흘끔거리는 눈빛이 스쳤기 때문이다. 이 사람은 아직 발현되지 않은 살인광이다. 나는 그의 현재 갈망을 가지고 그를 시험해보고 어떻게 풀려나가는지 보아야겠다. 그러면 더 많은 것을 알게 될 것이다.\n\n오후 10시. — 나는 그를 다시 찾아갔는데, 구석에 앉아 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있는 것을 보았다. 내가 들어가자 그는 내 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 고양이를 갖게 해달라고, 자기 구원이 거기에 달려 있다고 애원했다. 그러나 나는 단호하게 그것은 안 된다고 말했고, 그러자 그는 한마디도 없이 물러가, 내가 그를 처음 발견했던 그 구석에 앉아 손가락을 물어뜯었다. 아침 일찍 그를 보러 가야겠다.\n\n7월 20일. — 간병인이 회진을 돌기 전, 아주 일찍 렌필드를 찾아갔다. 그는 일어나서 콧노래를 흥얼거리고 있었다. 그는 아껴두었던 설탕을 창가에 펼쳐놓고 있었고, 보아하니 다시 파리 잡기를 시작하고 있었다. 그것도 명랑하고 기꺼운 태도로 말이다. 나는 그의 새들을 찾아 둘러보았으나 보이지 않기에, 새들이 어디 있느냐고 물었다. 그는 돌아보지도 않고, 새들이 모두 날아가버렸다고 대답했다. 방 안에 깃털 몇 개가 흩어져 있었고, 그의 베개에는 핏방울 하나가 떨어져 있었다. 나는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 가서 간수에게 그날 낮 동안 그에게 무언가 이상한 점이 있으면 나에게 보고하라고 일렀다.\n\n오전 11시. — 방금 간병인이 나를 찾아와, 렌필드가 몹시 토했는데 깃털을 한 무더기나 게워냈다고 말했다. \"제 생각엔 말입니다, 선생님,\" 그가 말했다, \"이자가 제 새들을 먹어버린 것 같습니다. 그것도 그냥 산 채로 잡아서 날것으로 먹어버린 거예요!\"\n\n오후 11시. — 오늘 밤 나는 렌필드에게, 그조차도 잠들게 할 만큼 강한 아편제를 먹이고, 그의 수첩을 가져와 살펴보았다. 요즈음 내 머릿속을 윙윙거리며 맴돌던 그 생각이 이제 완성되었고, 이론이 입증되었다. 내 살인광은 특이한 종류다. 나는 그를 위해 새로운 분류 항목을 만들어, 그를 식생광(食生狂, 생명을 먹는 미치광이)이라고 불러야 할 것이다. 그가 바라는 것은 가능한 한 많은 생명을 흡수하는 것이며, 그는 그것을 누적적인 방식으로 이루어내려고 작정해두고 있다. 그는 거미 한 마리에게 파리 여럿을 주었고, 새 한 마리에게 거미 여럿을 주었으며, 그런 다음 그 많은 새를 먹게 할 고양이를 원했다. 그 다음 단계는 무엇이었을까? 이 실험을 완성해보는 것도 거의 가치 있는 일일 것이다. 충분한 명분만 있다면 해볼 수도 있으리라. 사람들은 생체 해부를 비웃었지만, 오늘날 그 성과를 보라! 가장 어렵고도 가장 중요한 측면에서, 곧 두뇌에 대한 지식에서 과학을 진전시키지 못할 까닭이 무엇인가? 내가 만일 그런 정신 하나의 비밀만이라도 손에 넣는다면 — 단 한 명의 광인의 공상에 대한 열쇠라도 쥔다면 — 나는 내 분야의 과학을, 버든샌더슨의 생리학이나 페리어의 두뇌 지식 따위가 아무것도 아닐 정도의 경지로까지 끌어올릴 수 있으리라. 충분한 명분만 있다면! 이 일을 너무 깊이 생각해서는 안 되겠다. 안 그러면 유혹에 빠질지도 모른다. 충분한 명분이 나에게는 저울을 기울게 할 수도 있으니, 나 역시 선천적으로 비범한 두뇌를 가진 사람일 수도 있지 않은가?\n\n그 사람은 얼마나 잘 추론하는가. 광인들은 언제나 자기 나름의 범위 안에서는 그렇다. 그가 한 사람의 목숨을 몇 개의 생명으로 치는지, 아니면 단 하나로만 치는지 궁금하다. 그는 회계 장부를 더없이 정확하게 마감하고, 오늘 새 기록을 시작했다. 우리 가운데 몇이나 매일 자기 삶의 하루하루마다 새 기록을 시작하는가?\n\n나에게는 나의 새로운 희망과 더불어 내 삶 전체가 끝나버린 것이 바로 어제 일 같고, 그리하여 나도 참으로 새 기록을 시작한 듯하다. 위대한 기록자께서 나를 결산하시고, 손익의 잔액으로 내 장부를 마감하실 그날까지 그러할 것이다. 아, 루시여, 루시여, 나는 그대에게 화를 낼 수도 없고, 그대의 행복을 누리는 내 친구에게 화를 낼 수도 없다. 다만 나는 희망 없이 기다리며 일할 수밖에 없다. 일이다! 일이다!\n\n저기 가엾은 미친 친구가 가진 것만큼이나 강한 명분 하나만이라도 나에게 있다면 — 나를 일하게 만들 선하고 사심 없는 명분 하나만 있다면 — 그것이야말로 참된 행복이리라.\n\n「미나 머리의 일기」\n\n7월 26일. — 나는 불안하고, 여기에 내 마음을 털어놓으면 위안이 된다. 그것은 마치 자기 자신에게 속삭이면서 동시에 귀 기울여 듣는 것 같다. 그리고 속기 부호에는 또 무언가, 그냥 글을 쓰는 것과는 다른 데가 있다. 나는 루시 때문에, 그리고 조너선 때문에 마음이 무겁다. 한동안 조너선에게서 소식을 듣지 못해 몹시 걱정했는데, 어제 늘 그토록 친절하신 호킨스 씨가 그에게서 온 편지 한 통을 나에게 보내주셨다. 나는 그분께 혹시 소식을 들으셨느냐고 편지를 써서 물었었는데, 그분은 동봉한 편지가 방금 도착했다고 하셨다. 그것은 드라큘라 성에서 부친, 단 한 줄짜리 편지로, 그가 곧 집으로 출발한다는 내용이었다. 그것은 조너선답지 않다. 나는 그 편지를 이해할 수 없고, 그것이 나를 불안하게 만든다. 게다가 루시는, 그토록 건강한데도, 요즈음 다시 몽유병으로 걷는 옛 버릇이 도졌다. 그녀의 어머니가 나에게 그 이야기를 하셨고, 우리는 내가 매일 밤 우리 방문을 잠그기로 정했다. 웨스튼라 부인은 몽유병자들이 늘 집 지붕 위나 절벽 가장자리를 따라 나갔다가, 갑자기 잠이 깨어 사방에 메아리치는 절망의 비명을 지르며 떨어진다는 생각을 갖고 계신다. 가엾은 분, 루시 때문에 걱정하시는 건 당연하다. 그분은 나에게, 자기 남편, 곧 루시의 아버지도 같은 버릇이 있었다고, 막지 않으면 밤중에 일어나 옷을 차려입고 밖으로 나가곤 했다고 말씀하셨다. 루시는 가을에 결혼할 예정이라, 벌써부터 자기 드레스와 집을 어떻게 꾸밀지를 계획하고 있다. 나도 같은 처지이니 그녀의 마음에 공감한다. 다만 조너선과 나는 아주 소박하게 살림을 시작할 것이고, 겨우 수지를 맞추려고 애써야 할 것이다. 홈우드 씨 — 그는 고댈밍 경의 외아들인 아서 홈우드 님인데 — 가 머지않아 이곳으로 올라올 것이다. 그의 부친이 편찮으셔서 그가 도시를 떠날 수 있게 되는 대로 곧 말이다. 사랑스러운 루시는 그가 올 때까지 그 순간순간을 헤아리고 있는 것 같다. 그녀는 그를 교회 묘지 절벽의 그 자리로 데려가 휘트비의 아름다움을 보여주고 싶어 한다. 짐작건대 그녀를 어지럽히는 것은 바로 그 기다림이리라. 그가 도착하면 그녀는 괜찮아질 것이다.\n\n7월 27일. — 조너선에게서 소식이 없다. 나는 그가 점점 걱정되는데, 왜 그래야 하는지는 모르겠다. 다만 단 한 줄이라도 좋으니 그가 편지를 써주었으면 정말 좋겠다. 루시는 그 어느 때보다도 많이 걸어 다니고, 매일 밤 그녀가 방 안을 돌아다니는 통에 나는 잠에서 깬다. 다행히 날씨가 너무 더워서 그녀가 감기에 걸릴 일은 없다. 그러나 그래도 이 걱정과 끊임없이 잠이 깨는 것이 나에게 영향을 미치기 시작해, 나 자신도 신경이 곤두서고 잠을 설치게 되었다. 다행히도 루시의 건강은 잘 유지되고 있다. 홈우드 씨는 위중하게 병이 나신 부친을 뵈러 갑자기 링으로 불려갔다. 루시는 그를 만나는 일이 미뤄진 것에 애를 태우지만, 그것이 그녀의 용모에는 영향을 미치지 않는다. 그녀는 살이 조금 올랐고, 두 뺨은 사랑스러운 장밋빛이다. 전에 그녀에게 있던 그 빈혈기 도는 모습은 사라졌다. 이 모든 것이 계속되기를 기도한다.\n\n8월 3일. — 또 한 주가 지났는데, 조너선에게서 소식이 없다. 내가 소식을 들어온 호킨스 씨에게조차 아무런 연락이 없다. 아, 부디 그가 병이 난 게 아니길 바란다. 병이 났다면 분명 편지를 썼을 것이다. 나는 그의 그 마지막 편지를 들여다보지만, 어쩐지 그것이 나를 만족시키지 못한다. 그것은 그가 쓴 것 같지가 않은데, 그러면서도 그의 필체다. 그 점에는 틀림이 없다. 루시는 지난 한 주 동안 몽유병으로 걷는 일이 많지 않았으나, 그녀에게는 내가 이해할 수 없는 기이한 집중 같은 것이 있다. 잠든 중에도 그녀는 나를 지켜보는 것 같다. 그녀는 문을 열어보려 하고, 잠겨 있는 것을 알면 열쇠를 찾아 방 안을 돌아다닌다.\n\n8월 6일. — 또 사흘이 지났는데, 소식이 없다. 이 불안한 기다림이 점점 끔찍해지고 있다. 어디로 편지를 쓰거나 어디로 가야 할지만 알아도 마음이 한결 편할 텐데, 그 마지막 편지 이후로 아무도 조너선에 대해 한마디도 듣지 못했다. 나는 그저 하느님께 인내를 달라고 기도할 수밖에 없다. 루시는 그 어느 때보다도 더 흥분을 잘하지만, 그 밖에는 건강하다. 어젯밤은 무척 위협적이었고, 어부들은 우리에게 폭풍이 닥칠 것이라고 말한다. 나는 그것을 잘 지켜보고 날씨를 읽는 법을 배워봐야겠다. 오늘은 잿빛 날이고, 내가 글을 쓰는 지금 해는 케틀네스 위 높이 두꺼운 구름 속에 가려져 있다. 모든 것이 잿빛이다 — 그 사이에서 에메랄드처럼 보이는 푸른 풀만 빼고. 잿빛의 흙투성이 바위, 저 먼 가장자리에 햇살이 터져 물든 잿빛 구름이 잿빛 바다 위에 드리워 있고, 그 바다로 모래톱들이 잿빛 손가락처럼 뻗어 있다. 바다는 얕은 여울과 모래펄 위로 굉음을 내며 밀려드는데, 그 소리가 내륙으로 떠도는 바다 안개에 잠겨 먹먹하다. 수평선은 잿빛 안개 속에 사라졌다. 모든 것이 광대하다. 구름은 거대한 바위처럼 쌓여 있고, 바다 위에는 어떤 파멸의 전조처럼 들리는 \"웅웅거리는 소리\"가 깔려 있다. 검은 형체들이 해변 여기저기에 있는데, 때로는 안개에 반쯤 휩싸여 \"걸어 다니는 나무 같은 사람들\"처럼 보인다. 고기잡이배들은 집을 향해 내달리며, 항구로 쓸려 들어오면서 너울에 솟구쳤다 가라앉았다 하며 뱃전까지 기울어진다. 저기 늙은 스웨일스 씨가 온다. 그는 나를 향해 곧장 오고 있는데, 모자를 들어 올리는 모양새로 보아 이야기를 하고 싶어 한다는 걸 알 수 있다…….\n\n나는 이 가엾은 노인의 변한 모습에 무척 마음이 뭉클했다. 그는 내 옆에 앉아 매우 부드러운 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"아가씨에게 할 말이 있수.\" 나는 그가 마음이 편치 않다는 걸 알아챘으므로, 그의 가엾고 늙어 주름진 손을 내 손으로 잡고 마음껏 이야기해보시라고 청했다. 그러자 그는 자기 손을 내 손에 맡긴 채 말했다.\n\n\"미안허우, 아가씨. 내가 지난 몇 주 동안 죽은 자들이니 뭐니 하는 그 못된 소리들을 늘어놓아서 아가씨를 놀라게 했을 텐데. 하지만 그건 진심이 아니었수. 내가 떠나고 나거든 그걸 기억해주었으면 허우. 우리 같은 늙은이들은 정신이 흐릿해지고 한 발은 이미 무덤 구덩이에 걸쳐 있어서, 그걸 떠올리는 게 영 달갑지 않고, 또 그걸 무서워하는 것처럼 느끼고 싶지도 않다우. 그래서 내가 그걸 우습게 떠벌리게 된 거지, 그렇게라도 내 마음을 좀 북돋우려고 말이여. 하지만, 아가씨, 정말이지 나는 죽는 게 무섭지 않수, 조금도. 다만 어쩔 수 없는 게 아니라면 죽고 싶지가 않을 뿐이여. 이제 내 때가 가까웠을 거유, 나도 늙었고, 백 년이란 건 누구라도 바라기엔 너무 긴 세월이니까. 나는 그 백 년에 어찌나 가까워졌는지, 저 위의 어르신이 벌써 큰 낫을 갈고 계신단 말이여. 알다시피, 나는 이런 걸 한꺼번에 농지거리하는 버릇을 떨쳐내질 못하겠수. 입이라는 건 늘 하던 대로 놀려대니까. 머잖아 어느 날엔가 죽음의 천사가 날 위해 나팔을 불 거유. 하지만 슬퍼하거나 울지 마우, 아가씨!\" — 내가 울고 있는 것을 보았기 때문이다 — \"설령 그가 바로 오늘 밤에 온다 해도 나는 그 부름에 응하기를 마다하지 않을 거유. 삶이란 건 어쨌거나 우리가 하고 있는 그것 말고 다른 무언가를 기다리는 것일 뿐이고, 죽음이야말로 우리가 마땅히 믿고 의지할 수 있는 전부니까. 하지만 나는 만족허우, 그게 나한테 오고 있고, 그것도 빨리 오고 있으니까 말이여, 아가씨. 어쩌면 우리가 이렇게 바라보며 궁금해하는 동안에도 오고 있는지 모르지. 어쩌면 저 바다 너머에서 불어오는, 상실과 난파와 쓰라린 고통과 슬픈 마음을 함께 싣고 오는 저 바람 속에 있는지도 모르고. 보우! 보우!\" 그가 갑자기 소리쳤다. \"저 바람 속에, 그리고 저 너머 안개 속에 무언가가 있는데, 그게 죽음처럼 들리고, 보이고, 맛이 나고, 냄새가 나는구먼. 그게 공기 중에 있어. 나는 그게 오는 게 느껴진다우. 주여, 제 부름이 올 때 제가 기쁘게 응답하게 하소서!\" 그는 경건하게 두 팔을 들어 올리고 모자를 벗었다. 마치 기도라도 하듯 입술이 움직였다. 몇 분간의 침묵 끝에 그는 일어나 나와 악수하고, 나를 축복하고, 작별을 고하더니 절뚝거리며 떠나갔다. 이 모든 것이 나를 뭉클하게 했고, 또 무척 심란하게 만들었다.\n\n겨드랑이에 망원경을 끼고 해안 경비원이 다가왔을 때 나는 반가웠다. 그는 늘 그렇듯 멈춰 서서 나와 이야기를 나누었지만, 줄곧 어떤 이상한 배 한 척을 바라보고 있었다.\n\n\"저 배를 도무지 알 수가 없네요,\" 그가 말했다. \"생긴 걸로 봐선 러시아 배인데, 아주 괴상하게 이리저리 흔들리고 있어요. 도무지 제 갈 길을 정하지 못하고 있군요. 폭풍이 오는 걸 아는 것 같은데, 북쪽 먼바다로 올라갈지 여기로 들어올지를 결정하지 못하는 모양입니다. 저기 또 보세요! 키를 어찌나 괴상하게 잡았는지, 키잡이의 손을 통 따르질 않아요. 바람이 한 번 불 때마다 방향을 바꿔버립니다. 내일 이맘때가 되기 전에 저 배에 대해 더 듣게 될 겁니다.\"\n\n(미나 머리의 일기에 붙여둠.)\n\n본지 통신원 보도.\n\n휘트비.\n\n기록에 남은 가장 크고 가장 갑작스러운 폭풍 가운데 하나가 방금 이곳에서 지나갔는데, 그 결과 또한 기이하고 유례없는 것이었다. 날씨는 다소 후텁지근했으나, 8월에 결코 드문 정도는 아니었다. 토요일 저녁은 일찍이 없었을 만큼 청명했고, 휴일을 즐기려는 사람들의 큰 무리가 어제 멀그레이브 숲, 로빈 후드 만, 리그 밀, 런즈윅, 스테이스, 그리고 휘트비 인근의 여러 행락지를 찾아 나섰다. 증기선 에마호와 스카버러호는 해안을 오르내리며 유람을 했고, 휘트비를 오가는 \"나들이\"가 유난히 많았다. 오후가 되기 전까지 날씨는 유난히 청명했는데, 그때 이스트 클리프 묘지를 자주 찾아 그 높직한 자리에서 북쪽과 동쪽으로 트인 드넓은 바다를 바라보던 한담객 몇이, 북서쪽 하늘 높이 갑자기 나타난 \"암말 꼬리\" 구름에 사람들의 주의를 환기시켰다. 그때 바람은 남서쪽에서 불고 있었는데, 기압계 용어로 \"2급: 가벼운 산들바람\"으로 분류되는 온순한 정도였다. 근무 중이던 해안경비대원이 즉시 보고했고, 반세기가 넘도록 이스트 클리프에서 날씨의 징후를 살펴온 한 늙은 어부는 갑작스러운 폭풍이 닥치리라고 단호한 어조로 예언했다. 해질녘이 다가오는 모습이 어찌나 아름답고, 찬란한 빛깔의 구름 덩이가 어찌나 장엄했던지, 그 아름다움을 즐기려고 옛 묘지의 절벽길에는 제법 많은 사람이 모여들었다. 서쪽 하늘을 가로질러 대담하게 솟아 있는 케틀네스의 검은 덩어리 아래로 해가 잠기기 전에, 그 지는 길은 온갖 노을빛의 무수한 구름으로 수놓였다 — 불꽃빛, 자줏빛, 분홍빛, 초록빛, 보랏빛, 그리고 온갖 금빛으로. 군데군데 크지는 않으나 거의 완전한 검정으로 보이는 덩어리들이, 거대한 실루엣처럼 또렷한 윤곽을 드러내며 갖가지 형상으로 떠 있었다. 이 광경은 화가들에게 헛되지 않았으니, 분명 \"대폭풍의 서곡\"을 담은 몇몇 스케치가 내년 5월 왕립미술원과 왕립수채화협회의 벽을 장식하게 될 것이다. 한 사람 이상의 선장이 그 자리에서, 그들이 배의 종류에 따라 부르는 \"코블\"이나 \"뮬\"을 폭풍이 지나갈 때까지 항구에 남겨두기로 마음을 굳혔다. 저녁 내내 바람은 완전히 잦아들었고, 자정에는 죽은 듯한 고요와 후텁지근한 열기, 그리고 천둥이 다가올 때 예민한 사람들에게 영향을 미치는 그 짙은 긴장감이 감돌았다. 바다에 보이는 불빛은 몇 개 되지 않았으니, 보통은 해안에 바짝 붙어 다니던 연안 증기선들조차 바다 쪽으로 멀찍이 떨어져 있었고, 고깃배도 거의 보이지 않았기 때문이다. 눈에 띄는 유일한 돛은 돛을 모두 펼친 외국 스쿠너선이었는데, 보아하니 서쪽으로 가고 있었다. 그 배가 시야에 머무는 동안 선원들의 무모함 혹은 무지함은 끝없는 논평의 소재가 되었고, 위험을 무릅쓰지 말고 돛을 줄이라는 신호를 보내려는 시도도 있었다. 밤이 완전히 내려앉기 전, 그 배는 일렁이는 너울 위에서 가만히 흔들리며 돛을 하릴없이 펄럭이는 모습으로 마지막에 보였다,\n\n\"그려놓은 바다 위 그려놓은 배처럼 하릴없이.\"\n\n열 시가 조금 못 되어 대기의 고요는 자못 숨막힐 지경이 되었고, 그 적막이 어찌나 또렷했던지 내륙에서 양이 우는 소리나 마을의 개가 짖는 소리가 또렷이 들렸으며, 부두의 악단이 연주하는 흥겨운 프랑스 곡조는 자연의 침묵이 빚는 거대한 조화 속의 불협화음 같았다. 자정이 조금 지나자 바다 너머에서 기이한 소리가 들려왔고, 머리 위 높은 곳의 대기가 기이하고 희미하며 공허한 우르릉 소리를 실어 나르기 시작했다.\n\n그러더니 아무런 예고도 없이 폭풍이 터졌다. 그 당시에는 믿기 어려웠고, 나중에도 도무지 실감할 수 없는 빠르기로, 자연의 모습 전체가 일순간 격렬하게 뒤틀렸다. 파도는 점점 더 사납게 솟구쳐 저마다 앞의 것을 타넘었고, 불과 몇 분도 지나지 않아 방금까지 유리알 같던 바다는 포효하며 집어삼키는 괴물처럼 변했다. 흰 물마루의 파도가 평평한 모래밭을 미친 듯이 후려치고 비탈진 절벽을 타고 솟구쳤다. 또 어떤 것들은 부두를 넘어, 그 물보라로 휘트비 항 양쪽 부두 끝에 솟은 등대의 등불을 휩쓸었다. 바람은 천둥처럼 포효했고, 어찌나 세차게 불었던지 건장한 사내들조차 발을 디디고 서거나 쇠기둥을 단단히 부여잡기조차 힘들 지경이었다. 구경꾼 무리를 부두 전체에서 물러나게 할 필요가 생겼으니, 그러지 않았다면 그 밤의 사망자가 몇 곱절로 늘어났을 것이다. 그때의 어려움과 위험을 더하려는 듯, 바다 안개 덩어리가 내륙으로 떠밀려 들어왔다 — 희고 축축한 구름이 유령처럼 휩쓸고 지나갔는데, 어찌나 눅눅하고 축축하고 차가웠던지, 바다에서 목숨을 잃은 자들의 혼령이 죽음의 끈적한 손으로 살아 있는 형제들을 어루만지는 것이라 상상하기에 그리 큰 상상력도 필요치 않았으며, 바다 안개의 자락이 휩쓸고 지날 때마다 적잖은 사람들이 몸서리쳤다. 이따금 안개가 걷히면, 이제 빽빽하고 빠르게 내리치는 번개의 섬광 속에 어느 정도 거리의 바다가 보였고, 그 뒤를 이어 그토록 갑작스러운 천둥소리가 따라오니 머리 위 하늘 전체가 폭풍의 발걸음이 주는 충격에 떨고 있는 듯했다.\n\n그렇게 드러난 광경 가운데 어떤 것들은 헤아릴 수 없이 장엄하고 마음을 사로잡을 만큼 흥미로웠다 — 산처럼 높이 솟구치는 바다는 파도마다 거대한 흰 거품 덩어리를 하늘로 내던졌고, 폭풍은 그것을 낚아채어 허공으로 휘몰아가는 듯했다. 군데군데 누더기 같은 돛을 단 고깃배가 돌풍을 피해 피난처를 향해 미친 듯이 내달렸고, 이따금 폭풍에 시달리는 바닷새의 흰 날개가 보였다. 이스트 클리프 꼭대기에는 새 탐조등이 시험할 준비가 되어 있었으나, 아직 가동된 적은 없었다. 그것을 맡은 대원들이 작동 상태로 만들어, 안개가 밀려드는 틈틈이 그 빛으로 바다 표면을 훑었다. 한두 번은 그 빛이 더없이 효과적이었으니, 뱃전이 물에 잠긴 고깃배 한 척이 항구로 내달려 들어올 때, 비추어 주는 빛의 인도 덕분에 부두에 부딪히는 위험을 피할 수 있었던 경우가 그러했다. 배 한 척이 항구의 안전에 다다를 때마다 해안의 군중 속에서 기쁨의 함성이 터졌는데, 그 함성은 한순간 강풍을 가르는 듯하다가 이내 그 거센 흐름에 휩쓸려 사라졌다.\n\n이윽고 탐조등이 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에서 돛을 모두 펼친 스쿠너선 한 척을 찾아냈는데, 보아하니 저녁 일찍 눈에 띄었던 바로 그 배였다. 그 무렵 바람은 동쪽으로 돌아 있었고, 절벽 위의 구경꾼들은 그 배가 이제 처한 무서운 위험을 깨닫고 몸서리쳤다. 그 배와 항구 사이에는, 숱한 좋은 배들이 때때로 화를 입었던 그 거대한 평평한 암초가 가로놓여 있었고, 지금 부는 방향의 바람으로는 그 배가 항구 입구에 닿기란 전혀 불가능할 터였다. 이제 거의 만조 시각이었으나, 파도가 어찌나 컸던지 그 골짜기 사이로 해안의 얕은 바닥이 거의 드러나 보일 지경이었고, 돛을 모두 펼친 그 스쿠너선은 어찌나 빠르게 내달리는지, 어느 늙은 뱃사람의 말마따나 \"어디든 가서 처박힐 게 분명한데, 지옥이라도 마다 않을 기세\"였다. 그때 또 한 번 바다 안개가 밀려들었는데, 지금까지의 그 무엇보다 더 짙었다 — 잿빛 장막처럼 만물을 덮어버리는 듯한 눅눅한 안개 덩어리로, 사람들에게 남겨진 것은 오로지 청각 기관뿐이었으니, 폭풍의 포효와 천둥의 굉음과 거대한 물결의 우르릉 소리가 그 축축한 망각 속을 뚫고 전보다 더욱 크게 들려왔기 때문이다. 탐조등의 광선은 충돌이 예상되는 이스트 피어 너머 항구 어귀에 고정되었고, 사람들은 숨죽여 기다렸다. 바람이 갑자기 북동쪽으로 방향을 틀었고, 남은 바다 안개가 그 돌풍에 녹아 사라졌다. 그러자, 참으로 놀랍게도, 두 부두 사이로, 파도에서 파도로 뛰어넘으며 곤두박질치는 속도로 내달려, 그 기이한 스쿠너선이 돛을 모두 펼친 채 돌풍에 떠밀려 들어와 항구의 안전에 다다랐다. 탐조등이 그 배를 따라갔고, 그 배를 본 모든 이가 몸서리쳤으니, 키에 묶여 있는 것이 시체였기 때문이다. 머리를 늘어뜨린 그 시체는 배가 움직일 때마다 끔찍하게 이리저리 흔들렸다. 갑판 위에는 다른 어떤 형체도 전혀 보이지 않았다. 그 배가 기적이라도 일어난 듯, 죽은 사람의 손 외에는 아무도 조종하지 않은 채 항구를 찾아 들어왔음을 깨닫자 모든 이에게 커다란 경외가 엄습했다! 그러나 이 모든 일은 이 글을 쓰는 것보다도 더 빠르게 일어났다. 그 스쿠너선은 멈추지 않고, 항구를 가로질러 내달리더니, 이스트 클리프 아래로 돌출한 부두의 남동쪽 모퉁이, 곧 이 고장에서 테이트 힐 피어라 불리는 곳에 숱한 조수와 숱한 폭풍이 쓸어다 쌓은 그 모래와 자갈 더미 위에 처박혔다.\n\n배가 모래 더미 위로 올라타며 당연히 상당한 충격이 있었다. 모든 활대와 밧줄과 버팀줄이 팽팽히 당겨졌고, 윗부분의 삭구 일부가 무너져 내렸다. 그러나 무엇보다 기이한 것은, 배가 해안에 닿는 바로 그 순간, 거대한 개 한 마리가 충격에 튕겨 오르기라도 한 듯 아래에서 갑판 위로 솟구쳐 올라, 앞으로 달려가더니 뱃머리에서 모래 위로 뛰어내린 것이다. 그 개는 가파른 절벽을 향해 곧장 내달렸는데, 그곳은 묘지가 이스트 피어로 이어지는 좁은 길 위로 어찌나 가파르게 매달려 있는지 평평한 묘석들 가운데 일부 — 휘트비 방언으로 \"트러프스턴스\" 혹은 \"스루스톤스\"라 부르는 것들 — 가 떠받치던 절벽이 무너져 내린 자리 위로 실제로 비죽 튀어나와 있는 곳이었다. 개는 어둠 속으로 사라졌는데, 그 어둠은 탐조등 초점 바로 너머에서 한층 짙어진 듯했다.\n\n마침 그때 테이트 힐 피어에는 아무도 없었으니, 그 근처에 집을 둔 사람들은 모두 잠자리에 들었거나 위쪽 언덕에 나가 있었기 때문이다. 그리하여 항구 동쪽에서 근무하던 해안경비대원이 즉시 그 작은 부두로 달려 내려와 가장 먼저 배에 올랐다. 탐조등을 다루던 대원들은 항구 어귀를 샅샅이 훑었으나 아무것도 보지 못하자, 이번에는 그 버려진 배에 빛을 돌려 그곳에 고정했다. 해안경비대원이 고물 쪽으로 달려가 키 옆에 이르러 그것을 살피려 몸을 숙였다가, 어떤 갑작스러운 감정에 사로잡히기라도 한 듯 즉시 흠칫 물러섰다. 이것이 사람들의 호기심을 자극한 듯, 제법 많은 이들이 달려가기 시작했다. 웨스트 클리프에서 도개교를 거쳐 테이트 힐 피어까지는 빙 돌아가는 먼 길이지만, 본지 통신원은 제법 잘 달리는 편이라 군중보다 한참 앞서 도착했다. 그러나 내가 당도했을 때 이미 부두에는 군중이 모여 있었는데, 해안경비대와 경찰이 그들이 배에 오르는 것을 막고 있었다. 경비대장의 호의로, 나는 본지 통신원 자격으로 갑판에 오르도록 허락받아, 키에 실제로 묶여 있는 그 죽은 뱃사람을 본 소수의 무리 가운데 하나가 되었다.\n\n해안경비대원이 놀란 것도, 아니 경외감마저 느낀 것도 이상한 일이 아니었으니, 그런 광경을 볼 수 있는 일은 흔치 않기 때문이다. 그 사내는 그저 두 손이 한쪽이 다른 쪽 위로 포개진 채 키의 살에 묶여 있었다. 안쪽 손과 나무 사이에는 십자고상이 있었고, 그것이 매달린 묵주 알들이 양쪽 손목과 키를 함께 둘러 묶었으며, 그 모두가 동여맨 끈으로 단단히 고정되어 있었다. 그 가엾은 사내는 한때 앉아 있었을지도 모르나, 돛이 펄럭이고 들이치면서 키의 방향타까지 그 힘이 미쳐 그를 이리저리 끌어당겼던 탓에, 그를 묶은 끈이 살을 뼈까지 파고들어 있었다. 사물의 상태에 대해 정확한 기록이 작성되었고, 나보다 곧바로 뒤이어 도착한 의사 — 이스트 엘리엇 플레이스 33번지의 외과의 J. M. 캐핀 — 가 검진을 마친 뒤, 그 사내가 죽은 지 족히 이틀은 되었을 것이라고 단언했다. 그의 주머니에는 조심스레 코르크로 막은 병 하나가 있었는데, 작게 둘둘 만 종이쪽지 하나 외에는 비어 있었고, 그 종이는 항해일지의 부록임이 드러났다. 해안경비대원은 그 사내가 제 손으로 자기 손을 묶고 이로 매듭을 조였을 것이라고 말했다. 해안경비대원이 가장 먼저 배에 오른 사실은, 나중에 해사법원에서 얼마간의 분쟁을 덜어줄지도 모른다. 해안경비대원은 버려진 배에 가장 먼저 오른 민간인에게 주어지는 권리인 구난 보수를 청구할 수 없기 때문이다. 그러나 벌써부터 법조계 사람들이 입방아를 찧기 시작했고, 한 젊은 법학도는 소유주의 권리가 이미 완전히 박탈되었다고 큰 소리로 주장했는데, 위임된 점유의 증거까지는 아니라도 그 상징인 키가 죽은 손에 쥐여 있으니 그 재산이 영구양도금지법에 위배되는 방식으로 점유되고 있다는 것이었다. 그 죽은 키잡이가, 죽음에 이르기까지 그 명예로운 경계와 수호의 자리를 지켰던 — 어린 카사비안카의 그것에 못지않게 고결한 그 굳건함을 보이며 — 그 자리에서 정중히 옮겨져, 검시를 기다리도록 영안실에 안치되었음은 굳이 말할 필요도 없다.\n\n벌써 그 갑작스러운 폭풍은 지나가고 있고, 그 맹위도 수그러들고 있다. 군중은 집으로 흩어지고 있으며, 요크셔 구릉지 위로 하늘이 붉게 물들기 시작한다. 다음 호에 늦지 않게, 폭풍 속에 그토록 기적적으로 항구를 찾아든 그 버려진 배에 관한 더 자세한 내용을 보내겠다.\n\n휘트비.\n\n8월 9일. — 어젯밤 폭풍 속에 버려진 배가 기이하게 도착한 일의 후일담은 그 일 자체보다도 거의 더 놀랍다. 알고 보니 그 스쿠너선은 바르나에서 온 러시아 배로, 이름은 데메테르호였다. 배는 거의 전부 은모래 바닥짐을 실었고, 화물은 소량뿐이었다 — 흙을 가득 채운 커다란 나무 상자 여러 개였다. 이 화물은 더 크레슨트 7번지의 휘트비 변호사 S. F. 빌링턴 씨 앞으로 탁송된 것이었는데, 그는 오늘 아침 배에 올라 자기 앞으로 탁송된 물품을 정식으로 인수했다. 러시아 영사 또한 용선계약 당사자를 대리하여 정식으로 배를 인수하고, 항만세 등 일체를 납부했다. 오늘 이곳에서는 그 기이한 우연 말고는 화젯거리가 없다. 무역위원회 관리들은 현행 규정이 빠짐없이 준수되었는지를 매우 까다롭게 확인했다. 이 일이 \"아흐레 동안의 화제\"가 될 것이므로, 그들은 분명 나중에 불평이 나올 빌미를 남기지 않으려고 단단히 마음먹은 것이다. 배가 좌초할 때 뭍에 내린 그 개를 두고 적잖은 관심이 일었고, 휘트비에서 꽤 세력이 큰 동물학대방지협회 회원 가운데 적잖은 이들이 그 짐승을 거두려고 애썼다. 그러나 모두의 실망스럽게도, 개는 찾을 수 없었다. 마을에서 완전히 사라져버린 듯했다. 어쩌면 겁에 질려 황야로 달아나, 아직도 거기 공포에 떨며 숨어 있는지도 모른다. 이런 가능성을 두려운 눈으로 보는 이들도 있으니, 나중에 그 개가 그 자체로 위험거리가 될까 해서다. 분명 사나운 짐승이기 때문이다. 오늘 이른 아침, 테이트 힐 피어 가까이 사는 석탄 상인의 큰 개, 잡종 마스티프 한 마리가 주인의 마당 맞은편 길바닥에서 죽은 채 발견되었다. 그 개는 싸움을 벌였고, 분명 사나운 상대를 만났던 것이니, 목이 찢겨 나갔고 배가 마치 사나운 발톱에 베인 듯 갈라져 있었기 때문이다.\n\n추가. — 무역위원회 검사관의 호의로, 나는 데메테르호의 항해일지를 살펴볼 수 있었는데, 그것은 사흘 전까지는 정연했으나, 사라진 사람들에 관한 사실 외에는 특별히 흥미로운 내용이 없었다. 그러나 가장 흥미로운 것은 병 속에서 발견된 종이쪽지에 관한 것으로, 그것은 오늘 검시에서 공개되었다. 그 둘이 함께 펼쳐 보이는 것보다 더 기이한 이야기를 나는 일찍이 마주친 적이 없다. 감출 동기가 없으므로 나는 그것을 쓰도록 허락받았고, 이에 따라 항해술과 적하감독에 관한 전문적인 세부사항만 생략한 채 그 사본을 보낸다. 거의 마치 선장이 깊은 바다에 채 들어서기도 전에 일종의 광기에 사로잡혔고, 그것이 항해 내내 끈질기게 깊어진 것처럼 보인다. 물론 내 진술은 에누리해서 받아들여야 하니, 시간이 촉박한 가운데 친절히 번역해준 러시아 영사관 서기의 구술을 받아 적은 것이기 때문이다.\n데메테르호의 항해일지.\n\n바르나에서 휘트비까지.\n\n7월 18일 기록. 너무도 기이한 일들이 일어나고 있어, 이제부터 뭍에 닿을 때까지 정확한 기록을 남기겠다.\n\n7월 6일, 화물 적재를 마침. 은모래와 흙 상자들. 정오에 출항. 동풍, 신선함. 선원은 다섯 명 ... 항해사 둘, 요리사, 그리고 나(선장).\n\n7월 11일, 새벽에 보스포루스 해협에 진입. 터키 세관원들이 승선. 뇌물. 모두 이상 없음. 오후 4시에 항해 재개.\n\n7월 12일, 다르다넬스 해협을 통과. 또 세관원들과 경비 함대의 기함선. 또 뇌물. 관리들의 일처리는 철저했으나 빨랐다. 우리를 어서 보내고 싶어 했다. 어두워질 무렵 다도해로 진입.\n\n7월 13일, 마타판 곶을 지남. 선원들이 무언가로 불만스러워함. 겁먹은 듯했으나 털어놓으려 하지 않음.\n\n7월 14일, 선원들 때문에 다소 불안했다. 모두 전에 나와 함께 항해한 적이 있는 한결같은 자들이다. 항해사도 무엇이 잘못되었는지 알아내지 못했다. 그들은 그저 무언가가 있다고만 말하며 성호를 그었다. 그날 항해사가 그중 한 명에게 화를 내고 그를 때렸다. 격렬한 다툼을 예상했으나, 모두 조용했다.\n\n7월 16일, 항해사가 아침에 선원 중 한 명인 페트로프스키가 사라졌다고 보고했다. 까닭을 알 수 없었다. 어젯밤 여덟 점종에 좌현 당직을 섰고 아브라모프와 교대했으나, 침상으로 가지 않았다. 선원들은 어느 때보다 풀이 죽었다. 모두 이런 일이 있으리라 예상했다고 말하면서도, 배 안에 무언가가 있다는 말 외에는 더 이상 말하려 하지 않았다. 항해사는 그들에게 몹시 짜증을 냈다. 앞으로 무슨 말썽이 생길까 두려웠다.\n\n7월 17일, 어제, 선원 중 한 명인 올가렌이 내 선실로 와서, 겁에 질린 투로, 배 안에 낯선 사내가 있는 것 같다고 털어놓았다. 그는 당직을 서다가 비바람이 몰아쳐 갑판실 뒤에 몸을 피하고 있었는데, 그때 키가 크고 마른, 선원 누구와도 닮지 않은 사내가 승강구 계단으로 올라와 갑판을 따라 앞으로 가더니 사라지는 것을 보았다고 했다. 그는 조심스레 뒤따랐으나, 뱃머리에 이르렀을 때 아무도 없었고 승강구는 모두 닫혀 있었다고 했다. 그는 미신적 공포에 사로잡혀 있었고, 나는 그 공포가 번질까 걱정스럽다. 그것을 가라앉히려고, 나는 오늘 배 전체를 이물에서 고물까지 샅샅이 뒤지겠다.\n\n그날 늦게, 나는 선원 전원을 불러 모아, 그들이 배 안에 누군가 있다고 분명히 여기고 있으니 이물에서 고물까지 뒤져보자고 말했다. 일등항해사는 화를 냈다. 어리석은 짓이며, 그런 어리석은 생각에 굴복하면 선원들의 사기가 무너진다고 했다. 자기가 쇠지렛대로 그들이 말썽 부리지 않도록 책임지겠다고 했다. 나는 그에게 키를 맡기고, 나머지는 등불을 들고 나란히 줄지어 철저히 수색을 시작했다. 우리는 구석구석 빠짐없이 뒤졌다. 커다란 나무 상자들밖에 없었으므로, 사람이 숨을 만한 후미진 구석은 없었다. 수색이 끝나자 선원들은 크게 안도하여 즐거이 일터로 돌아갔다. 일등항해사는 얼굴을 찌푸렸으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n7월 22일. — 지난 사흘간 거친 날씨였고, 전원이 돛에 매달려 분주했다 — 겁먹을 겨를이 없었다. 선원들은 두려움을 잊은 듯하다. 항해사도 다시 명랑해졌고, 모두 사이가 좋다. 궂은 날씨 속의 노고를 두고 선원들을 칭찬했다. 지브롤터를 지나 해협을 빠져나왔다. 모두 무사하다.\n\n7월 24일. — 이 배에는 어떤 운명이 드리워 있는 듯하다. 이미 일손 하나가 부족한 채, 앞으로 험한 날씨가 기다리는 비스케이 만에 들어서는데, 어젯밤 또 한 사람이 사라졌다 — 자취를 감추었다. 첫 번째 사람처럼, 그도 당직을 마치고 나서 다시는 보이지 않았다. 선원들은 모두 공포에 사로잡혀, 혼자 있기를 두려워하니 이중 당직을 서게 해달라는 연명 청원서를 냈다. 항해사는 화를 냈다. 무슨 말썽이 생길까 두렵다. 그 아니면 선원들이 무슨 난폭한 짓을 저지를 것 같다.\n\n7월 28일. — 일종의 소용돌이 속에서 나흘간 지옥을 헤맸고, 바람은 폭풍이었다. 누구도 잠을 자지 못했다. 선원들은 모두 기진맥진했다. 당직을 세울 만한 자가 아무도 없으니 어떻게 당직을 세워야 할지 막막하다. 이등항해사가 자청해 키를 잡고 망을 보아, 선원들에게 몇 시간이나마 눈을 붙이게 했다. 바람은 잦아들고 있다. 바다는 여전히 무섭지만, 배가 더 안정되어 덜 느껴진다.\n\n7월 29일. — 또 비극이 일어났다. 선원들이 너무 지쳐 이중 당직을 설 수 없어 오늘 밤 단독 당직을 세웠다. 아침 당직이 갑판에 올라왔을 때 키잡이 외에는 아무도 찾을 수 없었다. 고함을 질렀고, 모두 갑판으로 올라왔다. 철저히 수색했으나, 아무도 찾지 못했다. 이제 이등항해사가 없고, 선원들은 공포에 사로잡혔다. 항해사와 나는 앞으로 무장하고 어떤 원인의 징후라도 기다리기로 합의했다.\n\n7월 30일. — 어젯밤. 영국에 가까워지고 있어 기뻤다. 날씨는 청명하고 돛은 모두 펼쳤다. 기진맥진하여 잠자리에 들었다. 푹 잤다. 당직 선원과 키잡이가 둘 다 사라졌다고 항해사가 알려와 잠에서 깼다. 이제 배를 부릴 사람은 나와 항해사, 그리고 선원 둘뿐이다.\n\n8월 1일. — 이틀간 안개가 끼었고, 돛 하나 보이지 않는다. 영국 해협에 들어서면 구조 신호를 보내거나 어디든 들어갈 수 있으리라 기대했었다. 돛을 부릴 인원이 없으니 바람에 떠밀려 가는 수밖에 없다. 돛을 내릴 엄두가 나지 않으니, 다시 올릴 수가 없기 때문이다. 우리는 어떤 무서운 운명을 향해 떠내려가고 있는 듯하다. 항해사는 이제 두 선원 누구보다도 사기가 꺾여 있다. 그의 더 강한 기질이 도리어 안으로 그 자신을 갉아먹은 듯하다. 선원들은 두려움을 넘어, 최악을 각오한 채 묵묵히 참을성 있게 일한다. 그들은 러시아인이고, 그는 루마니아인이다.\n\n8월 2일, 자정. — 몇 분간의 잠에서, 내 선창 바깥인 듯한 곳에서 들려온 비명에 깨어났다. 안개 속에서는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 갑판으로 뛰쳐나가다 항해사와 부딪혔다. 그는 비명을 듣고 달려왔으나 당직 선원의 흔적이 없다고 했다. 또 한 사람이 사라졌다. 주여, 우리를 도우소서! 항해사 말로는, 안개가 한순간 걷힐 때 노스 포어랜드를 보았으니 우리가 도버 해협을 지났을 게 분명하다고 했다. 마침 그 사내가 비명을 지르는 소리를 들었을 때였다. 그렇다면 우리는 이제 북해에 나와 있는 것이고, 우리와 함께 움직이는 듯한 이 안개 속에서는 오직 신만이 우리를 인도하실 수 있다. 그런데 신은 우리를 버리신 듯하다.\n\n8월 3일. — 자정에 나는 키 잡은 자와 교대하러 갔는데, 거기에 이르러 보니 아무도 없었다. 바람은 한결같았고, 우리는 그것을 등지고 달리고 있어 배가 좌우로 흔들리지 않았다. 나는 감히 키를 떠날 수 없어 항해사를 불렀다. 몇 초 뒤 그는 플란넬 잠옷 차림으로 갑판으로 뛰쳐 올라왔다. 그의 눈빛은 사납고 얼굴은 초췌했으며, 나는 그의 이성이 무너졌을까 몹시 두렵다. 그는 내게 바짝 다가와, 마치 공기조차 들을까 두려운 듯 내 귀에 입을 대고 쉰 목소리로 속삭였다. \"그것이 여기 있어요. 이제 알겠어요. 어젯밤 당직 때 그것을 보았어요. 사람 같은데, 키가 크고 마르고 소름 끼치게 창백했어요. 그것은 뱃머리에서 밖을 내다보고 있었어요. 나는 그 뒤로 살금살금 다가가 칼로 찔렀어요. 그런데 칼은 공기처럼 텅 빈 그것을 그대로 뚫고 지나갔어요.\" 그렇게 말하면서 그는 칼을 꺼내 허공을 사납게 찔렀다. 그러더니 말을 이었다. \"하지만 그것은 여기 있고, 나는 그것을 찾아낼 거예요. 그것은 선창에, 어쩌면 저 상자들 중 하나에 있어요. 내가 하나하나 나사를 풀어 들여다보겠어요. 당신은 키를 부리세요.\" 그러고는 경고하는 눈빛으로 손가락을 입술에 댄 채 아래로 내려갔다. 사나운 바람이 일기 시작하여, 나는 키를 떠날 수 없었다. 그가 연장통과 등불을 들고 다시 갑판으로 나와 앞쪽 승강구로 내려가는 것을 보았다. 그는 미쳤다, 완전히, 발광하도록 미쳤다. 내가 그를 막으려 해봐야 소용없다. 그는 그 큰 상자들을 해치지 못한다. 그것들은 송장에 \"점토\"로 기재되어 있으니, 그것들을 이리저리 끌어대는 일이야말로 그가 할 수 있는 가장 무해한 짓이다. 그러니 나는 여기 머물러 키를 지키며 이 기록을 쓴다. 나는 그저 신을 믿고 안개가 걷히기를 기다릴 수밖에 없다. 그때 가서 지금 부는 바람으로 어느 항구로도 키를 잡아갈 수 없거든, 돛을 내리고 표류하며 구조 신호를 보내겠다....\n\n이제 거의 다 끝났다. 항해사가 한결 차분해져 나오기를 막 바라기 시작했을 때 — 그가 선창에서 무언가를 두드리는 소리가 들렸고, 일하는 것이 그에게는 이로우니 — 승강구에서 갑작스럽고 소스라치는 비명이 솟구쳐 올라와 내 피를 얼어붙게 했고, 그가 총에서 튕겨 나오듯 갑판으로 올라왔다 — 눈을 부라리고 얼굴이 공포로 일그러진, 광란하는 미치광이였다. \"살려줘요! 살려줘요!\" 그가 외쳤다. 그러더니 안개의 장막을 둘러보았다. 그의 공포는 절망으로 바뀌었고, 그는 차분한 목소리로 말했다. \"당신도 함께 가는 게 좋아요, 선장님, 너무 늦기 전에요. 그가 저기 있어요. 이제 비밀을 알았어요. 바다가 나를 그에게서 구해줄 거예요. 남은 건 그것뿐이에요!\" 내가 한마디 하거나 그를 붙들려고 앞으로 나서기도 전에, 그는 뱃전으로 뛰어올라 거침없이 바다로 몸을 던졌다. 짐작건대 이제 나도 그 비밀을 안다. 선원들을 하나하나 없앤 것이 바로 이 미치광이였고, 이제 그 자신도 그들을 뒤따른 것이다. 신이여, 저를 도우소서! 항구에 닿으면 이 온갖 끔찍한 일들을 어떻게 설명해야 하나? 내가 항구에 닿거든 말이다! 그런 일이 과연 있을까?\n\n8월 4일. — 여전히 안개, 해돋이도 뚫지 못한다. 나는 해가 떴음을 안다, 내가 뱃사람이기 때문이다. 그 밖에 어떻게 아는지는 모르겠다. 나는 감히 아래로 내려갈 수 없었고, 감히 키를 떠날 수도 없었다. 그래서 밤새 여기 머물렀는데, 밤의 어스름 속에서 나는 그것을 — 그를 보았다! 신이여 저를 용서하소서, 하지만 항해사가 바다에 몸을 던진 것은 옳았다. 사내답게 죽는 편이 나았다. 뱃사람답게 푸른 바다에서 죽는 것을 누구도 탓할 수 없으리라. 하지만 나는 선장이니, 내 배를 떠나서는 안 된다. 그러나 나는 이 악마인지 괴물인지를 보기 좋게 따돌리겠다. 기력이 다하기 시작하면 내 손을 키에 묶고, 그 손과 함께, 그가 — 그것이! — 감히 건드리지 못하는 것을 함께 묶겠다. 그러면 순풍이 불든 역풍이 불든, 나는 내 영혼과 선장으로서의 명예를 지키겠다. 나는 점점 기력이 빠지고, 밤이 다가온다. 만약 그가 다시 내 얼굴을 마주 볼 수 있게 되면, 나는 행동할 시간이 없을지도 모른다.... 우리가 난파하거든, 아마도 이 병이 발견되리니, 그것을 발견한 이들이 부디 알아주기를. 그렇지 못하거든, ... 그래도 모든 이가 내가 내 소임에 충실했음을 알게 되리라. 신과 복되신 동정녀와 성인들이여, 제 의무를 다하려는 가엾고 무지한 영혼을 도우소서....\n\n물론 평결은 미결로 났다. 제시할 증거가 없었다. 그 사내 자신이 살인을 저질렀는지 아닌지를 이제 말할 수 있는 이는 아무도 없다. 이곳 사람들은 그 선장이 그야말로 영웅이라고 거의 한결같이 여기며, 그에게 공개 장례를 치러주기로 했다. 벌써 그의 시신을 배들의 행렬과 함께 에스크 강을 따라 한참 거슬러 올라갔다가 다시 테이트 힐 피어로 데려와 수도원 계단을 올라가도록 하는 절차가 마련되었다. 그는 절벽 위 묘지에 묻힐 것이기 때문이다. 벌써 백 척이 넘는 배의 주인들이 그를 무덤까지 따르고 싶다며 이름을 올렸다.\n\n그 거대한 개의 흔적은 끝내 찾지 못했는데, 이를 두고 애석해하는 이가 많다. 지금의 여론대로라면 그 개는 마을에서 거두어 키우게 되었을 것이기 때문이다. 내일이면 장례가 치러질 것이고, 이로써 또 하나의 \"바다의 수수께끼\"가 끝을 맺을 것이다.\n\n미나 머리의 일기.\n\n8월 8일. — 루시는 밤새도록 몹시 잠을 설쳤고, 나 또한 잠들 수 없었다. 폭풍은 무시무시했고, 굴뚝갓 사이에서 요란하게 우르릉거릴 때면 나는 몸서리쳤다. 날카로운 돌풍이 한 차례 몰아칠 때면 마치 먼 곳의 대포 소리 같았다. 이상하게도 루시는 깨지 않았다. 그러나 두 번이나 일어나 옷을 차려입었다. 다행히도 그때마다 나는 제때 깨어, 그녀를 깨우지 않고 옷을 벗겨 다시 침대에 눕힐 수 있었다. 이 몽유병은 참으로 기이한 것이니, 그녀의 의지가 무슨 물리적 방식으로든 가로막히면, 그 의도는, 설령 어떤 의도가 있다 해도, 사라져버리고, 그녀는 거의 정확히 자기 일상의 틀에 다시 몸을 맡기기 때문이다.\n\n이른 아침, 우리 둘은 일어나 항구로 내려가 밤사이 무슨 일이 있었는지 보러 갔다. 나와 있는 사람은 거의 없었고, 해는 밝고 공기는 맑고 신선했으나, 그 크고 험상궂어 보이는 파도가 — 마루를 덮은 거품이 눈처럼 희었기에 도리어 제 몸은 검어 보였다 — 항구의 좁은 어귀로 밀치고 들어왔다, 마치 군중 사이를 비집고 가는 윽박지르는 사내처럼. 어쩐지 나는 조너선이 어젯밤 바다가 아니라 뭍에 있어 다행이라는 생각이 들었다. 그러나, 아, 그는 뭍에 있을까 바다에 있을까? 그는 어디에, 어떻게 있을까? 나는 그가 너무도 걱정스러워진다. 무엇을 해야 할지 알기만 하면, 무엇이든 할 수 있기만 하면!\n\n8월 10일. — 오늘 가엾은 그 선장의 장례는 더없이 가슴 뭉클했다. 항구의 모든 배가 다 나온 듯했고, 관은 테이트 힐 피어에서 묘지까지 줄곧 선장들이 메고 갔다. 루시도 나와 함께 왔고, 우리는 일찍 우리의 옛 자리로 갔는데, 그동안 배들의 행렬은 강을 거슬러 고가교까지 올라갔다가 다시 내려왔다. 우리는 더없이 좋은 전망을 누렸고, 행렬을 거의 내내 볼 수 있었다. 그 가엾은 사내는 우리 자리에서 아주 가까운 곳에 묻혔으므로, 때가 되자 우리는 그 자리에 서서 모든 것을 지켜보았다. 가엾은 루시는 몹시 심란해 보였다. 그녀는 내내 안절부절못하며 불안해했고, 밤마다 꾸는 꿈이 그녀를 괴롭히고 있다고밖에 생각할 수 없다. 그녀는 한 가지 점에서 자못 이상한데, 안절부절못할 까닭이 무엇이든 있다는 것을 나에게 인정하려 하지 않는다. 혹은 까닭이 있다 해도, 그녀 자신도 그것을 이해하지 못한다. 게다가 또 하나의 까닭이 있으니, 오늘 아침 가엾은 노인 스웨일스 씨가 우리 자리에서 목이 부러진 채 죽은 채로 발견된 것이다. 의사의 말마따나 그는 분명 무슨 두려움에 사로잡혀 자리에 뒤로 넘어진 것이니, 그의 얼굴에는 사람들이 보기에 몸서리쳐질 만한 공포와 경악의 표정이 어려 있었기 때문이다. 가엾고 정겨운 노인! 어쩌면 그는 죽어가는 눈으로 죽음을 보았는지도 모른다! 루시는 어찌나 다정하고 예민한지 다른 사람들보다 그런 기운을 한층 날카롭게 느낀다. 방금도 그녀는 내가 별로 마음에 두지 않은 사소한 일에 자못 심란해했는데, 나 역시 동물을 무척 좋아하면서도 그랬다. 이곳에 자주 배를 보러 올라오던 한 사내가 자기 개를 데리고 왔다. 그 개는 늘 그와 함께 있다. 둘 다 조용한 편이고, 나는 그 사내가 화내는 것도, 그 개가 짖는 것도 본 적이 없다. 그런데 예배가 진행되는 동안 그 개는 우리와 함께 자리에 있던 주인에게 오려 하지 않고, 몇 발짝 떨어져 멈춘 채 짖고 울부짖었다. 주인이 다정하게, 그다음엔 매섭게, 그다음엔 성난 목소리로 타일렀으나, 개는 오지도 않고 소란을 멈추지도 않았다. 개는 일종의 광란 상태로, 눈은 사납고, 온 털은 고양이가 싸움에 나설 때 꼬리털처럼 곤두서 있었다. 마침내 사내도 화가 나서, 뛰어 내려가 개를 걷어차고, 목덜미를 잡아 반쯤 끌고 반쯤 던지다시피 하여 자리가 박힌 그 묘석 위에 올려놓았다. 돌에 닿는 순간 그 가엾은 짐승은 잠잠해지더니 온통 떨기 시작했다. 개는 달아나려 하지 않고, 몸을 웅크린 채 부들부들 떨며 잔뜩 겁에 질려 움츠러들었는데, 그 처지가 어찌나 가련했던지 나는 효과는 없었으나 달래주려 애썼다. 루시도 가엾이 여기는 마음이 가득했으나, 개를 만지려 들지는 않고, 어딘가 괴로워하는 눈빛으로 그것을 바라보았다. 나는 그녀가 너무도 예민한 천성이라 이 세상을 탈 없이 살아가지 못할까 몹시 두렵다. 분명 오늘 밤 그녀는 이 일을 꿈에서 볼 것이다. 이 모든 일의 뒤엉킴이 — 죽은 사람이 키를 잡아 항구로 들어온 배, 십자고상과 묵주와 함께 키에 묶여 있던 그의 모습, 가슴 뭉클한 장례, 한순간 광란하고 한순간 공포에 떨던 개가 — 모두 그녀의 꿈에 재료가 되어줄 것이다.\n\n내 생각에는 그녀가 몸을 완전히 지치게 하여 잠자리에 드는 것이 가장 좋을 듯하니, 나는 그녀를 데리고 절벽을 따라 로빈 후드 만까지 멀리 다녀오겠다. 그러면 그녀도 몽유병을 일으킬 기색이 그리 많지 않을 것이다.\n\n# MINA MURRAY’S JOURNAL\n\n「미나 머리의 일기」\n같은 날, 밤 11시. — 아, 너무도 피곤하다! 일기를 쓰는 것을 하나의 의무로 삼아두지 않았더라면 오늘 밤은 펴보지도 않았을 것이다. 우리는 멋진 산책을 했다. 루시는 얼마 후 들떠서 명랑해졌는데, 등대 가까운 들판에서 사랑스러운 소 몇 마리가 우리 쪽으로 코를 들이밀며 다가와 우리 넋을 쏙 빼놓은 덕분이었던 것 같다. 우리는 물론 본능적인 두려움을 빼고는 모든 것을 잊어버린 듯했고, 그것이 마치 석판을 깨끗이 닦아내어 새 출발을 하게 해준 것 같았다. 우리는 로빈 후드 만(灣)의 아담하고 예스러운 작은 여관에서 근사하게 \"푸짐한 차\"를 들었는데, 활처럼 휜 내닫이창이 바닷가의 해초 덮인 바위들 바로 위로 나 있었다. 우리의 왕성한 식욕을 보았다면 \"신여성\"들은 충격을 받았을 것이다. 남자들은 더 너그럽다. 그들에게 복이 있기를! 그러고 나서 우리는 집으로 걸어왔는데, 쉬느라 몇 번, 아니 여러 번 멈췄고, 마음속은 사나운 황소들에 대한 끊임없는 두려움으로 가득했다. 루시는 정말 지쳐 있었고, 우리는 되도록 빨리 살그머니 잠자리에 들 생각이었다. 그러나 젊은 부목사가 찾아왔고, 웨스튼라 부인이 그에게 저녁식사를 하고 가라고 청했다. 루시도 나도 졸음이라는 적과 한바탕 싸움을 벌여야 했다. 내 쪽 싸움이 힘겨웠음을 나는 안다. 나는 자못 영웅적이었던 것이다. 언젠가 주교들이 모여, 아무리 권유를 받아도 저녁식사를 하지 않으며 처녀들이 언제 피곤한지를 알아차리는 새로운 부류의 부목사를 길러내는 일을 의논해야 하리라고 생각한다. 루시는 잠들어 가만히 숨쉬고 있다. 평소보다 두 뺨에 혈색이 돌고, 아, 어찌나 사랑스러워 보이는지. 홈우드 씨가 응접실에서만 그녀를 보고도 사랑에 빠졌다면, 지금의 그녀를 본다면 뭐라고 할지 궁금하다. 언젠가 \"신여성\" 작가들 중 누군가는, 남녀가 청혼을 하거나 받아들이기 전에 서로의 잠든 모습을 볼 수 있어야 한다는 생각을 내놓을 것이다. 하지만 짐작건대 신여성은 앞으로 받아들이는 일 따위에 자신을 낮추지 않으리라. 청혼은 그녀가 직접 할 것이다. 게다가 그 일을 아주 멋지게 해낼 테고! 거기에 얼마간 위안이 있다. 오늘 밤 나는 무척 행복하다. 사랑스러운 루시가 한결 나아진 듯 보이기 때문이다. 정말이지 그녀가 고비를 넘겼고, 꿈자리 때문에 겪던 곤란에서 벗어난 것이라고 믿는다. 조너선의 소식만 알 수 있다면 나는 더없이 행복할 텐데…… 신이여 그를 축복하시고 지켜주소서.\n\n8월 11일, 새벽 3시. — 다시 일기를 쓴다. 이제 잠이 오지 않으니 차라리 글이라도 쓰는 편이 낫겠다. 너무 마음이 흔들려 잠을 이룰 수가 없다. 우리는 참으로 큰 모험을, 참으로 가슴 졸이는 일을 겪었다. 나는 일기를 덮자마자 잠이 들었다…… 그러다 갑자기 완전히 잠이 깨어 몸을 일으켜 앉았는데, 끔찍한 공포감과 주위가 텅 빈 듯한 어떤 느낌에 휩싸였다. 방이 어두워 루시의 침대가 보이지 않기에, 나는 가만히 다가가 그녀를 더듬어 찾았다. 침대는 비어 있었다. 성냥을 켜보니 그녀는 방 안에 없었다. 문은 닫혀 있었으나, 내가 그대로 두었던 대로 잠겨 있지는 않았다. 요즘 들어 유난히 편찮으신 그녀의 어머니를 깨우기가 두려워, 나는 옷을 대충 걸치고 그녀를 찾아 나설 채비를 했다. 방을 나서려는데 문득, 그녀가 입은 옷이 그녀의 몽유(夢遊) 의도에 어떤 실마리를 줄지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다. 실내복을 입었다면 집 안에 있다는 뜻이고, 외출복이라면 밖이라는 뜻이었다. 실내복도 외출복도 둘 다 제자리에 있었다. \"다행이야,\" 하고 나는 혼잣말을 했다. \"잠옷 차림이니 멀리 가지는 못했을 거야.\" 나는 아래층으로 달려 내려가 거실을 들여다보았다. 거기 없었다! 그다음 나는 점점 커지는 두려움에 가슴이 얼어붙은 채, 집 안의 열린 방들을 모두 살펴보았다. 마침내 현관문에 이르러 보니 문이 열려 있었다. 활짝 열린 것은 아니었으나, 자물쇠의 걸쇠가 채워지지 않았다. 이 집 사람들은 밤마다 문을 꼼꼼히 잠그므로, 나는 루시가 그 차림 그대로 밖으로 나간 것이 틀림없다고 두려워했다. 무슨 일이 벌어질지 생각할 겨를도 없었다. 막연하면서도 압도적인 두려움이 온갖 자잘한 생각을 흐려놓았다. 나는 크고 두툼한 숄을 집어 들고 밖으로 달려 나갔다. 크레센트에 이르렀을 때 시계가 한 시를 치고 있었고, 사람 그림자 하나 보이지 않았다. 나는 노스 테라스를 따라 달렸으나, 내가 보리라 예상한 흰 형체의 자취는 어디에도 없었다. 부두 위 웨스트 클리프 가장자리에서 나는 항구 너머 이스트 클리프 쪽을 바라보았다. 우리가 가장 좋아하는 그 자리에 루시가 있기를 바라는, 아니 두려워하는 — 어느 쪽인지 나도 모르겠다 — 마음으로. 밝은 보름달이 떠 있었고, 묵직한 검은 구름이 빠르게 흘러가며 온 풍경을 명암이 명멸하는 디오라마로 만들었다. 한두 순간 동안은 아무것도 보이지 않았으니, 구름 그림자가 성 메리 성당과 그 주위를 온통 가렸기 때문이다. 그러다 구름이 지나가자 폐허가 된 수도원이 시야에 들어왔다. 칼로 벤 듯 날카로운 좁은 빛줄기의 가장자리가 옮겨 가면서, 성당과 묘지가 차츰 모습을 드러냈다. 내 예상이 무엇이었든 그것은 어긋나지 않았으니, 바로 거기, 우리가 가장 좋아하는 그 자리에, 달의 은빛이 눈처럼 흰, 반쯤 기대 누운 형체를 비추고 있었기 때문이다. 구름이 너무 빨리 다가와 많은 것을 보지는 못했으니, 거의 곧바로 그림자가 빛 위로 내려앉았기 때문이다. 그러나 흰 형체가 빛나는 그 자리 뒤에 무언가 어두운 것이 서서, 그 위로 몸을 굽히고 있는 것처럼 보였다. 그것이 무엇인지, 사람인지 짐승인지 나는 분간할 수 없었다. 나는 한 번 더 살펴볼 겨를도 없이, 가파른 계단을 날듯이 내려가 부두로, 그리고 어시장을 지나 다리로 향했는데, 그것이 이스트 클리프로 가는 유일한 길이었다. 마을은 죽은 듯이 고요했으니, 사람 하나 보이지 않았다. 나는 그것이 다행이라 여겼으니, 가엾은 루시의 그런 모습을 목격할 사람을 원치 않았기 때문이다. 시간도 거리도 끝이 없는 듯했고, 수도원으로 오르는 끝없는 계단을 힘겹게 오르는 동안 두 무릎은 후들거리고 숨은 가빠졌다. 나는 분명 빨리 갔건만, 마치 두 발에 납덩이가 매달린 듯, 몸의 마디마디가 녹슨 듯 느껴졌다. 거의 꼭대기에 다다랐을 때 그 자리와 흰 형체가 보였으니, 이제 가까이 와 그림자가 드리운 사이로도 그것을 분간할 만했기 때문이다. 반쯤 기대 누운 흰 형체 위로 길고 검은 무언가가 몸을 굽히고 있는 것이 틀림없었다. 나는 겁에 질려 \"루시! 루시!\" 하고 불렀고, 무언가가 고개를 들었는데, 내가 선 자리에서도 흰 얼굴과 붉게 번득이는 두 눈이 보였다. 루시는 대답하지 않았고, 나는 묘지 입구로 달려갔다. 안으로 들어서자 성당이 나와 그 자리 사이를 가로막아, 한동안 나는 그녀를 시야에서 놓쳤다. 다시 시야에 들어왔을 때 구름은 지나가 있었고, 달빛이 어찌나 환히 비추던지 나는 루시가 머리를 그 자리 등받이에 기댄 채 반쯤 누워 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그녀는 완전히 혼자였고, 주위에는 살아 있는 것의 자취라곤 하나도 없었다.\n\n내가 그녀 위로 몸을 굽히자 그녀가 아직 잠들어 있음을 알 수 있었다. 입술이 벌어져 있었고, 그녀는 숨을 쉬고 있었는데 — 평소처럼 가만한 숨이 아니라, 매번 숨마다 폐를 가득 채우려 안간힘을 쓰는 듯, 길고 무거운 헐떡임이었다. 내가 가까이 다가가자, 그녀는 잠결에 손을 들어 잠옷 깃을 끌어당겨 목을 단단히 여몄다. 그러면서 그녀의 몸에 작은 떨림이 스쳤는데, 마치 추위를 느끼는 듯했다. 나는 따뜻한 숄을 그녀에게 둘러주고 그 가장자리를 목 둘레에 단단히 여며주었으니, 그렇게 헐벗은 채로 밤공기에 치명적인 한기라도 들까 두려웠기 때문이다. 그녀를 단번에 깨우기가 겁이 나, 그녀를 돌보느라 두 손을 자유롭게 쓰려고 나는 커다란 안전핀으로 그녀의 목께에 숄을 여며 고정했다. 그런데 조바심 탓에 서툴렀던지 핀으로 그녀를 꼬집었거나 찌른 모양으로, 이윽고 그녀의 숨결이 한결 잦아들었을 때 그녀는 다시 손을 목으로 가져가며 신음했다. 그녀를 조심스레 잘 감싼 뒤, 나는 내 신발을 그녀의 발에 신기고 아주 부드럽게 그녀를 깨우기 시작했다. 처음에는 반응이 없었으나, 차츰 그녀는 잠결에 점점 더 불안해하며 이따금 신음하고 한숨을 내쉬었다. 마침내 시간이 빠르게 흐르고 있었고, 또 여러 다른 이유로도 나는 그녀를 당장 집으로 데려가고 싶었기에, 더 세게 흔들었더니 마침내 그녀가 눈을 뜨고 깨어났다. 그녀는 나를 보고도 놀라는 기색이 없었는데, 물론 자기가 어디 있는지 단번에 깨닫지 못했기 때문이었다. 루시는 언제나 깰 때 어여쁜데, 몸이 추위로 얼었을 것이 분명하고, 밤중에 묘지에서 헐벗은 채 깨어나 마음이 적잖이 질겁했을 그런 때에도 그녀는 우아함을 잃지 않았다. 그녀는 조금 떨며 나에게 매달렸다. 당장 함께 집으로 가자고 하자 그녀는 한마디 없이, 어린아이처럼 고분고분 몸을 일으켰다. 우리가 지나가는 동안 자갈이 내 발을 아프게 했고, 루시는 내가 움찔하는 것을 알아챘다. 그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 내 신발을 도로 신으라고 한사코 우겼으나, 나는 그러지 않았다. 그래도 묘지 밖 오솔길에 이르니, 폭풍우가 남긴 물웅덩이가 있어, 나는 한 발씩 번갈아 다른 발에 비벼 진흙을 발에 발랐으니, 집으로 가는 길에 혹 누구를 마주치더라도 내 맨발을 아무도 알아채지 못하게 하려는 것이었다.\n\n운이 우리 편이어서, 우리는 사람 하나 마주치지 않고 집에 닿았다. 한 번 우리 앞 거리를 지나가는, 술이 좀 덜 깬 듯한 남자 하나를 보았으나, 우리는 그가 이곳에 흔한 그런 골목 — 가파르고 작은 샛길, 스코틀랜드에서는 \"윈드\"라고 부르는 — 으로 사라질 때까지 어느 문간에 숨었다. 내내 심장이 어찌나 크게 뛰던지 이따금 기절할 것만 같았다. 나는 루시 걱정으로 가득했는데, 그녀의 건강을 위해서, 곧 한기에 노출되어 탈이 날까 봐서만이 아니라, 혹 이 일이 소문이라도 날 경우 그녀의 평판을 위해서이기도 했다. 우리가 안에 들어와 발을 씻고 함께 감사의 기도를 올린 뒤, 나는 그녀를 침대에 뉘어 이불을 덮어주었다. 잠들기 전에 그녀는 자기 몽유 사건에 대해 누구에게도, 심지어 자기 어머니에게도 한마디도 하지 말아달라고 청했다 — 아니, 간청했다. 처음에 나는 약속하기를 망설였으나, 그녀 어머니의 건강 상태를, 그리고 그런 일을 알게 되면 얼마나 애를 태우실지를 생각하고, 또 그런 이야기가 새어 나갈 경우 얼마나 왜곡될지 — 아니, 틀림없이 왜곡되고 말 것을 생각하니, 그러는 편이 현명하겠다 싶었다. 내가 옳게 한 것이기를 바란다. 나는 문을 잠갔고, 열쇠를 손목에 묶어두었으니, 어쩌면 다시는 방해받지 않을지도 모르겠다. 루시는 곤히 잠들어 있다. 새벽의 어스름이 바다 위로 높이, 멀리 비치고 있다……\n\n같은 날, 정오. — 모든 것이 순조롭다. 루시는 내가 깨울 때까지 잤고, 돌아눕지조차 않은 듯했다. 간밤의 모험이 그녀에게 해를 끼친 것 같지는 않다. 오히려 그것이 그녀에게 이로웠으니, 오늘 아침 그녀는 몇 주 만에 가장 좋아 보인다. 안전핀을 다루다 서툴러 그녀에게 상처를 입힌 것을 알고 나는 마음이 아팠다. 사실 자칫 심각할 수도 있었으니, 그녀의 목 살갗이 뚫려 있었던 것이다. 분명 늘어진 살갗 한 점을 집어 올려 꿰뚫었던 모양으로, 바늘로 찌른 듯한 작고 붉은 점 두 개가 있고, 그녀의 잠옷 깃에는 핏방울 하나가 묻어 있었다. 내가 사과하며 그 일을 마음 쓰자, 그녀는 웃으며 나를 다독였고, 아무 느낌조차 없었다고 말했다. 다행히 너무 작아서 흉터는 남지 않을 것이다.\n\n같은 날, 밤. — 우리는 행복한 하루를 보냈다. 공기는 맑고 햇살은 밝았으며, 시원한 산들바람이 불었다. 우리는 점심을 싸들고 멀그레이브 숲으로 갔는데, 웨스튼라 부인은 길을 따라 마차로 가고, 루시와 나는 절벽 오솔길로 걸어가 문 앞에서 부인과 합류했다. 나는 좀 서글퍼졌으니, 조너선이 나와 함께 있었더라면 이 일이 얼마나 완전히 행복했을까 하는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 어쩌랴! 나는 그저 참고 견뎌야 한다. 저녁에 우리는 카지노 테라스를 거닐며 슈포어와 매켄지의 좋은 음악을 듣고, 일찍 잠자리에 들었다. 루시는 한동안 보지 못한 편안한 모습이었고, 곧바로 잠이 들었다. 오늘 밤은 별 탈이 없으리라 예상하지만, 나는 전과 똑같이 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 챙겨둘 것이다.\n\n8월 12일. — 내 예상은 틀렸으니, 밤사이 두 번이나 루시가 밖으로 나가려 하는 통에 잠을 깼기 때문이다. 그녀는 잠결에도 문이 잠긴 것을 알고 조금 짜증이 난 듯했고, 마지못해 못 이기는 척 침대로 돌아갔다. 나는 새벽녘에 깨어, 창밖에서 새들이 지저귀는 소리를 들었다. 루시도 깨어났는데, 반갑게도 전날 아침보다 한층 더 좋아 보였다. 예전의 그 명랑한 태도가 모두 되돌아온 듯, 그녀는 다가와 내 곁에 파고들어 아서에 대한 이야기를 모두 들려주었다. 나는 조너선이 얼마나 걱정스러운지를 말했고, 그러자 그녀는 나를 위로하려 애썼다. 음, 어느 정도는 성공했으니, 동정이 사실을 바꿀 수는 없어도 그것을 더 견딜 만하게 해주는 데는 도움이 되기 때문이다.\n\n8월 13일. — 또 한 번 조용한 하루를 보내고, 전과 같이 열쇠를 손목에 묶고 잠자리에 들었다. 나는 밤중에 다시 깨어, 루시가 침대에 일어나 앉은 채 여전히 잠든 상태로 창을 가리키고 있는 것을 발견했다. 나는 조용히 일어나 차양을 옆으로 젖히고 밖을 내다보았다. 환한 달빛이었고, 하나의 거대하고 고요한 신비로 어우러진 바다와 하늘 위로 부드럽게 어린 빛의 정취는 말로 다 할 수 없이 아름다웠다. 나와 달빛 사이로 큰 박쥐 한 마리가 휙휙 날며, 커다랗게 빙빙 도는 원을 그리며 오갔다. 한두 번은 아주 가까이 다가왔으나, 짐작건대 나를 보고 놀란 듯, 항구를 가로질러 수도원 쪽으로 날아가버렸다. 내가 창가에서 돌아왔을 때 루시는 다시 누워 평온히 잠들어 있었다. 그녀는 밤새 다시 뒤척이지 않았다.\n\n8월 14일. — 온종일 이스트 클리프에서 책을 읽고 글을 썼다. 루시도 나만큼이나 이 자리를 사랑하게 된 듯, 점심이나 차, 저녁식사 때문에 집으로 돌아갈 시간이 되어도 그녀를 떼어놓기가 어렵다. 오늘 오후 그녀는 묘한 말을 했다. 우리는 저녁식사를 하러 집으로 돌아오는 길에, 웨스트 피어에서 올라오는 계단 꼭대기에 이르러, 늘 그러듯 풍경을 보려고 멈춰 섰다. 하늘 낮게 걸린 지는 해가 막 케틀네스 너머로 떨어지고 있었다. 붉은 빛이 이스트 클리프와 옛 수도원 위로 던져져, 온 세상을 아름다운 장밋빛 광휘 속에 잠기게 하는 듯했다. 우리는 한동안 말이 없었는데, 갑자기 루시가 혼잣말하듯 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"그 붉은 눈이 또! 똑같아.\" 아무런 맥락도 없이 튀어나온 어찌나 야릇한 말이던지 나는 적잖이 놀랐다. 나는 루시를 빤히 쳐다보는 것처럼 보이지 않으면서도 그녀를 잘 볼 수 있도록 몸을 조금 돌렸는데, 그녀가 반쯤 꿈꾸는 듯한 상태로, 얼굴에 내가 도무지 알 수 없는 묘한 표정을 띠고 있는 것을 보았다. 그래서 나는 아무 말 없이 그녀의 시선을 따라갔다. 그녀는 우리 자리 쪽을 바라보고 있는 듯했는데, 그곳에는 어두운 형체 하나가 홀로 앉아 있었다. 나도 좀 놀랐으니, 한순간 그 낯선 이가 불타는 화염 같은 커다란 두 눈을 가진 것처럼 보였기 때문이다. 그러나 다시 보니 그 착각은 사라졌다. 붉은 햇빛이 우리 자리 뒤의 성 메리 성당 창문에 비치고 있었고, 해가 기울면서 굴절과 반사가 꼭 빛이 움직이는 것처럼 보이게 할 만큼 변했던 것이다. 나는 루시에게 그 특이한 현상을 일러주었고, 그녀는 흠칫하며 제정신으로 돌아왔으나, 그래도 슬퍼 보였다. 어쩌면 저 위에서 겪은 그 끔찍한 밤을 떠올리고 있었는지도 모른다. 우리는 그 일을 결코 입에 올리지 않는다. 그래서 나는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 우리는 저녁식사를 하러 집으로 돌아갔다. 루시는 머리가 아프다며 일찍 잠자리에 들었다. 나는 그녀가 잠든 것을 보고, 잠시 산책을 나갔다. 나는 절벽을 따라 서쪽으로 걸으며 달콤한 슬픔에 잠겼으니, 조너선을 생각하고 있었기 때문이다. 집으로 돌아오는 길에 — 그때는 환한 달빛이었고, 어찌나 환하던지 우리가 묵는 크레센트 구역의 정면은 그늘에 잠겨 있었어도 모든 것이 잘 보였다 — 나는 우리 창을 흘끗 올려다보다, 루시의 머리가 밖으로 내밀어진 것을 보았다. 어쩌면 그녀가 나를 기다리며 내다보는 것인가 싶어, 나는 손수건을 펼쳐 흔들었다. 그녀는 알아채지도, 아무 움직임도 보이지 않았다. 바로 그때 달빛이 건물 모퉁이를 돌아 들어와, 그 빛이 창에 떨어졌다. 거기에 루시가 분명히 있었으니, 머리를 창턱 옆면에 기대고 두 눈을 감은 채였다. 그녀는 곤히 잠들어 있었고, 그녀 곁 창턱에는 제법 큼직한 새처럼 보이는 무언가가 앉아 있었다. 나는 그녀가 한기가 들까 두려워 위층으로 달려갔으나, 방에 들어서자 그녀는 곤히 잠든 채, 숨을 거칠게 몰아쉬며 다시 침대 쪽으로 물러나고 있었다. 그녀는 마치 추위로부터 목을 보호하려는 듯, 한 손을 목에 대고 있었다.\n\n나는 그녀를 깨우지 않고, 따뜻하게 이불을 덮어주었다. 문이 잠겼는지, 창이 단단히 잠겼는지 단속해 두었다.\n\n그녀는 잠든 모습이 어찌나 사랑스러운지. 그러나 평소보다 얼굴이 창백하고, 두 눈 밑에 내가 마음에 들지 않는, 핼쑥하고 까칠한 기색이 어려 있다. 그녀가 무언가로 애를 태우고 있는 것이 아닐까 걱정스럽다. 그것이 무엇인지 알아낼 수 있다면 좋으련만.\n\n8월 15일. — 평소보다 늦게 일어났다. 루시는 나른하고 지쳐 있었고, 우리가 불려 일어난 뒤에도 계속 잤다. 우리는 아침식사 때 기쁜 소식에 깜짝 놀랐다. 아서의 아버지가 차도가 있어, 결혼이 곧 이루어지기를 바란다는 것이었다. 루시는 조용한 기쁨으로 가득하고, 그녀의 어머니는 기뻐하면서도 한편으로 서운해한다. 그날 늦게 부인은 나에게 그 까닭을 말해주었다. 루시를 온전히 자기 것으로 두지 못하고 떠나보내야 해서 슬프지만, 머지않아 그녀를 지켜줄 누군가가 생긴다는 것이 기쁘다는 것이었다. 가엾고 사랑스러운 부인! 부인은 자기가 사형선고를 받았음을 나에게 털어놓았다. 부인은 루시에게는 말하지 않았고, 나에게 비밀을 지킬 것을 약속하게 했다. 의사가 부인에게, 길어야 몇 달 안에 죽게 될 것이라고 했다는 것이니, 심장이 약해지고 있기 때문이었다. 언제라도, 지금이라도, 갑작스러운 충격을 받으면 거의 틀림없이 목숨을 잃으리라는 것이었다. 아, 루시의 그 끔찍한 몽유의 밤 일을 부인에게 숨긴 것은 현명한 일이었다.\n\n8월 17일. — 꼬박 이틀 동안 일기를 쓰지 못했다. 글을 쓸 마음이 나지 않았다. 어떤 어두운 장막 같은 것이 우리의 행복 위로 드리워지는 듯하다. 조너선에게서는 아무 소식이 없고, 루시는 점점 쇠약해지는 듯하며, 그녀 어머니의 남은 시간은 끝을 향해 줄어들고 있다. 나는 루시가 지금처럼 시들어가는 까닭을 이해할 수 없다. 그녀는 잘 먹고 잘 자고 신선한 공기를 즐긴다. 그런데도 내내 두 뺨의 장밋빛은 바래어가고, 날이 갈수록 더 약해지고 더 나른해진다. 밤이면 나는 그녀가 공기를 갈구하듯 헐떡이는 소리를 듣는다. 나는 밤이면 늘 방문 열쇠를 손목에 묶어두지만, 그녀는 일어나 방 안을 돌아다니고 열린 창가에 앉는다. 간밤에는 잠에서 깨어보니 그녀가 밖으로 몸을 내밀고 있었는데, 깨우려 해도 깨울 수가 없었다. 그녀는 기절해 있었던 것이다. 가까스로 그녀를 정신 차리게 했을 때 그녀는 물처럼 기운이 없었고, 길고 고통스럽게 숨을 몰아쉬는 사이사이 소리 없이 흐느꼈다. 어쩌다 창가에 와 있게 되었느냐고 묻자 그녀는 고개를 저으며 돌아누웠다. 그녀가 몸이 좋지 않은 것이 그 불운한 안전핀 자국 탓이 아니기를 바란다. 방금 그녀가 잠든 사이 목을 살펴보았는데, 그 작은 상처들이 아물지 않은 듯하다. 여전히 벌어져 있고, 오히려 전보다 더 커졌으며, 가장자리가 희미하게 희다. 가운데가 붉은, 작고 흰 점 같다. 하루 이틀 안에 아물지 않으면, 나는 의사가 그것을 살펴보도록 고집할 것이다.\n\n「휘트비의 변호사 새뮤얼 F. 빌링턴 부자(父子) 상회가 런던의 카터, 패터슨 상회 귀하에게 보낸 편지」\n\n8월 17일.\n\n근계(謹啓) —\n\n이에 그레이트 노던 철도편으로 보낸 화물의 송장(送狀)을 동봉하오. 화물은 킹스 크로스 화물역에 도착하는 즉시 퍼플리트 인근 카팩스로 배달되어야 하오. 그 집은 현재 비어 있으나, 동봉한 열쇠들을 확인하시기 바라며, 모든 열쇠에는 꼬리표가 달려 있소.\n\n이 탁송품을 이루는 상자 쉰 개를, 집의 일부를 이루며 동봉한 약도(略圖)에 'A'로 표시된 반쯤 허물어진 건물 안에 들여놓아 주시기 바라오. 그곳은 저택의 옛 예배당이므로 귀사 직원이 어렵지 않게 알아볼 것이오. 화물은 오늘 밤 9시 30분 기차로 떠나, 내일 오후 4시 30분에 킹스 크로스에 도착할 예정이오. 우리 의뢰인이 가능한 한 빠른 배달을 바라므로, 지정된 시각에 킹스 크로스에 운반조를 대기시켜 즉시 화물을 목적지로 운반해 주신다면 감사하겠소. 귀사 부서의 대금 지불 관련 통상 절차로 인해 생길 수 있는 일체의 지연을 피하고자, 이에 십 파운드(10£)짜리 수표를 동봉하니, 수령하시거든 알려주시기 바라오. 비용이 이 금액에 못 미친다면 차액을 돌려주시면 되고, 초과한다면 귀사의 연락을 받는 즉시 차액 수표를 보내드리겠소. 떠나실 때 열쇠들은 집의 중앙 홀에 두고 가시기 바라며, 그리하면 소유주가 자기 여벌 열쇠로 집에 들어가 그것들을 받을 수 있을 것이오.\n\n만사에 최대한 신속히 처리해 주실 것을 거듭 청하는 것을 두고, 부디 우리가 상업적 예의의 도를 넘었다고는 여기지 말아 주시오.\n\n이만 줄이오, 근계 —\n\n충실한,\n\n새뮤얼 F. 빌링턴 부자 상회.\n「런던의 카터, 패터슨 상회가 휘트비의 빌링턴 부자 상회 귀하에게 보낸 편지」\n\n8월 21일.\n\n근계 —\n\n10£를 수령하였음을 알리며, 이에 동봉한 영수 계산서에 나타난 대로 초과액 1£ 17실링 9펜스의 수표를 돌려드리오. 화물은 지시에 정확히 따라 배달되었으며, 열쇠들은 지시대로 꾸러미에 담아 중앙 홀에 두었소.\n\n이만 줄이오, 근계 —\n\n삼가 올림.\n\n미나 머리의 일기.\n\n「미나 머리의 일기」\n8월 18일. — 오늘 나는 행복하여, 묘지의 그 자리에 앉아 글을 쓴다. 루시는 더없이 좋아졌다. 간밤에 그녀는 밤새 잘 잤고, 단 한 번도 나를 깨우지 않았다. 두 뺨에 벌써 장밋빛이 돌아오는 듯한데, 아직도 가엾을 만큼 창백하고 핼쑥해 보인다. 그녀가 어떤 식으로든 빈혈이라면 이해할 수 있으련만, 그렇지가 않다. 그녀는 명랑하고 생기와 쾌활함으로 가득하다. 그 모든 병적인 과묵함이 사라진 듯, 그녀는 방금 나에게 — 내가 굳이 일깨워질 필요도 없는데 — 그날 밤 일을, 그리고 그곳, 바로 이 자리에서 내가 그녀가 잠든 것을 발견했음을 일깨워 주었다. 그러면서 그녀는 장난스럽게 부츠 뒤꿈치로 돌판을 톡톡 치며 말했다.\n\n\"내 가엾은 작은 발들이 그땐 별 소리도 안 냈겠지! 가엾은 스웨일스 영감님이라면, 그건 내가 조디를 깨우고 싶지 않았기 때문이라고 말했을 거야.\" 그녀가 이렇게 말이 많은 기분이기에, 나는 그날 밤 꿈을 꾸기라도 했느냐고 물었다. 대답하기 전에, 그 사랑스럽게 찌푸려지는 표정이 그녀의 이마에 떠올랐는데, 아서가 — 나는 그녀의 습관을 따라 그를 아서라고 부른다 — 사랑한다고 하는 바로 그 표정이었다. 사실 그가 그러는 것도 무리는 아니다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 반쯤 꿈꾸는 듯한 투로, 마치 그것을 스스로 떠올려 보려는 듯 말을 이었다.\n\n\"꿈을 꾼 건 아주 아니었는데, 모든 게 진짜인 것 같았어. 나는 그저 이 자리에 있고 싶었어 — 왜인지는 모르겠어. 무언가가 무서웠거든 — 그게 뭔지는 모르겠어. 잠들어 있었던 것 같은데도, 거리를 지나 다리를 건넌 게 기억나. 내가 지나갈 때 물고기 한 마리가 펄쩍 뛰어올라, 나는 그걸 보려고 몸을 굽혔어. 그리고 계단을 올라가는데 개들이 잔뜩 짖는 소리가 들렸어 — 마치 온 마을이 한꺼번에 짖어대는 개들로 가득 찬 것 같았어. 그러고 나서 길고 검고 붉은 눈을 한 무언가에 대한 어렴풋한 기억이 났어, 꼭 우리가 노을 속에서 본 그것처럼. 그리고 아주 달콤하면서도 아주 쓴 무언가가 한꺼번에 내 주위를 온통 감쌌어. 그러더니 나는 깊은 초록 물속으로 가라앉는 것 같았고, 귓속에서 노랫소리가 울렸어, 물에 빠진 사람에게 그런 것이 들린다고 들은 그대로. 그러고는 모든 것이 나에게서 멀어져 가는 듯했어. 내 영혼이 몸에서 빠져나가 공중을 떠도는 것 같았어. 한번은 웨스트 등대가 바로 내 발밑에 있었던 것 같은 기억이 나. 그러더니 무언가 괴로운 느낌이 들었어, 마치 내가 지진 속에 있는 것처럼. 그러고는 다시 돌아와 보니 네가 내 몸을 흔들고 있었어. 네가 그러는 걸 느끼기 전에 먼저 보았어.\"\n\n그러더니 그녀는 웃기 시작했다. 나는 그것이 좀 으스스하게 여겨져, 숨죽인 채 그녀의 말에 귀를 기울였다. 나는 그것이 영 마음에 들지 않아, 그녀의 마음을 그 일에 붙들어 두지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어, 우리는 다른 화제로 흘러갔고, 루시는 다시 예전의 그녀다워졌다. 우리가 집에 닿았을 때 신선한 바람이 그녀에게 활기를 불어넣어, 창백한 두 뺨이 정말로 한층 장밋빛이 되어 있었다. 그녀의 어머니는 그녀를 보고 기뻐했고, 우리 모두는 함께 무척 행복한 저녁을 보냈다.\n\n8월 19일. — 기쁨, 기쁨, 기쁨! 온통 기쁨만은 아니지만. 마침내 조너선의 소식이 왔다. 그 사랑스러운 사람은 그동안 앓았던 것이다. 그래서 편지를 쓰지 못했던 것이다. 이제 그것을 알고 나니, 나는 그것을 생각하기도 입에 올리기도 두렵지 않다. 호킨스 씨가 나에게 편지를 부쳐주었고, 자신도 친히, 아, 어찌나 다정하게 써 보냈는지. 나는 아침에 떠나 조너선에게로 건너가, 필요하다면 그를 간호하는 일을 돕고, 그를 집으로 데려와야 한다. 호킨스 씨는 우리가 거기서 결혼한다 해도 나쁘지 않을 것이라고 한다. 나는 그 착한 수녀님의 편지를 두고 어찌나 울었던지, 그것이 놓인 내 가슴에 닿아 젖은 것이 느껴질 정도다. 그것은 조너선에 관한 것이니, 내 심장 곁에 두어야 한다. 그가 내 심장 속에 있으니까. 내 여정은 모두 짜여 있고, 짐도 다 꾸렸다. 갈아입을 옷 한 벌만 챙긴다. 루시가 내 트렁크를 런던으로 가져가, 내가 부치라고 할 때까지 맡아둘 것이다. 어쩌면…… 더 쓰지 말아야겠다. 그건 내 남편 조너선에게 직접 말하려고 아껴두어야겠다. 그가 보고 만진 편지가, 우리가 만날 때까지 나를 위로해 줄 것이다.\n\n「부다페스트 성 요셉·성 마리아 병원의 아가타 수녀가 빌헬미나 머리 양에게 보낸 편지」\n\n8월 12일.\n\n부인께 —\n\n저는 조너선 하커 씨의 청에 따라 이 글을 씁니다. 그분은 신과 성 요셉, 성 마리아 덕분에 경과가 좋으시나, 아직 손수 글을 쓸 만큼 기력이 없으십니다. 그분은 격심한 뇌염을 앓으시며 거의 여섯 주 동안 저희의 보살핌을 받아오셨습니다. 그분은 당신께 사랑을 전해달라 하시며, 또 이 우편으로 제가 그분을 대신하여 엑서터의 피터 호킨스 씨께 편지를 써, 삼가 공손한 인사와 함께, 지체된 것을 죄송히 여기며 자신의 일은 모두 끝마쳤음을 전한다고 말씀드리라 하셨습니다. 그분은 산중의 저희 요양원에서 몇 주간의 휴양이 필요하시겠으나, 그 뒤에는 돌아가실 것입니다. 그분은, 지니신 돈이 넉넉지 않으나, 이곳 체류비를 치르고자 하시니, 도움이 필요한 다른 이들이 도움을 받지 못하는 일이 없도록 함이라고 전해달라 하셨습니다.\n\n저를 믿어 주소서.\n\n동정과 온갖 축복을 담아 올립니다.\n아가타 수녀.\n\n추신. — 제 환자분이 잠드신 틈에, 좀 더 말씀드리고자 이 편지를 다시 엽니다. 그분은 당신에 관한 모든 것을, 그리고 당신이 머지않아 자신의 아내가 되리라는 것을 저에게 말씀해 주셨습니다. 두 분께 온갖 축복이 있기를! 그분은 — 저희 의사 말씀으로는 — 어떤 무서운 충격을 받으셨고, 헛소리 속에서 그분의 횡설수설은 끔찍했습니다. 늑대와 독약과 피에 대해, 유령과 악마에 대해, 그리고 차마 무엇이라 말씀드리기 두려운 것들에 대해서였습니다. 앞으로 오랫동안 그분을 이런 종류의 것으로 자극할 만한 일이 전혀 없도록 늘 조심하소서. 그분이 앓으신 그런 병의 흔적은 쉽게 사그라지지 않습니다. 저희가 진작 편지를 드렸어야 했으나, 그분의 가족이나 벗에 대해 아무것도 몰랐고, 그분에게는 누구라도 알아볼 만한 것이 아무것도 없었습니다. 그분은 클라우젠부르크에서 오는 기차를 타고 오셨는데, 그곳 역장이 차장에게 전한 바로는, 그분이 집으로 가는 차표를 달라고 외치며 역으로 뛰어들었다 합니다. 그 격한 거동으로 보아 영국인임을 알아챈 그들은, 기차가 닿는, 그쪽 방향으로 가장 먼 역까지의 차표를 그분께 드렸다 합니다.\n\n그분이 잘 보살핌을 받고 있음을 믿으소서. 그분은 그 다정함과 온화함으로 모든 이의 마음을 사로잡으셨습니다. 그분은 정말로 경과가 좋으시며, 몇 주 안에 완전히 본래의 모습을 되찾으시리라 저는 의심치 않습니다. 그러나 안전을 위해 그분을 보살피소서. 신과 성 요셉, 성 마리아께 기도하건대, 두 분께 많고 많은 행복한 세월이 있기를 빕니다.\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n8월 19일. — 간밤에 렌필드에게 이상하고도 갑작스러운 변화가 일어났다. 여덟 시쯤 그는 흥분하기 시작하더니, 사냥개가 사냥감을 가리킬 때처럼 여기저기 킁킁대며 냄새를 맡았다. 그 태도에 놀란 간병인은, 내가 그에게 관심이 있음을 알기에 그에게 말을 시켜보았다. 그는 평소 간병인에게 공손하고 때로는 비굴하기까지 한데, 간병인 말로는 오늘 밤에는 자못 거만했다고 한다. 그와는 도무지 말을 섞으려 들지 않았다. 그가 한 말이라곤 이것뿐이었다.\n\n\"너랑은 얘기하고 싶지 않다. 넌 이제 안중에도 없어. 주인님께서 가까이 와 계시거든.\"\n\n간병인은 그것이 그를 사로잡은 어떤 갑작스러운 형태의 종교적 광증이라고 생각한다. 그렇다면 우리는 험한 일에 대비해야 하니, 살인과 종교의 광증을 한꺼번에 지닌 힘센 사내는 위험할 수 있기 때문이다. 그 조합은 끔찍한 것이다. 아홉 시에 나는 직접 그를 찾아갔다. 그가 나를 대하는 태도는 간병인을 대하던 것과 똑같았으니, 그 숭고한 자기도취 속에서는 나와 간병인의 차이가 그에게 아무것도 아닌 듯했다. 종교적 광증으로 보이는데, 머지않아 그는 자기 자신이 신이라고 여기게 될 것이다. 사람과 사람 사이의 이 지극히 사소한 차이들은 전능한 존재에게는 너무도 하찮은 것이다. 이 광인들은 어쩌면 이리도 제 속을 드러내는지! 진짜 신은 참새 한 마리가 떨어지는 것조차 살피시거늘, 인간의 허영에서 만들어진 신은 독수리와 참새 사이에 아무 차이도 보지 못한다. 아, 사람들이 알기만 한다면!\n\n삼십 분 남짓 렌필드는 점점 더 격렬하게 흥분해 갔다. 나는 그를 지켜보는 척하지 않았으나, 그래도 빈틈없이 관찰하고 있었다. 별안간 그의 눈에 그 교활한 빛이 떠올랐으니, 광인이 어떤 생각에 사로잡혔을 때 우리가 늘 보게 되는 그것이었고, 그와 더불어 정신병원 간병인들이 너무도 잘 알게 되는, 머리와 등을 슬쩍 놀리는 그 움직임도 함께였다. 그는 아주 조용해지더니, 가서 체념한 듯 침대 가장자리에 앉아, 생기 없는 눈으로 허공을 바라보았다. 나는 그의 무관심이 진짜인지 아니면 그저 꾸민 것인지 알아보려고, 그의 애완동물 이야기로 그를 이끌어 보려 했으니, 그것은 한 번도 그의 관심을 끌지 못한 적이 없는 화제였다. 처음에 그는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았으나, 마침내 짜증스럽게 말했다.\n\n\"그놈들 다 귀찮아! 나는 그것들에 눈곱만큼도 관심 없어.\"\n\n\"뭐라고요?\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"거미들에게 관심이 없다는 말을 하려는 건 아니겠지요?\" (요즘 거미가 그의 취미여서, 공책은 작은 숫자들의 행렬로 채워져 가고 있다.) 이에 그는 수수께끼처럼 대답했다.\n\n\"신부의 들러리 처녀들은 신부가 오기를 기다리는 눈을 즐겁게 하나, 신부가 가까이 다가오면 그때엔 처녀들은 가득 찬 눈에 더는 빛나지 않는 법이오.\"\n\n그는 자신의 말을 설명하려 하지 않았고, 내가 그와 함께 있는 내내 고집스레 침대에 앉아 있었다.\n\n오늘 밤 나는 지치고 마음이 가라앉아 있다. 루시를 생각하지 않을 수 없고, 일이 얼마나 달라질 수 있었을까 하는 생각을 떨칠 수 없다. 당장 잠들지 못한다면, 근대의 모르페우스인 클로랄을 — C₂HCl₃O·H₂O를! 이것이 습관이 되도록 두지 않게끔 조심해야 한다. 아니, 오늘 밤은 한 방울도 먹지 않겠다! 나는 루시를 생각했으니, 그 둘을 뒤섞어 그녀를 욕되게 하지 않겠다. 정 그래야 한다면, 오늘 밤은 잠 못 드는 밤이 되리라……\n\n나중에. — 그 결심을 한 것이 다행이고, 그것을 지킨 것이 더더욱 다행이다. 나는 이리저리 뒤척이며 누워 시계가 겨우 두 번 치는 소리를 들었을 뿐인데, 야간 당직이 병동에서 올라와, 렌필드가 달아났다고 알렸다. 나는 옷을 걸치고 곧장 달려 내려갔다. 내 환자는 함부로 돌아다니게 두기에는 너무 위험한 인물이다. 그의 그런 생각들은 낯선 이들에게 위험하게 작용할 수 있다. 간병인이 나를 기다리고 있었다. 그는 채 십 분도 안 되어 그를 보았다고 했는데, 문에 난 관찰용 작은 구멍으로 들여다보았을 때 그는 침대에서 잠든 듯 보였다는 것이다. 창문이 비틀려 뜯겨 나가는 소리에 주의가 끌렸다고 했다. 그는 달려가, 그의 두 발이 창문 너머로 사라지는 것을 보고 곧바로 나에게 사람을 보냈던 것이다. 그는 잠옷 차림뿐이니, 멀리 가지는 못할 것이다. 간병인은, 문으로 건물을 빠져나가는 사이 그를 놓칠 수도 있으니, 그를 뒤쫓는 것보다 그가 어디로 가는지 지켜보는 편이 더 쓸모 있으리라 여겼다. 그는 덩치가 커서 창문을 빠져나갈 수 없었다. 나는 몸이 가늘어, 그의 도움으로 발부터 먼저 빠져나갔고, 우리가 땅에서 겨우 몇 자 위에 있었던 터라 다치지 않고 내려섰다. 간병인은 환자가 왼쪽으로 갔고 곧장 일직선으로 나아갔다고 일러주어, 나는 되도록 빨리 달렸다. 나무들이 늘어선 띠를 빠져나가자, 흰 형체 하나가 우리 부지와 저 버려진 집의 부지를 가르는 높은 담을 기어오르는 것이 보였다.\n\n나는 곧장 되돌아 달려가, 당직에게 즉시 서너 명을 데리고 카팩스의 부지로 나를 따라오라고 일렀으니, 우리 친구가 위험할 수 있기 때문이었다. 나는 직접 사다리를 구해, 담을 넘어 반대편으로 내려섰다. 렌필드의 형체가 막 집 모퉁이 뒤로 사라지는 것이 보여, 나는 그를 뒤쫓아 달렸다. 집 저편에서 나는 그가 예배당의 낡은 쇠테 두른 떡갈나무 문에 바싹 몸을 붙이고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그는 누군가에게 말을 하고 있는 듯했으나, 나는 그를 놀라게 해 달아나게 할까 봐, 그가 하는 말을 들을 만큼 가까이 가기가 두려웠다. 빗나간 벌떼를 쫓는 일은, 탈주의 발작이 도진 벌거벗은 미치광이를 쫓는 일에 비하면 아무것도 아니다! 그러나 몇 분이 지나자 그가 주위의 아무것도 알아차리지 못한다는 것을 알 수 있어, 나는 큰맘 먹고 그에게 더 가까이 다가갔다 — 게다가 내 부하들이 이제 담을 넘어와 그를 에워싸 죄어들고 있었으므로 더욱 그랬다. 나는 그가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다.\n\n\"저는 당신의 분부를 받들고자 여기 있나이다, 주인님. 저는 당신의 종이오니, 당신께서 저에게 상을 내리시리이다. 저는 충실할 것이옵니다. 저는 오래, 멀리서 당신을 흠모해 왔나이다. 이제 당신께서 가까이 오셨으니, 저는 당신의 명을 기다리오며, 당신께서 좋은 것들을 나누어 주실 적에 저를 그냥 지나치지는 않으시겠지요, 그렇지요, 친애하는 주인님?\"\n\n그는 어쨌거나 이기적인 늙은 거지다. 그는 자기가 진짜 임재(臨在) 앞에 있다고 믿는 순간에조차 떡과 물고기를 생각한다. 그의 광증들은 놀라운 조합을 이룬다. 우리가 그를 죄어들었을 때 그는 호랑이처럼 싸웠다. 그는 어마어마하게 힘이 세니, 사람이라기보다 들짐승에 가까웠다. 나는 미치광이가 그토록 광란하는 분노에 휩싸인 것을 일찍이 본 적이 없다. 그리고 다시는 보지 않기를 바란다. 그의 힘과 위험성을 제때에 알아낸 것은 다행한 일이다. 그와 같은 힘과 결의를 지녔다면, 그는 우리에 갇히기 전에 험한 짓을 저질렀을지도 모른다. 어쨌든 이제 그는 안전하게 갇혀 있다. 잭 셰퍼드 자신이라도 그를 옭아매고 있는 구속복에서 풀려날 수 없을 것이며, 그는 푹신하게 댄 방 안에서 벽에 쇠사슬로 매여 있다. 그의 비명은 때로 끔찍하지만, 그 뒤에 따르는 침묵은 더더욱 섬뜩하니, 그가 몸짓 하나하나마다 살인을 꾀하고 있기 때문이다.\n\n방금 그가 처음으로 조리 있는 말을 했다.\n\n\"저는 참고 기다리겠나이다, 주인님. 오고 있어 — 오고 있어 — 오고 있어!\"\n\n그래서 나는 그 암시를 알아채고, 나도 돌아왔다. 너무 흥분해서 잠을 이룰 수 없었으나, 이 일기가 나를 가라앉혀 주었으니, 오늘 밤은 좀 잘 수 있을 듯하다.\n\n「미나 하커가 루시 웨스텐라에게 보내는 편지」\n\n부다페스트, 8월 24일.\n\n사랑하는 루시에게—\n우리가 휘트비 기차역에서 헤어진 뒤로 일어난 일들을 네가 무척 궁금해하리라는 것을 나는 안다. 그래, 얘야, 나는 무사히 헐에 도착했고, 함부르크행 배를 탔으며, 거기서 다시 기차를 타고 이곳까지 왔단다. 여정에 대해서는 거의 아무것도 기억나지 않는 듯한데, 다만 내가 조너선에게로 가고 있다는 것, 그리고 간병을 해야 할 테니 잘 수 있는 만큼 자두는 편이 낫겠다는 것만은 알고 있었지…. 나는 내 사랑하는 사람을 만났단다. 아, 어찌나 야위고 창백하고 약해 보이던지. 그이의 그 사랑스러운 눈에서 결의가 모두 사라졌고, 내가 네게 말했던 그 얼굴에 어렸던 조용한 위엄도 자취를 감추었어. 그이는 자기 자신의 잔해에 지나지 않으며, 오랫동안 자신에게 일어난 일을 아무것도 기억하지 못해. 적어도 내가 그렇게 믿어주기를 바라고, 나는 결코 캐묻지 않을 거야. 그이는 무언가 끔찍한 충격을 받았는데, 그것을 떠올리려 한다면 그이의 가엾은 머리에 무리가 갈까 봐 두려워. 착한 사람이자 천생 간호사인 애거사 수녀님이 말하기를, 그이가 제정신이 아닐 때 무시무시한 것들에 대해 헛소리를 했다는구나. 나는 수녀님께 그게 무엇이었는지 말해달라고 했지만, 수녀님은 그저 성호를 그으며, 결코 말할 수 없다고, 병자의 헛소리는 신의 비밀이니, 간호사가 그 소명으로 인해 그것을 듣게 되더라도 그 신뢰를 존중해야 한다고만 하셨어. 수녀님은 다정하고 선한 분이라, 이튿날 내가 괴로워하는 것을 보시고 다시 그 이야기를 꺼내셨는데, 내 가엾은 사람이 헛소리한 내용은 결코 입에 담을 수 없다고 하시면서도 이렇게 덧붙이셨단다. ‘이만큼은 말씀드릴 수 있어요, 아가씨. 그분이 스스로 무슨 잘못을 저질러서가 아니랍니다. 그리고 장차 그분의 아내가 되실 분으로서, 걱정하실 까닭은 조금도 없어요. 그분은 당신을, 또 당신에게 진 빚을 잊지 않으셨답니다. 그분이 두려워한 것은 어떤 인간도 다룰 수 없는 크고 무서운 것들이었어요.’ 그 다정한 분은 내 가엾은 사람이 다른 여자를 사랑하게 되었을까 봐 내가 질투할지도 모른다고 생각하셨던 게 분명해. 내가 조너선을 두고 질투를 한다는 발상이라니! 그렇지만 얘야, 가만히 속삭이건대, 다른 여자가 화근이 아니라는 것을 알았을 때 나는 온몸에 기쁨의 전율이 흘렀단다. 나는 지금 그이의 머리맡에 앉아, 그이가 자는 동안 그 얼굴을 바라볼 수 있는 자리에 있어. 그이가 깨어나고 있어!…\n\n그이는 깨어나자 외투를 가져다 달라고 했어. 주머니에서 무언가를 꺼내고 싶다는 거였지. 내가 애거사 수녀님께 부탁하자 수녀님이 그이의 물건을 모두 가져다주셨어. 그것들 가운데 그이의 수첩이 있는 것을 보고, 나는 그것을 좀 보여달라고 청하려 했어—그러면 그이의 고뇌에 대한 어떤 실마리를 찾을 수 있으리라는 것을 그때 알았으니까—그러나 그이는 내 바람을 내 눈에서 읽었던 모양으로, 잠시 혼자 있고 싶다며 나를 창가로 보냈어. 그러고는 다시 나를 불렀고, 내가 다가가자 그이는 수첩 위에 손을 얹은 채 무척 엄숙하게 말했어—\n\n‘빌헬미나’—그 순간 나는 그이가 더없이 진지하다는 것을 알았는데, 청혼한 이래로 그이가 나를 그 이름으로 부른 적이 한 번도 없었기 때문이야—‘여보, 당신은 부부 사이의 신뢰에 대한 내 생각을 알지. 비밀도 숨김도 있어서는 안 된다는 것을. 나는 큰 충격을 받았고, 그게 무엇이었는지 생각하려 들면 머리가 빙빙 도는 것 같아. 그게 모두 실제였는지 아니면 미치광이의 꿈이었는지 알 수가 없어. 당신도 알다시피 나는 뇌염을 앓았고, 그건 곧 미쳤다는 뜻이지. 비밀은 여기 있지만, 나는 그것을 알고 싶지 않아. 나는 우리의 결혼과 더불어 이곳에서 내 삶을 새로 시작하고 싶어.’ 얘야, 우리는 절차가 다 끝나는 대로 결혼하기로 결정했단다. ‘빌헬미나, 당신은 내 무지를 함께 나눌 마음이 있소? 여기 수첩이 있소. 받아서 간직해주오. 원한다면 읽어도 좋소. 다만 내게는 결코 알리지 말아주오. 정말로, 잠들었든 깨어 있든, 멀쩡하든 미쳤든, 여기 기록된 그 쓰라린 시간들로 되돌아가야 할 어떤 엄숙한 의무가 내게 닥치지 않는 한은.’ 그이는 기진하여 도로 누웠고, 나는 그 수첩을 그이의 베개 밑에 넣고 그이에게 입맞추었어. 나는 애거사 수녀님께 원장 수녀님께 청하여 우리 결혼식을 오늘 오후에 올릴 수 있게 해달라고 부탁했고, 지금 그 답을 기다리고 있어….\n\n수녀님이 와서, 영국 선교 교회의 사제를 불러오도록 사람을 보냈다고 알려주셨어. 우리는 한 시간 뒤, 아니 그 직후에 조너선이 깨어나는 대로 결혼식을 올리게 될 거야….\n\n루시, 그 시간이 오고 또 지나갔어. 나는 무척 엄숙한 마음이지만, 정말이지 더없이 행복해. 조너선은 그 시각이 조금 지나 깨어났고, 모든 준비가 되어 있었어. 그이는 베개로 받쳐 침대에 일으켜 앉았지. 그이는 ‘맹세합니다’라는 말을 굳세고 또렷하게 답했어. 나는 거의 말을 할 수가 없었어. 가슴이 너무 벅차서 그 짧은 말조차 목이 메는 듯했지. 다정한 수녀님들은 어찌나 친절하셨던지. 부디 신께 비노니, 나는 그분들도, 또 내가 떠맡은 그 엄숙하고도 달콤한 책임도 결코, 결코 잊지 않을 거야. 내 결혼 선물에 대해 네게 말해줘야겠다. 사제와 수녀님들이 나를 남편과 단둘이 남겨두고 떠나자—아, 루시, ‘내 남편’이라는 말을 쓰는 건 이번이 처음이구나—남편과 단둘이 남게 되자, 나는 그이의 베개 밑에서 수첩을 꺼내어 흰 종이에 싸고, 내 목에 둘렀던 연한 푸른 리본 한 조각으로 묶은 다음, 봉랍으로 매듭 위를 봉했어. 그리고 내 도장으로는 결혼반지를 썼단다. 그러고는 거기에 입맞추고 남편에게 보여주며, 이대로 간직하겠다고, 그러면 그것이 우리가 평생 서로를 믿었다는 외적이고 눈에 보이는 표징이 되리라고 말했어. 그이 자신의 소중한 일을 위해서가 아니라면, 혹은 어떤 준엄한 의무를 위해서가 아니라면 결코 그것을 열지 않겠다고. 그러자 그이는 내 손을 자기 손에 잡았는데, 아, 루시, 그이가 자기 아내의 손을 잡은 것은 그때가 처음이었어. 그이는 그것이 이 넓은 세상에서 가장 소중한 것이라고, 필요하다면 그것을 얻기 위해 그 모든 과거를 다시 한번 겪을 수도 있다고 말했어. 가엾은 그이는 과거의 일부를 말하려 했던 거지만, 아직 시간을 가늠하지 못하니, 처음에는 달뿐 아니라 해까지도 헷갈린다 해도 나는 이상하게 여기지 않을 거야.\n\n그래, 얘야, 내가 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠니? 나는 그저 내가 이 넓은 세상에서 가장 행복한 여자라고, 그리고 그이에게 줄 것이라곤 나 자신과 내 삶과 내 신뢰뿐이며, 그것들과 더불어 내 평생의 사랑과 의무가 함께 간다고 말할 수 있을 따름이었어. 그리고 얘야, 그이가 나에게 입맞추고 그 가엾고 약한 두 손으로 나를 끌어당겼을 때, 그것은 우리 사이의 더없이 엄숙한 서약과도 같았단다….\n\n루시야, 내가 왜 이 모든 것을 네게 말하는지 아니? 이것이 나에게 모두 달콤하기 때문만이 아니라, 네가 나에게 무척 소중한 사람이었고 또 지금도 그렇기 때문이야. 네가 학창 시절을 마치고 인생이라는 세계를 준비하러 나왔을 때, 네 벗이자 길잡이가 된 것은 내게 주어진 특권이었지. 이제 나는 네가, 더없이 행복한 아내의 눈으로, 의무가 나를 어디로 이끌었는지 보아주기를 바라. 그래서 너의 결혼 생활에서도 너 또한 나처럼 온전히 행복하기를. 얘야, 전능하신 신께 비노니, 네 삶이 약속하는 그대로이기를. 모진 바람도, 의무를 저버리는 일도, 불신도 없는, 기나긴 햇살의 나날이기를. 네게 아무런 고통도 없기를 빌 수는 없겠지, 그것은 결코 있을 수 없는 일이니까. 그러나 네가 지금의 나처럼 언제나 행복하기를 진정 바란단다. 잘 있어, 얘야. 이 편지는 곧장 부칠 것이고, 어쩌면 머지않아 다시 쓰게 될지도 몰라. 이만 멈춰야겠다. 조너선이 깨어나고 있으니—남편을 돌봐야 하니까!\n언제나 너를 사랑하는\n\n미나 하커.\n「루시 웨스텐라가 미나 하커에게 보내는 편지」\n\n휘트비, 8월 30일.\n\n사랑하는 미나에게—\n\n바다만큼의 사랑과 수백만 번의 입맞춤을 보내며, 부디 곧 네 남편과 함께 너의 집에 자리잡기를. 네가 어서 돌아와 이곳 우리와 함께 지낼 수 있다면 좋으련만. 이 강한 공기가 조너선을 곧 회복시켜줄 거야. 나는 아주 말끔히 회복되었거든. 나는 가마우지 같은 식욕이 있고, 활기가 넘치며, 잠도 잘 잔단다. 내가 몽유병을 완전히 떨쳐냈다는 걸 알면 기뻐하겠지. 일주일 동안 침대에서 한 번도 나가지 않은 것 같아. 그러니까, 밤에 일단 침대에 들면 말이야. 아서는 내가 살이 오르고 있다고 해. 그러고 보니, 아서가 여기 와 있다는 걸 깜빡하고 말 안 했구나. 우리는 함께 산책도 하고, 마차도 타고, 말도 타고, 보트도 젓고, 테니스도 치고, 낚시도 한단다. 그리고 나는 그를 그 어느 때보다 사랑해. 그는 자기가 나를 더 사랑한다고 말하지만, 나는 그 말을 의심하지. 처음에 그는 그때보다 더 나를 사랑할 수는 없다고 했었거든. 하지만 이건 다 부질없는 소리지. 저기 그가 나를 부르고 있어. 그러니 지금은 이만, 너를 사랑하는\n루시로부터.\n\n추신.—어머니께서 안부를 전하셔. 가엾은 분, 좀 나아지신 것 같아.\n추추신.—우리는 9월 28일에 결혼하기로 했어.\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n8월 20일.—렌필드의 증례는 더욱더 흥미로워지고 있다. 그는 이제 상당히 잠잠해져서, 발작이 멈추는 시기가 있다. 발작이 있은 뒤 첫 주 동안 그는 끊임없이 난폭했다. 그러던 어느 날 밤, 달이 막 떠오를 무렵 그는 잠잠해지더니, 혼잣말로 “이제 기다릴 수 있어. 이제 기다릴 수 있어”라고 중얼거렸다. 간호인이 와서 알려주기에 나는 곧장 내려가 그를 살펴보았다. 그는 여전히 구속복을 입은 채 충전실에 있었으나, 얼굴에서 그 충혈된 기색은 사라졌고, 눈에는 예전의 그 애원하는 듯한—거의 “비굴한”이라 할 만한—부드러움이 어려 있었다. 나는 그의 현재 상태에 만족하여 그를 풀어주도록 지시했다. 간호인들은 망설였으나, 결국 군말 없이 내 뜻을 따랐다. 기이하게도 그 환자는 그들의 불신을 알아챌 만큼의 유머가 있었는데, 내게 가까이 다가오더니 줄곧 그들을 곁눈질하면서 속삭이듯 말했다—\n\n“저들은 제가 선생님을 해칠 수 있다고 생각하나 봐요! 제가 선생님을 해친다니요! 바보들 같으니!”\n\n이 가엾은 미치광이의 마음속에서조차 내가 다른 사람들과 구별된다는 것을 알게 되니 어쩐지 마음이 흐뭇했다. 그러나 그렇다 해도 나는 그의 생각을 따라가지 못하겠다. 내가 그와 어떤 공통점이 있어서, 말하자면 우리가 한편에 서게 된다는 뜻으로 받아들여야 하는가. 아니면 그가 나로부터 얻어야 할 어떤 엄청난 이로움이 있어서 내 안녕이 그에게 필요하다는 것인가. 나중에 알아내야겠다. 오늘 밤 그는 말을 하지 않으려 한다. 새끼고양이를, 아니 다 자란 고양이를 주겠다 해도 솔깃해하지 않는다. 그는 그저 이렇게만 말한다. “고양이 따위엔 관심 없어요. 지금 생각할 게 더 많거든요. 그리고 기다릴 수 있어요. 기다릴 수 있다고요.”\n\n얼마 후 나는 그를 떠났다. 간호인의 말로는 그가 동트기 직전까지 잠잠하다가, 그때부터 불안해지기 시작해 마침내 난폭해졌으며, 끝내는 발작에 빠져 어찌나 기진했던지 일종의 혼수 상태로 까무러쳤다고 한다.\n\n…사흘 밤 동안 똑같은 일이 일어났다—온종일 난폭하다가 달이 뜨고부터 해가 뜰 때까지 잠잠해진다. 그 원인에 대한 실마리를 잡을 수 있다면 좋으련만. 마치 오고 가는 어떤 영향력이 있는 것만 같다. 좋은 생각이 났다! 오늘 밤 우리는 멀쩡한 머리로 미친 머리와 겨뤄보겠다. 그는 앞서 우리의 도움 없이 탈출했으니, 오늘 밤은 우리의 도움으로 탈출하게 하리라. 우리는 그에게 기회를 주고, 필요할 경우에 대비해 따라갈 사람들을 대기시켜두겠다….\n\n8월 23일.—“예기치 않은 일은 늘 일어난다.” 디즈레일리는 인생을 참으로 잘 알았다. 우리의 새는 새장이 열린 것을 보고도 날아가려 하지 않았으니, 우리의 그 모든 정교한 계획은 헛수고가 되고 말았다. 어쨌든 우리는 한 가지는 입증했다. 잠잠한 시기가 상당한 시간 동안 지속된다는 것을. 앞으로 우리는 날마다 몇 시간씩 그의 결박을 풀어줄 수 있을 것이다. 나는 야간 간호인에게, 그가 일단 잠잠해지면 해뜨기 한 시간 전까지 그저 충전실에 가두어두라고 지시했다. 비록 그의 정신은 그것을 음미하지 못하더라도, 이 가엾은 영혼의 몸만큼은 그 안식을 누릴 것이다. 들으라! 또 예기치 않은 일이로다! 나를 부른다. 환자가 또다시 탈출한 것이다.\n\n이후.—또 한 번의 밤의 소동. 렌필드는 간호인이 살피러 방에 들어오는 순간까지 교묘하게 기다렸다. 그러고는 간호인을 지나쳐 뛰쳐나가 복도를 따라 내달렸다. 나는 간호인들에게 따라가라고 일렀다. 그는 다시 그 버려진 저택의 부지로 들어갔고, 우리는 그가 똑같은 자리, 곧 오래된 예배당 문에 몸을 바싹 붙이고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그는 나를 보더니 광포해졌고, 간호인들이 제때 붙들지 않았더라면 나를 죽이려 들었을 것이다. 우리가 그를 붙들고 있는 동안 기이한 일이 일어났다. 그는 갑자기 두 배로 발버둥치다가, 그만큼이나 돌연 차분해졌다. 나는 본능적으로 주위를 둘러보았으나 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 그때 나는 환자의 눈길을 포착하고 그것을 따라가 보았으나, 달빛 어린 하늘을 응시하는 그 눈길이 좇는 것이라곤 서쪽을 향해 소리 없이 유령처럼 날갯짓하며 날아가는 커다란 박쥐 한 마리뿐, 아무것도 짚어낼 수 없었다. 박쥐는 보통 빙빙 돌며 팔랑팔랑 나는데, 이놈은 자기가 갈 곳을 알고 있거나 무슨 제 나름의 의도라도 있는 듯 곧장 앞으로만 나아가는 것 같았다. 환자는 시시각각 차분해지더니, 이윽고 말했다—\n\n“저를 묶을 필요 없어요. 얌전히 가겠습니다!” 우리는 별 탈 없이 저택으로 돌아왔다. 나는 그의 그 차분함에 무언가 불길한 것이 있음을 느끼며, 이 밤을 결코 잊지 않을 것이다….\n\n「루시 웨스텐라의 일기」\n\n힐링엄, 8월 24일.—나도 미나를 본받아 이것저것 적어두어야겠다. 그러면 우리가 만났을 때 긴 이야기를 나눌 수 있을 테니. 그게 언제가 될지 모르겠구나. 그 애가 다시 나와 함께 있어준다면 좋으련만, 나는 너무도 불행하다. 어젯밤 나는 휘트비에 있을 때처럼 또 꿈을 꾸는 것 같았다. 어쩌면 공기가 바뀐 탓이거나, 다시 집에 돌아온 탓일지도 모르겠다. 모든 것이 내게는 어둡고 끔찍한데, 아무것도 기억나지 않기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 막연한 두려움에 사로잡혀 있고, 너무도 약하고 지친 듯하다. 아서가 점심을 먹으러 왔을 때, 그는 나를 보더니 무척 슬퍼하는 기색이었는데, 나는 명랑한 척할 기운조차 없었다. 오늘 밤 어머니 방에서 잘 수 있을지 모르겠다. 핑계를 대고 시도해봐야겠다.\n\n8월 25일.—또 한 번의 험한 밤. 어머니는 내 제안을 그리 반기지 않으시는 듯했다. 어머니 자신도 그리 편치 않으신 모양이고, 분명 나를 걱정시킬까 봐 염려하시는 것이리라. 나는 깨어 있으려 애썼고 한동안은 성공했다. 그러나 시계가 열두 시를 칠 때 그 소리에 선잠에서 깨어난 것을 보면, 그새 잠이 들고 있었던 모양이다. 창문에서 무언가 긁거나 퍼덕이는 듯한 소리가 났지만, 나는 개의치 않았고, 더는 기억나지 않는 것을 보면 그때 잠이 들었던 게 틀림없다. 또 험한 꿈들. 그것들을 기억할 수 있다면 좋으련만. 오늘 아침 나는 끔찍이도 약하다. 얼굴은 송장처럼 창백하고, 목이 아프다. 폐에 무슨 이상이 있는 게 분명한데, 도무지 숨을 충분히 들이쉴 수가 없는 것 같다. 아서가 오면 기운을 내려 애써야겠다. 그러지 않으면 나를 이런 꼴로 보고 그가 비참해할 게 뻔하니까.\n「아서 홈우드가 수어드 박사에게 보내는 편지」\n\n앨버말 호텔, 8월 31일.\n\n친애하는 잭에게—\n\n자네에게 한 가지 부탁이 있네. 루시가 아프다네. 그러니까, 무슨 특별한 병이 있는 건 아니지만, 안색이 끔찍하고 날마다 더 나빠지고 있어. 무슨 까닭이라도 있느냐고 그녀에게 물어보았네. 그녀의 어머니께는 차마 여쭐 수가 없었어. 지금 같은 건강 상태에서 따님에 대해 그 가엾은 부인의 마음을 어지럽히는 것은 치명적일 테니까. 웨스텐라 부인은 자신의 운명이 정해졌다고—심장병이라고—내게 털어놓으셨는데, 가엾은 루시는 아직 그것을 모른다네. 무언가가 내 사랑하는 여인의 마음을 좀먹고 있는 게 분명해. 그녀를 생각하면 나는 거의 제정신이 아닐 지경이야. 그녀를 보면 가슴이 쓰리네. 나는 그녀에게 자네더러 봐달라고 청하겠다고 말했고, 그녀는 처음엔 망설였지만—이보게, 그 까닭은 나도 안다네—마침내 동의했네. 자네에게는 괴로운 일이 되리라는 것을 아네, 오랜 벗이여. 그러나 그건 그녀를 위한 일이니, 나는 청하기를 주저해서는 안 되고, 자네 또한 행하기를 주저해서는 안 되네. 내일 두 시에 힐링엄으로 점심을 들러 와주게. 웨스텐라 부인의 의심을 사지 않도록 말이야. 점심 뒤에 루시가 자네와 단둘이 있을 기회를 마련할 걸세. 나는 차 마실 때쯤 들르겠네. 그러면 함께 자리를 뜰 수 있겠지. 나는 불안으로 가득 차 있어서, 자네가 그녀를 본 뒤 되도록 빨리 자네와 단둘이 상의하고 싶네. 부디 어김없이 와주게!\n아서.\n\n「아서 홈우드가 수어드에게 보내는 전보」\n\n9월 1일.\n\n부친께서 위독하시어 뵈러 오라는 부름을 받았음. 편지 쓰겠음. 오늘 밤 우편으로 링으로 소상히 답신 바람. 필요하면 전보 치게.\n「수어드 박사가 아서 홈우드에게 보내는 편지」\n\n9월 2일.\n\n친애하는 오랜 벗 아트에게—\n\n웨스텐라 양의 건강에 관해 서둘러 알려두건대, 내 소견으로는 어떤 기능적 장애도, 내가 아는 어떤 질환도 없네. 그와 동시에, 나는 그녀의 안색에 결코 만족하지 못하겠어. 내가 지난번 보았을 때와는 비참하리만큼 달라졌더군. 물론 자네도 명심해두어야 할 것은, 내가 바라는 만큼 충분히 진찰할 기회를 얻지 못했다는 점이네. 다름 아닌 우리의 우정 자체가, 의학도 관습도 메울 수 없는 약간의 곤란을 빚는다네. 차라리 무슨 일이 있었는지를 자네에게 정확히 들려주고, 어느 정도는 자네 스스로 결론을 끌어내도록 맡기는 편이 낫겠네. 그러고 나서 내가 무엇을 했고 또 무엇을 하려는지 말하겠네.\n\n웨스텐라 양은 겉보기에 명랑한 기분으로 있었네. 그녀의 어머니가 함께 있었는데, 나는 몇 초 만에 그녀가 아는 한껏을 다해 어머니를 속이고 걱정을 끼치지 않으려 애쓰고 있음을 알아차렸네. 그녀가 얼마나 조심해야 하는지를, 알지는 못하더라도 짐작은 하고 있는 게 틀림없어. 우리는 단둘이 점심을 들었는데, 다들 명랑해지려 애쓴 덕분에, 그 수고에 대한 일종의 보답으로 우리 사이에 얼마간의 진정한 명랑함이 감돌았네. 그러고 나서 웨스텐라 부인이 누우러 가시고, 루시는 나와 단둘이 남았지. 우리는 그녀의 내실로 들어갔는데, 거기 이르기까지 그녀의 명랑함은 유지되었네. 하인들이 들락거렸으니까. 그러나 문이 닫히자마자 그녀의 얼굴에서 가면이 떨어졌고, 그녀는 깊은 한숨과 함께 의자에 주저앉아 손으로 눈을 가렸네. 그녀의 들떴던 기분이 무너진 것을 보고, 나는 즉시 그 반작용을 이용해 진단을 내리려 했어. 그녀는 무척 다정하게 내게 말했네—\n\n‘제 자신에 대해 이야기하는 게 얼마나 싫은지 모르겠어요.’ 나는 의사의 비밀은 신성한 것이지만 자네가 그녀를 몹시 걱정하고 있다는 점을 상기시켰네. 그녀는 곧장 내 뜻을 알아채고는 한마디로 그 문제를 정리해버렸어. ‘아서에게는 무엇이든 마음대로 말씀하세요. 저 자신은 아무래도 좋아요. 다 그이를 위해서예요!’ 그러니 나는 아무 거리낌이 없네.\n\n나는 그녀가 다소 혈색이 없다는 것을 쉽게 알 수 있었지만, 흔히 보이는 빈혈 징후는 보이지 않았네. 그러다 우연히 나는 실제로 그녀의 혈액 상태를 검사할 수 있게 되었어. 뻑뻑한 창문을 열다가 끈이 끊어지는 바람에, 그녀가 깨진 유리에 손을 살짝 베인 거지. 그 자체로는 사소한 일이었지만, 내게는 분명한 기회를 주었네. 나는 피 몇 방울을 받아 분석해보았어. 정성 분석 결과는 지극히 정상이었고, 내가 추론하기로는 그 자체로 활력 넘치는 건강 상태를 보여주었네. 다른 신체적 사항에서도 나는 걱정할 필요가 없음을 충분히 확인했어. 그러나 어딘가에 원인이 있을 수밖에 없으므로, 나는 그것이 무언가 정신적인 것임에 틀림없다는 결론에 이르렀네. 그녀는 이따금 숨을 제대로 쉬기 어렵다고, 그리고 무겁고 혼곤한 잠을 자며 자신을 겁먹게 하는 꿈을 꾸지만 그에 관해서는 아무것도 기억하지 못한다고 하소연하네. 그녀는 어릴 적에 몽유병이 있었고, 휘트비에 있을 때 그 버릇이 도졌으며, 한번은 밤중에 걸어 나가 이스트 클리프까지 갔는데 거기서 머리 양이 자신을 발견했다고 말하네. 그러나 근래에는 그 버릇이 다시 나타나지 않았다고 내게 장담하더군. 나는 갈피를 잡지 못해, 내가 아는 최선의 일을 했네. 암스테르담에 계신 나의 오랜 벗이자 스승이신 판 헬싱 교수께 편지를 드렸어. 그분은 난해한 질병에 관해서라면 세상 누구 못지않게 잘 아시는 분이지. 나는 그분께 건너와주십사 청했고, 자네가 모든 비용을 부담하겠다고 했으니, 그분께 자네가 누구이며 웨스텐라 양과 어떤 관계인지를 말씀드렸네. 이보게, 이것은 자네의 뜻에 따른 것이니, 나는 그녀를 위해 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 더없이 자랑스럽고 기쁘게 할 따름이야. 판 헬싱 교수는, 내가 알기로, 개인적인 연유로라도 나를 위해서라면 무슨 일이든 해주실 분이니, 어떤 명분으로 오시든 우리는 그분의 뜻을 받들어야 하네. 그분은 겉보기엔 독단적인 분이지만, 이는 그분이 누구보다 자신이 무슨 말을 하는지를 잘 알고 계시기 때문이야. 그분은 철학자이자 형이상학자이며, 당대의 가장 진보한 과학자 가운데 한 분이고, 또 내가 믿건대, 완전히 열린 마음을 지니신 분이네. 여기에 강철 같은 담력, 얼음장처럼 냉철한 기질, 불굴의 결의, 자제력, 그리고 미덕을 넘어 축복의 경지에 이른 관용, 게다가 세상에 뛰는 그 어떤 심장보다도 자애롭고 진실한 마음—이런 것들이 그분이 인류를 위해 행하시는 그 고귀한 사업의 자질을 이루지. 이론에서나 실제에서나 그러한데, 그분의 견해는 그분의 모든 것을 아우르는 연민만큼이나 넓기 때문이야. 내가 이런 사실들을 자네에게 들려주는 것은, 내가 어찌하여 그분을 그토록 신뢰하는지 자네가 알게 하기 위해서네. 나는 그분께 즉시 와주십사 청했네. 나는 내일 웨스텐라 양을 다시 보겠네. 그녀가 스토어스 백화점에서 나를 만나기로 했어. 너무 이르게 내 방문을 되풀이하여 그녀의 어머니를 놀라게 하지 않으려는 것이지.\n\n언제나 자네의 벗,\n\n존 수어드.\n\n「의학박사, 철학박사, 문학박사 등등의 아브라함 판 헬싱이 수어드 박사에게 보내는 편지」\n9월 2일.\n\n나의 좋은 벗에게—\n자네 편지를 받자마자 나는 벌써 자네에게 가는 길일세. 다행히도 나는 나를 믿어준 이들 누구에게도 폐를 끼치지 않고 지금 당장 떠날 수 있네. 사정이 달랐다면 나를 믿어준 이들에게 좋지 못한 일이었을 테지. 나는 벗이 소중히 여기는 이들을 돕도록 나를 부를 때 그 벗에게로 가니 말일세. 자네 벗에게 이렇게 전해주게. 그때 우리의 또 다른 벗이, 너무 긴장한 나머지 그 칼을 놓치는 바람에 생긴 괴저의 독을, 자네가 내 상처에서 그토록 재빨리 빨아내주었을 때, 그가 내 도움을 필요로 하고 자네가 그것을 청했던 그때 자네는 그의 그 막대한 재산이 할 수 있는 그 무엇보다도 더 많은 일을 그를 위해 해주었다고 말일세. 그러나 자네 벗을 위해 무언가를 한다는 것은 거기에 더해진 기쁨이라네. 내가 가는 것은 자네에게로일세. 그러니 그레이트 이스턴 호텔에 내 방을 잡아두게. 내가 가까이 있을 수 있도록 말일세. 그리고 부디 내일 너무 늦지 않게 그 젊은 아가씨를 볼 수 있도록 주선해주게. 나는 그날 밤 이리로 돌아와야 할지도 모르니. 그러나 필요하다면 사흘 뒤에 다시 오겠고, 부득이하다면 더 오래 머물겠네. 그때까지 잘 있게, 나의 벗 존.\n\n판 헬싱.\n\n「수어드 박사가 아서 홈우드 경에게 보내는 편지」\n9월 3일.\n\n친애하는 아트에게—\n판 헬싱 교수가 왔다 가셨네. 그분은 나와 함께 힐링엄으로 가셨는데, 루시의 분별 덕분에 그녀의 어머니가 밖에서 점심을 들고 계셔서, 우리는 그녀와 단둘이 있을 수 있었네. 판 헬싱 교수는 환자를 무척 세심하게 진찰하셨어. 그분이 내게 보고하시기로 했고, 나는 자네에게 알려주겠네. 물론 나는 줄곧 함께 있었던 것은 아니니까. 그분은, 안타깝게도, 무척 염려하시는 듯한데, 좀 더 생각해봐야겠다고 하시네. 내가 우리의 우정과, 자네가 이 일에서 나를 믿고 맡긴 사정을 말씀드리자, 그분은 이렇게 말씀하셨네. ‘자네가 생각하는 바를 모두 그에게 말해주게. 내 생각도, 자네가 짐작할 수 있다면, 그리고 그러고 싶다면 말해주게나. 아니, 농담이 아닐세. 이건 농담이 아니라 생사가 걸린 일이고, 어쩌면 그 이상이야.’ 나는 그게 무슨 뜻이냐고 여쭈었네. 그분이 무척 진지하셨으니까. 이는 우리가 시내로 돌아왔을 때였는데, 그분은 암스테르담으로 돌아가는 길에 오르기 전 차를 한 잔 드시던 참이었네. 그분은 더는 아무런 실마리도 주려 하지 않으셨네. 아트, 나를 탓하지 말게. 그분의 그 과묵함이야말로 그분의 온 두뇌가 그녀를 위해 작동하고 있다는 뜻이니까. 때가 오면 그분은 충분히 분명하게 말씀하실 걸세, 틀림없이. 그래서 나는 그분께, 마치 『데일리 텔레그래프』에 실을 묘사 위주의 특집 기사라도 쓰듯이, 그저 우리 방문의 경위를 글로 적어두겠다고 말씀드렸네. 그분은 눈치채지 못하신 듯했지만, 런던의 검댕이 당신이 이곳에서 학생이던 시절만큼은 그리 심하지 않다고 한마디 하시더군. 그분이 어떻게든 해주실 수 있다면 나는 내일 그분의 보고서를 받게 될 걸세. 어쨌든 편지는 받게 될 거야.\n\n자, 방문에 관해서라면. 루시는 내가 처음 보았던 날보다 더 명랑했고, 확실히 안색도 나아 보였네. 자네를 그토록 심란하게 했던 그 송장 같은 기색이 어느 정도 가셨고, 호흡도 정상이었어. 그녀는 (늘 그렇듯) 교수님께 무척 다정하게 대하며 그분을 편안하게 해드리려 애썼네. 비록 그 가엾은 여인이 그러느라 무던히도 힘겨운 싸움을 하고 있음을 나는 알 수 있었지만 말이야. 판 헬싱 교수도 그것을 알아채셨다고 믿네. 내가 예전부터 알던, 그 덥수룩한 눈썹 아래로 스치는 날카로운 눈빛을 보았으니까. 그러고 나서 그분은 우리 자신이나 질병에 관한 것만 빼고 온갖 이야기를 나누기 시작하셨는데, 어찌나 끝없이 다정하셨던지, 나는 가엾은 루시의 짐짓 꾸며낸 활기가 진짜 활기로 녹아드는 것을 볼 수 있었네. 그러고는, 겉으로는 아무런 변화도 없이, 그분은 대화를 부드럽게 당신의 방문 쪽으로 돌리시더니, 정중하게 말씀하셨네—\n\n‘사랑스러운 아가씨, 나는 더없이 큰 기쁨을 느낀다오. 아가씨가 그토록 많은 사랑을 받고 있으니 말이오. 그건 대단한 것이라오, 아가씨. 비록 내 눈에 보이지 않는 무언가가 있다 하더라도 말이오. 사람들이 내게 그러더군, 아가씨가 기분이 가라앉아 있고, 송장처럼 창백하다고 말이오. 그들에게 나는 이렇게 말한다오. “흥!”’ 그러고는 나를 향해 손가락을 탁 튕기시고는 말을 이으셨네. ‘하지만 아가씨와 나는 그들이 얼마나 틀렸는지 보여줍시다. 저 친구가’—그러면서 그분은, 언젠가 한 특정한 자리에서, 아니 그 자리 직후에—그분은 내게 그 일을 상기시키기를 잊는 법이 없는데—강의실에서 학생들에게 나를 가리키실 때와 똑같은 눈빛과 몸짓으로 나를 가리키셨네—‘젊은 아가씨에 대해 무얼 알겠소? 저 친구한테는 가지고 놀 미치광이들이 있고, 그들을 행복으로, 또 그들을 사랑하는 이들에게로 되돌려놓는 일이 있다오. 그건 대단한 일이고, 아, 그래서 우리가 그런 행복을 베풀 수 있다는 점에서 보답도 있지요. 하지만 젊은 아가씨라니! 저 친구한테는 아내도 딸도 없고, 또 젊은이는 젊은이에게 제 속을 털어놓지 않고, 나처럼 그토록 많은 슬픔과 그 까닭들을 겪어온 늙은이에게 털어놓는 법이라오. 그러니, 아가씨, 우리는 저 친구를 정원으로 내보내 담배나 피우게 하고, 아가씨와 나는 단둘이 잠깐 이야기나 나눕시다.’ 나는 그 뜻을 알아채고 이리저리 거닐었는데, 이윽고 교수님이 창가로 오셔서 나를 안으로 부르셨네. 그분은 심각한 표정이셨지만 이렇게 말씀하셨어. ‘세심하게 진찰해보았지만, 기능적인 원인은 없네. 피를 많이 잃었다는 자네 의견에는 나도 동의하네. 잃은 적이 있었지만, 지금은 아니야. 그러나 그녀의 상태는 결코 빈혈성이 아닐세. 나는 그녀에게 하녀를 보내달라고 청했네. 한두 가지만 물어보아서, 혹여 아무것도 놓치는 일이 없도록 말이지. 그 하녀가 무슨 말을 할지는 나도 잘 아네. 그렇지만 원인은 있어. 매사에는 언제나 원인이 있는 법이지. 나는 집으로 돌아가 생각해봐야겠네. 자네는 내게 날마다 전보를 보내주게. 그리고 까닭이 있다면 다시 오겠네. 이 병은—온전히 건강하지 못한 것은 다 병이니까—내 흥미를 끄는 데다, 그 사랑스러운 아가씨, 그녀도 내 흥미를 끄네. 그녀는 나를 사로잡아. 그러니 그녀를 위해서라면, 설령 자네나 이 병을 위해서가 아니더라도, 나는 오겠네.’\n\n말했다시피, 단둘이 있을 때조차 그분은 더는 한마디도 하려 하지 않으셨네. 그러니 이제, 아트, 내가 아는 것은 자네도 모두 알게 된 셈이야. 나는 빈틈없이 지켜보겠네. 자네의 가엾은 부친께서 차도가 있으시길 바라네. 둘 다 그토록 소중한 두 사람 사이에 그런 처지에 놓인다는 것은, 친애하는 오랜 벗이여, 자네에게 끔찍한 일임에 틀림없어. 나는 자네의 부친에 대한 의무관을 알고, 자네가 그것을 지키는 것이 옳다고 보네. 그러나 필요하다면, 즉시 루시에게 오라고 자네에게 기별하겠네. 그러니 내게서 소식을 듣기 전에는 지나치게 걱정하지 말게.\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n9월 4일.—식동물성(食動物性) 환자가 여전히 우리의 관심을 붙들어둔다. 그는 단 한 번 발작을 일으켰는데, 그것은 어제 평소답지 않은 시각에 일어났다. 정오를 알리는 종이 울리기 직전, 그는 안절부절못하기 시작했다. 간호인은 그 징후를 알고 즉시 지원을 불렀다. 다행히도 사람들이 달려와 때마침 도착했으니, 정오를 알리는 종이 울리자 그가 어찌나 난폭해졌던지 그들의 온 힘을 다해야 그를 붙들 수 있었기 때문이다. 그러나 오 분쯤 지나자 그는 점점 더 잠잠해지더니, 마침내 일종의 우울 상태에 빠져들어 지금까지 그 상태에 머물러 있다. 간호인이 말하기를, 그가 발작 중에 지른 비명은 참으로 소름 끼쳤다고 한다. 내가 들어갔을 때, 나는 그에게 겁먹은 다른 환자 몇몇을 돌보느라 손이 모자랄 지경이었다. 사실 나는 그 효과를 충분히 이해할 수 있는데, 그 소리는 꽤 떨어져 있던 나조차 동요시켰기 때문이다. 지금은 요양원의 저녁 식사 시간이 지났는데도, 내 환자는 아직 한구석에 앉아 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있다. 그의 얼굴에는 멍하고 시무룩하며 비탄에 잠긴 표정이 어려 있는데, 무언가를 직접 드러낸다기보다는 암시하는 듯하다. 나는 그것을 도무지 이해하지 못하겠다.\n\n이후.—내 환자에게 또 한 번의 변화가 있었다. 다섯 시에 나는 그를 들여다보았는데, 예전처럼 행복하고 만족스러워 보였다. 그는 파리를 잡아먹고 있었고, 충전재의 두둑한 부분 사이 문 가장자리에 손톱으로 자국을 내어 자신이 잡은 것을 기록하고 있었다. 그는 나를 보더니 다가와 자신의 못된 행실을 사과하고는, 무척 비굴하고 굽신거리는 투로 자기 방으로 다시 데려다 달라고, 그리고 수첩을 돌려달라고 청했다. 나는 그의 비위를 맞춰주는 게 좋겠다 싶었다. 그래서 그는 창문을 연 채 자기 방으로 돌아가 있다. 그는 차에 넣을 설탕을 창턱에 펼쳐놓고 파리를 제법 풍성하게 거두어들이고 있다. 이제 그는 파리를 먹지 않고, 예전처럼 상자에 넣고 있으며, 벌써 거미를 찾으려고 방 구석구석을 살피고 있다. 나는 그에게 지난 며칠에 관해 이야기를 시켜보려 했는데, 그의 생각에 대한 어떤 실마리든 내게는 엄청난 도움이 될 터였기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 응하지 않았다. 한두 순간 그는 무척 슬퍼 보이더니, 나에게라기보다는 차라리 자기 자신에게 하는 듯한, 어딘가 아득한 목소리로 말했다—\n\n“다 끝났어! 다 끝났어! 그분이 나를 버렸어. 내 스스로 하지 않는 한 이제 내게는 희망이 없어!” 그러고는 갑자기 단호한 태도로 나를 돌아보며 말했다. “선생님, 제게 좀 잘해주셔서 설탕을 조금 더 주시지 않겠어요? 그게 제게 좋을 것 같아요.”\n\n“그럼 파리는?” 내가 물었다.\n\n“네! 파리도 그걸 좋아하고, 저는 파리를 좋아하지요. 그러니 저는 그걸 좋아하는 거예요.” 그런데도 세상에는 미치광이가 논리를 따지지 못한다고 여길 만큼 아는 게 적은 사람들이 있다. 나는 그에게 두 배의 양을 마련해주었고, 짐작건대 세상 그 누구 못지않게 행복한 사람을 남겨두고 자리를 떴다. 그의 마음을 헤아릴 수 있다면 좋으련만.\n\n자정.—그에게 또 한 번의 변화가 있었다. 나는 웨스텐라 양을 보러 갔다가—그녀는 한결 나아 보였다—막 돌아와, 우리 정문에 서서 석양을 바라보고 있었는데, 그때 또 한 번 그가 고함지르는 소리가 들렸다. 그의 방이 건물의 이쪽에 있어서, 아침보다 더 잘 들렸다. 그 불그레한 빛과 먹빛 그림자, 그리고 더러운 물 위에서처럼 더러운 구름 위에도 어리는 그 온갖 경이로운 빛깔을 머금은, 런던 위로 펼쳐진 연기 자욱한 황혼의 그 놀라운 아름다움으로부터 고개를 돌려, 내 차디찬 돌 건물의 그 모든 음울한 준엄함을, 그 안에 그득한 숨 쉬는 비참함을, 그리고 그 모든 것을 견뎌야 하는 내 자신의 황량한 마음을 깨닫는 것은, 내게 하나의 충격이었다. 내가 그에게 다다랐을 때 마침 해가 지고 있었고, 나는 그의 창문으로 그 붉은 원반이 가라앉는 것을 보았다. 그것이 가라앉을수록 그는 점점 덜 광란했고, 해가 잠겨드는 바로 그 순간, 그는 자신을 붙들고 있던 손들에서 미끄러져 내려, 축 늘어진 덩어리가 되어 바닥에 쓰러졌다. 그러나 광인들이 지닌 지적 회복력이란 참으로 놀라운 것이어서, 몇 분 안에 그는 아주 차분하게 일어서더니 주위를 둘러보았다. 나는 간호인들에게 그를 붙들지 말라고 신호했는데, 그가 무엇을 할지 보고 싶었기 때문이다. 그는 곧장 창문 쪽으로 가서 설탕 부스러기를 쓸어내고는, 파리 상자를 집어 들어 그 안의 것을 바깥에 비워버리고 상자를 내던졌다. 그러고는 창문을 닫고 건너와 침대에 앉았다. 이 모든 것이 나를 놀라게 하여, 나는 그에게 물었다. “이제 파리를 더는 안 기를 텐가?”\n\n“네,” 그가 말했다. “그런 쓰레기 같은 짓은 이제 진절머리가 나요!” 그는 확실히 놀라우리만큼 흥미로운 연구 대상이다. 그의 마음이나 그 돌연한 발작의 원인을 한 자락이라도 엿볼 수 있다면 좋으련만. 가만, 어쩌면 결국 실마리가 있을지도 모른다. 오늘 그의 발작이 어찌하여 정오와 일몰에 일어났는지를 알아낼 수만 있다면 말이다. 어떤 본성들에게는, 마치 때때로 달이 다른 이들에게 그러하듯, 특정한 시기에 해의 악의 어린 영향력이 작용하는 것일 수 있을까? 두고 볼 일이다.\n\n「런던의 수어드가 암스테르담의 판 헬싱에게 보내는 전보」\n\n9월 4일.—환자 오늘도 여전히 호전됨.\n\n「런던의 수어드가 암스테르담의 판 헬싱에게 보내는 전보」\n\n9월 5일.—환자 크게 호전됨. 식욕 좋고, 잠 자연스러우며, 기분 좋고, 혈색 돌아오는 중.\n\n「런던의 수어드가 암스테르담의 판 헬싱에게 보내는 전보」\n\n9월 6일.—끔찍하게 악화됨. 즉시 오시오. 한 시간도 지체 마시오. 홈우드에게 보낼 전보는 당신을 뵐 때까지 미뤄두겠소.\n\n「수어드 박사가 아서 홈우드 님에게 보내는 편지」\n\n“9월 6일.\n\n“친애하는 아트에게,—\n“오늘 내 소식은 그리 좋지 못하네. 오늘 아침 루시가 다소 나빠졌어. 그러나 거기서 한 가지 좋은 일이 생겼네. 웨스튼라 부인은 당연히 루시 일로 걱정하던 차에, 딸에 관해 내게 의사로서 진찰을 청했다네. 나는 이 기회를 틈타, 위대한 전문의이신 내 옛 스승 반 헬싱이 나와 함께 머물러 계실 예정이라고, 그래서 부인을 나와 그분께 함께 맡기겠노라고 말씀드렸네. 그러니 이제 우리는 부인을 지나치게 놀라게 하지 않고도 드나들 수 있게 되었지. 부인에게는 충격이 곧 급사를 뜻할 터인데, 루시의 약한 상태에서 그런 일이 벌어진다면 더없이 참담한 결과가 될 테니 말이네. 우리 모두, 가엾은 친구여, 사방이 어려움으로 둘러싸여 있네. 그러나 신의 가호로 무사히 헤쳐 나가게 되겠지. 무슨 일이 생기면 편지하겠네. 그러니 내게서 소식이 없거든 그저 내가 소식을 기다리고 있는 것이라 여기게나. 바삐 줄이며,\n\n“언제나 자네의 벗,\n\n“존 수어드.”\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n9월 7일. — 리버풀 가에서 만났을 때 반 헬싱이 나에게 처음 건넨 말은 이것이었다.\n\n“우리 젊은 친구, 그녀를 사랑하는 그 청년에게 무어라 말한 게 있는가?”\n\n“아니요,” 하고 내가 답했다. “전보에서 말씀드린 대로 선생님을 뵐 때까지 기다렸습니다. 그저 웨스튼라 양의 상태가 좋지 못하여 선생님께서 오신다는 것, 그리고 필요하면 알려주겠다는 것만 적어 편지를 보냈습니다.”\n\n“옳네, 친구,” 그가 말했다. “아주 옳아! 그가 아직은 모르는 편이 낫네. 어쩌면 영영 모르게 될 수도 있지. 나는 그러기를 기도하네. 그러나 필요하다면 그때는 모든 것을 알게 될 것이야. 그리고, 나의 좋은 친구 존, 한 가지 일러두겠네. 자네는 미친 사람들을 상대하지. 사람은 누구나 이런저런 식으로 미쳐 있다네. 그러니 자네가 미친 자들을 신중히 다루듯, 신께서 만드신 미친 자들 — 곧 나머지 온 세상 사람들 — 도 그렇게 다루게. 미친 자들에게는 자네가 무엇을 하는지, 왜 하는지 말하지 않지. 무슨 생각을 하는지도 말하지 않고. 그렇게 해서 지식을 제자리에, 그것이 쉴 수 있는 곳에 — 제 동류를 둘레에 끌어모아 번식할 수 있는 곳에 — 간직하는 것이지. 자네와 나는 우리가 아는 것을 아직은 여기, 그리고 여기에 간직해 두세.” 그는 내 가슴과 이마를 차례로 짚더니, 자신에게도 똑같이 했다. “나는 지금 나 나름의 생각이 있네. 나중에 자네에게 펼쳐 보이겠네.”\n\n“왜 지금은 안 됩니까?” 하고 내가 물었다. “도움이 될지도 모르고, 어떤 결론에 이를 수도 있지 않습니까.” 그는 걸음을 멈추고 나를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n“나의 친구 존, 곡식이 자라났을 때, 아직 여물기도 전에 — 어머니 대지의 젖이 아직 그 안에 있고 햇살이 아직 그것을 금빛으로 물들이기 시작하지도 않았을 때 — 농부는 이삭을 뽑아 거친 손으로 비벼서 푸른 겉껍질을 불어 날려 보내고는 자네에게 이렇게 말하지. ‘보시오! 좋은 곡식이오. 때가 되면 좋은 수확이 되겠소.’” 나는 그 비유의 뜻을 알아듣지 못하여 그렇게 말했다. 그러자 그는 답 대신 손을 뻗어 내 귀를 잡고는, 오래전 강의 시절에 곧잘 하던 것처럼 장난스레 잡아당기며 말했다. “좋은 농부가 그때 자네에게 그리 말하는 것은 그가 알기 때문일세. 그러나 그때가 되기 전에는 아니지. 그렇다고 좋은 농부가 심어 놓은 곡식을 자라는지 보겠다고 파헤치는 일은 없네. 그건 농사 놀이를 하는 아이들이나 하는 짓이지, 농사를 제 평생의 일로 삼는 사람이 할 짓이 아니야. 이제 알겠는가, 친구 존? 나는 내 곡식을 뿌렸고, 그것을 싹틔우는 일은 자연의 몫이지. 그것이 싹을 틔우기만 한다면 어떤 약속이 있는 것이고, 나는 이삭이 부풀기 시작할 때까지 기다리는 것일세.” 그는 말을 끊었으니, 내가 알아들었음을 분명히 보았던 것이다. 그러고는 매우 엄숙하게 말을 이었다.\n\n“자네는 언제나 꼼꼼한 학생이었고, 자네의 증례 기록부는 늘 다른 누구의 것보다 빼곡했지. 그때 자네는 학생일 뿐이었으나, 이제는 한 사람의 권위자라네. 나는 그 좋은 습관이 사라지지 않았으리라 믿네. 친구여, 기억하게. 지식은 기억보다 강하니, 우리는 더 약한 쪽을 믿어서는 안 되네. 설령 자네가 그 좋은 습관을 유지하지 못했더라도, 한 가지 말해 두겠네. 우리 사랑스러운 아가씨의 이 증례는 — 명심하게, 어쩌면이라고 말하는 것일세 — 우리와 다른 이들에게 어찌나 흥미로운 것이 될 수 있는지, 자네 나라 사람들 말마따나 나머지 모든 것이 저울추를 들어 올리지 못할 만큼일 수도 있네. 그러니 잘 적어 두게. 무엇 하나 사소하다고 흘려서는 안 되네. 충고하건대 자네의 의심과 추측까지도 기록에 남겨 두게. 훗날 자네의 짐작이 얼마나 들어맞았는지 들여다보는 것이 흥미로울지도 모르니. 우리는 성공이 아니라 실패에서 배우는 법일세!”\n\n내가 루시의 증세를 설명했을 때 — 전과 같았으나 한없이 더 두드러진 것이었다 — 그는 매우 엄숙한 표정을 지었으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그는 여러 기구와 약품이 든 가방을 가지고 왔는데, 이는 그가 언젠가 강의에서 치료술을 다루는 자의 장비를 두고 “우리 이로운 직업의 섬뜩한 도구 일습”이라 부른 바로 그것이었다. 우리가 안으로 안내되자 웨스튼라 부인이 우리를 맞았다. 그녀는 놀란 듯했으나, 내가 예상했던 것만큼은 결코 아니었다. 자연은 그 자비로운 기분의 하나로, 죽음조차도 그 자체의 공포에 대한 해독제를 어느 정도 지니도록 정해 두었다. 여기, 어떤 충격이든 치명적일 수 있는 경우에, 사정이 그렇게 짜여 있어, 무슨 까닭에서인지 자기 자신에게 직접 닿지 않는 일들은 — 그토록 애지중지하는 딸의 끔찍한 변화조차도 — 그녀에게 미치지 못하는 듯하다. 그것은 마치 자연의 여신이 이물질의 둘레에 무감각한 조직의 외피를 둘러, 그렇지 않았다면 접촉으로 해를 입혔을 것을 악으로부터 보호하는 방식과도 비슷하다. 만일 이것이 정해진 이기심이라면, 우리는 누군가를 자기중심주의라는 악덕으로 단죄하기 전에 잠시 멈추어야 할 것이다. 그 원인에는 우리가 아는 것보다 더 깊은 뿌리가 있을지도 모르니 말이다.\n\n나는 영적 병리학의 이 국면에 대한 내 지식을 활용하여, 부인이 루시 곁에 있거나 딸의 병을 꼭 필요한 정도 이상으로 생각하지 않도록 하나의 규칙을 정해 두었다. 그녀는 선뜻 따랐는데, 어찌나 선뜻이던지 나는 다시금 생명을 위해 싸우는 자연의 손길을 보았다. 반 헬싱과 나는 루시의 방으로 안내되었다. 어제 그녀를 보고 충격을 받았다면, 오늘은 그녀를 보고 소름이 끼쳤다. 그녀는 섬뜩하리만치, 백악처럼 창백했다. 입술과 잇몸에서마저 붉은빛이 가신 듯했고, 얼굴 뼈가 두드러지게 도드라졌으며, 그 숨소리는 보기에도 듣기에도 고통스러웠다. 반 헬싱의 얼굴은 대리석처럼 굳어졌고, 그의 눈썹은 콧등 위에서 거의 맞닿을 만큼 좁혀졌다. 루시는 꼼짝없이 누워 말할 기운조차 없어 보였으므로, 우리는 한동안 모두 말이 없었다. 그러다 반 헬싱이 나에게 손짓을 했고, 우리는 조용히 방을 나섰다. 문을 닫는 순간 그는 복도를 따라 재빨리 걸어 열려 있던 옆방으로 가더니, 나를 얼른 끌어들이고 문을 닫았다. “맙소사!” 그가 말했다. “이건 끔찍하네. 한시도 지체할 수 없어. 그녀는 심장이 제대로 뛰는 데 필요한 피가 모자라 죽고 말 걸세. 당장 수혈을 해야 하네. 자네인가, 나인가?”\n\n“제가 더 젊고 튼튼합니다, 선생님. 저여야 합니다.”\n\n“그럼 당장 채비하게. 내가 가방을 가져오지. 나는 준비가 되어 있네.”\n\n나는 그와 함께 아래층으로 내려갔다. 내려가는데 현관문에서 노크 소리가 났다. 우리가 현관에 이르렀을 때 하녀가 막 문을 연 참이었고, 아서가 빠르게 들어서고 있었다. 그는 나에게 달려와 다급한 속삭임으로 말했다.\n\n“잭, 나는 너무도 불안했네. 자네 편지의 행간을 읽고는 줄곧 괴로워했지. 아버님이 좀 나아지셔서 내 눈으로 직접 보려고 이렇게 달려온 걸세. 저분이 반 헬싱 박사 아니신가? 와 주셔서 정말 감사합니다, 선생님.” 처음 그 청년에게 시선이 닿았을 때, 교수는 이런 때에 끼어든 것을 못마땅해했다. 그러나 이제 그의 다부진 체격을 살피고 그에게서 뿜어 나오는 듯한 굳센 젊음의 기운을 알아보자, 그의 두 눈이 번뜩였다. 그는 지체 없이 손을 내밀며 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n“이보게, 자네는 때맞춰 와 주었네. 자네가 우리 사랑스러운 아가씨를 사랑하는 그 청년이지. 그녀는 위중하네, 아주, 아주 위중해. 아니, 이 사람아, 그렇게 굴면 안 되네.” 그가 갑자기 창백해지며 거의 기절할 듯 의자에 주저앉았기 때문이다. “자네가 그녀를 도와야 하네. 자네는 살아 있는 그 누구보다도 많은 것을 할 수 있고, 자네의 용기야말로 가장 큰 도움일세.”\n\n“제가 무엇을 할 수 있습니까?” 아서가 쉰 목소리로 물었다. “말씀해 주십시오, 그러면 하겠습니다. 제 목숨은 그녀의 것이고, 그녀를 위해서라면 제 몸의 마지막 한 방울 피까지 바치겠습니다.” 교수에게는 짙은 해학적인 면이 있었으니, 나는 오래전부터의 경험으로 그의 대답에 그런 기미가 깃들어 있음을 알아챌 수 있었다.\n\n“이보게 젊은이, 나는 그렇게까지 청하지는 않네 — 마지막 한 방울까지는 말이야!”\n\n“제가 무엇을 하면 됩니까?” 그의 눈에는 불길이 일었고, 벌어진 콧구멍이 결의로 떨렸다. 반 헬싱이 그의 어깨를 탁 쳤다. “이리 오게!” 그가 말했다. “자네는 사내일세. 그리고 우리에게 필요한 것이 바로 사내야. 자네는 나보다, 내 친구 존보다 낫네.” 아서가 어리둥절해 보이자 교수는 다정한 투로 설명을 이어갔다.\n\n“젊은 아가씨가 위중하네, 아주 위중해. 그녀에게는 피가 필요하고, 피를 얻지 못하면 죽고 말 걸세. 내 친구 존과 나는 의논을 했네. 우리는 이른바 수혈이라는 것을 하려는 참인데 — 피로 가득 찬 한 사람의 혈관에서, 피를 갈망하며 텅 빈 혈관으로 옮기는 것일세. 존이 자기 피를 주기로 했었네, 나보다 더 젊고 튼튼하니까” — 여기서 아서가 내 손을 잡고 말없이 힘껏 쥐었다 — “그러나 이제 자네가 왔으니, 자네가 우리보다 낫네, 늙었든 젊었든, 우리는 생각의 세계에서 너무 많이 애쓰는 사람들이지. 우리 신경은 자네만큼 차분하지 못하고, 우리 피도 자네만큼 맑지 못하네!” 아서가 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n“제가 얼마나 기꺼이 그녀를 위해 죽을 수 있는지 아신다면, 이해하실 텐데요——”\n\n그는 목이 메는 듯한 소리를 내며 말을 멈추었다.\n\n“착한 청년이로군!” 반 헬싱이 말했다. “머지않아 자네는 사랑하는 이를 위해 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다 했다는 사실에 행복해질 걸세. 자, 이제 와서 잠자코 있게. 일을 시작하기 전에 한 번 그녀에게 입을 맞춰도 좋네. 그러나 그다음에는 가야 하네. 내가 신호하면 떠나야 해. 부인께는 한마디도 하지 말게. 그분이 어떤 상태인지 자네도 알지 않나! 충격이 있어서는 안 되네. 이 일을 알게 되는 것 자체가 충격일 테니. 자, 가세!”\n\n우리는 모두 루시의 방으로 올라갔다. 아서는 지시받은 대로 밖에 남았다. 루시는 고개를 돌려 우리를 바라보았으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 그녀는 잠든 것이 아니라, 그저 너무 약해서 애쓸 기운이 없었던 것이다. 그녀의 눈이 우리에게 말을 건넸으니, 그것이 전부였다. 반 헬싱은 가방에서 몇 가지를 꺼내 보이지 않게 작은 탁자 위에 놓았다. 그러고는 진정제를 섞어, 침대로 다가가 밝은 목소리로 말했다.\n\n“자, 아가씨, 여기 약이 있어요. 착한 아이답게 쭉 들이켜요. 보세요, 삼키기 쉽도록 이렇게 일으켜 드릴게요. 그래요.” 그녀는 애써 무사히 그것을 삼켰다.\n\n약이 효과를 내기까지 그토록 오래 걸리는 것이 나는 놀라웠다. 사실 이것이야말로 그녀가 얼마나 쇠약한지를 가늠케 하는 것이었다. 졸음이 그녀의 눈꺼풀에 어른거리기 시작하기까지의 시간이 끝없이 느껴졌다. 그러나 마침내 진정제가 효력을 드러내기 시작했고, 그녀는 깊은 잠에 빠져들었다. 교수는 흡족해하자 아서를 방 안으로 불러들여 외투를 벗으라고 일렀다. 그러고는 덧붙였다. “내가 탁자를 가져오는 동안 그 한 번의 가벼운 입맞춤을 해도 좋네. 친구 존, 나를 좀 도와주게!” 그리하여 그가 그녀에게 몸을 굽히는 동안 우리 둘 다 그쪽을 보지 않았다.\n\n반 헬싱이 나를 향해 돌아서며 말했다.\n\n“이 청년은 매우 젊고 튼튼한 데다 피가 워낙 맑으니, 우리는 그것의 섬유소를 제거할 필요가 없겠네.”\n\n그러고는 신속하게, 그러나 더없이 빈틈없는 방식으로 반 헬싱은 그 시술을 했다. 수혈이 진행됨에 따라 가엾은 루시의 두 뺨에 생명 같은 것이 되돌아오는 듯했고, 점점 창백해지는 아서의 얼굴 너머로는 그 기쁨이 그야말로 환히 빛나는 듯했다. 얼마 뒤 나는 불안해지기 시작했으니, 아무리 그가 건장한 사내라 해도 피의 손실이 아서에게 영향을 미치고 있었기 때문이다. 아서를 약하게 만든 것이 그녀를 부분적으로밖에 회복시키지 못한다는 사실은, 루시의 몸이 얼마나 끔찍한 부담을 겪었을지를 짐작케 했다. 그러나 교수의 얼굴은 굳어 있었고, 그는 손에 시계를 든 채 서서 눈을 환자에게 두었다가 다시 아서에게 두기를 거듭했다. 나는 내 심장이 뛰는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 이윽고 그가 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. “한순간도 움직이지 말게. 충분해. 자네는 이 친구를 돌보게. 나는 그녀를 살피지.” 모든 것이 끝났을 때, 나는 아서가 얼마나 약해졌는지 알 수 있었다. 나는 상처를 싸매고 그를 데리고 나오려 그의 팔을 잡았는데, 그때 반 헬싱이 돌아보지도 않고 말했다 — 이 사람은 뒤통수에도 눈이 달린 모양이다.\n\n“이 용감한 연인은 또 한 번의 입맞춤을 받을 자격이 있다고 생각하네. 이제 곧 받게 될 걸세.” 그러고는 시술을 마쳤으므로, 그는 환자의 머리맡 베개를 매만졌다. 그렇게 하는 사이, 그녀가 늘 목에 두르고 있는 듯한 좁은 검은 벨벳 띠 — 연인이 그녀에게 준 오래된 다이아몬드 버클로 채운 — 가 살짝 위로 끌려 올라가, 그녀의 목에 난 붉은 자국이 드러났다. 아서는 그것을 알아채지 못했으나, 나는 반 헬싱이 감정을 드러내는 한 가지 방식인 그 깊은 들숨의 쉭 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 그는 그 순간에는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 나를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. “이제 우리 용감한 젊은 연인을 데리고 내려가, 포트와인을 좀 마시게 하고 잠시 눕혀 두게. 그런 다음 그는 집에 가서 쉬고, 푹 자고 많이 먹어야 하네. 그래야 사랑하는 이에게 그토록 바친 것을 다시 채울 수 있을 테니. 그는 여기 머물러서는 안 되네. 잠깐! 잠시만. 이보게, 결과가 궁금하시겠지요. 그렇다면, 시술이 모든 면에서 성공적이었다는 사실을 가지고 가시오. 자네는 이번에 그녀의 목숨을 구했네. 그러니 집에 가서, 할 수 있는 모든 것이 다 되었다는 마음의 평안을 안고 편히 쉬게나. 그녀가 회복되면 내가 모든 것을 그녀에게 말해 주겠네. 자네가 한 일로 그녀가 자네를 덜 사랑하게 되는 일은 결코 없을 걸세. 잘 가게.”\n\n아서가 떠나자 나는 방으로 돌아갔다. 루시는 평온하게 잠들어 있었으나, 그 숨결은 한결 힘찼다. 가슴이 오르내리며 침대보가 움직이는 것이 보였다. 침대 곁에는 반 헬싱이 앉아 그녀를 골똘히 바라보고 있었다. 벨벳 띠가 다시 붉은 자국을 덮고 있었다. 나는 교수에게 속삭이며 물었다.\n\n“저 목의 자국을 어떻게 보십니까?”\n\n“자네는 어떻게 보는가?”\n\n“아직 살펴보지 못했습니다,” 하고 나는 답하고는, 그 자리에서 곧장 띠를 풀었다. 바깥쪽 경정맥 바로 위에 두 개의 찔린 자국이 있었는데, 크지는 않았으나 성해 보이지는 않았다. 질병의 징후는 없었으나, 가장자리가 무언가에 갈린 듯 희고 닳아 보였다. 곧바로 내 머릿속에는, 이 상처가 — 그것이 무엇이든 간에 — 그 뚜렷한 피의 손실의 원인일지도 모른다는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그러나 나는 그 생각이 떠오르기가 무섭게 떨쳐 버렸으니, 그런 일은 있을 수 없기 때문이었다. 그 소녀가 수혈 전과 같은 창백함에 이르도록 흘렸을 만큼의 피라면, 침대 전체가 진홍빛으로 흠뻑 젖었을 것이다.\n\n“어떤가?” 하고 반 헬싱이 물었다.\n\n“글쎄요,” 하고 내가 말했다. “저는 도무지 알 수가 없습니다.” 교수가 일어섰다. “나는 오늘 밤 암스테르담으로 돌아가야 하네,” 하고 그가 말했다. “거기 내가 필요한 책과 물건들이 있어. 자네는 밤새 여기 남아, 그녀에게서 한순간도 눈을 떼서는 안 되네.”\n\n“간호사를 둘까요?” 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n“우리가 가장 좋은 간호사일세, 자네와 나 말이야. 자네는 밤새 지키게. 그녀가 잘 먹는지, 무엇도 그녀를 방해하지 않는지 살피게. 밤새 한숨도 자서는 안 되네. 잠은 나중에 자도 되네, 자네와 나 말이야. 나는 되도록 빨리 돌아오겠네. 그러면 우리는 시작할 수 있을 걸세.”\n\n“시작이라뇨?” 하고 내가 말했다. “도대체 무슨 말씀이십니까?”\n\n“두고 보면 알게 될 걸세!” 그가 서둘러 나가며 답했다. 그는 잠시 후 다시 돌아와 문 안으로 고개를 들이밀고는, 경고하듯 손가락을 치켜세우며 말했다.\n\n“명심하게, 그녀는 자네에게 맡겨졌네. 자네가 그녀를 떠나 해를 입게 한다면, 자네는 이후로 결코 편히 잠들지 못할 걸세!”\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기 — 계속」\n\n9월 8일. — 나는 밤새도록 루시 곁을 지켰다. 마약 성분의 효력은 땅거미가 질 무렵 저절로 풀렸고, 그녀는 자연스레 깨어났다. 그녀는 시술 전과는 딴사람 같아 보였다. 기분마저 좋았고 행복한 활기로 가득했으나, 나는 그녀가 겪은 극심한 탈진의 흔적을 볼 수 있었다. 반 헬싱 박사가 내게 그녀 곁을 지키라고 지시했다고 웨스튼라 부인에게 말하자, 부인은 딸의 되살아난 기력과 더없이 좋은 기분을 들며 그 생각을 거의 코웃음 치듯 일축했다. 그러나 나는 단호히 버티며 긴 밤샘을 위한 채비를 했다. 하녀가 그녀의 잠자리를 봐 준 뒤, 나는 그사이 저녁을 먹고 들어와 침대 곁에 자리를 잡았다. 그녀는 조금도 반대하지 않고, 내 눈길과 마주칠 때마다 고마운 듯 나를 바라보았다. 한참 만에 그녀는 잠에 빠져드는 듯하더니, 안간힘을 쓰며 정신을 가다듬어 잠을 떨쳐 냈다. 이것이 여러 번 되풀이되었는데, 시간이 흐를수록 더 안간힘을 쓰고 그 간격은 짧아졌다. 그녀가 잠들고 싶어 하지 않는 것이 분명했으므로, 나는 곧장 그 문제를 꺼냈다.\n\n“잠들고 싶지 않으신가요?”\n\n“네. 무서워요.”\n\n“잠드는 게 무섭다니! 어째서요? 잠은 우리 모두가 갈망하는 은혜인데요.”\n\n“아, 선생님이 저 같으셨다면 — 잠이 선생님께 공포의 전조였다면 그러지 않으실 거예요!”\n\n“공포의 전조라니! 도대체 무슨 말씀이신가요?”\n\n“모르겠어요. 아, 모르겠어요. 그래서 더 끔찍한 거예요. 이 모든 쇠약함이 잠 속에서 저에게 닥쳐와요. 그래서 잠이라는 생각 자체가 두려워질 지경이에요.”\n\n“하지만, 아가씨, 오늘 밤은 주무셔도 됩니다. 제가 여기서 지켜보고 있으니, 아무 일도 없으리라고 약속드리지요.”\n\n“아, 선생님은 믿을 수 있어요!” 나는 그 기회를 붙잡아 말했다. “나쁜 꿈의 기미가 조금이라도 보이면 곧장 깨워 드리겠다고 약속하지요.”\n\n“그래 주시겠어요? 아, 정말 그러시겠어요? 저에게 어찌 이리 잘해 주시는지요. 그럼 자겠어요!” 그리고 그 말과 거의 동시에 그녀는 깊은 안도의 한숨을 내쉬고는, 뒤로 기대어 잠이 들었다.\n\n밤새도록 나는 그녀 곁에서 지켰다. 그녀는 한 번도 뒤척이지 않고, 깊고 평온하며 생명을 주고 건강을 주는 잠에 끝없이 빠져 있었다. 입술은 살짝 벌어졌고, 가슴은 추처럼 규칙적으로 오르내렸다. 얼굴에는 미소가 어려 있었고, 그녀의 마음의 평화를 어지럽힐 나쁜 꿈은 찾아오지 않은 것이 분명했다.\n\n이른 아침에 그녀의 하녀가 왔으므로, 나는 그녀를 하녀에게 맡기고 집으로 돌아왔다. 신경 쓰이는 일이 많았기 때문이다. 나는 반 헬싱과 아서에게 시술의 더없이 좋은 결과를 알리는 짧은 전보를 보냈다. 산더미처럼 밀린 내 일을 다 정리하는 데 하루가 꼬박 걸렸다. 식육성(食肉性) 환자에 관해 물어볼 수 있게 되었을 때는 이미 날이 어두웠다. 보고는 좋았다. 그는 지난 하루 낮밤 동안 아주 조용했다는 것이었다. 내가 저녁을 먹는 동안 암스테르담의 반 헬싱에게서 전보가 왔는데, 가까이 있는 편이 좋을지 모르니 오늘 밤 힐링엄에 가 있으라는 것, 그리고 자신은 야간 우편 열차로 출발하여 이른 아침에 나와 합류하겠다는 내용이었다.\n\n9월 9일. — 힐링엄에 도착했을 때 나는 꽤나 지치고 녹초가 되어 있었다. 이틀 밤 동안 거의 한숨도 자지 못했고, 내 머리는 뇌의 피로를 알리는 그 마비 같은 것을 느끼기 시작하고 있었다. 루시는 일어나 있었고 기분이 좋았다. 나와 악수하면서 그녀는 내 얼굴을 날카롭게 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n“오늘 밤은 선생님이 밤을 새우시면 안 돼요. 녹초가 되셨잖아요. 저는 이제 완전히 나았어요. 정말이에요. 밤을 새워야 한다면, 제가 선생님 곁에서 새우겠어요.” 나는 그 점을 두고 다투지 않고, 가서 저녁을 먹었다. 루시도 함께 와 주었고, 그 사랑스러운 존재로 활기를 돋워 준 덕에 나는 훌륭한 식사를 했으며, 더없이 훌륭한 그 포트와인을 두어 잔 마셨다. 그러고서 루시는 나를 위층으로 데려가, 자기 방 옆방을 보여 주었다. 거기에는 아늑한 불이 타고 있었다. “자,” 하고 그녀가 말했다. “선생님은 여기 계셔야 해요. 저는 이 문도, 제 방 문도 열어 두겠어요. 소파에 누우셔도 돼요. 환자가 지평선 위에 있는 한, 선생님 같은 의사분들을 침대로 끌고 갈 만한 것은 아무것도 없다는 걸 저도 아니까요. 뭔가 필요하면 제가 부를 테니, 그러면 곧장 와 주시면 돼요.” 나는 그저 따를 수밖에 없었으니, 나는 “녹초”가 되어 있어 애써 본들 밤을 새울 수가 없었기 때문이다. 그리하여 그녀가 무엇이든 필요하면 나를 부르겠다는 약속을 다시 하자, 나는 소파에 누워 모든 것을 까맣게 잊었다.\n\n「루시 웨스튼라의 일기」\n\n9월 9일. — 오늘 밤 나는 너무도 행복하다. 그토록 비참하게 약해져 있던 터라, 생각하고 움직일 수 있다는 것이 마치 강철 같은 하늘에서 동풍이 오래 불어 댄 끝에 햇살을 느끼는 것만 같다. 어쩐 일인지 아서가 아주, 아주 가까이 있는 듯 느껴진다. 그의 존재가 나를 둘러싸고 따뜻하게 감싸는 것 같다. 짐작건대 병과 쇠약함이란 이기적인 것이어서 우리 내면의 눈과 연민을 자기 자신에게로 돌려놓는 반면, 건강과 기력은 사랑을 풀어놓아, 생각과 느낌 속에서 그가 마음껏 떠돌 수 있게 하는 것이리라. 내 생각이 어디에 가 있는지 나는 안다. 아서가 알기만 한다면! 내 사랑, 내 사랑, 깨어 있는 내 귀가 그러하듯, 잠든 당신의 귀도 분명 간질거릴 거예요. 아, 어젯밤의 그 더없이 행복한 안식이라니! 그 다정하고 좋은 수어드 박사가 나를 지켜보는 가운데 나는 어찌나 잘 잤던지. 그리고 오늘 밤 나는 잠들기를 두려워하지 않으리라. 그가 가까이서, 부르면 닿을 곳에 있으니 말이다. 나에게 이토록 잘해 주는 모든 이에게 감사를! 신께 감사를! 잘 자요, 아서.\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n9월 10일. — 나는 내 머리에 닿은 교수의 손길을 느끼고는, 순식간에 놀라 깨어났다. 그것이야말로 어쨌든 우리가 정신병원에서 배우는 것들 가운데 하나다.\n\n“우리 환자는 좀 어떤가?”\n\n“제가 그녀 곁을 떠날 때는, 아니 그녀가 저를 떠날 때는 괜찮았습니다,” 하고 내가 답했다.\n\n“자, 가서 보세,” 하고 그가 말했다. 그리하여 우리는 함께 방으로 들어갔다.\n\n블라인드가 내려져 있어, 나는 그것을 살며시 올리러 갔고, 그동안 반 헬싱은 그 부드러운, 고양이 같은 걸음으로 침대 쪽으로 다가갔다.\n\n내가 블라인드를 올려 아침 햇살이 방 안에 쏟아져 들자, 나는 교수의 낮은 들숨의 쉭 소리를 들었다. 그 소리가 얼마나 드문 것인지 알았던 터라, 치명적인 공포가 내 심장을 꿰뚫었다. 내가 다가가자 그는 뒤로 물러섰고, “하느님 맙소사!”라는 그의 공포에 찬 외침은 그 고통스러운 얼굴로 더 강조할 필요도 없었다. 그는 손을 들어 침대를 가리켰는데, 그 무쇠 같은 얼굴은 일그러진 채 잿빛으로 핏기가 가셔 있었다. 나는 무릎이 떨려 오는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n* 하느님 맙소사: 원문은 독일어 “Gott in Himmel!”\n거기 침대 위에, 기절한 듯이 가엾은 루시가 누워 있었다. 그 어느 때보다도 끔찍하게 희고 핼쑥해 보였다. 입술마저 희었고, 잇몸은 마치 오랜 병을 앓은 시신에게서 더러 보는 것처럼 이에서 움츠러든 듯했다. 반 헬싱이 분노에 발을 들어 쾅 구르려 했으나, 그의 평생의 본능과 오랜 세월의 습관이 그를 붙들어, 그는 다시 발을 살며시 내려놓았다. “빨리!” 그가 말했다. “브랜디를 가져오게.” 나는 식당으로 날아가 유리병을 들고 돌아왔다. 그는 그것으로 가엾은 흰 입술을 적셨고, 우리는 함께 손바닥과 손목과 심장을 문질렀다. 그가 그녀의 심장을 짚어 보더니, 애끓는 몇 순간의 긴장 끝에 말했다.\n\n“너무 늦지는 않았네. 비록 미약하나 뛰고 있어. 우리 일은 모두 헛수고가 되었네. 다시 시작해야 하네. 이번에는 젊은 아서가 여기 없으니, 친구 존, 이번에는 자네 자신에게 청할 수밖에 없네.” 그렇게 말하면서 그는 가방을 뒤져 수혈 기구를 꺼내고 있었다. 나는 외투를 벗고 셔츠 소매를 걷어 올렸다. 지금 당장은 마취제를 쓸 가능성도 없었고, 그럴 필요도 없었다. 그리하여 우리는 한순간도 지체하지 않고 시술을 시작했다. 얼마쯤 뒤 — 그리 짧은 시간도 아니었으니, 아무리 기꺼이 바친다 해도 제 피가 빠져나가는 것은 끔찍한 느낌이다 — 반 헬싱이 경고하듯 손가락을 치켜들었다. “움직이지 말게,” 하고 그가 말했다. “그러나 기력이 살아나면서 그녀가 깨어날까 두렵네. 그러면 위험해질 걸세, 아, 너무도 위험해. 하지만 내가 예방 조치를 하겠네. 모르핀을 피하 주사로 놓아 주지.” 그러고는 신속하고 능란하게 자신의 의도를 실행에 옮겼다. 루시에게 미친 영향은 나쁘지 않았으니, 기절이 미묘하게 마약성 잠으로 녹아들어 가는 듯했기 때문이다. 그 창백한 두 뺨과 입술에 희미한 핏기가 슬며시 되돌아오는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 일종의 개인적인 자부심을 느꼈다. 제 핏줄의 피가 사랑하는 여인의 혈관으로 빨려 들어가는 것을 느끼는 것이 어떤 것인지는, 직접 겪어 보기 전에는 누구도 알지 못한다.\n\n교수는 나를 비판적으로 지켜보았다. “이쯤이면 됐네,” 하고 그가 말했다. “벌써요?” 하고 나는 항변했다. “아트에게서는 훨씬 더 많이 뽑으셨잖습니까.” 그러자 그는 슬픈 듯한 미소를 지으며 답했다.\n\n“그는 그녀의 연인이고, 약혼자일세. 자네는 그녀를 위해, 그리고 다른 이들을 위해 할 일이, 할 일이 많아. 지금은 이 정도면 충분하네.”\n\n우리가 시술을 멈추자 그는 루시를 돌보았고, 나는 내 절개 부위를 손가락으로 눌렀다. 나는 그가 나를 살펴 줄 짬을 기다리며 누워 있었으니, 어질하고 약간 속이 메스꺼웠기 때문이다. 이윽고 그는 내 상처를 싸매고는, 나더러 아래층으로 내려가 와인을 한 잔 마시라고 했다. 내가 방을 나서는데 그가 뒤따라와 반쯤 속삭이듯 말했다.\n\n“명심하게, 이 일은 한마디도 발설해서는 안 되네. 우리 젊은 연인이 전처럼 불쑥 나타나거든, 그에게 한마디도 말하지 말게. 그는 곧장 겁을 먹을 것이고, 게다가 질투까지 하게 될 걸세. 그래서는 안 되네. 알겠지!”\n\n내가 돌아오자 그는 나를 찬찬히 살피더니 말했다.\n\n“자네는 그리 나빠 보이지 않는군. 방으로 들어가 소파에 누워 잠시 쉬게. 그런 다음 든든히 아침을 먹고 이리로 와서 나와 합류하게.”\n\n나는 그의 지시를 따랐으니, 그것이 얼마나 옳고 현명한 것인지 알았기 때문이다. 나는 내 몫을 다했고, 이제 내 다음 의무는 기력을 유지하는 것이었다. 나는 무척 약해진 느낌이었고, 그 쇠약함 속에서 방금 벌어진 일에 대한 놀라움이 어느 정도 가셨다. 그러나 나는 소파에서 잠이 들었으니, 루시가 어떻게 그토록 퇴행해 버렸는지, 그리고 어떻게 그 어디에도 흔적 하나 없이 그토록 많은 피를 빼앗길 수 있었는지를 거듭거듭 의아해하면서였다. 잠결에도 그 의아함을 이어 갔던 모양으로, 자나 깨나 내 생각은 늘 그녀의 목에 난 작은 찔린 자국들과, 비록 자그마하긴 했으나 너덜너덜하고 지친 듯한 그 가장자리로 되돌아왔다.\n\n루시는 한낮까지 잘 잤고, 깨어났을 때는 꽤 좋아지고 기력도 있었으나, 전날만큼은 결코 아니었다. 반 헬싱은 그녀를 살펴본 뒤, 나에게 그녀를 한순간도 떠나지 말라는 엄한 당부를 남기고는 산책을 나갔다. 나는 현관에서 그가 가장 가까운 전신국으로 가는 길을 묻는 목소리를 들을 수 있었다.\n\n루시는 나와 거리낌 없이 이야기를 나누었고, 무슨 일이 있었는지 전혀 의식하지 못하는 듯했다. 나는 그녀를 즐겁게 하고 흥미를 잃지 않게 하려 애썼다. 그녀의 어머니가 보러 올라왔을 때, 부인은 아무런 변화도 알아채지 못하는 듯, 나에게 고마운 듯 말했다.\n\n“수어드 박사님, 해 주신 모든 일에 우리는 너무도 많은 신세를 졌어요. 하지만 이제 정말로 무리하지 않도록 조심하셔야 해요. 박사님도 안색이 창백하세요. 박사님을 간호하고 좀 돌봐 줄 아내가 필요하시군요. 정말이에요!” 그녀가 말하는 동안 루시는 새빨개졌으나, 그것은 잠깐일 뿐이었다. 그 가엾고 쇠한 핏줄이 머리로 그토록 뜻밖에 피가 몰리는 것을 오래 버티지 못했기 때문이다. 그 반작용은 지나친 창백함으로 나타났고, 그녀는 애원하는 눈빛으로 나를 바라보았다. 나는 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕이고는 손가락을 입술에 갖다 댔다. 그러자 그녀는 한숨을 쉬며 베개에 파묻혀 뒤로 기댔다.\n\n반 헬싱은 두어 시간 뒤에 돌아와, 이윽고 나에게 말했다. “자, 자네는 집에 가서 많이 먹고 충분히 마시게. 몸을 튼튼히 하게나. 나는 오늘 밤 여기 머물러, 내가 직접 아가씨 곁을 지키겠네. 자네와 나는 이 증례를 지켜보아야 하고, 다른 누구도 알게 해서는 안 되네. 나에게는 중대한 이유가 있어. 아니, 그것을 묻지는 말게. 자네 좋을 대로 생각하게나. 가장 있을 법하지 않은 것조차도 두려워 말고 생각해 보게. 잘 자게.”\n\n현관에서 하녀 둘이 나에게 다가와, 자기들이나 둘 중 하나가 루시 아가씨 곁을 지켜도 되지 않겠느냐고 물었다. 그들은 그렇게 하게 해 달라고 애원했다. 그러나 내가 반 헬싱 박사나 내가 지키는 것이 그분의 뜻이라고 하자, 그들은 그 “외국인 신사분”에게 대신 청을 넣어 달라고 자못 애처롭게 부탁했다. 나는 그들의 다정함에 크게 마음이 움직였다. 어쩌면 내가 지금 약해져 있기 때문일 수도, 또 어쩌면 그것이 루시를 위한 일이었기 때문일 수도 있으나, 그들의 헌신은 분명히 드러났다. 나는 여인의 다정함의 비슷한 예를 거듭거듭 보아 왔으니 말이다. 나는 때맞춰 이곳에 돌아와 늦은 저녁을 먹었다. 회진을 돌았는데 — 모두 무사했다 — 그리고 잠이 오기를 기다리며 이 글을 적어 둔다. 잠이 오고 있다.\n\n9월 11일. — 오늘 오후 나는 힐링엄으로 건너갔다. 반 헬싱은 더없이 기분이 좋았고, 루시는 한결 나아져 있었다. 내가 도착한 지 얼마 안 되어, 외국에서 온 큰 소포 하나가 교수에게 도착했다. 그는 자못 거창하게 — 물론 짐짓 꾸민 것이었지만 — 그것을 열어, 한 다발의 흰 꽃을 꺼내 보였다.\n\n“이건 아가씨를 위한 거예요, 루시 양,” 하고 그가 말했다.\n\n“저를 위해서요? 어머, 반 헬싱 박사님!”\n\n“그렇소, 아가씨. 하지만 가지고 놀라는 게 아니에요. 이건 약이랍니다.” 여기서 루시가 얼굴을 찡그렸다. “아니, 이건 달인 물이나 역겨운 형태로 먹는 게 아니에요. 그러니 그 매력적인 코를 그렇게 찡그릴 필요 없어요. 안 그러면 내가 내 친구 아서에게, 그가 그토록 사랑하는 그 아름다움이 이리 일그러지는 걸 보면서 어떤 괴로움을 겪어야 할지 일러바칠 테니. 아하, 어여쁜 아가씨, 그러니 그 예쁜 코가 도로 반듯해지는군요. 이건 약효가 있는 거지만, 아가씨는 그 방식을 모를 거예요. 내가 그것을 아가씨 창문에 두고, 예쁜 화환을 만들어 아가씨 목에 둘러 줄 테니, 그러면 푹 잘 수 있을 거예요. 오, 그렇고말고요! 이것들은 연꽃처럼 아가씨의 근심을 잊게 해 준답니다. 그 향기가 어찌나 레테의 강물과도 같고, 콩키스타도르들이 플로리다에서 찾아 헤매다가 너무 늦게야 발견한 그 젊음의 샘물과도 같은지요.”\n\n* 레테: 그리스 신화에서 마시면 과거를 잊게 된다는 망각의 강.\n그가 말하는 동안 루시는 꽃을 살피며 냄새를 맡고 있었다. 이제 그녀는 반쯤 웃고 반쯤 질색하며 그것을 내던지며 말했다.\n\n“어머, 박사님, 그저 저를 놀리시는 거죠. 아니, 이 꽃은 그냥 흔한 마늘이잖아요.”\n\n놀랍게도 반 헬싱이 일어서더니 그 무쇠 같은 턱을 굳히고 무성한 눈썹을 맞붙인 채, 온통 준엄하게 말했다.\n\n“나를 두고 농을 하면 안 돼요! 나는 결코 농담하지 않아요! 내가 하는 모든 일에는 엄정한 목적이 있어요. 경고하건대, 나를 거스르지 말아요. 조심해요, 자신을 위해서가 아니라면 다른 이들을 위해서라도.” 그러고는 가엾은 루시가, 능히 그럴 만하게 겁먹은 것을 보고는, 한결 부드럽게 말을 이었다. “오, 아가씨, 내 사랑스러운 이여, 나를 두려워하지 말아요. 나는 오직 아가씨를 위해 하는 것이니. 이 흔하디흔한 꽃들에는 아가씨에게 이로운 효험이 많이 있답니다. 보세요, 내가 직접 이것을 아가씨 방에 둘 테니. 아가씨가 두를 화환도 내가 직접 만들지요. 하지만 쉿! 그렇게 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는 다른 이들에게는 말하지 말아요. 우리는 따라야 하고, 침묵은 따름의 한 부분이며, 따름은 아가씨를 튼튼하고 건강하게 만들어, 아가씨를 기다리는 사랑하는 팔에 안기게 해 줄 거예요. 자, 잠시 가만히 앉아 있어요. 친구 존, 나와 함께 갑시다. 자네가 내 마늘로 방을 꾸미는 걸 도와주게. 이건 줄곧 하를럼에서 온 것인데, 거기서는 내 친구 판데르폴이 일 년 내내 자기 온실에서 이 풀을 기른다네. 내가 어제 전보를 쳐야 했지, 그러지 않았으면 이게 여기 와 있지 못했을 걸세.”\n\n우리는 꽃을 가지고 방으로 들어갔다. 교수의 행동은 분명 기이했고, 내가 들어 본 그 어떤 약전(藥典)에서도 찾아볼 수 없는 것이었다. 먼저 그는 창문을 닫아 단단히 걸쇠를 채웠다. 다음으로 꽃 한 줌을 집어, 들어올지 모를 한 줄기 바람마다 마늘 냄새를 잔뜩 머금게 하려는 듯, 창틀 전체에 그것을 문질렀다. 그러고는 그 한 다발로 문설주를 위아래로, 양옆으로 두루 문질렀고, 같은 식으로 벽난로 둘레도 문질렀다. 그 모든 것이 내게는 괴이쩍게 보였으므로, 이윽고 나는 말했다.\n\n“글쎄요, 교수님, 저는 선생님이 하시는 일에 늘 이유가 있다는 걸 압니다만, 이건 정말이지 저를 어리둥절하게 합니다. 여기 회의론자가 없으니 다행입니다. 있었다면 선생님이 악령을 막아 내려고 무슨 주문을 거는 중이라고 했을 테니까요.”\n\n“어쩌면 그러는 중일지도 모르지!” 그는 루시가 목에 두를 화환을 만들기 시작하며 조용히 답했다.\n\n그러고서 우리는 루시가 잘 채비를 마치기를 기다렸고, 그녀가 침대에 들자 그는 다가가 손수 마늘 화환을 그녀의 목에 둘러 주었다. 그가 그녀에게 한 마지막 말은 이러했다.\n\n“이걸 흐트러뜨리지 않도록 조심해요. 그리고 설령 방이 답답하게 느껴지더라도, 오늘 밤은 창문도 문도 열지 말아요.”\n\n“약속할게요,” 하고 루시가 말했다. “그리고 저에게 베풀어 주신 모든 친절에 두 분 모두께 천 번 감사드려요! 아, 제가 무슨 일을 했기에 이런 벗들을 두는 복을 받은 걸까요?”\n\n기다리고 있던 내 삯마차를 타고 집을 나서면서 반 헬싱이 말했다.\n\n“오늘 밤 나는 평안히 잘 수 있겠네. 잠이 필요하기도 하고 — 이틀간의 여행에, 그 사이 낮에는 책을 많이 읽었고, 다가올 그날에 대한 근심도 많았으며, 한숨도 자지 않고 밤을 새웠으니 말이야. 내일 아침 일찍 자네가 나를 데리러 오게. 그러면 우리는 함께 우리 어여쁜 아가씨를 보러 가세. 내가 부려 놓은 ‘주문’ 덕에 그토록 더 튼튼해진 그녀를 말이야. 호! 호!”\n\n그가 어찌나 자신만만해 보이던지, 나는 이틀 전 나 자신의 자신감과 그 해로운 결과를 떠올리며, 경외감과 어렴풋한 공포를 느꼈다. 그것을 친구에게 말하기를 망설이게 한 것은 분명 내 약해진 상태 탓이었으리라. 그러나 나는 그것을, 흘리지 못한 눈물처럼 더욱더 사무치게 느꼈다.\n\n「루시 웨스튼라의 일기」\n\n9월 12일. — 모두들 어찌나 나에게 잘해주는지. 나는 그 다정한 판 헬싱 박사가 정말 좋다. 그가 어째서 그 꽃들을 그토록 염려했는지 궁금하다. 그가 어찌나 사납던지 나를 정말로 겁먹게 했다. 그렇지만 그가 옳았던 게 분명한 것이, 나는 벌써 그 꽃들에서 위안을 느끼고 있으니 말이다. 어쩐 일인지 오늘 밤은 혼자 있는 것이 두렵지 않고, 두려움 없이 잠들 수 있을 것 같다. 창밖에서 무언가 퍼덕이는 소리가 들려도 개의치 않을 것이다. 아, 요즈음 나는 잠과 그토록 자주 끔찍한 싸움을 벌여왔다. 잠 못 이루는 고통이거나, 잠이 두려운 고통이거나, 잠 속에 나를 기다리는 그 알 수 없는 공포라니! 어떤 사람들은 얼마나 복된가. 그들의 삶에는 두려움도, 무서움도 없으니. 그들에게 잠은 밤마다 찾아오는 축복이요, 달콤한 꿈밖에는 가져다주지 않으니. 그래, 오늘 밤 나는 여기에서 잠을 기다리며, 연극 속의 오필리아처럼 \"처녀의 화관과 처녀의 헌화\"에 둘러싸여 누워 있다. 나는 전에는 마늘을 결코 좋아하지 않았으나, 오늘 밤은 그것이 즐겁기만 하다! 그 냄새에는 평화가 깃들어 있다. 벌써 잠이 오는 것이 느껴진다. 모두들, 안녕히.\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n9월 13일. — 버클리 호텔에 들렀더니 판 헬싱이 여느 때처럼 시간을 정확히 지켜 와 있었다. 호텔에서 부른 마차가 기다리고 있었다. 교수는 요즘 늘 가지고 다니는 가방을 챙겼다.\n\n모든 것을 정확히 기록해 두겠다. 판 헬싱과 나는 여덟 시에 힐링엄에 도착했다. 화창한 아침이었다. 밝은 햇살과 초가을의 상쾌한 기운이 마치 자연의 한 해 작업이 완성되는 것 같았다. 나뭇잎들은 온갖 아름다운 빛깔로 물들고 있었으나, 아직 나무에서 떨어지기 시작하지는 않았다. 우리가 들어서자 아침 거실에서 나오는 웨스튼라 부인과 마주쳤다. 그녀는 늘 일찍 일어나는 사람이다. 그녀는 우리를 따뜻하게 맞으며 말했다.\n\n\"루시가 좋아졌다는 걸 알면 기뻐하시겠지요. 그 사랑스러운 아이가 아직 자고 있답니다. 방을 들여다보고 그 애를 보았지만, 깨울까 봐 들어가지는 않았어요.\" 교수는 미소를 지으며 무척 기뻐하는 표정이었다. 그는 두 손을 비비며 말했다.\n\n\"아하! 내가 병세를 제대로 진단했다고 생각했지요. 내 치료가 듣고 있군요.\" 그러자 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"공을 모두 박사님께만 돌리시면 안 됩니다. 오늘 아침 루시의 상태는 부분적으로 제 덕분이기도 하니까요.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지요, 부인?\" 교수가 물었다.\n\n\"실은 밤중에 그 사랑스러운 아이가 걱정되어 방에 들어가 보았답니다. 그 애는 깊이 자고 있었어요 — 어찌나 깊이 자던지 내가 들어가도 깨지 않더군요. 그런데 방 안이 끔찍하게 답답했어요. 그 지독하고 냄새가 강한 꽃들이 사방에 잔뜩 놓여 있었고, 게다가 그 애는 목에 그 꽃을 한 다발이나 두르고 있더군요. 나는 그 무거운 냄새가 허약한 그 애에게 너무 부담스러울까 봐, 모두 치워버리고 신선한 공기가 조금 들어오도록 창문을 약간 열어놓았어요. 분명 그 애를 보면 만족하실 거예요.\"\n\n그녀는 평소 일찍 아침을 들던 자신의 내실로 자리를 옮겨갔다. 그녀가 말하는 동안 나는 교수의 얼굴을 지켜보았는데, 그 얼굴이 잿빛으로 변하는 것을 보았다. 가엾은 부인이 곁에 있는 동안에는 그는 자제력을 유지할 수 있었으니, 그녀의 상태를 알았고 충격이 얼마나 해로울지 알았던 까닭이다. 그는 부인이 방으로 들어가도록 문을 잡아주면서 실제로 그녀에게 미소까지 지었다. 그러나 그녀가 사라지자마자 그는 갑작스레, 그리고 거칠게 나를 식당으로 끌어들이고 문을 닫았다.\n\n그때 나는 난생처음으로 판 헬싱이 무너지는 모습을 보았다. 그는 일종의 말 없는 절망 속에 두 손을 머리 위로 치켜들었다가, 이내 어찌할 바를 모르겠다는 듯 두 손바닥을 마주쳐댔다. 마침내 그는 의자에 주저앉아 두 손으로 얼굴을 감싸고는, 마치 그의 심장을 쥐어짜는 데서 솟아나오는 듯한 크고 마른 흐느낌으로 오열하기 시작했다. 그러더니 그는 마치 온 우주에 호소하듯 다시 두 팔을 치켜들었다. \"하느님! 하느님! 하느님!\" 그가 말했다. \"우리가 무엇을 했기에, 이 가엾은 것이 무엇을 했기에 우리가 이토록 시달려야 한단 말이오? 그런 일들이, 그것도 그런 식으로 일어나야만 하다니, 옛 이교의 세계에서 내려온 운명이 아직도 우리 가운데 도사리고 있단 말이오? 이 가엾은 어머니는 아무것도 모른 채, 그리고 자기 딴에는 가장 잘하는 일이라 여기며, 제 딸의 몸과 영혼을 잃게 하는 그런 일을 저질렀소. 그런데도 우리는 그녀에게 말해서는 안 되고, 경고조차 해서는 안 되오. 그렇지 않으면 그녀가 죽고, 그러면 둘 다 죽을 테니. 아, 우리가 어찌 이리 시달리는가! 악마의 온갖 힘이 어찌 이리 우리를 거스르는가!\" 갑자기 그가 벌떡 일어섰다. \"갑시다.\" 그가 말했다. \"갑시다. 우리는 살펴보고 움직여야 하오. 악마든 악마가 아니든, 아니 모든 악마가 한꺼번에 덤벼든다 해도 상관없소. 우리는 그래도 그와 싸우는 거요.\" 그는 가방을 가지러 현관문 쪽으로 갔고, 우리는 함께 루시의 방으로 올라갔다.\n\n나는 다시 한 번 블라인드를 걷어 올렸고, 그동안 판 헬싱은 침대로 다가갔다. 이번에는 그가 전과 똑같이 끔찍하게 밀랍 같은 창백함을 띤 가엾은 얼굴을 들여다보면서도 흠칫 놀라지 않았다. 그는 준엄한 슬픔과 한없는 연민이 어린 표정을 짓고 있었다.\n\n\"내가 예상했던 대로군.\" 그가 그 많은 것을 의미하던 쉭쉭거리는 숨소리로 중얼거렸다. 그는 한마디 말도 없이 가서 문을 잠그더니, 작은 탁자 위에 또 한 번의 수혈 수술을 위한 기구들을 늘어놓기 시작했다. 나는 이미 오래전에 그 필요를 알아차리고 외투를 벗기 시작했으나, 그가 경고하듯 손을 들어 나를 막았다. \"안 돼요!\" 그가 말했다. \"오늘은 당신이 집도해야 하오. 피는 내가 댈 테니. 당신은 이미 쇠약해져 있소.\" 그렇게 말하면서 그는 외투를 벗고 셔츠 소매를 걷어올렸다.\n\n다시 수술, 다시 마취제, 다시 잿빛 뺨에 약간의 혈색이 돌아오고, 건강한 잠의 고른 숨결이 돌아왔다. 이번에는 판 헬싱이 기력을 회복하고 쉬는 동안 내가 지켜보았다.\n\n이윽고 그는 기회를 보아 웨스튼라 부인에게, 자기와 상의하지 않고는 루시의 방에서 아무것도 치워서는 안 된다고, 그 꽃들은 약효가 있는 것이며 그 냄새를 들이마시는 것이 치료법의 일부라고 일러두었다. 그러고 나서 그는 오늘 밤과 내일 밤 자신이 지킬 것이며, 내가 와야 할 때 기별을 보내겠다고 말하며 이 환자의 간호를 직접 맡았다.\n\n다시 한 시간이 지나자 루시는 잠에서 깨어났는데, 생기 있고 밝아 보였으며, 그 끔찍한 시련을 겪고도 그다지 나빠진 것 같지 않았다.\n\n이 모든 것이 무슨 뜻일까? 정신병자들 사이에서 오래 살아온 내 습성이 나 자신의 머리에까지 영향을 미치기 시작한 것은 아닐까 하는 생각이 들기 시작한다.\n\n「루시 웨스튼라의 일기」\n\n9월 17일. — 나흘 밤낮의 평온. 나는 다시 어찌나 기운이 나는지 나 자신을 거의 알아보지 못할 지경이다. 마치 어떤 길고 긴 악몽을 지나, 막 깨어나 아름다운 햇살을 보고 아침의 신선한 공기를 내 주위에서 느끼는 것만 같다. 길고 불안한 기다림과 두려움의 시간들에 대한, 희미한 반쪽짜리 기억이 남아 있다. 현재의 고통을 더 사무치게 할 희망의 아픔조차 없던 어둠. 그러고는 긴 망각의 시간들, 그리고 마치 잠수부가 거대한 물의 압력을 뚫고 솟아오르듯 다시 삶으로 떠오르던 것. 그러나 판 헬싱 박사가 곁에 있어준 뒤로는 이 모든 나쁜 꿈이 사라진 듯하다. 나를 까무러칠 만큼 겁주던 그 소리들 — 창문을 두드리는 퍼덕임, 그토록 가깝게 들리던 먼 목소리들, 어디서 오는지 모를, 무엇을 하라는 것인지도 모를 명령을 내리던 거친 소리들 — 이 모두 그쳤다. 이제 나는 잠에 대한 아무런 두려움 없이 잠자리에 든다. 깨어 있으려 애쓰지조차 않는다. 나는 마늘이 제법 좋아졌고, 하를럼에서 날마다 한 상자씩 나에게 도착한다. 오늘 밤 판 헬싱 박사는 떠난다. 암스테르담에서 하루를 보내야 하기 때문이다. 그러나 내게는 지켜보는 이가 필요 없다. 혼자 있어도 될 만큼 충분히 나아졌으니까. 어머니를 위해서, 그리고 사랑하는 아서를 위해서, 또 그토록 친절히 대해준 우리 모든 벗들을 위해서, 하느님께 감사드린다! 변화조차 느끼지 못할 것이다. 지난밤 판 헬싱 박사는 의자에 앉은 채로 많은 시간을 잤으니까. 깨어보니 그가 잠들어 있는 것을 두 번이나 보았다. 그러나 나뭇가지인지 박쥐인지 무언가가 거의 성난 듯이 창유리를 두드려댔는데도, 나는 다시 잠드는 것이 두렵지 않았다.\n\n「폴 몰 가제트」, 9월 18일\n탈출한 늑대.\n\n본지 취재기자의 위험천만한 모험.\n동물원 사육사와의 인터뷰.\n\n여러 차례 문의하고 거의 그만큼 많은 거절을 당한 끝에, 그리고 \"폴 몰 가제트\"라는 말을 일종의 부적처럼 끊임없이 입에 올린 끝에, 나는 동물원에서 늑대 부서가 포함된 구역의 사육사를 가까스로 찾아낼 수 있었다. 토머스 빌더는 코끼리 사육장 뒤편 울타리 안의 오두막 가운데 하나에 사는데, 내가 그를 찾아냈을 때 그는 막 차를 마시려고 자리에 앉던 참이었다. 토머스와 그의 아내는 손님 접대를 좋아하는 사람들로, 나이 지긋하고 자식이 없는데, 내가 누린 그들의 환대가 평균치라면 그들의 삶은 꽤나 편안할 것임이 틀림없다. 사육사는 저녁식사가 끝나고 우리 모두가 만족할 때까지는 자신이 \"일 이야기\"라 부르는 것을 시작하려 하지 않았다. 그러더니 식탁이 치워지고 그가 파이프에 불을 붙이고 나서 말했다.\n\n\"자, 선생, 이제 묻고 싶은 걸 물어보슈. 식사 전에 직업 이야기를 마다한 건 양해해 주슈. 우리 구역의 늑대며 자칼이며 하이에나한테 차를 먹이고 나서야 질문을 시작하니까 말이오.\"\n\n\"질문을 시작한다는 게 무슨 뜻입니까?\" 그를 수다스러운 기분으로 끌어들이고 싶어서 내가 물었다.\n\n\"막대기로 대가리를 후려치는 게 한 방법이고, 귀를 긁어주는 게 또 한 방법이지. 돈푼깨나 있는 양반들이 제 계집한테 으스대고 싶을 때 말이오. 첫 번째 거야 나도 별로 꺼리지 않소 — 먹이를 던져주기 전에 막대기로 후려치는 거 말이오. 하지만 말하자면 셰리주와 커피를 들고 나서야 귀 긁어주기를 시도하지요. 명심하슈,\" 그가 철학자처럼 덧붙였다. \"저 짐승들이나 우리나 같은 천성이 다분히 있소. 여기 선생이 와서 내 일에 관해 이것저것 묻고 있는데, 나는 어찌나 심통이 나던지 그 빌어먹을 반 파운드짜리만 아니었으면 대답하느니 차라리 선생이 뒈지는 꼴을 봤을 거요. 선생이 비꼬는 투로, 내가 선생한테 질문해도 되는지 관리소장한테 물어봐 달라느냐고 했을 때조차도 말이오. 내가 기분 나쁘게 굴 것도 없이 지옥에나 가라고 했지 않소?\"\n\n\"그랬지요.\"\n\n\"그리고 선생이 내가 상스러운 말을 쓴다고 신고하겠다고 했을 때, 그게 내 대가리를 후려치는 거였소. 하지만 그 반 파운드짜리가 다 풀어줬지요. 나는 싸울 생각이 없었으니, 먹이가 나오길 기다렸다가 늑대며 사자며 호랑이가 하는 대로 으르렁대기만 했소. 하지만 이런, 선생 양반, 이제 늙은 마누라가 차 케이크 한 조각을 내 입에 쑤셔넣어 주고, 그 빌어먹을 낡은 찻주전자로 속을 씻어내려 주고, 내가 담뱃불도 붙였으니, 이제 마음껏 내 귀를 긁어보슈. 으르렁거리는 소리 하나 안 나올 테니. 어서 질문이나 하슈. 선생이 뭘 노리는지 알지요. 바로 그 탈출한 늑대 말이오.\"\n\n\"바로 그겁니다. 그것에 대한 당신의 견해를 듣고 싶습니다. 어떻게 일어난 일인지 말씀해 주십시오. 그 사실들을 알고 나면, 그 원인이 무엇이라고 생각하시는지, 그리고 이 사건 전체가 어떻게 끝날 거라고 보시는지 말씀해 주시기 바랍니다.\"\n\n\"좋소, 나리. 자초지종은 대충 이렇소. 우리가 버시커라고 부르던 그 늑대는 노르웨이에서 잠라크네 가게로 온 회색 늑대 세 마리 가운데 하나로, 사 년 전에 그자한테서 사들인 거요. 점잖고 행실 바른 늑대로, 말썽이랄 만한 걸 부린 적이 없소. 이 안의 어떤 짐승보다도 그놈이 밖으로 나가고 싶어 했다는 게 더 놀랍단 말이오. 하지만, 어쨌든, 늑대란 건 계집이나 마찬가지로 믿을 게 못 되지요.\"\n\n\"저이 말은 신경 쓰지 마슈, 선생!\" 톰 부인이 명랑하게 웃으며 끼어들었다. \"저이가 짐승들을 하도 오래 돌보다 보니 정말이지 저 자신이 늙은 늑대 같다니까요! 그래도 악의는 없는 사람이에요.\"\n\n\"자, 선생, 어제 먹이를 주고 나서 두 시간쯤 됐을 때였소, 처음 소란을 들은 게. 나는 병든 어린 퓨마한테 깔아줄 짚을 원숭이 사육장에 마련하던 참이었소. 그런데 깽깽대고 울부짖는 소리가 들리길래 곧장 달려왔지요. 거기 버시커가 미친 것처럼 창살을 물어뜯고 있었소, 마치 밖으로 나가고 싶어 안달이라도 난 듯이 말이오. 그날은 사람이 별로 없었고, 가까이엔 딱 한 사람, 키 크고 마른 친구가 있었는데, 매부리코에 끝이 뾰족한 턱수염을 길렀고 그 사이로 흰 털이 몇 가닥 섞여 있었소. 그자는 굳고 차가운 눈빛에 붉은 눈을 하고 있었는데, 나는 어쩐지 그자가 싫었소. 짐승들이 화가 난 게 바로 그자 때문인 것 같았으니까. 그자는 손에 흰 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 끼고 있었는데, 나한테 짐승들을 가리키며 이러는 거요. '사육사 양반, 이 늑대들이 뭔가에 화가 난 모양이오.'\n\n\"'아마 당신 때문이겠지요,' 하고 내가 말했소. 그자가 으스대는 꼴이 마음에 안 들었거든. 그자는 내가 바라던 대로 화를 내진 않고, 일종의 거만한 미소를 지었는데, 입안에 희고 날카로운 이가 가득했소. '오, 아니오, 저들이 나를 좋아할 리가 없지,' 하고 그자가 말했소.\n\n\"'아니, 좋아하고말고요,' 하고 내가 그자를 흉내 내며 말했소. '저놈들은 차 마실 때쯤이면 이를 닦을 뼈다귀 한두 개를 늘 좋아하는데, 당신은 한 자루 가득 가졌으니 말이오.'\n\n\"그런데, 참 이상한 일이었소만, 짐승들이 우리가 이야기하는 걸 보더니 드러눕는 거요. 그리고 내가 버시커한테 다가가니 그놈이 여느 때처럼 제 귀를 쓰다듬게 두는 거요. 그 사람이 다가오더니, 세상에, 그자도 손을 뻗어 그 늙은 늑대의 귀를 쓰다듬지 뭐요!\n\n\"'조심하슈,' 하고 내가 말했소. '버시커는 날래니까.'\n\n\"'괜찮소,' 하고 그자가 말했소. '나는 이놈들에 익숙하니까!'\n\n\"'당신도 이 일을 하시오?' 하고 내가 모자를 벗으며 물었소. 늑대나 뭐 그런 걸 거래하는 사람은 사육사한테 좋은 친구니까 말이오.\n\n\"'아니오,' 하고 그자가 말했소. '꼭 이 일을 하는 건 아니지만, 여러 마리를 애완으로 길러봤소.' 그러고는 영주처럼 정중하게 모자를 들어 올리고 걸어가 버렸소. 늙은 버시커는 그자가 보이지 않을 때까지 줄곧 그자를 바라보더니, 가서 구석에 드러누워 저녁 내내 나오려 하질 않았소. 자, 어젯밤, 달이 떠오르자마자 여기 늑대들이 모두 울부짖기 시작했소. 울부짖을 만한 게 아무것도 없었는데 말이오. 근처엔 아무도 없었소, 공원 도로 쪽 정원 뒤편 어딘가에서 분명히 개를 부르던 누군가 말고는. 한두 번 나가서 다 괜찮은지 살펴봤는데, 괜찮았고, 그러더니 울부짖음이 그쳤소. 자정이 되기 직전에 나는 잠자리에 들기 전에 한 바퀴 둘러봤는데, 이런 빌어먹을, 늙은 버시커 우리 맞은편에 갔더니 창살이 부러지고 비틀려 있고 우리가 비어 있는 거요. 그게 내가 확실히 아는 전부요.\"\n\n\"다른 누군가가 무언가를 본 사람은 없습니까?\"\n\n\"우리 정원사 하나가 그 무렵에 음악회에서 집으로 돌아오던 길에, 큰 회색 개가 정원 울타리를 뚫고 나오는 걸 봤다는 거요. 적어도 그자 말은 그런데, 나 자신은 별로 믿질 않소. 그자가 정말 봤다면 집에 돌아가서 제 마누라한테 한마디도 안 했을 리가 없고, 늑대가 탈출한 게 알려진 뒤에야, 그것도 우리가 밤새도록 공원에서 버시커를 찾아 헤맨 뒤에야 비로소 뭔가를 봤던 게 기억났다니 말이오. 내 생각엔 그 음악회가 그자 머릿속에 들어앉은 거지요.\"\n\n\"자, 빌더 씨, 그 늑대가 탈출한 것을 어떻게든 설명해 주실 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 선생,\" 그가 수상쩍은 겸손을 보이며 말했다. \"할 수 있을 것 같소만, 선생이 그 이론에 만족하실지는 모르겠소.\"\n\n\"분명 만족할 겁니다. 당신처럼 경험으로 그 짐승들을 아는 사람이 그래도 그럴듯한 추측 하나 내놓지 못한다면, 대체 누가 시도라도 해볼 수 있겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그럼, 선생, 나는 이렇게 설명하겠소. 내가 보기엔 그 늑대가 탈출한 건 — 그저 밖으로 나가고 싶어서였소.\"\n\n토머스와 그의 아내가 둘 다 이 농담에 어찌나 호탕하게 웃던지, 나는 그것이 전에도 써먹은 적이 있는 농담이며, 그 설명 전체가 그저 공들인 익살임을 알 수 있었다. 나는 이 훌륭한 토머스와 익살을 겨룰 재간은 없었으나, 그의 마음에 이르는 더 확실한 길은 안다고 여겼으므로, 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"자, 빌더 씨, 그 첫 번째 반 파운드짜리는 다 치른 걸로 하고, 여기 그 형제뻘 되는 또 하나가 당신이 앞으로 어떻게 될 거라고 생각하는지 말해 주면 주인을 기다리고 있습니다.\"\n\n\"옳소, 선생,\" 그가 기운차게 말했다. \"선생을 좀 놀린 건 양해해 줄 줄 아오. 하지만 여기 늙은 마누라가 나한테 윙크를 했거든, 계속하라는 거나 마찬가지였단 말이오.\"\n\n\"세상에, 원!\" 노부인이 말했다.\n\n\"내 의견은 이렇소. 그 늑대는 어딘가에 숨어 있소. 기억 못 하던 그 정원사는 늑대가 말보다 빠르게 북쪽으로 내달리더라고 했지만, 나는 그자를 믿지 않소. 왜냐면 선생, 늑대는 개보다 더 잘 내달리지 못하거든, 그렇게 생겨먹질 않았으니까. 늑대는 이야기책에서나 멋진 놈이고, 떼를 지어서 저들보다 더 겁먹은 무언가를 쫓을 때야 지독한 소리를 내며 그게 뭐든 물어뜯어 죽일 수도 있겠지요. 하지만, 이런, 선생, 실제로 늑대란 건 그저 비루한 짐승이라, 좋은 개보다 절반만큼도 영리하거나 대담하지 못하고, 싸움 기질은 그 사분의 일도 안 되오. 이놈은 싸워본 적도, 제 먹이를 마련해 본 적도 없으니, 십중팔구 공원 어딘가에 숨어서 벌벌 떨고 있을 거요. 그리고 생각이란 걸 한다면, 아침거리를 어디서 구할까 궁리하고 있겠지요. 아니면 어느 지하실 입구로 내려가 석탄 창고에 들어가 있을지도 모르오. 이런, 어느 요리사가 어둠 속에서 그놈의 초록빛 눈이 제 쪽으로 번득이는 걸 보면 얼마나 기겁을 할까! 먹이를 못 구하면 찾아 나설 수밖에 없을 테고, 어쩌면 운 좋게 때맞춰 정육점을 만날지도 모르지요. 그렇지 못하고, 어느 유모가 군인이랑 산책하러 나가면서 유아차에 아기를 두고 가버린다면 — 글쎄, 그때는 인구 조사에서 아기 하나가 줄어든다 해도 나는 놀라지 않을 거요. 그게 전부요.\"\n\n내가 그에게 반 파운드짜리를 건네던 참에, 무언가가 창문에 불쑥 부딪쳐 올랐고, 빌더 씨의 얼굴이 놀라서 본래 길이의 두 배로 늘어났다.\n\n\"어이쿠, 맙소사!\" 그가 말했다. \"저기 늙은 버시커가 제 발로 돌아온 거 아니오!\"\n\n그는 문으로 가서 그것을 열었다. 내가 보기엔 더없이 불필요한 행동이었다. 나는 야생 동물이란 우리 사이에 확실히 견고한 장애물이 있을 때만큼 보기 좋은 적이 없다고 늘 생각해 왔는데, 한 차례의 개인적 경험이 그 생각을 누그러뜨리기는커녕 오히려 굳혀 놓았다.\n\n그러나 어쨌든 습관만 한 것은 없으니, 빌더도 그의 아내도 그 늑대를 두고 내가 개를 두고 그러는 것 이상으로는 마음 쓰지 않았다. 짐승 자체도, 모든 그림책 늑대의 시조 — 빨간 두건의 한때의 친구가 변장한 채 그녀의 신뢰를 흔들 때만큼이나 온순하고 행실이 발랐다.\n\n이 광경 전체는 이루 말할 수 없는 희극과 비애의 혼합이었다. 반나절 동안 런던을 마비시키고 온 시내 아이들을 신발 속에서 벌벌 떨게 했던 그 사악한 늑대가, 거기 일종의 뉘우치는 기색으로 있었고, 마치 늑대판 탕자라도 되는 양 환영받고 귀여움을 받았다. 늙은 빌더는 더없이 다정한 염려로 그놈을 구석구석 살폈고, 그 뉘우치는 놈을 다 살피고 나서 말했다.\n\n\"보슈, 이 가엾은 늙은 녀석이 무슨 곤경에든 빠질 줄 내 알았다니까. 내가 줄곧 그러지 않았소? 여기 그놈 머리가 온통 베이고 깨진 유리투성이요. 어디 빌어먹을 담장이라도 넘어온 게로군. 사람들이 담장 꼭대기에 깨진 병을 박아놓도록 허락하는 건 부끄러운 일이오. 이게 바로 그 결과란 말이오. 이리 오너라, 버시커.\"\n\n그는 늑대를 데려가 우리에 가두고, 적어도 양에서만큼은 살진 송아지의 기본 조건은 충족시킬 만한 고기 한 점을 던져준 다음, 보고하러 떠났다.\n\n나 역시 오늘 동물원의 이 기이한 소동에 관해 단독으로 주어진 유일한 정보를 보도하러 나왔다.\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n9월 17일. — 나는 저녁식사 후 서재에서 장부를 정리하느라 분주했는데, 다른 일이 밀리고 루시를 여러 번 찾아간 탓에 장부가 한심하게 밀려 있었던 것이다. 갑자기 문이 벌컥 열리더니, 내 환자가 격정으로 일그러진 얼굴을 하고 뛰어들어왔다. 나는 아연실색했으니, 환자가 제 발로 관리소장의 서재에 들어오는 일이란 거의 없기 때문이다. 그는 한순간도 멈추지 않고 곧장 나에게 달려들었다. 그는 손에 만찬용 식칼을 들고 있었는데, 그가 위험하다는 것을 알아챈 나는 우리 사이에 탁자를 두려고 애썼다. 그러나 그는 나에게는 너무 빠르고 너무 힘이 셌다. 내가 균형을 잡기도 전에 그가 나를 내리쳐 내 왼쪽 손목을 제법 심하게 베었기 때문이다. 그러나 그가 다시 내리치기 전에 내가 오른손을 먹였고, 그는 바닥에 벌렁 나동그라졌다. 내 손목은 줄줄 피를 흘려, 작은 웅덩이가 카펫 위로 흘러내렸다. 나는 내 친구가 더는 시도할 작정이 아님을 알아채고는, 줄곧 엎드린 그 형체에서 경계의 눈을 떼지 않은 채 내 손목을 동여매는 데 골몰했다. 간병인들이 뛰어들어오고 우리가 그에게 주의를 돌렸을 때, 그가 하고 있던 짓은 정말이지 나를 역겹게 했다. 그는 바닥에 배를 깔고 엎드려, 개처럼, 내 다친 손목에서 떨어진 피를 핥아먹고 있었다. 그는 쉽게 제압되었고, 놀랍게도 간병인들을 따라 아주 얌전히 갔는데, 다만 거듭거듭 이렇게 되뇌었다. \"피는 생명이다! 피는 생명이다!\"\n\n나는 지금 당장은 피를 잃을 처지가 못 된다. 요즈음 내 건강에 해로울 만큼 너무 많은 피를 잃었고, 게다가 루시의 병과 그 끔찍한 국면들에서 오는 오랜 긴장이 나를 짓누르고 있다. 나는 과도하게 흥분했고 지쳐 있으니, 휴식이, 휴식이, 휴식이 필요하다. 다행히 판 헬싱이 나를 부르지 않았으니, 나는 잠을 포기하지 않아도 된다. 오늘 밤은 도저히 잠 없이는 견딜 수 없을 것 같다.\n\n「전보, 앤트워프의 판 헬싱이 카팩스의 수어드에게.」\n\n(주(州)가 명시되지 않은 탓에 서식스의 카팩스로 발송되어, 스물두 시간이나 늦게 배달됨.)\n\n\"9월 17일. — 오늘 밤 반드시 힐링엄에 있을 것. 줄곧 지켜보지 못한다면 자주 들러서 꽃들이 놓인 그대로 있는지 확인할 것. 매우 중요함. 결코 빠뜨리지 말 것. 도착하는 대로 되도록 빨리 당신에게 가겠음.\"\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n9월 18일. — 막 런던행 기차를 타러 떠나는 길이다. 판 헬싱의 전보가 도착해 나는 경악에 휩싸였다. 온전한 하룻밤을 잃었으니, 하룻밤 사이에 무슨 일이 일어날 수 있는지 나는 쓰라린 경험으로 안다. 물론 모든 것이 무사할 가능성도 있지만, 그동안 무슨 일이 일어났을지도 모른다. 분명 어떤 끔찍한 운명이 우리 위에 드리워져 있어, 우리가 하려는 모든 일을 온갖 가능한 우연이 가로막는 것이리라. 나는 이 실린더를 가지고 가서, 루시의 축음기에 내 기록을 마저 마칠 수 있을 것이다.\n\n「루시 웨스튼라가 남긴 비망록.」\n\n9월 17일. 밤. — 나는 이것을 써서, 누구든 보도록 남겨둔다. 그래야 그 누구도 어쩌다 나 때문에 곤경에 빠지는 일이 없을 테니. 이것은 오늘 밤 일어난 일에 대한 정확한 기록이다. 나는 쇠약함으로 죽어가는 것이 느껴지고, 글을 쓸 기력조차 거의 없으나, 쓰다가 죽는 한이 있어도 이것만은 해야만 한다.\n\n나는 여느 때처럼 잠자리에 들었는데, 판 헬싱 박사가 지시한 대로 꽃들이 놓여 있도록 신경을 쓰고 나서 곧 잠이 들었다.\n\n나는 창문을 두드리는 퍼덕임에 잠이 깼다. 그것은 휘트비의 절벽에서 그 몽유병으로 헤매다 미나가 나를 구해준 이후에 시작된 것으로, 이제 나는 그 소리를 아주 잘 안다. 나는 두렵지는 않았으나, 판 헬싱 박사가 그러겠다고 했던 대로 수어드 박사가 옆방에 있어서 그를 부를 수 있으면 좋겠다고 바랐다. 나는 잠들려 애썼지만 그럴 수 없었다. 그러자 잠에 대한 그 오랜 두려움이 밀려와, 나는 깨어 있기로 마음먹었다. 짓궂게도 잠은 내가 원치 않을 때라야 찾아오려 했다. 그래서 나는 혼자 있는 게 두려워, 문을 열고 소리쳤다. \"거기 누구 없나요?\" 대답이 없었다. 나는 어머니를 깨우기가 두려워 다시 문을 닫았다. 그때 밖에서, 관목숲에서 개의 것 같으면서도 더 사납고 더 깊은 일종의 울부짖음이 들렸다. 나는 창가로 가서 밖을 내다보았으나, 분명 창문에 날개를 부딪쳐대던 큰 박쥐 한 마리 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 그래서 나는 다시 잠자리로 돌아왔으나, 잠들지 않기로 작정했다. 이윽고 문이 열리더니 어머니가 들여다보았다. 내가 움직이는 것을 보고 내가 자고 있지 않음을 알아채고는, 들어와서 내 곁에 앉았다. 어머니는 평소보다도 더 다정하고 부드럽게 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"네가 마음에 걸려서, 얘야, 네가 괜찮은지 보러 들어왔단다.\"\n\n나는 어머니가 거기 앉아 있다가 감기에 걸릴까 봐, 들어와서 나와 함께 자자고 청했다. 그래서 어머니는 침대로 들어와 내 곁에 누웠다. 어머니는 잠시만 있다가 자기 침대로 돌아갈 거라며 실내복을 벗지 않았다. 어머니가 내 품에, 내가 어머니 품에 안겨 누워 있는데, 그 퍼덕임과 부딪침이 다시 창문으로 찾아왔다. 어머니는 흠칫 놀라 조금 겁을 먹고 소리쳤다. \"저게 뭐냐?\" 나는 어머니를 달래려 애썼고, 마침내 성공하여 어머니는 잠잠히 누웠다. 그러나 나는 어머니의 가엾은 심장이 여전히 무섭게 뛰는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 잠시 후 관목숲에서 다시 그 나직한 울부짖음이 들렸고, 곧이어 창문에서 와장창 소리가 나며 깨진 유리가 잔뜩 바닥으로 쏟아졌다. 창문 블라인드가 밀려들어오는 바람에 뒤로 펄럭였고, 깨진 유리창의 구멍에는 거대하고 수척한 회색 늑대의 머리가 있었다. 어머니는 겁에 질려 소리치며 안간힘을 써서 일어나 앉으려 했고, 도움이 될 만한 것이라면 무엇이든 미친 듯이 움켜쥐려 했다. 그러다 다른 것들과 함께, 판 헬싱 박사가 내 목에 두르라고 고집했던 그 꽃 화환을 움켜쥐고는 나에게서 잡아 뜯어냈다. 한두 순간 어머니는 늑대를 가리키며 일어나 앉아 있었고, 어머니의 목에서는 기이하고 끔찍한 그르렁 소리가 났다. 그러더니 어머니는 마치 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 쓰러졌고, 어머니의 머리가 내 이마를 들이받아 한두 순간 나를 어지럽게 했다. 방과 그 주위 온 사방이 빙빙 도는 것 같았다. 나는 창문에 눈을 못박고 있었으나, 늑대는 머리를 빼냈고, 무수히 많은 작은 티끌들이 깨진 창문으로 불려 들어와, 마치 사막에 모래폭풍이 일 때 여행자들이 묘사하는 그 먼지 기둥처럼 빙글빙글 맴도는 것 같았다. 나는 몸을 움직이려 애썼으나, 무언가 주문에 걸린 듯했고, 사랑하는 어머니의 가엾은 몸이 — 이미 차갑게 식어가는 듯한, 그 다정한 심장이 멎어버린 그 몸이 — 나를 짓눌렀다. 그러고는 한동안 아무것도 기억나지 않았다.\n\n다시 의식을 되찾기까지 시간은 길게 느껴지지는 않았으나, 매우, 매우 끔찍했다. 어딘가 가까이에서 조종(弔鐘)이 울리고 있었고, 동네 사방의 개들이 모두 울부짖고 있었다. 그리고 우리 관목숲에서, 바로 바깥인 듯한 곳에서 나이팅게일 한 마리가 노래하고 있었다. 나는 고통과 공포와 쇠약함으로 멍하고 얼떨떨했으나, 그 나이팅게일의 소리는 마치 돌아가신 어머니가 나를 위로하러 돌아온 목소리 같았다. 그 소리는 하녀들도 깨운 모양이었으니, 그들의 맨발이 내 방 문밖에서 후닥닥거리는 소리가 들렸기 때문이다. 나는 그들을 불렀고, 그들이 들어왔다. 그리고 무슨 일이 일어났는지, 침대 위에서 나를 짓누르고 있는 것이 무엇인지를 보자, 그들은 비명을 질렀다. 깨진 창문으로 바람이 밀려들어왔고, 문이 쾅 닫혔다. 내가 일어나자 그들은 사랑하는 어머니의 시신을 들어 올려, 침대 위에 홑이불로 덮어 뉘었다. 그들이 모두 어찌나 겁먹고 신경이 곤두서 있던지, 나는 그들에게 식당으로 가서 각자 포도주를 한 잔씩 마시라고 일렀다. 문이 한순간 벌컥 열렸다가 다시 닫혔다. 하녀들은 비명을 지르더니, 무리지어 식당으로 갔다. 그리고 나는 가지고 있던 꽃들을 사랑하는 어머니의 가슴 위에 놓아드렸다. 그것들이 거기 놓이자 나는 판 헬싱 박사가 나에게 했던 말이 떠올랐으나, 그것들을 치우고 싶지 않았다. 게다가 이제 하인 몇몇에게 나와 함께 밤을 새우게 할 참이었으니까. 나는 하녀들이 돌아오지 않는 것이 의아했다. 그들을 불렀으나 대답이 없어서, 나는 그들을 찾으러 식당으로 갔다.\n\n무슨 일이 일어났는지 보았을 때 내 가슴은 철렁 내려앉았다. 그들 넷이 모두 바닥에 맥없이 쓰러져 거칠게 숨을 쉬고 있었다. 셰리주 유리병이 탁자 위에 반쯤 차 있었으나, 묘하고 매캐한 냄새가 감돌았다. 나는 의심이 들어 그 유리병을 살폈다. 거기서는 아편팅크 냄새가 났고, 찬장을 보니 어머니의 주치의가 어머니에게 — 오! 쓰던 — 아니, 썼던 그 병이 비어 있었다. 나는 어떻게 해야 하나? 어떻게 해야 하나? 나는 어머니가 있는 방으로 돌아와 있다. 나는 어머니 곁을 떠날 수 없고, 누군가 약을 먹여놓은 잠든 하인들 말고는 혼자다. 죽은 이와 단둘이! 나는 밖으로 나갈 엄두가 나지 않으니, 깨진 창문 너머로 늑대의 나직한 울부짖음이 들리기 때문이다.\n\n공기는 온통 티끌로 가득한 듯, 창문에서 들어오는 외풍에 떠다니며 맴돌고, 등불은 푸르게 흐릿하게 타오른다. 나는 어떻게 해야 하나? 하느님, 이 밤 저를 해악으로부터 지켜주소서! 나는 이 종이를 내 가슴속에 숨겨두겠다. 그들이 나를 염하러 올 때 거기서 이것을 발견하도록. 사랑하는 어머니가 가셨다! 이제 나도 갈 때다. 안녕히, 사랑하는 아서, 혹시 내가 이 밤을 넘기지 못한다면. 하느님이 당신을 지켜주시기를, 사랑하는 이여, 그리고 하느님이 나를 도와주시기를!\n\n# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY\n\n9월 18일. — 나는 곧장 마차를 몰아 힐링엄으로 가서 일찍 도착했다. 마차는 대문에 세워둔 채 나 혼자 길을 걸어 올라갔다. 루시나 그녀의 어머니를 깨울까 두려워, 그저 하인 하나가 문으로 나오기를 바라며 가만히 문을 두드리고 되도록 조용히 초인종을 울렸다. 한참이 지나도 아무 응답이 없기에 다시 두드리고 울렸으나, 여전히 대답이 없었다. 이런 시각에 — 그때는 벌써 열 시였다 — 자리에 누워 있다니, 나는 하인들의 게으름을 욕하면서 다시 더 조바심을 내며 울리고 두드렸으나, 그래도 응답이 없었다. 그때까지는 하인들만 탓했으나, 이제 끔찍한 두려움이 나를 엄습하기 시작했다. 이 적막은 우리를 점점 옥죄어오는 듯한 운명의 사슬에서 또 하나의 고리일 뿐인가? 내가 너무 늦게 찾아온, 정녕 죽음의 집이란 말인가? 만일 루시가 또 그 끔찍한 발작 가운데 하나를 일으켰다면, 몇 분, 아니 몇 초의 지체조차 그녀에게는 몇 시간의 위험을 뜻할 수 있음을 나는 알고 있었다. 그래서 나는 어디든 우연히라도 들어갈 곳이 있나 찾아보려고 집을 한 바퀴 돌았다.\n\n들어갈 방도를 찾을 수 없었다. 창문과 문은 모조리 걸쇠가 걸리고 잠겨 있었으므로, 나는 어쩔 도리 없이 현관으로 돌아왔다. 그렇게 돌아오는데, 빠르게 몰아치는 말발굽의 다급한 또각또각 소리가 들렸다. 그것은 대문에서 멈추었고, 몇 초 뒤 나는 길을 달려 올라오는 반 헬싱과 마주쳤다. 그는 나를 보자 숨을 헐떡이며 외쳤다.\n\n\"그럼 자네였군, 그것도 방금 도착한 거고. 그녀는 어떤가? 우리가 너무 늦은 건가? 내 전보를 받지 못했나?\"\n\n나는 되도록 빠르고 또박또박, 그의 전보를 오늘 아침 일찍에야 받았으며 이리로 오는 데 한순간도 허비하지 않았노라고, 그리고 집 안의 누구도 내 기척을 듣게 할 수 없었노라고 대답했다. 그는 잠시 멈춰 서서 모자를 들어 올리며 엄숙히 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 우리가 너무 늦은 모양일세. 신의 뜻이 이루어지기를!\" 그러고는 늘 그렇듯 다시 기운을 되찾으며 말을 이었다. \"자, 들어갈 길이 트여 있지 않다면, 우리가 하나 뚫어야지. 지금 우리에게는 시간이 전부일세.\"\n\n우리는 집 뒤편으로 돌아갔는데, 거기에 부엌 창문이 하나 있었다. 교수는 자기 가방에서 작은 외과용 톱을 꺼내 나에게 건네주며, 창문을 막고 있는 쇠창살을 가리켰다. 나는 곧장 거기에 달려들어 이내 세 개를 끊어냈다. 그런 다음 길고 가느다란 칼로 창틀의 걸쇠를 밀어 젖히고 창문을 열었다. 나는 교수가 들어가는 것을 돕고 나서 그를 따라 들어갔다. 부엌에도, 바로 곁에 붙은 하인 방에도 아무도 없었다. 우리는 지나가는 길에 방마다 다 열어보았는데, 덧문 틈으로 새어드는 빛에 어슴푸레한 식당에서, 하녀 넷이 바닥에 쓰러져 있는 것을 발견했다. 그들을 죽었다고 여길 필요는 없었으니, 그르렁대는 숨소리와 방 안에 진동하는 아편 팅크의 매캐한 냄새가 그들의 상태에 대해 한 점 의심도 남기지 않았던 까닭이다. 반 헬싱과 나는 서로를 마주보았고, 자리를 뜨면서 그가 말했다. \"저들은 나중에 돌볼 수 있네.\" 그러고 나서 우리는 루시의 방으로 올라갔다. 우리는 한두 순간 문 앞에 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였으나, 들리는 소리는 아무것도 없었다. 핏기 잃은 얼굴과 떨리는 손으로 우리는 가만히 문을 열고 방 안에 들어섰다.\n\n우리가 본 것을 어떻게 그려낼 수 있을까? 침대에는 두 여인이 누워 있었으니, 루시와 그녀의 어머니였다. 어머니가 가장 안쪽에 누워 있었고, 흰 시트를 덮고 있었는데, 그 가장자리가 깨진 창문으로 들이친 바람에 젖혀져, 공포의 빛이 못 박힌 듯 굳어버린 핼쑥한 흰 얼굴이 드러나 있었다. 그 곁에는 루시가 누워 있었으니, 얼굴은 핏기가 없고 더욱더 핼쑥했다. 그녀의 목에 둘러져 있던 꽃들은 어머니의 가슴 위에서 발견되었고, 그녀의 목은 드러나 있어 우리가 전에 눈여겨본 그 작은 상처 두 개를 보여주었으나, 끔찍하리만치 희고 너덜너덜해 보였다. 교수는 말없이 침대 위로 몸을 숙여, 그의 머리가 가엾은 루시의 가슴에 거의 닿을 듯했다. 그러더니 무언가에 귀 기울이는 사람처럼 재빨리 고개를 돌렸고, 벌떡 몸을 일으키며 나에게 외쳤다.\n\n\"아직 너무 늦지는 않았네! 빨리! 빨리! 브랜디를 가져오게!\"\n\n나는 아래층으로 날듯이 내려가 그것을 들고 돌아왔는데, 탁자 위에서 발견한 셰리주 병처럼 이것에도 약이 타져 있을까 봐 냄새를 맡고 맛을 보는 일을 잊지 않았다. 하녀들은 여전히 숨을 쉬고 있었으나 좀 더 뒤척였고, 나는 마취제 기운이 가시고 있는 것이라 짐작했다. 나는 확인하느라 머물지 않고 반 헬싱에게 돌아갔다. 그는 지난번에 했던 것처럼 그녀의 입술과 잇몸에, 그리고 손목과 손바닥에 브랜디를 문질렀다. 그는 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"이건 내가 하겠네, 지금으로선 할 수 있는 전부지. 자넨 가서 저 하녀들을 깨우게. 젖은 수건으로 얼굴을 후려치게, 그것도 세게. 저들에게 열을 내고 불을 지펴 따뜻한 목욕물을 마련하게 하게. 이 가엾은 사람이 곁에 누운 저 사람만큼이나 차네. 우리가 더 손을 쓰기 전에 우선 몸을 데워야 할 걸세.\"\n\n나는 곧장 가서, 별 어려움 없이 여인 셋을 깨웠다. 넷째는 그저 어린 처녀였는데, 약기운이 그녀에게는 더 세게 들었던 게 분명하여, 나는 그녀를 소파 위로 들어 올려 잠을 자게 두었다. 다른 이들은 처음에는 멍해 있었으나, 기억이 되살아나면서 발작하듯 울고 흐느꼈다. 그러나 나는 그들에게 단호히 대하여 말을 하게 두지 않았다. 나는 한 목숨을 잃는 것만으로도 충분히 끔찍한데, 그들이 꾸물거리면 루시 아가씨를 희생시키게 될 거라고 일렀다. 그리하여 그들은 흐느끼고 울면서, 반쯤 걸친 옷차림 그대로 제 할 일을 하러 가, 불을 지피고 물을 데웠다. 다행히 부엌과 보일러의 불은 아직 살아 있었고, 더운물도 모자라지 않았다. 우리는 욕조를 마련해 루시를 있는 그대로 안아 내려다 그 안에 뉘었다. 우리가 그녀의 팔다리를 문질러 데우느라 분주할 때, 현관문에서 노크 소리가 났다. 하녀 하나가 달려 나가, 옷을 좀 더 걸치고는 문을 열었다. 그러고는 돌아와, 홈우드 씨가 전한 말을 가지고 온 신사가 있다고 우리에게 속삭였다. 나는 그저, 지금은 아무도 만날 수 없으니 기다려야 한다고 그에게 전하라고 일렀다. 그녀는 그 말을 들고 갔고, 나는 우리 일에 골몰하느라 그에 대해서는 깨끗이 잊어버렸다.\n\n내 모든 경험을 통틀어, 나는 교수가 그토록 필사적으로 진지하게 일하는 것을 본 적이 없었다. 나는 — 그도 알았듯이 — 그것이 죽음과 벌이는 정면 대결임을 알았고, 잠시 손을 멈춘 사이에 그에게 그렇게 말했다. 그는 내가 알아듣지 못할 투로, 그러나 그의 얼굴이 지을 수 있는 가장 준엄한 표정으로 대답했다.\n\n\"그것이 전부라면, 나는 지금 우리가 선 이 자리에서 멈추고, 그녀가 평온 속으로 스러져가도록 내버려 두겠네. 그녀의 지평선 너머 삶에는 아무 빛도 보이지 않으니 말일세.\" 그는, 가능하다면, 더욱 새로워지고 더욱 미친 듯한 기세로 일을 계속해 나갔다.\n\n이윽고 우리는 둘 다 그 열기가 어느 정도 효력을 내기 시작했음을 의식하게 되었다. 청진기에 잡히는 루시의 심장 박동이 조금 더 또렷이 들렸고, 그녀의 폐도 알아챌 만큼 움직였다. 반 헬싱의 얼굴은 거의 환히 빛났고, 우리가 그녀를 욕조에서 들어 올려 물기를 닦으려 더운 시트로 감쌀 때 그가 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"첫 번째 승리는 우리 것일세! 왕에게 장군!\"\n\n우리는 루시를 그동안 채비를 마쳐둔 다른 방으로 옮겨 침대에 뉘고, 브랜디 몇 방울을 그녀의 목구멍에 흘려넣었다. 나는 반 헬싱이 부드러운 비단 손수건을 그녀의 목에 둘러 매는 것을 눈여겨보았다. 그녀는 여전히 의식이 없었고, 우리가 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 나빠진 상태였다. 아니, 더 나빠졌다고는 못 해도 그에 못지않았다.\n\n반 헬싱은 여인 하나를 불러들여, 우리가 돌아올 때까지 그녀 곁에 있으면서 잠시도 눈을 떼지 말라고 일렀다. 그러고는 나에게 방에서 나가자고 손짓했다.\n\n\"어떻게 해야 할지 의논해야 하네.\" 우리가 계단을 내려가면서 그가 말했다. 현관에서 그는 식당 문을 열었고, 우리가 안으로 들어서자 그는 등 뒤로 문을 조심스레 닫았다. 덧문은 열려 있었으나, 영국의 하층 계급 여인들이 언제나 엄격히 지키는 그 죽음의 예법에 따라 차양은 이미 내려져 있었다. 그래서 방은 어슴푸레 어두웠다. 그러나 우리 목적에는 충분히 밝았다. 반 헬싱의 준엄함은 당혹스러운 기색으로 다소 누그러져 있었다. 그는 분명 무언가에 대해 마음을 졸이며 괴로워하고 있었으므로, 나는 잠시 기다렸고, 그가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"이제 우리가 무엇을 해야 하나? 어디로 도움을 구하러 가야 하나? 우리는 또 한 번 수혈을 해야 하네, 그것도 곧, 그러지 않으면 저 가엾은 처녀의 목숨은 한 시간어치 값도 못 될 걸세. 자네는 이미 기진맥진했고, 나 또한 기진맥진했네. 나는 저 여인들을 믿기가 두렵네, 설령 그들이 응할 용기가 있다 한들 말일세. 그녀를 위해 제 핏줄을 열어줄 누군가를 어찌 구한단 말인가?\"\n\n\"그건 그렇고, 나는 어떻소?\"\n\n그 목소리는 방 건너편 소파에서 들려왔는데, 그 음색은 내 가슴에 안도와 기쁨을 안겨주었으니, 그것이 퀸시 모리스의 목소리였던 까닭이다. 그 첫 소리에 반 헬싱은 성난 듯 움찔했으나, 내가 \"퀸시 모리스!\" 하고 외치며 두 손을 내밀고 그에게 달려가자 그의 얼굴은 부드러워지고 두 눈에 반가운 빛이 돌았다.\n\n\"무슨 일로 여기 왔나?\" 우리의 손이 맞잡히자 내가 외쳤다.\n\n\"아트 때문인 것 같네.\"\n\n그는 나에게 전보 한 통을 건넸다.\n\n\"사흘째 수어드에게서 소식이 없어 몹시 불안하오. 나는 떠날 수가 없소. 아버지는 여전히 같은 상태요. 루시가 어떤지 소식을 전해주오. 지체하지 마시오. — 홈우드.\"\n\n\"내가 마침 딱 좋은 때에 온 것 같네. 알다시피 나에게 무엇을 하라고 말하기만 하면 되네.\"\n\n반 헬싱은 성큼 앞으로 나아가 그의 손을 잡고, 그의 두 눈을 똑바로 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"여인이 곤경에 처했을 때, 용감한 사내의 피야말로 이 세상에서 가장 좋은 것이라네. 자넨 틀림없는 사내일세. 자, 악마가 제아무리 기를 쓰고 우리에게 맞선다 해도, 신께서는 우리가 필요로 할 때 사내들을 보내주시는군.\"\n\n다시 한번 우리는 그 끔찍한 수술을 치렀다. 나는 그 세세한 과정을 차마 다 적을 마음이 나지 않는다. 루시는 무서운 충격을 받았고 그것이 전보다 더 심하게 그녀에게 나타났으니, 많은 피가 그녀의 핏줄로 들어갔음에도 그녀의 몸은 다른 때만큼 잘 반응하지 않았던 까닭이다. 다시 삶으로 돌아오려는 그녀의 안간힘은 보기에도 듣기에도 끔찍한 것이었다. 그러나 심장과 폐의 작용이 모두 나아졌고, 반 헬싱은 지난번처럼 모르핀을 피하주사했는데, 효과가 좋았다. 그녀의 혼절은 깊은 잠으로 바뀌었다. 교수가 지켜보는 동안, 나는 퀸시 모리스와 함께 아래층으로 내려가, 기다리고 있던 마부 하나에게 삯을 치르라고 하녀 하나를 보냈다. 나는 퀸시에게 포도주 한 잔을 마시게 한 뒤 누워 있게 두고, 요리사에게 든든한 아침식사를 차려두라고 일렀다. 그러다가 문득 한 가지 생각이 떠올라, 나는 지금 루시가 있는 방으로 되돌아갔다. 내가 가만히 들어서니, 반 헬싱이 손에 편지지 한두 장을 들고 있는 것을 발견했다. 그는 분명 그것을 읽은 듯했고, 한 손을 이마에 짚고 앉아 그것을 곱씹어 생각하고 있었다. 그의 얼굴에는 어떤 의혹이 풀린 사람처럼 엄숙한 만족의 빛이 어려 있었다. 그는 그 편지를 나에게 건네며 이렇게만 말했다. \"우리가 그녀를 욕조로 옮길 때 루시의 가슴에서 떨어진 걸세.\"\n\n내가 그것을 다 읽고 나서 교수를 바라보며 서 있다가, 잠시 후 그에게 물었다. \"도대체 이게 다 무슨 뜻입니까? 그녀가 미쳤던, 아니면 미친 겁니까? 아니면 대체 어떤 끔찍한 위험입니까?\" 나는 너무도 어리둥절하여 더 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다. 반 헬싱은 손을 내밀어 그 편지를 받으며 말했다.\n\n\"지금은 그 일로 마음 쓰지 말게. 당분간은 잊어버리게. 머지않아 자네는 그 모든 것을 알고 이해하게 될 걸세. 다만 그건 나중 일이지. 그건 그렇고, 자네가 나에게 하려던 말은 무엇이었나?\" 이 말이 나를 현실로 돌아오게 했고, 나는 다시 온전히 내 자신이 되었다.\n\n\"사망 진단서에 관해 말씀드리려 왔습니다. 우리가 적절하고 현명하게 처신하지 않으면 검시가 있을지도 모르고, 그러면 저 편지를 내놓아야만 할 겁니다. 검시는 필요 없게 되기를 저는 바라고 있습니다. 만일 검시가 열린다면, 다른 게 안 죽인다 해도 그것만으로도 분명 가엾은 루시를 죽이고 말 테니까요. 저도 알고, 선생님도 아시고, 그녀를 진료한 다른 의사도 압니다. 웨스튼라 부인에게 심장병이 있었다는 것을요. 그러니 우리는 그녀가 그 병으로 죽었다고 증명할 수 있습니다. 당장 진단서를 작성합시다. 제가 직접 그것을 호적계로 가지고 가, 이어서 장의사에게도 들르겠습니다.\"\n\n\"좋네, 오, 나의 벗 존이여! 잘 생각했네! 참으로 루시 아가씨는, 그녀를 에워싼 적들로 보면 비록 가엾을지언정, 적어도 그녀를 사랑하는 벗들로 보면 행복한 사람일세. 하나, 둘, 셋, 모두가 그녀를 위해 제 핏줄을 열었고, 게다가 늙은이 하나까지 더했지. 아, 그렇네, 나는 아네, 벗 존이여. 나는 장님이 아닐세! 그래서 나는 자네를 더욱 사랑하네! 자, 이제 가게.\"\n\n현관에서 나는 퀸시 모리스와 마주쳤는데, 그는 아서에게 보낼 전보를 들고 있었다. 웨스튼라 부인이 죽었다는 것, 루시 또한 앓았으나 이제 차도가 있다는 것, 그리고 반 헬싱과 내가 그녀 곁에 있다는 내용이었다. 나는 그에게 내가 어디로 가는지 일렀고, 그는 나를 서둘러 내보냈으나, 내가 나서려는데 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"돌아오거든, 잭, 우리끼리만 두어 마디 나눌 수 있겠나?\" 나는 고개를 끄덕여 답하고 나갔다. 호적 등록에는 별 어려움이 없었고, 동네 장의사와 저녁에 와서 관 치수를 재고 절차를 마련하기로 약속했다.\n\n내가 돌아오니 퀸시가 나를 기다리고 있었다. 나는 그에게, 루시에 대해 알아본 뒤 곧 보러 오겠다고 일렀고, 그녀의 방으로 올라갔다. 그녀는 여전히 잠들어 있었고, 교수는 보아하니 그녀 곁의 자리에서 꼼짝도 하지 않은 듯했다. 그가 손가락을 입술에 갖다 대는 것으로 보아, 나는 그녀가 머지않아 깨어나리라 그가 짐작하고 있으며 자연의 순리를 앞당길까 두려워하는 것이라고 헤아렸다. 그래서 나는 퀸시에게 내려가, 그를 아침식사방으로 데리고 갔는데, 그곳은 차양이 내려져 있지 않아 다른 방들보다 조금 더 밝았다. 아니, 덜 음울했다고 하는 편이 낫겠다. 우리끼리만 있게 되자 그가 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"잭 수어드, 내가 끼어들 자격도 없는 곳에 함부로 나서고 싶진 않네. 하지만 이건 예사로운 경우가 아니야. 자네도 알다시피 나는 저 처녀를 사랑했고 그녀와 결혼하고 싶었네. 비록 그건 이제 다 지나간 일이지만, 그래도 나는 그녀가 걱정스러운 것을 어쩔 수 없네. 대체 그녀에게 무슨 문제가 있는 건가? 저 네덜란드 양반은 — 훌륭한 노신사야, 나도 그건 알 수 있네 — 자네 둘이 방에 들어왔을 때, 또 한 번 수혈을 해야 한다고, 그리고 자네와 그 양반 둘 다 기진맥진했다고 말했네. 자, 나도 자네들 의사 양반들이 은밀히 이야기를 나누고, 사사로이 의논하는 바를 남이 알려 들어선 안 된다는 걸 잘 아네. 하지만 이건 예사로운 일이 아니고, 그것이 무엇이든 간에 나도 내 몫을 다했네. 그렇지 않은가?\"\n\n\"그렇네.\" 내가 말하자 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"내 생각에 자네와 반 헬싱도 오늘 내가 한 것을 이미 했을 것 같네. 그렇지 않은가?\"\n\n\"그렇네.\"\n\n\"그리고 아트도 거기 끼어 있었던 것 같네. 나흘 전 그의 집에서 그를 봤을 때 그가 이상해 보이더군. 내가 팜파스에 있을 때, 아끼던 암말 한 마리가 하룻밤 새에 풀밭에 쓰러진 일이 있은 뒤로, 무엇이 그렇게 빨리 쇠하는 걸 본 적이 없네. 흡혈박쥐라 부르는 그 커다란 박쥐 가운데 하나가 밤에 그 말에게 달려들었는데, 놈이 실컷 빨아먹은 데다 핏줄이 열린 채로 남겨졌으니, 말은 일어설 만한 피도 남지 않아, 나는 누워 있는 그 말의 몸에 총알을 박아 넣어야 했네. 잭, 비밀을 저버리지 않고 말할 수 있다면 말해주게, 아서가 처음이었지, 그렇지 않은가?\" 그렇게 말하는 동안 그 가엾은 친구는 몹시 불안해 보였다. 그는 자기가 사랑하는 여인에 대한 마음 졸이는 초조함에 시달리고 있었고, 그녀를 에워싼 듯한 그 무서운 수수께끼를 도무지 알지 못하는 것이 그의 고통을 더욱 깊게 했다. 그의 가슴은 진정 피 흘리고 있었고, 무너져 내리지 않으려고 버티는 데에는 그의 사내다움이 — 그것도 왕에 견줄 만큼 넘치는 사내다움이 — 죄다 필요했다. 나는 대답하기 전에 잠시 망설였으니, 교수가 비밀로 간직하고자 하는 어떤 것도 누설해서는 안 된다고 느꼈던 까닭이다. 그러나 그는 이미 너무 많은 것을 알았고 너무 많은 것을 짐작하고 있었으므로, 대답하지 않을 이유가 없었다. 그래서 나는 같은 말로 대답했다. \"그렇네.\"\n\n\"그래서 이게 얼마나 오래 계속된 건가?\"\n\n\"열흘쯤.\"\n\n\"열흘이라! 그렇다면 내 생각에, 잭 수어드, 우리 모두가 사랑하는 저 가엾고 어여쁜 사람이 그동안 제 핏줄에 건장한 사내 넷의 피를 받았다는 말이로군. 이 사람아, 그녀의 온몸으로도 그걸 다 담지 못할 텐데.\" 그러더니 그는 나에게 바짝 다가와 사납게 반쯤 속삭이는 투로 말했다. \"무엇이 그걸 빼내간 건가?\"\n\n나는 고개를 저었다. \"그것이,\" 내가 말했다. \"바로 핵심일세. 반 헬싱은 그 일로 그저 미칠 지경이고, 나는 어찌할 바를 모르겠네. 짐작조차 해볼 수가 없네. 루시를 제대로 지켜야 한다는 우리의 온갖 계산을 어그러뜨린 자잘한 사정들이 잇따라 일어났지. 하지만 그런 일은 다시는 없을 걸세. 우리는 모든 게 잘되든 — 잘못되든, 결판이 날 때까지 여기 머물 거네.\" 퀸시가 손을 내밀었다. \"나도 끼워주게.\" 그가 말했다. \"자네와 저 네덜란드 양반이 무엇을 하라 일러주면, 나는 그것을 하겠네.\"\n\n오후 늦게 그녀가 깨어났을 때, 루시의 첫 동작은 가슴께를 더듬는 것이었는데, 놀랍게도 반 헬싱이 나에게 읽으라고 주었던 그 편지를 꺼내는 것이었다. 그 세심한 교수가, 깨어났을 때 그녀가 놀랄까 봐 그것을 본래 있던 자리에 도로 넣어두었던 것이다. 이윽고 그녀의 눈길이 반 헬싱에게, 그리고 나에게도 닿더니 환해졌다. 그러고 나서 그녀는 방을 둘러보았고, 자기가 어디에 있는지 알아채고는 몸서리를 쳤다. 그녀는 크게 울부짖으며, 그 가엾고 야윈 두 손을 핏기 없는 얼굴 앞으로 가져갔다. 우리 둘 다 그것이 무슨 뜻인지 알았으니 — 그녀가 어머니의 죽음을 온전히 깨달았다는 것이었다. 그래서 우리는 할 수 있는 한 그녀를 위로해보려 애썼다. 동정이 그녀를 다소 누그러뜨린 것은 분명했으나, 그녀는 생각도 기력도 몹시 가라앉아, 오래도록 소리 없이 가냘프게 울었다. 우리는 이제부터 우리 둘 중 하나, 혹은 둘 다 늘 그녀 곁에 머물 거라고 일렀고, 그것이 그녀를 위로하는 듯했다. 해 질 무렵 그녀는 깜빡 잠이 들었다. 여기서 매우 기이한 일이 일어났다. 그녀는 잠든 채로 가슴에서 그 편지를 꺼내 두 갈래로 찢었다. 반 헬싱이 다가가 그녀에게서 그 조각들을 빼냈다. 그래도 그녀는, 마치 그 종이가 여전히 손에 들려 있기라도 한 듯 찢는 동작을 계속했다. 마침내 그녀는 두 손을 들어 올려, 그 조각들을 흩뿌리기라도 하듯 펼쳤다. 반 헬싱은 놀란 듯했고, 무언가 골똘히 생각하듯 미간을 모았으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n9월 19일. — 지난밤 내내 그녀는 띄엄띄엄 잠을 잤는데, 늘 잠드는 것을 두려워했고, 잠에서 깰 때마다 다소 더 쇠약해져 있었다. 교수와 나는 번갈아 지켜보았으며, 우리는 한순간도 그녀 곁을 비워두지 않았다. 퀸시 모리스는 자기 속내에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 나는 그가 밤새도록 집 둘레를 돌고 또 돌며 순찰했음을 알았다.\n\n날이 밝자, 그 샅샅이 비추는 빛이 가엾은 루시의 기력이 무너진 자취를 드러냈다. 그녀는 고개를 돌리는 것조차 거의 못 했고, 받아 넘길 수 있는 적은 양분도 그녀에게 아무 도움이 되지 못하는 듯했다. 이따금 그녀는 잠이 들었는데, 반 헬싱과 나는 둘 다 잠들었을 때와 깨어 있을 때 그녀의 차이를 눈여겨보았다. 잠들었을 때 그녀는, 더 초췌해 보이긴 했어도 더 강해 보였고, 숨소리는 더 부드러웠다. 벌어진 입은 잇몸이 핏기 없이 이에서 물러난 것을 드러냈는데, 그래서 이는 평소보다 영락없이 더 길고 날카로워 보였다. 깨어났을 때는 눈매의 부드러움이 분명 그 표정을 바꾸어, 비록 죽어가는 모습일망정 그녀는 본래의 자기 자신으로 보였다. 오후에 그녀가 아서를 찾기에, 우리는 그에게 전보를 쳤다. 퀸시가 정거장으로 그를 마중하러 갔다.\n\n그가 도착했을 때는 거의 여섯 시였고, 해가 충만하고 따스하게 지고 있었으며, 붉은 빛이 창문으로 흘러들어 핏기 없는 두 뺨에 좀 더 혈색을 입혀주었다. 그녀를 본 순간 아서는 그저 복받치는 감정에 목이 메었고, 우리 가운데 누구도 입을 떼지 못했다. 흘러간 그 몇 시간 사이에 잠의 발작이, 아니 잠처럼 보이는 혼수상태가 더 잦아져, 대화가 가능한 틈은 짧아졌다. 그러나 아서가 곁에 있는 것은 자극제처럼 작용하는 듯했다. 그녀는 조금 기운을 차려, 우리가 도착한 이래로 보인 적 없이 그에게 한층 밝게 말을 건넸다. 그 또한 마음을 다잡고 되도록 명랑하게 말했으므로, 모든 것에서 최선이 이끌어졌다.\n\n이제 거의 한 시였고, 그와 반 헬싱이 그녀 곁에 앉아 있다. 나는 십오 분 뒤에 그들과 교대하기로 했고, 지금 이것을 루시의 축음기에 기록하고 있다. 여섯 시까지 그들은 쉬어보기로 했다. 나는 내일이면 우리의 간호가 끝나지 않을까 두렵다. 충격이 너무도 컸기 때문이다. 가엾은 그 아이는 끝내 기운을 차리지 못한다. 신이여, 우리 모두를 도우소서.\n편지, 미나 하커가 루시 웨스튼라에게.\n\n(그녀가 뜯어보지 못함.)\n\n\"9월 17일.\n\n\"나의 가장 사랑하는 루시에게, —\n\n\"네 소식을 들은 지, 아니 사실 내가 편지를 쓴 지도 한 세월이 흐른 것만 같구나. 내 소식 보따리를 다 읽고 나면, 네가 내 모든 잘못을 용서해 주리라는 걸 나는 알아. 자, 나는 내 남편을 무사히 되찾았단다. 우리가 엑서터에 닿았을 때 마차 한 대가 우리를 기다리고 있었고, 그 안에는, 통풍 발작이 있으셨는데도, 호킨스 씨가 계셨어. 그분은 우리를 자신의 집으로 데려가셨는데, 그곳에는 우리 모두를 위한 좋고 편안한 방들이 마련되어 있었고, 우리는 함께 저녁을 들었지. 저녁식사 뒤에 호킨스 씨가 말씀하셨어. —\n\n\"'얘들아, 나는 너희의 건강과 번영을 위해 잔을 들고 싶구나. 부디 너희 둘에게 온갖 축복이 함께하기를. 나는 너희 둘을 어릴 적부터 알았고, 사랑과 자랑으로 너희가 자라는 것을 지켜보았다. 이제 나는 너희가 나와 함께 여기를 너희 보금자리로 삼기를 바란다. 나에게는 자식도 핏줄도 하나 남지 않았으니, 모두 떠나갔고, 나는 유언으로 너희에게 모든 것을 남겼다.' 조너선과 그 노인이 손을 맞잡을 때 나는 울었단다, 사랑하는 루시야. 우리의 저녁은 참으로, 참으로 행복했어.\n\n\"그리하여 우리는 이 아름답고 오래된 집에 자리 잡게 되었고, 내 침실에서도 응접실에서도, 나는 대성당 안뜰의 커다란 느릅나무들을 볼 수 있어. 그 굵고 검은 줄기들이 대성당의 오래된 노란 돌을 배경으로 우뚝 서 있고, 머리 위에서는 띠까마귀들이 까악까악 울며 온종일 까마귀들의 — 그리고 사람들의 — 방식대로 떠들고 수다를 떠는 소리가 들려. 굳이 말할 것도 없이, 나는 살림을 정리하고 집안을 꾸리느라 분주하단다. 조너선과 호킨스 씨는 온종일 바쁘셔. 이제 조너선이 동업자가 되었으니, 호킨스 씨가 그에게 의뢰인들에 관한 모든 것을 일러주고 싶어 하시거든.\n\n\"네 사랑하는 어머니는 어떻게 지내시니? 하루나 이틀이라도 시내로 달려 올라가 너를 보고 싶지만, 어깨에 진 일이 너무 많아 아직은 엄두를 못 내겠어. 그리고 조너선은 여전히 보살핌이 필요하단다. 그이는 다시 살이 좀 오르기 시작했지만, 그 긴 병으로 끔찍이 쇠약해졌어. 지금도 이따금 잠결에 갑자기 화들짝 놀라 깨어나서는, 내가 어르고 달래어 평소의 평온함으로 돌려놓을 때까지 온통 떨고 있단다. 그래도, 신께 감사하게도, 날이 갈수록 그런 일이 점점 뜸해지고 있으니, 머지않아 아주 사라지리라 나는 믿어. 자, 이제 내 소식을 다 전했으니, 네 소식을 물어볼게. 너는 언제 결혼하니, 어디서, 누가 식을 주례하고, 무엇을 입을 거니, 그리고 공개 결혼식이니 아니면 비공개 결혼식이니? 다 이야기해 줘, 얘야. 무엇이든 다 이야기해 줘. 너에게 관심 있는 일이라면 나에게 소중하지 않을 게 하나도 없으니까. 조너선이 자기 '정중한 인사'를 전해 달라고 부탁하지만, 호킨스 앤드 하커라는 중요한 회사의 막내 동업자로서 그것만으로는 흡족하지 않은 것 같아. 그러니 네가 나를 사랑하고, 그이가 나를 사랑하고, 내가 그 동사의 온갖 시제와 어법으로 너를 사랑하니, 대신 나는 그저 그이의 '사랑'을 너에게 전한다. 안녕, 나의 가장 사랑하는 루시야, 너에게 온갖 축복을 빈다.\n\n\"너의,\n\n\"미나 하커.\"\n패트릭 헤너시 의학박사, 영국 왕립외과의사회·아일랜드 왕립의사회 회원 등등이 의학박사 존 수어드에게 보낸 보고서.\n\n\"9월 20일.\n\n\"친애하는 선생님께, —\n\n\"선생님의 뜻에 따라, 제가 맡은 모든 것의 상황에 대한 보고서를 동봉합니다…… 환자 렌필드에 관해서는 더 말씀드릴 것이 있습니다. 그는 또 한 번 발작을 일으켰는데, 끔찍한 결말로 치달을 수도 있었으나, 다행히도 그렇게 된 덕분에 어떤 불행한 결과도 따르지 않았습니다. 오늘 오후, 일꾼 둘이 탄 짐마차 한 대가, 그 부지가 우리 부지와 맞닿은 빈집에 — 기억하시겠지만, 환자가 두 번이나 달아났던 그 집입니다 — 들렀습니다. 그 사람들은 이곳이 낯선 터라, 길을 묻고자 우리 대문 앞에 멈춰 수위에게 다가갔습니다. 저는 마침 저녁식사 뒤에 한 대 피우면서 서재 창밖을 내다보고 있었는데, 그 가운데 하나가 집 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보았습니다. 그가 렌필드의 방 창문을 지나칠 때, 환자가 안에서 그를 욕하기 시작하여, 혀에 올릴 수 있는 온갖 더러운 욕설을 그에게 퍼부었습니다. 제법 점잖아 보이던 그 사람은 '입이나 다물어, 이 상스러운 거지 같은 놈아'라고 받아치는 것으로 그쳤는데, 그러자 우리 환자는 그가 자기 것을 빼앗고 자기를 죽이려 한다고 몰아세우며, 자기가 그 일로 교수형을 당하는 한이 있어도 그를 막겠다고 말했습니다. 제가 창문을 열고 그 사람에게 신경 쓰지 말라고 손짓하자, 그는 그곳을 한 번 둘러보고 자기가 어떤 곳에 발을 들였는지 가늠하고는 이렇게 말하는 것으로 그쳤습니다. '맙소사, 나리, 빌어먹을 정신병원에서 무슨 소리를 듣든 저야 개의치 않습니다요. 저런 들짐승 같은 놈하고 한집에 살아야 하는 나리하고 주인 양반이 딱하구먼요.' 그러고는 제법 공손하게 길을 물었고, 저는 그 빈집의 대문이 어디인지 일러주었습니다. 그는 떠나갔고, 우리 환자의 위협과 저주와 욕설이 그 뒤를 따랐습니다. 저는 그의 분노에 무슨 까닭이라도 알아낼 수 있을까 하여 아래로 내려갔으니, 그는 평소 그토록 얌전한 사람인 데다, 그 난폭한 발작 말고는 그런 일이 한 번도 없었던 까닭입니다. 놀랍게도 저는 그가 아주 차분하고 더없이 상냥한 태도로 있는 것을 발견했습니다. 제가 그 일에 대해 이야기를 끌어내보려 했으나, 그는 천연덕스레 제가 무슨 말을 하는 거냐고 되묻더니, 그 일을 까맣게 잊고 있는 양 저를 믿게 만들었습니다. 그러나 유감스럽게도, 그것은 그의 교활함을 보여주는 또 하나의 사례일 뿐이었으니, 반 시간도 못 되어 저는 그에 대한 소식을 다시 듣게 되었기 때문입니다. 이번에 그는 자기 방 창문을 부수고 빠져나가, 길을 달려 내려가고 있었습니다. 저는 시중꾼들에게 따라오라 외치고 그를 뒤쫓았으니, 그가 무슨 해코지를 작정한 게 아닐까 두려웠던 까닭입니다. 앞서 지나갔던 바로 그 짐마차가, 커다란 나무 상자 몇 개를 싣고 길을 내려오는 것을 보았을 때, 제 두려움은 사실로 드러났습니다. 그 사람들은 격렬한 노동이라도 한 듯 얼굴이 벌게져 이마를 훔치고 있었습니다. 제가 그에게 다다르기도 전에 환자는 그들에게 달려들어, 그중 하나를 짐마차에서 끌어내리고는 그의 머리를 땅바닥에 짓찧기 시작했습니다. 제가 바로 그 순간 그를 붙들지 않았더라면, 저는 그가 그 자리에서 당장 그 사람을 죽였으리라 믿습니다. 다른 사내가 뛰어내려, 그의 묵직한 채찍 손잡이로 그의 머리를 후려쳤습니다. 무서운 일격이었으나, 그는 아랑곳하지 않는 듯, 그자마저 붙들고는 우리 셋과 맞붙어, 우리를 새끼고양이라도 되는 양 이리저리 끌고 다녔습니다. 아시다시피 저는 가벼운 체구가 아니고, 다른 둘도 둘 다 건장한 사내들이었습니다. 처음에 그는 말없이 싸웠으나, 우리가 그를 제압하기 시작하고 시중꾼들이 그에게 구속복을 입히려 하자, 그는 고함치기 시작했습니다. '내가 놈들을 좌절시키겠다! 놈들이 나에게서 빼앗지 못하게 하겠다! 놈들이 나를 조금씩 죽이게 두지 않겠다! 나는 나의 주인이자 군주를 위해 싸우겠다!' 그 밖에도 온갖 비슷한 두서없는 헛소리를 늘어놓았습니다. 그를 다시 집으로 데려와 완충실에 넣기까지는 상당한 애를 먹어야 했습니다. 시중꾼 가운데 하나인 하디는 손가락 하나가 부러졌습니다. 그러나 제가 그것을 잘 맞춰놓았으니, 그는 잘 회복되고 있습니다.\n\n\"그 두 짐꾼은 처음에는 손해 배상 소송을 걸겠다고 큰소리치며 위협하여, 법의 온갖 처벌을 우리에게 퍼붓겠다고 으름장을 놓았습니다. 그러나 그들의 위협에는, 둘이서 한 가냘픈 미치광이에게 진 데 대한 어떤 에두른 변명이 섞여 있었습니다. 그들은 무거운 상자들을 나르고 짐마차에 들어 올리느라 기력을 다 써버리지만 않았더라면 그를 단번에 처치했을 거라고 했습니다. 그들은 진 또 다른 까닭으로, 일의 먼지투성이 성질 때문에 빠지게 된 유별난 갈증과, 그들의 노동 현장에서 어떤 술집이든 괘씸하리만치 멀리 떨어져 있었던 점을 들었습니다. 저는 그들의 속내를 충분히 알아채고, 독한 그로그 한 잔, 아니 그보다 좀 더 같은 것을 마시게 한 뒤, 각자 손에 1소버린씩 쥐여주자, 그들은 그 습격을 대수롭지 않게 여기며, 이 글을 쓰는 사람처럼 '그렇게 빌어먹게 좋은 양반'을 만나는 즐거움을 위해서라면 언제든 더 고약한 미치광이라도 상대하겠노라고 호언했습니다. 저는 필요할지 몰라 그들의 이름과 주소를 받아두었습니다. 그것은 다음과 같습니다. — 그레이트 월워스, 킹 조지 로드, 더딩스 렌츠의 잭 스몰릿, 그리고 베스널 그린, 가이드 코트, 피터 팔리스 로의 토머스 스넬링입니다. 둘 다 소호, 오렌지 마스터스 야드의 운송·선적 회사 해리스 앤드 선스에 고용되어 있습니다.\n\n\"이곳에서 일어나는 관심 끄는 일은 무엇이든 선생님께 보고드리겠으며, 중요한 일이 있으면 즉시 전보를 치겠습니다.\n\"믿어 주시기를, 친애하는 선생님께,\n\n\"충실한 벗,\n\"패트릭 헤너시.\"\n\n편지, 미나 하커가 루시 웨스튼라에게.\n\n(그녀가 뜯어보지 못함.)\n\n\"9월 18일.\n\n\"나의 가장 사랑하는 루시에게, —\n\n\"우리에게 참으로 슬픈 변고가 닥쳤어. 호킨스 씨가 아주 갑작스레 돌아가셨단다. 우리에게 그리 슬픈 일은 아니라고 여길 이도 있겠지만, 우리 둘 다 그분을 무척이나 사랑하게 되어, 정말이지 아버지를 잃은 것만 같아. 나는 아버지도 어머니도 알지 못하고 자랐기에, 그 다정한 노인의 죽음은 나에게 진정 큰 타격이란다. 조너선은 몹시 상심해 있어. 그것은 단지 그이가, 평생 그를 도와주고 이제 마지막에 와서는 자기 친아들처럼 대하며, 우리처럼 검소하게 자란 사람들에게는 탐욕의 꿈을 넘어서는 재산을 남겨준 그 다정하고 선한 분을 위해 슬픔을, 깊은 슬픔을 느끼기 때문만은 아니야. 조너선은 또 다른 까닭으로도 그것을 느낀단다. 그이는 그것이 자기에게 지운 책임의 무게가 자기를 불안하게 만든다고 해. 그이는 자기 자신을 의심하기 시작했어. 나는 그이의 기운을 북돋워 보려 애쓰고, 내가 그이를 믿는 것이 그이가 자기 자신을 믿는 데 도움이 되긴 해. 하지만 그이가 겪은 그 심각한 충격이 가장 심하게 나타나는 곳이 바로 여기야. 아, 그이 같은 다정하고 소박하고 고귀하고 강인한 천성이 — 우리의 다정하고 선한 벗의 도움으로 몇 년 만에 사무원에서 주인으로 올라설 수 있게 해준 그 천성이 — 그 힘의 정수마저 사라질 만큼 상하다니, 너무도 가혹한 일이야. 용서해 줘, 얘야, 너 자신의 행복 한가운데에서 내 근심으로 너를 성가시게 한다면. 하지만, 사랑하는 루시야, 나는 누군가에게 털어놓아야만 해. 조너선 앞에서 씩씩하고 명랑한 모습을 꾸며 보이는 긴장이 나를 힘들게 하는데, 이곳에는 마음을 터놓을 사람이 아무도 없으니까. 모레는 우리가 그래야만 하지만, 나는 런던으로 올라가는 게 두려워. 가엾은 호킨스 씨가 유언으로 자기 아버지의 무덤에 함께 묻히기를 남기셨거든. 친척이라곤 전혀 없으니, 조너선이 상주를 맡아야 할 거야. 나는 너를 보러 잠깐이라도, 단 몇 분만이라도 들르려 애써볼게, 사랑하는 이야. 너를 성가시게 한 걸 용서해 줘. 온갖 축복과 함께,\n\"너를 사랑하는\n\n\"미나 하커.\"\n\n수어드 박사의 일기.\n\n9월 20일. — 오직 결심과 습관만이 오늘 밤 내가 이 일기를 적게 해준다. 나는 너무도 비참하고, 너무도 기운이 가라앉았으며, 세상과 그 안의 모든 것에, 삶 자체까지 포함하여 너무도 진저리가 나서, 지금 이 순간 죽음의 천사가 날갯짓하는 소리를 듣는다 해도 개의치 않으리라. 그리고 그자는 요즘 그 음산한 날개를 무언가 목적을 두고 퍼덕여 왔으니 — 루시의 어머니와 아서의 아버지, 그리고 이제는…… 내 할 일이나 계속하자.\n\n나는 정해진 대로 루시를 지키던 반 헬싱과 교대했다. 우리는 아서도 가서 쉬기를 바랐으나, 그는 처음에는 거절했다. 낮 동안 우리가 그의 도움이 필요할 거라고, 그리고 루시가 고통받지 않도록 우리 모두가 휴식 부족으로 쓰러져서는 안 된다고 일러주고서야 비로소 그는 가기로 했다. 반 헬싱은 그에게 무척 다정했다. \"이리 오게, 내 아이.\" 그가 말했다. \"나와 함께 가세. 자네는 병약하고 쇠약한 데다, 우리가 아는 그 기력의 소모뿐 아니라 많은 슬픔과 많은 마음의 고통을 겪었네. 자네는 혼자 있어서는 안 되네. 혼자 있다는 것은 두려움과 불안으로 가득 차는 것이니 말일세. 큰 불이 피워져 있고 소파가 둘 있는 응접실로 가세. 자네는 한쪽에 눕고 나는 다른 쪽에 누우면, 우리가 말을 하지 않더라도, 설령 잠이 든다 해도, 우리의 동병상련이 서로에게 위로가 될 걸세.\" 아서는 베개 위에 누운 루시의 얼굴에 — 그것은 얇은 아마포보다 거의 더 희었다 — 미련 어린 눈길을 던지며 그와 함께 떠났다. 그녀는 아주 가만히 누워 있었고, 나는 모든 것이 제대로 되어 있는지 보려고 방을 둘러보았다. 교수가 이 방에서도 저쪽 방에서처럼 마늘을 쓰려던 그의 뜻을 실행에 옮긴 것을 나는 알아볼 수 있었다. 창틀 전체에서 마늘 냄새가 진동했고, 반 헬싱이 그녀에게 두르고 있게 한 비단 손수건 위로, 루시의 목둘레에는 그 냄새 나는 꽃으로 엮은 거친 화관이 둘러져 있었다. 루시는 다소 그르렁거리며 숨을 쉬고 있었고, 벌어진 입이 핏기 없는 잇몸을 드러내, 그녀의 얼굴은 더없이 나쁜 상태였다. 그녀의 이는, 그 어슴푸레하고 불확실한 빛 속에서, 아침나절보다 더 길고 날카로워 보였다. 특히 어떤 빛의 장난인지, 송곳니가 나머지보다 더 길고 날카로워 보였다. 나는 그녀 곁에 앉았는데, 이윽고 그녀가 불안하게 몸을 뒤척였다. 바로 그 순간 창문에서 무언가 둔하게 퍼덕이거나 부딪치는 듯한 소리가 났다. 나는 가만히 그쪽으로 다가가 차양 모서리로 밖을 엿보았다. 보름달이 환했고, 나는 그 소리가 커다란 박쥐 한 마리가 내는 것임을 볼 수 있었다. 그것은 — 비록 어슴푸레하나마 그 빛에 이끌린 게 분명한데 — 빙빙 맴돌며 이따금 날개로 창문을 쳤다. 내가 자리로 돌아오니, 루시가 조금 몸을 움직여, 목에서 마늘꽃을 떼어내 버린 것을 발견했다. 나는 되도록 잘 그것을 도로 놓아두고, 그녀를 지켜보며 앉아 있었다.\n\n이윽고 그녀가 깨어났고, 나는 반 헬싱이 처방한 대로 그녀에게 음식을 주었다. 그녀는 조금밖에, 그것도 맥없이 받아먹었다. 그녀에게는 이제, 그때껏 그녀의 병을 그토록 두드러지게 했던, 삶과 기력을 향한 그 무의식적인 안간힘이 없는 듯했다. 의식을 차리는 순간 그녀가 마늘꽃을 자기 가슴께로 바싹 끌어당기는 것이 나에게는 기이하게 다가왔다. 그르렁대는 숨소리와 함께 그 무기력한 상태에 빠질 때마다 꽃을 멀리하다가, 깨어나면 그것을 바싹 그러쥐는 것은 분명 이상한 일이었다. 이 점에 대해서는 어떤 착각의 여지도 있을 수 없었으니, 뒤따른 긴 시간 동안 그녀는 잠들고 깨어나기를 여러 차례 거듭하며 그 두 동작을 몇 번이고 되풀이했기 때문이다.\n\n여섯 시에 반 헬싱이 나와 교대하러 왔다. 그때 아서는 깜빡 잠이 들어 있었고, 그는 자비롭게도 그를 계속 자게 두었다. 루시의 얼굴을 보았을 때, 나는 그가 쉭 하고 숨을 들이켜는 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 그는 나에게 날카로이 속삭였다. \"차양을 올리게. 빛이 필요하네!\" 그러고는 몸을 숙여, 그의 얼굴이 루시의 얼굴에 거의 닿을 듯이 하여 그녀를 찬찬히 살폈다. 그는 꽃들을 치우고 그녀의 목에서 비단 손수건을 들어 올렸다. 그렇게 하면서 그는 움찔 물러섰고, 나는 그가 목구멍에서 삼키듯 내뱉은 탄식, \"마인 고트!\"를 들을 수 있었다. 나도 몸을 숙여 들여다보았는데, 그것을 보는 순간 어떤 기이한 한기가 나를 엄습했다.\n\n목의 상처가 완전히 사라져 있었다.\n\n꼬박 오 분 동안 반 헬싱은 더없이 준엄한 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보며 서 있었다. 그러더니 나에게 돌아서서 차분히 말했다.\n\n\"그녀는 죽어가고 있네. 이제 오래지 않을 걸세. 잘 새겨두게, 그녀가 의식이 있는 채로 죽느냐 잠든 채로 죽느냐는 큰 차이가 있을 걸세. 저 가엾은 청년을 깨워, 와서 마지막을 보게 하게. 그는 우리를 믿고, 우리는 그에게 약속했으니 말일세.\"\n\n나는 식당으로 가서 그를 깨웠다. 그는 한순간 멍해 있었으나, 덧문 가장자리로 햇살이 흘러드는 것을 보고는 자기가 늦었다고 생각하여 두려움을 내비쳤다. 나는 루시가 여전히 잠들어 있다고 그를 안심시켰으나, 되도록 부드럽게, 반 헬싱과 나 둘 다 끝이 가까웠음을 두려워한다고 일렀다. 그는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고는 소파 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 미끄러지듯 주저앉아, 거기에서 한 일 분쯤, 고개를 파묻은 채 기도하며 머물렀는데, 그의 두 어깨가 슬픔으로 떨렸다. 나는 그의 손을 잡아 일으켜 세웠다. \"이리 오게.\" 내가 말했다. \"나의 다정한 친구여, 자네의 용기를 다 끌어모으게. 그것이 그녀에게 가장 좋고 가장 편안할 걸세.\"\n\n우리가 루시의 방으로 들어서자, 나는 반 헬싱이 늘 그렇듯 앞서 헤아려, 매무새를 바로잡고 모든 것을 되도록 보기 좋게 꾸며두었음을 알 수 있었다. 그는 루시의 머리칼까지 빗겨놓아, 그것이 평소처럼 햇살 같은 잔물결을 이루며 베개 위에 놓여 있게 했다. 우리가 방에 들어서자 그녀는 눈을 떴고, 그를 보더니 가만히 속삭였다.\n\n\"아서! 오, 내 사랑, 와줘서 정말 기뻐요!\" 그가 입 맞추려 몸을 숙이는데, 반 헬싱이 그에게 물러서라고 손짓했다. \"안 되네.\" 그가 속삭였다. \"아직은! 그녀의 손을 잡게. 그게 그녀를 더 위로할 걸세.\"\n\n그리하여 아서는 그녀의 손을 잡고 곁에 무릎을 꿇었고, 그녀는 그 부드러운 윤곽 하나하나가 천사 같은 두 눈의 아름다움과 어우러져, 더없이 고운 모습이었다. 이윽고 그녀의 두 눈이 차츰 감기고, 그녀는 잠 속으로 가라앉았다. 잠깐 동안 그녀의 가슴이 부드럽게 오르내렸고, 숨결이 지친 아이의 그것처럼 드나들었다.\n\n그러다가 어느새, 내가 밤중에 눈여겨본 그 기이한 변화가 찾아왔다. 그녀의 숨소리가 그르렁거리기 시작했고, 입이 벌어지며, 핏기 없는 잇몸이 물러나 이를 그 어느 때보다 더 길고 날카로워 보이게 했다. 어떤 잠결의 몽롱하고 흐릿한, 의식 없는 상태로 그녀가 두 눈을 떴는데, 그 눈은 이제 흐리멍덩하면서도 동시에 매정했다. 그러고는 그녀의 입술에서 한 번도 들어본 적 없는, 부드럽고 관능적인 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"아서! 오, 내 사랑, 와줘서 정말 기뻐요! 키스해 줘요!\" 아서는 그녀에게 입 맞추려 열심히 몸을 숙였다. 그러나 그 순간, 나처럼 그녀의 목소리에 놀랐던 반 헬싱이 그에게 덮치듯 달려들어, 두 손으로 그의 목덜미를 붙잡고는, 그가 지녔으리라고는 내가 결코 생각지 못한 맹렬한 힘으로 그를 뒤로 끌어당겨, 실로 방을 가로지르다시피 그를 내동댕이쳤다.\n\n\"목숨을 걸고서라도 안 되네!\" 그가 말했다. \"자네의 살아 있는 영혼을 걸고, 그녀의 영혼을 걸고서라도 안 되네!\" 그러고는 궁지에 몰린 사자처럼 그 둘 사이에 버티고 섰다.\n\n아서는 너무도 얼떨떨하여 한순간 무엇을 해야 할지, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알지 못했다. 그리고 어떤 난폭한 충동에 사로잡히기도 전에 그는 그 자리와 그 상황을 깨닫고는, 말없이 서서 기다렸다.\n\n나는 반 헬싱이 그랬듯 루시에게서 눈을 떼지 않았고, 우리는 분노 같은 경련이 그림자처럼 그녀의 얼굴을 스치는 것을 보았다. 날카로운 이가 맞부딪쳐 갈렸다. 그러더니 그녀의 두 눈이 감기고, 그녀는 무겁게 숨을 쉬었다.\n\n그러고는 곧 그녀가 더없이 부드러운 두 눈을 떠, 그 가엾고 핏기 없고 야윈 손을 내밀어 반 헬싱의 크고 갈색 손을 잡았다. 그것을 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겨 그녀는 거기에 입을 맞추었다. \"나의 진정한 벗이여.\" 그녀가 희미한 목소리로, 그러나 이루 말할 수 없는 애잔함으로 말했다. \"나의 진정한 벗이자, 그이의 벗이여! 오, 그이를 지켜주세요, 그리고 저에게 평안을 주세요!\"\n\n\"내 맹세하리다!\" 그가, 맹세를 기록하는 사람처럼 그녀 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 한 손을 치켜들며 엄숙히 말했다. 그러고는 아서에게 돌아서서 그에게 말했다. \"이리 오게, 내 아이. 그녀의 손을 자네 손에 잡고, 그녀의 이마에 입을 맞추게, 단 한 번만일세.\"\n\n그들의 입술 대신 그들의 눈길이 마주쳤다. 그렇게 그들은 헤어졌다.\n\n루시의 두 눈이 감겼다. 가까이서 지켜보고 있던 반 헬싱이 아서의 팔을 잡아 그를 끌어냈다.\n\n그러고는 루시의 숨소리가 다시 그르렁거리더니, 별안간 그것이 멎었다.\n\n\"다 끝났네.\" 반 헬싱이 말했다. \"그녀는 죽었어!\"\n\n나는 아서의 팔을 잡고 응접실로 그를 데려갔다. 그는 거기에 주저앉아 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고는, 보고 있던 내가 거의 무너져 내릴 만큼 흐느꼈다.\n\n나는 방으로 돌아가, 반 헬싱이 가엾은 루시를 바라보고 있는 것을 발견했는데, 그의 얼굴은 그 어느 때보다 준엄했다. 그녀의 몸에 어떤 변화가 일어나 있었다. 죽음이 그녀의 아름다움 일부를 되돌려주어, 이마와 두 뺨이 그 흐르는 듯한 윤곽을 얼마간 되찾았고, 입술마저 그 죽은 듯한 창백함을 잃었다. 마치 더 이상 심장의 작용에 쓰일 일이 없게 된 피가, 죽음의 모진 자취를 되도록 덜 거칠게 만들려고 흘러간 것만 같았다.\n\n\"우리는 그녀가 잠든 채 죽어간다고 여겼고, 죽어가는 동안 잠들었다고 여겼네.\"\n\n나는 반 헬싱 곁에 서서 말했다.\n\n\"아, 이제 됐습니다, 가엾은 처녀, 그녀에게도 마침내 평안이 찾아왔군요. 이것이 끝입니다!\"\n\n그는 나에게 돌아서서, 무거운 엄숙함으로 말했다.\n\n\"그렇지 않네. 아아! 그렇지 않아. 이것은 시작일 뿐일세!\"\n\n내가 그에게 무슨 뜻이냐고 묻자, 그는 그저 고개를 저으며 대답했다.\n\n\"우리는 아직 아무것도 할 수 없네. 기다려 보세.\"\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」(계속)\n\n장례식은 다음다음 날로 잡혔으니, 루시와 그녀의 어머니를 함께 묻기 위함이었다. 나는 그 모든 끔찍한 절차를 도맡아 처리했는데, 정중한 장의사는 자기 직원들도 자신이 지닌 굽실거리는 듯한 상냥함을 어느 정도 앓고 있다는 — 혹은 그 복을 누리고 있다는 — 것을 입증해 보였다. 망자에게 마지막 손길을 베푸는 여인조차, 임종의 방에서 나오면서 은밀하게, 동업자끼리의 정을 담은 투로 나에게 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"참으로 아름다운 시신이세요, 선생님. 이분을 모시는 게 그야말로 영광이지요. 우리 가게의 자랑이 되실 거라고 해도 결코 지나친 말이 아니랍니다!\"\n\n판 헬싱이 한 번도 멀리 떨어져 있지 않았다는 것을 나는 알아차렸다. 집안 일이 어수선했던 까닭에 그럴 수 있었다. 가까이에 친척이 없었고, 아서는 다음 날 아버지의 장례식에 참석하러 돌아가야 했으므로, 우리는 마땅히 부를 만한 사람에게 알릴 수가 없었다. 이런 사정이었으므로 판 헬싱과 나는 서류 따위를 살피는 일을 떠맡았다. 그는 루시의 서류를 자기가 직접 들여다보겠다고 고집했다. 나는 그 까닭을 물었으니, 그가 외국인인지라 영국의 법적 요건을 잘 알지 못하여 무지로 인해 쓸데없는 분란을 일으킬까 두려웠기 때문이다. 그가 나에게 답했다.\n\n\"나도 아오, 나도 알아요. 내가 의사이면서 또한 법률가라는 걸 잊은 게요. 그러나 이건 온전히 법 때문만은 아니오. 그건 당신도 알고 있었으니, 검시관을 피했을 때 말이오. 나는 그 사람보다 더한 것을 피해야 하오. 더 있을지도 모르오, 이런 서류가 말이오.\"\n\n이렇게 말하면서 그는 수첩에서, 루시의 가슴속에 있다가 그녀가 잠결에 찢어버린 그 비망록을 꺼냈다.\n\n\"고(故) 웨스텐라 부인의 변호사를 찾거든, 부인의 서류는 모두 봉인하고 오늘 밤 그에게 편지를 쓰시오. 나는 이 방과 루시 양의 옛 방에서 밤새 지키며, 무엇이 있을지 내가 직접 찾아보겠소. 그녀의 바로 그 생각들이 낯선 이의 손에 들어가는 것은 좋지 않소.\"\n\n나는 내 몫의 일을 계속하여, 또 반 시간 만에 웨스텐라 부인의 변호사 이름과 주소를 찾아 그에게 편지를 썼다. 그 가엾은 부인의 서류는 모두 정연했고, 매장 장소에 관한 분명한 지시도 적혀 있었다. 편지를 막 봉했을 때, 놀랍게도 판 헬싱이 방으로 들어오며 말했다.\n\n\"도와줄까요, 존? 나는 한가하니, 괜찮다면 당신을 돕겠소.\"\n\n\"찾던 것은 구하셨소?\" 하고 내가 묻자, 그가 답했다.\n\n\"딱히 무엇을 찾은 것은 아니오. 그저 찾을 수 있기를 바랐고, 또 있던 것을 다 찾았소 — 편지 몇 통과 비망록 몇 장, 그리고 새로 시작한 일기 하나뿐이었소. 그러나 지금 내가 가지고 있으니, 당분간은 그것들에 대해 아무 말도 하지 맙시다. 내일 저녁에 그 가엾은 청년을 만나, 그의 허락을 얻어 그 가운데 얼마를 쓰겠소.\"\n\n당면한 일을 마치자 그가 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"자, 이제 존, 우리 잠자리에 드는 게 좋겠소. 당신도 나도 잠이 필요하고, 기운을 되찾을 휴식도 필요하오. 내일은 할 일이 많겠지만, 오늘 밤만큼은 우리가 할 일이 없소. 아아!\"\n\n잠자리에 들기 전에 우리는 가엾은 루시를 보러 갔다. 장의사가 일을 잘해놓은 것이 분명했으니, 방은 작은 '빈소(殯所)'로 꾸며져 있었다. 아름다운 흰 꽃이 한가득 어우러져, 죽음은 될 수 있는 한 덜 혐오스럽게 다루어져 있었다. 수의 자락이 얼굴 위에 덮여 있었는데, 교수가 몸을 굽혀 그것을 살며시 젖히자, 우리 둘 다 눈앞의 아름다움에 흠칫 놀랐다. 키 큰 밀랍 양초들이 그것을 잘 살펴볼 만큼 충분한 빛을 비추고 있었다. 루시의 모든 사랑스러움이 죽음 속에서 되돌아와 있었고, 흘러간 시간들은 \"부패의 지우는 손길\"의 자취를 남기기는커녕 오히려 살아 있을 적의 아름다움을 되살려놓아, 내가 시신을 바라보고 있다는 것을 정말이지 내 눈으로 믿을 수 없을 지경이었다.\n\n교수는 엄숙하고 침통한 표정이었다. 그는 나만큼 그녀를 사랑하지 않았으니, 그의 눈에 눈물이 고일 까닭은 없었다. 그는 나에게 \"내가 돌아올 때까지 여기 있으시오\" 하고 말하고는 방을 나갔다. 그는 현관에서 기다리고 있었으나 아직 열지 않은 상자에서 야생 마늘 한 움큼을 가지고 돌아와, 침대 위와 그 둘레의 다른 꽃들 사이에 그 꽃을 놓았다. 그러고는 목에서, 옷깃 안쪽에서 작은 금 십자고상을 꺼내어 그녀의 입 위에 놓았다. 그는 수의를 제자리로 되돌려놓았고, 우리는 그 방을 떠나왔다.\n\n내가 내 방에서 옷을 벗고 있을 때, 그가 미리 알리는 노크를 하고 들어와서는 곧바로 말하기 시작했다.\n\n\"내일 날이 저물기 전에 부검용 칼 한 벌을 가져다주었으면 하오.\"\n\n\"부검을 해야 하오?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"그렇기도 하고 아니기도 하오. 나는 시술을 하고 싶지만, 당신이 생각하는 것과는 다르오. 지금 말해줄 테니, 다른 누구에게도 한마디도 하지 마시오. 나는 그녀의 머리를 자르고 심장을 꺼내고 싶소. 아! 외과의사인 당신이 그토록 충격을 받다니! 다른 이들을 몸서리치게 만드는 생사의 수술을 손끝 하나, 가슴 한 번 떨지 않고 해내는 것을 내가 봐온 당신이 말이오. 오, 그러나 잊어서는 안 되겠지, 나의 친애하는 벗 존, 당신이 그녀를 사랑했다는 것을. 나도 그것을 잊지 않았으니, 시술은 내가 할 것이고 당신은 거들기만 하면 되오. 오늘 밤 하고 싶지만, 아서를 생각하면 그래서는 안 되오. 그는 내일 아버지의 장례식이 끝나면 자유로워질 것이고, 그녀를 — 그것을 보고 싶어할 것이오. 그러니 그녀가 다음 날을 위해 관에 안치되거든, 모두가 잠든 뒤에 당신과 내가 옵시다. 관 뚜껑의 나사를 풀고 우리 시술을 하고, 그런 다음 모두 제자리로 돌려놓아, 우리 둘 말고는 아무도 모르게 합시다.\"\n\n\"하지만 도대체 왜 그런 일을 해야 하오? 그 처녀는 죽었소. 무슨 필요로 그 가엾은 몸을 훼손한단 말이오? 부검의 필요도 없고 그것으로 얻을 것도 없다면 — 그녀에게도, 우리에게도, 과학에도, 인간의 지식에도 아무 이로움이 없다면 — 왜 하는 거요? 그런 까닭도 없이 한다면 그건 끔찍한 일이오.\"\n\n대답 대신 그는 내 어깨에 손을 얹고, 한없는 다정함을 담아 말했다.\n\n\"벗 존, 나는 피 흘리는 당신의 가엾은 마음이 가엾소. 또 그렇게 피 흘리기에 더욱 당신을 사랑하오. 할 수만 있다면 당신이 지고 있는 짐을 내가 떠맡고 싶소. 그러나 당신이 알지 못하는 것들이 있고, 머지않아 당신은 그것을 알게 될 것이며, 비록 유쾌한 일들은 아니나, 알게 된 것을 두고 나를 축복하게 될 것이오. 존, 나의 아들 같은 이여, 당신은 이제 여러 해 동안 나의 벗이었는데, 내가 까닭 없이 무언가를 하는 것을 본 적이 있소? 내가 틀릴 수도 있소 — 나도 사람일 뿐이니. 그러나 나는 내가 하는 모든 일을 믿소. 큰 환난이 닥쳤을 때 당신이 나를 부른 것도 바로 이런 까닭에서가 아니었소? 그렇소! 내가 아서로 하여금 — 비록 그녀가 죽어가고 있었음에도 — 자기 연인에게 입 맞추지 못하게 하고 온 힘을 다해 그를 떼어냈을 때, 당신은 놀라지, 아니 질겁하지 않았소? 그렇소! 그런데도 그녀가 그토록 아름답게 죽어가는 눈으로, 또 그토록 가냘픈 목소리로 어떻게 나에게 고마워했는지, 그리고 그녀가 내 거칠고 늙은 손에 입 맞추며 나를 축복했는지 당신은 보았소? 그렇소! 또 내가 그녀에게 약속을 맹세하는 것을, 그래서 그녀가 고마워하며 눈을 감은 것을 당신은 듣지 못했소? 그렇소!\n\n자, 이제 내가 하려는 모든 일에는 그럴 만한 이유가 있소. 당신은 여러 해 동안 나를 믿어주었소. 지난 몇 주 동안, 충분히 의심할 만큼 기이한 일들이 있었을 때에도 당신은 나를 믿어주었소. 조금만 더 나를 믿어주시오, 벗 존. 당신이 나를 믿어주지 않는다면, 나는 내가 생각하는 바를 말해야 하는데, 그것은 아마 좋지 않을 것이오. 그리고 내가 일을 한다면 — 믿든 안 믿든 나는 할 테지만 — 내 벗이 나를 믿어주지 않는 채로 일을 한다면, 나는 무거운 마음으로 일하며, 오, 온갖 도움과 용기가 다 필요한 그때에 너무나도 외로움을 느낄 것이오!\" 그는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 엄숙하게 이어갔다. \"벗 존, 우리 앞에는 기이하고 무서운 날들이 놓여 있소. 우리 둘이 되지 말고 하나가 되어, 그렇게 좋은 결말을 향해 일합시다. 나를 믿어주지 않겠소?\"\n\n나는 그의 손을 잡고 그에게 약속했다. 그가 나갈 때 나는 내 방문을 열어두고, 그가 자기 방으로 들어가 문을 닫는 것을 지켜보았다. 내가 꼼짝 않고 서 있을 때, 하녀 하나가 복도를 따라 소리 없이 지나가는 것을 보았다 — 그녀는 나에게 등을 돌리고 있어 나를 보지 못했다 — 그러고는 루시가 누워 있는 방으로 들어갔다. 그 광경에 나는 마음이 뭉클했다. 헌신이란 참으로 드문 것이며, 우리가 사랑하는 이에게 청하지도 않은 헌신을 베푸는 이들에게 우리는 그토록 고마운 마음을 품게 마련이다. 여기 한 가엾은 처녀가, 죽음에 대해 당연히 품게 마련인 두려움을 떨치고, 자기가 사랑하던 여주인의 관 곁을 홀로 지키러 가고 있었으니, 그 가엾은 흙덩이가 영원한 안식에 들 때까지 외롭지 않도록 하려는 것이었다…….\n\n내가 오래도록 깊이 잠들었던 모양이다. 판 헬싱이 내 방으로 들어와 나를 깨웠을 때는 이미 환한 대낮이었으니. 그는 내 침대맡으로 다가와 말했다.\n\n\"칼은 신경 쓰지 않아도 되오. 우리는 그 일을 하지 않을 거요.\"\n\n\"왜요?\" 하고 내가 물었다. 전날 밤 그의 엄숙함이 나에게 큰 인상을 남긴 까닭이었다.\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 그가 침통하게 말했다, \"너무 늦었거나 — 아니면 너무 이르기 때문이오. 보시오!\" 여기서 그는 작은 금 십자고상을 들어 보였다. \"이것이 밤사이 도둑맞았소.\"\n\n\"도둑맞다니 어떻게요,\" 하고 내가 의아하여 물었다, \"지금 가지고 계시면서요?\"\n\n\"그것을 훔친 못된 인간에게서, 죽은 자와 산 자를 함께 턴 그 여자에게서 도로 되찾았기 때문이오. 그녀에게는 반드시 벌이 내릴 것이나, 내 손을 거치지는 않을 것이오. 그녀는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 온전히 알지 못했고, 그렇게 모른 채 그저 훔쳤을 뿐이오. 이제 우리는 기다려야 하오.\"\n\n그는 그 말을 남기고 떠나, 나에게 곱씹어야 할 새로운 수수께끼, 씨름해야 할 새로운 난제를 남겼다.\n\n오전나절은 음울한 시간이었으나, 정오에 변호사가 왔다. 홀먼·선스·마퀀드 앤드 리더데일 사(社)의 마퀀드 씨였다. 그는 매우 상냥했고 우리가 한 일을 무척 고맙게 여겼으며, 세부적인 일에 관한 온갖 수고를 우리 손에서 덜어주었다. 점심을 드는 동안 그는 웨스텐라 부인이 얼마 전부터 심장으로 인한 급사를 예감하여 자기 일을 완벽하게 정리해두었다고 말했다. 그는 우리에게, 직계 후손이 없어 이제 가문의 먼 분가로 되돌아가게 된 루시 아버지의 한정 상속 재산 일부를 제외하면, 부동산과 동산을 망라한 전 재산이 모조리 아서 홈우드에게 남겨졌다고 알려주었다. 그만큼 이야기하고 나서 그는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"솔직히 우리는 그런 식의 유언 처분을 막으려고 최선을 다했고, 따님이 무일푼이 되거나 혼인 관계에 대해 마땅히 가져야 할 만큼 자유롭게 처신하지 못하게 될 수도 있는 몇 가지 사태를 짚어 보였습니다. 사실 우리가 이 문제를 너무 강하게 밀어붙여 거의 충돌할 뻔했는데, 부인께서 우리에게 자기 뜻을 받들 작정이냐 아니냐를 물으셨기 때문입니다. 물론 그때 우리로서는 받아들이는 것 말고 다른 도리가 없었지요. 우리는 원칙적으로 옳았고, 백 번 중 아흔아홉 번은 사태가 흘러가는 논리로 보아 우리 판단의 정확함이 입증되었을 것입니다. 그러나 솔직히, 이 경우에는 다른 어떤 형태의 처분도 부인의 뜻을 이루는 것을 불가능하게 만들었으리라는 점을 인정해야겠습니다. 왜냐하면 부인께서 따님보다 먼저 돌아가심으로써 따님이 그 재산을 소유하게 되었을 터인데, 설령 따님이 어머니보다 단 오 분을 더 살았다 하더라도, 유언이 없을 경우 — 이런 경우에 유언이란 사실상 불가능한 일이지요 — 따님이 사망하면 그 재산은 무유언 상속으로 처리되었을 것이기 때문입니다. 그렇게 되면 가달밍 경께서는, 비록 그토록 가까운 벗이었음에도, 세상 어디에도 권리를 주장할 수 없었을 것이고, 먼 친척인 상속인들은 생판 남에 대한 감상적인 이유로 자기들의 정당한 권리를 포기하지는 않았을 것입니다. 단언컨대, 선생님들, 저는 이 결과를 기뻐하고 있습니다, 더없이 기뻐하고 있어요.\"\n\n그는 좋은 사람이었으나, 그토록 큰 비극 가운데 자기가 공적으로 관여한 그 작은 한 부분을 두고 기뻐하는 모습은, 공감적 이해의 한계를 보여주는 산 교훈이었다.\n\n그는 오래 머물지 않고, 그날 오후 늦게 다시 들러 가달밍 경을 만나뵙겠다고 했다. 그러나 그가 다녀간 것은 우리에게 어느 정도 위안이 되었으니, 우리가 한 어떤 행위에 대해서도 적대적인 비난을 두려워할 필요가 없으리라는 확신을 주었기 때문이다. 아서는 다섯 시에 오기로 되어 있었으므로, 우리는 그보다 조금 전에 임종의 방을 찾았다. 그것은 참으로 임종의 방이었으니, 이제 어머니와 딸이 함께 그 안에 누워 있었기 때문이다. 장의사는 자기 직분에 충실하여 자기 물품을 될 수 있는 한 잘 진열해놓았으나, 그곳에는 영안실 같은 분위기가 감돌아 우리 기운을 단번에 가라앉혔다. 판 헬싱은 전날의 배치를 그대로 유지하라고 지시하며, 가달밍 경이 곧 올 터인데 자기 약혼녀의 유해만 따로 보는 편이 그의 감정에 덜 괴로울 것이라고 설명했다. 장의사는 자기의 어리석음에 흠칫 놀란 듯 애써 우리가 전날 밤 떠날 때의 상태로 모든 것을 되돌려놓았고, 그리하여 아서가 왔을 때 우리가 막아줄 수 있는 그런 감정의 충격은 덜어졌다.\n\n가엾은 친구! 그는 절망적으로 슬프고 무너진 모습이었다. 그의 그 다부진 사내다움조차 숱하게 시달린 감정의 긴장 아래 다소 움츠러든 듯 보였다. 나는 그가 자기 아버지에게 참으로 진실하고 헌신적으로 정을 쏟았음을 알고 있었으니, 그를, 그것도 이런 때에 잃은 것은 그에게 쓰라린 타격이었다. 그는 나에게 여전히 따뜻했고, 판 헬싱에게는 다정하고 정중했다. 그러나 나는 그에게 어떤 거북함이 있는 것을 보지 않을 수 없었다. 교수도 그것을 알아차리고, 나에게 그를 위층으로 데려가라는 몸짓을 했다. 나는 그렇게 하여, 그가 그녀와 온전히 단둘이 있고 싶어하리라 여겨 방문 앞에서 그를 두고 가려 했으나, 그는 내 팔을 잡고 나를 안으로 이끌며 쉰 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"자네도 그 사람을 사랑했지, 친구. 그 사람이 나에게 다 이야기해줬어. 자네보다 그 사람 마음에 더 가까이 자리한 벗은 없었네. 그 사람을 위해 자네가 해준 모든 일을 어떻게 고마워해야 할지 모르겠어. 아직 생각이 안 돼…….\"\n\n여기서 그는 갑자기 무너져 내려, 두 팔로 내 어깨를 끌어안고 내 가슴에 머리를 묻은 채 울부짖었다.\n\n\"오, 잭! 잭! 난 어쩌면 좋아! 온 삶이 한꺼번에 나에게서 사라져버린 것 같고, 이 넓은 세상에 내가 살아갈 이유가 아무것도 없어.\"\n\n나는 할 수 있는 한 그를 위로했다. 이런 경우에 사내들은 많은 말이 필요치 않다. 손을 한 번 꽉 쥐어주는 것, 어깨를 감싼 팔에 힘을 주는 것, 함께 흐느끼는 것이 사내의 마음에 소중한 공감의 표현이다. 나는 그의 흐느낌이 잦아들 때까지 꼼짝 않고 잠자코 서 있다가, 부드럽게 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"가서 그 사람을 보게.\"\n\n우리는 함께 침대로 다가갔고, 나는 그녀의 얼굴에서 고운 천을 걷어냈다. 세상에! 그녀는 어찌나 아름다웠던지. 시간이 흐를수록 그녀의 사랑스러움이 더해가는 듯했다. 그것은 나를 다소 두렵게도, 놀랍게도 했다. 아서로 말하자면, 그는 부들부들 떨기 시작하더니 마침내 학질에 걸린 듯 의혹으로 몸을 떨었다. 마침내 그는 긴 침묵 끝에 희미한 속삭임으로 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"잭, 그 사람 정말 죽은 건가?\"\n\n나는 슬프게 그렇다고 단언하고는 — 그런 끔찍한 의혹은 내가 막을 수 있는 한 한순간이라도 더 살려두어서는 안 된다고 느꼈으므로 — 죽은 뒤에 얼굴이 누그러지고 심지어 젊은 시절의 아름다움으로 되돌아가는 일이 흔히 있으며, 죽음에 앞서 격심하거나 오래 끄는 고통이 있었던 경우에는 특히 그러하다는 점을 일러주었다. 그것으로 의혹이 깨끗이 가신 듯했고, 그는 한동안 침상 곁에 무릎 꿇고 앉아 사랑스럽게, 오래도록 그녀를 바라본 뒤 몸을 돌렸다. 나는 관을 마련해야 하니 이것이 작별이 되어야 한다고 그에게 말했다. 그러자 그는 다시 가서 그녀의 죽은 손을 자기 손에 쥐고 입 맞추고는, 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 이마에 입 맞추었다. 그는 물러나오면서 어깨 너머로 애틋하게 그녀를 돌아보았다.\n\n나는 그를 응접실에 남겨두고, 판 헬싱에게 그가 작별을 고했다고 알렸다. 그러자 판 헬싱은 부엌으로 가서 장의사 일꾼들에게 채비를 진행하여 관 뚜껑의 나사를 죄라고 일렀다. 그가 다시 방에서 나왔을 때 나는 그에게 아서의 물음을 전했고, 그는 답했다.\n\n\"놀랍지 않소. 방금 나조차 한순간 의심했으니!\"\n\n우리는 다 함께 저녁을 들었고, 가엾은 아서가 애써 마음을 다잡으려 하는 것을 나는 알 수 있었다. 판 헬싱은 저녁 내내 말이 없었으나, 우리가 시가에 불을 붙였을 때 그가 말했다.\n\n\"경(卿)…….\" 그러나 아서가 그의 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"아뇨, 아니에요, 제발 그렇게 부르지 마세요! 적어도 아직은요. 용서하세요, 선생님. 무례하게 굴려던 건 아닙니다. 다만 제가 입은 상실이 너무도 최근의 일이라서요.\"\n\n교수가 매우 다정하게 답했다.\n\n\"내가 그 이름을 쓴 것은 그저 어찌 불러야 할지 몰라서였소. '미스터'라고 부를 수는 없고, 또 나는 당신을 사랑하게 되었으니 말이오 — 그렇소, 친애하는 청년이여, 아서로서 당신을 사랑하게 되었소.\"\n\n아서는 손을 내밀어 그 노인의 손을 따뜻하게 잡았다.\n\n\"마음대로 불러주세요,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"저는 늘 벗이라는 칭호를 누리고 싶습니다. 그리고 제 가엾은 연인에게 베풀어주신 선생님의 호의에 어떻게 감사를 드려야 할지 말을 찾을 수가 없다는 것을 말씀드리고 싶어요.\" 그는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 이어갔다. \"그 사람은 저보다도 더 잘 선생님의 호의를 헤아렸을 거라는 걸 압니다. 그리고 그때 제가 선생님께서 그렇게 하실 적에 무례했거나 어떤 식으로든 모자랐다면 — 기억하시지요\" — 교수가 고개를 끄덕였다 — \"용서해주셔야 합니다.\"\n\n그가 침통한 다정함으로 답했다.\n\n\"그때 당신이 나를 온전히 믿기란 어려운 일이었음을 나도 아오. 그런 난폭함을 믿으려면 그것을 이해해야 하는데, 짐작건대 당신은 지금도 나를 믿지 못하고 — 믿을 수 없고 — 있소, 아직 이해하지 못하기 때문이오. 그리고 당신이 이해할 수 없을 때 — 이해해서는 안 될 때 — 그런데도 내가 당신에게 믿어달라고 할 때가 앞으로 또 있을지 모르오. 그러나 당신의 믿음이 나에게 온전하고 완전해질 때, 햇빛 그 자체가 환히 비치듯 당신이 이해하게 될 때가 올 것이오. 그때에는 당신이 처음부터 끝까지 나를 축복하게 될 것이니, 당신 자신을 위해, 그리고 다른 이들을 위해, 또 내가 지키기로 맹세한 그 가여운 이를 위해서 말이오.\"\n\n\"그리고, 정말로, 정말이지, 선생님,\" 하고 아서가 따뜻하게 말했다, \"저는 모든 면에서 선생님을 믿겠습니다. 선생님께서 매우 고귀한 마음을 지니셨다는 것을 알고 또 믿으며, 선생님은 잭의 벗이고, 또 그 사람의 벗이셨으니까요. 원하시는 대로 하셔도 됩니다.\"\n\n교수는 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 두어 번 목을 가다듬더니, 마침내 입을 열었다.\n\n\"이제 한 가지 물어봐도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"물론입니다.\"\n\n\"웨스텐라 부인이 당신에게 전 재산을 남겼다는 것을 아시오?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 가엾은 분. 그건 생각조차 못 했습니다.\"\n\n\"그러니 그것이 모두 당신 것이므로, 당신은 그것을 마음대로 처리할 권리가 있소. 나는 당신이 루시 양의 모든 서류와 편지를 내가 읽을 수 있도록 허락해주기를 바라오. 믿어주시오, 이것은 한가한 호기심이 아니오. 나에게는 동기가 있고, 분명히 그녀라면 그것을 인정했을 것이오. 나는 그것들을 모두 여기 가지고 있소. 모든 것이 당신 것임을 알기 전에 내가 그것들을 거두어두었으니, 낯선 손이 그것들을 만지지 못하도록 — 낯선 눈이 그 글을 꿰뚫어 그녀의 영혼을 들여다보지 못하도록 말이오. 괜찮다면 내가 그것들을 간직하겠소. 당신조차 아직은 그것들을 보아서는 안 되나, 내가 안전하게 보관하겠소. 한마디도 잃어버리는 일이 없을 것이고, 때가 무르익으면 당신에게 돌려주겠소. 어려운 부탁이긴 하나, 들어주겠지요, 루시를 위해서?\"\n\n아서가 옛 모습 그대로, 진심을 담아 또렷이 말했다.\n\n\"판 헬싱 박사님, 원하시는 대로 하셔도 됩니다. 이렇게 말씀드리는 것이 제 사랑하는 이가 인정했을 일을 하는 것이라고 느낍니다. 때가 올 때까지 저는 질문으로 선생님을 성가시게 하지 않겠습니다.\"\n\n늙은 교수가 자리에서 일어서며 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"당신 말이 옳소. 우리 모두에게 고통이 있을 것이오. 그러나 온통 고통만은 아닐 것이며, 이 고통이 마지막도 아닐 것이오. 우리도, 당신도 — 누구보다도 당신이, 친애하는 청년이여 — 단 물에 이르기 전에 쓴 물을 건너야 할 것이오. 그러나 우리는 마음이 용감하고 사심이 없어야 하며, 우리 의무를 다해야 하오. 그러면 모든 것이 잘될 것이오!\"\n\n그날 밤 나는 아서의 방에 있는 소파에서 잤다. 판 헬싱은 도무지 잠자리에 들지 않았다. 그는 집안을 순찰하듯 이리저리 오갔고, 루시가 야생 마늘꽃을 흩뿌린 채 관 속에 누워 있는 방에서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않았다. 그 마늘꽃은 백합과 장미의 향기를 뚫고, 무겁고 압도적인 냄새를 밤 속으로 내뿜고 있었다.\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」\n\n9월 22일. — 엑서터로 가는 기차 안. 조너선은 자고 있다.\n\n지난번 기록을 한 것이 바로 어제 같은데, 그 사이에 얼마나 많은 일이 있었던가. 그때는 휘트비에서, 온 세상이 내 앞에 펼쳐져 있었고, 조너선은 멀리 떠나 소식조차 없었다. 그런데 이제는 조너선과 결혼했고, 조너선은 변호사이자 공동 경영자가 되어 부유해졌으며, 자기 사업의 주인이 되었고, 호킨스 씨는 돌아가셔서 땅에 묻혔으며, 조너선은 그를 해칠지도 모를 또 한 차례의 발작을 겪었다. 언젠가 그가 나에게 그 일에 대해 물을지도 모른다. 모두 적어둔다. 내 속기 솜씨가 녹슬어버렸으니 — 뜻밖의 번영이 우리에게 무슨 짓을 하는지 보라 — 어쨌든 연습 삼아 다시 다듬어두는 것도 좋겠다…….\n\n장례식은 매우 소박하고 매우 엄숙했다. 그 자리에는 우리와 하인들, 엑서터에서 온 그의 옛 친구 한둘, 그의 런던 대리인, 그리고 법학협회 회장인 존 팩스턴 경을 대리하는 한 신사가 있을 뿐이었다. 조너선과 나는 손을 맞잡고 서서, 우리의 가장 좋고 가장 소중한 벗이 우리 곁을 떠났음을 느꼈다…….\n\n우리는 조용히 도시로 돌아와, 하이드 파크 코너까지 합승마차를 탔다. 조너선은 잠시 로(Row)에 들어가 보면 내가 흥미로워하리라 생각하여, 우리는 그곳에 앉았다. 그러나 사람이 거의 없어서, 그 많은 빈 의자들을 보는 것이 쓸쓸하고 황량했다. 그것이 우리에게 집의 빈 의자를 떠올리게 했으므로, 우리는 일어나 피커딜리를 따라 걸어 내려갔다. 조너선은 내가 학교에 들어가기 전 옛날에 그랬던 식으로 내 팔을 잡고 있었다. 나는 그것이 무척 온당치 못하다고 느꼈으니, 다른 처녀들에게 몇 해씩 예법과 품위를 가르치다 보면 그 까다로움이 자기 안에도 조금은 배어들게 마련이기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 조너선이었고, 내 남편이었으며, 우리를 보는 사람 가운데 아는 이도 없었으므로 — 또 본들 우리는 개의치 않았으므로 — 우리는 계속 걸었다. 줄리아노 상점 앞 사륜마차에 앉아 있는, 커다란 수레바퀴 같은 모자를 쓴 무척 아름다운 처녀를 바라보고 있을 때, 조너선이 어찌나 세게 내 팔을 움켜쥐는지 아플 지경이었고, 그가 숨죽여 말했다. \"맙소사!\" 나는 조너선이 늘 걱정스러우니, 어떤 신경 발작이 그를 다시 흔들어놓을까 두렵기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 얼른 그를 돌아보며 무엇이 그를 어지럽혔는지 물었다.\n\n그는 몹시 창백했고, 반쯤은 공포로 반쯤은 경악으로, 부리 같은 코에 검은 콧수염과 뾰족한 턱수염을 기른 키 크고 마른 사내를 응시하는 그의 두 눈이 튀어나올 듯했다. 그 사내 역시 그 예쁜 처녀를 지켜보고 있었다. 그는 어찌나 뚫어지게 그녀를 바라보던지 우리 둘 중 누구도 보지 못했고, 그래서 나는 그를 똑똑히 볼 수 있었다. 그의 얼굴은 좋은 얼굴이 아니었다. 굳고, 잔인하고, 음탕했으며, 입술이 어찌나 붉은지 더욱 희게 보이는 큼직한 흰 이는 짐승의 것처럼 뾰족했다. 조너선이 어찌나 그를 빤히 응시하던지, 나는 그가 알아챌까 봐 두려웠다. 그가 그토록 사납고 험상궂어 보여서, 그것을 언짢게 여기지나 않을까 겁이 났다. 나는 조너선에게 왜 그렇게 동요하는지 물었고, 그는 마치 내가 자기만큼이나 그 일을 다 알고 있다고 여기는 듯 답했다. \"저자가 누군지 보이오?\"\n\n\"아뇨, 여보,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"저 사람을 모르겠어요. 누구예요?\" 그의 대답은 나를 충격에 빠뜨리고 전율케 하는 듯했으니, 마치 자기가 말을 건네는 상대가 나, 미나라는 것을 모르는 듯이 한 말이었기 때문이다.\n\n\"바로 그자요!\"\n\n가엾은 사람은 무언가에 분명 겁에 질려 있었다 — 몹시도 겁에 질려 있었다. 정말이지 그가 기댈, 그리고 그를 떠받칠 내가 곁에 없었더라면 그는 주저앉고 말았으리라고 나는 믿는다. 그는 줄곧 응시했다. 한 사내가 작은 꾸러미를 들고 가게에서 나와 그것을 그 부인에게 건네자, 부인은 마차를 몰고 떠났다. 그 검은 사내는 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않았고, 마차가 피커딜리를 따라 올라가자 같은 방향으로 뒤따라가 이륜마차를 불러 세웠다. 조너선은 줄곧 그를 눈으로 좇으며, 혼잣말하듯 말했다.\n\n\"저자는 백작인 것 같소, 그런데 젊어졌소. 맙소사, 만일 그렇다면! 오, 맙소사! 맙소사! 알기만 한다면! 알기만 한다면!\" 그가 어찌나 스스로를 괴롭히는지, 나는 무슨 질문이라도 하여 그의 마음을 그 일에 붙들어둘까 두려워 잠자코 있었다. 나는 조용히 그를 이끌었고, 그는 내 팔을 잡고 순순히 따라왔다. 우리는 조금 더 걷다가 그린 파크로 들어가 한동안 앉아 있었다. 가을치고는 더운 날이었고, 그늘진 곳에 편안한 자리가 있었다. 몇 분간 허공을 응시하던 조너선의 눈이 감기더니, 그는 내 어깨에 머리를 기댄 채 조용히 잠으로 빠져들었다. 나는 그것이 그에게 가장 좋은 일이라 여겨 깨우지 않았다. 이십 분쯤 지나 그는 깨어나 매우 명랑하게 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"아니, 미나, 내가 잠들었던 거요! 오, 이렇게 무례해서 미안하오. 자, 어디서 차나 한 잔 합시다.\" 그는 자기 병중에 이 일이 떠올리게 한 모든 것을 잊어버렸던 것처럼, 그 검은 낯선 자에 대한 일을 까맣게 잊은 것이 분명했다. 나는 이렇게 망각으로 빠져드는 것이 마음에 들지 않으니, 뇌에 어떤 손상을 일으키거나 그것을 지속시킬지도 모르기 때문이다. 나는 그에게 물어서는 안 되겠으니, 이로움보다 해를 더 끼칠까 두려운 까닭이다. 그러나 나는 어떻게든 그의 해외 여정에 관한 사실을 알아내야만 한다. 두렵지만, 이제 그 꾸러미를 열어 거기 무엇이 쓰여 있는지 알아야 할 때가 온 듯하다. 오, 조너선, 내가 잘못을 저지른다면 당신은 나를 용서하리라는 걸 알아요. 그러나 이것은 다 당신을 위한 거예요.\n\n나중에. — 모든 면에서 슬픈 귀가였다 — 우리에게 그토록 잘해주었던 그 소중한 분이 떠나 텅 빈 집, 가벼운 병의 재발로 여전히 창백하고 어지러워하는 조너선. 그리고 이제 판 헬싱이라는 — 그가 누구인지는 모르나 — 사람으로부터 온 전보 한 통.\n\n\"웨스텐라 부인이 닷새 전에, 그리고 루시가 그저께 세상을 떠났다는 소식을 들으시면 비통하실 것입니다. 두 분 다 오늘 묻혔습니다.\"\n\n오, 몇 마디 말 속에 담긴 이 풍성한 슬픔이여! 가엾은 웨스텐라 부인! 가엾은 루시! 가버렸구나, 가버렸어, 다시는 우리에게 돌아오지 못하리! 그리고 가엾고 가엾은 아서, 자기 삶에서 그토록 고운 존재를 잃다니! 우리 모두가 우리의 시련을 견딜 수 있도록 신이여 도우소서.\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n9월 22일. — 모두 끝났다. 아서는 링으로 돌아갔고, 퀸시 모리스를 데리고 갔다. 퀸시는 어찌나 훌륭한 사내인지! 나는 진심으로, 그가 루시의 죽음에 대해 우리 가운데 누구 못지않게 괴로워했다고 믿는다. 그러나 그는 그것을 도덕적인 바이킹처럼 의연히 견뎌냈다. 미국이 그런 사내들을 계속 길러낼 수 있다면, 그 나라는 참으로 세계의 강국이 될 것이다. 판 헬싱은 여정에 앞서 휴식을 취하느라 누워 있다. 그는 오늘 밤 암스테르담으로 건너가지만, 내일 밤에는 돌아온다고 한다. 직접 가서야만 할 수 있는 몇 가지 처리를 하려는 것뿐이라고 했다. 그런 뒤에는 가능하다면 나와 함께 머물 것이라고 했다. 런던에서 할 일이 있는데 시간이 좀 걸릴지도 모른다고 했다. 가엾은 노인! 나는 지난 한 주의 긴장이 그의 무쇠 같은 힘마저 꺾어놓았을까 두렵다. 매장하는 내내 그가 자기 자신에게 어떤 끔찍한 억제를 가하고 있는 것을 나는 볼 수 있었다. 모든 것이 끝났을 때, 우리는 아서 곁에 서 있었는데, 그 가엾은 친구는 자기 피가 루시의 혈관으로 수혈되었던 그 시술에서 자기가 한 역할에 대해 이야기하고 있었다. 나는 판 헬싱의 얼굴이 번갈아 하얘졌다 보랏빛이 되었다 하는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 아서는 그때 이후로 자기들 둘이 정말로 결혼한 것 같고 신의 눈에 그녀가 자기 아내인 것 같다고 말하고 있었다. 우리 가운데 누구도 다른 수혈들에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 우리 누구도 결코 하지 않을 것이다. 아서와 퀸시는 함께 역으로 떠났고, 판 헬싱과 나는 이곳으로 왔다. 마차 안에 단둘이 되자마자 그는 영락없는 히스테리 발작에 굴복하고 말았다. 그 뒤로 그는 나에게 그것이 히스테리였음을 부인하며, 그저 매우 끔찍한 상황 아래 자기 유머 감각이 고개를 든 것뿐이었다고 우겼다. 그는 눈물이 날 때까지 웃었고, 나는 누군가 우리를 보고 오해할까 봐 차양을 내려야 했다. 그러다가 그는 다시 웃을 때까지 울었고, 마치 여자가 그러듯 웃고 우는 것을 한꺼번에 했다. 나는 이런 상황에서 여자를 대하듯 그에게 엄하게 대하려 했으나, 아무 소용이 없었다. 신경의 강함이나 약함을 드러내는 방식에서 남자와 여자는 어찌나 다른지! 그러다가 그의 얼굴이 다시 침통하고 엄숙해졌을 때 나는 그에게 무엇 때문에 그렇게 즐거워하는지, 또 왜 하필 이런 때에 그러는지 물었다. 그의 대답은 어떤 면에서 그다운 것이었으니, 논리적이고 강력하며 알 수 없는 것이었기 때문이다. 그가 말했다.\n\n\"아, 당신은 이해하지 못하오, 벗 존. 내가 웃는다고 해서 슬프지 않다고는 생각지 마시오. 보시오, 웃음이 나를 목 메이게 할 때조차 나는 울었소. 그러나 내가 울 때 온통 슬프기만 하다고도 더는 생각지 마시오. 웃음이란 놈은 마찬가지로 찾아오니 말이오. 당신 문을 두드리며 '들어가도 될까요?' 하고 말하는 웃음은 진정한 웃음이 아니라는 것을 늘 명심하시오. 아니오! 그놈은 왕이라서, 자기가 원할 때 원하는 식으로 찾아오오. 그놈은 누구에게도 청하지 않고, 알맞은 때를 고르지도 않소. 그놈은 그저 '나 여기 있소' 하고 말하오. 보시오, 예를 들면 나는 그토록 사랑스러운 젊은 처녀를 위해 가슴이 미어지도록 슬퍼하오. 나는 늙고 지쳤음에도 그녀를 위해 내 피를 주었소. 내 시간을, 내 솜씨를, 내 잠을 주었소. 그녀가 모든 것을 가질 수 있도록 내 다른 환자들이 아쉬워하게 두었소. 그런데도 나는 바로 그녀의 무덤에서 웃을 수 있소 — 무덤 파는 인부의 삽에서 흙덩이가 그녀의 관 위로 떨어지며 내 가슴을 향해 '쿵! 쿵!' 하고, 마침내 내 뺨에서 핏기를 물리칠 때조차 웃을 수 있소. 내 가슴은 그 가엾은 청년을 위해 피 흘리오 — 만일 내가 복을 받아 내 아들이 살아 있었더라면 꼭 내 아들 또래였을, 머리칼도 눈도 똑같은 그 사랑스러운 청년을 위해서 말이오. 자, 당신은 이제 내가 왜 그를 그토록 사랑하는지 알겠지. 그런데도 그가 내 남편의 가슴을 깊이 찌르는 말을, 또 다른 누구에게도 — 심지어 당신에게도 그렇지 않을 만큼, 벗 존, 우리는 부자지간보다는 경험에 있어 더 대등하니 — 내 아버지의 가슴을 그에게로 사무치게 만드는 말을 할 때조차, 바로 그런 순간에도 웃음의 왕이 나에게 찾아와 내 귀에 대고 외치고 고함치오, '나 여기 있소! 나 여기 있소!' 하고, 마침내 피가 다시 춤추며 돌아와 그놈이 함께 지니고 다니는 햇빛을 얼마쯤 내 뺨으로 가져올 때까지 말이오. 오, 벗 존, 이것은 기이한 세상, 슬픈 세상, 비참함과 비탄과 환난으로 가득한 세상이오. 그런데도 웃음의 왕이 찾아오면 그놈은 그 모두를 자기가 연주하는 가락에 맞추어 춤추게 만든다오. 피 흘리는 가슴이며, 교회 묘지의 마른 뼈며, 떨어지며 타들어가는 눈물이며 — 그 모두가 그놈이 웃음기 없는 입으로 빚어내는 음악에 맞추어 함께 춤춘다오. 그리고 믿어주시오, 벗 존, 그놈이 찾아오는 것은 좋은 일이고 친절한 일이오. 아, 우리 남자와 여자는 서로 다른 방향으로 잡아당기는 긴장으로 팽팽히 당겨진 밧줄과도 같소. 그러면 눈물이 나오. 그리고 밧줄에 내리는 비처럼, 눈물은 우리를 다잡아주오, 아마 긴장이 너무 커져 우리가 끊어질 때까지 말이오. 그러나 웃음의 왕은 햇빛처럼 찾아와 다시 그 긴장을 풀어주오. 그러면 우리는 그것이 무엇이든 우리의 수고를 계속 견뎌내게 되오.\"\n\n나는 그의 생각을 알아듣지 못한 척하여 그에게 상처를 주고 싶지 않았다. 그러나 나는 아직 그가 웃은 까닭을 이해하지 못했으므로 그에게 물었다. 그가 나에게 답할 때 그의 얼굴은 엄숙해졌고, 그는 전혀 다른 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그 모든 것의 그 음울한 아이러니 말이오 — 그토록 사랑스러운 부인이 꽃으로 단장한 채, 살아 있는 듯이 어찌나 고와 보이던지 우리가 하나씩 하나씩 그녀가 정말로 죽은 것인지 의아해할 정도였소. 그녀가 그 외딴 교회 묘지의 그토록 훌륭한 대리석 집에, 그녀의 일가가 그토록 많이 잠들어 있는 곳에, 그녀를 사랑했고 그녀가 사랑했던 어머니와 함께 눕혀졌소. 그리고 그 거룩한 종이 '뎅! 뎅! 뎅!' 하고 그토록 슬프고 느리게 울리고, 천사의 흰옷을 걸친 그 거룩한 사람들이 책을 읽는 척하면서도 줄곧 그 눈은 책장에 가 있지 않고, 우리 모두는 머리를 숙이고 있었소. 그리고 그 모두가 무엇을 위해서였소? 그녀는 죽었소. 그러니! 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n\"글쎄요, 정말이지, 교수님,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"저는 그 모든 것에서 웃을 만한 구석을 도무지 찾을 수가 없습니다. 아니, 선생님의 설명은 그것을 전보다 더 풀기 어려운 수수께끼로 만들 뿐이에요. 그러나 설령 장례 예식이 우스꽝스러웠다 한들, 가엾은 아서와 그의 슬픔은 어쩌고요? 아니, 그의 가슴은 그야말로 미어지고 있었는데요.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소. 자기 피를 그녀의 혈관에 수혈한 것이 그녀를 진정한 자기 신부로 만들었다고 그가 말하지 않았소?\"\n\n\"그랬지요, 그리고 그것은 그에게 달콤하고 위안이 되는 생각이었습니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소. 그러나 거기에 한 가지 난처한 점이 있소, 벗 존. 만일 그렇다면, 다른 이들은 어찌 되는 거요? 호, 호! 그렇다면 그 그토록 사랑스러운 처녀는 일처다부의 여인이고, 또 나는, 내 가엾은 아내가 나에게는 죽은 것이나 교회법으로는 살아 있고, 정신이 다 나가버렸음에도 그러하니 — 이제는 아내 아닌 그 사람에게 충실한 남편인 나조차, 중혼자(重婚者)인 셈이오.\"\n\n\"저는 거기서도 무엇이 우스운지 모르겠습니다!\" 하고 내가 말했다. 또 나는 그가 그런 말을 하는 것이 그리 달갑지 않았다. 그는 내 팔에 손을 얹고 말했다.\n\n\"벗 존, 내가 아프게 했다면 용서하시오. 나는 다른 이들에게는, 상처가 될 만한 때에는 내 감정을 드러내지 않고, 오직 당신에게만, 내가 믿을 수 있는 나의 오랜 벗에게만 드러내오. 만일 내가 웃고 싶었던 그때 당신이 바로 그 내 가슴속을 들여다볼 수 있었더라면, 만일 웃음이 찾아왔을 때 그럴 수 있었더라면, 만일 지금, 웃음의 왕이 자기 왕관과 자기에게 딸린 모든 것을 챙겨 떠난 — 그놈은 멀리, 멀리, 그것도 오래, 오래도록 나에게서 떠나가니 — 지금 당신이 그럴 수 있다면, 아마 당신은 누구보다도 나를 가장 가엾게 여길 것이오.\"\n\n나는 그의 어조에 담긴 다정함에 마음이 뭉클하여 그 까닭을 물었다.\n\n\"내가 알기 때문이오!\"\n\n그리고 이제 우리는 모두 뿔뿔이 흩어졌다. 그리고 오랜 세월 외로움이 음울한 날개를 펼치고 우리의 지붕 위에 둥지를 틀 것이다. 루시는 그녀 일가의 무덤에, 붐비는 런던에서 멀리 떨어진 외딴 교회 묘지의 한 으리으리한 죽음의 집에 누워 있다. 그곳은 공기가 맑고, 햄스테드 언덕 위로 해가 떠오르며, 들꽃이 절로 자라는 곳이다.\n\n이로써 나는 이 일기를 끝맺을 수 있다. 그리고 내가 또 다른 일기를 시작하게 될지는 오직 신만이 아신다. 만일 그러게 된다면, 혹은 이 일기를 다시 펼치게 되더라도, 그것은 다른 사람들과 다른 주제를 다루기 위해서일 것이다. 내 삶의 로맨스가 이야기된 이 끝자락에서, 내 필생의 일이라는 실타래를 다시 집어 들기에 앞서, 나는 슬프게, 또 희망 없이 말한다.\n\n\"끝.\"\n「웨스트민스터 가제트」, 9월 25일.\n\n# A HAMPSTEAD MYSTERY.\n\n햄스테드의 괴사건.\n요즘 들어 햄스테드 인근은, 표제 기사를 쓰는 이들에게 \"켄싱턴의 공포\"라거나 \"찌르는 여인\", \"검은 옷의 여인\"으로 알려졌던 사건들과 비슷한 양상으로 흘러가는 일련의 사건들로 떠들썩하다. 지난 이삼일 동안 어린아이들이 집에서 멀리 벗어나거나 히스에서 놀다가 돌아오지 않는 경우가 여러 건 발생했다. 이 모든 경우에 아이들은 너무 어려서 자기 일을 제대로 알아들을 만하게 설명하지 못했으나, 그 변명들의 공통점은 자기들이 \"예쁜 부인\"과 함께 있었다는 것이었다. 아이들이 보이지 않게 된 것은 언제나 저녁 늦은 시각이었고, 두 차례는 이튿날 이른 아침에야 발견되었다. 인근에서는 대체로, 처음 사라진 아이가 자기가 떠나 있었던 이유로 \"예쁜 부인\"이 함께 산책 가자고 했다는 것을 댔으므로, 다른 아이들이 그 말을 주워듣고 형편 닿는 대로 써먹은 것이라고들 추측한다. 이것이 더욱 자연스러운 까닭은, 요즘 어린것들이 가장 좋아하는 놀이가 서로를 꾀어내어 데려가는 것이기 때문이다. 한 통신원이 우리에게 알려오기를, 그 조그만 꼬마들 가운데 몇몇이 \"예쁜 부인\" 흉내를 내는 것을 보면 더없이 우습다고 한다. 그는, 우리 풍자화가 가운데 더러는 그 실제 모습과 그림을 견주어봄으로써 기괴한 것의 아이러니에 관해 한 수 배울 수도 있겠다고 말한다. \"예쁜 부인\"이 이런 야외 공연에서 인기 있는 배역이 되는 것은 다만 인간 본성의 일반 원리에 들어맞을 뿐이다. 우리 통신원은 천연덕스럽게 말하기를, 엘런 테리조차 이 꾀죄죄한 얼굴의 조그만 아이들이 흉내 내는 — 심지어 스스로 그렇다고 여기기까지 하는 — 그 모습만큼 그렇게 사랑스럽도록 매력적일 수는 없으리라고 한다.\n\n그러나 이 문제에는 어쩌면 심각한 측면이 있을지도 모르니, 아이들 가운데 몇몇은, 사실 밤에 사라졌던 아이들 모두는 목에 살짝 찢기거나 다친 상처가 있었기 때문이다. 그 상처들은 쥐나 작은 개가 냈을 법한 것으로, 하나하나로는 그리 대수롭지 않으나, 그것들을 입히는 짐승이 무엇이든 그 나름의 체계나 방식을 지니고 있음을 보여주는 듯하다. 관할 구역 경찰은 햄스테드 히스 안팎에서 길 잃은 아이들, 특히 아주 어린아이들을, 또 주변에 있을지 모를 떠돌이 개를 면밀히 살피라는 지시를 받았다.\n\n「웨스트민스터 가제트」, 9월 25일.\n특별 호외.\n\n햄스테드의 공포.\n\n또 한 아이가 다치다.\n\n\"예쁜 부인.\"\n방금 우리에게 들어온 소식에 따르면, 어젯밤 사라졌던 또 한 아이가 오늘 아침 늦게야, 햄스테드 히스의 슈터스 힐 쪽 — 아마 다른 구역들보다 인적이 덜한 곳일 — 가시금작화 덤불 아래에서 발견되었다고 한다. 그 아이의 목에는 다른 경우들에서 눈에 띈 것과 똑같은 조그만 상처가 있었다. 아이는 몹시 쇠약했고 무척 야위어 보였다. 그 아이 역시, 어느 정도 기운을 차린 뒤에는 \"예쁜 부인\"에게 꾀여 끌려갔다는 그 흔한 이야기를 들려주었다.\n\n# MINA HARKER’S JOURNAL\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」\n9월 23일. — 조너선이 험한 밤을 보낸 뒤 한결 나아졌다. 그에게 할 일이 많다는 것이 어찌나 다행인지, 그 덕에 끔찍한 일들에서 마음을 돌릴 수 있으니 말이다. 그리고 아, 이제 그가 새 지위의 책무에 짓눌리지 않게 된 것이 나는 기쁘기 그지없다. 나는 그가 자기 본분에 충실하리라는 것을 알고 있었고, 이제 나의 조너선이 출세의 정점에 올라 자신에게 주어진 온갖 임무에 발맞추어 나아가는 것을 보니 어찌나 자랑스러운지. 그는 종일 밖에 있다가 늦게야 돌아올 텐데, 점심도 집에서 들 수 없다고 했다. 집안일은 다 마쳤으니, 그의 외국 일기를 가져다 방에 틀어박혀 읽어보아야겠다…….\n\n9월 24일. — 어젯밤에는 도무지 쓸 마음이 나지 않았다. 조너선의 그 끔찍한 기록이 나를 너무도 뒤흔들어 놓았기 때문이다. 가엾은 사람! 그것이 사실이든 한낱 상상이든, 그가 얼마나 고통받았을 것인가. 그 안에 조금이라도 진실이 있는 것일까. 그가 뇌염을 앓고 나서 그 모든 끔찍한 것들을 적은 것일까, 아니면 그 모든 일에 어떤 까닭이 있었던 것일까. 아마 나는 결코 알 수 없으리라, 감히 그에게 그 이야기를 꺼낼 수 없으니……. 그런데 어제 우리가 본 그 남자라니! 그는 그를 꽤 확신하는 듯했다……. 가엾은 사람! 짐작건대 장례식이 그를 뒤흔들어 어떤 생각의 줄기를 떠올리게 만든 것이리라……. 그는 그 모든 것을 스스로 믿고 있다. 우리 혼인날에 그가 했던 말이 떠오른다. \"잠들었든 깨어 있든, 미쳤든 제정신이든, 그 쓰라린 시간으로 되돌아가야 할 어떤 엄숙한 의무가 나에게 닥치지 않는 한.\" 그 모든 것을 관통하는 어떤 연속의 실마리가 있는 듯하다……. 그 무서운 백작이 런던으로 오고 있었다니……. 만일 그것이 사실이라면, 그래서 그가 우글거리는 수백만의 무리 속으로 런던에 왔다면……. 어떤 엄숙한 의무가 있을지도 모른다. 그리고 그것이 닥치거든 우리는 결코 움츠려서는 안 된다……. 나는 준비해 두리라. 지금 당장 타자기를 가져다 옮겨 적기를 시작하겠다. 그러면 필요할 때 다른 이들의 눈에 대비할 수 있으리라. 그리고 만일 그것이 쓰이게 된다면, 그때 내가 준비되어 있다면, 가엾은 조너선이 동요하지 않아도 될지 모른다. 내가 그를 대신해 말할 수 있고, 그가 그것 때문에 괴로워하거나 시달리지 않게 할 수 있으니. 언젠가 조너선이 그 신경증을 완전히 떨쳐낸다면 그 모든 것을 나에게 들려주고 싶어 할지도 모르고, 그러면 나는 그에게 이것저것 물어 사실을 알아내고, 어떻게 그를 위로할 수 있을지 헤아릴 수 있으리라.\n「판 헬싱이 하커 부인에게 보낸 편지」\n\n\"9월 24일.\n\n(친전)\n\n친애하는 부인께,\n\n루시 웨스텐라 양의 부고라는 슬픈 소식을 전해 드린 친구의 자격으로 이렇게 편지를 쓰는 것을 용서해 주시기를 바라오. 고댈밍 경의 호의로 나는 그녀의 편지와 서류들을 읽을 권한을 얻었는데, 더없이 중요한 어떤 문제들에 깊이 마음을 쓰고 있는 까닭이오. 그 가운데에서 부인이 보낸 편지 몇 통을 발견했고, 두 분이 얼마나 가까운 벗이었으며 부인이 그녀를 얼마나 사랑했는지를 알게 되었소. 아, 미나 부인, 그 사랑을 두고 간청하오니, 나를 도와주시오. 내가 청하는 것은 다른 이들의 선을 위함이니 — 큰 잘못을 바로잡고, 부인이 알 수 있는 것보다 어쩌면 더 크고 끔찍한 화근을 거두어 들이기 위함이오. 부인을 뵐 수 있겠소? 나를 믿으셔도 좋소. 나는 존 수어드 박사와 고댈밍 경(루시 양의 아서였던 사람)의 벗이오. 지금으로서는 이 일을 모두에게 비밀로 해 두어야 하오. 내가 가도 좋다는 특전과 더불어 어디로, 언제 가야 할지 일러 주신다면, 나는 곧장 엑서터로 부인을 뵈러 가겠소. 부인, 너그러이 용서해 주시기를 비오. 나는 가엾은 루시에게 보낸 부인의 편지들을 읽었고, 부인이 얼마나 선한지, 부군이 얼마나 고통받는지를 아오. 그러니 비오니, 가능하다면 부군께는 알리지 마시오, 해가 될까 두려운 까닭이오. 다시 한 번 용서를 구하며, 나를 너그러이 보아 주시오.\n판 헬싱.\"\n\n「하커 부인이 판 헬싱에게 보낸 전보」\n\n\"9월 25일. — 가능하시다면 오늘 10시 15분 기차로 오십시오. 들르시는 어느 때든 뵐 수 있습니다.\n빌헬미나 하커.\"\n\n# MINA HARKER’S JOURNAL.\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」\n9월 25일. — 판 헬싱 박사의 방문이 다가올수록 나는 몹시 들뜬 마음을 가눌 수가 없으니, 어쩐지 그것이 조너선의 슬픈 경험에 어떤 빛을 던져 주리라는 기대가 들기 때문이다. 또한 그가 가엾은 루시의 마지막 병환을 돌보았으니, 그녀에 관해 모든 것을 일러 줄 수 있으리라. 그가 오는 까닭이 바로 그것이다. 조너선에 관한 일이 아니라 루시와 그녀의 몽유에 관한 일인 것이다. 그렇다면 나는 이제 진실을 결코 알 수 없으리라! 내가 어쩌면 이리 어리석은가. 그 끔찍한 일기가 내 상상을 사로잡아 모든 것을 제 빛깔로 물들이는구나. 물론 루시에 관한 일이다. 그 가엾은 사람에게 옛 버릇이 도졌고, 절벽 위에서의 그 무서운 밤이 그녀를 병들게 했음에 틀림없다. 내 일에 정신이 팔려, 그 뒤로 그녀가 얼마나 아팠는지조차 거의 잊고 있었다. 그녀는 절벽에서의 몽유 사건과 내가 그 모든 것을 안다는 사실을 그에게 이야기했을 것이다. 그래서 그는 이제 그가 이해할 수 있도록, 그녀가 아는 것을 내게서 듣고 싶어 하는 것이다. 그것을 웨스텐라 부인께 한마디도 하지 않은 것이 옳았기를 바란다. 내 어떤 행위가, 그것이 설령 하지 않은 행위라 할지라도, 가엾은 루시에게 해를 끼쳤다면 나는 결코 나 자신을 용서할 수 없으리라. 또한 판 헬싱 박사가 나를 탓하지 않기를 바란다. 근래 너무도 많은 근심과 시름을 겪은 터라, 지금으로서는 더 이상은 견딜 수 없을 것만 같다.\n\n이따금 한바탕 우는 것이 우리 모두에게 좋은 듯하다 — 비가 그러하듯 공기를 맑게 해 주는 것이다. 어제 일기를 읽은 것이 나를 뒤흔든 탓일 수도 있고, 또 오늘 아침 조너선이 떠나 꼬박 하루 낮밤을 나와 떨어져 있게 된 탓일 수도 있다. 혼인한 뒤로 우리가 헤어진 것은 이번이 처음이다. 부디 그 사람이 몸조심하기를, 그리고 그를 뒤흔들 어떤 일도 일어나지 않기를 빈다. 두 시다. 이제 곧 박사가 도착할 것이다. 그가 묻지 않는 한 조너선의 일기에 관해서는 아무 말도 하지 않으리라. 내 일기를 타자로 옮겨 둔 것이 어찌나 다행인지, 그가 루시에 관해 묻거든 그것을 건네줄 수 있을 테니, 많은 물음을 덜 수 있으리라.\n\n이후. — 그가 왔다 갔다. 아, 이 얼마나 기이한 만남인가, 그리고 이 모든 것이 내 머리를 어찌나 어지럽게 하는지! 나는 꿈을 꾸는 사람 같다. 이 모든 것이, 아니 그 일부라도 정말 있을 수 있는 일일까? 만일 내가 조너선의 일기를 먼저 읽지 않았더라면 그 가능성조차 결코 받아들이지 못했으리라. 가엾고 가엾은, 사랑하는 조너선! 그가 얼마나 고통받았을 것인가. 부디 선하신 하느님께서, 이 모든 일이 그를 다시 뒤흔들지 않게 하시기를. 나는 그것으로부터 그를 지키려 애쓰리라. 그러나 어쩌면 그것은 그에게 위안이자 도움이 될지도 모른다 — 비록 끔찍하고 그 여파가 무섭기는 하나 — 자기 눈과 귀와 머리가 자기를 속이지 않았으며, 그것이 모두 사실임을 확실히 아는 것은 말이다. 어쩌면 그를 괴롭히는 것은 의심일지도 모른다. 그 의심이 걷히고 나면, 깨어 있을 때든 꿈속에서든 어느 쪽이 진실로 드러나든, 그는 더 흡족해하고 그 충격을 더 잘 견딜 수 있게 될지도 모른다. 판 헬싱 박사가 아서의 벗이자 수어드 박사의 벗이고, 그들이 루시를 돌보게 하려 그를 네덜란드에서 그 먼 길을 데려온 것이라면, 그는 영리한 사람일 뿐 아니라 선한 사람임에 틀림없다. 그를 만나 보니, 그가 선하고 다정하며 고결한 천성을 지닌 사람임이 느껴진다. 그가 내일 오거든 나는 조너선에 관해 물어보리라. 그러면, 하느님이 도우신다면, 이 모든 슬픔과 시름이 좋은 끝으로 이어질지도 모른다. 나는 한때 인터뷰하는 일을 익혀 보고 싶다고 생각하곤 했다. 「엑서터 뉴스」에 있는 조너선의 친구가 그에게 말하기를, 그런 일에는 기억이 전부라고 했다 — 나중에 다듬어야 할지언정 거의 모든 말을 빠짐없이 정확히 받아 적을 수 있어야 한다고. 여기 보기 드문 인터뷰가 있었으니, 나는 그것을 그대로 옮겨 적어 보려 한다.\n\n문을 두드리는 소리가 난 것은 두 시 반이었다. 나는 두 손으로 용기를 그러쥐고 기다렸다. 몇 분 뒤 메리가 문을 열고 \"판 헬싱 박사님\"이라고 알렸다.\n\n나는 일어서서 절을 했고, 그가 나에게로 다가왔다. 중키에 다부진 몸집의 사내로, 넓고 깊은 가슴 위로 어깨를 곧게 젖혔으며, 목은 머리가 목 위에 얹히듯 몸통 위에 균형 있게 얹혀 있었다. 머리를 가누는 자세는 보는 이로 하여금 곧바로 사색과 힘을 떠올리게 한다. 머리는 고결하고, 크기가 알맞으며, 넓고, 귀 뒤쪽이 크다. 말끔히 면도한 얼굴에는 단단한 사각 턱, 크고 단호하며 잘 움직이는 입, 꽤 곧되 빠르고 예민한 콧구멍을 지닌 큼직한 코가 드러나 있는데, 그 콧구멍은 크고 무성한 눈썹이 내려오고 입이 다물릴 때면 벌어지는 듯하다. 이마는 넓고 수려하여, 처음에는 거의 곧추 솟다가 멀찍이 벌어진 두 개의 혹 혹은 융기 위로 뒤로 비스듬히 물러나는데, 그런 이마인지라 불그스름한 머리칼이 도무지 그 위로 흘러내리지 못하고 자연히 뒤와 옆으로 넘어간다. 크고 짙푸른 눈은 멀찍이 벌어져 있으며, 그 사내의 기분에 따라 재빠르고 부드럽거나 준엄하다. 그가 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"하커 부인이시지요?\" 나는 그렇다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다.\n\n\"미나 머리 양이셨고요?\" 나는 다시 그렇다고 했다.\n\n\"내가 만나러 온 사람은, 그 가엾은 루시 웨스텐라 양의 벗이었던 미나 머리요. 미나 부인, 내가 온 것은 고인 때문이오.\"\n\n\"선생님,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"선생님이 루시 웨스텐라의 벗이자 조력자였다는 것보다 더 좋은 자격으로 저에게 다가오실 수는 없을 거예요.\" 그러고는 손을 내밀었다. 그는 그 손을 잡고 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"아, 미나 부인, 나는 그 가엾은 백합 같은 아가씨의 벗이라면 분명 선한 사람이리라 알고 있었소만, 아직 모르고 있던 것이 있었으니——\" 그는 정중한 인사로 말을 맺었다. 무슨 일로 나를 보러 오셨느냐고 묻자, 그는 곧바로 시작했다.\n\n\"나는 부인이 루시 양에게 보낸 편지들을 읽었소. 용서하시오, 그러나 나는 어디선가 알아보기를 시작해야 했고, 물어볼 사람이 아무도 없었소. 부인이 휘트비에서 그녀와 함께 있었다는 것을 아오. 그녀는 이따금 일기를 썼는데 — 놀란 표정을 지을 것 없소, 미나 부인. 부인이 떠난 뒤에 부인을 본떠 시작한 것이오 — 그 일기에서 그녀는 어떤 일들을 어떤 몽유에서 비롯된 것으로 미루어 적으면서, 부인이 자기를 구해 주었다고 써 놓았소. 그래서 나는 몹시 막막한 가운데 부인을 찾아와, 그토록 다정하신 마음으로 기억나는 것을 모두 들려주십사 청하는 것이오.\"\n\n\"제 생각에는, 판 헬싱 박사님, 그 일에 관해 모두 말씀드릴 수 있을 것 같습니다.\"\n\n\"아, 그렇다면 사실과 세부를 잘 기억하시는군요. 젊은 부인들이 다 그렇지는 않은데.\"\n\n\"아니에요, 박사님. 그때 모두 적어 두었거든요. 원하시면 보여 드릴 수 있어요.\"\n\n\"오, 미나 부인, 고맙겠소. 큰 호의를 베푸시는 것이오.\" 나는 그를 조금 어리둥절하게 만들고 싶은 유혹을 떨칠 수가 없었다 — 짐작건대 그 최초의 사과 맛이 우리 입속에 아직 조금 남아 있는 탓이리라 — 그래서 속기로 쓴 일기를 그에게 건넸다. 그는 고마운 인사와 함께 그것을 받아들고 말했다.\n\n\"읽어 봐도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"원하신다면요.\" 나는 되도록 시치미를 떼며 답했다. 그가 그것을 펼치자, 한순간 그의 얼굴이 일그러졌다. 그러더니 일어서서 절을 했다.\n\n\"오, 참으로 영리하신 부인이구려!\" 그가 말했다. \"나는 오래전부터 조너선 씨가 감사할 줄 아는 사람임을 알고 있었소만, 보시오, 그 부인이 좋은 것을 다 지녔구려. 나에게 이리 큰 영광을, 이리 큰 도움을 베풀어 이것을 나 대신 읽어 주지 않겠소? 아아! 나는 속기를 모르오.\" 이쯤 되자 내 작은 장난도 끝났고, 나는 거의 부끄러워졌다. 그래서 반짇고리에서 타자로 친 사본을 꺼내 그에게 건넸다.\n\n\"용서하세요,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"어쩔 수가 없었어요. 그런데 저는 박사님이 물으려 하시는 것이 사랑스러운 루시에 관한 일이리라 생각했고, 그래서 기다리느라 시간을 허비하지 않으시도록 — 저를 위해서가 아니라, 박사님의 시간이 분명 귀하리라는 것을 아는 까닭에 — 박사님을 위해 타자기로 옮겨 두었어요.\"\n\n그는 그것을 받아들었고, 그의 눈이 반짝였다. \"참으로 선하시구려,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"지금 읽어 봐도 되겠소? 다 읽고 나면 몇 가지 여쭐 것이 있을지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"얼마든지요,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"제가 점심을 일러두는 동안 읽으세요. 그런 다음 식사를 하면서 저에게 물으시면 돼요.\" 그는 절을 하고는 빛을 등진 채 의자에 자리를 잡았고, 서류에 푹 빠져들었다. 나는 그가 방해받지 않도록, 무엇보다 그것을 위해 점심을 살피러 갔다. 돌아와 보니 그는 흥분으로 얼굴이 온통 달아오른 채 방 안을 황급히 오락가락하고 있었다. 그는 나에게로 달려와 두 손을 잡았다.\n\n\"오, 미나 부인,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"내가 부인께 진 빚을 어찌 말로 다하겠소? 이 서류는 햇살과도 같소. 나에게 문을 열어 주는구려. 나는 이토록 많은 빛에 어리둥절하고, 눈이 부시오. 그런데도 그 빛 뒤로 번번이 구름이 밀려드오. 그러나 그것은 부인이 알지 못하고, 알 수도 없는 일이오. 아, 그러나 나는 부인께 감사하오, 그토록 영리한 부인께. 부인,\" — 그는 이 말을 몹시 엄숙하게 했다 — \"언제든 에이브러햄 판 헬싱이 부인이나 부인의 사람들을 위해 무언가 할 수 있게 되거든, 부디 나에게 알려 주시기를 믿겠소. 벗으로서 부인을 섬길 수 있다면 그것은 기쁨이요 즐거움이리다. 벗으로서, 그러나 내가 여태 배운 모든 것, 내가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 부인과 부인이 사랑하는 이들을 위해 바치겠소. 삶에는 어둠이 있고, 또 빛이 있소. 부인은 그 빛 가운데 하나요. 부인은 행복한 삶, 좋은 삶을 누릴 것이며, 부군은 부인으로 하여 복받을 것이오.\"\n\n\"하지만 박사님, 저를 너무 칭찬하세요. 그리고 — 그리고 박사님은 저를 모르시잖아요.\"\n\n\"부인을 모른다니 — 늙은 데다 평생토록 남자와 여자를 연구해 온 내가, 두뇌와 그에 딸린 모든 것, 그로부터 비롯되는 모든 것을 전공으로 삼아 온 내가 말이오! 게다가 나는 부인이 나를 위해 그토록 곱게 써 주신, 줄마다 진실이 배어 나오는 그 일기를 읽었소. 가엾은 루시에게 보낸, 부인의 혼인과 신뢰를 적은 그 다정한 편지를 읽은 내가, 부인을 모른다니! 오, 미나 부인, 선한 여인은 자기 삶을 온통 드러내는 법이오. 날마다, 시간마다, 분마다, 천사들이 읽을 수 있는 그런 것들을 말이오. 그리고 알기를 바라는 우리 남자들에게도 그 천사의 눈 같은 것이 조금은 있소. 부군은 고결한 천성이고, 부인 또한 고결하오. 부인이 신뢰할 줄 아는 까닭이오. 신뢰란 비루한 천성이 있는 곳에는 깃들 수 없는 법이니. 그리고 부군 — 그분에 관해 말해 주시오. 아주 건강하시오? 그 열병은 다 가셨고, 튼튼하고 정정하시오?\" 나는 여기서 조너선에 관해 물을 틈을 보았기에 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"거의 회복되었는데, 호킨스 씨의 죽음으로 크게 동요했어요.\" 그가 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"오, 그렇소, 아오, 아오. 부인의 지난 두 편지를 읽었소.\" 나는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그것이 그를 뒤흔들었나 봐요, 지난 목요일 우리가 시내에 갔을 때 그가 일종의 충격을 받았거든요.\"\n\n\"충격이라니, 뇌염을 앓은 지 그리 얼마 되지도 않아서! 좋지 않은 일이오. 어떤 종류의 충격이었소?\"\n\n\"무언가 끔찍한 것을, 그의 뇌염으로 이어졌던 무언가를 떠오르게 하는 누군가를 본 것 같다고 했어요.\" 그러자 그 모든 것이 한꺼번에 밀려들어 나를 짓누르는 듯했다. 조너선에 대한 연민, 그가 겪은 공포, 그의 일기에 담긴 그 무서운 수수께끼 전부, 그리고 그 뒤로 줄곧 나를 짓눌러 온 두려움이 한데 뒤엉켜 몰려들었다. 짐작건대 나는 발작에 가까운 상태였던 것 같다. 나는 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 치켜들며, 제 남편을 다시 낫게 해 달라고 애원했으니 말이다. 그는 내 손을 잡아 일으켜 소파에 앉히고는 내 곁에 앉았다. 그는 내 손을 자기 손에 쥐고서, 아, 한없이 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"내 삶은 메마르고 외로운 삶이고, 일이 너무 많아 우정을 나눌 겨를이 별로 없었소. 그러나 내 벗 존 수어드의 부름을 받아 이곳에 온 뒤로, 나는 그토록 많은 선한 이들을 알게 되었고 그토록 고결한 모습을 보았기에, 그 어느 때보다도 — 그리고 나이가 들수록 더해 가는 — 내 삶의 외로움을 느끼게 되었소. 그러니 부디 믿어 주시오, 나는 부인에 대한 존경으로 가득 차 이곳에 왔으며, 부인은 나에게 희망을 주셨소 — 내가 찾고 있는 그것에 대한 희망이 아니라, 삶을 행복하게 만들어 줄 선한 여인이 아직 남아 있다는 희망 말이오. 그 삶과 그 진실이 앞으로 태어날 아이들에게 좋은 본보기가 되어 줄 선한 여인 말이오. 나는 기쁘오, 이곳에서 부인께 조금이나마 쓸모가 될 수 있어 기쁘오. 부군이 고통받는다면, 그것은 내 연구와 경험의 범위 안에서의 고통이기 때문이오. 약속하건대, 그를 위해 내가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 기꺼이 하겠소 — 그의 삶을 강건하고 사내답게, 그리고 부인의 삶을 행복하게 만들기 위한 모든 것을. 이제 부인은 무얼 좀 드셔야 하오. 부인은 지나치게 긴장했고 어쩌면 지나치게 근심하고 있소. 부군 조너선은 부인이 이리 핼쑥한 것을 보고 싶어 하지 않을 것이오. 사랑하는 이가 있는 곳에서 그가 싫어하는 일은 그에게 좋지 않은 법이오. 그러니 그를 위해서라도 부인은 먹고 웃어야 하오. 부인은 루시에 관해 모두 말씀하셨으니, 이제 그 이야기는 더 하지 맙시다, 부인을 괴롭게 할까 두려운 까닭이오. 나는 오늘 밤 엑서터에 머물 텐데, 부인이 들려주신 것을 곰곰이 생각해 보고 싶기 때문이오. 그리고 다 생각하고 나면, 괜찮다면 부인께 몇 가지 여쭙겠소. 그리고 그때 부인도 부군 조너선의 시름에 관해 아는 만큼 들려주시오, 그러나 아직은 아니오. 지금은 드셔야 하오. 그 뒤에 모두 말씀해 주시오.\"\n\n점심을 든 뒤 우리가 응접실로 돌아왔을 때, 그가 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"이제 그분에 관해 모두 말해 주시오.\" 막상 이 위대하고 학식 깊은 사람에게 말하려니, 나는 그가 나를 나약하고 어리석은 사람으로, 조너선을 미치광이로 여길까 봐 — 그 일기는 모든 것이 너무도 기이하니 — 두려워지기 시작했고, 말을 잇기가 망설여졌다. 그러나 그는 너무도 다정하고 친절했으며, 돕겠다고 약속했고, 나는 그를 믿었기에 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"판 헬싱 박사님, 제가 말씀드리려는 것은 너무도 기이해서, 저나 제 남편을 비웃으시면 안 돼요. 저는 어제부터 일종의 의심에 들떠 열에 들뜬 상태였어요. 저에게 친절히 대해 주시고, 제가 아주 기이한 일들을 반쯤이나마 믿었다 해서 어리석다 여기지 말아 주세요.\" 그는 말로써도, 그 태도로써도 나를 안심시키며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 부인, 내가 이곳에 온 까닭이 된 그 일이 얼마나 기이한지 부인이 안다면, 도리어 웃으실 사람은 부인일 거요. 나는 누구의 믿음이든, 그것이 아무리 기이할지라도 가벼이 여기지 않는 법을 배웠소. 나는 열린 마음을 지니려 애써 왔소. 그리고 그 마음을 닫을 수 있는 것은 삶의 평범한 일들이 아니라, 기이한 일들, 비범한 일들, 그것이 미친 것인지 제정신인지 의심하게 만드는 그런 일들이오.\"\n\n\"고맙습니다, 고맙습니다, 천 번이라도! 제 마음의 짐을 덜어 주셨어요. 허락해 주신다면 박사님께 읽으실 서류 하나를 드리겠어요. 길지만 타자기로 옮겨 두었어요. 그것이 제 시름과 조너선의 시름을 말해 줄 거예요. 그가 외국에 있을 때 쓴 일기와, 일어난 모든 일의 사본이에요. 저는 그것에 관해 감히 아무 말도 못 하겠어요. 박사님이 직접 읽으시고 판단해 주세요. 그런 다음 박사님을 다시 뵐 때, 부디 친절히 어떻게 생각하시는지 말씀해 주세요.\"\n\n\"약속하오,\" 하고 그가 서류를 받으며 말했다. \"내일 아침, 되도록 빨리, 괜찮다면 부인과 부군을 뵈러 오겠소.\"\n\n\"조너선이 열한 시 반에 올 거예요. 그러니 점심을 함께 드시러 오셔서 그때 그를 만나세요. 3시 34분 급행을 타시면 여덟 시 전에 패딩턴에 닿으실 거예요.\" 그는 내가 즉석에서 기차편을 꿰고 있는 것에 놀랐지만, 내가 엑서터를 오가는 모든 기차편을 외워 두었다는 것은 모른다. 조너선이 바쁠 때 돕기 위해서다.\n그리하여 그는 서류를 가지고 떠났고, 나는 여기 앉아 생각에 잠겨 있다 — 무엇을 생각하는지도 모른 채.\n\n「판 헬싱이 하커 부인에게 보낸 편지」(인편으로)\n\n\"9월 25일, 여섯 시.\n\n친애하는 미나 부인께,\n\n부군의 그 놀라운 일기를 읽었소. 의심 없이 편히 주무셔도 좋소. 기이하고 끔찍하기는 하나, 그것은 사실이오! 내 목숨을 걸어도 좋소. 다른 이들에게는 더 나쁜 일일지도 모르나, 그분과 부인에게는 두려워할 것이 없소. 그분은 고결한 사람이오. 사람을 겪어 온 경험으로 부인께 말하건대, 그 벽을 타고 내려가 그 방으로 들어간 일 — 그렇소, 게다가 두 번씩이나 들어간 일 — 그런 일을 해낸 사람은 한 번의 충격으로 영영 망가질 사람이 아니오. 그의 두뇌와 심장은 모두 멀쩡하오. 이것은 그를 만나 보기도 전에 내가 맹세할 수 있소. 그러니 안심하시오. 다른 일들에 관해 그에게 물을 것이 많소. 오늘 부인을 뵈러 온 것이 나에게는 복이었으니, 단번에 너무도 많은 것을 알게 되어 나는 또다시 눈이 부시오 — 그 어느 때보다 더 눈이 부시오. 그래서 나는 생각을 좀 해야겠소.\n더없이 충실한 벗,\n\n에이브러햄 판 헬싱.\"\n\n「하커 부인이 판 헬싱에게 보낸 편지」\n\n\"9월 25일, 오후 여섯 시 반.\n\n친애하는 판 헬싱 박사님께,\n제 마음의 큰 짐을 덜어 준 박사님의 친절한 편지에 천 번 감사드립니다. 그런데도 만일 그것이 사실이라면, 세상에 얼마나 끔찍한 일들이 있는 것이며, 그 남자가, 그 괴물이 정말로 런던에 있다면 이 얼마나 무서운 일인지요! 생각만 해도 두렵습니다. 지금 이 편지를 쓰는 중에 조너선에게서 전보를 받았는데, 오늘 밤 6시 25분 기차로 론서스턴을 떠나 10시 18분에 이곳에 닿을 거라고 합니다. 그러니 오늘 밤은 두려울 것이 없겠어요. 그러니 저희와 점심을 드시는 대신, 너무 이르지 않으시다면 여덟 시에 아침을 드시러 와 주시겠어요? 바쁘시면 10시 30분 기차로 떠나실 수 있는데, 그러면 2시 35분에 패딩턴에 닿으실 거예요. 이 편지에는 답하지 마세요. 소식이 없으면 아침을 드시러 오시는 것으로 알겠습니다.\n\n부디 믿어 주세요,\n\n박사님의 충실하고 감사하는 벗,\n미나 하커.\"\n\n「조너선 하커의 일기」\n\n9월 26일. — 이 일기에 다시는 쓰지 않으리라 여겼건만, 그때가 왔다. 어젯밤 집에 돌아오니 미나가 저녁을 차려 놓았고, 저녁을 들고 나자 미나는 판 헬싱의 방문에 관해, 두 일기를 옮겨 적어 그에게 건넨 일에 관해, 그리고 그녀가 나 때문에 얼마나 마음 졸였는지를 들려주었다. 그녀는 내가 적어 둔 모든 것이 사실이라는 박사의 편지를 보여 주었다. 그것이 나를 새사람으로 만든 듯하다. 나를 쓰러뜨린 것은 그 모든 일이 과연 실제였는가 하는 의심이었다. 나는 무력하고, 어둠 속에 있으며, 모든 것을 못 믿는 듯한 기분이었다. 그러나 이제 그것이 사실임을 아니, 나는 두렵지 않다, 백작조차도. 그렇다면 그는 결국 런던에 들어오려던 자신의 계략에 성공한 것이고, 내가 본 것은 바로 그였다. 그는 더 젊어졌으니, 대체 어떻게? 미나의 말이 조금이라도 사실이라면, 판 헬싱이야말로 그의 가면을 벗기고 그를 추적해 낼 사람이다. 우리는 늦게까지 앉아 그 모든 것을 의논했다. 미나가 옷을 갈아입고 있으니, 나는 잠시 후 호텔로 들러 그를 데려와야겠다…….\n\n내 생각에 그는 나를 보고 놀란 듯했다. 그가 있는 방에 들어가 내 소개를 하자, 그는 내 어깨를 잡고 내 얼굴을 빛 쪽으로 돌리더니, 날카롭게 살펴본 뒤에 말했다.\n\n\"그런데 미나 부인이 그대가 아프다고, 충격을 받았다고 일러주었는데.\" 이 다정하고 강인한 얼굴의 노인이 내 아내를 \"미나 부인\"이라 부르는 것을 들으니 어찌나 우습던지. 나는 미소를 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"제가 아팠던 건 사실이고, 충격을 받은 것도 사실이지요. 하지만 선생님이 이미 저를 낫게 해 주셨어요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 말이오?\"\n\"어젯밤 미나에게 보내신 편지로요. 저는 의심에 빠져 있었고, 그러자 모든 것이 비현실의 빛깔을 띠어, 무엇을 믿어야 할지 — 제 감각의 증거조차 — 알 수가 없었어요. 무엇을 믿어야 할지 모르니, 무엇을 해야 할지도 몰랐고, 그래서 그저 여태껏 제 삶의 정해진 홈이었던 일을 계속할 수밖에 없었지요. 그런데 그 홈이 더는 소용없어졌고, 저는 제 자신마저 못 믿게 되었어요. 박사님, 모든 것을, 자기 자신조차 의심한다는 것이 어떤 것인지 박사님은 모르실 거예요. 아니요, 모르세요. 그런 눈썹을 지니고서는 그러실 수가 없지요.\" 그는 흐뭇한 듯, 웃으며 말했다.\n\n\"그래! 그대는 관상가로구려. 나는 이곳에서 시간마다 더 배우는구려. 아주 즐거운 마음으로 그대의 아침 식사에 가는 길이오. 그리고, 아, 그대여, 늙은이의 칭찬을 용서하시오만, 그대는 부인으로 하여 복받은 사람이오.\" 나는 그가 미나를 칭찬하는 말이라면 하루 종일이라도 들을 수 있었기에, 그저 고개를 끄덕이고 잠자코 서 있었다.\n\n\"그분은 하느님의 여인 가운데 하나로, 우리 남자들과 다른 여인들에게 우리가 들어갈 수 있는 천국이 있으며 그 빛이 이 땅에도 있을 수 있음을 보여 주려, 그분 손수 빚으신 사람이오. 그토록 참되고, 그토록 다정하며, 그토록 고결하고, 그토록 자기를 내세우지 않으니 — 그리고 그것은, 단언하건대, 이토록 회의적이고 이기적인 시대에 참으로 대단한 것이오. 그리고 그대 말이오 — 나는 가엾은 루시 양에게 보낸 편지를 모두 읽었는데, 그중 몇 통은 그대 이야기를 하고 있어, 나는 며칠 전부터 다른 이들의 글로써 그대를 알고 있었소. 그러나 그대의 참된 모습을 본 것은 어젯밤부터요. 나에게 손을 내밀어 주겠소? 그리고 우리 평생토록 벗이 됩시다.\"\n\n우리는 악수를 나누었는데, 그가 어찌나 진지하고 다정하던지 나는 그만 목이 메었다.\n\n\"그럼 이제,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"도움을 좀 더 청해도 되겠소? 나에게는 해야 할 큰 일이 있는데, 그 처음은 아는 것이오. 여기서 그대가 나를 도울 수 있소. 그대가 트란실바니아로 가기 전에 무슨 일이 있었는지 말해 줄 수 있겠소? 나중에 다른 종류의 도움을 더 청할지도 모르나, 우선은 이것이면 되오.\"\n\n\"보세요, 선생님,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"선생님이 하셔야 할 일이 백작과 관계된 것입니까?\"\n\n\"그렇소,\" 하고 그가 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 저는 온 마음과 영혼을 다해 선생님과 함께하겠습니다. 10시 30분 기차로 가시니 그것들을 읽을 시간은 없으시겠지만, 제가 서류 뭉치를 가져다 드릴게요. 가져가셔서 기차에서 읽으시면 됩니다.\"\n\n아침 식사 뒤 나는 그를 역까지 배웅했다. 헤어질 때 그가 말했다.\n\n\"내가 사람을 보내거든 시내로 와 주시오, 미나 부인도 함께 데려오고.\"\n\n\"부르시면 저희 둘 다 가겠습니다,\" 하고 내가 말했다.\n\n나는 그에게 조간신문과 전날 밤의 런던 신문들을 사다 주었는데, 우리가 객차 창가에서 이야기하며 기차가 떠나기를 기다리는 동안 그는 그것들을 뒤적이고 있었다. 그러다 문득 그중 하나에서 — 「웨스트민스터 가제트」였는데, 나는 색깔로 알아보았다 — 무언가에 눈길이 걸린 듯하더니, 그의 얼굴이 새파랗게 질렸다. 그는 무언가를 골똘히 읽으며 혼자 끙끙거렸다. \"마인 고트! 마인 고트! 이리도 빨리! 이리도 빨리!\" 그 순간 그는 나를 기억하지 못하는 듯했다. 바로 그때 기적이 울리고 기차가 움직이기 시작했다. 그제야 그는 정신을 차리고 창밖으로 몸을 내밀어 손을 흔들며 외쳤다. \"미나 부인께 안부를! 되도록 빨리 편지하겠소.\"\n* 마인 고트: 독일어로 \"하느님 맙소사\"라는 뜻.\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n9월 26일. — 참으로 끝이라는 것은 없는 모양이다. \"끝\"이라고 적은 지 일주일도 되지 않았건만, 나는 이렇게 또다시 새로 시작하고 있다, 아니 오히려 같은 기록을 이어가고 있다. 오늘 오후까지는 끝난 일을 떠올릴 까닭이 없었다. 렌필드는 사실상 예전 어느 때 못지않게 제정신이 되어 있었다. 그는 이미 파리 사업을 한참 진척시켜 놓았고, 막 거미 분야에도 손을 대기 시작한 터라, 나에게 별 골칫거리가 되지 않았다. 일요일에 쓴 아서의 편지를 받았는데, 그것으로 미루어 그는 놀라우리만치 잘 견뎌 내고 있는 듯하다. 퀸시 모리스가 그와 함께 있는데, 그것이 큰 도움이 되니, 퀸시 자신이 솟구치는 활기의 샘 같은 사람인 까닭이다. 퀸시도 나에게 몇 자 적어 보냈는데, 그에게서 아서가 옛 쾌활함을 조금씩 되찾기 시작했다는 소식을 들었다. 그러니 그들에 관해서라면 내 마음은 놓인다. 나 자신으로 말하자면, 예전에 품었던 그 열의로 다시 일에 자리 잡아 가던 참이라, 가엾은 루시가 내게 남긴 상처가 아물어 가고 있다고 제법 말할 수 있을 정도였다. 그러나 이제 모든 것이 다시 헤집어졌고, 그 끝이 어찌 될지는 오직 하느님만이 아신다. 내 생각에 판 헬싱도 자기가 그 끝을 안다고 여기는 듯하나, 그는 한 번에 호기심을 돋울 만큼만 흘릴 뿐이다. 그는 어제 엑서터로 가 거기서 밤을 보냈다. 오늘 돌아와서, 다섯 시 반쯤 거의 방 안으로 뛰어들다시피 하더니, 어젯밤의 「웨스트민스터 가제트」를 내 손에 쑥 밀어 넣었다.\n\n\"이걸 어떻게 생각하나?\" 그가 물러서서 팔짱을 끼며 물었다.\n\n나는 신문을 훑어보았는데, 정말이지 그가 무슨 말을 하는지 알 수 없었기 때문이다. 그러자 그는 그것을 내게서 도로 가져가더니, 햄스테드에서 아이들이 꾐에 빠져 끌려갔다는 한 단락을 가리켜 보였다. 그것은 나에게 별로 와닿지 않았는데, 아이들 목에 난 작은 구멍 모양의 상처를 묘사한 대목에 이르러서였다. 어떤 생각이 퍼뜩 떠올라 나는 고개를 들었다. \"어떤가?\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"가엾은 루시의 것과 같군요.\"\n\n\"그래서 자네는 그걸 어떻게 보나?\"\n\n\"단지 어떤 공통된 원인이 있다는 것이지요. 그녀에게 상처를 입힌 것이 무엇이었든, 그것이 이 아이들에게도 상처를 입혔다는 거고요.\" 나는 그의 대답을 온전히 이해하지 못했다.\n\n\"그것은 간접적으로는 사실이나, 직접적으로는 아니야.\"\n\n\"무슨 말씀이신지요, 교수님?\" 하고 내가 물었다. 나는 그의 진지함을 조금은 가볍게 받아들이고 싶었으니 — 어쨌든 나흘간의 휴식과, 타들어 가는 듯 사람을 괴롭히던 시름에서 벗어난 자유가 사람의 기운을 돋우는 데 보탬이 되기는 하는 것이다 — 그러나 그의 얼굴을 보자 마음이 가라앉았다. 가엾은 루시 때문에 절망에 빠져 있던 그 와중에도 그가 이보다 더 준엄해 보인 적은 없었다.\n\n\"말해 보게!\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"저는 아무 의견도 함부로 낼 수가 없습니다. 무엇을 생각해야 할지 모르겠고, 어떤 추측을 세울 만한 자료도 없으니까요.\"\n\n\"존, 친구여, 자네는 가엾은 루시가 무엇 때문에 죽었는지 짐작 가는 바가 전혀 없단 말인가. 그 모든 암시를 받고도, 사건들로뿐만 아니라 나로부터도 받고서도 말일세?\"\n\n\"큰 출혈 혹은 혈액 소모에 뒤따른 신경 쇠약 때문이지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 그 피는 어떻게 잃거나 소모되었나?\" 나는 고개를 저었다. 그는 다가와 내 곁에 앉더니 말을 이었다.\n\n\"자네는 영리한 사람일세, 존. 추론을 잘하고, 그 재기가 대담하지. 그러나 자네는 편견이 너무 심해. 자네는 자네 눈으로 하여금 보게 하지도, 자네 귀로 하여금 듣게 하지도 않고, 자네 일상 밖에 있는 것은 자네에게 아무 의미가 없는 게야. 자네가 이해할 수 없으면서도 엄연히 존재하는 것들이 있다고는 생각지 않나? 어떤 이들은 다른 이들이 보지 못하는 것을 본다고는 생각지 않나? 그러나 오래된 것이든 새로운 것이든, 사람의 눈으로 헤아려서는 안 될 것들이 있으니, 그들이 다른 사람들에게서 전해 들은 어떤 것들을 안다고 — 혹은 안다고 여기기 — 때문이지. 아, 모든 것을 설명하려 드는 것이 우리 과학의 결함일세. 그리고 설명하지 못하면, 설명할 것이 아무것도 없다고 말하지. 그러나 우리는 날마다 우리 둘레에서 스스로 새롭다고 여기는 새 믿음들이 자라나는 것을 보네. 그것들은 사실 낡은 것이면서 젊은 척하는 것들이지 — 오페라 극장의 멋쟁이 귀부인들처럼 말일세. 짐작건대 자네는 육체의 전이 같은 것은 믿지 않겠지. 아니라고? 물질화도? 아니라고? 성신체(星身體)도? 아니라고? 생각을 읽는 것도? 아니라고? 최면술도——\"\n\n\"믿습니다,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"샤르코가 그것을 꽤 잘 입증했지요.\" 그는 미소 지으며 말을 이었다. \"그렇다면 자네는 그것에 관해서는 수긍하는군. 그렇지? 그렇다면 물론 자네는 그것이 어떻게 작용하는지 이해하고, 위대한 샤르코의 — 아, 이제는 세상에 없으니 애석하구나! — 정신을 따라, 그가 영향을 미치는 환자의 영혼 깊숙한 곳까지 들어갈 수 있겠지. 아니라고? 그렇다면, 존, 친구여, 자네가 그저 사실만 받아들이고, 전제에서 결론에 이르는 과정은 백지로 두는 데 만족한다고 여겨도 되겠나? 아니라고? 그렇다면 말해 보게 — 나는 두뇌를 연구하는 사람이니 — 어찌하여 자네는 최면술은 받아들이면서 생각 읽기는 물리치는가. 친구여, 내가 말해 두겠는데, 오늘날 전기 과학에서 행해지는 일들 가운데에는, 전기를 발견한 바로 그 사람들이라면 불경하다 여겼을 것들이 있다네 — 그들 자신도 그리 오래지 않은 옛날이라면 마법사로 화형당했을 사람들인데 말일세. 삶에는 언제나 신비가 있는 법이지. 어찌하여 므두셀라는 구백 년을 살았고, '늙은 파'는 백육십구 년을 살았건만, 그 가엾은 루시는 가엾은 핏줄에 네 남자의 피를 받고도 단 하루조차 살지 못했을까? 만일 그녀가 단 하루만 더 살았더라면 우리는 그녀를 구할 수 있었을 텐데 말일세. 자네는 삶과 죽음의 모든 신비를 아는가? 비교 해부학의 전부를 알아서, 어찌하여 짐승의 성질이 어떤 사람에게는 있고 어떤 사람에게는 없는지 말할 수 있는가? 다른 거미들은 작은 채로 일찍 죽건만, 어찌하여 그 한 마리 거대한 거미는 옛 스페인 성당의 탑 속에서 수백 년을 살며 자라고 또 자라, 마침내 내려와서는 성당의 등잔 기름을 죄다 마실 수 있었는지 말할 수 있는가? 어찌하여 팜파스에서, 그렇지, 또 다른 곳에서도, 밤이 되면 찾아와 소와 말의 핏줄을 열어 그 핏줄을 바싹 빨아먹는 박쥐들이 있는지, 어찌하여 서쪽 바다의 어떤 섬들에는 온종일 나무에 매달려 있어 — 본 사람들은 거대한 견과나 꼬투리 같다고 묘사하는 — 박쥐들이 있어, 날이 더워 선원들이 갑판에서 잠들면 그들 위로 날아 내려와서는, 그러고는 — 그러고는 아침이면 미나 양만큼이나 새하얗게 죽은 사람들이 발견되는지, 자네는 말할 수 있는가?\"\n\n\"맙소사, 교수님!\" 하고 나는 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. \"루시가 그런 박쥐에게 물렸으며, 그런 것이 19세기 런던에 있다는 말씀입니까?\" 그는 잠자코 있으라는 듯 손을 저으며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"어찌하여 거북은 여러 세대의 사람들보다 더 오래 사는지, 어찌하여 코끼리는 여러 왕조를 지켜볼 만큼 살고 또 사는지, 어찌하여 앵무새는 고양이나 개에게 물리거나 다른 병에 걸리지 않는 한 결코 죽지 않는지, 자네는 말할 수 있는가? 어찌하여 모든 시대와 모든 곳에서 사람들은, 허락받기만 하면 영원히 살아가는 소수가 있다고, 죽을 수 없는 남자와 여자가 있다고 믿어 왔는지 말할 수 있는가? 우리 모두가 알듯이 — 과학이 그 사실을 보증했으니 — 수천 년 동안 바위 속에 갇혀 있던 두꺼비들이 있었네. 세상이 젊었던 때부터 그것을 가둘 만큼만 작은 구멍 하나 속에 갇혀서 말이지. 인도의 고행자가 어찌하여 스스로 죽어 매장되고, 그 무덤을 봉인하고 그 위에 곡식을 뿌려, 그 곡식이 자라 베이고 다시 뿌려져 베이기를 거듭한 뒤에, 사람들이 와서 깨지지 않은 봉인을 걷어 내면, 거기 그 인도 고행자가 죽지 않은 채 누워 있다가, 일어나 예전처럼 사람들 사이를 걸어 다니는지, 자네는 말할 수 있는가?\" 여기서 나는 그의 말을 가로막았다. 나는 점점 어리둥절해지고 있었다. 그가 자연의 기이한 사례들과 가능한 불가능들의 목록을 어찌나 내 머릿속에 가득 쏟아붓는지, 내 상상력에 불이 붙기 시작했던 것이다. 나는 그가 나에게 어떤 가르침을 주려 한다는 것을 어렴풋이 알았으니, 오래전 암스테르담의 그의 서재에서 그러곤 했던 것처럼 말이다. 그러나 그때 그는 으레 본론을 먼저 일러주어, 내가 줄곧 생각의 대상을 마음에 두고 있을 수 있게 했다. 그런데 지금은 그런 도움이 없었으니, 그래도 나는 그를 따라가고 싶어 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"교수님, 저를 다시 교수님의 귀여운 학생이 되게 해 주십시오. 논제를 일러 주셔서, 말씀을 이어 가시는 동안 제가 교수님의 지식을 적용할 수 있게 해 주십시오. 지금 저는 제정신인 사람이 아니라 미친 사람이 어떤 생각을 좇듯, 마음속에서 이 점에서 저 점으로 옮겨 다니고 있습니다. 마치 안개 속 늪을 허우적대며 헤쳐 나가는 풋내기처럼, 제가 어디로 가는지도 모르면서 그저 앞으로 나아가려는 맹목적인 안간힘으로 이 풀 덤불에서 저 풀 덤불로 건너뛰는 기분입니다.\"\n\n\"좋은 비유일세,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그럼 일러 주지. 내 논제는 이것일세. 나는 자네가 믿기를 바라네.\"\n\n\"무엇을 믿으라는 말씀이신지요?\"\n\n\"자네가 믿을 수 없는 것들을 믿으라는 걸세. 예를 들어 보겠네. 언젠가 어떤 미국인이 믿음을 이렇게 정의했다는 이야기를 들었지. '사실이 아님을 우리가 아는 것들을 믿게 해 주는 능력'이라고 말일세. 적어도 나는 그 사람의 말을 따르네. 그가 뜻한 바는, 우리가 열린 마음을 지녀야 하며, 작은 바위가 철도 화차를 멈춰 세우듯, 작은 진실 한 조각이 큰 진실의 돌진을 가로막게 두어서는 안 된다는 것일세. 우리는 먼저 작은 진실을 얻지. 좋아! 그것을 간직하고, 소중히 여기네. 그러나 그렇더라도 그것이 스스로를 우주의 진실 전부라 여기게 두어서는 안 되는 게야.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 교수님은 제가 어떤 기이한 문제에 관하여, 이전의 어떤 확신이 제 마음의 수용성을 해치게 두지 말라는 말씀이시군요. 제가 교수님의 가르침을 옳게 읽은 것인지요?\"\n\n\"아, 자네는 여전히 내가 가장 아끼는 제자일세. 자네를 가르치는 보람이 있어. 이제 자네가 이해하려는 마음을 먹었으니, 이해를 향한 첫걸음을 뗀 셈이지. 그래, 자네는 그 아이들 목에 난 그 작은 구멍들이 루시 양에게 구멍을 낸 바로 그것에 의해 생긴 것이라 여기나?\"\n\n\"그런 듯합니다.\" 그는 일어서서 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 자네는 틀렸어. 오, 차라리 그렇다면 좋으련만! 그러나 아아! 아닐세. 그보다 더 나쁘다네, 훨씬, 훨씬 더 나빠.\"\n\n\"제발, 판 헬싱 교수님, 대체 무슨 말씀이십니까?\" 하고 나는 외쳤다.\n\n그는 절망스러운 몸짓으로 의자에 몸을 던지고는, 식탁에 두 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 말했다.\n\n\"그 구멍들은 루시 양이 낸 것일세!\"\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」 — 계속.\n\n한동안은 격렬한 분노가 나를 사로잡았다. 마치 그가 생전의 루시의 뺨을 후려치기라도 한 것 같았다. 나는 탁자를 세게 내리치고 일어서며 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"반 헬싱 박사, 미치셨소?\" 그는 고개를 들어 나를 바라보았는데, 어쩐 일인지 그의 얼굴에 어린 부드러움이 단번에 나를 진정시켰다. \"차라리 미쳤더라면!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"이런 진실에 견주면 광기란 견디기 쉬운 것이오. 아, 나의 벗이여, 어찌하여 내가 이토록 멀리 에둘러 왔겠소? 어찌하여 이토록 간단한 일을 말하는 데 그리도 오래 걸렸겠소? 내가 당신을 미워해서, 평생 미워해왔기 때문이겠소? 당신에게 고통을 주고 싶어서였겠소? 당신이 내 목숨을, 그것도 끔찍한 죽음에서 구해주었던 그때의 일에 대해 이제 와서 복수하고 싶어서였겠소? 아, 아니오!\"\n\n\"용서하시오,\" 하고 내가 말했다. 그는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"나의 벗이여, 그것은 내가 당신에게 이 일을 알리는 데 있어 부드럽고자 했기 때문이오. 당신이 그토록 사랑스러운 여인을 사랑했음을 내가 알기에 말이오. 그러나 지금도 나는 당신이 믿어주리라고 기대하지는 않소. 어떤 추상적 진실이든 단번에 받아들이기란 무척 어려운 법이라, 늘 그것이 '아니'라고 믿어왔던 것을 두고 우리는 그런 일이 가능한지조차 의심할 수가 있소. 하물며 이토록 슬픈 구체적 진실, 그것도 루시 양과 같은 분에 관한 진실을 받아들이기란 더더욱 어려운 일이오. 오늘 밤 나는 그것을 증명하러 가오. 감히 나와 함께 가시겠소?\"\n\n이 말에 나는 휘청거렸다. 사람이란 그런 진실을 증명하고 싶어하지 않는 법이다. 다만 바이런만은 이 부류에서 예외였으니, 질투에 관해서는 그러했다.\n\n\"그리고 그가 가장 혐오하던 바로 그 진실을 증명하더라.\"\n\n그는 내가 망설이는 것을 보고 입을 열었다.\n\n\"논리는 간단하오. 이번에는 안개 낀 늪에서 풀더미에서 풀더미로 건너뛰는 미치광이의 논리가 아니오. 만일 사실이 아니라면 증명은 위안이 될 것이고, 최악의 경우라도 해가 되지는 않을 것이오. 그러나 만일 사실이라면! 아, 바로 거기에 두려움이 있소. 그러나 바로 그 두려움이 나의 일을 도와줄 것이오. 그 안에는 믿음을 향한 어떤 필요가 깃들어 있으니 말이오. 자, 내가 제안하는 바를 일러주겠소. 첫째, 우리가 지금 가서 병원에 있는 그 아이를 보는 것이오. 신문에서 아이가 있다고 한 노스 병원의 빈센트 박사는 나의 벗이고, 또한 당신이 암스테르담에서 수업을 들었으니 당신의 벗이기도 할 것이오. 그는 두 벗에게는 사례를 보여주지 않더라도, 두 과학자에게는 보여줄 것이오. 우리는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 다만 알고 싶을 뿐이라고만 할 것이오. 그러고 나서…….\"\n\n\"그러고 나서요?\" 그는 주머니에서 열쇠 하나를 꺼내 들어 보였다. \"그러고 나서 당신과 나, 우리는 루시가 누워 있는 그 묘지에서 밤을 보내는 것이오. 이것이 그 무덤을 잠그는 열쇠요. 아서에게 건네주라고 관 만드는 사람에게서 받아둔 것이오.\" 내 가슴은 철렁 내려앉았으니, 우리 앞에 어떤 무시무시한 시련이 놓여 있음을 느꼈기 때문이다. 그러나 나로서는 어쩔 도리가 없었으므로, 나는 낼 수 있는 만큼 용기를 끌어모아, 오후가 지나가고 있으니 서두르는 편이 낫겠다고 말했다…….\n\n우리가 가보니 아이는 깨어 있었다. 한숨 자고 음식도 좀 먹어, 전반적으로 잘 회복되고 있었다. 빈센트 박사가 아이의 목에서 붕대를 풀어 우리에게 그 상처를 보여주었다. 루시의 목에 있던 것과의 유사성은 의심할 여지가 없었다. 다만 더 작고 가장자리가 더 새것처럼 보일 뿐, 그것이 전부였다. 우리가 빈센트에게 무엇 때문이라고 보느냐고 묻자, 그는 어떤 짐승의 물린 자국임이 틀림없다고, 어쩌면 쥐였을 것이라고 답했다. 그러나 그 자신으로서는 런던 북부 고지대에 그토록 많은 박쥐 가운데 하나가 아닐까 하는 쪽으로 마음이 기운다고 했다. \"그토록 많은 무해한 박쥐들 가운데,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"남방에서 온 더 악성인 종의 야생 표본이 더러 섞여 있을 수도 있지요. 어떤 선원이 한 마리를 집에 데려왔다가 그것이 용케 달아났을 수도 있고, 동물원에서 어린 놈이 풀려났을 수도, 혹은 거기서 흡혈박쥐로부터 한 마리가 태어났을 수도 있고요. 이런 일은 일어나는 법이니까요, 아시다시피. 불과 열흘 전에도 늑대 한 마리가 빠져나가, 제가 알기로는 이 방향으로 추적되었답니다. 그 후로 일주일 동안 아이들은 히스 들판에서나 동네 골목마다 빨간 모자 놀이만 하더니, 이 '아름다운 부인' 소동이 일어난 뒤로는 아주 신이 난 모양이에요. 이 가엾은 꼬마 녀석조차, 오늘 깨어나서는 간호사에게 나가도 되느냐고 물었답니다. 왜 나가고 싶으냐고 묻자, '아름다운 부인'과 놀고 싶다고 했다는군요.\"\n\n\"바라건대,\" 하고 반 헬싱이 말했다. \"이 아이를 집으로 보낼 때 부모에게 아이를 단단히 지켜보라고 주의를 주십시오. 이렇게 떠돌고 싶어하는 충동이 무엇보다 위험하니 말이오. 아이가 또 하룻밤이라도 밖에 머물게 된다면 아마 치명적일 것이오. 그러나 어떻든 며칠은 내보내지 않으시겠지요?\"\n\n\"물론입니다, 적어도 일주일은요. 상처가 낫지 않으면 더 오래 두지요.\"\n\n병원 방문이 우리가 예상했던 것보다 더 오래 걸려, 우리가 나왔을 때에는 해가 이미 기울어 있었다. 반 헬싱은 사위가 얼마나 어두운지를 보고 말했다.\n\n\"서두를 것 없소. 생각보다 시간이 늦었구려. 자, 어디 식사할 곳을 찾읍시다. 그러고 나서 우리 갈 길을 가도록 합시다.\"\n\n우리는 \"잭 스트로스 캐슬\"에서 자전거를 탄 무리와 그 밖의 사람들 틈에 끼여 식사를 했는데, 그들은 유쾌하게 떠들썩했다. 열 시쯤 우리는 그 여관에서 출발했다. 그때는 무척 어두웠고, 드문드문 놓인 가로등은 일단 그 불빛이 미치는 범위를 벗어나자 오히려 어둠을 더 깊게 만들었다. 교수는 우리가 가야 할 길을 분명히 익혀두었던 모양으로 망설임 없이 나아갔다. 그러나 나로서는 어디가 어딘지 도무지 종잡을 수 없었다. 더 멀리 갈수록 마주치는 사람은 점점 줄어, 마침내 평소의 순찰을 도는 기마 경찰대와 마주쳤을 때에는 다소 놀랄 정도였다. 마침내 우리는 묘지의 담에 이르러 그것을 타고 넘었다. 약간의 어려움 끝에 — 무척 어두운 데다 온통 낯설게 보였으므로 — 우리는 웨스텐라가의 무덤을 찾아냈다. 교수는 열쇠를 꺼내 삐걱거리는 문을 열고는, 뒤로 물러서서 공손하게, 그러나 무의식적으로 나더러 먼저 들어가라고 손짓했다. 이토록 섬뜩한 자리에서 우선권을 양보하는 그 정중함에는 절묘한 아이러니가 깃들어 있었다. 내 동행은 재빨리 나를 따라 들어와, 그 자물쇠가 용수철식이 아니라 떨어지는 식임을 조심스레 확인한 뒤 문을 신중히 닫았다. 용수철식이었다면 우리는 곤경에 빠졌을 터였다. 그러고 나서 그는 가방을 더듬어 성냥갑과 양초 토막을 꺼내 불을 밝히기 시작했다. 그 무덤은 낮에, 그리고 싱싱한 꽃으로 둘러싸여 있을 때에도 음산하고 으스스해 보였다. 그러나 이제, 며칠이 지나 꽃들이 시들어 축 늘어지고, 흰빛은 녹슨 빛으로, 푸른빛은 갈색으로 변하고, 거미와 딱정벌레가 다시 그 익숙한 지배권을 되찾았으며, 세월에 변색된 돌과, 먼지가 엉겨붙은 회반죽, 녹슬고 눅눅한 쇠붙이, 빛바랜 놋쇠, 흐려진 은도금이 촛불의 희미한 가물거림을 되비추고 있으니, 그 효과는 상상조차 할 수 없을 만큼 더 처참하고 누추했다. 그것은 생명이 — 동물의 생명이 — 사라질 수 있는 유일한 것은 아니라는 생각을 거역할 수 없이 전해주었다.\n\n반 헬싱은 자기 일을 체계적으로 해나갔다. 관의 명판을 읽을 수 있도록 촛불을 들었는데, 떨어지는 촛농이 금속에 닿자 곧 굳어 흰 얼룩을 이루도록 든 채로, 그는 루시의 관임을 확인했다. 가방을 다시 한 번 뒤져 그는 나사돌리개를 꺼냈다.\n\n\"무얼 하려는 거요?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"관을 열려는 것이오. 당신은 끝내 확신하게 될 것이오.\" 곧바로 그는 나사를 빼내기 시작하더니, 마침내 뚜껑을 들어올려, 그 아래 납으로 된 안틀을 드러냈다. 그 광경은 나로서는 거의 견디기 어려운 것이었다. 그것은 살아 있을 적 잠든 그녀의 옷을 벗기는 일이 그러했을 만큼이나 죽은 이에 대한 모욕처럼 여겨졌다. 나는 정말로 그의 손을 붙잡아 막으려 했다. 그는 다만 \"보게 될 것이오,\" 하고 말하고는, 다시 가방을 더듬어 자그마한 실톱을 꺼냈다. 나사돌리개를 납에 대고 재빠르게 아래로 찔러넣자 — 그 바람에 나는 움찔했다 — 작은 구멍이 났는데, 그래도 톱끝을 넣기에는 충분히 큰 것이었다. 나는 일주일 묵은 시신에서 가스가 확 뿜어져 나오리라 예상했다. 우리 의사들은 위험을 연구해야 하는 처지라 그런 일에는 익숙해질 수밖에 없는데, 그래도 나는 문 쪽으로 물러섰다. 그러나 교수는 한순간도 멈추지 않았다. 그는 납관의 한쪽을 따라 두어 자쯤 톱질해 내려간 다음, 가로질러, 다시 다른 쪽을 따라 내려갔다. 그렇게 떨어져나온 가장자리의 테두리를 잡아 관의 발치 쪽으로 젖히고는, 촛불을 그 틈으로 들어올려, 나더러 들여다보라고 손짓했다.\n\n나는 가까이 다가가 들여다보았다. 관은 비어 있었다.\n\n분명 그것은 나에게 놀라운 일이었고, 적잖은 충격을 안겼다. 그러나 반 헬싱은 흔들림이 없었다. 그는 이제 그 어느 때보다도 자기 입지에 대한 확신을 굳혔고, 그래서 더욱 대담하게 자기 일을 밀고 나갈 수 있었다. \"이제 만족하오, 존?\" 하고 그가 물었다.\n\n나는 그에게 대답하면서, 내 천성 속의 그 완강한 따지기 기질이 온통 깨어나는 것을 느꼈다.\n\n\"루시의 몸이 저 관 속에 없다는 것은 만족스럽게 알았소. 그러나 그것이 증명하는 것은 단 한 가지요.\"\n\n\"그게 무엇이오, 존?\"\n\n\"여기에 그 몸이 없다는 것이오.\"\n\n\"거기까지는 옳은 논리요,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그러나 당신은 그것이 여기 없는 것을 어떻게 설명하려오 — 아니, 어떻게 설명할 수 있겠소?\"\n\n\"어쩌면 시신 도굴꾼일 수도 있지요,\" 하고 내가 넌지시 말했다. \"장의사의 일꾼 가운데 누군가가 훔쳤을 수도 있고요.\" 나는 내가 어리석은 소리를 하고 있음을 느꼈으나, 그래도 그것이 내가 댈 수 있는 유일하게 현실적인 이유였다. 교수는 한숨을 내쉬었다. \"허허!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"우리에게는 더 많은 증거가 있어야겠소. 나와 함께 갑시다.\"\n\n그는 관 뚜껑을 다시 덮고, 자기 물건을 모두 챙겨 가방에 넣고는, 불을 끄고 양초도 가방에 넣었다. 우리는 문을 열고 나왔다. 우리 뒤에서 그는 문을 닫아 잠갔다. 그는 나에게 열쇠를 건네며 말했다. \"당신이 보관하겠소? 확실히 해두는 편이 좋겠소.\" 나는 웃었다 — 그리 유쾌한 웃음은 아니었음을 말해두어야겠다 — 그러면서 그더러 가지고 있으라고 손짓했다. \"열쇠는 아무것도 아니오,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"복제본이 있을 수도 있고, 어쨌든 그런 종류의 자물쇠를 따는 것쯤은 어렵지 않으니까요.\" 그는 아무 말 없이 열쇠를 자기 주머니에 넣었다. 그러고는 자기는 묘지의 한쪽을 지킬 테니 나더러 다른 쪽을 지키라고 일렀다. 나는 주목나무 한 그루 뒤에 자리를 잡았고, 그의 검은 형체가 움직이다가 사이에 놓인 비석들과 나무들에 가려 시야에서 사라지는 것을 지켜보았다.\n\n그것은 외로운 불침번이었다. 자리를 잡은 직후 멀리서 시계가 열두 시를 치는 소리를 들었고, 이윽고 한 시, 두 시가 울렸다. 나는 몸이 시리고 신경이 곤두선 데다, 나를 이런 일에 끌어들인 교수에게도, 따라온 나 자신에게도 화가 났다. 너무 추웠고 너무 졸렸으니 예리하게 관찰할 수도 없었고, 그렇다고 맡은 소임을 저버릴 만큼 졸린 것도 아니어서, 한마디로 나는 음울하고 비참한 시간을 보냈다.\n\n문득 돌아서다가, 나는 무덤에서 가장 먼 쪽 묘지 가장자리의 어두운 주목나무 두 그루 사이로 흰 줄기 같은 무언가가 움직이는 것을 본 듯했다. 동시에 교수가 있는 쪽 땅에서 검은 덩어리가 움직여 서둘러 그쪽으로 향했다. 그래서 나도 움직였다. 그러나 나는 비석들과 난간으로 둘러쳐진 무덤들을 돌아가야 했고, 무덤들에 걸려 비틀거렸다. 하늘은 잔뜩 흐렸고, 어딘가 멀리서 이른 수탉이 울었다. 조금 떨어진 곳, 교회로 이어지는 길을 표시하는 드문드문한 노간주나무 줄 너머로, 희고 어렴풋한 형체가 무덤 쪽으로 휙 스쳐갔다. 무덤 자체는 나무에 가려져 있어, 그 형체가 어디로 사라졌는지는 볼 수 없었다. 내가 처음 흰 형체를 보았던 곳에서 실제로 무언가 움직이는 바스락 소리가 들려, 그쪽으로 가보니 교수가 자그마한 아이 하나를 품에 안고 있었다. 그는 나를 보자 아이를 내밀며 말했다.\n\n\"이제 만족하오?\"\n\n\"아니오,\" 하고 나는 스스로도 공격적이라고 느낀 투로 말했다.\n\n\"이 아이가 보이지 않소?\"\n\n\"보입니다, 아이로군요. 그러나 누가 이 아이를 여기 데려왔단 말이오? 그리고 다치기는 했소?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"보면 알겠지요,\" 하고 교수가 말했다. 그리고 우리는 한마음으로 묘지에서 빠져나왔는데, 그가 잠든 아이를 안고 있었다.\n\n조금 떨어진 곳까지 가서, 우리는 나무가 우거진 곳으로 들어가 성냥을 켜고 아이의 목을 살펴보았다. 긁힌 자국도 흉터도 하나 없었다.\n\n\"내 말이 옳았지요?\" 하고 나는 의기양양하게 물었다.\n\n\"우리가 때마침 닿은 것이오,\" 하고 교수가 고마워하며 말했다.\n\n이제 우리는 이 아이를 어떻게 할지 정해야 했으므로, 그것을 의논했다. 만일 아이를 경찰서로 데려간다면 우리는 밤사이의 행적을 어느 정도 설명해야 할 터였다. 적어도 우리가 어떻게 이 아이를 찾게 되었는지 무언가 진술을 해야 했을 것이다. 그래서 마침내 우리는 아이를 히스 들판으로 데려가, 순경이 오는 소리가 들리면 그가 틀림없이 찾을 만한 곳에 놓아두기로 했다. 그러고 나서 가능한 한 빨리 집으로 가는 길을 찾기로 했다. 모든 것이 잘 풀렸다. 햄프스테드 히스의 가장자리에서 우리는 순경의 묵직한 발소리를 들었고, 아이를 길에 눕히고는, 그가 등불을 이리저리 비추다가 아이를 발견할 때까지 기다리며 지켜보았다. 그의 놀란 외침이 들렸고, 그러자 우리는 조용히 자리를 떴다. 다행히도 우리는 \"스패니어즈\" 근처에서 마차 한 대를 잡아 시내로 향했다.\n\n잠이 오지 않아 이 기록을 적는다. 그러나 반 헬싱이 정오에 나를 부르러 오기로 했으니, 몇 시간이라도 자보려 애써야겠다. 그는 또 다른 원정에 자기와 함께 가야 한다고 우긴다.\n\n9월 27일. — 우리가 시도하기에 적당한 기회를 찾은 것은 두 시가 되어서였다. 정오에 거행된 장례가 모두 끝나고, 조문객 가운데 마지막으로 처진 이들마저 느릿느릿 자리를 뜬 뒤, 오리나무 덤불 뒤에서 조심스레 살펴보니 묘지기가 자기 뒤로 문을 잠그는 것이 보였다. 그제야 우리는 원한다면 아침까지는 안전하다는 것을 알았다. 그러나 교수는 우리에게 길어야 한 시간 이상은 필요치 않을 것이라고 말했다. 다시금 나는 그 끔찍한 현실감을, 그 어떤 상상의 노력도 어울리지 않게 여겨지는 그 감각을 느꼈다. 그리고 우리가 이 불경한 일로 자초하고 있는 법적 위험을 또렷이 깨달았다. 게다가 나는 이 모든 일이 너무도 부질없게 느껴졌다. 거의 일주일이나 죽어 있던 여인이 정말로 죽었는지 보겠다고 납관을 여는 일도 터무니없었거니와, 우리 자신의 두 눈으로 본 증거로 관이 비어 있음을 알면서 다시 무덤을 여는 일은 이제 어리석음의 극치로만 여겨졌다. 그래도 나는 어깨를 으쓱하고는 잠자코 있었으니, 반 헬싱에게는 누가 항의하든 제 길을 가는 버릇이 있었기 때문이다. 그는 열쇠를 꺼내 묘실을 열고, 또다시 정중하게 나더러 먼저 들어가라고 손짓했다. 그곳은 지난밤만큼 으스스하지는 않았으나, 아, 햇살이 쏟아져 들어오니 말할 수 없이 초라해 보였다. 반 헬싱이 루시의 관 쪽으로 걸어갔고, 나는 그 뒤를 따랐다. 그는 몸을 숙여 다시 납 테두리를 젖혔다. 그러자 놀라움과 경악의 충격이 나를 꿰뚫었다.\n\n거기 루시가 누워 있었다, 마치 그녀의 장례 전날 밤에 우리가 보았던 그 모습 그대로. 그녀는 가능하다면 그 어느 때보다도 더 환하게 아름다웠다. 나는 그녀가 죽었다는 것을 믿을 수가 없었다. 입술은 붉었으니, 아니, 전보다 더 붉었고, 두 뺨에는 고운 혈색이 어려 있었다.\n\n\"이건 무슨 속임수요?\" 하고 내가 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"이제 확신하겠소?\" 교수가 대답으로 말했다. 그리고 그렇게 말하면서 그는 손을 뻗어, 나를 몸서리치게 하는 방식으로, 죽은 입술을 젖혀 흰 이를 드러내 보였다.\n\n\"보시오,\" 하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"보시오, 전보다 더 날카롭기까지 하오. 이것과 이것으로\" — 그러면서 그는 송곳니 하나와 그 아래의 이를 만졌다 — \"어린아이들이 물릴 수 있는 것이오. 이제 믿겠소, 존?\" 다시 한 번 따지려 드는 적개심이 내 안에서 깨어났다. 나는 그가 시사하는 그 압도적인 생각을 받아들일 수가 없었다. 그래서, 그 순간조차 스스로 부끄럽게 여긴 반박을 시도하며 나는 말했다.\n\n\"지난밤 이후에 누군가 그녀를 여기 갖다놓았을 수도 있지요.\"\n\n\"그렇소? 그럴 수도 있겠지, 한데 누가 말이오?\"\n\n\"모르겠소. 누군가 그리한 것이지요.\"\n\n\"그런데도 그녀는 일주일이나 죽어 있었소. 대개의 사람들은 그 시간이면 이렇게 보이지 않을 것이오.\" 나는 이에 답할 말이 없어 잠자코 있었다. 반 헬싱은 내 침묵을 알아차린 것 같지 않았으니, 어쨌든 그는 분함도 의기양양함도 내보이지 않았다. 그는 죽은 여인의 얼굴을 유심히 들여다보며 눈꺼풀을 들어 눈을 살피고, 다시 한 번 입술을 벌려 이를 살폈다. 그러고는 나를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"여기, 기록된 모든 것과는 다른 한 가지가 있소. 여기에는 흔한 경우와는 다른 어떤 이중의 삶이 있소. 그녀는 몽유 상태로, 잠결에 거닐던 중에 흡혈귀에게 물렸소 — 아, 당신이 놀라는구려. 그것을 모르고 있었군, 존. 그러나 나중에 모든 것을 알게 될 것이오 — 몽유 상태에서야말로 그가 더 많은 피를 가져가기에 가장 좋았던 것이오. 몽유 상태에서 그녀는 죽었고, 또한 몽유 상태로 그녀는 불사자(不死者)가 되었소. 그래서 그녀가 다른 모든 이와 다른 것이오. 보통 불사자가 제 집에서 잠들 때면\" — 그렇게 말하며 그는 흡혈귀에게 '집'이 무엇인지를 가리키려는 듯 팔을 크게 휘둘렀다 — \"그 얼굴이 정체를 드러내는 법이지만, 이토록 사랑스러웠던 그녀는, 불사자가 아니었을 적에는 흔한 죽은 자의 무로 돌아가는 것이오. 보시오, 거기에는 아무런 악의가 없소. 그러니 내가 그녀를 잠든 채로 죽여야 한다는 것이 더더욱 어려운 일이오.\" 이 말에 내 피가 싸늘하게 식었고, 내가 반 헬싱의 이론을 받아들이고 있다는 사실이 차츰 머리에 떠오르기 시작했다. 그러나 만일 그녀가 정말로 죽었다면, 그녀를 죽인다는 생각에 무슨 공포가 있겠는가? 그가 나를 올려다보았는데, 분명 내 얼굴에 어린 변화를 본 모양이었다. 그는 거의 기쁜 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"아, 이제 믿는구려?\"\n\n나는 대답했다. \"한꺼번에 너무 몰아붙이지는 마시오. 받아들일 용의는 있소. 이 피비린내 나는 일을 어떻게 할 작정이오?\"\n\n\"나는 그녀의 머리를 잘라내고 입에 마늘을 채운 다음, 그녀의 몸에 말뚝을 박을 것이오.\" 내가 사랑했던 여인의 몸을 그토록 훼손한다고 생각하니 몸서리가 쳐졌다. 그래도 그 느낌은 내가 예상했던 만큼 강하지는 않았다. 사실 나는 이 존재, 반 헬싱이 불사자라 부르는 이것의 존재 앞에서 몸서리치고, 이것을 혐오하기 시작하고 있었다. 사랑이란 온통 주관적인 것일까, 아니면 온통 객관적인 것일까?\n\n나는 반 헬싱이 시작하기를 꽤 오래 기다렸으나, 그는 마치 생각에 잠긴 듯 서 있었다. 이윽고 그는 가방의 잠금쇠를 딸깍 닫으며 말했다.\n\n\"내가 줄곧 생각해서, 무엇이 최선인지 마음을 정했소. 단지 내 마음 내키는 대로 한다면 지금, 이 순간에 해야 할 일을 하겠지만, 따라야 할 다른 일들이, 그것도 우리가 알지 못하기에 천 배는 더 어려운 일들이 있소. 이것은 간단하오. 그녀는 아직 누구의 목숨도 앗지 않았소, 비록 그것은 시간문제이지만 말이오. 지금 손을 쓴다면 그녀에게서 위험을 영영 거두어버릴 수 있을 것이오. 그러나 그렇게 되면 우리는 아서가 필요해질지도 모르는데, 그에게 이 일을 어떻게 말한단 말이오? 루시의 목에 난 상처를 보고, 병원에서 그 아이의 목에 난 그토록 비슷한 상처를 본 당신, 지난밤 빈 관을 보고 오늘은 죽은 지 일주일이 다 되도록 더 발그레하고 더 아름다워지기만 했을 뿐 변하지 않은 여인으로 가득 찬 관을 본 당신 — 이것을 알고, 또 지난밤 아이를 묘지로 데려온 흰 형체를 알면서도, 당신 자신의 감각으로조차 믿지 못했거늘, 그렇다면 그 어느 것도 알지 못하는 아서가 믿어주기를 내 어찌 기대할 수 있겠소? 그녀가 죽어가며 입맞춤하려 할 때 내가 그를 떼어놓았을 적에도 그는 나를 의심했소. 나는 그가 나를 용서한 줄 아오. 어떤 잘못된 생각에서 내가 그로 하여금 마땅히 해야 할 작별 인사를 못 하게 만든 일을 두고 말이오. 그런데 그는 또 어떤 더 잘못된 생각에서 이 여인이 산 채로 매장되었다고 여길지도 모르고, 무엇보다도 가장 잘못된 생각에서 우리가 그녀를 죽였다고 여길지도 모르오. 그러면 그는 그녀를 죽인 것이 바로 우리, 그릇된 생각에 빠진 우리이며, 우리의 그 생각 탓이라고 되받아 따질 것이오. 그리하여 그는 늘 무척 불행할 것이오. 게다가 그는 결코 확신할 수 없을 텐데, 그것이 무엇보다 가장 나쁜 일이오. 그는 때로 자기가 사랑한 그녀가 산 채로 묻혔다고 생각할 것이고, 그러면 그녀가 겪었을 고통의 공포가 그의 꿈을 물들일 것이오. 또 어떤 때는 우리가 옳았을지도 모른다고, 그토록 사랑하던 이가 결국 불사자였다고 생각할 것이오. 안 되오! 나는 한 번 그에게 말해보았고, 그 후로 많은 것을 알게 되었소. 이제 그 모든 것이 진실임을 아니, 그가 단것에 닿기 위해서는 쓰디쓴 물을 헤치고 가야 한다는 것을 십만 배는 더 확실히 아오. 가엾은 친구, 그는 천국의 얼굴마저 그에게 캄캄하게 보이게 만들 한 시간을 겪어야 하오. 그러고 나서야 우리는 모두를 위해 옳게 행하고 그에게 평안을 줄 수 있소. 내 마음은 정해졌소. 자, 갑시다. 당신은 오늘 밤 당신의 정신병원 집으로 돌아가, 만사가 괜찮은지 살피시오. 나로 말하면, 나는 이 밤을 나 나름의 방식으로 이 묘지에서 보내겠소. 내일 밤 열 시에 버클리 호텔로 나를 찾아오시오. 나는 아서도 오라고 사람을 보내겠고, 또 피를 준 그 훌륭한 미국 청년에게도 그리하겠소. 나중에 우리 모두에게 할 일이 있을 것이오. 나는 피커딜리까지 당신과 함께 가서 거기서 식사를 하겠소. 해가 지기 전에 이곳으로 돌아와야 하니 말이오.\"\n\n그리하여 우리는 무덤을 잠그고 그곳을 떠나, 그리 어렵지 않게 묘지 담을 넘어, 마차를 타고 피커딜리로 돌아왔다.\n\n「반 헬싱이 버클리 호텔에서 자신의 여행 가방에 남겨둔, 의학박사 존 수어드 앞으로 된 쪽지.」\n\n(전달되지 않음.)\n\n\"9월 27일.\n\n벗 존에게,\n\n혹 무슨 일이 생길 경우에 대비하여 이것을 쓰오. 나는 홀로 저 묘지에서 지키러 가오. 불사자인 루시 양이 오늘 밤에는 나가지 않아, 내일 밤에는 그만큼 더 갈망에 차게 될 것이니 나는 그것이 흡족하오. 그러므로 나는 그녀가 싫어하는 몇 가지 — 마늘과 십자고상 — 을 놓아두어 무덤의 문을 봉할 것이오. 그녀는 불사자로서 아직 어리니, 그것에 따를 것이오. 더욱이 이것들은 다만 그녀가 나오는 것을 막을 뿐, 그녀가 들어가고 싶어하는 것까지 막지는 못할지도 모르오. 그럴 때 불사자는 필사적이라, 그것이 무엇이든 가장 저항이 적은 길을 찾아내고야 마니 말이오. 나는 해 질 녘부터 해 뜬 뒤까지 밤새 가까이 있을 것이며, 만일 알아낼 만한 것이 있다면 알아낼 것이오. 루시 양에 대해서나 그녀로부터는 나는 두려움이 없소. 그러나 그녀를 불사자로 만든 그 다른 자는 이제 그녀의 무덤을 찾아 은신처로 삼을 힘을 지녔소. 그는 교활하오, 조너선 군에게서 듣기로도, 또 그가 루시 양의 목숨을 두고 우리와 농락하며 줄곧 우리를 우롱하여 우리가 진 그 방식으로 보아도 그러하오. 그리고 여러모로 불사자는 강하오. 그는 늘 그 손에 스무 명의 힘을 지녔소. 루시 양에게 우리의 힘을 준 우리 넷의 힘조차 모두 그에게 가 있는 것이오. 게다가 그는 자기 늑대를 불러낼 수도, 또 내가 알지 못하는 무언가를 불러낼 수도 있소. 그러니 만일 그가 오늘 밤 그곳에 온다면 그는 나를 만나게 될 것이오. 그러나 다른 누구도 만나지 못할 것이오 — 너무 늦을 때까지는 말이오. 그러나 어쩌면 그는 그곳을 노리지 않을지도 모르오. 그가 그래야 할 이유가 없으니, 그의 사냥터는 불사자 여인이 잠들고 한 늙은이가 지키는 묘지보다 사냥감이 더 그득하니 말이오.\n\n그러므로 나는 만일의 경우에 대비하여 이것을 쓰오……. 이것과 함께 있는 서류들, 곧 하커와 그 밖의 사람들의 일기를 가져다 읽으시오. 그런 다음 이 거대한 불사자를 찾아내어 그 머리를 자르고 심장을 태우거나 거기에 말뚝을 박아, 세상이 그에게서 벗어나 쉴 수 있게 하시오.\n\n만일 그리되거든, 안녕히.\n\n반 헬싱.\"\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기.」\n9월 28일. — 하룻밤 잘 자고 나면 사람에게 얼마나 좋은 일이 생기는지 놀랍기만 하다. 어제 나는 반 헬싱의 그 끔찍한 생각을 거의 받아들이려 했다. 그러나 이제 그것들은 상식에 대한 모욕으로서 내 앞에 섬뜩하게 불거져 나오는 것 같다. 그가 그 모든 것을 믿고 있다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다. 그의 정신이 어떤 식으로든 균형을 잃은 것이 아닌가 하는 생각이 든다. 분명 이 모든 불가사의한 일들에는 어떤 합리적인 설명이 있을 것이다. 혹 교수 자신이 그 일을 저지른 것은 아닐까? 그는 비정상적으로 영리한 사람이라, 만일 머리가 돈다면 어떤 고정관념에 관한 자기 의도를 놀라운 방식으로 실행에 옮길 것이다. 그렇게 생각하기는 싫고, 실로 반 헬싱이 미쳤음을 알게 되는 것도 다른 일 못지않게 큰 불가사의가 되겠지만, 어쨌든 나는 그를 주의 깊게 지켜보겠다. 이 수수께끼에 대해 어떤 실마리를 얻을 수도 있으리라.\n\n9월 29일, 아침. ……어젯밤, 열 시가 조금 못 되어 아서와 퀸시가 반 헬싱의 방으로 들어왔다. 그는 우리에게 자기가 우리가 해주기를 바라는 바를 모두 일러주었는데, 특히 아서에게 말을 건넸으니, 마치 우리 모두의 의지가 그에게 집중되어 있기라도 한 것 같았다. 그는 먼저, 우리 모두 자기와 함께 가주기를 바란다고 말하며 입을 열었다. \"왜냐하면,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"그곳에서 해야 할 막중한 의무가 있기 때문이오. 분명 내 편지에 놀라셨겠지요?\" 이 물음은 곧장 고달밍 경에게 던져진 것이었다.\n\n\"놀랐습니다. 한동안 적잖이 마음이 어지러웠지요. 요즘 우리 집안에 워낙 많은 우환이 있어서, 더는 사양하고 싶을 정도입니다. 게다가 무슨 뜻인지도 궁금했고요. 퀸시와 그 일을 의논해보았습니다만, 의논할수록 더 어리둥절해지기만 하더군요. 이제 저로서는 무슨 일이든 그 의미에 대해 도무지 갈피를 못 잡겠다고 말할 수밖에요.\"\n\n\"저도요,\" 하고 퀸시 모리스가 짤막하게 말했다.\n\n\"오,\" 하고 교수가 말했다. \"그렇다면 두 분 다, 시작에 닿기까지조차 한참을 거슬러 가야 하는 여기 이 존보다는 출발점에 더 가까운 셈이구려.\"\n\n내가 한마디도 하지 않았는데도 그는 분명 내가 옛 회의(懷疑)의 심정으로 되돌아간 것을 알아차린 것이었다. 그러고는 다른 두 사람을 돌아보며, 그는 더없이 진지하게 말했다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 내가 좋다고 여기는 일을 할 수 있도록 두 분의 허락을 구하오. 이것이 큰 부탁임을 나는 아오. 그리고 내가 무엇을 하려는지 알게 되면, 그제야 비로소 그것이 얼마나 큰 부탁인지도 알게 될 것이오. 그러니 부디 영문도 모른 채 나에게 약속해주기를 청해도 되겠소? 그래서 나중에, 비록 한동안은 나에게 화가 날지라도 — 그럴 가능성을 나 스스로 감추어서는 안 되겠지만 — 무슨 일이든 두 분 자신을 탓하지는 않도록 말이오.\"\n\n\"그건 어쨌든 솔직한 말씀이군요,\" 하고 퀸시가 끼어들었다. \"저는 교수님을 두고 보증하겠습니다. 그 속내가 무엇인지는 잘 모르겠습니다만, 정직한 분임은 맹세할 수 있습니다. 저에게는 그것으로 충분합니다.\"\n\n\"고맙소, 그대여,\" 하고 반 헬싱이 자랑스럽게 말했다. \"나는 그대를 신뢰할 만한 벗 하나로 헤아리는 영예를 누렸거니와, 그런 보증은 나에게 소중하오.\" 그가 손을 내밀자, 퀸시가 그 손을 잡았다.\n\n그러자 아서가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"반 헬싱 박사, 저는 스코틀랜드 사람들 말마따나 '자루 속의 돼지를 사는' 것은 그리 내키지 않습니다. 그리고 만일 그것이 신사로서의 제 명예나 기독교도로서의 제 신앙에 관계되는 일이라면, 저는 그런 약속을 할 수 없습니다. 박사께서 의도하시는 바가 이 둘 가운데 어느 것도 침해하지 않는다고 보증해주신다면, 저는 당장 동의하겠습니다. 비록 도무지 박사께서 무엇을 겨냥하시는지는 이해할 수 없지만요.\"\n\n\"그 단서를 받아들이겠소,\" 하고 반 헬싱이 말했다. \"내가 그대에게 청하는 것은 다만, 혹 내 어떤 행동을 비난해야 한다고 느껴지거든, 먼저 그것을 잘 헤아려 그것이 그대의 유보 조건을 침해하지 않음을 확인해달라는 것뿐이오.\"\n\n\"좋습니다!\" 하고 아서가 말했다. \"그것이야말로 공정한 일이지요. 자, 이제 예비 절충이 끝났으니, 우리가 무엇을 해야 하는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\"\n\n\"그대가 나와 함께, 그것도 은밀히, 킹스테드의 묘지로 가주기를 바라오.\"\n\n아서의 얼굴이 어두워졌고, 그는 어리둥절한 듯이 말했다.\n\n\"가엾은 루시가 묻혀 있는 곳 말입니까?\" 교수가 고개를 숙였다. 아서가 말을 이었다. \"그래서 거기 가서는요?\"\n\n\"무덤에 들어가는 것이오!\" 아서가 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n\"교수님, 진심이십니까, 아니면 무슨 끔찍한 농담인가요? 죄송합니다, 진심이신 것을 알겠군요.\" 그는 다시 앉았으나, 위엄을 지키는 사람처럼 꼿꼿하고 당당하게 앉는 것을 나는 볼 수 있었다. 그가 다시 물을 때까지 침묵이 흘렀다.\n\n\"그래서 무덤에 들어가서는요?\"\n\n\"관을 여는 것이오.\"\n\n\"이건 너무합니다!\" 하고 그가 성을 내며 다시 일어섰다. \"저는 사리에 맞는 일이라면 무엇이든 참을 용의가 있습니다. 그러나 이것은 — 이 무덤에 대한 모독은 — 그것도…….\" 그는 그야말로 분에 받쳐 목이 메었다. 교수는 가엾다는 듯이 그를 바라보았다.\n\n\"단 하나의 고통이라도 그대에게서 덜어줄 수 있다면, 가엾은 벗이여,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"하느님께서 아시거니와, 나는 그리하겠소. 그러나 오늘 밤 우리의 발은 가시밭길을 디뎌야 하오. 그러지 않으면 훗날, 그리고 영영, 그대가 사랑하는 그 발이 불길의 길을 걸어야 하오!\"\n\n아서는 굳어진 창백한 얼굴로 올려다보며 말했다.\n\n\"말씀 조심하시지요, 선생, 조심하십시오!\"\n\n\"내가 할 말을 들어보는 것이 좋지 않겠소?\" 하고 반 헬싱이 말했다. \"그러면 적어도 내 의도의 한계만큼은 알게 될 것이오. 계속해도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"그쯤이야 좋습니다,\" 하고 모리스가 끼어들었다.\n\n잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 반 헬싱은, 분명 안간힘을 쓰며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"루시 양은 죽었소. 그렇지 않소? 그렇소! 그러니 그녀에게 잘못될 일이란 있을 수 없소. 그러나 만일 그녀가 죽지 않았다면…….\"\n\n아서가 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n\"맙소사!\" 하고 그가 외쳤다. \"무슨 말씀이십니까? 무슨 착오라도 있었던 겁니까? 그녀가 산 채로 매장되기라도 했단 말입니까?\" 그는 희망조차 누그러뜨릴 수 없는 비통함 속에서 신음했다.\n\n\"그녀가 살아 있다고 말한 것은 아니오, 나의 아이여. 나는 그리 생각하지도 않소. 다만 그녀가 불사자일지도 모른다는 데까지만 말하는 것이오.\"\n\n\"불사자라니! 살아 있지는 않다니! 무슨 말씀이십니까? 이게 다 악몽입니까, 아니면 대체 무엇입니까?\"\n\n\"사람이 그저 어렴풋이 짐작만 할 수 있는, 세월이 흐르고 흘러도 다만 일부분밖에 풀어내지 못하는 신비들이 있소. 나를 믿으시오, 우리는 지금 그런 신비 하나의 문턱에 서 있소. 그러나 나는 아직 말을 끝내지 않았소. 내가 죽은 루시 양의 머리를 잘라내도 되겠소?\"\n\n\"하늘과 땅에 맹세코, 안 됩니다!\" 하고 아서가 격정의 폭풍 속에서 외쳤다. \"이 넓은 세상을 다 준다 해도 저는 그녀의 시신을 훼손하는 데에는 결코 동의하지 않겠습니다. 반 헬싱 박사, 너무 저를 시험하시는군요. 제가 박사께 무슨 짓을 했기에 이토록 저를 괴롭히시는 겁니까? 그 가엾고 사랑스러운 처녀가 무슨 짓을 했기에 박사께서 그녀의 무덤에 그런 치욕을 안기려 하시는 겁니까? 그런 말을 입에 담으시다니 박사께서 미치신 겁니까, 아니면 그런 말을 듣고 있는 제가 미친 겁니까? 그런 모독을 더는 생각조차 마십시오. 저는 박사께서 하시는 그 어떤 일에도 동의하지 않겠습니다. 저에게는 그녀의 무덤을 능욕으로부터 지킬 의무가 있고, 맹세코 저는 그것을 다하겠습니다!\"\n\n반 헬싱은 그동안 줄곧 앉아 있던 자리에서 일어나, 엄숙하고 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"고달밍 경, 나에게도 다해야 할 의무가 있소. 다른 이들에 대한 의무, 그대에 대한 의무, 죽은 이에 대한 의무 말이오. 그리고 맹세코 나는 그것을 다하겠소! 지금 내가 그대에게 청하는 것은 다만, 나와 함께 가서 보고 들어달라는 것뿐이오. 그리고 만일 훗날 내가 같은 청을 했을 때, 그대가 나보다도 더 그 일이 이루어지기를 바라지 않거든, 그때는 — 그때는 나에게 어떻게 보이든 나는 내 의무를 다하겠소. 그러고 나서 경의 뜻에 따라, 그대가 원하는 때와 곳에서 그대에게 해명할 수 있도록 내 몸을 그대의 처분에 맡기겠소.\" 그의 목소리가 약간 갈라졌고, 그는 연민 가득한 목소리로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그러나 부디 청하건대, 나에게 화를 품은 채 떠나지는 마시오. 종종 하기에 달갑지 않은, 때로는 내 가슴을 쥐어짜는 일들로 점철된 긴 평생에, 나는 지금처럼 이토록 무거운 임무를 맡아본 적이 없소. 나를 믿으시오, 혹 그대가 나에 대한 마음을 바꿀 때가 온다면, 그대의 눈길 한 번이 이 슬픈 시간을 모두 씻어주리니, 나는 그대를 슬픔에서 구하기 위해 사람이 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 하겠소. 한번 생각해보시오. 무엇 때문에 내가 이토록 많은 수고와 이토록 많은 슬픔을 떠안겠소? 나는 내 나라에서 이곳까지 와서, 내가 할 수 있는 선을 행하려 하고 있소. 처음에는 나의 벗 존을 기쁘게 하기 위해, 그다음에는 사랑스러운 젊은 숙녀를 돕기 위해서였는데, 나 또한 그녀를 사랑하게 되었소. 그녀를 위해 — 이런 말을 이렇게 많이 하기가 부끄럽지만, 나는 호의에서 말하는 것이오 — 나는 그대가 준 것을 주었소. 곧 내 혈관의 피 말이오. 그대처럼 그녀의 연인도 아니고 다만 그녀의 의사이자 벗이었을 뿐인 내가 그것을 주었소. 나는 그녀에게 내 낮과 밤을 바쳤소 — 죽기 전에도, 죽은 뒤에도. 그리고 내 죽음이 지금, 그녀가 죽은 불사자가 된 이 마당에라도 그녀에게 도움이 된다면, 나는 기꺼이 그것을 내어주겠소.\" 그는 이 말을 무척 엄숙하고도 다정한 긍지로 말했고, 아서는 이에 크게 감동했다. 그는 노인의 손을 잡고 떨리는 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"아, 그것을 생각하기란 괴롭고, 저는 이해할 수도 없습니다. 그러나 적어도 박사와 함께 가서 기다려 보겠습니다.\"\n\n우리가 낮은 담을 넘어 묘지 안으로 들어선 것은 정각 열두 시 십오 분 전이었다. 밤은 어두웠고, 하늘을 가로질러 빠르게 흘러가는 짙은 구름의 갈라진 틈 사이로 이따금 달빛이 비쳤다. 우리는 모두 어떻게든 서로 가까이 붙어 있었고, 판 헬싱이 약간 앞장서서 길을 인도했다. 무덤 가까이 이르렀을 때 나는 아서를 유심히 살폈으니, 이토록 슬픈 기억이 서린 곳에 가까이 다가가는 일이 그를 무너뜨리지나 않을까 두려웠던 까닭이다. 그러나 그는 잘 견뎌냈다. 나는 이 일의 신비로움 자체가 어떤 식으로든 그의 슬픔을 누그러뜨리는 작용을 한 것이라 여겼다. 교수는 문을 열었고, 여러 가지 이유로 우리 사이에 당연한 망설임이 이는 것을 보고는, 자신이 먼저 들어감으로써 그 난처함을 풀어주었다. 나머지 우리도 뒤따라 들어갔고, 그는 문을 닫았다. 그러고는 어둠 등(燈)을 켜고 관을 가리켰다. 아서가 머뭇거리며 앞으로 나섰다. 판 헬싱이 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"자네는 어제 나와 함께 여기 있었지. 저 관 속에 루시 양의 시신이 있었나?\"\n\n\"있었습니다.\" 교수는 나머지 사람들을 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"들으셨지요. 그런데도 나와 같이 믿지 않는 이가 없습니다.\" 그는 드라이버를 들어 다시 관 뚜껑을 떼어냈다. 아서는 몹시 창백했으나 말없이 지켜보았다. 뚜껑이 벗겨지자 그는 앞으로 나섰다. 그는 분명 납으로 된 관이 있다는 것을 몰랐거나, 적어도 거기까지 생각이 미치지는 못했던 모양이다. 납에 난 찢긴 자국을 보았을 때, 한순간 피가 그의 얼굴로 솟구쳤으나 곧 다시 가셔서, 그는 소름 끼칠 만큼 창백한 채로 있었다. 그는 여전히 말이 없었다. 판 헬싱이 납의 가장자리를 도로 밀어 젖히자, 우리는 모두 안을 들여다보고는 흠칫 물러섰다.\n\n관은 비어 있었다!\n\n몇 분 동안 아무도 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그 침묵을 깬 것은 퀸시 모리스였다.\n\n\"교수님, 저는 교수님을 믿고 보증했습니다. 교수님의 말씀이면 그것으로 족합니다. 평소라면 이런 것을 묻지 않았을 겁니다. 의심을 내비쳐 교수님을 욕보이는 짓은 하지 않았을 거예요. 그러나 이것은 어떤 명예나 불명예를 넘어서는 수수께끼입니다. 이게 교수님이 하신 일입니까?\"\n\n\"내가 신성하게 여기는 모든 것을 두고 맹세하건대, 나는 그녀를 옮기지도 손대지도 않았소. 일어난 일은 이러하오. 이틀 전 밤에 내 벗 수어드와 내가 이곳에 왔소. 부디 믿으시오, 좋은 뜻으로 왔던 것이오. 그때 봉인되어 있던 저 관을 내가 열었고, 우리는 그것이 지금처럼 비어 있는 것을 보았소. 그런 뒤 기다렸더니, 무언가 흰 것이 나무들 사이로 다가오는 것을 보았소. 이튿날 우리는 낮에 이곳에 왔고, 그녀는 저기에 누워 있었소. 그렇지 않았나, 존?\"\n\n\"그렇습니다.\"\n\"그날 밤 우리는 가까스로 때를 맞추었소. 또 한 명의 작디작은 아이가 사라졌는데, 다행히도 우리는 그 아이를 무덤들 사이에서 다친 데 없이 찾아냈소. 어제는 해가 지기 전에 이곳에 왔으니, 해가 지면 비로소 불사귀가 움직일 수 있기 때문이오. 나는 해가 뜰 때까지 밤새 여기서 기다렸으나 아무것도 보지 못했소. 아마도 그것은, 불사귀가 견디지 못하는 마늘을, 그리고 그들이 꺼리는 다른 것들을 저 문의 죔쇠 위에 발라두었던 까닭일 것이오. 어젯밤에는 빠져나간 일이 없었으니, 오늘 밤 해가 지기 전에 나는 마늘과 다른 것들을 치웠소. 그래서 우리가 이 빈 관을 보게 된 것이오. 그러나 조금만 참아주시오. 여기까지도 기이한 것이 많소. 보이지도 들리지도 않게 나와 함께 밖에서 기다려주시오. 훨씬 더 기이한 일들이 아직 남아 있소. 자, 그러면\" — 여기서 그는 등의 어두운 덮개를 닫았다 — \"이제 밖으로 나갑시다.\" 그는 문을 열었고, 우리는 줄지어 나갔으며, 그가 맨 마지막으로 나와 등 뒤로 문을 잠갔다.\n\n아! 그러나 그 지하 납골소의 공포를 겪은 뒤 밤공기는 어찌나 신선하고 맑게 느껴지던지. 구름이 빠르게 흘러가는 것을, 그리고 질주하는 구름들이 서로 엇갈리며 지나가는 사이로 달빛이 스쳐 지나가는 것을 — 마치 한 사람의 생애의 기쁨과 슬픔처럼 — 보는 것은 어찌나 감미로웠던가. 죽음과 부패의 더러움이 없는 신선한 공기를 들이마시는 것은 어찌나 감미로웠던가. 언덕 너머 하늘이 붉게 물든 것을 보고, 멀리서 대도시의 삶을 알리는 둔탁한 굉음을 듣는 것은 어찌나 사람의 마음을 따스하게 했던가. 우리는 저마다의 방식으로 엄숙했고 또 감정에 압도되어 있었다. 아서는 말이 없었으며, 내가 보기에 그는 이 수수께끼의 목적과 그 깊은 뜻을 헤아려보려 애쓰고 있었다. 나 자신은 제법 인내심을 가지고 있었으며, 다시금 의심을 던져버리고 판 헬싱의 결론을 받아들이는 쪽으로 반쯤 기울어 있었다. 퀸시 모리스는 모든 것을 받아들이는 사람, 그것도 자기가 걸 수 있는 모든 것을 건 채 냉철한 용기의 정신으로 받아들이는 사람다운 침착함을 보였다. 담배를 피울 수 없었으므로 그는 제법 큼직한 씹는 담배 한 덩이를 잘라 씹기 시작했다. 판 헬싱으로 말하자면, 그는 분명한 목적을 가지고 무언가에 몰두해 있었다. 먼저 그는 가방에서 흰 냅킨에 정성스레 말려 있는, 얇고 웨이퍼 같은 비스킷처럼 보이는 것을 한 덩이 꺼냈다. 다음으로 그는 반죽이나 퍼티 같은 희끄무레한 것을 두 손 가득 꺼냈다. 그는 웨이퍼를 잘게 부수어 두 손 사이에서 그 덩어리에 이겨 넣었다. 그런 다음 그것을 가져다 가느다란 가닥으로 말아, 무덤 문과 그 문틀 사이의 틈에 끼워 넣기 시작했다. 나는 이것이 다소 의아하여, 가까이 있었던 까닭에 무엇을 하고 있는지 그에게 물었다. 아서와 퀸시도 궁금했던지 가까이 다가왔다. 그가 대답했다.\n\n\"나는 불사귀가 들어가지 못하도록 이 무덤을 봉하고 있는 것이오.\"\n\n\"그런데 거기 넣으신 그것으로 그게 될까요?\" 하고 퀸시가 물었다. \"맙소사! 이게 무슨 장난입니까?\"\n\n\"장난이 아니오.\"\n\"지금 쓰고 계신 것이 무엇입니까?\" 이번에는 아서가 물었다. 판 헬싱은 경건하게 모자를 들어 올리며 대답했다.\n\n\"성체(聖體)요. 암스테르담에서 가져왔소. 나에게는 사용을 허락받은 면벌(免罰)이 있소.\" 그것은 우리 가운데 가장 회의적인 사람마저 질리게 한 대답이었으며, 우리는 저마다, 교수처럼 진지한 목적, 그에게 가장 신성한 것마저 이렇게 쓸 수 있는 목적 앞에서는 의심한다는 것이 불가능하다고 느꼈다. 우리는 경건한 침묵 속에서 무덤 가까이, 그러나 다가오는 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않게 각자에게 정해진 자리를 잡았다. 나는 다른 이들이, 특히 아서가 가여웠다. 나 자신은 앞서 여러 번 이곳을 찾아오며 이 끔찍한 감시의 견습을 거친 셈이었으나, 그런 나조차도, 한 시간 전까지 그 증거들을 부정하던 내 가슴마저 속에서 내려앉는 것을 느꼈다. 무덤들이 그토록 소름 끼치게 희게 보인 적은 결코 없었다. 사이프러스나 주목, 노간주나무가 그토록 장례식 같은 음울함의 화신으로 보인 적은 결코 없었다. 나무나 풀이 그토록 불길하게 흔들리고 바스락거린 적은 결코 없었다. 가지가 그토록 알 수 없게 삐걱거린 적은 결코 없었다. 그리고 멀리서 들려오는 개들의 울부짖음이 그토록 비통한 전조를 밤새 실어 보낸 적은 결코 없었다.\n\n오랜 침묵이, 크고 쓰라린 공허가 이어졌고, 이윽고 교수에게서 날카로운 \"쉿—!\" 소리가 났다. 그가 가리켰다. 그러자 저 멀리 주목이 늘어선 길 아래로 흰 형체 하나가 다가오는 것이 보였다. 가슴에 검은 무언가를 안은, 어렴풋한 흰 형체였다. 그 형체가 멈추었고, 바로 그 순간 한 줄기 달빛이 질주하는 구름 덩어리 위로 떨어지며, 무덤의 수의를 걸친 검은 머리의 여인을 소스라치게 또렷이 드러내 보였다. 우리는 얼굴을 볼 수 없었으니, 그것이 우리가 보기에 금발의 아이로 보이는 무언가 위로 숙여져 있었던 까닭이다. 잠깐의 멈춤이 있었고, 이어 아이가 잠결에 내지르거나 개가 난롯불 앞에 누워 꿈을 꿀 때 내는 것 같은, 날카롭고 자그마한 외침이 들렸다. 우리는 앞으로 뛰쳐나가려 했으나, 주목 뒤에 서 있던 교수가 경고하듯 든 손이 우리 눈에 들어와 우리를 제지했다. 그리고 우리가 보고 있는 사이 흰 형체가 다시 앞으로 움직였다. 이제는 우리가 똑똑히 볼 수 있을 만큼 가까웠고, 달빛도 여전히 비치고 있었다. 우리가 루시 웨스튼라의 이목구비를 알아보았을 때, 내 심장은 얼음장처럼 차가워졌고, 아서가 숨을 헐떡이는 소리가 들렸다. 루시 웨스튼라였다. 그러나 어찌나 달라져 있었던가. 그 다정함은 금강석처럼 단단하고 무자비한 잔인함으로, 그 순결함은 음탕한 방종으로 바뀌어 있었다. 판 헬싱이 앞으로 나섰고, 그의 손짓에 따라 우리도 모두 나아갔다. 우리 넷은 무덤 문 앞에 한 줄로 늘어섰다. 판 헬싱이 등을 들어 올려 덮개를 열자, 루시의 얼굴에 모여 떨어지는 빛 아래로, 그 입술이 갓 흘린 피로 진홍빛이며, 핏물이 턱을 타고 흘러내려 흰 아마포 수의의 순결함을 더럽힌 것이 보였다.\n\n우리는 공포에 몸서리쳤다. 떨리는 불빛 속에서 나는 판 헬싱의 강철 같은 담력마저 무너진 것을 볼 수 있었다. 아서는 내 곁에 있었는데, 만일 내가 그의 팔을 붙들어 부축하지 않았더라면 그는 쓰러졌을 것이다.\n\n루시가 — 우리 앞에 있는 그것이 그녀의 모습을 하고 있었으므로 나는 그것을 루시라 부른다 — 우리를 보더니, 불시에 붙들린 고양이가 내는 것 같은 성난 으르렁거림과 함께 뒤로 물러섰다. 그러더니 그 눈이 우리를 훑었다. 모양과 빛깔은 루시의 눈이었다. 그러나 우리가 알던 그 맑고 부드러운 눈동자가 아니라, 더럽혀지고 지옥불로 가득 찬 루시의 눈이었다. 그 순간 내 사랑의 마지막 자취는 증오와 혐오로 바뀌었다. 만일 그때 그녀를 죽여야 했다면 나는 사나운 기쁨으로 그 일을 해낼 수 있었으리라. 그녀는 우리를 바라보는 동안, 그 눈이 부정한 빛으로 타올랐고, 얼굴에는 음탕한 미소가 어렸다. 오, 하느님, 그것을 보는 것이 나를 어찌나 몸서리치게 했던가! 그녀는 그때까지 개가 뼈다귀를 두고 으르렁대듯 그르렁거리며 가슴에 악착같이 끌어안고 있던 아이를, 악마처럼 무정하게, 아무렇지도 않은 동작으로 땅바닥에 내던졌다. 아이는 날카로운 비명을 지르고는 그 자리에 누워 신음했다. 그 행동에는 냉혹함이 서려 있어 아서에게서 신음을 자아냈다. 그녀가 두 팔을 벌리고 음탕한 미소를 띤 채 그에게 다가가자, 그는 뒤로 물러서며 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다.\n\n그러나 그녀는 여전히 다가오며, 나른하고 음탕한 우아함으로 말했다.\n\n\"이리 와요, 아서. 저 사람들을 두고 나에게로 와요. 내 팔이 당신을 애타게 그리워하고 있어요. 와요, 우리 함께 쉬어요. 와요, 내 남편이여, 와요!\"\n\n그녀의 목소리에는 무언가 악마처럼 감미로운 것이 — 유리가 부딪칠 때의 그 짜릿한 울림 같은 것이 — 깃들어 있어, 다른 이에게 건네진 말이었음에도 그것을 들은 우리의 머릿속까지 울려 퍼졌다. 아서로 말하자면, 그는 어떤 주문에 걸린 듯했다. 얼굴에서 두 손을 떼며 그는 두 팔을 활짝 벌렸다. 그녀가 그 팔을 향해 뛰어들려는 찰나, 판 헬싱이 앞으로 달려나가 두 사람 사이에 그의 자그마한 금 십자고상을 들이댔다. 그녀는 그것에서 움찔 물러섰고, 갑자기 일그러진 분노로 가득 찬 얼굴로, 마치 무덤 안으로 들어가려는 듯 그를 스쳐 내달렸다.\n\n그러나 문에서 한두 자쯤 떨어진 곳에 이르자, 그녀는 거역할 수 없는 어떤 힘에 붙들린 듯 멈춰 섰다. 그러더니 돌아섰고, 그녀의 얼굴이 환하게 쏟아지는 달빛과, 이제 판 헬싱의 강철 같은 담력에 더는 떨림이 없는 등불에 드러났다. 나는 그토록 좌절된 악의가 어린 얼굴을 본 적이 없으며, 그런 얼굴이 사람의 눈에 다시 보이는 일은 결코 없기를 바란다. 그 아름다운 빛깔은 검푸르게 변했고, 두 눈은 지옥불의 불꽃을 내뿜는 듯했으며, 미간은 살의 주름이 메두사의 뱀들이 똬리를 튼 것처럼 일그러졌고, 그 어여쁜, 피로 물든 입은 그리스와 일본의 격정의 가면처럼 네모나게 벌어졌다. 만일 죽음을 뜻하는 얼굴이 있다면 — 눈빛으로 사람을 죽일 수 있다면 — 우리는 그 순간 바로 그것을 본 것이었다.\n\n그리하여 영원처럼 느껴지는 꼬박 삼십 초 동안, 그녀는 치켜든 십자고상과, 신성하게 봉해진 자신의 출입구 사이에 머물러 있었다. 판 헬싱이 아서에게 물으며 그 침묵을 깼다.\n\n\"대답해주시오, 오 나의 벗이여! 내 일을 계속해도 되겠소?\"\n\n아서는 무릎을 꿇고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 대답했다.\n\n\"당신 뜻대로 하시오, 벗이여. 당신 뜻대로 하시오. 이런 끔찍한 일이 다시는 있을 수 없겠지요.\" 그러고는 그는 영혼 깊이 신음했다. 퀸시와 나는 동시에 그에게 다가가 그의 팔을 붙들었다. 판 헬싱이 등을 내려놓을 때 등이 닫히는 딸깍 소리가 들렸다. 그는 무덤 가까이 다가가, 거기에 끼워두었던 성스러운 표상의 일부를 틈에서 떼어내기 시작했다. 그가 뒤로 물러섰을 때, 그 순간 우리 자신의 몸만큼이나 실재하는 육신을 지닌 그 여인이 칼날 하나 겨우 들어갈 만한 틈으로 빠져 들어가는 것을 보고, 우리는 모두 경악에 차 소름 끼치며 지켜보았다. 교수가 침착하게 퍼티 가닥을 다시 문 가장자리에 붙이는 것을 보았을 때, 우리는 모두 안도의 기쁨을 느꼈다.\n\n이 일이 끝나자, 그는 아이를 들어 올리며 말했다.\n\n\"자 이제, 벗들이여, 우리는 내일까지 더는 할 일이 없소. 정오에 장례식이 있으니, 우리는 그 얼마 뒤에 모두 이곳으로 다시 올 것이오. 고인의 벗들은 두 시면 모두 떠날 것이고, 묘지기가 문을 잠그면 우리는 남아 있을 것이오. 그때 할 일이 더 있소. 그러나 오늘 밤 같은 일은 아니오. 이 어린것으로 말하자면, 크게 상한 데는 없고 내일 밤이면 괜찮아질 것이오. 우리는 지난밤처럼 이 아이를 경찰이 발견할 만한 곳에 두고, 그런 뒤 집으로 가겠소.\" 그는 아서에게 가까이 다가가 말했다.\n\n\"내 벗 아서, 그대는 쓰라린 시련을 겪었소. 그러나 나중에, 그대가 돌이켜볼 때면 그것이 어찌하여 필요했는지 알게 될 것이오. 그대는 지금 쓴 물 가운데 있소, 내 아이여. 내일 이맘때면, 하느님의 뜻이라면, 그대는 그 물을 다 건너 단물을 마시게 될 것이오. 그러니 너무 슬퍼하지 마시오. 그때까지 나는 그대에게 나를 용서해달라 청하지 않겠소.\"\n\n아서와 퀸시는 나와 함께 집으로 왔고, 우리는 오는 길에 서로를 위로하려 애썼다. 아이를 안전하게 두고 온 데다 지쳐 있었으므로, 우리는 모두 어느 정도는 참된 잠을 잤다.\n\n9월 29일, 밤. — 정각 열두 시 조금 전에 우리 셋 — 아서, 퀸시 모리스, 그리고 나 — 은 교수를 찾아갔다. 우리가 약속이라도 한 듯 모두 검은 옷을 입고 있는 것을 알아챈 것은 묘한 일이었다. 물론 아서는 깊은 상중이었으므로 검은 옷을 입었으나, 나머지 우리는 본능적으로 그리한 것이었다. 우리는 한 시 반쯤 묘지에 닿아, 관계자들의 눈에 띄지 않게 이리저리 거닐었고, 그리하여 인부들이 일을 마치고 묘지기가 모두 떠났다고 믿어 문을 잠갔을 때, 그곳은 온전히 우리 차지가 되었다. 판 헬싱은 작은 검은 가방 대신 길쭉한 가죽 가방을 가지고 있었는데, 크리켓 가방 같은 것이었다. 보아하니 제법 묵직했다.\n\n우리만 남고, 길 위로 멀어지는 마지막 발소리가 잦아드는 것을 들은 뒤, 우리는 말없이, 마치 미리 정해놓은 뜻에 따르듯, 교수를 따라 무덤으로 갔다. 그는 문을 열었고, 우리는 안으로 들어가 뒤로 문을 닫았다. 그러고는 그가 가방에서 등을 꺼내 불을 켰고, 밀랍 양초 두 자루도 꺼냈는데, 불을 붙인 뒤 그 끝을 녹여 다른 관들 위에 붙여, 일하기에 충분한 빛을 내게 했다. 그가 다시 루시의 관 뚜껑을 들어 올렸을 때 우리는 모두 들여다보았다 — 아서는 사시나무처럼 떨고 있었다 — 그리고 시신이 죽음의 아름다움을 온전히 간직한 채 거기 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 그러나 내 마음에는 사랑이라곤 없었고, 다만 루시의 영혼 없이 그 모습만을 빼앗아 간 그 더러운 것에 대한 혐오만이 있었다. 바라보는 동안 아서의 얼굴마저 굳어지는 것을 나는 볼 수 있었다. 이윽고 그가 판 헬싱에게 말했다.\n\n\"이것이 정말 루시의 시신입니까, 아니면 그녀의 모습을 한 악령일 뿐입니까?\"\n\n\"그녀의 몸이오. 그러면서도 그것이 아니오. 그러나 조금만 기다리시오. 그러면 그대들은 모두 그녀가 본디 그러했고 또 그러한 모습으로 그녀를 보게 될 것이오.\"\n\n거기 누운 그녀는 루시의 악몽 같았다. 뾰족한 이빨, 피로 물든 음탕한 입 — 그것을 보는 것만으로도 몸서리치게 했다 — 그 온통 육욕적이고 영혼 없는 모습은, 루시의 그 다정한 순결함에 대한 악마 같은 조롱처럼 보였다. 판 헬싱은 여느 때처럼 차근차근, 가방에서 여러 물건을 꺼내 쓸 수 있도록 늘어놓기 시작했다. 먼저 그는 납땜인두와 약간의 배관용 땜납을, 그다음에는 작은 기름등을 꺼냈는데, 무덤 한구석에서 불을 붙이자 그것은 푸른 불꽃으로 맹렬한 열을 내며 타는 가스를 뿜어냈다. 다음으로는 수술용 칼들을 손 닿는 곳에 놓았고, 마지막으로 두께가 두 치 반이나 세 치쯤, 길이가 석 자쯤 되는 둥근 나무 말뚝을 꺼냈다. 그 한쪽 끝은 불에 그슬려 단단하게 만든 뒤 뾰족하게 깎여 있었다. 이 말뚝과 함께, 가정에서 석탄 창고의 덩어리를 깰 때 쓰는 것 같은 묵직한 망치가 나왔다. 나에게 의사가 어떤 종류든 일을 준비하는 모습은 자극이 되고 기운을 돋우는 것이었으나, 이 물건들이 아서와 퀸시 두 사람에게 미친 영향은 일종의 경악이었다. 그러나 그들은 둘 다 용기를 잃지 않고, 말없이 조용히 있었다.\n\n모든 준비가 끝나자 판 헬싱이 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 무엇이든 하기에 앞서, 이것을 먼저 말해두겠소. 이는 옛사람들의, 그리고 불사귀의 힘을 연구해온 모든 이들의 학문과 경험에서 나온 것이오. 그들이 그런 존재가 될 때, 그 변화와 함께 불멸의 저주가 따르오. 그들은 죽을 수 없으며, 시대를 거듭하며 새로운 희생자를 더하고 세상의 악을 불려나가야만 하오. 불사귀의 먹이가 되어 죽는 자는 모두 스스로 불사귀가 되어 제 동족을 먹이로 삼게 되기 때문이오. 그리하여 그 고리는, 물에 던진 돌에서 퍼지는 물결처럼, 점점 더 넓게 끝없이 번져가오. 벗 아서, 만일 그대가, 가엾은 루시가 죽기 전에 그대가 아는 그 입맞춤을 받았더라면, 또는 어젯밤 그대가 그녀에게 두 팔을 벌렸을 때 그리했더라면, 그대도 머지않아, 그대가 죽었을 때, 동유럽에서 부르는 말로 노스페라투가 되어, 우리를 그토록 공포로 채우는 그런 불사귀들을 끝없이 더 만들어냈을 것이오. 이 가엾은 부인의 행로는 이제 막 시작되었을 뿐이오. 그녀가 피를 빨아먹은 저 아이들은 아직 그리 크게 상하지는 않았소. 그러나 그녀가 불사귀로 계속 살아간다면, 그들은 점점 더 피를 잃을 것이고, 그녀가 그들에게 미치는 힘으로 그들은 그녀에게로 오게 될 것이오. 그리하여 그녀는 그 사악하기 짝이 없는 입으로 그들의 피를 빨아들이는 것이오. 그러나 만일 그녀가 참으로 죽는다면, 그 모든 것이 멎소. 목에 난 그 자그마한 상처들은 사라지고, 그들은 무슨 일이 있었는지 영영 모른 채 제 놀이로 돌아갈 것이오. 그러나 무엇보다 가장 복된 일은, 지금 불사귀가 된 이 존재가 참된 죽음으로 안식하게 될 때, 우리가 사랑하는 그 가엾은 부인의 영혼이 다시 자유로워지리라는 것이오. 밤마다 사악함을 행하고 낮마다 그것을 제 것으로 삼으며 더욱 타락해가는 대신, 그녀는 다른 천사들과 함께 제자리를 차지하게 될 것이오. 그러니, 나의 벗이여, 그녀를 자유롭게 하는 일격을 가하는 손은 그녀에게 복된 손이 될 것이오. 나는 이 일을 기꺼이 맡겠소. 그러나 우리 가운데 그럴 더 나은 권리를 가진 이가 없겠소? 훗날, 잠이 오지 않는 고요한 밤에 이렇게 생각하는 것이 어찌 기쁨이 아니겠소. '그녀를 별들에게로 보낸 것은 내 손이었다. 그녀를 가장 사랑한 이의 손이었다. 그녀가 스스로 고를 수 있었다면 누구보다 그녀 자신이 골랐을 바로 그 손이었다.' 우리 가운데 그런 이가 있거든 나에게 말해주시오.\"\n\n우리는 모두 아서를 바라보았다. 그 역시 우리 모두가 본 것을, 곧 루시를 부정한 기억이 아니라 거룩한 기억으로 우리에게 되돌려줄 손이 바로 그의 손이어야 한다는 그 한없는 다정함을 알아챘다. 그는 앞으로 나서며, 손은 떨리고 얼굴은 눈처럼 창백했으나 용감하게 말했다.\n\n\"나의 참된 벗이여, 부서진 내 가슴 밑바닥에서부터 그대에게 감사하오. 내가 무엇을 해야 할지 일러주시오. 결코 주저하지 않으리다!\" 판 헬싱은 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고 말했다.\n\n\"용감한 젊은이여! 한순간의 용기면 끝나오. 이 말뚝을 그녀의 몸에 박아 넣어야 하오. 끔찍한 시련이 될 것이오 — 그 점에 대해서는 속지 마시오 — 그러나 잠깐일 뿐이며, 그러고 나면 그대는 그대가 겪은 고통이 컸던 것 이상으로 기뻐하게 될 것이오. 이 음산한 무덤에서 그대는 마치 허공을 밟는 듯 가벼이 걸어 나오게 될 것이오. 그러나 일단 시작하거든 결코 주저해서는 안 되오. 다만 우리가, 그대의 참된 벗들이 그대를 둘러싸고 있으며, 내내 그대를 위해 기도하고 있다는 것만 생각하시오.\"\n\n\"계속하시오.\" 아서가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. \"내가 무엇을 해야 할지 일러주시오.\"\n\n\"이 말뚝을 왼손에 쥐고 끝을 심장 위에 댈 채비를 하고, 망치는 오른손에 드시오. 그런 다음 우리가 죽은 이를 위한 기도를 시작하면 — 내가 읽을 것이오, 여기 책이 있소, 나머지 사람들은 따라 하시오 — 하느님의 이름으로 내리치시오. 그리하여 우리가 사랑하는 죽은 이에게 모든 것이 평안하고, 불사귀가 사라지도록 하시오.\"\n\n아서는 말뚝과 망치를 들었고, 일단 그 마음이 행동에 굳어지자 그의 손은 떨리지도, 흔들리지조차 않았다. 판 헬싱은 미사 경본을 펼쳐 읽기 시작했고, 퀸시와 나는 최선을 다해 따라 했다. 아서는 끝을 심장 위에 댔는데, 내가 보니 흰 살에 그 자국이 파이는 것이 보였다. 그러고는 그가 온 힘을 다해 내리쳤다.\n\n관 속의 그것이 몸부림쳤다. 그리고 벌어진 붉은 입술에서 소름 끼치는, 피를 얼어붙게 하는 비명이 터져 나왔다. 몸은 흔들리고 떨리며 거칠게 비틀려 뒤틀렸다. 날카로운 흰 이빨은 입술이 베일 때까지 맞부딪쳤고, 입은 진홍빛 거품으로 범벅이 되었다. 그러나 아서는 결코 주저하지 않았다. 떨림 없는 그의 팔이 오르내리며 자비를 베푸는 말뚝을 점점 더 깊이 박아 넣을 때, 그는 토르 신상(神像) 같았으며, 그동안 꿰뚫린 심장에서 솟은 피가 그 둘레로 콸콸 분출되었다. 그의 얼굴은 굳어 있었고, 그를 통해 드높은 사명이 빛나는 듯했다. 그 광경은 우리에게 용기를 주어, 우리의 목소리가 그 작은 납골소를 두루 울리는 듯했다.\n\n그러더니 몸의 몸부림과 떨림이 잦아들었고, 이빨이 맞부딪치고 얼굴이 떨리는 듯하더니, 마침내 그것은 가만히 누웠다. 그 끔찍한 일이 끝났다.\n\n망치가 아서의 손에서 떨어졌다. 그는 비틀거렸고, 우리가 붙잡지 않았더라면 쓰러졌을 것이다. 그의 이마에서 굵은 땀방울이 솟았고, 숨은 끊어질 듯 헐떡였다. 그것은 실로 그에게 무서운 부담이었으며, 만일 인간적인 헤아림을 넘어서는 것이 그를 그 일로 떠밀지 않았더라면 그는 결코 그것을 끝까지 해내지 못했을 것이다. 몇 분 동안 우리는 그에게 너무 정신이 팔려 관 쪽을 바라보지 못했다. 그러나 우리가 바라보았을 때, 놀란 경탄의 웅성거림이 우리 사이로 번져 갔다. 우리가 어찌나 간절히 들여다보았던지, 땅바닥에 앉아 있던 아서가 일어나 다가와 함께 들여다보았다. 그러자 기쁘고 기이한 빛이 그의 얼굴에 번지며, 그 위에 드리워 있던 공포의 음울함을 말끔히 걷어냈다.\n\n거기, 관 속에는, 우리가 그토록 두려워하고, 또 그것을 멸하는 일이 그럴 자격이 가장 큰 이에게 특권으로 주어질 만큼 미워하게 된 그 더러운 것이 더는 누워 있지 않았다. 다만 살아 있을 때 우리가 보았던 그대로의 루시가, 견줄 데 없이 다정하고 순결한 얼굴로 누워 있었다. 살아 있을 때 우리가 보았던 그대로, 근심과 고통과 쇠약의 흔적이 거기 남아 있는 것은 사실이었다. 그러나 그것들은 모두 우리에게 정겨웠으니, 우리가 알던 그녀의 참모습을 그대로 새겨두고 있었기 때문이다. 우리는 너 나 할 것 없이, 쇠약해진 얼굴과 몸 위에 햇살처럼 깃든 그 거룩한 평온이, 영원히 다스릴 그 평온의 지상의 징표이자 표상일 뿐임을 느꼈다.\n\n판 헬싱이 다가와 아서의 어깨에 손을 얹고 말했다.\n\n\"그러면 이제, 내 벗 아서, 사랑하는 젊은이여, 나는 용서받지 못한 것이오?\"\n\n그가 노인의 손을 제 손에 잡아 입술로 가져가 누르며 대답할 때, 무서운 긴장에 대한 반작용이 밀려왔다.\n\n\"용서라니요! 내 사랑하는 이에게 그 영혼을 다시 돌려주고, 나에게 평안을 주신 당신께 하느님의 축복이 있기를.\" 그는 교수의 어깨에 두 손을 얹고 그 가슴에 머리를 묻은 채 한동안 소리 없이 울었으며, 우리는 미동도 없이 서 있었다. 그가 고개를 들자 판 헬싱이 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"그러면 이제, 내 아이여, 그대는 그녀에게 입맞춰도 좋소. 원한다면 그녀의 죽은 입술에 입맞추시오. 그녀가 고를 수 있었다면 그녀가 바랐을 그대로. 이제 그녀는 이빨을 드러내고 히죽거리는 악마가 아니오 — 더는 영원토록 더러운 것이 아니오. 그녀는 더는 악마의 불사귀가 아니오. 그녀는 하느님의 참된 죽은 이이며, 그 영혼은 그분과 함께 있소!\"\n\n아서가 몸을 숙여 그녀에게 입맞추었고, 그런 다음 우리는 그와 퀸시를 무덤 밖으로 내보냈다. 교수와 나는 말뚝의 윗부분을 톱으로 잘라내고, 그 끝은 몸속에 그대로 두었다. 그러고는 머리를 잘라내고 입에 마늘을 채웠다. 우리는 납으로 된 관을 땜질하고, 관 뚜껑을 나사로 조였으며, 우리 물건들을 챙겨 그곳을 떠났다. 교수가 문을 잠근 뒤 그 열쇠를 아서에게 주었다.\n\n밖은 공기가 감미로웠고, 해가 빛났으며, 새들이 노래했고, 마치 온 자연이 다른 가락에 맞춰진 듯했다. 어디에나 기쁨과 즐거움과 평화가 있었으니, 한 가지 일에서 우리 자신이 안식을 얻었으며, 비록 누그러진 기쁨이긴 했어도 우리는 기뻤기 때문이다.\n\n우리가 자리를 뜨기 전에 판 헬싱이 말했다.\n\n\"자 이제, 벗들이여, 우리 일의 한 단계가, 우리 자신에게 가장 가슴 아픈 한 단계가 끝났소. 그러나 더 큰 일이 남아 있소. 이 모든 우리 슬픔의 장본인을 찾아내어 그를 근절하는 일이오. 나에게는 우리가 좇을 수 있는 단서가 있소. 그러나 그것은 길고도 어려운 일이며, 거기에는 위험과 고통이 따르오. 그대들 모두 나를 돕지 않겠소? 우리는 모두 믿게 되었소 — 그렇지 않소? 그렇다면 우리는 우리의 의무를 보지 않겠소? 그렇소! 그리고 우리는 쓰라린 끝까지 나아가겠다고 약속하지 않겠소?\"\n\n우리는 차례로 그의 손을 잡았고, 그 약속이 이루어졌다. 그런 다음 우리가 자리를 뜰 때 교수가 말했다.\n\n\"이틀 뒤 밤에 그대들은 일곱 시에 나와 만나 벗 존과 함께 저녁을 들도록 하시오. 나는 다른 두 사람을, 그대들이 아직 알지 못하는 두 사람을 간청하여 부르겠소. 그리고 우리의 모든 일을 보여주고 우리의 계획을 펼쳐 보일 채비를 하겠소. 벗 존, 자네는 나와 함께 집으로 가세. 의논할 것이 많고, 자네가 나를 도와줄 수 있으니. 오늘 밤 나는 암스테르담으로 떠나지만 내일 밤에는 돌아오겠네. 그러면 우리의 위대한 추적이 시작되오. 그러나 먼저 나는 할 말이 많으니, 그래야 그대들이 무엇을 해야 하고 무엇을 두려워해야 할지 알게 될 것이오. 그런 다음 우리의 약속을 서로에게 새로이 다지도록 합시다. 무서운 일이 우리 앞에 놓여 있고, 일단 우리 발을 쟁기 날에 디딘 이상 우리는 물러서서는 안 되기 때문이오.\"\n\n버클리 호텔에 도착하자, 반 헬싱은 자신을 기다리고 있던 전보 한 통을 발견했다.\n\n\"기차로 올라가는 중. 조너선은 휘트비에 있음. 중대한 소식 있음. — 미나 하커.\"\n\n교수는 무척 기뻐했다. \"아, 그 놀라운 미나 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"여인들 가운데 진주 같은 분이지! 그분이 오시는데 나는 머물 수가 없네. 그분은 자네 집으로 가셔야 하네, 존. 자네가 역에 마중을 나가야 하네. 가는 길에 전보를 쳐서 그분이 채비를 할 수 있게 하게.\"\n\n전보를 보낸 뒤 그는 차를 한 잔 마셨고, 그러면서 조너선 하커가 외국에 있을 때 쓴 일기에 관해 이야기해 주며, 그것을 타자로 친 사본과 더불어 휘트비에서 쓴 하커 부인의 일기 사본도 나에게 건넸다. \"이것들을 받아두게,\" 그가 말했다. \"그리고 잘 연구해 두게. 내가 돌아올 무렵이면 자네는 모든 사실을 손에 쥐고 있을 것이고, 그러면 우리는 더 잘 추적에 나설 수 있을 걸세. 안전하게 간수하게. 그 안에 보물이 많이 들어 있으니. 자네에게는 온 믿음이 다 필요할 걸세. 오늘 같은 경험을 한 자네라 할지라도 말이야. 여기 적힌 것은,\" 그는 말하면서 서류 뭉치 위에 손을 무겁고도 엄숙하게 얹었다. \"자네와 나, 그리고 다른 많은 이들에게 종말의 시작이 될 수도 있네. 아니면 이 땅을 걸어다니는 언데드의 조종(弔鐘)을 울리게 될 수도 있고. 부디 열린 마음으로 모두 읽어주게. 그리고 여기 적힌 이야기에 무엇이든 보탤 수 있거든 그리해주게. 그것이 무엇보다 중요하니. 자네도 이 모든 기이한 일들의 일기를 써왔지, 그렇지 않은가? 그렇지! 그러면 우리가 만날 때 이 모두를 함께 살펴보세.\" 그러고 나서 그는 떠날 채비를 했고, 곧 마차를 타고 리버풀 스트리트로 떠났다. 나는 패딩턴으로 향했고, 기차가 들어오기 약 십오 분 전쯤 그곳에 도착했다.\n\n도착하는 승강장에 흔한 그 부산스러운 식으로 군중이 흩어져 갔고, 나는 손님을 놓치지나 않을까 불안해지기 시작했다. 그때 상냥한 얼굴에 단아해 보이는 여인이 내게 다가와, 한 번 재빨리 훑어보고는 말했다. \"수어드 박사님이시죠?\"\n\n\"그리고 당신은 하커 부인이시군요!\" 나는 즉시 답했고, 그러자 그녀가 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"가엾고 사랑하던 루시가 묘사한 것으로 박사님인 줄 알아봤어요. 하지만——\" 그녀는 문득 말을 멈췄고, 갑작스러운 홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에 번졌다.\n\n내 뺨에 떠오른 홍조가 어쩐 일인지 우리 둘 모두의 마음을 편하게 해주었으니, 그것이 그녀 자신의 홍조에 대한 무언의 대답이 되었던 것이다. 나는 그녀의 짐을 챙겼는데, 그 가운데에는 타자기도 있었다. 그리고 가정부에게 전보를 쳐서 하커 부인을 위해 거실과 침실을 즉시 준비하도록 일러둔 뒤, 우리는 지하철을 타고 펜처치 스트리트로 향했다.\n\n이윽고 우리는 도착했다. 물론 그녀는 그곳이 정신병원임을 알고 있었으나, 우리가 들어설 때 그녀가 몸서리치는 것을 억누르지 못하는 것을 나는 볼 수 있었다.\n\n그녀는 괜찮다면 곧 내 서재로 오겠다고 말했는데, 할 말이 많기 때문이라고 했다. 그래서 나는 그녀를 기다리며 이렇게 축음기 일기의 기록을 마무리하고 있다. 아직 반 헬싱이 내게 남긴 서류들을 들여다볼 기회는 없었으나, 그것들은 내 앞에 펼쳐져 있다. 나는 그녀가 무언가에 관심을 갖도록 해서, 그것들을 읽을 기회를 얻어야 한다. 그녀는 시간이 얼마나 소중한지, 우리가 손에 든 일이 어떤 것인지 모른다. 그녀를 놀라게 하지 않도록 조심해야 한다. 그녀가 왔다!\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」\n\n9월 29일. — 몸단장을 한 뒤 나는 수어드 박사의 서재로 내려갔다. 문 앞에서 나는 잠시 멈춰 섰으니, 그가 누군가와 이야기하는 소리를 들은 것 같았기 때문이다. 그러나 그가 서두르라고 당부했으므로 나는 문을 두드렸고, 그가 \"들어오세요\" 하고 외치자 안으로 들어갔다.\n\n몹시 놀랍게도 그와 함께 있는 사람은 아무도 없었다. 그는 완전히 혼자였고, 그의 맞은편 탁자 위에는 묘사로 미루어 단번에 축음기임을 알 수 있는 물건이 놓여 있었다. 나는 그것을 한 번도 본 적이 없어서 무척 흥미로웠다.\n\n\"오래 기다리시게 한 건 아니었으면 좋겠는데요,\" 내가 말했다. \"다만 박사님이 이야기하시는 소리가 들려서 문 앞에 서 있었어요. 누군가와 함께 계신 줄 알았거든요.\"\n\n\"아,\" 그가 미소를 지으며 답했다. \"그저 일기를 기록하고 있었을 뿐입니다.\"\n\n\"일기를요?\" 나는 놀라서 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"네,\" 그가 답했다. \"저는 이 안에 일기를 적습니다.\" 그는 말하면서 축음기에 손을 얹었다. 나는 그것에 무척 흥분되어 불쑥 내뱉었다.\n\n\"어머, 이건 속기보다도 낫네요! 무슨 말이든 들려주실 수 있나요?\"\n\n\"물론이죠,\" 그는 선뜻 답하더니, 말하게 작동시키려고 일어섰다. 그러다가 그는 멈칫했고, 곤혹스러운 표정이 그의 얼굴에 번졌다.\n\n\"실은,\" 그가 어색하게 말을 꺼냈다. \"저는 여기에 제 일기만 적는데, 그것이 거의 전부—— 거의 전부 제 환자들에 관한 것이라서, 좀 곤란할 수도—— 그러니까, 제 말은——\" 그는 말을 멈췄고, 나는 그가 당혹감에서 벗어나도록 도우려 했다.\n\n\"박사님은 마지막에 사랑하던 루시를 돌보셨잖아요. 그녀가 어떻게 죽었는지 들려주세요. 그녀에 관한 것이라면 무엇이든 무척 고맙게 받아들이겠어요. 그녀는 제게 무척, 무척 소중한 사람이었으니까요.\"\n\n놀랍게도 그는 얼굴에 공포에 질린 표정을 띤 채 답했다.\n\n\"그녀의 죽음에 관해 말씀드리라고요? 천만의 말씀입니다!\"\n\n\"왜 안 되죠?\" 내가 물었으니, 어떤 무겁고 끔찍한 느낌이 나를 엄습해 오고 있었기 때문이다. 그는 다시 멈칫했고, 나는 그가 핑계를 지어내려 애쓰고 있는 것을 알 수 있었다. 마침내 그가 더듬거리며 말했다.\n\n\"그러니까, 저는 일기의 어느 특정한 부분을 어떻게 골라내야 할지를 모릅니다.\" 그가 말하는 동안에도 한 가지 생각이 그에게 떠올랐는지, 그는 무심결의 단순함으로, 다른 목소리로, 아이 같은 천진함으로 말했다. \"그건 정말 사실입니다, 명예를 걸고. 정말이에요!\" 나는 미소 짓지 않을 수 없었고, 그러자 그는 얼굴을 찡그렸다. \"그때 제 속을 다 드러내 버렸네요!\" 그가 말했다. \"그런데 말이죠, 저는 지난 몇 달 동안 일기를 적어 왔으면서도, 나중에 찾아보고 싶을 때 어느 특정한 부분을 어떻게 찾아낼지는 단 한 번도 떠올린 적이 없었답니다.\" 이쯤에서 내 마음은 정해졌으니, 루시를 돌본 의사의 일기라면 그 끔찍한 존재에 관한 우리의 지식의 총합에 무언가를 보탤 만한 것이 있을지 모른다고 여겼던 것이다. 그래서 나는 대담하게 말했다.\n\n\"그러면, 수어드 박사님, 그 일기를 제 타자기로 옮겨 적게 해주시는 게 좋겠어요.\" 그는 말하면서 그야말로 죽은 사람처럼 새파랗게 질렸다.\n\n\"안 됩니다! 안 돼요! 절대 안 돼요! 무슨 일이 있어도 그 끔찍한 이야기를 당신께 알려드릴 수는 없습니다!\"\n\n그렇다면 그것은 끔찍한 일이었던 것이다. 내 직감이 옳았다! 잠시 나는 생각에 잠겼고, 무언가를, 혹은 나를 도와줄 어떤 기회를 무심결에 찾으며 방 안을 둘러보던 내 눈이 탁자 위에 놓인 한 무더기의 타자 친 서류에 가 닿았다. 그의 눈이 내 눈의 그 시선을 알아채고는, 자신도 모르게 그 방향을 따라갔다. 그 서류 뭉치가 눈에 들어오자 그는 내 뜻을 깨달았다.\n\n\"박사님은 저를 모르세요,\" 내가 말했다. \"저 서류들—— 제 일기와, 제가 타자로 친 제 남편의 일기까지 읽으시고 나면, 저를 더 잘 알게 되실 거예요. 저는 이 대의를 위해 제 마음속 온갖 생각을 바치는 데 한 번도 주저한 적이 없어요. 하지만 물론, 박사님은 아직 저를 모르시지요. 그러니 박사님이 저를 그렇게까지 믿어주시리라 기대해서는 안 되겠지요.\"\n\n그는 분명 고귀한 천성을 지닌 사람이다. 가엾고 사랑하던 루시가 그에 관해 옳게 보았던 것이다. 그는 일어서서 커다란 서랍 하나를 열었는데, 그 안에는 검은 밀랍을 입힌 속이 빈 금속 원통들이 여러 개 가지런히 정리되어 있었다. 그가 말했다.\n\n\"부인 말씀이 전적으로 옳습니다. 저는 부인을 몰랐기 때문에 믿지 못했던 겁니다. 그러나 이제는 부인을 압니다. 그리고 한마디 드리자면, 저는 진작에 부인을 알아봤어야 했어요. 루시가 부인에게 제 이야기를 했다는 걸 압니다. 그녀는 제게도 부인 이야기를 했지요. 제가 할 수 있는 단 하나의 속죄를 하게 해주시겠습니까? 이 원통들을 가져가 들어보세요. 처음 여섯 개쯤은 제 개인적인 것이라 부인을 소름끼치게 하지는 않을 겁니다. 그러면 부인은 저를 더 잘 알게 되시겠지요. 그때쯤이면 저녁식사가 준비될 겁니다. 그동안 저는 이 서류들 가운데 일부를 읽으며, 어떤 것들을 더 잘 이해할 수 있게 될 겁니다.\" 그는 손수 축음기를 내 거실로 가져와 나를 위해 맞춰 주었다. 이제 나는 무언가 즐거운 것을 알게 되리라, 그렇게 확신한다. 이미 한쪽 면은 알고 있는 진실한 사랑 이야기의 다른 한쪽 면을 그것이 내게 들려줄 테니…….\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n9월 29일. — 나는 조너선 하커의 그 놀라운 일기와 그의 아내의 또 다른 일기에 어찌나 빠져들었던지, 생각도 없이 시간을 흘려보냈다. 하녀가 저녁식사를 알리러 왔을 때 하커 부인은 아직 내려오지 않았으므로, 나는 \"아마 피곤하신 모양이니, 저녁식사를 한 시간 미루도록 하지\"라고 말하고는 하던 일을 계속했다. 하커 부인의 일기를 막 다 읽었을 때 그녀가 들어왔다. 그녀는 상냥하리만치 예뻐 보였으나 무척 슬퍼 보였고, 두 눈은 울어서 붉어져 있었다. 이것이 어쩐 일인지 나를 몹시 움직였다. 요즈음 나에게는 눈물을 흘릴 만한 일이 있었으니, 하느님은 아신다! 그러나 나는 그 눈물의 위안을 누릴 수 없었다. 그런데 이제 방금 흘린 눈물로 빛나는 그 상냥한 두 눈을 보니, 그것이 곧장 내 가슴을 파고들었다. 그래서 나는 되도록 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"제가 부인을 무척 괴롭게 해드린 건 아닐까 몹시 걱정됩니다.\"\n\n\"오, 아니에요, 저를 괴롭게 하신 게 아니에요,\" 그녀가 답했다. \"하지만 박사님의 슬픔에 말로 다 못할 만큼 마음이 흔들렸어요. 그건 놀라운 기계지만, 잔인하리만치 진실해요. 바로 그 목소리로, 그것이 박사님 가슴의 고뇌를 제게 들려주었어요. 마치 어떤 영혼이 전능하신 하느님께 부르짖는 것 같았어요. 그 말소리를 다시는 누구도 들어서는 안 돼요! 보세요, 저는 쓸모 있게 되려고 애썼어요. 제 타자기로 그 말들을 옮겨 적었으니, 이제 제가 들었던 것처럼 다른 누구도 박사님의 가슴이 뛰는 소리를 들을 필요는 없어요.\"\n\n\"누구도 알 필요가 없고, 누구도 알게 되지 않을 겁니다,\" 나는 낮은 목소리로 말했다. 그녀는 내 손 위에 자신의 손을 얹고 무척 엄숙하게 말했다. \"아, 하지만 그들은 반드시 알아야 해요!\"\n\n\"알아야만 한다고요! 하지만 왜죠?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"알아야 한다고요! 하지만 왜죠?\" 내가 물었다.\n\"그건 그 끔찍한 이야기의 일부, 가엾고 사랑하던 루시의 죽음과 그것을 불러온 모든 일의 일부니까요. 우리 앞에 놓인, 이 땅에서 그 끔찍한 괴물을 없애야 하는 싸움에서 우리는 얻을 수 있는 모든 지식과 모든 도움을 가져야 하니까요. 박사님이 제게 주신 그 원통들에는 박사님이 제게 알리려고 하신 것 이상의 것이 담겨 있었던 것 같아요. 하지만 박사님의 기록 속에는 이 어두운 수수께끼를 비추는 빛이 많이 있다는 걸 알 수 있어요. 제가 돕도록 허락해 주시겠지요? 저는 어느 지점까지는 모두 알고 있어요. 그리고 박사님의 일기는 9월 7일까지밖에 데려다주지 않았지만, 가엾은 루시가 어떻게 시달렸는지, 그 끔찍한 운명이 어떻게 빚어지고 있었는지 저는 이미 알아요. 조너선과 저는 반 헬싱 교수님이 저희를 만나신 이래로 밤낮없이 일해 왔어요. 교수님은 더 많은 정보를 얻으러 휘트비로 가셨고, 내일 이곳으로 오셔서 저희를 도우실 거예요. 우리끼리는 비밀을 둘 필요가 없어요. 함께, 그리고 온전한 신뢰로 일한다면, 우리 가운데 누군가가 어둠 속에 남아 있는 것보다 분명 더 강해질 수 있어요.\" 그녀가 어찌나 호소하듯 나를 바라보면서, 동시에 그 태도에서 어찌나 큰 용기와 결의를 드러내던지, 나는 즉시 그녀의 뜻에 굴복하고 말았다. \"그러십시오,\" 내가 말했다. \"이 일에서 부인 뜻대로 하십시오. 제가 잘못하는 것이라면 하느님께서 용서해 주시기를! 아직 알아야 할 끔찍한 것들이 있습니다. 그러나 가엾은 루시의 죽음에 이르는 길을 그렇게까지 걸어오셨다면, 부인은 어둠 속에 머무는 것에 만족하지 못하시리라는 걸 압니다. 아니, 그 끝이—— 바로 그 끝이—— 부인께 한 줄기 평화의 빛을 줄지도 모르지요. 자, 저녁식사가 준비됐습니다. 우리 앞에 놓인 일을 위해 서로를 강하게 지켜줘야 합니다. 우리에게는 잔인하고 무서운 임무가 있으니까요. 식사를 마치시면 나머지를 알게 되실 거고, 저는 부인이 묻는 어떤 물음에도 답하겠습니다—— 비록 그 자리에 있던 우리에게는 분명했던 것이라도, 부인이 이해하지 못하는 것이 있다면 말입니다.\"\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」\n\n9월 29일. — 저녁식사를 마친 뒤 나는 수어드 박사와 함께 그의 서재로 왔다. 그는 내 방에서 축음기를 다시 가져왔고, 나는 내 타자기를 들었다. 그는 나를 편안한 의자에 앉히고는, 일어서지 않고도 손이 닿을 수 있게 축음기를 맞춰 주었으며, 잠시 멈추고 싶을 때 그것을 멈추는 법을 보여주었다. 그러고 나서 그는 무척 사려 깊게 의자를 가져와 내게 등을 돌리고 앉았으니, 내가 되도록 편하게 있을 수 있게 하기 위해서였다. 그러고는 읽기 시작했다. 나는 두 갈래로 갈라진 금속을 두 귀에 대고 귀를 기울였다.\n\n루시의 죽음에 관한 그 끔찍한 이야기, 그리고—— 그리고 그 뒤에 이어진 모든 일이 끝났을 때, 나는 의자에 맥없이 기대 누웠다. 다행히 나는 잘 기절하는 성미는 아니다. 수어드 박사는 나를 보더니 공포에 질린 외마디 소리를 지르며 벌떡 일어나, 급히 찬장에서 휴대용 병 하나를 꺼내 내게 브랜디를 조금 주었고, 그것이 몇 분 만에 나를 어느 정도 회복시켜 주었다. 머릿속이 온통 어지러웠고, 그 숱한 공포를 뚫고 사랑하고 또 사랑하던 루시가 마침내 평화를 찾았다는 거룩한 한 줄기 빛이 비쳐 오지 않았더라면, 나는 소란을 피우지 않고서는 그것을 견뎌낼 수 없었으리라 싶다. 모든 것이 어찌나 거칠고, 수수께끼 같고, 기이한지, 만일 내가 트란실바니아에서의 조너선의 경험을 몰랐더라면 나는 믿을 수 없었을 것이다. 하지만 그것을 알고 있었기에, 나는 무엇을 믿어야 할지 알 수 없었고, 그래서 다른 무언가에 몰두함으로써 그 곤경에서 벗어났다. 나는 타자기의 덮개를 벗기고 수어드 박사에게 말했다.\n\n\"이제 이걸 모두 옮겨 적게 해주세요. 반 헬싱 박사님이 오실 때 우리는 준비가 되어 있어야 해요. 저는 조너선에게 휘트비에서 런던에 도착하는 대로 이리로 오라고 전보를 쳤어요. 이 일에서는 날짜가 전부이고, 우리가 모든 자료를 갖춰 모든 항목을 시간 순서대로 정리해 둔다면 많은 일을 해낸 셈이 될 거라고 생각해요. 박사님은 고달밍 경과 모리스 씨도 오신다고 하셨죠. 그분들이 오실 때 그분께 알려드릴 수 있도록 해두어요.\" 그래서 그는 축음기를 느린 속도로 맞춰 주었고, 나는 일곱 번째 원통의 처음부터 타자를 치기 시작했다. 나는 복사지를 써서, 나머지 모든 것과 마찬가지로 일기의 사본을 세 부 만들었다. 다 끝냈을 때는 시간이 늦었으나, 수어드 박사는 환자들을 돌아보는 일을 하러 다녔다. 그 일을 마치고 나서 그는 돌아와 내 곁에 앉아 책을 읽었으니, 내가 일하는 동안 너무 외롭지 않게 해주려는 것이었다. 그는 어찌나 선하고 사려 깊은지. 세상은 선한 사람들로 가득한 것 같다—— 비록 그 안에 괴물들이 있다고 할지라도. 그를 떠나기 전에, 나는 엑서터 역에서 어느 석간신문에 실린 무언가를 읽고 교수가 동요했던 일을 조너선이 일기에 적어둔 것을 떠올렸다. 그래서 수어드 박사가 신문을 보관해 둔다는 것을 알고, 나는 「웨스트민스터 가제트」와 「폴 몰 가제트」의 철을 빌려 내 방으로 가져왔다. 내가 오려 두었던 「데일리그래프」와 「휘트비 가제트」가 드라큘라 백작이 상륙했을 때 휘트비에서 일어난 끔찍한 사건들을 이해하는 데 얼마나 큰 도움이 되었는지 기억하므로, 나는 그 이후의 석간신문들을 훑어볼 것이다. 그러면 아마 무언가 새로운 빛을 얻게 될지도 모른다. 졸리지 않으니, 일이 나를 차분하게 해주는 데 도움이 될 것이다.\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n9월 30일. — 하커 씨가 아홉 시에 도착했다. 그는 출발하기 직전에 아내의 전보를 받았다고 한다. 얼굴로 판단할 수 있다면, 그는 보기 드물게 영민하고 활력이 넘친다. 만일 이 일기가 사실이라면—— 그리고 나 자신의 놀라운 경험들로 미루어 보면 사실일 수밖에 없는데—— 그는 또한 대단한 담력을 지닌 사람이다. 그 지하 납골당으로 두 번째로 내려간 것은 놀랄 만한 대담함이었다. 그가 그 일을 기록한 것을 읽고 나서, 나는 사내다움의 훌륭한 본보기를 만나리라 예상했으나, 오늘 이곳에 온 조용하고 사무적인 신사를 만나리라고는 거의 생각지 못했다.\n\n나중에. — 점심식사 후 하커와 그의 아내는 자기들 방으로 돌아갔고, 조금 전 내가 지나갈 때 타자기 두드리는 소리가 들렸다. 그들은 열심히 매달려 있다. 하커 부인의 말로는, 그들은 가진 모든 증거 조각을 시간 순서대로 엮고 있다고 한다. 하커는 휘트비에서 그 상자들을 받기로 한 수하인과 런던에서 그것들을 맡았던 운송업자들 사이에 오간 편지들을 손에 넣었다. 그는 지금 내 일기를 그의 아내가 타자로 친 사본을 읽고 있다. 그들이 그것에서 무엇을 알아낼지 궁금하다. 여기 그것이 있다…….\n\n바로 옆집이 백작의 은신처일 수도 있다는 생각이 한 번도 떠오르지 않았다는 것이 이상하다! 환자 렌필드의 행동에서 단서가 충분히 있었음을 하느님은 아신다! 그 집 매입에 관한 편지 뭉치가 타자 사본과 함께 있었다. 아, 우리가 그것들을 좀 더 일찍 손에 넣기만 했더라도 가엾은 루시를 구할 수 있었을지 모른다! 그만두자. 그 길로 가면 광기가 도사리고 있다! 하커가 돌아와 다시 자료를 맞춰 보고 있다. 그는 저녁식사 무렵이면 하나로 연결된 온전한 이야기를 보여줄 수 있을 거라고 한다. 그는 그동안 내가 렌필드를 만나봐야 한다고 생각하는데, 지금까지 그가 백작의 오고 감을 보여주는 일종의 색인 노릇을 해왔기 때문이다. 나는 아직 이것을 잘 모르겠으나, 날짜를 손에 넣고 나면 알게 되리라 짐작한다. 하커 부인이 내 원통들을 타자로 옮겨 둔 것이 얼마나 다행인지! 그렇지 않았더라면 우리는 결코 그 날짜들을 찾아낼 수 없었을 것이다…….\n\n나는 렌필드가 두 손을 모은 채 인자하게 미소 지으며 자기 방에 평온히 앉아 있는 것을 보았다. 그 순간 그는 내가 본 어떤 사람 못지않게 제정신으로 보였다. 나는 앉아서 그와 여러 주제로 이야기를 나눴는데, 그는 그 모두를 자연스럽게 다루었다. 그러고 나서 그는 스스로, 집으로 돌아가는 이야기를 꺼냈는데, 내가 아는 한 이곳에 머무는 동안 한 번도 입에 올린 적 없는 주제였다. 사실 그는 당장 퇴원하게 될 거라고 꽤 자신 있게 말했다. 만일 내가 하커와 대화를 나누고 그 편지들과 그의 발작 날짜들을 읽지 않았더라면, 나는 잠깐 관찰한 뒤 그의 퇴원 서류에 서명할 채비가 되어 있었으리라 믿는다. 하지만 지금으로서는 나는 어렴풋이 의심하고 있다. 그 모든 발작은 어떤 식으로든 백작의 근접과 연관되어 있었다. 그렇다면 이 완전한 만족은 무엇을 뜻하는가? 흡혈귀의 궁극적 승리에 관해 그의 본능이 충족되었을 수 있는 것일까? 잠깐. 그 자신이 동물을 먹는 자이고, 버려진 집의 예배당 문 밖에서 미친 듯이 헛소리를 할 때 그는 늘 \"주인\"에 관해 말했다. 이 모든 것이 우리의 생각을 뒷받침하는 듯하다. 그러나 잠시 후 나는 자리를 떴으니, 내 친구는 지금 너무 멀쩡한 상태라 질문으로 너무 깊이 캐묻기에는 안전하지 않았기 때문이다. 그가 생각하기 시작할지도 모르고, 그러면—— ! 그래서 나는 자리를 떴다. 나는 그의 이 조용한 기분이 미덥지 않다. 그래서 나는 간병인에게 그를 가까이서 살펴보고, 필요할 경우에 대비해 구속복을 준비해 두라고 일러두었다.\n\n「조너선 하커의 일기」\n\n9월 29일, 런던행 기차 안에서. — 빌링턴 씨로부터 자신이 할 수 있는 한 어떤 정보든 주겠다는 정중한 전갈을 받았을 때, 나는 휘트비로 내려가 현장에서 내가 원하는 문의를 하는 것이 최선이라고 생각했다. 이제 내 목표는 백작의 그 끔찍한 화물이 런던의 어느 곳에 자리 잡았는지 그 행방을 추적하는 것이었다. 나중에 우리는 그것을 처리할 수 있게 될지도 모른다. 착하고 어린 청년인 아들 빌링턴이 역에서 나를 맞아, 자기 아버지의 집으로 데려갔는데, 그들은 내가 그날 밤을 그곳에서 묵어야 한다고 정해 두고 있었다. 그들은 진정한 요크셔식 환대로 손님을 후하게 대접했다. 손님에게 모든 것을 다 내주되, 마음대로 하도록 자유롭게 내버려 두는 것이다. 그들은 모두 내가 바쁘고, 내 체류가 짧다는 것을 알고 있었으며, 빌링턴 씨는 그 상자들의 탁송에 관한 모든 서류를 자기 사무실에 준비해 두었다. 백작의 사악한 계략을 알기 전에 그의 탁자 위에서 보았던 편지 가운데 하나를 다시 보니 거의 섬뜩한 느낌이 들었다. 모든 것이 면밀히 궁리되어, 체계적으로, 정밀하게 처리되어 있었다. 그는 자신의 의도가 실행되는 길에 우연히 놓일지 모르는 모든 장애에 대비해 둔 것 같았다. 미국식 표현을 빌리자면, 그는 \"어떤 요행도 바라지 않았으며\", 그의 지시가 이행된 그 완벽한 정확성은 단순히 그 치밀함의 논리적 결과였다. 나는 송장을 보고 그것을 적어 두었다. \"실험용으로 쓸 보통 흙 쉰 상자.\" 또한 카터 패터슨사에 보낸 편지의 사본과 그들의 회신도 보았는데, 이 둘 모두의 사본을 나는 손에 넣었다. 이것이 빌링턴 씨가 내게 줄 수 있는 정보의 전부였으므로, 나는 항구로 내려가 해안경비대원들과 세관 관리들, 그리고 항만장을 만났다. 그들은 모두 이미 이 고장의 전설로 자리 잡아 가고 있는 그 배의 기이한 입항에 관해 할 말이 있었으나, \"보통 흙 쉰 상자\"라는 단순한 설명에 무엇도 보탤 수 있는 사람은 없었다. 그러고 나서 나는 역장을 만났는데, 그는 친절하게도 실제로 그 상자들을 받았던 사람들과 나를 연결해 주었다. 그들의 계산은 목록과 정확히 일치했고, 그 상자들이 \"지독하게 무거웠다\"는 것과 그것들을 옮기는 일이 목마른 일이었다는 것 외에는 더 보탤 것이 없었다. 그들 가운데 하나는, \"나리 같은 어떤 신사분\"이 자기들의 수고에 대한 고마움을 무언가 마실 것의 형태로 표해 주지 않은 것이 야박한 처사라고 덧붙였다. 또 다른 하나는, 그때 생겨난 갈증이 어찌나 심했던지 그동안 흘러간 시간으로도 그것이 완전히 가시지 않았다는 단서를 달았다. 말할 것도 없이, 나는 떠나기 전에 이 원망의 근원을 영영, 그리고 넉넉하게 없애 주도록 챙겼다.\n\n9월 30일. — 역장은 친절하게도 자신의 옛 동료인 킹스 크로스 역장에게 보내는 소개장을 한 줄 써주었고, 그래서 내가 아침에 그곳에 도착했을 때 나는 그에게 상자들의 도착에 관해 물어볼 수 있었다. 그 역시 즉시 나를 해당 관리들과 연결해 주었고, 나는 그들의 계산이 원래의 송장과 정확히 맞아떨어지는 것을 보았다. 이곳에서는 비정상적인 갈증을 얻을 기회가 한정되어 있었으나, 그래도 그 기회는 훌륭하게 활용되어 있었으니, 나는 또다시 사후(事後)의 방식으로 그 결과를 처리해야만 했다.\n\n거기에서 나는 카터 패터슨사의 본사로 갔는데, 그곳에서 더없이 정중한 대접을 받았다. 그들은 일지와 편지 대장에서 그 거래를 찾아보고는, 즉시 더 자세한 사항을 알아보려고 킹스 크로스 사무소로 전화를 걸었다. 다행히도 그 짐을 운반했던 인부들이 일감을 기다리고 있어서, 담당자는 즉시 그들을 보내 주었고, 그 가운데 하나를 통해 운송장과 카팩스로의 상자 배달에 관련된 모든 서류도 함께 보내 주었다. 여기서도 나는 계산이 정확히 들어맞는 것을 알았다. 운송업자의 인부들은 적힌 글의 빈약함을 몇 가지 세부 사항으로 보충해 줄 수 있었다. 곧 알게 되었지만, 이 세부 사항들은 거의 오로지 그 일의 먼지투성이 성질과, 그로 인해 일꾼들에게 생겨난 갈증에 관한 것이었다. 내가 이 나라의 통화를 매개로 하여, 나중에 그 이로운 해악을 가시게 할 기회를 제공하자, 그 인부들 가운데 하나가 말했다.\n\n\"나리, 그 집은요, 제가 들어가 본 데 중에 제일 괴상한 데였습죠. 이런 제기랄! 백 년은 손도 안 댄 것 같더라구요. 먼지가 어찌나 두텁게 쌓였던지 뼈 다칠 걱정 없이 그 위에서 잘 수도 있을 정도였습죠. 게다가 어찌나 방치됐던지 그 안에서 케케묵은 예루살렘 냄새라도 맡을 수 있을 것 같았다니까요. 하지만 그 오래된 예배당—— 그게 압권이었습죠, 진짜로요! 저랑 제 동료는 거기서 한시바삐 못 나가서 안달이 났습죠. 맙소사, 해 진 다음에 거기 있으라면 한 순간에 일 파운드를 줘도 안 할 겁니다요.\"\n\n그 집에 들어가 본 적이 있는 나로서는 그의 말을 충분히 믿을 수 있었다. 그러나 만일 그가 내가 아는 것을 알았더라면, 내 생각에 그는 자기 값을 더 올렸을 것이다.\n\n이제 한 가지는 분명히 알게 되었다. 데메테르호를 타고 바르나에서 휘트비로 도착한 그 상자들이 전부 카팩스의 오래된 예배당에 무사히 부려졌다는 것이다. 그 이후로 일부가 옮겨지지 않았다면 그곳에 쉰 개가 있어야 한다—— 수어드 박사의 일기로 미루어 보면 일부가 옮겨진 것이 아닐까 두렵지만 말이다.\n\n나는 렌필드가 상자들을 공격했을 때 카팩스에서 그것들을 실어 간 운송업자를 만나 보려고 한다. 이 단서를 따라가면 우리는 많은 것을 알게 될지도 모른다.\n\n나중에. — 미나와 나는 온종일 일했고, 모든 서류를 정리해 두었다.\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」\n\n9월 30일. — 나는 어찌나 기쁜지 나 자신을 어떻게 추슬러야 할지 모를 지경이다. 짐작건대 이것은 나를 줄곧 따라다니던 두려움에 대한 반작용이다. 이 끔찍한 일과, 그의 오래된 상처가 다시 헤집어지는 것이 조너선에게 해롭게 작용하지 않을까 하는 두려움 말이다. 나는 그가 휘트비로 떠나는 것을 되도록 의연한 얼굴로 지켜보았으나, 속으로는 불안으로 마음이 아팠다. 그러나 그 일이 오히려 그에게 도움이 되었다. 그는 지금처럼 결연한 적도, 강인한 적도, 화산 같은 활력으로 가득 찬 적도 없었다. 그것은 꼭 그 선하고 사랑스러운 반 헬싱 교수님이 말씀하신 그대로다. 그는 진짜 강단 있는 사람이며, 더 약한 천성이라면 죽고 말 압박 속에서 오히려 나아진다는 것이다. 그는 생기와 희망과 결의로 가득 차서 돌아왔고, 우리는 오늘 밤을 위해 모든 것을 정리해 두었다. 나는 흥분으로 어쩔 줄을 모르겠다. 짐작건대 백작처럼 그렇게 쫓기는 것이라면 무엇이든 가엾게 여겨야 마땅하리라. 바로 그것이 문제다. 이 존재는 인간이 아니다—— 짐승조차 아니다. 가엾은 루시의 죽음에 관한 수어드 박사의 기록과 그 뒤에 이어진 일을 읽으면, 마음속 연민의 샘마저 메말라 버리기에 충분하다.\n\n나중에. — 고달밍 경과 모리스 씨가 우리가 예상한 것보다 일찍 도착했다. 수어드 박사는 일로 외출 중이었고 조너선을 데리고 갔으므로, 내가 그들을 맞아야 했다. 그것은 나에게 고통스러운 만남이었으니, 불과 몇 달 전 가엾고 사랑하던 루시의 온갖 희망을 되살아나게 했기 때문이다. 물론 그들은 루시가 내 이야기를 하는 것을 들은 적이 있었고, 반 헬싱 박사 역시 모리스 씨의 표현을 빌리자면 한껏 \"내 자랑을 늘어놓은\" 모양이었다. 가엾은 사람들, 그들 둘 다 내가 그들이 루시에게 한 청혼에 관해 죄다 알고 있다는 것을 모른다. 그들은 내가 얼마나 알고 있는지 모르므로, 무슨 말을 하거나 무엇을 해야 할지 잘 몰랐고, 그래서 무난한 주제에만 머물러야 했다. 그러나 나는 그 문제를 곰곰이 생각해 본 끝에, 내가 할 수 있는 최선은 그들에게 지금까지의 일들을 빠짐없이 알려주는 것이라는 결론에 이르렀다. 나는 수어드 박사의 일기에서 그들이 루시의 죽음—— 그녀의 진짜 죽음—— 의 자리에 있었다는 것을, 그래서 내가 때가 되기 전에 어떤 비밀을 누설할까 두려워할 필요가 없다는 것을 알고 있었다. 그래서 나는 되도록 잘 설명하여, 내가 모든 서류와 일기를 다 읽었으며, 남편과 내가 그것들을 타자로 옮긴 뒤 막 정리를 마쳤다고 그들에게 말했다. 나는 그들 각자에게 서재에서 읽으라고 사본을 한 부씩 주었다. 고달밍 경은 자기 사본을 받아 뒤적이더니—— 그것은 꽤 두툼한 뭉치였다—— 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"이걸 다 부인이 쓰셨습니까, 하커 부인?\"\n\n내가 고개를 끄덕이자 그는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"저는 이게 무슨 뜻인지 잘 모르겠습니다. 하지만 여러분 모두 어찌나 선하고 친절하시며, 어찌나 진지하고 정력적으로 일해 오셨던지, 제가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 여러분의 생각을 눈을 가린 채 받아들이고 여러분을 도우려 애쓰는 것뿐입니다. 저는 이미, 사람이 죽는 그 순간까지 겸손하게 만들 만한, 사실을 받아들이는 한 가지 교훈을 얻었습니다. 게다가, 부인이 제 가엾은 루시를 사랑하셨다는 걸 압니다——\" 여기서 그는 돌아서서 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다. 나는 그의 목소리에 어린 눈물을 들을 수 있었다. 모리스 씨는 본능적인 섬세함으로, 잠시 그의 어깨에 손을 얹었다가, 조용히 방을 걸어 나갔다. 짐작건대 여성의 천성에는, 남자가 자신의 사내다움을 깎아내린다는 느낌 없이 그 앞에서 무너지고 부드럽고 감정적인 면의 심정을 토로할 수 있게 해주는 무언가가 있는 모양이다. 고달밍 경은 나와 단둘이 남게 되자 소파에 앉아 완전히, 그리고 거리낌 없이 무너져 내렸기 때문이다. 나는 그의 곁에 앉아 그의 손을 잡았다. 그가 이것을 내 주제넘은 짓으로 여기지 않았기를, 그리고 만일 그가 나중에 이 일을 떠올리더라도 결코 그런 생각을 품지 않기를 바란다. 이런 생각은 그를 모욕하는 것이다. 나는 그가 결코 그러지 않으리라는 것을 안다—— 그는 더없이 참된 신사이니. 나는 그에게 말했으니, 그의 가슴이 무너져 내리고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었기 때문이다.\n\n\"저는 사랑하던 루시를 사랑했고, 그녀가 당신께 어떤 사람이었는지, 당신이 그녀에게 어떤 사람이었는지 알아요. 그녀와 저는 자매 같은 사이였어요. 그리고 이제 그녀가 떠났으니, 당신의 슬픔 속에서 제가 당신께 누이 같은 사람이 되게 해주시지 않겠어요? 비록 그 깊이를 헤아릴 수는 없으나, 당신이 어떤 슬픔을 겪었는지 알아요. 만일 공감과 연민이 당신의 고통에 도움이 될 수 있다면, 제가 작은 도움이라도 되게 해주시지 않겠어요—— 루시를 위해서라도요?\"\n\n순식간에 그 가엾은 사람은 슬픔에 압도되고 말았다. 내게는 그가 요즈음 줄곧 말없이 견뎌 온 모든 것이 한꺼번에 터져 나온 것처럼 보였다. 그는 완전히 격정에 사로잡혀, 펼친 두 손을 들어 올려 더없는 슬픔의 고통 속에서 손바닥을 마주쳤다. 그는 일어섰다가 다시 앉았고, 눈물이 그의 뺨을 타고 비 오듯 흘러내렸다. 나는 그에게 한없는 연민을 느꼈고, 생각할 겨를도 없이 두 팔을 벌렸다. 그는 흐느끼며 내 어깨에 머리를 기대고 지친 아이처럼 울었고, 그러는 동안 감정에 북받쳐 몸을 떨었다.\n\n우리 여자들에게는 어딘가 모성 같은 것이 있어, 그 모성의 정신이 불러일으켜질 때면 사소한 일들을 넘어서게 만든다. 나는 이 슬픔에 잠긴 커다란 남자의 머리가 내게 기대어 있는 것을, 마치 언젠가 내 가슴에 안길지도 모를 아기의 머리인 양 느꼈고, 그가 내 친자식이기라도 한 듯 그의 머리칼을 쓰다듬었다. 그때 나는 이 모든 것이 얼마나 기이한지 조금도 생각하지 못했다.\n\n잠시 후 그의 흐느낌이 멎었고, 그는 사과의 말과 함께 몸을 일으켰으나, 자신의 감정을 감추려 하지는 않았다. 그는 지난 며칠간 밤낮으로—— 지치는 낮과 잠 못 이루는 밤 동안—— 사람이 슬픔의 때에 마땅히 누군가에게 말해야 하는 식으로 누구와도 이야기를 나눌 수 없었다고 내게 말했다. 그에게 공감을 베풀어 줄 만한 여인도, 또 그의 슬픔을 둘러싼 끔찍한 정황 탓에 그가 거리낌 없이 이야기를 나눌 만한 여인도 없었다는 것이다. \"이제는 제가 얼마나 괴로웠는지 알겠습니다,\" 그는 눈물을 닦으며 말했다. \"하지만 오늘 부인의 그 다정한 공감이 제게 얼마나 큰 것이었는지는—— 아직도 모르겠고, 다른 누구도 결코 알 수 없을 겁니다. 시간이 지나면 더 잘 알게 되겠지요. 그리고 믿어 주세요, 제가 지금도 고마워하지 않는 것은 아니지만, 제 고마움은 깨달음과 더불어 자라날 겁니다. 우리 평생토록, 저를 오라비 같은 사람이 되게 해주시지 않겠습니까—— 사랑하던 루시를 위해서라도요?\"\n\n\"사랑하던 루시를 위해서요,\" 나는 우리가 손을 맞잡으며 말했다. \"그래요, 그리고 부인 자신을 위해서도요,\" 그가 덧붙였다. \"만일 한 사내의 존경과 고마움이 얻을 만한 가치가 있는 것이라면, 부인은 오늘 제 것을 얻으셨으니까요. 만일 앞날에 부인께 한 사내의 도움이 필요한 때가 온다면, 믿어 주세요, 부인은 헛되이 부르지 않으실 겁니다. 부디 그런 때가 와서 부인 삶의 햇살을 깨뜨리는 일이 결코 없기를 하느님께 빕니다. 그러나 만일 언젠가 그런 때가 온다면, 제게 알려 주시겠다고 약속해 주세요.\" 그가 어찌나 진지했고, 그의 슬픔이 어찌나 생생했던지, 나는 그것이 그에게 위안이 되리라 느껴 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"약속할게요.\"\n\n복도를 따라 걸어오다가 나는 모리스 씨가 창밖을 내다보고 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 내 발소리를 듣고 돌아섰다. \"아트는 좀 어떻습니까?\" 그가 말했다. 그러고는 내 붉어진 눈을 알아채고 말을 이었다. \"아, 그를 위로해 주셨군요. 가엾은 친구! 그에게는 그게 필요하지요. 한 사내가 마음의 괴로움에 빠져 있을 때 그를 도울 수 있는 건 오직 여인뿐이니까요. 그런데 그를 위로해 줄 사람이 아무도 없었지요.\"\n\n그는 자신의 괴로움을 어찌나 의연하게 견디던지, 나는 그를 위해 가슴이 미어졌다. 나는 그의 손에 들린 원고를 보았고, 그가 그것을 읽으면 내가 얼마나 알고 있는지 깨달으리라는 것을 알았다. 그래서 나는 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"마음의 괴로움을 겪는 모든 분을 제가 위로해 드릴 수 있다면 좋겠어요. 제가 당신의 벗이 되게 해주시겠어요? 그리고 위로가 필요하시면 제게 와 주시겠어요? 제가 왜 이런 말을 하는지는 나중에 아시게 될 거예요.\" 그는 내가 진심임을 알아채고는, 몸을 숙여 내 손을 잡아 입술로 가져가 입맞췄다. 그토록 용감하고 헌신적인 영혼에게는 보잘것없는 위로로만 보였기에, 나는 충동적으로 몸을 숙여 그에게 입을 맞췄다. 그의 눈에 눈물이 차올랐고, 잠시 목이 메었다. 그는 무척 차분하게 말했다.\n\n\"아가씨, 당신은 그 진심 어린 친절을 살아 있는 한 결코 후회하지 않으실 겁니다!\" 그러고는 자기 친구에게로 서재로 들어갔다.\n\n\"아가씨!\"—— 그가 루시에게 썼던 바로 그 말이었다. 아, 그러나 그는 자신이 참된 벗임을 스스로 증명해 보였다!\n\n# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n9월 30일. — 다섯 시에 집에 돌아와 보니, 고달밍과 모리스가 이미 도착했을 뿐만 아니라 하커와 그의 훌륭한 아내가 작성하여 정리해둔 여러 일기와 편지의 사본을 벌써 다 살펴본 뒤였다. 하커는 헤네시 박사가 나에게 편지로 일러준 운송업자 인부들을 만나러 갔다가 아직 돌아오지 않았다. 하커 부인이 우리에게 차 한 잔을 내주었는데, 나는 이 낡은 집에서 살게 된 이래 처음으로 이곳이 진정 집처럼 느껴졌다고 솔직히 말할 수 있다. 차를 다 마시자 하커 부인이 말했다.\n\n\"수어드 박사님, 한 가지 청을 드려도 될까요? 박사님의 환자인 렌필드 씨를 만나 보고 싶어요. 부디 만나게 해주세요. 박사님이 일기에 적으신 그분 이야기가 너무도 흥미로워서요!\" 그녀의 표정이 어찌나 간절하고 어여뻤던지 나는 거절할 수가 없었고, 또 거절할 까닭도 전혀 없었다. 그래서 나는 그녀를 데리고 갔다. 방에 들어가서 나는 그 남자에게 어떤 부인이 만나고 싶어 한다고 일러주었다. 그러자 그는 그저 \"왜요?\" 하고 되물을 뿐이었다.\n\n\"이 집을 두루 둘러보시는 중인데, 집 안의 모든 사람을 만나 보고 싶어 하시오.\" 내가 답했다. \"아, 그럼 좋습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"들어오시라고 하세요, 얼마든지요. 다만 방을 좀 치울 동안 잠깐만 기다려 주십시오.\" 그가 방을 치우는 방식은 기이했다. 내가 미처 말릴 새도 없이, 그는 상자 안의 파리와 거미를 모조리 꿀꺽 삼켜버린 것이다. 어떤 간섭을 두려워하거나 시기하고 있음이 분명했다. 그 역겨운 일을 끝내자 그는 명랑하게 \"부인을 들어오시게 하세요\"라고 말하고는, 침대 가장자리에 머리를 숙이고 앉았으나, 눈꺼풀만은 치켜뜨고 있어 그녀가 들어오는 모습을 볼 수 있게 했다. 한순간 나는 그에게 살의가 있을지도 모른다고 생각했다. 내 서재에서 나를 덮치기 직전에 그가 얼마나 조용했는지를 떠올리고, 그가 그녀에게 달려들려 하면 곧바로 붙잡을 수 있는 자리에 서 있도록 신경을 썼다. 그녀는 어떤 광인에게서도 단번에 존중을 이끌어낼 만한 편안한 우아함으로 방에 들어섰다. 편안함이야말로 미친 사람들이 가장 존중하는 자질 가운데 하나인 것이다. 그녀는 상냥하게 미소 지으며 그에게 다가가 손을 내밀었다.\n\n\"안녕하세요, 렌필드 씨.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"보시다시피 저는 당신을 알고 있어요. 수어드 박사님이 당신 이야기를 해주셨거든요.\" 그는 곧바로 대꾸하지 않고, 얼굴을 굳게 찌푸린 채 그녀를 위아래로 뚫어지게 훑어보았다. 그 표정은 이내 놀라움으로, 다시 의혹으로 바뀌었다. 그러더니 나를 몹시 놀라게도, 그가 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 박사가 결혼하고 싶어 했던 그 아가씨가 아니지요? 그럴 리가 없어요, 아시다시피 그 여자는 죽었으니까요.\" 하커 부인이 다정하게 미소 지으며 답했다.\n\n\"아, 아니에요! 저에겐 제 남편이 있고, 저는 수어드 박사님을 뵙기 전에, 그분이 저를 보기도 전에 이미 그와 결혼했답니다. 저는 하커 부인이에요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 여기서 무얼 하고 계시죠?\"\n\n\"제 남편과 저는 수어드 박사님 댁에 손님으로 머물고 있어요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 머물지 마세요.\"\n\n\"하지만 어째서죠?\" 나는 이런 식의 대화가 나에게 그렇듯 하커 부인에게도 유쾌하지 않으리라는 생각이 들어 끼어들었다.\n\n\"내가 누군가와 결혼하고 싶어 했다는 걸 어떻게 알았소?\" 그의 대답은 그저 경멸에 찬 것이었다. 그는 잠깐 말을 멈추고 하커 부인에게서 나에게로 눈길을 돌렸다가, 곧바로 다시 그녀에게로 거두며 말했다.\n\n\"참으로 멍청한 질문이군요!\"\n\n\"저는 전혀 그렇게 생각하지 않아요, 렌필드 씨.\" 하커 부인이 곧바로 나를 두둔하며 말했다. 그는 나에게 보였던 경멸만큼이나 큰 예의와 존중을 담아 그녀에게 답했다.\n\n\"하커 부인, 물론 부인께서도 이해하시겠지요. 우리 주인장처럼 사랑받고 존경받는 사람이라면, 그에 관한 모든 것이 우리의 작은 공동체에서 관심거리가 되는 법입니다. 수어드 박사는 그의 집안사람들과 친구들뿐 아니라, 환자들에게서까지도 사랑을 받습니다. 그런데 그 환자들 가운데 더러는 정신의 균형이 온전치 못해 원인과 결과를 왜곡하기 쉽지요. 저 자신이 정신병원의 수용자였던 까닭에, 그 수용자 가운데 더러의 궤변적 경향이 '비원인(non causa)'과 '논점 일탈(ignoratio elenchi)'의 오류로 기우는 것을 알아차리지 않을 수 없습니다.\" 나는 이 새로운 전개에 그야말로 눈이 휘둥그레졌다. 여기, 내가 아끼는 광인이 — 내가 여태껏 만나본 가운데 가장 전형적인 유형의 광인이 — 세련된 신사의 태도로 근원적인 철학을 논하고 있는 것이다. 혹시 하커 부인의 존재가 그의 기억 속 어떤 현(絃)을 건드린 것은 아닐까 싶었다. 만일 이 새로운 국면이 자발적인 것이거나, 어떤 식으로든 그녀의 무의식적인 영향에서 비롯된 것이라면, 그녀는 분명 보기 드문 재능이나 힘을 지닌 것이리라.\n\n우리는 한동안 더 이야기를 이어갔다. 그가 겉보기에 꽤 분별 있어 보이자, 그녀는 처음 시작할 때 나를 미심쩍은 눈으로 바라보면서, 그를 그가 좋아하는 화제로 이끌어보았다. 나는 또 한 번 놀랐다. 그가 완전한 제정신의 공정함으로 그 문제를 다루었기 때문이다. 그는 어떤 것들을 언급할 때 심지어 자기 자신을 예로 들기까지 했다.\n\n\"보십시오, 저 자신이 바로 기이한 믿음을 가졌던 사람의 한 예입니다. 사실 제 친구들이 놀라 저를 통제 아래 두어야 한다고 고집한 것도 무리는 아니었지요. 저는 생명이 실재하며 끊임없이 이어지는 하나의 실체라고, 그래서 아무리 창조의 단계에서 낮은 것일지라도 살아 있는 것을 수없이 삼키면 생명을 무한히 늘릴 수 있다고 믿곤 했습니다. 때로는 그 믿음이 어찌나 강했던지 실제로 사람의 목숨을 빼앗으려 한 적도 있었지요. 여기 계신 박사님이 증언해 주실 겁니다. 언젠가 제가 그분을 죽이려 한 적이 있는데, 그것은 그분의 피라는 매개를 통해 그분의 생명을 제 몸에 동화시켜 제 생명력을 강화하려는 목적에서였습니다 — 물론 '피가 곧 생명이니라'라는 성경 구절에 기대어서 말이지요. 하기야 어떤 엉터리 약장수가 그 자명한 이치를 그야말로 경멸스러울 만큼 천박하게 만들어버리긴 했지만요. 그렇지 않습니까, 박사님?\" 나는 고개를 끄덕여 동의했다. 너무도 놀라 무엇을 생각해야 할지, 무슨 말을 해야 할지조차 알 수 없었기 때문이다. 채 오 분도 지나지 않아 그가 거미와 파리를 삼키는 것을 내가 보았다는 사실이 도무지 믿기지 않았다. 시계를 보니 판 헬싱을 마중하러 역에 나가야 할 시각이었으므로, 나는 하커 부인에게 이만 자리를 떠야겠다고 말했다. 그녀는 곧바로 따라나섰는데, 떠나기 전 렌필드 씨에게 상냥하게 말했다. \"안녕히 계세요, 당신께 더 즐거운 형편에서 자주 뵐 수 있기를 바라요.\" 그러자 나를 놀라게도 그가 이렇게 답했다.\n\n\"안녕히 가십시오, 친애하는 부인. 부디 제가 그 어여쁜 얼굴을 두 번 다시 보지 않게 되기를 하느님께 비옵니다. 하느님께서 당신을 축복하고 지켜주시기를!\"\n\n판 헬싱을 마중하러 역에 나갈 때 나는 젊은이들을 집에 남겨두고 갔다. 가엾은 아트는 루시가 처음 앓아누운 이래로 보지 못했던 만큼 한결 밝아 보였고, 퀸시도 오랜만에 본디의 명랑한 모습을 되찾은 듯했다.\n\n판 헬싱은 소년처럼 활기차고 날렵하게 객차에서 내렸다. 그는 단번에 나를 알아보고 달려와 말했다.\n\n\"아, 존, 다들 어떻게 지내나? 잘 지내고? 그래! 나는 분주했네, 필요하다면 여기 머물 작정으로 왔으니까. 내 일은 다 정리했고, 할 이야기도 많네. 미나 부인은 자네와 함께 있나? 그렇군. 그리고 그 훌륭한 남편도? 그리고 아서와 내 친구 퀸시도 함께 있나? 좋아!\"\n\n집으로 마차를 몰고 가는 동안 나는 그에게 그간의 일을, 그리고 하커 부인의 제안 덕분에 내 일기가 어떻게 쓸모를 갖게 되었는지를 이야기했다. 그러자 교수가 내 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"아, 그 놀라운 미나 부인! 그녀는 남자의 두뇌를 — 재능을 많이 타고난 남자라야 가질 법한 두뇌를 — 그리고 여자의 마음을 지녔네. 자비로우신 하느님께서 그토록 훌륭한 조합을 만드실 때에는 어떤 목적이 있으셨던 게야, 내 말을 믿게. 존, 지금까지는 운이 그 부인을 우리에게 도움이 되게 해주었네. 그러나 오늘 밤 이후로 그녀는 이 끔찍한 일에 관여해서는 안 되네. 그토록 큰 위험을 무릅쓰게 하는 건 좋지 않아. 우리 남자들은 이 괴물을 없애기로 결심했네 — 아니, 우리가 맹세하지 않았는가? — 그러나 그것은 여자가 할 몫이 아니네. 설령 그녀가 해를 입지 않는다 해도, 그토록 많고 그토록 숱한 공포 속에서 그녀의 심장이 견뎌내지 못할지도 모르네. 그리고 그 후에 그녀는 고통받을 수 있어 — 깨어 있을 때는 신경 때문에, 잠들었을 때는 꿈 때문에 말이야. 게다가 그녀는 젊은 부인이고 결혼한 지도 얼마 되지 않았네. 지금은 아니더라도 언젠가는 생각해야 할 다른 일들이 있을지도 모르지. 자네 말로는 그녀가 모든 것을 다 기록했다 하니, 그렇다면 우리와 의논은 해야겠지. 그러나 내일 그녀는 이 일과 작별하고, 우리만 나아가야 하네.\" 나는 진심으로 그의 말에 동의했다. 그러고 나서 나는 그가 없는 동안 우리가 알아낸 것을 그에게 말해주었다. 드라큘라가 사들인 집이 바로 내 집 바로 옆집이라는 사실을. 그는 깜짝 놀랐고, 큰 근심이 그를 덮치는 듯했다. \"아, 그것을 진작 알았더라면!\" 그가 말했다. \"그랬다면 우리가 제때 그에게 닿아 가엾은 루시를 구할 수 있었을 텐데. 하지만 자네들 말마따나 '이미 엎질러진 우유는 뒤늦게 울부짖지 않는 법'이지. 그것은 생각하지 말고, 끝까지 우리 길을 가세.\" 그러고 나서 그는 침묵에 잠겼고, 그 침묵은 우리가 내 집 대문에 들어설 때까지 이어졌다. 우리가 저녁식사를 준비하러 가기 전에 그는 하커 부인에게 말했다.\n\n\"미나 부인, 내 친구 존에게서 들었소만, 부인과 부군께서 지금 이 순간까지 있었던 모든 일을 정확한 순서로 정리해 두셨다더군요.\"\n\n\"이 순간까지는 아니에요, 교수님.\" 그녀가 충동적으로 말했다. \"오늘 아침까지예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 어찌하여 지금까지가 아니오? 우리는 지금까지 그 사소한 일들이 모두 얼마나 좋은 빛을 비춰주었는지 보아왔소. 우리는 우리의 비밀을 털어놓았고, 그러고도 털어놓은 사람 누구도 그로 인해 손해를 본 이가 없소.\"\n\n하커 부인은 얼굴을 붉히기 시작했고, 주머니에서 종이 한 장을 꺼내며 말했다.\n\n\"판 헬싱 박사님, 이것을 읽어보시고, 이것이 들어가야 하는지 말씀해 주세요. 오늘 하루의 제 기록이에요. 저 또한 지금으로서는 아무리 사소한 일이라도 모두 적어둘 필요가 있음을 알았어요. 하지만 이 안에는 개인적인 것 말고는 별것이 없어요. 들어가야 할까요?\" 교수는 그것을 진지하게 통독하더니 돌려주며 말했다.\n\n\"부인께서 원치 않으신다면 들어가지 않아도 되오. 하지만 나는 그것이 들어가기를 바라오. 그것은 부군께서 부인을 한층 더 사랑하게 만들 뿐이고, 부인의 벗인 우리 모두가 부인을 더욱 존경하게 — 아울러 더욱 존중하고 사랑하게 — 할 따름이오.\" 그녀는 다시 한 번 얼굴을 붉히고 환하게 미소 지으며 그것을 도로 받았다.\n\n이리하여 이제 바로 이 시각까지, 우리가 가진 모든 기록은 완전하게 순서대로 갖추어졌다. 교수는 한 부를 가져가, 저녁식사 후 그리고 아홉 시로 정해진 우리의 회합 전에 살펴보기로 했다. 나머지 우리는 모든 것을 이미 다 읽었다. 그러니 서재에서 모일 때면 우리 모두가 사실들을 알고 있을 것이고, 이 끔찍하고 알 수 없는 적과 맞설 우리의 전투 계획을 짤 수 있을 것이다.\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」\n\n9월 30일. — 여섯 시에 있었던 저녁식사 두 시간 후 수어드 박사의 서재에 모였을 때, 우리는 자신도 모르는 사이에 일종의 위원회나 평의회 같은 것을 이루었다. 판 헬싱 교수가 식탁 상석에 앉았는데, 그가 방에 들어오자 수어드 박사가 그 자리로 안내한 것이었다. 교수는 나를 그의 오른쪽, 바로 옆자리에 앉히고 서기 역할을 맡아달라고 부탁했다. 조너선은 내 옆에 앉았다. 우리 맞은편에는 고달밍 경, 수어드 박사, 그리고 모리스 씨가 있었다 — 고달밍 경이 교수 옆이었고, 수어드 박사가 가운데였다. 교수가 말했다.\n\n\"여러분 모두 이 서류들에 담긴 사실들을 알고 계신 것으로 보아도 좋겠지요.\" 우리 모두가 동의를 표하자 그는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 우리가 상대해야 할 적이 어떤 종류인지 여러분에게 조금 말씀드리는 게 좋겠습니다. 그런 다음 나를 위해 확인된 이 사내의 내력을 조금 알려드리겠소. 그러고 나서 우리가 어떻게 행동할지 의논하고, 그에 따라 대비책을 세울 수 있을 것이오.\n\n흡혈귀라는 존재가 실재하오. 우리 가운데 몇몇은 그들이 존재한다는 증거를 가지고 있소. 설령 우리 자신의 불행한 경험이라는 증거가 없다 하더라도, 과거의 가르침과 기록만으로도 제정신인 사람들에게는 충분한 증거가 되오. 처음에는 나도 회의적이었음을 인정하오. 만일 오랜 세월에 걸쳐 마음을 열어두도록 스스로를 단련하지 않았더라면, 나는 그 사실이 내 귀에 천둥처럼 울릴 그때까지 믿지 못했을 것이오. '보라! 보라! 내가 증명하노라, 내가 증명하노라.' 아! 지금 아는 것을 처음부터 알았더라면 — 아니, 그에 대해 짐작이라도 했더라면 — 우리 가운데 그토록 그녀를 사랑했던 많은 이들에게 그 소중한 한 생명이 지켜졌을 것이오. 그러나 그것은 지나간 일이오. 그러니 우리는 우리가 구할 수 있는 동안, 다른 가엾은 영혼들이 스러지지 않도록 힘써 일해야 하오. 노스페라투는 한 번 쏘면 죽고 마는 벌처럼 죽지 않소. 그는 다만 더 강해질 뿐이오. 그리고 더 강해진 만큼 악을 행할 힘도 더 커지오. 우리 가운데 있는 이 흡혈귀는 그 자체로 장정 스무 명만큼의 힘을 지녔소. 그의 교활함은 인간을 넘어서니, 그것은 수백 년 세월의 산물이기 때문이오. 그는 여전히 강령술(降靈術)의 도움을 받고 있는데, 그 어원이 가리키듯 죽은 자를 통한 점복(占卜)이며, 그가 가까이 다가갈 수 있는 모든 죽은 자가 그의 명령 아래 있소. 그는 짐승이며, 짐승 이상이오. 그는 무정함에서 악마이며, 그에게는 심장이라는 것이 없소. 그는 제약 안에서나마 마음대로, 언제든, 어디든, 그가 취할 수 있는 어떤 형상으로든 나타날 수 있소. 그는 자기 영역 안에서 자연의 요소들을 — 폭풍, 안개, 천둥을 — 부릴 수 있소. 그는 더 하찮은 온갖 것들을, 즉 쥐와 올빼미와 박쥐를 — 나방과 여우와 늑대를 부릴 수 있소. 그는 커질 수도 작아질 수도 있소. 그리고 때로는 사라졌다가 누구도 모르게 나타날 수 있소. 그렇다면 우리는 그를 없애기 위한 우리의 공격을 어떻게 시작해야 하겠소? 그가 있는 곳을 어떻게 찾아내며, 또 찾아낸 뒤에 어떻게 없앨 수 있겠소? 벗들이여, 이것은 큰일이오. 우리가 떠맡는 것은 끔찍한 과업이며, 용감한 자조차 몸서리치게 할 결과가 따를 수도 있소. 이 싸움에서 우리가 실패하면 그가 반드시 이길 것이고, 그러면 우리는 어떻게 되겠소? 목숨이야 아무것도 아니오, 나는 그것을 개의치 않소. 그러나 여기서 실패하는 것은 단순히 삶이냐 죽음이냐의 문제가 아니오. 그것은 우리가 그와 같이 되는 것이오. 우리가 이후로 그처럼 밤의 추악한 존재가 되는 것이오 — 심장도 양심도 없이, 우리가 가장 사랑하는 이들의 육신과 영혼을 먹잇감으로 삼으면서 말이오. 우리에게 천국의 문은 영원히 닫힐 것이오. 누가 우리를 위해 그 문을 다시 열어주겠소? 우리는 영원토록 모두에게 혐오받으며 살아갈 것이오. 하느님의 햇빛 위에 묻은 오점이요, 인간을 위해 죽으신 그분의 옆구리에 박힌 화살이 되는 것이오. 그러나 우리는 의무와 얼굴을 맞대고 있소. 이런 경우에 우리가 움츠러들어야 하겠소? 나로 말하면, 아니라고 하겠소. 하기야 나는 늙었고, 햇빛과 아름다운 곳들과 새들의 노래와 음악과 사랑이 있는 삶은 이미 저 뒤로 멀어졌소. 여러분 다른 이들은 젊소. 더러는 슬픔을 겪었으나, 앞으로 누릴 좋은 날들이 아직 남아 있소. 여러분은 무어라 답하시겠소?\"\n\n그가 말하는 동안 조너선이 내 손을 잡았다. 그의 손이 뻗어오는 것을 보았을 때, 아, 나는 우리가 처한 위험의 끔찍한 본질이 그를 압도하고 있는 것은 아닐까 너무도 두려웠다. 그러나 그 손길을 느끼는 것은 나에게 생명과도 같았다 — 그토록 강하고, 그토록 자신에 차 있고, 그토록 단호한 손길이었다. 용감한 사내의 손은 그 자체로 말을 할 수 있으니, 그 음악을 듣는 데에는 여자의 사랑조차 필요치 않은 것이다.\n\n교수가 말을 마쳤을 때 남편이 내 눈을 들여다보았고, 나도 그의 눈을 들여다보았다. 우리 사이에는 말이 필요 없었다.\n\n\"미나와 저 자신을 대신해 답하겠습니다.\" 그가 말했다.\n\n\"저도 끼워 주십시오, 교수님.\" 퀸시 모리스 씨가 여느 때처럼 간결하게 말했다.\n\n\"저도 함께하겠습니다.\" 고달밍 경이 말했다. \"다른 이유가 없더라도, 루시를 위해서요.\"\n\n수어드 박사는 그저 고개를 끄덕였다. 교수는 일어서더니 황금 십자고상을 식탁에 내려놓고 양옆으로 손을 내밀었다. 나는 그의 오른손을, 고달밍 경은 그의 왼손을 잡았다. 조너선은 왼손으로 내 오른손을 잡고 모리스 씨에게로 팔을 뻗었다. 이렇게 우리 모두가 손을 맞잡으면서 우리의 엄숙한 맹약이 이루어졌다. 나는 심장이 얼음처럼 차가워지는 것을 느꼈으나, 물러서리라는 생각은 떠오르지조차 않았다. 우리는 다시 자리에 앉았고, 판 헬싱 박사는 일종의 명랑함을 띠고 말을 이어갔는데, 그것은 진지한 작업이 시작되었음을 보여주는 것이었다. 이 일은 삶의 다른 어떤 거래와 마찬가지로 진지하게, 또 사무적인 방식으로 다루어져야 했다.\n\n\"자, 우리가 무엇과 맞서 싸워야 하는지 여러분은 아셨소. 그러나 우리 또한 힘이 없지 않소. 우리에게는 결속의 힘이 있는데 — 이는 흡혈귀 족속에게는 허락되지 않은 힘이오. 우리에게는 과학의 자원이 있소. 우리는 자유로이 행동하고 사고할 수 있소. 그리고 낮의 시간도 밤의 시간도 똑같이 우리의 것이오. 사실 우리의 힘이 미치는 한, 그 힘에는 족쇄가 없으며, 우리는 그것을 자유로이 쓸 수 있소. 우리에게는 하나의 대의에 헌신하는 마음이 있고, 이뤄야 할 목적이 있는데 그것은 이기적인 것이 아니오. 이런 것들은 큰 힘이오.\n\n이제 우리에게 맞선 그 일반적인 힘들이 어디까지 제약을 받는지, 또 그 개인이 어떻게 그렇지 못한지를 살펴봅시다. 요컨대, 흡혈귀 일반의 제약과, 특히 이 흡혈귀의 제약을 헤아려봅시다.\n\n우리가 의지할 것이라곤 전승과 미신뿐이오. 이런 것들은 처음에는 대단해 보이지 않소, 더구나 생사의 문제일 때에는 — 아니, 삶이냐 죽음이냐를 넘어서는 문제일 때에는 말이오. 그러나 우리는 그것으로 만족해야 하오. 첫째로는 그래야만 하기 때문이오 — 우리가 마음대로 쓸 다른 수단이 없으니 말이오 — 둘째로는, 따지고 보면 이런 것들이 — 전승과 미신이 — 전부이기 때문이오. 흡혈귀에 대한 믿음이 — 안타깝게도 우리에게는 아니지만! — 다른 이들에게는 바로 그것들 위에 놓여 있지 않소? 일 년 전이라면 우리 가운데 누가 이 과학적이고 회의적이며 사실 위주인 19세기 한복판에서 그런 가능성을 받아들였겠소? 우리는 우리 눈앞에서 입증된 믿음마저 비웃었소. 그러니 흡혈귀라는 것과, 그의 제약과 그를 처치하는 법에 대한 믿음이 지금으로서는 같은 토대 위에 놓여 있다고 받아들입시다. 한 가지 말해두겠소, 그는 인간이 살아온 곳이라면 어디서나 알려져 있소. 옛 그리스에서, 옛 로마에서, 그는 독일 전역에서, 프랑스에서, 인도에서, 심지어 케르소네소스에서도 번성하오. 그리고 우리에게서 모든 면에서 그토록 먼 중국에서도 그는 있으며, 사람들은 오늘날까지 그를 두려워하오. 그는 광포한 아이슬란드 전사, 악마가 낳은 훈족, 슬라브족, 색슨족, 마자르족의 뒤를 따라왔소. 그러니 여기까지가 우리가 의지해 행동할 수 있는 전부이며, 한 가지 말해두겠는데, 그 믿음의 매우 많은 부분이 우리 자신의 그 불행한 경험에서 본 것으로 입증되오. 흡혈귀는 계속 살아가며, 단지 세월이 흐른다고 죽지 않소. 그는 산 자의 피로 살을 찌울 수 있을 때 번성하오. 더 나아가, 우리는 우리 가운데서 그가 심지어 젊어질 수도 있음을 보았소. 그의 특별한 양식이 넉넉할 때면 그의 생명력이 왕성해지고, 마치 스스로 새로워지는 듯이 보인다는 것을 말이오. 그러나 그는 이 양식 없이는 번성하지 못하오. 그는 다른 이들처럼 먹지 않소. 몇 주를 그와 함께 지낸 우리 벗 조너선조차 그가 먹는 것을 한 번도 보지 못했소, 결코! 그는 그림자를 드리우지 않소. 조너선이 또한 관찰했듯이, 그는 거울에 비치지 않소. 그는 한 손에 여러 사람 몫의 힘을 지녔소 — 조너선이 그가 늑대들을 막으려고 문을 닫았을 때, 또 역마차에서 그가 그를 거들었을 때를 다시 증언으로 삼으시오. 그는 늑대로 둔갑할 수 있소, 휘트비에 배가 닿았을 때 그가 개를 갈가리 찢었던 데서 우리가 짐작하듯이 말이오. 그는 박쥐가 될 수 있소, 휘트비에서 미나 부인이 창가에서 그를 본 것처럼, 또 우리 벗 존이 이 바로 옆집에서 그가 날아가는 것을 본 것처럼, 또 내 벗 퀸시가 루시 양의 창가에서 그를 본 것처럼 말이오. 그는 스스로 만들어낸 안개에 싸여 올 수 있소 — 저 고귀한 선장이 이를 입증해 주었소. 그러나 우리가 아는 바로는, 그가 이 안개를 만들 수 있는 거리는 한정되어 있고, 그것은 오직 자기 주위에만 미칠 뿐이오. 그는 달빛을 타고 원소의 티끌처럼 올 수 있소 — 조너선이 드라큘라 성에서 그 자매들을 본 것처럼 또한 말이오. 그는 그토록 작아질 수 있소 — 우리 자신이 루시 양이 안식에 들기 전에 무덤 문에서 머리카락 한 올만 한 틈으로 빠져나가는 것을 보았소. 그는 일단 길을 찾기만 하면, 아무리 단단히 봉해졌든, 심지어 불로 녹여 붙여졌든 — 여러분이 땜질이라 부르는 것 말이오 — 무엇에서든 나올 수 있고 무엇으로든 들어갈 수 있소. 그는 어둠 속에서 볼 수 있소 — 절반이 빛에서 닫혀 있는 이 세상에서 이는 작지 않은 힘이오. 아, 그러나 내 말을 끝까지 들으시오. 그는 이 모든 것을 할 수 있으나, 그렇다고 자유롭지는 않소. 아니, 그는 갤리선의 노예보다도, 감방에 갇힌 광인보다도 더한 죄수요. 그는 마음 내키는 대로 갈 수 없소. 자연에 속하지 않은 그가 그럼에도 자연의 법칙 가운데 몇 가지에는 복종해야 하오 — 어째서인지는 우리도 모르오. 그는 처음에는 어디에도 들어갈 수 없소, 그 집안의 누군가가 그에게 들어오라고 청하지 않는 한 말이오. 하지만 그다음부터는 마음대로 드나들 수 있소. 그의 힘은 모든 악한 것들의 힘이 그러하듯 날이 밝아오면 멈추오. 오직 특정한 때에만 그는 한정된 자유를 누릴 수 있소. 그가 매여 있는 곳에 있지 않을 때에는, 정오나 정확한 일출 혹은 일몰의 순간에만 자신의 모습을 바꿀 수 있소. 이런 것들을 우리는 전해 들었고, 우리의 이 기록에서 우리는 추론으로 그 증거를 가지고 있소. 그리하여, 그가 자기 흙의 집, 관(棺)의 집, 지옥의 집, 즉 신성을 박탈당한 그 장소를 가지고 있을 때에는 — 휘트비에서 그가 자살자의 무덤으로 갔을 때 우리가 보았듯이 — 자기 제약 안에서 마음대로 할 수 있는 반면, 그 밖의 때에는 오로지 그 시각이 와야만 모습을 바꿀 수 있소. 또한 그는 조류가 멈춘 때나 만조일 때에만 흐르는 물을 건널 수 있다고도 하오. 그리고 그를 너무도 괴롭혀 무력하게 만드는 것들이 있는데, 우리가 아는 마늘이 그러하오. 그리고 신성한 것들로 말하면, 우리가 결의할 때 바로 지금 우리 가운데 있었던 이 상징, 내 십자고상 같은 것에 대해 그는 아무것도 아니지만, 그것들이 있는 자리에서는 멀리 떨어져 조용히 경의를 표하며 자리할 따름이오. 또 다른 것들도 있는데, 우리가 그를 찾는 중에 필요할지 모르니 여러분에게 일러두겠소. 그의 관 위에 놓인 들장미 가지는 그가 거기서 움직이지 못하게 하오. 관 속에 쏘아 넣은 신성한 총알은 그를 죽여 참된 죽음에 이르게 하오. 그리고 그를 꿰뚫는 말뚝으로 말하면, 우리는 이미 그것이 가져다주는 안식을 알고 있소. 또 안식을 주는 잘린 머리도 있소. 우리는 우리 눈으로 그것을 보았소.\n\n그리하여 한때 사람이었던 이 자의 거처를 찾아내면, 우리가 아는 것에 따른다면 그를 관에 가두고 없앨 수 있소. 그러나 그는 영리하오. 나는 부다페스트 대학의 내 벗 아르미니우스에게 그의 기록을 작성해 달라고 부탁했소. 그리고 그는 있는 온갖 자료로부터, 이 사내가 어떤 자였는지를 나에게 일러주었소. 그는 분명, 터키 땅의 바로 그 변경에서 큰 강 건너 터키군을 상대로 이름을 떨친 그 영주 드라큘라였을 것이오. 만일 그렇다면 그는 예사 사람이 아니었소. 그 시대에, 그리고 이후 수백 년 동안 그는 '숲 너머의 땅'의 아들들 가운데 가장 영리하고 가장 교활하며, 또한 가장 용감한 자로 일컬어졌기 때문이오. 그 강력한 두뇌와 그 무쇠 같은 결단이 그와 함께 무덤으로 갔고, 지금도 우리에게 맞서 도열해 있소. 아르미니우스의 말로는, 드라큘라 가문은 위대하고 고귀한 혈통이었으나, 이따금 동시대인들로부터 악마와 거래했다고 여겨진 자손들이 나오기도 했다 하오. 그들은 헤르만슈타트 호수 위 산중에 있는 스콜로만체에서 악마의 비밀을 배웠는데, 그곳에서는 악마가 열 번째 학생을 자기 몫으로 가져가오. 기록에는 '스트레고이카' — 마녀, '오르도그'와 '포콜' — 사탄과 지옥 같은 말들이 있고, 어느 필사본에서는 바로 이 드라큘라가 '밤피르'로 일컬어지는데, 우리 모두 그 뜻을 너무도 잘 아오. 바로 이자의 혈통에서 위대한 사내들과 선량한 여인들이 나왔고, 그들의 무덤이 이 추악함이 깃들 수 있는 유일한 땅을 성스럽게 만드오. 이 악한 것이 온갖 선한 것에 깊이 뿌리내리고 있다는 것이야말로 그 공포 가운데 결코 작지 않은 것이니, 거룩한 기억이 메마른 토양에서는 그가 안식할 수 없기 때문이오.\"\n\n그들이 이야기하는 동안 모리스 씨는 줄곧 창문을 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었는데, 이제 그가 조용히 일어나 방을 나갔다. 잠시 사이를 두었다가 교수가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이제 우리는 무엇을 할지 정해야 하오. 우리에게는 여기 많은 자료가 있고, 우리는 우리의 작전을 짜는 데로 나아가야 하오. 우리는 조너선의 조사를 통해 성에서 휘트비로 흙이 든 상자 쉰 개가 왔고, 그것들이 모두 카팩스로 인도되었음을 알고 있소. 또한 그 상자들 가운데 적어도 일부는 옮겨졌음을 알고 있소. 내 생각에 우리의 첫걸음은, 나머지 전부가 우리가 오늘 바라본 저 담장 너머 그 집 안에 남아 있는지, 아니면 그 이상이 옮겨졌는지를 확인하는 것이어야 하오. 만일 후자라면, 우리는 추적해야 하오 ——\"\n\n여기서 우리는 매우 놀라운 방식으로 말이 끊겼다. 집 밖에서 권총 발사음이 들렸다. 창유리가 총알에 산산이 부서졌고, 그 총알은 창문턱 위쪽에서 튕겨나가 방의 먼 벽에 부딪혔다. 아무래도 나는 마음속으로 겁쟁이인 모양으로, 비명을 질렀다. 남자들은 모두 벌떡 일어섰고, 고달밍 경이 창문으로 날듯이 달려가 미닫이창을 들어 올렸다. 그가 그렇게 하는 동안 밖에서 모리스 씨의 목소리가 들렸다.\n\n\"미안합니다! 여러분을 놀라게 한 것 같군요. 안으로 들어가 자초지종을 말씀드리겠습니다.\" 잠시 후 그가 들어와 말했다.\n\n\"제가 어리석은 짓을 했습니다, 하커 부인, 진심으로 용서를 빕니다. 부인을 무척이나 놀라게 해드린 것 같군요. 하지만 실은, 교수님이 말씀하시는 동안 커다란 박쥐 한 마리가 와서 창문턱에 앉았던 겁니다. 요즈음 일들 탓에 그 망할 짐승들에 대한 공포가 어찌나 깊어졌는지 도무지 견딜 수가 없어서, 밖으로 나가 한 방 쏘려 했던 거지요. 요사이 저녁마다 한 마리라도 보이면 그래 왔거든요. 그때 자네는 그것 때문에 나를 비웃곤 했지, 아트.\"\n\n\"맞혔나?\" 판 헬싱 박사가 물었다.\n\n\"모르겠습니다. 못 맞힌 것 같아요, 숲속으로 날아가 버렸거든요.\" 그는 더 말하지 않고 자리에 앉았고, 교수가 다시 자신의 설명을 이어가기 시작했다.\n\n\"우리는 이 상자들을 하나하나 추적해야 하오. 그리고 준비가 되면, 우리는 이 괴물을 그 소굴에서 사로잡거나 죽이거나 해야 하오. 아니면, 말하자면 그 흙을 멸균해서 그가 더 이상 거기서 안전을 구하지 못하게 해야 하오. 그리하여 마침내 우리는 정오와 일몰 사이의 시각에 사람의 모습을 한 그를 찾아내, 그가 가장 약할 때 그와 맞붙을 수 있을 것이오.\n\n그리고 이제 미나 부인, 부인에게는 모든 일이 잘 풀릴 때까지 오늘 밤이 마지막이오. 부인은 우리에게 너무도 소중하여 그런 위험을 무릅쓰게 할 수 없소. 오늘 밤 우리가 헤어지고 나면, 부인은 더 이상 묻지 마시오. 우리가 때가 되면 모든 것을 말씀드리겠소. 우리는 남자들이고 견딜 수 있소. 그러나 부인은 우리의 별이요 우리의 희망이 되어주셔야 하오. 그리고 부인이 우리처럼 위험에 처해 있지 않다면 우리는 한결 자유로이 행동할 수 있을 것이오.\"\n\n남자들 모두가, 심지어 조너선조차 안도하는 듯했다. 그러나 나로서는 그들이 나를 염려하느라 위험을 무릅쓰고, 어쩌면 — 힘이야말로 최선의 안전인데 — 그들의 안전을 떨어뜨리는 것이 좋게 여겨지지 않았다. 그러나 그들의 결심은 굳었고, 비록 나에게는 삼키기 쓰라린 알약이었으나, 나는 나를 향한 그들의 기사도적인 보살핌을 받아들이는 것 말고는 아무 말도 할 수 없었다.\n\n모리스 씨가 다시 논의를 이어갔다.\n\n\"지체할 시간이 없으니, 저는 지금 당장 그의 집을 살펴보자는 데 한 표 던지겠습니다. 그에게는 시간이 전부입니다. 우리 쪽의 신속한 행동이 또 다른 희생자를 구할 수도 있고요.\"\n\n행동의 때가 그토록 가까이 다가오자 내 마음이 약해지기 시작했음을 나는 인정한다. 그러나 나는 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 만일 내가 그들의 일에 걸림돌이나 방해물로 비친다면, 그들이 아예 나를 그들의 의논에서 빼버릴지도 모른다는 더 큰 두려움이 있었기 때문이다. 그들은 이제 집 안으로 들어갈 수단을 갖추고 카팩스로 떠났다.\n\n남자들답게, 그들은 나에게 자리에 들어 잠을 자라고 일렀다. 마치 사랑하는 이들이 위험에 처해 있는데 여자가 잠을 잘 수 있기라도 한 것처럼! 나는 누워서 자는 척하려 한다. 조너선이 돌아왔을 때 나로 인해 근심이 더해지지 않도록 말이다.\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n10월 1일, 새벽 4시. — 우리가 막 집을 나서려 할 때, 렌필드로부터 나에게 급한 전갈이 왔는데, 더없이 중요한 할 말이 있으니 곧바로 자기를 만나 줄 수 있겠느냐는 것이었다. 나는 전갈을 가져온 이에게, 아침에 그의 바람을 들어주겠다고, 지금은 마침 바쁘다고 전하라 일렀다. 간병인이 덧붙였다.\n\n\"몹시 간곡하게 매달립니다, 선생님. 그가 저렇게 애가 단 모습은 처음 봅니다. 곧 만나주시지 않으면 발작을 한바탕 일으키지나 않을지 모르겠습니다.\" 나는 그 사람이 까닭 없이 이런 말을 할 리 없음을 알았으므로 말했다. \"알았네, 지금 가지.\" 그러고는 다른 이들에게 잠깐만 나를 기다려 달라고 청했다. 가서 내 '환자'를 만나봐야 한다고 말이다.\n\n\"나도 데려가 주게, 존.\" 교수가 말했다. \"자네 일기에 적힌 그의 사례가 나에게 무척 흥미로운데, 그것이 이따금 우리 사례와도 관련이 있었지. 나는 그를 꼭 보고 싶네, 더구나 그의 정신이 흐트러져 있을 때라면 말이야.\"\n\n\"저도 가도 되겠습니까?\" 고달밍 경이 물었다.\n\n\"저도요?\" 퀸시 모리스가 말했다. \"저도 가도 될까요?\" 하커가 말했다. 나는 고개를 끄덕였고, 우리 모두 함께 복도를 내려갔다.\n\n우리는 그가 상당히 흥분한 상태에 있는 것을 보았으나, 그의 말과 태도는 내가 여태껏 본 어느 때보다 훨씬 이성적이었다. 그에게는 자기 자신에 대한 보기 드문 이해가 있었는데, 그것은 내가 광인에게서 한 번도 본 적 없는 것이었다. 그리고 그는 자신의 논리가 완전히 제정신인 다른 이들에게도 통하리라는 것을 당연하게 여겼다. 우리 넷이 모두 방에 들어갔으나, 처음에는 다른 이들 누구도 아무 말 하지 않았다. 그의 청은, 내가 당장 그를 병원에서 풀어주어 집으로 보내달라는 것이었다. 그는 자신이 완전히 회복되었다는 논거를 들어 이를 뒷받침했고, 현재의 자기 정신이 멀쩡함을 그 증거로 댔다. \"여러분의 벗들께 호소합니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"이분들께서는 어쩌면 제 사례를 판단해 주시는 것을 마다하지 않으시겠지요. 그런데 박사님, 아직 저를 소개해 주지 않으셨군요.\" 나는 어찌나 놀랐던지, 정신병원에서 광인을 소개한다는 기이함이 그 순간에는 떠오르지조차 않았다. 게다가 그 사내의 태도에는 어떤 위엄이, 대등함이 몸에 밴 듯한 면모가 너무도 짙어, 나는 곧바로 소개를 했다. \"고달밍 경, 판 헬싱 교수, 텍사스의 퀸시 모리스 씨, 그리고 렌필드 씨요.\" 그는 한 사람 한 사람과 차례로 악수하며 말했다.\n\n\"고달밍 경, 저는 윈덤에서 경의 부친을 모실 영광을 누렸지요. 경께서 작위를 지니신 것으로 미루어 그분이 이미 세상에 안 계심을 알게 되어 슬픕니다. 그분은 그를 아는 모든 이에게 사랑받고 존경받은 분이셨고, 듣자 하니 젊은 시절에는 더비 경마가 열리는 밤마다 크게 사랑받은, 럼을 태운 펀치의 고안자셨다지요. 모리스 씨, 당신은 당신의 위대한 주(州)를 자랑스러워하셔야 합니다. 그 주가 연방에 받아들여진 것은 하나의 선례가 되어, 훗날 극지와 열대가 성조기와 동맹을 맺게 될 때 멀리 미치는 영향을 끼칠 수도 있겠지요. 먼로주의가 정치적 우화로서 제 참된 자리를 차지하게 되면, 조약의 힘이 언젠가 거대한 확장의 동력임을 입증할지도 모릅니다. 판 헬싱을 만나는 기쁨을 두고 누군들 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠습니까? 선생, 저는 관례적인 경칭을 모두 생략하는 데 대해 양해를 구하지 않겠습니다. 한 개인이 뇌 물질의 연속적 진화를 발견함으로써 치료학에 혁명을 일으켰다면, 관례적인 형식은 어울리지 않습니다. 그것은 그를 한낱 부류의 하나로 한정하는 듯이 보일 테니까요. 여러분, 국적으로든, 혈통으로든, 혹은 타고난 재능을 지녔음으로든, 움직이는 세상에서 저마다의 자리를 차지하기에 합당하신 여러분을 증인으로 삼아 말씀드립니다. 저는 적어도 자유를 온전히 누리고 있는 대다수의 사람들만큼은 제정신입니다. 그리고 박사님, 과학자일 뿐 아니라 인도주의자이자 법의학자이신 당신은, 저를 예외적인 사정 아래 있는 자로 헤아려 대하는 것을 도덕적 의무로 여기시리라 확신합니다.\" 그는 이 마지막 호소를, 그 나름의 매력이 없지 않은, 정중하고 확신에 찬 태도로 했다.\n\n우리 모두 적잖이 동요한 듯하다. 나로 말하면, 그 사내의 성격과 내력을 알고 있음에도, 그의 이성이 회복되었다는 확신에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그래서 나는 그에게 그의 제정신에 대해 만족한다고, 아침에 그의 석방에 필요한 절차를 알아보겠다고 말하고 싶은 강한 충동을 느꼈다. 그러나 그토록 중대한 언명을 하기 전에 기다리는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했으니, 이 특정한 환자가 빠지기 쉬운 급작스러운 변화를 나는 예전부터 알고 있었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 그가 매우 빠르게 호전되고 있는 것으로 보인다고, 아침에 그와 좀 더 길게 이야기를 나눈 뒤 그의 바람을 들어주는 쪽으로 무엇을 할 수 있을지 살펴보겠다는 일반적인 언급에 그치고 만족하기로 했다. 이것은 그를 전혀 만족시키지 못했으니, 그가 재빨리 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 박사님, 박사님께서 제 바람을 거의 헤아리지 못하시는 것이 아닌가 두렵습니다. 저는 당장 — 여기서 — 지금 — 바로 이 시각에 — 가능하다면 바로 이 순간에 가고 싶습니다. 시간이 급박하며, 저 늙은 낫잡이와의 우리의 암묵적 합의에서 그것은 계약의 본질입니다. 수어드 박사님처럼 훌륭한 의사 앞에 이토록 간단하면서도 이토록 중대한 바람을 내놓기만 하면, 그것이 이루어지는 것은 틀림없으리라 저는 확신합니다.\" 그는 나를 날카롭게 바라보다가, 내 얼굴에서 부정의 빛을 읽고는 다른 이들에게로 고개를 돌려 그들을 면밀히 살폈다. 충분한 반응을 얻지 못하자 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"제 짐작이 빗나간 것일 수도 있습니까?\"\n\n\"빗나갔소.\" 나는 솔직하게, 그러나 동시에, 스스로 느끼기에도 무자비하게 말했다. 상당한 사이가 흐른 뒤 그가 천천히 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 저는 청의 근거를 바꾸는 수밖에 없겠군요. 이 양보를 — 은혜든, 특권이든, 무어라 부르시든 — 청하게 해주십시오. 이런 경우에 저는 개인적인 이유에서가 아니라 다른 이들을 위하여 간청하는 것으로 만족합니다. 저는 제 이유 전부를 말씀드릴 처지가 못 됩니다. 그러나 그 이유들이 훌륭하고, 온당하며, 이기적이지 않고, 가장 드높은 의무감에서 비롯된 것임을 제 말로 믿어주셔도 좋다고 단언합니다. 선생, 만일 당신이 제 마음속을 들여다보실 수 있다면, 저를 움직이는 그 심정에 온전히 찬동하실 것입니다. 아니, 그 이상으로, 당신은 저를 당신의 가장 훌륭하고 진실한 벗들 가운데 하나로 꼽으실 것입니다.\" 그는 다시 우리 모두를 날카롭게 바라보았다. 나는 그의 지적 방식 전체가 이렇게 급작스레 바뀐 것이 그저 그의 광기의 또 다른 형태나 국면일 뿐이라는 확신이 점점 커졌다. 그래서 모든 광인이 그러하듯 그가 결국 제 본색을 드러내리라는 것을 경험으로 알기에, 나는 그가 조금 더 말하도록 내버려두기로 마음먹었다. 판 헬싱은 더없이 강렬한 눈빛으로 그를 응시하고 있었는데, 그 시선의 고정된 집중으로 무성한 눈썹이 거의 맞붙을 지경이었다. 그가 렌필드에게 말했는데, 그 어조는 그때에는 나를 놀라게 하지 않았으나 나중에 떠올려 보았을 때에야 놀라웠다 — 그것은 마치 대등한 사람을 대하는 듯한 어조였기 때문이다.\n\n\"오늘 밤 자유로워지고 싶은 진짜 이유를 솔직히 말해줄 수는 없겠소? 만일 그대가 나만이라도 — 편견 없이, 마음을 열어두는 습관을 지닌 한낱 낯선 사람을 — 납득시킨다면, 수어드 박사가 자기 위험을 무릅쓰고 자기 책임 아래 그대가 구하는 특권을 주리라는 것을 내가 책임지겠소.\" 그는 슬프게 고개를 저었고, 얼굴에는 가슴을 에는 회한의 빛이 어렸다. 교수가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"자, 보시오, 곰곰이 생각해 보시오. 그대는 우리에게 자신이 완전히 분별 있음을 각인시키려 하니, 가장 드높은 정도의 이성이라는 특권을 주장하는 셈이오. 그대는 이러는데, 정작 그대의 제정신은 의심할 만한 이유가 있소, 그대가 바로 이 결함 때문에 아직 의료적 치료에서 풀려나지 못했으니 말이오. 만일 그대가 가장 현명한 길을 택하려는 우리의 노력을 돕지 않는다면, 그대 자신이 우리에게 지운 의무를 우리가 어찌 다하겠소? 현명해지시오, 그리고 우리를 도우시오. 그러면 우리도 할 수 있다면 그대가 바람을 이루도록 도우리다.\" 그는 여전히 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n\"판 헬싱 박사님, 저는 드릴 말씀이 없습니다. 박사님의 논리는 완벽하며, 만일 제가 말할 자유가 있다면 저는 한순간도 망설이지 않을 것입니다. 그러나 저는 이 문제에서 제 자신의 주인이 아닙니다. 저는 그저 저를 믿어달라고 청할 수 있을 뿐입니다. 만일 거절당한다면, 그 책임은 저에게 있지 않습니다.\" 나는 이제 이 장면을 끝낼 때라고 생각했다. 너무도 우스꽝스러울 만큼 심각해지고 있었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 그저 이렇게 말하며 문 쪽으로 갔다.\n\n\"자, 벗들이여, 우리에게는 할 일이 있소. 잘 자시오.\"\n\n그러나 내가 문 가까이 다가가자, 환자에게 새로운 변화가 일어났다. 그가 어찌나 빠르게 나를 향해 다가오는지, 순간 나는 그가 또 살의를 품은 공격을 하려는 것이 아닐까 두려웠다. 그러나 내 두려움은 근거 없는 것이었으니, 그가 애원하듯 두 손을 들어 올리고는 사람의 마음을 움직이는 태도로 자신의 간청을 했기 때문이다. 그는 자신의 바로 그 지나친 격정이 오히려 우리를 예전의 관계로 되돌려 자신에게 불리하게 작용하고 있음을 알아채자, 한층 더 격앙된 모습을 보였다. 나는 판 헬싱을 흘끗 바라보았고, 내 확신이 그의 눈에도 비치는 것을 보았다. 그래서 나는 더 엄격해지지는 않더라도 태도를 조금 더 단단히 굳히고, 그의 노력이 소용없음을 그에게 몸짓으로 알렸다. 나는 전에도 그에게서 이와 같이 점점 커지는 흥분을 본 적이 있었는데, 그가 그 무렵 골똘히 생각하던 어떤 청을 — 이를테면 고양이를 갖고 싶어 했을 때처럼 — 해야 할 때였다. 그래서 나는 이번에도 그가 똑같이 시무룩한 체념으로 무너져 내리는 것을 보게 되리라 마음의 준비를 하고 있었다. 그러나 내 예상은 빗나갔으니, 자신의 호소가 성공하지 못하리라는 것을 알아채자 그는 그야말로 광란의 상태에 빠졌기 때문이다. 그는 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 들어 올려, 애처로운 탄원으로 손을 비비 꼬며, 뺨으로 눈물을 줄줄 흘리고 온 얼굴과 온몸으로 더없이 깊은 격정을 드러낸 채, 간청의 격류를 쏟아냈다.\n\n\"수어드 박사님, 부디 간청드립니다, 아, 부디 애원합니다, 당장 저를 이 집에서 내보내 주십시오. 어떻게든, 어디로든 저를 보내 주십시오. 채찍과 사슬을 든 간수들을 딸려 보내십시오. 그들이 저를 구속복에 묶어, 수갑과 족쇄를 채워 감옥으로 끌고 가도 좋습니다. 하지만 이곳에서만은 나가게 해주십시오. 저를 여기 붙들어 둠으로써 무슨 일을 저지르시는지 박사님은 모르십니다. 저는 제 마음 깊은 곳에서 — 제 영혼 깊은 곳에서 말씀드리는 것입니다. 박사님은 누구를, 또 어떻게 해치시는지 모르십니다. 그리고 저는 말할 수 없습니다. 아, 가엾은 저여! 저는 말할 수 없습니다. 당신이 신성하게 여기는 모든 것을 두고 — 당신이 소중히 여기는 모든 것을 두고 — 잃어버린 당신의 사랑을 두고 — 살아 있는 당신의 희망을 두고 — 전능하신 분을 위해서라도, 저를 이곳에서 내보내 제 영혼을 죄로부터 구해 주십시오! 제 말이 들리지 않으십니까? 이해하지 못하시겠습니까? 끝내 깨닫지 못하시겠습니까? 제가 지금 제정신이고 진지하다는 것을, 제가 발작에 빠진 광인이 아니라 제 영혼을 위해 싸우는 제정신인 사람임을 모르시겠습니까? 아, 들어주십시오! 들어주십시오! 저를 보내 주십시오! 저를 보내 주십시오! 저를 보내 주십시오!\"\n\n나는 이것이 길어질수록 그가 더 거칠어져 발작을 일으키리라고 생각했다. 그래서 그의 손을 잡고 그를 일으켜 세웠다.\n\n\"자,\" 나는 엄하게 말했다. \"이제 그만하시오. 이미 충분히 했소. 침대로 가서 좀 더 신중하게 처신하도록 애써 보시오.\"\n\n그는 갑자기 멈추고 여러 순간 동안 나를 뚫어지게 바라보았다. 그러더니 한마디 말도 없이 일어나, 자리를 옮겨 침대 가장자리에 앉았다. 무너짐이 찾아온 것이다, 예전처럼, 바로 내가 예상했던 대로.\n\n우리 일행 가운데 마지막으로 내가 방을 나설 때, 그가 조용하고 교양 있는 목소리로 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"수어드 박사님, 부디 나중에라도 저에게 공정을 베푸시어, 제가 오늘 밤 당신을 납득시키려 할 수 있는 일을 다 했다는 것을 마음에 새겨 주시기 바랍니다.\"\n\n# JONATHAN HARKER’S JOURNAL\n\n「조너선 하커의 일기」\n10월 1일, 새벽 5시. — 나는 가벼운 마음으로 일행과 함께 수색에 나섰다. 미나가 그토록 완전히 굳세고 건강해 보인 적은 일찍이 없었던 것 같기 때문이다. 그녀가 한발 물러나 우리 남자들에게 이 일을 맡기기로 동의해주어 나는 무척 기쁘다. 어쩐지 나로서는 그녀가 이 끔찍한 일에 발을 들였다는 것 자체가 두려웠다. 그러나 이제 그녀의 몫은 끝났고, 모든 이야기가 한 점도 빠짐없이 들어맞도록 짜맞추어진 것이 그녀의 정력과 두뇌와 선견지명 덕분이니, 그녀로서는 제 역할이 마무리되었으며, 이제부터 나머지는 우리에게 맡겨도 좋다고 여겨 마땅하다. 우리는 렌필드 씨와의 그 장면으로 모두 조금씩 마음이 어지러웠던 것 같다. 그의 방을 나설 때 우리는 서재로 돌아올 때까지 말이 없었다. 그러다 모리스 씨가 수어드 박사에게 말했다.\n\n\"이봐, 잭, 저 사람이 허세를 부린 게 아니라면, 내가 본 미치광이 중에 가장 제정신에 가까운 자일세. 확신할 수는 없지만, 내 생각에 그에게는 어떤 진지한 목적이 있었던 것 같고, 만일 그랬다면 기회를 얻지 못한 건 그에게 꽤 가혹한 일이었네.\" 고달밍 경과 나는 잠자코 있었으나, 반 헬싱 박사가 말을 보탰다.\n\n\"존, 자네는 나보다 미치광이를 잘 알고, 나는 그게 다행스럽네. 만일 결정이 내 몫이었다면 그 마지막 발작적인 분출이 있기 전에 나는 그를 풀어주었을 테니 두렵네. 그러나 우리는 살아가며 배우는 법이고, 지금 우리가 맡은 일에서는, 내 친구 퀸시라면 말하듯, 어떤 모험도 해서는 안 되네. 모든 것이 지금 이대로가 최선이야.\" 수어드 박사는 마치 꿈을 꾸는 듯한 투로 두 사람 모두에게 답하는 것 같았다.\n\n\"동의하지 않는다고는 못하겠습니다. 저 사람이 평범한 미치광이였다면 나는 그를 믿어보는 모험을 감행했을 겁니다. 그러나 그는 어쩐지 색인처럼 백작과 얽혀 있는 것 같아서, 그의 별난 짓을 도왔다가 무언가 잘못을 저지르게 될까 두렵습니다. 그가 거의 똑같이 열렬하게 고양이를 달라고 기도하다가, 곧이어 이빨로 내 목을 물어뜯으려 한 것을 잊을 수가 없어요. 게다가 그는 백작을 '주인이자 지배자'라 불렀으니, 어떤 악마적인 방식으로 백작을 도우려 밖으로 나가고 싶어 하는 건지도 모릅니다. 그 끔찍한 것에게는 늑대와 쥐들, 그리고 제 동족이 그를 도우니, 제 짐작에 그는 점잖은 미치광이 하나쯤 이용하기를 마다하지 않을 겁니다. 그래도 그가 진지해 보였던 건 분명합니다. 그저 우리가 최선의 일을 했기를 바랄 뿐입니다. 이런 일들은, 우리가 손에 쥔 이 거친 일과 더불어 사람의 기력을 빼놓는군요.\" 교수는 다가가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고는 진중하고도 자상한 투로 말했다.\n\n\"존, 두려워 말게. 우리는 매우 슬프고 끔찍한 사건에서 우리의 의무를 다하려 애쓰고 있을 뿐이며, 우리가 최선이라 여기는 대로 할 수 있을 뿐이네. 선하신 하느님의 자비 말고 우리가 또 무엇을 바랄 수 있겠는가?\" 고달밍 경은 잠시 자리를 비웠다가 이제 돌아왔다. 그는 작은 은제 호각을 들어 보이며 말했다.\n\n\"저 낡은 곳은 쥐가 들끓을지도 모르네. 만일 그렇다면, 나는 부르기만 하면 되는 해독제를 마련해두었지.\" 담을 넘은 뒤 우리는 그 집으로 향했는데, 달빛이 환히 비칠 때면 잔디밭 나무 그늘에 몸을 숨기도록 조심했다. 현관에 이르자 교수는 가방을 열어 여러 가지 물건을 꺼내 계단 위에 내려놓고, 그것들을 네 무더기로 나누었는데, 분명 각자에게 하나씩 돌아가도록 한 것이었다. 그러고 나서 그가 말했다.\n\n\"여러분, 우리는 무시무시한 위험 속으로 들어가고 있으며, 여러 가지 무기가 필요합니다. 우리의 적은 단지 영적인 존재만이 아닙니다. 그가 스무 사람의 힘을 지녔다는 것, 그리고 우리의 목이나 기도는 평범한 종류여서 — 따라서 부러지거나 으스러질 수 있으나 — 그의 것은 한낱 힘에는 굴복하지 않는다는 것을 기억하십시오. 더 힘센 사람, 혹은 모든 면에서 그보다 더 강한 무리가 어떤 때에는 그를 붙들 수는 있으나, 우리가 그에게 상처 입을 수 있는 만큼 그를 다치게 할 수는 없습니다. 그러니 우리는 그의 손길로부터 우리 자신을 지켜야 합니다. 이것을 가슴 가까이 지니십시오.\" — 그는 이렇게 말하며 작은 은제 십자고상을 들어, 그에게 가장 가까이 있던 나에게 내밀었다 — \"이 꽃들을 목에 두르십시오.\" — 여기서 그는 시든 마늘꽃을 엮은 화환을 나에게 건넸다 — \"좀 더 세속적인 다른 적들을 위해서는 이 권총과 이 칼을. 그리고 모든 일에 도움이 되도록, 가슴에 매달 수 있는 이 아주 작은 전기등을. 그리고 무엇보다도 마지막에 쓸 이것을, 우리는 함부로 더럽혀서는 안 됩니다.\" 그것은 성체(聖體)의 일부였는데, 그는 그것을 봉투에 넣어 나에게 건넸다. 나머지 사람들에게도 똑같이 채비를 갖추어주었다. \"자,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"존, 만능 열쇠는 어디 있나? 그것으로 문을 열 수 있다면, 전에 루시 양 때처럼 창문으로 집을 부수고 들어갈 필요는 없겠지.\"\n\n수어드 박사는 만능 열쇠를 한두 개 시험해보았는데, 외과의로서의 그 기계적인 손재주가 큰 도움이 되었다. 곧 그는 맞는 것을 하나 찾아냈고, 앞뒤로 조금 놀려보자 빗장이 풀리며 녹슨 쇳소리와 함께 뒤로 젖혀졌다. 우리가 문을 밀자 녹슨 경첩이 삐걱거리며 문이 천천히 열렸다. 그것은 수어드 박사의 일기에서 웨스텐라 양의 무덤이 열리던 장면이 나에게 전해주었던 인상과 놀랍도록 닮아 있었다. 다른 사람들에게도 똑같은 생각이 떠오른 듯, 약속이라도 한 듯 모두가 움찔 물러섰다. 교수가 가장 먼저 앞으로 나아가, 열린 문 안으로 발을 들였다.\n\n\"인 마누스 투아스, 도미네!\" 하고 그는 문지방을 넘으며 성호를 그었다. 우리는 등불을 켰을 때 행여 길에서 누군가의 주의를 끌까 봐, 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다. 교수는 빠져나가야 할 때 서두르게 되면 행여 안에서 문을 열지 못할까 봐, 자물쇠를 조심스레 살펴보았다. 그러고 나서 우리는 모두 등불을 켜고 수색에 나섰다.\n* 인 마누스 투아스, 도미네: 라틴어로 \"당신의 손에 맡기나이다, 주여.\"\n\n자그마한 등불에서 나온 빛이 서로 교차하거나, 우리 몸의 그늘이 커다란 그림자를 드리우면서 온갖 기묘한 형상으로 떨어졌다. 나는 우리 가운데 누군가 다른 이가 있다는 느낌을 도무지 떨쳐낼 수 없었다. 이 음산한 주변 환경 탓에 그토록 강렬하게 되살아난, 트란실바니아에서의 그 끔찍한 경험에 대한 기억 때문이었으리라. 이 느낌은 우리 모두에게 공통된 것이었던 듯한데, 다른 이들도 소리가 날 때마다, 새 그림자가 생길 때마다 어깨 너머로 돌아보는 것을, 꼭 내가 그러고 있다고 느꼈던 것과 똑같이 하는 것을 보았기 때문이다.\n\n집 안 전체가 먼지로 자욱했다. 바닥은 보기에 몇 인치는 쌓인 듯했는데, 최근 발자국이 난 곳만은 예외여서, 등불을 그쪽으로 낮추어보니 먼지가 갈라진 자리에 징 박은 구두 자국이 보였다. 벽은 먼지로 보풀이 일고 무거웠으며, 구석에는 거미줄이 뭉텅뭉텅 엉겨 있었는데, 그 위에 먼지가 쌓여 무게에 못 이겨 일부가 찢겨 내려, 마치 낡고 너덜너덜한 누더기처럼 보였다. 현관의 탁자 위에는 커다란 열쇠 꾸러미가 있었고, 저마다 세월에 누렇게 바랜 꼬리표가 달려 있었다. 그것들은 여러 차례 쓰인 적이 있었으니, 탁자 위에는 교수가 그 꾸러미를 들어 올렸을 때 드러난 것과 비슷한, 먼지 담요에 난 비슷한 자국이 여럿 있었기 때문이다. 그가 나를 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"자네는 이곳을 알고 있네, 조너선. 자네는 이곳의 지도를 베껴 그렸으니, 적어도 우리보다는 더 잘 알지. 예배당으로 가는 길은 어느 쪽인가?\" 나는 지난번 방문 때 그곳에 들어가 보지는 못했으나, 그 방향에 대해서는 짐작이 있었다. 그래서 앞장을 섰고, 몇 번 길을 잘못 든 끝에 쇠띠로 두른, 낮고 아치형인 떡갈나무 문 앞에 서게 되었다. \"여기가 그곳이군,\" 하고 교수는 매입에 관한 내 원래의 서신 묶음에서 베껴낸 그 집의 작은 지도에 등불을 비추며 말했다. 우리는 조금 애를 먹은 끝에 꾸러미에서 그 열쇠를 찾아 문을 열었다. 우리는 어느 정도의 불쾌함은 각오하고 있었으니, 문을 여는 동안 희미하고 고약한 냄새가 풍기는 공기가 틈으로 새어 나오는 듯했기 때문이다. 그러나 우리 가운데 누구도 우리가 맞닥뜨린 그런 악취는 예상하지 못했다. 다른 이들은 백작을 가까이서 마주한 적이 전혀 없었고, 내가 그를 보았을 때는 그가 제 방에서 단식 상태에 있거나, 혹은 신선한 피로 배를 채운 채 한데에 트인 폐허 건물에 있을 때였다. 그러나 이곳은 작고 답답한 데다, 오랜 폐쇄로 공기가 고이고 더러워져 있었다. 더 고약한 공기 사이로, 어떤 마른 독기(毒氣)처럼 흙냄새가 풍겨 왔다. 그러나 그 악취 자체를 내가 어떻게 묘사할 수 있을까? 그것은 죽음이 빚어내는 온갖 병폐와 피의 톡 쏘는 매캐한 냄새로 이루어졌을 뿐 아니라, 마치 부패 그 자체가 부패해버린 것 같았다. 윽! 그것을 생각만 해도 속이 메스껍다. 그 괴물이 내쉰 숨결 하나하나가 이곳에 들러붙어 그 역겨움을 한층 짙게 한 것만 같았다.\n\n여느 상황이었다면 그런 악취만으로도 우리의 일은 거기서 끝장났을 것이다. 그러나 이것은 여느 경우가 아니었고, 우리가 떠맡은 그 높고도 무시무시한 목적이 우리에게 한낱 육체적인 고려를 뛰어넘는 힘을 주었다. 처음 메스꺼운 한 모금에 절로 움츠러든 뒤, 우리는 너 나 할 것 없이 그 역겨운 곳이 마치 장미 정원이라도 되는 양 일에 착수했다.\n\n우리는 그곳을 정밀하게 조사했는데, 시작할 때 교수가 말했다.\n\n\"가장 먼저 할 일은 상자가 몇 개나 남았는지 보는 것입니다. 그런 다음 모든 구멍과 구석과 틈을 살펴, 나머지가 어디로 갔는지 단서를 얻을 수 있는지 봅시다.\" 몇 개가 남았는지는 한눈에 알 수 있었으니, 그 커다란 흙 궤짝들은 부피가 컸고, 헷갈릴 수가 없었기 때문이다.\n\n쉰 개 가운데 겨우 스물아홉 개만 남아 있었다! 한번은 나는 흠칫 놀랐다. 고달밍 경이 갑자기 돌아서서 아치형 문 너머 어두운 통로를 내다보는 것을 보고, 나도 내다보았는데, 한순간 심장이 멎는 듯했기 때문이다. 그늘 속 어딘가에서 내다보고 있는, 백작의 사악한 얼굴의 도드라진 윤곽 — 콧날, 붉은 눈, 붉은 입술, 소름 끼치는 창백함 — 이 보이는 것 같았다. 그것은 한순간뿐이었으니, 고달밍 경이 \"얼굴을 본 것 같았는데, 그저 그림자였군\" 하고는 다시 조사를 이어갔고, 나는 등불을 그쪽으로 돌려 통로 안으로 발을 들였다. 누군가의 흔적은 없었다. 그리고 모퉁이도, 문도, 어떤 종류의 틈도 없이 오로지 통로의 단단한 벽뿐이었으니, 그자에게조차 숨을 곳은 있을 수 없었다. 나는 두려움이 상상을 부추긴 것이라 여기고 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n몇 분 뒤 나는 모리스가 살펴보던 한 구석에서 갑자기 물러서는 것을 보았다. 우리는 모두 그의 움직임을 눈으로 좇았는데, 분명 우리에게 어떤 긴장감이 점점 차오르고 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 우리는 별처럼 반짝이는 인광(燐光)의 거대한 덩어리를 보았다. 우리는 모두 본능적으로 뒤로 물러섰다. 그곳 전체가 쥐들로 들끓기 시작하고 있었다.\n\n한두 순간 우리는 질겁한 채 서 있었으나, 고달밍 경만은 예외였으니, 그는 그런 비상사태에 대비해둔 듯했다. 그는 수어드 박사가 바깥에서 묘사했고 나 또한 직접 보았던, 그 쇠띠로 두른 커다란 떡갈나무 문으로 달려가, 자물쇠에 열쇠를 돌리고 거대한 빗장들을 빼낸 뒤 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다. 그러고는 주머니에서 그 작은 은제 호각을 꺼내, 낮고 날카로운 소리를 불었다. 수어드 박사의 집 뒤편에서 개들이 컹컹 짖는 소리로 응답이 왔고, 약 1분쯤 뒤 테리어 세 마리가 집 모퉁이를 돌아 쏜살같이 달려왔다. 우리도 모르는 사이에 우리는 모두 문 쪽으로 움직여 있었는데, 그렇게 움직이면서 나는 먼지가 몹시 어지럽혀져 있는 것을 알아차렸다. 실려 나간 상자들이 이 길로 옮겨졌던 것이다. 그러나 그 지나간 짧은 사이에도 쥐의 수는 엄청나게 불어나 있었다. 그것들은 한꺼번에 그곳을 온통 뒤덮으며 우글거리는 듯했으니, 등불빛이 그것들의 움직이는 검은 몸뚱이와 번뜩이는 사악한 눈에 비치자, 그곳은 마치 반딧불이가 점점이 박힌 흙더미처럼 보였다. 개들은 쏜살같이 달려들었으나, 문지방에 이르자 갑자기 멈춰 으르렁거리더니, 일제히 코를 쳐들고는 더없이 구슬픈 모양새로 울부짖기 시작했다. 쥐들은 수천 마리로 불어나고 있었고, 우리는 밖으로 나왔다.\n\n고달밍 경은 개 한 마리를 들어 안으로 데리고 들어가 바닥에 내려놓았다. 발이 땅에 닿는 순간 그놈은 용기를 되찾은 듯, 제 타고난 적들에게 달려들었다. 쥐들이 어찌나 빨리 그놈 앞에서 달아났는지, 그놈이 스무 마리쯤의 숨통을 끊어놓기도 전에, 이제 같은 식으로 들여보내진 다른 개들은 그 무리 전체가 사라지기 전에 사냥감을 거의 잡지 못했다.\n\n그것들이 사라지자 마치 어떤 사악한 존재가 떠나간 것만 같았다. 개들이 폴짝거리며 즐겁게 짖어대고, 쓰러진 적들에게 느닷없이 달려들어 이리저리 뒤집고 사납게 흔들며 공중으로 내던졌기 때문이다. 우리는 모두 기운이 솟는 것 같았다. 예배당 문을 열어 그 치명적인 공기가 정화된 덕분인지, 아니면 한데로 나와 안도를 느낀 덕분인지는 모르겠으나, 분명 두려움의 그림자가 마치 한 벌의 겉옷처럼 우리에게서 미끄러져 내렸고, 우리가 찾아온 그 일도 음산한 의미를 얼마간 잃었다. 그렇다고 우리의 결의가 조금이라도 느슨해진 것은 아니었다. 우리는 바깥문을 닫고 빗장을 지르고 자물쇠를 채운 뒤, 개들을 데리고 집 안 수색을 시작했다. 처음 방문 때 내가 남긴 발자국을 빼고는 손대지 않은 채로, 우리는 곳곳에서 엄청나게 쌓인 먼지 말고는 아무것도 찾지 못했다. 개들은 단 한 번도 불안한 기색을 보이지 않았고, 우리가 예배당으로 돌아왔을 때조차 마치 여름 숲에서 토끼 사냥이라도 한 양 폴짝거렸다.\n\n우리가 정면으로 빠져나왔을 때 동녘에서 아침이 빠르게 밝아오고 있었다. 반 헬싱 박사는 꾸러미에서 현관문 열쇠를 빼내, 정석대로 문을 잠그고는 그것을 끝내자 열쇠를 주머니에 넣었다.\n\n\"여기까지,\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"우리의 밤은 더없이 성공적이었습니다. 내가 두려워했을 법한 어떤 해도 우리에게 닥치지 않았고, 게다가 우리는 상자가 몇 개나 사라졌는지 알아냈습니다. 무엇보다도 나는, 우리의 이 첫걸음 — 어쩌면 가장 어렵고 위험한 걸음 — 이, 우리의 더없이 다정한 미나 부인을 끌어들이지 않고, 또 그녀가 결코 잊지 못할 공포의 광경과 소리와 냄새로 그녀의 깨어 있을 때나 잠든 때의 생각을 어지럽히지도 않고 이루어진 것이 기쁩니다. 또한 우리는 한 가지 교훈도 배웠으니, 특수한 것에서 추론하는 것이 허용된다면 이렇습니다. 백작의 명령을 따르는 그 짐승들조차 그의 영적인 힘에는 굴복하지 않는다는 것입니다. 보십시오, 그의 부름에 모여드는 이 쥐들은, 그가 성탑 꼭대기에서 당신을 향해 늑대들을 불러내고 또 저 가엾은 어미의 울부짖음을 부른 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 그에게 모여들면서도, 내 친구 아서의 그토록 작은 개들 앞에서는 죽기 살기로 달아납니다. 우리 앞에는 다른 일들, 다른 위험들, 다른 두려움들이 있습니다. 그리고 저 괴물 — 그는 오늘 밤이 짐승의 세계에 대한 자신의 힘을 쓰는 유일한, 혹은 마지막 때로 삼지는 않을 것입니다. 그가 다른 데로 갔다면, 좋습니다! 그것은 우리에게, 인간의 영혼을 걸고 두는 이 체스 경기에서 여러모로 '장군'을 부를 기회를 준 것이니까요. 자, 이제 집으로 갑시다. 새벽이 코앞이고, 우리는 첫날 밤의 일에 만족할 만한 이유가 있습니다. 위험으로 가득할지언정, 앞으로 숱한 밤과 낮을 더 보내야 하도록 정해져 있을지도 모릅니다. 그러나 우리는 나아가야 하며, 어떤 위험 앞에서도 우리는 움츠러들지 않을 것입니다.\"\n\n우리가 돌아왔을 때 그 집은 고요했다. 다만 멀리 떨어진 병동 하나에서 어떤 가엾은 자가 비명을 질러대고 있었고, 렌필드의 방에서 낮게 신음하는 소리가 들려올 뿐이었다. 그 가엾은 자는 분명, 미치광이들이 으레 그러하듯 부질없는 고통의 상념으로 스스로를 괴롭히고 있었으리라.\n\n나는 발끝으로 우리 방에 들어가, 미나가 잠들어 있는 것을 보았는데, 어찌나 부드럽게 숨을 쉬는지 귀를 가까이 대야 들릴 정도였다. 그녀는 평소보다 창백해 보인다. 오늘 밤의 모임이 그녀를 어지럽히지 않았기를 바란다. 나는 그녀가 우리의 앞으로의 일에서, 심지어 우리의 의논에서까지 빠지게 된 것이 참으로 고맙다. 그것은 여자가 견디기에는 너무도 큰 부담이다. 처음에는 그렇게 생각하지 않았으나, 이제는 더 잘 알게 되었다. 그러니 그것이 매듭지어진 것이 기쁘다. 그녀가 들으면 겁에 질릴 만한 일들이 있을지도 모른다. 그렇다고 그것을 그녀에게 숨기는 것은, 일단 그녀가 무언가 감추는 것이 있다고 의심하게 된다면, 차라리 말해주는 것보다 더 나쁠지도 모른다. 이제부터 우리의 일은 그녀에게 봉인된 책이 될 것이다. 적어도 모든 것이 끝났고 이 땅이 저승의 괴물에게서 풀려났다고 우리가 그녀에게 말해줄 수 있는 그때까지는. 우리처럼 서로 그토록 흉금을 터놓던 사이에 침묵을 지키기 시작하기란 어려우리라. 그러나 나는 단호해야 한다. 내일 나는 오늘 밤의 일에 대해 입을 다물 것이며, 무슨 일이 있었든 그것을 말하기를 거부할 것이다. 나는 그녀를 깨우지 않으려고 소파에 누워 쉰다.\n\n10월 1일, 나중에. — 우리가 모두 늦잠을 잔 것은 당연한 일이었으리라. 그날은 분주했고, 밤에는 도무지 쉬지 못했으니 말이다. 미나조차 그 피로를 느꼈음에 틀림없으니, 나는 해가 높이 뜰 때까지 잤음에도 그녀보다 먼저 깨어, 그녀가 깨어나기 전에 두세 번이나 불러야 했기 때문이다. 실로 그녀가 어찌나 깊이 잠들었던지, 잠깐 동안은 나를 알아보지 못하고, 마치 악몽에서 깨어난 이가 그러하듯 일종의 멍한 공포의 눈빛으로 나를 바라보았다. 그녀는 피곤하다고 조금 투정을 부렸고, 나는 그녀를 한낮까지 쉬게 두었다. 우리는 이제 스물한 개의 상자가 옮겨졌음을 알고 있으며, 만일 이 운반들 가운데 어느 한 번에 여러 개가 함께 실려 갔다면 우리는 그것들을 모두 추적할 수 있을지도 모른다. 그렇게 되면 물론 우리의 수고가 엄청나게 덜어질 것이고, 이 일은 빨리 처리할수록 좋다. 나는 오늘 토머스 스넬링을 찾아볼 것이다.\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n10월 1일. — 교수가 내 방으로 걸어 들어와 나를 깨웠을 때는 정오 무렵이었다. 그는 평소보다 더 유쾌하고 밝았으니, 지난밤의 일이 그의 마음에서 골똘히 짓누르던 무게를 얼마간 덜어준 것이 분명했다. 그는 지난밤의 모험을 죽 되짚어본 뒤 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"자네 환자가 나에게 무척 흥미롭네. 오늘 아침에 자네와 함께 그를 찾아가도 되겠나? 아니면 자네가 너무 바쁘다면, 괜찮다면 나 혼자라도 가겠네. 철학을 논하고 그토록 조리 있게 따지는 미치광이를 만나는 것은 나에게 새로운 경험일세.\" 나는 급히 처리해야 할 일이 좀 있었으므로, 그가 혼자 가준다면 기쁘겠다고 말했다. 그래야 그를 기다리게 하지 않아도 될 테니 말이다. 그래서 나는 간병인을 불러 필요한 지시를 내렸다. 교수가 방을 나서기 전에 나는 내 환자에게서 행여 잘못된 인상을 받지 않도록 그에게 주의를 주었다. \"하지만,\" 하고 그가 답했다. \"나는 그가 자기 자신에 대해, 그리고 살아 있는 것을 먹어치우는 그의 망상에 대해 이야기하기를 바라네. 어제 자네 일기에서 보니, 그가 미나 부인에게 한때 그런 믿음을 가졌었다고 말했더군. 왜 웃나, 존?\"\n\n\"용서하게,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"그러나 그 답은 여기 있네.\" 나는 그 타자로 친 글뭉치 위에 손을 얹었다. \"우리의 제정신이고 박식한 미치광이가 자기가 한때 생명을 어떻게 먹어치웠는지 바로 그렇게 진술했을 때, 그의 입은 사실 하커 부인이 방에 들어오기 직전에 그가 먹은 파리와 거미로 메스꺼운 상태였다네.\" 이번에는 반 헬싱이 웃었다. \"좋아!\" 하고 그가 말했다. \"자네 기억이 정확하군, 존. 내가 기억했어야 했는데. 그러나 정신병을 그토록 매혹적인 연구 대상으로 만드는 것은 바로 이 사고와 기억의 비뚤어짐일세. 어쩌면 나는 가장 지혜로운 자의 가르침에서보다 이 미치광이의 어리석음에서 더 많은 지식을 얻을지도 모르네. 누가 알겠나?\" 나는 일을 계속했고, 머지않아 손에 쥔 것을 끝마쳤다. 시간이 참으로 짧게 지난 듯했으나, 반 헬싱이 서재로 돌아와 있었다. \"내가 방해가 되나?\" 하고 그가 문가에 서서 정중하게 물었다.\n\n\"전혀,\" 하고 내가 답했다. \"들어오게. 내 일은 끝났고, 나는 한가해. 원한다면 지금 자네와 함께 갈 수 있네.\"\n\n\"그럴 필요 없네. 나는 그를 보았으니!\"\n\n\"그래서?\"\n\n\"내 생각에 그는 나를 대수롭지 않게 여기는 것 같네. 우리의 면담은 짧았어. 내가 그의 방에 들어갔을 때 그는 한가운데 등받이 없는 의자에 앉아 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고 있었는데, 그 얼굴은 시무룩한 불만의 표본이었네. 나는 되도록 명랑하게, 또 내가 짐짓 꾸밀 수 있는 만큼의 경의를 담아 그에게 말을 걸었네. 그는 아무런 대답도 하지 않았어. '날 모르겠소?' 하고 내가 물었네. 그의 대답은 안심이 되는 것이 아니었지. '당신을 잘 알다마다. 당신은 그 늙은 바보 반 헬싱이지. 당신 자신과 당신의 그 멍청한 두뇌 이론들을 어디 다른 데로 가져가버렸으면 좋겠군. 머리 둔한 네덜란드인들은 다 빌어먹을!' 그는 더는 한마디도 하지 않고, 마치 내가 방 안에 있지도 않은 양 나에게 무심한 채, 그 누그러질 줄 모르는 시무룩함 속에 앉아 있었네. 이렇게 이번에는 이 영리한 미치광이에게서 많은 것을 배울 내 기회가 사라졌네. 그러니 괜찮다면 나는 가서, 저 사랑스러운 영혼 미나 부인과 몇 마디 즐거운 말이나 나누며 기운을 차리겠네. 존, 그녀가 더는 아파하지 않아도 되고, 우리의 그 끔찍한 일들로 더는 시달리지 않아도 된다는 것이 나에게는 이루 말할 수 없이 기쁘다네. 우리가 그녀의 도움을 무척 그리워하게 되겠지만, 이렇게 하는 편이 낫네.\"\n\n\"진심으로 자네 말에 동의하네,\" 하고 나는 그가 이 문제에서 마음이 약해지기를 바라지 않았으므로 진지하게 답했다. \"하커 부인은 이 일에서 빠져 있는 편이 낫네. 우리에게는, 모두 세상 물정에 밝고 한평생 숱한 위급한 상황을 겪어온 사내들에게조차 사정이 꽤나 고약하지. 그러나 이곳은 여자가 있을 자리가 아닐세. 만일 그녀가 이 일과 계속 맞닿아 있었다면, 머지않아 어김없이 그녀는 망가졌을 걸세.\"\n\n그리하여 반 헬싱은 하커 부인 및 하커와 의논하러 갔고, 퀸시와 아트는 흙 상자에 관한 단서를 추적하러 모두 나가 있다. 나는 내 일을 한 바퀴 마저 돌고, 우리는 오늘 밤에 만날 것이다.\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」\n\n10월 1일. — 오늘처럼 내가 깜깜한 채로 따돌림당하는 것은 나에게 낯선 일이다. 조너선이 그토록 여러 해 동안 나에게 모든 것을 터놓아온 끝에, 그가 어떤 일들을, 그것도 가장 중요한 일들을 빤히 피하는 것을 보다니. 오늘 아침 나는 어제의 피로 끝에 늦잠을 잤고, 조너선도 늦었으나 그가 더 일찍 일어났다. 그는 나가기 전에 나에게 말을 걸었는데, 어느 때보다 다정하고 따뜻했으나, 백작의 집을 찾아갔을 때 무슨 일이 있었는지는 한마디도 꺼내지 않았다. 그런데도 그는 내가 얼마나 끔찍이 마음을 졸이고 있는지 분명 알았으리라. 가엾은 사람! 짐작건대 그것은 나보다 그를 더 괴롭혔을 것이다. 그들은 모두 내가 이 끔찍한 일에 더 끌려 들어가지 않는 편이 최선이라는 데 뜻을 모았고, 나도 묵묵히 따랐다. 그러나 그가 나에게 무언가를 숨긴다고 생각하다니! 그리고 이제 나는 바보처럼 울고 있다. 그것이 내 남편의 크나큰 사랑에서, 그리고 저 다른 굳센 사내들의 선하고도 선한 바람에서 나온 것임을 알면서도 말이다.\n\n그렇게 우니 마음이 좀 나아졌다. 자, 언젠가 조너선이 나에게 모든 것을 말해줄 것이다. 그리고 행여 그가 한순간이라도 내가 그에게 무언가를 숨긴다고 여기는 일이 없도록, 나는 여전히 평소처럼 일기를 쓴다. 그러면 만일 그가 내 신뢰를 의심하게 되거든, 나는 내 마음속의 온갖 생각을 그의 사랑스러운 눈이 읽도록 적어 그에게 보여줄 것이다. 오늘 나는 묘하게 슬프고 풀이 죽었다. 짐작건대 그 끔찍한 흥분에 대한 반작용이리라.\n\n지난밤 사내들이 떠난 뒤 나는 잠자리에 들었는데, 그저 그들이 그러라고 했기 때문이다. 졸리지는 않았고, 오히려 사람을 집어삼킬 듯한 불안으로 가득했다. 나는 조너선이 런던으로 나를 보러 온 이래로 줄곧 있어온 모든 일을 거듭 곱씹었고, 그 모든 것이 마치 운명이 어떤 정해진 종말을 향해 가차 없이 밀어붙이는, 끔찍한 비극처럼만 여겨졌다. 사람이 하는 모든 일은, 그것이 아무리 옳을지언정, 가장 통탄할 바로 그 일을 불러오는 것만 같다. 만일 내가 휘트비에 가지 않았더라면, 어쩌면 가엾은 루시가 지금 우리 곁에 있을지도 모른다. 그녀는 내가 오기 전까지는 그 묘지를 드나들지 않았고, 만일 그녀가 낮에 나와 함께 거기에 가지 않았더라면 잠결에 거기로 걸어가지도 않았을 것이며, 만일 그녀가 밤에 잠든 채로 거기에 가지 않았더라면 그 괴물도 그녀를 그렇게 파멸시키지 못했을 것이다. 아, 나는 어쩌자고 휘트비에 갔던가? 자, 또 운다! 오늘 나에게 대체 무슨 일이 일어난 건지 모르겠다. 나는 이것을 조너선에게 숨겨야 한다. 만일 그가 내가 한 아침에 두 번이나 울었다는 것을 안다면 — 제 일로는 운 적이 없고, 그가 눈물 한 방울 흘리게 한 적도 없는 내가 말이다 — 그 사랑스러운 사람은 애를 태우다 못해 가슴이 다 닳을 것이다. 나는 대담한 얼굴을 꾸밀 것이며, 설령 울고 싶은 기분이 들더라도 그가 결코 그것을 보게 하지는 않을 것이다. 짐작건대 그것은 우리 가엾은 여자들이 배워야 하는 교훈 가운데 하나이리라…….\n\n나는 지난밤 어떻게 잠들었는지 잘 기억나지 않는다. 개들이 갑자기 짖던 소리와, 이 아래 어딘가에 있는 렌필드 씨의 방에서 들려온, 마치 몹시 소란스러운 규모로 기도라도 하는 듯한 온갖 기이한 소리들을 들은 것은 기억난다. 그러고 나서 모든 것 위에 침묵이 깔렸는데, 어찌나 깊은 침묵이었던지 나는 흠칫 놀라 일어나 창밖을 내다보았다. 모든 것이 어둡고 고요했으며, 달빛이 드리운 검은 그림자들은 저마다 어떤 말없는 신비로 가득 차 있는 듯했다. 어느 것 하나 움직이는 것 없이, 모두가 죽음이나 운명처럼 음산하게 굳어 있는 듯했다. 그래서 잔디밭을 가로질러 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 느리게 집 쪽으로 기어오는 한 가닥 가느다란 흰 안개가, 마치 제 나름의 감각과 생명력을 지닌 것처럼 보였다. 내 생각의 곁길이 나에게 도움이 되었던 모양이다. 잠자리로 돌아왔을 때 나는 어떤 나른함이 나에게 스며드는 것을 느꼈으니 말이다. 나는 한동안 누워 있었으나 도무지 잠들지 못해, 일어나 다시 창밖을 내다보았다. 안개는 번지고 있었고, 이제 집 가까이까지 다가와, 마치 창문으로 슬그머니 기어오르기라도 하듯 벽에 두껍게 들러붙어 있는 것이 보였다. 그 가엾은 사람은 어느 때보다 더 시끄러웠는데, 그가 하는 말을 한마디도 알아들을 수는 없었으나, 그의 어조에서 어쩐지 어떤 격정적인 애원을 알아챌 수 있었다. 그러고 나서 몸싸움하는 소리가 들렸으니, 나는 간병인들이 그를 다루고 있음을 알았다. 나는 어찌나 겁이 났던지 살그머니 잠자리로 기어들어, 손가락으로 귀를 막고 이불을 머리 위까지 끌어당겼다. 그때 나는 조금도 졸리지 않았다. 적어도 나는 그렇게 생각했다. 그러나 나는 잠들었음에 틀림없으니, 꿈을 빼고는 아침에 조너선이 나를 깨울 때까지 아무것도 기억나지 않기 때문이다. 내가 어디에 있는지, 그리고 내 위로 몸을 굽힌 이가 조너선이라는 것을 깨닫는 데 얼마간 애와 약간의 시간이 든 것 같다. 내 꿈은 매우 기이했으며, 깨어 있을 때의 생각이 꿈속으로 녹아들거나 이어지는 방식을 거의 그대로 보여주는 것이었다.\n\n나는 내가 잠들어 있으며, 조너선이 돌아오기를 기다리고 있다고 생각했다. 나는 그가 몹시 걱정스러웠으나 어찌해볼 도리가 없었다. 내 발과 손과 머리가 무거이 짓눌려 있어, 무엇 하나 평소의 속도로 움직이질 못했다. 그렇게 나는 불안하게 잠들어 생각에 잠겼다. 그러다 공기가 무겁고 축축하고 차갑다는 것이 차츰 느껴지기 시작했다. 나는 이불을 얼굴에서 젖혔고, 놀랍게도 주위가 온통 어슴푸레하다는 것을 알았다. 조너선을 위해 켜두었으나 심지를 낮춰둔 가스등이, 분명 더 짙어져 방 안으로 쏟아져 든 안개를 뚫고 그저 자그마한 붉은 불꽃처럼만 비쳐 왔다. 그러자 내가 잠자리에 들기 전에 창문을 닫았다는 것이 떠올랐다. 나는 그 점을 확인하러 일어나려 했으나, 어떤 납덩이 같은 나른함이 내 사지를, 심지어 내 의지까지 옭아매는 듯했다. 나는 가만히 누워 견딜 뿐이었다. 그것이 전부였다. 나는 눈을 감았으나, 그래도 감은 눈꺼풀 사이로 여전히 볼 수 있었다. (우리의 꿈이 우리에게 부리는 농간이란, 또 우리가 얼마나 편리하게 상상해낼 수 있는지란 놀라운 일이다.) 안개는 점점 더 짙어졌고, 나는 이제 그것이 어떻게 들어오는지 볼 수 있었다. 그것이 마치 연기처럼 — 혹은 끓는 물의 흰 기세로 — 창문이 아니라 문틈을 통해 쏟아져 드는 것이 보였기 때문이다. 그것은 점점 더 짙어지더니, 마침내 방 안에서 일종의 구름 기둥으로 응집되는 듯했고, 그 꼭대기를 통해 나는 가스등 빛이 붉은 눈처럼 빛나는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그 구름 기둥이 이제 방 안에서 소용돌이치듯, 온갖 것들이 내 머릿속에서 소용돌이치기 시작했고, 그 모든 것을 뚫고 \"낮에는 구름 기둥, 밤에는 불기둥\"이라는 성경 구절이 떠올랐다. 정말로 그런 어떤 영적인 인도가 잠결의 나에게 다가오고 있는 것이었을까? 그러나 그 기둥은 낮의 인도와 밤의 인도를 다 갖추고 있었으니, 불은 그 붉은 눈 속에 있었고, 그 생각에 그 눈은 나에게 새로운 매혹으로 다가왔다. 그러다, 내가 바라보고 있자니, 그 불이 둘로 갈라져, 마치 안개를 뚫고 두 개의 붉은 눈처럼 나를 비추는 것 같았다. 바로 루시가, 절벽 위에서 스러져가는 햇빛이 성 마리아 교회의 창문을 비추던 그때, 잠깐 정신이 흐트러진 사이에 나에게 말해준 그런 눈이었다. 그 끔찍한 일들이 마치 조너선이 본 그 무시무시한 여인들이 달빛 속에서 소용돌이치는 안개를 뚫고 실체로 변해가던 것과 똑같았다는 공포가 갑자기 나를 덮쳤고, 꿈속에서 나는 정신을 잃었음에 틀림없으니, 모든 것이 새카만 어둠이 되어버렸기 때문이다. 상상이 부린 마지막 의식적인 안간힘은, 안개 밖에서 나를 향해 굽어보는 검푸르게 핏기 없는 흰 얼굴 하나를 나에게 보여준 것이었다. 나는 그런 꿈들을 조심해야 한다. 그것들이 너무 많아지면 사람의 이성을 흔들어놓을 테니 말이다. 나는 반 헬싱 박사나 수어드 박사에게 잠들게 해줄 무언가를 처방받았으면 싶지만, 그들을 놀라게 할까 두렵다. 지금 같은 때에 그런 꿈은 나를 향한 그들의 두려움 속에 짜여 들고 말 것이다. 오늘 밤 나는 자연스럽게 잠들려고 무던히 애써볼 것이다. 만일 그러지 못한다면, 내일 밤에는 그들에게 클로랄을 한 번 복용하게 해달라고 청할 것이다. 한 번쯤이야 나를 해치지 않을 것이고, 푹 잘 수 있게 해줄 것이다. 지난밤은 차라리 한숨도 자지 않았을 때보다 나를 더 지치게 했다.\n\n10월 2일, 밤 10시. — 지난밤 나는 잤으나, 꿈은 꾸지 않았다. 깊이 잠들었음에 틀림없으니, 조너선이 잠자리에 드는데도 나는 깨지 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 그 잠은 나를 개운하게 하지 못했으니, 오늘 나는 끔찍하게 약하고 맥이 없다. 나는 어제 온종일 책을 읽으려 애쓰거나, 누워서 꾸벅꾸벅 졸며 보냈다. 오후에 렌필드 씨가 나를 만날 수 있겠느냐고 청했다. 가엾은 사람, 그는 매우 점잖았고, 내가 나올 때 내 손에 입 맞추며 하느님의 가호를 빌어주었다. 어쩐지 그것이 나를 무척 흔들어놓았다. 그를 생각하면 눈물이 난다. 이것은 새로운 나약함이니, 나는 이를 조심해야 한다. 조너선이 내가 울었다는 것을 안다면 비참해할 것이다. 그와 다른 이들은 저녁 식사 때까지 나가 있었고, 모두 지친 채로 들어왔다. 나는 그들의 기운을 북돋우려고 할 수 있는 일을 했는데, 짐작건대 그 노력이 나에게 도움이 되었던 모양이다. 내가 얼마나 지쳐 있었는지를 잊었으니 말이다. 저녁 식사 후 그들은 나를 잠자리로 보냈고, 그들 말로는 다 함께 담배를 피우러 갔다고 했으나, 나는 그들이 그날 각자에게 일어난 일을 서로 이야기하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알았다. 조너선의 태도로 보아 그가 무언가 중요한 것을 전할 것이 있음을 알 수 있었다. 나는 마땅히 졸려야 할 만큼 졸리지는 않았다. 그래서 그들이 가기 전에 나는 수어드 박사에게, 전날 밤 잘 자지 못했으니 어떤 종류든 가벼운 진정제를 좀 달라고 청했다. 그는 매우 친절하게 수면용 물약을 지어 나에게 주며, 매우 순한 것이니 나를 해치지 않을 거라고 일러주었다…… 나는 그것을 마셨고, 여전히 멀찍이 비켜서 있는 잠을 기다리고 있다. 나는 내가 잘못한 것이 아니기를 바란다. 잠이 나에게 추파를 던지기 시작하자 새로운 두려움이 밀려오기 때문이다. 이렇게 스스로 깨어날 힘을 앗아버린 것이 어리석은 짓이었을지도 모른다는 두려움이다. 나에게 그 힘이 필요할지도 모르는데. 잠이 온다. 안녕히.\n\n# JONATHAN HARKER’S JOURNAL\n\n「조너선 하커의 일기」\n10월 1일, 저녁. — 베스널 그린에 있는 토머스 스넬링의 집에서 그를 찾아냈으나, 불행히도 그는 무엇 하나 기억할 만한 상태가 아니었다. 내가 온다는 기별에 맥주가 생긴다는 기대만으로도 너무 들떴던지, 예정된 술판을 너무 일찍 시작해버린 것이었다. 다만 점잖고 가난해 보이는 그의 아내에게서, 그가 스몰릿의 조수에 지나지 않으며, 두 인부 가운데 책임 있는 쪽은 스몰릿이라는 사실을 알게 되었다. 그래서 나는 월워스로 마차를 몰아갔고, 조지프 스몰릿 씨가 셔츠 바람으로 접시에 따라 늦은 차를 마시고 있는 것을 집에서 만났다. 그는 점잖고 영리한 사내로, 분명히 선량하고 믿음직한 노동자 부류였으며, 제 나름의 분별을 갖춘 인물이었다. 그는 상자들에 얽힌 일을 죄다 기억하고 있었고, 바지 엉덩이 어름의 어떤 알 수 없는 주머니에서 꺼낸, 놀랍도록 귀퉁이가 접힌 수첩 — 굵고 거의 지워진 연필로 상형문자 같은 기록이 적힌 — 을 보고 상자들의 행선지를 일러주었다. 그의 말로는 카팩스에서 실어 내어 마일 엔드 뉴 타운의 칙샌드 가 197번지에 부린 짐수레 하나에 여섯 개가 있었고, 또 버몬지의 자메이카 레인에 부린 것이 다시 여섯 개였다고 했다. 그렇다면 백작이 자신의 그 끔찍한 은신처들을 런던 전역에 흩뿌릴 작정이었다면, 이 장소들이 일차 배달지로 선택된 것이며, 나중에 더 폭넓게 분산시키려는 것이리라. 일이 이토록 체계적으로 이루어진 것을 보면, 그가 런던의 두 방면에만 자신을 한정시킬 작정은 아니었으리라는 생각이 들었다. 그는 이제 북안의 극동쪽, 남안의 동쪽, 그리고 남쪽에 자리를 잡은 것이었다. 북쪽과 서쪽이 그의 악마적인 계획에서 결코 빠질 리 없었다 — 시티 자체와, 남서쪽과 서쪽에 자리한 멋들어진 런던의 바로 심장부는 말할 것도 없었다. 나는 스몰릿에게로 돌아가, 카팩스에서 실려 나간 다른 상자가 있었는지 일러줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n그가 대답했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 나리, 저한테 아주 후하게 대해주셨으니까요\" — 나는 그에게 반 소버린을 주었던 것이다 — \"아는 건 죄다 말씀드리죠. 나흘 전 밤에 핀처스 골목 '토끼와 사냥개'에서 블록샘이라는 작자가 하는 말을 들었는데요, 자기랑 동료가 퍼플릿의 어떤 낡은 집에서 먼지투성이 고된 일을 했다더군요. 이런 일은 흔치가 않으니까, 어쩌면 샘 블록샘이 뭔가 일러드릴 수 있지 않을까 싶습니다요.\" 나는 그를 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 일러줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 주소를 구해다 주면 반 소버린을 더 줄 만한 값어치가 있을 거라고 했다. 그러자 그는 남은 차를 단숨에 들이켜고는 일어서며, 지금 당장 여기서부터 찾아 나서겠다고 했다. 문간에서 그는 멈춰 서더니 말했다.\n\n\"이거 보세요, 나리, 여기서 나리를 붙들어둘 까닭이 없습죠. 샘을 금방 찾을지도 모르고, 못 찾을지도 모릅니다요. 하지만 아무튼 오늘 밤엔 그 사람이 나리한테 뭘 많이 일러드릴 형편은 못 될 겁니다. 샘은 술이 들어가기 시작하면 아주 대단한 작자거든요. 우표 붙인 봉투에다 나리 주소를 적어 저한테 주시면, 샘이 어디 있는지 알아내서 오늘 밤 안으로 부쳐드리죠. 하지만 아침에 일찍 그 사람을 찾아가시는 게 나을 겁니다. 안 그러면 못 잡으실지도 몰라요. 샘은 간밤에 술을 아무리 마셔도 새벽같이 나가버리니까요.\"\n\n이건 죄다 실속 있는 말이어서, 아이 하나가 1페니를 받아 봉투 한 장과 종이 한 장을 사고 거스름돈은 가지러 나갔다. 아이가 돌아오자 나는 봉투에 주소를 적고 우표를 붙였으며, 스몰릿이 다시 한 번 주소를 알아내는 대로 충실히 부쳐주겠노라 약속하자, 나는 집으로 향했다. 어쨌든 우리는 자취를 좇고 있다. 오늘 밤은 피곤해서 자고 싶다. 미나는 곤히 잠들었는데, 얼굴빛이 좀 지나치게 창백하다. 눈가는 마치 울었던 것처럼 보인다. 가엾은 사람, 영문도 모른 채 캄캄한 데 갇혀 있는 것이 그녀를 애태우는 것이 분명하고, 그래서 나와 다른 이들에 대한 근심이 갑절로 깊어졌는지도 모른다. 그러나 지금 이대로가 가장 나은 것이다. 지금 이런 식으로 서운하고 애가 타는 편이, 그녀의 신경이 무너지는 것보다는 낫다. 의사들이 그녀를 이 끔찍한 일에서 떼어놓아야 한다고 고집한 것은 참으로 옳았다. 나는 굳건해야 하니, 이 침묵의 짐은 다름 아닌 나에게 지워진 것이기 때문이다. 나는 어떤 사정이 있더라도 그녀와 이 문제를 입에 올리지 않을 것이다. 사실 이것은 결국 그리 어려운 일이 아닐지도 모르니, 그녀 자신이 이 문제에 대해 말을 아끼게 되었고, 우리가 결정을 일러준 이후로는 백작이나 그의 소행에 대해 한마디도 하지 않았기 때문이다.\n\n10월 2일, 저녁. — 길고, 고되고, 가슴 졸이는 하루였다. 첫 우편으로 내가 주소를 적어 둔 봉투를 받았는데, 그 안에 더러운 종잇조각 하나가 들어 있었고, 거기엔 목수의 연필로 갈겨쓴 글씨가 적혀 있었다.\n\n\"샘 블록샘, 콕런 하숙집, 월워스 바텔 가 포터스 코트 4번지. 데피티를 찾으시오.\"\n\n나는 침대에서 그 편지를 받았고, 미나를 깨우지 않은 채 일어났다. 그녀는 나른하고 졸려 보였으며, 창백한 데다 영 좋지 않은 안색이었다. 나는 그녀를 깨우지 않기로, 그리고 이번 새로운 탐색에서 돌아오면 그녀를 엑서터로 돌려보낼 채비를 하기로 마음먹었다. 우리집에서 날마다 마음을 붙일 제 일거리를 가지고 지내는 편이, 영문도 모른 채 우리 틈에 끼여 여기 있는 것보다 그녀가 더 행복하리라는 생각이 든다. 수어드 박사는 잠깐밖에 보지 못했고, 어디로 가는지 일러주면서, 무엇이든 알아내는 대로 돌아와 나머지 사람들에게도 알리겠노라 약속했다. 나는 월워스로 마차를 몰았고, 다소 애를 먹은 끝에 포터스 코트를 찾아냈다. 스몰릿의 철자가 나를 헷갈리게 했으니, 포터스 코트(Potter's Court)가 아니라 포터스 코트(Poter's Court)를 물었던 것이다. 그래도 그 골목을 찾고 나니, 콕런의 하숙집을 알아내는 데는 어려움이 없었다. 문간에 나온 사내에게 \"데피티\"를 찾는다고 했더니, 그는 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그런 사람 몰라요. 여기 그런 사람 없수다. 평생 그런 이름은 들어본 적도 없습니다요. 여기든 어디든 그런 부류가 산다고는 믿어지지가 않네요.\" 나는 스몰릿의 편지를 꺼냈고, 그것을 읽으면서 그 골목 이름의 철자가 준 교훈이 길잡이가 될지도 모르겠다는 생각이 들었다. \"당신은 뭐 하는 사람이오?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"제가 데피티(부관리인)입니다요,\" 하고 그가 대답했다. 나는 곧 내가 제대로 짚었음을 알았다. 소리 나는 대로 적은 철자가 또다시 나를 헷갈리게 한 것이었다. 반 크라운의 사례비가 그 부관리인의 지식을 나에게 내어주게 했고, 나는 블록샘 씨가 — 간밤에 콕런의 하숙집에서 마신 맥주 기운을 잠으로 떨쳐냈던 — 그날 아침 다섯 시에 포플러로 일하러 떠났음을 알게 되었다. 그는 일터가 어디인지는 일러주지 못했으나, 그것이 무슨 \"신식 창고\" 같은 것이라는 어렴풋한 짐작은 갖고 있었다. 이 빈약한 단서를 들고 나는 포플러로 떠나야 했다. 그런 건물에 대해 그럴듯한 단서나마 얻은 것은 열두 시가 다 되어서였고, 그것도 인부 몇이 점심을 먹고 있던 어느 찻집에서였다. 그들 가운데 하나가 크로스 에인절 가에 새 \"냉장 보관\" 건물이 올라가고 있다고 일러주었는데, 이것이 \"신식 창고\"라는 조건에 들어맞기에, 나는 곧장 그리로 마차를 몰았다. 시무룩한 수위와 더 시무룩한 십장과 한 차례씩 이야기를 나누었는데, 둘 다 나라의 동전으로 누그러뜨리고 나니, 블록샘의 자취를 잡을 수 있었다. 사사로운 일로 그에게 몇 가지 물어볼 권리를 사는 셈치고 그의 하루 일당을 십장에게 치르겠다고 제안하자, 사람을 보내어 그를 불러왔다. 말투와 거동은 거칠었으나, 그는 제법 영리한 사내였다. 일러주는 대가를 치르겠노라 약속하고 선금을 쥐여주자, 그는 카팩스와 피커딜리의 어느 집 사이를 두 차례 오갔으며, 이 일을 위해 손수 빌린 말과 짐수레로 그 집에서 후자로 큰 상자 아홉 개 — \"엄청 무거운 것들\" — 를 실어 갔다고 일러주었다. 나는 피커딜리의 그 집 번지를 일러줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었고, 그는 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"글쎄요, 나리, 번지는 잊어버렸지만요, 큰 흰 교회인가 뭔가 그런 거에서 몇 집밖에 안 떨어져 있었어요. 지은 지 얼마 안 된 거요. 그것도 먼지투성이 낡은 집이긴 했지만요, 우리가 그 빌어먹을 상자들을 실어 낸 집의 먼지에 대면 아무것도 아니었습죠.\"\n\n\"두 집 다 비어 있었다면, 어떻게 안으로 들어갔소?\"\n\n\"퍼플릿 집에는 저를 고용한 그 영감님이 기다리고 있었어요. 영감님이 상자들 들어 올려서 짐수레에 싣는 걸 거들어주었습죠. 빌어먹을, 글쎄 제가 여태 본 중에 제일 힘센 작자였다니까요. 그것도 늙은 양반이, 흰 콧수염을 기르고, 어찌나 비쩍 말랐는지 그림자도 못 드리울 것 같은 사람이 말이에요.\"\n\n이 말이 어찌나 나를 오싹하게 했던지!\n\n\"아, 글쎄 그 양반이 상자 한쪽 끝을 마치 한 근짜리 차 봉지처럼 번쩍 들어 올리는데, 저는 헉헉대고 씩씩대고서야 겨우 제 쪽 끝을 세웠다니까요 — 그래도 제가 풋내기는 아니거든요.\"\n\n\"피커딜리의 그 집에는 어떻게 들어갔소?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"거기도 그 양반이 있었어요. 먼저 출발해서 저보다 앞서 갔던 게 분명해요. 제가 종을 울리니까 직접 나와서 문을 열고, 상자들 현관으로 나르는 걸 거들어주었거든요.\"\n\n\"아홉 개 전부 말이오?\" 하고 내가 물었다.\n\n\"예, 첫 짐에 다섯 개, 두 번째 짐에 네 개였습죠. 목이 어찌나 타던지, 어떻게 집에 돌아왔는지도 잘 기억이 안 나요.\" 나는 그의 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"상자들은 현관에 두었소?\"\n\n\"예, 큰 현관이었는데, 거기엔 아무것도 없었어요.\" 나는 일을 더 진척시켜 보려고 한 번 더 시도했다.\n\n\"열쇠는 안 가지고 있었소?\"\n\n\"열쇠고 뭐고 쓴 적이 없어요. 영감님이 직접 문을 열고, 제가 마차를 몰고 떠날 때 다시 닫았어요. 마지막은 잘 기억이 안 나는데 — 그건 맥주 탓이에요.\"\n\n\"그래서 그 집 번지는 기억 못 하는 거요?\"\n\n\"못 합니다요, 나리. 하지만 그건 어려울 게 없어요. 돌로 된 정면에 둥근 내닫이창이 달린 높은 집이고, 문까지 높은 계단이 나 있어요. 제가 그 계단은 압니다요, 동전 몇 닢 벌러 모여든 건달 셋이랑 상자를 거기로 날라야 했거든요. 영감님이 그들한테 1실링씩 줬는데, 그렇게 많이 받은 걸 보고 더 달라는 거예요. 그러니까 영감님이 그중 하나를 어깨를 잡아서 계단 아래로 내던질 것처럼 하니까, 다들 욕을 지껄이며 가버렸습죠.\" 나는 이 묘사면 그 집을 찾을 수 있겠다 싶어, 일러준 대가를 친구에게 치르고 피커딜리로 떠났다. 나는 새로운 고통스러운 깨달음을 얻었으니, 백작이 그 흙 상자들을 혼자서도 다룰 수 있다는 것이 분명해진 것이다. 그렇다면 시간이 귀했다. 이제 그가 어느 정도 분산을 이루어놓았으니, 제가 택한 때에 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 그 일을 마무리할 수 있을 터였다. 피커딜리 서커스에서 나는 마차를 보냈고, 서쪽으로 걸었다. 주니어 컨스티튜셔널을 지나자 그가 묘사한 집과 마주쳤고, 이것이 드라큘라가 마련한 다음 소굴임을 확신했다. 그 집은 오래 비어 있었던 것처럼 보였다. 창문은 먼지가 더껑이로 앉아 있었고, 덧문은 닫혀 있었다. 골조는 죄다 세월에 검게 변했고, 쇠붙이에서는 칠이 대부분 벗겨져 있었다. 얼마 전까지만 해도 발코니 앞에 큰 게시판이 있었던 것이 분명한데, 그것은 거칠게 뜯겨 나갔고, 그것을 받치던 기둥만 아직 남아 있었다. 발코니 난간 뒤로는 헐거워진 판자 몇 개가 보였는데, 그 갓 드러난 가장자리가 허옇게 보였다. 그 게시판을 온전히 볼 수만 있었다면 적잖은 값이라도 치렀을 것이니, 어쩌면 그 집 소유자에 대한 단서를 얻었을지도 몰랐기 때문이다. 나는 카팩스를 조사하고 사들였던 경험을 떠올렸고, 만일 전 소유자를 찾아낼 수만 있다면 그 집에 들어갈 어떤 방도를 찾아낼 수 있으리라는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었다.\n\n지금으로서는 피커딜리 쪽에서 알아낼 것이 없었고, 할 수 있는 일도 없었다. 그래서 나는 뒤쪽으로 돌아가, 이 방면에서 뭔가 알아낼 것이 없는지 보았다. 마구간 골목은 활기찼는데, 피커딜리의 집들은 대부분 사람이 살고 있었기 때문이다. 나는 주변에서 본 마부와 일꾼 한둘에게 그 빈집에 대해 뭔가 일러줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 그 가운데 하나는, 최근에 누가 그 집을 샀다는 말을 들었는데 누구한테서인지는 모르겠다고 했다. 그러나 그는, 아주 최근까지만 해도 \"매각\" 게시판이 걸려 있었다고, 그리고 어쩌면 부동산 중개인인 미첼·선스 앤드 캔디가 뭔가 일러줄 수 있을 거라고 했다. 그 게시판에서 그 회사 이름을 본 기억이 나는 것 같다는 것이었다. 나는 너무 다급해 보이고 싶지도, 알려준 자에게 내가 너무 많이 알거나 짐작한다는 것을 알리고 싶지도 않았으므로, 으레 하는 식으로 그에게 고맙다고 하고는 어슬렁어슬렁 자리를 떴다. 이제 땅거미가 지고 가을밤이 다가오고 있었으므로, 나는 시간을 조금도 허비하지 않았다. 버클리에 있는 인명록에서 미첼·선스 앤드 캔디의 주소를 알아낸 뒤, 나는 곧 색빌 가에 있는 그들의 사무소에 닿았다.\n\n나를 맞은 신사는 유난히 정중한 태도였으나, 그만큼이나 입이 무거웠다. 일단 그 피커딜리의 집이 — 그는 우리가 이야기를 나누는 내내 그것을 \"저택\"이라 불렀다 — 팔렸다고 일러준 뒤로는, 내 용건이 끝난 것으로 여겼다. 누가 그것을 사들였느냐고 묻자, 그는 눈을 조금 더 크게 뜨더니, 대답하기에 앞서 몇 초간 뜸을 들였다.\n\n\"팔렸습니다, 선생님.\"\n\n\"실례지만,\" 나는 똑같이 정중하게 말했다. \"누가 그것을 사들였는지 알고 싶은 특별한 사정이 있습니다.\"\n\n그는 다시 더 길게 뜸을 들이며, 눈썹을 한층 더 치켜올렸다. \"팔렸습니다, 선생님,\" 하는 것이 다시 그의 간결한 대답이었다.\n\n\"그쯤은,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"저에게 알려주셔도 괜찮지 않겠습니까.\"\n\n\"하지만 괜찮지 않습니다,\" 그가 대답했다. \"고객의 일은 미첼·선스 앤드 캔디의 손안에서 절대적으로 안전합니다.\" 이자는 분명 둘도 없는 잘난 체하는 자였고, 그와 입씨름해 봐야 소용이 없었다. 나는 그의 영역에서 그를 상대하는 것이 상책이겠다 싶어, 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"댁의 고객들은 신뢰를 이토록 단호히 지켜주는 분을 두었으니 복이 많습니다. 저 역시 전문직에 있는 사람입니다.\" 여기서 나는 그에게 내 명함을 건넸다. \"이 경우에는 호기심에서 움직이는 것이 아닙니다. 저는 고덜밍 경을 대신하여 행동하는 것이며, 경께서는 최근에 매물로 나온 것으로 아는 그 부동산에 대해 무언가 알고 싶어 하십니다.\" 이 말이 사정을 다르게 돌려놓았다. 그가 말했다.\n\n\"제가 할 수만 있다면 기꺼이 편의를 봐드리고 싶습니다, 하커 씨. 특히 경께는 더더욱 그러고 싶군요. 저희는 일찍이 그분이 아너러블 아서 홈우드이셨을 때, 그분을 위해 방을 임대하는 자그마한 일을 처리해 드린 적이 있습니다. 경의 주소를 알려주시면 제가 이 건에 대해 회사와 의논하겠으며, 어떤 경우에도 오늘 밤 우편으로 경께 연락드리겠습니다. 저희 규정을 그만큼이라도 벗어나 경께서 원하시는 정보를 드릴 수 있다면 더없이 기쁘겠습니다.\"\n\n나는 적이 아니라 우군을 확보하고 싶었으므로, 그에게 고맙다고 하고 수어드 박사의 주소를 일러준 뒤 자리를 떴다. 이제 날이 어두웠고, 나는 지치고 배가 고팠다. 나는 에어레이티드 브레드 컴퍼니에서 차 한 잔을 마시고, 다음 기차로 퍼플릿에 내려왔다.\n\n나머지 사람들은 모두 집에 있었다. 미나는 지치고 창백해 보였으나, 밝고 명랑하려고 안간힘을 썼다. 그녀에게 무언가를 숨겨야 했고 그로 인해 그녀를 불안하게 만들었다고 생각하니 가슴이 미어졌다. 다행스럽게도, 그녀가 우리의 회의를 옆에서 지켜보며 우리가 신뢰를 내보이지 않는 데서 오는 아픔을 느끼는 것도 오늘 밤이 마지막일 것이다. 그녀를 우리의 음울한 임무에서 떼어놓겠다는 현명한 결심을 지키는 데에 내 온 용기가 들었다. 그녀는 어쩐지 좀더 체념한 듯 보이는데, 아니면 그 화제 자체가 그녀에게 역겨워진 것인지도 모른다. 어쩌다 그 이야기가 나오기라도 하면 그녀는 정말로 몸서리를 치니 말이다. 우리가 때맞춰 결심한 것이 다행이니, 이런 심정인 그녀에게 우리의 늘어가는 지식은 고문이 되었을 것이다.\n\n우리끼리만 남을 때까지는 나머지 사람들에게 오늘의 발견을 일러줄 수 없었다. 그래서 저녁식사 뒤에 — 우리끼리도 체면을 차리느라 약간의 음악을 곁들인 뒤에 — 나는 미나를 방으로 데려가, 자도록 두고 나왔다. 사랑스러운 그 사람은 여느 때보다 더 다정하게 굴며, 마치 나를 붙들어두려는 듯 매달렸다. 그러나 이야기할 것이 많았으므로 나는 나왔다. 다행스럽게도, 이야기를 그만둔 것이 우리 사이를 조금도 달라지게 하지는 않았다.\n\n다시 아래층으로 내려가 보니, 나머지 사람들이 모두 서재의 난롯가에 모여 있었다. 나는 기차에서 여기까지 일기를 써둔 터라, 그들을 내 정보와 나란히 따라잡게 하는 가장 좋은 방편으로 그것을 그대로 읽어주었다. 다 읽고 나자 반 헬싱이 말했다.\n\n\"오늘은 큰일을 해냈군요, 친구 조너선. 우리가 사라진 상자들의 자취를 좇고 있는 것이 틀림없소. 그것들을 죄다 그 집에서 찾아낸다면, 우리 일도 끝에 가까워지는 거요. 그러나 몇 개라도 빠져 있다면, 찾아낼 때까지 뒤져야 하오. 그런 다음에 우리는 마지막 일격을 가하여, 그 비열한 놈을 진짜 죽음으로 몰아넣을 것이오.\" 우리는 한동안 모두 말없이 앉아 있었는데, 별안간 모리스 씨가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"그런데 말이오! 그 집에 우리가 어떻게 들어가지요?\"\n\n\"다른 집에는 들어갔잖소,\" 고덜밍 경이 재빨리 대답했다.\n\n\"하지만 아트, 이건 달라요. 카팩스는 우리가 침입했지만, 그땐 밤과 담장을 두른 정원이 우리를 가려주었소. 낮이든 밤이든 피커딜리에서 도둑질을 저지르는 건 영 다른 얘기일 거요. 솔직히, 그 중개소 친구가 우리한테 무슨 열쇠라도 구해주지 못한다면 어떻게 들어갈 수 있을지 모르겠소. 어쩌면 아침에 자네가 그자의 편지를 받으면 알게 될지도 모르겠지만.\" 고덜밍 경의 눈썹이 찌푸려졌고, 그는 일어나 방 안을 거닐었다. 이윽고 그는 멈춰 서서, 우리를 한 사람씩 차례로 돌아보며 말했다.\n\n\"퀸시 머리가 제대로 돌아가는군. 이 도둑질이라는 게 심각해지고 있어. 한 번은 무사히 넘어갔지만, 이제 우리한텐 만만찮은 일거리가 있는 셈이야 — 백작의 열쇠 꾸러미를 찾아내지 못하는 한 말이지.\"\n\n아침이 되기 전에는 딱히 할 수 있는 일이 없었고, 적어도 고덜밍 경이 미첼 측에서 연락을 받을 때까지 기다리는 것이 현명하리라 여겨졌으므로, 우리는 아침식사 때 이전에는 어떤 적극적인 행동도 취하지 않기로 했다. 우리는 한참 동안 앉아 담배를 피우며, 이 문제를 여러 측면과 관점에서 따져보았다. 나는 이 틈을 타 이 일기를 바로 이 순간까지 써둔다. 몹시 졸려 자러 가야겠다…….\n\n한 줄만 더. 미나는 곤히 잠들었고 숨소리가 고르다. 그녀의 이마는 잠결에도 무언가 생각하는 듯이 잔주름이 잡혀 있다. 여전히 너무 창백하지만, 오늘 아침처럼 그토록 초췌해 보이지는 않는다. 내일은 이 모든 것이 나아지기를 바란다. 그녀는 엑서터의 우리집에서 제 모습을 되찾을 것이다. 아, 그런데 졸리다!\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n10월 1일. — 렌필드 때문에 다시금 갈피를 잡을 수 없다. 그의 기분이 어찌나 빠르게 바뀌는지 따라잡기가 어려운데, 그 변화가 늘 그 자신의 안녕 이상의 무언가를 뜻하므로, 그것은 흥미롭다는 말로는 부족한 연구거리가 된다. 오늘 아침, 그가 반 헬싱을 내쫓은 뒤에 그를 보러 갔더니, 그의 태도는 운명을 호령하는 자의 그것이었다. 사실 그는 운명을 호령하고 있었다 — 주관적으로 말이다. 그는 한낱 지상의 것들 따위에는 정말이지 관심이 없었다. 그는 구름 위에 있었고, 우리 가엾은 인간들의 온갖 약점과 결핍을 내려다보고 있었다. 나는 이 기회를 살려 무언가 알아내야겠다 싶어, 그에게 물었다.\n\n\"요즘 파리들은 좀 어떻소?\" 그는 자못 우월한 투로 나에게 미소를 지었는데 — 말볼리오의 얼굴에나 어울렸을 법한 그런 미소였다 — 이렇게 대답했다.\n\n\"파리에게는, 선생, 한 가지 두드러진 특징이 있지요. 그 날개는 정신적 능력의 공중을 나는 힘을 상징합니다. 옛사람들이 영혼을 나비로 비유한 것은 참으로 옳았습니다!\"\n\n나는 그의 비유를 논리적으로 끝까지 밀어붙여 보려고 재빨리 말했다.\n\n\"오, 그럼 이제 당신이 노리는 건 영혼이로군요, 그렇소?\" 광기가 그의 이성을 어그러뜨려, 그의 얼굴에 어리둥절한 빛이 번졌고, 그는 좀처럼 본 적 없는 단호함으로 고개를 저으며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 아니오, 아니에요! 나는 영혼은 원하지 않습니다. 내가 원하는 건 오직 생명뿐이에요.\" 여기서 그는 밝아졌다. \"지금은 그것에도 꽤 무덤덤합니다. 생명은 괜찮아요. 나는 원하는 걸 다 가졌으니까요. 동물포식증을 연구하시려거든, 박사님, 새 환자를 구하셔야 할 겁니다!\"\n\n이 말에 나는 좀 어리둥절해져서, 그를 부추겼다.\n\n\"그럼 당신이 생명을 호령하는군요. 당신은 신쯤 되는 모양이오?\" 그는 형언할 수 없이 자애로운 우월감을 띠며 미소 지었다.\n\n\"오, 아니오! 신의 속성을 제 것인 양 참칭하는 일일랑 결코 없을 겁니다. 나는 그분의 특별히 영적인 소행에는 끼지조차 않습니다. 제 지적 입장을 말씀드리자면, 순전히 지상에 속한 일들에 관한 한, 나는 에녹이 영적으로 차지했던 위치쯤에 있다 하겠습니다!\" 이건 나에겐 난제였다. 그 순간 나는 에녹이 어떻게 들어맞는지 떠올릴 수 없었다. 그래서 단순한 질문을 던질 수밖에 없었는데, 그렇게 함으로써 이 광인의 눈에 내가 스스로를 낮추는 셈이 된다는 것을 느끼면서도 그리할 수밖에 없었다.\n\n\"그런데 왜 하필 에녹이오?\"\n\n\"그는 신과 함께 걸었으니까요.\" 나는 그 비유를 알 수 없었으나, 그것을 인정하기는 싫었다. 그래서 나는 그가 부인했던 것으로 되돌아갔다.\n\n\"그러니까 당신은 생명에는 관심이 없고, 영혼도 원하지 않는다는 거요. 어째서요?\" 나는 그를 당황하게 만들 작정으로, 다소 엄하게 재빨리 물음을 던졌다. 그 시도는 먹혀들었다. 한순간 그는 저도 모르게 옛날의 비굴한 태도로 되돌아가, 내 앞에 깊이 굽실거리며, 대답하면서 정말로 나에게 알랑거렸다.\n\n\"나는 영혼은 원하지 않습니다, 정말, 정말로요! 원치 않아요. 그것들을 가진들 쓸 데가 없어요. 나한텐 도무지 쓸모가 없을 겁니다. 그것들을 먹을 수도 없고 또는——\" 그는 별안간 입을 다물었고, 잔잔한 수면 위로 바람이 스치듯, 그의 얼굴에 옛 교활한 빛이 번졌다. \"그리고 박사님, 생명으로 말할 것 같으면, 그게 결국 다 뭡니까? 필요한 걸 죄다 가졌고, 앞으로도 결코 아쉬울 일이 없으리란 걸 안다면, 그게 전부지요. 나한텐 벗들이 있습니다 — 좋은 벗들이요 — 박사님 같은 분 말입니다.\" 이 말은 이루 말할 수 없이 교활한 곁눈질과 함께 나왔다. \"나는 생명의 방편이 결코 모자라지 않으리란 걸 압니다!\"\n\n내 생각에 그는 자신의 광기의 흐릿함 너머로 내 안의 어떤 적대감을 본 모양이었다. 그 즉시 그는 그와 같은 부류가 마지막으로 기대는 피난처 — 고집스러운 침묵 — 로 물러나 버렸기 때문이다. 잠시 후 나는 당분간 그에게 말을 거는 것이 소용없음을 알았다. 그는 시무룩했고, 그래서 나는 물러 나왔다.\n\n그날 늦게 그가 나를 불러달라고 사람을 보냈다. 평소 같으면 특별한 까닭 없이는 가지 않았을 테지만, 바로 지금은 그에게 흥미가 어찌나 동하는지 기꺼이 한 번 애써볼 만했다. 게다가 시간을 보내는 데 도움이 될 만한 무언가가 있다는 것이 반가웠다. 하커는 단서를 좇아 밖에 나가 있고, 고덜밍 경과 퀸시도 마찬가지다. 반 헬싱은 내 서재에 앉아 하커 부부가 마련한 기록을 들여다보고 있다. 그는 모든 세부를 정확히 앎으로써 어떤 단서에 가닿으리라 여기는 듯하다. 그는 까닭 없이 그 작업을 방해받고 싶어 하지 않는다. 그를 데리고 환자를 보러 갔을 수도 있었으나, 지난번에 내쫓긴 뒤라 다시 가고 싶어 하지 않을 것 같았다. 또 다른 까닭도 있었다. 렌필드가 제삼자 앞에서는 나와 단둘이 있을 때만큼 거리낌 없이 말하지 않을지도 모른다는 것이었다.\n\n가서 보니 그는 등받이 없는 의자에 앉아 방바닥 한가운데에 나와 있었는데, 이런 자세는 대개 그에게 어떤 정신적 활력이 있음을 일러주는 것이다. 내가 들어서자, 마치 그 물음이 입가에서 기다리고 있었던 듯 그는 대뜸 말했다.\n\n\"영혼은 어떻게 됐소?\" 그렇다면 내 짐작이 옳았던 것이 분명했다. 무의식적인 사고 작용이, 이 광인에게서마저 제 일을 하고 있었던 것이다. 나는 이 문제를 끝장내기로 마음먹었다. \"당신 자신은 그것에 대해 어떻게 생각하오?\" 하고 내가 물었다. 그는 한순간 대답하지 않고, 마치 대답할 영감을 찾으려는 듯 사방을, 위아래를 두루 살폈다.\n\n\"나는 영혼은 원하지 않아요!\" 그가 힘없이, 변명하는 투로 말했다. 그 문제가 그의 마음을 짓누르고 있는 듯했으므로, 나는 그것을 이용하기로 — \"오로지 다정하기 위해 모질게 굴기로\" 마음먹었다. 그래서 내가 말했다.\n\n\"당신은 생명을 좋아하고, 생명을 원하지요?\"\n\n\"오, 그럼요! 하지만 그건 괜찮아요. 그건 걱정하실 것 없어요!\"\n\n\"하지만,\" 하고 내가 물었다. \"영혼까지 얻지 않고서야 어떻게 생명을 얻겠소?\" 이 말이 그를 어리둥절하게 만든 듯하여, 나는 밀고 나갔다.\n\n\"언젠가 당신이 저 바깥으로 날아다니게 되면, 수천 마리 파리와 거미와 새와 고양이의 영혼이 당신 둘레에서 윙윙거리고 짹짹대고 야옹거릴 테니, 참 가관일 거요. 알다시피 당신은 그것들의 생명을 가졌으니, 그것들의 영혼도 감수해야 할 거란 말이오!\" 무언가가 그의 상상력을 건드린 듯, 그는 손가락을 귀에 갖다 대고 눈을 감았는데, 마치 어린 사내아이가 얼굴에 비누칠을 당할 때처럼 두 눈을 질끈 감았다. 거기엔 나를 뭉클하게 하는 애처로운 데가 있었다. 또한 그것은 나에게 한 가지 가르침을 주었으니, 내 앞에 있는 것은 한 아이 — 그저 한 아이일 뿐, 비록 이목구비는 닳고 턱의 까칠한 수염은 허옜지만 — 인 듯했기 때문이다. 그가 어떤 정신적 동요의 과정을 겪고 있는 것이 분명했고, 지난날 그의 기분들이 겉보기에 그와 무관한 일들을 어떻게 풀이해 왔는지를 아는 터라, 나는 되도록 그의 마음속으로 들어가 그와 함께 가보기로 했다. 첫걸음은 그에게 신뢰를 되살려 주는 것이었으므로, 나는 그가 막은 귀로도 알아듣도록 꽤 큰 소리로 물었다.\n\n\"파리들을 다시 불러 모으게 설탕이라도 좀 드릴까요?\" 그는 단박에 깨어나는 듯하더니 고개를 저었다. 그러고는 웃으며 대답했다.\n\n\"천만에요! 파리는 결국 보잘것없는 것들이에요!\" 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그는 덧붙였다. \"하지만 그래도 그것들의 영혼이 내 둘레에서 윙윙대는 건 싫어요.\"\n\n\"그럼 거미는?\" 하고 내가 이어 물었다.\n\n\"거미 따위! 거미가 무슨 소용이에요? 거기엔 먹을 것도 없고 또는——\" 그는 마치 금지된 화제가 떠오른 듯 별안간 멈췄다.\n\n'옳거니, 옳거니!' 나는 속으로 생각했다. '이자가 '마실(drink)'이라는 말 앞에서 별안간 멈춘 게 이번이 두 번째다. 무슨 뜻일까?' 렌필드는 자신이 실언했음을 스스로 알아챈 듯, 거기서 내 주의를 돌리려는 듯이 서둘러 말을 이었다.\n\n\"나는 그런 일들엔 도무지 관심이 없습니다. '쥐며 생쥐며 그런 자잘한 짐승들' 말이에요, 셰익스피어 식으로 말하자면요. '식료품 저장실의 닭 모이'쯤이라 부를 만한 것들이지요. 나는 그런 시시한 것일랑 죄다 졸업했습니다. 내 앞에 무엇이 놓여 있는지 아는 마당에, 나더러 그 하찮은 육식 동물 따위에 관심을 가지라는 건, 젓가락 한 쌍으로 분자를 집어 먹으라는 거나 다름없어요.\"\n\n\"알겠소,\" 하고 내가 말했다. \"당신은 이빨이 푹 맞물릴 만한 큰 것을 원하는군요? 코끼리로 아침식사를 하면 어떻겠소?\"\n\n\"무슨 그런 터무니없는 헛소리를 하십니까!\" 그가 너무 또렷이 깨어나고 있었으므로, 나는 그를 세게 몰아붙여 보기로 했다. \"궁금하군요,\" 하고 나는 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했다. \"코끼리의 영혼은 어떻게 생겼을지 말이오!\"\n\n내가 바라던 효과를 얻었으니, 그는 그 즉시 거드름을 떨던 데서 굴러떨어져 다시 아이가 되었다.\n\n\"나는 코끼리의 영혼도, 어떤 영혼도 원하지 않아요!\" 그가 말했다. 잠시 그는 풀이 죽어 앉아 있었다. 별안간 그는 벌떡 일어났는데, 두 눈이 이글거리고 격렬한 뇌의 흥분이 온몸에 드러났다. \"당신도 당신의 영혼도 지옥에나 가버려!\" 그가 소리쳤다. \"어째서 영혼 따위로 나를 들볶는 거요? 영혼을 생각할 것도 없이, 나를 걱정시키고 괴롭히고 어지럽히는 게 이미 충분치 않단 말이오!\" 그가 어찌나 적의에 차 보였던지, 또 한 차례 살인적인 발작을 일으키려는 게 아닌가 싶어, 나는 호각을 불었다. 그러나 그렇게 하자마자 그는 차분해지더니, 변명하듯 말했다.\n\n\"용서하십시오, 박사님. 제가 제정신이 아니었습니다. 도움 같은 건 필요 없으세요. 마음이 어찌나 어지러운지 짜증을 내기 쉽군요. 제가 마주하고 있는, 그리고 풀어내고 있는 문제를 아신다면, 저를 가엾이 여기시고, 참아주시고, 용서해 주실 겁니다. 부디 저를 구속복에 넣지는 말아주십시오. 저는 생각을 해야 하는데, 몸이 묶이면 자유로이 생각할 수가 없습니다. 분명 이해해 주시리라 믿습니다!\" 그는 분명 자제력이 있었다. 그래서 수발하는 자들이 왔을 때 나는 신경 쓰지 말라고 일렀고, 그들은 물러갔다. 렌필드는 그들이 나가는 것을 지켜보았다. 문이 닫히자 그는 자못 위엄 있고 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"수어드 박사님, 저에게 무척 사려 깊게 대해주셨습니다. 진심으로 박사님께 정말, 정말 감사드린다는 걸 믿어주십시오!\" 나는 이런 기분일 때 그를 두고 나오는 것이 좋겠다 싶어, 물러 나왔다. 이 사람의 상태에는 분명 곰곰이 따져볼 만한 무언가가 있다. 몇 가지 점들을 제대로 된 순서로 꿰어맞출 수만 있다면, 미국인 인터뷰어들이 말하는 \"이야깃거리\"가 될 듯하다. 그것들을 여기 적어둔다.\n\n'마시는 것'에 대해서는 입에 올리려 하지 않는다.\n\n어떤 것의 '영혼'을 짊어지게 된다는 생각을 두려워한다.\n\n앞날에 '생명'이 아쉬워질까 봐 두려워하지는 않는다.\n\n생명의 더 천한 형태들은 아예 경멸하면서도, 그것들의 영혼에 시달릴까 봐 두려워한다.\n\n논리적으로 이 모든 것이 한 방향을 가리킨다! 그는 어떤 더 높은 생명을 얻게 되리라는 어떤 종류의 확신을 갖고 있다. 그는 그 결과 — 영혼이라는 짐 — 를 두려워한다. 그렇다면 그가 바라보는 것은 인간의 생명이다!\n\n그리고 그 확신이라는 것은——?\n\n자비로우신 하느님! 백작이 그에게 다녀갔고, 무슨 새로운 공포의 계략이 진행되고 있는 것이다!\n\n이후. — 나는 회진을 마친 뒤 반 헬싱에게 가서 내 의심을 일러주었다. 그는 몹시 심각해졌다. 그리고 잠시 그 문제를 곰곰이 따져본 뒤, 나에게 렌필드에게로 데려가 달라고 청했다. 나는 그리했다. 우리가 문에 이르렀을 때, 그 광인이 안에서 흥겹게 노래하는 소리가 들렸는데, 이제는 까마득히 멀게만 느껴지는 그 시절에 그가 그러곤 했던 것처럼 말이다. 안으로 들어서자, 우리는 놀랍게도 그가 예전처럼 설탕을 펼쳐놓은 것을 보았다. 가을 기운에 굼떠진 파리들이 방 안으로 윙윙거리며 들기 시작하고 있었다. 우리는 그에게 앞서 나눈 대화의 화제를 꺼내게 하려 했으나, 그는 귀를 기울이려 하지 않았다. 그는 마치 우리가 거기 없는 것처럼 노래를 이어갔다. 그는 종잇조각 하나를 손에 넣어 수첩 모양으로 접고 있었다. 우리는 들어갈 때만큼이나 아무것도 모른 채 물러 나와야 했다.\n\n그는 참으로 기이한 환자다. 오늘 밤 그를 지켜봐야겠다.\n\n\"10월 1일.\n\n경께.\n\n저희는 언제든 경의 뜻을 받드는 것을 더없는 기쁨으로 여깁니다. 하커 씨를 통해 경을 대신하여 표명하신 바람과 관련하여, 피커딜리 347번지의 매매에 관한 다음의 정보를 삼가 알려드립니다. 본래의 매도인은 고(故) 아치볼드 윈터서필드 씨의 유언 집행인들입니다. 매수인은 외국의 한 귀족인 드 빌 백작으로, 그가 직접 매매를 성사시켰으며, 경께서 이런 상스러운 표현을 너그러이 보아주신다면, 매매 대금을 '현장에서' 지폐로 치렀습니다. 그 밖에는 그에 대해 저희가 아는 바가 전혀 없습니다.\n이만 줄이며, 경께,\n\n경의 충실한 종복들,\n\n미첼·선스 앤드 캔디 올림.\"\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n10월 2일. — 간밤에 나는 복도에 사람 하나를 세워두고, 렌필드의 방에서 들리는 어떤 소리든 정확히 기록하라고 일렀으며, 무언가 이상한 일이 있거든 나를 부르라고 지시해 두었다. 저녁식사 뒤에, 우리가 모두 서재의 난롯가에 모였을 때 — 하커 부인은 잠자리에 든 뒤였다 — 우리는 그날의 시도와 발견들을 의논했다. 하커만이 어떤 성과를 거둔 유일한 사람이었고, 우리는 그의 단서가 중요한 것일지 모른다는 큰 희망을 품고 있다.\n\n잠자리에 들기 전 나는 환자의 방으로 돌아가 관찰용 쪽문을 통해 안을 들여다보았다. 그는 곤히 잠들어 있었고, 가슴이 고른 숨결에 따라 오르내리고 있었다.\n\n오늘 아침 당직을 섰던 사내가, 자정 조금 지나 그가 안절부절못하며 다소 큰 소리로 기도를 계속 외었다고 보고했다. 나는 그게 전부냐고 물었고, 그는 들은 건 그것뿐이라고 대답했다. 그의 태도에 어딘가 미심쩍은 데가 있어, 나는 대놓고 자고 있었던 것 아니냐고 물었다. 그는 잠은 부인했으나, 잠시 \"졸았던\" 것은 시인했다. 사람을 감시하지 않고서는 믿을 수 없다니, 참으로 곤란한 노릇이다.\n\n오늘 하커는 단서를 좇아 밖에 나가 있고, 아트와 퀸시는 말들을 살피고 있다. 고덜밍은 말을 늘 채비시켜 두는 것이 좋겠다고 여기는데, 우리가 찾는 정보를 얻게 되면 한시도 지체할 틈이 없을 것이기 때문이다. 우리는 들여온 흙을 모조리 해 뜰 때와 해 질 때 사이에 무력화해야 한다. 그리하면 백작이 가장 약해진 때에, 달아날 은신처도 없는 채로 그를 붙잡게 될 것이다. 반 헬싱은 고대 의술에 관한 권위서 몇 권을 찾아보러 대영박물관에 갔다. 옛 의사들은 그들의 후학들이 받아들이지 않는 것들까지 고려했는데, 교수는 나중에 우리에게 쓸모 있을지 모를 마녀와 악마를 다루는 치료법들을 뒤지고 있다.\n\n이따금 나는 우리 모두가 미쳐 있는 것이 틀림없으며, 구속복을 입은 채로 제정신이 들 거라는 생각이 든다.\n\n이후. — 우리는 다시 모였다. 우리는 마침내 자취를 잡은 듯하며, 내일 우리의 일이 끝의 시작이 될지도 모른다. 렌필드의 잠잠함이 이와 무슨 관련이 있는 것인지 궁금하다. 그의 기분이 백작의 소행을 어찌나 그대로 따라왔던지, 그 괴물의 다가오는 파멸이 어떤 미묘한 방식으로 그에게 전해지고 있는지도 모른다. 오늘 내가 그와 논쟁을 벌인 때와 그가 다시 파리잡이로 돌아간 때 사이에 그의 마음속에서 무슨 일이 오갔는지 어떤 실마리라도 얻을 수 있다면, 우리에게 값진 단서를 주었을지도 모른다. 그는 지금 한동안은 잠잠해 보인다……. 정말 그럴까?—— 저 거친 비명은 그의 방에서 들려온 듯한데…….\n\n수발하는 자가 내 방으로 뛰어들어와, 렌필드가 어쩌다 무슨 사고를 당했다고 일러주었다. 그가 비명을 들었고, 가서 보니 그가 얼굴을 바닥에 박은 채 온통 피투성이로 쓰러져 있더라는 것이었다. 당장 가봐야 한다…….\n\n# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n10월 3일. — 지난번 기록을 남긴 이후로 일어난 모든 일을, 기억할 수 있는 한 정확하게 적어두겠다. 떠올릴 수 있는 사소한 것 하나라도 빠뜨려서는 안 된다. 모든 것을 차분하게 진행해야 한다.\n\n렌필드의 방에 들어섰을 때, 그는 번들거리는 피웅덩이 속에 왼쪽으로 누워 바닥에 쓰러져 있었다. 그를 옮기려 다가가자, 그가 무언가 끔찍한 상해를 입었음이 곧바로 드러났다. 몸의 각 부분 사이에는, 무기력한 정신이상자에게서조차 보이는 그런 목적의 통일성이 전혀 없는 듯했다. 얼굴이 드러나자 나는 그것이 바닥에 짓찧기라도 한 듯 끔찍하게 멍들어 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 사실 피웅덩이는 바로 그 얼굴의 상처에서 비롯된 것이었다. 시신 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 있던 간병인이 우리가 그를 돌려 눕힐 때 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"선생님, 제 생각에 이 사람 등뼈가 부러진 것 같습니다. 보세요, 오른팔과 오른다리, 그리고 얼굴 한쪽 전체가 마비되어 있어요.\" 어떻게 이런 일이 일어날 수 있었는지 간병인은 도무지 짐작하지 못했다. 그는 몹시 당황한 듯 눈썹을 잔뜩 모으며 말했다.\n\n\"이 두 가지를 도무지 이해할 수가 없습니다. 자기 머리를 바닥에 찧어서 얼굴을 저렇게 만들 수는 있죠. 에버스필드 정신병원에서 어떤 젊은 여자가 누가 손쓸 새도 없이 그러는 걸 한 번 본 적이 있습니다. 그리고 침대에서 떨어질 때 이상하게 꺾이면 목이 부러질 수도 있겠죠. 하지만 죽었다 깨어나도, 이 두 가지가 어떻게 같이 일어났는지는 도무지 상상이 안 됩니다. 등뼈가 부러졌으면 머리를 찧을 수 없었을 테고, 침대에서 떨어지기 전에 얼굴이 저랬다면 그 자국이 남아 있었을 테니까요.\" 나는 그에게 말했다.\n\n\"판 헬싱 박사님께 가서, 즉시 이리로 와주십사고 청하게. 한순간도 지체 없이 모셔와야겠네.\" 그 사람은 달려나갔고, 몇 분도 안 되어 교수가 실내복에 슬리퍼 차림으로 나타났다. 그는 바닥에 쓰러진 렌필드를 보자 잠시 그를 날카롭게 살피더니 나를 돌아보았다. 내 눈빛에서 내 생각을 알아챈 모양으로, 그는 분명 간병인의 귀를 의식하여 아주 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"아, 가엾은 사고로군! 이 사람은 아주 세심한 간호와 많은 보살핌이 필요할 걸세. 내가 직접 자네와 함께 있겠네. 다만 먼저 옷을 갖춰 입어야겠어. 자네가 여기 남아 있으면 몇 분 뒤에 합류하겠네.\"\n\n환자는 이제 코를 골듯 거친 숨을 몰아쉬고 있었고, 무언가 끔찍한 상해를 입었음을 쉽게 알아볼 수 있었다. 판 헬싱은 놀라운 신속함으로 수술 도구함을 들고 돌아왔다. 그는 분명 골똘히 생각하여 마음을 정한 듯, 환자를 살펴보기도 전에 나에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"간병인을 내보내게. 수술 뒤에 이 사람이 정신을 차렸을 때, 우리만 그와 함께 있어야 하네.\" 그래서 나는 말했다.\n\n\"지금은 이만하면 됐네, 시먼스. 당장 할 수 있는 건 다 했어. 자네는 순회를 도는 게 좋겠네. 판 헬싱 박사님이 수술을 하실 걸세. 어디든 이상한 일이 있으면 즉시 알려주게.\"\n\n그 사람이 물러가자, 우리는 환자를 엄밀히 진찰하기 시작했다. 얼굴의 상처는 표면적인 것이었고, 진짜 부상은 운동 영역까지 곧장 뻗은 두개골 함몰 골절이었다. 교수는 잠시 생각하더니 말했다.\n\n\"압력을 줄이고, 가능한 한 정상 상태로 되돌려놓아야 하네. 출혈이 번지는 속도가 이 사람 부상의 끔찍한 성격을 보여주고 있어. 운동 영역 전체가 침범당한 것 같네. 뇌의 출혈이 빠르게 늘어날 테니, 당장 두개골에 구멍을 뚫지 않으면 너무 늦어버릴지도 모르네.\" 그가 말하고 있을 때 문을 부드럽게 두드리는 소리가 났다. 내가 가서 문을 여니 바깥 복도에 잠옷과 슬리퍼 차림의 아서와 퀸시가 서 있었다. 전자가 먼저 말했다.\n\n\"자네 사람이 판 헬싱 박사님을 부르며 사고가 났다고 하는 걸 들었네. 그래서 퀸시를 깨웠지, 아니, 깨어 있었으니 그냥 불렀다는 게 맞겠군. 요즘은 무슨 일이 너무 빠르고 너무 기이하게 돌아가서 누구도 깊이 잠들 수가 없으니 말이야. 나는 내일 밤이면 지금까지와는 사정이 달라지리라고 생각해왔네. 우리는 지금까지 해온 것보다 좀 더 뒤를 돌아보고, 또 앞을 내다봐야 할 거야. 들어가도 되겠나?\" 나는 고개를 끄덕이고, 그들이 들어올 때까지 문을 잡아두었다가 다시 닫았다. 퀸시는 환자의 자세와 상태를 보고, 바닥의 끔찍한 웅덩이를 알아채자 나직이 말했다.\n\n\"맙소사! 이 사람한테 무슨 일이 일어난 건가? 가엾은, 가엾은 친구!\" 나는 그에게 간략히 사정을 일러주고, 수술 뒤에 그가 정신을 차릴 것으로 기대한다고, 적어도 잠깐 동안은 그러리라고 덧붙였다. 그는 곧 침대 가장자리에 가서 앉았고, 그 곁에 고달밍이 앉았다. 우리는 모두 참을성 있게 지켜보았다.\n\n\"우리는 기다리겠네.\" 판 헬싱이 말했다. \"두개골에 구멍을 뚫을 가장 좋은 자리를 정할 수 있을 만큼만, 가장 빠르고 완벽하게 핏덩이를 제거할 수 있도록 말일세. 출혈이 늘고 있는 게 분명하니까.\"\n\n우리가 기다린 그 몇 분은 무서울 만큼 더디게 흘렀다. 내 가슴은 끔찍하게 내려앉았고, 판 헬싱의 얼굴에서 나는 그 또한 앞으로 닥칠 일에 대해 어떤 두려움이나 불안을 느끼고 있음을 헤아렸다. 나는 렌필드가 입을 열어 할지도 모를 말이 두려웠다. 솔직히 생각하는 것조차 두려웠다. 그러나 무언가 다가오고 있다는 확신이 나를 사로잡았으니, 마치 죽음의 시계 소리를 들었다는 사람들에 관한 글을 읽었을 때와 같았다. 가엾은 사내의 숨결은 불규칙하게 헐떡이며 새어나왔다. 매 순간 그는 곧 눈을 뜨고 입을 열 것만 같았으나, 그러다가는 길게 코를 고는 듯한 숨이 이어지며 더 깊은 무감각 속으로 다시 빠져들곤 했다. 병상과 죽음에 익숙해진 나였지만, 이 긴장은 자라고 또 자라나 나를 짓눌렀다. 나는 내 심장이 뛰는 소리를 거의 들을 수 있을 정도였고, 관자놀이를 치고 지나가는 피가 망치질 소리처럼 울렸다. 마침내 침묵은 견디기 힘든 고통이 되었다. 나는 동료들을 한 사람씩 차례로 바라보았고, 상기된 얼굴과 땀 밴 이마에서 그들도 똑같은 고통을 견디고 있음을 알았다. 마치 머리 위 어딘가에서 어떤 무서운 종이 우리가 가장 예상치 못한 순간에 힘차게 울려퍼질 것만 같은, 신경을 곤두세우는 긴장이 우리 모두를 덮고 있었다.\n\n마침내 환자가 급속히 가라앉고 있음이 분명해지는 순간이 왔다. 그는 어느 순간에라도 죽을 수 있었다. 나는 교수를 올려다보았고, 그의 눈이 내 눈에 고정되어 있는 것을 알아챘다. 그는 얼굴을 엄숙하게 굳히며 말했다.\n\n\"지체할 시간이 없네. 이 사람의 말은 여러 목숨만큼의 값어치가 있을지도 모르네. 여기 서 있는 동안 줄곧 그렇게 생각했어. 어쩌면 한 영혼이 걸린 일일지도 모르네! 귀 바로 위쪽을 수술하겠네.\"\n\n그는 더 말하지 않고 수술을 시작했다. 잠시 동안 숨결은 계속 거칠게 코를 골았다. 그러다가 어찌나 긴 숨이 새어나오는지, 마치 가슴이 찢어질 것만 같았다. 갑자기 그가 눈을 떴고, 그 눈은 거칠고 무력한 응시에 못박혔다. 이것이 잠시 이어지더니, 곧 기쁜 놀라움으로 누그러졌고, 입술에서 안도의 한숨이 새어나왔다. 그는 경련하듯 몸을 움직였고, 그러면서 말했다.\n\n\"조용히 있겠습니다, 박사님. 저 사람들한테 구속복을 벗겨달라고 해주세요. 끔찍한 꿈을 꾸었는데, 그 탓에 너무 기운이 빠져서 움직일 수가 없어요. 제 얼굴이 왜 이렇죠? 온통 부어오른 것 같고, 지독하게 따끔거립니다.\" 그는 고개를 돌리려 했으나, 그렇게 애쓰는 것만으로도 눈빛이 다시 흐리멍덩해지는 듯해서 나는 그의 머리를 부드럽게 도로 뉘었다. 그러자 판 헬싱이 조용하고 엄숙한 어조로 말했다.\n\n\"꿈 이야기를 해주시오, 렌필드 씨.\" 그 목소리를 듣자 그의 얼굴은 일그러진 상처 속에서도 환해졌고, 그는 말했다.\n\n\"판 헬싱 박사님이시군요. 여기 와주시다니 정말 고맙습니다. 물을 좀 주세요, 입술이 말랐어요. 그러면 말씀드려 보겠습니다. 제가 꿈을 꾸었는데요.\" 그는 말을 멈추고 정신을 잃는 듯했고, 나는 조용히 퀸시를 불렀다. \"브랜디를 — 내 서재에 있네 — 어서!\" 그는 날 듯이 달려가 잔과 브랜디 병, 물병을 들고 돌아왔다. 우리가 바싹 마른 입술을 적셔주자 환자는 곧 되살아났다. 그러나 그 사이에도 그의 가엾은 상한 뇌는 계속 작동했던 모양으로, 완전히 정신을 차렸을 때 그는 내가 결코 잊지 못할 고뇌에 찬 혼란의 눈빛으로 나를 꿰뚫듯 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"내 자신을 속여서는 안 되지. 그건 꿈이 아니었어, 모두 가혹한 현실이었어.\" 그러더니 그의 눈은 방 안을 두리번거렸다. 침대 가장자리에 참을성 있게 앉아 있는 두 사람의 모습이 눈에 들어오자 그는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이미 확신하지 못했다 해도, 저 사람들을 보면 알 수 있었겠지.\" 한순간 그의 눈이 감겼다 — 고통이나 잠 때문이 아니라, 마치 자신의 모든 기능을 끌어모으려는 듯 의도적으로. 다시 눈을 떴을 때 그는 그때까지 보인 적 없는 더 큰 기운을 담아 다급하게 말했다.\n\n\"어서요, 박사님, 어서. 저는 죽어가고 있어요! 제게 남은 시간이 몇 분뿐임을 느낍니다. 그러고 나면 저는 죽음으로 — 아니, 더 나쁜 것으로 돌아가야 해요! 입술을 브랜디로 다시 적셔주세요. 죽기 전에, 아니 어쨌든 제 가엾은 으스러진 뇌가 죽기 전에 꼭 해야 할 말이 있습니다. 고맙습니다! 그날 밤, 박사님이 저를 두고 가신 뒤였어요, 제가 보내달라고 박사님께 애원했던 그날 밤이요. 그때 저는 말을 할 수 없었어요, 혀가 묶인 것 같았거든요. 하지만 그 점만 빼면 그때도 저는 지금만큼 제정신이었습니다. 박사님이 가신 뒤로 저는 오랫동안 절망의 고통 속에 있었어요, 몇 시간은 되는 것 같았죠. 그러다가 갑자기 평온이 찾아왔습니다. 머리가 다시 차갑게 식는 듯했고, 제가 어디에 있는지 깨달았어요. 저는 우리 집 뒤에서 개들이 짖는 소리를 들었지만, 그분이 계신 곳에서 나는 소리는 아니었어요!\" 그가 말하는 동안 판 헬싱의 눈은 한 번도 깜빡이지 않았으나, 그의 손이 뻗어나와 내 손과 마주쳐 꽉 움켜쥐었다. 그러나 그는 속내를 드러내지 않았다. 그는 가볍게 고개를 끄덕이며 낮은 목소리로 \"계속하시오\"라고 말했다. 렌필드는 말을 이어갔다.\n\n\"그분은 안개 속에서 창가로 다가왔어요, 전에도 자주 보았던 것처럼요. 하지만 그때는 형체가 또렷했어요 — 유령이 아니었고, 눈은 화가 난 사람처럼 사나웠습니다. 그분은 붉은 입으로 웃고 있었어요. 나무 띠 너머, 개들이 짖던 그쪽을 돌아보려고 고개를 돌렸을 때, 날카로운 흰 이빨이 달빛에 번득였습니다. 저는 처음엔 그분에게 들어오라고 청하지 않으려 했어요, 그분이 들어오고 싶어 한다는 걸 알면서도요 — 줄곧 그러길 바라왔던 것처럼요. 그러더니 그분이 제게 여러 가지를 약속하기 시작했어요 — 말이 아니라, 그걸 직접 보여주면서요.\" 교수가 한마디로 그의 말을 끊었다.\n\n\"어떻게 말이오?\"\n\"그것들을 실제로 일어나게 해서요. 햇빛이 비칠 때 파리를 들여보내곤 했던 것처럼요. 날개에 강철빛과 사파이어빛이 도는 크고 살진 놈들이요. 그리고 밤이면 등에 해골과 십자 뼈 무늬가 있는 큰 나방들도요.\" 판 헬싱은 그에게 고개를 끄덕이면서 나에게 무심결에 속삭였다.\n\n\"스핑크스나방속의 아케론티아 아트로포스 — 자네들이 '죽음의머리나방'이라 부르는 것 말일세?\" 환자는 멈추지 않고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그러더니 그분이 속삭이기 시작했어요. '쥐, 쥐, 쥐다! 수백, 수천, 수백만 마리, 하나하나가 다 생명이지. 그것들을 잡아먹을 개들도, 고양이들도. 모두 생명이야! 모두 붉은 피이고, 그 안에 여러 해의 생명이 담겨 있지. 그저 윙윙대는 파리 따위가 아니라고!' 저는 그분이 무엇을 할 수 있는지 보고 싶어서 그를 비웃었어요. 그러자 어두운 나무들 너머, 그분의 집에서 개들이 울부짖었습니다. 그분은 저를 창가로 손짓해 불렀어요. 저는 일어나 밖을 내다보았고, 그분은 두 손을 들어올리며 아무 말도 쓰지 않고 무언가를 부르는 듯했습니다. 어두운 덩어리가 풀밭 위로 퍼지며 불꽃의 형상처럼 밀려왔어요. 그러더니 그분이 안개를 좌우로 움직이자, 저는 거기에 눈이 빨갛게 타오르는 쥐가 수천 마리나 있는 것을 볼 수 있었습니다 — 그분의 눈처럼, 다만 더 작았죠. 그분이 손을 들어올리자 그것들이 모두 멈췄어요. 그리고 그분이 이렇게 말하는 듯했습니다. '이 모든 생명을 너에게 주마, 그래, 그리고 더 많고 더 큰 것을, 헤아릴 수 없는 세월에 걸쳐 주리라, 네가 엎드려 나를 경배한다면!' 그러더니 붉은 구름이, 피 빛깔 같은 것이 제 눈앞을 가리는 듯했고, 제가 무얼 하는지도 모르는 채 저는 어느새 창틀을 열고 그분께 이렇게 말하고 있었습니다. '들어오세요, 주인님!' 쥐들은 모두 사라졌지만, 그분은 겨우 한 치쯤 열린 창틈으로 방 안에 미끄러져 들어왔어요 — 마치 달님이 늘 아주 작은 틈으로 들어와 온전한 크기와 광휘로 제 앞에 서곤 했던 것처럼요.\"\n\n그의 목소리가 약해져서 나는 브랜디로 다시 그의 입술을 적셔주었고, 그는 말을 이었다. 그러나 그 사이에도 그의 기억은 계속 작동한 듯, 이야기는 더 앞으로 나아가 있었다. 나는 그를 다시 본래 지점으로 불러오려 했으나, 판 헬싱이 나에게 속삭였다. \"계속하게 두게. 끊지 말게. 그는 되돌아갈 수 없으니, 한번 생각의 실마리를 놓치면 아예 이어가지 못할지도 모르네.\" 그는 말을 이어갔다.\n\n\"하루 종일 저는 그분에게서 소식이 오기를 기다렸지만, 그분은 아무것도, 쉬파리 한 마리조차 보내주지 않았어요. 그래서 달이 떠올랐을 때 저는 그분에게 꽤 화가 나 있었습니다. 그분이 창문이 닫혀 있는데도 그 틈으로 미끄러져 들어오고, 노크조차 하지 않자 저는 그분에게 단단히 화가 났어요. 그분은 저를 비웃었고, 그 흰 얼굴이 붉은 눈을 번득이며 안개 속에서 내다보았어요. 그러고는 마치 이곳 전체가 자기 것이고 저는 아무것도 아니라는 듯이 행동했습니다. 제 곁을 지날 때 냄새조차 전과 같지 않았어요. 저는 그분을 붙잡을 수가 없었어요. 어쩐지 하커 부인이 방으로 들어온 것 같다고 생각했습니다.\"\n\n침대에 앉아 있던 두 사람이 일어나 다가와서는, 그가 볼 수 없도록 그의 뒤에 섰으나, 더 잘 들을 수 있는 자리였다. 두 사람 모두 말이 없었으나, 교수는 흠칫 놀라며 떨었다. 그러나 그의 얼굴은 한층 더 굳고 엄숙해졌다. 렌필드는 알아채지 못한 채 말을 이었다.\n\n\"오늘 오후에 하커 부인이 저를 보러 들어왔을 때, 부인은 예전 같지 않았어요. 마치 찻주전자에 물을 부어 희석한 뒤의 차 같았죠.\" 이 대목에서 우리는 모두 움찔했으나 아무도 입을 떼지 않았다. 그는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"부인이 입을 열기 전까지는 거기 있는 줄도 몰랐어요. 게다가 부인은 예전 같아 보이지 않았습니다. 저는 창백한 사람들은 좋아하지 않아요. 피가 많은 사람들이 좋은데, 부인은 그 피가 다 빠져나가 버린 것 같았어요. 그땐 거기까지 생각하지 못했지만, 부인이 떠나고 나서 저는 생각하기 시작했고, 그분이 부인에게서 생명을 빼앗아 가고 있었다는 걸 알자 미칠 듯이 화가 났습니다.\" 나도 그랬듯 다른 사람들도 떨고 있는 것을 나는 느낄 수 있었으나, 그 밖에는 우리 모두 가만히 있었다. \"그래서 오늘 밤 그분이 왔을 때 저는 그분을 맞을 채비가 되어 있었어요. 저는 안개가 슬며시 들어오는 것을 보고 그것을 꽉 붙잡았습니다. 미친 사람들은 비정상적인 힘을 낸다고 들었고, 저도 미친 사람이라는 걸 — 적어도 때때로는 — 알고 있었으니, 제 힘을 쓰기로 작정했지요. 그래요, 그분도 그것을 느꼈어요, 저와 맞붙어 싸우려고 안개에서 나와야 했으니까요. 저는 꽉 붙들었어요. 그리고 제가 이길 것 같다고 생각했습니다, 그분이 부인의 생명을 더는 빼앗아 가게 두지 않을 작정이었거든요 — 그분의 눈을 보기 전까지는요. 그 눈이 저를 태워버리듯 노려보자, 제 힘은 물처럼 빠져나갔어요. 그분은 그 사이로 빠져나갔고, 제가 그분에게 매달리려 하자 그분이 저를 들어올려 내동댕이쳤습니다. 제 눈앞에 붉은 구름이 끼었고, 천둥 같은 소리가 났으며, 안개는 문 밑으로 슬며시 빠져나가는 듯했어요.\" 그의 목소리는 점점 희미해졌고 숨결은 더 거칠게 코를 골았다. 판 헬싱이 본능적으로 일어섰다.\n\n\"이제 우리는 최악의 사정을 알게 되었네.\" 그가 말했다. \"그자가 여기 있고, 우리는 그자의 목적을 알아. 아직 너무 늦지 않았을지도 모르네. 무장하세 — 지난밤과 똑같이, 다만 한순간도 허비하지 말게. 잠시도 지체할 틈이 없네.\" 우리의 두려움을, 아니 우리의 확신을 말로 옮길 필요는 없었다 — 우리는 그것을 모두 함께 나누고 있었으니까. 우리는 모두 서둘러 각자의 방에서, 백작의 집에 들어갔을 때 지녔던 것과 똑같은 물건들을 가져왔다. 교수는 이미 그것을 준비해두고 있었고, 우리가 복도에서 마주치자 그것들을 의미심장하게 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"이것들은 결코 나를 떠나지 않네. 그리고 이 불행한 일이 끝날 때까지 떠나지 않을 걸세. 자네들도 현명하게 굴게, 친구들. 우리가 상대하는 것은 보통 적이 아니야. 아아! 아아! 그 사랑스러운 미나 부인이 고통받아야 하다니!\" 그는 말을 멈추었다. 그의 목소리는 떨리고 있었고, 나 자신의 가슴속에서는 분노가 앞섰는지 공포가 앞섰는지 나도 알 수 없었다.\n\n하커 부부의 방문 밖에서 우리는 걸음을 멈추었다. 아트와 퀸시가 머뭇거렸고, 후자가 말했다.\n\n\"부인을 깨워야 할까요?\"\n\n\"그래야 하네.\" 판 헬싱이 단호하게 말했다. \"문이 잠겨 있으면 내가 부수고 들어가겠네.\"\n\n\"부인을 몹시 놀라게 하지 않을까요? 숙녀의 방에 부수고 들어가는 건 예사로운 일이 아니니까요!\"\n\n판 헬싱이 엄숙하게 말했다. \"자네 말은 언제나 옳네. 하지만 이건 사느냐 죽느냐의 문제야. 의사에게는 모든 방이 다 같은 법이지. 설령 그렇지 않다 해도, 오늘 밤만큼은 나에게 모두가 하나일세. 존, 내가 손잡이를 돌렸을 때 문이 열리지 않거든, 자네가 어깨를 들이대고 밀게. 자네들도 함께. 자, 이제!\"\n\n그는 말하면서 손잡이를 돌렸으나 문은 꿈쩍하지 않았다. 우리는 몸을 던져 문을 들이받았고, 쾅 소리와 함께 문이 활짝 열리며 우리는 거의 곤두박질치듯 방 안으로 쓰러져 들어갔다. 교수는 실제로 넘어졌고, 그가 두 손과 무릎을 짚고 몸을 일으키는 너머로 나는 방 안을 보았다. 내가 본 것은 나를 소름끼치게 했다. 목덜미의 털이 곤두서는 듯했고, 심장이 멎는 것만 같았다.\n\n달빛이 어찌나 밝던지 두껍고 노란 블라인드를 통해서도 방 안은 알아볼 수 있을 만큼 환했다. 창가의 침대에는 조너선 하커가 누워 있었는데, 얼굴이 상기된 채 혼수상태에 빠진 듯 거칠게 숨을 쉬고 있었다. 침대의 가까운 쪽 가장자리에 바깥을 향해 무릎 꿇고 있는 것은 흰옷 차림의 그의 아내였다. 그녀 곁에는 검은 옷을 입은 키 크고 야윈 남자가 서 있었다. 그의 얼굴은 우리 쪽에서 돌려져 있었으나, 우리는 보는 순간 모두 그가 백작임을 알아보았다 — 모든 면에서, 심지어 이마의 흉터까지도. 그는 왼손으로 하커 부인의 두 손을 함께 붙들어 그녀의 팔을 한껏 당긴 채 떼어놓고 있었고, 오른손으로는 그녀의 목덜미를 움켜쥐어 그녀의 얼굴을 자기 가슴에 짓누르고 있었다. 그녀의 흰 잠옷은 피로 얼룩져 있었고, 찢겨 벌어진 옷 사이로 드러난 남자의 맨가슴에서는 가느다란 핏줄기가 흘러내리고 있었다. 두 사람의 자세는, 새끼 고양이에게 우유를 억지로 먹이려고 그 코를 우유 접시에 짓누르는 것과 끔찍하게도 닮아 있었다. 우리가 방 안으로 들이닥치자 백작은 얼굴을 돌렸고, 내가 묘사로만 들어왔던 그 지옥 같은 표정이 그 얼굴로 뛰어드는 듯했다. 그의 눈은 악마 같은 격정으로 붉게 타올랐고, 희고 매부리 같은 코의 큰 콧구멍은 활짝 벌어져 그 가장자리가 떨렸으며, 피가 뚝뚝 떨어지는 입의 도톰한 입술 뒤로 희고 날카로운 이빨이 야수의 그것처럼 맞부딪쳤다. 그는 마치 높은 곳에서 내던지듯 자기 희생자를 침대 위로 홱 뿌리치고는, 몸을 돌려 우리를 향해 덤벼들었다. 그러나 이때 교수는 이미 몸을 일으켜, 성체병(聖體餠)이 든 봉투를 그자를 향해 내밀고 있었다. 백작은 가엾은 루시가 무덤 밖에서 그랬던 것처럼 갑자기 멈춰서더니 움츠러들며 물러섰다. 우리가 십자고상을 치켜들고 다가가자 그자는 점점 더 뒤로 움츠러들었다. 거대한 검은 구름이 하늘을 가로질러 떠가면서 달빛이 갑자기 사라졌고, 퀸시가 켠 성냥불 아래 가스등이 확 타오르자, 우리 눈에는 희미한 수증기 외에는 아무것도 보이지 않았다. 우리가 바라보는 동안 그것은 문 밑으로 스며 빠져나갔는데, 문은 부서지며 활짝 열렸던 반동으로 도로 본래 자리로 돌아와 있었다. 판 헬싱과 아트와 나는 하커 부인 쪽으로 나아갔다. 이때쯤 그녀는 숨을 들이쉬었고, 그와 함께 어찌나 거칠고, 어찌나 귀를 찢는 듯하고, 어찌나 절망적인 비명을 질렀던지, 그 소리가 죽는 날까지 내 귓속에 울릴 것만 같은 생각이 지금도 든다. 몇 초 동안 그녀는 무력한 자세로 흐트러진 채 누워 있었다. 그녀의 얼굴은 끔찍하리만치 창백했고, 입술과 뺨과 턱에 묻은 피가 그 창백함을 한층 도드라지게 했다. 목에서는 가느다란 핏줄기가 흘러내렸고, 두 눈은 공포로 미쳐 있었다. 그러다 그녀는 가엾게 으스러진 두 손을 얼굴 앞으로 가져갔는데, 그 흰 손에는 백작의 끔찍한 움켜쥠이 남긴 붉은 자국이 찍혀 있었다. 그 손 뒤에서 낮고 황량한 흐느낌이 새어나왔는데, 그 소리에 비하면 그 끔찍한 비명조차 끝없는 슬픔의 순간적인 표현일 뿐인 듯했다. 판 헬싱이 앞으로 나아가 그녀의 몸 위로 이불을 부드럽게 덮어주었고, 한편 아트는 한순간 절망에 잠겨 그녀의 얼굴을 바라보다가 방 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다. 판 헬싱이 나에게 속삭였다.\n\n\"조너선은 우리가 흡혈귀가 일으킬 수 있다고 알고 있는 그런 혼수상태에 빠져 있네. 가엾은 미나 부인은 스스로 정신을 차릴 때까지 몇 분 동안은 우리가 어찌해볼 수가 없어. 나는 이 사람을 깨워야겠네!\" 그는 수건 끝을 찬물에 적셔 그것으로 그의 얼굴을 톡톡 두드리기 시작했다. 그동안 내내 그의 아내는 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채, 듣고 있기에도 가슴이 미어지는 모습으로 흐느꼈다. 나는 블라인드를 올리고 창밖을 내다보았다. 달빛이 환했다. 내가 바라보는 동안 퀸시 모리스가 잔디밭을 가로질러 달려가 커다란 주목 그늘에 몸을 숨기는 것이 보였다. 그가 왜 이러는지 짐작이 가지 않아 의아했으나, 바로 그 순간 하커가 부분적으로 의식을 되찾으며 다급하게 내지르는 소리를 듣고 나는 침대 쪽으로 돌아섰다. 그의 얼굴에는, 그럴 만도 했지만, 거친 경악의 빛이 어려 있었다. 그는 몇 초 동안 멍한 듯하더니, 곧 완전한 의식이 한꺼번에 그를 덮치는 듯 벌떡 일어났다. 그 빠른 움직임에 그의 아내가 깨어났고, 마치 그를 끌어안으려는 듯 두 팔을 뻗으며 그에게로 돌아섰다. 그러나 곧 그녀는 두 팔을 다시 거두어 두 팔꿈치를 모으고 손을 얼굴 앞에 가져다 댄 채, 침대가 다 흔들리도록 부들부들 떨었다.\n\n\"하느님 맙소사, 이게 대체 무슨 일이오?\" 하커가 외쳤다. \"수어드 박사, 판 헬싱 박사, 이게 뭡니까? 무슨 일이 일어난 거죠? 뭐가 잘못된 겁니까? 미나, 여보, 이게 뭐요? 저 피는 무슨 뜻이오? 하느님, 하느님! 결국 이렇게 되고 말았단 말입니까!\" 그러고는 무릎을 세우며 두 손을 거칠게 맞부딪쳤다. \"하느님, 우리를 도우소서! 이 사람을 도우소서! 오, 이 사람을 도우소서!\" 그는 빠른 동작으로 침대에서 뛰어내려 옷을 걸치기 시작했다 — 당장의 행동이 필요한 상황에 그의 안에 있는 사내가 온통 깨어난 것이다. \"무슨 일이 일어난 겁니까? 전부 다 말해주세요!\" 그는 멈추지 않고 외쳤다. \"판 헬싱 박사님, 박사님은 미나를 사랑하시잖습니까, 저는 압니다. 오, 이 사람을 구하려면 뭐든 해주세요. 아직 너무 멀리 갔을 리는 없습니다. 제가 그자를 찾는 동안 이 사람을 지켜주세요!\" 그의 아내는 공포와 전율과 비탄 속에서도 그에게 닥칠 어떤 분명한 위험을 알아챘다. 그녀는 곧바로 자신의 슬픔을 잊고 그를 붙들며 외쳤다.\n\n\"안 돼요! 안 돼요! 조너선, 당신은 나를 두고 가서는 안 돼요. 하느님도 아시지만, 오늘 밤 나는 충분히 고통받았어요. 그자가 당신을 해칠지도 모른다는 두려움까지 더해서는 안 돼요. 당신은 나와 함께 있어야 해요. 당신을 지켜줄 이 친구분들과 함께 있어요!\" 그녀가 말하는 동안 그녀의 표정은 광란에 빠진 듯했고, 그가 그녀에게 굴복하자 그녀는 그를 침대 곁에 앉도록 끌어당겨 그에게 격렬하게 매달렸다.\n\n판 헬싱과 나는 두 사람을 모두 진정시키려 애썼다. 교수는 자신의 작은 금빛 십자고상을 들어올리며 놀랍도록 침착하게 말했다.\n\n\"두려워하지 마시오, 부인. 우리가 여기 있소. 그리고 이것이 부인 가까이 있는 한 어떤 사악한 것도 다가올 수 없소. 부인은 오늘 밤 안전하오. 우리는 차분하게 마음을 가다듬고 함께 의논해야 하오.\" 그녀는 몸을 떨더니 입을 다물고, 남편의 가슴에 고개를 떨구었다. 그녀가 고개를 들었을 때, 그의 흰 잠옷에는 그녀의 입술이 닿았던 자리와, 목의 가느다랗게 벌어진 상처에서 핏방울이 떨어진 자리에 피가 묻어 있었다. 그것을 본 순간 그녀는 낮은 흐느낌과 함께 뒤로 물러서며, 흐느낌에 목이 메어 속삭였다.\n\n\"부정해, 부정해졌어! 나는 더는 그이를 만져서도 입맞춰서도 안 돼. 오, 이제 내가 그이의 가장 무서운 적이 되고, 그이가 가장 두려워해야 할 까닭이 바로 나라니.\" 이에 그는 단호하게 말했다.\n\n\"무슨 그런 소릴, 미나. 그런 말을 듣다니 나로서는 수치스럽소. 당신에 대해서 그런 말을 듣지 않겠소. 당신에게서도 그런 말을 듣지 않겠소. 만일 내 어떤 행위나 의지로든 우리 사이를 가로막는 무언가가 생긴다면, 하느님께서 내가 받아 마땅한 대로 나를 심판하시고, 이 시각보다 더 쓰라린 고통으로 나를 벌하시기를!\" 그는 두 팔을 뻗어 그녀를 가슴에 끌어안았고, 그녀는 한동안 그 품에 안겨 흐느꼈다. 그는 그녀의 숙인 머리 너머로 우리를 바라보았는데, 떨리는 콧구멍 위로 그의 눈은 젖어 깜빡이고 있었고, 입은 강철처럼 굳게 다물려 있었다. 한참 뒤 그녀의 흐느낌은 뜸해지고 더 희미해졌으며, 그러자 그는 나에게, 내가 보기에 그의 신경의 힘을 극도로 시험하는 듯한 애써 꾸민 침착함으로 말했다.\n\n\"이제, 수어드 박사, 모든 걸 다 말해주세요. 큰 줄거리는 너무도 잘 알고 있습니다. 그동안 있었던 일을 다 말해주세요.\" 나는 일어난 일을 그대로 그에게 일러주었고, 그는 겉보기에는 무덤덤하게 들었다. 그러나 백작의 무자비한 손이 그의 아내를, 그 끔찍하고 소름끼치는 자세로 입을 그의 가슴의 벌어진 상처에 댄 채 붙들고 있었다고 내가 말했을 때, 그의 콧구멍은 실룩였고 두 눈은 불타올랐다. 그 순간에조차 내 흥미를 끈 것은, 흰빛으로 굳은 격정이 숙인 머리 너머로 경련하듯 일렁이는 동안, 그 손은 헝클어진 머리칼을 다정하고 사랑스럽게 쓰다듬고 있었다는 점이었다. 내가 막 이야기를 마쳤을 때 퀸시와 고달밍이 문을 두드렸다. 그들은 우리의 부름에 따라 들어왔다. 판 헬싱이 나를 의아한 눈빛으로 바라보았다. 나는 그것이, 가능하다면 이 불행한 부부의 생각을 서로에게서, 또 그들 자신에게서 돌려놓는 데에 그들의 등장을 이용하자는 뜻으로 이해했다. 그래서 그에게 동의하여 고개를 끄덕이자, 그는 그들에게 무엇을 보았고 무엇을 했는지 물었다. 이에 고달밍 경이 대답했다.\n\n\"복도에서도, 우리 방 어디에서도 그자를 찾을 수 없었네. 서재도 들여다봤는데, 거기 있었던 흔적은 있었지만 그자는 가버렸더군. 다만 그자가——\" 그는 침대 위의 가엾게 늘어진 모습을 바라보며 갑자기 말을 멈추었다. 판 헬싱이 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"계속하게, 아서. 여기서는 더 이상의 은폐가 필요 없네. 이제 우리의 희망은 모든 것을 아는 데에 있어. 거리낌없이 말하게!\" 그래서 아트는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그자가 거기 있었어. 그래봐야 몇 초 동안이었겠지만, 그자가 그곳을 엉망진창으로 만들어놓았더군. 원고는 모조리 불태워졌고, 푸른 불꽃이 흰 잿더미 사이에서 일렁이고 있었네. 자네 축음기의 원통들도 불 속에 던져졌고, 밀랍이 불길을 키웠더군.\" 여기서 나는 끼어들었다. \"다행히 금고 안에 다른 사본이 있어!\" 그의 얼굴은 잠시 환해졌으나, 말을 이으며 다시 어두워졌다. \"그러고 나서 나는 아래층으로 달려갔지만, 그자의 자취는 찾을 수 없었네. 렌필드의 방을 들여다봤는데, 거기엔 아무 흔적도 없었어, 다만——!\" 그는 다시 말을 멈추었다. \"계속하게.\" 하커가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. 그러자 그는 고개를 숙이고 혀로 입술을 적시며 덧붙였다. \"다만 그 가엾은 친구가 죽어 있다는 것만 빼고는.\" 하커 부인이 고개를 들어 우리를 한 사람씩 번갈아 바라보며 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"하느님의 뜻이 이루어지기를!\" 나는 아트가 무언가를 감추고 있다는 느낌을 떨칠 수 없었으나, 그것이 어떤 의도가 있어서라고 여겼기에 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 판 헬싱이 모리스 쪽으로 돌아서며 물었다.\n\n\"그리고 자네는, 퀸시, 할 이야기가 좀 있나?\"\n\n\"조금요.\" 그가 대답했다. \"결국엔 큰일이 될지도 모르지만, 지금으로선 뭐라 말할 수가 없습니다. 가능하다면 백작이 집을 떠나 어디로 갈지 알아두는 게 좋겠다고 생각했어요. 그자를 보지는 못했지만, 박쥐 한 마리가 렌필드의 창문에서 날아올라 서쪽으로 펄럭이며 가는 걸 보았습니다. 저는 그자가 어떤 형체로든 카팩스로 돌아갈 거라고 예상했지만, 분명 다른 은신처를 찾아간 모양입니다. 오늘 밤에는 돌아오지 않을 겁니다. 동쪽 하늘이 붉어지고 있고 새벽이 가까웠으니까요. 우리는 내일 움직여야 합니다!\"\n\n그는 마지막 말을 이를 악문 채 내뱉었다. 아마 이삼 분쯤 침묵이 흘렀고, 나는 우리 심장이 뛰는 소리가 들리는 것만 같은 기분이 들었다. 그때 판 헬싱이 하커 부인의 머리에 아주 다정하게 손을 얹으며 말했다.\n\n\"이제, 미나 부인 — 가엾고, 사랑스럽고, 사랑스러운 미나 부인 — 무슨 일이 있었는지 정확하게 말해주시오. 하느님도 아시지만 나는 부인이 괴로워하기를 바라지 않소. 그러나 우리가 모든 것을 알아야 할 필요가 있소. 이제 그 어느 때보다도, 모든 일을 빠르고 날카롭게, 그리고 목숨을 건 진지함으로 해내야 하기 때문이오. 가능하다면 모든 것을 끝내야 할 날이 우리에게 가까이 다가왔소. 그리고 지금이야말로 우리가 살아남아 알아낼 수 있는 기회요.\"\n\n가엾고 사랑스러운 부인은 몸을 떨었고, 그녀가 남편을 더 가까이 끌어안고 그의 가슴에 고개를 점점 더 낮게 묻을 때 나는 그녀의 신경이 팽팽하게 긴장하는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그러다 그녀는 당당하게 고개를 들고 한 손을 판 헬싱에게 내밀었고, 그는 그 손을 잡아 몸을 굽혀 경건하게 입맞춘 뒤 꼭 쥐었다. 다른 한 손은 남편의 손에 맞잡혀 있었고, 남편은 다른 한 팔로 그녀를 보호하듯 감싸안고 있었다. 분명 생각을 가다듬는 듯한 침묵 끝에 그녀는 입을 열었다.\n\n\"저는 박사님께서 친절하게 주신 수면제를 먹었지만, 오랫동안 약이 듣지 않았어요. 오히려 더 말똥말똥해지는 것 같았고, 무수히 많은 끔찍한 환상이 제 마음속으로 밀려들기 시작했어요 — 모두가 죽음과 흡혈귀, 피와 고통과 고난에 얽힌 것들이었죠.\" 그녀의 남편이 자기도 모르게 신음하자 그녀는 그를 돌아보며 다정하게 말했다. \"괴로워하지 말아요, 여보. 당신은 용감하고 굳세야 해요, 그리고 이 끔찍한 일을 제가 헤쳐나가도록 도와줘야 해요. 이 무서운 일을 말로 옮기는 것이 제게 얼마나 큰 노력이 드는지 당신이 안다면, 제가 당신의 도움을 얼마나 필요로 하는지 이해할 거예요. 자, 저는 약이 제게 조금이라도 들으려면 제 의지로 약효를 도와야겠다고 생각했어요. 그래서 단단히 마음먹고 잠을 청했죠. 아니나 다를까, 곧 잠이 든 모양이에요, 그 뒤로는 아무것도 기억나지 않으니까요. 조너선이 들어온 것도 저를 깨우지 못했어요. 다음에 기억나는 순간에 그이가 제 곁에 누워 있었거든요. 방 안에는 전에 제가 보았던 것과 똑같은 가느다란 흰 안개가 있었어요. 그런데 박사님들이 이걸 아시는지 지금은 기억이 안 나네요. 나중에 보여드릴 제 일기에 적혀 있을 거예요. 저는 전에 찾아왔던 것과 똑같은 어렴풋한 공포와, 무언가가 곁에 있다는 똑같은 느낌을 받았어요. 조너선을 깨우려고 돌아섰지만, 그이가 어찌나 깊이 잠들었던지 마치 수면제를 먹은 게 제가 아니라 그이인 것만 같았어요. 깨우려 했지만 깨울 수가 없었죠. 그래서 저는 큰 두려움에 사로잡혀, 겁에 질린 채 주위를 둘러보았어요. 그러자 정말로 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았어요. 침대 곁에, 마치 안개에서 걸어나온 듯이 — 아니, 차라리 안개가 그의 형체로 바뀐 듯이, 안개가 완전히 사라져버렸으니까요 — 키 크고 야윈, 온통 검은 옷차림의 남자가 서 있었어요. 다른 분들의 묘사로 저는 그를 단번에 알아보았죠. 그 밀랍 같은 얼굴, 빛이 가느다란 흰 선을 그리며 떨어지던 높이 솟은 매부리코, 날카로운 흰 이빨이 사이로 드러나 보이는 벌어진 붉은 입술, 그리고 휘트비의 성 마리아 교회 창문에 비친 석양에서 보았던 듯한 그 붉은 눈. 또한 저는 조너선이 그를 내리쳤던 이마의 붉은 흉터도 알아보았어요. 한순간 제 심장이 멎었고, 비명을 지르고 싶었으나 몸이 마비된 채였어요. 그 멈춤 속에서 그는 일종의 날카롭고 베는 듯한 속삭임으로 말하며, 말하는 동안 조너선을 가리켰어요.\n\n'조용히 해라! 소리를 내면 이자를 잡아 네 눈앞에서 그 머리통을 박살내버리겠다.' 저는 소름이 끼쳤고, 너무 얼떨떨해서 아무것도 하거나 말할 수가 없었어요. 그는 비웃는 미소를 지으며 한 손을 제 어깨에 얹어 저를 꽉 붙들고는, 다른 손으로 제 목을 드러내며 이렇게 말했어요. '먼저, 내 수고에 대한 보답으로 약간의 원기를 보충해야지. 너는 가만히 있는 게 좋을 거다. 네 핏줄이 내 갈증을 달래준 게 이번이 처음도, 두번째도 아니니까!' 저는 얼떨떨했고, 이상하게도 그를 막고 싶지가 않았어요. 짐작건대, 그자의 손길이 희생자에게 닿으면 으레 그렇게 되는 것이, 그 끔찍한 저주의 일부인가 봐요. 그리고 오, 하느님, 하느님, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서! 그는 악취 나는 입술을 제 목에 갖다 댔어요!\" 그녀의 남편이 다시 신음했다. 그녀는 그의 손을 더 꽉 쥐고, 마치 다친 사람이 그인 양 가엾다는 듯 그를 바라보며 말을 이었다.\n\n\"저는 기운이 빠져나가는 것을 느꼈고, 반쯤 정신을 잃은 상태였어요. 이 끔찍한 일이 얼마나 오래 이어졌는지는 모르겠지만, 그가 그 더럽고 무섭게 비웃는 입을 떼기까지 긴 시간이 흐른 것만 같았어요. 그 입에서 신선한 피가 뚝뚝 떨어지는 것을 저는 보았어요!\" 그 기억이 한동안 그녀를 압도하는 듯, 그녀는 고개를 떨구며 그대로 주저앉을 뻔했으나 남편의 부축하는 팔이 그녀를 받쳤다. 그녀는 큰 노력으로 정신을 추스르고 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그러더니 그가 저를 비웃으며 말했어요. '그래서 너도 다른 자들처럼 네 머리로 내 머리에 맞서보겠다는 거냐. 너는 이자들이 나를 사냥하고 내 계획을 좌절시키는 걸 돕겠다는 거지! 이제 너는 알았고, 저들도 이미 일부는 알고 있으며, 머지않아 온전히 알게 될 것이다, 내 길을 가로막는다는 게 어떤 것인지를. 저들은 제 집 가까이에 쓸 데에나 기운을 아껴두었어야 했어. 저들이 나에게 — 저들이 태어나기 수백 년 전에 여러 나라를 호령하고, 그들을 위해 음모를 꾸미고, 그들을 위해 싸웠던 이 나에게 — 머리로 맞서는 동안, 나는 저들의 발밑을 파고들고 있었지. 그리고 저들이 가장 사랑하는 너는, 이제 나에게 내 살 중의 살이요, 내 피 중의 피요, 내 혈족 중의 혈족이며, 한동안은 나의 풍성한 포도즙 틀이요, 나중에는 나의 동반자이자 조력자가 될 것이다. 너는 차례로 복수를 받게 되리라. 저들 가운데 누구도 너의 필요를 섬기지 않을 자가 없을 테니까. 그러나 아직은 네가 한 짓에 대해 벌을 받아야지. 너는 나를 방해하는 일을 도왔으니, 이제 너는 내 부름에 와야 한다. 내 머리가 너에게 \"오라!\" 하고 말하면, 너는 내 명을 따르기 위해 뭍을 건너고 바다를 건너야 하리라. 그리고 그 목적을 위해, 이것을!' 그 말과 함께 그는 셔츠를 풀어헤치고, 길고 날카로운 손톱으로 자기 가슴의 핏줄을 갈랐어요. 피가 솟구치기 시작하자, 그는 한 손으로 제 두 손을 붙잡아 꽉 쥐고, 다른 손으로는 제 목을 움켜쥐어 제 입을 그 상처에 짓눌렀어요. 그래서 저는 숨이 막히거나, 아니면 그——을 조금 삼키거나 해야만 했어요. 오 하느님! 하느님! 제가 무슨 짓을 한 건가요? 평생 온순함과 의로움 속에서 걸으려 애써온 제가, 이런 운명을 받아 마땅한 무슨 짓을 했단 말입니까. 하느님, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서! 죽음보다 더한 위험에 처한 가엾은 영혼을 굽어살피소서. 그리고 자비로이, 이 여인을 아끼는 이들을 불쌍히 여기소서!\" 그러더니 그녀는 더럽혀진 것을 씻어내려는 듯 자기 입술을 문지르기 시작했다.\n\n그녀가 그 끔찍한 이야기를 하는 동안 동쪽 하늘이 밝아오기 시작했고, 모든 것이 점점 더 또렷해졌다. 하커는 가만히, 조용히 있었다. 그러나 그 무서운 이야기가 이어지는 동안 그의 얼굴 위로 잿빛이 번졌고, 아침 빛 속에서 그것은 점점 더 짙어져, 다가오는 새벽의 첫 붉은 줄기가 솟아오를 무렵에는 하얗게 센 머리칼을 배경으로 그 살갗이 거뭇하게 도드라져 보였다.\n\n우리는 함께 모여 행동에 나설 방도를 의논할 수 있을 때까지, 우리 가운데 한 사람이 이 불행한 부부의 부름이 닿는 곳에 머물기로 정해두었다.\n\n이것만은 분명하다. 오늘 떠오르는 해는, 그 나날의 운행이 그리는 그 광대한 궤도 어디에서도 이보다 더 비참한 집을 비추지 못하리라.\n\n# JONATHAN HARKER’S JOURNAL\n\n「조너선 하커의 일기」\n10월 3일. — 무언가 하지 않으면 미쳐버릴 것 같아 이 일기를 적는다. 지금은 여섯 시이고, 우리는 삼십 분 뒤에 서재에서 만나 무언가 먹기로 했다. 반 헬싱 박사와 수어드 박사가 먹지 않으면 우리가 최선을 다할 수 없다는 데 의견을 같이했기 때문이다. 우리의 최선이, 하느님은 아시겠지만, 오늘 절실히 필요할 것이다. 나는 틈이 날 때마다 계속 적어야 한다. 멈추어 생각할 엄두가 나지 않기 때문이다. 크든 작든 모든 것을 적어두어야 한다. 어쩌면 끝에 가서는 사소한 것들이 우리에게 가장 많은 것을 가르쳐줄지도 모른다. 그 가르침이 크든 작든, 미나나 나를 오늘보다 더 나쁜 처지로 데려다 놓을 수는 없었을 것이다. 그러나 우리는 믿고 희망을 품어야 한다. 가엾은 미나는 방금, 그 사랑스러운 두 뺨에 눈물을 흘리며, 우리의 믿음이 시험받는 것은 고난과 시련 속에서라고 — 우리는 계속 믿어야 하며, 하느님께서 끝까지 우리를 도우시리라고 말했다. 끝이라니! 오 하느님! 무슨 끝이란 말인가?…… 일을 하자! 일을!\n\n반 헬싱 박사와 수어드 박사가 가엾은 렌필드를 살펴보고 돌아왔을 때, 우리는 무엇을 해야 할지를 진지하게 의논했다. 먼저 수어드 박사는, 자신과 반 헬싱 박사가 아래층 방으로 내려갔을 때 렌필드가 바닥에 온통 무더기로 쓰러져 있는 것을 발견했다고 말했다. 그의 얼굴은 온통 멍들고 으스러져 함몰되어 있었고, 목뼈가 부러져 있었다.\n\n수어드 박사는 복도에서 근무 중이던 당직자에게 무슨 소리를 들었느냐고 물었다. 그는 자기가 앉아 있었는데 — 반쯤 졸고 있었다고 시인했다 — 방 안에서 큰 목소리들이 들렸고, 그 뒤 렌필드가 여러 번 큰 소리로 \"하느님! 하느님! 하느님!\" 하고 외쳤으며, 그러고 나서 무언가 쓰러지는 소리가 났고, 그가 방에 들어가 보니 그가 바닥에 얼굴을 박은 채 쓰러져 있었으니, 바로 박사들이 본 그대로였다고 말했다. 반 헬싱이 \"목소리들\"을 들었느냐 \"목소리\"를 들었느냐고 묻자, 그는 뭐라 말할 수 없다고 했다. 처음에는 둘인 것 같았으나 방에 아무도 없었으니 하나였을 수밖에 없다는 것이다. 필요하다면, \"하느님\"이라는 말은 환자가 한 것이라고 맹세할 수 있다고 했다. 수어드 박사는 우리끼리 남았을 때, 이 일을 파고들고 싶지 않다고 말했다. 검시(檢屍) 문제를 고려해야 하는데, 진실을 내세우는 것은 결코 안 될 일이니, 아무도 그것을 믿지 않을 것이기 때문이었다. 사정이 그러하니, 당직자의 증언을 토대로 침대에서 떨어져 생긴 불의의 사고사라는 진단서를 낼 수 있을 것이라고 그는 생각했다. 만일 검시관이 요구한다면 정식 검시가 있겠으나, 그 결과는 필연적으로 똑같을 것이었다.\n\n다음 단계를 어떻게 해야 할지 논의가 시작되었을 때, 우리가 가장 먼저 결정한 것은 미나에게 모든 것을 온전히 털어놓아야 한다는 것 — 어떤 종류의 것도, 아무리 고통스럽더라도 그녀에게 숨겨서는 안 된다는 것이었다. 그녀 자신도 그것이 현명한 일임에 동의했다. 그토록 용감하면서도 그토록 슬픔에 잠겨, 그토록 깊은 절망 속에 있는 그녀를 보는 것은 가슴 아픈 일이었다. \"숨기는 일이 있어서는 안 돼요.\" 그녀가 말했다. \"아아! 우리는 이미 너무 많이 숨겨왔어요. 게다가, 제가 이미 견뎌온 것보다 — 지금 겪고 있는 것보다 더한 고통을 이 세상 무엇도 제게 줄 수는 없어요! 무슨 일이 일어나든, 그것은 제게 새로운 희망이나 새로운 용기가 될 수밖에 없을 거예요!\" 반 헬싱은 그녀가 말하는 동안 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 불쑥, 그러나 조용히 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 친애하는 미나 부인, 이미 일어난 일을 생각하면, 두렵지 않으시오? 당신 자신을 위해서가 아니라, 당신으로 인해 다른 이들이 입을 해를 두고 말이오.\" 그녀의 얼굴은 굳어 그 선이 단단해졌으나, 두 눈은 순교자의 헌신으로 빛났다. 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"아, 아니에요! 제 마음은 이미 정해졌으니까요!\"\n\n\"무엇으로 말이오?\" 그가 부드럽게 물었고, 그동안 우리는 모두 아주 조용히 있었다. 저마다 나름의 방식으로 그녀가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 어렴풋이 짐작하고 있었기 때문이다. 그녀의 대답은 그저 사실을 진술하기라도 하듯, 거침없는 단순함으로 나왔다.\n\n\"제 안에서 — 저는 그것을 예리하게 지켜볼 거예요 — 제가 사랑하는 누군가에게 해가 될 조짐을 발견한다면, 저는 죽을 거예요!\"\n\n\"스스로 목숨을 끊지는 않으시겠지요?\" 그가 쉰 목소리로 물었다.\n\n\"끊을 거예요. 저를 사랑하여, 그런 고통과 그토록 절망적인 노력을 덜어줄 벗이 없다면요!\" 그녀는 그렇게 말하며 의미심장하게 그를 바라보았다. 그는 앉아 있었으나, 이제 일어서서 그녀에게 가까이 다가가 그녀의 머리에 손을 얹고는 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"내 아이여, 만일 그것이 당신에게 이롭다면 그리 해줄 이가 여기 있소. 나로 말하자면, 당신을 위해 그런 안락한 죽음을 마련해주는 일을, 그것이 최선이라면 지금 이 순간이라도, 하느님 앞의 내 셈에 떠맡을 수 있을 것이오. 아니, 그것이 안전한 일이기만 하다면! 그러나 내 아이여——\" 잠시 그는 목이 메는 듯했고, 큰 흐느낌이 그의 목구멍에서 솟아올랐다. 그는 그것을 삼켜 누르고는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"여기에는 당신과 죽음 사이를 가로막아 설 이들이 있소. 당신은 죽어서는 아니 되오. 어떤 손으로도 죽어서는 아니 되며, 무엇보다도 당신 자신의 손으로는 결코 아니 되오. 당신의 그 아름다운 삶을 더럽힌 그 다른 자가 진정으로 죽기 전까지는 당신은 죽어서는 아니 되오. 만일 그가 아직 산 채로 죽지 않은 존재로 남아 있다면, 당신의 죽음은 당신을 그와 같은 존재로 만들어버릴 것이기 때문이오. 아니, 당신은 살아야 하오! 죽음이 이루 말할 수 없는 은총처럼 보일지라도, 당신은 살기 위해 몸부림치고 애써야 하오. 죽음이 고통 속에서 오든 기쁨 속에서 오든, 낮에 오든 밤에 오든, 안전한 가운데 오든 위험한 가운데 오든, 당신은 죽음 그 자체와 싸워야 하오! 나는 당신의 살아 있는 영혼을 두고 명하노니, 이 거대한 악이 지나갈 때까지 당신은 죽지 마시오 — 아니, 죽음을 생각조차 하지 마시오.\" 가엾은 그녀는 죽은 사람처럼 창백해지더니, 충격에 떨었으니, 마치 밀물이 들어올 때 모래수렁이 흔들리고 떠는 것을 내가 본 것과 같았다. 우리는 모두 침묵했고, 아무것도 할 수 없었다. 마침내 그녀는 좀 더 차분해져 그를 돌아보며, 손을 내밀고는, 다정하게, 그러나 오! 그토록 슬프게 말했다.\n\n\"약속드릴게요, 친애하는 벗이여, 만일 하느님께서 저를 살게 하신다면, 저는 그리 살려고 애쓰겠어요. 그리하여, 그분의 좋으신 때에, 이 공포가 제게서 사라져버릴 때까지요.\" 그녀가 어찌나 착하고 용감했던지, 우리는 모두 그녀를 위해 일하고 견디리라는 다짐으로 우리 마음이 굳세어짐을 느꼈고, 무엇을 해야 할지 의논하기 시작했다. 나는 그녀에게, 금고 안의 모든 서류와, 앞으로 우리가 쓰게 될 모든 서류며 일기며 축음기 기록을 맡으라고, 그리고 전처럼 기록을 정리하라고 말했다. 그녀는 무엇이든 할 일이 있다는 전망에 기뻐했다 — 그토록 음울한 관심사에 \"기뻐했다\"는 말을 쓸 수 있다면 말이다.\n\n언제나처럼 반 헬싱은 누구보다 앞서 생각해두었고, 우리 일의 정확한 순서를 마련해두고 있었다.\n\n\"어쩌면 잘된 일이오,\" 그가 말했다. \"카팩스를 다녀온 뒤의 회의에서, 우리가 거기 놓여 있던 흙 상자들에 아무 손도 대지 않기로 결정한 것 말이오. 만일 그리했더라면 백작은 우리의 의도를 짐작했을 것이고, 나머지 것들에 대한 그런 시도를 좌절시키려고 미리 대책을 세웠을 게 틀림없소. 그러나 지금 그는 우리 의도를 모르고 있소. 아니, 그 이상으로, 십중팔구 그는 우리에게 자신의 은신처들을 못 쓰게 만들어, 예전처럼 그것들을 이용할 수 없게 할 그런 힘이 있다는 것조차 모르고 있소. 우리는 이제 그것들의 배치에 관한 지식에서 훨씬 더 앞서 있으니, 피커딜리의 집을 살펴보고 나면 그 마지막 하나까지 추적할 수 있을 것이오. 그러니 오늘은 우리의 날이며, 거기에 우리의 희망이 달려 있소. 오늘 아침 우리의 슬픔 위로 떠오른 해가 그 운행 속에서 우리를 지켜주오. 오늘 밤 해가 질 때까지, 그 괴물은 지금 지닌 형체가 무엇이든 그대로 유지해야만 하오. 그는 자신의 지상의 외피라는 한계 안에 갇혀 있소. 그는 허공으로 녹아들 수도, 틈이나 갈라진 틈새나 작은 구멍으로 사라질 수도 없소. 출입구를 지나려면 그는 인간처럼 문을 열어야만 하오. 그러니 우리는 오늘 그의 모든 은신처를 찾아내어 못 쓰게 만들어야 하오. 그리하여, 아직 그를 붙잡아 멸하지는 못하더라도, 붙잡아 멸하는 일이 머지않아 확실해질 어떤 곳으로 그를 궁지에 몰아넣을 것이오.\" 여기서 나는 벌떡 일어났으니, 미나의 생명과 행복이 그토록 소중하게 실린 분초가 우리에게서 날아가고 있다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었기 때문이다. 우리가 이야기를 나누는 동안에는 행동이 불가능했으니 말이다. 그러나 반 헬싱은 경고하듯 손을 들었다. \"아니, 조너선 벗이여,\" 그가 말했다. \"이 일에서는, 집으로 가는 가장 빠른 길이 가장 먼 길이오, 당신네 속담이 그리 말하듯 말이오. 때가 오면 우리는 모두 행동할 것이며, 필사적으로 빠르게 행동할 것이오. 그러나 생각해보시오, 십중팔구 이 상황의 열쇠는 피커딜리의 그 집에 있소. 백작은 사들인 집을 여럿 갖고 있을지 모르오. 그것들에 대해 그는 매매 증서와 열쇠와 그 밖의 것들을 갖고 있을 것이오. 그가 글을 쓰는 종이도 있을 것이고, 수표책도 있을 것이오. 그가 어딘가에 두고 있어야 할 소유물이 많이 있소. 어찌하여 이곳, 이토록 중심에 있고, 이토록 조용하며, 앞으로든 뒤로든 어느 시각에나 드나들 수 있는 곳, 바로 그 번잡한 통행 한가운데에 정작 눈여겨보는 이가 아무도 없는 이곳이 아니겠소. 우리는 그곳으로 가서 그 집을 수색할 것이오. 그리고 그것이 무엇을 품고 있는지 알아내면, 그때 우리 벗 아서가 사냥의 표현으로 '굴을 막는다'고 부르는 일을 하여, 우리의 늙은 여우를 몰아 잡을 것이오 — 그렇소? 그렇지 않소?\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당장 갑시다.\" 내가 외쳤다. \"우리는 그 귀하고 귀한 시간을 허비하고 있어요!\" 교수는 움직이지 않고 그저 말했다.\n\n\"그럼 우리가 피커딜리의 그 집에 어떻게 들어간단 말이오?\"\n\n\"어떻게든!\" 내가 외쳤다. \"필요하다면 부수고 들어가야지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신네 경찰은요? 그들은 어디에 있을 것이며, 무어라 하겠소?\"\n\n나는 말문이 막혔다. 그러나 그가 지체하기를 바란다면 거기에는 그럴 만한 충분한 이유가 있음을 알았다. 그래서 나는 되도록 차분하게 말했다.\n\n\"필요 이상으로 기다리지는 마세요. 제가 어떤 고문 속에 있는지 잘 아시잖아요.\"\n\n\"아, 내 아이여, 알다마다. 정녕 당신의 고뇌에 무언가를 더하고 싶은 마음은 없소. 그러나 생각해보시오, 온 세상이 움직이기 전에 우리가 무엇을 할 수 있겠소. 그때라야 우리의 때가 올 것이오. 나는 생각하고 또 생각했는데, 가장 단순한 방법이 무엇보다 가장 좋은 방법인 것 같소. 자, 우리는 그 집에 들어가고 싶으나 열쇠가 없소. 그렇지 않소?\" 나는 고개를 끄덕였다.\n\n\"이제 당신이 정말로 그 집의 주인인데도 여전히 들어갈 수 없다고 가정해봅시다. 그리고 당신에게 가택 침입자의 양심 같은 것이 전혀 없다고 생각해보시오. 그러면 어떻게 하겠소?\"\n\n\"점잖은 자물쇠공을 데려와, 저 대신 자물쇠를 따도록 시키겠지요.\"\n\n\"그럼 당신네 경찰이 끼어들겠소, 그렇지 않겠소?\"\n\n\"오, 아니요! 그 사람이 제대로 고용된 줄 안다면 안 그럴 거예요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면,\" 그는 말하면서 나를 예리하게 바라보았다. \"의문이 되는 것은 오직 고용한 사람의 양심과, 그 고용한 사람이 양심이 바른지 그른지에 대한 당신네 경찰의 판단뿐이오. 당신네 경찰이 그런 일에 신경을 쓰려면 정녕 마음을 읽어내는 데 열성적이고 영리한 사람들이어야 — 오, 어찌나 영리해야 — 하겠소! 아니, 아니오, 내 벗 조너선, 당신이 이 당신네 런던에서, 아니 세상 어느 도시에서든, 빈집 백 채의 자물쇠를 떼어내 보시오. 그런 일이 제대로 행해지는 방식대로, 그런 일이 제대로 행해지는 시각에 한다면, 아무도 끼어들지 않을 것이오. 나는 런던에 아주 멋진 집을 가진 어떤 신사 이야기를 읽은 적이 있소. 그가 여름 몇 달 동안 스위스로 가면서 집을 잠가두었더니, 어떤 도둑이 와서 뒤쪽 창문을 깨고 들어갔소. 그러더니 앞쪽 덧문을 열어젖히고는, 바로 경찰의 눈앞에서 문으로 드나들었소. 그러고는 그 집에서 경매를 열어 광고를 내고 큰 게시문을 붙였소. 그리고 그날이 오자, 그 집을 가진 다른 사람의 물건들을 유명한 경매인을 시켜 죄다 팔아치웠소. 그런 다음 건축업자에게 가서, 일정 기간 안에 집을 헐고 모두 치워간다는 계약을 맺어 그 집을 팔았소. 그리고 당신네 경찰과 그 밖의 당국이 할 수 있는 한 그를 도왔소. 그리고 그 주인이 스위스 휴가에서 돌아와 보니, 자기 집이 있던 자리에 빈 구덩이만 남아 있더라는 것이오. 이 모든 일이 격식대로 행해졌소. 우리의 일에서도 우리는 격식대로 할 것이오. 우리는 그때라야 별로 생각할 거리가 없는 경찰들이 이상하게 여길 만큼 너무 이르게 가지는 않을 것이오. 우리는 열 시가 지난 뒤에, 오가는 사람이 많을 때 갈 것이니, 우리가 정말로 그 집의 주인이라면 그런 일들이 행해질 그 시각에 말이오.\"\n격식: 원문은 '앙 레글(en règle)', 정식 절차에 맞게라는 뜻의 프랑스어.\n\n나는 그가 옳다는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없었고, 미나의 얼굴에 어린 끔찍한 절망이 조금은 누그러졌다. 그런 좋은 조언 속에 희망이 있었던 것이다. 반 헬싱은 말을 이었다.\n\n\"일단 그 집 안에 들어가면 우리는 더 많은 단서를 찾을지 모르오. 어쨌든 우리 가운데 몇은 거기 남고, 나머지는 흙 상자가 더 있는 다른 곳들을 — 버몬지와 마일엔드에서 — 찾으면 되오.\"\n\n고댈밍 경이 일어섰다. \"여기서 제가 좀 쓸모가 있을 것 같습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"제 사람들에게 전보를 쳐서 가장 편리한 곳에 말과 마차를 대기시키도록 하겠습니다.\"\n\n\"이보게, 친구,\" 모리스가 말했다. \"말을 타고 가야 할 경우에 대비해 모든 걸 갖추어두는 건 훌륭한 생각이야. 하지만 자네의 그 번쩍이는 마차에, 문장(紋章) 장식까지 단 것이 월워스나 마일엔드의 뒷골목에 나타난다면, 우리 목적에 비추어 너무 눈길을 끌지 않겠나? 내 생각엔 우리가 남쪽이나 동쪽으로 갈 때는 삯마차를 타야 할 것 같네. 그리고 가려는 동네 근처 어딘가에 그것마저 두고 가는 게 좋겠어.\"\n\n\"퀸시 벗이 옳소!\" 교수가 말했다. \"그의 머리는, 당신네 말로 수평을 잘 잡는다고 하는 그것이오. 우리가 하려는 일은 어려운 일이니, 가능하다면 누구도 우리를 지켜보는 일이 없기를 바라오.\"\n\n미나는 모든 일에 점점 더 관심을 보였고, 사태의 절박함이 그녀로 하여금 잠시나마 그날 밤의 끔찍한 경험을 잊는 데 도움이 되는 것을 보고 나는 기뻤다. 그녀는 무척이나, 무척이나 창백했고 — 거의 섬뜩할 지경이었다 — 너무 야위어 입술이 당겨져 이가 다소 두드러져 보였다. 이 마지막 점은 그녀에게 공연한 고통을 줄까 봐 입에 담지 않았다. 그러나 백작이 가엾은 루시의 피를 빨았을 때 그녀에게 일어난 일을 생각하니 내 핏줄의 피가 차갑게 식는 듯했다. 아직은 이가 더 날카로워지는 조짐은 없었다. 그러나 아직 시간이 얼마 지나지 않았으니, 두려워할 시간은 남아 있었다.\n\n우리의 노력의 순서와 우리 병력의 배치를 논의하게 되었을 때, 새로운 의혹의 원천들이 생겨났다. 마침내, 피커딜리로 출발하기 전에 가까이에 있는 백작의 은신처를 파괴하기로 합의했다. 만일 그가 너무 일찍 그것을 알아챈다 해도, 그리하면 우리는 파괴 작업에서 여전히 그보다 앞서 있을 것이며, 그가 순전히 물질적인 형체로, 가장 약한 상태로 나타난다면 우리에게 새로운 단서를 줄지도 모르는 일이었다.\n\n병력의 배치에 관해서는, 카팩스를 다녀온 뒤 우리 모두가 피커딜리의 집에 들어가되, 두 박사와 내가 거기 남고, 고댈밍 경과 퀸시는 월워스와 마일엔드의 은신처들을 찾아 파괴하자고 교수가 제안했다. 가능성이 높지는 않더라도, 백작이 낮 동안 피커딜리에 나타날 수도 있으며, 그렇다면 우리가 그 자리에서 그를 상대할 수 있을지 모른다고 교수는 역설했다. 어쨌든 우리는 병력을 갖추어 그를 뒤쫓을 수 있을 것이었다. 이 계획에 나는 극력 반대했으니, 적어도 내가 가는 문제에 관해서는 그러했다. 나는 남아서 미나를 지킬 작정이라고, 이 문제에 대해서는 마음을 굳혔다고 생각했기 때문이다. 그러나 미나는 내 반대를 들으려 하지 않았다. 그녀는 내가 쓸모 있을 어떤 법률 문제가 있을지도 모른다고, 백작의 서류 가운데 내가 트란실바니아에서 겪은 경험으로 이해할 수 있는 단서가 있을지도 모른다고, 그리고 사정이 이러하니 백작의 비범한 힘에 맞서려면 우리가 끌어모을 수 있는 모든 힘이 필요하다고 말했다. 나는 굴복할 수밖에 없었으니, 미나의 결심이 확고했기 때문이다. 그녀는 우리 모두가 함께 일하는 것이 자기에게는 마지막 희망이라고 말했다. \"저로 말하자면,\" 그녀가 말했다. \"저는 두려움이 없어요. 일은 더 나쁠 수 없을 만큼 나빠졌고, 무슨 일이 일어나든 거기에는 어떤 희망이나 위안의 요소가 있을 수밖에 없어요. 가세요, 여보! 하느님께서 원하신다면, 제가 혼자 있든 누군가 곁에 있든 똑같이 저를 지켜주실 수 있어요.\" 그래서 나는 벌떡 일어나 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 하느님의 이름으로 당장 갑시다. 우리는 시간을 잃고 있어요. 백작이 우리 생각보다 더 일찍 피커딜리에 올지도 몰라요.\"\n\n\"그렇지 않소!\" 반 헬싱이 손을 들며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 왜요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"잊었소,\" 그가 정말로 미소까지 지으며 말했다. \"어젯밤 그가 잔뜩 포식했으니, 늦게까지 잠들어 있으리라는 것을 말이오?\"\n\n내가 잊었다니! 내가 잊을 수나 있을까 — 잊을 수가 있을까! 우리 가운데 누구라도 그 끔찍한 광경을 잊을 수 있을까! 미나는 용감한 표정을 유지하려고 안간힘을 썼다. 그러나 고통이 그녀를 압도해, 그녀는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 신음하며 몸서리쳤다. 반 헬싱은 그녀의 무서운 경험을 떠올리게 할 의도가 없었다. 그는 그저 지적인 노력에 몰두하느라 그녀와 그 일에서 그녀가 한 역할을 잊었던 것이다. 자기가 한 말이 무엇인지 깨닫자, 그는 자신의 경솔함에 소스라치며 그녀를 위로하려 했다. \"오, 미나 부인,\" 그가 말했다. \"친애하는, 친애하는 미나 부인, 아아! 당신을 그토록 존경하는 내가 하필 그토록 분별없는 말을 하다니. 이 어리석은 늙은 입술과 이 어리석은 늙은 머리는 그럴 자격이 없소. 그러나 당신은 그것을 잊어주시겠지요, 그러시겠지요?\" 그는 말하면서 그녀 곁에 낮게 몸을 굽혔다. 그녀는 그의 손을 잡고, 눈물 너머로 그를 바라보며 쉰 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"아니에요, 저는 잊지 않을 거예요. 기억하는 것이 좋으니까요. 게다가 그와 함께 제 기억 속에는 당신에 관한 다정한 것이 너무도 많으니, 저는 그 모든 것을 한데 모아 받아들이겠어요. 자, 이제 여러분 모두 곧 떠나셔야 해요. 아침식사가 준비됐으니, 우리 모두 먹어서 기운을 차려야 해요.\"\n\n아침식사는 우리 모두에게 묘한 끼니였다. 우리는 명랑하려 애쓰며 서로를 북돋우려 했고, 미나가 우리 가운데 가장 밝고 가장 명랑했다. 식사가 끝나자 반 헬싱이 일어서서 말했다.\n\n\"자, 친애하는 벗들이여, 우리는 우리의 끔찍한 과업을 향해 나아가오. 우리는 모두 무장했소? 처음 우리 적의 은신처를 찾아갔던 그날 밤처럼, 육체적 공격뿐 아니라 유령 같은 공격에도 대비해 무장했소?\" 우리는 모두 그렇다고 확언했다. \"그렇다면 됐소. 자, 미나 부인, 어떤 경우에도 당신은 해가 질 때까지 여기서 완전히 안전하오. 그리고 그 전에 우리는 돌아올 것이오 — 만일—— 우리는 돌아올 것이오! 그러나 떠나기 전에 당신이 개인적인 공격에 대비해 무장하는 것을 보게 해주시오. 당신이 내려온 뒤로, 나는 그가 들어오지 못하도록 우리가 아는 그 물건들을 놓아 직접 당신의 방을 준비해두었소. 이제 당신 자신을 지키게 해주시오. 당신의 이마에 이 거룩한 성체(聖體)의 빵 한 조각을 대겠소, 성부와 성자와——의 이름으로.\"\n\n들을수록 우리 가슴을 얼어붙게 하는 무서운 비명이 터졌다. 그가 그 빵을 미나의 이마에 댔을 때, 그것이 이마를 지져버린 것이다 — 마치 새하얗게 달군 금속 조각이기라도 한 듯 살 속으로 파고들어 태워버린 것이다. 가엾은 내 사랑의 머리는, 신경이 그 고통을 받아들이는 것과 똑같이 빠르게 그 사실의 의미를 그녀에게 일러주었다. 그 둘이 어찌나 그녀를 압도했던지, 극도로 시달린 그녀의 본성이 그 무시무시한 비명으로 목소리를 낸 것이다. 그러나 그녀의 생각에 들어맞는 말은 재빨리 나왔다. 비명의 메아리가 채 공중에서 울려 사라지기도 전에 반응이 일어나, 그녀는 자기를 낮추는 고뇌 속에서 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉았다. 옛날의 나병환자가 자기 망토를 끌어당기듯, 자신의 아름다운 머리칼을 얼굴 위로 끌어당기며 그녀는 통곡했다.\n\n\"부정해요! 부정해요! 전능하신 분조차 제 더럽혀진 살을 피하시는군요! 저는 심판의 날까지 제 이마에 이 수치의 표를 지니고 살아야 해요.\" 모두가 멈칫했다. 나는 어찌할 수 없는 비탄의 고뇌 속에서 그녀 곁에 몸을 던져, 두 팔로 감싸 그녀를 꼭 끌어안았다. 몇 분 동안 우리의 슬픈 심장이 함께 고동쳤고, 그동안 우리를 둘러싼 벗들은 소리 없이 눈물을 흘리며 눈길을 돌렸다. 그때 반 헬싱이 돌아서서 엄숙하게 말했다. 어찌나 엄숙했던지, 나는 그가 어떤 식으로든 영감을 받아, 자기 자신을 넘어선 것들을 진술하고 있다고 느끼지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"하느님께서 친히, 심판의 날에 — 반드시 그러하시겠지만 — 이 땅과, 그분이 그 위에 두신 그분의 자녀들의 온갖 잘못을 바로잡으시기에 합당하다고 보실 때까지, 당신은 그 표를 지녀야 할지도 모르오. 그러나 오, 미나 부인, 내 사랑하는, 내 사랑하는 이여, 그 붉은 흉터, 무슨 일이 있었는지 하느님께서 아신다는 그 표가 사라져, 당신의 이마를 우리가 아는 그 마음만큼이나 순결하게 남길 그때를, 당신을 사랑하는 우리가 거기서 지켜볼 수 있기를. 우리가 살아 있는 것이 분명한 만큼이나 분명히, 하느님께서 우리를 짓누르는 이 무거운 짐을 들어올리시기에 옳다고 보실 때 그 흉터는 사라질 것이오. 그때까지 우리는 우리의 십자가를 지오, 그분의 아드님께서 그분의 뜻에 순종하여 그리하셨듯이 말이오. 어쩌면 우리는 그분의 기쁨에 쓰일, 선택된 도구일지 모르오. 그리고 그 다른 분이 채찍질과 수치를 통해, 눈물과 피를 통해, 의심과 두려움과, 하느님과 인간을 갈라놓는 그 모든 것을 통해 그러하셨듯, 우리도 그분의 분부에 따라 올라가는 것일지 모르오.\"\n\n그의 말 속에는 희망이 있었고, 위안이 있었다. 그리고 그것들은 체념으로 이끌었다. 미나와 나는 둘 다 그렇게 느꼈고, 동시에 우리는 저마다 노인의 한 손을 잡고 몸을 굽혀 그 손에 입을 맞추었다. 그런 다음 우리는 한마디 말도 없이 모두 함께 무릎을 꿇고, 모두 손을 맞잡은 채 서로에게 충실하기를 맹세했다. 우리 남자들은, 저마다 나름의 방식으로 사랑하는 그녀의 머리에서 슬픔의 너울을 걷어주기로 다짐했다. 그리고 우리 앞에 놓인 그 끔찍한 과업에 도움과 인도를 주십사 기도했다.\n\n이제 출발할 때가 되었다. 그래서 나는 미나에게 작별을 고했으니, 우리 둘 다 죽는 날까지 잊지 못할 이별이었다. 그리고 우리는 길을 나섰다.\n\n한 가지 나는 마음을 굳혔다. 만일 결국 미나가 흡혈귀가 되어야 한다는 것을 우리가 알게 된다면, 그녀가 그 알지 못할 끔찍한 땅으로 혼자 가게 두지는 않으리라는 것을. 옛날에 흡혈귀 하나가 곧 여럿을 뜻했던 것도 바로 이런 까닭이리라 짐작한다. 그들의 흉측한 육신이 오직 거룩한 흙 속에서만 쉴 수 있었듯이, 가장 거룩한 사랑이 그들의 소름 끼치는 대열을 채우는 모병관(募兵官) 노릇을 했던 것이다.\n\n우리는 별 어려움 없이 카팩스에 들어섰고, 모든 것이 처음 왔을 때와 똑같음을 알았다. 방치와 먼지와 부패로 그토록 무미건조한 이 환경 속에, 우리가 이미 아는 그런 공포의 근거가 있다는 것을 믿기란 어려웠다. 우리 마음이 굳어 있지 않았더라면, 또 우리를 채찍질하는 끔찍한 기억들이 없었더라면, 우리는 도저히 우리의 과업을 진행할 수 없었을 것이다. 집 안에서 우리는 어떤 서류도, 사용된 흔적도 찾지 못했다. 그리고 그 낡은 예배당에서 큰 상자들은 우리가 마지막으로 보았을 때 그대로의 모습이었다. 반 헬싱 박사가 그것들 앞에 선 우리에게 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"자, 벗들이여, 우리는 여기서 해야 할 의무가 있소. 우리는 이 흙을, 거룩한 기억들로 그토록 신성한 이 흙, 그가 그토록 흉악한 용도로 쓰려고 먼 땅에서 가져온 이 흙을 못 쓰게 만들어야 하오. 그는 이 흙이 거룩했기에 그것을 골랐소. 그리하여 우리는 그의 무기로 그를 물리치오, 우리가 그것을 한층 더 거룩하게 만드는 것이니 말이오. 그것은 인간의 그런 용도로 봉헌되었으나, 이제 우리는 그것을 하느님께 봉헌하오.\" 그는 말하면서 가방에서 나사돌리개와 렌치를 꺼냈고, 곧 상자들 가운데 하나의 뚜껑이 열렸다. 흙에서는 곰팡내 나고 답답한 냄새가 났다. 그러나 우리는 어쩐지 개의치 않는 듯했으니, 우리의 주의가 교수에게 집중되어 있었기 때문이다. 그는 상자에서 거룩한 성체의 빵 한 조각을 꺼내 경건하게 흙 위에 놓았고, 그런 다음 뚜껑을 덮어 나사로 단단히 조이기 시작했으며, 우리는 그가 일하는 동안 그를 도왔다.\n\n하나하나, 우리는 큰 상자들을 모두 똑같은 방식으로 처리했고, 겉보기에는 우리가 발견했던 그대로 그것들을 남겨두었다. 그러나 그 안에는 저마다 성체의 한 조각이 들어 있었다.\n\n우리가 등 뒤로 문을 닫았을 때, 교수가 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"이만큼은 이미 끝냈소. 만일 나머지 모든 것에서도 우리가 이토록 성공할 수 있다면, 오늘 저녁의 해넘이는 상아처럼 새하얗고 한 점 얼룩도 없는 미나 부인의 이마를 비출지도 모르오!\"\n\n기차를 잡으려 역으로 가는 길에 잔디밭을 가로질러 갈 때, 우리는 정신병원의 정면을 볼 수 있었다. 나는 간절히 바라보았고, 내 방 창문에서 미나를 보았다. 나는 그녀에게 손을 흔들었고, 그곳에서의 우리 일이 성공적으로 마무리되었음을 알리려 고개를 끄덕였다. 그녀는 알아들었다는 표시로 답하여 고개를 끄덕였다. 마지막으로 본 그녀는 작별의 손을 흔들고 있었다. 무거운 마음으로 우리는 역을 찾았고, 우리가 승강장에 닿았을 때 막 김을 뿜으며 들어오던 기차를 간신히 잡아탔다.\n\n이 글은 기차 안에서 적었다.\n\n피커딜리, 12시 30분. — 우리가 펜처치 가에 닿기 직전에 고댈밍 경이 내게 말했다.\n\n\"퀸시와 나는 자물쇠공을 찾아오겠소. 당신은 우리와 함께 가지 않는 게 좋겠소. 혹시 어려움이 생길 경우에 대비해서 말이오. 사정이 이러하니 우리가 빈집에 부수고 들어가는 것은 그리 나빠 보이지 않겠지만, 당신은 사무 변호사니, 법조협회가 당신더러 좀 더 분별이 있었어야 했다고 할지도 모르오.\" 나는 오명이라도 무릅쓰는 위험을 함께 나누지 못한다는 데 이의를 제기했으나, 그는 말을 이었다. \"게다가 우리 수가 너무 많지 않으면 눈길을 덜 끌 것이오. 내 작위(爵位)가 자물쇠공에게도, 마침 지나가는 어떤 경찰에게도 모든 걸 무난하게 해줄 것이오. 당신은 잭과 교수와 함께 가서 그린파크에, 그 집이 보이는 어딘가에 있는 게 좋겠소. 그리고 문이 열리고 자물쇠공이 떠나는 것이 보이거든, 모두 건너오시오. 우리가 당신들을 살피고 있다가 들여보내 주겠소.\"\n\n\"좋은 조언이오!\" 반 헬싱이 말했고, 그래서 우리는 더 말하지 않았다. 고댈밍과 모리스는 삯마차를 타고 서둘러 떠났고, 우리는 다른 마차로 뒤따랐다. 알링턴 가 모퉁이에서 우리 일행은 내려 그린파크로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 들어갔다. 우리의 그토록 많은 희망이 집중된 그 집이, 더 활기차고 산뜻해 보이는 이웃집들 사이에서, 버려진 채로 음울하고 고요하게 우뚝 솟아 있는 것을 보자 내 심장이 뛰었다. 우리는 잘 보이는 자리의 벤치에 앉아, 되도록 눈길을 덜 끌려고 시가를 피우기 시작했다. 나머지 사람들이 오기를 기다리는 동안 분초가 납덩이 같은 발걸음으로 지나가는 듯했다.\n\n마침내 우리는 사륜마차 한 대가 다가오는 것을 보았다. 거기서, 느긋한 태도로 고댈밍 경과 모리스가 내렸다. 그리고 마부석에서 골풀로 엮은 연장 바구니를 든 다부진 일꾼 하나가 내려왔다. 모리스가 삯마차 마부에게 삯을 치르자, 마부는 모자에 손을 대고는 마차를 몰아 떠났다. 둘은 함께 계단을 올랐고, 고댈밍 경이 무엇을 하기를 원하는지 가리켰다. 일꾼은 느긋하게 외투를 벗어 난간의 창살 끝 하나에 걸고는, 마침 그때 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어오는 경찰에게 무언가 말을 했다. 경찰은 동의하듯 고개를 끄덕였고, 그 남자는 무릎을 꿇고 자기 옆에 가방을 놓았다. 그는 가방을 뒤적이더니 연장 몇 가지를 골라 꺼내, 자기 옆에 가지런히 늘어놓았다. 그런 다음 그는 일어서서 열쇠 구멍을 들여다보고, 거기에 입김을 불어넣고는, 고용주들을 돌아보며 무언가 말을 했다. 고댈밍 경이 미소 지었고, 남자는 제법 큰 열쇠 꾸러미를 들어 올렸다. 그 가운데 하나를 골라, 마치 그것으로 길을 더듬듯 자물쇠를 쑤셔보기 시작했다. 한동안 만지작거리던 그는 두 번째 열쇠를, 그다음에는 세 번째 열쇠를 시험해보았다. 별안간 그가 살짝 밀자 문이 열렸고, 그와 다른 두 사람은 현관으로 들어갔다. 우리는 가만히 앉아 있었다. 내 시가는 맹렬히 타들어 갔으나, 반 헬싱의 것은 아예 차갑게 식어버렸다. 우리는 일꾼이 나와 자기 가방을 들고 들어가는 것을 보며 참을성 있게 기다렸다. 그런 다음 그는 무릎으로 문을 받쳐 반쯤 열어둔 채, 자물쇠에 열쇠 하나를 맞추었다. 그는 마침내 그것을 고댈밍 경에게 건넸고, 고댈밍 경은 지갑을 꺼내 그에게 무언가를 주었다. 남자는 모자에 손을 대고, 가방을 들고, 외투를 입고는 떠났다. 이 모든 거래에 조금이라도 주의를 기울이는 이는 아무도 없었다.\n\n그 남자가 완전히 떠나자, 우리 셋은 길을 건너 문을 두드렸다. 문은 곧바로 퀸시 모리스가 열어주었고, 그 옆에는 고댈밍 경이 시가에 불을 붙이며 서 있었다.\n\n\"이곳은 냄새가 지독하군요.\" 우리가 들어가자 후자가 말했다. 정말로 냄새가 지독했다 — 카팩스의 그 낡은 예배당처럼 — 그리고 우리의 앞선 경험으로 미루어, 백작이 이곳을 꽤 자유로이 써왔음이 우리에게 분명해 보였다. 우리는 공격에 대비해 모두 함께 붙어, 집을 살피러 움직였다. 우리가 강하고 교활한 적을 상대하고 있음을 알았고, 백작이 그 집 안에 있지 않으리라는 보장이 아직 없었기 때문이다. 현관 뒤쪽에 자리한 식당에서 우리는 흙 상자 여덟 개를 발견했다. 우리가 찾던 아홉 개 가운데 여덟 개뿐이었다! 우리의 일은 끝나지 않았고, 사라진 상자를 찾기 전까지는 결코 끝나지 않을 것이었다. 먼저 우리는, 좁은 돌포장 마당 너머로 어떤 마구간의 휑한 정면을 — 작은 집의 앞면처럼 보이도록 만들어진 것을 — 마주 보는 창문의 덧문을 열었다. 거기에는 창문이 없었으므로, 우리는 누가 엿볼까 두려워하지 않았다. 우리는 그 상자들을 살피는 데 시간을 조금도 허비하지 않았다. 우리가 가져온 연장으로 우리는 그것들을 하나하나 열어, 낡은 예배당의 그 다른 상자들을 처리했던 것처럼 처리했다. 백작이 지금 그 집 안에 없는 것이 우리에게 분명했고, 우리는 그의 소지품을 찾아 나섰다.\n\n지하에서 다락까지 나머지 방들을 대강 훑어본 뒤, 우리는 백작에게 속할 만한 소지품은 식당 안에 모두 있다는 결론에 이르렀다. 그래서 우리는 그것들을 세밀히 살펴보기 시작했다. 그것들은 커다란 식당 탁자 위에 일종의 질서 있는 무질서로 놓여 있었다. 피커딜리 집의 권리증서가 큰 뭉치로 있었고, 마일엔드와 버몬지 집들의 매매 증서, 편지지, 봉투, 펜과 잉크가 있었다. 모든 것이 먼지를 막으려고 얇은 포장지로 싸여 있었다. 또한 옷솔과, 머리솔과 빗, 그리고 물주전자와 대야가 있었는데 — 대야에는 마치 피로 물든 듯 불그스름한 더러운 물이 담겨 있었다. 마지막으로, 온갖 종류와 크기의 열쇠가 작은 무더기로 있었는데, 아마도 다른 집들에 속한 것들이리라. 이 마지막 발견물을 살펴본 뒤, 고댈밍 경과 퀸시 모리스는 동부와 남부에 있는 집들의 여러 주소를 정확히 적어두고, 그 열쇠들을 큰 꾸러미째 챙겨, 그곳들의 상자를 파괴하러 출발했다. 나머지 우리는, 낼 수 있는 만큼의 인내심으로, 그들이 돌아오기를 — 아니면 백작이 오기를 — 기다리고 있다.\n\n# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n10월 3일. — 고달밍과 퀸시 모리스가 오기를 기다리는 동안 시간이 끔찍하리만치 길게 느껴졌다. 교수는 우리의 정신을 줄곧 쓰게 함으로써 활발하게 유지하려 애썼다. 그가 이따금 하커에게 던지는 곁눈질을 보고 나는 그 자애로운 의도를 알 수 있었다. 가엾은 친구는 보기에도 처참한 비탄에 휩싸여 있다. 어젯밤만 해도 그는 솔직하고 행복해 보이는 사람으로, 강인하고 젊은 얼굴에 활기가 넘쳤고 머리칼은 짙은 갈색이었다. 그러나 오늘은 여위고 초췌한 노인이 되어, 그 흰 머리칼이 움푹 패고 불타는 눈, 슬픔이 새겨진 얼굴의 주름과 잘도 어울린다. 기력만은 아직 온전하다. 사실 그는 살아 있는 불꽃과도 같다. 이것이 어쩌면 그의 구원이 될지도 모른다. 모든 일이 잘되면 이것이 그를 절망의 시기를 무사히 넘기게 해줄 테고, 그러면 그는 어떤 식으로든 다시 삶의 현실로 깨어날 것이기 때문이다. 가엾은 친구, 나는 내 자신의 고통이 충분히 버겁다고 여겼으나, 그의 것에 비하면! 교수도 이를 잘 알고 있어, 그의 정신을 활발하게 유지하려 최선을 다하고 있다. 그가 줄곧 한 이야기는, 이런 처지에서는 정신을 온통 사로잡는 흥미로운 것이었다. 내가 기억할 수 있는 한 정확히 옮기면 이러하다.\n\n\"그 자료들이 내 손에 들어온 뒤로 나는 이 괴물에 관한 모든 서류를 거듭거듭 연구해왔소. 그리고 연구하면 할수록, 그를 철저히 짓밟아 없애야 할 필요가 더욱 커지는 듯하오. 처음부터 끝까지 그가 진보해온 흔적이 있소. 그의 힘뿐 아니라 그 힘에 대한 그의 앎의 흔적까지 말이오. 부다페스트의 내 벗 아르미니우스의 연구에서 알게 된 바로는, 그는 생전에 더없이 놀라운 인물이었소. 군인이요, 정치가요, 연금술사였는데 — 이 마지막 것은 당대 과학 지식의 가장 높은 발전이었소. 그는 강력한 두뇌와 비할 데 없는 학식, 그리고 두려움도 후회도 모르는 심장을 지녔소. 그는 스콜로만체에 다니기까지 마다하지 않았고, 당대의 어떤 지식의 갈래도 손대지 않은 것이 없었소. 한데 그의 안에서 두뇌의 능력은 육체의 죽음을 넘어 살아남았소. 다만 기억은 완전하지 못한 듯하오. 정신의 어떤 능력에서 그는 한낱 어린아이였고, 지금도 그러하오. 그러나 그는 자라고 있으며, 처음에는 어린애 같던 것들이 이제는 어른의 키에 이르렀소. 그는 실험을 하고 있고, 그것도 잘하고 있소. 만일 우리가 그의 앞길을 가로막지 않았더라면 그는 지금쯤 — 우리가 실패한다면 아직도 그리 될 수 있소 — 새로운 종족의 아비이자 키우는 자가 되었을 것이오. 그 종족의 길은 삶이 아니라 죽음을 거쳐 가야 하는 것이오.\"\n* 스콜로만체: 트란실바니아 전설에 나오는 악마가 운영한다는 마법 학교.\n\n하커는 신음하며 말했다. \"그런데 그 모든 것이 내 사랑하는 아내를 향해 겨누어져 있다니! 한데 그가 어떻게 실험을 한다는 말입니까? 그 지식이 우리가 그를 물리치는 데 도움이 될지도 모르잖습니까!\"\n\n\"그는 이곳에 온 이래로 줄곧, 더디지만 확실하게 자기 힘을 시험해왔소. 그 커다란 어린애 두뇌가 작동하고 있는 것이오. 우리로서는 다행스럽게도 그것이 아직은 어린애 두뇌요. 만일 그가 처음부터 어떤 일들을 감히 시도했더라면, 그는 진작에 우리 힘이 미치지 못하는 곳으로 가버렸을 테니까. 그러나 그는 성공할 작정이며, 앞에 수백 년이 놓인 자라면 기다리고 천천히 가는 여유를 부릴 수 있는 법이오. '천천히 서둘러라'가 그의 좌우명일 만하오.\"\n* 천천히 서둘러라: 라틴어 'Festina lente'.\n\n\"이해가 안 됩니다.\" 하커가 지친 목소리로 말했다. \"아, 좀 더 알기 쉽게 말씀해주십시오! 슬픔과 고통이 제 머리를 둔하게 하는 모양입니다.\"\n\n교수는 말하면서 다정하게 그의 어깨에 손을 얹었다.\n\n\"아, 이보게, 알기 쉽게 말하리다. 자네는 요사이 이 괴물이 어떻게 실험을 통해 슬금슬금 지식 속으로 기어들어왔는지 모르겠소? 그가 어떻게 그 동물을 먹는 환자를 이용해 우리 벗 존의 집에 들어가려 했는지 말이오. 흡혈귀란 그 뒤로는 언제 어떻게든 마음대로 올 수 있으나, 처음에는 반드시 집 안의 누군가가 청해야만 들어올 수 있기 때문이오. 그러나 이런 것들은 그의 가장 중요한 실험이 아니오. 처음에 그 큰 상자들이 모두 다른 자들의 손으로 옮겨진 것을 우리는 보지 않았소? 그때만 해도 그는 그렇게 해야만 하는 줄로만 알았소. 그러나 그 사이 내내 그 거대한 어린애 두뇌가 자라고 있었고, 그는 자기가 직접 상자를 옮길 수 있지 않을까 궁리하기 시작했소. 그래서 그는 거들기 시작했고, 이윽고 이 일이 잘되는 것을 알자 혼자서 모든 것을 옮겨보려 했소. 그렇게 그는 진보했고, 자기 무덤들을 여기저기 흩어놓았소. 그것들이 어디 숨겨져 있는지는 그 말고는 아무도 모르오. 그는 그것들을 땅속 깊이 묻어둘 작정이었을지도 모르오. 그래서 밤에만, 혹은 자기 모습을 바꿀 수 있는 그런 때에만 사용한다면, 그것들은 그에게 똑같이 쓸모가 있고, 그곳들이 그의 은신처임을 아무도 모를 것이오! 그러나 이보게, 절망하지 마시오. 이 지식은 그에게 너무 늦게 찾아왔소! 이미 그의 소굴은 하나만 빼고 모두 그에게 못 쓰게 되었고, 해지기 전에 그 하나마저 그리 될 것이오. 그러면 그는 움직이고 숨을 곳이 없게 되오. 내가 오늘 아침을 늦춘 것은 그것을 확실히 해두기 위해서였소. 그에게보다 우리에게 걸린 것이 더 크지 않소? 그렇다면 어찌 우리가 그보다 더 신중하지 않을 수 있겠소? 내 시계로 한 시가 되었으니, 모든 일이 잘되었다면 벗 아서와 퀸시는 벌써 우리에게 오는 길일 것이오. 오늘이 우리의 날이며, 우리는 느릴지언정 확실하게 가야 하고, 어떤 기회도 놓쳐서는 안 되오. 보시오! 저 자리에 없는 이들이 돌아오면 우리는 다섯이 되오.\"\n\n그가 말하는 동안, 우리는 현관문을 두드리는 소리, 곧 전보 배달 소년이 두 번 두드리는 우편배달부식 노크 소리에 화들짝 놀랐다. 우리는 한 충동에 이끌리듯 모두 현관으로 나갔고, 반 헬싱은 우리에게 조용히 하라며 손을 들어 보이고는 문으로 다가가 문을 열었다. 소년이 전보를 건넸다. 교수는 다시 문을 닫고, 수신인을 살핀 뒤 전보를 펼쳐 소리내어 읽었다.\n\n\"D를 조심하세요. 그가 방금 12시 45분에 카팩스에서 황급히 나와 남쪽으로 서둘러 갔어요. 한 바퀴 도는 것 같으며, 여러분을 만나려 할지도 몰라요. 미나.\"\n\n잠시 침묵이 흐르다가, 조너선 하커의 목소리가 그것을 깼다.\n\n\"이제, 하느님 감사합니다, 우리가 곧 만나게 되겠군요!\" 반 헬싱이 재빨리 그에게 돌아서서 말했다.\n\n\"하느님은 당신의 방식과 때로 행하실 것이오. 두려워하지도, 아직 기뻐하지도 마시오. 지금 이 순간 우리가 바라는 것이 우리의 파멸이 될 수도 있으니 말이오.\"\n\n\"전 이제 아무것도 상관없습니다.\" 그가 격하게 대답했다. \"이 짐승을 창조된 세상의 낯에서 지워버리는 것 말고는요. 그렇게만 할 수 있다면 제 영혼이라도 팔겠습니다!\"\n\n\"오, 쉿, 쉿, 이보게!\" 반 헬싱이 말했다. \"하느님은 이런 식으로 영혼을 사들이지 않으시오. 그리고 악마는 사들일지는 몰라도 신의를 지키지 않소. 그러나 하느님은 자비롭고 공정하시며, 자네의 고통과 그 사랑스러운 미나 부인에 대한 자네의 헌신을 아시오. 생각해보시오, 그녀가 자네의 그 거친 말을 듣기라도 한다면 그녀의 고통이 어찌 곱절이 되지 않겠소. 우리 가운데 누구도 두려워하지 마시오. 우리는 모두 이 대의에 헌신하고 있고, 오늘이 그 끝을 보게 될 것이오. 이제 행동할 때가 다가오고 있소. 오늘 이 흡혈귀는 인간의 힘에 한정되어 있어, 해질 때까지는 모습을 바꿀 수 없소. 그가 여기 도착하려면 시간이 걸릴 것이오 — 보시오, 한 시 이십 분이오 — 그러니 그가 아무리 빠르다 해도 여기 오기까지는 아직 얼마간 시간이 있소. 우리가 바라야 할 것은 아서 경과 퀸시가 먼저 도착하는 것이오.\"\n\n하커 부인의 전보를 받고 반 시간쯤 지났을 때, 현관문에서 조용하고도 단호한 노크 소리가 났다. 그것은 그저 수천 명의 신사들이 매시간 두드리는 것 같은 평범한 노크였으나, 그 소리에 교수와 내 심장이 요란하게 뛰었다. 우리는 서로를 마주보고 함께 현관으로 나갔다. 우리는 저마다 갖가지 무기를 쓸 채비를 하고 있었다 — 영적인 것은 왼손에, 치명적인 것은 오른손에. 반 헬싱이 빗장을 당겨 문을 반쯤 연 채로 양손을 행동할 채비를 한 채 뒤로 물러섰다. 문 가까이 계단 위에서 고달밍 경과 퀸시 모리스를 보았을 때, 우리 가슴의 기쁨이 분명 얼굴에 드러났을 것이다. 그들은 재빨리 들어와 뒤로 문을 닫았고, 고달밍 경이 현관을 따라 걸어오며 말했다.\n\n\"다 잘됐습니다. 두 곳을 다 찾아서, 각각 여섯 상자씩, 모두 없애버렸어요!\"\n\n\"없애요?\" 교수가 물었다.\n\n\"그가 못 쓰게 만들었지요!\" 우리는 한동안 잠자코 있었고, 이윽고 퀸시가 말했다.\n\n\"여기서 기다리는 것 말고는 할 일이 없군요. 하지만 그가 다섯 시까지 나타나지 않으면 우리는 출발해야 합니다. 해진 뒤에 하커 부인을 홀로 두어서는 안 되니까요.\"\n\n\"이제 곧 그가 여기 올 것이오.\" 수첩을 들여다보고 있던 반 헬싱이 말했다. \"잘 보시오, 부인의 전보에서 그는 카팩스에서 남쪽으로 갔다고 했소. 이는 그가 강을 건너려 했다는 뜻이고, 그것은 물이 잔잔할 때에만 할 수 있는데, 그 때라야 한 시 조금 전쯤이오. 그가 남쪽으로 갔다는 것은 우리에게 어떤 의미가 있소. 그는 아직 의심만 할 뿐이오. 그래서 카팩스에서 먼저, 방해받을 가능성이 가장 적다고 의심할 만한 곳으로 갔던 것이오. 두 분은 그보다 조금 앞서 버몬지에 있었던 게 틀림없소. 그가 아직 여기 오지 않은 것은 그가 다음으로 마일 엔드에 갔음을 보여주오. 거기서 그는 시간이 좀 걸렸을 것이오. 그러자면 어떤 식으로든 강을 건너 옮겨져야 했을 테니까. 내 말을 믿으시오, 친구들, 이제 우리는 오래 기다릴 필요가 없을 것이오. 우리는 어떤 기회도 헛되이 하지 않도록 공격 계획을 미리 세워두어야 하오. 쉿, 이제 시간이 없소. 모두 무기를 들으시오! 채비하시오!\" 그가 말하면서 경고하듯 손을 들어 보였다. 우리 모두 누군가 현관문 자물쇠에 열쇠를 살며시 꽂는 소리를 들을 수 있었기 때문이다.\n\n그런 순간에도 나는 지배적인 정신이 스스로를 내세우는 방식에 감탄하지 않을 수 없었다. 세계 각지에서의 온갖 사냥과 모험에서, 행동 계획을 짜는 일은 언제나 퀸시 모리스의 몫이었고, 아서와 나는 그에게 절대적으로 복종하는 데 익숙해 있었다. 이제 그 옛 습관이 본능적으로 되살아나는 듯했다. 방 안을 재빨리 둘러보고서 그는 즉시 우리의 공격 계획을 짰고, 한마디 말도 없이 몸짓만으로 우리를 저마다 제 위치에 세웠다. 반 헬싱과 하커와 나는 문 바로 뒤에 섰으니, 문이 열리면 교수가 문을 지키고 우리 둘은 들어오는 자와 문 사이로 끼어들 수 있게 했다. 고달밍은 뒤에, 퀸시는 앞에 서서, 창문 앞으로 움직일 채비를 하고 보이지 않는 자리에 섰다. 우리는 매초가 악몽처럼 느리게 흘러가게 만드는 긴장 속에서 기다렸다. 느리고 조심스러운 발소리가 현관을 따라왔다. 백작은 분명 무언가 기습에 대비하고 있었다 — 적어도 그것을 두려워하고 있었다.\n\n별안간 그는 단번에 방 안으로 뛰어들어, 우리 가운데 누구도 그를 막으려 손을 들기도 전에 우리 곁을 지나쳐 길을 텄다. 그 움직임에는 무언가 표범 같은 것이, 무언가 인간 같지 않은 것이 있어서, 우리 모두 그가 들이닥친 충격에서 도리어 정신을 차리게 하는 듯했다. 맨 먼저 움직인 것은 하커였다. 그는 재빠른 동작으로 집 앞쪽 방으로 통하는 문 앞에 몸을 던졌다. 백작이 우리를 보자 그의 얼굴에 끔찍한 으르렁거림 같은 것이 스쳐, 길고 뾰족한 송곳니가 드러났다. 그러나 그 사악한 미소는 곧 사자 같은 경멸의 차가운 응시로 바뀌었다. 우리 모두가 한 충동으로 그에게 다가가자 그의 표정이 다시 변했다. 더 잘 짜인 공격 계획이 없었던 것은 안타까운 일이었다. 바로 그 순간에도 나는 우리가 무엇을 해야 할지 의아했기 때문이다. 우리의 치명적인 무기가 우리에게 무슨 소용이 있을지 나 자신도 알 수 없었다. 하커는 분명 그것을 시험해볼 작정이었으니, 그는 자신의 커다란 쿠크리 칼을 빼들고 그에게 사납고 갑작스러운 일격을 가했다. 그 일격은 강력했다. 다만 백작이 악마처럼 빠르게 뒤로 뛴 덕분에 그가 살아남았을 뿐이다. 일 초만 늦었어도 그 예리한 칼날이 그의 심장을 꿰뚫었을 것이다. 사실 칼끝은 그의 외투 천만을 베어, 넓은 틈이 생겼고 그곳에서 지폐 한 뭉치와 금화 한 줄기가 쏟아져 나왔다. 백작의 얼굴 표정이 어찌나 지옥 같았던지, 나는 한순간 하커가 걱정되었다. 그가 또 한 번 내리치려 그 무서운 칼을 다시 높이 치켜드는 것을 보았는데도 말이다. 나는 본능적으로 보호하려는 충동에 이끌려 왼손에 십자고상과 성체를 든 채 앞으로 나아갔다. 거대한 힘이 내 팔을 타고 흐르는 것을 느꼈고, 우리 각자가 저마다 자발적으로 똑같은 동작을 취하자 괴물이 그 앞에서 움츠리며 물러나는 것을 보고도 나는 놀라지 않았다. 그때 백작의 얼굴에 떠오른 증오와 좌절된 악의, 분노와 지옥 같은 격분의 표정은 도저히 말로 옮길 수 없을 것이다. 그의 밀랍 같은 낯빛은 불타는 눈과의 대조로 푸르스름한 노란빛이 되었고, 이마의 붉은 흉터는 창백한 살갗 위에서 펄떡이는 상처처럼 도드라졌다. 다음 순간, 그는 하커의 일격이 떨어지기 전에 굽이치는 몸짓으로 그의 팔 아래로 빠져나가, 바닥에서 돈 한 줌을 움켜쥐고는 방을 가로질러 내달아 창문으로 몸을 던졌다. 깨진 유리가 와장창 반짝이며 부서지는 가운데, 그는 아래의 포석 깔린 마당으로 굴러떨어졌다. 유리가 산산이 부서지는 소리 사이로, 나는 소브린 금화 몇 개가 포석 위에 떨어지며 내는 \"땡그랑\" 하는 금속음을 들을 수 있었다.\n\n우리가 달려가서 보니, 그는 다친 데 없이 땅에서 솟구쳐 올랐다. 그는 계단을 뛰어올라 포석 깔린 마당을 가로지른 뒤 마구간 문을 밀어 열었다. 그곳에서 그는 돌아서서 우리에게 말했다.\n\n\"너희가 나를 좌절시킬 줄 아느냐, 너희가 — 그 핏기 없는 얼굴을 한 줄로 늘어세우고는, 마치 푸줏간의 양 떼처럼 말이다. 너희는 머지않아 후회하게 될 것이다, 너희 하나하나가! 너희는 나에게 쉴 곳을 남겨두지 않았다고 여기겠지만, 나에게는 더 있다. 내 복수는 이제 막 시작되었을 뿐! 나는 그것을 수백 년에 걸쳐 펼칠 것이고, 시간은 내 편이다. 너희가 모두 사랑하는 너희의 여인들은 이미 내 것이다. 그리고 그들을 통해 너희와 다른 자들도 머지않아 내 것이 되리라 — 내 명령을 따르고, 내가 먹이를 원할 때 내 자칼이 될 내 피조물 말이다. 흥!\" 경멸하는 비웃음과 함께 그는 재빨리 문을 빠져나갔고, 우리는 그가 뒤로 문을 잠그는 녹슨 빗장이 삐걱대는 소리를 들었다. 그 너머의 문 하나가 열렸다 닫혔다. 우리 가운데 가장 먼저 입을 연 것은 교수였다. 마구간을 통해 그를 뒤쫓기가 어렵다는 것을 깨닫고, 우리는 현관 쪽으로 움직였다.\n\n\"우리는 무언가를 알게 됐소 — 많은 것을! 그 호기로운 말에도 불구하고, 그는 우리를 두려워하오. 그는 시간을 두려워하고, 결핍을 두려워하오! 그렇지 않다면 어찌 그가 그토록 서둘렀겠소? 그의 말투 자체가 그를 드러내오, 내 귀가 나를 속인 게 아니라면 말이오. 어찌하여 저 돈을 가져갔겠소? 두 사람은 어서 뒤쫓으시오. 두 사람은 야수를 쫓는 사냥꾼이니, 그것을 잘 알 것이오. 나로서는, 그가 돌아오더라도 여기 있는 어떤 것도 그에게 쓸모가 없도록 확실히 해두겠소.\" 그는 이렇게 말하면서 남은 돈을 자기 주머니에 넣고, 하커가 남겨둔 그대로 묶음 속의 권리증서들을 챙긴 뒤, 나머지 것들을 모두 빈 벽난로 속으로 쓸어넣고는 성냥으로 불을 붙였다.\n\n고달밍과 모리스는 마당으로 달려나갔고, 하커는 백작을 뒤쫓으려 창문에서 몸을 내려뜨렸다. 그러나 백작은 마구간 문을 잠가버렸고, 그들이 그것을 억지로 열었을 무렵에는 그의 흔적이 보이지 않았다. 반 헬싱과 나는 집 뒤쪽에서 알아보려 했으나, 마구간 골목은 인적이 없었고 아무도 그가 떠나는 것을 보지 못했다.\n\n이제 오후도 저물어, 해질 때가 멀지 않았다. 우리는 우리의 일이 끝장났음을 인정해야 했다. 무거운 마음으로 우리는 교수의 말에 동의했다.\n\n\"미나 부인에게로 돌아갑시다 — 가엾고 가엾은 미나 부인에게. 지금 우리가 할 수 있는 일은 다 했소. 그리고 적어도 거기서는 우리가 그녀를 지킬 수 있소. 그러나 우리는 절망할 필요가 없소. 흙 상자가 단 하나 더 남아 있고, 우리는 그것을 찾아내려 애써야 하오. 그 일이 끝나면 모든 것이 아직 잘될 수도 있소.\" 그가 하커를 위로하려 애써 용감하게 말하는 것을 나는 알 수 있었다. 가엾은 친구는 완전히 무너져 있었다. 이따금 그는 참지 못하고 나직한 신음을 흘렸다 — 그는 자기 아내를 생각하고 있었다.\n\n슬픈 마음으로 우리는 내 집으로 돌아왔는데, 거기서 우리를 기다리고 있던 하커 부인을 보니, 그녀의 용기와 헌신에 걸맞은 명랑한 기색이었다. 그녀는 우리 얼굴을 보자 자신의 얼굴이 죽은 사람처럼 창백해졌다. 잠시 동안 그녀는 마치 남몰래 기도라도 하듯 눈을 감았다. 그러고는 명랑하게 말했다.\n\n\"여러분께 아무리 감사드려도 부족할 거예요. 오, 가엾은 내 사랑!\" 그녀는 이렇게 말하며 남편의 잿빛 머리를 두 손으로 감싸고 입맞추었다. \"당신의 가엾은 머리를 여기 누이고 쉬어요. 모든 것이 아직 잘될 거예요, 여보! 하느님께서 그 선하신 뜻으로 그리하고자 하신다면 우리를 지켜주실 거예요.\" 가엾은 친구는 신음했다. 그의 그 숭고한 비탄에는 말이 들어설 자리가 없었다.\n\n우리는 함께 마지못해 하는 식의 저녁식사를 했는데, 내 생각에 그것이 우리 모두를 어느 정도 기운나게 한 듯하다. 그것은 어쩌면 굶주린 사람들에게 음식이 주는 한낱 동물적인 온기였을지도 모른다 — 우리 가운데 누구도 아침식사 이후로 아무것도 먹지 못했으니 말이다 — 아니면 동료애가 도움이 되었을지도 모른다. 어쨌든 우리는 모두 덜 비참해졌고, 내일을 아주 희망 없는 것만은 아니라고 여기게 되었다. 약속한 대로 우리는 하커 부인에게 그동안 있었던 모든 일을 이야기했고, 그녀는 남편이 위험에 처한 듯한 대목에서는 이따금 눈처럼 하얗게 질리고, 그의 자기에 대한 헌신이 드러나는 대목에서는 또 이따금 붉어지면서도, 용감하고 차분하게 귀를 기울였다. 하커가 그토록 무모하게 백작에게 달려든 대목에 이르자, 그녀는 남편의 팔에 매달려, 마치 자기가 매달림으로써 닥쳐올 어떤 해로움으로부터도 그를 지킬 수 있기라도 한 듯 꼭 붙들었다. 그러나 그녀는 이야기가 모두 끝나 일이 바로 지금까지 이르고 나서야 비로소 입을 열었다. 그러고는 남편의 손을 놓지 않은 채 우리 가운데서 일어서서 말했다. 아, 내가 그 광경을 조금이라도 전할 수 있다면. 젊음과 생기의 빛나는 아름다움을 온통 갖춘 그 다정하고 다정한, 선하고 선한 여인이, 자기도 의식하고 있는 그 이마의 붉은 흉터를 — 우리는 그것이 어디서 어떻게 생겼는지를 떠올리며 이를 갈며 바라보았다 — 지닌 채 서 있는 모습을. 우리의 음울한 증오에 맞선 그녀의 사랑 어린 따뜻함을, 우리의 온갖 두려움과 의심에 맞선 그녀의 부드러운 믿음을. 그리고 상징의 차원에서는, 그 모든 선함과 순결함과 믿음을 지닌 그녀가 하느님으로부터 버림받은 자라는 것을 우리가 알고 있었음을.\n\n\"조너선,\" 그녀가 말했는데, 그 말은 사랑과 다정함이 어찌나 가득했던지 그녀의 입술에서 음악처럼 들렸다. \"조너선, 여보, 그리고 여러분 모두 나의 진실하고 진실한 벗들, 저는 여러분이 이 끔찍한 시간 내내 한 가지를 마음에 새겨두기를 바라요. 저는 여러분이 싸워야 한다는 것을 알아요 — 참된 루시가 이후에 살 수 있도록 거짓된 루시를 없앴듯이, 여러분이 없애야만 한다는 것을요. 하지만 그것은 증오의 일이 아니에요. 이 모든 비참을 빚어낸 그 가엾은 영혼이야말로 가장 슬픈 경우랍니다. 생각해보세요, 그 역시 자신의 더 나은 부분이 영적 불멸을 얻을 수 있도록 더 못한 부분에서 파멸될 때, 그의 기쁨이 어떠할지를요. 여러분은 그에게도 연민을 품어야 해요. 비록 그것이 그를 파멸시키는 여러분의 손을 멈추게 하지는 못하더라도 말이에요.\"\n\n그녀가 말하는 동안 나는 남편의 얼굴이 어두워지며 일그러지는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 마치 그의 안에 든 격정이 그의 존재를 그 속까지 오그라뜨리기라도 하는 듯이. 본능적으로 아내의 손을 잡은 그의 손아귀에 힘이 들어가, 마침내 그의 손가락 마디가 하얗게 보였다. 그녀는 분명 겪었을 그 고통에 움찔하지도 않고, 그 어느 때보다 더 애원하는 듯한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다. 그녀가 말을 멈추자 그는 벌떡 일어서며, 거의 그녀의 손에서 제 손을 뜯어내듯 하며 말했다.\n\n\"우리가 노리는 그자의 그 지상의 목숨을 없앨 수 있을 만큼만이라도, 하느님께서 그자를 내 손에 넘겨주시기를. 그 너머로 그의 영혼을 영원무궁토록 불타는 지옥으로 보낼 수 있다면, 나는 그렇게 하겠소!\"\n\n\"오, 쉿! 오, 쉿! 선하신 하느님의 이름으로요. 그런 말은 하지 말아요, 조너선, 내 남편. 안 그러면 당신이 나를 두려움과 공포로 짓눌러버릴 거예요. 생각해봐요, 여보 — 저는 이 길고 긴 하루 내내 그것을 생각해왔어요 — 어쩌면…… 언젠가는…… 저 역시도 그런 연민이 필요할지 모른다는 것을요. 그리고 당신 같은 어떤 다른 이가 — 그리고 똑같이 분노할 만한 이유를 가진 이가 — 저에게 그것을 거절할지도 모른다는 것을요! 오, 내 남편! 정말이지 다른 길이 있었다면 당신에게 이런 생각을 덜어주었을 거예요. 하지만 저는 하느님께서 당신의 그 거친 말을, 더없이 사랑이 깊고 모질게 상처 입은 한 사람의 가슴 찢어지는 통곡 외에 다른 것으로는 마음에 담아두지 않으시기를 기도해요. 오, 하느님, 이 가엾은 흰 머리칼이 그가 겪은 고난의 증거가 되게 하소서, 평생 아무 잘못도 하지 않았으나 이토록 많은 슬픔이 닥친 이 사람의 증거가 되게 하소서.\"\n\n이제 우리 남자들은 모두 눈물을 흘리고 있었다. 도저히 참을 수 없어, 우리는 드러내놓고 울었다. 그녀의 더 다정한 권고가 받아들여진 것을 보고 그녀도 울었다. 그녀의 남편은 그녀 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 두 팔로 그녀를 감싸안은 채, 그녀의 옷자락 주름 속에 얼굴을 묻었다. 반 헬싱이 우리에게 손짓했고, 우리는 그 사랑하는 두 마음을 그들의 하느님과 함께 둘만 있도록 남겨두고 살그머니 방을 빠져나왔다.\n\n그들이 물러가기 전에 교수는 흡혈귀가 올 것에 대비해 방을 단단히 손보고, 하커 부인에게 평안히 쉬어도 된다고 안심시켰다. 그녀는 그 믿음에 자신을 길들이려 애썼고, 분명 남편을 위해서 만족한 듯 보이려 애썼다. 그것은 용감한 분투였고, 내 생각으로는, 또한 내 믿음으로는, 보람이 없지 않았다. 반 헬싱은 어떤 비상시에든 둘 가운데 누구라도 울릴 수 있도록 종 하나를 가까이 두었다. 그들이 물러간 뒤, 퀸시와 고달밍과 나는 우리가 밤을 나누어 번갈아 앉아 지키며, 가엾고 상처 입은 부인의 안전을 살피기로 정했다. 첫 번째 번은 퀸시에게 돌아갔으니, 나머지 우리는 되도록 빨리 잠자리에 들어야 한다. 고달밍은 두 번째 번이라 이미 잠자리에 들었다. 이제 내 일이 끝났으니, 나도 잠자리에 들어야겠다.\n\n「조너선 하커의 일기」\n\n10월 3일에서 4일, 자정 무렵. — 나는 어제가 결코 끝나지 않을 것만 같았다. 깨어나면 사정이 달라져 있을 테고, 이제 어떤 변화든 더 나은 쪽일 수밖에 없으리라는 일종의 맹목적인 믿음에 사로잡혀, 나는 잠을 갈망했다. 헤어지기 전에 우리는 다음 단계가 무엇이 되어야 할지를 논의했으나, 아무 결론에도 이르지 못했다. 우리가 아는 것이라고는 흙 상자 하나가 남아 있다는 것, 그리고 그것이 어디 있는지는 백작만이 안다는 것뿐이었다. 만일 그가 숨어 지내기로 마음먹는다면, 그는 몇 해고 우리를 좌절시킬 수 있을 것이다. 그리고 그동안에! — 그 생각은 너무도 끔찍해서, 나는 지금조차 감히 그것을 떠올릴 수 없다. 다만 이것만은 안다. 일찍이 온전히 완벽한 여인이 있었다면, 그 사람은 바로 나의 가엾고 부당하게 상처 입은 사랑이라는 것을. 나는 어젯밤 그녀의 그 다정한 연민으로 그녀를 천 배는 더 사랑하게 되었다. 그 연민은 괴물에 대한 나 자신의 증오를 비열하게 보이게 했다. 분명 하느님께서는 그런 존재를 잃어 세상이 더 빈곤해지도록 허락하지 않으실 것이다. 이것이 나에게는 희망이다. 우리는 모두 이제 암초를 향해 떠밀려가고 있고, 믿음만이 우리의 유일한 닻이다. 하느님 감사합니다! 미나는 자고 있고, 꿈도 없이 자고 있다. 그토록 끔찍한 기억들을 밑바탕에 두고 있으니, 그녀의 꿈이 어떠할지 두렵다. 내가 보아온 바로, 그녀는 해질 무렵 이래로 이토록 평온한 적이 없었다. 그때, 한동안 그녀의 얼굴 위로 삼월의 거센 바람 뒤에 찾아오는 봄과도 같은 안식이 깃들었다. 그때는 그것이 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 붉은 노을의 부드러움이라고 여겼으나, 어쩐지 지금은 거기에 더 깊은 의미가 있는 것 같다. 나는 지쳐 있는데도 — 죽도록 지쳐 있는데도 — 잠이 오지 않는다. 그래도 나는 잠을 청해야 한다. 내일을 생각해야 하고, 또한 …… 하기까지 나에게는 쉴 틈이 없으니까.\n\n이후. — 나는 잠이 들었던 모양이다. 미나가 깨워서 깼는데, 그녀는 화들짝 놀란 얼굴로 침대에 일어나 앉아 있었다. 우리가 방을 어둠 속에 두지 않았으므로 나는 쉽게 볼 수 있었다. 그녀는 내 입에 경고하듯 손을 얹고는, 이제 내 귀에 속삭였다.\n\n\"쉿! 복도에 누군가 있어요!\" 나는 살그머니 일어나 방을 가로질러 가서 가만히 문을 열었다.\n\n바로 바깥, 매트리스 위에 모리스 씨가 또렷이 깨어 누워 있었다. 그는 나에게 속삭이며 조용히 하라고 경고의 손을 들었다.\n\n\"쉿! 침대로 돌아가십시오. 다 괜찮습니다. 우리 가운데 하나가 밤새 여기 있을 겁니다. 우리는 어떤 위험도 무릅쓸 생각이 없으니까요!\"\n\n그의 표정과 몸짓이 더 이상의 논의를 막았으므로, 나는 돌아와 미나에게 그대로 전했다. 그녀는 한숨을 쉬었고, 가엾고 창백한 얼굴에 그야말로 희미한 미소의 그림자가 스치더니, 두 팔로 나를 감싸안고 부드럽게 말했다.\n\n\"오, 선하고 용감한 사람들을 주셔서 하느님 감사합니다!\" 한숨과 함께 그녀는 다시 잠 속으로 가라앉았다. 나는 잠이 오지 않아 지금 이것을 쓰고 있으나, 다시 잠을 청해보아야 한다.\n\n10월 4일, 아침. — 밤사이 또 한 번 나는 미나에게 깨워졌다. 이번에는 우리 모두 단잠을 잤으니, 다가오는 새벽의 잿빛이 창문을 또렷한 직사각형으로 만들고 있었고, 가스 불꽃은 빛의 원반이라기보다 한 점 티끌 같았다. 그녀가 다급하게 나에게 말했다.\n\n\"가서 교수님을 불러와요. 당장 그분을 뵙고 싶어요.\"\n\n\"왜요?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"제게 생각이 있어요. 아마 밤사이에 떠올라, 제가 모르는 새 무르익은 것 같아요. 그분이 새벽이 오기 전에 저에게 최면을 걸어야 해요. 그러면 제가 말할 수 있을 거예요. 어서 가요, 여보. 시간이 가까워지고 있어요.\" 나는 문으로 갔다. 수어드 박사가 매트리스 위에서 쉬고 있다가, 나를 보더니 벌떡 일어섰다.\n\n\"무슨 일이 잘못됐습니까?\" 그가 놀라서 물었다.\n\n\"아닙니다.\" 내가 대답했다. \"다만 미나가 반 헬싱 박사님을 당장 뵙고 싶어 합니다.\"\n\n\"제가 가지요.\" 그는 이렇게 말하고는 교수의 방으로 서둘러 갔다.\n\n이삼 분쯤 뒤에 반 헬싱이 실내복 차림으로 방에 들어왔고, 모리스 씨와 고달밍 경은 문간에서 수어드 박사와 함께 이런저런 것을 묻고 있었다. 교수는 미나가 미소 짓는 것을 보자 — 그야말로 미소가 그의 얼굴에서 근심을 몰아냈다. 그는 두 손을 비비며 말했다.\n\n\"오, 친애하는 미나 부인, 이것은 정말이지 큰 변화로군요. 보시오! 벗 조너선, 우리는 오늘 예전 그대로의 우리 친애하는 미나 부인을 되찾았소!\" 그러고는 그녀에게 돌아서서 명랑하게 말했다. \"그래, 제가 당신을 위해 무엇을 하면 되겠소? 이런 시각에 부인이 아무 일도 없이 저를 찾지는 않았을 테니 말이오.\"\n\n\"제게 최면을 걸어주세요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"새벽이 오기 전에 해주세요. 그때라면 제가 말할 수 있을 것 같고, 거리낌 없이 말할 수 있을 것 같아요. 어서요, 시간이 얼마 없어요!\" 그는 한마디 말도 없이 그녀에게 침대에 일어나 앉으라고 손짓했다.\n\n그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보며, 그는 그녀의 머리 꼭대기에서부터 아래로 두 손을 번갈아 그녀 앞에서 움직이기 시작했다. 미나는 몇 분 동안 그를 뚫어지게 응시했는데, 그동안 나는 어떤 고비가 다가오고 있음을 느껴 내 심장이 망치질하듯 뛰었다. 차츰 그녀의 눈이 감겼고, 그녀는 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있었다. 가슴이 가만히 오르내리는 것으로만 그녀가 살아 있음을 알 수 있었다. 교수는 손짓을 몇 번 더 하다가 멈추었는데, 나는 그의 이마가 굵은 땀방울로 뒤덮인 것을 볼 수 있었다. 미나가 눈을 떴다. 그러나 그녀는 같은 여인 같지 않았다. 그녀의 눈에는 아득한 빛이 어려 있었고, 그녀의 목소리에는 나에게 낯선 슬픈 몽롱함이 깃들어 있었다. 교수는 침묵을 명하듯 손을 들고, 나에게 다른 이들을 데려오라고 손짓했다. 그들은 발끝으로 들어와 뒤로 문을 닫고는, 침대 발치에 서서 지켜보았다. 미나는 그들을 보지 못하는 듯했다. 그 정적을 반 헬싱의 목소리가 깨뜨렸는데, 그녀의 생각의 흐름을 끊지 않도록 낮고 고른 어조였다.\n\n\"당신은 어디 있소?\" 대답은 무덤덤하게 흘러나왔다.\n\n\"모르겠어요. 잠에게는 제 것이라 부를 곳이 없어요.\" 몇 분 동안 침묵이 흘렀다. 미나는 뻣뻣하게 앉아 있었고, 교수는 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보며 서 있었다. 나머지 우리는 감히 숨도 제대로 쉬지 못했다. 방이 점점 밝아지고 있었다. 반 헬싱 박사는 미나의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 나에게 차양을 걷으라고 손짓했다. 내가 그렇게 하자 날이 바로 우리에게 들이닥친 듯했다. 붉은 줄기가 솟구쳐 오르고, 장밋빛 빛이 방 안에 퍼지는 듯했다. 그 순간 교수가 다시 입을 열었다.\n\n\"이제 당신은 어디 있소?\" 대답은 몽롱하게, 그러나 의도를 담고 흘러나왔다. 마치 그녀가 무언가를 해석하고 있는 듯했다. 나는 그녀가 자신의 속기 기록을 읽을 때 똑같은 어조를 쓰는 것을 들은 적이 있다.\n\n\"모르겠어요. 모든 것이 제게 낯설어요!\"\n\n\"무엇이 보이오?\"\n\n\"아무것도 보이지 않아요. 온통 어두워요.\"\n\n\"무엇이 들리오?\" 나는 교수의 인내심 어린 목소리에 깃든 긴장을 알아챌 수 있었다.\n\n\"물이 찰싹이는 소리요. 물이 콸콸 흘러 지나가고, 작은 물결이 일어요. 바깥에서 그 소리가 들려요.\"\n\n\"그렇다면 당신은 배 위에 있소?\" 우리는 서로를 마주보며, 저마다 상대에게서 무언가를 알아내려 애썼다. 우리는 생각하기조차 두려웠다. 대답은 재빨리 흘러나왔다.\n\n\"오, 그래요!\"\n\n\"또 무엇이 들리오?\"\n\n\"머리 위에서 사람들이 이리저리 뛰어다니며 쿵쿵거리는 소리요. 사슬이 삐걱대는 소리와, 캡스턴의 멈춤쇠가 톱니에 떨어지며 내는 요란한 짤랑 소리도 들려요.\"\n\n\"당신은 무엇을 하고 있소?\"\n\n\"저는 가만히 있어요 — 오, 너무도 가만히. 마치 죽음 같아요!\" 목소리는 잠든 사람의 것 같은 깊은 숨결 속으로 잦아들었고, 떠 있던 눈이 다시 감겼다.\n\n이 무렵 해가 떠올라, 우리는 모두 한낮의 환한 빛 속에 있었다. 반 헬싱 박사는 미나의 어깨에 손을 얹고, 그녀의 머리를 부드럽게 베개 위에 누였다. 그녀는 잠시 잠든 아이처럼 누워 있다가, 긴 한숨과 함께 깨어나 자기를 둘러싼 우리 모두를 보고는 놀라워하며 응시했다. \"제가 잠결에 말을 했나요?\" 그녀가 한 말은 그뿐이었다. 그러나 그녀는 굳이 말하지 않아도 상황을 아는 듯했다. 다만 자기가 무슨 말을 했는지를 몹시 알고 싶어 했다. 교수가 그 대화를 되풀이해 들려주자, 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"그렇다면 한순간도 허비할 수 없어요. 아직 너무 늦지 않았을지도 몰라요!\" 모리스 씨와 고달밍 경이 문 쪽으로 나서려 했으나, 교수의 차분한 목소리가 그들을 불러세웠다.\n\n\"멈추시오, 친구들. 그 배가 어디 있었든, 그녀가 말하는 동안 닻을 올리고 있었소. 지금 이 순간 당신들의 그 거대한 런던 항구에는 닻을 올리는 배가 숱하게 많소. 그 가운데 당신들이 찾는 것이 어느 배겠소? 우리가 비록 어디로 이끌릴지는 모르나, 다시금 단서를 얻은 것을 하느님께 감사드리오. 우리는 다소 눈이 멀어 있었소. 인간의 방식대로 눈이 멀어 있었소. 우리가 돌이켜 볼 수 있을 때라야, 만일 우리가 볼 수 있었을 것을 볼 수만 있었더라면 내다보며 보았을 것을 비로소 보게 되니 말이오! 아아, 한데 이 문장은 진창이로군. 그렇지 않소? 우리는 이제 백작이 그 돈을 움켜쥐었을 때 그의 마음속에 무엇이 있었는지 알 수 있소. 조너선의 그 사나운 칼이 그를, 그조차 두려워하는 위험에 빠뜨렸는데도 말이오. 그는 달아날 작정이었소. 내 말을 들으시오, 달아날 작정이었소! 그는 흙 상자가 단 하나만 남고, 한 무리의 사람들이 여우를 쫓는 개들처럼 뒤를 따르는 마당에, 이 런던이 더 이상 자기 있을 곳이 아님을 알았던 것이오. 그는 자신의 마지막 흙 상자를 배에 실었고, 이 땅을 떠나는 것이오. 그는 달아날 생각이지만, 안 되오! 우리가 그를 뒤쫓을 것이오. 탤리 호! 벗 아서가 붉은 사냥복을 차려입을 때 외치듯 말이오! 우리의 그 늙은 여우는 교활하오. 오! 어찌나 교활한지, 우리도 꾀로써 뒤쫓아야 하오. 나 역시 교활하니, 머지않아 그의 마음을 읽어낼 것이오. 그동안 우리는 평안히 쉬어도 좋소. 우리 사이에는 그가 건너고 싶어 하지 않는 물이 있고, 설령 건너려 해도 건널 수 없는 물이 있으니 말이오 — 배가 육지에 닿지 않는 한, 그리고 닿더라도 만조나 물이 잔잔할 때에만 가능하니. 보시오, 해가 막 떠올랐고, 해질 때까지 온종일이 우리의 것이오. 우리 모두 목욕을 하고, 옷을 갈아입고, 우리 모두에게 필요한 아침식사를 합시다. 그가 우리와 같은 땅에 있지 않으니 이제 편히 먹을 수 있을 것이오.\" 미나가 애원하는 눈빛으로 그를 보며 물었다.\n\n\"하지만 그가 우리 곁을 떠났는데, 어찌하여 우리가 그를 더 쫓아야 하나요?\" 그는 그녀의 손을 잡고 다독이며 대답했다.\n\n\"아직은 내게 아무것도 묻지 마시오. 아침식사를 하고 나면, 그때 모든 질문에 답하리다.\" 그는 더는 말하려 하지 않았고, 우리는 옷을 갈아입으러 흩어졌다.\n\n아침식사 후에 미나가 자기 물음을 되풀이했다. 그는 잠시 그녀를 엄숙하게 바라보다가 슬프게 말했다.\n\n\"왜냐하면, 나의 친애하고 친애하는 미나 부인, 이제는 그 어느 때보다도 우리가 그를 찾아내야 하기 때문이오. 설령 지옥의 아가리까지 그를 뒤쫓아야 한다 해도 말이오!\" 그녀가 점점 창백해지며 가냘프게 물었다.\n\n\"왜요?\"\n\n\"왜냐하면,\" 그가 엄숙하게 대답했다. \"그는 수백 년을 살 수 있으나, 당신은 한낱 필멸의 여인이기 때문이오. 그가 한번 당신의 목에 그 표를 남긴 이상, 이제 시간이 두려워해야 할 것이 된 것이오.\"\n\n나는 마침 때맞춰 그녀가 앞으로 까무러쳐 쓰러지는 것을 받아 안았다.\n\n수어드 박사의 축음기 일기, 반 헬싱이 구술함\n\n조너선 하커에게.\n당신은 사랑하는 미나 부인 곁에 머물러 있어야 하오. 우리는 수색에 — 이것을 수색이라 부를 수 있다면 말이오, 사실 이것은 수색이 아니라 확인이며, 우리는 다만 확증을 구할 뿐이니 — 나서려 하오. 그러나 당신은 머물러 오늘 그녀를 돌보시오. 이것이 당신의 가장 훌륭하고 가장 성스러운 소임이오. 오늘은 어떤 것도 그를 이곳에서 찾을 수 없을 것이오. 당신이 알도록 일러두겠으니, 그러면 우리 넷이 이미 아는 바를 당신도 알게 될 것이오. 내가 그들에게 이야기했기 때문이오. 우리의 적인 그는 떠나버렸소. 그는 트란실바니아에 있는 자기 성으로 돌아갔소. 나는 마치 거대한 불의 손이 벽에 그것을 적어놓기라도 한 듯 그것을 잘 알고 있소. 그는 어떤 식으로든 이것을 준비해두었으며, 그 마지막 흙 상자는 어딘가로 실어 보낼 채비가 되어 있었소. 이를 위해 그는 돈을 챙겼고, 이를 위해 그는 막판에 서둘렀소, 해가 지기 전에 우리가 그를 붙잡지 못하도록 말이오. 그것이 그의 마지막 희망이었소, 다만 그가 가엾은 루시 양 — 그가 자기처럼 되었다고 여긴 — 이 그를 위해 열어두었으리라 생각한 무덤 속에 숨을 수도 있었던 것을 제하면 말이오. 그러나 시간이 없었소. 그것이 실패하자 그는 곧장 자신의 마지막 수단으로 — 만일 내가 이중의 뜻을 노린다면 마지막 토루(土壘)라 말할 수도 있겠소 — 향했소. 그는 영리하오, 오, 참으로 영리하오! 그는 여기서의 자기 노름이 끝났음을 알았고, 그래서 본국으로 돌아가기로 결심한 것이오. 그는 자기가 온 항로를 따라 가는 배를 찾아내어 그것을 탄 것이오. 우리는 이제 어떤 배인지, 어디로 향하는지 알아내러 떠나오. 그것을 알아내고 나면 돌아와 당신에게 모두 이야기하겠소. 그러면 우리는 새로운 희망으로 당신과 가엾고 사랑스러운 미나 부인을 위로하겠소. 곰곰이 생각해보면 그것은 희망이 될 것이오. 모든 것이 잃어버린 것은 아니라는 희망 말이오. 우리가 쫓는 바로 이 피조물은 런던에 이르기까지 수백 년이 걸렸소. 그런데도 단 하루 만에, 그의 처치 방법을 알아낸 우리는 그를 몰아냈소. 그는 많은 해악을 끼칠 만큼 강하고 우리처럼 고통받지 않지만, 유한한 존재요. 그러나 우리는 저마다 자기 목적 안에서 강하며, 함께라면 더욱 강하오. 다시 용기를 내시오, 미나 부인의 사랑하는 남편이여. 이 싸움은 이제 막 시작되었을 뿐이며, 끝내 우리가 이길 것이오 — 하느님께서 높은 곳에 앉으시어 당신의 자녀들을 지켜보심이 확실한 것만큼이나 확실하게. 그러니 우리가 돌아올 때까지 마음을 굳게 가지시오.\n\n반 헬싱.\n조너선 하커의 일기.\n\n10월 4일. — 내가 미나에게 축음기에 담긴 반 헬싱의 전언을 읽어주자, 가엾은 그녀의 얼굴이 눈에 띄게 밝아졌다. 백작이 이 나라를 떠났다는 확신만으로도 그녀는 이미 위안을 얻었고, 위안은 그녀에게 힘이 된다. 나로서는, 그의 무시무시한 위험이 우리와 정면으로 맞서고 있지 않은 지금, 그것을 믿기란 거의 불가능하게 여겨진다. 드라큘라 성에서 겪은 나 자신의 끔찍한 경험조차 오래전에 잊힌 꿈처럼 느껴진다. 이곳, 상쾌한 가을 공기 속, 밝은 햇살 아래에서는——\n\n아아! 어찌 믿지 않을 수 있겠는가! 생각에 잠겨 있던 와중에 내 시선이 가엾은 내 사랑의 흰 이마 위 붉은 흉터에 닿았다. 그것이 남아 있는 한, 믿지 않을 수는 없다. 그리고 훗날에도 그것의 기억 자체가 믿음을 수정처럼 맑게 지켜줄 것이다. 미나와 나는 한가로이 있기가 두려워, 모든 일기를 거듭거듭 다시 살펴보았다. 어쩐 일인지 그 현실이 매번 더 크게 다가오는데도, 고통과 두려움은 덜해지는 것 같다. 전체에 걸쳐 어떤 이끄는 목적 같은 것이 드러나 있어, 그것이 위안이 된다. 미나는 어쩌면 우리가 궁극의 선을 위한 도구일지도 모른다고 말한다. 그럴지도 모른다! 나도 그녀처럼 생각해보려 한다. 우리는 아직 서로 미래에 관해 한마디도 나누지 않았다. 교수님과 다른 이들이 조사를 마치고 돌아오는 것을 본 뒤까지 기다리는 편이 낫다.\n\n하루가, 내게 다시 이렇게 흘러갈 수 있으리라 생각해본 적 없을 만큼 빠르게 지나간다. 지금은 세 시다.\n\n미나 하커의 일기.\n\n10월 5일, 오후 5시. — 보고를 위한 우리의 모임. 참석자: 반 헬싱 교수, 고달밍 경, 수어드 박사, 퀸시 모리스 씨, 조너선 하커, 미나 하커.\n\n반 헬싱 박사가 드라큘라 백작이 어떤 배로 어디를 향해 달아났는지를 알아내기 위해 낮 동안 취한 조치들을 설명했다.\n\n\"그가 트란실바니아로 돌아가려 한다는 것을 알았으니, 나는 그가 분명 도나우 강 어귀로, 혹은 흑해 어딘가로 가야 하리라고 확신했소. 그가 온 길이 바로 그 길이었기 때문이오. 우리 앞에 놓인 것은 암담한 공백이었소. 알려지지 않은 것은 모두 대단해 보이는 법이오. 그래서 우리는 무거운 마음으로, 어젯밤 흑해로 떠난 배가 무엇인지 알아내러 나섰소. 미나 부인이 돛이 펼쳐졌다고 말했으니 그는 범선에 타고 있었소. 이런 배들은 타임스의 선박 목록에 실릴 만큼 중요하지 않으므로, 우리는 고달밍 경의 제안에 따라 당신들의 로이즈로 갔소. 그곳에는 아무리 작은 배라도 출항하는 모든 배의 기록이 있소. 거기서 우리는 조수에 맞춰 출항한 흑해행 배가 단 한 척뿐임을 알아냈소. 그 배는 차리나 캐서린호로, 둘리틀 부두에서 바르나로 떠나, 거기서 다시 다른 지역들로, 또 도나우 강을 거슬러 올라간다고 했소. '오호!' 하고 나는 말했소. '바로 이 배에 백작이 타고 있구나.' 그래서 우리는 둘리틀 부두로 떠났는데, 거기서 우리는 사무실이 어찌나 작은지 사람이 사무실보다 더 커 보이는 그런 나무 사무실 안의 한 사내를 만났소. 우리는 그에게 차리나 캐서린호의 동정을 물었소. 그는 욕을 많이 했고, 얼굴이 붉고 목소리가 컸으나, 그래도 좋은 친구였소. 그리고 퀸시가 주머니에서 무언가를, 둘둘 말 때 바스락 소리가 나는 것을 꺼내어 그가 옷 깊숙이 숨겨둔 아주 작은 주머니에 넣어주자, 그는 한층 더 좋은 친구이자 우리의 겸손한 종복이 되었소. 그는 우리와 함께 와서, 거칠고 성마른 사내들 여럿에게 물어주었소. 이들 또한 목이 덜 마르게 되자 더 좋은 친구들이 되었소. 그들은 피니 꽃이니 하는 말을 많이 했고, 내가 알아듣지 못하는 다른 말들도 했으나, 무슨 뜻인지는 짐작이 갔소. 그러나 어쨌든 그들은 우리가 알고자 하는 모든 것을 말해주었소.\n\n\"그들은 자기들끼리, 어제 오후 다섯 시쯤에 어떤 사내가 몹시 서둘러 왔다는 것을 우리에게 알려주었소. 키가 크고, 야위고 창백하며, 코가 높고 이가 아주 희며, 눈은 타오르는 듯한 사내였다고 하오. 그가 온통 검은 옷차림이었는데, 다만 그에게도 그 계절에도 어울리지 않는 밀짚모자를 쓰고 있었다는 것이오. 어떤 배가 흑해로 어디를 향해 떠나는지 급히 캐물으며 그는 돈을 마구 뿌렸다고 하오. 몇몇이 그를 사무실로, 그다음 배로 안내했는데, 그는 배에 오르려 하지 않고 건널 판자의 뭍 쪽 끝에 멈춰 서서 선장을 데려와달라고 청했소. 보수를 두둑이 받게 되리라는 말을 듣고 선장이 왔고, 처음에는 욕을 많이 했으나 그는 조건에 응했소. 그러자 그 야윈 사내는 갔고, 누군가 그에게 말과 수레를 어디서 빌릴 수 있는지 일러주었소. 그는 그리로 갔다가 곧 다시 왔는데, 커다란 상자 하나가 실린 수레를 손수 몰고 있었소. 그것을 배의 짐수레에 올리는 데에는 여럿이 달려들어야 했는데도, 그는 그것을 손수 내렸소. 그는 자기 상자를 어떻게 어디에 두어야 하는지 선장에게 한참 떠들어댔으나, 선장은 그것이 못마땅하여 여러 나라 말로 그에게 욕을 하며, 원한다면 와서 어디에 둘지 직접 보라고 했소. 그러나 그는 '아니'라고, 아직은 가지 않겠다고, 할 일이 많기 때문이라고 했소. 그러자 선장은 그에게, 서두르는 편이 좋을 거라고 — 피로 물든 말로 — 자기 배가 조수가 바뀌기 전에 — 피로 물든 — 그 자리를 떠날 것이기 때문이라고 — 피로 물든 — 말했소. 그러자 그 야윈 사내는 미소 지으며, 물론 자기는 적당하다고 여길 때 가야겠으나, 그렇게 빨리 가게 된다면 놀랄 거라고 했소. 선장은 다시 여러 나라 말로 욕을 했고, 그 야윈 사내는 그에게 허리를 굽혀 인사하며, 고맙다고, 출항 전에 배에 오르는 정도로 그의 친절에 폐를 끼치겠다고 했소. 마침내 선장은 그 어느 때보다 얼굴이 붉어져, 더 많은 나라 말로 그에게, 자기 배에 — 또한 피로 물든 — 프랑스놈은 — 피니 꽃이니 들러붙고 또한 피로 물든 — 필요 없다고 말했소. 그래서 그는 가까운 곳에 선박 서류를 살 수 있는 배가 어디 있을지 물어본 뒤 떠나갔소.\n\n\"아무도 그가 어디로 갔는지 몰랐고 '눈곱만큼도 신경 쓰지 않았소', 그들의 말마따나, 그들에겐 달리 생각할 일이 있었기 때문이오 — 또다시 피로 물든 말로. 머지않아 차리나 캐서린호가 예정대로 출항하지 못하리라는 것이 모두에게 분명해졌기 때문이오. 강에서 옅은 안개가 기어오르기 시작하더니, 점점 짙어지고 또 짙어져, 머지않아 짙은 안개가 그 배와 주위 일대를 온통 뒤덮었소. 선장은 여러 나라 말로 — 아주 여러 나라 말로 — 피니 꽃이니 피니 하는 여러 나라 말로 욕을 했으나, 그는 아무것도 할 수 없었소. 물은 자꾸자꾸 차올랐고, 그는 조수를 아예 놓쳐버릴까 두려워하기 시작했소. 그가 영 심사가 사나운 차에, 마침 만조 때 그 야윈 사내가 다시 건널 판자를 올라와 자기 상자가 어디에 실렸는지 보게 해달라고 청했소. 그러자 선장은, 그놈과 그놈의 상자가 — 낡아빠지고 피니 꽃이니 잔뜩 묻은 — 지옥에나 떨어졌으면 좋겠다고 대꾸했소. 그러나 그 야윈 사내는 화내지 않고, 일등항해사와 함께 내려가 그것이 놓인 자리를 보고는, 다시 올라와 안개 속 갑판에 한동안 서 있었소. 그는 혼자 빠져나간 게 분명한데, 아무도 그를 알아채지 못했기 때문이오. 사실 그들은 그를 안중에도 두지 않았소. 머지않아 안개가 걷히기 시작하더니, 모든 것이 다시 맑아졌기 때문이오. 목마름과 피니 꽃이니 하는 말투의 내 친구들은, 그때 강 위를 오르내리던 다른 뱃사람들에게 물어보았더니 부두 둘레에 깔린 것을 빼고는 안개를 본 사람이 거의 없었음을 선장이 알게 되었을 때, 그의 욕설이 평소의 여러 나라 말투마저 능가했고 그 어느 때보다 그림처럼 생생했다는 것을 이야기하며 웃었소. 어쨌든 그 배는 썰물을 타고 나갔고, 아침이면 틀림없이 강어귀 저 아래로 멀리 내려가 있었을 거요. 그들이 우리에게 이야기해주던 무렵에는 이미 바다로 한참 나아가 있었소.\n\n\"그리하여, 나의 사랑하는 미나 부인, 우리는 한동안 쉬어야 하는 처지가 되었소. 우리의 적이 안개를 마음대로 부리며 바다 위에서 도나우 강어귀로 가는 길에 있기 때문이오. 배를 모는 데에는, 아무리 빠르게 간다 해도 시간이 걸리오. 그리고 우리가 출발하면 우리는 육로로 더 빨리 가서, 거기서 그를 만나게 되오. 우리의 가장 큰 희망은 해돋이와 해넘이 사이에 그가 상자 안에 있을 때 그를 덮치는 것이오. 그때라면 그는 아무 저항도 할 수 없고, 우리는 마땅한 대로 그를 처치할 수 있을 것이오. 우리에게는 계획을 준비할 며칠이 있소. 우리는 그가 어디로 가는지 모두 알고 있소. 우리가 그 배의 선주를 만났는데, 그가 송장과 그 밖의 모든 서류를 보여주었기 때문이오. 우리가 찾는 상자는 바르나에서 하역되어, 거기서 신임장을 제시할 대리인 리스틱스라는 자에게 인도될 것이오. 그러면 우리의 상인 친구는 자기 몫을 다한 셈이 되오. 그가 무슨 잘못된 일이라도 있느냐고, 그렇다면 전보를 쳐 바르나에서 조사하게 할 수 있다고 물었을 때, 우리는 '아니'라고 했소. 해야 할 일이 경찰이나 세관의 소관이 아니기 때문이오. 그것은 오직 우리만의 손으로, 우리 나름의 방식으로 행해야 하오.\"\n\n반 헬싱 박사가 말을 마쳤을 때, 나는 그에게 백작이 그 배에 그대로 타고 있다는 것을 확신하느냐고 물었다. 그는 답했다. \"우리에게는 그것을 입증하는 가장 좋은 증거가 있소. 오늘 아침 최면 상태에서 나온 당신 자신의 증언 말이오.\" 나는 다시 그에게, 정말로 그들이 백작을 추적해야만 하느냐고 물었다. 아아! 나는 조너선이 나를 떠나는 것이 두렵고, 다른 이들이 가면 그도 분명 가리라는 것을 알기 때문이다. 그는 점점 격해지는 감정으로, 처음에는 차분하게 답했다. 그러나 말을 이어갈수록 그는 더 노여워지고 더 힘이 실려, 마침내 우리는 그가 그토록 오래 사람들 사이에서 지도자로 군림하게 한 그 개인적 위엄의 적어도 일부를 보지 않을 수 없었다.\n\n\"그렇소, 그것은 필요하오 — 필요하오 — 필요하오! 첫째로는 당신을 위해서, 다음으로는 인류를 위해서요. 이 괴물은, 제 한정된 활동 범위 안에서, 그리고 아직 어둠 속에서 자신의 그 보잘것없는 분수를 더듬으며 아무것도 모르던 한낱 육신에 불과했던 그 짧은 시간 동안에도, 이미 많은 해악을 끼쳤소. 이 모든 것을 나는 이 다른 이들에게 이야기했소. 당신은, 나의 사랑하는 미나 부인, 내 친구 존의 축음기에서, 혹은 당신 남편의 축음기에서 그것을 알게 될 것이오. 나는 그들에게, 사람도 없이 메마른 자신의 땅을 떠나, 사람의 생명이 들판에 늘어선 곡식의 무리처럼 가득 들어찬 새 땅으로 오는 그 일이 수백 년에 걸친 작업이었다는 것을 이야기했소. 그와 같은 또 다른 불사자(不死者)가 그가 한 일을 시도하려 한다면, 어쩌면 지나간 모든 세월도, 앞으로 올 모든 세월도 그를 돕지 못할 것이오. 이자의 경우에는, 비밀스럽고 깊고 강한 온갖 자연의 힘이 어떤 경이로운 방식으로 함께 작용했음이 분명하오. 그가 이 수백 년 동안 불사의 몸으로 살아온 바로 그곳은 지질학적·화학적 세계의 기이함으로 가득하오. 그곳에는 아무도 그 끝을 모르는 깊은 동굴과 균열이 있소. 화산들이 있었는데, 그중 어떤 분화구는 아직도 기이한 성질의 물과, 죽이거나 되살리는 기체를 내뿜고 있소. 의심할 바 없이, 이런 비밀스러운 힘들의 어떤 결합 속에는 기이한 방식으로 육신의 생명에 작용하는 자성이나 전기 같은 무언가가 있을 것이오. 그리고 그 자신 안에는 처음부터 어떤 위대한 자질들이 있었소. 모질고 전쟁이 잦던 시대에 그는 어떤 사내보다도 더 강철 같은 담력과, 더 치밀한 두뇌와, 더 용맹한 심장을 지녔다 하여 이름을 떨쳤소. 그의 안에서는 어떤 생명의 원리가 기이한 방식으로 그 극한에 이르렀고, 그의 육신이 강건함을 지키며 자라고 번성한 것처럼 그의 두뇌 또한 자랐소. 이 모든 것이, 분명 그에게 따르는 그 악마적 힘 없이도 이루어진 것이오. 그 힘은 선을 상징하며 선에서 나오는 권능 앞에 굴복할 수밖에 없기 때문이오. 그리고 이제 그가 우리에게 어떤 존재인지를 말하겠소. 그는 당신을 감염시켰소 — 오, 용서하시오, 나의 부인, 내가 이런 말을 해야만 하는 것을. 그러나 이것은 당신을 위해 하는 말이오. 그는 당신을, 설령 그가 더 이상 아무것도 하지 않는다 해도, 당신이 그저 살아가기만 하면 — 당신의 옛 다정한 방식대로 살아가기만 하면 — 그리하여 머지않아, 인간 공통의 운명이며 하느님의 재가를 받은 죽음이 당신을 그와 같이 만들어버릴, 그런 식으로 감염시켰소. 이런 일이 있어서는 안 되오! 우리는 그런 일이 있어서는 안 된다고 함께 맹세했소. 그리하여 우리는 하느님 그분의 뜻을 받드는 종이오. 곧, 세상이, 그리고 그분의 아드님께서 위해 돌아가신 사람들이, 그 존재 자체가 그분을 욕되게 할 괴물들에게 내맡겨지지 않게 하라는 뜻 말이오. 그분은 우리가 이미 한 영혼을 구원하도록 허락하셨고, 우리는 더 많은 영혼을 구원하러 옛 십자가의 기사들처럼 나아가오. 그들처럼 우리는 해돋이를 향해 나아갈 것이며, 그들처럼, 우리가 쓰러진다면 우리는 대의(大義)를 위해 쓰러지는 것이오.\" 그가 말을 멈추자 나는 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 백작은 그 좌절을 현명하게 받아들이지 않을까요? 영국에서 쫓겨난 이상, 호랑이가 자기를 몰아낸 마을을 피하듯이 그도 영국을 피하지 않을까요?\"\n\n\"아하!\" 그가 말했다. \"당신의 호랑이 비유가 내게 안성맞춤이오. 나도 그것을 가져다 쓰겠소. 인도에서 식인호(食人虎)라 부르는, 한번 사람의 피를 맛본 호랑이는 더 이상 다른 먹이에는 관심을 두지 않고, 그를 손에 넣을 때까지 끊임없이 어슬렁거리며 노리오. 우리가 우리 마을에서 몰아낸 이자 또한 호랑이요, 식인호이며, 그는 결코 어슬렁거리기를 멈추지 않소. 아니, 그 자신이 물러나 멀리 머물러 있을 자가 아니오. 그의 생애에, 그가 살아 있던 삶에서 그는 터키 국경을 넘어가 적의 본거지에서 그를 공격했소. 그는 격퇴당했으나, 그가 가만히 있었소? 아니오! 그는 다시, 또다시, 또다시 왔소. 그의 끈질김과 인내를 보시오. 그에게 있던 그 어린아이 같은 두뇌로도 그는 오래전에 큰 도시로 온다는 생각을 품었소. 그가 무엇을 하오? 그는 자기에게 가장 가망이 있는 세상의 모든 곳을 알아내오. 그런 다음 그는 그 과업을 준비하려고 신중하게 자리를 잡소. 그는 인내하며 자기 힘이 얼마나 되는지, 자기 권능이 무엇인지 정확히 알아내오. 그는 새로운 언어를 익히오. 그는 새로운 사교 생활을, 옛 방식의 새로운 환경을, 정치를, 법을, 재정을, 과학을, 자기가 있은 뒤로 생겨난 새 땅과 새 사람들의 습속을 배우오. 그가 잠깐 엿본 것이 그의 식욕을 돋우고 그의 욕망을 더욱 날카롭게 했을 뿐이오. 아니, 그것은 그가 두뇌에서 자라도록 돕기까지 했소. 그것은 그에게 처음의 그의 추측이 얼마나 옳았는지를 죄다 증명해주었기 때문이오. 그는 이것을 혼자서 해냈소. 완전히 혼자서! 잊힌 땅의 폐허가 된 무덤에서부터 말이오. 더 위대한 사상의 세계가 그에게 열린다면 그가 무엇인들 못 하겠소. 우리가 아는 바, 죽음 앞에서도 미소 지을 수 있는 자, 온 민족을 죽음으로 몰아가는 질병들 한가운데에서도 번성할 수 있는 자가 말이오. 오, 만일 그런 자가 악마가 아니라 하느님에게서 왔더라면, 우리의 이 오래된 세상에서 그가 얼마나 큰 선의 힘이 되었겠소. 그러나 우리는 세상을 자유롭게 하기로 맹세했소. 우리의 수고는 침묵 속에서, 우리의 노력은 모두 은밀히 이루어져야 하오. 사람들이 자기 눈으로 본 것조차 믿지 않는 이 계몽된 시대에, 현명한 자들의 의심은 그에게 가장 큰 힘이 될 것이기 때문이오. 그것은 단번에 그의 칼집이자 갑옷이 될 것이며, 우리를, 곧 사랑하는 한 사람의 안전을 위해 — 인류의 선을 위해, 또 하느님의 영예와 영광을 위해 우리 자신의 영혼마저 위태롭게 할 각오가 된 그의 적들을 파멸시킬 그의 무기가 될 것이오.\"\n\n전반적인 논의 끝에, 오늘 밤은 어떤 것도 확정하지 않기로, 우리 모두 그 사실들을 하룻밤 곱씹어보며 올바른 결론을 생각해내도록 애쓰기로 정했다. 내일 아침식사 때 우리는 다시 모여, 서로에게 각자의 결론을 알린 뒤에 어떤 명확한 행동 방침을 결정하기로 했다.\n\n오늘 밤 나는 놀라운 평화와 안식을 느낀다. 마치 어떤 출몰하던 존재가 내게서 사라진 것만 같다. 어쩌면……\n\n내 추측은 끝맺지 못했다, 끝맺을 수 없었다. 거울 속에서 내 이마 위 붉은 자국이 눈에 들어왔기 때문이다. 그리고 나는 내가 여전히 부정(不淨)하다는 것을 알았다.\n\n수어드 박사의 일기.\n\n10월 5일. — 우리는 모두 일찍 일어났는데, 잠이 우리 각자에게 큰 도움이 된 듯하다. 이른 아침식사 때 우리가 모였을 때에는, 우리 중 누구도 다시 맛보리라 기대하지 못했던 전반적인 명랑함이 감돌았다.\n\n인간 본성에는 참으로 놀라운 회복력이 있다. 무엇이든, 어떤 식으로든 — 심지어 죽음에 의해서라도 — 가로막던 원인이 제거되기만 하면, 우리는 희망과 즐거움의 본래 자리로 되돌아 날아간다. 우리가 식탁에 둘러앉아 있는 동안 나는 한 번 이상, 지난 며칠 전체가 한바탕 꿈이 아니었을까 하는 의아함에 눈을 떴다. 다만 하커 부인의 이마 위 붉은 얼룩을 보았을 때에야 나는 현실로 돌아왔다. 지금 이렇게 이 일을 진지하게 곱씹는 와중에도, 우리의 온갖 고난의 원인이 여전히 존재한다는 것을 실감하기란 거의 불가능하다. 하커 부인조차 한참 동안은 자신의 고난을 잊은 듯하다. 다만 이따금 무언가 그것을 그녀의 마음에 되살릴 때에야, 그녀는 자신의 끔찍한 흉터를 떠올린다. 우리는 삼십 분 뒤 이곳 내 서재에서 만나 우리의 행동 방침을 정하기로 했다. 나는 단 하나의 당면한 어려움을 보고 있는데, 이성보다는 본능으로 그것을 안다. 우리는 모두 솔직하게 이야기해야 할 텐데, 어쩐지 어떤 알 수 없는 방식으로 가엾은 하커 부인의 혀가 묶여 있는 것이 아닐까 두렵다. 나는 그녀가 자기 나름의 결론을 내린다는 것을 안다. 그리고 지금까지의 모든 일로 미루어 그것이 얼마나 명민하고 얼마나 참될지 짐작할 수 있다. 그러나 그녀는 그것을 입 밖에 내지 않으려 하거나, 낼 수 없는 것이다. 나는 이것을 반 헬싱에게 이야기했고, 그와 나는 단둘이 있을 때 그것을 의논하기로 했다. 짐작건대 그것은 그녀의 혈관에 들어간 그 끔찍한 독의 일부가 작용하기 시작한 것이리라. 백작은 그녀에게, 반 헬싱이 \"흡혈귀의 피의 세례\"라 부른 것을 베풀 때 자기 나름의 목적이 있었던 것이다. 글쎄, 좋은 것에서 스스로 증류되어 나오는 독도 있을지 모른다. 시체독의 존재조차 수수께끼인 시대에 우리는 무엇에도 놀라서는 안 된다! 한 가지 나는 안다. 만일 가엾은 하커 부인의 침묵에 관한 내 본능이 옳다면, 우리 앞에 놓인 일에는 끔찍한 어려움이 — 알 수 없는 위험이 있다는 것을. 그녀의 침묵을 강요하는 바로 그 힘이 그녀의 말을 강요할 수도 있다. 나는 더 이상 생각할 엄두가 나지 않는다. 그렇게 했다가는 내 생각 속에서 한 고귀한 여인을 욕되게 하고 말 테니!\n\n반 헬싱이 다른 이들보다 조금 일찍 내 서재로 올 것이다. 나는 그에게 이 문제를 꺼내보려 한다.\n\n나중에. — 교수가 들어왔을 때, 우리는 사태를 의논했다. 그가 무언가 하고 싶은 말을 마음에 두고 있으나, 그 화제를 꺼내기를 다소 망설이고 있음을 나는 알 수 있었다. 한동안 변죽을 울리던 그가 불쑥 말했다.\n\n\"존, 자네와 내가 단둘이서 이야기해야 할 것이 있네, 적어도 처음에는 말일세. 나중에는 다른 이들에게도 털어놓아야 할지 모르네.\" 그러더니 그가 말을 멈추었으므로 나는 기다렸다. 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"미나 부인, 우리의 가엾고 사랑스러운 미나 부인이 변해가고 있네.\" 내 최악의 두려움이 이렇게 확인되자 차가운 전율이 온몸을 훑고 지나갔다. 반 헬싱이 말을 이었다.\n\n\"루시 양의 슬픈 경험이 있으니, 이번에는 일이 너무 멀리 가기 전에 경계해야 하네. 우리의 과업은 이제 실로 그 어느 때보다 더 어려워졌고, 이 새로운 곤란은 매 시간을 더없이 중대하게 만드네. 나는 그녀의 얼굴에 흡혈귀의 특징이 들어서는 것을 볼 수 있네. 지금은 다만 아주, 아주 미미하지만, 미리 단정하지 않고 알아챌 눈만 있다면 보이는 것일세. 그녀의 이가 다소 날카로워졌고, 때때로 눈이 더 단단해지네. 그러나 이것이 전부가 아닐세. 이제 그녀에게는 자주 침묵이 찾아드네. 루시 양에게 그러했던 것처럼 말일세. 그녀는, 나중에 알려지기를 바라며 글을 쓸 때조차 입을 떼지 않았네. 그런데 내 두려움은 이것일세. 만일 그녀가 우리의 최면 상태를 통해 백작이 보고 듣는 것을 말할 수 있다면, 그녀에게 처음 최면을 걸었고, 그녀의 바로 그 피를 마셨으며 그녀로 하여금 자기 피를 마시게 한 그자가, 만일 그가 원한다면, 그녀의 마음을 강요하여 그녀가 아는 것을 자기에게 드러내게 할 수 있다는 것이 더더욱 참되지 않겠는가?\" 나는 동의하여 고개를 끄덕였다. 그가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"그렇다면 우리가 해야 할 일은 이것을 막는 것일세. 우리는 그녀가 우리의 의도를 모르게 해야 하며, 그리하면 그녀는 자기가 모르는 것을 말할 수 없네. 이것은 괴로운 과업일세! 오, 어찌나 괴로운지 생각만 해도 내 가슴이 미어지네. 그러나 그래야만 하네. 오늘 우리가 모일 때, 나는 그녀에게, 우리가 말하지 않으려는 어떤 이유로 그녀는 더 이상 우리의 회의에 들지 못하고, 다만 우리의 보호를 받아야 한다고 말해야 하네.\" 그는, 이미 그토록 고통받는 가엾은 영혼에게 어쩌면 자기가 안겨주어야 할지도 모를 고통을 생각하며 이마에 흥건히 솟은 땀을 닦았다. 나 또한 같은 결론에 이르렀다고 말해준다면 그에게 어떤 위안이 되리라는 것을 나는 알았다. 적어도 그것은 의심의 고통만은 덜어줄 테니까. 나는 그에게 그렇게 말해주었고, 그 효과는 내가 예상한 그대로였다.\n\n이제 우리의 전체 모임 시각이 가까워졌다. 반 헬싱은 모임을, 그리고 그 괴로운 자기 역할을 준비하러 자리를 떴다. 나는 그가 정말로 혼자서 기도하려는 것이리라 믿는다.\n\n나중에. — 우리 모임이 시작되자마자 반 헬싱과 나는 둘 다 개인적으로 큰 안도를 느꼈다. 하커 부인이 남편 편에 전갈을 보내, 지금은 우리와 함께하지 않겠다고, 우리가 그녀의 존재로 거북해하지 않고 자유로이 우리의 행동을 논의하는 편이 낫겠다고 했다는 것이다. 교수와 나는 한순간 서로를 바라보았는데, 어쩐지 둘 다 안도한 듯했다. 나로서는, 만일 하커 부인 스스로 그 위험을 깨달은 것이라면, 그것은 많은 위험만큼이나 많은 고통도 피한 셈이라고 생각했다. 그러한 사정에서 우리는, 묻는 눈빛과 그에 답하는 눈빛으로, 입술에 손가락을 댄 채, 우리 둘이 다시 단둘이 의논할 수 있게 될 때까지 우리의 의혹을 침묵 속에 묻어두기로 합의했다. 우리는 곧장 우리의 작전 계획에 들어갔다. 반 헬싱이 먼저 사실들을 우리 앞에 대강 풀어놓았다.\n\n\"차리나 캐서린호는 어제 아침 템스 강을 떠났소. 그 배가 일찍이 낸 적 있는 가장 빠른 속도로 가더라도 바르나에 닿기까지 적어도 삼 주는 걸릴 것이오. 그러나 우리는 육로로 같은 곳까지 사흘이면 갈 수 있소. 자, 우리가 아는 바, 백작이 부릴 수 있는 그런 날씨의 영향을 감안하여 배의 항해를 이틀 줄여 잡고, 우리에게 일어날지 모를 어떤 지체를 위해 꼬박 하루 낮밤을 잡는다면, 우리에게는 거의 이 주의 여유가 있는 셈이오. 그러니 완전히 안전하려면 우리는 늦어도 17일에는 이곳을 떠나야 하오. 그러면 우리는 어쨌든 배가 도착하기 하루 전에 바르나에 가 있게 되어, 필요할지 모를 준비를 갖출 수 있소. 물론 우리는 모두 무장하고 갈 것이오 — 육체적인 것뿐 아니라 영적인 것까지, 악한 것들에 맞서 무장하고서.\" 여기서 퀸시 모리스가 덧붙였다.\n\n\"제가 알기로 백작은 늑대의 나라에서 온 자이니, 어쩌면 그가 우리보다 먼저 거기 닿을지도 모릅니다. 저는 우리 무장에 윈체스터 소총을 보태자고 제안합니다. 그런 종류의 곤경이 주위에 있을 때 저는 윈체스터에 일종의 믿음을 갖고 있거든요. 아트, 우리가 토볼스크에서 늑대 떼에게 쫓기던 일을 기억하나? 그때 우리가 한 자루씩 연발총만 있었더라면 무엇인들 못 내놓았겠나!\"\n\n\"좋소!\" 반 헬싱이 말했다. \"윈체스터로 합시다. 퀸시의 머리는 언제나 빈틈없지만, 사냥할 일이 있을 때 가장 그렇소. 비유라는 게 늑대가 사람에게 위험한 것보다 학문에 더 욕된 것이라면 말이오. 그동안 우리가 여기서 할 수 있는 일은 없소. 그리고 내 생각에 바르나는 우리 중 누구에게도 낯선 곳이니, 더 일찍 거기로 가는 게 어떻겠소? 여기서 기다리는 것이나 거기서 기다리는 것이나 마찬가지로 긴 시간이오. 오늘 밤과 내일 우리는 채비를 갖출 수 있고, 그런 다음 모든 것이 순조롭다면 우리 넷이 여정에 오를 수 있소.\"\n\n\"우리 넷이라뇨?\" 하커가 우리를 한 사람씩 둘러보며 묻듯이 말했다.\n\n\"물론이오!\" 교수가 재빨리 답했다. \"당신은 남아서 당신의 그토록 사랑스러운 아내를 돌봐야 하오!\" 하커는 한동안 잠자코 있다가 공허한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"그 부분에 관해서는 아침에 이야기합시다. 미나와 상의하고 싶소.\" 나는 지금이야말로 반 헬싱이 그에게 우리의 계획을 그녀에게 발설하지 말라고 경고할 때라고 생각했다. 그러나 그는 아무 내색도 하지 않았다. 나는 의미심장하게 그를 바라보며 헛기침을 했다. 그러자 그는 답으로 손가락을 입술에 대고는 몸을 돌렸다.\n\n조너선 하커의 일기.\n\n10월 5일, 오후. — 오늘 아침 우리 모임이 끝난 뒤 한동안 나는 아무 생각도 할 수 없었다. 사태의 새로운 국면들이 내 마음을 활발한 사고가 들어설 자리가 없는 의아함의 상태에 빠뜨렸다. 미나가 그 논의에 일절 끼지 않겠다고 한 결심이 나를 생각에 잠기게 했다. 그러나 나는 그 일을 그녀와 따져 의논할 수 없었으므로, 다만 짐작할 수밖에 없었다. 나는 지금도 여전히 답에서 멀리 있다. 다른 이들이 그것을 받아들인 태도 또한 나를 어리둥절하게 했다. 지난번 우리가 그 문제를 이야기했을 때 우리는 우리 사이에 더 이상 어떤 것도 숨기지 않기로 합의했었다. 미나는 지금 어린아이처럼 차분하고 사랑스럽게 자고 있다. 그녀의 입술은 곡선을 그리고 얼굴은 행복으로 빛난다. 하느님 감사합니다, 그녀에게 아직 이런 순간들이 있다니.\n\n나중에. — 이 모든 것이 어찌나 기이한지. 나는 미나의 행복한 잠을 지켜보며 앉아 있었고, 내가 앞으로 누릴 수 있으리라 짐작되는 만큼 행복에 가까이 다가갔다. 저녁이 깊어가고, 더 낮게 가라앉는 해로부터 대지가 제 그림자를 받아들이면서, 방 안의 고요가 내게 점점 더 엄숙해졌다. 별안간 미나가 눈을 뜨더니, 다정하게 나를 바라보며 말했다.\n\n\"조너선, 당신의 명예를 걸고 내게 무언가 약속해주었으면 해요. 나에게 한 약속이되, 하느님께서 들으시는 가운데 성스럽게 한 약속, 내가 무릎을 꿇고 쓰라린 눈물로 당신에게 애원한다 해도 깨뜨려서는 안 될 약속이에요. 어서요, 지금 당장 내게 약속해주어야 해요.\"\n\n\"미나,\" 내가 말했다. \"그런 약속을, 나는 당장 할 수는 없소. 내게 그것을 할 권리가 없을지도 모르오.\"\n\n\"하지만, 여보,\" 그녀가, 그 눈이 북극성 같아 보일 만큼 영적인 간절함으로 말했다. \"이것을 바라는 건 나예요. 그리고 나 자신을 위한 것이 아니에요. 내가 옳지 않은지 반 헬싱 박사님께 물어보아도 돼요. 그분이 동의하지 않으시면 당신 좋을 대로 하면 돼요. 아니, 그뿐 아니라, 만일 나중에 여러분 모두가 동의하면, 당신은 그 약속에서 풀려나는 거예요.\"\n\n\"약속하겠소!\" 내가 말했고, 한순간 그녀는 더없이 행복해 보였다. 비록 내게는 그녀를 위한 모든 행복이 그녀의 이마 위 붉은 흉터로 막혀 있었지만. 그녀가 말했다.\n\n\"백작에 맞선 작전을 위해 세운 계획에 관해서는 내게 아무것도 말하지 않겠다고 약속해주세요. 말로도, 넌지시도, 암시로도 안 돼요. 이것이 내게 남아 있는 한, 어느 때라도요!\" 그러고는 엄숙하게 그 흉터를 가리켰다. 나는 그녀가 진심임을 알았고, 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"약속하겠소!\" 그리고 그렇게 말하는 순간 나는 그 순간부터 우리 사이에 문 하나가 닫혀버렸음을 느꼈다.\n\n나중에, 자정. — 미나는 저녁 내내 밝고 명랑했다. 어찌나 그러했던지 나머지 사람들도 모두, 다소 그녀의 쾌활함에 물들기라도 한 듯 용기를 얻는 것 같았다. 그 결과 나 자신조차 우리를 짓누르던 우울의 장막이 다소 걷힌 듯 느껴졌다. 우리는 모두 일찍 잠자리에 들었다. 미나는 지금 어린아이처럼 자고 있다. 그토록 끔찍한 고난 한가운데에서도 잠드는 능력이 그녀에게 남아 있다는 것은 놀라운 일이다. 그것을 하느님께 감사한다. 그때만이라도 그녀는 자기 근심을 잊을 수 있으니까. 어쩌면 그녀의 본보기가, 오늘 밤 그녀의 쾌활함이 그러했던 것처럼 내게 영향을 미칠지도 모른다. 나도 시도해보겠다. 오! 꿈 없는 잠이여.\n\n10월 6일, 아침. — 또 한 번의 놀라움. 미나가 어제와 거의 같은 시각에 나를 일찍 깨우더니, 반 헬싱 박사를 데려와달라고 청했다. 나는 또 한 번 최면을 걸 때구나 싶어, 두말없이 교수를 데리러 갔다. 그는 분명 그런 부름을 예상하고 있었던 듯, 자기 방에서 옷을 차려입은 그를 보았기 때문이다. 그의 방문은 빠끔히 열려 있어서, 그가 우리 방문 열리는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 그는 곧장 왔다. 방으로 들어서면서 그는 미나에게 다른 이들도 와도 되겠느냐고 물었다.\n\n\"아니요,\" 그녀가 아주 담담하게 말했다. \"그럴 필요는 없을 거예요. 박사님께서 그분들께 그대로 전해주시면 돼요. 저는 여러분의 여정에 함께 가야 해요.\"\n\n반 헬싱 박사도 나만큼이나 깜짝 놀랐다. 한순간 멈칫하더니 그가 물었다.\n\n\"하지만 왜요?\"\n\n\"저를 데려가셔야 해요. 저는 여러분과 함께 있는 편이 더 안전하고, 여러분 또한 더 안전해질 거예요.\"\n\n\"하지만 왜요, 사랑하는 미나 부인? 당신도 알다시피 당신의 안전은 우리의 가장 엄숙한 의무요. 우리는 위험 속으로 들어가는데, 당신은 그 위험에, 어떤 사정 때문에 — 지나간 일들 때문에 — 우리 중 누구보다도 더 노출되어 있거나, 그럴 수 있소.\" 그가 당혹스러워하며 말을 멈추었다.\n\n그녀는 답하면서 손가락을 들어 자기 이마를 가리켰다.\n\n\"알아요. 그것이 바로 제가 가야 하는 까닭이에요. 지금은, 해가 떠오르는 동안은 말씀드릴 수 있지만, 다시는 못 할지도 몰라요. 저는 백작이 저를 부르면 가야만 한다는 걸 알아요. 저는 그가 몰래 오라고 명하면, 제가 술책으로, 무슨 수를 써서든 — 조너선까지도 — 속여서 가야만 한다는 걸 알아요.\" 그녀가 그렇게 말하며 내게 보낸 그 눈빛을 하느님께서는 보셨으니, 만일 정말로 기록하는 천사가 있다면 그 눈빛은 그녀의 영원한 영예로 적혀 있을 것이다. 나는 다만 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥘 수 있을 뿐이었다. 나는 말할 수 없었다. 내 감정은 눈물의 위안조차 받기에 너무 벅찼다. 그녀가 말을 이었다.\n\n\"여러분 남자들은 용감하고 강해요. 여러분은 수가 많아서 강하지요. 혼자 지켜야 하는 사람의 인간적 인내를 무너뜨릴 것에도 여러분은 함께 맞설 수 있으니까요. 게다가 저는 도움이 될지도 몰라요. 여러분이 제게 최면을 걸어, 저 자신조차 모르는 것을 알아낼 수 있으니까요.\" 반 헬싱 박사가 매우 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"미나 부인, 당신은 언제나처럼 더없이 현명하오. 당신은 우리와 함께 가시오. 그리고 우리가 이루러 나아가는 그 일을 우리 함께 해냅시다.\" 그가 말을 마쳤을 때, 미나의 긴 침묵이 나로 하여금 그녀를 바라보게 했다. 그녀는 베개에 도로 기댄 채 잠들어 있었다. 내가 차양을 걷어 올려 햇빛이 방에 쏟아져 들어오게 했을 때조차 그녀는 깨지 않았다. 반 헬싱이 내게 조용히 함께 가자고 손짓했다. 우리는 그의 방으로 갔고, 일 분도 안 되어 고달밍 경과 수어드 박사, 모리스 씨도 우리와 함께했다. 그는 그들에게 미나가 한 말을 전한 뒤 말을 이었다.\n\n\"아침에 우리는 바르나로 떠나오. 우리는 이제 새로운 변수를 다루어야 하오. 미나 부인 말이오. 오, 그러나 그녀의 영혼은 참되오. 그녀가 우리에게 그만큼이나 이야기하는 것은 그녀에게 고통이오. 그러나 그것은 더없이 옳은 일이며, 우리는 때맞추어 경계하게 된 것이오. 어떤 기회도 놓쳐서는 안 되며, 바르나에서 우리는 그 배가 도착하는 순간 행동할 채비가 되어 있어야 하오.\"\n\n\"정확히 무엇을 해야 합니까?\" 모리스 씨가 간결하게 물었다. 교수가 답하기 전에 잠시 멈추었다.\n\n\"우리는 먼저 그 배에 오를 것이오. 그런 다음 그 상자를 확인하면, 거기에 들장미 가지를 놓을 것이오. 우리는 이것을 단단히 매어둘 것인데, 그것이 거기 있으면 아무도 빠져나올 수 없기 때문이오. 적어도 미신은 그렇게 말하오. 그리고 우리는 처음에는 미신에 기대야 하오. 그것은 옛 시절 인간의 믿음이었고, 그 뿌리는 여전히 믿음에 있소. 그런 다음, 우리가 찾는 기회가 와서 아무도 가까이서 볼 사람이 없을 때, 우리는 그 상자를 열 것이오. 그러면 — 그러면 모든 것이 잘될 것이오.\"\n\n\"저는 어떤 기회도 기다리지 않겠습니다,\" 모리스가 말했다. \"그 상자를 보면 저는 그것을 열어 그 괴물을 없애버리겠습니다. 설령 천 명이 지켜보고 있다 해도, 그리고 그 때문에 제가 다음 순간 사라져 없어진다 해도 말입니다!\" 나는 본능적으로 그의 손을 잡았는데, 그것은 강철 조각처럼 단단했다. 그는 내 눈빛을 이해한 것 같다. 그랬기를 바란다.\n\n\"착한 친구,\" 반 헬싱 박사가 말했다. \"용감한 친구일세. 퀸시는 영락없는 사내일세. 그 점을 하느님께서 그에게 복 주시기를. 얘들아, 내 말을 믿게, 우리 중 누구도 뒤처지거나 무슨 두려움으로 멈춰 서지 않을 걸세. 나는 다만 우리가 할 수 있는 일을 — 우리가 해야 할 일을 말하는 것뿐일세. 그러나 실로, 실로 우리는 우리가 무엇을 하게 될지 말할 수 없네. 일어날 수 있는 일이 너무 많고, 그 방식과 그 끝이 너무도 가지가지여서, 그 순간이 오기까지는 우리도 말할 수 없네. 우리는 모두, 온갖 방식으로 무장할 것이며, 끝낼 때가 오면 우리의 노력에 모자람이 없을 걸세. 자, 오늘은 우리의 모든 일을 정돈해두세. 우리에게 소중하고 우리에게 의지하는 다른 이들에게 관계되는 모든 일을 마무리해두세. 우리 중 누구도 그 끝이 무엇일지, 언제일지, 어떻게일지 말할 수 없으니 말일세. 나로 말하자면, 내 일은 정리되어 있고, 달리 할 일이 없으니, 나는 여행 준비를 하러 가겠네. 우리 여정에 필요한 표며 그 밖의 모든 것을 마련해두겠네.\"\n\n더 이상 할 말이 없어 우리는 헤어졌다. 나는 이제 내 세상사를 모두 정리하고, 무슨 일이 닥치든 채비를 갖추어두겠다……\n\n나중에. — 모두 끝냈다. 내 유언장이 작성되었고, 모든 것이 완비되었다. 미나가, 만일 그녀가 살아남는다면, 내 유일한 상속자다. 만일 그렇게 되지 못한다면, 우리에게 그토록 잘해준 다른 이들이 잔여 재산을 받게 될 것이다.\n\n이제 해넘이가 다가오고 있다. 미나의 불안이 내 주의를 그쪽으로 돌리게 한다. 정확한 해넘이의 시각이 드러낼 무언가가 그녀의 마음에 있음을 나는 확신한다. 이런 때들이 우리 모두에게 괴로운 시간이 되어가고 있다. 매번의 해돋이와 해넘이가 어떤 새로운 위험을 — 어떤 새로운 고통을 열어 보이기 때문이다. 그러나 그것은 하느님의 뜻 안에서 좋은 끝에 이르는 수단이 될지도 모른다. 나는 이 모든 것을 일기에 적는다. 내 사랑이 지금은 그것을 들어서는 안 되기 때문이다. 그러나 만일 그녀가 언젠가 그것을 다시 볼 수 있게 된다면, 그것들은 준비되어 있을 것이다.\n\n그녀가 나를 부르고 있다.\n\n# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n10월 11일, 저녁. — 조너선 하커가 나더러 이것을 기록해 달라고 부탁했다. 자기는 도저히 그 일을 감당할 수 없을 것 같다며, 정확한 기록을 남기고 싶다는 것이었다.\n\n해 질 무렵을 조금 앞두고 하커 부인을 만나러 오라는 청을 받았을 때, 우리 가운데 누구도 놀라지 않았으리라 생각한다. 우리는 최근 들어 일출과 일몰이 그녀에게 각별한 자유의 시간임을 이해하게 되었다. 그때에는 어떤 지배하는 힘도 그녀를 억누르거나 옭아매거나 행동으로 내몰지 못한 채, 그녀의 본래 자아가 드러날 수 있었다. 이러한 기분, 혹은 상태는 실제 일출이나 일몰보다 반 시간쯤, 혹은 그 이상 앞서 시작되어, 해가 높이 떠오르거나 구름이 지평선 위로 쏟아지는 햇살에 여전히 붉게 물들어 있는 동안까지 이어졌다. 처음에는 일종의 소극적인 상태가 나타나는데, 마치 어떤 매듭이 풀어진 듯한 것이었고, 곧이어 완전한 자유가 빠르게 뒤따랐다. 그러나 그 자유가 끝나면 본래의 상태로 되돌아가거나 다시 빠져드는 변화가 빠르게 찾아왔는데, 그에 앞서는 것은 오직 경고하는 듯한 침묵의 한순간뿐이었다.\n\n오늘 밤 우리가 만났을 때, 그녀는 다소 긴장한 듯했고, 내면의 갈등을 드러내는 온갖 기색을 띠고 있었다. 나로서는 그것을 그녀가 할 수 있는 가장 이른 순간에 격렬한 노력을 기울이고 있는 탓이라 여겼다. 그러나 채 몇 분도 지나지 않아 그녀는 자신을 완전히 다스리게 되었다. 그러고는 반쯤 기대어 있던 소파에 남편을 자기 곁에 앉도록 손짓하고, 우리 나머지에게는 의자를 가까이 끌어오게 했다. 남편의 손을 자기 손에 잡고 그녀가 입을 열었다.\n\n\"우리는 지금 모두 여기 함께, 자유롭게 모여 있어요. 어쩌면 이것이 마지막일지도 몰라요! 알아요, 여보. 당신이 끝까지 늘 저와 함께하리란 걸 알아요.\" 이 말은 남편에게 한 것이었는데, 우리가 보기에도 그의 손이 그녀의 손을 꼭 움켜쥐고 있었다. \"아침이면 우리는 우리의 임무를 향해 나서고, 우리 가운데 누구에게 무슨 일이 닥칠지는 오직 하느님만이 아실 거예요. 당신은 저를 데려가실 만큼 다정하시겠죠. 가엾고 연약한, 어쩌면 그 영혼이 이미 길을 잃은 — 아니, 아니에요, 아직은 아니지만, 어쨌든 위태로운 — 여인을 위해 용감하고 진실한 사내들이 할 수 있는 모든 일을 당신들이 해주시리란 걸 알아요. 하지만 제가 당신들과 같지 않다는 걸 기억하셔야 해요. 제 피 속에, 제 영혼 속에 저를 파멸시킬지도 모를 독이 있어요. 우리에게 어떤 구원이 오지 않는 한, 그것은 반드시 저를 파멸시키고 말 거예요. 오, 벗들이여, 제 영혼이 위태롭다는 걸 여러분도 저만큼이나 잘 아실 거예요. 그리고 제게 빠져나갈 길이 하나 있다는 걸 알지만, 여러분도 저도 그 길을 택해서는 안 돼요!\" 그녀는 차례로 우리 모두를 향해 애원하듯 바라보았는데, 남편에서 시작해 남편에서 끝맺었다.\n\n\"그 길이 무엇이오?\" 판 헬싱이 쉰 목소리로 물었다. \"우리가 택해서는 안 될 — 택할 수 없는 — 그 길이 무엇이오?\"\n\n\"더 큰 악이 완전히 이루어지기 전에, 제 손으로든 다른 누구의 손으로든, 제가 지금 죽는 것이에요. 일단 제가 죽기만 하면, 가엾은 루시에게 그러하셨듯이, 여러분이 제 불멸의 영혼을 풀어주실 수 있고 또 풀어주시리란 걸 저도 알고 여러분도 알아요. 만일 죽음이, 혹은 죽음에 대한 두려움이 앞을 가로막는 유일한 것이라면, 저는 저를 사랑하는 벗들 가운데서 지금 여기서 죽는 일을 마다하지 않을 거예요. 하지만 죽음이 전부는 아니에요. 우리 앞에 희망이 있고 쓰라린 임무가 남아 있는 이런 처지에서 죽는 것이 하느님의 뜻이라고는 믿을 수 없어요. 그러니 저로서는, 여기서 영원한 안식의 확실함을 포기하고, 이 세상이나 저 지하 세계가 품은 가장 시커먼 것들이 있을지도 모를 어둠 속으로 나아가겠어요!\" 우리는 모두 침묵했으니, 이것이 단지 서막일 뿐임을 본능적으로 알았기 때문이다. 다른 이들의 얼굴은 굳어 있었고, 하커의 얼굴은 잿빛으로 변했다. 어쩌면 그는 우리 가운데 누구보다도 무엇이 닥쳐올지를 잘 짐작했는지도 모른다. 그녀는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이것이 제가 공동의 몫으로 내놓을 수 있는 것이에요.\" 나는 그녀가 그런 자리에서, 그토록 진지하게 쓰는 그 기묘한 법률 용어를 알아채지 않을 수 없었다. \"여러분은 저마다 무엇을 내놓으시겠어요? 여러분의 목숨이겠지요, 저도 알아요.\" 그녀는 빠르게 말을 이었다. \"그건 용감한 사내들에게는 쉬운 일이지요. 여러분의 목숨은 하느님의 것이고, 여러분은 그것을 그분께 돌려드릴 수 있어요. 하지만 제게는 무엇을 내놓으시겠어요?\" 그녀는 다시 묻는 듯한 눈빛으로 바라보았으나, 이번에는 남편의 얼굴을 피했다. 퀸시는 알아들은 듯했다. 그가 고개를 끄덕이자 그녀의 얼굴이 환해졌다. \"그러면 제가 원하는 바를 분명히 말씀드리겠어요. 이제 우리 사이의 이 일에 조금의 의심도 남아서는 안 되니까요. 여러분 모두 — 당신마저도, 사랑하는 남편이여 — 약속해 주세요. 그때가 오거든, 저를 죽여주시겠다고요.\"\n\n\"그때가 언제요?\" 그것은 퀸시의 목소리였으나, 낮고 팽팽하게 긴장되어 있었다.\n\n\"제가 너무도 변해버려서 사는 것보다 죽는 편이 낫다고 여러분이 확신하게 될 때요. 제가 그렇게 육신으로 죽거든, 여러분은 한순간도 지체하지 말고 제 몸에 말뚝을 박고 제 목을 잘라주세요. 아니면 제게 안식을 주기 위해 필요한 무엇이든 해주세요!\"\n\n침묵이 흐른 뒤 가장 먼저 일어선 것은 퀸시였다. 그는 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그녀의 손을 자기 손에 잡고는 엄숙하게 말했다.\n\n\"저는 그저 거친 사내일 뿐이고, 어쩌면 그런 영예를 얻을 만큼 사내답게 살지 못했을지도 모릅니다. 하지만 제가 신성하고 소중히 여기는 모든 것을 걸고 부인께 맹세합니다. 그때가 오거든, 부인께서 우리에게 지우신 그 의무 앞에서 결코 물러서지 않겠습니다. 그리고 또 약속드리거니와, 저는 모든 것을 확실히 하겠습니다. 만일 조금이라도 의심스럽다면, 저는 그때가 왔다고 여기겠습니다!\"\n\n\"진실한 벗이여!\" 그녀가 빠르게 흘러내리는 눈물 속에서 할 수 있는 말이라곤 그것뿐이었고, 그녀는 몸을 굽혀 그의 손에 입을 맞추었다.\n\n\"나도 똑같이 맹세하오, 친애하는 미나 부인!\" 판 헬싱이 말했다.\n\n\"저도요!\" 고달밍 경이 말했고, 그들은 저마다 차례로 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 맹세를 했다. 나도 뒤따랐다. 그러자 그녀의 남편이 그녀를 향해 돌아섰는데, 그 눈은 핏기가 없었고, 머리칼의 눈처럼 흰빛마저 가라앉힐 만큼 푸르스름한 창백함을 띤 채 물었다.\n\n\"나도, 그런 약속을 해야 하오, 오, 내 아내여?\"\n\n\"당신도요, 사랑하는 이여.\" 그녀가 목소리와 눈빛에 한없는 연민의 그리움을 담아 말했다. \"물러서서는 안 돼요. 당신은 제게 가장 가깝고 가장 소중하며 온 세상이에요. 우리 영혼은 온 생애와 온 세월에 걸쳐 하나로 맺어져 있어요. 생각해 보세요, 여보. 용감한 사내들이 아내와 제 집안 여인들을 적의 손에 넘어가지 않게 하려고 죽인 때가 있었어요. 사랑하는 이들이 자기를 죽여달라고 애원했다고 해서 그들의 손이 더 떨린 것도 아니었어요. 그런 혹독한 시련의 때에, 그것은 사랑하는 이들을 향한 사내의 의무예요! 그리고 오, 여보, 만일 제가 어느 손에든 죽음을 맞아야 한다면, 저를 가장 사랑하는 이의 손에 맞게 해주세요. 판 헬싱 박사님, 가엾은 루시의 일에서, 그녀를 사랑하던 — \" 그녀는 얼핏 얼굴을 붉히며 말을 멈추고는 표현을 바꾸었다. \"— 그녀에게 평안을 줄 가장 마땅한 권리를 지녔던 이에게 베푸신 자비를 저는 잊지 않았어요. 만일 그때가 다시 온다면, 저를 짓누르는 이 끔찍한 굴레에서 저를 풀어준 것이 남편의 사랑 어린 손이었다는 사실을, 제 남편의 생애에 행복한 기억으로 만들어 주시길 박사님께 바라요.\"\n\n\"다시금 맹세하오!\" 교수의 울리는 목소리가 들려왔다. 하커 부인은 미소를, 정말로 미소를 지으며, 안도의 한숨과 함께 등을 기대고 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 이제 한마디 경고를 드릴게요. 절대로 잊으셔서는 안 될 경고예요. 그때가, 만일 그것이 정말 온다면, 빠르고 뜻밖에 닥칠지도 몰라요. 그런 경우에는 한순간도 지체하지 말고 기회를 잡으셔야 해요. 그런 때에 저 자신은 — 아니! 그때가 정말 온다면, 저는 분명히 — 여러분에게 맞서 여러분의 적과 한편이 되어 있을 거예요.\"\n\n\"한 가지 더 부탁이 있어요.\" 그녀는 이 말을 하면서 무척 엄숙해졌다. \"이건 다른 것처럼 절실하고 꼭 필요한 일은 아니지만, 만일 들어주신다면 한 가지를 해주셨으면 해요.\" 우리는 모두 동의했으나 아무도 말하지 않았다. 말할 필요가 없었던 것이다.\n\n\"장례 기도문을 읽어주셨으면 해요.\" 그녀는 남편의 깊은 신음에 말이 끊겼다. 그의 손을 자기 손에 잡아 가슴 위에 얹고는 말을 이었다. \"언젠가 제 위에서 그것을 읽어주셔야 해요. 이 무서운 사태가 어떻게 끝나든, 그것은 우리 모두에게, 혹은 그 가운데 누군가에게 다정한 위안이 될 거예요. 당신이, 사랑하는 이여, 그것을 읽어주시길 바라요. 그러면 무슨 일이 닥치든 그것은 제 기억 속에 영원히 당신의 목소리로 남을 테니까요!\"\n\n\"하지만 오, 내 사랑.\" 그가 애원했다. \"죽음은 당신에게서 멀리 떨어져 있소.\"\n\n\"아니에요.\" 그녀가 경고하듯 손을 들어 올리며 말했다. \"저는 지금 이 순간, 흙으로 된 무덤의 무게가 무겁게 저를 짓누르는 것보다 더 깊이 죽음 속에 잠겨 있어요!\"\n\n\"오, 내 아내여, 정말 읽어야 하오?\" 그가 시작하기에 앞서 말했다.\n\n\"그것이 제게 위안이 될 거예요, 여보!\" 그녀가 한 말은 그것뿐이었다. 그녀가 책을 준비해 주자 그는 읽기 시작했다.\n\n\"내가 어떻게 — 그 누가 어떻게 — 그 기이한 광경을, 그 엄숙함과 음울함과 슬픔과 공포를, 그러면서도 그 감미로움을 이야기할 수 있을까. 거룩하거나 가슴 저미는 그 무엇에서도 쓰라린 진실의 우스꽝스러운 흉내밖에 보지 못하는 회의론자라 할지라도, 병들고 비탄에 잠긴 그 부인을 둘러싸고 무릎 꿇은 사랑과 헌신으로 뭉친 작은 무리를 보았다면, 혹은 감정에 너무도 메어 자주 멈춰야 할 만큼 끊기는 목소리로 죽은 자의 장례에서 그 소박하고 아름다운 기도문을 읽어 내려가던 남편의 그 애틋한 정열의 목소리를 들었다면, 그 마음이 녹아내리고 말았으리라. 나는 — 나는 더 이상 쓸 수가 없다 — 말이 — 그리고 — 모, 목소리가 — 나, 나를 저버린다!\"\n\n그녀의 직감은 옳았다. 그 모든 것이 기이했고, 그 강력한 영향을 당시에 느꼈던 우리에게마저 훗날 아무리 괴이하게 여겨질지라도, 그것은 우리에게 큰 위안이 되었다. 그리고 하커 부인이 영혼의 자유로부터 다시 빠져드는 변화가 다가옴을 보여주는 그 침묵도, 우리 가운데 누구에게도 우리가 두려워했던 만큼 절망으로 가득해 보이지는 않았다.\n\n「조너선 하커의 일기」\n\n10월 15일, 바르나. — 우리는 12일 아침에 채링 크로스를 떠나 같은 날 밤 파리에 닿았고, 우리를 위해 잡아둔 오리엔트 특급의 자리에 올랐다. 우리는 밤낮으로 여행한 끝에 다섯 시쯤 이곳에 도착했다. 고달밍 경은 그에게 온 전보가 있는지 알아보러 영사관에 갔고, 나머지 우리는 이 호텔 — \"오데수스\" — 로 곧장 왔다. 여정에 무슨 일들이 있었는지도 모르겠으나, 나는 앞으로 나아가고픈 마음이 너무 간절해 그런 것에 신경 쓸 겨를이 없었다. 차리나 카테리나호가 항구에 들어오기 전까지는 이 넓은 세상의 어떤 것도 내게는 흥미를 끌지 못할 것이다. 하느님, 감사합니다! 미나는 건강하고, 점점 기력을 되찾는 듯 보인다. 혈색이 돌아오고 있다. 그녀는 잠을 많이 잔다. 여행 내내 그녀는 거의 줄곧 잠들어 있었다. 그러나 일출과 일몰 직전에는 무척 깨어 있고 또렷이 깨어 있는데, 그런 때에 판 헬싱이 그녀에게 최면을 거는 것이 어느덧 습관이 되었다. 처음에는 어느 정도 노력이 필요해서 그가 여러 번 손짓을 해야 했으나, 이제 그녀는 마치 익숙해진 듯 곧바로 응하며, 거의 아무런 동작도 필요하지 않다. 그는 이런 특정한 순간들에 단지 의지를 발하기만 해도 되는 힘을 지닌 듯하고, 그녀의 생각은 그에게 복종한다. 그는 늘 그녀에게 무엇을 보고 들을 수 있느냐고 묻는다. 첫 번째 물음에 그녀는 이렇게 답한다.\n\n\"아무것도요. 온통 어둡습니다.\" 그리고 두 번째 물음에는 이렇게 답한다.\n\n\"배에 찰싹이는 파도 소리와 스쳐 지나가는 물소리가 들려요. 돛과 밧줄이 팽팽히 당겨지고 돛대와 활대가 삐걱거려요. 바람이 세차요 — 삭구(索具) 사이로 그 소리가 들리고, 뱃머리가 물거품을 뒤로 흩뿌려요.\" 차리나 카테리나호가 아직 바다 위에서, 바르나를 향해 서둘러 나아가고 있음이 분명하다. 고달밍 경이 방금 돌아왔다. 그는 전보 네 통을 받았는데, 우리가 출발한 이래 날마다 한 통씩이었고, 모두 같은 내용이었다. 차리나 카테리나호가 어디에서도 로이즈에 보고되지 않았다는 것이었다. 그는 런던을 떠나기 전에 자기 대리인더러 배가 보고되었는지를 알리는 전보를 날마다 보내도록 일러두었던 것이다. 보고되지 않았더라도 전갈을 받기로 했는데, 그래야 전선 저편에서 감시가 이루어지고 있음을 확신할 수 있었기 때문이다.\n* 로이즈: 영국 런던의 해상보험 조합으로, 전 세계 선박의 운항 정보를 집계했다.\n\n우리는 저녁을 먹고 일찍 잠자리에 들었다. 내일 우리는 부영사를 만나, 가능하면 배가 도착하는 즉시 승선하는 일에 관해 주선할 작정이다. 판 헬싱은 우리에게 기회가 있다면 일출과 일몰 사이에 배에 오르는 것이라고 말한다. 백작은 설령 박쥐의 모습을 취한다 해도 제 뜻대로 흐르는 물을 건널 수 없으니, 배를 떠날 수 없다. 그리고 의심을 사지 않고서는 — 그가 분명히 피하고 싶어 하는 일이거니와 — 감히 사람의 모습으로 변할 수 없으므로, 그는 상자 안에 머물러 있어야 한다. 그러니 우리가 일출 후에 승선할 수 있다면, 그는 우리 손아귀에 들어 있는 셈이다. 가엾은 루시에게 그랬던 것처럼, 그가 깨어나기 전에 상자를 열고 그를 확실히 처치할 수 있기 때문이다. 그가 우리에게서 받을 자비란 그리 대단치 않을 것이다. 관리나 선원들과는 큰 어려움을 겪지 않으리라 생각한다. 하느님, 감사합니다! 이곳은 뇌물이면 무엇이든 할 수 있는 나라이고, 우리에게는 돈이 넉넉하다. 우리가 모르는 새에 배가 일몰과 일출 사이에 입항하는 일이 없도록만 확실히 해두면, 우리는 안전할 것이다. 돈주머니 판사께서 이 사건을 해결해 주시리라 생각한다!\n\n10월 16일. — 미나의 보고는 여전히 똑같다. 찰싹이는 파도와 스쳐 지나가는 물소리, 어둠과 순풍. 우리는 분명 시간을 잘 맞추고 있고, 차리나 카테리나호의 소식을 들으면 준비가 되어 있을 것이다. 배가 다르다넬스 해협을 지나야 하므로, 어떤 보고든 분명히 받게 될 것이다.\n\n10월 17일. — 이제는 백작이 여행에서 돌아오는 것을 맞이할 모든 채비가 꽤 잘 갖추어진 듯하다. 고달밍은 선박 회사 측에 그 배에 실린 상자에 자기 친구에게서 도난당한 무언가가 들어 있을지도 모른다고 말하여, 자기 책임 아래 그것을 열어봐도 좋다는 반승낙을 얻어냈다. 선주는 그에게 선장으로 하여금 그가 배에서 원하는 무엇이든 할 수 있도록 모든 편의를 제공하라는 서류를 주었고, 바르나의 대리인에게도 비슷한 권한을 주는 서류를 주었다. 우리는 그 대리인을 만났는데, 그는 고달밍의 친절한 태도에 크게 감동했다. 우리는 모두 그가 우리 뜻을 돕기 위해 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 해주리라 확신했다. 우리는 상자를 열게 될 경우 무엇을 할지 이미 정해두었다. 만일 백작이 그 안에 있다면, 판 헬싱과 수어드가 즉시 그의 목을 자르고 그의 심장에 말뚝을 박을 것이다. 모리스와 고달밍과 나는, 설령 준비해 둘 무기를 써야 하는 한이 있더라도, 방해를 막을 것이다. 교수의 말로는, 우리가 백작의 몸을 그렇게 처리할 수 있다면 그것은 곧 먼지로 화하리라고 한다. 그런 경우에는 혹여 살인의 혐의가 일더라도 우리에게 불리한 증거가 남지 않을 것이다. 그러나 설령 그렇지 않더라도, 우리는 우리의 행위로 흥하거나 망할 것이며, 어쩌면 언젠가는 바로 이 기록이 우리 가운데 누군가와 교수형의 밧줄 사이에 끼어드는 증거가 될지도 모른다. 나로 말하자면, 그런 일이 닥친다면 더없이 감사한 마음으로 그 위험을 무릅쓸 것이다. 우리는 우리의 뜻을 이루기 위해 온갖 수단을 다 강구할 작정이다. 우리는 차리나 카테리나호가 목격되는 즉시 특별 전령을 통해 우리에게 알리도록 몇몇 관리들과 약조해 두었다.\n\n10월 24일. — 꼬박 한 주를 기다렸다. 날마다 고달밍에게 전보가 왔으나, 한결같은 이야기뿐이었다. \"아직 보고되지 않음.\" 미나의 아침저녁 최면 답변도 변함이 없다. 찰싹이는 파도, 스쳐 지나가는 물소리, 삐걱거리는 돛대.\n\n전보, 10월 24일.\n\n런던 로이즈의 루퍼스 스미스가, 바르나 주재 영국 부영사 편으로 고달밍 경에게.\n\n\"차리나 카테리나호, 오늘 아침 다르다넬스 해협에서 보고됨.\"\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n10월 25일. — 내 축음기가 어찌나 그리운지! 펜으로 일기를 쓰는 것은 내게 성가신 일이지만, 판 헬싱은 내가 꼭 그래야 한다고 말한다. 어제 고달밍이 로이즈에서 전보를 받았을 때 우리는 모두 흥분에 들끓었다. 나는 이제 사내들이 전장에서 출동 명령을 들었을 때 무엇을 느끼는지 알겠다. 우리 일행 가운데 하커 부인만이 아무런 감정의 기색도 드러내지 않았다. 하기야 그녀가 그러지 않은 것도 이상한 일은 아니다. 우리는 그녀가 그 일에 관해 아무것도 알지 못하도록 각별히 조심했고, 모두 그녀 앞에서는 어떤 흥분도 내보이지 않으려 애썼기 때문이다. 예전 같았으면 우리가 아무리 감추려 애써도 그녀가 분명 알아챘으리라 확신한다. 그러나 이 점에서 그녀는 지난 삼 주 동안 크게 변했다. 무기력이 그녀에게 점점 짙어가고, 비록 그녀가 강건하고 건강해 보이며 혈색을 얼마간 되찾고 있긴 하나, 판 헬싱과 나는 마음을 놓지 못한다. 우리는 그녀에 관해 자주 이야기를 나눈다. 그러나 다른 이들에게는 한마디도 하지 않았다. 우리가 이 문제에 관해 조금이라도 의심을 품고 있음을 하커가 안다면, 그것은 가엾은 하커의 가슴을 — 분명 그의 신경을 — 무너뜨리고 말 것이다. 판 헬싱은 그녀가 최면 상태에 있는 동안 그녀의 이를 무척 면밀히 살핀다고 내게 말했는데, 그의 말로는 이가 날카로워지기 시작하지 않는 한 그녀에게 변화가 일어날 적극적인 위험은 없다는 것이다. 만일 이 변화가 온다면, 조치를 취해야 할 것이다!… 우리는 둘 다 그 조치가 무엇이 되어야 할지를 알고 있으나, 서로에게 그 생각을 입 밖에 내지는 않는다. 우리 둘 가운데 누구도 그 임무 앞에서 — 생각만으로도 끔찍한 일이긴 하나 — 물러서지는 않을 것이다. \"안락사\"란 더없이 훌륭하고 위안을 주는 말이다! 나는 그 말을 지어낸 이가 누구든 그에게 감사한다.\n\n차리나 카테리나호가 런던에서 온 속도로 보아, 다르다넬스 해협에서 이곳까지는 약 스물네 시간의 항해 거리에 지나지 않는다. 따라서 배는 아침 어느 무렵엔가 도착할 것이다. 그러나 그 전에는 도저히 들어올 수 없으므로, 우리는 모두 일찍 잠자리에 들려 한다. 우리는 준비가 되어 있도록 한 시에 일어날 것이다.\n\n10월 25일, 정오. — 배가 도착했다는 소식은 아직 없다. 하커 부인의 오늘 아침 최면 보고도 여느 때와 같았으니, 우리는 언제든 소식을 받을 가능성이 있다. 우리 사내들은 모두 흥분의 열기에 들떠 있는데, 침착한 하커만은 예외다. 그의 손은 얼음처럼 차갑고, 한 시간 전에 보니 그는 이제 늘 지니고 다니는 그 커다란 구르카 칼의 날을 갈고 있었다. 그 \"쿠크리\"의 날이 저 단호하고 얼음처럼 차가운 손에 이끌려 백작의 목에 닿는 날이면, 백작에게는 불길한 일이 될 것이다!\n* 쿠크리(Kukri): 네팔 구르카족의 안쪽으로 휜 칼.\n\n판 헬싱과 나는 오늘 하커 부인 때문에 다소 놀랐다. 정오 무렵 그녀는 우리가 달가워하지 않는 일종의 무기력에 빠져들었는데, 다른 이들에게는 입을 다물었으나 우리 둘 다 그것이 마음에 걸렸다. 그녀는 아침 내내 불안해했던 터라, 처음에 우리는 그녀가 잠든 것을 알고 다행으로 여겼다. 그러나 그녀의 남편이 그녀가 너무 깊이 잠들어 깨울 수가 없다고 무심코 말하자, 우리는 직접 보려고 그녀의 방으로 갔다. 그녀는 자연스럽게 숨 쉬고 있었고, 너무도 건강하고 평온해 보여서, 우리는 그 잠이 그녀에게 그 무엇보다 좋다는 데 의견을 같이했다. 가엾은 여인, 그녀는 잊어야 할 것이 너무도 많으니, 잠이 그녀에게 망각을 가져다준다면 그것이 그녀에게 이로운 것도 당연한 일이다.\n\n이후. — 우리의 판단은 옳았다. 몇 시간의 상쾌한 잠에서 깨어났을 때, 그녀는 여러 날 동안보다 더 밝고 좋아 보였기 때문이다. 일몰에 그녀는 여느 때처럼 최면 보고를 했다. 백작은 흑해 어디에 있든, 제 목적지를 향해 서두르고 있다. 바라건대, 제 파멸을 향해!\n\n10월 26일. — 또 하루가 갔으나 차리나 카테리나호의 소식은 없다. 배는 지금쯤 이곳에 와 있어야 한다. 그것이 아직 어딘가를 항해하고 있음은 분명한데, 일출 때 하커 부인의 최면 보고가 여전히 같았기 때문이다. 배가 안개 탓에 이따금 정박하고 있을 가능성도 있다. 어젯밤에 들어온 몇몇 증기선들이 항구의 남북 양쪽에 안개 띠가 끼었다고 보고했다. 우리는 감시를 계속해야 하는데, 이제 배가 언제든 신호를 보내올 수 있기 때문이다.\n\n10월 27일, 정오. — 참으로 기이하다. 우리가 기다리는 배의 소식이 아직 없다. 하커 부인은 어젯밤과 오늘 아침에 여느 때처럼 보고했다. \"찰싹이는 파도와 스쳐 지나가는 물소리.\" 다만 그녀는 \"파도가 무척 약해졌다\"고 덧붙였다. 런던에서 온 전보들도 한결같았다. \"더 이상의 보고 없음.\" 판 헬싱은 몹시 불안해하며, 방금 내게 백작이 우리에게서 빠져나가고 있는 것이 두렵다고 말했다. 그는 의미심장하게 덧붙였다.\n\n\"나는 미나 부인의 그 무기력이 마음에 들지 않았소. 영혼과 기억은 가수면(假睡眠) 중에 기이한 일들을 할 수 있다오.\" 나는 그에게 더 물으려 했으나, 마침 그때 하커가 들어왔고, 그는 경고하듯 손을 들어 올렸다. 우리는 오늘 밤 일몰에 그녀가 최면 상태에서 더 자세히 말하도록 애써보아야 한다.\n\n10월 28일. — 전보. 런던의 루퍼스 스미스가, 바르나 주재 영국 부영사 편으로 고달밍 경에게.\n\n\"차리나 카테리나호, 오늘 한 시 갈라츠에 입항하는 것으로 보고됨.\"\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n10월 28일. — 갈라츠 도착을 알리는 전보가 왔을 때, 그것이 우리 가운데 누구에게도 예상했을 법한 만큼의 충격은 아니었던 것 같다. 사실 우리는 그 벼락이 어디서, 어떻게, 언제 떨어질지 알지 못했다. 그러나 우리 모두 무언가 기이한 일이 일어나리라 예감하고 있었던 것 같다. 바르나 도착이 늦어지면서, 우리는 저마다 일이 우리가 예상했던 그대로 되지는 않으리라 짐작하게 되었다. 우리는 그저 그 변화가 어디서 일어날지를 알게 되기만을 기다렸을 뿐이다. 그렇다 해도 그것은 역시 놀라운 일이었다. 짐작건대 자연이라는 것이 워낙 희망적인 바탕 위에서 작동하는지라, 우리는 우리 자신의 판단을 거슬러서까지, 일이 마땅히 되어야 할 대로 되리라 — 우리가 그렇게 되리라고 알고 있는 대로가 아니라 — 믿게 되는 모양이다. 초월주의란 인간에게는 도깨비불일지언정 천사들에게는 등대인 법이다. 그것은 묘한 경험이었고, 우리는 저마다 그것을 다르게 받아들였다. 판 헬싱은 마치 전능하신 분께 항의라도 하듯 잠시 손을 머리 위로 들어 올렸다. 그러나 그는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 몇 초 뒤에는 단호하게 굳은 얼굴로 일어섰다. 고달밍 경은 무척 창백해져서 가쁘게 숨을 몰아쉬며 앉아 있었다. 나 자신은 반쯤 망연자실하여 차례로 이 사람 저 사람을 의아하게 바라보았다. 퀸시 모리스는 내가 너무도 잘 아는 그 재빠른 동작으로 허리띠를 졸라맸다. 우리의 옛 방랑 시절에 그것은 \"행동\"을 뜻했다. 하커 부인은 섬뜩하게 창백해져서, 이마의 흉터가 타오르는 듯했으나, 그녀는 두 손을 온순히 모으고 기도하듯 위를 올려다보았다. 하커는 미소를 지었다 — 정말로 미소를 지었다 — 희망 없는 자의 어둡고 쓰라린 미소를. 그러나 동시에 그의 행동은 그의 표정과 어긋났으니, 그의 손이 본능적으로 그 커다란 쿠크리 칼의 손잡이를 더듬어 그 위에 머물렀던 것이다. \"갈라츠로 가는 다음 기차는 언제 출발하오?\" 판 헬싱이 우리 모두를 향해 물었다.\n\n\"내일 아침 여섯 시 반이에요!\" 우리는 모두 흠칫 놀랐으니, 그 답이 하커 부인에게서 나왔기 때문이다.\n\n\"대체 어떻게 아십니까?\" 아서가 말했다.\n\n\"잊으셨군요 — 아니면 어쩌면 모르셨을지도 몰라요. 조너선은 알고 판 헬싱 박사님도 아시지만 — 저는 기차에 환장한 사람이거든요. 엑서터의 집에서 저는 남편에게 도움이 되려고 늘 시간표를 짜두곤 했어요. 그게 때로 어찌나 쓸모가 있던지, 이제는 늘 시간표를 연구한답니다. 만일 무언가가 우리를 드라큘라 성으로 데려가게 된다면 우리는 갈라츠를 거쳐, 아니면 적어도 부쿠레슈티를 지나 가게 되리란 걸 알았기에, 저는 시각을 무척 꼼꼼히 익혀두었어요. 안타깝게도 익힐 만한 게 많지는 않은데, 내일 가는 기차라고는 제가 말씀드린 것 하나뿐이거든요.\"\n\n\"놀라운 여인이오!\" 교수가 중얼거렸다.\n\n\"특별 열차를 구할 수는 없겠소?\" 고달밍 경이 물었다. 판 헬싱은 고개를 저었다. \"어려울 것 같소. 이 나라는 당신네 나라나 내 나라와는 무척 다르오. 설령 특별 열차를 구한다 해도, 아마 우리의 정규 열차만큼 빨리 도착하지는 못할 거요. 게다가 우리에게는 준비해야 할 것이 있소. 우리는 궁리해야 하오. 자, 이제 일을 나눕시다. 당신, 아서 벗은, 역으로 가서 표를 구하고 아침에 우리가 떠날 수 있도록 모든 채비를 갖추시오. 당신, 조너선 벗은, 선박 대리인에게 가서 갈라츠의 대리인 앞으로 보내는 편지를, 이곳에서와 똑같이 배를 수색할 권한과 함께 받아 오시오. 모리스 퀸시, 당신은 부영사를 만나, 갈라츠에 있는 그의 동료의 협조와, 우리 길을 순조롭게 해주기 위해 그가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 얻어내어, 도나우 강을 건널 때 한시도 허비되지 않게 하시오. 존은 미나 부인과 나와 함께 남을 것이고, 우리는 의논을 할 거요. 그래야 혹 시간이 길어져 당신들이 지체되더라도, 내가 부인과 함께 여기 있어 보고를 받을 테니, 해가 언제 지든 상관없을 거요.\"\n\n\"그리고 저는,\" 하커 부인이 밝게, 그 오랜 나날 동안보다 더 옛 모습 그대로의 그녀답게 말했다. \"온갖 방식으로 도움이 되도록 애쓰겠고, 예전에 그랬듯 여러분을 위해 생각하고 글을 쓰겠어요. 무언가가 기이한 방식으로 제게서 빠져나가고 있어요. 요즈음보다 더 자유로워진 느낌이에요!\" 세 젊은이는 그 순간 그녀의 말이 지닌 의미를 깨달은 듯 더 행복해 보였다. 그러나 판 헬싱과 나는 서로를 향해 돌아서며, 저마다 무겁고 근심 어린 눈길과 마주쳤다. 그러나 그때는 아무 말도 하지 않았다.\n\n세 사람이 저마다의 임무를 위해 나가자, 판 헬싱은 하커 부인에게 일기 사본을 찾아, 성에서 쓴 하커의 일기 부분을 자기에게 찾아달라고 청했다. 그녀가 그것을 가지러 나가고, 문이 그녀의 등 뒤로 닫히자 그는 내게 말했다.\n\n\"우리는 같은 생각을 하고 있소! 솔직히 말해보시오!\"\n\n\"무언가 변화가 있습니다. 그것은 저를 괴롭게 하는 희망인데, 우리를 속일 수도 있기 때문입니다.\"\n\n\"바로 그렇소. 내가 왜 그녀에게 원고를 가져오라 청했는지 아시오?\"\n\n\"모르겠습니다.\" 내가 말했다. \"당신과 저만 따로 만날 기회를 얻으려는 것이 아니라면 말입니다.\"\n\n\"부분적으로는 옳소, 존 벗. 그러나 부분적으로만 그렇소. 나는 당신에게 무언가 말해주고 싶소. 그리고 오, 벗이여, 나는 크나큰 — 무서운 — 위험을 무릅쓰고 있소. 그러나 나는 그것이 옳다고 믿소. 미나 부인이 우리 둘의 이해를 모두 멈춰 세운 그 말을 한 순간, 내게 한 가지 영감이 떠올랐소. 사흘 전의 가수면 중에 백작은 그녀의 마음을 읽으려고 그녀에게 자기 영혼을 보냈소. 아니면 더 그럴 법하게는, 해 뜨고 질 때 그가 자유로워지듯, 물이 스쳐 지나가는 배 안의 흙 상자 속에 있는 자기를 그녀가 보러 가도록 그녀를 데려갔던 거요. 그때 그는 우리가 여기 있음을 알게 되었소. 관 상자 속에 갇힌 그보다, 보는 눈과 듣는 귀를 지니고 열린 삶을 사는 그녀가 알려줄 것이 더 많으니 말이오. 이제 그는 우리에게서 벗어나려고 갖은 애를 다 쓰고 있소. 지금으로서는 그녀가 필요 없는 거요.\n\n\"그는 그토록 깊은 자기 지식으로, 그녀가 자기 부름에 응해 오리라 확신하고 있소. 그러나 그는 그녀를 끊어버렸소 — 자기가 할 수 있는 대로, 그녀를 자기 힘 밖으로 끌어내어 그녀가 그에게 오지 못하게 한 거요. 아! 거기에 내 희망이 있소. 오랜 세월 사람의 것이었고 또 하느님의 은총을 잃지 않은 우리 사내의 두뇌가, 여러 세기 동안 제 무덤 속에 누워 있으면서 아직 우리만큼 자라지 못했고 오직 이기적으로만, 따라서 작게만 작동하는 그의 어린아이 두뇌보다 더 높이 이르리란 희망 말이오. 미나 부인이 오는군. 그녀의 가수면에 관해서는 그녀에게 한마디도 하지 마시오! 그녀는 그것을 모르오. 그리고 그것은 그녀를 압도하여, 바로 우리가 그녀의 모든 희망과 모든 용기를 필요로 할 때, 우리가 사내의 두뇌처럼 단련되었으면서도 사랑스러운 여인의 것이며 백작이 그녀에게 준, 그리고 그가 그렇게 여기지는 않아도 그가 온전히 거두어 갈 수는 없는 특별한 힘을 지닌 그 위대한 두뇌를 가장 필요로 할 때, 그녀를 절망에 빠뜨릴 거요. 쉿! 내가 말하게 두시오. 그러면 당신도 알게 될 거요. 오, 존, 벗이여, 우리는 무서운 궁지에 몰려 있소. 나는 전에 없이 두렵소. 우리는 오직 선하신 하느님을 믿을 수밖에 없소. 조용히! 그녀가 오는군!\"\n\n나는 교수가 무너져서, 루시가 죽었을 때 그랬던 것처럼 발작을 일으키려는 줄 알았다. 그러나 그는 안간힘을 써서 자신을 다스렸고, 하커 부인이 밝고 행복해 보이는 얼굴로, 일을 하느라 제 비참함은 잊은 듯이 방으로 사뿐히 들어왔을 때에는 완전히 신경의 평정을 되찾고 있었다. 그녀는 들어오면서 타자로 친 종이 여러 장을 판 헬싱에게 건넸다. 그는 그것을 엄숙하게 훑어보았는데, 읽어 내려가면서 그의 얼굴이 밝아졌다. 그러고는 손가락과 엄지 사이에 종이를 쥐고 말했다.\n\n\"존 벗, 이미 그토록 많은 경험을 지닌 당신에게 — 그리고 당신도, 친애하는 미나 부인, 젊으신 당신에게도 — 여기 한 가지 교훈이 있소. 생각하기를 결코 두려워하지 마시오. 어떤 어렴풋한 생각이 내 머릿속에서 자주 윙윙거렸으나, 나는 그것이 날개를 펴게 풀어놓기를 두려워했소. 이제 여기서, 더 많은 지식을 얻고 보니, 나는 그 어렴풋한 생각이 비롯된 곳으로 되돌아가, 그것이 결코 어렴풋한 생각 따위가 아님을 알게 되었소. 그것은 비록 너무 어려 아직 그 작은 날개를 쓸 만큼 강하지는 못하나, 온전한 하나의 생각이었던 거요. 아니, 내 벗 한스 안데르센의 '미운 오리 새끼'처럼, 그것은 오리 생각 따위가 전혀 아니라, 때가 와서 날개를 시험할 때면 그 큰 날개로 당당히 떠가는 커다란 백조 생각이오. 보시오, 조너선이 여기 적어놓은 것을 읽어보겠소.\n\n\"훗날의 시대에 거듭거듭 자기 군대를 이끌고 대하(大河)를 건너 터키 땅으로 쳐들어갔던 그 일족의 또 다른 자. 그는 격퇴당했을 때 다시, 또다시, 또다시 쳐들어왔으니, 비록 제 군대가 도륙당하던 피투성이 싸움터에서 홀로 돌아와야 했을지라도, 오직 자기만이 끝내 승리하리란 것을 알았기 때문이다.\"\n\n\"이것이 우리에게 무엇을 말해주오? 많지 않다고? 아니오! 백작의 어린아이 생각은 아무것도 보지 못하오. 그러기에 그가 그토록 거리낌 없이 말하는 거요. 당신의 사내 생각도 아무것도 보지 못하고, 내 사내 생각도 바로 방금 전까지는 아무것도 보지 못했소. 그렇소! 그러나 거기에, 자기 또한 그것이 무엇을 뜻하는지 — 무엇을 뜻할 수 있는지 — 알지 못한 채 생각 없이 말하는 누군가에게서 또 다른 말이 나오는 거요. 마치 가만히 머물러 있던 원소들이, 자연의 순리에 따라 제 길을 가다가 서로 닿으면 — 그러면 펑! 하늘 가득 번쩍이는 한 줄기 빛이 일어, 누군가를 멀게 하고 죽이고 파괴하지만, 또한 그 아래 온 대지를 수 리(里)에 걸쳐 환히 비추는 것과 같소. 그렇지 않소? 자, 설명하겠소. 우선, 당신은 범죄의 철학을 연구해 본 적이 있소? '예'이기도 하고 '아니오'이기도 하지. 당신은, 존, '예'요. 그것은 광기의 연구이니 말이오. 당신은, 미나 부인, '아니오'요. 범죄가 당신을 건드린 적이 없으니 — 단 한 번을 빼고는 말이오. 그래도 당신의 정신은 올바르게 작동하며, 개별에서 보편으로 함부로 추론하지 않소. 범죄자들에게는 이런 특성이 있소. 그것이 모든 나라에서 모든 시대에 걸쳐 너무도 한결같기에, 철학을 많이 알지 못하는 경찰조차 그것이 그러하다는 것을 경험적으로 알게 되오. 그것이 곧 경험적이라는 거요. 범죄자는 늘 한 가지 범죄에 매달리오 — 그것이 곧 범죄에 예정된 듯하여 다른 어떤 것도 마다하는 진짜 범죄자요. 이런 범죄자는 온전한 사내 두뇌를 지니지 못했소. 그는 영리하고 교활하며 임기응변에 능하지만, 두뇌에 관한 한 사내의 키에 이르지 못했소. 그는 많은 점에서 어린아이 두뇌를 지녔소. 그런데 우리의 이 범죄자 역시 범죄에 예정되어 있소. 그 또한 어린아이 두뇌를 지녔고, 그가 한 짓을 하는 것은 어린아이다운 짓이오. 작은 새, 작은 물고기, 작은 짐승은 원리로써가 아니라 경험으로써 배우오. 그리고 일단 할 줄을 배우면, 그에게는 거기서부터 더 많은 것을 할 출발점이 생기는 거요. '도스 포우 스토', 아르키메데스가 말했소. '내게 받침점을 다오, 그러면 세상을 움직이겠다!' 한 번 해보는 것이 곧 어린아이 두뇌가 사내 두뇌로 되는 받침점이오. 그리고 더 많은 것을 하려는 목적을 갖기 전까지, 그는 전에 했던 그대로, 매번 똑같은 짓을 거듭하는 거요! 오, 친애하는 부인, 당신의 눈이 열렸고, 당신에게는 그 번개의 섬광이 수 리에 걸친 모든 것을 비춰 보이고 있구려.\" 하커 부인이 두 손을 마주치기 시작했고 그녀의 눈이 반짝였기 때문이다. 그는 말을 이었다.\n\n\"이제 당신이 말해보시오. 우리 메마른 두 과학자에게, 그 너무도 빛나는 눈으로 무엇을 보는지 말해보시오.\" 그는 그녀가 말하는 동안 그녀의 손을 잡고 있었다. 그녀가 말하는 동안 그의 손가락과 엄지가 그녀의 맥박 위에 닿아 있었는데, 내 생각에는 본능적으로 무의식중에 그러는 듯했다.\n\n\"백작은 범죄자이고 범죄자 유형이에요. 노르다우와 롬브로소라면 그를 그렇게 분류했을 거예요. 그리고 범죄자인 한, 그는 불완전하게 형성된 정신을 지니고 있어요. 그래서 곤경에 처하면 그는 습관 속에서 방책을 찾아야 해요. 그의 과거가 단서이고, 우리가 아는 — 그것도 그 자신의 입에서 나온 — 그 과거의 한 페이지가 말해줘요. 전에 한 번, 모리스 씨라면 '궁지'라 부를 처지에 빠졌을 때, 그는 자기가 침략하려던 땅에서 제 나라로 돌아갔고, 거기서 목적을 잃지 않은 채 새로운 시도를 위해 자신을 채비했어요. 그는 자기 일에 더 잘 무장하고 다시 쳐들어왔고, 그리고 이겼어요. 그렇게 그는 새로운 땅을 침략하러 런던으로 왔어요. 그는 패배했고, 성공의 모든 희망이 사라지고 자기 존재가 위태로워지자, 그는 바다 건너 제 고향으로 달아났어요. 꼭 예전에 터키 땅에서 도나우 강 건너로 달아났던 것처럼요.\"\n\n\"좋소, 좋소! 오, 당신은 참으로 영리한 부인이오!\" 판 헬싱이 열렬히 외치며 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추었다. 잠시 후 그는 마치 우리가 병실에서 진찰을 하고 있기라도 한 듯 차분하게 내게 말했다.\n\n\"겨우 일흔둘이오. 그것도 이 모든 흥분 속에서 말이오. 나는 희망이 있소.\" 그는 다시 그녀를 향해 돌아서더니, 간절한 기대를 담아 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 계속하시오. 계속! 당신이 원한다면 더 들려줄 것이 있소. 두려워 마시오. 존도 나도 알고 있소. 어쨌든 나는 알고 있으니, 당신이 옳으면 그렇다고 말해주겠소. 두려움 없이 말해보시오!\"\n\n\"애써보겠어요. 하지만 제가 자기중심적으로 보이더라도 용서해 주세요.\"\n\n\"아니오! 두려워 마시오. 당신은 자기중심적이어야 하오. 우리가 생각하는 것이 바로 당신이니 말이오.\"\n\n\"그러면, 그는 범죄자이니 이기적이에요. 그리고 그의 지성이 작고 그의 행동이 이기심에 바탕을 두고 있으니, 그는 자신을 한 가지 목적에 가두고 있어요. 그 목적은 가차 없어요. 그가 자기 군대를 산산조각 나도록 버려두고 도나우 강 건너로 달아났듯이, 지금 그는 모든 것을 아랑곳하지 않고 오직 안전해지는 데에만 마음을 쏟고 있어요. 그러니 그 자신의 이기심이, 그 무서운 밤에 그가 제게 행사하게 된 그 끔찍한 힘에서 제 영혼을 어느 정도 풀어주고 있어요. 저는 그걸 느꼈어요! 오, 느꼈어요! 하느님의 크나큰 자비에 감사드려요! 제 영혼은 그 무서운 시간 이래 어느 때보다 자유로워졌어요. 그리고 저를 따라다니며 괴롭히는 것이라곤, 혹여 어떤 가수면이나 꿈속에서 그가 제 지식을 제 목적에 이용했을지도 모른다는 두려움뿐이에요.\" 교수가 일어섰다.\n\n\"그는 그렇게 당신의 정신을 이용했소. 그리고 그것으로써 그는 우리를 여기 바르나에 붙들어 둔 채, 그를 실은 배가 자욱한 안개를 헤치고 갈라츠로 내달리게 한 거요. 거기서 그는 틀림없이 우리에게서 달아날 준비를 해두었던 거요. 그러나 그의 어린아이 정신은 거기까지밖에 보지 못했소. 그리고 어쩌면, 하느님의 섭리에서 늘 그러하듯, 악행을 저지르는 자가 제 이기적인 이득을 위해 가장 믿었던 바로 그것이, 그에게 가장 큰 해가 되고 마는 것인지도 모르오. 위대한 시편 기자가 말하듯, 사냥꾼이 제가 놓은 덫에 걸리는 거요. 이제 그는 우리 모두의 자취에서 벗어났다고, 그토록 여러 시간을 벌어 우리에게서 달아났다고 여기니, 그의 이기적인 어린아이 두뇌는 그를 잠으로 꾀어 들일 거요. 그는 또한, 자기가 당신의 정신을 아는 일에서 스스로를 끊어버렸으니, 당신이 그를 알 길도 없으리라 여기고 있소. 바로 거기서 그가 실수하는 거요! 그가 당신에게 베푼 그 무서운 피의 세례가, 해 뜨고 질 때 당신이 자유로운 시간에 지금껏 해온 것처럼, 영혼으로 그에게 가도록 당신을 자유롭게 해주는 거요. 그런 때에 당신은 그의 뜻이 아니라 내 뜻으로 가는 거요. 그리고 당신과 다른 이들을 위한 이 좋은 힘은, 당신이 그의 손에 겪은 고통에서 얻어낸 것이오. 그가 그것을 모르고, 또 제 몸을 지키려고 우리가 어디 있는지를 아는 일에서마저 스스로를 끊어버린 만큼, 이제 그것은 더욱더 귀중하오. 그러나 우리는 이기적이지 않으며, 우리는 이 모든 어둠과 이 숱한 캄캄한 시간들을 거치는 동안 하느님께서 우리와 함께하신다고 믿소. 우리는 그를 뒤쫓을 것이오. 그리고 우리는 물러서지 않을 것이오. 설령 우리가 그와 같이 될 위험을 무릅쓰는 한이 있더라도 말이오. 존 벗, 이것은 위대한 시간이었소. 그리고 그것은 우리를 우리 길로 크게 나아가게 했소. 당신은 기록자가 되어 이 모든 것을 적어두어야 하오. 그래야 다른 이들이 제 일을 마치고 돌아왔을 때 그들에게 그것을 줄 수 있고, 그러면 그들도 우리처럼 알게 될 거요.\"\n\n그리하여 나는 그들이 돌아오기를 기다리는 동안 이것을 적었고, 하커 부인은 자기 타자기로, 우리에게 그 원고를 가져온 이후의 모든 것을 적었다.\n\n# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n10월 29일. — 이 글은 바르나에서 갈라츠로 가는 기차 안에서 쓴다. 어젯밤 우리는 모두 일몰이 되기 조금 전에 모였다. 우리 각자는 저마다 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 제 일을 해두었다. 생각과 노력과 기회가 닿는 한, 우리는 여정 전체에 대해, 그리고 갈라츠에 닿았을 때 해야 할 일에 대해 만반의 준비를 갖추었다. 늘 그 시각이 돌아오자 하커 부인은 최면을 받을 채비를 했다. 그리고 판 헬싱 쪽에서 평소보다 더 길고 더 진지하게 애를 쓴 끝에야 그녀는 가수면 상태로 빠져들었다. 보통은 그녀가 어떤 실마리만으로도 말을 했으나, 이번에는 무언가를 알아내기까지 교수가 그녀에게 질문을 던져야 했고, 그것도 꽤 단호하게 물어야 했다. 마침내 그녀의 대답이 흘러나왔다.\n\n\"아무것도 보이지 않아요. 우리는 멈춰 있어요. 철썩이는 파도는 없고, 다만 닻줄에 부드럽게 부딪치며 흐르는 물의 한결같은 소용돌이만 있어요. 가까이서, 또 멀리서 사람들이 부르는 목소리가 들려요. 노받이에서 노가 구르고 삐걱거리는 소리도요. 어디선가 대포가 발사돼요. 그 메아리는 아득히 먼 데서 울리는 것 같아요. 머리 위로 발 구르는 소리가 들리고, 밧줄과 사슬이 끌려가요. 이건 뭐죠? 한 줄기 빛이 비쳐요. 공기가 제 몸 위로 불어오는 게 느껴져요.\"\n\n여기서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다. 그녀는 소파에 누워 있던 자리에서 충동적인 듯 몸을 일으켜, 마치 무거운 것을 들어올리는 양 두 손바닥을 위로 향한 채 들어올렸다. 판 헬싱과 나는 알겠다는 듯 서로를 바라보았다. 퀸시는 눈썹을 살짝 치켜올리며 그녀를 뚫어지게 응시했고, 한편 하커의 손은 본능적으로 그의 쿠크리 칼자루를 움켜쥐었다. 긴 침묵이 흘렀다. 우리는 그녀가 말할 수 있는 시간이 지나가고 있음을 모두 알았으나, 무어라 말해봐야 소용없으리라는 것도 느꼈다. 갑자기 그녀가 똑바로 앉더니, 눈을 뜨면서 다정하게 말했다.\n\n\"여러분 중에 차 한 잔 드시고 싶은 분 안 계세요? 다들 무척 피곤하시겠어요!\" 우리로서는 그저 그녀를 기쁘게 해줄 수밖에 없었으므로 그렇게 하기로 했다. 그녀는 부산히 차를 가지러 나갔고, 그녀가 나가자 판 헬싱이 말했다.\n\n\"보십시오, 여러분. 그가 육지에 가까이 와 있습니다. 그는 흙 상자를 떠났어요. 하지만 아직 뭍에 올라야 합니다. 밤이라면 어딘가에 숨어 있을지도 모릅니다. 그러나 뭍으로 옮겨지지 않거나 배가 육지에 닿지 않으면 그는 육지에 이를 수 없습니다. 그런 경우 밤이라면 그는 형체를 바꾸어, 휘트비에서 그랬듯 뭍으로 뛰어오르거나 날아오를 수 있습니다. 하지만 그가 뭍에 오르기 전에 날이 밝으면, 그때는 누군가에게 옮겨지지 않는 한 빠져나갈 수 없습니다. 그리고 옮겨진다면, 세관원이 그 상자에 무엇이 들었는지 발견할지도 모릅니다. 그러니 결국, 만일 그가 오늘 밤이나 새벽 전에 뭍으로 빠져나가지 못한다면, 그에게는 하루가 통째로 사라지는 셈입니다. 그러면 우리는 제때에 닿을 수 있습니다. 그가 밤중에 빠져나가지 못하면 우리는 대낮에, 상자에 갇힌 채 우리 손아귀에 든 그를 덮칠 테니까요. 발각될까 두려워, 깨어 모습을 드러낸 제 본래의 모습으로는 감히 있지 못할 테니 말입니다.\"\n\n더 할 말이 없었으므로, 우리는 인내하며 새벽을 기다렸다. 그때라면 하커 부인에게서 더 많은 것을 알아낼 수 있을 터였다.\n\n오늘 이른 아침, 우리는 가수면 상태에서 나올 그녀의 응답을 숨죽인 채 애타게 기다렸다. 최면 단계에 드는 데 전보다도 더 오랜 시간이 걸렸고, 막상 그 상태가 왔을 때에는 완전히 해가 뜨기까지 남은 시간이 너무도 짧아, 우리는 절망하기 시작했다. 판 헬싱은 온 영혼을 그 노력에 쏟아붓는 듯했다. 마침내 그의 의지에 따라 그녀가 대답했다.\n\n\"온통 어두워요. 제 높이에서 철썩이는 물소리가 들리고, 나무와 나무가 맞부딪치는 듯한 삐걱대는 소리가 들려요.\" 그녀는 말을 멈추었고, 붉은 해가 솟아올랐다. 우리는 밤이 될 때까지 기다려야만 했다.\n\n그리하여 우리는 기대의 고통에 시달리며 갈라츠를 향해 달려가고 있다. 새벽 두세 시 사이에 도착할 예정이었으나, 벌써 부쿠레슈티에서부터 세 시간이나 늦어졌으니, 해가 뜬 뒤 한참이 지나서야 들어가게 될 게 분명하다. 그러므로 우리는 하커 부인에게서 두 차례 더 최면 전언을 들을 수 있을 것이며, 그 둘 중 하나든 둘 다든 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 더 밝혀줄지도 모른다.\n\n이후. — 일몰이 왔다가 지나갔다. 다행히도 그것은 방해될 것이 없는 때에 찾아왔다. 만일 우리가 역에 있을 때 일어났더라면 필요한 고요와 격리를 확보하지 못했을지도 모른다. 하커 부인은 오늘 아침보다도 더 더디게 최면의 영향에 응했다. 나는 백작의 감각을 읽어내는 그녀의 능력이, 우리가 그것을 가장 필요로 하는 바로 이때에 사그라들어버릴까 두렵다. 내 생각에는 그녀의 상상력이 작동하기 시작한 듯하다. 지금까지 가수면 상태에 들었을 때 그녀는 더없이 단순한 사실들에만 머물러 있었다. 이런 일이 계속된다면 끝내 우리를 그릇된 길로 이끌지도 모른다. 만일 그녀의 앎의 능력이 사그라드는 것과 똑같이 그녀에 대한 백작의 지배력도 사그라드는 것이라면 반가운 생각이겠으나, 그렇지 않을까 봐 나는 두렵다. 그녀가 입을 열었을 때, 그 말은 수수께끼 같았다.\n\n\"무언가가 빠져나가고 있어요. 차가운 바람처럼 제 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 게 느껴져요. 멀리서 어수선한 소리가 들려요. 낯선 언어로 말하는 사람들 같은, 사납게 떨어지는 물 같은, 그리고 늑대들이 울부짖는 소리 같은.\" 그녀는 말을 멈추었고 몸서리가 그녀를 훑고 지나갔는데, 그것은 몇 초 동안 점점 격렬해지더니, 마침내 그녀는 마치 중풍에 걸린 듯 떨었다. 그녀는 교수의 단호한 거듭된 물음에도 더는 아무 말이 없었다. 가수면에서 깨어났을 때 그녀는 차갑고, 지쳤으며, 나른했다. 그러나 정신만은 또렷했다. 그녀는 아무것도 기억하지 못했으나, 자기가 무슨 말을 했느냐고 물었다. 들려주자 그녀는 오래도록 말없이 그것을 깊이 곱씹었다.\n\n10월 30일, 오전 7시. — 우리는 이제 갈라츠 가까이에 와 있으니, 나중에는 글 쓸 겨를이 없을지도 모른다. 오늘 아침 일출을 우리 모두 애타게 기다렸다. 최면 가수면을 얻기가 점점 어려워지고 있음을 아는 판 헬싱은 평소보다 일찍 손동작을 시작했다. 그러나 그것은 정해진 시각에 이르기까지 아무 효력이 없다가, 해가 뜨기 겨우 일 분 전에야 한층 더 큰 어려움 끝에 그녀가 응했다. 교수는 지체 없이 물었고, 그녀의 대답도 그만큼 빠르게 흘러나왔다.\n\n\"온통 어두워요. 제 귀와 같은 높이에서 물이 소용돌이치며 지나가는 소리가 들리고, 나무와 나무가 삐걱대는 소리가 들려요. 소들이 멀리서 울어요. 또 다른 소리가 있어요, 묘한 소린데 마치……\" 그녀는 멈추었고 얼굴이 하얘지더니, 더욱더 하얘졌다.\n\n\"계속하시오. 계속! 말하시오, 명하노니!\" 판 헬싱이 고통에 찬 목소리로 말했다. 동시에 그의 눈에는 절망이 어렸으니, 솟아오른 해가 하커 부인의 창백한 얼굴마저 붉게 물들이고 있었기 때문이다. 그녀는 눈을 떴고, 우리 모두는 그녀가 다정하게, 그리고 보기에 더없이 무심하게 말하는 것에 흠칫 놀랐다.\n\n\"어머, 교수님, 제가 못한다는 걸 아시면서 왜 시키세요? 저는 아무것도 기억나지 않아요.\" 그러더니 우리 얼굴에 어린 경악의 빛을 보고는, 근심스러운 표정으로 이 사람 저 사람을 둘러보며 말했다.\n\n\"제가 무슨 말을 했죠? 제가 뭘 했나요? 저는 아무것도 몰라요. 다만 여기 누워 반쯤 잠들어 있다가, '계속하시오! 말하시오, 명하노니!' 하시는 말씀을 들었을 뿐이에요. 마치 제가 못된 아이라도 되는 양 명령하시는 걸 들으니 어찌나 우습던지요!\"\n\n\"오, 미나 부인,\" 그가 슬프게 말했다. \"이것이야말로, 만일 증거가 필요하다면, 내가 부인을 얼마나 사랑하고 존경하는지를 보여주는 증거지요. 부인을 위한 한마디, 그 어느 때보다 간절히 한 말이, 이토록 낯설게 들릴 수 있다니 말입니다. 내가 기꺼이 따르기를 자랑으로 여기는 그분께 명령을 내리는 것이었으니까요!\"\n\n기적 소리가 울린다. 우리는 갈라츠에 가까워지고 있다. 우리는 불안과 열망으로 활활 타오르고 있다.\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」\n\n10월 30일. — 모리스 씨가 나를, 전보로 방을 예약해둔 호텔로 데려다주었다. 그가 외국어를 하나도 못하는 까닭에 가장 빼기 쉬운 사람이었기 때문이다. 우리 일행의 배치는 바르나에서와 거의 같았는데, 다만 고달밍 경이 부영사에게 간 점이 달랐다. 우리가 몹시 급한 처지였으므로 그의 작위가 그 관리에게 어떤 즉각적인 보증쯤이 될 수 있을 터였기 때문이다. 조너선과 두 의사는 차리나 카테리나 호의 도착에 관한 자세한 사항을 알아보러 선박 대리점으로 갔다.\n\n이후. — 고달밍 경이 돌아왔다. 영사는 출타 중이고 부영사는 병중이라, 일상 업무는 한 서기가 처리하고 있었다. 그는 매우 친절했고, 자기 권한 내에서 무엇이든 해주겠다고 했다.\n\n「조너선 하커의 일기」\n\n10월 30일. — 아홉 시에 판 헬싱 박사와 수어드 박사, 그리고 나는 런던 햅굿 상회의 대리점인 매켄지 앤 슈타인코프 상회를 찾아갔다. 그들은 고달밍 경이 전보로 보낸 요청에 답하여, 그들 권한 내에서 우리에게 온갖 호의를 베풀어달라는 전보를 런던에서 받아둔 터였다. 그들은 더없이 친절하고 정중했고, 우리를 즉시 강 항구에 닻을 내리고 정박해 있는 차리나 카테리나 호에 태워 데려갔다. 거기서 우리는 도널슨이라는 선장을 만났는데, 그는 자기 항해에 대해 이야기해주었다. 그는 평생 그토록 순조로운 항해는 처음이었다고 했다.\n\n\"이보시오!\" 그가 말했다. \"그게 우리를 겁나게 합디다. 평균을 맞추느라 무슨 진귀한 불운으로 그 대가를 치러야 할 줄로 알았으니까요. 마치 악마가 제 속셈으로 돛에다 바람을 불어주기라도 하는 양, 등 뒤에서 바람을 받으며 런던에서 흑해까지 내달리는 건 영 께름칙한 일입니다. 그러는 내내 우리는 도무지 아무것도 알아낼 수가 없었어요. 배든 항구든 곶이든 가까이 다가가면 안개가 우리한테 내려앉아 우리와 함께 따라오다가, 그게 걷히고 나서 내다보면 아무것도, 빌어먹을 것 하나도 보이질 않았소. 우리는 신호도 못 보낸 채 지브롤터를 지나쳤고, 다르다넬스에 닿아 통과 허가를 받으려 기다려야 했을 때까지, 무엇 하나 부를 만한 거리 안에 있어본 적이 없었습니다. 처음엔 돛을 늦추고 안개가 걷힐 때까지 이리저리 떠돌까 했지만, 이따금 이런 생각이 들더군요. 악마가 우리를 빨리 흑해로 들여보낼 작정이라면, 우리가 원하든 말든 기어이 그리할 거라고요. 빠른 항해라면 선주들한테 우리 면목이 깎일 일도, 우리 영업에 해될 일도 아니지요. 게다가 제 속셈을 다 이룬 그 늙은 양반도, 자기를 막지 않은 우리한테 점잖게 고마워할 테고요.\" 이런 순박함과 교활함, 미신과 상업적 타산의 뒤섞임에 판 헬싱이 흥미를 느껴 말했다.\n\n\"이보시오, 그 악마는 더러 사람들이 여기는 것보다 훨씬 영리하답니다. 게다가 제 적수를 만났을 때를 알아보지요!\" 선장은 그 칭찬에 싫지 않은 기색으로 말을 이었다.\n\n\"보스포루스를 지나고 나니 선원들이 투덜거리기 시작합디다. 그중 몇몇, 루마니아인들이 와서, 우리가 런던을 막 떠나기 직전에 묘하게 생긴 늙은이가 배에 실어놓은 큰 상자를 바다에 던져버리자고 청하더군요. 나는 그들이 그 사내한테 눈총을 주고, 흉안을 막으려 두 손가락을 내미는 걸 본 적이 있소. 이보시오! 외국인들의 미신이란 그야말로 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없어요! 나는 그들을 얼른 제 일이나 하라고 쫓아버렸소만, 바로 뒤에 안개가 우리를 에워싸자 그들이 무언가에 대해 느낀 것과 비슷한 기분이 나도 좀 들더군요. 그게 그 큰 상자 때문이라곤 말하지 않겠소만. 자, 우리는 계속 나아갔고, 닷새 동안 안개가 걷히질 않으니 그냥 바람이 우리를 데려가게 내버려뒀소. 악마가 어딘가로 가고 싶어 한다면야, 뭐, 제대로 데려다놓을 테니까요. 안 그러면, 뭐, 어쨌든 우리는 빈틈없이 망을 볼 작정이었고요. 아니나 다를까, 내내 순탄한 뱃길에 깊은 물이었소. 그리고 이틀 전, 아침 해가 안개를 뚫고 비쳐들었을 때, 우리는 마침 갈라츠 맞은편 강에 와 있더이다. 루마니아인들은 길길이 날뛰며, 옳든 그르든 그 상자를 꺼내 강에 던져버리라고 나한테 우겼소. 나는 그들과 그 일을 두고 쇠지렛대로 입씨름을 해야 했지요. 마지막 한 놈이 머리를 감싸쥐고 갑판에서 일어났을 때엔, 흉안이고 뭐고 간에 선주들의 재산과 위탁물은 다뉴브 강물 속보다 내 손에 있는 편이 낫다는 걸 그들에게 납득시킨 셈이었소. 보시오, 그들은 이미 그 상자를 던지려고 갑판에 꺼내놓은 터였는데, 거기에 '바르나 경유 갈라츠'라고 적혀 있길래, 항구에서 짐을 부릴 때까지 그냥 놔뒀다가 한꺼번에 치워버릴 작정이었소. 그날은 짐을 별로 못 부려서, 밤새 닻을 내리고 머물러야 했지요. 그런데 이튿날 아침, 동트기 한 시간 전 산뜻하니 이른 시각에, 한 사내가 영국에서 자기 앞으로 적혀 온 지시서를 들고 배에 올라, 드라큘라 백작이라는 자에게 보내는 상자를 받겠다고 합디다. 아니나 다를까, 그 일은 곧바로 처리할 만한 것이었소. 그자의 서류는 다 멀쩡했고, 나는 그 빌어먹을 물건을 떼어버리게 되어 기뻤소. 나 자신도 그것 때문에 불안해지기 시작하던 참이었으니 말이오. 만일 악마가 정말로 이 배에 무슨 짐을 싣고 있었다면, 내 생각엔 다름 아닌 바로 그 물건이었지 싶소!\"\n\n\"그것을 가져간 사내의 이름이 무엇이었소?\" 판 헬싱 박사가 억눌린 열의로 물었다.\n\n\"얼른 일러드리리다!\" 그가 대답하더니, 선실로 내려가 \"이마누엘 힐데스하임\"이라 서명된 영수증을 가지고 나왔다. 주소는 부르겐 가 16번지였다. 우리는 이것이 선장이 아는 전부임을 알게 되었으므로, 고맙다는 인사를 하고 물러났다.\n\n우리는 힐데스하임을 그의 사무실에서 찾아냈는데, 그는 아델파이 극장에나 나올 법한 부류의 히브리인으로, 양 같은 코에 페즈를 쓰고 있었다. 그의 흥정은 현금으로 뾰족이 강조되었고 — 거기에 마침표를 찍는 건 우리 몫이었다 — 약간의 흥정 끝에 그는 자기가 아는 바를 우리에게 일러주었다. 그것은 단순하나 중요한 내용이었다. 그는 런던의 드빌 씨로부터 편지 한 통을 받았는데, 차리나 카테리나 호로 갈라츠에 도착할 상자 하나를, 가능하면 세관을 피하기 위해 일출 전에 받으라는 것이었다. 그는 이것을 강을 따라 항구까지 거래하는 슬로바키아인들을 상대하는 페트로프 스킨스키라는 자에게 맡기기로 되어 있었다. 그는 그 일의 대가로 영국 은행권 한 장을 받았으며, 그것은 다뉴브 국제은행에서 정식으로 금화로 환전되었다. 스킨스키가 그를 찾아왔을 때, 그는 짐삯을 아끼려고 그를 배로 데려가 상자를 넘겨주었다. 그것이 그가 아는 전부였다.\n\n그 다음 우리는 스킨스키를 찾아보았으나 찾을 수 없었다. 그에게 별 애정이 없어 보이는 이웃 하나는, 그가 이틀 전에 어디로인지 아무도 모르게 떠나버렸다고 했다. 이는 그의 집주인에 의해서도 확인되었는데, 그는 심부름꾼을 통해 집 열쇠와 밀린 집세를 영국 돈으로 받았다고 했다. 그것은 어젯밤 열 시에서 열한 시 사이의 일이었다. 우리는 다시 막다른 데에 이르렀다.\n\n우리가 이야기를 나누고 있을 때 한 사람이 달려와, 성 베드로 교회 묘지 담장 안에서 스킨스키의 시신이 발견되었으며, 마치 어떤 야수에게 당한 것처럼 목이 찢겨 벌어져 있었다고 헐떡이며 외쳤다. 우리가 이야기를 나누던 사람들이 그 끔찍한 광경을 보러 달려나갔고, 여인들은 \"이건 슬로바키아인의 짓이야!\" 하고 부르짖었다. 우리는 어떤 식으로든 그 사건에 휘말려 발이 묶이지 않으려고 서둘러 자리를 떴다.\n\n집으로 돌아오는 길에 우리는 어떤 확실한 결론에도 이르지 못했다. 우리는 모두 그 상자가 물길로 어딘가를 향해 가고 있음을 확신했으나, 그곳이 어디일지는 우리가 알아내야 할 터였다. 무거운 마음으로 우리는 호텔의 미나에게로 돌아왔다.\n\n우리가 함께 모였을 때 가장 먼저 한 일은, 미나를 다시 우리의 일에 끌어들이는 문제를 의논하는 것이었다. 사정이 절박해지고 있고, 그것은 위험천만하나마 적어도 하나의 가능성이었다. 그 첫 단계로, 나는 그녀에게 한 약속에서 풀려났다.\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」\n\n10월 30일, 저녁. — 그들은 너무도 지치고 기진하고 풀이 죽어 있어, 좀 쉬게 하기 전에는 할 수 있는 일이 없었다. 그래서 나는 모두에게 삼십 분쯤 누워 쉬라 하고, 그동안 내가 지금까지의 일을 모두 기록해두기로 했다. 나는 '여행자용' 타자기를 발명한 사람에게, 그리고 나를 위해 이것을 구해다준 모리스 씨에게 무척 고마운 마음이 든다. 펜으로 이 일을 써야 했더라면 나는 영 갈피를 못 잡았을 것이다…….\n\n다 끝냈다. 가엾고 사랑하는, 사랑하는 조너선, 그가 얼마나 고통을 겪었을지, 지금은 또 얼마나 고통받고 있을지. 그는 소파에 누워 거의 숨도 쉬지 않는 듯하고, 온몸이 무너져내린 것만 같다. 그의 미간은 찌푸려져 있고, 얼굴은 고통으로 일그러져 있다. 가엾은 사람, 어쩌면 생각에 잠겨 있는지도 모른다. 생각에 골몰하느라 온통 주름 잡힌 그의 얼굴이 보인다. 오! 내가 조금이나마 도울 수 있다면……. 나는 할 수 있는 일을 하리라.\n\n나는 판 헬싱 박사에게 청하여, 아직 보지 못한 서류를 모두 받아두었다……. 그들이 쉬는 동안 나는 모든 것을 찬찬히 훑어볼 것이며, 어쩌면 어떤 결론에 이를 수 있을지도 모른다. 나는 교수의 본을 따라, 내 앞에 놓인 사실들을 선입견 없이 생각해보려 한다…….\n\n나는 정말이지, 하느님의 섭리 아래 내가 하나의 발견을 해냈다고 믿는다. 지도를 가져다 살펴보아야겠다…….\n\n나는 그 어느 때보다도 내가 옳다고 확신한다. 새로운 결론이 갖추어졌으니, 일행을 한데 모아 그것을 읽어주어야겠다. 그들이 판단할 수 있을 것이다. 정확을 기하는 것이 좋고, 일 분 일 분이 소중하다.\n\n「미나 하커의 비망록」\n\n(그녀의 일기에 기재됨.)\n\n탐구의 토대. — 드라큘라 백작의 과제는 제 본거지로 돌아가는 것이다.\n\n(가) 그는 누군가에 의해 옮겨져야 한다. 이것은 명백하다. 만일 그가 뜻대로 스스로 움직일 힘이 있다면 그는 사람으로든, 늑대로든, 박쥐로든, 혹은 다른 어떤 방식으로든 갈 수 있을 것이기 때문이다. 그는 분명, 새벽과 일몰 사이에 나무 상자 속에 갇혀 있는 그 무력한 상태에서, 발각되거나 방해받는 것을 두려워한다.\n\n(나) 그는 어떻게 운반되는가? — 여기서는 하나하나 배제하는 과정이 우리에게 도움이 될 수 있다. 육로로, 철로로, 아니면 물길로?\n\n1. 육로로. — 끝없는 어려움이 있으니, 특히 도시를 벗어나는 데에 그러하다.\n\n(ㄱ) 사람들이 있다. 그리고 사람들은 호기심이 많아 캐묻는다. 그 상자 안에 무엇이 들었을지에 대한 한마디 귀띔, 한 가닥 추측, 한 점 의심이 그를 파멸시킬 것이다.\n\n(ㄴ) 통과해야 할 세관원과 입시세(入市稅) 징수원이 있거나, 있을 수 있다.\n\n(ㄷ) 추격자들이 따라올지도 모른다. 이것이 그의 가장 큰 두려움이다. 그리고 자기가 발각되는 것을 막고자 그는, 할 수 있는 한, 제 희생자인 나마저 밀어냈다!\n\n2. 철로로. — 상자를 맡아볼 사람이 없다. 상자는 지체될 운에 내맡겨질 수밖에 없을 것이며, 적들이 뒤를 쫓는 마당에 지체란 치명적일 것이다. 물론 그는 밤에 빠져나갈 수도 있을 것이다. 그러나 도망쳐 들 은신처 하나 없이 낯선 곳에 버려진다면 그는 어찌 되겠는가? 이는 그가 의도하는 바가 아니며, 그는 그런 위험을 무릅쓸 생각이 없다.\n\n3. 물길로. — 이것은 한 가지 점에서는 가장 안전한 길이나, 다른 한 가지 점에서는 가장 위험이 크다. 물 위에서 그는 밤을 빼고는 무력하다. 밤이라 해도 그가 불러낼 수 있는 것은 안개와 폭풍과 눈, 그리고 그의 늑대들뿐이다. 그러나 만일 난파한다면, 살아 있는 물이 무력한 그를 집어삼킬 것이고, 그는 정녕 끝장날 것이다. 그는 배를 육지로 몰아갈 수도 있을 것이다. 그러나 만일 그곳이 그가 자유로이 움직일 수 없는 낯선 땅이라면, 그의 처지는 여전히 절망적일 것이다.\n\n우리는 기록을 통해 그가 물 위에 있었음을 안다. 그러니 우리가 해야 할 일은 그것이 어떤 물길인지를 밝혀내는 것이다.\n\n가장 먼저 할 일은 그가 지금까지 정확히 무엇을 했는지를 헤아리는 것이다. 그러면 그의 다음 과제가 무엇일지에 대해 빛을 얻을 수 있을지도 모른다.\n\n첫째. — 우리는 그가 런던에서 한 일, 곧 시간에 쫓겨 할 수 있는 한 최선으로 일을 꾸려야 했던 그의 전반적인 행동 계획의 일부였던 일을 구별해내야 한다.\n\n둘째, 우리는 우리가 아는 사실들로부터 추측할 수 있는 한, 그가 이곳에서 무엇을 했는지를 살펴야 한다.\n\n첫째 일에 관하여, 그는 분명 갈라츠에 도착할 작정이었고, 우리가 그의 영국 탈출 경로를 알아내지 못하도록 속이려고 바르나로 송장(送狀)을 보냈다. 그러니 그때 그의 당면하고 유일한 목적은 빠져나가는 것이었다. 그 증거는, 상자를 일출 전에 통관시켜 가져가라고 이마누엘 힐데스하임에게 보낸 지시서이다. 또한 페트로프 스킨스키에게 보낸 지시도 있다. 이것은 우리가 추측할 수밖에 없으나, 스킨스키가 힐데스하임을 찾아온 것을 보면 어떤 편지나 전언이 있었음에 틀림없다.\n\n지금까지 그의 계획이 성공적이었음을 우리는 안다. 차리나 카테리나 호는 놀랍도록 빠른 항해를 했는데 — 어찌나 빨랐던지 도널슨 선장의 의심을 자아낼 정도였다 — 그러나 그의 미신이 그의 빈틈없음과 어우러져 백작을 위해 판을 짜준 셈이었고, 그는 순풍을 받으며 온갖 안개를 뚫고 내달려, 눈을 가린 채 갈라츠에 닿았다. 백작의 준비가 잘 되어 있었음은 이미 입증되었다. 힐데스하임은 상자를 통관시키고, 그것을 빼내어 스킨스키에게 넘겼다. 스킨스키는 그것을 받았고 — 여기서 우리는 자취를 놓친다. 우리가 아는 것은 다만 그 상자가 물 위 어딘가에서 움직여 가고 있다는 것뿐이다. 세관과 입시세는, 만일 있었다 해도, 피해버린 것이다.\n\n이제 우리는 백작이 도착한 뒤, 갈라츠의 뭍에서 했음에 틀림없는 일에 이른다.\n\n상자는 일출 전에 스킨스키에게 넘겨졌다. 일출이 되면 백작은 제 본래의 모습으로 나타날 수 있었다. 여기서 우리는, 어째서 하필 스킨스키가 이 일을 돕도록 택해졌는가를 묻게 된다. 내 남편의 일기에서 스킨스키는, 강을 따라 항구까지 거래하는 슬로바키아인들을 상대하는 자로 언급되어 있다. 그리고 그 살인이 슬로바키아인의 짓이라던 사람들의 말은, 그 부류에 대한 일반적인 반감을 드러냈다. 백작은 고립을 원했던 것이다.\n\n내 추측은 이러하다. 런던에서 백작은 제 성으로 돌아가는 데에 물길을, 가장 안전하고 은밀한 방도로 택하기로 결정했다. 그는 성에서 스가니에 의해 옮겨졌고, 그들은 아마도 그 짐을 슬로바키아인들에게 넘겼을 것이며, 그 슬로바키아인들이 상자들을 바르나로 가져갔을 것이다. 거기서 그것들이 런던행으로 선적되었으니 말이다. 그리하여 백작은 이런 일을 주선해줄 수 있는 사람들에 대한 정보를 갖고 있었다. 상자가 뭍에 올랐을 때, 일출 전이나 일몰 후에 그는 제 상자에서 나와, 스킨스키를 만나 상자를 어느 강으로 거슬러 올려보낼 운반을 주선하도록 일러두었다. 이것이 끝나고 만사가 차질 없이 진행되고 있음을 알자, 그는 자기가 생각하기에 자취를 지우려고 제 앞잡이를 살해해버렸다.\n\n나는 지도를 살펴보고, 슬로바키아인들이 거슬러 올라갔을 법한 가장 알맞은 강이 프루트 강 아니면 세레트 강임을 알아냈다. 나는 타자 기록에서, 가수면 상태에서 내가 소들이 우는 소리와 내 귀와 같은 높이에서 물이 소용돌이치는 소리, 그리고 나무가 삐걱대는 소리를 들었다는 것을 읽었다. 그렇다면 상자 속의 백작은 지붕 없는 배를 타고 어느 강 위에 있었던 것이며 — 그 배는 아마 노나 삿대로 움직였을 것이다. 강기슭이 가깝고 물살을 거슬러 가고 있었으니 말이다. 강을 따라 떠내려가는 중이었다면 그런 소리는 나지 않았을 것이다.\n\n물론 세레트 강도 프루트 강도 아닐 수 있으나, 우리는 더 조사해볼 수 있을 것이다. 그런데 이 두 강 가운데 프루트 강이 항행하기에 더 수월하나, 세레트 강은 푼두에서, 보르고 고개를 빙 둘러 거슬러 오르는 비스트리차 강과 합류한다. 그 강이 그리는 굽이는, 물길로 닿을 수 있는 한 분명 드라큘라 성에 가장 가깝다.\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」 — 계속\n\n내가 다 읽고 나자 조너선은 나를 품에 안고 입을 맞추었다. 다른 사람들은 내 두 손을 잡고 거듭 흔들었으며, 판 헬싱 박사가 말했다.\n\n\"우리의 사랑하는 미나 부인이 다시 한번 우리의 스승이 되었습니다. 우리가 눈멀어 있던 곳을 부인의 눈은 꿰뚫어 보았어요. 이제 우리는 다시 한번 자취를 좇게 되었고, 이번에는 성공할지도 모릅니다. 우리의 적은 가장 무력한 상태에 있습니다. 만일 우리가 낮에, 물 위에서 그를 덮칠 수 있다면, 우리 일은 끝납니다. 그는 앞서 출발했으나, 서두를 힘이 없습니다. 자기를 나르는 자들이 의심할까 봐 제 상자를 떠날 수 없으니 말입니다. 그들이 의심하게 되면, 그것은 곧 그들로 하여금 그를 강물에 던지게 만드는 셈이고, 그러면 그는 죽고 말 테니까요. 이를 그가 알고 있으니, 그리하지 않을 겁니다. 자, 여러분, 우리의 작전 회의로 들어갑시다. 바로 지금 여기서, 우리 각자가, 그리고 다 함께 무엇을 할지 계획을 세워야 합니다.\"\n\n\"나는 증기선을 한 척 구해 그를 뒤쫓겠소,\" 고달밍 경이 말했다.\n\n\"그리고 나는, 혹시라도 그가 뭍에 오를 경우에 대비해 강기슭을 따라 뒤쫓을 말을 구하겠소,\" 모리스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"좋소!\" 교수가 말했다. \"둘 다 좋습니다. 하지만 어느 쪽도 혼자 가서는 안 됩니다. 필요하다면 힘을 힘으로 제압할 병력이 있어야 합니다. 슬로바키아인들은 억세고 거칠며, 조잡한 무기를 지니고 다니니까요.\" 사내들이 모두 빙긋 웃었으니, 그들끼리 작은 무기고 하나를 지니고 다닌 까닭이었다. 모리스 씨가 말했다.\n\n\"윈체스터 소총을 몇 자루 가져왔습니다. 무리를 상대할 때 꽤 요긴하고, 늑대가 나올지도 모르니까요. 백작은, 기억하시겠지만, 또 다른 대비책도 마련했습니다. 하커 부인이 미처 다 듣거나 알아듣지 못한, 다른 자들에게 보낸 요청도 있었지요. 우리는 모든 점에서 만반의 준비를 갖춰야 합니다.\" 수어드 박사가 말했다.\n\n\"내 생각엔 내가 퀸시와 함께 가는 게 낫겠소. 우리는 함께 사냥하는 데 이골이 났으니, 우리 둘이 잘 무장하면 무엇이 닥치든 능히 맞설 수 있을 거요. 자네는 혼자 있어선 안 되네, 아트. 슬로바키아인들과 싸워야 할지도 모르고, 어쩌다 한 번 잘못 찔리기라도 하면 — 이자들이 총을 지니고 다니지는 않을 듯하니 말이오만 — 우리 계획이 죄다 어그러질 거요. 이번에는 요행에 맡길 일이 없어야 합니다. 백작의 머리와 몸뚱이가 갈라지고, 그가 다시 환생할 수 없음을 확신하기 전에는 우리는 쉬지 않을 거요.\" 그는 이렇게 말하며 조너선을 바라보았고, 조너선은 나를 바라보았다. 나는 가엾은 그이의 마음이 갈가리 찢기는 것을 알 수 있었다. 물론 그는 나와 함께 있고 싶어했다. 그러나 한편으로, 그 배 쪽 일이야말로 십중팔구 그…… 그…… 그…… 흡혈귀를 파멸시키는 일일 터였다. (어찌하여 나는 그 말을 쓰기를 망설였을까?) 그는 한동안 말이 없었고, 그가 침묵하는 동안 판 헬싱 박사가 말했다.\n\n\"조너선 군, 이건 두 가지 이유에서 자네 몫이라네. 첫째는, 자네가 젊고 용감하며 싸울 수 있고, 마지막 순간에는 온갖 기력이 다 필요할지 모르기 때문이지. 또 하나는, 자네와 자네 식구들에게 그토록 큰 화를 끼친 그것을 멸할 권리가 바로 자네에게 있기 때문일세. 미나 부인에 대해서는 걱정 말게. 허락된다면 그분은 내가 보살피겠네. 나는 늙었네. 내 다리는 예전만큼 빨리 달리지 못하고, 그리 오래 말을 타거나 필요한 만큼 추격하거나 살상 무기로 싸우는 데에도 익숙지 않다네. 그러나 나는 다른 쓸모가 있을 수 있어. 나는 다른 방식으로 싸울 수 있네. 그리고 필요하다면, 젊은이들 못지않게 죽을 수도 있고. 자, 이제 내 뜻을 말하겠네. 고달밍 경과 조너선 군 자네가 그 빠른 작은 증기선을 타고 강을 거슬러 올라가고, 한편으로 존과 퀸시가 그가 혹 뭍에 오를지도 모를 강기슭을 지키는 동안, 나는 미나 부인을 데리고 곧장 적의 나라 한복판으로 들어가겠네. 그 늙은 여우가 흐르는 물 위에 떠서, 뭍으로는 빠져나갈 수 없는 제 상자에 묶여 있는 동안 — 슬로바키아인 운반꾼들이 겁먹고 그를 버려 죽게 내버려둘까 봐 감히 제 관 상자의 뚜껑조차 들어올리지 못하는 동안 — 우리는 조너선이 갔던 길을 따라가겠네. 비스트리츠에서 보르고를 넘어 드라큘라 성으로 가는 길을 찾아갈 걸세. 그곳에서는 미나 부인의 최면 능력이 분명 도움이 될 테고, 우리가 그 운명의 장소에 가까워진 뒤 첫 일출이 지나면 — 그렇지 않고서는 온통 어둡고 알 수 없을 — 길을 찾아낼 걸세. 할 일이 많고, 또 거룩하게 만들어야 할 다른 장소들도 있으니, 그 독사 떼의 소굴을 깨끗이 쓸어버려야 한다네.\" 여기서 조너선이 발끈하여 그의 말을 가로막았다.\n\n\"판 헬싱 교수님, 그 가엾은 처지에다 그 악마의 병독에 물든 미나를, 바로 그자가 놓은 죽음의 덫 아가리 속으로 데려가시겠다는 말씀입니까? 천만에요! 천국을 준대도, 지옥을 준대도 안 됩니다!\" 그는 잠시 거의 말을 잇지 못하다가, 다시 말했다.\n\n\"그곳이 어떤 곳인지 아십니까? 바로 그 달빛마저 소름 끼치는 형상들로 살아 꿈틀거리고, 바람에 휘도는 티끌 한 점 한 점이 막 잉태된 게걸스러운 괴물인 — 그 지옥처럼 추악한 끔찍한 소굴을 보신 적이 있습니까? 흡혈귀의 입술이 교수님 목에 닿는 것을 느껴보신 적이 있습니까?\" 여기서 그는 나를 향해 돌아섰고, 그의 눈길이 내 이마에 닿자 그는 외마디 소리를 지르며 두 팔을 치켜올렸다. \"오, 하느님, 우리가 무슨 짓을 했기에 이런 공포가 우리에게 닥쳤단 말입니까!\" 그러고는 비참함에 무너져 소파에 주저앉았다. 교수가 맑고 부드러운 어조로 말하자, 그 목소리가 허공에 울리는 듯하며 우리 모두를 진정시켰다.\n\n\"오, 조너선 군, 내가 가려는 것은 바로 미나 부인을 그 끔찍한 곳에서 구하기 위함일세. 내가 그분을 그곳으로 데려간다니, 하느님 맙소사, 결코 안 될 일이지. 거기서는 해야 할 일이 — 거친 일이 — 있는데, 그것은 그분의 눈에 보여서는 안 되는 것일세. 여기 있는 우리 사내들은, 조너선 군만 빼고, 그곳이 정화되기 전에 무슨 일이 행해져야 하는지를 제 눈으로 똑똑히 보았네. 우리가 무서운 궁지에 처해 있음을 잊지 말게. 만일 백작이 이번에 우리에게서 빠져나간다면 — 그는 강하고 교묘하며 교활하니 — 한 세기 동안 잠들어 있기를 택할지도 모르고, 그러면 때가 되어 우리의 사랑하는 분이\" — 그는 내 손을 잡았다 — \"그에게 동무가 되어주러 가게 될 것이며, 조너선 군 자네가 본 그 다른 여인들과 같이 될 걸세. 자네는 그들의 게걸스러운 입술에 대해 우리에게 이야기했지. 백작이 그들에게 던져준 꿈틀거리는 자루를 움켜쥐며 내던 상스러운 웃음소리를 자네는 들었고. 자네가 몸서리치는 것도 무리가 아닐세. 자네에게 이토록 큰 고통을 주는 것을 용서하게나. 그러나 그것은 필요한 일이라네. 벗이여, 내가 어쩌면 내 목숨을 내놓으려는 그 일이, 절박한 필요에서 나온 것이 아니겠는가? 만일 누군가 그곳에 들어가 머물러야 한다면, 그들에게 동무가 되어주러 가야 할 사람은 바로 나일세.\"\n\n\"뜻대로 하십시오,\" 조너선이 온몸을 떨게 하는 흐느낌과 함께 말했다. \"우리는 하느님의 손안에 있습니다!\"\n\n이후. — 오, 이 용감한 사내들이 일하는 모습을 보니 내 마음이 든든해졌다. 사내들이 이토록 진지하고, 이토록 참되며, 이토록 용감할 때 여인이 어찌 그들을 사랑하지 않을 수 있으랴! 그리고 또한, 그것은 돈의 놀라운 힘을 새삼 생각하게 했다! 제대로 쓰일 때 돈이 못할 일이 무엇이며, 비열하게 쓰일 때는 또 무슨 일을 저지를 수 있으랴. 나는 고달밍 경이 부유하고, 그와, 역시 돈이 넉넉한 모리스 씨가 둘 다 그 돈을 이토록 아낌없이 쓰려 한다는 것이 너무도 고마웠다. 만일 그들이 그러지 않았더라면, 우리의 이 작은 원정대는 한 시간 안에 그럴 수 있듯 이토록 신속하게도, 이토록 잘 갖추어진 채로도 출발하지 못했을 테니 말이다. 우리 각자가 어떤 역할을 맡을지 정해진 지 채 세 시간도 되지 않았는데, 이제 고달밍 경과 조너선은 증기를 올려 언제라도 출발할 채비를 갖춘 멋진 증기선 한 척을 갖추었다. 수어드 박사와 모리스 씨는 잘 갖추어진 좋은 말 여섯 필을 마련했다. 우리는 구할 수 있는 온갖 종류의 지도와 도구를 다 갖추었다. 판 헬싱 교수와 나는 오늘 밤 11시 40분 기차로 베레슈티로 떠나, 거기서 마차를 구해 보르고 고개로 갈 것이다. 마차와 말을 사야 하므로 우리는 적잖은 현금을 챙겨가고 있다. 이 일에 관해 믿고 맡길 사람이 없으니 우리가 직접 몰 것이다. 교수는 매우 여러 나라 말을 어느 정도 아니, 우리는 그럭저럭 해나갈 것이다. 우리는 모두 무기를 지녔는데, 나에게까지도 대구경 권총 한 자루가 주어졌다. 조너선은 내가 나머지 사람들처럼 무장하지 않으면 마음이 놓이지 않을 것이다. 아아! 나는 나머지 사람들이 지닌 한 가지 무기만은 지닐 수가 없다. 내 이마의 흉터가 그것을 금하기 때문이다. 사랑하는 판 헬싱 박사는, 늑대가 나올지 모르니 나도 충분히 무장한 셈이라고 일러주며 나를 위로한다. 날씨는 시시각각 더 추워지고 있고, 경고하듯 왔다가 사라지는 눈보라가 인다.\n\n이후. — 사랑하는 그이에게 작별을 고하는 데에 내 온 용기를 다 짜내야 했다. 우리는 다시는 만나지 못할지도 모른다. 용기를 내, 미나! 교수가 너를 날카롭게 바라보고 있어. 그의 눈길은 하나의 경고야. 이제 눈물을 보여서는 안 돼 — 하느님께서 기쁨의 눈물을 흘리도록 허락하시는 게 아니라면.\n\n「조너선 하커의 일기」\n\n10월 30일. 밤. — 나는 증기선 화실(火室) 문에서 새어나오는 불빛에 의지해 이 글을 쓴다. 고달밍 경이 불을 때고 있다. 그는 이 일에 노련한 솜씨를 지녔는데, 여러 해 동안 템스 강에 제 증기선 한 척을, 또 노퍽 브로즈에 다른 한 척을 두고 부려온 까닭이다. 우리 계획에 관해서는, 결국 미나의 추측이 옳았다는 것, 그리고 만일 백작이 제 성으로 돌아가는 탈출로로 어떤 물길을 택했다면 그것은 세레트 강, 그리고 그 합류 지점에서 비스트리차 강이리라는 것으로 결론을 지었다. 우리는 강과 카르파티아 산맥 사이의 땅을 가로지를 지점으로, 북위 47도 언저리쯤이 택해졌으리라 보았다. 밤에 강을 거슬러 빠른 속도로 달리는 데에 우리는 아무 두려움이 없다. 물이 넉넉하고, 양쪽 기슭이 충분히 멀리 떨어져 있어 어둠 속에서도 증기 항행이 그리 어렵지 않을 만하기 때문이다. 고달밍 경은 나에게 잠시 자두라고 한다. 당장은 한 사람만 망을 봐도 족하다는 것이다. 그러나 나는 잠들 수가 없다 — 끔찍한 위험이 내 사랑하는 사람 위에 드리워 있고, 그녀가 그 무서운 곳으로 들어가는 마당에 내가 어찌 잠들 수 있으랴……. 나의 유일한 위안은 우리가 하느님의 손안에 있다는 것이다. 그 믿음이 아니라면, 사는 것보다 죽는 편이, 그리하여 이 모든 고난에서 벗어나는 편이 더 쉬울 것이다. 모리스 씨와 수어드 박사는 우리가 출발하기 전에 그들의 긴 여정에 올랐다. 그들은 오른쪽 기슭을 따라가되, 강의 넓은 줄기를 한눈에 볼 수 있고 강굽이를 일일이 따라가는 것을 피할 수 있는 더 높은 땅에 오를 만큼 충분히 떨어져 가기로 했다. 그들은, 처음 몇 구간은, 여분의 말들을 몰고 갈 두 사람을 데리고 가는데 — 모두 네 필이니, 사람들의 호기심을 자극하지 않기 위해서다. 그들이 그 사람들을 돌려보내고 나면 — 곧 그리할 것이다 — 말들은 그들 자신이 돌볼 것이다. 우리가 합세해야 할 필요가 생길지도 모르는데, 그렇게 되면 그들이 우리 일행 전체를 말에 태울 수 있다. 안장 하나는 떼었다 붙였다 할 수 있는 뿔이 달려 있어, 필요하면 미나에게 알맞게 쉬이 맞출 수 있다.\n\n우리는 거친 모험에 나서 있다. 이렇게, 어둠을 뚫고 내달리며, 강에서 솟아올라 우리를 후려치는 듯한 한기 속에서, 우리를 에워싼 밤의 온갖 신비로운 목소리들 가운데서, 그 모든 것이 사무친다. 우리는 알 수 없는 곳, 알 수 없는 길로 떠밀려가는 것만 같다. 어둡고 무시무시한 것들로 가득한 온 세계 속으로 말이다. 고달밍이 화실 문을 닫고 있다…….\n\n10월 31일. — 여전히 내달리는 중이다. 날이 밝았고, 고달밍은 자고 있다. 내가 망을 본다. 아침은 살을 에듯 춥다. 두꺼운 모피 외투를 입었는데도 화실의 열기가 고맙다. 지금까지 우리는 지붕 없는 배 몇 척밖에 지나치지 못했는데, 그중 어느 것에도 우리가 찾는 것만 한 크기의 상자나 꾸러미는 실려 있지 않았다. 사람들은 우리가 그들에게 전기등을 비출 때마다 겁에 질려, 무릎을 꿇고 기도를 올렸다.\n\n11월 1일, 저녁. — 온종일 아무 소식이 없다. 우리가 찾는 그런 것을 도무지 찾지 못했다. 우리는 이제 비스트리차 강으로 접어들었다. 만일 우리 추측이 틀렸다면 우리의 기회는 사라진 것이다. 우리는 크든 작든 모든 배를 샅샅이 뒤졌다. 오늘 이른 아침, 한 배의 선원들이 우리를 관선(官船)으로 여겨 그에 맞게 대접했다. 우리는 여기서 일을 매끄럽게 풀 방도를 보았으므로, 비스트리차 강이 세레트 강으로 흘러드는 푼두에서 루마니아 국기 하나를 구해 지금은 그것을 눈에 띄게 내걸고 다닌다. 그 뒤로 우리가 샅샅이 뒤진 모든 배에서 이 수법은 먹혀들었다. 사람들은 우리에게 온갖 깍듯한 예의를 차렸고, 우리가 무엇을 묻거나 무엇을 하기로 하든 단 한 번도 이의를 달지 않았다. 슬로바키아인 몇몇이 우리에게 일러주기를, 큰 배 한 척이 평소보다 빠른 속도로 그들 곁을 지나갔는데 그것은 갑판에 두 배의 선원을 태우고 있었기 때문이라고 했다. 이는 그들이 푼두에 닿기 전의 일이라, 그 배가 비스트리차 강으로 접어들었는지 아니면 세레트 강을 따라 계속 올라갔는지는 일러주지 못했다. 푼두에서는 그런 배에 대한 말을 도무지 들을 수 없었으니, 그 배는 밤중에 그곳을 지나친 게 분명하다. 나는 몹시 졸리다. 추위가 어쩌면 내게 영향을 미치기 시작한 모양이고, 사람의 몸이란 언젠가는 쉬어야 하는 법이다. 고달밍이 자기가 첫 망을 보겠다고 우긴다. 가엾고 사랑하는 미나와 나에게 베푼 그 모든 선의에 대해, 하느님께서 그를 축복하시기를.\n\n11월 2일, 아침. — 환한 대낮이다. 그 좋은 친구는 나를 깨우려 하지 않았다. 나를 깨우는 건 죄였으리라는 것이다. 내가 평온하게 자며 시름을 잊고 있었으니 말이다. 이토록 오래 자며 그가 밤새 망보게 한 것이 내게는 잔인하리만치 이기적으로 여겨지나, 그가 옳았다. 오늘 아침 나는 새사람이 되었다. 이렇게 여기 앉아 자는 그를 바라보며, 나는 기관을 돌보는 일도, 키를 잡는 일도, 망을 보는 일도 필요한 만큼 다 해낼 수 있다. 내 기운과 기력이 돌아오고 있음이 느껴진다. 지금 미나는, 그리고 판 헬싱은 어디쯤 있을까. 그들은 수요일 정오쯤 베레슈티에 닿았을 것이다. 마차와 말을 구하는 데 시간이 좀 걸렸을 테니, 만일 그들이 출발하여 부지런히 달렸다면 지금쯤 보르고 고개 언저리에 있을 것이다. 하느님께서 그들을 인도하고 도우시기를! 무슨 일이 벌어질지 생각하기조차 두렵다. 우리가 좀 더 빨리 갈 수만 있다면! 그러나 그럴 수가 없다. 기관은 고동치며 온 힘을 다하고 있다. 수어드 박사와 모리스 씨는 어찌 되어가고 있을까. 산에서 이 강으로 흘러내리는 물줄기가 끝없이 많은 듯하나, 그 가운데 그리 큰 것은 하나도 없으니 — 적어도 지금으로서는 그러하나, 겨울철과 눈이 녹을 때엔 의심할 바 없이 무서울 테지만 — 말 탄 두 사람이 그리 큰 장애를 만나지는 않았을 것이다. 슈트라스바에 닿기 전에 그들을 볼 수 있기를 바란다. 그때까지 우리가 백작을 따라잡지 못했다면, 다음에 어찌할지를 함께 의논할 필요가 생길지도 모르니 말이다.\n\n「수어드 박사의 일기」\n\n11월 2일. — 길에 오른 지 사흘째다. 아무 소식 없고, 설령 있었더라도 그것을 쓸 겨를이 없으니, 매 순간이 소중하기 때문이다. 우리는 말들에게 필요한 만큼의 휴식밖에 취하지 못했으나, 우리 둘은 놀랍도록 잘 견뎌내고 있다. 그 모험으로 가득했던 우리의 지난날이 쓸모를 드러내고 있다. 우리는 밀어붙여야 한다. 다시 증기선을 시야에 넣기 전에는 결코 마음이 편치 못할 것이다.\n\n11월 3일. — 우리는 푼두에서 증기선이 비스트리차 강을 거슬러 올라갔다는 말을 들었다. 이토록 춥지만 않다면 좋으련만. 눈이 올 조짐이 보인다. 만일 눈이 많이 쏟아지면 그것이 우리를 멈춰 세울 것이다. 그런 경우 우리는 썰매를 구해 러시아식으로 계속 나아가야 한다.\n\n11월 4일. — 오늘 우리는 증기선이 급류를 거슬러 오르려다 사고로 발이 묶였다는 말을 들었다. 슬로바키아인들의 배는, 밧줄의 도움과 노련한 키잡이 솜씨로 별 탈 없이 거슬러 오른다. 몇 척은 겨우 몇 시간 전에 거슬러 올라갔다. 고달밍 자신이 아마추어 정비공인지라, 증기선을 다시 손본 것은 분명 그였다. 마침내 그들은 현지인의 도움을 받아 무사히 급류를 거슬러 올랐고, 다시 추격에 나섰다. 그 사고로 배 상태가 조금도 나아지지 않은 게 아닐까 두렵다. 농부들이 우리에게 일러주기를, 배가 다시 잔잔한 물에 들어선 뒤에도 시야에 있는 내내 이따금 자꾸만 멈춰 서곤 했다는 것이다. 우리는 그 어느 때보다 세차게 밀어붙여야 한다. 우리의 도움이 머지않아 필요해질지도 모른다.\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」\n\n10월 31일. — 정오에 베레슈티에 도착했다. 교수가 나에게 일러주기를, 오늘 아침 동틀 무렵 그는 나를 거의 최면에 들게 할 수 없었으며, 내가 한 말이라곤 \"어둡고 고요해요\"뿐이었다고 한다. 그는 지금 마차와 말을 사러 나가 있다. 그는 나중에 말을 더 사보겠다고 하는데, 가는 길에 말을 갈아탈 수 있게 하려는 것이다. 우리 앞에는 70마일이 좀 넘는 길이 놓여 있다. 이 고장은 아름답고 더없이 흥미롭다. 만일 우리가 다른 처지에 있었더라면, 이 모든 것을 둘러보는 일이 얼마나 즐거웠을까. 만일 조너선과 내가 단둘이 이곳을 마차로 지나간다면 그 얼마나 큰 기쁨이었으랴. 멈춰 서서 사람들을 보고, 그들의 삶을 조금이나마 알고, 이 거칠고도 아름다운 온 고장과 예스러운 사람들의 온갖 빛깔과 그림 같은 정취로 우리의 마음과 기억을 채울 수 있다면! 그러나, 아아!—\n\n이후. — 판 헬싱 박사가 돌아왔다. 그는 마차와 말을 구했다. 우리는 저녁을 좀 들고, 한 시간 뒤에 출발하기로 했다. 여관 안주인이 우리를 위해 어마어마하게 큰 식료품 바구니를 꾸려주고 있는데, 한 무리의 병사들에게도 충분할 만큼으로 보인다. 교수는 그녀를 부추기면서, 다시 변변한 음식을 얻기까지 어쩌면 한 주가 걸릴지도 모른다고 나에게 속삭인다. 그는 장도 봐 와서, 모피 외투며 덮을 것이며 온갖 따뜻한 것을 놀랄 만큼 잔뜩 집으로 보내왔다. 우리가 추위에 떨 일은 없을 것이다.\n\n우리는 곧 출발한다. 우리에게 무슨 일이 벌어질지 생각하기조차 두렵다. 우리는 참으로 하느님의 손안에 있다. 무슨 일이 있을지는 오직 그분만이 아시며, 나는 슬프고 보잘것없는 내 영혼의 온 힘을 다해 그분께 기도한다. 부디 내 사랑하는 남편을 지켜주시기를. 무슨 일이 벌어지든, 조너선이 내가 그를 이루 말할 수 없이 사랑하고 존경했음을 알기를, 그리고 내 마지막의 가장 참된 생각이 언제나 그를 향하리라는 것을.\n\n# MINA HARKER’S JOURNAL\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」\n11월 1일. — 온종일 우리는 빠른 속도로 달렸다. 말들도 자신들이 친절히 다루어지고 있음을 아는 듯, 한 구간을 기꺼이 최고 속도로 달려주었다. 이제 말을 워낙 여러 번 바꾸었고 언제나 똑같은 일이 거듭되니, 우리는 이 여정이 수월하리라고 기대하게 되었다. 판 헬싱 박사는 말이 적다. 농부들에게는 자기가 비스트리차로 서둘러 가는 길이라고만 말하고, 말을 바꾸는 값을 후하게 치른다. 우리는 뜨거운 수프나 커피, 차를 마시고는 곧 다시 떠난다. 아름다운 고장이다. 상상할 수 있는 온갖 종류의 아름다움으로 가득하고, 사람들은 용감하고 강인하며 소박하고, 고운 품성으로 넘쳐 보인다. 그들은 정말이지 무척이나 미신을 믿는다. 우리가 처음 머문 집에서, 시중을 들던 여인이 내 이마의 흉터를 보더니 성호를 긋고는 흉안을 막으려고 나에게 손가락 두 개를 내밀었다. 그들은 일부러 음식에 마늘을 잔뜩 넣은 것 같은데, 나는 마늘을 견디지 못한다. 그 뒤로 나는 모자와 베일을 벗지 않도록 조심했고, 그래서 그들의 의심을 피할 수 있었다. 우리는 빠르게 달리고 있고, 소문을 퍼뜨릴 마부가 함께 있지 않으니 추문보다 앞서 나아간다. 그러나 흉안에 대한 두려움만은 가는 내내 우리 바로 뒤를 바짝 따라올 것이다. 교수님은 지칠 줄 모르는 듯하다. 나에게는 오랫동안 잠을 자게 하면서도 정작 자신은 온종일 조금도 쉬지 않으려 했다. 해질 무렵 그가 나에게 최면을 걸었는데, 내가 늘 하던 대로 \"어둠, 찰랑이는 물, 삐걱거리는 나무\"라고 대답했다고 한다. 그러니 우리의 적은 아직 강 위에 있다. 조너선을 생각하기가 두렵지만, 어쩐 일인지 이제는 그에 대해서도, 나 자신에 대해서도 두렵지 않다. 나는 말이 준비되기를 어느 농가에서 기다리며 이 글을 쓰고 있다. 판 헬싱 박사는 잠들어 있다. 가엾은 분, 무척 지치고 늙고 잿빛으로 보이지만, 그 입매만은 정복자처럼 굳게 다물려 있다. 잠결에도 결의가 배어 있다. 잘 출발하고 나면 내가 마차를 몰며 그를 쉬게 해야겠다. 우리에게는 여러 날이 남아 있으니, 무엇보다 그의 힘이 가장 필요할 때 쓰러져서는 안 된다고 일러두어야겠다…… 모든 준비가 끝났다. 우리는 곧 떠난다.\n\n11월 2일, 아침. — 나는 성공했고, 우리는 밤새 번갈아 마차를 몰았다. 이제 날이 밝았는데, 환하지만 춥다. 공기 속에 묘한 무거움이 감돈다. 더 나은 말이 없어 무거움이라고 하지만, 내 말은 그것이 우리 둘 다를 짓누른다는 뜻이다. 무척 춥고, 따뜻한 모피만이 우리를 견딜 만하게 해준다. 동틀 녘에 판 헬싱이 나에게 최면을 걸었다. 내가 \"어둠, 삐걱거리는 나무, 으르렁거리는 물\"이라고 대답했다 하니, 그들이 거슬러 올라감에 따라 강이 변하고 있는 것이다. 부디 내 사랑하는 이가 필요 이상의 위험을 무릅쓰지 않기를 빈다. 그러나 우리는 신의 손안에 있다.\n\n11월 2일, 밤. — 온종일 마차를 몰았다. 갈수록 고장은 더 거칠어지고, 베레슈티에서는 그토록 멀고 지평선 위로 그토록 낮게 보이던 카르파티아의 거대한 산줄기들이 이제는 우리를 에워싸며 앞쪽으로 우뚝 솟아오르는 듯하다. 우리는 둘 다 기운차 보인다. 서로를 북돋우려 애쓰다 보니, 그렇게 하는 가운데 스스로도 힘을 얻는 듯하다. 판 헬싱 박사는 아침이면 보르고 고개에 닿으리라고 한다. 이제 이곳에는 집이 거의 없어서, 교수님은 더는 말을 바꾸지 못할지 모르니 마지막으로 얻은 말로 끝까지 가야 할 것이라고 한다. 그는 우리가 바꾼 두 마리에 더해 두 마리를 더 얻어두어서, 이제 우리에게는 어설프나마 네 마리 한 마차가 생겼다. 어여쁜 말들은 참을성 있고 순해서 우리를 성가시게 하지 않는다. 다른 여행자들에게 시달릴 일도 없으니, 나조차도 마차를 몰 수 있다. 우리는 날이 밝을 때 고개에 닿을 것이다. 그 전에 도착하고 싶지는 않다. 그래서 우리는 서두르지 않고, 번갈아 한참씩 쉰다. 아, 내일은 우리에게 무엇을 가져다줄까? 우리는 내 가엾은 사랑하는 이가 그토록 고통받았던 곳을 찾아간다. 부디 신께서 우리를 올바로 인도해주시고, 우리 둘 모두에게 소중한, 이토록 무서운 위험에 처한 이들과 내 남편을 굽어살펴주시기를. 나로 말하면, 나는 그분 보시기에 합당하지 못하다. 아아! 나는 그분 눈에 부정한 존재이며, 그분께서 나를 그분의 진노를 사지 않은 자들 가운데 하나로 그분 앞에 서게 해주시기 전까지는 그러할 것이다.\n\n「에이브러햄 판 헬싱의 비망록」\n\n11월 4일. — 이것은 혹여 그를 다시 보지 못할 경우를 대비하여, 런던 퍼플리트의 의학박사 존 슈어드, 나의 오랜 참벗에게 남기는 글이다. 이것이 사정을 밝혀줄지도 모른다. 지금은 아침이고, 나는 밤새도록 꺼뜨리지 않고 지켜온 불가에서 이 글을 쓴다. 미나 부인이 나를 도왔다. 춥다, 춥다. 어찌나 추운지 잿빛의 무거운 하늘이 눈을 잔뜩 머금고 있어, 그것이 내리면 땅이 그것을 받아들이려 단단히 굳어가고 있으니 겨우내 그대로 쌓여 있을 것이다. 그것이 미나 부인에게 영향을 미친 듯하다. 그녀는 온종일 머리가 무거워 평소의 그녀답지 않았다. 그녀는 자고, 자고, 또 잔다! 평소 그토록 기민하던 사람이 온종일 말 그대로 아무것도 하지 않았다. 입맛마저 잃었다. 틈날 때마다 그토록 충실히 글을 쓰던 그녀가 자신의 작은 일기에 아무것도 적지 않았다. 무언가가 나에게 모든 것이 온전치 않다고 속삭인다. 그래도 오늘 밤 그녀는 한결 생기가 돈다. 온종일의 긴 잠이 그녀를 새롭게 하고 되살린 듯, 이제 그녀는 여느 때처럼 더없이 다정하고 환하다. 해질 무렵 나는 그녀에게 최면을 걸어보려 했으나, 아아! 아무 효과가 없었다. 그 힘은 날마다 점점 약해지더니, 오늘 밤에는 아예 나를 저버렸다. 그래, 신의 뜻이 이루어지기를. 그것이 무엇이든, 그것이 어디로 이끌든!\n\n이제 그간의 기록으로 넘어가자. 미나 부인이 자신의 속기로 적지 못하니, 내가 나의 거추장스러운 옛 방식으로나마 적어야겠다. 우리의 하루하루가 기록되지 않은 채 지나가지 않도록.\n\n우리는 어제 아침 해가 뜬 직후 보르고 고개에 닿았다. 새벽의 기미가 보이자 나는 최면을 걸 채비를 했다. 우리는 마차를 세우고, 방해받지 않도록 내려섰다. 나는 모피로 잠자리를 만들었고, 미나 부인은 누워서 여느 때처럼, 그러나 어느 때보다 더디고 짧게 최면 잠에 몸을 맡겼다. 전과 마찬가지로 대답이 돌아왔다. \"어둠, 그리고 소용돌이치는 물.\" 그러고 나서 그녀는 환하고 빛나는 모습으로 깨어났고, 우리는 길을 이어 곧 고개에 닿았다. 이때 이곳에서 그녀는 온통 열의로 불타올랐다. 어떤 새로운 인도의 힘이 그녀 안에서 드러난 듯, 그녀는 한 길을 가리키며 말했다.\n\n\"이쪽이에요.\"\n\n\"어떻게 아시오?\" 내가 물었다.\n\n\"당연히 알지요.\" 그녀가 대답하고, 잠시 멈추었다가 덧붙였다. \"내 조너선이 이 길을 지나가며 그 여정을 적지 않았던가요?\"\n\n처음에는 다소 이상하게 여겼으나, 곧 나는 그런 샛길이 하나뿐임을 알았다. 그 길은 거의 쓰이지 않으며, 더 넓고 단단하고 더 많이 쓰이는 부코비나에서 비스트리차로 가는 역마찻길과는 사뭇 달랐다.\n\n그리하여 우리는 이 길을 따라 내려갔다. 다른 길들을 만나면 — 그것들이 길인지조차 늘 확신할 수는 없었으니, 버려진 데다 옅은 눈이 내려 있었기 때문이다 — 말들만이 알았다. 나는 고삐를 늦추어 그들에게 맡겼고, 그들은 그토록 참을성 있게 나아갔다. 이윽고 우리는 조너선이 그 놀라운 일기에 적어둔 온갖 것들을 찾아냈다. 그러고 나서 우리는 길고 긴 여러 시간을 계속 나아갔다. 처음에 나는 미나 부인에게 자라고 일렀고, 그녀는 애써 보더니 성공했다. 그녀는 내내 잤다. 마침내 나는 의심이 일어 그녀를 깨워보려 했다. 그러나 그녀는 계속 잤고, 아무리 해도 나는 그녀를 깨울 수 없었다. 그녀를 해칠까 두려워 너무 세게 시도하고 싶지는 않았다. 그녀가 많은 고통을 겪었음을, 그리고 때로는 잠이 그녀에게 더없이 소중함을 알기 때문이다. 나 자신도 깜빡 졸았던 모양이다. 갑자기 무언가 잘못을 저지른 듯한 죄책감이 들었고, 정신을 차려 보니 나는 손에 고삐를 쥔 채 똑바로 앉아 있었고, 착한 말들은 여느 때처럼 터덜터덜 나아가고 있었다. 내려다보니 미나 부인은 여전히 잠들어 있었다. 어느덧 해질 무렵이 멀지 않았는데, 눈 위로 햇빛이 커다란 노란 물결처럼 흘러서, 산이 그토록 가파르게 솟은 곳에 우리가 길고 큰 그림자를 드리웠다. 우리는 올라가고, 또 올라가고 있었으며, 모든 것이 아, 어찌나 거칠고 바위투성이인지, 마치 세상의 끝인 것만 같았다.\n\n그러고서 나는 미나 부인을 깨웠다. 이번에는 그녀가 별 어려움 없이 깨어났고, 나는 그녀를 최면 잠에 들게 하려 했다. 그러나 그녀는, 마치 내가 거기 없는 듯, 잠들지 않았다. 그래도 나는 시도하고 또 시도하다가, 문득 나 자신과 그녀가 어둠 속에 있음을 알았다. 둘러보니 해가 이미 진 것이었다. 미나 부인이 웃었고, 나는 돌아서서 그녀를 바라보았다. 그녀는 이제 완전히 깨어 있었으며, 우리가 처음 백작의 집에 들어섰던 그 카팩스의 밤 이래로 내가 본 적 없을 만큼 좋아 보였다. 나는 놀랐고 그래서 마음이 편치 않았다. 그러나 그녀가 나를 향해 그토록 환하고 다정하고 살뜰하니, 나는 모든 두려움을 잊었다. 나는 불을 피웠다. 우리는 땔감을 챙겨 왔던 것이다. 내가 말들을 풀어 가린 곳에 매어 먹이는 동안 그녀는 음식을 차렸다. 불가로 돌아와 보니 그녀가 내 저녁상을 차려두었다. 나는 그녀를 도우려 했으나, 그녀는 미소 지으며 자기는 이미 먹었다고, 너무 배가 고파서 기다릴 수 없었다고 했다. 나는 그것이 마음에 들지 않았고 심각한 의심이 일었다. 그러나 그녀를 놀라게 할까 두려워 그 일에 대해 입을 다물었다. 그녀가 거들어주고 나는 혼자 먹었다. 그러고서 우리는 모피를 두르고 불가에 누웠고, 나는 내가 지킬 테니 자라고 그녀에게 일렀다. 그러나 곧 나는 망보는 일을 까맣게 잊었다. 그러다 문득 내가 지켜야 한다는 것이 떠올라 보니, 그녀는 가만히, 그러나 깨어 누운 채 그토록 빛나는 눈으로 나를 바라보고 있었다. 한 번, 두 번 같은 일이 거듭되었고, 나는 아침이 되기 전까지 꽤 많이 잤다. 깨어나 나는 그녀에게 최면을 걸어보려 했으나, 아아! 그녀는 순순히 눈을 감았으되 잠들지 못했다. 해가 떠오르고, 떠오르고, 또 떠올랐다. 그러고 나서야 그녀에게 잠이 찾아왔으나, 너무 늦었고, 또 너무 깊어 깨어나지 않았다. 나는 말들에 마구를 채우고 모든 채비를 마친 뒤, 그녀를 들어 올려 잠든 채로 마차에 눕혀야 했다. 부인은 여전히 잠들어 있었고, 잠결에 전보다 더 건강하고 더 혈색이 좋아 보였다. 그리고 나는 그것이 마음에 들지 않았다. 그리고 나는 두렵다, 두렵다, 두렵다! 나는 온갖 것이 두렵다. 생각하는 것조차 두렵지만, 나는 내 길을 가야만 한다. 우리가 건 판돈은 삶과 죽음, 아니 그 이상의 것이니, 우리는 물러서서는 안 된다.\n\n11월 5일, 아침. — 모든 것을 정확히 적어두자. 그대와 내가 함께 기이한 일들을 더러 보았다 해도, 그대는 처음에는 나, 판 헬싱이 미쳤다고 — 그 숱한 공포와 그토록 오래 이어진 신경의 긴장이 마침내 내 머리를 돌게 했다고 — 여길지도 모르니.\n\n어제 온종일 우리는 점점 더 산에 가까워지며, 점점 더 거칠고 황량한 땅으로 들어갔다. 거대하고 험상궂은 절벽들과 쏟아지는 물이 많았고, 자연이 한때 광란의 잔치라도 벌인 듯했다. 미나 부인은 여전히 자고 또 잤다. 나는 배가 고파 그것을 채웠으나, 음식을 위해서조차 그녀를 깨울 수 없었다. 나는 그 흡혈귀의 세례로 더럽혀진 그녀에게 이 땅의 치명적인 마력이 깃든 것은 아닐까 두려워지기 시작했다. \"그래,\" 하고 나는 혼잣말했다. \"그녀가 온종일 잔다면, 나는 밤에 자지 않으면 될 일이다.\" 우리가 험한 길을 — 오래되고 변변치 못한 종류의 길이나마 길이 있었으니 — 따라 가는 동안, 나는 고개를 떨군 채 잠들었다. 또다시 나는 죄책감과 시간이 흘렀다는 느낌과 함께 깨어났고, 미나 부인은 여전히 잠들어 있고 해는 낮게 내려와 있었다. 그러나 모든 것이 정말로 달라져 있었다. 험상궂은 산들은 더 멀어진 듯했고, 우리는 가파르게 솟은 언덕 꼭대기 가까이에 있었는데, 그 정상에는 조너선이 일기에 적은 바로 그런 성이 서 있었다. 나는 동시에 환호하고 또 두려워했다. 이제, 좋든 나쁘든, 끝이 가까웠기 때문이다.\n\n나는 미나 부인을 깨우고 다시 최면을 걸어보려 했으나, 아아! 너무 늦을 때까지 소용이 없었다. 그러고서, 우리에게 큰 어둠이 내리기 전에 — 해가 진 뒤에도 하늘이 사라진 해를 눈 위에 반사하여 한동안 모든 것이 거대한 황혼에 잠겼으므로 — 나는 말들을 풀어 그나마 가릴 만한 곳에서 먹였다. 그러고 나서 불을 피우고, 그 곁에 이제 깨어나 어느 때보다 매혹적인 미나 부인을 담요에 둘러싸여 편안히 앉게 했다. 나는 음식을 차렸으나, 그녀는 그저 배가 고프지 않다며 먹으려 하지 않았다. 나는 그녀를 억지로 권하지 않았으니, 소용없음을 알았기 때문이다. 그러나 나 자신은 먹었다. 이제 모든 일을 위해 강해져야만 했기 때문이다. 그런 다음, 무슨 일이 일어날지 모른다는 두려움에, 나는 미나 부인이 앉은 자리 둘레로 그녀가 편히 있을 만큼 큰 원을 그렸다. 그리고 그 원 위로 성체병(聖體餠) 얼마를 뿌리고, 잘게 부수어 모든 곳이 빈틈없이 지켜지게 했다. 그녀는 내내 가만히 앉아 있었다. 죽은 사람처럼 그토록 가만히. 그리고 점점 더 하얘져서 눈보다 더 창백해졌고, 한마디도 하지 않았다. 그러나 내가 가까이 가자 그녀는 나에게 매달렸고, 나는 그 가엾은 영혼이 머리끝에서 발끝까지 떨고 있음을, 만지기에도 고통스러운 떨림으로 떨고 있음을 알 수 있었다. 그녀가 좀 더 잠잠해졌을 때, 나는 이윽고 말했다.\n\n\"불가로 건너오지 않겠소?\" 그녀가 무엇을 할 수 있는지 시험해보고 싶었던 것이다. 그녀는 순순히 일어섰으나, 한 걸음을 떼고는 멈추어, 벼락 맞은 사람처럼 서 있었다.\n\n\"왜 더 나아가지 않소?\" 내가 물었다. 그녀는 고개를 젓고는 되돌아와 제자리에 앉았다. 그러고서 잠에서 깨어난 사람처럼 눈을 크게 뜨고 나를 바라보며, 그저 이렇게 말했다.\n\n\"못 가겠어요!\" 그러고는 입을 다물었다. 나는 기뻤다. 그녀가 할 수 없는 일이라면, 우리가 두려워하는 그 어떤 것도 할 수 없음을 알았기 때문이다. 비록 그녀의 몸에는 위험이 있을지 몰라도, 그녀의 영혼은 안전했던 것이다!\n\n이윽고 말들이 비명을 지르기 시작하더니, 내가 가서 달랠 때까지 매어둔 줄을 잡아챘다. 내 손이 자기들 몸에 닿자 그들은 기쁜 듯 낮게 울며 내 손을 핥았고, 한동안 잠잠해졌다. 밤새 나는 여러 번 그들에게 갔고, 마침내 온 자연이 가장 가라앉는 추운 시각에 이르렀다. 그리고 내가 갈 때마다 그들은 잠잠해졌다. 그 추운 시각에 불이 사위어가기 시작했고, 나는 그것을 돋우려 막 걸음을 내디디려던 참이었다. 이제 눈이 휘몰아치며 흩날렸고, 그와 함께 차가운 안개가 몰려왔다. 어둠 속에서도 눈 위에는 늘 그렇듯 어떤 빛이 감돌았고, 마치 눈보라와 안개의 자락이 옷자락을 끄는 여인들의 형상을 띠는 듯했다. 모든 것이 죽은 듯, 음산한 침묵에 잠겨 있었는데, 다만 말들이 최악의 무엇을 두려워하듯 울며 움츠릴 뿐이었다. 나는 두려워지기 시작했다. 끔찍한 두려움이었다. 그러나 그때 내가 서 있는 그 원 안에서 안전하다는 느낌이 찾아왔다. 또한 나는 이 모든 상상이 밤과 어둠과, 내가 겪어온 불안과 그 모든 무서운 근심 탓이라고 생각하기 시작했다. 마치 조너선의 그 모든 끔찍한 경험에 대한 기억이 나를 농락하는 듯했다. 눈송이와 안개가 빙빙 돌며 맴돌기 시작하더니, 마침내 나는 그를 입맞추려 했던 그 여인들의 어렴풋한 모습을 흘끗 보는 듯했다. 그러자 말들은 점점 더 낮게 움츠리며, 고통에 빠진 사람처럼 두려움에 신음했다. 그들에게는 떨치고 달아날 만한 광란의 공포조차 없었다. 이 기괴한 형체들이 다가와 맴돌 때 나는 사랑하는 미나 부인이 두려웠다. 그녀를 바라보았으나, 그녀는 차분히 앉아 나를 향해 미소 지었다. 내가 불을 돋우려 그쪽으로 걸음을 떼려 하자, 그녀는 나를 붙들어 만류하며, 마치 꿈결에 듣는 목소리처럼, 그토록 낮게 속삭였다.\n\n\"안 돼요! 안 돼요! 밖으로 나가지 마세요. 여기 있어야 안전해요!\" 나는 그녀에게로 돌아서서, 그 눈을 들여다보며 말했다.\n\n\"하지만 당신은? 내가 두려운 것은 바로 당신이오!\" 그러자 그녀가 웃었다. 낮고 비현실적인 웃음이었다. 그리고 말했다.\n\n\"나를 위해 두려워한다고요! 왜 나를 위해 두려워하세요? 이 세상 누구도 나만큼 그들로부터 안전하지 않은걸요.\" 그 말의 뜻을 내가 의아해하는 동안, 한 줄기 바람이 불꽃을 솟구치게 했고, 나는 그녀 이마의 붉은 흉터를 보았다. 그제야, 아아! 나는 알았다. 설령 몰랐더라도 곧 알게 되었을 것이니, 안개와 눈으로 맴돌던 형체들이 더 가까이 다가왔으나, 언제나 그 신성한 원 밖에 머물렀기 때문이다. 그러고서 그들은 형체를 갖추기 시작하더니, 마침내 — 신께서 내 이성을 거두어가지 않으셨다면, 나는 내 두 눈으로 그것을 보았으므로 — 조너선이 방에서 보았던, 그의 목에 입맞추려 했던 바로 그 세 여인이 실제 살을 갖추고 내 앞에 있었다. 나는 알아보았다. 흔들리는 풍만한 자태, 빛나는 단단한 눈, 흰 이, 발그레한 빛깔, 관능적인 입술을. 그들은 줄곧 가엾고 사랑하는 미나 부인을 향해 미소 지었다. 그리고 그들의 웃음이 밤의 침묵을 뚫고 들려올 때, 그들은 서로 팔을 휘감고 그녀를 가리키며, 조너선이 유리잔의 견딜 수 없는 감미로움 같다고 했던 그 달콤하고 짜릿한 목소리로 말했다.\n\n\"이리 와, 자매여. 우리에게 와. 와! 와!\" 두려움에 나는 가엾은 미나 부인에게로 돌아섰고, 내 가슴은 기쁨에 불꽃처럼 솟구쳤다. 아! 그 고운 눈에 어린 공포, 그 혐오, 그 전율이 내 가슴에 온통 희망뿐인 이야기를 들려주었기 때문이다. 신께 감사하게도, 그녀는 아직 그들 가운데 하나가 아니었다. 나는 곁에 있던 땔감 얼마를 움켜쥐고, 성체병 얼마를 내밀며, 불을 향해 그들 쪽으로 나아갔다. 그들은 내 앞에서 물러서며, 낮고 끔찍한 웃음을 웃었다. 나는 불을 돋우었고, 그들이 두렵지 않았다. 우리가 우리의 방비 안에서 안전함을 알았기 때문이다. 그들은 내가 그렇게 무장하고 있는 한 나에게 다가올 수 없었고, 미나 부인이 원 안에 머무는 한 그녀에게도 다가올 수 없었다. 그녀가 원을 벗어날 수 없는 만큼이나 그들도 들어올 수 없었던 것이다. 말들은 신음을 그치고 땅에 가만히 누워 있었다. 눈이 그들 위로 부드럽게 내려 점점 하얘졌다. 나는 그 가엾은 짐승들에게는 이제 더는 공포가 없음을 알았다.\n\n그렇게 우리는 새벽의 붉은빛이 눈 어린 어둠을 뚫고 내릴 때까지 머물렀다. 나는 황량하고 두려웠으며, 비탄과 공포로 가득했다. 그러나 그 아름다운 해가 지평선을 오르기 시작하자 나에게 다시 생명이 돌아왔다. 새벽이 처음 밝아올 때, 그 끔찍한 형체들은 소용돌이치는 안개와 눈 속으로 녹아들었다. 투명한 어둠의 자락들이 성 쪽으로 물러가더니 사라졌다.\n\n새벽이 다가오자 나는 본능적으로 미나 부인에게 돌아서서 그녀에게 최면을 걸려 했다. 그러나 그녀는 깊고 갑작스러운 잠에 빠져 있어, 나는 그녀를 깨울 수 없었다. 잠든 채로나마 최면을 걸어보려 했으나, 그녀는 아무런 반응도, 조금도 보이지 않았다. 그리고 날이 밝았다. 나는 아직 움직이기가 두렵다. 나는 불을 피우고 말들을 살펴보았는데, 모두 죽어 있었다. 오늘 나는 이곳에서 할 일이 많고, 해가 높이 뜰 때까지 기다리고 있다. 내가 가야 할 곳들 가운데에는, 눈과 안개가 가린다 해도 그 햇빛이 나에게 안전이 되어줄 곳이 있을지도 모르기 때문이다.\n\n나는 아침을 들어 기운을 차린 뒤, 나의 끔찍한 일에 나설 것이다. 미나 부인은 아직 잠들어 있다. 그리고, 신께 감사하게도! 그녀는 잠결에 평온하다……\n\n「조너선 하커의 일기」\n\n11월 4일, 저녁. — 증기선의 사고는 우리에게 끔찍한 일이었다. 그것만 아니었다면 우리는 진작 그 배를 따라잡았을 것이고, 지금쯤 사랑하는 미나는 자유로워졌을 것이다. 그 끔찍한 곳 근처 황야에 나가 있을 그녀를 생각하기가 두렵다. 우리는 말을 구했고, 그 자취를 따라간다. 고댈밍이 채비를 하는 동안 이것을 적어둔다. 우리에게는 무기가 있다. 슈가니들이 싸울 작정이라면 조심해야 할 것이다. 아, 모리스와 슈어드만 우리와 함께 있다면. 우리는 그저 희망을 품을 수밖에! 더 적지 못한다면, 잘 있어요, 미나! 신께서 그대를 축복하고 지켜주시기를.\n* 슈가니: 트란실바니아 지방의 집시 무리.\n\n「슈어드 박사의 일기」\n\n11월 5일. — 동틀 녘에 우리는 앞쪽에서 슈가니 무리가 짐수레를 끌고 강에서 부리나케 달아나는 것을 보았다. 그들은 수레를 무리 지어 에워싸고는, 마치 쫓기는 듯 서둘러 갔다. 눈이 가볍게 내리고 있고, 공기 속에 묘한 흥분이 감돈다. 우리 자신의 감정 탓일지도 모르나, 그 침울함은 기이하다. 멀리서 늑대들의 울부짖음이 들린다. 눈이 그들을 산에서 내려오게 하니, 우리 모두에게, 그리고 사방에서 위험이 도사린다. 말들이 거의 준비되었고, 우리는 곧 떠난다. 우리는 누군가의 죽음을 향해 달려간다. 그것이 누구일지, 어디일지, 무엇일지, 언제일지, 어떻게일지는 오직 신만이 아신다……\n\n「판 헬싱 박사의 비망록」\n\n11월 5일, 오후. — 적어도 나는 제정신이다. 그것을 증명하는 일이 끔찍하기는 했으나, 어쨌든 그 자비에 신께 감사한다. 미나 부인을 신성한 원 안에 잠들게 두고, 나는 성으로 향했다. 베레슈티에서 마차에 실어 온 대장간 망치가 쓸모 있었다. 문은 모두 열려 있었으나, 어떤 악의나 불운이 그것을 닫아 내가 일단 들어간 뒤 나오지 못하게 될까 봐, 나는 녹슨 돌쩌귀에서 문짝을 떼어냈다. 조너선의 쓰라린 경험이 여기서 나에게 도움이 되었다. 그의 일기를 기억하여 나는 옛 예배당으로 가는 길을 찾았다. 내 일이 그곳에 있음을 알았기 때문이다. 공기는 숨막혔다. 무슨 유황 연기 같은 것이 있어, 때때로 나를 어지럽게 했다. 내 귀에 무언가 울려대는 것인지, 아니면 멀리서 늑대들의 울부짖음이 들리는 것인지 알 수 없었다. 그때 나는 사랑하는 미나 부인이 떠올랐고, 끔찍한 곤경에 빠졌다. 진퇴양난이 나를 그 두 뿔 사이에 끼워두고 있었다.\n\n그녀를, 나는 감히 이 안으로 데려올 수 없어, 그 신성한 원 안에 흡혈귀로부터 안전하게 남겨두었다. 그러나 그곳에도 늑대는 있을 터였다! 나는 내 일이 여기 있으며, 늑대에 관해서는 그것이 신의 뜻이라면 따를 수밖에 없다고 마음을 다잡았다. 어쨌든 그 너머에 있는 것은 죽음과 자유뿐이었다. 그래서 나는 그녀를 위해 그렇게 택했다. 그것이 나 자신을 위한 선택이었다면 쉬웠을 것이다. 흡혈귀의 무덤에 누워 있느니 늑대의 아가리 속에 쉬는 편이 나았을 테니! 그리하여 나는 내 일을 계속하기로 택했다.\n\n나는 찾아야 할 무덤이 적어도 셋임을 알았다. 깃들어 있는 무덤들이었다. 그래서 나는 찾고, 또 찾아, 그중 하나를 찾아냈다. 그녀는 흡혈귀의 잠 속에 누워 있었는데, 생명과 관능적인 아름다움이 어찌나 충만하던지, 나는 마치 살인을 저지르러 온 듯 몸서리쳤다. 아, 의심할 바 없이 옛날, 이런 일들이 있던 시절에, 나와 같은 일을 하러 나선 많은 사내가 끝내 마음이 약해지고 이어 용기마저 꺾였으리라. 그래서 그는 미루고, 미루고, 또 미루다가, 마침내 그 음란한 언데드의 순전한 아름다움과 매혹에 최면이 걸리고 만다. 그리고 그는 머물고 또 머물러, 마침내 해가 지고 흡혈귀의 잠이 끝난다. 그러면 그 아름다운 여인의 고운 눈이 떠져 사랑을 담아 바라보고, 관능적인 입이 입맞춤을 내밀고 — 사내는 약하다. 그리하여 흡혈귀의 무리에 희생자가 하나 더 남게 된다. 음산하고 소름 끼치는 언데드의 대열을 불릴 자가 하나 더!……\n\n분명 무슨 매혹이 있다. 나는 그저 그런 존재가 곁에 있다는 것만으로도 마음이 흔들렸으니, 비록 백작의 소굴에서 나던 그 끔찍한 냄새가 감돌고, 세월에 닳고 수백 년의 먼지가 무겁게 내려앉은 무덤 속에 누워 있는 모습이었음에도. 그렇다, 나는 흔들렸다. 온 목적과 증오의 동기를 품은 나, 판 헬싱이 — 나는 지체하고픈 갈망에 흔들렸으니, 그것이 내 능력을 마비시키고 내 영혼 자체를 무겁게 막는 듯했다. 어쩌면 자연스러운 잠에 대한 욕구와 그 기이하게 숨막히는 공기가 나를 압도하기 시작한 것이었는지도 모른다. 분명한 것은 내가 잠으로 빠져들고 있었다는 것이니, 달콤한 매혹에 굴복하는 자의 눈뜬 잠이었다. 그때 눈에 잦아든 공기를 뚫고 길고 낮은 통곡이 들려왔는데, 어찌나 비탄과 연민으로 가득했던지 나팔 소리처럼 나를 깨웠다. 그것은 내가 들은 사랑하는 미나 부인의 목소리였기 때문이다.\n\n그러고서 나는 다시 끔찍한 일에 마음을 다잡고, 무덤의 뚜껑들을 비틀어 떼어내어 자매들 가운데 또 하나, 다른 검은 머리의 여인을 찾아냈다. 다시금 매혹에 사로잡히기 시작할까 두려워, 나는 앞서 그 자매에게 그랬듯 멈추어 그녀를 바라보지는 못했다. 그러나 나는 계속 찾아, 이윽고 무척 사랑받던 이를 위해 만든 듯한 높고 거대한 무덤에서, 조너선처럼 나도 안개의 미립자들로부터 몸을 추스르는 것을 보았던 또 한 명의 고운 자매를 찾아냈다. 그녀는 보기에 어찌나 아름답고, 어찌나 눈부시게 곱고, 어찌나 절묘하게 관능적이던지, 내 안에 깃든, 같은 남자들 가운데 더러를 여자를 사랑하고 지키도록 부르는 그 사내로서의 본능이 새로운 감정으로 내 머리를 어지럽게 했다. 그러나 신께 감사하게도, 사랑하는 미나 부인의 그 영혼의 통곡이 아직 내 귀에서 사라지지 않았다. 그래서 그 마력이 나에게 더 깊이 미치기 전에, 나는 나의 거친 일에 마음을 다잡았다. 이때쯤 나는 내가 아는 한 예배당의 무덤을 모두 뒤졌다. 그리고 밤중에 우리 둘레에 있던 이 언데드 망령은 셋뿐이었으므로, 나는 더 활동하는 언데드는 없으리라 여겼다. 나머지 모든 것보다 더 위엄 있는 거대한 무덤이 하나 더 있었다. 그것은 거대하고 고귀하게 균형이 잡혀 있었다. 그 위에는 단 한마디가 새겨져 있었다.\n\n드라큘라.\n그러니 이것이 그토록 많은 자를 거느린 흡혈귀 왕의 언데드 거처였다. 그 비어 있음이, 내가 알던 바를 확실히 하라고 웅변하듯 말해주었다. 내 무서운 일로 이 여인들을 죽은 본래의 모습으로 되돌리기에 앞서, 나는 드라큘라의 무덤에 성체병 얼마를 놓아두어, 언데드인 그를 그곳에서 영원히 내쫓았다.\n\n그러고서 나의 끔찍한 일이 시작되었고, 나는 그것을 두려워했다. 하나뿐이었다면 비교적 쉬웠을 것이다. 그러나 셋이라니! 공포의 행위를 한 차례 겪은 뒤에 두 번을 더 시작해야 하다니. 사랑스러운 루시 양에게도 그토록 끔찍했거늘, 수백 년을 살아남아 세월이 흐르며 힘을 더한, 할 수만 있다면 그 더러운 목숨을 위해 싸웠을 이 기이한 존재들에게는 오죽하겠는가……\n\n아, 내 벗 존이여, 그것은 도살의 일이었네. 다른 죽은 이들과, 그토록 두려움의 장막이 드리운 산 자들에 대한 생각으로 마음을 다잡지 않았더라면 나는 계속할 수 없었을 걸세. 모든 것이 끝날 때까지, 신께 감사하게도 내 담력은 버텨주었으나, 지금도 나는 떨고 또 떨고 있다네. 첫 번째에서 그 안식과, 영혼이 구원받았다는 깨달음이듯 최후의 소멸이 오기 직전에 그 위로 슬며시 번지던 그 기쁨을 보지 못했더라면, 나는 나의 도살을 더는 이어가지 못했을 걸세. 말뚝이 박혀 들어갈 때의 그 끔찍한 비명, 몸부림치는 형체의 요동침, 피거품 이는 입술을 견뎌내지 못했을 걸세. 나는 공포에 질려 달아나 내 일을 끝맺지 못한 채 두었을 걸세. 그러나 끝났네! 그리고 그 가엾은 영혼들을, 이제 나는 가여워하며 눈물 흘릴 수 있다네. 그들이 사라지기 직전 짧은 한순간, 저마다 죽음의 온전한 잠 속에 평온히 누워 있던 모습을 떠올리노라면. 벗 존이여, 내 칼이 저마다의 머리를 베어내자마자, 온몸이 녹아내려 본래의 흙으로 부서지기 시작했으니, 마치 수백 년 전에 왔어야 할 죽음이 마침내 자기를 내세워 단번에 큰 소리로 \"내가 여기 있노라!\"라고 외치는 듯했네.\n\n성을 떠나기 전에 나는 그 입구들을 단단히 막아, 언데드인 백작이 다시는 그곳에 들어갈 수 없게 했다.\n\n내가 미나 부인이 잠들어 있는 원 안으로 들어서자, 그녀는 잠에서 깨어났고, 나를 보더니 내가 너무 많은 것을 견뎌냈다며 고통스럽게 부르짖었다.\n\n\"이리 오세요!\" 그녀가 말했다. \"이 무서운 곳에서 떠나요! 우리에게 다가오고 있는, 나는 알아요, 내 남편을 맞으러 가요.\" 그녀는 야위고 창백하고 약해 보였다. 그러나 그 눈은 맑았고 열정으로 빛났다. 나는 그녀의 창백함과 병약함이 반가웠다. 내 마음이 그 발그레한 흡혈귀의 잠에 대한 생생한 공포로 가득했기 때문이다.\n\n그리하여 우리는 믿음과 희망을 품고, 그러나 여전히 두려움으로 가득한 채, 우리의 벗들을 — 그리고 미나 부인이 우리를 맞으러 오고 있음을 안다고 나에게 말하는 그를 — 맞으러 동쪽으로 간다.\n\n「미나 하커의 일기」\n\n11월 6일. — 교수님과 내가 조너선이 오고 있음을 내가 아는 그 동쪽을 향해 길을 나선 것은 오후 늦은 시각이었다. 길은 가파른 내리막이었으나 우리는 빨리 가지 못했으니, 무거운 담요와 두르개를 챙겨 가야 했기 때문이다. 추위와 눈 속에서 온기 없이 남겨질지도 모를 가능성을 우리는 감히 무릅쓸 수 없었다. 먹을 것도 얼마간 챙겨 가야 했으니, 우리가 완전한 황무지에 있었고, 눈발 사이로 보이는 한 사람이 사는 흔적조차 없었기 때문이다. 한 마일쯤 갔을 때, 나는 힘겨운 걸음에 지쳐 쉬려고 주저앉았다. 그러고서 우리는 뒤를 돌아보았고, 드라큘라 성의 또렷한 윤곽이 하늘을 가르는 곳을 보았다. 우리는 그 성이 자리한 언덕 아래로 그토록 깊이 내려와 있어서, 카르파티아 산맥의 원근의 각도가 성보다 훨씬 아래에 있었던 것이다. 우리는 그 온전한 장엄함을 보았다. 깎아지른 절벽 정상에 천 피트 높이로 자리한 그 성은, 사방으로 인접한 산의 가파른 비탈과 그 사이에 거대한 틈을 둔 듯했다. 그곳에는 무언가 거칠고 으스스한 데가 있었다. 우리는 멀리서 늑대들이 울부짖는 소리를 들을 수 있었다. 그들은 멀리 있었으나, 소리를 죽이는 눈발을 뚫고 둔하게 전해 오는 그 소리에도 공포가 가득했다. 나는 판 헬싱 박사가 주위를 살피는 모습에서, 그가 습격을 당할 경우 우리가 덜 노출될 어떤 전략적 지점을 찾으려 하고 있음을 알았다. 험한 길은 여전히 아래로 이어졌다. 우리는 쌓인 눈 사이로 그 길을 따라갈 수 있었다.\n\n이윽고 교수님이 나에게 신호를 보냈고, 나는 일어나 그에게로 갔다. 그는 멋진 자리를 찾아냈는데, 바위 안의 일종의 자연스러운 우묵한 곳으로, 두 개의 큰 바위 사이에 문간 같은 입구가 있었다. 그는 내 손을 잡아 안으로 이끌었다. \"보시오!\" 그가 말했다. \"여기 있으면 가려질 것이오. 그리고 늑대들이 오더라도 나는 그들을 하나씩 맞상대할 수 있소.\" 그는 우리의 모피를 들여와 나를 위해 아늑한 보금자리를 만들고, 먹을 것을 꺼내어 나에게 억지로 권했다. 그러나 나는 먹을 수 없었다. 먹으려 해보는 것조차 나에게는 역겨웠고, 그를 기쁘게 해주고 싶은 마음은 굴뚝같았으나 도저히 시도할 수가 없었다. 그는 무척 슬퍼 보였으나, 나를 나무라지는 않았다. 갑에서 쌍안경을 꺼내 들고, 그는 바위 꼭대기에 서서 지평선을 살피기 시작했다. 갑자기 그가 외쳤다.\n\n\"보시오! 미나 부인, 보시오! 보시오!\" 나는 벌떡 일어나 바위 위 그의 곁에 섰다. 그는 나에게 쌍안경을 건네며 가리켰다. 이제 눈은 더 거세게 내리며 사납게 휘몰아쳤으니, 강풍이 불기 시작했기 때문이다. 그래도 눈보라 사이사이 멎는 때가 있어, 나는 멀리까지 둘러볼 수 있었다. 우리가 있는 높은 곳에서는 아주 먼 데까지 볼 수 있었다. 멀리, 눈의 하얀 황무지 너머로, 강이 굽이굽이 휘돌아 가며 검은 리본처럼 누워 있는 것이 보였다. 우리 바로 앞, 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 — 실은 어찌나 가까운지 우리가 진작 알아채지 못한 것이 의아할 정도였는데 — 말 탄 사내들의 무리가 서둘러 오고 있었다. 그들 한가운데에는 수레가 있었는데, 길의 험한 굴곡마다 마치 개의 꼬리가 흔들리듯 좌우로 휩쓸리는 긴 짐수레였다. 눈을 배경으로 윤곽이 드러난 그들의 옷차림으로 나는 그들이 농부이거나 어떤 부류의 집시임을 알 수 있었다.\n\n수레 위에는 커다란 네모난 궤짝이 있었다. 그것을 보자 내 심장이 뛰었으니, 끝이 다가오고 있음을 느꼈기 때문이다. 이제 저녁이 가까워지고 있었고, 나는 잘 알고 있었다. 해가 지면, 그때까지 거기 갇혀 있던 그것이 새로이 자유를 얻어 여러 모습 가운데 어느 것으로든 모든 추격을 따돌릴 수 있음을. 두려움에 나는 교수님에게로 돌아섰는데, 그러나 당황스럽게도 그는 거기 없었다. 다음 순간, 나는 그가 내 아래에 있는 것을 보았다. 그는 바위 둘레에, 우리가 지난밤 몸을 가렸던 것과 같은 원을 그려둔 것이었다. 그것을 다 그리고 나서 그는 다시 내 곁에 서서 말했다.\n\n\"적어도 여기서는 그로부터 당신이 안전할 것이오!\" 그는 나에게서 쌍안경을 받아 들고, 다음번 눈이 멎었을 때 우리 아래의 온 들판을 훑었다. \"보시오.\" 그가 말했다. \"그들이 빨리 오고 있소. 말에 채찍질을 하며, 있는 힘껏 질주하고 있소.\" 그는 잠시 멈추었다가 공허한 목소리로 이어 말했다.\n\n\"그들은 해가 지기 전에 닿으려고 달리고 있소. 우리가 너무 늦었을지도 모르오. 신의 뜻이 이루어지기를!\" 또 한 차례 앞을 가리는 눈보라가 휘몰아쳐 온 풍경을 지워버렸다. 그러나 그것은 곧 지나갔고, 다시금 그의 쌍안경이 평원에 고정되었다. 그때 갑작스러운 외침이 들렸다.\n\n\"보시오! 보시오! 보시오! 저것 보시오, 말 탄 두 사람이 빠르게 따라오고 있소, 남쪽에서 올라오고 있소. 퀸시와 존임에 틀림없소. 쌍안경을 받으시오. 눈이 모두 지워버리기 전에 보시오!\" 나는 그것을 받아 보았다. 두 사람은 슈어드 박사와 모리스 씨인 듯했다. 어쨌든 그 둘 가운데 조너선은 없음을 나는 알았다. 동시에 나는 조너선이 멀지 않은 곳에 있음을 알았다. 둘러보니, 다가오는 무리의 북쪽으로 또 다른 두 사람이 목숨을 건 듯한 속도로 말을 달리고 있는 것이 보였다. 그중 하나는 조너선임을 나는 알았고, 다른 하나는 물론 고댈밍 경이리라 여겼다. 그들 역시 수레를 거느린 무리를 쫓고 있었다. 내가 이를 교수님에게 말하자 그는 학생처럼 환호성을 질렀고, 눈발에 시야가 보이지 않게 될 때까지 골똘히 바라본 뒤, 윈체스터 소총을 우리 은신처 입구의 바위에 기대어 언제든 쓸 수 있게 두었다. \"그들이 모두 모여들고 있소.\" 그가 말했다. \"때가 오면 우리는 사방에 집시들을 두게 될 것이오.\" 나는 권총을 꺼내어 손 닿는 곳에 두었으니, 우리가 이야기를 나누는 동안 늑대들의 울부짖음이 더 크고 더 가까이 들려왔기 때문이다. 눈보라가 한순간 잦아들었을 때 우리는 다시 바라보았다. 그토록 굵은 눈송이가 우리 가까이에 내리는데, 그 너머로는 해가 먼 산봉우리들을 향해 가라앉으며 점점 더 밝게 빛나는 것이 기이했다. 쌍안경으로 우리 사방을 훑으니, 여기저기 점들이 하나씩, 둘씩, 셋씩, 그리고 더 큰 무리를 이루어 움직이는 것이 보였다. 늑대들이 먹잇감을 노리고 모여들고 있었던 것이다.\n\n우리가 기다리는 동안 매 순간이 한 세월처럼 느껴졌다. 이제 바람이 사나운 돌풍으로 몰아쳤고, 빙빙 도는 소용돌이로 우리를 휩쓸며 눈이 맹렬히 흩날렸다. 어떤 때에는 한 팔 길이 앞도 보이지 않았다. 그러나 또 어떤 때에는, 공허한 소리를 내며 바람이 우리 곁을 스쳐 지날 때, 우리 둘레의 공간이 트여 멀리까지 볼 수 있을 듯했다. 우리는 근래 일출과 일몰을 살피는 데 워낙 익숙해진 터라, 그때가 언제일지 꽤 정확히 알았다. 그리고 우리는 머지않아 해가 지리라는 것을 알았다. 우리 시계로 보아, 갖가지 무리가 우리 가까이로 모여들기 시작하기 전까지 우리가 그 바위 은신처에서 기다린 것이 한 시간도 채 못 된다는 것이 믿기 어려웠다. 이제 바람은 더 사납고 더 매서운 돌풍으로, 더 한결같이 북쪽에서 불어왔다. 그것이 눈구름을 우리에게서 몰아낸 듯, 이따금 돌풍이 일 때를 빼고는 눈이 그치다시피 했다. 우리는 각 무리의 개개인을, 쫓기는 자들과 쫓는 자들을 또렷이 분간할 수 있었다. 기이하게도 쫓기는 자들은 자신들이 쫓기고 있음을 깨닫지 못하는 듯했고, 적어도 개의치 않는 듯했다. 그러나 그들은 해가 산봉우리 위로 점점 더 낮게 떨어지자 곱절의 속도로 서두르는 듯했다.\n\n그들은 점점 더 가까이 다가왔다. 교수님과 나는 바위 뒤에 몸을 웅크리고 무기를 겨눌 채비를 했다. 나는 그가 그들을 결코 지나가게 하지 않으리라 작정했음을 알 수 있었다. 그들은 하나같이 우리가 있음을 전혀 알아채지 못했다.\n\n별안간 두 목소리가 외쳤다. \"멈춰라!\" 하나는 격정으로 높이 올라간 내 조너선의 목소리였고, 다른 하나는 모리스 씨의 강하고 단호한, 차분한 명령의 어조였다. 집시들은 그 말을 알아듣지 못했을지 모르나, 어떤 언어로 그 말이 내뱉어졌든 그 어조만은 오해할 여지가 없었다. 본능적으로 그들은 고삐를 당겼고, 그 순간 고댈밍 경과 조너선이 한쪽에서, 슈어드 박사와 모리스 씨가 다른 쪽에서 달려들었다. 집시들의 두목은, 켄타우로스처럼 말 위에 앉은 멋진 풍모의 사내였는데, 그들에게 물러나라고 손짓하고는 사나운 목소리로 동료들에게 계속 나아가라는 무슨 말을 내뱉었다. 그들이 말을 후려치자 말들이 앞으로 튀어나갔다. 그러나 우리 쪽 네 사내가 윈체스터 소총을 들어, 오해할 여지 없는 태도로 멈추라고 명령했다. 같은 순간 판 헬싱 박사와 나는 바위 뒤에서 일어나 그들에게 무기를 겨누었다. 자신들이 포위되었음을 본 사내들은 고삐를 죄어 멈추어 섰다. 두목이 그들에게로 돌아서서 한마디를 내뱉자, 집시 무리의 모든 사내가 저마다 지닌 무기를, 칼이든 권총이든 빼 들고 공격할 채비를 갖추었다. 한순간에 싸움이 붙었다.\n\n두목은 고삐를 잰싸게 놀려 자기 말을 앞으로 내몰고는, 먼저 해를 — 이제 산봉우리에 바짝 내려와 있는 — 가리키고 다음으로 성을 가리키며, 내가 알아듣지 못하는 무슨 말을 했다. 그 대답으로 우리 쪽 네 사내가 모두 말에서 몸을 던져 수레를 향해 달려들었다. 그토록 위험에 처한 조너선을 보고 나는 끔찍한 두려움을 느꼈어야 마땅했으나, 나에게도 나머지 사람들과 마찬가지로 전투의 열기가 올랐던 모양이다. 나는 두려움을 느끼지 않았고, 오직 무언가를 하고 싶다는 거칠고 솟구치는 욕망만을 느꼈다. 우리 쪽 무리의 잰 움직임을 본 집시 두목이 명령을 내리자, 그의 부하들이 곧장 수레 둘레에 일종의 질서 없는 안간힘으로 모여들어, 저마다 그 명령을 따르려는 열의에 서로를 떠밀고 밀쳤다.\n\n이 와중에 나는 사내들의 고리 한쪽에서는 조너선이, 다른 쪽에서는 퀸시가 수레로 길을 뚫고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그들이 해가 지기 전에 자신들의 일을 끝맺으려 작정했음이 분명했다. 그 무엇도 그들을 멈추거나 가로막을 수조차 없는 듯했다. 앞을 막은 집시들의 겨눈 무기도, 번득이는 칼도, 뒤에서 울부짖는 늑대들도 그들의 주의를 끄는 것조차 못하는 듯했다. 조너선의 맹렬함과 그 목적의 명백한 한결같음이 그의 앞에 있는 자들을 위압하는 듯했다. 본능적으로 그들은 움츠리며 옆으로 비켜 그를 지나가게 했다. 한순간에 그는 수레 위로 뛰어올라, 믿기 어려울 만한 힘으로 그 거대한 궤짝을 들어 올려 바퀴 너머 땅으로 내던졌다. 그동안 모리스 씨는 슈가니의 고리 가운데 자기 쪽을 뚫고 지나가기 위해 힘을 써야 했다. 내가 숨죽이고 조너선을 지켜보는 내내, 나는 곁눈질로 그가 필사적으로 앞으로 밀고 나아가는 것을, 그리고 그가 그들을 뚫고 길을 내자 집시들의 칼이 번득이며 그를 베는 것을 보았다. 그는 자신의 커다란 보위 나이프로 막아냈고, 처음에는 그도 무사히 헤쳐 나온 줄 알았다. 그러나 그가 이제 막 수레에서 뛰어내린 조너선의 곁으로 뛰어들 때, 나는 그가 왼손으로 옆구리를 움켜쥐고 있고, 그 손가락 사이로 피가 솟구치는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그럼에도 그는 지체하지 않았으니, 조너선이 필사적인 힘으로 궤짝의 한쪽 끝에 달려들어 그 커다란 쿠크리 칼로 뚜껑을 비틀어 떼어내려 하자, 그는 다른 쪽 끝을 보위 나이프로 미친 듯이 공격했다. 두 사내의 안간힘에 뚜껑이 들리기 시작했다. 못들이 빠른 끼익 소리를 내며 빠졌고, 궤짝의 윗판이 젖혀졌다.\n\n이때쯤 집시들은 자신들이 윈체스터의 겨눔 아래 놓여, 고댈밍 경과 슈어드 박사의 처분에 달렸음을 보고 항복하여 더는 저항하지 않았다. 해는 산봉우리에 거의 닿아 있었고, 무리 전체의 그림자가 눈 위로 길게 드리워졌다. 나는 백작이 궤짝 안 흙 위에 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 수레에서 거칠게 떨어질 때 흩어진 흙이 얼마간 그 위를 덮고 있었다. 그는 밀랍 인형처럼 죽은 듯 창백했고, 붉은 눈은 내가 너무도 잘 아는 그 끔찍한 앙심의 빛으로 이글거렸다.\n\n내가 바라보는 순간, 그 눈이 지는 해를 보았고, 거기 어린 증오의 빛이 승리의 빛으로 바뀌었다.\n\n그러나 그 찰나에, 조너선의 커다란 칼이 휘둘려 번득였다. 나는 그것이 목을 베어 가르는 것을 보고 비명을 질렀다. 같은 순간 모리스 씨의 보위 나이프가 심장에 박혀 들었다.\n\n그것은 기적과도 같았다. 그러나 바로 우리 눈앞에서, 거의 숨 한 번 쉬는 사이에, 온몸이 가루로 부서져 우리의 시야에서 사라졌다.\n\n내가 살아 있는 한 기쁘게 여길 것은, 그 최후의 소멸의 순간에조차 그 얼굴에 평화의 빛이 어려 있었다는 것이다. 거기 그런 빛이 깃들 수 있으리라고는 나는 결코 상상하지 못했을 것이다.\n\n이제 드라큘라 성이 붉은 하늘을 배경으로 도드라졌고, 그 부서진 흉벽의 돌 하나하나가 지는 햇빛에 또렷이 윤곽을 드러냈다.\n\n집시들은 그 죽은 자의 기이한 사라짐을 어떤 식으로든 우리 탓으로 여기고는, 한마디 말도 없이 돌아서서 죽기 살기로 말을 몰아 달아났다. 말을 타지 못한 자들은 짐수레에 뛰어올라, 말 탄 자들에게 자기들을 버리지 말라고 외쳤다. 안전한 거리로 물러나 있던 늑대들이 그들의 뒤를 따라가며, 우리만 남겨두었다.\n\n땅에 주저앉아 있던 모리스 씨는 한쪽 팔꿈치에 몸을 기댄 채, 손으로 옆구리를 누르고 있었다. 피가 여전히 손가락 사이로 솟구쳤다. 나는 그에게로 달려갔으니, 이제 신성한 원이 나를 막지 않았기 때문이다. 두 의사도 그리했다. 조너선이 그의 뒤에 무릎을 꿇었고, 부상당한 그는 머리를 그의 어깨에 기댔다. 한숨과 함께 그는 가냘픈 힘으로, 피로 더럽혀지지 않은 자기 손으로 내 손을 잡았다. 그는 내 얼굴에서 내 마음의 비통함을 보았던 모양으로, 나를 향해 미소 지으며 말했다.\n\n\"이렇게 조금이나마 도움이 되었다니 더없이 기쁩니다! 아, 하느님!\" 그가 갑자기 외치며, 안간힘을 다해 일어나 앉으려 하면서 나를 가리켰다. \"이를 위해서라면 죽을 가치가 있었습니다! 보세요! 보세요!\"\n\n이제 해는 산봉우리 바로 위에 내려와 있었고, 붉은 빛줄기가 내 얼굴에 비쳐, 내 얼굴이 장밋빛에 잠겼다. 한마음으로 사내들이 무릎을 꿇었고, 그들의 눈이 그의 손가락이 가리키는 곳을 좇으며 모두에게서 깊고 진실한 \"아멘\"이 터져 나왔다. 죽어가는 이가 말했다.\n\n\"이제 신께 감사를, 모든 것이 헛되지 않았으니! 보세요! 그녀의 이마가 저 눈보다 더 티 없습니다! 저주가 사라졌어요!\"\n\n그리고, 우리의 쓰라린 비탄 속에, 미소를 띤 채 말없이, 그는 숨을 거두었다. 용맹한 신사로서.\n\n부기(附記)\n칠 년 전 우리는 모두 그 불길을 헤치고 지나왔다. 그 뒤로 우리 가운데 더러가 누린 행복은, 우리가 견뎌낸 고통에 충분히 값할 만한 것이라고 우리는 생각한다. 우리 아들의 생일이 퀸시 모리스가 죽은 바로 그날이라는 것은 미나와 나에게 더해진 기쁨이다. 그 애 어머니는, 내가 알기로, 우리의 용감한 벗의 영혼 가운데 얼마가 그 애에게로 옮겨갔다는 은밀한 믿음을 품고 있다. 그 애의 여러 이름은 우리 작은 사내들의 무리 전체를 하나로 잇는다. 그러나 우리는 그 애를 퀸시라고 부른다.\n\n올여름 우리는 트란실바니아로 여행을 떠나, 우리에게 그토록 생생하고 끔찍한 기억으로 가득했던, 그리고 지금도 그러한 그 옛 땅을 두루 돌아보았다. 우리가 우리 두 눈으로 보고 우리 두 귀로 들은 일들이 살아 있는 진실이었다는 것을 믿기란 거의 불가능했다. 그 모든 일의 흔적은 깡그리 지워져 있었다. 성은 여전히 예전처럼 황량한 폐허 위로 높이 솟아 있었다.\n\n집으로 돌아와 우리는 그 옛 시절에 대해 이야기를 나누었다. 우리 모두 절망 없이 돌아볼 수 있는 시절이었으니, 고댈밍과 슈어드 둘 다 행복하게 결혼했기 때문이다. 나는 그토록 오래전 우리가 돌아온 이래로 줄곧 보관해온 금고에서 그 서류들을 꺼냈다. 우리는 한 가지 사실에 새삼 놀랐는데, 그 기록을 이루는 그 방대한 자료 가운데 진본 문서라 할 만한 것이 거의 하나도 없다는 것이었다. 미나와 슈어드와 나의 후기 수첩들, 그리고 판 헬싱의 비망록을 빼면, 한 무더기의 타자 친 글뿐이었다. 설령 우리가 원한다 해도, 이것들을 이토록 황당한 이야기의 증거로 받아들여 달라고 그 누구에게도 청하기 어려울 것이다. 판 헬싱은 우리 아들을 무릎에 앉히고는 이렇게 모든 것을 요약하며 말했다.\n\"우리에게는 아무 증거도 필요 없네. 우리는 누구에게도 우리를 믿어 달라 청하지 않는다네! 이 아이는 언젠가 제 어머니가 얼마나 용감하고 의로운 여인인지 알게 될 걸세. 이미 그 애는 어머니의 다정함과 사랑 어린 보살핌을 알고 있지. 훗날 그 애는 어떤 사내들이 그녀를 어찌나 사랑했던지 그녀를 위해 그토록 많은 것을 무릅썼는지 이해하게 될 걸세.\"\n\n조너선 하커.\n끝";
if(typeof BODIES_SENT!=="undefined") BODIES_SENT["gb-345"]=[[["(Kept in shorthand.)\n3 May.", "5월 3일. 비스트리차."], ["Bistritz.--Left Munich at 8:35 P. M., on 1st May, arriving at Vienna early next morning; should have arrived at 6:46, but train was an hour late.", "— 5월 1일 오후 8시 35분에 뮌헨을 떠나 이튿날 새벽 빈에 도착. 6시 46분에 도착해야 했으나 기차가 한 시간 연착되었다."], ["Buda-Pesth seems a wonderful place, from the glimpse which I got of it from the train and the little I could walk through the streets.", "부다페스트는 기차에서 흘끗 본 것과 거리를 잠깐 거닐어본 정도이지만, 참으로 놀라운 곳인 듯하다."], ["I feared to go very far from the station, as we had arrived late and would start as near the correct time as possible.", "도착이 늦은 데다 가능한 한 정시에 가깝게 출발해야 했으므로 역에서 멀리 가기가 두려웠다."], ["The impression I had was that we were leaving the West and entering the East; the most western of splendid bridges over the Danube, which is here of noble width and depth, took us among the traditions of Turkish rule.", "내가 받은 인상은 우리가 서방을 떠나 동방으로 들어서고 있다는 것이었다. 이곳에서 당당한 폭과 깊이를 자랑하는 도나우 강에 놓인 화려한 다리들 가운데 가장 서쪽의 것을 건너자, 우리는 터키 지배의 전통 속으로 들어서게 되었다."]], [["We left in pretty good time, and came after nightfall to Klausenburgh.", "우리는 꽤 정확한 시각에 출발하여, 날이 저문 뒤 클라우젠부르크에 닿았다."], ["Here I stopped for the night at the Hotel Royale.", "나는 이곳 로열 호텔에서 하룻밤 묵었다."], ["I had for dinner, or rather supper, a chicken done up some way with red pepper, which was very good but thirsty.", "저녁, 아니 야식으로 닭고기를 무언가 붉은 고추를 넣어 요리한 것을 먹었는데, 아주 맛이 좋았으나 목이 몹시 말랐다. (비망."], ["(Mem., get recipe for Mina.) I asked the waiter, and he said it was called “paprika hendl,” and that, as it was a national dish, I should be able to get it anywhere along the Carpathians.", "미나에게 줄 요리법을 알아둘 것.) 웨이터에게 물었더니 \"파프리카 헨들\"이라고 부른다며, 이것이 국민 요리이므로 카르파티아 산맥을 따라 어디서든 먹을 수 있을 거라고 했다."], ["I found my smattering of German very useful here; indeed, I don’t know how I should be able to get on without it.", "이곳에서 내 어설픈 독일어가 무척 쓸모 있음을 알았다. 사실 그것이 없었다면 어떻게 헤쳐 나갔을지 모르겠다."]], [["Having had some time at my disposal when in London, I had visited the British Museum, and made search among the books and maps in the library regarding Transylvania; it had struck me that some foreknowledge of the country could hardly fail to have some importance in dealing with a nobleman of that country.", "런던에 있을 때 시간 여유가 좀 있어, 나는 대영박물관을 찾아가 도서관의 책과 지도들 가운데 트란실바니아에 관한 것을 뒤져보았다. 그 나라의 귀족을 상대하는 데에는 그 지역에 대한 사전 지식이 어느 정도 중요하지 않을 리 없다는 생각이 들었던 것이다."], ["I find that the district he named is in the extreme east of the country, just on the borders of three states, Transylvania, Moldavia and Bukovina, in the midst of the Carpathian mountains; one of the wildest and least known portions of Europe.", "그가 말한 지역은 그 나라의 극동쪽, 트란실바니아·몰다비아·부코비나 세 지방의 경계가 만나는 바로 그곳, 카르파티아 산맥 한가운데에 있었다. 유럽에서 가장 거칠고 가장 알려지지 않은 지역 가운데 하나였다."], ["I was not able to light on any map or work giving the exact locality of the Castle Dracula, as there are no maps of this country as yet to compare with our own Ordnance Survey maps; but I found that Bistritz, the post town named by Count Dracula, is a fairly well-known place.", "드라큘라 성의 정확한 위치를 알려주는 지도나 책은 찾을 수 없었는데, 이 나라에는 아직 우리의 육지측량부 지도에 견줄 만한 지도가 없기 때문이었다. 그러나 드라큘라 백작이 말한 역참 도시 비스트리차는 꽤 잘 알려진 곳임을 알 수 있었다."], ["I shall enter here some of my notes, as they may refresh my memory when I talk over my travels with Mina.", "미나와 여행 이야기를 나눌 때 기억을 되살리는 데 도움이 될지 모르니, 여기에 내 메모 몇 가지를 적어둔다."]], [["In the population of Transylvania there are four distinct nationalities: Saxons in the South, and mixed with them the Wallachs, who are the descendants of the Dacians; Magyars in the West, and Szekelys in the East and North.", "트란실바니아의 주민 가운데에는 뚜렷이 구별되는 네 민족이 있다. 남부의 색슨인, 그리고 그들과 섞여 사는 왈라키아인은 다키아인의 후손이다. 서부의 마자르인, 동부와 북부의 세케이인이 있다."], ["I am going among the latter, who claim to be descended from Attila and the Huns.", "나는 후자 쪽으로 가는 길인데, 그들은 자신들이 아틸라와 훈족의 후손이라고 주장한다."], ["This may be so, for when the Magyars conquered the country in the eleventh century they found the Huns settled in it.", "그럴 법도 한 것이, 11세기에 마자르인이 이 나라를 정복했을 때 이미 훈족이 이곳에 정착해 있었기 때문이다."], ["I read that every known superstition in the world is gathered into the horseshoe of the Carpathians, as if it were the centre of some sort of imaginative whirlpool; if so my stay may be very interesting.", "세상에 알려진 온갖 미신이 카르파티아의 말발굽 모양 산맥 안에 모여 있다고, 마치 그곳이 어떤 상상의 소용돌이 중심이기라도 한 것처럼 적혀 있는 글을 읽었다. 그렇다면 내 체류는 매우 흥미로울지도 모르겠다. (비망."], ["(Mem., I must ask the Count all about them.)", "백작에게 그 미신들에 관해 모두 물어볼 것.)"]], [["I did not sleep well, though my bed was comfortable enough, for I had all sorts of queer dreams.", "잠자리가 충분히 편안했는데도 나는 잠을 설쳤다. 온갖 기이한 꿈에 시달렸기 때문이다."], ["There was a dog howling all night under my window, which may have had something to do with it; or it may have been the paprika, for I had to drink up all the water in my carafe, and was still thirsty.", "밤새도록 창문 아래에서 개 한 마리가 짖어댔는데, 그것이 한몫했을 수도 있겠다. 아니면 파프리카 탓이었을지도 모른다. 물병의 물을 다 마셔버리고도 여전히 목이 말랐으니 말이다."], ["Towards morning I slept and was wakened by the continuous knocking at my door, so I guess I must have been sleeping soundly then.", "새벽녘에야 잠이 들었다가, 문을 끊임없이 두드리는 소리에 잠이 깼으니, 그때에는 꽤 깊이 잠들었던 모양이다."], ["I had for breakfast more paprika, and a sort of porridge of maize flour which they said was “mamaliga,” and egg-plant stuffed with forcemeat, a very excellent dish, which they call “impletata.”", "아침식사로 또 파프리카와, 그들이 \"마말리가\"라고 부르는 옥수숫가루 죽 같은 것, 그리고 다진 고기를 채운 가지요리를 먹었는데, 이것은 무척 훌륭한 음식으로 \"임플레타타\"라고 부른다. (비망."], ["(Mem., get recipe for this also.) I had to hurry breakfast, for the train started a little before eight, or rather it ought to have done so, for after rushing to the station at 7:30 I had to sit in the carriage for more than an hour before we began to move.", "이 요리법도 알아둘 것.) 아침식사를 서둘러야 했으니, 기차가 8시 조금 전에 출발하기로 — 아니, 출발하기로 되어 있었기 때문이다. 7시 30분에 역으로 달려갔으나, 열차가 움직이기 시작하기 전까지 한 시간도 넘게 객차에 앉아 기다려야 했다."], ["It seems to me that the further east you go the more unpunctual are the trains.", "동쪽으로 갈수록 기차는 더 시간을 지키지 않는 것 같다."], ["What ought they to be in China?", "중국에서는 대체 어떨까?"]], [["All day long we seemed to dawdle through a country which was full of beauty of every kind.", "온종일 우리는 온갖 종류의 아름다움이 가득한 시골을 느릿느릿 지나는 듯했다."], ["Sometimes we saw little towns or castles on the top of steep hills such as we see in old missals; sometimes we ran by rivers and streams which seemed from the wide stony margin on each side of them to be subject to great floods.", "때로는 옛 기도서에서 보는 것 같은 가파른 언덕 꼭대기의 작은 마을이나 성들이 보였고, 때로는 강과 시내를 따라 달렸는데, 양편의 넓은 자갈밭으로 보아 큰 홍수가 잦은 모양이었다."], ["It takes a lot of water, and running strong, to sweep the outside edge of a river clear.", "강의 바깥쪽 가장자리까지 씻어내려면 거센 물이 많이 흘러야 한다."], ["At every station there were groups of people, sometimes crowds, and in all sorts of attire.", "정거장마다 사람들이 무리지어, 때로는 떼지어 모여 있었고, 차림새도 가지가지였다."], ["Some of them were just like the peasants at home or those I saw coming through France and Germany, with short jackets and round hats and home-made trousers; but others were very picturesque.", "더러는 고향의 농부들이나 내가 프랑스와 독일을 거쳐 오며 본 사람들과 똑같이, 짧은 윗옷에 둥근 모자, 손수 지은 바지 차림이었다. 그러나 다른 이들은 매우 그림처럼 보였다."], ["The women looked pretty, except when you got near them, but they were very clumsy about the waist.", "여인들은 가까이 다가가기 전까지는 예뻐 보였으나, 허리께가 무척 둔해 보였다."], ["They had all full white sleeves of some kind or other, and most of them had big belts with a lot of strips of something fluttering from them like the dresses in a ballet, but of course there were petticoats under them.", "모두 어떤 식으로든 헐렁한 흰 소매가 달린 옷을 입었고, 대부분 큼직한 허리띠를 둘렀는데, 거기에서 발레 의상처럼 무언가의 가닥들이 잔뜩 나부꼈으나, 물론 그 안에는 속치마를 받쳐 입고 있었다."], ["The strangest figures we saw were the Slovaks, who were more barbarian than the rest, with their big cow-boy hats, great baggy dirty-white trousers, white linen shirts, and enormous heavy leather belts, nearly a foot wide, all studded over with brass nails.", "우리가 본 가장 기이한 차림은 슬로바키아인들로, 다른 이들보다 더 야만스러워 보였다. 그들은 카우보이 같은 큰 모자에, 헐렁하고 더러운 흰빛 바지, 흰 아마포 셔츠, 그리고 거의 한 자나 되는 너비에 놋쇠 못이 빼곡히 박힌 엄청나게 무거운 가죽 허리띠를 두르고 있었다."], ["They wore high boots, with their trousers tucked into them, and had long black hair and heavy black moustaches.", "그들은 긴 장화를 신고 그 안에 바지를 쑤셔넣었으며, 머리칼은 길고 검었고 콧수염도 검고 무성했다."], ["They are very picturesque, but do not look prepossessing.", "매우 그림 같았으나 호감이 가는 모습은 아니었다."], ["On the stage they would be set down at once as some old Oriental band of brigands.", "무대 위라면 당장 동방의 옛 산적 떼쯤으로 여겨졌을 것이다."], ["They are, however, I am told, very harmless and rather wanting in natural self-assertion.", "그러나 듣자 하니 그들은 매우 무해하며, 오히려 천성적으로 자기를 내세울 줄 모르는 편이라고 한다."]], [["It was on the dark side of twilight when we got to Bistritz, which is a very interesting old place.", "비스트리차에 닿은 것은 황혼이 어둑해질 무렵이었는데, 이곳은 매우 흥미로운 옛 도시다."], ["Being practically on the frontier--for the Borgo Pass leads from it into Bukovina--it has had a very stormy existence, and it certainly shows marks of it.", "사실상 국경에 자리잡고 있어서 — 보르고 고개가 이곳에서 부코비나로 이어지기 때문이다 — 매우 파란만장한 역사를 겪었고, 분명 그 흔적이 남아 있다."], ["Fifty years ago a series of great fires took place, which made terrible havoc on five separate occasions.", "오십 년 전에 큰 화재가 연이어 일어나 다섯 차례나 끔찍한 참화를 빚었다."], ["At the very beginning of the seventeenth century it underwent a siege of three weeks and lost 13,000 people, the casualties of war proper being assisted by famine and disease.", "17세기 초에는 삼 주에 걸친 포위 공격을 당해 1만 3천 명을 잃었는데, 정작 전투로 인한 사상자에 기근과 질병까지 더해진 것이었다."]], [["Count Dracula had directed me to go to the Golden Krone Hotel, which I found, to my great delight, to be thoroughly old-fashioned, for of course I wanted to see all I could of the ways of the country.", "드라큘라 백작은 나에게 골든 크로네 호텔로 가라고 일러주었는데, 가서 보니 더없이 기뻐하게도 그곳은 철저히 옛스러운 곳이었다. 물론 나는 이 나라의 풍습을 가능한 한 모두 보고 싶었던 것이다."], ["I was evidently expected, for when I got near the door I faced a cheery-looking elderly woman in the usual peasant dress--white undergarment with long double apron, front, and back, of coloured stuff fitting almost too tight for modesty.", "내가 올 것을 분명히 기다리고 있었던 모양으로, 문 가까이 다가가자 흔한 농부 차림 — 흰 속옷에, 앞뒤로 긴 이중 앞치마를 두른 색색의 천이 정숙해 보이기에는 좀 지나치다 싶을 만큼 몸에 꼭 맞는 — 의 명랑해 보이는 늙수그레한 여인과 마주쳤다."], ["When I came close she bowed and said, “The Herr Englishman?”", "가까이 다가가자 그녀는 절을 하며 \"영국 신사분이신가요?\"라고 물었다."], ["“Yes,” I said, “Jonathan Harker.”", "\"그렇소,\" 하고 내가 답했다."], ["She smiled, and gave some message to an elderly man in white shirt-sleeves, who had followed her to the door.", "\"조너선 하커요.\" 그녀는 미소를 짓더니, 문까지 그녀를 따라온 흰 셔츠 차림의 늙수그레한 남자에게 무언가 말을 전했다."], ["He went, but immediately returned with a letter:--", "그는 나갔다가 곧 편지 한 통을 들고 돌아왔다."]], [["“My Friend.--Welcome to the Carpathians.", "\"나의 벗에게. — 카르파티아에 온 것을 환영하오."], ["I am anxiously expecting you.", "나는 당신을 애타게 기다리고 있소."], ["Sleep well to-night. At three to-morrow the diligence will start for Bukovina; a place on it is kept for you.", "오늘 밤은 푹 자도록 하시오. 내일 세 시에 부코비나행 역마차가 출발할 것이며, 당신의 자리는 잡아두었소."], ["At the Borgo Pass my carriage will await you and will bring you to me.", "보르고 고개에서 내 마차가 당신을 기다렸다가 나에게로 데려올 것이오."], ["I trust that your journey from London has been a happy one, and that you will enjoy your stay in my beautiful land.", "런던에서의 여정이 즐거웠기를, 그리고 나의 아름다운 땅에서의 체류를 즐기게 되기를 바라오. 당신의 벗,"]], [["“Your friend,", "드라큘라.\""]], [["4 May.--I found that my landlord had got a letter from the Count, directing him to secure the best place on the coach for me; but on making inquiries as to details he seemed somewhat reticent, and pretended that he could not understand my German.", "5월 4일. — 알고 보니 호텔 주인도 백작으로부터 편지를 받았는데, 나를 위해 역마차의 가장 좋은 자리를 잡아두라는 지시였다. 그러나 자세한 것을 물어보자 그는 어쩐지 말을 아끼며, 내 독일어를 알아듣지 못하는 척했다."], ["This could not be true, because up to then he had understood it perfectly; at least, he answered my questions exactly as if he did.", "그럴 리가 없는 것이, 그때까지는 완벽히 알아들었기 때문이다. 적어도 내 물음에 정확히 답한 것으로 보아 알아듣는 듯했다."], ["He and his wife, the old lady who had received me, looked at each other in a frightened sort of way.", "그와 그의 아내, 곧 나를 맞아준 늙은 부인은 겁먹은 듯한 눈빛으로 서로를 마주보았다."], ["He mumbled out that the money had been sent in a letter, and that was all he knew.", "그는 돈은 편지에 동봉되어 왔으며, 자기가 아는 건 그뿐이라고 우물거렸다."], ["When I asked him if he knew Count Dracula, and could tell me anything of his castle, both he and his wife crossed themselves, and, saying that they knew nothing at all, simply refused to speak further.", "드라큘라 백작을 아느냐, 그의 성에 대해 무언가 말해줄 수 있느냐고 묻자, 그와 그의 아내는 둘 다 성호를 긋고는, 자기들은 아무것도 모른다고 하면서 더는 입을 떼려 하지 않았다."], ["It was so near the time of starting that I had no time to ask any one else, for it was all very mysterious and not by any means comforting.", "출발 시각이 너무 가까워 다른 사람에게 물어볼 겨를도 없었으니, 이 모든 것이 참으로 알 수 없고 결코 마음 편한 일이 아니었다."]], [["Just before I was leaving, the old lady came up to my room and said in a very hysterical way:", "내가 막 떠나려 할 때, 늙은 부인이 내 방으로 올라와 몹시 신경질적인 투로 말했다."]], [["“Must you go? Oh! young Herr, must you go?”", "\"꼭 가셔야 하나요? 아!"], ["She was in such an excited state that she seemed to have lost her grip of what German she knew, and mixed it all up with some other language which I did not know at all.", "젊은 양반, 꼭 가셔야 해요?\" 그녀가 어찌나 흥분한 상태였던지, 알던 독일어마저 종잡지 못하고 내가 전혀 모르는 다른 언어와 온통 뒤섞어버렸다."], ["I was just able to follow her by asking many questions. When I told her that I must go at once, and that I was engaged on important business, she asked again:", "나는 여러 번 되물은 끝에야 겨우 그녀의 말을 따라잡을 수 있었다. 내가 당장 떠나야 하며 중요한 일로 가는 길이라고 말하자, 그녀는 다시 물었다."]], [["“Do you know what day it is?”", "\"오늘이 무슨 날인지 아세요?\""], ["I answered that it was the fourth of May.", "5월 4일이라고 내가 답했다."], ["She shook her head as she said again:", "그녀는 고개를 저으며 다시 말했다."]], [["“Oh, yes!", "\"아, 그럼요!"], ["I know that!", "그건 저도 알아요!"], ["I know that, but do you know what day it is?”", "그건 알지만, 오늘이 무슨 날인지 아시느냐고요?\""], ["On my saying that I did not understand, she went on:", "무슨 말인지 모르겠다고 하자, 그녀는 말을 이었다."]], [["“It is the eve of St. George’s Day.", "\"오늘은 성 게오르기우스 축일 전야예요."], ["Do you not know that to-night, when the clock strikes midnight, all the evil things in the world will have full sway?", "오늘 밤 자정을 알리는 종이 울리면 세상의 온갖 악한 것들이 활개를 친다는 걸 모르세요?"], ["Do you know where you are going, and what you are going to?”", "어디로 가시는지, 무엇을 향해 가시는지 아시기나 하세요?\""], ["She was in such evident distress that I tried to comfort her, but without effect.", "그녀가 어찌나 애가 타하는 게 분명한지, 나는 그녀를 달래보려 했으나 소용없었다."], ["Finally she went down on her knees and implored me not to go; at least to wait a day or two before starting.", "마침내 그녀는 무릎을 꿇고, 가지 말라고, 적어도 하루 이틀이라도 출발을 미루라고 애원했다."], ["It was all very ridiculous but I did not feel comfortable.", "모두 우스꽝스러운 일이었지만 나는 마음이 편치 않았다."], ["However, there was business to be done, and I could allow nothing to interfere with it.", "그러나 처리해야 할 일이 있었고, 어떤 것도 그것을 방해하게 둘 수는 없었다."], ["I therefore tried to raise her up, and said, as gravely as I could, that I thanked her, but my duty was imperative, and that I must go.", "그래서 나는 그녀를 일으키려 하면서, 되도록 진지하게 고맙다고, 그러나 내 임무는 시급하니 가야만 한다고 말했다."], ["She then rose and dried her eyes, and taking a crucifix from her neck offered it to me.", "그러자 그녀는 일어서서 눈물을 닦고는, 목에서 십자고상(十字苦像)을 풀어 나에게 내밀었다."], ["I did not know what to do, for, as an English Churchman, I have been taught to regard such things as in some measure idolatrous, and yet it seemed so ungracious to refuse an old lady meaning so well and in such a state of mind.", "나는 어찌해야 할지 몰랐으니, 영국 국교회 신자로서 그런 것을 어느 정도 우상숭배적인 물건으로 여기도록 배웠던 까닭이다. 그렇다고 이토록 호의를 품고 또 이런 마음 상태에 있는 늙은 부인의 청을 거절하기란 너무도 매정한 일로 여겨졌다."], ["She saw, I suppose, the doubt in my face, for she put the rosary round my neck, and said, “For your mother’s sake,” and went out of the room.", "짐작건대 그녀는 내 얼굴에 어린 망설임을 보았던 모양으로, 묵주를 내 목에 걸어주며 \"당신 어머니를 봐서라도\"라고 말하고는 방을 나갔다."], ["I am writing up this part of the diary whilst I am waiting for the coach, which is, of course, late; and the crucifix is still round my neck.", "나는 지금 역마차를 기다리며 일기의 이 대목을 적고 있는데, 마차는 물론 늦고 있고, 십자고상은 여전히 내 목에 걸려 있다."], ["Whether it is the old lady’s fear, or the many ghostly traditions of this place, or the crucifix itself, I do not know, but I am not feeling nearly as easy in my mind as usual.", "그 늙은 부인의 두려움 탓인지, 이 고장의 숱한 유령 전설 탓인지, 아니면 십자고상 그 자체 탓인지는 모르겠으나, 나는 평소만큼 마음이 편하지가 않다."], ["If this book should ever reach Mina before I do, let it bring my good-bye. Here comes the coach!", "만일 이 책이 나보다 먼저 미나에게 닿거든, 부디 내 작별 인사를 전해주기를. 마차가 온다!"]], [["5 May. The Castle.--The grey of the morning has passed, and the sun is high over the distant horizon, which seems jagged, whether with trees or hills I know not, for it is so far off that big things and little are mixed.", "5월 5일. 성(城). — 아침의 잿빛은 가시고, 해가 먼 지평선 위로 높이 떴는데, 그 지평선이 들쭉날쭉한 것이 나무 탓인지 언덕 탓인지는 알 수 없다."], ["I am not sleepy, and, as I am not to be called till I awake, naturally I write till sleep comes.", "너무 멀어서 큰 것과 작은 것이 한데 뒤섞여 보이기 때문이다. 졸리지도 않고, 내가 깰 때까지 깨우지 말라고 했으니, 자연히 잠이 올 때까지 글을 쓴다."], ["There are many odd things to put down, and, lest who reads them may fancy that I dined too well before I left Bistritz, let me put down my dinner exactly.", "적어둘 이상한 일들이 많은데, 이 글을 읽는 이가 내가 비스트리차를 떠나기 전에 식사를 너무 잘한 탓이라 여길까 봐, 내 저녁식사를 정확히 적어둔다."], ["I dined on what they called “robber steak”--bits of bacon, onion, and beef, seasoned with red pepper, and strung on sticks and roasted over the fire, in the simple style of the London cat’s meat!", "나는 그들이 \"강도 스테이크\"라 부르는 것을 먹었는데 — 베이컨과 양파, 쇠고기 조각에 붉은 고추로 양념하여 꼬챙이에 꿰어 불에 구운 것으로, 런던의 고양이 먹이만큼이나 단순한 방식이었다!"], ["The wine was Golden Mediasch, which produces a queer sting on the tongue, which is, however, not disagreeable.", "포도주는 골든 메디아슈였는데, 혀에 묘하게 톡 쏘는 맛을 남겼지만 불쾌하지는 않았다."], ["I had only a couple of glasses of this, and nothing else.", "나는 이것을 두어 잔밖에 마시지 않았고, 다른 것은 들지 않았다."]], [["When I got on the coach the driver had not taken his seat, and I saw him talking with the landlady.", "내가 마차에 올랐을 때 마부는 아직 자리에 앉지 않은 채 여주인과 이야기를 나누고 있었다."], ["They were evidently talking of me, for every now and then they looked at me, and some of the people who were sitting on the bench outside the door--which they call by a name meaning “word-bearer”--came and listened, and then looked at me, most of them pityingly.", "분명 내 이야기를 하고 있었으니, 이따금 나를 쳐다보았기 때문이다. 그리고 문밖의 긴 의자에 — 그들은 그것을 \"말 전하는 사람\"이라는 뜻의 이름으로 불렀다 — 앉아 있던 몇몇 사람들이 다가와 귀를 기울이더니, 나를 바라보았는데, 대부분 가엾다는 표정이었다."], ["I could hear a lot of words often repeated, queer words, for there were many nationalities in the crowd; so I quietly got my polyglot dictionary from my bag and looked them out.", "자주 되풀이되는 말이 여럿 들렸는데, 군중 가운데 여러 민족이 섞여 있었으므로 기이한 말들이었다. 그래서 나는 가방에서 여러 나라 말 사전을 슬그머니 꺼내어 찾아보았다."], ["I must say they were not cheering to me, for amongst them were “Ordog”--Satan, “pokol”--hell, “stregoica”--witch, “vrolok”", "솔직히 말하건대 그 말들은 나에게 위안이 되지 못했으니, 그 가운데에는 \"오르도그\" — 사탄, \"포콜\" — 지옥, \"스트레고이카\""], ["and “vlkoslak”--both of which mean the same thing, one being Slovak and the other Servian for something that is either were-wolf or vampire.", "— 마녀, \"브롤로크\"와 \"블코슬라크\" — 둘 다 같은 뜻으로, 하나는 슬로바키아어, 다른 하나는 세르비아어로 늑대인간 혹은 흡혈귀를 뜻하는 말이 있었기 때문이다. (비망."], ["(Mem., I must ask the Count about these superstitions)", "이 미신들에 관해 백작에게 물어볼 것.)"]], [["When we started, the crowd round the inn door, which had by this time swelled to a considerable size, all made the sign of the cross and pointed two fingers towards me.", "우리가 출발할 때, 그 무렵에는 상당히 불어나 있던 여관 문 앞의 군중이 모두 성호를 긋고 나를 향해 두 손가락을 내밀었다."], ["With some difficulty I got a fellow-passenger to tell me what they meant; he would not answer at first, but on learning that I was English, he explained that it was a charm or guard against the evil eye.", "나는 어렵사리 동행 한 사람을 붙들어 그것이 무슨 뜻인지 물었다. 그는 처음에는 답하려 하지 않았으나, 내가 영국인임을 알자 그것이 흉안(凶眼)을 막는 부적이나 방패라고 설명해주었다."], ["This was not very pleasant for me, just starting for an unknown place to meet an unknown man; but every one seemed so kind-hearted, and so sorrowful, and so sympathetic that I could not but be touched.", "알지 못하는 곳으로, 알지 못하는 사람을 만나러 막 떠나는 나에게 그리 유쾌한 일은 아니었다. 그러나 다들 어찌나 마음씨가 곱고, 어찌나 애처로워하고, 어찌나 동정 어린 표정이었던지 나는 감동하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["I shall never forget the last glimpse which I had of the inn-yard and its crowd of picturesque figures, all crossing themselves, as they stood round the wide archway, with its background of rich foliage of oleander and orange trees in green tubs clustered in the centre of the yard.", "여관 마당과 그 안에 그림처럼 모여 선 사람들의 무리가, 안마당 한가운데 녹색 화분에 무리지어 심긴 협죽도와 오렌지 나무의 무성한 잎을 배경으로, 넓은 아치문 둘레에 서서 모두 성호를 긋던 그 마지막 광경을 나는 결코 잊지 못할 것이다."], ["Then our driver, whose wide linen drawers covered the whole front of the box-seat--“gotza” they call them--cracked his big whip over his four small horses, which ran abreast, and we set off on our journey.", "그때 우리 마부가 — 그의 넓은 아마포 바지가 마부석 앞면을 온통 덮고 있었는데, 그들은 그것을 \"고차\"라 부른다 — 나란히 달리는 네 마리 작은 말 위로 큰 채찍을 휘둘러 소리를 내자, 우리는 여정에 올랐다."]], [["I soon lost sight and recollection of ghostly fears in the beauty of the scene as we drove along, although had I known the language, or rather languages, which my fellow-passengers were speaking, I might not have been able to throw them off so easily.", "마차가 나아가는 동안 풍경의 아름다움 속에서 나는 곧 유령 같은 두려움을 보지도, 기억하지도 못하게 되었다. 하기야 동행들이 쓰는 언어를, 아니 언어들을 알아들었더라면 그토록 쉽게 떨쳐내지는 못했을지도 모른다."], ["Before us lay a green sloping land full of forests and woods, with here and there steep hills, crowned with clumps of trees or with farmhouses, the blank gable end to the road.", "우리 앞에는 숲과 삼림이 가득한 푸르게 비탈진 땅이 펼쳐졌고, 군데군데 가파른 언덕이 나무 무더기나 농가로 꼭대기를 이루고 있었는데, 농가의 휑한 박공 끝이 길을 향하고 있었다."], ["There was everywhere a bewildering mass of fruit blossom--apple, plum, pear, cherry; and as we drove by I could see the green grass under the trees spangled with the fallen petals.", "어디에나 사과, 자두, 배, 벚나무 등 과일꽃이 어지러울 만큼 무더기로 피어 있었고, 지나가면서 보니 나무 아래 푸른 잔디가 떨어진 꽃잎으로 점점이 수놓여 있었다."], ["In and out amongst these green hills of what they call here the “Mittel Land” ran the road, losing itself as it swept round the grassy curve, or was shut out by the straggling ends of pine woods, which here and there ran down the hillsides like tongues of flame.", "이곳에서 \"미텔 란트\"라 부르는 이 푸른 언덕들 사이로 길이 들고 나며, 잔디 덮인 굽이를 돌아 사라지기도 하고, 군데군데 불길의 혀처럼 산비탈을 타고 내려온 소나무 숲의 들쭉날쭉한 끝자락에 가려지기도 했다."], ["The road was rugged, but still we seemed to fly over it with a feverish haste.", "길은 험했으나, 그래도 우리는 열에 들뜬 듯 서둘러 그 위를 날듯이 달리는 것 같았다."], ["I could not understand then what the haste meant, but the driver was evidently bent on losing no time in reaching Borgo Prund.", "그 서두름이 무엇을 뜻하는지 그때는 알 수 없었으나, 마부는 분명 보르고 프룬트에 닿기까지 한시도 지체하지 않으려는 작정이었다."], ["I was told that this road is in summertime excellent, but that it had not yet been put in order after the winter snows.", "듣자 하니 이 길은 여름철에는 더없이 좋으나, 겨울 눈이 내린 뒤로 아직 정비되지 않았다고 했다."], ["In this respect it is different from the general run of roads in the Carpathians, for it is an old tradition that they are not to be kept in too good order.", "이 점에서 이 길은 카르파티아의 보통 길들과는 다른데, 길을 너무 좋게 관리해서는 안 된다는 오랜 전통이 있기 때문이다."], ["Of old the Hospadars would not repair them, lest the Turk should think that they were preparing to bring in foreign troops, and so hasten the war which was always really at loading point.", "옛날에 호스파다르들은 길을 보수하려 하지 않았으니, 터키인들이 그들이 외국 군대를 끌어들일 채비를 한다고 여겨, 언제나 정작 일촉즉발의 상태에 있던 전쟁을 재촉하지나 않을까 해서였다."]], [["Beyond the green swelling hills of the Mittel Land rose mighty slopes of forest up to the lofty steeps of the Carpathians themselves.", "미텔 란트의 푸르게 부풀어오른 언덕들 너머로는 카르파티아 산맥 자체의 드높은 가파른 봉우리까지 거대한 숲의 비탈이 솟아 있었다."], ["Right and left of us they towered, with the afternoon sun falling full upon them and bringing out all the glorious colours of this beautiful range, deep blue and purple in the shadows of the peaks, green and brown where grass and rock mingled, and an endless perspective of jagged rock and pointed crags, till these were themselves lost in the distance, where the snowy peaks rose grandly.", "그것들은 우리 좌우로 우뚝 솟아, 오후의 햇살을 가득 받으며 이 아름다운 산맥의 온갖 영광스러운 빛깔을 드러냈다. 봉우리들의 그늘진 곳은 짙은 청색과 보라색, 풀과 바위가 뒤섞인 곳은 초록과 갈색이었고, 들쭉날쭉한 바위와 뾰족한 절벽이 끝없이 이어지다가, 마침내 그것들 자체도 멀리 사라지는 그곳에서는 눈 덮인 봉우리들이 장엄하게 솟아 있었다."], ["Here and there seemed mighty rifts in the mountains, through which, as the sun began to sink, we saw now and again the white gleam of falling water.", "군데군데 산에 거대한 틈이 벌어진 듯한 곳이 보였고, 해가 기울기 시작하면서 그 틈으로 이따금 떨어지는 물줄기의 흰 빛이 반짝이는 것이 보였다."], ["One of my companions touched my arm as we swept round the base of a hill and opened up the lofty, snow-covered peak of a mountain, which seemed, as we wound on our serpentine way, to be right before us:--", "우리가 어느 언덕 기슭을 돌아 나오자, 우리가 구불구불한 길을 따라 나아갈 때 바로 앞에 있는 듯이 보이는, 눈 덮인 드높은 산봉우리가 모습을 드러냈는데, 그때 동행 하나가 내 팔을 건드렸다."]], [["“Look!", "\"보시오!"], ["Isten szek!”--“God’s seat!”--and he crossed himself reverently.", "이슈텐 세크!\" — \"신의 옥좌!\" — 그러고 나서 그는 경건하게 성호를 그었다."]], [["As we wound on our endless way, and the sun sank lower and lower behind us, the shadows of the evening began to creep round us.", "우리가 끝없는 길을 따라 구불구불 나아가고, 해가 우리 뒤로 점점 더 낮게 가라앉으면서, 저녁의 그림자가 우리 주위로 기어들기 시작했다."], ["This was emphasised by the fact that the snowy mountain-top still held the sunset, and seemed to glow out with a delicate cool pink.", "눈 덮인 산꼭대기는 여전히 석양을 머금어 은은하고 서늘한 분홍빛으로 빛나는 듯했으므로, 그 어둠은 더욱 도드라졌다."], ["Here and there we passed Cszeks and Slovaks, all in picturesque attire, but I noticed that goitre was painfully prevalent.", "군데군데 우리는 체히인과 슬로바키아인을 지나쳤는데, 모두 그림 같은 차림이었으나, 갑상선종(甲狀腺腫)이 가슴 아플 만큼 흔한 것이 눈에 띄었다."], ["By the roadside were many crosses, and as we swept by, my companions all crossed themselves.", "길가에는 십자가가 많이 서 있었고, 우리가 지나칠 때마다 동행들은 모두 성호를 그었다."], ["Here and there was a peasant man or woman kneeling before a shrine, who did not even turn round as we approached, but seemed in the self-surrender of devotion to have neither eyes nor ears for the outer world.", "군데군데 한 농부 남자나 여자가 사당 앞에 무릎 꿇고 있었는데, 우리가 다가가도 돌아보지조차 않았고, 신앙에 자신을 온통 내맡긴 나머지 바깥세상에 대해서는 눈도 귀도 없는 듯했다."], ["There were many things new to me: for instance, hay-ricks in the trees, and here and there very beautiful masses of weeping birch, their white stems shining like silver through the delicate green of the leaves.", "나에게 새로운 것이 많았으니, 이를테면 나무 위에 얹어둔 건초더미라든가, 군데군데 매우 아름답게 무리진 능수자작나무는 그 흰 줄기가 여린 초록 잎새 사이로 은빛처럼 반짝였다."], ["Now and again we passed a leiter-wagon--the ordinary peasant’s cart--with its long, snake-like vertebra, calculated to suit the inequalities of the road.", "이따금 우리는 라이터바겐 — 흔한 농부의 수레 — 을 지나쳤는데, 그 길고 뱀 같은 척추 모양의 차체는 길의 울퉁불퉁함에 맞도록 고안된 것이었다."], ["On this were sure to be seated quite a group of home-coming peasants, the Cszeks with their white, and the Slovaks with their coloured, sheepskins, the latter carrying lance-fashion their long staves, with axe at end.", "그 위에는 으레 집으로 돌아가는 농부들이 한 무리 올라타 있었는데, 체히인은 흰 양가죽을, 슬로바키아인은 색색의 양가죽을 둘렀고, 후자는 끝에 도끼가 달린 긴 막대기를 창처럼 들고 있었다."], ["As the evening fell it began to get very cold, and the growing twilight seemed to merge into one dark mistiness the gloom of the trees, oak, beech, and pine, though in the valleys which ran deep between the spurs of the hills, as we ascended through the Pass, the dark firs stood out here and there against the background of late-lying snow.", "저녁이 내리면서 몹시 추워지기 시작했고, 짙어가는 황혼은 떡갈나무, 너도밤나무, 소나무의 음울함을 하나의 어두운 안개 속으로 녹여내는 듯했다. 다만 우리가 고개를 따라 오르는 동안, 언덕 줄기 사이로 깊게 뻗은 골짜기에서는 검은 전나무들이 늦게까지 남은 눈을 배경으로 군데군데 우뚝 솟아 있었다."], ["Sometimes, as the road was cut through the pine woods that seemed in the darkness to be closing down upon us, great masses of greyness, which here and there bestrewed the trees, produced a peculiarly weird and solemn effect, which carried on the thoughts and grim fancies engendered earlier in the evening, when the falling sunset threw into strange relief the ghost-like clouds which amongst the Carpathians seem to wind ceaselessly through the valleys.", "이따금 길이 소나무 숲을 가로질러 뚫려 있었는데, 어둠 속에서 그 숲이 우리를 향해 내려덮이는 듯했고, 군데군데 나무를 뒤덮은 거대한 잿빛 덩어리가 묘하게 으스스하고 엄숙한 느낌을 자아내어, 그날 저녁 일찍 — 기우는 석양이 카르파티아 골짜기 사이로 끊임없이 휘감겨 도는 듯한 유령 같은 구름을 기이하게 두드러지게 했을 때 — 떠올랐던 생각과 음산한 공상을 이어가게 했다. 때로는 언덕이 어찌나 가파른지, 마부가 서두르는데도 말들은 천천히 갈 수밖에 없었다."], ["Sometimes the hills were so steep that, despite our driver’s haste, the horses could only go slowly.", "나는 고향에서 하듯 내려서 걸어 올라가고 싶었으나, 마부는 들으려고도 하지 않았다. \"안 됩니다, 안 돼요,\""], ["I wished to get down and walk up them, as we do at home, but the driver would not hear of it.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"여기서 걸으시면 안 됩니다. 개들이 너무 사납거든요.\""], ["“No, no,” he said; “you must not walk here; the dogs are too fierce”; and then he added, with what he evidently meant for grim pleasantry--for he looked round to catch the approving smile of the rest--“and you may have enough of such matters before you go to sleep.”", "그러고는 분명 음산한 농담이랍시고 — 다른 이들의 동의하는 미소를 구하려는 듯 둘러보았으니 — 덧붙였다. \"그리고 잠자리에 들기 전에 그런 일이라면 진저리나게 겪게 될지도 모르지요.\""], ["The only stop he would make was a moment’s pause to light his lamps.", "그가 멈춘 것은 등불을 켜기 위해 잠시 쉰 것뿐이었다."]], [["When it grew dark there seemed to be some excitement amongst the passengers, and they kept speaking to him, one after the other, as though urging him to further speed.", "날이 어두워지자 승객들 사이에 어떤 동요가 이는 듯했고, 그들은 더 속력을 내라고 재촉하기라도 하듯 차례차례 마부에게 말을 걸었다."], ["He lashed the horses unmercifully with his long whip, and with wild cries of encouragement urged them on to further exertions.", "그는 긴 채찍으로 말들을 무자비하게 후려치며, 거친 격려의 외침으로 말들을 몰아 더욱 분발하게 했다."], ["Then through the darkness I could see a sort of patch of grey light ahead of us, as though there were a cleft in the hills.", "그러자 어둠 너머로 우리 앞쪽에 잿빛 빛이 비치는 듯한 자리가 보였는데, 마치 언덕에 갈라진 틈이 있는 것 같았다."], ["The excitement of the passengers grew greater; the crazy coach rocked on its great leather springs, and swayed like a boat tossed on a stormy sea. I had to hold on.", "승객들의 동요는 더 커졌고, 낡은 마차는 커다란 가죽 용수철 위에서 흔들리며, 거친 바다에 내던져진 배처럼 출렁였다. 나는 붙들고 있어야 했다."], ["The road grew more level, and we appeared to fly along.", "길이 더 평탄해지더니, 우리는 날듯이 달리는 것 같았다."], ["Then the mountains seemed to come nearer to us on each side and to frown down upon us; we were entering on the Borgo Pass.", "그러더니 산들이 양편에서 우리에게 더 가까이 다가와 우리를 내려다보며 노려보는 듯했다. 우리는 보르고 고개로 들어서고 있었다."], ["One by one several of the passengers offered me gifts, which they pressed upon me with an earnestness which would take no denial; these were certainly of an odd and varied kind, but each was given in simple good faith, with a kindly word, and a blessing, and that strange mixture of fear-meaning movements which I had seen outside the hotel at Bistritz--the sign of the cross and the guard against the evil eye.", "승객들이 하나둘 나에게 선물을 내밀었는데, 그것들을 거절을 용납하지 않을 만큼 간곡한 태도로 안겨주었다. 그것들은 분명 기이하고 가지각색이었으나, 하나하나가 소박한 선의로, 다정한 말과 축복과 함께, 그리고 비스트리차의 호텔 밖에서 보았던 그 두려움을 뜻하는 몸짓들의 기이한 뒤섞임 — 성호를 긋는 것과 흉안을 막는 손짓 — 과 함께 건네진 것이었다."], ["Then, as we flew along, the driver leaned forward, and on each side the passengers, craning over the edge of the coach, peered eagerly into the darkness.", "그러더니 우리가 날듯이 달리는 동안, 마부는 몸을 앞으로 기울였고, 양편의 승객들은 마차 가장자리 너머로 목을 빼고 어둠 속을 열심히 들여다보았다."], ["It was evident that something very exciting was either happening or expected, but though I asked each passenger, no one would give me the slightest explanation.", "무언가 매우 흥분되는 일이 벌어지고 있거나 곧 일어나리라는 것이 분명했으나, 승객마다 물어보아도 누구 하나 나에게 조금도 설명해주려 하지 않았다."], ["This state of excitement kept on for some little time; and at last we saw before us the Pass opening out on the eastern side.", "이런 흥분 상태는 한동안 이어졌고, 마침내 우리 앞에 고개가 동쪽으로 트이는 것이 보였다."], ["There were dark, rolling clouds overhead, and in the air the heavy, oppressive sense of thunder.", "머리 위에는 검은 구름이 굽이치며 떠 있었고, 공기 중에는 천둥을 머금은 무겁고 답답한 기운이 감돌았다."], ["It seemed as though the mountain range had separated two atmospheres, and that now we had got into the thunderous one.", "마치 산맥이 두 개의 대기를 갈라놓았고, 이제 우리가 천둥이 이는 쪽으로 들어선 듯했다."], ["I was now myself looking out for the conveyance which was to take me to the Count.", "이제 나는 나를 백작에게 데려다줄 마차를 직접 살펴보고 있었다."], ["Each moment I expected to see the glare of lamps through the blackness; but all was dark.", "매 순간 어둠 너머로 등불의 불빛이 보이기를 기대했으나, 사방이 어두웠다."], ["The only light was the flickering rays of our own lamps, in which the steam from our hard-driven horses rose in a white cloud.", "유일한 빛은 우리 마차 등불의 깜박이는 광선뿐으로, 그 빛 속에서 거세게 몰린 말들의 김이 흰 구름처럼 피어올랐다."], ["We could see now the sandy road lying white before us, but there was on it no sign of a vehicle.", "이제 우리 앞에 모랫길이 희끄무레하게 뻗어 있는 것이 보였으나, 그 위에 마차의 흔적은 없었다."], ["The passengers drew back with a sigh of gladness, which seemed to mock my own disappointment.", "승객들은 안도의 한숨을 내쉬며 몸을 뒤로 젖혔는데, 그 모습이 마치 나 자신의 실망을 조롱하는 것 같았다."], ["I was already thinking what I had best do, when the driver, looking at his watch, said to the others something which I could hardly hear, it was spoken so quietly and in so low a tone; I thought it was “An hour less than the time.”", "내가 이제 어찌하는 게 최선일지 궁리하고 있을 때, 마부가 시계를 보더니 다른 이들에게 무어라 말했는데, 어찌나 조용하고 낮은 소리로 말했던지 거의 알아들을 수 없었다. \"예정보다 한 시간 이르군\"이라고 한 것 같았다."], ["Then turning to me, he said in German worse than my own:--", "그러더니 나를 돌아보며 내 것보다 더 서툰 독일어로 말했다."]], [["“There is no carriage here.", "\"여기엔 마차가 없습니다."], ["The Herr is not expected after all.", "결국 나리는 기다려지지 않는 모양입니다."], ["He will now come on to Bukovina, and return to-morrow or the next day; better the next day.”", "이제 부코비나까지 갔다가, 내일이나 모레 돌아오시는 게 좋겠어요. 모레가 더 낫겠지요.\""], ["Whilst he was speaking the horses began to neigh and snort and plunge wildly, so that the driver had to hold them up.", "그가 말하는 동안 말들이 울부짖고 콧김을 내뿜으며 거칠게 날뛰기 시작해서 마부가 붙들어야 했다."], ["Then, amongst a chorus of screams from the peasants and a universal crossing of themselves, a calèche, with four horses, drove up behind us, overtook us, and drew up beside the coach.", "그때, 농부들의 비명 소리가 한바탕 일고 모두가 성호를 긋는 가운데, 네 마리 말이 끄는 칼레슈 한 대가 우리 뒤에서 다가와 우리를 따라잡더니 마차 옆에 멈춰 섰다."], ["I could see from the flash of our lamps, as the rays fell on them, that the horses were coal-black and splendid animals.", "우리 등불의 빛이 말들 위로 비치며 번뜩였는데, 그 빛에 그것들이 칠흑같이 검은, 훌륭한 짐승들임을 알 수 있었다."], ["They were driven by a tall man, with a long brown beard and a great black hat, which seemed to hide his face from us.", "마차를 모는 이는 키 큰 남자로, 긴 갈색 수염에 큼직한 검은 모자를 썼는데, 그 모자가 우리에게서 그의 얼굴을 가리는 듯했다."], ["I could only see the gleam of a pair of very bright eyes, which seemed red in the lamplight, as he turned to us.", "그가 우리 쪽으로 몸을 돌렸을 때, 등불 빛에 붉게 보이는 매우 빛나는 한 쌍의 눈이 번뜩이는 것만 겨우 보였다."], ["He said to the driver:--", "그가 마부에게 말했다."]], [["“You are early to-night, my friend.”", "\"오늘 밤은 일찍 왔군, 친구.\""], ["The man stammered in reply:--", "마부가 더듬거리며 대답했다."]], [["“The English Herr was in a hurry,”", "\"영국 나리께서 급하셨거든요.\""], ["to which the stranger replied:--", "그러자 낯선 이가 대답했다."]], [["“That is why, I suppose, you wished him to go on to Bukovina.", "\"그래서 자네가 이분을 부코비나까지 모셔가게 하고 싶었던 거겠지."], ["You cannot deceive me, my friend; I know too much, and my horses are swift.”", "나를 속일 수는 없네, 친구. 나는 너무 많이 알고, 내 말들은 빠르니까.\""], ["As he spoke he smiled, and the lamplight fell on a hard-looking mouth, with very red lips and sharp-looking teeth, as white as ivory.", "그가 말하면서 미소 짓자, 등불 빛이 단단해 보이는 입매를 비추었는데, 입술은 무척 붉고 날카로워 보이는 이는 상아처럼 희었다."], ["One of my companions whispered to another the line from Burger’s “Lenore”:--", "내 동행 가운데 하나가 다른 이에게 뷔르거의 「레노레」의 한 구절을 속삭였다."]], [["“Denn die Todten reiten schnell”-- (“For the dead travel fast.”)", "\"덴 디 토텐 라이텐 슈넬\" — (\"죽은 자는 빨리 달리는 법이니.\")"]], [["The strange driver evidently heard the words, for he looked up with a gleaming smile. The passenger turned his face away, at the same time putting out his two fingers and crossing himself.", "그 낯선 마부는 분명 그 말을 들었으니, 번뜩이는 미소를 지으며 고개를 들었기 때문이다. 그 승객은 얼굴을 돌리는 동시에 두 손가락을 내밀고 성호를 그었다."], ["“Give me the Herr’s luggage,”", "\"나리의 짐을 이리 주게,\""], ["said the driver; and with exceeding alacrity my bags were handed out and put in the calèche.", "하고 마부가 말했다. 그러자 무척 잽싸게 내 가방들이 건네져 칼레슈에 실렸다."], ["Then I descended from the side of the coach, as the calèche was close alongside, the driver helping me with a hand which caught my arm in a grip of steel; his strength must have been prodigious.", "칼레슈가 바로 옆에 붙어 있었으므로, 나는 마차 옆에서 내려섰는데, 마부가 강철 같은 힘으로 내 팔을 움켜쥐며 손을 내밀어 거들어주었다. 그의 힘은 어마어마한 것임에 틀림없었다."], ["Without a word he shook his reins, the horses turned, and we swept into the darkness of the Pass.", "그는 말없이 고삐를 흔들었고, 말들이 방향을 틀자 우리는 고개의 어둠 속으로 휩쓸려 들어갔다."], ["As I looked back I saw the steam from the horses of the coach by the light of the lamps, and projected against it the figures of my late companions crossing themselves.", "뒤돌아보니 등불 빛에 마차 말들의 김이 보였고, 그것을 배경으로 좀 전의 동행들이 성호를 긋는 모습이 비쳤다."], ["Then the driver cracked his whip and called to his horses, and off they swept on their way to Bukovina.", "그러더니 마부가 채찍을 휘둘러 소리를 내며 말들을 부르자, 그들은 부코비나를 향한 길을 휩쓸 듯 떠나갔다."], ["As they sank into the darkness I felt a strange chill, and a lonely feeling came over me; but a cloak was thrown over my shoulders, and a rug across my knees, and the driver said in excellent German:--", "그들이 어둠 속으로 가라앉아 사라지자 나는 기묘한 한기를 느꼈고, 외로운 기분이 엄습했다. 그러나 망토가 내 어깨에 걸쳐지고, 무릎에 깔개가 덮였으며, 마부가 훌륭한 독일어로 말했다."]], [["“The night is chill, mein Herr, and my master the Count bade me take all care of you.", "\"밤이 쌀쌀합니다, 나리. 제 주인이신 백작님께서 나리를 극진히 보살피라 분부하셨습니다."], ["There is a flask of slivovitz (the plum brandy of the country) underneath the seat, if you should require it.”", "좌석 아래에 슬리보비츠(이 나라의 자두 브랜디) 한 병이 있으니, 필요하시면 드십시오.\""], ["I did not take any, but it was a comfort to know it was there all the same.", "나는 마시지 않았으나, 그것이 거기 있다는 것을 아는 것만으로도 위안이 되었다."], ["I felt a little strangely, and not a little frightened.", "나는 약간 기묘한 기분이었고, 적잖이 겁이 났다."], ["I think had there been any alternative I should have taken it, instead of prosecuting that unknown night journey.", "만일 다른 방도가 있었더라면, 그 알 수 없는 밤 여정을 강행하느니 차라리 그쪽을 택했을 것이다."], ["The carriage went at a hard pace straight along, then we made a complete turn and went along another straight road.", "마차는 곧장 빠른 속도로 나아갔고, 그러다 우리는 완전히 한 바퀴 돌아 또 다른 곧은 길을 따라 나아갔다."], ["It seemed to me that we were simply going over and over the same ground again; and so I took note of some salient point, and found that this was so.", "내 생각에는 우리가 그저 같은 곳을 몇 번이고 돌고 있는 것 같았다. 그래서 나는 두드러진 지점 하나를 기억해두었고, 과연 그러함을 알았다."], ["I would have liked to have asked the driver what this all meant, but I really feared to do so, for I thought that, placed as I was, any protest would have had no effect in case there had been an intention to delay.", "마부에게 이게 다 무슨 영문인지 묻고 싶었으나, 정말이지 그러기가 두려웠으니, 내 처지에서는 만일 지체시키려는 의도가 있다면 어떤 항의도 소용없으리라 생각했기 때문이다."], ["By-and-by, however, as I was curious to know how time was passing, I struck a match, and by its flame looked at my watch; it was within a few minutes of midnight.", "그러나 이윽고 나는 시간이 어떻게 흐르고 있는지 궁금하여 성냥을 켰고, 그 불빛으로 시계를 보았다. 자정이 몇 분 남지 않았다."], ["This gave me a sort of shock, for I suppose the general superstition about midnight was increased by my recent experiences.", "이것이 나에게 일종의 충격을 주었으니, 짐작건대 자정에 관한 흔한 미신이 최근의 경험으로 더 부풀려졌던 모양이다."], ["I waited with a sick feeling of suspense.", "나는 메스꺼운 긴장 속에서 기다렸다."]], [["Then a dog began to howl somewhere in a farmhouse far down the road--a long, agonised wailing, as if from fear.", "그때 길 저 아래 어느 농가에서 개 한 마리가 울부짖기 시작했다 — 마치 두려움에 사로잡힌 듯, 길고 고통스러운 통곡이었다."], ["The sound was taken up by another dog, and then another and another, till, borne on the wind which now sighed softly through the Pass, a wild howling began, which seemed to come from all over the country, as far as the imagination could grasp it through the gloom of the night.", "그 소리를 다른 개가 받았고, 또 다른 개가, 또 다른 개가 받더니, 이제 고개 사이로 부드럽게 한숨짓듯 부는 바람을 타고, 거친 울부짖음이 일기 시작했다. 그것은 밤의 어둠을 통해 상상이 미칠 수 있는 데까지, 온 고장에서 들려오는 듯했다."], ["At the first howl the horses began to strain and rear, but the driver spoke to them soothingly, and they quieted down, but shivered and sweated as though after a runaway from sudden fright.", "첫 울부짖음에 말들이 안달하며 앞발을 들고 일어서기 시작했으나, 마부가 달래듯 말을 건네자 진정되었다. 그래도 갑작스러운 공포에 내달리다 멈춘 듯 부르르 떨며 땀을 흘렸다."], ["Then, far off in the distance, from the mountains on each side of us began a louder and a sharper howling--that of wolves--which affected both the horses and myself in the same way--for I was minded to jump from the calèche and run, whilst they reared again and plunged madly, so that the driver had to use all his great strength to keep them from bolting.", "그러더니 멀리, 우리 양편의 산에서 더 크고 더 날카로운 울부짖음이 — 늑대들의 울부짖음이 — 일기 시작했는데, 그것은 말들과 나에게 똑같은 영향을 끼쳤다. 나는 칼레슈에서 뛰어내려 달아나고 싶었고, 말들은 다시 앞발을 들고 일어서며 미친 듯이 날뛰어, 마부가 그 엄청난 힘을 다해야 말들이 내빼는 것을 막을 수 있었다."], ["In a few minutes, however, my own ears got accustomed to the sound, and the horses so far became quiet that the driver was able to descend and to stand before them.", "그러나 몇 분 지나자 내 귀도 그 소리에 익숙해졌고, 말들도 어느 정도 진정되어 마부가 내려서서 그 앞에 설 수 있게 되었다."], ["He petted and soothed them, and whispered something in their ears, as I have heard of horse-tamers doing, and with extraordinary effect, for under his caresses they became quite manageable again, though they still trembled.", "그는 말들을 어르고 달래며 그 귀에 무언가 속삭였는데, 말 길들이는 이들이 그리한다고 들은 적이 있었거니와, 그 효과는 놀라웠다. 그의 어루만짐을 받자 말들은 여전히 떨고는 있었으나 다시 꽤 다루기 쉬워졌기 때문이다."], ["The driver again took his seat, and shaking his reins, started off at a great pace.", "마부는 다시 자리에 앉아 고삐를 흔들며 빠른 속도로 출발했다."], ["This time, after going to the far side of the Pass, he suddenly turned down a narrow roadway which ran sharply to the right.", "이번에는 고개의 먼 쪽까지 가더니, 갑자기 오른쪽으로 가파르게 꺾이는 좁은 길로 접어들었다."]], [["Soon we were hemmed in with trees, which in places arched right over the roadway till we passed as through a tunnel; and again great frowning rocks guarded us boldly on either side.", "이내 우리는 나무들에 둘러싸였는데, 그 나무들이 군데군데 길 위로 아치를 이루어, 우리는 마치 터널을 지나듯 나아갔다. 그리고 다시 노려보는 듯한 거대한 바위들이 양편에서 우리를 굳건히 호위했다."], ["Though we were in shelter, we could hear the rising wind, for it moaned and whistled through the rocks, and the branches of the trees crashed together as we swept along.", "우리는 가려진 곳에 있었으나, 이는 바람이 점점 거세지는 소리를 들을 수 있었으니, 바람은 바위 사이로 신음하고 휘파람 소리를 냈고, 우리가 휩쓸 듯 지나가는 동안 나뭇가지들이 서로 부딪쳐 우지끈거렸다."], ["It grew colder and colder still, and fine, powdery snow began to fall, so that soon we and all around us were covered with a white blanket.", "점점 더 추워지더니, 곱고 가루 같은 눈이 내리기 시작해, 이내 우리와 우리 주위의 모든 것이 흰 담요에 덮였다."], ["The keen wind still carried the howling of the dogs, though this grew fainter as we went on our way.", "매서운 바람은 여전히 개들의 울부짖음을 실어 날랐으나, 우리가 길을 나아갈수록 그 소리는 점점 희미해졌다."], ["The baying of the wolves sounded nearer and nearer, as though they were closing round on us from every side.", "늑대들의 울부짖음은 마치 사방에서 우리를 에워싸며 좁혀 오기라도 하듯 점점 더 가까이 들려왔다."], ["I grew dreadfully afraid, and the horses shared my fear.", "나는 끔찍하게 겁이 났고, 말들도 내 두려움을 함께 느꼈다."], ["The driver, however, was not in the least disturbed; he kept turning his head to left and right, but I could not see anything through the darkness.", "그러나 마부는 조금도 동요하지 않았다. 그는 줄곧 고개를 좌우로 돌렸으나, 나는 어둠 속에서 아무것도 볼 수 없었다."]], [["Suddenly, away on our left, I saw a faint flickering blue flame.", "갑자기 우리 왼편 저쪽에서, 희미하게 깜박이는 푸른 불꽃이 보였다."], ["The driver saw it at the same moment; he at once checked the horses, and, jumping to the ground, disappeared into the darkness.", "마부도 동시에 그것을 보았다. 그는 즉시 말들을 멈추더니, 땅으로 뛰어내려 어둠 속으로 사라졌다."], ["I did not know what to do, the less as the howling of the wolves grew closer; but while I wondered the driver suddenly appeared again, and without a word took his seat, and we resumed our journey.", "늑대들의 울부짖음이 점점 가까워지는 터라 더더욱 나는 어찌해야 할지 몰랐다. 그러나 내가 어리둥절해하는 동안 마부가 갑자기 다시 나타나, 말없이 자리에 앉았고, 우리는 여정을 재개했다."], ["I think I must have fallen asleep and kept dreaming of the incident, for it seemed to be repeated endlessly, and now looking back, it is like a sort of awful nightmare.", "내 생각에는 잠이 들어 그 일을 계속 꿈꾼 모양인데, 그 일이 끝없이 되풀이되는 것 같았다. 이제 돌이켜보면 그것은 일종의 끔찍한 악몽 같다."], ["Once the flame appeared so near the road, that even in the darkness around us I could watch the driver’s motions.", "한번은 그 불꽃이 길 가까이 나타나, 우리 주위의 어둠 속에서도 나는 마부의 동작을 지켜볼 수 있었다."], ["He went rapidly to where the blue flame arose--it must have been very faint, for it did not seem to illumine the place around it at all--and gathering a few stones, formed them into some device.", "그는 푸른 불꽃이 이는 곳으로 잽싸게 가더니 — 그 불꽃은 매우 희미했던 것이 틀림없으니, 주위를 조금도 비추지 않는 듯했다 — 돌 몇 개를 모아 어떤 형상을 만들었다."], ["Once there appeared a strange optical effect: when he stood between me and the flame he did not obstruct it, for I could see its ghostly flicker all the same.", "한번은 기이한 착시가 일어났는데, 그가 나와 불꽃 사이에 섰는데도 불꽃을 가리지 못했으니, 나는 그 유령 같은 깜박임을 변함없이 볼 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["This startled me, but as the effect was only momentary, I took it that my eyes deceived me straining through the darkness.", "이것이 나를 놀라게 했으나, 그 현상이 한순간뿐이었으므로, 나는 어둠을 뚫어보느라 애쓴 내 눈이 나를 속인 것이려니 여겼다."], ["Then for a time there were no blue flames, and we sped onwards through the gloom, with the howling of the wolves around us, as though they were following in a moving circle.", "그러고는 한동안 푸른 불꽃이 보이지 않았고, 우리는 어둠을 뚫고 앞으로 내달렸는데, 늑대들의 울부짖음이 마치 움직이는 원을 이루어 우리를 뒤따르기라도 하듯 우리 주위를 맴돌았다."]], [["At last there came a time when the driver went further afield than he had yet gone, and during his absence, the horses began to tremble worse than ever and to snort and scream with fright.", "마침내 마부가 그때까지 갔던 것보다 더 멀리 나아간 때가 왔는데, 그가 없는 동안 말들이 그 어느 때보다 심하게 떨기 시작하고, 콧김을 내뿜으며 공포에 질려 비명을 질러댔다."], ["I could not see any cause for it, for the howling of the wolves had ceased altogether; but just then the moon, sailing through the black clouds, appeared behind the jagged crest of a beetling, pine-clad rock, and by its light I saw around us a ring of wolves, with white teeth and lolling red tongues, with long, sinewy limbs and shaggy hair.", "나는 그 까닭을 알 수 없었으니, 늑대들의 울부짖음이 완전히 멎었기 때문이다. 그러나 바로 그때, 검은 구름 사이를 헤치며 떠가던 달이 소나무로 덮인 험준한 바위의 들쭉날쭉한 마루 뒤에서 모습을 드러냈고, 그 빛에 나는 우리 주위로 늑대 떼가 둥글게 에워싸고 있는 것을 보았다. 흰 이빨에 붉은 혀를 늘어뜨리고, 길고 힘줄이 불거진 다리에 털이 텁수룩했다."], ["They were a hundred times more terrible in the grim silence which held them than even when they howled.", "그것들은 울부짖을 때보다도, 그것들을 사로잡은 그 음산한 침묵 속에서 백 배는 더 무시무시했다."], ["For myself, I felt a sort of paralysis of fear.", "나로 말하자면 일종의 공포에 의한 마비를 느꼈다."], ["It is only when a man feels himself face to face with such horrors that he can understand their true import.", "사람이 그런 공포와 정면으로 마주할 때에야 비로소 그 참된 의미를 이해할 수 있는 법이다."]], [["All at once the wolves began to howl as though the moonlight had had some peculiar effect on them.", "별안간 늑대들이, 마치 달빛이 그것들에게 어떤 기묘한 영향을 끼치기라도 한 듯, 울부짖기 시작했다."], ["The horses jumped about and reared, and looked helplessly round with eyes that rolled in a way painful to see; but the living ring of terror encompassed them on every side; and they had perforce to remain within it.", "말들은 펄쩍펄쩍 뛰며 앞발을 들고 일어섰고, 보기에 고통스러울 만큼 눈알을 굴리며 어쩔 줄 모르고 주위를 둘러보았다. 그러나 살아 있는 공포의 고리가 사방에서 그것들을 에워쌌으니, 그것들은 어쩔 수 없이 그 안에 머물러 있을 수밖에 없었다."], ["I called to the coachman to come, for it seemed to me that our only chance was to try to break out through the ring and to aid his approach.", "나는 마부에게 오라고 외쳤으니, 우리의 유일한 가망은 그 고리를 뚫고 나가 그가 다가오는 것을 도우려는 것뿐인 듯했기 때문이다."], ["I shouted and beat the side of the calèche, hoping by the noise to scare the wolves from that side, so as to give him a chance of reaching the trap.", "나는 소리를 지르고 칼레슈 옆구리를 두드렸는데, 그 소음으로 그쪽의 늑대들을 겁주어 쫓아내, 그가 마차에 닿을 틈을 주려는 것이었다."], ["How he came there, I know not, but I heard his voice raised in a tone of imperious command, and looking towards the sound, saw him stand in the roadway.", "그가 어떻게 거기 왔는지는 모르겠으나, 나는 위엄 있는 명령조로 높아진 그의 목소리를 들었고, 소리 나는 쪽을 바라보니 그가 길 한가운데 서 있는 것이 보였다."], ["As he swept his long arms, as though brushing aside some impalpable obstacle, the wolves fell back and back further still.", "그가 마치 무슨 만질 수 없는 장애물을 옆으로 밀어내듯 긴 팔을 휘젓자, 늑대들이 뒤로, 더 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["Just then a heavy cloud passed across the face of the moon, so that we were again in darkness.", "바로 그때 짙은 구름 한 점이 달의 얼굴을 가로질러, 우리는 다시 어둠 속에 잠겼다."]], [["When I could see again the driver was climbing into the calèche, and the wolves had disappeared.", "내가 다시 볼 수 있게 되었을 때, 마부는 칼레슈에 기어오르고 있었고, 늑대들은 사라지고 없었다."], ["This was all so strange and uncanny that a dreadful fear came upon me, and I was afraid to speak or move.", "이 모든 것이 어찌나 기이하고 으스스했던지, 끔찍한 공포가 나를 덮쳐, 나는 말하기도 움직이기도 두려웠다."], ["The time seemed interminable as we swept on our way, now in almost complete darkness, for the rolling clouds obscured the moon.", "우리가 휩쓸 듯 길을 나아가는 동안 시간이 끝없이 흐르는 것 같았는데, 이제는 굽이치는 구름이 달을 가려 거의 완전한 어둠 속이었다."], ["We kept on ascending, with occasional periods of quick descent, but in the main always ascending.", "우리는 이따금 빠르게 내려가는 때도 있었으나, 대체로 줄곧 오르막을 올랐다."], ["Suddenly, I became conscious of the fact that the driver was in the act of pulling up the horses in the courtyard of a vast ruined castle, from whose tall black windows came no ray of light, and whose broken battlements showed a jagged line against the moonlit sky.", "그러다 문득 나는 마부가 거대한 폐허가 된 성의 안마당에서 말들을 멈춰 세우는 중임을 깨달았다. 그 성의 높다란 검은 창들에서는 한 줄기 빛도 새어나오지 않았고, 부서진 흉벽은 달빛 어린 하늘을 배경으로 들쭉날쭉한 윤곽을 드러내고 있었다."]], [["5 May.--I must have been asleep, for certainly if I had been fully awake I must have noticed the approach of such a remarkable place. In the gloom the courtyard looked of considerable size, and as several dark ways led from it under great round arches, it perhaps seemed bigger than it really is. I have not yet been able to see it by daylight.", "「조너선 하커의 일기」 (이어서)"]], [["When the calèche stopped, the driver jumped down and held out his hand to assist me to alight.", "5월 5일. — 나는 분명 잠이 들었던 모양이다. 만일 완전히 깨어 있었더라면 이토록 예사롭지 않은 곳에 다가가는 것을 알아채지 못했을 리 없으니 말이다."], ["Again I could not but notice his prodigious strength. His hand actually seemed like a steel vice that could have crushed mine if he had chosen.", "어스름 속에서 안마당은 상당히 넓어 보였고, 거기서 커다란 둥근 아치 아래로 어두운 통로가 여럿 뻗어 있어, 아마 실제보다 더 커 보였을 것이다. 아직 대낮에 그곳을 볼 기회는 없었다."], ["Then he took out my traps, and placed them on the ground beside me as I stood close to a great door, old and studded with large iron nails, and set in a projecting doorway of massive stone.", "마차가 멈추자 마부가 뛰어내려 내가 내리는 것을 돕고자 손을 내밀었다. 다시금 나는 그의 엄청난 힘을 알아채지 않을 수 없었다. 그의 손은 마음만 먹으면 내 손을 으스러뜨릴 수 있는 강철 죔쇠 같았다."], ["I could see even in the dim light that the stone was massively carved, but that the carving had been much worn by time and weather.", "그러고 나서 그는 내 짐을 꺼내 내 곁의 땅바닥에 놓아주었는데, 그때 나는 크고 오래된, 커다란 쇠못이 빼곡히 박힌, 돌출된 육중한 석조 문틀에 앉은 거대한 문 가까이에 서 있었다."], ["As I stood, the driver jumped again into his seat and shook the reins; the horses started forward, and trap and all disappeared down one of the dark openings.", "희미한 빛 속에서도 그 돌이 육중하게 조각되어 있으나, 그 조각이 세월과 풍상으로 몹시 닳았음을 알 수 있었다. 내가 서 있는 동안 마부는 다시 자리로 뛰어올라 고삐를 흔들었다. 말들이 앞으로 나아가더니, 마차와 짐 모두 어두운 통로 가운데 하나로 사라졌다."]], [["I stood in silence where I was, for I did not know what to do.", "나는 어찌해야 할지 몰라 그 자리에 말없이 서 있었다."], ["Of bell or knocker there was no sign; through these frowning walls and dark window openings it was not likely that my voice could penetrate.", "종이나 문고리 같은 것은 흔적도 없었다. 이 찌푸린 듯한 벽과 어두운 창구멍을 뚫고 내 목소리가 닿을 성싶지도 않았다."], ["The time I waited seemed endless, and I felt doubts and fears crowding upon me.", "기다리는 시간이 끝없이 느껴졌고, 의심과 두려움이 밀려드는 것을 느꼈다."], ["What sort of place had I come to, and among what kind of people?", "나는 대체 어떤 곳에, 어떤 부류의 사람들 사이에 온 것인가?"], ["What sort of grim adventure was it on which I had embarked?", "내가 발을 들인 것은 대체 어떤 무시무시한 모험인가?"], ["Was this a customary incident in the life of a solicitor’s clerk sent out to explain the purchase of a London estate to a foreigner?", "런던의 부동산 매입을 외국인에게 설명하러 파견된 사무 변호사 서기의 삶에서 이런 일이 흔히 있는 일이던가? 사무 변호사 서기라니!"], ["Solicitor’s clerk! Mina would not like that.", "미나가 그것을 좋아하지 않을 것이다."], ["Solicitor--for just before leaving London I got word that my examination was successful; and I am now a full-blown solicitor!", "변호사라 해야지 — 런던을 떠나기 직전에 내 시험이 합격했다는 소식을 받았으니, 이제 나는 어엿한 변호사다!"], ["I began to rub my eyes and pinch myself to see if I were awake.", "나는 깨어 있는지 확인하려고 눈을 비비고 살을 꼬집기 시작했다."], ["It all seemed like a horrible nightmare to me, and I expected that I should suddenly awake, and find myself at home, with the dawn struggling in through the windows, as I had now and again felt in the morning after a day of overwork.", "모든 것이 끔찍한 악몽 같았고, 과로한 다음 날 아침이면 이따금 느끼곤 했듯이, 문득 깨어나 새벽빛이 창으로 비집고 드는 우리 집에 있는 나 자신을 발견하리라고 기대했다."], ["But my flesh answered the pinching test, and my eyes were not to be deceived.", "그러나 내 살은 꼬집는 시험에 답했고, 내 눈은 속을 것이 아니었다."], ["I was indeed awake and among the Carpathians.", "나는 정말로 깨어 있었고, 카르파티아 한가운데에 있었다."], ["All I could do now was to be patient, and to wait the coming of the morning.", "이제 내가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 참고서 아침이 오기를 기다리는 것뿐이었다."]], [["Just as I had come to this conclusion I heard a heavy step approaching behind the great door, and saw through the chinks the gleam of a coming light.", "내가 막 이 결론에 이르렀을 때, 거대한 문 뒤로 묵직한 발소리가 다가오는 것이 들렸고, 틈새로 다가오는 불빛이 어른거리는 것이 보였다."], ["Then there was the sound of rattling chains and the clanking of massive bolts drawn back.", "그러고 나서 쇠사슬이 절그럭거리는 소리와 육중한 빗장이 뒤로 당겨지며 철컹거리는 소리가 났다."], ["A key was turned with the loud grating noise of long disuse, and the great door swung back.", "오래 쓰지 않아 요란하게 삐걱대는 소리를 내며 열쇠가 돌아갔고, 거대한 문이 뒤로 열렸다."]], [["Within, stood a tall old man, clean shaven save for a long white moustache, and clad in black from head to foot, without a single speck of colour about him anywhere.", "안에는 키 큰 노인이 서 있었는데, 긴 흰 콧수염만 빼고는 말끔히 면도를 했고, 머리끝에서 발끝까지 검은 옷을 입었으며, 어디에도 다른 빛깔이라곤 한 점도 없었다."], ["He held in his hand an antique silver lamp, in which the flame burned without chimney or globe of any kind, throwing long quivering shadows as it flickered in the draught of the open door.", "그는 손에 골동품 같은 은제 등잔을 들고 있었는데, 그 불꽃은 등피나 둥근 갓 따위 없이 타오르며, 열린 문으로 들이치는 바람에 일렁일 때마다 길게 떨리는 그림자를 던졌다."], ["The old man motioned me in with his right hand with a courtly gesture, saying in excellent English, but with a strange intonation:--", "노인은 오른손으로 정중한 몸짓을 지으며 나를 안으로 청했는데, 훌륭한 영어였으나 기이한 억양으로 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Welcome to my house!", "\"내 집에 온 것을 환영하오!"], ["Enter freely and of your own will!”", "자유로이, 그대 자신의 뜻으로 들어오시오!\""], ["He made no motion of stepping to meet me, but stood like a statue, as though his gesture of welcome had fixed him into stone.", "그는 나를 맞으러 한 발짝도 내딛지 않고, 마치 환영의 몸짓이 그를 돌로 굳혀버리기라도 한 듯 조각상처럼 서 있었다."], ["The instant, however, that I had stepped over the threshold, he moved impulsively forward, and holding out his hand grasped mine with a strength which made me wince, an effect which was not lessened by the fact that it seemed as cold as ice--more like the hand of a dead than a living man.", "그러나 내가 문지방을 넘어선 순간, 그는 충동적으로 앞으로 나서더니, 손을 내밀어 내 손을 움켜쥐었는데 그 힘이 어찌나 셌던지 나는 움찔했다. 그 손이 얼음장처럼 차가운 — 산 사람의 손이라기보다 죽은 사람의 손에 가까운 — 느낌이었던 탓에 그 효과는 줄어들지 않았다."], ["Again he said:--", "그가 다시 말했다."]], [["“Welcome to my house. Come freely.", "\"내 집에 온 것을 환영하오."], ["Go safely; and leave something of the happiness you bring!”", "자유로이 들어오시오. 무사히 떠나시오."], ["The strength of the handshake was so much akin to that which I had noticed in the driver, whose face I had not seen, that for a moment I doubted if it were not the same person to whom I was speaking; so to make sure, I said interrogatively:--", "그리고 그대가 가져온 행복의 얼마간을 남기고 가시오!\" 그 악수의 힘이 얼굴은 보지 못했던 그 마부에게서 느꼈던 것과 어찌나 비슷했던지, 나는 한순간 내가 말을 건네는 이가 바로 그 사람이 아닌가 의심했다. 그래서 확인하려고, 나는 묻는 투로 말했다."]], [["“Count Dracula?” He bowed in a courtly way as he replied:--", "\"드라큘라 백작이신지요?\" 그는 정중하게 절을 하며 대답했다."]], [["“I am Dracula; and I bid you welcome, Mr. Harker, to my house.", "\"나는 드라큘라요. 하커 씨, 내 집에 온 것을 환영하오."], ["Come in; the night air is chill, and you must need to eat and rest.”", "들어오시오. 밤공기가 차고, 그대는 분명 먹고 쉬어야 할 것이오.\""], ["As he was speaking, he put the lamp on a bracket on the wall, and stepping out, took my luggage; he had carried it in before I could forestall him.", "그는 말하면서 등잔을 벽의 받침대에 올려놓고, 밖으로 나서더니 내 짐을 들었다. 내가 미처 앞질러 막기도 전에 그는 짐을 안으로 들여놓았다."], ["I protested but he insisted:--", "내가 만류했으나 그는 고집했다."]], [["“Nay, sir, you are my guest.", "\"아니오, 선생. 그대는 내 손님이오."], ["It is late, and my people are not available.", "시간도 늦었고, 내 사람들도 지금은 부릴 수가 없소."], ["Let me see to your comfort myself.”", "그대의 편안함은 내가 직접 살피게 해주시오.\""], ["He insisted on carrying my traps along the passage, and then up a great winding stair, and along another great passage, on whose stone floor our steps rang heavily.", "그는 통로를 따라, 그러고는 거대한 나선형 계단을 올라, 또 다른 큰 통로를 따라 내 짐을 나르겠다고 고집했는데, 그 돌바닥 위로 우리 발소리가 묵직하게 울렸다."], ["At the end of this he threw open a heavy door, and I rejoiced to see within a well-lit room in which a table was spread for supper, and on whose mighty hearth a great fire of logs, freshly replenished, flamed and flared.", "그 끝에서 그는 육중한 문 하나를 활짝 열었고, 나는 그 안에 환히 밝혀진 방을 보고 기뻤다. 그 방에는 저녁식사를 차린 식탁이 펼쳐져 있었고, 거대한 벽난로에서는 갓 더 지핀 통나무 장작더미가 활활 타오르며 너울거렸다."]], [["The Count halted, putting down my bags, closed the door, and crossing the room, opened another door, which led into a small octagonal room lit by a single lamp, and seemingly without a window of any sort.", "백작은 멈추어 서서 내 가방을 내려놓고 문을 닫은 뒤, 방을 가로질러 또 다른 문을 열었는데, 그 문은 등잔 하나로만 밝혀진, 어떤 종류의 창문도 없어 보이는 작은 팔각형 방으로 이어졌다."], ["Passing through this, he opened another door, and motioned me to enter.", "이곳을 지나 그는 또 다른 문을 열고는 나에게 들어가라고 손짓했다."], ["It was a welcome sight; for here was a great bedroom well lighted and warmed with another log fire,--also added to but lately, for the top logs were fresh--which sent a hollow roar up the wide chimney.", "반가운 광경이었다. 그곳에는 또 다른 통나무 불로 환히 밝혀지고 따뜻해진 큰 침실이 있었던 것이다 — 이 불 역시 얼마 전에 더 지핀 것으로, 맨 위 통나무가 새것이었으니 — 그 불이 넓은 굴뚝 위로 공허한 울음소리를 내며 타올랐다."], ["The Count himself left my luggage inside and withdrew, saying, before he closed the door:--", "백작은 직접 내 짐을 안에 들여놓고 물러났는데, 문을 닫기 전에 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“You will need, after your journey, to refresh yourself by making your toilet. I trust you will find all you wish.", "\"여행 끝이니, 그대는 몸단장을 하여 기운을 차려야 할 것이오. 원하는 것이 모두 갖추어져 있기를 바라오."], ["When you are ready, come into the other room, where you will find your supper prepared.”", "채비가 되거든 저쪽 방으로 오시오. 그곳에 그대의 저녁식사가 차려져 있을 것이오.\""]], [["The light and warmth and the Count’s courteous welcome seemed to have dissipated all my doubts and fears.", "빛과 온기, 그리고 백작의 정중한 환대가 내 모든 의심과 두려움을 흩어버린 듯했다."], ["Having then reached my normal state, I discovered that I was half famished with hunger; so making a hasty toilet, I went into the other room.", "그렇게 정상적인 상태로 돌아오자, 나는 내가 허기로 반쯤 굶주려 있음을 알아챘다. 그래서 서둘러 몸단장을 마치고 저쪽 방으로 갔다."]], [["I found supper already laid out.", "저녁식사는 이미 차려져 있었다."], ["My host, who stood on one side of the great fireplace, leaning against the stonework, made a graceful wave of his hand to the table, and said:--", "거대한 벽난로 한쪽에 서서 석조 부분에 기대어 있던 주인은 우아하게 손을 저어 식탁을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“I pray you, be seated and sup how you please.", "\"청하건대, 앉아서 마음껏 드시오."], ["You will, I trust, excuse me that I do not join you; but I have dined already, and I do not sup.”", "내가 함께하지 못하는 것을 부디 양해해주시오. 나는 이미 식사를 했고, 야식은 들지 않으니 말이오.\""]], [["I handed to him the sealed letter which Mr. Hawkins had entrusted to me.", "나는 호킨스 씨가 나에게 맡긴 봉인된 편지를 그에게 건넸다."], ["He opened it and read it gravely; then, with a charming smile, he handed it to me to read.", "그는 그것을 뜯어 엄숙히 읽었다. 그러고는 매혹적인 미소를 지으며 나에게 읽어보라고 건네주었다."], ["One passage of it, at least, gave me a thrill of pleasure.", "적어도 그 가운데 한 대목은 나에게 기쁨의 전율을 주었다."]], [["“I must regret that an attack of gout, from which malady I am a constant sufferer, forbids absolutely any travelling on my part for some time to come; but I am happy to say I can send a sufficient substitute, one in whom I have every possible confidence.", "\"통풍 발작 때문에 — 나는 이 병으로 늘 고생하고 있소 — 당분간은 어떤 여행도 절대로 할 수 없게 된 것을 유감으로 여기지 않을 수 없소. 그러나 충분한 대리인을 보낼 수 있게 되어 기쁘오. 내가 온갖 신뢰를 다 두고 있는 사람이오."], ["He is a young man, full of energy and talent in his own way, and of a very faithful disposition.", "그는 젊은이로, 나름대로 활기와 재능이 넘치며 매우 충직한 성품을 지녔소."], ["He is discreet and silent, and has grown into manhood in my service.", "그는 신중하고 입이 무거우며, 내 밑에서 일하며 어른이 되었소."], ["He shall be ready to attend on you when you will during his stay, and shall take your instructions in all matters.”", "그가 머무는 동안 그대가 원하실 때 언제든 그대를 모실 채비가 되어 있을 것이며, 모든 일에서 그대의 지시를 받을 것이오.\""]], [["The Count himself came forward and took off the cover of a dish, and I fell to at once on an excellent roast chicken.", "백작이 직접 앞으로 나와 접시 덮개를 벗겨주었고, 나는 곧장 훌륭한 통닭구이에 손을 댔다."], ["This, with some cheese and a salad and a bottle of old Tokay, of which I had two glasses, was my supper.", "이것에 치즈와 샐러드 약간, 그리고 오래된 토카이 포도주 한 병 — 나는 그것을 두 잔 마셨다 — 이 내 저녁식사였다."], ["During the time I was eating it the Count asked me many questions as to my journey, and I told him by degrees all I had experienced.", "내가 그것을 먹는 동안 백작은 내 여행에 관해 여러 가지를 물었고, 나는 차츰차츰 내가 겪은 모든 것을 그에게 이야기했다."]], [["By this time I had finished my supper, and by my host’s desire had drawn up a chair by the fire and begun to smoke a cigar which he offered me, at the same time excusing himself that he did not smoke.", "이즈음 나는 저녁식사를 마쳤고, 주인이 권하는 대로 벽난로 옆으로 의자를 끌어다 놓고 그가 권한 시가를 피우기 시작했는데, 그러면서 그는 자기는 담배를 피우지 않는다며 양해를 구했다."], ["I had now an opportunity of observing him, and found him of a very marked physiognomy.", "이제 나는 그를 관찰할 기회를 얻었고, 그의 인상이 매우 두드러진다는 것을 알았다."]], [["His face was a strong--a very strong--aquiline, with high bridge of the thin nose and peculiarly arched nostrils; with lofty domed forehead, and hair growing scantily round the temples but profusely elsewhere.", "그의 얼굴은 억센 — 매우 억센 — 매부리 형이었고, 가느다란 코는 콧대가 높고 콧구멍이 유난히 활처럼 휘어 있었다. 이마는 우뚝 솟아 둥글었고, 머리칼은 관자놀이 둘레에는 듬성듬성하나 다른 곳에는 무성하게 자랐다."], ["His eyebrows were very massive, almost meeting over the nose, and with bushy hair that seemed to curl in its own profusion.", "눈썹은 매우 짙어 코 위에서 거의 맞닿을 듯했고, 무성한 털이 그 자체의 풍성함으로 곱슬거리는 듯했다."], ["The mouth, so far as I could see it under the heavy moustache, was fixed and rather cruel-looking, with peculiarly sharp white teeth; these protruded over the lips, whose remarkable ruddiness showed astonishing vitality in a man of his years.", "입은, 두꺼운 콧수염 아래로 보이는 한에서는, 굳게 다물려 다소 잔인해 보였고, 유난히 날카로운 흰 이가 있었다. 그 이들은 입술 위로 튀어나와 있었는데, 그 입술의 놀라운 붉은빛은 그 나이의 사람치고는 놀라운 생기를 드러냈다."], ["For the rest, his ears were pale, and at the tops extremely pointed; the chin was broad and strong, and the cheeks firm though thin.", "그 밖에 그의 귀는 창백했고, 끝이 몹시 뾰족했으며, 턱은 넓고 억셌고, 뺨은 여위었으나 단단했다."], ["The general effect was one of extraordinary pallor.", "전체적인 인상은 유난한 창백함이었다."]], [["Hitherto I had noticed the backs of his hands as they lay on his knees in the firelight, and they had seemed rather white and fine; but seeing them now close to me, I could not but notice that they were rather coarse--broad, with squat fingers.", "지금까지 나는 그의 손이 불빛 속에서 무릎 위에 놓여 있을 때 그 손등을 보았는데, 다소 희고 고와 보였다. 그러나 이제 그것을 가까이서 보니, 그것이 다소 거칠다는 것을 — 넓적하고 손가락이 뭉툭하다는 것을 — 알아채지 않을 수 없었다."], ["Strange to say, there were hairs in the centre of the palm.", "이상하게도 손바닥 한가운데에 털이 나 있었다."], ["The nails were long and fine, and cut to a sharp point.", "손톱은 길고 가늘었으며, 날카로운 끝으로 깎여 있었다."], ["As the Count leaned over me and his hands touched me, I could not repress a shudder.", "백작이 나에게 몸을 기울여 그의 손이 나를 스쳤을 때, 나는 몸서리를 억누를 수 없었다."], ["It may have been that his breath was rank, but a horrible feeling of nausea came over me, which, do what I would, I could not conceal.", "그의 입김이 역겨웠던 탓일 수도 있겠으나, 끔찍한 구역질이 밀려와, 아무리 애를 써도 감출 수가 없었다."], ["The Count, evidently noticing it, drew back; and with a grim sort of smile, which showed more than he had yet done his protuberant teeth, sat himself down again on his own side of the fireplace.", "백작은 그것을 알아챈 것이 분명한지 뒤로 물러났고, 지금껏 보였던 것보다 더 그 튀어나온 이를 드러내는 음산한 미소를 지으며 다시 벽난로의 제 쪽에 앉았다."], ["We were both silent for a while; and as I looked towards the window I saw the first dim streak of the coming dawn.", "우리는 한동안 둘 다 말이 없었다. 그리고 내가 창문 쪽을 바라보니 다가오는 새벽의 첫 희미한 빛줄기가 보였다."], ["There seemed a strange stillness over everything; but as I listened I heard as if from down below in the valley the howling of many wolves.", "모든 것 위에 기이한 정적이 깃든 듯했다. 그러나 귀를 기울이자, 마치 저 아래 골짜기에서 들려오는 듯한 늑대 여러 마리의 울부짖음이 들렸다."], ["The Count’s eyes gleamed, and he said:--", "백작의 두 눈이 번득였고, 그가 말했다."]], [["“Listen to them--the children of the night.", "\"저들의 소리를 들어보시오 — 밤의 자식들이오."], ["What music they make!” Seeing, I suppose, some expression in my face strange to him, he added:--", "얼마나 멋진 음악을 만들어내는지!\" 짐작건대 그에게 낯선 어떤 표정을 내 얼굴에서 보았는지, 그가 덧붙였다."]], [["“Ah, sir, you dwellers in the city cannot enter into the feelings of the hunter.”", "\"아, 선생, 도시에 사는 그대들은 사냥꾼의 심정을 헤아릴 수 없소.\""], ["Then he rose and said:--", "그러고는 일어서서 말했다."]], [["“But you must be tired.", "\"그러나 그대는 분명 피곤할 것이오."], ["Your bedroom is all ready, and to-morrow you shall sleep as late as you will.", "그대의 침실은 모두 채비가 되어 있으니, 내일은 원하는 만큼 늦게까지 자도 좋소."], ["I have to be away till the afternoon; so sleep well and dream well!”", "나는 오후까지 자리를 비워야 하오. 그러니 푹 자고, 좋은 꿈을 꾸시오!\""], ["With a courteous bow, he opened for me himself the door to the octagonal room, and I entered my bedroom....", "정중하게 절을 하며 그는 직접 나를 위해 팔각형 방으로 통하는 문을 열어주었고, 나는 내 침실로 들어갔다……."]], [["I am all in a sea of wonders.", "나는 온통 경이의 바다 속에 있다."], ["I doubt; I fear; I think strange things, which I dare not confess to my own soul.", "나는 의심한다. 나는 두려워한다. 나는 내 영혼에마저 감히 고백하지 못할 기이한 것들을 생각한다."], ["God keep me, if only for the sake of those dear to me!", "신이여, 나를 지켜주소서, 적어도 나에게 소중한 이들을 위해서라도!"]], [["7 May.--It is again early morning, but I have rested and enjoyed the last twenty-four hours.", "5월 7일. — 다시 이른 아침이지만, 나는 지난 스물네 시간을 쉬며 즐겁게 보냈다."], ["I slept till late in the day, and awoke of my own accord.", "나는 낮 늦게까지 자고 저절로 깨어났다."], ["When I had dressed myself I went into the room where we had supped, and found a cold breakfast laid out, with coffee kept hot by the pot being placed on the hearth.", "옷을 차려입고 우리가 저녁식사를 했던 방으로 가니, 차가운 아침식사가 차려져 있었고, 커피는 주전자를 벽난로에 올려놓아 따뜻하게 유지되고 있었다."], ["There was a card on the table, on which was written:--", "식탁 위에는 카드 한 장이 놓여 있었는데, 거기에 이렇게 적혀 있었다."]], [["“I have to be absent for a while.", "\"나는 잠시 자리를 비워야 하오."], ["Do not wait for me.--D.”", "나를 기다리지 마시오. — D.\""], ["I set to and enjoyed a hearty meal.", "나는 시작하여 든든한 식사를 즐겼다."], ["When I had done, I looked for a bell, so that I might let the servants know I had finished; but I could not find one.", "식사를 마치고 나는 종을 찾아보았다. 하인들에게 내가 식사를 끝냈음을 알리기 위해서였다."], ["There are certainly odd deficiencies in the house, considering the extraordinary evidences of wealth which are round me.", "그러나 종을 찾을 수 없었다. 내 주위에 둘러싼 부의 놀라운 증거를 생각하면, 이 집에는 확실히 기이한 결핍이 있다."], ["The table service is of gold, and so beautifully wrought that it must be of immense value.", "식탁 식기는 금으로 되어 있는데, 어찌나 아름답게 세공되었는지 분명 엄청난 값어치가 있을 것이다."], ["The curtains and upholstery of the chairs and sofas and the hangings of my bed are of the costliest and most beautiful fabrics, and must have been of fabulous value when they were made, for they are centuries old, though in excellent order.", "의자와 소파의 커튼과 천, 그리고 내 침대의 휘장은 가장 값비싸고 가장 아름다운 직물로 되어 있어, 만들어졌을 당시에는 엄청난 값어치가 있었을 것이 분명한데, 수백 년이나 되었는데도 더없이 좋은 상태였다."], ["I saw something like them in Hampton Court, but there they were worn and frayed and moth-eaten.", "나는 햄프턴 코트에서 그와 비슷한 것을 본 적이 있으나, 그곳의 것은 닳고 해지고 좀먹어 있었다."], ["But still in none of the rooms is there a mirror.", "그러나 어느 방에도 여전히 거울은 없다."], ["There is not even a toilet glass on my table, and I had to get the little shaving glass from my bag before I could either shave or brush my hair.", "내 탁자에는 화장 거울조차 없어, 면도를 하거나 머리를 빗으려면 가방에서 작은 면도 거울을 꺼내야 했다."], ["I have not yet seen a servant anywhere, or heard a sound near the castle except the howling of wolves.", "나는 아직 어디에서도 하인을 본 적이 없고, 성 근처에서 늑대 울음소리 말고는 어떤 소리도 듣지 못했다."], ["Some time after I had finished my meal--I do not know whether to call it breakfast or dinner, for it was between five and six o’clock when I had it--I looked about for something to read, for I did not like to go about the castle until I had asked the Count’s permission.", "식사를 마치고 얼마쯤 지난 뒤 — 그것을 아침식사라 불러야 할지 저녁식사라 불러야 할지 모르겠다. 그것을 먹은 것이 다섯 시에서 여섯 시 사이였으니 — 나는 읽을거리를 찾아보았다. 백작의 허락을 구하기 전에는 성안을 돌아다니고 싶지 않았기 때문이다."], ["There was absolutely nothing in the room, book, newspaper, or even writing materials; so I opened another door in the room and found a sort of library.", "방안에는 책이든 신문이든, 심지어 필기도구든 정말 아무것도 없었다. 그래서 방의 또 다른 문을 열어보니 서재 같은 곳이 있었다."], ["The door opposite mine I tried, but found it locked.", "내 방 맞은편 문을 열어보려 했으나 잠겨 있었다."]], [["In the library I found, to my great delight, a vast number of English books, whole shelves full of them, and bound volumes of magazines and newspapers.", "서재에서 나는 더없이 기쁘게도 수많은 영어 책들을 발견했다. 책장 가득히, 잡지와 신문의 제본된 권들도 있었다."], ["A table in the centre was littered with English magazines and newspapers, though none of them were of very recent date.", "한가운데 탁자에는 영어 잡지와 신문이 어지러이 널려 있었는데, 최근 날짜의 것은 하나도 없었다."], ["The books were of the most varied kind--history, geography, politics, political economy, botany, geology, law--all relating to England and English life and customs and manners.", "책들은 더없이 다양한 종류였다 — 역사, 지리, 정치, 정치경제, 식물학, 지질학, 법률 — 모두 영국과 영국의 생활, 풍습, 예법에 관한 것이었다."], ["There were even such books of reference as the London Directory, the “Red” and “Blue” books, Whitaker’s Almanac, the Army and Navy Lists, and--it somehow gladdened my heart to see it--the Law List.", "심지어 런던 인명록, \"적서(赤書)\"와 \"청서(靑書)\", 위터커 연감, 육군 및 해군 명부, 그리고 — 어쩐지 그것을 보니 마음이 흐뭇했는데 — 법조 명부 같은 참고 서적들까지 있었다."]], [["Whilst I was looking at the books, the door opened, and the Count entered.", "내가 책들을 바라보고 있는데 문이 열리고 백작이 들어왔다."], ["He saluted me in a hearty way, and hoped that I had had a good night’s rest.", "그는 정겹게 나에게 인사하고는, 내가 밤에 잘 쉬었기를 바란다고 했다."], ["Then he went on:--", "그러고는 말을 이었다."]], [["“I am glad you found your way in here, for I am sure there is much that will interest you.", "\"그대가 이곳으로 길을 찾아든 것이 기쁘오. 그대의 흥미를 끌 만한 것이 분명 많을 테니 말이오."], ["These companions”--and he laid his hand on some of the books--“have been good friends to me, and for some years past, ever since I had the idea of going to London, have given me many, many hours of pleasure.", "이 동무들이\" — 그러면서 그는 몇몇 책 위에 손을 얹었다 — \"나에게 좋은 벗이 되어주었고, 지난 몇 해 동안, 런던으로 갈 생각을 품은 이래로, 나에게 수많은, 수많은 즐거운 시간을 안겨주었소."], ["Through them I have come to know your great England; and to know her is to love her.", "이것들을 통해 나는 그대의 위대한 영국을 알게 되었소. 그리고 영국을 안다는 것은 영국을 사랑하는 것이오."], ["I long to go through the crowded streets of your mighty London, to be in the midst of the whirl and rush of humanity, to share its life, its change, its death, and all that makes it what it is.", "나는 그대의 웅장한 런던의 붐비는 거리를 거닐고, 인류의 소용돌이와 분주함 한가운데에 있으며, 그 삶과 그 변화와 그 죽음, 그리고 그것을 그것이게 하는 그 모든 것을 함께하기를 갈망하오."], ["But alas!", "그러나 아아!"], ["as yet I only know your tongue through books.", "아직 나는 책을 통해서만 그대의 언어를 알 뿐이오."], ["To you, my friend, I look that I know it to speak.”", "나의 벗이여, 그것을 말로 할 줄 알게 되는 것을 그대에게 기대하오.\""]], [["“But, Count,”", "\"하지만, 백작님,\""], ["I said, “you know and speak English thoroughly!”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"당신은 영어를 완벽하게 알고 또 말하시지 않습니까!\""], ["He bowed gravely.", "그는 엄숙히 절을 했다."]], [["“I thank you, my friend, for your all too-flattering estimate, but yet I fear that I am but a little way on the road I would travel.", "\"나의 벗이여, 너무도 과분한 평가를 해주니 고맙소. 그러나 아직도 나는 내가 가고자 하는 길을 조금밖에 가지 못했음을 두려워하오."], ["True, I know the grammar and the words, but yet I know not how to speak them.”", "사실 나는 문법과 단어는 알지만, 아직 그것을 어떻게 말해야 하는지는 모른다오.\""]], [["“Indeed,”", "\"정말이지,\""], ["I said, “you speak excellently.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"당신은 훌륭하게 말씀하십니다.\""]], [["“Not so,” he answered.", "\"그렇지 않소,\" 하고 그가 대답했다."], ["“Well, I know that, did I move and speak in your London, none there are who would not know me for a stranger.", "\"글쎄, 나는 알고 있소. 내가 그대의 런던에서 거동하고 말한다면, 그곳에서 나를 이방인으로 알아보지 못할 자가 없으리라는 것을."], ["That is not enough for me.", "나에게는 그것으로 충분치 않소."], ["Here I am noble; I am boyar; the common people know me, and I am master.", "이곳에서 나는 귀족이오. 나는 보야르요. 평민들은 나를 알고, 나는 주인이오."], ["But a stranger in a strange land, he is no one; men know him not--and to know not is to care not for.", "그러나 낯선 땅의 이방인은 아무도 아니오. 사람들은 그를 알지 못하고 — 알지 못한다는 것은 마음을 두지 않는다는 것이오."], ["I am content if I am like the rest, so that no man stops if he see me, or pause in his speaking if he hear my words, ‘Ha, ha!", "나는 다른 이들과 같기만 하다면 만족하오. 그래서 나를 보아도 멈춰 서는 자가 없고, 내 말을 들어도 말을 멈추고 '하, 하! 이방인이로군!'"], ["a stranger!’ I have been so long master that I would be master still--or at least that none other should be master of me.", "하지 않는다면 말이오. 나는 너무도 오래 주인이었기에 여전히 주인이고 싶소 — 적어도 다른 누구도 나의 주인이 되지는 않게 하고 싶소."], ["You come to me not alone as agent of my friend Peter Hawkins, of Exeter, to tell me all about my new estate in London.", "그대는 단지 엑서터의 내 벗 피터 호킨스의 대리인으로서, 런던의 내 새 부동산에 관해 모두 일러주러 나에게 온 것만은 아니오."], ["You shall, I trust, rest here with me awhile, so that by our talking I may learn the English intonation; and I would that you tell me when I make error, even of the smallest, in my speaking.", "부디 그대가 여기 나와 함께 얼마간 머물러주기를 바라오. 그리하여 우리의 대화로 내가 영어의 억양을 익힐 수 있도록 말이오. 그리고 내가 말하다가 아주 사소한 것이라도 잘못을 저지르거든 그대가 일러주기를 바라오."], ["I am sorry that I had to be away so long to-day; but you will, I know, forgive one who has so many important affairs in hand.”", "오늘 내가 그토록 오래 자리를 비워야 했던 것은 미안하오. 그러나 그대는, 분명히, 그토록 많은 중대한 일을 떠맡고 있는 자를 용서해줄 것이오.\""]], [["Of course I said all I could about being willing, and asked if I might come into that room when I chose.", "물론 나는 기꺼이 그러겠다고 할 수 있는 말을 다 했고, 내가 원할 때 그 방으로 와도 되는지 물었다."], ["He answered: “Yes, certainly,” and added:--", "그는 \"그렇소, 물론이오\"라고 대답하고는 덧붙였다."]], [["“You may go anywhere you wish in the castle, except where the doors are locked, where of course you will not wish to go.", "\"그대는 문이 잠긴 곳을 빼고는 성안 어디든 원하는 곳으로 가도 좋소. 잠긴 곳에는 물론 그대도 가고 싶지 않을 테지만 말이오."], ["There is reason that all things are as they are, and did you see with my eyes and know with my knowledge, you would perhaps better understand.”", "모든 것이 지금 이대로 있는 데에는 까닭이 있소. 그대가 내 눈으로 보고 내 지식으로 안다면, 아마 더 잘 이해하게 될 것이오.\""], ["I said I was sure of this, and then he went on:--", "나는 분명 그러리라고 말했고, 그러자 그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“We are in Transylvania; and Transylvania is not England.", "\"우리는 트란실바니아에 있소. 그리고 트란실바니아는 영국이 아니오."], ["Our ways are not your ways, and there shall be to you many strange things.", "우리의 방식은 그대의 방식이 아니며, 그대에게는 기이한 것들이 많을 것이오."], ["Nay, from what you have told me of your experiences already, you know something of what strange things there may be.”", "아니, 그대가 이미 겪은 일들을 나에게 이야기해준 것으로 보아, 그대는 어떤 기이한 것들이 있을 수 있는지 얼마간 알고 있구려.\""]], [["This led to much conversation; and as it was evident that he wanted to talk, if only for talking’s sake, I asked him many questions regarding things that had already happened to me or come within my notice.", "이 말에서 많은 대화가 이어졌다. 그리고 그가 말 자체를 위해서라도 이야기하고 싶어한다는 것이 분명했으므로, 나는 이미 나에게 일어났거나 내 눈에 들어왔던 일들에 관해 그에게 여러 가지를 물었다."], ["Sometimes he sheered off the subject, or turned the conversation by pretending not to understand; but generally he answered all I asked most frankly.", "때로 그는 화제를 비껴가거나, 못 알아듣는 척하며 대화를 돌리기도 했다. 그러나 대체로 그는 내가 묻는 모든 것에 더없이 솔직하게 대답했다."], ["Then as time went on, and I had got somewhat bolder, I asked him of some of the strange things of the preceding night, as, for instance, why the coachman went to the places where he had seen the blue flames.", "그러다 시간이 지나 내가 다소 대담해지자, 나는 전날 밤의 기이한 일들 가운데 몇 가지를 그에게 물었다. 이를테면, 어째서 마부가 푸른 불꽃이 보이던 곳으로 갔는가 하는 것이었다."], ["He then explained to me that it was commonly believed that on a certain night of the year--last night, in fact, when all evil spirits are supposed to have unchecked sway--a blue flame is seen over any place where treasure has been concealed.", "그러자 그는, 한 해의 어느 특정한 밤 — 사실 어젯밤으로, 온갖 악령이 거침없이 위세를 떨친다고 여겨지는 밤 — 에는 보물이 숨겨진 곳 위에 푸른 불꽃이 보인다고 흔히들 믿는다고 나에게 설명해주었다. \"그 보물이 숨겨져 있다는 데에는,\""], ["“That treasure has been hidden,”", "하고 그가 말을 이었다."], ["he went on, “in the region through which you came last night, there can be but little doubt; for it was the ground fought over for centuries by the Wallachian, the Saxon, and the Turk.", "\"그대가 어젯밤 지나온 그 지역에 말이오, 의심의 여지가 거의 없소. 그곳은 수백 년 동안 왈라키아인, 색슨인, 터키인이 다투어 싸운 땅이었기 때문이오."], ["Why, there is hardly a foot of soil in all this region that has not been enriched by the blood of men, patriots or invaders.", "아니, 이 온 지역에서 사람의 피로, 애국자든 침략자든 그 피로 비옥해지지 않은 땅은 한 뼘도 없을 지경이오."], ["In old days there were stirring times, when the Austrian and the Hungarian came up in hordes, and the patriots went out to meet them--men and women, the aged and the children too--and waited their coming on the rocks above the passes, that they might sweep destruction on them with their artificial avalanches.", "옛날에는 격동의 시절이 있었소. 오스트리아인과 헝가리인이 떼지어 몰려오면, 애국자들은 그들을 맞으러 나갔소 — 남자도 여자도, 노인도 아이도 — 그리고 고개 위쪽 바위에서 그들이 오기를 기다렸다가, 인공으로 일으킨 눈사태로 그들에게 파멸을 쓸어 내릴 수 있도록 말이오."], ["When the invader was triumphant he found but little, for whatever there was had been sheltered in the friendly soil.”", "침략자가 승리했을 때에도 그가 얻은 것은 거의 없었으니, 무엇이 있었든 정다운 땅속에 감추어져 있었기 때문이오.\""]], [["“But how,”", "\"하지만 어떻게,\""], ["said I, “can it have remained so long undiscovered, when there is a sure index to it if men will but take the trouble to look?”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"사람들이 수고를 마다하지 않고 찾아보기만 한다면 확실한 표지가 있는데, 어찌 그토록 오래 발견되지 않은 채로 남아 있을 수 있단 말입니까?\""], ["The Count smiled, and as his lips ran back over his gums, the long, sharp, canine teeth showed out strangely; he answered:--", "백작은 미소를 지었고, 그의 입술이 잇몸 위로 뒤로 당겨지자 길고 날카로운 송곳니가 기이하게 드러났다. 그가 대답했다."]], [["“Because your peasant is at heart a coward and a fool!", "\"그대의 농부는 마음속으로는 겁쟁이에 바보이기 때문이오!"], ["Those flames only appear on one night; and on that night no man of this land will, if he can help it, stir without his doors.", "그 불꽃들은 단 하룻밤에만 나타나오. 그리고 그 밤에는 이 땅의 어떤 사람도, 어쩔 수 있다면, 제 집 문밖으로 나서지 않을 것이오."], ["And, dear sir, even if he did he would not know what to do.", "그리고, 선생, 설령 나선다 해도 그는 무엇을 해야 할지 모를 것이오."], ["Why, even the peasant that you tell me of who marked the place of the flame would not know where to look in daylight even for his own work.", "아니, 그대가 나에게 말한, 불꽃이 있던 자리를 표시해 두었다는 그 농부조차도 대낮에는 제가 한 일이 어디인지조차 찾지 못할 것이오."], ["Even you would not, I dare be sworn, be able to find these places again?”", "그대조차도, 내 감히 맹세하건대, 그 장소들을 다시 찾을 수 없을 것이오, 안 그렇소?\""]], [["“There you are right,” I said.", "\"그 점은 옳습니다,\" 하고 내가 말했다."], ["“I know no more than the dead where even to look for them.”", "\"저는 그것들을 어디서 찾아야 할지조차 죽은 자만큼이나 알지 못합니다.\""], ["Then we drifted into other matters.", "그러고 나서 우리는 다른 문제들로 흘러갔다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"자,\""], ["he said at last, “tell me of London and of the house which you have procured for me.”", "하고 마침내 그가 말했다. \"런던에 대해, 그리고 그대가 나를 위해 구해준 그 집에 대해 이야기해주시오.\""], ["With an apology for my remissness, I went into my own room to get the papers from my bag.", "나는 내 태만을 사과하고, 가방에서 서류를 꺼내러 내 방으로 갔다."], ["Whilst I was placing them in order I heard a rattling of china and silver in the next room, and as I passed through, noticed that the table had been cleared and the lamp lit, for it was by this time deep into the dark.", "서류를 정리하고 있는데 옆방에서 도자기와 은그릇이 달그락거리는 소리가 들렸고, 지나가면서 보니 식탁은 치워지고 등잔이 밝혀져 있었다. 이즈음에는 깊은 어둠이 깔려 있었기 때문이다."], ["The lamps were also lit in the study or library, and I found the Count lying on the sofa, reading, of all things in the world, an English Bradshaw’s Guide.", "서재, 곧 도서실에도 등잔이 밝혀져 있었고, 나는 백작이 소파에 누워, 세상에서 하필이면, 영국의 브래드쇼 안내서를 읽고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["When I came in he cleared the books and papers from the table; and with him I went into plans and deeds and figures of all sorts.", "내가 들어가자 그는 탁자에서 책과 서류를 치웠다. 그리고 나는 그와 함께 온갖 종류의 도면과 증서와 수치를 살폈다."], ["He was interested in everything, and asked me a myriad questions about the place and its surroundings.", "그는 모든 것에 관심을 보였고, 그 장소와 그 주변에 관해 수많은 질문을 나에게 던졌다."], ["He clearly had studied beforehand all he could get on the subject of the neighbourhood, for he evidently at the end knew very much more than I did.", "그는 분명 그 인근에 관해 손에 넣을 수 있는 모든 것을 미리 연구해 두었던 것이, 결국 나보다 훨씬 더 많이 알고 있는 것이 분명했기 때문이다."], ["When I remarked this, he answered:--", "내가 이 점을 말하자 그가 대답했다."]], [["“Well, but, my friend, is it not needful that I should?", "\"글쎄, 하지만, 나의 벗이여, 내가 그래야 하는 것이 마땅하지 않소?"], ["When I go there I shall be all alone, and my friend Harker Jonathan--nay, pardon me, I fall into my country’s habit of putting your patronymic first--my friend Jonathan Harker will not be by my side to correct and aid me.", "내가 그곳에 가면 나는 완전히 혼자일 것이고, 내 벗 하커 조너선은 — 아니, 미안하오, 그대의 성을 앞에 두는 우리 나라의 습관에 빠졌구려 — 내 벗 조너선 하커는 나를 바로잡고 도와주려고 내 곁에 있지 않을 테니 말이오."], ["He will be in Exeter, miles away, probably working at papers of the law with my other friend, Peter Hawkins. So!”", "그는 몇 마일이나 떨어진 엑서터에 있을 것이고, 아마 내 또 다른 벗 피터 호킨스와 함께 법률 서류를 다루고 있겠지. 그렇소!\""]], [["We went thoroughly into the business of the purchase of the estate at Purfleet.", "우리는 퍼플리트의 부동산 매입 건을 철저히 살폈다."], ["When I had told him the facts and got his signature to the necessary papers, and had written a letter with them ready to post to Mr. Hawkins, he began to ask me how I had come across so suitable a place.", "내가 그에게 사실들을 다 일러주고, 필요한 서류에 그의 서명을 받고, 그것들과 함께 호킨스 씨에게 부칠 편지까지 써두자, 그는 어떻게 그토록 알맞은 곳을 찾아냈는지 나에게 묻기 시작했다."], ["I read to him the notes which I had made at the time, and which I inscribe here:--", "나는 그 당시에 적어 두었던 메모를 그에게 읽어주었는데, 여기에 그것을 적어둔다."]], [["“At Purfleet, on a by-road, I came across just such a place as seemed to be required, and where was displayed a dilapidated notice that the place was for sale.", "\"퍼플리트에서, 샛길을 따라가다가, 나는 꼭 요구되는 듯한 그런 곳을 우연히 발견했는데, 거기에는 그곳이 매물이라는 낡아빠진 안내문이 내걸려 있었다."], ["It is surrounded by a high wall, of ancient structure, built of heavy stones, and has not been repaired for a large number of years. The closed gates are of heavy old oak and iron, all eaten with rust.", "그곳은 묵직한 돌로 쌓은 고색창연한 높은 담으로 둘러싸여 있고, 오랜 세월 동안 보수된 적이 없었다. 닫힌 대문은 묵직한 오래된 참나무와 쇠로 되어 있는데, 모두 녹으로 삭아 있었다."]], [["“The estate is called Carfax, no doubt a corruption of the old Quatre Face, as the house is four-sided, agreeing with the cardinal points of the compass.", "이 부동산은 카팩스라 불리는데, 의심할 바 없이 옛 콰트르 파스가 와전된 것이다. 그 집이 사방으로 되어 있어 나침반의 기본 방위와 들어맞기 때문이다."], ["It contains in all some twenty acres, quite surrounded by the solid stone wall above mentioned.", "그곳은 모두 합쳐 약 이십 에이커쯤 되며, 앞서 말한 단단한 돌담으로 빙 둘러싸여 있다."], ["There are many trees on it, which make it in places gloomy, and there is a deep, dark-looking pond or small lake, evidently fed by some springs, as the water is clear and flows away in a fair-sized stream.", "그곳에는 나무가 많아 군데군데 음침하고, 깊고 어두워 보이는 못, 곧 작은 호수가 있는데, 물이 맑고 제법 큰 시내를 이루며 흘러 나가는 것으로 보아 분명 어떤 샘물에서 물을 받는 듯하다."], ["The house is very large and of all periods back, I should say, to mediæval times, for one part is of stone immensely thick, with only a few windows high up and heavily barred with iron.", "집은 매우 크고, 내가 보기에 중세 시대까지 거슬러 올라가는 온갖 시대의 것이 섞여 있다. 한 부분은 엄청나게 두꺼운 돌로 되어 있고, 높은 곳에 창문이 몇 개밖에 없으며 쇠로 육중하게 빗장이 질러져 있기 때문이다."], ["It looks like part of a keep, and is close to an old chapel or church.", "그것은 성채의 일부처럼 보이고, 오래된 예배당, 곧 교회 가까이에 있다."], ["I could not enter it, as I had not the key of the door leading to it from the house, but I have taken with my kodak views of it from various points.", "집에서 거기로 통하는 문의 열쇠가 없어 나는 그 안으로 들어갈 수 없었으나, 코닥으로 여러 지점에서 그곳의 모습을 찍어 두었다."], ["The house has been added to, but in a very straggling way, and I can only guess at the amount of ground it covers, which must be very great.", "집은 증축되었으나 매우 들쭉날쭉한 방식으로 되어 있어, 그것이 차지하는 땅의 넓이는 짐작만 할 뿐인데, 분명 매우 클 것이다."], ["There are but few houses close at hand, one being a very large house only recently added to and formed into a private lunatic asylum.", "가까이에 집은 몇 채 없는데, 한 채는 최근에야 증축되어 사립 정신병원으로 꾸며진 매우 큰 집이다."], ["It is not, however, visible from the grounds.”", "그러나 그것은 이 부지에서는 보이지 않는다.\""]], [["When I had finished, he said:--", "내가 다 읽고 나자 그가 말했다."]], [["“I am glad that it is old and big.", "\"그곳이 오래되고 크다니 기쁘오."], ["I myself am of an old family, and to live in a new house would kill me.", "나 자신이 오래된 가문 출신이라, 새 집에서 사는 것은 나를 죽일 것이오."], ["A house cannot be made habitable in a day; and, after all, how few days go to make up a century.", "집은 하루 만에 살 만하게 만들어질 수 없소. 그리고 결국, 한 세기를 이루는 데에 며칠이나 필요하겠소."], ["I rejoice also that there is a chapel of old times.", "또 옛 시대의 예배당이 있다니 그것도 기쁘오."], ["We Transylvanian nobles love not to think that our bones may lie amongst the common dead.", "우리 트란실바니아 귀족은 우리 뼈가 천한 죽은 자들 사이에 묻힐지도 모른다는 생각을 좋아하지 않소."], ["I seek not gaiety nor mirth, not the bright voluptuousness of much sunshine and sparkling waters which please the young and gay.", "나는 환락도 흥겨움도, 젊고 명랑한 이들을 기쁘게 하는 풍부한 햇빛과 반짝이는 물의 밝은 관능도 구하지 않소. 나는 더 이상 젊지 않소."], ["I am no longer young; and my heart, through weary years of mourning over the dead, is not attuned to mirth.", "그리고 내 마음은, 죽은 자들을 애도하는 지친 세월을 거치며, 흥겨움에 맞추어져 있지 않소."], ["Moreover, the walls of my castle are broken; the shadows are many, and the wind breathes cold through the broken battlements and casements.", "게다가 내 성의 벽은 허물어져 있소. 그림자가 많고, 부서진 흉벽과 창틀 사이로 바람이 차갑게 숨쉬오."], ["I love the shade and the shadow, and would be alone with my thoughts when I may.”", "나는 그늘과 그림자를 사랑하며, 할 수 있을 때면 내 생각과 더불어 혼자이고 싶소.\""], ["Somehow his words and his look did not seem to accord, or else it was that his cast of face made his smile look malignant and saturnine.", "어쩐지 그의 말과 그의 표정은 들어맞지 않는 듯했다. 아니면 그의 얼굴 생김새가 그의 미소를 악의에 차고 음울해 보이게 한 것이었으리라."]], [["Presently, with an excuse, he left me, asking me to put all my papers together.", "이윽고 그는 양해를 구하고 나를 떠나며, 내 서류를 모두 한데 모아 두라고 부탁했다."], ["He was some little time away, and I began to look at some of the books around me.", "그는 잠시 자리를 비웠고, 나는 내 주위의 책 몇 권을 살펴보기 시작했다."], ["One was an atlas, which I found opened naturally at England, as if that map had been much used.", "한 권은 지도책이었는데, 자연스럽게 영국 면에서 펼쳐지는 것이 마치 그 지도가 많이 사용된 것 같았다."], ["On looking at it I found in certain places little rings marked, and on examining these I noticed that one was near London on the east side, manifestly where his new estate was situated; the other two were Exeter, and Whitby on the Yorkshire coast.", "그것을 보니 어떤 곳들에 작은 동그라미가 표시되어 있었고, 그것들을 살펴보니 하나는 런던의 동쪽 가까이로, 분명 그의 새 부동산이 자리한 곳이었다. 다른 두 개는 엑서터와, 요크셔 해안의 휘트비였다."]], [["It was the better part of an hour when the Count returned. “Aha!”", "백작이 돌아온 것은 한 시간이 다 되어갈 무렵이었다. \"아하!\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said; “still at your books? Good!", "\"아직도 책을 보고 있구려? 좋소!"], ["But you must not work always.", "그러나 언제나 일만 해서는 안 되오."], ["Come; I am informed that your supper is ready.”", "자, 그대의 저녁식사가 준비되었다고 알려 왔소.\""], ["He took my arm, and we went into the next room, where I found an excellent supper ready on the table.", "그는 내 팔을 잡았고, 우리는 옆방으로 갔는데, 거기에는 훌륭한 저녁식사가 식탁에 준비되어 있었다."], ["The Count again excused himself, as he had dined out on his being away from home.", "백작은 집을 비웠던 동안 밖에서 식사를 했다며 다시 양해를 구했다."], ["But he sat as on the previous night, and chatted whilst I ate.", "그러나 그는 전날 밤처럼 앉아, 내가 먹는 동안 이야기를 나누었다."], ["After supper I smoked, as on the last evening, and the Count stayed with me, chatting and asking questions on every conceivable subject, hour after hour.", "식사 후 나는 지난 저녁처럼 담배를 피웠고, 백작은 나와 함께 머물며 생각할 수 있는 온갖 주제에 관해 이야기하고 질문하기를 한 시간이고 한 시간이고 이어갔다."], ["I felt that it was getting very late indeed, but I did not say anything, for I felt under obligation to meet my host’s wishes in every way.", "나는 정말로 매우 늦어지고 있음을 느꼈으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다. 모든 면에서 주인의 바람을 맞추어야 할 의무를 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["I was not sleepy, as the long sleep yesterday had fortified me; but I could not help experiencing that chill which comes over one at the coming of the dawn, which is like, in its way, the turn of the tide.", "어제 오래 잔 덕에 기운이 났던 터라 졸리지는 않았다. 그러나 새벽이 다가올 때 사람에게 엄습하는 그 한기를 느끼지 않을 수 없었는데, 그것은 나름대로 조수가 바뀌는 때와 비슷하다."], ["They say that people who are near death die generally at the change to the dawn or at the turn of the tide; any one who has when tired, and tied as it were to his post, experienced this change in the atmosphere can well believe it.", "죽음에 가까운 사람들은 대체로 새벽으로 바뀔 때나 조수가 바뀔 때 죽는다고들 한다. 지쳐 있으면서도 말하자면 제 자리에 매여, 대기의 이 변화를 겪어본 사람이라면 누구든 그것을 충분히 믿을 수 있을 것이다."], ["All at once we heard the crow of a cock coming up with preternatural shrillness through the clear morning air; Count Dracula, jumping to his feet, said:--", "갑자기 우리는 맑은 아침 공기를 뚫고 초자연적인 날카로움으로 솟아오르는 수탉 울음소리를 들었다. 드라큘라 백작은 벌떡 일어서며 말했다."]], [["“Why, there is the morning again! How remiss I am to let you stay up so long.", "\"아니, 또 아침이 왔구려! 그대를 이토록 오래 깨어 있게 하다니, 내가 어찌 이리 무심한지."], ["You must make your conversation regarding my dear new country of England less interesting, so that I may not forget how time flies by us,”", "그대는 나의 사랑스러운 새 나라 영국에 관한 그대의 이야기를 좀 덜 흥미롭게 해주어야겠소. 그래야 시간이 우리 곁을 흘러가는 것을 내가 잊지 않을 테니 말이오.\""], ["and, with a courtly bow, he quickly left me.", "그러고는 정중하게 절을 하며 그는 서둘러 나를 떠났다."]], [["I went into my own room and drew the curtains, but there was little to notice; my window opened into the courtyard, all I could see was the warm grey of quickening sky.", "나는 내 방으로 들어가 커튼을 쳤으나, 눈여겨볼 것은 거의 없었다. 내 창문은 안마당으로 나 있어, 보이는 것이라곤 깨어나는 하늘의 따뜻한 잿빛뿐이었다."], ["So I pulled the curtains again, and have written of this day.", "그래서 나는 다시 커튼을 치고, 이 하루에 관해 적었다."]], [["8 May.--I began to fear as I wrote in this book that I was getting too diffuse; but now I am glad that I went into detail from the first, for there is something so strange about this place and all in it that I cannot but feel uneasy.", "5월 8일. — 이 책에 글을 쓰면서 나는 내가 너무 장황해지는 것이 아닌가 두려워지기 시작했다. 그러나 이제 나는 처음부터 세세하게 적은 것이 기쁘다."], ["I wish I were safe out of it, or that I had never come. It may be that this strange night-existence is telling on me; but would that that were all!", "이곳과 이곳의 모든 것에는 무언가 너무도 기이한 데가 있어 나는 불안을 느끼지 않을 수 없기 때문이다. 나는 이곳에서 무사히 벗어나 있기를, 아니면 아예 오지 않았기를 바란다."], ["If there were any one to talk to I could bear it, but there is no one.", "어쩌면 이 기이한 밤의 삶이 나에게 영향을 미치고 있는지도 모른다. 그러나 그것만이라면 좋으련만!"], ["I have only the Count to speak with, and he!--I fear I am myself the only living soul within the place.", "이야기 나눌 누군가가 있다면 나는 견딜 수 있겠으나, 아무도 없다. 나에게는 말을 건넬 상대라곤 백작뿐인데, 그런데 그가!"], ["Let me be prosaic so far as facts can be; it will help me to bear up, and imagination must not run riot with me.", "— 두렵건대 이곳에서 살아 있는 영혼이라곤 나 자신뿐인 듯하다. 사실에 관한 한 나는 무미건조하게 굴자. 그것이 내가 버텨내는 데 도움이 될 것이고, 상상이 나를 제멋대로 날뛰게 두어서는 안 된다."], ["If it does I am lost.", "만일 그렇게 되면 나는 끝장이다."], ["Let me say at once how I stand--or seem to.", "내가 어떤 처지에 있는지 — 혹은 있는 듯한지 — 곧장 말해 두자."]], [["I only slept a few hours when I went to bed, and feeling that I could not sleep any more, got up.", "나는 잠자리에 들었을 때 몇 시간밖에 자지 못했고, 더는 잘 수 없겠다 싶어 일어났다."], ["I had hung my shaving glass by the window, and was just beginning to shave.", "나는 면도 거울을 창가에 걸어두었고, 막 면도를 시작하려던 참이었다."], ["Suddenly I felt a hand on my shoulder, and heard the Count’s voice saying to me, “Good-morning.”", "갑자기 어깨에 손이 닿는 것이 느껴졌고, 백작이 나에게 \"안녕히 주무셨소\" 하고 말하는 목소리가 들렸다."], ["I started, for it amazed me that I had not seen him, since the reflection of the glass covered the whole room behind me.", "나는 깜짝 놀랐다. 거울에 비친 상이 내 뒤의 방 전체를 담고 있었는데도 그를 보지 못했다는 것이 놀라웠기 때문이다."], ["In starting I had cut myself slightly, but did not notice it at the moment.", "놀라는 바람에 나는 살짝 베었으나, 그 순간에는 알아채지 못했다."], ["Having answered the Count’s salutation, I turned to the glass again to see how I had been mistaken.", "백작의 인사에 답하고 나서, 나는 내가 어떻게 잘못 보았는지 알아보려고 다시 거울을 향했다."], ["This time there could be no error, for the man was close to me, and I could see him over my shoulder.", "이번에는 착오일 리가 없었다. 그 사람이 내 가까이에 있어 어깨 너머로 그를 볼 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["But there was no reflection of him in the mirror!", "그러나 거울에는 그의 모습이 비치지 않았다!"], ["The whole room behind me was displayed; but there was no sign of a man in it, except myself.", "내 뒤의 방 전체가 비쳤으나, 그 안에는 나 자신 말고는 사람의 흔적이 없었다."], ["This was startling, and, coming on the top of so many strange things, was beginning to increase that vague feeling of uneasiness which I always have when the Count is near; but at the instant I saw that the cut had bled a little, and the blood was trickling over my chin.", "이것은 깜짝 놀랄 일이었고, 그토록 많은 기이한 일들에 더해진 터라, 백작이 가까이 있을 때면 늘 느끼는 그 막연한 불안감을 키우기 시작했다. 그러나 바로 그 순간 나는 베인 자리에서 피가 조금 났고, 피가 턱을 타고 흐르는 것을 보았다."], ["I laid down the razor, turning as I did so half round to look for some sticking plaster.", "나는 면도칼을 내려놓고, 그러면서 반쯤 돌아서서 반창고를 찾았다."], ["When the Count saw my face, his eyes blazed with a sort of demoniac fury, and he suddenly made a grab at my throat.", "백작은 내 얼굴을 보자 두 눈에 일종의 악마적인 분노가 타올랐고, 갑자기 내 목을 움켜쥐려 달려들었다."], ["I drew away, and his hand touched the string of beads which held the crucifix.", "내가 몸을 빼자 그의 손이 십자고상을 매단 묵주 끈에 닿았다."], ["It made an instant change in him, for the fury passed so quickly that I could hardly believe that it was ever there.", "그것은 그를 순식간에 바꾸어 놓았으니, 그 분노가 어찌나 빨리 가셨던지 나는 그것이 정말로 거기 있었던 것인지 도무지 믿을 수 없을 정도였다."]], [["“Take care,”", "\"조심하시오,\""], ["he said, “take care how you cut yourself.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"베이지 않도록 조심하시오."], ["It is more dangerous than you think in this country.”", "이 나라에서는 그대가 생각하는 것보다 더 위험하오.\""], ["Then seizing the shaving glass, he went on: “And this is the wretched thing that has done the mischief.", "그러고는 면도 거울을 움켜쥐고 말을 이었다. \"그리고 이것이 그 못된 짓을 한 형편없는 물건이오."], ["It is a foul bauble of man’s vanity.", "인간의 허영심이 빚은 더러운 노리개지."], ["Away with it!”", "치워버려야 하오!\""], ["and opening the heavy window with one wrench of his terrible hand, he flung out the glass, which was shattered into a thousand pieces on the stones of the courtyard far below. Then he withdrew without a word.", "그러고는 그 끔찍한 손으로 한 번 비틀어 육중한 창문을 열고는 거울을 밖으로 던져버렸고, 거울은 저 아래 안마당의 돌바닥에 떨어져 산산조각이 났다. 그러고 나서 그는 한마디 말도 없이 물러갔다."], ["It is very annoying, for I do not see how I am to shave, unless in my watch-case or the bottom of the shaving-pot, which is fortunately of metal.", "이것은 매우 성가신 일인데, 회중시계 뚜껑이나, 다행히 금속으로 된 면도용 그릇 바닥이 아니고서는 어떻게 면도를 해야 할지 모르겠기 때문이다."]], [["When I went into the dining-room, breakfast was prepared; but I could not find the Count anywhere.", "내가 식당으로 갔을 때 아침식사는 준비되어 있었다. 그러나 어디에서도 백작을 찾을 수 없었다."], ["So I breakfasted alone. It is strange that as yet I have not seen the Count eat or drink.", "그래서 나는 혼자 아침을 먹었다. 아직 백작이 먹거나 마시는 것을 보지 못한 것이 이상하다."], ["He must be a very peculiar man! After breakfast I did a little exploring in the castle.", "그는 분명 매우 별난 사람임에 틀림없다! 아침식사 후 나는 성안을 조금 탐험했다."], ["I went out on the stairs, and found a room looking towards the South.", "나는 계단으로 나가 남쪽을 향한 방 하나를 발견했다."], ["The view was magnificent, and from where I stood there was every opportunity of seeing it.", "경치는 장관이었고, 내가 선 곳에서 그것을 볼 기회가 충분했다."], ["The castle is on the very edge of a terrible precipice.", "성은 끔찍한 절벽의 바로 가장자리에 있다."], ["A stone falling from the window would fall a thousand feet without touching anything!", "창문에서 떨어진 돌은 아무것에도 닿지 않고 천 피트를 떨어질 것이다!"], ["As far as the eye can reach is a sea of green tree tops, with occasionally a deep rift where there is a chasm.", "눈이 닿는 데까지 초록빛 나무 우듬지의 바다였고, 군데군데 협곡이 있는 깊은 틈이 있었다."], ["Here and there are silver threads where the rivers wind in deep gorges through the forests.", "여기저기에 은빛 실 같은 것들이 보였는데, 강이 숲을 가로질러 깊은 골짜기를 굽이쳐 흐르는 것이었다."]], [["But I am not in heart to describe beauty, for when I had seen the view I explored further; doors, doors, doors everywhere, and all locked and bolted.", "그러나 나는 아름다움을 묘사할 마음이 들지 않으니, 그 경치를 본 뒤 나는 더 탐험했기 때문이다. 문, 문, 어디에나 문이었고, 모두 잠기고 빗장이 질러져 있었다."], ["In no place save from the windows in the castle walls is there an available exit.", "성벽의 창문 말고는 어디에도 나갈 수 있는 출구가 없었다."]], [["The castle is a veritable prison, and I am a prisoner!", "성은 그야말로 감옥이고, 나는 죄수다!"]], [["When I found that I was a prisoner a sort of wild feeling came over me.", "「조너선 하커의 일기」(계속)\n내가 갇힌 몸임을 깨닫자 일종의 광적인 감정이 나를 사로잡았다."], ["I rushed up and down the stairs, trying every door and peering out of every window I could find; but after a little the conviction of my helplessness overpowered all other feelings.", "나는 계단을 오르내리며 닥치는 대로 문이란 문은 모두 열어보고, 눈에 띄는 창이란 창은 모두 내다보았다. 그러나 얼마 지나지 않아 내가 무력하다는 확신이 다른 모든 감정을 압도했다."], ["When I look back after a few hours I think I must have been mad for the time, for I behaved much as a rat does in a trap.", "몇 시간이 지난 뒤 돌이켜보면, 그때 나는 잠시 제정신이 아니었던 모양이다. 마치 덫에 걸린 쥐처럼 행동했으니 말이다."], ["When, however, the conviction had come to me that I was helpless I sat down quietly--as quietly as I have ever done anything in my life--and began to think over what was best to be done.", "그러나 내가 무력하다는 확신이 들자, 나는 조용히 — 내 평생 그토록 조용히 무언가를 한 적이 없을 만큼 조용히 — 자리에 앉아, 어떻게 하는 것이 최선일지 곰곰이 생각하기 시작했다."], ["I am thinking still, and as yet have come to no definite conclusion.", "나는 지금도 생각하고 있으나, 아직 뚜렷한 결론에는 이르지 못했다."], ["Of one thing only am I certain; that it is no use making my ideas known to the Count.", "다만 한 가지만은 확실하다. 곧, 내 생각을 백작에게 알려보아야 아무 소용이 없다는 것이다."], ["He knows well that I am imprisoned; and as he has done it himself, and has doubtless his own motives for it, he would only deceive me if I trusted him fully with the facts.", "그는 내가 갇혀 있음을 잘 알고 있다. 게다가 그 일을 그 자신이 저질렀고, 거기에는 의심할 바 없이 그 나름의 동기가 있을 터이니, 내가 이 사실을 그에게 모두 털어놓고 믿는다면 그는 나를 속이기만 할 것이다."], ["So far as I can see, my only plan will be to keep my knowledge and my fears to myself, and my eyes open.", "내가 보기에 내 유일한 방책은 내가 아는 것과 두려워하는 것을 나 혼자만 간직하고, 눈을 부릅뜨고 있는 것뿐이다."], ["I am, I know, either being deceived, like a baby, by my own fears, or else I am in desperate straits; and if the latter be so, I need, and shall need, all my brains to get through.", "나는 알고 있다. 나는 갓난아기처럼 내 자신의 두려움에 속고 있거나, 아니면 절체절명의 궁지에 빠져 있거나 둘 중 하나라는 것을. 그리고 만일 후자라면, 나는 이 고비를 헤쳐 나가기 위해 내 모든 지혜가 필요하며, 앞으로도 필요할 것이다."]], [["I had hardly come to this conclusion when I heard the great door below shut, and knew that the Count had returned.", "이런 결론에 막 다다랐을 때, 나는 아래쪽의 큰 문이 닫히는 소리를 듣고 백작이 돌아왔음을 알았다."], ["He did not come at once into the library, so I went cautiously to my own room and found him making the bed.", "그는 곧바로 서재로 오지 않았으므로, 나는 조심스레 내 방으로 갔는데, 가서 보니 그가 침대를 정돈하고 있었다."], ["This was odd, but only confirmed what I had all along thought--that there were no servants in the house.", "이것은 이상한 일이었으나, 내가 줄곧 생각해온 바 — 곧 이 집에는 하인이 없다는 사실 — 을 확인해줄 뿐이었다."], ["When later I saw him through the chink of the hinges of the door laying the table in the dining-room, I was assured of it; for if he does himself all these menial offices, surely it is proof that there is no one else to do them.", "그 뒤에 그가 식당에서 식탁을 차리고 있는 것을 문 경첩 틈으로 보았을 때, 나는 그것을 확신하게 되었다. 그가 이런 잡일을 모두 손수 한다면, 그것을 대신 해줄 다른 사람이 없다는 증거임이 분명하기 때문이다."], ["This gave me a fright, for if there is no one else in the castle, it must have been the Count himself who was the driver of the coach that brought me here.", "이것은 나를 두렵게 했으니, 만일 이 성에 다른 사람이 없다면, 나를 이곳까지 태워온 마차의 마부가 바로 백작 자신이었음에 틀림없기 때문이다."], ["This is a terrible thought; for if so, what does it mean that he could control the wolves, as he did, by only holding up his hand in silence.", "이것은 끔찍한 생각이다. 만일 그렇다면, 그가 그랬듯이 말없이 손을 들어올리는 것만으로 늑대들을 부릴 수 있었다는 것은 대체 무슨 뜻인가?"], ["How was it that all the people at Bistritz and on the coach had some terrible fear for me? What meant the giving of the crucifix, of the garlic, of the wild rose, of the mountain ash?", "비스트리차의 사람들과 마차 안의 사람들이 모두 나를 위해 그토록 끔찍하게 두려워한 것은 어찌된 일인가? 십자고상과 마늘과 들장미와 마가목을 건넨 것은 무슨 뜻이었던가?"], ["Bless that good, good woman who hung the crucifix round my neck!", "내 목에 십자고상을 걸어준 그 착하고 착한 부인에게 축복이 있기를!"], ["for it is a comfort and a strength to me whenever I touch it.", "그것을 만질 때마다 그것이 나에게 위안과 힘이 되어주니 말이다."], ["It is odd that a thing which I have been taught to regard with disfavour and as idolatrous should in a time of loneliness and trouble be of help.", "내가 못마땅하게, 또 우상숭배적인 것으로 여기도록 배워온 물건이 외로움과 곤경의 때에 도움이 된다는 것은 참으로 기이한 일이다."], ["Is it that there is something in the essence of the thing itself, or that it is a medium, a tangible help, in conveying memories of sympathy and comfort?", "그것은 그 물건 자체의 본질에 무언가가 있기 때문일까, 아니면 그것이 동정과 위안의 기억을 전해주는 매개물, 곧 손에 잡히는 도움이기 때문일까?"], ["Some time, if it may be, I must examine this matter and try to make up my mind about it.", "언젠가 가능하다면, 나는 이 문제를 살펴 마음을 정해보아야겠다."], ["In the meantime I must find out all I can about Count Dracula, as it may help me to understand.", "그동안에는 드라큘라 백작에 관해 알 수 있는 모든 것을 알아내야겠다. 그것이 이해에 도움이 될지도 모르니."], ["To-night he may talk of himself, if I turn the conversation that way.", "오늘 밤 내가 대화를 그쪽으로 돌린다면, 그가 자신에 관해 이야기할지도 모른다."], ["I must be very careful, however, not to awake his suspicion.", "그러나 그의 의심을 사지 않도록 매우 조심해야 한다."]], [["Midnight.--I have had a long talk with the Count.", "자정. — 나는 백작과 긴 이야기를 나누었다."], ["I asked him a few questions on Transylvania history, and he warmed up to the subject wonderfully.", "트란실바니아 역사에 관해 몇 가지 물었더니, 그는 그 주제에 놀랄 만큼 열을 올렸다."], ["In his speaking of things and people, and especially of battles, he spoke as if he had been present at them all.", "사물과 사람들에 관해, 특히 전투에 관해 이야기할 때, 그는 마치 그 모든 자리에 직접 있었던 것처럼 말했다."], ["This he afterwards explained by saying that to a boyar the pride of his house and name is his own pride, that their glory is his glory, that their fate is his fate.", "이것을 그는 나중에 이렇게 설명했다. 보야르에게는 가문과 이름의 긍지가 곧 자신의 긍지이며, 그들의 영광이 곧 그의 영광이요, 그들의 운명이 곧 그의 운명이라고."], ["Whenever he spoke of his house he always said “we,” and spoke almost in the plural, like a king speaking.", "그는 자기 가문에 관해 이야기할 때면 언제나 \"우리\"라고 했고, 마치 왕이 말하듯 거의 복수형으로 말했다."], ["I wish I could put down all he said exactly as he said it, for to me it was most fascinating.", "그가 한 말을 모두 그가 말한 그대로 적어둘 수 있다면 좋으련만. 나에게 그것은 더없이 매혹적이었으니 말이다."], ["It seemed to have in it a whole history of the country.", "그 안에 이 나라의 역사 전체가 담겨 있는 듯했다."], ["He grew excited as he spoke, and walked about the room pulling his great white moustache and grasping anything on which he laid his hands as though he would crush it by main strength.", "그는 이야기하면서 점점 흥분하여, 방 안을 거닐며 큼직한 흰 콧수염을 잡아당기고, 손에 닿는 것은 무엇이든 마치 온 힘으로 으스러뜨리려는 듯 움켜쥐었다. 그가 한 말 가운데 한 대목을 나는 되도록 그대로 옮겨 적어두겠다."], ["One thing he said which I shall put down as nearly as I can; for it tells in its way the story of his race:--", "그것이 그 나름의 방식으로 그 종족의 이야기를 전해주기 때문이다. * 보야르: 동유럽의 귀족 계급."]], [["“We Szekelys have a right to be proud, for in our veins flows the blood of many brave races who fought as the lion fights, for lordship.", "\"우리 세케이인은 긍지를 가질 자격이 있소. 우리의 핏줄에는 사자가 싸우듯 패권을 위해 싸운 숱한 용맹한 종족들의 피가 흐르고 있으니 말이오."], ["Here, in the whirlpool of European races, the Ugric tribe bore down from Iceland the fighting spirit which Thor and Wodin gave them, which their Berserkers displayed to such fell intent on the seaboards of Europe, ay, and of Asia and Africa too, till the peoples thought that the were-wolves themselves had come.", "이곳, 유럽 종족들의 소용돌이 속으로, 우그리아 부족이 아이슬란드에서 토르와 보덴이 그들에게 준 그 투쟁의 기상을 가지고 밀고 내려왔소. 그들의 베르세르크들이 유럽의 해안에서, 그렇소, 아시아와 아프리카의 해안에서도 그토록 무시무시한 의지로 그 기상을 떨쳐 보였으니, 사람들은 늑대인간 자체가 나타난 줄로 여겼소."], ["Here, too, when they came, they found the Huns, whose warlike fury had swept the earth like a living flame, till the dying peoples held that in their veins ran the blood of those old witches, who, expelled from Scythia had mated with the devils in the desert.", "이곳에서, 그들이 왔을 때, 그들은 또한 훈족과 마주쳤소. 그 호전적인 광포함은 살아 있는 불길처럼 온 땅을 휩쓸어, 죽어가던 백성들은 그들의 핏줄에 스키타이에서 쫓겨나 사막에서 악마들과 짝지은 그 옛 마녀들의 피가 흐른다고 믿었소. 어리석은 자들, 어리석은 자들!"], ["Fools, fools! What devil or what witch was ever so great as Attila, whose blood is in these veins?”", "이 핏줄 속에 그 피가 흐르고 있는 아틸라만큼 위대한 악마나 마녀가 일찍이 어디 있었소?\""], ["He held up his arms.", "그는 두 팔을 들어올렸다."], ["“Is it a wonder that we were a conquering race; that we were proud; that when the Magyar, the Lombard, the Avar, the Bulgar, or the Turk poured his thousands on our frontiers, we drove them back?", "\"우리가 정복하는 종족이었다는 것이, 우리가 긍지를 가졌다는 것이, 마자르인이든 롬바르드인이든 아바르인이든 불가르인이든 터키인이든 그 수천의 무리를 우리 국경으로 쏟아부었을 때 우리가 그들을 물리쳤다는 것이 놀라운 일이오?"], ["Is it strange that when Arpad and his legions swept through the Hungarian fatherland he found us here when he reached the frontier; that the Honfoglalas was completed there?", "아르파드와 그의 군단이 헝가리 조국을 휩쓸고 지나갔을 때, 그가 국경에 이르러 이곳에서 우리를 발견했다는 것이, 혼포글랄라시가 그곳에서 완성되었다는 것이 이상한 일이오?"], ["And when the Hungarian flood swept eastward, the Szekelys were claimed as kindred by the victorious Magyars, and to us for centuries was trusted the guarding of the frontier of Turkey-land; ay, and more than that, endless duty of the frontier guard, for, as the Turks say, ‘water sleeps, and enemy is sleepless.’ Who more gladly than we throughout the Four Nations received the ‘bloody sword,’ or at its warlike call flocked quicker to the standard of the King?", "그리고 헝가리의 물결이 동쪽으로 밀려갔을 때, 세케이인은 승리한 마자르인에게 한 핏줄로 인정받았고, 그리하여 수 세기 동안 우리에게 터키 땅의 국경 수비가 맡겨졌소. 그렇소, 그뿐 아니라, 국경 수비병의 끝없는 임무까지도 말이오. 터키인들이 말하듯, '물은 잠들어도 적은 잠들지 않는' 법이니."], ["When was redeemed that great shame of my nation, the shame of Cassova, when the flags of the Wallach and the Magyar went down beneath the Crescent?", "네 민족 가운데 누가 우리보다 더 기꺼이 '피 묻은 칼'을 받았으며, 그 호전적인 부름에 누가 우리보다 더 빨리 왕의 깃발 아래로 모여들었소?"], ["Who was it but one of my own race who as Voivode crossed the Danube and beat the Turk on his own ground? This was a Dracula indeed!", "왈라키아인과 마자르인의 깃발이 초승달 깃발 아래 무너졌던, 내 민족의 그 크나큰 치욕, 카소바의 치욕을 씻은 것은 언제였소?"], ["Woe was it that his own unworthy brother, when he had fallen, sold his people to the Turk and brought the shame of slavery on them!", "보이보드로서 도나우 강을 건너 터키인을 그들의 땅에서 무찌른 것이 내 종족의 한 사람이 아니고 누구였소? 이야말로 진정한 드라큘라였소!"], ["Was it not this Dracula, indeed, who inspired that other of his race who in a later age again and again brought his forces over the great river into Turkey-land; who, when he was beaten back, came again, and again, and again, though he had to come alone from the bloody field where his troops were being slaughtered, since he knew that he alone could ultimately triumph!", "슬프게도, 그가 쓰러진 뒤 그의 못난 아우가 자기 백성을 터키인에게 팔아넘겨 그들에게 노예의 치욕을 안겼으니! 후대에 거듭거듭 자기 군대를 이끌고 그 큰 강을 건너 터키 땅으로 들어간, 격퇴당하고도 다시, 또다시, 또다시 쳐들어간, 그의 군대가 도륙당하는 피의 전장에서 홀로 돌아와야 했음에도 끝내 자기 혼자만이 승리할 수 있음을 알았기에 그리한, 그 종족의 또 다른 한 사람을 분기시킨 것이 바로 이 드라큘라가 아니었소?"], ["They said that he thought only of himself. Bah!", "사람들은 그가 오직 자기 자신만을 생각했다고 했소. 흥!"], ["what good are peasants without a leader?", "지휘자 없는 농민이 무슨 소용이오?"], ["Where ends the war without a brain and heart to conduct it?", "그것을 이끌 두뇌와 심장이 없으면 전쟁은 어디서 끝나겠소?"], ["Again, when, after the battle of Mohács, we threw off the Hungarian yoke, we of the Dracula blood were amongst their leaders, for our spirit would not brook that we were not free.", "또한, 모하치 전투 뒤에 우리가 헝가리의 멍에를 떨쳐버렸을 때, 우리 드라큘라의 피를 이은 자들이 그 지도자들 가운데 있었으니, 우리의 기상은 우리가 자유롭지 못함을 견디지 못했기 때문이오."], ["Ah, young sir, the Szekelys--and the Dracula as their heart’s blood, their brains, and their swords--can boast a record that mushroom growths like the Hapsburgs and the Romanoffs can never reach.", "아, 젊은 양반, 세케이인은 — 그리고 그들의 심장의 피요, 두뇌요, 칼인 드라큘라 가문은 — 합스부르크나 로마노프 같은 버섯처럼 솟아난 것들이 결코 따를 수 없는 기록을 자랑할 수 있소."], ["The warlike days are over.", "호전의 시대는 끝났소."], ["Blood is too precious a thing in these days of dishonourable peace; and the glories of the great races are as a tale that is told.”", "이 명예롭지 못한 평화의 시대에 피는 너무도 귀한 것이 되었고, 위대한 종족들의 영광은 다 들려준 옛이야기가 되어버렸소.\""]], [["It was by this time close on morning, and we went to bed.", "이 무렵에는 어느덧 아침이 가까워, 우리는 잠자리에 들었다. (비망."], ["(Mem., this diary seems horribly like the beginning of the “Arabian Nights,”", "이 일기는 어쩐지 「아라비안나이트」의 첫머리와 끔찍하리만큼 닮은 듯하다."], ["for everything has to break off at cockcrow--or like the ghost of Hamlet’s father.)", "모든 것이 닭 우는 소리에 끊겨야 하니 말이다. 아니면 햄릿의 아버지 유령과도 닮았다.)"]], [["12 May.--Let me begin with facts--bare, meagre facts, verified by books and figures, and of which there can be no doubt.", "5월 12일. — 사실부터, 곧 책과 수치로 검증된, 의심할 여지가 없는 적나라하고 빈약한 사실부터 시작하자."], ["I must not confuse them with experiences which will have to rest on my own observation, or my memory of them.", "나는 그것을 내 자신의 관찰이나 그에 대한 기억에 의지할 수밖에 없는 경험들과 혼동해서는 안 된다."], ["Last evening when the Count came from his room he began by asking me questions on legal matters and on the doing of certain kinds of business.", "어젯밤 백작은 자기 방에서 나오자, 먼저 법률 문제와 어떤 종류의 사업 처리에 관해 나에게 질문을 하기 시작했다."], ["I had spent the day wearily over books, and, simply to keep my mind occupied, went over some of the matters I had been examining at Lincoln’s Inn.", "나는 그날 하루를 책과 씨름하며 지루하게 보냈는데, 그저 머리를 다른 데에 쓰려고 링컨 법학원에서 검토하던 몇 가지 문제를 다시 짚어보던 참이었다."], ["There was a certain method in the Count’s inquiries, so I shall try to put them down in sequence; the knowledge may somehow or some time be useful to me.", "백작의 질문에는 어떤 방법론적 짜임새가 있었으므로, 나는 그것을 차례대로 적어보려 한다. 이 지식이 어떻게든, 어느 때엔가 나에게 쓸모가 있을지도 모르니."]], [["First, he asked if a man in England might have two solicitors or more.", "먼저 그는 영국에서 한 사람이 변호사를 둘 이상 둘 수 있는지 물었다."], ["I told him he might have a dozen if he wished, but that it would not be wise to have more than one solicitor engaged in one transaction, as only one could act at a time, and that to change would be certain to militate against his interest.", "나는 원한다면 열둘이라도 둘 수 있으나, 한 거래에 한 명 이상의 변호사를 쓰는 것은 현명하지 못하다고 답했다. 한 번에 한 사람만 일을 처리할 수 있고, 변호사를 바꾸는 것은 틀림없이 그의 이익에 불리하게 작용할 것이기 때문이라고 했다."], ["He seemed thoroughly to understand, and went on to ask if there would be any practical difficulty in having one man to attend, say, to banking, and another to look after shipping, in case local help were needed in a place far from the home of the banking solicitor.", "그는 충분히 이해한 듯하더니, 이어서 만일 은행 거래를 맡은 변호사의 본거지에서 멀리 떨어진 곳에서 현지의 도움이 필요할 경우, 한 사람에게는 이를테면 은행 일을, 다른 사람에게는 선적 일을 맡기는 데에 실제로 어떤 어려움이 있는지 물었다."], ["I asked him to explain more fully, so that I might not by any chance mislead him, so he said:--", "나는 혹시라도 그를 그릇 인도하지 않도록 좀 더 자세히 설명해달라고 청했고, 그러자 그가 말했다."]], [["“I shall illustrate.", "\"예를 들어 설명하리다."], ["Your friend and mine, Mr. Peter Hawkins, from under the shadow of your beautiful cathedral at Exeter, which is far from London, buys for me through your good self my place at London. Good!", "당신의 친구이자 나의 친구인 피터 호킨스 씨가, 런던에서 멀리 떨어진 엑서터의 그 아름다운 대성당 그늘 아래에서, 당신이라는 훌륭한 분을 통해 나를 위하여 런던의 부동산을 사들인다고 합시다. 좋소! 그런데 여기서 솔직히 말해두리다."], ["Now here let me say frankly, lest you should think it strange that I have sought the services of one so far off from London instead of some one resident there, that my motive was that no local interest might be served save my wish only; and as one of London residence might, perhaps, have some purpose of himself or friend to serve, I went thus afield to seek my agent, whose labours should be only to my interest.", "내가 런던에 사는 누군가가 아니라 런던에서 그토록 멀리 떨어진 사람의 도움을 구한 것을 당신이 이상하게 여길지 모르니 말이오. 나의 동기는, 오직 나의 바람 외에 어떤 현지의 이해관계도 끼어들지 않도록 하려는 것이었소. 런던에 사는 사람이라면 어쩌면 자기 자신이나 친구의 어떤 속셈을 챙기려 할지도 모르기에, 나는 이렇게 멀리까지 와서 오직 나의 이익을 위해서만 일할 대리인을 찾은 것이오."], ["Now, suppose I, who have much of affairs, wish to ship goods, say, to Newcastle, or Durham, or Harwich, or Dover, might it not be that it could with more ease be done by consigning to one in these ports?”", "자, 이제 처리할 일이 많은 내가 이를테면 뉴캐슬이나 더럼, 하리치, 또는 도버로 물건을 부치고자 한다면, 그 항구들에 있는 누군가에게 맡기는 편이 더 수월하지 않겠소?\""], ["I answered that certainly it would be most easy, but that we solicitors had a system of agency one for the other, so that local work could be done locally on instruction from any solicitor, so that the client, simply placing himself in the hands of one man, could have his wishes carried out by him without further trouble.", "나는 분명 그편이 가장 수월할 것이나, 우리 변호사들은 서로를 위한 대리 제도를 갖추고 있어, 어느 변호사의 지시에 따라 현지의 일은 현지에서 처리될 수 있다고 답했다. 그러므로 의뢰인은 그저 한 사람에게 몸을 맡기기만 하면, 더 이상의 수고 없이 그 사람을 통해 자신의 바람을 이룰 수 있다고 했다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["said he, “I could be at liberty to direct myself. Is it not so?”", "그가 말했다. \"나는 자유로이 직접 지시할 수도 있는 것 아니오?\""]], [["“Of course,”", "\"물론입니다.\""], ["I replied; and “such is often done by men of business, who do not like the whole of their affairs to be known by any one person.”", "나는 답했다. \"사업가들 가운데에는 자기 일의 전모를 어느 한 사람이 다 알게 되는 것을 꺼려 종종 그렇게 하는 이들이 있습니다.\""]], [["“Good!” he said, and then went on to ask about the means of making consignments and the forms to be gone through, and of all sorts of difficulties which might arise, but by forethought could be guarded against.", "\"좋소!\" 그가 말하더니, 이어서 물건을 부치는 방법과 거쳐야 할 절차들, 그리고 일어날 법한 온갖 어려움들과, 그러나 미리 헤아려 대비할 수 있는 것들에 관해 물었다."], ["I explained all these things to him to the best of my ability, and he certainly left me under the impression that he would have made a wonderful solicitor, for there was nothing that he did not think of or foresee.", "나는 이 모든 것을 내 능력껏 그에게 설명해주었는데, 그는 분명 자기가 훌륭한 변호사가 되었으리라는 인상을 나에게 남겼다. 그가 생각하거나 내다보지 못한 것이 하나도 없었기 때문이다."], ["For a man who was never in the country, and who did not evidently do much in the way of business, his knowledge and acumen were wonderful.", "한 번도 그 나라에 가본 적이 없고, 또 사업 방면으로 별다른 일을 해본 적이 없는 게 분명한 사람치고는, 그의 지식과 통찰력은 놀라웠다."], ["When he had satisfied himself on these points of which he had spoken, and I had verified all as well as I could by the books available, he suddenly stood up and said:--", "그가 이야기한 이 점들에 관해 스스로 만족하고, 또 내가 구할 수 있는 책으로 모든 것을 되도록 잘 검증해주자, 그는 갑자기 일어서서 말했다."]], [["“Have you written since your first letter to our friend Mr. Peter Hawkins, or to any other?”", "\"우리의 친구 피터 호킨스 씨에게, 또는 다른 누구에게라도, 첫 편지 이후로 편지를 쓴 적이 있소?\""], ["It was with some bitterness in my heart that I answered that I had not, that as yet I had not seen any opportunity of sending letters to anybody.", "나는 가슴에 약간의 쓰라림을 안고, 그런 적이 없으며, 아직까지 누구에게도 편지를 부칠 기회를 보지 못했다고 답했다."]], [["“Then write now, my young friend,”", "\"그러면 지금 쓰시오, 나의 젊은 친구.\""], ["he said, laying a heavy hand on my shoulder: “write to our friend and to any other; and say, if it will please you, that you shall stay with me until a month from now.”", "그가 묵직한 손을 내 어깨에 얹으며 말했다. \"우리의 친구에게, 그리고 다른 누구에게라도 쓰시오. 그리고 괜찮다면, 지금부터 한 달 뒤까지 나와 함께 머물 것이라고 적으시오.\""]], [["“Do you wish me to stay so long?”", "\"제가 그토록 오래 머물기를 바라십니까?\""], ["I asked, for my heart grew cold at the thought.", "나는 물었다. 그 생각에 가슴이 서늘해졌기 때문이다."]], [["“I desire it much; nay, I will take no refusal.", "\"진심으로 그것을 바라오. 아니, 거절은 받아들이지 않겠소."], ["When your master, employer, what you will, engaged that someone should come on his behalf, it was understood that my needs only were to be consulted. I have not stinted.", "당신의 주인이든, 고용주든, 무어라 부르든 그가 자기를 대신해 누군가를 보내기로 약속했을 때, 오직 나의 필요만을 살피기로 양해된 것이오. 나는 인색하게 굴지 않았소."], ["Is it not so?”", "그렇지 않소?\""]], [["What could I do but bow acceptance? It was Mr.", "내가 고개 숙여 승낙하는 것 말고 무엇을 할 수 있었겠는가?"], ["Hawkins’s interest, not mine, and I had to think of him, not myself; and besides, while Count Dracula was speaking, there was that in his eyes and in his bearing which made me remember that I was a prisoner, and that if I wished it I could have no choice.", "그것은 나의 이익이 아니라 호킨스 씨의 이익이었으니, 나는 나 자신이 아니라 그를 생각해야 했다. 게다가 드라큘라 백작이 말하는 동안, 그의 눈빛과 그의 태도에는 내가 갇힌 몸이며, 내가 원한다 한들 나에게는 아무런 선택의 여지가 없음을 떠올리게 하는 무언가가 있었다."], ["The Count saw his victory in my bow, and his mastery in the trouble of my face, for he began at once to use them, but in his own smooth, resistless way:--", "백작은 내가 고개를 숙이는 데에서 자신의 승리를 보았고, 내 얼굴에 어린 곤혹스러움에서 자신의 지배력을 보았다. 그는 곧바로 그것을 써먹기 시작했으니, 다만 그 나름의 매끄럽고 거역할 수 없는 방식으로였다."]], [["“I pray you, my good young friend, that you will not discourse of things other than business in your letters.", "\"부탁하오, 나의 좋은 젊은 친구, 편지에 사업 외의 일은 적지 말기를."], ["It will doubtless please your friends to know that you are well, and that you look forward to getting home to them.", "당신이 잘 지내고 있으며, 그들에게로 돌아갈 날을 고대하고 있다는 것을 알면 당신의 벗들도 분명 기뻐할 것이오."], ["Is it not so?”", "그렇지 않소?\""], ["As he spoke he handed me three sheets of note-paper and three envelopes.", "그는 이렇게 말하며 나에게 편지지 석 장과 봉투 세 개를 건넸다."], ["They were all of the thinnest foreign post, and looking at them, then at him, and noticing his quiet smile, with the sharp, canine teeth lying over the red underlip, I understood as well as if he had spoken that I should be careful what I wrote, for he would be able to read it.", "모두 더없이 얇은 외국제 편지지였는데, 그것들을 보고, 이어서 그를 보고, 붉은 아랫입술 위로 드러난 날카로운 송곳니와 함께 그가 짓는 조용한 미소를 알아챘을 때, 나는 그가 말로 한 것이나 다름없이 분명히 깨달았다. 곧, 내가 쓰는 것을 그가 읽을 수 있을 터이니 무엇을 쓰는지 조심해야 한다는 것을."], ["So I determined to write only formal notes now, but to write fully to Mr. Hawkins in secret, and also to Mina, for to her I could write in shorthand, which would puzzle the Count, if he did see it.", "그래서 나는 이제 형식적인 짧은 편지만 쓰되, 호킨스 씨에게는 몰래 자세히 쓰고, 또 미나에게도 그리하기로 마음먹었다. 미나에게는 속기로 쓸 수 있으니, 백작이 설령 그것을 본다 해도 어리둥절할 것이기 때문이다."], ["When I had written my two letters I sat quiet, reading a book whilst the Count wrote several notes, referring as he wrote them to some books on his table.", "두 통의 편지를 다 쓰고 나서, 나는 조용히 앉아 책을 읽었고, 그동안 백작은 식탁 위의 책 몇 권을 참조해가며 여러 통의 편지를 썼다."], ["Then he took up my two and placed them with his own, and put by his writing materials, after which, the instant the door had closed behind him, I leaned over and looked at the letters, which were face down on the table.", "그러고 나서 그는 내 두 통을 집어 자기 것과 함께 두고, 필기도구를 치웠다. 그가 나가고 문이 등 뒤에서 닫히는 순간, 나는 몸을 기울여 식탁 위에 엎어놓인 편지들을 들여다보았다."], ["I felt no compunction in doing so, for under the circumstances I felt that I should protect myself in every way I could.", "그렇게 하는 데에 아무런 거리낌도 느끼지 않았으니, 이런 처지에서는 내가 할 수 있는 모든 방법으로 나 자신을 지켜야 한다고 느꼈기 때문이다."]], [["One of the letters was directed to Samuel F. Billington, No.", "편지 가운데 하나는 휘트비 더 크레슨트 7번지의 새뮤얼 F."], ["7, The Crescent, Whitby, another to Herr Leutner, Varna; the third was to Coutts & Co., London, and the fourth to Herren Klopstock & Billreuth, bankers, Buda-Pesth.", "빌링턴 앞으로, 또 하나는 바르나의 로이트너 씨 앞으로 되어 있었다. 세 번째는 런던의 쿠츠 상회 앞으로, 네 번째는 부다페스트의 은행가 클롭슈토크 운트 빌로이트 상사 앞으로 되어 있었다."], ["The second and fourth were unsealed.", "두 번째와 네 번째는 봉해져 있지 않았다."], ["I was just about to look at them when I saw the door-handle move.", "내가 막 그것들을 들여다보려는데 문손잡이가 움직이는 것이 보였다."], ["I sank back in my seat, having just had time to replace the letters as they had been and to resume my book before the Count, holding still another letter in his hand, entered the room.", "나는 자리에 도로 주저앉았는데, 편지들을 본래 있던 대로 되돌려놓고 다시 책을 집어들 시간을 가까스로 가진 직후, 백작이 또 다른 편지 한 통을 손에 든 채 방으로 들어왔다."], ["He took up the letters on the table and stamped them carefully, and then turning to me, said:--", "그는 식탁 위의 편지들을 집어 조심스레 우표를 붙이고, 그러고는 나를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“I trust you will forgive me, but I have much work to do in private this evening.", "\"용서해주기 바라오. 오늘 저녁에는 혼자서 처리할 일이 많소."], ["You will, I hope, find all things as you wish.”", "모든 것이 당신 바라는 대로 갖추어져 있기를 바라오.\""], ["At the door he turned, and after a moment’s pause said:--", "문가에서 그는 돌아서더니, 잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 말했다."]], [["“Let me advise you, my dear young friend--nay, let me warn you with all seriousness, that should you leave these rooms you will not by any chance go to sleep in any other part of the castle.", "\"한마디 충고하리다, 나의 친애하는 젊은 친구 — 아니, 더없이 진지하게 경고하리다. 만일 이 방들을 떠나게 되거든, 무슨 일이 있어도 이 성의 다른 어느 곳에서도 잠들지 마시오."], ["It is old, and has many memories, and there are bad dreams for those who sleep unwisely.", "이 성은 오래되었고 많은 기억을 품고 있어, 어리석게 잠드는 자에게는 흉한 꿈이 따르오."], ["Be warned!", "명심하시오!"], ["Should sleep now or ever overcome you, or be like to do, then haste to your own chamber or to these rooms, for your rest will then be safe.", "지금이든 언제든 잠이 당신을 덮치거나 덮칠 듯하거든, 곧장 당신의 방이나 이 방들로 서둘러 가시오. 그래야 당신의 휴식이 안전할 것이오."], ["But if you be not careful in this respect, then”--He finished his speech in a gruesome way, for he motioned with his hands as if he were washing them.", "그러나 이 점에서 조심하지 않는다면, 그때에는 —\" 그는 으스스한 방식으로 말을 맺었으니, 마치 손을 씻는 듯한 시늉을 했기 때문이다. 나는 똑똑히 알아들었다."], ["I quite understood; my only doubt was as to whether any dream could be more terrible than the unnatural, horrible net of gloom and mystery which seemed closing around me.", "다만 한 가지 의심스러운 것은, 어떤 꿈인들 나를 에워싸며 조여드는 듯한 이 부자연스럽고 끔찍한 음울함과 수수께끼의 그물보다 더 무서울 수 있겠느냐는 것이었다."]], [["Later.--I endorse the last words written, but this time there is no doubt in question.", "그 뒤. — 나는 앞서 적은 마지막 말을 그대로 두지만, 이번에는 의심할 여지가 없다."], ["I shall not fear to sleep in any place where he is not.", "나는 그가 없는 곳이라면 어디서든 두려움 없이 잠들 것이다."], ["I have placed the crucifix over the head of my bed--I imagine that my rest is thus freer from dreams; and there it shall remain.", "나는 십자고상을 침대 머리맡에 두었다. 그렇게 하면 내 휴식이 꿈에서 더 자유로워지리라 여겨서이다. 그것은 그곳에 그대로 둘 것이다."]], [["When he left me I went to my room.", "그가 나를 떠난 뒤 나는 내 방으로 갔다."], ["After a little while, not hearing any sound, I came out and went up the stone stair to where I could look out towards the South.", "잠시 뒤 아무 소리도 들리지 않기에, 나는 밖으로 나와 돌계단을 올라 남쪽을 내다볼 수 있는 곳으로 갔다."], ["There was some sense of freedom in the vast expanse, inaccessible though it was to me, as compared with the narrow darkness of the courtyard.", "비록 나로서는 닿을 수 없는 곳이었으나, 그 광활한 펼쳐짐에는 안마당의 좁은 어둠에 비하면 어떤 자유로움의 느낌이 있었다."], ["Looking out on this, I felt that I was indeed in prison, and I seemed to want a breath of fresh air, though it were of the night.", "그것을 내다보며 나는 내가 정말로 감옥에 갇혀 있음을 느꼈고, 비록 밤공기일망정 신선한 바람을 한 모금 쐬고 싶어졌다."], ["I am beginning to feel this nocturnal existence tell on me.", "나는 이 밤의 생활이 나에게 영향을 미치기 시작했음을 느끼고 있다."], ["It is destroying my nerve.", "그것이 내 신경을 망가뜨리고 있다."], ["I start at my own shadow, and am full of all sorts of horrible imaginings.", "나는 내 그림자에도 흠칫 놀라고, 온갖 끔찍한 상상으로 가득 차 있다."], ["God knows that there is ground for my terrible fear in this accursed place!", "이 저주받은 곳에 내 끔찍한 두려움의 근거가 있음을 신은 아신다!"], ["I looked out over the beautiful expanse, bathed in soft yellow moonlight till it was almost as light as day.", "나는 부드러운 노란 달빛에 잠겨 거의 대낮처럼 밝아진 그 아름다운 펼쳐짐을 내다보았다."], ["In the soft light the distant hills became melted, and the shadows in the valleys and gorges of velvety blackness.", "그 부드러운 빛 속에서 먼 언덕들은 녹아드는 듯했고, 골짜기와 협곡의 그림자는 벨벳 같은 검은빛이었다."], ["The mere beauty seemed to cheer me; there was peace and comfort in every breath I drew.", "그저 그 아름다움만으로도 내 마음이 밝아지는 듯했다. 내가 들이쉬는 숨결마다 평화와 위안이 깃들어 있었다."], ["As I leaned from the window my eye was caught by something moving a storey below me, and somewhat to my left, where I imagined, from the order of the rooms, that the windows of the Count’s own room would look out.", "창에서 몸을 내미는데, 한 층 아래, 내 왼쪽으로 다소 치우친 곳에서 무언가 움직이는 것이 내 눈길을 끌었다. 방들의 배치로 미루어, 백작 자신의 방 창문이 내다보일 만한 곳이라고 짐작되는 자리였다."], ["The window at which I stood was tall and deep, stone-mullioned, and though weatherworn, was still complete; but it was evidently many a day since the case had been there.", "내가 선 창은 높고 깊었으며, 돌 중간설주가 있었고, 풍상에 닳기는 했어도 여전히 온전했다. 그러나 창틀이 그곳에 있었던 것은 분명 오래전의 일인 듯했다."], ["I drew back behind the stonework, and looked carefully out.", "나는 돌벽 뒤로 물러나 조심스레 밖을 내다보았다."]], [["What I saw was the Count’s head coming out from the window.", "내가 본 것은 창에서 나오는 백작의 머리였다."], ["I did not see the face, but I knew the man by the neck and the movement of his back and arms.", "얼굴은 보이지 않았으나, 나는 목과 등과 팔의 움직임으로 그가 누구인지 알 수 있었다."], ["In any case I could not mistake the hands which I had had so many opportunities of studying.", "어쨌거나 그토록 여러 번 살펴볼 기회가 있었던 그 손만은 잘못 볼 리 없었다."], ["I was at first interested and somewhat amused, for it is wonderful how small a matter will interest and amuse a man when he is a prisoner.", "처음에 나는 흥미가 일었고, 다소 재미있기도 했다. 사람이 갇힌 몸이 되면 얼마나 사소한 일에도 흥미를 느끼고 재미있어하는지 참으로 놀라운 일이다."], ["But my very feelings changed to repulsion and terror when I saw the whole man slowly emerge from the window and begin to crawl down the castle wall over that dreadful abyss, face down with his cloak spreading out around him like great wings.", "그러나 그 사람 전체가 창에서 서서히 나와, 그 무시무시한 심연 위로 성벽을 타고 기어 내려가기 시작하는 것을 보았을 때 — 얼굴을 아래로 향한 채, 망토를 거대한 날개처럼 사방으로 펼치고 — 나의 그 감정은 혐오와 공포로 바뀌었다."], ["At first I could not believe my eyes.", "처음에 나는 내 눈을 믿을 수 없었다."], ["I thought it was some trick of the moonlight, some weird effect of shadow; but I kept looking, and it could be no delusion.", "달빛의 어떤 장난이려니, 그림자가 빚어낸 어떤 기괴한 효과려니 여겼다. 그러나 계속 지켜보았으나, 그것은 결코 착각일 수 없었다."], ["I saw the fingers and toes grasp the corners of the stones, worn clear of the mortar by the stress of years, and by thus using every projection and inequality move downwards with considerable speed, just as a lizard moves along a wall.", "나는 그의 손가락과 발가락이, 오랜 세월의 풍파에 모르타르가 떨어져나가 드러난 돌의 모서리들을 움켜쥐는 것을, 그리하여 온갖 돌출부와 굴곡을 이용하여 마치 도마뱀이 벽을 기어가듯 상당한 속도로 아래로 내려가는 것을 보았다."]], [["What manner of man is this, or what manner of creature is it in the semblance of man?", "이것은 어떤 종류의 인간인가, 아니면 인간의 형상을 한 어떤 종류의 생물인가?"], ["I feel the dread of this horrible place overpowering me; I am in fear--in awful fear--and there is no escape for me; I am encompassed about with terrors that I dare not think of....", "나는 이 끔찍한 곳에 대한 두려움이 나를 압도하는 것을 느낀다. 나는 두렵다 — 끔찍하게 두렵다 — 그리고 나에게는 빠져나갈 길이 없다. 나는 감히 생각조차 할 수 없는 공포들에 둘러싸여 있다……."]], [["15 May.--Once more have I seen the Count go out in his lizard fashion.", "5월 15일. — 나는 또다시 백작이 그 도마뱀 같은 방식으로 나가는 것을 보았다."], ["He moved downwards in a sidelong way, some hundred feet down, and a good deal to the left.", "그는 비스듬히 아래로, 백 피트쯤 내려갔고, 상당히 왼쪽으로 치우쳐갔다."], ["He vanished into some hole or window.", "그는 어떤 구멍인지 창인지로 사라졌다."], ["When his head had disappeared, I leaned out to try and see more, but without avail--the distance was too great to allow a proper angle of sight.", "그의 머리가 사라진 뒤, 나는 더 보려고 몸을 내밀었으나 소용이 없었다 — 거리가 너무 멀어 제대로 된 시야 각도를 얻을 수 없었던 것이다."], ["I knew he had left the castle now, and thought to use the opportunity to explore more than I had dared to do as yet.", "이제 그가 성을 떠났음을 알았으므로, 나는 지금까지 감히 엄두 내지 못했던 것보다 더 많이 탐색해볼 기회로 삼고자 했다."], ["I went back to the room, and taking a lamp, tried all the doors.", "나는 방으로 돌아가 등불을 들고 모든 문을 열어보았다."], ["They were all locked, as I had expected, and the locks were comparatively new; but I went down the stone stairs to the hall where I had entered originally.", "예상대로 모두 잠겨 있었고, 자물쇠는 비교적 새것이었다. 그러나 나는 돌계단을 내려가 처음에 들어왔던 현관으로 갔다."], ["I found I could pull back the bolts easily enough and unhook the great chains; but the door was locked, and the key was gone!", "빗장은 어렵지 않게 뒤로 밀어낼 수 있었고, 큰 사슬도 풀 수 있었다. 그러나 문은 잠겨 있었고, 열쇠는 사라지고 없었다!"], ["That key must be in the Count’s room; I must watch should his door be unlocked, so that I may get it and escape.", "그 열쇠는 분명 백작의 방에 있을 것이다. 나는 그의 방문이 열려 있는지 지켜보아, 열쇠를 손에 넣어 탈출해야 한다."], ["I went on to make a thorough examination of the various stairs and passages, and to try the doors that opened from them.", "나는 계속해서 여러 계단과 통로를 샅샅이 살피고, 거기서 열리는 문들을 열어보았다."], ["One or two small rooms near the hall were open, but there was nothing to see in them except old furniture, dusty with age and moth-eaten.", "현관 가까이의 작은 방 한둘은 열려 있었으나, 그 안에는 세월에 먼지가 앉고 좀이 슨 낡은 가구 말고는 볼 것이 없었다."], ["At last, however, I found one door at the top of the stairway which, though it seemed to be locked, gave a little under pressure.", "그러나 마침내 나는 계단 꼭대기에서 문 하나를 발견했는데, 잠긴 듯 보였으나 밀자 약간 움직였다."], ["I tried it harder, and found that it was not really locked, but that the resistance came from the fact that the hinges had fallen somewhat, and the heavy door rested on the floor.", "더 세게 밀어보니, 실은 잠긴 것이 아니라, 경첩이 다소 내려앉아 묵직한 문이 바닥에 닿아 있어서 그렇게 버티는 것임을 알았다."], ["Here was an opportunity which I might not have again, so I exerted myself, and with many efforts forced it back so that I could enter.", "다시는 없을지 모를 기회였으므로, 나는 힘을 다해 여러 번 애쓴 끝에 문을 밀어젖혀 들어갈 수 있었다."], ["I was now in a wing of the castle further to the right than the rooms I knew and a storey lower down.", "이제 나는 내가 아는 방들보다 더 오른쪽으로, 또 한 층 아래로 치우친 성의 한 부속 건물 안에 있었다."], ["From the windows I could see that the suite of rooms lay along to the south of the castle, the windows of the end room looking out both west and south.", "창문들로 보아 그 방들은 성의 남쪽을 따라 죽 이어져 있었고, 끝 방의 창문은 서쪽과 남쪽 양편으로 내다보였다."], ["On the latter side, as well as to the former, there was a great precipice.", "후자 쪽으로도, 전자 쪽으로도 커다란 절벽이 있었다."], ["The castle was built on the corner of a great rock, so that on three sides it was quite impregnable, and great windows were placed here where sling, or bow, or culverin could not reach, and consequently light and comfort, impossible to a position which had to be guarded, were secured.", "성은 거대한 바위의 모퉁이 위에 세워져 있어, 세 방면으로는 도무지 난공불락이었고, 투석기나 활이나 컬버린포가 닿을 수 없는 이곳에 큰 창문들이 자리잡고 있었다. 그리하여 수비해야 하는 자리에서는 불가능한 빛과 안락함이 확보되어 있었다."], ["To the west was a great valley, and then, rising far away, great jagged mountain fastnesses, rising peak on peak, the sheer rock studded with mountain ash and thorn, whose roots clung in cracks and crevices and crannies of the stone.", "서쪽으로는 큰 골짜기가 있었고, 그 너머로 멀리, 봉우리에 봉우리를 잇대어 솟아오른 들쭉날쭉한 산악 요새들이 솟아 있었다. 그 깎아지른 바위에는 마가목과 가시나무가 점점이 박혀 있었는데, 그 뿌리는 돌의 갈라진 틈과 균열과 구멍에 매달려 있었다."], ["This was evidently the portion of the castle occupied by the ladies in bygone days, for the furniture had more air of comfort than any I had seen.", "이곳은 분명 지난날 귀부인들이 거처하던 성의 한 부분이었다. 가구가 내가 본 어떤 것보다도 더 안락한 분위기를 풍기고 있었기 때문이다."], ["The windows were curtainless, and the yellow moonlight, flooding in through the diamond panes, enabled one to see even colours, whilst it softened the wealth of dust which lay over all and disguised in some measure the ravages of time and the moth.", "창문에는 커튼이 없었고, 마름모꼴 유리창으로 쏟아져 들어오는 노란 달빛 덕분에 빛깔까지도 분간할 수 있었으며, 그 빛은 모든 것 위에 두텁게 쌓인 먼지를 부드럽게 가려주고, 세월과 좀이 끼친 손상을 어느 정도 감추어주었다."], ["My lamp seemed to be of little effect in the brilliant moonlight, but I was glad to have it with me, for there was a dread loneliness in the place which chilled my heart and made my nerves tremble.", "내 등불은 그 찬란한 달빛 속에서 거의 효력이 없는 듯했으나, 그래도 그것을 가지고 있는 것이 다행스러웠다. 그곳에는 내 심장을 서늘하게 하고 신경을 떨리게 하는 무시무시한 외로움이 깃들어 있었기 때문이다."], ["Still, it was better than living alone in the rooms which I had come to hate from the presence of the Count, and after trying a little to school my nerves, I found a soft quietude come over me.", "그래도 백작이 있어 미워하게 된 그 방들에서 혼자 지내는 것보다는 나았다. 그래서 나는 신경을 다잡으려 잠시 애쓴 끝에, 부드러운 고요가 나를 감싸오는 것을 느꼈다. 나는 지금 여기, 작은 떡갈나무 탁자 앞에 앉아 있다."], ["Here I am, sitting at a little oak table where in old times possibly some fair lady sat to pen, with much thought and many blushes, her ill-spelt love-letter, and writing in my diary in shorthand all that has happened since I closed it last.", "옛날에는 어쩌면 어느 아리따운 귀부인이 이 탁자 앞에 앉아, 깊은 생각과 숱한 얼굴 붉힘 속에 철자가 서툰 연애편지를 써내려갔을지도 모른다. 그리고 나는 일기를 마지막으로 덮은 이후 일어난 모든 일을 속기로 내 일기에 적고 있다."], ["It is nineteenth century up-to-date with a vengeance.", "이야말로 더없이 철저한 19세기의 최신 방식이다."], ["And yet, unless my senses deceive me, the old centuries had, and have, powers of their own which mere “modernity” cannot kill.", "그럼에도, 내 감각이 나를 속이는 것이 아니라면, 그 옛 세기들에는 한낱 '현대성'으로는 결코 죽일 수 없는 그들 나름의 힘이 있었고, 또 지금도 있다."]], [["Later: the Morning of 16 May.--God preserve my sanity, for to this I am reduced.", "그 뒤, 5월 16일 아침. — 신이여, 부디 내 정신을 지켜주소서. 나는 여기까지 내몰렸나이다."], ["Safety and the assurance of safety are things of the past.", "안전과 안전에 대한 확신은 이미 지나간 일이 되었다."], ["Whilst I live on here there is but one thing to hope for, that I may not go mad, if, indeed, I be not mad already.", "내가 이곳에서 살아가는 한 바랄 수 있는 것은 오직 하나, 만일 이미 미치지 않았다면, 내가 미치지 않는 것뿐이다."], ["If I be sane, then surely it is maddening to think that of all the foul things that lurk in this hateful place the Count is the least dreadful to me; that to him alone I can look for safety, even though this be only whilst I can serve his purpose.", "만일 내가 제정신이라면, 이 가증스러운 곳에 도사린 온갖 더러운 것들 가운데 백작이 나에게는 가장 덜 끔찍한 존재라는 것을, 비록 내가 그의 목적에 쓸모가 있는 동안에 한할지언정 오직 그에게서만 안전을 구할 수 있다는 것을 생각하면, 이는 분명 사람을 미치게 하는 일이다."], ["Great God!", "위대한 신이여!"], ["merciful God!", "자비로운 신이여!"], ["Let me be calm, for out of that way lies madness indeed.", "부디 내가 침착하게 하소서. 그 길의 끝에는 정말로 광기가 놓여 있으니."], ["I begin to get new lights on certain things which have puzzled me.", "나는 그동안 나를 어리둥절하게 했던 어떤 일들에 관해 새로운 깨달음을 얻기 시작한다."], ["Up to now I never quite knew what Shakespeare meant when he made Hamlet say:--", "지금까지 나는 셰익스피어가 햄릿에게 이런 말을 시켰을 때 그것이 무슨 뜻인지 결코 제대로 알지 못했다."]], [["“My tablets!", "\"내 수첩을!"], ["quick, my tablets!", "어서, 내 수첩을!"], ["’Tis meet that I put it down,” etc.,", "이것을 적어두는 것이 마땅하다.\" 운운."]], [["for now, feeling as though my own brain were unhinged or as if the shock had come which must end in its undoing, I turn to my diary for repose.", "이제 마치 내 자신의 머리가 빗장이 풀린 듯, 혹은 마침내 그것을 무너뜨리고야 말 그 충격이 닥쳐온 듯한 느낌이 들기에, 나는 안식을 찾아 내 일기로 눈을 돌린다."], ["The habit of entering accurately must help to soothe me.", "정확히 기록하는 습관이 나를 진정시키는 데에 도움이 될 것임에 틀림없다."]], [["The Count’s mysterious warning frightened me at the time; it frightens me more now when I think of it, for in future he has a fearful hold upon me.", "백작의 알 수 없는 경고는 그때에도 나를 두렵게 했으나, 지금 그것을 떠올리니 더욱 두렵다. 이제부터 그가 나에 대해 무서운 지배력을 쥐게 되었기 때문이다."], ["I shall fear to doubt what he may say!", "나는 그가 하는 말을 의심하기조차 두려워하게 될 것이다!"]], [["When I had written in my diary and had fortunately replaced the book and pen in my pocket I felt sleepy.", "일기를 다 쓰고, 다행히 책과 펜을 도로 주머니에 넣고 나자 졸음이 몰려왔다."], ["The Count’s warning came into my mind, but I took a pleasure in disobeying it.", "백작의 경고가 떠올랐으나, 나는 그것을 거역하는 데에 일종의 즐거움을 느꼈다."], ["The sense of sleep was upon me, and with it the obstinacy which sleep brings as outrider.", "졸음이 나를 덮쳐왔고, 그와 함께 잠이 길잡이처럼 데려오는 고집이 일었다."], ["The soft moonlight soothed, and the wide expanse without gave a sense of freedom which refreshed me.", "부드러운 달빛은 마음을 달래주었고, 바깥의 광활한 펼쳐짐은 나를 새롭게 하는 자유로움의 느낌을 주었다."], ["I determined not to return to-night to the gloom-haunted rooms, but to sleep here, where, of old, ladies had sat and sung and lived sweet lives whilst their gentle breasts were sad for their menfolk away in the midst of remorseless wars.", "나는 오늘 밤 그 음울함이 도사린 방들로 돌아가지 않고, 옛날에 귀부인들이 앉아 노래하며 — 가혹한 전쟁 한복판에 나가 있는 자기 남정네들 때문에 그 부드러운 가슴이 슬픔에 잠긴 채로도 — 아름다운 삶을 살았던 이곳에서 잠들기로 마음먹었다."], ["I drew a great couch out of its place near the corner, so that as I lay, I could look at the lovely view to east and south, and unthinking of and uncaring for the dust, composed myself for sleep.", "나는 모퉁이 가까이에 놓인 큰 긴 의자를 끌어내, 누웠을 때 동쪽과 남쪽의 아름다운 경치를 바라볼 수 있도록 했다. 그리고 먼지 따위는 생각하지도, 개의치도 않고 잠을 청했다. 나는 분명 잠이 들었던 모양이다."], ["I suppose I must have fallen asleep; I hope so, but I fear, for all that followed was startlingly real--so real that now sitting here in the broad, full sunlight of the morning, I cannot in the least believe that it was all sleep.", "그러기를 바라지만, 두렵기도 하다. 뒤이어 일어난 모든 일이 소스라치게 생생했기 때문이다 — 어찌나 생생한지, 지금 환한 아침 햇살 속에 이렇게 앉아 있으면서도, 나는 그것이 모두 꿈이었다고는 도무지 믿을 수가 없다."]], [["I was not alone.", "나는 혼자가 아니었다."], ["The room was the same, unchanged in any way since I came into it; I could see along the floor, in the brilliant moonlight, my own footsteps marked where I had disturbed the long accumulation of dust.", "방은 내가 들어왔을 때와 똑같았고, 어떤 식으로도 달라지지 않았다. 찬란한 달빛 속에서, 나는 바닥을 따라 내가 오래 쌓인 먼지를 흩뜨려놓은 곳에 찍힌 내 발자국을 볼 수 있었다."], ["In the moonlight opposite me were three young women, ladies by their dress and manner.", "내 맞은편 달빛 속에 세 젊은 여인이 있었는데, 옷차림과 거동으로 보아 귀부인들이었다."], ["I thought at the time that I must be dreaming when I saw them, for, though the moonlight was behind them, they threw no shadow on the floor.", "그들을 보았을 때 나는 분명 꿈을 꾸고 있는 것이려니 여겼다. 달빛이 그들 뒤에 있었는데도, 그들은 바닥에 그림자를 드리우지 않았기 때문이다."], ["They came close to me, and looked at me for some time, and then whispered together.", "그들은 내게 가까이 다가와, 한참 동안 나를 바라보더니, 자기들끼리 소곤거렸다."], ["Two were dark, and had high aquiline noses, like the Count, and great dark, piercing eyes that seemed to be almost red when contrasted with the pale yellow moon.", "둘은 검은 머리에, 백작처럼 높다란 매부리코를 가졌고, 창백한 노란 달빛과 대비되어 거의 붉어 보이는 크고 검은, 꿰뚫는 듯한 눈을 가졌다."], ["The other was fair, as fair as can be, with great wavy masses of golden hair and eyes like pale sapphires.", "나머지 하나는 금발이었는데, 더없이 아름다운 금발로, 물결치는 풍성한 황금빛 머리채와 옅은 사파이어 같은 눈을 가졌다."], ["I seemed somehow to know her face, and to know it in connection with some dreamy fear, but I could not recollect at the moment how or where.", "나는 어쩐지 그녀의 얼굴을 아는 듯했고, 어떤 꿈결 같은 두려움과 결부지어 아는 듯했으나, 그 순간에는 어떻게, 또 어디서였는지 도무지 떠올릴 수 없었다."], ["All three had brilliant white teeth that shone like pearls against the ruby of their voluptuous lips.", "셋 모두 관능적인 루비빛 입술과 대비되어 진주처럼 빛나는 눈부신 흰 이를 가졌다."], ["There was something about them that made me uneasy, some longing and at the same time some deadly fear.", "그들에게는 나를 불안하게 하는 무언가가, 어떤 갈망과 동시에 어떤 치명적인 두려움이 있었다."], ["I felt in my heart a wicked, burning desire that they would kiss me with those red lips.", "나는 마음속으로, 그들이 그 붉은 입술로 나에게 입맞춰주었으면 하는 사악하고 불타는 욕망을 느꼈다."], ["It is not good to note this down, lest some day it should meet Mina’s eyes and cause her pain; but it is the truth.", "이것을 적어두는 것은 좋지 못한 일이다. 언젠가 미나의 눈에 띄어 그녀를 아프게 할지 모르니. 그러나 이것은 진실이다."], ["They whispered together, and then they all three laughed--such a silvery, musical laugh, but as hard as though the sound never could have come through the softness of human lips.", "그들은 자기들끼리 소곤거리더니, 셋 다 함께 웃었다 — 그토록 은빛으로 낭랑한 웃음, 그러나 그 소리는 결코 인간의 입술의 부드러움을 거쳐 나올 수 없었으리라 싶을 만큼 모질었다."], ["It was like the intolerable, tingling sweetness of water-glasses when played on by a cunning hand.", "그것은 능란한 손이 유리잔들을 켤 때 울리는, 참을 수 없이 짜릿하게 감미로운 소리 같았다."], ["The fair girl shook her head coquettishly, and the other two urged her on.", "금발 처녀가 요염하게 고개를 저었고, 다른 두 여인이 그녀를 부추겼다."], ["One said:--", "하나가 말했다."]], [["“Go on!", "\"어서!"], ["You are first, and we shall follow; yours is the right to begin.”", "네가 먼저야, 우리는 뒤따를게. 시작할 권리는 너에게 있어.\""], ["The other added:--", "다른 하나가 덧붙였다."]], [["“He is young and strong; there are kisses for us all.”", "\"이 남자는 젊고 튼튼해. 우리 모두에게 돌아갈 입맞춤이 있어.\""], ["I lay quiet, looking out under my eyelashes in an agony of delightful anticipation.", "나는 가만히 누워, 황홀한 기대의 고통 속에서 속눈썹 아래로 내다보았다."], ["The fair girl advanced and bent over me till I could feel the movement of her breath upon me.", "금발 처녀가 다가와 내 위로 몸을 굽혔고, 마침내 나는 그녀의 숨결이 일렁이는 것을 느낄 수 있었다."], ["Sweet it was in one sense, honey-sweet, and sent the same tingling through the nerves as her voice, but with a bitter underlying the sweet, a bitter offensiveness, as one smells in blood.", "그것은 어떤 의미로는 달콤했다, 꿀처럼 달콤했고, 그녀의 목소리와 똑같은 짜릿함을 신경을 타고 보내왔다. 그러나 그 달콤함 밑에는 쓴맛이, 피에서 맡아지는 것 같은 쓰디쓴 역겨움이 깔려 있었다."]], [["I was afraid to raise my eyelids, but looked out and saw perfectly under the lashes.", "나는 눈꺼풀을 들어올리기가 두려웠으나, 속눈썹 아래로 내다보니 완벽하게 보였다."], ["The girl went on her knees, and bent over me, simply gloating.", "처녀는 무릎을 꿇고 내 위로 몸을 굽혀, 그저 음탕하게 나를 탐했다."], ["There was a deliberate voluptuousness which was both thrilling and repulsive, and as she arched her neck she actually licked her lips like an animal, till I could see in the moonlight the moisture shining on the scarlet lips and on the red tongue as it lapped the white sharp teeth.", "거기에는 짜릿하면서도 혐오스러운 어떤 계산된 관능이 있었으니, 그녀가 목을 활처럼 젖히며 정말로 짐승처럼 입술을 핥는 통에, 나는 달빛 속에서 그 진홍빛 입술 위에, 또 희고 날카로운 이를 핥는 붉은 혀 위에 침이 번득이는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Lower and lower went her head as the lips went below the range of my mouth and chin and seemed about to fasten on my throat.", "그녀의 머리는 점점 더 낮게 내려와, 입술이 내 입과 턱의 높이 아래로 내려갔고, 이윽고 내 목에 들러붙을 듯했다."], ["Then she paused, and I could hear the churning sound of her tongue as it licked her teeth and lips, and could feel the hot breath on my neck.", "그러다 그녀는 멈추었고, 나는 그녀의 혀가 이와 입술을 핥는 휘젓는 듯한 소리를 들을 수 있었으며, 내 목에 닿는 뜨거운 숨결을 느낄 수 있었다."], ["Then the skin of my throat began to tingle as one’s flesh does when the hand that is to tickle it approaches nearer--nearer.", "이윽고 내 목의 살갗이 따끔거리기 시작했다, 마치 간지럽힐 손이 점점 더, 더 가까이 다가올 때 살갗이 그러하듯이."], ["I could feel the soft, shivering touch of the lips on the super-sensitive skin of my throat, and the hard dents of two sharp teeth, just touching and pausing there.", "나는 더없이 예민해진 내 목의 살갗에 닿는 입술의 부드럽게 떨리는 감촉을, 그리고 막 닿아 그곳에서 멈춘 두 날카로운 이의 단단한 자국을 느낄 수 있었다."], ["I closed my eyes in a languorous ecstasy and waited--waited with beating heart.", "나는 나른한 황홀경 속에 눈을 감고 기다렸다 — 두근거리는 가슴으로 기다렸다."]], [["But at that instant, another sensation swept through me as quick as lightning.", "그러나 그 순간, 또 다른 감각이 번개처럼 빠르게 나를 휩쓸고 지나갔다."], ["I was conscious of the presence of the Count, and of his being as if lapped in a storm of fury.", "나는 백작의 존재를, 그리고 그가 마치 분노의 폭풍에 휩싸인 듯한 것을 알아차렸다."], ["As my eyes opened involuntarily I saw his strong hand grasp the slender neck of the fair woman and with giant’s power draw it back, the blue eyes transformed with fury, the white teeth champing with rage, and the fair cheeks blazing red with passion.", "나도 모르게 눈이 뜨이자, 나는 그의 억센 손이 금발 여인의 가녀린 목을 움켜쥐고 거인 같은 힘으로 잡아채는 것을 보았다. 푸른 눈은 분노로 일그러졌고, 흰 이는 노여움으로 으드득거렸으며, 어여쁜 두 뺨은 격정으로 시뻘겋게 타올랐다."], ["But the Count!", "그러나 백작이라니!"], ["Never did I imagine such wrath and fury, even to the demons of the pit.", "나는 지옥 구덩이의 악마들에게서조차 그런 진노와 광포함을 상상해본 적이 없다."], ["His eyes were positively blazing.", "그의 두 눈은 정말로 이글거리고 있었다."], ["The red light in them was lurid, as if the flames of hell-fire blazed behind them.", "그 안의 붉은 빛은 마치 지옥불의 화염이 그 뒤에서 타오르기라도 하는 듯 음산했다."], ["His face was deathly pale, and the lines of it were hard like drawn wires; the thick eyebrows that met over the nose now seemed like a heaving bar of white-hot metal.", "그의 얼굴은 죽은 사람처럼 창백했고, 그 윤곽은 팽팽히 당긴 철사처럼 굳어 있었다. 콧등 위에서 맞붙은 그 짙은 눈썹은 이제 마치 백열로 달구어진 쇠막대가 일렁이는 것 같았다."], ["With a fierce sweep of his arm, he hurled the woman from him, and then motioned to the others, as though he were beating them back; it was the same imperious gesture that I had seen used to the wolves.", "그는 사납게 팔을 휘둘러 그 여인을 자기에게서 내던지고, 그러고는 다른 여인들을 향해 마치 그들을 쳐서 물리치기라도 하듯 손짓했다. 그것은 늑대들에게 쓰는 것을 내가 보았던, 바로 그 위압적인 몸짓이었다."], ["In a voice which, though low and almost in a whisper seemed to cut through the air and then ring round the room he said:--", "낮고 거의 속삭임에 가까웠으나 공기를 가르고 이윽고 방 안을 빙 둘러 울리는 듯한 목소리로 그가 말했다."]], [["“How dare you touch him, any of you?", "\"어찌 감히 너희 가운데 누구든 이자에게 손을 대느냐?"], ["How dare you cast eyes on him when I had forbidden it? Back, I tell you all!", "내가 금했거늘 어찌 감히 이자에게 눈을 돌리느냐? 물러서라, 너희 모두에게 이르노라!"], ["This man belongs to me!", "이 사람은 나의 것이다!"], ["Beware how you meddle with him, or you’ll have to deal with me.”", "이자에게 함부로 손댈 생각일랑 마라. 그랬다간 나를 상대해야 할 테니.\""], ["The fair girl, with a laugh of ribald coquetry, turned to answer him:--", "금발 처녀가 음탕하고 요염한 웃음을 띠며 그에게 대꾸하려 돌아섰다."]], [["“You yourself never loved; you never love!”", "\"당신이야말로 결코 사랑한 적이 없잖아요."], ["On this the other women joined, and such a mirthless, hard, soulless laughter rang through the room that it almost made me faint to hear; it seemed like the pleasure of fiends.", "당신은 사랑이란 걸 모르잖아요!\" 이 말에 다른 여인들도 가세하여, 어찌나 흥 없고 모질고 혼이 없는 웃음이 방 안에 울려퍼지던지, 듣고 있는 나는 거의 까무러칠 지경이었다. 그것은 마치 악귀들의 환락 같았다."], ["Then the Count turned, after looking at my face attentively, and said in a soft whisper:--", "그러자 백작이 내 얼굴을 유심히 들여다본 뒤 돌아서서, 부드러운 속삭임으로 말했다."]], [["“Yes, I too can love; you yourselves can tell it from the past.", "\"그래, 나도 사랑할 수 있다. 너희도 지난날을 보면 알 수 있을 것이다."], ["Is it not so?", "그렇지 않으냐?"], ["Well, now I promise you that when I am done with him you shall kiss him at your will. Now go! go!", "자, 이제 너희에게 약속하마. 내가 이자를 다 쓰고 나거든, 너희 마음대로 그에게 입맞춰도 좋다."], ["I must awaken him, for there is work to be done.”", "자 이제 가라! 가라! 나는 그를 깨워야 한다. 해야 할 일이 있으니.\""]], [["“Are we to have nothing to-night?”", "\"오늘 밤은 우리에게 아무것도 없나요?\""], ["said one of them, with a low laugh, as she pointed to the bag which he had thrown upon the floor, and which moved as though there were some living thing within it.", "그들 가운데 하나가 낮게 웃으며, 그가 바닥에 내던진 자루를 가리키며 말했다. 그 자루는 마치 안에 무언가 살아 있는 것이 들어 있기라도 한 듯 꿈틀거렸다."], ["For answer he nodded his head.", "대답 대신 그는 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["One of the women jumped forward and opened it.", "여인들 가운데 하나가 앞으로 뛰어나가 그것을 열었다."], ["If my ears did not deceive me there was a gasp and a low wail, as of a half-smothered child.", "내 귀가 나를 속인 것이 아니라면, 거기서 반쯤 숨이 막힌 아이의 것 같은 헐떡임과 낮은 흐느낌이 새어나왔다."], ["The women closed round, whilst I was aghast with horror; but as I looked they disappeared, and with them the dreadful bag.", "여인들이 빙 둘러쌌고, 나는 공포에 질려 기겁했다. 그러나 내가 바라보는 사이 그들은 사라졌고, 그 무시무시한 자루도 그들과 함께 사라졌다."], ["There was no door near them, and they could not have passed me without my noticing.", "그들 가까이에는 문이 없었고, 그들이 나를 지나쳤다면 내가 알아차리지 못했을 리 없다."], ["They simply seemed to fade into the rays of the moonlight and pass out through the window, for I could see outside the dim, shadowy forms for a moment before they entirely faded away.", "그들은 그저 달빛 속으로 녹아들어 창문을 통해 빠져나가는 듯했으니, 바깥에서 그 흐릿하고 어슴푸레한 형체들이 완전히 사라지기 전에 잠시 보였기 때문이다."]], [["Then the horror overcame me, and I sank down unconscious.", "그러자 공포가 나를 압도했고, 나는 의식을 잃고 쓰러졌다."]], [["I awoke in my own bed.", "나는 내 침대에서 깨어났다."], ["If it be that I had not dreamt, the Count must have carried me here.", "만일 내가 꿈을 꾼 것이 아니라면, 백작이 나를 이리로 옮겨놓았음이 틀림없다."], ["I tried to satisfy myself on the subject, but could not arrive at any unquestionable result.", "나는 이 일을 두고 마음을 가라앉혀 보려 했으나, 의심의 여지 없는 결론에는 이를 수 없었다."], ["To be sure, there were certain small evidences, such as that my clothes were folded and laid by in a manner which was not my habit.", "물론 사소한 증거들이 몇 가지 있기는 했다. 이를테면 내 옷이 내 버릇과는 다른 방식으로 개켜져 한쪽에 놓여 있었다는 것이다."], ["My watch was still unwound, and I am rigorously accustomed to wind it the last thing before going to bed, and many such details.", "내 시계는 여전히 태엽이 감겨 있지 않았는데, 나는 잠자리에 들기 전 마지막으로 반드시 시계태엽을 감는 습관이 철저했으며, 그 밖에도 이런 자질구레한 일들이 많았다."], ["But these things are no proof, for they may have been evidences that my mind was not as usual, and, from some cause or another, I had certainly been much upset.", "그러나 이런 것들은 증거가 되지 못한다. 내 정신이 평소 같지 않았다는, 그리고 어떤 까닭에선지 내가 분명 몹시 동요했다는 증거일 뿐일지도 모르기 때문이다."], ["I must watch for proof.", "나는 증거를 찾아내야 한다."], ["Of one thing I am glad: if it was that the Count carried me here and undressed me, he must have been hurried in his task, for my pockets are intact.", "한 가지 다행스러운 것이 있다. 만일 백작이 나를 이리로 옮겨와 옷을 벗긴 것이라면, 그는 그 일을 서둘러 했음이 틀림없다. 내 주머니가 그대로였으니 말이다."], ["I am sure this diary would have been a mystery to him which he would not have brooked.", "이 일기가 그에게는 풀 수 없는 수수께끼였을 것이며, 그는 그것을 그냥 두지 않았으리라 나는 확신한다."], ["He would have taken or destroyed it.", "가져갔거나 없애버렸을 것이다."], ["As I look round this room, although it has been to me so full of fear, it is now a sort of sanctuary, for nothing can be more dreadful than those awful women, who were--who are--waiting to suck my blood.", "이 방을 둘러보니, 비록 이곳이 나에게 그토록 공포로 가득 찬 곳이었으나 지금은 일종의 성소(聖所)처럼 느껴진다. 내 피를 빨려고 기다리고 있던 — 아니, 기다리고 있는 그 끔찍한 여인들보다 더 무서운 것은 없으니 말이다."]], [["18 May.--I have been down to look at that room again in daylight, for I must know the truth.", "5월 18일. — 나는 그 방을 대낮에 다시 보려고 내려가 보았다. 진실을 반드시 알아야만 했기 때문이다."], ["When I got to the doorway at the top of the stairs I found it closed.", "계단 꼭대기의 문간에 이르니 문이 닫혀 있었다."], ["It had been so forcibly driven against the jamb that part of the woodwork was splintered.", "어찌나 거센 힘으로 문설주에 밀어붙여졌던지 목재 일부가 쪼개져 있었다."], ["I could see that the bolt of the lock had not been shot, but the door is fastened from the inside.", "자물쇠의 빗장이 걸려 있지 않은 것은 알 수 있었으나, 문은 안쪽에서 잠겨 있었다."], ["I fear it was no dream, and must act on this surmise.", "그것이 꿈이 아니었을까 두렵고, 이 추측에 따라 행동해야만 한다."]], [["19 May.--I am surely in the toils.", "5월 19일. — 나는 분명 덫에 걸려 있다."], ["Last night the Count asked me in the suavest tones to write three letters, one saying that my work here was nearly done, and that I should start for home within a few days, another that I was starting on the next morning from the time of the letter, and the third that I had left the castle and arrived at Bistritz.", "어젯밤 백작은 더없이 부드러운 어조로 나에게 편지 세 통을 쓰라고 했다. 하나는 이곳에서의 일이 거의 끝났으니 며칠 안에 집으로 떠나겠다는 것, 또 하나는 편지를 쓴 날로부터 다음 날 아침에 출발하겠다는 것, 셋째는 성을 떠나 비스트리차에 도착했다는 것이었다."], ["I would fain have rebelled, but felt that in the present state of things it would be madness to quarrel openly with the Count whilst I am so absolutely in his power; and to refuse would be to excite his suspicion and to arouse his anger.", "나는 기꺼이 거역하고 싶었으나, 내가 이토록 철저히 그의 손아귀에 들어 있는 지금의 상황에서 백작과 대놓고 다투는 것은 미친 짓이라 느꼈다. 거절한다는 것은 그의 의심을 사고 그의 분노를 일으키는 일이 될 터였다."], ["He knows that I know too much, and that I must not live, lest I be dangerous to him; my only chance is to prolong my opportunities.", "그는 내가 너무 많이 안다는 것, 그리고 내가 그에게 위험한 존재가 될까 하여 살려두어서는 안 된다는 것을 알고 있다."], ["Something may occur which will give me a chance to escape.", "내게 남은 유일한 기회는 기회의 시간을 늘리는 것뿐이다."], ["I saw in his eyes something of that gathering wrath which was manifest when he hurled that fair woman from him.", "무언가 일이 벌어져 탈출할 기회를 줄지도 모른다. 그가 그 아름다운 여인을 내던질 때 드러났던 그 부풀어오르는 분노 같은 것을 나는 그의 눈에서 보았다."], ["He explained to me that posts were few and uncertain, and that my writing now would ensure ease of mind to my friends; and he assured me with so much impressiveness that he would countermand the later letters, which would be held over at Bistritz until due time in case chance would admit of my prolonging my stay, that to oppose him would have been to create new suspicion.", "그는 우편이 드물고 또 믿을 수 없으며, 내가 지금 글을 써두면 내 벗들의 마음을 편하게 해줄 것이라고 설명했다. 그리고 혹시 내 체류를 연장할 형편이 된다면 나중의 편지들은 비스트리차에 적당한 때까지 보관해두었다가 취소시키겠노라고, 어찌나 무게 있는 어조로 장담하던지, 그에게 맞선다는 것은 새로운 의심을 불러일으키는 일이 되었을 것이다."], ["I therefore pretended to fall in with his views, and asked him what dates I should put on the letters.", "그래서 나는 그의 뜻을 따르는 척하며, 편지에 어떤 날짜를 적어야 하느냐고 물었다."], ["He calculated a minute, and then said:--", "그는 잠시 셈을 하더니 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“The first should be June 12, the second June 19, and the third June 29.”", "\"첫 번째 것은 6월 12일, 두 번째 것은 6월 19일, 세 번째 것은 6월 29일이오.\""]], [["I know now the span of my life. God help me!", "이제 나는 내 목숨의 길이를 안다. 하느님, 부디 저를 도우소서!"]], [["28 May.--There is a chance of escape, or at any rate of being able to send word home.", "5월 28일. — 탈출할 기회가, 아니 적어도 집으로 소식을 전할 수 있는 기회가 생겼다."], ["A band of Szgany have come to the castle, and are encamped in the courtyard. These Szgany are gipsies; I have notes of them in my book.", "한 무리의 즈가니가 성에 와서 안마당에 진을 쳤다. 이 즈가니는 집시들로, 나는 내 수첩에 그들에 관한 메모를 적어두었다."], ["They are peculiar to this part of the world, though allied to the ordinary gipsies all the world over.", "그들은 온 세상에 흔한 보통 집시들과 한 갈래이긴 하나, 이 지방 특유의 사람들이다."], ["There are thousands of them in Hungary and Transylvania, who are almost outside all law.", "헝가리와 트란실바니아에는 그들이 수천 명이나 되며, 거의 모든 법의 바깥에 있다."], ["They attach themselves as a rule to some great noble or boyar, and call themselves by his name.", "그들은 대개 어떤 대귀족이나 보야르(귀족)에게 몸을 의탁하고는 그의 이름을 따 자신들을 부른다."], ["They are fearless and without religion, save superstition, and they talk only their own varieties of the Romany tongue.", "그들은 두려움을 모르고, 미신을 빼면 종교도 없으며, 자기들 나름의 로마니어 사투리만을 쓴다."]], [["I shall write some letters home, and shall try to get them to have them posted.", "* 보야르: 동유럽의 귀족 계급. 나는 집으로 보낼 편지를 몇 통 써서, 그들에게 부쳐달라고 부탁해 볼 작정이다."], ["I have already spoken them through my window to begin acquaintanceship.", "나는 이미 친분을 트려고 창문 너머로 그들에게 말을 건넸다."], ["They took their hats off and made obeisance and many signs, which, however, I could not understand any more than I could their spoken language....", "그들은 모자를 벗고 절을 하며 여러 가지 손짓을 했으나, 그 손짓도 그들의 말만큼이나 도무지 알아들을 수 없었다……."]], [["I have written the letters.", "편지를 다 썼다."], ["Mina’s is in shorthand, and I simply ask Mr. Hawkins to communicate with her.", "미나에게 보내는 것은 속기로 적었고, 호킨스 씨에게는 그저 그녀에게 연락해달라고 부탁했다."], ["To her I have explained my situation, but without the horrors which I may only surmise.", "그녀에게는 내 처지를 설명했으나, 짐작만 할 뿐인 그 공포에 대해서는 적지 않았다."], ["It would shock and frighten her to death were I to expose my heart to her.", "만일 내 속마음을 그녀에게 드러낸다면 그녀는 충격과 두려움에 죽고 말 것이다."], ["Should the letters not carry, then the Count shall not yet know my secret or the extent of my knowledge....", "혹시 편지가 전해지지 않더라도, 백작은 아직 내 비밀이나 내가 아는 것의 정도를 알지는 못할 것이다……."]], [["I have given the letters; I threw them through the bars of my window with a gold piece, and made what signs I could to have them posted.", "편지를 건넸다. 금화 한 닢과 함께 창살 사이로 던져 넣고, 부쳐달라고 할 수 있는 한 손짓을 다 해 보였다."], ["The man who took them pressed them to his heart and bowed, and then put them in his cap. I could do no more.", "그것을 받아든 남자는 편지를 가슴에 꼭 누르며 고개를 숙이더니, 모자 속에 넣었다. 나로서는 더 할 수 있는 일이 없었다."], ["I stole back to the study, and began to read.", "나는 살그머니 서재로 돌아와 책을 읽기 시작했다."], ["As the Count did not come in, I have written here....", "백작이 들어오지 않기에, 여기 이렇게 적는다……."]], [["The Count has come.", "백작이 왔다."], ["He sat down beside me, and said in his smoothest voice as he opened two letters:--", "그는 내 곁에 앉더니, 편지 두 통을 펼치며 더없이 매끄러운 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“The Szgany has given me these, of which, though I know not whence they come, I shall, of course, take care.", "\"즈가니가 나에게 이것들을 주었소. 어디서 난 것인지는 모르겠으나, 물론 내가 잘 간수하겠소. 보시오!\""], ["See!”--he must have looked at it--“one is from you, and to my friend Peter Hawkins; the other”--here he caught sight of the strange symbols as he opened the envelope, and the dark look came into his face, and his eyes blazed wickedly--“the other is a vile thing, an outrage upon friendship and hospitality!", "— 그는 그것을 들여다보았던 모양이다 — \"하나는 당신이 쓴 것으로, 내 벗 피터 호킨스에게 보내는 것이군. 다른 하나는\" — 여기서 그는 봉투를 뜯다가 그 이상한 기호들을 보고는, 얼굴에 어두운 빛이 떠오르고 두 눈이 사악하게 번득였다 — \"다른 하나는 비열한 것이오."], ["It is not signed. Well!", "우정과 환대에 대한 모독이오!"], ["so it cannot matter to us.”", "서명도 되어 있지 않군. 흥!"], ["And he calmly held letter and envelope in the flame of the lamp till they were consumed.", "그러니 우리에게는 아무 상관 없겠소.\" 그러고는 편지와 봉투를 램프 불꽃에 차분히 갖다 대어 다 태워버렸다."], ["Then he went on:--", "그런 다음 말을 이었다."]], [["“The letter to Hawkins--that I shall, of course, send on, since it is yours.", "\"호킨스에게 보내는 편지는, 당신이 쓴 것이니 물론 내가 부쳐드리겠소."], ["Your letters are sacred to me.", "당신의 편지는 나에게 신성한 것이오."], ["Your pardon, my friend, that unknowingly I did break the seal.", "미안하오, 나의 벗, 나도 모르고 봉인을 뜯고 말았구려."], ["Will you not cover it again?”", "다시 봉해주지 않겠소?\""], ["He held out the letter to me, and with a courteous bow handed me a clean envelope.", "그는 나에게 편지를 내밀더니, 정중히 고개를 숙이며 깨끗한 봉투 하나를 건넸다."], ["I could only redirect it and hand it to him in silence.", "나는 잠자코 다시 수신인 주소를 적어 그에게 넘겨주는 수밖에 없었다."], ["When he went out of the room I could hear the key turn softly.", "그가 방을 나가자 열쇠가 살그머니 돌아가는 소리가 들렸다."], ["A minute later I went over and tried it, and the door was locked.", "잠시 후 가서 문을 흔들어 보았더니, 문은 잠겨 있었다."]], [["When, an hour or two after, the Count came quietly into the room, his coming awakened me, for I had gone to sleep on the sofa.", "한두 시간쯤 지난 뒤 백작이 소리 없이 방으로 들어왔는데, 그가 들어오는 바람에 나는 잠에서 깨었다. 소파에서 잠이 들었던 것이다."], ["He was very courteous and very cheery in his manner, and seeing that I had been sleeping, he said:--", "그는 매우 정중하고 무척 명랑한 태도였으며, 내가 자고 있던 것을 보더니 말했다."]], [["“So, my friend, you are tired? Get to bed.", "\"이런, 나의 벗, 피곤하오? 침대에 가서 누우시오."], ["There is the surest rest.", "거기가 가장 확실한 안식처요."], ["I may not have the pleasure to talk to-night, since there are many labours to me; but you will sleep, I pray.”", "오늘 밤은 할 일이 많아 당신과 이야기할 즐거움은 누리지 못하겠으나, 부디 잘 자기를 바라오.\""], ["I passed to my room and went to bed, and, strange to say, slept without dreaming. Despair has its own calms.", "나는 내 방으로 건너가 잠자리에 들었고, 이상하게도 꿈 하나 꾸지 않고 잤다. 절망에는 절망 나름의 평온이 있는 법이다."]], [["31 May.--This morning when I woke I thought I would provide myself with some paper and envelopes from my bag and keep them in my pocket, so that I might write in case I should get an opportunity, but again a surprise, again a shock!", "5월 31일. — 오늘 아침 잠에서 깨어, 가방에서 종이와 봉투를 좀 꺼내 주머니에 넣어두었다가 기회가 생기면 편지를 쓸 수 있도록 해야겠다고 생각했다. 그런데 또 한 번 놀라움이, 또 한 번 충격이 닥쳤다!"]], [["Every scrap of paper was gone, and with it all my notes, my memoranda, relating to railways and travel, my letter of credit, in fact all that might be useful to me were I once outside the castle.", "종잇조각이란 종잇조각은 죄다 사라지고 없었으며, 그와 함께 철도며 여행에 관한 내 메모와 비망록, 신용장까지, 요컨대 일단 성 밖으로 나가기만 하면 나에게 쓸모가 있을 만한 것이 모조리 없어졌다."], ["I sat and pondered awhile, and then some thought occurred to me, and I made search of my portmanteau and in the wardrobe where I had placed my clothes.", "나는 잠시 앉아 곰곰이 생각하다가, 문득 어떤 생각이 떠올라 내 여행 가방과, 옷을 넣어두었던 옷장을 뒤져보았다."]], [["The suit in which I had travelled was gone, and also my overcoat and rug; I could find no trace of them anywhere.", "내가 여행할 때 입었던 양복이 사라졌고, 외투와 무릎덮개도 없었다. 어디에서도 그 흔적을 찾을 수 없었다."], ["This looked like some new scheme of villainy....", "이것은 무언가 새로운 악행의 술책처럼 보였다……."]], [["17 June.--This morning, as I was sitting on the edge of my bed cudgelling my brains, I heard without a cracking of whips and pounding and scraping of horses’ feet up the rocky path beyond the courtyard.", "6월 17일. — 오늘 아침, 침대 가장자리에 앉아 머리를 쥐어짜고 있는데, 밖에서 채찍이 갈라지는 소리와 안마당 너머 바위투성이 길을 올라오는 말발굽이 쿵쿵 짓밟고 긁히는 소리가 들렸다."], ["With joy I hurried to the window, and saw drive into the yard two great leiter-wagons, each drawn by eight sturdy horses, and at the head of each pair a Slovak, with his wide hat, great nail-studded belt, dirty sheepskin, and high boots.", "나는 기쁨에 들떠 서둘러 창가로 갔고, 마당으로 들어오는 커다란 짐마차 두 대를 보았다. 저마다 건장한 말 여덟 마리가 끌고 있었으며, 각 마차의 앞쪽 두 마리 곁에는 슬로바키아인이 한 명씩 있었는데, 챙 넓은 모자에, 못이 빼곡히 박힌 큼직한 허리띠, 더러운 양가죽 옷, 긴 장화 차림이었다."], ["They had also their long staves in hand.", "그들은 손에 긴 막대기도 들고 있었다."], ["I ran to the door, intending to descend and try and join them through the main hall, as I thought that way might be opened for them.", "나는 문으로 달려갔다. 그들을 위해 본관으로 통하는 길이 열려 있으리라 생각하고, 아래로 내려가 큰 홀을 거쳐 그들과 합류해 보려는 것이었다."], ["Again a shock: my door was fastened on the outside.", "또 한 번 충격이 닥쳤다. 내 방문이 바깥에서 잠겨 있었던 것이다."]], [["Then I ran to the window and cried to them.", "그래서 나는 창가로 달려가 그들에게 소리쳤다."], ["They looked up at me stupidly and pointed, but just then the “hetman” of the Szgany came out, and seeing them pointing to my window, said something, at which they laughed.", "그들은 멍하니 나를 올려다보며 손가락질을 했으나, 바로 그때 즈가니의 \"헤트만\"(우두머리)이 나오더니, 그들이 내 창문을 가리키는 것을 보고는 무언가 말을 했고, 그 말에 그들은 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["Henceforth no effort of mine, no piteous cry or agonised entreaty, would make them even look at me.", "그 뒤로는 내가 아무리 애를 써도, 아무리 애처롭게 외치고 애끓도록 사정해도 그들은 나를 쳐다보려고조차 하지 않았다."], ["They resolutely turned away.", "그들은 단호히 등을 돌렸다."], ["The leiter-wagons contained great, square boxes, with handles of thick rope; these were evidently empty by the ease with which the Slovaks handled them, and by their resonance as they were roughly moved.", "그 짐마차에는 굵은 밧줄 손잡이가 달린 크고 네모난 상자들이 실려 있었다. 슬로바키아인들이 그것들을 다루는 손놀림이 쉬운 것으로 보아, 또 거칠게 옮길 때 울리는 소리로 보아, 분명 빈 상자들이었다."], ["When they were all unloaded and packed in a great heap in one corner of the yard, the Slovaks were given some money by the Szgany, and spitting on it for luck, lazily went each to his horse’s head.", "그것들을 모두 부려 마당 한구석에 큰 무더기로 쌓아놓자, 슬로바키아인들은 즈가니에게서 돈을 좀 받더니, 행운을 빌며 거기에 침을 뱉고는 느릿느릿 저마다 제 말의 머리께로 갔다."], ["Shortly afterwards, I heard the cracking of their whips die away in the distance.", "곧이어 그들의 채찍 소리가 멀리서 잦아드는 것이 들렸다."]], [["24 June, before morning.--Last night the Count left me early, and locked himself into his own room.", "* 헤트만: 동유럽에서 군사·집단의 우두머리를 가리키는 칭호. 6월 24일, 동트기 전."], ["As soon as I dared I ran up the winding stair, and looked out of the window, which opened south.", "— 어젯밤 백작은 일찍 나를 떠나 자기 방에 틀어박혔다. 엄두가 나자마자 나는 나선 계단을 뛰어올라가, 남쪽으로 난 창문 밖을 내다보았다."], ["I thought I would watch for the Count, for there is something going on.", "무언가 일이 벌어지고 있으니, 백작을 지켜보아야겠다고 생각했던 것이다."], ["The Szgany are quartered somewhere in the castle and are doing work of some kind.", "즈가니는 성 어딘가에 숙소를 잡고 무슨 일인가를 하고 있다. 나는 그것을 안다."], ["I know it, for now and then I hear a far-away muffled sound as of mattock and spade, and, whatever it is, it must be the end of some ruthless villainy.", "이따금 곡괭이질과 삽질 같은 멀고 둔탁한 소리가 들려오기 때문이며, 그것이 무엇이든 어떤 무자비한 악행의 끝일 것이 틀림없다."]], [["I had been at the window somewhat less than half an hour, when I saw something coming out of the Count’s window.", "창가에 머문 지 채 반 시간도 못 되었을 때, 나는 무언가가 백작의 창문에서 나오는 것을 보았다."], ["I drew back and watched carefully, and saw the whole man emerge.", "나는 뒤로 물러나 조심스레 지켜보았고, 그 사람의 온몸이 빠져나오는 것을 보았다."], ["It was a new shock to me to find that he had on the suit of clothes which I had worn whilst travelling here, and slung over his shoulder the terrible bag which I had seen the women take away. There could be no doubt as to his quest, and in my garb, too!", "새삼 충격이었던 것은, 그가 내가 이곳으로 오는 길에 입었던 양복을 입고 있었으며, 어깨에는 그 여인들이 가져가던 그 끔찍한 자루를 메고 있었다는 점이다. 그의 행각이 무엇인지, 게다가 내 옷차림을 하고 있으니, 의심의 여지가 없었다!"], ["This, then, is his new scheme of evil: that he will allow others to see me, as they think, so that he may both leave evidence that I have been seen in the towns or villages posting my own letters, and that any wickedness which he may do shall by the local people be attributed to me.", "그러니 이것이 그의 새로운 악의 술책인 것이다. 그는 사람들로 하여금 나를, 그들 생각으로는 나를 보았다고 여기게 함으로써, 내가 그 도시나 마을에서 내 편지를 직접 부치는 모습을 사람들이 보았다는 증거를 남기는 한편, 그가 저지를지 모를 온갖 악행을 그곳 주민들이 나의 짓으로 돌리게 하려는 것이다."]], [["It makes me rage to think that this can go on, and whilst I am shut up here, a veritable prisoner, but without that protection of the law which is even a criminal’s right and consolation.", "이런 일이 계속될 수 있다는 것, 그것도 내가 이곳에 갇혀, 그야말로 죄수의 신세이면서, 죄인에게마저 주어지는 권리이자 위안인 그 법의 보호조차 받지 못한 채 갇혀 있는 동안 그러하다는 것을 생각하면 나는 분노가 치민다."]], [["I thought I would watch for the Count’s return, and for a long time sat doggedly at the window.", "나는 백작이 돌아오는 것을 지켜보아야겠다고 생각하고, 오랫동안 끈질기게 창가에 앉아 있었다."], ["Then I began to notice that there were some quaint little specks floating in the rays of the moonlight.", "그러다 달빛 줄기 속에 무언가 기이한 작은 알갱이들이 떠다니는 것이 눈에 띄기 시작했다."], ["They were like the tiniest grains of dust, and they whirled round and gathered in clusters in a nebulous sort of way.", "그것들은 더없이 작은 먼지 알갱이 같았는데, 빙글빙글 돌며 어슴푸레하게 무리를 지어 모여들었다."], ["I watched them with a sense of soothing, and a sort of calm stole over me.", "나는 마음이 누그러지는 느낌으로 그것들을 바라보았고, 일종의 평온이 슬며시 나를 감쌌다."], ["I leaned back in the embrasure in a more comfortable position, so that I could enjoy more fully the aërial gambolling.", "나는 그 공중의 춤을 더 마음껏 즐길 수 있도록, 창의 후미진 자리에 한결 편안한 자세로 몸을 기댔다."]], [["Something made me start up, a low, piteous howling of dogs somewhere far below in the valley, which was hidden from my sight.", "무언가가 나를 벌떡 일어나게 했다. 내 시야에서 가려진 저 아래 골짜기 어딘가에서 개들이 낮고 애처롭게 울부짖는 소리였다."], ["Louder it seemed to ring in my ears, and the floating motes of dust to take new shapes to the sound as they danced in the moonlight.", "그 소리가 점점 더 크게 내 귓속에 울리는 듯했고, 떠다니던 먼지 티끌들은 달빛 속에서 춤추며 그 소리에 맞추어 새로운 모양을 빚어내는 듯했다."], ["I felt myself struggling to awake to some call of my instincts; nay, my very soul was struggling, and my half-remembered sensibilities were striving to answer the call.", "나는 어떤 본능의 부름에 깨어나려고 발버둥치는 나 자신을 느꼈다. 아니, 내 영혼 자체가 발버둥치고 있었고, 반쯤만 기억나는 내 감각들이 그 부름에 응하려 안간힘을 쓰고 있었다."], ["I was becoming hypnotised!", "나는 최면에 빠져들고 있었던 것이다!"], ["Quicker and quicker danced the dust; the moonbeams seemed to quiver as they went by me into the mass of gloom beyond.", "먼지는 점점 더 빠르게 춤을 추었고, 달빛은 내 곁을 지나 저 너머 어둠의 덩어리 속으로 흘러들며 가물거리는 듯했다."], ["More and more they gathered till they seemed to take dim phantom shapes.", "그것들은 점점 더 모여들더니 마침내 어렴풋한 유령의 형상을 띠는 듯했다."], ["And then I started, broad awake and in full possession of my senses, and ran screaming from the place.", "그때 나는 흠칫 놀라, 완전히 깨어나 온전히 제정신으로 돌아와서는, 비명을 지르며 그곳에서 달아났다."], ["The phantom shapes, which were becoming gradually materialised from the moonbeams, were those of the three ghostly women to whom I was doomed.", "달빛에서 차츰 형체를 갖추어가던 그 유령의 형상들은, 내가 운명적으로 매여 있는 그 세 유령 같은 여인들의 것이었다."], ["I fled, and felt somewhat safer in my own room, where there was no moonlight and where the lamp was burning brightly.", "나는 달아났고, 달빛도 없고 램프가 환히 타고 있는 내 방으로 돌아오니 다소 안전한 느낌이 들었다."]], [["When a couple of hours had passed I heard something stirring in the Count’s room, something like a sharp wail quickly suppressed; and then there was silence, deep, awful silence, which chilled me.", "두어 시간쯤 지났을 때, 나는 백작의 방에서 무언가 움직이는 소리를 들었다. 날카로운 비명이 재빨리 눌려 잦아드는 것 같은 소리였다. 그러고는 정적이, 깊고 무서운 정적이 찾아와 나를 오싹하게 했다."], ["With a beating heart, I tried the door; but I was locked in my prison, and could do nothing.", "나는 두근거리는 가슴으로 문을 흔들어 보았으나, 나는 내 감옥에 갇혀 있어 아무것도 할 수 없었다."], ["I sat down and simply cried.", "나는 주저앉아 그저 울었다."]], [["As I sat I heard a sound in the courtyard without--the agonised cry of a woman.", "그렇게 앉아 있는데, 바깥 안마당에서 어떤 소리가 들렸다. 한 여인의 애끓는 울부짖음이었다."], ["I rushed to the window, and throwing it up, peered out between the bars.", "나는 창가로 달려가 창을 밀어 올리고는 창살 사이로 밖을 내다보았다."], ["There, indeed, was a woman with dishevelled hair, holding her hands over her heart as one distressed with running.", "거기에는 정말로 한 여인이, 머리는 헝클어진 채, 마치 뛰어와 숨이 가쁜 사람처럼 두 손을 가슴에 얹고 서 있었다."], ["She was leaning against a corner of the gateway.", "그녀는 문간의 한 모퉁이에 기대어 있었다."], ["When she saw my face at the window she threw herself forward, and shouted in a voice laden with menace:--", "창문에 비친 내 얼굴을 보더니 그녀는 앞으로 몸을 내던지며, 위협이 가득 실린 목소리로 외쳤다."]], [["“Monster, give me my child!”", "\"이 괴물아, 내 아이를 내놔라!\""]], [["She threw herself on her knees, and raising up her hands, cried the same words in tones which wrung my heart.", "그녀는 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 치켜들며, 내 가슴을 쥐어짜는 듯한 어조로 같은 말을 외쳤다."], ["Then she tore her hair and beat her breast, and abandoned herself to all the violences of extravagant emotion.", "그러고는 머리를 쥐어뜯고 가슴을 치며, 격렬한 감정의 온갖 발작에 자신을 내맡겼다."], ["Finally, she threw herself forward, and, though I could not see her, I could hear the beating of her naked hands against the door.", "마침내 그녀는 앞으로 몸을 던졌고, 나는 그녀를 볼 수는 없었으나, 그녀가 맨손으로 문을 두드리는 소리는 들을 수 있었다."]], [["Somewhere high overhead, probably on the tower, I heard the voice of the Count calling in his harsh, metallic whisper.", "저 위 어딘가 높은 곳, 아마도 탑에서, 백작이 거칠고 쇳소리 나는 속삭임으로 부르는 목소리가 들렸다."], ["His call seemed to be answered from far and wide by the howling of wolves.", "그의 부름에 사방 멀리서 늑대들의 울부짖음이 응답하는 듯했다."], ["Before many minutes had passed a pack of them poured, like a pent-up dam when liberated, through the wide entrance into the courtyard.", "몇 분도 채 지나기 전에 늑대 떼가, 마치 막아두었던 둑이 터진 듯, 넓은 입구를 통해 안마당으로 쏟아져 들어왔다."]], [["There was no cry from the woman, and the howling of the wolves was but short.", "여인에게서는 비명 소리가 없었고, 늑대들의 울부짖음도 짧을 뿐이었다."], ["Before long they streamed away singly, licking their lips.", "이윽고 그것들은 입술을 핥으며 한 마리씩 빠져나갔다."]], [["I could not pity her, for I knew now what had become of her child, and she was better dead.", "나는 그녀를 가엾이 여길 수 없었다. 그녀의 아이가 어떻게 되었는지 이제 알았기 때문이며, 그녀로서는 죽는 편이 차라리 나았다."]], [["What shall I do?", "나는 어찌해야 하나?"], ["what can I do? How can I escape from this dreadful thing of night and gloom and fear?", "내가 무엇을 할 수 있나? 밤과 어둠과 공포로 이루어진 이 끔찍한 것으로부터 나는 어떻게 벗어날 수 있단 말인가?"]], [["25 June, morning.--No man knows till he has suffered from the night how sweet and how dear to his heart and eye the morning can be.", "6월 25일, 아침. — 밤의 고통을 겪어보기 전에는 그 누구도 아침이 자기 마음과 눈에 얼마나 달콤하고 소중할 수 있는지 알지 못한다."], ["When the sun grew so high this morning that it struck the top of the great gateway opposite my window, the high spot which it touched seemed to me as if the dove from the ark had lighted there.", "오늘 아침 해가 높이 떠올라 내 창문 맞은편 큰 문간의 꼭대기에 비치자, 그 빛이 닿은 높은 자리가 마치 방주에서 날아온 비둘기가 내려앉은 곳인 양 여겨졌다."], ["My fear fell from me as if it had been a vaporous garment which dissolved in the warmth.", "내 공포가, 마치 온기에 녹아 사라지는 안개 같은 옷이라도 되는 듯 나에게서 떨어져 나갔다."], ["I must take action of some sort whilst the courage of the day is upon me.", "낮의 용기가 나에게 깃들어 있는 동안 나는 어떤 식으로든 행동에 나서야 한다."], ["Last night one of my post-dated letters went to post, the first of that fatal series which is to blot out the very traces of my existence from the earth.", "어젯밤 날짜를 미리 적은 내 편지 가운데 하나가 부쳐졌는데, 이 땅에서 내 존재의 흔적마저 지워버릴 그 치명적인 연속물의 첫 번째 것이다."]], [["Let me not think of it. Action!", "이 일은 생각하지 말자. 행동이다!"]], [["It has always been at night-time that I have been molested or threatened, or in some way in danger or in fear.", "내가 시달리거나 위협받거나, 또는 어떤 식으로든 위험에 처하거나 두려움에 떨었던 것은 언제나 밤이었다."], ["I have not yet seen the Count in the daylight.", "나는 아직 대낮에 백작을 본 적이 없다."], ["Can it be that he sleeps when others wake, that he may be awake whilst they sleep?", "그는 남들이 깨어 있을 때 자고, 남들이 잘 때 깨어 있을 수 있도록 하는 것이 아닐까?"], ["If I could only get into his room!", "그의 방에만 들어갈 수 있다면!"], ["But there is no possible way.", "그러나 그럴 방도가 없다."], ["The door is always locked, no way for me.", "문은 늘 잠겨 있으니, 나로서는 길이 없다."]], [["Yes, there is a way, if one dares to take it.", "그렇다, 한 가지 길이 있다, 감히 그것을 택하기만 한다면."], ["Where his body has gone why may not another body go?", "그의 몸이 갔던 곳이라면, 어찌 다른 몸이라고 못 가겠는가?"], ["I have seen him myself crawl from his window.", "나는 그가 자기 창문에서 기어 나오는 것을 내 눈으로 보았다."], ["Why should not I imitate him, and go in by his window?", "어찌 나라고 그를 흉내 내어 그의 창문으로 들어가지 못하겠는가?"], ["The chances are desperate, but my need is more desperate still.", "가망은 절망적이나, 내 처지는 그보다 더 절망적이다. 나는 그것을 감행하겠다."], ["I shall risk it. At the worst it can only be death; and a man’s death is not a calf’s, and the dreaded Hereafter may still be open to me.", "최악이라야 죽음일 뿐이며, 사람의 죽음은 송아지의 죽음과는 다르니, 그 두려운 내세(來世)가 아직 나에게 열려 있을지도 모른다. 하느님, 이 일에서 저를 도우소서!"], ["God help me in my task!", "잘 있으오, 미나, 내가 실패한다면."], ["Good-bye, Mina, if I fail; good-bye, my faithful friend and second father; good-bye, all, and last of all Mina!", "잘 있으십시오, 나의 충실한 벗이자 또 한 분의 아버지여. 모두들 잘 있으오, 그리고 마지막으로 또, 미나여!"]], [["Same day, later.--I have made the effort, and God, helping me, have come safely back to this room.", "같은 날, 나중에. — 나는 그 일을 시도했고, 하느님의 도우심으로 이 방에 무사히 돌아왔다."], ["I must put down every detail in order.", "모든 것을 빠짐없이 순서대로 적어두어야겠다."], ["I went whilst my courage was fresh straight to the window on the south side, and at once got outside on the narrow ledge of stone which runs around the building on this side.", "나는 용기가 식기 전에 곧장 남쪽 창문으로 가서, 곧바로 이쪽 면을 따라 건물을 빙 두른 좁은 돌 턱으로 나섰다."], ["The stones are big and roughly cut, and the mortar has by process of time been washed away between them.", "돌들은 크고 거칠게 깎여 있었으며, 그 사이의 회반죽은 오랜 세월에 씻겨 없어진 상태였다."], ["I took off my boots, and ventured out on the desperate way.", "나는 장화를 벗고 그 절망적인 길로 나섰다."], ["I looked down once, so as to make sure that a sudden glimpse of the awful depth would not overcome me, but after that kept my eyes away from it.", "끔찍한 깊이가 문득 눈에 들어와 나를 압도하지 않도록 한 번만 아래를 내려다보았고, 그 뒤로는 거기서 눈을 거두었다."], ["I knew pretty well the direction and distance of the Count’s window, and made for it as well as I could, having regard to the opportunities available.", "나는 백작의 창문이 어느 방향에 얼마쯤 떨어져 있는지 꽤 잘 알고 있었으므로, 주어진 발판들을 살피면서 할 수 있는 한 그쪽으로 나아갔다."], ["I did not feel dizzy--I suppose I was too excited--and the time seemed ridiculously short till I found myself standing on the window-sill and trying to raise up the sash.", "어지럽지는 않았다 — 너무 흥분해 있었던 모양이다 — 그리고 어느새 창턱에 서서 창틀을 들어 올리려 하고 있었으니, 시간은 우습도록 짧게 느껴졌다."], ["I was filled with agitation, however, when I bent down and slid feet foremost in through the window.", "그러나 몸을 숙여 발부터 창문 안으로 미끄러져 들어갈 때에는 동요로 가슴이 가득 찼다."], ["Then I looked around for the Count, but, with surprise and gladness, made a discovery. The room was empty!", "그런 다음 나는 백작을 찾아 둘러보았으나, 놀랍고도 기쁘게도 한 가지 발견을 했다. 방이 비어 있었던 것이다!"], ["It was barely furnished with odd things, which seemed to have never been used; the furniture was something the same style as that in the south rooms, and was covered with dust.", "방에는 한 번도 쓰인 적 없어 보이는 잡다한 물건들이 듬성듬성 놓여 있을 뿐이었다. 가구는 남쪽 방들의 것과 다소 같은 양식이었으며, 먼지가 덮여 있었다."], ["I looked for the key, but it was not in the lock, and I could not find it anywhere.", "나는 열쇠를 찾아보았으나 자물쇠에 꽂혀 있지 않았고, 어디에서도 찾을 수 없었다."], ["The only thing I found was a great heap of gold in one corner--gold of all kinds, Roman, and British, and Austrian, and Hungarian, and Greek and Turkish money, covered with a film of dust, as though it had lain long in the ground.", "내가 찾아낸 유일한 것은 한구석에 쌓인 거대한 금더미였다 — 로마, 영국, 오스트리아, 헝가리, 그리스, 터키의 온갖 화폐가, 마치 땅속에 오래 묻혀 있었던 듯 먼지의 막을 뒤집어쓰고 있었다."], ["None of it that I noticed was less than three hundred years old.", "내가 눈여겨본 것 중에 삼백 년이 안 된 것은 하나도 없었다."], ["There were also chains and ornaments, some jewelled, but all of them old and stained.", "사슬과 장신구도 있었는데, 더러는 보석이 박혀 있었으나 하나같이 오래되고 빛이 바래 있었다."]], [["At one corner of the room was a heavy door.", "방 한쪽 구석에는 묵직한 문이 하나 있었다."], ["I tried it, for, since I could not find the key of the room or the key of the outer door, which was the main object of my search, I must make further examination, or all my efforts would be in vain.", "나는 그것을 흔들어 보았다. 방의 열쇠도, 내가 찾는 주된 목적이던 바깥문의 열쇠도 찾을 수 없는 마당이니, 더 살펴보지 않으면 내 모든 노력이 헛수고가 될 터였기 때문이다."], ["It was open, and led through a stone passage to a circular stairway, which went steeply down.", "문은 열려 있었고, 돌로 된 통로를 지나 가파르게 아래로 내려가는 원형 계단으로 이어졌다."], ["I descended, minding carefully where I went, for the stairs were dark, being only lit by loopholes in the heavy masonry.", "나는 발걸음을 조심스레 살피며 내려갔다. 계단은 두꺼운 석조 벽에 난 총안(銃眼)에서 들어오는 빛만으로 비칠 뿐이라 어두웠기 때문이다."], ["At the bottom there was a dark, tunnel-like passage, through which came a deathly, sickly odour, the odour of old earth newly turned.", "맨 아래에는 어둡고 굴 같은 통로가 있었고, 그곳으로부터 죽음 같은, 메스꺼운 냄새가, 갓 파헤친 오래된 흙냄새가 풍겨 왔다."], ["As I went through the passage the smell grew closer and heavier.", "통로를 지날수록 냄새는 더 가까워지고 더 짙어졌다."], ["At last I pulled open a heavy door which stood ajar, and found myself in an old, ruined chapel, which had evidently been used as a graveyard.", "마침내 나는 반쯤 열려 있던 묵직한 문 하나를 잡아당겨 열고는, 오래되어 허물어진 예배당 안에 서 있는 나 자신을 발견했는데, 분명 묘지로 쓰여 온 곳이었다."], ["The roof was broken, and in two places were steps leading to vaults, but the ground had recently been dug over, and the earth placed in great wooden boxes, manifestly those which had been brought by the Slovaks.", "지붕은 무너져 있었고, 두 군데에는 지하 납골소로 내려가는 계단이 있었으나, 땅은 최근에 파헤쳐졌고, 그 흙은 커다란 나무 상자들에, 분명 슬로바키아인들이 가져온 그 상자들에 담겨 있었다."], ["There was nobody about, and I made search for any further outlet, but there was none.", "주위에는 아무도 없었으며, 나는 더 빠져나갈 출구가 있는지 찾아보았으나 없었다."], ["Then I went over every inch of the ground, so as not to lose a chance.", "그래서 나는 한 치의 기회도 놓치지 않으려고 땅을 한 뼘 한 뼘 샅샅이 살폈다."], ["I went down even into the vaults, where the dim light struggled, although to do so was a dread to my very soul.", "어슴푸레한 빛이 간신히 비쳐드는 지하 납골소까지도 내려갔는데, 그렇게 하는 것이 내 영혼 자체에 두려운 일이긴 했다."], ["Into two of these I went, but saw nothing except fragments of old coffins and piles of dust; in the third, however, I made a discovery.", "그중 두 곳에 들어가 보았으나 낡은 관 조각들과 먼지 더미밖에 보이지 않았다. 그러나 세 번째 곳에서 나는 한 가지 발견을 했다."]], [["There, in one of the great boxes, of which there were fifty in all, on a pile of newly dug earth, lay the Count!", "거기, 모두 합쳐 쉰 개나 되는 큰 상자들 가운데 하나에, 갓 파낸 흙더미 위에, 백작이 누워 있었다!"], ["He was either dead or asleep, I could not say which--for the eyes were open and stony, but without the glassiness of death--and the cheeks had the warmth of life through all their pallor; the lips were as red as ever.", "그가 죽은 것인지 잠든 것인지 나로서는 알 수 없었다 — 두 눈은 뜬 채 돌처럼 굳어 있었으나 죽음의 흐릿함은 없었고, 두 뺨은 온통 창백하면서도 살아 있는 온기를 띠었으며, 입술은 여느 때처럼 붉었다."], ["But there was no sign of movement, no pulse, no breath, no beating of the heart.", "그러나 움직임의 기색은 전혀 없었고, 맥박도, 숨결도, 심장의 고동도 없었다."], ["I bent over him, and tried to find any sign of life, but in vain.", "나는 그 위로 몸을 숙여 생명의 흔적을 찾아보려 했으나 헛일이었다."], ["He could not have lain there long, for the earthy smell would have passed away in a few hours.", "그가 거기 누운 지 오래되었을 리는 없었으니, 오래되었다면 그 흙냄새가 몇 시간 안에 가셨을 것이기 때문이다."], ["By the side of the box was its cover, pierced with holes here and there.", "상자 옆에는 군데군데 구멍이 뚫린 그 뚜껑이 놓여 있었다."], ["I thought he might have the keys on him, but when I went to search I saw the dead eyes, and in them, dead though they were, such a look of hate, though unconscious of me or my presence, that I fled from the place, and leaving the Count’s room by the window, crawled again up the castle wall.", "나는 그가 열쇠를 몸에 지니고 있을지도 모른다고 생각했으나, 뒤져보려고 다가가자 그 죽은 눈이 보였고, 비록 죽은 눈이었으나, 또 나나 내 존재를 의식하지는 못했으나, 그 안에 어찌나 깊은 증오의 빛이 어려 있던지, 나는 그곳에서 달아나, 백작의 방을 창문으로 빠져나와 다시 성벽을 기어올랐다."], ["Regaining my room, I threw myself panting upon the bed and tried to think....", "내 방에 돌아온 나는 헐떡이며 침대에 몸을 던지고 생각해 보려 애썼다……."]], [["29 June.--To-day is the date of my last letter, and the Count has taken steps to prove that it was genuine, for again I saw him leave the castle by the same window, and in my clothes.", "6월 29일. — 오늘은 내 마지막 편지의 날짜이며, 백작은 그것이 진짜임을 입증할 조치를 취했다. 그가 또다시 같은 창문으로, 내 옷을 입고 성을 떠나는 것을 보았기 때문이다."], ["As he went down the wall, lizard fashion, I wished I had a gun or some lethal weapon, that I might destroy him; but I fear that no weapon wrought alone by man’s hand would have any effect on him.", "그가 도마뱀처럼 벽을 타고 내려갈 때, 나는 총이나 무슨 살상 무기라도 있어서 그를 없애버릴 수 있었으면 했다. 그러나 사람의 손만으로 만들어진 무기로는 그에게 아무런 효력도 없을까 두렵다."], ["I dared not wait to see him return, for I feared to see those weird sisters.", "나는 그가 돌아오는 것을 기다려 볼 엄두가 나지 않았다. 그 기괴한 자매들을 볼까 두려웠기 때문이다."], ["I came back to the library, and read there till I fell asleep.", "나는 서재로 돌아와, 잠이 들 때까지 거기서 책을 읽었다."]], [["I was awakened by the Count, who looked at me as grimly as a man can look as he said:--", "나는 백작 때문에 잠에서 깨었는데, 그는 사람이 지을 수 있는 가장 험상궂은 표정으로 나를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“To-morrow, my friend, we must part.", "\"내일이면, 나의 벗, 우리는 헤어져야 하오."], ["You return to your beautiful England, I to some work which may have such an end that we may never meet.", "당신은 당신의 아름다운 영국으로 돌아가고, 나는 어쩌면 다시는 만나지 못할 그런 결말을 맞을지도 모를 어떤 일에 나서야 하오."], ["Your letter home has been despatched; to-morrow I shall not be here, but all shall be ready for your journey.", "당신이 집으로 보내는 편지는 부쳐졌소. 내일이면 나는 여기 없겠으나, 당신의 여정을 위한 모든 준비는 갖추어져 있을 것이오."], ["In the morning come the Szgany, who have some labours of their own here, and also come some Slovaks.", "아침이면 이곳에 자기들 나름의 일이 있는 즈가니가 오고, 슬로바키아인들도 좀 올 것이오."], ["When they have gone, my carriage shall come for you, and shall bear you to the Borgo Pass to meet the diligence from Bukovina to Bistritz.", "그들이 떠나면 내 마차가 당신을 데리러 와서, 부코비나에서 비스트리차로 가는 역마차를 만나도록 보르고 고개까지 당신을 태워다 줄 것이오."], ["But I am in hopes that I shall see more of you at Castle Dracula.”", "그러나 나는 당신을 드라큘라 성에서 더 보게 되기를 바라고 있소.\""], ["I suspected him, and determined to test his sincerity.", "나는 그를 의심했고, 그의 진심을 시험해 보기로 했다."], ["Sincerity!", "진심이라니!"], ["It seems like a profanation of the word to write it in connection with such a monster, so asked him point-blank:--", "그런 괴물과 결부지어 이 말을 적는다는 것이 그 말에 대한 모독처럼 여겨지나, 어쨌든 나는 단도직입적으로 물었다."]], [["“Why may I not go to-night?”", "\"어찌하여 오늘 밤에는 갈 수 없는 것이오?\""]], [["“Because, dear sir, my coachman and horses are away on a mission.”", "\"그건, 친애하는 선생, 내 마부와 말들이 어떤 볼일로 나가 있기 때문이오.\""]], [["“But I would walk with pleasure.", "\"그러나 나는 기꺼이 걸어가겠소."], ["I want to get away at once.” He smiled, such a soft, smooth, diabolical smile that I knew there was some trick behind his smoothness.", "당장 떠나고 싶소.\" 그는 미소를 지었는데, 어찌나 부드럽고 매끄러운, 그러나 악마 같은 미소였던지, 나는 그 매끄러움 뒤에 무슨 술책이 숨어 있음을 알았다."], ["He said:--", "그가 말했다."]], [["“And your baggage?”", "\"그럼 당신의 짐은?\""]], [["“I do not care about it.", "\"그런 건 상관없소."], ["I can send for it some other time.”", "나중에 사람을 보내 가져가게 하면 되오.\""]], [["The Count stood up, and said, with a sweet courtesy which made me rub my eyes, it seemed so real:--", "백작은 일어서더니, 어찌나 진실해 보였던지 내 눈을 비비게 할 만큼 달콤한 정중함으로 말했다."]], [["“You English have a saying which is close to my heart, for its spirit is that which rules our boyars: ‘Welcome the coming; speed the parting guest.’ Come with me, my dear young friend.", "\"당신네 영국인에게는 내 마음에 꼭 드는 격언이 하나 있소. 그 정신이 곧 우리 보야르를 지배하는 것이기 때문이오. '오는 손님은 반기고, 가는 손님은 서둘러 보내라.'"], ["Not an hour shall you wait in my house against your will, though sad am I at your going, and that you so suddenly desire it. Come!”", "함께 가십시다, 친애하는 나의 젊은 벗이여. 당신의 떠남이 슬프고, 또 당신이 그토록 갑작스레 그것을 바라는 것이 슬프기는 하나, 단 한 시간도 당신을 뜻에 반하여 내 집에 붙들어두지는 않겠소. 갑시다!\""], ["With a stately gravity, he, with the lamp, preceded me down the stairs and along the hall. Suddenly he stopped.", "위엄 있고 묵직한 태도로 그는 램프를 들고 나를 앞장서 계단을 내려가 홀을 따라 걸었다. 그러더니 문득 걸음을 멈추었다."]], [["“Hark!”", "\"쉿!\""]], [["Close at hand came the howling of many wolves.", "바로 가까이에서 수많은 늑대들의 울부짖음이 들려왔다."], ["It was almost as if the sound sprang up at the rising of his hand, just as the music of a great orchestra seems to leap under the bâton of the conductor.", "그것은 마치 그가 손을 들어 올림과 동시에 그 소리가 솟구쳐 오르는 듯, 흡사 큰 관현악단의 음악이 지휘자의 지휘봉 아래 뛰어오르는 것 같았다."], ["After a pause of a moment, he proceeded, in his stately way, to the door, drew back the ponderous bolts, unhooked the heavy chains, and began to draw it open.", "잠시 멈추었다가 그는 그 위엄 있는 태도로 문 쪽으로 나아가, 묵직한 빗장을 뒤로 물리고, 두꺼운 사슬을 풀어, 문을 당겨 열기 시작했다."]], [["To my intense astonishment I saw that it was unlocked.", "나는 그것이 잠겨 있지 않은 것을 보고 몹시 놀랐다."], ["Suspiciously, I looked all round, but could see no key of any kind.", "미심쩍어 사방을 다 둘러보았으나, 어떤 종류의 열쇠도 보이지 않았다."]], [["As the door began to open, the howling of the wolves without grew louder and angrier; their red jaws, with champing teeth, and their blunt-clawed feet as they leaped, came in through the opening door.", "문이 열리기 시작하자 바깥 늑대들의 울부짖음은 더 크고 더 사납게 변했다. 그것들의 붉은 아가리가, 으드득거리는 이빨과 함께, 그리고 펄쩍 뛰어오를 때의 뭉툭한 발톱 달린 발이, 열리는 문틈으로 들어왔다."], ["I knew then that to struggle at the moment against the Count was useless.", "그때 나는 지금 이 순간 백작에게 맞서 발버둥치는 것이 부질없다는 것을 알았다."], ["With such allies as these at his command, I could do nothing.", "이런 것들을 수하로 부리는 그에게 나는 아무것도 할 수 없었다."], ["But still the door continued slowly to open, and only the Count’s body stood in the gap.", "그래도 문은 천천히 계속 열려갔고, 그 틈을 막고 선 것은 오직 백작의 몸뚱이뿐이었다."], ["Suddenly it struck me that this might be the moment and means of my doom; I was to be given to the wolves, and at my own instigation.", "문득, 어쩌면 이것이 내 파멸의 순간이자 그 방편일지도 모른다는 생각이 머리를 스쳤다. 나는, 그것도 내 스스로의 부추김으로, 늑대들에게 넘겨지려는 것이었다."], ["There was a diabolical wickedness in the idea great enough for the Count, and as a last chance I cried out:--", "이 발상에는 백작에게 어울릴 만큼 악마적인 사악함이 담겨 있었고, 나는 마지막 기회로 외쳤다."]], [["“Shut the door; I shall wait till morning!”", "\"문을 닫으시오. 아침까지 기다리겠소!\""], ["and covered my face with my hands to hide my tears of bitter disappointment.", "그리고 두 손으로 얼굴을 가려, 쓰디쓴 실망의 눈물을 감추었다."], ["With one sweep of his powerful arm, the Count threw the door shut, and the great bolts clanged and echoed through the hall as they shot back into their places.", "백작은 그 억센 팔을 한 번 휘둘러 문을 닫았고, 커다란 빗장들이 제자리로 되돌아가며 홀 안을 쾅 하고 울리며 메아리쳤다."]], [["In silence we returned to the library, and after a minute or two I went to my own room.", "우리는 말없이 서재로 돌아왔고, 한두 분쯤 뒤에 나는 내 방으로 갔다."], ["The last I saw of Count Dracula was his kissing his hand to me; with a red light of triumph in his eyes, and with a smile that Judas in hell might be proud of.", "드라큘라 백작을 마지막으로 본 것은, 그가 나에게 손에 입을 맞추어 보내던 모습이었다. 두 눈에는 붉은 승리의 빛이 어려 있었고, 입가에는 지옥의 유다라도 자랑스러워했을 법한 미소가 떠올라 있었다."]], [["When I was in my room and about to lie down, I thought I heard a whispering at my door.", "내 방에 들어가 막 누우려 할 때, 나는 문 밖에서 무언가 속삭이는 소리를 들은 것 같았다."], ["I went to it softly and listened.", "나는 살그머니 문으로 다가가 귀를 기울였다."], ["Unless my ears deceived me, I heard the voice of the Count:--", "내 귀가 나를 속인 것이 아니라면, 나는 백작의 목소리를 들었다."]], [["“Back, back, to your own place!", "\"물러가라, 물러가, 너희 자리로!"], ["Your time is not yet come. Wait! Have patience!", "너희 때는 아직 오지 않았다. 기다려라! 참아라!"], ["To-night is mine.", "오늘 밤은 내 것이다."], ["To-morrow night is yours!”", "내일 밤은 너희 것이다!\""], ["There was a low, sweet ripple of laughter, and in a rage I threw open the door, and saw without the three terrible women licking their lips.", "낮고 달콤한 웃음소리의 물결이 일었고, 나는 분노에 차 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다. 그러자 밖에는 그 무서운 세 여인이 입술을 핥고 있었다."], ["As I appeared they all joined in a horrible laugh, and ran away.", "내가 나타나자 그들은 다 함께 소름 끼치는 웃음을 터뜨리며 달아났다."]], [["I came back to my room and threw myself on my knees.", "나는 내 방으로 돌아와 무릎을 꿇었다."], ["It is then so near the end? To-morrow! to-morrow!", "그렇다면 끝이 이토록 가까운 것인가? 내일! 내일!"], ["Lord, help me, and those to whom I am dear!", "주여, 저를, 그리고 저를 사랑하는 이들을 도우소서!"]], [["30 June, morning.--These may be the last words I ever write in this diary.", "6월 30일, 아침. — 이것이 내가 이 일기에 적는 마지막 말이 될지도 모른다."], ["I slept till just before the dawn, and when I woke threw myself on my knees, for I determined that if Death came he should find me ready.", "나는 동트기 직전까지 잤고, 깨어나서는 무릎을 꿇었다. 죽음이 찾아온다면, 그것이 나를 채비된 모습으로 발견하게 하리라 마음먹었기 때문이다."]], [["At last I felt that subtle change in the air, and knew that the morning had come.", "마침내 나는 공기 속의 그 미묘한 변화를 느꼈고, 아침이 왔음을 알았다."], ["Then came the welcome cock-crow, and I felt that I was safe.", "이윽고 반가운 닭 울음소리가 들려왔고, 나는 내가 안전하다고 느꼈다."], ["With a glad heart, I opened my door and ran down to the hall.", "기쁜 마음으로 나는 문을 열고 홀로 달려 내려갔다."], ["I had seen that the door was unlocked, and now escape was before me. With hands that trembled with eagerness, I unhooked the chains and drew back the massive bolts.", "문이 잠겨 있지 않은 것을 보아두었던 터라, 이제 탈출이 내 앞에 놓여 있었다. 조바심에 떨리는 손으로 나는 사슬을 풀고 육중한 빗장을 뒤로 물렸다."]], [["But the door would not move.", "그러나 문은 꼼짝도 하지 않았다."], ["Despair seized me.", "절망이 나를 사로잡았다."], ["I pulled, and pulled, at the door, and shook it till, massive as it was, it rattled in its casement.", "나는 문을 당기고 또 당기며, 그토록 육중한 문이 문틀 안에서 덜컹거릴 때까지 흔들어댔다."], ["I could see the bolt shot.", "빗장이 걸려 있는 것이 보였다."], ["It had been locked after I left the Count.", "내가 백작을 떠난 뒤에 잠긴 것이었다."]], [["Then a wild desire took me to obtain that key at any risk, and I determined then and there to scale the wall again and gain the Count’s room.", "그러자 어떤 위험을 무릅쓰고서라도 그 열쇠를 손에 넣겠다는 격렬한 욕구가 나를 사로잡았고, 나는 그 자리에서 곧장 다시 벽을 기어올라 백작의 방으로 가기로 마음먹었다."], ["He might kill me, but death now seemed the happier choice of evils.", "그가 나를 죽일지도 몰랐으나, 이제 죽음은 차라리 더 행복한 쪽의 화(禍)로 여겨졌다."], ["Without a pause I rushed up to the east window, and scrambled down the wall, as before, into the Count’s room.", "나는 한순간도 멈추지 않고 동쪽 창문으로 달려가, 전처럼 벽을 기어 내려가 백작의 방으로 들어갔다."], ["It was empty, but that was as I expected.", "방은 비어 있었으나, 그것은 내가 예상한 대로였다."], ["I could not see a key anywhere, but the heap of gold remained.", "어디에서도 열쇠는 보이지 않았으나, 금더미는 그대로 있었다."], ["I went through the door in the corner and down the winding stair and along the dark passage to the old chapel. I knew now well enough where to find the monster I sought.", "나는 구석의 문을 지나 나선 계단을 내려가, 어두운 통로를 따라 그 오래된 예배당으로 갔다. 이제 나는 내가 찾는 그 괴물을 어디서 찾아야 하는지 충분히 잘 알고 있었다."]], [["The great box was in the same place, close against the wall, but the lid was laid on it, not fastened down, but with the nails ready in their places to be hammered home.", "그 큰 상자는 벽에 바짝 붙은, 같은 자리에 있었으나, 뚜껑이 못으로 박혀 있지는 않은 채 그 위에 덮여만 있었는데, 못은 박아 넣을 채비를 한 듯 제자리에 꽂혀 있었다."], ["I knew I must reach the body for the key, so I raised the lid, and laid it back against the wall; and then I saw something which filled my very soul with horror.", "열쇠를 얻으려면 그 몸에 닿아야 한다는 것을 알았기에, 나는 뚜껑을 들어 올려 벽에 기대어 놓았다. 그러자 나는 내 영혼 자체를 공포로 가득 채우는 무언가를 보았다."], ["There lay the Count, but looking as if his youth had been half renewed, for the white hair and moustache were changed to dark iron-grey; the cheeks were fuller, and the white skin seemed ruby-red underneath; the mouth was redder than ever, for on the lips were gouts of fresh blood, which trickled from the corners of the mouth and ran over the chin and neck.", "거기 백작이 누워 있었는데, 흡사 젊음이 절반쯤 되살아난 것처럼 보였다. 흰 머리칼과 콧수염은 짙은 무쇠빛 회색으로 바뀌었고, 두 뺨은 더 통통해졌으며, 흰 살갗 아래는 루비처럼 붉어 보였다. 입은 어느 때보다 더 붉었으니, 입술 위에 신선한 피가 방울방울 맺혀 입가에서 흘러내려 턱과 목으로 흐르고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Even the deep, burning eyes seemed set amongst swollen flesh, for the lids and pouches underneath were bloated.", "깊고 이글거리는 두 눈마저 부풀어 오른 살 속에 박힌 듯했으니, 눈꺼풀과 그 아래 처진 살이 부어 있었다."], ["It seemed as if the whole awful creature were simply gorged with blood.", "마치 그 끔찍한 괴물 전체가 그저 피로 잔뜩 배불러 있는 듯했다."], ["He lay like a filthy leech, exhausted with his repletion.", "그는 포식으로 늘어진 더러운 거머리처럼 누워 있었다."], ["I shuddered as I bent over to touch him, and every sense in me revolted at the contact; but I had to search, or I was lost.", "나는 그를 만지려 몸을 숙이면서 몸서리를 쳤고, 내 안의 모든 감각이 그 접촉에 거부감을 일으켰다. 그러나 나는 뒤져야만 했으니, 그러지 않으면 나는 끝장이었다."], ["The coming night might see my own body a banquet in a similar way to those horrid three.", "다가오는 밤이면 내 몸뚱이도 그 끔찍한 세 여인의 잔치처럼 비슷한 식으로 잔칫상이 될지도 몰랐다."], ["I felt all over the body, but no sign could I find of the key.", "나는 그 몸을 구석구석 더듬어 보았으나, 열쇠의 흔적은 찾을 수 없었다."], ["Then I stopped and looked at the Count.", "그러다 나는 멈추고 백작을 바라보았다."], ["There was a mocking smile on the bloated face which seemed to drive me mad.", "그 부어오른 얼굴에는 조롱하는 미소가 어려 있었는데, 그것은 나를 미치게 만들 것만 같았다."], ["This was the being I was helping to transfer to London, where, perhaps, for centuries to come he might, amongst its teeming millions, satiate his lust for blood, and create a new and ever-widening circle of semi-demons to batten on the helpless.", "이자가 바로, 내가 런던으로 옮겨가도록 돕고 있는 그 존재였다. 그곳에 가면 그는 어쩌면 앞으로 수백 년 동안, 그 우글거리는 수백만 명 가운데서 자기의 피에 대한 욕망을 채우고, 무력한 자들에게 빌붙어 살아갈 반(半)악마들의 무리를, 새롭고 끝없이 넓어져 가는 무리를 만들어낼지도 몰랐다."], ["The very thought drove me mad.", "그 생각만으로도 나는 미칠 것 같았다."], ["A terrible desire came upon me to rid the world of such a monster.", "이런 괴물을 세상에서 없애버리고 싶다는 무서운 욕망이 나를 사로잡았다."], ["There was no lethal weapon at hand, but I seized a shovel which the workmen had been using to fill the cases, and lifting it high, struck, with the edge downward, at the hateful face.", "손 닿는 곳에 살상 무기는 없었으나, 나는 인부들이 상자에 흙을 채우는 데 쓰던 삽 하나를 움켜잡고는, 그것을 높이 쳐들어 날이 아래로 가게 하여 그 가증스러운 얼굴을 내리쳤다."], ["But as I did so the head turned, and the eyes fell full upon me, with all their blaze of basilisk horror.", "그러나 내가 그렇게 하는 순간 머리가 돌아가, 두 눈이 온전히 나에게로 떨어졌는데, 거기에는 바실리스크 같은 공포의 불길이 가득했다."], ["The sight seemed to paralyse me, and the shovel turned in my hand and glanced from the face, merely making a deep gash above the forehead.", "그 광경에 나는 마비되는 듯했고, 삽은 내 손안에서 비틀려 그 얼굴을 빗맞고 미끄러지면서, 이마 위에 깊은 상처만 하나 냈을 뿐이다."], ["The shovel fell from my hand across the box, and as I pulled it away the flange of the blade caught the edge of the lid which fell over again, and hid the horrid thing from my sight.", "삽은 내 손에서 떨어져 상자를 가로질러 놓였고, 내가 그것을 빼내려 하자 칼날의 가장자리가 뚜껑의 모서리에 걸려, 뚜껑이 다시 위로 덮이며 그 끔찍한 것을 내 시야에서 가렸다."], ["The last glimpse I had was of the bloated face, blood-stained and fixed with a grin of malice which would have held its own in the nethermost hell.", "마지막으로 흘끗 본 것은 그 부어오른 얼굴이었는데, 피로 얼룩지고, 더없이 깊은 지옥에서도 능히 버텨낼 만한 악의의 히죽거림으로 굳어 있었다."]], [["I thought and thought what should be my next move, but my brain seemed on fire, and I waited with a despairing feeling growing over me.", "나는 다음에 무엇을 해야 할지 생각하고 또 생각했으나, 머릿속이 불타는 듯했고, 절망감이 점점 더 나를 뒤덮어 가는 가운데 나는 기다렸다."], ["As I waited I heard in the distance a gipsy song sung by merry voices coming closer, and through their song the rolling of heavy wheels and the cracking of whips; the Szgany and the Slovaks of whom the Count had spoken were coming.", "그렇게 기다리는데, 멀리서 흥겨운 목소리들이 부르는 집시 노래가 점점 가까이 다가오는 것이 들렸고, 그 노랫소리 사이로 무거운 바퀴가 굴러오는 소리와 채찍이 갈라지는 소리가 들렸다. 백작이 말한 그 즈가니와 슬로바키아인들이 오고 있는 것이었다."], ["With a last look around and at the box which contained the vile body, I ran from the place and gained the Count’s room, determined to rush out at the moment the door should be opened.", "마지막으로 주위를, 그리고 그 비열한 몸뚱이가 든 상자를 한 번 둘러본 뒤, 나는 그곳에서 달아나 백작의 방으로 들어가서는, 문이 열리는 순간 뛰쳐나가기로 마음먹었다."], ["With strained ears, I listened, and heard downstairs the grinding of the key in the great lock and the falling back of the heavy door.", "나는 귀를 곤두세우고 들었고, 아래층에서 커다란 자물쇠에 열쇠가 갈리는 소리와 묵직한 문이 열려 젖혀지는 소리를 들었다."], ["There must have been some other means of entry, or some one had a key for one of the locked doors.", "무언가 다른 출입 방도가 있었거나, 아니면 누군가가 잠긴 문들 가운데 하나의 열쇠를 가지고 있었던 것이리라."], ["Then there came the sound of many feet tramping and dying away in some passage which sent up a clanging echo.", "그러고는 여러 발이 쿵쿵 짓밟으며 걸어가다가, 쩌렁쩌렁한 메아리를 울려 보내는 어떤 통로에서 잦아드는 소리가 들렸다."], ["I turned to run down again towards the vault, where I might find the new entrance; but at the moment there seemed to come a violent puff of wind, and the door to the winding stair blew to with a shock that set the dust from the lintels flying.", "나는 다시 지하 납골소 쪽으로 달려 내려가, 그 새로운 입구를 찾아보려고 돌아섰다. 그런데 바로 그 순간 거센 바람이 한 차례 훅 불어오는 듯하더니, 나선 계단으로 통하는 문이 쾅 하고 닫혀, 그 충격으로 상인방(上引枋)에서 먼지가 풀썩 날렸다."], ["When I ran to push it open, I found that it was hopelessly fast.", "그것을 밀어 열려고 달려갔으나, 그것은 어찌해 볼 도리 없이 단단히 닫혀 있었다."], ["I was again a prisoner, and the net of doom was closing round me more closely.", "나는 또다시 죄수가 되었고, 파멸의 그물이 점점 더 바짝 나를 옥죄어 오고 있었다."]], [["As I write there is in the passage below a sound of many tramping feet and the crash of weights being set down heavily, doubtless the boxes, with their freight of earth.", "이 글을 쓰는 지금, 아래 통로에서 여러 발이 쿵쿵 짓밟는 소리와 무거운 것들이 둔중하게 내려놓이는 소리가 들린다. 분명 흙을 실은 그 상자들이리라. 망치질 소리가 들린다."], ["There is a sound of hammering; it is the box being nailed down. Now I can hear the heavy feet tramping again along the hall, with many other idle feet coming behind them.", "상자에 못을 박는 소리다. 이제 무거운 발들이 다시 홀을 따라 쿵쿵 걸어가는 소리가 들리고, 그 뒤로 빈둥거리는 여러 다른 발들이 따라오는 소리도 들린다."]], [["The door is shut, and the chains rattle; there is a grinding of the key in the lock; I can hear the key withdraw: then another door opens and shuts; I hear the creaking of lock and bolt.", "문이 닫히고, 사슬이 절그럭거린다. 자물쇠에 열쇠가 갈리고, 열쇠를 빼는 소리가 들린다. 그러더니 또 다른 문이 열렸다 닫히고, 자물쇠와 빗장이 삐걱대는 소리가 들린다."]], [["Hark!", "쉿!"], ["in the courtyard and down the rocky way the roll of heavy wheels, the crack of whips, and the chorus of the Szgany as they pass into the distance.", "안마당에서, 그리고 바위투성이 길 아래로, 무거운 바퀴가 굴러가는 소리, 채찍이 갈라지는 소리, 그리고 멀리 지나가는 즈가니의 합창 소리가 들린다."]], [["I am alone in the castle with those awful women. Faugh!", "나는 그 끔찍한 여인들과 함께 성에 홀로 남았다. 윽!"], ["Mina is a woman, and there is nought in common.", "미나도 여자이나, 그들과는 어떤 공통점도 없다."], ["They are devils of the Pit!", "그들은 구렁텅이에서 온 악마들이다!"]], [["I shall not remain alone with them; I shall try to scale the castle wall farther than I have yet attempted.", "나는 그들과 단둘이 남아 있지는 않겠다. 지금까지 시도한 것보다 더 멀리 성벽을 기어 내려가 보겠다."], ["I shall take some of the gold with me, lest I want it later. I may find a way from this dreadful place.", "나중에 필요할지 모르니 금을 좀 가지고 가겠다. 어쩌면 이 끔찍한 곳에서 빠져나갈 길을 찾을지도 모른다."]], [["And then away for home!", "그리고 그다음엔 집으로!"], ["away to the quickest and nearest train!", "가장 빠르고 가장 가까운 기차로!"], ["away from this cursed spot, from this cursed land, where the devil and his children still walk with earthly feet!", "이 저주받은 곳에서, 이 저주받은 땅에서, 악마와 그 자식들이 아직도 지상의 발로 걸어 다니는 이곳에서 벗어나는 것이다!"]], [["At least God’s mercy is better than that of these monsters, and the precipice is steep and high. At its foot a man may sleep--as a man.", "적어도 하느님의 자비가 이 괴물들의 자비보다는 나으며, 그 벼랑은 가파르고 높다. 그 기슭에서라면 사람은 잠들 수 있으리라 — 사람으로서."], ["Good-bye, all! Mina!", "모두들 잘 있으오! 미나여!"]], [["“My dearest Lucy,--", "「미나 머리 양이 루시 웨스텐라 양에게 보내는 편지」 「5월 9일."]], [["“Forgive my long delay in writing, but I have been simply overwhelmed with work.", "「나의 가장 사랑하는 루시에게.—\n「편지가 이리 늦어진 것을 용서해줘."], ["The life of an assistant schoolmistress is sometimes trying.", "일에 그야말로 파묻혀 지냈단다. 보조 여교사의 삶이란 때로 고된 법이지."], ["I am longing to be with you, and by the sea, where we can talk together freely and build our castles in the air.", "나는 너와 함께, 그것도 바닷가에서, 마음껏 이야기를 나누며 허황된 공중누각을 지어보고 싶어 견딜 수가 없어."], ["I have been working very hard lately, because I want to keep up with Jonathan’s studies, and I have been practising shorthand very assiduously.", "요즘 무척 열심히 공부하고 있는데, 조너선의 학업을 따라가고 싶은 까닭이야. 그래서 속기를 아주 부지런히 연습하고 있지."], ["When we are married I shall be able to be useful to Jonathan, and if I can stenograph well enough I can take down what he wants to say in this way and write it out for him on the typewriter, at which also I am practising very hard.", "우리가 결혼하면 나는 조너선에게 도움이 될 수 있을 테고, 속기를 충분히 잘하게 되면 그가 하고 싶은 말을 이런 식으로 받아 적었다가 타자기로 옮겨 적어줄 수 있을 거야. 타자기도 아주 열심히 연습하고 있단다."], ["He and I sometimes write letters in shorthand, and he is keeping a stenographic journal of his travels abroad.", "그와 나는 가끔 속기로 편지를 주고받는데, 그는 외국 여행기를 속기로 일기에 적고 있어."], ["When I am with you I shall keep a diary in the same way.", "너와 함께 있게 되면 나도 그런 식으로 일기를 쓰려고 해."], ["I don’t mean one of those two-pages-to-the-week-with-Sunday-squeezed-in-a-corner diaries, but a sort of journal which I can write in whenever I feel inclined.", "일요일을 한구석에 욱여넣은, 한 주에 두 쪽짜리 그런 일기 말고, 마음이 내킬 때면 언제든 쓸 수 있는 그런 일지 말이야."], ["I do not suppose there will be much of interest to other people; but it is not intended for them.", "다른 사람들에게 흥미로울 만한 것은 별로 없을 거라고 생각하지만, 어차피 그들을 위한 것이 아니니까."], ["I may show it to Jonathan some day if there is in it anything worth sharing, but it is really an exercise book.", "함께 나눌 만한 무언가가 그 안에 있다면 언젠가 조너선에게 보여줄지도 모르지만, 사실은 연습장 같은 거야."], ["I shall try to do what I see lady journalists do: interviewing and writing descriptions and trying to remember conversations.", "나는 여기자들이 하는 것을 따라 해볼 생각이야. 인터뷰를 하고, 묘사를 적고, 나눈 대화를 기억해내려 애쓰는 거지."], ["I am told that, with a little practice, one can remember all that goes on or that one hears said during a day.", "조금만 연습하면 하루 동안 일어난 일이나 들은 말을 모두 기억할 수 있다고 하더라."], ["However, we shall see.", "어쨌든 두고 보면 알겠지."], ["I will tell you of my little plans when we meet.", "우리가 만나면 내 자그마한 계획들을 들려줄게."], ["I have just had a few hurried lines from Jonathan from Transylvania.", "방금 조너선에게서 트란실바니아발 짤막한 몇 줄을 급히 받았어."], ["He is well, and will be returning in about a week.", "그는 잘 지내고 있고, 일주일쯤 뒤면 돌아온대."], ["I am longing to hear all his news.", "그의 소식을 모두 듣고 싶어 견딜 수가 없어."], ["It must be so nice to see strange countries.", "낯선 나라들을 보는 건 참 멋진 일이겠지."], ["I wonder if we--I mean Jonathan and I--shall ever see them together.", "우리가—그러니까 조너선과 내가—언젠가 함께 그곳들을 보게 될까."], ["There is the ten o’clock bell ringing. Good-bye.", "마침 열 시를 알리는 종이 울리네. 잘 있어."]], [["“Your loving", "「너를 사랑하는\n「미나."]], [["“Tell me all the news when you write.", "「편지 쓸 때 모든 소식을 다 들려줘."], ["You have not told me anything for a long time.", "오랫동안 아무것도 말해주지 않았잖아."], ["I hear rumours, and especially of a tall, handsome, curly-haired man???”", "소문이 들리는데, 특히 키 크고 잘생긴 곱슬머리 남자에 관한 소문이…???」 「루시 웨스텐라가 미나 머리에게 보내는 편지.」"]], [["“17, Chatham Street,", "「채텀 가 17번지."]], [["“Wednesday.", "「수요일."]], [["“My dearest Mina,--", "「나의 가장 사랑하는 미나에게.—"]], [["“I must say you tax me very unfairly with being a bad correspondent.", "「네가 나더러 편지를 잘 안 쓴다고 몹시도 부당하게 몰아세운다는 말을 꼭 해야겠어."], ["I wrote to you twice since we parted, and your last letter was only your second.", "우리가 헤어진 뒤로 나는 너에게 두 번이나 편지를 썼는데, 네가 마지막으로 보낸 건 겨우 두 번째 편지였잖아."], ["Besides, I have nothing to tell you.", "게다가 나는 들려줄 이야기가 없어."], ["There is really nothing to interest you.", "정말이지 너에게 흥미로울 만한 게 하나도 없단다."], ["Town is very pleasant just now, and we go a good deal to picture-galleries and for walks and rides in the park.", "요즘 도회지는 무척 즐겁고, 우리는 그림 전시관에도 자주 가고 공원으로 산책이며 승마도 많이 다녀."], ["As to the tall, curly-haired man, I suppose it was the one who was with me at the last Pop.", "그 키 크고 곱슬머리 남자로 말하자면, 지난번 팝 음악회 때 나와 함께 있던 사람일 거야."], ["Some one has evidently been telling tales. That was Mr.", "누군가가 분명 소문을 퍼뜨렸나 봐. 그건 홈우드 씨였어."], ["Holmwood. He often comes to see us, and he and mamma get on very well together; they have so many things to talk about in common.", "그는 우리를 보러 자주 오는데, 그와 엄마는 아주 사이가 좋아. 함께 이야기 나눌 거리가 무척 많거든."], ["We met some time ago a man that would just do for you, if you were not already engaged to Jonathan.", "얼마 전에 우리는 한 남자를 만났는데, 네가 이미 조너선과 약혼한 사이만 아니었다면 꼭 너에게 어울릴 사람이야."], ["He is an excellent parti, being handsome, well off, and of good birth.", "그는 더없이 훌륭한 신랑감이거든. 잘생기고, 형편도 넉넉하고, 가문도 좋아."], ["He is a doctor and really clever.", "의사인데 정말로 영리하단다."], ["Just fancy!", "글쎄 상상해봐!"], ["He is only nine-and-twenty, and he has an immense lunatic asylum all under his own care. Mr.", "그는 겨우 스물아홉인데, 거대한 정신병원을 혼자서 도맡아 관리하고 있어."], ["Holmwood introduced him to me, and he called here to see us, and often comes now.", "홈우드 씨가 그를 나에게 소개해주었는데, 그가 우리를 보러 여기까지 찾아왔고, 이제는 자주 온단다."], ["I think he is one of the most resolute men I ever saw, and yet the most calm.", "내가 본 사람 가운데 가장 결단력 있으면서도 가장 침착한 사람인 것 같아."], ["He seems absolutely imperturbable.", "그는 도무지 흔들리는 법이 없어 보여."], ["I can fancy what a wonderful power he must have over his patients.", "그가 환자들에게 얼마나 놀라운 힘을 발휘할지 상상이 가."], ["He has a curious habit of looking one straight in the face, as if trying to read one’s thoughts.", "그에게는 사람의 얼굴을 똑바로 들여다보는 묘한 버릇이 있는데, 마치 그 속내를 읽어내려는 것 같아."], ["He tries this on very much with me, but I flatter myself he has got a tough nut to crack.", "나에게도 이걸 자주 시도하는데, 내심 나는 그가 만만찮은 상대를 만났다고 자부하고 있어."], ["I know that from my glass.", "그건 내 거울을 보면 알지."], ["Do you ever try to read your own face?", "너는 네 얼굴을 읽어보려 한 적이 있니?"], ["I do, and I can tell you it is not a bad study, and gives you more trouble than you can well fancy if you have never tried it.", "나는 해봐. 분명히 말하건대 그건 꽤 괜찮은 공부거리이고, 한 번도 해보지 않았다면 짐작도 못할 만큼 너를 애먹인단다."], ["He says that I afford him a curious psychological study, and I humbly think I do.", "그는 내가 자기에게 흥미로운 심리학적 연구 대상이라고 하는데, 변변치 못하나마 나도 그런 것 같아."], ["I do not, as you know, take sufficient interest in dress to be able to describe the new fashions.", "너도 알다시피 나는 새로운 유행을 묘사할 만큼 옷차림에 충분히 관심이 없어."], ["Dress is a bore.", "옷이란 따분한 것이지."], ["That is slang again, but never mind; Arthur says that every day.", "또 속어를 썼네, 그래도 상관없어. 아서는 매일 그 말을 하니까."], ["There, it is all out.", "자, 다 털어놓았네."], ["Mina, we have told all our secrets to each other since we were children; we have slept together and eaten together, and laughed and cried together; and now, though I have spoken, I would like to speak more.", "미나, 우리는 어린아이였던 시절부터 서로에게 모든 비밀을 다 말해왔지. 함께 자고 함께 먹고, 함께 웃고 함께 울었잖아. 그런데 이렇게 말을 꺼내고 보니 더 말하고 싶어."], ["Oh, Mina, couldn’t you guess?", "아, 미나, 너는 짐작 못하겠니?"], ["I love him. I am blushing as I write, for although I think he loves me, he has not told me so in words.", "나는 그를 사랑해. 이 글을 쓰면서도 얼굴이 붉어지는데, 그도 나를 사랑한다고 생각은 하지만 그가 말로 그렇게 표현한 적은 없거든."], ["But oh, Mina, I love him; I love him; I love him!", "하지만 아, 미나, 나는 그를 사랑해."], ["There, that does me good.", "그를 사랑해. 그를 사랑해!"], ["I wish I were with you, dear, sitting by the fire undressing, as we used to sit; and I would try to tell you what I feel.", "자, 이렇게 말하니 마음이 한결 가볍네. 네가 곁에 있다면 좋을 텐데. 예전에 그러던 것처럼 난롯가에 앉아 옷을 벗으며, 내가 느끼는 것을 너에게 들려주려 애써볼 텐데."], ["I do not know how I am writing this even to you.", "너에게조차 이걸 어떻게 쓰고 있는지 나도 모르겠어."], ["I am afraid to stop, or I should tear up the letter, and I don’t want to stop, for I do so want to tell you all.", "멈추기가 두려워. 멈추면 이 편지를 찢어버릴 것 같거든. 그리고 멈추고 싶지도 않아, 너에게 모든 걸 정말이지 너무도 말하고 싶으니까."], ["Let me hear from you at once, and tell me all that you think about it.", "당장 답장 줘. 그리고 이 일을 어떻게 생각하는지 다 말해줘."], ["Mina, I must stop. Good-night.", "미나, 이제 그만 써야겠어. 잘 자."], ["Bless me in your prayers; and, Mina, pray for my happiness.", "기도할 때 나를 축복해줘. 그리고 미나, 내 행복을 위해 기도해줘. 「루시."]], [["“P.S.--I need not tell you this is a secret.", "「추신.—이건 비밀이라는 말은 굳이 안 해도 알겠지."], ["Good-night again.", "다시 한번 잘 자."]], [["“L.”", "「엘.」"]], [["“24 May. “My dearest Mina,--", "「루시 웨스텐라가 미나 머리에게 보내는 편지.」"]], [["“Thanks, and thanks, and thanks again for your sweet letter. It was so nice to be able to tell you and to have your sympathy.", "「5월 24일. 「나의 가장 사랑하는 미나에게.—"]], [["“My dear, it never rains but it pours. How true the old proverbs are.", "「고마워, 고마워, 다시 한번 고마워, 그 다정한 편지를 보내줘서. 너에게 털어놓을 수 있고 또 네 위로를 받을 수 있어서 정말 좋았어."], ["Here am I, who shall be twenty in September, and yet I never had a proposal till to-day, not a real proposal, and to-day I have had three.", "「얘, 비가 왔다 하면 퍼붓는다더니. 옛 속담이 어찌나 들어맞는지. 나는 9월이면 스무 살이 되는데도 오늘까지 한 번도 청혼을 받아본 적이 없었어, 진짜 청혼 말이야."], ["Just fancy! THREE proposals in one day!", "그런데 오늘 세 번이나 받았단다."], ["Isn’t it awful!", "글쎄 상상해봐!"], ["I feel sorry, really and truly sorry, for two of the poor fellows.", "하루에 청혼을 세 번이라니! 끔찍하지 않니!"], ["Oh, Mina, I am so happy that I don’t know what to do with myself. And three proposals!", "그 가엾은 두 사람에게는 정말로, 진정으로 미안한 마음이야. 아, 미나, 나는 너무 행복해서 어쩔 줄을 모르겠어."], ["But, for goodness’ sake, don’t tell any of the girls, or they would be getting all sorts of extravagant ideas and imagining themselves injured and slighted if in their very first day at home they did not get six at least.", "게다가 청혼을 세 번이라니! 하지만 제발, 다른 여자애들한테는 한마디도 하지 마. 안 그러면 별별 터무니없는 생각을 다 하면서, 집에 돌아온 첫날에 적어도 여섯 번은 받지 못하면 자기가 상처받고 무시당했다고 여길 테니까."], ["Some girls are so vain! You and I, Mina dear, who are engaged and are going to settle down soon soberly into old married women, can despise vanity.", "어떤 여자애들은 어찌나 허영심이 많은지! 약혼해서 곧 차분한 노부인네로 자리잡을 너와 나, 사랑하는 미나야, 우리는 허영 따위는 경멸할 수 있지."], ["Well, I must tell you about the three, but you must keep it a secret, dear, from every one, except, of course, Jonathan.", "자, 그 세 사람 이야기를 들려줘야겠는데, 너는 이걸 비밀로 지켜야 해, 얘, 모든 사람에게, 물론 조너선만 빼고 말이야."], ["You will tell him, because I would, if I were in your place, certainly tell Arthur.", "너는 그에게 말하겠지. 내가 네 처지라면 분명 아서에게 말할 테니까."], ["A woman ought to tell her husband everything--don’t you think so, dear?--and I must be fair.", "여자는 남편에게 모든 걸 말해야 하잖아—그렇게 생각하지 않니, 얘?—나도 공정해야겠지."], ["Men like women, certainly their wives, to be quite as fair as they are; and women, I am afraid, are not always quite as fair as they should be.", "남자들은 여자가, 분명 자기 아내가, 자기들만큼 떳떳하기를 바라는데, 여자들은 안타깝게도 늘 마땅한 만큼 떳떳하지는 못한 것 같아."], ["Well, my dear, number One came just before lunch.", "자, 얘, 첫 번째 사람은 점심 직전에 왔어."], ["I told you of him, Dr. John Seward, the lunatic-asylum man, with the strong jaw and the good forehead.", "내가 너에게 말했던 그 사람, 정신병원을 맡고 있는 존 수어드 박사야. 억센 턱과 멋진 이마를 가진."], ["He was very cool outwardly, but was nervous all the same.", "그는 겉으로는 무척 침착했지만 그래도 긴장하고 있었어."], ["He had evidently been schooling himself as to all sorts of little things, and remembered them; but he almost managed to sit down on his silk hat, which men don’t generally do when they are cool, and then when he wanted to appear at ease he kept playing with a lancet in a way that made me nearly scream.", "그는 분명 온갖 자잘한 것들을 미리 연습해두고 잊지 않고 있었지. 그런데 하마터면 자기 실크해트를 깔고 앉을 뻔했는데, 침착한 남자라면 보통 그러지 않는 법이거든. 그러고는 편안해 보이려 할 때 외과용 칼을 만지작거리는 통에 나는 하마터면 비명을 지를 뻔했어."], ["He spoke to me, Mina, very straightforwardly.", "그는 나에게, 미나, 아주 솔직하게 말했어."], ["He told me how dear I was to him, though he had known me so little, and what his life would be with me to help and cheer him.", "비록 나를 안 지는 얼마 되지 않았지만 내가 자기에게 얼마나 소중한지, 내가 곁에서 돕고 격려해준다면 자기 삶이 어떠할지를 들려주었지."], ["He was going to tell me how unhappy he would be if I did not care for him, but when he saw me cry he said that he was a brute and would not add to my present trouble.", "내가 자기를 마음에 두지 않으면 자기가 얼마나 불행할지를 말하려 했는데, 내가 우는 걸 보더니 자기는 짐승만도 못한 사람이라며 지금 내 괴로움에 더 보태지 않겠다고 하더라."], ["Then he broke off and asked if I could love him in time; and when I shook my head his hands trembled, and then with some hesitation he asked me if I cared already for any one else.", "그러고는 말을 멈추고, 언젠가 자기를 사랑할 수 있겠느냐고 물었어. 내가 고개를 젓자 그의 두 손이 떨렸고, 그러더니 다소 머뭇거리며 이미 다른 누군가를 마음에 두고 있느냐고 묻더라."], ["He put it very nicely, saying that he did not want to wring my confidence from me, but only to know, because if a woman’s heart was free a man might have hope.", "그는 그걸 아주 점잖게 표현했어. 내 속내를 억지로 짜내려는 게 아니라 그저 알고 싶을 뿐이라며, 여자의 마음이 자유롭다면 남자도 희망을 품을 수 있으니까, 라고 말이야."], ["And then, Mina, I felt a sort of duty to tell him that there was some one.", "그래서, 미나, 나는 그에게 어떤 사람이 있다고 말해야 한다는 일종의 의무감을 느꼈어."], ["I only told him that much, and then he stood up, and he looked very strong and very grave as he took both my hands in his and said he hoped I would be happy, and that if I ever wanted a friend I must count him one of my best.", "딱 그만큼만 말했더니, 그는 일어서서 내 두 손을 자기 두 손에 감싸쥐고는, 무척 단단하고 무척 엄숙한 얼굴로, 내가 행복하기를 바란다고, 그리고 내가 언젠가 벗이 필요하거든 자기를 가장 좋은 벗 가운데 하나로 여겨달라고 말했어."], ["Oh, Mina dear, I can’t help crying: and you must excuse this letter being all blotted.", "아, 사랑하는 미나, 나는 울지 않을 수가 없어. 이 편지가 온통 얼룩진 걸 부디 양해해줘."], ["Being proposed to is all very nice and all that sort of thing, but it isn’t at all a happy thing when you have to see a poor fellow, whom you know loves you honestly, going away and looking all broken-hearted, and to know that, no matter what he may say at the moment, you are passing quite out of his life.", "청혼을 받는 건 다 좋고 그런 멋진 일이지만, 너를 진심으로 사랑하는 줄 아는 가엾은 사람이 가슴이 온통 찢어진 얼굴로 떠나가는 것을 지켜보아야 하고, 또 그가 그 순간에 무슨 말을 하든 너는 그의 삶에서 완전히 사라져가고 있다는 걸 알아야 하는 건 결코 행복한 일이 아니야."], ["My dear, I must stop here at present, I feel so miserable, though I am so happy.", "얘, 지금은 여기서 그만 써야겠어. 이렇게 행복한데도 너무 비참한 기분이 들어."]], [["“Evening.", "「저녁."]], [["“Arthur has just gone, and I feel in better spirits than when I left off, so I can go on telling you about the day.", "「아서가 방금 떠났는데, 글을 멈추었을 때보다 기분이 한결 나아져서 그날 이야기를 계속 들려줄 수 있겠어."], ["Well, my dear, number Two came after lunch.", "자, 얘, 두 번째 사람은 점심 뒤에 왔어."], ["He is such a nice fellow, an American from Texas, and he looks so young and so fresh that it seems almost impossible that he has been to so many places and has had such adventures.", "그는 정말 멋진 사람인데, 텍사스에서 온 미국인이야. 어찌나 젊고 싱싱해 보이는지, 그가 그토록 많은 곳을 다니고 그토록 많은 모험을 했다는 게 거의 믿기지 않을 정도야."], ["I sympathise with poor Desdemona when she had such a dangerous stream poured in her ear, even by a black man.", "나는 가엾은 데스데모나가, 비록 흑인의 입에서 나온 것일망정, 그토록 위태로운 이야기들을 귓속에 쏟아부음받았을 때의 심정에 공감이 가."], ["I suppose that we women are such cowards that we think a man will save us from fears, and we marry him.", "우리 여자들은 어찌나 겁이 많은지, 남자가 우리를 두려움에서 구해주리라 여기고 그와 결혼하는 것 같아."], ["I know now what I would do if I were a man and wanted to make a girl love me.", "내가 만약 남자여서 한 여자가 나를 사랑하게 만들고 싶다면 어떻게 할지 이제 알겠어."], ["No, I don’t, for there was Mr. Morris telling us his stories, and Arthur never told any, and yet---- My dear, I am somewhat previous. Mr.", "아니, 모르겠어. 모리스 씨가 우리에게 자기 이야기들을 들려준 데 반해, 아서는 단 하나도 들려준 적이 없는데도 그런데도—얘, 내가 좀 앞질러 갔네."], ["Quincey P. Morris found me alone.", "퀸시 피 모리스 씨는 나를 혼자 있는 데서 발견했어."], ["It seems that a man always does find a girl alone.", "남자란 늘 여자가 혼자 있을 때를 잘도 찾아내는 것 같아."], ["No, he doesn’t, for Arthur tried twice to make a chance, and I helping him all I could; I am not ashamed to say it now.", "아니, 그렇지 않아. 아서는 두 번이나 기회를 만들려고 애썼고, 나도 할 수 있는 한 그를 도왔으니까."], ["I must tell you beforehand that Mr.", "이제는 그 말을 하기가 부끄럽지 않아."], ["Morris doesn’t always speak slang--that is to say, he never does so to strangers or before them, for he is really well educated and has exquisite manners--but he found out that it amused me to hear him talk American slang, and whenever I was present, and there was no one to be shocked, he said such funny things.", "미리 말해두어야겠는데, 모리스 씨가 늘 속어를 쓰는 건 아니야—그러니까 낯선 사람들에게나 그들 앞에서는 결코 그러지 않아. 사실 교육을 잘 받았고 예의범절도 더없이 훌륭한 사람이거든—그런데 내가 자기의 미국식 속어를 듣는 걸 재미있어한다는 걸 알아채고는, 내가 곁에 있고 또 놀랄 사람이 아무도 없을 때면 그렇게 우스운 말들을 하더라."], ["I am afraid, my dear, he has to invent it all, for it fits exactly into whatever else he has to say.", "얘, 안타깝지만 그는 그걸 죄다 지어내는 게 분명해. 무슨 말을 하든 거기에 정확히 들어맞으니 말이야."], ["But this is a way slang has.", "하지만 속어란 원래 그런 거지."], ["I do not know myself if I shall ever speak slang; I do not know if Arthur likes it, as I have never heard him use any as yet. Well, Mr.", "나 자신도 언젠가 속어를 쓰게 될지 모르겠어. 아서가 그걸 좋아하는지도 모르겠고, 그가 아직껏 속어를 쓰는 걸 들어본 적이 없으니까."], ["Morris sat down beside me and looked as happy and jolly as he could, but I could see all the same that he was very nervous.", "자, 모리스 씨가 내 곁에 앉아 한껏 행복하고 유쾌해 보이려 애썼지만, 그래도 나는 그가 무척 긴장한 걸 알아볼 수 있었어."], ["He took my hand in his, and said ever so sweetly:--", "그는 내 손을 자기 손에 쥐고 더없이 다정하게 말했어.—"]], [["“‘Miss Lucy, I know I ain’t good enough to regulate the fixin’s of your little shoes, but I guess if you wait till you find a man that is you will go join them seven young women with the lamps when you quit.", "「'루시 양, 나는 당신의 그 자그마한 신발의 끈 매무새를 다듬어드릴 자격조차 없는 사람인 줄 압니다. 하지만 그럴 자격이 있는 남자를 찾을 때까지 기다리신다면, 당신은 결국 등불을 든 그 일곱 처녀들 곁으로 가게 되고 말 겁니다."], ["Won’t you just hitch up alongside of me and let us go down the long road together, driving in double harness?’", "그러지 마시고 그냥 나와 짝을 지어, 두 마리가 한 멍에를 메고 마차를 몰듯, 함께 그 긴 길을 가지 않으시겠습니까?'"]], [["“Well, he did look so good-humoured and so jolly that it didn’t seem half so hard to refuse him as it did poor Dr.", "「자, 그가 어찌나 사람 좋고 유쾌해 보였던지, 가엾은 수어드 박사를 거절할 때만큼 거절하기가 어렵게 느껴지지는 않았어."], ["Seward; so I said, as lightly as I could, that I did not know anything of hitching, and that I wasn’t broken to harness at all yet.", "그래서 나는 되도록 가볍게, 나는 멍에를 메는 것에 대해선 아무것도 모르고, 아직 마구에 길들지도 전혀 않았다고 말했어."], ["Then he said that he had spoken in a light manner, and he hoped that if he had made a mistake in doing so on so grave, so momentous, an occasion for him, I would forgive him.", "그러자 그는 자기가 가벼운 투로 말했다고, 자기로서는 이토록 엄숙하고 이토록 중대한 자리에서 그런 잘못을 저질렀다면 부디 용서해달라고 했어."], ["He really did look serious when he was saying it, and I couldn’t help feeling a bit serious too--I know, Mina, you will think me a horrid flirt--though I couldn’t help feeling a sort of exultation that he was number two in one day.", "그 말을 할 때 그는 정말로 진지해 보였고, 나도 덩달아 조금 진지해지지 않을 수 없었어—미나, 나를 끔찍한 바람둥이로 여기겠지—그래도 그가 하루에 두 번째 청혼자라는 데에 일종의 우쭐함을 느끼지 않을 수 없었지."], ["And then, my dear, before I could say a word he began pouring out a perfect torrent of love-making, laying his very heart and soul at my feet.", "그러더니, 얘, 내가 한마디 꺼내기도 전에 그는 완벽한 사랑 고백의 격류를 쏟아내기 시작했어. 자기 심장과 영혼을 통째로 내 발치에 내려놓듯이 말이야."], ["He looked so earnest over it that I shall never again think that a man must be playful always, and never earnest, because he is merry at times.", "그가 그것을 너무도 진지하게 하는 통에, 나는 다시는 남자가 늘 장난스러워야 하고, 가끔 유쾌하다고 해서 결코 진지할 수 없다고는 여기지 않게 될 거야."], ["I suppose he saw something in my face which checked him, for he suddenly stopped, and said with a sort of manly fervour that I could have loved him for if I had been free:--", "내 얼굴에서 무언가를 본 모양으로 그는 갑자기 멈추더니, 내가 자유로운 몸이었다면 그것만으로도 사랑할 수 있었을 그런 사내다운 열정으로 말했어.—"]], [["“‘Lucy, you are an honest-hearted girl, I know.", "「'루시, 당신은 마음이 정직한 분이에요, 나는 알아요."], ["I should not be here speaking to you as I am now if I did not believe you clean grit, right through to the very depths of your soul.", "당신의 영혼 가장 깊은 곳까지 그야말로 진국이라고 믿지 않았다면, 나는 지금처럼 이렇게 당신에게 말하고 있지 않을 겁니다."], ["Tell me, like one good fellow to another, is there any one else that you care for?", "한 좋은 벗이 다른 벗에게 묻듯이 말해주세요. 당신이 마음에 둔 다른 누군가가 있습니까?"], ["And if there is I’ll never trouble you a hair’s breadth again, but will be, if you will let me, a very faithful friend.’", "만약 있다면 나는 다시는 당신을 터럭만큼도 괴롭히지 않고, 당신이 허락한다면, 더없이 충실한 벗이 되겠습니다.'"]], [["“My dear Mina, why are men so noble when we women are so little worthy of them?", "「사랑하는 미나야, 우리 여자들은 그들에게 그토록 어울리지 않는데 남자들은 어찌 이리 고결할까?"], ["Here was I almost making fun of this great-hearted, true gentleman.", "나는 이 큰 마음을 지닌 참된 신사를 거의 놀리다시피 하고 있었던 거야."], ["I burst into tears--I am afraid, my dear, you will think this a very sloppy letter in more ways than one--and I really felt very badly.", "나는 울음을 터뜨렸어—얘, 이 편지가 여러모로 정말 칠칠치 못한 편지라고 여기겠지—나는 정말로 마음이 몹시 안 좋았어."], ["Why can’t they let a girl marry three men, or as many as want her, and save all this trouble?", "어째서 한 여자가 세 남자, 아니 자기를 원하는 만큼 많은 남자와 결혼해서 이 모든 괴로움을 덜 수는 없는 걸까?"], ["But this is heresy, and I must not say it.", "하지만 이건 이단 같은 소리니 입에 담아선 안 되겠지."], ["I am glad to say that, though I was crying, I was able to look into Mr. Morris’s brave eyes, and I told him out straight:--", "다행히도 나는 울면서도 모리스 씨의 용감한 눈을 바라볼 수 있었고, 그에게 솔직하게 말했어.—"]], [["“‘Yes, there is some one I love, though he has not told me yet that he even loves me.’ I was right to speak to him so frankly, for quite a light came into his face, and he put out both his hands and took mine--I think I put them into his--and said in a hearty way:--", "「'네, 내가 사랑하는 사람이 있어요. 비록 그는 아직 나를 사랑한다는 말조차 해준 적이 없지만요.' 그렇게 솔직히 말하기를 잘했어. 그의 얼굴에 환한 빛이 어리더니, 그가 두 손을 내밀어 내 손을 잡았거든—내가 그의 손에 내 손을 맡긴 것 같기도 해—그러고는 진심 어린 투로 말했어.—"]], [["“‘That’s my brave girl.", "「'역시 내 용감한 아가씨로군요."], ["It’s better worth being late for a chance of winning you than being in time for any other girl in the world.", "당신을 얻을 기회를 위해 늦는 편이, 세상 어떤 다른 여자에게 제때 닿는 것보다 더 값진 일이지요."], ["Don’t cry, my dear.", "울지 마세요, 그대여."], ["If it’s for me, I’m a hard nut to crack; and I take it standing up.", "나 때문이라면, 나는 만만찮은 사내랍니다. 나는 이걸 똑바로 서서 받아들이겠어요."], ["If that other fellow doesn’t know his happiness, well, he’d better look for it soon, or he’ll have to deal with me.", "만약 그 다른 친구가 자기 행복을 모른다면, 글쎄, 어서 그걸 찾아보는 게 좋을 거예요. 안 그러면 나를 상대해야 할 테니까. 아가씨, 당신의 정직함과 용기가 나를 벗으로 만들었어요."], ["Little girl, your honesty and pluck have made me a friend, and that’s rarer than a lover; it’s more unselfish anyhow.", "그건 연인보다 더 드문 것이고, 어쨌든 더 욕심 없는 것이지요. 그대여, 나는 이제부터 저세상에 갈 때까지 꽤나 외로운 길을 걷게 되겠지요."], ["My dear, I’m going to have a pretty lonely walk between this and Kingdom Come.", "입맞춤 한 번만 해주지 않겠어요? 이따금 어둠을 물리치는 데 도움이 될 무언가가 될 테니까요."], ["Won’t you give me one kiss?", "당신은 그래도 돼요, 알다시피, 원한다면요."], ["It’ll be something to keep off the darkness now and then.", "그 다른 좋은 친구는—그는 좋은 사람일 거예요, 그대여, 그것도 훌륭한 사람일 거예요."], ["You can, you know, if you like, for that other good fellow--he must be a good fellow, my dear, and a fine fellow, or you could not love him--hasn’t spoken yet.’ That quite won me, Mina, for it was brave and sweet of him, and noble, too, to a rival--wasn’t it?--and he so sad; so I leant over and kissed him.", "안 그러면 당신이 그를 사랑할 리 없으니까요—아직 말을 꺼내지도 않았으니까요.' 그 말에 나는 완전히 마음을 빼앗기고 말았어, 미나. 그건 정말이지 용감하고 다정한 일이었고, 게다가 연적에게마저 고결한 일이었거든—그렇지 않니?—게다가 그는 그토록 슬퍼하고 있었지."], ["He stood up with my two hands in his, and as he looked down into my face--I am afraid I was blushing very much--he said:--", "그래서 나는 몸을 기울여 그에게 입을 맞추었어. 그는 내 두 손을 자기 손에 쥐고 일어서서, 내 얼굴을 내려다보며—나는 무척 얼굴을 붉히고 있었던 것 같아—이렇게 말했어.—"]], [["“‘Little girl, I hold your hand, and you’ve kissed me, and if these things don’t make us friends nothing ever will.", "「'아가씨, 내가 당신의 손을 잡았고, 당신이 나에게 입을 맞추었으니, 이것들이 우리를 벗으로 만들지 못한다면 그 무엇도 결코 그러지 못할 거예요."], ["Thank you for your sweet honesty to me, and good-bye.’ He wrung my hand, and taking up his hat, went straight out of the room without looking back, without a tear or a quiver or a pause; and I am crying like a baby.", "나에게 베풀어준 당신의 그 다정한 정직함에 감사해요. 그럼 안녕히.' 그는 내 손을 꼭 쥐고는, 모자를 집어 들고, 뒤도 돌아보지 않고, 눈물 한 방울도 떨림도 멈칫거림도 없이 곧장 방을 나갔어."], ["Oh, why must a man like that be made unhappy when there are lots of girls about who would worship the very ground he trod on?", "그리고 나는 어린아이처럼 울고 있어. 아, 그가 밟고 다니는 땅마저 떠받들 여자애들이 사방에 널렸는데, 어째서 그런 남자가 불행해져야 하는 걸까?"], ["I know I would if I were free--only I don’t want to be free.", "내가 자유로운 몸이라면 나도 그럴 텐데—다만 나는 자유로워지고 싶지 않은 거야."], ["My dear, this quite upset me, and I feel I cannot write of happiness just at once, after telling you of it; and I don’t wish to tell of the number three until it can be all happy.", "얘, 이 일로 나는 마음이 온통 헝클어져서, 너에게 행복을 이야기한 직후에 곧바로 또 행복을 쓸 수는 없을 것 같아. 그리고 세 번째 사람 이야기는 모든 게 다 행복해질 수 있을 때까지는 말하고 싶지 않아."]], [["“Ever your loving", "「언제까지나 너를 사랑하는"]], [["“P.S.--Oh, about number Three--I needn’t tell you of number Three, need I?", "「루시. 「추신.—아, 세 번째 사람에 대해서는—굳이 세 번째 사람 이야기를 들려줄 필요는 없겠지?"], ["Besides, it was all so confused; it seemed only a moment from his coming into the room till both his arms were round me, and he was kissing me.", "게다가 모든 게 너무 정신없었어. 그가 방에 들어온 순간부터 그의 두 팔이 나를 감싸안고 그가 나에게 입을 맞추기까지가 그저 한순간 같았으니까."], ["I am very, very happy, and I don’t know what I have done to deserve it.", "나는 정말, 정말 행복해. 내가 무슨 일을 했기에 이런 자격이 생겼는지 모르겠어."], ["I must only try in the future to show that I am not ungrateful to God for all His goodness to me in sending to me such a lover, such a husband, and such a friend.", "다만 앞으로는 나에게 이런 연인을, 이런 남편을, 이런 벗을 보내주신 하느님의 그 모든 은총에 내가 감사할 줄 모르는 사람이 아님을 보여드리려 애쓸 수밖에."]], [["“Good-bye.”", "「그럼 안녕.」"]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기.」"]], [["(Kept in phonograph)", "(축음기로 기록함)"]], [["25 May.--Ebb tide in appetite to-day.", "5월 25일.—오늘은 식욕이 썰물처럼 빠져나갔다."], ["Cannot eat, cannot rest, so diary instead.", "먹을 수도, 쉴 수도 없으니, 대신 일기를 쓴다."], ["Since my rebuff of yesterday I have a sort of empty feeling; nothing in the world seems of sufficient importance to be worth the doing....", "어제 거절당한 뒤로 일종의 공허한 느낌이 든다. 세상 그 무엇도 할 만한 가치가 있을 만큼 충분히 중요해 보이지 않는다.……"], ["As I knew that the only cure for this sort of thing was work, I went down amongst the patients.", "이런 종류의 일에 유일한 치료법이 일이라는 것을 알기에, 나는 환자들에게로 내려갔다."], ["I picked out one who has afforded me a study of much interest.", "나는 무척 흥미로운 연구거리가 되어준 한 사람을 골라냈다."], ["He is so quaint that I am determined to understand him as well as I can.", "그가 어찌나 기묘한지, 나는 그를 되도록 잘 이해해보기로 마음먹었다."], ["To-day I seemed to get nearer than ever before to the heart of his mystery.", "오늘은 그 어느 때보다 그의 수수께끼의 핵심에 가까이 다가간 것 같았다."]], [["I questioned him more fully than I had ever done, with a view to making myself master of the facts of his hallucination.", "나는 그의 환각에 관한 사실들을 파악하기 위해, 그 어느 때보다 더 충분히 그에게 질문했다."], ["In my manner of doing it there was, I now see, something of cruelty.", "지금 와서 보니, 그렇게 하는 나의 태도에는 어딘가 잔인한 구석이 있었다."], ["I seemed to wish to keep him to the point of his madness--a thing which I avoid with the patients as I would the mouth of hell.", "나는 그를 그의 광기의 요점에 붙들어두려는 것 같았는데—이는 내가 환자들을 대할 때 지옥의 구렁을 피하듯 피하려 하는 일이었다."]], [["(Mem., under what circumstances would I not avoid the pit of hell?) Omnia Romæ venalia sunt.", "(비망록. 어떤 상황에서라면 나는 지옥의 구렁을 피하지 않으려 할까?) 옴니아 로마이 베날리아 순트. 지옥에도 값이 있다!"], ["Hell has its price! verb. sap.", "현명한 자에게는 한마디면 족하다."], ["If there be anything behind this instinct it will be valuable to trace it afterwards accurately, so I had better commence to do so, therefore--", "만일 이 본능 뒤에 무언가가 있다면 나중에 그것을 정확히 추적해 보는 일이 가치 있으리라. 그러니 지금부터 그렇게 시작하는 편이 낫겠다. 따라서--"]], [["R. M. Renfield, ætat 59.--Sanguine temperament; great physical strength; morbidly excitable; periods of gloom, ending in some fixed idea which I cannot make out.", "알 엠 렌필드, 59세.—다혈질의 기질, 대단한 체력, 병적인 흥분성, 침울한 시기를 거쳐 내가 종잡을 수 없는 어떤 고정관념으로 끝맺음."], ["I presume that the sanguine temperament itself and the disturbing influence end in a mentally-accomplished finish; a possibly dangerous man, probably dangerous if unselfish.", "짐작건대 이 다혈질의 기질 자체와 교란하는 영향이 정신적으로 완성된 결말로 이어지는 듯하다. 위험할 수 있는 인물, 사심이 없다면 아마 위험한 인물."], ["In selfish men caution is as secure an armour for their foes as for themselves.", "사심이 있는 자들의 경우, 신중함은 그들 자신에게만큼이나 적들에게도 든든한 갑옷이 된다."], ["What I think of on this point is, when self is the fixed point the centripetal force is balanced with the centrifugal; when duty, a cause, etc., is the fixed point, the latter force is paramount, and only accident or a series of accidents can balance it.", "이 점에 관해 내가 생각하는 바는 이렇다. 자아가 그 고정점일 때는 구심력과 원심력이 균형을 이루지만, 의무나 어떤 대의 따위가 고정점일 때는 후자의 힘이 압도적이어서, 오직 우연이나 일련의 우연들만이 그것을 상쇄할 수 있다. 「퀸시 피 모리스가 아서 홈우드 경에게 보내는 편지.」"]], [["“25 May.", "「5월 25일."]], [["“My dear Art,--", "「친애하는 아트에게.—"]], [["“We’ve told yarns by the camp-fire in the prairies; and dressed one another’s wounds after trying a landing at the Marquesas; and drunk healths on the shore of Titicaca.", "「우리는 대초원의 모닥불 가에서 이야기를 나누었고, 마르키즈 제도에 상륙을 시도하다 서로의 상처를 싸매주었으며, 티티카카 호숫가에서 건배를 들었지."], ["There are more yarns to be told, and other wounds to be healed, and another health to be drunk.", "아직 나눌 이야기가 더 있고, 치료할 상처가 더 있고, 들 건배가 또 하나 남았네."], ["Won’t you let this be at my camp-fire to-morrow night?", "내일 밤 내 모닥불 가에서 그렇게 하지 않으려나?"], ["I have no hesitation in asking you, as I know a certain lady is engaged to a certain dinner-party, and that you are free.", "자네에게 청하는 데 망설임이 없는 것은, 어떤 숙녀분께서 어떤 만찬 약속이 있고 그래서 자네가 자유로운 몸이라는 걸 알기 때문이지."], ["There will only be one other, our old pal at the Korea, Jack Seward.", "다른 한 사람이 더 올 텐데, 코리아 호 시절의 옛 동료인 잭 수어드라네."], ["He’s coming, too, and we both want to mingle our weeps over the wine-cup, and to drink a health with all our hearts to the happiest man in all the wide world, who has won the noblest heart that God has made and the best worth winning.", "그도 올 거야. 우리 둘 다 술잔을 사이에 두고 함께 눈물을 섞고 싶고, 또 온 마음을 다해, 하느님이 빚으신 가장 고결하고 가장 얻을 가치 있는 마음을 얻어낸, 이 드넓은 세상에서 가장 행복한 남자를 위해 건배를 들고 싶으니까."], ["We promise you a hearty welcome, and a loving greeting, and a health as true as your own right hand.", "우리는 자네에게 진심 어린 환영과 다정한 인사, 그리고 자네 오른손만큼이나 진실된 축배를 약속하네."], ["We shall both swear to leave you at home if you drink too deep to a certain pair of eyes. Come!", "자네가 어떤 한 쌍의 눈동자를 위해 너무 깊이 들이켠다면, 우리 둘 다 자네를 집에 두고 가겠노라 맹세하지. 오게나!"]], [["“Yours, as ever and always,", "「언제까지나 변함없이 자네의 벗,"]], [["Telegram from Arthur Holmwood to Quincey P. Morris.", "「퀸시 피 모리스.」 「아서 홈우드가 퀸시 피 모리스에게 보내는 전보.」"]], [["“26 May.", "「5월 26일."]], [["“Count me in every time.", "「언제든 나를 끼워주게."], ["I bear messages which will make both your ears tingle.", "자네들 두 사람의 귀가 다 얼얼해질 소식을 전하러 가겠네. 「아트.」"]], [["# MINA MURRAY’S JOURNAL", "# MINA MURRAY’S JOURNAL"]], [["24 July.", "「미나 머리의 일기」"], ["Whitby.--Lucy met me at the station, looking sweeter and lovelier than ever, and we drove up to the house at the Crescent in which they have rooms.", "7월 24일. 휘트비. — 루시가 역으로 마중을 나왔는데, 그 어느 때보다도 더 사랑스럽고 어여뻐 보였다. 우리는 크레슨트에 있는, 그들이 방을 얻어 묵고 있는 집으로 마차를 타고 올라갔다."], ["This is a lovely place.", "이곳은 아름다운 곳이다."], ["The little river, the Esk, runs through a deep valley, which broadens out as it comes near the harbour.", "작은 강 에스크가 깊은 골짜기 사이로 흐르는데, 항구에 가까워질수록 골짜기가 넓어진다."], ["A great viaduct runs across, with high piers, through which the view seems somehow further away than it really is.", "거대한 고가교가 높은 교각을 세우고 가로질러 놓여 있는데, 그 너머로 보이는 풍경은 어쩐지 실제보다 더 멀어 보인다."], ["The valley is beautifully green, and it is so steep that when you are on the high land on either side you look right across it, unless you are near enough to see down.", "골짜기는 아름답게 푸르고, 어찌나 가파른지 양편의 높은 땅에 서면 골짜기를 곧장 가로질러 건너편이 보인다. 아래를 들여다볼 만큼 가까이 다가가지 않는 한은 그렇다."], ["The houses of the old town--the side away from us--are all red-roofed, and seem piled up one over the other anyhow, like the pictures we see of Nuremberg.", "우리 쪽 반대편에 있는 옛 시가지의 집들은 모두 붉은 지붕이고, 마치 우리가 그림에서 보는 뉘른베르크처럼 아무렇게나 차곡차곡 포개어 쌓아 올린 듯하다."], ["Right over the town is the ruin of Whitby Abbey, which was sacked by the Danes, and which is the scene of part of “Marmion,” where the girl was built up in the wall.", "시가지 바로 위에는 휘트비 수도원의 폐허가 있는데, 이곳은 덴마크인들에게 약탈당한 곳이며, 「마미언」의 일부 무대가 된 곳으로, 거기에서 한 소녀가 벽 속에 산 채로 갇혔다고 한다."], ["It is a most noble ruin, of immense size, and full of beautiful and romantic bits; there is a legend that a white lady is seen in one of the windows.", "그것은 더없이 장엄한 폐허로, 규모가 어마어마하며 아름답고 낭만적인 정취가 가득하다. 창문 가운데 하나에서 흰옷 입은 여인이 보인다는 전설도 있다."], ["Between it and the town there is another church, the parish one, round which is a big graveyard, all full of tombstones.", "그 폐허와 시가지 사이에는 또 다른 교회, 곧 교구 교회가 있고, 그 둘레에는 비석으로 가득한 큰 묘지가 있다."], ["This is to my mind the nicest spot in Whitby, for it lies right over the town, and has a full view of the harbour and all up the bay to where the headland called Kettleness stretches out into the sea.", "내 생각에는 이곳이 휘트비에서 가장 좋은 자리인데, 시가지 바로 위에 자리잡고 있어 항구가 한눈에 들어오고, 케틀네스라 부르는 곶이 바다로 뻗어나간 곳까지 만(灣) 전체가 다 보이기 때문이다."], ["It descends so steeply over the harbour that part of the bank has fallen away, and some of the graves have been destroyed.", "항구 위로 어찌나 가파르게 내리뻗었는지 둑의 일부가 무너져내렸고, 무덤 몇 기는 부서져버렸다."], ["In one place part of the stonework of the graves stretches out over the sandy pathway far below.", "한 곳에서는 무덤의 석조물 일부가 저 멀리 아래의 모래 오솔길 위로 비죽 튀어나와 있다."], ["There are walks, with seats beside them, through the churchyard; and people go and sit there all day long looking at the beautiful view and enjoying the breeze.", "교회 묘지에는 옆에 의자를 둔 산책로가 나 있는데, 사람들은 온종일 그곳에 앉아 아름다운 경치를 바라보며 바람을 즐긴다."], ["I shall come and sit here very often myself and work.", "나도 이곳에 자주 와서 앉아 일을 할 것이다."], ["Indeed, I am writing now, with my book on my knee, and listening to the talk of three old men who are sitting beside me.", "사실 지금도 무릎에 공책을 올려놓고 글을 쓰면서, 내 옆에 앉은 노인 셋이 나누는 이야기에 귀를 기울이고 있다."], ["They seem to do nothing all day but sit up here and talk.", "그들은 온종일 여기 앉아 이야기하는 것 말고는 달리 하는 일이 없는 듯하다."]], [["The harbour lies below me, with, on the far side, one long granite wall stretching out into the sea, with a curve outwards at the end of it, in the middle of which is a lighthouse.", "항구는 내 발치 아래에 펼쳐져 있는데, 저편에는 화강암으로 쌓은 긴 방파제 하나가 바다로 뻗어나가 끝에서 바깥쪽으로 휘어져 있고, 그 휘어진 한가운데에 등대가 서 있다."], ["A heavy sea-wall runs along outside of it.", "그 바깥쪽으로는 묵직한 방파제가 나란히 뻗어 있다."], ["On the near side, the sea-wall makes an elbow crooked inversely, and its end too has a lighthouse.", "이쪽 편에서는 방파제가 반대 방향으로 팔꿈치처럼 꺾여 있고, 그 끝에도 등대가 하나 있다."], ["Between the two piers there is a narrow opening into the harbour, which then suddenly widens.", "두 방파제 사이로 항구로 들어가는 좁은 입구가 있는데, 그 안으로 들어서면 항구가 갑자기 넓어진다."]], [["It is nice at high water; but when the tide is out it shoals away to nothing, and there is merely the stream of the Esk, running between banks of sand, with rocks here and there.", "만조 때는 보기 좋지만, 썰물이 빠지면 물이 얕아져 거의 사라지고, 그저 모래톱 사이를 흐르는 에스크 강의 물줄기와 군데군데 바위만 남는다."], ["Outside the harbour on this side there rises for about half a mile a great reef, the sharp edge of which runs straight out from behind the south lighthouse.", "이쪽 편 항구 바깥으로는 반 마일쯤에 걸쳐 큰 암초가 솟아 있는데, 그 날카로운 가장자리가 남쪽 등대 뒤에서 곧장 바다로 뻗어나가 있다."], ["At the end of it is a buoy with a bell, which swings in bad weather, and sends in a mournful sound on the wind.", "그 끝에는 종이 달린 부표가 있어, 날씨가 사나우면 흔들리며 바람결에 구슬픈 소리를 실어 보낸다."], ["They have a legend here that when a ship is lost bells are heard out at sea.", "이곳에는 배가 난파하면 먼바다에서 종소리가 들린다는 전설이 있다."], ["I must ask the old man about this; he is coming this way....", "이것에 대해 저 노인에게 물어봐야겠다. 마침 그가 이쪽으로 오고 있다……."]], [["He is a funny old man.", "그는 우스꽝스러운 노인이다."], ["He must be awfully old, for his face is all gnarled and twisted like the bark of a tree.", "얼굴이 온통 나무껍질처럼 옹이지고 비틀려 있는 걸 보면 무척 늙었음이 틀림없다."], ["He tells me that he is nearly a hundred, and that he was a sailor in the Greenland fishing fleet when Waterloo was fought.", "그는 자기가 거의 백 살이 다 되었으며, 워털루 전투가 벌어졌을 때 그린란드 어선단에서 선원으로 일했다고 한다."], ["He is, I am afraid, a very sceptical person, for when I asked him about the bells at sea and the White Lady at the abbey he said very brusquely:--", "안타깝게도 그는 매우 회의적인 사람인데, 내가 바다의 종소리와 수도원의 흰옷 입은 여인에 대해 묻자 그는 아주 퉁명스럽게 말했다."]], [["“I wouldn’t fash masel’ about them, miss.", "\"그딴 거 신경 안 쓸라우, 아가씨."], ["Them things be all wore out.", "그런 것들은 죄다 한물간 거지."], ["Mind, I don’t say that they never was, but I do say that they wasn’t in my time.", "명심허우, 그런 게 아주 없었다는 게 아니라, 내가 살아온 시절엔 없었다는 거유."], ["They be all very well for comers and trippers, an’ the like, but not for a nice young lady like you.", "그런 건 외지에서 온 사람이나 놀러온 사람들, 뭐 그런 부류한테나 그럴듯헌 거지, 아가씨처럼 곱상한 젊은 여자한테 할 소리는 아니유."], ["Them feet-folks from York and Leeds that be always eatin’ cured herrin’s an’ drinkin’ tea an’ lookin’ out to buy cheap jet would creed aught.", "요크나 리즈에서 걸어 들어온 그 작자들, 늘 절인 청어나 처먹고 차나 들이켜고 싸구려 흑옥(黑玉)이나 사려고 두리번거리는 그런 작자들이야 뭐든 곧이듣겠지."], ["I wonder masel’ who’d be bothered tellin’ lies to them--even the newspapers, which is full of fool-talk.” I thought he would be a good person to learn interesting things from, so I asked him if he would mind telling me something about the whale-fishing in the old days.", "누가 일부러 그들헌테 거짓말을 해줄 생각을 다 헐꼬, 나도 참 궁금허네 — 신문조차도, 바보 같은 소리로 가득한 그 신문조차도 말이여.\" 나는 그가 흥미로운 이야기를 들려줄 만한 사람이겠거니 싶어, 옛날의 고래잡이에 대해 이야기를 좀 해주지 않겠느냐고 물었다."], ["He was just settling himself to begin when the clock struck six, whereupon he laboured to get up, and said:--", "그가 막 이야기를 시작하려고 자세를 고쳐 앉는데 시계가 여섯 시를 알렸고, 그러자 그는 끙끙대며 몸을 일으키더니 말했다."]], [["“I must gang ageeanwards home now, miss.", "\"이제 집으로 돌아가야겠수, 아가씨."], ["My grand-daughter doesn’t like to be kept waitin’ when the tea is ready, for it takes me time to crammle aboon the grees, for there be a many of ’em; an’, miss, I lack belly-timber sairly by the clock.”", "내 손녀딸이 차 준비가 다 됐는데 날 기다리게 하는 걸 안 좋아해서 말이여. 나야 계단을 기어오르는 데 시간이 한참 걸리니까, 그 계단이 어찌나 많은지. 그리구 아가씨, 시계로 보면 난 뱃속 양식이 영 부족허단 말이여.\""]], [["He hobbled away, and I could see him hurrying, as well as he could, down the steps. The steps are a great feature on the place.", "그는 절뚝거리며 떠났고, 나는 그가 할 수 있는 한 서둘러 계단을 내려가는 모습을 볼 수 있었다. 그 계단은 이곳의 큰 명물이다."], ["They lead from the town up to the church, there are hundreds of them--I do not know how many--and they wind up in a delicate curve; the slope is so gentle that a horse could easily walk up and down them.", "시가지에서 교회까지 이어지는 그 계단은 수백 개나 되는데 — 정확히 몇 개인지는 모른다 — 우아한 곡선을 그리며 굽이굽이 올라간다. 경사가 어찌나 완만한지 말도 쉽게 오르내릴 수 있을 정도다."], ["I think they must originally have had something to do with the abbey. I shall go home too.", "본디 수도원과 무슨 관련이 있었으리라 생각한다. 나도 집으로 돌아가야겠다."], ["Lucy went out visiting with her mother, and as they were only duty calls, I did not go.", "루시는 어머니와 함께 방문을 나갔는데, 그저 의례적인 방문일 뿐이어서 나는 따라가지 않았다."], ["They will be home by this.", "이제쯤이면 돌아왔을 것이다."]], [["1 August.--I came up here an hour ago with Lucy, and we had a most interesting talk with my old friend and the two others who always come and join him.", "8월 1일. — 나는 한 시간 전에 루시와 함께 이곳으로 올라왔는데, 내 늙은 친구와, 언제나 그에게 와서 합류하는 다른 두 사람과 무척 흥미로운 이야기를 나누었다."], ["He is evidently the Sir Oracle of them, and I should think must have been in his time a most dictatorial person.", "그는 분명 그들 가운데 신탁(神託)을 내리는 어른이며, 한창때에는 매우 독선적인 사람이었으리라 짐작된다."], ["He will not admit anything, and downfaces everybody.", "그는 어떤 것도 인정하려 들지 않고, 모두를 면박 준다."], ["If he can’t out-argue them he bullies them, and then takes their silence for agreement with his views.", "논쟁에서 이기지 못하면 윽박지르고, 그러고는 상대의 침묵을 자기 견해에 동의한 것으로 받아들인다."], ["Lucy was looking sweetly pretty in her white lawn frock; she has got a beautiful colour since she has been here.", "루시는 흰색 론 천 원피스를 입고 사랑스럽도록 예뻐 보였다. 이곳에 온 뒤로 그녀의 혈색이 아름다워졌다."], ["I noticed that the old men did not lose any time in coming up and sitting near her when we sat down.", "우리가 자리에 앉자 노인들이 한시도 지체하지 않고 다가와 그녀 곁에 앉는 것을 나는 알아챘다."], ["She is so sweet with old people; I think they all fell in love with her on the spot.", "그녀는 노인들에게 어찌나 다정한지, 모두가 그 자리에서 그녀에게 반해버린 것 같다."], ["Even my old man succumbed and did not contradict her, but gave me double share instead.", "내 늙은 친구조차도 굴복하여 그녀의 말에 반박하지 않았고, 대신 나에게 두 배로 면박을 주었다."], ["I got him on the subject of the legends, and he went off at once into a sort of sermon.", "나는 그를 전설 이야기로 끌어들였고, 그는 곧장 일종의 설교를 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["I must try to remember it and put it down:--", "그것을 기억해두었다가 적어두어야겠다."]], [["“It be all fool-talk, lock, stock, and barrel; that’s what it be, an’ nowt else.", "\"그딴 건 죄다 바보 같은 소리여, 통째로 몽땅 다 말이여. 그게 다지, 다른 건 없어."], ["These bans an’ wafts an’ boh-ghosts an’ barguests an’ bogles an’ all anent them is only fit to set bairns an’ dizzy women a-belderin’.", "그 저주니, 망령이니, 도깨비니, 요귀(妖鬼)니, 귀신이니, 그것들에 관한 모든 게 그저 어린애나 정신없는 여편네들 비명이나 지르게 하기 딱 좋은 거지."], ["They be nowt but air-blebs.", "그것들은 다 헛된 물거품일 뿐이여."], ["They, an’ all grims an’ signs an’ warnin’s, be all invented by parsons an’ illsome beuk-bodies an’ railway touters to skeer an’ scunner hafflin’s, an’ to get folks to do somethin’ that they don’t other incline to.", "그것들이며, 온갖 흉조니 징조니 경고니 하는 것들은 죄다 목사들이며 못된 책장수들이며 철도 호객꾼들이 어린것들 겁주고 질겁하게 해서, 사람들로 하여금 안 그러면 안 할 짓을 하게 만들려고 지어낸 거여."], ["It makes me ireful to think o’ them.", "그것들을 생각하면 부아가 치미는구먼."], ["Why, it’s them that, not content with printin’ lies on paper an’ preachin’ them out of pulpits, does want to be cuttin’ them on the tombstones.", "아니 글쎄, 종이에 거짓말을 박아내고 강단에서 거짓말을 설교하는 걸로도 모자라서, 이젠 비석에다 그걸 새기려고 든단 말이여. 자, 여기 둘레를 한번 둘러보우, 어느 쪽이든 좋수."], ["Look here all around you in what airt ye will; all them steans, holdin’ up their heads as well as they can out of their pride, is acant--simply tumblin’ down with the weight o’ the lies wrote on them, ‘Here lies the body’ or ‘Sacred to the memory’ wrote on all of them, an’ yet in nigh half of them there bean’t no bodies at all; an’ the memories of them bean’t cared a pinch of snuff about, much less sacred.", "저 비석들이 죄다, 제 잘난 멋에 머리를 빳빳이 쳐들고 있지만, 다들 한쪽으로 기울어 있다우 — 거기 적힌 거짓말 무게에 못 이겨 그냥 폭삭 주저앉고 있는 거지. '여기 잠들다'느니 '추억을 기리며'느니 죄다 그렇게 적혀 있지만, 그 절반쯤에는 아예 시신이 없다우. 그리구 그들에 대한 추억이라는 것도 코담배 한 줌만큼도 마음 쓰는 이가 없지, 기리기는커녕 말이여."], ["Lies all of them, nothin’ but lies of one kind or another!", "죄다 거짓말이여, 이러쿵저러쿵 갖가지 거짓말일 뿐이라우!"], ["My gog, but it’ll be a quare scowderment at the Day of Judgment when they come tumblin’ up in their death-sarks, all jouped together an’ tryin’ to drag their tombsteans with them to prove how good they was; some of them trimmlin’ and ditherin’, with their hands that dozzened an’ slippy from lyin’ in the sea that they can’t even keep their grup o’ them.”", "아이고 맙소사, 최후의 심판 날에 그자들이 죄다 수의(壽衣) 차림으로 한데 뒤엉켜 비척비척 기어오르며, 제가 얼마나 착했는지 증명하겠다고 제 비석을 질질 끌고 오려 들 때면 그것 참 가관일 거구먼. 더러는 바다에 누워 있던 탓에 손이 다 곱고 미끄러워서 비석을 붙잡지도 못한 채 부들부들 벌벌 떨겠지.\""]], [["I could see from the old fellow’s self-satisfied air and the way in which he looked round for the approval of his cronies that he was “showing off,” so I put in a word to keep him going:--", "나는 그 노인의 흡족해하는 태도와, 친구들의 동의를 구하려고 둘러보는 모양새로 보아 그가 \"뽐내고\" 있음을 알 수 있었으므로, 그가 계속 이야기하도록 한마디 거들었다."]], [["“Oh, Mr. Swales, you can’t be serious.", "\"어머, 스웨일스 씨, 진심은 아니시겠지요."], ["Surely these tombstones are not all wrong?”", "설마 이 비석들이 모두 다 틀렸겠어요?\""]], [["“Yabblins!", "\"아마도 그렇진 않겠지!"], ["There may be a poorish few not wrong, savin’ where they make out the people too good; for there be folk that do think a balm-bowl be like the sea, if only it be their own.", "틀리지 않은 게 몇 개쯤은 있을 거유, 하기야 그 사람들을 너무 좋게 꾸며놓은 데는 빼고 말이여. 자기 것이기만 하면 세숫대야가 바다만 하다고 여기는 작자들이 있으니까."], ["The whole thing be only lies.", "어쨌든 그 전체가 죄다 거짓말이여."], ["Now look you here; you come here a stranger, an’ you see this kirk-garth.”", "자, 여기 보우. 아가씨는 여기 낯선 사람으로 와서 이 교회 묘지를 보고 있지.\""], ["I nodded, for I thought it better to assent, though I did not quite understand his dialect.", "나는 고개를 끄덕였는데, 그의 사투리를 다 알아듣지는 못했지만 동의하는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했기 때문이다."], ["I knew it had something to do with the church.", "그것이 교회와 관계있는 말이라는 건 알았다."], ["He went on: “And you consate that all these steans be aboon folk that be happed here, snod an’ snog?”", "그는 말을 이었다. \"그럼 아가씨는 이 비석들이 죄다 여기 곱게 편안히 묻힌 사람들 위에 놓인 거라고 짐작하시겠지?\""], ["I assented again.", "나는 다시 동의했다."], ["“Then that be just where the lie comes in.", "\"바로 거기서 거짓말이 끼어드는 거여."], ["Why, there be scores of these lay-beds that be toom as old Dun’s ’bacca-box on Friday night.”", "아니 글쎄, 이 무덤 자리들 가운데 수십 개가 금요일 밤 던 영감네 담배통처럼 텅텅 비어 있다우.\""], ["He nudged one of his companions, and they all laughed.", "그는 동료 하나를 팔꿈치로 쿡 찌르더니, 다들 웃음을 터뜨렸다."], ["“And my gog! how could they be otherwise?", "\"아이고 맙소사! 어찌 안 그러겠수?"], ["Look at that one, the aftest abaft the bier-bank: read it!”", "저거 좀 보우, 상여 길 맨 끝에 있는 저거 말이여. 한번 읽어보우!\""], ["I went over and read:--", "나는 건너가서 읽었다."]], [["“Edward Spencelagh, master mariner, murdered by pirates off the coast of Andres, April, 1854, æt. 30.”", "\"에드워드 스펜슬러, 선장(船長), 1854년 4월 안드레스 연안에서 해적에게 살해당하다, 향년 30세.\""], ["When I came back Mr. Swales went on:--", "내가 돌아오자 스웨일스 씨는 말을 이었다."]], [["“Who brought him home, I wonder, to hap him here?", "\"누가 저 사람을 집으로 데려와서 여기 묻었을까, 나는 그게 궁금하구먼."], ["Murdered off the coast of Andres! an’ you consated his body lay under!", "안드레스 연안에서 살해당했는데! 그런데도 아가씨는 그 시신이 저 아래 누워 있다고 짐작했단 말이지!"], ["Why, I could name ye a dozen whose bones lie in the Greenland seas above”--he pointed northwards--“or where the currents may have drifted them.", "아니 글쎄, 그린란드 바다 저 위에 뼈가 누워 있는 사람들을 내 열댓 명은 꼽을 수 있다우 —\" 그가 북쪽을 가리켰다 \"— 아니면 해류가 어디로 떠밀어갔는지도 모를 사람들이지."], ["There be the steans around ye.", "아가씨 둘레에 비석들이 있잖수."], ["Ye can, with your young eyes, read the small-print of the lies from here.", "아가씨야 젊은 눈이니, 여기서 그 거짓말의 잔글씨까지 다 읽을 수 있을 거유."], ["This Braithwaite Lowrey--I knew his father, lost in the Lively off Greenland in ’20; or Andrew Woodhouse, drowned in the same seas in 1777; or John Paxton, drowned off Cape Farewell a year later; or old John Rawlings, whose grandfather sailed with me, drowned in the Gulf of Finland in ’50.", "이 브레이스웨이트 라우리 — 내가 그 아비를 알았는데, 20년에 그린란드 앞바다에서 '라이블리'호와 함께 사라졌지. 또 앤드루 우드하우스, 1777년에 같은 바다에서 익사했고, 존 팩스턴, 그 이듬해에 페어웰 곶 앞바다에서 익사했고, 늙은 존 롤링스, 그 할아비가 나랑 같이 배를 탔었는데, 50년에 핀란드 만에서 익사했지."], ["Do ye think that all these men will have to make a rush to Whitby when the trumpet sounds?", "아가씨는 이 사람들이 죄다 나팔 소리가 울리면 휘트비로 부랴부랴 달려와야 한다고 생각하우?"], ["I have me antherums aboot it!", "나는 그게 영 미심쩍단 말이여!"], ["I tell ye that when they got here they’d be jommlin’ an’ jostlin’ one another that way that it ’ud be like a fight up on the ice in the old days, when we’d be at one another from daylight to dark, an’ tryin’ to tie up our cuts by the light of the aurora borealis.”", "내 장담컨대, 그들이 여기 닿으면 서로 밀치고 떠밀고 하면서 한바탕 난리가 날 거유, 옛날 얼음판 위에서 우리가 동틀 녘부터 해 질 녘까지 서로 치고받다가 북극광 빛에 비춰가며 상처를 싸매던 그 싸움판처럼 말이여.\""], ["This was evidently local pleasantry, for the old man cackled over it, and his cronies joined in with gusto.", "이건 분명 이 고장식 농담이었으니, 노인은 그 말에 깔깔 웃어댔고, 그의 친구들도 신이 나서 함께 웃었다."]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["I said, “surely you are not quite correct, for you start on the assumption that all the poor people, or their spirits, will have to take their tombstones with them on the Day of Judgment.", "내가 말했다, \"정말로 옳지만은 않으세요. 모든 가엾은 사람들이, 아니면 그 혼령들이 최후의 심판 날에 제 비석을 가지고 가야 한다는 전제에서 출발하고 계시잖아요."], ["Do you think that will be really necessary?”", "정말로 그래야만 한다고 생각하세요?\""]], [["“Well, what else be they tombstones for?", "\"아니, 그럼 그 비석들이 뭣 하러 있는 건데?"], ["Answer me that, miss!”", "그것 좀 대답해보우, 아가씨!\""]], [["“To please their relatives, I suppose.”", "\"유족들을 기쁘게 하려는 거겠지요, 아마도.\""]], [["“To please their relatives, you suppose!”", "\"유족들을 기쁘게 하려는 거다, 아마도!\""], ["This he said with intense scorn.", "그는 이 말을 지독한 경멸을 담아 내뱉었다."], ["“How will it pleasure their relatives to know that lies is wrote over them, and that everybody in the place knows that they be lies?”", "\"거기에 거짓말이 적혀 있고, 이 동네 사람이 다 그게 거짓말인 줄 안다는 걸 알면 그게 유족들한테 무슨 기쁨이 되겠수?\""], ["He pointed to a stone at our feet which had been laid down as a slab, on which the seat was rested, close to the edge of the cliff.", "그는 우리 발치의, 의자를 받치는 받침대로 깔아놓은, 절벽 가장자리에 바짝 붙은 돌 하나를 가리켰다."], ["“Read the lies on that thruff-stean,”", "\"저 묘석에 적힌 거짓말을 읽어보우,\""], ["he said. The letters were upside down to me from where I sat, but Lucy was more opposite to them, so she leant over and read:--", "그가 말했다. 내가 앉은 자리에서는 글자가 거꾸로 보였지만, 루시는 그것을 더 마주보는 위치에 있어서 몸을 숙여 읽었다."]], [["“Sacred to the memory of George Canon, who died, in the hope of a glorious resurrection, on July, 29, 1873, falling from the rocks at Kettleness.", "\"케틀네스 바위에서 떨어져, 영광스러운 부활을 소망하며 1873년 7월 29일에 세상을 떠난 조지 캐넌을 기리며."], ["This tomb was erected by his sorrowing mother to her dearly beloved son.", "이 무덤은 슬픔에 잠긴 어머니가 더없이 사랑하는 아들을 위해 세웠노라."], ["‘He was the only son of his mother, and she was a widow.’ Really, Mr. Swales, I don’t see anything very funny in that!”", "'그는 어머니의 외아들이었으며, 그 어머니는 과부였더라.' 정말이지, 스웨일스 씨, 저는 여기서 우스운 점을 하나도 못 찾겠어요!\""], ["She spoke her comment very gravely and somewhat severely.", "그녀는 자기 의견을 매우 진지하게, 다소 엄하게 말했다."]], [["“Ye don’t see aught funny! Ha! ha!", "\"우스운 게 안 보인다고! 하! 하!"], ["But that’s because ye don’t gawm the sorrowin’ mother was a hell-cat that hated him because he was acrewk’d--a regular lamiter he was--an’ he hated her so that he committed suicide in order that she mightn’t get an insurance she put on his life.", "그건 아가씨가 그 슬픔에 잠긴 어머니가 실은 못된 마귀할멈이었다는 걸 모르니까 그렇지. 그 어미가 아들을 미워한 건 그 애가 절름발이여서였다우 — 영락없는 앉은뱅이였지 — 그리구 그 애는 어미를 어찌나 미워했던지, 어미가 제 목숨에 들어둔 보험금을 못 타게 하려고 일부러 자살을 했다우."], ["He blew nigh the top of his head off with an old musket that they had for scarin’ the crows with.", "까마귀 쫓는 데 쓰던 낡은 머스킷 총으로 제 머리통을 거의 절반이나 날려버렸지."], ["’Twarn’t for crows then, for it brought the clegs and the dowps to him.", "그래서 까마귀 대신 쇠파리랑 까마귀 떼만 그자한테 꼬였구먼."], ["That’s the way he fell off the rocks.", "그게 바로 그 애가 바위에서 떨어진 사연이여."], ["And, as to hopes of a glorious resurrection, I’ve often heard him say masel’ that he hoped he’d go to hell, for his mother was so pious that she’d be sure to go to heaven, an’ he didn’t want to addle where she was.", "그리구 영광스러운 부활을 소망했다는 거 말인데, 나는 그자가 제 입으로 지옥에 가고 싶다고 하는 걸 여러 번 들었다우. 제 어미가 어찌나 신앙심이 깊은지 틀림없이 천국에 갈 텐데, 자기는 어미가 있는 데서 썩고 싶지 않다는 거였지."], ["Now isn’t that stean at any rate”--he hammered it with his stick as he spoke--“a pack of lies?", "자, 어쨌든 저 비석이 —\" 그는 말하면서 지팡이로 그것을 탕탕 두드렸다 \"— 새빨간 거짓말 덩어리가 아니냐고?"], ["and won’t it make Gabriel keckle when Geordie comes pantin’ up the grees with the tombstean balanced on his hump, and asks it to be took as evidence!”", "그리구 최후의 심판 날에 조디가 제 등의 혹 위에 비석을 얹고 헐떡거리며 계단을 기어 올라와서, 이걸 증거로 받아달라고 하면 가브리엘 천사가 픽 웃지 않겠수!\""]], [["I did not know what to say, but Lucy turned the conversation as she said, rising up:--", "나는 무슨 말을 해야 할지 몰랐는데, 루시가 일어서며 화제를 돌렸다."]], [["“Oh, why did you tell us of this?", "\"아, 그런 말씀을 왜 우리한테 해주셨어요?"], ["It is my favourite seat, and I cannot leave it; and now I find I must go on sitting over the grave of a suicide.”", "여기는 제가 제일 좋아하는 자리라 떠날 수가 없는데, 이제 보니 저는 자살한 사람의 무덤 위에 계속 앉아 있어야 하는 거잖아요.\""]], [["“That won’t harm ye, my pretty; an’ it may make poor Geordie gladsome to have so trim a lass sittin’ on his lap.", "\"그래도 그게 아가씨를 해치진 않을 거유, 어여쁜 아가씨. 그리구 이렇게 참한 처녀가 제 무릎에 앉아 있으면 가엾은 조디도 외려 기뻐할지 모르지."], ["That won’t hurt ye. Why, I’ve sat here off an’ on for nigh twenty years past, an’ it hasn’t done me no harm.", "그게 아가씨를 다치게 하진 않아. 아니 글쎄, 나도 지난 이십 년 가까이 여기 들락날락 앉아 있었지만 아무 탈도 없었다우."], ["Don’t ye fash about them as lies under ye, or that doesn’ lie there either!", "아가씨 밑에 누워 있는 자들이나, 아니 거기 누워 있지도 않은 자들 때문에 안달복달하지 마우!"], ["It’ll be time for ye to be getting scart when ye see the tombsteans all run away with, and the place as bare as a stubble-field.", "비석들이 죄다 달아나버리고, 이 자리가 그루터기 밭처럼 텅 비어버린 걸 보거든 그때 가서 겁먹어도 늦지 않다우."], ["There’s the clock, an’ I must gang.", "저기 시계 소리가 나는구먼, 나는 이제 가봐야겠수."], ["My service to ye, ladies!”", "그럼 안녕히 계시우, 아가씨들!\""], ["And off he hobbled.", "그러고는 절뚝거리며 떠나갔다."]], [["Lucy and I sat awhile, and it was all so beautiful before us that we took hands as we sat; and she told me all over again about Arthur and their coming marriage.", "루시와 나는 한동안 앉아 있었는데, 눈앞의 풍경이 어찌나 아름다운지 우리는 앉은 채로 서로 손을 잡았다. 그리고 그녀는 아서와 다가오는 결혼식에 대해 처음부터 다시 다 이야기해주었다."], ["That made me just a little heart-sick, for I haven’t heard from Jonathan for a whole month.", "그 이야기에 나는 마음이 조금 아렸으니, 조너선에게서 꼬박 한 달째 소식을 듣지 못했기 때문이다."]], [["The same day. I came up here alone, for I am very sad.", "같은 날. 나는 이곳에 혼자 올라왔는데, 몹시 슬프기 때문이다."], ["There was no letter for me.", "나에게 온 편지는 없었다."], ["I hope there cannot be anything the matter with Jonathan.", "조너선에게 무슨 일이 있는 건 아니길 바란다."], ["The clock has just struck nine.", "방금 시계가 아홉 시를 쳤다."], ["I see the lights scattered all over the town, sometimes in rows where the streets are, and sometimes singly; they run right up the Esk and die away in the curve of the valley.", "시가지 전체에 불빛이 흩어져 있는 것이 보이는데, 더러는 거리를 따라 줄지어 있고, 더러는 하나씩 따로 있다. 그 불빛들은 에스크 강을 따라 곧장 올라가다가 골짜기의 굽이에서 스러진다."], ["To my left the view is cut off by a black line of roof of the old house next the abbey.", "내 왼편으로는 수도원 옆 옛집 지붕의 검은 선에 시야가 가로막혀 있다."], ["The sheep and lambs are bleating in the fields away behind me, and there is a clatter of a donkey’s hoofs up the paved road below.", "내 뒤편 멀리 들판에서는 양들과 새끼 양들이 울고 있고, 아래쪽 포장된 길에서는 당나귀 발굽 소리가 또각또각 들려온다."], ["The band on the pier is playing a harsh waltz in good time, and further along the quay there is a Salvation Army meeting in a back street. Neither of the bands hears the other, but up here I hear and see them both.", "방파제의 악단은 거친 왈츠를 박자 맞춰 연주하고 있고, 부두를 따라 좀 더 가면 뒷골목에서 구세군 집회가 열리고 있다. 두 악단은 서로의 소리를 듣지 못하지만, 이 위에서는 두 소리가 다 들리고 두 광경이 다 보인다."], ["I wonder where Jonathan is and if he is thinking of me!", "조너선은 지금 어디에 있을까, 나를 생각하고 있을까!"], ["I wish he were here.", "그가 여기 있었으면 좋겠다."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["5 June.--The case of Renfield grows more interesting the more I get to understand the man.", "6월 5일. — 렌필드라는 사례는 그 사람을 이해하면 할수록 더욱 흥미로워진다."], ["He has certain qualities very largely developed; selfishness, secrecy, and purpose.", "그에게는 어떤 자질들이 매우 크게 발달해 있다. 이기심, 비밀스러움, 그리고 목적의식이다."], ["I wish I could get at what is the object of the latter.", "마지막 것의 목표가 무엇인지를 알아낼 수 있다면 좋겠다."], ["He seems to have some settled scheme of his own, but what it is I do not yet know.", "그에게는 자기 나름의 어떤 확고한 계획이 있는 듯한데, 그것이 무엇인지는 아직 모른다."], ["His redeeming quality is a love of animals, though, indeed, he has such curious turns in it that I sometimes imagine he is only abnormally cruel.", "그의 구원할 만한 자질은 동물을 사랑하는 것인데, 그러나 그 사랑에 어찌나 기이한 면이 있는지 나는 이따금 그가 그저 비정상적으로 잔인한 게 아닐까 싶기도 하다."], ["His pets are of odd sorts. Just now his hobby is catching flies.", "그가 기르는 것들은 별난 종류다. 지금 그의 취미는 파리 잡기다."], ["He has at present such a quantity that I have had myself to expostulate.", "현재 어찌나 많은 파리를 모았는지 나는 직접 타일러야 했다."], ["To my astonishment, he did not break out into a fury, as I expected, but took the matter in simple seriousness.", "놀랍게도 그는 내가 예상한 대로 분통을 터뜨리지 않고, 그 문제를 담담하고 진지하게 받아들였다."], ["He thought for a moment, and then said: “May I have three days?", "그는 잠시 생각하더니 말했다. \"사흘만 시간을 주시겠습니까?"], ["I shall clear them away.”", "다 치우겠습니다.\""], ["Of course, I said that would do.", "물론 나는 그래도 좋다고 했다."], ["I must watch him.", "그를 지켜봐야겠다."]], [["18 June.--He has turned his mind now to spiders, and has got several very big fellows in a box.", "6월 18일. — 그는 이제 거미로 마음을 돌려, 아주 큰 놈들 여러 마리를 상자 안에 모아두었다."], ["He keeps feeding them with his flies, and the number of the latter is becoming sensibly diminished, although he has used half his food in attracting more flies from outside to his room.", "그는 자기 파리들을 거미에게 계속 먹이고 있어서 파리의 수가 눈에 띄게 줄어들고 있다. 하지만 그는 자기 식량의 절반을 써서 바깥에서 더 많은 파리를 방 안으로 꾀어들이고 있다."]], [["1 July.--His spiders are now becoming as great a nuisance as his flies, and to-day I told him that he must get rid of them.", "7월 1일. — 그의 거미들이 이제 파리만큼이나 큰 골칫거리가 되어, 오늘 나는 그에게 거미들을 없애야 한다고 말했다."], ["He looked very sad at this, so I said that he must clear out some of them, at all events.", "그가 이 말에 무척 슬퍼 보이기에, 나는 적어도 일부는 치워야 한다고 했다."], ["He cheerfully acquiesced in this, and I gave him the same time as before for reduction.", "그는 이 말에 흔쾌히 응했고, 나는 전과 같은 시간을 줄여 없애도록 주었다."], ["He disgusted me much while with him, for when a horrid blow-fly, bloated with some carrion food, buzzed into the room, he caught it, held it exultantly for a few moments between his finger and thumb, and, before I knew what he was going to do, put it in his mouth and ate it.", "그와 함께 있는 동안 그는 나를 몹시 역겹게 했는데, 썩은 고기로 살이 통통하게 오른 끔찍한 검정파리 한 마리가 윙윙거리며 방 안으로 들어오자, 그는 그것을 붙잡아 잠시 의기양양하게 엄지와 검지 사이에 끼고 있다가, 내가 그가 무엇을 하려는지 알아채기도 전에 그것을 입에 넣고 먹어버렸다."], ["I scolded him for it, but he argued quietly that it was very good and very wholesome; that it was life, strong life, and gave life to him.", "내가 그 일로 그를 나무라자, 그는 그것이 아주 좋고 아주 몸에 이로우며, 그것은 생명, 강한 생명이고 자기에게 생명을 준다고 차분하게 우겼다."], ["This gave me an idea, or the rudiment of one.", "이것이 나에게 한 가지 생각을, 적어도 그 단초를 떠오르게 했다."], ["I must watch how he gets rid of his spiders.", "그가 거미들을 어떻게 없애는지 지켜봐야겠다."], ["He has evidently some deep problem in his mind, for he keeps a little note-book in which he is always jotting down something.", "그에게는 분명 마음속에 어떤 깊은 문제가 있는데, 그는 작은 수첩을 하나 지니고 다니며 거기에 늘 무언가를 끄적이고 있기 때문이다."], ["Whole pages of it are filled with masses of figures, generally single numbers added up in batches, and then the totals added in batches again, as though he were “focussing” some account, as the auditors put it.", "그 수첩의 여러 면이 숫자 더미로 가득 차 있는데, 대개 한 자리 숫자들을 무더기로 더하고, 그 합계를 다시 무더기로 더해놓은 것이, 마치 회계 감사관들의 표현대로 어떤 회계 장부를 \"맞추어보고\" 있는 것 같았다."]], [["8 July.--There is a method in his madness, and the rudimentary idea in my mind is growing.", "7월 8일. — 그의 광기에는 나름의 방법이 있고, 내 마음속의 그 어렴풋한 생각도 점점 자라나고 있다."], ["It will be a whole idea soon, and then, oh, unconscious cerebration!", "곧 온전한 생각이 될 것이며, 그러면 아, 무의식적 두뇌 작용이여!"], ["you will have to give the wall to your conscious brother.", "너는 너의 의식 있는 형제에게 길을 비켜주어야 하리라."], ["I kept away from my friend for a few days, so that I might notice if there were any change.", "나는 며칠 동안 내 친구를 멀리했는데, 무슨 변화가 있는지 살펴보려는 것이었다."], ["Things remain as they were except that he has parted with some of his pets and got a new one.", "그가 기르던 것들 가운데 일부를 처분하고 새로운 것을 하나 얻었다는 점을 빼면 상황은 전과 같았다."], ["He has managed to get a sparrow, and has already partially tamed it.", "그는 참새 한 마리를 손에 넣어 이미 어느 정도 길들여놓았다."], ["His means of taming is simple, for already the spiders have diminished.", "그 길들이는 수단은 단순한데, 이미 거미들이 줄어들었기 때문이다."], ["Those that do remain, however, are well fed, for he still brings in the flies by tempting them with his food.", "그러나 남아 있는 거미들은 잘 먹고 있는데, 그가 여전히 식량으로 꾀어들여 파리를 잡아들이기 때문이다."]], [["19 July.--We are progressing.", "7월 19일. — 우리는 진전을 보이고 있다."], ["My friend has now a whole colony of sparrows, and his flies and spiders are almost obliterated.", "내 친구는 이제 참새 한 무리를 통째로 거느리게 되었고, 그의 파리와 거미는 거의 자취를 감추었다."], ["When I came in he ran to me and said he wanted to ask me a great favour--a very, very great favour; and as he spoke he fawned on me like a dog.", "내가 들어가자 그는 나에게 달려와 큰 부탁이 하나 있다고, 아주아주 큰 부탁이라고 했다. 그리고 말하는 동안 개처럼 나에게 알랑거렸다."], ["I asked him what it was, and he said, with a sort of rapture in his voice and bearing:--", "무슨 부탁이냐고 묻자, 그는 목소리와 태도에 일종의 황홀감을 담아 말했다."]], [["“A kitten, a nice little, sleek playful kitten, that I can play with, and teach, and feed--and feed--and feed!”", "\"새끼고양이요, 윤기 흐르고 잘 노는 예쁘고 작은 새끼고양이 한 마리요. 같이 놀고, 가르치고, 먹이고 — 먹이고 — 먹일 수 있는 놈으로요!\""], ["I was not unprepared for this request, for I had noticed how his pets went on increasing in size and vivacity, but I did not care that his pretty family of tame sparrows should be wiped out in the same manner as the flies and the spiders; so I said I would see about it, and asked him if he would not rather have a cat than a kitten.", "나는 이 부탁에 전혀 대비가 없던 것은 아니었으니, 그가 기르는 것들이 어떻게 점점 덩치가 커지고 활기가 넘쳐 갔는지를 눈여겨봐 왔기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 그가 길들인 참새들의 그 예쁜 식구가 파리와 거미와 똑같은 방식으로 사라지는 것은 바라지 않았으므로, 알아보겠다고 하면서, 새끼고양이보다는 차라리 다 큰 고양이가 낫지 않겠느냐고 물었다."], ["His eagerness betrayed him as he answered:--", "그는 대답하면서 그 간절함으로 자기 속내를 드러내고 말았다."]], [["“Oh, yes, I would like a cat!", "\"아, 그럼요, 고양이라면 좋지요!"], ["I only asked for a kitten lest you should refuse me a cat.", "제가 새끼고양이를 청한 건 선생님이 고양이를 거절하실까 봐서였습니다."], ["No one would refuse me a kitten, would they?” I shook my head, and said that at present I feared it would not be possible, but that I would see about it.", "새끼고양이라면 아무도 거절하지 않을 테니까요, 안 그렇습니까?\" 나는 고개를 저으며, 지금으로서는 안 될 것 같지만 알아보겠다고 했다."], ["His face fell, and I could see a warning of danger in it, for there was a sudden fierce, sidelong look which meant killing.", "그의 얼굴이 어두워졌고, 나는 거기서 위험을 알리는 경고를 볼 수 있었으니, 갑자기 살기를 띤 사납고 흘끔거리는 눈빛이 스쳤기 때문이다."], ["The man is an undeveloped homicidal maniac.", "이 사람은 아직 발현되지 않은 살인광이다."], ["I shall test him with his present craving and see how it will work out; then I shall know more.", "나는 그의 현재 갈망을 가지고 그를 시험해보고 어떻게 풀려나가는지 보아야겠다. 그러면 더 많은 것을 알게 될 것이다."]], [["10 p. m.--I have visited him again and found him sitting in a corner brooding.", "오후 10시. — 나는 그를 다시 찾아갔는데, 구석에 앉아 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있는 것을 보았다."], ["When I came in he threw himself on his knees before me and implored me to let him have a cat; that his salvation depended upon it.", "내가 들어가자 그는 내 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 고양이를 갖게 해달라고, 자기 구원이 거기에 달려 있다고 애원했다."], ["I was firm, however, and told him that he could not have it, whereupon he went without a word, and sat down, gnawing his fingers, in the corner where I had found him.", "그러나 나는 단호하게 그것은 안 된다고 말했고, 그러자 그는 한마디도 없이 물러가, 내가 그를 처음 발견했던 그 구석에 앉아 손가락을 물어뜯었다."], ["I shall see him in the morning early.", "아침 일찍 그를 보러 가야겠다."]], [["20 July.--Visited Renfield very early, before the attendant went his rounds.", "7월 20일. — 간병인이 회진을 돌기 전, 아주 일찍 렌필드를 찾아갔다."], ["Found him up and humming a tune.", "그는 일어나서 콧노래를 흥얼거리고 있었다."], ["He was spreading out his sugar, which he had saved, in the window, and was manifestly beginning his fly-catching again; and beginning it cheerfully and with a good grace.", "그는 아껴두었던 설탕을 창가에 펼쳐놓고 있었고, 보아하니 다시 파리 잡기를 시작하고 있었다. 그것도 명랑하고 기꺼운 태도로 말이다."], ["I looked around for his birds, and not seeing them, asked him where they were.", "나는 그의 새들을 찾아 둘러보았으나 보이지 않기에, 새들이 어디 있느냐고 물었다."], ["He replied, without turning round, that they had all flown away.", "그는 돌아보지도 않고, 새들이 모두 날아가버렸다고 대답했다."], ["There were a few feathers about the room and on his pillow a drop of blood.", "방 안에 깃털 몇 개가 흩어져 있었고, 그의 베개에는 핏방울 하나가 떨어져 있었다."], ["I said nothing, but went and told the keeper to report to me if there were anything odd about him during the day.", "나는 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 가서 간수에게 그날 낮 동안 그에게 무언가 이상한 점이 있으면 나에게 보고하라고 일렀다."]], [["11 a. m.--The attendant has just been to me to say that Renfield has been very sick and has disgorged a whole lot of feathers.", "오전 11시. — 방금 간병인이 나를 찾아와, 렌필드가 몹시 토했는데 깃털을 한 무더기나 게워냈다고 말했다."], ["“My belief is, doctor,”", "\"제 생각엔 말입니다, 선생님,\""], ["he said, “that he has eaten his birds, and that he just took and ate them raw!”", "그가 말했다, \"이자가 제 새들을 먹어버린 것 같습니다. 그것도 그냥 산 채로 잡아서 날것으로 먹어버린 거예요!\""]], [["11 p. m.--I gave Renfield a strong opiate to-night, enough to make even him sleep, and took away his pocket-book to look at it.", "오후 11시. — 오늘 밤 나는 렌필드에게, 그조차도 잠들게 할 만큼 강한 아편제를 먹이고, 그의 수첩을 가져와 살펴보았다."], ["The thought that has been buzzing about my brain lately is complete, and the theory proved.", "요즈음 내 머릿속을 윙윙거리며 맴돌던 그 생각이 이제 완성되었고, 이론이 입증되었다."], ["My homicidal maniac is of a peculiar kind.", "내 살인광은 특이한 종류다."], ["I shall have to invent a new classification for him, and call him a zoöphagous (life-eating) maniac; what he desires is to absorb as many lives as he can, and he has laid himself out to achieve it in a cumulative way.", "나는 그를 위해 새로운 분류 항목을 만들어, 그를 식생광(食生狂, 생명을 먹는 미치광이)이라고 불러야 할 것이다. 그가 바라는 것은 가능한 한 많은 생명을 흡수하는 것이며, 그는 그것을 누적적인 방식으로 이루어내려고 작정해두고 있다."], ["He gave many flies to one spider and many spiders to one bird, and then wanted a cat to eat the many birds.", "그는 거미 한 마리에게 파리 여럿을 주었고, 새 한 마리에게 거미 여럿을 주었으며, 그런 다음 그 많은 새를 먹게 할 고양이를 원했다."], ["What would have been his later steps?", "그 다음 단계는 무엇이었을까?"], ["It would almost be worth while to complete the experiment.", "이 실험을 완성해보는 것도 거의 가치 있는 일일 것이다."], ["It might be done if there were only a sufficient cause.", "충분한 명분만 있다면 해볼 수도 있으리라."], ["Men sneered at vivisection, and yet look at its results to-day!", "사람들은 생체 해부를 비웃었지만, 오늘날 그 성과를 보라!"], ["Why not advance science in its most difficult and vital aspect--the knowledge of the brain?", "가장 어렵고도 가장 중요한 측면에서, 곧 두뇌에 대한 지식에서 과학을 진전시키지 못할 까닭이 무엇인가?"], ["Had I even the secret of one such mind--did I hold the key to the fancy of even one lunatic--I might advance my own branch of science to a pitch compared with which Burdon-Sanderson’s physiology or Ferrier’s brain-knowledge would be as nothing.", "내가 만일 그런 정신 하나의 비밀만이라도 손에 넣는다면 — 단 한 명의 광인의 공상에 대한 열쇠라도 쥔다면 — 나는 내 분야의 과학을, 버든샌더슨의 생리학이나 페리어의 두뇌 지식 따위가 아무것도 아닐 정도의 경지로까지 끌어올릴 수 있으리라. 충분한 명분만 있다면!"], ["If only there were a sufficient cause!", "이 일을 너무 깊이 생각해서는 안 되겠다."], ["I must not think too much of this, or I may be tempted; a good cause might turn the scale with me, for may not I too be of an exceptional brain, congenitally?", "안 그러면 유혹에 빠질지도 모른다. 충분한 명분이 나에게는 저울을 기울게 할 수도 있으니, 나 역시 선천적으로 비범한 두뇌를 가진 사람일 수도 있지 않은가?"]], [["How well the man reasoned; lunatics always do within their own scope.", "그 사람은 얼마나 잘 추론하는가. 광인들은 언제나 자기 나름의 범위 안에서는 그렇다."], ["I wonder at how many lives he values a man, or if at only one.", "그가 한 사람의 목숨을 몇 개의 생명으로 치는지, 아니면 단 하나로만 치는지 궁금하다."], ["He has closed the account most accurately, and to-day begun a new record.", "그는 회계 장부를 더없이 정확하게 마감하고, 오늘 새 기록을 시작했다."], ["How many of us begin a new record with each day of our lives?", "우리 가운데 몇이나 매일 자기 삶의 하루하루마다 새 기록을 시작하는가?"]], [["To me it seems only yesterday that my whole life ended with my new hope, and that truly I began a new record.", "나에게는 나의 새로운 희망과 더불어 내 삶 전체가 끝나버린 것이 바로 어제 일 같고, 그리하여 나도 참으로 새 기록을 시작한 듯하다."], ["So it will be until the Great Recorder sums me up and closes my ledger account with a balance to profit or loss.", "위대한 기록자께서 나를 결산하시고, 손익의 잔액으로 내 장부를 마감하실 그날까지 그러할 것이다."], ["Oh, Lucy, Lucy, I cannot be angry with you, nor can I be angry with my friend whose happiness is yours; but I must only wait on hopeless and work. Work! work!", "아, 루시여, 루시여, 나는 그대에게 화를 낼 수도 없고, 그대의 행복을 누리는 내 친구에게 화를 낼 수도 없다. 다만 나는 희망 없이 기다리며 일할 수밖에 없다. 일이다! 일이다!"]], [["If I only could have as strong a cause as my poor mad friend there--a good, unselfish cause to make me work--that would be indeed happiness.", "저기 가엾은 미친 친구가 가진 것만큼이나 강한 명분 하나만이라도 나에게 있다면 — 나를 일하게 만들 선하고 사심 없는 명분 하나만 있다면 — 그것이야말로 참된 행복이리라."]], [["Mina Murray’s Journal.", "「미나 머리의 일기」"]], [["26 July.--I am anxious, and it soothes me to express myself here; it is like whispering to one’s self and listening at the same time.", "7월 26일. — 나는 불안하고, 여기에 내 마음을 털어놓으면 위안이 된다. 그것은 마치 자기 자신에게 속삭이면서 동시에 귀 기울여 듣는 것 같다."], ["And there is also something about the shorthand symbols that makes it different from writing. I am unhappy about Lucy and about Jonathan.", "그리고 속기 부호에는 또 무언가, 그냥 글을 쓰는 것과는 다른 데가 있다. 나는 루시 때문에, 그리고 조너선 때문에 마음이 무겁다."], ["I had not heard from Jonathan for some time, and was very concerned; but yesterday dear Mr. Hawkins, who is always so kind, sent me a letter from him.", "한동안 조너선에게서 소식을 듣지 못해 몹시 걱정했는데, 어제 늘 그토록 친절하신 호킨스 씨가 그에게서 온 편지 한 통을 나에게 보내주셨다."], ["I had written asking him if he had heard, and he said the enclosed had just been received.", "나는 그분께 혹시 소식을 들으셨느냐고 편지를 써서 물었었는데, 그분은 동봉한 편지가 방금 도착했다고 하셨다."], ["It is only a line dated from Castle Dracula, and says that he is just starting for home.", "그것은 드라큘라 성에서 부친, 단 한 줄짜리 편지로, 그가 곧 집으로 출발한다는 내용이었다."], ["That is not like Jonathan; I do not understand it, and it makes me uneasy.", "그것은 조너선답지 않다. 나는 그 편지를 이해할 수 없고, 그것이 나를 불안하게 만든다."], ["Then, too, Lucy, although she is so well, has lately taken to her old habit of walking in her sleep.", "게다가 루시는, 그토록 건강한데도, 요즈음 다시 몽유병으로 걷는 옛 버릇이 도졌다."], ["Her mother has spoken to me about it, and we have decided that I am to lock the door of our room every night. Mrs.", "그녀의 어머니가 나에게 그 이야기를 하셨고, 우리는 내가 매일 밤 우리 방문을 잠그기로 정했다."], ["Westenra has got an idea that sleep-walkers always go out on roofs of houses and along the edges of cliffs and then get suddenly wakened and fall over with a despairing cry that echoes all over the place.", "웨스튼라 부인은 몽유병자들이 늘 집 지붕 위나 절벽 가장자리를 따라 나갔다가, 갑자기 잠이 깨어 사방에 메아리치는 절망의 비명을 지르며 떨어진다는 생각을 갖고 계신다."], ["Poor dear, she is naturally anxious about Lucy, and she tells me that her husband, Lucy’s father, had the same habit; that he would get up in the night and dress himself and go out, if he were not stopped.", "가엾은 분, 루시 때문에 걱정하시는 건 당연하다. 그분은 나에게, 자기 남편, 곧 루시의 아버지도 같은 버릇이 있었다고, 막지 않으면 밤중에 일어나 옷을 차려입고 밖으로 나가곤 했다고 말씀하셨다."], ["Lucy is to be married in the autumn, and she is already planning out her dresses and how her house is to be arranged.", "루시는 가을에 결혼할 예정이라, 벌써부터 자기 드레스와 집을 어떻게 꾸밀지를 계획하고 있다. 나도 같은 처지이니 그녀의 마음에 공감한다."], ["I sympathise with her, for I do the same, only Jonathan and I will start in life in a very simple way, and shall have to try to make both ends meet. Mr. Holmwood--he is the Hon.", "다만 조너선과 나는 아주 소박하게 살림을 시작할 것이고, 겨우 수지를 맞추려고 애써야 할 것이다. 홈우드 씨 — 그는 고댈밍 경의 외아들인 아서 홈우드 님인데 — 가 머지않아 이곳으로 올라올 것이다."], ["Arthur Holmwood, only son of Lord Godalming--is coming up here very shortly--as soon as he can leave town, for his father is not very well, and I think dear Lucy is counting the moments till he comes.", "그의 부친이 편찮으셔서 그가 도시를 떠날 수 있게 되는 대로 곧 말이다. 사랑스러운 루시는 그가 올 때까지 그 순간순간을 헤아리고 있는 것 같다."], ["She wants to take him up to the seat on the churchyard cliff and show him the beauty of Whitby.", "그녀는 그를 교회 묘지 절벽의 그 자리로 데려가 휘트비의 아름다움을 보여주고 싶어 한다."], ["I daresay it is the waiting which disturbs her; she will be all right when he arrives.", "짐작건대 그녀를 어지럽히는 것은 바로 그 기다림이리라. 그가 도착하면 그녀는 괜찮아질 것이다."]], [["27 July.--No news from Jonathan.", "7월 27일. — 조너선에게서 소식이 없다."], ["I am getting quite uneasy about him, though why I should I do not know; but I do wish that he would write, if it were only a single line.", "나는 그가 점점 걱정되는데, 왜 그래야 하는지는 모르겠다. 다만 단 한 줄이라도 좋으니 그가 편지를 써주었으면 정말 좋겠다."], ["Lucy walks more than ever, and each night I am awakened by her moving about the room.", "루시는 그 어느 때보다도 많이 걸어 다니고, 매일 밤 그녀가 방 안을 돌아다니는 통에 나는 잠에서 깬다."], ["Fortunately, the weather is so hot that she cannot get cold; but still the anxiety and the perpetually being wakened is beginning to tell on me, and I am getting nervous and wakeful myself.", "다행히 날씨가 너무 더워서 그녀가 감기에 걸릴 일은 없다. 그러나 그래도 이 걱정과 끊임없이 잠이 깨는 것이 나에게 영향을 미치기 시작해, 나 자신도 신경이 곤두서고 잠을 설치게 되었다."], ["Thank God, Lucy’s health keeps up. Mr.", "다행히도 루시의 건강은 잘 유지되고 있다."], ["Holmwood has been suddenly called to Ring to see his father, who has been taken seriously ill.", "홈우드 씨는 위중하게 병이 나신 부친을 뵈러 갑자기 링으로 불려갔다."], ["Lucy frets at the postponement of seeing him, but it does not touch her looks; she is a trifle stouter, and her cheeks are a lovely rose-pink.", "루시는 그를 만나는 일이 미뤄진 것에 애를 태우지만, 그것이 그녀의 용모에는 영향을 미치지 않는다. 그녀는 살이 조금 올랐고, 두 뺨은 사랑스러운 장밋빛이다."], ["She has lost that anæmic look which she had.", "전에 그녀에게 있던 그 빈혈기 도는 모습은 사라졌다."], ["I pray it will all last.", "이 모든 것이 계속되기를 기도한다."]], [["3 August.--Another week gone, and no news from Jonathan, not even to Mr.", "8월 3일. — 또 한 주가 지났는데, 조너선에게서 소식이 없다."], ["Hawkins, from whom I have heard. Oh, I do hope he is not ill.", "내가 소식을 들어온 호킨스 씨에게조차 아무런 연락이 없다. 아, 부디 그가 병이 난 게 아니길 바란다."], ["He surely would have written.", "병이 났다면 분명 편지를 썼을 것이다."], ["I look at that last letter of his, but somehow it does not satisfy me.", "나는 그의 그 마지막 편지를 들여다보지만, 어쩐지 그것이 나를 만족시키지 못한다."], ["It does not read like him, and yet it is his writing.", "그것은 그가 쓴 것 같지가 않은데, 그러면서도 그의 필체다."], ["There is no mistake of that.", "그 점에는 틀림이 없다."], ["Lucy has not walked much in her sleep the last week, but there is an odd concentration about her which I do not understand; even in her sleep she seems to be watching me.", "루시는 지난 한 주 동안 몽유병으로 걷는 일이 많지 않았으나, 그녀에게는 내가 이해할 수 없는 기이한 집중 같은 것이 있다. 잠든 중에도 그녀는 나를 지켜보는 것 같다."], ["She tries the door, and finding it locked, goes about the room searching for the key.", "그녀는 문을 열어보려 하고, 잠겨 있는 것을 알면 열쇠를 찾아 방 안을 돌아다닌다."]], [["6 August.--Another three days, and no news.", "8월 6일. — 또 사흘이 지났는데, 소식이 없다."], ["This suspense is getting dreadful.", "이 불안한 기다림이 점점 끔찍해지고 있다."], ["If I only knew where to write to or where to go to, I should feel easier; but no one has heard a word of Jonathan since that last letter.", "어디로 편지를 쓰거나 어디로 가야 할지만 알아도 마음이 한결 편할 텐데, 그 마지막 편지 이후로 아무도 조너선에 대해 한마디도 듣지 못했다."], ["I must only pray to God for patience.", "나는 그저 하느님께 인내를 달라고 기도할 수밖에 없다."], ["Lucy is more excitable than ever, but is otherwise well.", "루시는 그 어느 때보다도 더 흥분을 잘하지만, 그 밖에는 건강하다."], ["Last night was very threatening, and the fishermen say that we are in for a storm.", "어젯밤은 무척 위협적이었고, 어부들은 우리에게 폭풍이 닥칠 것이라고 말한다."], ["I must try to watch it and learn the weather signs.", "나는 그것을 잘 지켜보고 날씨를 읽는 법을 배워봐야겠다."], ["To-day is a grey day, and the sun as I write is hidden in thick clouds, high over Kettleness.", "오늘은 잿빛 날이고, 내가 글을 쓰는 지금 해는 케틀네스 위 높이 두꺼운 구름 속에 가려져 있다."], ["Everything is grey--except the green grass, which seems like emerald amongst it; grey earthy rock; grey clouds, tinged with the sunburst at the far edge, hang over the grey sea, into which the sand-points stretch like grey fingers.", "모든 것이 잿빛이다 — 그 사이에서 에메랄드처럼 보이는 푸른 풀만 빼고. 잿빛의 흙투성이 바위, 저 먼 가장자리에 햇살이 터져 물든 잿빛 구름이 잿빛 바다 위에 드리워 있고, 그 바다로 모래톱들이 잿빛 손가락처럼 뻗어 있다."], ["The sea is tumbling in over the shallows and the sandy flats with a roar, muffled in the sea-mists drifting inland.", "바다는 얕은 여울과 모래펄 위로 굉음을 내며 밀려드는데, 그 소리가 내륙으로 떠도는 바다 안개에 잠겨 먹먹하다."], ["The horizon is lost in a grey mist.", "수평선은 잿빛 안개 속에 사라졌다."], ["All is vastness; the clouds are piled up like giant rocks, and there is a “brool” over the sea that sounds like some presage of doom.", "모든 것이 광대하다. 구름은 거대한 바위처럼 쌓여 있고, 바다 위에는 어떤 파멸의 전조처럼 들리는 \"웅웅거리는 소리\"가 깔려 있다."], ["Dark figures are on the beach here and there, sometimes half shrouded in the mist, and seem “men like trees walking.”", "검은 형체들이 해변 여기저기에 있는데, 때로는 안개에 반쯤 휩싸여 \"걸어 다니는 나무 같은 사람들\"처럼 보인다."], ["The fishing-boats are racing for home, and rise and dip in the ground swell as they sweep into the harbour, bending to the scuppers.", "고기잡이배들은 집을 향해 내달리며, 항구로 쓸려 들어오면서 너울에 솟구쳤다 가라앉았다 하며 뱃전까지 기울어진다."], ["Here comes old Mr. Swales.", "저기 늙은 스웨일스 씨가 온다."], ["He is making straight for me, and I can see, by the way he lifts his hat, that he wants to talk....", "그는 나를 향해 곧장 오고 있는데, 모자를 들어 올리는 모양새로 보아 이야기를 하고 싶어 한다는 걸 알 수 있다……."]], [["I have been quite touched by the change in the poor old man.", "나는 이 가엾은 노인의 변한 모습에 무척 마음이 뭉클했다."], ["When he sat down beside me, he said in a very gentle way:--", "그는 내 옆에 앉아 매우 부드러운 어조로 말했다."]], [["“I want to say something to you, miss.”", "\"아가씨에게 할 말이 있수.\""], ["I could see he was not at ease, so I took his poor old wrinkled hand in mine and asked him to speak fully; so he said, leaving his hand in mine:--", "나는 그가 마음이 편치 않다는 걸 알아챘으므로, 그의 가엾고 늙어 주름진 손을 내 손으로 잡고 마음껏 이야기해보시라고 청했다. 그러자 그는 자기 손을 내 손에 맡긴 채 말했다."]], [["“I’m afraid, my deary, that I must have shocked you by all the wicked things I’ve been sayin’ about the dead, and such like, for weeks past; but I didn’t mean them, and I want ye to remember that when I’m gone.", "\"미안허우, 아가씨. 내가 지난 몇 주 동안 죽은 자들이니 뭐니 하는 그 못된 소리들을 늘어놓아서 아가씨를 놀라게 했을 텐데. 하지만 그건 진심이 아니었수."], ["We aud folks that be daffled, and with one foot abaft the krok-hooal, don’t altogether like to think of it, and we don’t want to feel scart of it; an’ that’s why I’ve took to makin’ light of it, so that I’d cheer up my own heart a bit.", "내가 떠나고 나거든 그걸 기억해주었으면 허우. 우리 같은 늙은이들은 정신이 흐릿해지고 한 발은 이미 무덤 구덩이에 걸쳐 있어서, 그걸 떠올리는 게 영 달갑지 않고, 또 그걸 무서워하는 것처럼 느끼고 싶지도 않다우. 그래서 내가 그걸 우습게 떠벌리게 된 거지, 그렇게라도 내 마음을 좀 북돋우려고 말이여."], ["But, Lord love ye, miss, I ain’t afraid of dyin’, not a bit; only I don’t want to die if I can help it.", "하지만, 아가씨, 정말이지 나는 죽는 게 무섭지 않수, 조금도. 다만 어쩔 수 없는 게 아니라면 죽고 싶지가 않을 뿐이여."], ["My time must be nigh at hand now, for I be aud, and a hundred years is too much for any man to expect; and I’m so nigh it that the Aud Man is already whettin’ his scythe.", "이제 내 때가 가까웠을 거유, 나도 늙었고, 백 년이란 건 누구라도 바라기엔 너무 긴 세월이니까. 나는 그 백 년에 어찌나 가까워졌는지, 저 위의 어르신이 벌써 큰 낫을 갈고 계신단 말이여."], ["Ye see, I can’t get out o’ the habit of caffin’ about it all at once; the chafts will wag as they be used to.", "알다시피, 나는 이런 걸 한꺼번에 농지거리하는 버릇을 떨쳐내질 못하겠수. 입이라는 건 늘 하던 대로 놀려대니까."], ["Some day soon the Angel of Death will sound his trumpet for me.", "머잖아 어느 날엔가 죽음의 천사가 날 위해 나팔을 불 거유."], ["But don’t ye dooal an’ greet, my deary!”--for he saw that I was crying--“if he should come this very night I’d not refuse to answer his call.", "하지만 슬퍼하거나 울지 마우, 아가씨!\" — 내가 울고 있는 것을 보았기 때문이다 — \"설령 그가 바로 오늘 밤에 온다 해도 나는 그 부름에 응하기를 마다하지 않을 거유."], ["For life be, after all, only a waitin’ for somethin’ else than what we’re doin’; and death be all that we can rightly depend on.", "삶이란 건 어쨌거나 우리가 하고 있는 그것 말고 다른 무언가를 기다리는 것일 뿐이고, 죽음이야말로 우리가 마땅히 믿고 의지할 수 있는 전부니까."], ["But I’m content, for it’s comin’ to me, my deary, and comin’ quick.", "하지만 나는 만족허우, 그게 나한테 오고 있고, 그것도 빨리 오고 있으니까 말이여, 아가씨."], ["It may be comin’ while we be lookin’ and wonderin’.", "어쩌면 우리가 이렇게 바라보며 궁금해하는 동안에도 오고 있는지 모르지."], ["Maybe it’s in that wind out over the sea that’s bringin’ with it loss and wreck, and sore distress, and sad hearts. Look! look!”", "어쩌면 저 바다 너머에서 불어오는, 상실과 난파와 쓰라린 고통과 슬픈 마음을 함께 싣고 오는 저 바람 속에 있는지도 모르고. 보우! 보우!\""], ["he cried suddenly.", "그가 갑자기 소리쳤다."], ["“There’s something in that wind and in the hoast beyont that sounds, and looks, and tastes, and smells like death.", "\"저 바람 속에, 그리고 저 너머 안개 속에 무언가가 있는데, 그게 죽음처럼 들리고, 보이고, 맛이 나고, 냄새가 나는구먼. 그게 공기 중에 있어."], ["It’s in the air; I feel it comin’.", "나는 그게 오는 게 느껴진다우."], ["Lord, make me answer cheerful when my call comes!”", "주여, 제 부름이 올 때 제가 기쁘게 응답하게 하소서!\""], ["He held up his arms devoutly, and raised his hat.", "그는 경건하게 두 팔을 들어 올리고 모자를 벗었다."], ["His mouth moved as though he were praying.", "마치 기도라도 하듯 입술이 움직였다."], ["After a few minutes’ silence, he got up, shook hands with me, and blessed me, and said good-bye, and hobbled off. It all touched me, and upset me very much.", "몇 분간의 침묵 끝에 그는 일어나 나와 악수하고, 나를 축복하고, 작별을 고하더니 절뚝거리며 떠나갔다. 이 모든 것이 나를 뭉클하게 했고, 또 무척 심란하게 만들었다."]], [["I was glad when the coastguard came along, with his spy-glass under his arm.", "겨드랑이에 망원경을 끼고 해안 경비원이 다가왔을 때 나는 반가웠다."], ["He stopped to talk with me, as he always does, but all the time kept looking at a strange ship.", "그는 늘 그렇듯 멈춰 서서 나와 이야기를 나누었지만, 줄곧 어떤 이상한 배 한 척을 바라보고 있었다."]], [["“I can’t make her out,”", "\"저 배를 도무지 알 수가 없네요,\""], ["he said; “she’s a Russian, by the look of her; but she’s knocking about in the queerest way.", "그가 말했다. \"생긴 걸로 봐선 러시아 배인데, 아주 괴상하게 이리저리 흔들리고 있어요."], ["She doesn’t know her mind a bit; she seems to see the storm coming, but can’t decide whether to run up north in the open, or to put in here.", "도무지 제 갈 길을 정하지 못하고 있군요. 폭풍이 오는 걸 아는 것 같은데, 북쪽 먼바다로 올라갈지 여기로 들어올지를 결정하지 못하는 모양입니다."], ["Look there again!", "저기 또 보세요!"], ["She is steered mighty strangely, for she doesn’t mind the hand on the wheel; changes about with every puff of wind.", "키를 어찌나 괴상하게 잡았는지, 키잡이의 손을 통 따르질 않아요. 바람이 한 번 불 때마다 방향을 바꿔버립니다."], ["We’ll hear more of her before this time to-morrow.”", "내일 이맘때가 되기 전에 저 배에 대해 더 듣게 될 겁니다.\""]], [["(Pasted in Mina Murray’s Journal.)", "(미나 머리의 일기에 붙여둠.)"]], [["From a Correspondent.", "본지 통신원 보도."]], [["Whitby.", "휘트비."]], [["One of the greatest and suddenest storms on record has just been experienced here, with results both strange and unique.", "기록에 남은 가장 크고 가장 갑작스러운 폭풍 가운데 하나가 방금 이곳에서 지나갔는데, 그 결과 또한 기이하고 유례없는 것이었다."], ["The weather had been somewhat sultry, but not to any degree uncommon in the month of August.", "날씨는 다소 후텁지근했으나, 8월에 결코 드문 정도는 아니었다."], ["Saturday evening was as fine as was ever known, and the great body of holiday-makers laid out yesterday for visits to Mulgrave Woods, Robin Hood’s Bay, Rig Mill, Runswick, Staithes, and the various trips in the neighbourhood of Whitby.", "토요일 저녁은 일찍이 없었을 만큼 청명했고, 휴일을 즐기려는 사람들의 큰 무리가 어제 멀그레이브 숲, 로빈 후드 만, 리그 밀, 런즈윅, 스테이스, 그리고 휘트비 인근의 여러 행락지를 찾아 나섰다."], ["The steamers Emma and Scarborough made trips up and down the coast, and there was an unusual amount of “tripping” both to and from Whitby.", "증기선 에마호와 스카버러호는 해안을 오르내리며 유람을 했고, 휘트비를 오가는 \"나들이\"가 유난히 많았다."], ["The day was unusually fine till the afternoon, when some of the gossips who frequent the East Cliff churchyard, and from that commanding eminence watch the wide sweep of sea visible to the north and east, called attention to a sudden show of “mares’-tails”", "오후가 되기 전까지 날씨는 유난히 청명했는데, 그때 이스트 클리프 묘지를 자주 찾아 그 높직한 자리에서 북쪽과 동쪽으로 트인 드넓은 바다를 바라보던 한담객 몇이, 북서쪽 하늘 높이 갑자기 나타난 \"암말 꼬리\""], ["high in the sky to the north-west.", "구름에 사람들의 주의를 환기시켰다."], ["The wind was then blowing from the south-west in the mild degree which in barometrical language is ranked “No. 2: light breeze.”", "그때 바람은 남서쪽에서 불고 있었는데, 기압계 용어로 \"2급: 가벼운 산들바람\"으로 분류되는 온순한 정도였다."], ["The coastguard on duty at once made report, and one old fisherman, who for more than half a century has kept watch on weather signs from the East Cliff, foretold in an emphatic manner the coming of a sudden storm.", "근무 중이던 해안경비대원이 즉시 보고했고, 반세기가 넘도록 이스트 클리프에서 날씨의 징후를 살펴온 한 늙은 어부는 갑작스러운 폭풍이 닥치리라고 단호한 어조로 예언했다."], ["The approach of sunset was so very beautiful, so grand in its masses of splendidly-coloured clouds, that there was quite an assemblage on the walk along the cliff in the old churchyard to enjoy the beauty.", "해질녘이 다가오는 모습이 어찌나 아름답고, 찬란한 빛깔의 구름 덩이가 어찌나 장엄했던지, 그 아름다움을 즐기려고 옛 묘지의 절벽길에는 제법 많은 사람이 모여들었다."], ["Before the sun dipped below the black mass of Kettleness, standing boldly athwart the western sky, its downward way was marked by myriad clouds of every sunset-colour--flame, purple, pink, green, violet, and all the tints of gold; with here and there masses not large, but of seemingly absolute blackness, in all sorts of shapes, as well outlined as colossal silhouettes.", "서쪽 하늘을 가로질러 대담하게 솟아 있는 케틀네스의 검은 덩어리 아래로 해가 잠기기 전에, 그 지는 길은 온갖 노을빛의 무수한 구름으로 수놓였다 — 불꽃빛, 자줏빛, 분홍빛, 초록빛, 보랏빛, 그리고 온갖 금빛으로."], ["The experience was not lost on the painters, and doubtless some of the sketches of the “Prelude to the Great Storm” will grace the R. A. and R. I.", "군데군데 크지는 않으나 거의 완전한 검정으로 보이는 덩어리들이, 거대한 실루엣처럼 또렷한 윤곽을 드러내며 갖가지 형상으로 떠 있었다."], ["walls in May next. More than one captain made up his mind then and there that his “cobble” or his “mule,”", "이 광경은 화가들에게 헛되지 않았으니, 분명 \"대폭풍의 서곡\"을 담은 몇몇 스케치가 내년 5월 왕립미술원과 왕립수채화협회의 벽을 장식하게 될 것이다."], ["as they term the different classes of boats, would remain in the harbour till the storm had passed. The wind fell away entirely during the evening, and at midnight there was a dead calm, a sultry heat, and that prevailing intensity which, on the approach of thunder, affects persons of a sensitive nature.", "한 사람 이상의 선장이 그 자리에서, 그들이 배의 종류에 따라 부르는 \"코블\"이나 \"뮬\"을 폭풍이 지나갈 때까지 항구에 남겨두기로 마음을 굳혔다. 저녁 내내 바람은 완전히 잦아들었고, 자정에는 죽은 듯한 고요와 후텁지근한 열기, 그리고 천둥이 다가올 때 예민한 사람들에게 영향을 미치는 그 짙은 긴장감이 감돌았다."], ["There were but few lights in sight at sea, for even the coasting steamers, which usually “hug” the shore so closely, kept well to seaward, and but few fishing-boats were in sight.", "바다에 보이는 불빛은 몇 개 되지 않았으니, 보통은 해안에 바짝 붙어 다니던 연안 증기선들조차 바다 쪽으로 멀찍이 떨어져 있었고, 고깃배도 거의 보이지 않았기 때문이다."], ["The only sail noticeable was a foreign schooner with all sails set, which was seemingly going westwards.", "눈에 띄는 유일한 돛은 돛을 모두 펼친 외국 스쿠너선이었는데, 보아하니 서쪽으로 가고 있었다."], ["The foolhardiness or ignorance of her officers was a prolific theme for comment whilst she remained in sight, and efforts were made to signal her to reduce sail in face of her danger.", "그 배가 시야에 머무는 동안 선원들의 무모함 혹은 무지함은 끝없는 논평의 소재가 되었고, 위험을 무릅쓰지 말고 돛을 줄이라는 신호를 보내려는 시도도 있었다."], ["Before the night shut down she was seen with sails idly flapping as she gently rolled on the undulating swell of the sea,", "밤이 완전히 내려앉기 전, 그 배는 일렁이는 너울 위에서 가만히 흔들리며 돛을 하릴없이 펄럭이는 모습으로 마지막에 보였다,"]], [["“As idle as a painted ship upon a painted ocean.”", "\"그려놓은 바다 위 그려놓은 배처럼 하릴없이.\""]], [["Shortly before ten o’clock the stillness of the air grew quite oppressive, and the silence was so marked that the bleating of a sheep inland or the barking of a dog in the town was distinctly heard, and the band on the pier, with its lively French air, was like a discord in the great harmony of nature’s silence.", "열 시가 조금 못 되어 대기의 고요는 자못 숨막힐 지경이 되었고, 그 적막이 어찌나 또렷했던지 내륙에서 양이 우는 소리나 마을의 개가 짖는 소리가 또렷이 들렸으며, 부두의 악단이 연주하는 흥겨운 프랑스 곡조는 자연의 침묵이 빚는 거대한 조화 속의 불협화음 같았다."], ["A little after midnight came a strange sound from over the sea, and high overhead the air began to carry a strange, faint, hollow booming.", "자정이 조금 지나자 바다 너머에서 기이한 소리가 들려왔고, 머리 위 높은 곳의 대기가 기이하고 희미하며 공허한 우르릉 소리를 실어 나르기 시작했다."]], [["Then without warning the tempest broke.", "그러더니 아무런 예고도 없이 폭풍이 터졌다."], ["With a rapidity which, at the time, seemed incredible, and even afterwards is impossible to realize, the whole aspect of nature at once became convulsed.", "그 당시에는 믿기 어려웠고, 나중에도 도무지 실감할 수 없는 빠르기로, 자연의 모습 전체가 일순간 격렬하게 뒤틀렸다."], ["The waves rose in growing fury, each overtopping its fellow, till in a very few minutes the lately glassy sea was like a roaring and devouring monster.", "파도는 점점 더 사납게 솟구쳐 저마다 앞의 것을 타넘었고, 불과 몇 분도 지나지 않아 방금까지 유리알 같던 바다는 포효하며 집어삼키는 괴물처럼 변했다."], ["White-crested waves beat madly on the level sands and rushed up the shelving cliffs; others broke over the piers, and with their spume swept the lanthorns of the lighthouses which rise from the end of either pier of Whitby Harbour.", "흰 물마루의 파도가 평평한 모래밭을 미친 듯이 후려치고 비탈진 절벽을 타고 솟구쳤다. 또 어떤 것들은 부두를 넘어, 그 물보라로 휘트비 항 양쪽 부두 끝에 솟은 등대의 등불을 휩쓸었다."], ["The wind roared like thunder, and blew with such force that it was with difficulty that even strong men kept their feet, or clung with grim clasp to the iron stanchions.", "바람은 천둥처럼 포효했고, 어찌나 세차게 불었던지 건장한 사내들조차 발을 디디고 서거나 쇠기둥을 단단히 부여잡기조차 힘들 지경이었다."], ["It was found necessary to clear the entire piers from the mass of onlookers, or else the fatalities of the night would have been increased manifold.", "구경꾼 무리를 부두 전체에서 물러나게 할 필요가 생겼으니, 그러지 않았다면 그 밤의 사망자가 몇 곱절로 늘어났을 것이다."], ["To add to the difficulties and dangers of the time, masses of sea-fog came drifting inland--white, wet clouds, which swept by in ghostly fashion, so dank and damp and cold that it needed but little effort of imagination to think that the spirits of those lost at sea were touching their living brethren with the clammy hands of death, and many a one shuddered as the wreaths of sea-mist swept by.", "그때의 어려움과 위험을 더하려는 듯, 바다 안개 덩어리가 내륙으로 떠밀려 들어왔다 — 희고 축축한 구름이 유령처럼 휩쓸고 지나갔는데, 어찌나 눅눅하고 축축하고 차가웠던지, 바다에서 목숨을 잃은 자들의 혼령이 죽음의 끈적한 손으로 살아 있는 형제들을 어루만지는 것이라 상상하기에 그리 큰 상상력도 필요치 않았으며, 바다 안개의 자락이 휩쓸고 지날 때마다 적잖은 사람들이 몸서리쳤다."], ["At times the mist cleared, and the sea for some distance could be seen in the glare of the lightning, which now came thick and fast, followed by such sudden peals of thunder that the whole sky overhead seemed trembling under the shock of the footsteps of the storm.", "이따금 안개가 걷히면, 이제 빽빽하고 빠르게 내리치는 번개의 섬광 속에 어느 정도 거리의 바다가 보였고, 그 뒤를 이어 그토록 갑작스러운 천둥소리가 따라오니 머리 위 하늘 전체가 폭풍의 발걸음이 주는 충격에 떨고 있는 듯했다."]], [["Some of the scenes thus revealed were of immeasurable grandeur and of absorbing interest--the sea, running mountains high, threw skywards with each wave mighty masses of white foam, which the tempest seemed to snatch at and whirl away into space; here and there a fishing-boat, with a rag of sail, running madly for shelter before the blast; now and again the white wings of a storm-tossed sea-bird.", "그렇게 드러난 광경 가운데 어떤 것들은 헤아릴 수 없이 장엄하고 마음을 사로잡을 만큼 흥미로웠다 — 산처럼 높이 솟구치는 바다는 파도마다 거대한 흰 거품 덩어리를 하늘로 내던졌고, 폭풍은 그것을 낚아채어 허공으로 휘몰아가는 듯했다. 군데군데 누더기 같은 돛을 단 고깃배가 돌풍을 피해 피난처를 향해 미친 듯이 내달렸고, 이따금 폭풍에 시달리는 바닷새의 흰 날개가 보였다."], ["On the summit of the East Cliff the new searchlight was ready for experiment, but had not yet been tried.", "이스트 클리프 꼭대기에는 새 탐조등이 시험할 준비가 되어 있었으나, 아직 가동된 적은 없었다."], ["The officers in charge of it got it into working order, and in the pauses of the inrushing mist swept with it the surface of the sea.", "그것을 맡은 대원들이 작동 상태로 만들어, 안개가 밀려드는 틈틈이 그 빛으로 바다 표면을 훑었다."], ["Once or twice its service was most effective, as when a fishing-boat, with gunwale under water, rushed into the harbour, able, by the guidance of the sheltering light, to avoid the danger of dashing against the piers.", "한두 번은 그 빛이 더없이 효과적이었으니, 뱃전이 물에 잠긴 고깃배 한 척이 항구로 내달려 들어올 때, 비추어 주는 빛의 인도 덕분에 부두에 부딪히는 위험을 피할 수 있었던 경우가 그러했다."], ["As each boat achieved the safety of the port there was a shout of joy from the mass of people on shore, a shout which for a moment seemed to cleave the gale and was then swept away in its rush.", "배 한 척이 항구의 안전에 다다를 때마다 해안의 군중 속에서 기쁨의 함성이 터졌는데, 그 함성은 한순간 강풍을 가르는 듯하다가 이내 그 거센 흐름에 휩쓸려 사라졌다."]], [["Before long the searchlight discovered some distance away a schooner with all sails set, apparently the same vessel which had been noticed earlier in the evening.", "이윽고 탐조등이 멀찍이 떨어진 곳에서 돛을 모두 펼친 스쿠너선 한 척을 찾아냈는데, 보아하니 저녁 일찍 눈에 띄었던 바로 그 배였다."], ["The wind had by this time backed to the east, and there was a shudder amongst the watchers on the cliff as they realized the terrible danger in which she now was.", "그 무렵 바람은 동쪽으로 돌아 있었고, 절벽 위의 구경꾼들은 그 배가 이제 처한 무서운 위험을 깨닫고 몸서리쳤다."], ["Between her and the port lay the great flat reef on which so many good ships have from time to time suffered, and, with the wind blowing from its present quarter, it would be quite impossible that she should fetch the entrance of the harbour.", "그 배와 항구 사이에는, 숱한 좋은 배들이 때때로 화를 입었던 그 거대한 평평한 암초가 가로놓여 있었고, 지금 부는 방향의 바람으로는 그 배가 항구 입구에 닿기란 전혀 불가능할 터였다."], ["It was now nearly the hour of high tide, but the waves were so great that in their troughs the shallows of the shore were almost visible, and the schooner, with all sails set, was rushing with such speed that, in the words of one old salt, “she must fetch up somewhere, if it was only in hell.”", "이제 거의 만조 시각이었으나, 파도가 어찌나 컸던지 그 골짜기 사이로 해안의 얕은 바닥이 거의 드러나 보일 지경이었고, 돛을 모두 펼친 그 스쿠너선은 어찌나 빠르게 내달리는지, 어느 늙은 뱃사람의 말마따나 \"어디든 가서 처박힐 게 분명한데, 지옥이라도 마다 않을 기세\"였다."], ["Then came another rush of sea-fog, greater than any hitherto--a mass of dank mist, which seemed to close on all things like a grey pall, and left available to men only the organ of hearing, for the roar of the tempest, and the crash of the thunder, and the booming of the mighty billows came through the damp oblivion even louder than before.", "그때 또 한 번 바다 안개가 밀려들었는데, 지금까지의 그 무엇보다 더 짙었다 — 잿빛 장막처럼 만물을 덮어버리는 듯한 눅눅한 안개 덩어리로, 사람들에게 남겨진 것은 오로지 청각 기관뿐이었으니, 폭풍의 포효와 천둥의 굉음과 거대한 물결의 우르릉 소리가 그 축축한 망각 속을 뚫고 전보다 더욱 크게 들려왔기 때문이다."], ["The rays of the searchlight were kept fixed on the harbour mouth across the East Pier, where the shock was expected, and men waited breathless.", "탐조등의 광선은 충돌이 예상되는 이스트 피어 너머 항구 어귀에 고정되었고, 사람들은 숨죽여 기다렸다."], ["The wind suddenly shifted to the north-east, and the remnant of the sea-fog melted in the blast; and then, mirabile dictu, between the piers, leaping from wave to wave as it rushed at headlong speed, swept the strange schooner before the blast, with all sail set, and gained the safety of the harbour.", "바람이 갑자기 북동쪽으로 방향을 틀었고, 남은 바다 안개가 그 돌풍에 녹아 사라졌다. 그러자, 참으로 놀랍게도, 두 부두 사이로, 파도에서 파도로 뛰어넘으며 곤두박질치는 속도로 내달려, 그 기이한 스쿠너선이 돛을 모두 펼친 채 돌풍에 떠밀려 들어와 항구의 안전에 다다랐다."], ["The searchlight followed her, and a shudder ran through all who saw her, for lashed to the helm was a corpse, with drooping head, which swung horribly to and fro at each motion of the ship.", "탐조등이 그 배를 따라갔고, 그 배를 본 모든 이가 몸서리쳤으니, 키에 묶여 있는 것이 시체였기 때문이다. 머리를 늘어뜨린 그 시체는 배가 움직일 때마다 끔찍하게 이리저리 흔들렸다."], ["No other form could be seen on deck at all.", "갑판 위에는 다른 어떤 형체도 전혀 보이지 않았다."], ["A great awe came on all as they realised that the ship, as if by a miracle, had found the harbour, unsteered save by the hand of a dead man!", "그 배가 기적이라도 일어난 듯, 죽은 사람의 손 외에는 아무도 조종하지 않은 채 항구를 찾아 들어왔음을 깨닫자 모든 이에게 커다란 경외가 엄습했다!"], ["However, all took place more quickly than it takes to write these words.", "그러나 이 모든 일은 이 글을 쓰는 것보다도 더 빠르게 일어났다."], ["The schooner paused not, but rushing across the harbour, pitched herself on that accumulation of sand and gravel washed by many tides and many storms into the south-east corner of the pier jutting under the East Cliff, known locally as Tate Hill Pier.", "그 스쿠너선은 멈추지 않고, 항구를 가로질러 내달리더니, 이스트 클리프 아래로 돌출한 부두의 남동쪽 모퉁이, 곧 이 고장에서 테이트 힐 피어라 불리는 곳에 숱한 조수와 숱한 폭풍이 쓸어다 쌓은 그 모래와 자갈 더미 위에 처박혔다."]], [["There was of course a considerable concussion as the vessel drove up on the sand heap.", "배가 모래 더미 위로 올라타며 당연히 상당한 충격이 있었다."], ["Every spar, rope, and stay was strained, and some of the “top-hammer” came crashing down.", "모든 활대와 밧줄과 버팀줄이 팽팽히 당겨졌고, 윗부분의 삭구 일부가 무너져 내렸다."], ["But, strangest of all, the very instant the shore was touched, an immense dog sprang up on deck from below, as if shot up by the concussion, and running forward, jumped from the bow on the sand.", "그러나 무엇보다 기이한 것은, 배가 해안에 닿는 바로 그 순간, 거대한 개 한 마리가 충격에 튕겨 오르기라도 한 듯 아래에서 갑판 위로 솟구쳐 올라, 앞으로 달려가더니 뱃머리에서 모래 위로 뛰어내린 것이다."], ["Making straight for the steep cliff, where the churchyard hangs over the laneway to the East Pier so steeply that some of the flat tombstones--“thruff-steans” or “through-stones,”", "그 개는 가파른 절벽을 향해 곧장 내달렸는데, 그곳은 묘지가 이스트 피어로 이어지는 좁은 길 위로 어찌나 가파르게 매달려 있는지 평평한 묘석들 가운데 일부 — 휘트비 방언으로 \"트러프스턴스\""], ["as they call them in the Whitby vernacular--actually project over where the sustaining cliff has fallen away, it disappeared in the darkness, which seemed intensified just beyond the focus of the searchlight.", "혹은 \"스루스톤스\"라 부르는 것들 — 가 떠받치던 절벽이 무너져 내린 자리 위로 실제로 비죽 튀어나와 있는 곳이었다. 개는 어둠 속으로 사라졌는데, 그 어둠은 탐조등 초점 바로 너머에서 한층 짙어진 듯했다."]], [["It so happened that there was no one at the moment on Tate Hill Pier, as all those whose houses are in close proximity were either in bed or were out on the heights above.", "마침 그때 테이트 힐 피어에는 아무도 없었으니, 그 근처에 집을 둔 사람들은 모두 잠자리에 들었거나 위쪽 언덕에 나가 있었기 때문이다."], ["Thus the coastguard on duty on the eastern side of the harbour, who at once ran down to the little pier, was the first to climb on board.", "그리하여 항구 동쪽에서 근무하던 해안경비대원이 즉시 그 작은 부두로 달려 내려와 가장 먼저 배에 올랐다."], ["The men working the searchlight, after scouring the entrance of the harbour without seeing anything, then turned the light on the derelict and kept it there.", "탐조등을 다루던 대원들은 항구 어귀를 샅샅이 훑었으나 아무것도 보지 못하자, 이번에는 그 버려진 배에 빛을 돌려 그곳에 고정했다."], ["The coastguard ran aft, and when he came beside the wheel, bent over to examine it, and recoiled at once as though under some sudden emotion.", "해안경비대원이 고물 쪽으로 달려가 키 옆에 이르러 그것을 살피려 몸을 숙였다가, 어떤 갑작스러운 감정에 사로잡히기라도 한 듯 즉시 흠칫 물러섰다."], ["This seemed to pique general curiosity, and quite a number of people began to run.", "이것이 사람들의 호기심을 자극한 듯, 제법 많은 이들이 달려가기 시작했다."], ["It is a good way round from the West Cliff by the Drawbridge to Tate Hill Pier, but your correspondent is a fairly good runner, and came well ahead of the crowd.", "웨스트 클리프에서 도개교를 거쳐 테이트 힐 피어까지는 빙 돌아가는 먼 길이지만, 본지 통신원은 제법 잘 달리는 편이라 군중보다 한참 앞서 도착했다."], ["When I arrived, however, I found already assembled on the pier a crowd, whom the coastguard and police refused to allow to come on board.", "그러나 내가 당도했을 때 이미 부두에는 군중이 모여 있었는데, 해안경비대와 경찰이 그들이 배에 오르는 것을 막고 있었다."], ["By the courtesy of the chief boatman, I was, as your correspondent, permitted to climb on deck, and was one of a small group who saw the dead seaman whilst actually lashed to the wheel.", "경비대장의 호의로, 나는 본지 통신원 자격으로 갑판에 오르도록 허락받아, 키에 실제로 묶여 있는 그 죽은 뱃사람을 본 소수의 무리 가운데 하나가 되었다."]], [["It was no wonder that the coastguard was surprised, or even awed, for not often can such a sight have been seen.", "해안경비대원이 놀란 것도, 아니 경외감마저 느낀 것도 이상한 일이 아니었으니, 그런 광경을 볼 수 있는 일은 흔치 않기 때문이다."], ["The man was simply fastened by his hands, tied one over the other, to a spoke of the wheel.", "그 사내는 그저 두 손이 한쪽이 다른 쪽 위로 포개진 채 키의 살에 묶여 있었다."], ["Between the inner hand and the wood was a crucifix, the set of beads on which it was fastened being around both wrists and wheel, and all kept fast by the binding cords.", "안쪽 손과 나무 사이에는 십자고상이 있었고, 그것이 매달린 묵주 알들이 양쪽 손목과 키를 함께 둘러 묶었으며, 그 모두가 동여맨 끈으로 단단히 고정되어 있었다."], ["The poor fellow may have been seated at one time, but the flapping and buffeting of the sails had worked through the rudder of the wheel and dragged him to and fro, so that the cords with which he was tied had cut the flesh to the bone.", "그 가엾은 사내는 한때 앉아 있었을지도 모르나, 돛이 펄럭이고 들이치면서 키의 방향타까지 그 힘이 미쳐 그를 이리저리 끌어당겼던 탓에, 그를 묶은 끈이 살을 뼈까지 파고들어 있었다."], ["Accurate note was made of the state of things, and a doctor--Surgeon J. M. Caffyn, of 33, East Elliot Place--who came immediately after me, declared, after making examination, that the man must have been dead for quite two days.", "사물의 상태에 대해 정확한 기록이 작성되었고, 나보다 곧바로 뒤이어 도착한 의사 — 이스트 엘리엇 플레이스 33번지의 외과의 J. M. 캐핀 — 가 검진을 마친 뒤, 그 사내가 죽은 지 족히 이틀은 되었을 것이라고 단언했다."], ["In his pocket was a bottle, carefully corked, empty save for a little roll of paper, which proved to be the addendum to the log.", "그의 주머니에는 조심스레 코르크로 막은 병 하나가 있었는데, 작게 둘둘 만 종이쪽지 하나 외에는 비어 있었고, 그 종이는 항해일지의 부록임이 드러났다."], ["The coastguard said the man must have tied up his own hands, fastening the knots with his teeth.", "해안경비대원은 그 사내가 제 손으로 자기 손을 묶고 이로 매듭을 조였을 것이라고 말했다."], ["The fact that a coastguard was the first on board may save some complications, later on, in the Admiralty Court; for coastguards cannot claim the salvage which is the right of the first civilian entering on a derelict.", "해안경비대원이 가장 먼저 배에 오른 사실은, 나중에 해사법원에서 얼마간의 분쟁을 덜어줄지도 모른다. 해안경비대원은 버려진 배에 가장 먼저 오른 민간인에게 주어지는 권리인 구난 보수를 청구할 수 없기 때문이다."], ["Already, however, the legal tongues are wagging, and one young law student is loudly asserting that the rights of the owner are already completely sacrificed, his property being held in contravention of the statutes of mortmain, since the tiller, as emblemship, if not proof, of delegated possession, is held in a dead hand.", "그러나 벌써부터 법조계 사람들이 입방아를 찧기 시작했고, 한 젊은 법학도는 소유주의 권리가 이미 완전히 박탈되었다고 큰 소리로 주장했는데, 위임된 점유의 증거까지는 아니라도 그 상징인 키가 죽은 손에 쥐여 있으니 그 재산이 영구양도금지법에 위배되는 방식으로 점유되고 있다는 것이었다."], ["It is needless to say that the dead steersman has been reverently removed from the place where he held his honourable watch and ward till death--a steadfastness as noble as that of the young Casabianca--and placed in the mortuary to await inquest.", "그 죽은 키잡이가, 죽음에 이르기까지 그 명예로운 경계와 수호의 자리를 지켰던 — 어린 카사비안카의 그것에 못지않게 고결한 그 굳건함을 보이며 — 그 자리에서 정중히 옮겨져, 검시를 기다리도록 영안실에 안치되었음은 굳이 말할 필요도 없다."]], [["Already the sudden storm is passing, and its fierceness is abating; crowds are scattering homeward, and the sky is beginning to redden over the Yorkshire wolds.", "벌써 그 갑작스러운 폭풍은 지나가고 있고, 그 맹위도 수그러들고 있다. 군중은 집으로 흩어지고 있으며, 요크셔 구릉지 위로 하늘이 붉게 물들기 시작한다."], ["I shall send, in time for your next issue, further details of the derelict ship which found her way so miraculously into harbour in the storm.", "다음 호에 늦지 않게, 폭풍 속에 그토록 기적적으로 항구를 찾아든 그 버려진 배에 관한 더 자세한 내용을 보내겠다."]], [["Whitby", "휘트비."]], [["9 August.--The sequel to the strange arrival of the derelict in the storm last night is almost more startling than the thing itself.", "8월 9일. — 어젯밤 폭풍 속에 버려진 배가 기이하게 도착한 일의 후일담은 그 일 자체보다도 거의 더 놀랍다."], ["It turns out that the schooner is a Russian from Varna, and is called the Demeter.", "알고 보니 그 스쿠너선은 바르나에서 온 러시아 배로, 이름은 데메테르호였다."], ["She is almost entirely in ballast of silver sand, with only a small amount of cargo--a number of great wooden boxes filled with mould.", "배는 거의 전부 은모래 바닥짐을 실었고, 화물은 소량뿐이었다 — 흙을 가득 채운 커다란 나무 상자 여러 개였다."], ["This cargo was consigned to a Whitby solicitor, Mr. S. F.", "이 화물은 더 크레슨트 7번지의 휘트비 변호사 S. F."], ["Billington, of 7, The Crescent, who this morning went aboard and formally took possession of the goods consigned to him.", "빌링턴 씨 앞으로 탁송된 것이었는데, 그는 오늘 아침 배에 올라 자기 앞으로 탁송된 물품을 정식으로 인수했다."], ["The Russian consul, too, acting for the charter-party, took formal possession of the ship, and paid all harbour dues, etc.", "러시아 영사 또한 용선계약 당사자를 대리하여 정식으로 배를 인수하고, 항만세 등 일체를 납부했다."], ["Nothing is talked about here to-day except the strange coincidence; the officials of the Board of Trade have been most exacting in seeing that every compliance has been made with existing regulations.", "오늘 이곳에서는 그 기이한 우연 말고는 화젯거리가 없다. 무역위원회 관리들은 현행 규정이 빠짐없이 준수되었는지를 매우 까다롭게 확인했다."], ["As the matter is to be a “nine days’ wonder,” they are evidently determined that there shall be no cause of after complaint.", "이 일이 \"아흐레 동안의 화제\"가 될 것이므로, 그들은 분명 나중에 불평이 나올 빌미를 남기지 않으려고 단단히 마음먹은 것이다."], ["A good deal of interest was abroad concerning the dog which landed when the ship struck, and more than a few of the members of the S. P. C. A., which is very strong in Whitby, have tried to befriend the animal.", "배가 좌초할 때 뭍에 내린 그 개를 두고 적잖은 관심이 일었고, 휘트비에서 꽤 세력이 큰 동물학대방지협회 회원 가운데 적잖은 이들이 그 짐승을 거두려고 애썼다."], ["To the general disappointment, however, it was not to be found; it seems to have disappeared entirely from the town.", "그러나 모두의 실망스럽게도, 개는 찾을 수 없었다. 마을에서 완전히 사라져버린 듯했다."], ["It may be that it was frightened and made its way on to the moors, where it is still hiding in terror.", "어쩌면 겁에 질려 황야로 달아나, 아직도 거기 공포에 떨며 숨어 있는지도 모른다."], ["There are some who look with dread on such a possibility, lest later on it should in itself become a danger, for it is evidently a fierce brute.", "이런 가능성을 두려운 눈으로 보는 이들도 있으니, 나중에 그 개가 그 자체로 위험거리가 될까 해서다. 분명 사나운 짐승이기 때문이다."], ["Early this morning a large dog, a half-bred mastiff belonging to a coal merchant close to Tate Hill Pier, was found dead in the roadway opposite to its master’s yard.", "오늘 이른 아침, 테이트 힐 피어 가까이 사는 석탄 상인의 큰 개, 잡종 마스티프 한 마리가 주인의 마당 맞은편 길바닥에서 죽은 채 발견되었다."], ["It had been fighting, and manifestly had had a savage opponent, for its throat was torn away, and its belly was slit open as if with a savage claw.", "그 개는 싸움을 벌였고, 분명 사나운 상대를 만났던 것이니, 목이 찢겨 나갔고 배가 마치 사나운 발톱에 베인 듯 갈라져 있었기 때문이다."]], [["Later.--By the kindness of the Board of Trade inspector, I have been permitted to look over the log-book of the Demeter, which was in order up to within three days, but contained nothing of special interest except as to facts of missing men.", "추가. — 무역위원회 검사관의 호의로, 나는 데메테르호의 항해일지를 살펴볼 수 있었는데, 그것은 사흘 전까지는 정연했으나, 사라진 사람들에 관한 사실 외에는 특별히 흥미로운 내용이 없었다."], ["The greatest interest, however, is with regard to the paper found in the bottle, which was to-day produced at the inquest; and a more strange narrative than the two between them unfold it has not been my lot to come across.", "그러나 가장 흥미로운 것은 병 속에서 발견된 종이쪽지에 관한 것으로, 그것은 오늘 검시에서 공개되었다. 그 둘이 함께 펼쳐 보이는 것보다 더 기이한 이야기를 나는 일찍이 마주친 적이 없다."], ["As there is no motive for concealment, I am permitted to use them, and accordingly send you a rescript, simply omitting technical details of seamanship and supercargo.", "감출 동기가 없으므로 나는 그것을 쓰도록 허락받았고, 이에 따라 항해술과 적하감독에 관한 전문적인 세부사항만 생략한 채 그 사본을 보낸다."], ["It almost seems as though the captain had been seized with some kind of mania before he had got well into blue water, and that this had developed persistently throughout the voyage.", "거의 마치 선장이 깊은 바다에 채 들어서기도 전에 일종의 광기에 사로잡혔고, 그것이 항해 내내 끈질기게 깊어진 것처럼 보인다."], ["Of course my statement must be taken cum grano, since I am writing from the dictation of a clerk of the Russian consul, who kindly translated for me, time being short.", "물론 내 진술은 에누리해서 받아들여야 하니, 시간이 촉박한 가운데 친절히 번역해준 러시아 영사관 서기의 구술을 받아 적은 것이기 때문이다. 데메테르호의 항해일지."]], [["Varna to Whitby.", "바르나에서 휘트비까지."]], [["Written 18 July, things so strange happening, that I shall keep accurate note henceforth till we land.", "7월 18일 기록. 너무도 기이한 일들이 일어나고 있어, 이제부터 뭍에 닿을 때까지 정확한 기록을 남기겠다."]], [["On 6 July we finished taking in cargo, silver sand and boxes of earth.", "7월 6일, 화물 적재를 마침. 은모래와 흙 상자들."], ["At noon set sail.", "정오에 출항."], ["East wind, fresh.", "동풍, 신선함."], ["Crew, five hands ...", "선원은 다섯 명 ..."], ["two mates, cook, and myself (captain).", "항해사 둘, 요리사, 그리고 나(선장)."]], [["On 11 July at dawn entered Bosphorus.", "7월 11일, 새벽에 보스포루스 해협에 진입."], ["Boarded by Turkish Customs officers.", "터키 세관원들이 승선. 뇌물."], ["Backsheesh. All correct.", "모두 이상 없음."], ["Under way at 4 p. m.", "오후 4시에 항해 재개."]], [["On 12 July through Dardanelles.", "7월 12일, 다르다넬스 해협을 통과."], ["More Customs officers and flagboat of guarding squadron. Backsheesh again.", "또 세관원들과 경비 함대의 기함선. 또 뇌물. 관리들의 일처리는 철저했으나 빨랐다."], ["Work of officers thorough, but quick. Want us off soon.", "우리를 어서 보내고 싶어 했다."], ["At dark passed into Archipelago.", "어두워질 무렵 다도해로 진입."]], [["On 13 July passed Cape Matapan.", "7월 13일, 마타판 곶을 지남."], ["Crew dissatisfied about something.", "선원들이 무언가로 불만스러워함."], ["Seemed scared, but would not speak out.", "겁먹은 듯했으나 털어놓으려 하지 않음."]], [["On 14 July was somewhat anxious about crew.", "7월 14일, 선원들 때문에 다소 불안했다."], ["Men all steady fellows, who sailed with me before.", "모두 전에 나와 함께 항해한 적이 있는 한결같은 자들이다."], ["Mate could not make out what was wrong; they only told him there was something, and crossed themselves.", "항해사도 무엇이 잘못되었는지 알아내지 못했다. 그들은 그저 무언가가 있다고만 말하며 성호를 그었다."], ["Mate lost temper with one of them that day and struck him.", "그날 항해사가 그중 한 명에게 화를 내고 그를 때렸다."], ["Expected fierce quarrel, but all was quiet.", "격렬한 다툼을 예상했으나, 모두 조용했다."]], [["On 16 July mate reported in the morning that one of crew, Petrofsky, was missing.", "7월 16일, 항해사가 아침에 선원 중 한 명인 페트로프스키가 사라졌다고 보고했다."], ["Could not account for it.", "까닭을 알 수 없었다."], ["Took larboard watch eight bells last night; was relieved by Abramoff, but did not go to bunk.", "어젯밤 여덟 점종에 좌현 당직을 섰고 아브라모프와 교대했으나, 침상으로 가지 않았다."], ["Men more downcast than ever.", "선원들은 어느 때보다 풀이 죽었다."], ["All said they expected something of the kind, but would not say more than there was something aboard.", "모두 이런 일이 있으리라 예상했다고 말하면서도, 배 안에 무언가가 있다는 말 외에는 더 이상 말하려 하지 않았다."], ["Mate getting very impatient with them; feared some trouble ahead.", "항해사는 그들에게 몹시 짜증을 냈다. 앞으로 무슨 말썽이 생길까 두려웠다."]], [["On 17 July, yesterday, one of the men, Olgaren, came to my cabin, and in an awestruck way confided to me that he thought there was a strange man aboard the ship.", "7월 17일, 어제, 선원 중 한 명인 올가렌이 내 선실로 와서, 겁에 질린 투로, 배 안에 낯선 사내가 있는 것 같다고 털어놓았다."], ["He said that in his watch he had been sheltering behind the deck-house, as there was a rain-storm, when he saw a tall, thin man, who was not like any of the crew, come up the companion-way, and go along the deck forward, and disappear.", "그는 당직을 서다가 비바람이 몰아쳐 갑판실 뒤에 몸을 피하고 있었는데, 그때 키가 크고 마른, 선원 누구와도 닮지 않은 사내가 승강구 계단으로 올라와 갑판을 따라 앞으로 가더니 사라지는 것을 보았다고 했다."], ["He followed cautiously, but when he got to bows found no one, and the hatchways were all closed.", "그는 조심스레 뒤따랐으나, 뱃머리에 이르렀을 때 아무도 없었고 승강구는 모두 닫혀 있었다고 했다."], ["He was in a panic of superstitious fear, and I am afraid the panic may spread.", "그는 미신적 공포에 사로잡혀 있었고, 나는 그 공포가 번질까 걱정스럽다."], ["To allay it, I shall to-day search entire ship carefully from stem to stern.", "그것을 가라앉히려고, 나는 오늘 배 전체를 이물에서 고물까지 샅샅이 뒤지겠다."]], [["Later in the day I got together the whole crew, and told them, as they evidently thought there was some one in the ship, we would search from stem to stern.", "그날 늦게, 나는 선원 전원을 불러 모아, 그들이 배 안에 누군가 있다고 분명히 여기고 있으니 이물에서 고물까지 뒤져보자고 말했다. 일등항해사는 화를 냈다."], ["First mate angry; said it was folly, and to yield to such foolish ideas would demoralise the men; said he would engage to keep them out of trouble with a handspike.", "어리석은 짓이며, 그런 어리석은 생각에 굴복하면 선원들의 사기가 무너진다고 했다. 자기가 쇠지렛대로 그들이 말썽 부리지 않도록 책임지겠다고 했다."], ["I let him take the helm, while the rest began thorough search, all keeping abreast, with lanterns: we left no corner unsearched.", "나는 그에게 키를 맡기고, 나머지는 등불을 들고 나란히 줄지어 철저히 수색을 시작했다. 우리는 구석구석 빠짐없이 뒤졌다."], ["As there were only the big wooden boxes, there were no odd corners where a man could hide.", "커다란 나무 상자들밖에 없었으므로, 사람이 숨을 만한 후미진 구석은 없었다."], ["Men much relieved when search over, and went back to work cheerfully. First mate scowled, but said nothing.", "수색이 끝나자 선원들은 크게 안도하여 즐거이 일터로 돌아갔다. 일등항해사는 얼굴을 찌푸렸으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["22 July.--Rough weather last three days, and all hands busy with sails--no time to be frightened.", "7월 22일. — 지난 사흘간 거친 날씨였고, 전원이 돛에 매달려 분주했다 — 겁먹을 겨를이 없었다."], ["Men seem to have forgotten their dread.", "선원들은 두려움을 잊은 듯하다."], ["Mate cheerful again, and all on good terms.", "항해사도 다시 명랑해졌고, 모두 사이가 좋다."], ["Praised men for work in bad weather.", "궂은 날씨 속의 노고를 두고 선원들을 칭찬했다."], ["Passed Gibralter and out through Straits. All well.", "지브롤터를 지나 해협을 빠져나왔다. 모두 무사하다."]], [["24 July.--There seems some doom over this ship.", "7월 24일. — 이 배에는 어떤 운명이 드리워 있는 듯하다."], ["Already a hand short, and entering on the Bay of Biscay with wild weather ahead, and yet last night another man lost--disappeared.", "이미 일손 하나가 부족한 채, 앞으로 험한 날씨가 기다리는 비스케이 만에 들어서는데, 어젯밤 또 한 사람이 사라졌다 — 자취를 감추었다."], ["Like the first, he came off his watch and was not seen again.", "첫 번째 사람처럼, 그도 당직을 마치고 나서 다시는 보이지 않았다."], ["Men all in a panic of fear; sent a round robin, asking to have double watch, as they fear to be alone. Mate angry.", "선원들은 모두 공포에 사로잡혀, 혼자 있기를 두려워하니 이중 당직을 서게 해달라는 연명 청원서를 냈다. 항해사는 화를 냈다."], ["Fear there will be some trouble, as either he or the men will do some violence.", "무슨 말썽이 생길까 두렵다. 그 아니면 선원들이 무슨 난폭한 짓을 저지를 것 같다."]], [["28 July.--Four days in hell, knocking about in a sort of maelstrom, and the wind a tempest.", "7월 28일. — 일종의 소용돌이 속에서 나흘간 지옥을 헤맸고, 바람은 폭풍이었다."], ["No sleep for any one.", "누구도 잠을 자지 못했다."], ["Men all worn out. Hardly know how to set a watch, since no one fit to go on.", "선원들은 모두 기진맥진했다. 당직을 세울 만한 자가 아무도 없으니 어떻게 당직을 세워야 할지 막막하다."], ["Second mate volunteered to steer and watch, and let men snatch a few hours’ sleep.", "이등항해사가 자청해 키를 잡고 망을 보아, 선원들에게 몇 시간이나마 눈을 붙이게 했다."], ["Wind abating; seas still terrific, but feel them less, as ship is steadier.", "바람은 잦아들고 있다. 바다는 여전히 무섭지만, 배가 더 안정되어 덜 느껴진다."]], [["29 July.--Another tragedy.", "7월 29일. — 또 비극이 일어났다."], ["Had single watch to-night, as crew too tired to double.", "선원들이 너무 지쳐 이중 당직을 설 수 없어 오늘 밤 단독 당직을 세웠다."], ["When morning watch came on deck could find no one except steersman.", "아침 당직이 갑판에 올라왔을 때 키잡이 외에는 아무도 찾을 수 없었다."], ["Raised outcry, and all came on deck.", "고함을 질렀고, 모두 갑판으로 올라왔다."], ["Thorough search, but no one found.", "철저히 수색했으나, 아무도 찾지 못했다."], ["Are now without second mate, and crew in a panic.", "이제 이등항해사가 없고, 선원들은 공포에 사로잡혔다."], ["Mate and I agreed to go armed henceforth and wait for any sign of cause.", "항해사와 나는 앞으로 무장하고 어떤 원인의 징후라도 기다리기로 합의했다."]], [["30 July.--Last night.", "7월 30일. — 어젯밤."], ["Rejoiced we are nearing England.", "영국에 가까워지고 있어 기뻤다."], ["Weather fine, all sails set.", "날씨는 청명하고 돛은 모두 펼쳤다."], ["Retired worn out; slept soundly; awaked by mate telling me that both man of watch and steersman missing.", "기진맥진하여 잠자리에 들었다. 푹 잤다. 당직 선원과 키잡이가 둘 다 사라졌다고 항해사가 알려와 잠에서 깼다."], ["Only self and mate and two hands left to work ship.", "이제 배를 부릴 사람은 나와 항해사, 그리고 선원 둘뿐이다."]], [["1 August.--Two days of fog, and not a sail sighted.", "8월 1일. — 이틀간 안개가 끼었고, 돛 하나 보이지 않는다."], ["Had hoped when in the English Channel to be able to signal for help or get in somewhere.", "영국 해협에 들어서면 구조 신호를 보내거나 어디든 들어갈 수 있으리라 기대했었다."], ["Not having power to work sails, have to run before wind.", "돛을 부릴 인원이 없으니 바람에 떠밀려 가는 수밖에 없다."], ["Dare not lower, as could not raise them again.", "돛을 내릴 엄두가 나지 않으니, 다시 올릴 수가 없기 때문이다."], ["We seem to be drifting to some terrible doom.", "우리는 어떤 무서운 운명을 향해 떠내려가고 있는 듯하다."], ["Mate now more demoralised than either of men.", "항해사는 이제 두 선원 누구보다도 사기가 꺾여 있다."], ["His stronger nature seems to have worked inwardly against himself.", "그의 더 강한 기질이 도리어 안으로 그 자신을 갉아먹은 듯하다."], ["Men are beyond fear, working stolidly and patiently, with minds made up to worst.", "선원들은 두려움을 넘어, 최악을 각오한 채 묵묵히 참을성 있게 일한다."], ["They are Russian, he Roumanian.", "그들은 러시아인이고, 그는 루마니아인이다."]], [["2 August, midnight.--Woke up from few minutes’ sleep by hearing a cry, seemingly outside my port.", "8월 2일, 자정. — 몇 분간의 잠에서, 내 선창 바깥인 듯한 곳에서 들려온 비명에 깨어났다."], ["Could see nothing in fog.", "안개 속에서는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["Rushed on deck, and ran against mate.", "갑판으로 뛰쳐나가다 항해사와 부딪혔다."], ["Tells me heard cry and ran, but no sign of man on watch. One more gone.", "그는 비명을 듣고 달려왔으나 당직 선원의 흔적이 없다고 했다. 또 한 사람이 사라졌다."], ["Lord, help us!", "주여, 우리를 도우소서!"], ["Mate says we must be past Straits of Dover, as in a moment of fog lifting he saw North Foreland, just as he heard the man cry out.", "항해사 말로는, 안개가 한순간 걷힐 때 노스 포어랜드를 보았으니 우리가 도버 해협을 지났을 게 분명하다고 했다. 마침 그 사내가 비명을 지르는 소리를 들었을 때였다."], ["If so we are now off in the North Sea, and only God can guide us in the fog, which seems to move with us; and God seems to have deserted us.", "그렇다면 우리는 이제 북해에 나와 있는 것이고, 우리와 함께 움직이는 듯한 이 안개 속에서는 오직 신만이 우리를 인도하실 수 있다. 그런데 신은 우리를 버리신 듯하다."]], [["3 August.--At midnight I went to relieve the man at the wheel, and when I got to it found no one there.", "8월 3일. — 자정에 나는 키 잡은 자와 교대하러 갔는데, 거기에 이르러 보니 아무도 없었다."], ["The wind was steady, and as we ran before it there was no yawing.", "바람은 한결같았고, 우리는 그것을 등지고 달리고 있어 배가 좌우로 흔들리지 않았다."], ["I dared not leave it, so shouted for the mate.", "나는 감히 키를 떠날 수 없어 항해사를 불렀다."], ["After a few seconds he rushed up on deck in his flannels.", "몇 초 뒤 그는 플란넬 잠옷 차림으로 갑판으로 뛰쳐 올라왔다."], ["He looked wild-eyed and haggard, and I greatly fear his reason has given way.", "그의 눈빛은 사납고 얼굴은 초췌했으며, 나는 그의 이성이 무너졌을까 몹시 두렵다."], ["He came close to me and whispered hoarsely, with his mouth to my ear, as though fearing the very air might hear: “It is here; I know it, now.", "그는 내게 바짝 다가와, 마치 공기조차 들을까 두려운 듯 내 귀에 입을 대고 쉰 목소리로 속삭였다. \"그것이 여기 있어요. 이제 알겠어요."], ["On the watch last night I saw It, like a man, tall and thin, and ghastly pale.", "어젯밤 당직 때 그것을 보았어요. 사람 같은데, 키가 크고 마르고 소름 끼치게 창백했어요."], ["It was in the bows, and looking out.", "그것은 뱃머리에서 밖을 내다보고 있었어요."], ["I crept behind It, and gave It my knife; but the knife went through It, empty as the air.”", "나는 그 뒤로 살금살금 다가가 칼로 찔렀어요. 그런데 칼은 공기처럼 텅 빈 그것을 그대로 뚫고 지나갔어요.\""], ["And as he spoke he took his knife and drove it savagely into space.", "그렇게 말하면서 그는 칼을 꺼내 허공을 사납게 찔렀다. 그러더니 말을 이었다."], ["Then he went on: “But It is here, and I’ll find It.", "\"하지만 그것은 여기 있고, 나는 그것을 찾아낼 거예요."], ["It is in the hold, perhaps in one of those boxes.", "그것은 선창에, 어쩌면 저 상자들 중 하나에 있어요."], ["I’ll unscrew them one by one and see.", "내가 하나하나 나사를 풀어 들여다보겠어요."], ["You work the helm.”", "당신은 키를 부리세요.\""], ["And, with a warning look and his finger on his lip, he went below.", "그러고는 경고하는 눈빛으로 손가락을 입술에 댄 채 아래로 내려갔다."], ["There was springing up a choppy wind, and I could not leave the helm.", "사나운 바람이 일기 시작하여, 나는 키를 떠날 수 없었다."], ["I saw him come out on deck again with a tool-chest and a lantern, and go down the forward hatchway.", "그가 연장통과 등불을 들고 다시 갑판으로 나와 앞쪽 승강구로 내려가는 것을 보았다. 그는 미쳤다, 완전히, 발광하도록 미쳤다."], ["He is mad, stark, raving mad, and it’s no use my trying to stop him.", "내가 그를 막으려 해봐야 소용없다. 그는 그 큰 상자들을 해치지 못한다."], ["He can’t hurt those big boxes: they are invoiced as “clay,” and to pull them about is as harmless a thing as he can do.", "그것들은 송장에 \"점토\"로 기재되어 있으니, 그것들을 이리저리 끌어대는 일이야말로 그가 할 수 있는 가장 무해한 짓이다."], ["So here I stay, and mind the helm, and write these notes.", "그러니 나는 여기 머물러 키를 지키며 이 기록을 쓴다."], ["I can only trust in God and wait till the fog clears.", "나는 그저 신을 믿고 안개가 걷히기를 기다릴 수밖에 없다."], ["Then, if I can’t steer to any harbour with the wind that is, I shall cut down sails and lie by, and signal for help....", "그때 가서 지금 부는 바람으로 어느 항구로도 키를 잡아갈 수 없거든, 돛을 내리고 표류하며 구조 신호를 보내겠다...."]], [["It is nearly all over now.", "이제 거의 다 끝났다."], ["Just as I was beginning to hope that the mate would come out calmer--for I heard him knocking away at something in the hold, and work is good for him--there came up the hatchway a sudden, startled scream, which made my blood run cold, and up on the deck he came as if shot from a gun--a raging madman, with his eyes rolling and his face convulsed with fear.", "항해사가 한결 차분해져 나오기를 막 바라기 시작했을 때 — 그가 선창에서 무언가를 두드리는 소리가 들렸고, 일하는 것이 그에게는 이로우니 — 승강구에서 갑작스럽고 소스라치는 비명이 솟구쳐 올라와 내 피를 얼어붙게 했고, 그가 총에서 튕겨 나오듯 갑판으로 올라왔다 — 눈을 부라리고 얼굴이 공포로 일그러진, 광란하는 미치광이였다."], ["“Save me!", "\"살려줘요!"], ["save me!”", "살려줘요!\""], ["he cried, and then looked round on the blanket of fog.", "그가 외쳤다. 그러더니 안개의 장막을 둘러보았다."], ["His horror turned to despair, and in a steady voice he said: “You had better come too, captain, before it is too late. He is there.", "그의 공포는 절망으로 바뀌었고, 그는 차분한 목소리로 말했다. \"당신도 함께 가는 게 좋아요, 선장님, 너무 늦기 전에요."], ["I know the secret now.", "그가 저기 있어요."], ["The sea will save me from Him, and it is all that is left!”", "이제 비밀을 알았어요. 바다가 나를 그에게서 구해줄 거예요."], ["Before I could say a word, or move forward to seize him, he sprang on the bulwark and deliberately threw himself into the sea.", "남은 건 그것뿐이에요!\" 내가 한마디 하거나 그를 붙들려고 앞으로 나서기도 전에, 그는 뱃전으로 뛰어올라 거침없이 바다로 몸을 던졌다."], ["I suppose I know the secret too, now.", "짐작건대 이제 나도 그 비밀을 안다."], ["It was this madman who had got rid of the men one by one, and now he has followed them himself. God help me!", "선원들을 하나하나 없앤 것이 바로 이 미치광이였고, 이제 그 자신도 그들을 뒤따른 것이다. 신이여, 저를 도우소서!"], ["How am I to account for all these horrors when I get to port?", "항구에 닿으면 이 온갖 끔찍한 일들을 어떻게 설명해야 하나?"], ["When I get to port!", "내가 항구에 닿거든 말이다!"], ["Will that ever be?", "그런 일이 과연 있을까?"]], [["4 August.--Still fog, which the sunrise cannot pierce.", "8월 4일. — 여전히 안개, 해돋이도 뚫지 못한다."], ["I know there is sunrise because I am a sailor, why else I know not.", "나는 해가 떴음을 안다, 내가 뱃사람이기 때문이다. 그 밖에 어떻게 아는지는 모르겠다."], ["I dared not go below, I dared not leave the helm; so here all night I stayed, and in the dimness of the night I saw It--Him!", "나는 감히 아래로 내려갈 수 없었고, 감히 키를 떠날 수도 없었다. 그래서 밤새 여기 머물렀는데, 밤의 어스름 속에서 나는 그것을 — 그를 보았다!"], ["God forgive me, but the mate was right to jump overboard.", "신이여 저를 용서하소서, 하지만 항해사가 바다에 몸을 던진 것은 옳았다."], ["It was better to die like a man; to die like a sailor in blue water no man can object.", "사내답게 죽는 편이 나았다. 뱃사람답게 푸른 바다에서 죽는 것을 누구도 탓할 수 없으리라. 하지만 나는 선장이니, 내 배를 떠나서는 안 된다."], ["But I am captain, and I must not leave my ship.", "그러나 나는 이 악마인지 괴물인지를 보기 좋게 따돌리겠다."], ["But I shall baffle this fiend or monster, for I shall tie my hands to the wheel when my strength begins to fail, and along with them I shall tie that which He--It!--dare not touch; and then, come good wind or foul, I shall save my soul, and my honour as a captain.", "기력이 다하기 시작하면 내 손을 키에 묶고, 그 손과 함께, 그가 — 그것이! — 감히 건드리지 못하는 것을 함께 묶겠다. 그러면 순풍이 불든 역풍이 불든, 나는 내 영혼과 선장으로서의 명예를 지키겠다."], ["I am growing weaker, and the night is coming on.", "나는 점점 기력이 빠지고, 밤이 다가온다."], ["If He can look me in the face again, I may not have time to act....", "만약 그가 다시 내 얼굴을 마주 볼 수 있게 되면, 나는 행동할 시간이 없을지도 모른다...."], ["If we are wrecked, mayhap this bottle may be found, and those who find it may understand; if not, ...", "우리가 난파하거든, 아마도 이 병이 발견되리니, 그것을 발견한 이들이 부디 알아주기를. 그렇지 못하거든, ..."], ["well, then all men shall know that I have been true to my trust.", "그래도 모든 이가 내가 내 소임에 충실했음을 알게 되리라."], ["God and the Blessed Virgin and the saints help a poor ignorant soul trying to do his duty....", "신과 복되신 동정녀와 성인들이여, 제 의무를 다하려는 가엾고 무지한 영혼을 도우소서...."]], [["Of course the verdict was an open one.", "물론 평결은 미결로 났다."], ["There is no evidence to adduce; and whether or not the man himself committed the murders there is now none to say.", "제시할 증거가 없었다. 그 사내 자신이 살인을 저질렀는지 아닌지를 이제 말할 수 있는 이는 아무도 없다."], ["The folk here hold almost universally that the captain is simply a hero, and he is to be given a public funeral.", "이곳 사람들은 그 선장이 그야말로 영웅이라고 거의 한결같이 여기며, 그에게 공개 장례를 치러주기로 했다."], ["Already it is arranged that his body is to be taken with a train of boats up the Esk for a piece and then brought back to Tate Hill Pier and up the abbey steps; for he is to be buried in the churchyard on the cliff.", "벌써 그의 시신을 배들의 행렬과 함께 에스크 강을 따라 한참 거슬러 올라갔다가 다시 테이트 힐 피어로 데려와 수도원 계단을 올라가도록 하는 절차가 마련되었다. 그는 절벽 위 묘지에 묻힐 것이기 때문이다."], ["The owners of more than a hundred boats have already given in their names as wishing to follow him to the grave.", "벌써 백 척이 넘는 배의 주인들이 그를 무덤까지 따르고 싶다며 이름을 올렸다."]], [["No trace has ever been found of the great dog; at which there is much mourning, for, with public opinion in its present state, he would, I believe, be adopted by the town.", "그 거대한 개의 흔적은 끝내 찾지 못했는데, 이를 두고 애석해하는 이가 많다. 지금의 여론대로라면 그 개는 마을에서 거두어 키우게 되었을 것이기 때문이다."], ["To-morrow will see the funeral; and so will end this one more “mystery of the sea.”", "내일이면 장례가 치러질 것이고, 이로써 또 하나의 \"바다의 수수께끼\"가 끝을 맺을 것이다."]], [["Mina Murray’s Journal.", "미나 머리의 일기."]], [["8 August.--Lucy was very restless all night, and I, too, could not sleep.", "8월 8일. — 루시는 밤새도록 몹시 잠을 설쳤고, 나 또한 잠들 수 없었다."], ["The storm was fearful, and as it boomed loudly among the chimney-pots, it made me shudder.", "폭풍은 무시무시했고, 굴뚝갓 사이에서 요란하게 우르릉거릴 때면 나는 몸서리쳤다."], ["When a sharp puff came it seemed to be like a distant gun.", "날카로운 돌풍이 한 차례 몰아칠 때면 마치 먼 곳의 대포 소리 같았다."], ["Strangely enough, Lucy did not wake; but she got up twice and dressed herself.", "이상하게도 루시는 깨지 않았다. 그러나 두 번이나 일어나 옷을 차려입었다."], ["Fortunately, each time I awoke in time and managed to undress her without waking her, and got her back to bed.", "다행히도 그때마다 나는 제때 깨어, 그녀를 깨우지 않고 옷을 벗겨 다시 침대에 눕힐 수 있었다."], ["It is a very strange thing, this sleep-walking, for as soon as her will is thwarted in any physical way, her intention, if there be any, disappears, and she yields herself almost exactly to the routine of her life.", "이 몽유병은 참으로 기이한 것이니, 그녀의 의지가 무슨 물리적 방식으로든 가로막히면, 그 의도는, 설령 어떤 의도가 있다 해도, 사라져버리고, 그녀는 거의 정확히 자기 일상의 틀에 다시 몸을 맡기기 때문이다."]], [["Early in the morning we both got up and went down to the harbour to see if anything had happened in the night.", "이른 아침, 우리 둘은 일어나 항구로 내려가 밤사이 무슨 일이 있었는지 보러 갔다."], ["There were very few people about, and though the sun was bright, and the air clear and fresh, the big, grim-looking waves, that seemed dark themselves because the foam that topped them was like snow, forced themselves in through the narrow mouth of the harbour--like a bullying man going through a crowd.", "나와 있는 사람은 거의 없었고, 해는 밝고 공기는 맑고 신선했으나, 그 크고 험상궂어 보이는 파도가 — 마루를 덮은 거품이 눈처럼 희었기에 도리어 제 몸은 검어 보였다 — 항구의 좁은 어귀로 밀치고 들어왔다, 마치 군중 사이를 비집고 가는 윽박지르는 사내처럼."], ["Somehow I felt glad that Jonathan was not on the sea last night, but on land. But, oh, is he on land or sea?", "어쩐지 나는 조너선이 어젯밤 바다가 아니라 뭍에 있어 다행이라는 생각이 들었다. 그러나, 아, 그는 뭍에 있을까 바다에 있을까?"], ["Where is he, and how? I am getting fearfully anxious about him.", "그는 어디에, 어떻게 있을까? 나는 그가 너무도 걱정스러워진다."], ["If I only knew what to do, and could do anything!", "무엇을 해야 할지 알기만 하면, 무엇이든 할 수 있기만 하면!"]], [["10 August.--The funeral of the poor sea-captain to-day was most touching.", "8월 10일. — 오늘 가엾은 그 선장의 장례는 더없이 가슴 뭉클했다."], ["Every boat in the harbour seemed to be there, and the coffin was carried by captains all the way from Tate Hill Pier up to the churchyard.", "항구의 모든 배가 다 나온 듯했고, 관은 테이트 힐 피어에서 묘지까지 줄곧 선장들이 메고 갔다."], ["Lucy came with me, and we went early to our old seat, whilst the cortège of boats went up the river to the Viaduct and came down again.", "루시도 나와 함께 왔고, 우리는 일찍 우리의 옛 자리로 갔는데, 그동안 배들의 행렬은 강을 거슬러 고가교까지 올라갔다가 다시 내려왔다."], ["We had a lovely view, and saw the procession nearly all the way.", "우리는 더없이 좋은 전망을 누렸고, 행렬을 거의 내내 볼 수 있었다."], ["The poor fellow was laid to rest quite near our seat so that we stood on it when the time came and saw everything.", "그 가엾은 사내는 우리 자리에서 아주 가까운 곳에 묻혔으므로, 때가 되자 우리는 그 자리에 서서 모든 것을 지켜보았다."], ["Poor Lucy seemed much upset.", "가엾은 루시는 몹시 심란해 보였다."], ["She was restless and uneasy all the time, and I cannot but think that her dreaming at night is telling on her.", "그녀는 내내 안절부절못하며 불안해했고, 밤마다 꾸는 꿈이 그녀를 괴롭히고 있다고밖에 생각할 수 없다."], ["She is quite odd in one thing: she will not admit to me that there is any cause for restlessness; or if there be, she does not understand it herself.", "그녀는 한 가지 점에서 자못 이상한데, 안절부절못할 까닭이 무엇이든 있다는 것을 나에게 인정하려 하지 않는다. 혹은 까닭이 있다 해도, 그녀 자신도 그것을 이해하지 못한다."], ["There is an additional cause in that poor old Mr. Swales was found dead this morning on our seat, his neck being broken.", "게다가 또 하나의 까닭이 있으니, 오늘 아침 가엾은 노인 스웨일스 씨가 우리 자리에서 목이 부러진 채 죽은 채로 발견된 것이다."], ["He had evidently, as the doctor said, fallen back in the seat in some sort of fright, for there was a look of fear and horror on his face that the men said made them shudder.", "의사의 말마따나 그는 분명 무슨 두려움에 사로잡혀 자리에 뒤로 넘어진 것이니, 그의 얼굴에는 사람들이 보기에 몸서리쳐질 만한 공포와 경악의 표정이 어려 있었기 때문이다."], ["Poor dear old man!", "가엾고 정겨운 노인!"], ["Perhaps he had seen Death with his dying eyes!", "어쩌면 그는 죽어가는 눈으로 죽음을 보았는지도 모른다!"], ["Lucy is so sweet and sensitive that she feels influences more acutely than other people do.", "루시는 어찌나 다정하고 예민한지 다른 사람들보다 그런 기운을 한층 날카롭게 느낀다."], ["Just now she was quite upset by a little thing which I did not much heed, though I am myself very fond of animals.", "방금도 그녀는 내가 별로 마음에 두지 않은 사소한 일에 자못 심란해했는데, 나 역시 동물을 무척 좋아하면서도 그랬다."], ["One of the men who came up here often to look for the boats was followed by his dog.", "이곳에 자주 배를 보러 올라오던 한 사내가 자기 개를 데리고 왔다."], ["The dog is always with him.", "그 개는 늘 그와 함께 있다."], ["They are both quiet persons, and I never saw the man angry, nor heard the dog bark.", "둘 다 조용한 편이고, 나는 그 사내가 화내는 것도, 그 개가 짖는 것도 본 적이 없다."], ["During the service the dog would not come to its master, who was on the seat with us, but kept a few yards off, barking and howling.", "그런데 예배가 진행되는 동안 그 개는 우리와 함께 자리에 있던 주인에게 오려 하지 않고, 몇 발짝 떨어져 멈춘 채 짖고 울부짖었다."], ["Its master spoke to it gently, and then harshly, and then angrily; but it would neither come nor cease to make a noise.", "주인이 다정하게, 그다음엔 매섭게, 그다음엔 성난 목소리로 타일렀으나, 개는 오지도 않고 소란을 멈추지도 않았다."], ["It was in a sort of fury, with its eyes savage, and all its hairs bristling out like a cat’s tail when puss is on the war-path.", "개는 일종의 광란 상태로, 눈은 사납고, 온 털은 고양이가 싸움에 나설 때 꼬리털처럼 곤두서 있었다."], ["Finally the man, too, got angry, and jumped down and kicked the dog, and then took it by the scruff of the neck and half dragged and half threw it on the tombstone on which the seat is fixed.", "마침내 사내도 화가 나서, 뛰어 내려가 개를 걷어차고, 목덜미를 잡아 반쯤 끌고 반쯤 던지다시피 하여 자리가 박힌 그 묘석 위에 올려놓았다."], ["The moment it touched the stone the poor thing became quiet and fell all into a tremble.", "돌에 닿는 순간 그 가엾은 짐승은 잠잠해지더니 온통 떨기 시작했다."], ["It did not try to get away, but crouched down, quivering and cowering, and was in such a pitiable state of terror that I tried, though without effect, to comfort it.", "개는 달아나려 하지 않고, 몸을 웅크린 채 부들부들 떨며 잔뜩 겁에 질려 움츠러들었는데, 그 처지가 어찌나 가련했던지 나는 효과는 없었으나 달래주려 애썼다."], ["Lucy was full of pity, too, but she did not attempt to touch the dog, but looked at it in an agonised sort of way.", "루시도 가엾이 여기는 마음이 가득했으나, 개를 만지려 들지는 않고, 어딘가 괴로워하는 눈빛으로 그것을 바라보았다."], ["I greatly fear that she is of too super-sensitive a nature to go through the world without trouble.", "나는 그녀가 너무도 예민한 천성이라 이 세상을 탈 없이 살아가지 못할까 몹시 두렵다."], ["She will be dreaming of this to-night, I am sure.", "분명 오늘 밤 그녀는 이 일을 꿈에서 볼 것이다."], ["The whole agglomeration of things--the ship steered into port by a dead man; his attitude, tied to the wheel with a crucifix and beads; the touching funeral; the dog, now furious and now in terror--will all afford material for her dreams.", "이 모든 일의 뒤엉킴이 — 죽은 사람이 키를 잡아 항구로 들어온 배, 십자고상과 묵주와 함께 키에 묶여 있던 그의 모습, 가슴 뭉클한 장례, 한순간 광란하고 한순간 공포에 떨던 개가 — 모두 그녀의 꿈에 재료가 되어줄 것이다."]], [["I think it will be best for her to go to bed tired out physically, so I shall take her for a long walk by the cliffs to Robin Hood’s Bay and back.", "내 생각에는 그녀가 몸을 완전히 지치게 하여 잠자리에 드는 것이 가장 좋을 듯하니, 나는 그녀를 데리고 절벽을 따라 로빈 후드 만까지 멀리 다녀오겠다."], ["She ought not to have much inclination for sleep-walking then.", "그러면 그녀도 몽유병을 일으킬 기색이 그리 많지 않을 것이다."]], [["# MINA MURRAY’S JOURNAL", "# MINA MURRAY’S JOURNAL"]], [["Same day, 11 o’clock p.", "「미나 머리의 일기」"], ["m.--Oh, but I am tired!", "같은 날, 밤 11시."], ["If it were not that I had made my diary a duty I should not open it to-night. We had a lovely walk.", "— 아, 너무도 피곤하다! 일기를 쓰는 것을 하나의 의무로 삼아두지 않았더라면 오늘 밤은 펴보지도 않았을 것이다."], ["Lucy, after a while, was in gay spirits, owing, I think, to some dear cows who came nosing towards us in a field close to the lighthouse, and frightened the wits out of us.", "우리는 멋진 산책을 했다. 루시는 얼마 후 들떠서 명랑해졌는데, 등대 가까운 들판에서 사랑스러운 소 몇 마리가 우리 쪽으로 코를 들이밀며 다가와 우리 넋을 쏙 빼놓은 덕분이었던 것 같다."], ["I believe we forgot everything except, of course, personal fear, and it seemed to wipe the slate clean and give us a fresh start.", "우리는 물론 본능적인 두려움을 빼고는 모든 것을 잊어버린 듯했고, 그것이 마치 석판을 깨끗이 닦아내어 새 출발을 하게 해준 것 같았다."], ["We had a capital “severe tea” at Robin Hood’s Bay in a sweet little old-fashioned inn, with a bow-window right over the seaweed-covered rocks of the strand.", "우리는 로빈 후드 만(灣)의 아담하고 예스러운 작은 여관에서 근사하게 \"푸짐한 차\"를 들었는데, 활처럼 휜 내닫이창이 바닷가의 해초 덮인 바위들 바로 위로 나 있었다."], ["I believe we should have shocked the “New Woman”", "우리의 왕성한 식욕을 보았다면 \"신여성\"들은 충격을 받았을 것이다."], ["with our appetites.", "남자들은 더 너그럽다."], ["Men are more tolerant, bless them! Then we walked home with some, or rather many, stoppages to rest, and with our hearts full of a constant dread of wild bulls.", "그들에게 복이 있기를! 그러고 나서 우리는 집으로 걸어왔는데, 쉬느라 몇 번, 아니 여러 번 멈췄고, 마음속은 사나운 황소들에 대한 끊임없는 두려움으로 가득했다."], ["Lucy was really tired, and we intended to creep off to bed as soon as we could.", "루시는 정말 지쳐 있었고, 우리는 되도록 빨리 살그머니 잠자리에 들 생각이었다."], ["The young curate came in, however, and Mrs. Westenra asked him to stay for supper.", "그러나 젊은 부목사가 찾아왔고, 웨스튼라 부인이 그에게 저녁식사를 하고 가라고 청했다."], ["Lucy and I had both a fight for it with the dusty miller; I know it was a hard fight on my part, and I am quite heroic.", "루시도 나도 졸음이라는 적과 한바탕 싸움을 벌여야 했다. 내 쪽 싸움이 힘겨웠음을 나는 안다. 나는 자못 영웅적이었던 것이다."], ["I think that some day the bishops must get together and see about breeding up a new class of curates, who don’t take supper, no matter how they may be pressed to, and who will know when girls are tired.", "언젠가 주교들이 모여, 아무리 권유를 받아도 저녁식사를 하지 않으며 처녀들이 언제 피곤한지를 알아차리는 새로운 부류의 부목사를 길러내는 일을 의논해야 하리라고 생각한다."], ["Lucy is asleep and breathing softly.", "루시는 잠들어 가만히 숨쉬고 있다."], ["She has more colour in her cheeks than usual, and looks, oh, so sweet.", "평소보다 두 뺨에 혈색이 돌고, 아, 어찌나 사랑스러워 보이는지."], ["If Mr. Holmwood fell in love with her seeing her only in the drawing-room, I wonder what he would say if he saw her now.", "홈우드 씨가 응접실에서만 그녀를 보고도 사랑에 빠졌다면, 지금의 그녀를 본다면 뭐라고 할지 궁금하다."], ["Some of the “New Women”", "언젠가 \"신여성\""], ["writers will some day start an idea that men and women should be allowed to see each other asleep before proposing or accepting.", "작가들 중 누군가는, 남녀가 청혼을 하거나 받아들이기 전에 서로의 잠든 모습을 볼 수 있어야 한다는 생각을 내놓을 것이다."], ["But I suppose the New Woman won’t condescend in future to accept; she will do the proposing herself.", "하지만 짐작건대 신여성은 앞으로 받아들이는 일 따위에 자신을 낮추지 않으리라. 청혼은 그녀가 직접 할 것이다."], ["And a nice job she will make of it, too!", "게다가 그 일을 아주 멋지게 해낼 테고!"], ["There’s some consolation in that.", "거기에 얼마간 위안이 있다."], ["I am so happy to-night, because dear Lucy seems better.", "오늘 밤 나는 무척 행복하다. 사랑스러운 루시가 한결 나아진 듯 보이기 때문이다."], ["I really believe she has turned the corner, and that we are over her troubles with dreaming.", "정말이지 그녀가 고비를 넘겼고, 꿈자리 때문에 겪던 곤란에서 벗어난 것이라고 믿는다."], ["I should be quite happy if I only knew if Jonathan....", "조너선의 소식만 알 수 있다면 나는 더없이 행복할 텐데……"], ["God bless and keep him.", "신이여 그를 축복하시고 지켜주소서."]], [["11 August, 3 a. m.--Diary again.", "8월 11일, 새벽 3시."], ["No sleep now, so I may as well write. I am too agitated to sleep.", "— 다시 일기를 쓴다. 이제 잠이 오지 않으니 차라리 글이라도 쓰는 편이 낫겠다."], ["We have had such an adventure, such an agonising experience. I fell asleep as soon as I had closed my diary....", "너무 마음이 흔들려 잠을 이룰 수가 없다. 우리는 참으로 큰 모험을, 참으로 가슴 졸이는 일을 겪었다."], ["Suddenly I became broad awake, and sat up, with a horrible sense of fear upon me, and of some feeling of emptiness around me.", "나는 일기를 덮자마자 잠이 들었다…… 그러다 갑자기 완전히 잠이 깨어 몸을 일으켜 앉았는데, 끔찍한 공포감과 주위가 텅 빈 듯한 어떤 느낌에 휩싸였다."], ["The room was dark, so I could not see Lucy’s bed; I stole across and felt for her.", "방이 어두워 루시의 침대가 보이지 않기에, 나는 가만히 다가가 그녀를 더듬어 찾았다."], ["The bed was empty.", "침대는 비어 있었다."], ["I lit a match and found that she was not in the room. The door was shut, but not locked, as I had left it.", "성냥을 켜보니 그녀는 방 안에 없었다. 문은 닫혀 있었으나, 내가 그대로 두었던 대로 잠겨 있지는 않았다."], ["I feared to wake her mother, who has been more than usually ill lately, so threw on some clothes and got ready to look for her.", "요즘 들어 유난히 편찮으신 그녀의 어머니를 깨우기가 두려워, 나는 옷을 대충 걸치고 그녀를 찾아 나설 채비를 했다."], ["As I was leaving the room it struck me that the clothes she wore might give me some clue to her dreaming intention.", "방을 나서려는데 문득, 그녀가 입은 옷이 그녀의 몽유(夢遊) 의도에 어떤 실마리를 줄지도 모른다는 생각이 들었다."], ["Dressing-gown would mean house; dress, outside. Dressing-gown and dress were both in their places.", "실내복을 입었다면 집 안에 있다는 뜻이고, 외출복이라면 밖이라는 뜻이었다. 실내복도 외출복도 둘 다 제자리에 있었다."], ["“Thank God,”", "\"다행이야,\""], ["I said to myself, “she cannot be far, as she is only in her nightdress.”", "하고 나는 혼잣말을 했다. \"잠옷 차림이니 멀리 가지는 못했을 거야.\""], ["I ran downstairs and looked in the sitting-room.", "나는 아래층으로 달려 내려가 거실을 들여다보았다."], ["Not there!", "거기 없었다!"], ["Then I looked in all the other open rooms of the house, with an ever-growing fear chilling my heart.", "그다음 나는 점점 커지는 두려움에 가슴이 얼어붙은 채, 집 안의 열린 방들을 모두 살펴보았다."], ["Finally I came to the hall door and found it open.", "마침내 현관문에 이르러 보니 문이 열려 있었다."], ["It was not wide open, but the catch of the lock had not caught.", "활짝 열린 것은 아니었으나, 자물쇠의 걸쇠가 채워지지 않았다."], ["The people of the house are careful to lock the door every night, so I feared that Lucy must have gone out as she was.", "이 집 사람들은 밤마다 문을 꼼꼼히 잠그므로, 나는 루시가 그 차림 그대로 밖으로 나간 것이 틀림없다고 두려워했다."], ["There was no time to think of what might happen; a vague, overmastering fear obscured all details.", "무슨 일이 벌어질지 생각할 겨를도 없었다. 막연하면서도 압도적인 두려움이 온갖 자잘한 생각을 흐려놓았다."], ["I took a big, heavy shawl and ran out.", "나는 크고 두툼한 숄을 집어 들고 밖으로 달려 나갔다."], ["The clock was striking one as I was in the Crescent, and there was not a soul in sight.", "크레센트에 이르렀을 때 시계가 한 시를 치고 있었고, 사람 그림자 하나 보이지 않았다."], ["I ran along the North Terrace, but could see no sign of the white figure which I expected.", "나는 노스 테라스를 따라 달렸으나, 내가 보리라 예상한 흰 형체의 자취는 어디에도 없었다."], ["At the edge of the West Cliff above the pier I looked across the harbour to the East Cliff, in the hope or fear--I don’t know which--of seeing Lucy in our favourite seat.", "부두 위 웨스트 클리프 가장자리에서 나는 항구 너머 이스트 클리프 쪽을 바라보았다. 우리가 가장 좋아하는 그 자리에 루시가 있기를 바라는, 아니 두려워하는 — 어느 쪽인지 나도 모르겠다 — 마음으로."], ["There was a bright full moon, with heavy black, driving clouds, which threw the whole scene into a fleeting diorama of light and shade as they sailed across.", "밝은 보름달이 떠 있었고, 묵직한 검은 구름이 빠르게 흘러가며 온 풍경을 명암이 명멸하는 디오라마로 만들었다."], ["For a moment or two I could see nothing, as the shadow of a cloud obscured St. Mary’s Church and all around it.", "한두 순간 동안은 아무것도 보이지 않았으니, 구름 그림자가 성 메리 성당과 그 주위를 온통 가렸기 때문이다."], ["Then as the cloud passed I could see the ruins of the abbey coming into view; and as the edge of a narrow band of light as sharp as a sword-cut moved along, the church and the churchyard became gradually visible.", "그러다 구름이 지나가자 폐허가 된 수도원이 시야에 들어왔다. 칼로 벤 듯 날카로운 좁은 빛줄기의 가장자리가 옮겨 가면서, 성당과 묘지가 차츰 모습을 드러냈다."], ["Whatever my expectation was, it was not disappointed, for there, on our favourite seat, the silver light of the moon struck a half-reclining figure, snowy white.", "내 예상이 무엇이었든 그것은 어긋나지 않았으니, 바로 거기, 우리가 가장 좋아하는 그 자리에, 달의 은빛이 눈처럼 흰, 반쯤 기대 누운 형체를 비추고 있었기 때문이다."], ["The coming of the cloud was too quick for me to see much, for shadow shut down on light almost immediately; but it seemed to me as though something dark stood behind the seat where the white figure shone, and bent over it.", "구름이 너무 빨리 다가와 많은 것을 보지는 못했으니, 거의 곧바로 그림자가 빛 위로 내려앉았기 때문이다. 그러나 흰 형체가 빛나는 그 자리 뒤에 무언가 어두운 것이 서서, 그 위로 몸을 굽히고 있는 것처럼 보였다."], ["What it was, whether man or beast, I could not tell; I did not wait to catch another glance, but flew down the steep steps to the pier and along by the fish-market to the bridge, which was the only way to reach the East Cliff.", "그것이 무엇인지, 사람인지 짐승인지 나는 분간할 수 없었다. 나는 한 번 더 살펴볼 겨를도 없이, 가파른 계단을 날듯이 내려가 부두로, 그리고 어시장을 지나 다리로 향했는데, 그것이 이스트 클리프로 가는 유일한 길이었다."], ["The town seemed as dead, for not a soul did I see; I rejoiced that it was so, for I wanted no witness of poor Lucy’s condition.", "마을은 죽은 듯이 고요했으니, 사람 하나 보이지 않았다. 나는 그것이 다행이라 여겼으니, 가엾은 루시의 그런 모습을 목격할 사람을 원치 않았기 때문이다."], ["The time and distance seemed endless, and my knees trembled and my breath came laboured as I toiled up the endless steps to the abbey.", "시간도 거리도 끝이 없는 듯했고, 수도원으로 오르는 끝없는 계단을 힘겹게 오르는 동안 두 무릎은 후들거리고 숨은 가빠졌다."], ["I must have gone fast, and yet it seemed to me as if my feet were weighted with lead, and as though every joint in my body were rusty.", "나는 분명 빨리 갔건만, 마치 두 발에 납덩이가 매달린 듯, 몸의 마디마디가 녹슨 듯 느껴졌다."], ["When I got almost to the top I could see the seat and the white figure, for I was now close enough to distinguish it even through the spells of shadow.", "거의 꼭대기에 다다랐을 때 그 자리와 흰 형체가 보였으니, 이제 가까이 와 그림자가 드리운 사이로도 그것을 분간할 만했기 때문이다."], ["There was undoubtedly something, long and black, bending over the half-reclining white figure.", "반쯤 기대 누운 흰 형체 위로 길고 검은 무언가가 몸을 굽히고 있는 것이 틀림없었다."], ["I called in fright, “Lucy! Lucy!”", "나는 겁에 질려 \"루시! 루시!\""], ["and something raised a head, and from where I was I could see a white face and red, gleaming eyes.", "하고 불렀고, 무언가가 고개를 들었는데, 내가 선 자리에서도 흰 얼굴과 붉게 번득이는 두 눈이 보였다."], ["Lucy did not answer, and I ran on to the entrance of the churchyard.", "루시는 대답하지 않았고, 나는 묘지 입구로 달려갔다."], ["As I entered, the church was between me and the seat, and for a minute or so I lost sight of her.", "안으로 들어서자 성당이 나와 그 자리 사이를 가로막아, 한동안 나는 그녀를 시야에서 놓쳤다."], ["When I came in view again the cloud had passed, and the moonlight struck so brilliantly that I could see Lucy half reclining with her head lying over the back of the seat.", "다시 시야에 들어왔을 때 구름은 지나가 있었고, 달빛이 어찌나 환히 비추던지 나는 루시가 머리를 그 자리 등받이에 기댄 채 반쯤 누워 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["She was quite alone, and there was not a sign of any living thing about.", "그녀는 완전히 혼자였고, 주위에는 살아 있는 것의 자취라곤 하나도 없었다."]], [["When I bent over her I could see that she was still asleep.", "내가 그녀 위로 몸을 굽히자 그녀가 아직 잠들어 있음을 알 수 있었다."], ["Her lips were parted, and she was breathing--not softly as usual with her, but in long, heavy gasps, as though striving to get her lungs full at every breath.", "입술이 벌어져 있었고, 그녀는 숨을 쉬고 있었는데 — 평소처럼 가만한 숨이 아니라, 매번 숨마다 폐를 가득 채우려 안간힘을 쓰는 듯, 길고 무거운 헐떡임이었다."], ["As I came close, she put up her hand in her sleep and pulled the collar of her nightdress close around her throat.", "내가 가까이 다가가자, 그녀는 잠결에 손을 들어 잠옷 깃을 끌어당겨 목을 단단히 여몄다."], ["Whilst she did so there came a little shudder through her, as though she felt the cold.", "그러면서 그녀의 몸에 작은 떨림이 스쳤는데, 마치 추위를 느끼는 듯했다."], ["I flung the warm shawl over her, and drew the edges tight round her neck, for I dreaded lest she should get some deadly chill from the night air, unclad as she was.", "나는 따뜻한 숄을 그녀에게 둘러주고 그 가장자리를 목 둘레에 단단히 여며주었으니, 그렇게 헐벗은 채로 밤공기에 치명적인 한기라도 들까 두려웠기 때문이다."], ["I feared to wake her all at once, so, in order to have my hands free that I might help her, I fastened the shawl at her throat with a big safety-pin; but I must have been clumsy in my anxiety and pinched or pricked her with it, for by-and-by, when her breathing became quieter, she put her hand to her throat again and moaned.", "그녀를 단번에 깨우기가 겁이 나, 그녀를 돌보느라 두 손을 자유롭게 쓰려고 나는 커다란 안전핀으로 그녀의 목께에 숄을 여며 고정했다. 그런데 조바심 탓에 서툴렀던지 핀으로 그녀를 꼬집었거나 찌른 모양으로, 이윽고 그녀의 숨결이 한결 잦아들었을 때 그녀는 다시 손을 목으로 가져가며 신음했다."], ["When I had her carefully wrapped up I put my shoes on her feet and then began very gently to wake her.", "그녀를 조심스레 잘 감싼 뒤, 나는 내 신발을 그녀의 발에 신기고 아주 부드럽게 그녀를 깨우기 시작했다."], ["At first she did not respond; but gradually she became more and more uneasy in her sleep, moaning and sighing occasionally.", "처음에는 반응이 없었으나, 차츰 그녀는 잠결에 점점 더 불안해하며 이따금 신음하고 한숨을 내쉬었다."], ["At last, as time was passing fast, and, for many other reasons, I wished to get her home at once, I shook her more forcibly, till finally she opened her eyes and awoke.", "마침내 시간이 빠르게 흐르고 있었고, 또 여러 다른 이유로도 나는 그녀를 당장 집으로 데려가고 싶었기에, 더 세게 흔들었더니 마침내 그녀가 눈을 뜨고 깨어났다."], ["She did not seem surprised to see me, as, of course, she did not realise all at once where she was.", "그녀는 나를 보고도 놀라는 기색이 없었는데, 물론 자기가 어디 있는지 단번에 깨닫지 못했기 때문이었다."], ["Lucy always wakes prettily, and even at such a time, when her body must have been chilled with cold, and her mind somewhat appalled at waking unclad in a churchyard at night, she did not lose her grace.", "루시는 언제나 깰 때 어여쁜데, 몸이 추위로 얼었을 것이 분명하고, 밤중에 묘지에서 헐벗은 채 깨어나 마음이 적잖이 질겁했을 그런 때에도 그녀는 우아함을 잃지 않았다."], ["She trembled a little, and clung to me; when I told her to come at once with me home she rose without a word, with the obedience of a child.", "그녀는 조금 떨며 나에게 매달렸다. 당장 함께 집으로 가자고 하자 그녀는 한마디 없이, 어린아이처럼 고분고분 몸을 일으켰다."], ["As we passed along, the gravel hurt my feet, and Lucy noticed me wince.", "우리가 지나가는 동안 자갈이 내 발을 아프게 했고, 루시는 내가 움찔하는 것을 알아챘다."], ["She stopped and wanted to insist upon my taking my shoes; but I would not.", "그녀는 걸음을 멈추고 내 신발을 도로 신으라고 한사코 우겼으나, 나는 그러지 않았다."], ["However, when we got to the pathway outside the churchyard, where there was a puddle of water, remaining from the storm, I daubed my feet with mud, using each foot in turn on the other, so that as we went home, no one, in case we should meet any one, should notice my bare feet.", "그래도 묘지 밖 오솔길에 이르니, 폭풍우가 남긴 물웅덩이가 있어, 나는 한 발씩 번갈아 다른 발에 비벼 진흙을 발에 발랐으니, 집으로 가는 길에 혹 누구를 마주치더라도 내 맨발을 아무도 알아채지 못하게 하려는 것이었다."]], [["Fortune favoured us, and we got home without meeting a soul.", "운이 우리 편이어서, 우리는 사람 하나 마주치지 않고 집에 닿았다."], ["Once we saw a man, who seemed not quite sober, passing along a street in front of us; but we hid in a door till he had disappeared up an opening such as there are here, steep little closes, or “wynds,” as they call them in Scotland.", "한 번 우리 앞 거리를 지나가는, 술이 좀 덜 깬 듯한 남자 하나를 보았으나, 우리는 그가 이곳에 흔한 그런 골목 — 가파르고 작은 샛길, 스코틀랜드에서는 \"윈드\"라고 부르는 — 으로 사라질 때까지 어느 문간에 숨었다."], ["My heart beat so loud all the time that sometimes I thought I should faint.", "내내 심장이 어찌나 크게 뛰던지 이따금 기절할 것만 같았다."], ["I was filled with anxiety about Lucy, not only for her health, lest she should suffer from the exposure, but for her reputation in case the story should get wind.", "나는 루시 걱정으로 가득했는데, 그녀의 건강을 위해서, 곧 한기에 노출되어 탈이 날까 봐서만이 아니라, 혹 이 일이 소문이라도 날 경우 그녀의 평판을 위해서이기도 했다."], ["When we got in, and had washed our feet, and had said a prayer of thankfulness together, I tucked her into bed.", "우리가 안에 들어와 발을 씻고 함께 감사의 기도를 올린 뒤, 나는 그녀를 침대에 뉘어 이불을 덮어주었다."], ["Before falling asleep she asked--even implored--me not to say a word to any one, even her mother, about her sleep-walking adventure.", "잠들기 전에 그녀는 자기 몽유 사건에 대해 누구에게도, 심지어 자기 어머니에게도 한마디도 하지 말아달라고 청했다 — 아니, 간청했다."], ["I hesitated at first to promise; but on thinking of the state of her mother’s health, and how the knowledge of such a thing would fret her, and thinking, too, of how such a story might become distorted--nay, infallibly would--in case it should leak out, I thought it wiser to do so. I hope I did right.", "처음에 나는 약속하기를 망설였으나, 그녀 어머니의 건강 상태를, 그리고 그런 일을 알게 되면 얼마나 애를 태우실지를 생각하고, 또 그런 이야기가 새어 나갈 경우 얼마나 왜곡될지 — 아니, 틀림없이 왜곡되고 말 것을 생각하니, 그러는 편이 현명하겠다 싶었다. 내가 옳게 한 것이기를 바란다."], ["I have locked the door, and the key is tied to my wrist, so perhaps I shall not be again disturbed.", "나는 문을 잠갔고, 열쇠를 손목에 묶어두었으니, 어쩌면 다시는 방해받지 않을지도 모르겠다."], ["Lucy is sleeping soundly; the reflex of the dawn is high and far over the sea....", "루시는 곤히 잠들어 있다. 새벽의 어스름이 바다 위로 높이, 멀리 비치고 있다……"]], [["Same day, noon.--All goes well.", "같은 날, 정오. — 모든 것이 순조롭다."], ["Lucy slept till I woke her and seemed not to have even changed her side.", "루시는 내가 깨울 때까지 잤고, 돌아눕지조차 않은 듯했다."], ["The adventure of the night does not seem to have harmed her; on the contrary, it has benefited her, for she looks better this morning than she has done for weeks.", "간밤의 모험이 그녀에게 해를 끼친 것 같지는 않다. 오히려 그것이 그녀에게 이로웠으니, 오늘 아침 그녀는 몇 주 만에 가장 좋아 보인다."], ["I was sorry to notice that my clumsiness with the safety-pin hurt her.", "안전핀을 다루다 서툴러 그녀에게 상처를 입힌 것을 알고 나는 마음이 아팠다."], ["Indeed, it might have been serious, for the skin of her throat was pierced.", "사실 자칫 심각할 수도 있었으니, 그녀의 목 살갗이 뚫려 있었던 것이다."], ["I must have pinched up a piece of loose skin and have transfixed it, for there are two little red points like pin-pricks, and on the band of her nightdress was a drop of blood.", "분명 늘어진 살갗 한 점을 집어 올려 꿰뚫었던 모양으로, 바늘로 찌른 듯한 작고 붉은 점 두 개가 있고, 그녀의 잠옷 깃에는 핏방울 하나가 묻어 있었다."], ["When I apologised and was concerned about it, she laughed and petted me, and said she did not even feel it.", "내가 사과하며 그 일을 마음 쓰자, 그녀는 웃으며 나를 다독였고, 아무 느낌조차 없었다고 말했다."], ["Fortunately it cannot leave a scar, as it is so tiny.", "다행히 너무 작아서 흉터는 남지 않을 것이다."]], [["Same day, night.--We passed a happy day.", "같은 날, 밤. — 우리는 행복한 하루를 보냈다."], ["The air was clear, and the sun bright, and there was a cool breeze.", "공기는 맑고 햇살은 밝았으며, 시원한 산들바람이 불었다."], ["We took our lunch to Mulgrave Woods, Mrs. Westenra driving by the road and Lucy and I walking by the cliff-path and joining her at the gate.", "우리는 점심을 싸들고 멀그레이브 숲으로 갔는데, 웨스튼라 부인은 길을 따라 마차로 가고, 루시와 나는 절벽 오솔길로 걸어가 문 앞에서 부인과 합류했다."], ["I felt a little sad myself, for I could not but feel how absolutely happy it would have been had Jonathan been with me. But there!", "나는 좀 서글퍼졌으니, 조너선이 나와 함께 있었더라면 이 일이 얼마나 완전히 행복했을까 하는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었기 때문이다. 그러나 어쩌랴!"], ["I must only be patient.", "나는 그저 참고 견뎌야 한다."], ["In the evening we strolled in the Casino Terrace, and heard some good music by Spohr and Mackenzie, and went to bed early.", "저녁에 우리는 카지노 테라스를 거닐며 슈포어와 매켄지의 좋은 음악을 듣고, 일찍 잠자리에 들었다."], ["Lucy seems more restful than she has been for some time, and fell asleep at once.", "루시는 한동안 보지 못한 편안한 모습이었고, 곧바로 잠이 들었다."], ["I shall lock the door and secure the key the same as before, though I do not expect any trouble to-night.", "오늘 밤은 별 탈이 없으리라 예상하지만, 나는 전과 똑같이 문을 잠그고 열쇠를 챙겨둘 것이다."]], [["12 August.--My expectations were wrong, for twice during the night I was wakened by Lucy trying to get out.", "8월 12일. — 내 예상은 틀렸으니, 밤사이 두 번이나 루시가 밖으로 나가려 하는 통에 잠을 깼기 때문이다."], ["She seemed, even in her sleep, to be a little impatient at finding the door shut, and went back to bed under a sort of protest.", "그녀는 잠결에도 문이 잠긴 것을 알고 조금 짜증이 난 듯했고, 마지못해 못 이기는 척 침대로 돌아갔다."], ["I woke with the dawn, and heard the birds chirping outside of the window.", "나는 새벽녘에 깨어, 창밖에서 새들이 지저귀는 소리를 들었다."], ["Lucy woke, too, and, I was glad to see, was even better than on the previous morning.", "루시도 깨어났는데, 반갑게도 전날 아침보다 한층 더 좋아 보였다."], ["All her old gaiety of manner seemed to have come back, and she came and snuggled in beside me and told me all about Arthur.", "예전의 그 명랑한 태도가 모두 되돌아온 듯, 그녀는 다가와 내 곁에 파고들어 아서에 대한 이야기를 모두 들려주었다."], ["I told her how anxious I was about Jonathan, and then she tried to comfort me.", "나는 조너선이 얼마나 걱정스러운지를 말했고, 그러자 그녀는 나를 위로하려 애썼다."], ["Well, she succeeded somewhat, for, though sympathy can’t alter facts, it can help to make them more bearable.", "음, 어느 정도는 성공했으니, 동정이 사실을 바꿀 수는 없어도 그것을 더 견딜 만하게 해주는 데는 도움이 되기 때문이다."]], [["13 August.--Another quiet day, and to bed with the key on my wrist as before.", "8월 13일. — 또 한 번 조용한 하루를 보내고, 전과 같이 열쇠를 손목에 묶고 잠자리에 들었다."], ["Again I awoke in the night, and found Lucy sitting up in bed, still asleep, pointing to the window.", "나는 밤중에 다시 깨어, 루시가 침대에 일어나 앉은 채 여전히 잠든 상태로 창을 가리키고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["I got up quietly, and pulling aside the blind, looked out.", "나는 조용히 일어나 차양을 옆으로 젖히고 밖을 내다보았다."], ["It was brilliant moonlight, and the soft effect of the light over the sea and sky--merged together in one great, silent mystery--was beautiful beyond words.", "환한 달빛이었고, 하나의 거대하고 고요한 신비로 어우러진 바다와 하늘 위로 부드럽게 어린 빛의 정취는 말로 다 할 수 없이 아름다웠다."], ["Between me and the moonlight flitted a great bat, coming and going in great whirling circles.", "나와 달빛 사이로 큰 박쥐 한 마리가 휙휙 날며, 커다랗게 빙빙 도는 원을 그리며 오갔다."], ["Once or twice it came quite close, but was, I suppose, frightened at seeing me, and flitted away across the harbour towards the abbey.", "한두 번은 아주 가까이 다가왔으나, 짐작건대 나를 보고 놀란 듯, 항구를 가로질러 수도원 쪽으로 날아가버렸다."], ["When I came back from the window Lucy had lain down again, and was sleeping peacefully.", "내가 창가에서 돌아왔을 때 루시는 다시 누워 평온히 잠들어 있었다."], ["She did not stir again all night.", "그녀는 밤새 다시 뒤척이지 않았다."]], [["14 August.--On the East Cliff, reading and writing all day.", "8월 14일. — 온종일 이스트 클리프에서 책을 읽고 글을 썼다."], ["Lucy seems to have become as much in love with the spot as I am, and it is hard to get her away from it when it is time to come home for lunch or tea or dinner.", "루시도 나만큼이나 이 자리를 사랑하게 된 듯, 점심이나 차, 저녁식사 때문에 집으로 돌아갈 시간이 되어도 그녀를 떼어놓기가 어렵다."], ["This afternoon she made a funny remark.", "오늘 오후 그녀는 묘한 말을 했다."], ["We were coming home for dinner, and had come to the top of the steps up from the West Pier and stopped to look at the view, as we generally do.", "우리는 저녁식사를 하러 집으로 돌아오는 길에, 웨스트 피어에서 올라오는 계단 꼭대기에 이르러, 늘 그러듯 풍경을 보려고 멈춰 섰다."], ["The setting sun, low down in the sky, was just dropping behind Kettleness; the red light was thrown over on the East Cliff and the old abbey, and seemed to bathe everything in a beautiful rosy glow.", "하늘 낮게 걸린 지는 해가 막 케틀네스 너머로 떨어지고 있었다. 붉은 빛이 이스트 클리프와 옛 수도원 위로 던져져, 온 세상을 아름다운 장밋빛 광휘 속에 잠기게 하는 듯했다."], ["We were silent for a while, and suddenly Lucy murmured as if to herself:--", "우리는 한동안 말이 없었는데, 갑자기 루시가 혼잣말하듯 중얼거렸다."]], [["“His red eyes again! They are just the same.”", "\"그 붉은 눈이 또! 똑같아.\""], ["It was such an odd expression, coming apropos of nothing, that it quite startled me.", "아무런 맥락도 없이 튀어나온 어찌나 야릇한 말이던지 나는 적잖이 놀랐다."], ["I slewed round a little, so as to see Lucy well without seeming to stare at her, and saw that she was in a half-dreamy state, with an odd look on her face that I could not quite make out; so I said nothing, but followed her eyes.", "나는 루시를 빤히 쳐다보는 것처럼 보이지 않으면서도 그녀를 잘 볼 수 있도록 몸을 조금 돌렸는데, 그녀가 반쯤 꿈꾸는 듯한 상태로, 얼굴에 내가 도무지 알 수 없는 묘한 표정을 띠고 있는 것을 보았다. 그래서 나는 아무 말 없이 그녀의 시선을 따라갔다."], ["She appeared to be looking over at our own seat, whereon was a dark figure seated alone.", "그녀는 우리 자리 쪽을 바라보고 있는 듯했는데, 그곳에는 어두운 형체 하나가 홀로 앉아 있었다."], ["I was a little startled myself, for it seemed for an instant as if the stranger had great eyes like burning flames; but a second look dispelled the illusion.", "나도 좀 놀랐으니, 한순간 그 낯선 이가 불타는 화염 같은 커다란 두 눈을 가진 것처럼 보였기 때문이다."], ["The red sunlight was shining on the windows of St.", "그러나 다시 보니 그 착각은 사라졌다."], ["Mary’s Church behind our seat, and as the sun dipped there was just sufficient change in the refraction and reflection to make it appear as if the light moved.", "붉은 햇빛이 우리 자리 뒤의 성 메리 성당 창문에 비치고 있었고, 해가 기울면서 굴절과 반사가 꼭 빛이 움직이는 것처럼 보이게 할 만큼 변했던 것이다."], ["I called Lucy’s attention to the peculiar effect, and she became herself with a start, but she looked sad all the same; it may have been that she was thinking of that terrible night up there.", "나는 루시에게 그 특이한 현상을 일러주었고, 그녀는 흠칫하며 제정신으로 돌아왔으나, 그래도 슬퍼 보였다. 어쩌면 저 위에서 겪은 그 끔찍한 밤을 떠올리고 있었는지도 모른다. 우리는 그 일을 결코 입에 올리지 않는다."], ["We never refer to it; so I said nothing, and we went home to dinner.", "그래서 나는 아무 말도 하지 않았고, 우리는 저녁식사를 하러 집으로 돌아갔다."], ["Lucy had a headache and went early to bed.", "루시는 머리가 아프다며 일찍 잠자리에 들었다."], ["I saw her asleep, and went out for a little stroll myself; I walked along the cliffs to the westward, and was full of sweet sadness, for I was thinking of Jonathan.", "나는 그녀가 잠든 것을 보고, 잠시 산책을 나갔다. 나는 절벽을 따라 서쪽으로 걸으며 달콤한 슬픔에 잠겼으니, 조너선을 생각하고 있었기 때문이다."], ["When coming home--it was then bright moonlight, so bright that, though the front of our part of the Crescent was in shadow, everything could be well seen--I threw a glance up at our window, and saw Lucy’s head leaning out.", "집으로 돌아오는 길에 — 그때는 환한 달빛이었고, 어찌나 환하던지 우리가 묵는 크레센트 구역의 정면은 그늘에 잠겨 있었어도 모든 것이 잘 보였다 — 나는 우리 창을 흘끗 올려다보다, 루시의 머리가 밖으로 내밀어진 것을 보았다."], ["I thought that perhaps she was looking out for me, so I opened my handkerchief and waved it.", "어쩌면 그녀가 나를 기다리며 내다보는 것인가 싶어, 나는 손수건을 펼쳐 흔들었다."], ["She did not notice or make any movement whatever.", "그녀는 알아채지도, 아무 움직임도 보이지 않았다."], ["Just then, the moonlight crept round an angle of the building, and the light fell on the window.", "바로 그때 달빛이 건물 모퉁이를 돌아 들어와, 그 빛이 창에 떨어졌다."], ["There distinctly was Lucy with her head lying up against the side of the window-sill and her eyes shut.", "거기에 루시가 분명히 있었으니, 머리를 창턱 옆면에 기대고 두 눈을 감은 채였다."], ["She was fast asleep, and by her, seated on the window-sill, was something that looked like a good-sized bird.", "그녀는 곤히 잠들어 있었고, 그녀 곁 창턱에는 제법 큼직한 새처럼 보이는 무언가가 앉아 있었다."], ["I was afraid she might get a chill, so I ran upstairs, but as I came into the room she was moving back to her bed, fast asleep, and breathing heavily; she was holding her hand to her throat, as though to protect it from cold.", "나는 그녀가 한기가 들까 두려워 위층으로 달려갔으나, 방에 들어서자 그녀는 곤히 잠든 채, 숨을 거칠게 몰아쉬며 다시 침대 쪽으로 물러나고 있었다. 그녀는 마치 추위로부터 목을 보호하려는 듯, 한 손을 목에 대고 있었다."]], [["I did not wake her, but tucked her up warmly; I have taken care that the door is locked and the window securely fastened.", "나는 그녀를 깨우지 않고, 따뜻하게 이불을 덮어주었다. 문이 잠겼는지, 창이 단단히 잠겼는지 단속해 두었다."]], [["She looks so sweet as she sleeps; but she is paler than is her wont, and there is a drawn, haggard look under her eyes which I do not like.", "그녀는 잠든 모습이 어찌나 사랑스러운지. 그러나 평소보다 얼굴이 창백하고, 두 눈 밑에 내가 마음에 들지 않는, 핼쑥하고 까칠한 기색이 어려 있다."], ["I fear she is fretting about something.", "그녀가 무언가로 애를 태우고 있는 것이 아닐까 걱정스럽다."], ["I wish I could find out what it is.", "그것이 무엇인지 알아낼 수 있다면 좋으련만."]], [["15 August.--Rose later than usual.", "8월 15일. — 평소보다 늦게 일어났다."], ["Lucy was languid and tired, and slept on after we had been called.", "루시는 나른하고 지쳐 있었고, 우리가 불려 일어난 뒤에도 계속 잤다."], ["We had a happy surprise at breakfast.", "우리는 아침식사 때 기쁜 소식에 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["Arthur’s father is better, and wants the marriage to come off soon.", "아서의 아버지가 차도가 있어, 결혼이 곧 이루어지기를 바란다는 것이었다."], ["Lucy is full of quiet joy, and her mother is glad and sorry at once.", "루시는 조용한 기쁨으로 가득하고, 그녀의 어머니는 기뻐하면서도 한편으로 서운해한다."], ["Later on in the day she told me the cause.", "그날 늦게 부인은 나에게 그 까닭을 말해주었다."], ["She is grieved to lose Lucy as her very own, but she is rejoiced that she is soon to have some one to protect her.", "루시를 온전히 자기 것으로 두지 못하고 떠나보내야 해서 슬프지만, 머지않아 그녀를 지켜줄 누군가가 생긴다는 것이 기쁘다는 것이었다."], ["Poor dear, sweet lady!", "가엾고 사랑스러운 부인!"], ["She confided to me that she has got her death-warrant.", "부인은 자기가 사형선고를 받았음을 나에게 털어놓았다."], ["She has not told Lucy, and made me promise secrecy; her doctor told her that within a few months, at most, she must die, for her heart is weakening.", "부인은 루시에게는 말하지 않았고, 나에게 비밀을 지킬 것을 약속하게 했다. 의사가 부인에게, 길어야 몇 달 안에 죽게 될 것이라고 했다는 것이니, 심장이 약해지고 있기 때문이었다."], ["At any time, even now, a sudden shock would be almost sure to kill her.", "언제라도, 지금이라도, 갑작스러운 충격을 받으면 거의 틀림없이 목숨을 잃으리라는 것이었다."], ["Ah, we were wise to keep from her the affair of the dreadful night of Lucy’s sleep-walking.", "아, 루시의 그 끔찍한 몽유의 밤 일을 부인에게 숨긴 것은 현명한 일이었다."]], [["17 August.--No diary for two whole days.", "8월 17일. — 꼬박 이틀 동안 일기를 쓰지 못했다."], ["I have not had the heart to write.", "글을 쓸 마음이 나지 않았다."], ["Some sort of shadowy pall seems to be coming over our happiness.", "어떤 어두운 장막 같은 것이 우리의 행복 위로 드리워지는 듯하다."], ["No news from Jonathan, and Lucy seems to be growing weaker, whilst her mother’s hours are numbering to a close.", "조너선에게서는 아무 소식이 없고, 루시는 점점 쇠약해지는 듯하며, 그녀 어머니의 남은 시간은 끝을 향해 줄어들고 있다."], ["I do not understand Lucy’s fading away as she is doing.", "나는 루시가 지금처럼 시들어가는 까닭을 이해할 수 없다."], ["She eats well and sleeps well, and enjoys the fresh air; but all the time the roses in her cheeks are fading, and she gets weaker and more languid day by day; at night I hear her gasping as if for air.", "그녀는 잘 먹고 잘 자고 신선한 공기를 즐긴다. 그런데도 내내 두 뺨의 장밋빛은 바래어가고, 날이 갈수록 더 약해지고 더 나른해진다. 밤이면 나는 그녀가 공기를 갈구하듯 헐떡이는 소리를 듣는다."], ["I keep the key of our door always fastened to my wrist at night, but she gets up and walks about the room, and sits at the open window.", "나는 밤이면 늘 방문 열쇠를 손목에 묶어두지만, 그녀는 일어나 방 안을 돌아다니고 열린 창가에 앉는다."], ["Last night I found her leaning out when I woke up, and when I tried to wake her I could not; she was in a faint.", "간밤에는 잠에서 깨어보니 그녀가 밖으로 몸을 내밀고 있었는데, 깨우려 해도 깨울 수가 없었다. 그녀는 기절해 있었던 것이다."], ["When I managed to restore her she was as weak as water, and cried silently between long, painful struggles for breath.", "가까스로 그녀를 정신 차리게 했을 때 그녀는 물처럼 기운이 없었고, 길고 고통스럽게 숨을 몰아쉬는 사이사이 소리 없이 흐느꼈다."], ["When I asked her how she came to be at the window she shook her head and turned away.", "어쩌다 창가에 와 있게 되었느냐고 묻자 그녀는 고개를 저으며 돌아누웠다."], ["I trust her feeling ill may not be from that unlucky prick of the safety-pin.", "그녀가 몸이 좋지 않은 것이 그 불운한 안전핀 자국 탓이 아니기를 바란다."], ["I looked at her throat just now as she lay asleep, and the tiny wounds seem not to have healed.", "방금 그녀가 잠든 사이 목을 살펴보았는데, 그 작은 상처들이 아물지 않은 듯하다."], ["They are still open, and, if anything, larger than before, and the edges of them are faintly white.", "여전히 벌어져 있고, 오히려 전보다 더 커졌으며, 가장자리가 희미하게 희다."], ["They are like little white dots with red centres.", "가운데가 붉은, 작고 흰 점 같다."], ["Unless they heal within a day or two, I shall insist on the doctor seeing about them.", "하루 이틀 안에 아물지 않으면, 나는 의사가 그것을 살펴보도록 고집할 것이다."]], [["Letter, Samuel F.", "「휘트비의 변호사 새뮤얼 F."], ["Billington & Son, Solicitors, Whitby, to Messrs. Carter, Paterson & Co., London.", "빌링턴 부자(父子) 상회가 런던의 카터, 패터슨 상회 귀하에게 보낸 편지」"]], [["“17 August.", "8월 17일."]], [["“Dear Sirs,--", "근계(謹啓) —"]], [["“Herewith please receive invoice of goods sent by Great Northern Railway.", "이에 그레이트 노던 철도편으로 보낸 화물의 송장(送狀)을 동봉하오."], ["Same are to be delivered at Carfax, near Purfleet, immediately on receipt at goods station King’s Cross.", "화물은 킹스 크로스 화물역에 도착하는 즉시 퍼플리트 인근 카팩스로 배달되어야 하오."], ["The house is at present empty, but enclosed please find keys, all of which are labelled.", "그 집은 현재 비어 있으나, 동봉한 열쇠들을 확인하시기 바라며, 모든 열쇠에는 꼬리표가 달려 있소."]], [["“You will please deposit the boxes, fifty in number, which form the consignment, in the partially ruined building forming part of the house and marked ‘A’ on rough diagram enclosed.", "이 탁송품을 이루는 상자 쉰 개를, 집의 일부를 이루며 동봉한 약도(略圖)에 'A'로 표시된 반쯤 허물어진 건물 안에 들여놓아 주시기 바라오."], ["Your agent will easily recognise the locality, as it is the ancient chapel of the mansion.", "그곳은 저택의 옛 예배당이므로 귀사 직원이 어렵지 않게 알아볼 것이오."], ["The goods leave by the train at 9:30 to-night, and will be due at King’s Cross at 4:30 to-morrow afternoon.", "화물은 오늘 밤 9시 30분 기차로 떠나, 내일 오후 4시 30분에 킹스 크로스에 도착할 예정이오."], ["As our client wishes the delivery made as soon as possible, we shall be obliged by your having teams ready at King’s Cross at the time named and forthwith conveying the goods to destination.", "우리 의뢰인이 가능한 한 빠른 배달을 바라므로, 지정된 시각에 킹스 크로스에 운반조를 대기시켜 즉시 화물을 목적지로 운반해 주신다면 감사하겠소."], ["In order to obviate any delays possible through any routine requirements as to payment in your departments, we enclose cheque herewith for ten pounds (£10), receipt of which please acknowledge.", "귀사 부서의 대금 지불 관련 통상 절차로 인해 생길 수 있는 일체의 지연을 피하고자, 이에 십 파운드(10£)짜리 수표를 동봉하니, 수령하시거든 알려주시기 바라오."], ["Should the charge be less than this amount, you can return balance; if greater, we shall at once send cheque for difference on hearing from you.", "비용이 이 금액에 못 미친다면 차액을 돌려주시면 되고, 초과한다면 귀사의 연락을 받는 즉시 차액 수표를 보내드리겠소."], ["You are to leave the keys on coming away in the main hall of the house, where the proprietor may get them on his entering the house by means of his duplicate key.", "떠나실 때 열쇠들은 집의 중앙 홀에 두고 가시기 바라며, 그리하면 소유주가 자기 여벌 열쇠로 집에 들어가 그것들을 받을 수 있을 것이오."]], [["“Pray do not take us as exceeding the bounds of business courtesy in pressing you in all ways to use the utmost expedition.", "만사에 최대한 신속히 처리해 주실 것을 거듭 청하는 것을 두고, 부디 우리가 상업적 예의의 도를 넘었다고는 여기지 말아 주시오."]], [["_“We are, dear Sirs,", "이만 줄이오, 근계 —"]], [["“Faithfully yours,", "충실한,"]], [["Letter, Messrs.", "새뮤얼 F."], ["Carter, Paterson & Co., London, to Messrs. Billington & Son, Whitby.", "빌링턴 부자 상회. 「런던의 카터, 패터슨 상회가 휘트비의 빌링턴 부자 상회 귀하에게 보낸 편지」"]], [["“21 August.", "8월 21일."]], [["“Dear Sirs,--", "근계 —"]], [["“We beg to acknowledge £10 received and to return cheque £1 17s. 9d, amount of overplus, as shown in receipted account herewith.", "10£를 수령하였음을 알리며, 이에 동봉한 영수 계산서에 나타난 대로 초과액 1£ 17실링 9펜스의 수표를 돌려드리오."], ["Goods are delivered in exact accordance with instructions, and keys left in parcel in main hall, as directed.", "화물은 지시에 정확히 따라 배달되었으며, 열쇠들은 지시대로 꾸러미에 담아 중앙 홀에 두었소."]], [["“We are, dear Sirs,", "이만 줄이오, 근계 —"]], [["“Yours respectfully.", "삼가 올림."]], [["Mina Murray’s Journal.", "미나 머리의 일기."]], [["18 August.--I am happy to-day, and write sitting on the seat in the churchyard.", "「미나 머리의 일기」 8월 18일. — 오늘 나는 행복하여, 묘지의 그 자리에 앉아 글을 쓴다."], ["Lucy is ever so much better.", "루시는 더없이 좋아졌다."], ["Last night she slept well all night, and did not disturb me once.", "간밤에 그녀는 밤새 잘 잤고, 단 한 번도 나를 깨우지 않았다."], ["The roses seem coming back already to her cheeks, though she is still sadly pale and wan-looking.", "두 뺨에 벌써 장밋빛이 돌아오는 듯한데, 아직도 가엾을 만큼 창백하고 핼쑥해 보인다."], ["If she were in any way anæmic I could understand it, but she is not.", "그녀가 어떤 식으로든 빈혈이라면 이해할 수 있으련만, 그렇지가 않다."], ["She is in gay spirits and full of life and cheerfulness.", "그녀는 명랑하고 생기와 쾌활함으로 가득하다."], ["All the morbid reticence seems to have passed from her, and she has just reminded me, as if I needed any reminding, of that night, and that it was here, on this very seat, I found her asleep.", "그 모든 병적인 과묵함이 사라진 듯, 그녀는 방금 나에게 — 내가 굳이 일깨워질 필요도 없는데 — 그날 밤 일을, 그리고 그곳, 바로 이 자리에서 내가 그녀가 잠든 것을 발견했음을 일깨워 주었다."], ["As she told me she tapped playfully with the heel of her boot on the stone slab and said:--", "그러면서 그녀는 장난스럽게 부츠 뒤꿈치로 돌판을 톡톡 치며 말했다."]], [["“My poor little feet didn’t make much noise then!", "\"내 가엾은 작은 발들이 그땐 별 소리도 안 냈겠지!"], ["I daresay poor old Mr. Swales would have told me that it was because I didn’t want to wake up Geordie.”", "가엾은 스웨일스 영감님이라면, 그건 내가 조디를 깨우고 싶지 않았기 때문이라고 말했을 거야.\""], ["As she was in such a communicative humour, I asked her if she had dreamed at all that night.", "그녀가 이렇게 말이 많은 기분이기에, 나는 그날 밤 꿈을 꾸기라도 했느냐고 물었다."], ["Before she answered, that sweet, puckered look came into her forehead, which Arthur--I call him Arthur from her habit--says he loves; and, indeed, I don’t wonder that he does.", "대답하기 전에, 그 사랑스럽게 찌푸려지는 표정이 그녀의 이마에 떠올랐는데, 아서가 — 나는 그녀의 습관을 따라 그를 아서라고 부른다 — 사랑한다고 하는 바로 그 표정이었다. 사실 그가 그러는 것도 무리는 아니다."], ["Then she went on in a half-dreaming kind of way, as if trying to recall it to herself:--", "그러고 나서 그녀는 반쯤 꿈꾸는 듯한 투로, 마치 그것을 스스로 떠올려 보려는 듯 말을 이었다."]], [["“I didn’t quite dream; but it all seemed to be real.", "\"꿈을 꾼 건 아주 아니었는데, 모든 게 진짜인 것 같았어."], ["I only wanted to be here in this spot--I don’t know why, for I was afraid of something--I don’t know what.", "나는 그저 이 자리에 있고 싶었어 — 왜인지는 모르겠어. 무언가가 무서웠거든 — 그게 뭔지는 모르겠어."], ["I remember, though I suppose I was asleep, passing through the streets and over the bridge.", "잠들어 있었던 것 같은데도, 거리를 지나 다리를 건넌 게 기억나. 내가 지나갈 때 물고기 한 마리가 펄쩍 뛰어올라, 나는 그걸 보려고 몸을 굽혔어."], ["A fish leaped as I went by, and I leaned over to look at it, and I heard a lot of dogs howling--the whole town seemed as if it must be full of dogs all howling at once--as I went up the steps.", "그리고 계단을 올라가는데 개들이 잔뜩 짖는 소리가 들렸어 — 마치 온 마을이 한꺼번에 짖어대는 개들로 가득 찬 것 같았어. 그러고 나서 길고 검고 붉은 눈을 한 무언가에 대한 어렴풋한 기억이 났어, 꼭 우리가 노을 속에서 본 그것처럼. 그리고 아주 달콤하면서도 아주 쓴 무언가가 한꺼번에 내 주위를 온통 감쌌어."], ["Then I had a vague memory of something long and dark with red eyes, just as we saw in the sunset, and something very sweet and very bitter all around me at once; and then I seemed sinking into deep green water, and there was a singing in my ears, as I have heard there is to drowning men; and then everything seemed passing away from me; my soul seemed to go out from my body and float about the air.", "그러더니 나는 깊은 초록 물속으로 가라앉는 것 같았고, 귓속에서 노랫소리가 울렸어, 물에 빠진 사람에게 그런 것이 들린다고 들은 그대로. 그러고는 모든 것이 나에게서 멀어져 가는 듯했어. 내 영혼이 몸에서 빠져나가 공중을 떠도는 것 같았어."], ["I seem to remember that once the West Lighthouse was right under me, and then there was a sort of agonising feeling, as if I were in an earthquake, and I came back and found you shaking my body.", "한번은 웨스트 등대가 바로 내 발밑에 있었던 것 같은 기억이 나. 그러더니 무언가 괴로운 느낌이 들었어, 마치 내가 지진 속에 있는 것처럼. 그러고는 다시 돌아와 보니 네가 내 몸을 흔들고 있었어."], ["I saw you do it before I felt you.”", "네가 그러는 걸 느끼기 전에 먼저 보았어.\""]], [["Then she began to laugh.", "그러더니 그녀는 웃기 시작했다."], ["It seemed a little uncanny to me, and I listened to her breathlessly.", "나는 그것이 좀 으스스하게 여겨져, 숨죽인 채 그녀의 말에 귀를 기울였다."], ["I did not quite like it, and thought it better not to keep her mind on the subject, so we drifted on to other subjects, and Lucy was like her old self again.", "나는 그것이 영 마음에 들지 않아, 그녀의 마음을 그 일에 붙들어 두지 않는 편이 낫겠다 싶어, 우리는 다른 화제로 흘러갔고, 루시는 다시 예전의 그녀다워졌다."], ["When we got home the fresh breeze had braced her up, and her pale cheeks were really more rosy.", "우리가 집에 닿았을 때 신선한 바람이 그녀에게 활기를 불어넣어, 창백한 두 뺨이 정말로 한층 장밋빛이 되어 있었다."], ["Her mother rejoiced when she saw her, and we all spent a very happy evening together.", "그녀의 어머니는 그녀를 보고 기뻐했고, 우리 모두는 함께 무척 행복한 저녁을 보냈다."]], [["19 August.--Joy, joy, joy!", "8월 19일. — 기쁨, 기쁨, 기쁨!"], ["although not all joy.", "온통 기쁨만은 아니지만."], ["At last, news of Jonathan.", "마침내 조너선의 소식이 왔다."], ["The dear fellow has been ill; that is why he did not write.", "그 사랑스러운 사람은 그동안 앓았던 것이다. 그래서 편지를 쓰지 못했던 것이다."], ["I am not afraid to think it or say it, now that I know. Mr.", "이제 그것을 알고 나니, 나는 그것을 생각하기도 입에 올리기도 두렵지 않다."], ["Hawkins sent me on the letter, and wrote himself, oh, so kindly. I am to leave in the morning and go over to Jonathan, and to help to nurse him if necessary, and to bring him home. Mr.", "호킨스 씨가 나에게 편지를 부쳐주었고, 자신도 친히, 아, 어찌나 다정하게 써 보냈는지. 나는 아침에 떠나 조너선에게로 건너가, 필요하다면 그를 간호하는 일을 돕고, 그를 집으로 데려와야 한다."], ["Hawkins says it would not be a bad thing if we were to be married out there.", "호킨스 씨는 우리가 거기서 결혼한다 해도 나쁘지 않을 것이라고 한다."], ["I have cried over the good Sister’s letter till I can feel it wet against my bosom, where it lies.", "나는 그 착한 수녀님의 편지를 두고 어찌나 울었던지, 그것이 놓인 내 가슴에 닿아 젖은 것이 느껴질 정도다."], ["It is of Jonathan, and must be next my heart, for he is in my heart.", "그것은 조너선에 관한 것이니, 내 심장 곁에 두어야 한다. 그가 내 심장 속에 있으니까."], ["My journey is all mapped out, and my luggage ready.", "내 여정은 모두 짜여 있고, 짐도 다 꾸렸다."], ["I am only taking one change of dress; Lucy will bring my trunk to London and keep it till I send for it, for it may be that ...", "갈아입을 옷 한 벌만 챙긴다. 루시가 내 트렁크를 런던으로 가져가, 내가 부치라고 할 때까지 맡아둘 것이다. 어쩌면……"], ["I must write no more; I must keep it to say to Jonathan, my husband.", "더 쓰지 말아야겠다. 그건 내 남편 조너선에게 직접 말하려고 아껴두어야겠다."], ["The letter that he has seen and touched must comfort me till we meet.", "그가 보고 만진 편지가, 우리가 만날 때까지 나를 위로해 줄 것이다."]], [["Letter, Sister Agatha, Hospital of St. Joseph and Ste. Mary, Buda-Pesth, to Miss Wilhelmina Murray.", "「부다페스트 성 요셉·성 마리아 병원의 아가타 수녀가 빌헬미나 머리 양에게 보낸 편지」"]], [["“12 August.", "8월 12일."]], [["“Dear Madam,--", "부인께 —"]], [["“I write by desire of Mr. Jonathan Harker, who is himself not strong enough to write, though progressing well, thanks to God and St.", "저는 조너선 하커 씨의 청에 따라 이 글을 씁니다. 그분은 신과 성 요셉, 성 마리아 덕분에 경과가 좋으시나, 아직 손수 글을 쓸 만큼 기력이 없으십니다."], ["Joseph and Ste. Mary. He has been under our care for nearly six weeks, suffering from a violent brain fever.", "그분은 격심한 뇌염을 앓으시며 거의 여섯 주 동안 저희의 보살핌을 받아오셨습니다."], ["He wishes me to convey his love, and to say that by this post I write for him to Mr. Peter Hawkins, Exeter, to say, with his dutiful respects, that he is sorry for his delay, and that all of his work is completed.", "그분은 당신께 사랑을 전해달라 하시며, 또 이 우편으로 제가 그분을 대신하여 엑서터의 피터 호킨스 씨께 편지를 써, 삼가 공손한 인사와 함께, 지체된 것을 죄송히 여기며 자신의 일은 모두 끝마쳤음을 전한다고 말씀드리라 하셨습니다."], ["He will require some few weeks’ rest in our sanatorium in the hills, but will then return.", "그분은 산중의 저희 요양원에서 몇 주간의 휴양이 필요하시겠으나, 그 뒤에는 돌아가실 것입니다."], ["He wishes me to say that he has not sufficient money with him, and that he would like to pay for his staying here, so that others who need shall not be wanting for help.", "그분은, 지니신 돈이 넉넉지 않으나, 이곳 체류비를 치르고자 하시니, 도움이 필요한 다른 이들이 도움을 받지 못하는 일이 없도록 함이라고 전해달라 하셨습니다."]], [["“Believe me,", "저를 믿어 주소서."]], [["“Yours, with sympathy and all blessings,", "동정과 온갖 축복을 담아 올립니다. 아가타 수녀."]], [["“P. S.--My patient being asleep, I open this to let you know something more.", "추신. — 제 환자분이 잠드신 틈에, 좀 더 말씀드리고자 이 편지를 다시 엽니다."], ["He has told me all about you, and that you are shortly to be his wife.", "그분은 당신에 관한 모든 것을, 그리고 당신이 머지않아 자신의 아내가 되리라는 것을 저에게 말씀해 주셨습니다."], ["All blessings to you both!", "두 분께 온갖 축복이 있기를!"], ["He has had some fearful shock--so says our doctor--and in his delirium his ravings have been dreadful; of wolves and poison and blood; of ghosts and demons; and I fear to say of what.", "그분은 — 저희 의사 말씀으로는 — 어떤 무서운 충격을 받으셨고, 헛소리 속에서 그분의 횡설수설은 끔찍했습니다. 늑대와 독약과 피에 대해, 유령과 악마에 대해, 그리고 차마 무엇이라 말씀드리기 두려운 것들에 대해서였습니다."], ["Be careful with him always that there may be nothing to excite him of this kind for a long time to come; the traces of such an illness as his do not lightly die away.", "앞으로 오랫동안 그분을 이런 종류의 것으로 자극할 만한 일이 전혀 없도록 늘 조심하소서. 그분이 앓으신 그런 병의 흔적은 쉽게 사그라지지 않습니다."], ["We should have written long ago, but we knew nothing of his friends, and there was on him nothing that any one could understand.", "저희가 진작 편지를 드렸어야 했으나, 그분의 가족이나 벗에 대해 아무것도 몰랐고, 그분에게는 누구라도 알아볼 만한 것이 아무것도 없었습니다."], ["He came in the train from Klausenburg, and the guard was told by the station-master there that he rushed into the station shouting for a ticket for home.", "그분은 클라우젠부르크에서 오는 기차를 타고 오셨는데, 그곳 역장이 차장에게 전한 바로는, 그분이 집으로 가는 차표를 달라고 외치며 역으로 뛰어들었다 합니다."], ["Seeing from his violent demeanour that he was English, they gave him a ticket for the furthest station on the way thither that the train reached.", "그 격한 거동으로 보아 영국인임을 알아챈 그들은, 기차가 닿는, 그쪽 방향으로 가장 먼 역까지의 차표를 그분께 드렸다 합니다."]], [["“Be assured that he is well cared for.", "그분이 잘 보살핌을 받고 있음을 믿으소서."], ["He has won all hearts by his sweetness and gentleness.", "그분은 그 다정함과 온화함으로 모든 이의 마음을 사로잡으셨습니다."], ["He is truly getting on well, and I have no doubt will in a few weeks be all himself.", "그분은 정말로 경과가 좋으시며, 몇 주 안에 완전히 본래의 모습을 되찾으시리라 저는 의심치 않습니다."], ["But be careful of him for safety’s sake.", "그러나 안전을 위해 그분을 보살피소서."], ["There are, I pray God and St. Joseph and Ste. Mary, many, many, happy years for you both.”", "신과 성 요셉, 성 마리아께 기도하건대, 두 분께 많고 많은 행복한 세월이 있기를 빕니다."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["19 August.--Strange and sudden change in Renfield last night.", "8월 19일. — 간밤에 렌필드에게 이상하고도 갑작스러운 변화가 일어났다."], ["About eight o’clock he began to get excited and sniff about as a dog does when setting.", "여덟 시쯤 그는 흥분하기 시작하더니, 사냥개가 사냥감을 가리킬 때처럼 여기저기 킁킁대며 냄새를 맡았다."], ["The attendant was struck by his manner, and knowing my interest in him, encouraged him to talk.", "그 태도에 놀란 간병인은, 내가 그에게 관심이 있음을 알기에 그에게 말을 시켜보았다."], ["He is usually respectful to the attendant and at times servile; but to-night, the man tells me, he was quite haughty.", "그는 평소 간병인에게 공손하고 때로는 비굴하기까지 한데, 간병인 말로는 오늘 밤에는 자못 거만했다고 한다."], ["Would not condescend to talk with him at all. All he would say was:--", "그와는 도무지 말을 섞으려 들지 않았다. 그가 한 말이라곤 이것뿐이었다."]], [["“I don’t want to talk to you: you don’t count now; the Master is at hand.”", "\"너랑은 얘기하고 싶지 않다. 넌 이제 안중에도 없어. 주인님께서 가까이 와 계시거든.\""]], [["The attendant thinks it is some sudden form of religious mania which has seized him.", "간병인은 그것이 그를 사로잡은 어떤 갑작스러운 형태의 종교적 광증이라고 생각한다."], ["If so, we must look out for squalls, for a strong man with homicidal and religious mania at once might be dangerous.", "그렇다면 우리는 험한 일에 대비해야 하니, 살인과 종교의 광증을 한꺼번에 지닌 힘센 사내는 위험할 수 있기 때문이다."], ["The combination is a dreadful one.", "그 조합은 끔찍한 것이다."], ["At nine o’clock I visited him myself.", "아홉 시에 나는 직접 그를 찾아갔다."], ["His attitude to me was the same as that to the attendant; in his sublime self-feeling the difference between myself and attendant seemed to him as nothing.", "그가 나를 대하는 태도는 간병인을 대하던 것과 똑같았으니, 그 숭고한 자기도취 속에서는 나와 간병인의 차이가 그에게 아무것도 아닌 듯했다."], ["It looks like religious mania, and he will soon think that he himself is God.", "종교적 광증으로 보이는데, 머지않아 그는 자기 자신이 신이라고 여기게 될 것이다."], ["These infinitesimal distinctions between man and man are too paltry for an Omnipotent Being.", "사람과 사람 사이의 이 지극히 사소한 차이들은 전능한 존재에게는 너무도 하찮은 것이다."], ["How these madmen give themselves away!", "이 광인들은 어쩌면 이리도 제 속을 드러내는지!"], ["The real God taketh heed lest a sparrow fall; but the God created from human vanity sees no difference between an eagle and a sparrow.", "진짜 신은 참새 한 마리가 떨어지는 것조차 살피시거늘, 인간의 허영에서 만들어진 신은 독수리와 참새 사이에 아무 차이도 보지 못한다."], ["Oh, if men only knew!", "아, 사람들이 알기만 한다면!"]], [["For half an hour or more Renfield kept getting excited in greater and greater degree.", "삼십 분 남짓 렌필드는 점점 더 격렬하게 흥분해 갔다."], ["I did not pretend to be watching him, but I kept strict observation all the same.", "나는 그를 지켜보는 척하지 않았으나, 그래도 빈틈없이 관찰하고 있었다."], ["All at once that shifty look came into his eyes which we always see when a madman has seized an idea, and with it the shifty movement of the head and back which asylum attendants come to know so well.", "별안간 그의 눈에 그 교활한 빛이 떠올랐으니, 광인이 어떤 생각에 사로잡혔을 때 우리가 늘 보게 되는 그것이었고, 그와 더불어 정신병원 간병인들이 너무도 잘 알게 되는, 머리와 등을 슬쩍 놀리는 그 움직임도 함께였다."], ["He became quite quiet, and went and sat on the edge of his bed resignedly, and looked into space with lack-lustre eyes.", "그는 아주 조용해지더니, 가서 체념한 듯 침대 가장자리에 앉아, 생기 없는 눈으로 허공을 바라보았다."], ["I thought I would find out if his apathy were real or only assumed, and tried to lead him to talk of his pets, a theme which had never failed to excite his attention.", "나는 그의 무관심이 진짜인지 아니면 그저 꾸민 것인지 알아보려고, 그의 애완동물 이야기로 그를 이끌어 보려 했으니, 그것은 한 번도 그의 관심을 끌지 못한 적이 없는 화제였다."], ["At first he made no reply, but at length said testily:--", "처음에 그는 아무 대꾸도 하지 않았으나, 마침내 짜증스럽게 말했다."]], [["“Bother them all!", "\"그놈들 다 귀찮아!"], ["I don’t care a pin about them.”", "나는 그것들에 눈곱만큼도 관심 없어.\""]], [["“What?” I said.", "\"뭐라고요?\""], ["“You don’t mean to tell me you don’t care about spiders?”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"거미들에게 관심이 없다는 말을 하려는 건 아니겠지요?\""], ["(Spiders at present are his hobby and the note-book is filling up with columns of small figures.) To this he answered enigmatically:--", "(요즘 거미가 그의 취미여서, 공책은 작은 숫자들의 행렬로 채워져 가고 있다.) 이에 그는 수수께끼처럼 대답했다."]], [["“The bride-maidens rejoice the eyes that wait the coming of the bride; but when the bride draweth nigh, then the maidens shine not to the eyes that are filled.”", "\"신부의 들러리 처녀들은 신부가 오기를 기다리는 눈을 즐겁게 하나, 신부가 가까이 다가오면 그때엔 처녀들은 가득 찬 눈에 더는 빛나지 않는 법이오.\""]], [["He would not explain himself, but remained obstinately seated on his bed all the time I remained with him.", "그는 자신의 말을 설명하려 하지 않았고, 내가 그와 함께 있는 내내 고집스레 침대에 앉아 있었다."]], [["I am weary to-night and low in spirits.", "오늘 밤 나는 지치고 마음이 가라앉아 있다."], ["I cannot but think of Lucy, and how different things might have been.", "루시를 생각하지 않을 수 없고, 일이 얼마나 달라질 수 있었을까 하는 생각을 떨칠 수 없다."], ["If I don’t sleep at once, chloral, the modern Morpheus--C{2}HCl{3}O. H_{2}O!", "당장 잠들지 못한다면, 근대의 모르페우스인 클로랄을 — C₂HCl₃O·H₂O를!"], ["I must be careful not to let it grow into a habit.", "이것이 습관이 되도록 두지 않게끔 조심해야 한다."], ["No, I shall take none to-night!", "아니, 오늘 밤은 한 방울도 먹지 않겠다!"], ["I have thought of Lucy, and I shall not dishonour her by mixing the two.", "나는 루시를 생각했으니, 그 둘을 뒤섞어 그녀를 욕되게 하지 않겠다."], ["If need be, to-night shall be sleepless....", "정 그래야 한다면, 오늘 밤은 잠 못 드는 밤이 되리라……"]], [["Later.--Glad I made the resolution; gladder that I kept to it.", "나중에. — 그 결심을 한 것이 다행이고, 그것을 지킨 것이 더더욱 다행이다."], ["I had lain tossing about, and had heard the clock strike only twice, when the night-watchman came to me, sent up from the ward, to say that Renfield had escaped.", "나는 이리저리 뒤척이며 누워 시계가 겨우 두 번 치는 소리를 들었을 뿐인데, 야간 당직이 병동에서 올라와, 렌필드가 달아났다고 알렸다."], ["I threw on my clothes and ran down at once; my patient is too dangerous a person to be roaming about.", "나는 옷을 걸치고 곧장 달려 내려갔다. 내 환자는 함부로 돌아다니게 두기에는 너무 위험한 인물이다."], ["Those ideas of his might work out dangerously with strangers.", "그의 그런 생각들은 낯선 이들에게 위험하게 작용할 수 있다."], ["The attendant was waiting for me.", "간병인이 나를 기다리고 있었다."], ["He said he had seen him not ten minutes before, seemingly asleep in his bed, when he had looked through the observation-trap in the door.", "그는 채 십 분도 안 되어 그를 보았다고 했는데, 문에 난 관찰용 작은 구멍으로 들여다보았을 때 그는 침대에서 잠든 듯 보였다는 것이다."], ["His attention was called by the sound of the window being wrenched out.", "창문이 비틀려 뜯겨 나가는 소리에 주의가 끌렸다고 했다."], ["He ran back and saw his feet disappear through the window, and had at once sent up for me.", "그는 달려가, 그의 두 발이 창문 너머로 사라지는 것을 보고 곧바로 나에게 사람을 보냈던 것이다."], ["He was only in his night-gear, and cannot be far off.", "그는 잠옷 차림뿐이니, 멀리 가지는 못할 것이다."], ["The attendant thought it would be more useful to watch where he should go than to follow him, as he might lose sight of him whilst getting out of the building by the door.", "간병인은, 문으로 건물을 빠져나가는 사이 그를 놓칠 수도 있으니, 그를 뒤쫓는 것보다 그가 어디로 가는지 지켜보는 편이 더 쓸모 있으리라 여겼다."], ["He is a bulky man, and couldn’t get through the window.", "그는 덩치가 커서 창문을 빠져나갈 수 없었다."], ["I am thin, so, with his aid, I got out, but feet foremost, and, as we were only a few feet above ground, landed unhurt.", "나는 몸이 가늘어, 그의 도움으로 발부터 먼저 빠져나갔고, 우리가 땅에서 겨우 몇 자 위에 있었던 터라 다치지 않고 내려섰다."], ["The attendant told me the patient had gone to the left, and had taken a straight line, so I ran as quickly as I could.", "간병인은 환자가 왼쪽으로 갔고 곧장 일직선으로 나아갔다고 일러주어, 나는 되도록 빨리 달렸다."], ["As I got through the belt of trees I saw a white figure scale the high wall which separates our grounds from those of the deserted house.", "나무들이 늘어선 띠를 빠져나가자, 흰 형체 하나가 우리 부지와 저 버려진 집의 부지를 가르는 높은 담을 기어오르는 것이 보였다."]], [["I ran back at once, told the watchman to get three or four men immediately and follow me into the grounds of Carfax, in case our friend might be dangerous.", "나는 곧장 되돌아 달려가, 당직에게 즉시 서너 명을 데리고 카팩스의 부지로 나를 따라오라고 일렀으니, 우리 친구가 위험할 수 있기 때문이었다."], ["I got a ladder myself, and crossing the wall, dropped down on the other side.", "나는 직접 사다리를 구해, 담을 넘어 반대편으로 내려섰다."], ["I could see Renfield’s figure just disappearing behind the angle of the house, so I ran after him.", "렌필드의 형체가 막 집 모퉁이 뒤로 사라지는 것이 보여, 나는 그를 뒤쫓아 달렸다."], ["On the far side of the house I found him pressed close against the old ironbound oak door of the chapel.", "집 저편에서 나는 그가 예배당의 낡은 쇠테 두른 떡갈나무 문에 바싹 몸을 붙이고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["He was talking, apparently to some one, but I was afraid to go near enough to hear what he was saying, lest I might frighten him, and he should run off.", "그는 누군가에게 말을 하고 있는 듯했으나, 나는 그를 놀라게 해 달아나게 할까 봐, 그가 하는 말을 들을 만큼 가까이 가기가 두려웠다."], ["Chasing an errant swarm of bees is nothing to following a naked lunatic, when the fit of escaping is upon him!", "빗나간 벌떼를 쫓는 일은, 탈주의 발작이 도진 벌거벗은 미치광이를 쫓는 일에 비하면 아무것도 아니다!"], ["After a few minutes, however, I could see that he did not take note of anything around him, and so ventured to draw nearer to him--the more so as my men had now crossed the wall and were closing him in. I heard him say:--", "그러나 몇 분이 지나자 그가 주위의 아무것도 알아차리지 못한다는 것을 알 수 있어, 나는 큰맘 먹고 그에게 더 가까이 다가갔다 — 게다가 내 부하들이 이제 담을 넘어와 그를 에워싸 죄어들고 있었으므로 더욱 그랬다. 나는 그가 이렇게 말하는 것을 들었다."]], [["“I am here to do Your bidding, Master.", "\"저는 당신의 분부를 받들고자 여기 있나이다, 주인님."], ["I am Your slave, and You will reward me, for I shall be faithful.", "저는 당신의 종이오니, 당신께서 저에게 상을 내리시리이다. 저는 충실할 것이옵니다."], ["I have worshipped You long and afar off.", "저는 오래, 멀리서 당신을 흠모해 왔나이다."], ["Now that You are near, I await Your commands, and You will not pass me by, will You, dear Master, in Your distribution of good things?”", "이제 당신께서 가까이 오셨으니, 저는 당신의 명을 기다리오며, 당신께서 좋은 것들을 나누어 주실 적에 저를 그냥 지나치지는 않으시겠지요, 그렇지요, 친애하는 주인님?\""]], [["He is a selfish old beggar anyhow.", "그는 어쨌거나 이기적인 늙은 거지다."], ["He thinks of the loaves and fishes even when he believes he is in a Real Presence.", "그는 자기가 진짜 임재(臨在) 앞에 있다고 믿는 순간에조차 떡과 물고기를 생각한다."], ["His manias make a startling combination.", "그의 광증들은 놀라운 조합을 이룬다."], ["When we closed in on him he fought like a tiger.", "우리가 그를 죄어들었을 때 그는 호랑이처럼 싸웠다."], ["He is immensely strong, for he was more like a wild beast than a man.", "그는 어마어마하게 힘이 세니, 사람이라기보다 들짐승에 가까웠다."], ["I never saw a lunatic in such a paroxysm of rage before; and I hope I shall not again.", "나는 미치광이가 그토록 광란하는 분노에 휩싸인 것을 일찍이 본 적이 없다."], ["It is a mercy that we have found out his strength and his danger in good time.", "그리고 다시는 보지 않기를 바란다. 그의 힘과 위험성을 제때에 알아낸 것은 다행한 일이다."], ["With strength and determination like his, he might have done wild work before he was caged.", "그와 같은 힘과 결의를 지녔다면, 그는 우리에 갇히기 전에 험한 짓을 저질렀을지도 모른다."], ["He is safe now at any rate. Jack Sheppard himself couldn’t get free from the strait-waistcoat that keeps him restrained, and he’s chained to the wall in the padded room.", "어쨌든 이제 그는 안전하게 갇혀 있다. 잭 셰퍼드 자신이라도 그를 옭아매고 있는 구속복에서 풀려날 수 없을 것이며, 그는 푹신하게 댄 방 안에서 벽에 쇠사슬로 매여 있다."], ["His cries are at times awful, but the silences that follow are more deadly still, for he means murder in every turn and movement.", "그의 비명은 때로 끔찍하지만, 그 뒤에 따르는 침묵은 더더욱 섬뜩하니, 그가 몸짓 하나하나마다 살인을 꾀하고 있기 때문이다."]], [["Just now he spoke coherent words for the first time:--", "방금 그가 처음으로 조리 있는 말을 했다."]], [["“I shall be patient, Master.", "\"저는 참고 기다리겠나이다, 주인님."], ["It is coming--coming--coming!”", "오고 있어 — 오고 있어 — 오고 있어!\""]], [["So I took the hint, and came too.", "그래서 나는 그 암시를 알아채고, 나도 돌아왔다."], ["I was too excited to sleep, but this diary has quieted me, and I feel I shall get some sleep to-night.", "너무 흥분해서 잠을 이룰 수 없었으나, 이 일기가 나를 가라앉혀 주었으니, 오늘 밤은 좀 잘 수 있을 듯하다."]], [["“Buda-Pesth, 24 August.", "「미나 하커가 루시 웨스텐라에게 보내는 편지」"]], [["“My dearest Lucy,--", "부다페스트, 8월 24일."]], [["“I know you will be anxious to hear all that has happened since we parted at the railway station at Whitby.", "사랑하는 루시에게—\n우리가 휘트비 기차역에서 헤어진 뒤로 일어난 일들을 네가 무척 궁금해하리라는 것을 나는 안다."], ["Well, my dear, I got to Hull all right, and caught the boat to Hamburg, and then the train on here.", "그래, 얘야, 나는 무사히 헐에 도착했고, 함부르크행 배를 탔으며, 거기서 다시 기차를 타고 이곳까지 왔단다."], ["I feel that I can hardly recall anything of the journey, except that I knew I was coming to Jonathan, and, that as I should have to do some nursing, I had better get all the sleep I could....", "여정에 대해서는 거의 아무것도 기억나지 않는 듯한데, 다만 내가 조너선에게로 가고 있다는 것, 그리고 간병을 해야 할 테니 잘 수 있는 만큼 자두는 편이 낫겠다는 것만은 알고 있었지…."], ["I found my dear one, oh, so thin and pale and weak-looking.", "나는 내 사랑하는 사람을 만났단다. 아, 어찌나 야위고 창백하고 약해 보이던지."], ["All the resolution has gone out of his dear eyes, and that quiet dignity which I told you was in his face has vanished.", "그이의 그 사랑스러운 눈에서 결의가 모두 사라졌고, 내가 네게 말했던 그 얼굴에 어렸던 조용한 위엄도 자취를 감추었어."], ["He is only a wreck of himself, and he does not remember anything that has happened to him for a long time past.", "그이는 자기 자신의 잔해에 지나지 않으며, 오랫동안 자신에게 일어난 일을 아무것도 기억하지 못해."], ["At least, he wants me to believe so, and I shall never ask.", "적어도 내가 그렇게 믿어주기를 바라고, 나는 결코 캐묻지 않을 거야."], ["He has had some terrible shock, and I fear it might tax his poor brain if he were to try to recall it.", "그이는 무언가 끔찍한 충격을 받았는데, 그것을 떠올리려 한다면 그이의 가엾은 머리에 무리가 갈까 봐 두려워."], ["Sister Agatha, who is a good creature and a born nurse, tells me that he raved of dreadful things whilst he was off his head.", "착한 사람이자 천생 간호사인 애거사 수녀님이 말하기를, 그이가 제정신이 아닐 때 무시무시한 것들에 대해 헛소리를 했다는구나."], ["I wanted her to tell me what they were; but she would only cross herself, and say she would never tell; that the ravings of the sick were the secrets of God, and that if a nurse through her vocation should hear them, she should respect her trust.", "나는 수녀님께 그게 무엇이었는지 말해달라고 했지만, 수녀님은 그저 성호를 그으며, 결코 말할 수 없다고, 병자의 헛소리는 신의 비밀이니, 간호사가 그 소명으로 인해 그것을 듣게 되더라도 그 신뢰를 존중해야 한다고만 하셨어."], ["She is a sweet, good soul, and the next day, when she saw I was troubled, she opened up the subject again, and after saying that she could never mention what my poor dear raved about, added: ‘I can tell you this much, my dear: that it was not about anything which he has done wrong himself; and you, as his wife to be, have no cause to be concerned.", "수녀님은 다정하고 선한 분이라, 이튿날 내가 괴로워하는 것을 보시고 다시 그 이야기를 꺼내셨는데, 내 가엾은 사람이 헛소리한 내용은 결코 입에 담을 수 없다고 하시면서도 이렇게 덧붙이셨단다. ‘이만큼은 말씀드릴 수 있어요, 아가씨. 그분이 스스로 무슨 잘못을 저질러서가 아니랍니다."], ["He has not forgotten you or what he owes to you.", "그리고 장차 그분의 아내가 되실 분으로서, 걱정하실 까닭은 조금도 없어요."], ["His fear was of great and terrible things, which no mortal can treat of.’ I do believe the dear soul thought I might be jealous lest my poor dear should have fallen in love with any other girl.", "그분은 당신을, 또 당신에게 진 빚을 잊지 않으셨답니다. 그분이 두려워한 것은 어떤 인간도 다룰 수 없는 크고 무서운 것들이었어요.’ 그 다정한 분은 내 가엾은 사람이 다른 여자를 사랑하게 되었을까 봐 내가 질투할지도 모른다고 생각하셨던 게 분명해."], ["The idea of my being jealous about Jonathan!", "내가 조너선을 두고 질투를 한다는 발상이라니!"], ["And yet, my dear, let me whisper, I felt a thrill of joy through me when I knew that no other woman was a cause of trouble.", "그렇지만 얘야, 가만히 속삭이건대, 다른 여자가 화근이 아니라는 것을 알았을 때 나는 온몸에 기쁨의 전율이 흘렀단다."], ["I am now sitting by his bedside, where I can see his face while he sleeps.", "나는 지금 그이의 머리맡에 앉아, 그이가 자는 동안 그 얼굴을 바라볼 수 있는 자리에 있어."], ["He is waking!...", "그이가 깨어나고 있어!…"]], [["“When he woke he asked me for his coat, as he wanted to get something from the pocket; I asked Sister Agatha, and she brought all his things.", "그이는 깨어나자 외투를 가져다 달라고 했어. 주머니에서 무언가를 꺼내고 싶다는 거였지. 내가 애거사 수녀님께 부탁하자 수녀님이 그이의 물건을 모두 가져다주셨어."], ["I saw that amongst them was his note-book, and was going to ask him to let me look at it--for I knew then that I might find some clue to his trouble--but I suppose he must have seen my wish in my eyes, for he sent me over to the window, saying he wanted to be quite alone for a moment.", "그것들 가운데 그이의 수첩이 있는 것을 보고, 나는 그것을 좀 보여달라고 청하려 했어—그러면 그이의 고뇌에 대한 어떤 실마리를 찾을 수 있으리라는 것을 그때 알았으니까—그러나 그이는 내 바람을 내 눈에서 읽었던 모양으로, 잠시 혼자 있고 싶다며 나를 창가로 보냈어."], ["Then he called me back, and when I came he had his hand over the note-book, and he said to me very solemnly:--", "그러고는 다시 나를 불렀고, 내가 다가가자 그이는 수첩 위에 손을 얹은 채 무척 엄숙하게 말했어—"]], [["“‘Wilhelmina’--I knew then that he was in deadly earnest, for he has never called me by that name since he asked me to marry him--‘you know, dear, my ideas of the trust between husband and wife: there should be no secret, no concealment.", "‘빌헬미나’—그 순간 나는 그이가 더없이 진지하다는 것을 알았는데, 청혼한 이래로 그이가 나를 그 이름으로 부른 적이 한 번도 없었기 때문이야—‘여보, 당신은 부부 사이의 신뢰에 대한 내 생각을 알지. 비밀도 숨김도 있어서는 안 된다는 것을."], ["I have had a great shock, and when I try to think of what it is I feel my head spin round, and I do not know if it was all real or the dreaming of a madman.", "나는 큰 충격을 받았고, 그게 무엇이었는지 생각하려 들면 머리가 빙빙 도는 것 같아. 그게 모두 실제였는지 아니면 미치광이의 꿈이었는지 알 수가 없어."], ["You know I have had brain fever, and that is to be mad.", "당신도 알다시피 나는 뇌염을 앓았고, 그건 곧 미쳤다는 뜻이지."], ["The secret is here, and I do not want to know it.", "비밀은 여기 있지만, 나는 그것을 알고 싶지 않아."], ["I want to take up my life here, with our marriage.’ For, my dear, we had decided to be married as soon as the formalities are complete.", "나는 우리의 결혼과 더불어 이곳에서 내 삶을 새로 시작하고 싶어.’ 얘야, 우리는 절차가 다 끝나는 대로 결혼하기로 결정했단다."], ["‘Are you willing, Wilhelmina, to share my ignorance?", "‘빌헬미나, 당신은 내 무지를 함께 나눌 마음이 있소? 여기 수첩이 있소."], ["Here is the book.", "받아서 간직해주오."], ["Take it and keep it, read it if you will, but never let me know; unless, indeed, some solemn duty should come upon me to go back to the bitter hours, asleep or awake, sane or mad, recorded here.’ He fell back exhausted, and I put the book under his pillow, and kissed him.", "원한다면 읽어도 좋소. 다만 내게는 결코 알리지 말아주오. 정말로, 잠들었든 깨어 있든, 멀쩡하든 미쳤든, 여기 기록된 그 쓰라린 시간들로 되돌아가야 할 어떤 엄숙한 의무가 내게 닥치지 않는 한은.’ 그이는 기진하여 도로 누웠고, 나는 그 수첩을 그이의 베개 밑에 넣고 그이에게 입맞추었어."], ["I have asked Sister Agatha to beg the Superior to let our wedding be this afternoon, and am waiting her reply....", "나는 애거사 수녀님께 원장 수녀님께 청하여 우리 결혼식을 오늘 오후에 올릴 수 있게 해달라고 부탁했고, 지금 그 답을 기다리고 있어…."]], [["“She has come and told me that the chaplain of the English mission church has been sent for.", "수녀님이 와서, 영국 선교 교회의 사제를 불러오도록 사람을 보냈다고 알려주셨어."], ["We are to be married in an hour, or as soon after as Jonathan awakes....", "우리는 한 시간 뒤, 아니 그 직후에 조너선이 깨어나는 대로 결혼식을 올리게 될 거야…."]], [["“Lucy, the time has come and gone.", "루시, 그 시간이 오고 또 지나갔어."], ["I feel very solemn, but very, very happy.", "나는 무척 엄숙한 마음이지만, 정말이지 더없이 행복해."], ["Jonathan woke a little after the hour, and all was ready, and he sat up in bed, propped up with pillows.", "조너선은 그 시각이 조금 지나 깨어났고, 모든 준비가 되어 있었어. 그이는 베개로 받쳐 침대에 일으켜 앉았지."], ["He answered his ‘I will’ firmly and strongly.", "그이는 ‘맹세합니다’라는 말을 굳세고 또렷하게 답했어."], ["I could hardly speak; my heart was so full that even those words seemed to choke me.", "나는 거의 말을 할 수가 없었어. 가슴이 너무 벅차서 그 짧은 말조차 목이 메는 듯했지."], ["The dear sisters were so kind.", "다정한 수녀님들은 어찌나 친절하셨던지."], ["Please God, I shall never, never forget them, nor the grave and sweet responsibilities I have taken upon me.", "부디 신께 비노니, 나는 그분들도, 또 내가 떠맡은 그 엄숙하고도 달콤한 책임도 결코, 결코 잊지 않을 거야."], ["I must tell you of my wedding present.", "내 결혼 선물에 대해 네게 말해줘야겠다."], ["When the chaplain and the sisters had left me alone with my husband--oh, Lucy, it is the first time I have written the words ‘my husband’--left me alone with my husband, I took the book from under his pillow, and wrapped it up in white paper, and tied it with a little bit of pale blue ribbon which was round my neck, and sealed it over the knot with sealing-wax, and for my seal I used my wedding ring.", "사제와 수녀님들이 나를 남편과 단둘이 남겨두고 떠나자—아, 루시, ‘내 남편’이라는 말을 쓰는 건 이번이 처음이구나—남편과 단둘이 남게 되자, 나는 그이의 베개 밑에서 수첩을 꺼내어 흰 종이에 싸고, 내 목에 둘렀던 연한 푸른 리본 한 조각으로 묶은 다음, 봉랍으로 매듭 위를 봉했어. 그리고 내 도장으로는 결혼반지를 썼단다."], ["Then I kissed it and showed it to my husband, and told him that I would keep it so, and then it would be an outward and visible sign for us all our lives that we trusted each other; that I would never open it unless it were for his own dear sake or for the sake of some stern duty.", "그러고는 거기에 입맞추고 남편에게 보여주며, 이대로 간직하겠다고, 그러면 그것이 우리가 평생 서로를 믿었다는 외적이고 눈에 보이는 표징이 되리라고 말했어. 그이 자신의 소중한 일을 위해서가 아니라면, 혹은 어떤 준엄한 의무를 위해서가 아니라면 결코 그것을 열지 않겠다고."], ["Then he took my hand in his, and oh, Lucy, it was the first time he took his wife’s hand, and said that it was the dearest thing in all the wide world, and that he would go through all the past again to win it, if need be.", "그러자 그이는 내 손을 자기 손에 잡았는데, 아, 루시, 그이가 자기 아내의 손을 잡은 것은 그때가 처음이었어. 그이는 그것이 이 넓은 세상에서 가장 소중한 것이라고, 필요하다면 그것을 얻기 위해 그 모든 과거를 다시 한번 겪을 수도 있다고 말했어."], ["The poor dear meant to have said a part of the past, but he cannot think of time yet, and I shall not wonder if at first he mixes up not only the month, but the year.", "가엾은 그이는 과거의 일부를 말하려 했던 거지만, 아직 시간을 가늠하지 못하니, 처음에는 달뿐 아니라 해까지도 헷갈린다 해도 나는 이상하게 여기지 않을 거야."]], [["“Well, my dear, what could I say? I could only tell him that I was the happiest woman in all the wide world, and that I had nothing to give him except myself, my life, and my trust, and that with these went my love and duty for all the days of my life.", "그래, 얘야, 내가 무슨 말을 할 수 있었겠니? 나는 그저 내가 이 넓은 세상에서 가장 행복한 여자라고, 그리고 그이에게 줄 것이라곤 나 자신과 내 삶과 내 신뢰뿐이며, 그것들과 더불어 내 평생의 사랑과 의무가 함께 간다고 말할 수 있을 따름이었어."], ["And, my dear, when he kissed me, and drew me to him with his poor weak hands, it was like a very solemn pledge between us....", "그리고 얘야, 그이가 나에게 입맞추고 그 가엾고 약한 두 손으로 나를 끌어당겼을 때, 그것은 우리 사이의 더없이 엄숙한 서약과도 같았단다…."]], [["“Lucy dear, do you know why I tell you all this?", "루시야, 내가 왜 이 모든 것을 네게 말하는지 아니?"], ["It is not only because it is all sweet to me, but because you have been, and are, very dear to me.", "이것이 나에게 모두 달콤하기 때문만이 아니라, 네가 나에게 무척 소중한 사람이었고 또 지금도 그렇기 때문이야."], ["It was my privilege to be your friend and guide when you came from the schoolroom to prepare for the world of life.", "네가 학창 시절을 마치고 인생이라는 세계를 준비하러 나왔을 때, 네 벗이자 길잡이가 된 것은 내게 주어진 특권이었지."], ["I want you to see now, and with the eyes of a very happy wife, whither duty has led me; so that in your own married life you too may be all happy as I am.", "이제 나는 네가, 더없이 행복한 아내의 눈으로, 의무가 나를 어디로 이끌었는지 보아주기를 바라. 그래서 너의 결혼 생활에서도 너 또한 나처럼 온전히 행복하기를."], ["My dear, please Almighty God, your life may be all it promises: a long day of sunshine, with no harsh wind, no forgetting duty, no distrust.", "얘야, 전능하신 신께 비노니, 네 삶이 약속하는 그대로이기를. 모진 바람도, 의무를 저버리는 일도, 불신도 없는, 기나긴 햇살의 나날이기를."], ["I must not wish you no pain, for that can never be; but I do hope you will be always as happy as I am now.", "네게 아무런 고통도 없기를 빌 수는 없겠지, 그것은 결코 있을 수 없는 일이니까. 그러나 네가 지금의 나처럼 언제나 행복하기를 진정 바란단다."], ["Good-bye, my dear.", "잘 있어, 얘야."], ["I shall post this at once, and, perhaps, write you very soon again.", "이 편지는 곧장 부칠 것이고, 어쩌면 머지않아 다시 쓰게 될지도 몰라."], ["I must stop, for Jonathan is waking--I must attend to my husband!", "이만 멈춰야겠다. 조너선이 깨어나고 있으니—남편을 돌봐야 하니까! 언제나 너를 사랑하는"]], [["“Your ever-loving", "미나 하커. 「루시 웨스텐라가 미나 하커에게 보내는 편지」"]], [["“Whitby, 30 August.", "휘트비, 8월 30일."]], [["“My dearest Mina,--", "사랑하는 미나에게—"]], [["“Oceans of love and millions of kisses, and may you soon be in your own home with your husband.", "바다만큼의 사랑과 수백만 번의 입맞춤을 보내며, 부디 곧 네 남편과 함께 너의 집에 자리잡기를."], ["I wish you could be coming home soon enough to stay with us here.", "네가 어서 돌아와 이곳 우리와 함께 지낼 수 있다면 좋으련만."], ["The strong air would soon restore Jonathan; it has quite restored me.", "이 강한 공기가 조너선을 곧 회복시켜줄 거야. 나는 아주 말끔히 회복되었거든."], ["I have an appetite like a cormorant, am full of life, and sleep well.", "나는 가마우지 같은 식욕이 있고, 활기가 넘치며, 잠도 잘 잔단다."], ["You will be glad to know that I have quite given up walking in my sleep.", "내가 몽유병을 완전히 떨쳐냈다는 걸 알면 기뻐하겠지."], ["I think I have not stirred out of my bed for a week, that is when I once got into it at night.", "일주일 동안 침대에서 한 번도 나가지 않은 것 같아. 그러니까, 밤에 일단 침대에 들면 말이야."], ["Arthur says I am getting fat.", "아서는 내가 살이 오르고 있다고 해."], ["By the way, I forgot to tell you that Arthur is here.", "그러고 보니, 아서가 여기 와 있다는 걸 깜빡하고 말 안 했구나."], ["We have such walks and drives, and rides, and rowing, and tennis, and fishing together; and I love him more than ever.", "우리는 함께 산책도 하고, 마차도 타고, 말도 타고, 보트도 젓고, 테니스도 치고, 낚시도 한단다. 그리고 나는 그를 그 어느 때보다 사랑해."], ["He tells me that he loves me more, but I doubt that, for at first he told me that he couldn’t love me more than he did then.", "그는 자기가 나를 더 사랑한다고 말하지만, 나는 그 말을 의심하지. 처음에 그는 그때보다 더 나를 사랑할 수는 없다고 했었거든."], ["But this is nonsense.", "하지만 이건 다 부질없는 소리지."], ["There he is, calling to me.", "저기 그가 나를 부르고 있어."], ["So no more just at present from your loving", "그러니 지금은 이만, 너를 사랑하는\n루시로부터."]], [["“P. S.--Mother sends her love.", "추신.—어머니께서 안부를 전하셔."], ["She seems better, poor dear. “P. P.", "가엾은 분, 좀 나아지신 것 같아."], ["S.--We are to be married on 28 September.”", "추추신.—우리는 9월 28일에 결혼하기로 했어."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["20 August.--The case of Renfield grows even more interesting.", "8월 20일.—렌필드의 증례는 더욱더 흥미로워지고 있다."], ["He has now so far quieted that there are spells of cessation from his passion.", "그는 이제 상당히 잠잠해져서, 발작이 멈추는 시기가 있다."], ["For the first week after his attack he was perpetually violent.", "발작이 있은 뒤 첫 주 동안 그는 끊임없이 난폭했다."], ["Then one night, just as the moon rose, he grew quiet, and kept murmuring to himself: “Now I can wait; now I can wait.”", "그러던 어느 날 밤, 달이 막 떠오를 무렵 그는 잠잠해지더니, 혼잣말로 “이제 기다릴 수 있어. 이제 기다릴 수 있어”라고 중얼거렸다."], ["The attendant came to tell me, so I ran down at once to have a look at him.", "간호인이 와서 알려주기에 나는 곧장 내려가 그를 살펴보았다."], ["He was still in the strait-waistcoat and in the padded room, but the suffused look had gone from his face, and his eyes had something of their old pleading--I might almost say, “cringing”--softness.", "그는 여전히 구속복을 입은 채 충전실에 있었으나, 얼굴에서 그 충혈된 기색은 사라졌고, 눈에는 예전의 그 애원하는 듯한—거의 “비굴한”이라 할 만한—부드러움이 어려 있었다."], ["I was satisfied with his present condition, and directed him to be relieved.", "나는 그의 현재 상태에 만족하여 그를 풀어주도록 지시했다."], ["The attendants hesitated, but finally carried out my wishes without protest.", "간호인들은 망설였으나, 결국 군말 없이 내 뜻을 따랐다."], ["It was a strange thing that the patient had humour enough to see their distrust, for, coming close to me, he said in a whisper, all the while looking furtively at them:--", "기이하게도 그 환자는 그들의 불신을 알아챌 만큼의 유머가 있었는데, 내게 가까이 다가오더니 줄곧 그들을 곁눈질하면서 속삭이듯 말했다—"]], [["“They think I could hurt you! Fancy me hurting you!", "“저들은 제가 선생님을 해칠 수 있다고 생각하나 봐요! 제가 선생님을 해친다니요!"], ["The fools!”", "바보들 같으니!”"]], [["It was soothing, somehow, to the feelings to find myself dissociated even in the mind of this poor madman from the others; but all the same I do not follow his thought.", "이 가엾은 미치광이의 마음속에서조차 내가 다른 사람들과 구별된다는 것을 알게 되니 어쩐지 마음이 흐뭇했다. 그러나 그렇다 해도 나는 그의 생각을 따라가지 못하겠다."], ["Am I to take it that I have anything in common with him, so that we are, as it were, to stand together; or has he to gain from me some good so stupendous that my well-being is needful to him?", "내가 그와 어떤 공통점이 있어서, 말하자면 우리가 한편에 서게 된다는 뜻으로 받아들여야 하는가. 아니면 그가 나로부터 얻어야 할 어떤 엄청난 이로움이 있어서 내 안녕이 그에게 필요하다는 것인가."], ["I must find out later on. To-night he will not speak.", "나중에 알아내야겠다. 오늘 밤 그는 말을 하지 않으려 한다."], ["Even the offer of a kitten or even a full-grown cat will not tempt him.", "새끼고양이를, 아니 다 자란 고양이를 주겠다 해도 솔깃해하지 않는다."], ["He will only say: “I don’t take any stock in cats.", "그는 그저 이렇게만 말한다. “고양이 따위엔 관심 없어요."], ["I have more to think of now, and I can wait; I can wait.”", "지금 생각할 게 더 많거든요. 그리고 기다릴 수 있어요. 기다릴 수 있다고요.”"]], [["After a while I left him.", "얼마 후 나는 그를 떠났다."], ["The attendant tells me that he was quiet until just before dawn, and that then he began to get uneasy, and at length violent, until at last he fell into a paroxysm which exhausted him so that he swooned into a sort of coma.", "간호인의 말로는 그가 동트기 직전까지 잠잠하다가, 그때부터 불안해지기 시작해 마침내 난폭해졌으며, 끝내는 발작에 빠져 어찌나 기진했던지 일종의 혼수 상태로 까무러쳤다고 한다."]], [["... Three nights has the same thing happened--violent all day then quiet from moonrise to sunrise.", "…사흘 밤 동안 똑같은 일이 일어났다—온종일 난폭하다가 달이 뜨고부터 해가 뜰 때까지 잠잠해진다."], ["I wish I could get some clue to the cause. It would almost seem as if there was some influence which came and went.", "그 원인에 대한 실마리를 잡을 수 있다면 좋으련만. 마치 오고 가는 어떤 영향력이 있는 것만 같다."], ["Happy thought!", "좋은 생각이 났다!"], ["We shall to-night play sane wits against mad ones.", "오늘 밤 우리는 멀쩡한 머리로 미친 머리와 겨뤄보겠다."], ["He escaped before without our help; to-night he shall escape with it.", "그는 앞서 우리의 도움 없이 탈출했으니, 오늘 밤은 우리의 도움으로 탈출하게 하리라."], ["We shall give him a chance, and have the men ready to follow in case they are required....", "우리는 그에게 기회를 주고, 필요할 경우에 대비해 따라갈 사람들을 대기시켜두겠다…."]], [["23 August.--“The unexpected always happens.”", "8월 23일.—“예기치 않은 일은 늘 일어난다.”"], ["How well Disraeli knew life.", "디즈레일리는 인생을 참으로 잘 알았다."], ["Our bird when he found the cage open would not fly, so all our subtle arrangements were for nought.", "우리의 새는 새장이 열린 것을 보고도 날아가려 하지 않았으니, 우리의 그 모든 정교한 계획은 헛수고가 되고 말았다."], ["At any rate, we have proved one thing; that the spells of quietness last a reasonable time.", "어쨌든 우리는 한 가지는 입증했다. 잠잠한 시기가 상당한 시간 동안 지속된다는 것을."], ["We shall in future be able to ease his bonds for a few hours each day.", "앞으로 우리는 날마다 몇 시간씩 그의 결박을 풀어줄 수 있을 것이다."], ["I have given orders to the night attendant merely to shut him in the padded room, when once he is quiet, until an hour before sunrise.", "나는 야간 간호인에게, 그가 일단 잠잠해지면 해뜨기 한 시간 전까지 그저 충전실에 가두어두라고 지시했다."], ["The poor soul’s body will enjoy the relief even if his mind cannot appreciate it. Hark!", "비록 그의 정신은 그것을 음미하지 못하더라도, 이 가엾은 영혼의 몸만큼은 그 안식을 누릴 것이다. 들으라!"], ["The unexpected again!", "또 예기치 않은 일이로다!"], ["I am called; the patient has once more escaped.", "나를 부른다. 환자가 또다시 탈출한 것이다."]], [["Later.--Another night adventure.", "이후.—또 한 번의 밤의 소동."], ["Renfield artfully waited until the attendant was entering the room to inspect.", "렌필드는 간호인이 살피러 방에 들어오는 순간까지 교묘하게 기다렸다."], ["Then he dashed out past him and flew down the passage.", "그러고는 간호인을 지나쳐 뛰쳐나가 복도를 따라 내달렸다."], ["I sent word for the attendants to follow.", "나는 간호인들에게 따라가라고 일렀다."], ["Again he went into the grounds of the deserted house, and we found him in the same place, pressed against the old chapel door.", "그는 다시 그 버려진 저택의 부지로 들어갔고, 우리는 그가 똑같은 자리, 곧 오래된 예배당 문에 몸을 바싹 붙이고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["When he saw me he became furious, and had not the attendants seized him in time, he would have tried to kill me.", "그는 나를 보더니 광포해졌고, 간호인들이 제때 붙들지 않았더라면 나를 죽이려 들었을 것이다."], ["As we were holding him a strange thing happened.", "우리가 그를 붙들고 있는 동안 기이한 일이 일어났다."], ["He suddenly redoubled his efforts, and then as suddenly grew calm.", "그는 갑자기 두 배로 발버둥치다가, 그만큼이나 돌연 차분해졌다."], ["I looked round instinctively, but could see nothing.", "나는 본능적으로 주위를 둘러보았으나 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["Then I caught the patient’s eye and followed it, but could trace nothing as it looked into the moonlit sky except a big bat, which was flapping its silent and ghostly way to the west.", "그때 나는 환자의 눈길을 포착하고 그것을 따라가 보았으나, 달빛 어린 하늘을 응시하는 그 눈길이 좇는 것이라곤 서쪽을 향해 소리 없이 유령처럼 날갯짓하며 날아가는 커다란 박쥐 한 마리뿐, 아무것도 짚어낼 수 없었다."], ["Bats usually wheel and flit about, but this one seemed to go straight on, as if it knew where it was bound for or had some intention of its own.", "박쥐는 보통 빙빙 돌며 팔랑팔랑 나는데, 이놈은 자기가 갈 곳을 알고 있거나 무슨 제 나름의 의도라도 있는 듯 곧장 앞으로만 나아가는 것 같았다."], ["The patient grew calmer every instant, and presently said:--", "환자는 시시각각 차분해지더니, 이윽고 말했다—"]], [["“You needn’t tie me; I shall go quietly!”", "“저를 묶을 필요 없어요. 얌전히 가겠습니다!”"], ["Without trouble we came back to the house.", "우리는 별 탈 없이 저택으로 돌아왔다."], ["I feel there is something ominous in his calm, and shall not forget this night....", "나는 그의 그 차분함에 무언가 불길한 것이 있음을 느끼며, 이 밤을 결코 잊지 않을 것이다…."]], [["Lucy Westenra’s Diary", "「루시 웨스텐라의 일기」"]], [["Hillingham, 24 August.--I must imitate Mina, and keep writing things down.", "힐링엄, 8월 24일.—나도 미나를 본받아 이것저것 적어두어야겠다."], ["Then we can have long talks when we do meet.", "그러면 우리가 만났을 때 긴 이야기를 나눌 수 있을 테니."], ["I wonder when it will be.", "그게 언제가 될지 모르겠구나."], ["I wish she were with me again, for I feel so unhappy.", "그 애가 다시 나와 함께 있어준다면 좋으련만, 나는 너무도 불행하다."], ["Last night I seemed to be dreaming again just as I was at Whitby.", "어젯밤 나는 휘트비에 있을 때처럼 또 꿈을 꾸는 것 같았다."], ["Perhaps it is the change of air, or getting home again.", "어쩌면 공기가 바뀐 탓이거나, 다시 집에 돌아온 탓일지도 모르겠다."], ["It is all dark and horrid to me, for I can remember nothing; but I am full of vague fear, and I feel so weak and worn out.", "모든 것이 내게는 어둡고 끔찍한데, 아무것도 기억나지 않기 때문이다. 그러나 나는 막연한 두려움에 사로잡혀 있고, 너무도 약하고 지친 듯하다."], ["When Arthur came to lunch he looked quite grieved when he saw me, and I hadn’t the spirit to try to be cheerful.", "아서가 점심을 먹으러 왔을 때, 그는 나를 보더니 무척 슬퍼하는 기색이었는데, 나는 명랑한 척할 기운조차 없었다."], ["I wonder if I could sleep in mother’s room to-night.", "오늘 밤 어머니 방에서 잘 수 있을지 모르겠다."], ["I shall make an excuse and try.", "핑계를 대고 시도해봐야겠다."]], [["25 August.--Another bad night.", "8월 25일.—또 한 번의 험한 밤."], ["Mother did not seem to take to my proposal.", "어머니는 내 제안을 그리 반기지 않으시는 듯했다."], ["She seems not too well herself, and doubtless she fears to worry me.", "어머니 자신도 그리 편치 않으신 모양이고, 분명 나를 걱정시킬까 봐 염려하시는 것이리라."], ["I tried to keep awake, and succeeded for a while; but when the clock struck twelve it waked me from a doze, so I must have been falling asleep.", "나는 깨어 있으려 애썼고 한동안은 성공했다. 그러나 시계가 열두 시를 칠 때 그 소리에 선잠에서 깨어난 것을 보면, 그새 잠이 들고 있었던 모양이다."], ["There was a sort of scratching or flapping at the window, but I did not mind it, and as I remember no more, I suppose I must then have fallen asleep.", "창문에서 무언가 긁거나 퍼덕이는 듯한 소리가 났지만, 나는 개의치 않았고, 더는 기억나지 않는 것을 보면 그때 잠이 들었던 게 틀림없다."], ["More bad dreams.", "또 험한 꿈들."], ["I wish I could remember them.", "그것들을 기억할 수 있다면 좋으련만."], ["This morning I am horribly weak.", "오늘 아침 나는 끔찍이도 약하다."], ["My face is ghastly pale, and my throat pains me.", "얼굴은 송장처럼 창백하고, 목이 아프다."], ["It must be something wrong with my lungs, for I don’t seem ever to get air enough.", "폐에 무슨 이상이 있는 게 분명한데, 도무지 숨을 충분히 들이쉴 수가 없는 것 같다. 아서가 오면 기운을 내려 애써야겠다."], ["I shall try to cheer up when Arthur comes, or else I know he will be miserable to see me so.", "그러지 않으면 나를 이런 꼴로 보고 그가 비참해할 게 뻔하니까. 「아서 홈우드가 수어드 박사에게 보내는 편지」"]], [["“Albemarle Hotel, 31 August.", "앨버말 호텔, 8월 31일."]], [["“My dear Jack,--", "친애하는 잭에게—"]], [["“I want you to do me a favour.", "자네에게 한 가지 부탁이 있네."], ["Lucy is ill; that is, she has no special disease, but she looks awful, and is getting worse every day.", "루시가 아프다네. 그러니까, 무슨 특별한 병이 있는 건 아니지만, 안색이 끔찍하고 날마다 더 나빠지고 있어."], ["I have asked her if there is any cause; I do not dare to ask her mother, for to disturb the poor lady’s mind about her daughter in her present state of health would be fatal. Mrs.", "무슨 까닭이라도 있느냐고 그녀에게 물어보았네. 그녀의 어머니께는 차마 여쭐 수가 없었어. 지금 같은 건강 상태에서 따님에 대해 그 가엾은 부인의 마음을 어지럽히는 것은 치명적일 테니까."], ["Westenra has confided to me that her doom is spoken--disease of the heart--though poor Lucy does not know it yet.", "웨스텐라 부인은 자신의 운명이 정해졌다고—심장병이라고—내게 털어놓으셨는데, 가엾은 루시는 아직 그것을 모른다네."], ["I am sure that there is something preying on my dear girl’s mind.", "무언가가 내 사랑하는 여인의 마음을 좀먹고 있는 게 분명해."], ["I am almost distracted when I think of her; to look at her gives me a pang.", "그녀를 생각하면 나는 거의 제정신이 아닐 지경이야. 그녀를 보면 가슴이 쓰리네."], ["I told her I should ask you to see her, and though she demurred at first--I know why, old fellow--she finally consented.", "나는 그녀에게 자네더러 봐달라고 청하겠다고 말했고, 그녀는 처음엔 망설였지만—이보게, 그 까닭은 나도 안다네—마침내 동의했네."], ["It will be a painful task for you, I know, old friend, but it is for her sake, and I must not hesitate to ask, or you to act.", "자네에게는 괴로운 일이 되리라는 것을 아네, 오랜 벗이여. 그러나 그건 그녀를 위한 일이니, 나는 청하기를 주저해서는 안 되고, 자네 또한 행하기를 주저해서는 안 되네."], ["You are to come to lunch at Hillingham to-morrow, two o’clock, so as not to arouse any suspicion in Mrs.", "내일 두 시에 힐링엄으로 점심을 들러 와주게. 웨스텐라 부인의 의심을 사지 않도록 말이야. 점심 뒤에 루시가 자네와 단둘이 있을 기회를 마련할 걸세."], ["Westenra, and after lunch Lucy will take an opportunity of being alone with you.", "나는 차 마실 때쯤 들르겠네. 그러면 함께 자리를 뜰 수 있겠지."], ["I shall come in for tea, and we can go away together; I am filled with anxiety, and want to consult with you alone as soon as I can after you have seen her. Do not fail!", "나는 불안으로 가득 차 있어서, 자네가 그녀를 본 뒤 되도록 빨리 자네와 단둘이 상의하고 싶네. 부디 어김없이 와주게! 아서."]], [["Telegram, Arthur Holmwood to Seward.", "「아서 홈우드가 수어드에게 보내는 전보」"]], [["“1 September.", "9월 1일."]], [["“Am summoned to see my father, who is worse.", "부친께서 위독하시어 뵈러 오라는 부름을 받았음."], ["Am writing.", "편지 쓰겠음."], ["Write me fully by to-night’s post to Ring.", "오늘 밤 우편으로 링으로 소상히 답신 바람. 필요하면 전보 치게."], ["Wire me if necessary.”", "「수어드 박사가 아서 홈우드에게 보내는 편지」"]], [["“2 September.", "9월 2일."]], [["“My dear old fellow,--", "친애하는 오랜 벗 아트에게—"]], [["“With regard to Miss Westenra’s health I hasten to let you know at once that in my opinion there is not any functional disturbance or any malady that I know of.", "웨스텐라 양의 건강에 관해 서둘러 알려두건대, 내 소견으로는 어떤 기능적 장애도, 내가 아는 어떤 질환도 없네."], ["At the same time, I am not by any means satisfied with her appearance; she is woefully different from what she was when I saw her last.", "그와 동시에, 나는 그녀의 안색에 결코 만족하지 못하겠어. 내가 지난번 보았을 때와는 비참하리만큼 달라졌더군."], ["Of course you must bear in mind that I did not have full opportunity of examination such as I should wish; our very friendship makes a little difficulty which not even medical science or custom can bridge over.", "물론 자네도 명심해두어야 할 것은, 내가 바라는 만큼 충분히 진찰할 기회를 얻지 못했다는 점이네. 다름 아닌 우리의 우정 자체가, 의학도 관습도 메울 수 없는 약간의 곤란을 빚는다네."], ["I had better tell you exactly what happened, leaving you to draw, in a measure, your own conclusions.", "차라리 무슨 일이 있었는지를 자네에게 정확히 들려주고, 어느 정도는 자네 스스로 결론을 끌어내도록 맡기는 편이 낫겠네."], ["I shall then say what I have done and propose doing.", "그러고 나서 내가 무엇을 했고 또 무엇을 하려는지 말하겠네."]], [["“I found Miss Westenra in seemingly gay spirits.", "웨스텐라 양은 겉보기에 명랑한 기분으로 있었네."], ["Her mother was present, and in a few seconds I made up my mind that she was trying all she knew to mislead her mother and prevent her from being anxious.", "그녀의 어머니가 함께 있었는데, 나는 몇 초 만에 그녀가 아는 한껏을 다해 어머니를 속이고 걱정을 끼치지 않으려 애쓰고 있음을 알아차렸네."], ["I have no doubt she guesses, if she does not know, what need of caution there is.", "그녀가 얼마나 조심해야 하는지를, 알지는 못하더라도 짐작은 하고 있는 게 틀림없어."], ["We lunched alone, and as we all exerted ourselves to be cheerful, we got, as some kind of reward for our labours, some real cheerfulness amongst us.", "우리는 단둘이 점심을 들었는데, 다들 명랑해지려 애쓴 덕분에, 그 수고에 대한 일종의 보답으로 우리 사이에 얼마간의 진정한 명랑함이 감돌았네."], ["Then Mrs. Westenra went to lie down, and Lucy was left with me.", "그러고 나서 웨스텐라 부인이 누우러 가시고, 루시는 나와 단둘이 남았지."], ["We went into her boudoir, and till we got there her gaiety remained, for the servants were coming and going.", "우리는 그녀의 내실로 들어갔는데, 거기 이르기까지 그녀의 명랑함은 유지되었네. 하인들이 들락거렸으니까."], ["As soon as the door was closed, however, the mask fell from her face, and she sank down into a chair with a great sigh, and hid her eyes with her hand.", "그러나 문이 닫히자마자 그녀의 얼굴에서 가면이 떨어졌고, 그녀는 깊은 한숨과 함께 의자에 주저앉아 손으로 눈을 가렸네."], ["When I saw that her high spirits had failed, I at once took advantage of her reaction to make a diagnosis.", "그녀의 들떴던 기분이 무너진 것을 보고, 나는 즉시 그 반작용을 이용해 진단을 내리려 했어."], ["She said to me very sweetly:--", "그녀는 무척 다정하게 내게 말했네—"]], [["“‘I cannot tell you how I loathe talking about myself.’ I reminded her that a doctor’s confidence was sacred, but that you were grievously anxious about her.", "‘제 자신에 대해 이야기하는 게 얼마나 싫은지 모르겠어요.’ 나는 의사의 비밀은 신성한 것이지만 자네가 그녀를 몹시 걱정하고 있다는 점을 상기시켰네."], ["She caught on to my meaning at once, and settled that matter in a word.", "그녀는 곧장 내 뜻을 알아채고는 한마디로 그 문제를 정리해버렸어."], ["‘Tell Arthur everything you choose.", "‘아서에게는 무엇이든 마음대로 말씀하세요."], ["I do not care for myself, but all for him!’ So I am quite free.", "저 자신은 아무래도 좋아요. 다 그이를 위해서예요!’ 그러니 나는 아무 거리낌이 없네."]], [["“I could easily see that she is somewhat bloodless, but I could not see the usual anæmic signs, and by a chance I was actually able to test the quality of her blood, for in opening a window which was stiff a cord gave way, and she cut her hand slightly with broken glass.", "나는 그녀가 다소 혈색이 없다는 것을 쉽게 알 수 있었지만, 흔히 보이는 빈혈 징후는 보이지 않았네. 그러다 우연히 나는 실제로 그녀의 혈액 상태를 검사할 수 있게 되었어. 뻑뻑한 창문을 열다가 끈이 끊어지는 바람에, 그녀가 깨진 유리에 손을 살짝 베인 거지."], ["It was a slight matter in itself, but it gave me an evident chance, and I secured a few drops of the blood and have analysed them.", "그 자체로는 사소한 일이었지만, 내게는 분명한 기회를 주었네. 나는 피 몇 방울을 받아 분석해보았어."], ["The qualitative analysis gives a quite normal condition, and shows, I should infer, in itself a vigorous state of health.", "정성 분석 결과는 지극히 정상이었고, 내가 추론하기로는 그 자체로 활력 넘치는 건강 상태를 보여주었네."], ["In other physical matters I was quite satisfied that there is no need for anxiety; but as there must be a cause somewhere, I have come to the conclusion that it must be something mental.", "다른 신체적 사항에서도 나는 걱정할 필요가 없음을 충분히 확인했어. 그러나 어딘가에 원인이 있을 수밖에 없으므로, 나는 그것이 무언가 정신적인 것임에 틀림없다는 결론에 이르렀네."], ["She complains of difficulty in breathing satisfactorily at times, and of heavy, lethargic sleep, with dreams that frighten her, but regarding which she can remember nothing.", "그녀는 이따금 숨을 제대로 쉬기 어렵다고, 그리고 무겁고 혼곤한 잠을 자며 자신을 겁먹게 하는 꿈을 꾸지만 그에 관해서는 아무것도 기억하지 못한다고 하소연하네."], ["She says that as a child she used to walk in her sleep, and that when in Whitby the habit came back, and that once she walked out in the night and went to East Cliff, where Miss Murray found her; but she assures me that of late the habit has not returned.", "그녀는 어릴 적에 몽유병이 있었고, 휘트비에 있을 때 그 버릇이 도졌으며, 한번은 밤중에 걸어 나가 이스트 클리프까지 갔는데 거기서 머리 양이 자신을 발견했다고 말하네. 그러나 근래에는 그 버릇이 다시 나타나지 않았다고 내게 장담하더군."], ["I am in doubt, and so have done the best thing I know of; I have written to my old friend and master, Professor Van Helsing, of Amsterdam, who knows as much about obscure diseases as any one in the world.", "나는 갈피를 잡지 못해, 내가 아는 최선의 일을 했네. 암스테르담에 계신 나의 오랜 벗이자 스승이신 판 헬싱 교수께 편지를 드렸어. 그분은 난해한 질병에 관해서라면 세상 누구 못지않게 잘 아시는 분이지."], ["I have asked him to come over, and as you told me that all things were to be at your charge, I have mentioned to him who you are and your relations to Miss Westenra.", "나는 그분께 건너와주십사 청했고, 자네가 모든 비용을 부담하겠다고 했으니, 그분께 자네가 누구이며 웨스텐라 양과 어떤 관계인지를 말씀드렸네."], ["This, my dear fellow, is in obedience to your wishes, for I am only too proud and happy to do anything I can for her.", "이보게, 이것은 자네의 뜻에 따른 것이니, 나는 그녀를 위해 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 더없이 자랑스럽고 기쁘게 할 따름이야."], ["Van Helsing would, I know, do anything for me for a personal reason, so, no matter on what ground he comes, we must accept his wishes.", "판 헬싱 교수는, 내가 알기로, 개인적인 연유로라도 나를 위해서라면 무슨 일이든 해주실 분이니, 어떤 명분으로 오시든 우리는 그분의 뜻을 받들어야 하네."], ["He is a seemingly arbitrary man, but this is because he knows what he is talking about better than any one else.", "그분은 겉보기엔 독단적인 분이지만, 이는 그분이 누구보다 자신이 무슨 말을 하는지를 잘 알고 계시기 때문이야."], ["He is a philosopher and a metaphysician, and one of the most advanced scientists of his day; and he has, I believe, an absolutely open mind.", "그분은 철학자이자 형이상학자이며, 당대의 가장 진보한 과학자 가운데 한 분이고, 또 내가 믿건대, 완전히 열린 마음을 지니신 분이네."], ["This, with an iron nerve, a temper of the ice-brook, an indomitable resolution, self-command, and toleration exalted from virtues to blessings, and the kindliest and truest heart that beats--these form his equipment for the noble work that he is doing for mankind--work both in theory and practice, for his views are as wide as his all-embracing sympathy.", "여기에 강철 같은 담력, 얼음장처럼 냉철한 기질, 불굴의 결의, 자제력, 그리고 미덕을 넘어 축복의 경지에 이른 관용, 게다가 세상에 뛰는 그 어떤 심장보다도 자애롭고 진실한 마음—이런 것들이 그분이 인류를 위해 행하시는 그 고귀한 사업의 자질을 이루지. 이론에서나 실제에서나 그러한데, 그분의 견해는 그분의 모든 것을 아우르는 연민만큼이나 넓기 때문이야."], ["I tell you these facts that you may know why I have such confidence in him.", "내가 이런 사실들을 자네에게 들려주는 것은, 내가 어찌하여 그분을 그토록 신뢰하는지 자네가 알게 하기 위해서네."], ["I have asked him to come at once.", "나는 그분께 즉시 와주십사 청했네."], ["I shall see Miss Westenra to-morrow again.", "나는 내일 웨스텐라 양을 다시 보겠네."], ["She is to meet me at the Stores, so that I may not alarm her mother by too early a repetition of my call.", "그녀가 스토어스 백화점에서 나를 만나기로 했어. 너무 이르게 내 방문을 되풀이하여 그녀의 어머니를 놀라게 하지 않으려는 것이지."]], [["“Yours always,", "언제나 자네의 벗,"]], [["“2 September.", "존 수어드."]], [["“My good Friend,--", "「의학박사, 철학박사, 문학박사 등등의 아브라함 판 헬싱이 수어드 박사에게 보내는 편지」 9월 2일."]], [["“When I have received your letter I am already coming to you.", "나의 좋은 벗에게—\n자네 편지를 받자마자 나는 벌써 자네에게 가는 길일세."], ["By good fortune I can leave just at once, without wrong to any of those who have trusted me.", "다행히도 나는 나를 믿어준 이들 누구에게도 폐를 끼치지 않고 지금 당장 떠날 수 있네."], ["Were fortune other, then it were bad for those who have trusted, for I come to my friend when he call me to aid those he holds dear.", "사정이 달랐다면 나를 믿어준 이들에게 좋지 못한 일이었을 테지. 나는 벗이 소중히 여기는 이들을 돕도록 나를 부를 때 그 벗에게로 가니 말일세."], ["Tell your friend that when that time you suck from my wound so swiftly the poison of the gangrene from that knife that our other friend, too nervous, let slip, you did more for him when he wants my aids and you call for them than all his great fortune could do.", "자네 벗에게 이렇게 전해주게. 그때 우리의 또 다른 벗이, 너무 긴장한 나머지 그 칼을 놓치는 바람에 생긴 괴저의 독을, 자네가 내 상처에서 그토록 재빨리 빨아내주었을 때, 그가 내 도움을 필요로 하고 자네가 그것을 청했던 그때 자네는 그의 그 막대한 재산이 할 수 있는 그 무엇보다도 더 많은 일을 그를 위해 해주었다고 말일세. 그러나 자네 벗을 위해 무언가를 한다는 것은 거기에 더해진 기쁨이라네."], ["But it is pleasure added to do for him, your friend; it is to you that I come.", "내가 가는 것은 자네에게로일세. 그러니 그레이트 이스턴 호텔에 내 방을 잡아두게."], ["Have then rooms for me at the Great Eastern Hotel, so that I may be near to hand, and please it so arrange that we may see the young lady not too late on to-morrow, for it is likely that I may have to return here that night.", "내가 가까이 있을 수 있도록 말일세. 그리고 부디 내일 너무 늦지 않게 그 젊은 아가씨를 볼 수 있도록 주선해주게. 나는 그날 밤 이리로 돌아와야 할지도 모르니."], ["But if need be I shall come again in three days, and stay longer if it must.", "그러나 필요하다면 사흘 뒤에 다시 오겠고, 부득이하다면 더 오래 머물겠네."], ["Till then good-bye, my friend John.", "그때까지 잘 있게, 나의 벗 존."]], [["“3 September.", "판 헬싱."]], [["“My dear Art,--", "「수어드 박사가 아서 홈우드 경에게 보내는 편지」 9월 3일."]], [["“Van Helsing has come and gone.", "친애하는 아트에게—\n판 헬싱 교수가 왔다 가셨네."], ["He came on with me to Hillingham, and found that, by Lucy’s discretion, her mother was lunching out, so that we were alone with her.", "그분은 나와 함께 힐링엄으로 가셨는데, 루시의 분별 덕분에 그녀의 어머니가 밖에서 점심을 들고 계셔서, 우리는 그녀와 단둘이 있을 수 있었네."], ["Van Helsing made a very careful examination of the patient.", "판 헬싱 교수는 환자를 무척 세심하게 진찰하셨어."], ["He is to report to me, and I shall advise you, for of course I was not present all the time.", "그분이 내게 보고하시기로 했고, 나는 자네에게 알려주겠네. 물론 나는 줄곧 함께 있었던 것은 아니니까."], ["He is, I fear, much concerned, but says he must think.", "그분은, 안타깝게도, 무척 염려하시는 듯한데, 좀 더 생각해봐야겠다고 하시네."], ["When I told him of our friendship and how you trust to me in the matter, he said: ‘You must tell him all you think.", "내가 우리의 우정과, 자네가 이 일에서 나를 믿고 맡긴 사정을 말씀드리자, 그분은 이렇게 말씀하셨네. ‘자네가 생각하는 바를 모두 그에게 말해주게."], ["Tell him what I think, if you can guess it, if you will.", "내 생각도, 자네가 짐작할 수 있다면, 그리고 그러고 싶다면 말해주게나."], ["Nay, I am not jesting.", "아니, 농담이 아닐세."], ["This is no jest, but life and death, perhaps more.’ I asked what he meant by that, for he was very serious.", "이건 농담이 아니라 생사가 걸린 일이고, 어쩌면 그 이상이야.’ 나는 그게 무슨 뜻이냐고 여쭈었네. 그분이 무척 진지하셨으니까."], ["This was when we had come back to town, and he was having a cup of tea before starting on his return to Amsterdam.", "이는 우리가 시내로 돌아왔을 때였는데, 그분은 암스테르담으로 돌아가는 길에 오르기 전 차를 한 잔 드시던 참이었네."], ["He would not give me any further clue.", "그분은 더는 아무런 실마리도 주려 하지 않으셨네."], ["You must not be angry with me, Art, because his very reticence means that all his brains are working for her good.", "아트, 나를 탓하지 말게. 그분의 그 과묵함이야말로 그분의 온 두뇌가 그녀를 위해 작동하고 있다는 뜻이니까."], ["He will speak plainly enough when the time comes, be sure.", "때가 오면 그분은 충분히 분명하게 말씀하실 걸세, 틀림없이."], ["So I told him I would simply write an account of our visit, just as if I were doing a descriptive special article for The Daily Telegraph.", "그래서 나는 그분께, 마치 『데일리 텔레그래프』에 실을 묘사 위주의 특집 기사라도 쓰듯이, 그저 우리 방문의 경위를 글로 적어두겠다고 말씀드렸네."], ["He seemed not to notice, but remarked that the smuts in London were not quite so bad as they used to be when he was a student here.", "그분은 눈치채지 못하신 듯했지만, 런던의 검댕이 당신이 이곳에서 학생이던 시절만큼은 그리 심하지 않다고 한마디 하시더군."], ["I am to get his report to-morrow if he can possibly make it.", "그분이 어떻게든 해주실 수 있다면 나는 내일 그분의 보고서를 받게 될 걸세."], ["In any case I am to have a letter.", "어쨌든 편지는 받게 될 거야."]], [["“Well, as to the visit.", "자, 방문에 관해서라면."], ["Lucy was more cheerful than on the day I first saw her, and certainly looked better.", "루시는 내가 처음 보았던 날보다 더 명랑했고, 확실히 안색도 나아 보였네."], ["She had lost something of the ghastly look that so upset you, and her breathing was normal.", "자네를 그토록 심란하게 했던 그 송장 같은 기색이 어느 정도 가셨고, 호흡도 정상이었어."], ["She was very sweet to the professor (as she always is), and tried to make him feel at ease; though I could see that the poor girl was making a hard struggle for it.", "그녀는 (늘 그렇듯) 교수님께 무척 다정하게 대하며 그분을 편안하게 해드리려 애썼네. 비록 그 가엾은 여인이 그러느라 무던히도 힘겨운 싸움을 하고 있음을 나는 알 수 있었지만 말이야."], ["I believe Van Helsing saw it, too, for I saw the quick look under his bushy brows that I knew of old.", "판 헬싱 교수도 그것을 알아채셨다고 믿네. 내가 예전부터 알던, 그 덥수룩한 눈썹 아래로 스치는 날카로운 눈빛을 보았으니까."], ["Then he began to chat of all things except ourselves and diseases and with such an infinite geniality that I could see poor Lucy’s pretense of animation merge into reality.", "그러고 나서 그분은 우리 자신이나 질병에 관한 것만 빼고 온갖 이야기를 나누기 시작하셨는데, 어찌나 끝없이 다정하셨던지, 나는 가엾은 루시의 짐짓 꾸며낸 활기가 진짜 활기로 녹아드는 것을 볼 수 있었네."], ["Then, without any seeming change, he brought the conversation gently round to his visit, and suavely said:--", "그러고는, 겉으로는 아무런 변화도 없이, 그분은 대화를 부드럽게 당신의 방문 쪽으로 돌리시더니, 정중하게 말씀하셨네—"]], [["“‘My dear young miss, I have the so great pleasure because you are so much beloved.", "‘사랑스러운 아가씨, 나는 더없이 큰 기쁨을 느낀다오. 아가씨가 그토록 많은 사랑을 받고 있으니 말이오."], ["That is much, my dear, ever were there that which I do not see.", "그건 대단한 것이라오, 아가씨. 비록 내 눈에 보이지 않는 무언가가 있다 하더라도 말이오."], ["They told me you were down in the spirit, and that you were of a ghastly pale.", "사람들이 내게 그러더군, 아가씨가 기분이 가라앉아 있고, 송장처럼 창백하다고 말이오."], ["To them I say: “Pouf!”’ And he snapped his fingers at me and went on: ‘But you and I shall show them how wrong they are.", "그들에게 나는 이렇게 말한다오. “흥!”’ 그러고는 나를 향해 손가락을 탁 튕기시고는 말을 이으셨네. ‘하지만 아가씨와 나는 그들이 얼마나 틀렸는지 보여줍시다."], ["How can he’--and he pointed at me with the same look and gesture as that with which once he pointed me out to his class, on, or rather after, a particular occasion which he never fails to remind me of--‘know anything of a young ladies?", "저 친구가’—그러면서 그분은, 언젠가 한 특정한 자리에서, 아니 그 자리 직후에—그분은 내게 그 일을 상기시키기를 잊는 법이 없는데—강의실에서 학생들에게 나를 가리키실 때와 똑같은 눈빛과 몸짓으로 나를 가리키셨네—‘젊은 아가씨에 대해 무얼 알겠소?"], ["He has his madmans to play with, and to bring them back to happiness, and to those that love them.", "저 친구한테는 가지고 놀 미치광이들이 있고, 그들을 행복으로, 또 그들을 사랑하는 이들에게로 되돌려놓는 일이 있다오."], ["It is much to do, and, oh, but there are rewards, in that we can bestow such happiness.", "그건 대단한 일이고, 아, 그래서 우리가 그런 행복을 베풀 수 있다는 점에서 보답도 있지요."], ["But the young ladies!", "하지만 젊은 아가씨라니!"], ["He has no wife nor daughter, and the young do not tell themselves to the young, but to the old, like me, who have known so many sorrows and the causes of them.", "저 친구한테는 아내도 딸도 없고, 또 젊은이는 젊은이에게 제 속을 털어놓지 않고, 나처럼 그토록 많은 슬픔과 그 까닭들을 겪어온 늙은이에게 털어놓는 법이라오."], ["So, my dear, we will send him away to smoke the cigarette in the garden, whiles you and I have little talk all to ourselves.’ I took the hint, and strolled about, and presently the professor came to the window and called me in.", "그러니, 아가씨, 우리는 저 친구를 정원으로 내보내 담배나 피우게 하고, 아가씨와 나는 단둘이 잠깐 이야기나 나눕시다.’ 나는 그 뜻을 알아채고 이리저리 거닐었는데, 이윽고 교수님이 창가로 오셔서 나를 안으로 부르셨네. 그분은 심각한 표정이셨지만 이렇게 말씀하셨어."], ["He looked grave, but said: ‘I have made careful examination, but there is no functional cause.", "‘세심하게 진찰해보았지만, 기능적인 원인은 없네. 피를 많이 잃었다는 자네 의견에는 나도 동의하네."], ["With you I agree that there has been much blood lost; it has been, but is not.", "잃은 적이 있었지만, 지금은 아니야. 그러나 그녀의 상태는 결코 빈혈성이 아닐세."], ["But the conditions of her are in no way anæmic.", "나는 그녀에게 하녀를 보내달라고 청했네."], ["I have asked her to send me her maid, that I may ask just one or two question, that so I may not chance to miss nothing.", "한두 가지만 물어보아서, 혹여 아무것도 놓치는 일이 없도록 말이지. 그 하녀가 무슨 말을 할지는 나도 잘 아네. 그렇지만 원인은 있어."], ["I know well what she will say.", "매사에는 언제나 원인이 있는 법이지."], ["And yet there is cause; there is always cause for everything.", "나는 집으로 돌아가 생각해봐야겠네. 자네는 내게 날마다 전보를 보내주게."], ["I must go back home and think.", "그리고 까닭이 있다면 다시 오겠네."], ["You must send to me the telegram every day; and if there be cause I shall come again. The disease--for not to be all well is a disease--interest me, and the sweet young dear, she interest me too.", "이 병은—온전히 건강하지 못한 것은 다 병이니까—내 흥미를 끄는 데다, 그 사랑스러운 아가씨, 그녀도 내 흥미를 끄네. 그녀는 나를 사로잡아."], ["She charm me, and for her, if not for you or disease, I come.’", "그러니 그녀를 위해서라면, 설령 자네나 이 병을 위해서가 아니더라도, 나는 오겠네.’"]], [["“As I tell you, he would not say a word more, even when we were alone. And so now, Art, you know all I know.", "말했다시피, 단둘이 있을 때조차 그분은 더는 한마디도 하려 하지 않으셨네. 그러니 이제, 아트, 내가 아는 것은 자네도 모두 알게 된 셈이야."], ["I shall keep stern watch.", "나는 빈틈없이 지켜보겠네."], ["I trust your poor father is rallying.", "자네의 가엾은 부친께서 차도가 있으시길 바라네."], ["It must be a terrible thing to you, my dear old fellow, to be placed in such a position between two people who are both so dear to you.", "둘 다 그토록 소중한 두 사람 사이에 그런 처지에 놓인다는 것은, 친애하는 오랜 벗이여, 자네에게 끔찍한 일임에 틀림없어."], ["I know your idea of duty to your father, and you are right to stick to it; but, if need be, I shall send you word to come at once to Lucy; so do not be over-anxious unless you hear from me.”", "나는 자네의 부친에 대한 의무관을 알고, 자네가 그것을 지키는 것이 옳다고 보네. 그러나 필요하다면, 즉시 루시에게 오라고 자네에게 기별하겠네. 그러니 내게서 소식을 듣기 전에는 지나치게 걱정하지 말게."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["4 September.--Zoöphagous patient still keeps up our interest in him.", "9월 4일.—식동물성(食動物性) 환자가 여전히 우리의 관심을 붙들어둔다."], ["He had only one outburst and that was yesterday at an unusual time.", "그는 단 한 번 발작을 일으켰는데, 그것은 어제 평소답지 않은 시각에 일어났다."], ["Just before the stroke of noon he began to grow restless.", "정오를 알리는 종이 울리기 직전, 그는 안절부절못하기 시작했다."], ["The attendant knew the symptoms, and at once summoned aid.", "간호인은 그 징후를 알고 즉시 지원을 불렀다."], ["Fortunately the men came at a run, and were just in time, for at the stroke of noon he became so violent that it took all their strength to hold him.", "다행히도 사람들이 달려와 때마침 도착했으니, 정오를 알리는 종이 울리자 그가 어찌나 난폭해졌던지 그들의 온 힘을 다해야 그를 붙들 수 있었기 때문이다."], ["In about five minutes, however, he began to get more and more quiet, and finally sank into a sort of melancholy, in which state he has remained up to now.", "그러나 오 분쯤 지나자 그는 점점 더 잠잠해지더니, 마침내 일종의 우울 상태에 빠져들어 지금까지 그 상태에 머물러 있다."], ["The attendant tells me that his screams whilst in the paroxysm were really appalling; I found my hands full when I got in, attending to some of the other patients who were frightened by him.", "간호인이 말하기를, 그가 발작 중에 지른 비명은 참으로 소름 끼쳤다고 한다. 내가 들어갔을 때, 나는 그에게 겁먹은 다른 환자 몇몇을 돌보느라 손이 모자랄 지경이었다."], ["Indeed, I can quite understand the effect, for the sounds disturbed even me, though I was some distance away.", "사실 나는 그 효과를 충분히 이해할 수 있는데, 그 소리는 꽤 떨어져 있던 나조차 동요시켰기 때문이다."], ["It is now after the dinner-hour of the asylum, and as yet my patient sits in a corner brooding, with a dull, sullen, woe-begone look in his face, which seems rather to indicate than to show something directly.", "지금은 요양원의 저녁 식사 시간이 지났는데도, 내 환자는 아직 한구석에 앉아 골똘히 생각에 잠겨 있다. 그의 얼굴에는 멍하고 시무룩하며 비탄에 잠긴 표정이 어려 있는데, 무언가를 직접 드러낸다기보다는 암시하는 듯하다."], ["I cannot quite understand it.", "나는 그것을 도무지 이해하지 못하겠다."]], [["Later.--Another change in my patient. At five o’clock I looked in on him, and found him seemingly as happy and contented as he used to be.", "이후.—내 환자에게 또 한 번의 변화가 있었다. 다섯 시에 나는 그를 들여다보았는데, 예전처럼 행복하고 만족스러워 보였다."], ["He was catching flies and eating them, and was keeping note of his capture by making nail-marks on the edge of the door between the ridges of padding.", "그는 파리를 잡아먹고 있었고, 충전재의 두둑한 부분 사이 문 가장자리에 손톱으로 자국을 내어 자신이 잡은 것을 기록하고 있었다."], ["When he saw me, he came over and apologised for his bad conduct, and asked me in a very humble, cringing way to be led back to his own room and to have his note-book again.", "그는 나를 보더니 다가와 자신의 못된 행실을 사과하고는, 무척 비굴하고 굽신거리는 투로 자기 방으로 다시 데려다 달라고, 그리고 수첩을 돌려달라고 청했다."], ["I thought it well to humour him: so he is back in his room with the window open.", "나는 그의 비위를 맞춰주는 게 좋겠다 싶었다. 그래서 그는 창문을 연 채 자기 방으로 돌아가 있다."], ["He has the sugar of his tea spread out on the window-sill, and is reaping quite a harvest of flies.", "그는 차에 넣을 설탕을 창턱에 펼쳐놓고 파리를 제법 풍성하게 거두어들이고 있다."], ["He is not now eating them, but putting them into a box, as of old, and is already examining the corners of his room to find a spider.", "이제 그는 파리를 먹지 않고, 예전처럼 상자에 넣고 있으며, 벌써 거미를 찾으려고 방 구석구석을 살피고 있다."], ["I tried to get him to talk about the past few days, for any clue to his thoughts would be of immense help to me; but he would not rise.", "나는 그에게 지난 며칠에 관해 이야기를 시켜보려 했는데, 그의 생각에 대한 어떤 실마리든 내게는 엄청난 도움이 될 터였기 때문이다."], ["For a moment or two he looked very sad, and said in a sort of far-away voice, as though saying it rather to himself than to me:--", "그러나 그는 응하지 않았다. 한두 순간 그는 무척 슬퍼 보이더니, 나에게라기보다는 차라리 자기 자신에게 하는 듯한, 어딘가 아득한 목소리로 말했다—"]], [["“All over!", "“다 끝났어!"], ["all over!", "다 끝났어!"], ["He has deserted me.", "그분이 나를 버렸어."], ["No hope for me now unless I do it for myself!”", "내 스스로 하지 않는 한 이제 내게는 희망이 없어!”"], ["Then suddenly turning to me in a resolute way, he said: “Doctor, won’t you be very good to me and let me have a little more sugar?", "그러고는 갑자기 단호한 태도로 나를 돌아보며 말했다. “선생님, 제게 좀 잘해주셔서 설탕을 조금 더 주시지 않겠어요?"], ["I think it would be good for me.”", "그게 제게 좋을 것 같아요.”"]], [["“And the flies?” I said.", "“그럼 파리는?” 내가 물었다."]], [["“Yes!", "“네!"], ["The flies like it, too, and I like the flies; therefore I like it.”", "파리도 그걸 좋아하고, 저는 파리를 좋아하지요. 그러니 저는 그걸 좋아하는 거예요.”"], ["And there are people who know so little as to think that madmen do not argue.", "그런데도 세상에는 미치광이가 논리를 따지지 못한다고 여길 만큼 아는 게 적은 사람들이 있다."], ["I procured him a double supply, and left him as happy a man as, I suppose, any in the world.", "나는 그에게 두 배의 양을 마련해주었고, 짐작건대 세상 그 누구 못지않게 행복한 사람을 남겨두고 자리를 떴다."], ["I wish I could fathom his mind.", "그의 마음을 헤아릴 수 있다면 좋으련만."]], [["Midnight.--Another change in him.", "자정.—그에게 또 한 번의 변화가 있었다."], ["I had been to see Miss Westenra, whom I found much better, and had just returned, and was standing at our own gate looking at the sunset, when once more I heard him yelling.", "나는 웨스텐라 양을 보러 갔다가—그녀는 한결 나아 보였다—막 돌아와, 우리 정문에 서서 석양을 바라보고 있었는데, 그때 또 한 번 그가 고함지르는 소리가 들렸다."], ["As his room is on this side of the house, I could hear it better than in the morning. It was a shock to me to turn from the wonderful smoky beauty of a sunset over London, with its lurid lights and inky shadows and all the marvellous tints that come on foul clouds even as on foul water, and to realise all the grim sternness of my own cold stone building, with its wealth of breathing misery, and my own desolate heart to endure it all.", "그의 방이 건물의 이쪽에 있어서, 아침보다 더 잘 들렸다. 그 불그레한 빛과 먹빛 그림자, 그리고 더러운 물 위에서처럼 더러운 구름 위에도 어리는 그 온갖 경이로운 빛깔을 머금은, 런던 위로 펼쳐진 연기 자욱한 황혼의 그 놀라운 아름다움으로부터 고개를 돌려, 내 차디찬 돌 건물의 그 모든 음울한 준엄함을, 그 안에 그득한 숨 쉬는 비참함을, 그리고 그 모든 것을 견뎌야 하는 내 자신의 황량한 마음을 깨닫는 것은, 내게 하나의 충격이었다."], ["I reached him just as the sun was going down, and from his window saw the red disc sink.", "내가 그에게 다다랐을 때 마침 해가 지고 있었고, 나는 그의 창문으로 그 붉은 원반이 가라앉는 것을 보았다."], ["As it sank he became less and less frenzied; and just as it dipped he slid from the hands that held him, an inert mass, on the floor. It is wonderful, however, what intellectual recuperative power lunatics have, for within a few minutes he stood up quite calmly and looked around him.", "그것이 가라앉을수록 그는 점점 덜 광란했고, 해가 잠겨드는 바로 그 순간, 그는 자신을 붙들고 있던 손들에서 미끄러져 내려, 축 늘어진 덩어리가 되어 바닥에 쓰러졌다. 그러나 광인들이 지닌 지적 회복력이란 참으로 놀라운 것이어서, 몇 분 안에 그는 아주 차분하게 일어서더니 주위를 둘러보았다."], ["I signalled to the attendants not to hold him, for I was anxious to see what he would do.", "나는 간호인들에게 그를 붙들지 말라고 신호했는데, 그가 무엇을 할지 보고 싶었기 때문이다."], ["He went straight over to the window and brushed out the crumbs of sugar; then he took his fly-box, and emptied it outside, and threw away the box; then he shut the window, and crossing over, sat down on his bed.", "그는 곧장 창문 쪽으로 가서 설탕 부스러기를 쓸어내고는, 파리 상자를 집어 들어 그 안의 것을 바깥에 비워버리고 상자를 내던졌다. 그러고는 창문을 닫고 건너와 침대에 앉았다."], ["All this surprised me, so I asked him: “Are you not going to keep flies any more?”", "이 모든 것이 나를 놀라게 하여, 나는 그에게 물었다. “이제 파리를 더는 안 기를 텐가?”"]], [["“No,”", "“네,”"], ["said he; “I am sick of all that rubbish!”", "그가 말했다. “그런 쓰레기 같은 짓은 이제 진절머리가 나요!”"], ["He certainly is a wonderfully interesting study.", "그는 확실히 놀라우리만큼 흥미로운 연구 대상이다."], ["I wish I could get some glimpse of his mind or of the cause of his sudden passion.", "그의 마음이나 그 돌연한 발작의 원인을 한 자락이라도 엿볼 수 있다면 좋으련만."], ["Stop; there may be a clue after all, if we can find why to-day his paroxysms came on at high noon and at sunset.", "가만, 어쩌면 결국 실마리가 있을지도 모른다. 오늘 그의 발작이 어찌하여 정오와 일몰에 일어났는지를 알아낼 수만 있다면 말이다."], ["Can it be that there is a malign influence of the sun at periods which affects certain natures--as at times the moon does others?", "어떤 본성들에게는, 마치 때때로 달이 다른 이들에게 그러하듯, 특정한 시기에 해의 악의 어린 영향력이 작용하는 것일 수 있을까?"], ["We shall see.", "두고 볼 일이다."]], [["Telegram, Seward, London, to Van Helsing, Amsterdam.", "「런던의 수어드가 암스테르담의 판 헬싱에게 보내는 전보」"]], [["“4 September.--Patient still better to-day.”", "9월 4일.—환자 오늘도 여전히 호전됨."]], [["Telegram, Seward, London, to Van Helsing, Amsterdam.", "「런던의 수어드가 암스테르담의 판 헬싱에게 보내는 전보」"]], [["“5 September.--Patient greatly improved.", "9월 5일.—환자 크게 호전됨."], ["Good appetite; sleeps naturally; good spirits; colour coming back.”", "식욕 좋고, 잠 자연스러우며, 기분 좋고, 혈색 돌아오는 중."]], [["Telegram, Seward, London, to Van Helsing, Amsterdam.", "「런던의 수어드가 암스테르담의 판 헬싱에게 보내는 전보」"]], [["“6 September.--Terrible change for the worse.", "9월 6일.—끔찍하게 악화됨. 즉시 오시오."], ["Come at once; do not lose an hour.", "한 시간도 지체 마시오."], ["I hold over telegram to Holmwood till have seen you.”", "홈우드에게 보낼 전보는 당신을 뵐 때까지 미뤄두겠소."]], [["“6 September.", "「수어드 박사가 아서 홈우드 님에게 보내는 편지」"]], [["“My dear Art,--", "“9월 6일."]], [["“My news to-day is not so good. Lucy this morning had gone back a bit.", "“친애하는 아트에게,—\n“오늘 내 소식은 그리 좋지 못하네."], ["There is, however, one good thing which has arisen from it; Mrs.", "오늘 아침 루시가 다소 나빠졌어. 그러나 거기서 한 가지 좋은 일이 생겼네."], ["Westenra was naturally anxious concerning Lucy, and has consulted me professionally about her.", "웨스튼라 부인은 당연히 루시 일로 걱정하던 차에, 딸에 관해 내게 의사로서 진찰을 청했다네."], ["I took advantage of the opportunity, and told her that my old master, Van Helsing, the great specialist, was coming to stay with me, and that I would put her in his charge conjointly with myself; so now we can come and go without alarming her unduly, for a shock to her would mean sudden death, and this, in Lucy’s weak condition, might be disastrous to her.", "나는 이 기회를 틈타, 위대한 전문의이신 내 옛 스승 반 헬싱이 나와 함께 머물러 계실 예정이라고, 그래서 부인을 나와 그분께 함께 맡기겠노라고 말씀드렸네. 그러니 이제 우리는 부인을 지나치게 놀라게 하지 않고도 드나들 수 있게 되었지. 부인에게는 충격이 곧 급사를 뜻할 터인데, 루시의 약한 상태에서 그런 일이 벌어진다면 더없이 참담한 결과가 될 테니 말이네."], ["We are hedged in with difficulties, all of us, my poor old fellow; but, please God, we shall come through them all right.", "우리 모두, 가엾은 친구여, 사방이 어려움으로 둘러싸여 있네. 그러나 신의 가호로 무사히 헤쳐 나가게 되겠지."], ["If any need I shall write, so that, if you do not hear from me, take it for granted that I am simply waiting for news.", "무슨 일이 생기면 편지하겠네. 그러니 내게서 소식이 없거든 그저 내가 소식을 기다리고 있는 것이라 여기게나."], ["In haste", "바삐 줄이며,"]], [["“Yours ever,", "“언제나 자네의 벗,"]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "“존 수어드.”"]], [["7 September.--The first thing Van Helsing said to me when we met at Liverpool Street was:--", "「수어드 박사의 일기」 9월 7일. — 리버풀 가에서 만났을 때 반 헬싱이 나에게 처음 건넨 말은 이것이었다."]], [["“Have you said anything to our young friend the lover of her?”", "“우리 젊은 친구, 그녀를 사랑하는 그 청년에게 무어라 말한 게 있는가?”"]], [["“No,” I said.", "“아니요,”"], ["“I waited till I had seen you, as I said in my telegram.", "하고 내가 답했다. “전보에서 말씀드린 대로 선생님을 뵐 때까지 기다렸습니다."], ["I wrote him a letter simply telling him that you were coming, as Miss Westenra was not so well, and that I should let him know if need be.”", "그저 웨스튼라 양의 상태가 좋지 못하여 선생님께서 오신다는 것, 그리고 필요하면 알려주겠다는 것만 적어 편지를 보냈습니다.”"]], [["“Right, my friend,”", "“옳네, 친구,”"], ["he said, “quite right!", "그가 말했다. “아주 옳아!"], ["Better he not know as yet; perhaps he shall never know.", "그가 아직은 모르는 편이 낫네. 어쩌면 영영 모르게 될 수도 있지."], ["I pray so; but if it be needed, then he shall know all.", "나는 그러기를 기도하네. 그러나 필요하다면 그때는 모든 것을 알게 될 것이야."], ["And, my good friend John, let me caution you.", "그리고, 나의 좋은 친구 존, 한 가지 일러두겠네."], ["You deal with the madmen.", "자네는 미친 사람들을 상대하지."], ["All men are mad in some way or the other; and inasmuch as you deal discreetly with your madmen, so deal with God’s madmen, too--the rest of the world.", "사람은 누구나 이런저런 식으로 미쳐 있다네. 그러니 자네가 미친 자들을 신중히 다루듯, 신께서 만드신 미친 자들 — 곧 나머지 온 세상 사람들 — 도 그렇게 다루게."], ["You tell not your madmen what you do nor why you do it; you tell them not what you think.", "미친 자들에게는 자네가 무엇을 하는지, 왜 하는지 말하지 않지. 무슨 생각을 하는지도 말하지 않고."], ["So you shall keep knowledge in its place, where it may rest--where it may gather its kind around it and breed.", "그렇게 해서 지식을 제자리에, 그것이 쉴 수 있는 곳에 — 제 동류를 둘레에 끌어모아 번식할 수 있는 곳에 — 간직하는 것이지."], ["You and I shall keep as yet what we know here, and here.”", "자네와 나는 우리가 아는 것을 아직은 여기, 그리고 여기에 간직해 두세.”"], ["He touched me on the heart and on the forehead, and then touched himself the same way.", "그는 내 가슴과 이마를 차례로 짚더니, 자신에게도 똑같이 했다."], ["“I have for myself thoughts at the present.", "“나는 지금 나 나름의 생각이 있네."], ["Later I shall unfold to you.”", "나중에 자네에게 펼쳐 보이겠네.”"]], [["“Why not now?”", "“왜 지금은 안 됩니까?”"], ["I asked. “It may do some good; we may arrive at some decision.”", "하고 내가 물었다. “도움이 될지도 모르고, 어떤 결론에 이를 수도 있지 않습니까.”"], ["He stopped and looked at me, and said:--", "그는 걸음을 멈추고 나를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“My friend John, when the corn is grown, even before it has ripened--while the milk of its mother-earth is in him, and the sunshine has not yet begun to paint him with his gold, the husbandman he pull the ear and rub him between his rough hands, and blow away the green chaff, and say to you: ‘Look!", "“나의 친구 존, 곡식이 자라났을 때, 아직 여물기도 전에 — 어머니 대지의 젖이 아직 그 안에 있고 햇살이 아직 그것을 금빛으로 물들이기 시작하지도 않았을 때 — 농부는 이삭을 뽑아 거친 손으로 비벼서 푸른 겉껍질을 불어 날려 보내고는 자네에게 이렇게 말하지. ‘보시오!"], ["he’s good corn; he will make good crop when the time comes.’”", "좋은 곡식이오. 때가 되면 좋은 수확이 되겠소.’”"], ["I did not see the application, and told him so.", "나는 그 비유의 뜻을 알아듣지 못하여 그렇게 말했다."], ["For reply he reached over and took my ear in his hand and pulled it playfully, as he used long ago to do at lectures, and said: “The good husbandman tell you so then because he knows, but not till then.", "그러자 그는 답 대신 손을 뻗어 내 귀를 잡고는, 오래전 강의 시절에 곧잘 하던 것처럼 장난스레 잡아당기며 말했다. “좋은 농부가 그때 자네에게 그리 말하는 것은 그가 알기 때문일세. 그러나 그때가 되기 전에는 아니지."], ["But you do not find the good husbandman dig up his planted corn to see if he grow; that is for the children who play at husbandry, and not for those who take it as of the work of their life.", "그렇다고 좋은 농부가 심어 놓은 곡식을 자라는지 보겠다고 파헤치는 일은 없네. 그건 농사 놀이를 하는 아이들이나 하는 짓이지, 농사를 제 평생의 일로 삼는 사람이 할 짓이 아니야."], ["See you now, friend John?", "이제 알겠는가, 친구 존?"], ["I have sown my corn, and Nature has her work to do in making it sprout; if he sprout at all, there’s some promise; and I wait till the ear begins to swell.”", "나는 내 곡식을 뿌렸고, 그것을 싹틔우는 일은 자연의 몫이지. 그것이 싹을 틔우기만 한다면 어떤 약속이 있는 것이고, 나는 이삭이 부풀기 시작할 때까지 기다리는 것일세.”"], ["He broke off, for he evidently saw that I understood.", "그는 말을 끊었으니, 내가 알아들었음을 분명히 보았던 것이다."], ["Then he went on, and very gravely:--", "그러고는 매우 엄숙하게 말을 이었다."]], [["“You were always a careful student, and your case-book was ever more full than the rest.", "“자네는 언제나 꼼꼼한 학생이었고, 자네의 증례 기록부는 늘 다른 누구의 것보다 빼곡했지."], ["You were only student then; now you are master, and I trust that good habit have not fail.", "그때 자네는 학생일 뿐이었으나, 이제는 한 사람의 권위자라네. 나는 그 좋은 습관이 사라지지 않았으리라 믿네."], ["Remember, my friend, that knowledge is stronger than memory, and we should not trust the weaker.", "친구여, 기억하게. 지식은 기억보다 강하니, 우리는 더 약한 쪽을 믿어서는 안 되네."], ["Even if you have not kept the good practise, let me tell you that this case of our dear miss is one that may be--mind, I say may be--of such interest to us and others that all the rest may not make him kick the beam, as your peoples say. Take then good note of it.", "설령 자네가 그 좋은 습관을 유지하지 못했더라도, 한 가지 말해 두겠네. 우리 사랑스러운 아가씨의 이 증례는 — 명심하게, 어쩌면이라고 말하는 것일세 — 우리와 다른 이들에게 어찌나 흥미로운 것이 될 수 있는지, 자네 나라 사람들 말마따나 나머지 모든 것이 저울추를 들어 올리지 못할 만큼일 수도 있네."], ["Nothing is too small.", "그러니 잘 적어 두게."], ["I counsel you, put down in record even your doubts and surmises.", "무엇 하나 사소하다고 흘려서는 안 되네. 충고하건대 자네의 의심과 추측까지도 기록에 남겨 두게."], ["Hereafter it may be of interest to you to see how true you guess.", "훗날 자네의 짐작이 얼마나 들어맞았는지 들여다보는 것이 흥미로울지도 모르니."], ["We learn from failure, not from success!”", "우리는 성공이 아니라 실패에서 배우는 법일세!”"]], [["When I described Lucy’s symptoms--the same as before, but infinitely more marked--he looked very grave, but said nothing.", "내가 루시의 증세를 설명했을 때 — 전과 같았으나 한없이 더 두드러진 것이었다 — 그는 매우 엄숙한 표정을 지었으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["He took with him a bag in which were many instruments and drugs, “the ghastly paraphernalia of our beneficial trade,” as he once called, in one of his lectures, the equipment of a professor of the healing craft.", "그는 여러 기구와 약품이 든 가방을 가지고 왔는데, 이는 그가 언젠가 강의에서 치료술을 다루는 자의 장비를 두고 “우리 이로운 직업의 섬뜩한 도구 일습”이라 부른 바로 그것이었다."], ["When we were shown in, Mrs. Westenra met us.", "우리가 안으로 안내되자 웨스튼라 부인이 우리를 맞았다."], ["She was alarmed, but not nearly so much as I expected to find her.", "그녀는 놀란 듯했으나, 내가 예상했던 것만큼은 결코 아니었다."], ["Nature in one of her beneficent moods has ordained that even death has some antidote to its own terrors.", "자연은 그 자비로운 기분의 하나로, 죽음조차도 그 자체의 공포에 대한 해독제를 어느 정도 지니도록 정해 두었다."], ["Here, in a case where any shock may prove fatal, matters are so ordered that, from some cause or other, the things not personal--even the terrible change in her daughter to whom she is so attached--do not seem to reach her.", "여기, 어떤 충격이든 치명적일 수 있는 경우에, 사정이 그렇게 짜여 있어, 무슨 까닭에서인지 자기 자신에게 직접 닿지 않는 일들은 — 그토록 애지중지하는 딸의 끔찍한 변화조차도 — 그녀에게 미치지 못하는 듯하다."], ["It is something like the way Dame Nature gathers round a foreign body an envelope of some insensitive tissue which can protect from evil that which it would otherwise harm by contact.", "그것은 마치 자연의 여신이 이물질의 둘레에 무감각한 조직의 외피를 둘러, 그렇지 않았다면 접촉으로 해를 입혔을 것을 악으로부터 보호하는 방식과도 비슷하다."], ["If this be an ordered selfishness, then we should pause before we condemn any one for the vice of egoism, for there may be deeper root for its causes than we have knowledge of.", "만일 이것이 정해진 이기심이라면, 우리는 누군가를 자기중심주의라는 악덕으로 단죄하기 전에 잠시 멈추어야 할 것이다. 그 원인에는 우리가 아는 것보다 더 깊은 뿌리가 있을지도 모르니 말이다."]], [["I used my knowledge of this phase of spiritual pathology, and laid down a rule that she should not be present with Lucy or think of her illness more than was absolutely required.", "나는 영적 병리학의 이 국면에 대한 내 지식을 활용하여, 부인이 루시 곁에 있거나 딸의 병을 꼭 필요한 정도 이상으로 생각하지 않도록 하나의 규칙을 정해 두었다."], ["She assented readily, so readily that I saw again the hand of Nature fighting for life.", "그녀는 선뜻 따랐는데, 어찌나 선뜻이던지 나는 다시금 생명을 위해 싸우는 자연의 손길을 보았다."], ["Van Helsing and I were shown up to Lucy’s room.", "반 헬싱과 나는 루시의 방으로 안내되었다."], ["If I was shocked when I saw her yesterday, I was horrified when I saw her to-day.", "어제 그녀를 보고 충격을 받았다면, 오늘은 그녀를 보고 소름이 끼쳤다."], ["She was ghastly, chalkily pale; the red seemed to have gone even from her lips and gums, and the bones of her face stood out prominently; her breathing was painful to see or hear.", "그녀는 섬뜩하리만치, 백악처럼 창백했다. 입술과 잇몸에서마저 붉은빛이 가신 듯했고, 얼굴 뼈가 두드러지게 도드라졌으며, 그 숨소리는 보기에도 듣기에도 고통스러웠다."], ["Van Helsing’s face grew set as marble, and his eyebrows converged till they almost touched over his nose.", "반 헬싱의 얼굴은 대리석처럼 굳어졌고, 그의 눈썹은 콧등 위에서 거의 맞닿을 만큼 좁혀졌다."], ["Lucy lay motionless, and did not seem to have strength to speak, so for a while we were all silent.", "루시는 꼼짝없이 누워 말할 기운조차 없어 보였으므로, 우리는 한동안 모두 말이 없었다."], ["Then Van Helsing beckoned to me, and we went gently out of the room.", "그러다 반 헬싱이 나에게 손짓을 했고, 우리는 조용히 방을 나섰다."], ["The instant we had closed the door he stepped quickly along the passage to the next door, which was open. Then he pulled me quickly in with him and closed the door.", "문을 닫는 순간 그는 복도를 따라 재빨리 걸어 열려 있던 옆방으로 가더니, 나를 얼른 끌어들이고 문을 닫았다. “맙소사!”"], ["“My God!” he said; “this is dreadful.", "그가 말했다. “이건 끔찍하네."], ["There is no time to be lost.", "한시도 지체할 수 없어."], ["She will die for sheer want of blood to keep the heart’s action as it should be.", "그녀는 심장이 제대로 뛰는 데 필요한 피가 모자라 죽고 말 걸세."], ["There must be transfusion of blood at once. Is it you or me?”", "당장 수혈을 해야 하네. 자네인가, 나인가?”"]], [["“I am younger and stronger, Professor.", "“제가 더 젊고 튼튼합니다, 선생님."], ["It must be me.”", "저여야 합니다.”"]], [["“Then get ready at once.", "“그럼 당장 채비하게."], ["I will bring up my bag. I am prepared.”", "내가 가방을 가져오지. 나는 준비가 되어 있네.”"]], [["I went downstairs with him, and as we were going there was a knock at the hall-door.", "나는 그와 함께 아래층으로 내려갔다. 내려가는데 현관문에서 노크 소리가 났다."], ["When we reached the hall the maid had just opened the door, and Arthur was stepping quickly in.", "우리가 현관에 이르렀을 때 하녀가 막 문을 연 참이었고, 아서가 빠르게 들어서고 있었다."], ["He rushed up to me, saying in an eager whisper:--", "그는 나에게 달려와 다급한 속삭임으로 말했다."]], [["“Jack, I was so anxious. I read between the lines of your letter, and have been in an agony.", "“잭, 나는 너무도 불안했네. 자네 편지의 행간을 읽고는 줄곧 괴로워했지."], ["The dad was better, so I ran down here to see for myself.", "아버님이 좀 나아지셔서 내 눈으로 직접 보려고 이렇게 달려온 걸세."], ["Is not that gentleman Dr. Van Helsing?", "저분이 반 헬싱 박사 아니신가?"], ["I am so thankful to you, sir, for coming.”", "와 주셔서 정말 감사합니다, 선생님.”"], ["When first the Professor’s eye had lit upon him he had been angry at his interruption at such a time; but now, as he took in his stalwart proportions and recognised the strong young manhood which seemed to emanate from him, his eyes gleamed.", "처음 그 청년에게 시선이 닿았을 때, 교수는 이런 때에 끼어든 것을 못마땅해했다. 그러나 이제 그의 다부진 체격을 살피고 그에게서 뿜어 나오는 듯한 굳센 젊음의 기운을 알아보자, 그의 두 눈이 번뜩였다."], ["Without a pause he said to him gravely as he held out his hand:--", "그는 지체 없이 손을 내밀며 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“Sir, you have come in time.", "“이보게, 자네는 때맞춰 와 주었네."], ["You are the lover of our dear miss.", "자네가 우리 사랑스러운 아가씨를 사랑하는 그 청년이지."], ["She is bad, very, very bad.", "그녀는 위중하네, 아주, 아주 위중해."], ["Nay, my child, do not go like that.”", "아니, 이 사람아, 그렇게 굴면 안 되네.”"], ["For he suddenly grew pale and sat down in a chair almost fainting.", "그가 갑자기 창백해지며 거의 기절할 듯 의자에 주저앉았기 때문이다."], ["“You are to help her.", "“자네가 그녀를 도와야 하네."], ["You can do more than any that live, and your courage is your best help.”", "자네는 살아 있는 그 누구보다도 많은 것을 할 수 있고, 자네의 용기야말로 가장 큰 도움일세.”"]], [["“What can I do?” asked Arthur hoarsely.", "“제가 무엇을 할 수 있습니까?” 아서가 쉰 목소리로 물었다."], ["“Tell me, and I shall do it.", "“말씀해 주십시오, 그러면 하겠습니다."], ["My life is hers, and I would give the last drop of blood in my body for her.”", "제 목숨은 그녀의 것이고, 그녀를 위해서라면 제 몸의 마지막 한 방울 피까지 바치겠습니다.”"], ["The Professor has a strongly humorous side, and I could from old knowledge detect a trace of its origin in his answer:--", "교수에게는 짙은 해학적인 면이 있었으니, 나는 오래전부터의 경험으로 그의 대답에 그런 기미가 깃들어 있음을 알아챌 수 있었다."]], [["“My young sir, I do not ask so much as that--not the last!”", "“이보게 젊은이, 나는 그렇게까지 청하지는 않네 — 마지막 한 방울까지는 말이야!”"]], [["“What shall I do?” There was fire in his eyes, and his open nostril quivered with intent.", "“제가 무엇을 하면 됩니까?” 그의 눈에는 불길이 일었고, 벌어진 콧구멍이 결의로 떨렸다."], ["Van Helsing slapped him on the shoulder.", "반 헬싱이 그의 어깨를 탁 쳤다."], ["“Come!” he said.", "“이리 오게!”"], ["“You are a man, and it is a man we want.", "그가 말했다. “자네는 사내일세."], ["You are better than me, better than my friend John.”", "그리고 우리에게 필요한 것이 바로 사내야."], ["Arthur looked bewildered, and the Professor went on by explaining in a kindly way:--", "자네는 나보다, 내 친구 존보다 낫네.” 아서가 어리둥절해 보이자 교수는 다정한 투로 설명을 이어갔다."]], [["“Young miss is bad, very bad.", "“젊은 아가씨가 위중하네, 아주 위중해."], ["She wants blood, and blood she must have or die.", "그녀에게는 피가 필요하고, 피를 얻지 못하면 죽고 말 걸세."], ["My friend John and I have consulted; and we are about to perform what we call transfusion of blood--to transfer from full veins of one to the empty veins which pine for him.", "내 친구 존과 나는 의논을 했네. 우리는 이른바 수혈이라는 것을 하려는 참인데 — 피로 가득 찬 한 사람의 혈관에서, 피를 갈망하며 텅 빈 혈관으로 옮기는 것일세."], ["John was to give his blood, as he is the more young and strong than me”--here Arthur took my hand and wrung it hard in silence--“but, now you are here, you are more good than us, old or young, who toil much in the world of thought.", "존이 자기 피를 주기로 했었네, 나보다 더 젊고 튼튼하니까” — 여기서 아서가 내 손을 잡고 말없이 힘껏 쥐었다 — “그러나 이제 자네가 왔으니, 자네가 우리보다 낫네, 늙었든 젊었든, 우리는 생각의 세계에서 너무 많이 애쓰는 사람들이지."], ["Our nerves are not so calm and our blood not so bright than yours!”", "우리 신경은 자네만큼 차분하지 못하고, 우리 피도 자네만큼 맑지 못하네!”"], ["Arthur turned to him and said:--", "아서가 그를 향해 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“If you only knew how gladly I would die for her you would understand----”", "“제가 얼마나 기꺼이 그녀를 위해 죽을 수 있는지 아신다면, 이해하실 텐데요——”"]], [["He stopped, with a sort of choke in his voice.", "그는 목이 메는 듯한 소리를 내며 말을 멈추었다."]], [["“Good boy!” said Van Helsing.", "“착한 청년이로군!” 반 헬싱이 말했다."], ["“In the not-so-far-off you will be happy that you have done all for her you love.", "“머지않아 자네는 사랑하는 이를 위해 할 수 있는 모든 것을 다 했다는 사실에 행복해질 걸세."], ["Come now and be silent.", "자, 이제 와서 잠자코 있게."], ["You shall kiss her once before it is done, but then you must go; and you must leave at my sign.", "일을 시작하기 전에 한 번 그녀에게 입을 맞춰도 좋네. 그러나 그다음에는 가야 하네. 내가 신호하면 떠나야 해."], ["Say no word to Madame; you know how it is with her!", "부인께는 한마디도 하지 말게. 그분이 어떤 상태인지 자네도 알지 않나!"], ["There must be no shock; any knowledge of this would be one.", "충격이 있어서는 안 되네. 이 일을 알게 되는 것 자체가 충격일 테니."], ["Come!”", "자, 가세!”"]], [["We all went up to Lucy’s room.", "우리는 모두 루시의 방으로 올라갔다."], ["Arthur by direction remained outside.", "아서는 지시받은 대로 밖에 남았다."], ["Lucy turned her head and looked at us, but said nothing.", "루시는 고개를 돌려 우리를 바라보았으나 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["She was not asleep, but she was simply too weak to make the effort.", "그녀는 잠든 것이 아니라, 그저 너무 약해서 애쓸 기운이 없었던 것이다."], ["Her eyes spoke to us; that was all. Van Helsing took some things from his bag and laid them on a little table out of sight.", "그녀의 눈이 우리에게 말을 건넸으니, 그것이 전부였다. 반 헬싱은 가방에서 몇 가지를 꺼내 보이지 않게 작은 탁자 위에 놓았다."], ["Then he mixed a narcotic, and coming over to the bed, said cheerily:--", "그러고는 진정제를 섞어, 침대로 다가가 밝은 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Now, little miss, here is your medicine.", "“자, 아가씨, 여기 약이 있어요."], ["Drink it off, like a good child.", "착한 아이답게 쭉 들이켜요."], ["See, I lift you so that to swallow is easy. Yes.”", "보세요, 삼키기 쉽도록 이렇게 일으켜 드릴게요. 그래요.”"], ["She had made the effort with success.", "그녀는 애써 무사히 그것을 삼켰다."]], [["It astonished me how long the drug took to act.", "약이 효과를 내기까지 그토록 오래 걸리는 것이 나는 놀라웠다."], ["This, in fact, marked the extent of her weakness.", "사실 이것이야말로 그녀가 얼마나 쇠약한지를 가늠케 하는 것이었다."], ["The time seemed endless until sleep began to flicker in her eyelids.", "졸음이 그녀의 눈꺼풀에 어른거리기 시작하기까지의 시간이 끝없이 느껴졌다."], ["At last, however, the narcotic began to manifest its potency; and she fell into a deep sleep.", "그러나 마침내 진정제가 효력을 드러내기 시작했고, 그녀는 깊은 잠에 빠져들었다."], ["When the Professor was satisfied he called Arthur into the room, and bade him strip off his coat.", "교수는 흡족해하자 아서를 방 안으로 불러들여 외투를 벗으라고 일렀다. 그러고는 덧붙였다."], ["Then he added: “You may take that one little kiss whiles I bring over the table.", "“내가 탁자를 가져오는 동안 그 한 번의 가벼운 입맞춤을 해도 좋네."], ["Friend John, help to me!”", "친구 존, 나를 좀 도와주게!”"], ["So neither of us looked whilst he bent over her.", "그리하여 그가 그녀에게 몸을 굽히는 동안 우리 둘 다 그쪽을 보지 않았다."]], [["Van Helsing turning to me, said:", "반 헬싱이 나를 향해 돌아서며 말했다."]], [["“He is so young and strong and of blood so pure that we need not defibrinate it.”", "“이 청년은 매우 젊고 튼튼한 데다 피가 워낙 맑으니, 우리는 그것의 섬유소를 제거할 필요가 없겠네.”"]], [["Then with swiftness, but with absolute method, Van Helsing performed the operation.", "그러고는 신속하게, 그러나 더없이 빈틈없는 방식으로 반 헬싱은 그 시술을 했다."], ["As the transfusion went on something like life seemed to come back to poor Lucy’s cheeks, and through Arthur’s growing pallor the joy of his face seemed absolutely to shine.", "수혈이 진행됨에 따라 가엾은 루시의 두 뺨에 생명 같은 것이 되돌아오는 듯했고, 점점 창백해지는 아서의 얼굴 너머로는 그 기쁨이 그야말로 환히 빛나는 듯했다."], ["After a bit I began to grow anxious, for the loss of blood was telling on Arthur, strong man as he was.", "얼마 뒤 나는 불안해지기 시작했으니, 아무리 그가 건장한 사내라 해도 피의 손실이 아서에게 영향을 미치고 있었기 때문이다."], ["It gave me an idea of what a terrible strain Lucy’s system must have undergone that what weakened Arthur only partially restored her.", "아서를 약하게 만든 것이 그녀를 부분적으로밖에 회복시키지 못한다는 사실은, 루시의 몸이 얼마나 끔찍한 부담을 겪었을지를 짐작케 했다."], ["But the Professor’s face was set, and he stood watch in hand and with his eyes fixed now on the patient and now on Arthur.", "그러나 교수의 얼굴은 굳어 있었고, 그는 손에 시계를 든 채 서서 눈을 환자에게 두었다가 다시 아서에게 두기를 거듭했다."], ["I could hear my own heart beat.", "나는 내 심장이 뛰는 소리를 들을 수 있었다."], ["Presently he said in a soft voice: “Do not stir an instant.", "이윽고 그가 부드러운 목소리로 말했다. “한순간도 움직이지 말게. 충분해."], ["It is enough. You attend him; I will look to her.”", "자네는 이 친구를 돌보게. 나는 그녀를 살피지.”"], ["When all was over I could see how much Arthur was weakened.", "모든 것이 끝났을 때, 나는 아서가 얼마나 약해졌는지 알 수 있었다."], ["I dressed the wound and took his arm to bring him away, when Van Helsing spoke without turning round--the man seems to have eyes in the back of his head:--", "나는 상처를 싸매고 그를 데리고 나오려 그의 팔을 잡았는데, 그때 반 헬싱이 돌아보지도 않고 말했다 — 이 사람은 뒤통수에도 눈이 달린 모양이다."]], [["“The brave lover, I think, deserve another kiss, which he shall have presently.”", "“이 용감한 연인은 또 한 번의 입맞춤을 받을 자격이 있다고 생각하네."], ["And as he had now finished his operation, he adjusted the pillow to the patient’s head.", "이제 곧 받게 될 걸세.” 그러고는 시술을 마쳤으므로, 그는 환자의 머리맡 베개를 매만졌다."], ["As he did so the narrow black velvet band which she seems always to wear round her throat, buckled with an old diamond buckle which her lover had given her, was dragged a little up, and showed a red mark on her throat.", "그렇게 하는 사이, 그녀가 늘 목에 두르고 있는 듯한 좁은 검은 벨벳 띠 — 연인이 그녀에게 준 오래된 다이아몬드 버클로 채운 — 가 살짝 위로 끌려 올라가, 그녀의 목에 난 붉은 자국이 드러났다."], ["Arthur did not notice it, but I could hear the deep hiss of indrawn breath which is one of Van Helsing’s ways of betraying emotion.", "아서는 그것을 알아채지 못했으나, 나는 반 헬싱이 감정을 드러내는 한 가지 방식인 그 깊은 들숨의 쉭 소리를 들을 수 있었다."], ["He said nothing at the moment, but turned to me, saying: “Now take down our brave young lover, give him of the port wine, and let him lie down a while.", "그는 그 순간에는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 나를 향해 돌아서며 말했다. “이제 우리 용감한 젊은 연인을 데리고 내려가, 포트와인을 좀 마시게 하고 잠시 눕혀 두게."], ["He must then go home and rest, sleep much and eat much, that he may be recruited of what he has so given to his love. He must not stay here. Hold!", "그런 다음 그는 집에 가서 쉬고, 푹 자고 많이 먹어야 하네. 그래야 사랑하는 이에게 그토록 바친 것을 다시 채울 수 있을 테니."], ["a moment. I may take it, sir, that you are anxious of result.", "그는 여기 머물러서는 안 되네. 잠깐! 잠시만. 이보게, 결과가 궁금하시겠지요."], ["Then bring it with you that in all ways the operation is successful.", "그렇다면, 시술이 모든 면에서 성공적이었다는 사실을 가지고 가시오."], ["You have saved her life this time, and you can go home and rest easy in mind that all that can be is.", "자네는 이번에 그녀의 목숨을 구했네. 그러니 집에 가서, 할 수 있는 모든 것이 다 되었다는 마음의 평안을 안고 편히 쉬게나."], ["I shall tell her all when she is well; she shall love you none the less for what you have done.", "그녀가 회복되면 내가 모든 것을 그녀에게 말해 주겠네. 자네가 한 일로 그녀가 자네를 덜 사랑하게 되는 일은 결코 없을 걸세."], ["Good-bye.”", "잘 가게.”"]], [["When Arthur had gone I went back to the room.", "아서가 떠나자 나는 방으로 돌아갔다."], ["Lucy was sleeping gently, but her breathing was stronger; I could see the counterpane move as her breast heaved.", "루시는 평온하게 잠들어 있었으나, 그 숨결은 한결 힘찼다. 가슴이 오르내리며 침대보가 움직이는 것이 보였다."], ["By the bedside sat Van Helsing, looking at her intently.", "침대 곁에는 반 헬싱이 앉아 그녀를 골똘히 바라보고 있었다."], ["The velvet band again covered the red mark.", "벨벳 띠가 다시 붉은 자국을 덮고 있었다."], ["I asked the Professor in a whisper:--", "나는 교수에게 속삭이며 물었다."]], [["“What do you make of that mark on her throat?”", "“저 목의 자국을 어떻게 보십니까?”"]], [["“What do you make of it?”", "“자네는 어떻게 보는가?”"]], [["“I have not examined it yet,”", "“아직 살펴보지 못했습니다,”"], ["I answered, and then and there proceeded to loose the band.", "하고 나는 답하고는, 그 자리에서 곧장 띠를 풀었다."], ["Just over the external jugular vein there were two punctures, not large, but not wholesome-looking.", "바깥쪽 경정맥 바로 위에 두 개의 찔린 자국이 있었는데, 크지는 않았으나 성해 보이지는 않았다."], ["There was no sign of disease, but the edges were white and worn-looking, as if by some trituration.", "질병의 징후는 없었으나, 가장자리가 무언가에 갈린 듯 희고 닳아 보였다."], ["It at once occurred to me that this wound, or whatever it was, might be the means of that manifest loss of blood; but I abandoned the idea as soon as formed, for such a thing could not be.", "곧바로 내 머릿속에는, 이 상처가 — 그것이 무엇이든 간에 — 그 뚜렷한 피의 손실의 원인일지도 모른다는 생각이 떠올랐다. 그러나 나는 그 생각이 떠오르기가 무섭게 떨쳐 버렸으니, 그런 일은 있을 수 없기 때문이었다."], ["The whole bed would have been drenched to a scarlet with the blood which the girl must have lost to leave such a pallor as she had before the transfusion.", "그 소녀가 수혈 전과 같은 창백함에 이르도록 흘렸을 만큼의 피라면, 침대 전체가 진홍빛으로 흠뻑 젖었을 것이다."]], [["“Well?”", "“어떤가?”"], ["said Van Helsing.", "하고 반 헬싱이 물었다."]], [["“Well,”", "“글쎄요,”"], ["said I, “I can make nothing of it.”", "하고 내가 말했다. “저는 도무지 알 수가 없습니다.”"], ["The Professor stood up. “I must go back to Amsterdam to-night,”", "교수가 일어섰다. “나는 오늘 밤 암스테르담으로 돌아가야 하네,”"], ["he said. “There are books and things there which I want.", "하고 그가 말했다. “거기 내가 필요한 책과 물건들이 있어."], ["You must remain here all the night, and you must not let your sight pass from her.”", "자네는 밤새 여기 남아, 그녀에게서 한순간도 눈을 떼서는 안 되네.”"]], [["“Shall I have a nurse?” I asked.", "“간호사를 둘까요?” 하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“We are the best nurses, you and I.", "“우리가 가장 좋은 간호사일세, 자네와 나 말이야."], ["You keep watch all night; see that she is well fed, and that nothing disturbs her.", "자네는 밤새 지키게. 그녀가 잘 먹는지, 무엇도 그녀를 방해하지 않는지 살피게."], ["You must not sleep all the night.", "밤새 한숨도 자서는 안 되네."], ["Later on we can sleep, you and I.", "잠은 나중에 자도 되네, 자네와 나 말이야."], ["I shall be back as soon as possible. And then we may begin.”", "나는 되도록 빨리 돌아오겠네. 그러면 우리는 시작할 수 있을 걸세.”"]], [["“May begin?”", "“시작이라뇨?”"], ["I said. “What on earth do you mean?”", "하고 내가 말했다. “도대체 무슨 말씀이십니까?”"]], [["“We shall see!” he answered, as he hurried out.", "“두고 보면 알게 될 걸세!” 그가 서둘러 나가며 답했다."], ["He came back a moment later and put his head inside the door and said with warning finger held up:--", "그는 잠시 후 다시 돌아와 문 안으로 고개를 들이밀고는, 경고하듯 손가락을 치켜세우며 말했다."]], [["“Remember, she is your charge.", "“명심하게, 그녀는 자네에게 맡겨졌네."], ["If you leave her, and harm befall, you shall not sleep easy hereafter!”", "자네가 그녀를 떠나 해를 입게 한다면, 자네는 이후로 결코 편히 잠들지 못할 걸세!”"]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary--continued.", "「수어드 박사의 일기 — 계속」"]], [["8 September.--I sat up all night with Lucy.", "9월 8일. — 나는 밤새도록 루시 곁을 지켰다."], ["The opiate worked itself off towards dusk, and she waked naturally; she looked a different being from what she had been before the operation.", "마약 성분의 효력은 땅거미가 질 무렵 저절로 풀렸고, 그녀는 자연스레 깨어났다. 그녀는 시술 전과는 딴사람 같아 보였다."], ["Her spirits even were good, and she was full of a happy vivacity, but I could see evidences of the absolute prostration which she had undergone.", "기분마저 좋았고 행복한 활기로 가득했으나, 나는 그녀가 겪은 극심한 탈진의 흔적을 볼 수 있었다."], ["When I told Mrs. Westenra that Dr. Van Helsing had directed that I should sit up with her she almost pooh-poohed the idea, pointing out her daughter’s renewed strength and excellent spirits.", "반 헬싱 박사가 내게 그녀 곁을 지키라고 지시했다고 웨스튼라 부인에게 말하자, 부인은 딸의 되살아난 기력과 더없이 좋은 기분을 들며 그 생각을 거의 코웃음 치듯 일축했다."], ["I was firm, however, and made preparations for my long vigil.", "그러나 나는 단호히 버티며 긴 밤샘을 위한 채비를 했다."], ["When her maid had prepared her for the night I came in, having in the meantime had supper, and took a seat by the bedside.", "하녀가 그녀의 잠자리를 봐 준 뒤, 나는 그사이 저녁을 먹고 들어와 침대 곁에 자리를 잡았다."], ["She did not in any way make objection, but looked at me gratefully whenever I caught her eye.", "그녀는 조금도 반대하지 않고, 내 눈길과 마주칠 때마다 고마운 듯 나를 바라보았다."], ["After a long spell she seemed sinking off to sleep, but with an effort seemed to pull herself together and shook it off.", "한참 만에 그녀는 잠에 빠져드는 듯하더니, 안간힘을 쓰며 정신을 가다듬어 잠을 떨쳐 냈다."], ["This was repeated several times, with greater effort and with shorter pauses as the time moved on.", "이것이 여러 번 되풀이되었는데, 시간이 흐를수록 더 안간힘을 쓰고 그 간격은 짧아졌다."], ["It was apparent that she did not want to sleep, so I tackled the subject at once:--", "그녀가 잠들고 싶어 하지 않는 것이 분명했으므로, 나는 곧장 그 문제를 꺼냈다."]], [["“You do not want to go to sleep?”", "“잠들고 싶지 않으신가요?”"]], [["“No; I am afraid.”", "“네. 무서워요.”"]], [["“Afraid to go to sleep! Why so?", "“잠드는 게 무섭다니! 어째서요?"], ["It is the boon we all crave for.”", "잠은 우리 모두가 갈망하는 은혜인데요.”"]], [["“Ah, not if you were like me--if sleep was to you a presage of horror!”", "“아, 선생님이 저 같으셨다면 — 잠이 선생님께 공포의 전조였다면 그러지 않으실 거예요!”"]], [["“A presage of horror!", "“공포의 전조라니!"], ["What on earth do you mean?”", "도대체 무슨 말씀이신가요?”"]], [["“I don’t know; oh, I don’t know.", "“모르겠어요. 아, 모르겠어요."], ["And that is what is so terrible.", "그래서 더 끔찍한 거예요."], ["All this weakness comes to me in sleep; until I dread the very thought.”", "이 모든 쇠약함이 잠 속에서 저에게 닥쳐와요. 그래서 잠이라는 생각 자체가 두려워질 지경이에요.”"]], [["“But, my dear girl, you may sleep to-night.", "“하지만, 아가씨, 오늘 밤은 주무셔도 됩니다."], ["I am here watching you, and I can promise that nothing will happen.”", "제가 여기서 지켜보고 있으니, 아무 일도 없으리라고 약속드리지요.”"]], [["“Ah, I can trust you!”", "“아, 선생님은 믿을 수 있어요!”"], ["I seized the opportunity, and said: “I promise you that if I see any evidence of bad dreams I will wake you at once.”", "나는 그 기회를 붙잡아 말했다. “나쁜 꿈의 기미가 조금이라도 보이면 곧장 깨워 드리겠다고 약속하지요.”"]], [["“You will?", "“그래 주시겠어요?"], ["Oh, will you really?", "아, 정말 그러시겠어요?"], ["How good you are to me. Then I will sleep!”", "저에게 어찌 이리 잘해 주시는지요. 그럼 자겠어요!”"], ["And almost at the word she gave a deep sigh of relief, and sank back, asleep.", "그리고 그 말과 거의 동시에 그녀는 깊은 안도의 한숨을 내쉬고는, 뒤로 기대어 잠이 들었다."]], [["All night long I watched by her.", "밤새도록 나는 그녀 곁에서 지켰다."], ["She never stirred, but slept on and on in a deep, tranquil, life-giving, health-giving sleep.", "그녀는 한 번도 뒤척이지 않고, 깊고 평온하며 생명을 주고 건강을 주는 잠에 끝없이 빠져 있었다."], ["Her lips were slightly parted, and her breast rose and fell with the regularity of a pendulum.", "입술은 살짝 벌어졌고, 가슴은 추처럼 규칙적으로 오르내렸다."], ["There was a smile on her face, and it was evident that no bad dreams had come to disturb her peace of mind.", "얼굴에는 미소가 어려 있었고, 그녀의 마음의 평화를 어지럽힐 나쁜 꿈은 찾아오지 않은 것이 분명했다."]], [["In the early morning her maid came, and I left her in her care and took myself back home, for I was anxious about many things.", "이른 아침에 그녀의 하녀가 왔으므로, 나는 그녀를 하녀에게 맡기고 집으로 돌아왔다. 신경 쓰이는 일이 많았기 때문이다."], ["I sent a short wire to Van Helsing and to Arthur, telling them of the excellent result of the operation.", "나는 반 헬싱과 아서에게 시술의 더없이 좋은 결과를 알리는 짧은 전보를 보냈다."], ["My own work, with its manifold arrears, took me all day to clear off; it was dark when I was able to inquire about my zoöphagous patient.", "산더미처럼 밀린 내 일을 다 정리하는 데 하루가 꼬박 걸렸다. 식육성(食肉性) 환자에 관해 물어볼 수 있게 되었을 때는 이미 날이 어두웠다."], ["The report was good; he had been quite quiet for the past day and night.", "보고는 좋았다. 그는 지난 하루 낮밤 동안 아주 조용했다는 것이었다."], ["A telegram came from Van Helsing at Amsterdam whilst I was at dinner, suggesting that I should be at Hillingham to-night, as it might be well to be at hand, and stating that he was leaving by the night mail and would join me early in the morning.", "내가 저녁을 먹는 동안 암스테르담의 반 헬싱에게서 전보가 왔는데, 가까이 있는 편이 좋을지 모르니 오늘 밤 힐링엄에 가 있으라는 것, 그리고 자신은 야간 우편 열차로 출발하여 이른 아침에 나와 합류하겠다는 내용이었다."]], [["9 September.--I was pretty tired and worn out when I got to Hillingham.", "9월 9일. — 힐링엄에 도착했을 때 나는 꽤나 지치고 녹초가 되어 있었다."], ["For two nights I had hardly had a wink of sleep, and my brain was beginning to feel that numbness which marks cerebral exhaustion.", "이틀 밤 동안 거의 한숨도 자지 못했고, 내 머리는 뇌의 피로를 알리는 그 마비 같은 것을 느끼기 시작하고 있었다."], ["Lucy was up and in cheerful spirits.", "루시는 일어나 있었고 기분이 좋았다."], ["When she shook hands with me she looked sharply in my face and said:--", "나와 악수하면서 그녀는 내 얼굴을 날카롭게 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“No sitting up to-night for you.", "“오늘 밤은 선생님이 밤을 새우시면 안 돼요."], ["You are worn out.", "녹초가 되셨잖아요."], ["I am quite well again; indeed, I am; and if there is to be any sitting up, it is I who will sit up with you.”", "저는 이제 완전히 나았어요. 정말이에요. 밤을 새워야 한다면, 제가 선생님 곁에서 새우겠어요.”"], ["I would not argue the point, but went and had my supper.", "나는 그 점을 두고 다투지 않고, 가서 저녁을 먹었다."], ["Lucy came with me, and, enlivened by her charming presence, I made an excellent meal, and had a couple of glasses of the more than excellent port.", "루시도 함께 와 주었고, 그 사랑스러운 존재로 활기를 돋워 준 덕에 나는 훌륭한 식사를 했으며, 더없이 훌륭한 그 포트와인을 두어 잔 마셨다."], ["Then Lucy took me upstairs, and showed me a room next her own, where a cozy fire was burning.", "그러고서 루시는 나를 위층으로 데려가, 자기 방 옆방을 보여 주었다. 거기에는 아늑한 불이 타고 있었다. “자,”"], ["“Now,” she said, “you must stay here.", "하고 그녀가 말했다. “선생님은 여기 계셔야 해요."], ["I shall leave this door open and my door too.", "저는 이 문도, 제 방 문도 열어 두겠어요."], ["You can lie on the sofa for I know that nothing would induce any of you doctors to go to bed whilst there is a patient above the horizon.", "소파에 누우셔도 돼요. 환자가 지평선 위에 있는 한, 선생님 같은 의사분들을 침대로 끌고 갈 만한 것은 아무것도 없다는 걸 저도 아니까요."], ["If I want anything I shall call out, and you can come to me at once.”", "뭔가 필요하면 제가 부를 테니, 그러면 곧장 와 주시면 돼요.”"], ["I could not but acquiesce, for I was “dog-tired,” and could not have sat up had I tried.", "나는 그저 따를 수밖에 없었으니, 나는 “녹초”가 되어 있어 애써 본들 밤을 새울 수가 없었기 때문이다."], ["So, on her renewing her promise to call me if she should want anything, I lay on the sofa, and forgot all about everything.", "그리하여 그녀가 무엇이든 필요하면 나를 부르겠다는 약속을 다시 하자, 나는 소파에 누워 모든 것을 까맣게 잊었다."]], [["Lucy Westenra’s Diary.", "「루시 웨스튼라의 일기」"]], [["9 September.--I feel so happy to-night.", "9월 9일. — 오늘 밤 나는 너무도 행복하다."], ["I have been so miserably weak, that to be able to think and move about is like feeling sunshine after a long spell of east wind out of a steel sky.", "그토록 비참하게 약해져 있던 터라, 생각하고 움직일 수 있다는 것이 마치 강철 같은 하늘에서 동풍이 오래 불어 댄 끝에 햇살을 느끼는 것만 같다."], ["Somehow Arthur feels very, very close to me.", "어쩐 일인지 아서가 아주, 아주 가까이 있는 듯 느껴진다."], ["I seem to feel his presence warm about me.", "그의 존재가 나를 둘러싸고 따뜻하게 감싸는 것 같다."], ["I suppose it is that sickness and weakness are selfish things and turn our inner eyes and sympathy on ourselves, whilst health and strength give Love rein, and in thought and feeling he can wander where he wills.", "짐작건대 병과 쇠약함이란 이기적인 것이어서 우리 내면의 눈과 연민을 자기 자신에게로 돌려놓는 반면, 건강과 기력은 사랑을 풀어놓아, 생각과 느낌 속에서 그가 마음껏 떠돌 수 있게 하는 것이리라."], ["I know where my thoughts are.", "내 생각이 어디에 가 있는지 나는 안다."], ["If Arthur only knew!", "아서가 알기만 한다면!"], ["My dear, my dear, your ears must tingle as you sleep, as mine do waking.", "내 사랑, 내 사랑, 깨어 있는 내 귀가 그러하듯, 잠든 당신의 귀도 분명 간질거릴 거예요."], ["Oh, the blissful rest of last night!", "아, 어젯밤의 그 더없이 행복한 안식이라니!"], ["How I slept, with that dear, good Dr. Seward watching me.", "그 다정하고 좋은 수어드 박사가 나를 지켜보는 가운데 나는 어찌나 잘 잤던지."], ["And to-night I shall not fear to sleep, since he is close at hand and within call.", "그리고 오늘 밤 나는 잠들기를 두려워하지 않으리라. 그가 가까이서, 부르면 닿을 곳에 있으니 말이다."], ["Thank everybody for being so good to me!", "나에게 이토록 잘해 주는 모든 이에게 감사를!"], ["Thank God!", "신께 감사를!"], ["Good-night, Arthur.", "잘 자요, 아서."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["10 September.--I was conscious of the Professor’s hand on my head, and started awake all in a second.", "9월 10일. — 나는 내 머리에 닿은 교수의 손길을 느끼고는, 순식간에 놀라 깨어났다."], ["That is one of the things that we learn in an asylum, at any rate.", "그것이야말로 어쨌든 우리가 정신병원에서 배우는 것들 가운데 하나다."]], [["“And how is our patient?”", "“우리 환자는 좀 어떤가?”"]], [["“Well, when I left her, or rather when she left me,”", "“제가 그녀 곁을 떠날 때는, 아니 그녀가 저를 떠날 때는 괜찮았습니다,”"], ["I answered.", "하고 내가 답했다."]], [["“Come, let us see,”", "“자, 가서 보세,”"], ["he said. And together we went into the room.", "하고 그가 말했다. 그리하여 우리는 함께 방으로 들어갔다."]], [["The blind was down, and I went over to raise it gently, whilst Van Helsing stepped, with his soft, cat-like tread, over to the bed.", "블라인드가 내려져 있어, 나는 그것을 살며시 올리러 갔고, 그동안 반 헬싱은 그 부드러운, 고양이 같은 걸음으로 침대 쪽으로 다가갔다."]], [["As I raised the blind, and the morning sunlight flooded the room, I heard the Professor’s low hiss of inspiration, and knowing its rarity, a deadly fear shot through my heart.", "내가 블라인드를 올려 아침 햇살이 방 안에 쏟아져 들자, 나는 교수의 낮은 들숨의 쉭 소리를 들었다. 그 소리가 얼마나 드문 것인지 알았던 터라, 치명적인 공포가 내 심장을 꿰뚫었다."], ["As I passed over he moved back, and his exclamation of horror, “Gott in Himmel!” needed no enforcement from his agonised face.", "내가 다가가자 그는 뒤로 물러섰고, “하느님 맙소사!”라는 그의 공포에 찬 외침은 그 고통스러운 얼굴로 더 강조할 필요도 없었다."], ["He raised his hand and pointed to the bed, and his iron face was drawn and ashen white.", "그는 손을 들어 침대를 가리켰는데, 그 무쇠 같은 얼굴은 일그러진 채 잿빛으로 핏기가 가셔 있었다."], ["I felt my knees begin to tremble.", "나는 무릎이 떨려 오는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["There on the bed, seemingly in a swoon, lay poor Lucy, more horribly white and wan-looking than ever.", "* 하느님 맙소사: 원문은 독일어 “Gott in Himmel!” 거기 침대 위에, 기절한 듯이 가엾은 루시가 누워 있었다."], ["Even the lips were white, and the gums seemed to have shrunken back from the teeth, as we sometimes see in a corpse after a prolonged illness.", "그 어느 때보다도 끔찍하게 희고 핼쑥해 보였다. 입술마저 희었고, 잇몸은 마치 오랜 병을 앓은 시신에게서 더러 보는 것처럼 이에서 움츠러든 듯했다."], ["Van Helsing raised his foot to stamp in anger, but the instinct of his life and all the long years of habit stood to him, and he put it down again softly.", "반 헬싱이 분노에 발을 들어 쾅 구르려 했으나, 그의 평생의 본능과 오랜 세월의 습관이 그를 붙들어, 그는 다시 발을 살며시 내려놓았다. “빨리!”"], ["“Quick!” he said.", "그가 말했다."], ["“Bring the brandy.”", "“브랜디를 가져오게.”"], ["I flew to the dining-room, and returned with the decanter.", "나는 식당으로 날아가 유리병을 들고 돌아왔다."], ["He wetted the poor white lips with it, and together we rubbed palm and wrist and heart.", "그는 그것으로 가엾은 흰 입술을 적셨고, 우리는 함께 손바닥과 손목과 심장을 문질렀다."], ["He felt her heart, and after a few moments of agonising suspense said:--", "그가 그녀의 심장을 짚어 보더니, 애끓는 몇 순간의 긴장 끝에 말했다."]], [["“It is not too late.", "“너무 늦지는 않았네."], ["It beats, though but feebly.", "비록 미약하나 뛰고 있어."], ["All our work is undone; we must begin again.", "우리 일은 모두 헛수고가 되었네. 다시 시작해야 하네."], ["There is no young Arthur here now; I have to call on you yourself this time, friend John.”", "이번에는 젊은 아서가 여기 없으니, 친구 존, 이번에는 자네 자신에게 청할 수밖에 없네.”"], ["As he spoke, he was dipping into his bag and producing the instruments for transfusion; I had taken off my coat and rolled up my shirt-sleeve.", "그렇게 말하면서 그는 가방을 뒤져 수혈 기구를 꺼내고 있었다. 나는 외투를 벗고 셔츠 소매를 걷어 올렸다."], ["There was no possibility of an opiate just at present, and no need of one; and so, without a moment’s delay, we began the operation.", "지금 당장은 마취제를 쓸 가능성도 없었고, 그럴 필요도 없었다. 그리하여 우리는 한순간도 지체하지 않고 시술을 시작했다."], ["After a time--it did not seem a short time either, for the draining away of one’s blood, no matter how willingly it be given, is a terrible feeling--Van Helsing held up a warning finger.", "얼마쯤 뒤 — 그리 짧은 시간도 아니었으니, 아무리 기꺼이 바친다 해도 제 피가 빠져나가는 것은 끔찍한 느낌이다 — 반 헬싱이 경고하듯 손가락을 치켜들었다. “움직이지 말게,”"], ["“Do not stir,”", "하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “but I fear that with growing strength she may wake; and that would make danger, oh, so much danger.", "“그러나 기력이 살아나면서 그녀가 깨어날까 두렵네. 그러면 위험해질 걸세, 아, 너무도 위험해."], ["But I shall precaution take.", "하지만 내가 예방 조치를 하겠네."], ["I shall give hypodermic injection of morphia.”", "모르핀을 피하 주사로 놓아 주지.”"], ["He proceeded then, swiftly and deftly, to carry out his intent.", "그러고는 신속하고 능란하게 자신의 의도를 실행에 옮겼다."], ["The effect on Lucy was not bad, for the faint seemed to merge subtly into the narcotic sleep.", "루시에게 미친 영향은 나쁘지 않았으니, 기절이 미묘하게 마약성 잠으로 녹아들어 가는 듯했기 때문이다."], ["It was with a feeling of personal pride that I could see a faint tinge of colour steal back into the pallid cheeks and lips.", "그 창백한 두 뺨과 입술에 희미한 핏기가 슬며시 되돌아오는 것을 보았을 때, 나는 일종의 개인적인 자부심을 느꼈다."], ["No man knows, till he experiences it, what it is to feel his own life-blood drawn away into the veins of the woman he loves.", "제 핏줄의 피가 사랑하는 여인의 혈관으로 빨려 들어가는 것을 느끼는 것이 어떤 것인지는, 직접 겪어 보기 전에는 누구도 알지 못한다."]], [["The Professor watched me critically.", "교수는 나를 비판적으로 지켜보았다."], ["“That will do,” he said.", "“이쯤이면 됐네,” 하고 그가 말했다."], ["“Already?”", "“벌써요?”"], ["I remonstrated.", "하고 나는 항변했다."], ["“You took a great deal more from Art.”", "“아트에게서는 훨씬 더 많이 뽑으셨잖습니까.”"], ["To which he smiled a sad sort of smile as he replied:--", "그러자 그는 슬픈 듯한 미소를 지으며 답했다."]], [["“He is her lover, her fiancé.", "“그는 그녀의 연인이고, 약혼자일세."], ["You have work, much work, to do for her and for others; and the present will suffice.”", "자네는 그녀를 위해, 그리고 다른 이들을 위해 할 일이, 할 일이 많아. 지금은 이 정도면 충분하네.”"]], [["When we stopped the operation, he attended to Lucy, whilst I applied digital pressure to my own incision.", "우리가 시술을 멈추자 그는 루시를 돌보았고, 나는 내 절개 부위를 손가락으로 눌렀다."], ["I laid down, whilst I waited his leisure to attend to me, for I felt faint and a little sick.", "나는 그가 나를 살펴 줄 짬을 기다리며 누워 있었으니, 어질하고 약간 속이 메스꺼웠기 때문이다."], ["By-and-by he bound up my wound, and sent me downstairs to get a glass of wine for myself.", "이윽고 그는 내 상처를 싸매고는, 나더러 아래층으로 내려가 와인을 한 잔 마시라고 했다."], ["As I was leaving the room, he came after me, and half whispered:--", "내가 방을 나서는데 그가 뒤따라와 반쯤 속삭이듯 말했다."]], [["“Mind, nothing must be said of this.", "“명심하게, 이 일은 한마디도 발설해서는 안 되네."], ["If our young lover should turn up unexpected, as before, no word to him.", "우리 젊은 연인이 전처럼 불쑥 나타나거든, 그에게 한마디도 말하지 말게."], ["It would at once frighten him and enjealous him, too.", "그는 곧장 겁을 먹을 것이고, 게다가 질투까지 하게 될 걸세."], ["There must be none. So!”", "그래서는 안 되네. 알겠지!”"]], [["When I came back he looked at me carefully, and then said:--", "내가 돌아오자 그는 나를 찬찬히 살피더니 말했다."]], [["“You are not much the worse.", "“자네는 그리 나빠 보이지 않는군."], ["Go into the room, and lie on your sofa, and rest awhile; then have much breakfast, and come here to me.”", "방으로 들어가 소파에 누워 잠시 쉬게. 그런 다음 든든히 아침을 먹고 이리로 와서 나와 합류하게.”"]], [["I followed out his orders, for I knew how right and wise they were.", "나는 그의 지시를 따랐으니, 그것이 얼마나 옳고 현명한 것인지 알았기 때문이다."], ["I had done my part, and now my next duty was to keep up my strength.", "나는 내 몫을 다했고, 이제 내 다음 의무는 기력을 유지하는 것이었다."], ["I felt very weak, and in the weakness lost something of the amazement at what had occurred.", "나는 무척 약해진 느낌이었고, 그 쇠약함 속에서 방금 벌어진 일에 대한 놀라움이 어느 정도 가셨다."], ["I fell asleep on the sofa, however, wondering over and over again how Lucy had made such a retrograde movement, and how she could have been drained of so much blood with no sign anywhere to show for it.", "그러나 나는 소파에서 잠이 들었으니, 루시가 어떻게 그토록 퇴행해 버렸는지, 그리고 어떻게 그 어디에도 흔적 하나 없이 그토록 많은 피를 빼앗길 수 있었는지를 거듭거듭 의아해하면서였다."], ["I think I must have continued my wonder in my dreams, for, sleeping and waking, my thoughts always came back to the little punctures in her throat and the ragged, exhausted appearance of their edges--tiny though they were.", "잠결에도 그 의아함을 이어 갔던 모양으로, 자나 깨나 내 생각은 늘 그녀의 목에 난 작은 찔린 자국들과, 비록 자그마하긴 했으나 너덜너덜하고 지친 듯한 그 가장자리로 되돌아왔다."]], [["Lucy slept well into the day, and when she woke she was fairly well and strong, though not nearly so much so as the day before.", "루시는 한낮까지 잘 잤고, 깨어났을 때는 꽤 좋아지고 기력도 있었으나, 전날만큼은 결코 아니었다."], ["When Van Helsing had seen her, he went out for a walk, leaving me in charge, with strict injunctions that I was not to leave her for a moment.", "반 헬싱은 그녀를 살펴본 뒤, 나에게 그녀를 한순간도 떠나지 말라는 엄한 당부를 남기고는 산책을 나갔다."], ["I could hear his voice in the hall, asking the way to the nearest telegraph office.", "나는 현관에서 그가 가장 가까운 전신국으로 가는 길을 묻는 목소리를 들을 수 있었다."]], [["Lucy chatted with me freely, and seemed quite unconscious that anything had happened.", "루시는 나와 거리낌 없이 이야기를 나누었고, 무슨 일이 있었는지 전혀 의식하지 못하는 듯했다."], ["I tried to keep her amused and interested.", "나는 그녀를 즐겁게 하고 흥미를 잃지 않게 하려 애썼다."], ["When her mother came up to see her, she did not seem to notice any change whatever, but said to me gratefully:--", "그녀의 어머니가 보러 올라왔을 때, 부인은 아무런 변화도 알아채지 못하는 듯, 나에게 고마운 듯 말했다."]], [["“We owe you so much, Dr. Seward, for all you have done, but you really must now take care not to overwork yourself.", "“수어드 박사님, 해 주신 모든 일에 우리는 너무도 많은 신세를 졌어요. 하지만 이제 정말로 무리하지 않도록 조심하셔야 해요."], ["You are looking pale yourself.", "박사님도 안색이 창백하세요."], ["You want a wife to nurse and look after you a bit; that you do!”", "박사님을 간호하고 좀 돌봐 줄 아내가 필요하시군요. 정말이에요!”"], ["As she spoke, Lucy turned crimson, though it was only momentarily, for her poor wasted veins could not stand for long such an unwonted drain to the head.", "그녀가 말하는 동안 루시는 새빨개졌으나, 그것은 잠깐일 뿐이었다. 그 가엾고 쇠한 핏줄이 머리로 그토록 뜻밖에 피가 몰리는 것을 오래 버티지 못했기 때문이다."], ["The reaction came in excessive pallor as she turned imploring eyes on me.", "그 반작용은 지나친 창백함으로 나타났고, 그녀는 애원하는 눈빛으로 나를 바라보았다."], ["I smiled and nodded, and laid my finger on my lips; with a sigh, she sank back amid her pillows.", "나는 미소 지으며 고개를 끄덕이고는 손가락을 입술에 갖다 댔다. 그러자 그녀는 한숨을 쉬며 베개에 파묻혀 뒤로 기댔다."]], [["Van Helsing returned in a couple of hours, and presently said to me: “Now you go home, and eat much and drink enough.", "반 헬싱은 두어 시간 뒤에 돌아와, 이윽고 나에게 말했다. “자, 자네는 집에 가서 많이 먹고 충분히 마시게."], ["Make yourself strong.", "몸을 튼튼히 하게나."], ["I stay here to-night, and I shall sit up with little miss myself.", "나는 오늘 밤 여기 머물러, 내가 직접 아가씨 곁을 지키겠네."], ["You and I must watch the case, and we must have none other to know.", "자네와 나는 이 증례를 지켜보아야 하고, 다른 누구도 알게 해서는 안 되네."], ["I have grave reasons.", "나에게는 중대한 이유가 있어."], ["No, do not ask them; think what you will.", "아니, 그것을 묻지는 말게. 자네 좋을 대로 생각하게나."], ["Do not fear to think even the most not-probable.", "가장 있을 법하지 않은 것조차도 두려워 말고 생각해 보게."], ["Good-night.”", "잘 자게.”"]], [["In the hall two of the maids came to me, and asked if they or either of them might not sit up with Miss Lucy.", "현관에서 하녀 둘이 나에게 다가와, 자기들이나 둘 중 하나가 루시 아가씨 곁을 지켜도 되지 않겠느냐고 물었다."], ["They implored me to let them; and when I said it was Dr. Van Helsing’s wish that either he or I should sit up, they asked me quite piteously to intercede with the “foreign gentleman.”", "그들은 그렇게 하게 해 달라고 애원했다. 그러나 내가 반 헬싱 박사나 내가 지키는 것이 그분의 뜻이라고 하자, 그들은 그 “외국인 신사분”에게 대신 청을 넣어 달라고 자못 애처롭게 부탁했다."], ["I was much touched by their kindness.", "나는 그들의 다정함에 크게 마음이 움직였다."], ["Perhaps it is because I am weak at present, and perhaps because it was on Lucy’s account, that their devotion was manifested; for over and over again have I seen similar instances of woman’s kindness.", "어쩌면 내가 지금 약해져 있기 때문일 수도, 또 어쩌면 그것이 루시를 위한 일이었기 때문일 수도 있으나, 그들의 헌신은 분명히 드러났다. 나는 여인의 다정함의 비슷한 예를 거듭거듭 보아 왔으니 말이다."], ["I got back here in time for a late dinner; went my rounds--all well; and set this down whilst waiting for sleep.", "나는 때맞춰 이곳에 돌아와 늦은 저녁을 먹었다. 회진을 돌았는데 — 모두 무사했다 — 그리고 잠이 오기를 기다리며 이 글을 적어 둔다."], ["It is coming.", "잠이 오고 있다."]], [["11 September.--This afternoon I went over to Hillingham.", "9월 11일. — 오늘 오후 나는 힐링엄으로 건너갔다."], ["Found Van Helsing in excellent spirits, and Lucy much better.", "반 헬싱은 더없이 기분이 좋았고, 루시는 한결 나아져 있었다."], ["Shortly after I had arrived, a big parcel from abroad came for the Professor.", "내가 도착한 지 얼마 안 되어, 외국에서 온 큰 소포 하나가 교수에게 도착했다."], ["He opened it with much impressment--assumed, of course--and showed a great bundle of white flowers.", "그는 자못 거창하게 — 물론 짐짓 꾸민 것이었지만 — 그것을 열어, 한 다발의 흰 꽃을 꺼내 보였다."]], [["“These are for you, Miss Lucy,” he said.", "“이건 아가씨를 위한 거예요, 루시 양,” 하고 그가 말했다."]], [["“For me?", "“저를 위해서요?"], ["Oh, Dr. Van Helsing!”", "어머, 반 헬싱 박사님!”"]], [["“Yes, my dear, but not for you to play with.", "“그렇소, 아가씨. 하지만 가지고 놀라는 게 아니에요."], ["These are medicines.”", "이건 약이랍니다.”"], ["Here Lucy made a wry face.", "여기서 루시가 얼굴을 찡그렸다."], ["“Nay, but they are not to take in a decoction or in nauseous form, so you need not snub that so charming nose, or I shall point out to my friend Arthur what woes he may have to endure in seeing so much beauty that he so loves so much distort.", "“아니, 이건 달인 물이나 역겨운 형태로 먹는 게 아니에요. 그러니 그 매력적인 코를 그렇게 찡그릴 필요 없어요. 안 그러면 내가 내 친구 아서에게, 그가 그토록 사랑하는 그 아름다움이 이리 일그러지는 걸 보면서 어떤 괴로움을 겪어야 할지 일러바칠 테니."], ["Aha, my pretty miss, that bring the so nice nose all straight again.", "아하, 어여쁜 아가씨, 그러니 그 예쁜 코가 도로 반듯해지는군요."], ["This is medicinal, but you do not know how.", "이건 약효가 있는 거지만, 아가씨는 그 방식을 모를 거예요."], ["I put him in your window, I make pretty wreath, and hang him round your neck, so that you sleep well.", "내가 그것을 아가씨 창문에 두고, 예쁜 화환을 만들어 아가씨 목에 둘러 줄 테니, 그러면 푹 잘 수 있을 거예요."], ["Oh yes! they, like the lotus flower, make your trouble forgotten.", "오, 그렇고말고요! 이것들은 연꽃처럼 아가씨의 근심을 잊게 해 준답니다."], ["It smell so like the waters of Lethe, and of that fountain of youth that the Conquistadores sought for in the Floridas, and find him all too late.”", "그 향기가 어찌나 레테의 강물과도 같고, 콩키스타도르들이 플로리다에서 찾아 헤매다가 너무 늦게야 발견한 그 젊음의 샘물과도 같은지요.”"]], [["Whilst he was speaking, Lucy had been examining the flowers and smelling them.", "* 레테: 그리스 신화에서 마시면 과거를 잊게 된다는 망각의 강. 그가 말하는 동안 루시는 꽃을 살피며 냄새를 맡고 있었다."], ["Now she threw them down, saying, with half-laughter, and half-disgust:--", "이제 그녀는 반쯤 웃고 반쯤 질색하며 그것을 내던지며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, Professor, I believe you are only putting up a joke on me.", "“어머, 박사님, 그저 저를 놀리시는 거죠."], ["Why, these flowers are only common garlic.”", "아니, 이 꽃은 그냥 흔한 마늘이잖아요.”"]], [["To my surprise, Van Helsing rose up and said with all his sternness, his iron jaw set and his bushy eyebrows meeting:--", "놀랍게도 반 헬싱이 일어서더니 그 무쇠 같은 턱을 굳히고 무성한 눈썹을 맞붙인 채, 온통 준엄하게 말했다."]], [["“No trifling with me! I never jest!", "“나를 두고 농을 하면 안 돼요!"], ["There is grim purpose in all I do; and I warn you that you do not thwart me.", "나는 결코 농담하지 않아요! 내가 하는 모든 일에는 엄정한 목적이 있어요. 경고하건대, 나를 거스르지 말아요."], ["Take care, for the sake of others if not for your own.”", "조심해요, 자신을 위해서가 아니라면 다른 이들을 위해서라도.”"], ["Then seeing poor Lucy scared, as she might well be, he went on more gently: “Oh, little miss, my dear, do not fear me.", "그러고는 가엾은 루시가, 능히 그럴 만하게 겁먹은 것을 보고는, 한결 부드럽게 말을 이었다. “오, 아가씨, 내 사랑스러운 이여, 나를 두려워하지 말아요."], ["I only do for your good; but there is much virtue to you in those so common flowers.", "나는 오직 아가씨를 위해 하는 것이니. 이 흔하디흔한 꽃들에는 아가씨에게 이로운 효험이 많이 있답니다."], ["See, I place them myself in your room.", "보세요, 내가 직접 이것을 아가씨 방에 둘 테니."], ["I make myself the wreath that you are to wear.", "아가씨가 두를 화환도 내가 직접 만들지요."], ["But hush!", "하지만 쉿!"], ["no telling to others that make so inquisitive questions.", "그렇게 꼬치꼬치 캐묻는 다른 이들에게는 말하지 말아요."], ["We must obey, and silence is a part of obedience; and obedience is to bring you strong and well into loving arms that wait for you.", "우리는 따라야 하고, 침묵은 따름의 한 부분이며, 따름은 아가씨를 튼튼하고 건강하게 만들어, 아가씨를 기다리는 사랑하는 팔에 안기게 해 줄 거예요."], ["Now sit still awhile.", "자, 잠시 가만히 앉아 있어요."], ["Come with me, friend John, and you shall help me deck the room with my garlic, which is all the way from Haarlem, where my friend Vanderpool raise herb in his glass-houses all the year.", "친구 존, 나와 함께 갑시다. 자네가 내 마늘로 방을 꾸미는 걸 도와주게. 이건 줄곧 하를럼에서 온 것인데, 거기서는 내 친구 판데르폴이 일 년 내내 자기 온실에서 이 풀을 기른다네."], ["I had to telegraph yesterday, or they would not have been here.”", "내가 어제 전보를 쳐야 했지, 그러지 않았으면 이게 여기 와 있지 못했을 걸세.”"]], [["We went into the room, taking the flowers with us.", "우리는 꽃을 가지고 방으로 들어갔다."], ["The Professor’s actions were certainly odd and not to be found in any pharmacopoeia that I ever heard of.", "교수의 행동은 분명 기이했고, 내가 들어 본 그 어떤 약전(藥典)에서도 찾아볼 수 없는 것이었다."], ["First he fastened up the windows and latched them securely; next, taking a handful of the flowers, he rubbed them all over the sashes, as though to ensure that every whiff of air that might get in would be laden with the garlic smell.", "먼저 그는 창문을 닫아 단단히 걸쇠를 채웠다. 다음으로 꽃 한 줌을 집어, 들어올지 모를 한 줄기 바람마다 마늘 냄새를 잔뜩 머금게 하려는 듯, 창틀 전체에 그것을 문질렀다."], ["Then with the wisp he rubbed all over the jamb of the door, above, below, and at each side, and round the fireplace in the same way.", "그러고는 그 한 다발로 문설주를 위아래로, 양옆으로 두루 문질렀고, 같은 식으로 벽난로 둘레도 문질렀다."], ["It all seemed grotesque to me, and presently I said:--", "그 모든 것이 내게는 괴이쩍게 보였으므로, 이윽고 나는 말했다."]], [["“Well, Professor, I know you always have a reason for what you do, but this certainly puzzles me.", "“글쎄요, 교수님, 저는 선생님이 하시는 일에 늘 이유가 있다는 걸 압니다만, 이건 정말이지 저를 어리둥절하게 합니다."], ["It is well we have no sceptic here, or he would say that you were working some spell to keep out an evil spirit.”", "여기 회의론자가 없으니 다행입니다. 있었다면 선생님이 악령을 막아 내려고 무슨 주문을 거는 중이라고 했을 테니까요.”"]], [["“Perhaps I am!” he answered quietly as he began to make the wreath which Lucy was to wear round her neck.", "“어쩌면 그러는 중일지도 모르지!” 그는 루시가 목에 두를 화환을 만들기 시작하며 조용히 답했다."]], [["We then waited whilst Lucy made her toilet for the night, and when she was in bed he came and himself fixed the wreath of garlic round her neck.", "그러고서 우리는 루시가 잘 채비를 마치기를 기다렸고, 그녀가 침대에 들자 그는 다가가 손수 마늘 화환을 그녀의 목에 둘러 주었다."], ["The last words he said to her were:--", "그가 그녀에게 한 마지막 말은 이러했다."]], [["“Take care you do not disturb it; and even if the room feel close, do not to-night open the window or the door.”", "“이걸 흐트러뜨리지 않도록 조심해요. 그리고 설령 방이 답답하게 느껴지더라도, 오늘 밤은 창문도 문도 열지 말아요.”"]], [["“I promise,”", "“약속할게요,”"], ["said Lucy, “and thank you both a thousand times for all your kindness to me!", "하고 루시가 말했다. “그리고 저에게 베풀어 주신 모든 친절에 두 분 모두께 천 번 감사드려요!"], ["Oh, what have I done to be blessed with such friends?”", "아, 제가 무슨 일을 했기에 이런 벗들을 두는 복을 받은 걸까요?”"]], [["As we left the house in my fly, which was waiting, Van Helsing said:--", "기다리고 있던 내 삯마차를 타고 집을 나서면서 반 헬싱이 말했다."]], [["“To-night I can sleep in peace, and sleep I want--two nights of travel, much reading in the day between, and much anxiety on the day to follow, and a night to sit up, without to wink.", "“오늘 밤 나는 평안히 잘 수 있겠네. 잠이 필요하기도 하고 — 이틀간의 여행에, 그 사이 낮에는 책을 많이 읽었고, 다가올 그날에 대한 근심도 많았으며, 한숨도 자지 않고 밤을 새웠으니 말이야."], ["To-morrow in the morning early you call for me, and we come together to see our pretty miss, so much more strong for my ‘spell’ which I have work. Ho! ho!”", "내일 아침 일찍 자네가 나를 데리러 오게. 그러면 우리는 함께 우리 어여쁜 아가씨를 보러 가세. 내가 부려 놓은 ‘주문’ 덕에 그토록 더 튼튼해진 그녀를 말이야. 호! 호!”"]], [["He seemed so confident that I, remembering my own confidence two nights before and with the baneful result, felt awe and vague terror.", "그가 어찌나 자신만만해 보이던지, 나는 이틀 전 나 자신의 자신감과 그 해로운 결과를 떠올리며, 경외감과 어렴풋한 공포를 느꼈다."], ["It must have been my weakness that made me hesitate to tell it to my friend, but I felt it all the more, like unshed tears.", "그것을 친구에게 말하기를 망설이게 한 것은 분명 내 약해진 상태 탓이었으리라. 그러나 나는 그것을, 흘리지 못한 눈물처럼 더욱더 사무치게 느꼈다."]], [["Lucy Westenra’s Diary.", "「루시 웨스튼라의 일기」"]], [["12 September.--How good they all are to me.", "9월 12일. — 모두들 어찌나 나에게 잘해주는지."], ["I quite love that dear Dr. Van Helsing.", "나는 그 다정한 판 헬싱 박사가 정말 좋다."], ["I wonder why he was so anxious about these flowers.", "그가 어째서 그 꽃들을 그토록 염려했는지 궁금하다."], ["He positively frightened me, he was so fierce.", "그가 어찌나 사납던지 나를 정말로 겁먹게 했다."], ["And yet he must have been right, for I feel comfort from them already.", "그렇지만 그가 옳았던 게 분명한 것이, 나는 벌써 그 꽃들에서 위안을 느끼고 있으니 말이다."], ["Somehow, I do not dread being alone to-night, and I can go to sleep without fear.", "어쩐 일인지 오늘 밤은 혼자 있는 것이 두렵지 않고, 두려움 없이 잠들 수 있을 것 같다."], ["I shall not mind any flapping outside the window.", "창밖에서 무언가 퍼덕이는 소리가 들려도 개의치 않을 것이다."], ["Oh, the terrible struggle that I have had against sleep so often of late; the pain of the sleeplessness, or the pain of the fear of sleep, with such unknown horrors as it has for me!", "아, 요즈음 나는 잠과 그토록 자주 끔찍한 싸움을 벌여왔다. 잠 못 이루는 고통이거나, 잠이 두려운 고통이거나, 잠 속에 나를 기다리는 그 알 수 없는 공포라니!"], ["How blessed are some people, whose lives have no fears, no dreads; to whom sleep is a blessing that comes nightly, and brings nothing but sweet dreams.", "어떤 사람들은 얼마나 복된가. 그들의 삶에는 두려움도, 무서움도 없으니. 그들에게 잠은 밤마다 찾아오는 축복이요, 달콤한 꿈밖에는 가져다주지 않으니."], ["Well, here I am to-night, hoping for sleep, and lying like Ophelia in the play, with “virgin crants and maiden strewments.”", "그래, 오늘 밤 나는 여기에서 잠을 기다리며, 연극 속의 오필리아처럼 \"처녀의 화관과 처녀의 헌화\"에 둘러싸여 누워 있다."], ["I never liked garlic before, but to-night it is delightful!", "나는 전에는 마늘을 결코 좋아하지 않았으나, 오늘 밤은 그것이 즐겁기만 하다!"], ["There is peace in its smell; I feel sleep coming already.", "그 냄새에는 평화가 깃들어 있다. 벌써 잠이 오는 것이 느껴진다."], ["Good-night, everybody.", "모두들, 안녕히."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["13 September.--Called at the Berkeley and found Van Helsing, as usual, up to time.", "9월 13일. — 버클리 호텔에 들렀더니 판 헬싱이 여느 때처럼 시간을 정확히 지켜 와 있었다."], ["The carriage ordered from the hotel was waiting.", "호텔에서 부른 마차가 기다리고 있었다."], ["The Professor took his bag, which he always brings with him now.", "교수는 요즘 늘 가지고 다니는 가방을 챙겼다."]], [["Let all be put down exactly.", "모든 것을 정확히 기록해 두겠다."], ["Van Helsing and I arrived at Hillingham at eight o’clock.", "판 헬싱과 나는 여덟 시에 힐링엄에 도착했다."], ["It was a lovely morning; the bright sunshine and all the fresh feeling of early autumn seemed like the completion of nature’s annual work.", "화창한 아침이었다. 밝은 햇살과 초가을의 상쾌한 기운이 마치 자연의 한 해 작업이 완성되는 것 같았다."], ["The leaves were turning to all kinds of beautiful colours, but had not yet begun to drop from the trees.", "나뭇잎들은 온갖 아름다운 빛깔로 물들고 있었으나, 아직 나무에서 떨어지기 시작하지는 않았다."], ["When we entered we met Mrs. Westenra coming out of the morning room.", "우리가 들어서자 아침 거실에서 나오는 웨스튼라 부인과 마주쳤다."], ["She is always an early riser.", "그녀는 늘 일찍 일어나는 사람이다."], ["She greeted us warmly and said:--", "그녀는 우리를 따뜻하게 맞으며 말했다."]], [["“You will be glad to know that Lucy is better.", "\"루시가 좋아졌다는 걸 알면 기뻐하시겠지요."], ["The dear child is still asleep. I looked into her room and saw her, but did not go in, lest I should disturb her.”", "그 사랑스러운 아이가 아직 자고 있답니다. 방을 들여다보고 그 애를 보았지만, 깨울까 봐 들어가지는 않았어요.\""], ["The Professor smiled, and looked quite jubilant.", "교수는 미소를 지으며 무척 기뻐하는 표정이었다."], ["He rubbed his hands together, and said:--", "그는 두 손을 비비며 말했다."]], [["“Aha!", "\"아하!"], ["I thought I had diagnosed the case.", "내가 병세를 제대로 진단했다고 생각했지요."], ["My treatment is working,”", "내 치료가 듣고 있군요.\""], ["to which she answered:--", "그러자 그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“You must not take all the credit to yourself, doctor. Lucy’s state this morning is due in part to me.”", "\"공을 모두 박사님께만 돌리시면 안 됩니다. 오늘 아침 루시의 상태는 부분적으로 제 덕분이기도 하니까요.\""]], [["“How you do mean, ma’am?” asked the Professor.", "\"무슨 말씀이신지요, 부인?\" 교수가 물었다."]], [["“Well, I was anxious about the dear child in the night, and went into her room.", "\"실은 밤중에 그 사랑스러운 아이가 걱정되어 방에 들어가 보았답니다."], ["She was sleeping soundly--so soundly that even my coming did not wake her.", "그 애는 깊이 자고 있었어요 — 어찌나 깊이 자던지 내가 들어가도 깨지 않더군요."], ["But the room was awfully stuffy.", "그런데 방 안이 끔찍하게 답답했어요."], ["There were a lot of those horrible, strong-smelling flowers about everywhere, and she had actually a bunch of them round her neck.", "그 지독하고 냄새가 강한 꽃들이 사방에 잔뜩 놓여 있었고, 게다가 그 애는 목에 그 꽃을 한 다발이나 두르고 있더군요."], ["I feared that the heavy odour would be too much for the dear child in her weak state, so I took them all away and opened a bit of the window to let in a little fresh air.", "나는 그 무거운 냄새가 허약한 그 애에게 너무 부담스러울까 봐, 모두 치워버리고 신선한 공기가 조금 들어오도록 창문을 약간 열어놓았어요."], ["You will be pleased with her, I am sure.”", "분명 그 애를 보면 만족하실 거예요.\""]], [["She moved off into her boudoir, where she usually breakfasted early.", "그녀는 평소 일찍 아침을 들던 자신의 내실로 자리를 옮겨갔다."], ["As she had spoken, I watched the Professor’s face, and saw it turn ashen grey.", "그녀가 말하는 동안 나는 교수의 얼굴을 지켜보았는데, 그 얼굴이 잿빛으로 변하는 것을 보았다."], ["He had been able to retain his self-command whilst the poor lady was present, for he knew her state and how mischievous a shock would be; he actually smiled on her as he held open the door for her to pass into her room.", "가엾은 부인이 곁에 있는 동안에는 그는 자제력을 유지할 수 있었으니, 그녀의 상태를 알았고 충격이 얼마나 해로울지 알았던 까닭이다. 그는 부인이 방으로 들어가도록 문을 잡아주면서 실제로 그녀에게 미소까지 지었다."], ["But the instant she had disappeared he pulled me, suddenly and forcibly, into the dining-room and closed the door.", "그러나 그녀가 사라지자마자 그는 갑작스레, 그리고 거칠게 나를 식당으로 끌어들이고 문을 닫았다."]], [["Then, for the first time in my life, I saw Van Helsing break down.", "그때 나는 난생처음으로 판 헬싱이 무너지는 모습을 보았다."], ["He raised his hands over his head in a sort of mute despair, and then beat his palms together in a helpless way; finally he sat down on a chair, and putting his hands before his face, began to sob, with loud, dry sobs that seemed to come from the very racking of his heart.", "그는 일종의 말 없는 절망 속에 두 손을 머리 위로 치켜들었다가, 이내 어찌할 바를 모르겠다는 듯 두 손바닥을 마주쳐댔다. 마침내 그는 의자에 주저앉아 두 손으로 얼굴을 감싸고는, 마치 그의 심장을 쥐어짜는 데서 솟아나오는 듯한 크고 마른 흐느낌으로 오열하기 시작했다."], ["Then he raised his arms again, as though appealing to the whole universe. “God! God! God!” he said.", "그러더니 그는 마치 온 우주에 호소하듯 다시 두 팔을 치켜들었다. \"하느님! 하느님! 하느님!\" 그가 말했다."], ["“What have we done, what has this poor thing done, that we are so sore beset?", "\"우리가 무엇을 했기에, 이 가엾은 것이 무엇을 했기에 우리가 이토록 시달려야 한단 말이오?"], ["Is there fate amongst us still, sent down from the pagan world of old, that such things must be, and in such way?", "그런 일들이, 그것도 그런 식으로 일어나야만 하다니, 옛 이교의 세계에서 내려온 운명이 아직도 우리 가운데 도사리고 있단 말이오?"], ["This poor mother, all unknowing, and all for the best as she think, does such thing as lose her daughter body and soul; and we must not tell her, we must not even warn her, or she die, and then both die.", "이 가엾은 어머니는 아무것도 모른 채, 그리고 자기 딴에는 가장 잘하는 일이라 여기며, 제 딸의 몸과 영혼을 잃게 하는 그런 일을 저질렀소. 그런데도 우리는 그녀에게 말해서는 안 되고, 경고조차 해서는 안 되오. 그렇지 않으면 그녀가 죽고, 그러면 둘 다 죽을 테니."], ["Oh, how we are beset! How are all the powers of the devils against us!”", "아, 우리가 어찌 이리 시달리는가! 악마의 온갖 힘이 어찌 이리 우리를 거스르는가!\""], ["Suddenly he jumped to his feet.", "갑자기 그가 벌떡 일어섰다. \"갑시다.\""], ["“Come,” he said, “come, we must see and act.", "그가 말했다. \"갑시다. 우리는 살펴보고 움직여야 하오."], ["Devils or no devils, or all the devils at once, it matters not; we fight him all the same.”", "악마든 악마가 아니든, 아니 모든 악마가 한꺼번에 덤벼든다 해도 상관없소. 우리는 그래도 그와 싸우는 거요.\""], ["He went to the hall-door for his bag; and together we went up to Lucy’s room.", "그는 가방을 가지러 현관문 쪽으로 갔고, 우리는 함께 루시의 방으로 올라갔다."]], [["Once again I drew up the blind, whilst Van Helsing went towards the bed.", "나는 다시 한 번 블라인드를 걷어 올렸고, 그동안 판 헬싱은 침대로 다가갔다."], ["This time he did not start as he looked on the poor face with the same awful, waxen pallor as before.", "이번에는 그가 전과 똑같이 끔찍하게 밀랍 같은 창백함을 띤 가엾은 얼굴을 들여다보면서도 흠칫 놀라지 않았다."], ["He wore a look of stern sadness and infinite pity.", "그는 준엄한 슬픔과 한없는 연민이 어린 표정을 짓고 있었다."]], [["“As I expected,” he murmured, with that hissing inspiration of his which meant so much.", "\"내가 예상했던 대로군.\" 그가 그 많은 것을 의미하던 쉭쉭거리는 숨소리로 중얼거렸다."], ["Without a word he went and locked the door, and then began to set out on the little table the instruments for yet another operation of transfusion of blood.", "그는 한마디 말도 없이 가서 문을 잠그더니, 작은 탁자 위에 또 한 번의 수혈 수술을 위한 기구들을 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["I had long ago recognised the necessity, and begun to take off my coat, but he stopped me with a warning hand. “No!”", "나는 이미 오래전에 그 필요를 알아차리고 외투를 벗기 시작했으나, 그가 경고하듯 손을 들어 나를 막았다. \"안 돼요!\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said. “To-day you must operate.", "\"오늘은 당신이 집도해야 하오."], ["I shall provide.", "피는 내가 댈 테니."], ["You are weakened already.”", "당신은 이미 쇠약해져 있소.\""], ["As he spoke he took off his coat and rolled up his shirt-sleeve.", "그렇게 말하면서 그는 외투를 벗고 셔츠 소매를 걷어올렸다."]], [["Again the operation; again the narcotic; again some return of colour to the ashy cheeks, and the regular breathing of healthy sleep.", "다시 수술, 다시 마취제, 다시 잿빛 뺨에 약간의 혈색이 돌아오고, 건강한 잠의 고른 숨결이 돌아왔다."], ["This time I watched whilst Van Helsing recruited himself and rested.", "이번에는 판 헬싱이 기력을 회복하고 쉬는 동안 내가 지켜보았다."]], [["Presently he took an opportunity of telling Mrs. Westenra that she must not remove anything from Lucy’s room without consulting him; that the flowers were of medicinal value, and that the breathing of their odour was a part of the system of cure.", "이윽고 그는 기회를 보아 웨스튼라 부인에게, 자기와 상의하지 않고는 루시의 방에서 아무것도 치워서는 안 된다고, 그 꽃들은 약효가 있는 것이며 그 냄새를 들이마시는 것이 치료법의 일부라고 일러두었다."], ["Then he took over the care of the case himself, saying that he would watch this night and the next and would send me word when to come.", "그러고 나서 그는 오늘 밤과 내일 밤 자신이 지킬 것이며, 내가 와야 할 때 기별을 보내겠다고 말하며 이 환자의 간호를 직접 맡았다."]], [["After another hour Lucy waked from her sleep, fresh and bright and seemingly not much the worse for her terrible ordeal.", "다시 한 시간이 지나자 루시는 잠에서 깨어났는데, 생기 있고 밝아 보였으며, 그 끔찍한 시련을 겪고도 그다지 나빠진 것 같지 않았다."]], [["What does it all mean?", "이 모든 것이 무슨 뜻일까?"], ["I am beginning to wonder if my long habit of life amongst the insane is beginning to tell upon my own brain.", "정신병자들 사이에서 오래 살아온 내 습성이 나 자신의 머리에까지 영향을 미치기 시작한 것은 아닐까 하는 생각이 들기 시작한다."]], [["Lucy Westenra’s Diary.", "「루시 웨스튼라의 일기」"]], [["17 September.--Four days and nights of peace.", "9월 17일. — 나흘 밤낮의 평온."], ["I am getting so strong again that I hardly know myself. It is as if I had passed through some long nightmare, and had just awakened to see the beautiful sunshine and feel the fresh air of the morning around me.", "나는 다시 어찌나 기운이 나는지 나 자신을 거의 알아보지 못할 지경이다. 마치 어떤 길고 긴 악몽을 지나, 막 깨어나 아름다운 햇살을 보고 아침의 신선한 공기를 내 주위에서 느끼는 것만 같다."], ["I have a dim half-remembrance of long, anxious times of waiting and fearing; darkness in which there was not even the pain of hope to make present distress more poignant: and then long spells of oblivion, and the rising back to life as a diver coming up through a great press of water.", "길고 불안한 기다림과 두려움의 시간들에 대한, 희미한 반쪽짜리 기억이 남아 있다. 현재의 고통을 더 사무치게 할 희망의 아픔조차 없던 어둠. 그러고는 긴 망각의 시간들, 그리고 마치 잠수부가 거대한 물의 압력을 뚫고 솟아오르듯 다시 삶으로 떠오르던 것."], ["Since, however, Dr. Van Helsing has been with me, all this bad dreaming seems to have passed away; the noises that used to frighten me out of my wits--the flapping against the windows, the distant voices which seemed so close to me, the harsh sounds that came from I know not where and commanded me to do I know not what--have all ceased.", "그러나 판 헬싱 박사가 곁에 있어준 뒤로는 이 모든 나쁜 꿈이 사라진 듯하다. 나를 까무러칠 만큼 겁주던 그 소리들 — 창문을 두드리는 퍼덕임, 그토록 가깝게 들리던 먼 목소리들, 어디서 오는지 모를, 무엇을 하라는 것인지도 모를 명령을 내리던 거친 소리들 — 이 모두 그쳤다."], ["I go to bed now without any fear of sleep.", "이제 나는 잠에 대한 아무런 두려움 없이 잠자리에 든다."], ["I do not even try to keep awake.", "깨어 있으려 애쓰지조차 않는다."], ["I have grown quite fond of the garlic, and a boxful arrives for me every day from Haarlem.", "나는 마늘이 제법 좋아졌고, 하를럼에서 날마다 한 상자씩 나에게 도착한다. 오늘 밤 판 헬싱 박사는 떠난다."], ["To-night Dr. Van Helsing is going away, as he has to be for a day in Amsterdam.", "암스테르담에서 하루를 보내야 하기 때문이다. 그러나 내게는 지켜보는 이가 필요 없다."], ["But I need not be watched; I am well enough to be left alone. Thank God for mother’s sake, and dear Arthur’s, and for all our friends who have been so kind!", "혼자 있어도 될 만큼 충분히 나아졌으니까. 어머니를 위해서, 그리고 사랑하는 아서를 위해서, 또 그토록 친절히 대해준 우리 모든 벗들을 위해서, 하느님께 감사드린다!"], ["I shall not even feel the change, for last night Dr. Van Helsing slept in his chair a lot of the time.", "변화조차 느끼지 못할 것이다. 지난밤 판 헬싱 박사는 의자에 앉은 채로 많은 시간을 잤으니까."], ["I found him asleep twice when I awoke; but I did not fear to go to sleep again, although the boughs or bats or something napped almost angrily against the window-panes.", "깨어보니 그가 잠들어 있는 것을 두 번이나 보았다. 그러나 나뭇가지인지 박쥐인지 무언가가 거의 성난 듯이 창유리를 두드려댔는데도, 나는 다시 잠드는 것이 두렵지 않았다."]], [["“The Pall Mall Gazette,” 18 September.", "「폴 몰 가제트」, 9월 18일\n탈출한 늑대."]], [["Interview with the Keeper in the Zoölogical Gardens.", "본지 취재기자의 위험천만한 모험. 동물원 사육사와의 인터뷰."]], [["After many inquiries and almost as many refusals, and perpetually using the words “Pall Mall Gazette” as a sort of talisman, I managed to find the keeper of the section of the Zoölogical Gardens in which the wolf department is included.", "여러 차례 문의하고 거의 그만큼 많은 거절을 당한 끝에, 그리고 \"폴 몰 가제트\"라는 말을 일종의 부적처럼 끊임없이 입에 올린 끝에, 나는 동물원에서 늑대 부서가 포함된 구역의 사육사를 가까스로 찾아낼 수 있었다."], ["Thomas Bilder lives in one of the cottages in the enclosure behind the elephant-house, and was just sitting down to his tea when I found him.", "토머스 빌더는 코끼리 사육장 뒤편 울타리 안의 오두막 가운데 하나에 사는데, 내가 그를 찾아냈을 때 그는 막 차를 마시려고 자리에 앉던 참이었다."], ["Thomas and his wife are hospitable folk, elderly, and without children, and if the specimen I enjoyed of their hospitality be of the average kind, their lives must be pretty comfortable.", "토머스와 그의 아내는 손님 접대를 좋아하는 사람들로, 나이 지긋하고 자식이 없는데, 내가 누린 그들의 환대가 평균치라면 그들의 삶은 꽤나 편안할 것임이 틀림없다."], ["The keeper would not enter on what he called “business” until the supper was over, and we were all satisfied.", "사육사는 저녁식사가 끝나고 우리 모두가 만족할 때까지는 자신이 \"일 이야기\"라 부르는 것을 시작하려 하지 않았다."], ["Then when the table was cleared, and he had lit his pipe, he said:--", "그러더니 식탁이 치워지고 그가 파이프에 불을 붙이고 나서 말했다."]], [["“Now, sir, you can go on and arsk me what you want.", "\"자, 선생, 이제 묻고 싶은 걸 물어보슈."], ["You’ll excoose me refoosin’ to talk of perfeshunal subjects afore meals.", "식사 전에 직업 이야기를 마다한 건 양해해 주슈."], ["I gives the wolves and the jackals and the hyenas in all our section their tea afore I begins to arsk them questions.”", "우리 구역의 늑대며 자칼이며 하이에나한테 차를 먹이고 나서야 질문을 시작하니까 말이오.\""]], [["“How do you mean, ask them questions?”", "\"질문을 시작한다는 게 무슨 뜻입니까?\""], ["I queried, wishful to get him into a talkative humour.", "그를 수다스러운 기분으로 끌어들이고 싶어서 내가 물었다."]], [["“’Ittin’ of them over the ’ead with a pole is one way; scratchin’ of their hears is another, when gents as is flush wants a bit of a show-orf to their gals.", "\"막대기로 대가리를 후려치는 게 한 방법이고, 귀를 긁어주는 게 또 한 방법이지. 돈푼깨나 있는 양반들이 제 계집한테 으스대고 싶을 때 말이오."], ["I don’t so much mind the fust--the ’ittin’ with a pole afore I chucks in their dinner; but I waits till they’ve ’ad their sherry and kawffee, so to speak, afore I tries on with the ear-scratchin’.", "첫 번째 거야 나도 별로 꺼리지 않소 — 먹이를 던져주기 전에 막대기로 후려치는 거 말이오. 하지만 말하자면 셰리주와 커피를 들고 나서야 귀 긁어주기를 시도하지요."], ["Mind you,”", "명심하슈,\""], ["he added philosophically, “there’s a deal of the same nature in us as in them theer animiles.", "그가 철학자처럼 덧붙였다. \"저 짐승들이나 우리나 같은 천성이 다분히 있소."], ["Here’s you a-comin’ and arskin’ of me questions about my business, and I that grumpy-like that only for your bloomin’ ’arf-quid I’d ’a’ seen you blowed fust ’fore I’d answer.", "여기 선생이 와서 내 일에 관해 이것저것 묻고 있는데, 나는 어찌나 심통이 나던지 그 빌어먹을 반 파운드짜리만 아니었으면 대답하느니 차라리 선생이 뒈지는 꼴을 봤을 거요."], ["Not even when you arsked me sarcastic-like if I’d like you to arsk the Superintendent if you might arsk me questions.", "선생이 비꼬는 투로, 내가 선생한테 질문해도 되는지 관리소장한테 물어봐 달라느냐고 했을 때조차도 말이오."], ["Without offence did I tell yer to go to ’ell?”", "내가 기분 나쁘게 굴 것도 없이 지옥에나 가라고 했지 않소?\""]], [["“You did.”", "\"그랬지요.\""]], [["“An’ when you said you’d report me for usin’ of obscene language that was ’ittin’ me over the ’ead; but the ’arf-quid made that all right.", "\"그리고 선생이 내가 상스러운 말을 쓴다고 신고하겠다고 했을 때, 그게 내 대가리를 후려치는 거였소. 하지만 그 반 파운드짜리가 다 풀어줬지요."], ["I weren’t a-goin’ to fight, so I waited for the food, and did with my ’owl as the wolves, and lions, and tigers does.", "나는 싸울 생각이 없었으니, 먹이가 나오길 기다렸다가 늑대며 사자며 호랑이가 하는 대로 으르렁대기만 했소."], ["But, Lor’ love yer ’art, now that the old ’ooman has stuck a chunk of her tea-cake in me, an’ rinsed me out with her bloomin’ old teapot, and I’ve lit hup, you may scratch my ears for all you’re worth, and won’t git even a growl out of me.", "하지만 이런, 선생 양반, 이제 늙은 마누라가 차 케이크 한 조각을 내 입에 쑤셔넣어 주고, 그 빌어먹을 낡은 찻주전자로 속을 씻어내려 주고, 내가 담뱃불도 붙였으니, 이제 마음껏 내 귀를 긁어보슈. 으르렁거리는 소리 하나 안 나올 테니."], ["Drive along with your questions.", "어서 질문이나 하슈."], ["I know what yer a-comin’ at, that ’ere escaped wolf.”", "선생이 뭘 노리는지 알지요. 바로 그 탈출한 늑대 말이오.\""]], [["“Exactly. I want you to give me your view of it.", "\"바로 그겁니다. 그것에 대한 당신의 견해를 듣고 싶습니다."], ["Just tell me how it happened; and when I know the facts I’ll get you to say what you consider was the cause of it, and how you think the whole affair will end.”", "어떻게 일어난 일인지 말씀해 주십시오. 그 사실들을 알고 나면, 그 원인이 무엇이라고 생각하시는지, 그리고 이 사건 전체가 어떻게 끝날 거라고 보시는지 말씀해 주시기 바랍니다.\""]], [["“All right, guv’nor.", "\"좋소, 나리."], ["This ’ere is about the ’ole story.", "자초지종은 대충 이렇소."], ["That ’ere wolf what we called Bersicker was one of three grey ones that came from Norway to Jamrach’s, which we bought off him four years ago.", "우리가 버시커라고 부르던 그 늑대는 노르웨이에서 잠라크네 가게로 온 회색 늑대 세 마리 가운데 하나로, 사 년 전에 그자한테서 사들인 거요."], ["He was a nice well-behaved wolf, that never gave no trouble to talk of.", "점잖고 행실 바른 늑대로, 말썽이랄 만한 걸 부린 적이 없소."], ["I’m more surprised at ’im for wantin’ to get out nor any other animile in the place.", "이 안의 어떤 짐승보다도 그놈이 밖으로 나가고 싶어 했다는 게 더 놀랍단 말이오."], ["But, there, you can’t trust wolves no more nor women.”", "하지만, 어쨌든, 늑대란 건 계집이나 마찬가지로 믿을 게 못 되지요.\""]], [["“Don’t you mind him, sir!” broke in Mrs.", "\"저이 말은 신경 쓰지 마슈, 선생!\""], ["Tom, with a cheery laugh. “’E’s got mindin’ the animiles so long that blest if he ain’t like a old wolf ’isself!", "톰 부인이 명랑하게 웃으며 끼어들었다. \"저이가 짐승들을 하도 오래 돌보다 보니 정말이지 저 자신이 늙은 늑대 같다니까요!"], ["But there ain’t no ’arm in ’im.”", "그래도 악의는 없는 사람이에요.\""]], [["“Well, sir, it was about two hours after feedin’ yesterday when I first hear my disturbance.", "\"자, 선생, 어제 먹이를 주고 나서 두 시간쯤 됐을 때였소, 처음 소란을 들은 게."], ["I was makin’ up a litter in the monkey-house for a young puma which is ill; but when I heard the yelpin’ and ’owlin’ I kem away straight.", "나는 병든 어린 퓨마한테 깔아줄 짚을 원숭이 사육장에 마련하던 참이었소. 그런데 깽깽대고 울부짖는 소리가 들리길래 곧장 달려왔지요."], ["There was Bersicker a-tearin’ like a mad thing at the bars as if he wanted to get out.", "거기 버시커가 미친 것처럼 창살을 물어뜯고 있었소, 마치 밖으로 나가고 싶어 안달이라도 난 듯이 말이오."], ["There wasn’t much people about that day, and close at hand was only one man, a tall, thin chap, with a ’ook nose and a pointed beard, with a few white hairs runnin’ through it.", "그날은 사람이 별로 없었고, 가까이엔 딱 한 사람, 키 크고 마른 친구가 있었는데, 매부리코에 끝이 뾰족한 턱수염을 길렀고 그 사이로 흰 털이 몇 가닥 섞여 있었소."], ["He had a ’ard, cold look and red eyes, and I took a sort of mislike to him, for it seemed as if it was ’im as they was hirritated at.", "그자는 굳고 차가운 눈빛에 붉은 눈을 하고 있었는데, 나는 어쩐지 그자가 싫었소. 짐승들이 화가 난 게 바로 그자 때문인 것 같았으니까."], ["He ’ad white kid gloves on ’is ’ands, and he pointed out the animiles to me and says: ‘Keeper, these wolves seem upset at something.’", "그자는 손에 흰 새끼염소 가죽 장갑을 끼고 있었는데, 나한테 짐승들을 가리키며 이러는 거요. '사육사 양반, 이 늑대들이 뭔가에 화가 난 모양이오.'"]], [["“‘Maybe it’s you,’ says I, for I did not like the airs as he give ’isself.", "\"'아마 당신 때문이겠지요,' 하고 내가 말했소. 그자가 으스대는 꼴이 마음에 안 들었거든."], ["He didn’t git angry, as I ’oped he would, but he smiled a kind of insolent smile, with a mouth full of white, sharp teeth.", "그자는 내가 바라던 대로 화를 내진 않고, 일종의 거만한 미소를 지었는데, 입안에 희고 날카로운 이가 가득했소."], ["‘Oh no, they wouldn’t like me,’ ’e says.", "'오, 아니오, 저들이 나를 좋아할 리가 없지,' 하고 그자가 말했소."]], [["“‘Ow yes, they would,’ says I, a-imitatin’ of him.", "\"'아니, 좋아하고말고요,' 하고 내가 그자를 흉내 내며 말했소."], ["‘They always likes a bone or two to clean their teeth on about tea-time, which you ’as a bagful.’", "'저놈들은 차 마실 때쯤이면 이를 닦을 뼈다귀 한두 개를 늘 좋아하는데, 당신은 한 자루 가득 가졌으니 말이오.'"]], [["“Well, it was a odd thing, but when the animiles see us a-talkin’ they lay down, and when I went over to Bersicker he let me stroke his ears same as ever.", "\"그런데, 참 이상한 일이었소만, 짐승들이 우리가 이야기하는 걸 보더니 드러눕는 거요. 그리고 내가 버시커한테 다가가니 그놈이 여느 때처럼 제 귀를 쓰다듬게 두는 거요."], ["That there man kem over, and blessed but if he didn’t put in his hand and stroke the old wolf’s ears too!", "그 사람이 다가오더니, 세상에, 그자도 손을 뻗어 그 늙은 늑대의 귀를 쓰다듬지 뭐요!"]], [["“‘Tyke care,’ says I.", "\"'조심하슈,' 하고 내가 말했소."], ["‘Bersicker is quick.’", "'버시커는 날래니까.'"]], [["“‘Never mind,’ he says.", "\"'괜찮소,' 하고 그자가 말했소."], ["‘I’m used to ’em!’", "'나는 이놈들에 익숙하니까!'"]], [["“‘Are you in the business yourself?’ I says, tyking off my ’at, for a man what trades in wolves, anceterer, is a good friend to keepers.", "\"'당신도 이 일을 하시오?' 하고 내가 모자를 벗으며 물었소. 늑대나 뭐 그런 걸 거래하는 사람은 사육사한테 좋은 친구니까 말이오."]], [["“‘No,’ says he, ‘not exactly in the business, but I ’ave made pets of several.’ And with that he lifts his ’at as perlite as a lord, and walks away.", "\"'아니오,' 하고 그자가 말했소. '꼭 이 일을 하는 건 아니지만, 여러 마리를 애완으로 길러봤소.'"], ["Old Bersicker kep’ a-lookin’ arter ’im till ’e was out of sight, and then went and lay down in a corner and wouldn’t come hout the ’ole hevening.", "그러고는 영주처럼 정중하게 모자를 들어 올리고 걸어가 버렸소. 늙은 버시커는 그자가 보이지 않을 때까지 줄곧 그자를 바라보더니, 가서 구석에 드러누워 저녁 내내 나오려 하질 않았소."], ["Well, larst night, so soon as the moon was hup, the wolves here all began a-’owling.", "자, 어젯밤, 달이 떠오르자마자 여기 늑대들이 모두 울부짖기 시작했소."], ["There warn’t nothing for them to ’owl at.", "울부짖을 만한 게 아무것도 없었는데 말이오."], ["There warn’t no one near, except some one that was evidently a-callin’ a dog somewheres out back of the gardings in the Park road.", "근처엔 아무도 없었소, 공원 도로 쪽 정원 뒤편 어딘가에서 분명히 개를 부르던 누군가 말고는."], ["Once or twice I went out to see that all was right, and it was, and then the ’owling stopped.", "한두 번 나가서 다 괜찮은지 살펴봤는데, 괜찮았고, 그러더니 울부짖음이 그쳤소."], ["Just before twelve o’clock I just took a look round afore turnin’ in, an’, bust me, but when I kem opposite to old Bersicker’s cage I see the rails broken and twisted about and the cage empty.", "자정이 되기 직전에 나는 잠자리에 들기 전에 한 바퀴 둘러봤는데, 이런 빌어먹을, 늙은 버시커 우리 맞은편에 갔더니 창살이 부러지고 비틀려 있고 우리가 비어 있는 거요."], ["And that’s all I know for certing.”", "그게 내가 확실히 아는 전부요.\""]], [["“Did any one else see anything?”", "\"다른 누군가가 무언가를 본 사람은 없습니까?\""]], [["“One of our gard’ners was a-comin’ ’ome about that time from a ’armony, when he sees a big grey dog comin’ out through the garding ’edges.", "\"우리 정원사 하나가 그 무렵에 음악회에서 집으로 돌아오던 길에, 큰 회색 개가 정원 울타리를 뚫고 나오는 걸 봤다는 거요."], ["At least, so he says, but I don’t give much for it myself, for if he did ’e never said a word about it to his missis when ’e got ’ome, and it was only after the escape of the wolf was made known, and we had been up all night-a-huntin’ of the Park for Bersicker, that he remembered seein’ anything.", "적어도 그자 말은 그런데, 나 자신은 별로 믿질 않소. 그자가 정말 봤다면 집에 돌아가서 제 마누라한테 한마디도 안 했을 리가 없고, 늑대가 탈출한 게 알려진 뒤에야, 그것도 우리가 밤새도록 공원에서 버시커를 찾아 헤맨 뒤에야 비로소 뭔가를 봤던 게 기억났다니 말이오."], ["My own belief was that the ’armony ’ad got into his ’ead.”", "내 생각엔 그 음악회가 그자 머릿속에 들어앉은 거지요.\""]], [["“Now, Mr. Bilder, can you account in any way for the escape of the wolf?”", "\"자, 빌더 씨, 그 늑대가 탈출한 것을 어떻게든 설명해 주실 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“Well, sir,”", "\"글쎄요, 선생,\""], ["he said, with a suspicious sort of modesty, “I think I can; but I don’t know as ’ow you’d be satisfied with the theory.”", "그가 수상쩍은 겸손을 보이며 말했다. \"할 수 있을 것 같소만, 선생이 그 이론에 만족하실지는 모르겠소.\""]], [["“Certainly I shall.", "\"분명 만족할 겁니다."], ["If a man like you, who knows the animals from experience, can’t hazard a good guess at any rate, who is even to try?”", "당신처럼 경험으로 그 짐승들을 아는 사람이 그래도 그럴듯한 추측 하나 내놓지 못한다면, 대체 누가 시도라도 해볼 수 있겠습니까?\""]], [["“Well then, sir, I accounts for it this way; it seems to me that ’ere wolf escaped--simply because he wanted to get out.”", "\"그럼, 선생, 나는 이렇게 설명하겠소. 내가 보기엔 그 늑대가 탈출한 건 — 그저 밖으로 나가고 싶어서였소.\""]], [["From the hearty way that both Thomas and his wife laughed at the joke I could see that it had done service before, and that the whole explanation was simply an elaborate sell.", "토머스와 그의 아내가 둘 다 이 농담에 어찌나 호탕하게 웃던지, 나는 그것이 전에도 써먹은 적이 있는 농담이며, 그 설명 전체가 그저 공들인 익살임을 알 수 있었다."], ["I couldn’t cope in badinage with the worthy Thomas, but I thought I knew a surer way to his heart, so I said:--", "나는 이 훌륭한 토머스와 익살을 겨룰 재간은 없었으나, 그의 마음에 이르는 더 확실한 길은 안다고 여겼으므로, 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Now, Mr. Bilder, we’ll consider that first half-sovereign worked off, and this brother of his is waiting to be claimed when you’ve told me what you think will happen.”", "\"자, 빌더 씨, 그 첫 번째 반 파운드짜리는 다 치른 걸로 하고, 여기 그 형제뻘 되는 또 하나가 당신이 앞으로 어떻게 될 거라고 생각하는지 말해 주면 주인을 기다리고 있습니다.\""]], [["“Right y’are, sir,” he said briskly.", "\"옳소, 선생,\" 그가 기운차게 말했다."], ["“Ye’ll excoose me, I know, for a-chaffin’ of ye, but the old woman here winked at me, which was as much as telling me to go on.”", "\"선생을 좀 놀린 건 양해해 줄 줄 아오. 하지만 여기 늙은 마누라가 나한테 윙크를 했거든, 계속하라는 거나 마찬가지였단 말이오.\""]], [["“Well, I never!”", "\"세상에, 원!\""], ["said the old lady.", "노부인이 말했다."]], [["“My opinion is this: that ’ere wolf is a-’idin’ of, somewheres.", "\"내 의견은 이렇소. 그 늑대는 어딘가에 숨어 있소."], ["The gard’ner wot didn’t remember said he was a-gallopin’ northward faster than a horse could go; but I don’t believe him, for, yer see, sir, wolves don’t gallop no more nor dogs does, they not bein’ built that way.", "기억 못 하던 그 정원사는 늑대가 말보다 빠르게 북쪽으로 내달리더라고 했지만, 나는 그자를 믿지 않소. 왜냐면 선생, 늑대는 개보다 더 잘 내달리지 못하거든, 그렇게 생겨먹질 않았으니까."], ["Wolves is fine things in a storybook, and I dessay when they gets in packs and does be chivyin’ somethin’ that’s more afeared than they is they can make a devil of a noise and chop it up, whatever it is.", "늑대는 이야기책에서나 멋진 놈이고, 떼를 지어서 저들보다 더 겁먹은 무언가를 쫓을 때야 지독한 소리를 내며 그게 뭐든 물어뜯어 죽일 수도 있겠지요."], ["But, Lor’ bless you, in real life a wolf is only a low creature, not half so clever or bold as a good dog; and not half a quarter so much fight in ’im.", "하지만, 이런, 선생, 실제로 늑대란 건 그저 비루한 짐승이라, 좋은 개보다 절반만큼도 영리하거나 대담하지 못하고, 싸움 기질은 그 사분의 일도 안 되오."], ["This one ain’t been used to fightin’ or even to providin’ for hisself, and more like he’s somewhere round the Park a-’idin’ an’ a-shiverin’ of, and, if he thinks at all, wonderin’ where he is to get his breakfast from; or maybe he’s got down some area and is in a coal-cellar.", "이놈은 싸워본 적도, 제 먹이를 마련해 본 적도 없으니, 십중팔구 공원 어딘가에 숨어서 벌벌 떨고 있을 거요. 그리고 생각이란 걸 한다면, 아침거리를 어디서 구할까 궁리하고 있겠지요. 아니면 어느 지하실 입구로 내려가 석탄 창고에 들어가 있을지도 모르오."], ["My eye, won’t some cook get a rum start when she sees his green eyes a-shining at her out of the dark!", "이런, 어느 요리사가 어둠 속에서 그놈의 초록빛 눈이 제 쪽으로 번득이는 걸 보면 얼마나 기겁을 할까!"], ["If he can’t get food he’s bound to look for it, and mayhap he may chance to light on a butcher’s shop in time.", "먹이를 못 구하면 찾아 나설 수밖에 없을 테고, 어쩌면 운 좋게 때맞춰 정육점을 만날지도 모르지요."], ["If he doesn’t, and some nursemaid goes a-walkin’ orf with a soldier, leavin’ of the hinfant in the perambulator--well, then I shouldn’t be surprised if the census is one babby the less.", "그렇지 못하고, 어느 유모가 군인이랑 산책하러 나가면서 유아차에 아기를 두고 가버린다면 — 글쎄, 그때는 인구 조사에서 아기 하나가 줄어든다 해도 나는 놀라지 않을 거요."], ["That’s all.”", "그게 전부요.\""]], [["I was handing him the half-sovereign, when something came bobbing up against the window, and Mr. Bilder’s face doubled its natural length with surprise.", "내가 그에게 반 파운드짜리를 건네던 참에, 무언가가 창문에 불쑥 부딪쳐 올랐고, 빌더 씨의 얼굴이 놀라서 본래 길이의 두 배로 늘어났다."]], [["“God bless me!”", "\"어이쿠, 맙소사!\""], ["he said. “If there ain’t old Bersicker come back by ’isself!”", "그가 말했다. \"저기 늙은 버시커가 제 발로 돌아온 거 아니오!\""]], [["He went to the door and opened it; a most unnecessary proceeding it seemed to me.", "그는 문으로 가서 그것을 열었다. 내가 보기엔 더없이 불필요한 행동이었다."], ["I have always thought that a wild animal never looks so well as when some obstacle of pronounced durability is between us; a personal experience has intensified rather than diminished that idea.", "나는 야생 동물이란 우리 사이에 확실히 견고한 장애물이 있을 때만큼 보기 좋은 적이 없다고 늘 생각해 왔는데, 한 차례의 개인적 경험이 그 생각을 누그러뜨리기는커녕 오히려 굳혀 놓았다."]], [["After all, however, there is nothing like custom, for neither Bilder nor his wife thought any more of the wolf than I should of a dog.", "그러나 어쨌든 습관만 한 것은 없으니, 빌더도 그의 아내도 그 늑대를 두고 내가 개를 두고 그러는 것 이상으로는 마음 쓰지 않았다."], ["The animal itself was as peaceful and well-behaved as that father of all picture-wolves--Red Riding Hood’s quondam friend, whilst moving her confidence in masquerade.", "짐승 자체도, 모든 그림책 늑대의 시조 — 빨간 두건의 한때의 친구가 변장한 채 그녀의 신뢰를 흔들 때만큼이나 온순하고 행실이 발랐다."]], [["The whole scene was an unutterable mixture of comedy and pathos.", "이 광경 전체는 이루 말할 수 없는 희극과 비애의 혼합이었다."], ["The wicked wolf that for half a day had paralysed London and set all the children in the town shivering in their shoes, was there in a sort of penitent mood, and was received and petted like a sort of vulpine prodigal son.", "반나절 동안 런던을 마비시키고 온 시내 아이들을 신발 속에서 벌벌 떨게 했던 그 사악한 늑대가, 거기 일종의 뉘우치는 기색으로 있었고, 마치 늑대판 탕자라도 되는 양 환영받고 귀여움을 받았다."], ["Old Bilder examined him all over with most tender solicitude, and when he had finished with his penitent said:--", "늙은 빌더는 더없이 다정한 염려로 그놈을 구석구석 살폈고, 그 뉘우치는 놈을 다 살피고 나서 말했다."]], [["“There, I knew the poor old chap would get into some kind of trouble; didn’t I say it all along?", "\"보슈, 이 가엾은 늙은 녀석이 무슨 곤경에든 빠질 줄 내 알았다니까. 내가 줄곧 그러지 않았소?"], ["Here’s his head all cut and full of broken glass.", "여기 그놈 머리가 온통 베이고 깨진 유리투성이요."], ["’E’s been a-gettin’ over some bloomin’ wall or other.", "어디 빌어먹을 담장이라도 넘어온 게로군."], ["It’s a shyme that people are allowed to top their walls with broken bottles.", "사람들이 담장 꼭대기에 깨진 병을 박아놓도록 허락하는 건 부끄러운 일이오."], ["This ’ere’s what comes of it.", "이게 바로 그 결과란 말이오."], ["Come along, Bersicker.”", "이리 오너라, 버시커.\""]], [["He took the wolf and locked him up in a cage, with a piece of meat that satisfied, in quantity at any rate, the elementary conditions of the fatted calf, and went off to report.", "그는 늑대를 데려가 우리에 가두고, 적어도 양에서만큼은 살진 송아지의 기본 조건은 충족시킬 만한 고기 한 점을 던져준 다음, 보고하러 떠났다."]], [["I came off, too, to report the only exclusive information that is given to-day regarding the strange escapade at the Zoo.", "나 역시 오늘 동물원의 이 기이한 소동에 관해 단독으로 주어진 유일한 정보를 보도하러 나왔다."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["17 September.--I was engaged after dinner in my study posting up my books, which, through press of other work and the many visits to Lucy, had fallen sadly into arrear.", "9월 17일. — 나는 저녁식사 후 서재에서 장부를 정리하느라 분주했는데, 다른 일이 밀리고 루시를 여러 번 찾아간 탓에 장부가 한심하게 밀려 있었던 것이다."], ["Suddenly the door was burst open, and in rushed my patient, with his face distorted with passion.", "갑자기 문이 벌컥 열리더니, 내 환자가 격정으로 일그러진 얼굴을 하고 뛰어들어왔다."], ["I was thunderstruck, for such a thing as a patient getting of his own accord into the Superintendent’s study is almost unknown.", "나는 아연실색했으니, 환자가 제 발로 관리소장의 서재에 들어오는 일이란 거의 없기 때문이다."], ["Without an instant’s pause he made straight at me.", "그는 한순간도 멈추지 않고 곧장 나에게 달려들었다."], ["He had a dinner-knife in his hand, and, as I saw he was dangerous, I tried to keep the table between us.", "그는 손에 만찬용 식칼을 들고 있었는데, 그가 위험하다는 것을 알아챈 나는 우리 사이에 탁자를 두려고 애썼다."], ["He was too quick and too strong for me, however; for before I could get my balance he had struck at me and cut my left wrist rather severely.", "그러나 그는 나에게는 너무 빠르고 너무 힘이 셌다. 내가 균형을 잡기도 전에 그가 나를 내리쳐 내 왼쪽 손목을 제법 심하게 베었기 때문이다."], ["Before he could strike again, however, I got in my right and he was sprawling on his back on the floor.", "그러나 그가 다시 내리치기 전에 내가 오른손을 먹였고, 그는 바닥에 벌렁 나동그라졌다."], ["My wrist bled freely, and quite a little pool trickled on to the carpet.", "내 손목은 줄줄 피를 흘려, 작은 웅덩이가 카펫 위로 흘러내렸다."], ["I saw that my friend was not intent on further effort, and occupied myself binding up my wrist, keeping a wary eye on the prostrate figure all the time.", "나는 내 친구가 더는 시도할 작정이 아님을 알아채고는, 줄곧 엎드린 그 형체에서 경계의 눈을 떼지 않은 채 내 손목을 동여매는 데 골몰했다."], ["When the attendants rushed in, and we turned our attention to him, his employment positively sickened me.", "간병인들이 뛰어들어오고 우리가 그에게 주의를 돌렸을 때, 그가 하고 있던 짓은 정말이지 나를 역겹게 했다."], ["He was lying on his belly on the floor licking up, like a dog, the blood which had fallen from my wounded wrist.", "그는 바닥에 배를 깔고 엎드려, 개처럼, 내 다친 손목에서 떨어진 피를 핥아먹고 있었다."], ["He was easily secured, and, to my surprise, went with the attendants quite placidly, simply repeating over and over again: “The blood is the life!", "그는 쉽게 제압되었고, 놀랍게도 간병인들을 따라 아주 얌전히 갔는데, 다만 거듭거듭 이렇게 되뇌었다. \"피는 생명이다!"], ["The blood is the life!”", "피는 생명이다!\""]], [["I cannot afford to lose blood just at present; I have lost too much of late for my physical good, and then the prolonged strain of Lucy’s illness and its horrible phases is telling on me.", "나는 지금 당장은 피를 잃을 처지가 못 된다. 요즈음 내 건강에 해로울 만큼 너무 많은 피를 잃었고, 게다가 루시의 병과 그 끔찍한 국면들에서 오는 오랜 긴장이 나를 짓누르고 있다."], ["I am over-excited and weary, and I need rest, rest, rest.", "나는 과도하게 흥분했고 지쳐 있으니, 휴식이, 휴식이, 휴식이 필요하다."], ["Happily Van Helsing has not summoned me, so I need not forego my sleep; to-night I could not well do without it.", "다행히 판 헬싱이 나를 부르지 않았으니, 나는 잠을 포기하지 않아도 된다. 오늘 밤은 도저히 잠 없이는 견딜 수 없을 것 같다."]], [["Telegram, Van Helsing, Antwerp, to Seward, Carfax.", "「전보, 앤트워프의 판 헬싱이 카팩스의 수어드에게.」"]], [["(Sent to Carfax, Sussex, as no county given; delivered late by twenty-two hours.)", "(주(州)가 명시되지 않은 탓에 서식스의 카팩스로 발송되어, 스물두 시간이나 늦게 배달됨.)"]], [["“17 September.--Do not fail to be at Hillingham to-night.", "\"9월 17일. — 오늘 밤 반드시 힐링엄에 있을 것."], ["If not watching all the time frequently, visit and see that flowers are as placed; very important; do not fail.", "줄곧 지켜보지 못한다면 자주 들러서 꽃들이 놓인 그대로 있는지 확인할 것. 매우 중요함. 결코 빠뜨리지 말 것."], ["Shall be with you as soon as possible after arrival.”", "도착하는 대로 되도록 빨리 당신에게 가겠음.\""]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["18 September.--Just off for train to London.", "9월 18일. — 막 런던행 기차를 타러 떠나는 길이다."], ["The arrival of Van Helsing’s telegram filled me with dismay. A whole night lost, and I know by bitter experience what may happen in a night.", "판 헬싱의 전보가 도착해 나는 경악에 휩싸였다. 온전한 하룻밤을 잃었으니, 하룻밤 사이에 무슨 일이 일어날 수 있는지 나는 쓰라린 경험으로 안다."], ["Of course it is possible that all may be well, but what may have happened?", "물론 모든 것이 무사할 가능성도 있지만, 그동안 무슨 일이 일어났을지도 모른다."], ["Surely there is some horrible doom hanging over us that every possible accident should thwart us in all we try to do.", "분명 어떤 끔찍한 운명이 우리 위에 드리워져 있어, 우리가 하려는 모든 일을 온갖 가능한 우연이 가로막는 것이리라."], ["I shall take this cylinder with me, and then I can complete my entry on Lucy’s phonograph.", "나는 이 실린더를 가지고 가서, 루시의 축음기에 내 기록을 마저 마칠 수 있을 것이다."]], [["Memorandum left by Lucy Westenra.", "「루시 웨스튼라가 남긴 비망록.」"]], [["17 September.", "9월 17일. 밤."], ["Night.--I write this and leave it to be seen, so that no one may by any chance get into trouble through me.", "— 나는 이것을 써서, 누구든 보도록 남겨둔다. 그래야 그 누구도 어쩌다 나 때문에 곤경에 빠지는 일이 없을 테니."], ["This is an exact record of what took place to-night.", "이것은 오늘 밤 일어난 일에 대한 정확한 기록이다."], ["I feel I am dying of weakness, and have barely strength to write, but it must be done if I die in the doing.", "나는 쇠약함으로 죽어가는 것이 느껴지고, 글을 쓸 기력조차 거의 없으나, 쓰다가 죽는 한이 있어도 이것만은 해야만 한다."]], [["I went to bed as usual, taking care that the flowers were placed as Dr. Van Helsing directed, and soon fell asleep.", "나는 여느 때처럼 잠자리에 들었는데, 판 헬싱 박사가 지시한 대로 꽃들이 놓여 있도록 신경을 쓰고 나서 곧 잠이 들었다."]], [["I was waked by the flapping at the window, which had begun after that sleep-walking on the cliff at Whitby when Mina saved me, and which now I know so well.", "나는 창문을 두드리는 퍼덕임에 잠이 깼다. 그것은 휘트비의 절벽에서 그 몽유병으로 헤매다 미나가 나를 구해준 이후에 시작된 것으로, 이제 나는 그 소리를 아주 잘 안다."], ["I was not afraid, but I did wish that Dr. Seward was in the next room--as Dr. Van Helsing said he would be--so that I might have called him.", "나는 두렵지는 않았으나, 판 헬싱 박사가 그러겠다고 했던 대로 수어드 박사가 옆방에 있어서 그를 부를 수 있으면 좋겠다고 바랐다."], ["I tried to go to sleep, but could not.", "나는 잠들려 애썼지만 그럴 수 없었다."], ["Then there came to me the old fear of sleep, and I determined to keep awake.", "그러자 잠에 대한 그 오랜 두려움이 밀려와, 나는 깨어 있기로 마음먹었다."], ["Perversely sleep would try to come then when I did not want it; so, as I feared to be alone, I opened my door and called out: “Is there anybody there?”", "짓궂게도 잠은 내가 원치 않을 때라야 찾아오려 했다. 그래서 나는 혼자 있는 게 두려워, 문을 열고 소리쳤다. \"거기 누구 없나요?\""], ["There was no answer.", "대답이 없었다."], ["I was afraid to wake mother, and so closed my door again.", "나는 어머니를 깨우기가 두려워 다시 문을 닫았다."], ["Then outside in the shrubbery I heard a sort of howl like a dog’s, but more fierce and deeper.", "그때 밖에서, 관목숲에서 개의 것 같으면서도 더 사납고 더 깊은 일종의 울부짖음이 들렸다."], ["I went to the window and looked out, but could see nothing, except a big bat, which had evidently been buffeting its wings against the window.", "나는 창가로 가서 밖을 내다보았으나, 분명 창문에 날개를 부딪쳐대던 큰 박쥐 한 마리 말고는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["So I went back to bed again, but determined not to go to sleep.", "그래서 나는 다시 잠자리로 돌아왔으나, 잠들지 않기로 작정했다."], ["Presently the door opened, and mother looked in; seeing by my moving that I was not asleep, came in, and sat by me.", "이윽고 문이 열리더니 어머니가 들여다보았다. 내가 움직이는 것을 보고 내가 자고 있지 않음을 알아채고는, 들어와서 내 곁에 앉았다."], ["She said to me even more sweetly and softly than her wont:--", "어머니는 평소보다도 더 다정하고 부드럽게 나에게 말했다."]], [["“I was uneasy about you, darling, and came in to see that you were all right.”", "\"네가 마음에 걸려서, 얘야, 네가 괜찮은지 보러 들어왔단다.\""]], [["I feared she might catch cold sitting there, and asked her to come in and sleep with me, so she came into bed, and lay down beside me; she did not take off her dressing gown, for she said she would only stay a while and then go back to her own bed.", "나는 어머니가 거기 앉아 있다가 감기에 걸릴까 봐, 들어와서 나와 함께 자자고 청했다. 그래서 어머니는 침대로 들어와 내 곁에 누웠다. 어머니는 잠시만 있다가 자기 침대로 돌아갈 거라며 실내복을 벗지 않았다."], ["As she lay there in my arms, and I in hers, the flapping and buffeting came to the window again.", "어머니가 내 품에, 내가 어머니 품에 안겨 누워 있는데, 그 퍼덕임과 부딪침이 다시 창문으로 찾아왔다."], ["She was startled and a little frightened, and cried out: “What is that?”", "어머니는 흠칫 놀라 조금 겁을 먹고 소리쳤다. \"저게 뭐냐?\""], ["I tried to pacify her, and at last succeeded, and she lay quiet; but I could hear her poor dear heart still beating terribly.", "나는 어머니를 달래려 애썼고, 마침내 성공하여 어머니는 잠잠히 누웠다. 그러나 나는 어머니의 가엾은 심장이 여전히 무섭게 뛰는 소리를 들을 수 있었다."], ["After a while there was the low howl again out in the shrubbery, and shortly after there was a crash at the window, and a lot of broken glass was hurled on the floor.", "잠시 후 관목숲에서 다시 그 나직한 울부짖음이 들렸고, 곧이어 창문에서 와장창 소리가 나며 깨진 유리가 잔뜩 바닥으로 쏟아졌다."], ["The window blind blew back with the wind that rushed in, and in the aperture of the broken panes there was the head of a great, gaunt grey wolf.", "창문 블라인드가 밀려들어오는 바람에 뒤로 펄럭였고, 깨진 유리창의 구멍에는 거대하고 수척한 회색 늑대의 머리가 있었다."], ["Mother cried out in a fright, and struggled up into a sitting posture, and clutched wildly at anything that would help her.", "어머니는 겁에 질려 소리치며 안간힘을 써서 일어나 앉으려 했고, 도움이 될 만한 것이라면 무엇이든 미친 듯이 움켜쥐려 했다."], ["Amongst other things, she clutched the wreath of flowers that Dr. Van Helsing insisted on my wearing round my neck, and tore it away from me.", "그러다 다른 것들과 함께, 판 헬싱 박사가 내 목에 두르라고 고집했던 그 꽃 화환을 움켜쥐고는 나에게서 잡아 뜯어냈다."], ["For a second or two she sat up, pointing at the wolf, and there was a strange and horrible gurgling in her throat; then she fell over--as if struck with lightning, and her head hit my forehead and made me dizzy for a moment or two.", "한두 순간 어머니는 늑대를 가리키며 일어나 앉아 있었고, 어머니의 목에서는 기이하고 끔찍한 그르렁 소리가 났다. 그러더니 어머니는 마치 벼락이라도 맞은 듯 쓰러졌고, 어머니의 머리가 내 이마를 들이받아 한두 순간 나를 어지럽게 했다."], ["The room and all round seemed to spin round.", "방과 그 주위 온 사방이 빙빙 도는 것 같았다."], ["I kept my eyes fixed on the window, but the wolf drew his head back, and a whole myriad of little specks seemed to come blowing in through the broken window, and wheeling and circling round like the pillar of dust that travellers describe when there is a simoon in the desert.", "나는 창문에 눈을 못박고 있었으나, 늑대는 머리를 빼냈고, 무수히 많은 작은 티끌들이 깨진 창문으로 불려 들어와, 마치 사막에 모래폭풍이 일 때 여행자들이 묘사하는 그 먼지 기둥처럼 빙글빙글 맴도는 것 같았다."], ["I tried to stir, but there was some spell upon me, and dear mother’s poor body, which seemed to grow cold already--for her dear heart had ceased to beat--weighed me down; and I remembered no more for a while.", "나는 몸을 움직이려 애썼으나, 무언가 주문에 걸린 듯했고, 사랑하는 어머니의 가엾은 몸이 — 이미 차갑게 식어가는 듯한, 그 다정한 심장이 멎어버린 그 몸이 — 나를 짓눌렀다. 그러고는 한동안 아무것도 기억나지 않았다."]], [["The time did not seem long, but very, very awful, till I recovered consciousness again.", "다시 의식을 되찾기까지 시간은 길게 느껴지지는 않았으나, 매우, 매우 끔찍했다."], ["Somewhere near, a passing bell was tolling; the dogs all round the neighbourhood were howling; and in our shrubbery, seemingly just outside, a nightingale was singing.", "어딘가 가까이에서 조종(弔鐘)이 울리고 있었고, 동네 사방의 개들이 모두 울부짖고 있었다. 그리고 우리 관목숲에서, 바로 바깥인 듯한 곳에서 나이팅게일 한 마리가 노래하고 있었다."], ["I was dazed and stupid with pain and terror and weakness, but the sound of the nightingale seemed like the voice of my dead mother come back to comfort me.", "나는 고통과 공포와 쇠약함으로 멍하고 얼떨떨했으나, 그 나이팅게일의 소리는 마치 돌아가신 어머니가 나를 위로하러 돌아온 목소리 같았다."], ["The sounds seemed to have awakened the maids, too, for I could hear their bare feet pattering outside my door.", "그 소리는 하녀들도 깨운 모양이었으니, 그들의 맨발이 내 방 문밖에서 후닥닥거리는 소리가 들렸기 때문이다."], ["I called to them, and they came in, and when they saw what had happened, and what it was that lay over me on the bed, they screamed out.", "나는 그들을 불렀고, 그들이 들어왔다. 그리고 무슨 일이 일어났는지, 침대 위에서 나를 짓누르고 있는 것이 무엇인지를 보자, 그들은 비명을 질렀다."], ["The wind rushed in through the broken window, and the door slammed to.", "깨진 창문으로 바람이 밀려들어왔고, 문이 쾅 닫혔다."], ["They lifted off the body of my dear mother, and laid her, covered up with a sheet, on the bed after I had got up.", "내가 일어나자 그들은 사랑하는 어머니의 시신을 들어 올려, 침대 위에 홑이불로 덮어 뉘었다."], ["They were all so frightened and nervous that I directed them to go to the dining-room and have each a glass of wine.", "그들이 모두 어찌나 겁먹고 신경이 곤두서 있던지, 나는 그들에게 식당으로 가서 각자 포도주를 한 잔씩 마시라고 일렀다."], ["The door flew open for an instant and closed again.", "문이 한순간 벌컥 열렸다가 다시 닫혔다."], ["The maids shrieked, and then went in a body to the dining-room; and I laid what flowers I had on my dear mother’s breast.", "하녀들은 비명을 지르더니, 무리지어 식당으로 갔다. 그리고 나는 가지고 있던 꽃들을 사랑하는 어머니의 가슴 위에 놓아드렸다."], ["When they were there I remembered what Dr. Van Helsing had told me, but I didn’t like to remove them, and, besides, I would have some of the servants to sit up with me now.", "그것들이 거기 놓이자 나는 판 헬싱 박사가 나에게 했던 말이 떠올랐으나, 그것들을 치우고 싶지 않았다. 게다가 이제 하인 몇몇에게 나와 함께 밤을 새우게 할 참이었으니까."], ["I was surprised that the maids did not come back.", "나는 하녀들이 돌아오지 않는 것이 의아했다."], ["I called them, but got no answer, so I went to the dining-room to look for them.", "그들을 불렀으나 대답이 없어서, 나는 그들을 찾으러 식당으로 갔다."]], [["My heart sank when I saw what had happened.", "무슨 일이 일어났는지 보았을 때 내 가슴은 철렁 내려앉았다."], ["They all four lay helpless on the floor, breathing heavily.", "그들 넷이 모두 바닥에 맥없이 쓰러져 거칠게 숨을 쉬고 있었다."], ["The decanter of sherry was on the table half full, but there was a queer, acrid smell about.", "셰리주 유리병이 탁자 위에 반쯤 차 있었으나, 묘하고 매캐한 냄새가 감돌았다."], ["I was suspicious, and examined the decanter.", "나는 의심이 들어 그 유리병을 살폈다."], ["It smelt of laudanum, and looking on the sideboard, I found that the bottle which mother’s doctor uses for her--oh! did use--was empty.", "거기서는 아편팅크 냄새가 났고, 찬장을 보니 어머니의 주치의가 어머니에게 — 오! 쓰던 — 아니, 썼던 그 병이 비어 있었다."], ["What am I to do?", "나는 어떻게 해야 하나?"], ["what am I to do?", "어떻게 해야 하나?"], ["I am back in the room with mother.", "나는 어머니가 있는 방으로 돌아와 있다."], ["I cannot leave her, and I am alone, save for the sleeping servants, whom some one has drugged.", "나는 어머니 곁을 떠날 수 없고, 누군가 약을 먹여놓은 잠든 하인들 말고는 혼자다."], ["Alone with the dead!", "죽은 이와 단둘이!"], ["I dare not go out, for I can hear the low howl of the wolf through the broken window.", "나는 밖으로 나갈 엄두가 나지 않으니, 깨진 창문 너머로 늑대의 나직한 울부짖음이 들리기 때문이다."]], [["The air seems full of specks, floating and circling in the draught from the window, and the lights burn blue and dim.", "공기는 온통 티끌로 가득한 듯, 창문에서 들어오는 외풍에 떠다니며 맴돌고, 등불은 푸르게 흐릿하게 타오른다."], ["What am I to do?", "나는 어떻게 해야 하나?"], ["God shield me from harm this night!", "하느님, 이 밤 저를 해악으로부터 지켜주소서!"], ["I shall hide this paper in my breast, where they shall find it when they come to lay me out.", "나는 이 종이를 내 가슴속에 숨겨두겠다. 그들이 나를 염하러 올 때 거기서 이것을 발견하도록."], ["My dear mother gone!", "사랑하는 어머니가 가셨다!"], ["It is time that I go too.", "이제 나도 갈 때다."], ["Good-bye, dear Arthur, if I should not survive this night. God keep you, dear, and God help me!", "안녕히, 사랑하는 아서, 혹시 내가 이 밤을 넘기지 못한다면. 하느님이 당신을 지켜주시기를, 사랑하는 이여, 그리고 하느님이 나를 도와주시기를!"]], [["# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY", "# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY"]], [["18 September.--I drove at once to Hillingham and arrived early.", "9월 18일. — 나는 곧장 마차를 몰아 힐링엄으로 가서 일찍 도착했다."], ["Keeping my cab at the gate, I went up the avenue alone.", "마차는 대문에 세워둔 채 나 혼자 길을 걸어 올라갔다."], ["I knocked gently and rang as quietly as possible, for I feared to disturb Lucy or her mother, and hoped to only bring a servant to the door.", "루시나 그녀의 어머니를 깨울까 두려워, 그저 하인 하나가 문으로 나오기를 바라며 가만히 문을 두드리고 되도록 조용히 초인종을 울렸다."], ["After a while, finding no response, I knocked and rang again; still no answer.", "한참이 지나도 아무 응답이 없기에 다시 두드리고 울렸으나, 여전히 대답이 없었다."], ["I cursed the laziness of the servants that they should lie abed at such an hour--for it was now ten o’clock--and so rang and knocked again, but more impatiently, but still without response.", "이런 시각에 — 그때는 벌써 열 시였다 — 자리에 누워 있다니, 나는 하인들의 게으름을 욕하면서 다시 더 조바심을 내며 울리고 두드렸으나, 그래도 응답이 없었다."], ["Hitherto I had blamed only the servants, but now a terrible fear began to assail me.", "그때까지는 하인들만 탓했으나, 이제 끔찍한 두려움이 나를 엄습하기 시작했다."], ["Was this desolation but another link in the chain of doom which seemed drawing tight around us?", "이 적막은 우리를 점점 옥죄어오는 듯한 운명의 사슬에서 또 하나의 고리일 뿐인가?"], ["Was it indeed a house of death to which I had come, too late?", "내가 너무 늦게 찾아온, 정녕 죽음의 집이란 말인가?"], ["I knew that minutes, even seconds of delay, might mean hours of danger to Lucy, if she had had again one of those frightful relapses; and I went round the house to try if I could find by chance an entry anywhere.", "만일 루시가 또 그 끔찍한 발작 가운데 하나를 일으켰다면, 몇 분, 아니 몇 초의 지체조차 그녀에게는 몇 시간의 위험을 뜻할 수 있음을 나는 알고 있었다. 그래서 나는 어디든 우연히라도 들어갈 곳이 있나 찾아보려고 집을 한 바퀴 돌았다."]], [["I could find no means of ingress.", "들어갈 방도를 찾을 수 없었다."], ["Every window and door was fastened and locked, and I returned baffled to the porch.", "창문과 문은 모조리 걸쇠가 걸리고 잠겨 있었으므로, 나는 어쩔 도리 없이 현관으로 돌아왔다."], ["As I did so, I heard the rapid pit-pat of a swiftly driven horse’s feet.", "그렇게 돌아오는데, 빠르게 몰아치는 말발굽의 다급한 또각또각 소리가 들렸다."], ["They stopped at the gate, and a few seconds later I met Van Helsing running up the avenue.", "그것은 대문에서 멈추었고, 몇 초 뒤 나는 길을 달려 올라오는 반 헬싱과 마주쳤다."], ["When he saw me, he gasped out:--", "그는 나를 보자 숨을 헐떡이며 외쳤다."]], [["“Then it was you, and just arrived.", "\"그럼 자네였군, 그것도 방금 도착한 거고."], ["How is she?", "그녀는 어떤가?"], ["Are we too late? Did you not get my telegram?”", "우리가 너무 늦은 건가? 내 전보를 받지 못했나?\""]], [["I answered as quickly and coherently as I could that I had only got his telegram early in the morning, and had not lost a minute in coming here, and that I could not make any one in the house hear me.", "나는 되도록 빠르고 또박또박, 그의 전보를 오늘 아침 일찍에야 받았으며 이리로 오는 데 한순간도 허비하지 않았노라고, 그리고 집 안의 누구도 내 기척을 듣게 할 수 없었노라고 대답했다."], ["He paused and raised his hat as he said solemnly:--", "그는 잠시 멈춰 서서 모자를 들어 올리며 엄숙히 말했다."]], [["“Then I fear we are too late.", "\"그렇다면 우리가 너무 늦은 모양일세."], ["God’s will be done!”", "신의 뜻이 이루어지기를!\""], ["With his usual recuperative energy, he went on: “Come.", "그러고는 늘 그렇듯 다시 기운을 되찾으며 말을 이었다."], ["If there be no way open to get in, we must make one.", "\"자, 들어갈 길이 트여 있지 않다면, 우리가 하나 뚫어야지."], ["Time is all in all to us now.”", "지금 우리에게는 시간이 전부일세.\""]], [["We went round to the back of the house, where there was a kitchen window.", "우리는 집 뒤편으로 돌아갔는데, 거기에 부엌 창문이 하나 있었다."], ["The Professor took a small surgical saw from his case, and handing it to me, pointed to the iron bars which guarded the window.", "교수는 자기 가방에서 작은 외과용 톱을 꺼내 나에게 건네주며, 창문을 막고 있는 쇠창살을 가리켰다."], ["I attacked them at once and had very soon cut through three of them.", "나는 곧장 거기에 달려들어 이내 세 개를 끊어냈다."], ["Then with a long, thin knife we pushed back the fastening of the sashes and opened the window.", "그런 다음 길고 가느다란 칼로 창틀의 걸쇠를 밀어 젖히고 창문을 열었다."], ["I helped the Professor in, and followed him. There was no one in the kitchen or in the servants’ rooms, which were close at hand.", "나는 교수가 들어가는 것을 돕고 나서 그를 따라 들어갔다. 부엌에도, 바로 곁에 붙은 하인 방에도 아무도 없었다."], ["We tried all the rooms as we went along, and in the dining-room, dimly lit by rays of light through the shutters, found four servant-women lying on the floor.", "우리는 지나가는 길에 방마다 다 열어보았는데, 덧문 틈으로 새어드는 빛에 어슴푸레한 식당에서, 하녀 넷이 바닥에 쓰러져 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["There was no need to think them dead, for their stertorous breathing and the acrid smell of laudanum in the room left no doubt as to their condition.", "그들을 죽었다고 여길 필요는 없었으니, 그르렁대는 숨소리와 방 안에 진동하는 아편 팅크의 매캐한 냄새가 그들의 상태에 대해 한 점 의심도 남기지 않았던 까닭이다."], ["Van Helsing and I looked at each other, and as we moved away he said: “We can attend to them later.”", "반 헬싱과 나는 서로를 마주보았고, 자리를 뜨면서 그가 말했다. \"저들은 나중에 돌볼 수 있네.\""], ["Then we ascended to Lucy’s room.", "그러고 나서 우리는 루시의 방으로 올라갔다."], ["For an instant or two we paused at the door to listen, but there was no sound that we could hear.", "우리는 한두 순간 문 앞에 멈춰 서서 귀를 기울였으나, 들리는 소리는 아무것도 없었다."], ["With white faces and trembling hands, we opened the door gently, and entered the room.", "핏기 잃은 얼굴과 떨리는 손으로 우리는 가만히 문을 열고 방 안에 들어섰다."]], [["How shall I describe what we saw?", "우리가 본 것을 어떻게 그려낼 수 있을까?"], ["On the bed lay two women, Lucy and her mother. The latter lay farthest in, and she was covered with a white sheet, the edge of which had been blown back by the draught through the broken window, showing the drawn, white face, with a look of terror fixed upon it.", "침대에는 두 여인이 누워 있었으니, 루시와 그녀의 어머니였다. 어머니가 가장 안쪽에 누워 있었고, 흰 시트를 덮고 있었는데, 그 가장자리가 깨진 창문으로 들이친 바람에 젖혀져, 공포의 빛이 못 박힌 듯 굳어버린 핼쑥한 흰 얼굴이 드러나 있었다."], ["By her side lay Lucy, with face white and still more drawn.", "그 곁에는 루시가 누워 있었으니, 얼굴은 핏기가 없고 더욱더 핼쑥했다."], ["The flowers which had been round her neck we found upon her mother’s bosom, and her throat was bare, showing the two little wounds which we had noticed before, but looking horribly white and mangled.", "그녀의 목에 둘러져 있던 꽃들은 어머니의 가슴 위에서 발견되었고, 그녀의 목은 드러나 있어 우리가 전에 눈여겨본 그 작은 상처 두 개를 보여주었으나, 끔찍하리만치 희고 너덜너덜해 보였다."], ["Without a word the Professor bent over the bed, his head almost touching poor Lucy’s breast; then he gave a quick turn of his head, as of one who listens, and leaping to his feet, he cried out to me:--", "교수는 말없이 침대 위로 몸을 숙여, 그의 머리가 가엾은 루시의 가슴에 거의 닿을 듯했다. 그러더니 무언가에 귀 기울이는 사람처럼 재빨리 고개를 돌렸고, 벌떡 몸을 일으키며 나에게 외쳤다."]], [["“It is not yet too late! Quick! quick!", "\"아직 너무 늦지는 않았네! 빨리! 빨리!"], ["Bring the brandy!”", "브랜디를 가져오게!\""]], [["I flew downstairs and returned with it, taking care to smell and taste it, lest it, too, were drugged like the decanter of sherry which I found on the table.", "나는 아래층으로 날듯이 내려가 그것을 들고 돌아왔는데, 탁자 위에서 발견한 셰리주 병처럼 이것에도 약이 타져 있을까 봐 냄새를 맡고 맛을 보는 일을 잊지 않았다."], ["The maids were still breathing, but more restlessly, and I fancied that the narcotic was wearing off.", "하녀들은 여전히 숨을 쉬고 있었으나 좀 더 뒤척였고, 나는 마취제 기운이 가시고 있는 것이라 짐작했다."], ["I did not stay to make sure, but returned to Van Helsing.", "나는 확인하느라 머물지 않고 반 헬싱에게 돌아갔다."], ["He rubbed the brandy, as on another occasion, on her lips and gums and on her wrists and the palms of her hands.", "그는 지난번에 했던 것처럼 그녀의 입술과 잇몸에, 그리고 손목과 손바닥에 브랜디를 문질렀다."], ["He said to me:--", "그는 나에게 말했다."]], [["“I can do this, all that can be at the present.", "\"이건 내가 하겠네, 지금으로선 할 수 있는 전부지."], ["You go wake those maids.", "자넨 가서 저 하녀들을 깨우게."], ["Flick them in the face with a wet towel, and flick them hard. Make them get heat and fire and a warm bath.", "젖은 수건으로 얼굴을 후려치게, 그것도 세게. 저들에게 열을 내고 불을 지펴 따뜻한 목욕물을 마련하게 하게."], ["This poor soul is nearly as cold as that beside her.", "이 가엾은 사람이 곁에 누운 저 사람만큼이나 차네."], ["She will need be heated before we can do anything more.”", "우리가 더 손을 쓰기 전에 우선 몸을 데워야 할 걸세.\""]], [["I went at once, and found little difficulty in waking three of the women.", "나는 곧장 가서, 별 어려움 없이 여인 셋을 깨웠다."], ["The fourth was only a young girl, and the drug had evidently affected her more strongly, so I lifted her on the sofa and let her sleep.", "넷째는 그저 어린 처녀였는데, 약기운이 그녀에게는 더 세게 들었던 게 분명하여, 나는 그녀를 소파 위로 들어 올려 잠을 자게 두었다."], ["The others were dazed at first, but as remembrance came back to them they cried and sobbed in a hysterical manner.", "다른 이들은 처음에는 멍해 있었으나, 기억이 되살아나면서 발작하듯 울고 흐느꼈다."], ["I was stern with them, however, and would not let them talk.", "그러나 나는 그들에게 단호히 대하여 말을 하게 두지 않았다."], ["I told them that one life was bad enough to lose, and that if they delayed they would sacrifice Miss Lucy.", "나는 한 목숨을 잃는 것만으로도 충분히 끔찍한데, 그들이 꾸물거리면 루시 아가씨를 희생시키게 될 거라고 일렀다."], ["So, sobbing and crying, they went about their way, half clad as they were, and prepared fire and water.", "그리하여 그들은 흐느끼고 울면서, 반쯤 걸친 옷차림 그대로 제 할 일을 하러 가, 불을 지피고 물을 데웠다."], ["Fortunately, the kitchen and boiler fires were still alive, and there was no lack of hot water.", "다행히 부엌과 보일러의 불은 아직 살아 있었고, 더운물도 모자라지 않았다."], ["We got a bath and carried Lucy out as she was and placed her in it.", "우리는 욕조를 마련해 루시를 있는 그대로 안아 내려다 그 안에 뉘었다."], ["Whilst we were busy chafing her limbs there was a knock at the hall door.", "우리가 그녀의 팔다리를 문질러 데우느라 분주할 때, 현관문에서 노크 소리가 났다."], ["One of the maids ran off, hurried on some more clothes, and opened it.", "하녀 하나가 달려 나가, 옷을 좀 더 걸치고는 문을 열었다."], ["Then she returned and whispered to us that there was a gentleman who had come with a message from Mr.", "그러고는 돌아와, 홈우드 씨가 전한 말을 가지고 온 신사가 있다고 우리에게 속삭였다."], ["Holmwood. I bade her simply tell him that he must wait, for we could see no one now.", "나는 그저, 지금은 아무도 만날 수 없으니 기다려야 한다고 그에게 전하라고 일렀다."], ["She went away with the message, and, engrossed with our work, I clean forgot all about him.", "그녀는 그 말을 들고 갔고, 나는 우리 일에 골몰하느라 그에 대해서는 깨끗이 잊어버렸다."]], [["I never saw in all my experience the Professor work in such deadly earnest.", "내 모든 경험을 통틀어, 나는 교수가 그토록 필사적으로 진지하게 일하는 것을 본 적이 없었다."], ["I knew--as he knew--that it was a stand-up fight with death, and in a pause told him so.", "나는 — 그도 알았듯이 — 그것이 죽음과 벌이는 정면 대결임을 알았고, 잠시 손을 멈춘 사이에 그에게 그렇게 말했다."], ["He answered me in a way that I did not understand, but with the sternest look that his face could wear:--", "그는 내가 알아듣지 못할 투로, 그러나 그의 얼굴이 지을 수 있는 가장 준엄한 표정으로 대답했다."]], [["“If that were all, I would stop here where we are now, and let her fade away into peace, for I see no light in life over her horizon.”", "\"그것이 전부라면, 나는 지금 우리가 선 이 자리에서 멈추고, 그녀가 평온 속으로 스러져가도록 내버려 두겠네. 그녀의 지평선 너머 삶에는 아무 빛도 보이지 않으니 말일세.\""], ["He went on with his work with, if possible, renewed and more frenzied vigour.", "그는, 가능하다면, 더욱 새로워지고 더욱 미친 듯한 기세로 일을 계속해 나갔다."]], [["Presently we both began to be conscious that the heat was beginning to be of some effect.", "이윽고 우리는 둘 다 그 열기가 어느 정도 효력을 내기 시작했음을 의식하게 되었다."], ["Lucy’s heart beat a trifle more audibly to the stethoscope, and her lungs had a perceptible movement.", "청진기에 잡히는 루시의 심장 박동이 조금 더 또렷이 들렸고, 그녀의 폐도 알아챌 만큼 움직였다."], ["Van Helsing’s face almost beamed, and as we lifted her from the bath and rolled her in a hot sheet to dry her he said to me:--", "반 헬싱의 얼굴은 거의 환히 빛났고, 우리가 그녀를 욕조에서 들어 올려 물기를 닦으려 더운 시트로 감쌀 때 그가 나에게 말했다."]], [["“The first gain is ours! Check to the King!”", "\"첫 번째 승리는 우리 것일세! 왕에게 장군!\""]], [["We took Lucy into another room, which had by now been prepared, and laid her in bed and forced a few drops of brandy down her throat.", "우리는 루시를 그동안 채비를 마쳐둔 다른 방으로 옮겨 침대에 뉘고, 브랜디 몇 방울을 그녀의 목구멍에 흘려넣었다."], ["I noticed that Van Helsing tied a soft silk handkerchief round her throat.", "나는 반 헬싱이 부드러운 비단 손수건을 그녀의 목에 둘러 매는 것을 눈여겨보았다."], ["She was still unconscious, and was quite as bad as, if not worse than, we had ever seen her.", "그녀는 여전히 의식이 없었고, 우리가 일찍이 본 적 없을 만큼 나빠진 상태였다. 아니, 더 나빠졌다고는 못 해도 그에 못지않았다."]], [["Van Helsing called in one of the women, and told her to stay with her and not to take her eyes off her till we returned, and then beckoned me out of the room.", "반 헬싱은 여인 하나를 불러들여, 우리가 돌아올 때까지 그녀 곁에 있으면서 잠시도 눈을 떼지 말라고 일렀다. 그러고는 나에게 방에서 나가자고 손짓했다."]], [["“We must consult as to what is to be done,”", "\"어떻게 해야 할지 의논해야 하네.\""], ["he said as we descended the stairs.", "우리가 계단을 내려가면서 그가 말했다."], ["In the hall he opened the dining-room door, and we passed in, he closing the door carefully behind him.", "현관에서 그는 식당 문을 열었고, 우리가 안으로 들어서자 그는 등 뒤로 문을 조심스레 닫았다."], ["The shutters had been opened, but the blinds were already down, with that obedience to the etiquette of death which the British woman of the lower classes always rigidly observes.", "덧문은 열려 있었으나, 영국의 하층 계급 여인들이 언제나 엄격히 지키는 그 죽음의 예법에 따라 차양은 이미 내려져 있었다."], ["The room was, therefore, dimly dark.", "그래서 방은 어슴푸레 어두웠다."], ["It was, however, light enough for our purposes.", "그러나 우리 목적에는 충분히 밝았다."], ["Van Helsing’s sternness was somewhat relieved by a look of perplexity.", "반 헬싱의 준엄함은 당혹스러운 기색으로 다소 누그러져 있었다."], ["He was evidently torturing his mind about something, so I waited for an instant, and he spoke:--", "그는 분명 무언가에 대해 마음을 졸이며 괴로워하고 있었으므로, 나는 잠시 기다렸고, 그가 입을 열었다."]], [["“What are we to do now?", "\"이제 우리가 무엇을 해야 하나?"], ["Where are we to turn for help?", "어디로 도움을 구하러 가야 하나?"], ["We must have another transfusion of blood, and that soon, or that poor girl’s life won’t be worth an hour’s purchase.", "우리는 또 한 번 수혈을 해야 하네, 그것도 곧, 그러지 않으면 저 가엾은 처녀의 목숨은 한 시간어치 값도 못 될 걸세."], ["You are exhausted already; I am exhausted too.", "자네는 이미 기진맥진했고, 나 또한 기진맥진했네."], ["I fear to trust those women, even if they would have courage to submit.", "나는 저 여인들을 믿기가 두렵네, 설령 그들이 응할 용기가 있다 한들 말일세."], ["What are we to do for some one who will open his veins for her?”", "그녀를 위해 제 핏줄을 열어줄 누군가를 어찌 구한단 말인가?\""]], [["“What’s the matter with me, anyhow?”", "\"그건 그렇고, 나는 어떻소?\""]], [["The voice came from the sofa across the room, and its tones brought relief and joy to my heart, for they were those of Quincey Morris.", "그 목소리는 방 건너편 소파에서 들려왔는데, 그 음색은 내 가슴에 안도와 기쁨을 안겨주었으니, 그것이 퀸시 모리스의 목소리였던 까닭이다."], ["Van Helsing started angrily at the first sound, but his face softened and a glad look came into his eyes as I cried out: “Quincey Morris!” and rushed towards him with outstretched hands.", "그 첫 소리에 반 헬싱은 성난 듯 움찔했으나, 내가 \"퀸시 모리스!\" 하고 외치며 두 손을 내밀고 그에게 달려가자 그의 얼굴은 부드러워지고 두 눈에 반가운 빛이 돌았다."]], [["“What brought you here?”", "\"무슨 일로 여기 왔나?\""], ["I cried as our hands met.", "우리의 손이 맞잡히자 내가 외쳤다."]], [["“I guess Art is the cause.”", "\"아트 때문인 것 같네.\""]], [["He handed me a telegram:--", "그는 나에게 전보 한 통을 건넸다."]], [["“Have not heard from Seward for three days, and am terribly anxious. Cannot leave.", "\"사흘째 수어드에게서 소식이 없어 몹시 불안하오. 나는 떠날 수가 없소."], ["Father still in same condition.", "아버지는 여전히 같은 상태요."], ["Send me word how Lucy is.", "루시가 어떤지 소식을 전해주오."], ["Do not delay.--HOLMWOOD.”", "지체하지 마시오. — 홈우드.\""]], [["“I think I came just in the nick of time.", "\"내가 마침 딱 좋은 때에 온 것 같네."], ["You know you have only to tell me what to do.”", "알다시피 나에게 무엇을 하라고 말하기만 하면 되네.\""]], [["Van Helsing strode forward, and took his hand, looking him straight in the eyes as he said:--", "반 헬싱은 성큼 앞으로 나아가 그의 손을 잡고, 그의 두 눈을 똑바로 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“A brave man’s blood is the best thing on this earth when a woman is in trouble.", "\"여인이 곤경에 처했을 때, 용감한 사내의 피야말로 이 세상에서 가장 좋은 것이라네."], ["You’re a man and no mistake.", "자넨 틀림없는 사내일세."], ["Well, the devil may work against us for all he’s worth, but God sends us men when we want them.”", "자, 악마가 제아무리 기를 쓰고 우리에게 맞선다 해도, 신께서는 우리가 필요로 할 때 사내들을 보내주시는군.\""]], [["Once again we went through that ghastly operation.", "다시 한번 우리는 그 끔찍한 수술을 치렀다."], ["I have not the heart to go through with the details.", "나는 그 세세한 과정을 차마 다 적을 마음이 나지 않는다."], ["Lucy had got a terrible shock and it told on her more than before, for though plenty of blood went into her veins, her body did not respond to the treatment as well as on the other occasions.", "루시는 무서운 충격을 받았고 그것이 전보다 더 심하게 그녀에게 나타났으니, 많은 피가 그녀의 핏줄로 들어갔음에도 그녀의 몸은 다른 때만큼 잘 반응하지 않았던 까닭이다."], ["Her struggle back into life was something frightful to see and hear.", "다시 삶으로 돌아오려는 그녀의 안간힘은 보기에도 듣기에도 끔찍한 것이었다."], ["However, the action of both heart and lungs improved, and Van Helsing made a subcutaneous injection of morphia, as before, and with good effect.", "그러나 심장과 폐의 작용이 모두 나아졌고, 반 헬싱은 지난번처럼 모르핀을 피하주사했는데, 효과가 좋았다."], ["Her faint became a profound slumber.", "그녀의 혼절은 깊은 잠으로 바뀌었다."], ["The Professor watched whilst I went downstairs with Quincey Morris, and sent one of the maids to pay off one of the cabmen who were waiting.", "교수가 지켜보는 동안, 나는 퀸시 모리스와 함께 아래층으로 내려가, 기다리고 있던 마부 하나에게 삯을 치르라고 하녀 하나를 보냈다."], ["I left Quincey lying down after having a glass of wine, and told the cook to get ready a good breakfast.", "나는 퀸시에게 포도주 한 잔을 마시게 한 뒤 누워 있게 두고, 요리사에게 든든한 아침식사를 차려두라고 일렀다."], ["Then a thought struck me, and I went back to the room where Lucy now was.", "그러다가 문득 한 가지 생각이 떠올라, 나는 지금 루시가 있는 방으로 되돌아갔다."], ["When I came softly in, I found Van Helsing with a sheet or two of note-paper in his hand.", "내가 가만히 들어서니, 반 헬싱이 손에 편지지 한두 장을 들고 있는 것을 발견했다."], ["He had evidently read it, and was thinking it over as he sat with his hand to his brow.", "그는 분명 그것을 읽은 듯했고, 한 손을 이마에 짚고 앉아 그것을 곱씹어 생각하고 있었다."], ["There was a look of grim satisfaction in his face, as of one who has had a doubt solved.", "그의 얼굴에는 어떤 의혹이 풀린 사람처럼 엄숙한 만족의 빛이 어려 있었다."], ["He handed me the paper saying only: “It dropped from Lucy’s breast when we carried her to the bath.”", "그는 그 편지를 나에게 건네며 이렇게만 말했다. \"우리가 그녀를 욕조로 옮길 때 루시의 가슴에서 떨어진 걸세.\""]], [["When I had read it, I stood looking at the Professor, and after a pause asked him: “In God’s name, what does it all mean?", "내가 그것을 다 읽고 나서 교수를 바라보며 서 있다가, 잠시 후 그에게 물었다. \"도대체 이게 다 무슨 뜻입니까?"], ["Was she, or is she, mad; or what sort of horrible danger is it?”", "그녀가 미쳤던, 아니면 미친 겁니까? 아니면 대체 어떤 끔찍한 위험입니까?\""], ["I was so bewildered that I did not know what to say more.", "나는 너무도 어리둥절하여 더 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알 수 없었다."], ["Van Helsing put out his hand and took the paper, saying:--", "반 헬싱은 손을 내밀어 그 편지를 받으며 말했다."]], [["“Do not trouble about it now.", "\"지금은 그 일로 마음 쓰지 말게."], ["Forget it for the present.", "당분간은 잊어버리게."], ["You shall know and understand it all in good time; but it will be later.", "머지않아 자네는 그 모든 것을 알고 이해하게 될 걸세. 다만 그건 나중 일이지."], ["And now what is it that you came to me to say?”", "그건 그렇고, 자네가 나에게 하려던 말은 무엇이었나?\""], ["This brought me back to fact, and I was all myself again.", "이 말이 나를 현실로 돌아오게 했고, 나는 다시 온전히 내 자신이 되었다."]], [["“I came to speak about the certificate of death.", "\"사망 진단서에 관해 말씀드리려 왔습니다."], ["If we do not act properly and wisely, there may be an inquest, and that paper would have to be produced.", "우리가 적절하고 현명하게 처신하지 않으면 검시가 있을지도 모르고, 그러면 저 편지를 내놓아야만 할 겁니다."], ["I am in hopes that we need have no inquest, for if we had it would surely kill poor Lucy, if nothing else did.", "검시는 필요 없게 되기를 저는 바라고 있습니다. 만일 검시가 열린다면, 다른 게 안 죽인다 해도 그것만으로도 분명 가엾은 루시를 죽이고 말 테니까요."], ["I know, and you know, and the other doctor who attended her knows, that Mrs.", "저도 알고, 선생님도 아시고, 그녀를 진료한 다른 의사도 압니다."], ["Westenra had disease of the heart, and we can certify that she died of it.", "웨스튼라 부인에게 심장병이 있었다는 것을요. 그러니 우리는 그녀가 그 병으로 죽었다고 증명할 수 있습니다."], ["Let us fill up the certificate at once, and I shall take it myself to the registrar and go on to the undertaker.”", "당장 진단서를 작성합시다. 제가 직접 그것을 호적계로 가지고 가, 이어서 장의사에게도 들르겠습니다.\""]], [["“Good, oh my friend John!", "\"좋네, 오, 나의 벗 존이여!"], ["Well thought of!", "잘 생각했네!"], ["Truly Miss Lucy, if she be sad in the foes that beset her, is at least happy in the friends that love her.", "참으로 루시 아가씨는, 그녀를 에워싼 적들로 보면 비록 가엾을지언정, 적어도 그녀를 사랑하는 벗들로 보면 행복한 사람일세."], ["One, two, three, all open their veins for her, besides one old man.", "하나, 둘, 셋, 모두가 그녀를 위해 제 핏줄을 열었고, 게다가 늙은이 하나까지 더했지."], ["Ah yes, I know, friend John; I am not blind!", "아, 그렇네, 나는 아네, 벗 존이여. 나는 장님이 아닐세!"], ["I love you all the more for it! Now go.”", "그래서 나는 자네를 더욱 사랑하네! 자, 이제 가게.\""]], [["In the hall I met Quincey Morris, with a telegram for Arthur telling him that Mrs.", "현관에서 나는 퀸시 모리스와 마주쳤는데, 그는 아서에게 보낼 전보를 들고 있었다."], ["Westenra was dead; that Lucy also had been ill, but was now going on better; and that Van Helsing and I were with her.", "웨스튼라 부인이 죽었다는 것, 루시 또한 앓았으나 이제 차도가 있다는 것, 그리고 반 헬싱과 내가 그녀 곁에 있다는 내용이었다."], ["I told him where I was going, and he hurried me out, but as I was going said:--", "나는 그에게 내가 어디로 가는지 일렀고, 그는 나를 서둘러 내보냈으나, 내가 나서려는데 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“When you come back, Jack, may I have two words with you all to ourselves?”", "\"돌아오거든, 잭, 우리끼리만 두어 마디 나눌 수 있겠나?\""], ["I nodded in reply and went out. I found no difficulty about the registration, and arranged with the local undertaker to come up in the evening to measure for the coffin and to make arrangements.", "나는 고개를 끄덕여 답하고 나갔다. 호적 등록에는 별 어려움이 없었고, 동네 장의사와 저녁에 와서 관 치수를 재고 절차를 마련하기로 약속했다."]], [["When I got back Quincey was waiting for me.", "내가 돌아오니 퀸시가 나를 기다리고 있었다."], ["I told him I would see him as soon as I knew about Lucy, and went up to her room.", "나는 그에게, 루시에 대해 알아본 뒤 곧 보러 오겠다고 일렀고, 그녀의 방으로 올라갔다."], ["She was still sleeping, and the Professor seemingly had not moved from his seat at her side.", "그녀는 여전히 잠들어 있었고, 교수는 보아하니 그녀 곁의 자리에서 꼼짝도 하지 않은 듯했다."], ["From his putting his finger to his lips, I gathered that he expected her to wake before long and was afraid of forestalling nature.", "그가 손가락을 입술에 갖다 대는 것으로 보아, 나는 그녀가 머지않아 깨어나리라 그가 짐작하고 있으며 자연의 순리를 앞당길까 두려워하는 것이라고 헤아렸다."], ["So I went down to Quincey and took him into the breakfast-room, where the blinds were not drawn down, and which was a little more cheerful, or rather less cheerless, than the other rooms.", "그래서 나는 퀸시에게 내려가, 그를 아침식사방으로 데리고 갔는데, 그곳은 차양이 내려져 있지 않아 다른 방들보다 조금 더 밝았다. 아니, 덜 음울했다고 하는 편이 낫겠다."], ["When we were alone, he said to me:--", "우리끼리만 있게 되자 그가 나에게 말했다."]], [["“Jack Seward, I don’t want to shove myself in anywhere where I’ve no right to be; but this is no ordinary case.", "\"잭 수어드, 내가 끼어들 자격도 없는 곳에 함부로 나서고 싶진 않네. 하지만 이건 예사로운 경우가 아니야."], ["You know I loved that girl and wanted to marry her; but, although that’s all past and gone, I can’t help feeling anxious about her all the same.", "자네도 알다시피 나는 저 처녀를 사랑했고 그녀와 결혼하고 싶었네. 비록 그건 이제 다 지나간 일이지만, 그래도 나는 그녀가 걱정스러운 것을 어쩔 수 없네."], ["What is it that’s wrong with her?", "대체 그녀에게 무슨 문제가 있는 건가?"], ["The Dutchman--and a fine old fellow he is; I can see that--said, that time you two came into the room, that you must have another transfusion of blood, and that both you and he were exhausted.", "저 네덜란드 양반은 — 훌륭한 노신사야, 나도 그건 알 수 있네 — 자네 둘이 방에 들어왔을 때, 또 한 번 수혈을 해야 한다고, 그리고 자네와 그 양반 둘 다 기진맥진했다고 말했네."], ["Now I know well that you medical men speak in camera, and that a man must not expect to know what they consult about in private.", "자, 나도 자네들 의사 양반들이 은밀히 이야기를 나누고, 사사로이 의논하는 바를 남이 알려 들어선 안 된다는 걸 잘 아네."], ["But this is no common matter, and, whatever it is, I have done my part.", "하지만 이건 예사로운 일이 아니고, 그것이 무엇이든 간에 나도 내 몫을 다했네."], ["Is not that so?”", "그렇지 않은가?\""]], [["“That’s so,”", "\"그렇네.\""], ["I said, and he went on:--", "내가 말하자 그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“I take it that both you and Van Helsing had done already what I did to-day.", "\"내 생각에 자네와 반 헬싱도 오늘 내가 한 것을 이미 했을 것 같네."], ["Is not that so?”", "그렇지 않은가?\""]], [["“That’s so.”", "\"그렇네.\""]], [["“And I guess Art was in it too. When I saw him four days ago down at his own place he looked queer.", "\"그리고 아트도 거기 끼어 있었던 것 같네. 나흘 전 그의 집에서 그를 봤을 때 그가 이상해 보이더군."], ["I have not seen anything pulled down so quick since I was on the Pampas and had a mare that I was fond of go to grass all in a night.", "내가 팜파스에 있을 때, 아끼던 암말 한 마리가 하룻밤 새에 풀밭에 쓰러진 일이 있은 뒤로, 무엇이 그렇게 빨리 쇠하는 걸 본 적이 없네."], ["One of those big bats that they call vampires had got at her in the night, and what with his gorge and the vein left open, there wasn’t enough blood in her to let her stand up, and I had to put a bullet through her as she lay.", "흡혈박쥐라 부르는 그 커다란 박쥐 가운데 하나가 밤에 그 말에게 달려들었는데, 놈이 실컷 빨아먹은 데다 핏줄이 열린 채로 남겨졌으니, 말은 일어설 만한 피도 남지 않아, 나는 누워 있는 그 말의 몸에 총알을 박아 넣어야 했네."], ["Jack, if you may tell me without betraying confidence, Arthur was the first, is not that so?”", "잭, 비밀을 저버리지 않고 말할 수 있다면 말해주게, 아서가 처음이었지, 그렇지 않은가?\""], ["As he spoke the poor fellow looked terribly anxious.", "그렇게 말하는 동안 그 가엾은 친구는 몹시 불안해 보였다."], ["He was in a torture of suspense regarding the woman he loved, and his utter ignorance of the terrible mystery which seemed to surround her intensified his pain.", "그는 자기가 사랑하는 여인에 대한 마음 졸이는 초조함에 시달리고 있었고, 그녀를 에워싼 듯한 그 무서운 수수께끼를 도무지 알지 못하는 것이 그의 고통을 더욱 깊게 했다."], ["His very heart was bleeding, and it took all the manhood of him--and there was a royal lot of it, too--to keep him from breaking down.", "그의 가슴은 진정 피 흘리고 있었고, 무너져 내리지 않으려고 버티는 데에는 그의 사내다움이 — 그것도 왕에 견줄 만큼 넘치는 사내다움이 — 죄다 필요했다. 나는 대답하기 전에 잠시 망설였으니, 교수가 비밀로 간직하고자 하는 어떤 것도 누설해서는 안 된다고 느꼈던 까닭이다."], ["I paused before answering, for I felt that I must not betray anything which the Professor wished kept secret; but already he knew so much, and guessed so much, that there could be no reason for not answering, so I answered in the same phrase: “That’s so.”", "그러나 그는 이미 너무 많은 것을 알았고 너무 많은 것을 짐작하고 있었으므로, 대답하지 않을 이유가 없었다. 그래서 나는 같은 말로 대답했다. \"그렇네.\""]], [["“And how long has this been going on?”", "\"그래서 이게 얼마나 오래 계속된 건가?\""]], [["“About ten days.”", "\"열흘쯤.\""]], [["“Ten days!", "\"열흘이라!"], ["Then I guess, Jack Seward, that that poor pretty creature that we all love has had put into her veins within that time the blood of four strong men.", "그렇다면 내 생각에, 잭 수어드, 우리 모두가 사랑하는 저 가엾고 어여쁜 사람이 그동안 제 핏줄에 건장한 사내 넷의 피를 받았다는 말이로군."], ["Man alive, her whole body wouldn’t hold it.”", "이 사람아, 그녀의 온몸으로도 그걸 다 담지 못할 텐데.\""], ["Then, coming close to me, he spoke in a fierce half-whisper: “What took it out?”", "그러더니 그는 나에게 바짝 다가와 사납게 반쯤 속삭이는 투로 말했다. \"무엇이 그걸 빼내간 건가?\""]], [["I shook my head.", "나는 고개를 저었다."], ["“That,”", "\"그것이,\""], ["I said, “is the crux.", "내가 말했다. \"바로 핵심일세."], ["Van Helsing is simply frantic about it, and I am at my wits’ end.", "반 헬싱은 그 일로 그저 미칠 지경이고, 나는 어찌할 바를 모르겠네."], ["I can’t even hazard a guess.", "짐작조차 해볼 수가 없네."], ["There has been a series of little circumstances which have thrown out all our calculations as to Lucy being properly watched.", "루시를 제대로 지켜야 한다는 우리의 온갖 계산을 어그러뜨린 자잘한 사정들이 잇따라 일어났지. 하지만 그런 일은 다시는 없을 걸세."], ["But these shall not occur again. Here we stay until all be well--or ill.”", "우리는 모든 게 잘되든 — 잘못되든, 결판이 날 때까지 여기 머물 거네.\""], ["Quincey held out his hand.", "퀸시가 손을 내밀었다."], ["“Count me in,”", "\"나도 끼워주게.\""], ["he said. “You and the Dutchman will tell me what to do, and I’ll do it.”", "그가 말했다. \"자네와 저 네덜란드 양반이 무엇을 하라 일러주면, 나는 그것을 하겠네.\""]], [["When she woke late in the afternoon, Lucy’s first movement was to feel in her breast, and, to my surprise, produced the paper which Van Helsing had given me to read.", "오후 늦게 그녀가 깨어났을 때, 루시의 첫 동작은 가슴께를 더듬는 것이었는데, 놀랍게도 반 헬싱이 나에게 읽으라고 주었던 그 편지를 꺼내는 것이었다."], ["The careful Professor had replaced it where it had come from, lest on waking she should be alarmed.", "그 세심한 교수가, 깨어났을 때 그녀가 놀랄까 봐 그것을 본래 있던 자리에 도로 넣어두었던 것이다."], ["Her eye then lit on Van Helsing and on me too, and gladdened.", "이윽고 그녀의 눈길이 반 헬싱에게, 그리고 나에게도 닿더니 환해졌다."], ["Then she looked around the room, and seeing where she was, shuddered; she gave a loud cry, and put her poor thin hands before her pale face.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 방을 둘러보았고, 자기가 어디에 있는지 알아채고는 몸서리를 쳤다. 그녀는 크게 울부짖으며, 그 가엾고 야윈 두 손을 핏기 없는 얼굴 앞으로 가져갔다."], ["We both understood what that meant--that she had realised to the full her mother’s death; so we tried what we could to comfort her.", "우리 둘 다 그것이 무슨 뜻인지 알았으니 — 그녀가 어머니의 죽음을 온전히 깨달았다는 것이었다. 그래서 우리는 할 수 있는 한 그녀를 위로해보려 애썼다."], ["Doubtless sympathy eased her somewhat, but she was very low in thought and spirit, and wept silently and weakly for a long time.", "동정이 그녀를 다소 누그러뜨린 것은 분명했으나, 그녀는 생각도 기력도 몹시 가라앉아, 오래도록 소리 없이 가냘프게 울었다."], ["We told her that either or both of us would now remain with her all the time, and that seemed to comfort her.", "우리는 이제부터 우리 둘 중 하나, 혹은 둘 다 늘 그녀 곁에 머물 거라고 일렀고, 그것이 그녀를 위로하는 듯했다."], ["Towards dusk she fell into a doze.", "해 질 무렵 그녀는 깜빡 잠이 들었다."], ["Here a very odd thing occurred.", "여기서 매우 기이한 일이 일어났다."], ["Whilst still asleep she took the paper from her breast and tore it in two.", "그녀는 잠든 채로 가슴에서 그 편지를 꺼내 두 갈래로 찢었다."], ["Van Helsing stepped over and took the pieces from her.", "반 헬싱이 다가가 그녀에게서 그 조각들을 빼냈다."], ["All the same, however, she went on with the action of tearing, as though the material were still in her hands; finally she lifted her hands and opened them as though scattering the fragments.", "그래도 그녀는, 마치 그 종이가 여전히 손에 들려 있기라도 한 듯 찢는 동작을 계속했다. 마침내 그녀는 두 손을 들어 올려, 그 조각들을 흩뿌리기라도 하듯 펼쳤다."], ["Van Helsing seemed surprised, and his brows gathered as if in thought, but he said nothing.", "반 헬싱은 놀란 듯했고, 무언가 골똘히 생각하듯 미간을 모았으나, 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["19 September.--All last night she slept fitfully, being always afraid to sleep, and something weaker when she woke from it.", "9월 19일. — 지난밤 내내 그녀는 띄엄띄엄 잠을 잤는데, 늘 잠드는 것을 두려워했고, 잠에서 깰 때마다 다소 더 쇠약해져 있었다."], ["The Professor and I took it in turns to watch, and we never left her for a moment unattended.", "교수와 나는 번갈아 지켜보았으며, 우리는 한순간도 그녀 곁을 비워두지 않았다."], ["Quincey Morris said nothing about his intention, but I knew that all night long he patrolled round and round the house.", "퀸시 모리스는 자기 속내에 대해 아무 말도 하지 않았으나, 나는 그가 밤새도록 집 둘레를 돌고 또 돌며 순찰했음을 알았다."]], [["When the day came, its searching light showed the ravages in poor Lucy’s strength.", "날이 밝자, 그 샅샅이 비추는 빛이 가엾은 루시의 기력이 무너진 자취를 드러냈다."], ["She was hardly able to turn her head, and the little nourishment which she could take seemed to do her no good.", "그녀는 고개를 돌리는 것조차 거의 못 했고, 받아 넘길 수 있는 적은 양분도 그녀에게 아무 도움이 되지 못하는 듯했다."], ["At times she slept, and both Van Helsing and I noticed the difference in her, between sleeping and waking.", "이따금 그녀는 잠이 들었는데, 반 헬싱과 나는 둘 다 잠들었을 때와 깨어 있을 때 그녀의 차이를 눈여겨보았다."], ["Whilst asleep she looked stronger, although more haggard, and her breathing was softer; her open mouth showed the pale gums drawn back from the teeth, which thus looked positively longer and sharper than usual; when she woke the softness of her eyes evidently changed the expression, for she looked her own self, although a dying one.", "잠들었을 때 그녀는, 더 초췌해 보이긴 했어도 더 강해 보였고, 숨소리는 더 부드러웠다. 벌어진 입은 잇몸이 핏기 없이 이에서 물러난 것을 드러냈는데, 그래서 이는 평소보다 영락없이 더 길고 날카로워 보였다. 깨어났을 때는 눈매의 부드러움이 분명 그 표정을 바꾸어, 비록 죽어가는 모습일망정 그녀는 본래의 자기 자신으로 보였다."], ["In the afternoon she asked for Arthur, and we telegraphed for him.", "오후에 그녀가 아서를 찾기에, 우리는 그에게 전보를 쳤다."], ["Quincey went off to meet him at the station.", "퀸시가 정거장으로 그를 마중하러 갔다."]], [["When he arrived it was nearly six o’clock, and the sun was setting full and warm, and the red light streamed in through the window and gave more colour to the pale cheeks.", "그가 도착했을 때는 거의 여섯 시였고, 해가 충만하고 따스하게 지고 있었으며, 붉은 빛이 창문으로 흘러들어 핏기 없는 두 뺨에 좀 더 혈색을 입혀주었다."], ["When he saw her, Arthur was simply choking with emotion, and none of us could speak.", "그녀를 본 순간 아서는 그저 복받치는 감정에 목이 메었고, 우리 가운데 누구도 입을 떼지 못했다."], ["In the hours that had passed, the fits of sleep, or the comatose condition that passed for it, had grown more frequent, so that the pauses when conversation was possible were shortened.", "흘러간 그 몇 시간 사이에 잠의 발작이, 아니 잠처럼 보이는 혼수상태가 더 잦아져, 대화가 가능한 틈은 짧아졌다. 그러나 아서가 곁에 있는 것은 자극제처럼 작용하는 듯했다."], ["Arthur’s presence, however, seemed to act as a stimulant; she rallied a little, and spoke to him more brightly than she had done since we arrived.", "그녀는 조금 기운을 차려, 우리가 도착한 이래로 보인 적 없이 그에게 한층 밝게 말을 건넸다."], ["He too pulled himself together, and spoke as cheerily as he could, so that the best was made of everything.", "그 또한 마음을 다잡고 되도록 명랑하게 말했으므로, 모든 것에서 최선이 이끌어졌다."]], [["It was now nearly one o’clock, and he and Van Helsing are sitting with her.", "이제 거의 한 시였고, 그와 반 헬싱이 그녀 곁에 앉아 있다."], ["I am to relieve them in a quarter of an hour, and I am entering this on Lucy’s phonograph.", "나는 십오 분 뒤에 그들과 교대하기로 했고, 지금 이것을 루시의 축음기에 기록하고 있다. 여섯 시까지 그들은 쉬어보기로 했다."], ["Until six o’clock they are to try to rest.", "나는 내일이면 우리의 간호가 끝나지 않을까 두렵다."], ["I fear that to-morrow will end our watching, for the shock has been too great; the poor child cannot rally.", "충격이 너무도 컸기 때문이다. 가엾은 그 아이는 끝내 기운을 차리지 못한다. 신이여, 우리 모두를 도우소서."], ["God help us all.", "편지, 미나 하커가 루시 웨스튼라에게."]], [["(Unopened by her.)", "(그녀가 뜯어보지 못함.)"]], [["“17 September.", "\"9월 17일."]], [["“My dearest Lucy,--", "\"나의 가장 사랑하는 루시에게, —"]], [["“It seems an age since I heard from you, or indeed since I wrote.", "\"네 소식을 들은 지, 아니 사실 내가 편지를 쓴 지도 한 세월이 흐른 것만 같구나."], ["You will pardon me, I know, for all my faults when you have read all my budget of news.", "내 소식 보따리를 다 읽고 나면, 네가 내 모든 잘못을 용서해 주리라는 걸 나는 알아."], ["Well, I got my husband back all right; when we arrived at Exeter there was a carriage waiting for us, and in it, though he had an attack of gout, Mr. Hawkins.", "자, 나는 내 남편을 무사히 되찾았단다. 우리가 엑서터에 닿았을 때 마차 한 대가 우리를 기다리고 있었고, 그 안에는, 통풍 발작이 있으셨는데도, 호킨스 씨가 계셨어."], ["He took us to his house, where there were rooms for us all nice and comfortable, and we dined together.", "그분은 우리를 자신의 집으로 데려가셨는데, 그곳에는 우리 모두를 위한 좋고 편안한 방들이 마련되어 있었고, 우리는 함께 저녁을 들었지."], ["After dinner Mr. Hawkins said:--", "저녁식사 뒤에 호킨스 씨가 말씀하셨어. —"]], [["“‘My dears, I want to drink your health and prosperity; and may every blessing attend you both.", "\"'얘들아, 나는 너희의 건강과 번영을 위해 잔을 들고 싶구나. 부디 너희 둘에게 온갖 축복이 함께하기를."], ["I know you both from children, and have, with love and pride, seen you grow up.", "나는 너희 둘을 어릴 적부터 알았고, 사랑과 자랑으로 너희가 자라는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["Now I want you to make your home here with me.", "이제 나는 너희가 나와 함께 여기를 너희 보금자리로 삼기를 바란다."], ["I have left to me neither chick nor child; all are gone, and in my will I have left you everything.’ I cried, Lucy dear, as Jonathan and the old man clasped hands.", "나에게는 자식도 핏줄도 하나 남지 않았으니, 모두 떠나갔고, 나는 유언으로 너희에게 모든 것을 남겼다.' 조너선과 그 노인이 손을 맞잡을 때 나는 울었단다, 사랑하는 루시야."], ["Our evening was a very, very happy one.", "우리의 저녁은 참으로, 참으로 행복했어."]], [["“So here we are, installed in this beautiful old house, and from both my bedroom and the drawing-room I can see the great elms of the cathedral close, with their great black stems standing out against the old yellow stone of the cathedral and I can hear the rooks overhead cawing and cawing and chattering and gossiping all day, after the manner of rooks--and humans.", "\"그리하여 우리는 이 아름답고 오래된 집에 자리 잡게 되었고, 내 침실에서도 응접실에서도, 나는 대성당 안뜰의 커다란 느릅나무들을 볼 수 있어. 그 굵고 검은 줄기들이 대성당의 오래된 노란 돌을 배경으로 우뚝 서 있고, 머리 위에서는 띠까마귀들이 까악까악 울며 온종일 까마귀들의 — 그리고 사람들의 — 방식대로 떠들고 수다를 떠는 소리가 들려."], ["I am busy, I need not tell you, arranging things and housekeeping. Jonathan and Mr.", "굳이 말할 것도 없이, 나는 살림을 정리하고 집안을 꾸리느라 분주하단다."], ["Hawkins are busy all day; for, now that Jonathan is a partner, Mr. Hawkins wants to tell him all about the clients.", "조너선과 호킨스 씨는 온종일 바쁘셔. 이제 조너선이 동업자가 되었으니, 호킨스 씨가 그에게 의뢰인들에 관한 모든 것을 일러주고 싶어 하시거든."]], [["“How is your dear mother getting on?", "\"네 사랑하는 어머니는 어떻게 지내시니?"], ["I wish I could run up to town for a day or two to see you, dear, but I dare not go yet, with so much on my shoulders; and Jonathan wants looking after still.", "하루나 이틀이라도 시내로 달려 올라가 너를 보고 싶지만, 어깨에 진 일이 너무 많아 아직은 엄두를 못 내겠어. 그리고 조너선은 여전히 보살핌이 필요하단다."], ["He is beginning to put some flesh on his bones again, but he was terribly weakened by the long illness; even now he sometimes starts out of his sleep in a sudden way and awakes all trembling until I can coax him back to his usual placidity.", "그이는 다시 살이 좀 오르기 시작했지만, 그 긴 병으로 끔찍이 쇠약해졌어. 지금도 이따금 잠결에 갑자기 화들짝 놀라 깨어나서는, 내가 어르고 달래어 평소의 평온함으로 돌려놓을 때까지 온통 떨고 있단다."], ["However, thank God, these occasions grow less frequent as the days go on, and they will in time pass away altogether, I trust.", "그래도, 신께 감사하게도, 날이 갈수록 그런 일이 점점 뜸해지고 있으니, 머지않아 아주 사라지리라 나는 믿어."], ["And now I have told you my news, let me ask yours.", "자, 이제 내 소식을 다 전했으니, 네 소식을 물어볼게."], ["When are you to be married, and where, and who is to perform the ceremony, and what are you to wear, and is it to be a public or a private wedding?", "너는 언제 결혼하니, 어디서, 누가 식을 주례하고, 무엇을 입을 거니, 그리고 공개 결혼식이니 아니면 비공개 결혼식이니?"], ["Tell me all about it, dear; tell me all about everything, for there is nothing which interests you which will not be dear to me.", "다 이야기해 줘, 얘야. 무엇이든 다 이야기해 줘. 너에게 관심 있는 일이라면 나에게 소중하지 않을 게 하나도 없으니까."], ["Jonathan asks me to send his ‘respectful duty,’ but I do not think that is good enough from the junior partner of the important firm Hawkins & Harker; and so, as you love me, and he loves me, and I love you with all the moods and tenses of the verb, I send you simply his ‘love’ instead.", "조너선이 자기 '정중한 인사'를 전해 달라고 부탁하지만, 호킨스 앤드 하커라는 중요한 회사의 막내 동업자로서 그것만으로는 흡족하지 않은 것 같아. 그러니 네가 나를 사랑하고, 그이가 나를 사랑하고, 내가 그 동사의 온갖 시제와 어법으로 너를 사랑하니, 대신 나는 그저 그이의 '사랑'을 너에게 전한다."], ["Good-bye, my dearest Lucy, and all blessings on you.", "안녕, 나의 가장 사랑하는 루시야, 너에게 온갖 축복을 빈다."]], [["“Yours,", "\"너의,"]], [["Report from Patrick Hennessey, M.", "\"미나 하커.\""], ["D., M. R. C. S. L. K. Q. C. P. I., etc., etc., to John Seward, M. D.", "패트릭 헤너시 의학박사, 영국 왕립외과의사회·아일랜드 왕립의사회 회원 등등이 의학박사 존 수어드에게 보낸 보고서."]], [["“20 September.", "\"9월 20일."]], [["“My dear Sir,--", "\"친애하는 선생님께, —"]], [["“In accordance with your wishes, I enclose report of the conditions of everything left in my charge....", "\"선생님의 뜻에 따라, 제가 맡은 모든 것의 상황에 대한 보고서를 동봉합니다……"], ["With regard to patient, Renfield, there is more to say.", "환자 렌필드에 관해서는 더 말씀드릴 것이 있습니다."], ["He has had another outbreak, which might have had a dreadful ending, but which, as it fortunately happened, was unattended with any unhappy results.", "그는 또 한 번 발작을 일으켰는데, 끔찍한 결말로 치달을 수도 있었으나, 다행히도 그렇게 된 덕분에 어떤 불행한 결과도 따르지 않았습니다."], ["This afternoon a carrier’s cart with two men made a call at the empty house whose grounds abut on ours--the house to which, you will remember, the patient twice ran away.", "오늘 오후, 일꾼 둘이 탄 짐마차 한 대가, 그 부지가 우리 부지와 맞닿은 빈집에 — 기억하시겠지만, 환자가 두 번이나 달아났던 그 집입니다 — 들렀습니다."], ["The men stopped at our gate to ask the porter their way, as they were strangers.", "그 사람들은 이곳이 낯선 터라, 길을 묻고자 우리 대문 앞에 멈춰 수위에게 다가갔습니다."], ["I was myself looking out of the study window, having a smoke after dinner, and saw one of them come up to the house.", "저는 마침 저녁식사 뒤에 한 대 피우면서 서재 창밖을 내다보고 있었는데, 그 가운데 하나가 집 쪽으로 다가오는 것을 보았습니다."], ["As he passed the window of Renfield’s room, the patient began to rate him from within, and called him all the foul names he could lay his tongue to.", "그가 렌필드의 방 창문을 지나칠 때, 환자가 안에서 그를 욕하기 시작하여, 혀에 올릴 수 있는 온갖 더러운 욕설을 그에게 퍼부었습니다."], ["The man, who seemed a decent fellow enough, contented himself by telling him to “shut up for a foul-mouthed beggar,” whereon our man accused him of robbing him and wanting to murder him and said that he would hinder him if he were to swing for it.", "제법 점잖아 보이던 그 사람은 '입이나 다물어, 이 상스러운 거지 같은 놈아'라고 받아치는 것으로 그쳤는데, 그러자 우리 환자는 그가 자기 것을 빼앗고 자기를 죽이려 한다고 몰아세우며, 자기가 그 일로 교수형을 당하는 한이 있어도 그를 막겠다고 말했습니다."], ["I opened the window and signed to the man not to notice, so he contented himself after looking the place over and making up his mind as to what kind of a place he had got to by saying: ‘Lor’ bless yer, sir, I wouldn’t mind what was said to me in a bloomin’ madhouse.", "제가 창문을 열고 그 사람에게 신경 쓰지 말라고 손짓하자, 그는 그곳을 한 번 둘러보고 자기가 어떤 곳에 발을 들였는지 가늠하고는 이렇게 말하는 것으로 그쳤습니다. '맙소사, 나리, 빌어먹을 정신병원에서 무슨 소리를 듣든 저야 개의치 않습니다요."], ["I pity ye and the guv’nor for havin’ to live in the house with a wild beast like that.’ Then he asked his way civilly enough, and I told him where the gate of the empty house was; he went away, followed by threats and curses and revilings from our man.", "저런 들짐승 같은 놈하고 한집에 살아야 하는 나리하고 주인 양반이 딱하구먼요.' 그러고는 제법 공손하게 길을 물었고, 저는 그 빈집의 대문이 어디인지 일러주었습니다. 그는 떠나갔고, 우리 환자의 위협과 저주와 욕설이 그 뒤를 따랐습니다."], ["I went down to see if I could make out any cause for his anger, since he is usually such a well-behaved man, and except his violent fits nothing of the kind had ever occurred.", "저는 그의 분노에 무슨 까닭이라도 알아낼 수 있을까 하여 아래로 내려갔으니, 그는 평소 그토록 얌전한 사람인 데다, 그 난폭한 발작 말고는 그런 일이 한 번도 없었던 까닭입니다."], ["I found him, to my astonishment, quite composed and most genial in his manner.", "놀랍게도 저는 그가 아주 차분하고 더없이 상냥한 태도로 있는 것을 발견했습니다."], ["I tried to get him to talk of the incident, but he blandly asked me questions as to what I meant, and led me to believe that he was completely oblivious of the affair.", "제가 그 일에 대해 이야기를 끌어내보려 했으나, 그는 천연덕스레 제가 무슨 말을 하는 거냐고 되묻더니, 그 일을 까맣게 잊고 있는 양 저를 믿게 만들었습니다."], ["It was, I am sorry to say, however, only another instance of his cunning, for within half an hour I heard of him again.", "그러나 유감스럽게도, 그것은 그의 교활함을 보여주는 또 하나의 사례일 뿐이었으니, 반 시간도 못 되어 저는 그에 대한 소식을 다시 듣게 되었기 때문입니다."], ["This time he had broken out through the window of his room, and was running down the avenue.", "이번에 그는 자기 방 창문을 부수고 빠져나가, 길을 달려 내려가고 있었습니다."], ["I called to the attendants to follow me, and ran after him, for I feared he was intent on some mischief.", "저는 시중꾼들에게 따라오라 외치고 그를 뒤쫓았으니, 그가 무슨 해코지를 작정한 게 아닐까 두려웠던 까닭입니다."], ["My fear was justified when I saw the same cart which had passed before coming down the road, having on it some great wooden boxes.", "앞서 지나갔던 바로 그 짐마차가, 커다란 나무 상자 몇 개를 싣고 길을 내려오는 것을 보았을 때, 제 두려움은 사실로 드러났습니다."], ["The men were wiping their foreheads, and were flushed in the face, as if with violent exercise.", "그 사람들은 격렬한 노동이라도 한 듯 얼굴이 벌게져 이마를 훔치고 있었습니다."], ["Before I could get up to him the patient rushed at them, and pulling one of them off the cart, began to knock his head against the ground.", "제가 그에게 다다르기도 전에 환자는 그들에게 달려들어, 그중 하나를 짐마차에서 끌어내리고는 그의 머리를 땅바닥에 짓찧기 시작했습니다."], ["If I had not seized him just at the moment I believe he would have killed the man there and then.", "제가 바로 그 순간 그를 붙들지 않았더라면, 저는 그가 그 자리에서 당장 그 사람을 죽였으리라 믿습니다."], ["The other fellow jumped down and struck him over the head with the butt-end of his heavy whip.", "다른 사내가 뛰어내려, 그의 묵직한 채찍 손잡이로 그의 머리를 후려쳤습니다."], ["It was a terrible blow; but he did not seem to mind it, but seized him also, and struggled with the three of us, pulling us to and fro as if we were kittens.", "무서운 일격이었으나, 그는 아랑곳하지 않는 듯, 그자마저 붙들고는 우리 셋과 맞붙어, 우리를 새끼고양이라도 되는 양 이리저리 끌고 다녔습니다."], ["You know I am no light weight, and the others were both burly men.", "아시다시피 저는 가벼운 체구가 아니고, 다른 둘도 둘 다 건장한 사내들이었습니다."], ["At first he was silent in his fighting; but as we began to master him, and the attendants were putting a strait-waistcoat on him, he began to shout: ‘I’ll frustrate them! They shan’t rob me!", "처음에 그는 말없이 싸웠으나, 우리가 그를 제압하기 시작하고 시중꾼들이 그에게 구속복을 입히려 하자, 그는 고함치기 시작했습니다. '내가 놈들을 좌절시키겠다!"], ["they shan’t murder me by inches!", "놈들이 나에게서 빼앗지 못하게 하겠다!"], ["I’ll fight for my Lord and Master!’ and all sorts of similar incoherent ravings.", "놈들이 나를 조금씩 죽이게 두지 않겠다! 나는 나의 주인이자 군주를 위해 싸우겠다!'"], ["It was with very considerable difficulty that they got him back to the house and put him in the padded room.", "그 밖에도 온갖 비슷한 두서없는 헛소리를 늘어놓았습니다. 그를 다시 집으로 데려와 완충실에 넣기까지는 상당한 애를 먹어야 했습니다."], ["One of the attendants, Hardy, had a finger broken.", "시중꾼 가운데 하나인 하디는 손가락 하나가 부러졌습니다."], ["However, I set it all right; and he is going on well.", "그러나 제가 그것을 잘 맞춰놓았으니, 그는 잘 회복되고 있습니다."]], [["“The two carriers were at first loud in their threats of actions for damages, and promised to rain all the penalties of the law on us.", "\"그 두 짐꾼은 처음에는 손해 배상 소송을 걸겠다고 큰소리치며 위협하여, 법의 온갖 처벌을 우리에게 퍼붓겠다고 으름장을 놓았습니다."], ["Their threats were, however, mingled with some sort of indirect apology for the defeat of the two of them by a feeble madman.", "그러나 그들의 위협에는, 둘이서 한 가냘픈 미치광이에게 진 데 대한 어떤 에두른 변명이 섞여 있었습니다."], ["They said that if it had not been for the way their strength had been spent in carrying and raising the heavy boxes to the cart they would have made short work of him.", "그들은 무거운 상자들을 나르고 짐마차에 들어 올리느라 기력을 다 써버리지만 않았더라면 그를 단번에 처치했을 거라고 했습니다."], ["They gave as another reason for their defeat the extraordinary state of drouth to which they had been reduced by the dusty nature of their occupation and the reprehensible distance from the scene of their labours of any place of public entertainment. I quite understood their drift, and after a stiff glass of grog, or rather more of the same, and with each a sovereign in hand, they made light of the attack, and swore that they would encounter a worse madman any day for the pleasure of meeting so ‘bloomin’ good a bloke’ as your correspondent.", "그들은 진 또 다른 까닭으로, 일의 먼지투성이 성질 때문에 빠지게 된 유별난 갈증과, 그들의 노동 현장에서 어떤 술집이든 괘씸하리만치 멀리 떨어져 있었던 점을 들었습니다. 저는 그들의 속내를 충분히 알아채고, 독한 그로그 한 잔, 아니 그보다 좀 더 같은 것을 마시게 한 뒤, 각자 손에 1소버린씩 쥐여주자, 그들은 그 습격을 대수롭지 않게 여기며, 이 글을 쓰는 사람처럼 '그렇게 빌어먹게 좋은 양반'을 만나는 즐거움을 위해서라면 언제든 더 고약한 미치광이라도 상대하겠노라고 호언했습니다."], ["I took their names and addresses, in case they might be needed.", "저는 필요할지 몰라 그들의 이름과 주소를 받아두었습니다."], ["They are as follows:--Jack Smollet, of Dudding’s Rents, King George’s Road, Great Walworth, and Thomas Snelling, Peter Farley’s Row, Guide Court, Bethnal Green.", "그것은 다음과 같습니다. — 그레이트 월워스, 킹 조지 로드, 더딩스 렌츠의 잭 스몰릿, 그리고 베스널 그린, 가이드 코트, 피터 팔리스 로의 토머스 스넬링입니다."], ["They are both in the employment of Harris & Sons, Moving and Shipment Company, Orange Master’s Yard, Soho.", "둘 다 소호, 오렌지 마스터스 야드의 운송·선적 회사 해리스 앤드 선스에 고용되어 있습니다."]], [["“I shall report to you any matter of interest occurring here, and shall wire you at once if there is anything of importance.", "\"이곳에서 일어나는 관심 끄는 일은 무엇이든 선생님께 보고드리겠으며, 중요한 일이 있으면 즉시 전보를 치겠습니다. \"믿어 주시기를, 친애하는 선생님께,"]], [["“Believe me, dear Sir,", "\"충실한 벗,\n\"패트릭 헤너시.\""]], [["“Yours faithfully,", "편지, 미나 하커가 루시 웨스튼라에게."]], [["(Unopened by her.)", "(그녀가 뜯어보지 못함.)"]], [["“18 September.", "\"9월 18일."]], [["“My dearest Lucy,--", "\"나의 가장 사랑하는 루시에게, —"]], [["“Such a sad blow has befallen us. Mr.", "\"우리에게 참으로 슬픈 변고가 닥쳤어."], ["Hawkins has died very suddenly.", "호킨스 씨가 아주 갑작스레 돌아가셨단다."], ["Some may not think it so sad for us, but we had both come to so love him that it really seems as though we had lost a father.", "우리에게 그리 슬픈 일은 아니라고 여길 이도 있겠지만, 우리 둘 다 그분을 무척이나 사랑하게 되어, 정말이지 아버지를 잃은 것만 같아."], ["I never knew either father or mother, so that the dear old man’s death is a real blow to me.", "나는 아버지도 어머니도 알지 못하고 자랐기에, 그 다정한 노인의 죽음은 나에게 진정 큰 타격이란다."], ["Jonathan is greatly distressed.", "조너선은 몹시 상심해 있어."], ["It is not only that he feels sorrow, deep sorrow, for the dear, good man who has befriended him all his life, and now at the end has treated him like his own son and left him a fortune which to people of our modest bringing up is wealth beyond the dream of avarice, but Jonathan feels it on another account.", "그것은 단지 그이가, 평생 그를 도와주고 이제 마지막에 와서는 자기 친아들처럼 대하며, 우리처럼 검소하게 자란 사람들에게는 탐욕의 꿈을 넘어서는 재산을 남겨준 그 다정하고 선한 분을 위해 슬픔을, 깊은 슬픔을 느끼기 때문만은 아니야. 조너선은 또 다른 까닭으로도 그것을 느낀단다."], ["He says the amount of responsibility which it puts upon him makes him nervous. He begins to doubt himself.", "그이는 그것이 자기에게 지운 책임의 무게가 자기를 불안하게 만든다고 해. 그이는 자기 자신을 의심하기 시작했어."], ["I try to cheer him up, and my belief in him helps him to have a belief in himself.", "나는 그이의 기운을 북돋워 보려 애쓰고, 내가 그이를 믿는 것이 그이가 자기 자신을 믿는 데 도움이 되긴 해."], ["But it is here that the grave shock that he experienced tells upon him the most.", "하지만 그이가 겪은 그 심각한 충격이 가장 심하게 나타나는 곳이 바로 여기야."], ["Oh, it is too hard that a sweet, simple, noble, strong nature such as his--a nature which enabled him by our dear, good friend’s aid to rise from clerk to master in a few years--should be so injured that the very essence of its strength is gone.", "아, 그이 같은 다정하고 소박하고 고귀하고 강인한 천성이 — 우리의 다정하고 선한 벗의 도움으로 몇 년 만에 사무원에서 주인으로 올라설 수 있게 해준 그 천성이 — 그 힘의 정수마저 사라질 만큼 상하다니, 너무도 가혹한 일이야."], ["Forgive me, dear, if I worry you with my troubles in the midst of your own happiness; but, Lucy dear, I must tell some one, for the strain of keeping up a brave and cheerful appearance to Jonathan tries me, and I have no one here that I can confide in.", "용서해 줘, 얘야, 너 자신의 행복 한가운데에서 내 근심으로 너를 성가시게 한다면. 하지만, 사랑하는 루시야, 나는 누군가에게 털어놓아야만 해. 조너선 앞에서 씩씩하고 명랑한 모습을 꾸며 보이는 긴장이 나를 힘들게 하는데, 이곳에는 마음을 터놓을 사람이 아무도 없으니까."], ["I dread coming up to London, as we must do the day after to-morrow; for poor Mr.", "모레는 우리가 그래야만 하지만, 나는 런던으로 올라가는 게 두려워."], ["Hawkins left in his will that he was to be buried in the grave with his father.", "가엾은 호킨스 씨가 유언으로 자기 아버지의 무덤에 함께 묻히기를 남기셨거든."], ["As there are no relations at all, Jonathan will have to be chief mourner.", "친척이라곤 전혀 없으니, 조너선이 상주를 맡아야 할 거야."], ["I shall try to run over to see you, dearest, if only for a few minutes.", "나는 너를 보러 잠깐이라도, 단 몇 분만이라도 들르려 애써볼게, 사랑하는 이야."], ["Forgive me for troubling you. With all blessings,", "너를 성가시게 한 걸 용서해 줘. 온갖 축복과 함께,\n\"너를 사랑하는"]], [["“Your loving", "\"미나 하커.\""]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "수어드 박사의 일기."]], [["20 September.--Only resolution and habit can let me make an entry to-night.", "9월 20일. — 오직 결심과 습관만이 오늘 밤 내가 이 일기를 적게 해준다."], ["I am too miserable, too low-spirited, too sick of the world and all in it, including life itself, that I would not care if I heard this moment the flapping of the wings of the angel of death.", "나는 너무도 비참하고, 너무도 기운이 가라앉았으며, 세상과 그 안의 모든 것에, 삶 자체까지 포함하여 너무도 진저리가 나서, 지금 이 순간 죽음의 천사가 날갯짓하는 소리를 듣는다 해도 개의치 않으리라."], ["And he has been flapping those grim wings to some purpose of late--Lucy’s mother and Arthur’s father, and now....", "그리고 그자는 요즘 그 음산한 날개를 무언가 목적을 두고 퍼덕여 왔으니 — 루시의 어머니와 아서의 아버지, 그리고 이제는……"], ["Let me get on with my work.", "내 할 일이나 계속하자."]], [["I duly relieved Van Helsing in his watch over Lucy.", "나는 정해진 대로 루시를 지키던 반 헬싱과 교대했다."], ["We wanted Arthur to go to rest also, but he refused at first.", "우리는 아서도 가서 쉬기를 바랐으나, 그는 처음에는 거절했다."], ["It was only when I told him that we should want him to help us during the day, and that we must not all break down for want of rest, lest Lucy should suffer, that he agreed to go.", "낮 동안 우리가 그의 도움이 필요할 거라고, 그리고 루시가 고통받지 않도록 우리 모두가 휴식 부족으로 쓰러져서는 안 된다고 일러주고서야 비로소 그는 가기로 했다."], ["Van Helsing was very kind to him.", "반 헬싱은 그에게 무척 다정했다."], ["“Come, my child,” he said; “come with me.", "\"이리 오게, 내 아이.\" 그가 말했다."], ["You are sick and weak, and have had much sorrow and much mental pain, as well as that tax on your strength that we know of.", "\"나와 함께 가세. 자네는 병약하고 쇠약한 데다, 우리가 아는 그 기력의 소모뿐 아니라 많은 슬픔과 많은 마음의 고통을 겪었네."], ["You must not be alone; for to be alone is to be full of fears and alarms.", "자네는 혼자 있어서는 안 되네. 혼자 있다는 것은 두려움과 불안으로 가득 차는 것이니 말일세."], ["Come to the drawing-room, where there is a big fire, and there are two sofas.", "큰 불이 피워져 있고 소파가 둘 있는 응접실로 가세."], ["You shall lie on one, and I on the other, and our sympathy will be comfort to each other, even though we do not speak, and even if we sleep.”", "자네는 한쪽에 눕고 나는 다른 쪽에 누우면, 우리가 말을 하지 않더라도, 설령 잠이 든다 해도, 우리의 동병상련이 서로에게 위로가 될 걸세.\""], ["Arthur went off with him, casting back a longing look on Lucy’s face, which lay in her pillow, almost whiter than the lawn.", "아서는 베개 위에 누운 루시의 얼굴에 — 그것은 얇은 아마포보다 거의 더 희었다 — 미련 어린 눈길을 던지며 그와 함께 떠났다."], ["She lay quite still, and I looked round the room to see that all was as it should be.", "그녀는 아주 가만히 누워 있었고, 나는 모든 것이 제대로 되어 있는지 보려고 방을 둘러보았다."], ["I could see that the Professor had carried out in this room, as in the other, his purpose of using the garlic; the whole of the window-sashes reeked with it, and round Lucy’s neck, over the silk handkerchief which Van Helsing made her keep on, was a rough chaplet of the same odorous flowers.", "교수가 이 방에서도 저쪽 방에서처럼 마늘을 쓰려던 그의 뜻을 실행에 옮긴 것을 나는 알아볼 수 있었다. 창틀 전체에서 마늘 냄새가 진동했고, 반 헬싱이 그녀에게 두르고 있게 한 비단 손수건 위로, 루시의 목둘레에는 그 냄새 나는 꽃으로 엮은 거친 화관이 둘러져 있었다."], ["Lucy was breathing somewhat stertorously, and her face was at its worst, for the open mouth showed the pale gums.", "루시는 다소 그르렁거리며 숨을 쉬고 있었고, 벌어진 입이 핏기 없는 잇몸을 드러내, 그녀의 얼굴은 더없이 나쁜 상태였다."], ["Her teeth, in the dim, uncertain light, seemed longer and sharper than they had been in the morning.", "그녀의 이는, 그 어슴푸레하고 불확실한 빛 속에서, 아침나절보다 더 길고 날카로워 보였다."], ["In particular, by some trick of the light, the canine teeth looked longer and sharper than the rest. I sat down by her, and presently she moved uneasily.", "특히 어떤 빛의 장난인지, 송곳니가 나머지보다 더 길고 날카로워 보였다. 나는 그녀 곁에 앉았는데, 이윽고 그녀가 불안하게 몸을 뒤척였다."], ["At the same moment there came a sort of dull flapping or buffeting at the window.", "바로 그 순간 창문에서 무언가 둔하게 퍼덕이거나 부딪치는 듯한 소리가 났다."], ["I went over to it softly, and peeped out by the corner of the blind.", "나는 가만히 그쪽으로 다가가 차양 모서리로 밖을 엿보았다."], ["There was a full moonlight, and I could see that the noise was made by a great bat, which wheeled round--doubtless attracted by the light, although so dim--and every now and again struck the window with its wings.", "보름달이 환했고, 나는 그 소리가 커다란 박쥐 한 마리가 내는 것임을 볼 수 있었다. 그것은 — 비록 어슴푸레하나마 그 빛에 이끌린 게 분명한데 — 빙빙 맴돌며 이따금 날개로 창문을 쳤다."], ["When I came back to my seat, I found that Lucy had moved slightly, and had torn away the garlic flowers from her throat. I replaced them as well as I could, and sat watching her.", "내가 자리로 돌아오니, 루시가 조금 몸을 움직여, 목에서 마늘꽃을 떼어내 버린 것을 발견했다. 나는 되도록 잘 그것을 도로 놓아두고, 그녀를 지켜보며 앉아 있었다."]], [["Presently she woke, and I gave her food, as Van Helsing had prescribed.", "이윽고 그녀가 깨어났고, 나는 반 헬싱이 처방한 대로 그녀에게 음식을 주었다."], ["She took but a little, and that languidly.", "그녀는 조금밖에, 그것도 맥없이 받아먹었다."], ["There did not seem to be with her now the unconscious struggle for life and strength that had hitherto so marked her illness.", "그녀에게는 이제, 그때껏 그녀의 병을 그토록 두드러지게 했던, 삶과 기력을 향한 그 무의식적인 안간힘이 없는 듯했다."], ["It struck me as curious that the moment she became conscious she pressed the garlic flowers close to her.", "의식을 차리는 순간 그녀가 마늘꽃을 자기 가슴께로 바싹 끌어당기는 것이 나에게는 기이하게 다가왔다."], ["It was certainly odd that whenever she got into that lethargic state, with the stertorous breathing, she put the flowers from her; but that when she waked she clutched them close.", "그르렁대는 숨소리와 함께 그 무기력한 상태에 빠질 때마다 꽃을 멀리하다가, 깨어나면 그것을 바싹 그러쥐는 것은 분명 이상한 일이었다."], ["There was no possibility of making any mistake about this, for in the long hours that followed, she had many spells of sleeping and waking and repeated both actions many times.", "이 점에 대해서는 어떤 착각의 여지도 있을 수 없었으니, 뒤따른 긴 시간 동안 그녀는 잠들고 깨어나기를 여러 차례 거듭하며 그 두 동작을 몇 번이고 되풀이했기 때문이다."]], [["At six o’clock Van Helsing came to relieve me.", "여섯 시에 반 헬싱이 나와 교대하러 왔다."], ["Arthur had then fallen into a doze, and he mercifully let him sleep on.", "그때 아서는 깜빡 잠이 들어 있었고, 그는 자비롭게도 그를 계속 자게 두었다."], ["When he saw Lucy’s face I could hear the sissing indraw of his breath, and he said to me in a sharp whisper: “Draw up the blind; I want light!”", "루시의 얼굴을 보았을 때, 나는 그가 쉭 하고 숨을 들이켜는 소리를 들을 수 있었고, 그는 나에게 날카로이 속삭였다. \"차양을 올리게. 빛이 필요하네!\""], ["Then he bent down, and, with his face almost touching Lucy’s, examined her carefully.", "그러고는 몸을 숙여, 그의 얼굴이 루시의 얼굴에 거의 닿을 듯이 하여 그녀를 찬찬히 살폈다."], ["He removed the flowers and lifted the silk handkerchief from her throat.", "그는 꽃들을 치우고 그녀의 목에서 비단 손수건을 들어 올렸다."], ["As he did so he started back, and I could hear his ejaculation, “Mein Gott!” as it was smothered in his throat.", "그렇게 하면서 그는 움찔 물러섰고, 나는 그가 목구멍에서 삼키듯 내뱉은 탄식, \"마인 고트!\"를 들을 수 있었다."], ["I bent over and looked, too, and as I noticed some queer chill came over me.", "나도 몸을 숙여 들여다보았는데, 그것을 보는 순간 어떤 기이한 한기가 나를 엄습했다."]], [["The wounds on the throat had absolutely disappeared.", "목의 상처가 완전히 사라져 있었다."]], [["For fully five minutes Van Helsing stood looking at her, with his face at its sternest.", "꼬박 오 분 동안 반 헬싱은 더없이 준엄한 얼굴로 그녀를 바라보며 서 있었다."], ["Then he turned to me and said calmly:--", "그러더니 나에게 돌아서서 차분히 말했다."]], [["“She is dying. It will not be long now.", "\"그녀는 죽어가고 있네. 이제 오래지 않을 걸세."], ["It will be much difference, mark me, whether she dies conscious or in her sleep.", "잘 새겨두게, 그녀가 의식이 있는 채로 죽느냐 잠든 채로 죽느냐는 큰 차이가 있을 걸세."], ["Wake that poor boy, and let him come and see the last; he trusts us, and we have promised him.”", "저 가엾은 청년을 깨워, 와서 마지막을 보게 하게. 그는 우리를 믿고, 우리는 그에게 약속했으니 말일세.\""]], [["I went to the dining-room and waked him.", "나는 식당으로 가서 그를 깨웠다."], ["He was dazed for a moment, but when he saw the sunlight streaming in through the edges of the shutters he thought he was late, and expressed his fear.", "그는 한순간 멍해 있었으나, 덧문 가장자리로 햇살이 흘러드는 것을 보고는 자기가 늦었다고 생각하여 두려움을 내비쳤다."], ["I assured him that Lucy was still asleep, but told him as gently as I could that both Van Helsing and I feared that the end was near.", "나는 루시가 여전히 잠들어 있다고 그를 안심시켰으나, 되도록 부드럽게, 반 헬싱과 나 둘 다 끝이 가까웠음을 두려워한다고 일렀다."], ["He covered his face with his hands, and slid down on his knees by the sofa, where he remained, perhaps a minute, with his head buried, praying, whilst his shoulders shook with grief.", "그는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고는 소파 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 미끄러지듯 주저앉아, 거기에서 한 일 분쯤, 고개를 파묻은 채 기도하며 머물렀는데, 그의 두 어깨가 슬픔으로 떨렸다."], ["I took him by the hand and raised him up. “Come,”", "나는 그의 손을 잡아 일으켜 세웠다. \"이리 오게.\""], ["I said, “my dear old fellow, summon all your fortitude: it will be best and easiest for her.”", "내가 말했다. \"나의 다정한 친구여, 자네의 용기를 다 끌어모으게. 그것이 그녀에게 가장 좋고 가장 편안할 걸세.\""]], [["When we came into Lucy’s room I could see that Van Helsing had, with his usual forethought, been putting matters straight and making everything look as pleasing as possible.", "우리가 루시의 방으로 들어서자, 나는 반 헬싱이 늘 그렇듯 앞서 헤아려, 매무새를 바로잡고 모든 것을 되도록 보기 좋게 꾸며두었음을 알 수 있었다."], ["He had even brushed Lucy’s hair, so that it lay on the pillow in its usual sunny ripples.", "그는 루시의 머리칼까지 빗겨놓아, 그것이 평소처럼 햇살 같은 잔물결을 이루며 베개 위에 놓여 있게 했다."], ["When we came into the room she opened her eyes, and seeing him, whispered softly:--", "우리가 방에 들어서자 그녀는 눈을 떴고, 그를 보더니 가만히 속삭였다."]], [["“Arthur!", "\"아서!"], ["Oh, my love, I am so glad you have come!”", "오, 내 사랑, 와줘서 정말 기뻐요!\""], ["He was stooping to kiss her, when Van Helsing motioned him back. “No,”", "그가 입 맞추려 몸을 숙이는데, 반 헬싱이 그에게 물러서라고 손짓했다. \"안 되네.\""], ["he whispered, “not yet!", "그가 속삭였다. \"아직은!"], ["Hold her hand; it will comfort her more.”", "그녀의 손을 잡게. 그게 그녀를 더 위로할 걸세.\""]], [["So Arthur took her hand and knelt beside her, and she looked her best, with all the soft lines matching the angelic beauty of her eyes.", "그리하여 아서는 그녀의 손을 잡고 곁에 무릎을 꿇었고, 그녀는 그 부드러운 윤곽 하나하나가 천사 같은 두 눈의 아름다움과 어우러져, 더없이 고운 모습이었다."], ["Then gradually her eyes closed, and she sank to sleep.", "이윽고 그녀의 두 눈이 차츰 감기고, 그녀는 잠 속으로 가라앉았다."], ["For a little bit her breast heaved softly, and her breath came and went like a tired child’s.", "잠깐 동안 그녀의 가슴이 부드럽게 오르내렸고, 숨결이 지친 아이의 그것처럼 드나들었다."]], [["And then insensibly there came the strange change which I had noticed in the night.", "그러다가 어느새, 내가 밤중에 눈여겨본 그 기이한 변화가 찾아왔다."], ["Her breathing grew stertorous, the mouth opened, and the pale gums, drawn back, made the teeth look longer and sharper than ever.", "그녀의 숨소리가 그르렁거리기 시작했고, 입이 벌어지며, 핏기 없는 잇몸이 물러나 이를 그 어느 때보다 더 길고 날카로워 보이게 했다."], ["In a sort of sleep-waking, vague, unconscious way she opened her eyes, which were now dull and hard at once, and said in a soft, voluptuous voice, such as I had never heard from her lips:--", "어떤 잠결의 몽롱하고 흐릿한, 의식 없는 상태로 그녀가 두 눈을 떴는데, 그 눈은 이제 흐리멍덩하면서도 동시에 매정했다. 그러고는 그녀의 입술에서 한 번도 들어본 적 없는, 부드럽고 관능적인 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Arthur!", "\"아서!"], ["Oh, my love, I am so glad you have come! Kiss me!”", "오, 내 사랑, 와줘서 정말 기뻐요! 키스해 줘요!\""], ["Arthur bent eagerly over to kiss her; but at that instant Van Helsing, who, like me, had been startled by her voice, swooped upon him, and catching him by the neck with both hands, dragged him back with a fury of strength which I never thought he could have possessed, and actually hurled him almost across the room.", "아서는 그녀에게 입 맞추려 열심히 몸을 숙였다. 그러나 그 순간, 나처럼 그녀의 목소리에 놀랐던 반 헬싱이 그에게 덮치듯 달려들어, 두 손으로 그의 목덜미를 붙잡고는, 그가 지녔으리라고는 내가 결코 생각지 못한 맹렬한 힘으로 그를 뒤로 끌어당겨, 실로 방을 가로지르다시피 그를 내동댕이쳤다."]], [["“Not for your life!”", "\"목숨을 걸고서라도 안 되네!\""], ["he said; “not for your living soul and hers!”", "그가 말했다. \"자네의 살아 있는 영혼을 걸고, 그녀의 영혼을 걸고서라도 안 되네!\""], ["And he stood between them like a lion at bay.", "그러고는 궁지에 몰린 사자처럼 그 둘 사이에 버티고 섰다."]], [["Arthur was so taken aback that he did not for a moment know what to do or say; and before any impulse of violence could seize him he realised the place and the occasion, and stood silent, waiting.", "아서는 너무도 얼떨떨하여 한순간 무엇을 해야 할지, 무슨 말을 해야 할지 알지 못했다. 그리고 어떤 난폭한 충동에 사로잡히기도 전에 그는 그 자리와 그 상황을 깨닫고는, 말없이 서서 기다렸다."]], [["I kept my eyes fixed on Lucy, as did Van Helsing, and we saw a spasm as of rage flit like a shadow over her face; the sharp teeth champed together.", "나는 반 헬싱이 그랬듯 루시에게서 눈을 떼지 않았고, 우리는 분노 같은 경련이 그림자처럼 그녀의 얼굴을 스치는 것을 보았다. 날카로운 이가 맞부딪쳐 갈렸다."], ["Then her eyes closed, and she breathed heavily.", "그러더니 그녀의 두 눈이 감기고, 그녀는 무겁게 숨을 쉬었다."]], [["Very shortly after she opened her eyes in all their softness, and putting out her poor, pale, thin hand, took Van Helsing’s great brown one; drawing it to her, she kissed it.", "그러고는 곧 그녀가 더없이 부드러운 두 눈을 떠, 그 가엾고 핏기 없고 야윈 손을 내밀어 반 헬싱의 크고 갈색 손을 잡았다. 그것을 자기 쪽으로 끌어당겨 그녀는 거기에 입을 맞추었다."], ["“My true friend,”", "\"나의 진정한 벗이여.\""], ["she said, in a faint voice, but with untellable pathos, “My true friend, and his!", "그녀가 희미한 목소리로, 그러나 이루 말할 수 없는 애잔함으로 말했다. \"나의 진정한 벗이자, 그이의 벗이여!"], ["Oh, guard him, and give me peace!”", "오, 그이를 지켜주세요, 그리고 저에게 평안을 주세요!\""]], [["“I swear it!”", "\"내 맹세하리다!\""], ["he said solemnly, kneeling beside her and holding up his hand, as one who registers an oath.", "그가, 맹세를 기록하는 사람처럼 그녀 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 한 손을 치켜들며 엄숙히 말했다."], ["Then he turned to Arthur, and said to him: “Come, my child, take her hand in yours, and kiss her on the forehead, and only once.”", "그러고는 아서에게 돌아서서 그에게 말했다. \"이리 오게, 내 아이. 그녀의 손을 자네 손에 잡고, 그녀의 이마에 입을 맞추게, 단 한 번만일세.\""]], [["Their eyes met instead of their lips; and so they parted.", "그들의 입술 대신 그들의 눈길이 마주쳤다. 그렇게 그들은 헤어졌다."]], [["Lucy’s eyes closed; and Van Helsing, who had been watching closely, took Arthur’s arm, and drew him away.", "루시의 두 눈이 감겼다. 가까이서 지켜보고 있던 반 헬싱이 아서의 팔을 잡아 그를 끌어냈다."]], [["And then Lucy’s breathing became stertorous again, and all at once it ceased.", "그러고는 루시의 숨소리가 다시 그르렁거리더니, 별안간 그것이 멎었다."]], [["“It is all over,”", "\"다 끝났네.\""], ["said Van Helsing.", "반 헬싱이 말했다."], ["“She is dead!”", "\"그녀는 죽었어!\""]], [["I took Arthur by the arm, and led him away to the drawing-room, where he sat down, and covered his face with his hands, sobbing in a way that nearly broke me down to see.", "나는 아서의 팔을 잡고 응접실로 그를 데려갔다. 그는 거기에 주저앉아 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고는, 보고 있던 내가 거의 무너져 내릴 만큼 흐느꼈다."]], [["I went back to the room, and found Van Helsing looking at poor Lucy, and his face was sterner than ever.", "나는 방으로 돌아가, 반 헬싱이 가엾은 루시를 바라보고 있는 것을 발견했는데, 그의 얼굴은 그 어느 때보다 준엄했다."], ["Some change had come over her body.", "그녀의 몸에 어떤 변화가 일어나 있었다."], ["Death had given back part of her beauty, for her brow and cheeks had recovered some of their flowing lines; even the lips had lost their deadly pallor.", "죽음이 그녀의 아름다움 일부를 되돌려주어, 이마와 두 뺨이 그 흐르는 듯한 윤곽을 얼마간 되찾았고, 입술마저 그 죽은 듯한 창백함을 잃었다."], ["It was as if the blood, no longer needed for the working of the heart, had gone to make the harshness of death as little rude as might be.", "마치 더 이상 심장의 작용에 쓰일 일이 없게 된 피가, 죽음의 모진 자취를 되도록 덜 거칠게 만들려고 흘러간 것만 같았다."]], [["“We thought her dying whilst she slept, And sleeping when she died.”", "\"우리는 그녀가 잠든 채 죽어간다고 여겼고, 죽어가는 동안 잠들었다고 여겼네.\""]], [["I stood beside Van Helsing, and said:--", "나는 반 헬싱 곁에 서서 말했다."]], [["“Ah, well, poor girl, there is peace for her at last.", "\"아, 이제 됐습니다, 가엾은 처녀, 그녀에게도 마침내 평안이 찾아왔군요."], ["It is the end!”", "이것이 끝입니다!\""]], [["He turned to me, and said with grave solemnity:--", "그는 나에게 돌아서서, 무거운 엄숙함으로 말했다."]], [["“Not so; alas!", "\"그렇지 않네. 아아!"], ["not so. It is only the beginning!”", "그렇지 않아. 이것은 시작일 뿐일세!\""]], [["When I asked him what he meant, he only shook his head and answered:--", "내가 그에게 무슨 뜻이냐고 묻자, 그는 그저 고개를 저으며 대답했다."]], [["“We can do nothing as yet.", "\"우리는 아직 아무것도 할 수 없네."], ["Wait and see.”", "기다려 보세.\""]], [["DR. SEWARD’S DIARY--continued.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」(계속)"]], [["The funeral was arranged for the next succeeding day, so that Lucy and her mother might be buried together.", "장례식은 다음다음 날로 잡혔으니, 루시와 그녀의 어머니를 함께 묻기 위함이었다."], ["I attended to all the ghastly formalities, and the urbane undertaker proved that his staff were afflicted--or blessed--with something of his own obsequious suavity. Even the woman who performed the last offices for the dead remarked to me, in a confidential, brother-professional way, when she had come out from the death-chamber:--", "나는 그 모든 끔찍한 절차를 도맡아 처리했는데, 정중한 장의사는 자기 직원들도 자신이 지닌 굽실거리는 듯한 상냥함을 어느 정도 앓고 있다는 — 혹은 그 복을 누리고 있다는 — 것을 입증해 보였다. 망자에게 마지막 손길을 베푸는 여인조차, 임종의 방에서 나오면서 은밀하게, 동업자끼리의 정을 담은 투로 나에게 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“She makes a very beautiful corpse, sir.", "\"참으로 아름다운 시신이세요, 선생님."], ["It’s quite a privilege to attend on her.", "이분을 모시는 게 그야말로 영광이지요."], ["It’s not too much to say that she will do credit to our establishment!”", "우리 가게의 자랑이 되실 거라고 해도 결코 지나친 말이 아니랍니다!\""]], [["I noticed that Van Helsing never kept far away. This was possible from the disordered state of things in the household.", "판 헬싱이 한 번도 멀리 떨어져 있지 않았다는 것을 나는 알아차렸다. 집안 일이 어수선했던 까닭에 그럴 수 있었다."], ["There were no relatives at hand; and as Arthur had to be back the next day to attend at his father’s funeral, we were unable to notify any one who should have been bidden.", "가까이에 친척이 없었고, 아서는 다음 날 아버지의 장례식에 참석하러 돌아가야 했으므로, 우리는 마땅히 부를 만한 사람에게 알릴 수가 없었다."], ["Under the circumstances, Van Helsing and I took it upon ourselves to examine papers, etc.", "이런 사정이었으므로 판 헬싱과 나는 서류 따위를 살피는 일을 떠맡았다."], ["He insisted upon looking over Lucy’s papers himself.", "그는 루시의 서류를 자기가 직접 들여다보겠다고 고집했다."], ["I asked him why, for I feared that he, being a foreigner, might not be quite aware of English legal requirements, and so might in ignorance make some unnecessary trouble.", "나는 그 까닭을 물었으니, 그가 외국인인지라 영국의 법적 요건을 잘 알지 못하여 무지로 인해 쓸데없는 분란을 일으킬까 두려웠기 때문이다."], ["He answered me:--", "그가 나에게 답했다."]], [["“I know; I know. You forget that I am a lawyer as well as a doctor.", "\"나도 아오, 나도 알아요. 내가 의사이면서 또한 법률가라는 걸 잊은 게요."], ["But this is not altogether for the law.", "그러나 이건 온전히 법 때문만은 아니오."], ["You knew that, when you avoided the coroner.", "그건 당신도 알고 있었으니, 검시관을 피했을 때 말이오."], ["I have more than him to avoid.", "나는 그 사람보다 더한 것을 피해야 하오."], ["There may be papers more--such as this.”", "더 있을지도 모르오, 이런 서류가 말이오.\""]], [["As he spoke he took from his pocket-book the memorandum which had been in Lucy’s breast, and which she had torn in her sleep.", "이렇게 말하면서 그는 수첩에서, 루시의 가슴속에 있다가 그녀가 잠결에 찢어버린 그 비망록을 꺼냈다."]], [["“When you find anything of the solicitor who is for the late Mrs. Westenra, seal all her papers, and write him to-night.", "\"고(故) 웨스텐라 부인의 변호사를 찾거든, 부인의 서류는 모두 봉인하고 오늘 밤 그에게 편지를 쓰시오."], ["For me, I watch here in the room and in Miss Lucy’s old room all night, and I myself search for what may be.", "나는 이 방과 루시 양의 옛 방에서 밤새 지키며, 무엇이 있을지 내가 직접 찾아보겠소."], ["It is not well that her very thoughts go into the hands of strangers.”", "그녀의 바로 그 생각들이 낯선 이의 손에 들어가는 것은 좋지 않소.\""]], [["I went on with my part of the work, and in another half hour had found the name and address of Mrs. Westenra’s solicitor and had written to him.", "나는 내 몫의 일을 계속하여, 또 반 시간 만에 웨스텐라 부인의 변호사 이름과 주소를 찾아 그에게 편지를 썼다."], ["All the poor lady’s papers were in order; explicit directions regarding the place of burial were given.", "그 가엾은 부인의 서류는 모두 정연했고, 매장 장소에 관한 분명한 지시도 적혀 있었다."], ["I had hardly sealed the letter, when, to my surprise, Van Helsing walked into the room, saying:--", "편지를 막 봉했을 때, 놀랍게도 판 헬싱이 방으로 들어오며 말했다."]], [["“Can I help you, friend John?", "\"도와줄까요, 존?"], ["I am free, and if I may, my service is to you.”", "나는 한가하니, 괜찮다면 당신을 돕겠소.\""]], [["“Have you got what you looked for?”", "\"찾던 것은 구하셨소?\""], ["I asked, to which he replied:--", "하고 내가 묻자, 그가 답했다."]], [["“I did not look for any specific thing.", "\"딱히 무엇을 찾은 것은 아니오."], ["I only hoped to find, and find I have, all that there was--only some letters and a few memoranda, and a diary new begun.", "그저 찾을 수 있기를 바랐고, 또 있던 것을 다 찾았소 — 편지 몇 통과 비망록 몇 장, 그리고 새로 시작한 일기 하나뿐이었소."], ["But I have them here, and we shall for the present say nothing of them.", "그러나 지금 내가 가지고 있으니, 당분간은 그것들에 대해 아무 말도 하지 맙시다."], ["I shall see that poor lad to-morrow evening, and, with his sanction, I shall use some.”", "내일 저녁에 그 가엾은 청년을 만나, 그의 허락을 얻어 그 가운데 얼마를 쓰겠소.\""]], [["When we had finished the work in hand, he said to me:--", "당면한 일을 마치자 그가 나에게 말했다."]], [["“And now, friend John, I think we may to bed.", "\"자, 이제 존, 우리 잠자리에 드는 게 좋겠소."], ["We want sleep, both you and I, and rest to recuperate.", "당신도 나도 잠이 필요하고, 기운을 되찾을 휴식도 필요하오."], ["To-morrow we shall have much to do, but for the to-night there is no need of us. Alas!”", "내일은 할 일이 많겠지만, 오늘 밤만큼은 우리가 할 일이 없소. 아아!\""]], [["Before turning in we went to look at poor Lucy.", "잠자리에 들기 전에 우리는 가엾은 루시를 보러 갔다."], ["The undertaker had certainly done his work well, for the room was turned into a small chapelle ardente.", "장의사가 일을 잘해놓은 것이 분명했으니, 방은 작은 '빈소(殯所)'로 꾸며져 있었다."], ["There was a wilderness of beautiful white flowers, and death was made as little repulsive as might be.", "아름다운 흰 꽃이 한가득 어우러져, 죽음은 될 수 있는 한 덜 혐오스럽게 다루어져 있었다."], ["The end of the winding-sheet was laid over the face; when the Professor bent over and turned it gently back, we both started at the beauty before us, the tall wax candles showing a sufficient light to note it well.", "수의 자락이 얼굴 위에 덮여 있었는데, 교수가 몸을 굽혀 그것을 살며시 젖히자, 우리 둘 다 눈앞의 아름다움에 흠칫 놀랐다. 키 큰 밀랍 양초들이 그것을 잘 살펴볼 만큼 충분한 빛을 비추고 있었다."], ["All Lucy’s loveliness had come back to her in death, and the hours that had passed, instead of leaving traces of “decay’s effacing fingers,” had but restored the beauty of life, till positively I could not believe my eyes that I was looking at a corpse.", "루시의 모든 사랑스러움이 죽음 속에서 되돌아와 있었고, 흘러간 시간들은 \"부패의 지우는 손길\"의 자취를 남기기는커녕 오히려 살아 있을 적의 아름다움을 되살려놓아, 내가 시신을 바라보고 있다는 것을 정말이지 내 눈으로 믿을 수 없을 지경이었다."]], [["The Professor looked sternly grave.", "교수는 엄숙하고 침통한 표정이었다."], ["He had not loved her as I had, and there was no need for tears in his eyes.", "그는 나만큼 그녀를 사랑하지 않았으니, 그의 눈에 눈물이 고일 까닭은 없었다."], ["He said to me: “Remain till I return,”", "그는 나에게 \"내가 돌아올 때까지 여기 있으시오\""], ["and left the room. He came back with a handful of wild garlic from the box waiting in the hall, but which had not been opened, and placed the flowers amongst the others on and around the bed.", "하고 말하고는 방을 나갔다. 그는 현관에서 기다리고 있었으나 아직 열지 않은 상자에서 야생 마늘 한 움큼을 가지고 돌아와, 침대 위와 그 둘레의 다른 꽃들 사이에 그 꽃을 놓았다."], ["Then he took from his neck, inside his collar, a little gold crucifix, and placed it over the mouth.", "그러고는 목에서, 옷깃 안쪽에서 작은 금 십자고상을 꺼내어 그녀의 입 위에 놓았다."], ["He restored the sheet to its place, and we came away.", "그는 수의를 제자리로 되돌려놓았고, 우리는 그 방을 떠나왔다."]], [["I was undressing in my own room, when, with a premonitory tap at the door, he entered, and at once began to speak:--", "내가 내 방에서 옷을 벗고 있을 때, 그가 미리 알리는 노크를 하고 들어와서는 곧바로 말하기 시작했다."]], [["“To-morrow I want you to bring me, before night, a set of post-mortem knives.”", "\"내일 날이 저물기 전에 부검용 칼 한 벌을 가져다주었으면 하오.\""]], [["“Must we make an autopsy?” I asked.", "\"부검을 해야 하오?\" 하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“Yes and no. I want to operate, but not as you think.", "\"그렇기도 하고 아니기도 하오. 나는 시술을 하고 싶지만, 당신이 생각하는 것과는 다르오."], ["Let me tell you now, but not a word to another.", "지금 말해줄 테니, 다른 누구에게도 한마디도 하지 마시오."], ["I want to cut off her head and take out her heart. Ah!", "나는 그녀의 머리를 자르고 심장을 꺼내고 싶소. 아!"], ["you a surgeon, and so shocked!", "외과의사인 당신이 그토록 충격을 받다니!"], ["You, whom I have seen with no tremble of hand or heart, do operations of life and death that make the rest shudder.", "다른 이들을 몸서리치게 만드는 생사의 수술을 손끝 하나, 가슴 한 번 떨지 않고 해내는 것을 내가 봐온 당신이 말이오."], ["Oh, but I must not forget, my dear friend John, that you loved her; and I have not forgotten it, for it is I that shall operate, and you must only help.", "오, 그러나 잊어서는 안 되겠지, 나의 친애하는 벗 존, 당신이 그녀를 사랑했다는 것을. 나도 그것을 잊지 않았으니, 시술은 내가 할 것이고 당신은 거들기만 하면 되오."], ["I would like to do it to-night, but for Arthur I must not; he will be free after his father’s funeral to-morrow, and he will want to see her--to see it.", "오늘 밤 하고 싶지만, 아서를 생각하면 그래서는 안 되오. 그는 내일 아버지의 장례식이 끝나면 자유로워질 것이고, 그녀를 — 그것을 보고 싶어할 것이오."], ["Then, when she is coffined ready for the next day, you and I shall come when all sleep.", "그러니 그녀가 다음 날을 위해 관에 안치되거든, 모두가 잠든 뒤에 당신과 내가 옵시다."], ["We shall unscrew the coffin-lid, and shall do our operation: and then replace all, so that none know, save we alone.”", "관 뚜껑의 나사를 풀고 우리 시술을 하고, 그런 다음 모두 제자리로 돌려놓아, 우리 둘 말고는 아무도 모르게 합시다.\""]], [["“But why do it at all? The girl is dead.", "\"하지만 도대체 왜 그런 일을 해야 하오? 그 처녀는 죽었소."], ["Why mutilate her poor body without need?", "무슨 필요로 그 가엾은 몸을 훼손한단 말이오?"], ["And if there is no necessity for a post-mortem and nothing to gain by it--no good to her, to us, to science, to human knowledge--why do it?", "부검의 필요도 없고 그것으로 얻을 것도 없다면 — 그녀에게도, 우리에게도, 과학에도, 인간의 지식에도 아무 이로움이 없다면 — 왜 하는 거요?"], ["Without such it is monstrous.”", "그런 까닭도 없이 한다면 그건 끔찍한 일이오.\""]], [["For answer he put his hand on my shoulder, and said, with infinite tenderness:--", "대답 대신 그는 내 어깨에 손을 얹고, 한없는 다정함을 담아 말했다."]], [["“Friend John, I pity your poor bleeding heart; and I love you the more because it does so bleed.", "\"벗 존, 나는 피 흘리는 당신의 가엾은 마음이 가엾소. 또 그렇게 피 흘리기에 더욱 당신을 사랑하오."], ["If I could, I would take on myself the burden that you do bear.", "할 수만 있다면 당신이 지고 있는 짐을 내가 떠맡고 싶소."], ["But there are things that you know not, but that you shall know, and bless me for knowing, though they are not pleasant things.", "그러나 당신이 알지 못하는 것들이 있고, 머지않아 당신은 그것을 알게 될 것이며, 비록 유쾌한 일들은 아니나, 알게 된 것을 두고 나를 축복하게 될 것이오."], ["John, my child, you have been my friend now many years, and yet did you ever know me to do any without good cause?", "존, 나의 아들 같은 이여, 당신은 이제 여러 해 동안 나의 벗이었는데, 내가 까닭 없이 무언가를 하는 것을 본 적이 있소?"], ["I may err--I am but man; but I believe in all I do.", "내가 틀릴 수도 있소 — 나도 사람일 뿐이니. 그러나 나는 내가 하는 모든 일을 믿소."], ["Was it not for these causes that you send for me when the great trouble came? Yes!", "큰 환난이 닥쳤을 때 당신이 나를 부른 것도 바로 이런 까닭에서가 아니었소? 그렇소!"], ["Were you not amazed, nay horrified, when I would not let Arthur kiss his love--though she was dying--and snatched him away by all my strength? Yes!", "내가 아서로 하여금 — 비록 그녀가 죽어가고 있었음에도 — 자기 연인에게 입 맞추지 못하게 하고 온 힘을 다해 그를 떼어냈을 때, 당신은 놀라지, 아니 질겁하지 않았소? 그렇소!"], ["And yet you saw how she thanked me, with her so beautiful dying eyes, her voice, too, so weak, and she kiss my rough old hand and bless me? Yes!", "그런데도 그녀가 그토록 아름답게 죽어가는 눈으로, 또 그토록 가냘픈 목소리로 어떻게 나에게 고마워했는지, 그리고 그녀가 내 거칠고 늙은 손에 입 맞추며 나를 축복했는지 당신은 보았소? 그렇소!"], ["And did you not hear me swear promise to her, that so she closed her eyes grateful? Yes!", "또 내가 그녀에게 약속을 맹세하는 것을, 그래서 그녀가 고마워하며 눈을 감은 것을 당신은 듣지 못했소? 그렇소!"]], [["“Well, I have good reason now for all I want to do.", "자, 이제 내가 하려는 모든 일에는 그럴 만한 이유가 있소."], ["You have for many years trust me; you have believe me weeks past, when there be things so strange that you might have well doubt.", "당신은 여러 해 동안 나를 믿어주었소. 지난 몇 주 동안, 충분히 의심할 만큼 기이한 일들이 있었을 때에도 당신은 나를 믿어주었소."], ["Believe me yet a little, friend John.", "조금만 더 나를 믿어주시오, 벗 존."], ["If you trust me not, then I must tell what I think; and that is not perhaps well.", "당신이 나를 믿어주지 않는다면, 나는 내가 생각하는 바를 말해야 하는데, 그것은 아마 좋지 않을 것이오."], ["And if I work--as work I shall, no matter trust or no trust--without my friend trust in me, I work with heavy heart and feel, oh! so lonely when I want all help and courage that may be!”", "그리고 내가 일을 한다면 — 믿든 안 믿든 나는 할 테지만 — 내 벗이 나를 믿어주지 않는 채로 일을 한다면, 나는 무거운 마음으로 일하며, 오, 온갖 도움과 용기가 다 필요한 그때에 너무나도 외로움을 느낄 것이오!\""], ["He paused a moment and went on solemnly: “Friend John, there are strange and terrible days before us.", "그는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 엄숙하게 이어갔다. \"벗 존, 우리 앞에는 기이하고 무서운 날들이 놓여 있소."], ["Let us not be two, but one, that so we work to a good end.", "우리 둘이 되지 말고 하나가 되어, 그렇게 좋은 결말을 향해 일합시다."], ["Will you not have faith in me?”", "나를 믿어주지 않겠소?\""]], [["I took his hand, and promised him.", "나는 그의 손을 잡고 그에게 약속했다."], ["I held my door open as he went away, and watched him go into his room and close the door.", "그가 나갈 때 나는 내 방문을 열어두고, 그가 자기 방으로 들어가 문을 닫는 것을 지켜보았다."], ["As I stood without moving, I saw one of the maids pass silently along the passage--she had her back towards me, so did not see me--and go into the room where Lucy lay.", "내가 꼼짝 않고 서 있을 때, 하녀 하나가 복도를 따라 소리 없이 지나가는 것을 보았다 — 그녀는 나에게 등을 돌리고 있어 나를 보지 못했다 — 그러고는 루시가 누워 있는 방으로 들어갔다."], ["The sight touched me.", "그 광경에 나는 마음이 뭉클했다."], ["Devotion is so rare, and we are so grateful to those who show it unasked to those we love. Here was a poor girl putting aside the terrors which she naturally had of death to go watch alone by the bier of the mistress whom she loved, so that the poor clay might not be lonely till laid to eternal rest....", "헌신이란 참으로 드문 것이며, 우리가 사랑하는 이에게 청하지도 않은 헌신을 베푸는 이들에게 우리는 그토록 고마운 마음을 품게 마련이다. 여기 한 가엾은 처녀가, 죽음에 대해 당연히 품게 마련인 두려움을 떨치고, 자기가 사랑하던 여주인의 관 곁을 홀로 지키러 가고 있었으니, 그 가엾은 흙덩이가 영원한 안식에 들 때까지 외롭지 않도록 하려는 것이었다……."]], [["I must have slept long and soundly, for it was broad daylight when Van Helsing waked me by coming into my room.", "내가 오래도록 깊이 잠들었던 모양이다. 판 헬싱이 내 방으로 들어와 나를 깨웠을 때는 이미 환한 대낮이었으니."], ["He came over to my bedside and said:--", "그는 내 침대맡으로 다가와 말했다."]], [["“You need not trouble about the knives; we shall not do it.”", "\"칼은 신경 쓰지 않아도 되오. 우리는 그 일을 하지 않을 거요.\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜요?\""], ["I asked. For his solemnity of the night before had greatly impressed me.", "하고 내가 물었다. 전날 밤 그의 엄숙함이 나에게 큰 인상을 남긴 까닭이었다."]], [["“Because,”", "\"왜냐하면,\""], ["he said sternly, “it is too late--or too early. See!”", "그가 침통하게 말했다, \"너무 늦었거나 — 아니면 너무 이르기 때문이오. 보시오!\""], ["Here he held up the little golden crucifix.", "여기서 그는 작은 금 십자고상을 들어 보였다."], ["“This was stolen in the night.”", "\"이것이 밤사이 도둑맞았소.\""]], [["“How, stolen,”", "\"도둑맞다니 어떻게요,\""], ["I asked in wonder, “since you have it now?”", "하고 내가 의아하여 물었다, \"지금 가지고 계시면서요?\""]], [["“Because I get it back from the worthless wretch who stole it, from the woman who robbed the dead and the living.", "\"그것을 훔친 못된 인간에게서, 죽은 자와 산 자를 함께 턴 그 여자에게서 도로 되찾았기 때문이오."], ["Her punishment will surely come, but not through me; she knew not altogether what she did and thus unknowing, she only stole.", "그녀에게는 반드시 벌이 내릴 것이나, 내 손을 거치지는 않을 것이오. 그녀는 자기가 무슨 짓을 하는지 온전히 알지 못했고, 그렇게 모른 채 그저 훔쳤을 뿐이오."], ["Now we must wait.”", "이제 우리는 기다려야 하오.\""]], [["He went away on the word, leaving me with a new mystery to think of, a new puzzle to grapple with.", "그는 그 말을 남기고 떠나, 나에게 곱씹어야 할 새로운 수수께끼, 씨름해야 할 새로운 난제를 남겼다."]], [["The forenoon was a dreary time, but at noon the solicitor came: Mr.", "오전나절은 음울한 시간이었으나, 정오에 변호사가 왔다."], ["Marquand, of Wholeman, Sons, Marquand & Lidderdale.", "홀먼·선스·마퀀드 앤드 리더데일 사(社)의 마퀀드 씨였다."], ["He was very genial and very appreciative of what we had done, and took off our hands all cares as to details.", "그는 매우 상냥했고 우리가 한 일을 무척 고맙게 여겼으며, 세부적인 일에 관한 온갖 수고를 우리 손에서 덜어주었다."], ["During lunch he told us that Mrs. Westenra had for some time expected sudden death from her heart, and had put her affairs in absolute order; he informed us that, with the exception of a certain entailed property of Lucy’s father’s which now, in default of direct issue, went back to a distant branch of the family, the whole estate, real and personal, was left absolutely to Arthur Holmwood.", "점심을 드는 동안 그는 웨스텐라 부인이 얼마 전부터 심장으로 인한 급사를 예감하여 자기 일을 완벽하게 정리해두었다고 말했다. 그는 우리에게, 직계 후손이 없어 이제 가문의 먼 분가로 되돌아가게 된 루시 아버지의 한정 상속 재산 일부를 제외하면, 부동산과 동산을 망라한 전 재산이 모조리 아서 홈우드에게 남겨졌다고 알려주었다."], ["When he had told us so much he went on:--", "그만큼 이야기하고 나서 그는 말을 이었다."]], [["“Frankly we did our best to prevent such a testamentary disposition, and pointed out certain contingencies that might leave her daughter either penniless or not so free as she should be to act regarding a matrimonial alliance.", "\"솔직히 우리는 그런 식의 유언 처분을 막으려고 최선을 다했고, 따님이 무일푼이 되거나 혼인 관계에 대해 마땅히 가져야 할 만큼 자유롭게 처신하지 못하게 될 수도 있는 몇 가지 사태를 짚어 보였습니다."], ["Indeed, we pressed the matter so far that we almost came into collision, for she asked us if we were or were not prepared to carry out her wishes.", "사실 우리가 이 문제를 너무 강하게 밀어붙여 거의 충돌할 뻔했는데, 부인께서 우리에게 자기 뜻을 받들 작정이냐 아니냐를 물으셨기 때문입니다."], ["Of course, we had then no alternative but to accept.", "물론 그때 우리로서는 받아들이는 것 말고 다른 도리가 없었지요."], ["We were right in principle, and ninety-nine times out of a hundred we should have proved, by the logic of events, the accuracy of our judgment.", "우리는 원칙적으로 옳았고, 백 번 중 아흔아홉 번은 사태가 흘러가는 논리로 보아 우리 판단의 정확함이 입증되었을 것입니다."], ["Frankly, however, I must admit that in this case any other form of disposition would have rendered impossible the carrying out of her wishes.", "그러나 솔직히, 이 경우에는 다른 어떤 형태의 처분도 부인의 뜻을 이루는 것을 불가능하게 만들었으리라는 점을 인정해야겠습니다."], ["For by her predeceasing her daughter the latter would have come into possession of the property, and, even had she only survived her mother by five minutes, her property would, in case there were no will--and a will was a practical impossibility in such a case--have been treated at her decease as under intestacy.", "왜냐하면 부인께서 따님보다 먼저 돌아가심으로써 따님이 그 재산을 소유하게 되었을 터인데, 설령 따님이 어머니보다 단 오 분을 더 살았다 하더라도, 유언이 없을 경우 — 이런 경우에 유언이란 사실상 불가능한 일이지요 — 따님이 사망하면 그 재산은 무유언 상속으로 처리되었을 것이기 때문입니다."], ["In which case Lord Godalming, though so dear a friend, would have had no claim in the world; and the inheritors, being remote, would not be likely to abandon their just rights, for sentimental reasons regarding an entire stranger.", "그렇게 되면 가달밍 경께서는, 비록 그토록 가까운 벗이었음에도, 세상 어디에도 권리를 주장할 수 없었을 것이고, 먼 친척인 상속인들은 생판 남에 대한 감상적인 이유로 자기들의 정당한 권리를 포기하지는 않았을 것입니다."], ["I assure you, my dear sirs, I am rejoiced at the result, perfectly rejoiced.”", "단언컨대, 선생님들, 저는 이 결과를 기뻐하고 있습니다, 더없이 기뻐하고 있어요.\""]], [["He was a good fellow, but his rejoicing at the one little part--in which he was officially interested--of so great a tragedy, was an object-lesson in the limitations of sympathetic understanding.", "그는 좋은 사람이었으나, 그토록 큰 비극 가운데 자기가 공적으로 관여한 그 작은 한 부분을 두고 기뻐하는 모습은, 공감적 이해의 한계를 보여주는 산 교훈이었다."]], [["He did not remain long, but said he would look in later in the day and see Lord Godalming.", "그는 오래 머물지 않고, 그날 오후 늦게 다시 들러 가달밍 경을 만나뵙겠다고 했다."], ["His coming, however, had been a certain comfort to us, since it assured us that we should not have to dread hostile criticism as to any of our acts.", "그러나 그가 다녀간 것은 우리에게 어느 정도 위안이 되었으니, 우리가 한 어떤 행위에 대해서도 적대적인 비난을 두려워할 필요가 없으리라는 확신을 주었기 때문이다."], ["Arthur was expected at five o’clock, so a little before that time we visited the death-chamber.", "아서는 다섯 시에 오기로 되어 있었으므로, 우리는 그보다 조금 전에 임종의 방을 찾았다."], ["It was so in very truth, for now both mother and daughter lay in it.", "그것은 참으로 임종의 방이었으니, 이제 어머니와 딸이 함께 그 안에 누워 있었기 때문이다."], ["The undertaker, true to his craft, had made the best display he could of his goods, and there was a mortuary air about the place that lowered our spirits at once.", "장의사는 자기 직분에 충실하여 자기 물품을 될 수 있는 한 잘 진열해놓았으나, 그곳에는 영안실 같은 분위기가 감돌아 우리 기운을 단번에 가라앉혔다."], ["Van Helsing ordered the former arrangement to be adhered to, explaining that, as Lord Godalming was coming very soon, it would be less harrowing to his feelings to see all that was left of his fiancée quite alone.", "판 헬싱은 전날의 배치를 그대로 유지하라고 지시하며, 가달밍 경이 곧 올 터인데 자기 약혼녀의 유해만 따로 보는 편이 그의 감정에 덜 괴로울 것이라고 설명했다."], ["The undertaker seemed shocked at his own stupidity and exerted himself to restore things to the condition in which we left them the night before, so that when Arthur came such shocks to his feelings as we could avoid were saved.", "장의사는 자기의 어리석음에 흠칫 놀란 듯 애써 우리가 전날 밤 떠날 때의 상태로 모든 것을 되돌려놓았고, 그리하여 아서가 왔을 때 우리가 막아줄 수 있는 그런 감정의 충격은 덜어졌다."]], [["Poor fellow!", "가엾은 친구!"], ["He looked desperately sad and broken; even his stalwart manhood seemed to have shrunk somewhat under the strain of his much-tried emotions.", "그는 절망적으로 슬프고 무너진 모습이었다. 그의 그 다부진 사내다움조차 숱하게 시달린 감정의 긴장 아래 다소 움츠러든 듯 보였다."], ["He had, I knew, been very genuinely and devotedly attached to his father; and to lose him, and at such a time, was a bitter blow to him.", "나는 그가 자기 아버지에게 참으로 진실하고 헌신적으로 정을 쏟았음을 알고 있었으니, 그를, 그것도 이런 때에 잃은 것은 그에게 쓰라린 타격이었다."], ["With me he was warm as ever, and to Van Helsing he was sweetly courteous; but I could not help seeing that there was some constraint with him.", "그는 나에게 여전히 따뜻했고, 판 헬싱에게는 다정하고 정중했다. 그러나 나는 그에게 어떤 거북함이 있는 것을 보지 않을 수 없었다."], ["The Professor noticed it, too, and motioned me to bring him upstairs.", "교수도 그것을 알아차리고, 나에게 그를 위층으로 데려가라는 몸짓을 했다."], ["I did so, and left him at the door of the room, as I felt he would like to be quite alone with her, but he took my arm and led me in, saying huskily:--", "나는 그렇게 하여, 그가 그녀와 온전히 단둘이 있고 싶어하리라 여겨 방문 앞에서 그를 두고 가려 했으나, 그는 내 팔을 잡고 나를 안으로 이끌며 쉰 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“You loved her too, old fellow; she told me all about it, and there was no friend had a closer place in her heart than you.", "\"자네도 그 사람을 사랑했지, 친구. 그 사람이 나에게 다 이야기해줬어. 자네보다 그 사람 마음에 더 가까이 자리한 벗은 없었네."], ["I don’t know how to thank you for all you have done for her.", "그 사람을 위해 자네가 해준 모든 일을 어떻게 고마워해야 할지 모르겠어."], ["I can’t think yet....”", "아직 생각이 안 돼…….\""]], [["Here he suddenly broke down, and threw his arms round my shoulders and laid his head on my breast, crying:--", "여기서 그는 갑자기 무너져 내려, 두 팔로 내 어깨를 끌어안고 내 가슴에 머리를 묻은 채 울부짖었다."]], [["“Oh, Jack! Jack!", "\"오, 잭! 잭!"], ["What shall I do!", "난 어쩌면 좋아!"], ["The whole of life seems gone from me all at once, and there is nothing in the wide world for me to live for.”", "온 삶이 한꺼번에 나에게서 사라져버린 것 같고, 이 넓은 세상에 내가 살아갈 이유가 아무것도 없어.\""]], [["I comforted him as well as I could.", "나는 할 수 있는 한 그를 위로했다."], ["In such cases men do not need much expression.", "이런 경우에 사내들은 많은 말이 필요치 않다."], ["A grip of the hand, the tightening of an arm over the shoulder, a sob in unison, are expressions of sympathy dear to a man’s heart.", "손을 한 번 꽉 쥐어주는 것, 어깨를 감싼 팔에 힘을 주는 것, 함께 흐느끼는 것이 사내의 마음에 소중한 공감의 표현이다."], ["I stood still and silent till his sobs died away, and then I said softly to him:--", "나는 그의 흐느낌이 잦아들 때까지 꼼짝 않고 잠자코 서 있다가, 부드럽게 그에게 말했다."]], [["“Come and look at her.”", "\"가서 그 사람을 보게.\""]], [["Together we moved over to the bed, and I lifted the lawn from her face. God!", "우리는 함께 침대로 다가갔고, 나는 그녀의 얼굴에서 고운 천을 걷어냈다. 세상에!"], ["how beautiful she was.", "그녀는 어찌나 아름다웠던지."], ["Every hour seemed to be enhancing her loveliness.", "시간이 흐를수록 그녀의 사랑스러움이 더해가는 듯했다."], ["It frightened and amazed me somewhat; and as for Arthur, he fell a-trembling, and finally was shaken with doubt as with an ague.", "그것은 나를 다소 두렵게도, 놀랍게도 했다. 아서로 말하자면, 그는 부들부들 떨기 시작하더니 마침내 학질에 걸린 듯 의혹으로 몸을 떨었다."], ["At last, after a long pause, he said to me in a faint whisper:--", "마침내 그는 긴 침묵 끝에 희미한 속삭임으로 나에게 말했다."]], [["“Jack, is she really dead?”", "\"잭, 그 사람 정말 죽은 건가?\""]], [["I assured him sadly that it was so, and went on to suggest--for I felt that such a horrible doubt should not have life for a moment longer than I could help--that it often happened that after death faces became softened and even resolved into their youthful beauty; that this was especially so when death had been preceded by any acute or prolonged suffering.", "나는 슬프게 그렇다고 단언하고는 — 그런 끔찍한 의혹은 내가 막을 수 있는 한 한순간이라도 더 살려두어서는 안 된다고 느꼈으므로 — 죽은 뒤에 얼굴이 누그러지고 심지어 젊은 시절의 아름다움으로 되돌아가는 일이 흔히 있으며, 죽음에 앞서 격심하거나 오래 끄는 고통이 있었던 경우에는 특히 그러하다는 점을 일러주었다."], ["It seemed to quite do away with any doubt, and, after kneeling beside the couch for a while and looking at her lovingly and long, he turned aside.", "그것으로 의혹이 깨끗이 가신 듯했고, 그는 한동안 침상 곁에 무릎 꿇고 앉아 사랑스럽게, 오래도록 그녀를 바라본 뒤 몸을 돌렸다."], ["I told him that that must be good-bye, as the coffin had to be prepared; so he went back and took her dead hand in his and kissed it, and bent over and kissed her forehead.", "나는 관을 마련해야 하니 이것이 작별이 되어야 한다고 그에게 말했다. 그러자 그는 다시 가서 그녀의 죽은 손을 자기 손에 쥐고 입 맞추고는, 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 이마에 입 맞추었다."], ["He came away, fondly looking back over his shoulder at her as he came.", "그는 물러나오면서 어깨 너머로 애틋하게 그녀를 돌아보았다."]], [["I left him in the drawing-room, and told Van Helsing that he had said good-bye; so the latter went to the kitchen to tell the undertaker’s men to proceed with the preparations and to screw up the coffin.", "나는 그를 응접실에 남겨두고, 판 헬싱에게 그가 작별을 고했다고 알렸다. 그러자 판 헬싱은 부엌으로 가서 장의사 일꾼들에게 채비를 진행하여 관 뚜껑의 나사를 죄라고 일렀다."], ["When he came out of the room again I told him of Arthur’s question, and he replied:--", "그가 다시 방에서 나왔을 때 나는 그에게 아서의 물음을 전했고, 그는 답했다."]], [["“I am not surprised.", "\"놀랍지 않소."], ["Just now I doubted for a moment myself!”", "방금 나조차 한순간 의심했으니!\""]], [["We all dined together, and I could see that poor Art was trying to make the best of things.", "우리는 다 함께 저녁을 들었고, 가엾은 아서가 애써 마음을 다잡으려 하는 것을 나는 알 수 있었다."], ["Van Helsing had been silent all dinner-time; but when we had lit our cigars he said--", "판 헬싱은 저녁 내내 말이 없었으나, 우리가 시가에 불을 붙였을 때 그가 말했다."]], [["“Lord----”; but Arthur interrupted him:--", "\"경(卿)…….\" 그러나 아서가 그의 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“No, no, not that, for God’s sake! not yet at any rate.", "\"아뇨, 아니에요, 제발 그렇게 부르지 마세요! 적어도 아직은요."], ["Forgive me, sir: I did not mean to speak offensively; it is only because my loss is so recent.”", "용서하세요, 선생님. 무례하게 굴려던 건 아닙니다. 다만 제가 입은 상실이 너무도 최근의 일이라서요.\""]], [["The Professor answered very sweetly:--", "교수가 매우 다정하게 답했다."]], [["“I only used that name because I was in doubt.", "\"내가 그 이름을 쓴 것은 그저 어찌 불러야 할지 몰라서였소."], ["I must not call you ‘Mr.,’ and I have grown to love you--yes, my dear boy, to love you--as Arthur.”", "'미스터'라고 부를 수는 없고, 또 나는 당신을 사랑하게 되었으니 말이오 — 그렇소, 친애하는 청년이여, 아서로서 당신을 사랑하게 되었소.\""]], [["Arthur held out his hand, and took the old man’s warmly.", "아서는 손을 내밀어 그 노인의 손을 따뜻하게 잡았다."]], [["“Call me what you will,”", "\"마음대로 불러주세요,\""], ["he said. “I hope I may always have the title of a friend.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"저는 늘 벗이라는 칭호를 누리고 싶습니다."], ["And let me say that I am at a loss for words to thank you for your goodness to my poor dear.”", "그리고 제 가엾은 연인에게 베풀어주신 선생님의 호의에 어떻게 감사를 드려야 할지 말을 찾을 수가 없다는 것을 말씀드리고 싶어요.\" 그는 잠시 말을 멈추었다가 이어갔다."], ["He paused a moment, and went on: “I know that she understood your goodness even better than I do; and if I was rude or in any way wanting at that time you acted so--you remember”--the Professor nodded--“you must forgive me.”", "\"그 사람은 저보다도 더 잘 선생님의 호의를 헤아렸을 거라는 걸 압니다. 그리고 그때 제가 선생님께서 그렇게 하실 적에 무례했거나 어떤 식으로든 모자랐다면 — 기억하시지요\" — 교수가 고개를 끄덕였다 — \"용서해주셔야 합니다.\""]], [["He answered with a grave kindness:--", "그가 침통한 다정함으로 답했다."]], [["“I know it was hard for you to quite trust me then, for to trust such violence needs to understand; and I take it that you do not--that you cannot--trust me now, for you do not yet understand.", "\"그때 당신이 나를 온전히 믿기란 어려운 일이었음을 나도 아오. 그런 난폭함을 믿으려면 그것을 이해해야 하는데, 짐작건대 당신은 지금도 나를 믿지 못하고 — 믿을 수 없고 — 있소, 아직 이해하지 못하기 때문이오."], ["And there may be more times when I shall want you to trust when you cannot--and may not--and must not yet understand.", "그리고 당신이 이해할 수 없을 때 — 이해해서는 안 될 때 — 그런데도 내가 당신에게 믿어달라고 할 때가 앞으로 또 있을지 모르오."], ["But the time will come when your trust shall be whole and complete in me, and when you shall understand as though the sunlight himself shone through.", "그러나 당신의 믿음이 나에게 온전하고 완전해질 때, 햇빛 그 자체가 환히 비치듯 당신이 이해하게 될 때가 올 것이오."], ["Then you shall bless me from first to last for your own sake, and for the sake of others and for her dear sake to whom I swore to protect.”", "그때에는 당신이 처음부터 끝까지 나를 축복하게 될 것이니, 당신 자신을 위해, 그리고 다른 이들을 위해, 또 내가 지키기로 맹세한 그 가여운 이를 위해서 말이오.\""]], [["“And, indeed, indeed, sir,”", "\"그리고, 정말로, 정말이지, 선생님,\""], ["said Arthur warmly, “I shall in all ways trust you.", "하고 아서가 따뜻하게 말했다, \"저는 모든 면에서 선생님을 믿겠습니다."], ["I know and believe you have a very noble heart, and you are Jack’s friend, and you were hers.", "선생님께서 매우 고귀한 마음을 지니셨다는 것을 알고 또 믿으며, 선생님은 잭의 벗이고, 또 그 사람의 벗이셨으니까요."], ["You shall do what you like.”", "원하시는 대로 하셔도 됩니다.\""]], [["The Professor cleared his throat a couple of times, as though about to speak, and finally said:--", "교수는 무슨 말을 하려는 듯 두어 번 목을 가다듬더니, 마침내 입을 열었다."]], [["“May I ask you something now?”", "\"이제 한 가지 물어봐도 되겠소?\""]], [["“Certainly.”", "\"물론입니다.\""]], [["“You know that Mrs. Westenra left you all her property?”", "\"웨스텐라 부인이 당신에게 전 재산을 남겼다는 것을 아시오?\""]], [["“No, poor dear; I never thought of it.”", "\"아뇨, 가엾은 분. 그건 생각조차 못 했습니다.\""]], [["“And as it is all yours, you have a right to deal with it as you will.", "\"그러니 그것이 모두 당신 것이므로, 당신은 그것을 마음대로 처리할 권리가 있소."], ["I want you to give me permission to read all Miss Lucy’s papers and letters.", "나는 당신이 루시 양의 모든 서류와 편지를 내가 읽을 수 있도록 허락해주기를 바라오."], ["Believe me, it is no idle curiosity.", "믿어주시오, 이것은 한가한 호기심이 아니오."], ["I have a motive of which, be sure, she would have approved.", "나에게는 동기가 있고, 분명히 그녀라면 그것을 인정했을 것이오."], ["I have them all here. I took them before we knew that all was yours, so that no strange hand might touch them--no strange eye look through words into her soul.", "나는 그것들을 모두 여기 가지고 있소. 모든 것이 당신 것임을 알기 전에 내가 그것들을 거두어두었으니, 낯선 손이 그것들을 만지지 못하도록 — 낯선 눈이 그 글을 꿰뚫어 그녀의 영혼을 들여다보지 못하도록 말이오."], ["I shall keep them, if I may; even you may not see them yet, but I shall keep them safe.", "괜찮다면 내가 그것들을 간직하겠소. 당신조차 아직은 그것들을 보아서는 안 되나, 내가 안전하게 보관하겠소."], ["No word shall be lost; and in the good time I shall give them back to you.", "한마디도 잃어버리는 일이 없을 것이고, 때가 무르익으면 당신에게 돌려주겠소."], ["It’s a hard thing I ask, but you will do it, will you not, for Lucy’s sake?”", "어려운 부탁이긴 하나, 들어주겠지요, 루시를 위해서?\""]], [["Arthur spoke out heartily, like his old self:--", "아서가 옛 모습 그대로, 진심을 담아 또렷이 말했다."]], [["“Dr. Van Helsing, you may do what you will.", "\"판 헬싱 박사님, 원하시는 대로 하셔도 됩니다."], ["I feel that in saying this I am doing what my dear one would have approved.", "이렇게 말씀드리는 것이 제 사랑하는 이가 인정했을 일을 하는 것이라고 느낍니다."], ["I shall not trouble you with questions till the time comes.”", "때가 올 때까지 저는 질문으로 선생님을 성가시게 하지 않겠습니다.\""]], [["The old Professor stood up as he said solemnly:--", "늙은 교수가 자리에서 일어서며 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“And you are right.", "\"당신 말이 옳소."], ["There will be pain for us all; but it will not be all pain, nor will this pain be the last.", "우리 모두에게 고통이 있을 것이오. 그러나 온통 고통만은 아닐 것이며, 이 고통이 마지막도 아닐 것이오."], ["We and you too--you most of all, my dear boy--will have to pass through the bitter water before we reach the sweet.", "우리도, 당신도 — 누구보다도 당신이, 친애하는 청년이여 — 단 물에 이르기 전에 쓴 물을 건너야 할 것이오."], ["But we must be brave of heart and unselfish, and do our duty, and all will be well!”", "그러나 우리는 마음이 용감하고 사심이 없어야 하며, 우리 의무를 다해야 하오. 그러면 모든 것이 잘될 것이오!\""]], [["I slept on a sofa in Arthur’s room that night.", "그날 밤 나는 아서의 방에 있는 소파에서 잤다."], ["Van Helsing did not go to bed at all.", "판 헬싱은 도무지 잠자리에 들지 않았다."], ["He went to and fro, as if patrolling the house, and was never out of sight of the room where Lucy lay in her coffin, strewn with the wild garlic flowers, which sent, through the odour of lily and rose, a heavy, overpowering smell into the night.", "그는 집안을 순찰하듯 이리저리 오갔고, 루시가 야생 마늘꽃을 흩뿌린 채 관 속에 누워 있는 방에서 한시도 눈을 떼지 않았다. 그 마늘꽃은 백합과 장미의 향기를 뚫고, 무겁고 압도적인 냄새를 밤 속으로 내뿜고 있었다."]], [["Mina Harker’s Journal.", "「미나 하커의 일기」"]], [["22 September.--In the train to Exeter.", "9월 22일. — 엑서터로 가는 기차 안."], ["Jonathan sleeping.", "조너선은 자고 있다."]], [["It seems only yesterday that the last entry was made, and yet how much between then, in Whitby and all the world before me, Jonathan away and no news of him; and now, married to Jonathan, Jonathan a solicitor, a partner, rich, master of his business, Mr.", "지난번 기록을 한 것이 바로 어제 같은데, 그 사이에 얼마나 많은 일이 있었던가. 그때는 휘트비에서, 온 세상이 내 앞에 펼쳐져 있었고, 조너선은 멀리 떠나 소식조차 없었다. 그런데 이제는 조너선과 결혼했고, 조너선은 변호사이자 공동 경영자가 되어 부유해졌으며, 자기 사업의 주인이 되었고, 호킨스 씨는 돌아가셔서 땅에 묻혔으며, 조너선은 그를 해칠지도 모를 또 한 차례의 발작을 겪었다."], ["Hawkins dead and buried, and Jonathan with another attack that may harm him. Some day he may ask me about it.", "언젠가 그가 나에게 그 일에 대해 물을지도 모른다."], ["Down it all goes.", "모두 적어둔다."], ["I am rusty in my shorthand--see what unexpected prosperity does for us--so it may be as well to freshen it up again with an exercise anyhow....", "내 속기 솜씨가 녹슬어버렸으니 — 뜻밖의 번영이 우리에게 무슨 짓을 하는지 보라 — 어쨌든 연습 삼아 다시 다듬어두는 것도 좋겠다……."]], [["The service was very simple and very solemn.", "장례식은 매우 소박하고 매우 엄숙했다."], ["There were only ourselves and the servants there, one or two old friends of his from Exeter, his London agent, and a gentleman representing Sir John Paxton, the President of the Incorporated Law Society.", "그 자리에는 우리와 하인들, 엑서터에서 온 그의 옛 친구 한둘, 그의 런던 대리인, 그리고 법학협회 회장인 존 팩스턴 경을 대리하는 한 신사가 있을 뿐이었다."], ["Jonathan and I stood hand in hand, and we felt that our best and dearest friend was gone from us....", "조너선과 나는 손을 맞잡고 서서, 우리의 가장 좋고 가장 소중한 벗이 우리 곁을 떠났음을 느꼈다……."]], [["We came back to town quietly, taking a ’bus to Hyde Park Corner.", "우리는 조용히 도시로 돌아와, 하이드 파크 코너까지 합승마차를 탔다."], ["Jonathan thought it would interest me to go into the Row for a while, so we sat down; but there were very few people there, and it was sad-looking and desolate to see so many empty chairs.", "조너선은 잠시 로(Row)에 들어가 보면 내가 흥미로워하리라 생각하여, 우리는 그곳에 앉았다. 그러나 사람이 거의 없어서, 그 많은 빈 의자들을 보는 것이 쓸쓸하고 황량했다."], ["It made us think of the empty chair at home; so we got up and walked down Piccadilly.", "그것이 우리에게 집의 빈 의자를 떠올리게 했으므로, 우리는 일어나 피커딜리를 따라 걸어 내려갔다."], ["Jonathan was holding me by the arm, the way he used to in old days before I went to school.", "조너선은 내가 학교에 들어가기 전 옛날에 그랬던 식으로 내 팔을 잡고 있었다."], ["I felt it very improper, for you can’t go on for some years teaching etiquette and decorum to other girls without the pedantry of it biting into yourself a bit; but it was Jonathan, and he was my husband, and we didn’t know anybody who saw us--and we didn’t care if they did--so on we walked.", "나는 그것이 무척 온당치 못하다고 느꼈으니, 다른 처녀들에게 몇 해씩 예법과 품위를 가르치다 보면 그 까다로움이 자기 안에도 조금은 배어들게 마련이기 때문이다. 그러나 그는 조너선이었고, 내 남편이었으며, 우리를 보는 사람 가운데 아는 이도 없었으므로 — 또 본들 우리는 개의치 않았으므로 — 우리는 계속 걸었다."], ["I was looking at a very beautiful girl, in a big cart-wheel hat, sitting in a victoria outside Guiliano’s, when I felt Jonathan clutch my arm so tight that he hurt me, and he said under his breath: “My God!”", "줄리아노 상점 앞 사륜마차에 앉아 있는, 커다란 수레바퀴 같은 모자를 쓴 무척 아름다운 처녀를 바라보고 있을 때, 조너선이 어찌나 세게 내 팔을 움켜쥐는지 아플 지경이었고, 그가 숨죽여 말했다. \"맙소사!\""], ["I am always anxious about Jonathan, for I fear that some nervous fit may upset him again; so I turned to him quickly, and asked him what it was that disturbed him.", "나는 조너선이 늘 걱정스러우니, 어떤 신경 발작이 그를 다시 흔들어놓을까 두렵기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 얼른 그를 돌아보며 무엇이 그를 어지럽혔는지 물었다."]], [["He was very pale, and his eyes seemed bulging out as, half in terror and half in amazement, he gazed at a tall, thin man, with a beaky nose and black moustache and pointed beard, who was also observing the pretty girl.", "그는 몹시 창백했고, 반쯤은 공포로 반쯤은 경악으로, 부리 같은 코에 검은 콧수염과 뾰족한 턱수염을 기른 키 크고 마른 사내를 응시하는 그의 두 눈이 튀어나올 듯했다. 그 사내 역시 그 예쁜 처녀를 지켜보고 있었다."], ["He was looking at her so hard that he did not see either of us, and so I had a good view of him.", "그는 어찌나 뚫어지게 그녀를 바라보던지 우리 둘 중 누구도 보지 못했고, 그래서 나는 그를 똑똑히 볼 수 있었다."], ["His face was not a good face; it was hard, and cruel, and sensual, and his big white teeth, that looked all the whiter because his lips were so red, were pointed like an animal’s.", "그의 얼굴은 좋은 얼굴이 아니었다. 굳고, 잔인하고, 음탕했으며, 입술이 어찌나 붉은지 더욱 희게 보이는 큼직한 흰 이는 짐승의 것처럼 뾰족했다."], ["Jonathan kept staring at him, till I was afraid he would notice.", "조너선이 어찌나 그를 빤히 응시하던지, 나는 그가 알아챌까 봐 두려웠다."], ["I feared he might take it ill, he looked so fierce and nasty.", "그가 그토록 사납고 험상궂어 보여서, 그것을 언짢게 여기지나 않을까 겁이 났다."], ["I asked Jonathan why he was disturbed, and he answered, evidently thinking that I knew as much about it as he did: “Do you see who it is?”", "나는 조너선에게 왜 그렇게 동요하는지 물었고, 그는 마치 내가 자기만큼이나 그 일을 다 알고 있다고 여기는 듯 답했다. \"저자가 누군지 보이오?\""]], [["“No, dear,”", "\"아뇨, 여보,\""], ["I said; “I don’t know him; who is it?”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"저 사람을 모르겠어요."], ["His answer seemed to shock and thrill me, for it was said as if he did not know that it was to me, Mina, to whom he was speaking:--", "누구예요?\" 그의 대답은 나를 충격에 빠뜨리고 전율케 하는 듯했으니, 마치 자기가 말을 건네는 상대가 나, 미나라는 것을 모르는 듯이 한 말이었기 때문이다."]], [["“It is the man himself!”", "\"바로 그자요!\""]], [["The poor dear was evidently terrified at something--very greatly terrified; I do believe that if he had not had me to lean on and to support him he would have sunk down.", "가엾은 사람은 무언가에 분명 겁에 질려 있었다 — 몹시도 겁에 질려 있었다. 정말이지 그가 기댈, 그리고 그를 떠받칠 내가 곁에 없었더라면 그는 주저앉고 말았으리라고 나는 믿는다."], ["He kept staring; a man came out of the shop with a small parcel, and gave it to the lady, who then drove off.", "그는 줄곧 응시했다. 한 사내가 작은 꾸러미를 들고 가게에서 나와 그것을 그 부인에게 건네자, 부인은 마차를 몰고 떠났다."], ["The dark man kept his eyes fixed on her, and when the carriage moved up Piccadilly he followed in the same direction, and hailed a hansom.", "그 검은 사내는 그녀에게서 눈을 떼지 않았고, 마차가 피커딜리를 따라 올라가자 같은 방향으로 뒤따라가 이륜마차를 불러 세웠다."], ["Jonathan kept looking after him, and said, as if to himself:--", "조너선은 줄곧 그를 눈으로 좇으며, 혼잣말하듯 말했다."]], [["“I believe it is the Count, but he has grown young.", "\"저자는 백작인 것 같소, 그런데 젊어졌소."], ["My God, if this be so!", "맙소사, 만일 그렇다면!"], ["Oh, my God! my God!", "오, 맙소사! 맙소사!"], ["If I only knew!", "알기만 한다면!"], ["if I only knew!”", "알기만 한다면!\""], ["He was distressing himself so much that I feared to keep his mind on the subject by asking him any questions, so I remained silent.", "그가 어찌나 스스로를 괴롭히는지, 나는 무슨 질문이라도 하여 그의 마음을 그 일에 붙들어둘까 두려워 잠자코 있었다."], ["I drew him away quietly, and he, holding my arm, came easily.", "나는 조용히 그를 이끌었고, 그는 내 팔을 잡고 순순히 따라왔다."], ["We walked a little further, and then went in and sat for a while in the Green Park.", "우리는 조금 더 걷다가 그린 파크로 들어가 한동안 앉아 있었다."], ["It was a hot day for autumn, and there was a comfortable seat in a shady place.", "가을치고는 더운 날이었고, 그늘진 곳에 편안한 자리가 있었다."], ["After a few minutes’ staring at nothing, Jonathan’s eyes closed, and he went quietly into a sleep, with his head on my shoulder.", "몇 분간 허공을 응시하던 조너선의 눈이 감기더니, 그는 내 어깨에 머리를 기댄 채 조용히 잠으로 빠져들었다."], ["I thought it was the best thing for him, so did not disturb him.", "나는 그것이 그에게 가장 좋은 일이라 여겨 깨우지 않았다."], ["In about twenty minutes he woke up, and said to me quite cheerfully:--", "이십 분쯤 지나 그는 깨어나 매우 명랑하게 나에게 말했다."]], [["“Why, Mina, have I been asleep!", "\"아니, 미나, 내가 잠들었던 거요!"], ["Oh, do forgive me for being so rude.", "오, 이렇게 무례해서 미안하오."], ["Come, and we’ll have a cup of tea somewhere.”", "자, 어디서 차나 한 잔 합시다.\""], ["He had evidently forgotten all about the dark stranger, as in his illness he had forgotten all that this episode had reminded him of.", "그는 자기 병중에 이 일이 떠올리게 한 모든 것을 잊어버렸던 것처럼, 그 검은 낯선 자에 대한 일을 까맣게 잊은 것이 분명했다."], ["I don’t like this lapsing into forgetfulness; it may make or continue some injury to the brain.", "나는 이렇게 망각으로 빠져드는 것이 마음에 들지 않으니, 뇌에 어떤 손상을 일으키거나 그것을 지속시킬지도 모르기 때문이다."], ["I must not ask him, for fear I shall do more harm than good; but I must somehow learn the facts of his journey abroad.", "나는 그에게 물어서는 안 되겠으니, 이로움보다 해를 더 끼칠까 두려운 까닭이다. 그러나 나는 어떻게든 그의 해외 여정에 관한 사실을 알아내야만 한다."], ["The time is come, I fear, when I must open that parcel, and know what is written.", "두렵지만, 이제 그 꾸러미를 열어 거기 무엇이 쓰여 있는지 알아야 할 때가 온 듯하다."], ["Oh, Jonathan, you will, I know, forgive me if I do wrong, but it is for your own dear sake.", "오, 조너선, 내가 잘못을 저지른다면 당신은 나를 용서하리라는 걸 알아요. 그러나 이것은 다 당신을 위한 거예요."]], [["Later.--A sad home-coming in every way--the house empty of the dear soul who was so good to us; Jonathan still pale and dizzy under a slight relapse of his malady; and now a telegram from Van Helsing, whoever he may be:--", "나중에. — 모든 면에서 슬픈 귀가였다 — 우리에게 그토록 잘해주었던 그 소중한 분이 떠나 텅 빈 집, 가벼운 병의 재발로 여전히 창백하고 어지러워하는 조너선. 그리고 이제 판 헬싱이라는 — 그가 누구인지는 모르나 — 사람으로부터 온 전보 한 통."]], [["“You will be grieved to hear that Mrs. Westenra died five days ago, and that Lucy died the day before yesterday.", "\"웨스텐라 부인이 닷새 전에, 그리고 루시가 그저께 세상을 떠났다는 소식을 들으시면 비통하실 것입니다."], ["They were both buried to-day.”", "두 분 다 오늘 묻혔습니다.\""]], [["Oh, what a wealth of sorrow in a few words!", "오, 몇 마디 말 속에 담긴 이 풍성한 슬픔이여!"], ["Poor Mrs. Westenra!", "가엾은 웨스텐라 부인!"], ["poor Lucy!", "가엾은 루시!"], ["Gone, gone, never to return to us! And poor, poor Arthur, to have lost such sweetness out of his life!", "가버렸구나, 가버렸어, 다시는 우리에게 돌아오지 못하리! 그리고 가엾고 가엾은 아서, 자기 삶에서 그토록 고운 존재를 잃다니!"], ["God help us all to bear our troubles.", "우리 모두가 우리의 시련을 견딜 수 있도록 신이여 도우소서."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["22 September.--It is all over.", "9월 22일. — 모두 끝났다."], ["Arthur has gone back to Ring, and has taken Quincey Morris with him.", "아서는 링으로 돌아갔고, 퀸시 모리스를 데리고 갔다."], ["What a fine fellow is Quincey!", "퀸시는 어찌나 훌륭한 사내인지!"], ["I believe in my heart of hearts that he suffered as much about Lucy’s death as any of us; but he bore himself through it like a moral Viking.", "나는 진심으로, 그가 루시의 죽음에 대해 우리 가운데 누구 못지않게 괴로워했다고 믿는다. 그러나 그는 그것을 도덕적인 바이킹처럼 의연히 견뎌냈다."], ["If America can go on breeding men like that, she will be a power in the world indeed.", "미국이 그런 사내들을 계속 길러낼 수 있다면, 그 나라는 참으로 세계의 강국이 될 것이다."], ["Van Helsing is lying down, having a rest preparatory to his journey.", "판 헬싱은 여정에 앞서 휴식을 취하느라 누워 있다."], ["He goes over to Amsterdam to-night, but says he returns to-morrow night; that he only wants to make some arrangements which can only be made personally.", "그는 오늘 밤 암스테르담으로 건너가지만, 내일 밤에는 돌아온다고 한다. 직접 가서야만 할 수 있는 몇 가지 처리를 하려는 것뿐이라고 했다."], ["He is to stop with me then, if he can; he says he has work to do in London which may take him some time.", "그런 뒤에는 가능하다면 나와 함께 머물 것이라고 했다. 런던에서 할 일이 있는데 시간이 좀 걸릴지도 모른다고 했다."], ["Poor old fellow!", "가엾은 노인!"], ["I fear that the strain of the past week has broken down even his iron strength.", "나는 지난 한 주의 긴장이 그의 무쇠 같은 힘마저 꺾어놓았을까 두렵다."], ["All the time of the burial he was, I could see, putting some terrible restraint on himself.", "매장하는 내내 그가 자기 자신에게 어떤 끔찍한 억제를 가하고 있는 것을 나는 볼 수 있었다."], ["When it was all over, we were standing beside Arthur, who, poor fellow, was speaking of his part in the operation where his blood had been transfused to his Lucy’s veins; I could see Van Helsing’s face grow white and purple by turns.", "모든 것이 끝났을 때, 우리는 아서 곁에 서 있었는데, 그 가엾은 친구는 자기 피가 루시의 혈관으로 수혈되었던 그 시술에서 자기가 한 역할에 대해 이야기하고 있었다. 나는 판 헬싱의 얼굴이 번갈아 하얘졌다 보랏빛이 되었다 하는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Arthur was saying that he felt since then as if they two had been really married and that she was his wife in the sight of God.", "아서는 그때 이후로 자기들 둘이 정말로 결혼한 것 같고 신의 눈에 그녀가 자기 아내인 것 같다고 말하고 있었다."], ["None of us said a word of the other operations, and none of us ever shall. Arthur and Quincey went away together to the station, and Van Helsing and I came on here.", "우리 가운데 누구도 다른 수혈들에 대해서는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 우리 누구도 결코 하지 않을 것이다. 아서와 퀸시는 함께 역으로 떠났고, 판 헬싱과 나는 이곳으로 왔다."], ["The moment we were alone in the carriage he gave way to a regular fit of hysterics.", "마차 안에 단둘이 되자마자 그는 영락없는 히스테리 발작에 굴복하고 말았다."], ["He has denied to me since that it was hysterics, and insisted that it was only his sense of humour asserting itself under very terrible conditions.", "그 뒤로 그는 나에게 그것이 히스테리였음을 부인하며, 그저 매우 끔찍한 상황 아래 자기 유머 감각이 고개를 든 것뿐이었다고 우겼다."], ["He laughed till he cried, and I had to draw down the blinds lest any one should see us and misjudge; and then he cried, till he laughed again; and laughed and cried together, just as a woman does.", "그는 눈물이 날 때까지 웃었고, 나는 누군가 우리를 보고 오해할까 봐 차양을 내려야 했다. 그러다가 그는 다시 웃을 때까지 울었고, 마치 여자가 그러듯 웃고 우는 것을 한꺼번에 했다."], ["I tried to be stern with him, as one is to a woman under the circumstances; but it had no effect.", "나는 이런 상황에서 여자를 대하듯 그에게 엄하게 대하려 했으나, 아무 소용이 없었다."], ["Men and women are so different in manifestations of nervous strength or weakness!", "신경의 강함이나 약함을 드러내는 방식에서 남자와 여자는 어찌나 다른지!"], ["Then when his face grew grave and stern again I asked him why his mirth, and why at such a time.", "그러다가 그의 얼굴이 다시 침통하고 엄숙해졌을 때 나는 그에게 무엇 때문에 그렇게 즐거워하는지, 또 왜 하필 이런 때에 그러는지 물었다."], ["His reply was in a way characteristic of him, for it was logical and forceful and mysterious.", "그의 대답은 어떤 면에서 그다운 것이었으니, 논리적이고 강력하며 알 수 없는 것이었기 때문이다."], ["He said:--", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Ah, you don’t comprehend, friend John.", "\"아, 당신은 이해하지 못하오, 벗 존."], ["Do not think that I am not sad, though I laugh.", "내가 웃는다고 해서 슬프지 않다고는 생각지 마시오."], ["See, I have cried even when the laugh did choke me.", "보시오, 웃음이 나를 목 메이게 할 때조차 나는 울었소."], ["But no more think that I am all sorry when I cry, for the laugh he come just the same.", "그러나 내가 울 때 온통 슬프기만 하다고도 더는 생각지 마시오. 웃음이란 놈은 마찬가지로 찾아오니 말이오."], ["Keep it always with you that laughter who knock at your door and say, ‘May I come in?’ is not the true laughter. No!", "당신 문을 두드리며 '들어가도 될까요?' 하고 말하는 웃음은 진정한 웃음이 아니라는 것을 늘 명심하시오. 아니오! 그놈은 왕이라서, 자기가 원할 때 원하는 식으로 찾아오오."], ["he is a king, and he come when and how he like.", "그놈은 누구에게도 청하지 않고, 알맞은 때를 고르지도 않소."], ["He ask no person; he choose no time of suitability.", "그놈은 그저 '나 여기 있소' 하고 말하오."], ["He say, ‘I am here.’ Behold, in example I grieve my heart out for that so sweet young girl; I give my blood for her, though I am old and worn; I give my time, my skill, my sleep; I let my other sufferers want that so she may have all.", "보시오, 예를 들면 나는 그토록 사랑스러운 젊은 처녀를 위해 가슴이 미어지도록 슬퍼하오. 나는 늙고 지쳤음에도 그녀를 위해 내 피를 주었소. 내 시간을, 내 솜씨를, 내 잠을 주었소."], ["And yet I can laugh at her very grave--laugh when the clay from the spade of the sexton drop upon her coffin and say ‘Thud!", "그녀가 모든 것을 가질 수 있도록 내 다른 환자들이 아쉬워하게 두었소. 그런데도 나는 바로 그녀의 무덤에서 웃을 수 있소 — 무덤 파는 인부의 삽에서 흙덩이가 그녀의 관 위로 떨어지며 내 가슴을 향해 '쿵! 쿵!'"], ["thud!’ to my heart, till it send back the blood from my cheek.", "하고, 마침내 내 뺨에서 핏기를 물리칠 때조차 웃을 수 있소."], ["My heart bleed for that poor boy--that dear boy, so of the age of mine own boy had I been so blessed that he live, and with his hair and eyes the same.", "내 가슴은 그 가엾은 청년을 위해 피 흘리오 — 만일 내가 복을 받아 내 아들이 살아 있었더라면 꼭 내 아들 또래였을, 머리칼도 눈도 똑같은 그 사랑스러운 청년을 위해서 말이오."], ["There, you know now why I love him so.", "자, 당신은 이제 내가 왜 그를 그토록 사랑하는지 알겠지."], ["And yet when he say things that touch my husband-heart to the quick, and make my father-heart yearn to him as to no other man--not even to you, friend John, for we are more level in experiences than father and son--yet even at such moment King Laugh he come to me and shout and bellow in my ear, ‘Here I am!", "그런데도 그가 내 남편의 가슴을 깊이 찌르는 말을, 또 다른 누구에게도 — 심지어 당신에게도 그렇지 않을 만큼, 벗 존, 우리는 부자지간보다는 경험에 있어 더 대등하니 — 내 아버지의 가슴을 그에게로 사무치게 만드는 말을 할 때조차, 바로 그런 순간에도 웃음의 왕이 나에게 찾아와 내 귀에 대고 외치고 고함치오, '나 여기 있소! 나 여기 있소!'"], ["here I am!’ till the blood come dance back and bring some of the sunshine that he carry with him to my cheek.", "하고, 마침내 피가 다시 춤추며 돌아와 그놈이 함께 지니고 다니는 햇빛을 얼마쯤 내 뺨으로 가져올 때까지 말이오."], ["Oh, friend John, it is a strange world, a sad world, a world full of miseries, and woes, and troubles; and yet when King Laugh come he make them all dance to the tune he play.", "오, 벗 존, 이것은 기이한 세상, 슬픈 세상, 비참함과 비탄과 환난으로 가득한 세상이오. 그런데도 웃음의 왕이 찾아오면 그놈은 그 모두를 자기가 연주하는 가락에 맞추어 춤추게 만든다오."], ["Bleeding hearts, and dry bones of the churchyard, and tears that burn as they fall--all dance together to the music that he make with that smileless mouth of him.", "피 흘리는 가슴이며, 교회 묘지의 마른 뼈며, 떨어지며 타들어가는 눈물이며 — 그 모두가 그놈이 웃음기 없는 입으로 빚어내는 음악에 맞추어 함께 춤춘다오."], ["And believe me, friend John, that he is good to come, and kind.", "그리고 믿어주시오, 벗 존, 그놈이 찾아오는 것은 좋은 일이고 친절한 일이오."], ["Ah, we men and women are like ropes drawn tight with strain that pull us different ways.", "아, 우리 남자와 여자는 서로 다른 방향으로 잡아당기는 긴장으로 팽팽히 당겨진 밧줄과도 같소."], ["Then tears come; and, like the rain on the ropes, they brace us up, until perhaps the strain become too great, and we break.", "그러면 눈물이 나오. 그리고 밧줄에 내리는 비처럼, 눈물은 우리를 다잡아주오, 아마 긴장이 너무 커져 우리가 끊어질 때까지 말이오."], ["But King Laugh he come like the sunshine, and he ease off the strain again; and we bear to go on with our labour, what it may be.”", "그러나 웃음의 왕은 햇빛처럼 찾아와 다시 그 긴장을 풀어주오. 그러면 우리는 그것이 무엇이든 우리의 수고를 계속 견뎌내게 되오.\""]], [["I did not like to wound him by pretending not to see his idea; but, as I did not yet understand the cause of his laughter, I asked him.", "나는 그의 생각을 알아듣지 못한 척하여 그에게 상처를 주고 싶지 않았다. 그러나 나는 아직 그가 웃은 까닭을 이해하지 못했으므로 그에게 물었다."], ["As he answered me his face grew stern, and he said in quite a different tone:--", "그가 나에게 답할 때 그의 얼굴은 엄숙해졌고, 그는 전혀 다른 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Oh, it was the grim irony of it all--this so lovely lady garlanded with flowers, that looked so fair as life, till one by one we wondered if she were truly dead; she laid in that so fine marble house in that lonely churchyard, where rest so many of her kin, laid there with the mother who loved her, and whom she loved; and that sacred bell going ‘Toll! toll!", "\"오, 그 모든 것의 그 음울한 아이러니 말이오 — 그토록 사랑스러운 부인이 꽃으로 단장한 채, 살아 있는 듯이 어찌나 고와 보이던지 우리가 하나씩 하나씩 그녀가 정말로 죽은 것인지 의아해할 정도였소. 그녀가 그 외딴 교회 묘지의 그토록 훌륭한 대리석 집에, 그녀의 일가가 그토록 많이 잠들어 있는 곳에, 그녀를 사랑했고 그녀가 사랑했던 어머니와 함께 눕혀졌소. 그리고 그 거룩한 종이 '뎅! 뎅! 뎅!'"], ["toll!’ so sad and slow; and those holy men, with the white garments of the angel, pretending to read books, and yet all the time their eyes never on the page; and all of us with the bowed head. And all for what?", "하고 그토록 슬프고 느리게 울리고, 천사의 흰옷을 걸친 그 거룩한 사람들이 책을 읽는 척하면서도 줄곧 그 눈은 책장에 가 있지 않고, 우리 모두는 머리를 숙이고 있었소. 그리고 그 모두가 무엇을 위해서였소?"], ["She is dead; so!", "그녀는 죽었소. 그러니!"], ["Is it not?”", "그렇지 않소?\""]], [["“Well, for the life of me, Professor,” I said, “I can’t see anything to laugh at in all that.", "\"글쎄요, 정말이지, 교수님,\" 하고 내가 말했다, \"저는 그 모든 것에서 웃을 만한 구석을 도무지 찾을 수가 없습니다."], ["Why, your explanation makes it a harder puzzle than before.", "아니, 선생님의 설명은 그것을 전보다 더 풀기 어려운 수수께끼로 만들 뿐이에요."], ["But even if the burial service was comic, what about poor Art and his trouble?", "그러나 설령 장례 예식이 우스꽝스러웠다 한들, 가엾은 아서와 그의 슬픔은 어쩌고요?"], ["Why, his heart was simply breaking.”", "아니, 그의 가슴은 그야말로 미어지고 있었는데요.\""]], [["“Just so. Said he not that the transfusion of his blood to her veins had made her truly his bride?”", "\"바로 그렇소. 자기 피를 그녀의 혈관에 수혈한 것이 그녀를 진정한 자기 신부로 만들었다고 그가 말하지 않았소?\""]], [["“Yes, and it was a sweet and comforting idea for him.”", "\"그랬지요, 그리고 그것은 그에게 달콤하고 위안이 되는 생각이었습니다.\""]], [["“Quite so.", "\"바로 그렇소."], ["But there was a difficulty, friend John.", "그러나 거기에 한 가지 난처한 점이 있소, 벗 존."], ["If so that, then what about the others? Ho, ho!", "만일 그렇다면, 다른 이들은 어찌 되는 거요? 호, 호!"], ["Then this so sweet maid is a polyandrist, and me, with my poor wife dead to me, but alive by Church’s law, though no wits, all gone--even I, who am faithful husband to this now-no-wife, am bigamist.”", "그렇다면 그 그토록 사랑스러운 처녀는 일처다부의 여인이고, 또 나는, 내 가엾은 아내가 나에게는 죽은 것이나 교회법으로는 살아 있고, 정신이 다 나가버렸음에도 그러하니 — 이제는 아내 아닌 그 사람에게 충실한 남편인 나조차, 중혼자(重婚者)인 셈이오.\""]], [["“I don’t see where the joke comes in there either!”", "\"저는 거기서도 무엇이 우스운지 모르겠습니다!\""], ["I said; and I did not feel particularly pleased with him for saying such things.", "하고 내가 말했다. 또 나는 그가 그런 말을 하는 것이 그리 달갑지 않았다."], ["He laid his hand on my arm, and said:--", "그는 내 팔에 손을 얹고 말했다."]], [["“Friend John, forgive me if I pain.", "\"벗 존, 내가 아프게 했다면 용서하시오."], ["I showed not my feeling to others when it would wound, but only to you, my old friend, whom I can trust.", "나는 다른 이들에게는, 상처가 될 만한 때에는 내 감정을 드러내지 않고, 오직 당신에게만, 내가 믿을 수 있는 나의 오랜 벗에게만 드러내오."], ["If you could have looked into my very heart then when I want to laugh; if you could have done so when the laugh arrived; if you could do so now, when King Laugh have pack up his crown, and all that is to him--for he go far, far away from me, and for a long, long time--maybe you would perhaps pity me the most of all.”", "만일 내가 웃고 싶었던 그때 당신이 바로 그 내 가슴속을 들여다볼 수 있었더라면, 만일 웃음이 찾아왔을 때 그럴 수 있었더라면, 만일 지금, 웃음의 왕이 자기 왕관과 자기에게 딸린 모든 것을 챙겨 떠난 — 그놈은 멀리, 멀리, 그것도 오래, 오래도록 나에게서 떠나가니 — 지금 당신이 그럴 수 있다면, 아마 당신은 누구보다도 나를 가장 가엾게 여길 것이오.\""]], [["I was touched by the tenderness of his tone, and asked why.", "나는 그의 어조에 담긴 다정함에 마음이 뭉클하여 그 까닭을 물었다."]], [["“Because I know!”", "\"내가 알기 때문이오!\""]], [["And now we are all scattered; and for many a long day loneliness will sit over our roofs with brooding wings.", "그리고 이제 우리는 모두 뿔뿔이 흩어졌다. 그리고 오랜 세월 외로움이 음울한 날개를 펼치고 우리의 지붕 위에 둥지를 틀 것이다."], ["Lucy lies in the tomb of her kin, a lordly death-house in a lonely churchyard, away from teeming London; where the air is fresh, and the sun rises over Hampstead Hill, and where wild flowers grow of their own accord.", "루시는 그녀 일가의 무덤에, 붐비는 런던에서 멀리 떨어진 외딴 교회 묘지의 한 으리으리한 죽음의 집에 누워 있다. 그곳은 공기가 맑고, 햄스테드 언덕 위로 해가 떠오르며, 들꽃이 절로 자라는 곳이다."]], [["So I can finish this diary; and God only knows if I shall ever begin another.", "이로써 나는 이 일기를 끝맺을 수 있다. 그리고 내가 또 다른 일기를 시작하게 될지는 오직 신만이 아신다."], ["If I do, or if I even open this again, it will be to deal with different people and different themes; for here at the end, where the romance of my life is told, ere I go back to take up the thread of my life-work, I say sadly and without hope,", "만일 그러게 된다면, 혹은 이 일기를 다시 펼치게 되더라도, 그것은 다른 사람들과 다른 주제를 다루기 위해서일 것이다. 내 삶의 로맨스가 이야기된 이 끝자락에서, 내 필생의 일이라는 실타래를 다시 집어 들기에 앞서, 나는 슬프게, 또 희망 없이 말한다."]], [["“The Westminster Gazette,” 25 September.", "\"끝.\" 「웨스트민스터 가제트」, 9월 25일."]], [["# A HAMPSTEAD MYSTERY.", "# A HAMPSTEAD MYSTERY."]], [["The neighbourhood of Hampstead is just at present exercised with a series of events which seem to run on lines parallel to those of what was known to the writers of headlines as “The Kensington Horror,” or “The Stabbing Woman,”", "햄스테드의 괴사건. 요즘 들어 햄스테드 인근은, 표제 기사를 쓰는 이들에게 \"켄싱턴의 공포\"라거나 \"찌르는 여인\", \"검은 옷의 여인\"으로 알려졌던 사건들과 비슷한 양상으로 흘러가는 일련의 사건들로 떠들썩하다."], ["or “The Woman in Black.” During the past two or three days several cases have occurred of young children straying from home or neglecting to return from their playing on the Heath.", "지난 이삼일 동안 어린아이들이 집에서 멀리 벗어나거나 히스에서 놀다가 돌아오지 않는 경우가 여러 건 발생했다."], ["In all these cases the children were too young to give any properly intelligible account of themselves, but the consensus of their excuses is that they had been with a “bloofer lady.”", "이 모든 경우에 아이들은 너무 어려서 자기 일을 제대로 알아들을 만하게 설명하지 못했으나, 그 변명들의 공통점은 자기들이 \"예쁜 부인\"과 함께 있었다는 것이었다."], ["It has always been late in the evening when they have been missed, and on two occasions the children have not been found until early in the following morning.", "아이들이 보이지 않게 된 것은 언제나 저녁 늦은 시각이었고, 두 차례는 이튿날 이른 아침에야 발견되었다."], ["It is generally supposed in the neighbourhood that, as the first child missed gave as his reason for being away that a “bloofer lady” had asked him to come for a walk, the others had picked up the phrase and used it as occasion served.", "인근에서는 대체로, 처음 사라진 아이가 자기가 떠나 있었던 이유로 \"예쁜 부인\"이 함께 산책 가자고 했다는 것을 댔으므로, 다른 아이들이 그 말을 주워듣고 형편 닿는 대로 써먹은 것이라고들 추측한다."], ["This is the more natural as the favourite game of the little ones at present is luring each other away by wiles.", "이것이 더욱 자연스러운 까닭은, 요즘 어린것들이 가장 좋아하는 놀이가 서로를 꾀어내어 데려가는 것이기 때문이다."], ["A correspondent writes us that to see some of the tiny tots pretending to be the “bloofer lady” is supremely funny.", "한 통신원이 우리에게 알려오기를, 그 조그만 꼬마들 가운데 몇몇이 \"예쁜 부인\" 흉내를 내는 것을 보면 더없이 우습다고 한다."], ["Some of our caricaturists might, he says, take a lesson in the irony of grotesque by comparing the reality and the picture.", "그는, 우리 풍자화가 가운데 더러는 그 실제 모습과 그림을 견주어봄으로써 기괴한 것의 아이러니에 관해 한 수 배울 수도 있겠다고 말한다."], ["It is only in accordance with general principles of human nature that the “bloofer lady”", "\"예쁜 부인\"이 이런 야외 공연에서 인기 있는 배역이 되는 것은 다만 인간 본성의 일반 원리에 들어맞을 뿐이다."], ["should be the popular rôle at these al fresco performances. Our correspondent naïvely says that even Ellen Terry could not be so winningly attractive as some of these grubby-faced little children pretend--and even imagine themselves--to be.", "우리 통신원은 천연덕스럽게 말하기를, 엘런 테리조차 이 꾀죄죄한 얼굴의 조그만 아이들이 흉내 내는 — 심지어 스스로 그렇다고 여기기까지 하는 — 그 모습만큼 그렇게 사랑스럽도록 매력적일 수는 없으리라고 한다."]], [["There is, however, possibly a serious side to the question, for some of the children, indeed all who have been missed at night, have been slightly torn or wounded in the throat.", "그러나 이 문제에는 어쩌면 심각한 측면이 있을지도 모르니, 아이들 가운데 몇몇은, 사실 밤에 사라졌던 아이들 모두는 목에 살짝 찢기거나 다친 상처가 있었기 때문이다."], ["The wounds seem such as might be made by a rat or a small dog, and although of not much importance individually, would tend to show that whatever animal inflicts them has a system or method of its own.", "그 상처들은 쥐나 작은 개가 냈을 법한 것으로, 하나하나로는 그리 대수롭지 않으나, 그것들을 입히는 짐승이 무엇이든 그 나름의 체계나 방식을 지니고 있음을 보여주는 듯하다."], ["The police of the division have been instructed to keep a sharp look-out for straying children, especially when very young, in and around Hampstead Heath, and for any stray dog which may be about.", "관할 구역 경찰은 햄스테드 히스 안팎에서 길 잃은 아이들, 특히 아주 어린아이들을, 또 주변에 있을지 모를 떠돌이 개를 면밀히 살피라는 지시를 받았다."]], [["“The Westminster Gazette,” 25 September.", "「웨스트민스터 가제트」, 9월 25일. 특별 호외."]], [["Extra Special.", "햄스테드의 공포."]], [["The “Bloofer Lady.”", "또 한 아이가 다치다."]], [["We have just received intelligence that another child, missed last night, was only discovered late in the morning under a furze bush at the Shooter’s Hill side of Hampstead Heath, which is, perhaps, less frequented than the other parts.", "\"예쁜 부인.\" 방금 우리에게 들어온 소식에 따르면, 어젯밤 사라졌던 또 한 아이가 오늘 아침 늦게야, 햄스테드 히스의 슈터스 힐 쪽 — 아마 다른 구역들보다 인적이 덜한 곳일 — 가시금작화 덤불 아래에서 발견되었다고 한다."], ["It has the same tiny wound in the throat as has been noticed in other cases.", "그 아이의 목에는 다른 경우들에서 눈에 띈 것과 똑같은 조그만 상처가 있었다."], ["It was terribly weak, and looked quite emaciated.", "아이는 몹시 쇠약했고 무척 야위어 보였다."], ["It too, when partially restored, had the common story to tell of being lured away by the “bloofer lady.”", "그 아이 역시, 어느 정도 기운을 차린 뒤에는 \"예쁜 부인\"에게 꾀여 끌려갔다는 그 흔한 이야기를 들려주었다."]], [["# MINA HARKER’S JOURNAL", "# MINA HARKER’S JOURNAL"]], [["23 September.--Jonathan is better after a bad night.", "「미나 하커의 일기」 9월 23일. — 조너선이 험한 밤을 보낸 뒤 한결 나아졌다."], ["I am so glad that he has plenty of work to do, for that keeps his mind off the terrible things; and oh, I am rejoiced that he is not now weighed down with the responsibility of his new position.", "그에게 할 일이 많다는 것이 어찌나 다행인지, 그 덕에 끔찍한 일들에서 마음을 돌릴 수 있으니 말이다. 그리고 아, 이제 그가 새 지위의 책무에 짓눌리지 않게 된 것이 나는 기쁘기 그지없다."], ["I knew he would be true to himself, and now how proud I am to see my Jonathan rising to the height of his advancement and keeping pace in all ways with the duties that come upon him.", "나는 그가 자기 본분에 충실하리라는 것을 알고 있었고, 이제 나의 조너선이 출세의 정점에 올라 자신에게 주어진 온갖 임무에 발맞추어 나아가는 것을 보니 어찌나 자랑스러운지."], ["He will be away all day till late, for he said he could not lunch at home.", "그는 종일 밖에 있다가 늦게야 돌아올 텐데, 점심도 집에서 들 수 없다고 했다."], ["My household work is done, so I shall take his foreign journal, and lock myself up in my room and read it....", "집안일은 다 마쳤으니, 그의 외국 일기를 가져다 방에 틀어박혀 읽어보아야겠다……."]], [["24 September.--I hadn’t the heart to write last night; that terrible record of Jonathan’s upset me so.", "9월 24일. — 어젯밤에는 도무지 쓸 마음이 나지 않았다. 조너선의 그 끔찍한 기록이 나를 너무도 뒤흔들어 놓았기 때문이다."], ["Poor dear!", "가엾은 사람!"], ["How he must have suffered, whether it be true or only imagination.", "그것이 사실이든 한낱 상상이든, 그가 얼마나 고통받았을 것인가."], ["I wonder if there is any truth in it at all.", "그 안에 조금이라도 진실이 있는 것일까."], ["Did he get his brain fever, and then write all those terrible things, or had he some cause for it all?", "그가 뇌염을 앓고 나서 그 모든 끔찍한 것들을 적은 것일까, 아니면 그 모든 일에 어떤 까닭이 있었던 것일까."], ["I suppose I shall never know, for I dare not open the subject to him....", "아마 나는 결코 알 수 없으리라, 감히 그에게 그 이야기를 꺼낼 수 없으니……."], ["And yet that man we saw yesterday!", "그런데 어제 우리가 본 그 남자라니!"], ["He seemed quite certain of him....", "그는 그를 꽤 확신하는 듯했다……."], ["Poor fellow!", "가엾은 사람!"], ["I suppose it was the funeral upset him and sent his mind back on some train of thought....", "짐작건대 장례식이 그를 뒤흔들어 어떤 생각의 줄기를 떠올리게 만든 것이리라……."], ["He believes it all himself.", "그는 그 모든 것을 스스로 믿고 있다."], ["I remember how on our wedding-day he said: “Unless some solemn duty come upon me to go back to the bitter hours, asleep or awake, mad or sane.”", "우리 혼인날에 그가 했던 말이 떠오른다. \"잠들었든 깨어 있든, 미쳤든 제정신이든, 그 쓰라린 시간으로 되돌아가야 할 어떤 엄숙한 의무가 나에게 닥치지 않는 한.\""], ["There seems to be through it all some thread of continuity....", "그 모든 것을 관통하는 어떤 연속의 실마리가 있는 듯하다……."], ["That fearful Count was coming to London....", "그 무서운 백작이 런던으로 오고 있었다니……."], ["If it should be, and he came to London, with his teeming millions....", "만일 그것이 사실이라면, 그래서 그가 우글거리는 수백만의 무리 속으로 런던에 왔다면……."], ["There may be a solemn duty; and if it come we must not shrink from it.... I shall be prepared.", "어떤 엄숙한 의무가 있을지도 모른다. 그리고 그것이 닥치거든 우리는 결코 움츠려서는 안 된다……."], ["I shall get my typewriter this very hour and begin transcribing.", "나는 준비해 두리라. 지금 당장 타자기를 가져다 옮겨 적기를 시작하겠다."], ["Then we shall be ready for other eyes if required.", "그러면 필요할 때 다른 이들의 눈에 대비할 수 있으리라."], ["And if it be wanted; then, perhaps, if I am ready, poor Jonathan may not be upset, for I can speak for him and never let him be troubled or worried with it at all.", "그리고 만일 그것이 쓰이게 된다면, 그때 내가 준비되어 있다면, 가엾은 조너선이 동요하지 않아도 될지 모른다. 내가 그를 대신해 말할 수 있고, 그가 그것 때문에 괴로워하거나 시달리지 않게 할 수 있으니."], ["If ever Jonathan quite gets over the nervousness he may want to tell me of it all, and I can ask him questions and find out things, and see how I may comfort him.", "언젠가 조너선이 그 신경증을 완전히 떨쳐낸다면 그 모든 것을 나에게 들려주고 싶어 할지도 모르고, 그러면 나는 그에게 이것저것 물어 사실을 알아내고, 어떻게 그를 위로할 수 있을지 헤아릴 수 있으리라. 「판 헬싱이 하커 부인에게 보낸 편지」"]], [["“24 September.", "\"9월 24일."]], [["(Confidence)", "(친전)"]], [["“Dear Madam,--", "친애하는 부인께,"]], [["“I pray you to pardon my writing, in that I am so far friend as that I sent to you sad news of Miss Lucy Westenra’s death.", "루시 웨스텐라 양의 부고라는 슬픈 소식을 전해 드린 친구의 자격으로 이렇게 편지를 쓰는 것을 용서해 주시기를 바라오."], ["By the kindness of Lord Godalming, I am empowered to read her letters and papers, for I am deeply concerned about certain matters vitally important. In them I find some letters from you, which show how great friends you were and how you love her.", "고댈밍 경의 호의로 나는 그녀의 편지와 서류들을 읽을 권한을 얻었는데, 더없이 중요한 어떤 문제들에 깊이 마음을 쓰고 있는 까닭이오. 그 가운데에서 부인이 보낸 편지 몇 통을 발견했고, 두 분이 얼마나 가까운 벗이었으며 부인이 그녀를 얼마나 사랑했는지를 알게 되었소."], ["Oh, Madam Mina, by that love, I implore you, help me.", "아, 미나 부인, 그 사랑을 두고 간청하오니, 나를 도와주시오."], ["It is for others’ good that I ask--to redress great wrong, and to lift much and terrible troubles--that may be more great than you can know. May it be that I see you?", "내가 청하는 것은 다른 이들의 선을 위함이니 — 큰 잘못을 바로잡고, 부인이 알 수 있는 것보다 어쩌면 더 크고 끔찍한 화근을 거두어 들이기 위함이오."], ["You can trust me.", "부인을 뵐 수 있겠소?"], ["I am friend of Dr.", "나를 믿으셔도 좋소."], ["John Seward and of Lord Godalming (that was Arthur of Miss Lucy).", "나는 존 수어드 박사와 고댈밍 경(루시 양의 아서였던 사람)의 벗이오."], ["I must keep it private for the present from all.", "지금으로서는 이 일을 모두에게 비밀로 해 두어야 하오."], ["I should come to Exeter to see you at once if you tell me I am privilege to come, and where and when.", "내가 가도 좋다는 특전과 더불어 어디로, 언제 가야 할지 일러 주신다면, 나는 곧장 엑서터로 부인을 뵈러 가겠소."], ["I implore your pardon, madam.", "부인, 너그러이 용서해 주시기를 비오."], ["I have read your letters to poor Lucy, and know how good you are and how your husband suffer; so I pray you, if it may be, enlighten him not, lest it may harm.", "나는 가엾은 루시에게 보낸 부인의 편지들을 읽었고, 부인이 얼마나 선한지, 부군이 얼마나 고통받는지를 아오. 그러니 비오니, 가능하다면 부군께는 알리지 마시오, 해가 될까 두려운 까닭이오."], ["Again your pardon, and forgive me.", "다시 한 번 용서를 구하며, 나를 너그러이 보아 주시오. 판 헬싱.\""]], [["Telegram, Mrs. Harker to Van Helsing.", "「하커 부인이 판 헬싱에게 보낸 전보」"]], [["“25 September.--Come to-day by quarter-past ten train if you can catch it.", "\"9월 25일. — 가능하시다면 오늘 10시 15분 기차로 오십시오."], ["Can see you any time you call.", "들르시는 어느 때든 뵐 수 있습니다. 빌헬미나 하커.\""]], [["# MINA HARKER’S JOURNAL.", "# MINA HARKER’S JOURNAL."]], [["25 September.--I cannot help feeling terribly excited as the time draws near for the visit of Dr.", "「미나 하커의 일기」 9월 25일."], ["Van Helsing, for somehow I expect that it will throw some light upon Jonathan’s sad experience; and as he attended poor dear Lucy in her last illness, he can tell me all about her.", "— 판 헬싱 박사의 방문이 다가올수록 나는 몹시 들뜬 마음을 가눌 수가 없으니, 어쩐지 그것이 조너선의 슬픈 경험에 어떤 빛을 던져 주리라는 기대가 들기 때문이다. 또한 그가 가엾은 루시의 마지막 병환을 돌보았으니, 그녀에 관해 모든 것을 일러 줄 수 있으리라."], ["That is the reason of his coming; it is concerning Lucy and her sleep-walking, and not about Jonathan.", "그가 오는 까닭이 바로 그것이다. 조너선에 관한 일이 아니라 루시와 그녀의 몽유에 관한 일인 것이다."], ["Then I shall never know the real truth now!", "그렇다면 나는 이제 진실을 결코 알 수 없으리라!"], ["How silly I am. That awful journal gets hold of my imagination and tinges everything with something of its own colour.", "내가 어쩌면 이리 어리석은가. 그 끔찍한 일기가 내 상상을 사로잡아 모든 것을 제 빛깔로 물들이는구나."], ["Of course it is about Lucy.", "물론 루시에 관한 일이다."], ["That habit came back to the poor dear, and that awful night on the cliff must have made her ill.", "그 가엾은 사람에게 옛 버릇이 도졌고, 절벽 위에서의 그 무서운 밤이 그녀를 병들게 했음에 틀림없다."], ["I had almost forgotten in my own affairs how ill she was afterwards.", "내 일에 정신이 팔려, 그 뒤로 그녀가 얼마나 아팠는지조차 거의 잊고 있었다."], ["She must have told him of her sleep-walking adventure on the cliff, and that I knew all about it; and now he wants me to tell him what she knows, so that he may understand.", "그녀는 절벽에서의 몽유 사건과 내가 그 모든 것을 안다는 사실을 그에게 이야기했을 것이다. 그래서 그는 이제 그가 이해할 수 있도록, 그녀가 아는 것을 내게서 듣고 싶어 하는 것이다."], ["I hope I did right in not saying anything of it to Mrs.", "그것을 웨스텐라 부인께 한마디도 하지 않은 것이 옳았기를 바란다."], ["Westenra; I should never forgive myself if any act of mine, were it even a negative one, brought harm on poor dear Lucy.", "내 어떤 행위가, 그것이 설령 하지 않은 행위라 할지라도, 가엾은 루시에게 해를 끼쳤다면 나는 결코 나 자신을 용서할 수 없으리라."], ["I hope, too, Dr. Van Helsing will not blame me; I have had so much trouble and anxiety of late that I feel I cannot bear more just at present.", "또한 판 헬싱 박사가 나를 탓하지 않기를 바란다. 근래 너무도 많은 근심과 시름을 겪은 터라, 지금으로서는 더 이상은 견딜 수 없을 것만 같다."]], [["I suppose a cry does us all good at times--clears the air as other rain does.", "이따금 한바탕 우는 것이 우리 모두에게 좋은 듯하다 — 비가 그러하듯 공기를 맑게 해 주는 것이다."], ["Perhaps it was reading the journal yesterday that upset me, and then Jonathan went away this morning to stay away from me a whole day and night, the first time we have been parted since our marriage.", "어제 일기를 읽은 것이 나를 뒤흔든 탓일 수도 있고, 또 오늘 아침 조너선이 떠나 꼬박 하루 낮밤을 나와 떨어져 있게 된 탓일 수도 있다. 혼인한 뒤로 우리가 헤어진 것은 이번이 처음이다."], ["I do hope the dear fellow will take care of himself, and that nothing will occur to upset him.", "부디 그 사람이 몸조심하기를, 그리고 그를 뒤흔들 어떤 일도 일어나지 않기를 빈다. 두 시다."], ["It is two o’clock, and the doctor will be here soon now. I shall say nothing of Jonathan’s journal unless he asks me.", "이제 곧 박사가 도착할 것이다. 그가 묻지 않는 한 조너선의 일기에 관해서는 아무 말도 하지 않으리라."], ["I am so glad I have type-written out my own journal, so that, in case he asks about Lucy, I can hand it to him; it will save much questioning.", "내 일기를 타자로 옮겨 둔 것이 어찌나 다행인지, 그가 루시에 관해 묻거든 그것을 건네줄 수 있을 테니, 많은 물음을 덜 수 있으리라."]], [["Later.--He has come and gone.", "이후. — 그가 왔다 갔다."], ["Oh, what a strange meeting, and how it all makes my head whirl round!", "아, 이 얼마나 기이한 만남인가, 그리고 이 모든 것이 내 머리를 어찌나 어지럽게 하는지!"], ["I feel like one in a dream.", "나는 꿈을 꾸는 사람 같다."], ["Can it be all possible, or even a part of it?", "이 모든 것이, 아니 그 일부라도 정말 있을 수 있는 일일까?"], ["If I had not read Jonathan’s journal first, I should never have accepted even a possibility.", "만일 내가 조너선의 일기를 먼저 읽지 않았더라면 그 가능성조차 결코 받아들이지 못했으리라."], ["Poor, poor, dear Jonathan!", "가엾고 가엾은, 사랑하는 조너선!"], ["How he must have suffered.", "그가 얼마나 고통받았을 것인가."], ["Please the good God, all this may not upset him again.", "부디 선하신 하느님께서, 이 모든 일이 그를 다시 뒤흔들지 않게 하시기를."], ["I shall try to save him from it; but it may be even a consolation and a help to him--terrible though it be and awful in its consequences--to know for certain that his eyes and ears and brain did not deceive him, and that it is all true.", "나는 그것으로부터 그를 지키려 애쓰리라. 그러나 어쩌면 그것은 그에게 위안이자 도움이 될지도 모른다 — 비록 끔찍하고 그 여파가 무섭기는 하나 — 자기 눈과 귀와 머리가 자기를 속이지 않았으며, 그것이 모두 사실임을 확실히 아는 것은 말이다."], ["It may be that it is the doubt which haunts him; that when the doubt is removed, no matter which--waking or dreaming--may prove the truth, he will be more satisfied and better able to bear the shock. Dr.", "어쩌면 그를 괴롭히는 것은 의심일지도 모른다. 그 의심이 걷히고 나면, 깨어 있을 때든 꿈속에서든 어느 쪽이 진실로 드러나든, 그는 더 흡족해하고 그 충격을 더 잘 견딜 수 있게 될지도 모른다."], ["Van Helsing must be a good man as well as a clever one if he is Arthur’s friend and Dr. Seward’s, and if they brought him all the way from Holland to look after Lucy.", "판 헬싱 박사가 아서의 벗이자 수어드 박사의 벗이고, 그들이 루시를 돌보게 하려 그를 네덜란드에서 그 먼 길을 데려온 것이라면, 그는 영리한 사람일 뿐 아니라 선한 사람임에 틀림없다."], ["I feel from having seen him that he is good and kind and of a noble nature.", "그를 만나 보니, 그가 선하고 다정하며 고결한 천성을 지닌 사람임이 느껴진다."], ["When he comes to-morrow I shall ask him about Jonathan; and then, please God, all this sorrow and anxiety may lead to a good end.", "그가 내일 오거든 나는 조너선에 관해 물어보리라. 그러면, 하느님이 도우신다면, 이 모든 슬픔과 시름이 좋은 끝으로 이어질지도 모른다."], ["I used to think I would like to practise interviewing; Jonathan’s friend on “The Exeter News”", "나는 한때 인터뷰하는 일을 익혀 보고 싶다고 생각하곤 했다."], ["told him that memory was everything in such work--that you must be able to put down exactly almost every word spoken, even if you had to refine some of it afterwards.", "「엑서터 뉴스」에 있는 조너선의 친구가 그에게 말하기를, 그런 일에는 기억이 전부라고 했다 — 나중에 다듬어야 할지언정 거의 모든 말을 빠짐없이 정확히 받아 적을 수 있어야 한다고."], ["Here was a rare interview; I shall try to record it verbatim.", "여기 보기 드문 인터뷰가 있었으니, 나는 그것을 그대로 옮겨 적어 보려 한다."]], [["It was half-past two o’clock when the knock came.", "문을 두드리는 소리가 난 것은 두 시 반이었다."], ["I took my courage à deux mains and waited.", "나는 두 손으로 용기를 그러쥐고 기다렸다."], ["In a few minutes Mary opened the door, and announced “Dr. Van Helsing.”", "몇 분 뒤 메리가 문을 열고 \"판 헬싱 박사님\"이라고 알렸다."]], [["I rose and bowed, and he came towards me; a man of medium weight, strongly built, with his shoulders set back over a broad, deep chest and a neck well balanced on the trunk as the head is on the neck.", "나는 일어서서 절을 했고, 그가 나에게로 다가왔다. 중키에 다부진 몸집의 사내로, 넓고 깊은 가슴 위로 어깨를 곧게 젖혔으며, 목은 머리가 목 위에 얹히듯 몸통 위에 균형 있게 얹혀 있었다."], ["The poise of the head strikes one at once as indicative of thought and power; the head is noble, well-sized, broad, and large behind the ears.", "머리를 가누는 자세는 보는 이로 하여금 곧바로 사색과 힘을 떠올리게 한다. 머리는 고결하고, 크기가 알맞으며, 넓고, 귀 뒤쪽이 크다."], ["The face, clean-shaven, shows a hard, square chin, a large, resolute, mobile mouth, a good-sized nose, rather straight, but with quick, sensitive nostrils, that seem to broaden as the big, bushy brows come down and the mouth tightens.", "말끔히 면도한 얼굴에는 단단한 사각 턱, 크고 단호하며 잘 움직이는 입, 꽤 곧되 빠르고 예민한 콧구멍을 지닌 큼직한 코가 드러나 있는데, 그 콧구멍은 크고 무성한 눈썹이 내려오고 입이 다물릴 때면 벌어지는 듯하다."], ["The forehead is broad and fine, rising at first almost straight and then sloping back above two bumps or ridges wide apart; such a forehead that the reddish hair cannot possibly tumble over it, but falls naturally back and to the sides.", "이마는 넓고 수려하여, 처음에는 거의 곧추 솟다가 멀찍이 벌어진 두 개의 혹 혹은 융기 위로 뒤로 비스듬히 물러나는데, 그런 이마인지라 불그스름한 머리칼이 도무지 그 위로 흘러내리지 못하고 자연히 뒤와 옆으로 넘어간다."], ["Big, dark blue eyes are set widely apart, and are quick and tender or stern with the man’s moods.", "크고 짙푸른 눈은 멀찍이 벌어져 있으며, 그 사내의 기분에 따라 재빠르고 부드럽거나 준엄하다."], ["He said to me:--", "그가 나에게 말했다."]], [["“Mrs. Harker, is it not?”", "\"하커 부인이시지요?\""], ["I bowed assent.", "나는 그렇다는 뜻으로 고개를 숙였다."]], [["“That was Miss Mina Murray?” Again I assented.", "\"미나 머리 양이셨고요?\" 나는 다시 그렇다고 했다."]], [["“It is Mina Murray that I came to see that was friend of that poor dear child Lucy Westenra.", "\"내가 만나러 온 사람은, 그 가엾은 루시 웨스텐라 양의 벗이었던 미나 머리요."], ["Madam Mina, it is on account of the dead I come.”", "미나 부인, 내가 온 것은 고인 때문이오.\""]], [["“Sir,”", "\"선생님,\""], ["I said, “you could have no better claim on me than that you were a friend and helper of Lucy Westenra.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"선생님이 루시 웨스텐라의 벗이자 조력자였다는 것보다 더 좋은 자격으로 저에게 다가오실 수는 없을 거예요.\""], ["And I held out my hand.", "그러고는 손을 내밀었다."], ["He took it and said tenderly:--", "그는 그 손을 잡고 다정하게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, Madam Mina, I knew that the friend of that poor lily girl must be good, but I had yet to learn----” He finished his speech with a courtly bow.", "\"아, 미나 부인, 나는 그 가엾은 백합 같은 아가씨의 벗이라면 분명 선한 사람이리라 알고 있었소만, 아직 모르고 있던 것이 있었으니——\" 그는 정중한 인사로 말을 맺었다."], ["I asked him what it was that he wanted to see me about, so he at once began:--", "무슨 일로 나를 보러 오셨느냐고 묻자, 그는 곧바로 시작했다."]], [["“I have read your letters to Miss Lucy.", "\"나는 부인이 루시 양에게 보낸 편지들을 읽었소."], ["Forgive me, but I had to begin to inquire somewhere, and there was none to ask.", "용서하시오, 그러나 나는 어디선가 알아보기를 시작해야 했고, 물어볼 사람이 아무도 없었소."], ["I know that you were with her at Whitby.", "부인이 휘트비에서 그녀와 함께 있었다는 것을 아오."], ["She sometimes kept a diary--you need not look surprised, Madam Mina; it was begun after you had left, and was in imitation of you--and in that diary she traces by inference certain things to a sleep-walking in which she puts down that you saved her.", "그녀는 이따금 일기를 썼는데 — 놀란 표정을 지을 것 없소, 미나 부인. 부인이 떠난 뒤에 부인을 본떠 시작한 것이오 — 그 일기에서 그녀는 어떤 일들을 어떤 몽유에서 비롯된 것으로 미루어 적으면서, 부인이 자기를 구해 주었다고 써 놓았소."], ["In great perplexity then I come to you, and ask you out of your so much kindness to tell me all of it that you can remember.”", "그래서 나는 몹시 막막한 가운데 부인을 찾아와, 그토록 다정하신 마음으로 기억나는 것을 모두 들려주십사 청하는 것이오.\""]], [["“I can tell you, I think, Dr. Van Helsing, all about it.”", "\"제 생각에는, 판 헬싱 박사님, 그 일에 관해 모두 말씀드릴 수 있을 것 같습니다.\""]], [["“Ah, then you have good memory for facts, for details?", "\"아, 그렇다면 사실과 세부를 잘 기억하시는군요."], ["It is not always so with young ladies.”", "젊은 부인들이 다 그렇지는 않은데.\""]], [["“No, doctor, but I wrote it all down at the time.", "\"아니에요, 박사님. 그때 모두 적어 두었거든요."], ["I can show it to you if you like.”", "원하시면 보여 드릴 수 있어요.\""]], [["“Oh, Madam Mina, I will be grateful; you will do me much favour.”", "\"오, 미나 부인, 고맙겠소. 큰 호의를 베푸시는 것이오.\""], ["I could not resist the temptation of mystifying him a bit--I suppose it is some of the taste of the original apple that remains still in our mouths--so I handed him the shorthand diary.", "나는 그를 조금 어리둥절하게 만들고 싶은 유혹을 떨칠 수가 없었다 — 짐작건대 그 최초의 사과 맛이 우리 입속에 아직 조금 남아 있는 탓이리라 — 그래서 속기로 쓴 일기를 그에게 건넸다."], ["He took it with a grateful bow, and said:--", "그는 고마운 인사와 함께 그것을 받아들고 말했다."]], [["“May I read it?”", "\"읽어 봐도 되겠소?\""]], [["“If you wish,”", "\"원하신다면요.\""], ["I answered as demurely as I could.", "나는 되도록 시치미를 떼며 답했다."], ["He opened it, and for an instant his face fell.", "그가 그것을 펼치자, 한순간 그의 얼굴이 일그러졌다."], ["Then he stood up and bowed.", "그러더니 일어서서 절을 했다."]], [["“Oh, you so clever woman!” he said.", "\"오, 참으로 영리하신 부인이구려!\""], ["“I knew long that Mr. Jonathan was a man of much thankfulness; but see, his wife have all the good things.", "그가 말했다. \"나는 오래전부터 조너선 씨가 감사할 줄 아는 사람임을 알고 있었소만, 보시오, 그 부인이 좋은 것을 다 지녔구려."], ["And will you not so much honour me and so help me as to read it for me? Alas!", "나에게 이리 큰 영광을, 이리 큰 도움을 베풀어 이것을 나 대신 읽어 주지 않겠소? 아아!"], ["I know not the shorthand.”", "나는 속기를 모르오.\""], ["By this time my little joke was over, and I was almost ashamed; so I took the typewritten copy from my workbasket and handed it to him.", "이쯤 되자 내 작은 장난도 끝났고, 나는 거의 부끄러워졌다. 그래서 반짇고리에서 타자로 친 사본을 꺼내 그에게 건넸다."]], [["“Forgive me,”", "\"용서하세요,\""], ["I said: “I could not help it; but I had been thinking that it was of dear Lucy that you wished to ask, and so that you might not have time to wait--not on my account, but because I know your time must be precious--I have written it out on the typewriter for you.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"어쩔 수가 없었어요. 그런데 저는 박사님이 물으려 하시는 것이 사랑스러운 루시에 관한 일이리라 생각했고, 그래서 기다리느라 시간을 허비하지 않으시도록 — 저를 위해서가 아니라, 박사님의 시간이 분명 귀하리라는 것을 아는 까닭에 — 박사님을 위해 타자기로 옮겨 두었어요.\""]], [["He took it and his eyes glistened.", "그는 그것을 받아들었고, 그의 눈이 반짝였다."], ["“You are so good,”", "\"참으로 선하시구려,\""], ["he said. “And may I read it now?", "하고 그가 말했다. \"지금 읽어 봐도 되겠소?"], ["I may want to ask you some things when I have read.”", "다 읽고 나면 몇 가지 여쭐 것이 있을지도 모르오.\""]], [["“By all means,”", "\"얼마든지요,\""], ["I said, “read it over whilst I order lunch; and then you can ask me questions whilst we eat.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"제가 점심을 일러두는 동안 읽으세요. 그런 다음 식사를 하면서 저에게 물으시면 돼요.\""], ["He bowed and settled himself in a chair with his back to the light, and became absorbed in the papers, whilst I went to see after lunch chiefly in order that he might not be disturbed.", "그는 절을 하고는 빛을 등진 채 의자에 자리를 잡았고, 서류에 푹 빠져들었다. 나는 그가 방해받지 않도록, 무엇보다 그것을 위해 점심을 살피러 갔다."], ["When I came back, I found him walking hurriedly up and down the room, his face all ablaze with excitement.", "돌아와 보니 그는 흥분으로 얼굴이 온통 달아오른 채 방 안을 황급히 오락가락하고 있었다."], ["He rushed up to me and took me by both hands.", "그는 나에게로 달려와 두 손을 잡았다."]], [["“Oh, Madam Mina,”", "\"오, 미나 부인,\""], ["he said, “how can I say what I owe to you?", "하고 그가 말했다. \"내가 부인께 진 빚을 어찌 말로 다하겠소?"], ["This paper is as sunshine.", "이 서류는 햇살과도 같소."], ["It opens the gate to me.", "나에게 문을 열어 주는구려."], ["I am daze, I am dazzle, with so much light, and yet clouds roll in behind the light every time.", "나는 이토록 많은 빛에 어리둥절하고, 눈이 부시오. 그런데도 그 빛 뒤로 번번이 구름이 밀려드오."], ["But that you do not, cannot, comprehend.", "그러나 그것은 부인이 알지 못하고, 알 수도 없는 일이오."], ["Oh, but I am grateful to you, you so clever woman.", "아, 그러나 나는 부인께 감사하오, 그토록 영리한 부인께. 부인,\""], ["Madam”--he said this very solemnly--“if ever Abraham Van Helsing can do anything for you or yours, I trust you will let me know.", "— 그는 이 말을 몹시 엄숙하게 했다 — \"언제든 에이브러햄 판 헬싱이 부인이나 부인의 사람들을 위해 무언가 할 수 있게 되거든, 부디 나에게 알려 주시기를 믿겠소."], ["It will be pleasure and delight if I may serve you as a friend; as a friend, but all I have ever learned, all I can ever do, shall be for you and those you love.", "벗으로서 부인을 섬길 수 있다면 그것은 기쁨이요 즐거움이리다. 벗으로서, 그러나 내가 여태 배운 모든 것, 내가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 부인과 부인이 사랑하는 이들을 위해 바치겠소."], ["There are darknesses in life, and there are lights; you are one of the lights.", "삶에는 어둠이 있고, 또 빛이 있소. 부인은 그 빛 가운데 하나요."], ["You will have happy life and good life, and your husband will be blessed in you.”", "부인은 행복한 삶, 좋은 삶을 누릴 것이며, 부군은 부인으로 하여 복받을 것이오.\""]], [["“But, doctor, you praise me too much, and--and you do not know me.”", "\"하지만 박사님, 저를 너무 칭찬하세요. 그리고 — 그리고 박사님은 저를 모르시잖아요.\""]], [["“Not know you--I, who am old, and who have studied all my life men and women; I, who have made my specialty the brain and all that belongs to him and all that follow from him!", "\"부인을 모른다니 — 늙은 데다 평생토록 남자와 여자를 연구해 온 내가, 두뇌와 그에 딸린 모든 것, 그로부터 비롯되는 모든 것을 전공으로 삼아 온 내가 말이오!"], ["And I have read your diary that you have so goodly written for me, and which breathes out truth in every line.", "게다가 나는 부인이 나를 위해 그토록 곱게 써 주신, 줄마다 진실이 배어 나오는 그 일기를 읽었소."], ["I, who have read your so sweet letter to poor Lucy of your marriage and your trust, not know you!", "가엾은 루시에게 보낸, 부인의 혼인과 신뢰를 적은 그 다정한 편지를 읽은 내가, 부인을 모른다니!"], ["Oh, Madam Mina, good women tell all their lives, and by day and by hour and by minute, such things that angels can read; and we men who wish to know have in us something of angels’ eyes.", "오, 미나 부인, 선한 여인은 자기 삶을 온통 드러내는 법이오. 날마다, 시간마다, 분마다, 천사들이 읽을 수 있는 그런 것들을 말이오. 그리고 알기를 바라는 우리 남자들에게도 그 천사의 눈 같은 것이 조금은 있소."], ["Your husband is noble nature, and you are noble too, for you trust, and trust cannot be where there is mean nature.", "부군은 고결한 천성이고, 부인 또한 고결하오. 부인이 신뢰할 줄 아는 까닭이오. 신뢰란 비루한 천성이 있는 곳에는 깃들 수 없는 법이니."], ["And your husband--tell me of him.", "그리고 부군 — 그분에 관해 말해 주시오."], ["Is he quite well?", "아주 건강하시오?"], ["Is all that fever gone, and is he strong and hearty?”", "그 열병은 다 가셨고, 튼튼하고 정정하시오?\""], ["I saw here an opening to ask him about Jonathan, so I said:--", "나는 여기서 조너선에 관해 물을 틈을 보았기에 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“He was almost recovered, but he has been greatly upset by Mr.", "\"거의 회복되었는데, 호킨스 씨의 죽음으로 크게 동요했어요.\""], ["Hawkins’s death.” He interrupted:--", "그가 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“Oh, yes, I know, I know.", "\"오, 그렇소, 아오, 아오."], ["I have read your last two letters.” I went on:--", "부인의 지난 두 편지를 읽었소.\" 나는 말을 이었다."]], [["“I suppose this upset him, for when we were in town on Thursday last he had a sort of shock.”", "\"그것이 그를 뒤흔들었나 봐요, 지난 목요일 우리가 시내에 갔을 때 그가 일종의 충격을 받았거든요.\""]], [["“A shock, and after brain fever so soon!", "\"충격이라니, 뇌염을 앓은 지 그리 얼마 되지도 않아서!"], ["That was not good.", "좋지 않은 일이오."], ["What kind of a shock was it?”", "어떤 종류의 충격이었소?\""]], [["“He thought he saw some one who recalled something terrible, something which led to his brain fever.”", "\"무언가 끔찍한 것을, 그의 뇌염으로 이어졌던 무언가를 떠오르게 하는 누군가를 본 것 같다고 했어요.\""], ["And here the whole thing seemed to overwhelm me in a rush.", "그러자 그 모든 것이 한꺼번에 밀려들어 나를 짓누르는 듯했다."], ["The pity for Jonathan, the horror which he experienced, the whole fearful mystery of his diary, and the fear that has been brooding over me ever since, all came in a tumult.", "조너선에 대한 연민, 그가 겪은 공포, 그의 일기에 담긴 그 무서운 수수께끼 전부, 그리고 그 뒤로 줄곧 나를 짓눌러 온 두려움이 한데 뒤엉켜 몰려들었다."], ["I suppose I was hysterical, for I threw myself on my knees and held up my hands to him, and implored him to make my husband well again.", "짐작건대 나는 발작에 가까운 상태였던 것 같다. 나는 그 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 치켜들며, 제 남편을 다시 낫게 해 달라고 애원했으니 말이다."], ["He took my hands and raised me up, and made me sit on the sofa, and sat by me; he held my hand in his, and said to me with, oh, such infinite sweetness:--", "그는 내 손을 잡아 일으켜 소파에 앉히고는 내 곁에 앉았다. 그는 내 손을 자기 손에 쥐고서, 아, 한없이 다정하게 말했다."]], [["“My life is a barren and lonely one, and so full of work that I have not had much time for friendships; but since I have been summoned to here by my friend John Seward I have known so many good people and seen such nobility that I feel more than ever--and it has grown with my advancing years--the loneliness of my life.", "\"내 삶은 메마르고 외로운 삶이고, 일이 너무 많아 우정을 나눌 겨를이 별로 없었소. 그러나 내 벗 존 수어드의 부름을 받아 이곳에 온 뒤로, 나는 그토록 많은 선한 이들을 알게 되었고 그토록 고결한 모습을 보았기에, 그 어느 때보다도 — 그리고 나이가 들수록 더해 가는 — 내 삶의 외로움을 느끼게 되었소."], ["Believe, me, then, that I come here full of respect for you, and you have given me hope--hope, not in what I am seeking of, but that there are good women still left to make life happy--good women, whose lives and whose truths may make good lesson for the children that are to be.", "그러니 부디 믿어 주시오, 나는 부인에 대한 존경으로 가득 차 이곳에 왔으며, 부인은 나에게 희망을 주셨소 — 내가 찾고 있는 그것에 대한 희망이 아니라, 삶을 행복하게 만들어 줄 선한 여인이 아직 남아 있다는 희망 말이오. 그 삶과 그 진실이 앞으로 태어날 아이들에게 좋은 본보기가 되어 줄 선한 여인 말이오."], ["I am glad, glad, that I may here be of some use to you; for if your husband suffer, he suffer within the range of my study and experience.", "나는 기쁘오, 이곳에서 부인께 조금이나마 쓸모가 될 수 있어 기쁘오. 부군이 고통받는다면, 그것은 내 연구와 경험의 범위 안에서의 고통이기 때문이오."], ["I promise you that I will gladly do all for him that I can--all to make his life strong and manly, and your life a happy one. Now you must eat.", "약속하건대, 그를 위해 내가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 기꺼이 하겠소 — 그의 삶을 강건하고 사내답게, 그리고 부인의 삶을 행복하게 만들기 위한 모든 것을. 이제 부인은 무얼 좀 드셔야 하오."], ["You are overwrought and perhaps over-anxious.", "부인은 지나치게 긴장했고 어쩌면 지나치게 근심하고 있소."], ["Husband Jonathan would not like to see you so pale; and what he like not where he love, is not to his good.", "부군 조너선은 부인이 이리 핼쑥한 것을 보고 싶어 하지 않을 것이오. 사랑하는 이가 있는 곳에서 그가 싫어하는 일은 그에게 좋지 않은 법이오."], ["Therefore for his sake you must eat and smile.", "그러니 그를 위해서라도 부인은 먹고 웃어야 하오."], ["You have told me all about Lucy, and so now we shall not speak of it, lest it distress.", "부인은 루시에 관해 모두 말씀하셨으니, 이제 그 이야기는 더 하지 맙시다, 부인을 괴롭게 할까 두려운 까닭이오."], ["I shall stay in Exeter to-night, for I want to think much over what you have told me, and when I have thought I will ask you questions, if I may.", "나는 오늘 밤 엑서터에 머물 텐데, 부인이 들려주신 것을 곰곰이 생각해 보고 싶기 때문이오. 그리고 다 생각하고 나면, 괜찮다면 부인께 몇 가지 여쭙겠소."], ["And then, too, you will tell me of husband Jonathan’s trouble so far as you can, but not yet.", "그리고 그때 부인도 부군 조너선의 시름에 관해 아는 만큼 들려주시오, 그러나 아직은 아니오."], ["You must eat now; afterwards you shall tell me all.”", "지금은 드셔야 하오. 그 뒤에 모두 말씀해 주시오.\""]], [["After lunch, when we went back to the drawing-room, he said to me:--", "점심을 든 뒤 우리가 응접실로 돌아왔을 때, 그가 나에게 말했다."]], [["“And now tell me all about him.”", "\"이제 그분에 관해 모두 말해 주시오.\""], ["When it came to speaking to this great learned man, I began to fear that he would think me a weak fool, and Jonathan a madman--that journal is all so strange--and I hesitated to go on.", "막상 이 위대하고 학식 깊은 사람에게 말하려니, 나는 그가 나를 나약하고 어리석은 사람으로, 조너선을 미치광이로 여길까 봐 — 그 일기는 모든 것이 너무도 기이하니 — 두려워지기 시작했고, 말을 잇기가 망설여졌다."], ["But he was so sweet and kind, and he had promised to help, and I trusted him, so I said:--", "그러나 그는 너무도 다정하고 친절했으며, 돕겠다고 약속했고, 나는 그를 믿었기에 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Dr. Van Helsing, what I have to tell you is so queer that you must not laugh at me or at my husband.", "\"판 헬싱 박사님, 제가 말씀드리려는 것은 너무도 기이해서, 저나 제 남편을 비웃으시면 안 돼요."], ["I have been since yesterday in a sort of fever of doubt; you must be kind to me, and not think me foolish that I have even half believed some very strange things.”", "저는 어제부터 일종의 의심에 들떠 열에 들뜬 상태였어요. 저에게 친절히 대해 주시고, 제가 아주 기이한 일들을 반쯤이나마 믿었다 해서 어리석다 여기지 말아 주세요.\""], ["He reassured me by his manner as well as his words when he said:--", "그는 말로써도, 그 태도로써도 나를 안심시키며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, my dear, if you only know how strange is the matter regarding which I am here, it is you who would laugh.", "\"오, 부인, 내가 이곳에 온 까닭이 된 그 일이 얼마나 기이한지 부인이 안다면, 도리어 웃으실 사람은 부인일 거요."], ["I have learned not to think little of any one’s belief, no matter how strange it be.", "나는 누구의 믿음이든, 그것이 아무리 기이할지라도 가벼이 여기지 않는 법을 배웠소."], ["I have tried to keep an open mind; and it is not the ordinary things of life that could close it, but the strange things, the extraordinary things, the things that make one doubt if they be mad or sane.”", "나는 열린 마음을 지니려 애써 왔소. 그리고 그 마음을 닫을 수 있는 것은 삶의 평범한 일들이 아니라, 기이한 일들, 비범한 일들, 그것이 미친 것인지 제정신인지 의심하게 만드는 그런 일들이오.\""]], [["“Thank you, thank you, a thousand times!", "\"고맙습니다, 고맙습니다, 천 번이라도!"], ["You have taken a weight off my mind.", "제 마음의 짐을 덜어 주셨어요."], ["If you will let me, I shall give you a paper to read.", "허락해 주신다면 박사님께 읽으실 서류 하나를 드리겠어요."], ["It is long, but I have typewritten it out.", "길지만 타자기로 옮겨 두었어요."], ["It will tell you my trouble and Jonathan’s.", "그것이 제 시름과 조너선의 시름을 말해 줄 거예요."], ["It is the copy of his journal when abroad, and all that happened.", "그가 외국에 있을 때 쓴 일기와, 일어난 모든 일의 사본이에요."], ["I dare not say anything of it; you will read for yourself and judge.", "저는 그것에 관해 감히 아무 말도 못 하겠어요. 박사님이 직접 읽으시고 판단해 주세요."], ["And then when I see you, perhaps, you will be very kind and tell me what you think.”", "그런 다음 박사님을 다시 뵐 때, 부디 친절히 어떻게 생각하시는지 말씀해 주세요.\""]], [["“I promise,”", "\"약속하오,\""], ["he said as I gave him the papers; “I shall in the morning, so soon as I can, come to see you and your husband, if I may.”", "하고 그가 서류를 받으며 말했다. \"내일 아침, 되도록 빨리, 괜찮다면 부인과 부군을 뵈러 오겠소.\""]], [["“Jonathan will be here at half-past eleven, and you must come to lunch with us and see him then; you could catch the quick 3:34 train, which will leave you at Paddington before eight.”", "\"조너선이 열한 시 반에 올 거예요. 그러니 점심을 함께 드시러 오셔서 그때 그를 만나세요. 3시 34분 급행을 타시면 여덟 시 전에 패딩턴에 닿으실 거예요.\""], ["He was surprised at my knowledge of the trains off-hand, but he does not know that I have made up all the trains to and from Exeter, so that I may help Jonathan in case he is in a hurry.", "그는 내가 즉석에서 기차편을 꿰고 있는 것에 놀랐지만, 내가 엑서터를 오가는 모든 기차편을 외워 두었다는 것은 모른다. 조너선이 바쁠 때 돕기 위해서다. 그리하여 그는 서류를 가지고 떠났고, 나는 여기 앉아 생각에 잠겨 있다 — 무엇을 생각하는지도 모른 채."]], [["So he took the papers with him and went away, and I sit here thinking--thinking I don’t know what.", "「판 헬싱이 하커 부인에게 보낸 편지」(인편으로)"]], [["“25 September, 6 o’clock.", "\"9월 25일, 여섯 시."]], [["“Dear Madam Mina,--", "친애하는 미나 부인께,"]], [["“I have read your husband’s so wonderful diary.", "부군의 그 놀라운 일기를 읽었소."], ["You may sleep without doubt.", "의심 없이 편히 주무셔도 좋소."], ["Strange and terrible as it is, it is true!", "기이하고 끔찍하기는 하나, 그것은 사실이오!"], ["I will pledge my life on it.", "내 목숨을 걸어도 좋소."], ["It may be worse for others; but for him and you there is no dread.", "다른 이들에게는 더 나쁜 일일지도 모르나, 그분과 부인에게는 두려워할 것이 없소."], ["He is a noble fellow; and let me tell you from experience of men, that one who would do as he did in going down that wall and to that room--ay, and going a second time--is not one to be injured in permanence by a shock.", "그분은 고결한 사람이오. 사람을 겪어 온 경험으로 부인께 말하건대, 그 벽을 타고 내려가 그 방으로 들어간 일 — 그렇소, 게다가 두 번씩이나 들어간 일 — 그런 일을 해낸 사람은 한 번의 충격으로 영영 망가질 사람이 아니오."], ["His brain and his heart are all right; this I swear, before I have even seen him; so be at rest.", "그의 두뇌와 심장은 모두 멀쩡하오. 이것은 그를 만나 보기도 전에 내가 맹세할 수 있소. 그러니 안심하시오."], ["I shall have much to ask him of other things.", "다른 일들에 관해 그에게 물을 것이 많소."], ["I am blessed that to-day I come to see you, for I have learn all at once so much that again I am dazzle--dazzle more than ever, and I must think.", "오늘 부인을 뵈러 온 것이 나에게는 복이었으니, 단번에 너무도 많은 것을 알게 되어 나는 또다시 눈이 부시오 — 그 어느 때보다 더 눈이 부시오. 그래서 나는 생각을 좀 해야겠소. 더없이 충실한 벗,"]], [["“Yours the most faithful,", "에이브러햄 판 헬싱.\""]], [["“25 September, 6:30 p. m.", "「하커 부인이 판 헬싱에게 보낸 편지」"]], [["“My dear Dr. Van Helsing,--", "\"9월 25일, 오후 여섯 시 반."]], [["“A thousand thanks for your kind letter, which has taken a great weight off my mind.", "친애하는 판 헬싱 박사님께,\n제 마음의 큰 짐을 덜어 준 박사님의 친절한 편지에 천 번 감사드립니다."], ["And yet, if it be true, what terrible things there are in the world, and what an awful thing if that man, that monster, be really in London! I fear to think.", "그런데도 만일 그것이 사실이라면, 세상에 얼마나 끔찍한 일들이 있는 것이며, 그 남자가, 그 괴물이 정말로 런던에 있다면 이 얼마나 무서운 일인지요! 생각만 해도 두렵습니다."], ["I have this moment, whilst writing, had a wire from Jonathan, saying that he leaves by the 6:25 to-night from Launceston and will be here at 10:18, so that I shall have no fear to-night.", "지금 이 편지를 쓰는 중에 조너선에게서 전보를 받았는데, 오늘 밤 6시 25분 기차로 론서스턴을 떠나 10시 18분에 이곳에 닿을 거라고 합니다. 그러니 오늘 밤은 두려울 것이 없겠어요."], ["Will you, therefore, instead of lunching with us, please come to breakfast at eight o’clock, if this be not too early for you?", "그러니 저희와 점심을 드시는 대신, 너무 이르지 않으시다면 여덟 시에 아침을 드시러 와 주시겠어요?"], ["You can get away, if you are in a hurry, by the 10:30 train, which will bring you to Paddington by 2:35.", "바쁘시면 10시 30분 기차로 떠나실 수 있는데, 그러면 2시 35분에 패딩턴에 닿으실 거예요."], ["Do not answer this, as I shall take it that, if I do not hear, you will come to breakfast.", "이 편지에는 답하지 마세요. 소식이 없으면 아침을 드시러 오시는 것으로 알겠습니다."]], [["“Believe me,", "부디 믿어 주세요,"]], [["“Your faithful and grateful friend,", "박사님의 충실하고 감사하는 벗,\n미나 하커.\""]], [["Jonathan Harker’s Journal.", "「조너선 하커의 일기」"]], [["26 September.--I thought never to write in this diary again, but the time has come.", "9월 26일. — 이 일기에 다시는 쓰지 않으리라 여겼건만, 그때가 왔다."], ["When I got home last night Mina had supper ready, and when we had supped she told me of Van Helsing’s visit, and of her having given him the two diaries copied out, and of how anxious she has been about me.", "어젯밤 집에 돌아오니 미나가 저녁을 차려 놓았고, 저녁을 들고 나자 미나는 판 헬싱의 방문에 관해, 두 일기를 옮겨 적어 그에게 건넨 일에 관해, 그리고 그녀가 나 때문에 얼마나 마음 졸였는지를 들려주었다."], ["She showed me in the doctor’s letter that all I wrote down was true.", "그녀는 내가 적어 둔 모든 것이 사실이라는 박사의 편지를 보여 주었다."], ["It seems to have made a new man of me.", "그것이 나를 새사람으로 만든 듯하다."], ["It was the doubt as to the reality of the whole thing that knocked me over. I felt impotent, and in the dark, and distrustful.", "나를 쓰러뜨린 것은 그 모든 일이 과연 실제였는가 하는 의심이었다. 나는 무력하고, 어둠 속에 있으며, 모든 것을 못 믿는 듯한 기분이었다."], ["But, now that I know, I am not afraid, even of the Count.", "그러나 이제 그것이 사실임을 아니, 나는 두렵지 않다, 백작조차도."], ["He has succeeded after all, then, in his design in getting to London, and it was he I saw.", "그렇다면 그는 결국 런던에 들어오려던 자신의 계략에 성공한 것이고, 내가 본 것은 바로 그였다."], ["He has got younger, and how?", "그는 더 젊어졌으니, 대체 어떻게?"], ["Van Helsing is the man to unmask him and hunt him out, if he is anything like what Mina says.", "미나의 말이 조금이라도 사실이라면, 판 헬싱이야말로 그의 가면을 벗기고 그를 추적해 낼 사람이다."], ["We sat late, and talked it all over.", "우리는 늦게까지 앉아 그 모든 것을 의논했다."], ["Mina is dressing, and I shall call at the hotel in a few minutes and bring him over....", "미나가 옷을 갈아입고 있으니, 나는 잠시 후 호텔로 들러 그를 데려와야겠다……."]], [["He was, I think, surprised to see me.", "내 생각에 그는 나를 보고 놀란 듯했다."], ["When I came into the room where he was, and introduced myself, he took me by the shoulder, and turned my face round to the light, and said, after a sharp scrutiny:--", "그가 있는 방에 들어가 내 소개를 하자, 그는 내 어깨를 잡고 내 얼굴을 빛 쪽으로 돌리더니, 날카롭게 살펴본 뒤에 말했다."]], [["“But Madam Mina told me you were ill, that you had had a shock.”", "\"그런데 미나 부인이 그대가 아프다고, 충격을 받았다고 일러주었는데.\""], ["It was so funny to hear my wife called “Madam Mina” by this kindly, strong-faced old man.", "이 다정하고 강인한 얼굴의 노인이 내 아내를 \"미나 부인\"이라 부르는 것을 들으니 어찌나 우습던지."], ["I smiled, and said:--", "나는 미소를 지으며 말했다."]], [["“I was ill, I have had a shock; but you have cured me already.” “And how?”", "\"제가 아팠던 건 사실이고, 충격을 받은 것도 사실이지요. 하지만 선생님이 이미 저를 낫게 해 주셨어요.\""]], [["“By your letter to Mina last night.", "\"어떻게 말이오?\" \"어젯밤 미나에게 보내신 편지로요."], ["I was in doubt, and then everything took a hue of unreality, and I did not know what to trust, even the evidence of my own senses.", "저는 의심에 빠져 있었고, 그러자 모든 것이 비현실의 빛깔을 띠어, 무엇을 믿어야 할지 — 제 감각의 증거조차 — 알 수가 없었어요."], ["Not knowing what to trust, I did not know what to do; and so had only to keep on working in what had hitherto been the groove of my life.", "무엇을 믿어야 할지 모르니, 무엇을 해야 할지도 몰랐고, 그래서 그저 여태껏 제 삶의 정해진 홈이었던 일을 계속할 수밖에 없었지요."], ["The groove ceased to avail me, and I mistrusted myself.", "그런데 그 홈이 더는 소용없어졌고, 저는 제 자신마저 못 믿게 되었어요."], ["Doctor, you don’t know what it is to doubt everything, even yourself.", "박사님, 모든 것을, 자기 자신조차 의심한다는 것이 어떤 것인지 박사님은 모르실 거예요."], ["No, you don’t; you couldn’t with eyebrows like yours.”", "아니요, 모르세요. 그런 눈썹을 지니고서는 그러실 수가 없지요.\""], ["He seemed pleased, and laughed as he said:--", "그는 흐뭇한 듯, 웃으며 말했다."]], [["“So! You are physiognomist.", "\"그래! 그대는 관상가로구려."], ["I learn more here with each hour.", "나는 이곳에서 시간마다 더 배우는구려."], ["I am with so much pleasure coming to you to breakfast; and, oh, sir, you will pardon praise from an old man, but you are blessed in your wife.”", "아주 즐거운 마음으로 그대의 아침 식사에 가는 길이오. 그리고, 아, 그대여, 늙은이의 칭찬을 용서하시오만, 그대는 부인으로 하여 복받은 사람이오.\""], ["I would listen to him go on praising Mina for a day, so I simply nodded and stood silent.", "나는 그가 미나를 칭찬하는 말이라면 하루 종일이라도 들을 수 있었기에, 그저 고개를 끄덕이고 잠자코 서 있었다."]], [["“She is one of God’s women, fashioned by His own hand to show us men and other women that there is a heaven where we can enter, and that its light can be here on earth.", "\"그분은 하느님의 여인 가운데 하나로, 우리 남자들과 다른 여인들에게 우리가 들어갈 수 있는 천국이 있으며 그 빛이 이 땅에도 있을 수 있음을 보여 주려, 그분 손수 빚으신 사람이오."], ["So true, so sweet, so noble, so little an egoist--and that, let me tell you, is much in this age, so sceptical and selfish. And you, sir--I have read all the letters to poor Miss Lucy, and some of them speak of you, so I know you since some days from the knowing of others; but I have seen your true self since last night.", "그토록 참되고, 그토록 다정하며, 그토록 고결하고, 그토록 자기를 내세우지 않으니 — 그리고 그것은, 단언하건대, 이토록 회의적이고 이기적인 시대에 참으로 대단한 것이오. 그리고 그대 말이오 — 나는 가엾은 루시 양에게 보낸 편지를 모두 읽었는데, 그중 몇 통은 그대 이야기를 하고 있어, 나는 며칠 전부터 다른 이들의 글로써 그대를 알고 있었소."], ["You will give me your hand, will you not?", "그러나 그대의 참된 모습을 본 것은 어젯밤부터요."], ["And let us be friends for all our lives.”", "나에게 손을 내밀어 주겠소? 그리고 우리 평생토록 벗이 됩시다.\""]], [["We shook hands, and he was so earnest and so kind that it made me quite choky.", "우리는 악수를 나누었는데, 그가 어찌나 진지하고 다정하던지 나는 그만 목이 메었다."]], [["“And now,”", "\"그럼 이제,\""], ["he said, “may I ask you for some more help?", "하고 그가 말했다. \"도움을 좀 더 청해도 되겠소?"], ["I have a great task to do, and at the beginning it is to know. You can help me here.", "나에게는 해야 할 큰 일이 있는데, 그 처음은 아는 것이오. 여기서 그대가 나를 도울 수 있소."], ["Can you tell me what went before your going to Transylvania?", "그대가 트란실바니아로 가기 전에 무슨 일이 있었는지 말해 줄 수 있겠소?"], ["Later on I may ask more help, and of a different kind; but at first this will do.”", "나중에 다른 종류의 도움을 더 청할지도 모르나, 우선은 이것이면 되오.\""]], [["“Look here, sir,”", "\"보세요, 선생님,\""], ["I said, “does what you have to do concern the Count?”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"선생님이 하셔야 할 일이 백작과 관계된 것입니까?\""]], [["“It does,”", "\"그렇소,\""], ["he said solemnly.", "하고 그가 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“Then I am with you heart and soul. As you go by the 10:30 train, you will not have time to read them; but I shall get the bundle of papers.", "\"그렇다면 저는 온 마음과 영혼을 다해 선생님과 함께하겠습니다. 10시 30분 기차로 가시니 그것들을 읽을 시간은 없으시겠지만, 제가 서류 뭉치를 가져다 드릴게요."], ["You can take them with you and read them in the train.”", "가져가셔서 기차에서 읽으시면 됩니다.\""]], [["After breakfast I saw him to the station.", "아침 식사 뒤 나는 그를 역까지 배웅했다."], ["When we were parting he said:--", "헤어질 때 그가 말했다."]], [["“Perhaps you will come to town if I send to you, and take Madam Mina too.”", "\"내가 사람을 보내거든 시내로 와 주시오, 미나 부인도 함께 데려오고.\""]], [["“We shall both come when you will,” I said.", "\"부르시면 저희 둘 다 가겠습니다,\" 하고 내가 말했다."]], [["I had got him the morning papers and the London papers of the previous night, and while we were talking at the carriage window, waiting for the train to start, he was turning them over.", "나는 그에게 조간신문과 전날 밤의 런던 신문들을 사다 주었는데, 우리가 객차 창가에서 이야기하며 기차가 떠나기를 기다리는 동안 그는 그것들을 뒤적이고 있었다."], ["His eyes suddenly seemed to catch something in one of them, “The Westminster Gazette”--I knew it by the colour--and he grew quite white.", "그러다 문득 그중 하나에서 — 「웨스트민스터 가제트」였는데, 나는 색깔로 알아보았다 — 무언가에 눈길이 걸린 듯하더니, 그의 얼굴이 새파랗게 질렸다."], ["He read something intently, groaning to himself: “Mein Gott!", "그는 무언가를 골똘히 읽으며 혼자 끙끙거렸다. \"마인 고트!"], ["Mein Gott!", "마인 고트!"], ["So soon!", "이리도 빨리!"], ["so soon!” I do not think he remembered me at the moment.", "이리도 빨리!\" 그 순간 그는 나를 기억하지 못하는 듯했다."], ["Just then the whistle blew, and the train moved off.", "바로 그때 기적이 울리고 기차가 움직이기 시작했다. 그제야 그는 정신을 차리고 창밖으로 몸을 내밀어 손을 흔들며 외쳤다."], ["This recalled him to himself, and he leaned out of the window and waved his hand, calling out: “Love to Madam Mina; I shall write so soon as ever I can.”", "\"미나 부인께 안부를! 되도록 빨리 편지하겠소.\" * 마인 고트: 독일어로 \"하느님 맙소사\"라는 뜻."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["26 September.--Truly there is no such thing as finality.", "9월 26일. — 참으로 끝이라는 것은 없는 모양이다."], ["Not a week since I said “Finis,” and yet here I am starting fresh again, or rather going on with the same record.", "\"끝\"이라고 적은 지 일주일도 되지 않았건만, 나는 이렇게 또다시 새로 시작하고 있다, 아니 오히려 같은 기록을 이어가고 있다."], ["Until this afternoon I had no cause to think of what is done.", "오늘 오후까지는 끝난 일을 떠올릴 까닭이 없었다."], ["Renfield had become, to all intents, as sane as he ever was.", "렌필드는 사실상 예전 어느 때 못지않게 제정신이 되어 있었다."], ["He was already well ahead with his fly business; and he had just started in the spider line also; so he had not been of any trouble to me.", "그는 이미 파리 사업을 한참 진척시켜 놓았고, 막 거미 분야에도 손을 대기 시작한 터라, 나에게 별 골칫거리가 되지 않았다."], ["I had a letter from Arthur, written on Sunday, and from it I gather that he is bearing up wonderfully well.", "일요일에 쓴 아서의 편지를 받았는데, 그것으로 미루어 그는 놀라우리만치 잘 견뎌 내고 있는 듯하다."], ["Quincey Morris is with him, and that is much of a help, for he himself is a bubbling well of good spirits.", "퀸시 모리스가 그와 함께 있는데, 그것이 큰 도움이 되니, 퀸시 자신이 솟구치는 활기의 샘 같은 사람인 까닭이다."], ["Quincey wrote me a line too, and from him I hear that Arthur is beginning to recover something of his old buoyancy; so as to them all my mind is at rest.", "퀸시도 나에게 몇 자 적어 보냈는데, 그에게서 아서가 옛 쾌활함을 조금씩 되찾기 시작했다는 소식을 들었다. 그러니 그들에 관해서라면 내 마음은 놓인다."], ["As for myself, I was settling down to my work with the enthusiasm which I used to have for it, so that I might fairly have said that the wound which poor Lucy left on me was becoming cicatrised.", "나 자신으로 말하자면, 예전에 품었던 그 열의로 다시 일에 자리 잡아 가던 참이라, 가엾은 루시가 내게 남긴 상처가 아물어 가고 있다고 제법 말할 수 있을 정도였다."], ["Everything is, however, now reopened; and what is to be the end God only knows.", "그러나 이제 모든 것이 다시 헤집어졌고, 그 끝이 어찌 될지는 오직 하느님만이 아신다."], ["I have an idea that Van Helsing thinks he knows, too, but he will only let out enough at a time to whet curiosity.", "내 생각에 판 헬싱도 자기가 그 끝을 안다고 여기는 듯하나, 그는 한 번에 호기심을 돋울 만큼만 흘릴 뿐이다."], ["He went to Exeter yesterday, and stayed there all night.", "그는 어제 엑서터로 가 거기서 밤을 보냈다."], ["To-day he came back, and almost bounded into the room at about half-past five o’clock, and thrust last night’s “Westminster Gazette” into my hand.", "오늘 돌아와서, 다섯 시 반쯤 거의 방 안으로 뛰어들다시피 하더니, 어젯밤의 「웨스트민스터 가제트」를 내 손에 쑥 밀어 넣었다."]], [["“What do you think of that?”", "\"이걸 어떻게 생각하나?\""], ["he asked as he stood back and folded his arms.", "그가 물러서서 팔짱을 끼며 물었다."]], [["I looked over the paper, for I really did not know what he meant; but he took it from me and pointed out a paragraph about children being decoyed away at Hampstead.", "나는 신문을 훑어보았는데, 정말이지 그가 무슨 말을 하는지 알 수 없었기 때문이다. 그러자 그는 그것을 내게서 도로 가져가더니, 햄스테드에서 아이들이 꾐에 빠져 끌려갔다는 한 단락을 가리켜 보였다."], ["It did not convey much to me, until I reached a passage where it described small punctured wounds on their throats.", "그것은 나에게 별로 와닿지 않았는데, 아이들 목에 난 작은 구멍 모양의 상처를 묘사한 대목에 이르러서였다."], ["An idea struck me, and I looked up.", "어떤 생각이 퍼뜩 떠올라 나는 고개를 들었다."], ["“Well?”", "\"어떤가?\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“It is like poor Lucy’s.”", "\"가엾은 루시의 것과 같군요.\""]], [["“And what do you make of it?”", "\"그래서 자네는 그걸 어떻게 보나?\""]], [["“Simply that there is some cause in common. Whatever it was that injured her has injured them.”", "\"단지 어떤 공통된 원인이 있다는 것이지요. 그녀에게 상처를 입힌 것이 무엇이었든, 그것이 이 아이들에게도 상처를 입혔다는 거고요.\""], ["I did not quite understand his answer:--", "나는 그의 대답을 온전히 이해하지 못했다."]], [["“That is true indirectly, but not directly.”", "\"그것은 간접적으로는 사실이나, 직접적으로는 아니야.\""]], [["“How do you mean, Professor?”", "\"무슨 말씀이신지요, 교수님?\""], ["I asked. I was a little inclined to take his seriousness lightly--for, after all, four days of rest and freedom from burning, harrowing anxiety does help to restore one’s spirits--but when I saw his face, it sobered me.", "하고 내가 물었다. 나는 그의 진지함을 조금은 가볍게 받아들이고 싶었으니 — 어쨌든 나흘간의 휴식과, 타들어 가는 듯 사람을 괴롭히던 시름에서 벗어난 자유가 사람의 기운을 돋우는 데 보탬이 되기는 하는 것이다 — 그러나 그의 얼굴을 보자 마음이 가라앉았다."], ["Never, even in the midst of our despair about poor Lucy, had he looked more stern.", "가엾은 루시 때문에 절망에 빠져 있던 그 와중에도 그가 이보다 더 준엄해 보인 적은 없었다."]], [["“Tell me!”", "\"말해 보게!\""], ["I said. “I can hazard no opinion.", "하고 내가 말했다. \"저는 아무 의견도 함부로 낼 수가 없습니다."], ["I do not know what to think, and I have no data on which to found a conjecture.”", "무엇을 생각해야 할지 모르겠고, 어떤 추측을 세울 만한 자료도 없으니까요.\""]], [["“Do you mean to tell me, friend John, that you have no suspicion as to what poor Lucy died of; not after all the hints given, not only by events, but by me?”", "\"존, 친구여, 자네는 가엾은 루시가 무엇 때문에 죽었는지 짐작 가는 바가 전혀 없단 말인가. 그 모든 암시를 받고도, 사건들로뿐만 아니라 나로부터도 받고서도 말일세?\""]], [["“Of nervous prostration following on great loss or waste of blood.”", "\"큰 출혈 혹은 혈액 소모에 뒤따른 신경 쇠약 때문이지요.\""]], [["“And how the blood lost or waste?”", "\"그럼 그 피는 어떻게 잃거나 소모되었나?\""], ["I shook my head.", "나는 고개를 저었다."], ["He stepped over and sat down beside me, and went on:--", "그는 다가와 내 곁에 앉더니 말을 이었다."]], [["“You are clever man, friend John; you reason well, and your wit is bold; but you are too prejudiced.", "\"자네는 영리한 사람일세, 존. 추론을 잘하고, 그 재기가 대담하지. 그러나 자네는 편견이 너무 심해."], ["You do not let your eyes see nor your ears hear, and that which is outside your daily life is not of account to you.", "자네는 자네 눈으로 하여금 보게 하지도, 자네 귀로 하여금 듣게 하지도 않고, 자네 일상 밖에 있는 것은 자네에게 아무 의미가 없는 게야."], ["Do you not think that there are things which you cannot understand, and yet which are; that some people see things that others cannot?", "자네가 이해할 수 없으면서도 엄연히 존재하는 것들이 있다고는 생각지 않나? 어떤 이들은 다른 이들이 보지 못하는 것을 본다고는 생각지 않나?"], ["But there are things old and new which must not be contemplate by men’s eyes, because they know--or think they know--some things which other men have told them.", "그러나 오래된 것이든 새로운 것이든, 사람의 눈으로 헤아려서는 안 될 것들이 있으니, 그들이 다른 사람들에게서 전해 들은 어떤 것들을 안다고 — 혹은 안다고 여기기 — 때문이지."], ["Ah, it is the fault of our science that it wants to explain all; and if it explain not, then it says there is nothing to explain.", "아, 모든 것을 설명하려 드는 것이 우리 과학의 결함일세. 그리고 설명하지 못하면, 설명할 것이 아무것도 없다고 말하지."], ["But yet we see around us every day the growth of new beliefs, which think themselves new; and which are yet but the old, which pretend to be young--like the fine ladies at the opera.", "그러나 우리는 날마다 우리 둘레에서 스스로 새롭다고 여기는 새 믿음들이 자라나는 것을 보네. 그것들은 사실 낡은 것이면서 젊은 척하는 것들이지 — 오페라 극장의 멋쟁이 귀부인들처럼 말일세."], ["I suppose now you do not believe in corporeal transference. No? Nor in materialisation. No?", "짐작건대 자네는 육체의 전이 같은 것은 믿지 않겠지. 아니라고? 물질화도? 아니라고?"], ["Nor in astral bodies. No?", "성신체(星身體)도? 아니라고?"], ["Nor in the reading of thought. No?", "생각을 읽는 것도? 아니라고?"], ["Nor in hypnotism----”", "최면술도——\""]], [["“Yes,” I said.", "\"믿습니다,\""], ["“Charcot has proved that pretty well.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"샤르코가 그것을 꽤 잘 입증했지요.\""], ["He smiled as he went on: “Then you are satisfied as to it. Yes?", "그는 미소 지으며 말을 이었다. \"그렇다면 자네는 그것에 관해서는 수긍하는군. 그렇지?"], ["And of course then you understand how it act, and can follow the mind of the great Charcot--alas that he is no more!--into the very soul of the patient that he influence. No?", "그렇다면 물론 자네는 그것이 어떻게 작용하는지 이해하고, 위대한 샤르코의 — 아, 이제는 세상에 없으니 애석하구나! — 정신을 따라, 그가 영향을 미치는 환자의 영혼 깊숙한 곳까지 들어갈 수 있겠지. 아니라고?"], ["Then, friend John, am I to take it that you simply accept fact, and are satisfied to let from premise to conclusion be a blank? No?", "그렇다면, 존, 친구여, 자네가 그저 사실만 받아들이고, 전제에서 결론에 이르는 과정은 백지로 두는 데 만족한다고 여겨도 되겠나? 아니라고?"], ["Then tell me--for I am student of the brain--how you accept the hypnotism and reject the thought reading.", "그렇다면 말해 보게 — 나는 두뇌를 연구하는 사람이니 — 어찌하여 자네는 최면술은 받아들이면서 생각 읽기는 물리치는가."], ["Let me tell you, my friend, that there are things done to-day in electrical science which would have been deemed unholy by the very men who discovered electricity--who would themselves not so long before have been burned as wizards.", "친구여, 내가 말해 두겠는데, 오늘날 전기 과학에서 행해지는 일들 가운데에는, 전기를 발견한 바로 그 사람들이라면 불경하다 여겼을 것들이 있다네 — 그들 자신도 그리 오래지 않은 옛날이라면 마법사로 화형당했을 사람들인데 말일세."], ["There are always mysteries in life.", "삶에는 언제나 신비가 있는 법이지."], ["Why was it that Methuselah lived nine hundred years, and ‘Old Parr’ one hundred and sixty-nine, and yet that poor Lucy, with four men’s blood in her poor veins, could not live even one day? For, had she live one more day, we could have save her.", "어찌하여 므두셀라는 구백 년을 살았고, '늙은 파'는 백육십구 년을 살았건만, 그 가엾은 루시는 가엾은 핏줄에 네 남자의 피를 받고도 단 하루조차 살지 못했을까? 만일 그녀가 단 하루만 더 살았더라면 우리는 그녀를 구할 수 있었을 텐데 말일세."], ["Do you know all the mystery of life and death?", "자네는 삶과 죽음의 모든 신비를 아는가?"], ["Do you know the altogether of comparative anatomy and can say wherefore the qualities of brutes are in some men, and not in others?", "비교 해부학의 전부를 알아서, 어찌하여 짐승의 성질이 어떤 사람에게는 있고 어떤 사람에게는 없는지 말할 수 있는가?"], ["Can you tell me why, when other spiders die small and soon, that one great spider lived for centuries in the tower of the old Spanish church and grew and grew, till, on descending, he could drink the oil of all the church lamps?", "다른 거미들은 작은 채로 일찍 죽건만, 어찌하여 그 한 마리 거대한 거미는 옛 스페인 성당의 탑 속에서 수백 년을 살며 자라고 또 자라, 마침내 내려와서는 성당의 등잔 기름을 죄다 마실 수 있었는지 말할 수 있는가?"], ["Can you tell me why in the Pampas, ay and elsewhere, there are bats that come at night and open the veins of cattle and horses and suck dry their veins; how in some islands of the Western seas there are bats which hang on the trees all day, and those who have seen describe as like giant nuts or pods, and that when the sailors sleep on the deck, because that it is hot, flit down on them, and then--and then in the morning are found dead men, white as even Miss Lucy was?”", "어찌하여 팜파스에서, 그렇지, 또 다른 곳에서도, 밤이 되면 찾아와 소와 말의 핏줄을 열어 그 핏줄을 바싹 빨아먹는 박쥐들이 있는지, 어찌하여 서쪽 바다의 어떤 섬들에는 온종일 나무에 매달려 있어 — 본 사람들은 거대한 견과나 꼬투리 같다고 묘사하는 — 박쥐들이 있어, 날이 더워 선원들이 갑판에서 잠들면 그들 위로 날아 내려와서는, 그러고는 — 그러고는 아침이면 미나 양만큼이나 새하얗게 죽은 사람들이 발견되는지, 자네는 말할 수 있는가?\""]], [["“Good God, Professor!”", "\"맙소사, 교수님!\""], ["I said, starting up. “Do you mean to tell me that Lucy was bitten by such a bat; and that such a thing is here in London in the nineteenth century?”", "하고 나는 벌떡 일어서며 말했다. \"루시가 그런 박쥐에게 물렸으며, 그런 것이 19세기 런던에 있다는 말씀입니까?\""], ["He waved his hand for silence, and went on:--", "그는 잠자코 있으라는 듯 손을 저으며 말을 이었다."]], [["“Can you tell me why the tortoise lives more long than generations of men; why the elephant goes on and on till he have seen dynasties; and why the parrot never die only of bite of cat or dog or other complaint?", "\"어찌하여 거북은 여러 세대의 사람들보다 더 오래 사는지, 어찌하여 코끼리는 여러 왕조를 지켜볼 만큼 살고 또 사는지, 어찌하여 앵무새는 고양이나 개에게 물리거나 다른 병에 걸리지 않는 한 결코 죽지 않는지, 자네는 말할 수 있는가?"], ["Can you tell me why men believe in all ages and places that there are some few who live on always if they be permit; that there are men and women who cannot die?", "어찌하여 모든 시대와 모든 곳에서 사람들은, 허락받기만 하면 영원히 살아가는 소수가 있다고, 죽을 수 없는 남자와 여자가 있다고 믿어 왔는지 말할 수 있는가?"], ["We all know--because science has vouched for the fact--that there have been toads shut up in rocks for thousands of years, shut in one so small hole that only hold him since the youth of the world.", "우리 모두가 알듯이 — 과학이 그 사실을 보증했으니 — 수천 년 동안 바위 속에 갇혀 있던 두꺼비들이 있었네. 세상이 젊었던 때부터 그것을 가둘 만큼만 작은 구멍 하나 속에 갇혀서 말이지."], ["Can you tell me how the Indian fakir can make himself to die and have been buried, and his grave sealed and corn sowed on it, and the corn reaped and be cut and sown and reaped and cut again, and then men come and take away the unbroken seal and that there lie the Indian fakir, not dead, but that rise up and walk amongst them as before?” Here I interrupted him.", "인도의 고행자가 어찌하여 스스로 죽어 매장되고, 그 무덤을 봉인하고 그 위에 곡식을 뿌려, 그 곡식이 자라 베이고 다시 뿌려져 베이기를 거듭한 뒤에, 사람들이 와서 깨지지 않은 봉인을 걷어 내면, 거기 그 인도 고행자가 죽지 않은 채 누워 있다가, 일어나 예전처럼 사람들 사이를 걸어 다니는지, 자네는 말할 수 있는가?\" 여기서 나는 그의 말을 가로막았다."], ["I was getting bewildered; he so crowded on my mind his list of nature’s eccentricities and possible impossibilities that my imagination was getting fired.", "나는 점점 어리둥절해지고 있었다. 그가 자연의 기이한 사례들과 가능한 불가능들의 목록을 어찌나 내 머릿속에 가득 쏟아붓는지, 내 상상력에 불이 붙기 시작했던 것이다."], ["I had a dim idea that he was teaching me some lesson, as long ago he used to do in his study at Amsterdam; but he used then to tell me the thing, so that I could have the object of thought in mind all the time.", "나는 그가 나에게 어떤 가르침을 주려 한다는 것을 어렴풋이 알았으니, 오래전 암스테르담의 그의 서재에서 그러곤 했던 것처럼 말이다. 그러나 그때 그는 으레 본론을 먼저 일러주어, 내가 줄곧 생각의 대상을 마음에 두고 있을 수 있게 했다."], ["But now I was without this help, yet I wanted to follow him, so I said:--", "그런데 지금은 그런 도움이 없었으니, 그래도 나는 그를 따라가고 싶어 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Professor, let me be your pet student again.", "\"교수님, 저를 다시 교수님의 귀여운 학생이 되게 해 주십시오."], ["Tell me the thesis, so that I may apply your knowledge as you go on.", "논제를 일러 주셔서, 말씀을 이어 가시는 동안 제가 교수님의 지식을 적용할 수 있게 해 주십시오."], ["At present I am going in my mind from point to point as a mad man, and not a sane one, follows an idea.", "지금 저는 제정신인 사람이 아니라 미친 사람이 어떤 생각을 좇듯, 마음속에서 이 점에서 저 점으로 옮겨 다니고 있습니다."], ["I feel like a novice lumbering through a bog in a mist, jumping from one tussock to another in the mere blind effort to move on without knowing where I am going.”", "마치 안개 속 늪을 허우적대며 헤쳐 나가는 풋내기처럼, 제가 어디로 가는지도 모르면서 그저 앞으로 나아가려는 맹목적인 안간힘으로 이 풀 덤불에서 저 풀 덤불로 건너뛰는 기분입니다.\""]], [["“That is good image,”", "\"좋은 비유일세,\""], ["he said. “Well, I shall tell you.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그럼 일러 주지."], ["My thesis is this: I want you to believe.”", "내 논제는 이것일세. 나는 자네가 믿기를 바라네.\""]], [["“To believe what?”", "\"무엇을 믿으라는 말씀이신지요?\""]], [["“To believe in things that you cannot.", "\"자네가 믿을 수 없는 것들을 믿으라는 걸세."], ["Let me illustrate.", "예를 들어 보겠네."], ["I heard once of an American who so defined faith: ‘that faculty which enables us to believe things which we know to be untrue.’ For one, I follow that man.", "언젠가 어떤 미국인이 믿음을 이렇게 정의했다는 이야기를 들었지. '사실이 아님을 우리가 아는 것들을 믿게 해 주는 능력'이라고 말일세. 적어도 나는 그 사람의 말을 따르네."], ["He meant that we shall have an open mind, and not let a little bit of truth check the rush of a big truth, like a small rock does a railway truck.", "그가 뜻한 바는, 우리가 열린 마음을 지녀야 하며, 작은 바위가 철도 화차를 멈춰 세우듯, 작은 진실 한 조각이 큰 진실의 돌진을 가로막게 두어서는 안 된다는 것일세."], ["We get the small truth first. Good!", "우리는 먼저 작은 진실을 얻지. 좋아!"], ["We keep him, and we value him; but all the same we must not let him think himself all the truth in the universe.”", "그것을 간직하고, 소중히 여기네. 그러나 그렇더라도 그것이 스스로를 우주의 진실 전부라 여기게 두어서는 안 되는 게야.\""]], [["“Then you want me not to let some previous conviction injure the receptivity of my mind with regard to some strange matter. Do I read your lesson aright?”", "\"그렇다면 교수님은 제가 어떤 기이한 문제에 관하여, 이전의 어떤 확신이 제 마음의 수용성을 해치게 두지 말라는 말씀이시군요. 제가 교수님의 가르침을 옳게 읽은 것인지요?\""]], [["“Ah, you are my favourite pupil still.", "\"아, 자네는 여전히 내가 가장 아끼는 제자일세."], ["It is worth to teach you.", "자네를 가르치는 보람이 있어."], ["Now that you are willing to understand, you have taken the first step to understand.", "이제 자네가 이해하려는 마음을 먹었으니, 이해를 향한 첫걸음을 뗀 셈이지."], ["You think then that those so small holes in the children’s throats were made by the same that made the hole in Miss Lucy?”", "그래, 자네는 그 아이들 목에 난 그 작은 구멍들이 루시 양에게 구멍을 낸 바로 그것에 의해 생긴 것이라 여기나?\""]], [["“I suppose so.”", "\"그런 듯합니다.\""], ["He stood up and said solemnly:--", "그는 일어서서 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“Then you are wrong.", "\"그렇다면 자네는 틀렸어."], ["Oh, would it were so!", "오, 차라리 그렇다면 좋으련만!"], ["but alas! no.", "그러나 아아! 아닐세."], ["It is worse, far, far worse.”", "그보다 더 나쁘다네, 훨씬, 훨씬 더 나빠.\""]], [["“In God’s name, Professor Van Helsing, what do you mean?” I cried.", "\"제발, 판 헬싱 교수님, 대체 무슨 말씀이십니까?\" 하고 나는 외쳤다."]], [["He threw himself with a despairing gesture into a chair, and placed his elbows on the table, covering his face with his hands as he spoke:--", "그는 절망스러운 몸짓으로 의자에 몸을 던지고는, 식탁에 두 팔꿈치를 괴고 두 손으로 얼굴을 가린 채 말했다."]], [["“They were made by Miss Lucy!”", "\"그 구멍들은 루시 양이 낸 것일세!\""]], [["DR. SEWARD’S DIARY--continued.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」 — 계속."]], [["For a while sheer anger mastered me; it was as if he had during her life struck Lucy on the face.", "한동안은 격렬한 분노가 나를 사로잡았다. 마치 그가 생전의 루시의 뺨을 후려치기라도 한 것 같았다."], ["I smote the table hard and rose up as I said to him:--", "나는 탁자를 세게 내리치고 일어서며 그에게 말했다."]], [["“Dr. Van Helsing, are you mad?”", "\"반 헬싱 박사, 미치셨소?\""], ["He raised his head and looked at me, and somehow the tenderness of his face calmed me at once.", "그는 고개를 들어 나를 바라보았는데, 어쩐 일인지 그의 얼굴에 어린 부드러움이 단번에 나를 진정시켰다."], ["“Would I were!”", "\"차라리 미쳤더라면!\""], ["he said. “Madness were easy to bear compared with truth like this.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"이런 진실에 견주면 광기란 견디기 쉬운 것이오."], ["Oh, my friend, why, think you, did I go so far round, why take so long to tell you so simple a thing?", "아, 나의 벗이여, 어찌하여 내가 이토록 멀리 에둘러 왔겠소? 어찌하여 이토록 간단한 일을 말하는 데 그리도 오래 걸렸겠소?"], ["Was it because I hate you and have hated you all my life?", "내가 당신을 미워해서, 평생 미워해왔기 때문이겠소?"], ["Was it because I wished to give you pain?", "당신에게 고통을 주고 싶어서였겠소?"], ["Was it that I wanted, now so late, revenge for that time when you saved my life, and from a fearful death? Ah no!”", "당신이 내 목숨을, 그것도 끔찍한 죽음에서 구해주었던 그때의 일에 대해 이제 와서 복수하고 싶어서였겠소? 아, 아니오!\""]], [["“Forgive me,” said I.", "\"용서하시오,\" 하고 내가 말했다."], ["He went on:--", "그는 말을 이었다."]], [["“My friend, it was because I wished to be gentle in the breaking to you, for I know you have loved that so sweet lady.", "\"나의 벗이여, 그것은 내가 당신에게 이 일을 알리는 데 있어 부드럽고자 했기 때문이오. 당신이 그토록 사랑스러운 여인을 사랑했음을 내가 알기에 말이오."], ["But even yet I do not expect you to believe.", "그러나 지금도 나는 당신이 믿어주리라고 기대하지는 않소."], ["It is so hard to accept at once any abstract truth, that we may doubt such to be possible when we have always believed the ‘no’ of it; it is more hard still to accept so sad a concrete truth, and of such a one as Miss Lucy.", "어떤 추상적 진실이든 단번에 받아들이기란 무척 어려운 법이라, 늘 그것이 '아니'라고 믿어왔던 것을 두고 우리는 그런 일이 가능한지조차 의심할 수가 있소. 하물며 이토록 슬픈 구체적 진실, 그것도 루시 양과 같은 분에 관한 진실을 받아들이기란 더더욱 어려운 일이오."], ["To-night I go to prove it.", "오늘 밤 나는 그것을 증명하러 가오."], ["Dare you come with me?”", "감히 나와 함께 가시겠소?\""]], [["This staggered me.", "이 말에 나는 휘청거렸다."], ["A man does not like to prove such a truth; Byron excepted from the category, jealousy.", "사람이란 그런 진실을 증명하고 싶어하지 않는 법이다. 다만 바이런만은 이 부류에서 예외였으니, 질투에 관해서는 그러했다."]], [["“And prove the very truth he most abhorred.”", "\"그리고 그가 가장 혐오하던 바로 그 진실을 증명하더라.\""]], [["He saw my hesitation, and spoke:--", "그는 내가 망설이는 것을 보고 입을 열었다."]], [["“The logic is simple, no madman’s logic this time, jumping from tussock to tussock in a misty bog.", "\"논리는 간단하오. 이번에는 안개 낀 늪에서 풀더미에서 풀더미로 건너뛰는 미치광이의 논리가 아니오."], ["If it be not true, then proof will be relief; at worst it will not harm. If it be true!", "만일 사실이 아니라면 증명은 위안이 될 것이고, 최악의 경우라도 해가 되지는 않을 것이오."], ["Ah, there is the dread; yet very dread should help my cause, for in it is some need of belief.", "그러나 만일 사실이라면! 아, 바로 거기에 두려움이 있소. 그러나 바로 그 두려움이 나의 일을 도와줄 것이오."], ["Come, I tell you what I propose: first, that we go off now and see that child in the hospital. Dr.", "그 안에는 믿음을 향한 어떤 필요가 깃들어 있으니 말이오. 자, 내가 제안하는 바를 일러주겠소."], ["Vincent, of the North Hospital, where the papers say the child is, is friend of mine, and I think of yours since you were in class at Amsterdam.", "첫째, 우리가 지금 가서 병원에 있는 그 아이를 보는 것이오. 신문에서 아이가 있다고 한 노스 병원의 빈센트 박사는 나의 벗이고, 또한 당신이 암스테르담에서 수업을 들었으니 당신의 벗이기도 할 것이오."], ["He will let two scientists see his case, if he will not let two friends.", "그는 두 벗에게는 사례를 보여주지 않더라도, 두 과학자에게는 보여줄 것이오."], ["We shall tell him nothing, but only that we wish to learn.", "우리는 아무 말도 하지 않고, 다만 알고 싶을 뿐이라고만 할 것이오."], ["And then----”", "그러고 나서…….\""]], [["“And then?” He took a key from his pocket and held it up.", "\"그러고 나서요?\" 그는 주머니에서 열쇠 하나를 꺼내 들어 보였다."], ["“And then we spend the night, you and I, in the churchyard where Lucy lies.", "\"그러고 나서 당신과 나, 우리는 루시가 누워 있는 그 묘지에서 밤을 보내는 것이오."], ["This is the key that lock the tomb.", "이것이 그 무덤을 잠그는 열쇠요."], ["I had it from the coffin-man to give to Arthur.”", "아서에게 건네주라고 관 만드는 사람에게서 받아둔 것이오.\""], ["My heart sank within me, for I felt that there was some fearful ordeal before us.", "내 가슴은 철렁 내려앉았으니, 우리 앞에 어떤 무시무시한 시련이 놓여 있음을 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["I could do nothing, however, so I plucked up what heart I could and said that we had better hasten, as the afternoon was passing....", "그러나 나로서는 어쩔 도리가 없었으므로, 나는 낼 수 있는 만큼 용기를 끌어모아, 오후가 지나가고 있으니 서두르는 편이 낫겠다고 말했다……."]], [["We found the child awake. It had had a sleep and taken some food, and altogether was going on well. Dr.", "우리가 가보니 아이는 깨어 있었다. 한숨 자고 음식도 좀 먹어, 전반적으로 잘 회복되고 있었다."], ["Vincent took the bandage from its throat, and showed us the punctures.", "빈센트 박사가 아이의 목에서 붕대를 풀어 우리에게 그 상처를 보여주었다."], ["There was no mistaking the similarity to those which had been on Lucy’s throat.", "루시의 목에 있던 것과의 유사성은 의심할 여지가 없었다."], ["They were smaller, and the edges looked fresher; that was all.", "다만 더 작고 가장자리가 더 새것처럼 보일 뿐, 그것이 전부였다."], ["We asked Vincent to what he attributed them, and he replied that it must have been a bite of some animal, perhaps a rat; but, for his own part, he was inclined to think that it was one of the bats which are so numerous on the northern heights of London.", "우리가 빈센트에게 무엇 때문이라고 보느냐고 묻자, 그는 어떤 짐승의 물린 자국임이 틀림없다고, 어쩌면 쥐였을 것이라고 답했다. 그러나 그 자신으로서는 런던 북부 고지대에 그토록 많은 박쥐 가운데 하나가 아닐까 하는 쪽으로 마음이 기운다고 했다."], ["“Out of so many harmless ones,”", "\"그토록 많은 무해한 박쥐들 가운데,\""], ["he said, “there may be some wild specimen from the South of a more malignant species.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"남방에서 온 더 악성인 종의 야생 표본이 더러 섞여 있을 수도 있지요."], ["Some sailor may have brought one home, and it managed to escape; or even from the Zoölogical Gardens a young one may have got loose, or one be bred there from a vampire.", "어떤 선원이 한 마리를 집에 데려왔다가 그것이 용케 달아났을 수도 있고, 동물원에서 어린 놈이 풀려났을 수도, 혹은 거기서 흡혈박쥐로부터 한 마리가 태어났을 수도 있고요."], ["These things do occur, you know.", "이런 일은 일어나는 법이니까요, 아시다시피."], ["Only ten days ago a wolf got out, and was, I believe, traced up in this direction.", "불과 열흘 전에도 늑대 한 마리가 빠져나가, 제가 알기로는 이 방향으로 추적되었답니다."], ["For a week after, the children were playing nothing but Red Riding Hood on the Heath and in every alley in the place until this ‘bloofer lady’ scare came along, since when it has been quite a gala-time with them.", "그 후로 일주일 동안 아이들은 히스 들판에서나 동네 골목마다 빨간 모자 놀이만 하더니, 이 '아름다운 부인' 소동이 일어난 뒤로는 아주 신이 난 모양이에요."], ["Even this poor little mite, when he woke up to-day, asked the nurse if he might go away.", "이 가엾은 꼬마 녀석조차, 오늘 깨어나서는 간호사에게 나가도 되느냐고 물었답니다."], ["When she asked him why he wanted to go, he said he wanted to play with the ‘bloofer lady.’”", "왜 나가고 싶으냐고 묻자, '아름다운 부인'과 놀고 싶다고 했다는군요.\""]], [["“I hope,”", "\"바라건대,\""], ["said Van Helsing, “that when you are sending the child home you will caution its parents to keep strict watch over it.", "하고 반 헬싱이 말했다. \"이 아이를 집으로 보낼 때 부모에게 아이를 단단히 지켜보라고 주의를 주십시오."], ["These fancies to stray are most dangerous; and if the child were to remain out another night, it would probably be fatal.", "이렇게 떠돌고 싶어하는 충동이 무엇보다 위험하니 말이오. 아이가 또 하룻밤이라도 밖에 머물게 된다면 아마 치명적일 것이오."], ["But in any case I suppose you will not let it away for some days?”", "그러나 어떻든 며칠은 내보내지 않으시겠지요?\""]], [["“Certainly not, not for a week at least; longer if the wound is not healed.”", "\"물론입니다, 적어도 일주일은요. 상처가 낫지 않으면 더 오래 두지요.\""]], [["Our visit to the hospital took more time than we had reckoned on, and the sun had dipped before we came out.", "병원 방문이 우리가 예상했던 것보다 더 오래 걸려, 우리가 나왔을 때에는 해가 이미 기울어 있었다."], ["When Van Helsing saw how dark it was, he said:--", "반 헬싱은 사위가 얼마나 어두운지를 보고 말했다."]], [["“There is no hurry.", "\"서두를 것 없소."], ["It is more late than I thought.", "생각보다 시간이 늦었구려."], ["Come, let us seek somewhere that we may eat, and then we shall go on our way.”", "자, 어디 식사할 곳을 찾읍시다. 그러고 나서 우리 갈 길을 가도록 합시다.\""]], [["We dined at “Jack Straw’s Castle” along with a little crowd of bicyclists and others who were genially noisy.", "우리는 \"잭 스트로스 캐슬\"에서 자전거를 탄 무리와 그 밖의 사람들 틈에 끼여 식사를 했는데, 그들은 유쾌하게 떠들썩했다."], ["About ten o’clock we started from the inn.", "열 시쯤 우리는 그 여관에서 출발했다."], ["It was then very dark, and the scattered lamps made the darkness greater when we were once outside their individual radius.", "그때는 무척 어두웠고, 드문드문 놓인 가로등은 일단 그 불빛이 미치는 범위를 벗어나자 오히려 어둠을 더 깊게 만들었다."], ["The Professor had evidently noted the road we were to go, for he went on unhesitatingly; but, as for me, I was in quite a mixup as to locality.", "교수는 우리가 가야 할 길을 분명히 익혀두었던 모양으로 망설임 없이 나아갔다. 그러나 나로서는 어디가 어딘지 도무지 종잡을 수 없었다."], ["As we went further, we met fewer and fewer people, till at last we were somewhat surprised when we met even the patrol of horse police going their usual suburban round.", "더 멀리 갈수록 마주치는 사람은 점점 줄어, 마침내 평소의 순찰을 도는 기마 경찰대와 마주쳤을 때에는 다소 놀랄 정도였다."], ["At last we reached the wall of the churchyard, which we climbed over.", "마침내 우리는 묘지의 담에 이르러 그것을 타고 넘었다."], ["With some little difficulty--for it was very dark, and the whole place seemed so strange to us--we found the Westenra tomb.", "약간의 어려움 끝에 — 무척 어두운 데다 온통 낯설게 보였으므로 — 우리는 웨스텐라가의 무덤을 찾아냈다."], ["The Professor took the key, opened the creaky door, and standing back, politely, but quite unconsciously, motioned me to precede him.", "교수는 열쇠를 꺼내 삐걱거리는 문을 열고는, 뒤로 물러서서 공손하게, 그러나 무의식적으로 나더러 먼저 들어가라고 손짓했다."], ["There was a delicious irony in the offer, in the courtliness of giving preference on such a ghastly occasion.", "이토록 섬뜩한 자리에서 우선권을 양보하는 그 정중함에는 절묘한 아이러니가 깃들어 있었다."], ["My companion followed me quickly, and cautiously drew the door to, after carefully ascertaining that the lock was a falling, and not a spring, one.", "내 동행은 재빨리 나를 따라 들어와, 그 자물쇠가 용수철식이 아니라 떨어지는 식임을 조심스레 확인한 뒤 문을 신중히 닫았다."], ["In the latter case we should have been in a bad plight.", "용수철식이었다면 우리는 곤경에 빠졌을 터였다."], ["Then he fumbled in his bag, and taking out a matchbox and a piece of candle, proceeded to make a light.", "그러고 나서 그는 가방을 더듬어 성냥갑과 양초 토막을 꺼내 불을 밝히기 시작했다."], ["The tomb in the day-time, and when wreathed with fresh flowers, had looked grim and gruesome enough; but now, some days afterwards, when the flowers hung lank and dead, their whites turning to rust and their greens to browns; when the spider and the beetle had resumed their accustomed dominance; when time-discoloured stone, and dust-encrusted mortar, and rusty, dank iron, and tarnished brass, and clouded silver-plating gave back the feeble glimmer of a candle, the effect was more miserable and sordid than could have been imagined.", "그 무덤은 낮에, 그리고 싱싱한 꽃으로 둘러싸여 있을 때에도 음산하고 으스스해 보였다. 그러나 이제, 며칠이 지나 꽃들이 시들어 축 늘어지고, 흰빛은 녹슨 빛으로, 푸른빛은 갈색으로 변하고, 거미와 딱정벌레가 다시 그 익숙한 지배권을 되찾았으며, 세월에 변색된 돌과, 먼지가 엉겨붙은 회반죽, 녹슬고 눅눅한 쇠붙이, 빛바랜 놋쇠, 흐려진 은도금이 촛불의 희미한 가물거림을 되비추고 있으니, 그 효과는 상상조차 할 수 없을 만큼 더 처참하고 누추했다."], ["It conveyed irresistibly the idea that life--animal life--was not the only thing which could pass away.", "그것은 생명이 — 동물의 생명이 — 사라질 수 있는 유일한 것은 아니라는 생각을 거역할 수 없이 전해주었다."]], [["Van Helsing went about his work systematically.", "반 헬싱은 자기 일을 체계적으로 해나갔다."], ["Holding his candle so that he could read the coffin plates, and so holding it that the sperm dropped in white patches which congealed as they touched the metal, he made assurance of Lucy’s coffin.", "관의 명판을 읽을 수 있도록 촛불을 들었는데, 떨어지는 촛농이 금속에 닿자 곧 굳어 흰 얼룩을 이루도록 든 채로, 그는 루시의 관임을 확인했다."], ["Another search in his bag, and he took out a turnscrew.", "가방을 다시 한 번 뒤져 그는 나사돌리개를 꺼냈다."]], [["“What are you going to do?” I asked.", "\"무얼 하려는 거요?\" 하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“To open the coffin.", "\"관을 열려는 것이오."], ["You shall yet be convinced.”", "당신은 끝내 확신하게 될 것이오.\""], ["Straightway he began taking out the screws, and finally lifted off the lid, showing the casing of lead beneath.", "곧바로 그는 나사를 빼내기 시작하더니, 마침내 뚜껑을 들어올려, 그 아래 납으로 된 안틀을 드러냈다."], ["The sight was almost too much for me.", "그 광경은 나로서는 거의 견디기 어려운 것이었다."], ["It seemed to be as much an affront to the dead as it would have been to have stripped off her clothing in her sleep whilst living; I actually took hold of his hand to stop him.", "그것은 살아 있을 적 잠든 그녀의 옷을 벗기는 일이 그러했을 만큼이나 죽은 이에 대한 모욕처럼 여겨졌다. 나는 정말로 그의 손을 붙잡아 막으려 했다."], ["He only said: “You shall see,”", "그는 다만 \"보게 될 것이오,\""], ["and again fumbling in his bag, took out a tiny fret-saw.", "하고 말하고는, 다시 가방을 더듬어 자그마한 실톱을 꺼냈다."], ["Striking the turnscrew through the lead with a swift downward stab, which made me wince, he made a small hole, which was, however, big enough to admit the point of the saw.", "나사돌리개를 납에 대고 재빠르게 아래로 찔러넣자 — 그 바람에 나는 움찔했다 — 작은 구멍이 났는데, 그래도 톱끝을 넣기에는 충분히 큰 것이었다."], ["I had expected a rush of gas from the week-old corpse.", "나는 일주일 묵은 시신에서 가스가 확 뿜어져 나오리라 예상했다."], ["We doctors, who have had to study our dangers, have to become accustomed to such things, and I drew back towards the door.", "우리 의사들은 위험을 연구해야 하는 처지라 그런 일에는 익숙해질 수밖에 없는데, 그래도 나는 문 쪽으로 물러섰다."], ["But the Professor never stopped for a moment; he sawed down a couple of feet along one side of the lead coffin, and then across, and down the other side.", "그러나 교수는 한순간도 멈추지 않았다. 그는 납관의 한쪽을 따라 두어 자쯤 톱질해 내려간 다음, 가로질러, 다시 다른 쪽을 따라 내려갔다."], ["Taking the edge of the loose flange, he bent it back towards the foot of the coffin, and holding up the candle into the aperture, motioned to me to look.", "그렇게 떨어져나온 가장자리의 테두리를 잡아 관의 발치 쪽으로 젖히고는, 촛불을 그 틈으로 들어올려, 나더러 들여다보라고 손짓했다."]], [["I drew near and looked. The coffin was empty.", "나는 가까이 다가가 들여다보았다. 관은 비어 있었다."]], [["It was certainly a surprise to me, and gave me a considerable shock, but Van Helsing was unmoved.", "분명 그것은 나에게 놀라운 일이었고, 적잖은 충격을 안겼다. 그러나 반 헬싱은 흔들림이 없었다."], ["He was now more sure than ever of his ground, and so emboldened to proceed in his task.", "그는 이제 그 어느 때보다도 자기 입지에 대한 확신을 굳혔고, 그래서 더욱 대담하게 자기 일을 밀고 나갈 수 있었다."], ["“Are you satisfied now, friend John?” he asked.", "\"이제 만족하오, 존?\" 하고 그가 물었다."]], [["I felt all the dogged argumentativeness of my nature awake within me as I answered him:--", "나는 그에게 대답하면서, 내 천성 속의 그 완강한 따지기 기질이 온통 깨어나는 것을 느꼈다."]], [["“I am satisfied that Lucy’s body is not in that coffin; but that only proves one thing.”", "\"루시의 몸이 저 관 속에 없다는 것은 만족스럽게 알았소. 그러나 그것이 증명하는 것은 단 한 가지요.\""]], [["“And what is that, friend John?”", "\"그게 무엇이오, 존?\""]], [["“That it is not there.”", "\"여기에 그 몸이 없다는 것이오.\""]], [["“That is good logic,”", "\"거기까지는 옳은 논리요,\""], ["he said, “so far as it goes. But how do you--how can you--account for it not being there?”", "하고 그가 말했다. \"그러나 당신은 그것이 여기 없는 것을 어떻게 설명하려오 — 아니, 어떻게 설명할 수 있겠소?\""]], [["“Perhaps a body-snatcher,”", "\"어쩌면 시신 도굴꾼일 수도 있지요,\""], ["I suggested. “Some of the undertaker’s people may have stolen it.”", "하고 내가 넌지시 말했다. \"장의사의 일꾼 가운데 누군가가 훔쳤을 수도 있고요.\""], ["I felt that I was speaking folly, and yet it was the only real cause which I could suggest.", "나는 내가 어리석은 소리를 하고 있음을 느꼈으나, 그래도 그것이 내가 댈 수 있는 유일하게 현실적인 이유였다."], ["The Professor sighed.", "교수는 한숨을 내쉬었다. \"허허!\""], ["“Ah well!” he said, “we must have more proof.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"우리에게는 더 많은 증거가 있어야겠소."], ["Come with me.”", "나와 함께 갑시다.\""]], [["He put on the coffin-lid again, gathered up all his things and placed them in the bag, blew out the light, and placed the candle also in the bag.", "그는 관 뚜껑을 다시 덮고, 자기 물건을 모두 챙겨 가방에 넣고는, 불을 끄고 양초도 가방에 넣었다. 우리는 문을 열고 나왔다."], ["We opened the door, and went out.", "우리 뒤에서 그는 문을 닫아 잠갔다."], ["Behind us he closed the door and locked it.", "그는 나에게 열쇠를 건네며 말했다."], ["He handed me the key, saying: “Will you keep it?", "\"당신이 보관하겠소? 확실히 해두는 편이 좋겠소.\""], ["You had better be assured.” I laughed--it was not a very cheerful laugh, I am bound to say--as I motioned him to keep it.", "나는 웃었다 — 그리 유쾌한 웃음은 아니었음을 말해두어야겠다 — 그러면서 그더러 가지고 있으라고 손짓했다. \"열쇠는 아무것도 아니오,\""], ["“A key is nothing,”", "하고 내가 말했다."], ["I said; “there may be duplicates; and anyhow it is not difficult to pick a lock of that kind.”", "\"복제본이 있을 수도 있고, 어쨌든 그런 종류의 자물쇠를 따는 것쯤은 어렵지 않으니까요.\""], ["He said nothing, but put the key in his pocket.", "그는 아무 말 없이 열쇠를 자기 주머니에 넣었다."], ["Then he told me to watch at one side of the churchyard whilst he would watch at the other.", "그러고는 자기는 묘지의 한쪽을 지킬 테니 나더러 다른 쪽을 지키라고 일렀다."], ["I took up my place behind a yew-tree, and I saw his dark figure move until the intervening headstones and trees hid it from my sight.", "나는 주목나무 한 그루 뒤에 자리를 잡았고, 그의 검은 형체가 움직이다가 사이에 놓인 비석들과 나무들에 가려 시야에서 사라지는 것을 지켜보았다."]], [["It was a lonely vigil.", "그것은 외로운 불침번이었다."], ["Just after I had taken my place I heard a distant clock strike twelve, and in time came one and two.", "자리를 잡은 직후 멀리서 시계가 열두 시를 치는 소리를 들었고, 이윽고 한 시, 두 시가 울렸다."], ["I was chilled and unnerved, and angry with the Professor for taking me on such an errand and with myself for coming.", "나는 몸이 시리고 신경이 곤두선 데다, 나를 이런 일에 끌어들인 교수에게도, 따라온 나 자신에게도 화가 났다."], ["I was too cold and too sleepy to be keenly observant, and not sleepy enough to betray my trust so altogether I had a dreary, miserable time.", "너무 추웠고 너무 졸렸으니 예리하게 관찰할 수도 없었고, 그렇다고 맡은 소임을 저버릴 만큼 졸린 것도 아니어서, 한마디로 나는 음울하고 비참한 시간을 보냈다."]], [["Suddenly, as I turned round, I thought I saw something like a white streak, moving between two dark yew-trees at the side of the churchyard farthest from the tomb; at the same time a dark mass moved from the Professor’s side of the ground, and hurriedly went towards it.", "문득 돌아서다가, 나는 무덤에서 가장 먼 쪽 묘지 가장자리의 어두운 주목나무 두 그루 사이로 흰 줄기 같은 무언가가 움직이는 것을 본 듯했다. 동시에 교수가 있는 쪽 땅에서 검은 덩어리가 움직여 서둘러 그쪽으로 향했다. 그래서 나도 움직였다."], ["Then I too moved; but I had to go round headstones and railed-off tombs, and I stumbled over graves.", "그러나 나는 비석들과 난간으로 둘러쳐진 무덤들을 돌아가야 했고, 무덤들에 걸려 비틀거렸다."], ["The sky was overcast, and somewhere far off an early cock crew.", "하늘은 잔뜩 흐렸고, 어딘가 멀리서 이른 수탉이 울었다."], ["A little way off, beyond a line of scattered juniper-trees, which marked the pathway to the church, a white, dim figure flitted in the direction of the tomb.", "조금 떨어진 곳, 교회로 이어지는 길을 표시하는 드문드문한 노간주나무 줄 너머로, 희고 어렴풋한 형체가 무덤 쪽으로 휙 스쳐갔다."], ["The tomb itself was hidden by trees, and I could not see where the figure disappeared.", "무덤 자체는 나무에 가려져 있어, 그 형체가 어디로 사라졌는지는 볼 수 없었다."], ["I heard the rustle of actual movement where I had first seen the white figure, and coming over, found the Professor holding in his arms a tiny child.", "내가 처음 흰 형체를 보았던 곳에서 실제로 무언가 움직이는 바스락 소리가 들려, 그쪽으로 가보니 교수가 자그마한 아이 하나를 품에 안고 있었다."], ["When he saw me he held it out to me, and said:--", "그는 나를 보자 아이를 내밀며 말했다."]], [["“Are you satisfied now?”", "\"이제 만족하오?\""]], [["“No,” I said, in a way that I felt was aggressive.", "\"아니오,\" 하고 나는 스스로도 공격적이라고 느낀 투로 말했다."]], [["“Do you not see the child?”", "\"이 아이가 보이지 않소?\""]], [["“Yes, it is a child, but who brought it here?", "\"보입니다, 아이로군요. 그러나 누가 이 아이를 여기 데려왔단 말이오?"], ["And is it wounded?” I asked.", "그리고 다치기는 했소?\" 하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“We shall see,”", "\"보면 알겠지요,\""], ["said the Professor, and with one impulse we took our way out of the churchyard, he carrying the sleeping child.", "하고 교수가 말했다. 그리고 우리는 한마음으로 묘지에서 빠져나왔는데, 그가 잠든 아이를 안고 있었다."]], [["When we had got some little distance away, we went into a clump of trees, and struck a match, and looked at the child’s throat.", "조금 떨어진 곳까지 가서, 우리는 나무가 우거진 곳으로 들어가 성냥을 켜고 아이의 목을 살펴보았다."], ["It was without a scratch or scar of any kind.", "긁힌 자국도 흉터도 하나 없었다."]], [["“Was I right?”", "\"내 말이 옳았지요?\""], ["I asked triumphantly.", "하고 나는 의기양양하게 물었다."]], [["“We were just in time,”", "\"우리가 때마침 닿은 것이오,\""], ["said the Professor thankfully.", "하고 교수가 고마워하며 말했다."]], [["We had now to decide what we were to do with the child, and so consulted about it.", "이제 우리는 이 아이를 어떻게 할지 정해야 했으므로, 그것을 의논했다."], ["If we were to take it to a police-station we should have to give some account of our movements during the night; at least, we should have had to make some statement as to how we had come to find the child.", "만일 아이를 경찰서로 데려간다면 우리는 밤사이의 행적을 어느 정도 설명해야 할 터였다. 적어도 우리가 어떻게 이 아이를 찾게 되었는지 무언가 진술을 해야 했을 것이다."], ["So finally we decided that we would take it to the Heath, and when we heard a policeman coming, would leave it where he could not fail to find it; we would then seek our way home as quickly as we could.", "그래서 마침내 우리는 아이를 히스 들판으로 데려가, 순경이 오는 소리가 들리면 그가 틀림없이 찾을 만한 곳에 놓아두기로 했다. 그러고 나서 가능한 한 빨리 집으로 가는 길을 찾기로 했다."], ["All fell out well.", "모든 것이 잘 풀렸다."], ["At the edge of Hampstead Heath we heard a policeman’s heavy tramp, and laying the child on the pathway, we waited and watched until he saw it as he flashed his lantern to and fro.", "햄프스테드 히스의 가장자리에서 우리는 순경의 묵직한 발소리를 들었고, 아이를 길에 눕히고는, 그가 등불을 이리저리 비추다가 아이를 발견할 때까지 기다리며 지켜보았다."], ["We heard his exclamation of astonishment, and then we went away silently.", "그의 놀란 외침이 들렸고, 그러자 우리는 조용히 자리를 떴다."], ["By good chance we got a cab near the “Spaniards,” and drove to town.", "다행히도 우리는 \"스패니어즈\" 근처에서 마차 한 대를 잡아 시내로 향했다."]], [["I cannot sleep, so I make this entry.", "잠이 오지 않아 이 기록을 적는다."], ["But I must try to get a few hours’ sleep, as Van Helsing is to call for me at noon.", "그러나 반 헬싱이 정오에 나를 부르러 오기로 했으니, 몇 시간이라도 자보려 애써야겠다."], ["He insists that I shall go with him on another expedition.", "그는 또 다른 원정에 자기와 함께 가야 한다고 우긴다."]], [["27 September.--It was two o’clock before we found a suitable opportunity for our attempt.", "9월 27일. — 우리가 시도하기에 적당한 기회를 찾은 것은 두 시가 되어서였다."], ["The funeral held at noon was all completed, and the last stragglers of the mourners had taken themselves lazily away, when, looking carefully from behind a clump of alder-trees, we saw the sexton lock the gate after him.", "정오에 거행된 장례가 모두 끝나고, 조문객 가운데 마지막으로 처진 이들마저 느릿느릿 자리를 뜬 뒤, 오리나무 덤불 뒤에서 조심스레 살펴보니 묘지기가 자기 뒤로 문을 잠그는 것이 보였다."], ["We knew then that we were safe till morning did we desire it; but the Professor told me that we should not want more than an hour at most.", "그제야 우리는 원한다면 아침까지는 안전하다는 것을 알았다. 그러나 교수는 우리에게 길어야 한 시간 이상은 필요치 않을 것이라고 말했다."], ["Again I felt that horrid sense of the reality of things, in which any effort of imagination seemed out of place; and I realised distinctly the perils of the law which we were incurring in our unhallowed work.", "다시금 나는 그 끔찍한 현실감을, 그 어떤 상상의 노력도 어울리지 않게 여겨지는 그 감각을 느꼈다. 그리고 우리가 이 불경한 일로 자초하고 있는 법적 위험을 또렷이 깨달았다."], ["Besides, I felt it was all so useless. Outrageous as it was to open a leaden coffin, to see if a woman dead nearly a week were really dead, it now seemed the height of folly to open the tomb again, when we knew, from the evidence of our own eyesight, that the coffin was empty.", "게다가 나는 이 모든 일이 너무도 부질없게 느껴졌다. 거의 일주일이나 죽어 있던 여인이 정말로 죽었는지 보겠다고 납관을 여는 일도 터무니없었거니와, 우리 자신의 두 눈으로 본 증거로 관이 비어 있음을 알면서 다시 무덤을 여는 일은 이제 어리석음의 극치로만 여겨졌다."], ["I shrugged my shoulders, however, and rested silent, for Van Helsing had a way of going on his own road, no matter who remonstrated.", "그래도 나는 어깨를 으쓱하고는 잠자코 있었으니, 반 헬싱에게는 누가 항의하든 제 길을 가는 버릇이 있었기 때문이다."], ["He took the key, opened the vault, and again courteously motioned me to precede.", "그는 열쇠를 꺼내 묘실을 열고, 또다시 정중하게 나더러 먼저 들어가라고 손짓했다."], ["The place was not so gruesome as last night, but oh, how unutterably mean-looking when the sunshine streamed in.", "그곳은 지난밤만큼 으스스하지는 않았으나, 아, 햇살이 쏟아져 들어오니 말할 수 없이 초라해 보였다."], ["Van Helsing walked over to Lucy’s coffin, and I followed.", "반 헬싱이 루시의 관 쪽으로 걸어갔고, 나는 그 뒤를 따랐다."], ["He bent over and again forced back the leaden flange; and then a shock of surprise and dismay shot through me.", "그는 몸을 숙여 다시 납 테두리를 젖혔다. 그러자 놀라움과 경악의 충격이 나를 꿰뚫었다."]], [["There lay Lucy, seemingly just as we had seen her the night before her funeral.", "거기 루시가 누워 있었다, 마치 그녀의 장례 전날 밤에 우리가 보았던 그 모습 그대로."], ["She was, if possible, more radiantly beautiful than ever; and I could not believe that she was dead.", "그녀는 가능하다면 그 어느 때보다도 더 환하게 아름다웠다. 나는 그녀가 죽었다는 것을 믿을 수가 없었다."], ["The lips were red, nay redder than before; and on the cheeks was a delicate bloom.", "입술은 붉었으니, 아니, 전보다 더 붉었고, 두 뺨에는 고운 혈색이 어려 있었다."]], [["“Is this a juggle?”", "\"이건 무슨 속임수요?\""], ["I said to him.", "하고 내가 그에게 말했다."]], [["“Are you convinced now?”", "\"이제 확신하겠소?\""], ["said the Professor in response, and as he spoke he put over his hand, and in a way that made me shudder, pulled back the dead lips and showed the white teeth.", "교수가 대답으로 말했다. 그리고 그렇게 말하면서 그는 손을 뻗어, 나를 몸서리치게 하는 방식으로, 죽은 입술을 젖혀 흰 이를 드러내 보였다."]], [["“See,”", "\"보시오,\""], ["he went on, “see, they are even sharper than before.", "하고 그가 말을 이었다. \"보시오, 전보다 더 날카롭기까지 하오."], ["With this and this”--and he touched one of the canine teeth and that below it--“the little children can be bitten.", "이것과 이것으로\" — 그러면서 그는 송곳니 하나와 그 아래의 이를 만졌다 — \"어린아이들이 물릴 수 있는 것이오."], ["Are you of belief now, friend John?” Once more, argumentative hostility woke within me.", "이제 믿겠소, 존?\" 다시 한 번 따지려 드는 적개심이 내 안에서 깨어났다."], ["I could not accept such an overwhelming idea as he suggested; so, with an attempt to argue of which I was even at the moment ashamed, I said:--", "나는 그가 시사하는 그 압도적인 생각을 받아들일 수가 없었다. 그래서, 그 순간조차 스스로 부끄럽게 여긴 반박을 시도하며 나는 말했다."]], [["“She may have been placed here since last night.”", "\"지난밤 이후에 누군가 그녀를 여기 갖다놓았을 수도 있지요.\""]], [["“Indeed?", "\"그렇소?"], ["That is so, and by whom?”", "그럴 수도 있겠지, 한데 누가 말이오?\""]], [["“I do not know. Some one has done it.”", "\"모르겠소. 누군가 그리한 것이지요.\""]], [["“And yet she has been dead one week.", "\"그런데도 그녀는 일주일이나 죽어 있었소."], ["Most peoples in that time would not look so.”", "대개의 사람들은 그 시간이면 이렇게 보이지 않을 것이오.\""], ["I had no answer for this, so was silent.", "나는 이에 답할 말이 없어 잠자코 있었다."], ["Van Helsing did not seem to notice my silence; at any rate, he showed neither chagrin nor triumph.", "반 헬싱은 내 침묵을 알아차린 것 같지 않았으니, 어쨌든 그는 분함도 의기양양함도 내보이지 않았다."], ["He was looking intently at the face of the dead woman, raising the eyelids and looking at the eyes, and once more opening the lips and examining the teeth.", "그는 죽은 여인의 얼굴을 유심히 들여다보며 눈꺼풀을 들어 눈을 살피고, 다시 한 번 입술을 벌려 이를 살폈다."], ["Then he turned to me and said:--", "그러고는 나를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“Here, there is one thing which is different from all recorded; here is some dual life that is not as the common.", "\"여기, 기록된 모든 것과는 다른 한 가지가 있소. 여기에는 흔한 경우와는 다른 어떤 이중의 삶이 있소."], ["She was bitten by the vampire when she was in a trance, sleep-walking--oh, you start; you do not know that, friend John, but you shall know it all later--and in trance could he best come to take more blood.", "그녀는 몽유 상태로, 잠결에 거닐던 중에 흡혈귀에게 물렸소 — 아, 당신이 놀라는구려. 그것을 모르고 있었군, 존. 그러나 나중에 모든 것을 알게 될 것이오 — 몽유 상태에서야말로 그가 더 많은 피를 가져가기에 가장 좋았던 것이오."], ["In trance she died, and in trance she is Un-Dead, too.", "몽유 상태에서 그녀는 죽었고, 또한 몽유 상태로 그녀는 불사자(不死者)가 되었소."], ["So it is that she differ from all other.", "그래서 그녀가 다른 모든 이와 다른 것이오."], ["Usually when the Un-Dead sleep at home”--as he spoke he made a comprehensive sweep of his arm to designate what to a vampire was “home”--“their face show what they are, but this so sweet that was when she not Un-Dead she go back to the nothings of the common dead.", "보통 불사자가 제 집에서 잠들 때면\" — 그렇게 말하며 그는 흡혈귀에게 '집'이 무엇인지를 가리키려는 듯 팔을 크게 휘둘렀다 — \"그 얼굴이 정체를 드러내는 법이지만, 이토록 사랑스러웠던 그녀는, 불사자가 아니었을 적에는 흔한 죽은 자의 무로 돌아가는 것이오. 보시오, 거기에는 아무런 악의가 없소."], ["There is no malign there, see, and so it make hard that I must kill her in her sleep.”", "그러니 내가 그녀를 잠든 채로 죽여야 한다는 것이 더더욱 어려운 일이오.\""], ["This turned my blood cold, and it began to dawn upon me that I was accepting Van Helsing’s theories; but if she were really dead, what was there of terror in the idea of killing her?", "이 말에 내 피가 싸늘하게 식었고, 내가 반 헬싱의 이론을 받아들이고 있다는 사실이 차츰 머리에 떠오르기 시작했다. 그러나 만일 그녀가 정말로 죽었다면, 그녀를 죽인다는 생각에 무슨 공포가 있겠는가?"], ["He looked up at me, and evidently saw the change in my face, for he said almost joyously:--", "그가 나를 올려다보았는데, 분명 내 얼굴에 어린 변화를 본 모양이었다. 그는 거의 기쁜 듯이 말했다."]], [["“Ah, you believe now?”", "\"아, 이제 믿는구려?\""]], [["I answered: “Do not press me too hard all at once.", "나는 대답했다. \"한꺼번에 너무 몰아붙이지는 마시오."], ["I am willing to accept.", "받아들일 용의는 있소."], ["How will you do this bloody work?”", "이 피비린내 나는 일을 어떻게 할 작정이오?\""]], [["“I shall cut off her head and fill her mouth with garlic, and I shall drive a stake through her body.”", "\"나는 그녀의 머리를 잘라내고 입에 마늘을 채운 다음, 그녀의 몸에 말뚝을 박을 것이오.\""], ["It made me shudder to think of so mutilating the body of the woman whom I had loved.", "내가 사랑했던 여인의 몸을 그토록 훼손한다고 생각하니 몸서리가 쳐졌다."], ["And yet the feeling was not so strong as I had expected.", "그래도 그 느낌은 내가 예상했던 만큼 강하지는 않았다."], ["I was, in fact, beginning to shudder at the presence of this being, this Un-Dead, as Van Helsing called it, and to loathe it.", "사실 나는 이 존재, 반 헬싱이 불사자라 부르는 이것의 존재 앞에서 몸서리치고, 이것을 혐오하기 시작하고 있었다."], ["Is it possible that love is all subjective, or all objective?", "사랑이란 온통 주관적인 것일까, 아니면 온통 객관적인 것일까?"]], [["I waited a considerable time for Van Helsing to begin, but he stood as if wrapped in thought.", "나는 반 헬싱이 시작하기를 꽤 오래 기다렸으나, 그는 마치 생각에 잠긴 듯 서 있었다."], ["Presently he closed the catch of his bag with a snap, and said:--", "이윽고 그는 가방의 잠금쇠를 딸깍 닫으며 말했다."]], [["“I have been thinking, and have made up my mind as to what is best.", "\"내가 줄곧 생각해서, 무엇이 최선인지 마음을 정했소."], ["If I did simply follow my inclining I would do now, at this moment, what is to be done; but there are other things to follow, and things that are thousand times more difficult in that them we do not know.", "단지 내 마음 내키는 대로 한다면 지금, 이 순간에 해야 할 일을 하겠지만, 따라야 할 다른 일들이, 그것도 우리가 알지 못하기에 천 배는 더 어려운 일들이 있소."], ["This is simple.", "이것은 간단하오."], ["She have yet no life taken, though that is of time; and to act now would be to take danger from her for ever.", "그녀는 아직 누구의 목숨도 앗지 않았소, 비록 그것은 시간문제이지만 말이오."], ["But then we may have to want Arthur, and how shall we tell him of this?", "지금 손을 쓴다면 그녀에게서 위험을 영영 거두어버릴 수 있을 것이오."], ["If you, who saw the wounds on Lucy’s throat, and saw the wounds so similar on the child’s at the hospital; if you, who saw the coffin empty last night and full to-day with a woman who have not change only to be more rose and more beautiful in a whole week, after she die--if you know of this and know of the white figure last night that brought the child to the churchyard, and yet of your own senses you did not believe, how, then, can I expect Arthur, who know none of those things, to believe?", "그러나 그렇게 되면 우리는 아서가 필요해질지도 모르는데, 그에게 이 일을 어떻게 말한단 말이오? 루시의 목에 난 상처를 보고, 병원에서 그 아이의 목에 난 그토록 비슷한 상처를 본 당신, 지난밤 빈 관을 보고 오늘은 죽은 지 일주일이 다 되도록 더 발그레하고 더 아름다워지기만 했을 뿐 변하지 않은 여인으로 가득 찬 관을 본 당신 — 이것을 알고, 또 지난밤 아이를 묘지로 데려온 흰 형체를 알면서도, 당신 자신의 감각으로조차 믿지 못했거늘, 그렇다면 그 어느 것도 알지 못하는 아서가 믿어주기를 내 어찌 기대할 수 있겠소?"], ["He doubted me when I took him from her kiss when she was dying.", "그녀가 죽어가며 입맞춤하려 할 때 내가 그를 떼어놓았을 적에도 그는 나를 의심했소."], ["I know he has forgiven me because in some mistaken idea I have done things that prevent him say good-bye as he ought; and he may think that in some more mistaken idea this woman was buried alive; and that in most mistake of all we have killed her.", "나는 그가 나를 용서한 줄 아오. 어떤 잘못된 생각에서 내가 그로 하여금 마땅히 해야 할 작별 인사를 못 하게 만든 일을 두고 말이오. 그런데 그는 또 어떤 더 잘못된 생각에서 이 여인이 산 채로 매장되었다고 여길지도 모르고, 무엇보다도 가장 잘못된 생각에서 우리가 그녀를 죽였다고 여길지도 모르오."], ["He will then argue back that it is we, mistaken ones, that have killed her by our ideas; and so he will be much unhappy always.", "그러면 그는 그녀를 죽인 것이 바로 우리, 그릇된 생각에 빠진 우리이며, 우리의 그 생각 탓이라고 되받아 따질 것이오. 그리하여 그는 늘 무척 불행할 것이오."], ["Yet he never can be sure; and that is the worst of all.", "게다가 그는 결코 확신할 수 없을 텐데, 그것이 무엇보다 가장 나쁜 일이오."], ["And he will sometimes think that she he loved was buried alive, and that will paint his dreams with horrors of what she must have suffered; and again, he will think that we may be right, and that his so beloved was, after all, an Un-Dead. No!", "그는 때로 자기가 사랑한 그녀가 산 채로 묻혔다고 생각할 것이고, 그러면 그녀가 겪었을 고통의 공포가 그의 꿈을 물들일 것이오. 또 어떤 때는 우리가 옳았을지도 모른다고, 그토록 사랑하던 이가 결국 불사자였다고 생각할 것이오. 안 되오!"], ["I told him once, and since then I learn much. Now, since I know it is all true, a hundred thousand times more do I know that he must pass through the bitter waters to reach the sweet.", "나는 한 번 그에게 말해보았고, 그 후로 많은 것을 알게 되었소. 이제 그 모든 것이 진실임을 아니, 그가 단것에 닿기 위해서는 쓰디쓴 물을 헤치고 가야 한다는 것을 십만 배는 더 확실히 아오."], ["He, poor fellow, must have one hour that will make the very face of heaven grow black to him; then we can act for good all round and send him peace.", "가엾은 친구, 그는 천국의 얼굴마저 그에게 캄캄하게 보이게 만들 한 시간을 겪어야 하오. 그러고 나서야 우리는 모두를 위해 옳게 행하고 그에게 평안을 줄 수 있소."], ["My mind is made up.", "내 마음은 정해졌소."], ["Let us go.", "자, 갑시다."], ["You return home for to-night to your asylum, and see that all be well.", "당신은 오늘 밤 당신의 정신병원 집으로 돌아가, 만사가 괜찮은지 살피시오."], ["As for me, I shall spend the night here in this churchyard in my own way.", "나로 말하면, 나는 이 밤을 나 나름의 방식으로 이 묘지에서 보내겠소."], ["To-morrow night you will come to me to the Berkeley Hotel at ten of the clock.", "내일 밤 열 시에 버클리 호텔로 나를 찾아오시오."], ["I shall send for Arthur to come too, and also that so fine young man of America that gave his blood.", "나는 아서도 오라고 사람을 보내겠고, 또 피를 준 그 훌륭한 미국 청년에게도 그리하겠소."], ["Later we shall all have work to do.", "나중에 우리 모두에게 할 일이 있을 것이오."], ["I come with you so far as Piccadilly and there dine, for I must be back here before the sun set.”", "나는 피커딜리까지 당신과 함께 가서 거기서 식사를 하겠소. 해가 지기 전에 이곳으로 돌아와야 하니 말이오.\""]], [["So we locked the tomb and came away, and got over the wall of the churchyard, which was not much of a task, and drove back to Piccadilly.", "그리하여 우리는 무덤을 잠그고 그곳을 떠나, 그리 어렵지 않게 묘지 담을 넘어, 마차를 타고 피커딜리로 돌아왔다."]], [["Note left by Van Helsing in his portmanteau, Berkeley Hotel directed to John Seward, M. D.", "「반 헬싱이 버클리 호텔에서 자신의 여행 가방에 남겨둔, 의학박사 존 수어드 앞으로 된 쪽지.」"]], [["(Not delivered.)", "(전달되지 않음.)"]], [["“27 September.", "\"9월 27일."]], [["“Friend John,--", "벗 존에게,"]], [["“I write this in case anything should happen.", "혹 무슨 일이 생길 경우에 대비하여 이것을 쓰오."], ["I go alone to watch in that churchyard.", "나는 홀로 저 묘지에서 지키러 가오."], ["It pleases me that the Un-Dead, Miss Lucy, shall not leave to-night, that so on the morrow night she may be more eager.", "불사자인 루시 양이 오늘 밤에는 나가지 않아, 내일 밤에는 그만큼 더 갈망에 차게 될 것이니 나는 그것이 흡족하오."], ["Therefore I shall fix some things she like not--garlic and a crucifix--and so seal up the door of the tomb.", "그러므로 나는 그녀가 싫어하는 몇 가지 — 마늘과 십자고상 — 을 놓아두어 무덤의 문을 봉할 것이오."], ["She is young as Un-Dead, and will heed.", "그녀는 불사자로서 아직 어리니, 그것에 따를 것이오."], ["Moreover, these are only to prevent her coming out; they may not prevail on her wanting to get in; for then the Un-Dead is desperate, and must find the line of least resistance, whatsoever it may be.", "더욱이 이것들은 다만 그녀가 나오는 것을 막을 뿐, 그녀가 들어가고 싶어하는 것까지 막지는 못할지도 모르오. 그럴 때 불사자는 필사적이라, 그것이 무엇이든 가장 저항이 적은 길을 찾아내고야 마니 말이오."], ["I shall be at hand all the night from sunset till after the sunrise, and if there be aught that may be learned I shall learn it.", "나는 해 질 녘부터 해 뜬 뒤까지 밤새 가까이 있을 것이며, 만일 알아낼 만한 것이 있다면 알아낼 것이오."], ["For Miss Lucy or from her, I have no fear; but that other to whom is there that she is Un-Dead, he have now the power to seek her tomb and find shelter.", "루시 양에 대해서나 그녀로부터는 나는 두려움이 없소. 그러나 그녀를 불사자로 만든 그 다른 자는 이제 그녀의 무덤을 찾아 은신처로 삼을 힘을 지녔소."], ["He is cunning, as I know from Mr. Jonathan and from the way that all along he have fooled us when he played with us for Miss Lucy’s life, and we lost; and in many ways the Un-Dead are strong.", "그는 교활하오, 조너선 군에게서 듣기로도, 또 그가 루시 양의 목숨을 두고 우리와 농락하며 줄곧 우리를 우롱하여 우리가 진 그 방식으로 보아도 그러하오. 그리고 여러모로 불사자는 강하오."], ["He have always the strength in his hand of twenty men; even we four who gave our strength to Miss Lucy it also is all to him.", "그는 늘 그 손에 스무 명의 힘을 지녔소. 루시 양에게 우리의 힘을 준 우리 넷의 힘조차 모두 그에게 가 있는 것이오."], ["Besides, he can summon his wolf and I know not what. So if it be that he come thither on this night he shall find me; but none other shall--until it be too late.", "게다가 그는 자기 늑대를 불러낼 수도, 또 내가 알지 못하는 무언가를 불러낼 수도 있소. 그러니 만일 그가 오늘 밤 그곳에 온다면 그는 나를 만나게 될 것이오."], ["But it may be that he will not attempt the place.", "그러나 다른 누구도 만나지 못할 것이오 — 너무 늦을 때까지는 말이오."], ["There is no reason why he should; his hunting ground is more full of game than the churchyard where the Un-Dead woman sleep, and the one old man watch.", "그러나 어쩌면 그는 그곳을 노리지 않을지도 모르오. 그가 그래야 할 이유가 없으니, 그의 사냥터는 불사자 여인이 잠들고 한 늙은이가 지키는 묘지보다 사냥감이 더 그득하니 말이오."]], [["“Therefore I write this in case....", "그러므로 나는 만일의 경우에 대비하여 이것을 쓰오……."], ["Take the papers that are with this, the diaries of Harker and the rest, and read them, and then find this great Un-Dead, and cut off his head and burn his heart or drive a stake through it, so that the world may rest from him.", "이것과 함께 있는 서류들, 곧 하커와 그 밖의 사람들의 일기를 가져다 읽으시오. 그런 다음 이 거대한 불사자를 찾아내어 그 머리를 자르고 심장을 태우거나 거기에 말뚝을 박아, 세상이 그에게서 벗어나 쉴 수 있게 하시오."]], [["“If it be so, farewell.", "만일 그리되거든, 안녕히."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "반 헬싱.\""]], [["28 September.--It is wonderful what a good night’s sleep will do for one.", "「수어드 박사의 일기.」 9월 28일. — 하룻밤 잘 자고 나면 사람에게 얼마나 좋은 일이 생기는지 놀랍기만 하다."], ["Yesterday I was almost willing to accept Van Helsing’s monstrous ideas; but now they seem to start out lurid before me as outrages on common sense. I have no doubt that he believes it all.", "어제 나는 반 헬싱의 그 끔찍한 생각을 거의 받아들이려 했다. 그러나 이제 그것들은 상식에 대한 모욕으로서 내 앞에 섬뜩하게 불거져 나오는 것 같다."], ["I wonder if his mind can have become in any way unhinged.", "그가 그 모든 것을 믿고 있다는 데에는 의심의 여지가 없다."], ["Surely there must be some rational explanation of all these mysterious things.", "그의 정신이 어떤 식으로든 균형을 잃은 것이 아닌가 하는 생각이 든다."], ["Is it possible that the Professor can have done it himself?", "분명 이 모든 불가사의한 일들에는 어떤 합리적인 설명이 있을 것이다."], ["He is so abnormally clever that if he went off his head he would carry out his intent with regard to some fixed idea in a wonderful way.", "혹 교수 자신이 그 일을 저지른 것은 아닐까? 그는 비정상적으로 영리한 사람이라, 만일 머리가 돈다면 어떤 고정관념에 관한 자기 의도를 놀라운 방식으로 실행에 옮길 것이다."], ["I am loath to think it, and indeed it would be almost as great a marvel as the other to find that Van Helsing was mad; but anyhow I shall watch him carefully. I may get some light on the mystery.", "그렇게 생각하기는 싫고, 실로 반 헬싱이 미쳤음을 알게 되는 것도 다른 일 못지않게 큰 불가사의가 되겠지만, 어쨌든 나는 그를 주의 깊게 지켜보겠다. 이 수수께끼에 대해 어떤 실마리를 얻을 수도 있으리라."]], [["29 September, morning.....", "9월 29일, 아침."], ["Last night, at a little before ten o’clock, Arthur and Quincey came into Van Helsing’s room; he told us all that he wanted us to do, but especially addressing himself to Arthur, as if all our wills were centred in his.", "……어젯밤, 열 시가 조금 못 되어 아서와 퀸시가 반 헬싱의 방으로 들어왔다. 그는 우리에게 자기가 우리가 해주기를 바라는 바를 모두 일러주었는데, 특히 아서에게 말을 건넸으니, 마치 우리 모두의 의지가 그에게 집중되어 있기라도 한 것 같았다."], ["He began by saying that he hoped we would all come with him too, “for,”", "그는 먼저, 우리 모두 자기와 함께 가주기를 바란다고 말하며 입을 열었다."], ["he said, “there is a grave duty to be done there.", "\"왜냐하면,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["You were doubtless surprised at my letter?”", "\"그곳에서 해야 할 막중한 의무가 있기 때문이오."], ["This query was directly addressed to Lord Godalming.", "분명 내 편지에 놀라셨겠지요?\" 이 물음은 곧장 고달밍 경에게 던져진 것이었다."]], [["“I was. It rather upset me for a bit.", "\"놀랐습니다. 한동안 적잖이 마음이 어지러웠지요."], ["There has been so much trouble around my house of late that I could do without any more.", "요즘 우리 집안에 워낙 많은 우환이 있어서, 더는 사양하고 싶을 정도입니다."], ["I have been curious, too, as to what you mean.", "게다가 무슨 뜻인지도 궁금했고요."], ["Quincey and I talked it over; but the more we talked, the more puzzled we got, till now I can say for myself that I’m about up a tree as to any meaning about anything.”", "퀸시와 그 일을 의논해보았습니다만, 의논할수록 더 어리둥절해지기만 하더군요. 이제 저로서는 무슨 일이든 그 의미에 대해 도무지 갈피를 못 잡겠다고 말할 수밖에요.\""]], [["“Me too,”", "\"저도요,\""], ["said Quincey Morris laconically.", "하고 퀸시 모리스가 짤막하게 말했다."]], [["“Oh,”", "\"오,\""], ["said the Professor, “then you are nearer the beginning, both of you, than friend John here, who has to go a long way back before he can even get so far as to begin.”", "하고 교수가 말했다. \"그렇다면 두 분 다, 시작에 닿기까지조차 한참을 거슬러 가야 하는 여기 이 존보다는 출발점에 더 가까운 셈이구려.\""]], [["It was evident that he recognised my return to my old doubting frame of mind without my saying a word.", "내가 한마디도 하지 않았는데도 그는 분명 내가 옛 회의(懷疑)의 심정으로 되돌아간 것을 알아차린 것이었다."], ["Then, turning to the other two, he said with intense gravity:--", "그러고는 다른 두 사람을 돌아보며, 그는 더없이 진지하게 말했다."]], [["“I want your permission to do what I think good this night.", "\"오늘 밤 내가 좋다고 여기는 일을 할 수 있도록 두 분의 허락을 구하오."], ["It is, I know, much to ask; and when you know what it is I propose to do you will know, and only then, how much.", "이것이 큰 부탁임을 나는 아오. 그리고 내가 무엇을 하려는지 알게 되면, 그제야 비로소 그것이 얼마나 큰 부탁인지도 알게 될 것이오."], ["Therefore may I ask that you promise me in the dark, so that afterwards, though you may be angry with me for a time--I must not disguise from myself the possibility that such may be--you shall not blame yourselves for anything.”", "그러니 부디 영문도 모른 채 나에게 약속해주기를 청해도 되겠소? 그래서 나중에, 비록 한동안은 나에게 화가 날지라도 — 그럴 가능성을 나 스스로 감추어서는 안 되겠지만 — 무슨 일이든 두 분 자신을 탓하지는 않도록 말이오.\""]], [["“That’s frank anyhow,”", "\"그건 어쨌든 솔직한 말씀이군요,\""], ["broke in Quincey.", "하고 퀸시가 끼어들었다."], ["“I’ll answer for the Professor.", "\"저는 교수님을 두고 보증하겠습니다."], ["I don’t quite see his drift, but I swear he’s honest; and that’s good enough for me.”", "그 속내가 무엇인지는 잘 모르겠습니다만, 정직한 분임은 맹세할 수 있습니다. 저에게는 그것으로 충분합니다.\""]], [["“I thank you, sir,”", "\"고맙소, 그대여,\""], ["said Van Helsing proudly.", "하고 반 헬싱이 자랑스럽게 말했다."], ["“I have done myself the honour of counting you one trusting friend, and such endorsement is dear to me.”", "\"나는 그대를 신뢰할 만한 벗 하나로 헤아리는 영예를 누렸거니와, 그런 보증은 나에게 소중하오.\""], ["He held out a hand, which Quincey took.", "그가 손을 내밀자, 퀸시가 그 손을 잡았다."]], [["Then Arthur spoke out:--", "그러자 아서가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Dr. Van Helsing, I don’t quite like to ‘buy a pig in a poke,’ as they say in Scotland, and if it be anything in which my honour as a gentleman or my faith as a Christian is concerned, I cannot make such a promise.", "\"반 헬싱 박사, 저는 스코틀랜드 사람들 말마따나 '자루 속의 돼지를 사는' 것은 그리 내키지 않습니다."], ["If you can assure me that what you intend does not violate either of these two, then I give my consent at once; though for the life of me, I cannot understand what you are driving at.”", "그리고 만일 그것이 신사로서의 제 명예나 기독교도로서의 제 신앙에 관계되는 일이라면, 저는 그런 약속을 할 수 없습니다. 박사께서 의도하시는 바가 이 둘 가운데 어느 것도 침해하지 않는다고 보증해주신다면, 저는 당장 동의하겠습니다. 비록 도무지 박사께서 무엇을 겨냥하시는지는 이해할 수 없지만요.\""]], [["“I accept your limitation,”", "\"그 단서를 받아들이겠소,\""], ["said Van Helsing, “and all I ask of you is that if you feel it necessary to condemn any act of mine, you will first consider it well and be satisfied that it does not violate your reservations.”", "하고 반 헬싱이 말했다. \"내가 그대에게 청하는 것은 다만, 혹 내 어떤 행동을 비난해야 한다고 느껴지거든, 먼저 그것을 잘 헤아려 그것이 그대의 유보 조건을 침해하지 않음을 확인해달라는 것뿐이오.\""]], [["“Agreed!”", "\"좋습니다!\""], ["said Arthur; “that is only fair.", "하고 아서가 말했다. \"그것이야말로 공정한 일이지요."], ["And now that the pourparlers are over, may I ask what it is we are to do?”", "자, 이제 예비 절충이 끝났으니, 우리가 무엇을 해야 하는지 여쭈어도 되겠습니까?\""]], [["“I want you to come with me, and to come in secret, to the churchyard at Kingstead.”", "\"그대가 나와 함께, 그것도 은밀히, 킹스테드의 묘지로 가주기를 바라오.\""]], [["Arthur’s face fell as he said in an amazed sort of way:--", "아서의 얼굴이 어두워졌고, 그는 어리둥절한 듯이 말했다."]], [["“Where poor Lucy is buried?”", "\"가엾은 루시가 묻혀 있는 곳 말입니까?\""], ["The Professor bowed.", "교수가 고개를 숙였다."], ["Arthur went on: “And when there?”", "아서가 말을 이었다. \"그래서 거기 가서는요?\""]], [["“To enter the tomb!”", "\"무덤에 들어가는 것이오!\""], ["Arthur stood up.", "아서가 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["“Professor, are you in earnest; or it is some monstrous joke?", "\"교수님, 진심이십니까, 아니면 무슨 끔찍한 농담인가요?"], ["Pardon me, I see that you are in earnest.”", "죄송합니다, 진심이신 것을 알겠군요.\""], ["He sat down again, but I could see that he sat firmly and proudly, as one who is on his dignity.", "그는 다시 앉았으나, 위엄을 지키는 사람처럼 꼿꼿하고 당당하게 앉는 것을 나는 볼 수 있었다."], ["There was silence until he asked again:--", "그가 다시 물을 때까지 침묵이 흘렀다."]], [["“And when in the tomb?”", "\"그래서 무덤에 들어가서는요?\""]], [["“To open the coffin.”", "\"관을 여는 것이오.\""]], [["“This is too much!”", "\"이건 너무합니다!\""], ["he said, angrily rising again.", "하고 그가 성을 내며 다시 일어섰다."], ["“I am willing to be patient in all things that are reasonable; but in this--this desecration of the grave--of one who----”", "\"저는 사리에 맞는 일이라면 무엇이든 참을 용의가 있습니다. 그러나 이것은 — 이 무덤에 대한 모독은 — 그것도…….\""], ["He fairly choked with indignation.", "그는 그야말로 분에 받쳐 목이 메었다."], ["The Professor looked pityingly at him.", "교수는 가엾다는 듯이 그를 바라보았다."]], [["“If I could spare you one pang, my poor friend,”", "\"단 하나의 고통이라도 그대에게서 덜어줄 수 있다면, 가엾은 벗이여,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “God knows I would.", "\"하느님께서 아시거니와, 나는 그리하겠소."], ["But this night our feet must tread in thorny paths; or later, and for ever, the feet you love must walk in paths of flame!”", "그러나 오늘 밤 우리의 발은 가시밭길을 디뎌야 하오. 그러지 않으면 훗날, 그리고 영영, 그대가 사랑하는 그 발이 불길의 길을 걸어야 하오!\""]], [["Arthur looked up with set white face and said:--", "아서는 굳어진 창백한 얼굴로 올려다보며 말했다."]], [["“Take care, sir, take care!”", "\"말씀 조심하시지요, 선생, 조심하십시오!\""]], [["“Would it not be well to hear what I have to say?” said Van Helsing.", "\"내가 할 말을 들어보는 것이 좋지 않겠소?\" 하고 반 헬싱이 말했다."], ["“And then you will at least know the limit of my purpose.", "\"그러면 적어도 내 의도의 한계만큼은 알게 될 것이오."], ["Shall I go on?”", "계속해도 되겠소?\""]], [["“That’s fair enough,” broke in Morris.", "\"그쯤이야 좋습니다,\" 하고 모리스가 끼어들었다."]], [["After a pause Van Helsing went on, evidently with an effort:--", "잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 반 헬싱은, 분명 안간힘을 쓰며 말을 이었다."]], [["“Miss Lucy is dead; is it not so? Yes!", "\"루시 양은 죽었소. 그렇지 않소? 그렇소!"], ["Then there can be no wrong to her.", "그러니 그녀에게 잘못될 일이란 있을 수 없소."], ["But if she be not dead----”", "그러나 만일 그녀가 죽지 않았다면…….\""]], [["Arthur jumped to his feet.", "아서가 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["“Good God!” he cried.", "\"맙소사!\" 하고 그가 외쳤다."], ["“What do you mean?", "\"무슨 말씀이십니까?"], ["Has there been any mistake; has she been buried alive?”", "무슨 착오라도 있었던 겁니까? 그녀가 산 채로 매장되기라도 했단 말입니까?\""], ["He groaned in anguish that not even hope could soften.", "그는 희망조차 누그러뜨릴 수 없는 비통함 속에서 신음했다."]], [["“I did not say she was alive, my child; I did not think it.", "\"그녀가 살아 있다고 말한 것은 아니오, 나의 아이여. 나는 그리 생각하지도 않소."], ["I go no further than to say that she might be Un-Dead.”", "다만 그녀가 불사자일지도 모른다는 데까지만 말하는 것이오.\""]], [["“Un-Dead!", "\"불사자라니!"], ["Not alive! What do you mean?", "살아 있지는 않다니! 무슨 말씀이십니까?"], ["Is this all a nightmare, or what is it?”", "이게 다 악몽입니까, 아니면 대체 무엇입니까?\""]], [["“There are mysteries which men can only guess at, which age by age they may solve only in part.", "\"사람이 그저 어렴풋이 짐작만 할 수 있는, 세월이 흐르고 흘러도 다만 일부분밖에 풀어내지 못하는 신비들이 있소."], ["Believe me, we are now on the verge of one.", "나를 믿으시오, 우리는 지금 그런 신비 하나의 문턱에 서 있소."], ["But I have not done. May I cut off the head of dead Miss Lucy?”", "그러나 나는 아직 말을 끝내지 않았소. 내가 죽은 루시 양의 머리를 잘라내도 되겠소?\""]], [["“Heavens and earth, no!”", "\"하늘과 땅에 맹세코, 안 됩니다!\""], ["cried Arthur in a storm of passion.", "하고 아서가 격정의 폭풍 속에서 외쳤다."], ["“Not for the wide world will I consent to any mutilation of her dead body. Dr.", "\"이 넓은 세상을 다 준다 해도 저는 그녀의 시신을 훼손하는 데에는 결코 동의하지 않겠습니다."], ["Van Helsing, you try me too far.", "반 헬싱 박사, 너무 저를 시험하시는군요."], ["What have I done to you that you should torture me so?", "제가 박사께 무슨 짓을 했기에 이토록 저를 괴롭히시는 겁니까?"], ["What did that poor, sweet girl do that you should want to cast such dishonour on her grave?", "그 가엾고 사랑스러운 처녀가 무슨 짓을 했기에 박사께서 그녀의 무덤에 그런 치욕을 안기려 하시는 겁니까?"], ["Are you mad to speak such things, or am I mad to listen to them?", "그런 말을 입에 담으시다니 박사께서 미치신 겁니까, 아니면 그런 말을 듣고 있는 제가 미친 겁니까?"], ["Don’t dare to think more of such a desecration; I shall not give my consent to anything you do.", "그런 모독을 더는 생각조차 마십시오. 저는 박사께서 하시는 그 어떤 일에도 동의하지 않겠습니다."], ["I have a duty to do in protecting her grave from outrage; and, by God, I shall do it!”", "저에게는 그녀의 무덤을 능욕으로부터 지킬 의무가 있고, 맹세코 저는 그것을 다하겠습니다!\""]], [["Van Helsing rose up from where he had all the time been seated, and said, gravely and sternly:--", "반 헬싱은 그동안 줄곧 앉아 있던 자리에서 일어나, 엄숙하고 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“My Lord Godalming, I, too, have a duty to do, a duty to others, a duty to you, a duty to the dead; and, by God, I shall do it!", "\"고달밍 경, 나에게도 다해야 할 의무가 있소. 다른 이들에 대한 의무, 그대에 대한 의무, 죽은 이에 대한 의무 말이오. 그리고 맹세코 나는 그것을 다하겠소!"], ["All I ask you now is that you come with me, that you look and listen; and if when later I make the same request you do not be more eager for its fulfilment even than I am, then--then I shall do my duty, whatever it may seem to me.", "지금 내가 그대에게 청하는 것은 다만, 나와 함께 가서 보고 들어달라는 것뿐이오. 그리고 만일 훗날 내가 같은 청을 했을 때, 그대가 나보다도 더 그 일이 이루어지기를 바라지 않거든, 그때는 — 그때는 나에게 어떻게 보이든 나는 내 의무를 다하겠소."], ["And then, to follow of your Lordship’s wishes I shall hold myself at your disposal to render an account to you, when and where you will.”", "그러고 나서 경의 뜻에 따라, 그대가 원하는 때와 곳에서 그대에게 해명할 수 있도록 내 몸을 그대의 처분에 맡기겠소.\""], ["His voice broke a little, and he went on with a voice full of pity:--", "그의 목소리가 약간 갈라졌고, 그는 연민 가득한 목소리로 말을 이었다."]], [["“But, I beseech you, do not go forth in anger with me.", "\"그러나 부디 청하건대, 나에게 화를 품은 채 떠나지는 마시오."], ["In a long life of acts which were often not pleasant to do, and which sometimes did wring my heart, I have never had so heavy a task as now.", "종종 하기에 달갑지 않은, 때로는 내 가슴을 쥐어짜는 일들로 점철된 긴 평생에, 나는 지금처럼 이토록 무거운 임무를 맡아본 적이 없소."], ["Believe me that if the time comes for you to change your mind towards me, one look from you will wipe away all this so sad hour, for I would do what a man can to save you from sorrow.", "나를 믿으시오, 혹 그대가 나에 대한 마음을 바꿀 때가 온다면, 그대의 눈길 한 번이 이 슬픈 시간을 모두 씻어주리니, 나는 그대를 슬픔에서 구하기 위해 사람이 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 하겠소."], ["Just think. For why should I give myself so much of labour and so much of sorrow?", "한번 생각해보시오. 무엇 때문에 내가 이토록 많은 수고와 이토록 많은 슬픔을 떠안겠소?"], ["I have come here from my own land to do what I can of good; at the first to please my friend John, and then to help a sweet young lady, whom, too, I came to love.", "나는 내 나라에서 이곳까지 와서, 내가 할 수 있는 선을 행하려 하고 있소. 처음에는 나의 벗 존을 기쁘게 하기 위해, 그다음에는 사랑스러운 젊은 숙녀를 돕기 위해서였는데, 나 또한 그녀를 사랑하게 되었소."], ["For her--I am ashamed to say so much, but I say it in kindness--I gave what you gave; the blood of my veins; I gave it, I, who was not, like you, her lover, but only her physician and her friend.", "그녀를 위해 — 이런 말을 이렇게 많이 하기가 부끄럽지만, 나는 호의에서 말하는 것이오 — 나는 그대가 준 것을 주었소. 곧 내 혈관의 피 말이오. 그대처럼 그녀의 연인도 아니고 다만 그녀의 의사이자 벗이었을 뿐인 내가 그것을 주었소."], ["I gave to her my nights and days--before death, after death; and if my death can do her good even now, when she is the dead Un-Dead, she shall have it freely.”", "나는 그녀에게 내 낮과 밤을 바쳤소 — 죽기 전에도, 죽은 뒤에도. 그리고 내 죽음이 지금, 그녀가 죽은 불사자가 된 이 마당에라도 그녀에게 도움이 된다면, 나는 기꺼이 그것을 내어주겠소.\""], ["He said this with a very grave, sweet pride, and Arthur was much affected by it.", "그는 이 말을 무척 엄숙하고도 다정한 긍지로 말했고, 아서는 이에 크게 감동했다."], ["He took the old man’s hand and said in a broken voice:--", "그는 노인의 손을 잡고 떨리는 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Oh, it is hard to think of it, and I cannot understand; but at least I shall go with you and wait.”", "\"아, 그것을 생각하기란 괴롭고, 저는 이해할 수도 없습니다. 그러나 적어도 박사와 함께 가서 기다려 보겠습니다.\""]], [["DR. SEWARD’S DIARY--continued\nIt was just a quarter before twelve o’clock when we got into the churchyard over the low wall.", "우리가 낮은 담을 넘어 묘지 안으로 들어선 것은 정각 열두 시 십오 분 전이었다."], ["The night was dark with occasional gleams of moonlight between the rents of the heavy clouds that scudded across the sky.", "밤은 어두웠고, 하늘을 가로질러 빠르게 흘러가는 짙은 구름의 갈라진 틈 사이로 이따금 달빛이 비쳤다."], ["We all kept somehow close together, with Van Helsing slightly in front as he led the way.", "우리는 모두 어떻게든 서로 가까이 붙어 있었고, 판 헬싱이 약간 앞장서서 길을 인도했다."], ["When we had come close to the tomb I looked well at Arthur, for I feared that the proximity to a place laden with so sorrowful a memory would upset him; but he bore himself well.", "무덤 가까이 이르렀을 때 나는 아서를 유심히 살폈으니, 이토록 슬픈 기억이 서린 곳에 가까이 다가가는 일이 그를 무너뜨리지나 않을까 두려웠던 까닭이다. 그러나 그는 잘 견뎌냈다."], ["I took it that the very mystery of the proceeding was in some way a counteractant to his grief.", "나는 이 일의 신비로움 자체가 어떤 식으로든 그의 슬픔을 누그러뜨리는 작용을 한 것이라 여겼다."], ["The Professor unlocked the door, and seeing a natural hesitation amongst us for various reasons, solved the difficulty by entering first himself.", "교수는 문을 열었고, 여러 가지 이유로 우리 사이에 당연한 망설임이 이는 것을 보고는, 자신이 먼저 들어감으로써 그 난처함을 풀어주었다."], ["The rest of us followed, and he closed the door.", "나머지 우리도 뒤따라 들어갔고, 그는 문을 닫았다."], ["He then lit a dark lantern and pointed to the coffin.", "그러고는 어둠 등(燈)을 켜고 관을 가리켰다."], ["Arthur stepped forward hesitatingly; Van Helsing said to me:--", "아서가 머뭇거리며 앞으로 나섰다. 판 헬싱이 나에게 말했다."]], [["“You were with me here yesterday.", "\"자네는 어제 나와 함께 여기 있었지."], ["Was the body of Miss Lucy in that coffin?”", "저 관 속에 루시 양의 시신이 있었나?\""]], [["“It was.” The Professor turned to the rest saying:--", "\"있었습니다.\" 교수는 나머지 사람들을 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“You hear; and yet there is no one who does not believe with me.”", "\"들으셨지요. 그런데도 나와 같이 믿지 않는 이가 없습니다.\""], ["He took his screwdriver and again took off the lid of the coffin.", "그는 드라이버를 들어 다시 관 뚜껑을 떼어냈다."], ["Arthur looked on, very pale but silent; when the lid was removed he stepped forward.", "아서는 몹시 창백했으나 말없이 지켜보았다. 뚜껑이 벗겨지자 그는 앞으로 나섰다."], ["He evidently did not know that there was a leaden coffin, or, at any rate, had not thought of it.", "그는 분명 납으로 된 관이 있다는 것을 몰랐거나, 적어도 거기까지 생각이 미치지는 못했던 모양이다."], ["When he saw the rent in the lead, the blood rushed to his face for an instant, but as quickly fell away again, so that he remained of a ghastly whiteness; he was still silent.", "납에 난 찢긴 자국을 보았을 때, 한순간 피가 그의 얼굴로 솟구쳤으나 곧 다시 가셔서, 그는 소름 끼칠 만큼 창백한 채로 있었다. 그는 여전히 말이 없었다."], ["Van Helsing forced back the leaden flange, and we all looked in and recoiled.", "판 헬싱이 납의 가장자리를 도로 밀어 젖히자, 우리는 모두 안을 들여다보고는 흠칫 물러섰다."]], [["The coffin was empty!", "관은 비어 있었다!"]], [["For several minutes no one spoke a word.", "몇 분 동안 아무도 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["The silence was broken by Quincey Morris:--", "그 침묵을 깬 것은 퀸시 모리스였다."]], [["“Professor, I answered for you.", "\"교수님, 저는 교수님을 믿고 보증했습니다."], ["Your word is all I want.", "교수님의 말씀이면 그것으로 족합니다."], ["I wouldn’t ask such a thing ordinarily--I wouldn’t so dishonour you as to imply a doubt; but this is a mystery that goes beyond any honour or dishonour.", "평소라면 이런 것을 묻지 않았을 겁니다. 의심을 내비쳐 교수님을 욕보이는 짓은 하지 않았을 거예요. 그러나 이것은 어떤 명예나 불명예를 넘어서는 수수께끼입니다."], ["Is this your doing?”", "이게 교수님이 하신 일입니까?\""]], [["“I swear to you by all that I hold sacred that I have not removed nor touched her.", "\"내가 신성하게 여기는 모든 것을 두고 맹세하건대, 나는 그녀를 옮기지도 손대지도 않았소."], ["What happened was this: Two nights ago my friend Seward and I came here--with good purpose, believe me.", "일어난 일은 이러하오. 이틀 전 밤에 내 벗 수어드와 내가 이곳에 왔소. 부디 믿으시오, 좋은 뜻으로 왔던 것이오."], ["I opened that coffin, which was then sealed up, and we found it, as now, empty.", "그때 봉인되어 있던 저 관을 내가 열었고, 우리는 그것이 지금처럼 비어 있는 것을 보았소."], ["We then waited, and saw something white come through the trees.", "그런 뒤 기다렸더니, 무언가 흰 것이 나무들 사이로 다가오는 것을 보았소."], ["The next day we came here in day-time, and she lay there.", "이튿날 우리는 낮에 이곳에 왔고, 그녀는 저기에 누워 있었소."], ["Did she not, friend John?” “Yes.”", "그렇지 않았나, 존?\""]], [["“That night we were just in time.", "\"그렇습니다.\" \"그날 밤 우리는 가까스로 때를 맞추었소."], ["One more so small child was missing, and we find it, thank God, unharmed amongst the graves.", "또 한 명의 작디작은 아이가 사라졌는데, 다행히도 우리는 그 아이를 무덤들 사이에서 다친 데 없이 찾아냈소."], ["Yesterday I came here before sundown, for at sundown the Un-Dead can move.", "어제는 해가 지기 전에 이곳에 왔으니, 해가 지면 비로소 불사귀가 움직일 수 있기 때문이오."], ["I waited here all the night till the sun rose, but I saw nothing.", "나는 해가 뜰 때까지 밤새 여기서 기다렸으나 아무것도 보지 못했소."], ["It was most probable that it was because I had laid over the clamps of those doors garlic, which the Un-Dead cannot bear, and other things which they shun.", "아마도 그것은, 불사귀가 견디지 못하는 마늘을, 그리고 그들이 꺼리는 다른 것들을 저 문의 죔쇠 위에 발라두었던 까닭일 것이오."], ["Last night there was no exodus, so to-night before the sundown I took away my garlic and other things.", "어젯밤에는 빠져나간 일이 없었으니, 오늘 밤 해가 지기 전에 나는 마늘과 다른 것들을 치웠소."], ["And so it is we find this coffin empty.", "그래서 우리가 이 빈 관을 보게 된 것이오."], ["But bear with me. So far there is much that is strange.", "그러나 조금만 참아주시오. 여기까지도 기이한 것이 많소."], ["Wait you with me outside, unseen and unheard, and things much stranger are yet to be.", "보이지도 들리지도 않게 나와 함께 밖에서 기다려주시오. 훨씬 더 기이한 일들이 아직 남아 있소."], ["So”--here he shut the dark slide of his lantern--“now to the outside.”", "자, 그러면\" — 여기서 그는 등의 어두운 덮개를 닫았다 — \"이제 밖으로 나갑시다.\""], ["He opened the door, and we filed out, he coming last and locking the door behind him.", "그는 문을 열었고, 우리는 줄지어 나갔으며, 그가 맨 마지막으로 나와 등 뒤로 문을 잠갔다."]], [["Oh!", "아!"], ["but it seemed fresh and pure in the night air after the terror of that vault.", "그러나 그 지하 납골소의 공포를 겪은 뒤 밤공기는 어찌나 신선하고 맑게 느껴지던지."], ["How sweet it was to see the clouds race by, and the passing gleams of the moonlight between the scudding clouds crossing and passing--like the gladness and sorrow of a man’s life; how sweet it was to breathe the fresh air, that had no taint of death and decay; how humanising to see the red lighting of the sky beyond the hill, and to hear far away the muffled roar that marks the life of a great city.", "구름이 빠르게 흘러가는 것을, 그리고 질주하는 구름들이 서로 엇갈리며 지나가는 사이로 달빛이 스쳐 지나가는 것을 — 마치 한 사람의 생애의 기쁨과 슬픔처럼 — 보는 것은 어찌나 감미로웠던가. 죽음과 부패의 더러움이 없는 신선한 공기를 들이마시는 것은 어찌나 감미로웠던가. 언덕 너머 하늘이 붉게 물든 것을 보고, 멀리서 대도시의 삶을 알리는 둔탁한 굉음을 듣는 것은 어찌나 사람의 마음을 따스하게 했던가."], ["Each in his own way was solemn and overcome.", "우리는 저마다의 방식으로 엄숙했고 또 감정에 압도되어 있었다."], ["Arthur was silent, and was, I could see, striving to grasp the purpose and the inner meaning of the mystery.", "아서는 말이 없었으며, 내가 보기에 그는 이 수수께끼의 목적과 그 깊은 뜻을 헤아려보려 애쓰고 있었다."], ["I was myself tolerably patient, and half inclined again to throw aside doubt and to accept Van Helsing’s conclusions.", "나 자신은 제법 인내심을 가지고 있었으며, 다시금 의심을 던져버리고 판 헬싱의 결론을 받아들이는 쪽으로 반쯤 기울어 있었다."], ["Quincey Morris was phlegmatic in the way of a man who accepts all things, and accepts them in the spirit of cool bravery, with hazard of all he has to stake.", "퀸시 모리스는 모든 것을 받아들이는 사람, 그것도 자기가 걸 수 있는 모든 것을 건 채 냉철한 용기의 정신으로 받아들이는 사람다운 침착함을 보였다."], ["Not being able to smoke, he cut himself a good-sized plug of tobacco and began to chew.", "담배를 피울 수 없었으므로 그는 제법 큼직한 씹는 담배 한 덩이를 잘라 씹기 시작했다."], ["As to Van Helsing, he was employed in a definite way.", "판 헬싱으로 말하자면, 그는 분명한 목적을 가지고 무언가에 몰두해 있었다."], ["First he took from his bag a mass of what looked like thin, wafer-like biscuit, which was carefully rolled up in a white napkin; next he took out a double-handful of some whitish stuff, like dough or putty.", "먼저 그는 가방에서 흰 냅킨에 정성스레 말려 있는, 얇고 웨이퍼 같은 비스킷처럼 보이는 것을 한 덩이 꺼냈다. 다음으로 그는 반죽이나 퍼티 같은 희끄무레한 것을 두 손 가득 꺼냈다."], ["He crumbled the wafer up fine and worked it into the mass between his hands.", "그는 웨이퍼를 잘게 부수어 두 손 사이에서 그 덩어리에 이겨 넣었다."], ["This he then took, and rolling it into thin strips, began to lay them into the crevices between the door and its setting in the tomb.", "그런 다음 그것을 가져다 가느다란 가닥으로 말아, 무덤 문과 그 문틀 사이의 틈에 끼워 넣기 시작했다."], ["I was somewhat puzzled at this, and being close, asked him what it was that he was doing.", "나는 이것이 다소 의아하여, 가까이 있었던 까닭에 무엇을 하고 있는지 그에게 물었다."], ["Arthur and Quincey drew near also, as they too were curious.", "아서와 퀸시도 궁금했던지 가까이 다가왔다."], ["He answered:--", "그가 대답했다."]], [["“I am closing the tomb, so that the Un-Dead may not enter.”", "\"나는 불사귀가 들어가지 못하도록 이 무덤을 봉하고 있는 것이오.\""]], [["“And is that stuff you have put there going to do it?”", "\"그런데 거기 넣으신 그것으로 그게 될까요?\""], ["asked Quincey. “Great Scott!", "하고 퀸시가 물었다. \"맙소사!"], ["Is this a game?” “It is.”", "이게 무슨 장난입니까?\""]], [["“What is that which you are using?”", "\"장난이 아니오.\""], ["This time the question was by Arthur.", "\"지금 쓰고 계신 것이 무엇입니까?\""], ["Van Helsing reverently lifted his hat as he answered:--", "이번에는 아서가 물었다. 판 헬싱은 경건하게 모자를 들어 올리며 대답했다."]], [["“The Host. I brought it from Amsterdam.", "\"성체(聖體)요. 암스테르담에서 가져왔소."], ["I have an Indulgence.” It was an answer that appalled the most sceptical of us, and we felt individually that in the presence of such earnest purpose as the Professor’s, a purpose which could thus use the to him most sacred of things, it was impossible to distrust.", "나에게는 사용을 허락받은 면벌(免罰)이 있소.\" 그것은 우리 가운데 가장 회의적인 사람마저 질리게 한 대답이었으며, 우리는 저마다, 교수처럼 진지한 목적, 그에게 가장 신성한 것마저 이렇게 쓸 수 있는 목적 앞에서는 의심한다는 것이 불가능하다고 느꼈다."], ["In respectful silence we took the places assigned to us close round the tomb, but hidden from the sight of any one approaching.", "우리는 경건한 침묵 속에서 무덤 가까이, 그러나 다가오는 누구의 눈에도 띄지 않게 각자에게 정해진 자리를 잡았다."], ["I pitied the others, especially Arthur.", "나는 다른 이들이, 특히 아서가 가여웠다."], ["I had myself been apprenticed by my former visits to this watching horror; and yet I, who had up to an hour ago repudiated the proofs, felt my heart sink within me.", "나 자신은 앞서 여러 번 이곳을 찾아오며 이 끔찍한 감시의 견습을 거친 셈이었으나, 그런 나조차도, 한 시간 전까지 그 증거들을 부정하던 내 가슴마저 속에서 내려앉는 것을 느꼈다. 무덤들이 그토록 소름 끼치게 희게 보인 적은 결코 없었다. 사이프러스나 주목, 노간주나무가 그토록 장례식 같은 음울함의 화신으로 보인 적은 결코 없었다."], ["Never did tombs look so ghastly white; never did cypress, or yew, or juniper so seem the embodiment of funereal gloom; never did tree or grass wave or rustle so ominously; never did bough creak so mysteriously; and never did the far-away howling of dogs send such a woeful presage through the night.", "나무나 풀이 그토록 불길하게 흔들리고 바스락거린 적은 결코 없었다. 가지가 그토록 알 수 없게 삐걱거린 적은 결코 없었다. 그리고 멀리서 들려오는 개들의 울부짖음이 그토록 비통한 전조를 밤새 실어 보낸 적은 결코 없었다."]], [["There was a long spell of silence, a big, aching void, and then from the Professor a keen “S-s-s-s!”", "오랜 침묵이, 크고 쓰라린 공허가 이어졌고, 이윽고 교수에게서 날카로운 \"쉿—!\" 소리가 났다. 그가 가리켰다."], ["He pointed; and far down the avenue of yews we saw a white figure advance--a dim white figure, which held something dark at its breast.", "그러자 저 멀리 주목이 늘어선 길 아래로 흰 형체 하나가 다가오는 것이 보였다. 가슴에 검은 무언가를 안은, 어렴풋한 흰 형체였다."], ["The figure stopped, and at the moment a ray of moonlight fell upon the masses of driving clouds and showed in startling prominence a dark-haired woman, dressed in the cerements of the grave.", "그 형체가 멈추었고, 바로 그 순간 한 줄기 달빛이 질주하는 구름 덩어리 위로 떨어지며, 무덤의 수의를 걸친 검은 머리의 여인을 소스라치게 또렷이 드러내 보였다."], ["We could not see the face, for it was bent down over what we saw to be a fair-haired child.", "우리는 얼굴을 볼 수 없었으니, 그것이 우리가 보기에 금발의 아이로 보이는 무언가 위로 숙여져 있었던 까닭이다."], ["There was a pause and a sharp little cry, such as a child gives in sleep, or a dog as it lies before the fire and dreams.", "잠깐의 멈춤이 있었고, 이어 아이가 잠결에 내지르거나 개가 난롯불 앞에 누워 꿈을 꿀 때 내는 것 같은, 날카롭고 자그마한 외침이 들렸다."], ["We were starting forward, but the Professor’s warning hand, seen by us as he stood behind a yew-tree, kept us back; and then as we looked the white figure moved forwards again.", "우리는 앞으로 뛰쳐나가려 했으나, 주목 뒤에 서 있던 교수가 경고하듯 든 손이 우리 눈에 들어와 우리를 제지했다. 그리고 우리가 보고 있는 사이 흰 형체가 다시 앞으로 움직였다."], ["It was now near enough for us to see clearly, and the moonlight still held.", "이제는 우리가 똑똑히 볼 수 있을 만큼 가까웠고, 달빛도 여전히 비치고 있었다."], ["My own heart grew cold as ice, and I could hear the gasp of Arthur, as we recognised the features of Lucy Westenra.", "우리가 루시 웨스튼라의 이목구비를 알아보았을 때, 내 심장은 얼음장처럼 차가워졌고, 아서가 숨을 헐떡이는 소리가 들렸다. 루시 웨스튼라였다."], ["Lucy Westenra, but yet how changed.", "그러나 어찌나 달라져 있었던가."], ["The sweetness was turned to adamantine, heartless cruelty, and the purity to voluptuous wantonness.", "그 다정함은 금강석처럼 단단하고 무자비한 잔인함으로, 그 순결함은 음탕한 방종으로 바뀌어 있었다."], ["Van Helsing stepped out, and, obedient to his gesture, we all advanced too; the four of us ranged in a line before the door of the tomb.", "판 헬싱이 앞으로 나섰고, 그의 손짓에 따라 우리도 모두 나아갔다. 우리 넷은 무덤 문 앞에 한 줄로 늘어섰다."], ["Van Helsing raised his lantern and drew the slide; by the concentrated light that fell on Lucy’s face we could see that the lips were crimson with fresh blood, and that the stream had trickled over her chin and stained the purity of her lawn death-robe.", "판 헬싱이 등을 들어 올려 덮개를 열자, 루시의 얼굴에 모여 떨어지는 빛 아래로, 그 입술이 갓 흘린 피로 진홍빛이며, 핏물이 턱을 타고 흘러내려 흰 아마포 수의의 순결함을 더럽힌 것이 보였다."]], [["We shuddered with horror.", "우리는 공포에 몸서리쳤다."], ["I could see by the tremulous light that even Van Helsing’s iron nerve had failed.", "떨리는 불빛 속에서 나는 판 헬싱의 강철 같은 담력마저 무너진 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Arthur was next to me, and if I had not seized his arm and held him up, he would have fallen.", "아서는 내 곁에 있었는데, 만일 내가 그의 팔을 붙들어 부축하지 않았더라면 그는 쓰러졌을 것이다."]], [["When Lucy--I call the thing that was before us Lucy because it bore her shape--saw us she drew back with an angry snarl, such as a cat gives when taken unawares; then her eyes ranged over us.", "루시가 — 우리 앞에 있는 그것이 그녀의 모습을 하고 있었으므로 나는 그것을 루시라 부른다 — 우리를 보더니, 불시에 붙들린 고양이가 내는 것 같은 성난 으르렁거림과 함께 뒤로 물러섰다. 그러더니 그 눈이 우리를 훑었다."], ["Lucy’s eyes in form and colour; but Lucy’s eyes unclean and full of hell-fire, instead of the pure, gentle orbs we knew.", "모양과 빛깔은 루시의 눈이었다. 그러나 우리가 알던 그 맑고 부드러운 눈동자가 아니라, 더럽혀지고 지옥불로 가득 찬 루시의 눈이었다."], ["At that moment the remnant of my love passed into hate and loathing; had she then to be killed, I could have done it with savage delight.", "그 순간 내 사랑의 마지막 자취는 증오와 혐오로 바뀌었다. 만일 그때 그녀를 죽여야 했다면 나는 사나운 기쁨으로 그 일을 해낼 수 있었으리라."], ["As she looked, her eyes blazed with unholy light, and the face became wreathed with a voluptuous smile.", "그녀는 우리를 바라보는 동안, 그 눈이 부정한 빛으로 타올랐고, 얼굴에는 음탕한 미소가 어렸다."], ["Oh, God, how it made me shudder to see it! With a careless motion, she flung to the ground, callous as a devil, the child that up to now she had clutched strenuously to her breast, growling over it as a dog growls over a bone.", "오, 하느님, 그것을 보는 것이 나를 어찌나 몸서리치게 했던가! 그녀는 그때까지 개가 뼈다귀를 두고 으르렁대듯 그르렁거리며 가슴에 악착같이 끌어안고 있던 아이를, 악마처럼 무정하게, 아무렇지도 않은 동작으로 땅바닥에 내던졌다."], ["The child gave a sharp cry, and lay there moaning.", "아이는 날카로운 비명을 지르고는 그 자리에 누워 신음했다."], ["There was a cold-bloodedness in the act which wrung a groan from Arthur; when she advanced to him with outstretched arms and a wanton smile he fell back and hid his face in his hands.", "그 행동에는 냉혹함이 서려 있어 아서에게서 신음을 자아냈다. 그녀가 두 팔을 벌리고 음탕한 미소를 띤 채 그에게 다가가자, 그는 뒤로 물러서며 두 손에 얼굴을 묻었다."]], [["She still advanced, however, and with a languorous, voluptuous grace, said:--", "그러나 그녀는 여전히 다가오며, 나른하고 음탕한 우아함으로 말했다."]], [["“Come to me, Arthur.", "\"이리 와요, 아서."], ["Leave these others and come to me.", "저 사람들을 두고 나에게로 와요."], ["My arms are hungry for you. Come, and we can rest together.", "내 팔이 당신을 애타게 그리워하고 있어요. 와요, 우리 함께 쉬어요."], ["Come, my husband, come!”", "와요, 내 남편이여, 와요!\""]], [["There was something diabolically sweet in her tones--something of the tingling of glass when struck--which rang through the brains even of us who heard the words addressed to another.", "그녀의 목소리에는 무언가 악마처럼 감미로운 것이 — 유리가 부딪칠 때의 그 짜릿한 울림 같은 것이 — 깃들어 있어, 다른 이에게 건네진 말이었음에도 그것을 들은 우리의 머릿속까지 울려 퍼졌다."], ["As for Arthur, he seemed under a spell; moving his hands from his face, he opened wide his arms.", "아서로 말하자면, 그는 어떤 주문에 걸린 듯했다. 얼굴에서 두 손을 떼며 그는 두 팔을 활짝 벌렸다."], ["She was leaping for them, when Van Helsing sprang forward and held between them his little golden crucifix.", "그녀가 그 팔을 향해 뛰어들려는 찰나, 판 헬싱이 앞으로 달려나가 두 사람 사이에 그의 자그마한 금 십자고상을 들이댔다."], ["She recoiled from it, and, with a suddenly distorted face, full of rage, dashed past him as if to enter the tomb.", "그녀는 그것에서 움찔 물러섰고, 갑자기 일그러진 분노로 가득 찬 얼굴로, 마치 무덤 안으로 들어가려는 듯 그를 스쳐 내달렸다."]], [["When within a foot or two of the door, however, she stopped, as if arrested by some irresistible force.", "그러나 문에서 한두 자쯤 떨어진 곳에 이르자, 그녀는 거역할 수 없는 어떤 힘에 붙들린 듯 멈춰 섰다."], ["Then she turned, and her face was shown in the clear burst of moonlight and by the lamp, which had now no quiver from Van Helsing’s iron nerves.", "그러더니 돌아섰고, 그녀의 얼굴이 환하게 쏟아지는 달빛과, 이제 판 헬싱의 강철 같은 담력에 더는 떨림이 없는 등불에 드러났다."], ["Never did I see such baffled malice on a face; and never, I trust, shall such ever be seen again by mortal eyes.", "나는 그토록 좌절된 악의가 어린 얼굴을 본 적이 없으며, 그런 얼굴이 사람의 눈에 다시 보이는 일은 결코 없기를 바란다."], ["The beautiful colour became livid, the eyes seemed to throw out sparks of hell-fire, the brows were wrinkled as though the folds of the flesh were the coils of Medusa’s snakes, and the lovely, blood-stained mouth grew to an open square, as in the passion masks of the Greeks and Japanese. If ever a face meant death--if looks could kill--we saw it at that moment.", "그 아름다운 빛깔은 검푸르게 변했고, 두 눈은 지옥불의 불꽃을 내뿜는 듯했으며, 미간은 살의 주름이 메두사의 뱀들이 똬리를 튼 것처럼 일그러졌고, 그 어여쁜, 피로 물든 입은 그리스와 일본의 격정의 가면처럼 네모나게 벌어졌다. 만일 죽음을 뜻하는 얼굴이 있다면 — 눈빛으로 사람을 죽일 수 있다면 — 우리는 그 순간 바로 그것을 본 것이었다."]], [["And so for full half a minute, which seemed an eternity, she remained between the lifted crucifix and the sacred closing of her means of entry.", "그리하여 영원처럼 느껴지는 꼬박 삼십 초 동안, 그녀는 치켜든 십자고상과, 신성하게 봉해진 자신의 출입구 사이에 머물러 있었다."], ["Van Helsing broke the silence by asking Arthur:--", "판 헬싱이 아서에게 물으며 그 침묵을 깼다."]], [["“Answer me, oh my friend!", "\"대답해주시오, 오 나의 벗이여!"], ["Am I to proceed in my work?”", "내 일을 계속해도 되겠소?\""]], [["Arthur threw himself on his knees, and hid his face in his hands, as he answered:--", "아서는 무릎을 꿇고 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채 대답했다."]], [["“Do as you will, friend; do as you will.", "\"당신 뜻대로 하시오, 벗이여. 당신 뜻대로 하시오."], ["There can be no horror like this ever any more;”", "이런 끔찍한 일이 다시는 있을 수 없겠지요.\""], ["and he groaned in spirit. Quincey and I simultaneously moved towards him, and took his arms.", "그러고는 그는 영혼 깊이 신음했다. 퀸시와 나는 동시에 그에게 다가가 그의 팔을 붙들었다."], ["We could hear the click of the closing lantern as Van Helsing held it down; coming close to the tomb, he began to remove from the chinks some of the sacred emblem which he had placed there.", "판 헬싱이 등을 내려놓을 때 등이 닫히는 딸깍 소리가 들렸다. 그는 무덤 가까이 다가가, 거기에 끼워두었던 성스러운 표상의 일부를 틈에서 떼어내기 시작했다."], ["We all looked on in horrified amazement as we saw, when he stood back, the woman, with a corporeal body as real at that moment as our own, pass in through the interstice where scarce a knife-blade could have gone.", "그가 뒤로 물러섰을 때, 그 순간 우리 자신의 몸만큼이나 실재하는 육신을 지닌 그 여인이 칼날 하나 겨우 들어갈 만한 틈으로 빠져 들어가는 것을 보고, 우리는 모두 경악에 차 소름 끼치며 지켜보았다."], ["We all felt a glad sense of relief when we saw the Professor calmly restoring the strings of putty to the edges of the door.", "교수가 침착하게 퍼티 가닥을 다시 문 가장자리에 붙이는 것을 보았을 때, 우리는 모두 안도의 기쁨을 느꼈다."]], [["When this was done, he lifted the child and said:", "이 일이 끝나자, 그는 아이를 들어 올리며 말했다."]], [["“Come now, my friends; we can do no more till to-morrow.", "\"자 이제, 벗들이여, 우리는 내일까지 더는 할 일이 없소."], ["There is a funeral at noon, so here we shall all come before long after that.", "정오에 장례식이 있으니, 우리는 그 얼마 뒤에 모두 이곳으로 다시 올 것이오."], ["The friends of the dead will all be gone by two, and when the sexton lock the gate we shall remain.", "고인의 벗들은 두 시면 모두 떠날 것이고, 묘지기가 문을 잠그면 우리는 남아 있을 것이오."], ["Then there is more to do; but not like this of to-night.", "그때 할 일이 더 있소. 그러나 오늘 밤 같은 일은 아니오."], ["As for this little one, he is not much harm, and by to-morrow night he shall be well.", "이 어린것으로 말하자면, 크게 상한 데는 없고 내일 밤이면 괜찮아질 것이오."], ["We shall leave him where the police will find him, as on the other night; and then to home.”", "우리는 지난밤처럼 이 아이를 경찰이 발견할 만한 곳에 두고, 그런 뒤 집으로 가겠소.\""], ["Coming close to Arthur, he said:--", "그는 아서에게 가까이 다가가 말했다."]], [["“My friend Arthur, you have had a sore trial; but after, when you look back, you will see how it was necessary.", "\"내 벗 아서, 그대는 쓰라린 시련을 겪었소. 그러나 나중에, 그대가 돌이켜볼 때면 그것이 어찌하여 필요했는지 알게 될 것이오."], ["You are now in the bitter waters, my child.", "그대는 지금 쓴 물 가운데 있소, 내 아이여."], ["By this time to-morrow you will, please God, have passed them, and have drunk of the sweet waters; so do not mourn overmuch.", "내일 이맘때면, 하느님의 뜻이라면, 그대는 그 물을 다 건너 단물을 마시게 될 것이오. 그러니 너무 슬퍼하지 마시오."], ["Till then I shall not ask you to forgive me.”", "그때까지 나는 그대에게 나를 용서해달라 청하지 않겠소.\""]], [["Arthur and Quincey came home with me, and we tried to cheer each other on the way.", "아서와 퀸시는 나와 함께 집으로 왔고, 우리는 오는 길에 서로를 위로하려 애썼다."], ["We had left the child in safety, and were tired; so we all slept with more or less reality of sleep.", "아이를 안전하게 두고 온 데다 지쳐 있었으므로, 우리는 모두 어느 정도는 참된 잠을 잤다."]], [["29 September, night.--A little before twelve o’clock we three--Arthur, Quincey Morris, and myself--called for the Professor.", "9월 29일, 밤. — 정각 열두 시 조금 전에 우리 셋 — 아서, 퀸시 모리스, 그리고 나 — 은 교수를 찾아갔다."], ["It was odd to notice that by common consent we had all put on black clothes.", "우리가 약속이라도 한 듯 모두 검은 옷을 입고 있는 것을 알아챈 것은 묘한 일이었다."], ["Of course, Arthur wore black, for he was in deep mourning, but the rest of us wore it by instinct.", "물론 아서는 깊은 상중이었으므로 검은 옷을 입었으나, 나머지 우리는 본능적으로 그리한 것이었다."], ["We got to the churchyard by half-past one, and strolled about, keeping out of official observation, so that when the gravediggers had completed their task and the sexton under the belief that every one had gone, had locked the gate, we had the place all to ourselves.", "우리는 한 시 반쯤 묘지에 닿아, 관계자들의 눈에 띄지 않게 이리저리 거닐었고, 그리하여 인부들이 일을 마치고 묘지기가 모두 떠났다고 믿어 문을 잠갔을 때, 그곳은 온전히 우리 차지가 되었다."], ["Van Helsing, instead of his little black bag, had with him a long leather one, something like a cricketing bag; it was manifestly of fair weight.", "판 헬싱은 작은 검은 가방 대신 길쭉한 가죽 가방을 가지고 있었는데, 크리켓 가방 같은 것이었다. 보아하니 제법 묵직했다."]], [["When we were alone and had heard the last of the footsteps die out up the road, we silently, and as if by ordered intention, followed the Professor to the tomb.", "우리만 남고, 길 위로 멀어지는 마지막 발소리가 잦아드는 것을 들은 뒤, 우리는 말없이, 마치 미리 정해놓은 뜻에 따르듯, 교수를 따라 무덤으로 갔다."], ["He unlocked the door, and we entered, closing it behind us.", "그는 문을 열었고, 우리는 안으로 들어가 뒤로 문을 닫았다."], ["Then he took from his bag the lantern, which he lit, and also two wax candles, which, when lighted, he stuck, by melting their own ends, on other coffins, so that they might give light sufficient to work by. When he again lifted the lid off Lucy’s coffin we all looked--Arthur trembling like an aspen--and saw that the body lay there in all its death-beauty.", "그러고는 그가 가방에서 등을 꺼내 불을 켰고, 밀랍 양초 두 자루도 꺼냈는데, 불을 붙인 뒤 그 끝을 녹여 다른 관들 위에 붙여, 일하기에 충분한 빛을 내게 했다. 그가 다시 루시의 관 뚜껑을 들어 올렸을 때 우리는 모두 들여다보았다 — 아서는 사시나무처럼 떨고 있었다 — 그리고 시신이 죽음의 아름다움을 온전히 간직한 채 거기 누워 있는 것을 보았다."], ["But there was no love in my own heart, nothing but loathing for the foul Thing which had taken Lucy’s shape without her soul.", "그러나 내 마음에는 사랑이라곤 없었고, 다만 루시의 영혼 없이 그 모습만을 빼앗아 간 그 더러운 것에 대한 혐오만이 있었다."], ["I could see even Arthur’s face grow hard as he looked.", "바라보는 동안 아서의 얼굴마저 굳어지는 것을 나는 볼 수 있었다."], ["Presently he said to Van Helsing:--", "이윽고 그가 판 헬싱에게 말했다."]], [["“Is this really Lucy’s body, or only a demon in her shape?”", "\"이것이 정말 루시의 시신입니까, 아니면 그녀의 모습을 한 악령일 뿐입니까?\""]], [["“It is her body, and yet not it.", "\"그녀의 몸이오. 그러면서도 그것이 아니오. 그러나 조금만 기다리시오."], ["But wait a while, and you all see her as she was, and is.”", "그러면 그대들은 모두 그녀가 본디 그러했고 또 그러한 모습으로 그녀를 보게 될 것이오.\""]], [["She seemed like a nightmare of Lucy as she lay there; the pointed teeth, the bloodstained, voluptuous mouth--which it made one shudder to see--the whole carnal and unspiritual appearance, seeming like a devilish mockery of Lucy’s sweet purity.", "거기 누운 그녀는 루시의 악몽 같았다. 뾰족한 이빨, 피로 물든 음탕한 입 — 그것을 보는 것만으로도 몸서리치게 했다 — 그 온통 육욕적이고 영혼 없는 모습은, 루시의 그 다정한 순결함에 대한 악마 같은 조롱처럼 보였다."], ["Van Helsing, with his usual methodicalness, began taking the various contents from his bag and placing them ready for use.", "판 헬싱은 여느 때처럼 차근차근, 가방에서 여러 물건을 꺼내 쓸 수 있도록 늘어놓기 시작했다."], ["First he took out a soldering iron and some plumbing solder, and then a small oil-lamp, which gave out, when lit in a corner of the tomb, gas which burned at fierce heat with a blue flame; then his operating knives, which he placed to hand; and last a round wooden stake, some two and a half or three inches thick and about three feet long.", "먼저 그는 납땜인두와 약간의 배관용 땜납을, 그다음에는 작은 기름등을 꺼냈는데, 무덤 한구석에서 불을 붙이자 그것은 푸른 불꽃으로 맹렬한 열을 내며 타는 가스를 뿜어냈다. 다음으로는 수술용 칼들을 손 닿는 곳에 놓았고, 마지막으로 두께가 두 치 반이나 세 치쯤, 길이가 석 자쯤 되는 둥근 나무 말뚝을 꺼냈다."], ["One end of it was hardened by charring in the fire, and was sharpened to a fine point.", "그 한쪽 끝은 불에 그슬려 단단하게 만든 뒤 뾰족하게 깎여 있었다."], ["With this stake came a heavy hammer, such as in households is used in the coal-cellar for breaking the lumps.", "이 말뚝과 함께, 가정에서 석탄 창고의 덩어리를 깰 때 쓰는 것 같은 묵직한 망치가 나왔다."], ["To me, a doctor’s preparations for work of any kind are stimulating and bracing, but the effect of these things on both Arthur and Quincey was to cause them a sort of consternation.", "나에게 의사가 어떤 종류든 일을 준비하는 모습은 자극이 되고 기운을 돋우는 것이었으나, 이 물건들이 아서와 퀸시 두 사람에게 미친 영향은 일종의 경악이었다."], ["They both, however, kept their courage, and remained silent and quiet.", "그러나 그들은 둘 다 용기를 잃지 않고, 말없이 조용히 있었다."]], [["When all was ready, Van Helsing said:--", "모든 준비가 끝나자 판 헬싱이 말했다."]], [["“Before we do anything, let me tell you this; it is out of the lore and experience of the ancients and of all those who have studied the powers of the Un-Dead.", "\"우리가 무엇이든 하기에 앞서, 이것을 먼저 말해두겠소. 이는 옛사람들의, 그리고 불사귀의 힘을 연구해온 모든 이들의 학문과 경험에서 나온 것이오."], ["When they become such, there comes with the change the curse of immortality; they cannot die, but must go on age after age adding new victims and multiplying the evils of the world; for all that die from the preying of the Un-Dead becomes themselves Un-Dead, and prey on their kind.", "그들이 그런 존재가 될 때, 그 변화와 함께 불멸의 저주가 따르오. 그들은 죽을 수 없으며, 시대를 거듭하며 새로운 희생자를 더하고 세상의 악을 불려나가야만 하오. 불사귀의 먹이가 되어 죽는 자는 모두 스스로 불사귀가 되어 제 동족을 먹이로 삼게 되기 때문이오."], ["And so the circle goes on ever widening, like as the ripples from a stone thrown in the water.", "그리하여 그 고리는, 물에 던진 돌에서 퍼지는 물결처럼, 점점 더 넓게 끝없이 번져가오."], ["Friend Arthur, if you had met that kiss which you know of before poor Lucy die; or again, last night when you open your arms to her, you would in time, when you had died, have become nosferatu, as they call it in Eastern Europe, and would all time make more of those Un-Deads that so have fill us with horror.", "벗 아서, 만일 그대가, 가엾은 루시가 죽기 전에 그대가 아는 그 입맞춤을 받았더라면, 또는 어젯밤 그대가 그녀에게 두 팔을 벌렸을 때 그리했더라면, 그대도 머지않아, 그대가 죽었을 때, 동유럽에서 부르는 말로 노스페라투가 되어, 우리를 그토록 공포로 채우는 그런 불사귀들을 끝없이 더 만들어냈을 것이오."], ["The career of this so unhappy dear lady is but just begun.", "이 가엾은 부인의 행로는 이제 막 시작되었을 뿐이오."], ["Those children whose blood she suck are not as yet so much the worse; but if she live on, Un-Dead, more and more they lose their blood and by her power over them they come to her; and so she draw their blood with that so wicked mouth.", "그녀가 피를 빨아먹은 저 아이들은 아직 그리 크게 상하지는 않았소. 그러나 그녀가 불사귀로 계속 살아간다면, 그들은 점점 더 피를 잃을 것이고, 그녀가 그들에게 미치는 힘으로 그들은 그녀에게로 오게 될 것이오. 그리하여 그녀는 그 사악하기 짝이 없는 입으로 그들의 피를 빨아들이는 것이오."], ["But if she die in truth, then all cease; the tiny wounds of the throats disappear, and they go back to their plays unknowing ever of what has been.", "그러나 만일 그녀가 참으로 죽는다면, 그 모든 것이 멎소. 목에 난 그 자그마한 상처들은 사라지고, 그들은 무슨 일이 있었는지 영영 모른 채 제 놀이로 돌아갈 것이오."], ["But of the most blessed of all, when this now Un-Dead be made to rest as true dead, then the soul of the poor lady whom we love shall again be free.", "그러나 무엇보다 가장 복된 일은, 지금 불사귀가 된 이 존재가 참된 죽음으로 안식하게 될 때, 우리가 사랑하는 그 가엾은 부인의 영혼이 다시 자유로워지리라는 것이오."], ["Instead of working wickedness by night and growing more debased in the assimilating of it by day, she shall take her place with the other Angels.", "밤마다 사악함을 행하고 낮마다 그것을 제 것으로 삼으며 더욱 타락해가는 대신, 그녀는 다른 천사들과 함께 제자리를 차지하게 될 것이오. 그러니, 나의 벗이여, 그녀를 자유롭게 하는 일격을 가하는 손은 그녀에게 복된 손이 될 것이오. 나는 이 일을 기꺼이 맡겠소."], ["So that, my friend, it will be a blessed hand for her that shall strike the blow that sets her free.", "그러나 우리 가운데 그럴 더 나은 권리를 가진 이가 없겠소? 훗날, 잠이 오지 않는 고요한 밤에 이렇게 생각하는 것이 어찌 기쁨이 아니겠소."], ["To this I am willing; but is there none amongst us who has a better right?", "'그녀를 별들에게로 보낸 것은 내 손이었다. 그녀를 가장 사랑한 이의 손이었다."], ["Will it be no joy to think of hereafter in the silence of the night when sleep is not: ‘It was my hand that sent her to the stars; it was the hand of him that loved her best; the hand that of all she would herself have chosen, had it been to her to choose?’ Tell me if there be such a one amongst us?”", "그녀가 스스로 고를 수 있었다면 누구보다 그녀 자신이 골랐을 바로 그 손이었다.' 우리 가운데 그런 이가 있거든 나에게 말해주시오.\""]], [["We all looked at Arthur.", "우리는 모두 아서를 바라보았다."], ["He saw, too, what we all did, the infinite kindness which suggested that his should be the hand which would restore Lucy to us as a holy, and not an unholy, memory; he stepped forward and said bravely, though his hand trembled, and his face was as pale as snow:--", "그 역시 우리 모두가 본 것을, 곧 루시를 부정한 기억이 아니라 거룩한 기억으로 우리에게 되돌려줄 손이 바로 그의 손이어야 한다는 그 한없는 다정함을 알아챘다. 그는 앞으로 나서며, 손은 떨리고 얼굴은 눈처럼 창백했으나 용감하게 말했다."]], [["“My true friend, from the bottom of my broken heart I thank you.", "\"나의 참된 벗이여, 부서진 내 가슴 밑바닥에서부터 그대에게 감사하오."], ["Tell me what I am to do, and I shall not falter!”", "내가 무엇을 해야 할지 일러주시오. 결코 주저하지 않으리다!\""], ["Van Helsing laid a hand on his shoulder, and said:--", "판 헬싱은 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고 말했다."]], [["“Brave lad! A moment’s courage, and it is done.", "\"용감한 젊은이여! 한순간의 용기면 끝나오."], ["This stake must be driven through her.", "이 말뚝을 그녀의 몸에 박아 넣어야 하오."], ["It will be a fearful ordeal--be not deceived in that--but it will be only a short time, and you will then rejoice more than your pain was great; from this grim tomb you will emerge as though you tread on air.", "끔찍한 시련이 될 것이오 — 그 점에 대해서는 속지 마시오 — 그러나 잠깐일 뿐이며, 그러고 나면 그대는 그대가 겪은 고통이 컸던 것 이상으로 기뻐하게 될 것이오. 이 음산한 무덤에서 그대는 마치 허공을 밟는 듯 가벼이 걸어 나오게 될 것이오."], ["But you must not falter when once you have begun.", "그러나 일단 시작하거든 결코 주저해서는 안 되오."], ["Only think that we, your true friends, are round you, and that we pray for you all the time.”", "다만 우리가, 그대의 참된 벗들이 그대를 둘러싸고 있으며, 내내 그대를 위해 기도하고 있다는 것만 생각하시오.\""]], [["“Go on,”", "\"계속하시오.\""], ["said Arthur hoarsely.", "아서가 쉰 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Tell me what I am to do.”", "\"내가 무엇을 해야 할지 일러주시오.\""]], [["“Take this stake in your left hand, ready to place the point over the heart, and the hammer in your right.", "\"이 말뚝을 왼손에 쥐고 끝을 심장 위에 댈 채비를 하고, 망치는 오른손에 드시오."], ["Then when we begin our prayer for the dead--I shall read him, I have here the book, and the others shall follow--strike in God’s name, that so all may be well with the dead that we love and that the Un-Dead pass away.”", "그런 다음 우리가 죽은 이를 위한 기도를 시작하면 — 내가 읽을 것이오, 여기 책이 있소, 나머지 사람들은 따라 하시오 — 하느님의 이름으로 내리치시오. 그리하여 우리가 사랑하는 죽은 이에게 모든 것이 평안하고, 불사귀가 사라지도록 하시오.\""]], [["Arthur took the stake and the hammer, and when once his mind was set on action his hands never trembled nor even quivered.", "아서는 말뚝과 망치를 들었고, 일단 그 마음이 행동에 굳어지자 그의 손은 떨리지도, 흔들리지조차 않았다."], ["Van Helsing opened his missal and began to read, and Quincey and I followed as well as we could.", "판 헬싱은 미사 경본을 펼쳐 읽기 시작했고, 퀸시와 나는 최선을 다해 따라 했다."], ["Arthur placed the point over the heart, and as I looked I could see its dint in the white flesh.", "아서는 끝을 심장 위에 댔는데, 내가 보니 흰 살에 그 자국이 파이는 것이 보였다."], ["Then he struck with all his might.", "그러고는 그가 온 힘을 다해 내리쳤다."]], [["The Thing in the coffin writhed; and a hideous, blood-curdling screech came from the opened red lips.", "관 속의 그것이 몸부림쳤다. 그리고 벌어진 붉은 입술에서 소름 끼치는, 피를 얼어붙게 하는 비명이 터져 나왔다."], ["The body shook and quivered and twisted in wild contortions; the sharp white teeth champed together till the lips were cut, and the mouth was smeared with a crimson foam.", "몸은 흔들리고 떨리며 거칠게 비틀려 뒤틀렸다. 날카로운 흰 이빨은 입술이 베일 때까지 맞부딪쳤고, 입은 진홍빛 거품으로 범벅이 되었다."], ["But Arthur never faltered. He looked like a figure of Thor as his untrembling arm rose and fell, driving deeper and deeper the mercy-bearing stake, whilst the blood from the pierced heart welled and spurted up around it.", "그러나 아서는 결코 주저하지 않았다. 떨림 없는 그의 팔이 오르내리며 자비를 베푸는 말뚝을 점점 더 깊이 박아 넣을 때, 그는 토르 신상(神像) 같았으며, 그동안 꿰뚫린 심장에서 솟은 피가 그 둘레로 콸콸 분출되었다."], ["His face was set, and high duty seemed to shine through it; the sight of it gave us courage so that our voices seemed to ring through the little vault.", "그의 얼굴은 굳어 있었고, 그를 통해 드높은 사명이 빛나는 듯했다. 그 광경은 우리에게 용기를 주어, 우리의 목소리가 그 작은 납골소를 두루 울리는 듯했다."]], [["And then the writhing and quivering of the body became less, and the teeth seemed to champ, and the face to quiver. Finally it lay still.", "그러더니 몸의 몸부림과 떨림이 잦아들었고, 이빨이 맞부딪치고 얼굴이 떨리는 듯하더니, 마침내 그것은 가만히 누웠다."], ["The terrible task was over.", "그 끔찍한 일이 끝났다."]], [["The hammer fell from Arthur’s hand.", "망치가 아서의 손에서 떨어졌다."], ["He reeled and would have fallen had we not caught him.", "그는 비틀거렸고, 우리가 붙잡지 않았더라면 쓰러졌을 것이다."], ["The great drops of sweat sprang from his forehead, and his breath came in broken gasps.", "그의 이마에서 굵은 땀방울이 솟았고, 숨은 끊어질 듯 헐떡였다."], ["It had indeed been an awful strain on him; and had he not been forced to his task by more than human considerations he could never have gone through with it.", "그것은 실로 그에게 무서운 부담이었으며, 만일 인간적인 헤아림을 넘어서는 것이 그를 그 일로 떠밀지 않았더라면 그는 결코 그것을 끝까지 해내지 못했을 것이다."], ["For a few minutes we were so taken up with him that we did not look towards the coffin.", "몇 분 동안 우리는 그에게 너무 정신이 팔려 관 쪽을 바라보지 못했다."], ["When we did, however, a murmur of startled surprise ran from one to the other of us.", "그러나 우리가 바라보았을 때, 놀란 경탄의 웅성거림이 우리 사이로 번져 갔다."], ["We gazed so eagerly that Arthur rose, for he had been seated on the ground, and came and looked too; and then a glad, strange light broke over his face and dispelled altogether the gloom of horror that lay upon it.", "우리가 어찌나 간절히 들여다보았던지, 땅바닥에 앉아 있던 아서가 일어나 다가와 함께 들여다보았다. 그러자 기쁘고 기이한 빛이 그의 얼굴에 번지며, 그 위에 드리워 있던 공포의 음울함을 말끔히 걷어냈다."]], [["There, in the coffin lay no longer the foul Thing that we had so dreaded and grown to hate that the work of her destruction was yielded as a privilege to the one best entitled to it, but Lucy as we had seen her in her life, with her face of unequalled sweetness and purity.", "거기, 관 속에는, 우리가 그토록 두려워하고, 또 그것을 멸하는 일이 그럴 자격이 가장 큰 이에게 특권으로 주어질 만큼 미워하게 된 그 더러운 것이 더는 누워 있지 않았다. 다만 살아 있을 때 우리가 보았던 그대로의 루시가, 견줄 데 없이 다정하고 순결한 얼굴로 누워 있었다."], ["True that there were there, as we had seen them in life, the traces of care and pain and waste; but these were all dear to us, for they marked her truth to what we knew.", "살아 있을 때 우리가 보았던 그대로, 근심과 고통과 쇠약의 흔적이 거기 남아 있는 것은 사실이었다. 그러나 그것들은 모두 우리에게 정겨웠으니, 우리가 알던 그녀의 참모습을 그대로 새겨두고 있었기 때문이다."], ["One and all we felt that the holy calm that lay like sunshine over the wasted face and form was only an earthly token and symbol of the calm that was to reign for ever.", "우리는 너 나 할 것 없이, 쇠약해진 얼굴과 몸 위에 햇살처럼 깃든 그 거룩한 평온이, 영원히 다스릴 그 평온의 지상의 징표이자 표상일 뿐임을 느꼈다."]], [["Van Helsing came and laid his hand on Arthur’s shoulder, and said to him:--", "판 헬싱이 다가와 아서의 어깨에 손을 얹고 말했다."]], [["“And now, Arthur my friend, dear lad, am I not forgiven?”", "\"그러면 이제, 내 벗 아서, 사랑하는 젊은이여, 나는 용서받지 못한 것이오?\""]], [["The reaction of the terrible strain came as he took the old man’s hand in his, and raising it to his lips, pressed it, and said:--", "그가 노인의 손을 제 손에 잡아 입술로 가져가 누르며 대답할 때, 무서운 긴장에 대한 반작용이 밀려왔다."]], [["“Forgiven!", "\"용서라니요!"], ["God bless you that you have given my dear one her soul again, and me peace.”", "내 사랑하는 이에게 그 영혼을 다시 돌려주고, 나에게 평안을 주신 당신께 하느님의 축복이 있기를.\""], ["He put his hands on the Professor’s shoulder, and laying his head on his breast, cried for a while silently, whilst we stood unmoving.", "그는 교수의 어깨에 두 손을 얹고 그 가슴에 머리를 묻은 채 한동안 소리 없이 울었으며, 우리는 미동도 없이 서 있었다."], ["When he raised his head Van Helsing said to him:--", "그가 고개를 들자 판 헬싱이 그에게 말했다."]], [["“And now, my child, you may kiss her.", "\"그러면 이제, 내 아이여, 그대는 그녀에게 입맞춰도 좋소."], ["Kiss her dead lips if you will, as she would have you to, if for her to choose.", "원한다면 그녀의 죽은 입술에 입맞추시오. 그녀가 고를 수 있었다면 그녀가 바랐을 그대로."], ["For she is not a grinning devil now--not any more a foul Thing for all eternity.", "이제 그녀는 이빨을 드러내고 히죽거리는 악마가 아니오 — 더는 영원토록 더러운 것이 아니오."], ["No longer she is the devil’s Un-Dead.", "그녀는 더는 악마의 불사귀가 아니오."], ["She is God’s true dead, whose soul is with Him!”", "그녀는 하느님의 참된 죽은 이이며, 그 영혼은 그분과 함께 있소!\""]], [["Arthur bent and kissed her, and then we sent him and Quincey out of the tomb; the Professor and I sawed the top off the stake, leaving the point of it in the body.", "아서가 몸을 숙여 그녀에게 입맞추었고, 그런 다음 우리는 그와 퀸시를 무덤 밖으로 내보냈다. 교수와 나는 말뚝의 윗부분을 톱으로 잘라내고, 그 끝은 몸속에 그대로 두었다."], ["Then we cut off the head and filled the mouth with garlic.", "그러고는 머리를 잘라내고 입에 마늘을 채웠다."], ["We soldered up the leaden coffin, screwed on the coffin-lid, and gathering up our belongings, came away.", "우리는 납으로 된 관을 땜질하고, 관 뚜껑을 나사로 조였으며, 우리 물건들을 챙겨 그곳을 떠났다."], ["When the Professor locked the door he gave the key to Arthur.", "교수가 문을 잠근 뒤 그 열쇠를 아서에게 주었다."]], [["Outside the air was sweet, the sun shone, and the birds sang, and it seemed as if all nature were tuned to a different pitch.", "밖은 공기가 감미로웠고, 해가 빛났으며, 새들이 노래했고, 마치 온 자연이 다른 가락에 맞춰진 듯했다."], ["There was gladness and mirth and peace everywhere, for we were at rest ourselves on one account, and we were glad, though it was with a tempered joy.", "어디에나 기쁨과 즐거움과 평화가 있었으니, 한 가지 일에서 우리 자신이 안식을 얻었으며, 비록 누그러진 기쁨이긴 했어도 우리는 기뻤기 때문이다."]], [["Before we moved away Van Helsing said:--", "우리가 자리를 뜨기 전에 판 헬싱이 말했다."]], [["“Now, my friends, one step of our work is done, one the most harrowing to ourselves.", "\"자 이제, 벗들이여, 우리 일의 한 단계가, 우리 자신에게 가장 가슴 아픈 한 단계가 끝났소."], ["But there remains a greater task: to find out the author of all this our sorrow and to stamp him out.", "그러나 더 큰 일이 남아 있소. 이 모든 우리 슬픔의 장본인을 찾아내어 그를 근절하는 일이오."], ["I have clues which we can follow; but it is a long task, and a difficult, and there is danger in it, and pain.", "나에게는 우리가 좇을 수 있는 단서가 있소. 그러나 그것은 길고도 어려운 일이며, 거기에는 위험과 고통이 따르오."], ["Shall you not all help me?", "그대들 모두 나를 돕지 않겠소?"], ["We have learned to believe, all of us--is it not so?", "우리는 모두 믿게 되었소 — 그렇지 않소?"], ["And since so, do we not see our duty? Yes!", "그렇다면 우리는 우리의 의무를 보지 않겠소? 그렇소!"], ["And do we not promise to go on to the bitter end?”", "그리고 우리는 쓰라린 끝까지 나아가겠다고 약속하지 않겠소?\""]], [["Each in turn, we took his hand, and the promise was made.", "우리는 차례로 그의 손을 잡았고, 그 약속이 이루어졌다."], ["Then said the Professor as we moved off:--", "그런 다음 우리가 자리를 뜰 때 교수가 말했다."]], [["“Two nights hence you shall meet with me and dine together at seven of the clock with friend John.", "\"이틀 뒤 밤에 그대들은 일곱 시에 나와 만나 벗 존과 함께 저녁을 들도록 하시오."], ["I shall entreat two others, two that you know not as yet; and I shall be ready to all our work show and our plans unfold.", "나는 다른 두 사람을, 그대들이 아직 알지 못하는 두 사람을 간청하여 부르겠소. 그리고 우리의 모든 일을 보여주고 우리의 계획을 펼쳐 보일 채비를 하겠소."], ["Friend John, you come with me home, for I have much to consult about, and you can help me.", "벗 존, 자네는 나와 함께 집으로 가세. 의논할 것이 많고, 자네가 나를 도와줄 수 있으니."], ["To-night I leave for Amsterdam, but shall return to-morrow night.", "오늘 밤 나는 암스테르담으로 떠나지만 내일 밤에는 돌아오겠네."], ["And then begins our great quest.", "그러면 우리의 위대한 추적이 시작되오."], ["But first I shall have much to say, so that you may know what is to do and to dread.", "그러나 먼저 나는 할 말이 많으니, 그래야 그대들이 무엇을 해야 하고 무엇을 두려워해야 할지 알게 될 것이오."], ["Then our promise shall be made to each other anew; for there is a terrible task before us, and once our feet are on the ploughshare we must not draw back.”", "그런 다음 우리의 약속을 서로에게 새로이 다지도록 합시다. 무서운 일이 우리 앞에 놓여 있고, 일단 우리 발을 쟁기 날에 디딘 이상 우리는 물러서서는 안 되기 때문이오.\""]], [["When we arrived at the Berkeley Hotel, Van Helsing found a telegram waiting for him:--", "버클리 호텔에 도착하자, 반 헬싱은 자신을 기다리고 있던 전보 한 통을 발견했다."]], [["“Am coming up by train.", "\"기차로 올라가는 중."], ["Jonathan at Whitby.", "조너선은 휘트비에 있음."], ["Important news.--MINA HARKER.”", "중대한 소식 있음. — 미나 하커.\""]], [["The Professor was delighted.", "교수는 무척 기뻐했다."], ["“Ah, that wonderful Madam Mina,”", "\"아, 그 놀라운 미나 부인,\" 그가 말했다."], ["he said, “pearl among women!", "\"여인들 가운데 진주 같은 분이지!"], ["She arrive, but I cannot stay.", "그분이 오시는데 나는 머물 수가 없네."], ["She must go to your house, friend John.", "그분은 자네 집으로 가셔야 하네, 존."], ["You must meet her at the station.", "자네가 역에 마중을 나가야 하네."], ["Telegraph her en route, so that she may be prepared.”", "가는 길에 전보를 쳐서 그분이 채비를 할 수 있게 하게.\""]], [["When the wire was despatched he had a cup of tea; over it he told me of a diary kept by Jonathan Harker when abroad, and gave me a typewritten copy of it, as also of Mrs.", "전보를 보낸 뒤 그는 차를 한 잔 마셨고, 그러면서 조너선 하커가 외국에 있을 때 쓴 일기에 관해 이야기해 주며, 그것을 타자로 친 사본과 더불어 휘트비에서 쓴 하커 부인의 일기 사본도 나에게 건넸다."], ["Harker’s diary at Whitby.", "\"이것들을 받아두게,\""], ["“Take these,”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, “and study them well.", "\"그리고 잘 연구해 두게."], ["When I have returned you will be master of all the facts, and we can then better enter on our inquisition.", "내가 돌아올 무렵이면 자네는 모든 사실을 손에 쥐고 있을 것이고, 그러면 우리는 더 잘 추적에 나설 수 있을 걸세."], ["Keep them safe, for there is in them much of treasure.", "안전하게 간수하게. 그 안에 보물이 많이 들어 있으니."], ["You will need all your faith, even you who have had such an experience as that of to-day.", "자네에게는 온 믿음이 다 필요할 걸세. 오늘 같은 경험을 한 자네라 할지라도 말이야."], ["What is here told,”", "여기 적힌 것은,\""], ["he laid his hand heavily and gravely on the packet of papers as he spoke, “may be the beginning of the end to you and me and many another; or it may sound the knell of the Un-Dead who walk the earth.", "그는 말하면서 서류 뭉치 위에 손을 무겁고도 엄숙하게 얹었다. \"자네와 나, 그리고 다른 많은 이들에게 종말의 시작이 될 수도 있네. 아니면 이 땅을 걸어다니는 언데드의 조종(弔鐘)을 울리게 될 수도 있고."], ["Read all, I pray you, with the open mind; and if you can add in any way to the story here told do so, for it is all-important.", "부디 열린 마음으로 모두 읽어주게. 그리고 여기 적힌 이야기에 무엇이든 보탤 수 있거든 그리해주게. 그것이 무엇보다 중요하니."], ["You have kept diary of all these so strange things; is it not so? Yes!", "자네도 이 모든 기이한 일들의 일기를 써왔지, 그렇지 않은가? 그렇지!"], ["Then we shall go through all these together when we meet.”", "그러면 우리가 만날 때 이 모두를 함께 살펴보세.\""], ["He then made ready for his departure, and shortly after drove off to Liverpool Street.", "그러고 나서 그는 떠날 채비를 했고, 곧 마차를 타고 리버풀 스트리트로 떠났다."], ["I took my way to Paddington, where I arrived about fifteen minutes before the train came in.", "나는 패딩턴으로 향했고, 기차가 들어오기 약 십오 분 전쯤 그곳에 도착했다."]], [["The crowd melted away, after the bustling fashion common to arrival platforms; and I was beginning to feel uneasy, lest I might miss my guest, when a sweet-faced, dainty-looking girl stepped up to me, and, after a quick glance, said: “Dr.", "도착하는 승강장에 흔한 그 부산스러운 식으로 군중이 흩어져 갔고, 나는 손님을 놓치지나 않을까 불안해지기 시작했다. 그때 상냥한 얼굴에 단아해 보이는 여인이 내게 다가와, 한 번 재빨리 훑어보고는 말했다."], ["Seward, is it not?”", "\"수어드 박사님이시죠?\""]], [["“And you are Mrs. Harker!”", "\"그리고 당신은 하커 부인이시군요!\""], ["I answered at once; whereupon she held out her hand.", "나는 즉시 답했고, 그러자 그녀가 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“I knew you from the description of poor dear Lucy; but----”", "\"가엾고 사랑하던 루시가 묘사한 것으로 박사님인 줄 알아봤어요. 하지만——\""], ["She stopped suddenly, and a quick blush overspread her face.", "그녀는 문득 말을 멈췄고, 갑작스러운 홍조가 그녀의 얼굴에 번졌다."]], [["The blush that rose to my own cheeks somehow set us both at ease, for it was a tacit answer to her own.", "내 뺨에 떠오른 홍조가 어쩐 일인지 우리 둘 모두의 마음을 편하게 해주었으니, 그것이 그녀 자신의 홍조에 대한 무언의 대답이 되었던 것이다."], ["I got her luggage, which included a typewriter, and we took the Underground to Fenchurch Street, after I had sent a wire to my housekeeper to have a sitting-room and bedroom prepared at once for Mrs. Harker.", "나는 그녀의 짐을 챙겼는데, 그 가운데에는 타자기도 있었다. 그리고 가정부에게 전보를 쳐서 하커 부인을 위해 거실과 침실을 즉시 준비하도록 일러둔 뒤, 우리는 지하철을 타고 펜처치 스트리트로 향했다."]], [["In due time we arrived.", "이윽고 우리는 도착했다."], ["She knew, of course, that the place was a lunatic asylum, but I could see that she was unable to repress a shudder when we entered.", "물론 그녀는 그곳이 정신병원임을 알고 있었으나, 우리가 들어설 때 그녀가 몸서리치는 것을 억누르지 못하는 것을 나는 볼 수 있었다."]], [["She told me that, if she might, she would come presently to my study, as she had much to say.", "그녀는 괜찮다면 곧 내 서재로 오겠다고 말했는데, 할 말이 많기 때문이라고 했다."], ["So here I am finishing my entry in my phonograph diary whilst I await her.", "그래서 나는 그녀를 기다리며 이렇게 축음기 일기의 기록을 마무리하고 있다."], ["As yet I have not had the chance of looking at the papers which Van Helsing left with me, though they lie open before me.", "아직 반 헬싱이 내게 남긴 서류들을 들여다볼 기회는 없었으나, 그것들은 내 앞에 펼쳐져 있다."], ["I must get her interested in something, so that I may have an opportunity of reading them.", "나는 그녀가 무언가에 관심을 갖도록 해서, 그것들을 읽을 기회를 얻어야 한다."], ["She does not know how precious time is, or what a task we have in hand.", "그녀는 시간이 얼마나 소중한지, 우리가 손에 든 일이 어떤 것인지 모른다."], ["I must be careful not to frighten her.", "그녀를 놀라게 하지 않도록 조심해야 한다."], ["Here she is!", "그녀가 왔다!"]], [["Mina Harker’s Journal.", "「미나 하커의 일기」"]], [["29 September.--After I had tidied myself, I went down to Dr. Seward’s study.", "9월 29일. — 몸단장을 한 뒤 나는 수어드 박사의 서재로 내려갔다."], ["At the door I paused a moment, for I thought I heard him talking with some one.", "문 앞에서 나는 잠시 멈춰 섰으니, 그가 누군가와 이야기하는 소리를 들은 것 같았기 때문이다."], ["As, however, he had pressed me to be quick, I knocked at the door, and on his calling out, “Come in,” I entered.", "그러나 그가 서두르라고 당부했으므로 나는 문을 두드렸고, 그가 \"들어오세요\" 하고 외치자 안으로 들어갔다."]], [["To my intense surprise, there was no one with him.", "몹시 놀랍게도 그와 함께 있는 사람은 아무도 없었다."], ["He was quite alone, and on the table opposite him was what I knew at once from the description to be a phonograph.", "그는 완전히 혼자였고, 그의 맞은편 탁자 위에는 묘사로 미루어 단번에 축음기임을 알 수 있는 물건이 놓여 있었다."], ["I had never seen one, and was much interested.", "나는 그것을 한 번도 본 적이 없어서 무척 흥미로웠다."]], [["“I hope I did not keep you waiting,”", "\"오래 기다리시게 한 건 아니었으면 좋겠는데요,\""], ["I said; “but I stayed at the door as I heard you talking, and thought there was some one with you.”", "내가 말했다. \"다만 박사님이 이야기하시는 소리가 들려서 문 앞에 서 있었어요. 누군가와 함께 계신 줄 알았거든요.\""]], [["“Oh,”", "\"아,\""], ["he replied with a smile, “I was only entering my diary.”", "그가 미소를 지으며 답했다. \"그저 일기를 기록하고 있었을 뿐입니다.\""]], [["“Your diary?”", "\"일기를요?\""], ["I asked him in surprise.", "나는 놀라서 그에게 물었다."]], [["“Yes,”", "\"네,\""], ["he answered.", "그가 답했다."], ["“I keep it in this.” As he spoke he laid his hand on the phonograph.", "\"저는 이 안에 일기를 적습니다.\" 그는 말하면서 축음기에 손을 얹었다."], ["I felt quite excited over it, and blurted out:--", "나는 그것에 무척 흥분되어 불쑥 내뱉었다."]], [["“Why, this beats even shorthand!", "\"어머, 이건 속기보다도 낫네요!"], ["May I hear it say something?”", "무슨 말이든 들려주실 수 있나요?\""]], [["“Certainly,”", "\"물론이죠,\""], ["he replied with alacrity, and stood up to put it in train for speaking. Then he paused, and a troubled look overspread his face.", "그는 선뜻 답하더니, 말하게 작동시키려고 일어섰다. 그러다가 그는 멈칫했고, 곤혹스러운 표정이 그의 얼굴에 번졌다."]], [["“The fact is,”", "\"실은,\""], ["he began awkwardly, “I only keep my diary in it; and as it is entirely--almost entirely--about my cases, it may be awkward--that is, I mean----”", "그가 어색하게 말을 꺼냈다. \"저는 여기에 제 일기만 적는데, 그것이 거의 전부—— 거의 전부 제 환자들에 관한 것이라서, 좀 곤란할 수도—— 그러니까, 제 말은——\""], ["He stopped, and I tried to help him out of his embarrassment:--", "그는 말을 멈췄고, 나는 그가 당혹감에서 벗어나도록 도우려 했다."]], [["“You helped to attend dear Lucy at the end.", "\"박사님은 마지막에 사랑하던 루시를 돌보셨잖아요."], ["Let me hear how she died; for all that I know of her, I shall be very grateful.", "그녀가 어떻게 죽었는지 들려주세요. 그녀에 관한 것이라면 무엇이든 무척 고맙게 받아들이겠어요."], ["She was very, very dear to me.”", "그녀는 제게 무척, 무척 소중한 사람이었으니까요.\""]], [["To my surprise, he answered, with a horrorstruck look in his face:--", "놀랍게도 그는 얼굴에 공포에 질린 표정을 띤 채 답했다."]], [["“Tell you of her death? Not for the wide world!”", "\"그녀의 죽음에 관해 말씀드리라고요? 천만의 말씀입니다!\""]], [["“Why not?”", "\"왜 안 되죠?\""], ["I asked, for some grave, terrible feeling was coming over me.", "내가 물었으니, 어떤 무겁고 끔찍한 느낌이 나를 엄습해 오고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Again he paused, and I could see that he was trying to invent an excuse.", "그는 다시 멈칫했고, 나는 그가 핑계를 지어내려 애쓰고 있는 것을 알 수 있었다."], ["At length he stammered out:--", "마침내 그가 더듬거리며 말했다."]], [["“You see, I do not know how to pick out any particular part of the diary.”", "\"그러니까, 저는 일기의 어느 특정한 부분을 어떻게 골라내야 할지를 모릅니다.\""], ["Even while he was speaking an idea dawned upon him, and he said with unconscious simplicity, in a different voice, and with the naïveté of a child: “That’s quite true, upon my honour.", "그가 말하는 동안에도 한 가지 생각이 그에게 떠올랐는지, 그는 무심결의 단순함으로, 다른 목소리로, 아이 같은 천진함으로 말했다. \"그건 정말 사실입니다, 명예를 걸고. 정말이에요!\""], ["Honest Indian!” I could not but smile, at which he grimaced.", "나는 미소 짓지 않을 수 없었고, 그러자 그는 얼굴을 찡그렸다."], ["“I gave myself away that time!”", "\"그때 제 속을 다 드러내 버렸네요!\""], ["he said. “But do you know that, although I have kept the diary for months past, it never once struck me how I was going to find any particular part of it in case I wanted to look it up?”", "그가 말했다. \"그런데 말이죠, 저는 지난 몇 달 동안 일기를 적어 왔으면서도, 나중에 찾아보고 싶을 때 어느 특정한 부분을 어떻게 찾아낼지는 단 한 번도 떠올린 적이 없었답니다.\""], ["By this time my mind was made up that the diary of a doctor who attended Lucy might have something to add to the sum of our knowledge of that terrible Being, and I said boldly:--", "이쯤에서 내 마음은 정해졌으니, 루시를 돌본 의사의 일기라면 그 끔찍한 존재에 관한 우리의 지식의 총합에 무언가를 보탤 만한 것이 있을지 모른다고 여겼던 것이다. 그래서 나는 대담하게 말했다."]], [["“Then, Dr. Seward, you had better let me copy it out for you on my typewriter.”", "\"그러면, 수어드 박사님, 그 일기를 제 타자기로 옮겨 적게 해주시는 게 좋겠어요.\""], ["He grew to a positively deathly pallor as he said:--", "그는 말하면서 그야말로 죽은 사람처럼 새파랗게 질렸다."]], [["“No! no! no!", "\"안 됩니다! 안 돼요!"], ["For all the world, I wouldn’t let you know that terrible story!”", "절대 안 돼요! 무슨 일이 있어도 그 끔찍한 이야기를 당신께 알려드릴 수는 없습니다!\""]], [["Then it was terrible; my intuition was right!", "그렇다면 그것은 끔찍한 일이었던 것이다."], ["For a moment I thought, and as my eyes ranged the room, unconsciously looking for something or some opportunity to aid me, they lit on a great batch of typewriting on the table.", "내 직감이 옳았다! 잠시 나는 생각에 잠겼고, 무언가를, 혹은 나를 도와줄 어떤 기회를 무심결에 찾으며 방 안을 둘러보던 내 눈이 탁자 위에 놓인 한 무더기의 타자 친 서류에 가 닿았다."], ["His eyes caught the look in mine, and, without his thinking, followed their direction.", "그의 눈이 내 눈의 그 시선을 알아채고는, 자신도 모르게 그 방향을 따라갔다."], ["As they saw the parcel he realised my meaning.", "그 서류 뭉치가 눈에 들어오자 그는 내 뜻을 깨달았다."]], [["“You do not know me,”", "\"박사님은 저를 모르세요,\""], ["I said. “When you have read those papers--my own diary and my husband’s also, which I have typed--you will know me better.", "내가 말했다. \"저 서류들—— 제 일기와, 제가 타자로 친 제 남편의 일기까지 읽으시고 나면, 저를 더 잘 알게 되실 거예요."], ["I have not faltered in giving every thought of my own heart in this cause; but, of course, you do not know me--yet; and I must not expect you to trust me so far.”", "저는 이 대의를 위해 제 마음속 온갖 생각을 바치는 데 한 번도 주저한 적이 없어요. 하지만 물론, 박사님은 아직 저를 모르시지요. 그러니 박사님이 저를 그렇게까지 믿어주시리라 기대해서는 안 되겠지요.\""]], [["He is certainly a man of noble nature; poor dear Lucy was right about him.", "그는 분명 고귀한 천성을 지닌 사람이다. 가엾고 사랑하던 루시가 그에 관해 옳게 보았던 것이다."], ["He stood up and opened a large drawer, in which were arranged in order a number of hollow cylinders of metal covered with dark wax, and said:--", "그는 일어서서 커다란 서랍 하나를 열었는데, 그 안에는 검은 밀랍을 입힌 속이 빈 금속 원통들이 여러 개 가지런히 정리되어 있었다. 그가 말했다."]], [["“You are quite right. I did not trust you because I did not know you.", "\"부인 말씀이 전적으로 옳습니다. 저는 부인을 몰랐기 때문에 믿지 못했던 겁니다."], ["But I know you now; and let me say that I should have known you long ago.", "그러나 이제는 부인을 압니다. 그리고 한마디 드리자면, 저는 진작에 부인을 알아봤어야 했어요."], ["I know that Lucy told you of me; she told me of you too.", "루시가 부인에게 제 이야기를 했다는 걸 압니다."], ["May I make the only atonement in my power?", "그녀는 제게도 부인 이야기를 했지요."], ["Take the cylinders and hear them--the first half-dozen of them are personal to me, and they will not horrify you; then you will know me better.", "제가 할 수 있는 단 하나의 속죄를 하게 해주시겠습니까? 이 원통들을 가져가 들어보세요. 처음 여섯 개쯤은 제 개인적인 것이라 부인을 소름끼치게 하지는 않을 겁니다."], ["Dinner will by then be ready.", "그러면 부인은 저를 더 잘 알게 되시겠지요."], ["In the meantime I shall read over some of these documents, and shall be better able to understand certain things.”", "그때쯤이면 저녁식사가 준비될 겁니다. 그동안 저는 이 서류들 가운데 일부를 읽으며, 어떤 것들을 더 잘 이해할 수 있게 될 겁니다.\""], ["He carried the phonograph himself up to my sitting-room and adjusted it for me.", "그는 손수 축음기를 내 거실로 가져와 나를 위해 맞춰 주었다."], ["Now I shall learn something pleasant, I am sure; for it will tell me the other side of a true love episode of which I know one side already....", "이제 나는 무언가 즐거운 것을 알게 되리라, 그렇게 확신한다. 이미 한쪽 면은 알고 있는 진실한 사랑 이야기의 다른 한쪽 면을 그것이 내게 들려줄 테니……."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["29 September.--I was so absorbed in that wonderful diary of Jonathan Harker and that other of his wife that I let the time run on without thinking. Mrs.", "9월 29일. — 나는 조너선 하커의 그 놀라운 일기와 그의 아내의 또 다른 일기에 어찌나 빠져들었던지, 생각도 없이 시간을 흘려보냈다."], ["Harker was not down when the maid came to announce dinner, so I said: “She is possibly tired; let dinner wait an hour,” and I went on with my work.", "하녀가 저녁식사를 알리러 왔을 때 하커 부인은 아직 내려오지 않았으므로, 나는 \"아마 피곤하신 모양이니, 저녁식사를 한 시간 미루도록 하지\"라고 말하고는 하던 일을 계속했다."], ["I had just finished Mrs. Harker’s diary, when she came in.", "하커 부인의 일기를 막 다 읽었을 때 그녀가 들어왔다."], ["She looked sweetly pretty, but very sad, and her eyes were flushed with crying.", "그녀는 상냥하리만치 예뻐 보였으나 무척 슬퍼 보였고, 두 눈은 울어서 붉어져 있었다."], ["This somehow moved me much.", "이것이 어쩐 일인지 나를 몹시 움직였다."], ["Of late I have had cause for tears, God knows!", "요즈음 나에게는 눈물을 흘릴 만한 일이 있었으니, 하느님은 아신다!"], ["but the relief of them was denied me; and now the sight of those sweet eyes, brightened with recent tears, went straight to my heart.", "그러나 나는 그 눈물의 위안을 누릴 수 없었다. 그런데 이제 방금 흘린 눈물로 빛나는 그 상냥한 두 눈을 보니, 그것이 곧장 내 가슴을 파고들었다."], ["So I said as gently as I could:--", "그래서 나는 되도록 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“I greatly fear I have distressed you.”", "\"제가 부인을 무척 괴롭게 해드린 건 아닐까 몹시 걱정됩니다.\""]], [["“Oh, no, not distressed me,”", "\"오, 아니에요, 저를 괴롭게 하신 게 아니에요,\""], ["she replied, “but I have been more touched than I can say by your grief.", "그녀가 답했다. \"하지만 박사님의 슬픔에 말로 다 못할 만큼 마음이 흔들렸어요."], ["That is a wonderful machine, but it is cruelly true.", "그건 놀라운 기계지만, 잔인하리만치 진실해요."], ["It told me, in its very tones, the anguish of your heart.", "바로 그 목소리로, 그것이 박사님 가슴의 고뇌를 제게 들려주었어요."], ["It was like a soul crying out to Almighty God.", "마치 어떤 영혼이 전능하신 하느님께 부르짖는 것 같았어요."], ["No one must hear them spoken ever again!", "그 말소리를 다시는 누구도 들어서는 안 돼요!"], ["See, I have tried to be useful.", "보세요, 저는 쓸모 있게 되려고 애썼어요."], ["I have copied out the words on my typewriter, and none other need now hear your heart beat, as I did.”", "제 타자기로 그 말들을 옮겨 적었으니, 이제 제가 들었던 것처럼 다른 누구도 박사님의 가슴이 뛰는 소리를 들을 필요는 없어요.\""]], [["“No one need ever know, shall ever know,”", "\"누구도 알 필요가 없고, 누구도 알게 되지 않을 겁니다,\""], ["I said in a low voice.", "나는 낮은 목소리로 말했다."], ["She laid her hand on mine and said very gravely:--\n“Ah, but they must!”", "그녀는 내 손 위에 자신의 손을 얹고 무척 엄숙하게 말했다. \"아, 하지만 그들은 반드시 알아야 해요!\""]], [["“Must! But why?”", "\"알아야만 한다고요! 하지만 왜죠?\""], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Because it is a part of the terrible story, a part of poor dear Lucy’s death and all that led to it; because in the struggle which we have before us to rid the earth of this terrible monster we must have all the knowledge and all the help which we can get.", "\"알아야 한다고요! 하지만 왜죠?\" 내가 물었다."], ["I think that the cylinders which you gave me contained more than you intended me to know; but I can see that there are in your record many lights to this dark mystery. You will let me help, will you not?", "\"그건 그 끔찍한 이야기의 일부, 가엾고 사랑하던 루시의 죽음과 그것을 불러온 모든 일의 일부니까요. 우리 앞에 놓인, 이 땅에서 그 끔찍한 괴물을 없애야 하는 싸움에서 우리는 얻을 수 있는 모든 지식과 모든 도움을 가져야 하니까요."], ["I know all up to a certain point; and I see already, though your diary only took me to 7 September, how poor Lucy was beset, and how her terrible doom was being wrought out.", "박사님이 제게 주신 그 원통들에는 박사님이 제게 알리려고 하신 것 이상의 것이 담겨 있었던 것 같아요. 하지만 박사님의 기록 속에는 이 어두운 수수께끼를 비추는 빛이 많이 있다는 걸 알 수 있어요. 제가 돕도록 허락해 주시겠지요?"], ["Jonathan and I have been working day and night since Professor Van Helsing saw us. He is gone to Whitby to get more information, and he will be here to-morrow to help us.", "저는 어느 지점까지는 모두 알고 있어요. 그리고 박사님의 일기는 9월 7일까지밖에 데려다주지 않았지만, 가엾은 루시가 어떻게 시달렸는지, 그 끔찍한 운명이 어떻게 빚어지고 있었는지 저는 이미 알아요."], ["We need have no secrets amongst us; working together and with absolute trust, we can surely be stronger than if some of us were in the dark.”", "조너선과 저는 반 헬싱 교수님이 저희를 만나신 이래로 밤낮없이 일해 왔어요. 교수님은 더 많은 정보를 얻으러 휘트비로 가셨고, 내일 이곳으로 오셔서 저희를 도우실 거예요. 우리끼리는 비밀을 둘 필요가 없어요."], ["She looked at me so appealingly, and at the same time manifested such courage and resolution in her bearing, that I gave in at once to her wishes.", "함께, 그리고 온전한 신뢰로 일한다면, 우리 가운데 누군가가 어둠 속에 남아 있는 것보다 분명 더 강해질 수 있어요.\" 그녀가 어찌나 호소하듯 나를 바라보면서, 동시에 그 태도에서 어찌나 큰 용기와 결의를 드러내던지, 나는 즉시 그녀의 뜻에 굴복하고 말았다. \"그러십시오,\""], ["“You shall,”", "내가 말했다."], ["I said, “do as you like in the matter. God forgive me if I do wrong!", "\"이 일에서 부인 뜻대로 하십시오. 제가 잘못하는 것이라면 하느님께서 용서해 주시기를!"], ["There are terrible things yet to learn of; but if you have so far travelled on the road to poor Lucy’s death, you will not be content, I know, to remain in the dark.", "아직 알아야 할 끔찍한 것들이 있습니다. 그러나 가엾은 루시의 죽음에 이르는 길을 그렇게까지 걸어오셨다면, 부인은 어둠 속에 머무는 것에 만족하지 못하시리라는 걸 압니다."], ["Nay, the end--the very end--may give you a gleam of peace.", "아니, 그 끝이—— 바로 그 끝이—— 부인께 한 줄기 평화의 빛을 줄지도 모르지요."], ["Come, there is dinner.", "자, 저녁식사가 준비됐습니다."], ["We must keep one another strong for what is before us; we have a cruel and dreadful task.", "우리 앞에 놓인 일을 위해 서로를 강하게 지켜줘야 합니다. 우리에게는 잔인하고 무서운 임무가 있으니까요."], ["When you have eaten you shall learn the rest, and I shall answer any questions you ask--if there be anything which you do not understand, though it was apparent to us who were present.”", "식사를 마치시면 나머지를 알게 되실 거고, 저는 부인이 묻는 어떤 물음에도 답하겠습니다—— 비록 그 자리에 있던 우리에게는 분명했던 것이라도, 부인이 이해하지 못하는 것이 있다면 말입니다.\""]], [["Mina Harker’s Journal.", "「미나 하커의 일기」"]], [["29 September.--After dinner I came with Dr. Seward to his study.", "9월 29일. — 저녁식사를 마친 뒤 나는 수어드 박사와 함께 그의 서재로 왔다."], ["He brought back the phonograph from my room, and I took my typewriter.", "그는 내 방에서 축음기를 다시 가져왔고, 나는 내 타자기를 들었다."], ["He placed me in a comfortable chair, and arranged the phonograph so that I could touch it without getting up, and showed me how to stop it in case I should want to pause.", "그는 나를 편안한 의자에 앉히고는, 일어서지 않고도 손이 닿을 수 있게 축음기를 맞춰 주었으며, 잠시 멈추고 싶을 때 그것을 멈추는 법을 보여주었다."], ["Then he very thoughtfully took a chair, with his back to me, so that I might be as free as possible, and began to read.", "그러고 나서 그는 무척 사려 깊게 의자를 가져와 내게 등을 돌리고 앉았으니, 내가 되도록 편하게 있을 수 있게 하기 위해서였다. 그러고는 읽기 시작했다."], ["I put the forked metal to my ears and listened.", "나는 두 갈래로 갈라진 금속을 두 귀에 대고 귀를 기울였다."]], [["When the terrible story of Lucy’s death, and--and all that followed, was done, I lay back in my chair powerless.", "루시의 죽음에 관한 그 끔찍한 이야기, 그리고—— 그리고 그 뒤에 이어진 모든 일이 끝났을 때, 나는 의자에 맥없이 기대 누웠다."], ["Fortunately I am not of a fainting disposition.", "다행히 나는 잘 기절하는 성미는 아니다."], ["When Dr. Seward saw me he jumped up with a horrified exclamation, and hurriedly taking a case-bottle from a cupboard, gave me some brandy, which in a few minutes somewhat restored me.", "수어드 박사는 나를 보더니 공포에 질린 외마디 소리를 지르며 벌떡 일어나, 급히 찬장에서 휴대용 병 하나를 꺼내 내게 브랜디를 조금 주었고, 그것이 몇 분 만에 나를 어느 정도 회복시켜 주었다."], ["My brain was all in a whirl, and only that there came through all the multitude of horrors, the holy ray of light that my dear, dear Lucy was at last at peace, I do not think I could have borne it without making a scene.", "머릿속이 온통 어지러웠고, 그 숱한 공포를 뚫고 사랑하고 또 사랑하던 루시가 마침내 평화를 찾았다는 거룩한 한 줄기 빛이 비쳐 오지 않았더라면, 나는 소란을 피우지 않고서는 그것을 견뎌낼 수 없었으리라 싶다."], ["It is all so wild, and mysterious, and strange that if I had not known Jonathan’s experience in Transylvania I could not have believed.", "모든 것이 어찌나 거칠고, 수수께끼 같고, 기이한지, 만일 내가 트란실바니아에서의 조너선의 경험을 몰랐더라면 나는 믿을 수 없었을 것이다."], ["As it was, I didn’t know what to believe, and so got out of my difficulty by attending to something else.", "하지만 그것을 알고 있었기에, 나는 무엇을 믿어야 할지 알 수 없었고, 그래서 다른 무언가에 몰두함으로써 그 곤경에서 벗어났다."], ["I took the cover off my typewriter, and said to Dr. Seward:--", "나는 타자기의 덮개를 벗기고 수어드 박사에게 말했다."]], [["“Let me write this all out now.", "\"이제 이걸 모두 옮겨 적게 해주세요."], ["We must be ready for Dr. Van Helsing when he comes.", "반 헬싱 박사님이 오실 때 우리는 준비가 되어 있어야 해요."], ["I have sent a telegram to Jonathan to come on here when he arrives in London from Whitby.", "저는 조너선에게 휘트비에서 런던에 도착하는 대로 이리로 오라고 전보를 쳤어요."], ["In this matter dates are everything, and I think that if we get all our material ready, and have every item put in chronological order, we shall have done much. You tell me that Lord Godalming and Mr.", "이 일에서는 날짜가 전부이고, 우리가 모든 자료를 갖춰 모든 항목을 시간 순서대로 정리해 둔다면 많은 일을 해낸 셈이 될 거라고 생각해요. 박사님은 고달밍 경과 모리스 씨도 오신다고 하셨죠."], ["Morris are coming too. Let us be able to tell him when they come.”", "그분들이 오실 때 그분께 알려드릴 수 있도록 해두어요.\""], ["He accordingly set the phonograph at a slow pace, and I began to typewrite from the beginning of the seventh cylinder.", "그래서 그는 축음기를 느린 속도로 맞춰 주었고, 나는 일곱 번째 원통의 처음부터 타자를 치기 시작했다."], ["I used manifold, and so took three copies of the diary, just as I had done with all the rest.", "나는 복사지를 써서, 나머지 모든 것과 마찬가지로 일기의 사본을 세 부 만들었다."], ["It was late when I got through, but Dr. Seward went about his work of going his round of the patients; when he had finished he came back and sat near me, reading, so that I did not feel too lonely whilst I worked.", "다 끝냈을 때는 시간이 늦었으나, 수어드 박사는 환자들을 돌아보는 일을 하러 다녔다. 그 일을 마치고 나서 그는 돌아와 내 곁에 앉아 책을 읽었으니, 내가 일하는 동안 너무 외롭지 않게 해주려는 것이었다."], ["How good and thoughtful he is; the world seems full of good men--even if there are monsters in it.", "그는 어찌나 선하고 사려 깊은지."], ["Before I left him I remembered what Jonathan put in his diary of the Professor’s perturbation at reading something in an evening paper at the station at Exeter; so, seeing that Dr. Seward keeps his newspapers, I borrowed the files of “The Westminster Gazette”", "세상은 선한 사람들로 가득한 것 같다—— 비록 그 안에 괴물들이 있다고 할지라도. 그를 떠나기 전에, 나는 엑서터 역에서 어느 석간신문에 실린 무언가를 읽고 교수가 동요했던 일을 조너선이 일기에 적어둔 것을 떠올렸다."], ["and “The Pall Mall Gazette,” and took them to my room. I remember how much “The Dailygraph”", "그래서 수어드 박사가 신문을 보관해 둔다는 것을 알고, 나는 「웨스트민스터 가제트」와 「폴 몰 가제트」의 철을 빌려 내 방으로 가져왔다."], ["and “The Whitby Gazette,” of which I had made cuttings, helped us to understand the terrible events at Whitby when Count Dracula landed, so I shall look through the evening papers since then, and perhaps I shall get some new light.", "내가 오려 두었던 「데일리그래프」와 「휘트비 가제트」가 드라큘라 백작이 상륙했을 때 휘트비에서 일어난 끔찍한 사건들을 이해하는 데 얼마나 큰 도움이 되었는지 기억하므로, 나는 그 이후의 석간신문들을 훑어볼 것이다. 그러면 아마 무언가 새로운 빛을 얻게 될지도 모른다."], ["I am not sleepy, and the work will help to keep me quiet.", "졸리지 않으니, 일이 나를 차분하게 해주는 데 도움이 될 것이다."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["30 September.--Mr.", "9월 30일."], ["Harker arrived at nine o’clock.", "— 하커 씨가 아홉 시에 도착했다."], ["He had got his wife’s wire just before starting.", "그는 출발하기 직전에 아내의 전보를 받았다고 한다."], ["He is uncommonly clever, if one can judge from his face, and full of energy.", "얼굴로 판단할 수 있다면, 그는 보기 드물게 영민하고 활력이 넘친다."], ["If this journal be true--and judging by one’s own wonderful experiences, it must be--he is also a man of great nerve.", "만일 이 일기가 사실이라면—— 그리고 나 자신의 놀라운 경험들로 미루어 보면 사실일 수밖에 없는데—— 그는 또한 대단한 담력을 지닌 사람이다."], ["That going down to the vault a second time was a remarkable piece of daring.", "그 지하 납골당으로 두 번째로 내려간 것은 놀랄 만한 대담함이었다."], ["After reading his account of it I was prepared to meet a good specimen of manhood, but hardly the quiet, business-like gentleman who came here to-day.", "그가 그 일을 기록한 것을 읽고 나서, 나는 사내다움의 훌륭한 본보기를 만나리라 예상했으나, 오늘 이곳에 온 조용하고 사무적인 신사를 만나리라고는 거의 생각지 못했다."]], [["Later.--After lunch Harker and his wife went back to their own room, and as I passed a while ago I heard the click of the typewriter.", "나중에. — 점심식사 후 하커와 그의 아내는 자기들 방으로 돌아갔고, 조금 전 내가 지나갈 때 타자기 두드리는 소리가 들렸다."], ["They are hard at it. Mrs.", "그들은 열심히 매달려 있다."], ["Harker says that they are knitting together in chronological order every scrap of evidence they have.", "하커 부인의 말로는, 그들은 가진 모든 증거 조각을 시간 순서대로 엮고 있다고 한다."], ["Harker has got the letters between the consignee of the boxes at Whitby and the carriers in London who took charge of them.", "하커는 휘트비에서 그 상자들을 받기로 한 수하인과 런던에서 그것들을 맡았던 운송업자들 사이에 오간 편지들을 손에 넣었다."], ["He is now reading his wife’s typescript of my diary.", "그는 지금 내 일기를 그의 아내가 타자로 친 사본을 읽고 있다."], ["I wonder what they make out of it. Here it is....", "그들이 그것에서 무엇을 알아낼지 궁금하다. 여기 그것이 있다……."]], [["Strange that it never struck me that the very next house might be the Count’s hiding-place!", "바로 옆집이 백작의 은신처일 수도 있다는 생각이 한 번도 떠오르지 않았다는 것이 이상하다!"], ["Goodness knows that we had enough clues from the conduct of the patient Renfield!", "환자 렌필드의 행동에서 단서가 충분히 있었음을 하느님은 아신다!"], ["The bundle of letters relating to the purchase of the house were with the typescript. Oh, if we had only had them earlier we might have saved poor Lucy!", "그 집 매입에 관한 편지 뭉치가 타자 사본과 함께 있었다. 아, 우리가 그것들을 좀 더 일찍 손에 넣기만 했더라도 가엾은 루시를 구할 수 있었을지 모른다! 그만두자."], ["Stop; that way madness lies! Harker has gone back, and is again collating his material.", "그 길로 가면 광기가 도사리고 있다! 하커가 돌아와 다시 자료를 맞춰 보고 있다."], ["He says that by dinner-time they will be able to show a whole connected narrative.", "그는 저녁식사 무렵이면 하나로 연결된 온전한 이야기를 보여줄 수 있을 거라고 한다."], ["He thinks that in the meantime I should see Renfield, as hitherto he has been a sort of index to the coming and going of the Count.", "그는 그동안 내가 렌필드를 만나봐야 한다고 생각하는데, 지금까지 그가 백작의 오고 감을 보여주는 일종의 색인 노릇을 해왔기 때문이다."], ["I hardly see this yet, but when I get at the dates I suppose I shall.", "나는 아직 이것을 잘 모르겠으나, 날짜를 손에 넣고 나면 알게 되리라 짐작한다."], ["What a good thing that Mrs. Harker put my cylinders into type!", "하커 부인이 내 원통들을 타자로 옮겨 둔 것이 얼마나 다행인지!"], ["We never could have found the dates otherwise....", "그렇지 않았더라면 우리는 결코 그 날짜들을 찾아낼 수 없었을 것이다……."]], [["I found Renfield sitting placidly in his room with his hands folded, smiling benignly.", "나는 렌필드가 두 손을 모은 채 인자하게 미소 지으며 자기 방에 평온히 앉아 있는 것을 보았다."], ["At the moment he seemed as sane as any one I ever saw.", "그 순간 그는 내가 본 어떤 사람 못지않게 제정신으로 보였다."], ["I sat down and talked with him on a lot of subjects, all of which he treated naturally.", "나는 앉아서 그와 여러 주제로 이야기를 나눴는데, 그는 그 모두를 자연스럽게 다루었다."], ["He then, of his own accord, spoke of going home, a subject he has never mentioned to my knowledge during his sojourn here.", "그러고 나서 그는 스스로, 집으로 돌아가는 이야기를 꺼냈는데, 내가 아는 한 이곳에 머무는 동안 한 번도 입에 올린 적 없는 주제였다."], ["In fact, he spoke quite confidently of getting his discharge at once.", "사실 그는 당장 퇴원하게 될 거라고 꽤 자신 있게 말했다."], ["I believe that, had I not had the chat with Harker and read the letters and the dates of his outbursts, I should have been prepared to sign for him after a brief time of observation.", "만일 내가 하커와 대화를 나누고 그 편지들과 그의 발작 날짜들을 읽지 않았더라면, 나는 잠깐 관찰한 뒤 그의 퇴원 서류에 서명할 채비가 되어 있었으리라 믿는다."], ["As it is, I am darkly suspicious. All those outbreaks were in some way linked with the proximity of the Count.", "하지만 지금으로서는 나는 어렴풋이 의심하고 있다. 그 모든 발작은 어떤 식으로든 백작의 근접과 연관되어 있었다."], ["What then does this absolute content mean?", "그렇다면 이 완전한 만족은 무엇을 뜻하는가?"], ["Can it be that his instinct is satisfied as to the vampire’s ultimate triumph?", "흡혈귀의 궁극적 승리에 관해 그의 본능이 충족되었을 수 있는 것일까? 잠깐."], ["Stay; he is himself zoöphagous, and in his wild ravings outside the chapel door of the deserted house he always spoke of “master.”", "그 자신이 동물을 먹는 자이고, 버려진 집의 예배당 문 밖에서 미친 듯이 헛소리를 할 때 그는 늘 \"주인\"에 관해 말했다."], ["This all seems confirmation of our idea.", "이 모든 것이 우리의 생각을 뒷받침하는 듯하다."], ["However, after a while I came away; my friend is just a little too sane at present to make it safe to probe him too deep with questions.", "그러나 잠시 후 나는 자리를 떴으니, 내 친구는 지금 너무 멀쩡한 상태라 질문으로 너무 깊이 캐묻기에는 안전하지 않았기 때문이다."], ["He might begin to think, and then--! So I came away.", "그가 생각하기 시작할지도 모르고, 그러면—— !"], ["I mistrust these quiet moods of his; so I have given the attendant a hint to look closely after him, and to have a strait-waistcoat ready in case of need.", "그래서 나는 자리를 떴다. 나는 그의 이 조용한 기분이 미덥지 않다. 그래서 나는 간병인에게 그를 가까이서 살펴보고, 필요할 경우에 대비해 구속복을 준비해 두라고 일러두었다."]], [["Jonathan Harker’s Journal.", "「조너선 하커의 일기」"]], [["29 September, in train to London.--When I received Mr.", "9월 29일, 런던행 기차 안에서."], ["Billington’s courteous message that he would give me any information in his power I thought it best to go down to Whitby and make, on the spot, such inquiries as I wanted.", "— 빌링턴 씨로부터 자신이 할 수 있는 한 어떤 정보든 주겠다는 정중한 전갈을 받았을 때, 나는 휘트비로 내려가 현장에서 내가 원하는 문의를 하는 것이 최선이라고 생각했다."], ["It was now my object to trace that horrid cargo of the Count’s to its place in London. Later, we may be able to deal with it.", "이제 내 목표는 백작의 그 끔찍한 화물이 런던의 어느 곳에 자리 잡았는지 그 행방을 추적하는 것이었다."], ["Billington junior, a nice lad, met me at the station, and brought me to his father’s house, where they had decided that I must stay the night. They are hospitable, with true Yorkshire hospitality: give a guest everything, and leave him free to do as he likes.", "나중에 우리는 그것을 처리할 수 있게 될지도 모른다. 착하고 어린 청년인 아들 빌링턴이 역에서 나를 맞아, 자기 아버지의 집으로 데려갔는데, 그들은 내가 그날 밤을 그곳에서 묵어야 한다고 정해 두고 있었다."], ["They all knew that I was busy, and that my stay was short, and Mr.", "그들은 진정한 요크셔식 환대로 손님을 후하게 대접했다."], ["Billington had ready in his office all the papers concerning the consignment of boxes.", "손님에게 모든 것을 다 내주되, 마음대로 하도록 자유롭게 내버려 두는 것이다."], ["It gave me almost a turn to see again one of the letters which I had seen on the Count’s table before I knew of his diabolical plans.", "그들은 모두 내가 바쁘고, 내 체류가 짧다는 것을 알고 있었으며, 빌링턴 씨는 그 상자들의 탁송에 관한 모든 서류를 자기 사무실에 준비해 두었다."], ["Everything had been carefully thought out, and done systematically and with precision.", "백작의 사악한 계략을 알기 전에 그의 탁자 위에서 보았던 편지 가운데 하나를 다시 보니 거의 섬뜩한 느낌이 들었다."], ["He seemed to have been prepared for every obstacle which might be placed by accident in the way of his intentions being carried out. To use an Americanism, he had “taken no chances,”", "모든 것이 면밀히 궁리되어, 체계적으로, 정밀하게 처리되어 있었다. 그는 자신의 의도가 실행되는 길에 우연히 놓일지 모르는 모든 장애에 대비해 둔 것 같았다."], ["and the absolute accuracy with which his instructions were fulfilled, was simply the logical result of his care. I saw the invoice, and took note of it: “Fifty cases of common earth, to be used for experimental purposes.”", "미국식 표현을 빌리자면, 그는 \"어떤 요행도 바라지 않았으며\", 그의 지시가 이행된 그 완벽한 정확성은 단순히 그 치밀함의 논리적 결과였다. 나는 송장을 보고 그것을 적어 두었다."], ["Also the copy of letter to Carter Paterson, and their reply; of both of these I got copies. This was all the information Mr.", "\"실험용으로 쓸 보통 흙 쉰 상자.\" 또한 카터 패터슨사에 보낸 편지의 사본과 그들의 회신도 보았는데, 이 둘 모두의 사본을 나는 손에 넣었다."], ["Billington could give me, so I went down to the port and saw the coastguards, the Customs officers and the harbour-master.", "이것이 빌링턴 씨가 내게 줄 수 있는 정보의 전부였으므로, 나는 항구로 내려가 해안경비대원들과 세관 관리들, 그리고 항만장을 만났다."], ["They had all something to say of the strange entry of the ship, which is already taking its place in local tradition; but no one could add to the simple description “Fifty cases of common earth.”", "그들은 모두 이미 이 고장의 전설로 자리 잡아 가고 있는 그 배의 기이한 입항에 관해 할 말이 있었으나, \"보통 흙 쉰 상자\"라는 단순한 설명에 무엇도 보탤 수 있는 사람은 없었다."], ["I then saw the station-master, who kindly put me in communication with the men who had actually received the boxes.", "그러고 나서 나는 역장을 만났는데, 그는 친절하게도 실제로 그 상자들을 받았던 사람들과 나를 연결해 주었다."], ["Their tally was exact with the list, and they had nothing to add except that the boxes were “main and mortal heavy,” and that shifting them was dry work.", "그들의 계산은 목록과 정확히 일치했고, 그 상자들이 \"지독하게 무거웠다\"는 것과 그것들을 옮기는 일이 목마른 일이었다는 것 외에는 더 보탤 것이 없었다."], ["One of them added that it was hard lines that there wasn’t any gentleman “such-like as yourself, squire,” to show some sort of appreciation of their efforts in a liquid form; another put in a rider that the thirst then generated was such that even the time which had elapsed had not completely allayed it.", "그들 가운데 하나는, \"나리 같은 어떤 신사분\"이 자기들의 수고에 대한 고마움을 무언가 마실 것의 형태로 표해 주지 않은 것이 야박한 처사라고 덧붙였다. 또 다른 하나는, 그때 생겨난 갈증이 어찌나 심했던지 그동안 흘러간 시간으로도 그것이 완전히 가시지 않았다는 단서를 달았다."], ["Needless to add, I took care before leaving to lift, for ever and adequately, this source of reproach.", "말할 것도 없이, 나는 떠나기 전에 이 원망의 근원을 영영, 그리고 넉넉하게 없애 주도록 챙겼다."]], [["30 September.--The station-master was good enough to give me a line to his old companion the station-master at King’s Cross, so that when I arrived there in the morning I was able to ask him about the arrival of the boxes.", "9월 30일. — 역장은 친절하게도 자신의 옛 동료인 킹스 크로스 역장에게 보내는 소개장을 한 줄 써주었고, 그래서 내가 아침에 그곳에 도착했을 때 나는 그에게 상자들의 도착에 관해 물어볼 수 있었다."], ["He, too, put me at once in communication with the proper officials, and I saw that their tally was correct with the original invoice.", "그 역시 즉시 나를 해당 관리들과 연결해 주었고, 나는 그들의 계산이 원래의 송장과 정확히 맞아떨어지는 것을 보았다."], ["The opportunities of acquiring an abnormal thirst had been here limited; a noble use of them had, however, been made, and again I was compelled to deal with the result in an ex post facto manner.", "이곳에서는 비정상적인 갈증을 얻을 기회가 한정되어 있었으나, 그래도 그 기회는 훌륭하게 활용되어 있었으니, 나는 또다시 사후(事後)의 방식으로 그 결과를 처리해야만 했다."]], [["From thence I went on to Carter Paterson’s central office, where I met with the utmost courtesy.", "거기에서 나는 카터 패터슨사의 본사로 갔는데, 그곳에서 더없이 정중한 대접을 받았다."], ["They looked up the transaction in their day-book and letter-book, and at once telephoned to their King’s Cross office for more details.", "그들은 일지와 편지 대장에서 그 거래를 찾아보고는, 즉시 더 자세한 사항을 알아보려고 킹스 크로스 사무소로 전화를 걸었다."], ["By good fortune, the men who did the teaming were waiting for work, and the official at once sent them over, sending also by one of them the way-bill and all the papers connected with the delivery of the boxes at Carfax.", "다행히도 그 짐을 운반했던 인부들이 일감을 기다리고 있어서, 담당자는 즉시 그들을 보내 주었고, 그 가운데 하나를 통해 운송장과 카팩스로의 상자 배달에 관련된 모든 서류도 함께 보내 주었다."], ["Here again I found the tally agreeing exactly; the carriers’ men were able to supplement the paucity of the written words with a few details.", "여기서도 나는 계산이 정확히 들어맞는 것을 알았다. 운송업자의 인부들은 적힌 글의 빈약함을 몇 가지 세부 사항으로 보충해 줄 수 있었다."], ["These were, I shortly found, connected almost solely with the dusty nature of the job, and of the consequent thirst engendered in the operators.", "곧 알게 되었지만, 이 세부 사항들은 거의 오로지 그 일의 먼지투성이 성질과, 그로 인해 일꾼들에게 생겨난 갈증에 관한 것이었다."], ["On my affording an opportunity, through the medium of the currency of the realm, of the allaying, at a later period, this beneficial evil, one of the men remarked:--", "내가 이 나라의 통화를 매개로 하여, 나중에 그 이로운 해악을 가시게 할 기회를 제공하자, 그 인부들 가운데 하나가 말했다."]], [["“That ’ere ’ouse, guv’nor, is the rummiest I ever was in. Blyme!", "\"나리, 그 집은요, 제가 들어가 본 데 중에 제일 괴상한 데였습죠."], ["but it ain’t been touched sence a hundred years.", "이런 제기랄! 백 년은 손도 안 댄 것 같더라구요."], ["There was dust that thick in the place that you might have slep’ on it without ’urtin’ of yer bones; an’ the place was that neglected that yer might ’ave smelled ole Jerusalem in it.", "먼지가 어찌나 두텁게 쌓였던지 뼈 다칠 걱정 없이 그 위에서 잘 수도 있을 정도였습죠. 게다가 어찌나 방치됐던지 그 안에서 케케묵은 예루살렘 냄새라도 맡을 수 있을 것 같았다니까요."], ["But the ole chapel--that took the cike, that did!", "하지만 그 오래된 예배당—— 그게 압권이었습죠, 진짜로요!"], ["Me and my mate, we thort we wouldn’t never git out quick enough.", "저랑 제 동료는 거기서 한시바삐 못 나가서 안달이 났습죠."], ["Lor’, I wouldn’t take less nor a quid a moment to stay there arter dark.”", "맙소사, 해 진 다음에 거기 있으라면 한 순간에 일 파운드를 줘도 안 할 겁니다요.\""]], [["Having been in the house, I could well believe him; but if he knew what I know, he would, I think, have raised his terms.", "그 집에 들어가 본 적이 있는 나로서는 그의 말을 충분히 믿을 수 있었다. 그러나 만일 그가 내가 아는 것을 알았더라면, 내 생각에 그는 자기 값을 더 올렸을 것이다."]], [["Of one thing I am now satisfied: that all the boxes which arrived at Whitby from Varna in the Demeter were safely deposited in the old chapel at Carfax.", "이제 한 가지는 분명히 알게 되었다. 데메테르호를 타고 바르나에서 휘트비로 도착한 그 상자들이 전부 카팩스의 오래된 예배당에 무사히 부려졌다는 것이다."], ["There should be fifty of them there, unless any have since been removed--as from Dr. Seward’s diary I fear.", "그 이후로 일부가 옮겨지지 않았다면 그곳에 쉰 개가 있어야 한다—— 수어드 박사의 일기로 미루어 보면 일부가 옮겨진 것이 아닐까 두렵지만 말이다."]], [["I shall try to see the carter who took away the boxes from Carfax when Renfield attacked them.", "나는 렌필드가 상자들을 공격했을 때 카팩스에서 그것들을 실어 간 운송업자를 만나 보려고 한다."], ["By following up this clue we may learn a good deal.", "이 단서를 따라가면 우리는 많은 것을 알게 될지도 모른다."]], [["Later.--Mina and I have worked all day, and we have put all the papers into order.", "나중에. — 미나와 나는 온종일 일했고, 모든 서류를 정리해 두었다."]], [["Mina Harker’s Journal", "「미나 하커의 일기」"]], [["30 September.--I am so glad that I hardly know how to contain myself.", "9월 30일. — 나는 어찌나 기쁜지 나 자신을 어떻게 추슬러야 할지 모를 지경이다."], ["It is, I suppose, the reaction from the haunting fear which I have had: that this terrible affair and the reopening of his old wound might act detrimentally on Jonathan.", "짐작건대 이것은 나를 줄곧 따라다니던 두려움에 대한 반작용이다. 이 끔찍한 일과, 그의 오래된 상처가 다시 헤집어지는 것이 조너선에게 해롭게 작용하지 않을까 하는 두려움 말이다."], ["I saw him leave for Whitby with as brave a face as I could, but I was sick with apprehension.", "나는 그가 휘트비로 떠나는 것을 되도록 의연한 얼굴로 지켜보았으나, 속으로는 불안으로 마음이 아팠다."], ["The effort has, however, done him good.", "그러나 그 일이 오히려 그에게 도움이 되었다."], ["He was never so resolute, never so strong, never so full of volcanic energy, as at present.", "그는 지금처럼 결연한 적도, 강인한 적도, 화산 같은 활력으로 가득 찬 적도 없었다."], ["It is just as that dear, good Professor Van Helsing said: he is true grit, and he improves under strain that would kill a weaker nature.", "그것은 꼭 그 선하고 사랑스러운 반 헬싱 교수님이 말씀하신 그대로다. 그는 진짜 강단 있는 사람이며, 더 약한 천성이라면 죽고 말 압박 속에서 오히려 나아진다는 것이다."], ["He came back full of life and hope and determination; we have got everything in order for to-night.", "그는 생기와 희망과 결의로 가득 차서 돌아왔고, 우리는 오늘 밤을 위해 모든 것을 정리해 두었다."], ["I feel myself quite wild with excitement.", "나는 흥분으로 어쩔 줄을 모르겠다."], ["I suppose one ought to pity any thing so hunted as is the Count.", "짐작건대 백작처럼 그렇게 쫓기는 것이라면 무엇이든 가엾게 여겨야 마땅하리라."], ["That is just it: this Thing is not human--not even beast. To read Dr.", "바로 그것이 문제다. 이 존재는 인간이 아니다—— 짐승조차 아니다."], ["Seward’s account of poor Lucy’s death, and what followed, is enough to dry up the springs of pity in one’s heart.", "가엾은 루시의 죽음에 관한 수어드 박사의 기록과 그 뒤에 이어진 일을 읽으면, 마음속 연민의 샘마저 메말라 버리기에 충분하다."]], [["Later.--Lord Godalming and Mr. Morris arrived earlier than we expected. Dr.", "나중에. — 고달밍 경과 모리스 씨가 우리가 예상한 것보다 일찍 도착했다."], ["Seward was out on business, and had taken Jonathan with him, so I had to see them.", "수어드 박사는 일로 외출 중이었고 조너선을 데리고 갔으므로, 내가 그들을 맞아야 했다."], ["It was to me a painful meeting, for it brought back all poor dear Lucy’s hopes of only a few months ago.", "그것은 나에게 고통스러운 만남이었으니, 불과 몇 달 전 가엾고 사랑하던 루시의 온갖 희망을 되살아나게 했기 때문이다."], ["Of course they had heard Lucy speak of me, and it seemed that Dr. Van Helsing, too, has been quite “blowing my trumpet,” as Mr.", "물론 그들은 루시가 내 이야기를 하는 것을 들은 적이 있었고, 반 헬싱 박사 역시 모리스 씨의 표현을 빌리자면 한껏 \"내 자랑을 늘어놓은\""], ["Morris expressed it.", "모양이었다."], ["Poor fellows, neither of them is aware that I know all about the proposals they made to Lucy.", "가엾은 사람들, 그들 둘 다 내가 그들이 루시에게 한 청혼에 관해 죄다 알고 있다는 것을 모른다."], ["They did not quite know what to say or do, as they were ignorant of the amount of my knowledge; so they had to keep on neutral subjects.", "그들은 내가 얼마나 알고 있는지 모르므로, 무슨 말을 하거나 무엇을 해야 할지 잘 몰랐고, 그래서 무난한 주제에만 머물러야 했다."], ["However, I thought the matter over, and came to the conclusion that the best thing I could do would be to post them in affairs right up to date.", "그러나 나는 그 문제를 곰곰이 생각해 본 끝에, 내가 할 수 있는 최선은 그들에게 지금까지의 일들을 빠짐없이 알려주는 것이라는 결론에 이르렀다."], ["I knew from Dr. Seward’s diary that they had been at Lucy’s death--her real death--and that I need not fear to betray any secret before the time.", "나는 수어드 박사의 일기에서 그들이 루시의 죽음—— 그녀의 진짜 죽음—— 의 자리에 있었다는 것을, 그래서 내가 때가 되기 전에 어떤 비밀을 누설할까 두려워할 필요가 없다는 것을 알고 있었다."], ["So I told them, as well as I could, that I had read all the papers and diaries, and that my husband and I, having typewritten them, had just finished putting them in order.", "그래서 나는 되도록 잘 설명하여, 내가 모든 서류와 일기를 다 읽었으며, 남편과 내가 그것들을 타자로 옮긴 뒤 막 정리를 마쳤다고 그들에게 말했다."], ["I gave them each a copy to read in the library.", "나는 그들 각자에게 서재에서 읽으라고 사본을 한 부씩 주었다."], ["When Lord Godalming got his and turned it over--it does make a pretty good pile--he said:--", "고달밍 경은 자기 사본을 받아 뒤적이더니—— 그것은 꽤 두툼한 뭉치였다—— 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Did you write all this, Mrs. Harker?”", "\"이걸 다 부인이 쓰셨습니까, 하커 부인?\""]], [["I nodded, and he went on:--", "내가 고개를 끄덕이자 그는 말을 이었다."]], [["“I don’t quite see the drift of it; but you people are all so good and kind, and have been working so earnestly and so energetically, that all I can do is to accept your ideas blindfold and try to help you.", "\"저는 이게 무슨 뜻인지 잘 모르겠습니다. 하지만 여러분 모두 어찌나 선하고 친절하시며, 어찌나 진지하고 정력적으로 일해 오셨던지, 제가 할 수 있는 일이라곤 여러분의 생각을 눈을 가린 채 받아들이고 여러분을 도우려 애쓰는 것뿐입니다."], ["I have had one lesson already in accepting facts that should make a man humble to the last hour of his life.", "저는 이미, 사람이 죽는 그 순간까지 겸손하게 만들 만한, 사실을 받아들이는 한 가지 교훈을 얻었습니다."], ["Besides, I know you loved my poor Lucy--” Here he turned away and covered his face with his hands.", "게다가, 부인이 제 가엾은 루시를 사랑하셨다는 걸 압니다——\" 여기서 그는 돌아서서 두 손으로 얼굴을 가렸다."], ["I could hear the tears in his voice. Mr.", "나는 그의 목소리에 어린 눈물을 들을 수 있었다."], ["Morris, with instinctive delicacy, just laid a hand for a moment on his shoulder, and then walked quietly out of the room.", "모리스 씨는 본능적인 섬세함으로, 잠시 그의 어깨에 손을 얹었다가, 조용히 방을 걸어 나갔다."], ["I suppose there is something in woman’s nature that makes a man free to break down before her and express his feelings on the tender or emotional side without feeling it derogatory to his manhood; for when Lord Godalming found himself alone with me he sat down on the sofa and gave way utterly and openly.", "짐작건대 여성의 천성에는, 남자가 자신의 사내다움을 깎아내린다는 느낌 없이 그 앞에서 무너지고 부드럽고 감정적인 면의 심정을 토로할 수 있게 해주는 무언가가 있는 모양이다. 고달밍 경은 나와 단둘이 남게 되자 소파에 앉아 완전히, 그리고 거리낌 없이 무너져 내렸기 때문이다. 나는 그의 곁에 앉아 그의 손을 잡았다."], ["I sat down beside him and took his hand. I hope he didn’t think it forward of me, and that if he ever thinks of it afterwards he never will have such a thought.", "그가 이것을 내 주제넘은 짓으로 여기지 않았기를, 그리고 만일 그가 나중에 이 일을 떠올리더라도 결코 그런 생각을 품지 않기를 바란다. 이런 생각은 그를 모욕하는 것이다."], ["There I wrong him; I know he never will--he is too true a gentleman.", "나는 그가 결코 그러지 않으리라는 것을 안다—— 그는 더없이 참된 신사이니."], ["I said to him, for I could see that his heart was breaking:--", "나는 그에게 말했으니, 그의 가슴이 무너져 내리고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었기 때문이다."]], [["“I loved dear Lucy, and I know what she was to you, and what you were to her.", "\"저는 사랑하던 루시를 사랑했고, 그녀가 당신께 어떤 사람이었는지, 당신이 그녀에게 어떤 사람이었는지 알아요."], ["She and I were like sisters; and now she is gone, will you not let me be like a sister to you in your trouble?", "그녀와 저는 자매 같은 사이였어요. 그리고 이제 그녀가 떠났으니, 당신의 슬픔 속에서 제가 당신께 누이 같은 사람이 되게 해주시지 않겠어요?"], ["I know what sorrows you have had, though I cannot measure the depth of them.", "비록 그 깊이를 헤아릴 수는 없으나, 당신이 어떤 슬픔을 겪었는지 알아요."], ["If sympathy and pity can help in your affliction, won’t you let me be of some little service--for Lucy’s sake?”", "만일 공감과 연민이 당신의 고통에 도움이 될 수 있다면, 제가 작은 도움이라도 되게 해주시지 않겠어요—— 루시를 위해서라도요?\""]], [["In an instant the poor dear fellow was overwhelmed with grief.", "순식간에 그 가엾은 사람은 슬픔에 압도되고 말았다."], ["It seemed to me that all that he had of late been suffering in silence found a vent at once.", "내게는 그가 요즈음 줄곧 말없이 견뎌 온 모든 것이 한꺼번에 터져 나온 것처럼 보였다."], ["He grew quite hysterical, and raising his open hands, beat his palms together in a perfect agony of grief.", "그는 완전히 격정에 사로잡혀, 펼친 두 손을 들어 올려 더없는 슬픔의 고통 속에서 손바닥을 마주쳤다."], ["He stood up and then sat down again, and the tears rained down his cheeks.", "그는 일어섰다가 다시 앉았고, 눈물이 그의 뺨을 타고 비 오듯 흘러내렸다."], ["I felt an infinite pity for him, and opened my arms unthinkingly.", "나는 그에게 한없는 연민을 느꼈고, 생각할 겨를도 없이 두 팔을 벌렸다."], ["With a sob he laid his head on my shoulder and cried like a wearied child, whilst he shook with emotion.", "그는 흐느끼며 내 어깨에 머리를 기대고 지친 아이처럼 울었고, 그러는 동안 감정에 북받쳐 몸을 떨었다."]], [["We women have something of the mother in us that makes us rise above smaller matters when the mother-spirit is invoked; I felt this big sorrowing man’s head resting on me, as though it were that of the baby that some day may lie on my bosom, and I stroked his hair as though he were my own child.", "우리 여자들에게는 어딘가 모성 같은 것이 있어, 그 모성의 정신이 불러일으켜질 때면 사소한 일들을 넘어서게 만든다. 나는 이 슬픔에 잠긴 커다란 남자의 머리가 내게 기대어 있는 것을, 마치 언젠가 내 가슴에 안길지도 모를 아기의 머리인 양 느꼈고, 그가 내 친자식이기라도 한 듯 그의 머리칼을 쓰다듬었다."], ["I never thought at the time how strange it all was.", "그때 나는 이 모든 것이 얼마나 기이한지 조금도 생각하지 못했다."]], [["After a little bit his sobs ceased, and he raised himself with an apology, though he made no disguise of his emotion.", "잠시 후 그의 흐느낌이 멎었고, 그는 사과의 말과 함께 몸을 일으켰으나, 자신의 감정을 감추려 하지는 않았다."], ["He told me that for days and nights past--weary days and sleepless nights--he had been unable to speak with any one, as a man must speak in his time of sorrow.", "그는 지난 며칠간 밤낮으로—— 지치는 낮과 잠 못 이루는 밤 동안—— 사람이 슬픔의 때에 마땅히 누군가에게 말해야 하는 식으로 누구와도 이야기를 나눌 수 없었다고 내게 말했다."], ["There was no woman whose sympathy could be given to him, or with whom, owing to the terrible circumstance with which his sorrow was surrounded, he could speak freely.", "그에게 공감을 베풀어 줄 만한 여인도, 또 그의 슬픔을 둘러싼 끔찍한 정황 탓에 그가 거리낌 없이 이야기를 나눌 만한 여인도 없었다는 것이다. \"이제는 제가 얼마나 괴로웠는지 알겠습니다,\""], ["“I know now how I suffered,”", "그는 눈물을 닦으며 말했다."], ["he said, as he dried his eyes, “but I do not know even yet--and none other can ever know--how much your sweet sympathy has been to me to-day.", "\"하지만 오늘 부인의 그 다정한 공감이 제게 얼마나 큰 것이었는지는—— 아직도 모르겠고, 다른 누구도 결코 알 수 없을 겁니다."], ["I shall know better in time; and believe me that, though I am not ungrateful now, my gratitude will grow with my understanding.", "시간이 지나면 더 잘 알게 되겠지요. 그리고 믿어 주세요, 제가 지금도 고마워하지 않는 것은 아니지만, 제 고마움은 깨달음과 더불어 자라날 겁니다."], ["You will let me be like a brother, will you not, for all our lives--for dear Lucy’s sake?”", "우리 평생토록, 저를 오라비 같은 사람이 되게 해주시지 않겠습니까—— 사랑하던 루시를 위해서라도요?\""]], [["“For dear Lucy’s sake,”", "\"사랑하던 루시를 위해서요,\""], ["I said as we clasped hands.", "나는 우리가 손을 맞잡으며 말했다."], ["“Ay, and for your own sake,”", "\"그래요, 그리고 부인 자신을 위해서도요,\""], ["he added, “for if a man’s esteem and gratitude are ever worth the winning, you have won mine to-day.", "그가 덧붙였다. \"만일 한 사내의 존경과 고마움이 얻을 만한 가치가 있는 것이라면, 부인은 오늘 제 것을 얻으셨으니까요."], ["If ever the future should bring to you a time when you need a man’s help, believe me, you will not call in vain.", "만일 앞날에 부인께 한 사내의 도움이 필요한 때가 온다면, 믿어 주세요, 부인은 헛되이 부르지 않으실 겁니다."], ["God grant that no such time may ever come to you to break the sunshine of your life; but if it should ever come, promise me that you will let me know.”", "부디 그런 때가 와서 부인 삶의 햇살을 깨뜨리는 일이 결코 없기를 하느님께 빕니다. 그러나 만일 언젠가 그런 때가 온다면, 제게 알려 주시겠다고 약속해 주세요.\""], ["He was so earnest, and his sorrow was so fresh, that I felt it would comfort him, so I said:--", "그가 어찌나 진지했고, 그의 슬픔이 어찌나 생생했던지, 나는 그것이 그에게 위안이 되리라 느껴 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“I promise.”", "\"약속할게요.\""]], [["As I came along the corridor I saw Mr. Morris looking out of a window.", "복도를 따라 걸어오다가 나는 모리스 씨가 창밖을 내다보고 있는 것을 보았다."], ["He turned as he heard my footsteps.", "그는 내 발소리를 듣고 돌아섰다."], ["“How is Art?” he said.", "\"아트는 좀 어떻습니까?\""], ["Then noticing my red eyes, he went on: “Ah, I see you have been comforting him.", "그가 말했다. 그러고는 내 붉어진 눈을 알아채고 말을 이었다. \"아, 그를 위로해 주셨군요."], ["Poor old fellow! he needs it.", "가엾은 친구! 그에게는 그게 필요하지요."], ["No one but a woman can help a man when he is in trouble of the heart; and he had no one to comfort him.”", "한 사내가 마음의 괴로움에 빠져 있을 때 그를 도울 수 있는 건 오직 여인뿐이니까요. 그런데 그를 위로해 줄 사람이 아무도 없었지요.\""]], [["He bore his own trouble so bravely that my heart bled for him.", "그는 자신의 괴로움을 어찌나 의연하게 견디던지, 나는 그를 위해 가슴이 미어졌다."], ["I saw the manuscript in his hand, and I knew that when he read it he would realise how much I knew; so I said to him:--", "나는 그의 손에 들린 원고를 보았고, 그가 그것을 읽으면 내가 얼마나 알고 있는지 깨달으리라는 것을 알았다. 그래서 나는 그에게 말했다."]], [["“I wish I could comfort all who suffer from the heart.", "\"마음의 괴로움을 겪는 모든 분을 제가 위로해 드릴 수 있다면 좋겠어요."], ["Will you let me be your friend, and will you come to me for comfort if you need it?", "제가 당신의 벗이 되게 해주시겠어요? 그리고 위로가 필요하시면 제게 와 주시겠어요?"], ["You will know, later on, why I speak.” He saw that I was in earnest, and stooping, took my hand, and raising it to his lips, kissed it.", "제가 왜 이런 말을 하는지는 나중에 아시게 될 거예요.\" 그는 내가 진심임을 알아채고는, 몸을 숙여 내 손을 잡아 입술로 가져가 입맞췄다."], ["It seemed but poor comfort to so brave and unselfish a soul, and impulsively I bent over and kissed him.", "그토록 용감하고 헌신적인 영혼에게는 보잘것없는 위로로만 보였기에, 나는 충동적으로 몸을 숙여 그에게 입을 맞췄다."], ["The tears rose in his eyes, and there was a momentary choking in his throat; he said quite calmly:--", "그의 눈에 눈물이 차올랐고, 잠시 목이 메었다. 그는 무척 차분하게 말했다."]], [["“Little girl, you will never regret that true-hearted kindness, so long as ever you live!”", "\"아가씨, 당신은 그 진심 어린 친절을 살아 있는 한 결코 후회하지 않으실 겁니다!\""], ["Then he went into the study to his friend.", "그러고는 자기 친구에게로 서재로 들어갔다."]], [["“Little girl!”--the very words he had used to Lucy, and oh, but he proved himself a friend!", "\"아가씨!\"—— 그가 루시에게 썼던 바로 그 말이었다. 아, 그러나 그는 자신이 참된 벗임을 스스로 증명해 보였다!"]], [["# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY", "# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY"]], [["30 September.--I got home at five o’clock, and found that Godalming and Morris had not only arrived, but had already studied the transcript of the various diaries and letters which Harker and his wonderful wife had made and arranged.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」 9월 30일. — 다섯 시에 집에 돌아와 보니, 고달밍과 모리스가 이미 도착했을 뿐만 아니라 하커와 그의 훌륭한 아내가 작성하여 정리해둔 여러 일기와 편지의 사본을 벌써 다 살펴본 뒤였다."], ["Harker had not yet returned from his visit to the carriers’ men, of whom Dr. Hennessey had written to me. Mrs.", "하커는 헤네시 박사가 나에게 편지로 일러준 운송업자 인부들을 만나러 갔다가 아직 돌아오지 않았다."], ["Harker gave us a cup of tea, and I can honestly say that, for the first time since I have lived in it, this old house seemed like home.", "하커 부인이 우리에게 차 한 잔을 내주었는데, 나는 이 낡은 집에서 살게 된 이래 처음으로 이곳이 진정 집처럼 느껴졌다고 솔직히 말할 수 있다."], ["When we had finished, Mrs. Harker said:--", "차를 다 마시자 하커 부인이 말했다."]], [["“Dr. Seward, may I ask a favour?", "\"수어드 박사님, 한 가지 청을 드려도 될까요?"], ["I want to see your patient, Mr. Renfield.", "박사님의 환자인 렌필드 씨를 만나 보고 싶어요."], ["Do let me see him.", "부디 만나게 해주세요."], ["What you have said of him in your diary interests me so much!”", "박사님이 일기에 적으신 그분 이야기가 너무도 흥미로워서요!\""], ["She looked so appealing and so pretty that I could not refuse her, and there was no possible reason why I should; so I took her with me.", "그녀의 표정이 어찌나 간절하고 어여뻤던지 나는 거절할 수가 없었고, 또 거절할 까닭도 전혀 없었다. 그래서 나는 그녀를 데리고 갔다."], ["When I went into the room, I told the man that a lady would like to see him; to which he simply answered: “Why?”", "방에 들어가서 나는 그 남자에게 어떤 부인이 만나고 싶어 한다고 일러주었다. 그러자 그는 그저 \"왜요?\" 하고 되물을 뿐이었다."]], [["“She is going through the house, and wants to see every one in it,” I answered.", "\"이 집을 두루 둘러보시는 중인데, 집 안의 모든 사람을 만나 보고 싶어 하시오.\""], ["“Oh, very well,”", "내가 답했다."], ["he said; “let her come in, by all means; but just wait a minute till I tidy up the place.”", "\"아, 그럼 좋습니다.\" 그가 말했다. \"들어오시라고 하세요, 얼마든지요."], ["His method of tidying was peculiar: he simply swallowed all the flies and spiders in the boxes before I could stop him.", "다만 방을 좀 치울 동안 잠깐만 기다려 주십시오.\" 그가 방을 치우는 방식은 기이했다."], ["It was quite evident that he feared, or was jealous of, some interference.", "내가 미처 말릴 새도 없이, 그는 상자 안의 파리와 거미를 모조리 꿀꺽 삼켜버린 것이다."], ["When he had got through his disgusting task, he said cheerfully: “Let the lady come in,” and sat down on the edge of his bed with his head down, but with his eyelids raised so that he could see her as she entered.", "어떤 간섭을 두려워하거나 시기하고 있음이 분명했다. 그 역겨운 일을 끝내자 그는 명랑하게 \"부인을 들어오시게 하세요\"라고 말하고는, 침대 가장자리에 머리를 숙이고 앉았으나, 눈꺼풀만은 치켜뜨고 있어 그녀가 들어오는 모습을 볼 수 있게 했다."], ["For a moment I thought that he might have some homicidal intent; I remembered how quiet he had been just before he attacked me in my own study, and I took care to stand where I could seize him at once if he attempted to make a spring at her.", "한순간 나는 그에게 살의가 있을지도 모른다고 생각했다. 내 서재에서 나를 덮치기 직전에 그가 얼마나 조용했는지를 떠올리고, 그가 그녀에게 달려들려 하면 곧바로 붙잡을 수 있는 자리에 서 있도록 신경을 썼다."], ["She came into the room with an easy gracefulness which would at once command the respect of any lunatic--for easiness is one of the qualities mad people most respect.", "그녀는 어떤 광인에게서도 단번에 존중을 이끌어낼 만한 편안한 우아함으로 방에 들어섰다. 편안함이야말로 미친 사람들이 가장 존중하는 자질 가운데 하나인 것이다."], ["She walked over to him, smiling pleasantly, and held out her hand.", "그녀는 상냥하게 미소 지으며 그에게 다가가 손을 내밀었다."]], [["“Good-evening, Mr. Renfield,”", "\"안녕하세요, 렌필드 씨.\""], ["said she.", "그녀가 말했다."], ["“You see, I know you, for Dr.", "\"보시다시피 저는 당신을 알고 있어요."], ["Seward has told me of you.” He made no immediate reply, but eyed her all over intently with a set frown on his face.", "수어드 박사님이 당신 이야기를 해주셨거든요.\" 그는 곧바로 대꾸하지 않고, 얼굴을 굳게 찌푸린 채 그녀를 위아래로 뚫어지게 훑어보았다."], ["This look gave way to one of wonder, which merged in doubt; then, to my intense astonishment, he said:--", "그 표정은 이내 놀라움으로, 다시 의혹으로 바뀌었다. 그러더니 나를 몹시 놀라게도, 그가 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“You’re not the girl the doctor wanted to marry, are you?", "\"당신은 박사가 결혼하고 싶어 했던 그 아가씨가 아니지요?"], ["You can’t be, you know, for she’s dead.” Mrs.", "그럴 리가 없어요, 아시다시피 그 여자는 죽었으니까요.\""], ["Harker smiled sweetly as she replied:--", "하커 부인이 다정하게 미소 지으며 답했다."]], [["“Oh no!", "\"아, 아니에요!"], ["I have a husband of my own, to whom I was married before I ever saw Dr. Seward, or he me.", "저에겐 제 남편이 있고, 저는 수어드 박사님을 뵙기 전에, 그분이 저를 보기도 전에 이미 그와 결혼했답니다."], ["I am Mrs. Harker.”", "저는 하커 부인이에요.\""]], [["“Then what are you doing here?”", "\"그렇다면 여기서 무얼 하고 계시죠?\""]], [["“My husband and I are staying on a visit with Dr. Seward.”", "\"제 남편과 저는 수어드 박사님 댁에 손님으로 머물고 있어요.\""]], [["“Then don’t stay.”", "\"그렇다면 머물지 마세요.\""]], [["“But why not?”", "\"하지만 어째서죠?\""], ["I thought that this style of conversation might not be pleasant to Mrs. Harker, any more than it was to me, so I joined in:--", "나는 이런 식의 대화가 나에게 그렇듯 하커 부인에게도 유쾌하지 않으리라는 생각이 들어 끼어들었다."]], [["“How did you know I wanted to marry any one?”", "\"내가 누군가와 결혼하고 싶어 했다는 걸 어떻게 알았소?\""], ["His reply was simply contemptuous, given in a pause in which he turned his eyes from Mrs. Harker to me, instantly turning them back again:--", "그의 대답은 그저 경멸에 찬 것이었다. 그는 잠깐 말을 멈추고 하커 부인에게서 나에게로 눈길을 돌렸다가, 곧바로 다시 그녀에게로 거두며 말했다."]], [["“What an asinine question!”", "\"참으로 멍청한 질문이군요!\""]], [["“I don’t see that at all, Mr. Renfield,” said Mrs.", "\"저는 전혀 그렇게 생각하지 않아요, 렌필드 씨.\""], ["Harker, at once championing me. He replied to her with as much courtesy and respect as he had shown contempt to me:--", "하커 부인이 곧바로 나를 두둔하며 말했다. 그는 나에게 보였던 경멸만큼이나 큰 예의와 존중을 담아 그녀에게 답했다."]], [["“You will, of course, understand, Mrs.", "\"하커 부인, 물론 부인께서도 이해하시겠지요."], ["Harker, that when a man is so loved and honoured as our host is, everything regarding him is of interest in our little community. Dr.", "우리 주인장처럼 사랑받고 존경받는 사람이라면, 그에 관한 모든 것이 우리의 작은 공동체에서 관심거리가 되는 법입니다."], ["Seward is loved not only by his household and his friends, but even by his patients, who, being some of them hardly in mental equilibrium, are apt to distort causes and effects.", "수어드 박사는 그의 집안사람들과 친구들뿐 아니라, 환자들에게서까지도 사랑을 받습니다. 그런데 그 환자들 가운데 더러는 정신의 균형이 온전치 못해 원인과 결과를 왜곡하기 쉽지요."], ["Since I myself have been an inmate of a lunatic asylum, I cannot but notice that the sophistic tendencies of some of its inmates lean towards the errors of non causa and ignoratio elenchi.”", "저 자신이 정신병원의 수용자였던 까닭에, 그 수용자 가운데 더러의 궤변적 경향이 '비원인(non causa)'과 '논점 일탈(ignoratio elenchi)'의 오류로 기우는 것을 알아차리지 않을 수 없습니다.\""], ["I positively opened my eyes at this new development.", "나는 이 새로운 전개에 그야말로 눈이 휘둥그레졌다."], ["Here was my own pet lunatic--the most pronounced of his type that I had ever met with--talking elemental philosophy, and with the manner of a polished gentleman.", "여기, 내가 아끼는 광인이 — 내가 여태껏 만나본 가운데 가장 전형적인 유형의 광인이 — 세련된 신사의 태도로 근원적인 철학을 논하고 있는 것이다."], ["I wonder if it was Mrs. Harker’s presence which had touched some chord in his memory.", "혹시 하커 부인의 존재가 그의 기억 속 어떤 현(絃)을 건드린 것은 아닐까 싶었다."], ["If this new phase was spontaneous, or in any way due to her unconscious influence, she must have some rare gift or power.", "만일 이 새로운 국면이 자발적인 것이거나, 어떤 식으로든 그녀의 무의식적인 영향에서 비롯된 것이라면, 그녀는 분명 보기 드문 재능이나 힘을 지닌 것이리라."]], [["We continued to talk for some time; and, seeing that he was seemingly quite reasonable, she ventured, looking at me questioningly as she began, to lead him to his favourite topic.", "우리는 한동안 더 이야기를 이어갔다. 그가 겉보기에 꽤 분별 있어 보이자, 그녀는 처음 시작할 때 나를 미심쩍은 눈으로 바라보면서, 그를 그가 좋아하는 화제로 이끌어보았다."], ["I was again astonished, for he addressed himself to the question with the impartiality of the completest sanity; he even took himself as an example when he mentioned certain things.", "나는 또 한 번 놀랐다. 그가 완전한 제정신의 공정함으로 그 문제를 다루었기 때문이다. 그는 어떤 것들을 언급할 때 심지어 자기 자신을 예로 들기까지 했다."]], [["“Why, I myself am an instance of a man who had a strange belief.", "\"보십시오, 저 자신이 바로 기이한 믿음을 가졌던 사람의 한 예입니다."], ["Indeed, it was no wonder that my friends were alarmed, and insisted on my being put under control.", "사실 제 친구들이 놀라 저를 통제 아래 두어야 한다고 고집한 것도 무리는 아니었지요."], ["I used to fancy that life was a positive and perpetual entity, and that by consuming a multitude of live things, no matter how low in the scale of creation, one might indefinitely prolong life.", "저는 생명이 실재하며 끊임없이 이어지는 하나의 실체라고, 그래서 아무리 창조의 단계에서 낮은 것일지라도 살아 있는 것을 수없이 삼키면 생명을 무한히 늘릴 수 있다고 믿곤 했습니다."], ["At times I held the belief so strongly that I actually tried to take human life.", "때로는 그 믿음이 어찌나 강했던지 실제로 사람의 목숨을 빼앗으려 한 적도 있었지요."], ["The doctor here will bear me out that on one occasion I tried to kill him for the purpose of strengthening my vital powers by the assimilation with my own body of his life through the medium of his blood--relying, of course, upon the Scriptural phrase, ‘For the blood is the life.’ Though, indeed, the vendor of a certain nostrum has vulgarised the truism to the very point of contempt.", "여기 계신 박사님이 증언해 주실 겁니다. 언젠가 제가 그분을 죽이려 한 적이 있는데, 그것은 그분의 피라는 매개를 통해 그분의 생명을 제 몸에 동화시켜 제 생명력을 강화하려는 목적에서였습니다 — 물론 '피가 곧 생명이니라'라는 성경 구절에 기대어서 말이지요. 하기야 어떤 엉터리 약장수가 그 자명한 이치를 그야말로 경멸스러울 만큼 천박하게 만들어버리긴 했지만요."], ["Isn’t that true, doctor?”", "그렇지 않습니까, 박사님?\""], ["I nodded assent, for I was so amazed that I hardly knew what to either think or say; it was hard to imagine that I had seen him eat up his spiders and flies not five minutes before.", "나는 고개를 끄덕여 동의했다. 너무도 놀라 무엇을 생각해야 할지, 무슨 말을 해야 할지조차 알 수 없었기 때문이다. 채 오 분도 지나지 않아 그가 거미와 파리를 삼키는 것을 내가 보았다는 사실이 도무지 믿기지 않았다."], ["Looking at my watch, I saw that I should go to the station to meet Van Helsing, so I told Mrs. Harker that it was time to leave.", "시계를 보니 판 헬싱을 마중하러 역에 나가야 할 시각이었으므로, 나는 하커 부인에게 이만 자리를 떠야겠다고 말했다."], ["She came at once, after saying pleasantly to Mr. Renfield: “Good-bye, and I hope I may see you often, under auspices pleasanter to yourself,”", "그녀는 곧바로 따라나섰는데, 떠나기 전 렌필드 씨에게 상냥하게 말했다. \"안녕히 계세요, 당신께 더 즐거운 형편에서 자주 뵐 수 있기를 바라요.\""], ["to which, to my astonishment, he replied:--", "그러자 나를 놀라게도 그가 이렇게 답했다."]], [["“Good-bye, my dear.", "\"안녕히 가십시오, 친애하는 부인."], ["I pray God I may never see your sweet face again.", "부디 제가 그 어여쁜 얼굴을 두 번 다시 보지 않게 되기를 하느님께 비옵니다."], ["May He bless and keep you!”", "하느님께서 당신을 축복하고 지켜주시기를!\""]], [["When I went to the station to meet Van Helsing I left the boys behind me.", "판 헬싱을 마중하러 역에 나갈 때 나는 젊은이들을 집에 남겨두고 갔다."], ["Poor Art seemed more cheerful than he has been since Lucy first took ill, and Quincey is more like his own bright self than he has been for many a long day.", "가엾은 아트는 루시가 처음 앓아누운 이래로 보지 못했던 만큼 한결 밝아 보였고, 퀸시도 오랜만에 본디의 명랑한 모습을 되찾은 듯했다."]], [["Van Helsing stepped from the carriage with the eager nimbleness of a boy.", "판 헬싱은 소년처럼 활기차고 날렵하게 객차에서 내렸다."], ["He saw me at once, and rushed up to me, saying:--", "그는 단번에 나를 알아보고 달려와 말했다."]], [["“Ah, friend John, how goes all? Well? So!", "\"아, 존, 다들 어떻게 지내나? 잘 지내고? 그래!"], ["I have been busy, for I come here to stay if need be.", "나는 분주했네, 필요하다면 여기 머물 작정으로 왔으니까."], ["All affairs are settled with me, and I have much to tell. Madam Mina is with you? Yes.", "내 일은 다 정리했고, 할 이야기도 많네. 미나 부인은 자네와 함께 있나? 그렇군."], ["And her so fine husband?", "그리고 그 훌륭한 남편도?"], ["And Arthur and my friend Quincey, they are with you, too? Good!”", "그리고 아서와 내 친구 퀸시도 함께 있나? 좋아!\""]], [["As I drove to the house I told him of what had passed, and of how my own diary had come to be of some use through Mrs. Harker’s suggestion; at which the Professor interrupted me:--", "집으로 마차를 몰고 가는 동안 나는 그에게 그간의 일을, 그리고 하커 부인의 제안 덕분에 내 일기가 어떻게 쓸모를 갖게 되었는지를 이야기했다. 그러자 교수가 내 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“Ah, that wonderful Madam Mina!", "\"아, 그 놀라운 미나 부인!"], ["She has man’s brain--a brain that a man should have were he much gifted--and a woman’s heart.", "그녀는 남자의 두뇌를 — 재능을 많이 타고난 남자라야 가질 법한 두뇌를 — 그리고 여자의 마음을 지녔네."], ["The good God fashioned her for a purpose, believe me, when He made that so good combination.", "자비로우신 하느님께서 그토록 훌륭한 조합을 만드실 때에는 어떤 목적이 있으셨던 게야, 내 말을 믿게."], ["Friend John, up to now fortune has made that woman of help to us; after to-night she must not have to do with this so terrible affair.", "존, 지금까지는 운이 그 부인을 우리에게 도움이 되게 해주었네. 그러나 오늘 밤 이후로 그녀는 이 끔찍한 일에 관여해서는 안 되네."], ["It is not good that she run a risk so great.", "그토록 큰 위험을 무릅쓰게 하는 건 좋지 않아."], ["We men are determined--nay, are we not pledged?--to destroy this monster; but it is no part for a woman.", "우리 남자들은 이 괴물을 없애기로 결심했네 — 아니, 우리가 맹세하지 않았는가? — 그러나 그것은 여자가 할 몫이 아니네."], ["Even if she be not harmed, her heart may fail her in so much and so many horrors; and hereafter she may suffer--both in waking, from her nerves, and in sleep, from her dreams.", "설령 그녀가 해를 입지 않는다 해도, 그토록 많고 그토록 숱한 공포 속에서 그녀의 심장이 견뎌내지 못할지도 모르네. 그리고 그 후에 그녀는 고통받을 수 있어 — 깨어 있을 때는 신경 때문에, 잠들었을 때는 꿈 때문에 말이야."], ["And, besides, she is young woman and not so long married; there may be other things to think of some time, if not now.", "게다가 그녀는 젊은 부인이고 결혼한 지도 얼마 되지 않았네. 지금은 아니더라도 언젠가는 생각해야 할 다른 일들이 있을지도 모르지."], ["You tell me she has wrote all, then she must consult with us; but to-morrow she say good-bye to this work, and we go alone.”", "자네 말로는 그녀가 모든 것을 다 기록했다 하니, 그렇다면 우리와 의논은 해야겠지. 그러나 내일 그녀는 이 일과 작별하고, 우리만 나아가야 하네.\""], ["I agreed heartily with him, and then I told him what we had found in his absence: that the house which Dracula had bought was the very next one to my own.", "나는 진심으로 그의 말에 동의했다. 그러고 나서 나는 그가 없는 동안 우리가 알아낸 것을 그에게 말해주었다. 드라큘라가 사들인 집이 바로 내 집 바로 옆집이라는 사실을."], ["He was amazed, and a great concern seemed to come on him.", "그는 깜짝 놀랐고, 큰 근심이 그를 덮치는 듯했다."], ["“Oh that we had known it before!”", "\"아, 그것을 진작 알았더라면!\""], ["he said, “for then we might have reached him in time to save poor Lucy.", "그가 말했다. \"그랬다면 우리가 제때 그에게 닿아 가엾은 루시를 구할 수 있었을 텐데."], ["However, ‘the milk that is spilt cries not out afterwards,’ as you say.", "하지만 자네들 말마따나 '이미 엎질러진 우유는 뒤늦게 울부짖지 않는 법'이지."], ["We shall not think of that, but go on our way to the end.”", "그것은 생각하지 말고, 끝까지 우리 길을 가세.\""], ["Then he fell into a silence that lasted till we entered my own gateway.", "그러고 나서 그는 침묵에 잠겼고, 그 침묵은 우리가 내 집 대문에 들어설 때까지 이어졌다."], ["Before we went to prepare for dinner he said to Mrs. Harker:--", "우리가 저녁식사를 준비하러 가기 전에 그는 하커 부인에게 말했다."]], [["“I am told, Madam Mina, by my friend John that you and your husband have put up in exact order all things that have been, up to this moment.”", "\"미나 부인, 내 친구 존에게서 들었소만, 부인과 부군께서 지금 이 순간까지 있었던 모든 일을 정확한 순서로 정리해 두셨다더군요.\""]], [["“Not up to this moment, Professor,”", "\"이 순간까지는 아니에요, 교수님.\""], ["she said impulsively, “but up to this morning.”", "그녀가 충동적으로 말했다. \"오늘 아침까지예요.\""]], [["“But why not up to now?", "\"하지만 어찌하여 지금까지가 아니오?"], ["We have seen hitherto how good light all the little things have made.", "우리는 지금까지 그 사소한 일들이 모두 얼마나 좋은 빛을 비춰주었는지 보아왔소."], ["We have told our secrets, and yet no one who has told is the worse for it.”", "우리는 우리의 비밀을 털어놓았고, 그러고도 털어놓은 사람 누구도 그로 인해 손해를 본 이가 없소.\""]], [["Mrs. Harker began to blush, and taking a paper from her pockets, she said:--", "하커 부인은 얼굴을 붉히기 시작했고, 주머니에서 종이 한 장을 꺼내며 말했다."]], [["“Dr. Van Helsing, will you read this, and tell me if it must go in.", "\"판 헬싱 박사님, 이것을 읽어보시고, 이것이 들어가야 하는지 말씀해 주세요."], ["It is my record of to-day.", "오늘 하루의 제 기록이에요."], ["I too have seen the need of putting down at present everything, however trivial; but there is little in this except what is personal.", "저 또한 지금으로서는 아무리 사소한 일이라도 모두 적어둘 필요가 있음을 알았어요. 하지만 이 안에는 개인적인 것 말고는 별것이 없어요."], ["Must it go in?”", "들어가야 할까요?\""], ["The Professor read it over gravely, and handed it back, saying:--", "교수는 그것을 진지하게 통독하더니 돌려주며 말했다."]], [["“It need not go in if you do not wish it; but I pray that it may.", "\"부인께서 원치 않으신다면 들어가지 않아도 되오. 하지만 나는 그것이 들어가기를 바라오."], ["It can but make your husband love you the more, and all us, your friends, more honour you--as well as more esteem and love.”", "그것은 부군께서 부인을 한층 더 사랑하게 만들 뿐이고, 부인의 벗인 우리 모두가 부인을 더욱 존경하게 — 아울러 더욱 존중하고 사랑하게 — 할 따름이오.\""], ["She took it back with another blush and a bright smile.", "그녀는 다시 한 번 얼굴을 붉히고 환하게 미소 지으며 그것을 도로 받았다."]], [["And so now, up to this very hour, all the records we have are complete and in order.", "이리하여 이제 바로 이 시각까지, 우리가 가진 모든 기록은 완전하게 순서대로 갖추어졌다."], ["The Professor took away one copy to study after dinner, and before our meeting, which is fixed for nine o’clock.", "교수는 한 부를 가져가, 저녁식사 후 그리고 아홉 시로 정해진 우리의 회합 전에 살펴보기로 했다."], ["The rest of us have already read everything; so when we meet in the study we shall all be informed as to facts, and can arrange our plan of battle with this terrible and mysterious enemy.", "나머지 우리는 모든 것을 이미 다 읽었다. 그러니 서재에서 모일 때면 우리 모두가 사실들을 알고 있을 것이고, 이 끔찍하고 알 수 없는 적과 맞설 우리의 전투 계획을 짤 수 있을 것이다."]], [["Mina Harker’s Journal.", "「미나 하커의 일기」"]], [["30 September.--When we met in Dr. Seward’s study two hours after dinner, which had been at six o’clock, we unconsciously formed a sort of board or committee.", "9월 30일. — 여섯 시에 있었던 저녁식사 두 시간 후 수어드 박사의 서재에 모였을 때, 우리는 자신도 모르는 사이에 일종의 위원회나 평의회 같은 것을 이루었다."], ["Professor Van Helsing took the head of the table, to which Dr. Seward motioned him as he came into the room.", "판 헬싱 교수가 식탁 상석에 앉았는데, 그가 방에 들어오자 수어드 박사가 그 자리로 안내한 것이었다."], ["He made me sit next to him on his right, and asked me to act as secretary; Jonathan sat next to me.", "교수는 나를 그의 오른쪽, 바로 옆자리에 앉히고 서기 역할을 맡아달라고 부탁했다."], ["Opposite us were Lord Godalming, Dr.", "조너선은 내 옆에 앉았다."], ["Seward, and Mr. Morris--Lord Godalming being next the Professor, and Dr. Seward in the centre.", "우리 맞은편에는 고달밍 경, 수어드 박사, 그리고 모리스 씨가 있었다 — 고달밍 경이 교수 옆이었고, 수어드 박사가 가운데였다."], ["The Professor said:--", "교수가 말했다."]], [["“I may, I suppose, take it that we are all acquainted with the facts that are in these papers.”", "\"여러분 모두 이 서류들에 담긴 사실들을 알고 계신 것으로 보아도 좋겠지요.\""], ["We all expressed assent, and he went on:--", "우리 모두가 동의를 표하자 그는 말을 이었다."]], [["“Then it were, I think good that I tell you something of the kind of enemy with which we have to deal.", "\"그렇다면 우리가 상대해야 할 적이 어떤 종류인지 여러분에게 조금 말씀드리는 게 좋겠습니다."], ["I shall then make known to you something of the history of this man, which has been ascertained for me. So we then can discuss how we shall act, and can take our measure according.", "그런 다음 나를 위해 확인된 이 사내의 내력을 조금 알려드리겠소. 그러고 나서 우리가 어떻게 행동할지 의논하고, 그에 따라 대비책을 세울 수 있을 것이오."]], [["“There are such beings as vampires; some of us have evidence that they exist.", "흡혈귀라는 존재가 실재하오. 우리 가운데 몇몇은 그들이 존재한다는 증거를 가지고 있소."], ["Even had we not the proof of our own unhappy experience, the teachings and the records of the past give proof enough for sane peoples.", "설령 우리 자신의 불행한 경험이라는 증거가 없다 하더라도, 과거의 가르침과 기록만으로도 제정신인 사람들에게는 충분한 증거가 되오. 처음에는 나도 회의적이었음을 인정하오. 만일 오랜 세월에 걸쳐 마음을 열어두도록 스스로를 단련하지 않았더라면, 나는 그 사실이 내 귀에 천둥처럼 울릴 그때까지 믿지 못했을 것이오. '보라! 보라!"], ["I admit that at the first I was sceptic.", "내가 증명하노라, 내가 증명하노라.' 아!"], ["Were it not that through long years I have train myself to keep an open mind, I could not have believe until such time as that fact thunder on my ear. ‘See! see!", "지금 아는 것을 처음부터 알았더라면 — 아니, 그에 대해 짐작이라도 했더라면 — 우리 가운데 그토록 그녀를 사랑했던 많은 이들에게 그 소중한 한 생명이 지켜졌을 것이오."], ["I prove; I prove.’ Alas!", "그러나 그것은 지나간 일이오."], ["Had I known at the first what now I know--nay, had I even guess at him--one so precious life had been spared to many of us who did love her.", "그러니 우리는 우리가 구할 수 있는 동안, 다른 가엾은 영혼들이 스러지지 않도록 힘써 일해야 하오. 노스페라투는 한 번 쏘면 죽고 마는 벌처럼 죽지 않소. 그는 다만 더 강해질 뿐이오."], ["But that is gone; and we must so work, that other poor souls perish not, whilst we can save.", "그리고 더 강해진 만큼 악을 행할 힘도 더 커지오. 우리 가운데 있는 이 흡혈귀는 그 자체로 장정 스무 명만큼의 힘을 지녔소. 그의 교활함은 인간을 넘어서니, 그것은 수백 년 세월의 산물이기 때문이오."], ["The nosferatu do not die like the bee when he sting once. He is only stronger; and being stronger, have yet more power to work evil.", "그는 여전히 강령술(降靈術)의 도움을 받고 있는데, 그 어원이 가리키듯 죽은 자를 통한 점복(占卜)이며, 그가 가까이 다가갈 수 있는 모든 죽은 자가 그의 명령 아래 있소. 그는 짐승이며, 짐승 이상이오."], ["This vampire which is amongst us is of himself so strong in person as twenty men; he is of cunning more than mortal, for his cunning be the growth of ages; he have still the aids of necromancy, which is, as his etymology imply, the divination by the dead, and all the dead that he can come nigh to are for him at command; he is brute, and more than brute; he is devil in callous, and the heart of him is not; he can, within limitations, appear at will when, and where, and in any of the forms that are to him; he can, within his range, direct the elements; the storm, the fog, the thunder; he can command all the meaner things: the rat, and the owl, and the bat--the moth, and the fox, and the wolf; he can grow and become small; and he can at times vanish and come unknown.", "그는 무정함에서 악마이며, 그에게는 심장이라는 것이 없소. 그는 제약 안에서나마 마음대로, 언제든, 어디든, 그가 취할 수 있는 어떤 형상으로든 나타날 수 있소. 그는 자기 영역 안에서 자연의 요소들을 — 폭풍, 안개, 천둥을 — 부릴 수 있소."], ["How then are we to begin our strike to destroy him?", "그는 더 하찮은 온갖 것들을, 즉 쥐와 올빼미와 박쥐를 — 나방과 여우와 늑대를 부릴 수 있소."], ["How shall we find his where; and having found it, how can we destroy?", "그는 커질 수도 작아질 수도 있소. 그리고 때로는 사라졌다가 누구도 모르게 나타날 수 있소."], ["My friends, this is much; it is a terrible task that we undertake, and there may be consequence to make the brave shudder.", "그렇다면 우리는 그를 없애기 위한 우리의 공격을 어떻게 시작해야 하겠소? 그가 있는 곳을 어떻게 찾아내며, 또 찾아낸 뒤에 어떻게 없앨 수 있겠소? 벗들이여, 이것은 큰일이오."], ["For if we fail in this our fight he must surely win; and then where end we? Life is nothings; I heed him not.", "우리가 떠맡는 것은 끔찍한 과업이며, 용감한 자조차 몸서리치게 할 결과가 따를 수도 있소. 이 싸움에서 우리가 실패하면 그가 반드시 이길 것이고, 그러면 우리는 어떻게 되겠소?"], ["But to fail here, is not mere life or death.", "목숨이야 아무것도 아니오, 나는 그것을 개의치 않소."], ["It is that we become as him; that we henceforward become foul things of the night like him--without heart or conscience, preying on the bodies and the souls of those we love best.", "그러나 여기서 실패하는 것은 단순히 삶이냐 죽음이냐의 문제가 아니오. 그것은 우리가 그와 같이 되는 것이오. 우리가 이후로 그처럼 밤의 추악한 존재가 되는 것이오 — 심장도 양심도 없이, 우리가 가장 사랑하는 이들의 육신과 영혼을 먹잇감으로 삼으면서 말이오."], ["To us for ever are the gates of heaven shut; for who shall open them to us again?", "우리에게 천국의 문은 영원히 닫힐 것이오. 누가 우리를 위해 그 문을 다시 열어주겠소?"], ["We go on for all time abhorred by all; a blot on the face of God’s sunshine; an arrow in the side of Him who died for man.", "우리는 영원토록 모두에게 혐오받으며 살아갈 것이오. 하느님의 햇빛 위에 묻은 오점이요, 인간을 위해 죽으신 그분의 옆구리에 박힌 화살이 되는 것이오."], ["But we are face to face with duty; and in such case must we shrink?", "그러나 우리는 의무와 얼굴을 맞대고 있소. 이런 경우에 우리가 움츠러들어야 하겠소?"], ["For me, I say, no; but then I am old, and life, with his sunshine, his fair places, his song of birds, his music and his love, lie far behind.", "나로 말하면, 아니라고 하겠소. 하기야 나는 늙었고, 햇빛과 아름다운 곳들과 새들의 노래와 음악과 사랑이 있는 삶은 이미 저 뒤로 멀어졌소."], ["You others are young.", "여러분 다른 이들은 젊소."], ["Some have seen sorrow; but there are fair days yet in store. What say you?”", "더러는 슬픔을 겪었으나, 앞으로 누릴 좋은 날들이 아직 남아 있소. 여러분은 무어라 답하시겠소?\""]], [["Whilst he was speaking, Jonathan had taken my hand.", "그가 말하는 동안 조너선이 내 손을 잡았다."], ["I feared, oh so much, that the appalling nature of our danger was overcoming him when I saw his hand stretch out; but it was life to me to feel its touch--so strong, so self-reliant, so resolute.", "그의 손이 뻗어오는 것을 보았을 때, 아, 나는 우리가 처한 위험의 끔찍한 본질이 그를 압도하고 있는 것은 아닐까 너무도 두려웠다. 그러나 그 손길을 느끼는 것은 나에게 생명과도 같았다 — 그토록 강하고, 그토록 자신에 차 있고, 그토록 단호한 손길이었다."], ["A brave man’s hand can speak for itself; it does not even need a woman’s love to hear its music.", "용감한 사내의 손은 그 자체로 말을 할 수 있으니, 그 음악을 듣는 데에는 여자의 사랑조차 필요치 않은 것이다."]], [["When the Professor had done speaking my husband looked in my eyes, and I in his; there was no need for speaking between us.", "교수가 말을 마쳤을 때 남편이 내 눈을 들여다보았고, 나도 그의 눈을 들여다보았다. 우리 사이에는 말이 필요 없었다."]], [["“I answer for Mina and myself,”", "\"미나와 저 자신을 대신해 답하겠습니다.\""], ["he said.", "그가 말했다."]], [["“Count me in, Professor,”", "\"저도 끼워 주십시오, 교수님.\""], ["said Mr. Quincey Morris, laconically as usual.", "퀸시 모리스 씨가 여느 때처럼 간결하게 말했다."]], [["“I am with you,”", "\"저도 함께하겠습니다.\""], ["said Lord Godalming, “for Lucy’s sake, if for no other reason.”", "고달밍 경이 말했다. \"다른 이유가 없더라도, 루시를 위해서요.\""]], [["Dr. Seward simply nodded.", "수어드 박사는 그저 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["The Professor stood up and, after laying his golden crucifix on the table, held out his hand on either side.", "교수는 일어서더니 황금 십자고상을 식탁에 내려놓고 양옆으로 손을 내밀었다."], ["I took his right hand, and Lord Godalming his left; Jonathan held my right with his left and stretched across to Mr.", "나는 그의 오른손을, 고달밍 경은 그의 왼손을 잡았다. 조너선은 왼손으로 내 오른손을 잡고 모리스 씨에게로 팔을 뻗었다."], ["Morris. So as we all took hands our solemn compact was made.", "이렇게 우리 모두가 손을 맞잡으면서 우리의 엄숙한 맹약이 이루어졌다."], ["I felt my heart icy cold, but it did not even occur to me to draw back.", "나는 심장이 얼음처럼 차가워지는 것을 느꼈으나, 물러서리라는 생각은 떠오르지조차 않았다."], ["We resumed our places, and Dr. Van Helsing went on with a sort of cheerfulness which showed that the serious work had begun.", "우리는 다시 자리에 앉았고, 판 헬싱 박사는 일종의 명랑함을 띠고 말을 이어갔는데, 그것은 진지한 작업이 시작되었음을 보여주는 것이었다."], ["It was to be taken as gravely, and in as businesslike a way, as any other transaction of life:--", "이 일은 삶의 다른 어떤 거래와 마찬가지로 진지하게, 또 사무적인 방식으로 다루어져야 했다."]], [["“Well, you know what we have to contend against; but we, too, are not without strength.", "\"자, 우리가 무엇과 맞서 싸워야 하는지 여러분은 아셨소. 그러나 우리 또한 힘이 없지 않소."], ["We have on our side power of combination--a power denied to the vampire kind; we have sources of science; we are free to act and think; and the hours of the day and the night are ours equally.", "우리에게는 결속의 힘이 있는데 — 이는 흡혈귀 족속에게는 허락되지 않은 힘이오. 우리에게는 과학의 자원이 있소. 우리는 자유로이 행동하고 사고할 수 있소."], ["In fact, so far as our powers extend, they are unfettered, and we are free to use them.", "그리고 낮의 시간도 밤의 시간도 똑같이 우리의 것이오. 사실 우리의 힘이 미치는 한, 그 힘에는 족쇄가 없으며, 우리는 그것을 자유로이 쓸 수 있소."], ["We have self-devotion in a cause, and an end to achieve which is not a selfish one.", "우리에게는 하나의 대의에 헌신하는 마음이 있고, 이뤄야 할 목적이 있는데 그것은 이기적인 것이 아니오."], ["These things are much.", "이런 것들은 큰 힘이오."]], [["“Now let us see how far the general powers arrayed against us are restrict, and how the individual cannot.", "이제 우리에게 맞선 그 일반적인 힘들이 어디까지 제약을 받는지, 또 그 개인이 어떻게 그렇지 못한지를 살펴봅시다."], ["In fine, let us consider the limitations of the vampire in general, and of this one in particular.", "요컨대, 흡혈귀 일반의 제약과, 특히 이 흡혈귀의 제약을 헤아려봅시다."]], [["“All we have to go upon are traditions and superstitions.", "우리가 의지할 것이라곤 전승과 미신뿐이오."], ["These do not at the first appear much, when the matter is one of life and death--nay of more than either life or death.", "이런 것들은 처음에는 대단해 보이지 않소, 더구나 생사의 문제일 때에는 — 아니, 삶이냐 죽음이냐를 넘어서는 문제일 때에는 말이오."], ["Yet must we be satisfied; in the first place because we have to be--no other means is at our control--and secondly, because, after all, these things--tradition and superstition--are everything.", "그러나 우리는 그것으로 만족해야 하오. 첫째로는 그래야만 하기 때문이오 — 우리가 마음대로 쓸 다른 수단이 없으니 말이오 — 둘째로는, 따지고 보면 이런 것들이 — 전승과 미신이 — 전부이기 때문이오."], ["Does not the belief in vampires rest for others--though not, alas!", "흡혈귀에 대한 믿음이 — 안타깝게도 우리에게는 아니지만!"], ["for us--on them? A year ago which of us would have received such a possibility, in the midst of our scientific, sceptical, matter-of-fact nineteenth century?", "— 다른 이들에게는 바로 그것들 위에 놓여 있지 않소? 일 년 전이라면 우리 가운데 누가 이 과학적이고 회의적이며 사실 위주인 19세기 한복판에서 그런 가능성을 받아들였겠소?"], ["We even scouted a belief that we saw justified under our very eyes.", "우리는 우리 눈앞에서 입증된 믿음마저 비웃었소."], ["Take it, then, that the vampire, and the belief in his limitations and his cure, rest for the moment on the same base.", "그러니 흡혈귀라는 것과, 그의 제약과 그를 처치하는 법에 대한 믿음이 지금으로서는 같은 토대 위에 놓여 있다고 받아들입시다."], ["For, let me tell you, he is known everywhere that men have been.", "한 가지 말해두겠소, 그는 인간이 살아온 곳이라면 어디서나 알려져 있소."], ["In old Greece, in old Rome; he flourish in Germany all over, in France, in India, even in the Chernosese; and in China, so far from us in all ways, there even is he, and the peoples fear him at this day.", "옛 그리스에서, 옛 로마에서, 그는 독일 전역에서, 프랑스에서, 인도에서, 심지어 케르소네소스에서도 번성하오. 그리고 우리에게서 모든 면에서 그토록 먼 중국에서도 그는 있으며, 사람들은 오늘날까지 그를 두려워하오."], ["He have follow the wake of the berserker Icelander, the devil-begotten Hun, the Slav, the Saxon, the Magyar.", "그는 광포한 아이슬란드 전사, 악마가 낳은 훈족, 슬라브족, 색슨족, 마자르족의 뒤를 따라왔소."], ["So far, then, we have all we may act upon; and let me tell you that very much of the beliefs are justified by what we have seen in our own so unhappy experience.", "그러니 여기까지가 우리가 의지해 행동할 수 있는 전부이며, 한 가지 말해두겠는데, 그 믿음의 매우 많은 부분이 우리 자신의 그 불행한 경험에서 본 것으로 입증되오."], ["The vampire live on, and cannot die by mere passing of the time; he can flourish when that he can fatten on the blood of the living.", "흡혈귀는 계속 살아가며, 단지 세월이 흐른다고 죽지 않소. 그는 산 자의 피로 살을 찌울 수 있을 때 번성하오."], ["Even more, we have seen amongst us that he can even grow younger; that his vital faculties grow strenuous, and seem as though they refresh themselves when his special pabulum is plenty.", "더 나아가, 우리는 우리 가운데서 그가 심지어 젊어질 수도 있음을 보았소. 그의 특별한 양식이 넉넉할 때면 그의 생명력이 왕성해지고, 마치 스스로 새로워지는 듯이 보인다는 것을 말이오."], ["But he cannot flourish without this diet; he eat not as others.", "그러나 그는 이 양식 없이는 번성하지 못하오. 그는 다른 이들처럼 먹지 않소."], ["Even friend Jonathan, who lived with him for weeks, did never see him to eat, never!", "몇 주를 그와 함께 지낸 우리 벗 조너선조차 그가 먹는 것을 한 번도 보지 못했소, 결코!"], ["He throws no shadow; he make in the mirror no reflect, as again Jonathan observe.", "그는 그림자를 드리우지 않소. 조너선이 또한 관찰했듯이, 그는 거울에 비치지 않소."], ["He has the strength of many of his hand--witness again Jonathan when he shut the door against the wolfs, and when he help him from the diligence too.", "그는 한 손에 여러 사람 몫의 힘을 지녔소 — 조너선이 그가 늑대들을 막으려고 문을 닫았을 때, 또 역마차에서 그가 그를 거들었을 때를 다시 증언으로 삼으시오."], ["He can transform himself to wolf, as we gather from the ship arrival in Whitby, when he tear open the dog; he can be as bat, as Madam Mina saw him on the window at Whitby, and as friend John saw him fly from this so near house, and as my friend Quincey saw him at the window of Miss Lucy.", "그는 늑대로 둔갑할 수 있소, 휘트비에 배가 닿았을 때 그가 개를 갈가리 찢었던 데서 우리가 짐작하듯이 말이오. 그는 박쥐가 될 수 있소, 휘트비에서 미나 부인이 창가에서 그를 본 것처럼, 또 우리 벗 존이 이 바로 옆집에서 그가 날아가는 것을 본 것처럼, 또 내 벗 퀸시가 루시 양의 창가에서 그를 본 것처럼 말이오."], ["He can come in mist which he create--that noble ship’s captain proved him of this; but, from what we know, the distance he can make this mist is limited, and it can only be round himself.", "그는 스스로 만들어낸 안개에 싸여 올 수 있소 — 저 고귀한 선장이 이를 입증해 주었소. 그러나 우리가 아는 바로는, 그가 이 안개를 만들 수 있는 거리는 한정되어 있고, 그것은 오직 자기 주위에만 미칠 뿐이오."], ["He come on moonlight rays as elemental dust--as again Jonathan saw those sisters in the castle of Dracula.", "그는 달빛을 타고 원소의 티끌처럼 올 수 있소 — 조너선이 드라큘라 성에서 그 자매들을 본 것처럼 또한 말이오."], ["He become so small--we ourselves saw Miss Lucy, ere she was at peace, slip through a hairbreadth space at the tomb door.", "그는 그토록 작아질 수 있소 — 우리 자신이 루시 양이 안식에 들기 전에 무덤 문에서 머리카락 한 올만 한 틈으로 빠져나가는 것을 보았소."], ["He can, when once he find his way, come out from anything or into anything, no matter how close it be bound or even fused up with fire--solder you call it.", "그는 일단 길을 찾기만 하면, 아무리 단단히 봉해졌든, 심지어 불로 녹여 붙여졌든 — 여러분이 땜질이라 부르는 것 말이오 — 무엇에서든 나올 수 있고 무엇으로든 들어갈 수 있소."], ["He can see in the dark--no small power this, in a world which is one half shut from the light. Ah, but hear me through.", "그는 어둠 속에서 볼 수 있소 — 절반이 빛에서 닫혀 있는 이 세상에서 이는 작지 않은 힘이오. 아, 그러나 내 말을 끝까지 들으시오."], ["He can do all these things, yet he is not free.", "그는 이 모든 것을 할 수 있으나, 그렇다고 자유롭지는 않소."], ["Nay; he is even more prisoner than the slave of the galley, than the madman in his cell.", "아니, 그는 갤리선의 노예보다도, 감방에 갇힌 광인보다도 더한 죄수요. 그는 마음 내키는 대로 갈 수 없소."], ["He cannot go where he lists; he who is not of nature has yet to obey some of nature’s laws--why we know not.", "자연에 속하지 않은 그가 그럼에도 자연의 법칙 가운데 몇 가지에는 복종해야 하오 — 어째서인지는 우리도 모르오."], ["He may not enter anywhere at the first, unless there be some one of the household who bid him to come; though afterwards he can come as he please.", "그는 처음에는 어디에도 들어갈 수 없소, 그 집안의 누군가가 그에게 들어오라고 청하지 않는 한 말이오. 하지만 그다음부터는 마음대로 드나들 수 있소."], ["His power ceases, as does that of all evil things, at the coming of the day.", "그의 힘은 모든 악한 것들의 힘이 그러하듯 날이 밝아오면 멈추오."], ["Only at certain times can he have limited freedom.", "오직 특정한 때에만 그는 한정된 자유를 누릴 수 있소."], ["If he be not at the place whither he is bound, he can only change himself at noon or at exact sunrise or sunset.", "그가 매여 있는 곳에 있지 않을 때에는, 정오나 정확한 일출 혹은 일몰의 순간에만 자신의 모습을 바꿀 수 있소."], ["These things are we told, and in this record of ours we have proof by inference.", "이런 것들을 우리는 전해 들었고, 우리의 이 기록에서 우리는 추론으로 그 증거를 가지고 있소."], ["Thus, whereas he can do as he will within his limit, when he have his earth-home, his coffin-home, his hell-home, the place unhallowed, as we saw when he went to the grave of the suicide at Whitby; still at other time he can only change when the time come.", "그리하여, 그가 자기 흙의 집, 관(棺)의 집, 지옥의 집, 즉 신성을 박탈당한 그 장소를 가지고 있을 때에는 — 휘트비에서 그가 자살자의 무덤으로 갔을 때 우리가 보았듯이 — 자기 제약 안에서 마음대로 할 수 있는 반면, 그 밖의 때에는 오로지 그 시각이 와야만 모습을 바꿀 수 있소. 또한 그는 조류가 멈춘 때나 만조일 때에만 흐르는 물을 건널 수 있다고도 하오."], ["It is said, too, that he can only pass running water at the slack or the flood of the tide.", "그리고 그를 너무도 괴롭혀 무력하게 만드는 것들이 있는데, 우리가 아는 마늘이 그러하오."], ["Then there are things which so afflict him that he has no power, as the garlic that we know of; and as for things sacred, as this symbol, my crucifix, that was amongst us even now when we resolve, to them he is nothing, but in their presence he take his place far off and silent with respect.", "그리고 신성한 것들로 말하면, 우리가 결의할 때 바로 지금 우리 가운데 있었던 이 상징, 내 십자고상 같은 것에 대해 그는 아무것도 아니지만, 그것들이 있는 자리에서는 멀리 떨어져 조용히 경의를 표하며 자리할 따름이오. 또 다른 것들도 있는데, 우리가 그를 찾는 중에 필요할지 모르니 여러분에게 일러두겠소."], ["There are others, too, which I shall tell you of, lest in our seeking we may need them.", "그의 관 위에 놓인 들장미 가지는 그가 거기서 움직이지 못하게 하오."], ["The branch of wild rose on his coffin keep him that he move not from it; a sacred bullet fired into the coffin kill him so that he be true dead; and as for the stake through him, we know already of its peace; or the cut-off head that giveth rest.", "관 속에 쏘아 넣은 신성한 총알은 그를 죽여 참된 죽음에 이르게 하오. 그리고 그를 꿰뚫는 말뚝으로 말하면, 우리는 이미 그것이 가져다주는 안식을 알고 있소. 또 안식을 주는 잘린 머리도 있소."], ["We have seen it with our eyes.", "우리는 우리 눈으로 그것을 보았소."]], [["“Thus when we find the habitation of this man-that-was, we can confine him to his coffin and destroy him, if we obey what we know.", "그리하여 한때 사람이었던 이 자의 거처를 찾아내면, 우리가 아는 것에 따른다면 그를 관에 가두고 없앨 수 있소. 그러나 그는 영리하오."], ["But he is clever. I have asked my friend Arminius, of Buda-Pesth University, to make his record; and, from all the means that are, he tell me of what he has been.", "나는 부다페스트 대학의 내 벗 아르미니우스에게 그의 기록을 작성해 달라고 부탁했소. 그리고 그는 있는 온갖 자료로부터, 이 사내가 어떤 자였는지를 나에게 일러주었소."], ["He must, indeed, have been that Voivode Dracula who won his name against the Turk, over the great river on the very frontier of Turkey-land.", "그는 분명, 터키 땅의 바로 그 변경에서 큰 강 건너 터키군을 상대로 이름을 떨친 그 영주 드라큘라였을 것이오."], ["If it be so, then was he no common man; for in that time, and for centuries after, he was spoken of as the cleverest and the most cunning, as well as the bravest of the sons of the ‘land beyond the forest.’ That mighty brain and that iron resolution went with him to his grave, and are even now arrayed against us.", "만일 그렇다면 그는 예사 사람이 아니었소. 그 시대에, 그리고 이후 수백 년 동안 그는 '숲 너머의 땅'의 아들들 가운데 가장 영리하고 가장 교활하며, 또한 가장 용감한 자로 일컬어졌기 때문이오. 그 강력한 두뇌와 그 무쇠 같은 결단이 그와 함께 무덤으로 갔고, 지금도 우리에게 맞서 도열해 있소."], ["The Draculas were, says Arminius, a great and noble race, though now and again were scions who were held by their coevals to have had dealings with the Evil One.", "아르미니우스의 말로는, 드라큘라 가문은 위대하고 고귀한 혈통이었으나, 이따금 동시대인들로부터 악마와 거래했다고 여겨진 자손들이 나오기도 했다 하오."], ["They learned his secrets in the Scholomance, amongst the mountains over Lake Hermanstadt, where the devil claims the tenth scholar as his due.", "그들은 헤르만슈타트 호수 위 산중에 있는 스콜로만체에서 악마의 비밀을 배웠는데, 그곳에서는 악마가 열 번째 학생을 자기 몫으로 가져가오."], ["In the records are such words as ‘stregoica’--witch, ‘ordog,’ and ‘pokol’--Satan and hell; and in one manuscript this very Dracula is spoken of as ‘wampyr,’ which we all understand too well.", "기록에는 '스트레고이카' — 마녀, '오르도그'와 '포콜' — 사탄과 지옥 같은 말들이 있고, 어느 필사본에서는 바로 이 드라큘라가 '밤피르'로 일컬어지는데, 우리 모두 그 뜻을 너무도 잘 아오."], ["There have been from the loins of this very one great men and good women, and their graves make sacred the earth where alone this foulness can dwell.", "바로 이자의 혈통에서 위대한 사내들과 선량한 여인들이 나왔고, 그들의 무덤이 이 추악함이 깃들 수 있는 유일한 땅을 성스럽게 만드오."], ["For it is not the least of its terrors that this evil thing is rooted deep in all good; in soil barren of holy memories it cannot rest.”", "이 악한 것이 온갖 선한 것에 깊이 뿌리내리고 있다는 것이야말로 그 공포 가운데 결코 작지 않은 것이니, 거룩한 기억이 메마른 토양에서는 그가 안식할 수 없기 때문이오.\""]], [["Whilst they were talking Mr. Morris was looking steadily at the window, and he now got up quietly, and went out of the room.", "그들이 이야기하는 동안 모리스 씨는 줄곧 창문을 뚫어지게 바라보고 있었는데, 이제 그가 조용히 일어나 방을 나갔다."], ["There was a little pause, and then the Professor went on:--", "잠시 사이를 두었다가 교수가 말을 이었다."]], [["“And now we must settle what we do.", "\"이제 우리는 무엇을 할지 정해야 하오."], ["We have here much data, and we must proceed to lay out our campaign.", "우리에게는 여기 많은 자료가 있고, 우리는 우리의 작전을 짜는 데로 나아가야 하오."], ["We know from the inquiry of Jonathan that from the castle to Whitby came fifty boxes of earth, all of which were delivered at Carfax; we also know that at least some of these boxes have been removed.", "우리는 조너선의 조사를 통해 성에서 휘트비로 흙이 든 상자 쉰 개가 왔고, 그것들이 모두 카팩스로 인도되었음을 알고 있소. 또한 그 상자들 가운데 적어도 일부는 옮겨졌음을 알고 있소."], ["It seems to me, that our first step should be to ascertain whether all the rest remain in the house beyond that wall where we look to-day; or whether any more have been removed.", "내 생각에 우리의 첫걸음은, 나머지 전부가 우리가 오늘 바라본 저 담장 너머 그 집 안에 남아 있는지, 아니면 그 이상이 옮겨졌는지를 확인하는 것이어야 하오."], ["If the latter, we must trace----”", "만일 후자라면, 우리는 추적해야 하오 ——\""]], [["Here we were interrupted in a very startling way.", "여기서 우리는 매우 놀라운 방식으로 말이 끊겼다."], ["Outside the house came the sound of a pistol-shot; the glass of the window was shattered with a bullet, which, ricochetting from the top of the embrasure, struck the far wall of the room.", "집 밖에서 권총 발사음이 들렸다. 창유리가 총알에 산산이 부서졌고, 그 총알은 창문턱 위쪽에서 튕겨나가 방의 먼 벽에 부딪혔다."], ["I am afraid I am at heart a coward, for I shrieked out.", "아무래도 나는 마음속으로 겁쟁이인 모양으로, 비명을 질렀다."], ["The men all jumped to their feet; Lord Godalming flew over to the window and threw up the sash.", "남자들은 모두 벌떡 일어섰고, 고달밍 경이 창문으로 날듯이 달려가 미닫이창을 들어 올렸다."], ["As he did so we heard Mr. Morris’s voice without:--", "그가 그렇게 하는 동안 밖에서 모리스 씨의 목소리가 들렸다."]], [["“Sorry! I fear I have alarmed you.", "\"미안합니다! 여러분을 놀라게 한 것 같군요."], ["I shall come in and tell you about it.”", "안으로 들어가 자초지종을 말씀드리겠습니다.\""], ["A minute later he came in and said:--", "잠시 후 그가 들어와 말했다."]], [["“It was an idiotic thing of me to do, and I ask your pardon, Mrs.", "\"제가 어리석은 짓을 했습니다, 하커 부인, 진심으로 용서를 빕니다."], ["Harker, most sincerely; I fear I must have frightened you terribly.", "부인을 무척이나 놀라게 해드린 것 같군요."], ["But the fact is that whilst the Professor was talking there came a big bat and sat on the window-sill.", "하지만 실은, 교수님이 말씀하시는 동안 커다란 박쥐 한 마리가 와서 창문턱에 앉았던 겁니다."], ["I have got such a horror of the damned brutes from recent events that I cannot stand them, and I went out to have a shot, as I have been doing of late of evenings, whenever I have seen one.", "요즈음 일들 탓에 그 망할 짐승들에 대한 공포가 어찌나 깊어졌는지 도무지 견딜 수가 없어서, 밖으로 나가 한 방 쏘려 했던 거지요. 요사이 저녁마다 한 마리라도 보이면 그래 왔거든요."], ["You used to laugh at me for it then, Art.”", "그때 자네는 그것 때문에 나를 비웃곤 했지, 아트.\""]], [["“Did you hit it?”", "\"맞혔나?\""], ["asked Dr. Van Helsing.", "판 헬싱 박사가 물었다."]], [["“I don’t know; I fancy not, for it flew away into the wood.”", "\"모르겠습니다. 못 맞힌 것 같아요, 숲속으로 날아가 버렸거든요.\""], ["Without saying any more he took his seat, and the Professor began to resume his statement:--", "그는 더 말하지 않고 자리에 앉았고, 교수가 다시 자신의 설명을 이어가기 시작했다."]], [["“We must trace each of these boxes; and when we are ready, we must either capture or kill this monster in his lair; or we must, so to speak, sterilise the earth, so that no more he can seek safety in it.", "\"우리는 이 상자들을 하나하나 추적해야 하오. 그리고 준비가 되면, 우리는 이 괴물을 그 소굴에서 사로잡거나 죽이거나 해야 하오. 아니면, 말하자면 그 흙을 멸균해서 그가 더 이상 거기서 안전을 구하지 못하게 해야 하오."], ["Thus in the end we may find him in his form of man between the hours of noon and sunset, and so engage with him when he is at his most weak.", "그리하여 마침내 우리는 정오와 일몰 사이의 시각에 사람의 모습을 한 그를 찾아내, 그가 가장 약할 때 그와 맞붙을 수 있을 것이오."]], [["“And now for you, Madam Mina, this night is the end until all be well.", "그리고 이제 미나 부인, 부인에게는 모든 일이 잘 풀릴 때까지 오늘 밤이 마지막이오."], ["You are too precious to us to have such risk.", "부인은 우리에게 너무도 소중하여 그런 위험을 무릅쓰게 할 수 없소."], ["When we part to-night, you no more must question.", "오늘 밤 우리가 헤어지고 나면, 부인은 더 이상 묻지 마시오."], ["We shall tell you all in good time.", "우리가 때가 되면 모든 것을 말씀드리겠소."], ["We are men and are able to bear; but you must be our star and our hope, and we shall act all the more free that you are not in the danger, such as we are.”", "우리는 남자들이고 견딜 수 있소. 그러나 부인은 우리의 별이요 우리의 희망이 되어주셔야 하오. 그리고 부인이 우리처럼 위험에 처해 있지 않다면 우리는 한결 자유로이 행동할 수 있을 것이오.\""]], [["All the men, even Jonathan, seemed relieved; but it did not seem to me good that they should brave danger and, perhaps, lessen their safety--strength being the best safety--through care of me; but their minds were made up, and, though it was a bitter pill for me to swallow, I could say nothing, save to accept their chivalrous care of me.", "남자들 모두가, 심지어 조너선조차 안도하는 듯했다. 그러나 나로서는 그들이 나를 염려하느라 위험을 무릅쓰고, 어쩌면 — 힘이야말로 최선의 안전인데 — 그들의 안전을 떨어뜨리는 것이 좋게 여겨지지 않았다. 그러나 그들의 결심은 굳었고, 비록 나에게는 삼키기 쓰라린 알약이었으나, 나는 나를 향한 그들의 기사도적인 보살핌을 받아들이는 것 말고는 아무 말도 할 수 없었다."]], [["Mr. Morris resumed the discussion:--", "모리스 씨가 다시 논의를 이어갔다."]], [["“As there is no time to lose, I vote we have a look at his house right now.", "\"지체할 시간이 없으니, 저는 지금 당장 그의 집을 살펴보자는 데 한 표 던지겠습니다."], ["Time is everything with him; and swift action on our part may save another victim.”", "그에게는 시간이 전부입니다. 우리 쪽의 신속한 행동이 또 다른 희생자를 구할 수도 있고요.\""]], [["I own that my heart began to fail me when the time for action came so close, but I did not say anything, for I had a greater fear that if I appeared as a drag or a hindrance to their work, they might even leave me out of their counsels altogether.", "행동의 때가 그토록 가까이 다가오자 내 마음이 약해지기 시작했음을 나는 인정한다. 그러나 나는 아무 말도 하지 않았으니, 만일 내가 그들의 일에 걸림돌이나 방해물로 비친다면, 그들이 아예 나를 그들의 의논에서 빼버릴지도 모른다는 더 큰 두려움이 있었기 때문이다."], ["They have now gone off to Carfax, with means to get into the house.", "그들은 이제 집 안으로 들어갈 수단을 갖추고 카팩스로 떠났다."]], [["Manlike, they had told me to go to bed and sleep; as if a woman can sleep when those she loves are in danger!", "남자들답게, 그들은 나에게 자리에 들어 잠을 자라고 일렀다. 마치 사랑하는 이들이 위험에 처해 있는데 여자가 잠을 잘 수 있기라도 한 것처럼!"], ["I shall lie down and pretend to sleep, lest Jonathan have added anxiety about me when he returns.", "나는 누워서 자는 척하려 한다. 조너선이 돌아왔을 때 나로 인해 근심이 더해지지 않도록 말이다."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["1 October, 4 a. m.--Just as we were about to leave the house, an urgent message was brought to me from Renfield to know if I would see him at once, as he had something of the utmost importance to say to me.", "10월 1일, 새벽 4시. — 우리가 막 집을 나서려 할 때, 렌필드로부터 나에게 급한 전갈이 왔는데, 더없이 중요한 할 말이 있으니 곧바로 자기를 만나 줄 수 있겠느냐는 것이었다."], ["I told the messenger to say that I would attend to his wishes in the morning; I was busy just at the moment.", "나는 전갈을 가져온 이에게, 아침에 그의 바람을 들어주겠다고, 지금은 마침 바쁘다고 전하라 일렀다."], ["The attendant added:--", "간병인이 덧붙였다."]], [["“He seems very importunate, sir.", "\"몹시 간곡하게 매달립니다, 선생님."], ["I have never seen him so eager.", "그가 저렇게 애가 단 모습은 처음 봅니다."], ["I don’t know but what, if you don’t see him soon, he will have one of his violent fits.”", "곧 만나주시지 않으면 발작을 한바탕 일으키지나 않을지 모르겠습니다.\" 나는 그 사람이 까닭 없이 이런 말을 할 리 없음을 알았으므로 말했다."], ["I knew the man would not have said this without some cause, so I said: “All right; I’ll go now”; and I asked the others to wait a few minutes for me, as I had to go and see my “patient.”", "\"알았네, 지금 가지.\" 그러고는 다른 이들에게 잠깐만 나를 기다려 달라고 청했다. 가서 내 '환자'를 만나봐야 한다고 말이다."]], [["“Take me with you, friend John,”", "\"나도 데려가 주게, 존.\""], ["said the Professor.", "교수가 말했다."], ["“His case in your diary interest me much, and it had bearing, too, now and again on our case.", "\"자네 일기에 적힌 그의 사례가 나에게 무척 흥미로운데, 그것이 이따금 우리 사례와도 관련이 있었지."], ["I should much like to see him, and especial when his mind is disturbed.”", "나는 그를 꼭 보고 싶네, 더구나 그의 정신이 흐트러져 있을 때라면 말이야.\""]], [["“May I come also?”", "\"저도 가도 되겠습니까?\""], ["asked Lord Godalming.", "고달밍 경이 물었다."]], [["“Me too?”", "\"저도요?\""], ["said Quincey Morris.", "퀸시 모리스가 말했다."], ["“May I come?”", "\"저도 가도 될까요?\""], ["said Harker.", "하커가 말했다."], ["I nodded, and we all went down the passage together.", "나는 고개를 끄덕였고, 우리 모두 함께 복도를 내려갔다."]], [["We found him in a state of considerable excitement, but far more rational in his speech and manner than I had ever seen him.", "우리는 그가 상당히 흥분한 상태에 있는 것을 보았으나, 그의 말과 태도는 내가 여태껏 본 어느 때보다 훨씬 이성적이었다."], ["There was an unusual understanding of himself, which was unlike anything I had ever met with in a lunatic; and he took it for granted that his reasons would prevail with others entirely sane.", "그에게는 자기 자신에 대한 보기 드문 이해가 있었는데, 그것은 내가 광인에게서 한 번도 본 적 없는 것이었다. 그리고 그는 자신의 논리가 완전히 제정신인 다른 이들에게도 통하리라는 것을 당연하게 여겼다."], ["We all four went into the room, but none of the others at first said anything.", "우리 넷이 모두 방에 들어갔으나, 처음에는 다른 이들 누구도 아무 말 하지 않았다."], ["His request was that I would at once release him from the asylum and send him home.", "그의 청은, 내가 당장 그를 병원에서 풀어주어 집으로 보내달라는 것이었다."], ["This he backed up with arguments regarding his complete recovery, and adduced his own existing sanity.", "그는 자신이 완전히 회복되었다는 논거를 들어 이를 뒷받침했고, 현재의 자기 정신이 멀쩡함을 그 증거로 댔다."], ["“I appeal to your friends,”", "\"여러분의 벗들께 호소합니다.\""], ["he said, “they will, perhaps, not mind sitting in judgment on my case.", "그가 말했다. \"이분들께서는 어쩌면 제 사례를 판단해 주시는 것을 마다하지 않으시겠지요."], ["By the way, you have not introduced me.”", "그런데 박사님, 아직 저를 소개해 주지 않으셨군요.\""], ["I was so much astonished, that the oddness of introducing a madman in an asylum did not strike me at the moment; and, besides, there was a certain dignity in the man’s manner, so much of the habit of equality, that I at once made the introduction: “Lord Godalming; Professor Van Helsing; Mr.", "나는 어찌나 놀랐던지, 정신병원에서 광인을 소개한다는 기이함이 그 순간에는 떠오르지조차 않았다. 게다가 그 사내의 태도에는 어떤 위엄이, 대등함이 몸에 밴 듯한 면모가 너무도 짙어, 나는 곧바로 소개를 했다. \"고달밍 경, 판 헬싱 교수, 텍사스의 퀸시 모리스 씨, 그리고 렌필드 씨요.\""], ["Quincey Morris, of Texas; Mr. Renfield.” He shook hands with each of them, saying in turn:--", "그는 한 사람 한 사람과 차례로 악수하며 말했다."]], [["“Lord Godalming, I had the honour of seconding your father at the Windham; I grieve to know, by your holding the title, that he is no more.", "\"고달밍 경, 저는 윈덤에서 경의 부친을 모실 영광을 누렸지요. 경께서 작위를 지니신 것으로 미루어 그분이 이미 세상에 안 계심을 알게 되어 슬픕니다."], ["He was a man loved and honoured by all who knew him; and in his youth was, I have heard, the inventor of a burnt rum punch, much patronised on Derby night. Mr.", "그분은 그를 아는 모든 이에게 사랑받고 존경받은 분이셨고, 듣자 하니 젊은 시절에는 더비 경마가 열리는 밤마다 크게 사랑받은, 럼을 태운 펀치의 고안자셨다지요."], ["Morris, you should be proud of your great state. Its reception into the Union was a precedent which may have far-reaching effects hereafter, when the Pole and the Tropics may hold alliance to the Stars and Stripes.", "모리스 씨, 당신은 당신의 위대한 주(州)를 자랑스러워하셔야 합니다. 그 주가 연방에 받아들여진 것은 하나의 선례가 되어, 훗날 극지와 열대가 성조기와 동맹을 맺게 될 때 멀리 미치는 영향을 끼칠 수도 있겠지요."], ["The power of Treaty may yet prove a vast engine of enlargement, when the Monroe doctrine takes its true place as a political fable.", "먼로주의가 정치적 우화로서 제 참된 자리를 차지하게 되면, 조약의 힘이 언젠가 거대한 확장의 동력임을 입증할지도 모릅니다."], ["What shall any man say of his pleasure at meeting Van Helsing?", "판 헬싱을 만나는 기쁨을 두고 누군들 무슨 말을 할 수 있겠습니까?"], ["Sir, I make no apology for dropping all forms of conventional prefix.", "선생, 저는 관례적인 경칭을 모두 생략하는 데 대해 양해를 구하지 않겠습니다."], ["When an individual has revolutionised therapeutics by his discovery of the continuous evolution of brain-matter, conventional forms are unfitting, since they would seem to limit him to one of a class.", "한 개인이 뇌 물질의 연속적 진화를 발견함으로써 치료학에 혁명을 일으켰다면, 관례적인 형식은 어울리지 않습니다. 그것은 그를 한낱 부류의 하나로 한정하는 듯이 보일 테니까요."], ["You, gentlemen, who by nationality, by heredity, or by the possession of natural gifts, are fitted to hold your respective places in the moving world, I take to witness that I am as sane as at least the majority of men who are in full possession of their liberties. And I am sure that you, Dr.", "여러분, 국적으로든, 혈통으로든, 혹은 타고난 재능을 지녔음으로든, 움직이는 세상에서 저마다의 자리를 차지하기에 합당하신 여러분을 증인으로 삼아 말씀드립니다. 저는 적어도 자유를 온전히 누리고 있는 대다수의 사람들만큼은 제정신입니다."], ["Seward, humanitarian and medico-jurist as well as scientist, will deem it a moral duty to deal with me as one to be considered as under exceptional circumstances.”", "그리고 박사님, 과학자일 뿐 아니라 인도주의자이자 법의학자이신 당신은, 저를 예외적인 사정 아래 있는 자로 헤아려 대하는 것을 도덕적 의무로 여기시리라 확신합니다.\""], ["He made this last appeal with a courtly air of conviction which was not without its own charm.", "그는 이 마지막 호소를, 그 나름의 매력이 없지 않은, 정중하고 확신에 찬 태도로 했다."]], [["I think we were all staggered.", "우리 모두 적잖이 동요한 듯하다."], ["For my own part, I was under the conviction, despite my knowledge of the man’s character and history, that his reason had been restored; and I felt under a strong impulse to tell him that I was satisfied as to his sanity, and would see about the necessary formalities for his release in the morning.", "나로 말하면, 그 사내의 성격과 내력을 알고 있음에도, 그의 이성이 회복되었다는 확신에 사로잡혀 있었다. 그래서 나는 그에게 그의 제정신에 대해 만족한다고, 아침에 그의 석방에 필요한 절차를 알아보겠다고 말하고 싶은 강한 충동을 느꼈다."], ["I thought it better to wait, however, before making so grave a statement, for of old I knew the sudden changes to which this particular patient was liable.", "그러나 그토록 중대한 언명을 하기 전에 기다리는 편이 낫겠다고 생각했으니, 이 특정한 환자가 빠지기 쉬운 급작스러운 변화를 나는 예전부터 알고 있었기 때문이다."], ["So I contented myself with making a general statement that he appeared to be improving very rapidly; that I would have a longer chat with him in the morning, and would then see what I could do in the direction of meeting his wishes.", "그래서 나는 그가 매우 빠르게 호전되고 있는 것으로 보인다고, 아침에 그와 좀 더 길게 이야기를 나눈 뒤 그의 바람을 들어주는 쪽으로 무엇을 할 수 있을지 살펴보겠다는 일반적인 언급에 그치고 만족하기로 했다."], ["This did not at all satisfy him, for he said quickly:--", "이것은 그를 전혀 만족시키지 못했으니, 그가 재빨리 말했다."]], [["“But I fear, Dr. Seward, that you hardly apprehend my wish.", "\"하지만 박사님, 박사님께서 제 바람을 거의 헤아리지 못하시는 것이 아닌가 두렵습니다."], ["I desire to go at once--here--now--this very hour--this very moment, if I may.", "저는 당장 — 여기서 — 지금 — 바로 이 시각에 — 가능하다면 바로 이 순간에 가고 싶습니다."], ["Time presses, and in our implied agreement with the old scytheman it is of the essence of the contract.", "시간이 급박하며, 저 늙은 낫잡이와의 우리의 암묵적 합의에서 그것은 계약의 본질입니다."], ["I am sure it is only necessary to put before so admirable a practitioner as Dr. Seward so simple, yet so momentous a wish, to ensure its fulfilment.”", "수어드 박사님처럼 훌륭한 의사 앞에 이토록 간단하면서도 이토록 중대한 바람을 내놓기만 하면, 그것이 이루어지는 것은 틀림없으리라 저는 확신합니다.\""], ["He looked at me keenly, and seeing the negative in my face, turned to the others, and scrutinised them closely.", "그는 나를 날카롭게 바라보다가, 내 얼굴에서 부정의 빛을 읽고는 다른 이들에게로 고개를 돌려 그들을 면밀히 살폈다."], ["Not meeting any sufficient response, he went on:--", "충분한 반응을 얻지 못하자 그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Is it possible that I have erred in my supposition?”", "\"제 짐작이 빗나간 것일 수도 있습니까?\""]], [["“You have,”", "\"빗나갔소.\""], ["I said frankly, but at the same time, as I felt, brutally. There was a considerable pause, and then he said slowly:--", "나는 솔직하게, 그러나 동시에, 스스로 느끼기에도 무자비하게 말했다. 상당한 사이가 흐른 뒤 그가 천천히 말했다."]], [["“Then I suppose I must only shift my ground of request.", "\"그렇다면 저는 청의 근거를 바꾸는 수밖에 없겠군요."], ["Let me ask for this concession--boon, privilege, what you will.", "이 양보를 — 은혜든, 특권이든, 무어라 부르시든 — 청하게 해주십시오."], ["I am content to implore in such a case, not on personal grounds, but for the sake of others.", "이런 경우에 저는 개인적인 이유에서가 아니라 다른 이들을 위하여 간청하는 것으로 만족합니다."], ["I am not at liberty to give you the whole of my reasons; but you may, I assure you, take it from me that they are good ones, sound and unselfish, and spring from the highest sense of duty.", "저는 제 이유 전부를 말씀드릴 처지가 못 됩니다. 그러나 그 이유들이 훌륭하고, 온당하며, 이기적이지 않고, 가장 드높은 의무감에서 비롯된 것임을 제 말로 믿어주셔도 좋다고 단언합니다."], ["Could you look, sir, into my heart, you would approve to the full the sentiments which animate me.", "선생, 만일 당신이 제 마음속을 들여다보실 수 있다면, 저를 움직이는 그 심정에 온전히 찬동하실 것입니다."], ["Nay, more, you would count me amongst the best and truest of your friends.”", "아니, 그 이상으로, 당신은 저를 당신의 가장 훌륭하고 진실한 벗들 가운데 하나로 꼽으실 것입니다.\""], ["Again he looked at us all keenly.", "그는 다시 우리 모두를 날카롭게 바라보았다."], ["I had a growing conviction that this sudden change of his entire intellectual method was but yet another form or phase of his madness, and so determined to let him go on a little longer, knowing from experience that he would, like all lunatics, give himself away in the end.", "나는 그의 지적 방식 전체가 이렇게 급작스레 바뀐 것이 그저 그의 광기의 또 다른 형태나 국면일 뿐이라는 확신이 점점 커졌다. 그래서 모든 광인이 그러하듯 그가 결국 제 본색을 드러내리라는 것을 경험으로 알기에, 나는 그가 조금 더 말하도록 내버려두기로 마음먹었다."], ["Van Helsing was gazing at him with a look of utmost intensity, his bushy eyebrows almost meeting with the fixed concentration of his look.", "판 헬싱은 더없이 강렬한 눈빛으로 그를 응시하고 있었는데, 그 시선의 고정된 집중으로 무성한 눈썹이 거의 맞붙을 지경이었다."], ["He said to Renfield in a tone which did not surprise me at the time, but only when I thought of it afterwards--for it was as of one addressing an equal:--", "그가 렌필드에게 말했는데, 그 어조는 그때에는 나를 놀라게 하지 않았으나 나중에 떠올려 보았을 때에야 놀라웠다 — 그것은 마치 대등한 사람을 대하는 듯한 어조였기 때문이다."]], [["“Can you not tell frankly your real reason for wishing to be free to-night?", "\"오늘 밤 자유로워지고 싶은 진짜 이유를 솔직히 말해줄 수는 없겠소?"], ["I will undertake that if you will satisfy even me--a stranger, without prejudice, and with the habit of keeping an open mind--Dr. Seward will give you, at his own risk and on his own responsibility, the privilege you seek.”", "만일 그대가 나만이라도 — 편견 없이, 마음을 열어두는 습관을 지닌 한낱 낯선 사람을 — 납득시킨다면, 수어드 박사가 자기 위험을 무릅쓰고 자기 책임 아래 그대가 구하는 특권을 주리라는 것을 내가 책임지겠소.\""], ["He shook his head sadly, and with a look of poignant regret on his face.", "그는 슬프게 고개를 저었고, 얼굴에는 가슴을 에는 회한의 빛이 어렸다."], ["The Professor went on:--", "교수가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Come, sir, bethink yourself.", "\"자, 보시오, 곰곰이 생각해 보시오."], ["You claim the privilege of reason in the highest degree, since you seek to impress us with your complete reasonableness.", "그대는 우리에게 자신이 완전히 분별 있음을 각인시키려 하니, 가장 드높은 정도의 이성이라는 특권을 주장하는 셈이오."], ["You do this, whose sanity we have reason to doubt, since you are not yet released from medical treatment for this very defect.", "그대는 이러는데, 정작 그대의 제정신은 의심할 만한 이유가 있소, 그대가 바로 이 결함 때문에 아직 의료적 치료에서 풀려나지 못했으니 말이오."], ["If you will not help us in our effort to choose the wisest course, how can we perform the duty which you yourself put upon us?", "만일 그대가 가장 현명한 길을 택하려는 우리의 노력을 돕지 않는다면, 그대 자신이 우리에게 지운 의무를 우리가 어찌 다하겠소?"], ["Be wise, and help us; and if we can we shall aid you to achieve your wish.”", "현명해지시오, 그리고 우리를 도우시오. 그러면 우리도 할 수 있다면 그대가 바람을 이루도록 도우리다.\""], ["He still shook his head as he said:--", "그는 여전히 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["“Dr. Van Helsing, I have nothing to say.", "\"판 헬싱 박사님, 저는 드릴 말씀이 없습니다."], ["Your argument is complete, and if I were free to speak I should not hesitate a moment; but I am not my own master in the matter.", "박사님의 논리는 완벽하며, 만일 제가 말할 자유가 있다면 저는 한순간도 망설이지 않을 것입니다. 그러나 저는 이 문제에서 제 자신의 주인이 아닙니다."], ["I can only ask you to trust me. If I am refused, the responsibility does not rest with me.”", "저는 그저 저를 믿어달라고 청할 수 있을 뿐입니다. 만일 거절당한다면, 그 책임은 저에게 있지 않습니다.\""], ["I thought it was now time to end the scene, which was becoming too comically grave, so I went towards the door, simply saying:--", "나는 이제 이 장면을 끝낼 때라고 생각했다. 너무도 우스꽝스러울 만큼 심각해지고 있었기 때문이다. 그래서 나는 그저 이렇게 말하며 문 쪽으로 갔다."]], [["“Come, my friends, we have work to do.", "\"자, 벗들이여, 우리에게는 할 일이 있소."], ["Good-night.”", "잘 자시오.\""]], [["As, however, I got near the door, a new change came over the patient.", "그러나 내가 문 가까이 다가가자, 환자에게 새로운 변화가 일어났다."], ["He moved towards me so quickly that for the moment I feared that he was about to make another homicidal attack.", "그가 어찌나 빠르게 나를 향해 다가오는지, 순간 나는 그가 또 살의를 품은 공격을 하려는 것이 아닐까 두려웠다."], ["My fears, however, were groundless, for he held up his two hands imploringly, and made his petition in a moving manner.", "그러나 내 두려움은 근거 없는 것이었으니, 그가 애원하듯 두 손을 들어 올리고는 사람의 마음을 움직이는 태도로 자신의 간청을 했기 때문이다."], ["As he saw that the very excess of his emotion was militating against him, by restoring us more to our old relations, he became still more demonstrative.", "그는 자신의 바로 그 지나친 격정이 오히려 우리를 예전의 관계로 되돌려 자신에게 불리하게 작용하고 있음을 알아채자, 한층 더 격앙된 모습을 보였다."], ["I glanced at Van Helsing, and saw my conviction reflected in his eyes; so I became a little more fixed in my manner, if not more stern, and motioned to him that his efforts were unavailing.", "나는 판 헬싱을 흘끗 바라보았고, 내 확신이 그의 눈에도 비치는 것을 보았다. 그래서 나는 더 엄격해지지는 않더라도 태도를 조금 더 단단히 굳히고, 그의 노력이 소용없음을 그에게 몸짓으로 알렸다."], ["I had previously seen something of the same constantly growing excitement in him when he had to make some request of which at the time he had thought much, such, for instance, as when he wanted a cat; and I was prepared to see the collapse into the same sullen acquiescence on this occasion.", "나는 전에도 그에게서 이와 같이 점점 커지는 흥분을 본 적이 있었는데, 그가 그 무렵 골똘히 생각하던 어떤 청을 — 이를테면 고양이를 갖고 싶어 했을 때처럼 — 해야 할 때였다. 그래서 나는 이번에도 그가 똑같이 시무룩한 체념으로 무너져 내리는 것을 보게 되리라 마음의 준비를 하고 있었다."], ["My expectation was not realised, for, when he found that his appeal would not be successful, he got into quite a frantic condition.", "그러나 내 예상은 빗나갔으니, 자신의 호소가 성공하지 못하리라는 것을 알아채자 그는 그야말로 광란의 상태에 빠졌기 때문이다."], ["He threw himself on his knees, and held up his hands, wringing them in plaintive supplication, and poured forth a torrent of entreaty, with the tears rolling down his cheeks, and his whole face and form expressive of the deepest emotion:--", "그는 무릎을 꿇고 두 손을 들어 올려, 애처로운 탄원으로 손을 비비 꼬며, 뺨으로 눈물을 줄줄 흘리고 온 얼굴과 온몸으로 더없이 깊은 격정을 드러낸 채, 간청의 격류를 쏟아냈다."]], [["“Let me entreat you, Dr. Seward, oh, let me implore you, to let me out of this house at once.", "\"수어드 박사님, 부디 간청드립니다, 아, 부디 애원합니다, 당장 저를 이 집에서 내보내 주십시오. 어떻게든, 어디로든 저를 보내 주십시오."], ["Send me away how you will and where you will; send keepers with me with whips and chains; let them take me in a strait-waistcoat, manacled and leg-ironed, even to a gaol; but let me go out of this.", "채찍과 사슬을 든 간수들을 딸려 보내십시오. 그들이 저를 구속복에 묶어, 수갑과 족쇄를 채워 감옥으로 끌고 가도 좋습니다. 하지만 이곳에서만은 나가게 해주십시오."], ["You don’t know what you do by keeping me here.", "저를 여기 붙들어 둠으로써 무슨 일을 저지르시는지 박사님은 모르십니다."], ["I am speaking from the depths of my heart--of my very soul.", "저는 제 마음 깊은 곳에서 — 제 영혼 깊은 곳에서 말씀드리는 것입니다."], ["You don’t know whom you wrong, or how; and I may not tell. Woe is me!", "박사님은 누구를, 또 어떻게 해치시는지 모르십니다. 그리고 저는 말할 수 없습니다."], ["I may not tell.", "아, 가엾은 저여!"], ["By all you hold sacred--by all you hold dear--by your love that is lost--by your hope that lives--for the sake of the Almighty, take me out of this and save my soul from guilt!", "저는 말할 수 없습니다. 당신이 신성하게 여기는 모든 것을 두고 — 당신이 소중히 여기는 모든 것을 두고 — 잃어버린 당신의 사랑을 두고 — 살아 있는 당신의 희망을 두고 — 전능하신 분을 위해서라도, 저를 이곳에서 내보내 제 영혼을 죄로부터 구해 주십시오!"], ["Can’t you hear me, man?", "제 말이 들리지 않으십니까?"], ["Can’t you understand?", "이해하지 못하시겠습니까?"], ["Will you never learn?", "끝내 깨닫지 못하시겠습니까?"], ["Don’t you know that I am sane and earnest now; that I am no lunatic in a mad fit, but a sane man fighting for his soul?", "제가 지금 제정신이고 진지하다는 것을, 제가 발작에 빠진 광인이 아니라 제 영혼을 위해 싸우는 제정신인 사람임을 모르시겠습니까? 아, 들어주십시오!"], ["Oh, hear me!", "들어주십시오!"], ["hear me! Let me go!", "저를 보내 주십시오!"], ["let me go! let me go!”", "저를 보내 주십시오! 저를 보내 주십시오!\""]], [["I thought that the longer this went on the wilder he would get, and so would bring on a fit; so I took him by the hand and raised him up.", "나는 이것이 길어질수록 그가 더 거칠어져 발작을 일으키리라고 생각했다. 그래서 그의 손을 잡고 그를 일으켜 세웠다."]], [["“Come,”", "\"자,\""], ["I said sternly, “no more of this; we have had quite enough already.", "나는 엄하게 말했다. \"이제 그만하시오. 이미 충분히 했소."], ["Get to your bed and try to behave more discreetly.”", "침대로 가서 좀 더 신중하게 처신하도록 애써 보시오.\""]], [["He suddenly stopped and looked at me intently for several moments.", "그는 갑자기 멈추고 여러 순간 동안 나를 뚫어지게 바라보았다."], ["Then, without a word, he rose and moving over, sat down on the side of the bed.", "그러더니 한마디 말도 없이 일어나, 자리를 옮겨 침대 가장자리에 앉았다."], ["The collapse had come, as on former occasion, just as I had expected.", "무너짐이 찾아온 것이다, 예전처럼, 바로 내가 예상했던 대로."]], [["When I was leaving the room, last of our party, he said to me in a quiet, well-bred voice:--", "우리 일행 가운데 마지막으로 내가 방을 나설 때, 그가 조용하고 교양 있는 목소리로 나에게 말했다."]], [["“You will, I trust, Dr. Seward, do me the justice to bear in mind, later on, that I did what I could to convince you to-night.”", "\"수어드 박사님, 부디 나중에라도 저에게 공정을 베푸시어, 제가 오늘 밤 당신을 납득시키려 할 수 있는 일을 다 했다는 것을 마음에 새겨 주시기 바랍니다.\""]], [["# JONATHAN HARKER’S JOURNAL", "# JONATHAN HARKER’S JOURNAL"]], [["1 October, 5 a.", "「조너선 하커의 일기」"], ["m.--I went with the party to the search with an easy mind, for I think I never saw Mina so absolutely strong and well.", "10월 1일, 새벽 5시. — 나는 가벼운 마음으로 일행과 함께 수색에 나섰다. 미나가 그토록 완전히 굳세고 건강해 보인 적은 일찍이 없었던 것 같기 때문이다."], ["I am so glad that she consented to hold back and let us men do the work.", "그녀가 한발 물러나 우리 남자들에게 이 일을 맡기기로 동의해주어 나는 무척 기쁘다."], ["Somehow, it was a dread to me that she was in this fearful business at all; but now that her work is done, and that it is due to her energy and brains and foresight that the whole story is put together in such a way that every point tells, she may well feel that her part is finished, and that she can henceforth leave the rest to us.", "어쩐지 나로서는 그녀가 이 끔찍한 일에 발을 들였다는 것 자체가 두려웠다. 그러나 이제 그녀의 몫은 끝났고, 모든 이야기가 한 점도 빠짐없이 들어맞도록 짜맞추어진 것이 그녀의 정력과 두뇌와 선견지명 덕분이니, 그녀로서는 제 역할이 마무리되었으며, 이제부터 나머지는 우리에게 맡겨도 좋다고 여겨 마땅하다."], ["We were, I think, all a little upset by the scene with Mr. Renfield.", "우리는 렌필드 씨와의 그 장면으로 모두 조금씩 마음이 어지러웠던 것 같다."], ["When we came away from his room we were silent till we got back to the study.", "그의 방을 나설 때 우리는 서재로 돌아올 때까지 말이 없었다."], ["Then Mr. Morris said to Dr. Seward:--", "그러다 모리스 씨가 수어드 박사에게 말했다."]], [["“Say, Jack, if that man wasn’t attempting a bluff, he is about the sanest lunatic I ever saw.", "\"이봐, 잭, 저 사람이 허세를 부린 게 아니라면, 내가 본 미치광이 중에 가장 제정신에 가까운 자일세."], ["I’m not sure, but I believe that he had some serious purpose, and if he had, it was pretty rough on him not to get a chance.”", "확신할 수는 없지만, 내 생각에 그에게는 어떤 진지한 목적이 있었던 것 같고, 만일 그랬다면 기회를 얻지 못한 건 그에게 꽤 가혹한 일이었네.\""], ["Lord Godalming and I were silent, but Dr. Van Helsing added:--", "고달밍 경과 나는 잠자코 있었으나, 반 헬싱 박사가 말을 보탰다."]], [["“Friend John, you know more of lunatics than I do, and I’m glad of it, for I fear that if it had been to me to decide I would before that last hysterical outburst have given him free.", "\"존, 자네는 나보다 미치광이를 잘 알고, 나는 그게 다행스럽네. 만일 결정이 내 몫이었다면 그 마지막 발작적인 분출이 있기 전에 나는 그를 풀어주었을 테니 두렵네."], ["But we live and learn, and in our present task we must take no chance, as my friend Quincey would say.", "그러나 우리는 살아가며 배우는 법이고, 지금 우리가 맡은 일에서는, 내 친구 퀸시라면 말하듯, 어떤 모험도 해서는 안 되네."], ["All is best as they are.” Dr.", "모든 것이 지금 이대로가 최선이야.\""], ["Seward seemed to answer them both in a dreamy kind of way:--", "수어드 박사는 마치 꿈을 꾸는 듯한 투로 두 사람 모두에게 답하는 것 같았다."]], [["“I don’t know but that I agree with you.", "\"동의하지 않는다고는 못하겠습니다."], ["If that man had been an ordinary lunatic I would have taken my chance of trusting him; but he seems so mixed up with the Count in an indexy kind of way that I am afraid of doing anything wrong by helping his fads.", "저 사람이 평범한 미치광이였다면 나는 그를 믿어보는 모험을 감행했을 겁니다. 그러나 그는 어쩐지 색인처럼 백작과 얽혀 있는 것 같아서, 그의 별난 짓을 도왔다가 무언가 잘못을 저지르게 될까 두렵습니다."], ["I can’t forget how he prayed with almost equal fervour for a cat, and then tried to tear my throat out with his teeth.", "그가 거의 똑같이 열렬하게 고양이를 달라고 기도하다가, 곧이어 이빨로 내 목을 물어뜯으려 한 것을 잊을 수가 없어요."], ["Besides, he called the Count ‘lord and master,’ and he may want to get out to help him in some diabolical way.", "게다가 그는 백작을 '주인이자 지배자'라 불렀으니, 어떤 악마적인 방식으로 백작을 도우려 밖으로 나가고 싶어 하는 건지도 모릅니다."], ["That horrid thing has the wolves and the rats and his own kind to help him, so I suppose he isn’t above trying to use a respectable lunatic.", "그 끔찍한 것에게는 늑대와 쥐들, 그리고 제 동족이 그를 도우니, 제 짐작에 그는 점잖은 미치광이 하나쯤 이용하기를 마다하지 않을 겁니다."], ["He certainly did seem earnest, though.", "그래도 그가 진지해 보였던 건 분명합니다."], ["I only hope we have done what is best.", "그저 우리가 최선의 일을 했기를 바랄 뿐입니다."], ["These things, in conjunction with the wild work we have in hand, help to unnerve a man.”", "이런 일들은, 우리가 손에 쥔 이 거친 일과 더불어 사람의 기력을 빼놓는군요.\""], ["The Professor stepped over, and laying his hand on his shoulder, said in his grave, kindly way:--", "교수는 다가가 그의 어깨에 손을 얹고는 진중하고도 자상한 투로 말했다."]], [["“Friend John, have no fear. We are trying to do our duty in a very sad and terrible case; we can only do as we deem best.", "\"존, 두려워 말게. 우리는 매우 슬프고 끔찍한 사건에서 우리의 의무를 다하려 애쓰고 있을 뿐이며, 우리가 최선이라 여기는 대로 할 수 있을 뿐이네."], ["What else have we to hope for, except the pity of the good God?”", "선하신 하느님의 자비 말고 우리가 또 무엇을 바랄 수 있겠는가?\""], ["Lord Godalming had slipped away for a few minutes, but now he returned.", "고달밍 경은 잠시 자리를 비웠다가 이제 돌아왔다."], ["He held up a little silver whistle, as he remarked:--", "그는 작은 은제 호각을 들어 보이며 말했다."]], [["“That old place may be full of rats, and if so, I’ve got an antidote on call.”", "\"저 낡은 곳은 쥐가 들끓을지도 모르네. 만일 그렇다면, 나는 부르기만 하면 되는 해독제를 마련해두었지.\""], ["Having passed the wall, we took our way to the house, taking care to keep in the shadows of the trees on the lawn when the moonlight shone out.", "담을 넘은 뒤 우리는 그 집으로 향했는데, 달빛이 환히 비칠 때면 잔디밭 나무 그늘에 몸을 숨기도록 조심했다."], ["When we got to the porch the Professor opened his bag and took out a lot of things, which he laid on the step, sorting them into four little groups, evidently one for each. Then he spoke:--", "현관에 이르자 교수는 가방을 열어 여러 가지 물건을 꺼내 계단 위에 내려놓고, 그것들을 네 무더기로 나누었는데, 분명 각자에게 하나씩 돌아가도록 한 것이었다. 그러고 나서 그가 말했다."]], [["“My friends, we are going into a terrible danger, and we need arms of many kinds.", "\"여러분, 우리는 무시무시한 위험 속으로 들어가고 있으며, 여러 가지 무기가 필요합니다."], ["Our enemy is not merely spiritual.", "우리의 적은 단지 영적인 존재만이 아닙니다."], ["Remember that he has the strength of twenty men, and that, though our necks or our windpipes are of the common kind--and therefore breakable or crushable--his are not amenable to mere strength.", "그가 스무 사람의 힘을 지녔다는 것, 그리고 우리의 목이나 기도는 평범한 종류여서 — 따라서 부러지거나 으스러질 수 있으나 — 그의 것은 한낱 힘에는 굴복하지 않는다는 것을 기억하십시오."], ["A stronger man, or a body of men more strong in all than him, can at certain times hold him; but they cannot hurt him as we can be hurt by him.", "더 힘센 사람, 혹은 모든 면에서 그보다 더 강한 무리가 어떤 때에는 그를 붙들 수는 있으나, 우리가 그에게 상처 입을 수 있는 만큼 그를 다치게 할 수는 없습니다."], ["We must, therefore, guard ourselves from his touch.", "그러니 우리는 그의 손길로부터 우리 자신을 지켜야 합니다. 이것을 가슴 가까이 지니십시오.\""], ["Keep this near your heart”--as he spoke he lifted a little silver crucifix and held it out to me, I being nearest to him--“put these flowers round your neck”--here he handed to me a wreath of withered garlic blossoms--“for other enemies more mundane, this revolver and this knife; and for aid in all, these so small electric lamps, which you can fasten to your breast; and for all, and above all at the last, this, which we must not desecrate needless.”", "— 그는 이렇게 말하며 작은 은제 십자고상을 들어, 그에게 가장 가까이 있던 나에게 내밀었다 — \"이 꽃들을 목에 두르십시오.\" — 여기서 그는 시든 마늘꽃을 엮은 화환을 나에게 건넸다 — \"좀 더 세속적인 다른 적들을 위해서는 이 권총과 이 칼을. 그리고 모든 일에 도움이 되도록, 가슴에 매달 수 있는 이 아주 작은 전기등을."], ["This was a portion of Sacred Wafer, which he put in an envelope and handed to me. Each of the others was similarly equipped.", "그리고 무엇보다도 마지막에 쓸 이것을, 우리는 함부로 더럽혀서는 안 됩니다.\" 그것은 성체(聖體)의 일부였는데, 그는 그것을 봉투에 넣어 나에게 건넸다."], ["“Now,” he said, “friend John, where are the skeleton keys?", "나머지 사람들에게도 똑같이 채비를 갖추어주었다. \"자,\" 하고 그가 말했다."], ["If so that we can open the door, we need not break house by the window, as before at Miss Lucy’s.”", "\"존, 만능 열쇠는 어디 있나? 그것으로 문을 열 수 있다면, 전에 루시 양 때처럼 창문으로 집을 부수고 들어갈 필요는 없겠지.\""]], [["Dr. Seward tried one or two skeleton keys, his mechanical dexterity as a surgeon standing him in good stead.", "수어드 박사는 만능 열쇠를 한두 개 시험해보았는데, 외과의로서의 그 기계적인 손재주가 큰 도움이 되었다."], ["Presently he got one to suit; after a little play back and forward the bolt yielded, and, with a rusty clang, shot back.", "곧 그는 맞는 것을 하나 찾아냈고, 앞뒤로 조금 놀려보자 빗장이 풀리며 녹슨 쇳소리와 함께 뒤로 젖혀졌다."], ["We pressed on the door, the rusty hinges creaked, and it slowly opened.", "우리가 문을 밀자 녹슨 경첩이 삐걱거리며 문이 천천히 열렸다."], ["It was startlingly like the image conveyed to me in Dr. Seward’s diary of the opening of Miss Westenra’s tomb; I fancy that the same idea seemed to strike the others, for with one accord they shrank back.", "그것은 수어드 박사의 일기에서 웨스텐라 양의 무덤이 열리던 장면이 나에게 전해주었던 인상과 놀랍도록 닮아 있었다. 다른 사람들에게도 똑같은 생각이 떠오른 듯, 약속이라도 한 듯 모두가 움찔 물러섰다."], ["The Professor was the first to move forward, and stepped into the open door.", "교수가 가장 먼저 앞으로 나아가, 열린 문 안으로 발을 들였다."]], [["“In manus tuas, Domine!” he said, crossing himself as he passed over the threshold.", "\"인 마누스 투아스, 도미네!\" 하고 그는 문지방을 넘으며 성호를 그었다."], ["We closed the door behind us, lest when we should have lit our lamps we should possibly attract attention from the road.", "우리는 등불을 켰을 때 행여 길에서 누군가의 주의를 끌까 봐, 등 뒤로 문을 닫았다."], ["The Professor carefully tried the lock, lest we might not be able to open it from within should we be in a hurry making our exit.", "교수는 빠져나가야 할 때 서두르게 되면 행여 안에서 문을 열지 못할까 봐, 자물쇠를 조심스레 살펴보았다. 그러고 나서 우리는 모두 등불을 켜고 수색에 나섰다."], ["Then we all lit our lamps and proceeded on our search.", "* 인 마누스 투아스, 도미네: 라틴어로 \"당신의 손에 맡기나이다, 주여.\""]], [["The light from the tiny lamps fell in all sorts of odd forms, as the rays crossed each other, or the opacity of our bodies threw great shadows.", "자그마한 등불에서 나온 빛이 서로 교차하거나, 우리 몸의 그늘이 커다란 그림자를 드리우면서 온갖 기묘한 형상으로 떨어졌다."], ["I could not for my life get away from the feeling that there was some one else amongst us.", "나는 우리 가운데 누군가 다른 이가 있다는 느낌을 도무지 떨쳐낼 수 없었다."], ["I suppose it was the recollection, so powerfully brought home to me by the grim surroundings, of that terrible experience in Transylvania.", "이 음산한 주변 환경 탓에 그토록 강렬하게 되살아난, 트란실바니아에서의 그 끔찍한 경험에 대한 기억 때문이었으리라."], ["I think the feeling was common to us all, for I noticed that the others kept looking over their shoulders at every sound and every new shadow, just as I felt myself doing.", "이 느낌은 우리 모두에게 공통된 것이었던 듯한데, 다른 이들도 소리가 날 때마다, 새 그림자가 생길 때마다 어깨 너머로 돌아보는 것을, 꼭 내가 그러고 있다고 느꼈던 것과 똑같이 하는 것을 보았기 때문이다."]], [["The whole place was thick with dust.", "집 안 전체가 먼지로 자욱했다."], ["The floor was seemingly inches deep, except where there were recent footsteps, in which on holding down my lamp I could see marks of hobnails where the dust was cracked.", "바닥은 보기에 몇 인치는 쌓인 듯했는데, 최근 발자국이 난 곳만은 예외여서, 등불을 그쪽으로 낮추어보니 먼지가 갈라진 자리에 징 박은 구두 자국이 보였다."], ["The walls were fluffy and heavy with dust, and in the corners were masses of spider’s webs, whereon the dust had gathered till they looked like old tattered rags as the weight had torn them partly down.", "벽은 먼지로 보풀이 일고 무거웠으며, 구석에는 거미줄이 뭉텅뭉텅 엉겨 있었는데, 그 위에 먼지가 쌓여 무게에 못 이겨 일부가 찢겨 내려, 마치 낡고 너덜너덜한 누더기처럼 보였다."], ["On a table in the hall was a great bunch of keys, with a time-yellowed label on each.", "현관의 탁자 위에는 커다란 열쇠 꾸러미가 있었고, 저마다 세월에 누렇게 바랜 꼬리표가 달려 있었다."], ["They had been used several times, for on the table were several similar rents in the blanket of dust, similar to that exposed when the Professor lifted them.", "그것들은 여러 차례 쓰인 적이 있었으니, 탁자 위에는 교수가 그 꾸러미를 들어 올렸을 때 드러난 것과 비슷한, 먼지 담요에 난 비슷한 자국이 여럿 있었기 때문이다."], ["He turned to me and said:--", "그가 나를 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“You know this place, Jonathan.", "\"자네는 이곳을 알고 있네, 조너선."], ["You have copied maps of it, and you know it at least more than we do.", "자네는 이곳의 지도를 베껴 그렸으니, 적어도 우리보다는 더 잘 알지."], ["Which is the way to the chapel?”", "예배당으로 가는 길은 어느 쪽인가?\""], ["I had an idea of its direction, though on my former visit I had not been able to get admission to it; so I led the way, and after a few wrong turnings found myself opposite a low, arched oaken door, ribbed with iron bands.", "나는 지난번 방문 때 그곳에 들어가 보지는 못했으나, 그 방향에 대해서는 짐작이 있었다. 그래서 앞장을 섰고, 몇 번 길을 잘못 든 끝에 쇠띠로 두른, 낮고 아치형인 떡갈나무 문 앞에 서게 되었다."], ["“This is the spot,”", "\"여기가 그곳이군,\""], ["said the Professor as he turned his lamp on a small map of the house, copied from the file of my original correspondence regarding the purchase.", "하고 교수는 매입에 관한 내 원래의 서신 묶음에서 베껴낸 그 집의 작은 지도에 등불을 비추며 말했다."], ["With a little trouble we found the key on the bunch and opened the door.", "우리는 조금 애를 먹은 끝에 꾸러미에서 그 열쇠를 찾아 문을 열었다."], ["We were prepared for some unpleasantness, for as we were opening the door a faint, malodorous air seemed to exhale through the gaps, but none of us ever expected such an odour as we encountered.", "우리는 어느 정도의 불쾌함은 각오하고 있었으니, 문을 여는 동안 희미하고 고약한 냄새가 풍기는 공기가 틈으로 새어 나오는 듯했기 때문이다. 그러나 우리 가운데 누구도 우리가 맞닥뜨린 그런 악취는 예상하지 못했다."], ["None of the others had met the Count at all at close quarters, and when I had seen him he was either in the fasting stage of his existence in his rooms or, when he was gloated with fresh blood, in a ruined building open to the air; but here the place was small and close, and the long disuse had made the air stagnant and foul.", "다른 이들은 백작을 가까이서 마주한 적이 전혀 없었고, 내가 그를 보았을 때는 그가 제 방에서 단식 상태에 있거나, 혹은 신선한 피로 배를 채운 채 한데에 트인 폐허 건물에 있을 때였다. 그러나 이곳은 작고 답답한 데다, 오랜 폐쇄로 공기가 고이고 더러워져 있었다."], ["There was an earthy smell, as of some dry miasma, which came through the fouler air.", "더 고약한 공기 사이로, 어떤 마른 독기(毒氣)처럼 흙냄새가 풍겨 왔다."], ["But as to the odour itself, how shall I describe it?", "그러나 그 악취 자체를 내가 어떻게 묘사할 수 있을까?"], ["It was not alone that it was composed of all the ills of mortality and with the pungent, acrid smell of blood, but it seemed as though corruption had become itself corrupt.", "그것은 죽음이 빚어내는 온갖 병폐와 피의 톡 쏘는 매캐한 냄새로 이루어졌을 뿐 아니라, 마치 부패 그 자체가 부패해버린 것 같았다. 윽!"], ["Faugh! it sickens me to think of it.", "그것을 생각만 해도 속이 메스껍다."], ["Every breath exhaled by that monster seemed to have clung to the place and intensified its loathsomeness.", "그 괴물이 내쉰 숨결 하나하나가 이곳에 들러붙어 그 역겨움을 한층 짙게 한 것만 같았다."]], [["Under ordinary circumstances such a stench would have brought our enterprise to an end; but this was no ordinary case, and the high and terrible purpose in which we were involved gave us a strength which rose above merely physical considerations.", "여느 상황이었다면 그런 악취만으로도 우리의 일은 거기서 끝장났을 것이다. 그러나 이것은 여느 경우가 아니었고, 우리가 떠맡은 그 높고도 무시무시한 목적이 우리에게 한낱 육체적인 고려를 뛰어넘는 힘을 주었다."], ["After the involuntary shrinking consequent on the first nauseous whiff, we one and all set about our work as though that loathsome place were a garden of roses.", "처음 메스꺼운 한 모금에 절로 움츠러든 뒤, 우리는 너 나 할 것 없이 그 역겨운 곳이 마치 장미 정원이라도 되는 양 일에 착수했다."]], [["We made an accurate examination of the place, the Professor saying as we began:--", "우리는 그곳을 정밀하게 조사했는데, 시작할 때 교수가 말했다."]], [["“The first thing is to see how many of the boxes are left; we must then examine every hole and corner and cranny and see if we cannot get some clue as to what has become of the rest.”", "\"가장 먼저 할 일은 상자가 몇 개나 남았는지 보는 것입니다. 그런 다음 모든 구멍과 구석과 틈을 살펴, 나머지가 어디로 갔는지 단서를 얻을 수 있는지 봅시다.\""], ["A glance was sufficient to show how many remained, for the great earth chests were bulky, and there was no mistaking them.", "몇 개가 남았는지는 한눈에 알 수 있었으니, 그 커다란 흙 궤짝들은 부피가 컸고, 헷갈릴 수가 없었기 때문이다."]], [["There were only twenty-nine left out of the fifty!", "쉰 개 가운데 겨우 스물아홉 개만 남아 있었다!"], ["Once I got a fright, for, seeing Lord Godalming suddenly turn and look out of the vaulted door into the dark passage beyond, I looked too, and for an instant my heart stood still.", "한번은 나는 흠칫 놀랐다. 고달밍 경이 갑자기 돌아서서 아치형 문 너머 어두운 통로를 내다보는 것을 보고, 나도 내다보았는데, 한순간 심장이 멎는 듯했기 때문이다."], ["Somewhere, looking out from the shadow, I seemed to see the high lights of the Count’s evil face, the ridge of the nose, the red eyes, the red lips, the awful pallor.", "그늘 속 어딘가에서 내다보고 있는, 백작의 사악한 얼굴의 도드라진 윤곽 — 콧날, 붉은 눈, 붉은 입술, 소름 끼치는 창백함 — 이 보이는 것 같았다."], ["It was only for a moment, for, as Lord Godalming said, “I thought I saw a face, but it was only the shadows,”", "그것은 한순간뿐이었으니, 고달밍 경이 \"얼굴을 본 것 같았는데, 그저 그림자였군\""], ["and resumed his inquiry, I turned my lamp in the direction, and stepped into the passage.", "하고는 다시 조사를 이어갔고, 나는 등불을 그쪽으로 돌려 통로 안으로 발을 들였다."], ["There was no sign of any one; and as there were no corners, no doors, no aperture of any kind, but only the solid walls of the passage, there could be no hiding-place even for him.", "누군가의 흔적은 없었다. 그리고 모퉁이도, 문도, 어떤 종류의 틈도 없이 오로지 통로의 단단한 벽뿐이었으니, 그자에게조차 숨을 곳은 있을 수 없었다."], ["I took it that fear had helped imagination, and said nothing.", "나는 두려움이 상상을 부추긴 것이라 여기고 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["A few minutes later I saw Morris step suddenly back from a corner, which he was examining.", "몇 분 뒤 나는 모리스가 살펴보던 한 구석에서 갑자기 물러서는 것을 보았다."], ["We all followed his movements with our eyes, for undoubtedly some nervousness was growing on us, and we saw a whole mass of phosphorescence, which twinkled like stars.", "우리는 모두 그의 움직임을 눈으로 좇았는데, 분명 우리에게 어떤 긴장감이 점점 차오르고 있었기 때문이다. 그리고 우리는 별처럼 반짝이는 인광(燐光)의 거대한 덩어리를 보았다."], ["We all instinctively drew back.", "우리는 모두 본능적으로 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["The whole place was becoming alive with rats.", "그곳 전체가 쥐들로 들끓기 시작하고 있었다."]], [["For a moment or two we stood appalled, all save Lord Godalming, who was seemingly prepared for such an emergency.", "한두 순간 우리는 질겁한 채 서 있었으나, 고달밍 경만은 예외였으니, 그는 그런 비상사태에 대비해둔 듯했다."], ["Rushing over to the great iron-bound oaken door, which Dr. Seward had described from the outside, and which I had seen myself, he turned the key in the lock, drew the huge bolts, and swung the door open.", "그는 수어드 박사가 바깥에서 묘사했고 나 또한 직접 보았던, 그 쇠띠로 두른 커다란 떡갈나무 문으로 달려가, 자물쇠에 열쇠를 돌리고 거대한 빗장들을 빼낸 뒤 문을 활짝 열어젖혔다."], ["Then, taking his little silver whistle from his pocket, he blew a low, shrill call.", "그러고는 주머니에서 그 작은 은제 호각을 꺼내, 낮고 날카로운 소리를 불었다."], ["It was answered from behind Dr. Seward’s house by the yelping of dogs, and after about a minute three terriers came dashing round the corner of the house.", "수어드 박사의 집 뒤편에서 개들이 컹컹 짖는 소리로 응답이 왔고, 약 1분쯤 뒤 테리어 세 마리가 집 모퉁이를 돌아 쏜살같이 달려왔다."], ["Unconsciously we had all moved towards the door, and as we moved I noticed that the dust had been much disturbed: the boxes which had been taken out had been brought this way.", "우리도 모르는 사이에 우리는 모두 문 쪽으로 움직여 있었는데, 그렇게 움직이면서 나는 먼지가 몹시 어지럽혀져 있는 것을 알아차렸다. 실려 나간 상자들이 이 길로 옮겨졌던 것이다."], ["But even in the minute that had elapsed the number of the rats had vastly increased.", "그러나 그 지나간 짧은 사이에도 쥐의 수는 엄청나게 불어나 있었다."], ["They seemed to swarm over the place all at once, till the lamplight, shining on their moving dark bodies and glittering, baleful eyes, made the place look like a bank of earth set with fireflies.", "그것들은 한꺼번에 그곳을 온통 뒤덮으며 우글거리는 듯했으니, 등불빛이 그것들의 움직이는 검은 몸뚱이와 번뜩이는 사악한 눈에 비치자, 그곳은 마치 반딧불이가 점점이 박힌 흙더미처럼 보였다."], ["The dogs dashed on, but at the threshold suddenly stopped and snarled, and then, simultaneously lifting their noses, began to howl in most lugubrious fashion.", "개들은 쏜살같이 달려들었으나, 문지방에 이르자 갑자기 멈춰 으르렁거리더니, 일제히 코를 쳐들고는 더없이 구슬픈 모양새로 울부짖기 시작했다."], ["The rats were multiplying in thousands, and we moved out.", "쥐들은 수천 마리로 불어나고 있었고, 우리는 밖으로 나왔다."]], [["Lord Godalming lifted one of the dogs, and carrying him in, placed him on the floor.", "고달밍 경은 개 한 마리를 들어 안으로 데리고 들어가 바닥에 내려놓았다."], ["The instant his feet touched the ground he seemed to recover his courage, and rushed at his natural enemies.", "발이 땅에 닿는 순간 그놈은 용기를 되찾은 듯, 제 타고난 적들에게 달려들었다."], ["They fled before him so fast that before he had shaken the life out of a score, the other dogs, who had by now been lifted in the same manner, had but small prey ere the whole mass had vanished.", "쥐들이 어찌나 빨리 그놈 앞에서 달아났는지, 그놈이 스무 마리쯤의 숨통을 끊어놓기도 전에, 이제 같은 식으로 들여보내진 다른 개들은 그 무리 전체가 사라지기 전에 사냥감을 거의 잡지 못했다."]], [["With their going it seemed as if some evil presence had departed, for the dogs frisked about and barked merrily as they made sudden darts at their prostrate foes, and turned them over and over and tossed them in the air with vicious shakes.", "그것들이 사라지자 마치 어떤 사악한 존재가 떠나간 것만 같았다. 개들이 폴짝거리며 즐겁게 짖어대고, 쓰러진 적들에게 느닷없이 달려들어 이리저리 뒤집고 사납게 흔들며 공중으로 내던졌기 때문이다."], ["We all seemed to find our spirits rise.", "우리는 모두 기운이 솟는 것 같았다."], ["Whether it was the purifying of the deadly atmosphere by the opening of the chapel door, or the relief which we experienced by finding ourselves in the open I know not; but most certainly the shadow of dread seemed to slip from us like a robe, and the occasion of our coming lost something of its grim significance, though we did not slacken a whit in our resolution.", "예배당 문을 열어 그 치명적인 공기가 정화된 덕분인지, 아니면 한데로 나와 안도를 느낀 덕분인지는 모르겠으나, 분명 두려움의 그림자가 마치 한 벌의 겉옷처럼 우리에게서 미끄러져 내렸고, 우리가 찾아온 그 일도 음산한 의미를 얼마간 잃었다. 그렇다고 우리의 결의가 조금이라도 느슨해진 것은 아니었다."], ["We closed the outer door and barred and locked it, and bringing the dogs with us, began our search of the house.", "우리는 바깥문을 닫고 빗장을 지르고 자물쇠를 채운 뒤, 개들을 데리고 집 안 수색을 시작했다."], ["We found nothing throughout except dust in extraordinary proportions, and all untouched save for my own footsteps when I had made my first visit.", "처음 방문 때 내가 남긴 발자국을 빼고는 손대지 않은 채로, 우리는 곳곳에서 엄청나게 쌓인 먼지 말고는 아무것도 찾지 못했다."], ["Never once did the dogs exhibit any symptom of uneasiness, and even when we returned to the chapel they frisked about as though they had been rabbit-hunting in a summer wood.", "개들은 단 한 번도 불안한 기색을 보이지 않았고, 우리가 예배당으로 돌아왔을 때조차 마치 여름 숲에서 토끼 사냥이라도 한 양 폴짝거렸다."]], [["The morning was quickening in the east when we emerged from the front. Dr.", "우리가 정면으로 빠져나왔을 때 동녘에서 아침이 빠르게 밝아오고 있었다."], ["Van Helsing had taken the key of the hall-door from the bunch, and locked the door in orthodox fashion, putting the key into his pocket when he had done.", "반 헬싱 박사는 꾸러미에서 현관문 열쇠를 빼내, 정석대로 문을 잠그고는 그것을 끝내자 열쇠를 주머니에 넣었다."]], [["“So far,”", "\"여기까지,\""], ["he said, “our night has been eminently successful.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"우리의 밤은 더없이 성공적이었습니다."], ["No harm has come to us such as I feared might be and yet we have ascertained how many boxes are missing.", "내가 두려워했을 법한 어떤 해도 우리에게 닥치지 않았고, 게다가 우리는 상자가 몇 개나 사라졌는지 알아냈습니다."], ["More than all do I rejoice that this, our first--and perhaps our most difficult and dangerous--step has been accomplished without the bringing thereinto our most sweet Madam Mina or troubling her waking or sleeping thoughts with sights and sounds and smells of horror which she might never forget.", "무엇보다도 나는, 우리의 이 첫걸음 — 어쩌면 가장 어렵고 위험한 걸음 — 이, 우리의 더없이 다정한 미나 부인을 끌어들이지 않고, 또 그녀가 결코 잊지 못할 공포의 광경과 소리와 냄새로 그녀의 깨어 있을 때나 잠든 때의 생각을 어지럽히지도 않고 이루어진 것이 기쁩니다."], ["One lesson, too, we have learned, if it be allowable to argue a particulari: that the brute beasts which are to the Count’s command are yet themselves not amenable to his spiritual power; for look, these rats that would come to his call, just as from his castle top he summon the wolves to your going and to that poor mother’s cry, though they come to him, they run pell-mell from the so little dogs of my friend Arthur.", "또한 우리는 한 가지 교훈도 배웠으니, 특수한 것에서 추론하는 것이 허용된다면 이렇습니다. 백작의 명령을 따르는 그 짐승들조차 그의 영적인 힘에는 굴복하지 않는다는 것입니다. 보십시오, 그의 부름에 모여드는 이 쥐들은, 그가 성탑 꼭대기에서 당신을 향해 늑대들을 불러내고 또 저 가엾은 어미의 울부짖음을 부른 것과 꼭 마찬가지로, 그에게 모여들면서도, 내 친구 아서의 그토록 작은 개들 앞에서는 죽기 살기로 달아납니다."], ["We have other matters before us, other dangers, other fears; and that monster--he has not used his power over the brute world for the only or the last time to-night.", "우리 앞에는 다른 일들, 다른 위험들, 다른 두려움들이 있습니다. 그리고 저 괴물 — 그는 오늘 밤이 짐승의 세계에 대한 자신의 힘을 쓰는 유일한, 혹은 마지막 때로 삼지는 않을 것입니다."], ["So be it that he has gone elsewhere. Good!", "그가 다른 데로 갔다면, 좋습니다!"], ["It has given us opportunity to cry ‘check’ in some ways in this chess game, which we play for the stake of human souls.", "그것은 우리에게, 인간의 영혼을 걸고 두는 이 체스 경기에서 여러모로 '장군'을 부를 기회를 준 것이니까요."], ["And now let us go home.", "자, 이제 집으로 갑시다."], ["The dawn is close at hand, and we have reason to be content with our first night’s work.", "새벽이 코앞이고, 우리는 첫날 밤의 일에 만족할 만한 이유가 있습니다."], ["It may be ordained that we have many nights and days to follow, if full of peril; but we must go on, and from no danger shall we shrink.”", "위험으로 가득할지언정, 앞으로 숱한 밤과 낮을 더 보내야 하도록 정해져 있을지도 모릅니다. 그러나 우리는 나아가야 하며, 어떤 위험 앞에서도 우리는 움츠러들지 않을 것입니다.\""]], [["The house was silent when we got back, save for some poor creature who was screaming away in one of the distant wards, and a low, moaning sound from Renfield’s room.", "우리가 돌아왔을 때 그 집은 고요했다. 다만 멀리 떨어진 병동 하나에서 어떤 가엾은 자가 비명을 질러대고 있었고, 렌필드의 방에서 낮게 신음하는 소리가 들려올 뿐이었다."], ["The poor wretch was doubtless torturing himself, after the manner of the insane, with needless thoughts of pain.", "그 가엾은 자는 분명, 미치광이들이 으레 그러하듯 부질없는 고통의 상념으로 스스로를 괴롭히고 있었으리라."]], [["I came tiptoe into our own room, and found Mina asleep, breathing so softly that I had to put my ear down to hear it.", "나는 발끝으로 우리 방에 들어가, 미나가 잠들어 있는 것을 보았는데, 어찌나 부드럽게 숨을 쉬는지 귀를 가까이 대야 들릴 정도였다."], ["She looks paler than usual.", "그녀는 평소보다 창백해 보인다."], ["I hope the meeting to-night has not upset her.", "오늘 밤의 모임이 그녀를 어지럽히지 않았기를 바란다."], ["I am truly thankful that she is to be left out of our future work, and even of our deliberations.", "나는 그녀가 우리의 앞으로의 일에서, 심지어 우리의 의논에서까지 빠지게 된 것이 참으로 고맙다."], ["It is too great a strain for a woman to bear.", "그것은 여자가 견디기에는 너무도 큰 부담이다."], ["I did not think so at first, but I know better now.", "처음에는 그렇게 생각하지 않았으나, 이제는 더 잘 알게 되었다."], ["Therefore I am glad that it is settled.", "그러니 그것이 매듭지어진 것이 기쁘다."], ["There may be things which would frighten her to hear; and yet to conceal them from her might be worse than to tell her if once she suspected that there was any concealment.", "그녀가 들으면 겁에 질릴 만한 일들이 있을지도 모른다. 그렇다고 그것을 그녀에게 숨기는 것은, 일단 그녀가 무언가 감추는 것이 있다고 의심하게 된다면, 차라리 말해주는 것보다 더 나쁠지도 모른다."], ["Henceforth our work is to be a sealed book to her, till at least such time as we can tell her that all is finished, and the earth free from a monster of the nether world.", "이제부터 우리의 일은 그녀에게 봉인된 책이 될 것이다. 적어도 모든 것이 끝났고 이 땅이 저승의 괴물에게서 풀려났다고 우리가 그녀에게 말해줄 수 있는 그때까지는."], ["I daresay it will be difficult to begin to keep silence after such confidence as ours; but I must be resolute, and to-morrow I shall keep dark over to-night’s doings, and shall refuse to speak of anything that has happened.", "우리처럼 서로 그토록 흉금을 터놓던 사이에 침묵을 지키기 시작하기란 어려우리라. 그러나 나는 단호해야 한다. 내일 나는 오늘 밤의 일에 대해 입을 다물 것이며, 무슨 일이 있었든 그것을 말하기를 거부할 것이다."], ["I rest on the sofa, so as not to disturb her.", "나는 그녀를 깨우지 않으려고 소파에 누워 쉰다."]], [["1 October, later.--I suppose it was natural that we should have all overslept ourselves, for the day was a busy one, and the night had no rest at all.", "10월 1일, 나중에. — 우리가 모두 늦잠을 잔 것은 당연한 일이었으리라. 그날은 분주했고, 밤에는 도무지 쉬지 못했으니 말이다."], ["Even Mina must have felt its exhaustion, for though I slept till the sun was high, I was awake before her, and had to call two or three times before she awoke.", "미나조차 그 피로를 느꼈음에 틀림없으니, 나는 해가 높이 뜰 때까지 잤음에도 그녀보다 먼저 깨어, 그녀가 깨어나기 전에 두세 번이나 불러야 했기 때문이다."], ["Indeed, she was so sound asleep that for a few seconds she did not recognize me, but looked at me with a sort of blank terror, as one looks who has been waked out of a bad dream.", "실로 그녀가 어찌나 깊이 잠들었던지, 잠깐 동안은 나를 알아보지 못하고, 마치 악몽에서 깨어난 이가 그러하듯 일종의 멍한 공포의 눈빛으로 나를 바라보았다."], ["She complained a little of being tired, and I let her rest till later in the day.", "그녀는 피곤하다고 조금 투정을 부렸고, 나는 그녀를 한낮까지 쉬게 두었다."], ["We now know of twenty-one boxes having been removed, and if it be that several were taken in any of these removals we may be able to trace them all.", "우리는 이제 스물한 개의 상자가 옮겨졌음을 알고 있으며, 만일 이 운반들 가운데 어느 한 번에 여러 개가 함께 실려 갔다면 우리는 그것들을 모두 추적할 수 있을지도 모른다."], ["Such will, of course, immensely simplify our labour, and the sooner the matter is attended to the better.", "그렇게 되면 물론 우리의 수고가 엄청나게 덜어질 것이고, 이 일은 빨리 처리할수록 좋다."], ["I shall look up Thomas Snelling to-day.", "나는 오늘 토머스 스넬링을 찾아볼 것이다."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["1 October.--It was towards noon when I was awakened by the Professor walking into my room.", "10월 1일. — 교수가 내 방으로 걸어 들어와 나를 깨웠을 때는 정오 무렵이었다."], ["He was more jolly and cheerful than usual, and it is quite evident that last night’s work has helped to take some of the brooding weight off his mind.", "그는 평소보다 더 유쾌하고 밝았으니, 지난밤의 일이 그의 마음에서 골똘히 짓누르던 무게를 얼마간 덜어준 것이 분명했다."], ["After going over the adventure of the night he suddenly said:--", "그는 지난밤의 모험을 죽 되짚어본 뒤 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“Your patient interests me much.", "\"자네 환자가 나에게 무척 흥미롭네."], ["May it be that with you I visit him this morning?", "오늘 아침에 자네와 함께 그를 찾아가도 되겠나?"], ["Or if that you are too occupy, I can go alone if it may be.", "아니면 자네가 너무 바쁘다면, 괜찮다면 나 혼자라도 가겠네."], ["It is a new experience to me to find a lunatic who talk philosophy, and reason so sound.”", "철학을 논하고 그토록 조리 있게 따지는 미치광이를 만나는 것은 나에게 새로운 경험일세.\""], ["I had some work to do which pressed, so I told him that if he would go alone I would be glad, as then I should not have to keep him waiting; so I called an attendant and gave him the necessary instructions.", "나는 급히 처리해야 할 일이 좀 있었으므로, 그가 혼자 가준다면 기쁘겠다고 말했다. 그래야 그를 기다리게 하지 않아도 될 테니 말이다. 그래서 나는 간병인을 불러 필요한 지시를 내렸다."], ["Before the Professor left the room I cautioned him against getting any false impression from my patient.", "교수가 방을 나서기 전에 나는 내 환자에게서 행여 잘못된 인상을 받지 않도록 그에게 주의를 주었다. \"하지만,\""], ["“But,” he answered, “I want him to talk of himself and of his delusion as to consuming live things.", "하고 그가 답했다. \"나는 그가 자기 자신에 대해, 그리고 살아 있는 것을 먹어치우는 그의 망상에 대해 이야기하기를 바라네."], ["He said to Madam Mina, as I see in your diary of yesterday, that he had once had such a belief.", "어제 자네 일기에서 보니, 그가 미나 부인에게 한때 그런 믿음을 가졌었다고 말했더군."], ["Why do you smile, friend John?”", "왜 웃나, 존?\""]], [["“Excuse me,”", "\"용서하게,\""], ["I said, “but the answer is here.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"그러나 그 답은 여기 있네.\""], ["I laid my hand on the type-written matter.", "나는 그 타자로 친 글뭉치 위에 손을 얹었다."], ["“When our sane and learned lunatic made that very statement of how he used to consume life, his mouth was actually nauseous with the flies and spiders which he had eaten just before Mrs. Harker entered the room.”", "\"우리의 제정신이고 박식한 미치광이가 자기가 한때 생명을 어떻게 먹어치웠는지 바로 그렇게 진술했을 때, 그의 입은 사실 하커 부인이 방에 들어오기 직전에 그가 먹은 파리와 거미로 메스꺼운 상태였다네.\""], ["Van Helsing smiled in turn. “Good!”", "이번에는 반 헬싱이 웃었다. \"좋아!\""], ["he said. “Your memory is true, friend John.", "하고 그가 말했다. \"자네 기억이 정확하군, 존."], ["I should have remembered.", "내가 기억했어야 했는데."], ["And yet it is this very obliquity of thought and memory which makes mental disease such a fascinating study.", "그러나 정신병을 그토록 매혹적인 연구 대상으로 만드는 것은 바로 이 사고와 기억의 비뚤어짐일세."], ["Perhaps I may gain more knowledge out of the folly of this madman than I shall from the teaching of the most wise.", "어쩌면 나는 가장 지혜로운 자의 가르침에서보다 이 미치광이의 어리석음에서 더 많은 지식을 얻을지도 모르네."], ["Who knows?” I went on with my work, and before long was through that in hand.", "누가 알겠나?\" 나는 일을 계속했고, 머지않아 손에 쥔 것을 끝마쳤다."], ["It seemed that the time had been very short indeed, but there was Van Helsing back in the study. “Do I interrupt?”", "시간이 참으로 짧게 지난 듯했으나, 반 헬싱이 서재로 돌아와 있었다. \"내가 방해가 되나?\""], ["he asked politely as he stood at the door.", "하고 그가 문가에 서서 정중하게 물었다."]], [["“Not at all,” I answered.", "\"전혀,\" 하고 내가 답했다."], ["“Come in.", "\"들어오게."], ["My work is finished, and I am free.", "내 일은 끝났고, 나는 한가해."], ["I can go with you now, if you like.", "원한다면 지금 자네와 함께 갈 수 있네.\""]], [["“It is needless; I have seen him!”", "\"그럴 필요 없네. 나는 그를 보았으니!\""]], [["“Well?”", "\"그래서?\""]], [["“I fear that he does not appraise me at much.", "\"내 생각에 그는 나를 대수롭지 않게 여기는 것 같네."], ["Our interview was short.", "우리의 면담은 짧았어."], ["When I entered his room he was sitting on a stool in the centre, with his elbows on his knees, and his face was the picture of sullen discontent.", "내가 그의 방에 들어갔을 때 그는 한가운데 등받이 없는 의자에 앉아 무릎에 팔꿈치를 괴고 있었는데, 그 얼굴은 시무룩한 불만의 표본이었네."], ["I spoke to him as cheerfully as I could, and with such a measure of respect as I could assume.", "나는 되도록 명랑하게, 또 내가 짐짓 꾸밀 수 있는 만큼의 경의를 담아 그에게 말을 걸었네."], ["He made no reply whatever.", "그는 아무런 대답도 하지 않았어."], ["“Don’t you know me?” I asked.", "'날 모르겠소?' 하고 내가 물었네."], ["His answer was not reassuring: “I know you well enough; you are the old fool Van Helsing.", "그의 대답은 안심이 되는 것이 아니었지. '당신을 잘 알다마다. 당신은 그 늙은 바보 반 헬싱이지."], ["I wish you would take yourself and your idiotic brain theories somewhere else.", "당신 자신과 당신의 그 멍청한 두뇌 이론들을 어디 다른 데로 가져가버렸으면 좋겠군."], ["Damn all thick-headed Dutchmen!”", "머리 둔한 네덜란드인들은 다 빌어먹을!'"], ["Not a word more would he say, but sat in his implacable sullenness as indifferent to me as though I had not been in the room at all.", "그는 더는 한마디도 하지 않고, 마치 내가 방 안에 있지도 않은 양 나에게 무심한 채, 그 누그러질 줄 모르는 시무룩함 속에 앉아 있었네."], ["Thus departed for this time my chance of much learning from this so clever lunatic; so I shall go, if I may, and cheer myself with a few happy words with that sweet soul Madam Mina.", "이렇게 이번에는 이 영리한 미치광이에게서 많은 것을 배울 내 기회가 사라졌네. 그러니 괜찮다면 나는 가서, 저 사랑스러운 영혼 미나 부인과 몇 마디 즐거운 말이나 나누며 기운을 차리겠네."], ["Friend John, it does rejoice me unspeakable that she is no more to be pained, no more to be worried with our terrible things.", "존, 그녀가 더는 아파하지 않아도 되고, 우리의 그 끔찍한 일들로 더는 시달리지 않아도 된다는 것이 나에게는 이루 말할 수 없이 기쁘다네."], ["Though we shall much miss her help, it is better so.”", "우리가 그녀의 도움을 무척 그리워하게 되겠지만, 이렇게 하는 편이 낫네.\""]], [["“I agree with you with all my heart,”", "\"진심으로 자네 말에 동의하네,\""], ["I answered earnestly, for I did not want him to weaken in this matter. “Mrs. Harker is better out of it.", "하고 나는 그가 이 문제에서 마음이 약해지기를 바라지 않았으므로 진지하게 답했다. \"하커 부인은 이 일에서 빠져 있는 편이 낫네."], ["Things are quite bad enough for us, all men of the world, and who have been in many tight places in our time; but it is no place for a woman, and if she had remained in touch with the affair, it would in time infallibly have wrecked her.”", "우리에게는, 모두 세상 물정에 밝고 한평생 숱한 위급한 상황을 겪어온 사내들에게조차 사정이 꽤나 고약하지. 그러나 이곳은 여자가 있을 자리가 아닐세. 만일 그녀가 이 일과 계속 맞닿아 있었다면, 머지않아 어김없이 그녀는 망가졌을 걸세.\""]], [["So Van Helsing has gone to confer with Mrs. Harker and Harker; Quincey and Art are all out following up the clues as to the earth-boxes.", "그리하여 반 헬싱은 하커 부인 및 하커와 의논하러 갔고, 퀸시와 아트는 흙 상자에 관한 단서를 추적하러 모두 나가 있다."], ["I shall finish my round of work and we shall meet to-night.", "나는 내 일을 한 바퀴 마저 돌고, 우리는 오늘 밤에 만날 것이다."]], [["Mina Harker’s Journal.", "「미나 하커의 일기」"]], [["1 October.--It is strange to me to be kept in the dark as I am to-day; after Jonathan’s full confidence for so many years, to see him manifestly avoid certain matters, and those the most vital of all.", "10월 1일. — 오늘처럼 내가 깜깜한 채로 따돌림당하는 것은 나에게 낯선 일이다. 조너선이 그토록 여러 해 동안 나에게 모든 것을 터놓아온 끝에, 그가 어떤 일들을, 그것도 가장 중요한 일들을 빤히 피하는 것을 보다니."], ["This morning I slept late after the fatigues of yesterday, and though Jonathan was late too, he was the earlier.", "오늘 아침 나는 어제의 피로 끝에 늦잠을 잤고, 조너선도 늦었으나 그가 더 일찍 일어났다."], ["He spoke to me before he went out, never more sweetly or tenderly, but he never mentioned a word of what had happened in the visit to the Count’s house.", "그는 나가기 전에 나에게 말을 걸었는데, 어느 때보다 다정하고 따뜻했으나, 백작의 집을 찾아갔을 때 무슨 일이 있었는지는 한마디도 꺼내지 않았다."], ["And yet he must have known how terribly anxious I was. Poor dear fellow!", "그런데도 그는 내가 얼마나 끔찍이 마음을 졸이고 있는지 분명 알았으리라."], ["I suppose it must have distressed him even more than it did me.", "가엾은 사람! 짐작건대 그것은 나보다 그를 더 괴롭혔을 것이다."], ["They all agreed that it was best that I should not be drawn further into this awful work, and I acquiesced.", "그들은 모두 내가 이 끔찍한 일에 더 끌려 들어가지 않는 편이 최선이라는 데 뜻을 모았고, 나도 묵묵히 따랐다."], ["But to think that he keeps anything from me!", "그러나 그가 나에게 무언가를 숨긴다고 생각하다니!"], ["And now I am crying like a silly fool, when I know it comes from my husband’s great love and from the good, good wishes of those other strong men.", "그리고 이제 나는 바보처럼 울고 있다. 그것이 내 남편의 크나큰 사랑에서, 그리고 저 다른 굳센 사내들의 선하고도 선한 바람에서 나온 것임을 알면서도 말이다."]], [["That has done me good.", "그렇게 우니 마음이 좀 나아졌다."], ["Well, some day Jonathan will tell me all; and lest it should ever be that he should think for a moment that I kept anything from him, I still keep my journal as usual.", "자, 언젠가 조너선이 나에게 모든 것을 말해줄 것이다. 그리고 행여 그가 한순간이라도 내가 그에게 무언가를 숨긴다고 여기는 일이 없도록, 나는 여전히 평소처럼 일기를 쓴다."], ["Then if he has feared of my trust I shall show it to him, with every thought of my heart put down for his dear eyes to read.", "그러면 만일 그가 내 신뢰를 의심하게 되거든, 나는 내 마음속의 온갖 생각을 그의 사랑스러운 눈이 읽도록 적어 그에게 보여줄 것이다."], ["I feel strangely sad and low-spirited to-day.", "오늘 나는 묘하게 슬프고 풀이 죽었다."], ["I suppose it is the reaction from the terrible excitement.", "짐작건대 그 끔찍한 흥분에 대한 반작용이리라."]], [["Last night I went to bed when the men had gone, simply because they told me to.", "지난밤 사내들이 떠난 뒤 나는 잠자리에 들었는데, 그저 그들이 그러라고 했기 때문이다."], ["I didn’t feel sleepy, and I did feel full of devouring anxiety.", "졸리지는 않았고, 오히려 사람을 집어삼킬 듯한 불안으로 가득했다."], ["I kept thinking over everything that has been ever since Jonathan came to see me in London, and it all seems like a horrible tragedy, with fate pressing on relentlessly to some destined end.", "나는 조너선이 런던으로 나를 보러 온 이래로 줄곧 있어온 모든 일을 거듭 곱씹었고, 그 모든 것이 마치 운명이 어떤 정해진 종말을 향해 가차 없이 밀어붙이는, 끔찍한 비극처럼만 여겨졌다."], ["Everything that one does seems, no matter how right it may be, to bring on the very thing which is most to be deplored.", "사람이 하는 모든 일은, 그것이 아무리 옳을지언정, 가장 통탄할 바로 그 일을 불러오는 것만 같다."], ["If I hadn’t gone to Whitby, perhaps poor dear Lucy would be with us now.", "만일 내가 휘트비에 가지 않았더라면, 어쩌면 가엾은 루시가 지금 우리 곁에 있을지도 모른다."], ["She hadn’t taken to visiting the churchyard till I came, and if she hadn’t come there in the day-time with me she wouldn’t have walked there in her sleep; and if she hadn’t gone there at night and asleep, that monster couldn’t have destroyed her as he did.", "그녀는 내가 오기 전까지는 그 묘지를 드나들지 않았고, 만일 그녀가 낮에 나와 함께 거기에 가지 않았더라면 잠결에 거기로 걸어가지도 않았을 것이며, 만일 그녀가 밤에 잠든 채로 거기에 가지 않았더라면 그 괴물도 그녀를 그렇게 파멸시키지 못했을 것이다."], ["Oh, why did I ever go to Whitby?", "아, 나는 어쩌자고 휘트비에 갔던가?"], ["There now, crying again! I wonder what has come over me to-day.", "자, 또 운다! 오늘 나에게 대체 무슨 일이 일어난 건지 모르겠다."], ["I must hide it from Jonathan, for if he knew that I had been crying twice in one morning--I, who never cried on my own account, and whom he has never caused to shed a tear--the dear fellow would fret his heart out.", "나는 이것을 조너선에게 숨겨야 한다. 만일 그가 내가 한 아침에 두 번이나 울었다는 것을 안다면 — 제 일로는 운 적이 없고, 그가 눈물 한 방울 흘리게 한 적도 없는 내가 말이다 — 그 사랑스러운 사람은 애를 태우다 못해 가슴이 다 닳을 것이다."], ["I shall put a bold face on, and if I do feel weepy, he shall never see it.", "나는 대담한 얼굴을 꾸밀 것이며, 설령 울고 싶은 기분이 들더라도 그가 결코 그것을 보게 하지는 않을 것이다."], ["I suppose it is one of the lessons that we poor women have to learn....", "짐작건대 그것은 우리 가엾은 여자들이 배워야 하는 교훈 가운데 하나이리라……."]], [["I can’t quite remember how I fell asleep last night.", "나는 지난밤 어떻게 잠들었는지 잘 기억나지 않는다."], ["I remember hearing the sudden barking of the dogs and a lot of queer sounds, like praying on a very tumultuous scale, from Mr. Renfield’s room, which is somewhere under this.", "개들이 갑자기 짖던 소리와, 이 아래 어딘가에 있는 렌필드 씨의 방에서 들려온, 마치 몹시 소란스러운 규모로 기도라도 하는 듯한 온갖 기이한 소리들을 들은 것은 기억난다."], ["And then there was silence over everything, silence so profound that it startled me, and I got up and looked out of the window.", "그러고 나서 모든 것 위에 침묵이 깔렸는데, 어찌나 깊은 침묵이었던지 나는 흠칫 놀라 일어나 창밖을 내다보았다."], ["All was dark and silent, the black shadows thrown by the moonlight seeming full of a silent mystery of their own.", "모든 것이 어둡고 고요했으며, 달빛이 드리운 검은 그림자들은 저마다 어떤 말없는 신비로 가득 차 있는 듯했다."], ["Not a thing seemed to be stirring, but all to be grim and fixed as death or fate; so that a thin streak of white mist, that crept with almost imperceptible slowness across the grass towards the house, seemed to have a sentience and a vitality of its own.", "어느 것 하나 움직이는 것 없이, 모두가 죽음이나 운명처럼 음산하게 굳어 있는 듯했다. 그래서 잔디밭을 가로질러 거의 알아챌 수 없을 만큼 느리게 집 쪽으로 기어오는 한 가닥 가느다란 흰 안개가, 마치 제 나름의 감각과 생명력을 지닌 것처럼 보였다."], ["I think that the digression of my thoughts must have done me good, for when I got back to bed I found a lethargy creeping over me.", "내 생각의 곁길이 나에게 도움이 되었던 모양이다. 잠자리로 돌아왔을 때 나는 어떤 나른함이 나에게 스며드는 것을 느꼈으니 말이다."], ["I lay a while, but could not quite sleep, so I got out and looked out of the window again.", "나는 한동안 누워 있었으나 도무지 잠들지 못해, 일어나 다시 창밖을 내다보았다."], ["The mist was spreading, and was now close up to the house, so that I could see it lying thick against the wall, as though it were stealing up to the windows.", "안개는 번지고 있었고, 이제 집 가까이까지 다가와, 마치 창문으로 슬그머니 기어오르기라도 하듯 벽에 두껍게 들러붙어 있는 것이 보였다."], ["The poor man was more loud than ever, and though I could not distinguish a word he said, I could in some way recognise in his tones some passionate entreaty on his part.", "그 가엾은 사람은 어느 때보다 더 시끄러웠는데, 그가 하는 말을 한마디도 알아들을 수는 없었으나, 그의 어조에서 어쩐지 어떤 격정적인 애원을 알아챌 수 있었다."], ["Then there was the sound of a struggle, and I knew that the attendants were dealing with him.", "그러고 나서 몸싸움하는 소리가 들렸으니, 나는 간병인들이 그를 다루고 있음을 알았다."], ["I was so frightened that I crept into bed, and pulled the clothes over my head, putting my fingers in my ears.", "나는 어찌나 겁이 났던지 살그머니 잠자리로 기어들어, 손가락으로 귀를 막고 이불을 머리 위까지 끌어당겼다."], ["I was not then a bit sleepy, at least so I thought; but I must have fallen asleep, for, except dreams, I do not remember anything until the morning, when Jonathan woke me.", "그때 나는 조금도 졸리지 않았다. 적어도 나는 그렇게 생각했다. 그러나 나는 잠들었음에 틀림없으니, 꿈을 빼고는 아침에 조너선이 나를 깨울 때까지 아무것도 기억나지 않기 때문이다."], ["I think that it took me an effort and a little time to realise where I was, and that it was Jonathan who was bending over me.", "내가 어디에 있는지, 그리고 내 위로 몸을 굽힌 이가 조너선이라는 것을 깨닫는 데 얼마간 애와 약간의 시간이 든 것 같다."], ["My dream was very peculiar, and was almost typical of the way that waking thoughts become merged in, or continued in, dreams.", "내 꿈은 매우 기이했으며, 깨어 있을 때의 생각이 꿈속으로 녹아들거나 이어지는 방식을 거의 그대로 보여주는 것이었다."]], [["I thought that I was asleep, and waiting for Jonathan to come back.", "나는 내가 잠들어 있으며, 조너선이 돌아오기를 기다리고 있다고 생각했다."], ["I was very anxious about him, and I was powerless to act; my feet, and my hands, and my brain were weighted, so that nothing could proceed at the usual pace.", "나는 그가 몹시 걱정스러웠으나 어찌해볼 도리가 없었다. 내 발과 손과 머리가 무거이 짓눌려 있어, 무엇 하나 평소의 속도로 움직이질 못했다."], ["And so I slept uneasily and thought.", "그렇게 나는 불안하게 잠들어 생각에 잠겼다."], ["Then it began to dawn upon me that the air was heavy, and dank, and cold.", "그러다 공기가 무겁고 축축하고 차갑다는 것이 차츰 느껴지기 시작했다."], ["I put back the clothes from my face, and found, to my surprise, that all was dim around.", "나는 이불을 얼굴에서 젖혔고, 놀랍게도 주위가 온통 어슴푸레하다는 것을 알았다."], ["The gaslight which I had left lit for Jonathan, but turned down, came only like a tiny red spark through the fog, which had evidently grown thicker and poured into the room.", "조너선을 위해 켜두었으나 심지를 낮춰둔 가스등이, 분명 더 짙어져 방 안으로 쏟아져 든 안개를 뚫고 그저 자그마한 붉은 불꽃처럼만 비쳐 왔다."], ["Then it occurred to me that I had shut the window before I had come to bed.", "그러자 내가 잠자리에 들기 전에 창문을 닫았다는 것이 떠올랐다."], ["I would have got out to make certain on the point, but some leaden lethargy seemed to chain my limbs and even my will.", "나는 그 점을 확인하러 일어나려 했으나, 어떤 납덩이 같은 나른함이 내 사지를, 심지어 내 의지까지 옭아매는 듯했다."], ["I lay still and endured; that was all.", "나는 가만히 누워 견딜 뿐이었다. 그것이 전부였다."], ["I closed my eyes, but could still see through my eyelids.", "나는 눈을 감았으나, 그래도 감은 눈꺼풀 사이로 여전히 볼 수 있었다."], ["(It is wonderful what tricks our dreams play us, and how conveniently we can imagine.) The mist grew thicker and thicker and I could see now how it came in, for I could see it like smoke--or with the white energy of boiling water--pouring in, not through the window, but through the joinings of the door.", "(우리의 꿈이 우리에게 부리는 농간이란, 또 우리가 얼마나 편리하게 상상해낼 수 있는지란 놀라운 일이다.) 안개는 점점 더 짙어졌고, 나는 이제 그것이 어떻게 들어오는지 볼 수 있었다. 그것이 마치 연기처럼 — 혹은 끓는 물의 흰 기세로 — 창문이 아니라 문틈을 통해 쏟아져 드는 것이 보였기 때문이다."], ["It got thicker and thicker, till it seemed as if it became concentrated into a sort of pillar of cloud in the room, through the top of which I could see the light of the gas shining like a red eye.", "그것은 점점 더 짙어지더니, 마침내 방 안에서 일종의 구름 기둥으로 응집되는 듯했고, 그 꼭대기를 통해 나는 가스등 빛이 붉은 눈처럼 빛나는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Things began to whirl through my brain just as the cloudy column was now whirling in the room, and through it all came the scriptural words “a pillar of cloud by day and of fire by night.”", "그 구름 기둥이 이제 방 안에서 소용돌이치듯, 온갖 것들이 내 머릿속에서 소용돌이치기 시작했고, 그 모든 것을 뚫고 \"낮에는 구름 기둥, 밤에는 불기둥\"이라는 성경 구절이 떠올랐다."], ["Was it indeed some such spiritual guidance that was coming to me in my sleep?", "정말로 그런 어떤 영적인 인도가 잠결의 나에게 다가오고 있는 것이었을까?"], ["But the pillar was composed of both the day and the night-guiding, for the fire was in the red eye, which at the thought got a new fascination for me; till, as I looked, the fire divided, and seemed to shine on me through the fog like two red eyes, such as Lucy told me of in her momentary mental wandering when, on the cliff, the dying sunlight struck the windows of St.", "그러나 그 기둥은 낮의 인도와 밤의 인도를 다 갖추고 있었으니, 불은 그 붉은 눈 속에 있었고, 그 생각에 그 눈은 나에게 새로운 매혹으로 다가왔다. 그러다, 내가 바라보고 있자니, 그 불이 둘로 갈라져, 마치 안개를 뚫고 두 개의 붉은 눈처럼 나를 비추는 것 같았다. 바로 루시가, 절벽 위에서 스러져가는 햇빛이 성 마리아 교회의 창문을 비추던 그때, 잠깐 정신이 흐트러진 사이에 나에게 말해준 그런 눈이었다."], ["Mary’s Church. Suddenly the horror burst upon me that it was thus that Jonathan had seen those awful women growing into reality through the whirling mist in the moonlight, and in my dream I must have fainted, for all became black darkness.", "그 끔찍한 일들이 마치 조너선이 본 그 무시무시한 여인들이 달빛 속에서 소용돌이치는 안개를 뚫고 실체로 변해가던 것과 똑같았다는 공포가 갑자기 나를 덮쳤고, 꿈속에서 나는 정신을 잃었음에 틀림없으니, 모든 것이 새카만 어둠이 되어버렸기 때문이다."], ["The last conscious effort which imagination made was to show me a livid white face bending over me out of the mist.", "상상이 부린 마지막 의식적인 안간힘은, 안개 밖에서 나를 향해 굽어보는 검푸르게 핏기 없는 흰 얼굴 하나를 나에게 보여준 것이었다."], ["I must be careful of such dreams, for they would unseat one’s reason if there were too much of them. I would get Dr.", "나는 그런 꿈들을 조심해야 한다. 그것들이 너무 많아지면 사람의 이성을 흔들어놓을 테니 말이다."], ["Van Helsing or Dr. Seward to prescribe something for me which would make me sleep, only that I fear to alarm them.", "나는 반 헬싱 박사나 수어드 박사에게 잠들게 해줄 무언가를 처방받았으면 싶지만, 그들을 놀라게 할까 두렵다."], ["Such a dream at the present time would become woven into their fears for me.", "지금 같은 때에 그런 꿈은 나를 향한 그들의 두려움 속에 짜여 들고 말 것이다."], ["To-night I shall strive hard to sleep naturally.", "오늘 밤 나는 자연스럽게 잠들려고 무던히 애써볼 것이다."], ["If I do not, I shall to-morrow night get them to give me a dose of chloral; that cannot hurt me for once, and it will give me a good night’s sleep.", "만일 그러지 못한다면, 내일 밤에는 그들에게 클로랄을 한 번 복용하게 해달라고 청할 것이다. 한 번쯤이야 나를 해치지 않을 것이고, 푹 잘 수 있게 해줄 것이다."], ["Last night tired me more than if I had not slept at all.", "지난밤은 차라리 한숨도 자지 않았을 때보다 나를 더 지치게 했다."]], [["2 October 10 p. m.--Last night I slept, but did not dream.", "10월 2일, 밤 10시. — 지난밤 나는 잤으나, 꿈은 꾸지 않았다."], ["I must have slept soundly, for I was not waked by Jonathan coming to bed; but the sleep has not refreshed me, for to-day I feel terribly weak and spiritless.", "깊이 잠들었음에 틀림없으니, 조너선이 잠자리에 드는데도 나는 깨지 않았기 때문이다. 그러나 그 잠은 나를 개운하게 하지 못했으니, 오늘 나는 끔찍하게 약하고 맥이 없다."], ["I spent all yesterday trying to read, or lying down dozing.", "나는 어제 온종일 책을 읽으려 애쓰거나, 누워서 꾸벅꾸벅 졸며 보냈다."], ["In the afternoon Mr. Renfield asked if he might see me.", "오후에 렌필드 씨가 나를 만날 수 있겠느냐고 청했다."], ["Poor man, he was very gentle, and when I came away he kissed my hand and bade God bless me.", "가엾은 사람, 그는 매우 점잖았고, 내가 나올 때 내 손에 입 맞추며 하느님의 가호를 빌어주었다."], ["Some way it affected me much; I am crying when I think of him.", "어쩐지 그것이 나를 무척 흔들어놓았다. 그를 생각하면 눈물이 난다."], ["This is a new weakness, of which I must be careful.", "이것은 새로운 나약함이니, 나는 이를 조심해야 한다."], ["Jonathan would be miserable if he knew I had been crying.", "조너선이 내가 울었다는 것을 안다면 비참해할 것이다."], ["He and the others were out till dinner-time, and they all came in tired.", "그와 다른 이들은 저녁 식사 때까지 나가 있었고, 모두 지친 채로 들어왔다."], ["I did what I could to brighten them up, and I suppose that the effort did me good, for I forgot how tired I was.", "나는 그들의 기운을 북돋우려고 할 수 있는 일을 했는데, 짐작건대 그 노력이 나에게 도움이 되었던 모양이다."], ["After dinner they sent me to bed, and all went off to smoke together, as they said, but I knew that they wanted to tell each other of what had occurred to each during the day; I could see from Jonathan’s manner that he had something important to communicate.", "내가 얼마나 지쳐 있었는지를 잊었으니 말이다. 저녁 식사 후 그들은 나를 잠자리로 보냈고, 그들 말로는 다 함께 담배를 피우러 갔다고 했으나, 나는 그들이 그날 각자에게 일어난 일을 서로 이야기하고 싶어 한다는 것을 알았다. 조너선의 태도로 보아 그가 무언가 중요한 것을 전할 것이 있음을 알 수 있었다."], ["I was not so sleepy as I should have been; so before they went I asked Dr. Seward to give me a little opiate of some kind, as I had not slept well the night before.", "나는 마땅히 졸려야 할 만큼 졸리지는 않았다. 그래서 그들이 가기 전에 나는 수어드 박사에게, 전날 밤 잘 자지 못했으니 어떤 종류든 가벼운 진정제를 좀 달라고 청했다."], ["He very kindly made me up a sleeping draught, which he gave to me, telling me that it would do me no harm, as it was very mild....", "그는 매우 친절하게 수면용 물약을 지어 나에게 주며, 매우 순한 것이니 나를 해치지 않을 거라고 일러주었다……"], ["I have taken it, and am waiting for sleep, which still keeps aloof.", "나는 그것을 마셨고, 여전히 멀찍이 비켜서 있는 잠을 기다리고 있다."], ["I hope I have not done wrong, for as sleep begins to flirt with me, a new fear comes: that I may have been foolish in thus depriving myself of the power of waking.", "나는 내가 잘못한 것이 아니기를 바란다. 잠이 나에게 추파를 던지기 시작하자 새로운 두려움이 밀려오기 때문이다. 이렇게 스스로 깨어날 힘을 앗아버린 것이 어리석은 짓이었을지도 모른다는 두려움이다."], ["I might want it. Here comes sleep.", "나에게 그 힘이 필요할지도 모르는데."], ["Good-night.", "잠이 온다. 안녕히."]], [["# JONATHAN HARKER’S JOURNAL", "# JONATHAN HARKER’S JOURNAL"]], [["1 October, evening.--I found Thomas Snelling in his house at Bethnal Green, but unhappily he was not in a condition to remember anything.", "「조너선 하커의 일기」 10월 1일, 저녁. — 베스널 그린에 있는 토머스 스넬링의 집에서 그를 찾아냈으나, 불행히도 그는 무엇 하나 기억할 만한 상태가 아니었다."], ["The very prospect of beer which my expected coming had opened to him had proved too much, and he had begun too early on his expected debauch.", "내가 온다는 기별에 맥주가 생긴다는 기대만으로도 너무 들떴던지, 예정된 술판을 너무 일찍 시작해버린 것이었다."], ["I learned, however, from his wife, who seemed a decent, poor soul, that he was only the assistant to Smollet, who of the two mates was the responsible person.", "다만 점잖고 가난해 보이는 그의 아내에게서, 그가 스몰릿의 조수에 지나지 않으며, 두 인부 가운데 책임 있는 쪽은 스몰릿이라는 사실을 알게 되었다."], ["So off I drove to Walworth, and found Mr. Joseph Smollet at home and in his shirtsleeves, taking a late tea out of a saucer.", "그래서 나는 월워스로 마차를 몰아갔고, 조지프 스몰릿 씨가 셔츠 바람으로 접시에 따라 늦은 차를 마시고 있는 것을 집에서 만났다."], ["He is a decent, intelligent fellow, distinctly a good, reliable type of workman, and with a headpiece of his own.", "그는 점잖고 영리한 사내로, 분명히 선량하고 믿음직한 노동자 부류였으며, 제 나름의 분별을 갖춘 인물이었다."], ["He remembered all about the incident of the boxes, and from a wonderful dog’s-eared notebook, which he produced from some mysterious receptacle about the seat of his trousers, and which had hieroglyphical entries in thick, half-obliterated pencil, he gave me the destinations of the boxes.", "그는 상자들에 얽힌 일을 죄다 기억하고 있었고, 바지 엉덩이 어름의 어떤 알 수 없는 주머니에서 꺼낸, 놀랍도록 귀퉁이가 접힌 수첩 — 굵고 거의 지워진 연필로 상형문자 같은 기록이 적힌 — 을 보고 상자들의 행선지를 일러주었다."], ["There were, he said, six in the cartload which he took from Carfax and left at 197, Chicksand Street, Mile End New Town, and another six which he deposited at Jamaica Lane, Bermondsey.", "그의 말로는 카팩스에서 실어 내어 마일 엔드 뉴 타운의 칙샌드 가 197번지에 부린 짐수레 하나에 여섯 개가 있었고, 또 버몬지의 자메이카 레인에 부린 것이 다시 여섯 개였다고 했다."], ["If then the Count meant to scatter these ghastly refuges of his over London, these places were chosen as the first of delivery, so that later he might distribute more fully.", "그렇다면 백작이 자신의 그 끔찍한 은신처들을 런던 전역에 흩뿌릴 작정이었다면, 이 장소들이 일차 배달지로 선택된 것이며, 나중에 더 폭넓게 분산시키려는 것이리라."], ["The systematic manner in which this was done made me think that he could not mean to confine himself to two sides of London.", "일이 이토록 체계적으로 이루어진 것을 보면, 그가 런던의 두 방면에만 자신을 한정시킬 작정은 아니었으리라는 생각이 들었다."], ["He was now fixed on the far east of the northern shore, on the east of the southern shore, and on the south.", "그는 이제 북안의 극동쪽, 남안의 동쪽, 그리고 남쪽에 자리를 잡은 것이었다."], ["The north and west were surely never meant to be left out of his diabolical scheme--let alone the City itself and the very heart of fashionable London in the south-west and west.", "북쪽과 서쪽이 그의 악마적인 계획에서 결코 빠질 리 없었다 — 시티 자체와, 남서쪽과 서쪽에 자리한 멋들어진 런던의 바로 심장부는 말할 것도 없었다."], ["I went back to Smollet, and asked him if he could tell us if any other boxes had been taken from Carfax.", "나는 스몰릿에게로 돌아가, 카팩스에서 실려 나간 다른 상자가 있었는지 일러줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["He replied:--", "그가 대답했다."]], [["“Well, guv’nor, you’ve treated me wery ’an’some”--I had given him half a sovereign--“an’ I’ll tell yer all I know.", "\"글쎄요, 나리, 저한테 아주 후하게 대해주셨으니까요\" — 나는 그에게 반 소버린을 주었던 것이다 — \"아는 건 죄다 말씀드리죠."], ["I heard a man by the name of Bloxam say four nights ago in the ’Are an’ ’Ounds, in Pincher’s Alley, as ’ow he an’ his mate ’ad ’ad a rare dusty job in a old ’ouse at Purfect.", "나흘 전 밤에 핀처스 골목 '토끼와 사냥개'에서 블록샘이라는 작자가 하는 말을 들었는데요, 자기랑 동료가 퍼플릿의 어떤 낡은 집에서 먼지투성이 고된 일을 했다더군요."], ["There ain’t a-many such jobs as this ’ere, an’ I’m thinkin’ that maybe Sam Bloxam could tell ye summut.”", "이런 일은 흔치가 않으니까, 어쩌면 샘 블록샘이 뭔가 일러드릴 수 있지 않을까 싶습니다요.\""], ["I asked if he could tell me where to find him. I told him that if he could get me the address it would be worth another half-sovereign to him.", "나는 그를 어디서 찾을 수 있는지 일러줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다. 주소를 구해다 주면 반 소버린을 더 줄 만한 값어치가 있을 거라고 했다."], ["So he gulped down the rest of his tea and stood up, saying that he was going to begin the search then and there.", "그러자 그는 남은 차를 단숨에 들이켜고는 일어서며, 지금 당장 여기서부터 찾아 나서겠다고 했다."], ["At the door he stopped, and said:--", "문간에서 그는 멈춰 서더니 말했다."]], [["“Look ’ere, guv’nor, there ain’t no sense in me a-keepin’ you ’ere.", "\"이거 보세요, 나리, 여기서 나리를 붙들어둘 까닭이 없습죠."], ["I may find Sam soon, or I mayn’t; but anyhow he ain’t like to be in a way to tell ye much to-night. Sam is a rare one when he starts on the booze.", "샘을 금방 찾을지도 모르고, 못 찾을지도 모릅니다요. 하지만 아무튼 오늘 밤엔 그 사람이 나리한테 뭘 많이 일러드릴 형편은 못 될 겁니다."], ["If you can give me a envelope with a stamp on it, and put yer address on it, I’ll find out where Sam is to be found and post it ye to-night.", "샘은 술이 들어가기 시작하면 아주 대단한 작자거든요. 우표 붙인 봉투에다 나리 주소를 적어 저한테 주시면, 샘이 어디 있는지 알아내서 오늘 밤 안으로 부쳐드리죠."], ["But ye’d better be up arter ’im soon in the mornin’, or maybe ye won’t ketch ’im; for Sam gets off main early, never mind the booze the night afore.”", "하지만 아침에 일찍 그 사람을 찾아가시는 게 나을 겁니다. 안 그러면 못 잡으실지도 몰라요. 샘은 간밤에 술을 아무리 마셔도 새벽같이 나가버리니까요.\""]], [["This was all practical, so one of the children went off with a penny to buy an envelope and a sheet of paper, and to keep the change.", "이건 죄다 실속 있는 말이어서, 아이 하나가 1페니를 받아 봉투 한 장과 종이 한 장을 사고 거스름돈은 가지러 나갔다."], ["When she came back, I addressed the envelope and stamped it, and when Smollet had again faithfully promised to post the address when found, I took my way to home.", "아이가 돌아오자 나는 봉투에 주소를 적고 우표를 붙였으며, 스몰릿이 다시 한 번 주소를 알아내는 대로 충실히 부쳐주겠노라 약속하자, 나는 집으로 향했다."], ["We’re on the track anyhow.", "어쨌든 우리는 자취를 좇고 있다."], ["I am tired to-night, and want sleep.", "오늘 밤은 피곤해서 자고 싶다."], ["Mina is fast asleep, and looks a little too pale; her eyes look as though she had been crying.", "미나는 곤히 잠들었는데, 얼굴빛이 좀 지나치게 창백하다. 눈가는 마치 울었던 것처럼 보인다."], ["Poor dear, I’ve no doubt it frets her to be kept in the dark, and it may make her doubly anxious about me and the others.", "가엾은 사람, 영문도 모른 채 캄캄한 데 갇혀 있는 것이 그녀를 애태우는 것이 분명하고, 그래서 나와 다른 이들에 대한 근심이 갑절로 깊어졌는지도 모른다."], ["But it is best as it is. It is better to be disappointed and worried in such a way now than to have her nerve broken.", "그러나 지금 이대로가 가장 나은 것이다. 지금 이런 식으로 서운하고 애가 타는 편이, 그녀의 신경이 무너지는 것보다는 낫다."], ["The doctors were quite right to insist on her being kept out of this dreadful business.", "의사들이 그녀를 이 끔찍한 일에서 떼어놓아야 한다고 고집한 것은 참으로 옳았다."], ["I must be firm, for on me this particular burden of silence must rest.", "나는 굳건해야 하니, 이 침묵의 짐은 다름 아닌 나에게 지워진 것이기 때문이다."], ["I shall not ever enter on the subject with her under any circumstances.", "나는 어떤 사정이 있더라도 그녀와 이 문제를 입에 올리지 않을 것이다."], ["Indeed, it may not be a hard task, after all, for she herself has become reticent on the subject, and has not spoken of the Count or his doings ever since we told her of our decision.", "사실 이것은 결국 그리 어려운 일이 아닐지도 모르니, 그녀 자신이 이 문제에 대해 말을 아끼게 되었고, 우리가 결정을 일러준 이후로는 백작이나 그의 소행에 대해 한마디도 하지 않았기 때문이다."]], [["2 October, evening.--A long and trying and exciting day.", "10월 2일, 저녁. — 길고, 고되고, 가슴 졸이는 하루였다."], ["By the first post I got my directed envelope with a dirty scrap of paper enclosed, on which was written with a carpenter’s pencil in a sprawling hand:--", "첫 우편으로 내가 주소를 적어 둔 봉투를 받았는데, 그 안에 더러운 종잇조각 하나가 들어 있었고, 거기엔 목수의 연필로 갈겨쓴 글씨가 적혀 있었다."]], [["“Sam Bloxam, Korkrans, 4, Poters Cort, Bartel Street, Walworth.", "\"샘 블록샘, 콕런 하숙집, 월워스 바텔 가 포터스 코트 4번지."], ["Arsk for the depite.”", "데피티를 찾으시오.\""]], [["I got the letter in bed, and rose without waking Mina.", "나는 침대에서 그 편지를 받았고, 미나를 깨우지 않은 채 일어났다."], ["She looked heavy and sleepy and pale, and far from well.", "그녀는 나른하고 졸려 보였으며, 창백한 데다 영 좋지 않은 안색이었다."], ["I determined not to wake her, but that, when I should return from this new search, I would arrange for her going back to Exeter.", "나는 그녀를 깨우지 않기로, 그리고 이번 새로운 탐색에서 돌아오면 그녀를 엑서터로 돌려보낼 채비를 하기로 마음먹었다."], ["I think she would be happier in our own home, with her daily tasks to interest her, than in being here amongst us and in ignorance. I only saw Dr.", "우리집에서 날마다 마음을 붙일 제 일거리를 가지고 지내는 편이, 영문도 모른 채 우리 틈에 끼여 여기 있는 것보다 그녀가 더 행복하리라는 생각이 든다."], ["Seward for a moment, and told him where I was off to, promising to come back and tell the rest so soon as I should have found out anything.", "수어드 박사는 잠깐밖에 보지 못했고, 어디로 가는지 일러주면서, 무엇이든 알아내는 대로 돌아와 나머지 사람들에게도 알리겠노라 약속했다."], ["I drove to Walworth and found, with some difficulty, Potter’s Court. Mr.", "나는 월워스로 마차를 몰았고, 다소 애를 먹은 끝에 포터스 코트를 찾아냈다."], ["Smollet’s spelling misled me, as I asked for Poter’s Court instead of Potter’s Court. However, when I had found the court, I had no difficulty in discovering Corcoran’s lodging-house.", "스몰릿의 철자가 나를 헷갈리게 했으니, 포터스 코트(Potter's Court)가 아니라 포터스 코트(Poter's Court)를 물었던 것이다. 그래도 그 골목을 찾고 나니, 콕런의 하숙집을 알아내는 데는 어려움이 없었다."], ["When I asked the man who came to the door for the “depite,” he shook his head, and said: “I dunno ’im.", "문간에 나온 사내에게 \"데피티\"를 찾는다고 했더니, 그는 고개를 저으며 말했다. \"그런 사람 몰라요."], ["There ain’t no such a person ’ere; I never ’eard of ’im in all my bloomin’ days.", "여기 그런 사람 없수다. 평생 그런 이름은 들어본 적도 없습니다요."], ["Don’t believe there ain’t nobody of that kind livin’ ere or anywheres.”", "여기든 어디든 그런 부류가 산다고는 믿어지지가 않네요.\""], ["I took out Smollet’s letter, and as I read it it seemed to me that the lesson of the spelling of the name of the court might guide me.", "나는 스몰릿의 편지를 꺼냈고, 그것을 읽으면서 그 골목 이름의 철자가 준 교훈이 길잡이가 될지도 모르겠다는 생각이 들었다. \"당신은 뭐 하는 사람이오?\""], ["“What are you?” I asked.", "하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“I’m the depity,” he answered.", "\"제가 데피티(부관리인)입니다요,\""], ["I saw at once that I was on the right track; phonetic spelling had again misled me.", "하고 그가 대답했다. 나는 곧 내가 제대로 짚었음을 알았다."], ["A half-crown tip put the deputy’s knowledge at my disposal, and I learned that Mr. Bloxam, who had slept off the remains of his beer on the previous night at Corcoran’s, had left for his work at Poplar at five o’clock that morning.", "소리 나는 대로 적은 철자가 또다시 나를 헷갈리게 한 것이었다. 반 크라운의 사례비가 그 부관리인의 지식을 나에게 내어주게 했고, 나는 블록샘 씨가 — 간밤에 콕런의 하숙집에서 마신 맥주 기운을 잠으로 떨쳐냈던 — 그날 아침 다섯 시에 포플러로 일하러 떠났음을 알게 되었다."], ["He could not tell me where the place of work was situated, but he had a vague idea that it was some kind of a “new-fangled ware’us”; and with this slender clue I had to start for Poplar.", "그는 일터가 어디인지는 일러주지 못했으나, 그것이 무슨 \"신식 창고\" 같은 것이라는 어렴풋한 짐작은 갖고 있었다. 이 빈약한 단서를 들고 나는 포플러로 떠나야 했다."], ["It was twelve o’clock before I got any satisfactory hint of such a building, and this I got at a coffee-shop, where some workmen were having their dinner.", "그런 건물에 대해 그럴듯한 단서나마 얻은 것은 열두 시가 다 되어서였고, 그것도 인부 몇이 점심을 먹고 있던 어느 찻집에서였다."], ["One of these suggested that there was being erected at Cross Angel Street a new “cold storage” building; and as this suited the condition of a “new-fangled ware’us,”", "그들 가운데 하나가 크로스 에인절 가에 새 \"냉장 보관\" 건물이 올라가고 있다고 일러주었는데, 이것이 \"신식 창고\"라는 조건에 들어맞기에, 나는 곧장 그리로 마차를 몰았다."], ["I at once drove to it. An interview with a surly gatekeeper and a surlier foreman, both of whom were appeased with the coin of the realm, put me on the track of Bloxam; he was sent for on my suggesting that I was willing to pay his day’s wages to his foreman for the privilege of asking him a few questions on a private matter.", "시무룩한 수위와 더 시무룩한 십장과 한 차례씩 이야기를 나누었는데, 둘 다 나라의 동전으로 누그러뜨리고 나니, 블록샘의 자취를 잡을 수 있었다. 사사로운 일로 그에게 몇 가지 물어볼 권리를 사는 셈치고 그의 하루 일당을 십장에게 치르겠다고 제안하자, 사람을 보내어 그를 불러왔다."], ["He was a smart enough fellow, though rough of speech and bearing.", "말투와 거동은 거칠었으나, 그는 제법 영리한 사내였다."], ["When I had promised to pay for his information and given him an earnest, he told me that he had made two journeys between Carfax and a house in Piccadilly, and had taken from this house to the latter nine great boxes--“main heavy ones”--with a horse and cart hired by him for this purpose.", "일러주는 대가를 치르겠노라 약속하고 선금을 쥐여주자, 그는 카팩스와 피커딜리의 어느 집 사이를 두 차례 오갔으며, 이 일을 위해 손수 빌린 말과 짐수레로 그 집에서 후자로 큰 상자 아홉 개 — \"엄청 무거운 것들\" — 를 실어 갔다고 일러주었다."], ["I asked him if he could tell me the number of the house in Piccadilly, to which he replied:--", "나는 피커딜리의 그 집 번지를 일러줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었고, 그는 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["“Well, guv’nor, I forgits the number, but it was only a few doors from a big white church or somethink of the kind, not long built.", "\"글쎄요, 나리, 번지는 잊어버렸지만요, 큰 흰 교회인가 뭔가 그런 거에서 몇 집밖에 안 떨어져 있었어요. 지은 지 얼마 안 된 거요."], ["It was a dusty old ’ouse, too, though nothin’ to the dustiness of the ’ouse we tooked the bloomin’ boxes from.”", "그것도 먼지투성이 낡은 집이긴 했지만요, 우리가 그 빌어먹을 상자들을 실어 낸 집의 먼지에 대면 아무것도 아니었습죠.\""]], [["“How did you get into the houses if they were both empty?”", "\"두 집 다 비어 있었다면, 어떻게 안으로 들어갔소?\""]], [["“There was the old party what engaged me a-waitin’ in the ’ouse at Purfleet.", "\"퍼플릿 집에는 저를 고용한 그 영감님이 기다리고 있었어요."], ["He ’elped me to lift the boxes and put them in the dray.", "영감님이 상자들 들어 올려서 짐수레에 싣는 걸 거들어주었습죠."], ["Curse me, but he was the strongest chap I ever struck, an’ him a old feller, with a white moustache, one that thin you would think he couldn’t throw a shadder.”", "빌어먹을, 글쎄 제가 여태 본 중에 제일 힘센 작자였다니까요. 그것도 늙은 양반이, 흰 콧수염을 기르고, 어찌나 비쩍 말랐는지 그림자도 못 드리울 것 같은 사람이 말이에요.\""]], [["How this phrase thrilled through me!", "이 말이 어찌나 나를 오싹하게 했던지!"]], [["“Why, ’e took up ’is end o’ the boxes like they was pounds of tea, and me a-puffin’ an’ a-blowin’ afore I could up-end mine anyhow--an’ I’m no chicken, neither.”", "\"아, 글쎄 그 양반이 상자 한쪽 끝을 마치 한 근짜리 차 봉지처럼 번쩍 들어 올리는데, 저는 헉헉대고 씩씩대고서야 겨우 제 쪽 끝을 세웠다니까요 — 그래도 제가 풋내기는 아니거든요.\""]], [["“How did you get into the house in Piccadilly?” I asked.", "\"피커딜리의 그 집에는 어떻게 들어갔소?\" 하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“He was there too.", "\"거기도 그 양반이 있었어요."], ["He must ’a’ started off and got there afore me, for when I rung of the bell he kem an’ opened the door ’isself an’ ’elped me to carry the boxes into the ’all.”", "먼저 출발해서 저보다 앞서 갔던 게 분명해요. 제가 종을 울리니까 직접 나와서 문을 열고, 상자들 현관으로 나르는 걸 거들어주었거든요.\""]], [["“The whole nine?” I asked.", "\"아홉 개 전부 말이오?\" 하고 내가 물었다."]], [["“Yus; there was five in the first load an’ four in the second.", "\"예, 첫 짐에 다섯 개, 두 번째 짐에 네 개였습죠."], ["It was main dry work, an’ I don’t so well remember ’ow I got ’ome.”", "목이 어찌나 타던지, 어떻게 집에 돌아왔는지도 잘 기억이 안 나요.\""], ["I interrupted him:--", "나는 그의 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“Were the boxes left in the hall?”", "\"상자들은 현관에 두었소?\""]], [["“Yus; it was a big ’all, an’ there was nothin’ else in it.”", "\"예, 큰 현관이었는데, 거기엔 아무것도 없었어요.\""], ["I made one more attempt to further matters:--", "나는 일을 더 진척시켜 보려고 한 번 더 시도했다."]], [["“You didn’t have any key?”", "\"열쇠는 안 가지고 있었소?\""]], [["“Never used no key nor nothink.", "\"열쇠고 뭐고 쓴 적이 없어요."], ["The old gent, he opened the door ’isself an’ shut it again when I druv off.", "영감님이 직접 문을 열고, 제가 마차를 몰고 떠날 때 다시 닫았어요."], ["I don’t remember the last time--but that was the beer.”", "마지막은 잘 기억이 안 나는데 — 그건 맥주 탓이에요.\""]], [["“And you can’t remember the number of the house?”", "\"그래서 그 집 번지는 기억 못 하는 거요?\""]], [["“No, sir. But ye needn’t have no difficulty about that.", "\"못 합니다요, 나리. 하지만 그건 어려울 게 없어요."], ["It’s a ’igh ’un with a stone front with a bow on it, an’ ’igh steps up to the door.", "돌로 된 정면에 둥근 내닫이창이 달린 높은 집이고, 문까지 높은 계단이 나 있어요."], ["I know them steps, ’avin’ ’ad to carry the boxes up with three loafers what come round to earn a copper.", "제가 그 계단은 압니다요, 동전 몇 닢 벌러 모여든 건달 셋이랑 상자를 거기로 날라야 했거든요."], ["The old gent give them shillin’s, an’ they seein’ they got so much, they wanted more; but ’e took one of them by the shoulder and was like to throw ’im down the steps, till the lot of them went away cussin’.”", "영감님이 그들한테 1실링씩 줬는데, 그렇게 많이 받은 걸 보고 더 달라는 거예요. 그러니까 영감님이 그중 하나를 어깨를 잡아서 계단 아래로 내던질 것처럼 하니까, 다들 욕을 지껄이며 가버렸습죠.\""], ["I thought that with this description I could find the house, so, having paid my friend for his information, I started off for Piccadilly. I had gained a new painful experience; the Count could, it was evident, handle the earth-boxes himself.", "나는 이 묘사면 그 집을 찾을 수 있겠다 싶어, 일러준 대가를 친구에게 치르고 피커딜리로 떠났다. 나는 새로운 고통스러운 깨달음을 얻었으니, 백작이 그 흙 상자들을 혼자서도 다룰 수 있다는 것이 분명해진 것이다."], ["If so, time was precious; for, now that he had achieved a certain amount of distribution, he could, by choosing his own time, complete the task unobserved.", "그렇다면 시간이 귀했다. 이제 그가 어느 정도 분산을 이루어놓았으니, 제가 택한 때에 남의 눈에 띄지 않고 그 일을 마무리할 수 있을 터였다."], ["At Piccadilly Circus I discharged my cab, and walked westward; beyond the Junior Constitutional I came across the house described, and was satisfied that this was the next of the lairs arranged by Dracula.", "피커딜리 서커스에서 나는 마차를 보냈고, 서쪽으로 걸었다. 주니어 컨스티튜셔널을 지나자 그가 묘사한 집과 마주쳤고, 이것이 드라큘라가 마련한 다음 소굴임을 확신했다."], ["The house looked as though it had been long untenanted.", "그 집은 오래 비어 있었던 것처럼 보였다."], ["The windows were encrusted with dust, and the shutters were up.", "창문은 먼지가 더껑이로 앉아 있었고, 덧문은 닫혀 있었다."], ["All the framework was black with time, and from the iron the paint had mostly scaled away.", "골조는 죄다 세월에 검게 변했고, 쇠붙이에서는 칠이 대부분 벗겨져 있었다."], ["It was evident that up to lately there had been a large notice-board in front of the balcony; it had, however, been roughly torn away, the uprights which had supported it still remaining.", "얼마 전까지만 해도 발코니 앞에 큰 게시판이 있었던 것이 분명한데, 그것은 거칠게 뜯겨 나갔고, 그것을 받치던 기둥만 아직 남아 있었다."], ["Behind the rails of the balcony I saw there were some loose boards, whose raw edges looked white.", "발코니 난간 뒤로는 헐거워진 판자 몇 개가 보였는데, 그 갓 드러난 가장자리가 허옇게 보였다."], ["I would have given a good deal to have been able to see the notice-board intact, as it would, perhaps, have given some clue to the ownership of the house.", "그 게시판을 온전히 볼 수만 있었다면 적잖은 값이라도 치렀을 것이니, 어쩌면 그 집 소유자에 대한 단서를 얻었을지도 몰랐기 때문이다."], ["I remembered my experience of the investigation and purchase of Carfax, and I could not but feel that if I could find the former owner there might be some means discovered of gaining access to the house.", "나는 카팩스를 조사하고 사들였던 경험을 떠올렸고, 만일 전 소유자를 찾아낼 수만 있다면 그 집에 들어갈 어떤 방도를 찾아낼 수 있으리라는 생각을 떨칠 수 없었다."]], [["There was at present nothing to be learned from the Piccadilly side, and nothing could be done; so I went round to the back to see if anything could be gathered from this quarter.", "지금으로서는 피커딜리 쪽에서 알아낼 것이 없었고, 할 수 있는 일도 없었다. 그래서 나는 뒤쪽으로 돌아가, 이 방면에서 뭔가 알아낼 것이 없는지 보았다."], ["The mews were active, the Piccadilly houses being mostly in occupation.", "마구간 골목은 활기찼는데, 피커딜리의 집들은 대부분 사람이 살고 있었기 때문이다."], ["I asked one or two of the grooms and helpers whom I saw around if they could tell me anything about the empty house.", "나는 주변에서 본 마부와 일꾼 한둘에게 그 빈집에 대해 뭔가 일러줄 수 있겠느냐고 물었다."], ["One of them said that he heard it had lately been taken, but he couldn’t say from whom. He told me, however, that up to very lately there had been a notice-board of “For Sale”", "그 가운데 하나는, 최근에 누가 그 집을 샀다는 말을 들었는데 누구한테서인지는 모르겠다고 했다. 그러나 그는, 아주 최근까지만 해도 \"매각\""], ["up, and that perhaps Mitchell, Sons, & Candy, the house agents, could tell me something, as he thought he remembered seeing the name of that firm on the board.", "게시판이 걸려 있었다고, 그리고 어쩌면 부동산 중개인인 미첼·선스 앤드 캔디가 뭔가 일러줄 수 있을 거라고 했다. 그 게시판에서 그 회사 이름을 본 기억이 나는 것 같다는 것이었다."], ["I did not wish to seem too eager, or to let my informant know or guess too much, so, thanking him in the usual manner, I strolled away.", "나는 너무 다급해 보이고 싶지도, 알려준 자에게 내가 너무 많이 알거나 짐작한다는 것을 알리고 싶지도 않았으므로, 으레 하는 식으로 그에게 고맙다고 하고는 어슬렁어슬렁 자리를 떴다."], ["It was now growing dusk, and the autumn night was closing in, so I did not lose any time.", "이제 땅거미가 지고 가을밤이 다가오고 있었으므로, 나는 시간을 조금도 허비하지 않았다."], ["Having learned the address of Mitchell, Sons, & Candy from a directory at the Berkeley, I was soon at their office in Sackville Street.", "버클리에 있는 인명록에서 미첼·선스 앤드 캔디의 주소를 알아낸 뒤, 나는 곧 색빌 가에 있는 그들의 사무소에 닿았다."]], [["The gentleman who saw me was particularly suave in manner, but uncommunicative in equal proportion.", "나를 맞은 신사는 유난히 정중한 태도였으나, 그만큼이나 입이 무거웠다."], ["Having once told me that the Piccadilly house--which throughout our interview he called a “mansion”--was sold, he considered my business as concluded.", "일단 그 피커딜리의 집이 — 그는 우리가 이야기를 나누는 내내 그것을 \"저택\"이라 불렀다 — 팔렸다고 일러준 뒤로는, 내 용건이 끝난 것으로 여겼다."], ["When I asked who had purchased it, he opened his eyes a thought wider, and paused a few seconds before replying:--", "누가 그것을 사들였느냐고 묻자, 그는 눈을 조금 더 크게 뜨더니, 대답하기에 앞서 몇 초간 뜸을 들였다."]], [["“It is sold, sir.”", "\"팔렸습니다, 선생님.\""]], [["“Pardon me,”", "\"실례지만,\""], ["I said, with equal politeness, “but I have a special reason for wishing to know who purchased it.”", "나는 똑같이 정중하게 말했다. \"누가 그것을 사들였는지 알고 싶은 특별한 사정이 있습니다.\""]], [["Again he paused longer, and raised his eyebrows still more.", "그는 다시 더 길게 뜸을 들이며, 눈썹을 한층 더 치켜올렸다."], ["“It is sold, sir,”", "\"팔렸습니다, 선생님,\""], ["was again his laconic reply.", "하는 것이 다시 그의 간결한 대답이었다."]], [["“Surely,”", "\"그쯤은,\""], ["I said, “you do not mind letting me know so much.”", "하고 내가 말했다. \"저에게 알려주셔도 괜찮지 않겠습니까.\""]], [["“But I do mind,” he answered.", "\"하지만 괜찮지 않습니다,\""], ["“The affairs of their clients are absolutely safe in the hands of Mitchell, Sons, & Candy.”", "그가 대답했다. \"고객의 일은 미첼·선스 앤드 캔디의 손안에서 절대적으로 안전합니다.\""], ["This was manifestly a prig of the first water, and there was no use arguing with him.", "이자는 분명 둘도 없는 잘난 체하는 자였고, 그와 입씨름해 봐야 소용이 없었다."], ["I thought I had best meet him on his own ground, so I said:--", "나는 그의 영역에서 그를 상대하는 것이 상책이겠다 싶어, 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“Your clients, sir, are happy in having so resolute a guardian of their confidence.", "\"댁의 고객들은 신뢰를 이토록 단호히 지켜주는 분을 두었으니 복이 많습니다."], ["I am myself a professional man.”", "저 역시 전문직에 있는 사람입니다.\""], ["Here I handed him my card.", "여기서 나는 그에게 내 명함을 건넸다."], ["“In this instance I am not prompted by curiosity; I act on the part of Lord Godalming, who wishes to know something of the property which was, he understood, lately for sale.”", "\"이 경우에는 호기심에서 움직이는 것이 아닙니다. 저는 고덜밍 경을 대신하여 행동하는 것이며, 경께서는 최근에 매물로 나온 것으로 아는 그 부동산에 대해 무언가 알고 싶어 하십니다.\""], ["These words put a different complexion on affairs.", "이 말이 사정을 다르게 돌려놓았다."], ["He said:--", "그가 말했다."]], [["“I would like to oblige you if I could, Mr. Harker, and especially would I like to oblige his lordship.", "\"제가 할 수만 있다면 기꺼이 편의를 봐드리고 싶습니다, 하커 씨. 특히 경께는 더더욱 그러고 싶군요."], ["We once carried out a small matter of renting some chambers for him when he was the Honourable Arthur Holmwood. If you will let me have his lordship’s address I will consult the House on the subject, and will, in any case, communicate with his lordship by to-night’s post.", "저희는 일찍이 그분이 아너러블 아서 홈우드이셨을 때, 그분을 위해 방을 임대하는 자그마한 일을 처리해 드린 적이 있습니다. 경의 주소를 알려주시면 제가 이 건에 대해 회사와 의논하겠으며, 어떤 경우에도 오늘 밤 우편으로 경께 연락드리겠습니다."], ["It will be a pleasure if we can so far deviate from our rules as to give the required information to his lordship.”", "저희 규정을 그만큼이라도 벗어나 경께서 원하시는 정보를 드릴 수 있다면 더없이 기쁘겠습니다.\""]], [["I wanted to secure a friend, and not to make an enemy, so I thanked him, gave the address at Dr. Seward’s and came away.", "나는 적이 아니라 우군을 확보하고 싶었으므로, 그에게 고맙다고 하고 수어드 박사의 주소를 일러준 뒤 자리를 떴다."], ["It was now dark, and I was tired and hungry.", "이제 날이 어두웠고, 나는 지치고 배가 고팠다."], ["I got a cup of tea at the Aërated Bread Company and came down to Purfleet by the next train.", "나는 에어레이티드 브레드 컴퍼니에서 차 한 잔을 마시고, 다음 기차로 퍼플릿에 내려왔다."]], [["I found all the others at home.", "나머지 사람들은 모두 집에 있었다."], ["Mina was looking tired and pale, but she made a gallant effort to be bright and cheerful, it wrung my heart to think that I had had to keep anything from her and so caused her inquietude.", "미나는 지치고 창백해 보였으나, 밝고 명랑하려고 안간힘을 썼다. 그녀에게 무언가를 숨겨야 했고 그로 인해 그녀를 불안하게 만들었다고 생각하니 가슴이 미어졌다."], ["Thank God, this will be the last night of her looking on at our conferences, and feeling the sting of our not showing our confidence.", "다행스럽게도, 그녀가 우리의 회의를 옆에서 지켜보며 우리가 신뢰를 내보이지 않는 데서 오는 아픔을 느끼는 것도 오늘 밤이 마지막일 것이다."], ["It took all my courage to hold to the wise resolution of keeping her out of our grim task.", "그녀를 우리의 음울한 임무에서 떼어놓겠다는 현명한 결심을 지키는 데에 내 온 용기가 들었다."], ["She seems somehow more reconciled; or else the very subject seems to have become repugnant to her, for when any accidental allusion is made she actually shudders.", "그녀는 어쩐지 좀더 체념한 듯 보이는데, 아니면 그 화제 자체가 그녀에게 역겨워진 것인지도 모른다. 어쩌다 그 이야기가 나오기라도 하면 그녀는 정말로 몸서리를 치니 말이다."], ["I am glad we made our resolution in time, as with such a feeling as this, our growing knowledge would be torture to her.", "우리가 때맞춰 결심한 것이 다행이니, 이런 심정인 그녀에게 우리의 늘어가는 지식은 고문이 되었을 것이다."]], [["I could not tell the others of the day’s discovery till we were alone; so after dinner--followed by a little music to save appearances even amongst ourselves--I took Mina to her room and left her to go to bed.", "우리끼리만 남을 때까지는 나머지 사람들에게 오늘의 발견을 일러줄 수 없었다. 그래서 저녁식사 뒤에 — 우리끼리도 체면을 차리느라 약간의 음악을 곁들인 뒤에 — 나는 미나를 방으로 데려가, 자도록 두고 나왔다."], ["The dear girl was more affectionate with me than ever, and clung to me as though she would detain me; but there was much to be talked of and I came away.", "사랑스러운 그 사람은 여느 때보다 더 다정하게 굴며, 마치 나를 붙들어두려는 듯 매달렸다. 그러나 이야기할 것이 많았으므로 나는 나왔다."], ["Thank God, the ceasing of telling things has made no difference between us.", "다행스럽게도, 이야기를 그만둔 것이 우리 사이를 조금도 달라지게 하지는 않았다."]], [["When I came down again I found the others all gathered round the fire in the study.", "다시 아래층으로 내려가 보니, 나머지 사람들이 모두 서재의 난롯가에 모여 있었다."], ["In the train I had written my diary so far, and simply read it off to them as the best means of letting them get abreast of my own information; when I had finished Van Helsing said:--", "나는 기차에서 여기까지 일기를 써둔 터라, 그들을 내 정보와 나란히 따라잡게 하는 가장 좋은 방편으로 그것을 그대로 읽어주었다. 다 읽고 나자 반 헬싱이 말했다."]], [["“This has been a great day’s work, friend Jonathan.", "\"오늘은 큰일을 해냈군요, 친구 조너선."], ["Doubtless we are on the track of the missing boxes.", "우리가 사라진 상자들의 자취를 좇고 있는 것이 틀림없소."], ["If we find them all in that house, then our work is near the end.", "그것들을 죄다 그 집에서 찾아낸다면, 우리 일도 끝에 가까워지는 거요."], ["But if there be some missing, we must search until we find them.", "그러나 몇 개라도 빠져 있다면, 찾아낼 때까지 뒤져야 하오."], ["Then shall we make our final coup, and hunt the wretch to his real death.”", "그런 다음에 우리는 마지막 일격을 가하여, 그 비열한 놈을 진짜 죽음으로 몰아넣을 것이오.\""], ["We all sat silent awhile and all at once Mr. Morris spoke:--", "우리는 한동안 모두 말없이 앉아 있었는데, 별안간 모리스 씨가 입을 열었다."]], [["“Say! how are we going to get into that house?”", "\"그런데 말이오! 그 집에 우리가 어떻게 들어가지요?\""]], [["“We got into the other,”", "\"다른 집에는 들어갔잖소,\""], ["answered Lord Godalming quickly.", "고덜밍 경이 재빨리 대답했다."]], [["“But, Art, this is different.", "\"하지만 아트, 이건 달라요."], ["We broke house at Carfax, but we had night and a walled park to protect us.", "카팩스는 우리가 침입했지만, 그땐 밤과 담장을 두른 정원이 우리를 가려주었소."], ["It will be a mighty different thing to commit burglary in Piccadilly, either by day or night.", "낮이든 밤이든 피커딜리에서 도둑질을 저지르는 건 영 다른 얘기일 거요."], ["I confess I don’t see how we are going to get in unless that agency duck can find us a key of some sort; perhaps we shall know when you get his letter in the morning.”", "솔직히, 그 중개소 친구가 우리한테 무슨 열쇠라도 구해주지 못한다면 어떻게 들어갈 수 있을지 모르겠소. 어쩌면 아침에 자네가 그자의 편지를 받으면 알게 될지도 모르겠지만.\""], ["Lord Godalming’s brows contracted, and he stood up and walked about the room.", "고덜밍 경의 눈썹이 찌푸려졌고, 그는 일어나 방 안을 거닐었다."], ["By-and-by he stopped and said, turning from one to another of us:--", "이윽고 그는 멈춰 서서, 우리를 한 사람씩 차례로 돌아보며 말했다."]], [["“Quincey’s head is level.", "\"퀸시 머리가 제대로 돌아가는군."], ["This burglary business is getting serious; we got off once all right; but we have now a rare job on hand--unless we can find the Count’s key basket.”", "이 도둑질이라는 게 심각해지고 있어. 한 번은 무사히 넘어갔지만, 이제 우리한텐 만만찮은 일거리가 있는 셈이야 — 백작의 열쇠 꾸러미를 찾아내지 못하는 한 말이지.\""]], [["As nothing could well be done before morning, and as it would be at least advisable to wait till Lord Godalming should hear from Mitchell’s, we decided not to take any active step before breakfast time.", "아침이 되기 전에는 딱히 할 수 있는 일이 없었고, 적어도 고덜밍 경이 미첼 측에서 연락을 받을 때까지 기다리는 것이 현명하리라 여겨졌으므로, 우리는 아침식사 때 이전에는 어떤 적극적인 행동도 취하지 않기로 했다."], ["For a good while we sat and smoked, discussing the matter in its various lights and bearings; I took the opportunity of bringing this diary right up to the moment.", "우리는 한참 동안 앉아 담배를 피우며, 이 문제를 여러 측면과 관점에서 따져보았다. 나는 이 틈을 타 이 일기를 바로 이 순간까지 써둔다."], ["I am very sleepy and shall go to bed....", "몹시 졸려 자러 가야겠다……."]], [["Just a line.", "한 줄만 더."], ["Mina sleeps soundly and her breathing is regular.", "미나는 곤히 잠들었고 숨소리가 고르다."], ["Her forehead is puckered up into little wrinkles, as though she thinks even in her sleep.", "그녀의 이마는 잠결에도 무언가 생각하는 듯이 잔주름이 잡혀 있다."], ["She is still too pale, but does not look so haggard as she did this morning.", "여전히 너무 창백하지만, 오늘 아침처럼 그토록 초췌해 보이지는 않는다."], ["To-morrow will, I hope, mend all this; she will be herself at home in Exeter.", "내일은 이 모든 것이 나아지기를 바란다. 그녀는 엑서터의 우리집에서 제 모습을 되찾을 것이다."], ["Oh, but I am sleepy!", "아, 그런데 졸리다!"]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["1 October.--I am puzzled afresh about Renfield.", "10월 1일. — 렌필드 때문에 다시금 갈피를 잡을 수 없다."], ["His moods change so rapidly that I find it difficult to keep touch of them, and as they always mean something more than his own well-being, they form a more than interesting study.", "그의 기분이 어찌나 빠르게 바뀌는지 따라잡기가 어려운데, 그 변화가 늘 그 자신의 안녕 이상의 무언가를 뜻하므로, 그것은 흥미롭다는 말로는 부족한 연구거리가 된다."], ["This morning, when I went to see him after his repulse of Van Helsing, his manner was that of a man commanding destiny.", "오늘 아침, 그가 반 헬싱을 내쫓은 뒤에 그를 보러 갔더니, 그의 태도는 운명을 호령하는 자의 그것이었다."], ["He was, in fact, commanding destiny--subjectively.", "사실 그는 운명을 호령하고 있었다 — 주관적으로 말이다."], ["He did not really care for any of the things of mere earth; he was in the clouds and looked down on all the weaknesses and wants of us poor mortals.", "그는 한낱 지상의 것들 따위에는 정말이지 관심이 없었다. 그는 구름 위에 있었고, 우리 가엾은 인간들의 온갖 약점과 결핍을 내려다보고 있었다."], ["I thought I would improve the occasion and learn something, so I asked him:--", "나는 이 기회를 살려 무언가 알아내야겠다 싶어, 그에게 물었다."]], [["“What about the flies these times?”", "\"요즘 파리들은 좀 어떻소?\""], ["He smiled on me in quite a superior sort of way--such a smile as would have become the face of Malvolio--as he answered me:--", "그는 자못 우월한 투로 나에게 미소를 지었는데 — 말볼리오의 얼굴에나 어울렸을 법한 그런 미소였다 — 이렇게 대답했다."]], [["“The fly, my dear sir, has one striking feature; its wings are typical of the aërial powers of the psychic faculties.", "\"파리에게는, 선생, 한 가지 두드러진 특징이 있지요. 그 날개는 정신적 능력의 공중을 나는 힘을 상징합니다."], ["The ancients did well when they typified the soul as a butterfly!”", "옛사람들이 영혼을 나비로 비유한 것은 참으로 옳았습니다!\""]], [["I thought I would push his analogy to its utmost logically, so I said quickly:--", "나는 그의 비유를 논리적으로 끝까지 밀어붙여 보려고 재빨리 말했다."]], [["“Oh, it is a soul you are after now, is it?” His madness foiled his reason, and a puzzled look spread over his face as, shaking his head with a decision which I had but seldom seen in him, he said:--", "\"오, 그럼 이제 당신이 노리는 건 영혼이로군요, 그렇소?\" 광기가 그의 이성을 어그러뜨려, 그의 얼굴에 어리둥절한 빛이 번졌고, 그는 좀처럼 본 적 없는 단호함으로 고개를 저으며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, no, oh no! I want no souls.", "\"오, 아니오, 아니에요!"], ["Life is all I want.”", "나는 영혼은 원하지 않습니다."], ["Here he brightened up; “I am pretty indifferent about it at present.", "내가 원하는 건 오직 생명뿐이에요.\" 여기서 그는 밝아졌다. \"지금은 그것에도 꽤 무덤덤합니다."], ["Life is all right; I have all I want.", "생명은 괜찮아요. 나는 원하는 걸 다 가졌으니까요."], ["You must get a new patient, doctor, if you wish to study zoöphagy!”", "동물포식증을 연구하시려거든, 박사님, 새 환자를 구하셔야 할 겁니다!\""]], [["This puzzled me a little, so I drew him on:--", "이 말에 나는 좀 어리둥절해져서, 그를 부추겼다."]], [["“Then you command life; you are a god, I suppose?”", "\"그럼 당신이 생명을 호령하는군요. 당신은 신쯤 되는 모양이오?\""], ["He smiled with an ineffably benign superiority.", "그는 형언할 수 없이 자애로운 우월감을 띠며 미소 지었다."]], [["“Oh no! Far be it from me to arrogate to myself the attributes of the Deity.", "\"오, 아니오! 신의 속성을 제 것인 양 참칭하는 일일랑 결코 없을 겁니다."], ["I am not even concerned in His especially spiritual doings.", "나는 그분의 특별히 영적인 소행에는 끼지조차 않습니다."], ["If I may state my intellectual position I am, so far as concerns things purely terrestrial, somewhat in the position which Enoch occupied spiritually!”", "제 지적 입장을 말씀드리자면, 순전히 지상에 속한 일들에 관한 한, 나는 에녹이 영적으로 차지했던 위치쯤에 있다 하겠습니다!\""], ["This was a poser to me.", "이건 나에겐 난제였다."], ["I could not at the moment recall Enoch’s appositeness; so I had to ask a simple question, though I felt that by so doing I was lowering myself in the eyes of the lunatic:--", "그 순간 나는 에녹이 어떻게 들어맞는지 떠올릴 수 없었다. 그래서 단순한 질문을 던질 수밖에 없었는데, 그렇게 함으로써 이 광인의 눈에 내가 스스로를 낮추는 셈이 된다는 것을 느끼면서도 그리할 수밖에 없었다."]], [["“And why with Enoch?”", "\"그런데 왜 하필 에녹이오?\""]], [["“Because he walked with God.”", "\"그는 신과 함께 걸었으니까요.\""], ["I could not see the analogy, but did not like to admit it; so I harked back to what he had denied:--", "나는 그 비유를 알 수 없었으나, 그것을 인정하기는 싫었다. 그래서 나는 그가 부인했던 것으로 되돌아갔다."]], [["“So you don’t care about life and you don’t want souls.", "\"그러니까 당신은 생명에는 관심이 없고, 영혼도 원하지 않는다는 거요."], ["Why not?”", "어째서요?\""], ["I put my question quickly and somewhat sternly, on purpose to disconcert him.", "나는 그를 당황하게 만들 작정으로, 다소 엄하게 재빨리 물음을 던졌다."], ["The effort succeeded; for an instant he unconsciously relapsed into his old servile manner, bent low before me, and actually fawned upon me as he replied:--", "그 시도는 먹혀들었다. 한순간 그는 저도 모르게 옛날의 비굴한 태도로 되돌아가, 내 앞에 깊이 굽실거리며, 대답하면서 정말로 나에게 알랑거렸다."]], [["“I don’t want any souls, indeed, indeed!", "\"나는 영혼은 원하지 않습니다, 정말, 정말로요!"], ["I don’t.", "원치 않아요."], ["I couldn’t use them if I had them; they would be no manner of use to me.", "그것들을 가진들 쓸 데가 없어요. 나한텐 도무지 쓸모가 없을 겁니다."], ["I couldn’t eat them or----”", "그것들을 먹을 수도 없고 또는——\""], ["He suddenly stopped and the old cunning look spread over his face, like a wind-sweep on the surface of the water. “And doctor, as to life, what is it after all?", "그는 별안간 입을 다물었고, 잔잔한 수면 위로 바람이 스치듯, 그의 얼굴에 옛 교활한 빛이 번졌다. \"그리고 박사님, 생명으로 말할 것 같으면, 그게 결국 다 뭡니까?"], ["When you’ve got all you require, and you know that you will never want, that is all.", "필요한 걸 죄다 가졌고, 앞으로도 결코 아쉬울 일이 없으리란 걸 안다면, 그게 전부지요."], ["I have friends--good friends--like you, Dr. Seward”; this was said with a leer of inexpressible cunning.", "나한텐 벗들이 있습니다 — 좋은 벗들이요 — 박사님 같은 분 말입니다.\" 이 말은 이루 말할 수 없이 교활한 곁눈질과 함께 나왔다."], ["“I know that I shall never lack the means of life!”", "\"나는 생명의 방편이 결코 모자라지 않으리란 걸 압니다!\""]], [["I think that through the cloudiness of his insanity he saw some antagonism in me, for he at once fell back on the last refuge of such as he--a dogged silence.", "내 생각에 그는 자신의 광기의 흐릿함 너머로 내 안의 어떤 적대감을 본 모양이었다. 그 즉시 그는 그와 같은 부류가 마지막으로 기대는 피난처 — 고집스러운 침묵 — 로 물러나 버렸기 때문이다."], ["After a short time I saw that for the present it was useless to speak to him.", "잠시 후 나는 당분간 그에게 말을 거는 것이 소용없음을 알았다."], ["He was sulky, and so I came away.", "그는 시무룩했고, 그래서 나는 물러 나왔다."]], [["Later in the day he sent for me. Ordinarily I would not have come without special reason, but just at present I am so interested in him that I would gladly make an effort.", "그날 늦게 그가 나를 불러달라고 사람을 보냈다. 평소 같으면 특별한 까닭 없이는 가지 않았을 테지만, 바로 지금은 그에게 흥미가 어찌나 동하는지 기꺼이 한 번 애써볼 만했다."], ["Besides, I am glad to have anything to help to pass the time.", "게다가 시간을 보내는 데 도움이 될 만한 무언가가 있다는 것이 반가웠다."], ["Harker is out, following up clues; and so are Lord Godalming and Quincey.", "하커는 단서를 좇아 밖에 나가 있고, 고덜밍 경과 퀸시도 마찬가지다."], ["Van Helsing sits in my study poring over the record prepared by the Harkers; he seems to think that by accurate knowledge of all details he will light upon some clue.", "반 헬싱은 내 서재에 앉아 하커 부부가 마련한 기록을 들여다보고 있다. 그는 모든 세부를 정확히 앎으로써 어떤 단서에 가닿으리라 여기는 듯하다."], ["He does not wish to be disturbed in the work, without cause.", "그는 까닭 없이 그 작업을 방해받고 싶어 하지 않는다."], ["I would have taken him with me to see the patient, only I thought that after his last repulse he might not care to go again.", "그를 데리고 환자를 보러 갔을 수도 있었으나, 지난번에 내쫓긴 뒤라 다시 가고 싶어 하지 않을 것 같았다."], ["There was also another reason: Renfield might not speak so freely before a third person as when he and I were alone.", "또 다른 까닭도 있었다. 렌필드가 제삼자 앞에서는 나와 단둘이 있을 때만큼 거리낌 없이 말하지 않을지도 모른다는 것이었다."]], [["I found him sitting out in the middle of the floor on his stool, a pose which is generally indicative of some mental energy on his part.", "가서 보니 그는 등받이 없는 의자에 앉아 방바닥 한가운데에 나와 있었는데, 이런 자세는 대개 그에게 어떤 정신적 활력이 있음을 일러주는 것이다."], ["When I came in, he said at once, as though the question had been waiting on his lips:--", "내가 들어서자, 마치 그 물음이 입가에서 기다리고 있었던 듯 그는 대뜸 말했다."]], [["“What about souls?” It was evident then that my surmise had been correct.", "\"영혼은 어떻게 됐소?\" 그렇다면 내 짐작이 옳았던 것이 분명했다."], ["Unconscious cerebration was doing its work, even with the lunatic.", "무의식적인 사고 작용이, 이 광인에게서마저 제 일을 하고 있었던 것이다."], ["I determined to have the matter out.", "나는 이 문제를 끝장내기로 마음먹었다."], ["“What about them yourself?” I asked.", "\"당신 자신은 그것에 대해 어떻게 생각하오?\""], ["He did not reply for a moment but looked all round him, and up and down, as though he expected to find some inspiration for an answer.", "하고 내가 물었다. 그는 한순간 대답하지 않고, 마치 대답할 영감을 찾으려는 듯 사방을, 위아래를 두루 살폈다."]], [["“I don’t want any souls!”", "\"나는 영혼은 원하지 않아요!\""], ["he said in a feeble, apologetic way.", "그가 힘없이, 변명하는 투로 말했다."], ["The matter seemed preying on his mind, and so I determined to use it--to “be cruel only to be kind.”", "그 문제가 그의 마음을 짓누르고 있는 듯했으므로, 나는 그것을 이용하기로 — \"오로지 다정하기 위해 모질게 굴기로\" 마음먹었다."], ["So I said:--", "그래서 내가 말했다."]], [["“You like life, and you want life?”", "\"당신은 생명을 좋아하고, 생명을 원하지요?\""]], [["“Oh yes!", "\"오, 그럼요!"], ["but that is all right; you needn’t worry about that!”", "하지만 그건 괜찮아요. 그건 걱정하실 것 없어요!\""]], [["“But,”", "\"하지만,\""], ["I asked, “how are we to get the life without getting the soul also?”", "하고 내가 물었다. \"영혼까지 얻지 않고서야 어떻게 생명을 얻겠소?\""], ["This seemed to puzzle him, so I followed it up:--", "이 말이 그를 어리둥절하게 만든 듯하여, 나는 밀고 나갔다."]], [["“A nice time you’ll have some time when you’re flying out there, with the souls of thousands of flies and spiders and birds and cats buzzing and twittering and miauing all round you.", "\"언젠가 당신이 저 바깥으로 날아다니게 되면, 수천 마리 파리와 거미와 새와 고양이의 영혼이 당신 둘레에서 윙윙거리고 짹짹대고 야옹거릴 테니, 참 가관일 거요."], ["You’ve got their lives, you know, and you must put up with their souls!”", "알다시피 당신은 그것들의 생명을 가졌으니, 그것들의 영혼도 감수해야 할 거란 말이오!\""], ["Something seemed to affect his imagination, for he put his fingers to his ears and shut his eyes, screwing them up tightly just as a small boy does when his face is being soaped.", "무언가가 그의 상상력을 건드린 듯, 그는 손가락을 귀에 갖다 대고 눈을 감았는데, 마치 어린 사내아이가 얼굴에 비누칠을 당할 때처럼 두 눈을 질끈 감았다."], ["There was something pathetic in it that touched me; it also gave me a lesson, for it seemed that before me was a child--only a child, though the features were worn, and the stubble on the jaws was white.", "거기엔 나를 뭉클하게 하는 애처로운 데가 있었다. 또한 그것은 나에게 한 가지 가르침을 주었으니, 내 앞에 있는 것은 한 아이 — 그저 한 아이일 뿐, 비록 이목구비는 닳고 턱의 까칠한 수염은 허옜지만 — 인 듯했기 때문이다."], ["It was evident that he was undergoing some process of mental disturbance, and, knowing how his past moods had interpreted things seemingly foreign to himself, I thought I would enter into his mind as well as I could and go with him.", "그가 어떤 정신적 동요의 과정을 겪고 있는 것이 분명했고, 지난날 그의 기분들이 겉보기에 그와 무관한 일들을 어떻게 풀이해 왔는지를 아는 터라, 나는 되도록 그의 마음속으로 들어가 그와 함께 가보기로 했다."], ["The first step was to restore confidence, so I asked him, speaking pretty loud so that he would hear me through his closed ears:--", "첫걸음은 그에게 신뢰를 되살려 주는 것이었으므로, 나는 그가 막은 귀로도 알아듣도록 꽤 큰 소리로 물었다."]], [["“Would you like some sugar to get your flies round again?”", "\"파리들을 다시 불러 모으게 설탕이라도 좀 드릴까요?\""], ["He seemed to wake up all at once, and shook his head.", "그는 단박에 깨어나는 듯하더니 고개를 저었다."], ["With a laugh he replied:--", "그러고는 웃으며 대답했다."]], [["“Not much!", "\"천만에요!"], ["flies are poor things, after all!”", "파리는 결국 보잘것없는 것들이에요!\""], ["After a pause he added, “But I don’t want their souls buzzing round me, all the same.”", "잠시 뜸을 들인 뒤 그는 덧붙였다. \"하지만 그래도 그것들의 영혼이 내 둘레에서 윙윙대는 건 싫어요.\""]], [["“Or spiders?” I went on.", "\"그럼 거미는?\" 하고 내가 이어 물었다."]], [["“Blow spiders!", "\"거미 따위!"], ["What’s the use of spiders?", "거미가 무슨 소용이에요?"], ["There isn’t anything in them to eat or”--he stopped suddenly, as though reminded of a forbidden topic.", "거기엔 먹을 것도 없고 또는——\" 그는 마치 금지된 화제가 떠오른 듯 별안간 멈췄다."]], [["“So, so!”", "'옳거니, 옳거니!'"], ["I thought to myself, “this is the second time he has suddenly stopped at the word ‘drink’; what does it mean?”", "나는 속으로 생각했다. '이자가 '마실(drink)'이라는 말 앞에서 별안간 멈춘 게 이번이 두 번째다."], ["Renfield seemed himself aware of having made a lapse, for he hurried on, as though to distract my attention from it:--", "무슨 뜻일까?' 렌필드는 자신이 실언했음을 스스로 알아챈 듯, 거기서 내 주의를 돌리려는 듯이 서둘러 말을 이었다."]], [["“I don’t take any stock at all in such matters.", "\"나는 그런 일들엔 도무지 관심이 없습니다."], ["‘Rats and mice and such small deer,’ as Shakespeare has it, ‘chicken-feed of the larder’ they might be called.", "'쥐며 생쥐며 그런 자잘한 짐승들' 말이에요, 셰익스피어 식으로 말하자면요. '식료품 저장실의 닭 모이'쯤이라 부를 만한 것들이지요."], ["I’m past all that sort of nonsense.", "나는 그런 시시한 것일랑 죄다 졸업했습니다."], ["You might as well ask a man to eat molecules with a pair of chop-sticks, as to try to interest me about the lesser carnivora, when I know of what is before me.”", "내 앞에 무엇이 놓여 있는지 아는 마당에, 나더러 그 하찮은 육식 동물 따위에 관심을 가지라는 건, 젓가락 한 쌍으로 분자를 집어 먹으라는 거나 다름없어요.\""]], [["“I see,”", "\"알겠소,\""], ["I said. “You want big things that you can make your teeth meet in?", "하고 내가 말했다. \"당신은 이빨이 푹 맞물릴 만한 큰 것을 원하는군요?"], ["How would you like to breakfast on elephant?”", "코끼리로 아침식사를 하면 어떻겠소?\""]], [["“What ridiculous nonsense you are talking!”", "\"무슨 그런 터무니없는 헛소리를 하십니까!\""], ["He was getting too wide awake, so I thought I would press him hard.", "그가 너무 또렷이 깨어나고 있었으므로, 나는 그를 세게 몰아붙여 보기로 했다."], ["“I wonder,”", "\"궁금하군요,\""], ["I said reflectively, “what an elephant’s soul is like!”", "하고 나는 생각에 잠긴 듯 말했다. \"코끼리의 영혼은 어떻게 생겼을지 말이오!\""]], [["The effect I desired was obtained, for he at once fell from his high-horse and became a child again.", "내가 바라던 효과를 얻었으니, 그는 그 즉시 거드름을 떨던 데서 굴러떨어져 다시 아이가 되었다."]], [["“I don’t want an elephant’s soul, or any soul at all!”", "\"나는 코끼리의 영혼도, 어떤 영혼도 원하지 않아요!\""], ["he said. For a few moments he sat despondently.", "그가 말했다. 잠시 그는 풀이 죽어 앉아 있었다."], ["Suddenly he jumped to his feet, with his eyes blazing and all the signs of intense cerebral excitement.", "별안간 그는 벌떡 일어났는데, 두 눈이 이글거리고 격렬한 뇌의 흥분이 온몸에 드러났다."], ["“To hell with you and your souls!”", "\"당신도 당신의 영혼도 지옥에나 가버려!\""], ["he shouted.", "그가 소리쳤다."], ["“Why do you plague me about souls?", "\"어째서 영혼 따위로 나를 들볶는 거요?"], ["Haven’t I got enough to worry, and pain, and distract me already, without thinking of souls!”", "영혼을 생각할 것도 없이, 나를 걱정시키고 괴롭히고 어지럽히는 게 이미 충분치 않단 말이오!\""], ["He looked so hostile that I thought he was in for another homicidal fit, so I blew my whistle. The instant, however, that I did so he became calm, and said apologetically:--", "그가 어찌나 적의에 차 보였던지, 또 한 차례 살인적인 발작을 일으키려는 게 아닌가 싶어, 나는 호각을 불었다. 그러나 그렇게 하자마자 그는 차분해지더니, 변명하듯 말했다."]], [["“Forgive me, Doctor; I forgot myself.", "\"용서하십시오, 박사님. 제가 제정신이 아니었습니다."], ["You do not need any help.", "도움 같은 건 필요 없으세요."], ["I am so worried in my mind that I am apt to be irritable.", "마음이 어찌나 어지러운지 짜증을 내기 쉽군요."], ["If you only knew the problem I have to face, and that I am working out, you would pity, and tolerate, and pardon me.", "제가 마주하고 있는, 그리고 풀어내고 있는 문제를 아신다면, 저를 가엾이 여기시고, 참아주시고, 용서해 주실 겁니다."], ["Pray do not put me in a strait-waistcoat.", "부디 저를 구속복에 넣지는 말아주십시오."], ["I want to think and I cannot think freely when my body is confined.", "저는 생각을 해야 하는데, 몸이 묶이면 자유로이 생각할 수가 없습니다."], ["I am sure you will understand!”", "분명 이해해 주시리라 믿습니다!\""], ["He had evidently self-control; so when the attendants came I told them not to mind, and they withdrew.", "그는 분명 자제력이 있었다. 그래서 수발하는 자들이 왔을 때 나는 신경 쓰지 말라고 일렀고, 그들은 물러갔다."], ["Renfield watched them go; when the door was closed he said, with considerable dignity and sweetness:--", "렌필드는 그들이 나가는 것을 지켜보았다. 문이 닫히자 그는 자못 위엄 있고 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“Dr. Seward, you have been very considerate towards me.", "\"수어드 박사님, 저에게 무척 사려 깊게 대해주셨습니다."], ["Believe me that I am very, very grateful to you!”", "진심으로 박사님께 정말, 정말 감사드린다는 걸 믿어주십시오!\""], ["I thought it well to leave him in this mood, and so I came away.", "나는 이런 기분일 때 그를 두고 나오는 것이 좋겠다 싶어, 물러 나왔다."], ["There is certainly something to ponder over in this man’s state.", "이 사람의 상태에는 분명 곰곰이 따져볼 만한 무언가가 있다."], ["Several points seem to make what the American interviewer calls “a story,” if one could only get them in proper order.", "몇 가지 점들을 제대로 된 순서로 꿰어맞출 수만 있다면, 미국인 인터뷰어들이 말하는 \"이야깃거리\"가 될 듯하다."], ["Here they are:--", "그것들을 여기 적어둔다."]], [["Will not mention “drinking.”", "'마시는 것'에 대해서는 입에 올리려 하지 않는다."]], [["Fears the thought of being burdened with the “soul” of anything.", "어떤 것의 '영혼'을 짊어지게 된다는 생각을 두려워한다."]], [["Has no dread of wanting “life” in the future.", "앞날에 '생명'이 아쉬워질까 봐 두려워하지는 않는다."]], [["Despises the meaner forms of life altogether, though he dreads being haunted by their souls.", "생명의 더 천한 형태들은 아예 경멸하면서도, 그것들의 영혼에 시달릴까 봐 두려워한다."]], [["Logically all these things point one way!", "논리적으로 이 모든 것이 한 방향을 가리킨다!"], ["he has assurance of some kind that he will acquire some higher life.", "그는 어떤 더 높은 생명을 얻게 되리라는 어떤 종류의 확신을 갖고 있다."], ["He dreads the consequence--the burden of a soul.", "그는 그 결과 — 영혼이라는 짐 — 를 두려워한다."], ["Then it is a human life he looks to!", "그렇다면 그가 바라보는 것은 인간의 생명이다!"]], [["And the assurance--?", "그리고 그 확신이라는 것은——?"]], [["Merciful God!", "자비로우신 하느님!"], ["the Count has been to him, and there is some new scheme of terror afoot!", "백작이 그에게 다녀갔고, 무슨 새로운 공포의 계략이 진행되고 있는 것이다!"]], [["Later.--I went after my round to Van Helsing and told him my suspicion.", "이후. — 나는 회진을 마친 뒤 반 헬싱에게 가서 내 의심을 일러주었다."], ["He grew very grave; and, after thinking the matter over for a while asked me to take him to Renfield.", "그는 몹시 심각해졌다. 그리고 잠시 그 문제를 곰곰이 따져본 뒤, 나에게 렌필드에게로 데려가 달라고 청했다."], ["I did so. As we came to the door we heard the lunatic within singing gaily, as he used to do in the time which now seems so long ago.", "나는 그리했다. 우리가 문에 이르렀을 때, 그 광인이 안에서 흥겹게 노래하는 소리가 들렸는데, 이제는 까마득히 멀게만 느껴지는 그 시절에 그가 그러곤 했던 것처럼 말이다."], ["When we entered we saw with amazement that he had spread out his sugar as of old; the flies, lethargic with the autumn, were beginning to buzz into the room.", "안으로 들어서자, 우리는 놀랍게도 그가 예전처럼 설탕을 펼쳐놓은 것을 보았다. 가을 기운에 굼떠진 파리들이 방 안으로 윙윙거리며 들기 시작하고 있었다."], ["We tried to make him talk of the subject of our previous conversation, but he would not attend.", "우리는 그에게 앞서 나눈 대화의 화제를 꺼내게 하려 했으나, 그는 귀를 기울이려 하지 않았다."], ["He went on with his singing, just as though we had not been present.", "그는 마치 우리가 거기 없는 것처럼 노래를 이어갔다."], ["He had got a scrap of paper and was folding it into a note-book.", "그는 종잇조각 하나를 손에 넣어 수첩 모양으로 접고 있었다."], ["We had to come away as ignorant as we went in.", "우리는 들어갈 때만큼이나 아무것도 모른 채 물러 나와야 했다."]], [["His is a curious case indeed; we must watch him to-night.", "그는 참으로 기이한 환자다. 오늘 밤 그를 지켜봐야겠다."]], [["“1 October.", "\"10월 1일."]], [["“My Lord,", "경께."]], [["“We are at all times only too happy to meet your wishes.", "저희는 언제든 경의 뜻을 받드는 것을 더없는 기쁨으로 여깁니다."], ["We beg, with regard to the desire of your Lordship, expressed by Mr. Harker on your behalf, to supply the following information concerning the sale and purchase of No. 347, Piccadilly.", "하커 씨를 통해 경을 대신하여 표명하신 바람과 관련하여, 피커딜리 347번지의 매매에 관한 다음의 정보를 삼가 알려드립니다."], ["The original vendors are the executors of the late Mr. Archibald Winter-Suffield.", "본래의 매도인은 고(故) 아치볼드 윈터서필드 씨의 유언 집행인들입니다."], ["The purchaser is a foreign nobleman, Count de Ville, who effected the purchase himself paying the purchase money in notes ‘over the counter,’ if your Lordship will pardon us using so vulgar an expression.", "매수인은 외국의 한 귀족인 드 빌 백작으로, 그가 직접 매매를 성사시켰으며, 경께서 이런 상스러운 표현을 너그러이 보아주신다면, 매매 대금을 '현장에서' 지폐로 치렀습니다. 그 밖에는 그에 대해 저희가 아는 바가 전혀 없습니다."], ["Beyond this we know nothing whatever of him.", "이만 줄이며, 경께,"]], [["“We are, my Lord,", "경의 충실한 종복들,"]], [["“Your Lordship’s humble servants,", "미첼·선스 앤드 캔디 올림.\""]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["2 October.--I placed a man in the corridor last night, and told him to make an accurate note of any sound he might hear from Renfield’s room, and gave him instructions that if there should be anything strange he was to call me.", "10월 2일. — 간밤에 나는 복도에 사람 하나를 세워두고, 렌필드의 방에서 들리는 어떤 소리든 정확히 기록하라고 일렀으며, 무언가 이상한 일이 있거든 나를 부르라고 지시해 두었다."], ["After dinner, when we had all gathered round the fire in the study--Mrs. Harker having gone to bed--we discussed the attempts and discoveries of the day.", "저녁식사 뒤에, 우리가 모두 서재의 난롯가에 모였을 때 — 하커 부인은 잠자리에 든 뒤였다 — 우리는 그날의 시도와 발견들을 의논했다."], ["Harker was the only one who had any result, and we are in great hopes that his clue may be an important one.", "하커만이 어떤 성과를 거둔 유일한 사람이었고, 우리는 그의 단서가 중요한 것일지 모른다는 큰 희망을 품고 있다."]], [["Before going to bed I went round to the patient’s room and looked in through the observation trap.", "잠자리에 들기 전 나는 환자의 방으로 돌아가 관찰용 쪽문을 통해 안을 들여다보았다."], ["He was sleeping soundly, and his heart rose and fell with regular respiration.", "그는 곤히 잠들어 있었고, 가슴이 고른 숨결에 따라 오르내리고 있었다."]], [["This morning the man on duty reported to me that a little after midnight he was restless and kept saying his prayers somewhat loudly.", "오늘 아침 당직을 섰던 사내가, 자정 조금 지나 그가 안절부절못하며 다소 큰 소리로 기도를 계속 외었다고 보고했다."], ["I asked him if that was all; he replied that it was all he heard.", "나는 그게 전부냐고 물었고, 그는 들은 건 그것뿐이라고 대답했다."], ["There was something about his manner so suspicious that I asked him point blank if he had been asleep.", "그의 태도에 어딘가 미심쩍은 데가 있어, 나는 대놓고 자고 있었던 것 아니냐고 물었다."], ["He denied sleep, but admitted to having “dozed” for a while.", "그는 잠은 부인했으나, 잠시 \"졸았던\" 것은 시인했다."], ["It is too bad that men cannot be trusted unless they are watched.", "사람을 감시하지 않고서는 믿을 수 없다니, 참으로 곤란한 노릇이다."]], [["To-day Harker is out following up his clue, and Art and Quincey are looking after horses.", "오늘 하커는 단서를 좇아 밖에 나가 있고, 아트와 퀸시는 말들을 살피고 있다."], ["Godalming thinks that it will be well to have horses always in readiness, for when we get the information which we seek there will be no time to lose.", "고덜밍은 말을 늘 채비시켜 두는 것이 좋겠다고 여기는데, 우리가 찾는 정보를 얻게 되면 한시도 지체할 틈이 없을 것이기 때문이다."], ["We must sterilise all the imported earth between sunrise and sunset; we shall thus catch the Count at his weakest, and without a refuge to fly to.", "우리는 들여온 흙을 모조리 해 뜰 때와 해 질 때 사이에 무력화해야 한다. 그리하면 백작이 가장 약해진 때에, 달아날 은신처도 없는 채로 그를 붙잡게 될 것이다."], ["Van Helsing is off to the British Museum looking up some authorities on ancient medicine.", "반 헬싱은 고대 의술에 관한 권위서 몇 권을 찾아보러 대영박물관에 갔다."], ["The old physicians took account of things which their followers do not accept, and the Professor is searching for witch and demon cures which may be useful to us later.", "옛 의사들은 그들의 후학들이 받아들이지 않는 것들까지 고려했는데, 교수는 나중에 우리에게 쓸모 있을지 모를 마녀와 악마를 다루는 치료법들을 뒤지고 있다."]], [["I sometimes think we must be all mad and that we shall wake to sanity in strait-waistcoats.", "이따금 나는 우리 모두가 미쳐 있는 것이 틀림없으며, 구속복을 입은 채로 제정신이 들 거라는 생각이 든다."]], [["Later.--We have met again.", "이후. — 우리는 다시 모였다."], ["We seem at last to be on the track, and our work of to-morrow may be the beginning of the end.", "우리는 마침내 자취를 잡은 듯하며, 내일 우리의 일이 끝의 시작이 될지도 모른다."], ["I wonder if Renfield’s quiet has anything to do with this.", "렌필드의 잠잠함이 이와 무슨 관련이 있는 것인지 궁금하다."], ["His moods have so followed the doings of the Count, that the coming destruction of the monster may be carried to him in some subtle way.", "그의 기분이 백작의 소행을 어찌나 그대로 따라왔던지, 그 괴물의 다가오는 파멸이 어떤 미묘한 방식으로 그에게 전해지고 있는지도 모른다."], ["If we could only get some hint as to what passed in his mind, between the time of my argument with him to-day and his resumption of fly-catching, it might afford us a valuable clue.", "오늘 내가 그와 논쟁을 벌인 때와 그가 다시 파리잡이로 돌아간 때 사이에 그의 마음속에서 무슨 일이 오갔는지 어떤 실마리라도 얻을 수 있다면, 우리에게 값진 단서를 주었을지도 모른다."], ["He is now seemingly quiet for a spell....", "그는 지금 한동안은 잠잠해 보인다……."], ["Is he?---- That wild yell seemed to come from his room....", "정말 그럴까?—— 저 거친 비명은 그의 방에서 들려온 듯한데……."]], [["The attendant came bursting into my room and told me that Renfield had somehow met with some accident.", "수발하는 자가 내 방으로 뛰어들어와, 렌필드가 어쩌다 무슨 사고를 당했다고 일러주었다."], ["He had heard him yell; and when he went to him found him lying on his face on the floor, all covered with blood.", "그가 비명을 들었고, 가서 보니 그가 얼굴을 바닥에 박은 채 온통 피투성이로 쓰러져 있더라는 것이었다."], ["I must go at once....", "당장 가봐야 한다……."]], [["# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY", "# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY"]], [["3 October.--Let me put down with exactness all that happened, as well as I can remember it, since last I made an entry.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」 10월 3일. — 지난번 기록을 남긴 이후로 일어난 모든 일을, 기억할 수 있는 한 정확하게 적어두겠다."], ["Not a detail that I can recall must be forgotten; in all calmness I must proceed.", "떠올릴 수 있는 사소한 것 하나라도 빠뜨려서는 안 된다. 모든 것을 차분하게 진행해야 한다."]], [["When I came to Renfield’s room I found him lying on the floor on his left side in a glittering pool of blood.", "렌필드의 방에 들어섰을 때, 그는 번들거리는 피웅덩이 속에 왼쪽으로 누워 바닥에 쓰러져 있었다."], ["When I went to move him, it became at once apparent that he had received some terrible injuries; there seemed none of that unity of purpose between the parts of the body which marks even lethargic sanity.", "그를 옮기려 다가가자, 그가 무언가 끔찍한 상해를 입었음이 곧바로 드러났다. 몸의 각 부분 사이에는, 무기력한 정신이상자에게서조차 보이는 그런 목적의 통일성이 전혀 없는 듯했다."], ["As the face was exposed I could see that it was horribly bruised, as though it had been beaten against the floor--indeed it was from the face wounds that the pool of blood originated.", "얼굴이 드러나자 나는 그것이 바닥에 짓찧기라도 한 듯 끔찍하게 멍들어 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 사실 피웅덩이는 바로 그 얼굴의 상처에서 비롯된 것이었다."], ["The attendant who was kneeling beside the body said to me as we turned him over:--", "시신 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 있던 간병인이 우리가 그를 돌려 눕힐 때 나에게 말했다."]], [["“I think, sir, his back is broken. See, both his right arm and leg and the whole side of his face are paralysed.”", "\"선생님, 제 생각에 이 사람 등뼈가 부러진 것 같습니다. 보세요, 오른팔과 오른다리, 그리고 얼굴 한쪽 전체가 마비되어 있어요.\""], ["How such a thing could have happened puzzled the attendant beyond measure.", "어떻게 이런 일이 일어날 수 있었는지 간병인은 도무지 짐작하지 못했다."], ["He seemed quite bewildered, and his brows were gathered in as he said:--", "그는 몹시 당황한 듯 눈썹을 잔뜩 모으며 말했다."]], [["“I can’t understand the two things. He could mark his face like that by beating his own head on the floor.", "\"이 두 가지를 도무지 이해할 수가 없습니다. 자기 머리를 바닥에 찧어서 얼굴을 저렇게 만들 수는 있죠."], ["I saw a young woman do it once at the Eversfield Asylum before anyone could lay hands on her.", "에버스필드 정신병원에서 어떤 젊은 여자가 누가 손쓸 새도 없이 그러는 걸 한 번 본 적이 있습니다."], ["And I suppose he might have broke his neck by falling out of bed, if he got in an awkward kink. But for the life of me I can’t imagine how the two things occurred.", "그리고 침대에서 떨어질 때 이상하게 꺾이면 목이 부러질 수도 있겠죠. 하지만 죽었다 깨어나도, 이 두 가지가 어떻게 같이 일어났는지는 도무지 상상이 안 됩니다."], ["If his back was broke, he couldn’t beat his head; and if his face was like that before the fall out of bed, there would be marks of it.”", "등뼈가 부러졌으면 머리를 찧을 수 없었을 테고, 침대에서 떨어지기 전에 얼굴이 저랬다면 그 자국이 남아 있었을 테니까요.\""], ["I said to him:--", "나는 그에게 말했다."]], [["“Go to Dr. Van Helsing, and ask him to kindly come here at once.", "\"판 헬싱 박사님께 가서, 즉시 이리로 와주십사고 청하게."], ["I want him without an instant’s delay.”", "한순간도 지체 없이 모셔와야겠네.\""], ["The man ran off, and within a few minutes the Professor, in his dressing gown and slippers, appeared.", "그 사람은 달려나갔고, 몇 분도 안 되어 교수가 실내복에 슬리퍼 차림으로 나타났다."], ["When he saw Renfield on the ground, he looked keenly at him a moment, and then turned to me.", "그는 바닥에 쓰러진 렌필드를 보자 잠시 그를 날카롭게 살피더니 나를 돌아보았다."], ["I think he recognised my thought in my eyes, for he said very quietly, manifestly for the ears of the attendant:--", "내 눈빛에서 내 생각을 알아챈 모양으로, 그는 분명 간병인의 귀를 의식하여 아주 조용히 말했다."]], [["“Ah, a sad accident!", "\"아, 가엾은 사고로군!"], ["He will need very careful watching, and much attention.", "이 사람은 아주 세심한 간호와 많은 보살핌이 필요할 걸세."], ["I shall stay with you myself; but I shall first dress myself.", "내가 직접 자네와 함께 있겠네. 다만 먼저 옷을 갖춰 입어야겠어."], ["If you will remain I shall in a few minutes join you.”", "자네가 여기 남아 있으면 몇 분 뒤에 합류하겠네.\""]], [["The patient was now breathing stertorously and it was easy to see that he had suffered some terrible injury.", "환자는 이제 코를 골듯 거친 숨을 몰아쉬고 있었고, 무언가 끔찍한 상해를 입었음을 쉽게 알아볼 수 있었다."], ["Van Helsing returned with extraordinary celerity, bearing with him a surgical case.", "판 헬싱은 놀라운 신속함으로 수술 도구함을 들고 돌아왔다."], ["He had evidently been thinking and had his mind made up; for, almost before he looked at the patient, he whispered to me:--", "그는 분명 골똘히 생각하여 마음을 정한 듯, 환자를 살펴보기도 전에 나에게 속삭였다."]], [["“Send the attendant away.", "\"간병인을 내보내게."], ["We must be alone with him when he becomes conscious, after the operation.” So I said:--", "수술 뒤에 이 사람이 정신을 차렸을 때, 우리만 그와 함께 있어야 하네.\" 그래서 나는 말했다."]], [["“I think that will do now, Simmons.", "\"지금은 이만하면 됐네, 시먼스."], ["We have done all that we can at present.", "당장 할 수 있는 건 다 했어."], ["You had better go your round, and Dr.", "자네는 순회를 도는 게 좋겠네."], ["Van Helsing will operate. Let me know instantly if there be anything unusual anywhere.”", "판 헬싱 박사님이 수술을 하실 걸세. 어디든 이상한 일이 있으면 즉시 알려주게.\""]], [["The man withdrew, and we went into a strict examination of the patient.", "그 사람이 물러가자, 우리는 환자를 엄밀히 진찰하기 시작했다."], ["The wounds of the face was superficial; the real injury was a depressed fracture of the skull, extending right up through the motor area.", "얼굴의 상처는 표면적인 것이었고, 진짜 부상은 운동 영역까지 곧장 뻗은 두개골 함몰 골절이었다."], ["The Professor thought a moment and said:--", "교수는 잠시 생각하더니 말했다."]], [["“We must reduce the pressure and get back to normal conditions, as far as can be; the rapidity of the suffusion shows the terrible nature of his injury.", "\"압력을 줄이고, 가능한 한 정상 상태로 되돌려놓아야 하네. 출혈이 번지는 속도가 이 사람 부상의 끔찍한 성격을 보여주고 있어."], ["The whole motor area seems affected.", "운동 영역 전체가 침범당한 것 같네."], ["The suffusion of the brain will increase quickly, so we must trephine at once or it may be too late.”", "뇌의 출혈이 빠르게 늘어날 테니, 당장 두개골에 구멍을 뚫지 않으면 너무 늦어버릴지도 모르네.\""], ["As he was speaking there was a soft tapping at the door.", "그가 말하고 있을 때 문을 부드럽게 두드리는 소리가 났다."], ["I went over and opened it and found in the corridor without, Arthur and Quincey in pajamas and slippers: the former spoke:--", "내가 가서 문을 여니 바깥 복도에 잠옷과 슬리퍼 차림의 아서와 퀸시가 서 있었다. 전자가 먼저 말했다."]], [["“I heard your man call up Dr. Van Helsing and tell him of an accident.", "\"자네 사람이 판 헬싱 박사님을 부르며 사고가 났다고 하는 걸 들었네."], ["So I woke Quincey or rather called for him as he was not asleep.", "그래서 퀸시를 깨웠지, 아니, 깨어 있었으니 그냥 불렀다는 게 맞겠군."], ["Things are moving too quickly and too strangely for sound sleep for any of us these times.", "요즘은 무슨 일이 너무 빠르고 너무 기이하게 돌아가서 누구도 깊이 잠들 수가 없으니 말이야."], ["I’ve been thinking that to-morrow night will not see things as they have been.", "나는 내일 밤이면 지금까지와는 사정이 달라지리라고 생각해왔네."], ["We’ll have to look back--and forward a little more than we have done.", "우리는 지금까지 해온 것보다 좀 더 뒤를 돌아보고, 또 앞을 내다봐야 할 거야."], ["May we come in?”", "들어가도 되겠나?\""], ["I nodded, and held the door open till they had entered; then I closed it again.", "나는 고개를 끄덕이고, 그들이 들어올 때까지 문을 잡아두었다가 다시 닫았다."], ["When Quincey saw the attitude and state of the patient, and noted the horrible pool on the floor, he said softly:--", "퀸시는 환자의 자세와 상태를 보고, 바닥의 끔찍한 웅덩이를 알아채자 나직이 말했다."]], [["“My God!", "\"맙소사!"], ["what has happened to him?", "이 사람한테 무슨 일이 일어난 건가?"], ["Poor, poor devil!”", "가엾은, 가엾은 친구!\""], ["I told him briefly, and added that we expected he would recover consciousness after the operation--for a short time, at all events.", "나는 그에게 간략히 사정을 일러주고, 수술 뒤에 그가 정신을 차릴 것으로 기대한다고, 적어도 잠깐 동안은 그러리라고 덧붙였다."], ["He went at once and sat down on the edge of the bed, with Godalming beside him; we all watched in patience.", "그는 곧 침대 가장자리에 가서 앉았고, 그 곁에 고달밍이 앉았다. 우리는 모두 참을성 있게 지켜보았다."]], [["“We shall wait,”", "\"우리는 기다리겠네.\""], ["said Van Helsing, “just long enough to fix the best spot for trephining, so that we may most quickly and perfectly remove the blood clot; for it is evident that the hæmorrhage is increasing.”", "판 헬싱이 말했다. \"두개골에 구멍을 뚫을 가장 좋은 자리를 정할 수 있을 만큼만, 가장 빠르고 완벽하게 핏덩이를 제거할 수 있도록 말일세. 출혈이 늘고 있는 게 분명하니까.\""]], [["The minutes during which we waited passed with fearful slowness.", "우리가 기다린 그 몇 분은 무서울 만큼 더디게 흘렀다."], ["I had a horrible sinking in my heart, and from Van Helsing’s face I gathered that he felt some fear or apprehension as to what was to come.", "내 가슴은 끔찍하게 내려앉았고, 판 헬싱의 얼굴에서 나는 그 또한 앞으로 닥칠 일에 대해 어떤 두려움이나 불안을 느끼고 있음을 헤아렸다."], ["I dreaded the words that Renfield might speak.", "나는 렌필드가 입을 열어 할지도 모를 말이 두려웠다."], ["I was positively afraid to think; but the conviction of what was coming was on me, as I have read of men who have heard the death-watch.", "솔직히 생각하는 것조차 두려웠다. 그러나 무언가 다가오고 있다는 확신이 나를 사로잡았으니, 마치 죽음의 시계 소리를 들었다는 사람들에 관한 글을 읽었을 때와 같았다."], ["The poor man’s breathing came in uncertain gasps.", "가엾은 사내의 숨결은 불규칙하게 헐떡이며 새어나왔다."], ["Each instant he seemed as though he would open his eyes and speak; but then would follow a prolonged stertorous breath, and he would relapse into a more fixed insensibility.", "매 순간 그는 곧 눈을 뜨고 입을 열 것만 같았으나, 그러다가는 길게 코를 고는 듯한 숨이 이어지며 더 깊은 무감각 속으로 다시 빠져들곤 했다."], ["Inured as I was to sick beds and death, this suspense grew, and grew upon me.", "병상과 죽음에 익숙해진 나였지만, 이 긴장은 자라고 또 자라나 나를 짓눌렀다."], ["I could almost hear the beating of my own heart; and the blood surging through my temples sounded like blows from a hammer.", "나는 내 심장이 뛰는 소리를 거의 들을 수 있을 정도였고, 관자놀이를 치고 지나가는 피가 망치질 소리처럼 울렸다."], ["The silence finally became agonising.", "마침내 침묵은 견디기 힘든 고통이 되었다."], ["I looked at my companions, one after another, and saw from their flushed faces and damp brows that they were enduring equal torture.", "나는 동료들을 한 사람씩 차례로 바라보았고, 상기된 얼굴과 땀 밴 이마에서 그들도 똑같은 고통을 견디고 있음을 알았다."], ["There was a nervous suspense over us all, as though overhead some dread bell would peal out powerfully when we should least expect it.", "마치 머리 위 어딘가에서 어떤 무서운 종이 우리가 가장 예상치 못한 순간에 힘차게 울려퍼질 것만 같은, 신경을 곤두세우는 긴장이 우리 모두를 덮고 있었다."]], [["At last there came a time when it was evident that the patient was sinking fast; he might die at any moment.", "마침내 환자가 급속히 가라앉고 있음이 분명해지는 순간이 왔다. 그는 어느 순간에라도 죽을 수 있었다."], ["I looked up at the Professor and caught his eyes fixed on mine.", "나는 교수를 올려다보았고, 그의 눈이 내 눈에 고정되어 있는 것을 알아챘다."], ["His face was sternly set as he spoke:--", "그는 얼굴을 엄숙하게 굳히며 말했다."]], [["“There is no time to lose.", "\"지체할 시간이 없네."], ["His words may be worth many lives; I have been thinking so, as I stood here.", "이 사람의 말은 여러 목숨만큼의 값어치가 있을지도 모르네. 여기 서 있는 동안 줄곧 그렇게 생각했어."], ["It may be there is a soul at stake!", "어쩌면 한 영혼이 걸린 일일지도 모르네!"], ["We shall operate just above the ear.”", "귀 바로 위쪽을 수술하겠네.\""]], [["Without another word he made the operation.", "그는 더 말하지 않고 수술을 시작했다."], ["For a few moments the breathing continued to be stertorous.", "잠시 동안 숨결은 계속 거칠게 코를 골았다."], ["Then there came a breath so prolonged that it seemed as though it would tear open his chest.", "그러다가 어찌나 긴 숨이 새어나오는지, 마치 가슴이 찢어질 것만 같았다."], ["Suddenly his eyes opened, and became fixed in a wild, helpless stare.", "갑자기 그가 눈을 떴고, 그 눈은 거칠고 무력한 응시에 못박혔다."], ["This was continued for a few moments; then it softened into a glad surprise, and from the lips came a sigh of relief.", "이것이 잠시 이어지더니, 곧 기쁜 놀라움으로 누그러졌고, 입술에서 안도의 한숨이 새어나왔다."], ["He moved convulsively, and as he did so, said:--", "그는 경련하듯 몸을 움직였고, 그러면서 말했다."]], [["“I’ll be quiet, Doctor.", "\"조용히 있겠습니다, 박사님."], ["Tell them to take off the strait-waistcoat.", "저 사람들한테 구속복을 벗겨달라고 해주세요."], ["I have had a terrible dream, and it has left me so weak that I cannot move.", "끔찍한 꿈을 꾸었는데, 그 탓에 너무 기운이 빠져서 움직일 수가 없어요."], ["What’s wrong with my face?", "제 얼굴이 왜 이렇죠?"], ["it feels all swollen, and it smarts dreadfully.”", "온통 부어오른 것 같고, 지독하게 따끔거립니다.\""], ["He tried to turn his head; but even with the effort his eyes seemed to grow glassy again so I gently put it back.", "그는 고개를 돌리려 했으나, 그렇게 애쓰는 것만으로도 눈빛이 다시 흐리멍덩해지는 듯해서 나는 그의 머리를 부드럽게 도로 뉘었다."], ["Then Van Helsing said in a quiet grave tone:--", "그러자 판 헬싱이 조용하고 엄숙한 어조로 말했다."]], [["“Tell us your dream, Mr. Renfield.”", "\"꿈 이야기를 해주시오, 렌필드 씨.\""], ["As he heard the voice his face brightened, through its mutilation, and he said:--", "그 목소리를 듣자 그의 얼굴은 일그러진 상처 속에서도 환해졌고, 그는 말했다."]], [["“That is Dr. Van Helsing.", "\"판 헬싱 박사님이시군요."], ["How good it is of you to be here.", "여기 와주시다니 정말 고맙습니다."], ["Give me some water, my lips are dry; and I shall try to tell you.", "물을 좀 주세요, 입술이 말랐어요. 그러면 말씀드려 보겠습니다."], ["I dreamed”--he stopped and seemed fainting, I called quietly to Quincey--“The brandy--it is in my study--quick!”", "제가 꿈을 꾸었는데요.\" 그는 말을 멈추고 정신을 잃는 듯했고, 나는 조용히 퀸시를 불렀다. \"브랜디를 — 내 서재에 있네 — 어서!\""], ["He flew and returned with a glass, the decanter of brandy and a carafe of water.", "그는 날 듯이 달려가 잔과 브랜디 병, 물병을 들고 돌아왔다."], ["We moistened the parched lips, and the patient quickly revived.", "우리가 바싹 마른 입술을 적셔주자 환자는 곧 되살아났다."], ["It seemed, however, that his poor injured brain had been working in the interval, for, when he was quite conscious, he looked at me piercingly with an agonised confusion which I shall never forget, and said:--", "그러나 그 사이에도 그의 가엾은 상한 뇌는 계속 작동했던 모양으로, 완전히 정신을 차렸을 때 그는 내가 결코 잊지 못할 고뇌에 찬 혼란의 눈빛으로 나를 꿰뚫듯 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“I must not deceive myself; it was no dream, but all a grim reality.”", "\"내 자신을 속여서는 안 되지. 그건 꿈이 아니었어, 모두 가혹한 현실이었어.\""], ["Then his eyes roved round the room; as they caught sight of the two figures sitting patiently on the edge of the bed he went on:--", "그러더니 그의 눈은 방 안을 두리번거렸다. 침대 가장자리에 참을성 있게 앉아 있는 두 사람의 모습이 눈에 들어오자 그는 말을 이었다."]], [["“If I were not sure already, I would know from them.”", "\"이미 확신하지 못했다 해도, 저 사람들을 보면 알 수 있었겠지.\""], ["For an instant his eyes closed--not with pain or sleep but voluntarily, as though he were bringing all his faculties to bear; when he opened them he said, hurriedly, and with more energy than he had yet displayed:--", "한순간 그의 눈이 감겼다 — 고통이나 잠 때문이 아니라, 마치 자신의 모든 기능을 끌어모으려는 듯 의도적으로. 다시 눈을 떴을 때 그는 그때까지 보인 적 없는 더 큰 기운을 담아 다급하게 말했다."]], [["“Quick, Doctor, quick. I am dying!", "\"어서요, 박사님, 어서. 저는 죽어가고 있어요!"], ["I feel that I have but a few minutes; and then I must go back to death--or worse!", "제게 남은 시간이 몇 분뿐임을 느낍니다. 그러고 나면 저는 죽음으로 — 아니, 더 나쁜 것으로 돌아가야 해요!"], ["Wet my lips with brandy again.", "입술을 브랜디로 다시 적셔주세요."], ["I have something that I must say before I die; or before my poor crushed brain dies anyhow.", "죽기 전에, 아니 어쨌든 제 가엾은 으스러진 뇌가 죽기 전에 꼭 해야 할 말이 있습니다."], ["Thank you!", "고맙습니다!"], ["It was that night after you left me, when I implored you to let me go away.", "그날 밤, 박사님이 저를 두고 가신 뒤였어요, 제가 보내달라고 박사님께 애원했던 그날 밤이요."], ["I couldn’t speak then, for I felt my tongue was tied; but I was as sane then, except in that way, as I am now.", "그때 저는 말을 할 수 없었어요, 혀가 묶인 것 같았거든요. 하지만 그 점만 빼면 그때도 저는 지금만큼 제정신이었습니다."], ["I was in an agony of despair for a long time after you left me; it seemed hours.", "박사님이 가신 뒤로 저는 오랫동안 절망의 고통 속에 있었어요, 몇 시간은 되는 것 같았죠."], ["Then there came a sudden peace to me.", "그러다가 갑자기 평온이 찾아왔습니다."], ["My brain seemed to become cool again, and I realised where I was.", "머리가 다시 차갑게 식는 듯했고, 제가 어디에 있는지 깨달았어요."], ["I heard the dogs bark behind our house, but not where He was!”", "저는 우리 집 뒤에서 개들이 짖는 소리를 들었지만, 그분이 계신 곳에서 나는 소리는 아니었어요!\""], ["As he spoke, Van Helsing’s eyes never blinked, but his hand came out and met mine and gripped it hard.", "그가 말하는 동안 판 헬싱의 눈은 한 번도 깜빡이지 않았으나, 그의 손이 뻗어나와 내 손과 마주쳐 꽉 움켜쥐었다."], ["He did not, however, betray himself; he nodded slightly and said: “Go on,” in a low voice.", "그러나 그는 속내를 드러내지 않았다. 그는 가볍게 고개를 끄덕이며 낮은 목소리로 \"계속하시오\"라고 말했다."], ["Renfield proceeded:--", "렌필드는 말을 이어갔다."]], [["“He came up to the window in the mist, as I had seen him often before; but he was solid then--not a ghost, and his eyes were fierce like a man’s when angry.", "\"그분은 안개 속에서 창가로 다가왔어요, 전에도 자주 보았던 것처럼요. 하지만 그때는 형체가 또렷했어요 — 유령이 아니었고, 눈은 화가 난 사람처럼 사나웠습니다."], ["He was laughing with his red mouth; the sharp white teeth glinted in the moonlight when he turned to look back over the belt of trees, to where the dogs were barking.", "그분은 붉은 입으로 웃고 있었어요. 나무 띠 너머, 개들이 짖던 그쪽을 돌아보려고 고개를 돌렸을 때, 날카로운 흰 이빨이 달빛에 번득였습니다."], ["I wouldn’t ask him to come in at first, though I knew he wanted to--just as he had wanted all along.", "저는 처음엔 그분에게 들어오라고 청하지 않으려 했어요, 그분이 들어오고 싶어 한다는 걸 알면서도요 — 줄곧 그러길 바라왔던 것처럼요."], ["Then he began promising me things--not in words but by doing them.” He was interrupted by a word from the Professor:--\n“How?”", "그러더니 그분이 제게 여러 가지를 약속하기 시작했어요 — 말이 아니라, 그걸 직접 보여주면서요.\" 교수가 한마디로 그의 말을 끊었다."]], [["“By making them happen; just as he used to send in the flies when the sun was shining.", "\"어떻게 말이오?\" \"그것들을 실제로 일어나게 해서요. 햇빛이 비칠 때 파리를 들여보내곤 했던 것처럼요."], ["Great big fat ones with steel and sapphire on their wings; and big moths, in the night, with skull and cross-bones on their backs.”", "날개에 강철빛과 사파이어빛이 도는 크고 살진 놈들이요. 그리고 밤이면 등에 해골과 십자 뼈 무늬가 있는 큰 나방들도요.\""], ["Van Helsing nodded to him as he whispered to me unconsciously:--", "판 헬싱은 그에게 고개를 끄덕이면서 나에게 무심결에 속삭였다."]], [["“The Acherontia Aitetropos of the Sphinges--what you call the ‘Death’s-head Moth’?”", "\"스핑크스나방속의 아케론티아 아트로포스 — 자네들이 '죽음의머리나방'이라 부르는 것 말일세?\""], ["The patient went on without stopping.", "환자는 멈추지 않고 말을 이었다."]], [["“Then he began to whisper: ‘Rats, rats, rats!", "\"그러더니 그분이 속삭이기 시작했어요."], ["Hundreds, thousands, millions of them, and every one a life; and dogs to eat them, and cats too.", "'쥐, 쥐, 쥐다! 수백, 수천, 수백만 마리, 하나하나가 다 생명이지. 그것들을 잡아먹을 개들도, 고양이들도."], ["All lives!", "모두 생명이야!"], ["all red blood, with years of life in it; and not merely buzzing flies!’ I laughed at him, for I wanted to see what he could do.", "모두 붉은 피이고, 그 안에 여러 해의 생명이 담겨 있지. 그저 윙윙대는 파리 따위가 아니라고!' 저는 그분이 무엇을 할 수 있는지 보고 싶어서 그를 비웃었어요."], ["Then the dogs howled, away beyond the dark trees in His house.", "그러자 어두운 나무들 너머, 그분의 집에서 개들이 울부짖었습니다."], ["He beckoned me to the window.", "그분은 저를 창가로 손짓해 불렀어요."], ["I got up and looked out, and He raised his hands, and seemed to call out without using any words.", "저는 일어나 밖을 내다보았고, 그분은 두 손을 들어올리며 아무 말도 쓰지 않고 무언가를 부르는 듯했습니다. 어두운 덩어리가 풀밭 위로 퍼지며 불꽃의 형상처럼 밀려왔어요. 그러더니 그분이 안개를 좌우로 움직이자, 저는 거기에 눈이 빨갛게 타오르는 쥐가 수천 마리나 있는 것을 볼 수 있었습니다 — 그분의 눈처럼, 다만 더 작았죠."], ["A dark mass spread over the grass, coming on like the shape of a flame of fire; and then He moved the mist to the right and left, and I could see that there were thousands of rats with their eyes blazing red--like His, only smaller.", "그분이 손을 들어올리자 그것들이 모두 멈췄어요. 그리고 그분이 이렇게 말하는 듯했습니다. '이 모든 생명을 너에게 주마, 그래, 그리고 더 많고 더 큰 것을, 헤아릴 수 없는 세월에 걸쳐 주리라, 네가 엎드려 나를 경배한다면!'"], ["He held up his hand, and they all stopped; and I thought he seemed to be saying: ‘All these lives will I give you, ay, and many more and greater, through countless ages, if you will fall down and worship me!’ And then a red cloud, like the colour of blood, seemed to close over my eyes; and before I knew what I was doing, I found myself opening the sash and saying to Him: ‘Come in, Lord and Master!’ The rats were all gone, but He slid into the room through the sash, though it was only open an inch wide--just as the Moon herself has often come in through the tiniest crack and has stood before me in all her size and splendour.”", "그러더니 붉은 구름이, 피 빛깔 같은 것이 제 눈앞을 가리는 듯했고, 제가 무얼 하는지도 모르는 채 저는 어느새 창틀을 열고 그분께 이렇게 말하고 있었습니다. '들어오세요, 주인님!' 쥐들은 모두 사라졌지만, 그분은 겨우 한 치쯤 열린 창틈으로 방 안에 미끄러져 들어왔어요 — 마치 달님이 늘 아주 작은 틈으로 들어와 온전한 크기와 광휘로 제 앞에 서곤 했던 것처럼요.\""]], [["His voice was weaker, so I moistened his lips with the brandy again, and he continued; but it seemed as though his memory had gone on working in the interval for his story was further advanced.", "그의 목소리가 약해져서 나는 브랜디로 다시 그의 입술을 적셔주었고, 그는 말을 이었다. 그러나 그 사이에도 그의 기억은 계속 작동한 듯, 이야기는 더 앞으로 나아가 있었다."], ["I was about to call him back to the point, but Van Helsing whispered to me: “Let him go on.", "나는 그를 다시 본래 지점으로 불러오려 했으나, 판 헬싱이 나에게 속삭였다."], ["Do not interrupt him; he cannot go back, and maybe could not proceed at all if once he lost the thread of his thought.”", "\"계속하게 두게. 끊지 말게. 그는 되돌아갈 수 없으니, 한번 생각의 실마리를 놓치면 아예 이어가지 못할지도 모르네.\""], ["He proceeded:--", "그는 말을 이어갔다."]], [["“All day I waited to hear from him, but he did not send me anything, not even a blow-fly, and when the moon got up I was pretty angry with him.", "\"하루 종일 저는 그분에게서 소식이 오기를 기다렸지만, 그분은 아무것도, 쉬파리 한 마리조차 보내주지 않았어요. 그래서 달이 떠올랐을 때 저는 그분에게 꽤 화가 나 있었습니다."], ["When he slid in through the window, though it was shut, and did not even knock, I got mad with him.", "그분이 창문이 닫혀 있는데도 그 틈으로 미끄러져 들어오고, 노크조차 하지 않자 저는 그분에게 단단히 화가 났어요."], ["He sneered at me, and his white face looked out of the mist with his red eyes gleaming, and he went on as though he owned the whole place, and I was no one.", "그분은 저를 비웃었고, 그 흰 얼굴이 붉은 눈을 번득이며 안개 속에서 내다보았어요. 그러고는 마치 이곳 전체가 자기 것이고 저는 아무것도 아니라는 듯이 행동했습니다."], ["He didn’t even smell the same as he went by me. I couldn’t hold him.", "제 곁을 지날 때 냄새조차 전과 같지 않았어요. 저는 그분을 붙잡을 수가 없었어요."], ["I thought that, somehow, Mrs. Harker had come into the room.”", "어쩐지 하커 부인이 방으로 들어온 것 같다고 생각했습니다.\""]], [["The two men sitting on the bed stood up and came over, standing behind him so that he could not see them, but where they could hear better.", "침대에 앉아 있던 두 사람이 일어나 다가와서는, 그가 볼 수 없도록 그의 뒤에 섰으나, 더 잘 들을 수 있는 자리였다."], ["They were both silent, but the Professor started and quivered; his face, however, grew grimmer and sterner still.", "두 사람 모두 말이 없었으나, 교수는 흠칫 놀라며 떨었다. 그러나 그의 얼굴은 한층 더 굳고 엄숙해졌다."], ["Renfield went on without noticing:--", "렌필드는 알아채지 못한 채 말을 이었다."]], [["“When Mrs. Harker came in to see me this afternoon she wasn’t the same; it was like tea after the teapot had been watered.”", "\"오늘 오후에 하커 부인이 저를 보러 들어왔을 때, 부인은 예전 같지 않았어요. 마치 찻주전자에 물을 부어 희석한 뒤의 차 같았죠.\""], ["Here we all moved, but no one said a word; he went on:--", "이 대목에서 우리는 모두 움찔했으나 아무도 입을 떼지 않았다. 그는 말을 이었다."]], [["“I didn’t know that she was here till she spoke; and she didn’t look the same.", "\"부인이 입을 열기 전까지는 거기 있는 줄도 몰랐어요. 게다가 부인은 예전 같아 보이지 않았습니다."], ["I don’t care for the pale people; I like them with lots of blood in them, and hers had all seemed to have run out.", "저는 창백한 사람들은 좋아하지 않아요. 피가 많은 사람들이 좋은데, 부인은 그 피가 다 빠져나가 버린 것 같았어요."], ["I didn’t think of it at the time; but when she went away I began to think, and it made me mad to know that He had been taking the life out of her.”", "그땐 거기까지 생각하지 못했지만, 부인이 떠나고 나서 저는 생각하기 시작했고, 그분이 부인에게서 생명을 빼앗아 가고 있었다는 걸 알자 미칠 듯이 화가 났습니다.\""], ["I could feel that the rest quivered, as I did, but we remained otherwise still.", "나도 그랬듯 다른 사람들도 떨고 있는 것을 나는 느낄 수 있었으나, 그 밖에는 우리 모두 가만히 있었다."], ["“So when He came to-night I was ready for Him.", "\"그래서 오늘 밤 그분이 왔을 때 저는 그분을 맞을 채비가 되어 있었어요."], ["I saw the mist stealing in, and I grabbed it tight.", "저는 안개가 슬며시 들어오는 것을 보고 그것을 꽉 붙잡았습니다."], ["I had heard that madmen have unnatural strength; and as I knew I was a madman--at times anyhow--I resolved to use my power.", "미친 사람들은 비정상적인 힘을 낸다고 들었고, 저도 미친 사람이라는 걸 — 적어도 때때로는 — 알고 있었으니, 제 힘을 쓰기로 작정했지요."], ["Ay, and He felt it too, for He had to come out of the mist to struggle with me.", "그래요, 그분도 그것을 느꼈어요, 저와 맞붙어 싸우려고 안개에서 나와야 했으니까요. 저는 꽉 붙들었어요."], ["I held tight; and I thought I was going to win, for I didn’t mean Him to take any more of her life, till I saw His eyes.", "그리고 제가 이길 것 같다고 생각했습니다, 그분이 부인의 생명을 더는 빼앗아 가게 두지 않을 작정이었거든요 — 그분의 눈을 보기 전까지는요."], ["They burned into me, and my strength became like water.", "그 눈이 저를 태워버리듯 노려보자, 제 힘은 물처럼 빠져나갔어요."], ["He slipped through it, and when I tried to cling to Him, He raised me up and flung me down.", "그분은 그 사이로 빠져나갔고, 제가 그분에게 매달리려 하자 그분이 저를 들어올려 내동댕이쳤습니다."], ["There was a red cloud before me, and a noise like thunder, and the mist seemed to steal away under the door.”", "제 눈앞에 붉은 구름이 끼었고, 천둥 같은 소리가 났으며, 안개는 문 밑으로 슬며시 빠져나가는 듯했어요.\""], ["His voice was becoming fainter and his breath more stertorous.", "그의 목소리는 점점 희미해졌고 숨결은 더 거칠게 코를 골았다."], ["Van Helsing stood up instinctively.", "판 헬싱이 본능적으로 일어섰다."]], [["“We know the worst now,” he said.", "\"이제 우리는 최악의 사정을 알게 되었네.\""], ["“He is here, and we know his purpose. It may not be too late.", "그가 말했다. \"그자가 여기 있고, 우리는 그자의 목적을 알아."], ["Let us be armed--the same as we were the other night, but lose no time; there is not an instant to spare.”", "아직 너무 늦지 않았을지도 모르네. 무장하세 — 지난밤과 똑같이, 다만 한순간도 허비하지 말게. 잠시도 지체할 틈이 없네.\""], ["There was no need to put our fear, nay our conviction, into words--we shared them in common.", "우리의 두려움을, 아니 우리의 확신을 말로 옮길 필요는 없었다 — 우리는 그것을 모두 함께 나누고 있었으니까."], ["We all hurried and took from our rooms the same things that we had when we entered the Count’s house.", "우리는 모두 서둘러 각자의 방에서, 백작의 집에 들어갔을 때 지녔던 것과 똑같은 물건들을 가져왔다."], ["The Professor had his ready, and as we met in the corridor he pointed to them significantly as he said:--", "교수는 이미 그것을 준비해두고 있었고, 우리가 복도에서 마주치자 그것들을 의미심장하게 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“They never leave me; and they shall not till this unhappy business is over.", "\"이것들은 결코 나를 떠나지 않네. 그리고 이 불행한 일이 끝날 때까지 떠나지 않을 걸세."], ["Be wise also, my friends.", "자네들도 현명하게 굴게, 친구들."], ["It is no common enemy that we deal with. Alas! alas!", "우리가 상대하는 것은 보통 적이 아니야. 아아! 아아!"], ["that that dear Madam Mina should suffer!”", "그 사랑스러운 미나 부인이 고통받아야 하다니!\""], ["He stopped; his voice was breaking, and I do not know if rage or terror predominated in my own heart.", "그는 말을 멈추었다. 그의 목소리는 떨리고 있었고, 나 자신의 가슴속에서는 분노가 앞섰는지 공포가 앞섰는지 나도 알 수 없었다."]], [["Outside the Harkers’ door we paused. Art and Quincey held back, and the latter said:--", "하커 부부의 방문 밖에서 우리는 걸음을 멈추었다. 아트와 퀸시가 머뭇거렸고, 후자가 말했다."]], [["“Should we disturb her?”", "\"부인을 깨워야 할까요?\""]], [["“We must,”", "\"그래야 하네.\""], ["said Van Helsing grimly.", "판 헬싱이 단호하게 말했다."], ["“If the door be locked, I shall break it in.”", "\"문이 잠겨 있으면 내가 부수고 들어가겠네.\""]], [["“May it not frighten her terribly?", "\"부인을 몹시 놀라게 하지 않을까요?"], ["It is unusual to break into a lady’s room!”", "숙녀의 방에 부수고 들어가는 건 예사로운 일이 아니니까요!\""]], [["Van Helsing said solemnly, “You are always right; but this is life and death.", "판 헬싱이 엄숙하게 말했다. \"자네 말은 언제나 옳네. 하지만 이건 사느냐 죽느냐의 문제야."], ["All chambers are alike to the doctor; and even were they not they are all as one to me to-night.", "의사에게는 모든 방이 다 같은 법이지. 설령 그렇지 않다 해도, 오늘 밤만큼은 나에게 모두가 하나일세."], ["Friend John, when I turn the handle, if the door does not open, do you put your shoulder down and shove; and you too, my friends. Now!”", "존, 내가 손잡이를 돌렸을 때 문이 열리지 않거든, 자네가 어깨를 들이대고 밀게. 자네들도 함께. 자, 이제!\""]], [["He turned the handle as he spoke, but the door did not yield.", "그는 말하면서 손잡이를 돌렸으나 문은 꿈쩍하지 않았다."], ["We threw ourselves against it; with a crash it burst open, and we almost fell headlong into the room.", "우리는 몸을 던져 문을 들이받았고, 쾅 소리와 함께 문이 활짝 열리며 우리는 거의 곤두박질치듯 방 안으로 쓰러져 들어갔다."], ["The Professor did actually fall, and I saw across him as he gathered himself up from hands and knees.", "교수는 실제로 넘어졌고, 그가 두 손과 무릎을 짚고 몸을 일으키는 너머로 나는 방 안을 보았다."], ["What I saw appalled me. I felt my hair rise like bristles on the back of my neck, and my heart seemed to stand still.", "내가 본 것은 나를 소름끼치게 했다. 목덜미의 털이 곤두서는 듯했고, 심장이 멎는 것만 같았다."]], [["The moonlight was so bright that through the thick yellow blind the room was light enough to see.", "달빛이 어찌나 밝던지 두껍고 노란 블라인드를 통해서도 방 안은 알아볼 수 있을 만큼 환했다."], ["On the bed beside the window lay Jonathan Harker, his face flushed and breathing heavily as though in a stupor.", "창가의 침대에는 조너선 하커가 누워 있었는데, 얼굴이 상기된 채 혼수상태에 빠진 듯 거칠게 숨을 쉬고 있었다."], ["Kneeling on the near edge of the bed facing outwards was the white-clad figure of his wife.", "침대의 가까운 쪽 가장자리에 바깥을 향해 무릎 꿇고 있는 것은 흰옷 차림의 그의 아내였다."], ["By her side stood a tall, thin man, clad in black.", "그녀 곁에는 검은 옷을 입은 키 크고 야윈 남자가 서 있었다."], ["His face was turned from us, but the instant we saw we all recognised the Count--in every way, even to the scar on his forehead.", "그의 얼굴은 우리 쪽에서 돌려져 있었으나, 우리는 보는 순간 모두 그가 백작임을 알아보았다 — 모든 면에서, 심지어 이마의 흉터까지도."], ["With his left hand he held both Mrs. Harker’s hands, keeping them away with her arms at full tension; his right hand gripped her by the back of the neck, forcing her face down on his bosom.", "그는 왼손으로 하커 부인의 두 손을 함께 붙들어 그녀의 팔을 한껏 당긴 채 떼어놓고 있었고, 오른손으로는 그녀의 목덜미를 움켜쥐어 그녀의 얼굴을 자기 가슴에 짓누르고 있었다."], ["Her white nightdress was smeared with blood, and a thin stream trickled down the man’s bare breast which was shown by his torn-open dress.", "그녀의 흰 잠옷은 피로 얼룩져 있었고, 찢겨 벌어진 옷 사이로 드러난 남자의 맨가슴에서는 가느다란 핏줄기가 흘러내리고 있었다."], ["The attitude of the two had a terrible resemblance to a child forcing a kitten’s nose into a saucer of milk to compel it to drink.", "두 사람의 자세는, 새끼 고양이에게 우유를 억지로 먹이려고 그 코를 우유 접시에 짓누르는 것과 끔찍하게도 닮아 있었다."], ["As we burst into the room, the Count turned his face, and the hellish look that I had heard described seemed to leap into it.", "우리가 방 안으로 들이닥치자 백작은 얼굴을 돌렸고, 내가 묘사로만 들어왔던 그 지옥 같은 표정이 그 얼굴로 뛰어드는 듯했다."], ["His eyes flamed red with devilish passion; the great nostrils of the white aquiline nose opened wide and quivered at the edge; and the white sharp teeth, behind the full lips of the blood-dripping mouth, champed together like those of a wild beast.", "그의 눈은 악마 같은 격정으로 붉게 타올랐고, 희고 매부리 같은 코의 큰 콧구멍은 활짝 벌어져 그 가장자리가 떨렸으며, 피가 뚝뚝 떨어지는 입의 도톰한 입술 뒤로 희고 날카로운 이빨이 야수의 그것처럼 맞부딪쳤다."], ["With a wrench, which threw his victim back upon the bed as though hurled from a height, he turned and sprang at us.", "그는 마치 높은 곳에서 내던지듯 자기 희생자를 침대 위로 홱 뿌리치고는, 몸을 돌려 우리를 향해 덤벼들었다."], ["But by this time the Professor had gained his feet, and was holding towards him the envelope which contained the Sacred Wafer.", "그러나 이때 교수는 이미 몸을 일으켜, 성체병(聖體餠)이 든 봉투를 그자를 향해 내밀고 있었다."], ["The Count suddenly stopped, just as poor Lucy had done outside the tomb, and cowered back.", "백작은 가엾은 루시가 무덤 밖에서 그랬던 것처럼 갑자기 멈춰서더니 움츠러들며 물러섰다."], ["Further and further back he cowered, as we, lifting our crucifixes, advanced.", "우리가 십자고상을 치켜들고 다가가자 그자는 점점 더 뒤로 움츠러들었다."], ["The moonlight suddenly failed, as a great black cloud sailed across the sky; and when the gaslight sprang up under Quincey’s match, we saw nothing but a faint vapour.", "거대한 검은 구름이 하늘을 가로질러 떠가면서 달빛이 갑자기 사라졌고, 퀸시가 켠 성냥불 아래 가스등이 확 타오르자, 우리 눈에는 희미한 수증기 외에는 아무것도 보이지 않았다."], ["This, as we looked, trailed under the door, which with the recoil from its bursting open, had swung back to its old position.", "우리가 바라보는 동안 그것은 문 밑으로 스며 빠져나갔는데, 문은 부서지며 활짝 열렸던 반동으로 도로 본래 자리로 돌아와 있었다."], ["Van Helsing, Art, and I moved forward to Mrs.", "판 헬싱과 아트와 나는 하커 부인 쪽으로 나아갔다."], ["Harker, who by this time had drawn her breath and with it had given a scream so wild, so ear-piercing, so despairing that it seems to me now that it will ring in my ears till my dying day.", "이때쯤 그녀는 숨을 들이쉬었고, 그와 함께 어찌나 거칠고, 어찌나 귀를 찢는 듯하고, 어찌나 절망적인 비명을 질렀던지, 그 소리가 죽는 날까지 내 귓속에 울릴 것만 같은 생각이 지금도 든다."], ["For a few seconds she lay in her helpless attitude and disarray.", "몇 초 동안 그녀는 무력한 자세로 흐트러진 채 누워 있었다."], ["Her face was ghastly, with a pallor which was accentuated by the blood which smeared her lips and cheeks and chin; from her throat trickled a thin stream of blood; her eyes were mad with terror.", "그녀의 얼굴은 끔찍하리만치 창백했고, 입술과 뺨과 턱에 묻은 피가 그 창백함을 한층 도드라지게 했다. 목에서는 가느다란 핏줄기가 흘러내렸고, 두 눈은 공포로 미쳐 있었다."], ["Then she put before her face her poor crushed hands, which bore on their whiteness the red mark of the Count’s terrible grip, and from behind them came a low desolate wail which made the terrible scream seem only the quick expression of an endless grief.", "그러다 그녀는 가엾게 으스러진 두 손을 얼굴 앞으로 가져갔는데, 그 흰 손에는 백작의 끔찍한 움켜쥠이 남긴 붉은 자국이 찍혀 있었다. 그 손 뒤에서 낮고 황량한 흐느낌이 새어나왔는데, 그 소리에 비하면 그 끔찍한 비명조차 끝없는 슬픔의 순간적인 표현일 뿐인 듯했다."], ["Van Helsing stepped forward and drew the coverlet gently over her body, whilst Art, after looking at her face for an instant despairingly, ran out of the room.", "판 헬싱이 앞으로 나아가 그녀의 몸 위로 이불을 부드럽게 덮어주었고, 한편 아트는 한순간 절망에 잠겨 그녀의 얼굴을 바라보다가 방 밖으로 뛰쳐나갔다."], ["Van Helsing whispered to me:--", "판 헬싱이 나에게 속삭였다."]], [["“Jonathan is in a stupor such as we know the Vampire can produce.", "\"조너선은 우리가 흡혈귀가 일으킬 수 있다고 알고 있는 그런 혼수상태에 빠져 있네."], ["We can do nothing with poor Madam Mina for a few moments till she recovers herself; I must wake him!”", "가엾은 미나 부인은 스스로 정신을 차릴 때까지 몇 분 동안은 우리가 어찌해볼 수가 없어."], ["He dipped the end of a towel in cold water and with it began to flick him on the face, his wife all the while holding her face between her hands and sobbing in a way that was heart-breaking to hear.", "나는 이 사람을 깨워야겠네!\" 그는 수건 끝을 찬물에 적셔 그것으로 그의 얼굴을 톡톡 두드리기 시작했다. 그동안 내내 그의 아내는 두 손에 얼굴을 묻은 채, 듣고 있기에도 가슴이 미어지는 모습으로 흐느꼈다."], ["I raised the blind, and looked out of the window.", "나는 블라인드를 올리고 창밖을 내다보았다."], ["There was much moonshine; and as I looked I could see Quincey Morris run across the lawn and hide himself in the shadow of a great yew-tree.", "달빛이 환했다. 내가 바라보는 동안 퀸시 모리스가 잔디밭을 가로질러 달려가 커다란 주목 그늘에 몸을 숨기는 것이 보였다."], ["It puzzled me to think why he was doing this; but at the instant I heard Harker’s quick exclamation as he woke to partial consciousness, and turned to the bed.", "그가 왜 이러는지 짐작이 가지 않아 의아했으나, 바로 그 순간 하커가 부분적으로 의식을 되찾으며 다급하게 내지르는 소리를 듣고 나는 침대 쪽으로 돌아섰다."], ["On his face, as there might well be, was a look of wild amazement.", "그의 얼굴에는, 그럴 만도 했지만, 거친 경악의 빛이 어려 있었다."], ["He seemed dazed for a few seconds, and then full consciousness seemed to burst upon him all at once, and he started up.", "그는 몇 초 동안 멍한 듯하더니, 곧 완전한 의식이 한꺼번에 그를 덮치는 듯 벌떡 일어났다."], ["His wife was aroused by the quick movement, and turned to him with her arms stretched out, as though to embrace him; instantly, however, she drew them in again, and putting her elbows together, held her hands before her face, and shuddered till the bed beneath her shook.", "그 빠른 움직임에 그의 아내가 깨어났고, 마치 그를 끌어안으려는 듯 두 팔을 뻗으며 그에게로 돌아섰다. 그러나 곧 그녀는 두 팔을 다시 거두어 두 팔꿈치를 모으고 손을 얼굴 앞에 가져다 댄 채, 침대가 다 흔들리도록 부들부들 떨었다."]], [["“In God’s name what does this mean?”", "\"하느님 맙소사, 이게 대체 무슨 일이오?\""], ["Harker cried out. “Dr.", "하커가 외쳤다."], ["Seward, Dr. Van Helsing, what is it?", "\"수어드 박사, 판 헬싱 박사, 이게 뭡니까?"], ["What has happened?", "무슨 일이 일어난 거죠?"], ["What is wrong?", "뭐가 잘못된 겁니까?"], ["Mina, dear, what is it?", "미나, 여보, 이게 뭐요?"], ["What does that blood mean?", "저 피는 무슨 뜻이오?"], ["My God, my God!", "하느님, 하느님!"], ["has it come to this!”", "결국 이렇게 되고 말았단 말입니까!\""], ["and, raising himself to his knees, he beat his hands wildly together.", "그러고는 무릎을 세우며 두 손을 거칠게 맞부딪쳤다. \"하느님, 우리를 도우소서!"], ["“Good God help us!", "이 사람을 도우소서!"], ["help her! oh, help her!”", "오, 이 사람을 도우소서!\""], ["With a quick movement he jumped from bed, and began to pull on his clothes,--all the man in him awake at the need for instant exertion.", "그는 빠른 동작으로 침대에서 뛰어내려 옷을 걸치기 시작했다 — 당장의 행동이 필요한 상황에 그의 안에 있는 사내가 온통 깨어난 것이다."], ["“What has happened?", "\"무슨 일이 일어난 겁니까?"], ["Tell me all about it!”", "전부 다 말해주세요!\""], ["he cried without pausing. “Dr.", "그는 멈추지 않고 외쳤다."], ["Van Helsing, you love Mina, I know. Oh, do something to save her.", "\"판 헬싱 박사님, 박사님은 미나를 사랑하시잖습니까, 저는 압니다."], ["It cannot have gone too far yet.", "오, 이 사람을 구하려면 뭐든 해주세요."], ["Guard her while I look for him!”", "아직 너무 멀리 갔을 리는 없습니다."], ["His wife, through her terror and horror and distress, saw some sure danger to him: instantly forgetting her own grief, she seized hold of him and cried out:--", "제가 그자를 찾는 동안 이 사람을 지켜주세요!\" 그의 아내는 공포와 전율과 비탄 속에서도 그에게 닥칠 어떤 분명한 위험을 알아챘다. 그녀는 곧바로 자신의 슬픔을 잊고 그를 붙들며 외쳤다."]], [["“No! no! Jonathan, you must not leave me.", "\"안 돼요! 안 돼요! 조너선, 당신은 나를 두고 가서는 안 돼요."], ["I have suffered enough to-night, God knows, without the dread of his harming you.", "하느님도 아시지만, 오늘 밤 나는 충분히 고통받았어요. 그자가 당신을 해칠지도 모른다는 두려움까지 더해서는 안 돼요."], ["You must stay with me.", "당신은 나와 함께 있어야 해요."], ["Stay with these friends who will watch over you!”", "당신을 지켜줄 이 친구분들과 함께 있어요!\""], ["Her expression became frantic as she spoke; and, he yielding to her, she pulled him down sitting on the bed side, and clung to him fiercely.", "그녀가 말하는 동안 그녀의 표정은 광란에 빠진 듯했고, 그가 그녀에게 굴복하자 그녀는 그를 침대 곁에 앉도록 끌어당겨 그에게 격렬하게 매달렸다."]], [["Van Helsing and I tried to calm them both.", "판 헬싱과 나는 두 사람을 모두 진정시키려 애썼다."], ["The Professor held up his little golden crucifix, and said with wonderful calmness:--", "교수는 자신의 작은 금빛 십자고상을 들어올리며 놀랍도록 침착하게 말했다."]], [["“Do not fear, my dear.", "\"두려워하지 마시오, 부인."], ["We are here; and whilst this is close to you no foul thing can approach.", "우리가 여기 있소. 그리고 이것이 부인 가까이 있는 한 어떤 사악한 것도 다가올 수 없소."], ["You are safe for to-night; and we must be calm and take counsel together.”", "부인은 오늘 밤 안전하오. 우리는 차분하게 마음을 가다듬고 함께 의논해야 하오.\""], ["She shuddered and was silent, holding down her head on her husband’s breast.", "그녀는 몸을 떨더니 입을 다물고, 남편의 가슴에 고개를 떨구었다."], ["When she raised it, his white night-robe was stained with blood where her lips had touched, and where the thin open wound in her neck had sent forth drops.", "그녀가 고개를 들었을 때, 그의 흰 잠옷에는 그녀의 입술이 닿았던 자리와, 목의 가느다랗게 벌어진 상처에서 핏방울이 떨어진 자리에 피가 묻어 있었다."], ["The instant she saw it she drew back, with a low wail, and whispered, amidst choking sobs:--", "그것을 본 순간 그녀는 낮은 흐느낌과 함께 뒤로 물러서며, 흐느낌에 목이 메어 속삭였다."]], [["“Unclean, unclean!", "\"부정해, 부정해졌어!"], ["I must touch him or kiss him no more.", "나는 더는 그이를 만져서도 입맞춰서도 안 돼."], ["Oh, that it should be that it is I who am now his worst enemy, and whom he may have most cause to fear.”", "오, 이제 내가 그이의 가장 무서운 적이 되고, 그이가 가장 두려워해야 할 까닭이 바로 나라니.\""], ["To this he spoke out resolutely:--", "이에 그는 단호하게 말했다."]], [["“Nonsense, Mina. It is a shame to me to hear such a word.", "\"무슨 그런 소릴, 미나. 그런 말을 듣다니 나로서는 수치스럽소."], ["I would not hear it of you; and I shall not hear it from you.", "당신에 대해서 그런 말을 듣지 않겠소. 당신에게서도 그런 말을 듣지 않겠소."], ["May God judge me by my deserts, and punish me with more bitter suffering than even this hour, if by any act or will of mine anything ever come between us!”", "만일 내 어떤 행위나 의지로든 우리 사이를 가로막는 무언가가 생긴다면, 하느님께서 내가 받아 마땅한 대로 나를 심판하시고, 이 시각보다 더 쓰라린 고통으로 나를 벌하시기를!\""], ["He put out his arms and folded her to his breast; and for a while she lay there sobbing.", "그는 두 팔을 뻗어 그녀를 가슴에 끌어안았고, 그녀는 한동안 그 품에 안겨 흐느꼈다."], ["He looked at us over her bowed head, with eyes that blinked damply above his quivering nostrils; his mouth was set as steel.", "그는 그녀의 숙인 머리 너머로 우리를 바라보았는데, 떨리는 콧구멍 위로 그의 눈은 젖어 깜빡이고 있었고, 입은 강철처럼 굳게 다물려 있었다."], ["After a while her sobs became less frequent and more faint, and then he said to me, speaking with a studied calmness which I felt tried his nervous power to the utmost:--", "한참 뒤 그녀의 흐느낌은 뜸해지고 더 희미해졌으며, 그러자 그는 나에게, 내가 보기에 그의 신경의 힘을 극도로 시험하는 듯한 애써 꾸민 침착함으로 말했다."]], [["“And now, Dr. Seward, tell me all about it.", "\"이제, 수어드 박사, 모든 걸 다 말해주세요."], ["Too well I know the broad fact; tell me all that has been.”", "큰 줄거리는 너무도 잘 알고 있습니다. 그동안 있었던 일을 다 말해주세요.\""], ["I told him exactly what had happened, and he listened with seeming impassiveness; but his nostrils twitched and his eyes blazed as I told how the ruthless hands of the Count had held his wife in that terrible and horrid position, with her mouth to the open wound in his breast.", "나는 일어난 일을 그대로 그에게 일러주었고, 그는 겉보기에는 무덤덤하게 들었다. 그러나 백작의 무자비한 손이 그의 아내를, 그 끔찍하고 소름끼치는 자세로 입을 그의 가슴의 벌어진 상처에 댄 채 붙들고 있었다고 내가 말했을 때, 그의 콧구멍은 실룩였고 두 눈은 불타올랐다."], ["It interested me, even at that moment, to see, that, whilst the face of white set passion worked convulsively over the bowed head, the hands tenderly and lovingly stroked the ruffled hair.", "그 순간에조차 내 흥미를 끈 것은, 흰빛으로 굳은 격정이 숙인 머리 너머로 경련하듯 일렁이는 동안, 그 손은 헝클어진 머리칼을 다정하고 사랑스럽게 쓰다듬고 있었다는 점이었다."], ["Just as I had finished, Quincey and Godalming knocked at the door.", "내가 막 이야기를 마쳤을 때 퀸시와 고달밍이 문을 두드렸다."], ["They entered in obedience to our summons.", "그들은 우리의 부름에 따라 들어왔다."], ["Van Helsing looked at me questioningly.", "판 헬싱이 나를 의아한 눈빛으로 바라보았다."], ["I understood him to mean if we were to take advantage of their coming to divert if possible the thoughts of the unhappy husband and wife from each other and from themselves; so on nodding acquiescence to him he asked them what they had seen or done.", "나는 그것이, 가능하다면 이 불행한 부부의 생각을 서로에게서, 또 그들 자신에게서 돌려놓는 데에 그들의 등장을 이용하자는 뜻으로 이해했다. 그래서 그에게 동의하여 고개를 끄덕이자, 그는 그들에게 무엇을 보았고 무엇을 했는지 물었다."], ["To which Lord Godalming answered:--", "이에 고달밍 경이 대답했다."]], [["“I could not see him anywhere in the passage, or in any of our rooms.", "\"복도에서도, 우리 방 어디에서도 그자를 찾을 수 없었네."], ["I looked in the study but, though he had been there, he had gone.", "서재도 들여다봤는데, 거기 있었던 흔적은 있었지만 그자는 가버렸더군."], ["He had, however----”", "다만 그자가——\""], ["He stopped suddenly, looking at the poor drooping figure on the bed.", "그는 침대 위의 가엾게 늘어진 모습을 바라보며 갑자기 말을 멈추었다."], ["Van Helsing said gravely:--", "판 헬싱이 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“Go on, friend Arthur.", "\"계속하게, 아서."], ["We want here no more concealments.", "여기서는 더 이상의 은폐가 필요 없네."], ["Our hope now is in knowing all.", "이제 우리의 희망은 모든 것을 아는 데에 있어."], ["Tell freely!”", "거리낌없이 말하게!\""], ["So Art went on:--", "그래서 아트는 말을 이었다."]], [["“He had been there, and though it could only have been for a few seconds, he made rare hay of the place.", "\"그자가 거기 있었어. 그래봐야 몇 초 동안이었겠지만, 그자가 그곳을 엉망진창으로 만들어놓았더군."], ["All the manuscript had been burned, and the blue flames were flickering amongst the white ashes; the cylinders of your phonograph too were thrown on the fire, and the wax had helped the flames.”", "원고는 모조리 불태워졌고, 푸른 불꽃이 흰 잿더미 사이에서 일렁이고 있었네. 자네 축음기의 원통들도 불 속에 던져졌고, 밀랍이 불길을 키웠더군.\""], ["Here I interrupted. “Thank God there is the other copy in the safe!”", "여기서 나는 끼어들었다. \"다행히 금고 안에 다른 사본이 있어!\""], ["His face lit for a moment, but fell again as he went on: “I ran downstairs then, but could see no sign of him.", "그의 얼굴은 잠시 환해졌으나, 말을 이으며 다시 어두워졌다. \"그러고 나서 나는 아래층으로 달려갔지만, 그자의 자취는 찾을 수 없었네."], ["I looked into Renfield’s room; but there was no trace there except----!”", "렌필드의 방을 들여다봤는데, 거기엔 아무 흔적도 없었어, 다만——!\""], ["Again he paused. “Go on,”", "그는 다시 말을 멈추었다. \"계속하게.\""], ["said Harker hoarsely; so he bowed his head and moistening his lips with his tongue, added: “except that the poor fellow is dead.” Mrs.", "하커가 쉰 목소리로 말했다. 그러자 그는 고개를 숙이고 혀로 입술을 적시며 덧붙였다. \"다만 그 가엾은 친구가 죽어 있다는 것만 빼고는.\""], ["Harker raised her head, looking from one to the other of us she said solemnly:--", "하커 부인이 고개를 들어 우리를 한 사람씩 번갈아 바라보며 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“God’s will be done!”", "\"하느님의 뜻이 이루어지기를!\""], ["I could not but feel that Art was keeping back something; but, as I took it that it was with a purpose, I said nothing.", "나는 아트가 무언가를 감추고 있다는 느낌을 떨칠 수 없었으나, 그것이 어떤 의도가 있어서라고 여겼기에 아무 말도 하지 않았다."], ["Van Helsing turned to Morris and asked:--", "판 헬싱이 모리스 쪽으로 돌아서며 물었다."]], [["“And you, friend Quincey, have you any to tell?”", "\"그리고 자네는, 퀸시, 할 이야기가 좀 있나?\""]], [["“A little,”", "\"조금요.\""], ["he answered.", "그가 대답했다."], ["“It may be much eventually, but at present I can’t say.", "\"결국엔 큰일이 될지도 모르지만, 지금으로선 뭐라 말할 수가 없습니다."], ["I thought it well to know if possible where the Count would go when he left the house. I did not see him; but I saw a bat rise from Renfield’s window, and flap westward.", "가능하다면 백작이 집을 떠나 어디로 갈지 알아두는 게 좋겠다고 생각했어요. 그자를 보지는 못했지만, 박쥐 한 마리가 렌필드의 창문에서 날아올라 서쪽으로 펄럭이며 가는 걸 보았습니다."], ["I expected to see him in some shape go back to Carfax; but he evidently sought some other lair.", "저는 그자가 어떤 형체로든 카팩스로 돌아갈 거라고 예상했지만, 분명 다른 은신처를 찾아간 모양입니다."], ["He will not be back to-night; for the sky is reddening in the east, and the dawn is close.", "오늘 밤에는 돌아오지 않을 겁니다. 동쪽 하늘이 붉어지고 있고 새벽이 가까웠으니까요."], ["We must work to-morrow!”", "우리는 내일 움직여야 합니다!\""]], [["He said the latter words through his shut teeth.", "그는 마지막 말을 이를 악문 채 내뱉었다."], ["For a space of perhaps a couple of minutes there was silence, and I could fancy that I could hear the sound of our hearts beating; then Van Helsing said, placing his hand very tenderly on Mrs. Harker’s head:--", "아마 이삼 분쯤 침묵이 흘렀고, 나는 우리 심장이 뛰는 소리가 들리는 것만 같은 기분이 들었다. 그때 판 헬싱이 하커 부인의 머리에 아주 다정하게 손을 얹으며 말했다."]], [["“And now, Madam Mina--poor, dear, dear Madam Mina--tell us exactly what happened.", "\"이제, 미나 부인 — 가엾고, 사랑스럽고, 사랑스러운 미나 부인 — 무슨 일이 있었는지 정확하게 말해주시오."], ["God knows that I do not want that you be pained; but it is need that we know all.", "하느님도 아시지만 나는 부인이 괴로워하기를 바라지 않소. 그러나 우리가 모든 것을 알아야 할 필요가 있소."], ["For now more than ever has all work to be done quick and sharp, and in deadly earnest.", "이제 그 어느 때보다도, 모든 일을 빠르고 날카롭게, 그리고 목숨을 건 진지함으로 해내야 하기 때문이오."], ["The day is close to us that must end all, if it may be so; and now is the chance that we may live and learn.”", "가능하다면 모든 것을 끝내야 할 날이 우리에게 가까이 다가왔소. 그리고 지금이야말로 우리가 살아남아 알아낼 수 있는 기회요.\""]], [["The poor, dear lady shivered, and I could see the tension of her nerves as she clasped her husband closer to her and bent her head lower and lower still on his breast.", "가엾고 사랑스러운 부인은 몸을 떨었고, 그녀가 남편을 더 가까이 끌어안고 그의 가슴에 고개를 점점 더 낮게 묻을 때 나는 그녀의 신경이 팽팽하게 긴장하는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Then she raised her head proudly, and held out one hand to Van Helsing who took it in his, and, after stooping and kissing it reverently, held it fast.", "그러다 그녀는 당당하게 고개를 들고 한 손을 판 헬싱에게 내밀었고, 그는 그 손을 잡아 몸을 굽혀 경건하게 입맞춘 뒤 꼭 쥐었다."], ["The other hand was locked in that of her husband, who held his other arm thrown round her protectingly.", "다른 한 손은 남편의 손에 맞잡혀 있었고, 남편은 다른 한 팔로 그녀를 보호하듯 감싸안고 있었다."], ["After a pause in which she was evidently ordering her thoughts, she began:--", "분명 생각을 가다듬는 듯한 침묵 끝에 그녀는 입을 열었다."]], [["“I took the sleeping draught which you had so kindly given me, but for a long time it did not act.", "\"저는 박사님께서 친절하게 주신 수면제를 먹었지만, 오랫동안 약이 듣지 않았어요."], ["I seemed to become more wakeful, and myriads of horrible fancies began to crowd in upon my mind--all of them connected with death, and vampires; with blood, and pain, and trouble.”", "오히려 더 말똥말똥해지는 것 같았고, 무수히 많은 끔찍한 환상이 제 마음속으로 밀려들기 시작했어요 — 모두가 죽음과 흡혈귀, 피와 고통과 고난에 얽힌 것들이었죠.\""], ["Her husband involuntarily groaned as she turned to him and said lovingly: “Do not fret, dear.", "그녀의 남편이 자기도 모르게 신음하자 그녀는 그를 돌아보며 다정하게 말했다. \"괴로워하지 말아요, 여보."], ["You must be brave and strong, and help me through the horrible task.", "당신은 용감하고 굳세야 해요, 그리고 이 끔찍한 일을 제가 헤쳐나가도록 도와줘야 해요."], ["If you only knew what an effort it is to me to tell of this fearful thing at all, you would understand how much I need your help.", "이 무서운 일을 말로 옮기는 것이 제게 얼마나 큰 노력이 드는지 당신이 안다면, 제가 당신의 도움을 얼마나 필요로 하는지 이해할 거예요."], ["Well, I saw I must try to help the medicine to its work with my will, if it was to do me any good, so I resolutely set myself to sleep.", "자, 저는 약이 제게 조금이라도 들으려면 제 의지로 약효를 도와야겠다고 생각했어요. 그래서 단단히 마음먹고 잠을 청했죠."], ["Sure enough sleep must soon have come to me, for I remember no more.", "아니나 다를까, 곧 잠이 든 모양이에요, 그 뒤로는 아무것도 기억나지 않으니까요."], ["Jonathan coming in had not waked me, for he lay by my side when next I remember.", "조너선이 들어온 것도 저를 깨우지 못했어요. 다음에 기억나는 순간에 그이가 제 곁에 누워 있었거든요."], ["There was in the room the same thin white mist that I had before noticed.", "방 안에는 전에 제가 보았던 것과 똑같은 가느다란 흰 안개가 있었어요."], ["But I forget now if you know of this; you will find it in my diary which I shall show you later.", "그런데 박사님들이 이걸 아시는지 지금은 기억이 안 나네요. 나중에 보여드릴 제 일기에 적혀 있을 거예요."], ["I felt the same vague terror which had come to me before and the same sense of some presence.", "저는 전에 찾아왔던 것과 똑같은 어렴풋한 공포와, 무언가가 곁에 있다는 똑같은 느낌을 받았어요."], ["I turned to wake Jonathan, but found that he slept so soundly that it seemed as if it was he who had taken the sleeping draught, and not I.", "조너선을 깨우려고 돌아섰지만, 그이가 어찌나 깊이 잠들었던지 마치 수면제를 먹은 게 제가 아니라 그이인 것만 같았어요."], ["I tried, but I could not wake him.", "깨우려 했지만 깨울 수가 없었죠."], ["This caused me a great fear, and I looked around terrified.", "그래서 저는 큰 두려움에 사로잡혀, 겁에 질린 채 주위를 둘러보았어요."], ["Then indeed, my heart sank within me: beside the bed, as if he had stepped out of the mist--or rather as if the mist had turned into his figure, for it had entirely disappeared--stood a tall, thin man, all in black.", "그러자 정말로 가슴이 철렁 내려앉았어요. 침대 곁에, 마치 안개에서 걸어나온 듯이 — 아니, 차라리 안개가 그의 형체로 바뀐 듯이, 안개가 완전히 사라져버렸으니까요 — 키 크고 야윈, 온통 검은 옷차림의 남자가 서 있었어요."], ["I knew him at once from the description of the others.", "다른 분들의 묘사로 저는 그를 단번에 알아보았죠."], ["The waxen face; the high aquiline nose, on which the light fell in a thin white line; the parted red lips, with the sharp white teeth showing between; and the red eyes that I had seemed to see in the sunset on the windows of St. Mary’s Church at Whitby.", "그 밀랍 같은 얼굴, 빛이 가느다란 흰 선을 그리며 떨어지던 높이 솟은 매부리코, 날카로운 흰 이빨이 사이로 드러나 보이는 벌어진 붉은 입술, 그리고 휘트비의 성 마리아 교회 창문에 비친 석양에서 보았던 듯한 그 붉은 눈."], ["I knew, too, the red scar on his forehead where Jonathan had struck him.", "또한 저는 조너선이 그를 내리쳤던 이마의 붉은 흉터도 알아보았어요."], ["For an instant my heart stood still, and I would have screamed out, only that I was paralysed.", "한순간 제 심장이 멎었고, 비명을 지르고 싶었으나 몸이 마비된 채였어요."], ["In the pause he spoke in a sort of keen, cutting whisper, pointing as he spoke to Jonathan:--", "그 멈춤 속에서 그는 일종의 날카롭고 베는 듯한 속삭임으로 말하며, 말하는 동안 조너선을 가리켰어요."]], [["“‘Silence!", "'조용히 해라!"], ["If you make a sound I shall take him and dash his brains out before your very eyes.’ I was appalled and was too bewildered to do or say anything.", "소리를 내면 이자를 잡아 네 눈앞에서 그 머리통을 박살내버리겠다.' 저는 소름이 끼쳤고, 너무 얼떨떨해서 아무것도 하거나 말할 수가 없었어요."], ["With a mocking smile, he placed one hand upon my shoulder and, holding me tight, bared my throat with the other, saying as he did so, ‘First, a little refreshment to reward my exertions.", "그는 비웃는 미소를 지으며 한 손을 제 어깨에 얹어 저를 꽉 붙들고는, 다른 손으로 제 목을 드러내며 이렇게 말했어요. '먼저, 내 수고에 대한 보답으로 약간의 원기를 보충해야지."], ["You may as well be quiet; it is not the first time, or the second, that your veins have appeased my thirst!’ I was bewildered, and, strangely enough, I did not want to hinder him.", "너는 가만히 있는 게 좋을 거다. 네 핏줄이 내 갈증을 달래준 게 이번이 처음도, 두번째도 아니니까!' 저는 얼떨떨했고, 이상하게도 그를 막고 싶지가 않았어요."], ["I suppose it is a part of the horrible curse that such is, when his touch is on his victim. And oh, my God, my God, pity me!", "짐작건대, 그자의 손길이 희생자에게 닿으면 으레 그렇게 되는 것이, 그 끔찍한 저주의 일부인가 봐요. 그리고 오, 하느님, 하느님, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서!"], ["He placed his reeking lips upon my throat!”", "그는 악취 나는 입술을 제 목에 갖다 댔어요!\""], ["Her husband groaned again.", "그녀의 남편이 다시 신음했다."], ["She clasped his hand harder, and looked at him pityingly, as if he were the injured one, and went on:--", "그녀는 그의 손을 더 꽉 쥐고, 마치 다친 사람이 그인 양 가엾다는 듯 그를 바라보며 말을 이었다."]], [["“I felt my strength fading away, and I was in a half swoon.", "\"저는 기운이 빠져나가는 것을 느꼈고, 반쯤 정신을 잃은 상태였어요."], ["How long this horrible thing lasted I know not; but it seemed that a long time must have passed before he took his foul, awful, sneering mouth away. I saw it drip with the fresh blood!”", "이 끔찍한 일이 얼마나 오래 이어졌는지는 모르겠지만, 그가 그 더럽고 무섭게 비웃는 입을 떼기까지 긴 시간이 흐른 것만 같았어요. 그 입에서 신선한 피가 뚝뚝 떨어지는 것을 저는 보았어요!\""], ["The remembrance seemed for a while to overpower her, and she drooped and would have sunk down but for her husband’s sustaining arm.", "그 기억이 한동안 그녀를 압도하는 듯, 그녀는 고개를 떨구며 그대로 주저앉을 뻔했으나 남편의 부축하는 팔이 그녀를 받쳤다."], ["With a great effort she recovered herself and went on:--", "그녀는 큰 노력으로 정신을 추스르고 말을 이었다."]], [["“Then he spoke to me mockingly, ‘And so you, like the others, would play your brains against mine.", "\"그러더니 그가 저를 비웃으며 말했어요. '그래서 너도 다른 자들처럼 네 머리로 내 머리에 맞서보겠다는 거냐."], ["You would help these men to hunt me and frustrate me in my designs!", "너는 이자들이 나를 사냥하고 내 계획을 좌절시키는 걸 돕겠다는 거지!"], ["You know now, and they know in part already, and will know in full before long, what it is to cross my path.", "이제 너는 알았고, 저들도 이미 일부는 알고 있으며, 머지않아 온전히 알게 될 것이다, 내 길을 가로막는다는 게 어떤 것인지를."], ["They should have kept their energies for use closer to home.", "저들은 제 집 가까이에 쓸 데에나 기운을 아껴두었어야 했어."], ["Whilst they played wits against me--against me who commanded nations, and intrigued for them, and fought for them, hundreds of years before they were born--I was countermining them.", "저들이 나에게 — 저들이 태어나기 수백 년 전에 여러 나라를 호령하고, 그들을 위해 음모를 꾸미고, 그들을 위해 싸웠던 이 나에게 — 머리로 맞서는 동안, 나는 저들의 발밑을 파고들고 있었지."], ["And you, their best beloved one, are now to me, flesh of my flesh; blood of my blood; kin of my kin; my bountiful wine-press for a while; and shall be later on my companion and my helper.", "그리고 저들이 가장 사랑하는 너는, 이제 나에게 내 살 중의 살이요, 내 피 중의 피요, 내 혈족 중의 혈족이며, 한동안은 나의 풍성한 포도즙 틀이요, 나중에는 나의 동반자이자 조력자가 될 것이다."], ["You shall be avenged in turn; for not one of them but shall minister to your needs.", "너는 차례로 복수를 받게 되리라. 저들 가운데 누구도 너의 필요를 섬기지 않을 자가 없을 테니까."], ["But as yet you are to be punished for what you have done.", "그러나 아직은 네가 한 짓에 대해 벌을 받아야지."], ["You have aided in thwarting me; now you shall come to my call.", "너는 나를 방해하는 일을 도왔으니, 이제 너는 내 부름에 와야 한다."], ["When my brain says “Come!”", "내 머리가 너에게 \"오라!\""], ["to you, you shall cross land or sea to do my bidding; and to that end this!’ With that he pulled open his shirt, and with his long sharp nails opened a vein in his breast.", "하고 말하면, 너는 내 명을 따르기 위해 뭍을 건너고 바다를 건너야 하리라. 그리고 그 목적을 위해, 이것을!' 그 말과 함께 그는 셔츠를 풀어헤치고, 길고 날카로운 손톱으로 자기 가슴의 핏줄을 갈랐어요."], ["When the blood began to spurt out, he took my hands in one of his, holding them tight, and with the other seized my neck and pressed my mouth to the wound, so that I must either suffocate or swallow some of the---- Oh my God!", "피가 솟구치기 시작하자, 그는 한 손으로 제 두 손을 붙잡아 꽉 쥐고, 다른 손으로는 제 목을 움켜쥐어 제 입을 그 상처에 짓눌렀어요. 그래서 저는 숨이 막히거나, 아니면 그——을 조금 삼키거나 해야만 했어요. 오 하느님! 하느님!"], ["my God! what have I done?", "제가 무슨 짓을 한 건가요?"], ["What have I done to deserve such a fate, I who have tried to walk in meekness and righteousness all my days. God pity me!", "평생 온순함과 의로움 속에서 걸으려 애써온 제가, 이런 운명을 받아 마땅한 무슨 짓을 했단 말입니까. 하느님, 저를 불쌍히 여기소서!"], ["Look down on a poor soul in worse than mortal peril; and in mercy pity those to whom she is dear!”", "죽음보다 더한 위험에 처한 가엾은 영혼을 굽어살피소서. 그리고 자비로이, 이 여인을 아끼는 이들을 불쌍히 여기소서!\""], ["Then she began to rub her lips as though to cleanse them from pollution.", "그러더니 그녀는 더럽혀진 것을 씻어내려는 듯 자기 입술을 문지르기 시작했다."]], [["As she was telling her terrible story, the eastern sky began to quicken, and everything became more and more clear.", "그녀가 그 끔찍한 이야기를 하는 동안 동쪽 하늘이 밝아오기 시작했고, 모든 것이 점점 더 또렷해졌다."], ["Harker was still and quiet; but over his face, as the awful narrative went on, came a grey look which deepened and deepened in the morning light, till when the first red streak of the coming dawn shot up, the flesh stood darkly out against the whitening hair.", "하커는 가만히, 조용히 있었다. 그러나 그 무서운 이야기가 이어지는 동안 그의 얼굴 위로 잿빛이 번졌고, 아침 빛 속에서 그것은 점점 더 짙어져, 다가오는 새벽의 첫 붉은 줄기가 솟아오를 무렵에는 하얗게 센 머리칼을 배경으로 그 살갗이 거뭇하게 도드라져 보였다."]], [["We have arranged that one of us is to stay within call of the unhappy pair till we can meet together and arrange about taking action.", "우리는 함께 모여 행동에 나설 방도를 의논할 수 있을 때까지, 우리 가운데 한 사람이 이 불행한 부부의 부름이 닿는 곳에 머물기로 정해두었다."]], [["Of this I am sure: the sun rises to-day on no more miserable house in all the great round of its daily course.", "이것만은 분명하다. 오늘 떠오르는 해는, 그 나날의 운행이 그리는 그 광대한 궤도 어디에서도 이보다 더 비참한 집을 비추지 못하리라."]], [["# JONATHAN HARKER’S JOURNAL", "# JONATHAN HARKER’S JOURNAL"]], [["3 October.--As I must do something or go mad, I write this diary.", "「조너선 하커의 일기」 10월 3일. — 무언가 하지 않으면 미쳐버릴 것 같아 이 일기를 적는다."], ["It is now six o’clock, and we are to meet in the study in half an hour and take something to eat; for Dr. Van Helsing and Dr.", "지금은 여섯 시이고, 우리는 삼십 분 뒤에 서재에서 만나 무언가 먹기로 했다. 반 헬싱 박사와 수어드 박사가 먹지 않으면 우리가 최선을 다할 수 없다는 데 의견을 같이했기 때문이다."], ["Seward are agreed that if we do not eat we cannot work our best.", "우리의 최선이, 하느님은 아시겠지만, 오늘 절실히 필요할 것이다."], ["Our best will be, God knows, required to-day.", "나는 틈이 날 때마다 계속 적어야 한다."], ["I must keep writing at every chance, for I dare not stop to think.", "멈추어 생각할 엄두가 나지 않기 때문이다. 크든 작든 모든 것을 적어두어야 한다."], ["All, big and little, must go down; perhaps at the end the little things may teach us most.", "어쩌면 끝에 가서는 사소한 것들이 우리에게 가장 많은 것을 가르쳐줄지도 모른다."], ["The teaching, big or little, could not have landed Mina or me anywhere worse than we are to-day.", "그 가르침이 크든 작든, 미나나 나를 오늘보다 더 나쁜 처지로 데려다 놓을 수는 없었을 것이다."], ["However, we must trust and hope.", "그러나 우리는 믿고 희망을 품어야 한다."], ["Poor Mina told me just now, with the tears running down her dear cheeks, that it is in trouble and trial that our faith is tested--that we must keep on trusting; and that God will aid us up to the end.", "가엾은 미나는 방금, 그 사랑스러운 두 뺨에 눈물을 흘리며, 우리의 믿음이 시험받는 것은 고난과 시련 속에서라고 — 우리는 계속 믿어야 하며, 하느님께서 끝까지 우리를 도우시리라고 말했다. 끝이라니!"], ["The end!", "오 하느님!"], ["oh my God! what end?...", "무슨 끝이란 말인가?……"], ["To work! To work!", "일을 하자! 일을!"]], [["When Dr. Van Helsing and Dr. Seward had come back from seeing poor Renfield, we went gravely into what was to be done.", "반 헬싱 박사와 수어드 박사가 가엾은 렌필드를 살펴보고 돌아왔을 때, 우리는 무엇을 해야 할지를 진지하게 의논했다."], ["First, Dr. Seward told us that when he and Dr. Van Helsing had gone down to the room below they had found Renfield lying on the floor, all in a heap.", "먼저 수어드 박사는, 자신과 반 헬싱 박사가 아래층 방으로 내려갔을 때 렌필드가 바닥에 온통 무더기로 쓰러져 있는 것을 발견했다고 말했다."], ["His face was all bruised and crushed in, and the bones of the neck were broken.", "그의 얼굴은 온통 멍들고 으스러져 함몰되어 있었고, 목뼈가 부러져 있었다."]], [["Dr. Seward asked the attendant who was on duty in the passage if he had heard anything.", "수어드 박사는 복도에서 근무 중이던 당직자에게 무슨 소리를 들었느냐고 물었다."], ["He said that he had been sitting down--he confessed to half dozing--when he heard loud voices in the room, and then Renfield had called out loudly several times, “God! God! God!”", "그는 자기가 앉아 있었는데 — 반쯤 졸고 있었다고 시인했다 — 방 안에서 큰 목소리들이 들렸고, 그 뒤 렌필드가 여러 번 큰 소리로 \"하느님! 하느님! 하느님!\""], ["after that there was a sound of falling, and when he entered the room he found him lying on the floor, face down, just as the doctors had seen him. Van Helsing asked if he had heard “voices”", "하고 외쳤으며, 그러고 나서 무언가 쓰러지는 소리가 났고, 그가 방에 들어가 보니 그가 바닥에 얼굴을 박은 채 쓰러져 있었으니, 바로 박사들이 본 그대로였다고 말했다."], ["or “a voice,” and he said he could not say; that at first it had seemed to him as if there were two, but as there was no one in the room it could have been only one.", "반 헬싱이 \"목소리들\"을 들었느냐 \"목소리\"를 들었느냐고 묻자, 그는 뭐라 말할 수 없다고 했다. 처음에는 둘인 것 같았으나 방에 아무도 없었으니 하나였을 수밖에 없다는 것이다."], ["He could swear to it, if required, that the word “God” was spoken by the patient. Dr.", "필요하다면, \"하느님\"이라는 말은 환자가 한 것이라고 맹세할 수 있다고 했다."], ["Seward said to us, when we were alone, that he did not wish to go into the matter; the question of an inquest had to be considered, and it would never do to put forward the truth, as no one would believe it.", "수어드 박사는 우리끼리 남았을 때, 이 일을 파고들고 싶지 않다고 말했다. 검시(檢屍) 문제를 고려해야 하는데, 진실을 내세우는 것은 결코 안 될 일이니, 아무도 그것을 믿지 않을 것이기 때문이었다."], ["As it was, he thought that on the attendant’s evidence he could give a certificate of death by misadventure in falling from bed.", "사정이 그러하니, 당직자의 증언을 토대로 침대에서 떨어져 생긴 불의의 사고사라는 진단서를 낼 수 있을 것이라고 그는 생각했다."], ["In case the coroner should demand it, there would be a formal inquest, necessarily to the same result.", "만일 검시관이 요구한다면 정식 검시가 있겠으나, 그 결과는 필연적으로 똑같을 것이었다."]], [["When the question began to be discussed as to what should be our next step, the very first thing we decided was that Mina should be in full confidence; that nothing of any sort--no matter how painful--should be kept from her.", "다음 단계를 어떻게 해야 할지 논의가 시작되었을 때, 우리가 가장 먼저 결정한 것은 미나에게 모든 것을 온전히 털어놓아야 한다는 것 — 어떤 종류의 것도, 아무리 고통스럽더라도 그녀에게 숨겨서는 안 된다는 것이었다."], ["She herself agreed as to its wisdom, and it was pitiful to see her so brave and yet so sorrowful, and in such a depth of despair.", "그녀 자신도 그것이 현명한 일임에 동의했다. 그토록 용감하면서도 그토록 슬픔에 잠겨, 그토록 깊은 절망 속에 있는 그녀를 보는 것은 가슴 아픈 일이었다."], ["“There must be no concealment,”", "\"숨기는 일이 있어서는 안 돼요.\""], ["she said, “Alas!", "그녀가 말했다. \"아아!"], ["we have had too much already.", "우리는 이미 너무 많이 숨겨왔어요."], ["And besides there is nothing in all the world that can give me more pain than I have already endured--than I suffer now!", "게다가, 제가 이미 견뎌온 것보다 — 지금 겪고 있는 것보다 더한 고통을 이 세상 무엇도 제게 줄 수는 없어요!"], ["Whatever may happen, it must be of new hope or of new courage to me!”", "무슨 일이 일어나든, 그것은 제게 새로운 희망이나 새로운 용기가 될 수밖에 없을 거예요!\""], ["Van Helsing was looking at her fixedly as she spoke, and said, suddenly but quietly:--", "반 헬싱은 그녀가 말하는 동안 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보다가, 불쑥, 그러나 조용히 말했다."]], [["“But dear Madam Mina, are you not afraid; not for yourself, but for others from yourself, after what has happened?”", "\"하지만 친애하는 미나 부인, 이미 일어난 일을 생각하면, 두렵지 않으시오? 당신 자신을 위해서가 아니라, 당신으로 인해 다른 이들이 입을 해를 두고 말이오.\""], ["Her face grew set in its lines, but her eyes shone with the devotion of a martyr as she answered:--", "그녀의 얼굴은 굳어 그 선이 단단해졌으나, 두 눈은 순교자의 헌신으로 빛났다. 그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“Ah no! for my mind is made up!”", "\"아, 아니에요! 제 마음은 이미 정해졌으니까요!\""]], [["“To what?”", "\"무엇으로 말이오?\""], ["he asked gently, whilst we were all very still; for each in our own way we had a sort of vague idea of what she meant.", "그가 부드럽게 물었고, 그동안 우리는 모두 아주 조용히 있었다. 저마다 나름의 방식으로 그녀가 무슨 뜻으로 하는 말인지 어렴풋이 짐작하고 있었기 때문이다."], ["Her answer came with direct simplicity, as though she were simply stating a fact:--", "그녀의 대답은 그저 사실을 진술하기라도 하듯, 거침없는 단순함으로 나왔다."]], [["“Because if I find in myself--and I shall watch keenly for it--a sign of harm to any that I love, I shall die!”", "\"제 안에서 — 저는 그것을 예리하게 지켜볼 거예요 — 제가 사랑하는 누군가에게 해가 될 조짐을 발견한다면, 저는 죽을 거예요!\""]], [["“You would not kill yourself?”", "\"스스로 목숨을 끊지는 않으시겠지요?\""], ["he asked, hoarsely.", "그가 쉰 목소리로 물었다."]], [["“I would; if there were no friend who loved me, who would save me such a pain, and so desperate an effort!”", "\"끊을 거예요. 저를 사랑하여, 그런 고통과 그토록 절망적인 노력을 덜어줄 벗이 없다면요!\""], ["She looked at him meaningly as she spoke.", "그녀는 그렇게 말하며 의미심장하게 그를 바라보았다."], ["He was sitting down; but now he rose and came close to her and put his hand on her head as he said solemnly:", "그는 앉아 있었으나, 이제 일어서서 그녀에게 가까이 다가가 그녀의 머리에 손을 얹고는 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“My child, there is such an one if it were for your good.", "\"내 아이여, 만일 그것이 당신에게 이롭다면 그리 해줄 이가 여기 있소."], ["For myself I could hold it in my account with God to find such an euthanasia for you, even at this moment if it were best. Nay, were it safe!", "나로 말하자면, 당신을 위해 그런 안락한 죽음을 마련해주는 일을, 그것이 최선이라면 지금 이 순간이라도, 하느님 앞의 내 셈에 떠맡을 수 있을 것이오. 아니, 그것이 안전한 일이기만 하다면!"], ["But my child----”", "그러나 내 아이여——\""], ["For a moment he seemed choked, and a great sob rose in his throat; he gulped it down and went on:--", "잠시 그는 목이 메는 듯했고, 큰 흐느낌이 그의 목구멍에서 솟아올랐다. 그는 그것을 삼켜 누르고는 말을 이었다."]], [["“There are here some who would stand between you and death. You must not die.", "\"여기에는 당신과 죽음 사이를 가로막아 설 이들이 있소. 당신은 죽어서는 아니 되오."], ["You must not die by any hand; but least of all by your own.", "어떤 손으로도 죽어서는 아니 되며, 무엇보다도 당신 자신의 손으로는 결코 아니 되오."], ["Until the other, who has fouled your sweet life, is true dead you must not die; for if he is still with the quick Un-Dead, your death would make you even as he is.", "당신의 그 아름다운 삶을 더럽힌 그 다른 자가 진정으로 죽기 전까지는 당신은 죽어서는 아니 되오. 만일 그가 아직 산 채로 죽지 않은 존재로 남아 있다면, 당신의 죽음은 당신을 그와 같은 존재로 만들어버릴 것이기 때문이오."], ["No, you must live!", "아니, 당신은 살아야 하오!"], ["You must struggle and strive to live, though death would seem a boon unspeakable.", "죽음이 이루 말할 수 없는 은총처럼 보일지라도, 당신은 살기 위해 몸부림치고 애써야 하오."], ["You must fight Death himself, though he come to you in pain or in joy; by the day, or the night; in safety or in peril!", "죽음이 고통 속에서 오든 기쁨 속에서 오든, 낮에 오든 밤에 오든, 안전한 가운데 오든 위험한 가운데 오든, 당신은 죽음 그 자체와 싸워야 하오!"], ["On your living soul I charge you that you do not die--nay, nor think of death--till this great evil be past.”", "나는 당신의 살아 있는 영혼을 두고 명하노니, 이 거대한 악이 지나갈 때까지 당신은 죽지 마시오 — 아니, 죽음을 생각조차 하지 마시오.\""], ["The poor dear grew white as death, and shock and shivered, as I have seen a quicksand shake and shiver at the incoming of the tide.", "가엾은 그녀는 죽은 사람처럼 창백해지더니, 충격에 떨었으니, 마치 밀물이 들어올 때 모래수렁이 흔들리고 떠는 것을 내가 본 것과 같았다."], ["We were all silent; we could do nothing.", "우리는 모두 침묵했고, 아무것도 할 수 없었다."], ["At length she grew more calm and turning to him said, sweetly, but oh!", "마침내 그녀는 좀 더 차분해져 그를 돌아보며, 손을 내밀고는, 다정하게, 그러나 오!"], ["so sorrowfully, as she held out her hand:--", "그토록 슬프게 말했다."]], [["“I promise you, my dear friend, that if God will let me live, I shall strive to do so; till, if it may be in His good time, this horror may have passed away from me.”", "\"약속드릴게요, 친애하는 벗이여, 만일 하느님께서 저를 살게 하신다면, 저는 그리 살려고 애쓰겠어요. 그리하여, 그분의 좋으신 때에, 이 공포가 제게서 사라져버릴 때까지요.\""], ["She was so good and brave that we all felt that our hearts were strengthened to work and endure for her, and we began to discuss what we were to do.", "그녀가 어찌나 착하고 용감했던지, 우리는 모두 그녀를 위해 일하고 견디리라는 다짐으로 우리 마음이 굳세어짐을 느꼈고, 무엇을 해야 할지 의논하기 시작했다."], ["I told her that she was to have all the papers in the safe, and all the papers or diaries and phonographs we might hereafter use; and was to keep the record as she had done before.", "나는 그녀에게, 금고 안의 모든 서류와, 앞으로 우리가 쓰게 될 모든 서류며 일기며 축음기 기록을 맡으라고, 그리고 전처럼 기록을 정리하라고 말했다."], ["She was pleased with the prospect of anything to do--if “pleased” could be used in connection with so grim an interest.", "그녀는 무엇이든 할 일이 있다는 전망에 기뻐했다 — 그토록 음울한 관심사에 \"기뻐했다\"는 말을 쓸 수 있다면 말이다."]], [["As usual Van Helsing had thought ahead of everyone else, and was prepared with an exact ordering of our work.", "언제나처럼 반 헬싱은 누구보다 앞서 생각해두었고, 우리 일의 정확한 순서를 마련해두고 있었다."]], [["“It is perhaps well,”", "\"어쩌면 잘된 일이오,\""], ["he said, “that at our meeting after our visit to Carfax we decided not to do anything with the earth-boxes that lay there.", "그가 말했다. \"카팩스를 다녀온 뒤의 회의에서, 우리가 거기 놓여 있던 흙 상자들에 아무 손도 대지 않기로 결정한 것 말이오."], ["Had we done so, the Count must have guessed our purpose, and would doubtless have taken measures in advance to frustrate such an effort with regard to the others; but now he does not know our intentions.", "만일 그리했더라면 백작은 우리의 의도를 짐작했을 것이고, 나머지 것들에 대한 그런 시도를 좌절시키려고 미리 대책을 세웠을 게 틀림없소. 그러나 지금 그는 우리 의도를 모르고 있소."], ["Nay, more, in all probability, he does not know that such a power exists to us as can sterilise his lairs, so that he cannot use them as of old.", "아니, 그 이상으로, 십중팔구 그는 우리에게 자신의 은신처들을 못 쓰게 만들어, 예전처럼 그것들을 이용할 수 없게 할 그런 힘이 있다는 것조차 모르고 있소."], ["We are now so much further advanced in our knowledge as to their disposition that, when we have examined the house in Piccadilly, we may track the very last of them. To-day, then, is ours; and in it rests our hope.", "우리는 이제 그것들의 배치에 관한 지식에서 훨씬 더 앞서 있으니, 피커딜리의 집을 살펴보고 나면 그 마지막 하나까지 추적할 수 있을 것이오. 그러니 오늘은 우리의 날이며, 거기에 우리의 희망이 달려 있소."], ["The sun that rose on our sorrow this morning guards us in its course.", "오늘 아침 우리의 슬픔 위로 떠오른 해가 그 운행 속에서 우리를 지켜주오."], ["Until it sets to-night, that monster must retain whatever form he now has.", "오늘 밤 해가 질 때까지, 그 괴물은 지금 지닌 형체가 무엇이든 그대로 유지해야만 하오."], ["He is confined within the limitations of his earthly envelope.", "그는 자신의 지상의 외피라는 한계 안에 갇혀 있소."], ["He cannot melt into thin air nor disappear through cracks or chinks or crannies.", "그는 허공으로 녹아들 수도, 틈이나 갈라진 틈새나 작은 구멍으로 사라질 수도 없소."], ["If he go through a doorway, he must open the door like a mortal.", "출입구를 지나려면 그는 인간처럼 문을 열어야만 하오."], ["And so we have this day to hunt out all his lairs and sterilise them.", "그러니 우리는 오늘 그의 모든 은신처를 찾아내어 못 쓰게 만들어야 하오."], ["So we shall, if we have not yet catch him and destroy him, drive him to bay in some place where the catching and the destroying shall be, in time, sure.”", "그리하여, 아직 그를 붙잡아 멸하지는 못하더라도, 붙잡아 멸하는 일이 머지않아 확실해질 어떤 곳으로 그를 궁지에 몰아넣을 것이오.\""], ["Here I started up for I could not contain myself at the thought that the minutes and seconds so preciously laden with Mina’s life and happiness were flying from us, since whilst we talked action was impossible.", "여기서 나는 벌떡 일어났으니, 미나의 생명과 행복이 그토록 소중하게 실린 분초가 우리에게서 날아가고 있다는 생각을 견딜 수 없었기 때문이다. 우리가 이야기를 나누는 동안에는 행동이 불가능했으니 말이다."], ["But Van Helsing held up his hand warningly.", "그러나 반 헬싱은 경고하듯 손을 들었다."], ["“Nay, friend Jonathan,”", "\"아니, 조너선 벗이여,\""], ["he said, “in this, the quickest way home is the longest way, so your proverb say.", "그가 말했다. \"이 일에서는, 집으로 가는 가장 빠른 길이 가장 먼 길이오, 당신네 속담이 그리 말하듯 말이오."], ["We shall all act and act with desperate quick, when the time has come.", "때가 오면 우리는 모두 행동할 것이며, 필사적으로 빠르게 행동할 것이오."], ["But think, in all probable the key of the situation is in that house in Piccadilly.", "그러나 생각해보시오, 십중팔구 이 상황의 열쇠는 피커딜리의 그 집에 있소."], ["The Count may have many houses which he has bought.", "백작은 사들인 집을 여럿 갖고 있을지 모르오."], ["Of them he will have deeds of purchase, keys and other things.", "그것들에 대해 그는 매매 증서와 열쇠와 그 밖의 것들을 갖고 있을 것이오."], ["He will have paper that he write on; he will have his book of cheques.", "그가 글을 쓰는 종이도 있을 것이고, 수표책도 있을 것이오."], ["There are many belongings that he must have somewhere; why not in this place so central, so quiet, where he come and go by the front or the back at all hour, when in the very vast of the traffic there is none to notice.", "그가 어딘가에 두고 있어야 할 소유물이 많이 있소. 어찌하여 이곳, 이토록 중심에 있고, 이토록 조용하며, 앞으로든 뒤로든 어느 시각에나 드나들 수 있는 곳, 바로 그 번잡한 통행 한가운데에 정작 눈여겨보는 이가 아무도 없는 이곳이 아니겠소."], ["We shall go there and search that house; and when we learn what it holds, then we do what our friend Arthur call, in his phrases of hunt ‘stop the earths’ and so we run down our old fox--so?", "우리는 그곳으로 가서 그 집을 수색할 것이오. 그리고 그것이 무엇을 품고 있는지 알아내면, 그때 우리 벗 아서가 사냥의 표현으로 '굴을 막는다'고 부르는 일을 하여, 우리의 늙은 여우를 몰아 잡을 것이오 — 그렇소?"], ["is it not?”", "그렇지 않소?\""]], [["“Then let us come at once,”", "\"그렇다면 당장 갑시다.\""], ["I cried, “we are wasting the precious, precious time!”", "내가 외쳤다. \"우리는 그 귀하고 귀한 시간을 허비하고 있어요!\""], ["The Professor did not move, but simply said:--", "교수는 움직이지 않고 그저 말했다."]], [["“And how are we to get into that house in Piccadilly?”", "\"그럼 우리가 피커딜리의 그 집에 어떻게 들어간단 말이오?\""]], [["“Any way!”", "\"어떻게든!\""], ["I cried.", "내가 외쳤다."], ["“We shall break in if need be.”", "\"필요하다면 부수고 들어가야지요.\""]], [["“And your police; where will they be, and what will they say?”", "\"그럼 당신네 경찰은요? 그들은 어디에 있을 것이며, 무어라 하겠소?\""]], [["I was staggered; but I knew that if he wished to delay he had a good reason for it.", "나는 말문이 막혔다. 그러나 그가 지체하기를 바란다면 거기에는 그럴 만한 충분한 이유가 있음을 알았다."], ["So I said, as quietly as I could:--", "그래서 나는 되도록 차분하게 말했다."]], [["“Don’t wait more than need be; you know, I am sure, what torture I am in.”", "\"필요 이상으로 기다리지는 마세요. 제가 어떤 고문 속에 있는지 잘 아시잖아요.\""]], [["“Ah, my child, that I do; and indeed there is no wish of me to add to your anguish.", "\"아, 내 아이여, 알다마다. 정녕 당신의 고뇌에 무언가를 더하고 싶은 마음은 없소."], ["But just think, what can we do, until all the world be at movement.", "그러나 생각해보시오, 온 세상이 움직이기 전에 우리가 무엇을 할 수 있겠소."], ["Then will come our time.", "그때라야 우리의 때가 올 것이오."], ["I have thought and thought, and it seems to me that the simplest way is the best of all.", "나는 생각하고 또 생각했는데, 가장 단순한 방법이 무엇보다 가장 좋은 방법인 것 같소. 자, 우리는 그 집에 들어가고 싶으나 열쇠가 없소."], ["Now we wish to get into the house, but we have no key; is it not so?” I nodded.", "그렇지 않소?\" 나는 고개를 끄덕였다."]], [["“Now suppose that you were, in truth, the owner of that house, and could not still get in; and think there was to you no conscience of the housebreaker, what would you do?”", "\"이제 당신이 정말로 그 집의 주인인데도 여전히 들어갈 수 없다고 가정해봅시다. 그리고 당신에게 가택 침입자의 양심 같은 것이 전혀 없다고 생각해보시오. 그러면 어떻게 하겠소?\""]], [["“I should get a respectable locksmith, and set him to work to pick the lock for me.”", "\"점잖은 자물쇠공을 데려와, 저 대신 자물쇠를 따도록 시키겠지요.\""]], [["“And your police, they would interfere, would they not?”", "\"그럼 당신네 경찰이 끼어들겠소, 그렇지 않겠소?\""]], [["“Oh, no! not if they knew the man was properly employed.”", "\"오, 아니요! 그 사람이 제대로 고용된 줄 안다면 안 그럴 거예요.\""]], [["“Then,”", "\"그렇다면,\""], ["he looked at me as keenly as he spoke, “all that is in doubt is the conscience of the employer, and the belief of your policemen as to whether or no that employer has a good conscience or a bad one.", "그는 말하면서 나를 예리하게 바라보았다. \"의문이 되는 것은 오직 고용한 사람의 양심과, 그 고용한 사람이 양심이 바른지 그른지에 대한 당신네 경찰의 판단뿐이오."], ["Your police must indeed be zealous men and clever--oh, so clever!--in reading the heart, that they trouble themselves in such matter.", "당신네 경찰이 그런 일에 신경을 쓰려면 정녕 마음을 읽어내는 데 열성적이고 영리한 사람들이어야 — 오, 어찌나 영리해야 — 하겠소!"], ["No, no, my friend Jonathan, you go take the lock off a hundred empty house in this your London, or of any city in the world; and if you do it as such things are rightly done, and at the time such things are rightly done, no one will interfere.", "아니, 아니오, 내 벗 조너선, 당신이 이 당신네 런던에서, 아니 세상 어느 도시에서든, 빈집 백 채의 자물쇠를 떼어내 보시오. 그런 일이 제대로 행해지는 방식대로, 그런 일이 제대로 행해지는 시각에 한다면, 아무도 끼어들지 않을 것이오."], ["I have read of a gentleman who owned a so fine house in London, and when he went for months of summer to Switzerland and lock up his house, some burglar came and broke window at back and got in.", "나는 런던에 아주 멋진 집을 가진 어떤 신사 이야기를 읽은 적이 있소. 그가 여름 몇 달 동안 스위스로 가면서 집을 잠가두었더니, 어떤 도둑이 와서 뒤쪽 창문을 깨고 들어갔소."], ["Then he went and made open the shutters in front and walk out and in through the door, before the very eyes of the police.", "그러더니 앞쪽 덧문을 열어젖히고는, 바로 경찰의 눈앞에서 문으로 드나들었소."], ["Then he have an auction in that house, and advertise it, and put up big notice; and when the day come he sell off by a great auctioneer all the goods of that other man who own them.", "그러고는 그 집에서 경매를 열어 광고를 내고 큰 게시문을 붙였소. 그리고 그날이 오자, 그 집을 가진 다른 사람의 물건들을 유명한 경매인을 시켜 죄다 팔아치웠소."], ["Then he go to a builder, and he sell him that house, making an agreement that he pull it down and take all away within a certain time.", "그런 다음 건축업자에게 가서, 일정 기간 안에 집을 헐고 모두 치워간다는 계약을 맺어 그 집을 팔았소."], ["And your police and other authority help him all they can.", "그리고 당신네 경찰과 그 밖의 당국이 할 수 있는 한 그를 도왔소."], ["And when that owner come back from his holiday in Switzerland he find only an empty hole where his house had been.", "그리고 그 주인이 스위스 휴가에서 돌아와 보니, 자기 집이 있던 자리에 빈 구덩이만 남아 있더라는 것이오."], ["This was all done en règle; and in our work we shall be en règle too.", "이 모든 일이 격식대로 행해졌소. 우리의 일에서도 우리는 격식대로 할 것이오."], ["We shall not go so early that the policemen who have then little to think of, shall deem it strange; but we shall go after ten o’clock, when there are many about, and such things would be done were we indeed owners of the house.”", "우리는 그때라야 별로 생각할 거리가 없는 경찰들이 이상하게 여길 만큼 너무 이르게 가지는 않을 것이오. 우리는 열 시가 지난 뒤에, 오가는 사람이 많을 때 갈 것이니, 우리가 정말로 그 집의 주인이라면 그런 일들이 행해질 그 시각에 말이오.\" 격식: 원문은 '앙 레글(en règle)', 정식 절차에 맞게라는 뜻의 프랑스어."]], [["I could not but see how right he was and the terrible despair of Mina’s face became relaxed a thought; there was hope in such good counsel.", "나는 그가 옳다는 것을 인정하지 않을 수 없었고, 미나의 얼굴에 어린 끔찍한 절망이 조금은 누그러졌다. 그런 좋은 조언 속에 희망이 있었던 것이다."], ["Van Helsing went on:--", "반 헬싱은 말을 이었다."]], [["“When once within that house we may find more clues; at any rate some of us can remain there whilst the rest find the other places where there be more earth-boxes--at Bermondsey and Mile End.”", "\"일단 그 집 안에 들어가면 우리는 더 많은 단서를 찾을지 모르오. 어쨌든 우리 가운데 몇은 거기 남고, 나머지는 흙 상자가 더 있는 다른 곳들을 — 버몬지와 마일엔드에서 — 찾으면 되오.\""]], [["Lord Godalming stood up.", "고댈밍 경이 일어섰다."], ["“I can be of some use here,” he said.", "\"여기서 제가 좀 쓸모가 있을 것 같습니다.\""], ["“I shall wire to my people to have horses and carriages where they will be most convenient.”", "그가 말했다. \"제 사람들에게 전보를 쳐서 가장 편리한 곳에 말과 마차를 대기시키도록 하겠습니다.\""]], [["“Look here, old fellow,”", "\"이보게, 친구,\""], ["said Morris, “it is a capital idea to have all ready in case we want to go horsebacking; but don’t you think that one of your snappy carriages with its heraldic adornments in a byway of Walworth or Mile End would attract too much attention for our purposes?", "모리스가 말했다. \"말을 타고 가야 할 경우에 대비해 모든 걸 갖추어두는 건 훌륭한 생각이야. 하지만 자네의 그 번쩍이는 마차에, 문장(紋章) 장식까지 단 것이 월워스나 마일엔드의 뒷골목에 나타난다면, 우리 목적에 비추어 너무 눈길을 끌지 않겠나?"], ["It seems to me that we ought to take cabs when we go south or east; and even leave them somewhere near the neighbourhood we are going to.”", "내 생각엔 우리가 남쪽이나 동쪽으로 갈 때는 삯마차를 타야 할 것 같네. 그리고 가려는 동네 근처 어딘가에 그것마저 두고 가는 게 좋겠어.\""]], [["“Friend Quincey is right!”", "\"퀸시 벗이 옳소!\""], ["said the Professor.", "교수가 말했다."], ["“His head is what you call in plane with the horizon.", "\"그의 머리는, 당신네 말로 수평을 잘 잡는다고 하는 그것이오."], ["It is a difficult thing that we go to do, and we do not want no peoples to watch us if so it may.”", "우리가 하려는 일은 어려운 일이니, 가능하다면 누구도 우리를 지켜보는 일이 없기를 바라오.\""]], [["Mina took a growing interest in everything and I was rejoiced to see that the exigency of affairs was helping her to forget for a time the terrible experience of the night.", "미나는 모든 일에 점점 더 관심을 보였고, 사태의 절박함이 그녀로 하여금 잠시나마 그날 밤의 끔찍한 경험을 잊는 데 도움이 되는 것을 보고 나는 기뻤다."], ["She was very, very pale--almost ghastly, and so thin that her lips were drawn away, showing her teeth in somewhat of prominence.", "그녀는 무척이나, 무척이나 창백했고 — 거의 섬뜩할 지경이었다 — 너무 야위어 입술이 당겨져 이가 다소 두드러져 보였다."], ["I did not mention this last, lest it should give her needless pain; but it made my blood run cold in my veins to think of what had occurred with poor Lucy when the Count had sucked her blood.", "이 마지막 점은 그녀에게 공연한 고통을 줄까 봐 입에 담지 않았다. 그러나 백작이 가엾은 루시의 피를 빨았을 때 그녀에게 일어난 일을 생각하니 내 핏줄의 피가 차갑게 식는 듯했다."], ["As yet there was no sign of the teeth growing sharper; but the time as yet was short, and there was time for fear.", "아직은 이가 더 날카로워지는 조짐은 없었다. 그러나 아직 시간이 얼마 지나지 않았으니, 두려워할 시간은 남아 있었다."]], [["When we came to the discussion of the sequence of our efforts and of the disposition of our forces, there were new sources of doubt.", "우리의 노력의 순서와 우리 병력의 배치를 논의하게 되었을 때, 새로운 의혹의 원천들이 생겨났다."], ["It was finally agreed that before starting for Piccadilly we should destroy the Count’s lair close at hand.", "마침내, 피커딜리로 출발하기 전에 가까이에 있는 백작의 은신처를 파괴하기로 합의했다."], ["In case he should find it out too soon, we should thus be still ahead of him in our work of destruction; and his presence in his purely material shape, and at his weakest, might give us some new clue.", "만일 그가 너무 일찍 그것을 알아챈다 해도, 그리하면 우리는 파괴 작업에서 여전히 그보다 앞서 있을 것이며, 그가 순전히 물질적인 형체로, 가장 약한 상태로 나타난다면 우리에게 새로운 단서를 줄지도 모르는 일이었다."]], [["As to the disposal of forces, it was suggested by the Professor that, after our visit to Carfax, we should all enter the house in Piccadilly; that the two doctors and I should remain there, whilst Lord Godalming and Quincey found the lairs at Walworth and Mile End and destroyed them.", "병력의 배치에 관해서는, 카팩스를 다녀온 뒤 우리 모두가 피커딜리의 집에 들어가되, 두 박사와 내가 거기 남고, 고댈밍 경과 퀸시는 월워스와 마일엔드의 은신처들을 찾아 파괴하자고 교수가 제안했다."], ["It was possible, if not likely, the Professor urged, that the Count might appear in Piccadilly during the day, and that if so we might be able to cope with him then and there.", "가능성이 높지는 않더라도, 백작이 낮 동안 피커딜리에 나타날 수도 있으며, 그렇다면 우리가 그 자리에서 그를 상대할 수 있을지 모른다고 교수는 역설했다."], ["At any rate, we might be able to follow him in force.", "어쨌든 우리는 병력을 갖추어 그를 뒤쫓을 수 있을 것이었다."], ["To this plan I strenuously objected, and so far as my going was concerned, for I said that I intended to stay and protect Mina, I thought that my mind was made up on the subject; but Mina would not listen to my objection.", "이 계획에 나는 극력 반대했으니, 적어도 내가 가는 문제에 관해서는 그러했다. 나는 남아서 미나를 지킬 작정이라고, 이 문제에 대해서는 마음을 굳혔다고 생각했기 때문이다. 그러나 미나는 내 반대를 들으려 하지 않았다."], ["She said that there might be some law matter in which I could be useful; that amongst the Count’s papers might be some clue which I could understand out of my experience in Transylvania; and that, as it was, all the strength we could muster was required to cope with the Count’s extraordinary power.", "그녀는 내가 쓸모 있을 어떤 법률 문제가 있을지도 모른다고, 백작의 서류 가운데 내가 트란실바니아에서 겪은 경험으로 이해할 수 있는 단서가 있을지도 모른다고, 그리고 사정이 이러하니 백작의 비범한 힘에 맞서려면 우리가 끌어모을 수 있는 모든 힘이 필요하다고 말했다."], ["I had to give in, for Mina’s resolution was fixed; she said that it was the last hope for her that we should all work together.", "나는 굴복할 수밖에 없었으니, 미나의 결심이 확고했기 때문이다. 그녀는 우리 모두가 함께 일하는 것이 자기에게는 마지막 희망이라고 말했다. \"저로 말하자면,\""], ["“As for me,”", "그녀가 말했다."], ["she said, “I have no fear.", "\"저는 두려움이 없어요."], ["Things have been as bad as they can be; and whatever may happen must have in it some element of hope or comfort.", "일은 더 나쁠 수 없을 만큼 나빠졌고, 무슨 일이 일어나든 거기에는 어떤 희망이나 위안의 요소가 있을 수밖에 없어요."], ["Go, my husband!", "가세요, 여보!"], ["God can, if He wishes it, guard me as well alone as with any one present.”", "하느님께서 원하신다면, 제가 혼자 있든 누군가 곁에 있든 똑같이 저를 지켜주실 수 있어요.\""], ["So I started up crying out: “Then in God’s name let us come at once, for we are losing time.", "그래서 나는 벌떡 일어나 외쳤다. \"그렇다면 하느님의 이름으로 당장 갑시다. 우리는 시간을 잃고 있어요."], ["The Count may come to Piccadilly earlier than we think.”", "백작이 우리 생각보다 더 일찍 피커딜리에 올지도 몰라요.\""]], [["“Not so!” said Van Helsing, holding up his hand.", "\"그렇지 않소!\" 반 헬싱이 손을 들며 말했다."]], [["“But why?”", "\"하지만 왜요?\""], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“Do you forget,”", "\"잊었소,\""], ["he said, with actually a smile, “that last night he banqueted heavily, and will sleep late?”", "그가 정말로 미소까지 지으며 말했다. \"어젯밤 그가 잔뜩 포식했으니, 늦게까지 잠들어 있으리라는 것을 말이오?\""]], [["Did I forget!", "내가 잊었다니!"], ["shall I ever--can I ever! Can any of us ever forget that terrible scene!", "내가 잊을 수나 있을까 — 잊을 수가 있을까! 우리 가운데 누구라도 그 끔찍한 광경을 잊을 수 있을까!"], ["Mina struggled hard to keep her brave countenance; but the pain overmastered her and she put her hands before her face, and shuddered whilst she moaned.", "미나는 용감한 표정을 유지하려고 안간힘을 썼다. 그러나 고통이 그녀를 압도해, 그녀는 두 손으로 얼굴을 가리고 신음하며 몸서리쳤다."], ["Van Helsing had not intended to recall her frightful experience.", "반 헬싱은 그녀의 무서운 경험을 떠올리게 할 의도가 없었다."], ["He had simply lost sight of her and her part in the affair in his intellectual effort.", "그는 그저 지적인 노력에 몰두하느라 그녀와 그 일에서 그녀가 한 역할을 잊었던 것이다."], ["When it struck him what he said, he was horrified at his thoughtlessness and tried to comfort her.", "자기가 한 말이 무엇인지 깨닫자, 그는 자신의 경솔함에 소스라치며 그녀를 위로하려 했다. \"오, 미나 부인,\""], ["“Oh, Madam Mina,”", "그가 말했다."], ["he said, “dear, dear Madam Mina, alas!", "\"친애하는, 친애하는 미나 부인, 아아!"], ["that I of all who so reverence you should have said anything so forgetful.", "당신을 그토록 존경하는 내가 하필 그토록 분별없는 말을 하다니."], ["These stupid old lips of mine and this stupid old head do not deserve so; but you will forget it, will you not?”", "이 어리석은 늙은 입술과 이 어리석은 늙은 머리는 그럴 자격이 없소. 그러나 당신은 그것을 잊어주시겠지요, 그러시겠지요?\""], ["He bent low beside her as he spoke; she took his hand, and looking at him through her tears, said hoarsely:--", "그는 말하면서 그녀 곁에 낮게 몸을 굽혔다. 그녀는 그의 손을 잡고, 눈물 너머로 그를 바라보며 쉰 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“No, I shall not forget, for it is well that I remember; and with it I have so much in memory of you that is sweet, that I take it all together.", "\"아니에요, 저는 잊지 않을 거예요. 기억하는 것이 좋으니까요. 게다가 그와 함께 제 기억 속에는 당신에 관한 다정한 것이 너무도 많으니, 저는 그 모든 것을 한데 모아 받아들이겠어요."], ["Now, you must all be going soon.", "자, 이제 여러분 모두 곧 떠나셔야 해요."], ["Breakfast is ready, and we must all eat that we may be strong.”", "아침식사가 준비됐으니, 우리 모두 먹어서 기운을 차려야 해요.\""]], [["Breakfast was a strange meal to us all.", "아침식사는 우리 모두에게 묘한 끼니였다."], ["We tried to be cheerful and encourage each other, and Mina was the brightest and most cheerful of us.", "우리는 명랑하려 애쓰며 서로를 북돋우려 했고, 미나가 우리 가운데 가장 밝고 가장 명랑했다."], ["When it was over, Van Helsing stood up and said:--", "식사가 끝나자 반 헬싱이 일어서서 말했다."]], [["“Now, my dear friends, we go forth to our terrible enterprise.", "\"자, 친애하는 벗들이여, 우리는 우리의 끔찍한 과업을 향해 나아가오."], ["Are we all armed, as we were on that night when first we visited our enemy’s lair; armed against ghostly as well as carnal attack?”", "우리는 모두 무장했소? 처음 우리 적의 은신처를 찾아갔던 그날 밤처럼, 육체적 공격뿐 아니라 유령 같은 공격에도 대비해 무장했소?\" 우리는 모두 그렇다고 확언했다."], ["We all assured him.", "\"그렇다면 됐소."], ["“Then it is well. Now, Madam Mina, you are in any case quite safe here until the sunset; and before then we shall return--if---- We shall return!", "자, 미나 부인, 어떤 경우에도 당신은 해가 질 때까지 여기서 완전히 안전하오. 그리고 그 전에 우리는 돌아올 것이오 — 만일—— 우리는 돌아올 것이오!"], ["But before we go let me see you armed against personal attack.", "그러나 떠나기 전에 당신이 개인적인 공격에 대비해 무장하는 것을 보게 해주시오."], ["I have myself, since you came down, prepared your chamber by the placing of things of which we know, so that He may not enter.", "당신이 내려온 뒤로, 나는 그가 들어오지 못하도록 우리가 아는 그 물건들을 놓아 직접 당신의 방을 준비해두었소."], ["Now let me guard yourself.", "이제 당신 자신을 지키게 해주시오."], ["On your forehead I touch this piece of Sacred Wafer in the name of the Father, the Son, and----”", "당신의 이마에 이 거룩한 성체(聖體)의 빵 한 조각을 대겠소, 성부와 성자와——의 이름으로.\""]], [["There was a fearful scream which almost froze our hearts to hear.", "들을수록 우리 가슴을 얼어붙게 하는 무서운 비명이 터졌다."], ["As he had placed the Wafer on Mina’s forehead, it had seared it--had burned into the flesh as though it had been a piece of white-hot metal.", "그가 그 빵을 미나의 이마에 댔을 때, 그것이 이마를 지져버린 것이다 — 마치 새하얗게 달군 금속 조각이기라도 한 듯 살 속으로 파고들어 태워버린 것이다."], ["My poor darling’s brain had told her the significance of the fact as quickly as her nerves received the pain of it; and the two so overwhelmed her that her overwrought nature had its voice in that dreadful scream.", "가엾은 내 사랑의 머리는, 신경이 그 고통을 받아들이는 것과 똑같이 빠르게 그 사실의 의미를 그녀에게 일러주었다. 그 둘이 어찌나 그녀를 압도했던지, 극도로 시달린 그녀의 본성이 그 무시무시한 비명으로 목소리를 낸 것이다."], ["But the words to her thought came quickly; the echo of the scream had not ceased to ring on the air when there came the reaction, and she sank on her knees on the floor in an agony of abasement.", "그러나 그녀의 생각에 들어맞는 말은 재빨리 나왔다. 비명의 메아리가 채 공중에서 울려 사라지기도 전에 반응이 일어나, 그녀는 자기를 낮추는 고뇌 속에서 바닥에 무릎을 꿇고 주저앉았다."], ["Pulling her beautiful hair over her face, as the leper of old his mantle, she wailed out:--", "옛날의 나병환자가 자기 망토를 끌어당기듯, 자신의 아름다운 머리칼을 얼굴 위로 끌어당기며 그녀는 통곡했다."]], [["“Unclean! Unclean!", "\"부정해요! 부정해요!"], ["Even the Almighty shuns my polluted flesh!", "전능하신 분조차 제 더럽혀진 살을 피하시는군요!"], ["I must bear this mark of shame upon my forehead until the Judgment Day.”", "저는 심판의 날까지 제 이마에 이 수치의 표를 지니고 살아야 해요.\""], ["They all paused.", "모두가 멈칫했다."], ["I had thrown myself beside her in an agony of helpless grief, and putting my arms around held her tight.", "나는 어찌할 수 없는 비탄의 고뇌 속에서 그녀 곁에 몸을 던져, 두 팔로 감싸 그녀를 꼭 끌어안았다."], ["For a few minutes our sorrowful hearts beat together, whilst the friends around us turned away their eyes that ran tears silently.", "몇 분 동안 우리의 슬픈 심장이 함께 고동쳤고, 그동안 우리를 둘러싼 벗들은 소리 없이 눈물을 흘리며 눈길을 돌렸다."], ["Then Van Helsing turned and said gravely; so gravely that I could not help feeling that he was in some way inspired, and was stating things outside himself:--", "그때 반 헬싱이 돌아서서 엄숙하게 말했다. 어찌나 엄숙했던지, 나는 그가 어떤 식으로든 영감을 받아, 자기 자신을 넘어선 것들을 진술하고 있다고 느끼지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“It may be that you may have to bear that mark till God himself see fit, as He most surely shall, on the Judgment Day, to redress all wrongs of the earth and of His children that He has placed thereon.", "\"하느님께서 친히, 심판의 날에 — 반드시 그러하시겠지만 — 이 땅과, 그분이 그 위에 두신 그분의 자녀들의 온갖 잘못을 바로잡으시기에 합당하다고 보실 때까지, 당신은 그 표를 지녀야 할지도 모르오."], ["And oh, Madam Mina, my dear, my dear, may we who love you be there to see, when that red scar, the sign of God’s knowledge of what has been, shall pass away, and leave your forehead as pure as the heart we know.", "그러나 오, 미나 부인, 내 사랑하는, 내 사랑하는 이여, 그 붉은 흉터, 무슨 일이 있었는지 하느님께서 아신다는 그 표가 사라져, 당신의 이마를 우리가 아는 그 마음만큼이나 순결하게 남길 그때를, 당신을 사랑하는 우리가 거기서 지켜볼 수 있기를."], ["For so surely as we live, that scar shall pass away when God sees right to lift the burden that is hard upon us.", "우리가 살아 있는 것이 분명한 만큼이나 분명히, 하느님께서 우리를 짓누르는 이 무거운 짐을 들어올리시기에 옳다고 보실 때 그 흉터는 사라질 것이오."], ["Till then we bear our Cross, as His Son did in obedience to His Will.", "그때까지 우리는 우리의 십자가를 지오, 그분의 아드님께서 그분의 뜻에 순종하여 그리하셨듯이 말이오."], ["It may be that we are chosen instruments of His good pleasure, and that we ascend to His bidding as that other through stripes and shame; through tears and blood; through doubts and fears, and all that makes the difference between God and man.”", "어쩌면 우리는 그분의 기쁨에 쓰일, 선택된 도구일지 모르오. 그리고 그 다른 분이 채찍질과 수치를 통해, 눈물과 피를 통해, 의심과 두려움과, 하느님과 인간을 갈라놓는 그 모든 것을 통해 그러하셨듯, 우리도 그분의 분부에 따라 올라가는 것일지 모르오.\""]], [["There was hope in his words, and comfort; and they made for resignation.", "그의 말 속에는 희망이 있었고, 위안이 있었다. 그리고 그것들은 체념으로 이끌었다."], ["Mina and I both felt so, and simultaneously we each took one of the old man’s hands and bent over and kissed it.", "미나와 나는 둘 다 그렇게 느꼈고, 동시에 우리는 저마다 노인의 한 손을 잡고 몸을 굽혀 그 손에 입을 맞추었다."], ["Then without a word we all knelt down together, and, all holding hands, swore to be true to each other.", "그런 다음 우리는 한마디 말도 없이 모두 함께 무릎을 꿇고, 모두 손을 맞잡은 채 서로에게 충실하기를 맹세했다."], ["We men pledged ourselves to raise the veil of sorrow from the head of her whom, each in his own way, we loved; and we prayed for help and guidance in the terrible task which lay before us.", "우리 남자들은, 저마다 나름의 방식으로 사랑하는 그녀의 머리에서 슬픔의 너울을 걷어주기로 다짐했다. 그리고 우리 앞에 놓인 그 끔찍한 과업에 도움과 인도를 주십사 기도했다."]], [["It was then time to start.", "이제 출발할 때가 되었다."], ["So I said farewell to Mina, a parting which neither of us shall forget to our dying day; and we set out.", "그래서 나는 미나에게 작별을 고했으니, 우리 둘 다 죽는 날까지 잊지 못할 이별이었다. 그리고 우리는 길을 나섰다."]], [["To one thing I have made up my mind: if we find out that Mina must be a vampire in the end, then she shall not go into that unknown and terrible land alone.", "한 가지 나는 마음을 굳혔다. 만일 결국 미나가 흡혈귀가 되어야 한다는 것을 우리가 알게 된다면, 그녀가 그 알지 못할 끔찍한 땅으로 혼자 가게 두지는 않으리라는 것을."], ["I suppose it is thus that in old times one vampire meant many; just as their hideous bodies could only rest in sacred earth, so the holiest love was the recruiting sergeant for their ghastly ranks.", "옛날에 흡혈귀 하나가 곧 여럿을 뜻했던 것도 바로 이런 까닭이리라 짐작한다. 그들의 흉측한 육신이 오직 거룩한 흙 속에서만 쉴 수 있었듯이, 가장 거룩한 사랑이 그들의 소름 끼치는 대열을 채우는 모병관(募兵官) 노릇을 했던 것이다."]], [["We entered Carfax without trouble and found all things the same as on the first occasion.", "우리는 별 어려움 없이 카팩스에 들어섰고, 모든 것이 처음 왔을 때와 똑같음을 알았다."], ["It was hard to believe that amongst so prosaic surroundings of neglect and dust and decay there was any ground for such fear as already we knew.", "방치와 먼지와 부패로 그토록 무미건조한 이 환경 속에, 우리가 이미 아는 그런 공포의 근거가 있다는 것을 믿기란 어려웠다."], ["Had not our minds been made up, and had there not been terrible memories to spur us on, we could hardly have proceeded with our task.", "우리 마음이 굳어 있지 않았더라면, 또 우리를 채찍질하는 끔찍한 기억들이 없었더라면, 우리는 도저히 우리의 과업을 진행할 수 없었을 것이다."], ["We found no papers, or any sign of use in the house; and in the old chapel the great boxes looked just as we had seen them last. Dr.", "집 안에서 우리는 어떤 서류도, 사용된 흔적도 찾지 못했다. 그리고 그 낡은 예배당에서 큰 상자들은 우리가 마지막으로 보았을 때 그대로의 모습이었다."], ["Van Helsing said to us solemnly as we stood before them:--", "반 헬싱 박사가 그것들 앞에 선 우리에게 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“And now, my friends, we have a duty here to do.", "\"자, 벗들이여, 우리는 여기서 해야 할 의무가 있소."], ["We must sterilise this earth, so sacred of holy memories, that he has brought from a far distant land for such fell use.", "우리는 이 흙을, 거룩한 기억들로 그토록 신성한 이 흙, 그가 그토록 흉악한 용도로 쓰려고 먼 땅에서 가져온 이 흙을 못 쓰게 만들어야 하오."], ["He has chosen this earth because it has been holy. Thus we defeat him with his own weapon, for we make it more holy still.", "그는 이 흙이 거룩했기에 그것을 골랐소. 그리하여 우리는 그의 무기로 그를 물리치오, 우리가 그것을 한층 더 거룩하게 만드는 것이니 말이오."], ["It was sanctified to such use of man, now we sanctify it to God.”", "그것은 인간의 그런 용도로 봉헌되었으나, 이제 우리는 그것을 하느님께 봉헌하오.\""], ["As he spoke he took from his bag a screwdriver and a wrench, and very soon the top of one of the cases was thrown open.", "그는 말하면서 가방에서 나사돌리개와 렌치를 꺼냈고, 곧 상자들 가운데 하나의 뚜껑이 열렸다."], ["The earth smelled musty and close; but we did not somehow seem to mind, for our attention was concentrated on the Professor.", "흙에서는 곰팡내 나고 답답한 냄새가 났다. 그러나 우리는 어쩐지 개의치 않는 듯했으니, 우리의 주의가 교수에게 집중되어 있었기 때문이다."], ["Taking from his box a piece of the Sacred Wafer he laid it reverently on the earth, and then shutting down the lid began to screw it home, we aiding him as he worked.", "그는 상자에서 거룩한 성체의 빵 한 조각을 꺼내 경건하게 흙 위에 놓았고, 그런 다음 뚜껑을 덮어 나사로 단단히 조이기 시작했으며, 우리는 그가 일하는 동안 그를 도왔다."]], [["One by one we treated in the same way each of the great boxes, and left them as we had found them to all appearance; but in each was a portion of the Host.", "하나하나, 우리는 큰 상자들을 모두 똑같은 방식으로 처리했고, 겉보기에는 우리가 발견했던 그대로 그것들을 남겨두었다. 그러나 그 안에는 저마다 성체의 한 조각이 들어 있었다."]], [["When we closed the door behind us, the Professor said solemnly:--", "우리가 등 뒤로 문을 닫았을 때, 교수가 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“So much is already done.", "\"이만큼은 이미 끝냈소."], ["If it may be that with all the others we can be so successful, then the sunset of this evening may shine on Madam Mina’s forehead all white as ivory and with no stain!”", "만일 나머지 모든 것에서도 우리가 이토록 성공할 수 있다면, 오늘 저녁의 해넘이는 상아처럼 새하얗고 한 점 얼룩도 없는 미나 부인의 이마를 비출지도 모르오!\""]], [["As we passed across the lawn on our way to the station to catch our train we could see the front of the asylum.", "기차를 잡으려 역으로 가는 길에 잔디밭을 가로질러 갈 때, 우리는 정신병원의 정면을 볼 수 있었다."], ["I looked eagerly, and in the window of my own room saw Mina.", "나는 간절히 바라보았고, 내 방 창문에서 미나를 보았다."], ["I waved my hand to her, and nodded to tell that our work there was successfully accomplished.", "나는 그녀에게 손을 흔들었고, 그곳에서의 우리 일이 성공적으로 마무리되었음을 알리려 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["She nodded in reply to show that she understood.", "그녀는 알아들었다는 표시로 답하여 고개를 끄덕였다."], ["The last I saw, she was waving her hand in farewell.", "마지막으로 본 그녀는 작별의 손을 흔들고 있었다."], ["It was with a heavy heart that we sought the station and just caught the train, which was steaming in as we reached the platform.", "무거운 마음으로 우리는 역을 찾았고, 우리가 승강장에 닿았을 때 막 김을 뿜으며 들어오던 기차를 간신히 잡아탔다."]], [["I have written this in the train.", "이 글은 기차 안에서 적었다."]], [["Piccadilly, 12:30 o’clock.--Just before we reached Fenchurch Street Lord Godalming said to me:--", "피커딜리, 12시 30분. — 우리가 펜처치 가에 닿기 직전에 고댈밍 경이 내게 말했다."]], [["“Quincey and I will find a locksmith.", "\"퀸시와 나는 자물쇠공을 찾아오겠소."], ["You had better not come with us in case there should be any difficulty; for under the circumstances it wouldn’t seem so bad for us to break into an empty house.", "당신은 우리와 함께 가지 않는 게 좋겠소. 혹시 어려움이 생길 경우에 대비해서 말이오."], ["But you are a solicitor and the Incorporated Law Society might tell you that you should have known better.”", "사정이 이러하니 우리가 빈집에 부수고 들어가는 것은 그리 나빠 보이지 않겠지만, 당신은 사무 변호사니, 법조협회가 당신더러 좀 더 분별이 있었어야 했다고 할지도 모르오.\""], ["I demurred as to my not sharing any danger even of odium, but he went on: “Besides, it will attract less attention if there are not too many of us.", "나는 오명이라도 무릅쓰는 위험을 함께 나누지 못한다는 데 이의를 제기했으나, 그는 말을 이었다. \"게다가 우리 수가 너무 많지 않으면 눈길을 덜 끌 것이오."], ["My title will make it all right with the locksmith, and with any policeman that may come along.", "내 작위(爵位)가 자물쇠공에게도, 마침 지나가는 어떤 경찰에게도 모든 걸 무난하게 해줄 것이오."], ["You had better go with Jack and the Professor and stay in the Green Park, somewhere in sight of the house; and when you see the door opened and the smith has gone away, do you all come across.", "당신은 잭과 교수와 함께 가서 그린파크에, 그 집이 보이는 어딘가에 있는 게 좋겠소. 그리고 문이 열리고 자물쇠공이 떠나는 것이 보이거든, 모두 건너오시오."], ["We shall be on the lookout for you, and shall let you in.”", "우리가 당신들을 살피고 있다가 들여보내 주겠소.\""]], [["“The advice is good!”", "\"좋은 조언이오!\""], ["said Van Helsing, so we said no more.", "반 헬싱이 말했고, 그래서 우리는 더 말하지 않았다."], ["Godalming and Morris hurried off in a cab, we following in another.", "고댈밍과 모리스는 삯마차를 타고 서둘러 떠났고, 우리는 다른 마차로 뒤따랐다."], ["At the corner of Arlington Street our contingent got out and strolled into the Green Park.", "알링턴 가 모퉁이에서 우리 일행은 내려 그린파크로 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어 들어갔다."], ["My heart beat as I saw the house on which so much of our hope was centred, looming up grim and silent in its deserted condition amongst its more lively and spruce-looking neighbours.", "우리의 그토록 많은 희망이 집중된 그 집이, 더 활기차고 산뜻해 보이는 이웃집들 사이에서, 버려진 채로 음울하고 고요하게 우뚝 솟아 있는 것을 보자 내 심장이 뛰었다."], ["We sat down on a bench within good view, and began to smoke cigars so as to attract as little attention as possible.", "우리는 잘 보이는 자리의 벤치에 앉아, 되도록 눈길을 덜 끌려고 시가를 피우기 시작했다."], ["The minutes seemed to pass with leaden feet as we waited for the coming of the others.", "나머지 사람들이 오기를 기다리는 동안 분초가 납덩이 같은 발걸음으로 지나가는 듯했다."]], [["At length we saw a four-wheeler drive up.", "마침내 우리는 사륜마차 한 대가 다가오는 것을 보았다."], ["Out of it, in leisurely fashion, got Lord Godalming and Morris; and down from the box descended a thick-set working man with his rush-woven basket of tools.", "거기서, 느긋한 태도로 고댈밍 경과 모리스가 내렸다. 그리고 마부석에서 골풀로 엮은 연장 바구니를 든 다부진 일꾼 하나가 내려왔다."], ["Morris paid the cabman, who touched his hat and drove away. Together the two ascended the steps, and Lord Godalming pointed out what he wanted done.", "모리스가 삯마차 마부에게 삯을 치르자, 마부는 모자에 손을 대고는 마차를 몰아 떠났다. 둘은 함께 계단을 올랐고, 고댈밍 경이 무엇을 하기를 원하는지 가리켰다."], ["The workman took off his coat leisurely and hung it on one of the spikes of the rail, saying something to a policeman who just then sauntered along.", "일꾼은 느긋하게 외투를 벗어 난간의 창살 끝 하나에 걸고는, 마침 그때 어슬렁어슬렁 걸어오는 경찰에게 무언가 말을 했다."], ["The policeman nodded acquiescence, and the man kneeling down placed his bag beside him.", "경찰은 동의하듯 고개를 끄덕였고, 그 남자는 무릎을 꿇고 자기 옆에 가방을 놓았다."], ["After searching through it, he took out a selection of tools which he produced to lay beside him in orderly fashion.", "그는 가방을 뒤적이더니 연장 몇 가지를 골라 꺼내, 자기 옆에 가지런히 늘어놓았다."], ["Then he stood up, looked into the keyhole, blew into it, and turning to his employers, made some remark.", "그런 다음 그는 일어서서 열쇠 구멍을 들여다보고, 거기에 입김을 불어넣고는, 고용주들을 돌아보며 무언가 말을 했다."], ["Lord Godalming smiled, and the man lifted a good-sized bunch of keys; selecting one of them, he began to probe the lock, as if feeling his way with it.", "고댈밍 경이 미소 지었고, 남자는 제법 큰 열쇠 꾸러미를 들어 올렸다. 그 가운데 하나를 골라, 마치 그것으로 길을 더듬듯 자물쇠를 쑤셔보기 시작했다."], ["After fumbling about for a bit he tried a second, and then a third.", "한동안 만지작거리던 그는 두 번째 열쇠를, 그다음에는 세 번째 열쇠를 시험해보았다."], ["All at once the door opened under a slight push from him, and he and the two others entered the hall.", "별안간 그가 살짝 밀자 문이 열렸고, 그와 다른 두 사람은 현관으로 들어갔다. 우리는 가만히 앉아 있었다."], ["We sat still; my own cigar burnt furiously, but Van Helsing’s went cold altogether.", "내 시가는 맹렬히 타들어 갔으나, 반 헬싱의 것은 아예 차갑게 식어버렸다."], ["We waited patiently as we saw the workman come out and bring in his bag.", "우리는 일꾼이 나와 자기 가방을 들고 들어가는 것을 보며 참을성 있게 기다렸다."], ["Then he held the door partly open, steadying it with his knees, whilst he fitted a key to the lock.", "그런 다음 그는 무릎으로 문을 받쳐 반쯤 열어둔 채, 자물쇠에 열쇠 하나를 맞추었다."], ["This he finally handed to Lord Godalming, who took out his purse and gave him something.", "그는 마침내 그것을 고댈밍 경에게 건넸고, 고댈밍 경은 지갑을 꺼내 그에게 무언가를 주었다."], ["The man touched his hat, took his bag, put on his coat and departed; not a soul took the slightest notice of the whole transaction.", "남자는 모자에 손을 대고, 가방을 들고, 외투를 입고는 떠났다. 이 모든 거래에 조금이라도 주의를 기울이는 이는 아무도 없었다."]], [["When the man had fairly gone, we three crossed the street and knocked at the door.", "그 남자가 완전히 떠나자, 우리 셋은 길을 건너 문을 두드렸다."], ["It was immediately opened by Quincey Morris, beside whom stood Lord Godalming lighting a cigar.", "문은 곧바로 퀸시 모리스가 열어주었고, 그 옆에는 고댈밍 경이 시가에 불을 붙이며 서 있었다."]], [["“The place smells so vilely,”", "\"이곳은 냄새가 지독하군요.\""], ["said the latter as we came in.", "우리가 들어가자 후자가 말했다."], ["It did indeed smell vilely--like the old chapel at Carfax--and with our previous experience it was plain to us that the Count had been using the place pretty freely.", "정말로 냄새가 지독했다 — 카팩스의 그 낡은 예배당처럼 — 그리고 우리의 앞선 경험으로 미루어, 백작이 이곳을 꽤 자유로이 써왔음이 우리에게 분명해 보였다."], ["We moved to explore the house, all keeping together in case of attack; for we knew we had a strong and wily enemy to deal with, and as yet we did not know whether the Count might not be in the house.", "우리는 공격에 대비해 모두 함께 붙어, 집을 살피러 움직였다. 우리가 강하고 교활한 적을 상대하고 있음을 알았고, 백작이 그 집 안에 있지 않으리라는 보장이 아직 없었기 때문이다."], ["In the dining-room, which lay at the back of the hall, we found eight boxes of earth.", "현관 뒤쪽에 자리한 식당에서 우리는 흙 상자 여덟 개를 발견했다."], ["Eight boxes only out of the nine, which we sought!", "우리가 찾던 아홉 개 가운데 여덟 개뿐이었다!"], ["Our work was not over, and would never be until we should have found the missing box.", "우리의 일은 끝나지 않았고, 사라진 상자를 찾기 전까지는 결코 끝나지 않을 것이었다."], ["First we opened the shutters of the window which looked out across a narrow stone-flagged yard at the blank face of a stable, pointed to look like the front of a miniature house.", "먼저 우리는, 좁은 돌포장 마당 너머로 어떤 마구간의 휑한 정면을 — 작은 집의 앞면처럼 보이도록 만들어진 것을 — 마주 보는 창문의 덧문을 열었다."], ["There were no windows in it, so we were not afraid of being over-looked.", "거기에는 창문이 없었으므로, 우리는 누가 엿볼까 두려워하지 않았다."], ["We did not lose any time in examining the chests. With the tools which we had brought with us we opened them, one by one, and treated them as we had treated those others in the old chapel.", "우리는 그 상자들을 살피는 데 시간을 조금도 허비하지 않았다. 우리가 가져온 연장으로 우리는 그것들을 하나하나 열어, 낡은 예배당의 그 다른 상자들을 처리했던 것처럼 처리했다."], ["It was evident to us that the Count was not at present in the house, and we proceeded to search for any of his effects.", "백작이 지금 그 집 안에 없는 것이 우리에게 분명했고, 우리는 그의 소지품을 찾아 나섰다."]], [["After a cursory glance at the rest of the rooms, from basement to attic, we came to the conclusion that the dining-room contained any effects which might belong to the Count; and so we proceeded to minutely examine them.", "지하에서 다락까지 나머지 방들을 대강 훑어본 뒤, 우리는 백작에게 속할 만한 소지품은 식당 안에 모두 있다는 결론에 이르렀다. 그래서 우리는 그것들을 세밀히 살펴보기 시작했다."], ["They lay in a sort of orderly disorder on the great dining-room table.", "그것들은 커다란 식당 탁자 위에 일종의 질서 있는 무질서로 놓여 있었다."], ["There were title deeds of the Piccadilly house in a great bundle; deeds of the purchase of the houses at Mile End and Bermondsey; note-paper, envelopes, and pens and ink.", "피커딜리 집의 권리증서가 큰 뭉치로 있었고, 마일엔드와 버몬지 집들의 매매 증서, 편지지, 봉투, 펜과 잉크가 있었다."], ["All were covered up in thin wrapping paper to keep them from the dust.", "모든 것이 먼지를 막으려고 얇은 포장지로 싸여 있었다."], ["There were also a clothes brush, a brush and comb, and a jug and basin--the latter containing dirty water which was reddened as if with blood.", "또한 옷솔과, 머리솔과 빗, 그리고 물주전자와 대야가 있었는데 — 대야에는 마치 피로 물든 듯 불그스름한 더러운 물이 담겨 있었다."], ["Last of all was a little heap of keys of all sorts and sizes, probably those belonging to the other houses.", "마지막으로, 온갖 종류와 크기의 열쇠가 작은 무더기로 있었는데, 아마도 다른 집들에 속한 것들이리라."], ["When we had examined this last find, Lord Godalming and Quincey Morris taking accurate notes of the various addresses of the houses in the East and the South, took with them the keys in a great bunch, and set out to destroy the boxes in these places. The rest of us are, with what patience we can, waiting their return--or the coming of the Count.", "이 마지막 발견물을 살펴본 뒤, 고댈밍 경과 퀸시 모리스는 동부와 남부에 있는 집들의 여러 주소를 정확히 적어두고, 그 열쇠들을 큰 꾸러미째 챙겨, 그곳들의 상자를 파괴하러 출발했다. 나머지 우리는, 낼 수 있는 만큼의 인내심으로, 그들이 돌아오기를 — 아니면 백작이 오기를 — 기다리고 있다."]], [["# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY", "# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY"]], [["3 October.--The time seemed terribly long whilst we were waiting for the coming of Godalming and Quincey Morris.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」 10월 3일. — 고달밍과 퀸시 모리스가 오기를 기다리는 동안 시간이 끔찍하리만치 길게 느껴졌다."], ["The Professor tried to keep our minds active by using them all the time.", "교수는 우리의 정신을 줄곧 쓰게 함으로써 활발하게 유지하려 애썼다."], ["I could see his beneficent purpose, by the side glances which he threw from time to time at Harker.", "그가 이따금 하커에게 던지는 곁눈질을 보고 나는 그 자애로운 의도를 알 수 있었다."], ["The poor fellow is overwhelmed in a misery that is appalling to see.", "가엾은 친구는 보기에도 처참한 비탄에 휩싸여 있다."], ["Last night he was a frank, happy-looking man, with strong, youthful face, full of energy, and with dark brown hair.", "어젯밤만 해도 그는 솔직하고 행복해 보이는 사람으로, 강인하고 젊은 얼굴에 활기가 넘쳤고 머리칼은 짙은 갈색이었다."], ["To-day he is a drawn, haggard old man, whose white hair matches well with the hollow burning eyes and grief-written lines of his face.", "그러나 오늘은 여위고 초췌한 노인이 되어, 그 흰 머리칼이 움푹 패고 불타는 눈, 슬픔이 새겨진 얼굴의 주름과 잘도 어울린다."], ["His energy is still intact; in fact, he is like a living flame.", "기력만은 아직 온전하다. 사실 그는 살아 있는 불꽃과도 같다."], ["This may yet be his salvation, for, if all go well, it will tide him over the despairing period; he will then, in a kind of way, wake again to the realities of life.", "이것이 어쩌면 그의 구원이 될지도 모른다. 모든 일이 잘되면 이것이 그를 절망의 시기를 무사히 넘기게 해줄 테고, 그러면 그는 어떤 식으로든 다시 삶의 현실로 깨어날 것이기 때문이다."], ["Poor fellow, I thought my own trouble was bad enough, but his----!", "가엾은 친구, 나는 내 자신의 고통이 충분히 버겁다고 여겼으나, 그의 것에 비하면!"], ["The Professor knows this well enough, and is doing his best to keep his mind active.", "교수도 이를 잘 알고 있어, 그의 정신을 활발하게 유지하려 최선을 다하고 있다."], ["What he has been saying was, under the circumstances, of absorbing interest.", "그가 줄곧 한 이야기는, 이런 처지에서는 정신을 온통 사로잡는 흥미로운 것이었다."], ["So well as I can remember, here it is:--", "내가 기억할 수 있는 한 정확히 옮기면 이러하다."]], [["“I have studied, over and over again since they came into my hands, all the papers relating to this monster; and the more I have studied, the greater seems the necessity to utterly stamp him out.", "\"그 자료들이 내 손에 들어온 뒤로 나는 이 괴물에 관한 모든 서류를 거듭거듭 연구해왔소. 그리고 연구하면 할수록, 그를 철저히 짓밟아 없애야 할 필요가 더욱 커지는 듯하오."], ["All through there are signs of his advance; not only of his power, but of his knowledge of it.", "처음부터 끝까지 그가 진보해온 흔적이 있소. 그의 힘뿐 아니라 그 힘에 대한 그의 앎의 흔적까지 말이오."], ["As I learned from the researches of my friend Arminus of Buda-Pesth, he was in life a most wonderful man.", "부다페스트의 내 벗 아르미니우스의 연구에서 알게 된 바로는, 그는 생전에 더없이 놀라운 인물이었소."], ["Soldier, statesman, and alchemist--which latter was the highest development of the science-knowledge of his time.", "군인이요, 정치가요, 연금술사였는데 — 이 마지막 것은 당대 과학 지식의 가장 높은 발전이었소."], ["He had a mighty brain, a learning beyond compare, and a heart that knew no fear and no remorse.", "그는 강력한 두뇌와 비할 데 없는 학식, 그리고 두려움도 후회도 모르는 심장을 지녔소."], ["He dared even to attend the Scholomance, and there was no branch of knowledge of his time that he did not essay.", "그는 스콜로만체에 다니기까지 마다하지 않았고, 당대의 어떤 지식의 갈래도 손대지 않은 것이 없었소."], ["Well, in him the brain powers survived the physical death; though it would seem that memory was not all complete.", "한데 그의 안에서 두뇌의 능력은 육체의 죽음을 넘어 살아남았소. 다만 기억은 완전하지 못한 듯하오."], ["In some faculties of mind he has been, and is, only a child; but he is growing, and some things that were childish at the first are now of man’s stature.", "정신의 어떤 능력에서 그는 한낱 어린아이였고, 지금도 그러하오. 그러나 그는 자라고 있으며, 처음에는 어린애 같던 것들이 이제는 어른의 키에 이르렀소. 그는 실험을 하고 있고, 그것도 잘하고 있소."], ["He is experimenting, and doing it well; and if it had not been that we have crossed his path he would be yet--he may be yet if we fail--the father or furtherer of a new order of beings, whose road must lead through Death, not Life.”", "만일 우리가 그의 앞길을 가로막지 않았더라면 그는 지금쯤 — 우리가 실패한다면 아직도 그리 될 수 있소 — 새로운 종족의 아비이자 키우는 자가 되었을 것이오. 그 종족의 길은 삶이 아니라 죽음을 거쳐 가야 하는 것이오.\" * 스콜로만체: 트란실바니아 전설에 나오는 악마가 운영한다는 마법 학교."]], [["Harker groaned and said, “And this is all arrayed against my darling!", "하커는 신음하며 말했다. \"그런데 그 모든 것이 내 사랑하는 아내를 향해 겨누어져 있다니!"], ["But how is he experimenting?", "한데 그가 어떻게 실험을 한다는 말입니까?"], ["The knowledge may help us to defeat him!”", "그 지식이 우리가 그를 물리치는 데 도움이 될지도 모르잖습니까!\""]], [["“He has all along, since his coming, been trying his power, slowly but surely; that big child-brain of his is working.", "\"그는 이곳에 온 이래로 줄곧, 더디지만 확실하게 자기 힘을 시험해왔소. 그 커다란 어린애 두뇌가 작동하고 있는 것이오."], ["Well for us, it is, as yet, a child-brain; for had he dared, at the first, to attempt certain things he would long ago have been beyond our power.", "우리로서는 다행스럽게도 그것이 아직은 어린애 두뇌요. 만일 그가 처음부터 어떤 일들을 감히 시도했더라면, 그는 진작에 우리 힘이 미치지 못하는 곳으로 가버렸을 테니까."], ["However, he means to succeed, and a man who has centuries before him can afford to wait and to go slow.", "그러나 그는 성공할 작정이며, 앞에 수백 년이 놓인 자라면 기다리고 천천히 가는 여유를 부릴 수 있는 법이오. '천천히 서둘러라'가 그의 좌우명일 만하오.\""], ["Festina lente may well be his motto.”", "* 천천히 서둘러라: 라틴어 'Festina lente'."]], [["“I fail to understand,” said Harker wearily.", "\"이해가 안 됩니다.\" 하커가 지친 목소리로 말했다."], ["“Oh, do be more plain to me!", "\"아, 좀 더 알기 쉽게 말씀해주십시오!"], ["Perhaps grief and trouble are dulling my brain.”", "슬픔과 고통이 제 머리를 둔하게 하는 모양입니다.\""]], [["The Professor laid his hand tenderly on his shoulder as he spoke:--", "교수는 말하면서 다정하게 그의 어깨에 손을 얹었다."]], [["“Ah, my child, I will be plain.", "\"아, 이보게, 알기 쉽게 말하리다."], ["Do you not see how, of late, this monster has been creeping into knowledge experimentally.", "자네는 요사이 이 괴물이 어떻게 실험을 통해 슬금슬금 지식 속으로 기어들어왔는지 모르겠소?"], ["How he has been making use of the zoöphagous patient to effect his entry into friend John’s home; for your Vampire, though in all afterwards he can come when and how he will, must at the first make entry only when asked thereto by an inmate.", "그가 어떻게 그 동물을 먹는 환자를 이용해 우리 벗 존의 집에 들어가려 했는지 말이오. 흡혈귀란 그 뒤로는 언제 어떻게든 마음대로 올 수 있으나, 처음에는 반드시 집 안의 누군가가 청해야만 들어올 수 있기 때문이오."], ["But these are not his most important experiments.", "그러나 이런 것들은 그의 가장 중요한 실험이 아니오."], ["Do we not see how at the first all these so great boxes were moved by others.", "처음에 그 큰 상자들이 모두 다른 자들의 손으로 옮겨진 것을 우리는 보지 않았소?"], ["He knew not then but that must be so.", "그때만 해도 그는 그렇게 해야만 하는 줄로만 알았소."], ["But all the time that so great child-brain of his was growing, and he began to consider whether he might not himself move the box.", "그러나 그 사이 내내 그 거대한 어린애 두뇌가 자라고 있었고, 그는 자기가 직접 상자를 옮길 수 있지 않을까 궁리하기 시작했소."], ["So he began to help; and then, when he found that this be all-right, he try to move them all alone.", "그래서 그는 거들기 시작했고, 이윽고 이 일이 잘되는 것을 알자 혼자서 모든 것을 옮겨보려 했소."], ["And so he progress, and he scatter these graves of him; and none but he know where they are hidden.", "그렇게 그는 진보했고, 자기 무덤들을 여기저기 흩어놓았소. 그것들이 어디 숨겨져 있는지는 그 말고는 아무도 모르오."], ["He may have intend to bury them deep in the ground.", "그는 그것들을 땅속 깊이 묻어둘 작정이었을지도 모르오."], ["So that he only use them in the night, or at such time as he can change his form, they do him equal well; and none may know these are his hiding-place!", "그래서 밤에만, 혹은 자기 모습을 바꿀 수 있는 그런 때에만 사용한다면, 그것들은 그에게 똑같이 쓸모가 있고, 그곳들이 그의 은신처임을 아무도 모를 것이오!"], ["But, my child, do not despair; this knowledge come to him just too late!", "그러나 이보게, 절망하지 마시오. 이 지식은 그에게 너무 늦게 찾아왔소!"], ["Already all of his lairs but one be sterilise as for him; and before the sunset this shall be so.", "이미 그의 소굴은 하나만 빼고 모두 그에게 못 쓰게 되었고, 해지기 전에 그 하나마저 그리 될 것이오."], ["Then he have no place where he can move and hide.", "그러면 그는 움직이고 숨을 곳이 없게 되오."], ["I delayed this morning that so we might be sure.", "내가 오늘 아침을 늦춘 것은 그것을 확실히 해두기 위해서였소."], ["Is there not more at stake for us than for him?", "그에게보다 우리에게 걸린 것이 더 크지 않소?"], ["Then why we not be even more careful than him?", "그렇다면 어찌 우리가 그보다 더 신중하지 않을 수 있겠소?"], ["By my clock it is one hour and already, if all be well, friend Arthur and Quincey are on their way to us.", "내 시계로 한 시가 되었으니, 모든 일이 잘되었다면 벗 아서와 퀸시는 벌써 우리에게 오는 길일 것이오."], ["To-day is our day, and we must go sure, if slow, and lose no chance. See!", "오늘이 우리의 날이며, 우리는 느릴지언정 확실하게 가야 하고, 어떤 기회도 놓쳐서는 안 되오. 보시오!"], ["there are five of us when those absent ones return.”", "저 자리에 없는 이들이 돌아오면 우리는 다섯이 되오.\""]], [["Whilst he was speaking we were startled by a knock at the hall door, the double postman’s knock of the telegraph boy.", "그가 말하는 동안, 우리는 현관문을 두드리는 소리, 곧 전보 배달 소년이 두 번 두드리는 우편배달부식 노크 소리에 화들짝 놀랐다."], ["We all moved out to the hall with one impulse, and Van Helsing, holding up his hand to us to keep silence, stepped to the door and opened it.", "우리는 한 충동에 이끌리듯 모두 현관으로 나갔고, 반 헬싱은 우리에게 조용히 하라며 손을 들어 보이고는 문으로 다가가 문을 열었다."], ["The boy handed in a despatch.", "소년이 전보를 건넸다."], ["The Professor closed the door again, and, after looking at the direction, opened it and read aloud.", "교수는 다시 문을 닫고, 수신인을 살핀 뒤 전보를 펼쳐 소리내어 읽었다."]], [["“Look out for D.", "\"D를 조심하세요."], ["He has just now, 12:45, come from Carfax hurriedly and hastened towards the South.", "그가 방금 12시 45분에 카팩스에서 황급히 나와 남쪽으로 서둘러 갔어요."], ["He seems to be going the round and may want to see you: Mina.”", "한 바퀴 도는 것 같으며, 여러분을 만나려 할지도 몰라요. 미나.\""]], [["There was a pause, broken by Jonathan Harker’s voice:--", "잠시 침묵이 흐르다가, 조너선 하커의 목소리가 그것을 깼다."]], [["“Now, God be thanked, we shall soon meet!”", "\"이제, 하느님 감사합니다, 우리가 곧 만나게 되겠군요!\""], ["Van Helsing turned to him quickly and said:--", "반 헬싱이 재빨리 그에게 돌아서서 말했다."]], [["“God will act in His own way and time.", "\"하느님은 당신의 방식과 때로 행하실 것이오."], ["Do not fear, and do not rejoice as yet; for what we wish for at the moment may be our undoings.”", "두려워하지도, 아직 기뻐하지도 마시오. 지금 이 순간 우리가 바라는 것이 우리의 파멸이 될 수도 있으니 말이오.\""]], [["“I care for nothing now,”", "\"전 이제 아무것도 상관없습니다.\""], ["he answered hotly, “except to wipe out this brute from the face of creation.", "그가 격하게 대답했다. \"이 짐승을 창조된 세상의 낯에서 지워버리는 것 말고는요."], ["I would sell my soul to do it!”", "그렇게만 할 수 있다면 제 영혼이라도 팔겠습니다!\""]], [["“Oh, hush, hush, my child!”", "\"오, 쉿, 쉿, 이보게!\""], ["said Van Helsing.", "반 헬싱이 말했다."], ["“God does not purchase souls in this wise; and the Devil, though he may purchase, does not keep faith.", "\"하느님은 이런 식으로 영혼을 사들이지 않으시오. 그리고 악마는 사들일지는 몰라도 신의를 지키지 않소."], ["But God is merciful and just, and knows your pain and your devotion to that dear Madam Mina.", "그러나 하느님은 자비롭고 공정하시며, 자네의 고통과 그 사랑스러운 미나 부인에 대한 자네의 헌신을 아시오."], ["Think you, how her pain would be doubled, did she but hear your wild words.", "생각해보시오, 그녀가 자네의 그 거친 말을 듣기라도 한다면 그녀의 고통이 어찌 곱절이 되지 않겠소."], ["Do not fear any of us, we are all devoted to this cause, and to-day shall see the end.", "우리 가운데 누구도 두려워하지 마시오. 우리는 모두 이 대의에 헌신하고 있고, 오늘이 그 끝을 보게 될 것이오."], ["The time is coming for action; to-day this Vampire is limit to the powers of man, and till sunset he may not change.", "이제 행동할 때가 다가오고 있소. 오늘 이 흡혈귀는 인간의 힘에 한정되어 있어, 해질 때까지는 모습을 바꿀 수 없소."], ["It will take him time to arrive here--see, it is twenty minutes past one--and there are yet some times before he can hither come, be he never so quick.", "그가 여기 도착하려면 시간이 걸릴 것이오 — 보시오, 한 시 이십 분이오 — 그러니 그가 아무리 빠르다 해도 여기 오기까지는 아직 얼마간 시간이 있소."], ["What we must hope for is that my Lord Arthur and Quincey arrive first.”", "우리가 바라야 할 것은 아서 경과 퀸시가 먼저 도착하는 것이오.\""]], [["About half an hour after we had received Mrs. Harker’s telegram, there came a quiet, resolute knock at the hall door.", "하커 부인의 전보를 받고 반 시간쯤 지났을 때, 현관문에서 조용하고도 단호한 노크 소리가 났다."], ["It was just an ordinary knock, such as is given hourly by thousands of gentlemen, but it made the Professor’s heart and mine beat loudly.", "그것은 그저 수천 명의 신사들이 매시간 두드리는 것 같은 평범한 노크였으나, 그 소리에 교수와 내 심장이 요란하게 뛰었다."], ["We looked at each other, and together moved out into the hall; we each held ready to use our various armaments--the spiritual in the left hand, the mortal in the right.", "우리는 서로를 마주보고 함께 현관으로 나갔다. 우리는 저마다 갖가지 무기를 쓸 채비를 하고 있었다 — 영적인 것은 왼손에, 치명적인 것은 오른손에."], ["Van Helsing pulled back the latch, and, holding the door half open, stood back, having both hands ready for action.", "반 헬싱이 빗장을 당겨 문을 반쯤 연 채로 양손을 행동할 채비를 한 채 뒤로 물러섰다."], ["The gladness of our hearts must have shown upon our faces when on the step, close to the door, we saw Lord Godalming and Quincey Morris.", "문 가까이 계단 위에서 고달밍 경과 퀸시 모리스를 보았을 때, 우리 가슴의 기쁨이 분명 얼굴에 드러났을 것이다."], ["They came quickly in and closed the door behind them, the former saying, as they moved along the hall:--", "그들은 재빨리 들어와 뒤로 문을 닫았고, 고달밍 경이 현관을 따라 걸어오며 말했다."]], [["“It is all right.", "\"다 잘됐습니다."], ["We found both places; six boxes in each and we destroyed them all!”", "두 곳을 다 찾아서, 각각 여섯 상자씩, 모두 없애버렸어요!\""]], [["“Destroyed?”", "\"없애요?\""], ["asked the Professor.", "교수가 물었다."]], [["“For him!” We were silent for a minute, and then Quincey said:--", "\"그가 못 쓰게 만들었지요!\" 우리는 한동안 잠자코 있었고, 이윽고 퀸시가 말했다."]], [["“There’s nothing to do but to wait here.", "\"여기서 기다리는 것 말고는 할 일이 없군요."], ["If, however, he doesn’t turn up by five o’clock, we must start off; for it won’t do to leave Mrs. Harker alone after sunset.”", "하지만 그가 다섯 시까지 나타나지 않으면 우리는 출발해야 합니다. 해진 뒤에 하커 부인을 홀로 두어서는 안 되니까요.\""]], [["“He will be here before long now,”", "\"이제 곧 그가 여기 올 것이오.\""], ["said Van Helsing, who had been consulting his pocket-book.", "수첩을 들여다보고 있던 반 헬싱이 말했다."], ["“Nota bene, in Madam’s telegram he went south from Carfax, that means he went to cross the river, and he could only do so at slack of tide, which should be something before one o’clock.", "\"잘 보시오, 부인의 전보에서 그는 카팩스에서 남쪽으로 갔다고 했소. 이는 그가 강을 건너려 했다는 뜻이고, 그것은 물이 잔잔할 때에만 할 수 있는데, 그 때라야 한 시 조금 전쯤이오."], ["That he went south has a meaning for us.", "그가 남쪽으로 갔다는 것은 우리에게 어떤 의미가 있소."], ["He is as yet only suspicious; and he went from Carfax first to the place where he would suspect interference least.", "그는 아직 의심만 할 뿐이오. 그래서 카팩스에서 먼저, 방해받을 가능성이 가장 적다고 의심할 만한 곳으로 갔던 것이오."], ["You must have been at Bermondsey only a short time before him.", "두 분은 그보다 조금 앞서 버몬지에 있었던 게 틀림없소."], ["That he is not here already shows that he went to Mile End next.", "그가 아직 여기 오지 않은 것은 그가 다음으로 마일 엔드에 갔음을 보여주오."], ["This took him some time; for he would then have to be carried over the river in some way.", "거기서 그는 시간이 좀 걸렸을 것이오. 그러자면 어떤 식으로든 강을 건너 옮겨져야 했을 테니까."], ["Believe me, my friends, we shall not have long to wait now.", "내 말을 믿으시오, 친구들, 이제 우리는 오래 기다릴 필요가 없을 것이오."], ["We should have ready some plan of attack, so that we may throw away no chance.", "우리는 어떤 기회도 헛되이 하지 않도록 공격 계획을 미리 세워두어야 하오."], ["Hush, there is no time now.", "쉿, 이제 시간이 없소."], ["Have all your arms!", "모두 무기를 들으시오!"], ["Be ready!”", "채비하시오!\""], ["He held up a warning hand as he spoke, for we all could hear a key softly inserted in the lock of the hall door.", "그가 말하면서 경고하듯 손을 들어 보였다. 우리 모두 누군가 현관문 자물쇠에 열쇠를 살며시 꽂는 소리를 들을 수 있었기 때문이다."]], [["I could not but admire, even at such a moment, the way in which a dominant spirit asserted itself.", "그런 순간에도 나는 지배적인 정신이 스스로를 내세우는 방식에 감탄하지 않을 수 없었다."], ["In all our hunting parties and adventures in different parts of the world, Quincey Morris had always been the one to arrange the plan of action, and Arthur and I had been accustomed to obey him implicitly.", "세계 각지에서의 온갖 사냥과 모험에서, 행동 계획을 짜는 일은 언제나 퀸시 모리스의 몫이었고, 아서와 나는 그에게 절대적으로 복종하는 데 익숙해 있었다."], ["Now, the old habit seemed to be renewed instinctively.", "이제 그 옛 습관이 본능적으로 되살아나는 듯했다."], ["With a swift glance around the room, he at once laid out our plan of attack, and, without speaking a word, with a gesture, placed us each in position.", "방 안을 재빨리 둘러보고서 그는 즉시 우리의 공격 계획을 짰고, 한마디 말도 없이 몸짓만으로 우리를 저마다 제 위치에 세웠다."], ["Van Helsing, Harker, and I were just behind the door, so that when it was opened the Professor could guard it whilst we two stepped between the incomer and the door.", "반 헬싱과 하커와 나는 문 바로 뒤에 섰으니, 문이 열리면 교수가 문을 지키고 우리 둘은 들어오는 자와 문 사이로 끼어들 수 있게 했다."], ["Godalming behind and Quincey in front stood just out of sight ready to move in front of the window.", "고달밍은 뒤에, 퀸시는 앞에 서서, 창문 앞으로 움직일 채비를 하고 보이지 않는 자리에 섰다."], ["We waited in a suspense that made the seconds pass with nightmare slowness.", "우리는 매초가 악몽처럼 느리게 흘러가게 만드는 긴장 속에서 기다렸다."], ["The slow, careful steps came along the hall; the Count was evidently prepared for some surprise--at least he feared it.", "느리고 조심스러운 발소리가 현관을 따라왔다. 백작은 분명 무언가 기습에 대비하고 있었다 — 적어도 그것을 두려워하고 있었다."]], [["Suddenly with a single bound he leaped into the room, winning a way past us before any of us could raise a hand to stay him.", "별안간 그는 단번에 방 안으로 뛰어들어, 우리 가운데 누구도 그를 막으려 손을 들기도 전에 우리 곁을 지나쳐 길을 텄다."], ["There was something so panther-like in the movement--something so unhuman, that it seemed to sober us all from the shock of his coming.", "그 움직임에는 무언가 표범 같은 것이, 무언가 인간 같지 않은 것이 있어서, 우리 모두 그가 들이닥친 충격에서 도리어 정신을 차리게 하는 듯했다."], ["The first to act was Harker, who, with a quick movement, threw himself before the door leading into the room in the front of the house.", "맨 먼저 움직인 것은 하커였다. 그는 재빠른 동작으로 집 앞쪽 방으로 통하는 문 앞에 몸을 던졌다."], ["As the Count saw us, a horrible sort of snarl passed over his face, showing the eye-teeth long and pointed; but the evil smile as quickly passed into a cold stare of lion-like disdain.", "백작이 우리를 보자 그의 얼굴에 끔찍한 으르렁거림 같은 것이 스쳐, 길고 뾰족한 송곳니가 드러났다. 그러나 그 사악한 미소는 곧 사자 같은 경멸의 차가운 응시로 바뀌었다."], ["His expression again changed as, with a single impulse, we all advanced upon him.", "우리 모두가 한 충동으로 그에게 다가가자 그의 표정이 다시 변했다."], ["It was a pity that we had not some better organised plan of attack, for even at the moment I wondered what we were to do.", "더 잘 짜인 공격 계획이 없었던 것은 안타까운 일이었다. 바로 그 순간에도 나는 우리가 무엇을 해야 할지 의아했기 때문이다."], ["I did not myself know whether our lethal weapons would avail us anything.", "우리의 치명적인 무기가 우리에게 무슨 소용이 있을지 나 자신도 알 수 없었다."], ["Harker evidently meant to try the matter, for he had ready his great Kukri knife and made a fierce and sudden cut at him.", "하커는 분명 그것을 시험해볼 작정이었으니, 그는 자신의 커다란 쿠크리 칼을 빼들고 그에게 사납고 갑작스러운 일격을 가했다."], ["The blow was a powerful one; only the diabolical quickness of the Count’s leap back saved him.", "그 일격은 강력했다. 다만 백작이 악마처럼 빠르게 뒤로 뛴 덕분에 그가 살아남았을 뿐이다."], ["A second less and the trenchant blade had shorne through his heart.", "일 초만 늦었어도 그 예리한 칼날이 그의 심장을 꿰뚫었을 것이다."], ["As it was, the point just cut the cloth of his coat, making a wide gap whence a bundle of bank-notes and a stream of gold fell out.", "사실 칼끝은 그의 외투 천만을 베어, 넓은 틈이 생겼고 그곳에서 지폐 한 뭉치와 금화 한 줄기가 쏟아져 나왔다."], ["The expression of the Count’s face was so hellish, that for a moment I feared for Harker, though I saw him throw the terrible knife aloft again for another stroke.", "백작의 얼굴 표정이 어찌나 지옥 같았던지, 나는 한순간 하커가 걱정되었다. 그가 또 한 번 내리치려 그 무서운 칼을 다시 높이 치켜드는 것을 보았는데도 말이다."], ["Instinctively I moved forward with a protective impulse, holding the Crucifix and Wafer in my left hand.", "나는 본능적으로 보호하려는 충동에 이끌려 왼손에 십자고상과 성체를 든 채 앞으로 나아갔다."], ["I felt a mighty power fly along my arm; and it was without surprise that I saw the monster cower back before a similar movement made spontaneously by each one of us.", "거대한 힘이 내 팔을 타고 흐르는 것을 느꼈고, 우리 각자가 저마다 자발적으로 똑같은 동작을 취하자 괴물이 그 앞에서 움츠리며 물러나는 것을 보고도 나는 놀라지 않았다."], ["It would be impossible to describe the expression of hate and baffled malignity--of anger and hellish rage--which came over the Count’s face.", "그때 백작의 얼굴에 떠오른 증오와 좌절된 악의, 분노와 지옥 같은 격분의 표정은 도저히 말로 옮길 수 없을 것이다."], ["His waxen hue became greenish-yellow by the contrast of his burning eyes, and the red scar on the forehead showed on the pallid skin like a palpitating wound.", "그의 밀랍 같은 낯빛은 불타는 눈과의 대조로 푸르스름한 노란빛이 되었고, 이마의 붉은 흉터는 창백한 살갗 위에서 펄떡이는 상처처럼 도드라졌다."], ["The next instant, with a sinuous dive he swept under Harker’s arm, ere his blow could fall, and, grasping a handful of the money from the floor, dashed across the room, threw himself at the window.", "다음 순간, 그는 하커의 일격이 떨어지기 전에 굽이치는 몸짓으로 그의 팔 아래로 빠져나가, 바닥에서 돈 한 줌을 움켜쥐고는 방을 가로질러 내달아 창문으로 몸을 던졌다."], ["Amid the crash and glitter of the falling glass, he tumbled into the flagged area below.", "깨진 유리가 와장창 반짝이며 부서지는 가운데, 그는 아래의 포석 깔린 마당으로 굴러떨어졌다."], ["Through the sound of the shivering glass I could hear the “ting” of the gold, as some of the sovereigns fell on the flagging.", "유리가 산산이 부서지는 소리 사이로, 나는 소브린 금화 몇 개가 포석 위에 떨어지며 내는 \"땡그랑\" 하는 금속음을 들을 수 있었다."]], [["We ran over and saw him spring unhurt from the ground.", "우리가 달려가서 보니, 그는 다친 데 없이 땅에서 솟구쳐 올랐다."], ["He, rushing up the steps, crossed the flagged yard, and pushed open the stable door.", "그는 계단을 뛰어올라 포석 깔린 마당을 가로지른 뒤 마구간 문을 밀어 열었다."], ["There he turned and spoke to us:--", "그곳에서 그는 돌아서서 우리에게 말했다."]], [["“You think to baffle me, you--with your pale faces all in a row, like sheep in a butcher’s.", "\"너희가 나를 좌절시킬 줄 아느냐, 너희가 — 그 핏기 없는 얼굴을 한 줄로 늘어세우고는, 마치 푸줏간의 양 떼처럼 말이다."], ["You shall be sorry yet, each one of you!", "너희는 머지않아 후회하게 될 것이다, 너희 하나하나가!"], ["You think you have left me without a place to rest; but I have more.", "너희는 나에게 쉴 곳을 남겨두지 않았다고 여기겠지만, 나에게는 더 있다."], ["My revenge is just begun!", "내 복수는 이제 막 시작되었을 뿐!"], ["I spread it over centuries, and time is on my side.", "나는 그것을 수백 년에 걸쳐 펼칠 것이고, 시간은 내 편이다."], ["Your girls that you all love are mine already; and through them you and others shall yet be mine--my creatures, to do my bidding and to be my jackals when I want to feed. Bah!”", "너희가 모두 사랑하는 너희의 여인들은 이미 내 것이다. 그리고 그들을 통해 너희와 다른 자들도 머지않아 내 것이 되리라 — 내 명령을 따르고, 내가 먹이를 원할 때 내 자칼이 될 내 피조물 말이다. 흥!\""], ["With a contemptuous sneer, he passed quickly through the door, and we heard the rusty bolt creak as he fastened it behind him.", "경멸하는 비웃음과 함께 그는 재빨리 문을 빠져나갔고, 우리는 그가 뒤로 문을 잠그는 녹슨 빗장이 삐걱대는 소리를 들었다."], ["A door beyond opened and shut.", "그 너머의 문 하나가 열렸다 닫혔다."], ["The first of us to speak was the Professor, as, realising the difficulty of following him through the stable, we moved toward the hall.", "우리 가운데 가장 먼저 입을 연 것은 교수였다. 마구간을 통해 그를 뒤쫓기가 어렵다는 것을 깨닫고, 우리는 현관 쪽으로 움직였다."]], [["“We have learnt something--much!", "\"우리는 무언가를 알게 됐소 — 많은 것을!"], ["Notwithstanding his brave words, he fears us; he fear time, he fear want!", "그 호기로운 말에도 불구하고, 그는 우리를 두려워하오. 그는 시간을 두려워하고, 결핍을 두려워하오!"], ["For if not, why he hurry so?", "그렇지 않다면 어찌 그가 그토록 서둘렀겠소?"], ["His very tone betray him, or my ears deceive.", "그의 말투 자체가 그를 드러내오, 내 귀가 나를 속인 게 아니라면 말이오."], ["Why take that money?", "어찌하여 저 돈을 가져갔겠소?"], ["You follow quick.", "두 사람은 어서 뒤쫓으시오."], ["You are hunters of wild beast, and understand it so.", "두 사람은 야수를 쫓는 사냥꾼이니, 그것을 잘 알 것이오."], ["For me, I make sure that nothing here may be of use to him, if so that he return.”", "나로서는, 그가 돌아오더라도 여기 있는 어떤 것도 그에게 쓸모가 없도록 확실히 해두겠소.\""], ["As he spoke he put the money remaining into his pocket; took the title-deeds in the bundle as Harker had left them, and swept the remaining things into the open fireplace, where he set fire to them with a match.", "그는 이렇게 말하면서 남은 돈을 자기 주머니에 넣고, 하커가 남겨둔 그대로 묶음 속의 권리증서들을 챙긴 뒤, 나머지 것들을 모두 빈 벽난로 속으로 쓸어넣고는 성냥으로 불을 붙였다."]], [["Godalming and Morris had rushed out into the yard, and Harker had lowered himself from the window to follow the Count.", "고달밍과 모리스는 마당으로 달려나갔고, 하커는 백작을 뒤쫓으려 창문에서 몸을 내려뜨렸다."], ["He had, however, bolted the stable door; and by the time they had forced it open there was no sign of him.", "그러나 백작은 마구간 문을 잠가버렸고, 그들이 그것을 억지로 열었을 무렵에는 그의 흔적이 보이지 않았다."], ["Van Helsing and I tried to make inquiry at the back of the house; but the mews was deserted and no one had seen him depart.", "반 헬싱과 나는 집 뒤쪽에서 알아보려 했으나, 마구간 골목은 인적이 없었고 아무도 그가 떠나는 것을 보지 못했다."]], [["It was now late in the afternoon, and sunset was not far off.", "이제 오후도 저물어, 해질 때가 멀지 않았다."], ["We had to recognise that our game was up; with heavy hearts we agreed with the Professor when he said:--", "우리는 우리의 일이 끝장났음을 인정해야 했다. 무거운 마음으로 우리는 교수의 말에 동의했다."]], [["“Let us go back to Madam Mina--poor, poor dear Madam Mina.", "\"미나 부인에게로 돌아갑시다 — 가엾고 가엾은 미나 부인에게."], ["All we can do just now is done; and we can there, at least, protect her.", "지금 우리가 할 수 있는 일은 다 했소. 그리고 적어도 거기서는 우리가 그녀를 지킬 수 있소."], ["But we need not despair.", "그러나 우리는 절망할 필요가 없소."], ["There is but one more earth-box, and we must try to find it; when that is done all may yet be well.”", "흙 상자가 단 하나 더 남아 있고, 우리는 그것을 찾아내려 애써야 하오. 그 일이 끝나면 모든 것이 아직 잘될 수도 있소.\""], ["I could see that he spoke as bravely as he could to comfort Harker.", "그가 하커를 위로하려 애써 용감하게 말하는 것을 나는 알 수 있었다."], ["The poor fellow was quite broken down; now and again he gave a low groan which he could not suppress--he was thinking of his wife.", "가엾은 친구는 완전히 무너져 있었다. 이따금 그는 참지 못하고 나직한 신음을 흘렸다 — 그는 자기 아내를 생각하고 있었다."]], [["With sad hearts we came back to my house, where we found Mrs. Harker waiting us, with an appearance of cheerfulness which did honour to her bravery and unselfishness.", "슬픈 마음으로 우리는 내 집으로 돌아왔는데, 거기서 우리를 기다리고 있던 하커 부인을 보니, 그녀의 용기와 헌신에 걸맞은 명랑한 기색이었다."], ["When she saw our faces, her own became as pale as death: for a second or two her eyes were closed as if she were in secret prayer; and then she said cheerfully:--", "그녀는 우리 얼굴을 보자 자신의 얼굴이 죽은 사람처럼 창백해졌다. 잠시 동안 그녀는 마치 남몰래 기도라도 하듯 눈을 감았다. 그러고는 명랑하게 말했다."]], [["“I can never thank you all enough.", "\"여러분께 아무리 감사드려도 부족할 거예요."], ["Oh, my poor darling!”", "오, 가엾은 내 사랑!\""], ["As she spoke, she took her husband’s grey head in her hands and kissed it--“Lay your poor head here and rest it.", "그녀는 이렇게 말하며 남편의 잿빛 머리를 두 손으로 감싸고 입맞추었다. \"당신의 가엾은 머리를 여기 누이고 쉬어요."], ["All will yet be well, dear!", "모든 것이 아직 잘될 거예요, 여보!"], ["God will protect us if He so will it in His good intent.”", "하느님께서 그 선하신 뜻으로 그리하고자 하신다면 우리를 지켜주실 거예요.\""], ["The poor fellow groaned.", "가엾은 친구는 신음했다."], ["There was no place for words in his sublime misery.", "그의 그 숭고한 비탄에는 말이 들어설 자리가 없었다."]], [["We had a sort of perfunctory supper together, and I think it cheered us all up somewhat.", "우리는 함께 마지못해 하는 식의 저녁식사를 했는데, 내 생각에 그것이 우리 모두를 어느 정도 기운나게 한 듯하다."], ["It was, perhaps, the mere animal heat of food to hungry people--for none of us had eaten anything since breakfast--or the sense of companionship may have helped us; but anyhow we were all less miserable, and saw the morrow as not altogether without hope. True to our promise, we told Mrs.", "그것은 어쩌면 굶주린 사람들에게 음식이 주는 한낱 동물적인 온기였을지도 모른다 — 우리 가운데 누구도 아침식사 이후로 아무것도 먹지 못했으니 말이다 — 아니면 동료애가 도움이 되었을지도 모른다. 어쨌든 우리는 모두 덜 비참해졌고, 내일을 아주 희망 없는 것만은 아니라고 여기게 되었다."], ["Harker everything which had passed; and although she grew snowy white at times when danger had seemed to threaten her husband, and red at others when his devotion to her was manifested, she listened bravely and with calmness.", "약속한 대로 우리는 하커 부인에게 그동안 있었던 모든 일을 이야기했고, 그녀는 남편이 위험에 처한 듯한 대목에서는 이따금 눈처럼 하얗게 질리고, 그의 자기에 대한 헌신이 드러나는 대목에서는 또 이따금 붉어지면서도, 용감하고 차분하게 귀를 기울였다."], ["When we came to the part where Harker had rushed at the Count so recklessly, she clung to her husband’s arm, and held it tight as though her clinging could protect him from any harm that might come.", "하커가 그토록 무모하게 백작에게 달려든 대목에 이르자, 그녀는 남편의 팔에 매달려, 마치 자기가 매달림으로써 닥쳐올 어떤 해로움으로부터도 그를 지킬 수 있기라도 한 듯 꼭 붙들었다."], ["She said nothing, however, till the narration was all done, and matters had been brought right up to the present time.", "그러나 그녀는 이야기가 모두 끝나 일이 바로 지금까지 이르고 나서야 비로소 입을 열었다. 그러고는 남편의 손을 놓지 않은 채 우리 가운데서 일어서서 말했다."], ["Then without letting go her husband’s hand she stood up amongst us and spoke.", "아, 내가 그 광경을 조금이라도 전할 수 있다면."], ["Oh, that I could give any idea of the scene; of that sweet, sweet, good, good woman in all the radiant beauty of her youth and animation, with the red scar on her forehead, of which she was conscious, and which we saw with grinding of our teeth--remembering whence and how it came; her loving kindness against our grim hate; her tender faith against all our fears and doubting; and we, knowing that so far as symbols went, she with all her goodness and purity and faith, was outcast from God.", "젊음과 생기의 빛나는 아름다움을 온통 갖춘 그 다정하고 다정한, 선하고 선한 여인이, 자기도 의식하고 있는 그 이마의 붉은 흉터를 — 우리는 그것이 어디서 어떻게 생겼는지를 떠올리며 이를 갈며 바라보았다 — 지닌 채 서 있는 모습을. 우리의 음울한 증오에 맞선 그녀의 사랑 어린 따뜻함을, 우리의 온갖 두려움과 의심에 맞선 그녀의 부드러운 믿음을. 그리고 상징의 차원에서는, 그 모든 선함과 순결함과 믿음을 지닌 그녀가 하느님으로부터 버림받은 자라는 것을 우리가 알고 있었음을."]], [["“Jonathan,”", "\"조너선,\""], ["she said, and the word sounded like music on her lips it was so full of love and tenderness, “Jonathan dear, and you all my true, true friends, I want you to bear something in mind through all this dreadful time.", "그녀가 말했는데, 그 말은 사랑과 다정함이 어찌나 가득했던지 그녀의 입술에서 음악처럼 들렸다. \"조너선, 여보, 그리고 여러분 모두 나의 진실하고 진실한 벗들, 저는 여러분이 이 끔찍한 시간 내내 한 가지를 마음에 새겨두기를 바라요."], ["I know that you must fight--that you must destroy even as you destroyed the false Lucy so that the true Lucy might live hereafter; but it is not a work of hate.", "저는 여러분이 싸워야 한다는 것을 알아요 — 참된 루시가 이후에 살 수 있도록 거짓된 루시를 없앴듯이, 여러분이 없애야만 한다는 것을요. 하지만 그것은 증오의 일이 아니에요."], ["That poor soul who has wrought all this misery is the saddest case of all.", "이 모든 비참을 빚어낸 그 가엾은 영혼이야말로 가장 슬픈 경우랍니다."], ["Just think what will be his joy when he, too, is destroyed in his worser part that his better part may have spiritual immortality.", "생각해보세요, 그 역시 자신의 더 나은 부분이 영적 불멸을 얻을 수 있도록 더 못한 부분에서 파멸될 때, 그의 기쁨이 어떠할지를요."], ["You must be pitiful to him, too, though it may not hold your hands from his destruction.”", "여러분은 그에게도 연민을 품어야 해요. 비록 그것이 그를 파멸시키는 여러분의 손을 멈추게 하지는 못하더라도 말이에요.\""]], [["As she spoke I could see her husband’s face darken and draw together, as though the passion in him were shrivelling his being to its core.", "그녀가 말하는 동안 나는 남편의 얼굴이 어두워지며 일그러지는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 마치 그의 안에 든 격정이 그의 존재를 그 속까지 오그라뜨리기라도 하는 듯이."], ["Instinctively the clasp on his wife’s hand grew closer, till his knuckles looked white.", "본능적으로 아내의 손을 잡은 그의 손아귀에 힘이 들어가, 마침내 그의 손가락 마디가 하얗게 보였다."], ["She did not flinch from the pain which I knew she must have suffered, but looked at him with eyes that were more appealing than ever.", "그녀는 분명 겪었을 그 고통에 움찔하지도 않고, 그 어느 때보다 더 애원하는 듯한 눈으로 그를 바라보았다."], ["As she stopped speaking he leaped to his feet, almost tearing his hand from hers as he spoke:--", "그녀가 말을 멈추자 그는 벌떡 일어서며, 거의 그녀의 손에서 제 손을 뜯어내듯 하며 말했다."]], [["“May God give him into my hand just for long enough to destroy that earthly life of him which we are aiming at.", "\"우리가 노리는 그자의 그 지상의 목숨을 없앨 수 있을 만큼만이라도, 하느님께서 그자를 내 손에 넘겨주시기를."], ["If beyond it I could send his soul for ever and ever to burning hell I would do it!”", "그 너머로 그의 영혼을 영원무궁토록 불타는 지옥으로 보낼 수 있다면, 나는 그렇게 하겠소!\""]], [["“Oh, hush! oh, hush!", "\"오, 쉿! 오, 쉿!"], ["in the name of the good God.", "선하신 하느님의 이름으로요."], ["Don’t say such things, Jonathan, my husband; or you will crush me with fear and horror.", "그런 말은 하지 말아요, 조너선, 내 남편. 안 그러면 당신이 나를 두려움과 공포로 짓눌러버릴 거예요."], ["Just think, my dear--I have been thinking all this long, long day of it--that ...", "생각해봐요, 여보 — 저는 이 길고 긴 하루 내내 그것을 생각해왔어요 — 어쩌면……"], ["perhaps ...", "언젠가는……"], ["some day ... I, too, may need such pity; and that some other like you--and with equal cause for anger--may deny it to me!", "저 역시도 그런 연민이 필요할지 모른다는 것을요. 그리고 당신 같은 어떤 다른 이가 — 그리고 똑같이 분노할 만한 이유를 가진 이가 — 저에게 그것을 거절할지도 모른다는 것을요!"], ["Oh, my husband!", "오, 내 남편!"], ["my husband, indeed I would have spared you such a thought had there been another way; but I pray that God may not have treasured your wild words, except as the heart-broken wail of a very loving and sorely stricken man.", "정말이지 다른 길이 있었다면 당신에게 이런 생각을 덜어주었을 거예요. 하지만 저는 하느님께서 당신의 그 거친 말을, 더없이 사랑이 깊고 모질게 상처 입은 한 사람의 가슴 찢어지는 통곡 외에 다른 것으로는 마음에 담아두지 않으시기를 기도해요."], ["Oh, God, let these poor white hairs go in evidence of what he has suffered, who all his life has done no wrong, and on whom so many sorrows have come.”", "오, 하느님, 이 가엾은 흰 머리칼이 그가 겪은 고난의 증거가 되게 하소서, 평생 아무 잘못도 하지 않았으나 이토록 많은 슬픔이 닥친 이 사람의 증거가 되게 하소서.\""]], [["We men were all in tears now. There was no resisting them, and we wept openly.", "이제 우리 남자들은 모두 눈물을 흘리고 있었다. 도저히 참을 수 없어, 우리는 드러내놓고 울었다."], ["She wept, too, to see that her sweeter counsels had prevailed.", "그녀의 더 다정한 권고가 받아들여진 것을 보고 그녀도 울었다."], ["Her husband flung himself on his knees beside her, and putting his arms round her, hid his face in the folds of her dress.", "그녀의 남편은 그녀 곁에 무릎을 꿇고 두 팔로 그녀를 감싸안은 채, 그녀의 옷자락 주름 속에 얼굴을 묻었다."], ["Van Helsing beckoned to us and we stole out of the room, leaving the two loving hearts alone with their God.", "반 헬싱이 우리에게 손짓했고, 우리는 그 사랑하는 두 마음을 그들의 하느님과 함께 둘만 있도록 남겨두고 살그머니 방을 빠져나왔다."]], [["Before they retired the Professor fixed up the room against any coming of the Vampire, and assured Mrs. Harker that she might rest in peace.", "그들이 물러가기 전에 교수는 흡혈귀가 올 것에 대비해 방을 단단히 손보고, 하커 부인에게 평안히 쉬어도 된다고 안심시켰다."], ["She tried to school herself to the belief, and, manifestly for her husband’s sake, tried to seem content.", "그녀는 그 믿음에 자신을 길들이려 애썼고, 분명 남편을 위해서 만족한 듯 보이려 애썼다."], ["It was a brave struggle; and was, I think and believe, not without its reward.", "그것은 용감한 분투였고, 내 생각으로는, 또한 내 믿음으로는, 보람이 없지 않았다."], ["Van Helsing had placed at hand a bell which either of them was to sound in case of any emergency.", "반 헬싱은 어떤 비상시에든 둘 가운데 누구라도 울릴 수 있도록 종 하나를 가까이 두었다."], ["When they had retired, Quincey, Godalming, and I arranged that we should sit up, dividing the night between us, and watch over the safety of the poor stricken lady.", "그들이 물러간 뒤, 퀸시와 고달밍과 나는 우리가 밤을 나누어 번갈아 앉아 지키며, 가엾고 상처 입은 부인의 안전을 살피기로 정했다."], ["The first watch falls to Quincey, so the rest of us shall be off to bed as soon as we can.", "첫 번째 번은 퀸시에게 돌아갔으니, 나머지 우리는 되도록 빨리 잠자리에 들어야 한다."], ["Godalming has already turned in, for his is the second watch.", "고달밍은 두 번째 번이라 이미 잠자리에 들었다."], ["Now that my work is done I, too, shall go to bed.", "이제 내 일이 끝났으니, 나도 잠자리에 들어야겠다."]], [["Jonathan Harker’s Journal.", "「조너선 하커의 일기」"]], [["3-4 October, close to midnight.--I thought yesterday would never end.", "10월 3일에서 4일, 자정 무렵. — 나는 어제가 결코 끝나지 않을 것만 같았다."], ["There was over me a yearning for sleep, in some sort of blind belief that to wake would be to find things changed, and that any change must now be for the better.", "깨어나면 사정이 달라져 있을 테고, 이제 어떤 변화든 더 나은 쪽일 수밖에 없으리라는 일종의 맹목적인 믿음에 사로잡혀, 나는 잠을 갈망했다."], ["Before we parted, we discussed what our next step was to be, but we could arrive at no result.", "헤어지기 전에 우리는 다음 단계가 무엇이 되어야 할지를 논의했으나, 아무 결론에도 이르지 못했다."], ["All we knew was that one earth-box remained, and that the Count alone knew where it was.", "우리가 아는 것이라고는 흙 상자 하나가 남아 있다는 것, 그리고 그것이 어디 있는지는 백작만이 안다는 것뿐이었다."], ["If he chooses to lie hidden, he may baffle us for years; and in the meantime!--the thought is too horrible, I dare not think of it even now.", "만일 그가 숨어 지내기로 마음먹는다면, 그는 몇 해고 우리를 좌절시킬 수 있을 것이다. 그리고 그동안에! — 그 생각은 너무도 끔찍해서, 나는 지금조차 감히 그것을 떠올릴 수 없다."], ["This I know: that if ever there was a woman who was all perfection, that one is my poor wronged darling.", "다만 이것만은 안다. 일찍이 온전히 완벽한 여인이 있었다면, 그 사람은 바로 나의 가엾고 부당하게 상처 입은 사랑이라는 것을."], ["I love her a thousand times more for her sweet pity of last night, a pity that made my own hate of the monster seem despicable.", "나는 어젯밤 그녀의 그 다정한 연민으로 그녀를 천 배는 더 사랑하게 되었다. 그 연민은 괴물에 대한 나 자신의 증오를 비열하게 보이게 했다."], ["Surely God will not permit the world to be the poorer by the loss of such a creature.", "분명 하느님께서는 그런 존재를 잃어 세상이 더 빈곤해지도록 허락하지 않으실 것이다."], ["This is hope to me.", "이것이 나에게는 희망이다."], ["We are all drifting reefwards now, and faith is our only anchor.", "우리는 모두 이제 암초를 향해 떠밀려가고 있고, 믿음만이 우리의 유일한 닻이다."], ["Thank God! Mina is sleeping, and sleeping without dreams.", "하느님 감사합니다! 미나는 자고 있고, 꿈도 없이 자고 있다."], ["I fear what her dreams might be like, with such terrible memories to ground them in.", "그토록 끔찍한 기억들을 밑바탕에 두고 있으니, 그녀의 꿈이 어떠할지 두렵다."], ["She has not been so calm, within my seeing, since the sunset.", "내가 보아온 바로, 그녀는 해질 무렵 이래로 이토록 평온한 적이 없었다."], ["Then, for a while, there came over her face a repose which was like spring after the blasts of March.", "그때, 한동안 그녀의 얼굴 위로 삼월의 거센 바람 뒤에 찾아오는 봄과도 같은 안식이 깃들었다."], ["I thought at the time that it was the softness of the red sunset on her face, but somehow now I think it has a deeper meaning.", "그때는 그것이 그녀의 얼굴에 어린 붉은 노을의 부드러움이라고 여겼으나, 어쩐지 지금은 거기에 더 깊은 의미가 있는 것 같다."], ["I am not sleepy myself, though I am weary--weary to death.", "나는 지쳐 있는데도 — 죽도록 지쳐 있는데도 — 잠이 오지 않는다."], ["However, I must try to sleep; for there is to-morrow to think of, and there is no rest for me until....", "그래도 나는 잠을 청해야 한다. 내일을 생각해야 하고, 또한 …… 하기까지 나에게는 쉴 틈이 없으니까."]], [["Later.--I must have fallen asleep, for I was awaked by Mina, who was sitting up in bed, with a startled look on her face.", "이후. — 나는 잠이 들었던 모양이다. 미나가 깨워서 깼는데, 그녀는 화들짝 놀란 얼굴로 침대에 일어나 앉아 있었다."], ["I could see easily, for we did not leave the room in darkness; she had placed a warning hand over my mouth, and now she whispered in my ear:--", "우리가 방을 어둠 속에 두지 않았으므로 나는 쉽게 볼 수 있었다. 그녀는 내 입에 경고하듯 손을 얹고는, 이제 내 귀에 속삭였다."]], [["“Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["there is someone in the corridor!”", "복도에 누군가 있어요!\""], ["I got up softly, and crossing the room, gently opened the door.", "나는 살그머니 일어나 방을 가로질러 가서 가만히 문을 열었다."]], [["Just outside, stretched on a mattress, lay Mr. Morris, wide awake.", "바로 바깥, 매트리스 위에 모리스 씨가 또렷이 깨어 누워 있었다."], ["He raised a warning hand for silence as he whispered to me:--", "그는 나에게 속삭이며 조용히 하라고 경고의 손을 들었다."]], [["“Hush!", "\"쉿!"], ["go back to bed; it is all right.", "침대로 돌아가십시오. 다 괜찮습니다."], ["One of us will be here all night.", "우리 가운데 하나가 밤새 여기 있을 겁니다."], ["We don’t mean to take any chances!”", "우리는 어떤 위험도 무릅쓸 생각이 없으니까요!\""]], [["His look and gesture forbade discussion, so I came back and told Mina.", "그의 표정과 몸짓이 더 이상의 논의를 막았으므로, 나는 돌아와 미나에게 그대로 전했다."], ["She sighed and positively a shadow of a smile stole over her poor, pale face as she put her arms round me and said softly:--", "그녀는 한숨을 쉬었고, 가엾고 창백한 얼굴에 그야말로 희미한 미소의 그림자가 스치더니, 두 팔로 나를 감싸안고 부드럽게 말했다."]], [["“Oh, thank God for good brave men!”", "\"오, 선하고 용감한 사람들을 주셔서 하느님 감사합니다!\""], ["With a sigh she sank back again to sleep.", "한숨과 함께 그녀는 다시 잠 속으로 가라앉았다."], ["I write this now as I am not sleepy, though I must try again.", "나는 잠이 오지 않아 지금 이것을 쓰고 있으나, 다시 잠을 청해보아야 한다."]], [["4 October, morning.--Once again during the night I was wakened by Mina.", "10월 4일, 아침. — 밤사이 또 한 번 나는 미나에게 깨워졌다."], ["This time we had all had a good sleep, for the grey of the coming dawn was making the windows into sharp oblongs, and the gas flame was like a speck rather than a disc of light.", "이번에는 우리 모두 단잠을 잤으니, 다가오는 새벽의 잿빛이 창문을 또렷한 직사각형으로 만들고 있었고, 가스 불꽃은 빛의 원반이라기보다 한 점 티끌 같았다."], ["She said to me hurriedly:--", "그녀가 다급하게 나에게 말했다."]], [["“Go, call the Professor.", "\"가서 교수님을 불러와요."], ["I want to see him at once.”", "당장 그분을 뵙고 싶어요.\""]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""], ["I asked.", "내가 물었다."]], [["“I have an idea. I suppose it must have come in the night, and matured without my knowing it.", "\"제게 생각이 있어요. 아마 밤사이에 떠올라, 제가 모르는 새 무르익은 것 같아요."], ["He must hypnotise me before the dawn, and then I shall be able to speak.", "그분이 새벽이 오기 전에 저에게 최면을 걸어야 해요. 그러면 제가 말할 수 있을 거예요."], ["Go quick, dearest; the time is getting close.”", "어서 가요, 여보. 시간이 가까워지고 있어요.\""], ["I went to the door. Dr.", "나는 문으로 갔다."], ["Seward was resting on the mattress, and, seeing me, he sprang to his feet.", "수어드 박사가 매트리스 위에서 쉬고 있다가, 나를 보더니 벌떡 일어섰다."]], [["“Is anything wrong?”", "\"무슨 일이 잘못됐습니까?\""], ["he asked, in alarm.", "그가 놀라서 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아닙니다.\""], ["I replied; “but Mina wants to see Dr. Van Helsing at once.”", "내가 대답했다. \"다만 미나가 반 헬싱 박사님을 당장 뵙고 싶어 합니다.\""]], [["“I will go,”", "\"제가 가지요.\""], ["he said, and hurried into the Professor’s room.", "그는 이렇게 말하고는 교수의 방으로 서둘러 갔다."]], [["In two or three minutes later Van Helsing was in the room in his dressing-gown, and Mr. Morris and Lord Godalming were with Dr. Seward at the door asking questions.", "이삼 분쯤 뒤에 반 헬싱이 실내복 차림으로 방에 들어왔고, 모리스 씨와 고달밍 경은 문간에서 수어드 박사와 함께 이런저런 것을 묻고 있었다."], ["When the Professor saw Mina smile--a positive smile ousted the anxiety of his face; he rubbed his hands as he said:--", "교수는 미나가 미소 짓는 것을 보자 — 그야말로 미소가 그의 얼굴에서 근심을 몰아냈다. 그는 두 손을 비비며 말했다."]], [["“Oh, my dear Madam Mina, this is indeed a change. See!", "\"오, 친애하는 미나 부인, 이것은 정말이지 큰 변화로군요. 보시오!"], ["friend Jonathan, we have got our dear Madam Mina, as of old, back to us to-day!”", "벗 조너선, 우리는 오늘 예전 그대로의 우리 친애하는 미나 부인을 되찾았소!\""], ["Then turning to her, he said, cheerfully: “And what am I do for you?", "그러고는 그녀에게 돌아서서 명랑하게 말했다. \"그래, 제가 당신을 위해 무엇을 하면 되겠소?"], ["For at this hour you do not want me for nothings.”", "이런 시각에 부인이 아무 일도 없이 저를 찾지는 않았을 테니 말이오.\""]], [["“I want you to hypnotise me!”", "\"제게 최면을 걸어주세요!\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["she said. “Do it before the dawn, for I feel that then I can speak, and speak freely.", "\"새벽이 오기 전에 해주세요. 그때라면 제가 말할 수 있을 것 같고, 거리낌 없이 말할 수 있을 것 같아요."], ["Be quick, for the time is short!”", "어서요, 시간이 얼마 없어요!\""], ["Without a word he motioned her to sit up in bed.", "그는 한마디 말도 없이 그녀에게 침대에 일어나 앉으라고 손짓했다."]], [["Looking fixedly at her, he commenced to make passes in front of her, from over the top of her head downward, with each hand in turn.", "그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보며, 그는 그녀의 머리 꼭대기에서부터 아래로 두 손을 번갈아 그녀 앞에서 움직이기 시작했다."], ["Mina gazed at him fixedly for a few minutes, during which my own heart beat like a trip hammer, for I felt that some crisis was at hand.", "미나는 몇 분 동안 그를 뚫어지게 응시했는데, 그동안 나는 어떤 고비가 다가오고 있음을 느껴 내 심장이 망치질하듯 뛰었다."], ["Gradually her eyes closed, and she sat, stock still; only by the gentle heaving of her bosom could one know that she was alive.", "차츰 그녀의 눈이 감겼고, 그녀는 꼼짝 않고 앉아 있었다. 가슴이 가만히 오르내리는 것으로만 그녀가 살아 있음을 알 수 있었다."], ["The Professor made a few more passes and then stopped, and I could see that his forehead was covered with great beads of perspiration.", "교수는 손짓을 몇 번 더 하다가 멈추었는데, 나는 그의 이마가 굵은 땀방울로 뒤덮인 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["Mina opened her eyes; but she did not seem the same woman.", "미나가 눈을 떴다. 그러나 그녀는 같은 여인 같지 않았다."], ["There was a far-away look in her eyes, and her voice had a sad dreaminess which was new to me.", "그녀의 눈에는 아득한 빛이 어려 있었고, 그녀의 목소리에는 나에게 낯선 슬픈 몽롱함이 깃들어 있었다."], ["Raising his hand to impose silence, the Professor motioned to me to bring the others in.", "교수는 침묵을 명하듯 손을 들고, 나에게 다른 이들을 데려오라고 손짓했다."], ["They came on tip-toe, closing the door behind them, and stood at the foot of the bed, looking on. Mina appeared not to see them.", "그들은 발끝으로 들어와 뒤로 문을 닫고는, 침대 발치에 서서 지켜보았다. 미나는 그들을 보지 못하는 듯했다."], ["The stillness was broken by Van Helsing’s voice speaking in a low level tone which would not break the current of her thoughts:--", "그 정적을 반 헬싱의 목소리가 깨뜨렸는데, 그녀의 생각의 흐름을 끊지 않도록 낮고 고른 어조였다."]], [["“Where are you?” The answer came in a neutral way:--", "\"당신은 어디 있소?\" 대답은 무덤덤하게 흘러나왔다."]], [["“I do not know.", "\"모르겠어요."], ["Sleep has no place it can call its own.”", "잠에게는 제 것이라 부를 곳이 없어요.\""], ["For several minutes there was silence.", "몇 분 동안 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["Mina sat rigid, and the Professor stood staring at her fixedly; the rest of us hardly dared to breathe.", "미나는 뻣뻣하게 앉아 있었고, 교수는 그녀를 뚫어지게 바라보며 서 있었다. 나머지 우리는 감히 숨도 제대로 쉬지 못했다."], ["The room was growing lighter; without taking his eyes from Mina’s face, Dr. Van Helsing motioned me to pull up the blind.", "방이 점점 밝아지고 있었다. 반 헬싱 박사는 미나의 얼굴에서 눈을 떼지 않은 채, 나에게 차양을 걷으라고 손짓했다."], ["I did so, and the day seemed just upon us. A red streak shot up, and a rosy light seemed to diffuse itself through the room.", "내가 그렇게 하자 날이 바로 우리에게 들이닥친 듯했다. 붉은 줄기가 솟구쳐 오르고, 장밋빛 빛이 방 안에 퍼지는 듯했다."], ["On the instant the Professor spoke again:--", "그 순간 교수가 다시 입을 열었다."]], [["“Where are you now?”", "\"이제 당신은 어디 있소?\""], ["The answer came dreamily, but with intention; it were as though she were interpreting something.", "대답은 몽롱하게, 그러나 의도를 담고 흘러나왔다. 마치 그녀가 무언가를 해석하고 있는 듯했다."], ["I have heard her use the same tone when reading her shorthand notes.", "나는 그녀가 자신의 속기 기록을 읽을 때 똑같은 어조를 쓰는 것을 들은 적이 있다."]], [["“I do not know.", "\"모르겠어요."], ["It is all strange to me!”", "모든 것이 제게 낯설어요!\""]], [["“What do you see?”", "\"무엇이 보이오?\""]], [["“I can see nothing; it is all dark.”", "\"아무것도 보이지 않아요. 온통 어두워요.\""]], [["“What do you hear?”", "\"무엇이 들리오?\""], ["I could detect the strain in the Professor’s patient voice.", "나는 교수의 인내심 어린 목소리에 깃든 긴장을 알아챌 수 있었다."]], [["“The lapping of water.", "\"물이 찰싹이는 소리요."], ["It is gurgling by, and little waves leap.", "물이 콸콸 흘러 지나가고, 작은 물결이 일어요."], ["I can hear them on the outside.”", "바깥에서 그 소리가 들려요.\""]], [["“Then you are on a ship?” We all looked at each other, trying to glean something each from the other.", "\"그렇다면 당신은 배 위에 있소?\" 우리는 서로를 마주보며, 저마다 상대에게서 무언가를 알아내려 애썼다."], ["We were afraid to think.", "우리는 생각하기조차 두려웠다."], ["The answer came quick:--", "대답은 재빨리 흘러나왔다."]], [["“Oh, yes!”", "\"오, 그래요!\""]], [["“What else do you hear?”", "\"또 무엇이 들리오?\""]], [["“The sound of men stamping overhead as they run about.", "\"머리 위에서 사람들이 이리저리 뛰어다니며 쿵쿵거리는 소리요."], ["There is the creaking of a chain, and the loud tinkle as the check of the capstan falls into the rachet.”", "사슬이 삐걱대는 소리와, 캡스턴의 멈춤쇠가 톱니에 떨어지며 내는 요란한 짤랑 소리도 들려요.\""]], [["“What are you doing?”", "\"당신은 무엇을 하고 있소?\""]], [["“I am still--oh, so still.", "\"저는 가만히 있어요 — 오, 너무도 가만히."], ["It is like death!”", "마치 죽음 같아요!\""], ["The voice faded away into a deep breath as of one sleeping, and the open eyes closed again.", "목소리는 잠든 사람의 것 같은 깊은 숨결 속으로 잦아들었고, 떠 있던 눈이 다시 감겼다."]], [["By this time the sun had risen, and we were all in the full light of day. Dr.", "이 무렵 해가 떠올라, 우리는 모두 한낮의 환한 빛 속에 있었다."], ["Van Helsing placed his hands on Mina’s shoulders, and laid her head down softly on her pillow.", "반 헬싱 박사는 미나의 어깨에 손을 얹고, 그녀의 머리를 부드럽게 베개 위에 누였다."], ["She lay like a sleeping child for a few moments, and then, with a long sigh, awoke and stared in wonder to see us all around her.", "그녀는 잠시 잠든 아이처럼 누워 있다가, 긴 한숨과 함께 깨어나 자기를 둘러싼 우리 모두를 보고는 놀라워하며 응시했다."], ["“Have I been talking in my sleep?” was all she said.", "\"제가 잠결에 말을 했나요?\" 그녀가 한 말은 그뿐이었다."], ["She seemed, however, to know the situation without telling, though she was eager to know what she had told.", "그러나 그녀는 굳이 말하지 않아도 상황을 아는 듯했다. 다만 자기가 무슨 말을 했는지를 몹시 알고 싶어 했다."], ["The Professor repeated the conversation, and she said:--", "교수가 그 대화를 되풀이해 들려주자, 그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Then there is not a moment to lose: it may not be yet too late!” Mr.", "\"그렇다면 한순간도 허비할 수 없어요. 아직 너무 늦지 않았을지도 몰라요!\""], ["Morris and Lord Godalming started for the door but the Professor’s calm voice called them back:--", "모리스 씨와 고달밍 경이 문 쪽으로 나서려 했으나, 교수의 차분한 목소리가 그들을 불러세웠다."]], [["“Stay, my friends.", "\"멈추시오, 친구들."], ["That ship, wherever it was, was weighing anchor whilst she spoke.", "그 배가 어디 있었든, 그녀가 말하는 동안 닻을 올리고 있었소."], ["There are many ships weighing anchor at the moment in your so great Port of London.", "지금 이 순간 당신들의 그 거대한 런던 항구에는 닻을 올리는 배가 숱하게 많소."], ["Which of them is it that you seek?", "그 가운데 당신들이 찾는 것이 어느 배겠소?"], ["God be thanked that we have once again a clue, though whither it may lead us we know not.", "우리가 비록 어디로 이끌릴지는 모르나, 다시금 단서를 얻은 것을 하느님께 감사드리오."], ["We have been blind somewhat; blind after the manner of men, since when we can look back we see what we might have seen looking forward if we had been able to see what we might have seen!", "우리는 다소 눈이 멀어 있었소. 인간의 방식대로 눈이 멀어 있었소. 우리가 돌이켜 볼 수 있을 때라야, 만일 우리가 볼 수 있었을 것을 볼 수만 있었더라면 내다보며 보았을 것을 비로소 보게 되니 말이오!"], ["Alas, but that sentence is a puddle; is it not?", "아아, 한데 이 문장은 진창이로군. 그렇지 않소?"], ["We can know now what was in the Count’s mind, when he seize that money, though Jonathan’s so fierce knife put him in the danger that even he dread.", "우리는 이제 백작이 그 돈을 움켜쥐었을 때 그의 마음속에 무엇이 있었는지 알 수 있소. 조너선의 그 사나운 칼이 그를, 그조차 두려워하는 위험에 빠뜨렸는데도 말이오. 그는 달아날 작정이었소."], ["He meant escape. Hear me, ESCAPE!", "내 말을 들으시오, 달아날 작정이었소!"], ["He saw that with but one earth-box left, and a pack of men following like dogs after a fox, this London was no place for him.", "그는 흙 상자가 단 하나만 남고, 한 무리의 사람들이 여우를 쫓는 개들처럼 뒤를 따르는 마당에, 이 런던이 더 이상 자기 있을 곳이 아님을 알았던 것이오."], ["He have take his last earth-box on board a ship, and he leave the land.", "그는 자신의 마지막 흙 상자를 배에 실었고, 이 땅을 떠나는 것이오."], ["He think to escape, but no!", "그는 달아날 생각이지만, 안 되오!"], ["we follow him. Tally Ho!", "우리가 그를 뒤쫓을 것이오. 탤리 호!"], ["as friend Arthur would say when he put on his red frock! Our old fox is wily; oh!", "벗 아서가 붉은 사냥복을 차려입을 때 외치듯 말이오! 우리의 그 늙은 여우는 교활하오. 오!"], ["so wily, and we must follow with wile.", "어찌나 교활한지, 우리도 꾀로써 뒤쫓아야 하오."], ["I, too, am wily and I think his mind in a little while.", "나 역시 교활하니, 머지않아 그의 마음을 읽어낼 것이오."], ["In meantime we may rest and in peace, for there are waters between us which he do not want to pass, and which he could not if he would--unless the ship were to touch the land, and then only at full or slack tide.", "그동안 우리는 평안히 쉬어도 좋소. 우리 사이에는 그가 건너고 싶어 하지 않는 물이 있고, 설령 건너려 해도 건널 수 없는 물이 있으니 말이오 — 배가 육지에 닿지 않는 한, 그리고 닿더라도 만조나 물이 잔잔할 때에만 가능하니."], ["See, and the sun is just rose, and all day to sunset is to us.", "보시오, 해가 막 떠올랐고, 해질 때까지 온종일이 우리의 것이오."], ["Let us take bath, and dress, and have breakfast which we all need, and which we can eat comfortably since he be not in the same land with us.”", "우리 모두 목욕을 하고, 옷을 갈아입고, 우리 모두에게 필요한 아침식사를 합시다. 그가 우리와 같은 땅에 있지 않으니 이제 편히 먹을 수 있을 것이오.\""], ["Mina looked at him appealingly as she asked:--", "미나가 애원하는 눈빛으로 그를 보며 물었다."]], [["“But why need we seek him further, when he is gone away from us?”", "\"하지만 그가 우리 곁을 떠났는데, 어찌하여 우리가 그를 더 쫓아야 하나요?\""], ["He took her hand and patted it as he replied:--", "그는 그녀의 손을 잡고 다독이며 대답했다."]], [["“Ask me nothings as yet.", "\"아직은 내게 아무것도 묻지 마시오."], ["When we have breakfast, then I answer all questions.”", "아침식사를 하고 나면, 그때 모든 질문에 답하리다.\""], ["He would say no more, and we separated to dress.", "그는 더는 말하려 하지 않았고, 우리는 옷을 갈아입으러 흩어졌다."]], [["After breakfast Mina repeated her question.", "아침식사 후에 미나가 자기 물음을 되풀이했다."], ["He looked at her gravely for a minute and then said sorrowfully:--", "그는 잠시 그녀를 엄숙하게 바라보다가 슬프게 말했다."]], [["“Because my dear, dear Madam Mina, now more than ever must we find him even if we have to follow him to the jaws of Hell!”", "\"왜냐하면, 나의 친애하고 친애하는 미나 부인, 이제는 그 어느 때보다도 우리가 그를 찾아내야 하기 때문이오. 설령 지옥의 아가리까지 그를 뒤쫓아야 한다 해도 말이오!\""], ["She grew paler as she asked faintly:--", "그녀가 점점 창백해지며 가냘프게 물었다."]], [["“Why?”", "\"왜요?\""]], [["“Because,”", "\"왜냐하면,\""], ["he answered solemnly, “he can live for centuries, and you are but mortal woman.", "그가 엄숙하게 대답했다. \"그는 수백 년을 살 수 있으나, 당신은 한낱 필멸의 여인이기 때문이오."], ["Time is now to be dreaded--since once he put that mark upon your throat.”", "그가 한번 당신의 목에 그 표를 남긴 이상, 이제 시간이 두려워해야 할 것이 된 것이오.\""]], [["I was just in time to catch her as she fell forward in a faint.", "나는 마침 때맞춰 그녀가 앞으로 까무러쳐 쓰러지는 것을 받아 안았다."]], [["This to Jonathan Harker.", "수어드 박사의 축음기 일기, 반 헬싱이 구술함"]], [["You are to stay with your dear Madam Mina.", "조너선 하커에게. 당신은 사랑하는 미나 부인 곁에 머물러 있어야 하오."], ["We shall go to make our search--if I can call it so, for it is not search but knowing, and we seek confirmation only.", "우리는 수색에 — 이것을 수색이라 부를 수 있다면 말이오, 사실 이것은 수색이 아니라 확인이며, 우리는 다만 확증을 구할 뿐이니 — 나서려 하오."], ["But do you stay and take care of her to-day.", "그러나 당신은 머물러 오늘 그녀를 돌보시오."], ["This is your best and most holiest office.", "이것이 당신의 가장 훌륭하고 가장 성스러운 소임이오."], ["This day nothing can find him here. Let me tell you that so you will know what we four know already, for I have tell them.", "오늘은 어떤 것도 그를 이곳에서 찾을 수 없을 것이오. 당신이 알도록 일러두겠으니, 그러면 우리 넷이 이미 아는 바를 당신도 알게 될 것이오."], ["He, our enemy, have gone away; he have gone back to his Castle in Transylvania.", "내가 그들에게 이야기했기 때문이오. 우리의 적인 그는 떠나버렸소. 그는 트란실바니아에 있는 자기 성으로 돌아갔소."], ["I know it so well, as if a great hand of fire wrote it on the wall.", "나는 마치 거대한 불의 손이 벽에 그것을 적어놓기라도 한 듯 그것을 잘 알고 있소."], ["He have prepare for this in some way, and that last earth-box was ready to ship somewheres.", "그는 어떤 식으로든 이것을 준비해두었으며, 그 마지막 흙 상자는 어딘가로 실어 보낼 채비가 되어 있었소."], ["For this he took the money; for this he hurry at the last, lest we catch him before the sun go down.", "이를 위해 그는 돈을 챙겼고, 이를 위해 그는 막판에 서둘렀소, 해가 지기 전에 우리가 그를 붙잡지 못하도록 말이오."], ["It was his last hope, save that he might hide in the tomb that he think poor Miss Lucy, being as he thought like him, keep open to him.", "그것이 그의 마지막 희망이었소, 다만 그가 가엾은 루시 양 — 그가 자기처럼 되었다고 여긴 — 이 그를 위해 열어두었으리라 생각한 무덤 속에 숨을 수도 있었던 것을 제하면 말이오."], ["But there was not of time.", "그러나 시간이 없었소."], ["When that fail he make straight for his last resource--his last earth-work I might say did I wish double entente.", "그것이 실패하자 그는 곧장 자신의 마지막 수단으로 — 만일 내가 이중의 뜻을 노린다면 마지막 토루(土壘)라 말할 수도 있겠소 — 향했소."], ["He is clever, oh, so clever!", "그는 영리하오, 오, 참으로 영리하오!"], ["he know that his game here was finish; and so he decide he go back home.", "그는 여기서의 자기 노름이 끝났음을 알았고, 그래서 본국으로 돌아가기로 결심한 것이오."], ["He find ship going by the route he came, and he go in it.", "그는 자기가 온 항로를 따라 가는 배를 찾아내어 그것을 탄 것이오."], ["We go off now to find what ship, and whither bound; when we have discover that, we come back and tell you all.", "우리는 이제 어떤 배인지, 어디로 향하는지 알아내러 떠나오. 그것을 알아내고 나면 돌아와 당신에게 모두 이야기하겠소."], ["Then we will comfort you and poor dear Madam Mina with new hope.", "그러면 우리는 새로운 희망으로 당신과 가엾고 사랑스러운 미나 부인을 위로하겠소."], ["For it will be hope when you think it over: that all is not lost.", "곰곰이 생각해보면 그것은 희망이 될 것이오. 모든 것이 잃어버린 것은 아니라는 희망 말이오."], ["This very creature that we pursue, he take hundreds of years to get so far as London; and yet in one day, when we know of the disposal of him we drive him out.", "우리가 쫓는 바로 이 피조물은 런던에 이르기까지 수백 년이 걸렸소. 그런데도 단 하루 만에, 그의 처치 방법을 알아낸 우리는 그를 몰아냈소."], ["He is finite, though he is powerful to do much harm and suffers not as we do.", "그는 많은 해악을 끼칠 만큼 강하고 우리처럼 고통받지 않지만, 유한한 존재요."], ["But we are strong, each in our purpose; and we are all more strong together.", "그러나 우리는 저마다 자기 목적 안에서 강하며, 함께라면 더욱 강하오."], ["Take heart afresh, dear husband of Madam Mina.", "다시 용기를 내시오, 미나 부인의 사랑하는 남편이여."], ["This battle is but begun, and in the end we shall win--so sure as that God sits on high to watch over His children.", "이 싸움은 이제 막 시작되었을 뿐이며, 끝내 우리가 이길 것이오 — 하느님께서 높은 곳에 앉으시어 당신의 자녀들을 지켜보심이 확실한 것만큼이나 확실하게."], ["Therefore be of much comfort till we return.", "그러니 우리가 돌아올 때까지 마음을 굳게 가지시오."]], [["Jonathan Harker’s Journal.", "반 헬싱. 조너선 하커의 일기."]], [["4 October.--When I read to Mina, Van Helsing’s message in the phonograph, the poor girl brightened up considerably.", "10월 4일. — 내가 미나에게 축음기에 담긴 반 헬싱의 전언을 읽어주자, 가엾은 그녀의 얼굴이 눈에 띄게 밝아졌다."], ["Already the certainty that the Count is out of the country has given her comfort; and comfort is strength to her.", "백작이 이 나라를 떠났다는 확신만으로도 그녀는 이미 위안을 얻었고, 위안은 그녀에게 힘이 된다."], ["For my own part, now that his horrible danger is not face to face with us, it seems almost impossible to believe in it.", "나로서는, 그의 무시무시한 위험이 우리와 정면으로 맞서고 있지 않은 지금, 그것을 믿기란 거의 불가능하게 여겨진다."], ["Even my own terrible experiences in Castle Dracula seem like a long-forgotten dream.", "드라큘라 성에서 겪은 나 자신의 끔찍한 경험조차 오래전에 잊힌 꿈처럼 느껴진다."], ["Here in the crisp autumn air in the bright sunlight----", "이곳, 상쾌한 가을 공기 속, 밝은 햇살 아래에서는——"]], [["Alas!", "아아!"], ["how can I disbelieve!", "어찌 믿지 않을 수 있겠는가!"], ["In the midst of my thought my eye fell on the red scar on my poor darling’s white forehead.", "생각에 잠겨 있던 와중에 내 시선이 가엾은 내 사랑의 흰 이마 위 붉은 흉터에 닿았다."], ["Whilst that lasts, there can be no disbelief.", "그것이 남아 있는 한, 믿지 않을 수는 없다."], ["And afterwards the very memory of it will keep faith crystal clear.", "그리고 훗날에도 그것의 기억 자체가 믿음을 수정처럼 맑게 지켜줄 것이다."], ["Mina and I fear to be idle, so we have been over all the diaries again and again.", "미나와 나는 한가로이 있기가 두려워, 모든 일기를 거듭거듭 다시 살펴보았다."], ["Somehow, although the reality seems greater each time, the pain and the fear seem less.", "어쩐 일인지 그 현실이 매번 더 크게 다가오는데도, 고통과 두려움은 덜해지는 것 같다."], ["There is something of a guiding purpose manifest throughout, which is comforting.", "전체에 걸쳐 어떤 이끄는 목적 같은 것이 드러나 있어, 그것이 위안이 된다."], ["Mina says that perhaps we are the instruments of ultimate good.", "미나는 어쩌면 우리가 궁극의 선을 위한 도구일지도 모른다고 말한다."], ["It may be! I shall try to think as she does.", "그럴지도 모른다! 나도 그녀처럼 생각해보려 한다."], ["We have never spoken to each other yet of the future.", "우리는 아직 서로 미래에 관해 한마디도 나누지 않았다."], ["It is better to wait till we see the Professor and the others after their investigations.", "교수님과 다른 이들이 조사를 마치고 돌아오는 것을 본 뒤까지 기다리는 편이 낫다."]], [["The day is running by more quickly than I ever thought a day could run for me again.", "하루가, 내게 다시 이렇게 흘러갈 수 있으리라 생각해본 적 없을 만큼 빠르게 지나간다."], ["It is now three o’clock.", "지금은 세 시다."]], [["Mina Harker’s Journal.", "미나 하커의 일기."]], [["5 October, 5 p. m.--Our meeting for report.", "10월 5일, 오후 5시. — 보고를 위한 우리의 모임."], ["Present: Professor Van Helsing, Lord Godalming, Dr. Seward, Mr. Quincey Morris, Jonathan Harker, Mina Harker.", "참석자: 반 헬싱 교수, 고달밍 경, 수어드 박사, 퀸시 모리스 씨, 조너선 하커, 미나 하커."]], [["Dr. Van Helsing described what steps were taken during the day to discover on what boat and whither bound Count Dracula made his escape:--", "반 헬싱 박사가 드라큘라 백작이 어떤 배로 어디를 향해 달아났는지를 알아내기 위해 낮 동안 취한 조치들을 설명했다."]], [["“As I knew that he wanted to get back to Transylvania, I felt sure that he must go by the Danube mouth; or by somewhere in the Black Sea, since by that way he come.", "\"그가 트란실바니아로 돌아가려 한다는 것을 알았으니, 나는 그가 분명 도나우 강 어귀로, 혹은 흑해 어딘가로 가야 하리라고 확신했소. 그가 온 길이 바로 그 길이었기 때문이오."], ["It was a dreary blank that was before us.", "우리 앞에 놓인 것은 암담한 공백이었소."], ["Omne ignotum pro magnifico; and so with heavy hearts we start to find what ships leave for the Black Sea last night.", "알려지지 않은 것은 모두 대단해 보이는 법이오. 그래서 우리는 무거운 마음으로, 어젯밤 흑해로 떠난 배가 무엇인지 알아내러 나섰소."], ["He was in sailing ship, since Madam Mina tell of sails being set.", "미나 부인이 돛이 펼쳐졌다고 말했으니 그는 범선에 타고 있었소."], ["These not so important as to go in your list of the shipping in the Times, and so we go, by suggestion of Lord Godalming, to your Lloyd’s, where are note of all ships that sail, however so small.", "이런 배들은 타임스의 선박 목록에 실릴 만큼 중요하지 않으므로, 우리는 고달밍 경의 제안에 따라 당신들의 로이즈로 갔소. 그곳에는 아무리 작은 배라도 출항하는 모든 배의 기록이 있소."], ["There we find that only one Black-Sea-bound ship go out with the tide.", "거기서 우리는 조수에 맞춰 출항한 흑해행 배가 단 한 척뿐임을 알아냈소."], ["She is the Czarina Catherine, and she sail from Doolittle’s Wharf for Varna, and thence on to other parts and up the Danube.", "그 배는 차리나 캐서린호로, 둘리틀 부두에서 바르나로 떠나, 거기서 다시 다른 지역들로, 또 도나우 강을 거슬러 올라간다고 했소. '오호!'"], ["‘Soh!’ said I, ‘this is the ship whereon is the Count.’ So off we go to Doolittle’s Wharf, and there we find a man in an office of wood so small that the man look bigger than the office.", "하고 나는 말했소. '바로 이 배에 백작이 타고 있구나.' 그래서 우리는 둘리틀 부두로 떠났는데, 거기서 우리는 사무실이 어찌나 작은지 사람이 사무실보다 더 커 보이는 그런 나무 사무실 안의 한 사내를 만났소."], ["From him we inquire of the goings of the Czarina Catherine.", "우리는 그에게 차리나 캐서린호의 동정을 물었소."], ["He swear much, and he red face and loud of voice, but he good fellow all the same; and when Quincey give him something from his pocket which crackle as he roll it up, and put it in a so small bag which he have hid deep in his clothing, he still better fellow and humble servant to us.", "그는 욕을 많이 했고, 얼굴이 붉고 목소리가 컸으나, 그래도 좋은 친구였소. 그리고 퀸시가 주머니에서 무언가를, 둘둘 말 때 바스락 소리가 나는 것을 꺼내어 그가 옷 깊숙이 숨겨둔 아주 작은 주머니에 넣어주자, 그는 한층 더 좋은 친구이자 우리의 겸손한 종복이 되었소."], ["He come with us, and ask many men who are rough and hot; these be better fellows too when they have been no more thirsty.", "그는 우리와 함께 와서, 거칠고 성마른 사내들 여럿에게 물어주었소. 이들 또한 목이 덜 마르게 되자 더 좋은 친구들이 되었소."], ["They say much of blood and bloom, and of others which I comprehend not, though I guess what they mean; but nevertheless they tell us all things which we want to know.", "그들은 피니 꽃이니 하는 말을 많이 했고, 내가 알아듣지 못하는 다른 말들도 했으나, 무슨 뜻인지는 짐작이 갔소. 그러나 어쨌든 그들은 우리가 알고자 하는 모든 것을 말해주었소."]], [["“They make known to us among them, how last afternoon at about five o’clock comes a man so hurry.", "\"그들은 자기들끼리, 어제 오후 다섯 시쯤에 어떤 사내가 몹시 서둘러 왔다는 것을 우리에게 알려주었소."], ["A tall man, thin and pale, with high nose and teeth so white, and eyes that seem to be burning.", "키가 크고, 야위고 창백하며, 코가 높고 이가 아주 희며, 눈은 타오르는 듯한 사내였다고 하오."], ["That he be all in black, except that he have a hat of straw which suit not him or the time.", "그가 온통 검은 옷차림이었는데, 다만 그에게도 그 계절에도 어울리지 않는 밀짚모자를 쓰고 있었다는 것이오."], ["That he scatter his money in making quick inquiry as to what ship sails for the Black Sea and for where.", "어떤 배가 흑해로 어디를 향해 떠나는지 급히 캐물으며 그는 돈을 마구 뿌렸다고 하오."], ["Some took him to the office and then to the ship, where he will not go aboard but halt at shore end of gang-plank, and ask that the captain come to him.", "몇몇이 그를 사무실로, 그다음 배로 안내했는데, 그는 배에 오르려 하지 않고 건널 판자의 뭍 쪽 끝에 멈춰 서서 선장을 데려와달라고 청했소."], ["The captain come, when told that he will be pay well; and though he swear much at the first he agree to term.", "보수를 두둑이 받게 되리라는 말을 듣고 선장이 왔고, 처음에는 욕을 많이 했으나 그는 조건에 응했소."], ["Then the thin man go and some one tell him where horse and cart can be hired.", "그러자 그 야윈 사내는 갔고, 누군가 그에게 말과 수레를 어디서 빌릴 수 있는지 일러주었소."], ["He go there and soon he come again, himself driving cart on which a great box; this he himself lift down, though it take several to put it on truck for the ship.", "그는 그리로 갔다가 곧 다시 왔는데, 커다란 상자 하나가 실린 수레를 손수 몰고 있었소. 그것을 배의 짐수레에 올리는 데에는 여럿이 달려들어야 했는데도, 그는 그것을 손수 내렸소."], ["He give much talk to captain as to how and where his box is to be place; but the captain like it not and swear at him in many tongues, and tell him that if he like he can come and see where it shall be.", "그는 자기 상자를 어떻게 어디에 두어야 하는지 선장에게 한참 떠들어댔으나, 선장은 그것이 못마땅하여 여러 나라 말로 그에게 욕을 하며, 원한다면 와서 어디에 둘지 직접 보라고 했소."], ["But he say ‘no’; that he come not yet, for that he have much to do.", "그러나 그는 '아니'라고, 아직은 가지 않겠다고, 할 일이 많기 때문이라고 했소."], ["Whereupon the captain tell him that he had better be quick--with blood--for that his ship will leave the place--of blood--before the turn of the tide--with blood.", "그러자 선장은 그에게, 서두르는 편이 좋을 거라고 — 피로 물든 말로 — 자기 배가 조수가 바뀌기 전에 — 피로 물든 — 그 자리를 떠날 것이기 때문이라고 — 피로 물든 — 말했소."], ["Then the thin man smile and say that of course he must go when he think fit; but he will be surprise if he go quite so soon.", "그러자 그 야윈 사내는 미소 지으며, 물론 자기는 적당하다고 여길 때 가야겠으나, 그렇게 빨리 가게 된다면 놀랄 거라고 했소."], ["The captain swear again, polyglot, and the thin man make him bow, and thank him, and say that he will so far intrude on his kindness as to come aboard before the sailing.", "선장은 다시 여러 나라 말로 욕을 했고, 그 야윈 사내는 그에게 허리를 굽혀 인사하며, 고맙다고, 출항 전에 배에 오르는 정도로 그의 친절에 폐를 끼치겠다고 했소."], ["Final the captain, more red than ever, and in more tongues tell him that he doesn’t want no Frenchmen--with bloom upon them and also with blood--in his ship--with blood on her also.", "마침내 선장은 그 어느 때보다 얼굴이 붉어져, 더 많은 나라 말로 그에게, 자기 배에 — 또한 피로 물든 — 프랑스놈은 — 피니 꽃이니 들러붙고 또한 피로 물든 — 필요 없다고 말했소."], ["And so, after asking where there might be close at hand a ship where he might purchase ship forms, he departed.", "그래서 그는 가까운 곳에 선박 서류를 살 수 있는 배가 어디 있을지 물어본 뒤 떠나갔소."]], [["“No one knew where he went ‘or bloomin’ well cared,’ as they said, for they had something else to think of--well with blood again; for it soon became apparent to all that the Czarina Catherine would not sail as was expected.", "\"아무도 그가 어디로 갔는지 몰랐고 '눈곱만큼도 신경 쓰지 않았소', 그들의 말마따나, 그들에겐 달리 생각할 일이 있었기 때문이오 — 또다시 피로 물든 말로. 머지않아 차리나 캐서린호가 예정대로 출항하지 못하리라는 것이 모두에게 분명해졌기 때문이오."], ["A thin mist began to creep up from the river, and it grew, and grew; till soon a dense fog enveloped the ship and all around her.", "강에서 옅은 안개가 기어오르기 시작하더니, 점점 짙어지고 또 짙어져, 머지않아 짙은 안개가 그 배와 주위 일대를 온통 뒤덮었소."], ["The captain swore polyglot--very polyglot--polyglot with bloom and blood; but he could do nothing.", "선장은 여러 나라 말로 — 아주 여러 나라 말로 — 피니 꽃이니 피니 하는 여러 나라 말로 욕을 했으나, 그는 아무것도 할 수 없었소."], ["The water rose and rose; and he began to fear that he would lose the tide altogether.", "물은 자꾸자꾸 차올랐고, 그는 조수를 아예 놓쳐버릴까 두려워하기 시작했소."], ["He was in no friendly mood, when just at full tide, the thin man came up the gang-plank again and asked to see where his box had been stowed.", "그가 영 심사가 사나운 차에, 마침 만조 때 그 야윈 사내가 다시 건널 판자를 올라와 자기 상자가 어디에 실렸는지 보게 해달라고 청했소."], ["Then the captain replied that he wished that he and his box--old and with much bloom and blood--were in hell.", "그러자 선장은, 그놈과 그놈의 상자가 — 낡아빠지고 피니 꽃이니 잔뜩 묻은 — 지옥에나 떨어졌으면 좋겠다고 대꾸했소."], ["But the thin man did not be offend, and went down with the mate and saw where it was place, and came up and stood awhile on deck in fog.", "그러나 그 야윈 사내는 화내지 않고, 일등항해사와 함께 내려가 그것이 놓인 자리를 보고는, 다시 올라와 안개 속 갑판에 한동안 서 있었소."], ["He must have come off by himself, for none notice him.", "그는 혼자 빠져나간 게 분명한데, 아무도 그를 알아채지 못했기 때문이오."], ["Indeed they thought not of him; for soon the fog begin to melt away, and all was clear again.", "사실 그들은 그를 안중에도 두지 않았소. 머지않아 안개가 걷히기 시작하더니, 모든 것이 다시 맑아졌기 때문이오."], ["My friends of the thirst and the language that was of bloom and blood laughed, as they told how the captain’s swears exceeded even his usual polyglot, and was more than ever full of picturesque, when on questioning other mariners who were on movement up and down on the river that hour, he found that few of them had seen any of fog at all, except where it lay round the wharf.", "목마름과 피니 꽃이니 하는 말투의 내 친구들은, 그때 강 위를 오르내리던 다른 뱃사람들에게 물어보았더니 부두 둘레에 깔린 것을 빼고는 안개를 본 사람이 거의 없었음을 선장이 알게 되었을 때, 그의 욕설이 평소의 여러 나라 말투마저 능가했고 그 어느 때보다 그림처럼 생생했다는 것을 이야기하며 웃었소."], ["However, the ship went out on the ebb tide; and was doubtless by morning far down the river mouth.", "어쨌든 그 배는 썰물을 타고 나갔고, 아침이면 틀림없이 강어귀 저 아래로 멀리 내려가 있었을 거요."], ["She was by then, when they told us, well out to sea.", "그들이 우리에게 이야기해주던 무렵에는 이미 바다로 한참 나아가 있었소."]], [["“And so, my dear Madam Mina, it is that we have to rest for a time, for our enemy is on the sea, with the fog at his command, on his way to the Danube mouth.", "\"그리하여, 나의 사랑하는 미나 부인, 우리는 한동안 쉬어야 하는 처지가 되었소. 우리의 적이 안개를 마음대로 부리며 바다 위에서 도나우 강어귀로 가는 길에 있기 때문이오."], ["To sail a ship takes time, go she never so quick; and when we start we go on land more quick, and we meet him there.", "배를 모는 데에는, 아무리 빠르게 간다 해도 시간이 걸리오. 그리고 우리가 출발하면 우리는 육로로 더 빨리 가서, 거기서 그를 만나게 되오."], ["Our best hope is to come on him when in the box between sunrise and sunset; for then he can make no struggle, and we may deal with him as we should.", "우리의 가장 큰 희망은 해돋이와 해넘이 사이에 그가 상자 안에 있을 때 그를 덮치는 것이오. 그때라면 그는 아무 저항도 할 수 없고, 우리는 마땅한 대로 그를 처치할 수 있을 것이오."], ["There are days for us, in which we can make ready our plan.", "우리에게는 계획을 준비할 며칠이 있소."], ["We know all about where he go; for we have seen the owner of the ship, who have shown us invoices and all papers that can be.", "우리는 그가 어디로 가는지 모두 알고 있소. 우리가 그 배의 선주를 만났는데, 그가 송장과 그 밖의 모든 서류를 보여주었기 때문이오."], ["The box we seek is to be landed in Varna, and to be given to an agent, one Ristics who will there present his credentials; and so our merchant friend will have done his part.", "우리가 찾는 상자는 바르나에서 하역되어, 거기서 신임장을 제시할 대리인 리스틱스라는 자에게 인도될 것이오. 그러면 우리의 상인 친구는 자기 몫을 다한 셈이 되오."], ["When he ask if there be any wrong, for that so, he can telegraph and have inquiry made at Varna, we say ‘no’; for what is to be done is not for police or of the customs.", "그가 무슨 잘못된 일이라도 있느냐고, 그렇다면 전보를 쳐 바르나에서 조사하게 할 수 있다고 물었을 때, 우리는 '아니'라고 했소. 해야 할 일이 경찰이나 세관의 소관이 아니기 때문이오."], ["It must be done by us alone and in our own way.”", "그것은 오직 우리만의 손으로, 우리 나름의 방식으로 행해야 하오.\""]], [["When Dr. Van Helsing had done speaking, I asked him if he were certain that the Count had remained on board the ship.", "반 헬싱 박사가 말을 마쳤을 때, 나는 그에게 백작이 그 배에 그대로 타고 있다는 것을 확신하느냐고 물었다. 그는 답했다."], ["He replied: “We have the best proof of that: your own evidence, when in the hypnotic trance this morning.”", "\"우리에게는 그것을 입증하는 가장 좋은 증거가 있소. 오늘 아침 최면 상태에서 나온 당신 자신의 증언 말이오.\""], ["I asked him again if it were really necessary that they should pursue the Count, for oh!", "나는 다시 그에게, 정말로 그들이 백작을 추적해야만 하느냐고 물었다. 아아!"], ["I dread Jonathan leaving me, and I know that he would surely go if the others went.", "나는 조너선이 나를 떠나는 것이 두렵고, 다른 이들이 가면 그도 분명 가리라는 것을 알기 때문이다."], ["He answered in growing passion, at first quietly.", "그는 점점 격해지는 감정으로, 처음에는 차분하게 답했다."], ["As he went on, however, he grew more angry and more forceful, till in the end we could not but see wherein was at least some of that personal dominance which made him so long a master amongst men:--", "그러나 말을 이어갈수록 그는 더 노여워지고 더 힘이 실려, 마침내 우리는 그가 그토록 오래 사람들 사이에서 지도자로 군림하게 한 그 개인적 위엄의 적어도 일부를 보지 않을 수 없었다."]], [["“Yes, it is necessary--necessary--necessary!", "\"그렇소, 그것은 필요하오 — 필요하오 — 필요하오!"], ["For your sake in the first, and then for the sake of humanity.", "첫째로는 당신을 위해서, 다음으로는 인류를 위해서요."], ["This monster has done much harm already, in the narrow scope where he find himself, and in the short time when as yet he was only as a body groping his so small measure in darkness and not knowing.", "이 괴물은, 제 한정된 활동 범위 안에서, 그리고 아직 어둠 속에서 자신의 그 보잘것없는 분수를 더듬으며 아무것도 모르던 한낱 육신에 불과했던 그 짧은 시간 동안에도, 이미 많은 해악을 끼쳤소."], ["All this have I told these others; you, my dear Madam Mina, will learn it in the phonograph of my friend John, or in that of your husband.", "이 모든 것을 나는 이 다른 이들에게 이야기했소. 당신은, 나의 사랑하는 미나 부인, 내 친구 존의 축음기에서, 혹은 당신 남편의 축음기에서 그것을 알게 될 것이오."], ["I have told them how the measure of leaving his own barren land--barren of peoples--and coming to a new land where life of man teems till they are like the multitude of standing corn, was the work of centuries.", "나는 그들에게, 사람도 없이 메마른 자신의 땅을 떠나, 사람의 생명이 들판에 늘어선 곡식의 무리처럼 가득 들어찬 새 땅으로 오는 그 일이 수백 년에 걸친 작업이었다는 것을 이야기했소."], ["Were another of the Un-Dead, like him, to try to do what he has done, perhaps not all the centuries of the world that have been, or that will be, could aid him.", "그와 같은 또 다른 불사자(不死者)가 그가 한 일을 시도하려 한다면, 어쩌면 지나간 모든 세월도, 앞으로 올 모든 세월도 그를 돕지 못할 것이오."], ["With this one, all the forces of nature that are occult and deep and strong must have worked together in some wondrous way.", "이자의 경우에는, 비밀스럽고 깊고 강한 온갖 자연의 힘이 어떤 경이로운 방식으로 함께 작용했음이 분명하오."], ["The very place, where he have been alive, Un-Dead for all these centuries, is full of strangeness of the geologic and chemical world.", "그가 이 수백 년 동안 불사의 몸으로 살아온 바로 그곳은 지질학적·화학적 세계의 기이함으로 가득하오."], ["There are deep caverns and fissures that reach none know whither.", "그곳에는 아무도 그 끝을 모르는 깊은 동굴과 균열이 있소."], ["There have been volcanoes, some of whose openings still send out waters of strange properties, and gases that kill or make to vivify.", "화산들이 있었는데, 그중 어떤 분화구는 아직도 기이한 성질의 물과, 죽이거나 되살리는 기체를 내뿜고 있소."], ["Doubtless, there is something magnetic or electric in some of these combinations of occult forces which work for physical life in strange way; and in himself were from the first some great qualities.", "의심할 바 없이, 이런 비밀스러운 힘들의 어떤 결합 속에는 기이한 방식으로 육신의 생명에 작용하는 자성이나 전기 같은 무언가가 있을 것이오. 그리고 그 자신 안에는 처음부터 어떤 위대한 자질들이 있었소."], ["In a hard and warlike time he was celebrate that he have more iron nerve, more subtle brain, more braver heart, than any man.", "모질고 전쟁이 잦던 시대에 그는 어떤 사내보다도 더 강철 같은 담력과, 더 치밀한 두뇌와, 더 용맹한 심장을 지녔다 하여 이름을 떨쳤소."], ["In him some vital principle have in strange way found their utmost; and as his body keep strong and grow and thrive, so his brain grow too.", "그의 안에서는 어떤 생명의 원리가 기이한 방식으로 그 극한에 이르렀고, 그의 육신이 강건함을 지키며 자라고 번성한 것처럼 그의 두뇌 또한 자랐소."], ["All this without that diabolic aid which is surely to him; for it have to yield to the powers that come from, and are, symbolic of good.", "이 모든 것이, 분명 그에게 따르는 그 악마적 힘 없이도 이루어진 것이오. 그 힘은 선을 상징하며 선에서 나오는 권능 앞에 굴복할 수밖에 없기 때문이오."], ["And now this is what he is to us. He have infect you--oh, forgive me, my dear, that I must say such; but it is for good of you that I speak.", "그리고 이제 그가 우리에게 어떤 존재인지를 말하겠소. 그는 당신을 감염시켰소 — 오, 용서하시오, 나의 부인, 내가 이런 말을 해야만 하는 것을."], ["He infect you in such wise, that even if he do no more, you have only to live--to live in your own old, sweet way; and so in time, death, which is of man’s common lot and with God’s sanction, shall make you like to him.", "그러나 이것은 당신을 위해 하는 말이오. 그는 당신을, 설령 그가 더 이상 아무것도 하지 않는다 해도, 당신이 그저 살아가기만 하면 — 당신의 옛 다정한 방식대로 살아가기만 하면 — 그리하여 머지않아, 인간 공통의 운명이며 하느님의 재가를 받은 죽음이 당신을 그와 같이 만들어버릴, 그런 식으로 감염시켰소."], ["This must not be! We have sworn together that it must not.", "이런 일이 있어서는 안 되오! 우리는 그런 일이 있어서는 안 된다고 함께 맹세했소."], ["Thus are we ministers of God’s own wish: that the world, and men for whom His Son die, will not be given over to monsters, whose very existence would defame Him.", "그리하여 우리는 하느님 그분의 뜻을 받드는 종이오. 곧, 세상이, 그리고 그분의 아드님께서 위해 돌아가신 사람들이, 그 존재 자체가 그분을 욕되게 할 괴물들에게 내맡겨지지 않게 하라는 뜻 말이오."], ["He have allowed us to redeem one soul already, and we go out as the old knights of the Cross to redeem more.", "그분은 우리가 이미 한 영혼을 구원하도록 허락하셨고, 우리는 더 많은 영혼을 구원하러 옛 십자가의 기사들처럼 나아가오."], ["Like them we shall travel towards the sunrise; and like them, if we fall, we fall in good cause.”", "그들처럼 우리는 해돋이를 향해 나아갈 것이며, 그들처럼, 우리가 쓰러진다면 우리는 대의(大義)를 위해 쓰러지는 것이오.\""], ["He paused and I said:--", "그가 말을 멈추자 나는 말했다."]], [["“But will not the Count take his rebuff wisely? Since he has been driven from England, will he not avoid it, as a tiger does the village from which he has been hunted?”", "\"하지만 백작은 그 좌절을 현명하게 받아들이지 않을까요? 영국에서 쫓겨난 이상, 호랑이가 자기를 몰아낸 마을을 피하듯이 그도 영국을 피하지 않을까요?\""]], [["“Aha!”", "\"아하!\""], ["he said, “your simile of the tiger good, for me, and I shall adopt him.", "그가 말했다. \"당신의 호랑이 비유가 내게 안성맞춤이오. 나도 그것을 가져다 쓰겠소."], ["Your man-eater, as they of India call the tiger who has once tasted blood of the human, care no more for the other prey, but prowl unceasing till he get him.", "인도에서 식인호(食人虎)라 부르는, 한번 사람의 피를 맛본 호랑이는 더 이상 다른 먹이에는 관심을 두지 않고, 그를 손에 넣을 때까지 끊임없이 어슬렁거리며 노리오."], ["This that we hunt from our village is a tiger, too, a man-eater, and he never cease to prowl.", "우리가 우리 마을에서 몰아낸 이자 또한 호랑이요, 식인호이며, 그는 결코 어슬렁거리기를 멈추지 않소."], ["Nay, in himself he is not one to retire and stay afar.", "아니, 그 자신이 물러나 멀리 머물러 있을 자가 아니오."], ["In his life, his living life, he go over the Turkey frontier and attack his enemy on his own ground; he be beaten back, but did he stay? No!", "그의 생애에, 그가 살아 있던 삶에서 그는 터키 국경을 넘어가 적의 본거지에서 그를 공격했소. 그는 격퇴당했으나, 그가 가만히 있었소? 아니오!"], ["He come again, and again, and again.", "그는 다시, 또다시, 또다시 왔소."], ["Look at his persistence and endurance.", "그의 끈질김과 인내를 보시오."], ["With the child-brain that was to him he have long since conceive the idea of coming to a great city.", "그에게 있던 그 어린아이 같은 두뇌로도 그는 오래전에 큰 도시로 온다는 생각을 품었소."], ["What does he do?", "그가 무엇을 하오?"], ["He find out the place of all the world most of promise for him.", "그는 자기에게 가장 가망이 있는 세상의 모든 곳을 알아내오."], ["Then he deliberately set himself down to prepare for the task.", "그런 다음 그는 그 과업을 준비하려고 신중하게 자리를 잡소."], ["He find in patience just how is his strength, and what are his powers.", "그는 인내하며 자기 힘이 얼마나 되는지, 자기 권능이 무엇인지 정확히 알아내오."], ["He study new tongues.", "그는 새로운 언어를 익히오."], ["He learn new social life; new environment of old ways, the politic, the law, the finance, the science, the habit of a new land and a new people who have come to be since he was.", "그는 새로운 사교 생활을, 옛 방식의 새로운 환경을, 정치를, 법을, 재정을, 과학을, 자기가 있은 뒤로 생겨난 새 땅과 새 사람들의 습속을 배우오."], ["His glimpse that he have had, whet his appetite only and enkeen his desire.", "그가 잠깐 엿본 것이 그의 식욕을 돋우고 그의 욕망을 더욱 날카롭게 했을 뿐이오."], ["Nay, it help him to grow as to his brain; for it all prove to him how right he was at the first in his surmises.", "아니, 그것은 그가 두뇌에서 자라도록 돕기까지 했소. 그것은 그에게 처음의 그의 추측이 얼마나 옳았는지를 죄다 증명해주었기 때문이오."], ["He have done this alone; all alone!", "그는 이것을 혼자서 해냈소. 완전히 혼자서!"], ["from a ruin tomb in a forgotten land.", "잊힌 땅의 폐허가 된 무덤에서부터 말이오."], ["What more may he not do when the greater world of thought is open to him.", "더 위대한 사상의 세계가 그에게 열린다면 그가 무엇인들 못 하겠소."], ["He that can smile at death, as we know him; who can flourish in the midst of diseases that kill off whole peoples.", "우리가 아는 바, 죽음 앞에서도 미소 지을 수 있는 자, 온 민족을 죽음으로 몰아가는 질병들 한가운데에서도 번성할 수 있는 자가 말이오."], ["Oh, if such an one was to come from God, and not the Devil, what a force for good might he not be in this old world of ours.", "오, 만일 그런 자가 악마가 아니라 하느님에게서 왔더라면, 우리의 이 오래된 세상에서 그가 얼마나 큰 선의 힘이 되었겠소."], ["But we are pledged to set the world free.", "그러나 우리는 세상을 자유롭게 하기로 맹세했소."], ["Our toil must be in silence, and our efforts all in secret; for in this enlightened age, when men believe not even what they see, the doubting of wise men would be his greatest strength.", "우리의 수고는 침묵 속에서, 우리의 노력은 모두 은밀히 이루어져야 하오. 사람들이 자기 눈으로 본 것조차 믿지 않는 이 계몽된 시대에, 현명한 자들의 의심은 그에게 가장 큰 힘이 될 것이기 때문이오."], ["It would be at once his sheath and his armour, and his weapons to destroy us, his enemies, who are willing to peril even our own souls for the safety of one we love--for the good of mankind, and for the honour and glory of God.”", "그것은 단번에 그의 칼집이자 갑옷이 될 것이며, 우리를, 곧 사랑하는 한 사람의 안전을 위해 — 인류의 선을 위해, 또 하느님의 영예와 영광을 위해 우리 자신의 영혼마저 위태롭게 할 각오가 된 그의 적들을 파멸시킬 그의 무기가 될 것이오.\""]], [["After a general discussion it was determined that for to-night nothing be definitely settled; that we should all sleep on the facts, and try to think out the proper conclusions.", "전반적인 논의 끝에, 오늘 밤은 어떤 것도 확정하지 않기로, 우리 모두 그 사실들을 하룻밤 곱씹어보며 올바른 결론을 생각해내도록 애쓰기로 정했다."], ["To-morrow, at breakfast, we are to meet again, and, after making our conclusions known to one another, we shall decide on some definite cause of action.", "내일 아침식사 때 우리는 다시 모여, 서로에게 각자의 결론을 알린 뒤에 어떤 명확한 행동 방침을 결정하기로 했다."]], [["I feel a wonderful peace and rest to-night.", "오늘 밤 나는 놀라운 평화와 안식을 느낀다."], ["It is as if some haunting presence were removed from me. Perhaps ...", "마치 어떤 출몰하던 존재가 내게서 사라진 것만 같다. 어쩌면……"]], [["My surmise was not finished, could not be; for I caught sight in the mirror of the red mark upon my forehead; and I knew that I was still unclean.", "내 추측은 끝맺지 못했다, 끝맺을 수 없었다. 거울 속에서 내 이마 위 붉은 자국이 눈에 들어왔기 때문이다. 그리고 나는 내가 여전히 부정(不淨)하다는 것을 알았다."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "수어드 박사의 일기."]], [["5 October.--We all rose early, and I think that sleep did much for each and all of us.", "10월 5일. — 우리는 모두 일찍 일어났는데, 잠이 우리 각자에게 큰 도움이 된 듯하다."], ["When we met at early breakfast there was more general cheerfulness than any of us had ever expected to experience again.", "이른 아침식사 때 우리가 모였을 때에는, 우리 중 누구도 다시 맛보리라 기대하지 못했던 전반적인 명랑함이 감돌았다."]], [["It is really wonderful how much resilience there is in human nature.", "인간 본성에는 참으로 놀라운 회복력이 있다."], ["Let any obstructing cause, no matter what, be removed in any way--even by death--and we fly back to first principles of hope and enjoyment.", "무엇이든, 어떤 식으로든 — 심지어 죽음에 의해서라도 — 가로막던 원인이 제거되기만 하면, 우리는 희망과 즐거움의 본래 자리로 되돌아 날아간다."], ["More than once as we sat around the table, my eyes opened in wonder whether the whole of the past days had not been a dream.", "우리가 식탁에 둘러앉아 있는 동안 나는 한 번 이상, 지난 며칠 전체가 한바탕 꿈이 아니었을까 하는 의아함에 눈을 떴다."], ["It was only when I caught sight of the red blotch on Mrs.", "다만 하커 부인의 이마 위 붉은 얼룩을 보았을 때에야 나는 현실로 돌아왔다."], ["Harker’s forehead that I was brought back to reality. Even now, when I am gravely revolving the matter, it is almost impossible to realise that the cause of all our trouble is still existent.", "지금 이렇게 이 일을 진지하게 곱씹는 와중에도, 우리의 온갖 고난의 원인이 여전히 존재한다는 것을 실감하기란 거의 불가능하다. 하커 부인조차 한참 동안은 자신의 고난을 잊은 듯하다."], ["Even Mrs. Harker seems to lose sight of her trouble for whole spells; it is only now and again, when something recalls it to her mind, that she thinks of her terrible scar.", "다만 이따금 무언가 그것을 그녀의 마음에 되살릴 때에야, 그녀는 자신의 끔찍한 흉터를 떠올린다. 우리는 삼십 분 뒤 이곳 내 서재에서 만나 우리의 행동 방침을 정하기로 했다."], ["We are to meet here in my study in half an hour and decide on our course of action.", "나는 단 하나의 당면한 어려움을 보고 있는데, 이성보다는 본능으로 그것을 안다."], ["I see only one immediate difficulty, I know it by instinct rather than reason: we shall all have to speak frankly; and yet I fear that in some mysterious way poor Mrs. Harker’s tongue is tied.", "우리는 모두 솔직하게 이야기해야 할 텐데, 어쩐지 어떤 알 수 없는 방식으로 가엾은 하커 부인의 혀가 묶여 있는 것이 아닐까 두렵다. 나는 그녀가 자기 나름의 결론을 내린다는 것을 안다."], ["I know that she forms conclusions of her own, and from all that has been I can guess how brilliant and how true they must be; but she will not, or cannot, give them utterance.", "그리고 지금까지의 모든 일로 미루어 그것이 얼마나 명민하고 얼마나 참될지 짐작할 수 있다. 그러나 그녀는 그것을 입 밖에 내지 않으려 하거나, 낼 수 없는 것이다."], ["I have mentioned this to Van Helsing, and he and I are to talk it over when we are alone.", "나는 이것을 반 헬싱에게 이야기했고, 그와 나는 단둘이 있을 때 그것을 의논하기로 했다."], ["I suppose it is some of that horrid poison which has got into her veins beginning to work.", "짐작건대 그것은 그녀의 혈관에 들어간 그 끔찍한 독의 일부가 작용하기 시작한 것이리라."], ["The Count had his own purposes when he gave her what Van Helsing called “the Vampire’s baptism of blood.”", "백작은 그녀에게, 반 헬싱이 \"흡혈귀의 피의 세례\"라 부른 것을 베풀 때 자기 나름의 목적이 있었던 것이다."], ["Well, there may be a poison that distils itself out of good things; in an age when the existence of ptomaines is a mystery we should not wonder at anything!", "글쎄, 좋은 것에서 스스로 증류되어 나오는 독도 있을지 모른다. 시체독의 존재조차 수수께끼인 시대에 우리는 무엇에도 놀라서는 안 된다! 한 가지 나는 안다."], ["One thing I know: that if my instinct be true regarding poor Mrs. Harker’s silences, then there is a terrible difficulty--an unknown danger--in the work before us.", "만일 가엾은 하커 부인의 침묵에 관한 내 본능이 옳다면, 우리 앞에 놓인 일에는 끔찍한 어려움이 — 알 수 없는 위험이 있다는 것을. 그녀의 침묵을 강요하는 바로 그 힘이 그녀의 말을 강요할 수도 있다."], ["The same power that compels her silence may compel her speech.", "나는 더 이상 생각할 엄두가 나지 않는다."], ["I dare not think further; for so I should in my thoughts dishonour a noble woman!", "그렇게 했다가는 내 생각 속에서 한 고귀한 여인을 욕되게 하고 말 테니!"]], [["Van Helsing is coming to my study a little before the others.", "반 헬싱이 다른 이들보다 조금 일찍 내 서재로 올 것이다."], ["I shall try to open the subject with him.", "나는 그에게 이 문제를 꺼내보려 한다."]], [["Later.--When the Professor came in, we talked over the state of things.", "나중에. — 교수가 들어왔을 때, 우리는 사태를 의논했다."], ["I could see that he had something on his mind which he wanted to say, but felt some hesitancy about broaching the subject.", "그가 무언가 하고 싶은 말을 마음에 두고 있으나, 그 화제를 꺼내기를 다소 망설이고 있음을 나는 알 수 있었다."], ["After beating about the bush a little, he said suddenly:--", "한동안 변죽을 울리던 그가 불쑥 말했다."]], [["“Friend John, there is something that you and I must talk of alone, just at the first at any rate.", "\"존, 자네와 내가 단둘이서 이야기해야 할 것이 있네, 적어도 처음에는 말일세."], ["Later, we may have to take the others into our confidence”; then he stopped, so I waited; he went on:--", "나중에는 다른 이들에게도 털어놓아야 할지 모르네.\" 그러더니 그가 말을 멈추었으므로 나는 기다렸다. 그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Madam Mina, our poor, dear Madam Mina is changing.”", "\"미나 부인, 우리의 가엾고 사랑스러운 미나 부인이 변해가고 있네.\""], ["A cold shiver ran through me to find my worst fears thus endorsed.", "내 최악의 두려움이 이렇게 확인되자 차가운 전율이 온몸을 훑고 지나갔다."], ["Van Helsing continued:--", "반 헬싱이 말을 이었다."]], [["“With the sad experience of Miss Lucy, we must this time be warned before things go too far.", "\"루시 양의 슬픈 경험이 있으니, 이번에는 일이 너무 멀리 가기 전에 경계해야 하네."], ["Our task is now in reality more difficult than ever, and this new trouble makes every hour of the direst importance.", "우리의 과업은 이제 실로 그 어느 때보다 더 어려워졌고, 이 새로운 곤란은 매 시간을 더없이 중대하게 만드네."], ["I can see the characteristics of the vampire coming in her face.", "나는 그녀의 얼굴에 흡혈귀의 특징이 들어서는 것을 볼 수 있네."], ["It is now but very, very slight; but it is to be seen if we have eyes to notice without to prejudge.", "지금은 다만 아주, 아주 미미하지만, 미리 단정하지 않고 알아챌 눈만 있다면 보이는 것일세."], ["Her teeth are some sharper, and at times her eyes are more hard.", "그녀의 이가 다소 날카로워졌고, 때때로 눈이 더 단단해지네."], ["But these are not all, there is to her the silence now often; as so it was with Miss Lucy.", "그러나 이것이 전부가 아닐세. 이제 그녀에게는 자주 침묵이 찾아드네. 루시 양에게 그러했던 것처럼 말일세."], ["She did not speak, even when she wrote that which she wished to be known later. Now my fear is this.", "그녀는, 나중에 알려지기를 바라며 글을 쓸 때조차 입을 떼지 않았네. 그런데 내 두려움은 이것일세."], ["If it be that she can, by our hypnotic trance, tell what the Count see and hear, is it not more true that he who have hypnotise her first, and who have drink of her very blood and make her drink of his, should, if he will, compel her mind to disclose to him that which she know?”", "만일 그녀가 우리의 최면 상태를 통해 백작이 보고 듣는 것을 말할 수 있다면, 그녀에게 처음 최면을 걸었고, 그녀의 바로 그 피를 마셨으며 그녀로 하여금 자기 피를 마시게 한 그자가, 만일 그가 원한다면, 그녀의 마음을 강요하여 그녀가 아는 것을 자기에게 드러내게 할 수 있다는 것이 더더욱 참되지 않겠는가?\""], ["I nodded acquiescence; he went on:--", "나는 동의하여 고개를 끄덕였다. 그가 말을 이었다."]], [["“Then, what we must do is to prevent this; we must keep her ignorant of our intent, and so she cannot tell what she know not.", "\"그렇다면 우리가 해야 할 일은 이것을 막는 것일세. 우리는 그녀가 우리의 의도를 모르게 해야 하며, 그리하면 그녀는 자기가 모르는 것을 말할 수 없네."], ["This is a painful task!", "이것은 괴로운 과업일세!"], ["Oh, so painful that it heart-break me to think of; but it must be.", "오, 어찌나 괴로운지 생각만 해도 내 가슴이 미어지네. 그러나 그래야만 하네."], ["When to-day we meet, I must tell her that for reason which we will not to speak she must not more be of our council, but be simply guarded by us.”", "오늘 우리가 모일 때, 나는 그녀에게, 우리가 말하지 않으려는 어떤 이유로 그녀는 더 이상 우리의 회의에 들지 못하고, 다만 우리의 보호를 받아야 한다고 말해야 하네.\""], ["He wiped his forehead, which had broken out in profuse perspiration at the thought of the pain which he might have to inflict upon the poor soul already so tortured.", "그는, 이미 그토록 고통받는 가엾은 영혼에게 어쩌면 자기가 안겨주어야 할지도 모를 고통을 생각하며 이마에 흥건히 솟은 땀을 닦았다."], ["I knew that it would be some sort of comfort to him if I told him that I also had come to the same conclusion; for at any rate it would take away the pain of doubt.", "나 또한 같은 결론에 이르렀다고 말해준다면 그에게 어떤 위안이 되리라는 것을 나는 알았다. 적어도 그것은 의심의 고통만은 덜어줄 테니까."], ["I told him, and the effect was as I expected.", "나는 그에게 그렇게 말해주었고, 그 효과는 내가 예상한 그대로였다."]], [["It is now close to the time of our general gathering.", "이제 우리의 전체 모임 시각이 가까워졌다."], ["Van Helsing has gone away to prepare for the meeting, and his painful part of it.", "반 헬싱은 모임을, 그리고 그 괴로운 자기 역할을 준비하러 자리를 떴다."], ["I really believe his purpose is to be able to pray alone.", "나는 그가 정말로 혼자서 기도하려는 것이리라 믿는다."]], [["Later.--At the very outset of our meeting a great personal relief was experienced by both Van Helsing and myself. Mrs.", "나중에. — 우리 모임이 시작되자마자 반 헬싱과 나는 둘 다 개인적으로 큰 안도를 느꼈다."], ["Harker had sent a message by her husband to say that she would not join us at present, as she thought it better that we should be free to discuss our movements without her presence to embarrass us.", "하커 부인이 남편 편에 전갈을 보내, 지금은 우리와 함께하지 않겠다고, 우리가 그녀의 존재로 거북해하지 않고 자유로이 우리의 행동을 논의하는 편이 낫겠다고 했다는 것이다."], ["The Professor and I looked at each other for an instant, and somehow we both seemed relieved.", "교수와 나는 한순간 서로를 바라보았는데, 어쩐지 둘 다 안도한 듯했다."], ["For my own part, I thought that if Mrs. Harker realised the danger herself, it was much pain as well as much danger averted.", "나로서는, 만일 하커 부인 스스로 그 위험을 깨달은 것이라면, 그것은 많은 위험만큼이나 많은 고통도 피한 셈이라고 생각했다."], ["Under the circumstances we agreed, by a questioning look and answer, with finger on lip, to preserve silence in our suspicions, until we should have been able to confer alone again.", "그러한 사정에서 우리는, 묻는 눈빛과 그에 답하는 눈빛으로, 입술에 손가락을 댄 채, 우리 둘이 다시 단둘이 의논할 수 있게 될 때까지 우리의 의혹을 침묵 속에 묻어두기로 합의했다."], ["We went at once into our Plan of Campaign.", "우리는 곧장 우리의 작전 계획에 들어갔다."], ["Van Helsing roughly put the facts before us first:--", "반 헬싱이 먼저 사실들을 우리 앞에 대강 풀어놓았다."]], [["“The Czarina Catherine left the Thames yesterday morning.", "\"차리나 캐서린호는 어제 아침 템스 강을 떠났소."], ["It will take her at the quickest speed she has ever made at least three weeks to reach Varna; but we can travel overland to the same place in three days.", "그 배가 일찍이 낸 적 있는 가장 빠른 속도로 가더라도 바르나에 닿기까지 적어도 삼 주는 걸릴 것이오. 그러나 우리는 육로로 같은 곳까지 사흘이면 갈 수 있소."], ["Now, if we allow for two days less for the ship’s voyage, owing to such weather influences as we know that the Count can bring to bear; and if we allow a whole day and night for any delays which may occur to us, then we have a margin of nearly two weeks.", "자, 우리가 아는 바, 백작이 부릴 수 있는 그런 날씨의 영향을 감안하여 배의 항해를 이틀 줄여 잡고, 우리에게 일어날지 모를 어떤 지체를 위해 꼬박 하루 낮밤을 잡는다면, 우리에게는 거의 이 주의 여유가 있는 셈이오."], ["Thus, in order to be quite safe, we must leave here on 17th at latest.", "그러니 완전히 안전하려면 우리는 늦어도 17일에는 이곳을 떠나야 하오."], ["Then we shall at any rate be in Varna a day before the ship arrives, and able to make such preparations as may be necessary.", "그러면 우리는 어쨌든 배가 도착하기 하루 전에 바르나에 가 있게 되어, 필요할지 모를 준비를 갖출 수 있소."], ["Of course we shall all go armed--armed against evil things, spiritual as well as physical.”", "물론 우리는 모두 무장하고 갈 것이오 — 육체적인 것뿐 아니라 영적인 것까지, 악한 것들에 맞서 무장하고서.\""], ["Here Quincey Morris added:--", "여기서 퀸시 모리스가 덧붙였다."]], [["“I understand that the Count comes from a wolf country, and it may be that he shall get there before us.", "\"제가 알기로 백작은 늑대의 나라에서 온 자이니, 어쩌면 그가 우리보다 먼저 거기 닿을지도 모릅니다."], ["I propose that we add Winchesters to our armament.", "저는 우리 무장에 윈체스터 소총을 보태자고 제안합니다."], ["I have a kind of belief in a Winchester when there is any trouble of that sort around.", "그런 종류의 곤경이 주위에 있을 때 저는 윈체스터에 일종의 믿음을 갖고 있거든요."], ["Do you remember, Art, when we had the pack after us at Tobolsk?", "아트, 우리가 토볼스크에서 늑대 떼에게 쫓기던 일을 기억하나?"], ["What wouldn’t we have given then for a repeater apiece!”", "그때 우리가 한 자루씩 연발총만 있었더라면 무엇인들 못 내놓았겠나!\""]], [["“Good!”", "\"좋소!\""], ["said Van Helsing, “Winchesters it shall be.", "반 헬싱이 말했다. \"윈체스터로 합시다."], ["Quincey’s head is level at all times, but most so when there is to hunt, metaphor be more dishonour to science than wolves be of danger to man.", "퀸시의 머리는 언제나 빈틈없지만, 사냥할 일이 있을 때 가장 그렇소. 비유라는 게 늑대가 사람에게 위험한 것보다 학문에 더 욕된 것이라면 말이오. 그동안 우리가 여기서 할 수 있는 일은 없소."], ["In the meantime we can do nothing here; and as I think that Varna is not familiar to any of us, why not go there more soon? It is as long to wait here as there.", "그리고 내 생각에 바르나는 우리 중 누구에게도 낯선 곳이니, 더 일찍 거기로 가는 게 어떻겠소? 여기서 기다리는 것이나 거기서 기다리는 것이나 마찬가지로 긴 시간이오."], ["To-night and to-morrow we can get ready, and then, if all be well, we four can set out on our journey.”", "오늘 밤과 내일 우리는 채비를 갖출 수 있고, 그런 다음 모든 것이 순조롭다면 우리 넷이 여정에 오를 수 있소.\""]], [["“We four?” said Harker interrogatively, looking from one to another of us.", "\"우리 넷이라뇨?\" 하커가 우리를 한 사람씩 둘러보며 묻듯이 말했다."]], [["“Of course!”", "\"물론이오!\""], ["answered the Professor quickly, “you must remain to take care of your so sweet wife!”", "교수가 재빨리 답했다. \"당신은 남아서 당신의 그토록 사랑스러운 아내를 돌봐야 하오!\""], ["Harker was silent for awhile and then said in a hollow voice:--", "하커는 한동안 잠자코 있다가 공허한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Let us talk of that part of it in the morning.", "\"그 부분에 관해서는 아침에 이야기합시다."], ["I want to consult with Mina.”", "미나와 상의하고 싶소.\""], ["I thought that now was the time for Van Helsing to warn him not to disclose our plans to her; but he took no notice.", "나는 지금이야말로 반 헬싱이 그에게 우리의 계획을 그녀에게 발설하지 말라고 경고할 때라고 생각했다. 그러나 그는 아무 내색도 하지 않았다."], ["I looked at him significantly and coughed.", "나는 의미심장하게 그를 바라보며 헛기침을 했다."], ["For answer he put his finger on his lips and turned away.", "그러자 그는 답으로 손가락을 입술에 대고는 몸을 돌렸다."]], [["Jonathan Harker’s Journal.", "조너선 하커의 일기."]], [["5 October, afternoon.--For some time after our meeting this morning I could not think.", "10월 5일, 오후. — 오늘 아침 우리 모임이 끝난 뒤 한동안 나는 아무 생각도 할 수 없었다."], ["The new phases of things leave my mind in a state of wonder which allows no room for active thought.", "사태의 새로운 국면들이 내 마음을 활발한 사고가 들어설 자리가 없는 의아함의 상태에 빠뜨렸다."], ["Mina’s determination not to take any part in the discussion set me thinking; and as I could not argue the matter with her, I could only guess.", "미나가 그 논의에 일절 끼지 않겠다고 한 결심이 나를 생각에 잠기게 했다. 그러나 나는 그 일을 그녀와 따져 의논할 수 없었으므로, 다만 짐작할 수밖에 없었다."], ["I am as far as ever from a solution now.", "나는 지금도 여전히 답에서 멀리 있다."], ["The way the others received it, too, puzzled me; the last time we talked of the subject we agreed that there was to be no more concealment of anything amongst us.", "다른 이들이 그것을 받아들인 태도 또한 나를 어리둥절하게 했다. 지난번 우리가 그 문제를 이야기했을 때 우리는 우리 사이에 더 이상 어떤 것도 숨기지 않기로 합의했었다."], ["Mina is sleeping now, calmly and sweetly like a little child.", "미나는 지금 어린아이처럼 차분하고 사랑스럽게 자고 있다."], ["Her lips are curved and her face beams with happiness.", "그녀의 입술은 곡선을 그리고 얼굴은 행복으로 빛난다."], ["Thank God, there are such moments still for her.", "하느님 감사합니다, 그녀에게 아직 이런 순간들이 있다니."]], [["Later.--How strange it all is.", "나중에. — 이 모든 것이 어찌나 기이한지."], ["I sat watching Mina’s happy sleep, and came as near to being happy myself as I suppose I shall ever be.", "나는 미나의 행복한 잠을 지켜보며 앉아 있었고, 내가 앞으로 누릴 수 있으리라 짐작되는 만큼 행복에 가까이 다가갔다."], ["As the evening drew on, and the earth took its shadows from the sun sinking lower, the silence of the room grew more and more solemn to me.", "저녁이 깊어가고, 더 낮게 가라앉는 해로부터 대지가 제 그림자를 받아들이면서, 방 안의 고요가 내게 점점 더 엄숙해졌다."], ["All at once Mina opened her eyes, and looking at me tenderly, said:--", "별안간 미나가 눈을 뜨더니, 다정하게 나를 바라보며 말했다."]], [["“Jonathan, I want you to promise me something on your word of honour.", "\"조너선, 당신의 명예를 걸고 내게 무언가 약속해주었으면 해요."], ["A promise made to me, but made holily in God’s hearing, and not to be broken though I should go down on my knees and implore you with bitter tears.", "나에게 한 약속이되, 하느님께서 들으시는 가운데 성스럽게 한 약속, 내가 무릎을 꿇고 쓰라린 눈물로 당신에게 애원한다 해도 깨뜨려서는 안 될 약속이에요."], ["Quick, you must make it to me at once.”", "어서요, 지금 당장 내게 약속해주어야 해요.\""]], [["“Mina,”", "\"미나,\""], ["I said, “a promise like that, I cannot make at once.", "내가 말했다. \"그런 약속을, 나는 당장 할 수는 없소."], ["I may have no right to make it.”", "내게 그것을 할 권리가 없을지도 모르오.\""]], [["“But, dear one,”", "\"하지만, 여보,\""], ["she said, with such spiritual intensity that her eyes were like pole stars, “it is I who wish it; and it is not for myself.", "그녀가, 그 눈이 북극성 같아 보일 만큼 영적인 간절함으로 말했다. \"이것을 바라는 건 나예요. 그리고 나 자신을 위한 것이 아니에요."], ["You can ask Dr. Van Helsing if I am not right; if he disagrees you may do as you will.", "내가 옳지 않은지 반 헬싱 박사님께 물어보아도 돼요. 그분이 동의하지 않으시면 당신 좋을 대로 하면 돼요."], ["Nay, more, if you all agree, later, you are absolved from the promise.”", "아니, 그뿐 아니라, 만일 나중에 여러분 모두가 동의하면, 당신은 그 약속에서 풀려나는 거예요.\""]], [["“I promise!”", "\"약속하겠소!\""], ["I said, and for a moment she looked supremely happy; though to me all happiness for her was denied by the red scar on her forehead.", "내가 말했고, 한순간 그녀는 더없이 행복해 보였다. 비록 내게는 그녀를 위한 모든 행복이 그녀의 이마 위 붉은 흉터로 막혀 있었지만."], ["She said:--", "그녀가 말했다."]], [["“Promise me that you will not tell me anything of the plans formed for the campaign against the Count.", "\"백작에 맞선 작전을 위해 세운 계획에 관해서는 내게 아무것도 말하지 않겠다고 약속해주세요."], ["Not by word, or inference, or implication; not at any time whilst this remains to me!”", "말로도, 넌지시도, 암시로도 안 돼요. 이것이 내게 남아 있는 한, 어느 때라도요!\""], ["and she solemnly pointed to the scar.", "그러고는 엄숙하게 그 흉터를 가리켰다."], ["I saw that she was in earnest, and said solemnly:--", "나는 그녀가 진심임을 알았고, 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“I promise!”", "\"약속하겠소!\""], ["and as I said it I felt that from that instant a door had been shut between us.", "그리고 그렇게 말하는 순간 나는 그 순간부터 우리 사이에 문 하나가 닫혀버렸음을 느꼈다."]], [["Later, midnight.--Mina has been bright and cheerful all the evening.", "나중에, 자정. — 미나는 저녁 내내 밝고 명랑했다."], ["So much so that all the rest seemed to take courage, as if infected somewhat with her gaiety; as a result even I myself felt as if the pall of gloom which weighs us down were somewhat lifted.", "어찌나 그러했던지 나머지 사람들도 모두, 다소 그녀의 쾌활함에 물들기라도 한 듯 용기를 얻는 것 같았다. 그 결과 나 자신조차 우리를 짓누르던 우울의 장막이 다소 걷힌 듯 느껴졌다."], ["We all retired early.", "우리는 모두 일찍 잠자리에 들었다."], ["Mina is now sleeping like a little child; it is a wonderful thing that her faculty of sleep remains to her in the midst of her terrible trouble.", "미나는 지금 어린아이처럼 자고 있다. 그토록 끔찍한 고난 한가운데에서도 잠드는 능력이 그녀에게 남아 있다는 것은 놀라운 일이다. 그것을 하느님께 감사한다."], ["Thank God for it, for then at least she can forget her care. Perhaps her example may affect me as her gaiety did to-night.", "그때만이라도 그녀는 자기 근심을 잊을 수 있으니까. 어쩌면 그녀의 본보기가, 오늘 밤 그녀의 쾌활함이 그러했던 것처럼 내게 영향을 미칠지도 모른다."], ["I shall try it. Oh! for a dreamless sleep.", "나도 시도해보겠다. 오! 꿈 없는 잠이여."]], [["6 October, morning.--Another surprise.", "10월 6일, 아침. — 또 한 번의 놀라움."], ["Mina woke me early, about the same time as yesterday, and asked me to bring Dr. Van Helsing.", "미나가 어제와 거의 같은 시각에 나를 일찍 깨우더니, 반 헬싱 박사를 데려와달라고 청했다."], ["I thought that it was another occasion for hypnotism, and without question went for the Professor. He had evidently expected some such call, for I found him dressed in his room.", "나는 또 한 번 최면을 걸 때구나 싶어, 두말없이 교수를 데리러 갔다. 그는 분명 그런 부름을 예상하고 있었던 듯, 자기 방에서 옷을 차려입은 그를 보았기 때문이다."], ["His door was ajar, so that he could hear the opening of the door of our room.", "그의 방문은 빠끔히 열려 있어서, 그가 우리 방문 열리는 소리를 들을 수 있었다."], ["He came at once; as he passed into the room, he asked Mina if the others might come, too.", "그는 곧장 왔다. 방으로 들어서면서 그는 미나에게 다른 이들도 와도 되겠느냐고 물었다."]], [["“No,”", "\"아니요,\""], ["she said quite simply, “it will not be necessary.", "그녀가 아주 담담하게 말했다. \"그럴 필요는 없을 거예요."], ["You can tell them just as well.", "박사님께서 그분들께 그대로 전해주시면 돼요."], ["I must go with you on your journey.”", "저는 여러분의 여정에 함께 가야 해요.\""]], [["Dr. Van Helsing was as startled as I was.", "반 헬싱 박사도 나만큼이나 깜짝 놀랐다."], ["After a moment’s pause he asked:--", "한순간 멈칫하더니 그가 물었다."]], [["“But why?”", "\"하지만 왜요?\""]], [["“You must take me with you. I am safer with you, and you shall be safer, too.”", "\"저를 데려가셔야 해요. 저는 여러분과 함께 있는 편이 더 안전하고, 여러분 또한 더 안전해질 거예요.\""]], [["“But why, dear Madam Mina?", "\"하지만 왜요, 사랑하는 미나 부인?"], ["You know that your safety is our solemnest duty.", "당신도 알다시피 당신의 안전은 우리의 가장 엄숙한 의무요."], ["We go into danger, to which you are, or may be, more liable than any of us from--from circumstances--things that have been.”", "우리는 위험 속으로 들어가는데, 당신은 그 위험에, 어떤 사정 때문에 — 지나간 일들 때문에 — 우리 중 누구보다도 더 노출되어 있거나, 그럴 수 있소.\""], ["He paused, embarrassed.", "그가 당혹스러워하며 말을 멈추었다."]], [["As she replied, she raised her finger and pointed to her forehead:--", "그녀는 답하면서 손가락을 들어 자기 이마를 가리켰다."]], [["“I know.", "\"알아요."], ["That is why I must go. I can tell you now, whilst the sun is coming up; I may not be able again.", "그것이 바로 제가 가야 하는 까닭이에요. 지금은, 해가 떠오르는 동안은 말씀드릴 수 있지만, 다시는 못 할지도 몰라요."], ["I know that when the Count wills me I must go.", "저는 백작이 저를 부르면 가야만 한다는 걸 알아요."], ["I know that if he tells me to come in secret, I must come by wile; by any device to hoodwink--even Jonathan.”", "저는 그가 몰래 오라고 명하면, 제가 술책으로, 무슨 수를 써서든 — 조너선까지도 — 속여서 가야만 한다는 걸 알아요.\""], ["God saw the look that she turned on me as she spoke, and if there be indeed a Recording Angel that look is noted to her everlasting honour. I could only clasp her hand.", "그녀가 그렇게 말하며 내게 보낸 그 눈빛을 하느님께서는 보셨으니, 만일 정말로 기록하는 천사가 있다면 그 눈빛은 그녀의 영원한 영예로 적혀 있을 것이다. 나는 다만 그녀의 손을 꼭 쥘 수 있을 뿐이었다."], ["I could not speak; my emotion was too great for even the relief of tears.", "나는 말할 수 없었다. 내 감정은 눈물의 위안조차 받기에 너무 벅찼다."], ["She went on:--", "그녀가 말을 이었다."]], [["“You men are brave and strong.", "\"여러분 남자들은 용감하고 강해요."], ["You are strong in your numbers, for you can defy that which would break down the human endurance of one who had to guard alone.", "여러분은 수가 많아서 강하지요. 혼자 지켜야 하는 사람의 인간적 인내를 무너뜨릴 것에도 여러분은 함께 맞설 수 있으니까요."], ["Besides, I may be of service, since you can hypnotise me and so learn that which even I myself do not know.” Dr.", "게다가 저는 도움이 될지도 몰라요. 여러분이 제게 최면을 걸어, 저 자신조차 모르는 것을 알아낼 수 있으니까요.\""], ["Van Helsing said very gravely:--", "반 헬싱 박사가 매우 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“Madam Mina, you are, as always, most wise.", "\"미나 부인, 당신은 언제나처럼 더없이 현명하오."], ["You shall with us come; and together we shall do that which we go forth to achieve.”", "당신은 우리와 함께 가시오. 그리고 우리가 이루러 나아가는 그 일을 우리 함께 해냅시다.\""], ["When he had spoken, Mina’s long spell of silence made me look at her.", "그가 말을 마쳤을 때, 미나의 긴 침묵이 나로 하여금 그녀를 바라보게 했다."], ["She had fallen back on her pillow asleep; she did not even wake when I had pulled up the blind and let in the sunlight which flooded the room.", "그녀는 베개에 도로 기댄 채 잠들어 있었다. 내가 차양을 걷어 올려 햇빛이 방에 쏟아져 들어오게 했을 때조차 그녀는 깨지 않았다."], ["Van Helsing motioned to me to come with him quietly.", "반 헬싱이 내게 조용히 함께 가자고 손짓했다."], ["We went to his room, and within a minute Lord Godalming, Dr. Seward, and Mr. Morris were with us also.", "우리는 그의 방으로 갔고, 일 분도 안 되어 고달밍 경과 수어드 박사, 모리스 씨도 우리와 함께했다."], ["He told them what Mina had said, and went on:--", "그는 그들에게 미나가 한 말을 전한 뒤 말을 이었다."]], [["“In the morning we shall leave for Varna.", "\"아침에 우리는 바르나로 떠나오."], ["We have now to deal with a new factor: Madam Mina.", "우리는 이제 새로운 변수를 다루어야 하오. 미나 부인 말이오."], ["Oh, but her soul is true.", "오, 그러나 그녀의 영혼은 참되오."], ["It is to her an agony to tell us so much as she has done; but it is most right, and we are warned in time.", "그녀가 우리에게 그만큼이나 이야기하는 것은 그녀에게 고통이오. 그러나 그것은 더없이 옳은 일이며, 우리는 때맞추어 경계하게 된 것이오."], ["There must be no chance lost, and in Varna we must be ready to act the instant when that ship arrives.”", "어떤 기회도 놓쳐서는 안 되며, 바르나에서 우리는 그 배가 도착하는 순간 행동할 채비가 되어 있어야 하오.\""]], [["“What shall we do exactly?”", "\"정확히 무엇을 해야 합니까?\""], ["asked Mr. Morris laconically.", "모리스 씨가 간결하게 물었다."], ["The Professor paused before replying:--", "교수가 답하기 전에 잠시 멈추었다."]], [["“We shall at the first board that ship; then, when we have identified the box, we shall place a branch of the wild rose on it.", "\"우리는 먼저 그 배에 오를 것이오. 그런 다음 그 상자를 확인하면, 거기에 들장미 가지를 놓을 것이오."], ["This we shall fasten, for when it is there none can emerge; so at least says the superstition.", "우리는 이것을 단단히 매어둘 것인데, 그것이 거기 있으면 아무도 빠져나올 수 없기 때문이오."], ["And to superstition must we trust at the first; it was man’s faith in the early, and it have its root in faith still.", "적어도 미신은 그렇게 말하오. 그리고 우리는 처음에는 미신에 기대야 하오. 그것은 옛 시절 인간의 믿음이었고, 그 뿌리는 여전히 믿음에 있소."], ["Then, when we get the opportunity that we seek, when none are near to see, we shall open the box, and--and all will be well.”", "그런 다음, 우리가 찾는 기회가 와서 아무도 가까이서 볼 사람이 없을 때, 우리는 그 상자를 열 것이오. 그러면 — 그러면 모든 것이 잘될 것이오.\""]], [["“I shall not wait for any opportunity,”", "\"저는 어떤 기회도 기다리지 않겠습니다,\""], ["said Morris.", "모리스가 말했다."], ["“When I see the box I shall open it and destroy the monster, though there were a thousand men looking on, and if I am to be wiped out for it the next moment!”", "\"그 상자를 보면 저는 그것을 열어 그 괴물을 없애버리겠습니다. 설령 천 명이 지켜보고 있다 해도, 그리고 그 때문에 제가 다음 순간 사라져 없어진다 해도 말입니다!\""], ["I grasped his hand instinctively and found it as firm as a piece of steel.", "나는 본능적으로 그의 손을 잡았는데, 그것은 강철 조각처럼 단단했다."], ["I think he understood my look; I hope he did.", "그는 내 눈빛을 이해한 것 같다. 그랬기를 바란다."]], [["“Good boy,” said Dr.", "\"착한 친구,\""], ["Van Helsing. “Brave boy.", "반 헬싱 박사가 말했다."], ["Quincey is all man.", "\"용감한 친구일세."], ["God bless him for it.", "퀸시는 영락없는 사내일세."], ["My child, believe me none of us shall lag behind or pause from any fear. I do but say what we may do--what we must do.", "그 점을 하느님께서 그에게 복 주시기를. 얘들아, 내 말을 믿게, 우리 중 누구도 뒤처지거나 무슨 두려움으로 멈춰 서지 않을 걸세."], ["But, indeed, indeed we cannot say what we shall do.", "나는 다만 우리가 할 수 있는 일을 — 우리가 해야 할 일을 말하는 것뿐일세."], ["There are so many things which may happen, and their ways and their ends are so various that until the moment we may not say.", "그러나 실로, 실로 우리는 우리가 무엇을 하게 될지 말할 수 없네. 일어날 수 있는 일이 너무 많고, 그 방식과 그 끝이 너무도 가지가지여서, 그 순간이 오기까지는 우리도 말할 수 없네."], ["We shall all be armed, in all ways; and when the time for the end has come, our effort shall not be lack.", "우리는 모두, 온갖 방식으로 무장할 것이며, 끝낼 때가 오면 우리의 노력에 모자람이 없을 걸세."], ["Now let us to-day put all our affairs in order.", "자, 오늘은 우리의 모든 일을 정돈해두세."], ["Let all things which touch on others dear to us, and who on us depend, be complete; for none of us can tell what, or when, or how, the end may be.", "우리에게 소중하고 우리에게 의지하는 다른 이들에게 관계되는 모든 일을 마무리해두세. 우리 중 누구도 그 끝이 무엇일지, 언제일지, 어떻게일지 말할 수 없으니 말일세."], ["As for me, my own affairs are regulate; and as I have nothing else to do, I shall go make arrangements for the travel.", "나로 말하자면, 내 일은 정리되어 있고, 달리 할 일이 없으니, 나는 여행 준비를 하러 가겠네."], ["I shall have all tickets and so forth for our journey.”", "우리 여정에 필요한 표며 그 밖의 모든 것을 마련해두겠네.\""]], [["There was nothing further to be said, and we parted.", "더 이상 할 말이 없어 우리는 헤어졌다."], ["I shall now settle up all my affairs of earth, and be ready for whatever may come....", "나는 이제 내 세상사를 모두 정리하고, 무슨 일이 닥치든 채비를 갖추어두겠다……"]], [["Later.--It is all done; my will is made, and all complete.", "나중에. — 모두 끝냈다. 내 유언장이 작성되었고, 모든 것이 완비되었다."], ["Mina if she survive is my sole heir. If it should not be so, then the others who have been so good to us shall have remainder.", "미나가, 만일 그녀가 살아남는다면, 내 유일한 상속자다. 만일 그렇게 되지 못한다면, 우리에게 그토록 잘해준 다른 이들이 잔여 재산을 받게 될 것이다."]], [["It is now drawing towards the sunset; Mina’s uneasiness calls my attention to it.", "이제 해넘이가 다가오고 있다. 미나의 불안이 내 주의를 그쪽으로 돌리게 한다."], ["I am sure that there is something on her mind which the time of exact sunset will reveal.", "정확한 해넘이의 시각이 드러낼 무언가가 그녀의 마음에 있음을 나는 확신한다."], ["These occasions are becoming harrowing times for us all, for each sunrise and sunset opens up some new danger--some new pain, which, however, may in God’s will be means to a good end.", "이런 때들이 우리 모두에게 괴로운 시간이 되어가고 있다. 매번의 해돋이와 해넘이가 어떤 새로운 위험을 — 어떤 새로운 고통을 열어 보이기 때문이다. 그러나 그것은 하느님의 뜻 안에서 좋은 끝에 이르는 수단이 될지도 모른다."], ["I write all these things in the diary since my darling must not hear them now; but if it may be that she can see them again, they shall be ready.", "나는 이 모든 것을 일기에 적는다. 내 사랑이 지금은 그것을 들어서는 안 되기 때문이다. 그러나 만일 그녀가 언젠가 그것을 다시 볼 수 있게 된다면, 그것들은 준비되어 있을 것이다."]], [["She is calling to me.", "그녀가 나를 부르고 있다."]], [["# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY", "# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY"]], [["11 October, Evening.--Jonathan Harker has asked me to note this, as he says he is hardly equal to the task, and he wants an exact record kept.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」 10월 11일, 저녁. — 조너선 하커가 나더러 이것을 기록해 달라고 부탁했다. 자기는 도저히 그 일을 감당할 수 없을 것 같다며, 정확한 기록을 남기고 싶다는 것이었다."]], [["I think that none of us were surprised when we were asked to see Mrs. Harker a little before the time of sunset.", "해 질 무렵을 조금 앞두고 하커 부인을 만나러 오라는 청을 받았을 때, 우리 가운데 누구도 놀라지 않았으리라 생각한다."], ["We have of late come to understand that sunrise and sunset are to her times of peculiar freedom; when her old self can be manifest without any controlling force subduing or restraining her, or inciting her to action.", "우리는 최근 들어 일출과 일몰이 그녀에게 각별한 자유의 시간임을 이해하게 되었다. 그때에는 어떤 지배하는 힘도 그녀를 억누르거나 옭아매거나 행동으로 내몰지 못한 채, 그녀의 본래 자아가 드러날 수 있었다."], ["This mood or condition begins some half hour or more before actual sunrise or sunset, and lasts till either the sun is high, or whilst the clouds are still aglow with the rays streaming above the horizon.", "이러한 기분, 혹은 상태는 실제 일출이나 일몰보다 반 시간쯤, 혹은 그 이상 앞서 시작되어, 해가 높이 떠오르거나 구름이 지평선 위로 쏟아지는 햇살에 여전히 붉게 물들어 있는 동안까지 이어졌다."], ["At first there is a sort of negative condition, as if some tie were loosened, and then the absolute freedom quickly follows; when, however, the freedom ceases the change-back or relapse comes quickly, preceded only by a spell of warning silence.", "처음에는 일종의 소극적인 상태가 나타나는데, 마치 어떤 매듭이 풀어진 듯한 것이었고, 곧이어 완전한 자유가 빠르게 뒤따랐다. 그러나 그 자유가 끝나면 본래의 상태로 되돌아가거나 다시 빠져드는 변화가 빠르게 찾아왔는데, 그에 앞서는 것은 오직 경고하는 듯한 침묵의 한순간뿐이었다."]], [["To-night, when we met, she was somewhat constrained, and bore all the signs of an internal struggle.", "오늘 밤 우리가 만났을 때, 그녀는 다소 긴장한 듯했고, 내면의 갈등을 드러내는 온갖 기색을 띠고 있었다."], ["I put it down myself to her making a violent effort at the earliest instant she could do so.", "나로서는 그것을 그녀가 할 수 있는 가장 이른 순간에 격렬한 노력을 기울이고 있는 탓이라 여겼다."], ["A very few minutes, however, gave her complete control of herself; then, motioning her husband to sit beside her on the sofa where she was half reclining, she made the rest of us bring chairs up close.", "그러나 채 몇 분도 지나지 않아 그녀는 자신을 완전히 다스리게 되었다. 그러고는 반쯤 기대어 있던 소파에 남편을 자기 곁에 앉도록 손짓하고, 우리 나머지에게는 의자를 가까이 끌어오게 했다."], ["Taking her husband’s hand in hers began:--", "남편의 손을 자기 손에 잡고 그녀가 입을 열었다."]], [["“We are all here together in freedom, for perhaps the last time!", "\"우리는 지금 모두 여기 함께, 자유롭게 모여 있어요. 어쩌면 이것이 마지막일지도 몰라요!"], ["I know, dear; I know that you will always be with me to the end.”", "알아요, 여보. 당신이 끝까지 늘 저와 함께하리란 걸 알아요.\""], ["This was to her husband whose hand had, as we could see, tightened upon hers.", "이 말은 남편에게 한 것이었는데, 우리가 보기에도 그의 손이 그녀의 손을 꼭 움켜쥐고 있었다."], ["“In the morning we go out upon our task, and God alone knows what may be in store for any of us.", "\"아침이면 우리는 우리의 임무를 향해 나서고, 우리 가운데 누구에게 무슨 일이 닥칠지는 오직 하느님만이 아실 거예요."], ["You are going to be so good to me as to take me with you. I know that all that brave earnest men can do for a poor weak woman, whose soul perhaps is lost--no, no, not yet, but is at any rate at stake--you will do.", "당신은 저를 데려가실 만큼 다정하시겠죠. 가엾고 연약한, 어쩌면 그 영혼이 이미 길을 잃은 — 아니, 아니에요, 아직은 아니지만, 어쨌든 위태로운 — 여인을 위해 용감하고 진실한 사내들이 할 수 있는 모든 일을 당신들이 해주시리란 걸 알아요."], ["But you must remember that I am not as you are.", "하지만 제가 당신들과 같지 않다는 걸 기억하셔야 해요."], ["There is a poison in my blood, in my soul, which may destroy me; which must destroy me, unless some relief comes to us.", "제 피 속에, 제 영혼 속에 저를 파멸시킬지도 모를 독이 있어요. 우리에게 어떤 구원이 오지 않는 한, 그것은 반드시 저를 파멸시키고 말 거예요."], ["Oh, my friends, you know as well as I do, that my soul is at stake; and though I know there is one way out for me, you must not and I must not take it!”", "오, 벗들이여, 제 영혼이 위태롭다는 걸 여러분도 저만큼이나 잘 아실 거예요. 그리고 제게 빠져나갈 길이 하나 있다는 걸 알지만, 여러분도 저도 그 길을 택해서는 안 돼요!\""], ["She looked appealingly to us all in turn, beginning and ending with her husband.", "그녀는 차례로 우리 모두를 향해 애원하듯 바라보았는데, 남편에서 시작해 남편에서 끝맺었다."]], [["“What is that way?”", "\"그 길이 무엇이오?\""], ["asked Van Helsing in a hoarse voice.", "판 헬싱이 쉰 목소리로 물었다."], ["“What is that way, which we must not--may not--take?”", "\"우리가 택해서는 안 될 — 택할 수 없는 — 그 길이 무엇이오?\""]], [["“That I may die now, either by my own hand or that of another, before the greater evil is entirely wrought.", "\"더 큰 악이 완전히 이루어지기 전에, 제 손으로든 다른 누구의 손으로든, 제가 지금 죽는 것이에요."], ["I know, and you know, that were I once dead you could and would set free my immortal spirit, even as you did my poor Lucy’s.", "일단 제가 죽기만 하면, 가엾은 루시에게 그러하셨듯이, 여러분이 제 불멸의 영혼을 풀어주실 수 있고 또 풀어주시리란 걸 저도 알고 여러분도 알아요."], ["Were death, or the fear of death, the only thing that stood in the way I would not shrink to die here, now, amidst the friends who love me.", "만일 죽음이, 혹은 죽음에 대한 두려움이 앞을 가로막는 유일한 것이라면, 저는 저를 사랑하는 벗들 가운데서 지금 여기서 죽는 일을 마다하지 않을 거예요."], ["But death is not all. I cannot believe that to die in such a case, when there is hope before us and a bitter task to be done, is God’s will.", "하지만 죽음이 전부는 아니에요. 우리 앞에 희망이 있고 쓰라린 임무가 남아 있는 이런 처지에서 죽는 것이 하느님의 뜻이라고는 믿을 수 없어요."], ["Therefore, I, on my part, give up here the certainty of eternal rest, and go out into the dark where may be the blackest things that the world or the nether world holds!”", "그러니 저로서는, 여기서 영원한 안식의 확실함을 포기하고, 이 세상이나 저 지하 세계가 품은 가장 시커먼 것들이 있을지도 모를 어둠 속으로 나아가겠어요!\""], ["We were all silent, for we knew instinctively that this was only a prelude.", "우리는 모두 침묵했으니, 이것이 단지 서막일 뿐임을 본능적으로 알았기 때문이다."], ["The faces of the others were set and Harker’s grew ashen grey; perhaps he guessed better than any of us what was coming.", "다른 이들의 얼굴은 굳어 있었고, 하커의 얼굴은 잿빛으로 변했다. 어쩌면 그는 우리 가운데 누구보다도 무엇이 닥쳐올지를 잘 짐작했는지도 모른다."], ["She continued:--", "그녀는 말을 이었다."]], [["“This is what I can give into the hotch-pot.”", "\"이것이 제가 공동의 몫으로 내놓을 수 있는 것이에요.\""], ["I could not but note the quaint legal phrase which she used in such a place, and with all seriousness.", "나는 그녀가 그런 자리에서, 그토록 진지하게 쓰는 그 기묘한 법률 용어를 알아채지 않을 수 없었다. \"여러분은 저마다 무엇을 내놓으시겠어요?"], ["“What will each of you give?", "여러분의 목숨이겠지요, 저도 알아요.\""], ["Your lives I know,” she went on quickly, “that is easy for brave men.", "그녀는 빠르게 말을 이었다. \"그건 용감한 사내들에게는 쉬운 일이지요."], ["Your lives are God’s, and you can give them back to Him; but what will you give to me?”", "여러분의 목숨은 하느님의 것이고, 여러분은 그것을 그분께 돌려드릴 수 있어요. 하지만 제게는 무엇을 내놓으시겠어요?\""], ["She looked again questioningly, but this time avoided her husband’s face.", "그녀는 다시 묻는 듯한 눈빛으로 바라보았으나, 이번에는 남편의 얼굴을 피했다."], ["Quincey seemed to understand; he nodded, and her face lit up.", "퀸시는 알아들은 듯했다. 그가 고개를 끄덕이자 그녀의 얼굴이 환해졌다."], ["“Then I shall tell you plainly what I want, for there must be no doubtful matter in this connection between us now.", "\"그러면 제가 원하는 바를 분명히 말씀드리겠어요. 이제 우리 사이의 이 일에 조금의 의심도 남아서는 안 되니까요."], ["You must promise me, one and all--even you, my beloved husband--that, should the time come, you will kill me.”", "여러분 모두 — 당신마저도, 사랑하는 남편이여 — 약속해 주세요. 그때가 오거든, 저를 죽여주시겠다고요.\""]], [["“What is that time?”", "\"그때가 언제요?\""], ["The voice was Quincey’s, but it was low and strained.", "그것은 퀸시의 목소리였으나, 낮고 팽팽하게 긴장되어 있었다."]], [["“When you shall be convinced that I am so changed that it is better that I die than I may live.", "\"제가 너무도 변해버려서 사는 것보다 죽는 편이 낫다고 여러분이 확신하게 될 때요."], ["When I am thus dead in the flesh, then you will, without a moment’s delay, drive a stake through me and cut off my head; or do whatever else may be wanting to give me rest!”", "제가 그렇게 육신으로 죽거든, 여러분은 한순간도 지체하지 말고 제 몸에 말뚝을 박고 제 목을 잘라주세요. 아니면 제게 안식을 주기 위해 필요한 무엇이든 해주세요!\""]], [["Quincey was the first to rise after the pause.", "침묵이 흐른 뒤 가장 먼저 일어선 것은 퀸시였다."], ["He knelt down before her and taking her hand in his said solemnly:--", "그는 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 그녀의 손을 자기 손에 잡고는 엄숙하게 말했다."]], [["“I’m only a rough fellow, who hasn’t, perhaps, lived as a man should to win such a distinction, but I swear to you by all that I hold sacred and dear that, should the time ever come, I shall not flinch from the duty that you have set us.", "\"저는 그저 거친 사내일 뿐이고, 어쩌면 그런 영예를 얻을 만큼 사내답게 살지 못했을지도 모릅니다. 하지만 제가 신성하고 소중히 여기는 모든 것을 걸고 부인께 맹세합니다. 그때가 오거든, 부인께서 우리에게 지우신 그 의무 앞에서 결코 물러서지 않겠습니다."], ["And I promise you, too, that I shall make all certain, for if I am only doubtful I shall take it that the time has come!”", "그리고 또 약속드리거니와, 저는 모든 것을 확실히 하겠습니다. 만일 조금이라도 의심스럽다면, 저는 그때가 왔다고 여기겠습니다!\""]], [["“My true friend!”", "\"진실한 벗이여!\""], ["was all she could say amid her fast-falling tears, as, bending over, she kissed his hand.", "그녀가 빠르게 흘러내리는 눈물 속에서 할 수 있는 말이라곤 그것뿐이었고, 그녀는 몸을 굽혀 그의 손에 입을 맞추었다."]], [["“I swear the same, my dear Madam Mina!”", "\"나도 똑같이 맹세하오, 친애하는 미나 부인!\""], ["said Van Helsing.", "판 헬싱이 말했다."]], [["“And I!”", "\"저도요!\""], ["said Lord Godalming, each of them in turn kneeling to her to take the oath.", "고달밍 경이 말했고, 그들은 저마다 차례로 그녀 앞에 무릎을 꿇고 맹세를 했다."], ["I followed, myself.", "나도 뒤따랐다."], ["Then her husband turned to her wan-eyed and with a greenish pallor which subdued the snowy whiteness of his hair, and asked:--", "그러자 그녀의 남편이 그녀를 향해 돌아섰는데, 그 눈은 핏기가 없었고, 머리칼의 눈처럼 흰빛마저 가라앉힐 만큼 푸르스름한 창백함을 띤 채 물었다."]], [["“And must I, too, make such a promise, oh, my wife?”", "\"나도, 그런 약속을 해야 하오, 오, 내 아내여?\""]], [["“You too, my dearest,”", "\"당신도요, 사랑하는 이여.\""], ["she said, with infinite yearning of pity in her voice and eyes.", "그녀가 목소리와 눈빛에 한없는 연민의 그리움을 담아 말했다."], ["“You must not shrink.", "\"물러서서는 안 돼요."], ["You are nearest and dearest and all the world to me; our souls are knit into one, for all life and all time.", "당신은 제게 가장 가깝고 가장 소중하며 온 세상이에요. 우리 영혼은 온 생애와 온 세월에 걸쳐 하나로 맺어져 있어요."], ["Think, dear, that there have been times when brave men have killed their wives and their womenkind, to keep them from falling into the hands of the enemy.", "생각해 보세요, 여보. 용감한 사내들이 아내와 제 집안 여인들을 적의 손에 넘어가지 않게 하려고 죽인 때가 있었어요."], ["Their hands did not falter any the more because those that they loved implored them to slay them.", "사랑하는 이들이 자기를 죽여달라고 애원했다고 해서 그들의 손이 더 떨린 것도 아니었어요."], ["It is men’s duty towards those whom they love, in such times of sore trial!", "그런 혹독한 시련의 때에, 그것은 사랑하는 이들을 향한 사내의 의무예요!"], ["And oh, my dear, if it is to be that I must meet death at any hand, let it be at the hand of him that loves me best. Dr.", "그리고 오, 여보, 만일 제가 어느 손에든 죽음을 맞아야 한다면, 저를 가장 사랑하는 이의 손에 맞게 해주세요."], ["Van Helsing, I have not forgotten your mercy in poor Lucy’s case to him who loved”--she stopped with a flying blush, and changed her phrase--“to him who had best right to give her peace.", "판 헬싱 박사님, 가엾은 루시의 일에서, 그녀를 사랑하던 — \" 그녀는 얼핏 얼굴을 붉히며 말을 멈추고는 표현을 바꾸었다. \"— 그녀에게 평안을 줄 가장 마땅한 권리를 지녔던 이에게 베푸신 자비를 저는 잊지 않았어요."], ["If that time shall come again, I look to you to make it a happy memory of my husband’s life that it was his loving hand which set me free from the awful thrall upon me.”", "만일 그때가 다시 온다면, 저를 짓누르는 이 끔찍한 굴레에서 저를 풀어준 것이 남편의 사랑 어린 손이었다는 사실을, 제 남편의 생애에 행복한 기억으로 만들어 주시길 박사님께 바라요.\""]], [["“Again I swear!” came the Professor’s resonant voice. Mrs.", "\"다시금 맹세하오!\" 교수의 울리는 목소리가 들려왔다."], ["Harker smiled, positively smiled, as with a sigh of relief she leaned back and said:--", "하커 부인은 미소를, 정말로 미소를 지으며, 안도의 한숨과 함께 등을 기대고 말했다."]], [["“And now one word of warning, a warning which you must never forget: this time, if it ever come, may come quickly and unexpectedly, and in such case you must lose no time in using your opportunity.", "\"그리고 이제 한마디 경고를 드릴게요. 절대로 잊으셔서는 안 될 경고예요. 그때가, 만일 그것이 정말 온다면, 빠르고 뜻밖에 닥칠지도 몰라요."], ["At such a time I myself might be--nay!", "그런 경우에는 한순간도 지체하지 말고 기회를 잡으셔야 해요."], ["if the time ever comes, shall be--leagued with your enemy against you.”", "그런 때에 저 자신은 — 아니! 그때가 정말 온다면, 저는 분명히 — 여러분에게 맞서 여러분의 적과 한편이 되어 있을 거예요.\""]], [["“One more request;”", "\"한 가지 더 부탁이 있어요.\""], ["she became very solemn as she said this, “it is not vital and necessary like the other, but I want you to do one thing for me, if you will.”", "그녀는 이 말을 하면서 무척 엄숙해졌다. \"이건 다른 것처럼 절실하고 꼭 필요한 일은 아니지만, 만일 들어주신다면 한 가지를 해주셨으면 해요.\""], ["We all acquiesced, but no one spoke; there was no need to speak:--", "우리는 모두 동의했으나 아무도 말하지 않았다. 말할 필요가 없었던 것이다."]], [["“I want you to read the Burial Service.”", "\"장례 기도문을 읽어주셨으면 해요.\""], ["She was interrupted by a deep groan from her husband; taking his hand in hers, she held it over her heart, and continued: “You must read it over me some day.", "그녀는 남편의 깊은 신음에 말이 끊겼다. 그의 손을 자기 손에 잡아 가슴 위에 얹고는 말을 이었다. \"언젠가 제 위에서 그것을 읽어주셔야 해요."], ["Whatever may be the issue of all this fearful state of things, it will be a sweet thought to all or some of us.", "이 무서운 사태가 어떻게 끝나든, 그것은 우리 모두에게, 혹은 그 가운데 누군가에게 다정한 위안이 될 거예요."], ["You, my dearest, will I hope read it, for then it will be in your voice in my memory for ever--come what may!”", "당신이, 사랑하는 이여, 그것을 읽어주시길 바라요. 그러면 무슨 일이 닥치든 그것은 제 기억 속에 영원히 당신의 목소리로 남을 테니까요!\""]], [["“But oh, my dear one,”", "\"하지만 오, 내 사랑.\""], ["he pleaded, “death is afar off from you.”", "그가 애원했다. \"죽음은 당신에게서 멀리 떨어져 있소.\""]], [["“Nay,” she said, holding up a warning hand.", "\"아니에요.\" 그녀가 경고하듯 손을 들어 올리며 말했다."], ["“I am deeper in death at this moment than if the weight of an earthly grave lay heavy upon me!”", "\"저는 지금 이 순간, 흙으로 된 무덤의 무게가 무겁게 저를 짓누르는 것보다 더 깊이 죽음 속에 잠겨 있어요!\""]], [["“Oh, my wife, must I read it?”", "\"오, 내 아내여, 정말 읽어야 하오?\""], ["he said, before he began.", "그가 시작하기에 앞서 말했다."]], [["“It would comfort me, my husband!”", "\"그것이 제게 위안이 될 거예요, 여보!\""], ["was all she said; and he began to read when she had got the book ready.", "그녀가 한 말은 그것뿐이었다. 그녀가 책을 준비해 주자 그는 읽기 시작했다."]], [["“How can I--how could any one--tell of that strange scene, its solemnity, its gloom, its sadness, its horror; and, withal, its sweetness.", "\"내가 어떻게 — 그 누가 어떻게 — 그 기이한 광경을, 그 엄숙함과 음울함과 슬픔과 공포를, 그러면서도 그 감미로움을 이야기할 수 있을까."], ["Even a sceptic, who can see nothing but a travesty of bitter truth in anything holy or emotional, would have been melted to the heart had he seen that little group of loving and devoted friends kneeling round that stricken and sorrowing lady; or heard the tender passion of her husband’s voice, as in tones so broken with emotion that often he had to pause, he read the simple and beautiful service from the Burial of the Dead. I--I cannot go on--words--and--v-voice--f-fail m-me!”", "거룩하거나 가슴 저미는 그 무엇에서도 쓰라린 진실의 우스꽝스러운 흉내밖에 보지 못하는 회의론자라 할지라도, 병들고 비탄에 잠긴 그 부인을 둘러싸고 무릎 꿇은 사랑과 헌신으로 뭉친 작은 무리를 보았다면, 혹은 감정에 너무도 메어 자주 멈춰야 할 만큼 끊기는 목소리로 죽은 자의 장례에서 그 소박하고 아름다운 기도문을 읽어 내려가던 남편의 그 애틋한 정열의 목소리를 들었다면, 그 마음이 녹아내리고 말았으리라. 나는 — 나는 더 이상 쓸 수가 없다 — 말이 — 그리고 — 모, 목소리가 — 나, 나를 저버린다!\""]], [["She was right in her instinct.", "그녀의 직감은 옳았다."], ["Strange as it all was, bizarre as it may hereafter seem even to us who felt its potent influence at the time, it comforted us much; and the silence, which showed Mrs. Harker’s coming relapse from her freedom of soul, did not seem so full of despair to any of us as we had dreaded.", "그 모든 것이 기이했고, 그 강력한 영향을 당시에 느꼈던 우리에게마저 훗날 아무리 괴이하게 여겨질지라도, 그것은 우리에게 큰 위안이 되었다. 그리고 하커 부인이 영혼의 자유로부터 다시 빠져드는 변화가 다가옴을 보여주는 그 침묵도, 우리 가운데 누구에게도 우리가 두려워했던 만큼 절망으로 가득해 보이지는 않았다."]], [["Jonathan Harker’s Journal.", "「조너선 하커의 일기」"]], [["15 October, Varna.--We left Charing Cross on the morning of the 12th, got to Paris the same night, and took the places secured for us in the Orient Express.", "10월 15일, 바르나. — 우리는 12일 아침에 채링 크로스를 떠나 같은 날 밤 파리에 닿았고, 우리를 위해 잡아둔 오리엔트 특급의 자리에 올랐다."], ["We travelled night and day, arriving here at about five o’clock.", "우리는 밤낮으로 여행한 끝에 다섯 시쯤 이곳에 도착했다."], ["Lord Godalming went to the Consulate to see if any telegram had arrived for him, whilst the rest of us came on to this hotel--“the Odessus.”", "고달밍 경은 그에게 온 전보가 있는지 알아보러 영사관에 갔고, 나머지 우리는 이 호텔 — \"오데수스\" — 로 곧장 왔다."], ["The journey may have had incidents; I was, however, too eager to get on, to care for them.", "여정에 무슨 일들이 있었는지도 모르겠으나, 나는 앞으로 나아가고픈 마음이 너무 간절해 그런 것에 신경 쓸 겨를이 없었다."], ["Until the Czarina Catherine comes into port there will be no interest for me in anything in the wide world. Thank God!", "차리나 카테리나호가 항구에 들어오기 전까지는 이 넓은 세상의 어떤 것도 내게는 흥미를 끌지 못할 것이다. 하느님, 감사합니다!"], ["Mina is well, and looks to be getting stronger; her colour is coming back.", "미나는 건강하고, 점점 기력을 되찾는 듯 보인다. 혈색이 돌아오고 있다."], ["She sleeps a great deal; throughout the journey she slept nearly all the time.", "그녀는 잠을 많이 잔다. 여행 내내 그녀는 거의 줄곧 잠들어 있었다."], ["Before sunrise and sunset, however, she is very wakeful and alert; and it has become a habit for Van Helsing to hypnotise her at such times.", "그러나 일출과 일몰 직전에는 무척 깨어 있고 또렷이 깨어 있는데, 그런 때에 판 헬싱이 그녀에게 최면을 거는 것이 어느덧 습관이 되었다."], ["At first, some effort was needed, and he had to make many passes; but now, she seems to yield at once, as if by habit, and scarcely any action is needed.", "처음에는 어느 정도 노력이 필요해서 그가 여러 번 손짓을 해야 했으나, 이제 그녀는 마치 익숙해진 듯 곧바로 응하며, 거의 아무런 동작도 필요하지 않다."], ["He seems to have power at these particular moments to simply will, and her thoughts obey him.", "그는 이런 특정한 순간들에 단지 의지를 발하기만 해도 되는 힘을 지닌 듯하고, 그녀의 생각은 그에게 복종한다."], ["He always asks her what she can see and hear.", "그는 늘 그녀에게 무엇을 보고 들을 수 있느냐고 묻는다."], ["She answers to the first:--", "첫 번째 물음에 그녀는 이렇게 답한다."]], [["“Nothing; all is dark.”", "\"아무것도요. 온통 어둡습니다.\""], ["And to the second:--", "그리고 두 번째 물음에는 이렇게 답한다."]], [["“I can hear the waves lapping against the ship, and the water rushing by.", "\"배에 찰싹이는 파도 소리와 스쳐 지나가는 물소리가 들려요."], ["Canvas and cordage strain and masts and yards creak.", "돛과 밧줄이 팽팽히 당겨지고 돛대와 활대가 삐걱거려요."], ["The wind is high--I can hear it in the shrouds, and the bow throws back the foam.”", "바람이 세차요 — 삭구(索具) 사이로 그 소리가 들리고, 뱃머리가 물거품을 뒤로 흩뿌려요.\""], ["It is evident that the Czarina Catherine is still at sea, hastening on her way to Varna.", "차리나 카테리나호가 아직 바다 위에서, 바르나를 향해 서둘러 나아가고 있음이 분명하다."], ["Lord Godalming has just returned.", "고달밍 경이 방금 돌아왔다."], ["He had four telegrams, one each day since we started, and all to the same effect: that the Czarina Catherine had not been reported to Lloyd’s from anywhere.", "그는 전보 네 통을 받았는데, 우리가 출발한 이래 날마다 한 통씩이었고, 모두 같은 내용이었다. 차리나 카테리나호가 어디에서도 로이즈에 보고되지 않았다는 것이었다."], ["He had arranged before leaving London that his agent should send him every day a telegram saying if the ship had been reported.", "그는 런던을 떠나기 전에 자기 대리인더러 배가 보고되었는지를 알리는 전보를 날마다 보내도록 일러두었던 것이다."], ["He was to have a message even if she were not reported, so that he might be sure that there was a watch being kept at the other end of the wire.", "보고되지 않았더라도 전갈을 받기로 했는데, 그래야 전선 저편에서 감시가 이루어지고 있음을 확신할 수 있었기 때문이다. * 로이즈: 영국 런던의 해상보험 조합으로, 전 세계 선박의 운항 정보를 집계했다."]], [["We had dinner and went to bed early.", "우리는 저녁을 먹고 일찍 잠자리에 들었다."], ["To-morrow we are to see the Vice-Consul, and to arrange, if we can, about getting on board the ship as soon as she arrives.", "내일 우리는 부영사를 만나, 가능하면 배가 도착하는 즉시 승선하는 일에 관해 주선할 작정이다."], ["Van Helsing says that our chance will be to get on the boat between sunrise and sunset.", "판 헬싱은 우리에게 기회가 있다면 일출과 일몰 사이에 배에 오르는 것이라고 말한다."], ["The Count, even if he takes the form of a bat, cannot cross the running water of his own volition, and so cannot leave the ship. As he dare not change to man’s form without suspicion--which he evidently wishes to avoid--he must remain in the box.", "백작은 설령 박쥐의 모습을 취한다 해도 제 뜻대로 흐르는 물을 건널 수 없으니, 배를 떠날 수 없다. 그리고 의심을 사지 않고서는 — 그가 분명히 피하고 싶어 하는 일이거니와 — 감히 사람의 모습으로 변할 수 없으므로, 그는 상자 안에 머물러 있어야 한다."], ["If, then, we can come on board after sunrise, he is at our mercy; for we can open the box and make sure of him, as we did of poor Lucy, before he wakes.", "그러니 우리가 일출 후에 승선할 수 있다면, 그는 우리 손아귀에 들어 있는 셈이다. 가엾은 루시에게 그랬던 것처럼, 그가 깨어나기 전에 상자를 열고 그를 확실히 처치할 수 있기 때문이다."], ["What mercy he shall get from us will not count for much.", "그가 우리에게서 받을 자비란 그리 대단치 않을 것이다."], ["We think that we shall not have much trouble with officials or the seamen. Thank God!", "관리나 선원들과는 큰 어려움을 겪지 않으리라 생각한다. 하느님, 감사합니다!"], ["this is the country where bribery can do anything, and we are well supplied with money.", "이곳은 뇌물이면 무엇이든 할 수 있는 나라이고, 우리에게는 돈이 넉넉하다."], ["We have only to make sure that the ship cannot come into port between sunset and sunrise without our being warned, and we shall be safe.", "우리가 모르는 새에 배가 일몰과 일출 사이에 입항하는 일이 없도록만 확실히 해두면, 우리는 안전할 것이다."], ["Judge Moneybag will settle this case, I think!", "돈주머니 판사께서 이 사건을 해결해 주시리라 생각한다!"]], [["16 October.--Mina’s report still the same: lapping waves and rushing water, darkness and favouring winds.", "10월 16일. — 미나의 보고는 여전히 똑같다. 찰싹이는 파도와 스쳐 지나가는 물소리, 어둠과 순풍."], ["We are evidently in good time, and when we hear of the Czarina Catherine we shall be ready.", "우리는 분명 시간을 잘 맞추고 있고, 차리나 카테리나호의 소식을 들으면 준비가 되어 있을 것이다."], ["As she must pass the Dardanelles we are sure to have some report.", "배가 다르다넬스 해협을 지나야 하므로, 어떤 보고든 분명히 받게 될 것이다."]], [["17 October.--Everything is pretty well fixed now, I think, to welcome the Count on his return from his tour.", "10월 17일. — 이제는 백작이 여행에서 돌아오는 것을 맞이할 모든 채비가 꽤 잘 갖추어진 듯하다."], ["Godalming told the shippers that he fancied that the box sent aboard might contain something stolen from a friend of his, and got a half consent that he might open it at his own risk.", "고달밍은 선박 회사 측에 그 배에 실린 상자에 자기 친구에게서 도난당한 무언가가 들어 있을지도 모른다고 말하여, 자기 책임 아래 그것을 열어봐도 좋다는 반승낙을 얻어냈다."], ["The owner gave him a paper telling the Captain to give him every facility in doing whatever he chose on board the ship, and also a similar authorisation to his agent at Varna.", "선주는 그에게 선장으로 하여금 그가 배에서 원하는 무엇이든 할 수 있도록 모든 편의를 제공하라는 서류를 주었고, 바르나의 대리인에게도 비슷한 권한을 주는 서류를 주었다."], ["We have seen the agent, who was much impressed with Godalming’s kindly manner to him, and we are all satisfied that whatever he can do to aid our wishes will be done.", "우리는 그 대리인을 만났는데, 그는 고달밍의 친절한 태도에 크게 감동했다. 우리는 모두 그가 우리 뜻을 돕기 위해 할 수 있는 일이라면 무엇이든 해주리라 확신했다."], ["We have already arranged what to do in case we get the box open.", "우리는 상자를 열게 될 경우 무엇을 할지 이미 정해두었다."], ["If the Count is there, Van Helsing and Seward will cut off his head at once and drive a stake through his heart.", "만일 백작이 그 안에 있다면, 판 헬싱과 수어드가 즉시 그의 목을 자르고 그의 심장에 말뚝을 박을 것이다."], ["Morris and Godalming and I shall prevent interference, even if we have to use the arms which we shall have ready.", "모리스와 고달밍과 나는, 설령 준비해 둘 무기를 써야 하는 한이 있더라도, 방해를 막을 것이다."], ["The Professor says that if we can so treat the Count’s body, it will soon after fall into dust.", "교수의 말로는, 우리가 백작의 몸을 그렇게 처리할 수 있다면 그것은 곧 먼지로 화하리라고 한다."], ["In such case there would be no evidence against us, in case any suspicion of murder were aroused.", "그런 경우에는 혹여 살인의 혐의가 일더라도 우리에게 불리한 증거가 남지 않을 것이다."], ["But even if it were not, we should stand or fall by our act, and perhaps some day this very script may be evidence to come between some of us and a rope.", "그러나 설령 그렇지 않더라도, 우리는 우리의 행위로 흥하거나 망할 것이며, 어쩌면 언젠가는 바로 이 기록이 우리 가운데 누군가와 교수형의 밧줄 사이에 끼어드는 증거가 될지도 모른다."], ["For myself, I should take the chance only too thankfully if it were to come.", "나로 말하자면, 그런 일이 닥친다면 더없이 감사한 마음으로 그 위험을 무릅쓸 것이다."], ["We mean to leave no stone unturned to carry out our intent.", "우리는 우리의 뜻을 이루기 위해 온갖 수단을 다 강구할 작정이다."], ["We have arranged with certain officials that the instant the Czarina Catherine is seen, we are to be informed by a special messenger.", "우리는 차리나 카테리나호가 목격되는 즉시 특별 전령을 통해 우리에게 알리도록 몇몇 관리들과 약조해 두었다."]], [["24 October.--A whole week of waiting.", "10월 24일. — 꼬박 한 주를 기다렸다."], ["Daily telegrams to Godalming, but only the same story: “Not yet reported.”", "날마다 고달밍에게 전보가 왔으나, 한결같은 이야기뿐이었다. \"아직 보고되지 않음.\""], ["Mina’s morning and evening hypnotic answer is unvaried: lapping waves, rushing water, and creaking masts.", "미나의 아침저녁 최면 답변도 변함이 없다. 찰싹이는 파도, 스쳐 지나가는 물소리, 삐걱거리는 돛대."]], [["Telegram, October 24th.", "전보, 10월 24일."]], [["Rufus Smith, Lloyd’s, London, to Lord Godalming, care of H. B. M. Vice-Consul, Varna.", "런던 로이즈의 루퍼스 스미스가, 바르나 주재 영국 부영사 편으로 고달밍 경에게."]], [["“Czarina Catherine reported this morning from Dardanelles.”", "\"차리나 카테리나호, 오늘 아침 다르다넬스 해협에서 보고됨.\""]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["25 October.--How I miss my phonograph!", "10월 25일. — 내 축음기가 어찌나 그리운지!"], ["To write diary with a pen is irksome to me; but Van Helsing says I must.", "펜으로 일기를 쓰는 것은 내게 성가신 일이지만, 판 헬싱은 내가 꼭 그래야 한다고 말한다."], ["We were all wild with excitement yesterday when Godalming got his telegram from Lloyd’s.", "어제 고달밍이 로이즈에서 전보를 받았을 때 우리는 모두 흥분에 들끓었다."], ["I know now what men feel in battle when the call to action is heard. Mrs.", "나는 이제 사내들이 전장에서 출동 명령을 들었을 때 무엇을 느끼는지 알겠다."], ["Harker, alone of our party, did not show any signs of emotion.", "우리 일행 가운데 하커 부인만이 아무런 감정의 기색도 드러내지 않았다."], ["After all, it is not strange that she did not; for we took special care not to let her know anything about it, and we all tried not to show any excitement when we were in her presence.", "하기야 그녀가 그러지 않은 것도 이상한 일은 아니다. 우리는 그녀가 그 일에 관해 아무것도 알지 못하도록 각별히 조심했고, 모두 그녀 앞에서는 어떤 흥분도 내보이지 않으려 애썼기 때문이다."], ["In old days she would, I am sure, have noticed, no matter how we might have tried to conceal it; but in this way she is greatly changed during the past three weeks.", "예전 같았으면 우리가 아무리 감추려 애써도 그녀가 분명 알아챘으리라 확신한다. 그러나 이 점에서 그녀는 지난 삼 주 동안 크게 변했다."], ["The lethargy grows upon her, and though she seems strong and well, and is getting back some of her colour, Van Helsing and I are not satisfied.", "무기력이 그녀에게 점점 짙어가고, 비록 그녀가 강건하고 건강해 보이며 혈색을 얼마간 되찾고 있긴 하나, 판 헬싱과 나는 마음을 놓지 못한다."], ["We talk of her often; we have not, however, said a word to the others.", "우리는 그녀에 관해 자주 이야기를 나눈다. 그러나 다른 이들에게는 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["It would break poor Harker’s heart--certainly his nerve--if he knew that we had even a suspicion on the subject.", "우리가 이 문제에 관해 조금이라도 의심을 품고 있음을 하커가 안다면, 그것은 가엾은 하커의 가슴을 — 분명 그의 신경을 — 무너뜨리고 말 것이다."], ["Van Helsing examines, he tells me, her teeth very carefully, whilst she is in the hypnotic condition, for he says that so long as they do not begin to sharpen there is no active danger of a change in her.", "판 헬싱은 그녀가 최면 상태에 있는 동안 그녀의 이를 무척 면밀히 살핀다고 내게 말했는데, 그의 말로는 이가 날카로워지기 시작하지 않는 한 그녀에게 변화가 일어날 적극적인 위험은 없다는 것이다."], ["If this change should come, it would be necessary to take steps!...", "만일 이 변화가 온다면, 조치를 취해야 할 것이다!…"], ["We both know what those steps would have to be, though we do not mention our thoughts to each other.", "우리는 둘 다 그 조치가 무엇이 되어야 할지를 알고 있으나, 서로에게 그 생각을 입 밖에 내지는 않는다."], ["We should neither of us shrink from the task--awful though it be to contemplate.", "우리 둘 가운데 누구도 그 임무 앞에서 — 생각만으로도 끔찍한 일이긴 하나 — 물러서지는 않을 것이다."], ["“Euthanasia” is an excellent and a comforting word!", "\"안락사\"란 더없이 훌륭하고 위안을 주는 말이다!"], ["I am grateful to whoever invented it.", "나는 그 말을 지어낸 이가 누구든 그에게 감사한다."]], [["It is only about 24 hours’ sail from the Dardanelles to here, at the rate the Czarina Catherine has come from London.", "차리나 카테리나호가 런던에서 온 속도로 보아, 다르다넬스 해협에서 이곳까지는 약 스물네 시간의 항해 거리에 지나지 않는다."], ["She should therefore arrive some time in the morning; but as she cannot possibly get in before then, we are all about to retire early.", "따라서 배는 아침 어느 무렵엔가 도착할 것이다. 그러나 그 전에는 도저히 들어올 수 없으므로, 우리는 모두 일찍 잠자리에 들려 한다."], ["We shall get up at one o’clock, so as to be ready.", "우리는 준비가 되어 있도록 한 시에 일어날 것이다."]], [["25 October, Noon.--No news yet of the ship’s arrival. Mrs.", "10월 25일, 정오. — 배가 도착했다는 소식은 아직 없다."], ["Harker’s hypnotic report this morning was the same as usual, so it is possible that we may get news at any moment.", "하커 부인의 오늘 아침 최면 보고도 여느 때와 같았으니, 우리는 언제든 소식을 받을 가능성이 있다."], ["We men are all in a fever of excitement, except Harker, who is calm; his hands are cold as ice, and an hour ago I found him whetting the edge of the great Ghoorka knife which he now always carries with him.", "우리 사내들은 모두 흥분의 열기에 들떠 있는데, 침착한 하커만은 예외다. 그의 손은 얼음처럼 차갑고, 한 시간 전에 보니 그는 이제 늘 지니고 다니는 그 커다란 구르카 칼의 날을 갈고 있었다."], ["It will be a bad lookout for the Count if the edge of that “Kukri” ever touches his throat, driven by that stern, ice-cold hand!", "그 \"쿠크리\"의 날이 저 단호하고 얼음처럼 차가운 손에 이끌려 백작의 목에 닿는 날이면, 백작에게는 불길한 일이 될 것이다! * 쿠크리(Kukri): 네팔 구르카족의 안쪽으로 휜 칼."]], [["Van Helsing and I were a little alarmed about Mrs. Harker to-day.", "판 헬싱과 나는 오늘 하커 부인 때문에 다소 놀랐다."], ["About noon she got into a sort of lethargy which we did not like; although we kept silence to the others, we were neither of us happy about it.", "정오 무렵 그녀는 우리가 달가워하지 않는 일종의 무기력에 빠져들었는데, 다른 이들에게는 입을 다물었으나 우리 둘 다 그것이 마음에 걸렸다."], ["She had been restless all the morning, so that we were at first glad to know that she was sleeping.", "그녀는 아침 내내 불안해했던 터라, 처음에 우리는 그녀가 잠든 것을 알고 다행으로 여겼다."], ["When, however, her husband mentioned casually that she was sleeping so soundly that he could not wake her, we went to her room to see for ourselves.", "그러나 그녀의 남편이 그녀가 너무 깊이 잠들어 깨울 수가 없다고 무심코 말하자, 우리는 직접 보려고 그녀의 방으로 갔다."], ["She was breathing naturally and looked so well and peaceful that we agreed that the sleep was better for her than anything else.", "그녀는 자연스럽게 숨 쉬고 있었고, 너무도 건강하고 평온해 보여서, 우리는 그 잠이 그녀에게 그 무엇보다 좋다는 데 의견을 같이했다."], ["Poor girl, she has so much to forget that it is no wonder that sleep, if it brings oblivion to her, does her good.", "가엾은 여인, 그녀는 잊어야 할 것이 너무도 많으니, 잠이 그녀에게 망각을 가져다준다면 그것이 그녀에게 이로운 것도 당연한 일이다."]], [["Later.--Our opinion was justified, for when after a refreshing sleep of some hours she woke up, she seemed brighter and better than she had been for days.", "이후. — 우리의 판단은 옳았다. 몇 시간의 상쾌한 잠에서 깨어났을 때, 그녀는 여러 날 동안보다 더 밝고 좋아 보였기 때문이다."], ["At sunset she made the usual hypnotic report.", "일몰에 그녀는 여느 때처럼 최면 보고를 했다."], ["Wherever he may be in the Black Sea, the Count is hurrying to his destination. To his doom, I trust!", "백작은 흑해 어디에 있든, 제 목적지를 향해 서두르고 있다. 바라건대, 제 파멸을 향해!"]], [["26 October.--Another day and no tidings of the Czarina Catherine.", "10월 26일. — 또 하루가 갔으나 차리나 카테리나호의 소식은 없다."], ["She ought to be here by now.", "배는 지금쯤 이곳에 와 있어야 한다."], ["That she is still journeying somewhere is apparent, for Mrs. Harker’s hypnotic report at sunrise was still the same.", "그것이 아직 어딘가를 항해하고 있음은 분명한데, 일출 때 하커 부인의 최면 보고가 여전히 같았기 때문이다."], ["It is possible that the vessel may be lying by, at times, for fog; some of the steamers which came in last evening reported patches of fog both to north and south of the port.", "배가 안개 탓에 이따금 정박하고 있을 가능성도 있다. 어젯밤에 들어온 몇몇 증기선들이 항구의 남북 양쪽에 안개 띠가 끼었다고 보고했다."], ["We must continue our watching, as the ship may now be signalled any moment.", "우리는 감시를 계속해야 하는데, 이제 배가 언제든 신호를 보내올 수 있기 때문이다."]], [["27 October, Noon.--Most strange; no news yet of the ship we wait for. Mrs.", "10월 27일, 정오. — 참으로 기이하다. 우리가 기다리는 배의 소식이 아직 없다."], ["Harker reported last night and this morning as usual: “lapping waves and rushing water,”", "하커 부인은 어젯밤과 오늘 아침에 여느 때처럼 보고했다. \"찰싹이는 파도와 스쳐 지나가는 물소리.\""], ["though she added that “the waves were very faint.”", "다만 그녀는 \"파도가 무척 약해졌다\"고 덧붙였다."], ["The telegrams from London have been the same: “no further report.”", "런던에서 온 전보들도 한결같았다. \"더 이상의 보고 없음.\""], ["Van Helsing is terribly anxious, and told me just now that he fears the Count is escaping us.", "판 헬싱은 몹시 불안해하며, 방금 내게 백작이 우리에게서 빠져나가고 있는 것이 두렵다고 말했다."], ["He added significantly:--", "그는 의미심장하게 덧붙였다."]], [["“I did not like that lethargy of Madam Mina’s.", "\"나는 미나 부인의 그 무기력이 마음에 들지 않았소."], ["Souls and memories can do strange things during trance.”", "영혼과 기억은 가수면(假睡眠) 중에 기이한 일들을 할 수 있다오.\""], ["I was about to ask him more, but Harker just then came in, and he held up a warning hand.", "나는 그에게 더 물으려 했으나, 마침 그때 하커가 들어왔고, 그는 경고하듯 손을 들어 올렸다."], ["We must try to-night at sunset to make her speak more fully when in her hypnotic state.", "우리는 오늘 밤 일몰에 그녀가 최면 상태에서 더 자세히 말하도록 애써보아야 한다."]], [["28 October.--Telegram.", "10월 28일. — 전보."], ["Rufus Smith, London, to Lord Godalming, care H. B. M. Vice Consul, Varna.", "런던의 루퍼스 스미스가, 바르나 주재 영국 부영사 편으로 고달밍 경에게."]], [["“Czarina Catherine reported entering Galatz at one o’clock to-day.”", "\"차리나 카테리나호, 오늘 한 시 갈라츠에 입항하는 것으로 보고됨.\""]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["28 October.--When the telegram came announcing the arrival in Galatz I do not think it was such a shock to any of us as might have been expected.", "10월 28일. — 갈라츠 도착을 알리는 전보가 왔을 때, 그것이 우리 가운데 누구에게도 예상했을 법한 만큼의 충격은 아니었던 것 같다."], ["True, we did not know whence, or how, or when, the bolt would come; but I think we all expected that something strange would happen.", "사실 우리는 그 벼락이 어디서, 어떻게, 언제 떨어질지 알지 못했다. 그러나 우리 모두 무언가 기이한 일이 일어나리라 예감하고 있었던 것 같다."], ["The delay of arrival at Varna made us individually satisfied that things would not be just as we had expected; we only waited to learn where the change would occur.", "바르나 도착이 늦어지면서, 우리는 저마다 일이 우리가 예상했던 그대로 되지는 않으리라 짐작하게 되었다. 우리는 그저 그 변화가 어디서 일어날지를 알게 되기만을 기다렸을 뿐이다."], ["None the less, however, was it a surprise.", "그렇다 해도 그것은 역시 놀라운 일이었다."], ["I suppose that nature works on such a hopeful basis that we believe against ourselves that things will be as they ought to be, not as we should know that they will be. Transcendentalism is a beacon to the angels, even if it be a will-o’-the-wisp to man.", "짐작건대 자연이라는 것이 워낙 희망적인 바탕 위에서 작동하는지라, 우리는 우리 자신의 판단을 거슬러서까지, 일이 마땅히 되어야 할 대로 되리라 — 우리가 그렇게 되리라고 알고 있는 대로가 아니라 — 믿게 되는 모양이다. 초월주의란 인간에게는 도깨비불일지언정 천사들에게는 등대인 법이다."], ["It was an odd experience and we all took it differently.", "그것은 묘한 경험이었고, 우리는 저마다 그것을 다르게 받아들였다."], ["Van Helsing raised his hand over his head for a moment, as though in remonstrance with the Almighty; but he said not a word, and in a few seconds stood up with his face sternly set.", "판 헬싱은 마치 전능하신 분께 항의라도 하듯 잠시 손을 머리 위로 들어 올렸다. 그러나 그는 한마디도 하지 않았고, 몇 초 뒤에는 단호하게 굳은 얼굴로 일어섰다."], ["Lord Godalming grew very pale, and sat breathing heavily.", "고달밍 경은 무척 창백해져서 가쁘게 숨을 몰아쉬며 앉아 있었다."], ["I was myself half stunned and looked in wonder at one after another.", "나 자신은 반쯤 망연자실하여 차례로 이 사람 저 사람을 의아하게 바라보았다."], ["Quincey Morris tightened his belt with that quick movement which I knew so well; in our old wandering days it meant “action.” Mrs.", "퀸시 모리스는 내가 너무도 잘 아는 그 재빠른 동작으로 허리띠를 졸라맸다. 우리의 옛 방랑 시절에 그것은 \"행동\"을 뜻했다."], ["Harker grew ghastly white, so that the scar on her forehead seemed to burn, but she folded her hands meekly and looked up in prayer.", "하커 부인은 섬뜩하게 창백해져서, 이마의 흉터가 타오르는 듯했으나, 그녀는 두 손을 온순히 모으고 기도하듯 위를 올려다보았다."], ["Harker smiled--actually smiled--the dark, bitter smile of one who is without hope; but at the same time his action belied his words, for his hands instinctively sought the hilt of the great Kukri knife and rested there.", "하커는 미소를 지었다 — 정말로 미소를 지었다 — 희망 없는 자의 어둡고 쓰라린 미소를. 그러나 동시에 그의 행동은 그의 표정과 어긋났으니, 그의 손이 본능적으로 그 커다란 쿠크리 칼의 손잡이를 더듬어 그 위에 머물렀던 것이다."], ["“When does the next train start for Galatz?”", "\"갈라츠로 가는 다음 기차는 언제 출발하오?\""], ["said Van Helsing to us generally.", "판 헬싱이 우리 모두를 향해 물었다."]], [["“At 6:30 to-morrow morning!”", "\"내일 아침 여섯 시 반이에요!\""], ["We all started, for the answer came from Mrs. Harker.", "우리는 모두 흠칫 놀랐으니, 그 답이 하커 부인에게서 나왔기 때문이다."]], [["“How on earth do you know?” said Art.", "\"대체 어떻게 아십니까?\" 아서가 말했다."]], [["“You forget--or perhaps you do not know, though Jonathan does and so does Dr. Van Helsing--that I am the train fiend.", "\"잊으셨군요 — 아니면 어쩌면 모르셨을지도 몰라요. 조너선은 알고 판 헬싱 박사님도 아시지만 — 저는 기차에 환장한 사람이거든요."], ["At home in Exeter I always used to make up the time-tables, so as to be helpful to my husband.", "엑서터의 집에서 저는 남편에게 도움이 되려고 늘 시간표를 짜두곤 했어요."], ["I found it so useful sometimes, that I always make a study of the time-tables now.", "그게 때로 어찌나 쓸모가 있던지, 이제는 늘 시간표를 연구한답니다."], ["I knew that if anything were to take us to Castle Dracula we should go by Galatz, or at any rate through Bucharest, so I learned the times very carefully.", "만일 무언가가 우리를 드라큘라 성으로 데려가게 된다면 우리는 갈라츠를 거쳐, 아니면 적어도 부쿠레슈티를 지나 가게 되리란 걸 알았기에, 저는 시각을 무척 꼼꼼히 익혀두었어요."], ["Unhappily there are not many to learn, as the only train to-morrow leaves as I say.”", "안타깝게도 익힐 만한 게 많지는 않은데, 내일 가는 기차라고는 제가 말씀드린 것 하나뿐이거든요.\""]], [["“Wonderful woman!”", "\"놀라운 여인이오!\""], ["murmured the Professor.", "교수가 중얼거렸다."]], [["“Can’t we get a special?”", "\"특별 열차를 구할 수는 없겠소?\""], ["asked Lord Godalming.", "고달밍 경이 물었다."], ["Van Helsing shook his head: “I fear not.", "판 헬싱은 고개를 저었다. \"어려울 것 같소."], ["This land is very different from yours or mine; even if we did have a special, it would probably not arrive as soon as our regular train.", "이 나라는 당신네 나라나 내 나라와는 무척 다르오. 설령 특별 열차를 구한다 해도, 아마 우리의 정규 열차만큼 빨리 도착하지는 못할 거요."], ["Moreover, we have something to prepare. We must think.", "게다가 우리에게는 준비해야 할 것이 있소."], ["Now let us organize.", "우리는 궁리해야 하오."], ["You, friend Arthur, go to the train and get the tickets and arrange that all be ready for us to go in the morning.", "자, 이제 일을 나눕시다. 당신, 아서 벗은, 역으로 가서 표를 구하고 아침에 우리가 떠날 수 있도록 모든 채비를 갖추시오."], ["Do you, friend Jonathan, go to the agent of the ship and get from him letters to the agent in Galatz, with authority to make search the ship just as it was here.", "당신, 조너선 벗은, 선박 대리인에게 가서 갈라츠의 대리인 앞으로 보내는 편지를, 이곳에서와 똑같이 배를 수색할 권한과 함께 받아 오시오."], ["Morris Quincey, you see the Vice-Consul, and get his aid with his fellow in Galatz and all he can do to make our way smooth, so that no times be lost when over the Danube.", "모리스 퀸시, 당신은 부영사를 만나, 갈라츠에 있는 그의 동료의 협조와, 우리 길을 순조롭게 해주기 위해 그가 할 수 있는 모든 것을 얻어내어, 도나우 강을 건널 때 한시도 허비되지 않게 하시오."], ["John will stay with Madam Mina and me, and we shall consult.", "존은 미나 부인과 나와 함께 남을 것이고, 우리는 의논을 할 거요."], ["For so if time be long you may be delayed; and it will not matter when the sun set, since I am here with Madam to make report.”", "그래야 혹 시간이 길어져 당신들이 지체되더라도, 내가 부인과 함께 여기 있어 보고를 받을 테니, 해가 언제 지든 상관없을 거요.\""]], [["“And I,” said Mrs.", "\"그리고 저는,\""], ["Harker brightly, and more like her old self than she had been for many a long day, “shall try to be of use in all ways, and shall think and write for you as I used to do.", "하커 부인이 밝게, 그 오랜 나날 동안보다 더 옛 모습 그대로의 그녀답게 말했다. \"온갖 방식으로 도움이 되도록 애쓰겠고, 예전에 그랬듯 여러분을 위해 생각하고 글을 쓰겠어요."], ["Something is shifting from me in some strange way, and I feel freer than I have been of late!”", "무언가가 기이한 방식으로 제게서 빠져나가고 있어요. 요즈음보다 더 자유로워진 느낌이에요!\""], ["The three younger men looked happier at the moment as they seemed to realise the significance of her words; but Van Helsing and I, turning to each other, met each a grave and troubled glance.", "세 젊은이는 그 순간 그녀의 말이 지닌 의미를 깨달은 듯 더 행복해 보였다. 그러나 판 헬싱과 나는 서로를 향해 돌아서며, 저마다 무겁고 근심 어린 눈길과 마주쳤다."], ["We said nothing at the time, however.", "그러나 그때는 아무 말도 하지 않았다."]], [["When the three men had gone out to their tasks Van Helsing asked Mrs. Harker to look up the copy of the diaries and find him the part of Harker’s journal at the Castle.", "세 사람이 저마다의 임무를 위해 나가자, 판 헬싱은 하커 부인에게 일기 사본을 찾아, 성에서 쓴 하커의 일기 부분을 자기에게 찾아달라고 청했다."], ["She went away to get it; when the door was shut upon her he said to me:--", "그녀가 그것을 가지러 나가고, 문이 그녀의 등 뒤로 닫히자 그는 내게 말했다."]], [["“We mean the same!", "\"우리는 같은 생각을 하고 있소!"], ["speak out!”", "솔직히 말해보시오!\""]], [["“There is some change.", "\"무언가 변화가 있습니다."], ["It is a hope that makes me sick, for it may deceive us.”", "그것은 저를 괴롭게 하는 희망인데, 우리를 속일 수도 있기 때문입니다.\""]], [["“Quite so.", "\"바로 그렇소."], ["Do you know why I asked her to get the manuscript?”", "내가 왜 그녀에게 원고를 가져오라 청했는지 아시오?\""]], [["“No!”", "\"모르겠습니다.\""], ["said I, “unless it was to get an opportunity of seeing me alone.”", "내가 말했다. \"당신과 저만 따로 만날 기회를 얻으려는 것이 아니라면 말입니다.\""]], [["“You are in part right, friend John, but only in part.", "\"부분적으로는 옳소, 존 벗. 그러나 부분적으로만 그렇소."], ["I want to tell you something.", "나는 당신에게 무언가 말해주고 싶소."], ["And oh, my friend, I am taking a great--a terrible--risk; but I believe it is right.", "그리고 오, 벗이여, 나는 크나큰 — 무서운 — 위험을 무릅쓰고 있소. 그러나 나는 그것이 옳다고 믿소."], ["In the moment when Madam Mina said those words that arrest both our understanding, an inspiration came to me.", "미나 부인이 우리 둘의 이해를 모두 멈춰 세운 그 말을 한 순간, 내게 한 가지 영감이 떠올랐소."], ["In the trance of three days ago the Count sent her his spirit to read her mind; or more like he took her to see him in his earth-box in the ship with water rushing, just as it go free at rise and set of sun.", "사흘 전의 가수면 중에 백작은 그녀의 마음을 읽으려고 그녀에게 자기 영혼을 보냈소. 아니면 더 그럴 법하게는, 해 뜨고 질 때 그가 자유로워지듯, 물이 스쳐 지나가는 배 안의 흙 상자 속에 있는 자기를 그녀가 보러 가도록 그녀를 데려갔던 거요."], ["He learn then that we are here; for she have more to tell in her open life with eyes to see and ears to hear than he, shut, as he is, in his coffin-box.", "그때 그는 우리가 여기 있음을 알게 되었소. 관 상자 속에 갇힌 그보다, 보는 눈과 듣는 귀를 지니고 열린 삶을 사는 그녀가 알려줄 것이 더 많으니 말이오."], ["Now he make his most effort to escape us.", "이제 그는 우리에게서 벗어나려고 갖은 애를 다 쓰고 있소."], ["At present he want her not.", "지금으로서는 그녀가 필요 없는 거요."]], [["“He is sure with his so great knowledge that she will come at his call; but he cut her off--take her, as he can do, out of his own power, that so she come not to him. Ah!", "\"그는 그토록 깊은 자기 지식으로, 그녀가 자기 부름에 응해 오리라 확신하고 있소. 그러나 그는 그녀를 끊어버렸소 — 자기가 할 수 있는 대로, 그녀를 자기 힘 밖으로 끌어내어 그녀가 그에게 오지 못하게 한 거요. 아!"], ["there I have hope that our man-brains that have been of man so long and that have not lost the grace of God, will come higher than his child-brain that lie in his tomb for centuries, that grow not yet to our stature, and that do only work selfish and therefore small.", "거기에 내 희망이 있소. 오랜 세월 사람의 것이었고 또 하느님의 은총을 잃지 않은 우리 사내의 두뇌가, 여러 세기 동안 제 무덤 속에 누워 있으면서 아직 우리만큼 자라지 못했고 오직 이기적으로만, 따라서 작게만 작동하는 그의 어린아이 두뇌보다 더 높이 이르리란 희망 말이오. 미나 부인이 오는군."], ["Here comes Madam Mina; not a word to her of her trance!", "그녀의 가수면에 관해서는 그녀에게 한마디도 하지 마시오!"], ["She know it not; and it would overwhelm her and make despair just when we want all her hope, all her courage; when most we want all her great brain which is trained like man’s brain, but is of sweet woman and have a special power which the Count give her, and which he may not take away altogether--though he think not so. Hush!", "그녀는 그것을 모르오. 그리고 그것은 그녀를 압도하여, 바로 우리가 그녀의 모든 희망과 모든 용기를 필요로 할 때, 우리가 사내의 두뇌처럼 단련되었으면서도 사랑스러운 여인의 것이며 백작이 그녀에게 준, 그리고 그가 그렇게 여기지는 않아도 그가 온전히 거두어 갈 수는 없는 특별한 힘을 지닌 그 위대한 두뇌를 가장 필요로 할 때, 그녀를 절망에 빠뜨릴 거요. 쉿! 내가 말하게 두시오."], ["let me speak, and you shall learn.", "그러면 당신도 알게 될 거요."], ["Oh, John, my friend, we are in awful straits.", "오, 존, 벗이여, 우리는 무서운 궁지에 몰려 있소."], ["I fear, as I never feared before.", "나는 전에 없이 두렵소."], ["We can only trust the good God. Silence!", "우리는 오직 선하신 하느님을 믿을 수밖에 없소. 조용히!"], ["here she comes!”", "그녀가 오는군!\""]], [["I thought that the Professor was going to break down and have hysterics, just as he had when Lucy died, but with a great effort he controlled himself and was at perfect nervous poise when Mrs. Harker tripped into the room, bright and happy-looking and, in the doing of work, seemingly forgetful of her misery.", "나는 교수가 무너져서, 루시가 죽었을 때 그랬던 것처럼 발작을 일으키려는 줄 알았다. 그러나 그는 안간힘을 써서 자신을 다스렸고, 하커 부인이 밝고 행복해 보이는 얼굴로, 일을 하느라 제 비참함은 잊은 듯이 방으로 사뿐히 들어왔을 때에는 완전히 신경의 평정을 되찾고 있었다."], ["As she came in, she handed a number of sheets of typewriting to Van Helsing.", "그녀는 들어오면서 타자로 친 종이 여러 장을 판 헬싱에게 건넸다."], ["He looked over them gravely, his face brightening up as he read.", "그는 그것을 엄숙하게 훑어보았는데, 읽어 내려가면서 그의 얼굴이 밝아졌다."], ["Then holding the pages between his finger and thumb he said:--", "그러고는 손가락과 엄지 사이에 종이를 쥐고 말했다."]], [["“Friend John, to you with so much of experience already--and you, too, dear Madam Mina, that are young--here is a lesson: do not fear ever to think.", "\"존 벗, 이미 그토록 많은 경험을 지닌 당신에게 — 그리고 당신도, 친애하는 미나 부인, 젊으신 당신에게도 — 여기 한 가지 교훈이 있소. 생각하기를 결코 두려워하지 마시오."], ["A half-thought has been buzzing often in my brain, but I fear to let him loose his wings.", "어떤 어렴풋한 생각이 내 머릿속에서 자주 윙윙거렸으나, 나는 그것이 날개를 펴게 풀어놓기를 두려워했소."], ["Here now, with more knowledge, I go back to where that half-thought come from and I find that he be no half-thought at all; that be a whole thought, though so young that he is not yet strong to use his little wings. Nay, like the “Ugly Duck”", "이제 여기서, 더 많은 지식을 얻고 보니, 나는 그 어렴풋한 생각이 비롯된 곳으로 되돌아가, 그것이 결코 어렴풋한 생각 따위가 아님을 알게 되었소. 그것은 비록 너무 어려 아직 그 작은 날개를 쓸 만큼 강하지는 못하나, 온전한 하나의 생각이었던 거요."], ["of my friend Hans Andersen, he be no duck-thought at all, but a big swan-thought that sail nobly on big wings, when the time come for him to try them.", "아니, 내 벗 한스 안데르센의 '미운 오리 새끼'처럼, 그것은 오리 생각 따위가 전혀 아니라, 때가 와서 날개를 시험할 때면 그 큰 날개로 당당히 떠가는 커다란 백조 생각이오."], ["See I read here what Jonathan have written:--", "보시오, 조너선이 여기 적어놓은 것을 읽어보겠소."]], [["“That other of his race who, in a later age, again and again, brought his forces over The Great River into Turkey Land; who, when he was beaten back, came again, and again, and again, though he had to come alone from the bloody field where his troops were being slaughtered, since he knew that he alone could ultimately triumph.”", "\"훗날의 시대에 거듭거듭 자기 군대를 이끌고 대하(大河)를 건너 터키 땅으로 쳐들어갔던 그 일족의 또 다른 자. 그는 격퇴당했을 때 다시, 또다시, 또다시 쳐들어왔으니, 비록 제 군대가 도륙당하던 피투성이 싸움터에서 홀로 돌아와야 했을지라도, 오직 자기만이 끝내 승리하리란 것을 알았기 때문이다.\""]], [["“What does this tell us? Not much? no!", "\"이것이 우리에게 무엇을 말해주오? 많지 않다고? 아니오!"], ["The Count’s child-thought see nothing; therefore he speak so free.", "백작의 어린아이 생각은 아무것도 보지 못하오. 그러기에 그가 그토록 거리낌 없이 말하는 거요."], ["Your man-thought see nothing; my man-thought see nothing, till just now. No!", "당신의 사내 생각도 아무것도 보지 못하고, 내 사내 생각도 바로 방금 전까지는 아무것도 보지 못했소. 그렇소!"], ["But there comes another word from some one who speak without thought because she, too, know not what it mean--what it might mean.", "그러나 거기에, 자기 또한 그것이 무엇을 뜻하는지 — 무엇을 뜻할 수 있는지 — 알지 못한 채 생각 없이 말하는 누군가에게서 또 다른 말이 나오는 거요."], ["Just as there are elements which rest, yet when in nature’s course they move on their way and they touch--then pouf!", "마치 가만히 머물러 있던 원소들이, 자연의 순리에 따라 제 길을 가다가 서로 닿으면 — 그러면 펑!"], ["and there comes a flash of light, heaven wide, that blind and kill and destroy some; but that show up all earth below for leagues and leagues.", "하늘 가득 번쩍이는 한 줄기 빛이 일어, 누군가를 멀게 하고 죽이고 파괴하지만, 또한 그 아래 온 대지를 수 리(里)에 걸쳐 환히 비추는 것과 같소. 그렇지 않소?"], ["Is it not so?", "자, 설명하겠소."], ["Well, I shall explain. To begin, have you ever study the philosophy of crime?", "우선, 당신은 범죄의 철학을 연구해 본 적이 있소? '예'이기도 하고 '아니오'이기도 하지."], ["‘Yes’ and ‘No.’ You, John, yes; for it is a study of insanity.", "당신은, 존, '예'요. 그것은 광기의 연구이니 말이오. 당신은, 미나 부인, '아니오'요."], ["You, no, Madam Mina; for crime touch you not--not but once.", "범죄가 당신을 건드린 적이 없으니 — 단 한 번을 빼고는 말이오."], ["Still, your mind works true, and argues not a particulari ad universale.", "그래도 당신의 정신은 올바르게 작동하며, 개별에서 보편으로 함부로 추론하지 않소."], ["There is this peculiarity in criminals.", "범죄자들에게는 이런 특성이 있소."], ["It is so constant, in all countries and at all times, that even police, who know not much from philosophy, come to know it empirically, that it is.", "그것이 모든 나라에서 모든 시대에 걸쳐 너무도 한결같기에, 철학을 많이 알지 못하는 경찰조차 그것이 그러하다는 것을 경험적으로 알게 되오."], ["That is to be empiric.", "그것이 곧 경험적이라는 거요."], ["The criminal always work at one crime--that is the true criminal who seems predestinate to crime, and who will of none other.", "범죄자는 늘 한 가지 범죄에 매달리오 — 그것이 곧 범죄에 예정된 듯하여 다른 어떤 것도 마다하는 진짜 범죄자요."], ["This criminal has not full man-brain.", "이런 범죄자는 온전한 사내 두뇌를 지니지 못했소."], ["He is clever and cunning and resourceful; but he be not of man-stature as to brain.", "그는 영리하고 교활하며 임기응변에 능하지만, 두뇌에 관한 한 사내의 키에 이르지 못했소."], ["He be of child-brain in much.", "그는 많은 점에서 어린아이 두뇌를 지녔소."], ["Now this criminal of ours is predestinate to crime also; he, too, have child-brain, and it is of the child to do what he have done.", "그런데 우리의 이 범죄자 역시 범죄에 예정되어 있소. 그 또한 어린아이 두뇌를 지녔고, 그가 한 짓을 하는 것은 어린아이다운 짓이오."], ["The little bird, the little fish, the little animal learn not by principle, but empirically; and when he learn to do, then there is to him the ground to start from to do more.", "작은 새, 작은 물고기, 작은 짐승은 원리로써가 아니라 경험으로써 배우오. 그리고 일단 할 줄을 배우면, 그에게는 거기서부터 더 많은 것을 할 출발점이 생기는 거요."], ["‘Dos pou sto,’ said Archimedes.", "'도스 포우 스토', 아르키메데스가 말했소."], ["‘Give me a fulcrum, and I shall move the world!’ To do once, is the fulcrum whereby child-brain become man-brain; and until he have the purpose to do more, he continue to do the same again every time, just as he have done before!", "'내게 받침점을 다오, 그러면 세상을 움직이겠다!' 한 번 해보는 것이 곧 어린아이 두뇌가 사내 두뇌로 되는 받침점이오. 그리고 더 많은 것을 하려는 목적을 갖기 전까지, 그는 전에 했던 그대로, 매번 똑같은 짓을 거듭하는 거요!"], ["Oh, my dear, I see that your eyes are opened, and that to you the lightning flash show all the leagues,”", "오, 친애하는 부인, 당신의 눈이 열렸고, 당신에게는 그 번개의 섬광이 수 리에 걸친 모든 것을 비춰 보이고 있구려.\""], ["for Mrs. Harker began to clap her hands and her eyes sparkled.", "하커 부인이 두 손을 마주치기 시작했고 그녀의 눈이 반짝였기 때문이다."], ["He went on:--", "그는 말을 이었다."]], [["“Now you shall speak.", "\"이제 당신이 말해보시오."], ["Tell us two dry men of science what you see with those so bright eyes.”", "우리 메마른 두 과학자에게, 그 너무도 빛나는 눈으로 무엇을 보는지 말해보시오.\""], ["He took her hand and held it whilst she spoke.", "그는 그녀가 말하는 동안 그녀의 손을 잡고 있었다."], ["His finger and thumb closed on her pulse, as I thought instinctively and unconsciously, as she spoke:--", "그녀가 말하는 동안 그의 손가락과 엄지가 그녀의 맥박 위에 닿아 있었는데, 내 생각에는 본능적으로 무의식중에 그러는 듯했다."]], [["“The Count is a criminal and of criminal type.", "\"백작은 범죄자이고 범죄자 유형이에요."], ["Nordau and Lombroso would so classify him, and quâ criminal he is of imperfectly formed mind.", "노르다우와 롬브로소라면 그를 그렇게 분류했을 거예요. 그리고 범죄자인 한, 그는 불완전하게 형성된 정신을 지니고 있어요."], ["Thus, in a difficulty he has to seek resource in habit.", "그래서 곤경에 처하면 그는 습관 속에서 방책을 찾아야 해요."], ["His past is a clue, and the one page of it that we know--and that from his own lips--tells that once before, when in what Mr.", "그의 과거가 단서이고, 우리가 아는 — 그것도 그 자신의 입에서 나온 — 그 과거의 한 페이지가 말해줘요."], ["Morris would call a ‘tight place,’ he went back to his own country from the land he had tried to invade, and thence, without losing purpose, prepared himself for a new effort.", "전에 한 번, 모리스 씨라면 '궁지'라 부를 처지에 빠졌을 때, 그는 자기가 침략하려던 땅에서 제 나라로 돌아갔고, 거기서 목적을 잃지 않은 채 새로운 시도를 위해 자신을 채비했어요."], ["He came again better equipped for his work; and won.", "그는 자기 일에 더 잘 무장하고 다시 쳐들어왔고, 그리고 이겼어요."], ["So he came to London to invade a new land.", "그렇게 그는 새로운 땅을 침략하러 런던으로 왔어요."], ["He was beaten, and when all hope of success was lost, and his existence in danger, he fled back over the sea to his home; just as formerly he had fled back over the Danube from Turkey Land.”", "그는 패배했고, 성공의 모든 희망이 사라지고 자기 존재가 위태로워지자, 그는 바다 건너 제 고향으로 달아났어요. 꼭 예전에 터키 땅에서 도나우 강 건너로 달아났던 것처럼요.\""]], [["“Good, good!", "\"좋소, 좋소!"], ["oh, you so clever lady!” said Van Helsing, enthusiastically, as he stooped and kissed her hand.", "오, 당신은 참으로 영리한 부인이오!\" 판 헬싱이 열렬히 외치며 몸을 굽혀 그녀의 손에 입을 맞추었다."], ["A moment later he said to me, as calmly as though we had been having a sick-room consultation:--", "잠시 후 그는 마치 우리가 병실에서 진찰을 하고 있기라도 한 듯 차분하게 내게 말했다."]], [["“Seventy-two only; and in all this excitement.", "\"겨우 일흔둘이오. 그것도 이 모든 흥분 속에서 말이오."], ["I have hope.”", "나는 희망이 있소.\""], ["Turning to her again, he said with keen expectation:--", "그는 다시 그녀를 향해 돌아서더니, 간절한 기대를 담아 말했다."]], [["“But go on. Go on!", "\"하지만 계속하시오. 계속!"], ["there is more to tell if you will.", "당신이 원한다면 더 들려줄 것이 있소."], ["Be not afraid; John and I know.", "두려워 마시오. 존도 나도 알고 있소."], ["I do in any case, and shall tell you if you are right.", "어쨌든 나는 알고 있으니, 당신이 옳으면 그렇다고 말해주겠소."], ["Speak, without fear!”", "두려움 없이 말해보시오!\""]], [["“I will try to; but you will forgive me if I seem egotistical.”", "\"애써보겠어요. 하지만 제가 자기중심적으로 보이더라도 용서해 주세요.\""]], [["“Nay!", "\"아니오!"], ["fear not, you must be egotist, for it is of you that we think.”", "두려워 마시오. 당신은 자기중심적이어야 하오. 우리가 생각하는 것이 바로 당신이니 말이오.\""]], [["“Then, as he is criminal he is selfish; and as his intellect is small and his action is based on selfishness, he confines himself to one purpose.", "\"그러면, 그는 범죄자이니 이기적이에요. 그리고 그의 지성이 작고 그의 행동이 이기심에 바탕을 두고 있으니, 그는 자신을 한 가지 목적에 가두고 있어요."], ["That purpose is remorseless.", "그 목적은 가차 없어요."], ["As he fled back over the Danube, leaving his forces to be cut to pieces, so now he is intent on being safe, careless of all.", "그가 자기 군대를 산산조각 나도록 버려두고 도나우 강 건너로 달아났듯이, 지금 그는 모든 것을 아랑곳하지 않고 오직 안전해지는 데에만 마음을 쏟고 있어요."], ["So his own selfishness frees my soul somewhat from the terrible power which he acquired over me on that dreadful night. I felt it!", "그러니 그 자신의 이기심이, 그 무서운 밤에 그가 제게 행사하게 된 그 끔찍한 힘에서 제 영혼을 어느 정도 풀어주고 있어요. 저는 그걸 느꼈어요!"], ["Oh, I felt it!", "오, 느꼈어요!"], ["Thank God, for His great mercy!", "하느님의 크나큰 자비에 감사드려요!"], ["My soul is freer than it has been since that awful hour; and all that haunts me is a fear lest in some trance or dream he may have used my knowledge for his ends.”", "제 영혼은 그 무서운 시간 이래 어느 때보다 자유로워졌어요. 그리고 저를 따라다니며 괴롭히는 것이라곤, 혹여 어떤 가수면이나 꿈속에서 그가 제 지식을 제 목적에 이용했을지도 모른다는 두려움뿐이에요.\""], ["The Professor stood up:--", "교수가 일어섰다."]], [["“He has so used your mind; and by it he has left us here in Varna, whilst the ship that carried him rushed through enveloping fog up to Galatz, where, doubtless, he had made preparation for escaping from us.", "\"그는 그렇게 당신의 정신을 이용했소. 그리고 그것으로써 그는 우리를 여기 바르나에 붙들어 둔 채, 그를 실은 배가 자욱한 안개를 헤치고 갈라츠로 내달리게 한 거요. 거기서 그는 틀림없이 우리에게서 달아날 준비를 해두었던 거요."], ["But his child-mind only saw so far; and it may be that, as ever is in God’s Providence, the very thing that the evil-doer most reckoned on for his selfish good, turns out to be his chiefest harm.", "그러나 그의 어린아이 정신은 거기까지밖에 보지 못했소. 그리고 어쩌면, 하느님의 섭리에서 늘 그러하듯, 악행을 저지르는 자가 제 이기적인 이득을 위해 가장 믿었던 바로 그것이, 그에게 가장 큰 해가 되고 마는 것인지도 모르오."], ["The hunter is taken in his own snare, as the great Psalmist says.", "위대한 시편 기자가 말하듯, 사냥꾼이 제가 놓은 덫에 걸리는 거요."], ["For now that he think he is free from every trace of us all, and that he has escaped us with so many hours to him, then his selfish child-brain will whisper him to sleep.", "이제 그는 우리 모두의 자취에서 벗어났다고, 그토록 여러 시간을 벌어 우리에게서 달아났다고 여기니, 그의 이기적인 어린아이 두뇌는 그를 잠으로 꾀어 들일 거요."], ["He think, too, that as he cut himself off from knowing your mind, there can be no knowledge of him to you; there is where he fail!", "그는 또한, 자기가 당신의 정신을 아는 일에서 스스로를 끊어버렸으니, 당신이 그를 알 길도 없으리라 여기고 있소. 바로 거기서 그가 실수하는 거요!"], ["That terrible baptism of blood which he give you makes you free to go to him in spirit, as you have as yet done in your times of freedom, when the sun rise and set.", "그가 당신에게 베푼 그 무서운 피의 세례가, 해 뜨고 질 때 당신이 자유로운 시간에 지금껏 해온 것처럼, 영혼으로 그에게 가도록 당신을 자유롭게 해주는 거요."], ["At such times you go by my volition and not by his; and this power to good of you and others, as you have won from your suffering at his hands.", "그런 때에 당신은 그의 뜻이 아니라 내 뜻으로 가는 거요. 그리고 당신과 다른 이들을 위한 이 좋은 힘은, 당신이 그의 손에 겪은 고통에서 얻어낸 것이오."], ["This is now all the more precious that he know it not, and to guard himself have even cut himself off from his knowledge of our where.", "그가 그것을 모르고, 또 제 몸을 지키려고 우리가 어디 있는지를 아는 일에서마저 스스로를 끊어버린 만큼, 이제 그것은 더욱더 귀중하오."], ["We, however, are not selfish, and we believe that God is with us through all this blackness, and these many dark hours.", "그러나 우리는 이기적이지 않으며, 우리는 이 모든 어둠과 이 숱한 캄캄한 시간들을 거치는 동안 하느님께서 우리와 함께하신다고 믿소."], ["We shall follow him; and we shall not flinch; even if we peril ourselves that we become like him.", "우리는 그를 뒤쫓을 것이오. 그리고 우리는 물러서지 않을 것이오. 설령 우리가 그와 같이 될 위험을 무릅쓰는 한이 있더라도 말이오."], ["Friend John, this has been a great hour; and it have done much to advance us on our way.", "존 벗, 이것은 위대한 시간이었소. 그리고 그것은 우리를 우리 길로 크게 나아가게 했소."], ["You must be scribe and write him all down, so that when the others return from their work you can give it to them; then they shall know as we do.”", "당신은 기록자가 되어 이 모든 것을 적어두어야 하오. 그래야 다른 이들이 제 일을 마치고 돌아왔을 때 그들에게 그것을 줄 수 있고, 그러면 그들도 우리처럼 알게 될 거요.\""]], [["And so I have written it whilst we wait their return, and Mrs. Harker has written with her typewriter all since she brought the MS. to us.", "그리하여 나는 그들이 돌아오기를 기다리는 동안 이것을 적었고, 하커 부인은 자기 타자기로, 우리에게 그 원고를 가져온 이후의 모든 것을 적었다."]], [["# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY", "# DR. SEWARD’S DIARY"]], [["29 October.--This is written in the train from Varna to Galatz.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」 10월 29일. — 이 글은 바르나에서 갈라츠로 가는 기차 안에서 쓴다."], ["Last night we all assembled a little before the time of sunset.", "어젯밤 우리는 모두 일몰이 되기 조금 전에 모였다."], ["Each of us had done his work as well as he could; so far as thought, and endeavour, and opportunity go, we are prepared for the whole of our journey, and for our work when we get to Galatz. When the usual time came round Mrs.", "우리 각자는 저마다 할 수 있는 한 최선을 다해 제 일을 해두었다. 생각과 노력과 기회가 닿는 한, 우리는 여정 전체에 대해, 그리고 갈라츠에 닿았을 때 해야 할 일에 대해 만반의 준비를 갖추었다."], ["Harker prepared herself for her hypnotic effort; and after a longer and more serious effort on the part of Van Helsing than has been usually necessary, she sank into the trance.", "늘 그 시각이 돌아오자 하커 부인은 최면을 받을 채비를 했다. 그리고 판 헬싱 쪽에서 평소보다 더 길고 더 진지하게 애를 쓴 끝에야 그녀는 가수면 상태로 빠져들었다."], ["Usually she speaks on a hint; but this time the Professor had to ask her questions, and to ask them pretty resolutely, before we could learn anything; at last her answer came:--", "보통은 그녀가 어떤 실마리만으로도 말을 했으나, 이번에는 무언가를 알아내기까지 교수가 그녀에게 질문을 던져야 했고, 그것도 꽤 단호하게 물어야 했다. 마침내 그녀의 대답이 흘러나왔다."]], [["“I can see nothing; we are still; there are no waves lapping, but only a steady swirl of water softly running against the hawser.", "\"아무것도 보이지 않아요. 우리는 멈춰 있어요. 철썩이는 파도는 없고, 다만 닻줄에 부드럽게 부딪치며 흐르는 물의 한결같은 소용돌이만 있어요."], ["I can hear men’s voices calling, near and far, and the roll and creak of oars in the rowlocks.", "가까이서, 또 멀리서 사람들이 부르는 목소리가 들려요. 노받이에서 노가 구르고 삐걱거리는 소리도요."], ["A gun is fired somewhere; the echo of it seems far away.", "어디선가 대포가 발사돼요. 그 메아리는 아득히 먼 데서 울리는 것 같아요."], ["There is tramping of feet overhead, and ropes and chains are dragged along.", "머리 위로 발 구르는 소리가 들리고, 밧줄과 사슬이 끌려가요."], ["What is this?", "이건 뭐죠?"], ["There is a gleam of light; I can feel the air blowing upon me.”", "한 줄기 빛이 비쳐요. 공기가 제 몸 위로 불어오는 게 느껴져요.\""]], [["Here she stopped. She had risen, as if impulsively, from where she lay on the sofa, and raised both her hands, palms upwards, as if lifting a weight.", "여기서 그녀는 말을 멈추었다. 그녀는 소파에 누워 있던 자리에서 충동적인 듯 몸을 일으켜, 마치 무거운 것을 들어올리는 양 두 손바닥을 위로 향한 채 들어올렸다."], ["Van Helsing and I looked at each other with understanding.", "판 헬싱과 나는 알겠다는 듯 서로를 바라보았다."], ["Quincey raised his eyebrows slightly and looked at her intently, whilst Harker’s hand instinctively closed round the hilt of his Kukri.", "퀸시는 눈썹을 살짝 치켜올리며 그녀를 뚫어지게 응시했고, 한편 하커의 손은 본능적으로 그의 쿠크리 칼자루를 움켜쥐었다."], ["There was a long pause.", "긴 침묵이 흘렀다."], ["We all knew that the time when she could speak was passing; but we felt that it was useless to say anything.", "우리는 그녀가 말할 수 있는 시간이 지나가고 있음을 모두 알았으나, 무어라 말해봐야 소용없으리라는 것도 느꼈다."], ["Suddenly she sat up, and, as she opened her eyes, said sweetly:--", "갑자기 그녀가 똑바로 앉더니, 눈을 뜨면서 다정하게 말했다."]], [["“Would none of you like a cup of tea?", "\"여러분 중에 차 한 잔 드시고 싶은 분 안 계세요?"], ["You must all be so tired!”", "다들 무척 피곤하시겠어요!\""], ["We could only make her happy, and so acquiesced.", "우리로서는 그저 그녀를 기쁘게 해줄 수밖에 없었으므로 그렇게 하기로 했다."], ["She bustled off to get tea; when she had gone Van Helsing said:--", "그녀는 부산히 차를 가지러 나갔고, 그녀가 나가자 판 헬싱이 말했다."]], [["“You see, my friends.", "\"보십시오, 여러분."], ["He is close to land: he has left his earth-chest.", "그가 육지에 가까이 와 있습니다. 그는 흙 상자를 떠났어요."], ["But he has yet to get on shore.", "하지만 아직 뭍에 올라야 합니다."], ["In the night he may lie hidden somewhere; but if he be not carried on shore, or if the ship do not touch it, he cannot achieve the land.", "밤이라면 어딘가에 숨어 있을지도 모릅니다. 그러나 뭍으로 옮겨지지 않거나 배가 육지에 닿지 않으면 그는 육지에 이를 수 없습니다."], ["In such case he can, if it be in the night, change his form and can jump or fly on shore, as he did at Whitby.", "그런 경우 밤이라면 그는 형체를 바꾸어, 휘트비에서 그랬듯 뭍으로 뛰어오르거나 날아오를 수 있습니다."], ["But if the day come before he get on shore, then, unless he be carried he cannot escape.", "하지만 그가 뭍에 오르기 전에 날이 밝으면, 그때는 누군가에게 옮겨지지 않는 한 빠져나갈 수 없습니다."], ["And if he be carried, then the customs men may discover what the box contain.", "그리고 옮겨진다면, 세관원이 그 상자에 무엇이 들었는지 발견할지도 모릅니다."], ["Thus, in fine, if he escape not on shore to-night, or before dawn, there will be the whole day lost to him.", "그러니 결국, 만일 그가 오늘 밤이나 새벽 전에 뭍으로 빠져나가지 못한다면, 그에게는 하루가 통째로 사라지는 셈입니다."], ["We may then arrive in time; for if he escape not at night we shall come on him in daytime, boxed up and at our mercy; for he dare not be his true self, awake and visible, lest he be discovered.”", "그러면 우리는 제때에 닿을 수 있습니다. 그가 밤중에 빠져나가지 못하면 우리는 대낮에, 상자에 갇힌 채 우리 손아귀에 든 그를 덮칠 테니까요. 발각될까 두려워, 깨어 모습을 드러낸 제 본래의 모습으로는 감히 있지 못할 테니 말입니다.\""]], [["There was no more to be said, so we waited in patience until the dawn; at which time we might learn more from Mrs. Harker.", "더 할 말이 없었으므로, 우리는 인내하며 새벽을 기다렸다. 그때라면 하커 부인에게서 더 많은 것을 알아낼 수 있을 터였다."]], [["Early this morning we listened, with breathless anxiety, for her response in her trance.", "오늘 이른 아침, 우리는 가수면 상태에서 나올 그녀의 응답을 숨죽인 채 애타게 기다렸다."], ["The hypnotic stage was even longer in coming than before; and when it came the time remaining until full sunrise was so short that we began to despair.", "최면 단계에 드는 데 전보다도 더 오랜 시간이 걸렸고, 막상 그 상태가 왔을 때에는 완전히 해가 뜨기까지 남은 시간이 너무도 짧아, 우리는 절망하기 시작했다."], ["Van Helsing seemed to throw his whole soul into the effort; at last, in obedience to his will she made reply:--", "판 헬싱은 온 영혼을 그 노력에 쏟아붓는 듯했다. 마침내 그의 의지에 따라 그녀가 대답했다."]], [["“All is dark.", "\"온통 어두워요."], ["I hear lapping water, level with me, and some creaking as of wood on wood.”", "제 높이에서 철썩이는 물소리가 들리고, 나무와 나무가 맞부딪치는 듯한 삐걱대는 소리가 들려요.\""], ["She paused, and the red sun shot up.", "그녀는 말을 멈추었고, 붉은 해가 솟아올랐다."], ["We must wait till to-night.", "우리는 밤이 될 때까지 기다려야만 했다."]], [["And so it is that we are travelling towards Galatz in an agony of expectation.", "그리하여 우리는 기대의 고통에 시달리며 갈라츠를 향해 달려가고 있다."], ["We are due to arrive between two and three in the morning; but already, at Bucharest, we are three hours late, so we cannot possibly get in till well after sun-up.", "새벽 두세 시 사이에 도착할 예정이었으나, 벌써 부쿠레슈티에서부터 세 시간이나 늦어졌으니, 해가 뜬 뒤 한참이 지나서야 들어가게 될 게 분명하다."], ["Thus we shall have two more hypnotic messages from Mrs. Harker; either or both may possibly throw more light on what is happening.", "그러므로 우리는 하커 부인에게서 두 차례 더 최면 전언을 들을 수 있을 것이며, 그 둘 중 하나든 둘 다든 무슨 일이 벌어지고 있는지 더 밝혀줄지도 모른다."]], [["Later.--Sunset has come and gone.", "이후. — 일몰이 왔다가 지나갔다."], ["Fortunately it came at a time when there was no distraction; for had it occurred whilst we were at a station, we might not have secured the necessary calm and isolation. Mrs.", "다행히도 그것은 방해될 것이 없는 때에 찾아왔다. 만일 우리가 역에 있을 때 일어났더라면 필요한 고요와 격리를 확보하지 못했을지도 모른다."], ["Harker yielded to the hypnotic influence even less readily than this morning.", "하커 부인은 오늘 아침보다도 더 더디게 최면의 영향에 응했다."], ["I am in fear that her power of reading the Count’s sensations may die away, just when we want it most.", "나는 백작의 감각을 읽어내는 그녀의 능력이, 우리가 그것을 가장 필요로 하는 바로 이때에 사그라들어버릴까 두렵다."], ["It seems to me that her imagination is beginning to work.", "내 생각에는 그녀의 상상력이 작동하기 시작한 듯하다."], ["Whilst she has been in the trance hitherto she has confined herself to the simplest of facts. If this goes on it may ultimately mislead us.", "지금까지 가수면 상태에 들었을 때 그녀는 더없이 단순한 사실들에만 머물러 있었다. 이런 일이 계속된다면 끝내 우리를 그릇된 길로 이끌지도 모른다."], ["If I thought that the Count’s power over her would die away equally with her power of knowledge it would be a happy thought; but I am afraid that it may not be so.", "만일 그녀의 앎의 능력이 사그라드는 것과 똑같이 그녀에 대한 백작의 지배력도 사그라드는 것이라면 반가운 생각이겠으나, 그렇지 않을까 봐 나는 두렵다."], ["When she did speak, her words were enigmatical:--", "그녀가 입을 열었을 때, 그 말은 수수께끼 같았다."]], [["“Something is going out; I can feel it pass me like a cold wind.", "\"무언가가 빠져나가고 있어요. 차가운 바람처럼 제 곁을 스쳐 지나가는 게 느껴져요."], ["I can hear, far off, confused sounds--as of men talking in strange tongues, fierce-falling water, and the howling of wolves.”", "멀리서 어수선한 소리가 들려요. 낯선 언어로 말하는 사람들 같은, 사납게 떨어지는 물 같은, 그리고 늑대들이 울부짖는 소리 같은.\""], ["She stopped and a shudder ran through her, increasing in intensity for a few seconds, till, at the end, she shook as though in a palsy.", "그녀는 말을 멈추었고 몸서리가 그녀를 훑고 지나갔는데, 그것은 몇 초 동안 점점 격렬해지더니, 마침내 그녀는 마치 중풍에 걸린 듯 떨었다."], ["She said no more, even in answer to the Professor’s imperative questioning.", "그녀는 교수의 단호한 거듭된 물음에도 더는 아무 말이 없었다."], ["When she woke from the trance, she was cold, and exhausted, and languid; but her mind was all alert.", "가수면에서 깨어났을 때 그녀는 차갑고, 지쳤으며, 나른했다. 그러나 정신만은 또렷했다."], ["She could not remember anything, but asked what she had said; when she was told, she pondered over it deeply for a long time and in silence.", "그녀는 아무것도 기억하지 못했으나, 자기가 무슨 말을 했느냐고 물었다. 들려주자 그녀는 오래도록 말없이 그것을 깊이 곱씹었다."]], [["30 October, 7 a. m.--We are near Galatz now, and I may not have time to write later.", "10월 30일, 오전 7시. — 우리는 이제 갈라츠 가까이에 와 있으니, 나중에는 글 쓸 겨를이 없을지도 모른다."], ["Sunrise this morning was anxiously looked for by us all.", "오늘 아침 일출을 우리 모두 애타게 기다렸다."], ["Knowing of the increasing difficulty of procuring the hypnotic trance, Van Helsing began his passes earlier than usual.", "최면 가수면을 얻기가 점점 어려워지고 있음을 아는 판 헬싱은 평소보다 일찍 손동작을 시작했다."], ["They produced no effect, however, until the regular time, when she yielded with a still greater difficulty, only a minute before the sun rose.", "그러나 그것은 정해진 시각에 이르기까지 아무 효력이 없다가, 해가 뜨기 겨우 일 분 전에야 한층 더 큰 어려움 끝에 그녀가 응했다."], ["The Professor lost no time in his questioning; her answer came with equal quickness:--", "교수는 지체 없이 물었고, 그녀의 대답도 그만큼 빠르게 흘러나왔다."]], [["“All is dark.", "\"온통 어두워요."], ["I hear water swirling by, level with my ears, and the creaking of wood on wood.", "제 귀와 같은 높이에서 물이 소용돌이치며 지나가는 소리가 들리고, 나무와 나무가 삐걱대는 소리가 들려요."], ["Cattle low far off.", "소들이 멀리서 울어요."], ["There is another sound, a queer one like----”", "또 다른 소리가 있어요, 묘한 소린데 마치……\""], ["She stopped and grew white, and whiter still.", "그녀는 멈추었고 얼굴이 하얘지더니, 더욱더 하얘졌다."]], [["“Go on; go on!", "\"계속하시오. 계속!"], ["Speak, I command you!”", "말하시오, 명하노니!\""], ["said Van Helsing in an agonised voice.", "판 헬싱이 고통에 찬 목소리로 말했다."], ["At the same time there was despair in his eyes, for the risen sun was reddening even Mrs. Harker’s pale face.", "동시에 그의 눈에는 절망이 어렸으니, 솟아오른 해가 하커 부인의 창백한 얼굴마저 붉게 물들이고 있었기 때문이다."], ["She opened her eyes, and we all started as she said, sweetly and seemingly with the utmost unconcern:--", "그녀는 눈을 떴고, 우리 모두는 그녀가 다정하게, 그리고 보기에 더없이 무심하게 말하는 것에 흠칫 놀랐다."]], [["“Oh, Professor, why ask me to do what you know I can’t?", "\"어머, 교수님, 제가 못한다는 걸 아시면서 왜 시키세요?"], ["I don’t remember anything.”", "저는 아무것도 기억나지 않아요.\""], ["Then, seeing the look of amazement on our faces, she said, turning from one to the other with a troubled look:--", "그러더니 우리 얼굴에 어린 경악의 빛을 보고는, 근심스러운 표정으로 이 사람 저 사람을 둘러보며 말했다."]], [["“What have I said?", "\"제가 무슨 말을 했죠?"], ["What have I done?", "제가 뭘 했나요?"], ["I know nothing, only that I was lying here, half asleep, and heard you say ‘go on!", "저는 아무것도 몰라요. 다만 여기 누워 반쯤 잠들어 있다가, '계속하시오! 말하시오, 명하노니!'"], ["speak, I command you!’ It seemed so funny to hear you order me about, as if I were a bad child!”", "하시는 말씀을 들었을 뿐이에요. 마치 제가 못된 아이라도 되는 양 명령하시는 걸 들으니 어찌나 우습던지요!\""]], [["“Oh, Madam Mina,”", "\"오, 미나 부인,\" 그가 슬프게 말했다."], ["he said, sadly, “it is proof, if proof be needed, of how I love and honour you, when a word for your good, spoken more earnest than ever, can seem so strange because it is to order her whom I am proud to obey!”", "\"이것이야말로, 만일 증거가 필요하다면, 내가 부인을 얼마나 사랑하고 존경하는지를 보여주는 증거지요. 부인을 위한 한마디, 그 어느 때보다 간절히 한 말이, 이토록 낯설게 들릴 수 있다니 말입니다. 내가 기꺼이 따르기를 자랑으로 여기는 그분께 명령을 내리는 것이었으니까요!\""]], [["The whistles are sounding; we are nearing Galatz.", "기적 소리가 울린다. 우리는 갈라츠에 가까워지고 있다."], ["We are on fire with anxiety and eagerness.", "우리는 불안과 열망으로 활활 타오르고 있다."]], [["Mina Harker’s Journal.", "「미나 하커의 일기」"]], [["30 October.--Mr.", "10월 30일."], ["Morris took me to the hotel where our rooms had been ordered by telegraph, he being the one who could best be spared, since he does not speak any foreign language.", "— 모리스 씨가 나를, 전보로 방을 예약해둔 호텔로 데려다주었다. 그가 외국어를 하나도 못하는 까닭에 가장 빼기 쉬운 사람이었기 때문이다."], ["The forces were distributed much as they had been at Varna, except that Lord Godalming went to the Vice-Consul, as his rank might serve as an immediate guarantee of some sort to the official, we being in extreme hurry.", "우리 일행의 배치는 바르나에서와 거의 같았는데, 다만 고달밍 경이 부영사에게 간 점이 달랐다. 우리가 몹시 급한 처지였으므로 그의 작위가 그 관리에게 어떤 즉각적인 보증쯤이 될 수 있을 터였기 때문이다."], ["Jonathan and the two doctors went to the shipping agent to learn particulars of the arrival of the Czarina Catherine.", "조너선과 두 의사는 차리나 카테리나 호의 도착에 관한 자세한 사항을 알아보러 선박 대리점으로 갔다."]], [["Later.--Lord Godalming has returned.", "이후. — 고달밍 경이 돌아왔다."], ["The Consul is away, and the Vice-Consul sick; so the routine work has been attended to by a clerk.", "영사는 출타 중이고 부영사는 병중이라, 일상 업무는 한 서기가 처리하고 있었다."], ["He was very obliging, and offered to do anything in his power.", "그는 매우 친절했고, 자기 권한 내에서 무엇이든 해주겠다고 했다."]], [["Jonathan Harker’s Journal.", "「조너선 하커의 일기」"]], [["30 October.--At nine o’clock Dr.", "10월 30일."], ["Van Helsing, Dr. Seward, and I called on Messrs. Mackenzie & Steinkoff, the agents of the London firm of Hapgood.", "— 아홉 시에 판 헬싱 박사와 수어드 박사, 그리고 나는 런던 햅굿 상회의 대리점인 매켄지 앤 슈타인코프 상회를 찾아갔다."], ["They had received a wire from London, in answer to Lord Godalming’s telegraphed request, asking us to show them any civility in their power.", "그들은 고달밍 경이 전보로 보낸 요청에 답하여, 그들 권한 내에서 우리에게 온갖 호의를 베풀어달라는 전보를 런던에서 받아둔 터였다."], ["They were more than kind and courteous, and took us at once on board the Czarina Catherine, which lay at anchor out in the river harbour.", "그들은 더없이 친절하고 정중했고, 우리를 즉시 강 항구에 닻을 내리고 정박해 있는 차리나 카테리나 호에 태워 데려갔다."], ["There we saw the Captain, Donelson by name, who told us of his voyage.", "거기서 우리는 도널슨이라는 선장을 만났는데, 그는 자기 항해에 대해 이야기해주었다."], ["He said that in all his life he had never had so favourable a run.", "그는 평생 그토록 순조로운 항해는 처음이었다고 했다."]], [["“Man!”", "\"이보시오!\""], ["he said, “but it made us afeard, for we expeckit that we should have to pay for it wi’ some rare piece o’ ill luck, so as to keep up the average.", "그가 말했다. \"그게 우리를 겁나게 합디다. 평균을 맞추느라 무슨 진귀한 불운으로 그 대가를 치러야 할 줄로 알았으니까요."], ["It’s no canny to run frae London to the Black Sea wi’ a wind ahint ye, as though the Deil himself were blawin’ on yer sail for his ain purpose.", "마치 악마가 제 속셈으로 돛에다 바람을 불어주기라도 하는 양, 등 뒤에서 바람을 받으며 런던에서 흑해까지 내달리는 건 영 께름칙한 일입니다."], ["An’ a’ the time we could no speer a thing.", "그러는 내내 우리는 도무지 아무것도 알아낼 수가 없었어요."], ["Gin we were nigh a ship, or a port, or a headland, a fog fell on us and travelled wi’ us, till when after it had lifted and we looked out, the deil a thing could we see.", "배든 항구든 곶이든 가까이 다가가면 안개가 우리한테 내려앉아 우리와 함께 따라오다가, 그게 걷히고 나서 내다보면 아무것도, 빌어먹을 것 하나도 보이질 않았소."], ["We ran by Gibraltar wi’oot bein’ able to signal; an’ till we came to the Dardanelles and had to wait to get our permit to pass, we never were within hail o’ aught.", "우리는 신호도 못 보낸 채 지브롤터를 지나쳤고, 다르다넬스에 닿아 통과 허가를 받으려 기다려야 했을 때까지, 무엇 하나 부를 만한 거리 안에 있어본 적이 없었습니다."], ["At first I inclined to slack off sail and beat about till the fog was lifted; but whiles, I thocht that if the Deil was minded to get us into the Black Sea quick, he was like to do it whether we would or no.", "처음엔 돛을 늦추고 안개가 걷힐 때까지 이리저리 떠돌까 했지만, 이따금 이런 생각이 들더군요. 악마가 우리를 빨리 흑해로 들여보낼 작정이라면, 우리가 원하든 말든 기어이 그리할 거라고요."], ["If we had a quick voyage it would be no to our miscredit wi’ the owners, or no hurt to our traffic; an’ the Old Mon who had served his ain purpose wad be decently grateful to us for no hinderin’ him.”", "빠른 항해라면 선주들한테 우리 면목이 깎일 일도, 우리 영업에 해될 일도 아니지요. 게다가 제 속셈을 다 이룬 그 늙은 양반도, 자기를 막지 않은 우리한테 점잖게 고마워할 테고요.\""], ["This mixture of simplicity and cunning, of superstition and commercial reasoning, aroused Van Helsing, who said:--", "이런 순박함과 교활함, 미신과 상업적 타산의 뒤섞임에 판 헬싱이 흥미를 느껴 말했다."]], [["“Mine friend, that Devil is more clever than he is thought by some; and he know when he meet his match!”", "\"이보시오, 그 악마는 더러 사람들이 여기는 것보다 훨씬 영리하답니다. 게다가 제 적수를 만났을 때를 알아보지요!\""], ["The skipper was not displeased with the compliment, and went on:--", "선장은 그 칭찬에 싫지 않은 기색으로 말을 이었다."]], [["“When we got past the Bosphorus the men began to grumble; some o’ them, the Roumanians, came and asked me to heave overboard a big box which had been put on board by a queer lookin’ old man just before we had started frae London.", "\"보스포루스를 지나고 나니 선원들이 투덜거리기 시작합디다. 그중 몇몇, 루마니아인들이 와서, 우리가 런던을 막 떠나기 직전에 묘하게 생긴 늙은이가 배에 실어놓은 큰 상자를 바다에 던져버리자고 청하더군요."], ["I had seen them speer at the fellow, and put out their twa fingers when they saw him, to guard against the evil eye. Man!", "나는 그들이 그 사내한테 눈총을 주고, 흉안을 막으려 두 손가락을 내미는 걸 본 적이 있소. 이보시오!"], ["but the supersteetion of foreigners is pairfectly rideeculous!", "외국인들의 미신이란 그야말로 우스꽝스럽기 짝이 없어요!"], ["I sent them aboot their business pretty quick; but as just after a fog closed in on us I felt a wee bit as they did anent something, though I wouldn’t say it was agin the big box.", "나는 그들을 얼른 제 일이나 하라고 쫓아버렸소만, 바로 뒤에 안개가 우리를 에워싸자 그들이 무언가에 대해 느낀 것과 비슷한 기분이 나도 좀 들더군요. 그게 그 큰 상자 때문이라곤 말하지 않겠소만."], ["Well, on we went, and as the fog didn’t let up for five days I joost let the wind carry us; for if the Deil wanted to get somewheres--well, he would fetch it up a’reet.", "자, 우리는 계속 나아갔고, 닷새 동안 안개가 걷히질 않으니 그냥 바람이 우리를 데려가게 내버려뒀소. 악마가 어딘가로 가고 싶어 한다면야, 뭐, 제대로 데려다놓을 테니까요."], ["An’ if he didn’t, well, we’d keep a sharp lookout anyhow.", "안 그러면, 뭐, 어쨌든 우리는 빈틈없이 망을 볼 작정이었고요."], ["Sure eneuch, we had a fair way and deep water all the time; and two days ago, when the mornin’ sun came through the fog, we found ourselves just in the river opposite Galatz.", "아니나 다를까, 내내 순탄한 뱃길에 깊은 물이었소. 그리고 이틀 전, 아침 해가 안개를 뚫고 비쳐들었을 때, 우리는 마침 갈라츠 맞은편 강에 와 있더이다."], ["The Roumanians were wild, and wanted me right or wrong to take out the box and fling it in the river.", "루마니아인들은 길길이 날뛰며, 옳든 그르든 그 상자를 꺼내 강에 던져버리라고 나한테 우겼소."], ["I had to argy wi’ them aboot it wi’ a handspike; an’ when the last o’ them rose off the deck wi’ his head in his hand, I had convinced them that, evil eye or no evil eye, the property and the trust of my owners were better in my hands than in the river Danube.", "나는 그들과 그 일을 두고 쇠지렛대로 입씨름을 해야 했지요. 마지막 한 놈이 머리를 감싸쥐고 갑판에서 일어났을 때엔, 흉안이고 뭐고 간에 선주들의 재산과 위탁물은 다뉴브 강물 속보다 내 손에 있는 편이 낫다는 걸 그들에게 납득시킨 셈이었소."], ["They had, mind ye, taken the box on the deck ready to fling in, and as it was marked Galatz via Varna, I thocht I’d let it lie till we discharged in the port an’ get rid o’t althegither.", "보시오, 그들은 이미 그 상자를 던지려고 갑판에 꺼내놓은 터였는데, 거기에 '바르나 경유 갈라츠'라고 적혀 있길래, 항구에서 짐을 부릴 때까지 그냥 놔뒀다가 한꺼번에 치워버릴 작정이었소."], ["We didn’t do much clearin’ that day, an’ had to remain the nicht at anchor; but in the mornin’, braw an’ airly, an hour before sun-up, a man came aboard wi’ an order, written to him from England, to receive a box marked for one Count Dracula.", "그날은 짐을 별로 못 부려서, 밤새 닻을 내리고 머물러야 했지요. 그런데 이튿날 아침, 동트기 한 시간 전 산뜻하니 이른 시각에, 한 사내가 영국에서 자기 앞으로 적혀 온 지시서를 들고 배에 올라, 드라큘라 백작이라는 자에게 보내는 상자를 받겠다고 합디다."], ["Sure eneuch the matter was one ready to his hand.", "아니나 다를까, 그 일은 곧바로 처리할 만한 것이었소."], ["He had his papers a’ reet, an’ glad I was to be rid o’ the dam’ thing, for I was beginnin’ masel’ to feel uneasy at it.", "그자의 서류는 다 멀쩡했고, 나는 그 빌어먹을 물건을 떼어버리게 되어 기뻤소. 나 자신도 그것 때문에 불안해지기 시작하던 참이었으니 말이오."], ["If the Deil did have any luggage aboord the ship, I’m thinkin’ it was nane ither than that same!”", "만일 악마가 정말로 이 배에 무슨 짐을 싣고 있었다면, 내 생각엔 다름 아닌 바로 그 물건이었지 싶소!\""]], [["“What was the name of the man who took it?” asked Dr.", "\"그것을 가져간 사내의 이름이 무엇이었소?\""], ["Van Helsing with restrained eagerness.", "판 헬싱 박사가 억눌린 열의로 물었다."]], [["“I’ll be tellin’ ye quick!”", "\"얼른 일러드리리다!\""], ["he answered, and, stepping down to his cabin, produced a receipt signed “Immanuel Hildesheim.”", "그가 대답하더니, 선실로 내려가 \"이마누엘 힐데스하임\"이라 서명된 영수증을 가지고 나왔다."], ["Burgen-strasse 16 was the address.", "주소는 부르겐 가 16번지였다."], ["We found out that this was all the Captain knew; so with thanks we came away.", "우리는 이것이 선장이 아는 전부임을 알게 되었으므로, 고맙다는 인사를 하고 물러났다."]], [["We found Hildesheim in his office, a Hebrew of rather the Adelphi Theatre type, with a nose like a sheep, and a fez.", "우리는 힐데스하임을 그의 사무실에서 찾아냈는데, 그는 아델파이 극장에나 나올 법한 부류의 히브리인으로, 양 같은 코에 페즈를 쓰고 있었다."], ["His arguments were pointed with specie--we doing the punctuation--and with a little bargaining he told us what he knew. This turned out to be simple but important.", "그의 흥정은 현금으로 뾰족이 강조되었고 — 거기에 마침표를 찍는 건 우리 몫이었다 — 약간의 흥정 끝에 그는 자기가 아는 바를 우리에게 일러주었다."], ["He had received a letter from Mr.", "그것은 단순하나 중요한 내용이었다."], ["de Ville of London, telling him to receive, if possible before sunrise so as to avoid customs, a box which would arrive at Galatz in the Czarina Catherine.", "그는 런던의 드빌 씨로부터 편지 한 통을 받았는데, 차리나 카테리나 호로 갈라츠에 도착할 상자 하나를, 가능하면 세관을 피하기 위해 일출 전에 받으라는 것이었다."], ["This he was to give in charge to a certain Petrof Skinsky, who dealt with the Slovaks who traded down the river to the port.", "그는 이것을 강을 따라 항구까지 거래하는 슬로바키아인들을 상대하는 페트로프 스킨스키라는 자에게 맡기기로 되어 있었다."], ["He had been paid for his work by an English bank note, which had been duly cashed for gold at the Danube International Bank.", "그는 그 일의 대가로 영국 은행권 한 장을 받았으며, 그것은 다뉴브 국제은행에서 정식으로 금화로 환전되었다."], ["When Skinsky had come to him, he had taken him to the ship and handed over the box, so as to save porterage.", "스킨스키가 그를 찾아왔을 때, 그는 짐삯을 아끼려고 그를 배로 데려가 상자를 넘겨주었다."], ["That was all he knew.", "그것이 그가 아는 전부였다."]], [["We then sought for Skinsky, but were unable to find him.", "그 다음 우리는 스킨스키를 찾아보았으나 찾을 수 없었다."], ["One of his neighbours, who did not seem to bear him any affection, said that he had gone away two days before, no one knew whither.", "그에게 별 애정이 없어 보이는 이웃 하나는, 그가 이틀 전에 어디로인지 아무도 모르게 떠나버렸다고 했다."], ["This was corroborated by his landlord, who had received by messenger the key of the house together with the rent due, in English money.", "이는 그의 집주인에 의해서도 확인되었는데, 그는 심부름꾼을 통해 집 열쇠와 밀린 집세를 영국 돈으로 받았다고 했다."], ["This had been between ten and eleven o’clock last night.", "그것은 어젯밤 열 시에서 열한 시 사이의 일이었다."], ["We were at a standstill again.", "우리는 다시 막다른 데에 이르렀다."]], [["Whilst we were talking one came running and breathlessly gasped out that the body of Skinsky had been found inside the wall of the churchyard of St. Peter, and that the throat had been torn open as if by some wild animal.", "우리가 이야기를 나누고 있을 때 한 사람이 달려와, 성 베드로 교회 묘지 담장 안에서 스킨스키의 시신이 발견되었으며, 마치 어떤 야수에게 당한 것처럼 목이 찢겨 벌어져 있었다고 헐떡이며 외쳤다."], ["Those we had been speaking with ran off to see the horror, the women crying out “This is the work of a Slovak!”", "우리가 이야기를 나누던 사람들이 그 끔찍한 광경을 보러 달려나갔고, 여인들은 \"이건 슬로바키아인의 짓이야!\""], ["We hurried away lest we should have been in some way drawn into the affair, and so detained.", "하고 부르짖었다. 우리는 어떤 식으로든 그 사건에 휘말려 발이 묶이지 않으려고 서둘러 자리를 떴다."]], [["As we came home we could arrive at no definite conclusion.", "집으로 돌아오는 길에 우리는 어떤 확실한 결론에도 이르지 못했다."], ["We were all convinced that the box was on its way, by water, to somewhere; but where that might be we would have to discover.", "우리는 모두 그 상자가 물길로 어딘가를 향해 가고 있음을 확신했으나, 그곳이 어디일지는 우리가 알아내야 할 터였다."], ["With heavy hearts we came home to the hotel to Mina.", "무거운 마음으로 우리는 호텔의 미나에게로 돌아왔다."]], [["When we met together, the first thing was to consult as to taking Mina again into our confidence.", "우리가 함께 모였을 때 가장 먼저 한 일은, 미나를 다시 우리의 일에 끌어들이는 문제를 의논하는 것이었다."], ["Things are getting desperate, and it is at least a chance, though a hazardous one.", "사정이 절박해지고 있고, 그것은 위험천만하나마 적어도 하나의 가능성이었다."], ["As a preliminary step, I was released from my promise to her.", "그 첫 단계로, 나는 그녀에게 한 약속에서 풀려났다."]], [["Mina Harker’s Journal.", "「미나 하커의 일기」"]], [["30 October, evening.--They were so tired and worn out and dispirited that there was nothing to be done till they had some rest; so I asked them all to lie down for half an hour whilst I should enter everything up to the moment.", "10월 30일, 저녁. — 그들은 너무도 지치고 기진하고 풀이 죽어 있어, 좀 쉬게 하기 전에는 할 수 있는 일이 없었다. 그래서 나는 모두에게 삼십 분쯤 누워 쉬라 하고, 그동안 내가 지금까지의 일을 모두 기록해두기로 했다."], ["I feel so grateful to the man who invented the “Traveller’s” typewriter, and to Mr.", "나는 '여행자용' 타자기를 발명한 사람에게, 그리고 나를 위해 이것을 구해다준 모리스 씨에게 무척 고마운 마음이 든다."], ["Morris for getting this one for me. I should have felt quite astray doing the work if I had to write with a pen....", "펜으로 이 일을 써야 했더라면 나는 영 갈피를 못 잡았을 것이다……."]], [["It is all done; poor dear, dear Jonathan, what he must have suffered, what must he be suffering now.", "다 끝냈다. 가엾고 사랑하는, 사랑하는 조너선, 그가 얼마나 고통을 겪었을지, 지금은 또 얼마나 고통받고 있을지."], ["He lies on the sofa hardly seeming to breathe, and his whole body appears in collapse.", "그는 소파에 누워 거의 숨도 쉬지 않는 듯하고, 온몸이 무너져내린 것만 같다."], ["His brows are knit; his face is drawn with pain.", "그의 미간은 찌푸려져 있고, 얼굴은 고통으로 일그러져 있다."], ["Poor fellow, maybe he is thinking, and I can see his face all wrinkled up with the concentration of his thoughts. Oh!", "가엾은 사람, 어쩌면 생각에 잠겨 있는지도 모른다. 생각에 골몰하느라 온통 주름 잡힌 그의 얼굴이 보인다. 오!"], ["if I could only help at all....", "내가 조금이나마 도울 수 있다면……."], ["I shall do what I can.", "나는 할 수 있는 일을 하리라."]], [["I have asked Dr. Van Helsing, and he has got me all the papers that I have not yet seen....", "나는 판 헬싱 박사에게 청하여, 아직 보지 못한 서류를 모두 받아두었다……."], ["Whilst they are resting, I shall go over all carefully, and perhaps I may arrive at some conclusion.", "그들이 쉬는 동안 나는 모든 것을 찬찬히 훑어볼 것이며, 어쩌면 어떤 결론에 이를 수 있을지도 모른다."], ["I shall try to follow the Professor’s example, and think without prejudice on the facts before me....", "나는 교수의 본을 따라, 내 앞에 놓인 사실들을 선입견 없이 생각해보려 한다……."]], [["I do believe that under God’s providence I have made a discovery.", "나는 정말이지, 하느님의 섭리 아래 내가 하나의 발견을 해냈다고 믿는다."], ["I shall get the maps and look over them....", "지도를 가져다 살펴보아야겠다……."]], [["I am more than ever sure that I am right.", "나는 그 어느 때보다도 내가 옳다고 확신한다."], ["My new conclusion is ready, so I shall get our party together and read it.", "새로운 결론이 갖추어졌으니, 일행을 한데 모아 그것을 읽어주어야겠다."], ["They can judge it; it is well to be accurate, and every minute is precious.", "그들이 판단할 수 있을 것이다. 정확을 기하는 것이 좋고, 일 분 일 분이 소중하다."]], [["Mina Harker’s Memorandum.", "「미나 하커의 비망록」"]], [["(Entered in her Journal.)", "(그녀의 일기에 기재됨.)"]], [["Ground of inquiry.--Count Dracula’s problem is to get back to his own place.", "탐구의 토대. — 드라큘라 백작의 과제는 제 본거지로 돌아가는 것이다."]], [["(a) He must be brought back by some one.", "(가) 그는 누군가에 의해 옮겨져야 한다."], ["This is evident; for had he power to move himself as he wished he could go either as man, or wolf, or bat, or in some other way.", "이것은 명백하다. 만일 그가 뜻대로 스스로 움직일 힘이 있다면 그는 사람으로든, 늑대로든, 박쥐로든, 혹은 다른 어떤 방식으로든 갈 수 있을 것이기 때문이다."], ["He evidently fears discovery or interference, in the state of helplessness in which he must be--confined as he is between dawn and sunset in his wooden box.", "그는 분명, 새벽과 일몰 사이에 나무 상자 속에 갇혀 있는 그 무력한 상태에서, 발각되거나 방해받는 것을 두려워한다."]], [["(b) How is he to be taken?--Here a process of exclusions may help us.", "(나) 그는 어떻게 운반되는가? — 여기서는 하나하나 배제하는 과정이 우리에게 도움이 될 수 있다."], ["By road, by rail, by water?", "육로로, 철로로, 아니면 물길로?"]], [["1. By Road.--There are endless difficulties, especially in leaving the city.", "1. 육로로. — 끝없는 어려움이 있으니, 특히 도시를 벗어나는 데에 그러하다."]], [["(x) There are people; and people are curious, and investigate.", "(ㄱ) 사람들이 있다. 그리고 사람들은 호기심이 많아 캐묻는다."], ["A hint, a surmise, a doubt as to what might be in the box, would destroy him.", "그 상자 안에 무엇이 들었을지에 대한 한마디 귀띔, 한 가닥 추측, 한 점 의심이 그를 파멸시킬 것이다."]], [["(y) There are, or there may be, customs and octroi officers to pass.", "(ㄴ) 통과해야 할 세관원과 입시세(入市稅) 징수원이 있거나, 있을 수 있다."]], [["(z) His pursuers might follow.", "(ㄷ) 추격자들이 따라올지도 모른다."], ["This is his highest fear; and in order to prevent his being betrayed he has repelled, so far as he can, even his victim--me!", "이것이 그의 가장 큰 두려움이다. 그리고 자기가 발각되는 것을 막고자 그는, 할 수 있는 한, 제 희생자인 나마저 밀어냈다!"]], [["2. By Rail.--There is no one in charge of the box.", "2. 철로로. — 상자를 맡아볼 사람이 없다."], ["It would have to take its chance of being delayed; and delay would be fatal, with enemies on the track.", "상자는 지체될 운에 내맡겨질 수밖에 없을 것이며, 적들이 뒤를 쫓는 마당에 지체란 치명적일 것이다."], ["True, he might escape at night; but what would he be, if left in a strange place with no refuge that he could fly to?", "물론 그는 밤에 빠져나갈 수도 있을 것이다. 그러나 도망쳐 들 은신처 하나 없이 낯선 곳에 버려진다면 그는 어찌 되겠는가?"], ["This is not what he intends; and he does not mean to risk it.", "이는 그가 의도하는 바가 아니며, 그는 그런 위험을 무릅쓸 생각이 없다."]], [["3. By Water.--Here is the safest way, in one respect, but with most danger in another.", "3. 물길로. — 이것은 한 가지 점에서는 가장 안전한 길이나, 다른 한 가지 점에서는 가장 위험이 크다."], ["On the water he is powerless except at night; even then he can only summon fog and storm and snow and his wolves.", "물 위에서 그는 밤을 빼고는 무력하다. 밤이라 해도 그가 불러낼 수 있는 것은 안개와 폭풍과 눈, 그리고 그의 늑대들뿐이다."], ["But were he wrecked, the living water would engulf him, helpless; and he would indeed be lost.", "그러나 만일 난파한다면, 살아 있는 물이 무력한 그를 집어삼킬 것이고, 그는 정녕 끝장날 것이다."], ["He could have the vessel drive to land; but if it were unfriendly land, wherein he was not free to move, his position would still be desperate.", "그는 배를 육지로 몰아갈 수도 있을 것이다. 그러나 만일 그곳이 그가 자유로이 움직일 수 없는 낯선 땅이라면, 그의 처지는 여전히 절망적일 것이다."]], [["We know from the record that he was on the water; so what we have to do is to ascertain what water.", "우리는 기록을 통해 그가 물 위에 있었음을 안다. 그러니 우리가 해야 할 일은 그것이 어떤 물길인지를 밝혀내는 것이다."]], [["The first thing is to realise exactly what he has done as yet; we may, then, get a light on what his later task is to be.", "가장 먼저 할 일은 그가 지금까지 정확히 무엇을 했는지를 헤아리는 것이다. 그러면 그의 다음 과제가 무엇일지에 대해 빛을 얻을 수 있을지도 모른다."]], [["Firstly.--We must differentiate between what he did in London as part of his general plan of action, when he was pressed for moments and had to arrange as best he could.", "첫째. — 우리는 그가 런던에서 한 일, 곧 시간에 쫓겨 할 수 있는 한 최선으로 일을 꾸려야 했던 그의 전반적인 행동 계획의 일부였던 일을 구별해내야 한다."]], [["Secondly we must see, as well as we can surmise it from the facts we know of, what he has done here.", "둘째, 우리는 우리가 아는 사실들로부터 추측할 수 있는 한, 그가 이곳에서 무엇을 했는지를 살펴야 한다."]], [["As to the first, he evidently intended to arrive at Galatz, and sent invoice to Varna to deceive us lest we should ascertain his means of exit from England; his immediate and sole purpose then was to escape.", "첫째 일에 관하여, 그는 분명 갈라츠에 도착할 작정이었고, 우리가 그의 영국 탈출 경로를 알아내지 못하도록 속이려고 바르나로 송장(送狀)을 보냈다. 그러니 그때 그의 당면하고 유일한 목적은 빠져나가는 것이었다."], ["The proof of this, is the letter of instructions sent to Immanuel Hildesheim to clear and take away the box before sunrise.", "그 증거는, 상자를 일출 전에 통관시켜 가져가라고 이마누엘 힐데스하임에게 보낸 지시서이다."], ["There is also the instruction to Petrof Skinsky.", "또한 페트로프 스킨스키에게 보낸 지시도 있다."], ["These we must only guess at; but there must have been some letter or message, since Skinsky came to Hildesheim.", "이것은 우리가 추측할 수밖에 없으나, 스킨스키가 힐데스하임을 찾아온 것을 보면 어떤 편지나 전언이 있었음에 틀림없다."]], [["That, so far, his plans were successful we know.", "지금까지 그의 계획이 성공적이었음을 우리는 안다."], ["The Czarina Catherine made a phenomenally quick journey--so much so that Captain Donelson’s suspicions were aroused; but his superstition united with his canniness played the Count’s game for him, and he ran with his favouring wind through fogs and all till he brought up blindfold at Galatz.", "차리나 카테리나 호는 놀랍도록 빠른 항해를 했는데 — 어찌나 빨랐던지 도널슨 선장의 의심을 자아낼 정도였다 — 그러나 그의 미신이 그의 빈틈없음과 어우러져 백작을 위해 판을 짜준 셈이었고, 그는 순풍을 받으며 온갖 안개를 뚫고 내달려, 눈을 가린 채 갈라츠에 닿았다."], ["That the Count’s arrangements were well made, has been proved.", "백작의 준비가 잘 되어 있었음은 이미 입증되었다."], ["Hildesheim cleared the box, took it off, and gave it to Skinsky.", "힐데스하임은 상자를 통관시키고, 그것을 빼내어 스킨스키에게 넘겼다."], ["Skinsky took it--and here we lose the trail.", "스킨스키는 그것을 받았고 — 여기서 우리는 자취를 놓친다."], ["We only know that the box is somewhere on the water, moving along.", "우리가 아는 것은 다만 그 상자가 물 위 어딘가에서 움직여 가고 있다는 것뿐이다."], ["The customs and the octroi, if there be any, have been avoided.", "세관과 입시세는, 만일 있었다 해도, 피해버린 것이다."]], [["Now we come to what the Count must have done after his arrival--on land, at Galatz.", "이제 우리는 백작이 도착한 뒤, 갈라츠의 뭍에서 했음에 틀림없는 일에 이른다."]], [["The box was given to Skinsky before sunrise.", "상자는 일출 전에 스킨스키에게 넘겨졌다."], ["At sunrise the Count could appear in his own form.", "일출이 되면 백작은 제 본래의 모습으로 나타날 수 있었다."], ["Here, we ask why Skinsky was chosen at all to aid in the work?", "여기서 우리는, 어째서 하필 스킨스키가 이 일을 돕도록 택해졌는가를 묻게 된다."], ["In my husband’s diary, Skinsky is mentioned as dealing with the Slovaks who trade down the river to the port; and the man’s remark, that the murder was the work of a Slovak, showed the general feeling against his class.", "내 남편의 일기에서 스킨스키는, 강을 따라 항구까지 거래하는 슬로바키아인들을 상대하는 자로 언급되어 있다. 그리고 그 살인이 슬로바키아인의 짓이라던 사람들의 말은, 그 부류에 대한 일반적인 반감을 드러냈다."], ["The Count wanted isolation.", "백작은 고립을 원했던 것이다."]], [["My surmise is, this: that in London the Count decided to get back to his castle by water, as the most safe and secret way.", "내 추측은 이러하다. 런던에서 백작은 제 성으로 돌아가는 데에 물길을, 가장 안전하고 은밀한 방도로 택하기로 결정했다."], ["He was brought from the castle by Szgany, and probably they delivered their cargo to Slovaks who took the boxes to Varna, for there they were shipped for London.", "그는 성에서 스가니에 의해 옮겨졌고, 그들은 아마도 그 짐을 슬로바키아인들에게 넘겼을 것이며, 그 슬로바키아인들이 상자들을 바르나로 가져갔을 것이다. 거기서 그것들이 런던행으로 선적되었으니 말이다."], ["Thus the Count had knowledge of the persons who could arrange this service.", "그리하여 백작은 이런 일을 주선해줄 수 있는 사람들에 대한 정보를 갖고 있었다."], ["When the box was on land, before sunrise or after sunset, he came out from his box, met Skinsky and instructed him what to do as to arranging the carriage of the box up some river.", "상자가 뭍에 올랐을 때, 일출 전이나 일몰 후에 그는 제 상자에서 나와, 스킨스키를 만나 상자를 어느 강으로 거슬러 올려보낼 운반을 주선하도록 일러두었다."], ["When this was done, and he knew that all was in train, he blotted out his traces, as he thought, by murdering his agent.", "이것이 끝나고 만사가 차질 없이 진행되고 있음을 알자, 그는 자기가 생각하기에 자취를 지우려고 제 앞잡이를 살해해버렸다."]], [["I have examined the map and find that the river most suitable for the Slovaks to have ascended is either the Pruth or the Sereth.", "나는 지도를 살펴보고, 슬로바키아인들이 거슬러 올라갔을 법한 가장 알맞은 강이 프루트 강 아니면 세레트 강임을 알아냈다."], ["I read in the typescript that in my trance I heard cows low and water swirling level with my ears and the creaking of wood.", "나는 타자 기록에서, 가수면 상태에서 내가 소들이 우는 소리와 내 귀와 같은 높이에서 물이 소용돌이치는 소리, 그리고 나무가 삐걱대는 소리를 들었다는 것을 읽었다."], ["The Count in his box, then, was on a river in an open boat--propelled probably either by oars or poles, for the banks are near and it is working against stream.", "그렇다면 상자 속의 백작은 지붕 없는 배를 타고 어느 강 위에 있었던 것이며 — 그 배는 아마 노나 삿대로 움직였을 것이다. 강기슭이 가깝고 물살을 거슬러 가고 있었으니 말이다."], ["There would be no such sound if floating down stream.", "강을 따라 떠내려가는 중이었다면 그런 소리는 나지 않았을 것이다."]], [["Of course it may not be either the Sereth or the Pruth, but we may possibly investigate further.", "물론 세레트 강도 프루트 강도 아닐 수 있으나, 우리는 더 조사해볼 수 있을 것이다."], ["Now of these two, the Pruth is the more easily navigated, but the Sereth is, at Fundu, joined by the Bistritza which runs up round the Borgo Pass.", "그런데 이 두 강 가운데 프루트 강이 항행하기에 더 수월하나, 세레트 강은 푼두에서, 보르고 고개를 빙 둘러 거슬러 오르는 비스트리차 강과 합류한다."], ["The loop it makes is manifestly as close to Dracula’s castle as can be got by water.", "그 강이 그리는 굽이는, 물길로 닿을 수 있는 한 분명 드라큘라 성에 가장 가깝다."]], [["Mina Harker’s Journal--continued.", "「미나 하커의 일기」 — 계속"]], [["When I had done reading, Jonathan took me in his arms and kissed me.", "내가 다 읽고 나자 조너선은 나를 품에 안고 입을 맞추었다."], ["The others kept shaking me by both hands, and Dr. Van Helsing said:--", "다른 사람들은 내 두 손을 잡고 거듭 흔들었으며, 판 헬싱 박사가 말했다."]], [["“Our dear Madam Mina is once more our teacher.", "\"우리의 사랑하는 미나 부인이 다시 한번 우리의 스승이 되었습니다."], ["Her eyes have been where we were blinded.", "우리가 눈멀어 있던 곳을 부인의 눈은 꿰뚫어 보았어요."], ["Now we are on the track once again, and this time we may succeed.", "이제 우리는 다시 한번 자취를 좇게 되었고, 이번에는 성공할지도 모릅니다."], ["Our enemy is at his most helpless; and if we can come on him by day, on the water, our task will be over.", "우리의 적은 가장 무력한 상태에 있습니다. 만일 우리가 낮에, 물 위에서 그를 덮칠 수 있다면, 우리 일은 끝납니다."], ["He has a start, but he is powerless to hasten, as he may not leave his box lest those who carry him may suspect; for them to suspect would be to prompt them to throw him in the stream where he perish.", "그는 앞서 출발했으나, 서두를 힘이 없습니다. 자기를 나르는 자들이 의심할까 봐 제 상자를 떠날 수 없으니 말입니다. 그들이 의심하게 되면, 그것은 곧 그들로 하여금 그를 강물에 던지게 만드는 셈이고, 그러면 그는 죽고 말 테니까요."], ["This he knows, and will not.", "이를 그가 알고 있으니, 그리하지 않을 겁니다."], ["Now men, to our Council of War; for, here and now, we must plan what each and all shall do.”", "자, 여러분, 우리의 작전 회의로 들어갑시다. 바로 지금 여기서, 우리 각자가, 그리고 다 함께 무엇을 할지 계획을 세워야 합니다.\""]], [["“I shall get a steam launch and follow him,”", "\"나는 증기선을 한 척 구해 그를 뒤쫓겠소,\""], ["said Lord Godalming.", "고달밍 경이 말했다."]], [["“And I, horses to follow on the bank lest by chance he land,”", "\"그리고 나는, 혹시라도 그가 뭍에 오를 경우에 대비해 강기슭을 따라 뒤쫓을 말을 구하겠소,\""], ["said Mr. Morris.", "모리스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“Good!”", "\"좋소!\""], ["said the Professor, “both good.", "교수가 말했다. \"둘 다 좋습니다."], ["But neither must go alone.", "하지만 어느 쪽도 혼자 가서는 안 됩니다."], ["There must be force to overcome force if need be; the Slovak is strong and rough, and he carries rude arms.”", "필요하다면 힘을 힘으로 제압할 병력이 있어야 합니다. 슬로바키아인들은 억세고 거칠며, 조잡한 무기를 지니고 다니니까요.\""], ["All the men smiled, for amongst them they carried a small arsenal.", "사내들이 모두 빙긋 웃었으니, 그들끼리 작은 무기고 하나를 지니고 다닌 까닭이었다."], ["Said Mr. Morris:--", "모리스 씨가 말했다."]], [["“I have brought some Winchesters; they are pretty handy in a crowd, and there may be wolves.", "\"윈체스터 소총을 몇 자루 가져왔습니다. 무리를 상대할 때 꽤 요긴하고, 늑대가 나올지도 모르니까요."], ["The Count, if you remember, took some other precautions; he made some requisitions on others that Mrs. Harker could not quite hear or understand.", "백작은, 기억하시겠지만, 또 다른 대비책도 마련했습니다. 하커 부인이 미처 다 듣거나 알아듣지 못한, 다른 자들에게 보낸 요청도 있었지요."], ["We must be ready at all points.” Dr.", "우리는 모든 점에서 만반의 준비를 갖춰야 합니다.\""], ["Seward said:--", "수어드 박사가 말했다."]], [["“I think I had better go with Quincey.", "\"내 생각엔 내가 퀸시와 함께 가는 게 낫겠소."], ["We have been accustomed to hunt together, and we two, well armed, will be a match for whatever may come along.", "우리는 함께 사냥하는 데 이골이 났으니, 우리 둘이 잘 무장하면 무엇이 닥치든 능히 맞설 수 있을 거요."], ["You must not be alone, Art.", "자네는 혼자 있어선 안 되네, 아트."], ["It may be necessary to fight the Slovaks, and a chance thrust--for I don’t suppose these fellows carry guns--would undo all our plans.", "슬로바키아인들과 싸워야 할지도 모르고, 어쩌다 한 번 잘못 찔리기라도 하면 — 이자들이 총을 지니고 다니지는 않을 듯하니 말이오만 — 우리 계획이 죄다 어그러질 거요."], ["There must be no chances, this time; we shall not rest until the Count’s head and body have been separated, and we are sure that he cannot re-incarnate.”", "이번에는 요행에 맡길 일이 없어야 합니다. 백작의 머리와 몸뚱이가 갈라지고, 그가 다시 환생할 수 없음을 확신하기 전에는 우리는 쉬지 않을 거요.\""], ["He looked at Jonathan as he spoke, and Jonathan looked at me.", "그는 이렇게 말하며 조너선을 바라보았고, 조너선은 나를 바라보았다."], ["I could see that the poor dear was torn about in his mind.", "나는 가엾은 그이의 마음이 갈가리 찢기는 것을 알 수 있었다."], ["Of course he wanted to be with me; but then the boat service would, most likely, be the one which would destroy the ...", "물론 그는 나와 함께 있고 싶어했다. 그러나 한편으로, 그 배 쪽 일이야말로 십중팔구 그…… 그…… 그……"], ["the ... the ... Vampire.", "흡혈귀를 파멸시키는 일일 터였다."], ["(Why did I hesitate to write the word?) He was silent awhile, and during his silence Dr. Van Helsing spoke:--", "(어찌하여 나는 그 말을 쓰기를 망설였을까?) 그는 한동안 말이 없었고, 그가 침묵하는 동안 판 헬싱 박사가 말했다."]], [["“Friend Jonathan, this is to you for twice reasons.", "\"조너선 군, 이건 두 가지 이유에서 자네 몫이라네."], ["First, because you are young and brave and can fight, and all energies may be needed at the last; and again that it is your right to destroy him--that--which has wrought such woe to you and yours.", "첫째는, 자네가 젊고 용감하며 싸울 수 있고, 마지막 순간에는 온갖 기력이 다 필요할지 모르기 때문이지. 또 하나는, 자네와 자네 식구들에게 그토록 큰 화를 끼친 그것을 멸할 권리가 바로 자네에게 있기 때문일세."], ["Be not afraid for Madam Mina; she will be my care, if I may.", "미나 부인에 대해서는 걱정 말게. 허락된다면 그분은 내가 보살피겠네."], ["I am old. My legs are not so quick to run as once; and I am not used to ride so long or to pursue as need be, or to fight with lethal weapons.", "나는 늙었네. 내 다리는 예전만큼 빨리 달리지 못하고, 그리 오래 말을 타거나 필요한 만큼 추격하거나 살상 무기로 싸우는 데에도 익숙지 않다네."], ["But I can be of other service; I can fight in other way.", "그러나 나는 다른 쓸모가 있을 수 있어. 나는 다른 방식으로 싸울 수 있네."], ["And I can die, if need be, as well as younger men.", "그리고 필요하다면, 젊은이들 못지않게 죽을 수도 있고."], ["Now let me say that what I would is this: while you, my Lord Godalming and friend Jonathan go in your so swift little steamboat up the river, and whilst John and Quincey guard the bank where perchance he might be landed, I will take Madam Mina right into the heart of the enemy’s country.", "자, 이제 내 뜻을 말하겠네. 고달밍 경과 조너선 군 자네가 그 빠른 작은 증기선을 타고 강을 거슬러 올라가고, 한편으로 존과 퀸시가 그가 혹 뭍에 오를지도 모를 강기슭을 지키는 동안, 나는 미나 부인을 데리고 곧장 적의 나라 한복판으로 들어가겠네."], ["Whilst the old fox is tied in his box, floating on the running stream whence he cannot escape to land--where he dares not raise the lid of his coffin-box lest his Slovak carriers should in fear leave him to perish--we shall go in the track where Jonathan went,--from Bistritz over the Borgo, and find our way to the Castle of Dracula.", "그 늙은 여우가 흐르는 물 위에 떠서, 뭍으로는 빠져나갈 수 없는 제 상자에 묶여 있는 동안 — 슬로바키아인 운반꾼들이 겁먹고 그를 버려 죽게 내버려둘까 봐 감히 제 관 상자의 뚜껑조차 들어올리지 못하는 동안 — 우리는 조너선이 갔던 길을 따라가겠네. 비스트리츠에서 보르고를 넘어 드라큘라 성으로 가는 길을 찾아갈 걸세."], ["Here, Madam Mina’s hypnotic power will surely help, and we shall find our way--all dark and unknown otherwise--after the first sunrise when we are near that fateful place.", "그곳에서는 미나 부인의 최면 능력이 분명 도움이 될 테고, 우리가 그 운명의 장소에 가까워진 뒤 첫 일출이 지나면 — 그렇지 않고서는 온통 어둡고 알 수 없을 — 길을 찾아낼 걸세."], ["There is much to be done, and other places to be made sanctify, so that that nest of vipers be obliterated.”", "할 일이 많고, 또 거룩하게 만들어야 할 다른 장소들도 있으니, 그 독사 떼의 소굴을 깨끗이 쓸어버려야 한다네.\""], ["Here Jonathan interrupted him hotly:--", "여기서 조너선이 발끈하여 그의 말을 가로막았다."]], [["“Do you mean to say, Professor Van Helsing, that you would bring Mina, in her sad case and tainted as she is with that devil’s illness, right into the jaws of his death-trap?", "\"판 헬싱 교수님, 그 가엾은 처지에다 그 악마의 병독에 물든 미나를, 바로 그자가 놓은 죽음의 덫 아가리 속으로 데려가시겠다는 말씀입니까? 천만에요!"], ["Not for the world! Not for Heaven or Hell!”", "천국을 준대도, 지옥을 준대도 안 됩니다!\""], ["He became almost speechless for a minute, and then went on:--", "그는 잠시 거의 말을 잇지 못하다가, 다시 말했다."]], [["“Do you know what the place is?", "\"그곳이 어떤 곳인지 아십니까?"], ["Have you seen that awful den of hellish infamy--with the very moonlight alive with grisly shapes, and every speck of dust that whirls in the wind a devouring monster in embryo?", "바로 그 달빛마저 소름 끼치는 형상들로 살아 꿈틀거리고, 바람에 휘도는 티끌 한 점 한 점이 막 잉태된 게걸스러운 괴물인 — 그 지옥처럼 추악한 끔찍한 소굴을 보신 적이 있습니까?"], ["Have you felt the Vampire’s lips upon your throat?”", "흡혈귀의 입술이 교수님 목에 닿는 것을 느껴보신 적이 있습니까?\""], ["Here he turned to me, and as his eyes lit on my forehead he threw up his arms with a cry: “Oh, my God, what have we done to have this terror upon us!”", "여기서 그는 나를 향해 돌아섰고, 그의 눈길이 내 이마에 닿자 그는 외마디 소리를 지르며 두 팔을 치켜올렸다. \"오, 하느님, 우리가 무슨 짓을 했기에 이런 공포가 우리에게 닥쳤단 말입니까!\""], ["and he sank down on the sofa in a collapse of misery.", "그러고는 비참함에 무너져 소파에 주저앉았다."], ["The Professor’s voice, as he spoke in clear, sweet tones, which seemed to vibrate in the air, calmed us all:--", "교수가 맑고 부드러운 어조로 말하자, 그 목소리가 허공에 울리는 듯하며 우리 모두를 진정시켰다."]], [["“Oh, my friend, it is because I would save Madam Mina from that awful place that I would go.", "\"오, 조너선 군, 내가 가려는 것은 바로 미나 부인을 그 끔찍한 곳에서 구하기 위함일세."], ["God forbid that I should take her into that place.", "내가 그분을 그곳으로 데려간다니, 하느님 맙소사, 결코 안 될 일이지."], ["There is work--wild work--to be done there, that her eyes may not see.", "거기서는 해야 할 일이 — 거친 일이 — 있는데, 그것은 그분의 눈에 보여서는 안 되는 것일세."], ["We men here, all save Jonathan, have seen with their own eyes what is to be done before that place can be purify.", "여기 있는 우리 사내들은, 조너선 군만 빼고, 그곳이 정화되기 전에 무슨 일이 행해져야 하는지를 제 눈으로 똑똑히 보았네."], ["Remember that we are in terrible straits.", "우리가 무서운 궁지에 처해 있음을 잊지 말게."], ["If the Count escape us this time--and he is strong and subtle and cunning--he may choose to sleep him for a century, and then in time our dear one”--he took my hand--“would come to him to keep him company, and would be as those others that you, Jonathan, saw.", "만일 백작이 이번에 우리에게서 빠져나간다면 — 그는 강하고 교묘하며 교활하니 — 한 세기 동안 잠들어 있기를 택할지도 모르고, 그러면 때가 되어 우리의 사랑하는 분이\" — 그는 내 손을 잡았다 — \"그에게 동무가 되어주러 가게 될 것이며, 조너선 군 자네가 본 그 다른 여인들과 같이 될 걸세."], ["You have told us of their gloating lips; you heard their ribald laugh as they clutched the moving bag that the Count threw to them.", "자네는 그들의 게걸스러운 입술에 대해 우리에게 이야기했지. 백작이 그들에게 던져준 꿈틀거리는 자루를 움켜쥐며 내던 상스러운 웃음소리를 자네는 들었고."], ["You shudder; and well may it be.", "자네가 몸서리치는 것도 무리가 아닐세."], ["Forgive me that I make you so much pain, but it is necessary.", "자네에게 이토록 큰 고통을 주는 것을 용서하게나. 그러나 그것은 필요한 일이라네."], ["My friend, is it not a dire need for the which I am giving, possibly my life?", "벗이여, 내가 어쩌면 내 목숨을 내놓으려는 그 일이, 절박한 필요에서 나온 것이 아니겠는가?"], ["If it were that any one went into that place to stay, it is I who would have to go to keep them company.”", "만일 누군가 그곳에 들어가 머물러야 한다면, 그들에게 동무가 되어주러 가야 할 사람은 바로 나일세.\""]], [["“Do as you will,”", "\"뜻대로 하십시오,\""], ["said Jonathan, with a sob that shook him all over, “we are in the hands of God!”", "조너선이 온몸을 떨게 하는 흐느낌과 함께 말했다. \"우리는 하느님의 손안에 있습니다!\""]], [["Later.--Oh, it did me good to see the way that these brave men worked.", "이후. — 오, 이 용감한 사내들이 일하는 모습을 보니 내 마음이 든든해졌다."], ["How can women help loving men when they are so earnest, and so true, and so brave!", "사내들이 이토록 진지하고, 이토록 참되며, 이토록 용감할 때 여인이 어찌 그들을 사랑하지 않을 수 있으랴!"], ["And, too, it made me think of the wonderful power of money!", "그리고 또한, 그것은 돈의 놀라운 힘을 새삼 생각하게 했다!"], ["What can it not do when it is properly applied; and what might it do when basely used.", "제대로 쓰일 때 돈이 못할 일이 무엇이며, 비열하게 쓰일 때는 또 무슨 일을 저지를 수 있으랴."], ["I felt so thankful that Lord Godalming is rich, and that both he and Mr. Morris, who also has plenty of money, are willing to spend it so freely.", "나는 고달밍 경이 부유하고, 그와, 역시 돈이 넉넉한 모리스 씨가 둘 다 그 돈을 이토록 아낌없이 쓰려 한다는 것이 너무도 고마웠다."], ["For if they did not, our little expedition could not start, either so promptly or so well equipped, as it will within another hour.", "만일 그들이 그러지 않았더라면, 우리의 이 작은 원정대는 한 시간 안에 그럴 수 있듯 이토록 신속하게도, 이토록 잘 갖추어진 채로도 출발하지 못했을 테니 말이다."], ["It is not three hours since it was arranged what part each of us was to do; and now Lord Godalming and Jonathan have a lovely steam launch, with steam up ready to start at a moment’s notice. Dr.", "우리 각자가 어떤 역할을 맡을지 정해진 지 채 세 시간도 되지 않았는데, 이제 고달밍 경과 조너선은 증기를 올려 언제라도 출발할 채비를 갖춘 멋진 증기선 한 척을 갖추었다."], ["Seward and Mr. Morris have half a dozen good horses, well appointed.", "수어드 박사와 모리스 씨는 잘 갖추어진 좋은 말 여섯 필을 마련했다."], ["We have all the maps and appliances of various kinds that can be had.", "우리는 구할 수 있는 온갖 종류의 지도와 도구를 다 갖추었다."], ["Professor Van Helsing and I are to leave by the 11:40 train to-night for Veresti, where we are to get a carriage to drive to the Borgo Pass.", "판 헬싱 교수와 나는 오늘 밤 11시 40분 기차로 베레슈티로 떠나, 거기서 마차를 구해 보르고 고개로 갈 것이다. 마차와 말을 사야 하므로 우리는 적잖은 현금을 챙겨가고 있다."], ["We are bringing a good deal of ready money, as we are to buy a carriage and horses.", "이 일에 관해 믿고 맡길 사람이 없으니 우리가 직접 몰 것이다."], ["We shall drive ourselves, for we have no one whom we can trust in the matter.", "교수는 매우 여러 나라 말을 어느 정도 아니, 우리는 그럭저럭 해나갈 것이다."], ["The Professor knows something of a great many languages, so we shall get on all right.", "우리는 모두 무기를 지녔는데, 나에게까지도 대구경 권총 한 자루가 주어졌다."], ["We have all got arms, even for me a large-bore revolver; Jonathan would not be happy unless I was armed like the rest. Alas!", "조너선은 내가 나머지 사람들처럼 무장하지 않으면 마음이 놓이지 않을 것이다. 아아!"], ["I cannot carry one arm that the rest do; the scar on my forehead forbids that. Dear Dr.", "나는 나머지 사람들이 지닌 한 가지 무기만은 지닐 수가 없다. 내 이마의 흉터가 그것을 금하기 때문이다."], ["Van Helsing comforts me by telling me that I am fully armed as there may be wolves; the weather is getting colder every hour, and there are snow-flurries which come and go as warnings.", "사랑하는 판 헬싱 박사는, 늑대가 나올지 모르니 나도 충분히 무장한 셈이라고 일러주며 나를 위로한다. 날씨는 시시각각 더 추워지고 있고, 경고하듯 왔다가 사라지는 눈보라가 인다."]], [["Later.--It took all my courage to say good-bye to my darling.", "이후. — 사랑하는 그이에게 작별을 고하는 데에 내 온 용기를 다 짜내야 했다."], ["We may never meet again.", "우리는 다시는 만나지 못할지도 모른다."], ["Courage, Mina!", "용기를 내, 미나!"], ["the Professor is looking at you keenly; his look is a warning.", "교수가 너를 날카롭게 바라보고 있어. 그의 눈길은 하나의 경고야."], ["There must be no tears now--unless it may be that God will let them fall in gladness.", "이제 눈물을 보여서는 안 돼 — 하느님께서 기쁨의 눈물을 흘리도록 허락하시는 게 아니라면."]], [["Jonathan Harker’s Journal.", "「조너선 하커의 일기」"]], [["October 30.", "10월 30일. 밤."], ["Night.--I am writing this in the light from the furnace door of the steam launch: Lord Godalming is firing up.", "— 나는 증기선 화실(火室) 문에서 새어나오는 불빛에 의지해 이 글을 쓴다. 고달밍 경이 불을 때고 있다."], ["He is an experienced hand at the work, as he has had for years a launch of his own on the Thames, and another on the Norfolk Broads.", "그는 이 일에 노련한 솜씨를 지녔는데, 여러 해 동안 템스 강에 제 증기선 한 척을, 또 노퍽 브로즈에 다른 한 척을 두고 부려온 까닭이다."], ["Regarding our plans, we finally decided that Mina’s guess was correct, and that if any waterway was chosen for the Count’s escape back to his Castle, the Sereth and then the Bistritza at its junction, would be the one.", "우리 계획에 관해서는, 결국 미나의 추측이 옳았다는 것, 그리고 만일 백작이 제 성으로 돌아가는 탈출로로 어떤 물길을 택했다면 그것은 세레트 강, 그리고 그 합류 지점에서 비스트리차 강이리라는 것으로 결론을 지었다."], ["We took it, that somewhere about the 47th degree, north latitude, would be the place chosen for the crossing the country between the river and the Carpathians.", "우리는 강과 카르파티아 산맥 사이의 땅을 가로지를 지점으로, 북위 47도 언저리쯤이 택해졌으리라 보았다. 밤에 강을 거슬러 빠른 속도로 달리는 데에 우리는 아무 두려움이 없다."], ["We have no fear in running at good speed up the river at night; there is plenty of water, and the banks are wide enough apart to make steaming, even in the dark, easy enough.", "물이 넉넉하고, 양쪽 기슭이 충분히 멀리 떨어져 있어 어둠 속에서도 증기 항행이 그리 어렵지 않을 만하기 때문이다. 고달밍 경은 나에게 잠시 자두라고 한다."], ["Lord Godalming tells me to sleep for a while, as it is enough for the present for one to be on watch. But I cannot sleep--how can I with the terrible danger hanging over my darling, and her going out into that awful place....", "당장은 한 사람만 망을 봐도 족하다는 것이다. 그러나 나는 잠들 수가 없다 — 끔찍한 위험이 내 사랑하는 사람 위에 드리워 있고, 그녀가 그 무서운 곳으로 들어가는 마당에 내가 어찌 잠들 수 있으랴……."], ["My only comfort is that we are in the hands of God.", "나의 유일한 위안은 우리가 하느님의 손안에 있다는 것이다."], ["Only for that faith it would be easier to die than to live, and so be quit of all the trouble. Mr.", "그 믿음이 아니라면, 사는 것보다 죽는 편이, 그리하여 이 모든 고난에서 벗어나는 편이 더 쉬울 것이다."], ["Morris and Dr. Seward were off on their long ride before we started; they are to keep up the right bank, far enough off to get on higher lands where they can see a good stretch of river and avoid the following of its curves.", "모리스 씨와 수어드 박사는 우리가 출발하기 전에 그들의 긴 여정에 올랐다. 그들은 오른쪽 기슭을 따라가되, 강의 넓은 줄기를 한눈에 볼 수 있고 강굽이를 일일이 따라가는 것을 피할 수 있는 더 높은 땅에 오를 만큼 충분히 떨어져 가기로 했다."], ["They have, for the first stages, two men to ride and lead their spare horses--four in all, so as not to excite curiosity.", "그들은, 처음 몇 구간은, 여분의 말들을 몰고 갈 두 사람을 데리고 가는데 — 모두 네 필이니, 사람들의 호기심을 자극하지 않기 위해서다."], ["When they dismiss the men, which shall be shortly, they shall themselves look after the horses.", "그들이 그 사람들을 돌려보내고 나면 — 곧 그리할 것이다 — 말들은 그들 자신이 돌볼 것이다."], ["It may be necessary for us to join forces; if so they can mount our whole party.", "우리가 합세해야 할 필요가 생길지도 모르는데, 그렇게 되면 그들이 우리 일행 전체를 말에 태울 수 있다."], ["One of the saddles has a movable horn, and can be easily adapted for Mina, if required.", "안장 하나는 떼었다 붙였다 할 수 있는 뿔이 달려 있어, 필요하면 미나에게 알맞게 쉬이 맞출 수 있다."]], [["It is a wild adventure we are on.", "우리는 거친 모험에 나서 있다."], ["Here, as we are rushing along through the darkness, with the cold from the river seeming to rise up and strike us; with all the mysterious voices of the night around us, it all comes home.", "이렇게, 어둠을 뚫고 내달리며, 강에서 솟아올라 우리를 후려치는 듯한 한기 속에서, 우리를 에워싼 밤의 온갖 신비로운 목소리들 가운데서, 그 모든 것이 사무친다."], ["We seem to be drifting into unknown places and unknown ways; into a whole world of dark and dreadful things.", "우리는 알 수 없는 곳, 알 수 없는 길로 떠밀려가는 것만 같다. 어둡고 무시무시한 것들로 가득한 온 세계 속으로 말이다."], ["Godalming is shutting the furnace door....", "고달밍이 화실 문을 닫고 있다……."]], [["31 October.--Still hurrying along.", "10월 31일. — 여전히 내달리는 중이다."], ["The day has come, and Godalming is sleeping.", "날이 밝았고, 고달밍은 자고 있다."], ["I am on watch.", "내가 망을 본다."], ["The morning is bitterly cold; the furnace heat is grateful, though we have heavy fur coats.", "아침은 살을 에듯 춥다. 두꺼운 모피 외투를 입었는데도 화실의 열기가 고맙다."], ["As yet we have passed only a few open boats, but none of them had on board any box or package of anything like the size of the one we seek.", "지금까지 우리는 지붕 없는 배 몇 척밖에 지나치지 못했는데, 그중 어느 것에도 우리가 찾는 것만 한 크기의 상자나 꾸러미는 실려 있지 않았다."], ["The men were scared every time we turned our electric lamp on them, and fell on their knees and prayed.", "사람들은 우리가 그들에게 전기등을 비출 때마다 겁에 질려, 무릎을 꿇고 기도를 올렸다."]], [["1 November, evening.--No news all day; we have found nothing of the kind we seek.", "11월 1일, 저녁. — 온종일 아무 소식이 없다. 우리가 찾는 그런 것을 도무지 찾지 못했다."], ["We have now passed into the Bistritza; and if we are wrong in our surmise our chance is gone.", "우리는 이제 비스트리차 강으로 접어들었다. 만일 우리 추측이 틀렸다면 우리의 기회는 사라진 것이다."], ["We have over-hauled every boat, big and little.", "우리는 크든 작든 모든 배를 샅샅이 뒤졌다."], ["Early this morning, one crew took us for a Government boat, and treated us accordingly.", "오늘 이른 아침, 한 배의 선원들이 우리를 관선(官船)으로 여겨 그에 맞게 대접했다."], ["We saw in this a way of smoothing matters, so at Fundu, where the Bistritza runs into the Sereth, we got a Roumanian flag which we now fly conspicuously.", "우리는 여기서 일을 매끄럽게 풀 방도를 보았으므로, 비스트리차 강이 세레트 강으로 흘러드는 푼두에서 루마니아 국기 하나를 구해 지금은 그것을 눈에 띄게 내걸고 다닌다."], ["With every boat which we have over-hauled since then this trick has succeeded; we have had every deference shown to us, and not once any objection to whatever we chose to ask or do.", "그 뒤로 우리가 샅샅이 뒤진 모든 배에서 이 수법은 먹혀들었다. 사람들은 우리에게 온갖 깍듯한 예의를 차렸고, 우리가 무엇을 묻거나 무엇을 하기로 하든 단 한 번도 이의를 달지 않았다."], ["Some of the Slovaks tell us that a big boat passed them, going at more than usual speed as she had a double crew on board.", "슬로바키아인 몇몇이 우리에게 일러주기를, 큰 배 한 척이 평소보다 빠른 속도로 그들 곁을 지나갔는데 그것은 갑판에 두 배의 선원을 태우고 있었기 때문이라고 했다."], ["This was before they came to Fundu, so they could not tell us whether the boat turned into the Bistritza or continued on up the Sereth.", "이는 그들이 푼두에 닿기 전의 일이라, 그 배가 비스트리차 강으로 접어들었는지 아니면 세레트 강을 따라 계속 올라갔는지는 일러주지 못했다."], ["At Fundu we could not hear of any such boat, so she must have passed there in the night.", "푼두에서는 그런 배에 대한 말을 도무지 들을 수 없었으니, 그 배는 밤중에 그곳을 지나친 게 분명하다."], ["I am feeling very sleepy; the cold is perhaps beginning to tell upon me, and nature must have rest some time.", "나는 몹시 졸리다. 추위가 어쩌면 내게 영향을 미치기 시작한 모양이고, 사람의 몸이란 언젠가는 쉬어야 하는 법이다."], ["Godalming insists that he shall keep the first watch. God bless him for all his goodness to poor dear Mina and me.", "고달밍이 자기가 첫 망을 보겠다고 우긴다. 가엾고 사랑하는 미나와 나에게 베푼 그 모든 선의에 대해, 하느님께서 그를 축복하시기를."]], [["2 November, morning.--It is broad daylight.", "11월 2일, 아침. — 환한 대낮이다."], ["That good fellow would not wake me.", "그 좋은 친구는 나를 깨우려 하지 않았다."], ["He says it would have been a sin to, for I slept peacefully and was forgetting my trouble.", "나를 깨우는 건 죄였으리라는 것이다. 내가 평온하게 자며 시름을 잊고 있었으니 말이다."], ["It seems brutally selfish to me to have slept so long, and let him watch all night; but he was quite right.", "이토록 오래 자며 그가 밤새 망보게 한 것이 내게는 잔인하리만치 이기적으로 여겨지나, 그가 옳았다."], ["I am a new man this morning; and, as I sit here and watch him sleeping, I can do all that is necessary both as to minding the engine, steering, and keeping watch.", "오늘 아침 나는 새사람이 되었다. 이렇게 여기 앉아 자는 그를 바라보며, 나는 기관을 돌보는 일도, 키를 잡는 일도, 망을 보는 일도 필요한 만큼 다 해낼 수 있다."], ["I can feel that my strength and energy are coming back to me.", "내 기운과 기력이 돌아오고 있음이 느껴진다."], ["I wonder where Mina is now, and Van Helsing.", "지금 미나는, 그리고 판 헬싱은 어디쯤 있을까."], ["They should have got to Veresti about noon on Wednesday.", "그들은 수요일 정오쯤 베레슈티에 닿았을 것이다."], ["It would take them some time to get the carriage and horses; so if they had started and travelled hard, they would be about now at the Borgo Pass. God guide and help them!", "마차와 말을 구하는 데 시간이 좀 걸렸을 테니, 만일 그들이 출발하여 부지런히 달렸다면 지금쯤 보르고 고개 언저리에 있을 것이다. 하느님께서 그들을 인도하고 도우시기를!"], ["I am afraid to think what may happen.", "무슨 일이 벌어질지 생각하기조차 두렵다."], ["If we could only go faster!", "우리가 좀 더 빨리 갈 수만 있다면!"], ["but we cannot; the engines are throbbing and doing their utmost.", "그러나 그럴 수가 없다. 기관은 고동치며 온 힘을 다하고 있다."], ["I wonder how Dr. Seward and Mr. Morris are getting on.", "수어드 박사와 모리스 씨는 어찌 되어가고 있을까."], ["There seem to be endless streams running down the mountains into this river, but as none of them are very large--at present, at all events, though they are terrible doubtless in winter and when the snow melts--the horsemen may not have met much obstruction.", "산에서 이 강으로 흘러내리는 물줄기가 끝없이 많은 듯하나, 그 가운데 그리 큰 것은 하나도 없으니 — 적어도 지금으로서는 그러하나, 겨울철과 눈이 녹을 때엔 의심할 바 없이 무서울 테지만 — 말 탄 두 사람이 그리 큰 장애를 만나지는 않았을 것이다."], ["I hope that before we get to Strasba we may see them; for if by that time we have not overtaken the Count, it may be necessary to take counsel together what to do next.", "슈트라스바에 닿기 전에 그들을 볼 수 있기를 바란다. 그때까지 우리가 백작을 따라잡지 못했다면, 다음에 어찌할지를 함께 의논할 필요가 생길지도 모르니 말이다."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「수어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["2 November.--Three days on the road.", "11월 2일. — 길에 오른 지 사흘째다."], ["No news, and no time to write it if there had been, for every moment is precious.", "아무 소식 없고, 설령 있었더라도 그것을 쓸 겨를이 없으니, 매 순간이 소중하기 때문이다."], ["We have had only the rest needful for the horses; but we are both bearing it wonderfully.", "우리는 말들에게 필요한 만큼의 휴식밖에 취하지 못했으나, 우리 둘은 놀랍도록 잘 견뎌내고 있다."], ["Those adventurous days of ours are turning up useful.", "그 모험으로 가득했던 우리의 지난날이 쓸모를 드러내고 있다."], ["We must push on; we shall never feel happy till we get the launch in sight again.", "우리는 밀어붙여야 한다. 다시 증기선을 시야에 넣기 전에는 결코 마음이 편치 못할 것이다."]], [["3 November.--We heard at Fundu that the launch had gone up the Bistritza.", "11월 3일. — 우리는 푼두에서 증기선이 비스트리차 강을 거슬러 올라갔다는 말을 들었다."], ["I wish it wasn’t so cold.", "이토록 춥지만 않다면 좋으련만."], ["There are signs of snow coming; and if it falls heavy it will stop us.", "눈이 올 조짐이 보인다. 만일 눈이 많이 쏟아지면 그것이 우리를 멈춰 세울 것이다."], ["In such case we must get a sledge and go on, Russian fashion.", "그런 경우 우리는 썰매를 구해 러시아식으로 계속 나아가야 한다."]], [["4 November.--To-day we heard of the launch having been detained by an accident when trying to force a way up the rapids.", "11월 4일. — 오늘 우리는 증기선이 급류를 거슬러 오르려다 사고로 발이 묶였다는 말을 들었다."], ["The Slovak boats get up all right, by aid of a rope and steering with knowledge.", "슬로바키아인들의 배는, 밧줄의 도움과 노련한 키잡이 솜씨로 별 탈 없이 거슬러 오른다."], ["Some went up only a few hours before.", "몇 척은 겨우 몇 시간 전에 거슬러 올라갔다."], ["Godalming is an amateur fitter himself, and evidently it was he who put the launch in trim again.", "고달밍 자신이 아마추어 정비공인지라, 증기선을 다시 손본 것은 분명 그였다."], ["Finally, they got up the rapids all right, with local help, and are off on the chase afresh.", "마침내 그들은 현지인의 도움을 받아 무사히 급류를 거슬러 올랐고, 다시 추격에 나섰다."], ["I fear that the boat is not any better for the accident; the peasantry tell us that after she got upon smooth water again, she kept stopping every now and again so long as she was in sight.", "그 사고로 배 상태가 조금도 나아지지 않은 게 아닐까 두렵다. 농부들이 우리에게 일러주기를, 배가 다시 잔잔한 물에 들어선 뒤에도 시야에 있는 내내 이따금 자꾸만 멈춰 서곤 했다는 것이다."], ["We must push on harder than ever; our help may be wanted soon.", "우리는 그 어느 때보다 세차게 밀어붙여야 한다. 우리의 도움이 머지않아 필요해질지도 모른다."]], [["Mina Harker’s Journal.", "「미나 하커의 일기」"]], [["31 October.--Arrived at Veresti at noon.", "10월 31일. — 정오에 베레슈티에 도착했다."], ["The Professor tells me that this morning at dawn he could hardly hypnotise me at all, and that all I could say was: “dark and quiet.”", "교수가 나에게 일러주기를, 오늘 아침 동틀 무렵 그는 나를 거의 최면에 들게 할 수 없었으며, 내가 한 말이라곤 \"어둡고 고요해요\"뿐이었다고 한다."], ["He is off now buying a carriage and horses.", "그는 지금 마차와 말을 사러 나가 있다."], ["He says that he will later on try to buy additional horses, so that we may be able to change them on the way.", "그는 나중에 말을 더 사보겠다고 하는데, 가는 길에 말을 갈아탈 수 있게 하려는 것이다."], ["We have something more than 70 miles before us.", "우리 앞에는 70마일이 좀 넘는 길이 놓여 있다."], ["The country is lovely, and most interesting; if only we were under different conditions, how delightful it would be to see it all.", "이 고장은 아름답고 더없이 흥미롭다. 만일 우리가 다른 처지에 있었더라면, 이 모든 것을 둘러보는 일이 얼마나 즐거웠을까."], ["If Jonathan and I were driving through it alone what a pleasure it would be.", "만일 조너선과 내가 단둘이 이곳을 마차로 지나간다면 그 얼마나 큰 기쁨이었으랴."], ["To stop and see people, and learn something of their life, and to fill our minds and memories with all the colour and picturesqueness of the whole wild, beautiful country and the quaint people! But, alas!--", "멈춰 서서 사람들을 보고, 그들의 삶을 조금이나마 알고, 이 거칠고도 아름다운 온 고장과 예스러운 사람들의 온갖 빛깔과 그림 같은 정취로 우리의 마음과 기억을 채울 수 있다면! 그러나, 아아!—"]], [["Later.--Dr. Van Helsing has returned.", "이후. — 판 헬싱 박사가 돌아왔다."], ["He has got the carriage and horses; we are to have some dinner, and to start in an hour.", "그는 마차와 말을 구했다. 우리는 저녁을 좀 들고, 한 시간 뒤에 출발하기로 했다."], ["The landlady is putting us up a huge basket of provisions; it seems enough for a company of soldiers.", "여관 안주인이 우리를 위해 어마어마하게 큰 식료품 바구니를 꾸려주고 있는데, 한 무리의 병사들에게도 충분할 만큼으로 보인다."], ["The Professor encourages her, and whispers to me that it may be a week before we can get any good food again.", "교수는 그녀를 부추기면서, 다시 변변한 음식을 얻기까지 어쩌면 한 주가 걸릴지도 모른다고 나에게 속삭인다."], ["He has been shopping too, and has sent home such a wonderful lot of fur coats and wraps, and all sorts of warm things.", "그는 장도 봐 와서, 모피 외투며 덮을 것이며 온갖 따뜻한 것을 놀랄 만큼 잔뜩 집으로 보내왔다."], ["There will not be any chance of our being cold.", "우리가 추위에 떨 일은 없을 것이다."]], [["We shall soon be off.", "우리는 곧 출발한다."], ["I am afraid to think what may happen to us.", "우리에게 무슨 일이 벌어질지 생각하기조차 두렵다."], ["We are truly in the hands of God.", "우리는 참으로 하느님의 손안에 있다."], ["He alone knows what may be, and I pray Him, with all the strength of my sad and humble soul, that He will watch over my beloved husband; that whatever may happen, Jonathan may know that I loved him and honoured him more than I can say, and that my latest and truest thought will be always for him.", "무슨 일이 있을지는 오직 그분만이 아시며, 나는 슬프고 보잘것없는 내 영혼의 온 힘을 다해 그분께 기도한다. 부디 내 사랑하는 남편을 지켜주시기를. 무슨 일이 벌어지든, 조너선이 내가 그를 이루 말할 수 없이 사랑하고 존경했음을 알기를, 그리고 내 마지막의 가장 참된 생각이 언제나 그를 향하리라는 것을."]], [["# MINA HARKER’S JOURNAL", "# MINA HARKER’S JOURNAL"]], [["1 November.--All day long we have travelled, and at a good speed.", "「미나 하커의 일기」 11월 1일. — 온종일 우리는 빠른 속도로 달렸다."], ["The horses seem to know that they are being kindly treated, for they go willingly their full stage at best speed.", "말들도 자신들이 친절히 다루어지고 있음을 아는 듯, 한 구간을 기꺼이 최고 속도로 달려주었다."], ["We have now had so many changes and find the same thing so constantly that we are encouraged to think that the journey will be an easy one. Dr.", "이제 말을 워낙 여러 번 바꾸었고 언제나 똑같은 일이 거듭되니, 우리는 이 여정이 수월하리라고 기대하게 되었다."], ["Van Helsing is laconic; he tells the farmers that he is hurrying to Bistritz, and pays them well to make the exchange of horses.", "판 헬싱 박사는 말이 적다. 농부들에게는 자기가 비스트리차로 서둘러 가는 길이라고만 말하고, 말을 바꾸는 값을 후하게 치른다."], ["We get hot soup, or coffee, or tea; and off we go.", "우리는 뜨거운 수프나 커피, 차를 마시고는 곧 다시 떠난다."], ["It is a lovely country; full of beauties of all imaginable kinds, and the people are brave, and strong, and simple, and seem full of nice qualities.", "아름다운 고장이다. 상상할 수 있는 온갖 종류의 아름다움으로 가득하고, 사람들은 용감하고 강인하며 소박하고, 고운 품성으로 넘쳐 보인다."], ["They are very, very superstitious.", "그들은 정말이지 무척이나 미신을 믿는다."], ["In the first house where we stopped, when the woman who served us saw the scar on my forehead, she crossed herself and put out two fingers towards me, to keep off the evil eye.", "우리가 처음 머문 집에서, 시중을 들던 여인이 내 이마의 흉터를 보더니 성호를 긋고는 흉안을 막으려고 나에게 손가락 두 개를 내밀었다."], ["I believe they went to the trouble of putting an extra amount of garlic into our food; and I can’t abide garlic.", "그들은 일부러 음식에 마늘을 잔뜩 넣은 것 같은데, 나는 마늘을 견디지 못한다."], ["Ever since then I have taken care not to take off my hat or veil, and so have escaped their suspicions.", "그 뒤로 나는 모자와 베일을 벗지 않도록 조심했고, 그래서 그들의 의심을 피할 수 있었다."], ["We are travelling fast, and as we have no driver with us to carry tales, we go ahead of scandal; but I daresay that fear of the evil eye will follow hard behind us all the way.", "우리는 빠르게 달리고 있고, 소문을 퍼뜨릴 마부가 함께 있지 않으니 추문보다 앞서 나아간다. 그러나 흉안에 대한 두려움만은 가는 내내 우리 바로 뒤를 바짝 따라올 것이다."], ["The Professor seems tireless; all day he would not take any rest, though he made me sleep for a long spell.", "교수님은 지칠 줄 모르는 듯하다. 나에게는 오랫동안 잠을 자게 하면서도 정작 자신은 온종일 조금도 쉬지 않으려 했다."], ["At sunset time he hypnotised me, and he says that I answered as usual “darkness, lapping water and creaking wood”; so our enemy is still on the river.", "해질 무렵 그가 나에게 최면을 걸었는데, 내가 늘 하던 대로 \"어둠, 찰랑이는 물, 삐걱거리는 나무\"라고 대답했다고 한다. 그러니 우리의 적은 아직 강 위에 있다."], ["I am afraid to think of Jonathan, but somehow I have now no fear for him, or for myself.", "조너선을 생각하기가 두렵지만, 어쩐 일인지 이제는 그에 대해서도, 나 자신에 대해서도 두렵지 않다."], ["I write this whilst we wait in a farmhouse for the horses to be got ready. Dr.", "나는 말이 준비되기를 어느 농가에서 기다리며 이 글을 쓰고 있다."], ["Van Helsing is sleeping.", "판 헬싱 박사는 잠들어 있다."], ["Poor dear, he looks very tired and old and grey, but his mouth is set as firmly as a conqueror’s; even in his sleep he is instinct with resolution.", "가엾은 분, 무척 지치고 늙고 잿빛으로 보이지만, 그 입매만은 정복자처럼 굳게 다물려 있다. 잠결에도 결의가 배어 있다."], ["When we have well started I must make him rest whilst I drive.", "잘 출발하고 나면 내가 마차를 몰며 그를 쉬게 해야겠다."], ["I shall tell him that we have days before us, and we must not break down when most of all his strength will be needed....", "우리에게는 여러 날이 남아 있으니, 무엇보다 그의 힘이 가장 필요할 때 쓰러져서는 안 된다고 일러두어야겠다……"], ["All is ready; we are off shortly.", "모든 준비가 끝났다. 우리는 곧 떠난다."]], [["2 November, morning.--I was successful, and we took turns driving all night; now the day is on us, bright though cold.", "11월 2일, 아침. — 나는 성공했고, 우리는 밤새 번갈아 마차를 몰았다. 이제 날이 밝았는데, 환하지만 춥다."], ["There is a strange heaviness in the air--I say heaviness for want of a better word; I mean that it oppresses us both.", "공기 속에 묘한 무거움이 감돈다. 더 나은 말이 없어 무거움이라고 하지만, 내 말은 그것이 우리 둘 다를 짓누른다는 뜻이다."], ["It is very cold, and only our warm furs keep us comfortable.", "무척 춥고, 따뜻한 모피만이 우리를 견딜 만하게 해준다."], ["At dawn Van Helsing hypnotised me; he says I answered “darkness, creaking wood and roaring water,” so the river is changing as they ascend.", "동틀 녘에 판 헬싱이 나에게 최면을 걸었다. 내가 \"어둠, 삐걱거리는 나무, 으르렁거리는 물\"이라고 대답했다 하니, 그들이 거슬러 올라감에 따라 강이 변하고 있는 것이다."], ["I do hope that my darling will not run any chance of danger--more than need be; but we are in God’s hands.", "부디 내 사랑하는 이가 필요 이상의 위험을 무릅쓰지 않기를 빈다. 그러나 우리는 신의 손안에 있다."]], [["2 November, night.--All day long driving.", "11월 2일, 밤. — 온종일 마차를 몰았다."], ["The country gets wilder as we go, and the great spurs of the Carpathians, which at Veresti seemed so far from us and so low on the horizon, now seem to gather round us and tower in front.", "갈수록 고장은 더 거칠어지고, 베레슈티에서는 그토록 멀고 지평선 위로 그토록 낮게 보이던 카르파티아의 거대한 산줄기들이 이제는 우리를 에워싸며 앞쪽으로 우뚝 솟아오르는 듯하다."], ["We both seem in good spirits; I think we make an effort each to cheer the other; in the doing so we cheer ourselves. Dr.", "우리는 둘 다 기운차 보인다. 서로를 북돋우려 애쓰다 보니, 그렇게 하는 가운데 스스로도 힘을 얻는 듯하다."], ["Van Helsing says that by morning we shall reach the Borgo Pass.", "판 헬싱 박사는 아침이면 보르고 고개에 닿으리라고 한다."], ["The houses are very few here now, and the Professor says that the last horse we got will have to go on with us, as we may not be able to change.", "이제 이곳에는 집이 거의 없어서, 교수님은 더는 말을 바꾸지 못할지 모르니 마지막으로 얻은 말로 끝까지 가야 할 것이라고 한다."], ["He got two in addition to the two we changed, so that now we have a rude four-in-hand.", "그는 우리가 바꾼 두 마리에 더해 두 마리를 더 얻어두어서, 이제 우리에게는 어설프나마 네 마리 한 마차가 생겼다."], ["The dear horses are patient and good, and they give us no trouble.", "어여쁜 말들은 참을성 있고 순해서 우리를 성가시게 하지 않는다."], ["We are not worried with other travellers, and so even I can drive.", "다른 여행자들에게 시달릴 일도 없으니, 나조차도 마차를 몰 수 있다."], ["We shall get to the Pass in daylight; we do not want to arrive before.", "우리는 날이 밝을 때 고개에 닿을 것이다. 그 전에 도착하고 싶지는 않다."], ["So we take it easy, and have each a long rest in turn.", "그래서 우리는 서두르지 않고, 번갈아 한참씩 쉰다."], ["Oh, what will to-morrow bring to us?", "아, 내일은 우리에게 무엇을 가져다줄까?"], ["We go to seek the place where my poor darling suffered so much.", "우리는 내 가엾은 사랑하는 이가 그토록 고통받았던 곳을 찾아간다."], ["God grant that we may be guided aright, and that He will deign to watch over my husband and those dear to us both, and who are in such deadly peril.", "부디 신께서 우리를 올바로 인도해주시고, 우리 둘 모두에게 소중한, 이토록 무서운 위험에 처한 이들과 내 남편을 굽어살펴주시기를."], ["As for me, I am not worthy in His sight. Alas!", "나로 말하면, 나는 그분 보시기에 합당하지 못하다. 아아!"], ["I am unclean to His eyes, and shall be until He may deign to let me stand forth in His sight as one of those who have not incurred His wrath.", "나는 그분 눈에 부정한 존재이며, 그분께서 나를 그분의 진노를 사지 않은 자들 가운데 하나로 그분 앞에 서게 해주시기 전까지는 그러할 것이다."]], [["Memorandum by Abraham Van Helsing.", "「에이브러햄 판 헬싱의 비망록」"]], [["4 November.--This to my old and true friend John Seward, M.D., of Purfleet, London, in case I may not see him. It may explain.", "11월 4일. — 이것은 혹여 그를 다시 보지 못할 경우를 대비하여, 런던 퍼플리트의 의학박사 존 슈어드, 나의 오랜 참벗에게 남기는 글이다."], ["It is morning, and I write by a fire which all the night I have kept alive--Madam Mina aiding me.", "이것이 사정을 밝혀줄지도 모른다. 지금은 아침이고, 나는 밤새도록 꺼뜨리지 않고 지켜온 불가에서 이 글을 쓴다."], ["It is cold, cold; so cold that the grey heavy sky is full of snow, which when it falls will settle for all winter as the ground is hardening to receive it.", "미나 부인이 나를 도왔다. 춥다, 춥다. 어찌나 추운지 잿빛의 무거운 하늘이 눈을 잔뜩 머금고 있어, 그것이 내리면 땅이 그것을 받아들이려 단단히 굳어가고 있으니 겨우내 그대로 쌓여 있을 것이다."], ["It seems to have affected Madam Mina; she has been so heavy of head all day that she was not like herself.", "그것이 미나 부인에게 영향을 미친 듯하다. 그녀는 온종일 머리가 무거워 평소의 그녀답지 않았다."], ["She sleeps, and sleeps, and sleeps!", "그녀는 자고, 자고, 또 잔다!"], ["She who is usual so alert, have done literally nothing all the day; she even have lost her appetite.", "평소 그토록 기민하던 사람이 온종일 말 그대로 아무것도 하지 않았다. 입맛마저 잃었다."], ["She make no entry into her little diary, she who write so faithful at every pause.", "틈날 때마다 그토록 충실히 글을 쓰던 그녀가 자신의 작은 일기에 아무것도 적지 않았다."], ["Something whisper to me that all is not well.", "무언가가 나에게 모든 것이 온전치 않다고 속삭인다."], ["However, to-night she is more vif.", "그래도 오늘 밤 그녀는 한결 생기가 돈다."], ["Her long sleep all day have refresh and restore her, for now she is all sweet and bright as ever.", "온종일의 긴 잠이 그녀를 새롭게 하고 되살린 듯, 이제 그녀는 여느 때처럼 더없이 다정하고 환하다."], ["At sunset I try to hypnotise her, but alas!", "해질 무렵 나는 그녀에게 최면을 걸어보려 했으나, 아아!"], ["with no effect; the power has grown less and less with each day, and to-night it fail me altogether.", "아무 효과가 없었다. 그 힘은 날마다 점점 약해지더니, 오늘 밤에는 아예 나를 저버렸다."], ["Well, God’s will be done--whatever it may be, and whithersoever it may lead!", "그래, 신의 뜻이 이루어지기를. 그것이 무엇이든, 그것이 어디로 이끌든!"]], [["Now to the historical, for as Madam Mina write not in her stenography, I must, in my cumbrous old fashion, that so each day of us may not go unrecorded.", "이제 그간의 기록으로 넘어가자. 미나 부인이 자신의 속기로 적지 못하니, 내가 나의 거추장스러운 옛 방식으로나마 적어야겠다. 우리의 하루하루가 기록되지 않은 채 지나가지 않도록."]], [["We got to the Borgo Pass just after sunrise yesterday morning.", "우리는 어제 아침 해가 뜬 직후 보르고 고개에 닿았다."], ["When I saw the signs of the dawn I got ready for the hypnotism.", "새벽의 기미가 보이자 나는 최면을 걸 채비를 했다."], ["We stopped our carriage, and got down so that there might be no disturbance.", "우리는 마차를 세우고, 방해받지 않도록 내려섰다."], ["I made a couch with furs, and Madam Mina, lying down, yield herself as usual, but more slow and more short time than ever, to the hypnotic sleep.", "나는 모피로 잠자리를 만들었고, 미나 부인은 누워서 여느 때처럼, 그러나 어느 때보다 더디고 짧게 최면 잠에 몸을 맡겼다."], ["As before, came the answer: “darkness and the swirling of water.”", "전과 마찬가지로 대답이 돌아왔다. \"어둠, 그리고 소용돌이치는 물.\""], ["Then she woke, bright and radiant and we go on our way and soon reach the Pass.", "그러고 나서 그녀는 환하고 빛나는 모습으로 깨어났고, 우리는 길을 이어 곧 고개에 닿았다."], ["At this time and place, she become all on fire with zeal; some new guiding power be in her manifested, for she point to a road and say:--", "이때 이곳에서 그녀는 온통 열의로 불타올랐다. 어떤 새로운 인도의 힘이 그녀 안에서 드러난 듯, 그녀는 한 길을 가리키며 말했다."]], [["“This is the way.”", "\"이쪽이에요.\""]], [["“How know you it?” I ask.", "\"어떻게 아시오?\" 내가 물었다."]], [["“Of course I know it,”", "\"당연히 알지요.\""], ["she answer, and with a pause, add: “Have not my Jonathan travelled it and wrote of his travel?”", "그녀가 대답하고, 잠시 멈추었다가 덧붙였다. \"내 조너선이 이 길을 지나가며 그 여정을 적지 않았던가요?\""]], [["At first I think somewhat strange, but soon I see that there be only one such by-road.", "처음에는 다소 이상하게 여겼으나, 곧 나는 그런 샛길이 하나뿐임을 알았다."], ["It is used but little, and very different from the coach road from the Bukovina to Bistritz, which is more wide and hard, and more of use.", "그 길은 거의 쓰이지 않으며, 더 넓고 단단하고 더 많이 쓰이는 부코비나에서 비스트리차로 가는 역마찻길과는 사뭇 달랐다."]], [["So we came down this road; when we meet other ways--not always were we sure that they were roads at all, for they be neglect and light snow have fallen--the horses know and they only.", "그리하여 우리는 이 길을 따라 내려갔다. 다른 길들을 만나면 — 그것들이 길인지조차 늘 확신할 수는 없었으니, 버려진 데다 옅은 눈이 내려 있었기 때문이다 — 말들만이 알았다."], ["I give rein to them, and they go on so patient. By-and-by we find all the things which Jonathan have note in that wonderful diary of him.", "나는 고삐를 늦추어 그들에게 맡겼고, 그들은 그토록 참을성 있게 나아갔다. 이윽고 우리는 조너선이 그 놀라운 일기에 적어둔 온갖 것들을 찾아냈다."], ["Then we go on for long, long hours and hours.", "그러고 나서 우리는 길고 긴 여러 시간을 계속 나아갔다."], ["At the first, I tell Madam Mina to sleep; she try, and she succeed.", "처음에 나는 미나 부인에게 자라고 일렀고, 그녀는 애써 보더니 성공했다."], ["She sleep all the time; till at the last, I feel myself to suspicious grow, and attempt to wake her.", "그녀는 내내 잤다. 마침내 나는 의심이 일어 그녀를 깨워보려 했다. 그러나 그녀는 계속 잤고, 아무리 해도 나는 그녀를 깨울 수 없었다."], ["But she sleep on, and I may not wake her though I try.", "그녀를 해칠까 두려워 너무 세게 시도하고 싶지는 않았다."], ["I do not wish to try too hard lest I harm her; for I know that she have suffer much, and sleep at times be all-in-all to her.", "그녀가 많은 고통을 겪었음을, 그리고 때로는 잠이 그녀에게 더없이 소중함을 알기 때문이다. 나 자신도 깜빡 졸았던 모양이다."], ["I think I drowse myself, for all of sudden I feel guilt, as though I have done something; I find myself bolt up, with the reins in my hand, and the good horses go along jog, jog, just as ever.", "갑자기 무언가 잘못을 저지른 듯한 죄책감이 들었고, 정신을 차려 보니 나는 손에 고삐를 쥔 채 똑바로 앉아 있었고, 착한 말들은 여느 때처럼 터덜터덜 나아가고 있었다."], ["I look down and find Madam Mina still sleep.", "내려다보니 미나 부인은 여전히 잠들어 있었다."], ["It is now not far off sunset time, and over the snow the light of the sun flow in big yellow flood, so that we throw great long shadow on where the mountain rise so steep.", "어느덧 해질 무렵이 멀지 않았는데, 눈 위로 햇빛이 커다란 노란 물결처럼 흘러서, 산이 그토록 가파르게 솟은 곳에 우리가 길고 큰 그림자를 드리웠다."], ["For we are going up, and up; and all is oh! so wild and rocky, as though it were the end of the world.", "우리는 올라가고, 또 올라가고 있었으며, 모든 것이 아, 어찌나 거칠고 바위투성이인지, 마치 세상의 끝인 것만 같았다."]], [["Then I arouse Madam Mina.", "그러고서 나는 미나 부인을 깨웠다."], ["This time she wake with not much trouble, and then I try to put her to hypnotic sleep.", "이번에는 그녀가 별 어려움 없이 깨어났고, 나는 그녀를 최면 잠에 들게 하려 했다."], ["But she sleep not, being as though I were not.", "그러나 그녀는, 마치 내가 거기 없는 듯, 잠들지 않았다."], ["Still I try and try, till all at once I find her and myself in dark; so I look round, and find that the sun have gone down.", "그래도 나는 시도하고 또 시도하다가, 문득 나 자신과 그녀가 어둠 속에 있음을 알았다. 둘러보니 해가 이미 진 것이었다."], ["Madam Mina laugh, and I turn and look at her.", "미나 부인이 웃었고, 나는 돌아서서 그녀를 바라보았다."], ["She is now quite awake, and look so well as I never saw her since that night at Carfax when we first enter the Count’s house.", "그녀는 이제 완전히 깨어 있었으며, 우리가 처음 백작의 집에 들어섰던 그 카팩스의 밤 이래로 내가 본 적 없을 만큼 좋아 보였다."], ["I am amaze, and not at ease then; but she is so bright and tender and thoughtful for me that I forget all fear.", "나는 놀랐고 그래서 마음이 편치 않았다. 그러나 그녀가 나를 향해 그토록 환하고 다정하고 살뜰하니, 나는 모든 두려움을 잊었다."], ["I light a fire, for we have brought supply of wood with us, and she prepare food while I undo the horses and set them, tethered in shelter, to feed.", "나는 불을 피웠다. 우리는 땔감을 챙겨 왔던 것이다. 내가 말들을 풀어 가린 곳에 매어 먹이는 동안 그녀는 음식을 차렸다."], ["Then when I return to the fire she have my supper ready.", "불가로 돌아와 보니 그녀가 내 저녁상을 차려두었다."], ["I go to help her; but she smile, and tell me that she have eat already--that she was so hungry that she would not wait.", "나는 그녀를 도우려 했으나, 그녀는 미소 지으며 자기는 이미 먹었다고, 너무 배가 고파서 기다릴 수 없었다고 했다."], ["I like it not, and I have grave doubts; but I fear to affright her, and so I am silent of it.", "나는 그것이 마음에 들지 않았고 심각한 의심이 일었다. 그러나 그녀를 놀라게 할까 두려워 그 일에 대해 입을 다물었다."], ["She help me and I eat alone; and then we wrap in fur and lie beside the fire, and I tell her to sleep while I watch.", "그녀가 거들어주고 나는 혼자 먹었다. 그러고서 우리는 모피를 두르고 불가에 누웠고, 나는 내가 지킬 테니 자라고 그녀에게 일렀다."], ["But presently I forget all of watching; and when I sudden remember that I watch, I find her lying quiet, but awake, and looking at me with so bright eyes.", "그러나 곧 나는 망보는 일을 까맣게 잊었다. 그러다 문득 내가 지켜야 한다는 것이 떠올라 보니, 그녀는 가만히, 그러나 깨어 누운 채 그토록 빛나는 눈으로 나를 바라보고 있었다."], ["Once, twice more the same occur, and I get much sleep till before morning.", "한 번, 두 번 같은 일이 거듭되었고, 나는 아침이 되기 전까지 꽤 많이 잤다."], ["When I wake I try to hypnotise her; but alas!", "깨어나 나는 그녀에게 최면을 걸어보려 했으나, 아아!"], ["though she shut her eyes obedient, she may not sleep.", "그녀는 순순히 눈을 감았으되 잠들지 못했다."], ["The sun rise up, and up, and up; and then sleep come to her too late, but so heavy that she will not wake.", "해가 떠오르고, 떠오르고, 또 떠올랐다. 그러고 나서야 그녀에게 잠이 찾아왔으나, 너무 늦었고, 또 너무 깊어 깨어나지 않았다."], ["I have to lift her up, and place her sleeping in the carriage when I have harnessed the horses and made all ready.", "나는 말들에 마구를 채우고 모든 채비를 마친 뒤, 그녀를 들어 올려 잠든 채로 마차에 눕혀야 했다."], ["Madam still sleep, and she look in her sleep more healthy and more redder than before. And I like it not.", "부인은 여전히 잠들어 있었고, 잠결에 전보다 더 건강하고 더 혈색이 좋아 보였다. 그리고 나는 그것이 마음에 들지 않았다."], ["And I am afraid, afraid, afraid!--I am afraid of all things--even to think but I must go on my way.", "그리고 나는 두렵다, 두렵다, 두렵다! 나는 온갖 것이 두렵다. 생각하는 것조차 두렵지만, 나는 내 길을 가야만 한다."], ["The stake we play for is life and death, or more than these, and we must not flinch.", "우리가 건 판돈은 삶과 죽음, 아니 그 이상의 것이니, 우리는 물러서서는 안 된다."]], [["5 November, morning.--Let me be accurate in everything, for though you and I have seen some strange things together, you may at the first think that I, Van Helsing, am mad--that the many horrors and the so long strain on nerves has at the last turn my brain.", "11월 5일, 아침. — 모든 것을 정확히 적어두자. 그대와 내가 함께 기이한 일들을 더러 보았다 해도, 그대는 처음에는 나, 판 헬싱이 미쳤다고 — 그 숱한 공포와 그토록 오래 이어진 신경의 긴장이 마침내 내 머리를 돌게 했다고 — 여길지도 모르니."]], [["All yesterday we travel, ever getting closer to the mountains, and moving into a more and more wild and desert land.", "어제 온종일 우리는 점점 더 산에 가까워지며, 점점 더 거칠고 황량한 땅으로 들어갔다."], ["There are great, frowning precipices and much falling water, and Nature seem to have held sometime her carnival.", "거대하고 험상궂은 절벽들과 쏟아지는 물이 많았고, 자연이 한때 광란의 잔치라도 벌인 듯했다."], ["Madam Mina still sleep and sleep; and though I did have hunger and appeased it, I could not waken her--even for food.", "미나 부인은 여전히 자고 또 잤다. 나는 배가 고파 그것을 채웠으나, 음식을 위해서조차 그녀를 깨울 수 없었다."], ["I began to fear that the fatal spell of the place was upon her, tainted as she is with that Vampire baptism.", "나는 그 흡혈귀의 세례로 더럽혀진 그녀에게 이 땅의 치명적인 마력이 깃든 것은 아닐까 두려워지기 시작했다. \"그래,\""], ["“Well,” said I to myself, “if it be that she sleep all the day, it shall also be that I do not sleep at night.”", "하고 나는 혼잣말했다. \"그녀가 온종일 잔다면, 나는 밤에 자지 않으면 될 일이다.\""], ["As we travel on the rough road, for a road of an ancient and imperfect kind there was, I held down my head and slept.", "우리가 험한 길을 — 오래되고 변변치 못한 종류의 길이나마 길이 있었으니 — 따라 가는 동안, 나는 고개를 떨군 채 잠들었다."], ["Again I waked with a sense of guilt and of time passed, and found Madam Mina still sleeping, and the sun low down.", "또다시 나는 죄책감과 시간이 흘렀다는 느낌과 함께 깨어났고, 미나 부인은 여전히 잠들어 있고 해는 낮게 내려와 있었다."], ["But all was indeed changed; the frowning mountains seemed further away, and we were near the top of a steep-rising hill, on summit of which was such a castle as Jonathan tell of in his diary.", "그러나 모든 것이 정말로 달라져 있었다. 험상궂은 산들은 더 멀어진 듯했고, 우리는 가파르게 솟은 언덕 꼭대기 가까이에 있었는데, 그 정상에는 조너선이 일기에 적은 바로 그런 성이 서 있었다."], ["At once I exulted and feared; for now, for good or ill, the end was near.", "나는 동시에 환호하고 또 두려워했다. 이제, 좋든 나쁘든, 끝이 가까웠기 때문이다."]], [["I woke Madam Mina, and again tried to hypnotise her; but alas!", "나는 미나 부인을 깨우고 다시 최면을 걸어보려 했으나, 아아!"], ["unavailing till too late.", "너무 늦을 때까지 소용이 없었다."], ["Then, ere the great dark came upon us--for even after down-sun the heavens reflected the gone sun on the snow, and all was for a time in a great twilight--I took out the horses and fed them in what shelter I could.", "그러고서, 우리에게 큰 어둠이 내리기 전에 — 해가 진 뒤에도 하늘이 사라진 해를 눈 위에 반사하여 한동안 모든 것이 거대한 황혼에 잠겼으므로 — 나는 말들을 풀어 그나마 가릴 만한 곳에서 먹였다."], ["Then I make a fire; and near it I make Madam Mina, now awake and more charming than ever, sit comfortable amid her rugs.", "그러고 나서 불을 피우고, 그 곁에 이제 깨어나 어느 때보다 매혹적인 미나 부인을 담요에 둘러싸여 편안히 앉게 했다."], ["I got ready food: but she would not eat, simply saying that she had not hunger.", "나는 음식을 차렸으나, 그녀는 그저 배가 고프지 않다며 먹으려 하지 않았다."], ["I did not press her, knowing her unavailingness.", "나는 그녀를 억지로 권하지 않았으니, 소용없음을 알았기 때문이다."], ["But I myself eat, for I must needs now be strong for all.", "그러나 나 자신은 먹었다. 이제 모든 일을 위해 강해져야만 했기 때문이다."], ["Then, with the fear on me of what might be, I drew a ring so big for her comfort, round where Madam Mina sat; and over the ring I passed some of the wafer, and I broke it fine so that all was well guarded.", "그런 다음, 무슨 일이 일어날지 모른다는 두려움에, 나는 미나 부인이 앉은 자리 둘레로 그녀가 편히 있을 만큼 큰 원을 그렸다. 그리고 그 원 위로 성체병(聖體餠) 얼마를 뿌리고, 잘게 부수어 모든 곳이 빈틈없이 지켜지게 했다."], ["She sat still all the time--so still as one dead; and she grew whiter and ever whiter till the snow was not more pale; and no word she said.", "그녀는 내내 가만히 앉아 있었다. 죽은 사람처럼 그토록 가만히. 그리고 점점 더 하얘져서 눈보다 더 창백해졌고, 한마디도 하지 않았다."], ["But when I drew near, she clung to me, and I could know that the poor soul shook her from head to feet with a tremor that was pain to feel.", "그러나 내가 가까이 가자 그녀는 나에게 매달렸고, 나는 그 가엾은 영혼이 머리끝에서 발끝까지 떨고 있음을, 만지기에도 고통스러운 떨림으로 떨고 있음을 알 수 있었다."], ["I said to her presently, when she had grown more quiet:--", "그녀가 좀 더 잠잠해졌을 때, 나는 이윽고 말했다."]], [["“Will you not come over to the fire?” for I wished to make a test of what she could.", "\"불가로 건너오지 않겠소?\" 그녀가 무엇을 할 수 있는지 시험해보고 싶었던 것이다."], ["She rose obedient, but when she have made a step she stopped, and stood as one stricken.", "그녀는 순순히 일어섰으나, 한 걸음을 떼고는 멈추어, 벼락 맞은 사람처럼 서 있었다."]], [["“Why not go on?” I asked.", "\"왜 더 나아가지 않소?\""], ["She shook her head, and, coming back, sat down in her place.", "내가 물었다. 그녀는 고개를 젓고는 되돌아와 제자리에 앉았다."], ["Then, looking at me with open eyes, as of one waked from sleep, she said simply:--", "그러고서 잠에서 깨어난 사람처럼 눈을 크게 뜨고 나를 바라보며, 그저 이렇게 말했다."]], [["“I cannot!”", "\"못 가겠어요!\""], ["and remained silent.", "그러고는 입을 다물었다."], ["I rejoiced, for I knew that what she could not, none of those that we dreaded could.", "나는 기뻤다. 그녀가 할 수 없는 일이라면, 우리가 두려워하는 그 어떤 것도 할 수 없음을 알았기 때문이다."], ["Though there might be danger to her body, yet her soul was safe!", "비록 그녀의 몸에는 위험이 있을지 몰라도, 그녀의 영혼은 안전했던 것이다!"]], [["Presently the horses began to scream, and tore at their tethers till I came to them and quieted them.", "이윽고 말들이 비명을 지르기 시작하더니, 내가 가서 달랠 때까지 매어둔 줄을 잡아챘다."], ["When they did feel my hands on them, they whinnied low as in joy, and licked at my hands and were quiet for a time.", "내 손이 자기들 몸에 닿자 그들은 기쁜 듯 낮게 울며 내 손을 핥았고, 한동안 잠잠해졌다."], ["Many times through the night did I come to them, till it arrive to the cold hour when all nature is at lowest; and every time my coming was with quiet of them.", "밤새 나는 여러 번 그들에게 갔고, 마침내 온 자연이 가장 가라앉는 추운 시각에 이르렀다. 그리고 내가 갈 때마다 그들은 잠잠해졌다."], ["In the cold hour the fire began to die, and I was about stepping forth to replenish it, for now the snow came in flying sweeps and with it a chill mist.", "그 추운 시각에 불이 사위어가기 시작했고, 나는 그것을 돋우려 막 걸음을 내디디려던 참이었다. 이제 눈이 휘몰아치며 흩날렸고, 그와 함께 차가운 안개가 몰려왔다."], ["Even in the dark there was a light of some kind, as there ever is over snow; and it seemed as though the snow-flurries and the wreaths of mist took shape as of women with trailing garments. All was in dead, grim silence only that the horses whinnied and cowered, as if in terror of the worst.", "어둠 속에서도 눈 위에는 늘 그렇듯 어떤 빛이 감돌았고, 마치 눈보라와 안개의 자락이 옷자락을 끄는 여인들의 형상을 띠는 듯했다. 모든 것이 죽은 듯, 음산한 침묵에 잠겨 있었는데, 다만 말들이 최악의 무엇을 두려워하듯 울며 움츠릴 뿐이었다."], ["I began to fear--horrible fears; but then came to me the sense of safety in that ring wherein I stood.", "나는 두려워지기 시작했다. 끔찍한 두려움이었다. 그러나 그때 내가 서 있는 그 원 안에서 안전하다는 느낌이 찾아왔다."], ["I began, too, to think that my imaginings were of the night, and the gloom, and the unrest that I have gone through, and all the terrible anxiety.", "또한 나는 이 모든 상상이 밤과 어둠과, 내가 겪어온 불안과 그 모든 무서운 근심 탓이라고 생각하기 시작했다."], ["It was as though my memories of all Jonathan’s horrid experience were befooling me; for the snow flakes and the mist began to wheel and circle round, till I could get as though a shadowy glimpse of those women that would have kissed him.", "마치 조너선의 그 모든 끔찍한 경험에 대한 기억이 나를 농락하는 듯했다. 눈송이와 안개가 빙빙 돌며 맴돌기 시작하더니, 마침내 나는 그를 입맞추려 했던 그 여인들의 어렴풋한 모습을 흘끗 보는 듯했다."], ["And then the horses cowered lower and lower, and moaned in terror as men do in pain.", "그러자 말들은 점점 더 낮게 움츠리며, 고통에 빠진 사람처럼 두려움에 신음했다."], ["Even the madness of fright was not to them, so that they could break away.", "그들에게는 떨치고 달아날 만한 광란의 공포조차 없었다."], ["I feared for my dear Madam Mina when these weird figures drew near and circled round.", "이 기괴한 형체들이 다가와 맴돌 때 나는 사랑하는 미나 부인이 두려웠다."], ["I looked at her, but she sat calm, and smiled at me; when I would have stepped to the fire to replenish it, she caught me and held me back, and whispered, like a voice that one hears in a dream, so low it was:--", "그녀를 바라보았으나, 그녀는 차분히 앉아 나를 향해 미소 지었다. 내가 불을 돋우려 그쪽으로 걸음을 떼려 하자, 그녀는 나를 붙들어 만류하며, 마치 꿈결에 듣는 목소리처럼, 그토록 낮게 속삭였다."]], [["“No! No! Do not go without.", "\"안 돼요! 안 돼요! 밖으로 나가지 마세요."], ["Here you are safe!”", "여기 있어야 안전해요!\""], ["I turned to her, and looking in her eyes, said:--", "나는 그녀에게로 돌아서서, 그 눈을 들여다보며 말했다."]], [["“But you?", "\"하지만 당신은?"], ["It is for you that I fear!”", "내가 두려운 것은 바로 당신이오!\""], ["whereat she laughed--a laugh, low and unreal, and said:--", "그러자 그녀가 웃었다. 낮고 비현실적인 웃음이었다. 그리고 말했다."]], [["“Fear for me! Why fear for me?", "\"나를 위해 두려워한다고요! 왜 나를 위해 두려워하세요?"], ["None safer in all the world from them than I am,”", "이 세상 누구도 나만큼 그들로부터 안전하지 않은걸요.\""], ["and as I wondered at the meaning of her words, a puff of wind made the flame leap up, and I see the red scar on her forehead.", "그 말의 뜻을 내가 의아해하는 동안, 한 줄기 바람이 불꽃을 솟구치게 했고, 나는 그녀 이마의 붉은 흉터를 보았다. 그제야, 아아!"], ["Then, alas!", "나는 알았다."], ["I knew. Did I not, I would soon have learned, for the wheeling figures of mist and snow came closer, but keeping ever without the Holy circle.", "설령 몰랐더라도 곧 알게 되었을 것이니, 안개와 눈으로 맴돌던 형체들이 더 가까이 다가왔으나, 언제나 그 신성한 원 밖에 머물렀기 때문이다."], ["Then they began to materialise till--if God have not take away my reason, for I saw it through my eyes--there were before me in actual flesh the same three women that Jonathan saw in the room, when they would have kissed his throat.", "그러고서 그들은 형체를 갖추기 시작하더니, 마침내 — 신께서 내 이성을 거두어가지 않으셨다면, 나는 내 두 눈으로 그것을 보았으므로 — 조너선이 방에서 보았던, 그의 목에 입맞추려 했던 바로 그 세 여인이 실제 살을 갖추고 내 앞에 있었다."], ["I knew the swaying round forms, the bright hard eyes, the white teeth, the ruddy colour, the voluptuous lips.", "나는 알아보았다. 흔들리는 풍만한 자태, 빛나는 단단한 눈, 흰 이, 발그레한 빛깔, 관능적인 입술을."], ["They smiled ever at poor dear Madam Mina; and as their laugh came through the silence of the night, they twined their arms and pointed to her, and said in those so sweet tingling tones that Jonathan said were of the intolerable sweetness of the water-glasses:--", "그들은 줄곧 가엾고 사랑하는 미나 부인을 향해 미소 지었다. 그리고 그들의 웃음이 밤의 침묵을 뚫고 들려올 때, 그들은 서로 팔을 휘감고 그녀를 가리키며, 조너선이 유리잔의 견딜 수 없는 감미로움 같다고 했던 그 달콤하고 짜릿한 목소리로 말했다."]], [["“Come, sister.", "\"이리 와, 자매여."], ["Come to us. Come! Come!”", "우리에게 와. 와! 와!\""], ["In fear I turned to my poor Madam Mina, and my heart with gladness leapt like flame; for oh!", "두려움에 나는 가엾은 미나 부인에게로 돌아섰고, 내 가슴은 기쁨에 불꽃처럼 솟구쳤다. 아!"], ["the terror in her sweet eyes, the repulsion, the horror, told a story to my heart that was all of hope.", "그 고운 눈에 어린 공포, 그 혐오, 그 전율이 내 가슴에 온통 희망뿐인 이야기를 들려주었기 때문이다."], ["God be thanked she was not, yet, of them. I seized some of the firewood which was by me, and holding out some of the Wafer, advanced on them towards the fire.", "신께 감사하게도, 그녀는 아직 그들 가운데 하나가 아니었다. 나는 곁에 있던 땔감 얼마를 움켜쥐고, 성체병 얼마를 내밀며, 불을 향해 그들 쪽으로 나아갔다."], ["They drew back before me, and laughed their low horrid laugh.", "그들은 내 앞에서 물러서며, 낮고 끔찍한 웃음을 웃었다."], ["I fed the fire, and feared them not; for I knew that we were safe within our protections.", "나는 불을 돋우었고, 그들이 두렵지 않았다. 우리가 우리의 방비 안에서 안전함을 알았기 때문이다."], ["They could not approach, me, whilst so armed, nor Madam Mina whilst she remained within the ring, which she could not leave no more than they could enter.", "그들은 내가 그렇게 무장하고 있는 한 나에게 다가올 수 없었고, 미나 부인이 원 안에 머무는 한 그녀에게도 다가올 수 없었다. 그녀가 원을 벗어날 수 없는 만큼이나 그들도 들어올 수 없었던 것이다."], ["The horses had ceased to moan, and lay still on the ground; the snow fell on them softly, and they grew whiter.", "말들은 신음을 그치고 땅에 가만히 누워 있었다. 눈이 그들 위로 부드럽게 내려 점점 하얘졌다."], ["I knew that there was for the poor beasts no more of terror.", "나는 그 가엾은 짐승들에게는 이제 더는 공포가 없음을 알았다."]], [["And so we remained till the red of the dawn to fall through the snow-gloom.", "그렇게 우리는 새벽의 붉은빛이 눈 어린 어둠을 뚫고 내릴 때까지 머물렀다."], ["I was desolate and afraid, and full of woe and terror; but when that beautiful sun began to climb the horizon life was to me again.", "나는 황량하고 두려웠으며, 비탄과 공포로 가득했다. 그러나 그 아름다운 해가 지평선을 오르기 시작하자 나에게 다시 생명이 돌아왔다."], ["At the first coming of the dawn the horrid figures melted in the whirling mist and snow; the wreaths of transparent gloom moved away towards the castle, and were lost.", "새벽이 처음 밝아올 때, 그 끔찍한 형체들은 소용돌이치는 안개와 눈 속으로 녹아들었다. 투명한 어둠의 자락들이 성 쪽으로 물러가더니 사라졌다."]], [["Instinctively, with the dawn coming, I turned to Madam Mina, intending to hypnotise her; but she lay in a deep and sudden sleep, from which I could not wake her.", "새벽이 다가오자 나는 본능적으로 미나 부인에게 돌아서서 그녀에게 최면을 걸려 했다. 그러나 그녀는 깊고 갑작스러운 잠에 빠져 있어, 나는 그녀를 깨울 수 없었다."], ["I tried to hypnotise through her sleep, but she made no response, none at all; and the day broke.", "잠든 채로나마 최면을 걸어보려 했으나, 그녀는 아무런 반응도, 조금도 보이지 않았다. 그리고 날이 밝았다."], ["I fear yet to stir. I have made my fire and have seen the horses, they are all dead.", "나는 아직 움직이기가 두렵다. 나는 불을 피우고 말들을 살펴보았는데, 모두 죽어 있었다."], ["To-day I have much to do here, and I keep waiting till the sun is up high; for there may be places where I must go, where that sunlight, though snow and mist obscure it, will be to me a safety.", "오늘 나는 이곳에서 할 일이 많고, 해가 높이 뜰 때까지 기다리고 있다. 내가 가야 할 곳들 가운데에는, 눈과 안개가 가린다 해도 그 햇빛이 나에게 안전이 되어줄 곳이 있을지도 모르기 때문이다."]], [["I will strengthen me with breakfast, and then I will to my terrible work.", "나는 아침을 들어 기운을 차린 뒤, 나의 끔찍한 일에 나설 것이다."], ["Madam Mina still sleeps; and, God be thanked!", "미나 부인은 아직 잠들어 있다. 그리고, 신께 감사하게도!"], ["she is calm in her sleep....", "그녀는 잠결에 평온하다……"]], [["Jonathan Harker’s Journal.", "「조너선 하커의 일기」"]], [["4 November, evening.--The accident to the launch has been a terrible thing for us.", "11월 4일, 저녁. — 증기선의 사고는 우리에게 끔찍한 일이었다."], ["Only for it we should have overtaken the boat long ago; and by now my dear Mina would have been free.", "그것만 아니었다면 우리는 진작 그 배를 따라잡았을 것이고, 지금쯤 사랑하는 미나는 자유로워졌을 것이다."], ["I fear to think of her, off on the wolds near that horrid place.", "그 끔찍한 곳 근처 황야에 나가 있을 그녀를 생각하기가 두렵다. 우리는 말을 구했고, 그 자취를 따라간다."], ["We have got horses, and we follow on the track.", "고댈밍이 채비를 하는 동안 이것을 적어둔다."], ["I note this whilst Godalming is getting ready. We have our arms.", "우리에게는 무기가 있다. 슈가니들이 싸울 작정이라면 조심해야 할 것이다."], ["The Szgany must look out if they mean fight.", "아, 모리스와 슈어드만 우리와 함께 있다면."], ["Oh, if only Morris and Seward were with us. We must only hope!", "우리는 그저 희망을 품을 수밖에! 더 적지 못한다면, 잘 있어요, 미나!"], ["If I write no more Good-bye, Mina! God bless and keep you.", "신께서 그대를 축복하고 지켜주시기를. * 슈가니: 트란실바니아 지방의 집시 무리."]], [["Dr. Seward’s Diary.", "「슈어드 박사의 일기」"]], [["5 November.--With the dawn we saw the body of Szgany before us dashing away from the river with their leiter-wagon.", "11월 5일. — 동틀 녘에 우리는 앞쪽에서 슈가니 무리가 짐수레를 끌고 강에서 부리나케 달아나는 것을 보았다."], ["They surrounded it in a cluster, and hurried along as though beset.", "그들은 수레를 무리 지어 에워싸고는, 마치 쫓기는 듯 서둘러 갔다."], ["The snow is falling lightly and there is a strange excitement in the air.", "눈이 가볍게 내리고 있고, 공기 속에 묘한 흥분이 감돈다."], ["It may be our own feelings, but the depression is strange.", "우리 자신의 감정 탓일지도 모르나, 그 침울함은 기이하다."], ["Far off I hear the howling of wolves; the snow brings them down from the mountains, and there are dangers to all of us, and from all sides.", "멀리서 늑대들의 울부짖음이 들린다. 눈이 그들을 산에서 내려오게 하니, 우리 모두에게, 그리고 사방에서 위험이 도사린다."], ["The horses are nearly ready, and we are soon off.", "말들이 거의 준비되었고, 우리는 곧 떠난다."], ["We ride to death of some one.", "우리는 누군가의 죽음을 향해 달려간다."], ["God alone knows who, or where, or what, or when, or how it may be....", "그것이 누구일지, 어디일지, 무엇일지, 언제일지, 어떻게일지는 오직 신만이 아신다……"]], [["Dr. Van Helsing’s Memorandum.", "「판 헬싱 박사의 비망록」"]], [["5 November, afternoon.--I am at least sane.", "11월 5일, 오후. — 적어도 나는 제정신이다."], ["Thank God for that mercy at all events, though the proving it has been dreadful.", "그것을 증명하는 일이 끔찍하기는 했으나, 어쨌든 그 자비에 신께 감사한다."], ["When I left Madam Mina sleeping within the Holy circle, I took my way to the castle.", "미나 부인을 신성한 원 안에 잠들게 두고, 나는 성으로 향했다."], ["The blacksmith hammer which I took in the carriage from Veresti was useful; though the doors were all open I broke them off the rusty hinges, lest some ill-intent or ill-chance should close them, so that being entered I might not get out.", "베레슈티에서 마차에 실어 온 대장간 망치가 쓸모 있었다. 문은 모두 열려 있었으나, 어떤 악의나 불운이 그것을 닫아 내가 일단 들어간 뒤 나오지 못하게 될까 봐, 나는 녹슨 돌쩌귀에서 문짝을 떼어냈다."], ["Jonathan’s bitter experience served me here.", "조너선의 쓰라린 경험이 여기서 나에게 도움이 되었다."], ["By memory of his diary I found my way to the old chapel, for I knew that here my work lay.", "그의 일기를 기억하여 나는 옛 예배당으로 가는 길을 찾았다. 내 일이 그곳에 있음을 알았기 때문이다."], ["The air was oppressive; it seemed as if there was some sulphurous fume, which at times made me dizzy.", "공기는 숨막혔다. 무슨 유황 연기 같은 것이 있어, 때때로 나를 어지럽게 했다."], ["Either there was a roaring in my ears or I heard afar off the howl of wolves.", "내 귀에 무언가 울려대는 것인지, 아니면 멀리서 늑대들의 울부짖음이 들리는 것인지 알 수 없었다."], ["Then I bethought me of my dear Madam Mina, and I was in terrible plight.", "그때 나는 사랑하는 미나 부인이 떠올랐고, 끔찍한 곤경에 빠졌다."], ["The dilemma had me between his horns.", "진퇴양난이 나를 그 두 뿔 사이에 끼워두고 있었다."]], [["Her, I had not dare to take into this place, but left safe from the Vampire in that Holy circle; and yet even there would be the wolf!", "그녀를, 나는 감히 이 안으로 데려올 수 없어, 그 신성한 원 안에 흡혈귀로부터 안전하게 남겨두었다. 그러나 그곳에도 늑대는 있을 터였다!"], ["I resolve me that my work lay here, and that as to the wolves we must submit, if it were God’s will.", "나는 내 일이 여기 있으며, 늑대에 관해서는 그것이 신의 뜻이라면 따를 수밖에 없다고 마음을 다잡았다."], ["At any rate it was only death and freedom beyond. So did I choose for her.", "어쨌든 그 너머에 있는 것은 죽음과 자유뿐이었다. 그래서 나는 그녀를 위해 그렇게 택했다."], ["Had it but been for myself the choice had been easy, the maw of the wolf were better to rest in than the grave of the Vampire!", "그것이 나 자신을 위한 선택이었다면 쉬웠을 것이다. 흡혈귀의 무덤에 누워 있느니 늑대의 아가리 속에 쉬는 편이 나았을 테니!"], ["So I make my choice to go on with my work.", "그리하여 나는 내 일을 계속하기로 택했다."]], [["I knew that there were at least three graves to find--graves that are inhabit; so I search, and search, and I find one of them.", "나는 찾아야 할 무덤이 적어도 셋임을 알았다. 깃들어 있는 무덤들이었다. 그래서 나는 찾고, 또 찾아, 그중 하나를 찾아냈다."], ["She lay in her Vampire sleep, so full of life and voluptuous beauty that I shudder as though I have come to do murder.", "그녀는 흡혈귀의 잠 속에 누워 있었는데, 생명과 관능적인 아름다움이 어찌나 충만하던지, 나는 마치 살인을 저지르러 온 듯 몸서리쳤다."], ["Ah, I doubt not that in old time, when such things were, many a man who set forth to do such a task as mine, found at the last his heart fail him, and then his nerve.", "아, 의심할 바 없이 옛날, 이런 일들이 있던 시절에, 나와 같은 일을 하러 나선 많은 사내가 끝내 마음이 약해지고 이어 용기마저 꺾였으리라."], ["So he delay, and delay, and delay, till the mere beauty and the fascination of the wanton Un-Dead have hypnotise him; and he remain on and on, till sunset come, and the Vampire sleep be over.", "그래서 그는 미루고, 미루고, 또 미루다가, 마침내 그 음란한 언데드의 순전한 아름다움과 매혹에 최면이 걸리고 만다. 그리고 그는 머물고 또 머물러, 마침내 해가 지고 흡혈귀의 잠이 끝난다."], ["Then the beautiful eyes of the fair woman open and look love, and the voluptuous mouth present to a kiss--and man is weak.", "그러면 그 아름다운 여인의 고운 눈이 떠져 사랑을 담아 바라보고, 관능적인 입이 입맞춤을 내밀고 — 사내는 약하다."], ["And there remain one more victim in the Vampire fold; one more to swell the grim and grisly ranks of the Un-Dead!...", "그리하여 흡혈귀의 무리에 희생자가 하나 더 남게 된다. 음산하고 소름 끼치는 언데드의 대열을 불릴 자가 하나 더!……"]], [["There is some fascination, surely, when I am moved by the mere presence of such an one, even lying as she lay in a tomb fretted with age and heavy with the dust of centuries, though there be that horrid odour such as the lairs of the Count have had.", "분명 무슨 매혹이 있다. 나는 그저 그런 존재가 곁에 있다는 것만으로도 마음이 흔들렸으니, 비록 백작의 소굴에서 나던 그 끔찍한 냄새가 감돌고, 세월에 닳고 수백 년의 먼지가 무겁게 내려앉은 무덤 속에 누워 있는 모습이었음에도."], ["Yes, I was moved--I, Van Helsing, with all my purpose and with my motive for hate--I was moved to a yearning for delay which seemed to paralyse my faculties and to clog my very soul.", "그렇다, 나는 흔들렸다. 온 목적과 증오의 동기를 품은 나, 판 헬싱이 — 나는 지체하고픈 갈망에 흔들렸으니, 그것이 내 능력을 마비시키고 내 영혼 자체를 무겁게 막는 듯했다."], ["It may have been that the need of natural sleep, and the strange oppression of the air were beginning to overcome me.", "어쩌면 자연스러운 잠에 대한 욕구와 그 기이하게 숨막히는 공기가 나를 압도하기 시작한 것이었는지도 모른다."], ["Certain it was that I was lapsing into sleep, the open-eyed sleep of one who yields to a sweet fascination, when there came through the snow-stilled air a long, low wail, so full of woe and pity that it woke me like the sound of a clarion.", "분명한 것은 내가 잠으로 빠져들고 있었다는 것이니, 달콤한 매혹에 굴복하는 자의 눈뜬 잠이었다. 그때 눈에 잦아든 공기를 뚫고 길고 낮은 통곡이 들려왔는데, 어찌나 비탄과 연민으로 가득했던지 나팔 소리처럼 나를 깨웠다."], ["For it was the voice of my dear Madam Mina that I heard.", "그것은 내가 들은 사랑하는 미나 부인의 목소리였기 때문이다."]], [["Then I braced myself again to my horrid task, and found by wrenching away tomb-tops one other of the sisters, the other dark one.", "그러고서 나는 다시 끔찍한 일에 마음을 다잡고, 무덤의 뚜껑들을 비틀어 떼어내어 자매들 가운데 또 하나, 다른 검은 머리의 여인을 찾아냈다."], ["I dared not pause to look on her as I had on her sister, lest once more I should begin to be enthrall; but I go on searching until, presently, I find in a high great tomb as if made to one much beloved that other fair sister which, like Jonathan I had seen to gather herself out of the atoms of the mist.", "다시금 매혹에 사로잡히기 시작할까 두려워, 나는 앞서 그 자매에게 그랬듯 멈추어 그녀를 바라보지는 못했다. 그러나 나는 계속 찾아, 이윽고 무척 사랑받던 이를 위해 만든 듯한 높고 거대한 무덤에서, 조너선처럼 나도 안개의 미립자들로부터 몸을 추스르는 것을 보았던 또 한 명의 고운 자매를 찾아냈다."], ["She was so fair to look on, so radiantly beautiful, so exquisitely voluptuous, that the very instinct of man in me, which calls some of my sex to love and to protect one of hers, made my head whirl with new emotion.", "그녀는 보기에 어찌나 아름답고, 어찌나 눈부시게 곱고, 어찌나 절묘하게 관능적이던지, 내 안에 깃든, 같은 남자들 가운데 더러를 여자를 사랑하고 지키도록 부르는 그 사내로서의 본능이 새로운 감정으로 내 머리를 어지럽게 했다."], ["But God be thanked, that soul-wail of my dear Madam Mina had not died out of my ears; and, before the spell could be wrought further upon me, I had nerved myself to my wild work.", "그러나 신께 감사하게도, 사랑하는 미나 부인의 그 영혼의 통곡이 아직 내 귀에서 사라지지 않았다. 그래서 그 마력이 나에게 더 깊이 미치기 전에, 나는 나의 거친 일에 마음을 다잡았다."], ["By this time I had searched all the tombs in the chapel, so far as I could tell; and as there had been only three of these Un-Dead phantoms around us in the night, I took it that there were no more of active Un-Dead existent.", "이때쯤 나는 내가 아는 한 예배당의 무덤을 모두 뒤졌다. 그리고 밤중에 우리 둘레에 있던 이 언데드 망령은 셋뿐이었으므로, 나는 더 활동하는 언데드는 없으리라 여겼다. 나머지 모든 것보다 더 위엄 있는 거대한 무덤이 하나 더 있었다."], ["There was one great tomb more lordly than all the rest; huge it was, and nobly proportioned. On it was but one word", "그것은 거대하고 고귀하게 균형이 잡혀 있었다. 그 위에는 단 한마디가 새겨져 있었다."]], [["This then was the Un-Dead home of the King-Vampire, to whom so many more were due.", "드라큘라. 그러니 이것이 그토록 많은 자를 거느린 흡혈귀 왕의 언데드 거처였다."], ["Its emptiness spoke eloquent to make certain what I knew.", "그 비어 있음이, 내가 알던 바를 확실히 하라고 웅변하듯 말해주었다."], ["Before I began to restore these women to their dead selves through my awful work, I laid in Dracula’s tomb some of the Wafer, and so banished him from it, Un-Dead, for ever.", "내 무서운 일로 이 여인들을 죽은 본래의 모습으로 되돌리기에 앞서, 나는 드라큘라의 무덤에 성체병 얼마를 놓아두어, 언데드인 그를 그곳에서 영원히 내쫓았다."]], [["Then began my terrible task, and I dreaded it.", "그러고서 나의 끔찍한 일이 시작되었고, 나는 그것을 두려워했다."], ["Had it been but one, it had been easy, comparative. But three!", "하나뿐이었다면 비교적 쉬웠을 것이다. 그러나 셋이라니!"], ["To begin twice more after I had been through a deed of horror; for if it was terrible with the sweet Miss Lucy, what would it not be with these strange ones who had survived through centuries, and who had been strengthened by the passing of the years; who would, if they could, have fought for their foul lives....", "공포의 행위를 한 차례 겪은 뒤에 두 번을 더 시작해야 하다니. 사랑스러운 루시 양에게도 그토록 끔찍했거늘, 수백 년을 살아남아 세월이 흐르며 힘을 더한, 할 수만 있다면 그 더러운 목숨을 위해 싸웠을 이 기이한 존재들에게는 오죽하겠는가……"]], [["Oh, my friend John, but it was butcher work; had I not been nerved by thoughts of other dead, and of the living over whom hung such a pall of fear, I could not have gone on.", "아, 내 벗 존이여, 그것은 도살의 일이었네. 다른 죽은 이들과, 그토록 두려움의 장막이 드리운 산 자들에 대한 생각으로 마음을 다잡지 않았더라면 나는 계속할 수 없었을 걸세."], ["I tremble and tremble even yet, though till all was over, God be thanked, my nerve did stand.", "모든 것이 끝날 때까지, 신께 감사하게도 내 담력은 버텨주었으나, 지금도 나는 떨고 또 떨고 있다네."], ["Had I not seen the repose in the first place, and the gladness that stole over it just ere the final dissolution came, as realisation that the soul had been won, I could not have gone further with my butchery.", "첫 번째에서 그 안식과, 영혼이 구원받았다는 깨달음이듯 최후의 소멸이 오기 직전에 그 위로 슬며시 번지던 그 기쁨을 보지 못했더라면, 나는 나의 도살을 더는 이어가지 못했을 걸세."], ["I could not have endured the horrid screeching as the stake drove home; the plunging of writhing form, and lips of bloody foam.", "말뚝이 박혀 들어갈 때의 그 끔찍한 비명, 몸부림치는 형체의 요동침, 피거품 이는 입술을 견뎌내지 못했을 걸세."], ["I should have fled in terror and left my work undone.", "나는 공포에 질려 달아나 내 일을 끝맺지 못한 채 두었을 걸세."], ["But it is over!", "그러나 끝났네!"], ["And the poor souls, I can pity them now and weep, as I think of them placid each in her full sleep of death for a short moment ere fading.", "그리고 그 가엾은 영혼들을, 이제 나는 가여워하며 눈물 흘릴 수 있다네. 그들이 사라지기 직전 짧은 한순간, 저마다 죽음의 온전한 잠 속에 평온히 누워 있던 모습을 떠올리노라면."], ["For, friend John, hardly had my knife severed the head of each, before the whole body began to melt away and crumble in to its native dust, as though the death that should have come centuries agone had at last assert himself and say at once and loud “I am here!”", "벗 존이여, 내 칼이 저마다의 머리를 베어내자마자, 온몸이 녹아내려 본래의 흙으로 부서지기 시작했으니, 마치 수백 년 전에 왔어야 할 죽음이 마침내 자기를 내세워 단번에 큰 소리로 \"내가 여기 있노라!\"라고 외치는 듯했네."]], [["Before I left the castle I so fixed its entrances that never more can the Count enter there Un-Dead.", "성을 떠나기 전에 나는 그 입구들을 단단히 막아, 언데드인 백작이 다시는 그곳에 들어갈 수 없게 했다."]], [["When I stepped into the circle where Madam Mina slept, she woke from her sleep, and, seeing, me, cried out in pain that I had endured too much.", "내가 미나 부인이 잠들어 있는 원 안으로 들어서자, 그녀는 잠에서 깨어났고, 나를 보더니 내가 너무 많은 것을 견뎌냈다며 고통스럽게 부르짖었다."]], [["“Come!” she said, “come away from this awful place!", "\"이리 오세요!\" 그녀가 말했다."], ["Let us go to meet my husband who is, I know, coming towards us.”", "\"이 무서운 곳에서 떠나요! 우리에게 다가오고 있는, 나는 알아요, 내 남편을 맞으러 가요.\""], ["She was looking thin and pale and weak; but her eyes were pure and glowed with fervour.", "그녀는 야위고 창백하고 약해 보였다. 그러나 그 눈은 맑았고 열정으로 빛났다."], ["I was glad to see her paleness and her illness, for my mind was full of the fresh horror of that ruddy vampire sleep.", "나는 그녀의 창백함과 병약함이 반가웠다. 내 마음이 그 발그레한 흡혈귀의 잠에 대한 생생한 공포로 가득했기 때문이다."]], [["And so with trust and hope, and yet full of fear, we go eastward to meet our friends--and him--whom Madam Mina tell me that she know are coming to meet us.", "그리하여 우리는 믿음과 희망을 품고, 그러나 여전히 두려움으로 가득한 채, 우리의 벗들을 — 그리고 미나 부인이 우리를 맞으러 오고 있음을 안다고 나에게 말하는 그를 — 맞으러 동쪽으로 간다."]], [["Mina Harker’s Journal.", "「미나 하커의 일기」"]], [["6 November.--It was late in the afternoon when the Professor and I took our way towards the east whence I knew Jonathan was coming.", "11월 6일. — 교수님과 내가 조너선이 오고 있음을 내가 아는 그 동쪽을 향해 길을 나선 것은 오후 늦은 시각이었다."], ["We did not go fast, though the way was steeply downhill, for we had to take heavy rugs and wraps with us; we dared not face the possibility of being left without warmth in the cold and the snow.", "길은 가파른 내리막이었으나 우리는 빨리 가지 못했으니, 무거운 담요와 두르개를 챙겨 가야 했기 때문이다. 추위와 눈 속에서 온기 없이 남겨질지도 모를 가능성을 우리는 감히 무릅쓸 수 없었다."], ["We had to take some of our provisions, too, for we were in a perfect desolation, and, so far as we could see through the snowfall, there was not even the sign of habitation.", "먹을 것도 얼마간 챙겨 가야 했으니, 우리가 완전한 황무지에 있었고, 눈발 사이로 보이는 한 사람이 사는 흔적조차 없었기 때문이다."], ["When we had gone about a mile, I was tired with the heavy walking and sat down to rest.", "한 마일쯤 갔을 때, 나는 힘겨운 걸음에 지쳐 쉬려고 주저앉았다."], ["Then we looked back and saw where the clear line of Dracula’s castle cut the sky; for we were so deep under the hill whereon it was set that the angle of perspective of the Carpathian mountains was far below it.", "그러고서 우리는 뒤를 돌아보았고, 드라큘라 성의 또렷한 윤곽이 하늘을 가르는 곳을 보았다. 우리는 그 성이 자리한 언덕 아래로 그토록 깊이 내려와 있어서, 카르파티아 산맥의 원근의 각도가 성보다 훨씬 아래에 있었던 것이다."], ["We saw it in all its grandeur, perched a thousand feet on the summit of a sheer precipice, and with seemingly a great gap between it and the steep of the adjacent mountain on any side.", "우리는 그 온전한 장엄함을 보았다. 깎아지른 절벽 정상에 천 피트 높이로 자리한 그 성은, 사방으로 인접한 산의 가파른 비탈과 그 사이에 거대한 틈을 둔 듯했다."], ["There was something wild and uncanny about the place.", "그곳에는 무언가 거칠고 으스스한 데가 있었다."], ["We could hear the distant howling of wolves.", "우리는 멀리서 늑대들이 울부짖는 소리를 들을 수 있었다."], ["They were far off, but the sound, even though coming muffled through the deadening snowfall, was full of terror.", "그들은 멀리 있었으나, 소리를 죽이는 눈발을 뚫고 둔하게 전해 오는 그 소리에도 공포가 가득했다."], ["I knew from the way Dr. Van Helsing was searching about that he was trying to seek some strategic point, where we would be less exposed in case of attack.", "나는 판 헬싱 박사가 주위를 살피는 모습에서, 그가 습격을 당할 경우 우리가 덜 노출될 어떤 전략적 지점을 찾으려 하고 있음을 알았다."], ["The rough roadway still led downwards; we could trace it through the drifted snow.", "험한 길은 여전히 아래로 이어졌다. 우리는 쌓인 눈 사이로 그 길을 따라갈 수 있었다."]], [["In a little while the Professor signalled to me, so I got up and joined him.", "이윽고 교수님이 나에게 신호를 보냈고, 나는 일어나 그에게로 갔다."], ["He had found a wonderful spot, a sort of natural hollow in a rock, with an entrance like a doorway between two boulders.", "그는 멋진 자리를 찾아냈는데, 바위 안의 일종의 자연스러운 우묵한 곳으로, 두 개의 큰 바위 사이에 문간 같은 입구가 있었다."], ["He took me by the hand and drew me in: “See!”", "그는 내 손을 잡아 안으로 이끌었다. \"보시오!\""], ["he said, “here you will be in shelter; and if the wolves do come I can meet them one by one.”", "그가 말했다. \"여기 있으면 가려질 것이오. 그리고 늑대들이 오더라도 나는 그들을 하나씩 맞상대할 수 있소.\""], ["He brought in our furs, and made a snug nest for me, and got out some provisions and forced them upon me.", "그는 우리의 모피를 들여와 나를 위해 아늑한 보금자리를 만들고, 먹을 것을 꺼내어 나에게 억지로 권했다."], ["But I could not eat; to even try to do so was repulsive to me, and, much as I would have liked to please him, I could not bring myself to the attempt.", "그러나 나는 먹을 수 없었다. 먹으려 해보는 것조차 나에게는 역겨웠고, 그를 기쁘게 해주고 싶은 마음은 굴뚝같았으나 도저히 시도할 수가 없었다."], ["He looked very sad, but did not reproach me.", "그는 무척 슬퍼 보였으나, 나를 나무라지는 않았다."], ["Taking his field-glasses from the case, he stood on the top of the rock, and began to search the horizon.", "갑에서 쌍안경을 꺼내 들고, 그는 바위 꼭대기에 서서 지평선을 살피기 시작했다."], ["Suddenly he called out:--", "갑자기 그가 외쳤다."]], [["“Look! Madam Mina, look!", "\"보시오!"], ["look!” I sprang up and stood beside him on the rock; he handed me his glasses and pointed.", "미나 부인, 보시오! 보시오!\" 나는 벌떡 일어나 바위 위 그의 곁에 섰다."], ["The snow was now falling more heavily, and swirled about fiercely, for a high wind was beginning to blow.", "그는 나에게 쌍안경을 건네며 가리켰다. 이제 눈은 더 거세게 내리며 사납게 휘몰아쳤으니, 강풍이 불기 시작했기 때문이다."], ["However, there were times when there were pauses between the snow flurries and I could see a long way round.", "그래도 눈보라 사이사이 멎는 때가 있어, 나는 멀리까지 둘러볼 수 있었다."], ["From the height where we were it was possible to see a great distance; and far off, beyond the white waste of snow, I could see the river lying like a black ribbon in kinks and curls as it wound its way.", "우리가 있는 높은 곳에서는 아주 먼 데까지 볼 수 있었다. 멀리, 눈의 하얀 황무지 너머로, 강이 굽이굽이 휘돌아 가며 검은 리본처럼 누워 있는 것이 보였다."], ["Straight in front of us and not far off--in fact, so near that I wondered we had not noticed before--came a group of mounted men hurrying along.", "우리 바로 앞, 그리 멀지 않은 곳에 — 실은 어찌나 가까운지 우리가 진작 알아채지 못한 것이 의아할 정도였는데 — 말 탄 사내들의 무리가 서둘러 오고 있었다."], ["In the midst of them was a cart, a long leiter-wagon which swept from side to side, like a dog’s tail wagging, with each stern inequality of the road.", "그들 한가운데에는 수레가 있었는데, 길의 험한 굴곡마다 마치 개의 꼬리가 흔들리듯 좌우로 휩쓸리는 긴 짐수레였다."], ["Outlined against the snow as they were, I could see from the men’s clothes that they were peasants or gypsies of some kind.", "눈을 배경으로 윤곽이 드러난 그들의 옷차림으로 나는 그들이 농부이거나 어떤 부류의 집시임을 알 수 있었다."]], [["On the cart was a great square chest.", "수레 위에는 커다란 네모난 궤짝이 있었다."], ["My heart leaped as I saw it, for I felt that the end was coming.", "그것을 보자 내 심장이 뛰었으니, 끝이 다가오고 있음을 느꼈기 때문이다."], ["The evening was now drawing close, and well I knew that at sunset the Thing, which was till then imprisoned there, would take new freedom and could in any of many forms elude all pursuit.", "이제 저녁이 가까워지고 있었고, 나는 잘 알고 있었다. 해가 지면, 그때까지 거기 갇혀 있던 그것이 새로이 자유를 얻어 여러 모습 가운데 어느 것으로든 모든 추격을 따돌릴 수 있음을."], ["In fear I turned to the Professor; to my consternation, however, he was not there.", "두려움에 나는 교수님에게로 돌아섰는데, 그러나 당황스럽게도 그는 거기 없었다."], ["An instant later, I saw him below me.", "다음 순간, 나는 그가 내 아래에 있는 것을 보았다."], ["Round the rock he had drawn a circle, such as we had found shelter in last night.", "그는 바위 둘레에, 우리가 지난밤 몸을 가렸던 것과 같은 원을 그려둔 것이었다."], ["When he had completed it he stood beside me again, saying:--", "그것을 다 그리고 나서 그는 다시 내 곁에 서서 말했다."]], [["“At least you shall be safe here from him!”", "\"적어도 여기서는 그로부터 당신이 안전할 것이오!\""], ["He took the glasses from me, and at the next lull of the snow swept the whole space below us. “See,”", "그는 나에게서 쌍안경을 받아 들고, 다음번 눈이 멎었을 때 우리 아래의 온 들판을 훑었다. \"보시오.\""], ["he said, “they come quickly; they are flogging the horses, and galloping as hard as they can.”", "그가 말했다. \"그들이 빨리 오고 있소. 말에 채찍질을 하며, 있는 힘껏 질주하고 있소.\""], ["He paused and went on in a hollow voice:--", "그는 잠시 멈추었다가 공허한 목소리로 이어 말했다."]], [["“They are racing for the sunset. We may be too late.", "\"그들은 해가 지기 전에 닿으려고 달리고 있소."], ["God’s will be done!”", "우리가 너무 늦었을지도 모르오."], ["Down came another blinding rush of driving snow, and the whole landscape was blotted out.", "신의 뜻이 이루어지기를!\" 또 한 차례 앞을 가리는 눈보라가 휘몰아쳐 온 풍경을 지워버렸다."], ["It soon passed, however, and once more his glasses were fixed on the plain.", "그러나 그것은 곧 지나갔고, 다시금 그의 쌍안경이 평원에 고정되었다."], ["Then came a sudden cry:--", "그때 갑작스러운 외침이 들렸다."]], [["“Look! Look! Look! See, two horsemen follow fast, coming up from the south.", "\"보시오! 보시오! 보시오! 저것 보시오, 말 탄 두 사람이 빠르게 따라오고 있소, 남쪽에서 올라오고 있소."], ["It must be Quincey and John.", "퀸시와 존임에 틀림없소."], ["Take the glass.", "쌍안경을 받으시오."], ["Look before the snow blots it all out!”", "눈이 모두 지워버리기 전에 보시오!\""], ["I took it and looked.", "나는 그것을 받아 보았다."], ["The two men might be Dr. Seward and Mr. Morris.", "두 사람은 슈어드 박사와 모리스 씨인 듯했다."], ["I knew at all events that neither of them was Jonathan.", "어쨌든 그 둘 가운데 조너선은 없음을 나는 알았다."], ["At the same time I knew that Jonathan was not far off; looking around I saw on the north side of the coming party two other men, riding at break-neck speed.", "동시에 나는 조너선이 멀지 않은 곳에 있음을 알았다. 둘러보니, 다가오는 무리의 북쪽으로 또 다른 두 사람이 목숨을 건 듯한 속도로 말을 달리고 있는 것이 보였다."], ["One of them I knew was Jonathan, and the other I took, of course, to be Lord Godalming.", "그중 하나는 조너선임을 나는 알았고, 다른 하나는 물론 고댈밍 경이리라 여겼다."], ["They, too, were pursuing the party with the cart.", "그들 역시 수레를 거느린 무리를 쫓고 있었다."], ["When I told the Professor he shouted in glee like a schoolboy, and, after looking intently till a snow fall made sight impossible, he laid his Winchester rifle ready for use against the boulder at the opening of our shelter.", "내가 이를 교수님에게 말하자 그는 학생처럼 환호성을 질렀고, 눈발에 시야가 보이지 않게 될 때까지 골똘히 바라본 뒤, 윈체스터 소총을 우리 은신처 입구의 바위에 기대어 언제든 쓸 수 있게 두었다."], ["“They are all converging,”", "\"그들이 모두 모여들고 있소.\""], ["he said. “When the time comes we shall have gypsies on all sides.”", "그가 말했다. \"때가 오면 우리는 사방에 집시들을 두게 될 것이오.\""], ["I got out my revolver ready to hand, for whilst we were speaking the howling of wolves came louder and closer.", "나는 권총을 꺼내어 손 닿는 곳에 두었으니, 우리가 이야기를 나누는 동안 늑대들의 울부짖음이 더 크고 더 가까이 들려왔기 때문이다."], ["When the snow storm abated a moment we looked again.", "눈보라가 한순간 잦아들었을 때 우리는 다시 바라보았다."], ["It was strange to see the snow falling in such heavy flakes close to us, and beyond, the sun shining more and more brightly as it sank down towards the far mountain tops.", "그토록 굵은 눈송이가 우리 가까이에 내리는데, 그 너머로는 해가 먼 산봉우리들을 향해 가라앉으며 점점 더 밝게 빛나는 것이 기이했다."], ["Sweeping the glass all around us I could see here and there dots moving singly and in twos and threes and larger numbers--the wolves were gathering for their prey.", "쌍안경으로 우리 사방을 훑으니, 여기저기 점들이 하나씩, 둘씩, 셋씩, 그리고 더 큰 무리를 이루어 움직이는 것이 보였다. 늑대들이 먹잇감을 노리고 모여들고 있었던 것이다."]], [["Every instant seemed an age whilst we waited.", "우리가 기다리는 동안 매 순간이 한 세월처럼 느껴졌다."], ["The wind came now in fierce bursts, and the snow was driven with fury as it swept upon us in circling eddies.", "이제 바람이 사나운 돌풍으로 몰아쳤고, 빙빙 도는 소용돌이로 우리를 휩쓸며 눈이 맹렬히 흩날렸다."], ["At times we could not see an arm’s length before us; but at others, as the hollow-sounding wind swept by us, it seemed to clear the air-space around us so that we could see afar off.", "어떤 때에는 한 팔 길이 앞도 보이지 않았다. 그러나 또 어떤 때에는, 공허한 소리를 내며 바람이 우리 곁을 스쳐 지날 때, 우리 둘레의 공간이 트여 멀리까지 볼 수 있을 듯했다."], ["We had of late been so accustomed to watch for sunrise and sunset, that we knew with fair accuracy when it would be; and we knew that before long the sun would set.", "우리는 근래 일출과 일몰을 살피는 데 워낙 익숙해진 터라, 그때가 언제일지 꽤 정확히 알았다. 그리고 우리는 머지않아 해가 지리라는 것을 알았다."], ["It was hard to believe that by our watches it was less than an hour that we waited in that rocky shelter before the various bodies began to converge close upon us.", "우리 시계로 보아, 갖가지 무리가 우리 가까이로 모여들기 시작하기 전까지 우리가 그 바위 은신처에서 기다린 것이 한 시간도 채 못 된다는 것이 믿기 어려웠다."], ["The wind came now with fiercer and more bitter sweeps, and more steadily from the north.", "이제 바람은 더 사납고 더 매서운 돌풍으로, 더 한결같이 북쪽에서 불어왔다."], ["It seemingly had driven the snow clouds from us, for, with only occasional bursts, the snow fell.", "그것이 눈구름을 우리에게서 몰아낸 듯, 이따금 돌풍이 일 때를 빼고는 눈이 그치다시피 했다."], ["We could distinguish clearly the individuals of each party, the pursued and the pursuers.", "우리는 각 무리의 개개인을, 쫓기는 자들과 쫓는 자들을 또렷이 분간할 수 있었다."], ["Strangely enough those pursued did not seem to realise, or at least to care, that they were pursued; they seemed, however, to hasten with redoubled speed as the sun dropped lower and lower on the mountain tops.", "기이하게도 쫓기는 자들은 자신들이 쫓기고 있음을 깨닫지 못하는 듯했고, 적어도 개의치 않는 듯했다. 그러나 그들은 해가 산봉우리 위로 점점 더 낮게 떨어지자 곱절의 속도로 서두르는 듯했다."]], [["Closer and closer they drew.", "그들은 점점 더 가까이 다가왔다."], ["The Professor and I crouched down behind our rock, and held our weapons ready; I could see that he was determined that they should not pass.", "교수님과 나는 바위 뒤에 몸을 웅크리고 무기를 겨눌 채비를 했다. 나는 그가 그들을 결코 지나가게 하지 않으리라 작정했음을 알 수 있었다."], ["One and all were quite unaware of our presence.", "그들은 하나같이 우리가 있음을 전혀 알아채지 못했다."]], [["All at once two voices shouted out to: “Halt!”", "별안간 두 목소리가 외쳤다. \"멈춰라!\""], ["One was my Jonathan’s, raised in a high key of passion; the other Mr. Morris’ strong resolute tone of quiet command.", "하나는 격정으로 높이 올라간 내 조너선의 목소리였고, 다른 하나는 모리스 씨의 강하고 단호한, 차분한 명령의 어조였다."], ["The gypsies may not have known the language, but there was no mistaking the tone, in whatever tongue the words were spoken.", "집시들은 그 말을 알아듣지 못했을지 모르나, 어떤 언어로 그 말이 내뱉어졌든 그 어조만은 오해할 여지가 없었다."], ["Instinctively they reined in, and at the instant Lord Godalming and Jonathan dashed up at one side and Dr. Seward and Mr. Morris on the other.", "본능적으로 그들은 고삐를 당겼고, 그 순간 고댈밍 경과 조너선이 한쪽에서, 슈어드 박사와 모리스 씨가 다른 쪽에서 달려들었다."], ["The leader of the gypsies, a splendid-looking fellow who sat his horse like a centaur, waved them back, and in a fierce voice gave to his companions some word to proceed.", "집시들의 두목은, 켄타우로스처럼 말 위에 앉은 멋진 풍모의 사내였는데, 그들에게 물러나라고 손짓하고는 사나운 목소리로 동료들에게 계속 나아가라는 무슨 말을 내뱉었다."], ["They lashed the horses which sprang forward; but the four men raised their Winchester rifles, and in an unmistakable way commanded them to stop. At the same moment Dr.", "그들이 말을 후려치자 말들이 앞으로 튀어나갔다. 그러나 우리 쪽 네 사내가 윈체스터 소총을 들어, 오해할 여지 없는 태도로 멈추라고 명령했다."], ["Van Helsing and I rose behind the rock and pointed our weapons at them.", "같은 순간 판 헬싱 박사와 나는 바위 뒤에서 일어나 그들에게 무기를 겨누었다."], ["Seeing that they were surrounded the men tightened their reins and drew up.", "자신들이 포위되었음을 본 사내들은 고삐를 죄어 멈추어 섰다."], ["The leader turned to them and gave a word at which every man of the gypsy party drew what weapon he carried, knife or pistol, and held himself in readiness to attack.", "두목이 그들에게로 돌아서서 한마디를 내뱉자, 집시 무리의 모든 사내가 저마다 지닌 무기를, 칼이든 권총이든 빼 들고 공격할 채비를 갖추었다."], ["Issue was joined in an instant.", "한순간에 싸움이 붙었다."]], [["The leader, with a quick movement of his rein, threw his horse out in front, and pointing first to the sun--now close down on the hill tops--and then to the castle, said something which I did not understand.", "두목은 고삐를 잰싸게 놀려 자기 말을 앞으로 내몰고는, 먼저 해를 — 이제 산봉우리에 바짝 내려와 있는 — 가리키고 다음으로 성을 가리키며, 내가 알아듣지 못하는 무슨 말을 했다."], ["For answer, all four men of our party threw themselves from their horses and dashed towards the cart.", "그 대답으로 우리 쪽 네 사내가 모두 말에서 몸을 던져 수레를 향해 달려들었다."], ["I should have felt terrible fear at seeing Jonathan in such danger, but that the ardour of battle must have been upon me as well as the rest of them; I felt no fear, but only a wild, surging desire to do something.", "그토록 위험에 처한 조너선을 보고 나는 끔찍한 두려움을 느꼈어야 마땅했으나, 나에게도 나머지 사람들과 마찬가지로 전투의 열기가 올랐던 모양이다. 나는 두려움을 느끼지 않았고, 오직 무언가를 하고 싶다는 거칠고 솟구치는 욕망만을 느꼈다."], ["Seeing the quick movement of our parties, the leader of the gypsies gave a command; his men instantly formed round the cart in a sort of undisciplined endeavour, each one shouldering and pushing the other in his eagerness to carry out the order.", "우리 쪽 무리의 잰 움직임을 본 집시 두목이 명령을 내리자, 그의 부하들이 곧장 수레 둘레에 일종의 질서 없는 안간힘으로 모여들어, 저마다 그 명령을 따르려는 열의에 서로를 떠밀고 밀쳤다."]], [["In the midst of this I could see that Jonathan on one side of the ring of men, and Quincey on the other, were forcing a way to the cart; it was evident that they were bent on finishing their task before the sun should set.", "이 와중에 나는 사내들의 고리 한쪽에서는 조너선이, 다른 쪽에서는 퀸시가 수레로 길을 뚫고 있는 것을 볼 수 있었다. 그들이 해가 지기 전에 자신들의 일을 끝맺으려 작정했음이 분명했다."], ["Nothing seemed to stop or even to hinder them. Neither the levelled weapons nor the flashing knives of the gypsies in front, nor the howling of the wolves behind, appeared to even attract their attention.", "그 무엇도 그들을 멈추거나 가로막을 수조차 없는 듯했다. 앞을 막은 집시들의 겨눈 무기도, 번득이는 칼도, 뒤에서 울부짖는 늑대들도 그들의 주의를 끄는 것조차 못하는 듯했다."], ["Jonathan’s impetuosity, and the manifest singleness of his purpose, seemed to overawe those in front of him; instinctively they cowered, aside and let him pass.", "조너선의 맹렬함과 그 목적의 명백한 한결같음이 그의 앞에 있는 자들을 위압하는 듯했다. 본능적으로 그들은 움츠리며 옆으로 비켜 그를 지나가게 했다."], ["In an instant he had jumped upon the cart, and, with a strength which seemed incredible, raised the great box, and flung it over the wheel to the ground.", "한순간에 그는 수레 위로 뛰어올라, 믿기 어려울 만한 힘으로 그 거대한 궤짝을 들어 올려 바퀴 너머 땅으로 내던졌다."], ["In the meantime, Mr. Morris had had to use force to pass through his side of the ring of Szgany.", "그동안 모리스 씨는 슈가니의 고리 가운데 자기 쪽을 뚫고 지나가기 위해 힘을 써야 했다."], ["All the time I had been breathlessly watching Jonathan I had, with the tail of my eye, seen him pressing desperately forward, and had seen the knives of the gypsies flash as he won a way through them, and they cut at him.", "내가 숨죽이고 조너선을 지켜보는 내내, 나는 곁눈질로 그가 필사적으로 앞으로 밀고 나아가는 것을, 그리고 그가 그들을 뚫고 길을 내자 집시들의 칼이 번득이며 그를 베는 것을 보았다."], ["He had parried with his great bowie knife, and at first I thought that he too had come through in safety; but as he sprang beside Jonathan, who had by now jumped from the cart, I could see that with his left hand he was clutching at his side, and that the blood was spurting through his fingers.", "그는 자신의 커다란 보위 나이프로 막아냈고, 처음에는 그도 무사히 헤쳐 나온 줄 알았다. 그러나 그가 이제 막 수레에서 뛰어내린 조너선의 곁으로 뛰어들 때, 나는 그가 왼손으로 옆구리를 움켜쥐고 있고, 그 손가락 사이로 피가 솟구치는 것을 볼 수 있었다."], ["He did not delay notwithstanding this, for as Jonathan, with desperate energy, attacked one end of the chest, attempting to prize off the lid with his great Kukri knife, he attacked the other frantically with his bowie.", "그럼에도 그는 지체하지 않았으니, 조너선이 필사적인 힘으로 궤짝의 한쪽 끝에 달려들어 그 커다란 쿠크리 칼로 뚜껑을 비틀어 떼어내려 하자, 그는 다른 쪽 끝을 보위 나이프로 미친 듯이 공격했다."], ["Under the efforts of both men the lid began to yield; the nails drew with a quick screeching sound, and the top of the box was thrown back.", "두 사내의 안간힘에 뚜껑이 들리기 시작했다. 못들이 빠른 끼익 소리를 내며 빠졌고, 궤짝의 윗판이 젖혀졌다."]], [["By this time the gypsies, seeing themselves covered by the Winchesters, and at the mercy of Lord Godalming and Dr. Seward, had given in and made no resistance.", "이때쯤 집시들은 자신들이 윈체스터의 겨눔 아래 놓여, 고댈밍 경과 슈어드 박사의 처분에 달렸음을 보고 항복하여 더는 저항하지 않았다."], ["The sun was almost down on the mountain tops, and the shadows of the whole group fell long upon the snow.", "해는 산봉우리에 거의 닿아 있었고, 무리 전체의 그림자가 눈 위로 길게 드리워졌다."], ["I saw the Count lying within the box upon the earth, some of which the rude falling from the cart had scattered over him.", "나는 백작이 궤짝 안 흙 위에 누워 있는 것을 보았다. 수레에서 거칠게 떨어질 때 흩어진 흙이 얼마간 그 위를 덮고 있었다."], ["He was deathly pale, just like a waxen image, and the red eyes glared with the horrible vindictive look which I knew too well.", "그는 밀랍 인형처럼 죽은 듯 창백했고, 붉은 눈은 내가 너무도 잘 아는 그 끔찍한 앙심의 빛으로 이글거렸다."]], [["As I looked, the eyes saw the sinking sun, and the look of hate in them turned to triumph.", "내가 바라보는 순간, 그 눈이 지는 해를 보았고, 거기 어린 증오의 빛이 승리의 빛으로 바뀌었다."]], [["But, on the instant, came the sweep and flash of Jonathan’s great knife.", "그러나 그 찰나에, 조너선의 커다란 칼이 휘둘려 번득였다."], ["I shrieked as I saw it shear through the throat; whilst at the same moment Mr. Morris’s bowie knife plunged into the heart.", "나는 그것이 목을 베어 가르는 것을 보고 비명을 질렀다. 같은 순간 모리스 씨의 보위 나이프가 심장에 박혀 들었다."]], [["It was like a miracle; but before our very eyes, and almost in the drawing of a breath, the whole body crumbled into dust and passed from our sight.", "그것은 기적과도 같았다. 그러나 바로 우리 눈앞에서, 거의 숨 한 번 쉬는 사이에, 온몸이 가루로 부서져 우리의 시야에서 사라졌다."]], [["I shall be glad as long as I live that even in that moment of final dissolution, there was in the face a look of peace, such as I never could have imagined might have rested there.", "내가 살아 있는 한 기쁘게 여길 것은, 그 최후의 소멸의 순간에조차 그 얼굴에 평화의 빛이 어려 있었다는 것이다. 거기 그런 빛이 깃들 수 있으리라고는 나는 결코 상상하지 못했을 것이다."]], [["The Castle of Dracula now stood out against the red sky, and every stone of its broken battlements was articulated against the light of the setting sun.", "이제 드라큘라 성이 붉은 하늘을 배경으로 도드라졌고, 그 부서진 흉벽의 돌 하나하나가 지는 햇빛에 또렷이 윤곽을 드러냈다."]], [["The gypsies, taking us as in some way the cause of the extraordinary disappearance of the dead man, turned, without a word, and rode away as if for their lives.", "집시들은 그 죽은 자의 기이한 사라짐을 어떤 식으로든 우리 탓으로 여기고는, 한마디 말도 없이 돌아서서 죽기 살기로 말을 몰아 달아났다."], ["Those who were unmounted jumped upon the leiter-wagon and shouted to the horsemen not to desert them.", "말을 타지 못한 자들은 짐수레에 뛰어올라, 말 탄 자들에게 자기들을 버리지 말라고 외쳤다."], ["The wolves, which had withdrawn to a safe distance, followed in their wake, leaving us alone.", "안전한 거리로 물러나 있던 늑대들이 그들의 뒤를 따라가며, 우리만 남겨두었다."]], [["Mr. Morris, who had sunk to the ground, leaned on his elbow, holding his hand pressed to his side; the blood still gushed through his fingers.", "땅에 주저앉아 있던 모리스 씨는 한쪽 팔꿈치에 몸을 기댄 채, 손으로 옆구리를 누르고 있었다. 피가 여전히 손가락 사이로 솟구쳤다."], ["I flew to him, for the Holy circle did not now keep me back; so did the two doctors.", "나는 그에게로 달려갔으니, 이제 신성한 원이 나를 막지 않았기 때문이다. 두 의사도 그리했다."], ["Jonathan knelt behind him and the wounded man laid back his head on his shoulder.", "조너선이 그의 뒤에 무릎을 꿇었고, 부상당한 그는 머리를 그의 어깨에 기댔다."], ["With a sigh he took, with a feeble effort, my hand in that of his own which was unstained.", "한숨과 함께 그는 가냘픈 힘으로, 피로 더럽혀지지 않은 자기 손으로 내 손을 잡았다."], ["He must have seen the anguish of my heart in my face, for he smiled at me and said:--", "그는 내 얼굴에서 내 마음의 비통함을 보았던 모양으로, 나를 향해 미소 지으며 말했다."]], [["“I am only too happy to have been of any service! Oh, God!”", "\"이렇게 조금이나마 도움이 되었다니 더없이 기쁩니다! 아, 하느님!\""], ["he cried suddenly, struggling up to a sitting posture and pointing to me, “It was worth for this to die! Look! look!”", "그가 갑자기 외치며, 안간힘을 다해 일어나 앉으려 하면서 나를 가리켰다. \"이를 위해서라면 죽을 가치가 있었습니다! 보세요! 보세요!\""]], [["The sun was now right down upon the mountain top, and the red gleams fell upon my face, so that it was bathed in rosy light.", "이제 해는 산봉우리 바로 위에 내려와 있었고, 붉은 빛줄기가 내 얼굴에 비쳐, 내 얼굴이 장밋빛에 잠겼다."], ["With one impulse the men sank on their knees and a deep and earnest “Amen” broke from all as their eyes followed the pointing of his finger.", "한마음으로 사내들이 무릎을 꿇었고, 그들의 눈이 그의 손가락이 가리키는 곳을 좇으며 모두에게서 깊고 진실한 \"아멘\"이 터져 나왔다."], ["The dying man spoke:--", "죽어가는 이가 말했다."]], [["“Now God be thanked that all has not been in vain! See!", "\"이제 신께 감사를, 모든 것이 헛되지 않았으니! 보세요!"], ["the snow is not more stainless than her forehead!", "그녀의 이마가 저 눈보다 더 티 없습니다!"], ["The curse has passed away!”", "저주가 사라졌어요!\""]], [["And, to our bitter grief, with a smile and in silence, he died, a gallant gentleman.", "그리고, 우리의 쓰라린 비탄 속에, 미소를 띤 채 말없이, 그는 숨을 거두었다. 용맹한 신사로서."]], [["Seven years ago we all went through the flames; and the happiness of some of us since then is, we think, well worth the pain we endured.", "부기(附記)\n칠 년 전 우리는 모두 그 불길을 헤치고 지나왔다. 그 뒤로 우리 가운데 더러가 누린 행복은, 우리가 견뎌낸 고통에 충분히 값할 만한 것이라고 우리는 생각한다."], ["It is an added joy to Mina and to me that our boy’s birthday is the same day as that on which Quincey Morris died.", "우리 아들의 생일이 퀸시 모리스가 죽은 바로 그날이라는 것은 미나와 나에게 더해진 기쁨이다."], ["His mother holds, I know, the secret belief that some of our brave friend’s spirit has passed into him.", "그 애 어머니는, 내가 알기로, 우리의 용감한 벗의 영혼 가운데 얼마가 그 애에게로 옮겨갔다는 은밀한 믿음을 품고 있다."], ["His bundle of names links all our little band of men together; but we call him Quincey.", "그 애의 여러 이름은 우리 작은 사내들의 무리 전체를 하나로 잇는다. 그러나 우리는 그 애를 퀸시라고 부른다."]], [["In the summer of this year we made a journey to Transylvania, and went over the old ground which was, and is, to us so full of vivid and terrible memories.", "올여름 우리는 트란실바니아로 여행을 떠나, 우리에게 그토록 생생하고 끔찍한 기억으로 가득했던, 그리고 지금도 그러한 그 옛 땅을 두루 돌아보았다."], ["It was almost impossible to believe that the things which we had seen with our own eyes and heard with our own ears were living truths.", "우리가 우리 두 눈으로 보고 우리 두 귀로 들은 일들이 살아 있는 진실이었다는 것을 믿기란 거의 불가능했다."], ["Every trace of all that had been was blotted out.", "그 모든 일의 흔적은 깡그리 지워져 있었다."], ["The castle stood as before, reared high above a waste of desolation.", "성은 여전히 예전처럼 황량한 폐허 위로 높이 솟아 있었다."]], [["When we got home we were talking of the old time--which we could all look back on without despair, for Godalming and Seward are both happily married.", "집으로 돌아와 우리는 그 옛 시절에 대해 이야기를 나누었다. 우리 모두 절망 없이 돌아볼 수 있는 시절이었으니, 고댈밍과 슈어드 둘 다 행복하게 결혼했기 때문이다. 나는 그토록 오래전 우리가 돌아온 이래로 줄곧 보관해온 금고에서 그 서류들을 꺼냈다."], ["I took the papers from the safe where they had been ever since our return so long ago.", "우리는 한 가지 사실에 새삼 놀랐는데, 그 기록을 이루는 그 방대한 자료 가운데 진본 문서라 할 만한 것이 거의 하나도 없다는 것이었다."], ["We were struck with the fact, that in all the mass of material of which the record is composed, there is hardly one authentic document; nothing but a mass of typewriting, except the later note-books of Mina and Seward and myself, and Van Helsing’s memorandum.", "미나와 슈어드와 나의 후기 수첩들, 그리고 판 헬싱의 비망록을 빼면, 한 무더기의 타자 친 글뿐이었다. 설령 우리가 원한다 해도, 이것들을 이토록 황당한 이야기의 증거로 받아들여 달라고 그 누구에게도 청하기 어려울 것이다. 판 헬싱은 우리 아들을 무릎에 앉히고는 이렇게 모든 것을 요약하며 말했다."], ["We could hardly ask any one, even did we wish to, to accept these as proofs of so wild a story.", "\"우리에게는 아무 증거도 필요 없네. 우리는 누구에게도 우리를 믿어 달라 청하지 않는다네! 이 아이는 언젠가 제 어머니가 얼마나 용감하고 의로운 여인인지 알게 될 걸세."], ["Van Helsing summed it all up as he said, with our boy on his knee:--", "이미 그 애는 어머니의 다정함과 사랑 어린 보살핌을 알고 있지. 훗날 그 애는 어떤 사내들이 그녀를 어찌나 사랑했던지 그녀를 위해 그토록 많은 것을 무릅썼는지 이해하게 될 걸세.\""]], [["“We want no proofs; we ask none to believe us! This boy will some day know what a brave and gallant woman his mother is. Already he knows her sweetness and loving care; later on he will understand how some men so loved her, that they did dare much for her sake.”", "조너선 하커. 끝"]]];
